const PRELOADED_QUESTIONS=[{"id":"pdf-torts-001","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"Unaware that a lawyer was in the county courthouse library late on a Friday afternoon, when it was unusual for anyone to be using the library, a clerk locked the library door and left. The lawyer found herself locked in when she tried to leave the library at 7:00 p.m. It was midnight before the lawyer's family could find out where she was and get her out. The lawyer was very annoyed by her detention but was not otherwise harmed by it. Does the lawyer have a viable claim for false imprisonment against the clerk?","choices":{"A":"No, because it was unusual for anyone to use the library late on a Friday afternoon.","B":"No, because the clerk did not intend to confine the lawyer.","C":"Yes, because the clerk should have checked to make sure no one was in the library before the clerk locked the door.","D":"Yes, because the lawyer was aware of being confined."},"answer":"B","explanation":"False imprisonment occurs when: the defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff to a bounded area and the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs OR sustains actual harm as a result of the confinement. Here, the lawyer found herself locked in the library after the clerk locked the door (aware of confinement). However, the clerk did not lock the door with the intent to confine the lawyer because the clerk was unaware that the lawyer was in the library (no intent). Therefore, the clerk is not liable for false imprisonment. (Choice A) The fact that it was unusual for anyone to use the library at that time may show that the clerk was not negligent in failing to detect the lawyer, but does not show whether the clerk acted intentionally. Therefore, this fact has no effect on the lawyer's false imprisonment claim. (Choice C) Whether the clerk should have checked to make sure no one was in the library before locking the door is a negligence issue (ie, what a reasonably prudent person in the clerk's position would have done). It is irrelevant to a claim for false imprisonment (an intentional tort requiring intent to confine the plaintiff). (Choice D) Although the lawyer was aware of the confinement, the clerk is not liable for false imprisonment because the clerk did not intend to confine the lawyer when the clerk locked the door.\n\nEducational objective: False imprisonment occurs when (1) a defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff to a bounded area and (2) the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs OR sustains actual harm as a result of the confinement.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 35 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (false imprisonment).","educationalObjective":"False imprisonment occurs when (1) a defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff to a bounded area and (2) the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs OR sustains actual harm as a result of the confinement.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 35 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (false imprisonment).","rule":"False imprisonment occurs when (1) a defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff to a bounded area and (2) the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs OR sustains actual harm as a result of the confinement.","irac":{"issue":"Does the lawyer have a viable claim for false imprisonment against the clerk?","rule":"False imprisonment occurs when (1) a defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff to a bounded area and (2) the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs OR sustains actual harm as a result of the confinement.","analysis":"False imprisonment occurs when: the defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff to a bounded area and the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs OR sustains actual harm as a result of the confinement. Here, the lawyer found herself locked in the library after the clerk locked the door (aware of confinement). However, the clerk did not lock the door with the intent to confine the lawyer because the clerk was unaware that the lawyer was in the library (no intent). Therefore, the clerk is not liable for false imprisonment. (Choice A) The fact that it was unusual for anyone to use the library at that time may show that the clerk was not negligent in failing to detect the lawyer, but does not show whether the clerk acted intentionally. Therefore, this fact has no effect on the lawyer's false imprisonment claim. (Choice C) Whether the clerk should have checked to make sure no one was in the library before locking the door is a negligence issue (ie, what a reasonably prudent person in the clerk's position would have done). It is irrelevant to a claim for false imprisonment (an intentional tort requiring intent to confine the plaintiff). (Choice D) Although the lawyer was aware of the confinement, the clerk is not liable for false imprisonment because the clerk did not intend to confine the lawyer when the clerk locked the door.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the clerk did not intend to confine the lawyer."},"questionZh":"周五下午，一位律师在县法院图书馆待到很晚，而通常这个时候图书馆里没人，一位办事员却不知道她还在里面，于是锁上了图书馆的门就离开了。\n\n晚上7点，这位律师试图离开时发现自己被锁在了里面。\n\n直到午夜，律师的家人才找到她并把她救了出来。\n\n律师对被拘留感到非常恼火，但并没有受到其他伤害。\n\n这位律师是否可以对办事员提起非法监禁的诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为周五下午很晚的时候图书馆里没人。","B":"否，因为办事员并非有意限制这位律师的人身自由。","C":"是，因为办事员在锁门之前应该确认图书馆里没有人。","D":"是，因为这位律师知道自己被限制了人身自由。"},"explanationZh":"非法拘禁（False imprisonment）的构成要件包括：被告故意将原告限制在特定范围内，且原告在被限制时知晓该限制，或者因该限制而遭受了实际损害。\n\n在本案中，虽然律师在职员锁门后发现自己被困在图书馆内（即知晓被限制），但职员锁门时并无限制律师行动的意图，因为职员当时并不知道律师在图书馆内（即缺乏故意）。\n\n因此，该职员不承担非法拘禁的法律责任。\n\n（选项 A）当时很少有人使用图书馆这一事实，或许能证明职员未发现律师并非出于疏忽，但这并不能证明职员的行为是否出于故意。\n\n因此，该事实对律师提出的非法拘禁索赔不产生影响。\n\n（选项 C）职员在锁门前是否应当检查以确保图书馆内无人，属于过失问题（即处于该职员位置的理性谨慎之人会如何行止的问题）。\n\n这与非法拘禁索赔（属于一种要求具备限制原告意图的故意侵权行为）无关。\n\n（选项 D）尽管律师知晓自己被限制，但职员不承担非法拘禁责任，因为职员在锁门时并无限制律师行动的意图。\n\n教学目标：非法拘禁的构成要件为：(1) 被告故意将原告限制在特定范围内；以及 (2) 原告在被限制时知晓该限制，或者因该限制而遭受了实际损害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 35 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（关于非法拘禁的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"非法监禁是指：(1) 被告故意将原告限制在限定区域内；(2) 原告在被限制时知晓其被限制，或因该限制而遭受实际伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师是否可以对书记员提起非法监禁诉讼？","rule":"非法监禁是指：(1) 被告故意将原告限制在限定区域内；(2) 原告在被限制时知晓其被限制，或因该限制而遭受实际伤害。","analysis":"非法拘禁（False imprisonment）的构成要件包括：被告故意将原告限制在特定范围内，且原告在被限制时知晓该限制，或者因该限制而遭受了实际损害。\n\n在本案中，虽然律师在职员锁门后发现自己被困在图书馆内（即知晓被限制），但职员锁门时并无限制律师行动的意图，因为职员当时并不知道律师在图书馆内（即缺乏故意）。\n\n因此，该职员不承担非法拘禁的法律责任。\n\n（选项 A）当时很少有人使用图书馆这一事实，或许能证明职员未发现律师并非出于疏忽，但这并不能证明职员的行为是否出于故意。\n\n因此，该事实对律师提出的非法拘禁索赔不产生影响。\n\n（选项 C）职员在锁门前是否应当检查以确保图书馆内无人，属于过失问题（即处于该职员位置的理性谨慎之人会如何行止的问题）。\n\n这与非法拘禁索赔（属于一种要求具备限制原告意图的故意侵权行为）无关。\n\n（选项 D）尽管律师知晓自己被限制，但职员不承担非法拘禁责任，因为职员在锁门时并无限制律师行动的意图。\n\n教学目标：非法拘禁的构成要件为：(1) 被告故意将原告限制在特定范围内；以及 (2) 原告在被限制时知晓该限制，或者因该限制而遭受了实际损害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 35 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（关于非法拘禁的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 B：否，因为办事员并非有意限制这位律师的人身自由。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"false imprisonment","ipa":"/fˈɔls ˌɪmpɹˈɪzənmənt/","syllables":"false im·pris·on·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"非法拘禁：故意将他人限制在有界区域，且其知情或受到实际损害","contextualMeaning":"非法拘禁：故意将他人限制在有界区域，且其知情或受到实际损害","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Does the lawyer have a viable claim for false imprisonment against the clerk?","synonyms":["unlawful confinement","wrongful detention"]},{"term":"intentional tort","ipa":"/ˌɪntˈɛnʃənəl tˈɔɹt/","syllables":"in·ten·tional tort","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"故意侵权：行为人具有所需故意并实施受保护利益的侵害","contextualMeaning":"故意侵权：行为人具有所需故意并实施受保护利益的侵害","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"It is irrelevant to a claim for false imprisonment (an intentional tort requiring intent to confine the plaintiff).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"bounded area","ipa":"/bˈaʊndəd ˈɛɹiə/","syllables":"bounded area","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"有界区域：受害人没有合理安全出口的限制空间","contextualMeaning":"有界区域：受害人没有合理安全出口的限制空间","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"False imprisonment occurs when (1) a defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff to a bounded area and (2) the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs OR sustains actual harm as a result of the confinement.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"actual harm","ipa":"/ˈæktʃəwəl hˈɑɹm/","syllables":"ac·tual harm","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"实际损害：身体、财产、经济或其他可认定的真实不利益","contextualMeaning":"实际损害：身体、财产、经济或其他可认定的真实不利益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"False imprisonment occurs when (1) a defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff to a bounded area and (2) the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs OR sustains actual harm as a result of the confinement.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"intentionally","ipa":"/ˌɪntˈɛnʃənəli/","syllables":"in·ten·tion·ally","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 故意地, 有意地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“intentionally”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"False imprisonment occurs when (1) a defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff to a bounded area and (2) the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs OR sustains actual harm as a result of the confinement.","synonyms":["deliberately","designedly","on purpose","purposely"]},{"term":"confinement","ipa":"/kʌnfˈaɪnmənt/","syllables":"con·fine·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 限制, 被禁闭, 分娩；[医] 分娩, 生产","contextualMeaning":"拘禁或限制：将人限制在特定区域内","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:confinements","sourceSentence":"False imprisonment occurs when (1) a defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff to a bounded area and (2) the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs OR sustains actual harm as a result of the confinement.","synonyms":["restriction","parturiency","labor","labour"]},{"term":"negligence","ipa":"/nˈɛɡlədʒəns/","syllables":"neg·li·gence","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 疏忽, 粗心大意, 忽视；[经] 疏忽, 过失","contextualMeaning":"过失：违反合理注意义务并造成法律可归责损害","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) Whether the clerk should have checked to make sure no one was in the library before locking the door is a negligence issue (ie, what a reasonably prudent person in the clerk's position would have done).","synonyms":["lack of due care","carelessness"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":1,"sourceQuestionPage":1,"sourceQuestionPages":[1],"sourceExplanationPages":[2],"sourceAnswerPages":[2],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-002","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"An avid baseball fan purchased a ticket to an afternoon game at great expense. The fan hoped to not only see his favorite team's powerhouse player break the all-time single- season home run record, but also catch the record-breaking baseball. The fan brought his baseball glove and positioned himself in a section of the ballpark where the greatest number of the player's home runs had landed. In the ninth inning, with the bases loaded and a full count, the player swung, and the baseball sailed over the right-field fence and descended toward the large crowd gathered in the fan's section. The fan shouted, \"It's mine!\" just as the baseball made contact with the palm of his glove, but a burly man reached for the ball at that same instant. In so doing, he propelled the baseball back onto the field, where it was retrieved by the opposing team's outfielder. The fan was unable to recover the baseball. The fan has sued the man for trespass to chattels. Is the fan likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man did not dispossess the fan of the baseball.","B":"No, because the man did not intend to propel the baseball back onto the field.","C":"Yes, because the man deprived the fan of his immediate right to possession of the baseball.","D":"Yes, because the man intentionally intermeddled with the fan's personal property."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's right of possession in tangible personal property (ie, chattel) through dispossession, use, or intermeddling. As a result, an action for trespass to chattels may be brought by anyone who had possession or the right to immediate possession of the chattel in question at the time of the interference. Here, the fan sued the man for trespass to chattels after the man reached for the baseball and propelled it back onto the field. But the fan had not yet caught the baseball (no possession) and had no more right to possess it than any other person in the crowd (Choice C). Therefore, the man could not dispossess the fan of the baseball, and the fan is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) The intent required for trespass to chattels is the intent to do the interfering act—eg, to reach out for the baseball. The defendant need not have intended to interfere with another's possession of tangible property—eg, to propel the baseball back onto the field. (Choice D) The man intermeddled (ie, caused physical contact) with the baseball and deprived the fan of possessing and using it by propelling it back onto the field. But since the baseball was not the fan's personal property, his trespass-to-chattels claim will fail.\n\nEducational objective: Anyone who had possession or the right to immediate possession of a chattel (ie, tangible personal property) at the time of the interference may bring an action for trespass to chattels.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 216–17 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (possession of and trespass to chattel).","educationalObjective":"Anyone who had possession or the right to immediate possession of a chattel (ie, tangible personal property) at the time of the interference may bring an action for trespass to chattels.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 216–17 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (possession of and trespass to chattel).","rule":"Anyone who had possession or the right to immediate possession of a chattel (ie, tangible personal property) at the time of the interference may bring an action for trespass to chattels.","irac":{"issue":"Is the fan likely to prevail?","rule":"Anyone who had possession or the right to immediate possession of a chattel (ie, tangible personal property) at the time of the interference may bring an action for trespass to chattels.","analysis":"Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's right of possession in tangible personal property (ie, chattel) through dispossession, use, or intermeddling. As a result, an action for trespass to chattels may be brought by anyone who had possession or the right to immediate possession of the chattel in question at the time of the interference. Here, the fan sued the man for trespass to chattels after the man reached for the baseball and propelled it back onto the field. But the fan had not yet caught the baseball (no possession) and had no more right to possess it than any other person in the crowd (Choice C). Therefore, the man could not dispossess the fan of the baseball, and the fan is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) The intent required for trespass to chattels is the intent to do the interfering act—eg, to reach out for the baseball. The defendant need not have intended to interfere with another's possession of tangible property—eg, to propel the baseball back onto the field. (Choice D) The man intermeddled (ie, caused physical contact) with the baseball and deprived the fan of possessing and using it by propelling it back onto the field. But since the baseball was not the fan's personal property, his trespass-to-chattels claim will fail.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the man did not dispossess the fan of the baseball."},"questionZh":"一位狂热的棒球迷花了大价钱买了一张下午比赛的球票。\n\n他不仅希望亲眼见证自己支持的球队的王牌球员打破单赛季本垒打纪录，还希望能接到那颗打破纪录的棒球。\n\n他带上了棒球手套，并占据了球场上该球员本垒打落点最多的区域。\n\n第九局，满垒满球数，球员挥棒，棒球飞过右外野围墙，落向聚集在球迷区的大批人群。\n\n就在棒球即将落入手套掌心的瞬间，球迷大喊一声“是我的！”，但就在同一时刻，一个身材魁梧的男人伸手去抓球。\n\n结果，他把球弹回了场内，被对方外野手捡了起来。\n\n球迷没能拿回棒球。\n\n他以侵犯动产为由起诉了该男子。\n\n球迷胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该男子并未剥夺球迷对棒球的占有。","B":"不，因为该男子并非有意将棒球扔回场内。","C":"是，因为该男子剥夺了球迷对棒球的直接占有权。","D":"是，因为该男子故意干涉了球迷的个人财产。"},"explanationZh":"“侵犯动产”（trespass to chattels）是指通过剥夺占有、使用或擅自触碰（intermeddling）等方式，故意干扰他人对有形动产（即“动产”）的占有权的行为。\n\n因此，在干扰行为发生时，任何实际占有该动产或享有即时占有权的人，均可提起侵犯动产之诉。\n\n在本案中，该男子伸手去拿棒球并将其掷回场内，随后球迷起诉该男子侵犯动产。\n\n然而，该球迷当时尚未接住棒球（即不构成占有），且其对该球的占有权并不优于人群中的其他人（选项 C）。\n\n因此，该男子并未剥夺该球迷对棒球的占有，该球迷胜诉的可能性不大。\n\n（选项 B）侵犯动产所需的“故意”是指实施干扰行为的故意——例如，伸手去拿棒球的意图。\n\n被告无需具有干扰他人占有有形动产的意图——例如，无需具有将棒球掷回场内的意图。\n\n（选项 D）该男子确实触碰了（即发生了物理接触）该棒球，并通过将其掷回场内剥夺了该球迷占有和使用该球的机会。\n\n但由于该棒球并非该球迷的个人财产，其关于侵犯动产的诉讼主张将无法成立。\n\n教学目标：在干扰行为发生时，任何实际占有动产（即有形动产）或享有即时占有权的人，均可提起侵犯动产之诉。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 216–17 条（美国法律协会，1981 年）（关于动产的占有及侵犯动产）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"任何人在干涉发生时占有或有权直接占有动产（即有形个人财产）的人，均可提起侵犯动产之诉。","iracZh":{"issue":"球迷胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"任何人在干涉发生时占有或有权直接占有动产（即有形个人财产）的人，均可提起侵犯动产之诉。","analysis":"“侵犯动产”（trespass to chattels）是指通过剥夺占有、使用或擅自触碰（intermeddling）等方式，故意干扰他人对有形动产（即“动产”）的占有权的行为。\n\n因此，在干扰行为发生时，任何实际占有该动产或享有即时占有权的人，均可提起侵犯动产之诉。\n\n在本案中，该男子伸手去拿棒球并将其掷回场内，随后球迷起诉该男子侵犯动产。\n\n然而，该球迷当时尚未接住棒球（即不构成占有），且其对该球的占有权并不优于人群中的其他人（选项 C）。\n\n因此，该男子并未剥夺该球迷对棒球的占有，该球迷胜诉的可能性不大。\n\n（选项 B）侵犯动产所需的“故意”是指实施干扰行为的故意——例如，伸手去拿棒球的意图。\n\n被告无需具有干扰他人占有有形动产的意图——例如，无需具有将棒球掷回场内的意图。\n\n（选项 D）该男子确实触碰了（即发生了物理接触）该棒球，并通过将其掷回场内剥夺了该球迷占有和使用该球的机会。\n\n但由于该棒球并非该球迷的个人财产，其关于侵犯动产的诉讼主张将无法成立。\n\n教学目标：在干扰行为发生时，任何实际占有动产（即有形动产）或享有即时占有权的人，均可提起侵犯动产之诉。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 216–17 条（美国法律协会，1981 年）（关于动产的占有及侵犯动产）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为该男子并未剥夺球迷对棒球的占有。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"trespass to chattels","ipa":"/tɹˈɛspˌæs tˈu tʃˈætəl/","syllables":"tres·pass to chat·tels","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"侵害动产：故意干扰他人动产并造成损害或剥夺使用","contextualMeaning":"侵害动产：故意干扰他人动产并造成损害或剥夺使用","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The fan has sued the man for trespass to chattels.","synonyms":["interference with chattels"]},{"term":"dispossession","ipa":"/.dispə'zeʃən/","syllables":"dis·pos·ses·sion","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 强占不动产, 驱逐, 夺取；[法] 剥夺, 抢夺, 强占","contextualMeaning":"剥夺占有：使有权人失去对动产的占有或控制","wordForm":"词；词形 s:dispossessions","sourceSentence":"Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's right of possession in tangible personal property (ie, chattel) through dispossession, use, or intermeddling.","synonyms":["eviction","legal ouster","exorcism"]},{"term":"caused","ipa":"/kˈɑzd/","syllables":"caused","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 引起；使发生（cause的过去分词）","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“caused”","wordForm":"词；原形 cause；词形 0:cause/1:dp","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) The man intermeddled (ie, caused physical contact) with the baseball and deprived the fan of possessing and using it by propelling it back onto the field.","synonyms":["causal agent","causal agency","campaign","crusade"]},{"term":"intermeddling","ipa":"/.intə'medl/","syllables":"in·ter·med·dling","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"[法] 多管闲事, 干涉他人","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“intermeddling”","wordForm":"名词；原形 intermeddle；词形 0:intermeddle/1:i","sourceSentence":"Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's right of possession in tangible personal property (ie, chattel) through dispossession, use, or intermeddling.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"interference","ipa":"/ˌɪntɝfˈɪɹəns/","syllables":"in·ter·fer·ence","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 冲突, 干涉；[计] 干扰","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“interference”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:interferences","sourceSentence":"Anyone who had possession or the right to immediate possession of a chattel (ie, tangible personal property) at the time of the interference may bring an action for trespass to chattels.","synonyms":["hindrance","hinderance","hitch","preventive"]},{"term":"interfere","ipa":"/ˌɪntɝfˈɪɹ/","syllables":"in·ter·fere","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vi. 妨碍, 冲突, 干涉, 抵触；[医] 干扰, 干涉, 阻碍, 碰腿(马)","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“interfere”","wordForm":"动词；词形 i:interfering/d:interfered/3:interferes/p:interfered","sourceSentence":"Anyone who had possession or the right to immediate possession of a chattel (ie, tangible personal property) at the time of the interference may bring an action for trespass to chattels.","synonyms":["intervene","step in","interpose"]},{"term":"dispossess","ipa":"/dˌɪspʌzˈɛs/","syllables":"dis·pos·sess","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 剥夺, 使失去, 逐出；[法] 抢夺, 霸占, 剥夺","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“dispossess”","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:dispossessed/p:dispossessed/i:dispossessing/3:dispossesses","sourceSentence":"No, because the man did not dispossess the fan of the baseball.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-1a2e161361d6c9d8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":2,"sourceQuestionPage":3,"sourceQuestionPages":[3],"sourceExplanationPages":[4,5],"sourceAnswerPages":[4,5],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-003","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A professional football player signed a written consent for his team's physician to perform a knee operation. After the athlete was under a general anesthetic, the physician asked a world-famous orthopedic surgeon to perform the operation. The surgeon's skills were superior to the physician's, and the operation was successful. In an action for battery against the surgeon, will the athlete prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the operation was successful.","B":"No, because the surgeon's skills were superior to the physician's.","C":"Yes, because the athlete did not agree to allow the surgeon to perform the operation.","D":"Yes, because the consent form was in writing."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Battery requires that a defendant intend to create contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) and cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Contact is harmful if it causes physical impairment (eg, alteration of the body), pain, or illness. However, a defendant is not liable for battery if the contact is consensual. Consent exists when the defendant's conduct is the same as, or substantially similar to, the conduct agreed to by the plaintiff. Therefore, consent is limited to the individual whose conduct was agreed to by the plaintiff. Here, the surgeon operated on the athlete's knee (intentional act), which physically impaired the athlete's body (harmful contact). And since the athlete only gave the physician written consent to perform the operation, not the surgeon (nonconsensual contact), the surgeon is liable for battery. (Choice A) Although the operation was successful, contact is harmful if it alters the plaintiff's body in any way, even if the alteration results in no other harm. Therefore, the surgeon is still liable for intentionally altering the athlete's body without his consent. (Choice B) The surgeon's superior skill does not negate the fact that he operated on the athlete without his consent and, therefore, does not absolve the surgeon of liability. (Choice D) Consent to contact can be verbal or written. But consent (verbal or written) is limited to the individual whose conduct was agreed to by the plaintiff.\n\nEducational objective: Consent only extends to conduct that is the same as, or substantially similar to, the particular conduct agreed to by the plaintiff. Therefore, consent is limited to the individual whose conduct was agreed to by the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (meaning of harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 892A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (effect of consent).","educationalObjective":"Consent only extends to conduct that is the same as, or substantially similar to, the particular conduct agreed to by the plaintiff. Therefore, consent is limited to the individual whose conduct was agreed to by the plaintiff.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (meaning of harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 892A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (effect of consent).","rule":"Consent only extends to conduct that is the same as, or substantially similar to, the particular conduct agreed to by the plaintiff. Therefore, consent is limited to the individual whose conduct was agreed to by the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"In an action for battery against the surgeon, will the athlete prevail?","rule":"Consent only extends to conduct that is the same as, or substantially similar to, the particular conduct agreed to by the plaintiff. Therefore, consent is limited to the individual whose conduct was agreed to by the plaintiff.","analysis":"Battery requires that a defendant intend to create contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) and cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Contact is harmful if it causes physical impairment (eg, alteration of the body), pain, or illness. However, a defendant is not liable for battery if the contact is consensual. Consent exists when the defendant's conduct is the same as, or substantially similar to, the conduct agreed to by the plaintiff. Therefore, consent is limited to the individual whose conduct was agreed to by the plaintiff. Here, the surgeon operated on the athlete's knee (intentional act), which physically impaired the athlete's body (harmful contact). And since the athlete only gave the physician written consent to perform the operation, not the surgeon (nonconsensual contact), the surgeon is liable for battery. (Choice A) Although the operation was successful, contact is harmful if it alters the plaintiff's body in any way, even if the alteration results in no other harm. Therefore, the surgeon is still liable for intentionally altering the athlete's body without his consent. (Choice B) The surgeon's superior skill does not negate the fact that he operated on the athlete without his consent and, therefore, does not absolve the surgeon of liability. (Choice D) Consent to contact can be verbal or written. But consent (verbal or written) is limited to the individual whose conduct was agreed to by the plaintiff.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the athlete did not agree to allow the surgeon to perform the operation."},"questionZh":"一名职业橄榄球运动员签署了一份书面同意书，授权其队医为其进行膝关节手术。\n\n运动员接受全身麻醉后，队医请来一位世界知名的骨科医生进行手术。\n\n这位外科医生的医术远胜于队医，手术也取得了成功。\n\n如果运动员起诉这位外科医生，指控其侵犯人身权，他能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为手术成功了。","B":"不能，因为这位外科医生的医术远胜于队医。","C":"能，因为运动员并未同意让这位外科医生进行手术。","D":"能，因为同意书是书面的。"},"explanationZh":"构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为，要求被告有意制造接触（或制造即将发生接触的恐惧感），并导致与原告发生有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n如果接触导致身体损伤（例如身体发生改变）、疼痛或疾病，则该接触被视为有害接触。\n\n然而，如果接触是经同意发生的，被告则不承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任。\n\n当被告的行为与原告同意的行为相同或实质上相似时，即视为存在同意。\n\n因此，同意仅限于针对原告所同意其行为的那个人。\n\n在本案中，外科医生对运动员的膝盖进行了手术（有意行为），这导致了运动员身体的损伤（有害接触）。\n\n由于运动员仅书面同意由该内科医生（physician）进行手术，而未同意由该外科医生（surgeon）进行手术（即未经同意的接触），因此该外科医生需承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任。\n\n（选项 A）尽管手术成功，但只要接触改变了原告的身体，即使这种改变未导致其他伤害，该接触仍被视为有害接触。\n\n因此，该外科医生因在未经同意的情况下有意改变运动员的身体而仍需承担责任。\n\n（选项 B）外科医生高超的医术并不能改变其在未经运动员同意的情况下进行手术的事实，因此也不能免除其责任。\n\n（选项 D）对接触的同意可以是口头的，也可以是书面的。\n\n但无论口头还是书面同意，其效力仅限于原告所同意其行为的那个人。\n\n教育目标：同意仅适用于与原告同意的特定行为相同或实质上相似的行为。\n\n因此，同意仅限于针对原告所同意其行为的那个人。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第13条（美国法律协会，1965年）（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第15条（美国法律协会，1965年）（有害接触的含义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第892A条（美国法律协会，1979年）（同意的效力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"同意仅适用于与原告同意的特定行为相同或实质上相似的行为。\n\n因此，同意仅限于原告同意实施该行为的个人。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果运动员起诉外科医生人身伤害，他能否胜诉？","rule":"同意仅适用于与原告同意的特定行为相同或实质上相似的行为。\n\n因此，同意仅限于原告同意实施该行为的个人。","analysis":"构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为，要求被告有意制造接触（或制造即将发生接触的恐惧感），并导致与原告发生有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n如果接触导致身体损伤（例如身体发生改变）、疼痛或疾病，则该接触被视为有害接触。\n\n然而，如果接触是经同意发生的，被告则不承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任。\n\n当被告的行为与原告同意的行为相同或实质上相似时，即视为存在同意。\n\n因此，同意仅限于针对原告所同意其行为的那个人。\n\n在本案中，外科医生对运动员的膝盖进行了手术（有意行为），这导致了运动员身体的损伤（有害接触）。\n\n由于运动员仅书面同意由该内科医生（physician）进行手术，而未同意由该外科医生（surgeon）进行手术（即未经同意的接触），因此该外科医生需承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任。\n\n（选项 A）尽管手术成功，但只要接触改变了原告的身体，即使这种改变未导致其他伤害，该接触仍被视为有害接触。\n\n因此，该外科医生因在未经同意的情况下有意改变运动员的身体而仍需承担责任。\n\n（选项 B）外科医生高超的医术并不能改变其在未经运动员同意的情况下进行手术的事实，因此也不能免除其责任。\n\n（选项 D）对接触的同意可以是口头的，也可以是书面的。\n\n但无论口头还是书面同意，其效力仅限于原告所同意其行为的那个人。\n\n教育目标：同意仅适用于与原告同意的特定行为相同或实质上相似的行为。\n\n因此，同意仅限于针对原告所同意其行为的那个人。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第13条（美国法律协会，1965年）（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第15条（美国法律协会，1965年）（有害接触的含义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第892A条（美国法律协会，1979年）（同意的效力）。","conclusion":"答案 C：能，因为运动员并未同意让这位外科医生进行手术。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"harmful or offensive contact","ipa":"/hˈɑɹmfəl ˈɔɹ ʌfˈɛnsɪv kˈɑntˌækt/","syllables":"harm·ful or of·fen·sive con·tact","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"有害或冒犯性接触：造成伤害或冒犯合理人格尊严的接触","contextualMeaning":"有害或冒犯性接触：造成伤害或冒犯合理人格尊严的接触","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Battery requires that a defendant intend to create contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) and cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"apprehension","ipa":"/ˌæpɹɪhˈɛnʃən/","syllables":"ap·pre·hen·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 理解, 忧惧, 逮捕；[医] 领会","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“apprehension”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:apprehensions","sourceSentence":"Battery requires that a defendant intend to create contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) and cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff.","synonyms":["arrest","catch","collar","pinch"]},{"term":"consent","ipa":"/kʌnsˈɛnt/","syllables":"con·sent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 同意, 许可；vi. 同意, 赞同","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“consent”","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:consented/s:consents/p:consented/i:consenting/3:consents","sourceSentence":"A professional football player signed a written consent for his team's physician to perform a knee operation.","synonyms":["accept","go for"]},{"term":"battery","ipa":"/bˈætɝi/","syllables":"bat·tery","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 电池, 殴打；[化] 蓄电池","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“battery”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:batteries","sourceSentence":"In an action for battery against the surgeon, will the athlete prevail?","synonyms":["assault and battery","barrage","barrage fire","bombardment"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-2180a95425946530"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":3,"sourceQuestionPage":6,"sourceQuestionPages":[6],"sourceExplanationPages":[7,8],"sourceAnswerPages":[7,8],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-004","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A car driven by the defendant entered land owned by and in the possession of the plaintiff, without her permission. Which of the following allegations, without additional facts, would provide a sufficient basis for a trespass claim by the plaintiff against the defendant?","choices":{"A":"The defendant knew that the land was the plaintiff's private property.","B":"The defendant negligently drove his car onto the plaintiff's land.","C":"The defendant purposely drove his car onto the plaintiff's land.","D":"The defendant's car damaged the plaintiff's land."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Types of trespass to land\n\nConduct Consent Harm\n\nIntentional Intentional entry onto Without plaintiff's (No harm required) plaintiff's land consent\n\nReckless Reckless entry onto Without plaintiff's Causing harm to land or plaintiff's land consent plaintiff\n\nNegligent Negligent entry onto Without plaintiff's Causing harm to land or plaintiff's land consent plaintiff\n\nFor an intentional trespass, a defendant must act with the intent to enter the plaintiff's land, which is either (1) the purpose to enter the plaintiff's land or (2) the knowledge that the entry is substantially certain to occur. If the plaintiff does not consent to the defendant's intentional entry, then the defendant is liable for intentional trespass. Here, the plaintiff did not give the defendant permission to enter her land. Therefore, the plaintiff can prevail on an intentional trespass claim if she can show that the defendant drove his car with the purpose to enter the plaintiff's land (or knowledge that the entry was substantially certain to occur). (Choice A) The fact that the defendant knew that the land was the plaintiff's private property has no bearing on the defendant's tortious conduct—the intentional, reckless, or negligent entry onto the plaintiff's land—and is therefore irrelevant to a trespass claim. (Choices B & D) A defendant is liable for negligent or reckless trespass when the plaintiff does not consent to the defendant's negligent or reckless entry AND the entry causes actual harm to the land or the plaintiff. Here, the defendant entered the plaintiff's land without permission. However, neither (1) the fact that the defendant drove negligently (or recklessly) nor (2) the fact that the defendant's car damaged the plaintiff's land can—on its own—support a claim for trespass. Both allegations are required.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant who intentionally enters the plaintiff's land without permission is liable for intentional trespass, even if the entry does not harm the land or the plaintiff. In contrast, a defendant whose entry results from negligent or reckless conduct is only liable for trespass if he/she causes actual damages.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (intentional trespass).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 165 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (reckless or negligent trespass).","educationalObjective":"A defendant who intentionally enters the plaintiff's land without permission is liable for intentional trespass, even if the entry does not harm the land or the plaintiff. In contrast, a defendant whose entry results from negligent or reckless conduct is only liable for trespass if he/she causes actual damages.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (intentional trespass).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 165 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (reckless or negligent trespass).","rule":"A defendant who intentionally enters the plaintiff's land without permission is liable for intentional trespass, even if the entry does not harm the land or the plaintiff. In contrast, a defendant whose entry results from negligent or reckless conduct is only liable for trespass if he/she causes actual damages.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following allegations, without additional facts, would provide a sufficient basis for a trespass claim by the plaintiff against the defendant?","rule":"A defendant who intentionally enters the plaintiff's land without permission is liable for intentional trespass, even if the entry does not harm the land or the plaintiff. In contrast, a defendant whose entry results from negligent or reckless conduct is only liable for trespass if he/she causes actual damages.","analysis":"Types of trespass to land\n\nConduct Consent Harm\n\nIntentional Intentional entry onto Without plaintiff's (No harm required) plaintiff's land consent\n\nReckless Reckless entry onto Without plaintiff's Causing harm to land or plaintiff's land consent plaintiff\n\nNegligent Negligent entry onto Without plaintiff's Causing harm to land or plaintiff's land consent plaintiff\n\nFor an intentional trespass, a defendant must act with the intent to enter the plaintiff's land, which is either (1) the purpose to enter the plaintiff's land or (2) the knowledge that the entry is substantially certain to occur. If the plaintiff does not consent to the defendant's intentional entry, then the defendant is liable for intentional trespass. Here, the plaintiff did not give the defendant permission to enter her land. Therefore, the plaintiff can prevail on an intentional trespass claim if she can show that the defendant drove his car with the purpose to enter the plaintiff's land (or knowledge that the entry was substantially certain to occur). (Choice A) The fact that the defendant knew that the land was the plaintiff's private property has no bearing on the defendant's tortious conduct—the intentional, reckless, or negligent entry onto the plaintiff's land—and is therefore irrelevant to a trespass claim. (Choices B & D) A defendant is liable for negligent or reckless trespass when the plaintiff does not consent to the defendant's negligent or reckless entry AND the entry causes actual harm to the land or the plaintiff. Here, the defendant entered the plaintiff's land without permission. However, neither (1) the fact that the defendant drove negligently (or recklessly) nor (2) the fact that the defendant's car damaged the plaintiff's land can—on its own—support a claim for trespass. Both allegations are required.","conclusion":"Answer C: The defendant purposely drove his car onto the plaintiff's land."},"questionZh":"被告驾驶的汽车未经原告许可驶入原告拥有并占有的土地。\n\n在没有其他事实的情况下，以下哪项指控足以构成原告对被告提起侵权诉讼的充分依据？","choicesZh":{"A":"被告明知该土地是原告的私有财产。","B":"被告疏忽驾驶汽车驶入原告的土地。","C":"被告故意驾驶汽车驶入原告的土地。","D":"被告的汽车损坏了原告的土地。"},"explanationZh":"侵犯土地权的类型 类型 | 行为 | 同意情况 | 损害后果 --- | --- | --- | --- 故意侵入 | 故意进入原告土地 | 未经原告同意 | 无需造成损害 鲁莽侵入 | 鲁莽进入原告土地 | 未经原告同意 | 导致土地或原告受损 过失侵入 | 过失进入原告土地 | 未经原告同意 | 导致土地或原告受损 对于故意侵入，被告必须怀有进入原告土地的意图，即：（1）以进入原告土地为目的，或（2）明知进入行为极有可能发生。\n\n如果原告未同意被告的故意进入行为，则被告须承担故意侵入的责任。\n\n在本案中，原告未许可被告进入其土地。\n\n因此，如果原告能证明被告驾驶车辆时意图进入原告土地（或明知进入行为极有可能发生），则原告可在故意侵入的诉讼主张中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）被告明知该土地为原告私有财产这一事实，与其侵权行为（即故意、鲁莽或过失进入原告土地）无关，因此与侵入诉讼主张无涉。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）当原告未同意被告的过失或鲁莽进入行为，且该进入行为导致土地或原告遭受实际损害时，被告须承担过失或鲁莽侵入的责任。\n\n在本案中，被告未经许可进入了原告土地。\n\n然而，仅凭（1）被告驾驶存在过失（或鲁莽）这一事实，或（2）被告车辆损坏原告土地这一事实，均不足以单独支持侵入的诉讼主张。\n\n这两项指控要素缺一不可。\n\n教育目标：未经许可故意进入原告土地的被告，即使未对土地或原告造成损害，也须承担故意侵入的责任。\n\n相比之下，因过失或鲁莽行为而进入土地的被告，仅在造成实际损害的情况下才须承担侵入责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 158 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于故意侵入）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第165条（美国法律学会，1965年）（鲁莽或过失侵入）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"未经许可故意驶入原告土地的被告，即使其驶入并未对土地或原告造成损害，也应承担故意侵权责任。\n\n相比之下，因疏忽或鲁莽行为导致的驶入，只有在造成实际损害的情况下才应承担侵权责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"下列哪项指控，在没有其他事实依据的情况下，足以构成原告对被告提起侵权诉讼的充分依据？","rule":"未经许可故意驶入原告土地的被告，即使其驶入并未对土地或原告造成损害，也应承担故意侵权责任。\n\n相比之下，因疏忽或鲁莽行为导致的驶入，只有在造成实际损害的情况下才应承担侵权责任。","analysis":"侵犯土地权的类型 类型 | 行为 | 同意情况 | 损害后果 --- | --- | --- | --- 故意侵入 | 故意进入原告土地 | 未经原告同意 | 无需造成损害 鲁莽侵入 | 鲁莽进入原告土地 | 未经原告同意 | 导致土地或原告受损 过失侵入 | 过失进入原告土地 | 未经原告同意 | 导致土地或原告受损 对于故意侵入，被告必须怀有进入原告土地的意图，即：（1）以进入原告土地为目的，或（2）明知进入行为极有可能发生。\n\n如果原告未同意被告的故意进入行为，则被告须承担故意侵入的责任。\n\n在本案中，原告未许可被告进入其土地。\n\n因此，如果原告能证明被告驾驶车辆时意图进入原告土地（或明知进入行为极有可能发生），则原告可在故意侵入的诉讼主张中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）被告明知该土地为原告私有财产这一事实，与其侵权行为（即故意、鲁莽或过失进入原告土地）无关，因此与侵入诉讼主张无涉。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）当原告未同意被告的过失或鲁莽进入行为，且该进入行为导致土地或原告遭受实际损害时，被告须承担过失或鲁莽侵入的责任。\n\n在本案中，被告未经许可进入了原告土地。\n\n然而，仅凭（1）被告驾驶存在过失（或鲁莽）这一事实，或（2）被告车辆损坏原告土地这一事实，均不足以单独支持侵入的诉讼主张。\n\n这两项指控要素缺一不可。\n\n教育目标：未经许可故意进入原告土地的被告，即使未对土地或原告造成损害，也须承担故意侵入的责任。\n\n相比之下，因过失或鲁莽行为而进入土地的被告，仅在造成实际损害的情况下才须承担侵入责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 158 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于故意侵入）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第165条（美国法律学会，1965年）（鲁莽或过失侵入）。","conclusion":"答案 C：被告故意驾驶汽车驶入原告的土地。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"trespass to land","ipa":"/tɹˈɛspˌæs tˈu lˈænd/","syllables":"tres·pass to land","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"侵入土地：故意进入、使物进入或拒绝离开他人土地","contextualMeaning":"侵入土地：故意进入、使物进入或拒绝离开他人土地","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Types of trespass to land Conduct Consent Harm Intentional Intentional entry onto Without plaintiff's (No harm required) plaintiff's land consent Reckless Reckless entry onto Without plaintiff's Causing harm to land or plaintiff's land consent plaintiff Negligent Negligent entry onto Without plaintiff's Causing harm to land or plaintiff's land consent plaintiff For an intentional trespass, a defendant must act with t","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"reckless","ipa":"/ɹˈɛkləs/","syllables":"reck·less","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 不介意的, 大意的, 鲁莽的, 不顾后果的；[法] 不注意的, 粗心大意的, 鲁莽的","contextualMeaning":"鲁莽的：明知重大不合理风险仍有意识地无视","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"In contrast, a defendant whose entry results from negligent or reckless conduct is only liable for trespass if he/she causes actual damages.","synonyms":["foolhardy","heady","rash","heedless"]},{"term":"recklessly","ipa":"/ɹˈɛkləsli/","syllables":"reck·lessly","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 不注意, 粗心大意, 不在乎, 鲁莽, 不顾后果, 不顾一切, 妄动","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“recklessly”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"However, neither (1) the fact that the defendant drove negligently (or recklessly) nor (2) the fact that the defendant's car damaged the plaintiff's land can—on its own—support a claim for trespass.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":4,"sourceQuestionPage":9,"sourceQuestionPages":[9],"sourceExplanationPages":[10,11],"sourceAnswerPages":[10,11],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-005","subject":"Torts","topic":"Privileges & Defenses","question":"A law student rented a furnished apartment. His landlord began to solicit his advice about her legal affairs, but he refused to provide it. The landlord then demanded that the student vacate the apartment immediately. The landlord also began engaging in a pattern of harassment, calling the student at home every evening and entering his apartment without his consent during times when he was at school. During these unauthorized visits, the landlord removed the handles from the bathroom and kitchen faucets, making the faucets unusable, but she did not touch any personal property belonging to the student. The lease has a year to run, and the student is still living in the apartment. The student has sued the landlord for trespass. Is he likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because he has no standing to sue for trespass.","B":"No, because the landlord caused no damage to his property.","C":"Yes, for compensatory damages only.","D":"Yes, for injunctive relief, compensatory damages, and punitive damages."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Types of remedies\n\nNominal damages Trivial sum of money (eg, $1) awarded to vindicate plaintiff's rights when no actual loss or damage occurs\n\nCompensatory Money awarded to compensate plaintiff for actual loss or damage (ie, damages actual damages)\n\nPunitive damages Money awarded to punish defendant for outrageous or evil conduct & to deter similar conduct in the future (ie, exemplary damages)\n\nInjunctive relief Court order commanding or prohibiting a specified action\n\nFor trespass, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant intentionally entered land in the plaintiff's possession without the plaintiff's permission. If the plaintiff does so, he/she may receive nominal damages or compensatory damages, as well as punitive damages and/or injunctive relief. Here, the student can establish a claim for trespass because the landlord intentionally (and repeatedly) entered the student's apartment without his consent. Therefore, the student will likely recover: compensatory damages – for the removal of the faucet handles punitive damages – to punish the landlord for his malicious and evil pattern of harassment injunctive relief – to stop the landlord's repeated and ongoing trespasses However, nominal damages are not recoverable because the student suffered actual loss and damage (ie, he can recover compensatory damages). (Choice A) A plaintiff has standing to sue for trespass if he/she has a possessory interest— the right to occupy or control property—in the land that the defendant intentionally entered. Here, even though the student does not own the apartment, he has a possessory interest in it (a lease). Therefore, he has standing to sue for trespass. (Choice B) The landlord did damage the student's leased property by removing the faucet handles, rendering them inoperable. But even if no damage had occurred, the student could still prevail because trespass does not require proof of actual loss or damage. However, the student could not recover compensatory damages. (Choice C) The student's recovery is not limited to compensatory damages because there is evidence to support punitive damages (landlord's malicious pattern of harassment) and injunctive relief (landlord's repeated and ongoing trespasses).\n\nEducational objective: Trespass is an intentional, unauthorized entry onto land in the plaintiff's possession (ie, land the plaintiff occupies or controls). The plaintiff can recover nominal damages (eg, $1) for a harmless trespass or compensatory damages for a harmful trespass. Punitive damages and/or injunctive relief may also be available.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (trespass).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 903 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (compensatory damages).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 907 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (nominal damages).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 908 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (punitive damages).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 945 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (injunctive relief for recovery of land).","educationalObjective":"Trespass is an intentional, unauthorized entry onto land in the plaintiff's possession (ie, land the plaintiff occupies or controls). The plaintiff can recover nominal damages (eg, $1) for a harmless trespass or compensatory damages for a harmful trespass. Punitive damages and/or injunctive relief may also be available.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (trespass).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 903 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (compensatory damages).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 907 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (nominal damages).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 908 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (punitive damages).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 945 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (injunctive relief for recovery of land).","rule":"Trespass is an intentional, unauthorized entry onto land in the plaintiff's possession (ie, land the plaintiff occupies or controls). The plaintiff can recover nominal damages (eg, $1) for a harmless trespass or compensatory damages for a harmful trespass. Punitive damages and/or injunctive relief may also be available.","irac":{"issue":"Is he likely to prevail?","rule":"Trespass is an intentional, unauthorized entry onto land in the plaintiff's possession (ie, land the plaintiff occupies or controls). The plaintiff can recover nominal damages (eg, $1) for a harmless trespass or compensatory damages for a harmful trespass. Punitive damages and/or injunctive relief may also be available.","analysis":"Types of remedies\n\nNominal damages Trivial sum of money (eg, $1) awarded to vindicate plaintiff's rights when no actual loss or damage occurs\n\nCompensatory Money awarded to compensate plaintiff for actual loss or damage (ie, damages actual damages)\n\nPunitive damages Money awarded to punish defendant for outrageous or evil conduct & to deter similar conduct in the future (ie, exemplary damages)\n\nInjunctive relief Court order commanding or prohibiting a specified action\n\nFor trespass, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant intentionally entered land in the plaintiff's possession without the plaintiff's permission. If the plaintiff does so, he/she may receive nominal damages or compensatory damages, as well as punitive damages and/or injunctive relief. Here, the student can establish a claim for trespass because the landlord intentionally (and repeatedly) entered the student's apartment without his consent. Therefore, the student will likely recover: compensatory damages – for the removal of the faucet handles punitive damages – to punish the landlord for his malicious and evil pattern of harassment injunctive relief – to stop the landlord's repeated and ongoing trespasses However, nominal damages are not recoverable because the student suffered actual loss and damage (ie, he can recover compensatory damages). (Choice A) A plaintiff has standing to sue for trespass if he/she has a possessory interest— the right to occupy or control property—in the land that the defendant intentionally entered. Here, even though the student does not own the apartment, he has a possessory interest in it (a lease). Therefore, he has standing to sue for trespass. (Choice B) The landlord did damage the student's leased property by removing the faucet handles, rendering them inoperable. But even if no damage had occurred, the student could still prevail because trespass does not require proof of actual loss or damage. However, the student could not recover compensatory damages. (Choice C) The student's recovery is not limited to compensatory damages because there is evidence to support punitive damages (landlord's malicious pattern of harassment) and injunctive relief (landlord's repeated and ongoing trespasses).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, for injunctive relief, compensatory damages, and punitive damages."},"questionZh":"一名法学院学生租了一套带家具的公寓。\n\n房东开始就其法律事务征求该学生的意见，但他拒绝提供建议。\n\n随后，房东要求该学生立即搬离公寓。\n\n房东还开始进行一系列骚扰行为：每晚给学生家中打电话，并在学生上课期间未经其同意进入其公寓。\n\n在这些未经许可的进入期间，房东拆除了浴室和厨房水龙头的把手，导致水龙头无法使用，但她并未触碰属于学生的任何个人物品。\n\n该租约尚有一年期限，且学生目前仍居住在该公寓内。\n\n该学生已就非法侵入（trespass）起诉房东。\n\n他胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为他没有提起非法侵入诉讼的资格（standing）。","B":"不大，因为房东未对其财产造成损害。","C":"会胜诉，但仅限于获得补偿性损害赔偿。","D":"会胜诉，且可获得禁令救济、补偿性损害赔偿及惩罚性损害赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"救济类型 名义损害赔偿金（Nominal damages）：在未发生实际损失或损害的情况下，为维护原告权利而判给的象征性金额（例如1美元）。 补偿性损害赔偿金（Compensatory damages）：为补偿原告的实际损失或损害而判给的金额。 惩罚性损害赔偿金（Punitive damages）：为惩罚被告的恶劣或邪恶行为，并威慑未来类似行为而判给的金额（也称“惩戒性损害赔偿金”/exemplary damages）。 禁令救济（Injunctive relief）：法院下令要求或禁止实施特定行为的命令。\n\n针对侵入土地（trespass）的诉讼，原告必须证明被告在未经许可的情况下，故意进入了原告占有的土地。若原告能证明这一点，则可能获得名义损害赔偿金或补偿性损害赔偿金，以及惩罚性损害赔偿金和/或禁令救济。\n\n在本案中，该学生可以提出侵入土地的索赔，因为房东在未征得其同意的情况下，故意（且反复）进入了该学生的公寓。\n\n因此，该学生很可能获得以下救济：补偿性损害赔偿金（针对拆除水龙头把手造成的损失）；惩罚性损害赔偿金（惩罚房东恶意且恶劣的骚扰行为）；禁令救济（制止房东反复且持续的侵入行为）。然而，该学生无法获得名义损害赔偿金，因为他遭受了实际损失和损害（即他可以获得补偿性损害赔偿金）。\n\n（选项 A）如果原告对被告故意进入的土地拥有占有权益（即占用或控制该财产的权利），则其具备提起侵入土地诉讼的资格（诉讼主体资格）。\n\n在本案中，尽管该学生不拥有该公寓的所有权，但他拥有占有权益（基于租赁合同）。\n\n因此，他有资格提起侵入土地诉讼。\n\n（选项 B）房东拆除水龙头把手导致其无法使用，确实损坏了该学生租赁的财产。\n\n但即使未发生损害，该学生仍可能胜诉，因为侵入土地诉讼并不要求证明存在实际损失或损害。\n\n不过，在这种情况下，该学生将无法获得补偿性损害赔偿金。\n\n（选项 C）该学生获得的赔偿不限于补偿性损害赔偿金，因为有证据支持判给惩罚性损害赔偿金（房东恶意的骚扰行为）和禁令救济（房东反复且持续的侵入行为）。\n\n教育目标：侵入土地是指故意、未经授权地进入原告占有的土地（即原告占用或控制的土地）。\n\n原告可就未造成实际损害的侵入行为获得象征性损害赔偿（例如1美元），或就造成损害的侵入行为获得补偿性损害赔偿。\n\n此外，原告还可能获得惩罚性损害赔偿和/或禁令救济。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第158条（美国法律协会，1965年）（侵入）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第903条（美国法律协会，1979年）（补偿性损害赔偿）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第907条（美国法律协会，1979年）（象征性损害赔偿）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第908条（美国法律协会，1979年）（惩罚性损害赔偿）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第945条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于收回土地的禁令救济）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"非法侵入是指故意且未经许可进入原告占有（即原告实际占用或控制）的土地。\n\n对于未造成实际损害的非法侵入，原告可获得象征性损害赔偿（例如1美元）；对于造成损害的非法侵入，原告可获得补偿性损害赔偿。\n\n此外，原告还可能获得惩罚性损害赔偿和/或禁令救济。","iracZh":{"issue":"他胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"非法侵入是指故意且未经许可进入原告占有（即原告实际占用或控制）的土地。\n\n对于未造成实际损害的非法侵入，原告可获得象征性损害赔偿（例如1美元）；对于造成损害的非法侵入，原告可获得补偿性损害赔偿。\n\n此外，原告还可能获得惩罚性损害赔偿和/或禁令救济。","analysis":"救济类型 名义损害赔偿金（Nominal damages）：在未发生实际损失或损害的情况下，为维护原告权利而判给的象征性金额（例如1美元）。 补偿性损害赔偿金（Compensatory damages）：为补偿原告的实际损失或损害而判给的金额。 惩罚性损害赔偿金（Punitive damages）：为惩罚被告的恶劣或邪恶行为，并威慑未来类似行为而判给的金额（也称“惩戒性损害赔偿金”/exemplary damages）。 禁令救济（Injunctive relief）：法院下令要求或禁止实施特定行为的命令。\n\n针对侵入土地（trespass）的诉讼，原告必须证明被告在未经许可的情况下，故意进入了原告占有的土地。若原告能证明这一点，则可能获得名义损害赔偿金或补偿性损害赔偿金，以及惩罚性损害赔偿金和/或禁令救济。\n\n在本案中，该学生可以提出侵入土地的索赔，因为房东在未征得其同意的情况下，故意（且反复）进入了该学生的公寓。\n\n因此，该学生很可能获得以下救济：补偿性损害赔偿金（针对拆除水龙头把手造成的损失）；惩罚性损害赔偿金（惩罚房东恶意且恶劣的骚扰行为）；禁令救济（制止房东反复且持续的侵入行为）。然而，该学生无法获得名义损害赔偿金，因为他遭受了实际损失和损害（即他可以获得补偿性损害赔偿金）。\n\n（选项 A）如果原告对被告故意进入的土地拥有占有权益（即占用或控制该财产的权利），则其具备提起侵入土地诉讼的资格（诉讼主体资格）。\n\n在本案中，尽管该学生不拥有该公寓的所有权，但他拥有占有权益（基于租赁合同）。\n\n因此，他有资格提起侵入土地诉讼。\n\n（选项 B）房东拆除水龙头把手导致其无法使用，确实损坏了该学生租赁的财产。\n\n但即使未发生损害，该学生仍可能胜诉，因为侵入土地诉讼并不要求证明存在实际损失或损害。\n\n不过，在这种情况下，该学生将无法获得补偿性损害赔偿金。\n\n（选项 C）该学生获得的赔偿不限于补偿性损害赔偿金，因为有证据支持判给惩罚性损害赔偿金（房东恶意的骚扰行为）和禁令救济（房东反复且持续的侵入行为）。\n\n教育目标：侵入土地是指故意、未经授权地进入原告占有的土地（即原告占用或控制的土地）。\n\n原告可就未造成实际损害的侵入行为获得象征性损害赔偿（例如1美元），或就造成损害的侵入行为获得补偿性损害赔偿。\n\n此外，原告还可能获得惩罚性损害赔偿和/或禁令救济。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第158条（美国法律协会，1965年）（侵入）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第903条（美国法律协会，1979年）（补偿性损害赔偿）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第907条（美国法律协会，1979年）（象征性损害赔偿）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第908条（美国法律协会，1979年）（惩罚性损害赔偿）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第945条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于收回土地的禁令救济）。","conclusion":"答案 D：会胜诉，且可获得禁令救济、补偿性损害赔偿及惩罚性损害赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"compensatory damages","ipa":"/kʌmpˈɛnsʌtˌɔɹi dˈæmədʒəz/","syllables":"com·pen·satory dam·ages","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"补偿性损害赔偿：使原告尽可能恢复至侵权未发生时状态","contextualMeaning":"补偿性损害赔偿：使原告尽可能恢复至侵权未发生时状态","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, for compensatory damages only.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"punitive damages","ipa":"/pjˈunətɪv dˈæmədʒəz/","syllables":"puni·tive dam·ages","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"惩罚性损害赔偿：针对恶意、故意或鲁莽行为的额外惩罚与威慑","contextualMeaning":"惩罚性损害赔偿：针对恶意、故意或鲁莽行为的额外惩罚与威慑","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, for injunctive relief, compensatory damages, and punitive damages.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"harmless","ipa":"/hˈɑɹmləs/","syllables":"harm·less","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 无害处的, 未受损害的, 无辜的, 无恶意的；[法] 无害的, 无恶意的, 无损害的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“harmless”","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"The plaintiff can recover nominal damages (eg, $1) for a harmless trespass or compensatory damages for a harmful trespass.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":5,"sourceQuestionPage":12,"sourceQuestionPages":[12],"sourceExplanationPages":[13,14],"sourceAnswerPages":[13,14],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-006","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A farmer kept antiques in an uninhabited farmhouse on his property. The farmhouse had been broken into several times in the past, and some of the farmer's goods had been stolen. Instead of posting \"No Trespassing\" signs, the farmer decided to install an alarm system to deter intruders. While the farmer was in the farmhouse installing the alarm system, he heard a window open in the adjoining room. The farmer crept very quietly to the door of the room, threw the door open, and found a 10-year-old intruder. The farmer immediately struck the child very hard in the face, breaking her nose. In an action on behalf of the child against the farmer to recover for the injury to her nose, is the child likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the farmer did not use deadly force.","B":"No, because the farmer had probable cause to believe that the child was a thief.","C":"Yes, because the farmer should have posted a \"No Trespassing\" sign.","D":"Yes, because the farmer used excessive force."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A defendant is only privileged to use the amount of force reasonably necessary to prevent or terminate a trespass (ie, privileged to use proportionate force). Therefore, if a trespass could have been deterred by less forceful means, then the defendant is liable to the trespasser for harm caused by the use of excessive force (unreasonable or unnecessary force used in defense of oneself or property). Here, the child entered the farmer's property without his consent (trespass), so the farmer was privileged to use reasonable force to remove her from his farmhouse (eg, by carrying the child off his property). Instead, the farmer used excessive force because he struck the girl very hard in the face and broke her nose. Therefore, the child will likely recover for the injury to her nose. (Choice A) To prevail on a claim for battery, the child need only show that the farmer's intentional infliction of harmful contact constitutes excessive force—not deadly force (force likely to cause death or serious bodily harm). (Choice B) Even if the farmer had probable cause to believe that the child was a thief, he was only privileged to use reasonable force to protect his property. (Choice C) A defendant is not required to post \"No Trespassing\" signs to use reasonable force in defense of property. And though the absence of a warning sign may be considered in determining whether a defendant took reasonable steps to stop the trespass, that analysis is unnecessary since the farmer clearly used excessive force.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant is only privileged to use the amount of force reasonably necessary to prevent or end a trespass. If the defendant uses excessive force (unreasonable or unnecessary force used in defense of oneself or property), then the defendant is liable for the harm caused to the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 81 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (amount of force allowed in defense of property).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 82 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (effect of excessive force).","educationalObjective":"A defendant is only privileged to use the amount of force reasonably necessary to prevent or end a trespass. If the defendant uses excessive force (unreasonable or unnecessary force used in defense of oneself or property), then the defendant is liable for the harm caused to the plaintiff.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 81 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (amount of force allowed in defense of property).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 82 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (effect of excessive force).","rule":"A defendant is only privileged to use the amount of force reasonably necessary to prevent or end a trespass. If the defendant uses excessive force (unreasonable or unnecessary force used in defense of oneself or property), then the defendant is liable for the harm caused to the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"In an action on behalf of the child against the farmer to recover for the injury to her nose, is the child likely to prevail?","rule":"A defendant is only privileged to use the amount of force reasonably necessary to prevent or end a trespass. If the defendant uses excessive force (unreasonable or unnecessary force used in defense of oneself or property), then the defendant is liable for the harm caused to the plaintiff.","analysis":"A defendant is only privileged to use the amount of force reasonably necessary to prevent or terminate a trespass (ie, privileged to use proportionate force). Therefore, if a trespass could have been deterred by less forceful means, then the defendant is liable to the trespasser for harm caused by the use of excessive force (unreasonable or unnecessary force used in defense of oneself or property). Here, the child entered the farmer's property without his consent (trespass), so the farmer was privileged to use reasonable force to remove her from his farmhouse (eg, by carrying the child off his property). Instead, the farmer used excessive force because he struck the girl very hard in the face and broke her nose. Therefore, the child will likely recover for the injury to her nose. (Choice A) To prevail on a claim for battery, the child need only show that the farmer's intentional infliction of harmful contact constitutes excessive force—not deadly force (force likely to cause death or serious bodily harm). (Choice B) Even if the farmer had probable cause to believe that the child was a thief, he was only privileged to use reasonable force to protect his property. (Choice C) A defendant is not required to post \"No Trespassing\" signs to use reasonable force in defense of property. And though the absence of a warning sign may be considered in determining whether a defendant took reasonable steps to stop the trespass, that analysis is unnecessary since the farmer clearly used excessive force.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the farmer used excessive force."},"questionZh":"一位农民将古董存放在其名下的一处无人居住的农舍里。\n\n该农舍过去曾多次遭人闯入，导致部分物品失窃。\n\n为了震慑入侵者，这位农民决定安装警报系统，而不是竖立“禁止擅入”的告示牌。\n\n当他在农舍内安装警报系统时，听到隔壁房间传来窗户开启的声音。\n\n他悄悄走到房门口，猛地推开门，发现了一名10岁的入侵者。\n\n农民随即重击该女童面部，导致其鼻梁骨折。\n\n若代表该女童向农民提起诉讼，要求赔偿其鼻部受伤的损失，该女童胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为农民并未动用致命武力。","B":"不会，因为农民有正当理由相信该女童是窃贼。","C":"会，因为农民本应竖立“禁止擅入”的告示牌。","D":"会，因为农民使用了过度的武力。"},"explanationZh":"被告仅有权使用为防止或终止侵入行为而合理必要的武力（即有权使用适度武力）。\n\n因此，如果本可通过较温和的手段阻止侵入，而被告却使用了过度武力（即在保卫自身或财产时使用了不合理或不必要的武力），则被告须对因使用过度武力而给侵入者造成的伤害承担责任。\n\n在本案中，该儿童未经农场主同意进入其领地（构成侵入），因此农场主有权使用合理武力将其带离农舍（例如，将其抱离领地）。\n\n然而，农场主使用了过度武力，因为他重击了女孩的面部并导致其鼻梁骨折。\n\n因此，该儿童很可能就其鼻部受伤获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）若要使针对故意侵害身体（battery）行为（battery）的索赔胜诉，该儿童只需证明农场主蓄意施加的有害接触构成了过度武力，而无需证明其使用了致命武力（即可能导致死亡或严重身体伤害的武力）。\n\n（选项 B）即使农场主有正当理由相信该儿童是小偷，他也仅有权使用合理武力来保护其财产。\n\n（选项 C）被告在为保护财产而使用合理武力时，无需设立“禁止入内”的警示牌。\n\n尽管在判定被告是否采取合理措施制止侵入行为时，可能会考虑是否缺乏警示牌这一因素，但由于农场主显然使用了过度武力，因此无需进行此类分析。\n\n教育目标：被告仅有权使用为防止或终止侵入行为而合理必要的武力。\n\n如果被告使用了过度武力（即在保卫自身或财产时使用了不合理或不必要的武力），则被告须对给原告造成的伤害承担责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 81 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于保卫财产时允许使用的武力程度）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 82 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于使用过度武力的后果）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"被告仅有权使用为防止或制止擅自闯入行为而合理必要的武力。\n\n如果被告使用了过度武力（即在防卫自身或财产时使用了不合理或不必要的武力），则须对给原告造成的伤害承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"若代表该女童向农民提起诉讼，要求赔偿其鼻部受伤的损失，该女童胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"被告仅有权使用为防止或制止擅自闯入行为而合理必要的武力。\n\n如果被告使用了过度武力（即在防卫自身或财产时使用了不合理或不必要的武力），则须对给原告造成的伤害承担责任。","analysis":"被告仅有权使用为防止或终止侵入行为而合理必要的武力（即有权使用适度武力）。\n\n因此，如果本可通过较温和的手段阻止侵入，而被告却使用了过度武力（即在保卫自身或财产时使用了不合理或不必要的武力），则被告须对因使用过度武力而给侵入者造成的伤害承担责任。\n\n在本案中，该儿童未经农场主同意进入其领地（构成侵入），因此农场主有权使用合理武力将其带离农舍（例如，将其抱离领地）。\n\n然而，农场主使用了过度武力，因为他重击了女孩的面部并导致其鼻梁骨折。\n\n因此，该儿童很可能就其鼻部受伤获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）若要使针对故意侵害身体（battery）行为（battery）的索赔胜诉，该儿童只需证明农场主蓄意施加的有害接触构成了过度武力，而无需证明其使用了致命武力（即可能导致死亡或严重身体伤害的武力）。\n\n（选项 B）即使农场主有正当理由相信该儿童是小偷，他也仅有权使用合理武力来保护其财产。\n\n（选项 C）被告在为保护财产而使用合理武力时，无需设立“禁止入内”的警示牌。\n\n尽管在判定被告是否采取合理措施制止侵入行为时，可能会考虑是否缺乏警示牌这一因素，但由于农场主显然使用了过度武力，因此无需进行此类分析。\n\n教育目标：被告仅有权使用为防止或终止侵入行为而合理必要的武力。\n\n如果被告使用了过度武力（即在保卫自身或财产时使用了不合理或不必要的武力），则被告须对给原告造成的伤害承担责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 81 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于保卫财产时允许使用的武力程度）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 82 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于使用过度武力的后果）。","conclusion":"答案 D：会，因为农民使用了过度的武力。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"defense of property","ipa":"/dɪfˈɛns ˈəv pɹˈɑpɝti/","syllables":"de·fense of prop·erty","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"财产防卫：在适当条件下使用合理非致命武力保护财产","contextualMeaning":"财产防卫：在适当条件下使用合理非致命武力保护财产","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) A defendant is not required to post \"No Trespassing\" signs to use reasonable force in defense of property.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"warning","ipa":"/wˈɔɹnɪŋ/","syllables":"warn·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 警告, 预告, 预兆, 通知；[法] 警告, 警戒, 预告； 警告的, 注意的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“warning”","wordForm":"名词；词形 0:warn/1:i/s:warnings/i:warning","sourceSentence":"And though the absence of a warning sign may be considered in determining whether a defendant took reasonable steps to stop the trespass, that analysis is unnecessary since the farmer clearly used excessive force.","synonyms":["admonition","monition","word of advice","admonitory"]},{"term":"trespasser","ipa":"/'trespəsə/","syllables":"tres·passer","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 侵害者, 违反者, 侵入者","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“trespasser”","wordForm":"词；词形 s:trespassers","sourceSentence":"Therefore, if a trespass could have been deterred by less forceful means, then the defendant is liable to the trespasser for harm caused by the use of excessive force (unreasonable or unnecessary force used in defense of oneself or property).","synonyms":["intruder","interloper"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-6c20994438db633c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":6,"sourceQuestionPage":15,"sourceQuestionPages":[15],"sourceExplanationPages":[16,17],"sourceAnswerPages":[16,17],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-007","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A motivational speaker walked onto a stage to perform before a sold-out crowd. As he was about to sit on a stool located center stage, a stagehand decided, as a practical joke, to pull the stool from beneath the speaker. This caused the speaker to fall backwards, and the crowd erupted with laughter. The speaker was highly embarrassed but did not sustain any physical injuries. The speaker has asserted a battery claim against the stagehand. Is the speaker likely to prevail on his claim?","choices":{"A":"No, because the speaker did not suffer any physical harm.","B":"No, because the stagehand only intended to pull a practical joke on the speaker.","C":"Yes, because the stagehand acted negligently.","D":"Yes, because the stagehand knew that the speaker was about to sit on the stool."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Battery occurs when a defendant intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or create the imminent apprehension thereof) AND causes such contact with the plaintiff. The intent requirement is met if the defendant acts with either: purpose – the desire to cause harmful / offensive contact or imminent apprehension thereof or knowledge – the substantial certainty that such contact or apprehension will result. Contact is harmful if it causes physical impairment, pain, or illness. Contact is offensive if it would offend a reasonable person's sense of dignity. Here, the stagehand knew that the speaker would fall to the ground (intent) because the stagehand saw that the speaker was about to sit when the stagehand pulled the stool out from under him. Since this intentional act caused the speaker to suffer an embarrassing fall (offensive contact), the speaker will likely prevail on his battery claim. (Choice A) A plaintiff need not suffer physical harm to recover for battery. That is because a battery can also occur if the defendant causes offensive contact (as seen here). (Choice B) The stagehand may have only acted with the desire to pull a practical joke on the speaker. But since the stagehand was substantially certain that harmful or offensive contact would result from pulling the stool out from under the speaker, the stagehand still acted with the requisite intent for battery. (Choice C) Battery requires proof that the defendant acted intentionally—not negligently (ie, failing to act as a reasonable person would have acted under similar circumstances).\n\nEducational objective: For battery, intent exists when the defendant acts with the purpose (desire) or knowledge (substantial certainty) that harmful or offensive contact, or the imminent apprehension thereof, will result.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 18 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by offensive contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 19 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of offensive contact).\n\nGarratt v. Dailey, 279 P.2d 1091, 1094 (Wash. 1955) (explaining that battery requires an intent to cause contact that results in harmful or offensive contact).","educationalObjective":"For battery, intent exists when the defendant acts with the purpose (desire) or knowledge (substantial certainty) that harmful or offensive contact, or the imminent apprehension thereof, will result.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 18 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by offensive contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 19 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of offensive contact).\n\nGarratt v. Dailey, 279 P.2d 1091, 1094 (Wash. 1955) (explaining that battery requires an intent to cause contact that results in harmful or offensive contact).","rule":"For battery, intent exists when the defendant acts with the purpose (desire) or knowledge (substantial certainty) that harmful or offensive contact, or the imminent apprehension thereof, will result.","irac":{"issue":"Is the speaker likely to prevail on his claim?","rule":"For battery, intent exists when the defendant acts with the purpose (desire) or knowledge (substantial certainty) that harmful or offensive contact, or the imminent apprehension thereof, will result.","analysis":"Battery occurs when a defendant intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or create the imminent apprehension thereof) AND causes such contact with the plaintiff. The intent requirement is met if the defendant acts with either: purpose – the desire to cause harmful / offensive contact or imminent apprehension thereof or knowledge – the substantial certainty that such contact or apprehension will result. Contact is harmful if it causes physical impairment, pain, or illness. Contact is offensive if it would offend a reasonable person's sense of dignity. Here, the stagehand knew that the speaker would fall to the ground (intent) because the stagehand saw that the speaker was about to sit when the stagehand pulled the stool out from under him. Since this intentional act caused the speaker to suffer an embarrassing fall (offensive contact), the speaker will likely prevail on his battery claim. (Choice A) A plaintiff need not suffer physical harm to recover for battery. That is because a battery can also occur if the defendant causes offensive contact (as seen here). (Choice B) The stagehand may have only acted with the desire to pull a practical joke on the speaker. But since the stagehand was substantially certain that harmful or offensive contact would result from pulling the stool out from under the speaker, the stagehand still acted with the requisite intent for battery. (Choice C) Battery requires proof that the defendant acted intentionally—not negligently (ie, failing to act as a reasonable person would have acted under similar circumstances).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the stagehand knew that the speaker was about to sit on the stool."},"questionZh":"一位励志演讲者走上舞台，准备为座无虚席的观众表演。\n\n当他正要坐到舞台中央的凳子上时，一名舞台工作人员出于恶作剧，将凳子从他脚下抽走。\n\n这导致演讲者向后摔倒，观众哄堂大笑。\n\n演讲者虽然非常尴尬，但并未受到任何身体伤害。\n\n演讲者已对该舞台工作人员提起人身伤害诉讼。\n\n演讲者胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为演讲者没有受到任何身体伤害。","B":"否，因为该舞台工作人员只是想和演讲者开个玩笑。","C":"是，因为该舞台工作人员的行为属于过失。","D":"是，因为该舞台工作人员明知演讲者即将坐到凳子上。"},"explanationZh":"当被告有意造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或引起对此类接触的即时恐惧感），并导致原告发生此类接触时，即构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权。\n\n若被告的行为出于以下任一心态，即满足“意图”要件：目的（即希望造成有害/冒犯性接触或引起对此类接触的即时恐惧感）或明知（即确信此类接触或恐惧感必然发生）。\n\n若接触导致身体损伤、疼痛或疾病，则该接触被视为有害的；若接触冒犯了理性人的尊严感，则被视为冒犯性的。\n\n在本案中，舞台工作人员知道演讲者会跌倒在地（具备意图），因为他在抽走演讲者身下的凳子时，看到演讲者正准备坐下。\n\n由于这一故意行为导致演讲者遭受了令人尴尬的跌倒（属于冒犯性接触），演讲者很可能在针对“故意侵害身体（battery）”的索赔诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）原告无需遭受身体伤害即可就“故意侵害身体（battery）”获得赔偿。\n\n这是因为，如果被告造成了冒犯性接触（如本案所示），也可能构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”。\n\n（选项 B）舞台工作人员的行为可能仅仅是出于想跟演讲者开个玩笑的念头。\n\n但由于他确信抽走凳子会导致有害或冒犯性接触，因此他仍具备构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”所需的意图。\n\n（选项 C）“故意侵害身体（battery）”侵权要求证明被告的行为是故意的，而非过失行为（即未能在类似情况下像理性人那样行事）。\n\n教育目标：就“故意侵害身体（battery）”侵权而言，当被告出于目的（希望）或明知（确信必然发生）而导致有害或冒犯性接触（或引起对此类接触的即时恐惧感）时，即存在意图。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 18 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因冒犯性接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 19 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（冒犯性接触的定义）。\n\nGarratt 诉 Dailey 案，279 P.2d 1091, 1094 (Wash. 1955)（阐明构成故意侵害身体（battery）须具备导致接触的意图，且该接触最终造成了有害或冒犯性的后果）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"就故意侵害身体（battery）罪而言，当被告人出于意图（意愿）或明知（基本确定）将导致有害或冒犯性的接触，或使他人即将面临此类接触时，即构成故意。","iracZh":{"issue":"发言者有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"就故意侵害身体（battery）罪而言，当被告人出于意图（意愿）或明知（基本确定）将导致有害或冒犯性的接触，或使他人即将面临此类接触时，即构成故意。","analysis":"当被告有意造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或引起对此类接触的即时恐惧感），并导致原告发生此类接触时，即构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权。\n\n若被告的行为出于以下任一心态，即满足“意图”要件：目的（即希望造成有害/冒犯性接触或引起对此类接触的即时恐惧感）或明知（即确信此类接触或恐惧感必然发生）。\n\n若接触导致身体损伤、疼痛或疾病，则该接触被视为有害的；若接触冒犯了理性人的尊严感，则被视为冒犯性的。\n\n在本案中，舞台工作人员知道演讲者会跌倒在地（具备意图），因为他在抽走演讲者身下的凳子时，看到演讲者正准备坐下。\n\n由于这一故意行为导致演讲者遭受了令人尴尬的跌倒（属于冒犯性接触），演讲者很可能在针对“故意侵害身体（battery）”的索赔诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）原告无需遭受身体伤害即可就“故意侵害身体（battery）”获得赔偿。\n\n这是因为，如果被告造成了冒犯性接触（如本案所示），也可能构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”。\n\n（选项 B）舞台工作人员的行为可能仅仅是出于想跟演讲者开个玩笑的念头。\n\n但由于他确信抽走凳子会导致有害或冒犯性接触，因此他仍具备构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”所需的意图。\n\n（选项 C）“故意侵害身体（battery）”侵权要求证明被告的行为是故意的，而非过失行为（即未能在类似情况下像理性人那样行事）。\n\n教育目标：就“故意侵害身体（battery）”侵权而言，当被告出于目的（希望）或明知（确信必然发生）而导致有害或冒犯性接触（或引起对此类接触的即时恐惧感）时，即存在意图。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 18 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因冒犯性接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 19 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（冒犯性接触的定义）。\n\nGarratt 诉 Dailey 案，279 P.2d 1091, 1094 (Wash. 1955)（阐明构成故意侵害身体（battery）须具备导致接触的意图，且该接触最终造成了有害或冒犯性的后果）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是，因为该舞台工作人员明知演讲者即将坐到凳子上。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"substantial certainty","ipa":"/sʌbstˈænʃəl sˈɝtənti/","syllables":"sub·stan·tial cer·tainty","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"高度确定性：明知侵害结果几乎必然发生，可满足故意要件","contextualMeaning":"高度确定性：明知侵害结果几乎必然发生，可满足故意要件","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"For battery, intent exists when the defendant acts with the purpose (desire) or knowledge (substantial certainty) that harmful or offensive contact, or the imminent apprehension thereof, will result.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"reasonable person","ipa":"/ɹˈizənəbəl pˈɝsən/","syllables":"rea·son·able per·son","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合理人：同等情境下具有通常谨慎程度的客观标准","contextualMeaning":"合理人：同等情境下具有通常谨慎程度的客观标准","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Contact is offensive if it would offend a reasonable person's sense of dignity.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-c0e67a766eb6a628"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":7,"sourceQuestionPage":18,"sourceQuestionPages":[18],"sourceExplanationPages":[19,20],"sourceAnswerPages":[19,20],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-008","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"The warden of a state prison prohibits the photographing of the face of any prisoner without the prisoner's consent. A news photographer wanted to photograph a notorious mobster incarcerated at the prison. To circumvent the warden's prohibition, the photographer flew over the prison exercise yard and photographed the mobster. A prisoner, who was imprisoned for a technical violation of a regulatory statute, happened to be standing next to the mobster when the photograph was taken. When the picture appeared in the press, the prisoner suffered severe emotional distress because he believed that his business associates and friends would think he was consorting with gangsters. The prisoner suffered no physical harm as the result of his emotional distress. The prisoner brought an action against the photographer for intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress. What is the best argument that the photographer can make in support of a motion for summary judgment?","choices":{"A":"As a news photographer, he was privileged to take photographs that others could not.","B":"No reasonable person could conclude that the photographer intended to photograph the prisoner.","C":"No reasonable person could conclude that the photographer's conduct was extreme and outrageous as to the prisoner.","D":"The prisoner did not suffer any physical injury arising from the emotional distress."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A defendant's motion for summary judgment will be granted when there is no genuine issue of material fact (ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor) and the defendant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (eg, at least one element of the plaintiff's claim lacks legally sufficient evidence). For intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress (IIED), the defendant's motion should be granted if it disproves one or more of the following elements: The defendant intended to cause the plaintiff severe emotional distress (or acted recklessly as to the risk of causing such distress).\n\nThe defendant's conduct was extreme and outrageous.\n\nThe defendant caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress.\n\nAccordingly, the photographer's best argument is that no reasonable person could find that his conduct was extreme and outrageous. (Choice A) News photographers are not immune from liability for IIED, so the photographer could be liable—like any other person—if all the elements of this claim are met. (Choice B) IIED requires the specific intent to cause severe emotional distress—not intent to do the act that caused the distress. Therefore, even if no reasonable person could find that the photographer intended to photograph the prisoner, the photographer's summary judgment motion would fail if a reasonable person could still find that he intended to cause the prisoner severe emotional distress (or recklessly caused that distress). (Choice D) Although most tort claims require proof of bodily harm before plaintiffs can recover for emotional harm, IIED claims allow plaintiffs to recover for stand-alone emotional harm (ie, emotional harm that does not result from bodily harm). Therefore, the prisoner's lack of physical injury does not support the photographer's motion for summary judgment.\n\nEducational objective: For intentional infliction of emotional distress, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress by acting in an extreme and outrageous manner.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 56(a) (summary judgment).","educationalObjective":"For intentional infliction of emotional distress, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress by acting in an extreme and outrageous manner.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 56(a) (summary judgment).","rule":"For intentional infliction of emotional distress, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress by acting in an extreme and outrageous manner.","irac":{"issue":"What is the best argument that the photographer can make in support of a motion for summary judgment?","rule":"For intentional infliction of emotional distress, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress by acting in an extreme and outrageous manner.","analysis":"A defendant's motion for summary judgment will be granted when there is no genuine issue of material fact (ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor) and the defendant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (eg, at least one element of the plaintiff's claim lacks legally sufficient evidence). For intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress (IIED), the defendant's motion should be granted if it disproves one or more of the following elements: The defendant intended to cause the plaintiff severe emotional distress (or acted recklessly as to the risk of causing such distress).\n\nThe defendant's conduct was extreme and outrageous.\n\nThe defendant caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress.\n\nAccordingly, the photographer's best argument is that no reasonable person could find that his conduct was extreme and outrageous. (Choice A) News photographers are not immune from liability for IIED, so the photographer could be liable—like any other person—if all the elements of this claim are met. (Choice B) IIED requires the specific intent to cause severe emotional distress—not intent to do the act that caused the distress. Therefore, even if no reasonable person could find that the photographer intended to photograph the prisoner, the photographer's summary judgment motion would fail if a reasonable person could still find that he intended to cause the prisoner severe emotional distress (or recklessly caused that distress). (Choice D) Although most tort claims require proof of bodily harm before plaintiffs can recover for emotional harm, IIED claims allow plaintiffs to recover for stand-alone emotional harm (ie, emotional harm that does not result from bodily harm). Therefore, the prisoner's lack of physical injury does not support the photographer's motion for summary judgment.","conclusion":"Answer C: No reasonable person could conclude that the photographer's conduct was extreme and outrageous as to the prisoner."},"questionZh":"某州立监狱的典狱长禁止在未经囚犯同意的情况下拍摄其面部照片。\n\n一位新闻摄影师想要拍摄一名关押在该监狱的臭名昭著的黑帮分子。\n\n为了规避典狱长的禁令，该摄影师乘飞机飞越监狱的活动场，拍下了这名黑帮分子的照片。\n\n拍摄时，一名因违反某项监管法规的技术性条款而入狱的囚犯恰好站在该黑帮分子身旁。\n\n当照片见诸媒体时，这名囚犯遭受了严重的精神痛苦，因为他担心自己的商业伙伴和朋友会误以为他与黑帮分子有来往。\n\n该囚犯并未因这种精神痛苦而遭受任何身体伤害。\n\n该囚犯针对该摄影师提起了诉讼，指控其故意或鲁莽地造成了精神痛苦。\n\n在支持简易判决动议时，该摄影师能提出的最有力的论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"作为一名新闻摄影师，他享有拍摄他人无法拍摄的照片的特权。","B":"任何理性之人都无法得出结论，认为该摄影师有意拍摄这名囚犯。","C":"任何理性之人都无法得出结论，认为该摄影师针对这名囚犯的行为属于极端且令人愤慨（outrageous）的行为。","D":"该囚犯并未因精神痛苦而遭受任何身体伤害。"},"explanationZh":"当不存在实质性事实争议（即没有合理的陪审团会做出有利于原告的裁决）且被告依法有权获得胜诉判决（例如，原告主张的至少一个构成要件缺乏法律上充分的证据）时，法院将批准被告提出的即决判决动议。\n\n对于故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦（IIED）的侵权主张，如果被告能反驳以下一个或多个构成要件，则其动议应获批准：被告有意造成原告严重的精神痛苦（或在造成此类痛苦的风险方面表现出鲁莽）。\n\n被告的行为极端且令人愤慨。\n\n被告导致原告遭受了严重的精神痛苦。\n\n因此，该摄影师最有力的抗辩理由是：没有理性的人会认为其行为属于极端且令人愤慨的行为。\n\n（选项 A）新闻摄影师并不享有免于承担 IIED 责任的特权；因此，如果该主张的所有构成要件均满足，该摄影师可能像任何其他人一样承担责任。\n\n（选项 B）IIED 要求具备造成严重精神痛苦的特定意图，而非仅仅是实施导致痛苦之行为的意图。\n\n因此，即使没有理性的人会认为该摄影师有意拍摄该囚犯，但如果理性的人仍可能认定他有意造成该囚犯严重的精神痛苦（或鲁莽地造成了该痛苦），那么该摄影师的即决判决动议仍将失败。\n\n（选项 D）尽管大多数侵权主张要求先证明存在身体伤害，原告方可就精神伤害获得赔偿，但 IIED 主张允许原告就独立的精神伤害（即并非由身体伤害引起的精神伤害）获得赔偿。\n\n因此，该囚犯未受身体伤害这一事实并不能支持摄影师提出的即决判决动议。\n\n教育目标：对于故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）的侵权主张，原告必须证明被告通过极端且令人愤慨的行为，故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受了严重的精神痛苦。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 56(a) 条（即决判决）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对于“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”的侵权行为，原告必须证明被告通过极端且令人愤慨的行为，故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受了严重的精神痛苦。","iracZh":{"issue":"在支持简易判决动议时，该摄影师能提出的最有力的论点是什么？","rule":"对于“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”的侵权行为，原告必须证明被告通过极端且令人愤慨的行为，故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受了严重的精神痛苦。","analysis":"当不存在实质性事实争议（即没有合理的陪审团会做出有利于原告的裁决）且被告依法有权获得胜诉判决（例如，原告主张的至少一个构成要件缺乏法律上充分的证据）时，法院将批准被告提出的即决判决动议。\n\n对于故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦（IIED）的侵权主张，如果被告能反驳以下一个或多个构成要件，则其动议应获批准：被告有意造成原告严重的精神痛苦（或在造成此类痛苦的风险方面表现出鲁莽）。\n\n被告的行为极端且令人愤慨。\n\n被告导致原告遭受了严重的精神痛苦。\n\n因此，该摄影师最有力的抗辩理由是：没有理性的人会认为其行为属于极端且令人愤慨的行为。\n\n（选项 A）新闻摄影师并不享有免于承担 IIED 责任的特权；因此，如果该主张的所有构成要件均满足，该摄影师可能像任何其他人一样承担责任。\n\n（选项 B）IIED 要求具备造成严重精神痛苦的特定意图，而非仅仅是实施导致痛苦之行为的意图。\n\n因此，即使没有理性的人会认为该摄影师有意拍摄该囚犯，但如果理性的人仍可能认定他有意造成该囚犯严重的精神痛苦（或鲁莽地造成了该痛苦），那么该摄影师的即决判决动议仍将失败。\n\n（选项 D）尽管大多数侵权主张要求先证明存在身体伤害，原告方可就精神伤害获得赔偿，但 IIED 主张允许原告就独立的精神伤害（即并非由身体伤害引起的精神伤害）获得赔偿。\n\n因此，该囚犯未受身体伤害这一事实并不能支持摄影师提出的即决判决动议。\n\n教育目标：对于故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）的侵权主张，原告必须证明被告通过极端且令人愤慨的行为，故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受了严重的精神痛苦。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 56(a) 条（即决判决）。","conclusion":"答案 C：任何理性之人都无法得出结论，认为该摄影师针对这名囚犯的行为属于极端且令人愤慨（outrageous）的行为。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"intentional infliction of emotional distress","ipa":"/ˌɪntˈɛnʃənəl ˌɪnflˈɪkʃən ˈəv ɪmˈoʊʃənəl dɪstɹˈɛs/","syllables":"in·ten·tional in·flic·tion of emo·tional dis·tress","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）","contextualMeaning":"故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"For intentional infliction of emotional distress, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress by acting in an extreme and outrageous manner.","synonyms":["IIED","outrage tort"]},{"term":"severe emotional distress","ipa":"/sʌvˈɪɹ ɪmˈoʊʃənəl dɪstɹˈɛs/","syllables":"se·vere emo·tional dis·tress","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"严重精神痛苦：强度或持续程度足以获得法律救济的精神损害","contextualMeaning":"严重精神痛苦：强度或持续程度足以获得法律救济的精神损害","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"When the picture appeared in the press, the prisoner suffered severe emotional distress because he believed that his business associates and friends would think he was consorting with gangsters.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"imprisoned","ipa":"/ˌɪmpɹˈɪzənd/","syllables":"im·pris·oned","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 下狱, 监禁( imprison的过去式和过去分词 )","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“imprisoned”","wordForm":"词；原形 imprison；词形 0:imprison/1:dp/d:imprisoned","sourceSentence":"A prisoner, who was imprisoned for a technical violation of a regulatory statute, happened to be standing next to the mobster when the photograph was taken.","synonyms":["incarcerate","lag","immure","put behind bars"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-90806fce196ba508"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":8,"sourceQuestionPage":21,"sourceQuestionPages":[21],"sourceExplanationPages":[22,23],"sourceAnswerPages":[22,23],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-009","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A boater, caught in a sudden storm and reasonably fearing that her boat would capsize, drove the boat up to a pier, exited the boat, and tied it to the pier. The pier was clearly marked with \"No Trespassing\" signs. The owner of the pier ran up to the boater and told her that the boat could not remain tied to his pier. The boater offered to pay the owner for the use of the pier but, over the boater's protest, the owner untied the boat and pushed it away from the pier. The boat was lost at sea. Is the boater likely to prevail in an action against the owner to recover the value of the boat?","choices":{"A":"No, because the owner told the boater that she could not tie her boat to the pier.","B":"No, because there was a possibility that the boat would not be damaged by the storm.","C":"Yes, because the boater offered to pay the owner for the use of his pier.","D":"Yes, because the boater was privileged to enter the owner's property to save her boat."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under the doctrine of private necessity, a trespass to land is privileged when the intrusion is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm. The landowner cannot stop the privileged trespass and is liable to the trespasser for damages resulting from any attempt to do so. Here, the boater tied her boat to the pier without the owner's consent (trespass) because she reasonably believed that the intrusion was necessary to protect her boat from capsizing in the sudden storm (private necessity). And though the owner had no right to stop her intrusion, he untied her boat and pushed it away from the pier, causing the boat to be lost at sea. As a result, the boater can recover the value of her boat from the owner. (Choice A) The boater had the right to tie her boat to the pier over the owner's protest to save it from capsizing in the storm (private necessity). Therefore, the owner's objection to the boater's use of the pier is irrelevant. (Choice B) Even if there was a possibility that the boat would not be damaged by the storm, the boater's trespass was still privileged because her intrusion reasonably appeared to be necessary to protect her boat. (Choice C) A person does not have to pay to use the landowner's property for private necessity. Therefore, the owner would still be liable for damage to the boat even if the boater had not offered to pay for the use of the pier.\n\nEducational objective: A landowner must permit a trespass arising from private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm) and is liable for damages resulting from an attempt to stop a privileged trespass.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 197 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of private necessity).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 77 cmt. d & e (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (defense of property).","educationalObjective":"A landowner must permit a trespass arising from private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm) and is liable for damages resulting from an attempt to stop a privileged trespass.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 197 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of private necessity).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 77 cmt. d & e (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (defense of property).","rule":"A landowner must permit a trespass arising from private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm) and is liable for damages resulting from an attempt to stop a privileged trespass.","irac":{"issue":"Is the boater likely to prevail in an action against the owner to recover the value of the boat?","rule":"A landowner must permit a trespass arising from private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm) and is liable for damages resulting from an attempt to stop a privileged trespass.","analysis":"Under the doctrine of private necessity, a trespass to land is privileged when the intrusion is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm. The landowner cannot stop the privileged trespass and is liable to the trespasser for damages resulting from any attempt to do so. Here, the boater tied her boat to the pier without the owner's consent (trespass) because she reasonably believed that the intrusion was necessary to protect her boat from capsizing in the sudden storm (private necessity). And though the owner had no right to stop her intrusion, he untied her boat and pushed it away from the pier, causing the boat to be lost at sea. As a result, the boater can recover the value of her boat from the owner. (Choice A) The boater had the right to tie her boat to the pier over the owner's protest to save it from capsizing in the storm (private necessity). Therefore, the owner's objection to the boater's use of the pier is irrelevant. (Choice B) Even if there was a possibility that the boat would not be damaged by the storm, the boater's trespass was still privileged because her intrusion reasonably appeared to be necessary to protect her boat. (Choice C) A person does not have to pay to use the landowner's property for private necessity. Therefore, the owner would still be liable for damage to the boat even if the boater had not offered to pay for the use of the pier.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the boater was privileged to enter the owner's property to save her boat."},"questionZh":"一位船主遭遇突如其来的风暴，担心船只倾覆，便将船开到码头，下船后将船系在码头上。\n\n码头上明明立着“禁止入内”的标志。\n\n码头的所有者跑过来告诉船主，她的船不能停在他的码头上。\n\n船主提出支付码头使用费，但码头所有者不顾船主的反对，解开缆绳，将船推离码头。\n\n船最终沉入大海。\n\n船主起诉码头所有者，要求赔偿船只损失，胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为码头所有者告诉船主她不能将船系在码头上。","B":"不可能，因为船只有可能不会被风暴损坏。","C":"可能，因为船主提出支付码头使用费。","D":"是的，因为船主有权进入船主的土地去救她的船。"},"explanationZh":"根据“私下必要性”（private necessity）原则，如果侵入行为对于保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害是必要的（或看起来有必要），则该侵入土地的行为属于“特权侵入”（privileged trespass）。\n\n土地所有者无权阻止这种特权侵入；若试图阻止并因此造成损害，所有者须向侵入者承担赔偿责任。\n\n在本案中，船主在未经码头所有者同意的情况下将船系在码头上（构成侵入），因为她有合理的理由相信，为了防止船只在突发风暴中倾覆，这种侵入行为是必要的（构成私下必要性）。\n\n尽管码头所有者无权阻止这一侵入行为，但他解开了船只并将其推离码头，导致船只在海上失踪。\n\n因此，船主有权向码头所有者索赔船只的价值。\n\n（选项 A）为了防止船只在风暴中倾覆，船主有权在码头所有者反对的情况下将船系在码头上（基于私下必要性）。\n\n因此，码头所有者对船主使用码头的反对意见不具有法律效力。\n\n（选项 B）即使船只实际上未必会因风暴受损，船主的侵入行为仍属于特权侵入，因为从当时情况看，该侵入行为对于保护船只是有必要的。\n\n（选项 C）因私下必要性而使用他人财产无需付费。\n\n因此，即使船主未提出为使用码头付费，码头所有者仍须对船只受损承担赔偿责任。\n\n教育目标：土地所有者必须容忍因私下必要性（即为保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害而有必要或看起来有必要进行的侵入）而产生的侵入行为；若试图阻止此类特权侵入并因此造成损害，所有者须承担赔偿责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 197 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（私下必要性的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 77 条注释 d 和 e (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（财产抗辩）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地所有者必须允许出于私人需要而进行的侵入（即为了保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害而进行的侵入，或合理地认为必要的侵入），并且对试图阻止这种享有特权的侵入行为所造成的损害承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"船主是否有可能在起诉船主以追回船只价值的诉讼中胜诉？","rule":"土地所有者必须允许出于私人需要而进行的侵入（即为了保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害而进行的侵入，或合理地认为必要的侵入），并且对试图阻止这种享有特权的侵入行为所造成的损害承担责任。","analysis":"根据“私下必要性”（private necessity）原则，如果侵入行为对于保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害是必要的（或看起来有必要），则该侵入土地的行为属于“特权侵入”（privileged trespass）。\n\n土地所有者无权阻止这种特权侵入；若试图阻止并因此造成损害，所有者须向侵入者承担赔偿责任。\n\n在本案中，船主在未经码头所有者同意的情况下将船系在码头上（构成侵入），因为她有合理的理由相信，为了防止船只在突发风暴中倾覆，这种侵入行为是必要的（构成私下必要性）。\n\n尽管码头所有者无权阻止这一侵入行为，但他解开了船只并将其推离码头，导致船只在海上失踪。\n\n因此，船主有权向码头所有者索赔船只的价值。\n\n（选项 A）为了防止船只在风暴中倾覆，船主有权在码头所有者反对的情况下将船系在码头上（基于私下必要性）。\n\n因此，码头所有者对船主使用码头的反对意见不具有法律效力。\n\n（选项 B）即使船只实际上未必会因风暴受损，船主的侵入行为仍属于特权侵入，因为从当时情况看，该侵入行为对于保护船只是有必要的。\n\n（选项 C）因私下必要性而使用他人财产无需付费。\n\n因此，即使船主未提出为使用码头付费，码头所有者仍须对船只受损承担赔偿责任。\n\n教育目标：土地所有者必须容忍因私下必要性（即为保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害而有必要或看起来有必要进行的侵入）而产生的侵入行为；若试图阻止此类特权侵入并因此造成损害，所有者须承担赔偿责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 197 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（私下必要性的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 77 条注释 d 和 e (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（财产抗辩）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为船主有权进入船主的土地去救她的船。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"private necessity","ipa":"/pɹˈaɪvət nʌsˈɛsəti/","syllables":"pri·vate ne·ces·sity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"私人紧急避险：为避免私人重大损害而侵入，通常仍须赔偿实际损失","contextualMeaning":"私人紧急避险：为避免私人重大损害而侵入，通常仍须赔偿实际损失","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A landowner must permit a trespass arising from private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm) and is liable for damages resulting from an attempt to stop a privileged trespass.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"intrusion","ipa":"/ˌɪntɹˈuʒən/","syllables":"in·tru·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 闯入, 侵扰；[医] [向内]突入","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“intrusion”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:intrusions","sourceSentence":"A landowner must permit a trespass arising from private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm) and is liable for damages resulting from an attempt to stop a privileged trespass.","synonyms":["trespass","encroachment","violation","usurpation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-ea6ed85af25f1fbe"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":9,"sourceQuestionPage":24,"sourceQuestionPages":[24],"sourceExplanationPages":[25,26],"sourceAnswerPages":[25,26],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-010","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A teenager was riding a bicycle when she saw a classmate walking toward her. The teenager rode quickly toward the classmate, knowing that he would think she would run into him on her current trajectory. The teenager was not purposefully trying to harm or touch him. The classmate saw the teenager riding toward him and yelled at her to stop. The teenager swerved at the last moment and avoided hitting him. The classmate had a panic attack because he thought that the teenager would hit him. Is the classmate likely to succeed if he sues the teenager for assault?","choices":{"A":"No, because the teenager did not make contact with the classmate.","B":"No, because the teenager did not purposefully try to harm or touch the classmate.","C":"Yes, because the teenager acted with the requisite intent.","D":"Yes, because the teenager's conduct was extreme and outrageous."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Assault occurs when a defendant's intentional act causes a plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent harmful or offensive bodily contact. The intent requirement is met when the defendant acts with either: purpose – the desire to cause apprehension of contact or knowledge – the substantial certainty that such apprehension will result. Here, the teenager rode her bicycle directly at her classmate, causing him to think that she would hit him (reasonable apprehension of imminent contact). The teenager did not purposefully try to harm or touch the classmate (Choice B). But the teenager knew with substantial certainty that the classmate would think she would run into him, so she acted with the requisite intent. As a result, the classmate is likely to succeed in a suit against the teenager for assault. (Choice A) Assault merely requires that the plaintiff be placed in apprehension of imminent contact. Actual bodily contact is not required. Therefore, the fact that the teenager did not make contact with the classmate is irrelevant. (Choice D) Extreme and outrageous conduct (ie, conduct that is unacceptable in civilized society) is an element of intentional infliction of emotional distress—not assault, which only requires intentional conduct.\n\nEducational objective: For assault, intent exists when a defendant acts with the purpose (desire) or knowledge (substantial certainty) that his/her conduct will cause apprehension of imminent harmful or offensive contact.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (defining assault).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 32 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining the requisite intent for assault).","educationalObjective":"For assault, intent exists when a defendant acts with the purpose (desire) or knowledge (substantial certainty) that his/her conduct will cause apprehension of imminent harmful or offensive contact.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (defining assault).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 32 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining the requisite intent for assault).","rule":"For assault, intent exists when a defendant acts with the purpose (desire) or knowledge (substantial certainty) that his/her conduct will cause apprehension of imminent harmful or offensive contact.","irac":{"issue":"Is the classmate likely to succeed if he sues the teenager for assault?","rule":"For assault, intent exists when a defendant acts with the purpose (desire) or knowledge (substantial certainty) that his/her conduct will cause apprehension of imminent harmful or offensive contact.","analysis":"Assault occurs when a defendant's intentional act causes a plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent harmful or offensive bodily contact. The intent requirement is met when the defendant acts with either: purpose – the desire to cause apprehension of contact or knowledge – the substantial certainty that such apprehension will result. Here, the teenager rode her bicycle directly at her classmate, causing him to think that she would hit him (reasonable apprehension of imminent contact). The teenager did not purposefully try to harm or touch the classmate (Choice B). But the teenager knew with substantial certainty that the classmate would think she would run into him, so she acted with the requisite intent. As a result, the classmate is likely to succeed in a suit against the teenager for assault. (Choice A) Assault merely requires that the plaintiff be placed in apprehension of imminent contact. Actual bodily contact is not required. Therefore, the fact that the teenager did not make contact with the classmate is irrelevant. (Choice D) Extreme and outrageous conduct (ie, conduct that is unacceptable in civilized society) is an element of intentional infliction of emotional distress—not assault, which only requires intentional conduct.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the teenager acted with the requisite intent."},"questionZh":"一名少女骑着自行车时，看到一位男同学正朝她走来。\n\n她迅速向该同学骑去，明知按当时的行进路线，对方会以为她要撞上他。\n\n该少女并无意伤害或触碰该同学。\n\n该同学见少女向自己骑来，便大声喊她停下。\n\n少女在最后一刻转向，避免了碰撞。\n\n该同学因以为会被撞到而引发了惊恐发作。\n\n如果该同学起诉少女构成“袭击”（assault），他胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会胜诉，因为少女并未与该同学发生身体接触。","B":"不会胜诉，因为少女并无意伤害或触碰该同学。","C":"会胜诉，因为少女的行为具备了必要的意图。","D":"会胜诉，因为少女的行为极端且令人愤慨。"},"explanationZh":"当被告的故意行为导致原告合理地预感到即将发生有害或冒犯性的身体接触时，即构成“袭击”（assault）。\n\n关于“故意”这一要件，若被告的行为出于以下任一心态，即视为满足要求：目的（即意图引起对接触的预感）或明知（即确信该行为会导致这种预感）。\n\n在本案中，该青少年骑自行车径直冲向其同学，致使该同学认为她会撞上自己（即对即将发生的接触产生了合理的预感）。\n\n该青少年并无伤害或接触该同学的意图（选项 B）。\n\n然而，该青少年明知（且确信）该同学会认为她将撞上自己，因此她的行为具备了构成袭击所需的故意要件。\n\n因此，该同学针对该青少年提起的袭击诉讼很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）袭击仅要求原告产生对即将发生接触的预感，并不要求发生实际的身体接触。\n\n因此，该青少年未与该同学发生接触这一事实并不影响定性。\n\n（选项 D）“极端且令人愤慨的行为”（即文明社会所无法容忍的行为）是“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（intentional infliction of emotional distress）这一侵权行为的构成要件，而非“袭击”的要件；袭击仅要求行为人具有故意。\n\n教育目标：就袭击而言，当被告的行为出于某种目的（意图）或明知（确信）其行为会导致他人预感到即将发生有害或冒犯性的接触时，即存在法律意义上的故意。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 21 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于袭击的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 32 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于袭击所需故意的解释）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"就“袭击”而言，当被告采取某种行为，且其目的（意愿）或认知（具有高度确定性）在于使他人预感到即将发生有害或冒犯性的接触时，即构成法律意义上的意图。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该同学起诉少女构成“袭击”，他胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"就“袭击”而言，当被告采取某种行为，且其目的（意愿）或认知（具有高度确定性）在于使他人预感到即将发生有害或冒犯性的接触时，即构成法律意义上的意图。","analysis":"当被告的故意行为导致原告合理地预感到即将发生有害或冒犯性的身体接触时，即构成“袭击”（assault）。\n\n关于“故意”这一要件，若被告的行为出于以下任一心态，即视为满足要求：目的（即意图引起对接触的预感）或明知（即确信该行为会导致这种预感）。\n\n在本案中，该青少年骑自行车径直冲向其同学，致使该同学认为她会撞上自己（即对即将发生的接触产生了合理的预感）。\n\n该青少年并无伤害或接触该同学的意图（选项 B）。\n\n然而，该青少年明知（且确信）该同学会认为她将撞上自己，因此她的行为具备了构成袭击所需的故意要件。\n\n因此，该同学针对该青少年提起的袭击诉讼很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）袭击仅要求原告产生对即将发生接触的预感，并不要求发生实际的身体接触。\n\n因此，该青少年未与该同学发生接触这一事实并不影响定性。\n\n（选项 D）“极端且令人愤慨的行为”（即文明社会所无法容忍的行为）是“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（intentional infliction of emotional distress）这一侵权行为的构成要件，而非“袭击”的要件；袭击仅要求行为人具有故意。\n\n教育目标：就袭击而言，当被告的行为出于某种目的（意图）或明知（确信）其行为会导致他人预感到即将发生有害或冒犯性的接触时，即存在法律意义上的故意。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 21 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于袭击的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 32 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于袭击所需故意的解释）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会胜诉，因为少女的行为具备了必要的意图。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"extreme and outrageous conduct","ipa":"/ɛkstɹˈim ənd aʊtɹˈeɪdʒəs kˈɑndəkt/","syllables":"ex·treme and out·ra·geous con·duct","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"极端恶劣行为：超越文明社会可容忍界限的行为","contextualMeaning":"极端恶劣行为：超越文明社会可容忍界限的行为","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) Extreme and outrageous conduct (ie, conduct that is unacceptable in civilized society) is an element of intentional infliction of emotional distress—not assault, which only requires intentional conduct.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"reasonable apprehension","ipa":"/ɹˈizənəbəl ˌæpɹɪhˈɛnʃən/","syllables":"rea·son·able ap·pre·hen·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合理预期恐惧：对即将发生的有害或冒犯性接触产生合理认知","contextualMeaning":"合理预期恐惧：对即将发生的有害或冒犯性接触产生合理认知","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Assault occurs when a defendant's intentional act causes a plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent harmful or offensive bodily contact.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"assault","ipa":"/ʌsˈɔlt/","syllables":"as·sault","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 攻击, 袭击；vt. 袭击, 攻击；vi. 发动攻击","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“assault”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:assaults/d:assaulted/i:assaulting/p:assaulted/3:assaults","sourceSentence":"Is the classmate likely to succeed if he sues the teenager for assault?","synonyms":["rape","violation","ravishment","attack"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-c0e67a766eb6a628"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":10,"sourceQuestionPage":27,"sourceQuestionPages":[27],"sourceExplanationPages":[28,29],"sourceAnswerPages":[28,29],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-011","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A college student and her roommate were invited to a Halloween party. The roommate decided to dress up as an anime character whose defining feature was bright green hair. The roommate planned to buy a wig, but the student convinced the roommate to allow the student to dye the roommate's hair using a temporary green dye that would wash out after a single shampoo. They went to a store where the roommate accidentally purchased a bottle of permanent dye. The student noticed that the dye was permanent but did not point out the roommate's mistake. Instead, the student applied the permanent, bright-green dye to the roommate's hair. Unable to dye her hair back in time for her next shift, the roommate lost her part-time job as a server. If the roommate sues the student for battery, is the roommate likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the roommate consented to having her hair dyed by the student.","B":"No, because the student did not misrepresent the permanent nature of the dye.","C":"Yes, because the roommate lost her part-time job as a server.","D":"Yes, because the student was aware of the roommate's mistake."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When a defendant intends to inflict contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) and causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff, the defendant is liable for battery absent some defense. Actual or express consent is a defense to battery (and other intentional torts) when the plaintiff's words or actions manifested a willingness to submit to the defendant's conduct. However, consent is not valid and will not serve as a defense if: the plaintiff consented because of a substantial mistake concerning the nature of the invasion of the plaintiff's interests or the extent of the expected harm and the defendant knew about the mistake or caused it by affirmative misrepresentation or fraud. Here, the student applied permanent, bright-green dye to the roommate's hair (harmful contact). Although she consented to having her hair dyed by the student, the roommate believed that the dye she had purchased was temporary (substantial mistake). The student was aware of the roommate's mistake but said nothing. Therefore, the roommate's consent is no defense, and the roommate will likely prevail in her suit against the student for battery (Choice A). (Choice B) Although the student did not misrepresent the permanent nature of the dye, the student knew that the roommate mistakenly believed it was temporary. This is enough to render the roommate's consent invalid. (Choice C) Battery does not require proof of actual harm, so evidence that the roommate lost her job does not provide a basis for her to prevail. Instead, this evidence goes to how much she can recover in damages once her battery claim is established.\n\nEducational objective: Consent is no defense to battery (or other intentional torts) if (1) the plaintiff gave consent because of a substantial mistake concerning the nature of the invasion of the plaintiff's interests or the extent of the expected harm and (2) the defendant knew about the mistake or caused it by affirmative misrepresentation or fraud.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 892B (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (consent under mistake, misrepresentation, duress).","educationalObjective":"Consent is no defense to battery (or other intentional torts) if (1) the plaintiff gave consent because of a substantial mistake concerning the nature of the invasion of the plaintiff's interests or the extent of the expected harm and (2) the defendant knew about the mistake or caused it by affirmative misrepresentation or fraud.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 892B (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (consent under mistake, misrepresentation, duress).","rule":"Consent is no defense to battery (or other intentional torts) if (1) the plaintiff gave consent because of a substantial mistake concerning the nature of the invasion of the plaintiff's interests or the extent of the expected harm and (2) the defendant knew about the mistake or caused it by affirmative misrepresentation or fraud.","irac":{"issue":"If the roommate sues the student for battery, is the roommate likely to prevail?","rule":"Consent is no defense to battery (or other intentional torts) if (1) the plaintiff gave consent because of a substantial mistake concerning the nature of the invasion of the plaintiff's interests or the extent of the expected harm and (2) the defendant knew about the mistake or caused it by affirmative misrepresentation or fraud.","analysis":"When a defendant intends to inflict contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) and causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff, the defendant is liable for battery absent some defense. Actual or express consent is a defense to battery (and other intentional torts) when the plaintiff's words or actions manifested a willingness to submit to the defendant's conduct. However, consent is not valid and will not serve as a defense if: the plaintiff consented because of a substantial mistake concerning the nature of the invasion of the plaintiff's interests or the extent of the expected harm and the defendant knew about the mistake or caused it by affirmative misrepresentation or fraud. Here, the student applied permanent, bright-green dye to the roommate's hair (harmful contact). Although she consented to having her hair dyed by the student, the roommate believed that the dye she had purchased was temporary (substantial mistake). The student was aware of the roommate's mistake but said nothing. Therefore, the roommate's consent is no defense, and the roommate will likely prevail in her suit against the student for battery (Choice A). (Choice B) Although the student did not misrepresent the permanent nature of the dye, the student knew that the roommate mistakenly believed it was temporary. This is enough to render the roommate's consent invalid. (Choice C) Battery does not require proof of actual harm, so evidence that the roommate lost her job does not provide a basis for her to prevail. Instead, this evidence goes to how much she can recover in damages once her battery claim is established.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the student was aware of the roommate's mistake."},"questionZh":"一名女大学生和她的室友受邀参加万圣节派对。\n\n室友决定扮成一个以亮绿色头发为标志性特征的动漫角色。\n\n室友本打算买顶假发，但这名学生说服了室友，让她用一种洗一次头就能洗掉的临时性绿色染发剂来染发。\n\n她们去了一家商店，室友不小心买了一瓶永久性染发剂。\n\n这名学生注意到了染发剂是永久性的，但没有指出室友的错误，而是直接把这种永久性的亮绿色染发剂涂在了室友的头发上。\n\n由于无法在下次轮班前把头发染回原来的颜色，室友失去了服务员的兼职工作。\n\n如果室友以故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）为由起诉这名学生，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为室友同意让这名学生为她染发。","B":"不会，因为这名学生没有就染发剂的永久性特征进行虚假陈述。","C":"会，因为室友失去了服务员的兼职工作。","D":"会，因为这名学生知晓室友的错误。"},"explanationZh":"当被告意图实施接触（或使原告产生即将遭受接触的恐惧），并导致了对原告的有害或冒犯性接触时，若无抗辩事由，被告即构成侵权（故意侵害身体（battery）/非法接触）。\n\n如果原告的言行表明其愿意接受被告的行为，则实际或明示的同意可作为针对故意侵害身体（battery）（及其他故意侵权行为）的抗辩事由。\n\n然而，若出现以下情况，同意即无效，也不能作为抗辩事由：原告因对自身权益受侵害的性质或预期伤害程度存在重大误解而表示同意，且被告明知该误解或通过积极的虚假陈述或欺诈行为导致了该误解。\n\n在本案中，该学生给室友的头发染上了永久性的亮绿色染料（属于有害接触）。\n\n尽管室友同意让该学生染发，但她误以为所购染料是暂时性的（属于重大误解）。\n\n该学生明知室友存在误解却未予说明。\n\n因此，室友的同意不能构成抗辩事由，且室友很可能在针对该学生的故意侵害身体（battery）侵权诉讼中胜诉（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）尽管该学生未就染料的永久性特征进行虚假陈述，但她明知室友误以为染料是暂时性的。\n\n这足以导致室友的同意无效。\n\n（选项 C）故意侵害身体（battery）侵权不要求证明实际伤害，因此室友失业的证据并不能作为其胜诉的依据。\n\n相反，该证据仅与确定故意侵害身体（battery）侵权成立后的损害赔偿金额有关。\n\n教育目标：若满足以下条件，同意不能作为针对故意侵害身体（battery）（或其他故意侵权行为）的抗辩事由：(1) 原告因对自身权益受侵害的性质或预期伤害程度存在重大误解而表示同意；(2) 被告明知该误解或通过积极的虚假陈述或欺诈行为导致了该误解。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 892B 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（关于基于误解、虚假陈述或胁迫作出的同意）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果出现以下情况，同意（consent）不能作为故意侵害身体（battery）（或其他故意侵权行为）的抗辩理由：（1）原告给予同意是基于对侵犯其利益的性质或预期损害程度的重大误解；以及（2）被告知道这一误解，或者通过积极的虚假陈述或欺诈行为导致了这一误解。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果室友以故意侵害身体（battery）为由起诉这名学生，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"如果出现以下情况，同意（consent）不能作为故意侵害身体（battery）（或其他故意侵权行为）的抗辩理由：（1）原告给予同意是基于对侵犯其利益的性质或预期损害程度的重大误解；以及（2）被告知道这一误解，或者通过积极的虚假陈述或欺诈行为导致了这一误解。","analysis":"当被告意图实施接触（或使原告产生即将遭受接触的恐惧），并导致了对原告的有害或冒犯性接触时，若无抗辩事由，被告即构成侵权（故意侵害身体（battery）/非法接触）。\n\n如果原告的言行表明其愿意接受被告的行为，则实际或明示的同意可作为针对故意侵害身体（battery）（及其他故意侵权行为）的抗辩事由。\n\n然而，若出现以下情况，同意即无效，也不能作为抗辩事由：原告因对自身权益受侵害的性质或预期伤害程度存在重大误解而表示同意，且被告明知该误解或通过积极的虚假陈述或欺诈行为导致了该误解。\n\n在本案中，该学生给室友的头发染上了永久性的亮绿色染料（属于有害接触）。\n\n尽管室友同意让该学生染发，但她误以为所购染料是暂时性的（属于重大误解）。\n\n该学生明知室友存在误解却未予说明。\n\n因此，室友的同意不能构成抗辩事由，且室友很可能在针对该学生的故意侵害身体（battery）侵权诉讼中胜诉（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）尽管该学生未就染料的永久性特征进行虚假陈述，但她明知室友误以为染料是暂时性的。\n\n这足以导致室友的同意无效。\n\n（选项 C）故意侵害身体（battery）侵权不要求证明实际伤害，因此室友失业的证据并不能作为其胜诉的依据。\n\n相反，该证据仅与确定故意侵害身体（battery）侵权成立后的损害赔偿金额有关。\n\n教育目标：若满足以下条件，同意不能作为针对故意侵害身体（battery）（或其他故意侵权行为）的抗辩事由：(1) 原告因对自身权益受侵害的性质或预期伤害程度存在重大误解而表示同意；(2) 被告明知该误解或通过积极的虚假陈述或欺诈行为导致了该误解。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 892B 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（关于基于误解、虚假陈述或胁迫作出的同意）。","conclusion":"答案 D：会，因为这名学生知晓室友的错误。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-ffe0c7d257e17828"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":11,"sourceQuestionPage":30,"sourceQuestionPages":[30],"sourceExplanationPages":[31,32],"sourceAnswerPages":[31,32],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-012","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A tile contractor was hired to install ceiling tiles in a new, multi-story office building. To speed up the installation process, the tile contractor rented scissor lifts from an equipment rental company. A sprinkler-system contractor who was also working on the building owned several identical scissor lifts that he was using to install sprinkler systems in the ceiling of each of the building's floors. The tile contractor mistakenly directed one of his employees to the wrong floor to retrieve the scissor lift from that floor. Consequently, the employee retrieved one of the lifts owned by the sprinkler-system contractor. The tile contractor used the lift for more than two weeks before the sprinkler-system contractor found and regained possession of the lift. The lift was in the same condition as when the tile contractor had taken it. The sprinkler-system contractor sued the tile contractor for trespass to chattels, and the tile contractor defended on the basis that his taking and use of the lift was a reasonable mistake. For whom is the court likely to rule?","choices":{"A":"For the sprinkler-system contractor, because a reasonable mistake is no defense to a trespass-to-chattels action.","B":"For the sprinkler-system contractor, because the tile contractor's mistake was not a mistake of law.","C":"For the tile contractor, because the condition of the lift had not been changed by his actions.","D":"For the tile contractor, because the sprinkler-system contractor's lift was identical to the ones rented by the tile contractor."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Trespass to chattels (ie, tangible personal property) occurs when the defendant intentionally interferes with the plaintiff's possessory rights by either: dispossessing the plaintiff of the chattel or using or intermeddling with the plaintiff's chattel. Only the intent to do the interfering act is necessary. Therefore, the defendant's belief, because of a mistake of law or fact, that he/she was privileged to act is no defense to a trespass-to-chattels action (Choice B). This is true even if the mistake was reasonable. Here, the tile contractor intentionally dispossessed the sprinkler-system contractor of his scissor lift for more than two weeks. The tile contractor's apparent belief that the lift taken was one of the lifts he had rented (mistake of fact) is no defense—no matter how reasonable that mistake may have been given that the lifts were identical (Choice D). For this reason, the court will likely rule for the sprinkler-system contractor. (Choice C) In cases of dispossession (as seen here), the plaintiff can recover for loss of use of the chattel without proof of actual harm (eg, damage to the chattel). Therefore, the tile contractor can be liable to the sprinkler-system contractor even though the tile contractor's actions had not changed the condition of the lift.\n\nEducational objective: Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's tangible personal property through dispossession, use, or intermeddling. The defendant's erroneous belief, because of a mistake of fact or law, that he/she was privileged to do the interfering act is no defense.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 244 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (effect of mistake on trespass to chattels).","educationalObjective":"Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's tangible personal property through dispossession, use, or intermeddling. The defendant's erroneous belief, because of a mistake of fact or law, that he/she was privileged to do the interfering act is no defense.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 244 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (effect of mistake on trespass to chattels).","rule":"Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's tangible personal property through dispossession, use, or intermeddling. The defendant's erroneous belief, because of a mistake of fact or law, that he/she was privileged to do the interfering act is no defense.","irac":{"issue":"For whom is the court likely to rule?","rule":"Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's tangible personal property through dispossession, use, or intermeddling. The defendant's erroneous belief, because of a mistake of fact or law, that he/she was privileged to do the interfering act is no defense.","analysis":"Trespass to chattels (ie, tangible personal property) occurs when the defendant intentionally interferes with the plaintiff's possessory rights by either: dispossessing the plaintiff of the chattel or using or intermeddling with the plaintiff's chattel. Only the intent to do the interfering act is necessary. Therefore, the defendant's belief, because of a mistake of law or fact, that he/she was privileged to act is no defense to a trespass-to-chattels action (Choice B). This is true even if the mistake was reasonable. Here, the tile contractor intentionally dispossessed the sprinkler-system contractor of his scissor lift for more than two weeks. The tile contractor's apparent belief that the lift taken was one of the lifts he had rented (mistake of fact) is no defense—no matter how reasonable that mistake may have been given that the lifts were identical (Choice D). For this reason, the court will likely rule for the sprinkler-system contractor. (Choice C) In cases of dispossession (as seen here), the plaintiff can recover for loss of use of the chattel without proof of actual harm (eg, damage to the chattel). Therefore, the tile contractor can be liable to the sprinkler-system contractor even though the tile contractor's actions had not changed the condition of the lift.","conclusion":"Answer A: For the sprinkler-system contractor, because a reasonable mistake is no defense to a trespass-to-chattels action."},"questionZh":"一家瓷砖承包商受雇在一栋新建的多层办公楼内安装天花板瓷砖。\n\n为了加快安装进度，该承包商向设备租赁公司租用了剪叉式升降机。\n\n另一家负责安装自动喷淋系统的承包商也在该大楼内施工，并拥有几台同样的升降机，用于在各楼层天花板安装喷淋系统。\n\n瓷砖承包商误指派一名员工去错误的楼层取回升降机。\n\n结果，该员工取走了属于喷淋系统承包商的一台升降机。\n\n瓷砖承包商使用了这台升降机两周多，之后喷淋系统承包商才找到并收回了该设备。\n\n归还时，升降机的状况与瓷砖承包商取走时完全相同。\n\n喷淋系统承包商以“侵犯动产”（trespass to chattels）为由起诉瓷砖承包商，而瓷砖承包商则辩称其取用升降机的行为是基于合理的错误认识。\n\n法院很可能会判决哪一方胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"判决喷淋系统承包商胜诉，因为“合理的错误认识”不能作为侵犯动产诉讼的抗辩理由。","B":"判决喷淋系统承包商胜诉，因为瓷砖承包商所犯的错误并非法律上的错误（mistake of law）。","C":"判决瓷砖承包商胜诉，因为他的行为并未改变升降机的状况。","D":"判决瓷砖承包商胜诉，因为喷淋系统承包商的升降机与瓷砖承包商租用的升降机完全相同。"},"explanationZh":"侵犯动产（即有形动产）是指被告故意干扰原告对动产的占有权，具体表现为：剥夺原告对动产的占有，或者使用、干涉原告的动产。\n\n构成该侵权行为仅需具备实施干扰行为的意图。\n\n因此，即便被告因法律或事实上的错误而误以为自己有权实施该行为，也不能作为抗辩理由（选项 B）；即使这种错误是合理的，也不例外。\n\n在本案中，瓷砖承包商故意剥夺了喷淋系统承包商对其剪叉式升降机的占有，时间长达两周多。\n\n尽管这些升降机外观相同，导致瓷砖承包商误以为该升降机是自己租赁的设备（属于事实错误），但这并不能构成抗辩理由，无论这种错误看起来多么合理（选项 D）。\n\n因此，法院很可能会判决喷淋系统承包商胜诉（选项 C）。\n\n在涉及剥夺占有的情形下（如本案），原告无需证明存在实际损害（例如动产本身的损坏），即可就动产使用权的丧失获得赔偿。\n\n因此，即使瓷砖承包商的行为未改变升降机的状况，他仍可能需向喷淋系统承包商承担责任。\n\n教学目标：侵犯动产是指通过剥夺占有、使用或干涉的方式，故意干扰他人有形动产的行为。\n\n被告因事实或法律错误而误以为自己有权实施干扰行为，不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 244 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于错误对侵犯动产行为的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“侵犯动产”是指通过剥夺占有、使用或擅自干预等方式，故意干扰他人有形动产的行为。\n\n即便被告因事实错误或法律错误而误以为自己有权实施该干扰行为，这种错误认识也不能构成抗辩理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院很可能会判决哪一方胜诉？","rule":"“侵犯动产”是指通过剥夺占有、使用或擅自干预等方式，故意干扰他人有形动产的行为。\n\n即便被告因事实错误或法律错误而误以为自己有权实施该干扰行为，这种错误认识也不能构成抗辩理由。","analysis":"侵犯动产（即有形动产）是指被告故意干扰原告对动产的占有权，具体表现为：剥夺原告对动产的占有，或者使用、干涉原告的动产。\n\n构成该侵权行为仅需具备实施干扰行为的意图。\n\n因此，即便被告因法律或事实上的错误而误以为自己有权实施该行为，也不能作为抗辩理由（选项 B）；即使这种错误是合理的，也不例外。\n\n在本案中，瓷砖承包商故意剥夺了喷淋系统承包商对其剪叉式升降机的占有，时间长达两周多。\n\n尽管这些升降机外观相同，导致瓷砖承包商误以为该升降机是自己租赁的设备（属于事实错误），但这并不能构成抗辩理由，无论这种错误看起来多么合理（选项 D）。\n\n因此，法院很可能会判决喷淋系统承包商胜诉（选项 C）。\n\n在涉及剥夺占有的情形下（如本案），原告无需证明存在实际损害（例如动产本身的损坏），即可就动产使用权的丧失获得赔偿。\n\n因此，即使瓷砖承包商的行为未改变升降机的状况，他仍可能需向喷淋系统承包商承担责任。\n\n教学目标：侵犯动产是指通过剥夺占有、使用或干涉的方式，故意干扰他人有形动产的行为。\n\n被告因事实或法律错误而误以为自己有权实施干扰行为，不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 244 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于错误对侵犯动产行为的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 A：判决喷淋系统承包商胜诉，因为“合理的错误认识”不能作为侵犯动产诉讼的抗辩理由。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"employee","ipa":"/ɛmplˈɔɪi/","syllables":"em·ployee","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 职员, 员工, 受雇人员；[化] 职工； 雇员","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“employee”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:employees","sourceSentence":"The tile contractor mistakenly directed one of his employees to the wrong floor to retrieve the scissor lift from that floor.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-1a2e161361d6c9d8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":12,"sourceQuestionPage":33,"sourceQuestionPages":[33],"sourceExplanationPages":[34,35],"sourceAnswerPages":[34,35],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-013","subject":"Torts","topic":"Privileges & Defenses","question":"A restaurant building was expanded into a vacant lot north of its original location. The contractor who was working on the restaurant expansion found that the north wall of the enlarged structure needed extensive support, so anchor rods and concrete were added. The supporting anchor rods and concrete extended into a neighboring property farther to the north at a point 20 feet below the surface. Although the anchor rods and concrete did not impact the surface of his land or its existing uses, the owner of the neighboring property has sued the restaurant owner for trespass. Which party is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The neighboring property owner, because he should have been given notice of the intrusion before it occurred.","B":"The neighboring property owner, because the restaurant intruded upon his property without his permission.","C":"The restaurant owner, because the decision to provide the additional support was reasonable.","D":"The restaurant owner, because the anchor rods and concrete did not disturb the neighboring property owner's peaceful enjoyment of his land."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Trespass is the intentional entry onto another's property without permission. The entry can occur on, beneath, or above the property. Here, the restaurant installed supporting anchor rods and concrete that entered the neighboring property 20 feet below the surface. Since the neighboring property owner did not consent to the sub-surface entry, he will likely prevail on his trespass claim. (Choice A) Even if the neighboring property owner had been given prior notice, he would prevail on his trespass claim if he did not consent to the intrusion. (Choice C) The reasonableness of the decision to provide additional support may show that the restaurant owner was not negligent—eg, he used reasonable care to prevent injury to the adjacent landowner. But reasonableness is irrelevant to trespass, which requires intentional conduct. (Choice D) Disturbance of peaceful enjoyment of the neighboring property is relevant to an action for private nuisance (a substantial and unreasonable interference with another's use or enjoyment of land)—not trespass.\n\nEducational objective: Trespass (an intentional entry onto another's property without permission) can occur on, beneath, or above the surface.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst.1965) (intentional trespass).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 159 (Am. Law Inst.1965) (trespass on, beneath, and above the surface).","educationalObjective":"Trespass (an intentional entry onto another's property without permission) can occur on, beneath, or above the surface.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst.1965) (intentional trespass).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 159 (Am. Law Inst.1965) (trespass on, beneath, and above the surface).","rule":"Trespass (an intentional entry onto another's property without permission) can occur on, beneath, or above the surface.","irac":{"issue":"Which party is likely to prevail?","rule":"Trespass (an intentional entry onto another's property without permission) can occur on, beneath, or above the surface.","analysis":"Trespass is the intentional entry onto another's property without permission. The entry can occur on, beneath, or above the property. Here, the restaurant installed supporting anchor rods and concrete that entered the neighboring property 20 feet below the surface. Since the neighboring property owner did not consent to the sub-surface entry, he will likely prevail on his trespass claim. (Choice A) Even if the neighboring property owner had been given prior notice, he would prevail on his trespass claim if he did not consent to the intrusion. (Choice C) The reasonableness of the decision to provide additional support may show that the restaurant owner was not negligent—eg, he used reasonable care to prevent injury to the adjacent landowner. But reasonableness is irrelevant to trespass, which requires intentional conduct. (Choice D) Disturbance of peaceful enjoyment of the neighboring property is relevant to an action for private nuisance (a substantial and unreasonable interference with another's use or enjoyment of land)—not trespass.","conclusion":"Answer B: The neighboring property owner, because the restaurant intruded upon his property without his permission."},"questionZh":"一家餐厅扩建至原址以北的一块空地上。\n\n负责餐厅扩建的承包商发现扩建后建筑的北墙需要大量支撑，因此加装了锚杆和混凝土。\n\n这些支撑锚杆和混凝土延伸至更北侧的邻近地块，深度达地表以下20英尺（约6米）。\n\n尽管这些锚杆和混凝土并未影响邻近地块的地表或其现有用途，但邻近地块的所有者仍以侵权为由起诉了餐厅老板。\n\n哪一方更有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"邻近地块的所有者，因为他本应在侵权发生前被告知此事。","B":"邻近地块的所有者，因为餐厅未经许可侵占了他的土地。","C":"餐厅老板，因为提供额外支撑的决定是合理的。","D":"餐厅老板，因为这些锚杆和混凝土并未干扰邻近地块所有者对其土地的正常使用。"},"explanationZh":"侵入（Trespass）是指未经许可故意进入他人财产范围的行为。\n\n这种进入可以发生在财产的地面、地下或上空。\n\n在本案中，该餐厅安装了支撑锚杆和混凝土结构，这些结构伸入了邻近物业地表以下20英尺处。\n\n由于邻近物业的所有者未同意这种进入地下的行为，他很可能会在侵入索赔诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）即使邻近物业所有者事先收到了通知，只要他未同意这种侵入行为，他仍能在侵入索赔诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）决定提供额外支撑的合理性或许能证明餐厅业主没有过失——例如，他已尽到合理注意义务以防止邻近土地所有者受到伤害。\n\n但合理性与侵入行为无关，因为侵入行为的构成要件是“故意”行为。\n\n（选项 D）干扰邻近物业的安宁享用属于私人妨害（private nuisance，即对他人土地使用或享用的实质性且不合理的干扰）诉讼的范畴，而非侵入诉讼。\n\n教育目标：侵入（即未经许可故意进入他人财产）可发生于财产的地面、地下或上空。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第158条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（故意侵入）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第159条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（在地面、地下及上空的侵入）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"非法侵入（未经许可故意进入他人财产）可能发生在地面上、地面下或地面以上。","iracZh":{"issue":"哪一方更有可能胜诉？","rule":"非法侵入（未经许可故意进入他人财产）可能发生在地面上、地面下或地面以上。","analysis":"侵入（Trespass）是指未经许可故意进入他人财产范围的行为。\n\n这种进入可以发生在财产的地面、地下或上空。\n\n在本案中，该餐厅安装了支撑锚杆和混凝土结构，这些结构伸入了邻近物业地表以下20英尺处。\n\n由于邻近物业的所有者未同意这种进入地下的行为，他很可能会在侵入索赔诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）即使邻近物业所有者事先收到了通知，只要他未同意这种侵入行为，他仍能在侵入索赔诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）决定提供额外支撑的合理性或许能证明餐厅业主没有过失——例如，他已尽到合理注意义务以防止邻近土地所有者受到伤害。\n\n但合理性与侵入行为无关，因为侵入行为的构成要件是“故意”行为。\n\n（选项 D）干扰邻近物业的安宁享用属于私人妨害（private nuisance，即对他人土地使用或享用的实质性且不合理的干扰）诉讼的范畴，而非侵入诉讼。\n\n教育目标：侵入（即未经许可故意进入他人财产）可发生于财产的地面、地下或上空。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第158条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（故意侵入）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第159条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（在地面、地下及上空的侵入）。","conclusion":"答案 B：邻近地块的所有者，因为餐厅未经许可侵占了他的土地。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"private nuisance","ipa":"/pɹˈaɪvət nˈusəns/","syllables":"pri·vate nui·sance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"私人妨害：对他人土地使用与享受造成重大且不合理干扰","contextualMeaning":"私人妨害：对他人土地使用与享受造成重大且不合理干扰","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) Disturbance of peaceful enjoyment of the neighboring property is relevant to an action for private nuisance (a substantial and unreasonable interference with another's use or enjoyment of land)—not trespass.","synonyms":["private interference with land use"]},{"term":"reasonableness","ipa":"/ɹˈizʌnʌbˌəlnəs/","syllables":"rea·son·able·ness","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[计] 合理性","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“reasonableness”","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) The reasonableness of the decision to provide additional support may show that the restaurant owner was not negligent—eg, he used reasonable care to prevent injury to the adjacent landowner.","synonyms":["tenability","tenableness","moderateness","modestness"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-af40f7a973958150"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":13,"sourceQuestionPage":36,"sourceQuestionPages":[36],"sourceExplanationPages":[37,38],"sourceAnswerPages":[37,38],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-014","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A customer pledged a stock certificate to a bank as security for a loan. Under its loan agreement with the customer, the bank was obligated to return the certificate once the loan was repaid. A year later, when the customer fully repaid the loan, the bank refused the customer's demand to return the certificate because the officer dealing with the loan had the mistaken belief that there was still a balance due. No one at the bank reviewed the records until two months later, at which time the error was discovered. The bank then offered to return the certificate, but the customer refused to accept it. At the time the customer pledged the certificate, the shares were worth $10,000; at the time the customer repaid the loan, the shares were worth $20,000; and at the time the bank offered to return the certificate, the shares were worth $5,000. If the customer brings an action against the bank based on conversion, how much, if anything, should the customer recover?","choices":{"A":"Nothing, because the bank lawfully came into possession of the certificate.","B":"$5,000, because that was the value of the shares when the customer refused to accept the certificate back.","C":"$10,000, because that was the value of the shares when the bank came into possession of the certificate.","D":"$20,000, because that was the value of the shares when the customer was entitled to the return of the certificate."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Conversion (an intentional and substantial interference with another's right to control a chattel) occurs when a defendant refuses to return a chattel upon demand to a plaintiff who is entitled to its immediate possession. In the event of a conversion, the plaintiff can recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion. Here, conversion occurred when the bank refused to return the customer's stock certificate even though the customer had fully repaid the loan and was entitled to immediate possession of the certificate pursuant to the loan agreement. Accordingly, the customer should recover the fair market value of the shares when the bank refused to return the certificate in response to the customer's rightful demand (the time of conversion)—$20,000. (Choice A) A defendant who refuses to return the plaintiff's chattel may be liable for conversion even if the defendant initially acquired the chattel lawfully. Therefore, though the bank lawfully obtained the stock certificate as security for the customer's loan, it committed conversion by refusing to return the certificate when the customer was entitled to immediate possession. (Choices B & C) The customer should recover the fair market value of the certificate at the time of the conversion—not when the bank first came into possession of the certificate or when the customer refused to accept it back two months later.\n\nEducational objective: Conversion occurs when a defendant refuses to return a chattel upon demand to a plaintiff entitled to its immediate possession, after which the plaintiff can recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 222A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of conversion).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 237 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (conversion by demand and refusal).","educationalObjective":"Conversion occurs when a defendant refuses to return a chattel upon demand to a plaintiff entitled to its immediate possession, after which the plaintiff can recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 222A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of conversion).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 237 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (conversion by demand and refusal).","rule":"Conversion occurs when a defendant refuses to return a chattel upon demand to a plaintiff entitled to its immediate possession, after which the plaintiff can recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion.","irac":{"issue":"If the customer brings an action against the bank based on conversion, how much, if anything, should the customer recover?","rule":"Conversion occurs when a defendant refuses to return a chattel upon demand to a plaintiff entitled to its immediate possession, after which the plaintiff can recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion.","analysis":"Conversion (an intentional and substantial interference with another's right to control a chattel) occurs when a defendant refuses to return a chattel upon demand to a plaintiff who is entitled to its immediate possession. In the event of a conversion, the plaintiff can recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion. Here, conversion occurred when the bank refused to return the customer's stock certificate even though the customer had fully repaid the loan and was entitled to immediate possession of the certificate pursuant to the loan agreement. Accordingly, the customer should recover the fair market value of the shares when the bank refused to return the certificate in response to the customer's rightful demand (the time of conversion)—$20,000. (Choice A) A defendant who refuses to return the plaintiff's chattel may be liable for conversion even if the defendant initially acquired the chattel lawfully. Therefore, though the bank lawfully obtained the stock certificate as security for the customer's loan, it committed conversion by refusing to return the certificate when the customer was entitled to immediate possession. (Choices B & C) The customer should recover the fair market value of the certificate at the time of the conversion—not when the bank first came into possession of the certificate or when the customer refused to accept it back two months later.","conclusion":"Answer D: $20,000, because that was the value of the shares when the customer was entitled to the return of the certificate."},"questionZh":"一位客户将股票证书抵押给银行作为贷款担保。\n\n根据与客户的贷款协议，银行有义务在贷款偿还后归还该股票证书。\n\n一年后，当客户全额偿还贷款时，银行拒绝了客户的归还要求，因为负责该贷款的职员误以为客户仍有未偿还的余额。\n\n两个月后，银行才有人审查记录，并发现了这一错误。\n\n银行随后提出归还股票证书，但客户拒绝接受。\n\n客户抵押股票证书时，股票价值10,000美元；客户偿还贷款时，股票价值20,000美元；银行提出归还股票证书时，股票价值5,000美元。\n\n如果客户以侵占为由对银行提起诉讼，客户应获得多少赔偿（如有）？","choicesZh":{"A":"无法获得任何赔偿，因为银行合法占有了该股票证书。","B":"5,000 美元，因为这是客户拒绝接受股票证书时股票的价值。","C":"10,000 美元，因为这是银行取得股票证书时股票的价值。","D":"20,000 美元，因为这是客户有权要求返还股票证书时股票的价值。"},"explanationZh":"侵占（conversion，指对他人控制动产的权利进行蓄意且实质性的干涉）发生于被告拒绝向有权立即占有某动产的原告归还该动产（经原告要求）之时。\n\n一旦发生侵占，原告有权获得该动产在侵占发生时的公平市场价值。\n\n在本案中，尽管客户已全额偿还贷款并根据贷款协议有权立即占有股票证书，但银行拒绝归还该证书，从而构成了侵占。\n\n因此，客户应获得该股票在银行拒绝归还证书（即侵占发生时）的公平市场价值——即20,000美元。\n\n（选项A）即使被告最初是合法取得某动产，若其拒绝归还原告的动产，仍可能构成侵占并承担责任。\n\n因此，尽管银行最初合法取得了作为贷款担保的股票证书，但在客户有权立即占有该证书时，银行拒绝归还的行为构成了侵占。\n\n（选项B与C）客户应获得该证书在侵占发生时的公平市场价值，而非银行最初占有该证书时或客户在两个月后拒绝收回该证书时的价值。\n\n教学目标：当被告拒绝向有权立即占有某动产的原告归还该动产（经原告要求）时，即构成侵占；此时，原告有权获得该动产在侵占发生时的公平市场价值。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第222A条（美国法律协会，1965年）（侵占的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第237条（美国法律协会，1965年）（因要求归还遭拒而构成的侵占）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"侵占是指被告拒绝应原告要求返还其有权立即占有的动产，之后原告可以收回该动产在侵占发生时的公平市场价值。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果客户以侵占为由对银行提起诉讼，客户应获得多少赔偿（如有）？","rule":"侵占是指被告拒绝应原告要求返还其有权立即占有的动产，之后原告可以收回该动产在侵占发生时的公平市场价值。","analysis":"侵占（conversion，指对他人控制动产的权利进行蓄意且实质性的干涉）发生于被告拒绝向有权立即占有某动产的原告归还该动产（经原告要求）之时。\n\n一旦发生侵占，原告有权获得该动产在侵占发生时的公平市场价值。\n\n在本案中，尽管客户已全额偿还贷款并根据贷款协议有权立即占有股票证书，但银行拒绝归还该证书，从而构成了侵占。\n\n因此，客户应获得该股票在银行拒绝归还证书（即侵占发生时）的公平市场价值——即20,000美元。\n\n（选项A）即使被告最初是合法取得某动产，若其拒绝归还原告的动产，仍可能构成侵占并承担责任。\n\n因此，尽管银行最初合法取得了作为贷款担保的股票证书，但在客户有权立即占有该证书时，银行拒绝归还的行为构成了侵占。\n\n（选项B与C）客户应获得该证书在侵占发生时的公平市场价值，而非银行最初占有该证书时或客户在两个月后拒绝收回该证书时的价值。\n\n教学目标：当被告拒绝向有权立即占有某动产的原告归还该动产（经原告要求）时，即构成侵占；此时，原告有权获得该动产在侵占发生时的公平市场价值。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第222A条（美国法律协会，1965年）（侵占的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第237条（美国法律协会，1965年）（因要求归还遭拒而构成的侵占）。","conclusion":"答案 D：20,000 美元，因为这是客户有权要求返还股票证书时股票的价值。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"conversion","ipa":"/kʌnvˈɝʒən/","syllables":"con·ver·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 转变, 转换, 改变宗教信仰, 换位法；[计] 转换","contextualMeaning":"侵占：严重干涉他人动产权利，通常按财产全部价值赔偿","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:conversions","sourceSentence":"If the customer brings an action against the bank based on conversion, how much, if anything, should the customer recover?","synonyms":["serious interference with chattel"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-8ca70e5c338a0cee"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":14,"sourceQuestionPage":39,"sourceQuestionPages":[39],"sourceExplanationPages":[40,41],"sourceAnswerPages":[40,41],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-015","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"While throwing warm-up pitches on the sidelines during a professional baseball game, a pitcher was continuously heckled by some spectators seated behind a wire mesh fence in the stands above the dugout. On several occasions, the pitcher turned and looked directly at the hecklers with a scowl on his face, but the heckling continued. The pitcher then wound up as though he was preparing to pitch in the direction of his catcher, but threw a fastball toward the hecklers instead. The ball passed through the wire mesh fence and struck a woman who was one of the hecklers. The woman brought an action against the pitcher and the team to recover damages for negligence and battery. The trial court entered judgment as a matter of law in favor of the defendants on the battery claim. The jury found for the defendants on the negligence claim because the jury determined that the pitcher could not foresee that the ball would pass through the wire mesh fence. The woman has appealed the judgment on the battery claim, contending that the trial court erred in entering judgment as a matter of law in favor of the defendants. On appeal, should the court affirm the judgment as a matter of law entered in favor of the pitcher on the battery claim?","choices":{"A":"No, because a jury could find that the pitcher's conduct was extreme and outrageous, and the cause of the woman's physical harm.","B":"No, if a jury could find on the evidence that the pitcher intended to cause the hecklers to fear being hit.","C":"Yes, because the jury found that the pitcher could not foresee that the ball would pass through the fence.","D":"Yes, if a jury could find on the evidence that the pitcher was mentally ill and that his act was the product of his mental illness."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A defendant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (ie, directed verdict) if the evidence presented at trial is not legally sufficient to support every element of the plaintiff's claim (ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor). Therefore, on appeal, the court should affirm the trial court's judgment on the battery claim if no reasonable jury could find that the defendant: intended to inflict harmful/offensive contact or imminent apprehension thereof and caused harmful/offensive contact with the plaintiff. Here, however, a reasonable jury could find on the evidence that the woman was struck by a baseball thrown by the pitcher (harmful contact). If a reasonable jury could also find that the pitcher threw the baseball with the intent to hit the hecklers (contact) or cause them to fear being hit (imminent apprehension), then the appellate court should reverse (ie, not affirm) the judgment as a matter of law and remand the case for a jury to decide whether a battery occurred. (Choice A) For battery, a jury does not need to find that the defendant's conduct was extreme and outrageous—only that the conduct was intentional. Therefore, even if a reasonable jury could find that the pitcher's conduct caused the woman's harm, the appellate court should reverse the judgment only if the jury could also find that the pitcher's conduct was intentional. (Choice C) Foreseeability is not an element for battery. Therefore, the jury's finding that the pitcher could not foresee that the ball would pass through the fence has no effect on the woman's battery claim. (Choice D) Although mental deficiency is considered when determining whether the defendant was capable of forming the intent required for battery, it is not a defense to battery. Therefore, the appellate court should not affirm the lower court's judgment merely because the jury could find that the pitcher was mentally ill and that his act was the product of his mental illness.\n\nEducational objective: For battery, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant (1) intended to inflict harmful/offensive contact or imminent apprehension thereof and (2) caused such contact with the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nFed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (judgment as a matter of law).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).","educationalObjective":"For battery, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant (1) intended to inflict harmful/offensive contact or imminent apprehension thereof and (2) caused such contact with the plaintiff.","references":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (judgment as a matter of law).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).","rule":"For battery, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant (1) intended to inflict harmful/offensive contact or imminent apprehension thereof and (2) caused such contact with the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"On appeal, should the court affirm the judgment as a matter of law entered in favor of the pitcher on the battery claim?","rule":"For battery, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant (1) intended to inflict harmful/offensive contact or imminent apprehension thereof and (2) caused such contact with the plaintiff.","analysis":"A defendant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (ie, directed verdict) if the evidence presented at trial is not legally sufficient to support every element of the plaintiff's claim (ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor). Therefore, on appeal, the court should affirm the trial court's judgment on the battery claim if no reasonable jury could find that the defendant: intended to inflict harmful/offensive contact or imminent apprehension thereof and caused harmful/offensive contact with the plaintiff. Here, however, a reasonable jury could find on the evidence that the woman was struck by a baseball thrown by the pitcher (harmful contact). If a reasonable jury could also find that the pitcher threw the baseball with the intent to hit the hecklers (contact) or cause them to fear being hit (imminent apprehension), then the appellate court should reverse (ie, not affirm) the judgment as a matter of law and remand the case for a jury to decide whether a battery occurred. (Choice A) For battery, a jury does not need to find that the defendant's conduct was extreme and outrageous—only that the conduct was intentional. Therefore, even if a reasonable jury could find that the pitcher's conduct caused the woman's harm, the appellate court should reverse the judgment only if the jury could also find that the pitcher's conduct was intentional. (Choice C) Foreseeability is not an element for battery. Therefore, the jury's finding that the pitcher could not foresee that the ball would pass through the fence has no effect on the woman's battery claim. (Choice D) Although mental deficiency is considered when determining whether the defendant was capable of forming the intent required for battery, it is not a defense to battery. Therefore, the appellate court should not affirm the lower court's judgment merely because the jury could find that the pitcher was mentally ill and that his act was the product of his mental illness.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, if a jury could find on the evidence that the pitcher intended to cause the hecklers to fear being hit."},"questionZh":"在一场职业棒球比赛中，一名投手在场边进行热身投球时，一些坐在球员休息区上方看台铁丝网围栏后的观众不断对他进行嘲讽。\n\n投手多次转身怒视这些嘲讽者，但嘲讽声依然持续。\n\n随后，投手摆出投球姿势，似乎要向捕手方向投球，但却投出了一个快速球，球径直飞向了那些嘲讽者。\n\n球穿过铁丝网，击中了一名正在嘲讽的女子。\n\n该女子随后起诉投手和球队，要求赔偿过失和人身伤害损失。\n\n初审法院依法判决被告人身伤害诉讼请求成立。\n\n陪审团也裁定被告人过失诉讼请求成立，因为陪审团认定投手无法预见球会穿过铁丝网。\n\n该女子已就故意侵害身体（battery）指控的判决提出上诉，认为初审法院在法律上错误地作出了有利于被告的判决。\n\n在上诉中，法院是否应维持原判，即在故意侵害身体（battery）指控中作出的有利于投手的判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为陪审团可以认定投手的行为极端且令人发指，并且是造成该女子身体伤害的原因。","B":"否，如果陪审团能够根据证据认定投手意图使起哄者害怕被击中。","C":"是，因为陪审团认定投手无法预见球会穿过围栏。","D":"是，如果陪审团能够根据证据认定投手患有精神疾病，并且他的行为是精神疾病的产物。"},"explanationZh":"如果庭审中提出的证据在法律上不足以支持原告主张的每一个构成要件（即没有理性的陪审团会做出有利于原告的裁决），被告即有权获得“法律上的判决”（即指示裁决/directed verdict）。\n\n因此，在上诉审中，如果没有任何理性的陪审团能认定被告具有实施有害/冒犯性接触或引起对此类接触之即时恐惧的意图，并导致了针对原告的有害/冒犯性接触，那么法院就应维持初审法院关于故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）指控的判决。\n\n然而在本案中，基于现有证据，理性的陪审团完全可能认定该女子被投手投出的棒球击中（即构成了有害接触）。\n\n如果理性的陪审团同时能认定投手投球的意图是为了击中那些起哄者（即接触）或使他们担心被击中（即即时恐惧），那么上诉法院就应当推翻（即不予维持）该“法律上的判决”，并将案件发回重审，由陪审团决定是否构成了故意侵害身体（battery）。\n\n（选项 A）对于故意侵害身体（battery）指控，陪审团无需认定被告的行为属于极端恶劣或令人发指的行为——只需认定该行为是故意的即可。\n\n因此，即使理性的陪审团认定投手的行为导致了该女子的伤害，上诉法院也只有在陪审团同时认定投手的行为是故意的情况下，才应推翻原判。\n\n（选项 C）可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）并非故意侵害身体（battery）指控的构成要件。\n\n因此，即便陪审团认定投手无法预见球会穿过围栏，这也不会影响该女子提出的故意侵害身体（battery）指控。\n\n（选项 D）尽管在判定被告是否有能力形成故意侵害身体（battery）所需的意图时会考虑其精神缺陷，但这并不能作为故意侵害身体（battery）指控的抗辩理由。\n\n因此，上诉法院不应仅仅因为陪审团认定投手患有精神疾病且其行为是该疾病的产物，就维持下级法院的判决。\n\n教学目标：对于故意侵害身体（battery）指控，原告必须证明被告 (1) 具有实施有害/冒犯性接触或引起对此类接触之即时恐惧的意图，以及 (2) 导致了针对原告的此类接触。\n\n参考文献 《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 50(a) 条（法律上的判决）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）》第1条（美国法律学会，2010年）（关于“意图”的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第13条（美国法律学会，1965年）（因有害接触而构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"就故意侵害身体（battery）罪而言，原告必须证明被告 (1) 意图造成有害/冒犯性的接触或使原告感到即将遭受此类接触，并且 (2) 实施了与原告的此类接触。","iracZh":{"issue":"在上诉中，法院是否应依法维持有利于投手的故意侵害身体（battery）罪判决？","rule":"就故意侵害身体（battery）罪而言，原告必须证明被告 (1) 意图造成有害/冒犯性的接触或使原告感到即将遭受此类接触，并且 (2) 实施了与原告的此类接触。","analysis":"如果庭审中提出的证据在法律上不足以支持原告主张的每一个构成要件（即没有理性的陪审团会做出有利于原告的裁决），被告即有权获得“法律上的判决”（即指示裁决/directed verdict）。\n\n因此，在上诉审中，如果没有任何理性的陪审团能认定被告具有实施有害/冒犯性接触或引起对此类接触之即时恐惧的意图，并导致了针对原告的有害/冒犯性接触，那么法院就应维持初审法院关于故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）指控的判决。\n\n然而在本案中，基于现有证据，理性的陪审团完全可能认定该女子被投手投出的棒球击中（即构成了有害接触）。\n\n如果理性的陪审团同时能认定投手投球的意图是为了击中那些起哄者（即接触）或使他们担心被击中（即即时恐惧），那么上诉法院就应当推翻（即不予维持）该“法律上的判决”，并将案件发回重审，由陪审团决定是否构成了故意侵害身体（battery）。\n\n（选项 A）对于故意侵害身体（battery）指控，陪审团无需认定被告的行为属于极端恶劣或令人发指的行为——只需认定该行为是故意的即可。\n\n因此，即使理性的陪审团认定投手的行为导致了该女子的伤害，上诉法院也只有在陪审团同时认定投手的行为是故意的情况下，才应推翻原判。\n\n（选项 C）可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）并非故意侵害身体（battery）指控的构成要件。\n\n因此，即便陪审团认定投手无法预见球会穿过围栏，这也不会影响该女子提出的故意侵害身体（battery）指控。\n\n（选项 D）尽管在判定被告是否有能力形成故意侵害身体（battery）所需的意图时会考虑其精神缺陷，但这并不能作为故意侵害身体（battery）指控的抗辩理由。\n\n因此，上诉法院不应仅仅因为陪审团认定投手患有精神疾病且其行为是该疾病的产物，就维持下级法院的判决。\n\n教学目标：对于故意侵害身体（battery）指控，原告必须证明被告 (1) 具有实施有害/冒犯性接触或引起对此类接触之即时恐惧的意图，以及 (2) 导致了针对原告的此类接触。\n\n参考文献 《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 50(a) 条（法律上的判决）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）》第1条（美国法律学会，2010年）（关于“意图”的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第13条（美国法律学会，1965年）（因有害接触而构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。","conclusion":"答案 B：否，如果陪审团能够根据证据认定投手意图使起哄者害怕被击中。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"product","ipa":"/pɹˈɑdəkt/","syllables":"prod·uct","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 产品, 结果, 乘积；[化] 生产物","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“product”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:products","sourceSentence":"Yes, if a jury could find on the evidence that the pitcher was mentally ill and that his act was the product of his mental illness.","synonyms":["merchandise","ware","production","mathematical product"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-3b664c20289fd466"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":15,"sourceQuestionPage":42,"sourceQuestionPages":[42],"sourceExplanationPages":[43,44,45],"sourceAnswerPages":[43,44,45],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-016","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A disgruntled former employee of a corporation posted false job listings online for his former position. The false job listings directed applicants to email multiple documents, including a resume, to the employee's former manager. The employee intended to generate enough email responses to impede performance of the corporation's computer system. However, the employee accidentally posted an email address belonging to a small business owner. As a result, the owner received hundreds of emails from job applicants. This made it hard for the owner to find emails relevant to her business but caused no functional disruption to her computer system. Upon discovering that the employee had posted the false job listings, the owner filed suit against the employee for trespass to chattels. Should the owner prevail in her lawsuit?","choices":{"A":"No, because the employee intended to interfere only with the corporation's computer system.","B":"No, because the owner cannot show that the employee's interference caused her actual damages.","C":"Yes, because the emails constituted an intentional invasion of another's property without permission or legal privilege.","D":"Yes, because the employee intentionally used or intermeddled with the owner's computer system."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Trespass to chattels (ie, tangible personal property) occurs when an actor intentionally interferes with another's right of possession by either: dispossessing the other person of the chattel or using or intermeddling with the other person's chattel. And under the doctrine of transferred intent, an actor's intent to commit trespass to chattels against one person will transfer to the actor's commission of the tort against a different person.* However, in the absence of actual damages (eg, actual harm to chattels), an action for trespass to chattels based on use or intermeddling will fail. Here, the employee intended to intermeddle with the corporation's computer system by having email responses to the false job listings sent to the employee's former manager. This intent to commit a trespass to chattels then transferred to the owner when the employee accidentally directed the emails to the owner instead (Choice A). But since the emails caused no functional disruption to the owner's computer system, the owner cannot show that the employee's interference caused her actual damages. Therefore, the owner should not prevail (Choice D). *Transferred intent applies if an actor intends to commit an intentional tort against one person but commits a different intentional tort against the same person, the same intentional tort against a different person, or a different intentional tort against a different person. (Choice C) A trespass to land (ie, real property) is defined as an intentional invasion of another's property without permission or legal privilege. But here, the owner is suing for trespass to chattels (ie, tangible personal property).\n\nEducational objective: Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's tangible personal property through dispossession, use, or intermeddling. The intent to commit this tort against one person will transfer to the commission of the tort against another person.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 217 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (ways of committing trespass to chattels).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 110 (Am. Law Inst. 2015) (transferred intent).","educationalObjective":"Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's tangible personal property through dispossession, use, or intermeddling. The intent to commit this tort against one person will transfer to the commission of the tort against another person.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 217 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (ways of committing trespass to chattels).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 110 (Am. Law Inst. 2015) (transferred intent).","rule":"Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's tangible personal property through dispossession, use, or intermeddling. The intent to commit this tort against one person will transfer to the commission of the tort against another person.","irac":{"issue":"Should the owner prevail in her lawsuit?","rule":"Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's tangible personal property through dispossession, use, or intermeddling. The intent to commit this tort against one person will transfer to the commission of the tort against another person.","analysis":"Trespass to chattels (ie, tangible personal property) occurs when an actor intentionally interferes with another's right of possession by either: dispossessing the other person of the chattel or using or intermeddling with the other person's chattel. And under the doctrine of transferred intent, an actor's intent to commit trespass to chattels against one person will transfer to the actor's commission of the tort against a different person.* However, in the absence of actual damages (eg, actual harm to chattels), an action for trespass to chattels based on use or intermeddling will fail. Here, the employee intended to intermeddle with the corporation's computer system by having email responses to the false job listings sent to the employee's former manager. This intent to commit a trespass to chattels then transferred to the owner when the employee accidentally directed the emails to the owner instead (Choice A). But since the emails caused no functional disruption to the owner's computer system, the owner cannot show that the employee's interference caused her actual damages. Therefore, the owner should not prevail (Choice D). *Transferred intent applies if an actor intends to commit an intentional tort against one person but commits a different intentional tort against the same person, the same intentional tort against a different person, or a different intentional tort against a different person. (Choice C) A trespass to land (ie, real property) is defined as an intentional invasion of another's property without permission or legal privilege. But here, the owner is suing for trespass to chattels (ie, tangible personal property).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the owner cannot show that the employee's interference caused her actual damages."},"questionZh":"某公司的一名前员工因心怀不满，在网上发布了针对其原职位的虚假招聘信息。\n\n这些虚假招聘信息指示求职者将包括简历在内的多份文件通过电子邮件发送给该员工的前任主管。\n\n该员工意图通过制造大量电子邮件回复来阻碍该公司计算机系统的正常运行。\n\n然而，该员工不慎发布了一位小企业主的电子邮件地址。\n\n结果，该企业主收到了数百封来自求职者的电子邮件。\n\n这导致该企业主难以找到与其业务相关的邮件，但并未对其计算机系统造成功能性损害。\n\n在发现该员工发布了虚假招聘信息后，该企业主以“侵犯动产”（trespass to chattels）为由对该员工提起诉讼。\n\n该企业主能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为该员工的意图仅在于干扰该公司的计算机系统。","B":"不能，因为该企业主无法证明该员工的干扰行为对其造成了实际损害。","C":"能，因为这些电子邮件构成了在未经许可或无法律特权的情况下对他人的财产进行的故意侵犯。","D":"能，因为该员工故意使用或干涉了该企业主的计算机系统。"},"explanationZh":"侵犯动产（即有形动产）是指行为人故意干扰他人占有权的行为，具体表现为：剥夺他人对动产的占有，或者使用、干涉他人的动产。\n\n根据“意图转移”原则，行为人针对某人实施侵犯动产行为的意图，可转移至其针对另一人实施该侵权行为的情形。* 然而，若无实际损害（例如动产遭受实际损坏），则基于“使用”或“干涉”而提起的侵犯动产之诉将无法成立。\n\n在本案中，该员工意图通过将针对虚假招聘信息的电子邮件回复发送给其前任经理，来干涉公司的计算机系统。\n\n当该员工不慎将邮件误发给所有者时，其侵犯动产的意图便转移到了针对所有者的行为上（选项 A）。\n\n但由于这些邮件未对所有者的计算机系统造成功能性干扰，所有者无法证明员工的干涉行为对其造成了实际损害。\n\n因此，所有者的诉讼请求不应获得支持（选项 D）。\n\n* “意图转移”原则适用于以下情形：行为人意图针对某人实施某种故意侵权行为，但最终针对同一人实施了另一种故意侵权行为，或针对另一人实施了同一种故意侵权行为，抑或针对另一人实施了另一种故意侵权行为。\n\n（选项 C）侵犯土地（即不动产）是指未经许可或无法律特权而故意侵入他人财产的行为。\n\n但本案中，所有者提起的是侵犯动产（即有形动产）之诉。\n\n教育目标：侵犯动产是指通过剥夺占有、使用或干涉的方式，故意干扰他人有形动产的行为。\n\n针对某人实施该侵权行为的意图，可转移至针对另一人实施该侵权行为的情形。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 217 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（侵犯动产的实施方式）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第 110 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2015)（意图转移）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“侵犯动产”是指通过剥夺占有、使用或干涉的方式，故意干扰他人有形动产的行为。\n\n针对某人实施该侵权行为的意图，可转移至针对另一人实施该侵权行为的情形中。","iracZh":{"issue":"该企业主能否胜诉？","rule":"“侵犯动产”是指通过剥夺占有、使用或干涉的方式，故意干扰他人有形动产的行为。\n\n针对某人实施该侵权行为的意图，可转移至针对另一人实施该侵权行为的情形中。","analysis":"侵犯动产（即有形动产）是指行为人故意干扰他人占有权的行为，具体表现为：剥夺他人对动产的占有，或者使用、干涉他人的动产。\n\n根据“意图转移”原则，行为人针对某人实施侵犯动产行为的意图，可转移至其针对另一人实施该侵权行为的情形。* 然而，若无实际损害（例如动产遭受实际损坏），则基于“使用”或“干涉”而提起的侵犯动产之诉将无法成立。\n\n在本案中，该员工意图通过将针对虚假招聘信息的电子邮件回复发送给其前任经理，来干涉公司的计算机系统。\n\n当该员工不慎将邮件误发给所有者时，其侵犯动产的意图便转移到了针对所有者的行为上（选项 A）。\n\n但由于这些邮件未对所有者的计算机系统造成功能性干扰，所有者无法证明员工的干涉行为对其造成了实际损害。\n\n因此，所有者的诉讼请求不应获得支持（选项 D）。\n\n* “意图转移”原则适用于以下情形：行为人意图针对某人实施某种故意侵权行为，但最终针对同一人实施了另一种故意侵权行为，或针对另一人实施了同一种故意侵权行为，抑或针对另一人实施了另一种故意侵权行为。\n\n（选项 C）侵犯土地（即不动产）是指未经许可或无法律特权而故意侵入他人财产的行为。\n\n但本案中，所有者提起的是侵犯动产（即有形动产）之诉。\n\n教育目标：侵犯动产是指通过剥夺占有、使用或干涉的方式，故意干扰他人有形动产的行为。\n\n针对某人实施该侵权行为的意图，可转移至针对另一人实施该侵权行为的情形。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 217 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（侵犯动产的实施方式）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第 110 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2015)（意图转移）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不能，因为该企业主无法证明该员工的干扰行为对其造成了实际损害。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"transferred intent","ipa":"/tɹænsfˈɝd ˌɪntˈɛnt/","syllables":"trans·ferred in·tent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"故意转移：对一人或一种侵权的故意依法转移至实际受害人与结果","contextualMeaning":"故意转移：对一人或一种侵权的故意依法转移至实际受害人与结果","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"And under the doctrine of transferred intent, an actor's intent to commit trespass to chattels against one person will transfer to the actor's commission of the tort against a different person.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-1a2e161361d6c9d8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":16,"sourceQuestionPage":46,"sourceQuestionPages":[46],"sourceExplanationPages":[47,48],"sourceAnswerPages":[47,48],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-017","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A pilot was flying his small plane when he experienced engine trouble and was forced to make an emergency landing. He landed the plane safely in a large yard behind a home located in a relatively remote area. Unfortunately, when he disembarked from the plane, he was attacked and injured by two large dogs kept by the homeowner to discourage trespassers. The homeowner, who had seen the plane land, had ordered the dogs to attack. Several months earlier, the homeowner had posted large signs around the perimeter of the yard warning of the dogs. Does the pilot have a viable claim against the homeowner for battery?","choices":{"A":"No, because the homeowner had provided adequate warning.","B":"No, because the pilot was a trespasser.","C":"Yes, because the pilot can invoke the privilege of necessity.","D":"Yes, because the pilot could not reasonably have been expected to see the warning signs posted by the homeowner."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Defenses for intentional harm to persons\n\n(justifications & excuses)\n\nDefenses Counterdefenses\n\nConsent Consent revoked Exceeded scope of consent No capacity to consent (eg, youth, incompetence) Consented to crime (eg, statutory rape) Fraud, mistake, duress Defense of self & others Used excessive force Unreasonable mistake Defendant was initial aggressor Defense of property Used excessive force Necessity (public & private) Arrest & Used excessive force Unreasonable mistake prevention of crime\n\nParental discipline Used excessive force\n\nBattery requires that the defendant intend to inflict contact (or apprehension of contact) and cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Although the defendant may generally use reasonable force (eg, battery) to stop a trespass (an intentional entry onto another's property without permission), force cannot be used to stop a trespass committed out of private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property). Here, the homeowner committed a battery when he ordered his dogs to attack the pilot (intent to inflict contact) and the dogs then attacked and injured the pilot (harmful contact) to stop his trespass. However, the homeowner's battery was not justified because the pilot had been forced to make an emergency landing on the homeowner's property to protect himself (private necessity). Therefore, the homeowner is liable for battery. (Choice A) The homeowner could use watchdogs to discourage trespassers, since he provided adequate warning of the dogs' presence. However, the homeowner could only use the dogs to inflict the same level of harm he was privileged to inflict directly. Since the homeowner could not use force to stop a trespass committed out of private necessity,\n\nneither could the dogs. Therefore, the warning signs do not excuse the homeowner's battery. (Choice B) Although the pilot intentionally entered the homeowner's property without permission (trespass), the homeowner could not use force to stop the pilot because his trespass was excused by private necessity. (Choice D) A landowner must take reasonable care to make a watchdog's presence known to probable intruders, not all intruders. Here, the homeowner took reasonable care to warn probable intruders about his watchdogs by posting large warning signs around the perimeter of his yard. Therefore, it does not matter that the pilot could not reasonably have been expected to see the warning signs from the air.\n\nEducational objective: Although a defendant is generally privileged to use reasonable force (eg, battery) to stop a trespass, the defendant cannot use force to stop a trespass committed out of private necessity (an emergency intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 77 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (privilege to use reasonable force).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 197 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (private necessity).","educationalObjective":"Although a defendant is generally privileged to use reasonable force (eg, battery) to stop a trespass, the defendant cannot use force to stop a trespass committed out of private necessity (an emergency intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 77 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (privilege to use reasonable force).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 197 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (private necessity).","rule":"Although a defendant is generally privileged to use reasonable force (eg, battery) to stop a trespass, the defendant cannot use force to stop a trespass committed out of private necessity (an emergency intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property).","irac":{"issue":"Does the pilot have a viable claim against the homeowner for battery?","rule":"Although a defendant is generally privileged to use reasonable force (eg, battery) to stop a trespass, the defendant cannot use force to stop a trespass committed out of private necessity (an emergency intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property).","analysis":"Defenses for intentional harm to persons\n\n(justifications & excuses)\n\nDefenses Counterdefenses\n\nConsent Consent revoked Exceeded scope of consent No capacity to consent (eg, youth, incompetence) Consented to crime (eg, statutory rape) Fraud, mistake, duress Defense of self & others Used excessive force Unreasonable mistake Defendant was initial aggressor Defense of property Used excessive force Necessity (public & private) Arrest & Used excessive force Unreasonable mistake prevention of crime\n\nParental discipline Used excessive force\n\nBattery requires that the defendant intend to inflict contact (or apprehension of contact) and cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Although the defendant may generally use reasonable force (eg, battery) to stop a trespass (an intentional entry onto another's property without permission), force cannot be used to stop a trespass committed out of private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property). Here, the homeowner committed a battery when he ordered his dogs to attack the pilot (intent to inflict contact) and the dogs then attacked and injured the pilot (harmful contact) to stop his trespass. However, the homeowner's battery was not justified because the pilot had been forced to make an emergency landing on the homeowner's property to protect himself (private necessity). Therefore, the homeowner is liable for battery. (Choice A) The homeowner could use watchdogs to discourage trespassers, since he provided adequate warning of the dogs' presence. However, the homeowner could only use the dogs to inflict the same level of harm he was privileged to inflict directly. Since the homeowner could not use force to stop a trespass committed out of private necessity,\n\nneither could the dogs. Therefore, the warning signs do not excuse the homeowner's battery. (Choice B) Although the pilot intentionally entered the homeowner's property without permission (trespass), the homeowner could not use force to stop the pilot because his trespass was excused by private necessity. (Choice D) A landowner must take reasonable care to make a watchdog's presence known to probable intruders, not all intruders. Here, the homeowner took reasonable care to warn probable intruders about his watchdogs by posting large warning signs around the perimeter of his yard. Therefore, it does not matter that the pilot could not reasonably have been expected to see the warning signs from the air.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the pilot can invoke the privilege of necessity."},"questionZh":"一名飞行员在驾驶小型飞机时遭遇引擎故障，被迫紧急迫降。\n\n他将飞机安全降落在位于相对偏远地区的一处住宅后方的大院子里。\n\n不幸的是，当他走出机舱时，遭到了房主饲养的两只大型犬的使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）并受伤；房主目睹了飞机降落的过程，并下令让狗发起使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）。\n\n几个月前，房主已在院子四周竖立了大型警示牌，提醒人们注意这些狗。\n\n针对房主的非法故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）指控，该飞行员是否有胜诉的可能？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为房主已经提供了充分的警告。","B":"没有，因为该飞行员属于侵入者。","C":"有，因为该飞行员可以援引“紧急避险”（privilege of necessity）作为抗辩理由。","D":"有，因为不能合理预期该飞行员能看到房主竖立的警示牌。"},"explanationZh":"针对故意伤害他人行为的抗辩 （正当理由与免责事由） 抗辩 反驳抗辩 同意；撤回同意；超出同意范围；无同意能力（如未成年、无行为能力）；同意遭受犯罪行为（如法定强奸）；欺诈、错误、胁迫 自卫与保护他人；使用了过度武力；不合理的错误认识；被告是最初的挑衅者 保护财产；使用了过度武力；紧急避险（公共及私人）；逮捕与防止犯罪；使用了过度武力；不合理的错误认识 父母管教；使用了过度武力 “故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）这一侵权行为要求被告有意造成身体接触（或使原告产生即将遭受接触的恐惧），并导致与原告发生有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n尽管被告通常可以使用合理的武力（例如实施故意侵害身体（battery）行为）来制止侵入行为（即未经许可故意进入他人财产），但不得使用武力制止因“私人紧急避险”而发生的侵入（即为保护自己、第三方或财产而必须、或看似必须进行的侵入）。\n\n在本案中，房主命令其狗使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）飞行员（具有造成接触的意图），随后狗使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）并伤害了飞行员（造成了有害接触），以此制止其侵入行为，这一过程构成了故意侵害身体（battery）。\n\n然而，房主的故意侵害身体（battery）行为不具备正当理由，因为飞行员是为了保护自身安全而被迫在房主财产上进行紧急迫降的（属于私人紧急避险）。\n\n因此，房主须对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任。\n\n（选项 A）房主可以使用看门狗来威慑侵入者，前提是他已就狗的存在提供了充分的警示。\n\n然而，房主仅能利用狗造成其本人有权直接造成的同等程度的伤害。\n\n既然房主无权使用武力制止因私人紧急避险而发生的侵入， 那么狗也不得这样做。\n\n因此，警示标志并不能免除房主故意侵害身体（battery）行为的责任。\n\n（选项 B）尽管飞行员未经许可故意进入房主财产（构成侵入），但房主不得使用武力制止飞行员，因为其侵入行为因私人紧急避险而获得豁免。\n\n（选项 D）土地所有者必须采取合理的注意措施，让潜在的侵入者知晓看门狗的存在，而非必须告知所有侵入者。\n\n在本案中，房主在院子周边张贴了大型警示标志，从而采取了合理的注意措施，向潜在侵入者发出了关于看门狗的警告。\n\n因此，即便无法合理预期飞行员能从空中看到警示标志，这一点也无关紧要。\n\n教学目标：尽管被告通常有权使用合理武力（例如，实施故意侵害身体（battery）行为）来制止侵入行为，但若侵入行为是出于“私法上的紧急避险”（即为保护自身、第三方或财产而有必要——或看似有必要——进行的紧急侵入），则被告不得使用武力加以制止。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第13条（美国法律协会，1965年）（因有害接触而构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第77条（美国法律协会，1965年）（使用合理武力的特权）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第197条（美国法律协会，1965年）（私法上的紧急避险）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"尽管被告通常有权使用合理武力（例如，实施非法故意侵害身体（battery）行为）来制止侵入行为，但被告不得使用武力来制止因“私人紧急避险”（即为保护自身、第三方或财产而必须或看似必须进行的紧急侵入）而发生的侵入行为。","iracZh":{"issue":"针对房主的非法故意侵害身体（battery）指控，该飞行员是否有胜诉的可能？","rule":"尽管被告通常有权使用合理武力（例如，实施非法故意侵害身体（battery）行为）来制止侵入行为，但被告不得使用武力来制止因“私人紧急避险”（即为保护自身、第三方或财产而必须或看似必须进行的紧急侵入）而发生的侵入行为。","analysis":"针对故意伤害他人行为的抗辩 （正当理由与免责事由） 抗辩 反驳抗辩 同意；撤回同意；超出同意范围；无同意能力（如未成年、无行为能力）；同意遭受犯罪行为（如法定强奸）；欺诈、错误、胁迫 自卫与保护他人；使用了过度武力；不合理的错误认识；被告是最初的挑衅者 保护财产；使用了过度武力；紧急避险（公共及私人）；逮捕与防止犯罪；使用了过度武力；不合理的错误认识 父母管教；使用了过度武力 “故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）这一侵权行为要求被告有意造成身体接触（或使原告产生即将遭受接触的恐惧），并导致与原告发生有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n尽管被告通常可以使用合理的武力（例如实施故意侵害身体（battery）行为）来制止侵入行为（即未经许可故意进入他人财产），但不得使用武力制止因“私人紧急避险”而发生的侵入（即为保护自己、第三方或财产而必须、或看似必须进行的侵入）。\n\n在本案中，房主命令其狗使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）飞行员（具有造成接触的意图），随后狗使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）并伤害了飞行员（造成了有害接触），以此制止其侵入行为，这一过程构成了故意侵害身体（battery）。\n\n然而，房主的故意侵害身体（battery）行为不具备正当理由，因为飞行员是为了保护自身安全而被迫在房主财产上进行紧急迫降的（属于私人紧急避险）。\n\n因此，房主须对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任。\n\n（选项 A）房主可以使用看门狗来威慑侵入者，前提是他已就狗的存在提供了充分的警示。\n\n然而，房主仅能利用狗造成其本人有权直接造成的同等程度的伤害。\n\n既然房主无权使用武力制止因私人紧急避险而发生的侵入， 那么狗也不得这样做。\n\n因此，警示标志并不能免除房主故意侵害身体（battery）行为的责任。\n\n（选项 B）尽管飞行员未经许可故意进入房主财产（构成侵入），但房主不得使用武力制止飞行员，因为其侵入行为因私人紧急避险而获得豁免。\n\n（选项 D）土地所有者必须采取合理的注意措施，让潜在的侵入者知晓看门狗的存在，而非必须告知所有侵入者。\n\n在本案中，房主在院子周边张贴了大型警示标志，从而采取了合理的注意措施，向潜在侵入者发出了关于看门狗的警告。\n\n因此，即便无法合理预期飞行员能从空中看到警示标志，这一点也无关紧要。\n\n教学目标：尽管被告通常有权使用合理武力（例如，实施故意侵害身体（battery）行为）来制止侵入行为，但若侵入行为是出于“私法上的紧急避险”（即为保护自身、第三方或财产而有必要——或看似有必要——进行的紧急侵入），则被告不得使用武力加以制止。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第13条（美国法律协会，1965年）（因有害接触而构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第77条（美国法律协会，1965年）（使用合理武力的特权）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第197条（美国法律协会，1965年）（私法上的紧急避险）。","conclusion":"答案 C：有，因为该飞行员可以援引“紧急避险”（privilege of necessity）作为抗辩理由。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":17,"sourceQuestionPage":49,"sourceQuestionPages":[49],"sourceExplanationPages":[50,51],"sourceAnswerPages":[50,51],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-018","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A man built a garage in his backyard that encroached two feet across the property line onto property owned by his neighbor. Thereafter, the man sold his property to a buyer. Prior to the man's sale to the buyer, the man and the neighbor were unaware that the garage encroached onto the neighbor's property. The neighbor later learned of the encroachment and she sued the man for damages for trespass. In this action, will the neighbor prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man no longer owns or possesses the garage.","B":"No, because the man was unaware of the encroachment when the garage was built.","C":"Yes, because the man knew where the garage was located, whether or not he knew where the property line was.","D":"Yes, unless the buyer was aware of the encroachment when he purchased the property."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Trespass to land\n\nRequires proof that:\n\ndefendant intentionally entered land in question and plaintiff did not consent to entry For trespass to land (ie, trespass quare clausum fregit), a defendant must intentionally enter or cause something to enter the land in question. If the plaintiff did not consent to the defendant's intentional entry, then the defendant is liable for trespass. It does not matter whether the defendant knew that the property belonged to the plaintiff or believed that the entry was permissible. Here, the man built a garage that encroached across the neighbor's property line. Although the man did not know that the garage was on his neighbor's property, he still intended for it to be on the land in question. And since the neighbor did not consent to that entry, the man is liable for trespass. (Choice A) If the defendant intentionally causes an object to enter and remain on the plaintiff's property, the defendant is liable for its continued trespass even after the defendant transfers his/her legal interest in the object to another. Since the man intentionally built his garage on the land in question, he is liable for its continued trespass even though he no longer owns it. (Choice B) Trespass requires that the defendant intend to be (or cause something to be) on the property in question, but the defendant need not know that the property belongs to the plaintiff. Therefore, it does not matter whether the man knew that the garage encroached onto the neighbor's property when it was built. (Choice D) A person who purchases property that encroaches onto the land of another is liable for trespass if he/she knows about the trespass and fails to remedy it. However, the sale does not absolve the seller of liability for trespass. Therefore, the man is liable for trespass even if the buyer was aware of the garage's encroachment.\n\nEducational objective: Trespass to land requires the intent to enter the land in question, but does not also require that the defendant know that the property belongs to the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of trespass to land).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (trespass to land by mistake).","educationalObjective":"Trespass to land requires the intent to enter the land in question, but does not also require that the defendant know that the property belongs to the plaintiff.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of trespass to land).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (trespass to land by mistake).","rule":"Trespass to land requires the intent to enter the land in question, but does not also require that the defendant know that the property belongs to the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"In this action, will the neighbor prevail?","rule":"Trespass to land requires the intent to enter the land in question, but does not also require that the defendant know that the property belongs to the plaintiff.","analysis":"Trespass to land\n\nRequires proof that:\n\ndefendant intentionally entered land in question and plaintiff did not consent to entry For trespass to land (ie, trespass quare clausum fregit), a defendant must intentionally enter or cause something to enter the land in question. If the plaintiff did not consent to the defendant's intentional entry, then the defendant is liable for trespass. It does not matter whether the defendant knew that the property belonged to the plaintiff or believed that the entry was permissible. Here, the man built a garage that encroached across the neighbor's property line. Although the man did not know that the garage was on his neighbor's property, he still intended for it to be on the land in question. And since the neighbor did not consent to that entry, the man is liable for trespass. (Choice A) If the defendant intentionally causes an object to enter and remain on the plaintiff's property, the defendant is liable for its continued trespass even after the defendant transfers his/her legal interest in the object to another. Since the man intentionally built his garage on the land in question, he is liable for its continued trespass even though he no longer owns it. (Choice B) Trespass requires that the defendant intend to be (or cause something to be) on the property in question, but the defendant need not know that the property belongs to the plaintiff. Therefore, it does not matter whether the man knew that the garage encroached onto the neighbor's property when it was built. (Choice D) A person who purchases property that encroaches onto the land of another is liable for trespass if he/she knows about the trespass and fails to remedy it. However, the sale does not absolve the seller of liability for trespass. Therefore, the man is liable for trespass even if the buyer was aware of the garage's encroachment.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the man knew where the garage was located, whether or not he knew where the property line was."},"questionZh":"一名男子在其后院建造了一个车库，车库侵占了邻居两英尺的土地。\n\n之后，该男子将房产出售给了买家。\n\n在出售房产之前，该男子和邻居均不知晓车库侵占了邻居的土地。\n\n邻居后来得知此事，并以侵权为由起诉该男子，要求赔偿损失。\n\n在此诉讼中，邻居能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为该男子不再拥有或占有该车库。","B":"不能，因为该男子在建造车库时并不知晓侵占行为。","C":"能，因为该男子知道车库的位置，无论他是否知道土地边界在哪里。","D":"能，除非买家在购买房产时知晓侵占行为。"},"explanationZh":"土地侵入（Trespass to land） 需证明以下要件： 被告有意进入相关土地，且原告未同意该进入行为。就土地侵入（即 *trespass quare clausum fregit*）而言，被告必须有意进入相关土地或致使某物进入该土地。\n\n若原告未同意被告的有意进入行为，则被告须承担侵入责任。\n\n被告是否知晓该财产属于原告，或是否误以为其进入行为是获准的，均不影响责任的认定。\n\n在本案中，该男子建造的车库越过了与邻居的边界线。\n\n尽管该男子并不知道车库建在了邻居的土地上，但他确实有意将车库建在该土地上。\n\n鉴于邻居未同意该进入行为，该男子须承担侵入责任。\n\n（选项 A）若被告有意致使某物进入并停留在原告的财产上，即使被告已将该物的所有权（或法律权益）转让给他人，仍须对持续存在的侵入行为承担责任。\n\n由于该男子有意将车库建在相关土地上，即便他已不再拥有该车库，仍须对持续存在的侵入行为承担责任。\n\n（选项 B）侵入行为要求被告有意进入（或致使某物进入）相关财产，但并不要求被告必须知晓该财产属于原告。\n\n因此，该男子在建造车库时是否知晓其侵占了邻居的土地，并不影响责任认定。\n\n（选项 D）若某人购入了一处侵占他人土地的财产，且在知晓该侵入情况后未予纠正，则该人须承担侵入责任。\n\n然而，出售行为并不能免除卖方的侵入责任。\n\n因此，即使买方知晓车库存在侵占行为，该男子仍须承担侵入责任。\n\n教育目标：土地侵入要求被告有意进入相关土地，但不要求被告必须知晓该财产属于原告。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 158 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（土地侵入的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第164条（美国法律学会，1965年）（因误解而造成的土地侵权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"侵占土地罪要求被告有进入涉案土地的意图，但并不要求被告明知该土地属于原告。","iracZh":{"issue":"在本案中，邻居会胜诉吗？","rule":"侵占土地罪要求被告有进入涉案土地的意图，但并不要求被告明知该土地属于原告。","analysis":"土地侵入（Trespass to land） 需证明以下要件： 被告有意进入相关土地，且原告未同意该进入行为。就土地侵入（即 *trespass quare clausum fregit*）而言，被告必须有意进入相关土地或致使某物进入该土地。\n\n若原告未同意被告的有意进入行为，则被告须承担侵入责任。\n\n被告是否知晓该财产属于原告，或是否误以为其进入行为是获准的，均不影响责任的认定。\n\n在本案中，该男子建造的车库越过了与邻居的边界线。\n\n尽管该男子并不知道车库建在了邻居的土地上，但他确实有意将车库建在该土地上。\n\n鉴于邻居未同意该进入行为，该男子须承担侵入责任。\n\n（选项 A）若被告有意致使某物进入并停留在原告的财产上，即使被告已将该物的所有权（或法律权益）转让给他人，仍须对持续存在的侵入行为承担责任。\n\n由于该男子有意将车库建在相关土地上，即便他已不再拥有该车库，仍须对持续存在的侵入行为承担责任。\n\n（选项 B）侵入行为要求被告有意进入（或致使某物进入）相关财产，但并不要求被告必须知晓该财产属于原告。\n\n因此，该男子在建造车库时是否知晓其侵占了邻居的土地，并不影响责任认定。\n\n（选项 D）若某人购入了一处侵占他人土地的财产，且在知晓该侵入情况后未予纠正，则该人须承担侵入责任。\n\n然而，出售行为并不能免除卖方的侵入责任。\n\n因此，即使买方知晓车库存在侵占行为，该男子仍须承担侵入责任。\n\n教育目标：土地侵入要求被告有意进入相关土地，但不要求被告必须知晓该财产属于原告。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 158 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（土地侵入的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第164条（美国法律学会，1965年）（因误解而造成的土地侵权）。","conclusion":"答案 C：能，因为该男子知道车库的位置，无论他是否知道土地边界在哪里。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":18,"sourceQuestionPage":52,"sourceQuestionPages":[52],"sourceExplanationPages":[53,54],"sourceAnswerPages":[53,54],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-019","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A mother and her six-year-old child were on a walk when the mother stopped to talk with an elderly neighbor. Because the child resented having his mother's attention diverted by the neighbor, the child angrily threw himself against the neighbor and knocked her to the ground. The neighbor suffered a broken wrist as a result of the fall. In an action for battery by the neighbor against the child, what is the strongest argument for liability?","choices":{"A":"The child intended to throw himself against the neighbor.","B":"The child was not justified in his anger.","C":"The child was old enough to appreciate that causing a fall could inflict serious injury.","D":"The child was old enough to appreciate the riskiness of his conduct."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Tort liability of minor children\n\nIntentional Liable if child was capable of forming requisite intent tort\n\nNegligence Liable if child failed to act as reasonable child of similar age, intelligence, & experience would have acted under similar circumstances\n\nBattery requires that the defendant: intend to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof) and cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Age is not a defense to intentional tort liability. Instead, a child's level of maturity is considered when determining whether he/she is capable of forming the requisite intent. Therefore, a child is liable for battery when the above elements are met, regardless of whether the child is old enough to appreciate the riskiness or consequences of his/her conduct (Choices C & D). Here, the strongest argument for liability is that the six-year-old child was mature enough to understand that he would cause harmful or offensive contact with the neighbor when he angrily threw himself against her. And since his intentional contact caused the neighbor to fall and break her wrist, the child is liable for battery. (Choice B) Although justification (eg, consent, self-defense) is a defense to battery, anger is never a valid justification.\n\nEducational objective: Battery requires the intent to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof). If a child is capable of forming that intent, the child may be held liable for battery.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 895I (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (tort liability for children).","educationalObjective":"Battery requires the intent to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof). If a child is capable of forming that intent, the child may be held liable for battery.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 895I (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (tort liability for children).","rule":"Battery requires the intent to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof). If a child is capable of forming that intent, the child may be held liable for battery.","irac":{"issue":"In an action for battery by the neighbor against the child, what is the strongest argument for liability?","rule":"Battery requires the intent to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof). If a child is capable of forming that intent, the child may be held liable for battery.","analysis":"Tort liability of minor children\n\nIntentional Liable if child was capable of forming requisite intent tort\n\nNegligence Liable if child failed to act as reasonable child of similar age, intelligence, & experience would have acted under similar circumstances\n\nBattery requires that the defendant: intend to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof) and cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Age is not a defense to intentional tort liability. Instead, a child's level of maturity is considered when determining whether he/she is capable of forming the requisite intent. Therefore, a child is liable for battery when the above elements are met, regardless of whether the child is old enough to appreciate the riskiness or consequences of his/her conduct (Choices C & D). Here, the strongest argument for liability is that the six-year-old child was mature enough to understand that he would cause harmful or offensive contact with the neighbor when he angrily threw himself against her. And since his intentional contact caused the neighbor to fall and break her wrist, the child is liable for battery. (Choice B) Although justification (eg, consent, self-defense) is a defense to battery, anger is never a valid justification.","conclusion":"Answer A: The child intended to throw himself against the neighbor."},"questionZh":"一位母亲带着六岁的孩子散步时，停下来与一位年长的邻居交谈。\n\n孩子因不满母亲的注意力被邻居分散，愤怒地冲向邻居，将其撞倒在地。\n\n邻居因摔倒导致手腕骨折。\n\n在邻居针对该儿童提起的故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）诉讼中，支持认定其承担责任的最有力论据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该儿童有意冲向邻居。","B":"该儿童的愤怒缺乏正当理由。","C":"该儿童已达到足以意识到导致他人摔倒可能造成严重伤害的年龄。","D":"该儿童已达到足以意识到其行为具有风险性的年龄。"},"explanationZh":"未成年子女的侵权责任 故意侵权：若儿童有能力形成必要的侵权意图，则须承担责任。 过失侵权：若儿童未能像同等年龄、智力和阅历的合理儿童在类似情况下那样行事，则须承担责任。\n\n故意侵害身体（battery）（Battery）侵权行为要求被告：有意造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并实际导致了与原告的有害或冒犯性接触。\n\n年龄不能作为免除故意侵权责任的抗辩理由；相反，在判定儿童是否有能力形成必要意图时，会考量其成熟程度。\n\n因此，只要满足上述要件，儿童即须对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任，无论其年龄是否足以理解其行为的风险或后果（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n在本案中，支持承担责任的最有力论据是：这名六岁儿童已足够成熟，能够理解当他愤怒地冲撞邻居时，会造成与邻居的有害或冒犯性接触。\n\n鉴于其故意接触行为导致邻居摔倒并造成手腕骨折，该儿童须对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任（选项 B）。\n\n尽管正当理由（如同意、正当防卫）可作为故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的抗辩理由，但愤怒绝非有效的正当理由。\n\n教育目标：故意侵害身体（battery）侵权要求具备造成有害或冒犯性接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感）的意图。\n\n若儿童有能力形成该意图，则可能须对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 895I 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（儿童的侵权责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"构成故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）需要具备造成有害或冒犯性接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感）的意图。\n\n如果儿童有能力形成这种意图，则可能需对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"在邻居针对该儿童提起的故意侵害身体（battery）诉讼中，支持认定其承担责任的最有力论据是什么？","rule":"构成故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）需要具备造成有害或冒犯性接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感）的意图。\n\n如果儿童有能力形成这种意图，则可能需对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任。","analysis":"未成年子女的侵权责任 故意侵权：若儿童有能力形成必要的侵权意图，则须承担责任。 过失侵权：若儿童未能像同等年龄、智力和阅历的合理儿童在类似情况下那样行事，则须承担责任。\n\n故意侵害身体（battery）（Battery）侵权行为要求被告：有意造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并实际导致了与原告的有害或冒犯性接触。\n\n年龄不能作为免除故意侵权责任的抗辩理由；相反，在判定儿童是否有能力形成必要意图时，会考量其成熟程度。\n\n因此，只要满足上述要件，儿童即须对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任，无论其年龄是否足以理解其行为的风险或后果（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n在本案中，支持承担责任的最有力论据是：这名六岁儿童已足够成熟，能够理解当他愤怒地冲撞邻居时，会造成与邻居的有害或冒犯性接触。\n\n鉴于其故意接触行为导致邻居摔倒并造成手腕骨折，该儿童须对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任（选项 B）。\n\n尽管正当理由（如同意、正当防卫）可作为故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的抗辩理由，但愤怒绝非有效的正当理由。\n\n教育目标：故意侵害身体（battery）侵权要求具备造成有害或冒犯性接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感）的意图。\n\n若儿童有能力形成该意图，则可能须对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 895I 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（儿童的侵权责任）。","conclusion":"答案 A：该儿童有意冲向邻居。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":19,"sourceQuestionPage":55,"sourceQuestionPages":[55],"sourceExplanationPages":[56,57],"sourceAnswerPages":[56,57],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-020","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"In an action for battery, the evidence established the following: The plaintiff was bad- tempered and, as the defendant knew, carried a gun and used it often; the plaintiff struck the defendant first; during the altercation, the plaintiff repeatedly tried to get to his gun; and the blows inflicted upon the plaintiff by the defendant resulted in the plaintiff being hospitalized. Which finding of fact would be most likely to result in a verdict for the defendant?","choices":{"A":"The defendant feared death or serious bodily harm.","B":"The defendant used no more force than he actually believed was necessary to protect himself against death or serious bodily harm.","C":"The defendant used no more force than he reasonably believed was necessary to protect himself against death or serious bodily harm.","D":"The defendant was justified in retaliating against the plaintiff because the plaintiff struck the first blow."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Deadly force in self-defense\n\nDefendant reasonably believes:\n\nplaintiff is about to intentionally inflict serious bodily harm or death upon defendant and plaintiff can only be stopped by defendant's immediate use of deadly force A defendant is not liable for an intentional tort (eg, battery) committed while acting in self- defense—the privilege to use reasonable force to protect oneself against the intentional infliction of harmful or offensive contact. However, the force used must be proportionate to the anticipated harm, so the defendant can only use deadly force in self-defense when he/she reasonably believes that the plaintiff: is about to intentionally inflict serious bodily harm or death upon the defendant and can only be stopped by the defendant's immediate use of deadly force. Here, the plaintiff struck the defendant first and repeatedly tried to grab his gun during the fight. Therefore, the defendant could reasonably believe that (1) the plaintiff was about to intentionally inflict serious bodily harm or death and (2) he could only stop the plaintiff with deadly blows. And since the defendant used no more force than he reasonably believed was necessary to protect himself, he can establish the self-defense privilege and obtain a verdict in his favor. (Choices A & B) Self-defense requires that the defendant reasonably fear serious bodily harm or death and reasonably believe that deadly force is required. Therefore, even if the defendant (1) feared that the plaintiff's actions threatened death or serious bodily harm or (2) used no more force than he actually believed was necessary to protect himself, he could still be liable for battery if his fear or actual belief was unreasonable. (Choice D) The self-defense privilege never permits retaliation and terminates once there is no threat of further harm (eg, the defendant would not be privileged to use force in self- defense if the plaintiff walked away after striking the first blow). Here, the defendant's conduct was justified because he reasonably believed that the plaintiff was about to shoot him—not because the plaintiff struck the first blow.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant is privileged to use force in self-defense when (1) the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff is about to intentionally inflict harmful or offensive contact and (2) the defendant uses no more force than he/she reasonably believes is necessary to prevent the threatened contact.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 65 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (force threatening death or serious bodily harm).\n\nTatman v. Cordingly, 672 P.2d 1286 (Wyo. 1983).","educationalObjective":"A defendant is privileged to use force in self-defense when (1) the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff is about to intentionally inflict harmful or offensive contact and (2) the defendant uses no more force than he/she reasonably believes is necessary to prevent the threatened contact.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 65 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (force threatening death or serious bodily harm).\n\nTatman v. Cordingly, 672 P.2d 1286 (Wyo. 1983).","rule":"A defendant is privileged to use force in self-defense when (1) the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff is about to intentionally inflict harmful or offensive contact and (2) the defendant uses no more force than he/she reasonably believes is necessary to prevent the threatened contact.","irac":{"issue":"Which finding of fact would be most likely to result in a verdict for the defendant?","rule":"A defendant is privileged to use force in self-defense when (1) the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff is about to intentionally inflict harmful or offensive contact and (2) the defendant uses no more force than he/she reasonably believes is necessary to prevent the threatened contact.","analysis":"Deadly force in self-defense\n\nDefendant reasonably believes:\n\nplaintiff is about to intentionally inflict serious bodily harm or death upon defendant and plaintiff can only be stopped by defendant's immediate use of deadly force A defendant is not liable for an intentional tort (eg, battery) committed while acting in self- defense—the privilege to use reasonable force to protect oneself against the intentional infliction of harmful or offensive contact. However, the force used must be proportionate to the anticipated harm, so the defendant can only use deadly force in self-defense when he/she reasonably believes that the plaintiff: is about to intentionally inflict serious bodily harm or death upon the defendant and can only be stopped by the defendant's immediate use of deadly force. Here, the plaintiff struck the defendant first and repeatedly tried to grab his gun during the fight. Therefore, the defendant could reasonably believe that (1) the plaintiff was about to intentionally inflict serious bodily harm or death and (2) he could only stop the plaintiff with deadly blows. And since the defendant used no more force than he reasonably believed was necessary to protect himself, he can establish the self-defense privilege and obtain a verdict in his favor. (Choices A & B) Self-defense requires that the defendant reasonably fear serious bodily harm or death and reasonably believe that deadly force is required. Therefore, even if the defendant (1) feared that the plaintiff's actions threatened death or serious bodily harm or (2) used no more force than he actually believed was necessary to protect himself, he could still be liable for battery if his fear or actual belief was unreasonable. (Choice D) The self-defense privilege never permits retaliation and terminates once there is no threat of further harm (eg, the defendant would not be privileged to use force in self- defense if the plaintiff walked away after striking the first blow). Here, the defendant's conduct was justified because he reasonably believed that the plaintiff was about to shoot him—not because the plaintiff struck the first blow.","conclusion":"Answer C: The defendant used no more force than he reasonably believed was necessary to protect himself against death or serious bodily harm."},"questionZh":"在一项关于非法故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）的诉讼中，证据确立了以下事实：原告脾气暴躁，且被告明知原告随身携带枪支并经常使用；原告先动手打了被告；在冲突过程中，原告多次试图去拿枪；被告对原告造成的打击导致原告住院。\n\n下列哪项事实认定最有可能导致有利于被告的判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"被告担心遭受死亡或严重身体伤害。","B":"被告使用的武力未超过其主观上认为为保护自己免受死亡或严重身体伤害所必需的限度。","C":"被告使用的武力未超过其合理认为为保护自己免受死亡或严重身体伤害所必需的限度。","D":"被告对原告进行反击是正当的，因为原告先动了手。"},"explanationZh":"自卫中的致命武力 被告有合理理由相信： 原告即将故意对被告造成严重身体伤害或死亡，且只有被告立即使用致命武力才能阻止原告。被告在行使自卫权时（即为保护自己免受故意造成的有害或冒犯性接触而使用合理武力的特权）实施的故意侵权行为（如故意侵害身体（battery）），不承担法律责任。\n\n然而，所使用的武力必须与预期的伤害相称；因此，被告只有在有合理理由相信原告即将故意对其造成严重身体伤害或死亡，且只有立即使用致命武力才能阻止原告时，方可使用致命武力进行自卫。\n\n在本案中，原告先动手打了被告，并在扭打过程中多次试图夺取被告的枪支。\n\n因此，被告有合理理由相信：(1) 原告即将故意造成严重身体伤害或死亡；(2) 他只能通过致命打击来阻止原告。\n\n鉴于被告使用的武力未超过其合理认为保护自身所需的限度，他可以主张自卫特权并获得有利判决。\n\n（选项 A 和 B）自卫要求被告合理地担心遭受严重身体伤害或死亡，并合理地相信有必要使用致命武力。\n\n因此，即使被告 (1) 担心原告的行为会威胁导致死亡或严重身体伤害，或 (2) 使用的武力未超过其主观认为保护自身所需的限度，但如果其担忧或主观信念是不合理的，他仍可能因故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担法律责任。\n\n（选项 D）自卫特权绝不允许报复行为，且一旦不再存在进一步伤害的威胁，该特权即告终止（例如，如果原告在打出第一拳后转身离开，被告便无权使用武力自卫）。\n\n在本案中，被告的行为是正当的，因为他有合理理由相信原告即将向他开枪——而非仅仅因为原告先动了手。\n\n教育目标：当 (1) 被告有合理理由相信原告即将故意造成有害或冒犯性接触，且 (2) 被告使用的武力未超过其合理认为防止该威胁接触所需的限度时，被告享有自卫使用武力的特权。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第65条（美国法律协会，1965年）（威胁致死或造成严重身体伤害的武力）。\n\nTatman 诉 Cordingly 案，672 P.2d 1286 (Wyo. 1983)。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当满足以下条件时，被告有权使用武力进行自卫：(1) 被告合理地认为原告即将故意施加有害或冒犯性的接触；以及 (2) 被告使用的武力未超过其合理认为为防止该受威胁接触所必需的限度。","iracZh":{"issue":"下列哪项事实认定最有可能导致有利于被告的判决？","rule":"当满足以下条件时，被告有权使用武力进行自卫：(1) 被告合理地认为原告即将故意施加有害或冒犯性的接触；以及 (2) 被告使用的武力未超过其合理认为为防止该受威胁接触所必需的限度。","analysis":"自卫中的致命武力 被告有合理理由相信： 原告即将故意对被告造成严重身体伤害或死亡，且只有被告立即使用致命武力才能阻止原告。被告在行使自卫权时（即为保护自己免受故意造成的有害或冒犯性接触而使用合理武力的特权）实施的故意侵权行为（如故意侵害身体（battery）），不承担法律责任。\n\n然而，所使用的武力必须与预期的伤害相称；因此，被告只有在有合理理由相信原告即将故意对其造成严重身体伤害或死亡，且只有立即使用致命武力才能阻止原告时，方可使用致命武力进行自卫。\n\n在本案中，原告先动手打了被告，并在扭打过程中多次试图夺取被告的枪支。\n\n因此，被告有合理理由相信：(1) 原告即将故意造成严重身体伤害或死亡；(2) 他只能通过致命打击来阻止原告。\n\n鉴于被告使用的武力未超过其合理认为保护自身所需的限度，他可以主张自卫特权并获得有利判决。\n\n（选项 A 和 B）自卫要求被告合理地担心遭受严重身体伤害或死亡，并合理地相信有必要使用致命武力。\n\n因此，即使被告 (1) 担心原告的行为会威胁导致死亡或严重身体伤害，或 (2) 使用的武力未超过其主观认为保护自身所需的限度，但如果其担忧或主观信念是不合理的，他仍可能因故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担法律责任。\n\n（选项 D）自卫特权绝不允许报复行为，且一旦不再存在进一步伤害的威胁，该特权即告终止（例如，如果原告在打出第一拳后转身离开，被告便无权使用武力自卫）。\n\n在本案中，被告的行为是正当的，因为他有合理理由相信原告即将向他开枪——而非仅仅因为原告先动了手。\n\n教育目标：当 (1) 被告有合理理由相信原告即将故意造成有害或冒犯性接触，且 (2) 被告使用的武力未超过其合理认为防止该威胁接触所需的限度时，被告享有自卫使用武力的特权。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第65条（美国法律协会，1965年）（威胁致死或造成严重身体伤害的武力）。\n\nTatman 诉 Cordingly 案，672 P.2d 1286 (Wyo. 1983)。","conclusion":"答案 C：被告使用的武力未超过其合理认为为保护自己免受死亡或严重身体伤害所必需的限度。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":20,"sourceQuestionPage":58,"sourceQuestionPages":[58],"sourceExplanationPages":[59,60],"sourceAnswerPages":[59,60],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-021","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A man owned a much-loved cat, worth about $25, that frequently trespassed on a neighbor's property. The neighbor repeatedly asked the man to keep the cat on his own property, but the trespasses did not diminish. Aware of the man's long-standing attachment to the cat, the neighbor killed the cat with a shotgun in full view of the man. As a consequence, the man suffered great emotional distress. In an action by the man against the neighbor, which of the following claims would be likely to result in the greatest recovery of compensatory damages?","choices":{"A":"Battery.","B":"Conversion.","C":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress.","D":"Trespass to a chattel."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Extreme and outrageous conduct\n\nFlagrant indecency Exploiting known & special vulnerability Abusing authority Repeated harassment Mnemonic: FEAR\n\nCompensatory damages are awarded as compensation for the plaintiff's actual harm (eg, medical bills, lost wages, repair costs). Generally, plaintiffs must prove bodily harm to receive compensation for emotional harm. However, intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) claims allow plaintiffs to recover compensatory damages for emotional distress without proof of bodily harm if they show that the defendant: engaged in extreme and outrageous conduct (eg, conduct affecting the plaintiff's known and special vulnerability) and intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress. Here, the neighbor knew of the man's long-standing attachment to the cat (special vulnerability) when he shot it in full view of the man (extreme and outrageous conduct). In doing so, the neighbor either knew his conduct would harm the man (intent) or disregarded the risk of harm (recklessness). And since that conduct caused the man great emotional harm (severe emotional distress), he can recover compensatory damages for that mental distress—an amount greater than the value of the cat—without proof of bodily harm. (Choice A) Battery claims compensate plaintiffs for bodily harm. But since the neighbor did not cause harmful or offensive contact with the man, he cannot recover damages for battery. (Choice B) Conversion claims compensate plaintiffs for the fair market value of their dispossessed (ie, lost) chattels. Although the man can prevail on a conversion claim because the neighbor shot his cat (dispossession of chattel), he can only recover the fair market value of the cat—$25. (Choice D) Trespass to chattel claims compensate plaintiffs for actual harm to their chattel. Here, the neighbor committed trespass to chattel when he killed the man's cat (harm to chattel). But since the cat was worth $25 and is now worth nothing, the man can only recover $25.\n\nEducational objective: Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) compensates a plaintiff for emotional harm—even without proof of bodily harm—if he/she can prove that the defendant (1)\n\nengaged in extreme and outrageous conduct and (2) intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional or reckless infliction of emotional harm).","educationalObjective":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) compensates a plaintiff for emotional harm—even without proof of bodily harm—if he/she can prove that the defendant (1)\n\nengaged in extreme and outrageous conduct and (2) intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional or reckless infliction of emotional harm).","rule":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) compensates a plaintiff for emotional harm—even without proof of bodily harm—if he/she can prove that the defendant (1)\n\nengaged in extreme and outrageous conduct and (2) intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the man against the neighbor, which of the following claims would be likely to result in the greatest recovery of compensatory damages?","rule":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) compensates a plaintiff for emotional harm—even without proof of bodily harm—if he/she can prove that the defendant (1)\n\nengaged in extreme and outrageous conduct and (2) intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress.","analysis":"Extreme and outrageous conduct\n\nFlagrant indecency Exploiting known & special vulnerability Abusing authority Repeated harassment Mnemonic: FEAR\n\nCompensatory damages are awarded as compensation for the plaintiff's actual harm (eg, medical bills, lost wages, repair costs). Generally, plaintiffs must prove bodily harm to receive compensation for emotional harm. However, intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) claims allow plaintiffs to recover compensatory damages for emotional distress without proof of bodily harm if they show that the defendant: engaged in extreme and outrageous conduct (eg, conduct affecting the plaintiff's known and special vulnerability) and intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress. Here, the neighbor knew of the man's long-standing attachment to the cat (special vulnerability) when he shot it in full view of the man (extreme and outrageous conduct). In doing so, the neighbor either knew his conduct would harm the man (intent) or disregarded the risk of harm (recklessness). And since that conduct caused the man great emotional harm (severe emotional distress), he can recover compensatory damages for that mental distress—an amount greater than the value of the cat—without proof of bodily harm. (Choice A) Battery claims compensate plaintiffs for bodily harm. But since the neighbor did not cause harmful or offensive contact with the man, he cannot recover damages for battery. (Choice B) Conversion claims compensate plaintiffs for the fair market value of their dispossessed (ie, lost) chattels. Although the man can prevail on a conversion claim because the neighbor shot his cat (dispossession of chattel), he can only recover the fair market value of the cat—$25. (Choice D) Trespass to chattel claims compensate plaintiffs for actual harm to their chattel. Here, the neighbor committed trespass to chattel when he killed the man's cat (harm to chattel). But since the cat was worth $25 and is now worth nothing, the man can only recover $25.","conclusion":"Answer C: Intentional infliction of emotional distress."},"questionZh":"一名男子养了一只他非常珍爱的猫（价值约25美元），这只猫经常闯入邻居的领地。\n\n邻居多次要求该男子将猫看管在自家范围内，但猫闯入邻居领地的情况并未减少。\n\n邻居深知该男子对这只猫感情深厚，却当着该男子的面用猎枪杀死了这只猫。\n\n结果，该男子遭受了巨大的精神痛苦。\n\n在该男子针对邻居提起的诉讼中，下列哪项诉讼主张最有可能获得数额最大的补偿性损害赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"故意侵害身体（battery）（Battery）。","B":"侵占动产（Conversion）。","C":"故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）（Intentional infliction of emotional distress）。","D":"侵犯动产（Trespass to a chattel）。"},"explanationZh":"极端且令人发指的行为 公然的不当行为；利用已知的特殊脆弱性；滥用职权；反复骚扰。记忆口诀：FEAR（恐惧）。 补偿性损害赔偿旨在弥补原告遭受的实际损害（例如医疗费、工资损失、维修费用）。\n\n通常，原告必须证明存在身体伤害才能获得针对精神伤害的赔偿。\n\n然而，在“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）的诉讼中，如果原告能证明被告实施了极端且令人发指的行为（例如针对原告已知特殊脆弱性的行为），并且故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦，则原告无需证明存在身体伤害即可获得精神痛苦的补偿性赔偿。\n\n在本案中，邻居在当着该男子的面射杀其猫时，已知晓该男子对这只猫有着深厚的感情（即“特殊脆弱性”），且该射杀行为属于“极端且令人发指的行为”。\n\n邻居在实施该行为时，要么明知其行为会伤害该男子（即“故意”），要么无视了造成伤害的风险（即“鲁莽”）。\n\n鉴于该行为给该男子造成了巨大的精神伤害（即“严重精神痛苦”），他无需证明存在身体伤害，即可就该精神痛苦获得补偿性赔偿——其金额超过了猫本身的价值。\n\n（选项 A）“故意侵害身体（battery）”（Battery）诉讼旨在赔偿原告遭受的身体伤害。\n\n但由于邻居并未与该男子发生有害或冒犯性的身体接触，该男子无法就故意侵害身体（battery）行为获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）“侵占动产”（Conversion）诉讼旨在赔偿原告因丧失动产（即动产灭失）而遭受的损失，赔偿金额为动产的公平市场价值。\n\n尽管该男子可以因邻居射杀其猫（导致动产丧失）而在侵占动产诉讼中胜诉，但他只能获得该猫的公平市场价值——即 25 美元。\n\n（选项 D）“侵犯动产”（Trespass to chattel）诉讼旨在赔偿原告动产遭受的实际损害。\n\n在本案中，邻居在杀死该男子的猫时构成了对动产的侵犯（即动产受损）。\n\n但由于该猫原值 25 美元，现已毫无价值，该男子只能获得 25 美元的赔偿。\n\n教育目标：“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）诉讼旨在赔偿原告遭受的精神伤害——即使没有身体伤害的证据——只要原告能证明被告：(1) 实施了极端且令人发指的行为；以及 (2) 故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第46条（美国法律学会，2012年）（故意或鲁莽造成精神伤害）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）这一诉因旨在赔偿原告遭受的精神伤害——即使没有身体伤害的证据——只要原告能证明被告：(1) 实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为；以及 (2) 故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。","iracZh":{"issue":"在该男子针对邻居提起的诉讼中，下列哪项诉讼主张最有可能获得数额最大的补偿性损害赔偿？","rule":"“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）这一诉因旨在赔偿原告遭受的精神伤害——即使没有身体伤害的证据——只要原告能证明被告：(1) 实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为；以及 (2) 故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。","analysis":"极端且令人发指的行为 公然的不当行为；利用已知的特殊脆弱性；滥用职权；反复骚扰。记忆口诀：FEAR（恐惧）。 补偿性损害赔偿旨在弥补原告遭受的实际损害（例如医疗费、工资损失、维修费用）。\n\n通常，原告必须证明存在身体伤害才能获得针对精神伤害的赔偿。\n\n然而，在“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）的诉讼中，如果原告能证明被告实施了极端且令人发指的行为（例如针对原告已知特殊脆弱性的行为），并且故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦，则原告无需证明存在身体伤害即可获得精神痛苦的补偿性赔偿。\n\n在本案中，邻居在当着该男子的面射杀其猫时，已知晓该男子对这只猫有着深厚的感情（即“特殊脆弱性”），且该射杀行为属于“极端且令人发指的行为”。\n\n邻居在实施该行为时，要么明知其行为会伤害该男子（即“故意”），要么无视了造成伤害的风险（即“鲁莽”）。\n\n鉴于该行为给该男子造成了巨大的精神伤害（即“严重精神痛苦”），他无需证明存在身体伤害，即可就该精神痛苦获得补偿性赔偿——其金额超过了猫本身的价值。\n\n（选项 A）“故意侵害身体（battery）”（Battery）诉讼旨在赔偿原告遭受的身体伤害。\n\n但由于邻居并未与该男子发生有害或冒犯性的身体接触，该男子无法就故意侵害身体（battery）行为获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）“侵占动产”（Conversion）诉讼旨在赔偿原告因丧失动产（即动产灭失）而遭受的损失，赔偿金额为动产的公平市场价值。\n\n尽管该男子可以因邻居射杀其猫（导致动产丧失）而在侵占动产诉讼中胜诉，但他只能获得该猫的公平市场价值——即 25 美元。\n\n（选项 D）“侵犯动产”（Trespass to chattel）诉讼旨在赔偿原告动产遭受的实际损害。\n\n在本案中，邻居在杀死该男子的猫时构成了对动产的侵犯（即动产受损）。\n\n但由于该猫原值 25 美元，现已毫无价值，该男子只能获得 25 美元的赔偿。\n\n教育目标：“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）诉讼旨在赔偿原告遭受的精神伤害——即使没有身体伤害的证据——只要原告能证明被告：(1) 实施了极端且令人发指的行为；以及 (2) 故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第46条（美国法律学会，2012年）（故意或鲁莽造成精神伤害）。","conclusion":"答案 C：故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）（Intentional infliction of emotional distress）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"recklessness","ipa":"/ɹˈɛkləsnəs/","syllables":"reck·less·ness","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[法] 鲁莽, 粗心大意","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“recklessness”","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"In doing so, the neighbor either knew his conduct would harm the man (intent) or disregarded the risk of harm (recklessness).","synonyms":["foolhardiness","rashness"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":21,"sourceQuestionPage":61,"sourceQuestionPages":[61],"sourceExplanationPages":[62,63],"sourceAnswerPages":[62,63],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-022","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A basketball player suffered a serious injury while participating in an impromptu basketball game at a public park. The injury occurred when the player and an opponent each tried to obtain possession of the ball when it rebounded from the backboard after a missed shot at the basket. During that encounter, the opponent swung his elbow to strike the player and injured him. The player now seeks compensation from the opponent. At the trial, evidence was introduced tending to prove that the game had been rough from the beginning, that elbows and knees had frequently been used to discourage interference by opposing players, and that the player had been one of those making liberal use of such tactics. In an action by the player against the opponent for battery, will the player likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the opponent did not intentionally use force that exceeded the player's consent.","B":"No, because the player impliedly consented to rough play.","C":"Yes, because the opponent intended to cause a harmful or offensive contact with the player.","D":"Yes, because the opponent intended to strike the player with his elbow."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Battery occurs when a defendant intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension of such contact) and causes contact that harms or offends the plaintiff. Consent (actual, apparent, or implied) is a defense to battery. Apparent consent exists when a reasonable person would have understood the plaintiff's conduct or custom—ie, accepted behavior in the community—to indicate consent. However, consent is ineffective if the defendant's conduct exceeds the scope of consent. Here, the opponent intentionally struck the player with his elbow, causing an injury (battery) (Choices C & D). However, the opponent reasonably understood that the player had consented to kneeing and elbowing that customarily occurred during the game (apparent consent) and the opponent's conduct (elbowing) did not exceed the scope of the player's apparent consent. Therefore, the opponent is not liable for battery. (Choice B) It is only implied that the player consented to customary rough play (eg, kneeing and elbowing)—not all rough play. Therefore, this question turns on whether the opponent's rough play was intentional and whether it exceeded the scope of the player's consent.\n\nEducational objective: Apparent consent is a defense to battery when consent can be reasonably implied from the plaintiff's conduct or from custom. However, consent is only a defense when the defendant's conduct falls within the scope of the plaintiff's consent.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 18 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by offensive contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 892A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (effect of consent).","educationalObjective":"Apparent consent is a defense to battery when consent can be reasonably implied from the plaintiff's conduct or from custom. However, consent is only a defense when the defendant's conduct falls within the scope of the plaintiff's consent.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 18 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by offensive contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 892A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (effect of consent).","rule":"Apparent consent is a defense to battery when consent can be reasonably implied from the plaintiff's conduct or from custom. However, consent is only a defense when the defendant's conduct falls within the scope of the plaintiff's consent.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the player against the opponent for battery, will the player likely prevail?","rule":"Apparent consent is a defense to battery when consent can be reasonably implied from the plaintiff's conduct or from custom. However, consent is only a defense when the defendant's conduct falls within the scope of the plaintiff's consent.","analysis":"Battery occurs when a defendant intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension of such contact) and causes contact that harms or offends the plaintiff. Consent (actual, apparent, or implied) is a defense to battery. Apparent consent exists when a reasonable person would have understood the plaintiff's conduct or custom—ie, accepted behavior in the community—to indicate consent. However, consent is ineffective if the defendant's conduct exceeds the scope of consent. Here, the opponent intentionally struck the player with his elbow, causing an injury (battery) (Choices C & D). However, the opponent reasonably understood that the player had consented to kneeing and elbowing that customarily occurred during the game (apparent consent) and the opponent's conduct (elbowing) did not exceed the scope of the player's apparent consent. Therefore, the opponent is not liable for battery. (Choice B) It is only implied that the player consented to customary rough play (eg, kneeing and elbowing)—not all rough play. Therefore, this question turns on whether the opponent's rough play was intentional and whether it exceeded the scope of the player's consent.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the opponent did not intentionally use force that exceeded the player's consent."},"questionZh":"一名篮球运动员在公园参加一场即兴篮球比赛时严重受伤。\n\n事发时，该球员和一名对手争抢篮板反弹回来的球。\n\n在争抢过程中，对手挥肘击打该球员，导致其受伤。\n\n该球员现向对手寻求赔偿。\n\n庭审中，有证据表明比赛从一开始就十分激烈，双方球员频繁使用肘击和膝击来阻止对方干扰，而该球员本人也经常使用此类动作。\n\n如果该球员以故意侵害身体（battery）罪起诉对手，他是否可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为对手并非故意使用超出该球员同意范围的武力。","B":"否，因为该球员默示同意了这种粗暴的比赛方式。","C":"是，因为对手意图对该球员造成有害或冒犯性的身体接触。","D":"是的，因为对方球员意图用肘部击打球员。"},"explanationZh":"当被告意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并实际导致了伤害或冒犯原告的接触时，即构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为。\n\n同意（无论是实际同意、表面同意还是默示同意）均可作为针对故意侵害身体（battery）指控的抗辩理由。\n\n当一个理性人根据原告的行为或惯例（即社区内普遍接受的行为）会将其理解为表示同意时，即存在“表面同意”。\n\n然而，如果被告的行为超出了同意的范围，则该同意无效。\n\n在本案中，对手故意用肘部击打该球员，造成了伤害（构成故意侵害身体（battery））（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n然而，对手有理由认为该球员已同意比赛中惯常发生的膝撞和肘击行为（表面同意），且对手的行为（肘击）并未超出该球员表面同意的范围。\n\n因此，对手无需对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担法律责任。\n\n（选项 B）球员仅被视为默示同意了惯常的粗野动作（如膝撞和肘击），而非同意所有形式的粗野动作。\n\n因此，本题的关键在于对手的粗野动作是否出于故意，以及该动作是否超出了球员同意的范围。\n\n教育目标：当根据原告的行为或惯例可以合理推断出同意时，“表面同意”可作为故意侵害身体（battery）侵权指控的抗辩理由。\n\n然而，只有当被告的行为处于原告同意的范围内时，同意才能构成有效的抗辩。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 18 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因冒犯性接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 892A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（同意的效力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当原告的行为或习惯可以合理推断出对方同意时，表见同意可以作为故意侵害身体（battery）罪的抗辩理由。\n\n但是，只有当被告的行为在原告同意的范围内时，表见同意才能作为抗辩理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果球员起诉对方球员故意侵害身体（battery），球员胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"当原告的行为或习惯可以合理推断出对方同意时，表见同意可以作为故意侵害身体（battery）罪的抗辩理由。\n\n但是，只有当被告的行为在原告同意的范围内时，表见同意才能作为抗辩理由。","analysis":"当被告意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并实际导致了伤害或冒犯原告的接触时，即构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为。\n\n同意（无论是实际同意、表面同意还是默示同意）均可作为针对故意侵害身体（battery）指控的抗辩理由。\n\n当一个理性人根据原告的行为或惯例（即社区内普遍接受的行为）会将其理解为表示同意时，即存在“表面同意”。\n\n然而，如果被告的行为超出了同意的范围，则该同意无效。\n\n在本案中，对手故意用肘部击打该球员，造成了伤害（构成故意侵害身体（battery））（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n然而，对手有理由认为该球员已同意比赛中惯常发生的膝撞和肘击行为（表面同意），且对手的行为（肘击）并未超出该球员表面同意的范围。\n\n因此，对手无需对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担法律责任。\n\n（选项 B）球员仅被视为默示同意了惯常的粗野动作（如膝撞和肘击），而非同意所有形式的粗野动作。\n\n因此，本题的关键在于对手的粗野动作是否出于故意，以及该动作是否超出了球员同意的范围。\n\n教育目标：当根据原告的行为或惯例可以合理推断出同意时，“表面同意”可作为故意侵害身体（battery）侵权指控的抗辩理由。\n\n然而，只有当被告的行为处于原告同意的范围内时，同意才能构成有效的抗辩。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 18 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因冒犯性接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 892A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（同意的效力）。","conclusion":"答案 A：否，因为对手并非故意使用超出该球员同意范围的武力。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-921890e7008cd023"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":22,"sourceQuestionPage":64,"sourceQuestionPages":[64],"sourceExplanationPages":[65,66],"sourceAnswerPages":[65,66],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-023","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"While a woman was riding her horse on what she thought was a public path, the owner of a house next to the path approached her while shaking a stick and shouting, \"Get off my property.\" Unknown to the woman, the path on which she was riding crossed the owner's private property. When the woman explained that she thought the path was a public trail, the owner cursed her, approached her horse, and struck the horse with his stick. As a result of the blow, the horse reared, causing the woman to fear that she would fall. However, the woman managed to stay on her horse, and then departed. Neither the woman nor the horse suffered bodily harm. In an action against the owner for damages, will the woman likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the owner was privileged to exclude trespassers from his property.","B":"No, because the woman suffered no physical harm.","C":"Yes, for battery and assault.","D":"Yes, for trespass to chattels."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Battery occurs when a defendant: intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof) and\n\ncauses harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff's body or an object intimately associated with the plaintiff's body (eg, clothing, dog on a leash).\n\nIn contrast, assault occurs when a defendant: intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof) and causes the plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent contact. Here, the owner intentionally struck the woman's horse with a stick, which (1) caused harmful contact with the woman's horse (battery) and (2) caused the woman to fear that she was about to fall (assault). And though a plaintiff cannot recover twice for the same injury or harm (ie, no double recovery), the owner's act of striking the horse caused the woman two distinct harms (contact and apprehension). Therefore, the woman can recover for battery AND assault. (Choice A) A landowner is privileged to use reasonable force to stop an unexcused trespass if the landowner (1) first asks the trespasser to leave and (2) reasonably believes that force is necessary to remove the trespasser. Although the owner told the woman to get off his property, there is no evidence that force was necessary to remove her (eg, had she told the owner she was staying). Therefore, the owner could not use force to exclude the woman. (Choice B) The harm requirements for battery (harmful or offensive contact) and assault (apprehension of contact) can be satisfied without proof of bodily harm. Therefore, the woman's lack of physical harm will not prevent her from recovering damages. (Choice D) Trespass to chattels (intentional interference with another's possession of tangible property) requires proof of actual damages—eg, impairment of the physical condition, quality, or value of the chattel. Since there is no evidence that the woman's horse was impaired (eg, unable to walk), she will not prevail on this claim.\n\nEducational objective: While the intent requirements for battery and assault are similar, the harm requirements for battery (harmful or offensive contact) and assault (apprehension of contact) are distinct.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 18 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by offensive contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of assault).","educationalObjective":"While the intent requirements for battery and assault are similar, the harm requirements for battery (harmful or offensive contact) and assault (apprehension of contact) are distinct.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 18 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by offensive contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of assault).","rule":"While the intent requirements for battery and assault are similar, the harm requirements for battery (harmful or offensive contact) and assault (apprehension of contact) are distinct.","irac":{"issue":"In an action against the owner for damages, will the woman likely prevail?","rule":"While the intent requirements for battery and assault are similar, the harm requirements for battery (harmful or offensive contact) and assault (apprehension of contact) are distinct.","analysis":"Battery occurs when a defendant: intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof) and\n\ncauses harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff's body or an object intimately associated with the plaintiff's body (eg, clothing, dog on a leash).\n\nIn contrast, assault occurs when a defendant: intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof) and causes the plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent contact. Here, the owner intentionally struck the woman's horse with a stick, which (1) caused harmful contact with the woman's horse (battery) and (2) caused the woman to fear that she was about to fall (assault). And though a plaintiff cannot recover twice for the same injury or harm (ie, no double recovery), the owner's act of striking the horse caused the woman two distinct harms (contact and apprehension). Therefore, the woman can recover for battery AND assault. (Choice A) A landowner is privileged to use reasonable force to stop an unexcused trespass if the landowner (1) first asks the trespasser to leave and (2) reasonably believes that force is necessary to remove the trespasser. Although the owner told the woman to get off his property, there is no evidence that force was necessary to remove her (eg, had she told the owner she was staying). Therefore, the owner could not use force to exclude the woman. (Choice B) The harm requirements for battery (harmful or offensive contact) and assault (apprehension of contact) can be satisfied without proof of bodily harm. Therefore, the woman's lack of physical harm will not prevent her from recovering damages. (Choice D) Trespass to chattels (intentional interference with another's possession of tangible property) requires proof of actual damages—eg, impairment of the physical condition, quality, or value of the chattel. Since there is no evidence that the woman's horse was impaired (eg, unable to walk), she will not prevail on this claim.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, for battery and assault."},"questionZh":"一名女子骑马行经一条她误以为是公共道路的路径时，路旁一处房产的主人挥舞着棍棒向她走来，并大喊：“离开我的地盘。”该女子并不知道，她骑行的这条路径穿过了该主人的私人领地。\n\n当女子解释说她以为这是条公共小径时，主人辱骂了她，并走近她的马，用棍棒击打马匹。\n\n这一击导致马匹受惊扬起前蹄，令女子担心自己会摔落马下。\n\n不过，女子最终稳住了身形，没有坠马，随后离开了现场。\n\n女子和马匹均未受到身体伤害。\n\n若该女子起诉房主索赔，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会胜诉，因为房主有权将侵入者驱逐出其私人领地。","B":"不会胜诉，因为该女子未受身体伤害。","C":"会胜诉，理由是遭受了故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）和人身威胁（assault）。","D":"会胜诉，理由是侵害动产（trespass to chattels）（trespass to chattels）。"},"explanationZh":"“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为发生于被告：意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并且 导致了与原告身体或与原告身体紧密相关的物体（例如衣物、牵引绳牵着的狗）发生有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n相比之下，“袭击”（assault）发生于被告：意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并导致原告合理地预感到接触即将发生。\n\n在本案中，马主用棍子故意击打该女子的马，这（1）导致了与该女子马匹的有害接触（构成故意侵害身体（battery）），并且（2）导致该女子担心自己即将坠马（构成袭击）。\n\n尽管原告不能就同一伤害或损害获得双重赔偿（即禁止双重获偿），但马主击打马匹的行为给该女子造成了两种截然不同的损害（身体接触和恐惧感）。\n\n因此，该女子既可以就故意侵害身体（battery）行为，也可以就袭击行为获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）如果土地所有者（1）先要求侵入者离开，并且（2）有理由相信必须使用武力才能驱逐侵入者，那么他有权使用合理的武力来制止无正当理由的侵入行为。\n\n尽管马主曾要求该女子离开其土地，但没有证据表明必须使用武力才能将其驱逐（例如，该女子并未表示拒绝离开）。\n\n因此，马主无权使用武力将该女子驱逐。\n\n（选项 B）构成故意侵害身体（battery）（有害或冒犯性接触）和袭击（预感到接触）所需的损害要件，无需证明存在身体上的伤害即可满足。\n\n因此，该女子未受身体伤害这一事实并不妨碍她获得损害赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）侵犯动产（故意干扰他人对有形财产的占有）要求证明存在实际损害——例如，动产的物理状况、质量或价值受损。\n\n由于没有证据表明该女子的马匹受到了损害（例如，无法行走），因此她无法在这一诉讼主张中胜诉。\n\n教育目标：虽然故意侵害身体（battery）和袭击在主观意图方面的要求相似，但两者在损害要件方面截然不同：故意侵害身体（battery）要求发生有害或冒犯性的接触，而袭击则要求产生对接触即将发生的恐惧感。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第18条（美国法律协会，1965年）（因冒犯性接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第21条（美国法律协会，1965年）（袭击的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"虽然故意侵害身体（battery）和人身威胁在主观意图方面的要求相似，但两者在损害后果方面的要求截然不同：故意侵害身体（battery）要求发生“有害或冒犯性的接触”，而人身威胁则要求受害者“预感到即将发生接触”。","iracZh":{"issue":"若该女子起诉房主索赔，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"虽然故意侵害身体（battery）和人身威胁在主观意图方面的要求相似，但两者在损害后果方面的要求截然不同：故意侵害身体（battery）要求发生“有害或冒犯性的接触”，而人身威胁则要求受害者“预感到即将发生接触”。","analysis":"“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为发生于被告：意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并且 导致了与原告身体或与原告身体紧密相关的物体（例如衣物、牵引绳牵着的狗）发生有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n相比之下，“袭击”（assault）发生于被告：意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并导致原告合理地预感到接触即将发生。\n\n在本案中，马主用棍子故意击打该女子的马，这（1）导致了与该女子马匹的有害接触（构成故意侵害身体（battery）），并且（2）导致该女子担心自己即将坠马（构成袭击）。\n\n尽管原告不能就同一伤害或损害获得双重赔偿（即禁止双重获偿），但马主击打马匹的行为给该女子造成了两种截然不同的损害（身体接触和恐惧感）。\n\n因此，该女子既可以就故意侵害身体（battery）行为，也可以就袭击行为获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）如果土地所有者（1）先要求侵入者离开，并且（2）有理由相信必须使用武力才能驱逐侵入者，那么他有权使用合理的武力来制止无正当理由的侵入行为。\n\n尽管马主曾要求该女子离开其土地，但没有证据表明必须使用武力才能将其驱逐（例如，该女子并未表示拒绝离开）。\n\n因此，马主无权使用武力将该女子驱逐。\n\n（选项 B）构成故意侵害身体（battery）（有害或冒犯性接触）和袭击（预感到接触）所需的损害要件，无需证明存在身体上的伤害即可满足。\n\n因此，该女子未受身体伤害这一事实并不妨碍她获得损害赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）侵犯动产（故意干扰他人对有形财产的占有）要求证明存在实际损害——例如，动产的物理状况、质量或价值受损。\n\n由于没有证据表明该女子的马匹受到了损害（例如，无法行走），因此她无法在这一诉讼主张中胜诉。\n\n教育目标：虽然故意侵害身体（battery）和袭击在主观意图方面的要求相似，但两者在损害要件方面截然不同：故意侵害身体（battery）要求发生有害或冒犯性的接触，而袭击则要求产生对接触即将发生的恐惧感。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第18条（美国法律协会，1965年）（因冒犯性接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第21条（美国法律协会，1965年）（袭击的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会胜诉，理由是遭受了故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）和人身威胁（assault）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-32cf692379394326"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":23,"sourceQuestionPage":67,"sourceQuestionPages":[67],"sourceExplanationPages":[68,69],"sourceAnswerPages":[68,69],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-024","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"As an encyclopedia salesman approached the grounds on which a woman's house was situated, the salesman saw a sign that said, \"No salesmen. Trespassers will be prosecuted. Proceed at your own risk.\" Although the salesman had not been invited to enter, he ignored the sign and drove up the driveway toward the house. As he rounded a curve, a powerful explosive charge buried in the driveway exploded, and the salesman was injured. Is the salesman likely to recover damages from the woman for his injuries?","choices":{"A":"No, because the salesman ignored the sign, which warned him against proceeding further.","B":"No, if the woman reasonably feared that intruders would come and harm her or her family.","C":"Yes, if the woman was responsible for the explosive charge under the driveway.","D":"Yes, unless the woman, when she planted the charge, intended only to deter, not to harm, a possible intruder."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Defenses & privileges to intentional torts\n\nDefense of Use of reasonable force to prevent or stop trespass Property\n\nDefense of Use of force reasonably necessary to prevent harm to third party Others\n\nParental Parent, guardian, or teacher may use reasonable force to discipline authority children\n\nConsent Actual – plaintiff willingly submits to contact Apparent – consent understood by plaintiff's conduct or custom Implied – plaintiff cannot consent & emergency action necessary to prevent serious harm Arrest Misdemeanor – plaintiff breached the peace in defendant's presence Felony – felony committed & defendant reasonably suspects plaintiff committed that felony Necessity Public – intrusion actually or apparently necessary to avert imminent public disaster Private – intrusion actually or apparently necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property Self-defense Use of reasonable force to prevent harm to self\n\nMnemonic: POPCANS\n\nA defendant is liable for battery if he/she intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof) and causes such contact with the plaintiff. Intent for battery exists when the defendant acts with either: purpose – the desire to cause contact or imminent apprehension of contact or knowledge – the substantial certainty that contact or imminent apprehension of contact will result. Here, the salesman was injured by an explosive charge that detonated in the woman's driveway (harmful contact). And since the woman would have been substantially certain that the charge would contact a person driving over it had she planted it under the driveway (intent), the salesman would likely recover damages for battery absent some defense. (Choice A) Assumption of the risk (the plaintiff's voluntary acceptance of a known risk of harm) is an affirmative defense to negligence or strict liability, but not intentional torts like\n\nbattery. Therefore, this defense does not apply here. Even if it did, the salesman did not knowingly or voluntarily accept the risk of harm because the woman's sign only warned of prosecution—not explosion. (Choice B) A person is privileged to use reasonable force to defend his/her property against trespassers. However, deadly force (eg, explosive charge) is only reasonable if the defendant reasonably believes that the trespasser is about to inflict death or serious bodily harm. Here, there was no such evidence, so the woman was not privileged to use deadly force. (Choice D) Battery requires the intent to inflict harmful / offensive contact or the immediate apprehension of such contact. Therefore, the woman would be liable for battery even if she only intended to deter possible intruders.\n\nEducational objective: Battery liability arises when a defendant intentionally (ie, purposefully or knowingly) inflicted harmful or offensive contact or (imminent apprehension thereof) and caused such contact with the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).","educationalObjective":"Battery liability arises when a defendant intentionally (ie, purposefully or knowingly) inflicted harmful or offensive contact or (imminent apprehension thereof) and caused such contact with the plaintiff.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).","rule":"Battery liability arises when a defendant intentionally (ie, purposefully or knowingly) inflicted harmful or offensive contact or (imminent apprehension thereof) and caused such contact with the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"Is the salesman likely to recover damages from the woman for his injuries?","rule":"Battery liability arises when a defendant intentionally (ie, purposefully or knowingly) inflicted harmful or offensive contact or (imminent apprehension thereof) and caused such contact with the plaintiff.","analysis":"Defenses & privileges to intentional torts\n\nDefense of Use of reasonable force to prevent or stop trespass Property\n\nDefense of Use of force reasonably necessary to prevent harm to third party Others\n\nParental Parent, guardian, or teacher may use reasonable force to discipline authority children\n\nConsent Actual – plaintiff willingly submits to contact Apparent – consent understood by plaintiff's conduct or custom Implied – plaintiff cannot consent & emergency action necessary to prevent serious harm Arrest Misdemeanor – plaintiff breached the peace in defendant's presence Felony – felony committed & defendant reasonably suspects plaintiff committed that felony Necessity Public – intrusion actually or apparently necessary to avert imminent public disaster Private – intrusion actually or apparently necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property Self-defense Use of reasonable force to prevent harm to self\n\nMnemonic: POPCANS\n\nA defendant is liable for battery if he/she intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof) and causes such contact with the plaintiff. Intent for battery exists when the defendant acts with either: purpose – the desire to cause contact or imminent apprehension of contact or knowledge – the substantial certainty that contact or imminent apprehension of contact will result. Here, the salesman was injured by an explosive charge that detonated in the woman's driveway (harmful contact). And since the woman would have been substantially certain that the charge would contact a person driving over it had she planted it under the driveway (intent), the salesman would likely recover damages for battery absent some defense. (Choice A) Assumption of the risk (the plaintiff's voluntary acceptance of a known risk of harm) is an affirmative defense to negligence or strict liability, but not intentional torts like\n\nbattery. Therefore, this defense does not apply here. Even if it did, the salesman did not knowingly or voluntarily accept the risk of harm because the woman's sign only warned of prosecution—not explosion. (Choice B) A person is privileged to use reasonable force to defend his/her property against trespassers. However, deadly force (eg, explosive charge) is only reasonable if the defendant reasonably believes that the trespasser is about to inflict death or serious bodily harm. Here, there was no such evidence, so the woman was not privileged to use deadly force. (Choice D) Battery requires the intent to inflict harmful / offensive contact or the immediate apprehension of such contact. Therefore, the woman would be liable for battery even if she only intended to deter possible intruders.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, if the woman was responsible for the explosive charge under the driveway."},"questionZh":"一位百科全书推销员来到一位女士家门前，看到一块牌子上写着：“禁止推销。擅入者将被起诉。后果自负。”尽管推销员并未被邀请进入，但他无视了牌子，驾车驶入车道，朝房子驶去。\n\n当他转弯时，埋在车道下的一枚威力强大的炸药爆炸，推销员受伤。\n\n推销员有可能就其受伤向该女士索赔吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为推销员无视了警告他不要继续前进的牌子。","B":"不可能，如果该女士有理由担心会有入侵者来伤害她或她的家人。","C":"有可能，如果该女士应对车道下的炸药负责。","D":"有可能，除非该女士在放置炸药时，其目的只是为了阻止而非伤害可能的入侵者。"},"explanationZh":"针对故意侵权行为的抗辩与特权 针对侵犯财产的抗辩：使用合理武力以防止或制止侵入行为。 针对涉及他人的抗辩：使用合理必要的武力以防止第三人受到伤害。 父母/监护人/教师的管教权：可使用合理武力管教儿童。 同意：实际同意（原告自愿接受接触）；表见同意（根据原告的行为或惯例可理解为同意）；默示同意（原告无法表达同意，且需采取紧急行动以防止严重伤害）。 逮捕：轻罪（原告在被告面前扰乱治安）；重罪（确已发生重罪，且被告有合理理由怀疑原告实施了该重罪）。 紧急避险：公共紧急避险（为避免迫在眉睫的公共灾难，实际上或表面上必须进行的侵入行为）；私人紧急避险（为保护自己、第三人或财产，实际上或表面上必须进行的侵入行为）。 自卫：使用合理武力防止自身受到伤害。 记忆口诀：POPCANS 如果被告意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并导致了与原告的此类接触，则被告须对故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）行为承担责任。\n\n构成故意侵害身体（battery）的意图存在于被告具有以下任一心态时：目的（意欲造成接触或使人产生接触即将发生的恐惧感）或明知（确信会导致接触或使人产生接触即将发生的恐惧感）。\n\n在本案中，推销员因女子车道上引爆的爆炸装置而受伤（属于有害接触）。\n\n鉴于如果女子将装置埋设在车道下，她应当确信该装置会接触到驾车经过的人（具备意图），因此，若无其他抗辩理由，推销员很可能就故意侵害身体（battery）行为获得损害赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”（原告自愿接受已知的伤害风险）是针对过失侵权或严格责任的抗辩理由，但不适用于故意侵害身体（battery）等故意侵权行为。\n\n因此，该抗辩在此不适用。\n\n即使适用，推销员也并未明知或自愿接受伤害风险，因为该女子的告示仅警告会面临起诉，并未提及爆炸风险。\n\n（选项 B）个人有权使用合理武力保护其财产免受侵入者侵害。\n\n然而，仅在被告有合理理由相信侵入者即将造成死亡或严重身体伤害的情况下，使用致命武力（如爆炸装置）才是合理的。\n\n本案中并无此类证据，因此该女子无权使用致命武力。\n\n(选项 D) 构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为，需要具备实施有害或冒犯性接触的意图，或者具备使受害者即刻预感到此类接触将要发生的意图。\n\n因此，即使该女子仅意在威慑潜在的入侵者，她仍须对“故意侵害身体（battery）”行为承担法律责任。\n\n教学目标：当被告有意（即蓄意或明知）实施有害或冒犯性接触（或使原告即刻预感到此类接触），并导致原告遭受此类接触时，即产生“故意侵害身体（battery）”侵权责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当被告故意（即蓄意或明知）对原告造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或使原告感到即将遭受此类接触，并导致该接触时，即构成故意侵害身体（battery）责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"该销售员是否有可能就其所受伤害向该女子索赔？","rule":"当被告故意（即蓄意或明知）对原告造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或使原告感到即将遭受此类接触，并导致该接触时，即构成故意侵害身体（battery）责任。","analysis":"针对故意侵权行为的抗辩与特权 针对侵犯财产的抗辩：使用合理武力以防止或制止侵入行为。 针对涉及他人的抗辩：使用合理必要的武力以防止第三人受到伤害。 父母/监护人/教师的管教权：可使用合理武力管教儿童。 同意：实际同意（原告自愿接受接触）；表见同意（根据原告的行为或惯例可理解为同意）；默示同意（原告无法表达同意，且需采取紧急行动以防止严重伤害）。 逮捕：轻罪（原告在被告面前扰乱治安）；重罪（确已发生重罪，且被告有合理理由怀疑原告实施了该重罪）。 紧急避险：公共紧急避险（为避免迫在眉睫的公共灾难，实际上或表面上必须进行的侵入行为）；私人紧急避险（为保护自己、第三人或财产，实际上或表面上必须进行的侵入行为）。 自卫：使用合理武力防止自身受到伤害。 记忆口诀：POPCANS 如果被告意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并导致了与原告的此类接触，则被告须对故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）行为承担责任。\n\n构成故意侵害身体（battery）的意图存在于被告具有以下任一心态时：目的（意欲造成接触或使人产生接触即将发生的恐惧感）或明知（确信会导致接触或使人产生接触即将发生的恐惧感）。\n\n在本案中，推销员因女子车道上引爆的爆炸装置而受伤（属于有害接触）。\n\n鉴于如果女子将装置埋设在车道下，她应当确信该装置会接触到驾车经过的人（具备意图），因此，若无其他抗辩理由，推销员很可能就故意侵害身体（battery）行为获得损害赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”（原告自愿接受已知的伤害风险）是针对过失侵权或严格责任的抗辩理由，但不适用于故意侵害身体（battery）等故意侵权行为。\n\n因此，该抗辩在此不适用。\n\n即使适用，推销员也并未明知或自愿接受伤害风险，因为该女子的告示仅警告会面临起诉，并未提及爆炸风险。\n\n（选项 B）个人有权使用合理武力保护其财产免受侵入者侵害。\n\n然而，仅在被告有合理理由相信侵入者即将造成死亡或严重身体伤害的情况下，使用致命武力（如爆炸装置）才是合理的。\n\n本案中并无此类证据，因此该女子无权使用致命武力。\n\n(选项 D) 构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为，需要具备实施有害或冒犯性接触的意图，或者具备使受害者即刻预感到此类接触将要发生的意图。\n\n因此，即使该女子仅意在威慑潜在的入侵者，她仍须对“故意侵害身体（battery）”行为承担法律责任。\n\n教学目标：当被告有意（即蓄意或明知）实施有害或冒犯性接触（或使原告即刻预感到此类接触），并导致原告遭受此类接触时，即产生“故意侵害身体（battery）”侵权责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 C：有可能，如果该女士应对车道下的炸药负责。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":24,"sourceQuestionPage":70,"sourceQuestionPages":[70],"sourceExplanationPages":[71,72],"sourceAnswerPages":[71,72],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-025","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"In a tavern, an intoxicated woman threatened to slash a man with a broken beer bottle she was holding. Another customer, who had not been threatened by the woman, forcefully grabbed the woman and locked her in the tavern's storeroom until the police could arrive. In the process, although the customer used reasonable force, the customer badly sprained the woman's wrist. Is the woman likely to recover in an action against the customer?","choices":{"A":"No, because the customer's conduct was privileged as a defense of others.","B":"Yes, based on battery only.","C":"Yes, based on false imprisonment only.","D":"Yes, based on both battery and false imprisonment."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Defense of others\n\nDefendant reasonably believes:\n\nplaintiff is about to inflict harmful or offensive contact upon person—ie, third party or plaintiff's own self person has, or would have had, right of self-defense and defendant's action is necessary to protect person The privilege to use reasonable force in the defense of others exists when the defendant reasonably believes that: the plaintiff is about to inflict harmful or offensive contact upon a third party the third party has the right of self-defense and the defendant's action is necessary to protect the third party. Here, the customer was privileged to grab the woman and lock her in the tavern's storeroom (reasonable force) to defend the man because the customer reasonably believed that: the woman was about to slash the man with the broken beer bottle in her hand the man had the right of self-defense and action was necessary to protect the man from the woman. Since the customer had the right to use reasonable force in defense of the man, the woman will not recover in an action against the customer. (Choices B & D) Battery occurs when a defendant intends to create contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) and causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Although the customer committed battery when he intentionally grabbed the woman and sprained her wrist, the customer is not liable because he was privileged to use reasonable force in the defense of another. (Choices C & D) False imprisonment occurs when the defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff within fixed boundaries and the plaintiff is aware of the confinement or harmed by it. Here, the customer falsely imprisoned the woman when he intentionally locked her in the storeroom, but the customer is not liable because his conduct was privileged.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant is privileged to use reasonable force in the defense of others when the defendant reasonably believes that (1) the plaintiff is about to inflict harmful or offensive contact upon a third party, (2) the third party has the right of self-defense, and (3) the defendant's action is necessary to protect the third party.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 76 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (defense of others).","educationalObjective":"A defendant is privileged to use reasonable force in the defense of others when the defendant reasonably believes that (1) the plaintiff is about to inflict harmful or offensive contact upon a third party, (2) the third party has the right of self-defense, and (3) the defendant's action is necessary to protect the third party.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 76 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (defense of others).","rule":"A defendant is privileged to use reasonable force in the defense of others when the defendant reasonably believes that (1) the plaintiff is about to inflict harmful or offensive contact upon a third party, (2) the third party has the right of self-defense, and (3) the defendant's action is necessary to protect the third party.","irac":{"issue":"Is the woman likely to recover in an action against the customer?","rule":"A defendant is privileged to use reasonable force in the defense of others when the defendant reasonably believes that (1) the plaintiff is about to inflict harmful or offensive contact upon a third party, (2) the third party has the right of self-defense, and (3) the defendant's action is necessary to protect the third party.","analysis":"Defense of others\n\nDefendant reasonably believes:\n\nplaintiff is about to inflict harmful or offensive contact upon person—ie, third party or plaintiff's own self person has, or would have had, right of self-defense and defendant's action is necessary to protect person The privilege to use reasonable force in the defense of others exists when the defendant reasonably believes that: the plaintiff is about to inflict harmful or offensive contact upon a third party the third party has the right of self-defense and the defendant's action is necessary to protect the third party. Here, the customer was privileged to grab the woman and lock her in the tavern's storeroom (reasonable force) to defend the man because the customer reasonably believed that: the woman was about to slash the man with the broken beer bottle in her hand the man had the right of self-defense and action was necessary to protect the man from the woman. Since the customer had the right to use reasonable force in defense of the man, the woman will not recover in an action against the customer. (Choices B & D) Battery occurs when a defendant intends to create contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) and causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Although the customer committed battery when he intentionally grabbed the woman and sprained her wrist, the customer is not liable because he was privileged to use reasonable force in the defense of another. (Choices C & D) False imprisonment occurs when the defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff within fixed boundaries and the plaintiff is aware of the confinement or harmed by it. Here, the customer falsely imprisoned the woman when he intentionally locked her in the storeroom, but the customer is not liable because his conduct was privileged.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the customer's conduct was privileged as a defense of others."},"questionZh":"在一家酒馆里，一名醉酒女子手持破啤酒瓶，威胁要割伤一名男子。\n\n另一位并未受到该女子威胁的顾客强行控制了她，并将她关在酒馆的储藏室里，直到警察赶到。\n\n在此过程中，尽管该顾客使用的是合理武力，但导致该女子的手腕严重扭伤。\n\n如果该女子对这名顾客提起诉讼，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为该顾客的行为属于“保护他人”的免责抗辩范畴。","B":"大，仅基于“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）这一理由。","C":"大，仅基于“非法拘禁”（false imprisonment）这一理由。","D":"大，同时基于“故意侵害身体（battery）”和“非法拘禁”这两个理由。"},"explanationZh":"保护他人（防卫他人） 被告有合理理由相信： 原告即将对某人（即第三方或原告自身）施加有害或冒犯性的接触；该人拥有（或本应拥有）自卫权，且被告的行动对于保护该人是必要的。当被告有合理理由相信以下情况时，即享有为保护他人而使用合理武力的特权：原告即将对第三方施加有害或冒犯性的接触；第三方拥有自卫权；且被告的行动对于保护第三方是必要的。\n\n在本案中，该顾客有权抓住该女子并将其锁在酒馆储藏室（属于合理武力），以保护那名男子，因为该顾客有合理理由相信：该女子正准备用手中的碎啤酒瓶割伤该男子；该男子拥有自卫权；且有必要采取行动保护该男子免受该女子的伤害。\n\n由于该顾客有权为保护该男子而使用合理武力，该女子在针对该顾客提起的诉讼中将无法获得胜诉。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）当被告有意造成接触（或使人产生即将发生接触的恐惧感）并导致与原告发生有害或冒犯性接触时，即构成故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）。\n\n尽管该顾客在有意抓住该女子并导致其手腕扭伤时实施了故意侵害身体（battery）行为，但他不承担法律责任，因为他享有为保护他人而使用合理武力的特权。\n\n（选项 C 和 D）当被告有意将原告限制在特定范围内，且原告意识到这种限制或因此受到伤害时，即构成非法监禁（false imprisonment）。\n\n在本案中，该顾客有意将该女子锁在储藏室的行为构成了非法监禁，但他不承担法律责任，因为其行为属于特权行为。\n\n教育目标：当被告有合理理由相信以下情况时，即享有为保护他人而使用合理武力的特权：(1) 原告即将对第三方施加有害或冒犯性的接触；(2) 第三方拥有自卫权；(3) 被告的行动对于保护第三方是必要的。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 76 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（保护他人）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当被告有合理理由相信以下情况时，其在保护他人时使用合理武力享有免责权：（1）原告即将对第三方实施有害或冒犯性的接触；（2）该第三方享有正当防卫权；以及（3）被告的行为对于保护该第三方是必要的。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该女子对这名顾客提起诉讼，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"当被告有合理理由相信以下情况时，其在保护他人时使用合理武力享有免责权：（1）原告即将对第三方实施有害或冒犯性的接触；（2）该第三方享有正当防卫权；以及（3）被告的行为对于保护该第三方是必要的。","analysis":"保护他人（防卫他人） 被告有合理理由相信： 原告即将对某人（即第三方或原告自身）施加有害或冒犯性的接触；该人拥有（或本应拥有）自卫权，且被告的行动对于保护该人是必要的。当被告有合理理由相信以下情况时，即享有为保护他人而使用合理武力的特权：原告即将对第三方施加有害或冒犯性的接触；第三方拥有自卫权；且被告的行动对于保护第三方是必要的。\n\n在本案中，该顾客有权抓住该女子并将其锁在酒馆储藏室（属于合理武力），以保护那名男子，因为该顾客有合理理由相信：该女子正准备用手中的碎啤酒瓶割伤该男子；该男子拥有自卫权；且有必要采取行动保护该男子免受该女子的伤害。\n\n由于该顾客有权为保护该男子而使用合理武力，该女子在针对该顾客提起的诉讼中将无法获得胜诉。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）当被告有意造成接触（或使人产生即将发生接触的恐惧感）并导致与原告发生有害或冒犯性接触时，即构成故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）。\n\n尽管该顾客在有意抓住该女子并导致其手腕扭伤时实施了故意侵害身体（battery）行为，但他不承担法律责任，因为他享有为保护他人而使用合理武力的特权。\n\n（选项 C 和 D）当被告有意将原告限制在特定范围内，且原告意识到这种限制或因此受到伤害时，即构成非法监禁（false imprisonment）。\n\n在本案中，该顾客有意将该女子锁在储藏室的行为构成了非法监禁，但他不承担法律责任，因为其行为属于特权行为。\n\n教育目标：当被告有合理理由相信以下情况时，即享有为保护他人而使用合理武力的特权：(1) 原告即将对第三方施加有害或冒犯性的接触；(2) 第三方拥有自卫权；(3) 被告的行动对于保护第三方是必要的。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 76 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（保护他人）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不大，因为该顾客的行为属于“保护他人”的免责抗辩范畴。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"defense of others","ipa":"/dɪfˈɛns ˈəv ˈəðɝz/","syllables":"de·fense of oth·ers","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"防卫他人：合理相信第三人面临侵害时使用相称武力","contextualMeaning":"防卫他人：合理相信第三人面临侵害时使用相称武力","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the customer's conduct was privileged as a defense of others.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"falsely","ipa":"/fˈɔlsli/","syllables":"falsely","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 虚伪地, 错误地, 不实地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“falsely”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Here, the customer falsely imprisoned the woman when he intentionally locked her in the storeroom, but the customer is not liable because his conduct was privileged.","synonyms":["incorrectly"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":25,"sourceQuestionPage":73,"sourceQuestionPages":[73],"sourceExplanationPages":[74,75],"sourceAnswerPages":[74,75],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-026","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A college student was asleep in his bed in a college dormitory when his roommate, in a drunken fury, entered their room intending to attack the student with an ice pick while he slept. The phone rang and awakened the student. The roommate retreated quickly and threw the ice pick under his own bed in the same room. The next day, the student heard from friends about the roommate's murderous plans and later found the ice pick under the roommate's bed. Even though the college expelled his roommate, the student remained extremely upset and afraid to sleep. In a suit against the roommate for assault, will the student prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the roommate did not touch the student.","B":"No, because the student was not awake when the roommate entered the room and was unaware until later that the roommate had intended to attack him.","C":"Yes, because it was reasonable for the student to feel afraid of sleeping in his room afterward.","D":"Yes, because the roommate intended to inflict serious harm."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Assault occurs when a defendant: intends to inflict (1) harmful or offensive contact or (2) imminent apprehension of such contact, and causes the plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent contact. Apprehension only exists when the plaintiff is aware of the defendant's act at the time it occurs. Here, the roommate intended to attack the student with an ice pick (harmful contact). However, the attempted attack did not cause the student apprehension of imminent contact because he was asleep and unaware of the attempted attack until the following day. Therefore, there was no assault. (Choice A) Assault only requires that the plaintiff be placed in apprehension of imminent contact. Actual bodily contact is not required, so the fact that the roommate never touched the student is irrelevant. (Choice C) For assault, there must be apprehension of imminent contact (without significant delay). Although the student's apprehension (afraid to sleep) may be reasonable, it developed after the failed attack. Therefore, the imminent apprehension requirement is not met. (Choice D) Proof of intent and harm is necessary for assault. Although the roommate had the requisite intent (to harm the student), he did not cause the requisite harm (apprehension of imminent contact).\n\nEducational objective: Assault requires proof of both intent AND harm. Harm for assault only exists when the plaintiff reasonably apprehends the defendant's intentional act at the time it occurs.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of assault).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (attempt to cause harm unknown to other).","educationalObjective":"Assault requires proof of both intent AND harm. Harm for assault only exists when the plaintiff reasonably apprehends the defendant's intentional act at the time it occurs.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of assault).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (attempt to cause harm unknown to other).","rule":"Assault requires proof of both intent AND harm. Harm for assault only exists when the plaintiff reasonably apprehends the defendant's intentional act at the time it occurs.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit against the roommate for assault, will the student prevail?","rule":"Assault requires proof of both intent AND harm. Harm for assault only exists when the plaintiff reasonably apprehends the defendant's intentional act at the time it occurs.","analysis":"Assault occurs when a defendant: intends to inflict (1) harmful or offensive contact or (2) imminent apprehension of such contact, and causes the plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent contact. Apprehension only exists when the plaintiff is aware of the defendant's act at the time it occurs. Here, the roommate intended to attack the student with an ice pick (harmful contact). However, the attempted attack did not cause the student apprehension of imminent contact because he was asleep and unaware of the attempted attack until the following day. Therefore, there was no assault. (Choice A) Assault only requires that the plaintiff be placed in apprehension of imminent contact. Actual bodily contact is not required, so the fact that the roommate never touched the student is irrelevant. (Choice C) For assault, there must be apprehension of imminent contact (without significant delay). Although the student's apprehension (afraid to sleep) may be reasonable, it developed after the failed attack. Therefore, the imminent apprehension requirement is not met. (Choice D) Proof of intent and harm is necessary for assault. Although the roommate had the requisite intent (to harm the student), he did not cause the requisite harm (apprehension of imminent contact).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the student was not awake when the roommate entered the room and was unaware until later that the roommate had intended to attack him."},"questionZh":"一名大学生在宿舍床上熟睡时，他的室友因醉酒而怒火中烧，闯入房间，企图趁其熟睡时用冰锥袭击他。\n\n此时电话铃声响起，将该学生惊醒。\n\n室友随即迅速撤退，并将冰锥扔到了同屋自己床铺的下方。\n\n次日，该学生从朋友处得知了室友的行凶企图，随后在室友床下发现了那把冰锥。\n\n尽管校方开除了该室友，但这名学生仍感到极度不安，甚至不敢睡觉。\n\n若该学生起诉室友犯有“袭击罪”（assault），他能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为室友并未接触到该学生。","B":"不能，因为室友进入房间时该学生处于熟睡状态，且直到事后才得知室友曾企图袭击自己。","C":"能，因为该学生事后对在宿舍睡觉感到恐惧是合理的。","D":"能，因为室友意图造成严重伤害。"},"explanationZh":"当被告意图造成 (1) 有害或冒犯性的接触，或 (2) 对此类接触的即时恐惧（即“即时接触的预期”），并导致原告对即时接触产生合理预期时，即构成侵权法上的“袭击”（assault）。\n\n只有当原告在被告行为发生时意识到该行为存在时，这种预期才会产生。\n\n在本案中，室友意图用冰镐使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）该学生（这属于有害接触）。\n\n然而，此次未遂使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）并未导致该学生产生即时接触的预期，因为他当时正在睡觉，直到第二天之前都未察觉到这次未遂使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）。\n\n因此，不构成袭击。\n\n（选项 A）袭击仅要求原告产生即时接触的预期。\n\n并不要求发生实际的身体接触，因此室友从未触碰过该学生这一事实在法律上无关紧要。\n\n（选项 C）构成袭击必须存在对即时接触的预期（且无显著的时间滞后）。\n\n尽管该学生产生的恐惧感（害怕睡觉）可能是合理的，但这种恐惧是在未遂使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）发生后才产生的。\n\n因此，不符合“即时预期”这一要件。\n\n（选项 D）证明意图和损害是构成袭击的必要条件。\n\n尽管室友具备了必要的意图（伤害该学生），但他并未造成必要的损害（即对即时接触的预期）。\n\n教学目标：袭击的构成需要同时证明意图和损害。\n\n就袭击而言，只有当原告在被告故意行为发生时合理地预见到该行为时，才存在法律意义上的损害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 21 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（袭击的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 22 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（意图造成损害但对方未察觉的情形）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"构成“袭击罪”既需要证明存在意图，也需要证明存在“伤害”。\n\n就袭击罪而言，只有当原告在被告实施故意行为的当下，合理地预感到该行为即将发生时，才构成法律意义上的“伤害”。","iracZh":{"issue":"若该学生起诉室友犯有“袭击罪”，他能胜诉吗？","rule":"构成“袭击罪”既需要证明存在意图，也需要证明存在“伤害”。\n\n就袭击罪而言，只有当原告在被告实施故意行为的当下，合理地预感到该行为即将发生时，才构成法律意义上的“伤害”。","analysis":"当被告意图造成 (1) 有害或冒犯性的接触，或 (2) 对此类接触的即时恐惧（即“即时接触的预期”），并导致原告对即时接触产生合理预期时，即构成侵权法上的“袭击”（assault）。\n\n只有当原告在被告行为发生时意识到该行为存在时，这种预期才会产生。\n\n在本案中，室友意图用冰镐使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）该学生（这属于有害接触）。\n\n然而，此次未遂使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）并未导致该学生产生即时接触的预期，因为他当时正在睡觉，直到第二天之前都未察觉到这次未遂使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）。\n\n因此，不构成袭击。\n\n（选项 A）袭击仅要求原告产生即时接触的预期。\n\n并不要求发生实际的身体接触，因此室友从未触碰过该学生这一事实在法律上无关紧要。\n\n（选项 C）构成袭击必须存在对即时接触的预期（且无显著的时间滞后）。\n\n尽管该学生产生的恐惧感（害怕睡觉）可能是合理的，但这种恐惧是在未遂使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）发生后才产生的。\n\n因此，不符合“即时预期”这一要件。\n\n（选项 D）证明意图和损害是构成袭击的必要条件。\n\n尽管室友具备了必要的意图（伤害该学生），但他并未造成必要的损害（即对即时接触的预期）。\n\n教学目标：袭击的构成需要同时证明意图和损害。\n\n就袭击而言，只有当原告在被告故意行为发生时合理地预见到该行为时，才存在法律意义上的损害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 21 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（袭击的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 22 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（意图造成损害但对方未察觉的情形）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不能，因为室友进入房间时该学生处于熟睡状态，且直到事后才得知室友曾企图袭击自己。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-6c18e43ac03f36b0"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":26,"sourceQuestionPage":76,"sourceQuestionPages":[76],"sourceExplanationPages":[77,78],"sourceAnswerPages":[77,78],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-027","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A landowner who owned a large tract of land in the mountains sought to protect a herd of wild deer that lived on part of the land. Although the landowner had posted signs that said, \"No Hunting—No Trespassing,\" hunters frequently intruded to kill the deer. Recently, the landowner built an eight-foot-high chain-link fence, topped by three strands of barbed wire, across a gully on her land that provided the only access to the area where the deer lived. A wildlife photographer asked the landowner for permission to enter the land to photograph the deer. Because the landowner feared that any publicity would encourage further intrusions by hunters, she denied the photographer's request. Frustrated, the photographer attempted to climb the fence. He became entangled in the barbed wire and suffered extensive lacerations. The wounds became infected and ultimately caused his death. The photographer's personal representative has sued the landowner. Is the personal representative likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the photographer entered the landowner's land after the landowner had refused him permission to do so and therefore was a trespasser.","B":"No, because the potential for harm created by the presence of the barbed wire was apparent.","C":"Yes, because the landowner had no property interest in the deer that entitled her to use force to protect them.","D":"Yes, because the landowner may not use deadly force to protect her land from intrusion."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Use of mechanical device to defend property\n\nType of device Examples Privileged use\n\nUnlikely to cause death or Barbed wire Reasonably necessary to protect against serious bodily harm fence intrusion Spiked wall not unreasonably dangerous and customarily used for this purpose or reasonable care taken to make use known Likely to cause death or Spring gun Reasonable belief that intruder threatens serious bodily harm Electric fence death or serious bodily injury Man-trap Almost every jurisdiction has enacted a survival statute that enables a decedent's personal representative to pursue tort claims on the decedent's behalf—so the photographer's personal representative can sue the landowner on the photographer's behalf. However, the landowner can assert that she was privileged to use reasonable force to defend her property from trespassers like the photographer. A mechanical device that is unlikely to cause death or serious bodily harm can be used for this purpose when: the use of such a device is reasonably necessary to protect the property from intrusion that particular device is not unreasonably dangerous and the device is customarily used for this purpose or reasonable care is taken to make its use known. Here, a barbed wire fence was reasonably necessary to protect the landowner's property since her \"No Trespassing—No Hunting\" signs were frequently disregarded. The fence was not unreasonably dangerous since an eight-foot-high fence topped with barbed wire threatens only the most determined of trespassers. And the potential for harm created by the barbed wire was apparent—not concealed. Therefore, the fence was a privileged means of protection, and the personal representative will not prevail. (Choice A) The photographer would have been a trespasser regardless of whether the landowner had refused him permission to enter. And since trespassers can recover for injuries caused by concealed or deadly devices (eg, spring guns), the photographer's trespasser status is not enough to defeat his personal representative's claim. (Choice C) Although the landowner had no property interest in the deer, she was entitled to use reasonable force to protect her property interest in the land. (Choice D) Deadly force may never be used merely to defend property. But barbed wire is not deadly force since it does not usually cause death or serious bodily harm.\n\nEducational objective: Nondeadly mechanical devices are a privileged means of protecting property when (1) such a device is reasonably necessary to prevent intrusions, (2) the device used is not unreasonably dangerous, and (3) the device is customarily used for this purpose or reasonable care is taken to make its use known.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 84 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (use of mechanical device to defend property).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 926 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (survival actions).","educationalObjective":"Nondeadly mechanical devices are a privileged means of protecting property when (1) such a device is reasonably necessary to prevent intrusions, (2) the device used is not unreasonably dangerous, and (3) the device is customarily used for this purpose or reasonable care is taken to make its use known.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 84 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (use of mechanical device to defend property).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 926 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (survival actions).","rule":"Nondeadly mechanical devices are a privileged means of protecting property when (1) such a device is reasonably necessary to prevent intrusions, (2) the device used is not unreasonably dangerous, and (3) the device is customarily used for this purpose or reasonable care is taken to make its use known.","irac":{"issue":"Is the personal representative likely to prevail?","rule":"Nondeadly mechanical devices are a privileged means of protecting property when (1) such a device is reasonably necessary to prevent intrusions, (2) the device used is not unreasonably dangerous, and (3) the device is customarily used for this purpose or reasonable care is taken to make its use known.","analysis":"Use of mechanical device to defend property\n\nType of device Examples Privileged use\n\nUnlikely to cause death or Barbed wire Reasonably necessary to protect against serious bodily harm fence intrusion Spiked wall not unreasonably dangerous and customarily used for this purpose or reasonable care taken to make use known Likely to cause death or Spring gun Reasonable belief that intruder threatens serious bodily harm Electric fence death or serious bodily injury Man-trap Almost every jurisdiction has enacted a survival statute that enables a decedent's personal representative to pursue tort claims on the decedent's behalf—so the photographer's personal representative can sue the landowner on the photographer's behalf. However, the landowner can assert that she was privileged to use reasonable force to defend her property from trespassers like the photographer. A mechanical device that is unlikely to cause death or serious bodily harm can be used for this purpose when: the use of such a device is reasonably necessary to protect the property from intrusion that particular device is not unreasonably dangerous and the device is customarily used for this purpose or reasonable care is taken to make its use known. Here, a barbed wire fence was reasonably necessary to protect the landowner's property since her \"No Trespassing—No Hunting\" signs were frequently disregarded. The fence was not unreasonably dangerous since an eight-foot-high fence topped with barbed wire threatens only the most determined of trespassers. And the potential for harm created by the barbed wire was apparent—not concealed. Therefore, the fence was a privileged means of protection, and the personal representative will not prevail. (Choice A) The photographer would have been a trespasser regardless of whether the landowner had refused him permission to enter. And since trespassers can recover for injuries caused by concealed or deadly devices (eg, spring guns), the photographer's trespasser status is not enough to defeat his personal representative's claim. (Choice C) Although the landowner had no property interest in the deer, she was entitled to use reasonable force to protect her property interest in the land. (Choice D) Deadly force may never be used merely to defend property. But barbed wire is not deadly force since it does not usually cause death or serious bodily harm.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the potential for harm created by the presence of the barbed wire was apparent."},"questionZh":"一位在山区拥有大片土地的地主，试图保护栖息于其部分土地上的一群野生鹿。\n\n尽管地主竖立了标有“禁止狩猎——禁止擅入”字样的告示牌，但仍有猎人频繁闯入猎杀这些鹿。\n\n最近，地主在其土地上的一处沟壑处架设了一道八英尺高的铁丝网围栏，并在顶部加装了三股带刺铁丝；该沟壑是通往鹿群栖息地的唯一通道。\n\n一位野生动物摄影师请求地主允许其进入该土地拍摄鹿群。\n\n由于担心任何宣传都会招致更多猎人闯入，地主拒绝了摄影师的请求。\n\n摄影师因受挫而试图翻越围栏，结果被带刺铁丝缠住并遭受严重割伤。\n\n伤口发生感染，最终导致其死亡。\n\n摄影师的遗产代理人起诉了地主。\n\n遗产代理人胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为摄影师是在地主拒绝其进入请求后擅自进入地主土地的，因此属于非法入侵者。","B":"不大，因为带刺铁丝网所构成的潜在伤害风险是显而易见的。","C":"大，因为地主对这些鹿并不享有使其有权使用武力进行保护的财产权。","D":"大，因为地主不得使用致命武力来保护其土地免受入侵。"},"explanationZh":"使用机械装置保卫财产 装置类型 | 示例 | 具有正当理由（特权）的使用情形 --- | --- | --- 不太可能导致死亡或严重人身伤害 | 铁丝网围栏 | 为防止入侵而合理必要；该装置本身不具有不合理的危险性，且通常用于此目的，或已采取合理措施告知他人该装置的存在 可能导致死亡或严重人身伤害 | 触发式枪支（spring gun）、电网、捕人陷阱 | 有合理理由相信入侵者会造成死亡或严重人身伤害 | 几乎所有司法管辖区都制定了“生存法案”（survival statute），允许死者的遗产代表（personal representative）代表死者提起侵权诉讼——因此，摄影师的遗产代表可以代表摄影师起诉土地所有者。\n\n然而，土地所有者可以主张，她有权使用合理的武力来保卫其财产，以防范像该摄影师这样的入侵者。\n\n在以下情况下，可以使用不太可能导致死亡或严重人身伤害的机械装置来实现这一目的：使用该装置对于保护财产免受入侵是合理必要的；该装置本身不具有不合理的危险性；且该装置通常用于此目的，或者已采取合理措施告知他人该装置的存在。\n\n在本案中，设置铁丝网围栏对于保护土地所有者的财产是合理必要的，因为她设置的“禁止擅入——禁止狩猎”的告示牌经常被无视。\n\n该围栏并不具有不合理的危险性，因为一道八英尺高且顶部装有铁丝网的围栏，通常只会阻挡那些最执意入侵的人。\n\n此外，铁丝网可能造成的伤害是显而易见的，而非隐蔽的。\n\n因此，该围栏属于正当的保护手段，遗产代表的诉讼请求将无法获得支持。\n\n（选项 A）无论土地所有者是否拒绝给予其进入许可，该摄影师都属于入侵者。\n\n鉴于入侵者可以就因隐蔽装置或致命装置（例如触发式枪支）造成的伤害获得赔偿，因此摄影师的入侵者身份并不足以驳回其遗产代表提出的索赔主张。\n\n（选项 C）尽管土地所有者对鹿不享有财产权益，但她有权使用合理的武力来保护其对土地的财产权益。\n\n（选项 D）绝不可仅仅为了保卫财产而使用致命武力。\n\n但铁丝网不属于致命武力，因为它通常不会导致死亡或严重人身伤害。\n\n教学目标：非致命性机械装置是保护财产的一种正当手段，前提是：(1) 使用该装置对于防止入侵而言在合理范围内是必要的；(2) 所用装置不具有不合理的危险性；以及 (3) 该装置通常用于此目的，或者已采取合理措施告知他人该装置的存在。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第84条（美国法律协会，1965年）（使用机械装置保护财产）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第926条（美国法律协会，1979年）（生存诉讼）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"非致命性机械装置是保护财产的一种正当手段，前提是：(1) 使用此类装置对于防止入侵是合理必要的；(2) 所用装置不具有不合理的危险性；以及 (3) 该装置通常用于此目的，或者已采取合理措施告知他人该装置的存在。","iracZh":{"issue":"遗产代理人胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"非致命性机械装置是保护财产的一种正当手段，前提是：(1) 使用此类装置对于防止入侵是合理必要的；(2) 所用装置不具有不合理的危险性；以及 (3) 该装置通常用于此目的，或者已采取合理措施告知他人该装置的存在。","analysis":"使用机械装置保卫财产 装置类型 | 示例 | 具有正当理由（特权）的使用情形 --- | --- | --- 不太可能导致死亡或严重人身伤害 | 铁丝网围栏 | 为防止入侵而合理必要；该装置本身不具有不合理的危险性，且通常用于此目的，或已采取合理措施告知他人该装置的存在 可能导致死亡或严重人身伤害 | 触发式枪支（spring gun）、电网、捕人陷阱 | 有合理理由相信入侵者会造成死亡或严重人身伤害 | 几乎所有司法管辖区都制定了“生存法案”（survival statute），允许死者的遗产代表（personal representative）代表死者提起侵权诉讼——因此，摄影师的遗产代表可以代表摄影师起诉土地所有者。\n\n然而，土地所有者可以主张，她有权使用合理的武力来保卫其财产，以防范像该摄影师这样的入侵者。\n\n在以下情况下，可以使用不太可能导致死亡或严重人身伤害的机械装置来实现这一目的：使用该装置对于保护财产免受入侵是合理必要的；该装置本身不具有不合理的危险性；且该装置通常用于此目的，或者已采取合理措施告知他人该装置的存在。\n\n在本案中，设置铁丝网围栏对于保护土地所有者的财产是合理必要的，因为她设置的“禁止擅入——禁止狩猎”的告示牌经常被无视。\n\n该围栏并不具有不合理的危险性，因为一道八英尺高且顶部装有铁丝网的围栏，通常只会阻挡那些最执意入侵的人。\n\n此外，铁丝网可能造成的伤害是显而易见的，而非隐蔽的。\n\n因此，该围栏属于正当的保护手段，遗产代表的诉讼请求将无法获得支持。\n\n（选项 A）无论土地所有者是否拒绝给予其进入许可，该摄影师都属于入侵者。\n\n鉴于入侵者可以就因隐蔽装置或致命装置（例如触发式枪支）造成的伤害获得赔偿，因此摄影师的入侵者身份并不足以驳回其遗产代表提出的索赔主张。\n\n（选项 C）尽管土地所有者对鹿不享有财产权益，但她有权使用合理的武力来保护其对土地的财产权益。\n\n（选项 D）绝不可仅仅为了保卫财产而使用致命武力。\n\n但铁丝网不属于致命武力，因为它通常不会导致死亡或严重人身伤害。\n\n教学目标：非致命性机械装置是保护财产的一种正当手段，前提是：(1) 使用该装置对于防止入侵而言在合理范围内是必要的；(2) 所用装置不具有不合理的危险性；以及 (3) 该装置通常用于此目的，或者已采取合理措施告知他人该装置的存在。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第84条（美国法律协会，1965年）（使用机械装置保护财产）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第926条（美国法律协会，1979年）（生存诉讼）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不大，因为带刺铁丝网所构成的潜在伤害风险是显而易见的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"dangerous","ipa":"/dˈeɪndʒɝəs/","syllables":"dan·ger·ous","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 危险的；[法] 危险的, 危害的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“dangerous”","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Nondeadly mechanical devices are a privileged means of protecting property when (1) such a device is reasonably necessary to prevent intrusions, (2) the device used is not unreasonably dangerous, and (3) the device is customarily used for this purpose or reasonable care is taken to make its use known.","synonyms":["grave","grievous","serious","severe"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":27,"sourceQuestionPage":79,"sourceQuestionPages":[79],"sourceExplanationPages":[80,81],"sourceAnswerPages":[80,81],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-028","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"Local police notified local gas station attendants that a robber had recently committed armed robberies at five gas stations in the city. The police said that the robber was a 75- year-old woman with white hair who drove a vintage, cream-colored Ford Thunderbird. Attendants were advised to call police if they saw her but not to attempt to apprehend her. Armed robbery is a felony under state law. A traveler was passing through the city on a cross-country journey. The traveler was a 75- year-old woman who had white hair and drove a vintage, cream-colored Ford Thunderbird. When the traveler drove into a gas station in the city, the owner thought that the traveler must be the robber wanted by the police. After checking the oil at the traveler's request, the owner falsely informed the traveler that she had a broken fan belt, that her car could not be driven without a new belt, that it would take him about an hour to replace it, and that she should stay in his office for consultation about the repair. The traveler was greatly annoyed that her journey was delayed, but she stayed in the owner's office while she waited for her car. The owner then telephoned the police and, within the hour, the police came and questioned the traveler. The police immediately determined that the traveler was not the robber, and the traveler resumed her journey without further delay. If the traveler sues the owner for false imprisonment, will the traveler likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the owner reasonably believed that the traveler was the robber.","B":"No, because the traveler suffered no physical or mental harm.","C":"Yes, because the owner lied to the traveler about the condition of her car.","D":"Yes, if the traveler reasonably believed she could not leave the owner's premises."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Private citizen's privilege to arrest\n\nFelony Felony was committed and Defendant reasonably suspects plaintiff committed that felony Misdemeanor Plaintiff committed breach of the peace (misdemeanor involving violence or likely causing imminent disturbance of public order) and Crime committed in defendant's presence False imprisonment occurs when a defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff within fixed boundaries and the plaintiff is aware of, or harmed by, the confinement. However, when the defendant is a private citizen (ie, not a police officer), the confinement is privileged when (1) a felony has been committed and (2) the defendant reasonably suspects that the plaintiff committed the felony. Here, the owner was privileged to confine—eg, arrest—the traveler because (1) five felony armed robberies had been committed at gas stations in the city and (2) the owner reasonably believed that the traveler committed those robberies since she and her car matched the police description. Therefore, the traveler will not prevail on her false imprisonment claim. (Choice B) For false imprisonment, proof of physical or mental harm is unnecessary if the traveler was aware of her confinement. Instead, her claim will fail because the owner was privileged to confine her. (Choices C & D) Even if the traveler can show that the owner lied to her about the condition of her car to prevent her from leaving (intent to confine) and that she reasonably believed she could not leave (confinement and awareness), her false imprisonment claim will fail because the owner had the right to confine her.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant is privileged to confine (eg, arrest) a plaintiff when a felony has been committed and the defendant reasonably suspects that the plaintiff committed it.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 35 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of false imprisonment).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 119(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (arrest without a warrant).","educationalObjective":"A defendant is privileged to confine (eg, arrest) a plaintiff when a felony has been committed and the defendant reasonably suspects that the plaintiff committed it.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 35 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of false imprisonment).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 119(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (arrest without a warrant).","rule":"A defendant is privileged to confine (eg, arrest) a plaintiff when a felony has been committed and the defendant reasonably suspects that the plaintiff committed it.","irac":{"issue":"If the traveler sues the owner for false imprisonment, will the traveler likely prevail?","rule":"A defendant is privileged to confine (eg, arrest) a plaintiff when a felony has been committed and the defendant reasonably suspects that the plaintiff committed it.","analysis":"Private citizen's privilege to arrest\n\nFelony Felony was committed and Defendant reasonably suspects plaintiff committed that felony Misdemeanor Plaintiff committed breach of the peace (misdemeanor involving violence or likely causing imminent disturbance of public order) and Crime committed in defendant's presence False imprisonment occurs when a defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff within fixed boundaries and the plaintiff is aware of, or harmed by, the confinement. However, when the defendant is a private citizen (ie, not a police officer), the confinement is privileged when (1) a felony has been committed and (2) the defendant reasonably suspects that the plaintiff committed the felony. Here, the owner was privileged to confine—eg, arrest—the traveler because (1) five felony armed robberies had been committed at gas stations in the city and (2) the owner reasonably believed that the traveler committed those robberies since she and her car matched the police description. Therefore, the traveler will not prevail on her false imprisonment claim. (Choice B) For false imprisonment, proof of physical or mental harm is unnecessary if the traveler was aware of her confinement. Instead, her claim will fail because the owner was privileged to confine her. (Choices C & D) Even if the traveler can show that the owner lied to her about the condition of her car to prevent her from leaving (intent to confine) and that she reasonably believed she could not leave (confinement and awareness), her false imprisonment claim will fail because the owner had the right to confine her.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the owner reasonably believed that the traveler was the robber."},"questionZh":"当地警方通知加油站工作人员，一名劫匪近期在市内五家加油站实施了持械抢劫。\n\n警方描述称，该劫匪是一名白发苍苍的75岁女性，驾驶一辆复古风格、奶油色的福特雷鸟（Ford Thunderbird）汽车。\n\n警方建议工作人员若发现此人，应报警而不要试图将其抓获。\n\n根据州法律，持械抢劫属于重罪。\n\n一位正在进行长途旅行的旅客途经该市。\n\n这名旅客也是一位白发苍苍的75岁女性，驾驶着一辆复古风格、奶油色的福特雷鸟汽车。\n\n当她驶入市内一家加油站时，店主认定她就是警方通缉的劫匪。\n\n在应旅客要求检查机油后，店主谎称她的风扇皮带断裂，不更换新皮带便无法行驶，更换需耗时约一小时，并要求她留在办公室商谈维修事宜。\n\n尽管因行程延误而感到非常恼火，该旅客还是留在店主办公室等待车辆维修。\n\n随后，店主致电警方；不到一小时，警察赶到并盘问了该旅客。\n\n警方很快确认该旅客并非劫匪，于是她继续踏上旅程，未再受延误。\n\n如果该旅客以非法拘禁为由起诉店主，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为店主有合理理由相信该旅客就是劫匪。","B":"不会，因为该旅客未遭受身体或精神上的伤害。","C":"会，因为店主就车辆状况向该旅客撒了谎。","D":"会，如果该旅客有合理理由相信自己无法离开店主的场所。"},"explanationZh":"普通公民的逮捕特权 重罪：已发生重罪，且被告有合理理由怀疑原告实施了该重罪。轻罪：原告实施了破坏安宁的行为（涉及暴力或可能导致即时公共秩序混乱的轻罪），且犯罪行为发生于被告在场时。非法拘禁是指被告故意将原告限制在特定范围内，且原告意识到该限制或因此受到伤害。\n\n然而，当被告为普通公民（即非警务人员）时，若满足以下条件，则该限制行为享有特权：（1）已发生重罪；（2）被告有合理理由怀疑原告实施了该重罪。\n\n在本案中，店主有权限制（例如逮捕）该旅行者，因为：（1）该市加油站已发生五起持械抢劫重罪；（2）店主有合理理由相信该旅行者实施了这些抢劫，因为她及其车辆特征与警方描述相符。\n\n因此，该旅行者关于非法拘禁的诉讼主张将无法成立。\n\n（选项 B）对于非法拘禁而言，若旅行者意识到自己被限制，则无需证明存在身体或精神伤害。\n\n相反，她的诉讼主张将因店主享有限制其自由的特权而失败。\n\n（选项 C 和 D）即使旅行者能证明店主为了阻止其离开而谎称其车辆状况有问题（具有限制意图），且她有合理理由相信自己无法离开（存在限制且其已意识到），她的非法拘禁诉讼主张仍将失败，因为店主有权限制其自由。\n\n教育目标：当发生重罪且被告有合理理由怀疑原告实施了该重罪时，被告享有限制（例如逮捕）原告的特权。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 35 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（非法拘禁的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 119(b) 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（无证逮捕）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当重罪发生且被告有合理理由怀疑原告实施了该犯罪时，被告有权限制原告的人身自由（例如实施逮捕）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该旅客以非法拘禁为由起诉店主，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"当重罪发生且被告有合理理由怀疑原告实施了该犯罪时，被告有权限制原告的人身自由（例如实施逮捕）。","analysis":"普通公民的逮捕特权 重罪：已发生重罪，且被告有合理理由怀疑原告实施了该重罪。轻罪：原告实施了破坏安宁的行为（涉及暴力或可能导致即时公共秩序混乱的轻罪），且犯罪行为发生于被告在场时。非法拘禁是指被告故意将原告限制在特定范围内，且原告意识到该限制或因此受到伤害。\n\n然而，当被告为普通公民（即非警务人员）时，若满足以下条件，则该限制行为享有特权：（1）已发生重罪；（2）被告有合理理由怀疑原告实施了该重罪。\n\n在本案中，店主有权限制（例如逮捕）该旅行者，因为：（1）该市加油站已发生五起持械抢劫重罪；（2）店主有合理理由相信该旅行者实施了这些抢劫，因为她及其车辆特征与警方描述相符。\n\n因此，该旅行者关于非法拘禁的诉讼主张将无法成立。\n\n（选项 B）对于非法拘禁而言，若旅行者意识到自己被限制，则无需证明存在身体或精神伤害。\n\n相反，她的诉讼主张将因店主享有限制其自由的特权而失败。\n\n（选项 C 和 D）即使旅行者能证明店主为了阻止其离开而谎称其车辆状况有问题（具有限制意图），且她有合理理由相信自己无法离开（存在限制且其已意识到），她的非法拘禁诉讼主张仍将失败，因为店主有权限制其自由。\n\n教育目标：当发生重罪且被告有合理理由怀疑原告实施了该重罪时，被告享有限制（例如逮捕）原告的特权。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 35 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（非法拘禁的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 119(b) 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（无证逮捕）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为店主有合理理由相信该旅客就是劫匪。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":28,"sourceQuestionPage":82,"sourceQuestionPages":[82],"sourceExplanationPages":[83,84],"sourceAnswerPages":[83,84],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-029","subject":"Torts","topic":"Privileges & Defenses","question":"A gardener's backyard, which is landscaped with expensive flowers and shrubs, is adjacent to a golf course. While a golfer was playing golf on the course, a thunderstorm suddenly began. As the golfer was returning to the clubhouse in his golf cart, lightning struck a tree on the course, and the tree began to fall in the golfer's direction. To avoid being hit by the tree, the golfer deliberately steered his cart onto the gardener's property, causing substantial damage to the gardener's expensive plants. In an action by the gardener against the golfer for damage to his plants, will the gardener likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the golfer's entry onto the gardener's property was occasioned by necessity and therefore privileged.","B":"No, because the lightning was an act of God.","C":"Yes, because the golfer's entry onto the gardener's property, though occasioned by necessity, was for the golfer's benefit.","D":"Yes, for nominal damages only, because the golfer was privileged to enter the gardener's property."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A defendant is generally liable for trespass when he/she intentionally enters the plaintiff's property without permission. A trespass may be excused by either of the following privileges: public necessity – an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect a large number of people from a public disaster (eg, hurricane, oil spill, spreading fire) private necessity – an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property But when a trespass arises from private necessity, the trespasser remains liable for actual damages caused by the trespass unless the entry was for the plaintiff's benefit (eg, to protect the plaintiff's property). Here, the golfer deliberately steered his cart onto the gardener's property without permission (trespass). However, the golfer's trespass was privileged because he was trying to protect himself from a tree falling in his direction (private necessity). But since the golfer's entry was for his own benefit—not the gardener's—the golfer is liable for damage his entry caused to the gardener's plants (actual damages). (Choice A) Although the golfer's trespass was excused by private necessity, the golfer is still liable for the damage he caused to the gardener's plants. In contrast, the golfer would not be liable for any damages had his trespass been committed out of public necessity. (Choice B) Although an act of God (an extraordinary act of nature—eg, the sudden storm) created the emergency situation that excused the golfer's trespass (private necessity), the golfer is still liable for the damage he caused to the gardener's plants. (Choice D) When a trespass is privileged due to private necessity, the landowner can recover actual damages—not nominal or punitive damages.\n\nEducational objective: Although a trespass is excused when it arises from private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property), the trespasser is still liable for actual damages caused by the trespass unless the entry was for the landowner's benefit.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (intentional trespass).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 196 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (public necessity).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 197 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (private necessity).","educationalObjective":"Although a trespass is excused when it arises from private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property), the trespasser is still liable for actual damages caused by the trespass unless the entry was for the landowner's benefit.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (intentional trespass).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 196 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (public necessity).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 197 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (private necessity).","rule":"Although a trespass is excused when it arises from private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property), the trespasser is still liable for actual damages caused by the trespass unless the entry was for the landowner's benefit.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the gardener against the golfer for damage to his plants, will the gardener likely prevail?","rule":"Although a trespass is excused when it arises from private necessity (an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property), the trespasser is still liable for actual damages caused by the trespass unless the entry was for the landowner's benefit.","analysis":"A defendant is generally liable for trespass when he/she intentionally enters the plaintiff's property without permission. A trespass may be excused by either of the following privileges: public necessity – an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect a large number of people from a public disaster (eg, hurricane, oil spill, spreading fire) private necessity – an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property But when a trespass arises from private necessity, the trespasser remains liable for actual damages caused by the trespass unless the entry was for the plaintiff's benefit (eg, to protect the plaintiff's property). Here, the golfer deliberately steered his cart onto the gardener's property without permission (trespass). However, the golfer's trespass was privileged because he was trying to protect himself from a tree falling in his direction (private necessity). But since the golfer's entry was for his own benefit—not the gardener's—the golfer is liable for damage his entry caused to the gardener's plants (actual damages). (Choice A) Although the golfer's trespass was excused by private necessity, the golfer is still liable for the damage he caused to the gardener's plants. In contrast, the golfer would not be liable for any damages had his trespass been committed out of public necessity. (Choice B) Although an act of God (an extraordinary act of nature—eg, the sudden storm) created the emergency situation that excused the golfer's trespass (private necessity), the golfer is still liable for the damage he caused to the gardener's plants. (Choice D) When a trespass is privileged due to private necessity, the landowner can recover actual damages—not nominal or punitive damages.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the golfer's entry onto the gardener's property, though occasioned by necessity, was for the golfer's benefit."},"questionZh":"一位园丁的后院种植着昂贵的花卉和灌木，毗邻高尔夫球场。\n\n一位高尔夫球手正在球场上打球时，突然下起了雷暴。\n\n当高尔夫球手驾驶高尔夫球车返回会所时，一道闪电击中了球场上的一棵树，树开始朝高尔夫球手的方向倒下。\n\n为了避免被树砸到，高尔夫球手故意将球车开进了园丁的土地，导致园丁昂贵的植物遭受了严重损坏。\n\n如果园丁起诉高尔夫球手，要求赔偿植物损失，园丁胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为高尔夫球手进入园丁土地是出于必要，因此园丁享有特权。","B":"不可能，因为闪电是不可抗力造成的。","C":"可能，因为高尔夫球手进入园丁土地虽然是出于必要，但是为了他自身的利益。","D":"是的，仅需支付象征性赔偿，因为高尔夫球手有权进入园丁的土地。"},"explanationZh":"被告若未经许可故意进入原告的财产，通常需承担侵入（trespass）责任。\n\n侵入行为若符合以下任一“特权”情形，可获免责：公共必要性（public necessity）——即为保护大量人群免受公共灾害（如飓风、石油泄漏、蔓延的火灾）侵害而必须（或看似必须）进行的侵入；私人必要性（private necessity）——即为保护自身、第三方或财产而必须（或看似必须）进行的侵入。然而，当侵入行为源于私人必要性时，侵入者仍须对因侵入造成的实际损害承担责任，除非其进入行为是为了原告的利益（例如，为了保护原告的财产）。\n\n在本案中，高尔夫球手未经许可故意将球车驶入园丁的财产范围内（构成侵入）。\n\n尽管该球手的侵入行为享有特权，因为他是为了保护自己免受倒向他的树木伤害（属于私人必要性），但由于其进入行为是为了自身利益而非园丁的利益，因此他必须对因进入而造成的园丁植物受损承担责任（即实际损害赔偿责任）。\n\n（选项 A）虽然该球手的侵入行为因私人必要性而获免责，但他仍须对造成的园丁植物损害承担责任。\n\n相比之下，如果其侵入行为是出于公共必要性，则无需承担任何损害赔偿责任。\n\n（选项 B）尽管“不可抗力”（即异常的自然现象，如突发风暴）造成了使该球手侵入行为获免责（基于私人必要性）的紧急情况，但他仍须对造成的园丁植物损害承担责任。\n\n（选项 D）当侵入行为因私人必要性而享有特权时，土地所有者可获得实际损害赔偿，但不能获得象征性损害赔偿或惩罚性损害赔偿。\n\n教育目标：虽然源于私人必要性（即为保护自身、第三方或财产而必须或看似必须进行的侵入）的侵入行为可获免责，但侵入者仍须对因侵入造成的实际损害承担责任，除非其进入行为是为了土地所有者的利益。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第158条（美国法律协会，1965年）（故意侵入）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第196条（美国法律学会，1965年）（公共紧急避险）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第197条（美国法律学会，1965年）（私人紧急避险）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"虽然出于私人需要（为了保护自身、第三方或财产而进行的侵入，或合理地认为必要的侵入）而进行的侵入行为可以免责，但除非进入是为了土地所有者的利益，否则侵入者仍需对侵入造成的实际损害承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果园丁起诉高尔夫球手损害其植物，园丁胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"虽然出于私人需要（为了保护自身、第三方或财产而进行的侵入，或合理地认为必要的侵入）而进行的侵入行为可以免责，但除非进入是为了土地所有者的利益，否则侵入者仍需对侵入造成的实际损害承担责任。","analysis":"被告若未经许可故意进入原告的财产，通常需承担侵入（trespass）责任。\n\n侵入行为若符合以下任一“特权”情形，可获免责：公共必要性（public necessity）——即为保护大量人群免受公共灾害（如飓风、石油泄漏、蔓延的火灾）侵害而必须（或看似必须）进行的侵入；私人必要性（private necessity）——即为保护自身、第三方或财产而必须（或看似必须）进行的侵入。然而，当侵入行为源于私人必要性时，侵入者仍须对因侵入造成的实际损害承担责任，除非其进入行为是为了原告的利益（例如，为了保护原告的财产）。\n\n在本案中，高尔夫球手未经许可故意将球车驶入园丁的财产范围内（构成侵入）。\n\n尽管该球手的侵入行为享有特权，因为他是为了保护自己免受倒向他的树木伤害（属于私人必要性），但由于其进入行为是为了自身利益而非园丁的利益，因此他必须对因进入而造成的园丁植物受损承担责任（即实际损害赔偿责任）。\n\n（选项 A）虽然该球手的侵入行为因私人必要性而获免责，但他仍须对造成的园丁植物损害承担责任。\n\n相比之下，如果其侵入行为是出于公共必要性，则无需承担任何损害赔偿责任。\n\n（选项 B）尽管“不可抗力”（即异常的自然现象，如突发风暴）造成了使该球手侵入行为获免责（基于私人必要性）的紧急情况，但他仍须对造成的园丁植物损害承担责任。\n\n（选项 D）当侵入行为因私人必要性而享有特权时，土地所有者可获得实际损害赔偿，但不能获得象征性损害赔偿或惩罚性损害赔偿。\n\n教育目标：虽然源于私人必要性（即为保护自身、第三方或财产而必须或看似必须进行的侵入）的侵入行为可获免责，但侵入者仍须对因侵入造成的实际损害承担责任，除非其进入行为是为了土地所有者的利益。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第158条（美国法律协会，1965年）（故意侵入）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第196条（美国法律学会，1965年）（公共紧急避险）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第197条（美国法律学会，1965年）（私人紧急避险）。","conclusion":"答案 C：可能，因为高尔夫球手进入园丁土地虽然是出于必要，但是为了他自身的利益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"public necessity","ipa":"/pˈəblɪk nʌsˈɛsəti/","syllables":"pub·lic ne·ces·sity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"公共紧急避险：为避免重大公共灾害而侵害财产的完全特权","contextualMeaning":"公共紧急避险：为避免重大公共灾害而侵害财产的完全特权","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A trespass may be excused by either of the following privileges: public necessity – an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect a large number of people from a public disaster (eg, hurricane, oil spill, spreading fire) private necessity – an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect oneself, third parties, or property But when a trespass arises from private neces","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-d390785eaa614fcd"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":29,"sourceQuestionPage":85,"sourceQuestionPages":[85],"sourceExplanationPages":[86,87],"sourceAnswerPages":[86,87],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-030","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A bus passenger was seated next to a woman he did not know. The woman stood to exit the bus, leaving a package on the seat. The passenger lightly tapped the woman on the back to get her attention and to inform her that she had forgotten her package. Because the woman had recently had back surgery, the tap was painful and caused her to twist and seriously injure her back. If the woman sues the passenger to recover for the back injury, will she likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because she is presumed to have consented to the ordinary contacts of daily life.","B":"No, because she was not put in apprehension by the touching.","C":"Yes, because she did not consent to the harmful contact.","D":"Yes, because the passenger's intentional touching seriously injured her."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A defendant is liable for battery if he/she intends to create contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) and causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. However, the defendant is not liable for battery if the plaintiff consented to such contact. There are three types of consent: actual consent – the plaintiff willingly submits to contact (eg, the plaintiff orders the defendant to hit him/her)\n\napparent consent – consent is reasonably understood based on the plaintiff's conduct or social customs (eg, shaking hands and other ordinary social contact)\n\nconsent implied by law – the plaintiff is unable to consent and emergency action is required to save the plaintiff's life or prevent serious harm (eg, administering CPR)\n\nHere, the passenger tapped the woman on her back to get her attention (intentional contact), causing her to seriously injure her back (harm). However, the passenger is not liable for battery because he could reasonably presume that the woman consented to ordinary contacts of daily life like a light tap on the back (apparent consent). (Choice B) The harm required for assault is apprehension of contact, whereas battery requires actual contact. Therefore, the fact that the woman was not put in apprehension by the touching is irrelevant to her battery claim. (Choice C) Although the passenger's intentional tap caused harmful contact with the woman, it is presumed that the woman consented to the passenger's ordinary social contact (apparent consent) unless she gave some indication suggesting otherwise (eg, had she told the passenger not to touch her). Since the woman gave no such indication, the passenger is not liable for battery. (Choice D) The severity of harm caused by an intentional touching has no effect on liability for battery (eg, pinching and punching equally support a battery claim). Therefore, the seriousness of the woman's injury does not matter.\n\nEducational objective: Consent (actual, apparent, or implied) is a defense to battery. Apparent consent exists when consent is reasonably understood based on the plaintiff's conduct or social customs.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 18 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by offensive contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 892(2) (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (apparent consent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 892A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (effect of consent).","educationalObjective":"Consent (actual, apparent, or implied) is a defense to battery. Apparent consent exists when consent is reasonably understood based on the plaintiff's conduct or social customs.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 18 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by offensive contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 892(2) (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (apparent consent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 892A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (effect of consent).","rule":"Consent (actual, apparent, or implied) is a defense to battery. Apparent consent exists when consent is reasonably understood based on the plaintiff's conduct or social customs.","irac":{"issue":"If the woman sues the passenger to recover for the back injury, will she likely prevail?","rule":"Consent (actual, apparent, or implied) is a defense to battery. Apparent consent exists when consent is reasonably understood based on the plaintiff's conduct or social customs.","analysis":"A defendant is liable for battery if he/she intends to create contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) and causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. However, the defendant is not liable for battery if the plaintiff consented to such contact. There are three types of consent: actual consent – the plaintiff willingly submits to contact (eg, the plaintiff orders the defendant to hit him/her)\n\napparent consent – consent is reasonably understood based on the plaintiff's conduct or social customs (eg, shaking hands and other ordinary social contact)\n\nconsent implied by law – the plaintiff is unable to consent and emergency action is required to save the plaintiff's life or prevent serious harm (eg, administering CPR)\n\nHere, the passenger tapped the woman on her back to get her attention (intentional contact), causing her to seriously injure her back (harm). However, the passenger is not liable for battery because he could reasonably presume that the woman consented to ordinary contacts of daily life like a light tap on the back (apparent consent). (Choice B) The harm required for assault is apprehension of contact, whereas battery requires actual contact. Therefore, the fact that the woman was not put in apprehension by the touching is irrelevant to her battery claim. (Choice C) Although the passenger's intentional tap caused harmful contact with the woman, it is presumed that the woman consented to the passenger's ordinary social contact (apparent consent) unless she gave some indication suggesting otherwise (eg, had she told the passenger not to touch her). Since the woman gave no such indication, the passenger is not liable for battery. (Choice D) The severity of harm caused by an intentional touching has no effect on liability for battery (eg, pinching and punching equally support a battery claim). Therefore, the seriousness of the woman's injury does not matter.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because she is presumed to have consented to the ordinary contacts of daily life."},"questionZh":"一名公交车乘客坐在一位他不认识的女士旁边。\n\n该女士起身准备下车时，将一个包裹落在了座位上。\n\n乘客轻轻拍了拍她的背部以引起注意，并提醒她忘了拿包裹。\n\n由于该女士近期刚做过背部手术，这一拍让她感到疼痛，并导致她身体扭转，造成背部严重受伤。\n\n如果该女士起诉这名乘客，要求赔偿其背部伤势，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为法律推定她已同意日常生活中的一般性身体接触。","B":"不大，因为这种接触并未使她产生遭受伤害的恐惧感。","C":"大，因为她并未同意这种造成伤害的接触。","D":"大，因为乘客的故意接触导致她严重受伤。"},"explanationZh":"如果被告有意制造接触（或使原告预感到即将发生接触），并导致了对原告的有害或冒犯性接触，则被告需承担故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）的法律责任。\n\n然而，如果原告同意此类接触，被告则无需承担责任。\n\n同意分为三种类型：实际同意——原告自愿接受接触（例如，原告要求被告击打自己）； 表面同意——根据原告的行为或社会习俗，可合理推断其表示同意（例如，握手及其他日常社交接触）； 法律默示同意——原告无法表达同意，且必须采取紧急行动以挽救其生命或防止严重伤害（例如，实施心肺复苏术）。 在本案中，乘客拍打该女子的背部以引起其注意（有意接触），导致其背部严重受伤（造成伤害）。\n\n然而，该乘客无需承担故意侵害身体（battery）责任，因为他可以合理地推定该女子同意诸如轻拍背部之类的日常社交接触（表面同意）。\n\n（选项 B）构成“袭击”（assault）所需的伤害是指对接触的预感（apprehension），而“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）则要求发生实际接触。\n\n因此，该女子未因接触而产生预感这一事实，与其提出的故意侵害身体（battery）索赔无关。\n\n（选项 C）尽管乘客有意的拍打导致了对该女子的有害接触，但除非该女子有相反的表示（例如，她曾告诉乘客不要触碰她），否则推定她同意乘客的日常社交接触（表面同意）。\n\n由于该女子未作此类表示，乘客无需承担故意侵害身体（battery）责任。\n\n（选项 D）有意接触所造成的伤害程度不影响故意侵害身体（battery）责任的认定（例如，捏和打拳同样支持故意侵害身体（battery）索赔）。\n\n因此，该女子受伤的严重程度无关紧要。\n\n教学目标：同意（实际同意、表面同意或默示同意）是针对故意侵害身体（battery）指控的一种抗辩理由。\n\n当根据原告的行为或社会习俗可合理推断其表示同意时，即存在表面同意。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第13条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第18条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因冒犯性接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第892(2)条（美国法律学会，1979年）（表见同意）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第892A条（美国法律学会，1979年）（同意的效力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"同意（包括明示同意、表见同意或默示同意）是针对“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）指控的一种抗辩理由。\n\n当根据原告的行为或社会习俗可以合理地理解为存在同意时，即构成表见同意。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该女士起诉这名乘客，要求赔偿其背部伤势，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"同意（包括明示同意、表见同意或默示同意）是针对“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）指控的一种抗辩理由。\n\n当根据原告的行为或社会习俗可以合理地理解为存在同意时，即构成表见同意。","analysis":"如果被告有意制造接触（或使原告预感到即将发生接触），并导致了对原告的有害或冒犯性接触，则被告需承担故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）的法律责任。\n\n然而，如果原告同意此类接触，被告则无需承担责任。\n\n同意分为三种类型：实际同意——原告自愿接受接触（例如，原告要求被告击打自己）； 表面同意——根据原告的行为或社会习俗，可合理推断其表示同意（例如，握手及其他日常社交接触）； 法律默示同意——原告无法表达同意，且必须采取紧急行动以挽救其生命或防止严重伤害（例如，实施心肺复苏术）。 在本案中，乘客拍打该女子的背部以引起其注意（有意接触），导致其背部严重受伤（造成伤害）。\n\n然而，该乘客无需承担故意侵害身体（battery）责任，因为他可以合理地推定该女子同意诸如轻拍背部之类的日常社交接触（表面同意）。\n\n（选项 B）构成“袭击”（assault）所需的伤害是指对接触的预感（apprehension），而“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）则要求发生实际接触。\n\n因此，该女子未因接触而产生预感这一事实，与其提出的故意侵害身体（battery）索赔无关。\n\n（选项 C）尽管乘客有意的拍打导致了对该女子的有害接触，但除非该女子有相反的表示（例如，她曾告诉乘客不要触碰她），否则推定她同意乘客的日常社交接触（表面同意）。\n\n由于该女子未作此类表示，乘客无需承担故意侵害身体（battery）责任。\n\n（选项 D）有意接触所造成的伤害程度不影响故意侵害身体（battery）责任的认定（例如，捏和打拳同样支持故意侵害身体（battery）索赔）。\n\n因此，该女子受伤的严重程度无关紧要。\n\n教学目标：同意（实际同意、表面同意或默示同意）是针对故意侵害身体（battery）指控的一种抗辩理由。\n\n当根据原告的行为或社会习俗可合理推断其表示同意时，即存在表面同意。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第13条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第18条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因冒犯性接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第892(2)条（美国法律学会，1979年）（表见同意）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第892A条（美国法律学会，1979年）（同意的效力）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不大，因为法律推定她已同意日常生活中的一般性身体接触。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-0e0764be5bdffabe"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":30,"sourceQuestionPage":88,"sourceQuestionPages":[88],"sourceExplanationPages":[89,90],"sourceAnswerPages":[89,90],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-031","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"An ordinance in a small town required all restaurants to designate smoking and nonsmoking sections for their customers. A cigarette smoker and a nonsmoker were seated at adjoining tables in a small restaurant. The smoker's table was in the smoking section, and the nonsmoker's table was in the nonsmoking section. When the smoker lit a cigarette, the nonsmoker politely requested that he not smoke, explaining that she had a severe allergy to cigarette smoke. The smoker ignored the nonsmoker's request and continued to smoke. As a result, the nonsmoker was hospitalized with a severe allergic reaction to the smoke. The nonsmoker brought a battery action against the smoker. Which of the following questions will NOT be an issue in the battery action?","choices":{"A":"Did the smoker intend to cause the nonsmoker's contact with the cigarette smoke?","B":"Does smoke have the physical properties necessary for making the kind of contact required for battery?","C":"Is contact with cigarette smoke from a lawful smoking section in a restaurant the kind of contact one must endure as a voluntary restaurant patron?","D":"Was the smoker's conduct unreasonable under the circumstances?"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Battery exists when a defendant intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof) and causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Consent to contact is a defense to battery. Therefore, the issues here are: whether the smoker intended to cause the nonsmoker's contact with the cigarette smoke (Choice A) whether smoke possesses the physical properties necessary to create contact (Choice B) whether contact with the cigarette smoke harmed or offended the nonsmoker whether the nonsmoker consented to the contact by voluntarily sitting near the lawful smoking section in the restaurant (Choice C) However, whether the smoker's conduct was unreasonable will not be an issue here. Intentional torts require proof that the defendant acted with intent (ie, the purpose or knowledge that one's act will cause a particular result). In contrast, negligence requires proof that the defendant failed to use reasonable care (ie, the level of care an ordinarily prudent person would have used under similar circumstances). Since battery is an intentional tort, it requires proof of intentional conduct. Proof of unreasonable conduct will not suffice.\n\nEducational objective: Battery requires proof that the defendant intended to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof). Proof of unreasonable conduct by the defendant will not suffice.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 13, 18 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of battery).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 3 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of negligence).","educationalObjective":"Battery requires proof that the defendant intended to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof). Proof of unreasonable conduct by the defendant will not suffice.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 13, 18 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of battery).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 3 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of negligence).","rule":"Battery requires proof that the defendant intended to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof). Proof of unreasonable conduct by the defendant will not suffice.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following questions will NOT be an issue in the battery action?","rule":"Battery requires proof that the defendant intended to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof). Proof of unreasonable conduct by the defendant will not suffice.","analysis":"Battery exists when a defendant intends to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or imminent apprehension thereof) and causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Consent to contact is a defense to battery. Therefore, the issues here are: whether the smoker intended to cause the nonsmoker's contact with the cigarette smoke (Choice A) whether smoke possesses the physical properties necessary to create contact (Choice B) whether contact with the cigarette smoke harmed or offended the nonsmoker whether the nonsmoker consented to the contact by voluntarily sitting near the lawful smoking section in the restaurant (Choice C) However, whether the smoker's conduct was unreasonable will not be an issue here. Intentional torts require proof that the defendant acted with intent (ie, the purpose or knowledge that one's act will cause a particular result). In contrast, negligence requires proof that the defendant failed to use reasonable care (ie, the level of care an ordinarily prudent person would have used under similar circumstances). Since battery is an intentional tort, it requires proof of intentional conduct. Proof of unreasonable conduct will not suffice.","conclusion":"Answer D: Was the smoker's conduct unreasonable under the circumstances?"},"questionZh":"某小镇的一项地方法规要求所有餐馆必须为顾客划分吸烟区和非吸烟区。\n\n在一家小餐馆里，一位吸烟者和一位非吸烟者坐在相邻的桌旁；吸烟者的桌子位于吸烟区，而非吸烟者的桌子位于非吸烟区。\n\n当吸烟者点燃香烟时，非吸烟者礼貌地请求对方不要吸烟，并解释说自己对烟雾有严重过敏反应。\n\n吸烟者无视了这一请求，继续吸烟。\n\n结果，非吸烟者因对烟雾产生严重过敏反应而住院治疗。\n\n随后，非吸烟者针对吸烟者提起了“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）诉讼。\n\n以下哪个问题**不**属于该“故意侵害身体（battery）”诉讼中的争议焦点？","choicesZh":{"A":"吸烟者是否有意导致非吸烟者接触到香烟烟雾？","B":"烟雾是否具备构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”所需接触形式的物理特性？","C":"在餐馆合法吸烟区产生的香烟烟雾接触，是否属于作为自愿光顾餐馆的顾客必须容忍的接触？","D":"在当时的情况下，吸烟者的行为是否不合理？"},"explanationZh":"当被告意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并导致原告发生此类接触时，即构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权。\n\n对接触表示同意是针对故意侵害身体（battery）指控的一种抗辩理由。\n\n因此，本案涉及的问题包括：吸烟者是否有意导致不吸烟者接触到香烟烟雾（选项 A）；烟雾是否具备造成接触所需的物理特性（选项 B）；接触香烟烟雾是否对不吸烟者造成了伤害或冒犯；以及不吸烟者自愿坐在餐厅合法吸烟区附近的行为是否构成对该接触的同意（选项 C）。然而，吸烟者的行为是否“不合理”并非本案的争议焦点。\n\n故意侵权要求证明被告具有主观故意（即具有某种目的，或明知其行为会导致特定结果）。\n\n相比之下，过失侵权要求证明被告未尽到合理的注意义务（即在类似情况下，一个通常谨慎的人应尽到的注意程度）。\n\n由于故意侵害身体（battery）属于故意侵权，因此必须证明存在故意行为；仅证明行为不合理是不够的。\n\n教学目标：故意侵害身体（battery）侵权要求证明被告意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感）。\n\n仅证明被告的行为不合理不足以构成故意侵害身体（battery）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于“意图”的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 13、18 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于“故意侵害身体（battery）”的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 3 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于“过失”的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”需要证明被告有意造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的紧迫恐惧感）。\n\n仅证明被告行为不合理是不够的。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪个问题**不**属于该“故意侵害身体（battery）”诉讼中的争议焦点？","rule":"构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”需要证明被告有意造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使人产生此类接触即将发生的紧迫恐惧感）。\n\n仅证明被告行为不合理是不够的。","analysis":"当被告意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），并导致原告发生此类接触时，即构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权。\n\n对接触表示同意是针对故意侵害身体（battery）指控的一种抗辩理由。\n\n因此，本案涉及的问题包括：吸烟者是否有意导致不吸烟者接触到香烟烟雾（选项 A）；烟雾是否具备造成接触所需的物理特性（选项 B）；接触香烟烟雾是否对不吸烟者造成了伤害或冒犯；以及不吸烟者自愿坐在餐厅合法吸烟区附近的行为是否构成对该接触的同意（选项 C）。然而，吸烟者的行为是否“不合理”并非本案的争议焦点。\n\n故意侵权要求证明被告具有主观故意（即具有某种目的，或明知其行为会导致特定结果）。\n\n相比之下，过失侵权要求证明被告未尽到合理的注意义务（即在类似情况下，一个通常谨慎的人应尽到的注意程度）。\n\n由于故意侵害身体（battery）属于故意侵权，因此必须证明存在故意行为；仅证明行为不合理是不够的。\n\n教学目标：故意侵害身体（battery）侵权要求证明被告意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感）。\n\n仅证明被告的行为不合理不足以构成故意侵害身体（battery）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于“意图”的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 13、18 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于“故意侵害身体（battery）”的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 3 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于“过失”的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 D：在当时的情况下，吸烟者的行为是否不合理？"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"designate","ipa":"/dˈɛzʌɡnˌeɪt/","syllables":"des·ig·nate","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 指定, 指明, 称呼；a. 已选出而未上任的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“designate”","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:designated/p:designated/i:designating/3:designates","sourceSentence":"An ordinance in a small town required all restaurants to designate smoking and nonsmoking sections for their customers.","synonyms":["intend","destine","specify","fate"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-454aa9267544bb37"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":31,"sourceQuestionPage":91,"sourceQuestionPages":[91],"sourceExplanationPages":[92,93],"sourceAnswerPages":[92,93],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-032","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"In the course of a bank holdup, a robber fired a gun at a guard. The guard drew his revolver and returned fire. One of the bullets fired by the guard ricocheted off the wall and struck a bystander. If the bystander asserts a battery claim against the guard, will she prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the guard did not intend to shoot the bystander.","B":"No, because the guard fired reasonably in his own defense.","C":"Yes, under the doctrine of transferred intent.","D":"Yes, unless the bystander was the robber's accomplice."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Self-defense is the privilege to use reasonable force to protect oneself against the intentional infliction of harmful or offensive contact by another. When acting in self- defense, the actor must use reasonable care to avoid harming bystanders (ie, refrain from unnecessarily dangerous conduct). If the actor fails to do so, then he/she is liable to any injured bystanders. Here, the guard was acting in self-defense when he returned fire against the armed robber. And though one of the guard's bullets ricocheted off the wall and struck a bystander, the guard used reasonable care because his responsive fire was not unnecessarily dangerous given the exigency of the encounter (eg, the guard used a revolver instead of a machine gun). Therefore, the bystander will not prevail on her battery claim. (Choices A & C) Liability for battery arises under the doctrine of transferred intent when the defendant intends to contact one person but instead contacts the plaintiff—even if the plaintiff was not a foreseeable victim. Here, although the guard only intended to shoot the robber, the guard's intent transferred to the bystander when she was hit by the guard's misfired bullet. But the bystander's battery claim will ultimately fail because the guard was privileged to shoot in self-defense and used reasonable care while doing so. (Choice D) The self-defense privilege shields a defendant from liability for an intentional tort committed against any third party, regardless of the third party's connection to the initial aggressor. Therefore, the bystander's battery claim would fail even if she was the robber's accomplice.\n\nEducational objective: An actor must use reasonable care to avoid harming bystanders when acting in self- defense. Otherwise, the actor will be liable to bystanders for any resulting harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 65 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (self-defense by deadly force).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 75 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (liability to third party for privileged conduct).","educationalObjective":"An actor must use reasonable care to avoid harming bystanders when acting in self- defense. Otherwise, the actor will be liable to bystanders for any resulting harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 65 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (self-defense by deadly force).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 75 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (liability to third party for privileged conduct).","rule":"An actor must use reasonable care to avoid harming bystanders when acting in self- defense. Otherwise, the actor will be liable to bystanders for any resulting harm.","irac":{"issue":"If the bystander asserts a battery claim against the guard, will she prevail?","rule":"An actor must use reasonable care to avoid harming bystanders when acting in self- defense. Otherwise, the actor will be liable to bystanders for any resulting harm.","analysis":"Self-defense is the privilege to use reasonable force to protect oneself against the intentional infliction of harmful or offensive contact by another. When acting in self- defense, the actor must use reasonable care to avoid harming bystanders (ie, refrain from unnecessarily dangerous conduct). If the actor fails to do so, then he/she is liable to any injured bystanders. Here, the guard was acting in self-defense when he returned fire against the armed robber. And though one of the guard's bullets ricocheted off the wall and struck a bystander, the guard used reasonable care because his responsive fire was not unnecessarily dangerous given the exigency of the encounter (eg, the guard used a revolver instead of a machine gun). Therefore, the bystander will not prevail on her battery claim. (Choices A & C) Liability for battery arises under the doctrine of transferred intent when the defendant intends to contact one person but instead contacts the plaintiff—even if the plaintiff was not a foreseeable victim. Here, although the guard only intended to shoot the robber, the guard's intent transferred to the bystander when she was hit by the guard's misfired bullet. But the bystander's battery claim will ultimately fail because the guard was privileged to shoot in self-defense and used reasonable care while doing so. (Choice D) The self-defense privilege shields a defendant from liability for an intentional tort committed against any third party, regardless of the third party's connection to the initial aggressor. Therefore, the bystander's battery claim would fail even if she was the robber's accomplice.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the guard fired reasonably in his own defense."},"questionZh":"在一次银行抢劫过程中，一名劫匪向保安开枪。\n\n保安拔出左轮手枪还击。\n\n保安射出的一颗子弹在墙壁上发生跳弹，击中了一名旁观者。\n\n如果该旁观者针对保安提出“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）的侵权索赔，她能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为保安并无意射击该旁观者。","B":"不能，因为保安是在正当防卫中采取了合理的开枪行为。","C":"能，根据“意图转移”（transferred intent）原则。","D":"能，除非该旁观者是劫匪的同伙。"},"explanationZh":"正当防卫是指为保护自身免受他人蓄意施加的有害或冒犯性接触而使用合理武力的权利。\n\n在进行正当防卫时，行为人必须尽到合理注意义务以避免伤害旁观者（即避免采取不必要的危险行为）。\n\n如果行为人未能做到这一点，则须对受伤害的旁观者承担责任。\n\n在本案中，保安在向持械抢劫犯还击时是在进行正当防卫。\n\n尽管保安的一发子弹因撞击墙壁反弹而击中了一名旁观者，但保安已尽到合理注意义务，因为鉴于当时情况的紧迫性，其还击行为并非不必要地危险（例如，保安使用的是左轮手枪而非机关枪）。\n\n因此，该旁观者提出的“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权索赔将无法获得支持。\n\n（选项 A 和 C）根据“意图转移”原则，当被告意图接触某人却接触了原告时（即使原告并非可预见的受害者），即产生“故意侵害身体（battery）”侵权责任。\n\n在本案中，尽管保安仅意图射击抢劫犯，但当旁观者被保安射出的流弹击中时，保安的意图转移到了该旁观者身上。\n\n然而，该旁观者的“故意侵害身体（battery）”索赔最终将不成立，因为保安享有正当防卫的权利，且在防卫过程中尽到了合理注意义务。\n\n（选项 D）正当防卫权可使被告免于承担因针对任何第三方实施蓄意侵权行为而产生的责任，无论该第三方与最初的加害人有何关联。\n\n因此，即使该旁观者是抢劫犯的同伙，她提出的“故意侵害身体（battery）”索赔也无法成立。\n\n教育目标：行为人在进行正当防卫时，必须尽到合理注意义务以避免伤害旁观者。\n\n否则，行为人须对由此造成的旁观者伤害承担责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 65 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（使用致命武力进行正当防卫）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 75 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（针对享有特权的行为对第三方承担的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"行为人在进行正当防卫时，必须尽到合理的注意义务以避免伤害旁观者。\n\n否则，行为人须对由此造成的伤害向旁观者承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该旁观者针对保安提出“故意侵害身体（battery）”的侵权索赔，她能胜诉吗？","rule":"行为人在进行正当防卫时，必须尽到合理的注意义务以避免伤害旁观者。\n\n否则，行为人须对由此造成的伤害向旁观者承担责任。","analysis":"正当防卫是指为保护自身免受他人蓄意施加的有害或冒犯性接触而使用合理武力的权利。\n\n在进行正当防卫时，行为人必须尽到合理注意义务以避免伤害旁观者（即避免采取不必要的危险行为）。\n\n如果行为人未能做到这一点，则须对受伤害的旁观者承担责任。\n\n在本案中，保安在向持械抢劫犯还击时是在进行正当防卫。\n\n尽管保安的一发子弹因撞击墙壁反弹而击中了一名旁观者，但保安已尽到合理注意义务，因为鉴于当时情况的紧迫性，其还击行为并非不必要地危险（例如，保安使用的是左轮手枪而非机关枪）。\n\n因此，该旁观者提出的“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权索赔将无法获得支持。\n\n（选项 A 和 C）根据“意图转移”原则，当被告意图接触某人却接触了原告时（即使原告并非可预见的受害者），即产生“故意侵害身体（battery）”侵权责任。\n\n在本案中，尽管保安仅意图射击抢劫犯，但当旁观者被保安射出的流弹击中时，保安的意图转移到了该旁观者身上。\n\n然而，该旁观者的“故意侵害身体（battery）”索赔最终将不成立，因为保安享有正当防卫的权利，且在防卫过程中尽到了合理注意义务。\n\n（选项 D）正当防卫权可使被告免于承担因针对任何第三方实施蓄意侵权行为而产生的责任，无论该第三方与最初的加害人有何关联。\n\n因此，即使该旁观者是抢劫犯的同伙，她提出的“故意侵害身体（battery）”索赔也无法成立。\n\n教育目标：行为人在进行正当防卫时，必须尽到合理注意义务以避免伤害旁观者。\n\n否则，行为人须对由此造成的旁观者伤害承担责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 65 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（使用致命武力进行正当防卫）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 75 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（针对享有特权的行为对第三方承担的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不能，因为保安是在正当防卫中采取了合理的开枪行为。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-a504b189709b5eb6"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":32,"sourceQuestionPage":94,"sourceQuestionPages":[94],"sourceExplanationPages":[95,96],"sourceAnswerPages":[95,96],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-033","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A man tied his dog to a bike rack in front of a store and left the dog there while he went inside to shop. The dog was usually friendly and placid. A five-year-old child started to tease the dog by pulling gently on its ears and tail. When the man emerged from the store and saw what the child was doing to the dog, he became extremely upset. Does the man have a viable claim against the child for trespass to chattels?","choices":{"A":"No, because the child did not injure the dog.","B":"No, because the child was too young to form the requisite intent.","C":"Yes, because the child touched the dog without the man's consent.","D":"Yes, because the child's acts caused the man extreme distress."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Trespass to chattels (ie, trespass de bonis asportatis) occurs when the defendant intentionally interferes with the plaintiff's possession of a chattel through dispossession, use, or intermeddling (ie, causing physical contact). Harm is inferred when the interference is through dispossession. But when the interference is through use or intermeddling, the plaintiff must prove one of the following harms: actual harm to the chattel (ie, impairment of its physical condition, quality, or value) substantial loss of use of the chattel or bodily harm to the plaintiff. Here, the child intentionally interfered with the man's dog (chattel) by pulling on its ears and tail (intermeddling). But there is no evidence that: the man's dog was injured (no actual harm) the man lost substantial use of his dog or the man suffered bodily harm. Therefore, the man does not have a viable claim against the child for trespass to chattels. (Choice B) A child of any age can commit an intentional tort like trespass to chattels as long as the child is capable of forming the requisite intent. Here, the child formed the intent to interfere with the man's possession of his dog through intermeddling when the child teased the dog by pulling its ears and tail. But since the child's intentional act did not cause the requisite harm, the child is not liable for trespass to chattels. (Choice C) Although the child touched the dog without the man's consent (intermeddling), the child is not liable for trespass to chattels because the child did not cause the requisite harm—actual harm to the dog, substantial loss of use of the dog, or bodily harm to the man. (Choice D) Trespass to chattels requires proof that the defendant's use of or intermeddling with the plaintiff's chattel caused the plaintiff bodily harm—not merely emotional harm. Therefore, the man's extreme emotional distress does not support this claim.\n\nEducational objective: Trespass to chattels by intentionally dispossessing the plaintiff of a chattel requires no proof of harm. However, intentional use of or intermeddling with the plaintiff's chattel requires proof of (1) actual harm to the chattel, (2) substantial loss of use of the chattel, or (3) bodily harm to the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 217 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (trespass to chattels).","educationalObjective":"Trespass to chattels by intentionally dispossessing the plaintiff of a chattel requires no proof of harm. However, intentional use of or intermeddling with the plaintiff's chattel requires proof of (1) actual harm to the chattel, (2) substantial loss of use of the chattel, or (3) bodily harm to the plaintiff.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 217 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (trespass to chattels).","rule":"Trespass to chattels by intentionally dispossessing the plaintiff of a chattel requires no proof of harm. However, intentional use of or intermeddling with the plaintiff's chattel requires proof of (1) actual harm to the chattel, (2) substantial loss of use of the chattel, or (3) bodily harm to the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"Does the man have a viable claim against the child for trespass to chattels?","rule":"Trespass to chattels by intentionally dispossessing the plaintiff of a chattel requires no proof of harm. However, intentional use of or intermeddling with the plaintiff's chattel requires proof of (1) actual harm to the chattel, (2) substantial loss of use of the chattel, or (3) bodily harm to the plaintiff.","analysis":"Trespass to chattels (ie, trespass de bonis asportatis) occurs when the defendant intentionally interferes with the plaintiff's possession of a chattel through dispossession, use, or intermeddling (ie, causing physical contact). Harm is inferred when the interference is through dispossession. But when the interference is through use or intermeddling, the plaintiff must prove one of the following harms: actual harm to the chattel (ie, impairment of its physical condition, quality, or value) substantial loss of use of the chattel or bodily harm to the plaintiff. Here, the child intentionally interfered with the man's dog (chattel) by pulling on its ears and tail (intermeddling). But there is no evidence that: the man's dog was injured (no actual harm) the man lost substantial use of his dog or the man suffered bodily harm. Therefore, the man does not have a viable claim against the child for trespass to chattels. (Choice B) A child of any age can commit an intentional tort like trespass to chattels as long as the child is capable of forming the requisite intent. Here, the child formed the intent to interfere with the man's possession of his dog through intermeddling when the child teased the dog by pulling its ears and tail. But since the child's intentional act did not cause the requisite harm, the child is not liable for trespass to chattels. (Choice C) Although the child touched the dog without the man's consent (intermeddling), the child is not liable for trespass to chattels because the child did not cause the requisite harm—actual harm to the dog, substantial loss of use of the dog, or bodily harm to the man. (Choice D) Trespass to chattels requires proof that the defendant's use of or intermeddling with the plaintiff's chattel caused the plaintiff bodily harm—not merely emotional harm. Therefore, the man's extreme emotional distress does not support this claim.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the child did not injure the dog."},"questionZh":"一名男子将狗拴在商店门前的自行车停放架上，随后进店购物。\n\n这只狗平时性情温和、安静。\n\n一名五岁儿童开始逗弄这只狗，轻轻拉扯它的耳朵和尾巴。\n\n当该男子从店里出来看到这一幕时，感到非常气愤。\n\n该男子能否就“侵犯动产”（trespass to chattels）向该儿童提出有效的索赔主张？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为该儿童并未伤害这只狗。","B":"不能，因为该儿童年龄太小，无法形成必要的意图。","C":"能，因为该儿童未经该男子同意触碰了这只狗。","D":"能，因为该儿童的行为给该男子造成了极度痛苦。"},"explanationZh":"侵犯动产（即“擅自搬走财物”之侵权行为，*trespass de bonis asportatis*）是指被告通过剥夺占有、使用或干预（即引起物理接触）的方式，故意干扰原告对动产的占有。\n\n若干扰方式为剥夺占有，则推定存在损害；但若干扰方式为使用或干预，原告必须证明存在以下损害之一：动产遭受实际损害（即物理状况、质量或价值受损）、动产的使用受到实质性剥夺，或原告遭受人身伤害。\n\n本案中，该儿童通过拉扯狗（动产）的耳朵和尾巴（属于干预行为），故意干扰了该男子对狗的占有。\n\n然而，没有证据表明：该男子的狗受伤（无实际损害）、该男子失去了对狗的实质性使用权，或该男子遭受了人身伤害。\n\n因此，该男子无法针对该儿童提出有效的侵犯动产之诉。\n\n（选项 B）只要儿童具备形成必要意图的能力，任何年龄的儿童都可能实施侵犯动产等故意侵权行为。\n\n本案中，该儿童在通过拉扯狗的耳朵和尾巴逗弄狗时，确实形成了干扰该男子占有其狗的意图（即干预意图）。\n\n但由于该儿童的故意行为并未造成必要的损害，因此该儿童不承担侵犯动产的责任。\n\n（选项 C）尽管该儿童在未经该男子同意的情况下触碰了狗（属于干预行为），但由于其未造成必要的损害——即狗遭受实际损害、狗的使用受到实质性剥夺或该男子遭受人身伤害——因此该儿童不承担侵犯动产的责任。\n\n（选项 D）侵犯动产之诉要求证明被告对原告动产的使用或干预造成了原告的人身伤害，而不仅仅是情感伤害。\n\n因此，该男子遭受的极度精神痛苦不足以支持该项诉讼主张。\n\n教育目标：若通过故意剥夺原告对动产的占有而构成侵犯动产，则无需证明存在损害。\n\n然而，若要构成对原告动产的故意使用或干涉，须证明存在以下情形之一：(1) 动产遭受实际损害；(2) 动产的使用受到实质性剥夺；或 (3) 原告遭受人身伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第217条（美国法律协会，1965年）（关于侵犯动产）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"若构成因故意剥夺原告对动产的占有而产生的“侵犯动产”行为，则无需证明造成了损害。\n\n然而，若构成因故意使用或干预原告动产而产生的“侵犯动产”行为，则必须证明：(1) 动产遭受了实际损害；(2) 动产的使用受到了实质性妨碍；或 (3) 原告遭受了人身伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"该男子能否就“侵犯动产”向该儿童提出有效的索赔主张？","rule":"若构成因故意剥夺原告对动产的占有而产生的“侵犯动产”行为，则无需证明造成了损害。\n\n然而，若构成因故意使用或干预原告动产而产生的“侵犯动产”行为，则必须证明：(1) 动产遭受了实际损害；(2) 动产的使用受到了实质性妨碍；或 (3) 原告遭受了人身伤害。","analysis":"侵犯动产（即“擅自搬走财物”之侵权行为，*trespass de bonis asportatis*）是指被告通过剥夺占有、使用或干预（即引起物理接触）的方式，故意干扰原告对动产的占有。\n\n若干扰方式为剥夺占有，则推定存在损害；但若干扰方式为使用或干预，原告必须证明存在以下损害之一：动产遭受实际损害（即物理状况、质量或价值受损）、动产的使用受到实质性剥夺，或原告遭受人身伤害。\n\n本案中，该儿童通过拉扯狗（动产）的耳朵和尾巴（属于干预行为），故意干扰了该男子对狗的占有。\n\n然而，没有证据表明：该男子的狗受伤（无实际损害）、该男子失去了对狗的实质性使用权，或该男子遭受了人身伤害。\n\n因此，该男子无法针对该儿童提出有效的侵犯动产之诉。\n\n（选项 B）只要儿童具备形成必要意图的能力，任何年龄的儿童都可能实施侵犯动产等故意侵权行为。\n\n本案中，该儿童在通过拉扯狗的耳朵和尾巴逗弄狗时，确实形成了干扰该男子占有其狗的意图（即干预意图）。\n\n但由于该儿童的故意行为并未造成必要的损害，因此该儿童不承担侵犯动产的责任。\n\n（选项 C）尽管该儿童在未经该男子同意的情况下触碰了狗（属于干预行为），但由于其未造成必要的损害——即狗遭受实际损害、狗的使用受到实质性剥夺或该男子遭受人身伤害——因此该儿童不承担侵犯动产的责任。\n\n（选项 D）侵犯动产之诉要求证明被告对原告动产的使用或干预造成了原告的人身伤害，而不仅仅是情感伤害。\n\n因此，该男子遭受的极度精神痛苦不足以支持该项诉讼主张。\n\n教育目标：若通过故意剥夺原告对动产的占有而构成侵犯动产，则无需证明存在损害。\n\n然而，若要构成对原告动产的故意使用或干涉，须证明存在以下情形之一：(1) 动产遭受实际损害；(2) 动产的使用受到实质性剥夺；或 (3) 原告遭受人身伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第217条（美国法律协会，1965年）（关于侵犯动产）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不能，因为该儿童并未伤害这只狗。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-1a2e161361d6c9d8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":33,"sourceQuestionPage":97,"sourceQuestionPages":[97],"sourceExplanationPages":[98,99],"sourceAnswerPages":[98,99],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-034","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A homeowner's next-door neighbor went into the homeowner's garage without permission and borrowed the homeowner's chain saw. The neighbor used the saw to clear broken branches from the trees on his property. After he had finished, the neighbor noticed several broken branches on the homeowner's trees that were in danger of falling on the homeowner's roof. While the neighbor was cutting the homeowner's branches, the saw broke. In a suit for conversion by the homeowner against the neighbor, will the homeowner recover?","choices":{"A":"No, because the neighbor did not intend to keep the saw.","B":"No, because the neighbor was using the saw to benefit the homeowner when it broke.","C":"Yes, for the actual damage to the saw.","D":"Yes, for the value of the saw when the neighbor borrowed it."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Trespass to chattels v. Conversion\n\nTrespass to Minor intentional interference with plaintiff's right to control chattel chattels Liable for actual damages—eg, cost of repairs, loss of use Conversion Substantial intentional interference with plaintiff's right to control chattel Liable for fair market value of chattel at time of conversion For conversion, the plaintiff must prove that: the defendant intentionally exerted dominion or control over the plaintiff's chattel and the interference was so severe that the plaintiff should recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion. Here, the neighbor converted the homeowner's saw when he borrowed it without permission and used it to clear broken tree branches on his property. Since the neighbor then broke the saw, the homeowner should recover the fair market value of the saw at the time the neighbor borrowed it—the time of conversion. (Choice A) Even though the neighbor did not intend to keep the saw, he is liable for conversion because he borrowed the saw without the homeowner's permission and broke it. (Choice B) A defendant who uses the plaintiff's chattel without permission may be liable for conversion even if he/she acts with the intent to benefit the plaintiff (here, cutting the homeowner's tree branches). (Choice C) Since the remedy for conversion is the fair market value of the chattel, the homeowner can recover the entire value of the saw—not just the actual damage to the saw (eg, repair costs). In contrast, the remedy for trespass to chattels is limited to actual damage.\n\nEducational objective: Conversion is an intentional exertion of dominion or control over chattel that so seriously interferes with the owner's right of control that the owner should recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 222A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (conversion).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 227 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (conversion by using chattel).","educationalObjective":"Conversion is an intentional exertion of dominion or control over chattel that so seriously interferes with the owner's right of control that the owner should recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 222A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (conversion).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 227 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (conversion by using chattel).","rule":"Conversion is an intentional exertion of dominion or control over chattel that so seriously interferes with the owner's right of control that the owner should recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit for conversion by the homeowner against the neighbor, will the homeowner recover?","rule":"Conversion is an intentional exertion of dominion or control over chattel that so seriously interferes with the owner's right of control that the owner should recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion.","analysis":"Trespass to chattels v. Conversion\n\nTrespass to Minor intentional interference with plaintiff's right to control chattel chattels Liable for actual damages—eg, cost of repairs, loss of use Conversion Substantial intentional interference with plaintiff's right to control chattel Liable for fair market value of chattel at time of conversion For conversion, the plaintiff must prove that: the defendant intentionally exerted dominion or control over the plaintiff's chattel and the interference was so severe that the plaintiff should recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion. Here, the neighbor converted the homeowner's saw when he borrowed it without permission and used it to clear broken tree branches on his property. Since the neighbor then broke the saw, the homeowner should recover the fair market value of the saw at the time the neighbor borrowed it—the time of conversion. (Choice A) Even though the neighbor did not intend to keep the saw, he is liable for conversion because he borrowed the saw without the homeowner's permission and broke it. (Choice B) A defendant who uses the plaintiff's chattel without permission may be liable for conversion even if he/she acts with the intent to benefit the plaintiff (here, cutting the homeowner's tree branches). (Choice C) Since the remedy for conversion is the fair market value of the chattel, the homeowner can recover the entire value of the saw—not just the actual damage to the saw (eg, repair costs). In contrast, the remedy for trespass to chattels is limited to actual damage.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, for the value of the saw when the neighbor borrowed it."},"questionZh":"房主的邻居未经允许进入房主的车库，借用了房主的链锯。\n\n邻居用链锯清理自家树木上的断枝。\n\n清理完毕后，邻居发现房主家的树上还有几根断枝，随时可能掉到房主家的屋顶上。\n\n邻居在清理断枝时，链锯断了。\n\n如果房主起诉邻居侵占，房主能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为邻居无意占有链锯。","B":"不能，因为邻居在链锯断之前是为了房主的利益而使用链锯。","C":"可以，可以赔偿链锯的实际损失。","D":"可以，可以赔偿链锯在邻居借用时的价值。"},"explanationZh":"侵害动产（trespass to chattels）（Trespass to chattels）与动产侵占（Conversion）的区别 侵害动产（trespass to chattels）：指对原告控制动产的权利进行的轻微故意干扰；责任范围限于实际损害（例如：修理费、使用价值损失）。动产侵占：指对原告控制动产的权利进行的实质性故意干扰；责任范围为侵占发生时该动产的公平市场价值。对于动产侵占，原告必须证明：被告故意对原告的动产行使支配权或控制权，且这种干扰严重到原告有权获得侵占发生时该动产的公平市场价值。\n\n在本案中，邻居未经许可借用房主的锯子，并用其清理自家地块上的断树枝，这一行为构成了动产侵占。\n\n由于邻居随后弄坏了锯子，房主有权获得该锯子在邻居借用时（即侵占发生时）的公平市场价值。\n\n（选项 A）尽管邻居并无意据为己有，但他仍需承担动产侵占的责任，因为他在未经房主许可的情况下借用了锯子并将其弄坏。\n\n（选项 B）未经许可使用原告动产的被告，即使其行为意在使原告受益（本案中为修剪房主的树枝），也可能构成动产侵占。\n\n（选项 C）由于动产侵占的救济方式是赔偿动产的公平市场价值，房主可以获得锯子的全部价值，而不仅仅是针对锯子的实际损害赔偿（如修理费）。\n\n相比之下，侵害动产（trespass to chattels）的救济仅限于实际损害赔偿。\n\n教育目标：动产侵占是指故意对动产行使支配权或控制权，且这种行为严重干扰了所有者的控制权，致使所有者有权获得侵占发生时该动产的公平市场价值。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 222A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（动产侵占）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 227 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因使用动产而构成的动产侵占）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"侵占是指故意对动产行使支配或控制权，严重妨碍所有者的控制权，以至于所有者应获得侵占发生时动产的公平市场价值赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"在房主起诉邻居侵占动产的诉讼中，房主能否胜诉？","rule":"侵占是指故意对动产行使支配或控制权，严重妨碍所有者的控制权，以至于所有者应获得侵占发生时动产的公平市场价值赔偿。","analysis":"侵害动产（trespass to chattels）（Trespass to chattels）与动产侵占（Conversion）的区别 侵害动产（trespass to chattels）：指对原告控制动产的权利进行的轻微故意干扰；责任范围限于实际损害（例如：修理费、使用价值损失）。动产侵占：指对原告控制动产的权利进行的实质性故意干扰；责任范围为侵占发生时该动产的公平市场价值。对于动产侵占，原告必须证明：被告故意对原告的动产行使支配权或控制权，且这种干扰严重到原告有权获得侵占发生时该动产的公平市场价值。\n\n在本案中，邻居未经许可借用房主的锯子，并用其清理自家地块上的断树枝，这一行为构成了动产侵占。\n\n由于邻居随后弄坏了锯子，房主有权获得该锯子在邻居借用时（即侵占发生时）的公平市场价值。\n\n（选项 A）尽管邻居并无意据为己有，但他仍需承担动产侵占的责任，因为他在未经房主许可的情况下借用了锯子并将其弄坏。\n\n（选项 B）未经许可使用原告动产的被告，即使其行为意在使原告受益（本案中为修剪房主的树枝），也可能构成动产侵占。\n\n（选项 C）由于动产侵占的救济方式是赔偿动产的公平市场价值，房主可以获得锯子的全部价值，而不仅仅是针对锯子的实际损害赔偿（如修理费）。\n\n相比之下，侵害动产（trespass to chattels）的救济仅限于实际损害赔偿。\n\n教育目标：动产侵占是指故意对动产行使支配权或控制权，且这种行为严重干扰了所有者的控制权，致使所有者有权获得侵占发生时该动产的公平市场价值。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 222A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（动产侵占）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 227 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因使用动产而构成的动产侵占）。","conclusion":"答案 D：可以，可以赔偿链锯在邻居借用时的价值。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"dominion","ipa":"/dʌmˈɪnjən/","syllables":"do·min·ion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 领土, 主权, 统治；[法] 统治权, 主权, 统治","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“dominion”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:dominions","sourceSentence":"Conversion is an intentional exertion of dominion or control over chattel that so seriously interferes with the owner's right of control that the owner should recover the fair market value of the chattel at the time of the conversion.","synonyms":["district","territory","territorial dominion","rule"]},{"term":"converted","ipa":"/kʌnvˈɝtɪd/","syllables":"con·verted","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 修改的；改变信仰的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“converted”","wordForm":"词；原形 convert；词形 0:convert/1:dp/p:converted/d:converted","sourceSentence":"Here, the neighbor converted the homeowner's saw when he borrowed it without permission and used it to clear broken tree branches on his property.","synonyms":["change","exchange","commute","change over"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":34,"sourceQuestionPage":100,"sourceQuestionPages":[100],"sourceExplanationPages":[101,102],"sourceAnswerPages":[101,102],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-035","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A host pointed an unloaded revolver at her guest, threatening to shoot him. The guest knew that the revolver was not loaded, and that the ammunition for the revolver was stored in a locked basement closet, two stories below where the two were then standing. In an action brought by the guest against the host for assault, will the guest be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the host did not intend to shoot her guest.","B":"No, because the host did not put her guest in apprehension of imminent contact.","C":"Yes, because the ammunition was accessible to the host.","D":"Yes, because the host threatened her guest with a revolver."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Assault requires that the defendant: intend to inflict (1) harmful or offensive contact or (2) imminent apprehension of such contact and cause the plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent contact. Apprehension only exists when the plaintiff believes that the defendant can cause imminent contact. Here, the guest did not believe that the host could cause imminent contact because the guest knew that the host's revolver was not loaded and that her ammunition was in a locked basement closet below. As a result, the guest was not in apprehension of imminent contact, and the host is not liable for assault. (Choice A) A defendant must intend to cause harmful or offensive contact OR imminent apprehension of such contact. Although the host did not intend to shoot the guest (the revolver was unloaded), she did intend to scare her guest by threatening to shoot him (imminent apprehension). Had the host succeeded in scaring the guest, the host would be liable for assault. (Choice C) Assault requires apprehension of imminent contact. Imminent means without significant delay. Although the ammunition was accessible to the host, it could not be retrieved without significant delay because it was locked in a closet two stories below. (Choice D) A threat alone does not constitute an assault. The threat must cause apprehension of imminent contact.\n\nEducational objective: An assault occurs when a defendant intentionally causes a plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent contact. Apprehension only exists when the plaintiff believes that the defendant can cause such contact.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of assault).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (apprehension requirement).","educationalObjective":"An assault occurs when a defendant intentionally causes a plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent contact. Apprehension only exists when the plaintiff believes that the defendant can cause such contact.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of assault).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (apprehension requirement).","rule":"An assault occurs when a defendant intentionally causes a plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent contact. Apprehension only exists when the plaintiff believes that the defendant can cause such contact.","irac":{"issue":"In an action brought by the guest against the host for assault, will the guest be likely to prevail?","rule":"An assault occurs when a defendant intentionally causes a plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent contact. Apprehension only exists when the plaintiff believes that the defendant can cause such contact.","analysis":"Assault requires that the defendant: intend to inflict (1) harmful or offensive contact or (2) imminent apprehension of such contact and cause the plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent contact. Apprehension only exists when the plaintiff believes that the defendant can cause imminent contact. Here, the guest did not believe that the host could cause imminent contact because the guest knew that the host's revolver was not loaded and that her ammunition was in a locked basement closet below. As a result, the guest was not in apprehension of imminent contact, and the host is not liable for assault. (Choice A) A defendant must intend to cause harmful or offensive contact OR imminent apprehension of such contact. Although the host did not intend to shoot the guest (the revolver was unloaded), she did intend to scare her guest by threatening to shoot him (imminent apprehension). Had the host succeeded in scaring the guest, the host would be liable for assault. (Choice C) Assault requires apprehension of imminent contact. Imminent means without significant delay. Although the ammunition was accessible to the host, it could not be retrieved without significant delay because it was locked in a closet two stories below. (Choice D) A threat alone does not constitute an assault. The threat must cause apprehension of imminent contact.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the host did not put her guest in apprehension of imminent contact."},"questionZh":"一位女主人用一把未上膛的左轮手枪指着她的客人，威胁要开枪。\n\n客人知道这把手枪没有子弹，而且子弹存放在地下室一个上锁的壁橱里，比他们当时所站的位置低两层。\n\n如果客人以袭击罪起诉女主人，客人胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为女主人并没有打算开枪射杀她的客人。","B":"否，因为女主人并没有让她的客人感到即将受到使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）。","C":"是，因为女主人可以轻易拿到子弹。","D":"是，因为女主人用左轮手枪威胁了她的客人。"},"explanationZh":"构成“袭击”（assault）的要件包括：被告有意造成 (1) 有害或冒犯性的接触，或 (2) 对此类接触即将发生的预感（apprehension），并使原告产生对接触即将发生的合理预感。\n\n只有当原告相信被告能够造成即将发生的接触时，这种“预感”才成立。\n\n在本案中，客人并不认为主人能造成即将发生的接触，因为他知道主人的左轮手枪未装子弹，且弹药锁在楼下地下室的壁橱里。\n\n因此，客人并未产生对接触即将发生的预感，主人不承担袭击的法律责任。\n\n（选项 A）被告必须有意造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或者有意造成对该类接触即将发生的预感。\n\n尽管主人无意向客人开枪（左轮手枪未装子弹），但她确实有意通过威胁开枪来吓唬客人（即意图造成“即将发生接触”的预感）。\n\n如果主人成功吓到了客人，她就需要承担袭击的法律责任。\n\n（选项 C）袭击要求原告产生对接触即将发生的预感。\n\n“即将发生”（imminent）意味着没有显著的延迟。\n\n尽管主人可以获取弹药，但由于弹药锁在两层楼下的壁橱里，无法在不产生显著延迟的情况下取到。\n\n（选项 D）单纯的威胁并不构成袭击。\n\n威胁必须引起对接触即将发生的预感。\n\n教学目标：当被告有意使原告产生对接触即将发生的合理预感时，即构成袭击。\n\n只有当原告相信被告能够造成此类接触时，这种预感才成立。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 21 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（袭击的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 24 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于预感的要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当被告故意使原告合理地感到即将受到使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）时，就构成了袭击。\n\n只有当原告相信被告能够造成这种使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）时，这种恐惧感才存在。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果客人起诉主人人身使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault），客人胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"当被告故意使原告合理地感到即将受到使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）时，就构成了袭击。\n\n只有当原告相信被告能够造成这种使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）时，这种恐惧感才存在。","analysis":"构成“袭击”（assault）的要件包括：被告有意造成 (1) 有害或冒犯性的接触，或 (2) 对此类接触即将发生的预感（apprehension），并使原告产生对接触即将发生的合理预感。\n\n只有当原告相信被告能够造成即将发生的接触时，这种“预感”才成立。\n\n在本案中，客人并不认为主人能造成即将发生的接触，因为他知道主人的左轮手枪未装子弹，且弹药锁在楼下地下室的壁橱里。\n\n因此，客人并未产生对接触即将发生的预感，主人不承担袭击的法律责任。\n\n（选项 A）被告必须有意造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或者有意造成对该类接触即将发生的预感。\n\n尽管主人无意向客人开枪（左轮手枪未装子弹），但她确实有意通过威胁开枪来吓唬客人（即意图造成“即将发生接触”的预感）。\n\n如果主人成功吓到了客人，她就需要承担袭击的法律责任。\n\n（选项 C）袭击要求原告产生对接触即将发生的预感。\n\n“即将发生”（imminent）意味着没有显著的延迟。\n\n尽管主人可以获取弹药，但由于弹药锁在两层楼下的壁橱里，无法在不产生显著延迟的情况下取到。\n\n（选项 D）单纯的威胁并不构成袭击。\n\n威胁必须引起对接触即将发生的预感。\n\n教学目标：当被告有意使原告产生对接触即将发生的合理预感时，即构成袭击。\n\n只有当原告相信被告能够造成此类接触时，这种预感才成立。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 21 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（袭击的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 24 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于预感的要求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：否，因为女主人并没有让她的客人感到即将受到使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-f72c99d82b7d6655"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":35,"sourceQuestionPage":103,"sourceQuestionPages":[103],"sourceExplanationPages":[104,105,106],"sourceAnswerPages":[104,105,106],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-036","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A well-known politician and former military service member had developed posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) after surviving a deadly explosion. A young woman, who had heard that the politician suffered from PTSD, decided to play a joke on him by setting off small fireworks several yards from him as he spoke at a college campus. The sudden loud noise startled the politician, causing him to lose his balance and fall down. For several months after the incident, the politician experienced flashbacks, nightmares, and severe anxiety. The politician filed a tort action against the young woman seeking damages for his emotional distress resulting from her reckless behavior. Will the politician be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the politician was not in the zone of danger as the fireworks were set off several yards away.","B":"No, because the young woman lacked the malicious intent necessary to support the politician's claim.","C":"Yes, because the young woman exploited the politician's known vulnerability.","D":"Yes, because the young woman's intent to assault the politician transfers to his claim for emotional distress."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Extreme and outrageous conduct\n\nFlagrant indecency Exploiting known & special vulnerability Abusing authority Repeated harassment Mnemonic: FEAR\n\nTo recover for intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED), the plaintiff must prove that the defendant: engaged in extreme and outrageous conduct\n\nintended to cause the plaintiff severe emotional distress OR recklessly disregarded the risk of causing such distress and\n\ncaused the plaintiff severe emotional distress.\n\nConduct is more likely to be extreme and outrageous if, among other things, the defendant knew of the plaintiff's special vulnerability and deliberately exploited it. Here, the young woman knew of and deliberately exploited the politician's PTSD (special vulnerability) when she set off fireworks within a few yards of him to play a joke on him (extreme and outrageous conduct). In doing so, she knew (intent) or was indifferent to the obvious risk (recklessness) that her conduct would cause the politician severe emotional distress. And because this conduct caused the politician to experience flashbacks, nightmares, and severe anxiety (severe emotional distress), he will likely prevail. (Choice A) Zone of danger is a theory of recovery for negligent infliction of emotional distress. It requires proof that the plaintiff was in danger of bodily injury and that the danger caused the plaintiff serious emotional distress. But here, the politician can recover for the emotional distress intentionally inflicted on him by the young woman's extreme and outrageous conduct—deliberately exploiting the politician's known vulnerability. (Choice B) A public figure (eg, politician) may recover for IIED by reason of publication only if the publication contained a false statement of fact that was made with actual malice. But since there was no publication here, actual malice is not needed for an IIED claim. (Choice D) Transferred intent exists when a person intends to commit an intentional tort (eg, assault) against a person but instead commits a different intentional tort (eg, battery) against that person. However, transferred intent generally does not apply to intentional infliction of emotional distress.\n\nEducational objective: Intentional infliction of emotional distress requires proof of extreme and outrageous conduct. Conduct is more likely to be extreme and outrageous if the defendant deliberately exploited a known vulnerability of the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress).","educationalObjective":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress requires proof of extreme and outrageous conduct. Conduct is more likely to be extreme and outrageous if the defendant deliberately exploited a known vulnerability of the plaintiff.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress).","rule":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress requires proof of extreme and outrageous conduct. Conduct is more likely to be extreme and outrageous if the defendant deliberately exploited a known vulnerability of the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"Will the politician be likely to prevail?","rule":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress requires proof of extreme and outrageous conduct. Conduct is more likely to be extreme and outrageous if the defendant deliberately exploited a known vulnerability of the plaintiff.","analysis":"Extreme and outrageous conduct\n\nFlagrant indecency Exploiting known & special vulnerability Abusing authority Repeated harassment Mnemonic: FEAR\n\nTo recover for intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED), the plaintiff must prove that the defendant: engaged in extreme and outrageous conduct\n\nintended to cause the plaintiff severe emotional distress OR recklessly disregarded the risk of causing such distress and\n\ncaused the plaintiff severe emotional distress.\n\nConduct is more likely to be extreme and outrageous if, among other things, the defendant knew of the plaintiff's special vulnerability and deliberately exploited it. Here, the young woman knew of and deliberately exploited the politician's PTSD (special vulnerability) when she set off fireworks within a few yards of him to play a joke on him (extreme and outrageous conduct). In doing so, she knew (intent) or was indifferent to the obvious risk (recklessness) that her conduct would cause the politician severe emotional distress. And because this conduct caused the politician to experience flashbacks, nightmares, and severe anxiety (severe emotional distress), he will likely prevail. (Choice A) Zone of danger is a theory of recovery for negligent infliction of emotional distress. It requires proof that the plaintiff was in danger of bodily injury and that the danger caused the plaintiff serious emotional distress. But here, the politician can recover for the emotional distress intentionally inflicted on him by the young woman's extreme and outrageous conduct—deliberately exploiting the politician's known vulnerability. (Choice B) A public figure (eg, politician) may recover for IIED by reason of publication only if the publication contained a false statement of fact that was made with actual malice. But since there was no publication here, actual malice is not needed for an IIED claim. (Choice D) Transferred intent exists when a person intends to commit an intentional tort (eg, assault) against a person but instead commits a different intentional tort (eg, battery) against that person. However, transferred intent generally does not apply to intentional infliction of emotional distress.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the young woman exploited the politician's known vulnerability."},"questionZh":"一位知名政治家兼退伍军人在经历了一次致命爆炸后患上了创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）。\n\n一名年轻女子得知该政治家患有PTSD后，决定对他搞个恶作剧：当他在大学校园发表演讲时，她在离他仅几码远的地方燃放了小型烟花。\n\n突如其来的巨大声响惊吓到了这位政治家，导致他失去平衡并摔倒在地。\n\n事发后的几个月里，该政治家饱受创伤重现（flashbacks）、噩梦和严重焦虑的困扰。\n\n该政治家针对这名年轻女子提起了侵权诉讼，就其鲁莽行为导致的精神痛苦寻求损害赔偿。\n\n这位政治家胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为烟花是在几码外燃放的，该政治家并未处于“危险区域”（zone of danger）内。","B":"不大，因为该年轻女子缺乏支持该政治家诉求所需的恶意。","C":"大，因为该年轻女子利用了该政治家已知的脆弱性。","D":"大，因为该年轻女子意图袭击该政治家的主观意图，可转移适用于其关于精神痛苦的索赔主张。"},"explanationZh":"极端且令人愤慨的行为 公然的不当行为；利用已知的特殊脆弱性；滥用职权；反复骚扰。记忆口诀：FEAR（恐惧）。 若要就“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）主张赔偿，原告必须证明被告：实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为； 意图导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦，或者罔顾导致此类痛苦的风险；以及 确实导致原告遭受了严重精神痛苦。\n\n如果被告明知原告存在某种特殊脆弱性并蓄意加以利用，那么其行为就更有可能被认定为极端且令人愤慨。\n\n在本案中，该年轻女子为了恶作剧，在距离那位政治家仅几码远的地方燃放烟花，此时她明知并蓄意利用了该政治家患有创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）这一特殊脆弱性（这构成了极端且令人愤慨的行为）。\n\n在实施该行为时，她明知（具有意图）或对显而易见的风险漠不关心（具有罔顾后果的鲁莽），即意识到其行为会导致该政治家遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n由于该行为确实导致该政治家出现了闪回、噩梦和严重焦虑（即严重精神痛苦），他很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）“危险区域”（Zone of danger）理论适用于因过失导致精神痛苦的索赔主张。\n\n该理论要求证明原告处于遭受身体伤害的危险之中，且该危险导致原告遭受了严重精神痛苦。\n\n但在本案中，该政治家可以因年轻女子极端且令人愤慨的行为（即蓄意利用其已知的脆弱性）而故意造成的精神痛苦获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）公众人物（如政治家）若因“发布”行为而就 IIED 寻求赔偿，则必须证明发布内容包含虚假事实陈述，且该陈述是在“实际恶意”下作出的。\n\n但本案不涉及发布行为，因此 IIED 索赔无需证明存在实际恶意。\n\n（选项 D）“意图转移”（Transferred intent）是指某人意图针对某人实施某种故意侵权行为（如袭击/assault），结果却针对该人实施了另一种故意侵权行为（如故意侵害身体（battery）/battery）。\n\n然而，意图转移原则通常不适用于“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”这一侵权类型。\n\n教育目标：“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”的索赔要求证明存在极端且令人愤慨的行为。\n\n如果被告蓄意利用原告已知的脆弱性，其行为就更有可能被认定为极端且令人愤慨。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》（Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability）\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害》第46条（美国法律协会，2012年）（故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"构成“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（intentional infliction of emotional distress）的侵权行为，需要证明存在极端且令人愤慨（extreme and outrageous）的行为。\n\n如果被告蓄意利用原告已知的脆弱性，则其行为更有可能被认定为极端且令人愤慨。","iracZh":{"issue":"这位政治家胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"构成“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（intentional infliction of emotional distress）的侵权行为，需要证明存在极端且令人愤慨（extreme and outrageous）的行为。\n\n如果被告蓄意利用原告已知的脆弱性，则其行为更有可能被认定为极端且令人愤慨。","analysis":"极端且令人愤慨的行为 公然的不当行为；利用已知的特殊脆弱性；滥用职权；反复骚扰。记忆口诀：FEAR（恐惧）。 若要就“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）主张赔偿，原告必须证明被告：实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为； 意图导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦，或者罔顾导致此类痛苦的风险；以及 确实导致原告遭受了严重精神痛苦。\n\n如果被告明知原告存在某种特殊脆弱性并蓄意加以利用，那么其行为就更有可能被认定为极端且令人愤慨。\n\n在本案中，该年轻女子为了恶作剧，在距离那位政治家仅几码远的地方燃放烟花，此时她明知并蓄意利用了该政治家患有创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）这一特殊脆弱性（这构成了极端且令人愤慨的行为）。\n\n在实施该行为时，她明知（具有意图）或对显而易见的风险漠不关心（具有罔顾后果的鲁莽），即意识到其行为会导致该政治家遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n由于该行为确实导致该政治家出现了闪回、噩梦和严重焦虑（即严重精神痛苦），他很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）“危险区域”（Zone of danger）理论适用于因过失导致精神痛苦的索赔主张。\n\n该理论要求证明原告处于遭受身体伤害的危险之中，且该危险导致原告遭受了严重精神痛苦。\n\n但在本案中，该政治家可以因年轻女子极端且令人愤慨的行为（即蓄意利用其已知的脆弱性）而故意造成的精神痛苦获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）公众人物（如政治家）若因“发布”行为而就 IIED 寻求赔偿，则必须证明发布内容包含虚假事实陈述，且该陈述是在“实际恶意”下作出的。\n\n但本案不涉及发布行为，因此 IIED 索赔无需证明存在实际恶意。\n\n（选项 D）“意图转移”（Transferred intent）是指某人意图针对某人实施某种故意侵权行为（如袭击/assault），结果却针对该人实施了另一种故意侵权行为（如故意侵害身体（battery）/battery）。\n\n然而，意图转移原则通常不适用于“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”这一侵权类型。\n\n教育目标：“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”的索赔要求证明存在极端且令人愤慨的行为。\n\n如果被告蓄意利用原告已知的脆弱性，其行为就更有可能被认定为极端且令人愤慨。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》（Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability）\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害》第46条（美国法律协会，2012年）（故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦）。","conclusion":"答案 C：大，因为该年轻女子利用了该政治家已知的脆弱性。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"zone of danger","ipa":"/zˈoʊn ˈəv dˈeɪndʒɝ/","syllables":"zone of dan·ger","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"危险区域：被告行为使人面临可预见身体危险的范围","contextualMeaning":"危险区域：被告行为使人面临可预见身体危险的范围","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the politician was not in the zone of danger as the fireworks were set off several yards away.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"actual malice","ipa":"/ˈæktʃəwəl mˈæləs/","syllables":"ac·tual mal·ice","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"实际恶意：明知虚假或鲁莽无视真伪","contextualMeaning":"实际恶意：明知虚假或鲁莽无视真伪","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) A public figure (eg, politician) may recover for IIED by reason of publication only if the publication contained a false statement of fact that was made with actual malice.","synonyms":["knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":36,"sourceQuestionPage":107,"sourceQuestionPages":[107],"sourceExplanationPages":[108,109],"sourceAnswerPages":[108,109],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-037","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"During a deer-hunting season open to rifle hunters, a hunter saw a deer in the forest. He shot his rifle at the deer, hoping to hit and kill it. Instead, he hit and injured a hiker. The hunter had not realized that the hiker was there. Does the injured hiker have an actionable battery claim against the hunter?","choices":{"A":"No, because the hunter did not intend to shoot the hiker.","B":"No, because the hunter did not make direct physical contact with the hiker.","C":"Yes, because the bullet from the hunter's rifle made direct physical contact with the hiker.","D":"Yes, because the hunter intentionally shot the rifle."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Battery requires that the defendant act with intent, which is either: the purpose of inflicting harmful / offensive contact or creating imminent apprehension thereof or the knowledge that such contact or apprehension is substantially certain to occur. If the intentional act causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff, then the defendant is liable for battery. Here, the hunter shot his rifle with the purpose of hitting the deer—not the hiker. Additionally, the hunter had no knowledge that the hiker was there, so the hunter could not have been substantially certain that he would hit the hiker when he shot his rifle. Since the hunter did not shoot his rifle with the intent to hit the hiker, the hunter is not liable for battery. (Choice B) Contact can be either direct (eg, the defendant punches the plaintiff) or indirect (eg, the defendant fires a bullet at the plaintiff). Although the hunter did not make direct physical contact with the hiker, the hunter made indirect contact by shooting the hiker. (Choice C) Battery requires proof of both intent and contact. While the bullet from the hunter's rifle made physical contact with the hiker, the hunter is not liable for battery because he did not intend to shoot the hiker. (Choice D) For battery, a defendant must intend to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or create the imminent apprehension thereof) with the plaintiff. Although the hunter intentionally shot the rifle, he is not liable for battery because he did not shoot with the intent to make contact with the hiker.\n\nEducational objective: Battery requires that a defendant act with the (1) purpose of inflicting harmful / offensive contact or creating the imminent apprehension thereof or (2) knowledge that such contact or apprehension is substantially certain to occur.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).","educationalObjective":"Battery requires that a defendant act with the (1) purpose of inflicting harmful / offensive contact or creating the imminent apprehension thereof or (2) knowledge that such contact or apprehension is substantially certain to occur.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).","rule":"Battery requires that a defendant act with the (1) purpose of inflicting harmful / offensive contact or creating the imminent apprehension thereof or (2) knowledge that such contact or apprehension is substantially certain to occur.","irac":{"issue":"Does the injured hiker have an actionable battery claim against the hunter?","rule":"Battery requires that a defendant act with the (1) purpose of inflicting harmful / offensive contact or creating the imminent apprehension thereof or (2) knowledge that such contact or apprehension is substantially certain to occur.","analysis":"Battery requires that the defendant act with intent, which is either: the purpose of inflicting harmful / offensive contact or creating imminent apprehension thereof or the knowledge that such contact or apprehension is substantially certain to occur. If the intentional act causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff, then the defendant is liable for battery. Here, the hunter shot his rifle with the purpose of hitting the deer—not the hiker. Additionally, the hunter had no knowledge that the hiker was there, so the hunter could not have been substantially certain that he would hit the hiker when he shot his rifle. Since the hunter did not shoot his rifle with the intent to hit the hiker, the hunter is not liable for battery. (Choice B) Contact can be either direct (eg, the defendant punches the plaintiff) or indirect (eg, the defendant fires a bullet at the plaintiff). Although the hunter did not make direct physical contact with the hiker, the hunter made indirect contact by shooting the hiker. (Choice C) Battery requires proof of both intent and contact. While the bullet from the hunter's rifle made physical contact with the hiker, the hunter is not liable for battery because he did not intend to shoot the hiker. (Choice D) For battery, a defendant must intend to inflict harmful or offensive contact (or create the imminent apprehension thereof) with the plaintiff. Although the hunter intentionally shot the rifle, he is not liable for battery because he did not shoot with the intent to make contact with the hiker.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the hunter did not intend to shoot the hiker."},"questionZh":"在允许步枪猎人狩猎的鹿季期间，一名猎人在森林里发现了一只鹿。\n\n他向鹿开枪，希望击毙它。\n\n结果，他却击中并打伤了一名徒步旅行者。\n\n猎人当时并没有意识到徒步旅行者的存在。\n\n受伤的徒步旅行者是否可以对猎人提起人身伤害诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为猎人并非有意射击徒步旅行者。","B":"否，因为猎人没有与徒步旅行者发生直接的身体接触。","C":"是，因为猎人步枪的子弹与徒步旅行者发生了直接的身体接触。","D":"是，因为猎人是故意开枪的。"},"explanationZh":"构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为，要求被告具有相应的意图，即：旨在造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或制造此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），或者明知此类接触或恐惧感极有可能发生。\n\n如果该故意行为导致了针对原告的有害或冒犯性接触，则被告须承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任。\n\n在本案中，猎人开枪的目的是射中鹿，而非徒步旅行者。\n\n此外，猎人并不知道徒步旅行者的存在，因此他在开枪时也不可能确信自己会击中徒步旅行者。\n\n由于猎人开枪并非出于击中徒步旅行者的意图，他不承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任。\n\n（选项 B）接触既可以是直接的（例如，被告挥拳击打原告），也可以是间接的（例如，被告向原告发射子弹）。\n\n尽管猎人未与徒步旅行者发生直接的身体接触，但他通过射击行为造成了间接接触。\n\n（选项 C）故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的成立需要同时证明存在意图和接触行为。\n\n虽然猎人枪中的子弹确实与徒步旅行者发生了身体接触，但由于猎人并无射击徒步旅行者的意图，他不承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任。\n\n（选项 D）对于故意侵害身体（battery）侵权而言，被告必须有意对原告造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或制造此类接触即将发生的恐惧感）。\n\n尽管猎人是故意开枪的，但他并不承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任，因为他开枪并非出于与徒步旅行者发生接触的意图。\n\n教育目标：故意侵害身体（battery）侵权要求被告的行为具备以下要素之一：（1）旨在造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或制造此类接触即将发生的恐惧感；或（2）明知此类接触或恐惧感极有可能发生。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"人身伤害罪要求被告的行为必须满足以下两个条件之一：(1) 意图造成有害/冒犯性接触或使受害人感到即将遭受此类接触/冒犯性接触；或 (2) 明知此类接触或即将遭受此类接触/冒犯性接触几乎肯定会发生。","iracZh":{"issue":"受伤的徒步者是否可以对猎人提起人身伤害诉讼？","rule":"人身伤害罪要求被告的行为必须满足以下两个条件之一：(1) 意图造成有害/冒犯性接触或使受害人感到即将遭受此类接触/冒犯性接触；或 (2) 明知此类接触或即将遭受此类接触/冒犯性接触几乎肯定会发生。","analysis":"构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为，要求被告具有相应的意图，即：旨在造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或制造此类接触即将发生的恐惧感），或者明知此类接触或恐惧感极有可能发生。\n\n如果该故意行为导致了针对原告的有害或冒犯性接触，则被告须承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任。\n\n在本案中，猎人开枪的目的是射中鹿，而非徒步旅行者。\n\n此外，猎人并不知道徒步旅行者的存在，因此他在开枪时也不可能确信自己会击中徒步旅行者。\n\n由于猎人开枪并非出于击中徒步旅行者的意图，他不承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任。\n\n（选项 B）接触既可以是直接的（例如，被告挥拳击打原告），也可以是间接的（例如，被告向原告发射子弹）。\n\n尽管猎人未与徒步旅行者发生直接的身体接触，但他通过射击行为造成了间接接触。\n\n（选项 C）故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的成立需要同时证明存在意图和接触行为。\n\n虽然猎人枪中的子弹确实与徒步旅行者发生了身体接触，但由于猎人并无射击徒步旅行者的意图，他不承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任。\n\n（选项 D）对于故意侵害身体（battery）侵权而言，被告必须有意对原告造成有害或冒犯性的接触（或制造此类接触即将发生的恐惧感）。\n\n尽管猎人是故意开枪的，但他并不承担故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的责任，因为他开枪并非出于与徒步旅行者发生接触的意图。\n\n教育目标：故意侵害身体（battery）侵权要求被告的行为具备以下要素之一：（1）旨在造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或制造此类接触即将发生的恐惧感；或（2）明知此类接触或恐惧感极有可能发生。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 A：否，因为猎人并非有意射击徒步旅行者。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-711f01d6450edc75"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":37,"sourceQuestionPage":110,"sourceQuestionPages":[110],"sourceExplanationPages":[111,112],"sourceAnswerPages":[111,112],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-038","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A jockey was seriously injured in a race when a rider cut sharply in front of the jockey's horse without adequate clearance. The two horses collided, causing the jockey to fall to the ground, sustaining injury. The State Racetrack Commission ruled that, by cutting in too sharply, the rider committed a foul in violation of racetrack rules requiring adequate clearance for crossing lanes. In an action by the jockey against the rider for battery, is the jockey likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the jockey assumed the risk of accidental injury inherent in participating in a horse race.","B":"No, unless the rider intended to cause impermissible contact between the two horses or apprehension of such contact by the jockey.","C":"Yes, because the rider was reckless in cutting in front of the jockey's horse.","D":"Yes, because the State Racetrack Commission determined that the rider committed a foul in violation of racetrack rules."},"answer":"B","explanation":"For battery, a defendant must (1) intend to inflict harmful/offensive contact or the imminent apprehension thereof and (2) cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Contact can occur between the plaintiff and the defendant OR between objects intimately associated with either party (eg, dog on a leash). Here, the jockey can prove that there was contact between the rider's and the jockey's horses (objects intimately associated with the parties) and that this contact caused the jockey to fall and sustain an injury (harmful contact). However, the jockey cannot prevail on her battery claim unless she can also prove that the rider cut in too sharply with the intent to cause harmful or offensive contact between the horses or cause the jockey imminent apprehension of such contact. (Choice A) Assumption of the risk is an affirmative defense that prevents a plaintiff from recovering damages for a harm caused by negligent or reckless conduct. However, this defense is not available when the plaintiff's injury is caused by the defendant's intentional conduct (eg, battery). (Choice C) Battery requires proof that the defendant acted intentionally (purpose or knowledge that one's act will cause a particular result), not merely recklessly (indifference to a known or obvious risk of harm). Therefore, evidence that the rider was reckless is irrelevant. (Choice D) Although the State Racetrack Commission ruled that the rider committed a foul, that ruling does not prove that the rider acted with the intent required for battery.\n\nEducational objective: Battery requires that a defendant (1) intend to inflict harmful/offensive contact or create the imminent apprehension thereof and (2) cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Contact can occur between the plaintiff and the defendant OR between objects intimately associated with either party.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 18 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of contact).","educationalObjective":"Battery requires that a defendant (1) intend to inflict harmful/offensive contact or create the imminent apprehension thereof and (2) cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Contact can occur between the plaintiff and the defendant OR between objects intimately associated with either party.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (definition of intent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 18 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of contact).","rule":"Battery requires that a defendant (1) intend to inflict harmful/offensive contact or create the imminent apprehension thereof and (2) cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Contact can occur between the plaintiff and the defendant OR between objects intimately associated with either party.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the jockey against the rider for battery, is the jockey likely to prevail?","rule":"Battery requires that a defendant (1) intend to inflict harmful/offensive contact or create the imminent apprehension thereof and (2) cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Contact can occur between the plaintiff and the defendant OR between objects intimately associated with either party.","analysis":"For battery, a defendant must (1) intend to inflict harmful/offensive contact or the imminent apprehension thereof and (2) cause harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff. Contact can occur between the plaintiff and the defendant OR between objects intimately associated with either party (eg, dog on a leash). Here, the jockey can prove that there was contact between the rider's and the jockey's horses (objects intimately associated with the parties) and that this contact caused the jockey to fall and sustain an injury (harmful contact). However, the jockey cannot prevail on her battery claim unless she can also prove that the rider cut in too sharply with the intent to cause harmful or offensive contact between the horses or cause the jockey imminent apprehension of such contact. (Choice A) Assumption of the risk is an affirmative defense that prevents a plaintiff from recovering damages for a harm caused by negligent or reckless conduct. However, this defense is not available when the plaintiff's injury is caused by the defendant's intentional conduct (eg, battery). (Choice C) Battery requires proof that the defendant acted intentionally (purpose or knowledge that one's act will cause a particular result), not merely recklessly (indifference to a known or obvious risk of harm). Therefore, evidence that the rider was reckless is irrelevant. (Choice D) Although the State Racetrack Commission ruled that the rider committed a foul, that ruling does not prove that the rider acted with the intent required for battery.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, unless the rider intended to cause impermissible contact between the two horses or apprehension of such contact by the jockey."},"questionZh":"一场比赛中，一名骑师因另一名骑手突然切入骑师的马匹前方，且未留出足够的安全距离而严重受伤。\n\n两匹马相撞，导致骑师摔倒在地并受伤。\n\n州赛马场委员会裁定，该骑手切入过猛，违反了赛马场关于交叉道安全距离的规定，构成犯规。\n\n如果骑师以人身伤害罪起诉该骑手，骑师胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为骑师承担了参与赛马运动中可能发生的意外伤害风险。","B":"否，除非该骑手意图造成两匹马之间发生不合规的接触，或使骑师担心发生此类接触。","C":"是，因为该骑手鲁莽地切入骑师的马匹前方。","D":"是的，因为州赛马场委员会认定骑手犯规，违反了赛马场规则。"},"explanationZh":"就“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）这一侵权行为而言，被告必须：(1) 意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或意图使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感（imminent apprehension）；以及 (2) 导致与原告发生有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n接触既可以发生在原告与被告之间，也可以发生在与任一方密切相关的物体之间（例如，牵引绳上的狗）。\n\n在本案中，骑师可以证明骑手所骑的马与她所骑的马（即与当事人密切相关的物体）之间发生了接触，且该接触导致她跌落并受伤（即有害接触）。\n\n然而，除非骑师能进一步证明骑手急剧切入赛道的行为是出于造成马匹间有害或冒犯性接触的意图，或是为了让骑师产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感，否则她的故意侵害身体（battery）索赔将无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”（assumption of the risk）是一种积极抗辩，旨在阻止原告因被告的疏忽或鲁莽行为所造成的伤害而获得损害赔偿。\n\n然而，当原告的伤害是由被告的故意行为（例如故意侵害身体（battery））引起时，该抗辩不适用。\n\n（选项 C）故意侵害身体（battery）行为的认定要求证明被告具有故意（即有目的或明知其行为会导致特定结果），而不仅仅是鲁莽（即对已知或明显的伤害风险漠不关心）。\n\n因此，证明骑手行为鲁莽的证据与本案无关。\n\n（选项 D）尽管州赛马委员会裁定骑手犯规，但该裁定并不能证明骑手在行为时具备构成故意侵害身体（battery）所需的故意。\n\n教育目标：故意侵害身体（battery）行为要求被告：(1) 意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或意图使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感；以及 (2) 导致与原告发生有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n接触既可以发生在原告与被告之间，也可以发生在与任一方密切相关的物体之间。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 18 条注释 c (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（接触的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"故意侵害身体（battery）罪要求被告 (1) 意图造成有害/冒犯性接触或使人感到即将遭受此类接触，并且 (2) 与原告发生有害或冒犯性接触。\n\n接触可能发生在原告和被告之间，也可能发生在与任何一方密切相关的物体之间。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果骑师起诉骑手故意侵害身体（battery）罪，骑师胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"故意侵害身体（battery）罪要求被告 (1) 意图造成有害/冒犯性接触或使人感到即将遭受此类接触，并且 (2) 与原告发生有害或冒犯性接触。\n\n接触可能发生在原告和被告之间，也可能发生在与任何一方密切相关的物体之间。","analysis":"就“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）这一侵权行为而言，被告必须：(1) 意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或意图使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感（imminent apprehension）；以及 (2) 导致与原告发生有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n接触既可以发生在原告与被告之间，也可以发生在与任一方密切相关的物体之间（例如，牵引绳上的狗）。\n\n在本案中，骑师可以证明骑手所骑的马与她所骑的马（即与当事人密切相关的物体）之间发生了接触，且该接触导致她跌落并受伤（即有害接触）。\n\n然而，除非骑师能进一步证明骑手急剧切入赛道的行为是出于造成马匹间有害或冒犯性接触的意图，或是为了让骑师产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感，否则她的故意侵害身体（battery）索赔将无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”（assumption of the risk）是一种积极抗辩，旨在阻止原告因被告的疏忽或鲁莽行为所造成的伤害而获得损害赔偿。\n\n然而，当原告的伤害是由被告的故意行为（例如故意侵害身体（battery））引起时，该抗辩不适用。\n\n（选项 C）故意侵害身体（battery）行为的认定要求证明被告具有故意（即有目的或明知其行为会导致特定结果），而不仅仅是鲁莽（即对已知或明显的伤害风险漠不关心）。\n\n因此，证明骑手行为鲁莽的证据与本案无关。\n\n（选项 D）尽管州赛马委员会裁定骑手犯规，但该裁定并不能证明骑手在行为时具备构成故意侵害身体（battery）所需的故意。\n\n教育目标：故意侵害身体（battery）行为要求被告：(1) 意图造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或意图使原告产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感；以及 (2) 导致与原告发生有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n接触既可以发生在原告与被告之间，也可以发生在与任一方密切相关的物体之间。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 18 条注释 c (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（接触的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 B：否，除非该骑手意图造成两匹马之间发生不合规的接触，或使骑师担心发生此类接触。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-f2922c27bcb72d43"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":38,"sourceQuestionPage":113,"sourceQuestionPages":[113],"sourceExplanationPages":[114,115,116],"sourceAnswerPages":[114,115,116],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-039","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"An inexperienced driver borrowed a car from her friend for the express purpose of driving it several blocks to the local drug store. Instead, the driver drove the car, which was then worth $12,000, to another city 100 miles away. While she was safely driving in the other city the next day, the car was hit by a negligently driven truck and sustained damage that will cost $3,000 to repair. If repaired, the car will be fully restored to its former condition. If the friend asserts a claim against the driver based on conversion, what is the maximum amount, if anything, that he can recover from the driver?","choices":{"A":"Nothing, because the driver was not negligent in causing the collision.","B":"$3,000.","C":"$3,000 plus damages for the loss of the use of the car during its repair.","D":"$12,000."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Conversion is an intentional exertion of dominion or control over the plaintiff's chattel that so seriously interferes with the plaintiff's ownership rights that the defendant is liable for the fair market value of the chattel at the time and place of the conversion. A defendant who initially uses the plaintiff's chattel with permission commits conversion when the defendant: intentionally uses the chattel in a manner that exceeds the scope of permission and seriously violates the plaintiff's right to control the chattel. Here, the driver had the friend's permission to borrow his car for the express purpose of driving it to the local drug store several blocks away. Instead, the driver intentionally exceeded the scope of permission when she drove the friend's car to another city 100 miles away and kept it overnight, which seriously violated the friend's right to control his car. Therefore, the driver committed conversion, and the friend can recover the fair market value of the car at the time the conversion occurred—$12,000. (Choice A) Conversion requires that the defendant's conduct be intentional (the purpose or knowledge that one's act will cause a particular result)—not negligent (the failure to act in the same manner a reasonably prudent person would act under similar circumstances). Additionally, the conversion occurred when the driver intentionally drove the friend's car to another city and kept it overnight. Therefore, evidence that the driver was not negligent (ie, drove safely) in causing the collision has no bearing on the friend's conversion claim. (Choice B & C) Trespass to chattels (minor intentional interference with the plaintiff's chattel) only allows the plaintiff to recover actual damages (eg, cost of repairs, loss of use). But because the friend sued for conversion, he can recover the fair market value of the car.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant who has permission to use the plaintiff's chattel commits conversion when he/she (1) intentionally uses the chattel in a way that exceeds the scope of permission and (2) seriously violates the plaintiff's right to control the chattel. The defendant is liable for the fair market value of the chattel at the time and place of the conversion.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 222A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of conversion).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 223 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (ways to commit conversion).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 228 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (conversion by exceeding authorization).","educationalObjective":"A defendant who has permission to use the plaintiff's chattel commits conversion when he/she (1) intentionally uses the chattel in a way that exceeds the scope of permission and (2) seriously violates the plaintiff's right to control the chattel. The defendant is liable for the fair market value of the chattel at the time and place of the conversion.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 222A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of conversion).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 223 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (ways to commit conversion).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 228 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (conversion by exceeding authorization).","rule":"A defendant who has permission to use the plaintiff's chattel commits conversion when he/she (1) intentionally uses the chattel in a way that exceeds the scope of permission and (2) seriously violates the plaintiff's right to control the chattel. The defendant is liable for the fair market value of the chattel at the time and place of the conversion.","irac":{"issue":"If the friend asserts a claim against the driver based on conversion, what is the maximum amount, if anything, that he can recover from the driver?","rule":"A defendant who has permission to use the plaintiff's chattel commits conversion when he/she (1) intentionally uses the chattel in a way that exceeds the scope of permission and (2) seriously violates the plaintiff's right to control the chattel. The defendant is liable for the fair market value of the chattel at the time and place of the conversion.","analysis":"Conversion is an intentional exertion of dominion or control over the plaintiff's chattel that so seriously interferes with the plaintiff's ownership rights that the defendant is liable for the fair market value of the chattel at the time and place of the conversion. A defendant who initially uses the plaintiff's chattel with permission commits conversion when the defendant: intentionally uses the chattel in a manner that exceeds the scope of permission and seriously violates the plaintiff's right to control the chattel. Here, the driver had the friend's permission to borrow his car for the express purpose of driving it to the local drug store several blocks away. Instead, the driver intentionally exceeded the scope of permission when she drove the friend's car to another city 100 miles away and kept it overnight, which seriously violated the friend's right to control his car. Therefore, the driver committed conversion, and the friend can recover the fair market value of the car at the time the conversion occurred—$12,000. (Choice A) Conversion requires that the defendant's conduct be intentional (the purpose or knowledge that one's act will cause a particular result)—not negligent (the failure to act in the same manner a reasonably prudent person would act under similar circumstances). Additionally, the conversion occurred when the driver intentionally drove the friend's car to another city and kept it overnight. Therefore, evidence that the driver was not negligent (ie, drove safely) in causing the collision has no bearing on the friend's conversion claim. (Choice B & C) Trespass to chattels (minor intentional interference with the plaintiff's chattel) only allows the plaintiff to recover actual damages (eg, cost of repairs, loss of use). But because the friend sued for conversion, he can recover the fair market value of the car.","conclusion":"Answer D: $12,000."},"questionZh":"一位缺乏经验的驾驶员向朋友借了一辆车，本意只是想把车开到几个街区外的当地药店。\n\n然而，她却把这辆当时价值 12,000 美元的车开到了 100 英里外的另一座城市。\n\n次日，当她在该市安全驾驶时，一辆因疏忽驾驶的卡车撞上了她的车，造成了需要 3,000 美元才能修复的损坏。\n\n如果进行维修，车辆将完全恢复到受损前的状况。\n\n如果这位朋友基于“侵占”（conversion）这一侵权行为向驾驶员提出索赔，他最多能从驾驶员那里获得多少赔偿（如果有的话）？","choicesZh":{"A":"无需赔偿，因为驾驶员在导致碰撞事故时并无过失。","B":"3,000 美元。","C":"3,000 美元，外加车辆维修期间因无法使用该车而产生的损失赔偿。","D":"12,000 美元。"},"explanationZh":"“侵占”（conversion）是指对原告动产行使支配权或控制权的故意行为，且该行为严重干扰了原告的所有权，致使被告须就侵占发生时及发生地的动产公平市场价值承担赔偿责任。\n\n若被告最初经许可使用原告动产，但在以下情况下即构成侵占：故意以超出许可范围的方式使用该动产，并严重侵犯了原告对该动产的控制权。\n\n在本案中，驾驶者获朋友许可借用车辆，其明确目的是将车开往几个街区外的当地药店。\n\n然而，驾驶者故意超出了许可范围，将车开往100英里外的另一座城市并过夜，这一行为严重侵犯了朋友对车辆的控制权。\n\n因此，驾驶者构成了侵占，朋友有权获得侵占发生时该车辆的公平市场价值——即12,000美元。\n\n（选项A）侵占要求被告的行为具有故意性（即具有导致特定结果的目的或明知其行为会导致该结果），而非过失（即未能在类似情况下像一个理性谨慎的人那样行事）。\n\n此外，侵占行为发生于驾驶者故意将车开往另一城市并过夜之时。\n\n因此，关于驾驶者在导致碰撞事故时并无过失（例如驾驶安全）的证据，与朋友提出的侵占索赔无关。\n\n（选项B与C）“侵犯动产权”（trespass to chattels，即对原告动产的轻微故意干扰）仅允许原告获得实际损害赔偿（例如维修费用、使用价值损失）。\n\n但由于朋友提起的是侵占之诉，他有权获得车辆的公平市场价值。\n\n教育目标：经许可使用原告动产的被告，若（1）故意以超出许可范围的方式使用该动产，且（2）严重侵犯了原告对该动产的控制权，即构成侵占。\n\n被告须就侵占发生时及发生地的动产公平市场价值承担赔偿责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第222A条（美国法律协会，1965年）（侵占的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第223条（美国法律协会，1965年）（实施侵占的方式）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第228条（美国法律协会，1965年）（因超越授权而构成的侵占）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果被告获准使用原告的动产，但在以下情况下即构成“侵占”（conversion）：(1) 故意以超出许可范围的方式使用该动产；以及 (2) 严重侵犯了原告对该动产的控制权。\n\n被告须就侵占发生时及发生地的动产公平市场价值承担赔偿责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果这位朋友基于“侵占”这一侵权行为向驾驶员提出索赔，他最多能从驾驶员那里获得多少赔偿（如果有的话）？","rule":"如果被告获准使用原告的动产，但在以下情况下即构成“侵占”（conversion）：(1) 故意以超出许可范围的方式使用该动产；以及 (2) 严重侵犯了原告对该动产的控制权。\n\n被告须就侵占发生时及发生地的动产公平市场价值承担赔偿责任。","analysis":"“侵占”（conversion）是指对原告动产行使支配权或控制权的故意行为，且该行为严重干扰了原告的所有权，致使被告须就侵占发生时及发生地的动产公平市场价值承担赔偿责任。\n\n若被告最初经许可使用原告动产，但在以下情况下即构成侵占：故意以超出许可范围的方式使用该动产，并严重侵犯了原告对该动产的控制权。\n\n在本案中，驾驶者获朋友许可借用车辆，其明确目的是将车开往几个街区外的当地药店。\n\n然而，驾驶者故意超出了许可范围，将车开往100英里外的另一座城市并过夜，这一行为严重侵犯了朋友对车辆的控制权。\n\n因此，驾驶者构成了侵占，朋友有权获得侵占发生时该车辆的公平市场价值——即12,000美元。\n\n（选项A）侵占要求被告的行为具有故意性（即具有导致特定结果的目的或明知其行为会导致该结果），而非过失（即未能在类似情况下像一个理性谨慎的人那样行事）。\n\n此外，侵占行为发生于驾驶者故意将车开往另一城市并过夜之时。\n\n因此，关于驾驶者在导致碰撞事故时并无过失（例如驾驶安全）的证据，与朋友提出的侵占索赔无关。\n\n（选项B与C）“侵犯动产权”（trespass to chattels，即对原告动产的轻微故意干扰）仅允许原告获得实际损害赔偿（例如维修费用、使用价值损失）。\n\n但由于朋友提起的是侵占之诉，他有权获得车辆的公平市场价值。\n\n教育目标：经许可使用原告动产的被告，若（1）故意以超出许可范围的方式使用该动产，且（2）严重侵犯了原告对该动产的控制权，即构成侵占。\n\n被告须就侵占发生时及发生地的动产公平市场价值承担赔偿责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第222A条（美国法律协会，1965年）（侵占的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第223条（美国法律协会，1965年）（实施侵占的方式）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第228条（美国法律协会，1965年）（因超越授权而构成的侵占）。","conclusion":"答案 D：12,000 美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-1e824aa5d34ef665"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":39,"sourceQuestionPage":117,"sourceQuestionPages":[117],"sourceExplanationPages":[118,119],"sourceAnswerPages":[118,119],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-040","subject":"Torts","topic":"Privileges & Defenses","question":"A driver was traveling along a highway during an unusually heavy rainstorm when the roadway began to flood. To protect his car from water damage, the driver pulled his car up a steep, unmarked driveway abutting the highway that led to a homeowner's residence. The driver left his car parked in the driveway and walked home, intending to return when the floodwater had subsided. Shortly after the driver started to walk home, the homeowner carefully rolled the car back down her driveway and parked it on the highway shoulder. The floodwater continued to rise and caused damage to the driver's car. If the driver sues the homeowner to recover for damage to his car, is the driver likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the driver intentionally drove his car onto the homeowner's property.","B":"No, because the homeowner was privileged to remove the car from her property.","C":"Yes, because the driver was privileged to park his car on the homeowner's property.","D":"Yes, because the homeowner did not post any \"no trespassing\" signs."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Doctrine of necessity\n\nPrivate An intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect necessity oneself, third parties, or property\n\nPublic An intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to avert an necessity imminent public disaster\n\nA trespass is privileged when it arises out of necessity—ie, when an intrusion is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to: protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm (private necessity) or avert an imminent public disaster (public necessity). A landowner cannot use force to stop a trespass arising from necessity and is liable for damages caused by force used against the trespassing person or property. Here, the driver was privileged to park his car on the homeowner's driveway because it was, or reasonably appeared to be, necessary to protect his car from the flood (private necessity). Therefore, the homeowner had no right to forcibly remove the driver's car down her driveway and onto the highway shoulder (Choice B). Since the homeowner's actions caused the car to flood, the driver will likely recover for damage to his car. (Choice A) The driver trespassed when he intentionally drove his car onto the homeowner's property without permission. And though a landowner can generally use reasonable force to stop a trespass (ie, defense of property), the landowner cannot do so when the trespass is privileged—as seen here. Therefore, the driver's trespass does not preclude him from recovering for the damage to his car. (Choice D) Trespass requires intent to enter the land—not intent to enter the land without the owner's consent. Therefore, the absence of \"no trespassing\" signs has no bearing on the driver's intent or his privilege to seek refuge on the landowner's driveway.\n\nEducational objective: A landowner must permit a trespass caused by necessity—an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to (1) protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm or (2) avert an imminent public disaster—for as long as the necessity exists.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 197 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (private necessity).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 77 cmts. d–e (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (defense of property).","educationalObjective":"A landowner must permit a trespass caused by necessity—an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to (1) protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm or (2) avert an imminent public disaster—for as long as the necessity exists.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 197 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (private necessity).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 77 cmts. d–e (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (defense of property).","rule":"A landowner must permit a trespass caused by necessity—an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to (1) protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm or (2) avert an imminent public disaster—for as long as the necessity exists.","irac":{"issue":"If the driver sues the homeowner to recover for damage to his car, is the driver likely to prevail?","rule":"A landowner must permit a trespass caused by necessity—an intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to (1) protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm or (2) avert an imminent public disaster—for as long as the necessity exists.","analysis":"Doctrine of necessity\n\nPrivate An intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to protect necessity oneself, third parties, or property\n\nPublic An intrusion that is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to avert an necessity imminent public disaster\n\nA trespass is privileged when it arises out of necessity—ie, when an intrusion is, or reasonably appears to be, necessary to: protect oneself, third parties, or property from serious harm (private necessity) or avert an imminent public disaster (public necessity). A landowner cannot use force to stop a trespass arising from necessity and is liable for damages caused by force used against the trespassing person or property. Here, the driver was privileged to park his car on the homeowner's driveway because it was, or reasonably appeared to be, necessary to protect his car from the flood (private necessity). Therefore, the homeowner had no right to forcibly remove the driver's car down her driveway and onto the highway shoulder (Choice B). Since the homeowner's actions caused the car to flood, the driver will likely recover for damage to his car. (Choice A) The driver trespassed when he intentionally drove his car onto the homeowner's property without permission. And though a landowner can generally use reasonable force to stop a trespass (ie, defense of property), the landowner cannot do so when the trespass is privileged—as seen here. Therefore, the driver's trespass does not preclude him from recovering for the damage to his car. (Choice D) Trespass requires intent to enter the land—not intent to enter the land without the owner's consent. Therefore, the absence of \"no trespassing\" signs has no bearing on the driver's intent or his privilege to seek refuge on the landowner's driveway.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the driver was privileged to park his car on the homeowner's property."},"questionZh":"一名司机在遭遇异常强烈的暴雨时驾车行驶在公路上，此时路面开始积水。\n\n为了保护车辆免受水害，司机将车开上了一条紧邻公路、通往某户民宅的陡峭且未设标志的车道。\n\n司机将车停在车道上后步行回家，打算等洪水退去后再回来取车。\n\n司机离开后不久，房主小心地将车倒回车道下方，停在了公路路肩上。\n\n随后水位继续上涨，导致司机的车辆受损。\n\n如果司机起诉房主以索赔车辆损失，他胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为司机是故意将车开上房主的私人领地的。","B":"不会，因为房主有权将该车辆移出其私人领地。","C":"会，因为司机有权将车停在房主的私人领地上。","D":"会，因为房主没有竖立任何“禁止擅入”的标志。"},"explanationZh":"紧急避险原则（Doctrine of necessity） 私力紧急避险（Private necessity）：为保护自身、第三方或财产而进行的（或看似有必要进行的）侵入行为。 公力紧急避险（Public necessity）：为避免迫在眉睫的公共灾难而进行的（或看似有必要进行的）侵入行为。 当侵入行为源于紧急避险时，该侵入即享有特权（即合法性）——即为了保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害（私力紧急避险），或为了避免迫在眉睫的公共灾难（公力紧急避险），而必须（或看似必须）进行侵入。\n\n土地所有者不得使用武力阻止因紧急避险而引发的侵入，且若因针对侵入者或其财产使用武力而造成损害，所有者须承担赔偿责任。\n\n在本案中，司机将车停在房主的车道上享有特权，因为这是为了保护其车辆免受洪水侵害所必需的（或看似必需的）行为（私力紧急避险）。\n\n因此，房主无权强行将司机的车从车道上移至公路路肩（选项 B）。\n\n由于房主的行为导致车辆被水淹，司机很可能就车辆受损获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）司机在未经许可的情况下故意将车开上房主的土地，构成了侵入。\n\n尽管土地所有者通常可以使用合理的武力阻止侵入（即财产防卫），但在侵入享有特权的情况下（如本案所示），所有者不得这样做。\n\n因此，司机的侵入行为并不妨碍他就车辆受损获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）构成侵入只需具备进入土地的意图，而无需具备“在未经所有者同意的情况下进入土地”的意图。\n\n因此，未设置“禁止侵入”标志这一事实，对司机的意图或其在土地所有者车道上寻求避险的特权均无影响。\n\n教育目标：土地所有者必须容忍因紧急避险而引发的侵入行为——即为了 (1) 保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害，或 (2) 避免迫在眉睫的公共灾难而必须（或看似必须）进行的侵入——只要这种紧急情况持续存在。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 197 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（私力紧急避险）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 77 条及相关评注。\n\nd–e (美国法律协会 1965年)（财产防卫）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地所有者必须允许因“紧急避险”（即为 (1) 保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害，或 (2) 避免迫在眉睫的公共灾难而必须进行，或看似必须进行的侵入行为）而引发的侵入行为，只要这种紧急情况持续存在。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果司机起诉房主以索赔车辆损失，他胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"土地所有者必须允许因“紧急避险”（即为 (1) 保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害，或 (2) 避免迫在眉睫的公共灾难而必须进行，或看似必须进行的侵入行为）而引发的侵入行为，只要这种紧急情况持续存在。","analysis":"紧急避险原则（Doctrine of necessity） 私力紧急避险（Private necessity）：为保护自身、第三方或财产而进行的（或看似有必要进行的）侵入行为。 公力紧急避险（Public necessity）：为避免迫在眉睫的公共灾难而进行的（或看似有必要进行的）侵入行为。 当侵入行为源于紧急避险时，该侵入即享有特权（即合法性）——即为了保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害（私力紧急避险），或为了避免迫在眉睫的公共灾难（公力紧急避险），而必须（或看似必须）进行侵入。\n\n土地所有者不得使用武力阻止因紧急避险而引发的侵入，且若因针对侵入者或其财产使用武力而造成损害，所有者须承担赔偿责任。\n\n在本案中，司机将车停在房主的车道上享有特权，因为这是为了保护其车辆免受洪水侵害所必需的（或看似必需的）行为（私力紧急避险）。\n\n因此，房主无权强行将司机的车从车道上移至公路路肩（选项 B）。\n\n由于房主的行为导致车辆被水淹，司机很可能就车辆受损获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）司机在未经许可的情况下故意将车开上房主的土地，构成了侵入。\n\n尽管土地所有者通常可以使用合理的武力阻止侵入（即财产防卫），但在侵入享有特权的情况下（如本案所示），所有者不得这样做。\n\n因此，司机的侵入行为并不妨碍他就车辆受损获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）构成侵入只需具备进入土地的意图，而无需具备“在未经所有者同意的情况下进入土地”的意图。\n\n因此，未设置“禁止侵入”标志这一事实，对司机的意图或其在土地所有者车道上寻求避险的特权均无影响。\n\n教育目标：土地所有者必须容忍因紧急避险而引发的侵入行为——即为了 (1) 保护自身、第三方或财产免受严重损害，或 (2) 避免迫在眉睫的公共灾难而必须（或看似必须）进行的侵入——只要这种紧急情况持续存在。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 197 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（私力紧急避险）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 77 条及相关评注。\n\nd–e (美国法律协会 1965年)（财产防卫）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，因为司机有权将车停在房主的私人领地上。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":40,"sourceQuestionPage":120,"sourceQuestionPages":[120],"sourceExplanationPages":[121,122],"sourceAnswerPages":[121,122],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-041","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A rancher and his neighbor were involved in a boundary dispute. In order to resolve their differences, each drove his truck to an open pasture area on his land where the two properties were separated by a fence. The rancher was accompanied by four friends, and the neighbor was alone. The neighbor got out of his truck and walked toward the fence. The rancher got out but simply stood by his truck. When the neighbor came over the fence, the rancher shot him, inflicting serious injury. In a battery action brought by the neighbor against the rancher, the rancher testified that he actually thought his neighbor was armed. However, the rancher could point to nothing that would have reasonably justified this belief. Is the neighbor likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the rancher suspected that the neighbor was armed.","B":"No, because the rancher was standing on his own property and had no obligation to retreat.","C":"Yes, because deadly force is never appropriate in a property dispute.","D":"Yes, because it was unreasonable for the rancher to consider the use of a gun necessary for self-defense."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The privilege to use reasonable force (eg, battery) in self-defense arises when the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff is about to inflict harmful or offensive contact on the defendant. The force used must be proportionate to the anticipated harm, so deadly force can only be used when the defendant reasonably believes that he/she is facing death or serious bodily injury. Here, the rancher committed a battery when he shot the neighbor, inflicting serious injury. The rancher alleges that he shot the neighbor (deadly force) in self-defense because he thought that the neighbor was armed when he exited the truck and walked toward the fence. However, the rancher could point to no evidence that would have reasonably justified his belief (eg, a friend's testimony that the neighbor had a gun holstered to his waist). Therefore, the rancher cannot show that he was privileged to use deadly force in self-defense and the neighbor will likely prevail on his battery claim. (Choice A) Since the rancher's suspicion that the neighbor was armed was not supported by any evidence (no reasonable belief), the rancher's use of deadly force was not justified. (Choice B) A person has a duty to retreat in lieu of using deadly force in self-defense, unless confronted with deadly force inside his/her own home (ie, no duty to retreat inside one's castle). Since the rancher was in an open pasture on his property—not inside his home—he had an obligation to retreat if he could safely do so. (Choice C) Although deadly force cannot be used to protect property, it can be used when a property dispute escalates into a situation in which a participant is in danger of death or serious bodily injury. However, the rancher could not reasonably believe that he was facing death or serious bodily injury during this property dispute, so he was not justified in using deadly force.\n\nEducational objective: The force used in self-defense must be proportionate to the anticipated harm. Therefore, deadly force can only be used when a person reasonably believes that he/she is facing death or serious injury.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 63 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (self-defense).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 65 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (self-defense by deadly force).","educationalObjective":"The force used in self-defense must be proportionate to the anticipated harm. Therefore, deadly force can only be used when a person reasonably believes that he/she is facing death or serious injury.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 63 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (self-defense).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 65 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (self-defense by deadly force).","rule":"The force used in self-defense must be proportionate to the anticipated harm. Therefore, deadly force can only be used when a person reasonably believes that he/she is facing death or serious injury.","irac":{"issue":"Is the neighbor likely to prevail?","rule":"The force used in self-defense must be proportionate to the anticipated harm. Therefore, deadly force can only be used when a person reasonably believes that he/she is facing death or serious injury.","analysis":"The privilege to use reasonable force (eg, battery) in self-defense arises when the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff is about to inflict harmful or offensive contact on the defendant. The force used must be proportionate to the anticipated harm, so deadly force can only be used when the defendant reasonably believes that he/she is facing death or serious bodily injury. Here, the rancher committed a battery when he shot the neighbor, inflicting serious injury. The rancher alleges that he shot the neighbor (deadly force) in self-defense because he thought that the neighbor was armed when he exited the truck and walked toward the fence. However, the rancher could point to no evidence that would have reasonably justified his belief (eg, a friend's testimony that the neighbor had a gun holstered to his waist). Therefore, the rancher cannot show that he was privileged to use deadly force in self-defense and the neighbor will likely prevail on his battery claim. (Choice A) Since the rancher's suspicion that the neighbor was armed was not supported by any evidence (no reasonable belief), the rancher's use of deadly force was not justified. (Choice B) A person has a duty to retreat in lieu of using deadly force in self-defense, unless confronted with deadly force inside his/her own home (ie, no duty to retreat inside one's castle). Since the rancher was in an open pasture on his property—not inside his home—he had an obligation to retreat if he could safely do so. (Choice C) Although deadly force cannot be used to protect property, it can be used when a property dispute escalates into a situation in which a participant is in danger of death or serious bodily injury. However, the rancher could not reasonably believe that he was facing death or serious bodily injury during this property dispute, so he was not justified in using deadly force.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because it was unreasonable for the rancher to consider the use of a gun necessary for self-defense."},"questionZh":"一位牧场主和他的邻居发生了边界纠纷。\n\n为了解决分歧，两人各自驾驶卡车来到自家土地上的一片开阔牧场，两块地产之间由一道围栏隔开。\n\n牧场主有四位朋友陪同，而邻居则是独自一人。\n\n邻居下了卡车，向围栏走去。\n\n牧场主也下了车，但只是站在自己的卡车旁。\n\n当邻居越过围栏走过来时，牧场主开枪击中了他，造成其重伤。\n\n在邻居针对牧场主提起的侵权诉讼（涉及非法故意侵害身体（battery）/人身伤害）中，牧场主作证称，他当时确实认为邻居携带了武器。\n\n然而，牧场主无法指出任何能合理支持这一想法的依据。\n\n邻居胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为牧场主当时怀疑邻居携带了武器。","B":"不会，因为牧场主当时站在自己的土地上，没有义务退避。","C":"会，因为在财产纠纷中，使用致命武力绝不适宜。","D":"会，因为牧场主认为有必要使用枪支进行自卫是不合理的。"},"explanationZh":"当被告有合理理由相信原告即将对其施加有害或冒犯性的身体接触时，被告便享有出于自卫而使用合理武力（例如，构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”/battery 行为的武力）的权利。\n\n所使用的武力必须与预期的伤害程度相称；因此，只有在被告有合理理由相信自己面临死亡或严重身体伤害的威胁时，方可使用致命武力。\n\n在本案中，牧场主开枪击伤邻居，造成严重伤害，这一行为构成了“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）。\n\n牧场主辩称，他开枪（使用致命武力）是出于自卫，因为他认为邻居下车走向围栏时携带了武器。\n\n然而，牧场主无法提供任何证据来证明其这种想法具有合理依据（例如，朋友作证称看到邻居腰间别着枪）。\n\n因此，牧场主无法证明其有权出于自卫使用致命武力，邻居很可能在“故意侵害身体（battery）”侵权诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）由于牧场主关于邻居携带武器的怀疑没有任何证据支持（即缺乏合理依据），其使用致命武力的行为是不正当的。\n\n（选项 B）在自卫时，若面临使用致命武力的抉择，当事人有义务先尝试撤退，除非是在自己家中面临致命武力威胁（即在“城堡”内无撤退义务）。\n\n由于牧场主当时身处其地产上的开阔牧场——而非家中——因此如果条件允许安全撤退，他就有义务撤退。\n\n（选项 C）尽管不得为了保护财产而使用致命武力，但如果财产纠纷升级，导致当事人面临死亡或严重身体伤害的危险，则可以使用致命武力。\n\n然而，在这起财产纠纷中，牧场主并无合理理由相信自己面临死亡或严重身体伤害的威胁，因此他使用致命武力的行为是不正当的。\n\n教育目标：自卫时使用的武力必须与预期的伤害程度相称。\n\n因此，只有在当事人有合理理由相信自己面临死亡或严重伤害的威胁时，方可使用致命武力。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 63 条 (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 63) (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于自卫的规定）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第65条（美国法律学会，1965年）（使用致命武力进行的自卫）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"自卫时使用的武力必须与预期的伤害程度相称。\n\n因此，只有在一个人合理地认为自己面临死亡或重伤威胁时，才可以使用致命武力。","iracZh":{"issue":"邻居胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"自卫时使用的武力必须与预期的伤害程度相称。\n\n因此，只有在一个人合理地认为自己面临死亡或重伤威胁时，才可以使用致命武力。","analysis":"当被告有合理理由相信原告即将对其施加有害或冒犯性的身体接触时，被告便享有出于自卫而使用合理武力（例如，构成“故意侵害身体（battery）”/battery 行为的武力）的权利。\n\n所使用的武力必须与预期的伤害程度相称；因此，只有在被告有合理理由相信自己面临死亡或严重身体伤害的威胁时，方可使用致命武力。\n\n在本案中，牧场主开枪击伤邻居，造成严重伤害，这一行为构成了“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）。\n\n牧场主辩称，他开枪（使用致命武力）是出于自卫，因为他认为邻居下车走向围栏时携带了武器。\n\n然而，牧场主无法提供任何证据来证明其这种想法具有合理依据（例如，朋友作证称看到邻居腰间别着枪）。\n\n因此，牧场主无法证明其有权出于自卫使用致命武力，邻居很可能在“故意侵害身体（battery）”侵权诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）由于牧场主关于邻居携带武器的怀疑没有任何证据支持（即缺乏合理依据），其使用致命武力的行为是不正当的。\n\n（选项 B）在自卫时，若面临使用致命武力的抉择，当事人有义务先尝试撤退，除非是在自己家中面临致命武力威胁（即在“城堡”内无撤退义务）。\n\n由于牧场主当时身处其地产上的开阔牧场——而非家中——因此如果条件允许安全撤退，他就有义务撤退。\n\n（选项 C）尽管不得为了保护财产而使用致命武力，但如果财产纠纷升级，导致当事人面临死亡或严重身体伤害的危险，则可以使用致命武力。\n\n然而，在这起财产纠纷中，牧场主并无合理理由相信自己面临死亡或严重身体伤害的威胁，因此他使用致命武力的行为是不正当的。\n\n教育目标：自卫时使用的武力必须与预期的伤害程度相称。\n\n因此，只有在当事人有合理理由相信自己面临死亡或严重伤害的威胁时，方可使用致命武力。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 63 条 (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 63) (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于自卫的规定）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第65条（美国法律学会，1965年）（使用致命武力进行的自卫）。","conclusion":"答案 D：会，因为牧场主认为有必要使用枪支进行自卫是不合理的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-bf3f4215744e926b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":41,"sourceQuestionPage":123,"sourceQuestionPages":[123],"sourceExplanationPages":[124,125],"sourceAnswerPages":[124,125],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-042","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A mother gave her daughter, a high school senior, permission to host a party at their house. Around midnight, the daughter's boyfriend confronted another boy, the daughter's neighbor, and the two boys began to push and throw punches at each other. The mother overheard the commotion and yelled at the boys to stop fighting. When they continued to fight, the mother approached the boys and placed herself between them. The boys were so angry that they were not aware that the mother had tried to intervene. Meaning to hit each other, the boyfriend and the neighbor both hit the mother. In a suit against the boyfriend and the neighbor for battery, will the mother likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the boys intended to cause harmful contact with each other and not with the mother.","B":"No, because the mother assumed the risk of injury when she placed herself between the boys.","C":"Yes, because the boyfriend intended to hit the neighbor and the neighbor intended to hit the boyfriend.","D":"Yes, because the boys, by continuing to fight, were substantially certain to cause harmful contact with the mother."},"answer":"C","explanation":"An actor may intend to commit an intentional tort against one person but instead commit: a different intentional tort against that same person the same intentional tort against a different person or a different intentional tort against a different person. When this occurs, the actor's initial intent transfers to the tort that was actually committed or to the person who was actually harmed. This doctrine of transferred intent applies to five intentional torts, including battery, which is the intentional infliction of harmful or offensive contact with another. Here, the boys intended to cause harmful contact with each other since the boyfriend intended to hit the neighbor and the neighbor intended to hit the boyfriend (intent to commit battery). Because the boys hit the mother instead (same intentional tort; different person), each boy's initial intent to commit a battery against the other transferred to the mother (Choice A). Therefore, she will likely prevail in her suit against the boys for battery. (Choice B) Assumption of the risk is a defense to claims alleging negligent or reckless conduct. It negates or reduces the defendant's liability when the plaintiff voluntarily accepted a known risk of harm. But since battery requires proof of intentional conduct, this defense is irrelevant. (Choice D) The intent element for battery is met if the defendant (1) acted with the purpose of causing harmful or offensive contact or imminent apprehension thereof or (2) knew that his/her action was substantially certain to cause that result. Here, the boys were unaware that the mother had tried to intervene, so they did not act with the intent (purpose or knowledge) to commit a battery against her.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of transferred intent, an actor's intent to commit an intentional tort against one person transfers to the actor's commission of (1) a different intentional tort against that same person, (2) the intended tort against a different person, or (3) a different intentional tort against a different person.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (intent defined).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 110 (Am. Law Inst. 2015) (transferred intent).","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of transferred intent, an actor's intent to commit an intentional tort against one person transfers to the actor's commission of (1) a different intentional tort against that same person, (2) the intended tort against a different person, or (3) a different intentional tort against a different person.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (intent defined).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 110 (Am. Law Inst. 2015) (transferred intent).","rule":"Under the doctrine of transferred intent, an actor's intent to commit an intentional tort against one person transfers to the actor's commission of (1) a different intentional tort against that same person, (2) the intended tort against a different person, or (3) a different intentional tort against a different person.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit against the boyfriend and the neighbor for battery, will the mother likely prevail?","rule":"Under the doctrine of transferred intent, an actor's intent to commit an intentional tort against one person transfers to the actor's commission of (1) a different intentional tort against that same person, (2) the intended tort against a different person, or (3) a different intentional tort against a different person.","analysis":"An actor may intend to commit an intentional tort against one person but instead commit: a different intentional tort against that same person the same intentional tort against a different person or a different intentional tort against a different person. When this occurs, the actor's initial intent transfers to the tort that was actually committed or to the person who was actually harmed. This doctrine of transferred intent applies to five intentional torts, including battery, which is the intentional infliction of harmful or offensive contact with another. Here, the boys intended to cause harmful contact with each other since the boyfriend intended to hit the neighbor and the neighbor intended to hit the boyfriend (intent to commit battery). Because the boys hit the mother instead (same intentional tort; different person), each boy's initial intent to commit a battery against the other transferred to the mother (Choice A). Therefore, she will likely prevail in her suit against the boys for battery. (Choice B) Assumption of the risk is a defense to claims alleging negligent or reckless conduct. It negates or reduces the defendant's liability when the plaintiff voluntarily accepted a known risk of harm. But since battery requires proof of intentional conduct, this defense is irrelevant. (Choice D) The intent element for battery is met if the defendant (1) acted with the purpose of causing harmful or offensive contact or imminent apprehension thereof or (2) knew that his/her action was substantially certain to cause that result. Here, the boys were unaware that the mother had tried to intervene, so they did not act with the intent (purpose or knowledge) to commit a battery against her.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the boyfriend intended to hit the neighbor and the neighbor intended to hit the boyfriend."},"questionZh":"一位母亲允许她读高三的女儿在家里举办派对。\n\n午夜时分，女儿的男友与另一名男孩（女儿的邻居）发生冲突，两人开始互相推搡并挥拳相向。\n\n母亲听到了喧闹声，便大声喝止他们停止打斗。\n\n当两人继续打斗时，母亲走上前去，挡在了他们中间。\n\n当时两人怒火中烧，并未察觉母亲试图介入。\n\n男友和邻居本意是使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）对方，结果却都打到了母亲身上。\n\n如果母亲起诉男友和邻居犯有故意侵害身体（battery）罪（battery），她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会胜诉，因为这两名男孩意图对彼此造成有害接触，而非针对母亲。","B":"不会胜诉，因为母亲在挡到两名男孩中间时，已承担了受伤的风险。","C":"会胜诉，因为男友意图故意侵害身体（battery）邻居，而邻居也意图故意侵害身体（battery）男友。","D":"会胜诉，因为这两名男孩在继续打斗的过程中，实际上已确定会对母亲造成有害接触。"},"explanationZh":"行为人可能意图针对某人实施某种故意侵权行为，但实际上却实施了以下行为之一：针对同一人的另一种故意侵权行为、针对另一人的同一种故意侵权行为，或针对另一人的另一种故意侵权行为。\n\n发生这种情况时，行为人最初的意图会转移到实际实施的侵权行为或实际受害的人身上。\n\n这种“意图转移”原则适用于五种故意侵权行为，其中包括“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery），即故意对他人的身体造成有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n在本案中，男孩们意图彼此造成有害接触，因为男友意图击打邻居，而邻居也意图击打男友（即具有实施故意侵害身体（battery）的意图）。\n\n由于男孩们实际上击中了母亲（属于同一种故意侵权行为，但针对的是不同的人），因此每个男孩最初针对对方实施故意侵害身体（battery）的意图便转移到了母亲身上（选项 A）。\n\n因此，她针对男孩们提起的故意侵害身体（battery）诉讼很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）“风险自担”（assumption of the risk）是针对过失或鲁莽行为指控的一种抗辩理由。\n\n当原告自愿接受已知的伤害风险时，该抗辩可免除或减轻被告的责任。\n\n但由于故意侵害身体（battery）侵权需要证明存在故意行为，因此该抗辩理由不适用。\n\n（选项 D）如果被告 (1) 采取行动的目的是造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或使他人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感，或者 (2) 明知其行为极有可能导致该结果，则满足故意侵害身体（battery）侵权中的意图要件。\n\n在本案中，男孩们并未察觉母亲试图进行干预，因此他们并无针对母亲实施故意侵害身体（battery）的意图（即无相关目的或明知）。\n\n教育目标：根据意图转移原则，行为人针对某人实施故意侵权行为的意图，会转移到行为人实际实施的以下行为上：(1) 针对同一人的另一种故意侵权行为，(2) 针对另一人的原定侵权行为，或 (3) 针对另一人的另一种故意侵权行为。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 110 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2015)（意图转移）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“意图转移”原则（doctrine of transferred intent），行为人针对某人实施故意侵权行为的意图，会转移到其针对同一人实施的另一种故意侵权行为、针对另一人实施的原本意图实施的侵权行为，或针对另一人实施的另一种故意侵权行为上。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果母亲起诉男友和邻居犯有故意侵害身体（battery）罪，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"根据“意图转移”原则（doctrine of transferred intent），行为人针对某人实施故意侵权行为的意图，会转移到其针对同一人实施的另一种故意侵权行为、针对另一人实施的原本意图实施的侵权行为，或针对另一人实施的另一种故意侵权行为上。","analysis":"行为人可能意图针对某人实施某种故意侵权行为，但实际上却实施了以下行为之一：针对同一人的另一种故意侵权行为、针对另一人的同一种故意侵权行为，或针对另一人的另一种故意侵权行为。\n\n发生这种情况时，行为人最初的意图会转移到实际实施的侵权行为或实际受害的人身上。\n\n这种“意图转移”原则适用于五种故意侵权行为，其中包括“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery），即故意对他人的身体造成有害或冒犯性的接触。\n\n在本案中，男孩们意图彼此造成有害接触，因为男友意图击打邻居，而邻居也意图击打男友（即具有实施故意侵害身体（battery）的意图）。\n\n由于男孩们实际上击中了母亲（属于同一种故意侵权行为，但针对的是不同的人），因此每个男孩最初针对对方实施故意侵害身体（battery）的意图便转移到了母亲身上（选项 A）。\n\n因此，她针对男孩们提起的故意侵害身体（battery）诉讼很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）“风险自担”（assumption of the risk）是针对过失或鲁莽行为指控的一种抗辩理由。\n\n当原告自愿接受已知的伤害风险时，该抗辩可免除或减轻被告的责任。\n\n但由于故意侵害身体（battery）侵权需要证明存在故意行为，因此该抗辩理由不适用。\n\n（选项 D）如果被告 (1) 采取行动的目的是造成有害或冒犯性的接触，或使他人产生此类接触即将发生的恐惧感，或者 (2) 明知其行为极有可能导致该结果，则满足故意侵害身体（battery）侵权中的意图要件。\n\n在本案中，男孩们并未察觉母亲试图进行干预，因此他们并无针对母亲实施故意侵害身体（battery）的意图（即无相关目的或明知）。\n\n教育目标：根据意图转移原则，行为人针对某人实施故意侵权行为的意图，会转移到行为人实际实施的以下行为上：(1) 针对同一人的另一种故意侵权行为，(2) 针对另一人的原定侵权行为，或 (3) 针对另一人的另一种故意侵权行为。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（意图的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 110 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2015)（意图转移）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会胜诉，因为男友意图故意侵害身体（battery）邻居，而邻居也意图故意侵害身体（battery）男友。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"intervene","ipa":"/ˌɪntɝvˈin/","syllables":"in·ter·vene","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vi. 插入, 调停, 干涉；[经] 进场干预","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“intervene”","wordForm":"动词；词形 p:intervened/d:intervened/3:intervenes/i:intervening","sourceSentence":"The boys were so angry that they were not aware that the mother had tried to intervene.","synonyms":["step in","interfere","interpose"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-3fae23713bef9bed"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":42,"sourceQuestionPage":126,"sourceQuestionPages":[126],"sourceExplanationPages":[127,128],"sourceAnswerPages":[127,128],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-043","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A landowner owns a large tract of land on the shore of a lake. A woman used to live next to a stream that runs along one boundary of the landowner's tract and into the lake. A channel cuts across the landowner's tract and connects the stream to the lake. From where the woman lived, the channel served as a convenient shortcut to the lake. Erroneously believing that the channel was a public waterway, the woman used to make frequent trips through the channel in her motorboat. However, she did not harm the landowner's tract. The woman ceased using the channel when she later learned that the landowner owns it. If the landowner asserts a claim for damages against the woman based on trespass, is the landowner likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the woman did not cause any harm to the landowner's tract.","B":"No, because the woman did not use the channel after learning of the landowner's ownership.","C":"No, because the woman used the channel when she believed it was a public waterway.","D":"Yes and the landowner is entitled to nominal damages, because the woman intentionally used the channel."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A defendant is liable for trespass to land (ie, trespass quare clausum fregit) if: the defendant intentionally entered the plaintiff's property and the plaintiff did not consent to the entry. Since trespass only requires intent to enter the property in question—not intent to enter the property of another—the defendant's mistaken belief that he/she had the right to enter the property is irrelevant (Choice C). Additionally, proof of harm is not required, so a defendant is liable for trespass even when the entry does not damage the plaintiff's land (Choice A). However, the plaintiff can only recover nominal damages (a small sum of money—eg, $1—awarded to vindicate the plaintiff's rights when no actual loss or damage occurred) for a harmless trespass. Here, the woman is liable for trespass because she (1) intentionally used the landowner's channel and (2) did not have his consent to do so. And though her trespass did not harm the landowner's tract, the landowner can still recover nominal damages. (Choice B) Since the woman intentionally entered the landowner's channel without his consent, she is liable for trespass. The fact that she ceased using the channel after learning of the landowner's ownership does not excuse her earlier trespass.\n\nEducational objective: Trespass (an intentional entry onto another's property without permission) does not require proof of harm, but the plaintiff can only recover nominal damages for a harmless trespass. And since trespass only requires intent to enter the land, the defendant's mistaken belief that the entry was permissible is no defense.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of trespass to land).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 163 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (harmless trespass).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (mistaken trespass).","educationalObjective":"Trespass (an intentional entry onto another's property without permission) does not require proof of harm, but the plaintiff can only recover nominal damages for a harmless trespass. And since trespass only requires intent to enter the land, the defendant's mistaken belief that the entry was permissible is no defense.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 158 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of trespass to land).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 163 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (harmless trespass).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (mistaken trespass).","rule":"Trespass (an intentional entry onto another's property without permission) does not require proof of harm, but the plaintiff can only recover nominal damages for a harmless trespass. And since trespass only requires intent to enter the land, the defendant's mistaken belief that the entry was permissible is no defense.","irac":{"issue":"If the landowner asserts a claim for damages against the woman based on trespass, is the landowner likely to prevail?","rule":"Trespass (an intentional entry onto another's property without permission) does not require proof of harm, but the plaintiff can only recover nominal damages for a harmless trespass. And since trespass only requires intent to enter the land, the defendant's mistaken belief that the entry was permissible is no defense.","analysis":"A defendant is liable for trespass to land (ie, trespass quare clausum fregit) if: the defendant intentionally entered the plaintiff's property and the plaintiff did not consent to the entry. Since trespass only requires intent to enter the property in question—not intent to enter the property of another—the defendant's mistaken belief that he/she had the right to enter the property is irrelevant (Choice C). Additionally, proof of harm is not required, so a defendant is liable for trespass even when the entry does not damage the plaintiff's land (Choice A). However, the plaintiff can only recover nominal damages (a small sum of money—eg, $1—awarded to vindicate the plaintiff's rights when no actual loss or damage occurred) for a harmless trespass. Here, the woman is liable for trespass because she (1) intentionally used the landowner's channel and (2) did not have his consent to do so. And though her trespass did not harm the landowner's tract, the landowner can still recover nominal damages. (Choice B) Since the woman intentionally entered the landowner's channel without his consent, she is liable for trespass. The fact that she ceased using the channel after learning of the landowner's ownership does not excuse her earlier trespass.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes and the landowner is entitled to nominal damages, because the woman intentionally used the channel."},"questionZh":"某地主在湖畔拥有一大片土地。\n\n一位女士曾居住在一条溪流旁，该溪流沿地主土地的一侧边界流淌并最终汇入湖中。\n\n地主土地上有一条水道，连接着这条溪流与湖泊。\n\n对于这位女士而言，这条水道是前往湖泊的便捷捷径。\n\n由于误以为该水道属于公共水路，该女士过去常驾驶摩托艇通过该水道。\n\n然而，她的行为并未对地主的土地造成损害。\n\n在得知该水道归地主所有后，该女士便停止了使用。\n\n如果地主以侵入土地（trespass）为由向该女士索赔，地主胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会胜诉，因为该女士未对地主的土地造成任何损害。","B":"不会胜诉，因为该女士在得知地主拥有该水道的所有权后，便不再使用它了。","C":"不会胜诉，因为该女士使用该水道时，误以为它是公共水路。","D":"会胜诉，且地主有权获得象征性赔偿，因为该女士是有意使用该水道的。"},"explanationZh":"如果被告故意进入原告的财产且未获得原告同意，则被告须对侵犯土地权（即“侵入他人封闭土地”之侵权行为，*trespass quare clausum fregit*）承担责任。\n\n由于侵权行为仅要求具备进入相关财产的意图——而非必须具备进入“他人”财产的意图——因此，被告误以为自己有权进入该财产的事实并不构成抗辩理由（选项 C）。\n\n此外，无需证明存在损害，因此即使进入行为未对原告的土地造成损害，被告仍须承担侵权责任（选项 A）。\n\n不过，对于未造成实际损害的侵权行为，原告仅能获得名义赔偿（即为维护原告权利而判给的一笔小数额金钱，例如 1 美元，适用于未发生实际损失或损害的情况）。\n\n在本案中，该女子须承担侵权责任，因为她 (1) 故意使用了土地所有者的水道，且 (2) 未获得其同意。\n\n尽管她的侵权行为未损害土地所有者的地块，但土地所有者仍可获得名义赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）由于该女子在未经同意的情况下故意进入土地所有者的水道，她须承担侵权责任。\n\n她在得知土地所有权归属后停止使用该水道的事实，并不能免除其先前侵权行为的责任。\n\n教育目标：侵犯土地权（指未经许可故意进入他人财产）无需证明存在损害，但对于未造成损害的侵权行为，原告仅能获得名义赔偿。\n\n此外，由于侵权行为仅要求具备进入土地的意图，被告误以为进入行为是允许的，这一事实不能构成抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 158 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于侵犯土地权的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 163 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于未造成损害的侵权行为）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 164 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于因误解而发生的侵权行为）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"侵入土地（即未经许可故意进入他人财产）无需证明造成了损害，但对于未造成损害的侵入行为，原告仅能获得象征性赔偿。\n\n此外，由于侵入土地仅要求具备进入土地的意图，被告误以为其进入行为是获准的，这一理由不能构成抗辩。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果地主以侵入土地为由向该女士索赔，地主胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"侵入土地（即未经许可故意进入他人财产）无需证明造成了损害，但对于未造成损害的侵入行为，原告仅能获得象征性赔偿。\n\n此外，由于侵入土地仅要求具备进入土地的意图，被告误以为其进入行为是获准的，这一理由不能构成抗辩。","analysis":"如果被告故意进入原告的财产且未获得原告同意，则被告须对侵犯土地权（即“侵入他人封闭土地”之侵权行为，*trespass quare clausum fregit*）承担责任。\n\n由于侵权行为仅要求具备进入相关财产的意图——而非必须具备进入“他人”财产的意图——因此，被告误以为自己有权进入该财产的事实并不构成抗辩理由（选项 C）。\n\n此外，无需证明存在损害，因此即使进入行为未对原告的土地造成损害，被告仍须承担侵权责任（选项 A）。\n\n不过，对于未造成实际损害的侵权行为，原告仅能获得名义赔偿（即为维护原告权利而判给的一笔小数额金钱，例如 1 美元，适用于未发生实际损失或损害的情况）。\n\n在本案中，该女子须承担侵权责任，因为她 (1) 故意使用了土地所有者的水道，且 (2) 未获得其同意。\n\n尽管她的侵权行为未损害土地所有者的地块，但土地所有者仍可获得名义赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）由于该女子在未经同意的情况下故意进入土地所有者的水道，她须承担侵权责任。\n\n她在得知土地所有权归属后停止使用该水道的事实，并不能免除其先前侵权行为的责任。\n\n教育目标：侵犯土地权（指未经许可故意进入他人财产）无需证明存在损害，但对于未造成损害的侵权行为，原告仅能获得名义赔偿。\n\n此外，由于侵权行为仅要求具备进入土地的意图，被告误以为进入行为是允许的，这一事实不能构成抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 158 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于侵犯土地权的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 163 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于未造成损害的侵权行为）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 164 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于因误解而发生的侵权行为）。","conclusion":"答案 D：会胜诉，且地主有权获得象征性赔偿，因为该女士是有意使用该水道的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-f402ca5029a4649a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":43,"sourceQuestionPage":129,"sourceQuestionPages":[129],"sourceExplanationPages":[130,131],"sourceAnswerPages":[130,131],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-044","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A real estate developer was trying to purchase land on which he intended to build a large commercial development. An elderly widow had rejected all of the developer's offers to buy her ancestral home, where she had lived all her life and which was located in the middle of the developer's planned development. Finally, the developer offered her $250,000. He told her that it was his last offer and that if she rejected it, state law authorized him to have her property condemned. The widow then consulted her nephew, a law student, who researched the question and advised her that the developer had no power of condemnation under state law. The widow had been frightened by the developer's threat and was outraged when she learned that the developer had lied to her. If the widow sues the developer for emotional distress damages, will she prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because it was not the developer's purpose to cause emotional distress.","B":"No, because the widow did not suffer emotional distress that was severe.","C":"Yes, because the developer's action was extreme and outrageous.","D":"Yes, because the widow was frightened and outraged."},"answer":"B","explanation":"For intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED), the plaintiff must prove that the defendant intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress—ie, distress that no reasonable person could be expected to endure—through extreme and outrageous conduct. The severity of the emotional distress depends on: the intensity and duration of the distress the egregiousness of the defendant's conduct and the presence of bodily harm. Here, the developer's threat to condemn the widow's ancestral home may have been reckless and possibly was extreme and outrageous. However, there is no evidence that her fright and outrage were intense or prolonged (eg, had she been scared for months, not moments) or that she suffered bodily harm (eg, had she experienced a heart attack upon hearing the threat). Therefore, the widow's emotional distress was not severe and her claim will fail. (Choice A) IIED requires intent (ie, purpose or knowledge) to cause severe emotional distress OR reckless disregard of the risk of causing that distress. Therefore, even if it was not the developer's purpose to cause the widow severe emotional distress, he could still be liable for IIED if he knew of or recklessly ignored that risk. (Choice C) Even if the developer's conduct was extreme and outrageous, the widow will not recover emotional distress damages since that conduct did not cause her severe emotional distress. (Choice D) Some degree of emotional distress (eg, temporary fright or anger) is expected as a part of life, so IIED requires that the plaintiff's emotional distress be severe (and the result of extreme and outrageous conduct). Here, though the widow was frightened and outraged, her emotional distress was not severe.\n\nEducational objective: Intentional infliction of emotional distress requires proof of severe emotional distress—ie, emotional distress beyond what a reasonable person could be expected to endure. The severity of emotional distress depends on (1) its intensity and duration, (2) the egregiousness of the defendant's conduct, and (3) the presence of bodily harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress).","educationalObjective":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress requires proof of severe emotional distress—ie, emotional distress beyond what a reasonable person could be expected to endure. The severity of emotional distress depends on (1) its intensity and duration, (2) the egregiousness of the defendant's conduct, and (3) the presence of bodily harm.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress).","rule":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress requires proof of severe emotional distress—ie, emotional distress beyond what a reasonable person could be expected to endure. The severity of emotional distress depends on (1) its intensity and duration, (2) the egregiousness of the defendant's conduct, and (3) the presence of bodily harm.","irac":{"issue":"If the widow sues the developer for emotional distress damages, will she prevail?","rule":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress requires proof of severe emotional distress—ie, emotional distress beyond what a reasonable person could be expected to endure. The severity of emotional distress depends on (1) its intensity and duration, (2) the egregiousness of the defendant's conduct, and (3) the presence of bodily harm.","analysis":"For intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED), the plaintiff must prove that the defendant intentionally or recklessly caused the plaintiff severe emotional distress—ie, distress that no reasonable person could be expected to endure—through extreme and outrageous conduct. The severity of the emotional distress depends on: the intensity and duration of the distress the egregiousness of the defendant's conduct and the presence of bodily harm. Here, the developer's threat to condemn the widow's ancestral home may have been reckless and possibly was extreme and outrageous. However, there is no evidence that her fright and outrage were intense or prolonged (eg, had she been scared for months, not moments) or that she suffered bodily harm (eg, had she experienced a heart attack upon hearing the threat). Therefore, the widow's emotional distress was not severe and her claim will fail. (Choice A) IIED requires intent (ie, purpose or knowledge) to cause severe emotional distress OR reckless disregard of the risk of causing that distress. Therefore, even if it was not the developer's purpose to cause the widow severe emotional distress, he could still be liable for IIED if he knew of or recklessly ignored that risk. (Choice C) Even if the developer's conduct was extreme and outrageous, the widow will not recover emotional distress damages since that conduct did not cause her severe emotional distress. (Choice D) Some degree of emotional distress (eg, temporary fright or anger) is expected as a part of life, so IIED requires that the plaintiff's emotional distress be severe (and the result of extreme and outrageous conduct). Here, though the widow was frightened and outraged, her emotional distress was not severe.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the widow did not suffer emotional distress that was severe."},"questionZh":"一位房地产开发商试图购入土地，计划开发一个大型商业项目。\n\n一位年迈的寡妇拒绝了开发商提出的所有收购其祖宅的报价；这处房产位于开发商规划项目的中心地带，也是她生活了一辈子的地方。\n\n最终，开发商向她开出了25万美元的报价。\n\n他告诉她，这是最后一次报价；如果她拒绝，根据州法律，他有权申请征收（或强制收购）她的房产。\n\n随后，这位寡妇咨询了身为法学院学生的侄子，侄子在研究相关法律后告知她，开发商并无权根据州法律进行征收。\n\n寡妇此前因开发商的威胁而感到恐惧，在得知开发商撒谎后，她感到非常愤怒。\n\n如果这位寡妇因精神痛苦（emotional distress）而起诉开发商要求赔偿，她能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为开发商的主观意图并非造成精神痛苦。","B":"不能，因为这位寡妇并未遭受严重程度的精神痛苦。","C":"能，因为开发商的行为极端且令人愤慨（extreme and outrageous）。","D":"能，因为这位寡妇感到了恐惧和愤怒。"},"explanationZh":"对于“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）这一侵权行为，原告必须证明被告通过极端且令人愤慨的行为，故意或鲁莽地导致了原告严重的精神痛苦——即常人无法忍受的痛苦。\n\n精神痛苦的严重程度取决于：痛苦的强度与持续时间、被告行为的恶劣程度，以及是否存在身体伤害。\n\n在本案中，开发商威胁要征收这位寡妇的祖宅，其行为可能是鲁莽的，也可能是极端且令人愤慨的。\n\n然而，没有证据表明她的惊恐和愤怒是强烈或持久的（例如，她是否经历了长达数月而非短暂瞬间的恐惧），也没有证据表明她遭受了身体伤害（例如，在听到威胁时是否突发心脏病）。\n\n因此，该寡妇所受的精神痛苦并不严重，她的诉讼请求将无法成立。\n\n（选项 A）IIED 要求行为人具有导致严重精神痛苦的意图（即目的或明知），或者鲁莽地忽视导致此类痛苦的风险。\n\n因此，即使开发商并无意导致该寡妇遭受严重精神痛苦，只要他明知或鲁莽地忽视了这种风险，仍可能需承担 IIED 的法律责任。\n\n（选项 C）即使开发商的行为属于极端且令人愤慨的行为，该寡妇也无法获得精神痛苦损害赔偿，因为该行为并未导致她遭受严重的精神痛苦。\n\n（选项 D）生活中难免会出现一定程度的精神痛苦（如短暂的惊恐或愤怒），因此 IIED 要求原告所受的精神痛苦必须是严重的（且由极端且令人愤慨的行为引起）。\n\n在本案中，尽管该寡妇感到惊恐和愤怒，但其精神痛苦并未达到严重的程度。\n\n教学目标：“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”要求证明存在严重的精神痛苦——即超出常人所能忍受程度的精神痛苦。\n\n精神痛苦的严重程度取决于：(1) 痛苦的强度与持续时间，(2) 被告行为的恶劣程度，以及 (3) 是否存在身体伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"构成“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（intentional infliction of emotional distress）的侵权行为，需要证明存在严重精神痛苦——即超出理性人所能承受限度的精神痛苦。\n\n精神痛苦的严重程度取决于：(1) 痛苦的强度与持续时间；(2) 被告行为的恶劣程度；以及 (3) 是否伴随身体伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果这位寡妇因精神痛苦而起诉开发商要求赔偿，她能胜诉吗？","rule":"构成“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（intentional infliction of emotional distress）的侵权行为，需要证明存在严重精神痛苦——即超出理性人所能承受限度的精神痛苦。\n\n精神痛苦的严重程度取决于：(1) 痛苦的强度与持续时间；(2) 被告行为的恶劣程度；以及 (3) 是否伴随身体伤害。","analysis":"对于“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）这一侵权行为，原告必须证明被告通过极端且令人愤慨的行为，故意或鲁莽地导致了原告严重的精神痛苦——即常人无法忍受的痛苦。\n\n精神痛苦的严重程度取决于：痛苦的强度与持续时间、被告行为的恶劣程度，以及是否存在身体伤害。\n\n在本案中，开发商威胁要征收这位寡妇的祖宅，其行为可能是鲁莽的，也可能是极端且令人愤慨的。\n\n然而，没有证据表明她的惊恐和愤怒是强烈或持久的（例如，她是否经历了长达数月而非短暂瞬间的恐惧），也没有证据表明她遭受了身体伤害（例如，在听到威胁时是否突发心脏病）。\n\n因此，该寡妇所受的精神痛苦并不严重，她的诉讼请求将无法成立。\n\n（选项 A）IIED 要求行为人具有导致严重精神痛苦的意图（即目的或明知），或者鲁莽地忽视导致此类痛苦的风险。\n\n因此，即使开发商并无意导致该寡妇遭受严重精神痛苦，只要他明知或鲁莽地忽视了这种风险，仍可能需承担 IIED 的法律责任。\n\n（选项 C）即使开发商的行为属于极端且令人愤慨的行为，该寡妇也无法获得精神痛苦损害赔偿，因为该行为并未导致她遭受严重的精神痛苦。\n\n（选项 D）生活中难免会出现一定程度的精神痛苦（如短暂的惊恐或愤怒），因此 IIED 要求原告所受的精神痛苦必须是严重的（且由极端且令人愤慨的行为引起）。\n\n在本案中，尽管该寡妇感到惊恐和愤怒，但其精神痛苦并未达到严重的程度。\n\n教学目标：“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”要求证明存在严重的精神痛苦——即超出常人所能忍受程度的精神痛苦。\n\n精神痛苦的严重程度取决于：(1) 痛苦的强度与持续时间，(2) 被告行为的恶劣程度，以及 (3) 是否存在身体伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不能，因为这位寡妇并未遭受严重程度的精神痛苦。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-ac9630b39b1a7ea7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":44,"sourceQuestionPage":132,"sourceQuestionPages":[132],"sourceExplanationPages":[133,134],"sourceAnswerPages":[133,134],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-045","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A young man was walking peacefully along a public street when he encountered a woman he had never seen before. Without provocation or warning, the woman picked up a rock and struck the young man with it. It was later established that the woman was mentally ill and suffered recurrent visual hallucinations. In an action by the young man against the woman for battery, which of the following, if supported by evidence, will be the woman's best defense?","choices":{"A":"The woman did not desire to cause harm to the young man.","B":"The woman did not know that she was striking a person.","C":"The woman did not understand that her act was wrongful.","D":"The woman thought the young man was about to attack her."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Deficient mental capacity is not a defense to liability for intentional torts. Instead, mental capacity is considered when determining whether the defendant acted with the requisite intent. For battery, the defendant must act with the intent to cause contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) with the plaintiff. Although the woman struck the young man with a rock, she is only liable for battery if she did so with the intent to strike the young man or cause him apprehension. Therefore, the woman's best defense is that she did not know (intent) she was striking a person (eg, her recurrent visual hallucinations caused her to perceive the young man as a tree or wild dog). (Choice A) Battery requires intent to cause contact or imminent apprehension of contact— not harm. Therefore, it only matters whether the woman intended to cause contact or apprehension, not whether she desired to harm the young man. (Choice C) To meet the intent requirement for battery, the woman only had to understand that she struck the young man with the rock. She did not need to understand that her act was wrongful. (Choice D) Self-defense is only available as a defense if the defendant reasonably believed that force was necessary. Here, the woman could not have reasonably believed that the young man was about to attack her because the young man was walking peacefully. Therefore, this defense is not available.\n\nEducational objective: Deficient mental capacity is not a defense to liability for intentional torts, but is considered in determining whether the defendant acted with the requisite intent. Battery requires the intent to cause contact or imminent apprehension of contact.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 895J (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (deficient mental capacity).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 8A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of intent).","educationalObjective":"Deficient mental capacity is not a defense to liability for intentional torts, but is considered in determining whether the defendant acted with the requisite intent. Battery requires the intent to cause contact or imminent apprehension of contact.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 895J (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (deficient mental capacity).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 13 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (battery by harmful contact).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 8A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of intent).","rule":"Deficient mental capacity is not a defense to liability for intentional torts, but is considered in determining whether the defendant acted with the requisite intent. Battery requires the intent to cause contact or imminent apprehension of contact.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the young man against the woman for battery, which of the following, if supported by evidence, will be the woman's best defense?","rule":"Deficient mental capacity is not a defense to liability for intentional torts, but is considered in determining whether the defendant acted with the requisite intent. Battery requires the intent to cause contact or imminent apprehension of contact.","analysis":"Deficient mental capacity is not a defense to liability for intentional torts. Instead, mental capacity is considered when determining whether the defendant acted with the requisite intent. For battery, the defendant must act with the intent to cause contact (or imminent apprehension of contact) with the plaintiff. Although the woman struck the young man with a rock, she is only liable for battery if she did so with the intent to strike the young man or cause him apprehension. Therefore, the woman's best defense is that she did not know (intent) she was striking a person (eg, her recurrent visual hallucinations caused her to perceive the young man as a tree or wild dog). (Choice A) Battery requires intent to cause contact or imminent apprehension of contact— not harm. Therefore, it only matters whether the woman intended to cause contact or apprehension, not whether she desired to harm the young man. (Choice C) To meet the intent requirement for battery, the woman only had to understand that she struck the young man with the rock. She did not need to understand that her act was wrongful. (Choice D) Self-defense is only available as a defense if the defendant reasonably believed that force was necessary. Here, the woman could not have reasonably believed that the young man was about to attack her because the young man was walking peacefully. Therefore, this defense is not available.","conclusion":"Answer B: The woman did not know that she was striking a person."},"questionZh":"一名年轻男子在公共街道上平静地行走时，遇到了一位素未谋面的女子。\n\n该女子在没有任何挑衅或警告的情况下，捡起一块石头砸向了这名年轻男子。\n\n事后证实，该女子患有精神疾病，并反复出现幻觉。\n\n如果该年轻男子以故意侵害身体（battery）罪起诉该女子，以下哪项（如有证据支持）将是该女子的最佳辩护理由？","choicesZh":{"A":"该女子并非有意伤害该年轻男子。","B":"该女子不知道自己是在打人。","C":"该女子不理解自己的行为是错误的。","D":"该女子认为该年轻男子即将使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）她。"},"explanationZh":"精神能力不足不能作为免除故意侵权责任的抗辩理由。\n\n相反，在判定被告是否具备必要的“意图”时，才会考量其精神能力。\n\n就“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）这一侵权行为而言，被告必须具有导致与原告发生接触（或使原告产生即将发生接触的恐惧感）的意图。\n\n尽管该女子用石头击中了那名年轻人，但只有在她主观上意图击中该年轻人或意图使其产生恐惧感的情况下，她才需承担故意侵害身体（battery）行为的法律责任。\n\n因此，该女子最有力的抗辩理由是：她并不知道（即缺乏相应意图）自己击中的是一个人（例如，由于反复发作的视幻觉，她将该年轻人误认为是一棵树或一只野狗）。\n\n（选项 A）故意侵害身体（battery）行为的构成要件是意图导致接触或使人产生即将发生接触的恐惧感，而非意图造成伤害。\n\n因此，关键在于该女子是否有意导致接触或产生恐惧感，而不在于她是否有意伤害该年轻人。\n\n（选项 C）要满足故意侵害身体（battery）行为的意图要件，该女子只需意识到自己用石头击中了该年轻人即可；她无需意识到自己的行为是不当的。\n\n（选项 D）只有在被告合理地认为有必要使用武力的情况下，正当防卫才能作为抗辩理由。\n\n在本案中，由于该年轻人当时正平静地行走，该女子不可能合理地认为他即将对自己发起使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）。\n\n因此，该抗辩理由不成立。\n\n教育目标：精神能力不足不能作为免除故意侵权责任的抗辩理由，但在判定被告是否具备必要的意图时会予以考量。\n\n故意侵害身体（battery）行为要求具备导致接触或使人产生即将发生接触的恐惧感的意图。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 895J 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（关于精神能力不足）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 8A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于意图的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"精神能力缺陷并非故意侵权行为的抗辩理由，但会在判断被告是否具有必要的故意时予以考虑。\n\n故意侵害身体（battery）罪要求被告具有造成身体接触或即将造成身体接触的意图。","iracZh":{"issue":"在一名年轻男子起诉一名女子故意侵害身体（battery）的案件中，如果以下哪项有证据支持，将是该女子最好的辩护理由？","rule":"精神能力缺陷并非故意侵权行为的抗辩理由，但会在判断被告是否具有必要的故意时予以考虑。\n\n故意侵害身体（battery）罪要求被告具有造成身体接触或即将造成身体接触的意图。","analysis":"精神能力不足不能作为免除故意侵权责任的抗辩理由。\n\n相反，在判定被告是否具备必要的“意图”时，才会考量其精神能力。\n\n就“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）这一侵权行为而言，被告必须具有导致与原告发生接触（或使原告产生即将发生接触的恐惧感）的意图。\n\n尽管该女子用石头击中了那名年轻人，但只有在她主观上意图击中该年轻人或意图使其产生恐惧感的情况下，她才需承担故意侵害身体（battery）行为的法律责任。\n\n因此，该女子最有力的抗辩理由是：她并不知道（即缺乏相应意图）自己击中的是一个人（例如，由于反复发作的视幻觉，她将该年轻人误认为是一棵树或一只野狗）。\n\n（选项 A）故意侵害身体（battery）行为的构成要件是意图导致接触或使人产生即将发生接触的恐惧感，而非意图造成伤害。\n\n因此，关键在于该女子是否有意导致接触或产生恐惧感，而不在于她是否有意伤害该年轻人。\n\n（选项 C）要满足故意侵害身体（battery）行为的意图要件，该女子只需意识到自己用石头击中了该年轻人即可；她无需意识到自己的行为是不当的。\n\n（选项 D）只有在被告合理地认为有必要使用武力的情况下，正当防卫才能作为抗辩理由。\n\n在本案中，由于该年轻人当时正平静地行走，该女子不可能合理地认为他即将对自己发起使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）。\n\n因此，该抗辩理由不成立。\n\n教育目标：精神能力不足不能作为免除故意侵权责任的抗辩理由，但在判定被告是否具备必要的意图时会予以考量。\n\n故意侵害身体（battery）行为要求具备导致接触或使人产生即将发生接触的恐惧感的意图。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 895J 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（关于精神能力不足）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 13 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于因有害接触构成的故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 8A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于意图的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该女子不知道自己是在打人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-6749e5f90c3c572f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":45,"sourceQuestionPage":135,"sourceQuestionPages":[135],"sourceExplanationPages":[136,137],"sourceAnswerPages":[136,137],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-046","subject":"Torts","topic":"Nuisance","question":"A company owned and operated a private golf course. One of the fairways on the course ran parallel to a navigable body of water that is rarely used by boats. The company was aware that golfers frequently but unintentionally hit golf balls into the water when playing that hole because there were no barriers to prevent the balls from going into the water. The golf balls resulted in pollution of the water in violation of a local ordinance that prohibits the pollution of bodies of water in the jurisdiction. A 12-year-old child was sailing on the water with his family when he was struck by a golf ball that a golfer unintentionally hit from the golf course toward the water. The child suffered a serious physical injury. The injured child's parents have filed a public nuisance action against the company, on behalf of their child, to recover for his injuries. Are the parents precluded from recovery?","choices":{"A":"No, because of the attractive nuisance doctrine.","B":"No, because the child suffered harm that was different from that suffered by the public at large.","C":"Yes, because the child did not have an ownership interest in land.","D":"Yes, because the golfer's action was unintentional."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Public nuisance is an unreasonable interference with a right common to the general public, which requires proof that: the defendant interfered with a public right—eg, affecting the public by polluting bodies of water—and\n\nthat interference was unreasonable—ie, it (1) significantly affected public health, safety, peace, or property rights or (2) violated an ordinance, statute, or administrative regulation.\n\nGenerally, only a public entity (eg, a state) can sue for public nuisance. However, a private individual can bring an action for public nuisance if that individual suffered special damage—ie, harm different from (not merely greater in extent or degree than) the harm sustained by the public at large. Here, the company interfered with a public right by polluting the navigable body of water with golf balls from its course. This interference was unreasonable because it violated a local ordinance. But since the child is a private individual, his parents will prevail on a public nuisance claim on his behalf only if they can prove that his harm was different from the harm (pollution) sustained by the public. The parents can likely do so because the child was struck by a golf ball while sailing (a rare use of the water). Therefore, they are not precluded from recovery. (Choice A) The attractive nuisance doctrine applies when a child trespasses on the defendant's property and is injured by an artificial condition on that property (not seen here). (Choice C) Private nuisance requires proof that the defendant interfered with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of property in which the plaintiff has an ownership interest. However, public nuisance (the claim asserted here) does not require that the plaintiff have an ownership interest. (Choice D) The fact that the golfer unintentionally hit the golf ball toward the water is irrelevant to determining whether the parents may recover from the company for public nuisance.\n\nEducational objective: A private individual can prevail in an action for public nuisance—ie, an unreasonable interference with a public right—if that individual can show harm different from that suffered by the public at large (ie, special damage).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821B (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defining public nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821C (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (explaining that a private plaintiff must prove special damage).","educationalObjective":"A private individual can prevail in an action for public nuisance—ie, an unreasonable interference with a public right—if that individual can show harm different from that suffered by the public at large (ie, special damage).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 821B (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defining public nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821C (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (explaining that a private plaintiff must prove special damage).","rule":"A private individual can prevail in an action for public nuisance—ie, an unreasonable interference with a public right—if that individual can show harm different from that suffered by the public at large (ie, special damage).","irac":{"issue":"Are the parents precluded from recovery?","rule":"A private individual can prevail in an action for public nuisance—ie, an unreasonable interference with a public right—if that individual can show harm different from that suffered by the public at large (ie, special damage).","analysis":"Public nuisance is an unreasonable interference with a right common to the general public, which requires proof that: the defendant interfered with a public right—eg, affecting the public by polluting bodies of water—and\n\nthat interference was unreasonable—ie, it (1) significantly affected public health, safety, peace, or property rights or (2) violated an ordinance, statute, or administrative regulation.\n\nGenerally, only a public entity (eg, a state) can sue for public nuisance. However, a private individual can bring an action for public nuisance if that individual suffered special damage—ie, harm different from (not merely greater in extent or degree than) the harm sustained by the public at large. Here, the company interfered with a public right by polluting the navigable body of water with golf balls from its course. This interference was unreasonable because it violated a local ordinance. But since the child is a private individual, his parents will prevail on a public nuisance claim on his behalf only if they can prove that his harm was different from the harm (pollution) sustained by the public. The parents can likely do so because the child was struck by a golf ball while sailing (a rare use of the water). Therefore, they are not precluded from recovery. (Choice A) The attractive nuisance doctrine applies when a child trespasses on the defendant's property and is injured by an artificial condition on that property (not seen here). (Choice C) Private nuisance requires proof that the defendant interfered with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of property in which the plaintiff has an ownership interest. However, public nuisance (the claim asserted here) does not require that the plaintiff have an ownership interest. (Choice D) The fact that the golfer unintentionally hit the golf ball toward the water is irrelevant to determining whether the parents may recover from the company for public nuisance.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the child suffered harm that was different from that suffered by the public at large."},"questionZh":"某公司拥有并运营着一个私人高尔夫球场。\n\n球场内的一条球道与一处可通航但极少有船只通行水域平行。\n\n由于缺乏阻挡设施，该公司明知高尔夫球手在打该洞时经常会无意中将球击入水中。\n\n这些高尔夫球造成了水体污染，违反了当地关于禁止污染辖区内水体的法规。\n\n一名12岁儿童随家人在水上航行时，被一名高尔夫球手从球场向水面无意击出的一颗高尔夫球击中，导致身体严重受伤。\n\n受伤儿童的父母代表其子女对该公司提起了“公害”（public nuisance）诉讼，以寻求赔偿。\n\n这对父母是否无法获得赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为适用“诱惑性危险物”原则（attractive nuisance doctrine）。","B":"不会，因为该儿童遭受的伤害与公众遭受的伤害不同。","C":"会，因为该儿童对土地不享有所有权权益。","D":"会，因为高尔夫球手的行为是无意的。"},"explanationZh":"“公害”（public nuisance）是指对公众共有权利的不合理干扰。认定公害需要证明：被告干扰了某项公共权利——例如，通过污染水体影响公众——且 该干扰是不合理的——即该干扰 (1) 严重影响了公众的健康、安全、安宁或财产权，或者 (2) 违反了地方法令、成文法或行政法规。\n\n通常，只有公共实体（如州政府）才能就公害提起诉讼。\n\n然而，如果私人遭受了“特殊损害”——即不同于（而不仅仅是程度或规模上重于）公众所受损害的伤害——该私人也可以就公害提起诉讼。\n\n在本案中，该公司通过将高尔夫球场的高尔夫球打入通航水域，干扰了公共权利。\n\n这种干扰因违反地方法令而被视为不合理。\n\n但由于该儿童属于私人，其父母若要代表他成功主张公害索赔，必须证明他所受的伤害不同于公众所受的伤害（即污染本身）。\n\n父母很可能能够证明这一点，因为该儿童是在进行航行活动（一种罕见的水域使用方式）时被高尔夫球击中的。\n\n因此，他们有权获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）“诱惑性危险物”原则（attractive nuisance doctrine）适用于儿童侵入被告财产并因该财产上的人造设施而受伤的情形（本案不涉及此情形）。\n\n（选项 C）“私害”（private nuisance）索赔要求证明被告干扰了原告对其拥有所有权权益的财产的使用和享用。\n\n然而，公害（本案所主张的诉由）并不要求原告拥有所有权权益。\n\n（选项 D）高尔夫球手无意中将球打向水域这一事实，与确定父母是否可就公害向公司索赔无关。\n\n教育目标：如果私人能够证明其遭受的伤害不同于公众所受的伤害（即“特殊损害”），则该私人可以在公害（即对公共权利的不合理干扰）诉讼中胜诉。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821B 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（定义公害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821C条（美国法律协会，1977年）（阐明私人原告必须证明存在特别损害）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果私人能够证明其遭受的伤害与公众遭受的伤害不同（即“特殊损害”），则该私人可以在公害诉讼（即对公共权利的不合理干扰）中胜诉。","iracZh":{"issue":"这对父母是否无法获得赔偿？","rule":"如果私人能够证明其遭受的伤害与公众遭受的伤害不同（即“特殊损害”），则该私人可以在公害诉讼（即对公共权利的不合理干扰）中胜诉。","analysis":"“公害”（public nuisance）是指对公众共有权利的不合理干扰。认定公害需要证明：被告干扰了某项公共权利——例如，通过污染水体影响公众——且 该干扰是不合理的——即该干扰 (1) 严重影响了公众的健康、安全、安宁或财产权，或者 (2) 违反了地方法令、成文法或行政法规。\n\n通常，只有公共实体（如州政府）才能就公害提起诉讼。\n\n然而，如果私人遭受了“特殊损害”——即不同于（而不仅仅是程度或规模上重于）公众所受损害的伤害——该私人也可以就公害提起诉讼。\n\n在本案中，该公司通过将高尔夫球场的高尔夫球打入通航水域，干扰了公共权利。\n\n这种干扰因违反地方法令而被视为不合理。\n\n但由于该儿童属于私人，其父母若要代表他成功主张公害索赔，必须证明他所受的伤害不同于公众所受的伤害（即污染本身）。\n\n父母很可能能够证明这一点，因为该儿童是在进行航行活动（一种罕见的水域使用方式）时被高尔夫球击中的。\n\n因此，他们有权获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）“诱惑性危险物”原则（attractive nuisance doctrine）适用于儿童侵入被告财产并因该财产上的人造设施而受伤的情形（本案不涉及此情形）。\n\n（选项 C）“私害”（private nuisance）索赔要求证明被告干扰了原告对其拥有所有权权益的财产的使用和享用。\n\n然而，公害（本案所主张的诉由）并不要求原告拥有所有权权益。\n\n（选项 D）高尔夫球手无意中将球打向水域这一事实，与确定父母是否可就公害向公司索赔无关。\n\n教育目标：如果私人能够证明其遭受的伤害不同于公众所受的伤害（即“特殊损害”），则该私人可以在公害（即对公共权利的不合理干扰）诉讼中胜诉。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821B 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（定义公害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821C条（美国法律协会，1977年）（阐明私人原告必须证明存在特别损害）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为该儿童遭受的伤害与公众遭受的伤害不同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"public nuisance","ipa":"/pˈəblɪk nˈusəns/","syllables":"pub·lic nui·sance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"公共妨害：不合理干扰公众共同权利","contextualMeaning":"公共妨害：不合理干扰公众共同权利","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The injured child's parents have filed a public nuisance action against the company, on behalf of their child, to recover for his injuries.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-6a3fe23a38e25b87"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":46,"sourceQuestionPage":138,"sourceQuestionPages":[138],"sourceExplanationPages":[139,140],"sourceAnswerPages":[139,140],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-047","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"An elderly woman had no living relatives or close friends. A much younger man who lived nearby befriended the woman in order to convince her to leave her house and other property to him. Shortly thereafter, a new family moved next door to the woman. The woman developed a close friendship with the family's 10-year-old child. When the woman later refused to execute a will in the man's favor, he blamed the child. He decided to cause the woman severe emotional distress by molesting the child in the woman's home while she was away. The man did so and told the woman about it. She suffered severe emotional distress that caused her to suffer a heart attack. The woman sued the man for intentional infliction of emotional distress. Will the woman be likely to prevail in that action?","choices":{"A":"No, because the woman was not present when the man molested the child.","B":"No, because the woman was not related to the child.","C":"Yes, because the man, in molesting the child, acted with the purpose of causing the woman severe emotional distress.","D":"Yes, because the woman suffered bodily injury."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) requires proof that the defendant was at least reckless as to the risk that his/her extreme and outrageous conduct would cause the plaintiff severe emotional distress. To establish this element in cases where the plaintiff's distress stems from conduct that physically or emotionally harmed a third party, the plaintiff must generally prove the following facts: The plaintiff contemporaneously perceived (ie, saw or overheard) the defendant's conduct. The plaintiff was a close relative (ie, immediate family member) of the harmed third party. The defendant knew that the plaintiff was present and closely related to the harmed third party. However, the plaintiff need not prove the above facts if the defendant's design or purpose was to cause severe emotional distress to the plaintiff. Here, the man molested the child (a third party) in the woman's home, which caused the woman to suffer severe emotional distress. The woman was neither present when the man molested the child (no contemporaneous perception) nor related to the child, which ordinarily would defeat her claim for IIED. But since the man acted with the purpose of causing the woman severe emotional distress, she will likely prevail (Choices A & B). (Choice D) Severe emotional distress is often accompanied by bodily injury (eg, heart attack). Although this helps prove that the emotional distress is genuine, recovery for IIED is not limited to cases in which bodily injury occurs.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant whose extreme and outrageous conduct has harmed a third party may be liable for intentional infliction of emotional distress if the defendant acted with the purpose of inflicting (and did inflict) severe emotional distress on the plaintiff—regardless of whether the plaintiff witnessed the conduct or was closely related to the third party.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46, cmt. m (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (emotional harm caused by harm to a third person).","educationalObjective":"A defendant whose extreme and outrageous conduct has harmed a third party may be liable for intentional infliction of emotional distress if the defendant acted with the purpose of inflicting (and did inflict) severe emotional distress on the plaintiff—regardless of whether the plaintiff witnessed the conduct or was closely related to the third party.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46, cmt. m (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (emotional harm caused by harm to a third person).","rule":"A defendant whose extreme and outrageous conduct has harmed a third party may be liable for intentional infliction of emotional distress if the defendant acted with the purpose of inflicting (and did inflict) severe emotional distress on the plaintiff—regardless of whether the plaintiff witnessed the conduct or was closely related to the third party.","irac":{"issue":"Will the woman be likely to prevail in that action?","rule":"A defendant whose extreme and outrageous conduct has harmed a third party may be liable for intentional infliction of emotional distress if the defendant acted with the purpose of inflicting (and did inflict) severe emotional distress on the plaintiff—regardless of whether the plaintiff witnessed the conduct or was closely related to the third party.","analysis":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) requires proof that the defendant was at least reckless as to the risk that his/her extreme and outrageous conduct would cause the plaintiff severe emotional distress. To establish this element in cases where the plaintiff's distress stems from conduct that physically or emotionally harmed a third party, the plaintiff must generally prove the following facts: The plaintiff contemporaneously perceived (ie, saw or overheard) the defendant's conduct. The plaintiff was a close relative (ie, immediate family member) of the harmed third party. The defendant knew that the plaintiff was present and closely related to the harmed third party. However, the plaintiff need not prove the above facts if the defendant's design or purpose was to cause severe emotional distress to the plaintiff. Here, the man molested the child (a third party) in the woman's home, which caused the woman to suffer severe emotional distress. The woman was neither present when the man molested the child (no contemporaneous perception) nor related to the child, which ordinarily would defeat her claim for IIED. But since the man acted with the purpose of causing the woman severe emotional distress, she will likely prevail (Choices A & B). (Choice D) Severe emotional distress is often accompanied by bodily injury (eg, heart attack). Although this helps prove that the emotional distress is genuine, recovery for IIED is not limited to cases in which bodily injury occurs.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the man, in molesting the child, acted with the purpose of causing the woman severe emotional distress."},"questionZh":"一位老妇人没有在世的亲属或密友。\n\n一名住在附近的年轻男子与她结交，企图说服她将房产及其他财产留给自己。\n\n不久后，一户新家庭搬到了老妇人隔壁，她与这户人家10岁的孩子建立了深厚的友谊。\n\n后来，当老妇人拒绝立遗嘱将财产留给该男子时，他将责任归咎于那个孩子。\n\n他决定通过在老妇人外出时猥亵该孩子，来给她造成严重的情绪困扰。\n\n该男子实施了这一行为，并将此事告诉了老妇人。\n\n老妇人因此遭受了严重的情绪困扰，并引发了心脏病发作。\n\n老妇人以“故意造成情绪困扰”为由起诉了该男子。\n\n她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为该男子猥亵孩子时，老妇人并不在现场。","B":"不大，因为老妇人与该孩子没有亲属关系。","C":"大，因为该男子猥亵孩子的行为，其目的是为了给老妇人造成严重的情绪困扰。","D":"大，因为老妇人遭受了身体伤害。"},"explanationZh":"“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）这一侵权行为的成立，要求证明被告对于其极端且令人愤慨的行为可能导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦这一风险，至少持有一种鲁莽（reckless）的心态。\n\n在原告的精神痛苦源于针对第三方的身体或精神伤害行为的情况下，若要确立这一构成要件，原告通常必须证明以下事实：原告在事发当时感知到了（即亲眼目睹或亲耳听到）被告的行为；原告是被伤害第三方近亲属（即直系亲属）；被告明知原告在场且与被伤害第三方关系密切。\n\n然而，如果被告的主观意图或目的就是为了给原告造成严重精神痛苦，则原告无需证明上述事实。\n\n在本案中，该男子在女子的家中猥亵了儿童（第三方），导致该女子遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n尽管该女子在男子猥亵儿童时并不在场（即缺乏“同时感知”），且与该儿童没有亲属关系——这些情况通常会导致其 IIED 索赔失败——但鉴于该男子的行为目的是为了给该女子造成严重精神痛苦，她很可能会胜诉（选项 A 和 B）。\n\n（选项 D）严重的精神痛苦往往伴随着身体伤害（例如心脏病发作）。\n\n虽然这有助于证明精神痛苦是真实的，但 IIED 的赔偿请求权并不局限于伴有身体伤害的情形。\n\n教育目标：如果被告实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为并伤害了第三方，且其主观目的是为了给原告造成（并确实造成了）严重精神痛苦，则被告可能需承担“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”的侵权责任——无论原告是否目睹了该行为，也无论原告是否与该第三方关系密切。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条，评注 m (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（因伤害第三方而造成的精神伤害）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果被告实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为并伤害了第三方，且其行为目的是为了给原告造成（并确实造成了）严重的情绪困扰，则被告可能需承担“故意造成情绪困扰”的法律责任——无论原告是否目睹了该行为，也无论原告与该第三方是否有亲属关系。","iracZh":{"issue":"她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"如果被告实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为并伤害了第三方，且其行为目的是为了给原告造成（并确实造成了）严重的情绪困扰，则被告可能需承担“故意造成情绪困扰”的法律责任——无论原告是否目睹了该行为，也无论原告与该第三方是否有亲属关系。","analysis":"“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）这一侵权行为的成立，要求证明被告对于其极端且令人愤慨的行为可能导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦这一风险，至少持有一种鲁莽（reckless）的心态。\n\n在原告的精神痛苦源于针对第三方的身体或精神伤害行为的情况下，若要确立这一构成要件，原告通常必须证明以下事实：原告在事发当时感知到了（即亲眼目睹或亲耳听到）被告的行为；原告是被伤害第三方近亲属（即直系亲属）；被告明知原告在场且与被伤害第三方关系密切。\n\n然而，如果被告的主观意图或目的就是为了给原告造成严重精神痛苦，则原告无需证明上述事实。\n\n在本案中，该男子在女子的家中猥亵了儿童（第三方），导致该女子遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n尽管该女子在男子猥亵儿童时并不在场（即缺乏“同时感知”），且与该儿童没有亲属关系——这些情况通常会导致其 IIED 索赔失败——但鉴于该男子的行为目的是为了给该女子造成严重精神痛苦，她很可能会胜诉（选项 A 和 B）。\n\n（选项 D）严重的精神痛苦往往伴随着身体伤害（例如心脏病发作）。\n\n虽然这有助于证明精神痛苦是真实的，但 IIED 的赔偿请求权并不局限于伴有身体伤害的情形。\n\n教育目标：如果被告实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为并伤害了第三方，且其主观目的是为了给原告造成（并确实造成了）严重精神痛苦，则被告可能需承担“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”的侵权责任——无论原告是否目睹了该行为，也无论原告是否与该第三方关系密切。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条，评注 m (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（因伤害第三方而造成的精神伤害）。","conclusion":"答案 C：大，因为该男子猥亵孩子的行为，其目的是为了给老妇人造成严重的情绪困扰。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"design","ipa":"/dɪzˈaɪn/","syllables":"de·sign","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 设计, 图样, 方案, 企图；v. 设计, 计划","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“design”","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:designed/s:designs/p:designed/i:designing/3:designs","sourceSentence":"However, the plaintiff need not prove the above facts if the defendant's design or purpose was to cause severe emotional distress to the plaintiff.","synonyms":["designing","pattern","figure","invention"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-453ef46f104c42a8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":47,"sourceQuestionPage":141,"sourceQuestionPages":[141],"sourceExplanationPages":[142,143],"sourceAnswerPages":[142,143],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-048","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A businessman operates a collection agency. He was trying to collect a $400 bill for medical services rendered to a young woman by a doctor. The businessman went to the young woman's house. When the young woman's mother answered the door, the businessman told her that he was there to collect a bill owed by the young woman. The mother told the businessman that because of the young woman's illness, she had been unemployed for six months, that she was still ill and unable to work, and that she would pay the bill as soon as she could. The businessman, in a loud voice, demanded to see the young woman and said that if he did not receive payment immediately, he would file a criminal complaint charging her with fraud. The young woman, who was severely distressed after hearing the conversation, came to the door. The businessman, in a loud voice, repeated his demand for immediate payment and his threat to use criminal process. If the young woman asserts a claim against the businessman based on infliction of emotional distress, is she likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the businessman's conduct created no risk of physical harm to the young woman.","B":"No, unless she suffered physical harm as a result of the businessman's conduct.","C":"Yes, because she suffered severe emotional distress as a result of the businessman's conduct.","D":"Yes, unless the bill for medical services was valid and past due."},"answer":"C","explanation":"For intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED), the plaintiff must prove that the defendant: behaved in an extreme and outrageous manner\n\nintended to cause the plaintiff severe emotional distress OR recklessly disregarded the risk of causing such distress and\n\ncaused the plaintiff severe emotional distress.\n\nAbusing a position of authority amounts to extreme and outrageous conduct when the conduct goes far beyond what is necessary to exercise that authority. For example, a creditor abuses his/her position of authority by threatening a debtor with unjustified criminal prosecution or by contacting the debtor's employer or neighbors. Here, the businessman (creditor) abused his position of authority when he unjustifiably threatened to file a criminal fraud complaint against the young woman for merely failing to pay a bill (extreme and outrageous conduct). In doing so, he was indifferent to the obvious risk that the threat would cause her severe emotional distress (recklessness). And since the young woman suffered such distress after hearing the businessman's threat, she will likely prevail on an IIED claim. (Choice A) Risk of physical harm is an element of the zone-of-danger theory for negligent infliction of emotional distress—not IIED. Therefore, the young woman can prove IIED even if the businessman's conduct created no risk of physical harm. (Choice B) IIED compensates persons who suffered severe emotional distress even when there is no proof of physical harm. As a result, the young woman's lack of physical harm will not affect the viability of her IIED claim. (Choice D) Even if the bill was valid and past due, the businessman could only use reasonable means to collect that debt. But since the businessman engaged in extreme and outrageous conduct that recklessly caused the young woman severe emotional distress, he is liable for IIED.\n\nEducational objective: Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) requires proof that the defendant engaged in extreme and outrageous conduct—eg, abusing a position of authority by going far beyond what is necessary to exercise that authority.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 cmts. d–e (2012) (intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress).","educationalObjective":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) requires proof that the defendant engaged in extreme and outrageous conduct—eg, abusing a position of authority by going far beyond what is necessary to exercise that authority.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 cmts. d–e (2012) (intentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress).","rule":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) requires proof that the defendant engaged in extreme and outrageous conduct—eg, abusing a position of authority by going far beyond what is necessary to exercise that authority.","irac":{"issue":"If the young woman asserts a claim against the businessman based on infliction of emotional distress, is she likely to prevail?","rule":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) requires proof that the defendant engaged in extreme and outrageous conduct—eg, abusing a position of authority by going far beyond what is necessary to exercise that authority.","analysis":"For intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED), the plaintiff must prove that the defendant: behaved in an extreme and outrageous manner\n\nintended to cause the plaintiff severe emotional distress OR recklessly disregarded the risk of causing such distress and\n\ncaused the plaintiff severe emotional distress.\n\nAbusing a position of authority amounts to extreme and outrageous conduct when the conduct goes far beyond what is necessary to exercise that authority. For example, a creditor abuses his/her position of authority by threatening a debtor with unjustified criminal prosecution or by contacting the debtor's employer or neighbors. Here, the businessman (creditor) abused his position of authority when he unjustifiably threatened to file a criminal fraud complaint against the young woman for merely failing to pay a bill (extreme and outrageous conduct). In doing so, he was indifferent to the obvious risk that the threat would cause her severe emotional distress (recklessness). And since the young woman suffered such distress after hearing the businessman's threat, she will likely prevail on an IIED claim. (Choice A) Risk of physical harm is an element of the zone-of-danger theory for negligent infliction of emotional distress—not IIED. Therefore, the young woman can prove IIED even if the businessman's conduct created no risk of physical harm. (Choice B) IIED compensates persons who suffered severe emotional distress even when there is no proof of physical harm. As a result, the young woman's lack of physical harm will not affect the viability of her IIED claim. (Choice D) Even if the bill was valid and past due, the businessman could only use reasonable means to collect that debt. But since the businessman engaged in extreme and outrageous conduct that recklessly caused the young woman severe emotional distress, he is liable for IIED.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because she suffered severe emotional distress as a result of the businessman's conduct."},"questionZh":"一位商人经营着一家催收机构。\n\n他试图追讨一笔400美元的医疗服务费，这笔费用源于一位医生为一名年轻女子提供的诊疗服务。\n\n商人来到了该女子的住所。\n\n当女子的母亲开门时，商人告知她是来追讨该女子所欠账单的。\n\n母亲向商人解释说，由于患病，该女子已失业六个月，目前仍处于病中无法工作，并表示会尽快支付这笔费用。\n\n商人高声要求见该女子，并声称如果不能立即收到款项，他将提起刑事控告，指控她犯有欺诈罪。\n\n该女子听到了这番对话，在极度不安的情绪下来到了门口。\n\n商人再次高声重申了立即付款的要求，并威胁要动用刑事法律手段。\n\n如果该女子基于“造成精神痛苦”（infliction of emotional distress）这一理由向商人提出索赔，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会胜诉，因为商人的行为并未给该女子带来身体伤害的风险。","B":"不会胜诉，除非她因商人的行为而遭受了身体伤害。","C":"会胜诉，因为她因商人的行为遭受了严重精神痛苦。","D":"会胜诉，除非该医疗服务账单是合法且已逾期的。"},"explanationZh":"对于“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）这一侵权行为，原告必须证明被告：实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为； 意图导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦，或者罔顾导致此类痛苦的风险；以及 确实导致了原告遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n当行为远远超出了行使职权或地位所需的必要限度时，滥用职权或地位即构成极端且令人愤慨的行为。\n\n例如，债权人通过威胁债务人进行无正当理由的刑事起诉，或联系债务人的雇主或邻居，即属于滥用职权或地位。\n\n在本案中，该商人（债权人）滥用了其地位，仅仅因为年轻女子未支付账单，便无正当理由地威胁要以欺诈罪对其提出刑事控告（这属于极端且令人愤慨的行为）。\n\n在实施该行为时，他对这种威胁会给该女子造成严重精神痛苦的明显风险漠不关心（即具有罔顾后果的鲁莽心态）。\n\n鉴于该女子在听到商人的威胁后确实遭受了此类精神痛苦，她很可能在 IIED 诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）遭受身体伤害的风险是过失造成精神痛苦（Negligent Infliction of Emotional Distress）中“危险区域”理论（zone-of-danger theory）的一个构成要素，而非 IIED 的要素。\n\n因此，即使商人的行为未造成身体伤害风险，该女子仍可证明 IIED 成立。\n\n（选项 B）即使没有身体伤害的证据，IIED 也能为遭受严重精神痛苦的人提供救济。\n\n因此，该女子未受身体伤害这一事实不会影响其 IIED 索赔的可行性。\n\n（选项 D）即使账单合法且已逾期，商人也只能采取合理的手段追讨债务。\n\n但由于该商人实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为，并鲁莽地导致该女子遭受严重精神痛苦，他须对 IIED 承担法律责任。\n\n教育目标：故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）（IIED）要求证明被告实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为——例如，滥用职权或地位，且行为远远超出了行使该职权或地位所需的必要限度。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条，评注 d–e (2012)（故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）的侵权主张要求证明被告实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为——例如，滥用职权，其行为远远超出了行使该职权所需的必要限度。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该女子基于“造成精神痛苦”这一理由向商人提出索赔，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）的侵权主张要求证明被告实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为——例如，滥用职权，其行为远远超出了行使该职权所需的必要限度。","analysis":"对于“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）这一侵权行为，原告必须证明被告：实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为； 意图导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦，或者罔顾导致此类痛苦的风险；以及 确实导致了原告遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n当行为远远超出了行使职权或地位所需的必要限度时，滥用职权或地位即构成极端且令人愤慨的行为。\n\n例如，债权人通过威胁债务人进行无正当理由的刑事起诉，或联系债务人的雇主或邻居，即属于滥用职权或地位。\n\n在本案中，该商人（债权人）滥用了其地位，仅仅因为年轻女子未支付账单，便无正当理由地威胁要以欺诈罪对其提出刑事控告（这属于极端且令人愤慨的行为）。\n\n在实施该行为时，他对这种威胁会给该女子造成严重精神痛苦的明显风险漠不关心（即具有罔顾后果的鲁莽心态）。\n\n鉴于该女子在听到商人的威胁后确实遭受了此类精神痛苦，她很可能在 IIED 诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）遭受身体伤害的风险是过失造成精神痛苦（Negligent Infliction of Emotional Distress）中“危险区域”理论（zone-of-danger theory）的一个构成要素，而非 IIED 的要素。\n\n因此，即使商人的行为未造成身体伤害风险，该女子仍可证明 IIED 成立。\n\n（选项 B）即使没有身体伤害的证据，IIED 也能为遭受严重精神痛苦的人提供救济。\n\n因此，该女子未受身体伤害这一事实不会影响其 IIED 索赔的可行性。\n\n（选项 D）即使账单合法且已逾期，商人也只能采取合理的手段追讨债务。\n\n但由于该商人实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为，并鲁莽地导致该女子遭受严重精神痛苦，他须对 IIED 承担法律责任。\n\n教育目标：故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）（IIED）要求证明被告实施了极端且令人愤慨的行为——例如，滥用职权或地位，且行为远远超出了行使该职权或地位所需的必要限度。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条，评注 d–e (2012)（故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会胜诉，因为她因商人的行为遭受了严重精神痛苦。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"employer","ipa":"/ɛmplˈɔɪɝ/","syllables":"em·ployer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 雇主, 老板；[经] 雇主, 业主","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“employer”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:employers","sourceSentence":"For example, a creditor abuses his/her position of authority by threatening a debtor with unjustified criminal prosecution or by contacting the debtor's employer or neighbors.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-941b1d1b41649848"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":48,"sourceQuestionPage":144,"sourceQuestionPages":[144],"sourceExplanationPages":[145,146],"sourceAnswerPages":[145,146],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-049","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A bank employee took substantial kickbacks to approve certain loans that later proved worthless. Upon learning of the kickbacks, the bank president fired the employee, telling him, \"If you are not out of this bank in ten minutes, I will have the guards throw you out bodily.\" The employee left at once. If the employee asserts an assault claim against the bank president, will the employee prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the bank president gave the employee ten minutes to leave.","B":"No, because the guards never touched the employee.","C":"Yes, because the bank president threatened the employee with harmful or offensive bodily contact.","D":"Yes, if the bank president intended to cause the employee severe emotional distress."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Assault requires that a defendant cause the plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent harmful or offensive contact. Imminent means without significant delay. Therefore, a defendant is not liable for assault if he/she only causes apprehension of future contact. Here, the employee was not placed in apprehension of imminent contact because the bank president threatened future harm—that he would have the guards throw the employee out bodily if he did not leave in ten minutes. Because the threat of future harm does not satisfy the imminent contact requirement, the bank president is not liable for assault. (Choice B) Assault only requires apprehension of imminent contact, so the employee does not need to prove that the guards actually touched him. In contrast, battery (an intentional act that causes harmful or offensive contact with another) requires proof of actual contact. (Choice C) Although the bank president threatened the employee with bodily contact, assault requires apprehension of imminent contact. Since the bank president threatened future contact (after ten minutes), he is not liable for assault. (Choice D) Severe emotional distress is not an element of assault. Rather, it is an element of intentional infliction of emotional distress (extreme or outrageous conduct that intentionally or recklessly causes a plaintiff severe emotional distress).\n\nEducational objective: Assault requires apprehension of imminent contact. Therefore, a threat of future contact does not support an assault claim.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of assault).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 29 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (imminence requirement).","educationalObjective":"Assault requires apprehension of imminent contact. Therefore, a threat of future contact does not support an assault claim.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of assault).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 29 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (imminence requirement).","rule":"Assault requires apprehension of imminent contact. Therefore, a threat of future contact does not support an assault claim.","irac":{"issue":"If the employee asserts an assault claim against the bank president, will the employee prevail?","rule":"Assault requires apprehension of imminent contact. Therefore, a threat of future contact does not support an assault claim.","analysis":"Assault requires that a defendant cause the plaintiff reasonable apprehension of imminent harmful or offensive contact. Imminent means without significant delay. Therefore, a defendant is not liable for assault if he/she only causes apprehension of future contact. Here, the employee was not placed in apprehension of imminent contact because the bank president threatened future harm—that he would have the guards throw the employee out bodily if he did not leave in ten minutes. Because the threat of future harm does not satisfy the imminent contact requirement, the bank president is not liable for assault. (Choice B) Assault only requires apprehension of imminent contact, so the employee does not need to prove that the guards actually touched him. In contrast, battery (an intentional act that causes harmful or offensive contact with another) requires proof of actual contact. (Choice C) Although the bank president threatened the employee with bodily contact, assault requires apprehension of imminent contact. Since the bank president threatened future contact (after ten minutes), he is not liable for assault. (Choice D) Severe emotional distress is not an element of assault. Rather, it is an element of intentional infliction of emotional distress (extreme or outrageous conduct that intentionally or recklessly causes a plaintiff severe emotional distress).","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the bank president gave the employee ten minutes to leave."},"questionZh":"一名银行员工收受巨额回扣，批准了一些后来被证明毫无价值的贷款。\n\n银行行长得知此事后，解雇了该员工，并威胁道：“如果你十分钟之内不离开这家银行，我就让保安把你扔出去。”该员工立即离开了。\n\n如果该员工对银行行长提起人身使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）诉讼，他能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为银行行长给了该员工十分钟时间离开。","B":"不能，因为保安没有碰该员工。","C":"可以，因为银行行长威胁要对该员工进行有害或冒犯性的身体接触。","D":"可以，如果银行行长意图对该员工造成严重的精神痛苦。"},"explanationZh":"“袭击”（assault）这一侵权行为要求被告使原告产生对“即刻”（imminent）发生的有害或冒犯性接触的合理恐惧。\n\n“即刻”意味着没有显著的时间延迟。\n\n因此，如果被告仅引起了对未来接触的恐惧，则不构成袭击。\n\n在本案中，该员工并未产生对即刻接触的恐惧，因为银行行长威胁的是未来的伤害——即如果员工不在十分钟内离开，行长将指使保安将其强行驱逐。\n\n由于对未来伤害的威胁不符合“即刻接触”的要求，银行行长不构成袭击。\n\n（选项 B）袭击仅要求产生对即刻接触的恐惧，因此员工无需证明保安实际上触碰了他。\n\n相比之下，“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery，即导致与他人发生有害或冒犯性接触的故意行为）则要求证明存在实际接触。\n\n（选项 C）尽管银行行长威胁要与员工发生身体接触，但袭击要求产生对即刻接触的恐惧。\n\n由于行长威胁的是未来的接触（十分钟后），因此他不构成袭击。\n\n（选项 D）严重的精神痛苦并非袭击的构成要件。\n\n相反，它是“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（intentional infliction of emotional distress，即故意或鲁莽地实施极端或令人愤慨的行为，导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦）这一侵权行为的构成要件。\n\n教育目标：袭击要求产生对即刻接触的恐惧。\n\n因此，对未来接触的威胁不能支持袭击的诉讼主张。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 21 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（袭击的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 29 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于“即刻性”的要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"人身使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）需要存在即将遭受身体接触的恐惧。\n\n因此，对未来身体接触的威胁不足以构成人身使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果雇员对银行总裁提出人身使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）指控，雇员会胜诉吗？","rule":"人身使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）需要存在即将遭受身体接触的恐惧。\n\n因此，对未来身体接触的威胁不足以构成人身使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）。","analysis":"“袭击”（assault）这一侵权行为要求被告使原告产生对“即刻”（imminent）发生的有害或冒犯性接触的合理恐惧。\n\n“即刻”意味着没有显著的时间延迟。\n\n因此，如果被告仅引起了对未来接触的恐惧，则不构成袭击。\n\n在本案中，该员工并未产生对即刻接触的恐惧，因为银行行长威胁的是未来的伤害——即如果员工不在十分钟内离开，行长将指使保安将其强行驱逐。\n\n由于对未来伤害的威胁不符合“即刻接触”的要求，银行行长不构成袭击。\n\n（选项 B）袭击仅要求产生对即刻接触的恐惧，因此员工无需证明保安实际上触碰了他。\n\n相比之下，“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery，即导致与他人发生有害或冒犯性接触的故意行为）则要求证明存在实际接触。\n\n（选项 C）尽管银行行长威胁要与员工发生身体接触，但袭击要求产生对即刻接触的恐惧。\n\n由于行长威胁的是未来的接触（十分钟后），因此他不构成袭击。\n\n（选项 D）严重的精神痛苦并非袭击的构成要件。\n\n相反，它是“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（intentional infliction of emotional distress，即故意或鲁莽地实施极端或令人愤慨的行为，导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦）这一侵权行为的构成要件。\n\n教育目标：袭击要求产生对即刻接触的恐惧。\n\n因此，对未来接触的威胁不能支持袭击的诉讼主张。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 21 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（袭击的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 29 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于“即刻性”的要求）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不能，因为银行行长给了该员工十分钟时间离开。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-f72c99d82b7d6655"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":49,"sourceQuestionPage":147,"sourceQuestionPages":[147],"sourceExplanationPages":[148,149,150],"sourceAnswerPages":[148,149,150],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-050","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A woman was crossing the street at a crosswalk. A man on the nearby sidewalk thought he saw a speeding automobile headed in the woman's direction even though the car was clearly about to turn onto a different street before it would reach her. As a result, the man ran into the street and pushed the woman out of the path of the car. The woman fell to the ground and broke her leg. In an action for battery brought by the woman against the man, will the woman likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man believed that the woman was actually in danger.","B":"No, because the man's intent was to save the woman, not to harm her.","C":"Yes, because the man could have shouted a warning instead of pushing the woman out of the way.","D":"Yes, because the man should have realized that the woman was not actually in danger."},"answer":"D","explanation":"If a defendant's intentional act causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff, then the defendant is liable for battery absent some defense (eg, actual, apparent, or implied consent). Consent is implied by law when: the plaintiff was in an emergency situation the defendant's contact was actually or apparently necessary to protect the plaintiff from the emergency the plaintiff could not consent to the defendant's action and the defendant reasonably believed that the plaintiff would have consented if given the opportunity. Here, the man intentionally pushed the woman, causing her to fall and break her leg (battery) because he believed that he needed to push the woman to prevent her from being hit by the car. However, he should have realized that there was no need to protect her because the car was clearly about to turn onto a different street before it would reach her (Choice A). Since there was no emergency, consent cannot be implied by law and—because the man has no other defense to the woman's battery claim—she will likely prevail. (Choice B) Battery requires intent to cause contact—not intent to cause harm. As a result, though the man never intended to harm the woman, he is still liable for battery because he intended to push her. (Choice C) The man's ability to shout a warning instead of pushing the woman would be relevant to whether the man's contact was actually or apparently necessary to protect her. However, this analysis is unnecessary because there was no emergency.\n\nEducational objective: Implied consent exists when (1) the plaintiff is in an emergency situation, (2) the defendant's contact is actually or apparently necessary to protect the plaintiff from the emergency, (3) the plaintiff is unable to consent, and (4) the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff would consent if given the opportunity.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 892D (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (consent implied by law).","educationalObjective":"Implied consent exists when (1) the plaintiff is in an emergency situation, (2) the defendant's contact is actually or apparently necessary to protect the plaintiff from the emergency, (3) the plaintiff is unable to consent, and (4) the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff would consent if given the opportunity.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 892D (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (consent implied by law).","rule":"Implied consent exists when (1) the plaintiff is in an emergency situation, (2) the defendant's contact is actually or apparently necessary to protect the plaintiff from the emergency, (3) the plaintiff is unable to consent, and (4) the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff would consent if given the opportunity.","irac":{"issue":"In an action for battery brought by the woman against the man, will the woman likely prevail?","rule":"Implied consent exists when (1) the plaintiff is in an emergency situation, (2) the defendant's contact is actually or apparently necessary to protect the plaintiff from the emergency, (3) the plaintiff is unable to consent, and (4) the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff would consent if given the opportunity.","analysis":"If a defendant's intentional act causes harmful or offensive contact with the plaintiff, then the defendant is liable for battery absent some defense (eg, actual, apparent, or implied consent). Consent is implied by law when: the plaintiff was in an emergency situation the defendant's contact was actually or apparently necessary to protect the plaintiff from the emergency the plaintiff could not consent to the defendant's action and the defendant reasonably believed that the plaintiff would have consented if given the opportunity. Here, the man intentionally pushed the woman, causing her to fall and break her leg (battery) because he believed that he needed to push the woman to prevent her from being hit by the car. However, he should have realized that there was no need to protect her because the car was clearly about to turn onto a different street before it would reach her (Choice A). Since there was no emergency, consent cannot be implied by law and—because the man has no other defense to the woman's battery claim—she will likely prevail. (Choice B) Battery requires intent to cause contact—not intent to cause harm. As a result, though the man never intended to harm the woman, he is still liable for battery because he intended to push her. (Choice C) The man's ability to shout a warning instead of pushing the woman would be relevant to whether the man's contact was actually or apparently necessary to protect her. However, this analysis is unnecessary because there was no emergency.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the man should have realized that the woman was not actually in danger."},"questionZh":"一名女子正在人行横道过马路。\n\n附近人行道上的一名男子以为他看到一辆疾驰的汽车正朝女子驶来，尽管那辆车显然会在到达女子之前拐入另一条街道。\n\n结果，该男子冲到马路上，将女子推开，使她免于被车撞到。\n\n女子摔倒在地，腿部骨折。\n\n如果该女子起诉该男子犯有故意侵害身体（battery）罪，她胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为该男子当时认为女子确实处于危险之中。","B":"否，因为该男子的本意是救女子，而不是伤害她。","C":"是，因为该男子本可以大声警告，而不是将女子推开。","D":"是，因为该男子应该意识到女子实际上并没有危险。"},"explanationZh":"如果被告的故意行为导致了与原告的有害或冒犯性接触，且不存在抗辩事由（如实际同意、表见同意或默示同意），则被告须对故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）行为承担责任。\n\n法律上的默示同意成立的条件包括：原告处于紧急情况中；被告的接触对于保护原告免受该紧急情况的危害是实际必要或表面上必要的；原告无法对被告的行为表示同意；以及被告有合理理由相信，若有机会，原告会表示同意。\n\n在本案中，该男子故意推了女子，导致其摔倒并骨折（构成故意侵害身体（battery）），因为他认为必须推开该女子以防止她被车撞到。\n\n然而，他本应意识到无需采取保护措施，因为那辆车在驶近该女子之前，显然即将转向另一条街道（选项 A）。\n\n由于不存在紧急情况，法律无法推定存在默示同意；又因该男子针对女子的故意侵害身体（battery）指控没有其他抗辩理由，该女子很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）故意侵害身体（battery）行为的构成要件是具有导致接触的意图，而非导致伤害的意图。\n\n因此，尽管该男子并无伤害该女子的意图，但他仍须对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任，因为他确实有推她的意图。\n\n（选项 C）该男子本可以通过大声呼喊发出警告而非推开女子，这一事实与判断其接触行为对于保护女子是否实际必要或表面上必要有关。\n\n然而，由于不存在紧急情况，无需进行此项分析。\n\n教育目标：默示同意成立的条件包括：(1) 原告处于紧急情况中；(2) 被告的接触对于保护原告免受该紧急情况的危害是实际必要或表面上必要的；(3) 原告无法表示同意；以及 (4) 被告有合理理由相信，若有机会，原告会表示同意。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 892D 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（法律上的默示同意）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"默示同意存在于以下情况：(1) 原告处于紧急情况；(2) 被告的接触对于保护原告免受紧急情况侵害而言是实际或明显必要的；(3) 原告无法表示同意；以及 (4) 被告有理由相信，如果给予原告机会，原告会表示同意。","iracZh":{"issue":"在女性对男性提起的故意侵害身体（battery）诉讼中，女性胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"默示同意存在于以下情况：(1) 原告处于紧急情况；(2) 被告的接触对于保护原告免受紧急情况侵害而言是实际或明显必要的；(3) 原告无法表示同意；以及 (4) 被告有理由相信，如果给予原告机会，原告会表示同意。","analysis":"如果被告的故意行为导致了与原告的有害或冒犯性接触，且不存在抗辩事由（如实际同意、表见同意或默示同意），则被告须对故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）行为承担责任。\n\n法律上的默示同意成立的条件包括：原告处于紧急情况中；被告的接触对于保护原告免受该紧急情况的危害是实际必要或表面上必要的；原告无法对被告的行为表示同意；以及被告有合理理由相信，若有机会，原告会表示同意。\n\n在本案中，该男子故意推了女子，导致其摔倒并骨折（构成故意侵害身体（battery）），因为他认为必须推开该女子以防止她被车撞到。\n\n然而，他本应意识到无需采取保护措施，因为那辆车在驶近该女子之前，显然即将转向另一条街道（选项 A）。\n\n由于不存在紧急情况，法律无法推定存在默示同意；又因该男子针对女子的故意侵害身体（battery）指控没有其他抗辩理由，该女子很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）故意侵害身体（battery）行为的构成要件是具有导致接触的意图，而非导致伤害的意图。\n\n因此，尽管该男子并无伤害该女子的意图，但他仍须对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任，因为他确实有推她的意图。\n\n（选项 C）该男子本可以通过大声呼喊发出警告而非推开女子，这一事实与判断其接触行为对于保护女子是否实际必要或表面上必要有关。\n\n然而，由于不存在紧急情况，无需进行此项分析。\n\n教育目标：默示同意成立的条件包括：(1) 原告处于紧急情况中；(2) 被告的接触对于保护原告免受该紧急情况的危害是实际必要或表面上必要的；(3) 原告无法表示同意；以及 (4) 被告有合理理由相信，若有机会，原告会表示同意。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 892D 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（法律上的默示同意）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是，因为该男子应该意识到女子实际上并没有危险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-ffe0c7d257e17828"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":50,"sourceQuestionPage":151,"sourceQuestionPages":[151],"sourceExplanationPages":[152,153],"sourceAnswerPages":[152,153],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-051","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"The manager of a department store noticed that a customer was carrying a scarf with her as she examined various items in the blouse department. The manager recognized the scarf as an expensive one carried by the store. The customer was trying to find a blouse that matched a color in the scarf and, after a while, found one. The manager then saw the customer put the scarf into her purse, pay for the blouse, and head for the door. The manager, who was eight inches taller than the customer, blocked the customer's way to the door and asked to see the scarf in the customer's purse. The customer produced the scarf, as well as a receipt showing that it had been purchased from the store on the previous day. The manager then told the customer there was no problem and stepped out of her way. In an action by the customer against the store for false imprisonment, which of the following, if supported by the evidence, will be the store's best defense?","choices":{"A":"The customer assumed the risk of being detained by carrying the scarf in public view and then putting it into her purse.","B":"The customer should have realized that her conduct would create a reasonable belief that facts existed warranting a privilege to detain her.","C":"The customer was not detained, but was merely questioned about the scarf.","D":"The manager had a reasonable belief that the customer was shoplifting and detained her only briefly for a reasonable investigation of the facts."},"answer":"D","explanation":"False imprisonment occurs when the defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff within fixed boundaries AND the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs or suffers actual harm as a result. The shopkeeper's privilege is a defense to false imprisonment when: the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff was shoplifting and detains the plaintiff for a reasonable amount of time necessary to conduct an investigation. Here, the manager reasonably believed that the customer was shoplifting when the customer put a scarf carried by the store in her purse and headed for the door without paying for the scarf. As a result, the manager stopped the customer to ask her about the scarf (conduct an investigation) and only detained her long enough to ask a question and see her receipt (reasonable amount of time). Therefore, the shopkeeper's privilege is the store's best defense to the customer's false imprisonment claim. (Choice A) Assumption of the risk (the plaintiff's voluntary acceptance of a known risk of harm) is only a defense to claims alleging negligent or reckless conduct. Since false imprisonment requires proof of intentional conduct, this defense is irrelevant. (Choice B) What the customer should have realized is an issue of contributory negligence (conduct that falls below the standard of care a plaintiff should use for his/her own protection). Contributory negligence is only a defense to negligence—not recklessness or intentional torts like false imprisonment. (Choice C) For false imprisonment, confinement occurs when the defendant restricts the plaintiff within fixed boundaries and causes an apparent lack of reasonable escape. The confinement can be for any length of time (eg, 1 minute or 1 day). Therefore, the customer was confined even though the manager only briefly blocked her exit to question her about the scarf.\n\nEducational objective: The shopkeeper's privilege is a defense to false imprisonment when the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff was shoplifting and detains the plaintiff for a reasonable amount of time to investigate the facts.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 35 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (false imprisonment).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 120A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (shopkeeper's privilege).","educationalObjective":"The shopkeeper's privilege is a defense to false imprisonment when the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff was shoplifting and detains the plaintiff for a reasonable amount of time to investigate the facts.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 35 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (false imprisonment).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 120A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (shopkeeper's privilege).","rule":"The shopkeeper's privilege is a defense to false imprisonment when the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff was shoplifting and detains the plaintiff for a reasonable amount of time to investigate the facts.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the customer against the store for false imprisonment, which of the following, if supported by the evidence, will be the store's best defense?","rule":"The shopkeeper's privilege is a defense to false imprisonment when the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff was shoplifting and detains the plaintiff for a reasonable amount of time to investigate the facts.","analysis":"False imprisonment occurs when the defendant intentionally confines the plaintiff within fixed boundaries AND the plaintiff is aware of the confinement at the time it occurs or suffers actual harm as a result. The shopkeeper's privilege is a defense to false imprisonment when: the defendant reasonably believes that the plaintiff was shoplifting and detains the plaintiff for a reasonable amount of time necessary to conduct an investigation. Here, the manager reasonably believed that the customer was shoplifting when the customer put a scarf carried by the store in her purse and headed for the door without paying for the scarf. As a result, the manager stopped the customer to ask her about the scarf (conduct an investigation) and only detained her long enough to ask a question and see her receipt (reasonable amount of time). Therefore, the shopkeeper's privilege is the store's best defense to the customer's false imprisonment claim. (Choice A) Assumption of the risk (the plaintiff's voluntary acceptance of a known risk of harm) is only a defense to claims alleging negligent or reckless conduct. Since false imprisonment requires proof of intentional conduct, this defense is irrelevant. (Choice B) What the customer should have realized is an issue of contributory negligence (conduct that falls below the standard of care a plaintiff should use for his/her own protection). Contributory negligence is only a defense to negligence—not recklessness or intentional torts like false imprisonment. (Choice C) For false imprisonment, confinement occurs when the defendant restricts the plaintiff within fixed boundaries and causes an apparent lack of reasonable escape. The confinement can be for any length of time (eg, 1 minute or 1 day). Therefore, the customer was confined even though the manager only briefly blocked her exit to question her about the scarf.","conclusion":"Answer D: The manager had a reasonable belief that the customer was shoplifting and detained her only briefly for a reasonable investigation of the facts."},"questionZh":"百货商店经理注意到一位顾客在衬衫区挑选商品时，手里拿着一条围巾。\n\n经理认出这条围巾是店里出售的高档围巾。\n\n顾客正在寻找一件与围巾颜色相配的衬衫，过了一会儿，她找到了一件。\n\n经理看到顾客把围巾放进手提包，付了衬衫的钱，然后走向门口。\n\n经理比顾客高出八英寸，便挡住了顾客的去路，要求查看顾客手提包里的围巾。\n\n顾客拿出了围巾，以及一张显示围巾是前一天在店里购买的收据。\n\n经理告诉顾客没问题，然后让开了路。\n\n如果顾客起诉商店非法拘禁，以下哪项（如果证据支持）将是商店的最佳辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"顾客将围巾带到公共场合并放入钱包，承担了被拘留的风险。","B":"顾客应当意识到，她的行为会使店员有理由相信存在足以构成拘留理由的事实。","C":"顾客并未被拘留，只是被询问了有关围巾的问题。","D":"店长有理由相信顾客正在行窃，并仅短暂拘留她以进行合理的调查。"},"explanationZh":"非法拘禁（False imprisonment）是指被告故意将原告限制在特定范围内，且原告在被拘禁时知晓该拘禁情况，或因此遭受了实际损害。\n\n当被告有合理理由相信原告涉嫌入店行窃，并为进行调查而将原告拘留合理时长时，“店主特权”（shopkeeper's privilege）可作为针对非法拘禁指控的抗辩理由。\n\n在本案中，当顾客将店内的一条围巾放入手提包并径直走向门口却未付款时，经理有合理理由相信该顾客涉嫌入店行窃。\n\n因此，经理拦下顾客询问围巾事宜（即进行调查），且拘留时间仅限于询问和查看收据所需的时间（即合理时长）。\n\n综上所述，“店主特权”是该商店针对顾客非法拘禁索赔的最佳抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”（即原告自愿接受已知的伤害风险）仅适用于针对过失或鲁莽行为的索赔抗辩。\n\n由于非法拘禁要求证明存在故意行为，因此该抗辩理由不适用。\n\n（选项 B）关于顾客本应意识到的情况，这属于“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）的问题（即原告未达到保护自身安全所需的注意标准）。\n\n与有过失（contributory negligence）仅适用于过失索赔的抗辩，而不适用于鲁莽行为或非法拘禁等故意侵权行为。\n\n（选项 C）就非法拘禁而言，当被告将原告限制在特定范围内且导致原告显然无法合理逃脱时，即构成拘禁。\n\n拘禁时间长短不限（例如 1 分钟或 1 天）。\n\n因此，尽管经理只是短暂阻拦顾客离开以询问围巾事宜，仍构成了对顾客的拘禁。\n\n教育目标：“店主特权”是指当被告有合理理由相信原告涉嫌入店行窃，并为调查事实而将原告拘留合理时长时，可作为非法拘禁指控的抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 35 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（非法拘禁）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 120A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（店主特权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当被告有理由相信原告正在行窃，并合理地拘留原告以调查事实时，店主特权可以作为非法拘禁的抗辩理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"在顾客起诉商店非法拘禁的诉讼中，如果证据支持，以下哪项将是商店的最佳抗辩理由？","rule":"当被告有理由相信原告正在行窃，并合理地拘留原告以调查事实时，店主特权可以作为非法拘禁的抗辩理由。","analysis":"非法拘禁（False imprisonment）是指被告故意将原告限制在特定范围内，且原告在被拘禁时知晓该拘禁情况，或因此遭受了实际损害。\n\n当被告有合理理由相信原告涉嫌入店行窃，并为进行调查而将原告拘留合理时长时，“店主特权”（shopkeeper's privilege）可作为针对非法拘禁指控的抗辩理由。\n\n在本案中，当顾客将店内的一条围巾放入手提包并径直走向门口却未付款时，经理有合理理由相信该顾客涉嫌入店行窃。\n\n因此，经理拦下顾客询问围巾事宜（即进行调查），且拘留时间仅限于询问和查看收据所需的时间（即合理时长）。\n\n综上所述，“店主特权”是该商店针对顾客非法拘禁索赔的最佳抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”（即原告自愿接受已知的伤害风险）仅适用于针对过失或鲁莽行为的索赔抗辩。\n\n由于非法拘禁要求证明存在故意行为，因此该抗辩理由不适用。\n\n（选项 B）关于顾客本应意识到的情况，这属于“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）的问题（即原告未达到保护自身安全所需的注意标准）。\n\n与有过失（contributory negligence）仅适用于过失索赔的抗辩，而不适用于鲁莽行为或非法拘禁等故意侵权行为。\n\n（选项 C）就非法拘禁而言，当被告将原告限制在特定范围内且导致原告显然无法合理逃脱时，即构成拘禁。\n\n拘禁时间长短不限（例如 1 分钟或 1 天）。\n\n因此，尽管经理只是短暂阻拦顾客离开以询问围巾事宜，仍构成了对顾客的拘禁。\n\n教育目标：“店主特权”是指当被告有合理理由相信原告涉嫌入店行窃，并为调查事实而将原告拘留合理时长时，可作为非法拘禁指控的抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 35 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（非法拘禁）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 120A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（店主特权）。","conclusion":"答案 D：店长有理由相信顾客正在行窃，并仅短暂拘留她以进行合理的调查。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"contributory negligence","ipa":"/kʌntɹˈɪbjʌtˌɔɹi nˈɛɡlədʒəns/","syllables":"con·trib·u·tory neg·li·gence","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"与有过失：传统规则下原告自身过失可能完全阻却赔偿","contextualMeaning":"与有过失：传统规则下原告自身过失可能完全阻却赔偿","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) What the customer should have realized is an issue of contributory negligence (conduct that falls below the standard of care a plaintiff should use for his/her own protection).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"standard of care","ipa":"/stˈændɝd ˈəv kˈɛɹ/","syllables":"stan·dard of care","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"注意标准：判断行为是否违反注意义务的规范","contextualMeaning":"注意标准：判断行为是否违反注意义务的规范","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) What the customer should have realized is an issue of contributory negligence (conduct that falls below the standard of care a plaintiff should use for his/her own protection).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"detain","ipa":"/dɪtˈeɪn/","syllables":"de·tain","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 扣留, 扣押, 耽搁；[法] 拘留, 扣押, 留住","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“detain”","wordForm":"词；词形 d:detained/i:detaining/p:detained/3:detains","sourceSentence":"The customer assumed the risk of being detained by carrying the scarf in public view and then putting it into her purse.","synonyms":["delay","hold up","stay","confine"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-f494d7fb0f1e2b57"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":51,"sourceQuestionPage":154,"sourceQuestionPages":[154],"sourceExplanationPages":[155,156],"sourceAnswerPages":[155,156],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-052","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A mother took her five-year-old child to a hospital emergency room for treatment. A doctor on the hospital staff molested the child while treating her. At the time, no one was in the treatment room except the doctor and the child; the mother had left the room to get a cup of coffee. Two weeks later, when the child told her mother what had occurred in the treatment room, the mother suffered severe emotional distress that caused her to become physically ill. In an action against the doctor by the mother on her own behalf to recover for intentional infliction of emotional distress, is the mother likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the mother was contributorily negligent in leaving the child alone with the doctor during treatment.","B":"No, because the mother was neither the direct victim of the doctor's conduct nor a contemporaneous witness.","C":"Yes, because the doctor's conduct was outrageous.","D":"Yes, because the mother's distress was the natural and foreseeable consequence of the doctor's conduct."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Theories of recovery for\n\nintentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress\n\nDirect Defendant engaged in extreme & outrageous conduct and Defendant intentionally or recklessly caused plaintiff severe emotional distress Bystander Defendant's extreme & outrageous conduct physically harmed plaintiff's close relative Defendant's conduct was intentional or reckless (ie, defendant knew plaintiff was present & closely related to injured person) and Plaintiff contemporaneously perceived defendant's conduct & suffered severe emotional distress There are two theories of recovery for intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED): (1) direct and (2) bystander. The direct theory applies when the plaintiff was the direct victim of the defendant's extreme and outrageous conduct. In contrast, the bystander theory applies when the plaintiff witnessed the defendant harming a close relative. Here, the child—not the mother—was the direct victim of the doctor's abusive conduct (no direct liability to mother). And though the doctor's conduct was extreme and outrageous (Choice C), the mother did not witness the doctor molest her child (no bystander liability). Therefore, the mother's IIED claim will likely fail. (Choice A) Contributory negligence (the failure to use the level of care a reasonable person would have used for his/her own protection) is an affirmative defense to claims for negligence—not intentional or reckless torts like IIED. (Choice D) Although the doctor's conduct caused the mother's distress, she cannot recover under either theory for IIED since she was not the direct victim of, or a witness to, that conduct.\n\nEducational objective: There are two theories of recovery for intentional infliction of emotional distress: (1) the direct theory—applies when the plaintiff was the direct victim of the defendant's conduct and (2) the bystander theory—applies when the plaintiff witnessed the defendant harming a close relative.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 cmt. m (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional infliction of emotional distress).","educationalObjective":"There are two theories of recovery for intentional infliction of emotional distress: (1) the direct theory—applies when the plaintiff was the direct victim of the defendant's conduct and (2) the bystander theory—applies when the plaintiff witnessed the defendant harming a close relative.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 cmt. m (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional infliction of emotional distress).","rule":"There are two theories of recovery for intentional infliction of emotional distress: (1) the direct theory—applies when the plaintiff was the direct victim of the defendant's conduct and (2) the bystander theory—applies when the plaintiff witnessed the defendant harming a close relative.","irac":{"issue":"In an action against the doctor by the mother on her own behalf to recover for intentional infliction of emotional distress, is the mother likely to prevail?","rule":"There are two theories of recovery for intentional infliction of emotional distress: (1) the direct theory—applies when the plaintiff was the direct victim of the defendant's conduct and (2) the bystander theory—applies when the plaintiff witnessed the defendant harming a close relative.","analysis":"Theories of recovery for\n\nintentional or reckless infliction of emotional distress\n\nDirect Defendant engaged in extreme & outrageous conduct and Defendant intentionally or recklessly caused plaintiff severe emotional distress Bystander Defendant's extreme & outrageous conduct physically harmed plaintiff's close relative Defendant's conduct was intentional or reckless (ie, defendant knew plaintiff was present & closely related to injured person) and Plaintiff contemporaneously perceived defendant's conduct & suffered severe emotional distress There are two theories of recovery for intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED): (1) direct and (2) bystander. The direct theory applies when the plaintiff was the direct victim of the defendant's extreme and outrageous conduct. In contrast, the bystander theory applies when the plaintiff witnessed the defendant harming a close relative. Here, the child—not the mother—was the direct victim of the doctor's abusive conduct (no direct liability to mother). And though the doctor's conduct was extreme and outrageous (Choice C), the mother did not witness the doctor molest her child (no bystander liability). Therefore, the mother's IIED claim will likely fail. (Choice A) Contributory negligence (the failure to use the level of care a reasonable person would have used for his/her own protection) is an affirmative defense to claims for negligence—not intentional or reckless torts like IIED. (Choice D) Although the doctor's conduct caused the mother's distress, she cannot recover under either theory for IIED since she was not the direct victim of, or a witness to, that conduct.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the mother was neither the direct victim of the doctor's conduct nor a contemporaneous witness."},"questionZh":"一位母亲带她五岁的孩子去医院急诊室就诊。\n\n医院的一名医生在治疗过程中猥亵了孩子。\n\n当时，诊室里只有医生和孩子，母亲离开诊室去喝咖啡了。\n\n两周后，孩子告诉母亲诊室里发生的事情，母亲因此遭受了严重的精神创伤，导致身体不适。\n\n如果母亲以自己的名义起诉该医生，要求赔偿其故意造成精神损害的损失，母亲胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为母亲在治疗期间将孩子单独留给医生，存在过失。","B":"不可能，因为母亲既不是医生行为的直接受害者，也不是当时的目击证人。","C":"可能，因为医生的行为令人发指。","D":"是的，因为母亲的痛苦是医生行为的自然且可预见的后果。"},"explanationZh":"针对“故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦”的索赔理论 直接受害理论：被告实施了极端且令人发指的行为，并故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。旁观者理论：被告极端且令人发指的行为对原告的近亲属造成了身体伤害；被告的行为是故意或鲁莽的（即被告明知原告在场且与受害者关系密切）；原告同时目睹了被告的行为并遭受了严重精神痛苦。\n\n针对“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）的索赔有两种理论：（1）直接受害理论和（2）旁观者理论。直接受害理论适用于原告是被告极端且令人发指行为的直接受害者的情况。\n\n相比之下，旁观者理论适用于原告目睹被告伤害其近亲属的情况。\n\n在本案中，孩子（而非母亲）是医生虐待行为的直接受害者（因此不存在对母亲的直接责任）。\n\n尽管医生的行为极端且令人发指（选项 C），但母亲并未目睹医生猥亵其孩子（因此不存在旁观者责任）。\n\n因此，母亲关于 IIED 的索赔很可能会失败。\n\n（选项 A）与有过失（contributory negligence）（即未能采取理性人在保护自身安全时应采取的注意程度）是针对过失索赔的抗辩理由，但不适用于像 IIED 这样的故意或鲁莽侵权行为。\n\n（选项 D）尽管医生的行为导致了母亲的精神痛苦，但她无法依据 IIED 的任何一种理论获得赔偿，因为她既不是该行为的直接受害者，也不是目击者。\n\n教育目标：针对“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”的索赔有两种理论：（1）直接受害理论——适用于原告是被告行为的直接受害者的情况；以及（2）旁观者理论——适用于原告目睹被告伤害其近亲属的情况。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条注释 m（美国法律协会，2012 年）（故意造成严重精神损害（IIED））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"针对故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）的赔偿有两种理论：（1）直接理论——适用于原告是被告行为的直接受害者的情况；（2）旁观者理论——适用于原告目睹被告伤害其近亲的情况。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果母亲以自身名义起诉医生，要求赔偿故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）的损失，母亲胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"针对故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）的赔偿有两种理论：（1）直接理论——适用于原告是被告行为的直接受害者的情况；（2）旁观者理论——适用于原告目睹被告伤害其近亲的情况。","analysis":"针对“故意或鲁莽造成精神痛苦”的索赔理论 直接受害理论：被告实施了极端且令人发指的行为，并故意或鲁莽地导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。旁观者理论：被告极端且令人发指的行为对原告的近亲属造成了身体伤害；被告的行为是故意或鲁莽的（即被告明知原告在场且与受害者关系密切）；原告同时目睹了被告的行为并遭受了严重精神痛苦。\n\n针对“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）的索赔有两种理论：（1）直接受害理论和（2）旁观者理论。直接受害理论适用于原告是被告极端且令人发指行为的直接受害者的情况。\n\n相比之下，旁观者理论适用于原告目睹被告伤害其近亲属的情况。\n\n在本案中，孩子（而非母亲）是医生虐待行为的直接受害者（因此不存在对母亲的直接责任）。\n\n尽管医生的行为极端且令人发指（选项 C），但母亲并未目睹医生猥亵其孩子（因此不存在旁观者责任）。\n\n因此，母亲关于 IIED 的索赔很可能会失败。\n\n（选项 A）与有过失（contributory negligence）（即未能采取理性人在保护自身安全时应采取的注意程度）是针对过失索赔的抗辩理由，但不适用于像 IIED 这样的故意或鲁莽侵权行为。\n\n（选项 D）尽管医生的行为导致了母亲的精神痛苦，但她无法依据 IIED 的任何一种理论获得赔偿，因为她既不是该行为的直接受害者，也不是目击者。\n\n教育目标：针对“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”的索赔有两种理论：（1）直接受害理论——适用于原告是被告行为的直接受害者的情况；以及（2）旁观者理论——适用于原告目睹被告伤害其近亲属的情况。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条注释 m（美国法律协会，2012 年）（故意造成严重精神损害（IIED））。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可能，因为母亲既不是医生行为的直接受害者，也不是当时的目击证人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":52,"sourceQuestionPage":157,"sourceQuestionPages":[157],"sourceExplanationPages":[158,159],"sourceAnswerPages":[158,159],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-053","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A judge in State A signed a death warrant for a convicted murderer. At a demonstration protesting the execution of the murderer, two opponents of the death penalty carried large signs that stated, \"The judge is a murderer!\" A television station broadcasted news coverage of the demonstration, including pictures of the signs carried by the two protestors. The broadcast caused the judge severe emotional distress. If the judge asserts a claim for damages for intentional infliction of emotional distress against the television station, will he likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the judge did not suffer physical harm as a consequence of the emotional distress caused by the signs.","B":"No, because the television station did not publish a false statement of fact with actual malice.","C":"Yes, because the assertion on the signs was extreme and outrageous.","D":"Yes, because the broadcast showing the signs caused the judge severe emotional distress."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) occurs when the defendant intentionally or recklessly causes the plaintiff severe emotional harm by engaging in extreme and outrageous conduct. But when the plaintiff is a public figure who alleges that his/her emotional distress was caused by the defendant's publication (eg, newspaper article, television broadcast), then the plaintiff must also show that the defendant published a false statement of fact with actual malice. Here, the judge (public figure) can show that the television station's broadcast caused him severe emotional distress. But since the broadcast merely published the protestors' political opinions—not false statements of fact—the judge's IIED claim will likely fail (Choice D). (Choice A) Proof of physical harm is generally required to recover damages for emotional harm. However, IIED does not require proof of physical harm—only proof of severe emotional distress. Therefore, the fact that the judge did not suffer physical harm due to his emotional distress will not defeat his IIED claim. (Choice C) Even if the protestors' message on the signs was extreme and outrageous, that message expressed an opinion—not a fact.\n\nEducational objective: To recover for intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) based on the defendant's publication, a public figure must show that the defendant (1) acted in an extreme and outrageous manner, (2) intentionally or recklessly caused the public figure severe emotional distress, and (3) published a false statement of fact with actual malice.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 cmt. f (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional infliction of emotional distress).\n\nHustler Magazine v. Falwell, 485 U.S. 46, 56 (1988) (analyzing intentional infliction of emotional distress claims brought by public figures and public officials).","educationalObjective":"To recover for intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) based on the defendant's publication, a public figure must show that the defendant (1) acted in an extreme and outrageous manner, (2) intentionally or recklessly caused the public figure severe emotional distress, and (3) published a false statement of fact with actual malice.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 46 cmt. f (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (intentional infliction of emotional distress).\n\nHustler Magazine v. Falwell, 485 U.S. 46, 56 (1988) (analyzing intentional infliction of emotional distress claims brought by public figures and public officials).","rule":"To recover for intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) based on the defendant's publication, a public figure must show that the defendant (1) acted in an extreme and outrageous manner, (2) intentionally or recklessly caused the public figure severe emotional distress, and (3) published a false statement of fact with actual malice.","irac":{"issue":"If the judge asserts a claim for damages for intentional infliction of emotional distress against the television station, will he likely prevail?","rule":"To recover for intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) based on the defendant's publication, a public figure must show that the defendant (1) acted in an extreme and outrageous manner, (2) intentionally or recklessly caused the public figure severe emotional distress, and (3) published a false statement of fact with actual malice.","analysis":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) occurs when the defendant intentionally or recklessly causes the plaintiff severe emotional harm by engaging in extreme and outrageous conduct. But when the plaintiff is a public figure who alleges that his/her emotional distress was caused by the defendant's publication (eg, newspaper article, television broadcast), then the plaintiff must also show that the defendant published a false statement of fact with actual malice. Here, the judge (public figure) can show that the television station's broadcast caused him severe emotional distress. But since the broadcast merely published the protestors' political opinions—not false statements of fact—the judge's IIED claim will likely fail (Choice D). (Choice A) Proof of physical harm is generally required to recover damages for emotional harm. However, IIED does not require proof of physical harm—only proof of severe emotional distress. Therefore, the fact that the judge did not suffer physical harm due to his emotional distress will not defeat his IIED claim. (Choice C) Even if the protestors' message on the signs was extreme and outrageous, that message expressed an opinion—not a fact.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the television station did not publish a false statement of fact with actual malice."},"questionZh":"A州的一名法官签署了对一名已定罪的谋杀犯的死刑执行令。\n\n在抗议处决该谋杀犯的示威活动中，两名反对死刑的人举着写有“法官是杀人犯！”的大牌子。\n\n一家电视台播出了这场示威活动的报道，其中包括两名抗议者举着的牌子的照片。\n\n该报道给法官造成了严重的精神痛苦。\n\n如果法官就故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）一事向电视台提起损害赔偿诉讼，他胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为法官并未因这些牌子造成的精神痛苦而遭受身体伤害。","B":"否，因为电视台并未出于恶意发布虚假事实陈述。","C":"是，因为牌子上的说法极端且令人愤慨。","D":"是，因为播出这些牌子的画面给法官造成了严重的精神痛苦。"},"explanationZh":"“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）是指被告通过极端且令人愤慨的行为，故意或鲁莽地给原告造成严重精神伤害的情形。\n\n然而，如果原告是公众人物，且声称其精神痛苦是由被告的发布行为（如报纸文章、电视广播）引起的，则原告还必须证明被告在发布时存在“实际恶意”（actual malice），即发布了虚假的事实陈述。\n\n在本案中，法官（作为公众人物）可以证明电视台的广播导致了他严重的精神痛苦。\n\n但由于该广播仅发布了抗议者的政治观点——而非虚假的事实陈述——因此法官的 IIED 诉讼请求很可能会败诉（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 A）通常，要就精神伤害获得损害赔偿，需要证明存在身体伤害。\n\n然而，IIED 并不要求证明身体伤害，仅需证明存在严重的精神痛苦。\n\n因此，法官未因精神痛苦而遭受身体伤害这一事实，并不会导致其 IIED 诉讼请求失败。\n\n（选项 C）即使抗议者标语上的信息极端且令人愤慨，该信息表达的仍是观点，而非事实。\n\n教育目标：若要基于被告的发布行为就“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）获得赔偿，公众人物必须证明被告：(1) 采取了极端且令人愤慨的行为；(2) 故意或鲁莽地导致该公众人物遭受严重精神痛苦；以及 (3) 怀有“实际恶意”发布了虚假的事实陈述。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条注释 f (美国法律协会，2012 年)（关于故意造成严重精神损害（IIED））。\n\n*Hustler Magazine v. Falwell*, 485 U.S. 46, 56 (1988)（分析由公众人物和公职人员提起的故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）之诉讼请求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"公众人物若要就被告的发布行为提起故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）（IIED）的诉讼，必须证明被告：(1) 行为极端且令人发指；(2) 故意或鲁莽地导致公众人物遭受严重的精神痛苦；(3) 出于恶意发布了虚假事实陈述。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法官支持公众人物就故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）向电视台提起损害赔偿诉讼，他胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"公众人物若要就被告的发布行为提起故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）（IIED）的诉讼，必须证明被告：(1) 行为极端且令人发指；(2) 故意或鲁莽地导致公众人物遭受严重的精神痛苦；(3) 出于恶意发布了虚假事实陈述。","analysis":"“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）是指被告通过极端且令人愤慨的行为，故意或鲁莽地给原告造成严重精神伤害的情形。\n\n然而，如果原告是公众人物，且声称其精神痛苦是由被告的发布行为（如报纸文章、电视广播）引起的，则原告还必须证明被告在发布时存在“实际恶意”（actual malice），即发布了虚假的事实陈述。\n\n在本案中，法官（作为公众人物）可以证明电视台的广播导致了他严重的精神痛苦。\n\n但由于该广播仅发布了抗议者的政治观点——而非虚假的事实陈述——因此法官的 IIED 诉讼请求很可能会败诉（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 A）通常，要就精神伤害获得损害赔偿，需要证明存在身体伤害。\n\n然而，IIED 并不要求证明身体伤害，仅需证明存在严重的精神痛苦。\n\n因此，法官未因精神痛苦而遭受身体伤害这一事实，并不会导致其 IIED 诉讼请求失败。\n\n（选项 C）即使抗议者标语上的信息极端且令人愤慨，该信息表达的仍是观点，而非事实。\n\n教育目标：若要基于被告的发布行为就“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（IIED）获得赔偿，公众人物必须证明被告：(1) 采取了极端且令人愤慨的行为；(2) 故意或鲁莽地导致该公众人物遭受严重精神痛苦；以及 (3) 怀有“实际恶意”发布了虚假的事实陈述。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 46 条注释 f (美国法律协会，2012 年)（关于故意造成严重精神损害（IIED））。\n\n*Hustler Magazine v. Falwell*, 485 U.S. 46, 56 (1988)（分析由公众人物和公职人员提起的故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）之诉讼请求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：否，因为电视台并未出于恶意发布虚假事实陈述。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"publish","ipa":"/pˈəblɪʃ/","syllables":"pub·lish","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 出版, 发行, 公开, 发表, 宣传, 公布；vi. 出版, 发行","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“publish”","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:published/p:published/i:publishing/3:publishes","sourceSentence":"No, because the television station did not publish a false statement of fact with actual malice.","synonyms":["bring out","put out","issue","release"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-9458a87ef66fda80"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":53,"sourceQuestionPage":160,"sourceQuestionPages":[160],"sourceExplanationPages":[161,162],"sourceAnswerPages":[161,162],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-054","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A woman who was driving down a busy street was lost, but she did not want to stop to figure out where she was going. Instead, she used her knees to control the steering wheel while she pulled up a map on her smartphone. As the woman approached an intersection, a truck driver coming from the opposite direction tried to make an illegal U-turn in front of her car. The woman looked up from her smartphone and immediately tried to grasp her steering wheel, but she was unable to swerve out of the way in time, so the truck crashed into her car. The woman filed a negligence action against the truck driver to recover $100,000 in damages for injuries sustained in the accident. At trial, the trier of fact established that the woman and the truck driver were equally at fault. Which of the following is an accurate statement of the damages that the woman can recover?","choices":{"A":"In a contributory negligence jurisdiction, the woman can recover $50,000.","B":"In a modified comparative-negligence jurisdiction, the woman can recover $50,000.","C":"In a modified comparative-negligence jurisdiction, the woman can recover nothing.","D":"In a pure comparative-negligence jurisdiction, the woman can recover nothing."},"answer":"B","explanation":"In traditional contributory-negligence jurisdictions, a plaintiff who fails to use reasonable care for his/her own safety and thereby contributes to his/her own injury is barred from recovering damages. However, almost all jurisdictions have adopted some form of comparative fault (ie, comparative negligence). There are two forms of comparative fault: Pure comparative fault (default rule on the MBE) – recovery is reduced by the plaintiff's percentage of fault\n\nModified comparative fault – the same as pure comparative fault, except that recovery is barred if the plaintiff's fault exceeds 50%*\n\nHere, the woman sought to recover $100,000 in damages from the truck driver. The trier of fact established that the woman and the truck driver were equally at fault for the accident. In a contributory negligence jurisdiction, the woman's fault would preclude her from recovering any damages (Choice A).\n\nIn a pure comparative-fault jurisdiction, the woman's full damages would be calculated by the trier of fact and then reduced by the proportion that the plaintiff's fault bears to the total harm ($100,000 - $50,000 = $50,000) (Choice D).\n\nIn a modified comparative-fault jurisdiction, the woman can recover because her fault did not exceed 50% (Choice C).* However, her total damages will be reduced by her percentage of fault ($100,000 - $50,000 = $50,000).\n\nTherefore, the most accurate statement of damages is that the woman can recover $50,000 in a modified comparative-fault jurisdiction. *This is the view in the vast majority of modified comparative-fault jurisdictions. However, in a small minority of these jurisdictions, a plaintiff recovers nothing when the plaintiff and defendant are equally at fault.\n\nEducational objective: In a pure comparative-fault jurisdiction, recovery is reduced by the plaintiff's percentage of fault. The same is true for a modified comparative-fault jurisdiction, except that recovery is barred if the plaintiff's fault exceeds 50%.\n\nReferences\n57B Am. Jur. 2d Negligence § 954 (2020) (comparative negligence, generally).","educationalObjective":"In a pure comparative-fault jurisdiction, recovery is reduced by the plaintiff's percentage of fault. The same is true for a modified comparative-fault jurisdiction, except that recovery is barred if the plaintiff's fault exceeds 50%.","references":"57B Am. Jur. 2d Negligence § 954 (2020) (comparative negligence, generally).","rule":"In a pure comparative-fault jurisdiction, recovery is reduced by the plaintiff's percentage of fault. The same is true for a modified comparative-fault jurisdiction, except that recovery is barred if the plaintiff's fault exceeds 50%.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is an accurate statement of the damages that the woman can recover?","rule":"In a pure comparative-fault jurisdiction, recovery is reduced by the plaintiff's percentage of fault. The same is true for a modified comparative-fault jurisdiction, except that recovery is barred if the plaintiff's fault exceeds 50%.","analysis":"In traditional contributory-negligence jurisdictions, a plaintiff who fails to use reasonable care for his/her own safety and thereby contributes to his/her own injury is barred from recovering damages. However, almost all jurisdictions have adopted some form of comparative fault (ie, comparative negligence). There are two forms of comparative fault: Pure comparative fault (default rule on the MBE) – recovery is reduced by the plaintiff's percentage of fault\n\nModified comparative fault – the same as pure comparative fault, except that recovery is barred if the plaintiff's fault exceeds 50%*\n\nHere, the woman sought to recover $100,000 in damages from the truck driver. The trier of fact established that the woman and the truck driver were equally at fault for the accident. In a contributory negligence jurisdiction, the woman's fault would preclude her from recovering any damages (Choice A).\n\nIn a pure comparative-fault jurisdiction, the woman's full damages would be calculated by the trier of fact and then reduced by the proportion that the plaintiff's fault bears to the total harm ($100,000 - $50,000 = $50,000) (Choice D).\n\nIn a modified comparative-fault jurisdiction, the woman can recover because her fault did not exceed 50% (Choice C).* However, her total damages will be reduced by her percentage of fault ($100,000 - $50,000 = $50,000).\n\nTherefore, the most accurate statement of damages is that the woman can recover $50,000 in a modified comparative-fault jurisdiction. *This is the view in the vast majority of modified comparative-fault jurisdictions. However, in a small minority of these jurisdictions, a plaintiff recovers nothing when the plaintiff and defendant are equally at fault.","conclusion":"Answer B: In a modified comparative-negligence jurisdiction, the woman can recover $50,000."},"questionZh":"一名女子在繁忙的街道上驾车时迷了路，但她不愿停车确认方向。\n\n相反，她用膝盖控制方向盘，同时在智能手机上查看地图。\n\n当她接近一个路口时，迎面驶来的一辆卡车试图在她车前违规掉头。\n\n该女子从手机上抬起头，立即试图抓住方向盘，但未能及时转向避让，导致卡车撞上了她的车。该女子针对卡车司机提起了过失诉讼，要求赔偿因事故造成的10万美元损失。\n\n在审判中，事实认定者判定该女子与卡车司机负有同等责任。关于该女子可获得的赔偿金额，下列哪项表述是正确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"在适用“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区，该女子可获得5万美元赔偿。","B":"在适用“修正型比较过失”（modified comparative-negligence）原则的司法管辖区，该女子可获得5万美元赔偿。","C":"在适用“修正型比较过失”原则的司法管辖区，该女子无法获得任何赔偿。","D":"在适用“纯粹比较过失”（pure comparative-negligence）原则的司法管辖区，该女子无法获得任何赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"在传统的“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）司法管辖区，如果原告未能对自身安全尽到合理注意义务，从而导致自身受伤，则其无权获得损害赔偿。\n\n然而，几乎所有司法管辖区都已采用某种形式的“比较过错”（comparative fault，即比较过失）原则。\n\n比较过错原则有两种形式：纯粹比较过错（Pure comparative fault，这是MBE考试中的默认规则）——原告获得的赔偿额需扣除其过错比例对应的金额； 修正比较过错（Modified comparative fault）——与纯粹比较过错原则相同，但如果原告的过错比例超过50%，则原告无权获得赔偿*。\n\n在本案中，该女子寻求向卡车司机索赔10万美元。事实认定者确定，该女子与卡车司机对事故负有同等责任。\n\n在适用与有过失（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区，该女子的过错将导致其无法获得任何赔偿（选项A）。\n\n在适用纯粹比较过错原则的司法管辖区，事实认定者会计算该女子的全部损害赔偿额，然后扣除原告过错占总损害的比例（10万美元 - 5万美元 = 5万美元）（选项D）。\n\n在适用修正比较过错原则的司法管辖区，该女子可以获得赔偿，因为她的过错比例未超过50%（选项C）*。不过，她的赔偿总额将扣除其过错比例对应的金额（10万美元 - 5万美元 = 5万美元）。\n\n因此，关于损害赔偿最准确的表述是：在适用修正比较过错原则的司法管辖区，该女子可获得5万美元的赔偿。\n\n*这是绝大多数适用修正比较过错原则的司法管辖区的观点。\n\n然而，在极少数此类司法管辖区中，如果原告与被告负有同等责任，原告将无法获得任何赔偿。\n\n教学目标：在纯粹比较过错原则下，原告获得的赔偿额需扣除其过错比例对应的金额。\n\n修正比较过错原则也是如此，区别仅在于：若原告过错比例超过50%，则原告无权获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 57B Am. Jur. 2d Negligence § 954 (2020)（关于比较过失的一般性论述）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在适用“纯粹比较过失”原则的司法管辖区，赔偿金额会根据原告的过失比例进行扣减。\n\n在适用“修正型比较过失”原则的司法管辖区情况亦是如此，但若原告的过失比例超过50%，则原告将丧失获得赔偿的权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于该女子可获得的赔偿金额，下列哪项表述是正确的？","rule":"在适用“纯粹比较过失”原则的司法管辖区，赔偿金额会根据原告的过失比例进行扣减。\n\n在适用“修正型比较过失”原则的司法管辖区情况亦是如此，但若原告的过失比例超过50%，则原告将丧失获得赔偿的权利。","analysis":"在传统的“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）司法管辖区，如果原告未能对自身安全尽到合理注意义务，从而导致自身受伤，则其无权获得损害赔偿。\n\n然而，几乎所有司法管辖区都已采用某种形式的“比较过错”（comparative fault，即比较过失）原则。\n\n比较过错原则有两种形式：纯粹比较过错（Pure comparative fault，这是MBE考试中的默认规则）——原告获得的赔偿额需扣除其过错比例对应的金额； 修正比较过错（Modified comparative fault）——与纯粹比较过错原则相同，但如果原告的过错比例超过50%，则原告无权获得赔偿*。\n\n在本案中，该女子寻求向卡车司机索赔10万美元。事实认定者确定，该女子与卡车司机对事故负有同等责任。\n\n在适用与有过失（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区，该女子的过错将导致其无法获得任何赔偿（选项A）。\n\n在适用纯粹比较过错原则的司法管辖区，事实认定者会计算该女子的全部损害赔偿额，然后扣除原告过错占总损害的比例（10万美元 - 5万美元 = 5万美元）（选项D）。\n\n在适用修正比较过错原则的司法管辖区，该女子可以获得赔偿，因为她的过错比例未超过50%（选项C）*。不过，她的赔偿总额将扣除其过错比例对应的金额（10万美元 - 5万美元 = 5万美元）。\n\n因此，关于损害赔偿最准确的表述是：在适用修正比较过错原则的司法管辖区，该女子可获得5万美元的赔偿。\n\n*这是绝大多数适用修正比较过错原则的司法管辖区的观点。\n\n然而，在极少数此类司法管辖区中，如果原告与被告负有同等责任，原告将无法获得任何赔偿。\n\n教学目标：在纯粹比较过错原则下，原告获得的赔偿额需扣除其过错比例对应的金额。\n\n修正比较过错原则也是如此，区别仅在于：若原告过错比例超过50%，则原告无权获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 57B Am. Jur. 2d Negligence § 954 (2020)（关于比较过失的一般性论述）。","conclusion":"答案 B：在适用“修正型比较过失”（modified comparative-negligence）原则的司法管辖区，该女子可获得5万美元赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"modified comparative negligence","ipa":"/mˈɑdʌfˌaɪd kʌmpˈɛɹətɪv nˈɛɡlədʒəns/","syllables":"mod·i·fied com·par·a·tive neg·li·gence","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"修正比较过失：原告达到法定责任比例后不得赔偿","contextualMeaning":"修正比较过失：原告达到法定责任比例后不得赔偿","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A woman who was driving down a busy street was lost, but she did not want to stop to figure out where she was going.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"pure comparative negligence","ipa":"/pjˈʊɹ kʌmpˈɛɹətɪv nˈɛɡlədʒəns/","syllables":"pure com·par·a·tive neg·li·gence","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"纯粹比较过失：按原告责任比例减少赔偿，不设比例门槛","contextualMeaning":"纯粹比较过失：按原告责任比例减少赔偿，不设比例门槛","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A woman who was driving down a busy street was lost, but she did not want to stop to figure out where she was going.","synonyms":["pure comparative fault"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-5fcba3003844f2f8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":54,"sourceQuestionPage":163,"sourceQuestionPages":[163],"sourceExplanationPages":[164,165],"sourceAnswerPages":[164,165],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-055","subject":"Torts","topic":"Vicarious Liability","question":"A plumbing company hired a worker to work at various construction sites. The worker used his own truck to travel between the company's warehouse and the construction sites, but the company fitted the truck with a rack for carrying plumbing pipes. The company paid the worker for traveling between the warehouse and the construction sites, but not for his drive to and from work. Because the worker was required to haul pipes on his truck while driving between the warehouse and the construction sites, the company asked the worker, before hiring him, whether he had a valid driver's license. The worker represented that he did, although in fact his license had been suspended because he had been convicted of recklessly causing motor vehicle collisions. The company made no effort to verify the worker's representation. While driving to work one morning in his truck, the worker carelessly caused a collision in which a woman was injured. In her subsequent action against the plumbing company, based on a theory of negligent hiring, is the woman likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the actions taken by the worker were not in the performance of his employment or job duties.","B":"No, because the worker was an independent contractor.","C":"Yes, because the company fitted the worker's truck with a pipe rack.","D":"Yes, because the company had a duty to ensure that its workers had valid driver's licenses."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Liability for negligent hiring arises when a defendant's failure to use reasonable care in hiring a competent worker causes the plaintiff harm. Therefore, a defendant is only liable for negligent hiring if there is a causal connection between the defendant's negligence in hiring the worker, the worker's employment or job duties, and the plaintiff's harm. Here, the plumbing company failed to use reasonable care when it made no effort to verify that the worker—who was paid to drive between the warehouse and various construction sites—had a valid driver's license (Choice D). But even though the worker carelessly collided with the woman, it occurred during his commute to work—not during his employment or performance of job duties (no causal connection). Therefore, the plumbing company is not liable for negligent hiring. (Choice B) A defendant can be liable for negligent hiring of an employee or an independent contractor. Therefore, the fact that the worker was an independent contractor will not defeat the woman's claim. Instead, her claim will fail because the worker was not acting in performance of his employment or job duties when the collision occurred. (Choice C) Although the plumbing company fitted the worker's truck with a pipe rack, it only paid him to drive the truck between the warehouse and construction sites—not to and from work. And since the collision occurred while he was driving to work (not while performing job duties), the company is not liable for the woman's injury.\n\nEducational objective: Liability for negligent hiring arises when a defendant's failure to use reasonable care in hiring a competent worker causes the plaintiff harm—ie, there must be some causal connection between the defendant's negligence in hiring the worker, the worker's employment or job duties, and the plaintiff's harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Agency § 7.05 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (negligent hiring).","educationalObjective":"Liability for negligent hiring arises when a defendant's failure to use reasonable care in hiring a competent worker causes the plaintiff harm—ie, there must be some causal connection between the defendant's negligence in hiring the worker, the worker's employment or job duties, and the plaintiff's harm.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Agency § 7.05 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (negligent hiring).","rule":"Liability for negligent hiring arises when a defendant's failure to use reasonable care in hiring a competent worker causes the plaintiff harm—ie, there must be some causal connection between the defendant's negligence in hiring the worker, the worker's employment or job duties, and the plaintiff's harm.","irac":{"issue":"In her subsequent action against the plumbing company, based on a theory of negligent hiring, is the woman likely to prevail?","rule":"Liability for negligent hiring arises when a defendant's failure to use reasonable care in hiring a competent worker causes the plaintiff harm—ie, there must be some causal connection between the defendant's negligence in hiring the worker, the worker's employment or job duties, and the plaintiff's harm.","analysis":"Liability for negligent hiring arises when a defendant's failure to use reasonable care in hiring a competent worker causes the plaintiff harm. Therefore, a defendant is only liable for negligent hiring if there is a causal connection between the defendant's negligence in hiring the worker, the worker's employment or job duties, and the plaintiff's harm. Here, the plumbing company failed to use reasonable care when it made no effort to verify that the worker—who was paid to drive between the warehouse and various construction sites—had a valid driver's license (Choice D). But even though the worker carelessly collided with the woman, it occurred during his commute to work—not during his employment or performance of job duties (no causal connection). Therefore, the plumbing company is not liable for negligent hiring. (Choice B) A defendant can be liable for negligent hiring of an employee or an independent contractor. Therefore, the fact that the worker was an independent contractor will not defeat the woman's claim. Instead, her claim will fail because the worker was not acting in performance of his employment or job duties when the collision occurred. (Choice C) Although the plumbing company fitted the worker's truck with a pipe rack, it only paid him to drive the truck between the warehouse and construction sites—not to and from work. And since the collision occurred while he was driving to work (not while performing job duties), the company is not liable for the woman's injury.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the actions taken by the worker were not in the performance of his employment or job duties."},"questionZh":"一家管道工程公司聘请了一名工人，负责在多个施工现场工作。\n\n该工人使用自己的卡车往返于公司仓库和施工现场，但公司在卡车上加装了用于运送管道的支架。\n\n公司向工人支付往返仓库与施工现场期间的费用，但不支付其上下班通勤的费用。\n\n由于工人需要在往返仓库与施工现场的途中用卡车运送管道，公司在聘用前询问了他是否持有有效的驾驶执照。\n\n工人声称自己持有有效驾照，但实际上他的驾照因曾被判犯有鲁莽驾驶导致机动车碰撞事故而被吊销。\n\n公司并未核实工人的这一说法。\n\n一天早上，该工人在驾驶卡车上班途中因疏忽引发碰撞事故，导致一名女子受伤。\n\n该女子随后基于“过失聘用”理论起诉管道工程公司，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为工人的行为并非在履行其受雇职责或工作任务期间发生。","B":"不大，因为该工人属于独立承包商。","C":"大，因为公司在工人的卡车上加装了管道支架。","D":"大，因为公司有义务确保其工人持有有效的驾驶执照。"},"explanationZh":"“过失雇佣”责任产生于被告在聘用胜任工作的员工时未尽到合理注意义务，从而导致原告受到伤害的情形。\n\n因此，被告仅在以下情况下才承担过失雇佣责任：被告在雇佣该员工时的过失、该员工的受雇状态或工作职责，与原告受到的伤害之间存在因果联系。\n\n在本案中，该管道公司未尽到合理注意义务，因为它未核实该员工（其受雇驾驶车辆往返于仓库与各施工现场之间）是否持有有效驾驶执照（选项 D）。\n\n然而，尽管该员工因疏忽大意撞到了那名女子，但事故发生在他上下班通勤途中，而非履行受雇职责或执行工作任务期间（即不存在因果联系）。\n\n因此，该管道公司不承担过失雇佣责任。\n\n（选项 B）被告可能因过失雇佣员工或独立承包商而承担责任。\n\n因此，该员工属于独立承包商这一事实并不会导致该女子的索赔请求被驳回。\n\n相反，她的索赔请求之所以无法成立，是因为事故发生时该员工并未在履行受雇职责或执行工作任务。\n\n（选项 C）尽管管道公司为该员工的卡车安装了管架，但公司仅支付报酬让他驾驶卡车往返于仓库和施工现场，并不包括上下班通勤。\n\n鉴于事故发生时他正驾车前往工作地点（而非执行工作任务），公司无需对该女子的伤害承担责任。\n\n教育目标：“过失雇佣”责任产生于被告在聘用胜任工作的员工时未尽到合理注意义务，从而导致原告受到伤害的情形——即被告在雇佣该员工时的过失、该员工的受雇状态或工作职责，与原告受到的伤害之间必须存在某种因果联系。\n\n参考文献 《代理法重述（第三版）》第 7.05 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（过失雇佣）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“过失聘用”责任的产生，是指被告在聘用胜任工作的员工时未能尽到合理的注意义务，从而导致原告受到伤害——即被告在聘用工人时的过失、工人的受雇职责或工作任务，以及原告受到的伤害之间，必须存在某种因果联系。","iracZh":{"issue":"该女子随后基于“过失聘用”理论起诉管道工程公司，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"“过失聘用”责任的产生，是指被告在聘用胜任工作的员工时未能尽到合理的注意义务，从而导致原告受到伤害——即被告在聘用工人时的过失、工人的受雇职责或工作任务，以及原告受到的伤害之间，必须存在某种因果联系。","analysis":"“过失雇佣”责任产生于被告在聘用胜任工作的员工时未尽到合理注意义务，从而导致原告受到伤害的情形。\n\n因此，被告仅在以下情况下才承担过失雇佣责任：被告在雇佣该员工时的过失、该员工的受雇状态或工作职责，与原告受到的伤害之间存在因果联系。\n\n在本案中，该管道公司未尽到合理注意义务，因为它未核实该员工（其受雇驾驶车辆往返于仓库与各施工现场之间）是否持有有效驾驶执照（选项 D）。\n\n然而，尽管该员工因疏忽大意撞到了那名女子，但事故发生在他上下班通勤途中，而非履行受雇职责或执行工作任务期间（即不存在因果联系）。\n\n因此，该管道公司不承担过失雇佣责任。\n\n（选项 B）被告可能因过失雇佣员工或独立承包商而承担责任。\n\n因此，该员工属于独立承包商这一事实并不会导致该女子的索赔请求被驳回。\n\n相反，她的索赔请求之所以无法成立，是因为事故发生时该员工并未在履行受雇职责或执行工作任务。\n\n（选项 C）尽管管道公司为该员工的卡车安装了管架，但公司仅支付报酬让他驾驶卡车往返于仓库和施工现场，并不包括上下班通勤。\n\n鉴于事故发生时他正驾车前往工作地点（而非执行工作任务），公司无需对该女子的伤害承担责任。\n\n教育目标：“过失雇佣”责任产生于被告在聘用胜任工作的员工时未尽到合理注意义务，从而导致原告受到伤害的情形——即被告在雇佣该员工时的过失、该员工的受雇状态或工作职责，与原告受到的伤害之间必须存在某种因果联系。\n\n参考文献 《代理法重述（第三版）》第 7.05 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（过失雇佣）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不大，因为工人的行为并非在履行其受雇职责或工作任务期间发生。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"independent contractor","ipa":"/ˌɪndɪpˈɛndənt kˈɑntɹˌæktɝ/","syllables":"in·de·pen·dent con·trac·tor","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"独立承包人：委托方通常不控制其工作方式的独立执行者","contextualMeaning":"独立承包人：委托方通常不控制其工作方式的独立执行者","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the worker was an independent contractor.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"employment","ipa":"/ɛmplˈɔɪmənt/","syllables":"em·ploy·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 雇用, 职业, 工作；[经] 职业, 雇用, 职工招请","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“employment”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:employments","sourceSentence":"No, because the actions taken by the worker were not in the performance of his employment or job duties.","synonyms":["work","use","usage","utilization"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-a81099615e6febe1"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":55,"sourceQuestionPage":166,"sourceQuestionPages":[166],"sourceExplanationPages":[167,168],"sourceAnswerPages":[167,168],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-056","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A photographer with no climbing experience fell while attempting to climb a mountain alone. He lay unconscious and critically injured on a ledge that was difficult to reach. A nearby tourist was seriously injured while trying to rescue the photographer. The tourist made a reasonable attempt to rescue the photographer, but his attempt failed, and the photographer died from his injuries before he could be reached. The tourist brought an action against the photographer's estate for compensation for his injuries. In this jurisdiction, the traditional common law rules relating to contributory negligence and assumption of risk remain in effect. Will the tourist prevail in his action against the photographer's estate?","choices":{"A":"No, because the tourist was contributorily negligent for undertaking a dangerous rescue.","B":"No, because the tourist's rescue attempt failed and therefore did not benefit the photographer.","C":"Yes, because the law should not discourage attempts to assist persons in helpless peril.","D":"Yes, because the photographer's peril arose from his own failure to use reasonable care."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Persons who endanger themselves or others owe a duty of reasonable care to potential rescuers because it is foreseeable that they could be injured in a rescue attempt. Under this rescue doctrine, a plaintiff-rescuer can establish a prima facie negligence case by proving that: he/she was injured while attempting to rescue another and that person's peril was caused by the defendant's failure to use reasonable care (ie, negligence). Here, the tourist was seriously injured while trying to rescue the photographer. The photographer's peril arose from his own failure to use reasonable care since he went mountain climbing alone and with no prior experience. Therefore, the tourist can establish a prima facie negligence case. But in this common law jurisdiction, the tourist is barred from recovery if he: knew that the rescue attempt was dangerous and could have proceeded in a reasonable alternative manner (ie, assumption of the risk) or\n\nacted unreasonably during the rescue attempt (ie, contributory negligence).\n\nThere is no evidence that the tourist could have helped the photographer in a reasonable alternative manner since he was critically injured on a ledge that was difficult to reach (no assumption of risk). And the tourist acted reasonably during the rescue attempt (no contributory negligence) (Choice A). As a result, he will likely prevail in an action against the photographer's estate. (Choices B & C) The rescue doctrine seeks to encourage (not discourage) attempts to assist persons in helpless peril. As a result, the tourist can prevail even though his reasonable rescue attempt failed and did not benefit the photographer. But the tourist must first show that the photographer's peril arose from his own negligence.\n\nEducational objective: The rescue doctrine allows a plaintiff-rescuer to establish a prima facie negligence case by proving that (1) he/she was injured while attempting to rescue another and (2) that person's peril was caused by the defendant's failure to use reasonable care.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 472 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a rescuer is not contributorily negligent for confronting danger unless the rescue attempt is unreasonable or he/she acts unreasonably).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 496E (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a rescuer does not voluntarily assume the risk if the defendant's tortious conduct left no reasonable alternative to avert harm to the rescuer or another).","educationalObjective":"The rescue doctrine allows a plaintiff-rescuer to establish a prima facie negligence case by proving that (1) he/she was injured while attempting to rescue another and (2) that person's peril was caused by the defendant's failure to use reasonable care.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 472 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a rescuer is not contributorily negligent for confronting danger unless the rescue attempt is unreasonable or he/she acts unreasonably).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 496E (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a rescuer does not voluntarily assume the risk if the defendant's tortious conduct left no reasonable alternative to avert harm to the rescuer or another).","rule":"The rescue doctrine allows a plaintiff-rescuer to establish a prima facie negligence case by proving that (1) he/she was injured while attempting to rescue another and (2) that person's peril was caused by the defendant's failure to use reasonable care.","irac":{"issue":"Will the tourist prevail in his action against the photographer's estate?","rule":"The rescue doctrine allows a plaintiff-rescuer to establish a prima facie negligence case by proving that (1) he/she was injured while attempting to rescue another and (2) that person's peril was caused by the defendant's failure to use reasonable care.","analysis":"Persons who endanger themselves or others owe a duty of reasonable care to potential rescuers because it is foreseeable that they could be injured in a rescue attempt. Under this rescue doctrine, a plaintiff-rescuer can establish a prima facie negligence case by proving that: he/she was injured while attempting to rescue another and that person's peril was caused by the defendant's failure to use reasonable care (ie, negligence). Here, the tourist was seriously injured while trying to rescue the photographer. The photographer's peril arose from his own failure to use reasonable care since he went mountain climbing alone and with no prior experience. Therefore, the tourist can establish a prima facie negligence case. But in this common law jurisdiction, the tourist is barred from recovery if he: knew that the rescue attempt was dangerous and could have proceeded in a reasonable alternative manner (ie, assumption of the risk) or\n\nacted unreasonably during the rescue attempt (ie, contributory negligence).\n\nThere is no evidence that the tourist could have helped the photographer in a reasonable alternative manner since he was critically injured on a ledge that was difficult to reach (no assumption of risk). And the tourist acted reasonably during the rescue attempt (no contributory negligence) (Choice A). As a result, he will likely prevail in an action against the photographer's estate. (Choices B & C) The rescue doctrine seeks to encourage (not discourage) attempts to assist persons in helpless peril. As a result, the tourist can prevail even though his reasonable rescue attempt failed and did not benefit the photographer. But the tourist must first show that the photographer's peril arose from his own negligence.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the photographer's peril arose from his own failure to use reasonable care."},"questionZh":"一名毫无攀岩经验的摄影师在独自登山时坠落，身受重伤并陷入昏迷，躺在一处难以抵达的岩架上。\n\n一名附近的游客在试图营救该摄影师时也受了重伤。\n\n该游客为营救摄影师采取了合理的行动，但未能成功；摄影师在救援人员抵达前因伤重身亡。\n\n该游客针对摄影师的遗产提起诉讼，要求赔偿其所受的伤害。\n\n在该司法管辖区，有关“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）和“自甘冒险”（assumption of risk）的传统普通法规则仍然有效。\n\n该游客针对摄影师遗产提起的诉讼会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该游客因进行危险的营救行动而存在与有过失（contributory negligence）。","B":"不会，因为该游客的营救尝试失败了，因此并未使摄影师受益。","C":"会，因为法律不应阻碍人们尝试援助处于无助险境中的他人。","D":"会，因为摄影师所处的险境源于其自身未能尽到合理的注意义务。"},"explanationZh":"置自身或他人于危险境地的人，对潜在的救援者负有合理的注意义务，因为可以预见救援者在尝试救援时可能会受伤。\n\n根据这一“救援原则”（rescue doctrine），作为原告的救援者若能证明以下两点，即可确立初步的过失侵权案情：其在试图救援他人时受伤，且该他人的险境是由被告未尽合理注意义务（即过失）造成的。\n\n在本案中，游客在试图营救摄影师时受了重伤。\n\n摄影师的险境源于其自身未尽合理注意义务，因为他在毫无经验的情况下独自登山。\n\n因此，该游客可以确立初步的过失侵权案情。\n\n但在该普通法司法管辖区内，如果游客存在以下情况，则无法获得赔偿：明知救援行动具有危险性，却本可采取合理的替代方式进行救援（即“自甘冒险”/assumption of the risk）；或者 在救援过程中行为不当（即“与有过失（contributory negligence）”/contributory negligence）。\n\n没有证据表明游客本可以采取合理的替代方式帮助摄影师，因为摄影师当时身受重伤，困于一处难以到达的岩架上（不构成自甘冒险）。\n\n此外，游客在救援过程中的行为是合理的（不构成与有过失（contributory negligence））（选项 A）。\n\n因此，他在针对摄影师遗产（estate）提起的诉讼中很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 B 和 C）救援原则旨在鼓励（而非阻碍）人们尝试援助处于无助险境中的他人。\n\n因此，即使游客合理的救援尝试未能成功，也未给摄影师带来实际助益，他仍可胜诉。\n\n但游客必须首先证明摄影师的险境是由其自身的过失造成的。\n\n教育目标：救援原则允许作为原告的救援者通过证明以下两点来确立初步的过失侵权案情：(1) 其在试图救援他人时受伤；(2) 该他人的险境是由被告未尽合理注意义务造成的。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 472 条 (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 472) (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明救援者因面对危险而不构成与有过失（contributory negligence），除非救援尝试本身不合理或其行为不合理）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第496E条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明：若被告的侵权行为未留下合理的替代方案以避免对施救者或他人造成伤害，则施救者不构成自愿承担风险）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“营救原则”（rescue doctrine）允许作为营救者的原告确立初步的过失案情，只要能证明：(1) 其在试图营救他人时受伤；以及 (2) 该他人的险境是由被告未能尽到合理的注意义务所造成的。","iracZh":{"issue":"该游客针对摄影师遗产提起的诉讼会胜诉吗？","rule":"“营救原则”（rescue doctrine）允许作为营救者的原告确立初步的过失案情，只要能证明：(1) 其在试图营救他人时受伤；以及 (2) 该他人的险境是由被告未能尽到合理的注意义务所造成的。","analysis":"置自身或他人于危险境地的人，对潜在的救援者负有合理的注意义务，因为可以预见救援者在尝试救援时可能会受伤。\n\n根据这一“救援原则”（rescue doctrine），作为原告的救援者若能证明以下两点，即可确立初步的过失侵权案情：其在试图救援他人时受伤，且该他人的险境是由被告未尽合理注意义务（即过失）造成的。\n\n在本案中，游客在试图营救摄影师时受了重伤。\n\n摄影师的险境源于其自身未尽合理注意义务，因为他在毫无经验的情况下独自登山。\n\n因此，该游客可以确立初步的过失侵权案情。\n\n但在该普通法司法管辖区内，如果游客存在以下情况，则无法获得赔偿：明知救援行动具有危险性，却本可采取合理的替代方式进行救援（即“自甘冒险”/assumption of the risk）；或者 在救援过程中行为不当（即“与有过失（contributory negligence）”/contributory negligence）。\n\n没有证据表明游客本可以采取合理的替代方式帮助摄影师，因为摄影师当时身受重伤，困于一处难以到达的岩架上（不构成自甘冒险）。\n\n此外，游客在救援过程中的行为是合理的（不构成与有过失（contributory negligence））（选项 A）。\n\n因此，他在针对摄影师遗产（estate）提起的诉讼中很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 B 和 C）救援原则旨在鼓励（而非阻碍）人们尝试援助处于无助险境中的他人。\n\n因此，即使游客合理的救援尝试未能成功，也未给摄影师带来实际助益，他仍可胜诉。\n\n但游客必须首先证明摄影师的险境是由其自身的过失造成的。\n\n教育目标：救援原则允许作为原告的救援者通过证明以下两点来确立初步的过失侵权案情：(1) 其在试图救援他人时受伤；(2) 该他人的险境是由被告未尽合理注意义务造成的。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 472 条 (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 472) (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明救援者因面对危险而不构成与有过失（contributory negligence），除非救援尝试本身不合理或其行为不合理）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第496E条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明：若被告的侵权行为未留下合理的替代方案以避免对施救者或他人造成伤害，则施救者不构成自愿承担风险）。","conclusion":"答案 D：会，因为摄影师所处的险境源于其自身未能尽到合理的注意义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"assumption of risk","ipa":"/ʌsˈəmpʃən ˈəv ɹˈɪsk/","syllables":"as·sump·tion of risk","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"自甘风险：原告明知并自愿面对特定风险所产生的抗辩","contextualMeaning":"自甘风险：原告明知并自愿面对特定风险所产生的抗辩","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"In this jurisdiction, the traditional common law rules relating to contributory negligence and assumption of risk remain in effect.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-2a756a6c55f40e4a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":56,"sourceQuestionPage":169,"sourceQuestionPages":[169],"sourceExplanationPages":[170,171],"sourceAnswerPages":[170,171],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-057","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A door attendant at a downtown hotel also served as the hotel's security guard. He regularly exchanged greetings with a woman who passed the hotel on her way to and from work. One day, the attendant saw a first-time hotel guest exit the hotel and proceed down the street behind the woman. Just after the two left the hotel's property, the attendant observed the guest reach into the woman's purse and remove her wallet. The attendant later confessed to the woman that he had failed to act despite seeing the guest take the wallet. The woman has brought a negligence action against the hotel to recover the money taken from her by the guest. Which of the following best describes the standard by which the attendant's conduct as an employee of the hotel should be judged in this matter?","choices":{"A":"As a security guard employed by an innkeeper, the attendant had a duty to prevent the hotel guest from harming the woman.","B":"As an employee of a business with no special relationship with the woman, the attendant was not under an affirmative duty to act.","C":"As an employee of a property owner, the attendant had a duty to warn the woman of the concealed danger that the pickpocketing guest presented.","D":"As an employee of an innkeeper, the attendant was required to act with the highest degree of care with regard to the woman and is responsible for even slight negligence."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Special relationships\n\nimposing duty to protect others\n\nParent/child Hospital/patient Employer/employees Shopkeeper/business invitees Common carrier/passengers Custodian/person in custody Innkeeper/guests Mnemonic: Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries\n\nA person generally has no duty to protect another from harm. However, a person in a special relationship with another may have an affirmative duty to use reasonable care to protect the other from harm arising within the scope of that relationship. For example, an innkeeper has a duty to use reasonable care to protect its guests while they are on the premises. Here, the hotel (innkeeper) did not have a special relationship with the woman, who was merely a passerby and not a guest at the hotel. Consequently, neither the hotel nor its employees had an affirmative duty to protect her from the pickpocketing guest. Therefore, the attendant's conduct as an employee of the hotel should be judged by this standard. (Choice A) A person with the actual ability and authority to control another may have a duty to use reasonable care to prevent that person from injuring others.* But here, even as the hotel's security guard, the attendant had no control over a hotel guest off the premises. *This duty generally is only imposed if the person in control knows or should know that the other is likely to commit the injuring act. And here, the attendant had no reason to know that the first-time guest was a pickpocket. (Choice C) Property owners (the hotel) generally owe no duty to persons off the premises (the woman) unless the owner or someone under his/her control conducts an activity on the premises that poses an unreasonable risk of harm to persons off the premises. Here, the pickpocketing guest was not under the hotel's control and did not act on the hotel's premises. (Choice D) At common law, innkeepers and their employees owed the highest duty of care to their guests and could be liable for even slight negligence. But here, the woman was not the hotel's guest. And even if she were, most courts today hold that an innkeeper and its employees are liable only for ordinary negligence.\n\nEducational objective: A person in a special relationship with another (eg, an innkeeper with its guests) has a duty to use reasonable care to protect the other from harm arising within the scope of that relationship.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Phys. & Emot. Harm § 40 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (duty based on special relationship).","educationalObjective":"A person in a special relationship with another (eg, an innkeeper with its guests) has a duty to use reasonable care to protect the other from harm arising within the scope of that relationship.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Phys. & Emot. Harm § 40 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (duty based on special relationship).","rule":"A person in a special relationship with another (eg, an innkeeper with its guests) has a duty to use reasonable care to protect the other from harm arising within the scope of that relationship.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best describes the standard by which the attendant's conduct as an employee of the hotel should be judged in this matter?","rule":"A person in a special relationship with another (eg, an innkeeper with its guests) has a duty to use reasonable care to protect the other from harm arising within the scope of that relationship.","analysis":"Special relationships\n\nimposing duty to protect others\n\nParent/child Hospital/patient Employer/employees Shopkeeper/business invitees Common carrier/passengers Custodian/person in custody Innkeeper/guests Mnemonic: Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries\n\nA person generally has no duty to protect another from harm. However, a person in a special relationship with another may have an affirmative duty to use reasonable care to protect the other from harm arising within the scope of that relationship. For example, an innkeeper has a duty to use reasonable care to protect its guests while they are on the premises. Here, the hotel (innkeeper) did not have a special relationship with the woman, who was merely a passerby and not a guest at the hotel. Consequently, neither the hotel nor its employees had an affirmative duty to protect her from the pickpocketing guest. Therefore, the attendant's conduct as an employee of the hotel should be judged by this standard. (Choice A) A person with the actual ability and authority to control another may have a duty to use reasonable care to prevent that person from injuring others.* But here, even as the hotel's security guard, the attendant had no control over a hotel guest off the premises. *This duty generally is only imposed if the person in control knows or should know that the other is likely to commit the injuring act. And here, the attendant had no reason to know that the first-time guest was a pickpocket. (Choice C) Property owners (the hotel) generally owe no duty to persons off the premises (the woman) unless the owner or someone under his/her control conducts an activity on the premises that poses an unreasonable risk of harm to persons off the premises. Here, the pickpocketing guest was not under the hotel's control and did not act on the hotel's premises. (Choice D) At common law, innkeepers and their employees owed the highest duty of care to their guests and could be liable for even slight negligence. But here, the woman was not the hotel's guest. And even if she were, most courts today hold that an innkeeper and its employees are liable only for ordinary negligence.","conclusion":"Answer B: As an employee of a business with no special relationship with the woman, the attendant was not under an affirmative duty to act."},"questionZh":"市中心一家酒店的门童兼任酒店保安。\n\n他经常与一位上下班途经酒店的女士打招呼。\n\n一天，门童看到一位初次入住的客人走出酒店，跟在女士身后沿着街道走去。\n\n两人刚离开酒店，门童就看到客人伸手到女士的包里，拿走了她的钱包。\n\n门童后来向女士坦白，尽管看到客人拿走钱包，但他却没有采取任何行动。\n\n女士已对酒店提起过失诉讼，要求追回被客人拿走的钱。\n\n以下哪项最能描述判断门童作为酒店员工在此事件中行为的标准？","choicesZh":{"A":"作为酒店老板雇佣的保安，门童有义务防止酒店客人伤害该女士。","B":"作为与该女士没有特殊关系的商家员工，门童没有主动采取行动的义务。","C":"作为业主的雇员，服务员有义务警告这位女士注意扒手客人可能带来的隐患。","D":"作为旅店老板的雇员，服务员必须以最高程度的谨慎对待这位女士，即使是轻微的疏忽也需承担责任。"},"explanationZh":"特殊关系 产生保护他人义务的情形 父母与子女；医院与患者；雇主与雇员；店主与受邀顾客；公共承运人与乘客；监管人与被监管人；旅馆经营者与住客。（记忆口诀：Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries —— 请协助消除导致伤害的安全隐患） 通常情况下，一个人没有义务保护他人免受伤害。\n\n然而，若两人之间存在特殊关系，则一方可能负有积极义务，需尽合理注意以保护另一方免受该关系范围内产生的伤害。\n\n例如，旅馆经营者有义务尽合理注意，保护其住客在旅馆场所内的安全。\n\n在本案中，酒店（旅馆经营者）与该女子之间不存在特殊关系，因为她仅仅是一名路人，而非酒店住客。\n\n因此，酒店及其员工均无积极义务保护她免受那位行窃住客的侵害。\n\n故而，该服务人员作为酒店员工的行为应依据此标准进行评判。\n\n（选项 A）若某人有实际能力和权限控制他人，则其可能负有尽合理注意以防止该他人伤害他人的义务。* 但在本案中，即便该服务人员是酒店保安，他也无法控制身处酒店场所之外的住客。\n\n* 这种义务通常仅在控制方已知或应知被控制方可能实施伤害行为时才适用。\n\n而在本案中，该服务人员并无理由知晓这名初次入住的客人是一名扒手。\n\n（选项 C）财产所有者（酒店）通常不对场所外的人员（该女子）负有义务，除非所有者或其控制下的人员在场所内开展了对场所外人员构成不合理伤害风险的活动。\n\n在本案中，行窃的住客并不受酒店控制，其行为也并非发生在酒店场所内。\n\n（选项 D）在普通法下，旅馆经营者及其员工对住客负有最高程度的注意义务，即便存在轻微疏忽也可能承担责任。\n\n但本案中，该女子并非酒店住客。\n\n即便她是住客，当今大多数法院也认定，旅馆经营者及其员工仅对普通疏忽承担责任。\n\n教育目标：与他人存在特殊关系的一方（例如，旅店经营者与住客）有义务尽合理注意，保护对方免受该关系范围内产生的伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：身体与精神伤害》第40条（美国法律协会，2012年）（基于特殊关系的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"与他人存在特殊关系的人（例如，旅店老板与其客人）有义务采取合理的谨慎措施，保护对方免受在该关系范围内产生的伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项最能描述评判服务员作为酒店雇员在此事件中行为的标准？","rule":"与他人存在特殊关系的人（例如，旅店老板与其客人）有义务采取合理的谨慎措施，保护对方免受在该关系范围内产生的伤害。","analysis":"特殊关系 产生保护他人义务的情形 父母与子女；医院与患者；雇主与雇员；店主与受邀顾客；公共承运人与乘客；监管人与被监管人；旅馆经营者与住客。（记忆口诀：Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries —— 请协助消除导致伤害的安全隐患） 通常情况下，一个人没有义务保护他人免受伤害。\n\n然而，若两人之间存在特殊关系，则一方可能负有积极义务，需尽合理注意以保护另一方免受该关系范围内产生的伤害。\n\n例如，旅馆经营者有义务尽合理注意，保护其住客在旅馆场所内的安全。\n\n在本案中，酒店（旅馆经营者）与该女子之间不存在特殊关系，因为她仅仅是一名路人，而非酒店住客。\n\n因此，酒店及其员工均无积极义务保护她免受那位行窃住客的侵害。\n\n故而，该服务人员作为酒店员工的行为应依据此标准进行评判。\n\n（选项 A）若某人有实际能力和权限控制他人，则其可能负有尽合理注意以防止该他人伤害他人的义务。* 但在本案中，即便该服务人员是酒店保安，他也无法控制身处酒店场所之外的住客。\n\n* 这种义务通常仅在控制方已知或应知被控制方可能实施伤害行为时才适用。\n\n而在本案中，该服务人员并无理由知晓这名初次入住的客人是一名扒手。\n\n（选项 C）财产所有者（酒店）通常不对场所外的人员（该女子）负有义务，除非所有者或其控制下的人员在场所内开展了对场所外人员构成不合理伤害风险的活动。\n\n在本案中，行窃的住客并不受酒店控制，其行为也并非发生在酒店场所内。\n\n（选项 D）在普通法下，旅馆经营者及其员工对住客负有最高程度的注意义务，即便存在轻微疏忽也可能承担责任。\n\n但本案中，该女子并非酒店住客。\n\n即便她是住客，当今大多数法院也认定，旅馆经营者及其员工仅对普通疏忽承担责任。\n\n教育目标：与他人存在特殊关系的一方（例如，旅店经营者与住客）有义务尽合理注意，保护对方免受该关系范围内产生的伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：身体与精神伤害》第40条（美国法律协会，2012年）（基于特殊关系的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 B：作为与该女士没有特殊关系的商家员工，门童没有主动采取行动的义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"duty of care","ipa":"/dˈuti ˈəv kˈɛɹ/","syllables":"duty of care","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"注意义务：法律要求行为人对可预见风险采取合理谨慎","contextualMeaning":"注意义务：法律要求行为人对可预见风险采取合理谨慎","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) At common law, innkeepers and their employees owed the highest duty of care to their guests and could be liable for even slight negligence.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"employed","ipa":"/ɛmplˈɔɪd/","syllables":"em·ployed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 雇佣（employ的过去分词）","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“employed”","wordForm":"形容词；原形 employ；词形 0:employ/1:dp/p:employed/d:employed","sourceSentence":"As a security guard employed by an innkeeper, the attendant had a duty to prevent the hotel guest from harming the woman.","synonyms":["employment","use","utilize","utilise"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":57,"sourceQuestionPage":172,"sourceQuestionPages":[172],"sourceExplanationPages":[173,174],"sourceAnswerPages":[173,174],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-058","subject":"Torts","topic":"Actual & Proximate Cause","question":"A construction company was digging a trench for a new sewer line in a street in a high- crime neighborhood. During the course of the construction, there had been many thefts of tools and equipment from the construction area. One night, the construction company's employees neglected to place warning lights around the trench. A delivery truck drove into the trench and broke an axle. While the truck driver was looking for help, thieves broke into the truck and stole $350,000 worth of goods. The delivery company sued the construction company to recover for the $350,000 loss and for the damage to its truck. The construction company has stipulated that it was negligent in failing to place warning lights around the trench and admits liability for damage to the truck, but it denies liability for the loss of the goods. On cross-motions for summary judgment on the claim for the goods, how should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"Deny both motions, because there is evidence to support a finding that the construction company should have realized that its negligence could create an opportunity for a third party to commit a crime.","B":"Grant the construction company's motion, because no one could have foreseen that the failure to place warning lights could result in the loss of a cargo of valuable goods.","C":"Grant the construction company's motion, because the criminal acts of third persons were a superseding cause of the loss.","D":"Grant the delivery company's motion, because but for the construction company's actions, the goods would not have been stolen."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Summary judgment should be granted when (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact and (2) no reasonable jury could find for the opposing party—thereby entitling the movant to judgment as a matter of law. Such motions are often brought in negligence claims, which require proof of the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. The causation element consists of two prongs: actual cause – the plaintiff's harm would not have occurred but for the defendant's negligence and\n\nproximate cause – the plaintiff's harm was a foreseeable consequence of the defendant's negligence.\n\nThe proximate-cause prong cannot be satisfied if there was a superseding cause—ie, an unforeseeable act or force that (1) occurred after the defendant's negligence and (2) contributed to the plaintiff's harm. And though criminal acts of third parties are generally unforeseeable, these acts are not superseding causes if the defendant had reason to know that his/her conduct would increase the risk that such acts would occur. Here, the construction company admitted liability for the damaged truck but argued that it was not liable for the stolen goods because the theft was a superseding cause. Since the construction site was in a high-crime neighborhood with many prior thefts, it is possible (but not certain) that the construction company should have known that its negligence could create an opportunity for a third party to commit a crime. As a result, there is a genuine issue of material fact that warrants the denial of both motions (Choices B & C). (Choice D) The goods would not have been stolen from the disabled truck but for the construction company's negligence (actual cause). However, this alone does not justify summary judgment for the delivery company since it must also establish proximate cause.\n\nEducational objective: Criminal acts of third parties are a superseding cause unless the defendant had reason to know that his/her negligence would increase the risk that such acts would occur.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 448 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a criminal act of a third party constitutes a superseding cause in a negligence action).","educationalObjective":"Criminal acts of third parties are a superseding cause unless the defendant had reason to know that his/her negligence would increase the risk that such acts would occur.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 448 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a criminal act of a third party constitutes a superseding cause in a negligence action).","rule":"Criminal acts of third parties are a superseding cause unless the defendant had reason to know that his/her negligence would increase the risk that such acts would occur.","irac":{"issue":"On cross-motions for summary judgment on the claim for the goods, how should the court rule?","rule":"Criminal acts of third parties are a superseding cause unless the defendant had reason to know that his/her negligence would increase the risk that such acts would occur.","analysis":"Summary judgment should be granted when (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact and (2) no reasonable jury could find for the opposing party—thereby entitling the movant to judgment as a matter of law. Such motions are often brought in negligence claims, which require proof of the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. The causation element consists of two prongs: actual cause – the plaintiff's harm would not have occurred but for the defendant's negligence and\n\nproximate cause – the plaintiff's harm was a foreseeable consequence of the defendant's negligence.\n\nThe proximate-cause prong cannot be satisfied if there was a superseding cause—ie, an unforeseeable act or force that (1) occurred after the defendant's negligence and (2) contributed to the plaintiff's harm. And though criminal acts of third parties are generally unforeseeable, these acts are not superseding causes if the defendant had reason to know that his/her conduct would increase the risk that such acts would occur. Here, the construction company admitted liability for the damaged truck but argued that it was not liable for the stolen goods because the theft was a superseding cause. Since the construction site was in a high-crime neighborhood with many prior thefts, it is possible (but not certain) that the construction company should have known that its negligence could create an opportunity for a third party to commit a crime. As a result, there is a genuine issue of material fact that warrants the denial of both motions (Choices B & C). (Choice D) The goods would not have been stolen from the disabled truck but for the construction company's negligence (actual cause). However, this alone does not justify summary judgment for the delivery company since it must also establish proximate cause.","conclusion":"Answer A: Deny both motions, because there is evidence to support a finding that the construction company should have realized that its negligence could create an opportunity for a third party to commit a crime."},"questionZh":"一家建筑公司在治安较差的街区挖掘一条新的污水管道沟渠。\n\n施工期间，工地上的工具和设备屡遭盗窃。\n\n一天晚上，建筑公司的员工疏忽大意，没有在沟渠周围安装警示灯。\n\n一辆送货卡车驶入沟渠，导致车轴断裂。\n\n卡车司机正在寻求帮助时，窃贼撬开车门，盗走了价值 35 万美元的货物。\n\n送货公司起诉建筑公司，要求赔偿 35 万美元的损失以及卡车的损坏。\n\n建筑公司承认其疏忽大意，未在沟渠周围安装警示灯，并承认对卡车的损坏负有责任，但否认对货物的损失负有责任。\n\n双方就货物损失的索赔提出简易判决动议，法院应如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回双方的动议，因为有证据表明，建筑公司本应意识到其疏忽可能为第三方犯罪创造机会。","B":"准许建筑公司的动议，因为无人能够预见未安装警示灯会导致贵重货物的损失。","C":"准许建筑公司的动议，因为第三方的犯罪行为是造成损失的替代原因。","D":"准许运输公司的动议，因为若非建筑公司的行为，货物就不会被盗。"},"explanationZh":"当满足以下条件时，法院应作出简易判决：(1) 不存在实质性事实争议，且 (2) 理性的陪审团不可能作出有利于对方当事人的裁决——从而使动议方有权获得法律上的胜诉判决。\n\n此类动议常出现于过失侵权诉讼中，该类诉讼要求证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系及损害后果。\n\n因果关系要素包含两个方面：事实因果关系（即若无被告的过失，原告的损害便不会发生）以及 法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（即原告遭受的损害是被告过失行为可预见的后果）。\n\n若存在“阻断因果关系的介入原因”（superseding cause）——即一种不可预见的、(1) 发生于被告过失行为之后且 (2) 促成了原告损害后果的行为或力量——则无法满足法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）要件。\n\n尽管第三方的犯罪行为通常被视为不可预见，但如果被告有理由知晓其行为会增加此类犯罪发生的风险，则该犯罪行为不构成阻断因果关系的介入原因。\n\n在本案中，建筑公司承认对受损卡车负有责任，但辩称不对被盗货物负责，理由是盗窃行为属于阻断因果关系的介入原因。\n\n鉴于施工现场位于犯罪率高且此前发生过多次盗窃案的街区，建筑公司完全可能（尽管不确定）应当预见到其过失行为可能为第三方实施犯罪提供机会。\n\n因此，本案存在实质性事实争议，法院应驳回双方的动议（选项 B 和 C）。\n\n(选项 D) 若无建筑公司的过失，货物便不会从故障卡车上被盗（事实因果关系）。\n\n然而，仅凭这一点不足以支持法院作出有利于运输公司的简易判决，因为运输公司还必须证明存在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）关系。\n\n教学目标：第三方的犯罪行为构成阻断因果关系的介入原因，除非被告有理由知晓其过失行为会增加此类犯罪发生的风险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 448 条 (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 448) (美国法律协会，1965 年)（阐述了在过失侵权诉讼中，第三方的犯罪行为何时构成阻断因果关系的介入原因）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非被告有理由知道其疏忽会增加此类犯罪行为发生的风险，否则第三方的犯罪行为是替代原因。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于双方就货物索赔提出的简易判决动议，法院应如何裁决？","rule":"除非被告有理由知道其疏忽会增加此类犯罪行为发生的风险，否则第三方的犯罪行为是替代原因。","analysis":"当满足以下条件时，法院应作出简易判决：(1) 不存在实质性事实争议，且 (2) 理性的陪审团不可能作出有利于对方当事人的裁决——从而使动议方有权获得法律上的胜诉判决。\n\n此类动议常出现于过失侵权诉讼中，该类诉讼要求证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系及损害后果。\n\n因果关系要素包含两个方面：事实因果关系（即若无被告的过失，原告的损害便不会发生）以及 法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（即原告遭受的损害是被告过失行为可预见的后果）。\n\n若存在“阻断因果关系的介入原因”（superseding cause）——即一种不可预见的、(1) 发生于被告过失行为之后且 (2) 促成了原告损害后果的行为或力量——则无法满足法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）要件。\n\n尽管第三方的犯罪行为通常被视为不可预见，但如果被告有理由知晓其行为会增加此类犯罪发生的风险，则该犯罪行为不构成阻断因果关系的介入原因。\n\n在本案中，建筑公司承认对受损卡车负有责任，但辩称不对被盗货物负责，理由是盗窃行为属于阻断因果关系的介入原因。\n\n鉴于施工现场位于犯罪率高且此前发生过多次盗窃案的街区，建筑公司完全可能（尽管不确定）应当预见到其过失行为可能为第三方实施犯罪提供机会。\n\n因此，本案存在实质性事实争议，法院应驳回双方的动议（选项 B 和 C）。\n\n(选项 D) 若无建筑公司的过失，货物便不会从故障卡车上被盗（事实因果关系）。\n\n然而，仅凭这一点不足以支持法院作出有利于运输公司的简易判决，因为运输公司还必须证明存在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）关系。\n\n教学目标：第三方的犯罪行为构成阻断因果关系的介入原因，除非被告有理由知晓其过失行为会增加此类犯罪发生的风险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 448 条 (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 448) (美国法律协会，1965 年)（阐述了在过失侵权诉讼中，第三方的犯罪行为何时构成阻断因果关系的介入原因）。","conclusion":"答案 A：驳回双方的动议，因为有证据表明，建筑公司本应意识到其疏忽可能为第三方犯罪创造机会。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"superseding cause","ipa":"/sˌupɝsˈidɪŋ kˈɑz/","syllables":"su·per·sed·ing cause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"替代原因：不可预见且足以切断被告法律近因的介入事件","contextualMeaning":"替代原因：不可预见且足以切断被告法律近因的介入事件","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Grant the construction company's motion, because the criminal acts of third persons were a superseding cause of the loss.","synonyms":["superseding intervening cause"]},{"term":"proximate cause","ipa":"/pɹˈɑksəmət kˈɑz/","syllables":"prox·i·mate cause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"法律近因：将责任限制于被告行为所产生的可预见风险范围","contextualMeaning":"法律近因：将责任限制于被告行为所产生的可预见风险范围","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The causation element consists of two prongs: actual cause – the plaintiff's harm would not have occurred but for the defendant's negligence and proximate cause – the plaintiff's harm was a foreseeable consequence of the defendant's negligence.","synonyms":["legal cause","scope-of-liability cause"]},{"term":"actual cause","ipa":"/ˈæktʃəwəl kˈɑz/","syllables":"ac·tual cause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"事实因果关系：被告行为在事实上造成了损害","contextualMeaning":"事实因果关系：被告行为在事实上造成了损害","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The causation element consists of two prongs: actual cause – the plaintiff's harm would not have occurred but for the defendant's negligence and proximate cause – the plaintiff's harm was a foreseeable consequence of the defendant's negligence.","synonyms":["cause in fact","factual cause"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-3ba457c8b68ee605"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":58,"sourceQuestionPage":175,"sourceQuestionPages":[175],"sourceExplanationPages":[176,177],"sourceAnswerPages":[176,177],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-059","subject":"Torts","topic":"Privileges & Defenses","question":"A man and his friend, who were both adults, went to a party. The man and the friend had many drinks at the party and became legally intoxicated. They decided to play a game of chance called \"Russian roulette\" using a gun loaded with one bullet. As part of the game, the man pointed the gun at the friend and, on her command, pulled the trigger. The man shot the friend in the shoulder. The friend has brought a negligence action against the man. Traditional defenses based on the plaintiff's conduct apply. What is likely to be the dispositive issue in this case?","choices":{"A":"Whether the friend could validly consent to the game.","B":"Whether the friend was also negligent.","C":"Whether the game constituted a joint venture.","D":"Whether the man was legally intoxicated when he began playing the game."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Contributory neligence\n\n(defense to negligence)\n\nTraditionalcontributorynegligence Plaintiff's negligence completely bars jurisdiction recovery\n\nPurecomparativenegligencejurisdiction* Plaintiff's negligence reduces recovery by plaintiff's proportionate fault (eg, plaintiff 90% at fault can recover 10% of damages)\n\n*Default rule on MBE.\n\nIn traditional common law jurisdictions (as seen here), contributory negligence is a complete defense to negligence when the plaintiff's failure to use reasonable care contributed to his/her injury. Here, the man negligently shot his friend during a game of Russian roulette. But the friend cannot recover from the man if she was contributorily negligent in becoming intoxicated at the party and agreeing to play the game. Therefore, whether the friend was also negligent is likely to be the dispositive issue in her negligence action against the man. (Choice A) Consent may be a defense to negligence if the plaintiff assumed the risk of harm. Here, the friend did not assent to a liability waiver (no express assumption). And since the friend was intoxicated, she may not have understood and voluntarily accepted the risk of Russian roulette (no implied assumption). As a result, the friend's case is more likely to turn on whether she was contributorily negligent. (Choice C) When multiple tortfeasors participate in a joint venture (eg, playing Russian roulette together), they are jointly and severally liable for any harm caused to nonparticipants. But the existence of a joint venture is irrelevant when the participants harm each other. (Choice D) Voluntarily intoxicated persons are held to the same \"reasonable person\" standard as sober persons in negligence actions, so the man's intoxication would not shield him from liability in this case.\n\nEducational objective: Under traditional common law rules, contributory negligence is a complete defense to negligence if the plaintiff's failure to use reasonable care contributed to his/her harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 467 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a plaintiff's contributory negligence is a complete bar to recovery for negligence).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 496D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that implied assumption of the risk is only a defense if the plaintiff understood the unreasonable character of the risk).","educationalObjective":"Under traditional common law rules, contributory negligence is a complete defense to negligence if the plaintiff's failure to use reasonable care contributed to his/her harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 467 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a plaintiff's contributory negligence is a complete bar to recovery for negligence).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 496D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that implied assumption of the risk is only a defense if the plaintiff understood the unreasonable character of the risk).","rule":"Under traditional common law rules, contributory negligence is a complete defense to negligence if the plaintiff's failure to use reasonable care contributed to his/her harm.","irac":{"issue":"What is likely to be the dispositive issue in this case?","rule":"Under traditional common law rules, contributory negligence is a complete defense to negligence if the plaintiff's failure to use reasonable care contributed to his/her harm.","analysis":"Contributory neligence\n\n(defense to negligence)\n\nTraditionalcontributorynegligence Plaintiff's negligence completely bars jurisdiction recovery\n\nPurecomparativenegligencejurisdiction* Plaintiff's negligence reduces recovery by plaintiff's proportionate fault (eg, plaintiff 90% at fault can recover 10% of damages)\n\n*Default rule on MBE.\n\nIn traditional common law jurisdictions (as seen here), contributory negligence is a complete defense to negligence when the plaintiff's failure to use reasonable care contributed to his/her injury. Here, the man negligently shot his friend during a game of Russian roulette. But the friend cannot recover from the man if she was contributorily negligent in becoming intoxicated at the party and agreeing to play the game. Therefore, whether the friend was also negligent is likely to be the dispositive issue in her negligence action against the man. (Choice A) Consent may be a defense to negligence if the plaintiff assumed the risk of harm. Here, the friend did not assent to a liability waiver (no express assumption). And since the friend was intoxicated, she may not have understood and voluntarily accepted the risk of Russian roulette (no implied assumption). As a result, the friend's case is more likely to turn on whether she was contributorily negligent. (Choice C) When multiple tortfeasors participate in a joint venture (eg, playing Russian roulette together), they are jointly and severally liable for any harm caused to nonparticipants. But the existence of a joint venture is irrelevant when the participants harm each other. (Choice D) Voluntarily intoxicated persons are held to the same \"reasonable person\" standard as sober persons in negligence actions, so the man's intoxication would not shield him from liability in this case.","conclusion":"Answer B: Whether the friend was also negligent."},"questionZh":"一名男子和他的朋友（均为成年人）去参加了一个聚会。\n\n两人在聚会上饮酒过量，达到了法律规定的醉酒状态。\n\n他们决定玩一种名为“俄罗斯轮盘赌”的博弈游戏，使用了一把装有一发子弹的枪。\n\n游戏过程中，该男子将枪口指向朋友，并在对方的指令下扣动了扳机，结果击中了朋友的肩膀。\n\n朋友随后对该男子提起了过失诉讼。\n\n本案适用基于原告行为的传统抗辩理由。\n\n本案的决定性争议焦点可能是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"朋友是否能有效同意参与该游戏。","B":"朋友是否存在过失。","C":"该游戏是否构成一种共同冒险（joint venture）关系。","D":"该男子在开始游戏时是否处于法律规定的醉酒状态。"},"explanationZh":"与有过失（contributory negligence）（Contributory negligence） （针对过失侵权的抗辩） 传统与有过失（contributory negligence）法域：原告的过失完全阻却其获得赔偿。 纯粹比较过失法域*：原告的过失按其过错比例扣减赔偿额（例如，原告承担90%的过错责任，则只能获得10%的损害赔偿）。\n\n*MBE（多州律师资格考试）中的默认规则。 在传统普通法法域（如本案情形）中，如果原告未能尽到合理注意义务并导致了自身受损，那么与有过失（contributory negligence）便构成针对过失侵权指控的完全抗辩。\n\n本案中，该男子在玩“俄罗斯轮盘赌”时因疏忽大意开枪击伤了其朋友。\n\n然而，如果该朋友因在聚会上饮酒致醉并同意参与该游戏而存在与有过失（contributory negligence），她便无法向该男子寻求赔偿。\n\n因此，该朋友针对该男子提起的过失侵权诉讼中，决定性问题很可能在于她本人是否存在过失。\n\n（选项 A）如果原告承担了损害风险，则“同意”可能构成针对过失侵权的抗辩。\n\n本案中，该朋友并未签署免责声明（无明示承担风险）。\n\n此外，由于该朋友处于醉酒状态，她可能并未理解并自愿接受玩“俄罗斯轮盘赌”的风险（无默示承担风险）。\n\n因此，该朋友的案件结果更有可能取决于她是否存在与有过失（contributory negligence）。\n\n（选项 C）当多个侵权行为人参与共同冒险活动（例如一起玩“俄罗斯轮盘赌”）时，他们对非参与者造成的任何损害承担连带责任。\n\n但在参与者相互造成损害的情况下，是否存在共同冒险活动则无关紧要。\n\n（选项 D）在过失侵权诉讼中，自愿饮酒致醉者与清醒者适用同样的“理性人”标准，因此该男子的醉酒状态并不能使其免除本案中的责任。\n\n教学目标：根据传统普通法规则，如果原告未能尽到合理注意义务并导致了自身受损，与有过失（contributory negligence）即构成针对过失侵权的完全抗辩。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第467条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）完全阻却其因过失侵权获得赔偿）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第496D条（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明：仅当原告理解该风险具有不合理性质时，默示承担风险方可构成抗辩）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据传统的普通法规则，如果原告未能尽到合理的注意义务，且这种疏忽促成了其自身受到的伤害，那么“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）便构成针对过失指控的完全抗辩理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"本案的决定性争议焦点可能是什么？","rule":"根据传统的普通法规则，如果原告未能尽到合理的注意义务，且这种疏忽促成了其自身受到的伤害，那么“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）便构成针对过失指控的完全抗辩理由。","analysis":"与有过失（contributory negligence）（Contributory negligence） （针对过失侵权的抗辩） 传统与有过失（contributory negligence）法域：原告的过失完全阻却其获得赔偿。 纯粹比较过失法域*：原告的过失按其过错比例扣减赔偿额（例如，原告承担90%的过错责任，则只能获得10%的损害赔偿）。\n\n*MBE（多州律师资格考试）中的默认规则。 在传统普通法法域（如本案情形）中，如果原告未能尽到合理注意义务并导致了自身受损，那么与有过失（contributory negligence）便构成针对过失侵权指控的完全抗辩。\n\n本案中，该男子在玩“俄罗斯轮盘赌”时因疏忽大意开枪击伤了其朋友。\n\n然而，如果该朋友因在聚会上饮酒致醉并同意参与该游戏而存在与有过失（contributory negligence），她便无法向该男子寻求赔偿。\n\n因此，该朋友针对该男子提起的过失侵权诉讼中，决定性问题很可能在于她本人是否存在过失。\n\n（选项 A）如果原告承担了损害风险，则“同意”可能构成针对过失侵权的抗辩。\n\n本案中，该朋友并未签署免责声明（无明示承担风险）。\n\n此外，由于该朋友处于醉酒状态，她可能并未理解并自愿接受玩“俄罗斯轮盘赌”的风险（无默示承担风险）。\n\n因此，该朋友的案件结果更有可能取决于她是否存在与有过失（contributory negligence）。\n\n（选项 C）当多个侵权行为人参与共同冒险活动（例如一起玩“俄罗斯轮盘赌”）时，他们对非参与者造成的任何损害承担连带责任。\n\n但在参与者相互造成损害的情况下，是否存在共同冒险活动则无关紧要。\n\n（选项 D）在过失侵权诉讼中，自愿饮酒致醉者与清醒者适用同样的“理性人”标准，因此该男子的醉酒状态并不能使其免除本案中的责任。\n\n教学目标：根据传统普通法规则，如果原告未能尽到合理注意义务并导致了自身受损，与有过失（contributory negligence）即构成针对过失侵权的完全抗辩。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第467条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）完全阻却其因过失侵权获得赔偿）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第496D条（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明：仅当原告理解该风险具有不合理性质时，默示承担风险方可构成抗辩）。","conclusion":"答案 B：朋友是否存在过失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"tortfeasors","ipa":"/'tɔ:t'fi:zə, -zɔ:/","syllables":"tort·fea·sors","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"(tortfeasor 的复数) n. 犯侵权行为者","contextualMeaning":"侵权行为人：实施侵权并可能承担责任的人","wordForm":"词；原形 tortfeasor；词形 0:tortfeasor/1:s","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) When multiple tortfeasors participate in a joint venture (eg, playing Russian roulette together), they are jointly and severally liable for any harm caused to nonparticipants.","synonyms":["wrongdoer","tort actor"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":59,"sourceQuestionPage":178,"sourceQuestionPages":[178],"sourceExplanationPages":[179,180],"sourceAnswerPages":[179,180],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-060","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A candidate for state senate campaigned by going door-to-door throughout his community. One day, the candidate visited the home of his wife's childhood friend. The friend was having an in-ground swimming pool installed in her backyard. After digging a deep hole, the pool contractor had temporarily suspended work due to a heavy rainstorm and had left the site without installing safety fencing around the hole. As the candidate and the friend were talking, they saw the friend's five-year-old child fall into the hole. The candidate ran outside, jumped into the hole, and pushed the child to safety. However, the candidate fell backward into the hole and lay submerged for several minutes in standing water left by the rainstorm. He was rushed to the hospital where he lingered in and out of consciousness for two months and then died. In a statement to the press, the pool contractor falsely asserted that the candidate and the friend were at fault because they were \"carrying on an extramarital affair instead of supervising the child.\" The personal representative of the candidate's estate has brought a survival action against the pool contractor and seeks to recover damages for the candidate's medical expenses, loss of income, and pain and suffering, as well as for harm to the candidate's reputation as a result of defamation. For which of the following is the personal representative LEAST likely to recover?","choices":{"A":"Loss of income.","B":"Medical expenses.","C":"Pain and suffering.","D":"Reputational harm."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Civil actions for tortious death\n\nType of Proper plaintiff Recoverable damages* action\n\nSurvival Decedent's personal Damages suffered by decedent prior to representative death Eg, medical expenses, pain & suffering Wrongful Decedent's spouse, next of Damages suffered by spouse or next of kin death kin, or personal as a result of decedent's death (no pain & representative suffering) Eg, loss of income, loss of companionship *If jurisdiction allows both actions, double recovery not permitted.\n\nAt common law, a tort action was extinguished upon the death of either the tortfeasor or the victim. But most states today have enacted survival statutes that enable the personal representative of a decedent's estate to pursue any claims the decedent would have had at the time of death. This includes claims for damages resulting from both personal injury and property damage, such as: the decedent's medical expenses from the time of injury to the time of death (Choice B) the decedent's loss of income during this time (Choice A) and the pain and suffering the decedent experienced as a result of his/her injury (Choice C). However, most states do not allow survival of tort actions involving intangible personal interests—eg, defamation, invasion of privacy. The reason is that these actions are considered too personal to survive the decedent's death. Therefore, the candidate's personal representative is least likely to recover for reputational harm resulting from defamation.\n\nEducational objective: The personal representative of a decedent's estate can bring a survival action to pursue claims the decedent would have had at the time of death, including claims for damages resulting from personal injury or property damage. But most states do not allow survival of tort actions involving intangible personal interests (eg, defamation).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 926 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (survival actions in tort).","educationalObjective":"The personal representative of a decedent's estate can bring a survival action to pursue claims the decedent would have had at the time of death, including claims for damages resulting from personal injury or property damage. But most states do not allow survival of tort actions involving intangible personal interests (eg, defamation).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 926 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (survival actions in tort).","rule":"The personal representative of a decedent's estate can bring a survival action to pursue claims the decedent would have had at the time of death, including claims for damages resulting from personal injury or property damage. But most states do not allow survival of tort actions involving intangible personal interests (eg, defamation).","irac":{"issue":"For which of the following is the personal representative LEAST likely to recover?","rule":"The personal representative of a decedent's estate can bring a survival action to pursue claims the decedent would have had at the time of death, including claims for damages resulting from personal injury or property damage. But most states do not allow survival of tort actions involving intangible personal interests (eg, defamation).","analysis":"Civil actions for tortious death\n\nType of Proper plaintiff Recoverable damages* action\n\nSurvival Decedent's personal Damages suffered by decedent prior to representative death Eg, medical expenses, pain & suffering Wrongful Decedent's spouse, next of Damages suffered by spouse or next of kin death kin, or personal as a result of decedent's death (no pain & representative suffering) Eg, loss of income, loss of companionship *If jurisdiction allows both actions, double recovery not permitted.\n\nAt common law, a tort action was extinguished upon the death of either the tortfeasor or the victim. But most states today have enacted survival statutes that enable the personal representative of a decedent's estate to pursue any claims the decedent would have had at the time of death. This includes claims for damages resulting from both personal injury and property damage, such as: the decedent's medical expenses from the time of injury to the time of death (Choice B) the decedent's loss of income during this time (Choice A) and the pain and suffering the decedent experienced as a result of his/her injury (Choice C). However, most states do not allow survival of tort actions involving intangible personal interests—eg, defamation, invasion of privacy. The reason is that these actions are considered too personal to survive the decedent's death. Therefore, the candidate's personal representative is least likely to recover for reputational harm resulting from defamation.","conclusion":"Answer D: Reputational harm."},"questionZh":"一位州参议院候选人在其选区内挨家挨户进行竞选活动。\n\n一天，该候选人拜访了他妻子的一位儿时好友。\n\n这位朋友当时正在后院安装一个嵌入式游泳池。\n\n泳池承包商在挖好深坑后，因遭遇暴雨暂时停工，且在离开现场时未在坑周围设置安全围栏。\n\n当候选人与朋友交谈时，他们目睹了朋友五岁的孩子跌入坑中。\n\n候选人冲到外面，跳入坑中，将孩子推到了安全地带。\n\n然而，候选人自己向后跌入坑中，并因暴雨留下的积水而浸没其中长达数分钟。\n\n他被紧急送往医院，在昏迷与清醒交替的状态下挣扎了两个月后去世。\n\n泳池承包商在向媒体发表声明时谎称，候选人和那位朋友有过错，因为他们当时“正在搞婚外情，而没有照看孩子”。\n\n候选人遗产的个人代表针对泳池承包商提起了“生存诉讼”（survival action），寻求就候选人的医疗费用、收入损失、痛苦与折磨，以及因诽谤造成的名誉损害获得赔偿。\n\n以下哪一项是个人代表**最不可能**获得赔偿的？","choicesZh":{"A":"收入损失。","B":"医疗费用。","C":"痛苦与折磨。","D":"名誉损害。"},"explanationZh":"因侵权致死引发的民事诉讼 诉讼类型 | 合格原告 | 可获赔偿* **生存诉讼** (Survival action) | 死者的遗产管理人（或遗嘱执行人） | 死者生前遭受的损害（例如：医疗费、痛苦与折磨） **不当致死诉讼** (Wrongful death action) | 死者的配偶、近亲属或遗产管理人 | 配偶或近亲属因死者死亡而遭受的损害（不包括痛苦与折磨）（例如：收入损失、丧失陪伴） *如果司法管辖区允许同时提起这两种诉讼，则不允许双重赔偿。\n\n在普通法下，侵权诉讼会因侵权行为人或受害人中任何一方的死亡而终止。\n\n但如今大多数州都制定了“生存法规”（survival statutes），允许死者遗产的管理人（或遗嘱执行人）继续追索死者生前本可提出的任何索赔。\n\n这包括因人身伤害和财产损失而产生的损害赔偿请求，例如：死者从受伤到死亡期间产生的医疗费（选项 B）、死者在此期间的收入损失（选项 A）以及死者因受伤而遭受的痛苦与折磨（选项 C）。\n\n然而，大多数州不允许涉及无形个人权益（如诽谤、侵犯隐私）的侵权诉讼在当事人死亡后继续进行。\n\n其原因是，这些诉讼被认为具有高度的人身专属性，不宜在死者死亡后继续存在。\n\n因此，死者的遗产管理人最不可能因诽谤造成的名誉损害而获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：死者遗产的管理人可以提起生存诉讼，追索死者生前本可提出的索赔，包括因人身伤害或财产损失而产生的损害赔偿请求。\n\n但大多数州不允许涉及无形个人权益（如诽谤）的侵权诉讼在当事人死亡后继续进行。\n\n参考文献 《合同法重述（第二次）》第 926 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（侵权法中的生存诉讼）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"死者遗产的个人代表可以提起生存诉讼，以主张死者在去世时本可享有的索赔权，包括因人身伤害或财产损失而产生的损害赔偿请求。\n\n但是，大多数州不允许涉及无形个人权益（例如诽谤）的侵权诉讼在死者去世后继续进行（即不承认此类诉讼的“生存性”）。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是个人代表**最不可能**获得赔偿的？","rule":"死者遗产的个人代表可以提起生存诉讼，以主张死者在去世时本可享有的索赔权，包括因人身伤害或财产损失而产生的损害赔偿请求。\n\n但是，大多数州不允许涉及无形个人权益（例如诽谤）的侵权诉讼在死者去世后继续进行（即不承认此类诉讼的“生存性”）。","analysis":"因侵权致死引发的民事诉讼 诉讼类型 | 合格原告 | 可获赔偿* **生存诉讼** (Survival action) | 死者的遗产管理人（或遗嘱执行人） | 死者生前遭受的损害（例如：医疗费、痛苦与折磨） **不当致死诉讼** (Wrongful death action) | 死者的配偶、近亲属或遗产管理人 | 配偶或近亲属因死者死亡而遭受的损害（不包括痛苦与折磨）（例如：收入损失、丧失陪伴） *如果司法管辖区允许同时提起这两种诉讼，则不允许双重赔偿。\n\n在普通法下，侵权诉讼会因侵权行为人或受害人中任何一方的死亡而终止。\n\n但如今大多数州都制定了“生存法规”（survival statutes），允许死者遗产的管理人（或遗嘱执行人）继续追索死者生前本可提出的任何索赔。\n\n这包括因人身伤害和财产损失而产生的损害赔偿请求，例如：死者从受伤到死亡期间产生的医疗费（选项 B）、死者在此期间的收入损失（选项 A）以及死者因受伤而遭受的痛苦与折磨（选项 C）。\n\n然而，大多数州不允许涉及无形个人权益（如诽谤、侵犯隐私）的侵权诉讼在当事人死亡后继续进行。\n\n其原因是，这些诉讼被认为具有高度的人身专属性，不宜在死者死亡后继续存在。\n\n因此，死者的遗产管理人最不可能因诽谤造成的名誉损害而获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：死者遗产的管理人可以提起生存诉讼，追索死者生前本可提出的索赔，包括因人身伤害或财产损失而产生的损害赔偿请求。\n\n但大多数州不允许涉及无形个人权益（如诽谤）的侵权诉讼在当事人死亡后继续进行。\n\n参考文献 《合同法重述（第二次）》第 926 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（侵权法中的生存诉讼）。","conclusion":"答案 D：名誉损害。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"survival action","ipa":"/sɝvˈaɪvəl ˈækʃən/","syllables":"sur·vival ac·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"权利存续之诉：死者生前已产生的请求权由遗产继续主张","contextualMeaning":"权利存续之诉：死者生前已产生的请求权由遗产继续主张","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"\" The personal representative of the candidate's estate has brought a survival action against the pool contractor and seeks to recover damages for the candidate's medical expenses, loss of income, and pain and suffering, as well as for harm to the candidate's reputation as a result of defamation.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":60,"sourceQuestionPage":181,"sourceQuestionPages":[181],"sourceExplanationPages":[182,183],"sourceAnswerPages":[182,183],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-061","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A driver negligently ran over a pedestrian. A bystander witnessed the accident from across the street. The bystander ran to the pedestrian, whom the bystander did not know, and administered first aid, but the pedestrian died in the bystander's arms. The bystander suffered serious emotional distress as a result of his failure to save the pedestrian's life, but he experienced no resulting physical manifestations. The bystander has brought a negligence action against the driver. Is the bystander likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the bystander assumed the risk.","B":"No, because the bystander had no familial or other preexisting relationship with the pedestrian.","C":"Yes, because danger invites rescue.","D":"Yes, because the bystander was in the zone of danger."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) has three theories of recovery: (1) zone of danger, (2) bystander, and (3) special situation. Liability under the bystander theory arises when: the defendant negligently causes serious bodily injury to a close relative of the plaintiff– bystander the plaintiff contemporaneously perceives (ie, sees or hears) that event and the plaintiff consequently suffers serious emotional distress. Here, the driver negligently caused the pedestrian serious bodily injury when the driver ran over the pedestrian. And though the bystander witnessed that accident, he will not prevail in an NIED claim because the pedestrian was a stranger—not the bystander's close relative. (Choice A) Assumption of risk is an affirmative defense to liability for negligence or recklessness when the plaintiff voluntarily exposed himself to a known or obvious risk of harm. Here, the bystander may have assumed the risk of emotional harm by voluntarily administering first aid to the pedestrian. But since the bystander cannot prove all the elements of an NIED claim, the merits of this defense are irrelevant. (Choice C) \"Danger invites rescue\" refers to the rescue doctrine, which applies when a defendant endangers a person and a rescuer attempts to aid that person. The defendant is liable for the rescuer's resulting physical harm. Although the bystander attempted to aid the pedestrian and suffered emotional distress, the bystander cannot recover under the rescue doctrine because he suffered no physical harm. (Choice D) Liability under the zone-of-danger theory of NIED arises when (1) the defendant's negligent conduct places the plaintiff in danger of immediate bodily harm and (2) that danger causes the plaintiff serious emotional distress. Here, the bystander cannot recover under this theory since he witnessed the accident from across the street and therefore was not in danger of immediate bodily harm.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant is liable for negligent infliction of emotional distress under the bystander theory when (1) the defendant negligently causes serious bodily injury to the plaintiff's close relative, (2) the plaintiff contemporaneously witnesses that event, and (3) the plaintiff consequently suffers serious emotional distress.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 48 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (bystander theory of recovery for negligent infliction of emotional distress).","educationalObjective":"A defendant is liable for negligent infliction of emotional distress under the bystander theory when (1) the defendant negligently causes serious bodily injury to the plaintiff's close relative, (2) the plaintiff contemporaneously witnesses that event, and (3) the plaintiff consequently suffers serious emotional distress.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 48 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (bystander theory of recovery for negligent infliction of emotional distress).","rule":"A defendant is liable for negligent infliction of emotional distress under the bystander theory when (1) the defendant negligently causes serious bodily injury to the plaintiff's close relative, (2) the plaintiff contemporaneously witnesses that event, and (3) the plaintiff consequently suffers serious emotional distress.","irac":{"issue":"Is the bystander likely to prevail?","rule":"A defendant is liable for negligent infliction of emotional distress under the bystander theory when (1) the defendant negligently causes serious bodily injury to the plaintiff's close relative, (2) the plaintiff contemporaneously witnesses that event, and (3) the plaintiff consequently suffers serious emotional distress.","analysis":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) has three theories of recovery: (1) zone of danger, (2) bystander, and (3) special situation. Liability under the bystander theory arises when: the defendant negligently causes serious bodily injury to a close relative of the plaintiff– bystander the plaintiff contemporaneously perceives (ie, sees or hears) that event and the plaintiff consequently suffers serious emotional distress. Here, the driver negligently caused the pedestrian serious bodily injury when the driver ran over the pedestrian. And though the bystander witnessed that accident, he will not prevail in an NIED claim because the pedestrian was a stranger—not the bystander's close relative. (Choice A) Assumption of risk is an affirmative defense to liability for negligence or recklessness when the plaintiff voluntarily exposed himself to a known or obvious risk of harm. Here, the bystander may have assumed the risk of emotional harm by voluntarily administering first aid to the pedestrian. But since the bystander cannot prove all the elements of an NIED claim, the merits of this defense are irrelevant. (Choice C) \"Danger invites rescue\" refers to the rescue doctrine, which applies when a defendant endangers a person and a rescuer attempts to aid that person. The defendant is liable for the rescuer's resulting physical harm. Although the bystander attempted to aid the pedestrian and suffered emotional distress, the bystander cannot recover under the rescue doctrine because he suffered no physical harm. (Choice D) Liability under the zone-of-danger theory of NIED arises when (1) the defendant's negligent conduct places the plaintiff in danger of immediate bodily harm and (2) that danger causes the plaintiff serious emotional distress. Here, the bystander cannot recover under this theory since he witnessed the accident from across the street and therefore was not in danger of immediate bodily harm.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the bystander had no familial or other preexisting relationship with the pedestrian."},"questionZh":"一名司机因疏忽撞倒了一名行人。\n\n一名旁观者在街对面目睹了这起事故。\n\n该旁观者跑向这名素不相识的行人并实施了急救，但行人最终死在了其怀中。\n\n旁观者因未能挽救行人的生命而遭受了严重的精神痛苦，但并未出现任何相应的身体症状。\n\n该旁观者针对司机提起了过失诉讼。\n\n该旁观者胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为该旁观者承担了风险。","B":"不大，因为该旁观者与该行人之间不存在亲属关系或其他既有关系。","C":"很大，因为“危险引发救援”（danger invites rescue）。","D":"很大，因为该旁观者处于危险区域内。"},"explanationZh":"过失导致精神痛苦（NIED）的索赔有三种理论依据：(1) 危险区域（zone of danger）理论，(2) 旁观者（bystander）理论，以及 (3) 特殊情形（special situation）理论。\n\n根据旁观者理论，若发生以下情况，被告需承担责任：被告因过失导致原告（即旁观者）的近亲属遭受严重身体伤害，原告同时目睹（即看见或听见）了该事件，并因此遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n在本案中，司机在碾压行人时因过失导致行人遭受严重身体伤害。\n\n尽管旁观者目睹了该事故，但他无法在 NIED 索赔中胜诉，因为该行人是陌生人，而非旁观者的近亲属。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”（assumption of risk）是针对过失或鲁莽行为责任的一种积极抗辩，适用于原告自愿置身于已知或明显的伤害风险之中的情形。\n\n在本案中，旁观者因自愿向行人提供急救，可能承担了遭受精神伤害的风险。\n\n但由于旁观者无法证明 NIED 索赔的所有构成要件，该抗辩是否成立已无关紧要。\n\n（选项 C）“危险招致救援”（danger invites rescue）指的是“救援原则”（rescue doctrine），该原则适用于被告使某人陷入危险，而救援者试图救助该人的情形；被告需对救援者因此遭受的身体伤害承担责任。\n\n尽管旁观者试图救助行人并遭受了精神痛苦，但他无法依据救援原则获得赔偿，因为他未遭受身体伤害。\n\n（选项 D）根据 NIED 的危险区域理论，若发生以下情况，被告需承担责任：(1) 被告的过失行为使原告面临即时身体伤害的危险，且 (2) 该危险导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n在本案中，旁观者无法依据该理论获得赔偿，因为他是从马路对面目睹事故的，并未面临即时身体伤害的危险。\n\n教育目标：根据旁观者理论，若发生以下情况，被告需对过失导致的精神痛苦承担责任：(1) 被告因过失导致原告的近亲属遭受严重身体伤害，(2) 原告同时目睹了该事件，以及 (3) 原告因此遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》\n\n针对身体与情感伤害 § 48 (美国法律协会 2012年)（因过失致使精神痛苦而主张赔偿的“旁观者理论”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“旁观者理论”（bystander theory），若要认定被告因过失造成精神痛苦（negligent infliction of emotional distress）而承担责任，须满足以下条件：(1) 被告过失导致原告的近亲属遭受严重身体伤害；(2) 原告同时目睹了该事件；(3) 原告因此遭受了严重的精神痛苦。","iracZh":{"issue":"该旁观者胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"根据“旁观者理论”（bystander theory），若要认定被告因过失造成精神痛苦（negligent infliction of emotional distress）而承担责任，须满足以下条件：(1) 被告过失导致原告的近亲属遭受严重身体伤害；(2) 原告同时目睹了该事件；(3) 原告因此遭受了严重的精神痛苦。","analysis":"过失导致精神痛苦（NIED）的索赔有三种理论依据：(1) 危险区域（zone of danger）理论，(2) 旁观者（bystander）理论，以及 (3) 特殊情形（special situation）理论。\n\n根据旁观者理论，若发生以下情况，被告需承担责任：被告因过失导致原告（即旁观者）的近亲属遭受严重身体伤害，原告同时目睹（即看见或听见）了该事件，并因此遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n在本案中，司机在碾压行人时因过失导致行人遭受严重身体伤害。\n\n尽管旁观者目睹了该事故，但他无法在 NIED 索赔中胜诉，因为该行人是陌生人，而非旁观者的近亲属。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”（assumption of risk）是针对过失或鲁莽行为责任的一种积极抗辩，适用于原告自愿置身于已知或明显的伤害风险之中的情形。\n\n在本案中，旁观者因自愿向行人提供急救，可能承担了遭受精神伤害的风险。\n\n但由于旁观者无法证明 NIED 索赔的所有构成要件，该抗辩是否成立已无关紧要。\n\n（选项 C）“危险招致救援”（danger invites rescue）指的是“救援原则”（rescue doctrine），该原则适用于被告使某人陷入危险，而救援者试图救助该人的情形；被告需对救援者因此遭受的身体伤害承担责任。\n\n尽管旁观者试图救助行人并遭受了精神痛苦，但他无法依据救援原则获得赔偿，因为他未遭受身体伤害。\n\n（选项 D）根据 NIED 的危险区域理论，若发生以下情况，被告需承担责任：(1) 被告的过失行为使原告面临即时身体伤害的危险，且 (2) 该危险导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n在本案中，旁观者无法依据该理论获得赔偿，因为他是从马路对面目睹事故的，并未面临即时身体伤害的危险。\n\n教育目标：根据旁观者理论，若发生以下情况，被告需对过失导致的精神痛苦承担责任：(1) 被告因过失导致原告的近亲属遭受严重身体伤害，(2) 原告同时目睹了该事件，以及 (3) 原告因此遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》\n\n针对身体与情感伤害 § 48 (美国法律协会 2012年)（因过失致使精神痛苦而主张赔偿的“旁观者理论”）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不大，因为该旁观者与该行人之间不存在亲属关系或其他既有关系。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-a1ff99e16d58710c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":61,"sourceQuestionPage":184,"sourceQuestionPages":[184],"sourceExplanationPages":[185,186],"sourceAnswerPages":[185,186],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-062","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A pregnant woman, whose due date for the delivery of her viable fetus was less than a month away, was walking in a parking lot and looking at her cell phone. She was hit by a car driven by a police officer, who had just received word of an emergency and carelessly failed to see the woman. Several days later, the woman gave birth to a child who suffered neurological damage as a result of the accident. The woman, on behalf of her child, brought a negligence suit against the police officer for damages associated with the physical injuries suffered by the child. The woman and the police officer were found to be equally at fault for the accident. The jurisdiction has adopted a modified comparative fault statute that bars a plaintiff from recovery against a defendant whose fault is less than or equal to that of the plaintiff. In the child's suit against the police officer, will the child be likely to recover for her injuries?","choices":{"A":"No, because the child was in utero at the time of the accident.","B":"No, because the firefighters' rule applies to police officers.","C":"Yes, because the child was viable at the time of the accident.","D":"Yes, because the woman was not at greater fault than the police officer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Scope of defendant's duty of care\n\nCardozo Duty owed only to persons who might be foreseeably harmed as a result of view defendant's negligence (ie, persons within zone of foreseeable harm)\n\n(majority rule)\n\nAndrews Duty owed to everyone on earth if anyone might be foreseeably harmed as a view result of defendant's negligence\n\n(minority rule)\n\nTo recover in a negligence action, the plaintiff must establish four elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. The majority rule is that the defendant owes a duty of care to the plaintiff only if the plaintiff is a member of the class of persons who might be foreseeably harmed as a result of the defendant's negligent conduct (sometimes called \"foreseeable plaintiffs\"). If a pregnant woman is a member of this class, then a duty is also owed to her unborn child—but only if the fetus was viable at the time the injury occurred. Here, the police officer owed a duty of care to the pregnant woman because it was foreseeable that someone in the parking lot might be harmed by the police officer's negligent driving. As a result, the police officer also owed a duty to the woman's child, who was unborn (ie, in utero) but viable at the time of the accident (Choice A). And since the police officer breached that duty by carelessly failing to see the woman and caused the child's neurological damage, the child can recover from the police officer for her injuries.* *The result would be the same under the minority rule (Andrews view) because the scope of duty under this rule is much broader—it extends to everyone if anyone might be foreseeably harmed. (Choice B) Although the firefighters' rule applies to police officers (and other emergency professionals), it does not excuse their negligence. Instead, it bars emergency professionals from recovering damages for injuries attributable to the special dangers of their job. (Choice D) The woman and the police officer were equally at fault for the accident. So had the woman been the plaintiff, she could not recover under this jurisdiction's modified comparative fault statute. But the plaintiff in this action is the child—not the woman. And since a negligent parent's fault is not imputed (ie, assigned) to a child plaintiff in a suit against a third party, the woman's relative fault is of no consequence here.\n\nEducational objective: Under the majority rule, a duty of care is owed to any person who might be foreseeably harmed by the defendant's negligent conduct. Additionally, any duty to a pregnant woman is also a duty to her unborn, viable child.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 281, cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that an actor can only be negligent toward the class of persons who might be foreseeably harmed by his/her conduct).","educationalObjective":"Under the majority rule, a duty of care is owed to any person who might be foreseeably harmed by the defendant's negligent conduct. Additionally, any duty to a pregnant woman is also a duty to her unborn, viable child.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 281, cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that an actor can only be negligent toward the class of persons who might be foreseeably harmed by his/her conduct).","rule":"Under the majority rule, a duty of care is owed to any person who might be foreseeably harmed by the defendant's negligent conduct. Additionally, any duty to a pregnant woman is also a duty to her unborn, viable child.","irac":{"issue":"In the child's suit against the police officer, will the child be likely to recover for her injuries?","rule":"Under the majority rule, a duty of care is owed to any person who might be foreseeably harmed by the defendant's negligent conduct. Additionally, any duty to a pregnant woman is also a duty to her unborn, viable child.","analysis":"Scope of defendant's duty of care\n\nCardozo Duty owed only to persons who might be foreseeably harmed as a result of view defendant's negligence (ie, persons within zone of foreseeable harm)\n\n(majority rule)\n\nAndrews Duty owed to everyone on earth if anyone might be foreseeably harmed as a view result of defendant's negligence\n\n(minority rule)\n\nTo recover in a negligence action, the plaintiff must establish four elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. The majority rule is that the defendant owes a duty of care to the plaintiff only if the plaintiff is a member of the class of persons who might be foreseeably harmed as a result of the defendant's negligent conduct (sometimes called \"foreseeable plaintiffs\"). If a pregnant woman is a member of this class, then a duty is also owed to her unborn child—but only if the fetus was viable at the time the injury occurred. Here, the police officer owed a duty of care to the pregnant woman because it was foreseeable that someone in the parking lot might be harmed by the police officer's negligent driving. As a result, the police officer also owed a duty to the woman's child, who was unborn (ie, in utero) but viable at the time of the accident (Choice A). And since the police officer breached that duty by carelessly failing to see the woman and caused the child's neurological damage, the child can recover from the police officer for her injuries.* *The result would be the same under the minority rule (Andrews view) because the scope of duty under this rule is much broader—it extends to everyone if anyone might be foreseeably harmed. (Choice B) Although the firefighters' rule applies to police officers (and other emergency professionals), it does not excuse their negligence. Instead, it bars emergency professionals from recovering damages for injuries attributable to the special dangers of their job. (Choice D) The woman and the police officer were equally at fault for the accident. So had the woman been the plaintiff, she could not recover under this jurisdiction's modified comparative fault statute. But the plaintiff in this action is the child—not the woman. And since a negligent parent's fault is not imputed (ie, assigned) to a child plaintiff in a suit against a third party, the woman's relative fault is of no consequence here.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the child was viable at the time of the accident."},"questionZh":"一名孕妇在停车场行走时查看手机，此时距离她怀中胎儿（已达存活能力阶段）的预产期已不足一个月。\n\n她被一名警官驾驶的车辆撞倒；该警官当时刚接到紧急任务通知，因疏忽大意未注意到这名女子。\n\n几天后，该女子产下一名婴儿，婴儿因事故遭受了神经损伤。\n\n该女子代表其子女对该警官提起过失诉讼，要求赔偿子女所受身体伤害相关的损失。\n\n经认定，该女子与警官对事故负有同等责任。\n\n该司法管辖区采用修正后的比较过失法规，规定若被告的过失程度小于或等于原告的过失程度，原告即无权向被告获得赔偿。\n\n在子女起诉警官的案件中，该子女是否很可能获得伤害赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为事故发生时该子女尚在母体内。","B":"不会，因为“消防员规则”（firefighters' rule）同样适用于警官。","C":"会，因为事故发生时该胎儿已达存活能力阶段。","D":"会，因为该女子的过失程度并未超过该警官。"},"explanationZh":"被告注意义务的范围 Cardozo 观点：仅对因被告疏忽而可能遭受可预见伤害的人负有注意义务（即处于可预见伤害范围内的人） （多数派规则） Andrews 观点：只要有人可能因被告疏忽而遭受可预见伤害，被告即对世上所有人负有注意义务 （少数派规则） 在过失诉讼中，原告必须确立四个要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系和损害后果。\n\n多数派规则认为，只有当原告属于因被告疏忽行为而可能遭受可预见伤害的人群（有时称为“可预见的受害者”）时，被告才对原告负有注意义务。\n\n如果孕妇属于该人群，则被告也对她未出生的孩子负有注意义务——但前提是胎儿在受伤时已具备生存能力（viable）。\n\n在本案中，警官对该孕妇负有注意义务，因为可以预见停车场内的人员可能因警官的疏忽驾驶而受到伤害。\n\n因此，警官也对该女子的孩子负有注意义务，尽管该孩子在事故发生时尚未出生（即在子宫内），但已具备生存能力（选项 A）。\n\n由于警官因疏忽未注意到该女子从而违反了该义务，并导致孩子遭受神经损伤，因此孩子可以就其伤害向警官寻求赔偿。* *根据少数派规则（Andrews 观点），结果也是一样的，因为该规则下的义务范围要宽泛得多——只要有人可能遭受可预见伤害，义务就延伸至所有人。\n\n（选项 B）尽管“消防员规则”（firefighters' rule）适用于警官（及其他紧急救援专业人员），但这并不免除他们的过失责任。\n\n相反，该规则禁止紧急救援专业人员就因其工作特殊危险性而造成的伤害寻求损害赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）该女子和警官对事故负有同等过错。\n\n因此，如果该女子作为原告，根据该司法管辖区的修正比较过错法规，她将无法获得赔偿。\n\n但本案的原告是孩子，而不是该女子。\n\n此外，由于在针对第三方的诉讼中，疏忽大意的父母的过错不会被归责于（即转嫁给）作为原告的子女，因此该名妇女的相对过错在此不产生法律后果。\n\n教育目标：根据多数派规则，对于任何可能因被告的疏忽行为而遭受可预见伤害的人，被告均负有注意义务。\n\n此外，对孕妇负有的注意义务同时也适用于其腹中已具备生存能力的胎儿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第281条，评注c（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明行为人仅对那些可能因其行为而遭受可预见伤害的人群负有注意义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据多数派规则，对于任何可能因被告过失行为而遭受可预见伤害的人，被告均负有注意义务。\n\n此外，对孕妇负有的注意义务同时也适用于其腹中已达存活能力阶段的胎儿。","iracZh":{"issue":"在子女起诉警官的案件中，该子女是否很可能获得伤害赔偿？","rule":"根据多数派规则，对于任何可能因被告过失行为而遭受可预见伤害的人，被告均负有注意义务。\n\n此外，对孕妇负有的注意义务同时也适用于其腹中已达存活能力阶段的胎儿。","analysis":"被告注意义务的范围 Cardozo 观点：仅对因被告疏忽而可能遭受可预见伤害的人负有注意义务（即处于可预见伤害范围内的人） （多数派规则） Andrews 观点：只要有人可能因被告疏忽而遭受可预见伤害，被告即对世上所有人负有注意义务 （少数派规则） 在过失诉讼中，原告必须确立四个要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系和损害后果。\n\n多数派规则认为，只有当原告属于因被告疏忽行为而可能遭受可预见伤害的人群（有时称为“可预见的受害者”）时，被告才对原告负有注意义务。\n\n如果孕妇属于该人群，则被告也对她未出生的孩子负有注意义务——但前提是胎儿在受伤时已具备生存能力（viable）。\n\n在本案中，警官对该孕妇负有注意义务，因为可以预见停车场内的人员可能因警官的疏忽驾驶而受到伤害。\n\n因此，警官也对该女子的孩子负有注意义务，尽管该孩子在事故发生时尚未出生（即在子宫内），但已具备生存能力（选项 A）。\n\n由于警官因疏忽未注意到该女子从而违反了该义务，并导致孩子遭受神经损伤，因此孩子可以就其伤害向警官寻求赔偿。* *根据少数派规则（Andrews 观点），结果也是一样的，因为该规则下的义务范围要宽泛得多——只要有人可能遭受可预见伤害，义务就延伸至所有人。\n\n（选项 B）尽管“消防员规则”（firefighters' rule）适用于警官（及其他紧急救援专业人员），但这并不免除他们的过失责任。\n\n相反，该规则禁止紧急救援专业人员就因其工作特殊危险性而造成的伤害寻求损害赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）该女子和警官对事故负有同等过错。\n\n因此，如果该女子作为原告，根据该司法管辖区的修正比较过错法规，她将无法获得赔偿。\n\n但本案的原告是孩子，而不是该女子。\n\n此外，由于在针对第三方的诉讼中，疏忽大意的父母的过错不会被归责于（即转嫁给）作为原告的子女，因此该名妇女的相对过错在此不产生法律后果。\n\n教育目标：根据多数派规则，对于任何可能因被告的疏忽行为而遭受可预见伤害的人，被告均负有注意义务。\n\n此外，对孕妇负有的注意义务同时也适用于其腹中已具备生存能力的胎儿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第281条，评注c（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明行为人仅对那些可能因其行为而遭受可预见伤害的人群负有注意义务）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，因为事故发生时该胎儿已达存活能力阶段。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"foreseeable plaintiff","ipa":"/fɔɹsˈiəbəl plˈeɪntəf/","syllables":"fore·see·able plain·tiff","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"可预见原告：处于被告行为可预见危险范围内的人","contextualMeaning":"可预见原告：处于被告行为可预见危险范围内的人","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The majority rule is that the defendant owes a duty of care to the plaintiff only if the plaintiff is a member of the class of persons who might be foreseeably harmed as a result of the defendant's negligent conduct (sometimes called \"foreseeable plaintiffs\").","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":62,"sourceQuestionPage":187,"sourceQuestionPages":[187],"sourceExplanationPages":[188,189],"sourceAnswerPages":[188,189],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-063","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A saleswoman, who was driving at an excessive speed, applied her brakes to stop at a traffic light. Due to damp, fallen leaves, her car skidded and came to a halt perpendicular to the roadway. A delivery truck driver, who was also driving at an excessive speed and was immediately behind the saleswoman, saw her car perpendicular to the roadway. Although the truck driver had sufficient distance to come to a slow, controlled stop, he decided not to slow down but, rather, to swerve to the left in an effort to go around the saleswoman's car. Due to oncoming traffic, the space was insufficient and the delivery truck collided with the car, severely injuring the saleswoman. The saleswoman filed a personal injury action against the truck driver in a jurisdiction in which contributory negligence is a bar to recovery. Will the saleswoman prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, if the jury finds that, in speeding, the saleswoman assumed the risk.","B":"No, if the jury finds that the saleswoman's conduct was in any way a legal cause of the accident.","C":"Yes, if the jury finds that the truck driver had the last clear chance.","D":"Yes, if the jury finds that the truck driver was more than 50% at fault."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Contributory negligence\n\n(traditional common law)\n\nGeneral rule Plaintiff’s negligence completely bars recovery\n\nException Recovery permitted if defendant:\n\n(last clear chance) had last clear chance to avoid plaintiff's injury and failed to use reasonable care to do so In this jurisdiction and at common law, the plaintiff's contributory negligence—ie, failure to use reasonable care for his/her own safety—is a complete bar to recovery. This is a harsh rule for plaintiffs because it ignores any negligence by the defendant. To mitigate this harshness, courts recognize an exception called the last-clear-chance rule. This rule allows a contributorily negligent plaintiff to recover if the defendant (1) had the last clear chance to avoid the plaintiff's injury but (2) failed to use reasonable care to do so. Here, the saleswoman's negligent, excessive speeding caused her car to skid and stop in the roadway. The truck driver saw this and had sufficient distance to come to a slow, controlled stop (last clear chance). Instead, he decided to swerve in an effort to go around her car (failure to use reasonable care). As a result, the saleswoman will likely prevail in her personal injury action against the truck driver. (Choice A) Assumption of the risk is a defense to negligence when the plaintiff voluntarily exposed him/herself to a known risk of harm. Here, although the saleswoman voluntarily exposed herself to the risks of her own excessive speeding, she did not voluntarily expose herself to the truck driver's negligence. (Choice B) The saleswoman's conduct may have been a legal (ie, proximate) cause of the accident if it was a foreseeable consequence of her excessive speed. But this would not preclude her recovery since the accident was also a foreseeable consequence of the truck driver's negligence and he had the last clear chance. (Choice D) Had this been a modified comparative negligence jurisdiction, the saleswoman's (plaintiff's) negligence would completely bar recovery unless the truck driver (defendant) was more than 50% at fault. But comparative fault is irrelevant in this traditional contributory negligence jurisdiction.\n\nEducational objective: The last-clear-chance rule allows a plaintiff to recover despite his/her contributory negligence if the defendant (1) had the last clear chance to avoid the plaintiff's injury and (2) failed to use reasonable care to do so.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 479–80 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a contributorily negligent plaintiff can still recover if the defendant had the last clear chance to avoid the plaintiff's harm).","educationalObjective":"The last-clear-chance rule allows a plaintiff to recover despite his/her contributory negligence if the defendant (1) had the last clear chance to avoid the plaintiff's injury and (2) failed to use reasonable care to do so.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 479–80 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a contributorily negligent plaintiff can still recover if the defendant had the last clear chance to avoid the plaintiff's harm).","rule":"The last-clear-chance rule allows a plaintiff to recover despite his/her contributory negligence if the defendant (1) had the last clear chance to avoid the plaintiff's injury and (2) failed to use reasonable care to do so.","irac":{"issue":"Will the saleswoman prevail?","rule":"The last-clear-chance rule allows a plaintiff to recover despite his/her contributory negligence if the defendant (1) had the last clear chance to avoid the plaintiff's injury and (2) failed to use reasonable care to do so.","analysis":"Contributory negligence\n\n(traditional common law)\n\nGeneral rule Plaintiff’s negligence completely bars recovery\n\nException Recovery permitted if defendant:\n\n(last clear chance) had last clear chance to avoid plaintiff's injury and failed to use reasonable care to do so In this jurisdiction and at common law, the plaintiff's contributory negligence—ie, failure to use reasonable care for his/her own safety—is a complete bar to recovery. This is a harsh rule for plaintiffs because it ignores any negligence by the defendant. To mitigate this harshness, courts recognize an exception called the last-clear-chance rule. This rule allows a contributorily negligent plaintiff to recover if the defendant (1) had the last clear chance to avoid the plaintiff's injury but (2) failed to use reasonable care to do so. Here, the saleswoman's negligent, excessive speeding caused her car to skid and stop in the roadway. The truck driver saw this and had sufficient distance to come to a slow, controlled stop (last clear chance). Instead, he decided to swerve in an effort to go around her car (failure to use reasonable care). As a result, the saleswoman will likely prevail in her personal injury action against the truck driver. (Choice A) Assumption of the risk is a defense to negligence when the plaintiff voluntarily exposed him/herself to a known risk of harm. Here, although the saleswoman voluntarily exposed herself to the risks of her own excessive speeding, she did not voluntarily expose herself to the truck driver's negligence. (Choice B) The saleswoman's conduct may have been a legal (ie, proximate) cause of the accident if it was a foreseeable consequence of her excessive speed. But this would not preclude her recovery since the accident was also a foreseeable consequence of the truck driver's negligence and he had the last clear chance. (Choice D) Had this been a modified comparative negligence jurisdiction, the saleswoman's (plaintiff's) negligence would completely bar recovery unless the truck driver (defendant) was more than 50% at fault. But comparative fault is irrelevant in this traditional contributory negligence jurisdiction.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, if the jury finds that the truck driver had the last clear chance."},"questionZh":"一位女售货员超速行驶，在红绿灯前踩刹车停车。\n\n由于路面湿滑，落叶堆积，她的车打滑后垂直于路面停了下来。\n\n紧随其后的送货卡车司机也超速行驶，他看到女售货员的车垂直于路面。\n\n尽管卡车司机有足够的距离可以缓慢平稳地停车，但他并没有减速，而是向左急转弯试图绕过女售货员的车。\n\n由于对面来车，空间不足，送货卡车撞上了女售货员的车，导致她严重受伤。\n\n女售货员在当地对卡车司机提起人身伤害诉讼，而当地法律规定，与有过失（contributory negligence）是驳回赔偿请求的依据。\n\n女售货员会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，如果陪审团认定女售货员超速行驶时承担了风险。","B":"否，如果陪审团认定女售货员的行为在任何方面是造成事故的法律原因。","C":"是，如果陪审团认定卡车司机拥有最后避免事故的机会。","D":"是，如果陪审团认定卡车司机负有超过50%的责任。"},"explanationZh":"与有过失（contributory negligence） （传统普通法） 一般规则：原告的过失完全阻却其获得赔偿。 例外情况：若被告具备以下条件，则允许原告获得赔偿： （最后明确机会）被告拥有避免原告受伤的“最后明确机会”，却未尽到合理的注意义务。在本司法管辖区及普通法下，原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）——即未对自身安全尽到合理注意义务——构成获得赔偿的完全障碍。\n\n这对原告而言是一项严苛的规则，因为它完全忽略了被告可能存在的过失。\n\n为缓解这种严苛性，法院确立了一项名为“最后明确机会”规则的例外。\n\n根据该规则，即便原告存在与有过失（contributory negligence），若被告 (1) 拥有避免原告受伤的最后明确机会，但 (2) 未尽到合理注意义务，原告仍可获得赔偿。\n\n在本案中，女推销员因过失超速驾驶导致车辆打滑并停在车道上。\n\n卡车司机看到了这一情况，且有足够的距离平稳、可控地停车（即拥有最后明确机会）。\n\n然而，他却选择转向试图绕过她的车（即未尽到合理注意义务）。\n\n因此，该女推销员针对卡车司机提起的个人伤害诉讼很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”是针对过失侵权的一种抗辩理由，适用于原告自愿置身于已知伤害风险之中的情形。\n\n在本案中，尽管女推销员自愿承担了因自身超速驾驶而产生的风险，但她并未自愿承担卡车司机过失行为所带来的风险。\n\n（选项 B）如果事故是女推销员超速驾驶的可预见后果，那么她的行为可能构成事故的法律原因（即法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause））。\n\n但这并不妨碍她获得赔偿，因为该事故也是卡车司机过失行为的可预见后果，且司机拥有最后明确机会。\n\n（选项 D）如果本案发生在实行“修正型比较过失”原则的司法管辖区，除非卡车司机（被告）的过失责任超过 50%，否则女推销员（原告）的过失将完全阻却其获得赔偿。\n\n但在本实行传统与有过失（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区内，比较过失原则并不适用。\n\n教育目标：“最后明确机会”规则（last-clear-chance rule）规定，即使原告存在促成损害的过失（contributory negligence），若被告 (1) 拥有避免原告受损的最后明确机会，且 (2) 未能尽到合理的注意义务以避免该损害，原告仍可获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第479–80条（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明若被告拥有避免原告受损的最后明确机会，则即便原告存在促成损害的过失，仍可获得赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“最后避免事故机会”规则允许原告即使存在过失，也能获得赔偿，前提是被告 (1) 拥有避免原告受伤的最后避免事故的机会，并且 (2) 未能尽到合理的注意义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"女售货员会胜诉吗？","rule":"“最后避免事故机会”规则允许原告即使存在过失，也能获得赔偿，前提是被告 (1) 拥有避免原告受伤的最后避免事故的机会，并且 (2) 未能尽到合理的注意义务。","analysis":"与有过失（contributory negligence） （传统普通法） 一般规则：原告的过失完全阻却其获得赔偿。 例外情况：若被告具备以下条件，则允许原告获得赔偿： （最后明确机会）被告拥有避免原告受伤的“最后明确机会”，却未尽到合理的注意义务。在本司法管辖区及普通法下，原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）——即未对自身安全尽到合理注意义务——构成获得赔偿的完全障碍。\n\n这对原告而言是一项严苛的规则，因为它完全忽略了被告可能存在的过失。\n\n为缓解这种严苛性，法院确立了一项名为“最后明确机会”规则的例外。\n\n根据该规则，即便原告存在与有过失（contributory negligence），若被告 (1) 拥有避免原告受伤的最后明确机会，但 (2) 未尽到合理注意义务，原告仍可获得赔偿。\n\n在本案中，女推销员因过失超速驾驶导致车辆打滑并停在车道上。\n\n卡车司机看到了这一情况，且有足够的距离平稳、可控地停车（即拥有最后明确机会）。\n\n然而，他却选择转向试图绕过她的车（即未尽到合理注意义务）。\n\n因此，该女推销员针对卡车司机提起的个人伤害诉讼很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”是针对过失侵权的一种抗辩理由，适用于原告自愿置身于已知伤害风险之中的情形。\n\n在本案中，尽管女推销员自愿承担了因自身超速驾驶而产生的风险，但她并未自愿承担卡车司机过失行为所带来的风险。\n\n（选项 B）如果事故是女推销员超速驾驶的可预见后果，那么她的行为可能构成事故的法律原因（即法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause））。\n\n但这并不妨碍她获得赔偿，因为该事故也是卡车司机过失行为的可预见后果，且司机拥有最后明确机会。\n\n（选项 D）如果本案发生在实行“修正型比较过失”原则的司法管辖区，除非卡车司机（被告）的过失责任超过 50%，否则女推销员（原告）的过失将完全阻却其获得赔偿。\n\n但在本实行传统与有过失（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区内，比较过失原则并不适用。\n\n教育目标：“最后明确机会”规则（last-clear-chance rule）规定，即使原告存在促成损害的过失（contributory negligence），若被告 (1) 拥有避免原告受损的最后明确机会，且 (2) 未能尽到合理的注意义务以避免该损害，原告仍可获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第479–80条（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明若被告拥有避免原告受损的最后明确机会，则即便原告存在促成损害的过失，仍可获得赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是，如果陪审团认定卡车司机拥有最后避免事故的机会。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"last clear chance","ipa":"/lˈæst klˈɪɹ tʃˈæns/","syllables":"last clear chance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"最后避免机会：传统与有过失制度下的原告救济规则","contextualMeaning":"最后避免机会：传统与有过失制度下的原告救济规则","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, if the jury finds that the truck driver had the last clear chance.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":63,"sourceQuestionPage":190,"sourceQuestionPages":[190],"sourceExplanationPages":[191,192],"sourceAnswerPages":[191,192],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-064","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"A patron at a diner suffered a severe allergic reaction after her waiter accidently served her the wrong meal. A doctor sitting at the adjoining booth noticed that the patron was struggling to breathe. However, the doctor did not provide any medical assistance because he had just completed a hectic 12-hour shift and wanted to enjoy his meal. The patron's throat swelled, and she eventually passed out. Later, the patron died due to oxygen deprivation. It was determined that swift medical attention would have prevented her death. The patron's spouse has brought a wrongful death action against the doctor in a jurisdiction that has enacted a Good Samaritan statute. Will the spouse likely recover?","choices":{"A":"No, because the doctor had no duty to aid the patron.","B":"No, because the patron assumed the risk of eating in the diner.","C":"Yes, because a reasonable doctor would have assisted the patron.","D":"Yes, because the jurisdiction has enacted a Good Samaritan statute."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A wrongful death action allows a decedent's spouse, next of kin, or personal representative to sue a defendant for wrongfully causing the decedent's death. These actions are typically based on negligence—ie, a breach of the defendant's duty to use reasonable care that caused physical harm. However, a defendant generally has no duty to aid a person facing a foreseeable risk of physical harm unless: the defendant's conduct created that risk or the defendant and that person share a special relationship. Here, the patron suffered a severe allergic reaction while she was eating at a diner. Although swift medical attention would have saved her life, the doctor sitting at an adjoining booth had no duty to aid her since his conduct did not create this risk and they did not share a special relationship. Therefore, the patron's spouse likely will not recover from the doctor.* *However, the patron's spouse may be able to recover from the diner since one of its employees created the risk by accidently serving the patron the wrong meal and a special relationship existed between the patron and the diner. (Choice B) Assumption of the risk is a defense that reduces or bars recovery if the injured person voluntarily exposed him/herself to a known risk of harm. This defense does not apply here because the patron did not know that the wrongly served meal would cause an allergic reaction. (Choice C) The fact that a reasonable doctor would have assisted the patron does not mean that the doctor here had a duty to do so. That is because the law generally does not require a defendant to aid another unless the defendant created the foreseeable risk of physical harm or had a special relationship with that person. (Choice D) Many states have enacted Good Samaritan laws that relieve medical professionals of malpractice liability for voluntarily rendering emergency first aid. These laws are designed to encourage medical professionals to provide such assistance without the threat of a lawsuit, but they do not create a duty to aid.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant generally has no duty to aid a plaintiff who is at risk of physical harm unless the defendant's conduct created that risk OR the defendant and the plaintiff share a special relationship.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 6 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence liability).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 7 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (general duty requirement).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 37 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (no duty to aid).","educationalObjective":"A defendant generally has no duty to aid a plaintiff who is at risk of physical harm unless the defendant's conduct created that risk OR the defendant and the plaintiff share a special relationship.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 6 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence liability).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 7 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (general duty requirement).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 37 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (no duty to aid).","rule":"A defendant generally has no duty to aid a plaintiff who is at risk of physical harm unless the defendant's conduct created that risk OR the defendant and the plaintiff share a special relationship.","irac":{"issue":"Will the spouse likely recover?","rule":"A defendant generally has no duty to aid a plaintiff who is at risk of physical harm unless the defendant's conduct created that risk OR the defendant and the plaintiff share a special relationship.","analysis":"A wrongful death action allows a decedent's spouse, next of kin, or personal representative to sue a defendant for wrongfully causing the decedent's death. These actions are typically based on negligence—ie, a breach of the defendant's duty to use reasonable care that caused physical harm. However, a defendant generally has no duty to aid a person facing a foreseeable risk of physical harm unless: the defendant's conduct created that risk or the defendant and that person share a special relationship. Here, the patron suffered a severe allergic reaction while she was eating at a diner. Although swift medical attention would have saved her life, the doctor sitting at an adjoining booth had no duty to aid her since his conduct did not create this risk and they did not share a special relationship. Therefore, the patron's spouse likely will not recover from the doctor.* *However, the patron's spouse may be able to recover from the diner since one of its employees created the risk by accidently serving the patron the wrong meal and a special relationship existed between the patron and the diner. (Choice B) Assumption of the risk is a defense that reduces or bars recovery if the injured person voluntarily exposed him/herself to a known risk of harm. This defense does not apply here because the patron did not know that the wrongly served meal would cause an allergic reaction. (Choice C) The fact that a reasonable doctor would have assisted the patron does not mean that the doctor here had a duty to do so. That is because the law generally does not require a defendant to aid another unless the defendant created the foreseeable risk of physical harm or had a special relationship with that person. (Choice D) Many states have enacted Good Samaritan laws that relieve medical professionals of malpractice liability for voluntarily rendering emergency first aid. These laws are designed to encourage medical professionals to provide such assistance without the threat of a lawsuit, but they do not create a duty to aid.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the doctor had no duty to aid the patron."},"questionZh":"一位在餐馆用餐的顾客因服务员误送餐点而引发了严重的过敏反应。\n\n邻桌的一位医生注意到该顾客呼吸困难。\n\n然而，这名医生并未提供任何医疗救助，因为他刚结束了繁忙的12小时轮班，只想享用自己的餐点。\n\n该顾客的喉部发生肿胀，最终昏迷。\n\n随后，该顾客因缺氧死亡。\n\n经认定，若能及时获得医疗救治，其死亡本可避免。\n\n该顾客的配偶在已颁布“好撒玛利亚人法”（Good Samaritan statute）的司法管辖区内，针对该医生提起了非正常死亡诉讼。\n\n该配偶胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为该医生没有义务救助该顾客。","B":"不大，因为该顾客在餐馆用餐时承担了相应的风险。","C":"大，因为一个理性的医生会向该顾客提供救助。","D":"大，因为该司法管辖区已颁布了“好撒玛利亚人法”。"},"explanationZh":"“不当致死之诉”（wrongful death action）允许死者的配偶、近亲属或遗产代理人起诉因不当行为导致死者死亡的被告。\n\n此类诉讼通常基于过失，即被告违反了采取合理注意义务从而导致人身伤害的行为。\n\n然而，被告通常没有义务救助面临可预见人身伤害风险的人，除非：被告的行为造成了该风险，或者被告与该人之间存在特殊关系。\n\n在本案中，顾客在餐馆用餐时发生了严重的过敏反应。\n\n尽管及时的医疗救治本可挽救她的生命，但坐在邻桌的医生并无义务救助她，因为他的行为并未造成该风险，且两人之间也不存在特殊关系。\n\n因此，顾客的配偶很可能无法从该医生处获得赔偿。* *不过，顾客的配偶或许能从餐馆获得赔偿，因为餐馆的一名员工因意外上错菜而造成了该风险，且顾客与餐馆之间存在特殊关系。\n\n（选项 B）“自担风险”（assumption of the risk）是一种抗辩理由，如果受害人自愿置身于已知的伤害风险之中，该抗辩可减少或排除赔偿。\n\n本案不适用此抗辩，因为顾客并不知道上错的餐点会导致过敏反应。\n\n（选项 C）尽管一个“理性谨慎的医生”本会向顾客提供协助，但这并不意味着本案中的医生有义务这样做。\n\n这是因为法律通常不要求被告救助他人，除非被告造成了可预见的人身伤害风险，或者与该人存在特殊关系。\n\n（选项 D）许多州制定了“好撒玛利亚人法”（Good Samaritan laws），免除医务人员因自愿提供紧急急救而承担的医疗事故责任。\n\n这些法律旨在鼓励医务人员在无诉讼风险的情况下提供此类援助，但它们并不创设救助义务。\n\n教育目标：被告通常没有义务救助面临人身伤害风险的原告，除非被告的行为造成了该风险，或者被告与原告之间存在特殊关系。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 6 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（过失责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第7条（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于一般注意义务的要求）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第37条（美国法律协会，2012年）（关于无救助义务的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"通常情况下，被告没有义务救助面临身体伤害风险的原告，除非该风险是由被告的行为造成的，或者被告与原告之间存在某种特殊关系。","iracZh":{"issue":"该配偶胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"通常情况下，被告没有义务救助面临身体伤害风险的原告，除非该风险是由被告的行为造成的，或者被告与原告之间存在某种特殊关系。","analysis":"“不当致死之诉”（wrongful death action）允许死者的配偶、近亲属或遗产代理人起诉因不当行为导致死者死亡的被告。\n\n此类诉讼通常基于过失，即被告违反了采取合理注意义务从而导致人身伤害的行为。\n\n然而，被告通常没有义务救助面临可预见人身伤害风险的人，除非：被告的行为造成了该风险，或者被告与该人之间存在特殊关系。\n\n在本案中，顾客在餐馆用餐时发生了严重的过敏反应。\n\n尽管及时的医疗救治本可挽救她的生命，但坐在邻桌的医生并无义务救助她，因为他的行为并未造成该风险，且两人之间也不存在特殊关系。\n\n因此，顾客的配偶很可能无法从该医生处获得赔偿。* *不过，顾客的配偶或许能从餐馆获得赔偿，因为餐馆的一名员工因意外上错菜而造成了该风险，且顾客与餐馆之间存在特殊关系。\n\n（选项 B）“自担风险”（assumption of the risk）是一种抗辩理由，如果受害人自愿置身于已知的伤害风险之中，该抗辩可减少或排除赔偿。\n\n本案不适用此抗辩，因为顾客并不知道上错的餐点会导致过敏反应。\n\n（选项 C）尽管一个“理性谨慎的医生”本会向顾客提供协助，但这并不意味着本案中的医生有义务这样做。\n\n这是因为法律通常不要求被告救助他人，除非被告造成了可预见的人身伤害风险，或者与该人存在特殊关系。\n\n（选项 D）许多州制定了“好撒玛利亚人法”（Good Samaritan laws），免除医务人员因自愿提供紧急急救而承担的医疗事故责任。\n\n这些法律旨在鼓励医务人员在无诉讼风险的情况下提供此类援助，但它们并不创设救助义务。\n\n教育目标：被告通常没有义务救助面临人身伤害风险的原告，除非被告的行为造成了该风险，或者被告与原告之间存在特殊关系。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 6 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（过失责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第7条（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于一般注意义务的要求）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第37条（美国法律协会，2012年）（关于无救助义务的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不大，因为该医生没有义务救助该顾客。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"wrongful death","ipa":"/ɹˈɔŋfəl dˈɛθ/","syllables":"wrong·ful death","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不当死亡之诉：特定受益人就受害人死亡造成的损失请求赔偿","contextualMeaning":"不当死亡之诉：特定受益人就受害人死亡造成的损失请求赔偿","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The patron's spouse has brought a wrongful death action against the doctor in a jurisdiction that has enacted a Good Samaritan statute.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-2aceb2eff0e4289d"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":64,"sourceQuestionPage":193,"sourceQuestionPages":[193],"sourceExplanationPages":[194,195],"sourceAnswerPages":[194,195],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-065","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"While driving his pickup truck with a friend riding in the open bed, the driver swerved, throwing his friend to the pavement. The friend sustained severe injuries. The friend had often ridden in the open bed of the truck, and on some of those occasions the driver had swerved to frighten his friend. The friend sued the driver to recover both compensatory damages for his injuries and punitive damages. Which cause of action would NOT permit the friend to recover punitive damages?","choices":{"A":"Assault.","B":"Battery.","C":"Negligence.","D":"Recklessness."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Damages in tort law fall into three main categories: Compensatory (ie, actual) damages – money awarded to compensate the plaintiff for his/her actual loss or harm\n\nNominal damages – a trivial sum of money (eg, $1) awarded to vindicate the plaintiff's rights when no actual loss or harm occurred\n\nPunitive (ie, exemplary) damages – money awarded to punish the defendant for outrageous, malicious, or evil conduct and to deter the defendant and others from engaging in similar conduct in the future\n\nCompensatory damages are available for any tort. But nominal and punitive damages are only available for torts involving intentional conduct (eg, assault, battery) or reckless conduct (eg, reckless driving) (Choices A, B & D).* A plaintiff cannot recover nominal damages for negligence since this claim requires proof of actual harm. Punitive damages are also not recoverable for negligence because these damages are designed to punish and deter outrageous, malicious, or evil conduct—not unreasonable conduct. *Punitive damages are prohibited in Nebraska and extremely limited in Louisiana, Massachusetts, Michigan, New Hampshire, and Washington.\n\nEducational objective: Punitive damages are imposed to punish and deter outrageous, malicious, or evil conduct. As a result, punitive damages may be awarded for torts involving intentional or reckless conduct. But these damages cannot be awarded for negligence, which involves mere unreasonable conduct.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 908 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (availability of punitive damages).","educationalObjective":"Punitive damages are imposed to punish and deter outrageous, malicious, or evil conduct. As a result, punitive damages may be awarded for torts involving intentional or reckless conduct. But these damages cannot be awarded for negligence, which involves mere unreasonable conduct.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 908 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (availability of punitive damages).","rule":"Punitive damages are imposed to punish and deter outrageous, malicious, or evil conduct. As a result, punitive damages may be awarded for torts involving intentional or reckless conduct. But these damages cannot be awarded for negligence, which involves mere unreasonable conduct.","irac":{"issue":"Which cause of action would NOT permit the friend to recover punitive damages?","rule":"Punitive damages are imposed to punish and deter outrageous, malicious, or evil conduct. As a result, punitive damages may be awarded for torts involving intentional or reckless conduct. But these damages cannot be awarded for negligence, which involves mere unreasonable conduct.","analysis":"Damages in tort law fall into three main categories: Compensatory (ie, actual) damages – money awarded to compensate the plaintiff for his/her actual loss or harm\n\nNominal damages – a trivial sum of money (eg, $1) awarded to vindicate the plaintiff's rights when no actual loss or harm occurred\n\nPunitive (ie, exemplary) damages – money awarded to punish the defendant for outrageous, malicious, or evil conduct and to deter the defendant and others from engaging in similar conduct in the future\n\nCompensatory damages are available for any tort. But nominal and punitive damages are only available for torts involving intentional conduct (eg, assault, battery) or reckless conduct (eg, reckless driving) (Choices A, B & D).* A plaintiff cannot recover nominal damages for negligence since this claim requires proof of actual harm. Punitive damages are also not recoverable for negligence because these damages are designed to punish and deter outrageous, malicious, or evil conduct—not unreasonable conduct. *Punitive damages are prohibited in Nebraska and extremely limited in Louisiana, Massachusetts, Michigan, New Hampshire, and Washington.","conclusion":"Answer C: Negligence."},"questionZh":"司机驾驶皮卡车时，其朋友坐在敞开的车厢内；司机突然转向，导致朋友被甩到路面上并受重伤。\n\n该朋友此前经常坐在该皮卡车的敞开式车厢内，司机也曾多次通过突然转向来吓唬他。\n\n该朋友起诉司机，要求获得因受伤而产生的补偿性损害赔偿以及惩罚性损害赔偿。\n\n下列哪种诉因**不**允许该朋友获得惩罚性损害赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"袭击（Assault）。","B":"故意侵害身体（battery）（Battery）。","C":"过失（Negligence）。","D":"鲁莽（Recklessness）。"},"explanationZh":"侵权法中的损害赔偿主要分为三类：补偿性（即实际）损害赔偿——旨在补偿原告实际损失或伤害的款项。 名义损害赔偿——在未发生实际损失或伤害的情况下，为维护原告权利而判给的象征性小额款项（例如1美元）。 惩罚性（即惩戒性）损害赔偿——旨在惩罚被告的恶劣、恶意或邪恶行为，并威慑被告及他人未来从事类似行为的款项。 任何侵权行为均可适用补偿性损害赔偿。\n\n但名义损害赔偿和惩罚性损害赔偿仅适用于涉及故意行为（如袭击、故意侵害身体（battery））或鲁莽行为（如鲁莽驾驶）的侵权情形（选项A、B和D）。* 原告无法就过失行为获得名义损害赔偿，因为此类主张要求证明存在实际伤害。\n\n过失行为也不适用惩罚性损害赔偿，因为此类赔偿旨在惩罚和威慑恶劣、恶意或邪恶的行为，而非仅仅是不合理的行为。\n\n* 内布拉斯加州禁止判给惩罚性损害赔偿；路易斯安那州、马萨诸塞州、密歇根州、新罕布什尔州和华盛顿州对惩罚性损害赔偿的适用也有极严格的限制。\n\n教育目标：判给惩罚性损害赔偿是为了惩罚和威慑恶劣、恶意或邪恶的行为。\n\n因此，涉及故意或鲁莽行为的侵权案件可能会判给惩罚性损害赔偿。\n\n但对于仅涉及不合理行为的过失案件，则不能判给此类赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第908条注释b（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于惩罚性损害赔偿的适用）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"惩罚性损害赔偿旨在惩罚和遏制令人愤慨、具有恶意或邪恶性质的行为。\n\n因此，对于涉及故意或鲁莽行为的侵权案件，法院可能会判给惩罚性损害赔偿。\n\n然而，对于仅涉及不合理行为的过失案件，则不能判给此类赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"下列哪种诉因**不**允许该朋友获得惩罚性损害赔偿？","rule":"惩罚性损害赔偿旨在惩罚和遏制令人愤慨、具有恶意或邪恶性质的行为。\n\n因此，对于涉及故意或鲁莽行为的侵权案件，法院可能会判给惩罚性损害赔偿。\n\n然而，对于仅涉及不合理行为的过失案件，则不能判给此类赔偿。","analysis":"侵权法中的损害赔偿主要分为三类：补偿性（即实际）损害赔偿——旨在补偿原告实际损失或伤害的款项。 名义损害赔偿——在未发生实际损失或伤害的情况下，为维护原告权利而判给的象征性小额款项（例如1美元）。 惩罚性（即惩戒性）损害赔偿——旨在惩罚被告的恶劣、恶意或邪恶行为，并威慑被告及他人未来从事类似行为的款项。 任何侵权行为均可适用补偿性损害赔偿。\n\n但名义损害赔偿和惩罚性损害赔偿仅适用于涉及故意行为（如袭击、故意侵害身体（battery））或鲁莽行为（如鲁莽驾驶）的侵权情形（选项A、B和D）。* 原告无法就过失行为获得名义损害赔偿，因为此类主张要求证明存在实际伤害。\n\n过失行为也不适用惩罚性损害赔偿，因为此类赔偿旨在惩罚和威慑恶劣、恶意或邪恶的行为，而非仅仅是不合理的行为。\n\n* 内布拉斯加州禁止判给惩罚性损害赔偿；路易斯安那州、马萨诸塞州、密歇根州、新罕布什尔州和华盛顿州对惩罚性损害赔偿的适用也有极严格的限制。\n\n教育目标：判给惩罚性损害赔偿是为了惩罚和威慑恶劣、恶意或邪恶的行为。\n\n因此，涉及故意或鲁莽行为的侵权案件可能会判给惩罚性损害赔偿。\n\n但对于仅涉及不合理行为的过失案件，则不能判给此类赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第908条注释b（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于惩罚性损害赔偿的适用）。","conclusion":"答案 C：过失（Negligence）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-c8c0b258633259a3"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":65,"sourceQuestionPage":196,"sourceQuestionPages":[196],"sourceExplanationPages":[197,198],"sourceAnswerPages":[197,198],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-066","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A shopping mall located near an interstate highway exit is surrounded by a paved parking area. Each entrance to the parking area has a sign reading, \"City Ordinance: NO TRESPASSING from midnight to 6 a.m.\" A weary cross-country traveler had begun to doze while driving. Concerned that he would cause a car accident, he looked for a place to sleep. At 2:00 a.m., the traveler saw the shopping mall from the road and exited the highway. In his exhausted state, the traveler failed to notice the city ordinance signs. The traveler pulled into the open gates of the parking area to rest and immediately fell asleep. An hour later, the traveler suffered a serious personal injury when a light pole fell onto his car. Unbeknownst to the mall owner, a mall patron had backed her truck into the pole the previous day. The damage to the light pole was not readily visible upon a reasonable inspection. However, an inspection by the maintenance company hired by the mall owner would have revealed that the pole was dangerously off-balance. The traveler sued the mall owner in a negligence action for damages stemming from personal injuries and property damage suffered when the light pole fell. In a jurisdiction that follows the traditional approach regarding a land possessor's duty to land entrants, which of the following is likely to be the mall owner's best defense?","choices":{"A":"The mall owner did not know of the risk posed by the light pole.","B":"The mall owner could not reasonably foresee that the traveler would enter the parking area.","C":"The traveler assumed the risk of injury when he entered the parking area at a prohibited time.","D":"The traveler's violation of the ordinance prohibiting trespassing was negligence per se."},"answer":"A","explanation":"To be liable for negligence, the defendant must have breached a duty owed to the plaintiff. Under the traditional approach, land possessors owe no duty to unknown or unanticipated trespassers.* But they do owe a limited duty to known or anticipated trespassers to: warn the trespasser about hidden, artificial dangers (ie, man-made dangers) that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by the trespasser and\n\nuse reasonable care while conducting activities on their land.\n\nHere, the traveler trespassed when he entered the parking lot at 2:00 a.m. because entry is prohibited between midnight and 6:00 a.m. Not only was his presence foreseeable, it was anticipated—as evidenced by the \"no trespassing\" sign at each entrance—so the mall owner owed the traveler a limited duty of care (Choice B). But since the mall owner did not know of the risk posed by the light pole, the mall owner did not breach any duty owed to the traveler and so cannot be liable for negligence. Therefore, that is the mall owner's best defense. *The traditional approach is followed in about half the jurisdictions. The other half follow the modern approach, under which land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants (except flagrant trespassers). (Choice C) Implied assumption of the risk arises when a plaintiff voluntarily encounters a known, specific risk. Here, the traveler did not know of the risk posed by the light pole. But even if he had, in most comparative fault jurisdictions (the default rule on the MBE), a plaintiff's assumption of the risk merely reduces recovery. (Choice D) The traveler's violation of the ordinance was not negligence per se since the harm he suffered as a result of the damaged light pole was likely not of the type that the \"no trespassing\" ordinance was designed to prevent. Even if it were, this would only establish that he was contributorily negligent, which would reduce (but not bar) his recovery.\n\nEducational objective: A land possessor owes a duty to known or anticipated trespassers to (1) warn them about hidden, artificial dangers that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by trespassers and (2) exercise reasonable care while conducting activities on the land.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 337 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (duty to known trespassers).","educationalObjective":"A land possessor owes a duty to known or anticipated trespassers to (1) warn them about hidden, artificial dangers that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by trespassers and (2) exercise reasonable care while conducting activities on the land.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 337 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (duty to known trespassers).","rule":"A land possessor owes a duty to known or anticipated trespassers to (1) warn them about hidden, artificial dangers that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by trespassers and (2) exercise reasonable care while conducting activities on the land.","irac":{"issue":"In a jurisdiction that follows the traditional approach regarding a land possessor's duty to land entrants, which of the following is likely to be the mall owner's best defense?","rule":"A land possessor owes a duty to known or anticipated trespassers to (1) warn them about hidden, artificial dangers that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by trespassers and (2) exercise reasonable care while conducting activities on the land.","analysis":"To be liable for negligence, the defendant must have breached a duty owed to the plaintiff. Under the traditional approach, land possessors owe no duty to unknown or unanticipated trespassers.* But they do owe a limited duty to known or anticipated trespassers to: warn the trespasser about hidden, artificial dangers (ie, man-made dangers) that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by the trespasser and\n\nuse reasonable care while conducting activities on their land.\n\nHere, the traveler trespassed when he entered the parking lot at 2:00 a.m. because entry is prohibited between midnight and 6:00 a.m. Not only was his presence foreseeable, it was anticipated—as evidenced by the \"no trespassing\" sign at each entrance—so the mall owner owed the traveler a limited duty of care (Choice B). But since the mall owner did not know of the risk posed by the light pole, the mall owner did not breach any duty owed to the traveler and so cannot be liable for negligence. Therefore, that is the mall owner's best defense. *The traditional approach is followed in about half the jurisdictions. The other half follow the modern approach, under which land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants (except flagrant trespassers). (Choice C) Implied assumption of the risk arises when a plaintiff voluntarily encounters a known, specific risk. Here, the traveler did not know of the risk posed by the light pole. But even if he had, in most comparative fault jurisdictions (the default rule on the MBE), a plaintiff's assumption of the risk merely reduces recovery. (Choice D) The traveler's violation of the ordinance was not negligence per se since the harm he suffered as a result of the damaged light pole was likely not of the type that the \"no trespassing\" ordinance was designed to prevent. Even if it were, this would only establish that he was contributorily negligent, which would reduce (but not bar) his recovery.","conclusion":"Answer A: The mall owner did not know of the risk posed by the light pole."},"questionZh":"一家位于州际公路出口附近的购物中心被铺设好的停车场环绕。\n\n停车场每个入口处都竖立着一块标牌，上面写着：“市政条例：午夜至凌晨6点禁止擅自进入（NO TRESPASSING）。”一位长途跋涉的旅客在驾车时开始打瞌睡。\n\n由于担心引发车祸，他寻找休息的地方。\n\n凌晨2点，该旅客在公路上看到了这家购物中心，于是驶离了高速公路。\n\n由于极度疲惫，他没有注意到那些关于市政条例的标牌。\n\n他将车开进敞开的停车场大门休息，并随即睡着了。\n\n一小时后，一根灯柱倒塌砸中了他的车，导致他遭受严重的人身伤害。\n\n购物中心业主并不知情的是，前一天曾有一位顾客驾驶卡车倒车时撞到了这根灯柱。\n\n通过合理的检查，灯柱的损坏情况并不容易被发现。\n\n然而，如果由业主聘请的维护公司进行检查，就能发现该灯柱已处于危险的不稳定状态。\n\n该旅客对购物中心业主提起过失诉讼，要求赔偿因灯柱倒塌而遭受的人身伤害及财产损失。\n\n在遵循关于土地占有人对进入者负有何种义务之传统原则的司法管辖区内，以下哪项最可能是购物中心业主的最佳抗辩理由？","choicesZh":{"A":"购物中心业主并不知晓该灯柱构成的风险。","B":"购物中心业主无法合理预见到该旅客会进入停车场。","C":"该旅客在禁止进入的时段进入停车场，即承担了受伤的风险。","D":"该旅客违反禁止擅自进入的市政条例，构成了“本身过失”（negligence per se）。"},"explanationZh":"要承担过失责任，被告必须违反了对原告所负的义务。\n\n根据传统做法，土地占有者对未知或未预料到的侵入者不负有义务。* 但对于已知或预料到的侵入者，他们负有有限的义务，即：警告侵入者注意那些土地占有者已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽的人为危险（即人造危险）；以及 在土地上进行活动时尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n在本案中，该旅行者于凌晨2:00进入停车场时构成了侵入，因为午夜至凌晨6:00期间禁止进入。他的出现不仅是可预见的，而且是预料之中的——每个入口处的“禁止侵入”标志即为证明——因此，商场业主对该旅行者负有有限的注意义务（选项B）。\n\n但由于商场业主并不知晓该灯柱构成的风险，因此商场业主并未违反其对旅行者负有的任何义务，也就无需承担过失责任。\n\n因此，这是商场业主最有力的抗辩理由。\n\n* 约一半的司法管辖区采用传统做法。\n\n另一半则采用现代做法，根据该做法，土地占有者对所有进入土地的人（恶劣的侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n（选项C）当原告自愿面对已知的特定风险时，即产生“默示承担风险”的问题。\n\n在本案中，该旅行者并不知晓灯柱构成的风险。\n\n但即使他知晓，在大多数适用比较过失原则的司法管辖区（这是MBE考试中的默认规则），原告承担风险的行为只会减少其获赔金额。\n\n（选项D）该旅行者违反地方法规的行为并不构成“本身过失”（negligence per se），因为他因受损灯柱而遭受的伤害，很可能不属于该“禁止侵入”法规旨在预防的伤害类型。\n\n即使属于此类伤害，这也只能证明他存在与有过失（contributory negligence），从而减少（而非完全排除）其获赔金额。\n\n教育目标：土地占有者对已知或预料到的侵入者负有义务，即：(1) 警告他们注意那些土地占有者已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽的人为危险；以及 (2) 在土地上进行活动时尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第337条（美国法律学会，1965年）（针对已知侵入者的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地占有人对已知或可预见的侵入者负有以下义务：(1) 警告他们注意那些土地占有人已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽的人造危险；以及 (2) 在土地上进行活动时尽到合理的注意义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"在遵循关于土地占有人对进入者负有何种义务之传统原则的司法管辖区内，以下哪项最可能是购物中心业主的最佳抗辩理由？","rule":"土地占有人对已知或可预见的侵入者负有以下义务：(1) 警告他们注意那些土地占有人已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽的人造危险；以及 (2) 在土地上进行活动时尽到合理的注意义务。","analysis":"要承担过失责任，被告必须违反了对原告所负的义务。\n\n根据传统做法，土地占有者对未知或未预料到的侵入者不负有义务。* 但对于已知或预料到的侵入者，他们负有有限的义务，即：警告侵入者注意那些土地占有者已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽的人为危险（即人造危险）；以及 在土地上进行活动时尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n在本案中，该旅行者于凌晨2:00进入停车场时构成了侵入，因为午夜至凌晨6:00期间禁止进入。他的出现不仅是可预见的，而且是预料之中的——每个入口处的“禁止侵入”标志即为证明——因此，商场业主对该旅行者负有有限的注意义务（选项B）。\n\n但由于商场业主并不知晓该灯柱构成的风险，因此商场业主并未违反其对旅行者负有的任何义务，也就无需承担过失责任。\n\n因此，这是商场业主最有力的抗辩理由。\n\n* 约一半的司法管辖区采用传统做法。\n\n另一半则采用现代做法，根据该做法，土地占有者对所有进入土地的人（恶劣的侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n（选项C）当原告自愿面对已知的特定风险时，即产生“默示承担风险”的问题。\n\n在本案中，该旅行者并不知晓灯柱构成的风险。\n\n但即使他知晓，在大多数适用比较过失原则的司法管辖区（这是MBE考试中的默认规则），原告承担风险的行为只会减少其获赔金额。\n\n（选项D）该旅行者违反地方法规的行为并不构成“本身过失”（negligence per se），因为他因受损灯柱而遭受的伤害，很可能不属于该“禁止侵入”法规旨在预防的伤害类型。\n\n即使属于此类伤害，这也只能证明他存在与有过失（contributory negligence），从而减少（而非完全排除）其获赔金额。\n\n教育目标：土地占有者对已知或预料到的侵入者负有义务，即：(1) 警告他们注意那些土地占有者已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽的人为危险；以及 (2) 在土地上进行活动时尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第337条（美国法律学会，1965年）（针对已知侵入者的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 A：购物中心业主并不知晓该灯柱构成的风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"negligence per se","ipa":"/nˈɛɡlədʒəns pˈɝ sˈeɪ/","syllables":"neg·li·gence per se","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"过失当然成立：违反保护特定人群、防止特定损害的法规可确立违反义务","contextualMeaning":"过失当然成立：违反保护特定人群、防止特定损害的法规可确立违反义务","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The traveler's violation of the ordinance prohibiting trespassing was negligence per se.","synonyms":["statutory negligence"]},{"term":"dangerously","ipa":"/dˈeɪndʒɝəsli/","syllables":"dan·ger·ously","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 危险, 不安全","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“dangerously”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"However, an inspection by the maintenance company hired by the mall owner would have revealed that the pole was dangerously off-balance.","synonyms":["perilously","hazardously"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-62f825ae654bfa61"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":66,"sourceQuestionPage":199,"sourceQuestionPages":[199],"sourceExplanationPages":[200,201],"sourceAnswerPages":[200,201],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-067","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A patient received anesthesia while giving birth. Upon awakening from the anesthesia, she discovered a severe burn on the inner portion of her right knee. The patient has brought a medical malpractice action in which she has joined all of the physicians and nurses who exercised control over her person, the delivery room, the medical procedures, and the equipment used during the period in which she was unconscious. The defendants have jointly moved for summary judgment. The patient has produced affidavits that establish that the applicable professional standard of care was violated. What would be the patient's best argument against the motion?","choices":{"A":"At least one of the defendants had control over whatever agency or instrumentality caused the patient's injury.","B":"The defendants were acting in concert.","C":"The patient has produced affidavits that establish that the applicable professional standard of care was violated.","D":"The patient was in no way responsible for her injury."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Res ipsa loquitur\n\n(medical malpractice claims)\n\nApplicability Plaintiff suffered unusual injury while unconscious during medical treatment At least one member of medical team had control over patient's body or instrumentality that caused injury Effect Creates inference that every member of medical team was negligent Places burden on each member to establish that he/she was not negligent Medical malpractice claims are based on negligence and generally require proof that the defendant violated a professional standard of care. But this can be difficult to prove when a complex procedure (eg, surgery) was performed by a team of medical professionals. In that case, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur lets the fact finder infer that every member of the medical team was negligent when: the patient suffered an unusual injury (ie, one that is usually caused by negligence) while unconscious and in the course of medical treatment and\n\nat least one member of the medical team had control over the patient's body or instrumentalities that might have caused the patient's injury.\n\nHere, the patient sued her medical team for a severe burn that she discovered upon awakening from anesthesia. And though the patient has affidavits showing that the professional standard of care was violated, she cannot identify which defendant was negligent (Choice C). But since at least one of the defendants had control over whatever instrumentality caused her unusual injury, the negligence of every defendant can be inferred. Therefore, this is the patient's best argument against the defendants' motion for summary judgment. (Choice B) Tortfeasors who act in concert (ie, by agreement) and injure the plaintiff are jointly and severally liable for the entire injury. But here, there is no evidence that the defendants were acting in concert, and it is possible that a single defendant caused the patient's burn. (Choice D) The patient was not responsible for her injury since she was anesthetized when it occurred. But her lack of responsibility does not prove that someone on her medical team was negligent.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur, it can be inferred that every member of a medical team was negligent when (1) the patient suffered an unusual injury while unconscious during medical treatment and (2) at least one member of the team had control over the patient's body or instrumentalities that might have caused the injury.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a defendant's negligence can be inferred based on the type of accident that injured the plaintiff).\n\nYbarra v. Spangard, 25 Cal. 2d 486, 494 (1944) (holding that all defendants who control an unconscious patient's body while the patient suffers unusual injuries are subject to an inference of negligence).","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur, it can be inferred that every member of a medical team was negligent when (1) the patient suffered an unusual injury while unconscious during medical treatment and (2) at least one member of the team had control over the patient's body or instrumentalities that might have caused the injury.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a defendant's negligence can be inferred based on the type of accident that injured the plaintiff).\n\nYbarra v. Spangard, 25 Cal. 2d 486, 494 (1944) (holding that all defendants who control an unconscious patient's body while the patient suffers unusual injuries are subject to an inference of negligence).","rule":"Under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur, it can be inferred that every member of a medical team was negligent when (1) the patient suffered an unusual injury while unconscious during medical treatment and (2) at least one member of the team had control over the patient's body or instrumentalities that might have caused the injury.","irac":{"issue":"What would be the patient's best argument against the motion?","rule":"Under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur, it can be inferred that every member of a medical team was negligent when (1) the patient suffered an unusual injury while unconscious during medical treatment and (2) at least one member of the team had control over the patient's body or instrumentalities that might have caused the injury.","analysis":"Res ipsa loquitur\n\n(medical malpractice claims)\n\nApplicability Plaintiff suffered unusual injury while unconscious during medical treatment At least one member of medical team had control over patient's body or instrumentality that caused injury Effect Creates inference that every member of medical team was negligent Places burden on each member to establish that he/she was not negligent Medical malpractice claims are based on negligence and generally require proof that the defendant violated a professional standard of care. But this can be difficult to prove when a complex procedure (eg, surgery) was performed by a team of medical professionals. In that case, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur lets the fact finder infer that every member of the medical team was negligent when: the patient suffered an unusual injury (ie, one that is usually caused by negligence) while unconscious and in the course of medical treatment and\n\nat least one member of the medical team had control over the patient's body or instrumentalities that might have caused the patient's injury.\n\nHere, the patient sued her medical team for a severe burn that she discovered upon awakening from anesthesia. And though the patient has affidavits showing that the professional standard of care was violated, she cannot identify which defendant was negligent (Choice C). But since at least one of the defendants had control over whatever instrumentality caused her unusual injury, the negligence of every defendant can be inferred. Therefore, this is the patient's best argument against the defendants' motion for summary judgment. (Choice B) Tortfeasors who act in concert (ie, by agreement) and injure the plaintiff are jointly and severally liable for the entire injury. But here, there is no evidence that the defendants were acting in concert, and it is possible that a single defendant caused the patient's burn. (Choice D) The patient was not responsible for her injury since she was anesthetized when it occurred. But her lack of responsibility does not prove that someone on her medical team was negligent.","conclusion":"Answer A: At least one of the defendants had control over whatever agency or instrumentality caused the patient's injury."},"questionZh":"一名患者在分娩过程中接受了麻醉。\n\n麻醉苏醒后，她发现右膝内侧有严重的烧伤。\n\n该患者提起了医疗事故诉讼，并将所有在患者处于无意识状态期间对患者本人、产房、医疗程序及所用设备行使控制权的医生和护士列为共同被告。\n\n被告方联合申请简易判决。\n\n患者已提交宣誓书，证明相关专业护理标准遭到了违反。\n\n针对该申请，患者最有力的论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"导致患者受伤的机构或器械至少由其中一名被告控制。","B":"被告们是协同行动的。","C":"患者已提交宣誓书，证明相关专业护理标准遭到了违反。","D":"患者对自己的受伤没有任何责任。"},"explanationZh":"“事实不言自明”原则 (Res ipsa loquitur) （医疗事故索赔） 适用条件：原告在接受医疗过程中处于无意识状态时遭受了异常伤害；医疗团队中至少有一名成员控制着患者的身体或导致伤害的器械/工具。法律效力：产生一种推论，即医疗团队的每一位成员均存在过失；要求每位成员承担举证责任，以证明其本人无过失。医疗事故索赔通常基于过失，且一般要求证明被告违反了专业护理标准。\n\n然而，当由一个医疗专业团队执行复杂程序（如手术）时，这一点往往难以证明。\n\n在此类情况下，“事实不言自明”原则允许事实认定者推断医疗团队的每一位成员均存在过失，前提是：患者在接受医疗过程中处于无意识状态时遭受了异常伤害（即通常由过失引起的伤害）；且 医疗团队中至少有一名成员控制着患者的身体或可能导致患者受伤的器械/工具。\n\n本案中，患者在麻醉苏醒后发现自己遭受了严重烧伤，并据此起诉了其医疗团队。\n\n尽管患者持有证明专业护理标准被违反的宣誓书，但她无法确定具体是哪位被告存在过失（选项 C）。\n\n然而，由于至少有一名被告控制着导致其异常伤害的器械/工具，因此可以推断所有被告均存在过失。\n\n因此，这是患者针对被告提出的即决判决动议所能提出的最有力论点。\n\n（选项 B）共同行动（即经协议一致行动）并伤害原告的侵权行为人，需对全部伤害承担连带责任。\n\n但本案中，没有证据表明被告存在共同行动，且有可能仅由一名被告导致了患者的烧伤。\n\n（选项 D）患者对其受伤不负有责任，因为受伤发生时她处于麻醉状态。\n\n但她无过错这一事实并不能证明医疗团队中的某人存在过失。\n\n教学目标：根据“事实不言自明”原则，当 (1) 患者在接受医疗过程中处于无意识状态时遭受异常伤害，且 (2) 团队中至少有一名成员控制着患者的身体或可能导致伤害的器械/工具时，即可推断医疗团队的每一位成员均存在过失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第328D条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐述了在何种情况下，可根据导致原告受伤的事故类型推断被告存在过失）。\n\n*Ybarra v. Spangard*案，25 Cal. 2d 486, 494 (1944)（裁定：在患者遭受异常伤害期间，凡控制处于无意识状态的患者身体的被告，均可被推断存在过失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”（res ipsa loquitur）原则，当（1）患者在接受医疗过程中处于无意识状态时遭受了异常伤害，且（2）医疗团队中至少有一名成员控制着患者的身体或可能导致伤害的器械时，可以推断医疗团队的每一位成员都存在疏忽。","iracZh":{"issue":"针对该申请，患者最有力的论点是什么？","rule":"根据“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”（res ipsa loquitur）原则，当（1）患者在接受医疗过程中处于无意识状态时遭受了异常伤害，且（2）医疗团队中至少有一名成员控制着患者的身体或可能导致伤害的器械时，可以推断医疗团队的每一位成员都存在疏忽。","analysis":"“事实不言自明”原则 (Res ipsa loquitur) （医疗事故索赔） 适用条件：原告在接受医疗过程中处于无意识状态时遭受了异常伤害；医疗团队中至少有一名成员控制着患者的身体或导致伤害的器械/工具。法律效力：产生一种推论，即医疗团队的每一位成员均存在过失；要求每位成员承担举证责任，以证明其本人无过失。医疗事故索赔通常基于过失，且一般要求证明被告违反了专业护理标准。\n\n然而，当由一个医疗专业团队执行复杂程序（如手术）时，这一点往往难以证明。\n\n在此类情况下，“事实不言自明”原则允许事实认定者推断医疗团队的每一位成员均存在过失，前提是：患者在接受医疗过程中处于无意识状态时遭受了异常伤害（即通常由过失引起的伤害）；且 医疗团队中至少有一名成员控制着患者的身体或可能导致患者受伤的器械/工具。\n\n本案中，患者在麻醉苏醒后发现自己遭受了严重烧伤，并据此起诉了其医疗团队。\n\n尽管患者持有证明专业护理标准被违反的宣誓书，但她无法确定具体是哪位被告存在过失（选项 C）。\n\n然而，由于至少有一名被告控制着导致其异常伤害的器械/工具，因此可以推断所有被告均存在过失。\n\n因此，这是患者针对被告提出的即决判决动议所能提出的最有力论点。\n\n（选项 B）共同行动（即经协议一致行动）并伤害原告的侵权行为人，需对全部伤害承担连带责任。\n\n但本案中，没有证据表明被告存在共同行动，且有可能仅由一名被告导致了患者的烧伤。\n\n（选项 D）患者对其受伤不负有责任，因为受伤发生时她处于麻醉状态。\n\n但她无过错这一事实并不能证明医疗团队中的某人存在过失。\n\n教学目标：根据“事实不言自明”原则，当 (1) 患者在接受医疗过程中处于无意识状态时遭受异常伤害，且 (2) 团队中至少有一名成员控制着患者的身体或可能导致伤害的器械/工具时，即可推断医疗团队的每一位成员均存在过失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第328D条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐述了在何种情况下，可根据导致原告受伤的事故类型推断被告存在过失）。\n\n*Ybarra v. Spangard*案，25 Cal. 2d 486, 494 (1944)（裁定：在患者遭受异常伤害期间，凡控制处于无意识状态的患者身体的被告，均可被推断存在过失）。","conclusion":"答案 A：导致患者受伤的机构或器械至少由其中一名被告控制。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":67,"sourceQuestionPage":202,"sourceQuestionPages":[202],"sourceExplanationPages":[203,204],"sourceAnswerPages":[203,204],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-068","subject":"Torts","topic":"Damages & Joint Liability","question":"An eight-year-old boy sometimes rode his bicycle down his driveway into a busy highway while unsupervised. The boy's parents were aware of this behavior but did nothing to stop it. One day, a driver had to stop her car suddenly to avoid colliding with the bike. Because of the sudden stop, the driver's two-year-old daughter, who was sitting on the seat without any restraint, was thrown into the dashboard and injured. Had the daughter been properly restrained in a baby car seat, as required by a state safety statute of which her mother was aware, she would not have been injured. In an action brought on the daughter's behalf against the eight-year-old boy's parents for her injuries, will she prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because parents are not vicariously liable for the negligent acts of their children.","B":"No, because the daughter's injury was attributable to the driver's knowing violation of a safety statute.","C":"Yes, because the boy riding into the highway was negligent and the proximate cause of the daughter's injuries.","D":"Yes, because the boy's parents knew that he sometimes drove into the highway, and they took no steps to prevent it."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Special relationships\n\nimposing duty to control others\n\nParent/Child Mental health professional/Patient Employer/Employee Custodian/Prisoner Mnemonic: Please Make Everyone Careful\n\nA defendant generally has no duty to control another's conduct unless the parties have a special relationship. Since parents and children have a special relationship, parents have a duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent their minor child from causing foreseeable harm to others. Therefore, parents are liable for negligence if they breach this duty and cause the plaintiff harm. Here, the parents had a duty to use reasonable care to prevent their eight-year-old son from harming others since they knew that he sometimes rode his bicycle into the highway (ie, it was foreseeable that he might harm others). They breached that duty when they failed to take reasonable steps to prevent their son from doing so (eg, by supervising him). And since their negligence caused (at least in part) the daughter's injuries, she will prevail. (Choices A & C) Parents are generally not vicariously liable for their child's negligence unless the child was acting as their agent (ie, with the authority to act on their behalf). Since there is no evidence that the boy was acting as his parents' agent, they are not vicariously liable for his negligence—even though that negligence was a cause of the daughter's injuries. However, the parents are still directly liable for their own negligence. (Choice B) Under the doctrine of joint and several liability, multiple tortfeasors can be liable together or separately for the plaintiff's entire harm. Here, the daughter's injury is attributable to multiple tortfeasors—the driver who knowingly violated a safety statute and the negligent parents. Therefore, the daughter could seek recovery for all her injuries from the driver and/or the parents.\n\nEducational objective: Parents owe a duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent their minor child from causing foreseeable harm to others.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 3 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence defined).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 41 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (parental duty to control child).","educationalObjective":"Parents owe a duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent their minor child from causing foreseeable harm to others.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 3 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence defined).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 41 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (parental duty to control child).","rule":"Parents owe a duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent their minor child from causing foreseeable harm to others.","irac":{"issue":"In an action brought on the daughter's behalf against the eight-year-old boy's parents for her injuries, will she prevail?","rule":"Parents owe a duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent their minor child from causing foreseeable harm to others.","analysis":"Special relationships\n\nimposing duty to control others\n\nParent/Child Mental health professional/Patient Employer/Employee Custodian/Prisoner Mnemonic: Please Make Everyone Careful\n\nA defendant generally has no duty to control another's conduct unless the parties have a special relationship. Since parents and children have a special relationship, parents have a duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent their minor child from causing foreseeable harm to others. Therefore, parents are liable for negligence if they breach this duty and cause the plaintiff harm. Here, the parents had a duty to use reasonable care to prevent their eight-year-old son from harming others since they knew that he sometimes rode his bicycle into the highway (ie, it was foreseeable that he might harm others). They breached that duty when they failed to take reasonable steps to prevent their son from doing so (eg, by supervising him). And since their negligence caused (at least in part) the daughter's injuries, she will prevail. (Choices A & C) Parents are generally not vicariously liable for their child's negligence unless the child was acting as their agent (ie, with the authority to act on their behalf). Since there is no evidence that the boy was acting as his parents' agent, they are not vicariously liable for his negligence—even though that negligence was a cause of the daughter's injuries. However, the parents are still directly liable for their own negligence. (Choice B) Under the doctrine of joint and several liability, multiple tortfeasors can be liable together or separately for the plaintiff's entire harm. Here, the daughter's injury is attributable to multiple tortfeasors—the driver who knowingly violated a safety statute and the negligent parents. Therefore, the daughter could seek recovery for all her injuries from the driver and/or the parents.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the boy's parents knew that he sometimes drove into the highway, and they took no steps to prevent it."},"questionZh":"一名八岁男孩有时会在无人看管的情况下，骑自行车从自家车道冲上繁忙的公路。\n\n男孩的父母知晓这一行为，却未采取任何措施加以制止。\n\n某日，一位女司机为避免撞上该自行车而不得不紧急刹车。\n\n由于这次急刹车，她那未受任何约束装置保护、坐在座位上的两岁女儿被甩向仪表盘并受伤。\n\n如果该女儿当时按照州安全法规的要求（其母亲已知晓该法规）被妥善安置在婴儿安全座椅内，她本不会受伤。\n\n若该女儿就其受伤一事起诉那名八岁男孩的父母，她能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为父母不对其子女的过失行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","B":"不能，因为该女儿的受伤归因于司机明知故犯地违反了安全法规。","C":"能，因为男孩骑车冲上公路的行为构成过失，且是导致该女儿受伤的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）。","D":"能，因为男孩的父母知道他有时会骑车冲上公路，却未采取措施防止此类行为发生。"},"explanationZh":"特殊关系 施加控制他人行为的义务 父母/子女；心理健康专业人员/患者；雇主/雇员；监管人/被监管者（囚犯）。记忆口诀：Please Make Everyone Careful（请让每个人都保持谨慎）。\n\n除非当事人之间存在特殊关系，否则被告通常没有义务控制他人的行为。由于父母与子女之间存在特殊关系，父母有义务尽到合理的注意，防止其未成年子女对他人的可预见伤害。\n\n因此，如果父母违反了这一义务并导致原告受到伤害，他们即构成过失并需承担责任。\n\n在本案中，父母有义务尽到合理的注意，防止其八岁的儿子伤害他人，因为他们知道儿子有时会骑自行车进入公路（即，儿子可能伤害他人是可预见的）。\n\n当他们未能采取合理措施（例如进行监管）来阻止儿子这样做时，他们便违反了该义务。\n\n由于他们的过失（至少部分地）导致了女儿受伤，女儿将胜诉。\n\n（选项 A 和 C）父母通常不对其子女的过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），除非子女是作为其代理人行事（即拥有代表他们行事的权限）。\n\n由于没有证据表明该男孩是作为其父母的代理人行事，因此父母不对其过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）——尽管该过失是导致女儿受伤的原因之一。\n\n然而，父母仍需对其自身的过失承担直接责任。\n\n（选项 B）根据连带责任原则，多个侵权行为人可共同或分别对原告遭受的全部损害承担责任。\n\n在本案中，女儿受到的伤害归因于多个侵权行为人——即明知违反安全法规的司机和有过失的父母。\n\n因此，女儿可以向司机和/或父母寻求对其全部伤害的赔偿。\n\n教育目标：父母有义务尽到合理的注意，防止其未成年子女对他人的可预见伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 3 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（过失的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任……》\n\n《人身与情感伤害法重述》第41条（美国法律学会，2012年）（父母管束子女的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"父母有义务尽到合理的注意，防止其未成年子女对他人的可预见伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"若该女儿就其受伤一事起诉那名八岁男孩的父母，她能胜诉吗？","rule":"父母有义务尽到合理的注意，防止其未成年子女对他人的可预见伤害。","analysis":"特殊关系 施加控制他人行为的义务 父母/子女；心理健康专业人员/患者；雇主/雇员；监管人/被监管者（囚犯）。记忆口诀：Please Make Everyone Careful（请让每个人都保持谨慎）。\n\n除非当事人之间存在特殊关系，否则被告通常没有义务控制他人的行为。由于父母与子女之间存在特殊关系，父母有义务尽到合理的注意，防止其未成年子女对他人的可预见伤害。\n\n因此，如果父母违反了这一义务并导致原告受到伤害，他们即构成过失并需承担责任。\n\n在本案中，父母有义务尽到合理的注意，防止其八岁的儿子伤害他人，因为他们知道儿子有时会骑自行车进入公路（即，儿子可能伤害他人是可预见的）。\n\n当他们未能采取合理措施（例如进行监管）来阻止儿子这样做时，他们便违反了该义务。\n\n由于他们的过失（至少部分地）导致了女儿受伤，女儿将胜诉。\n\n（选项 A 和 C）父母通常不对其子女的过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），除非子女是作为其代理人行事（即拥有代表他们行事的权限）。\n\n由于没有证据表明该男孩是作为其父母的代理人行事，因此父母不对其过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）——尽管该过失是导致女儿受伤的原因之一。\n\n然而，父母仍需对其自身的过失承担直接责任。\n\n（选项 B）根据连带责任原则，多个侵权行为人可共同或分别对原告遭受的全部损害承担责任。\n\n在本案中，女儿受到的伤害归因于多个侵权行为人——即明知违反安全法规的司机和有过失的父母。\n\n因此，女儿可以向司机和/或父母寻求对其全部伤害的赔偿。\n\n教育目标：父母有义务尽到合理的注意，防止其未成年子女对他人的可预见伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 3 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（过失的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任……》\n\n《人身与情感伤害法重述》第41条（美国法律学会，2012年）（父母管束子女的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 D：能，因为男孩的父母知道他有时会骑车冲上公路，却未采取措施防止此类行为发生。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"joint and several liability","ipa":"/dʒˈɔɪnt ənd sˈɛvɹəl lˌaɪʌbˈɪlɪti/","syllables":"joint and sev·eral li·a·bil·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"连带责任：一名共同侵权人可被要求承担全部不可分损害","contextualMeaning":"连带责任：一名共同侵权人可被要求承担全部不可分损害","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) Under the doctrine of joint and several liability, multiple tortfeasors can be liable together or separately for the plaintiff's entire harm.","synonyms":["full liability of each joint tortfeasor"]},{"term":"vicariously","ipa":"/vaɪkˈɛɹiəsli/","syllables":"vic·ar·i·ously","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 代理地, 担任代理者地；间接感受到地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“vicariously”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"No, because parents are not vicariously liable for the negligent acts of their children.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":68,"sourceQuestionPage":205,"sourceQuestionPages":[205],"sourceExplanationPages":[206,207],"sourceAnswerPages":[206,207],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-069","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A pedestrian who was blind took her guide dog to the veterinarian for a checkup. While she was waiting, she decided to get a coffee at the coffee shop across the street from the veterinarian's office. Without the assistance of her guide dog, the pedestrian left the veterinarian's office. She was able to find the crosswalk because it was just outside the entrance to the office. Once there, she pressed the crosswalk button on the light post. When the audible pedestrian signal indicated that it was safe to cross the street, the pedestrian stepped out into the crosswalk. Another person walking in the crosswalk accidentally bumped into the pedestrian, causing the pedestrian to unknowingly veer out of the crosswalk and into oncoming traffic. At the same time, the plaintiff was driving his car toward the pedestrian. In order to avoid hitting the pedestrian, the plaintiff swerved into a lamppost. The plaintiff sustained injuries as a result of the accident. The plaintiff has filed a negligence action against the pedestrian to recover damages for his injuries. Which of the following standards will the court apply to the pedestrian's conduct?","choices":{"A":"Whether the pedestrian acted in good faith when she crossed the street without her guide dog.","B":"Whether the pedestrian exercised the care of a reasonably careful blind person.","C":"Whether the pedestrian exercised the care of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances.","D":"Whether the pedestrian used her best efforts to safely cross the street without her guide dog."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nThe standard of care most often imposed in negligence cases is that of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances. A defendant's mental or emotional disabilities are not considered in determining whether conduct was negligent unless the defendant is a child. This means that the conduct of a defendant with a mental disability will be measured by the standard of someone of ordinary intelligence and knowledge. However, a defendant's particular physical characteristics (eg, blindness) are considered. This means that the conduct of a defendant with a physical disability will be measured by the standard of a reasonably careful person with the same disability. Therefore, the standard that the court will apply to the pedestrian's conduct is whether she exercised the care of a reasonably careful blind person (Choice C). (Choices A & D) The standard of care for negligence is an objective one, measured by what a reasonably prudent person with the same physical characteristics would have done. Whether a particular defendant acted in good faith or used his/her best efforts is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: In negligence cases, the conduct of a defendant with a physical disability (eg, blindness) will be measured by the standard of a reasonably careful person with the same disability.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 11 (2010) (standard of care for persons with a disability).","educationalObjective":"In negligence cases, the conduct of a defendant with a physical disability (eg, blindness) will be measured by the standard of a reasonably careful person with the same disability.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 11 (2010) (standard of care for persons with a disability).","rule":"In negligence cases, the conduct of a defendant with a physical disability (eg, blindness) will be measured by the standard of a reasonably careful person with the same disability.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following standards will the court apply to the pedestrian's conduct?","rule":"In negligence cases, the conduct of a defendant with a physical disability (eg, blindness) will be measured by the standard of a reasonably careful person with the same disability.","analysis":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nThe standard of care most often imposed in negligence cases is that of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances. A defendant's mental or emotional disabilities are not considered in determining whether conduct was negligent unless the defendant is a child. This means that the conduct of a defendant with a mental disability will be measured by the standard of someone of ordinary intelligence and knowledge. However, a defendant's particular physical characteristics (eg, blindness) are considered. This means that the conduct of a defendant with a physical disability will be measured by the standard of a reasonably careful person with the same disability. Therefore, the standard that the court will apply to the pedestrian's conduct is whether she exercised the care of a reasonably careful blind person (Choice C). (Choices A & D) The standard of care for negligence is an objective one, measured by what a reasonably prudent person with the same physical characteristics would have done. Whether a particular defendant acted in good faith or used his/her best efforts is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer B: Whether the pedestrian exercised the care of a reasonably careful blind person."},"questionZh":"一位失明的行人带着她的导盲犬去兽医诊所做检查。\n\n在等待期间，她决定去诊所街对面的咖啡店买杯咖啡。\n\n这位行人离开了兽医诊所，但没有带导盲犬。\n\n她找到了人行横道，因为它就在诊所入口外。\n\n到达那里后，她按下了灯柱上的人行横道按钮。\n\n当行人信号灯发出提示音，表明过马路安全时，她便步入了人行横道。\n\n另一位在人行横道上行走的人不小心撞到了她，导致她无意中偏离了人行横道，走向了迎面而来的车流。\n\n与此同时，原告正驾车驶向该行人。\n\n为了避免撞到行人，原告急打方向盘，结果撞上了路边的灯柱。\n\n原告在事故中受伤，并针对该行人提起了过失诉讼，以寻求对其伤害进行赔偿。\n\n法院将采用以下哪种标准来评判该行人的行为？","choicesZh":{"A":"该行人在未带导盲犬过马路时是否出于善意。","B":"该行人是否尽到了一个相当谨慎的盲人应尽的注意义务。","C":"在该情况下，该行人是否尽到了一个相当审慎的人应尽的注意义务。","D":"该行人在未带导盲犬的情况下，是否已尽最大努力安全过马路。"},"explanationZh":"过失侵权中的注意义务标准 成年人：必须像处于类似情况下的普通、理性成年人那样行事。 标准调整情形：身体残疾、具备卓越技能与知识、非自愿醉酒。标准不调整情形：精神与情绪障碍、自愿醉酒。专业人士：必须展现出与同类社区中其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和审慎程度。 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区）标准*。儿童：必须像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 从事危险成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准；五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意义务标准。\n\n在过失侵权案件中，最常适用的注意义务标准是“处于特定情况下的理性审慎之人”的标准。\n\n在判定行为是否存在过失时，不考虑被告的精神或情绪障碍（除非被告是儿童）。\n\n这意味着，患有精神障碍的被告，其行为将依据普通智力和知识水平的人的标准来衡量。\n\n然而，被告特定的身体特征（例如失明）会被纳入考量。\n\n这意味着，患有身体残疾的被告，其行为将依据具有相同残疾的理性审慎之人的标准来衡量。\n\n因此，法院将用来衡量该行人行为的标准是：她是否尽到了一个理性审慎的盲人应尽的注意义务（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 A 和 D）过失侵权中的注意义务标准是客观的，即以具有相同身体特征的理性审慎之人会采取的行动为衡量尺度。\n\n特定被告是否出于善意或已尽最大努力，均与判定无关。\n\n教育目标：在过失侵权案件中，患有身体残疾（例如失明）的被告，其行为将依据具有相同残疾的理性审慎之人的标准来衡量。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与情绪伤害责任》第 11 条 (2010)（关于残疾人士注意义务标准的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在过失案件中，对于有身体残疾（如失明）的被告，其行为将依据“具有相同残疾的相当谨慎的人”这一标准进行评判。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院将采用以下哪种标准来评判该行人的行为？","rule":"在过失案件中，对于有身体残疾（如失明）的被告，其行为将依据“具有相同残疾的相当谨慎的人”这一标准进行评判。","analysis":"过失侵权中的注意义务标准 成年人：必须像处于类似情况下的普通、理性成年人那样行事。 标准调整情形：身体残疾、具备卓越技能与知识、非自愿醉酒。标准不调整情形：精神与情绪障碍、自愿醉酒。专业人士：必须展现出与同类社区中其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和审慎程度。 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区）标准*。儿童：必须像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 从事危险成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准；五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意义务标准。\n\n在过失侵权案件中，最常适用的注意义务标准是“处于特定情况下的理性审慎之人”的标准。\n\n在判定行为是否存在过失时，不考虑被告的精神或情绪障碍（除非被告是儿童）。\n\n这意味着，患有精神障碍的被告，其行为将依据普通智力和知识水平的人的标准来衡量。\n\n然而，被告特定的身体特征（例如失明）会被纳入考量。\n\n这意味着，患有身体残疾的被告，其行为将依据具有相同残疾的理性审慎之人的标准来衡量。\n\n因此，法院将用来衡量该行人行为的标准是：她是否尽到了一个理性审慎的盲人应尽的注意义务（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 A 和 D）过失侵权中的注意义务标准是客观的，即以具有相同身体特征的理性审慎之人会采取的行动为衡量尺度。\n\n特定被告是否出于善意或已尽最大努力，均与判定无关。\n\n教育目标：在过失侵权案件中，患有身体残疾（例如失明）的被告，其行为将依据具有相同残疾的理性审慎之人的标准来衡量。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与情绪伤害责任》第 11 条 (2010)（关于残疾人士注意义务标准的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该行人是否尽到了一个相当谨慎的盲人应尽的注意义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":69,"sourceQuestionPage":208,"sourceQuestionPages":[208],"sourceExplanationPages":[209,210],"sourceAnswerPages":[209,210],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-070","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"A firstborn child was examined as an infant by a doctor who was a specialist in the diagnosis of speech and hearing impairments. Although the doctor should have concluded that the infant was totally deaf due to a hereditary condition, the doctor negligently concluded that the infant's hearing was normal. After the diagnosis, but before they learned that the infant was in fact deaf, the parents conceived a second child who also suffered total deafness due to the hereditary condition. The parents claim that they would not have conceived the second child had they known of the high probability of the hereditary condition. They have sought the advice of their attorney regarding which negligence action against the doctor is most likely to succeed. What sort of action against the doctor should the attorney recommend?","choices":{"A":"A medical malpractice action seeking damages on the second child's behalf for expenses related to his deafness, on the ground that the doctor's negligence caused him to be born deaf.","B":"A wrongful birth action by the parents for expenses they have incurred due to the second child's deafness, on the ground that but for the doctor's negligence, they would not have conceived the second child.","C":"A wrongful life action by the parents for expenses for the entire period of the second child's life, on the ground that but for the doctor's negligence, the second child would not have been born.","D":"A wrongful life action on the second child's behalf for expenses for the entire period of his life, on the ground that but for the doctor's negligence, he would not have been born."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Negligence claims for wrongfully causing conception, birth, or life\n\nType of claim Description Damages\n\nWrongful Parent's claim for birth of Cost of defective pregnancy/conception healthy but unwanted child procedure/device Pregnancy/delivery expenses Eg, negligently performed vasectomy, defective Cost of remedial measures contraceptive device Emotional distress Wrongful birth Parent's claim for birth of Majority view: unhealthy child Cost of accommodating child's special needs minus benefit of Eg, doctor failed to disclose having child information that would have led parent to avoid or Pain & suffering terminate pregnancy Emotional distress Minority view: no recovery Wrongful life Unhealthy child's claim for Majority view: no recovery unwanted life Minority view: cost of accommodating child's Eg, disabled child would not medical needs have been born absent physician's negligence A negligence action based on a theory of wrongful birth can only be brought by a parent and requires proof that: the parent and defendant had a patient-doctor relationship (duty)\n\nthe defendant negligently failed to diagnose or inform the parent of the substantial risk that his/her future child may suffer from a birth defect (breach)\n\nhad the parent known about that risk, the child would not have been conceived or born (causation) and\n\nthe parent suffered monetary loss due to the additional care and treatment of the child (damages).\n\nHere, the doctor negligently failed to conclude that the parents' first child was deaf due to a hereditary condition. Because of that negligence, the parents did not learn that their future children had a substantial risk of being born deaf until after their second child had been conceived. Since the parents would not have conceived their second child had the doctor\n\ninformed them of that risk, they should file a wrongful birth action for expenses incurred due to the second child's deafness. (Choice A) A medical malpractice action on behalf of the second child would require proof that the doctor's negligence caused the child physical harm. Here, though the doctor's negligence caused the child to be born, it did not cause the child to be born deaf (a hereditary condition). (Choices C & D) A wrongful life action alleges that an unhealthy child would not have been born absent a doctor's negligence. Most states do not permit wrongful life actions. In the states that do, the action can only be brought by or on behalf of the unhealthy child—not by the parents. Additionally, those states generally limit the child's recovery to expenses attributable to the disability—not all life expenses.\n\nEducational objective: A wrongful birth claim allows parents to recover for the birth of an unhealthy child if they can prove that (1) the doctor negligently failed to inform them of the substantial risk that their future child may have a birth defect and (2) had the parents known about that risk, the child would not have been conceived or born.","educationalObjective":"A wrongful birth claim allows parents to recover for the birth of an unhealthy child if they can prove that (1) the doctor negligently failed to inform them of the substantial risk that their future child may have a birth defect and (2) had the parents known about that risk, the child would not have been conceived or born.","references":"","rule":"A wrongful birth claim allows parents to recover for the birth of an unhealthy child if they can prove that (1) the doctor negligently failed to inform them of the substantial risk that their future child may have a birth defect and (2) had the parents known about that risk, the child would not have been conceived or born.","irac":{"issue":"What sort of action against the doctor should the attorney recommend?","rule":"A wrongful birth claim allows parents to recover for the birth of an unhealthy child if they can prove that (1) the doctor negligently failed to inform them of the substantial risk that their future child may have a birth defect and (2) had the parents known about that risk, the child would not have been conceived or born.","analysis":"Negligence claims for wrongfully causing conception, birth, or life\n\nType of claim Description Damages\n\nWrongful Parent's claim for birth of Cost of defective pregnancy/conception healthy but unwanted child procedure/device Pregnancy/delivery expenses Eg, negligently performed vasectomy, defective Cost of remedial measures contraceptive device Emotional distress Wrongful birth Parent's claim for birth of Majority view: unhealthy child Cost of accommodating child's special needs minus benefit of Eg, doctor failed to disclose having child information that would have led parent to avoid or Pain & suffering terminate pregnancy Emotional distress Minority view: no recovery Wrongful life Unhealthy child's claim for Majority view: no recovery unwanted life Minority view: cost of accommodating child's Eg, disabled child would not medical needs have been born absent physician's negligence A negligence action based on a theory of wrongful birth can only be brought by a parent and requires proof that: the parent and defendant had a patient-doctor relationship (duty)\n\nthe defendant negligently failed to diagnose or inform the parent of the substantial risk that his/her future child may suffer from a birth defect (breach)\n\nhad the parent known about that risk, the child would not have been conceived or born (causation) and\n\nthe parent suffered monetary loss due to the additional care and treatment of the child (damages).\n\nHere, the doctor negligently failed to conclude that the parents' first child was deaf due to a hereditary condition. Because of that negligence, the parents did not learn that their future children had a substantial risk of being born deaf until after their second child had been conceived. Since the parents would not have conceived their second child had the doctor\n\ninformed them of that risk, they should file a wrongful birth action for expenses incurred due to the second child's deafness. (Choice A) A medical malpractice action on behalf of the second child would require proof that the doctor's negligence caused the child physical harm. Here, though the doctor's negligence caused the child to be born, it did not cause the child to be born deaf (a hereditary condition). (Choices C & D) A wrongful life action alleges that an unhealthy child would not have been born absent a doctor's negligence. Most states do not permit wrongful life actions. In the states that do, the action can only be brought by or on behalf of the unhealthy child—not by the parents. Additionally, those states generally limit the child's recovery to expenses attributable to the disability—not all life expenses.","conclusion":"Answer B: A wrongful birth action by the parents for expenses they have incurred due to the second child's deafness, on the ground that but for the doctor's negligence, they would not have conceived the second child."},"questionZh":"一位专攻言语和听力障碍诊断的医生曾检查过一名婴儿（家中的长子）。\n\n尽管医生本应得出该婴儿因遗传性疾病而完全失聪的结论，却因疏忽大意而判定其听力正常。\n\n在做出该诊断之后、但在得知婴儿实际上已失聪之前，父母怀上了第二个孩子；该孩子也因同样的遗传性疾病而完全失聪。\n\n父母声称，如果当时知道该遗传性疾病的高发风险，他们就不会怀上第二个孩子。\n\n他们就针对该医生的哪种过失诉讼最有可能胜诉，向律师寻求建议。\n\n律师应建议针对该医生提起何种诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"代表第二个孩子提起的医疗事故损害赔偿诉讼，要求赔偿与其失聪相关的费用，理由是医生的过失导致他生来失聪。","B":"由父母提起的“不当生育”（wrongful birth）诉讼，要求赔偿因第二个孩子失聪而产生的费用，理由是若非医生的过失，他们就不会怀上第二个孩子。","C":"由父母提起的“不当生命”（wrongful life）诉讼，要求赔偿第二个孩子整个生命周期的费用，理由是若非医生的过失，第二个孩子就不会出生。","D":"代表第二个孩子提起的“不当生命”诉讼，要求赔偿其整个生命周期的费用，理由是若非医生的过失，他就不会出生。"},"explanationZh":"因不当导致受孕、出生或生命而提出的过失索赔 索赔类型 | 描述 | 损害赔偿 **不当受孕 (Wrongful conception)** | 父母因生下健康但非意愿中的孩子而提出的索赔（例如：因输精管结扎术操作疏忽或避孕器具缺陷导致受孕） | 妊娠/受孕程序或器具的费用；妊娠/分娩费用；补救措施费用；精神痛苦 **不当出生 (Wrongful birth)** | 父母因生下不健康孩子而提出的索赔（例如：医生未披露相关信息，而该信息本可促使父母避免受孕或终止妊娠） | 主流观点：满足孩子特殊需求的费用（扣除养育孩子的益处）；痛苦与折磨；精神痛苦。少数观点：不予赔偿 **不当生命 (Wrongful life)** | 不健康孩子因非意愿中的生命而提出的索赔（例如：若无医生过失，残疾孩子本不会出生） | 主流观点：不予赔偿。少数观点：满足孩子医疗需求的费用 基于“不当出生”理论提起的过失诉讼只能由父母提起，且需要证明：父母与被告之间存在医患关系（注意义务）； 被告过失地未能诊断出或告知父母其未来子女可能患有先天缺陷的重大风险（违反义务）； 如果父母知晓该风险，孩子本不会受孕或出生（因果关系）；以及 父母因孩子的额外护理和治疗而遭受了经济损失（损害赔偿）。\n\n在本案中，医生过失地未能断定父母的第一个孩子是因遗传性疾病而失聪。\n\n由于这种过失，父母直到第二个孩子受孕后才得知其未来子女有出生即失聪的重大风险。\n\n鉴于如果医生告知了该风险，父母本不会怀上第二个孩子，因此他们应就因第二个孩子失聪而产生的费用提起“不当出生”诉讼。\n\n（选项 A）代表第二个孩子提起的医疗事故诉讼需要证明医生的过失对孩子造成了身体伤害。\n\n在本案中，尽管医生的过失导致了孩子的出生，但并未导致孩子出生时失聪（这是一种遗传性疾病）。\n\n（选项 C 和 D）“不当生命”诉讼主张，若无医生的过失，不健康的孩子本不会出生。\n\n大多数州不允许提起“不当生命”诉讼。\n\n在允许此类诉讼的州，诉讼只能由患病子女本人或代表其提起，而不能由父母提起。\n\n此外，这些州通常将子女可获得的赔偿限制在与该缺陷相关的费用范围内，而不包括其一生的所有生活费用。\n\n教育目标：“不当出生”（wrongful birth）索赔允许父母因生下患病子女而获得赔偿，前提是他们能证明：(1) 医生存在疏忽，未告知他们未来子女可能存在出生缺陷的重大风险；以及 (2) 如果父母当时知晓该风险，该子女便不会受孕或出生。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“不当生育”诉讼允许父母因生下不健康的子女而获得赔偿，前提是他们能证明：(1) 医生疏忽大意，未告知他们未来子女可能存在出生缺陷的重大风险；以及 (2) 如果父母当时知晓该风险，该子女就不会被受孕或出生。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师应建议针对该医生提起何种诉讼？","rule":"“不当生育”诉讼允许父母因生下不健康的子女而获得赔偿，前提是他们能证明：(1) 医生疏忽大意，未告知他们未来子女可能存在出生缺陷的重大风险；以及 (2) 如果父母当时知晓该风险，该子女就不会被受孕或出生。","analysis":"因不当导致受孕、出生或生命而提出的过失索赔 索赔类型 | 描述 | 损害赔偿 **不当受孕 (Wrongful conception)** | 父母因生下健康但非意愿中的孩子而提出的索赔（例如：因输精管结扎术操作疏忽或避孕器具缺陷导致受孕） | 妊娠/受孕程序或器具的费用；妊娠/分娩费用；补救措施费用；精神痛苦 **不当出生 (Wrongful birth)** | 父母因生下不健康孩子而提出的索赔（例如：医生未披露相关信息，而该信息本可促使父母避免受孕或终止妊娠） | 主流观点：满足孩子特殊需求的费用（扣除养育孩子的益处）；痛苦与折磨；精神痛苦。少数观点：不予赔偿 **不当生命 (Wrongful life)** | 不健康孩子因非意愿中的生命而提出的索赔（例如：若无医生过失，残疾孩子本不会出生） | 主流观点：不予赔偿。少数观点：满足孩子医疗需求的费用 基于“不当出生”理论提起的过失诉讼只能由父母提起，且需要证明：父母与被告之间存在医患关系（注意义务）； 被告过失地未能诊断出或告知父母其未来子女可能患有先天缺陷的重大风险（违反义务）； 如果父母知晓该风险，孩子本不会受孕或出生（因果关系）；以及 父母因孩子的额外护理和治疗而遭受了经济损失（损害赔偿）。\n\n在本案中，医生过失地未能断定父母的第一个孩子是因遗传性疾病而失聪。\n\n由于这种过失，父母直到第二个孩子受孕后才得知其未来子女有出生即失聪的重大风险。\n\n鉴于如果医生告知了该风险，父母本不会怀上第二个孩子，因此他们应就因第二个孩子失聪而产生的费用提起“不当出生”诉讼。\n\n（选项 A）代表第二个孩子提起的医疗事故诉讼需要证明医生的过失对孩子造成了身体伤害。\n\n在本案中，尽管医生的过失导致了孩子的出生，但并未导致孩子出生时失聪（这是一种遗传性疾病）。\n\n（选项 C 和 D）“不当生命”诉讼主张，若无医生的过失，不健康的孩子本不会出生。\n\n大多数州不允许提起“不当生命”诉讼。\n\n在允许此类诉讼的州，诉讼只能由患病子女本人或代表其提起，而不能由父母提起。\n\n此外，这些州通常将子女可获得的赔偿限制在与该缺陷相关的费用范围内，而不包括其一生的所有生活费用。\n\n教育目标：“不当出生”（wrongful birth）索赔允许父母因生下患病子女而获得赔偿，前提是他们能证明：(1) 医生存在疏忽，未告知他们未来子女可能存在出生缺陷的重大风险；以及 (2) 如果父母当时知晓该风险，该子女便不会受孕或出生。","conclusion":"答案 B：由父母提起的“不当生育”（wrongful birth）诉讼，要求赔偿因第二个孩子失聪而产生的费用，理由是若非医生的过失，他们就不会怀上第二个孩子。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"defective","ipa":"/dɪfˈɛktɪv/","syllables":"de·fec·tive","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 有缺陷的, 欠缺的；n. 有缺陷的人, 不完全变化词","contextualMeaning":"有缺陷的：制造、设计或警示未达到法律安全要求","wordForm":"词；词形 s:defectives","sourceSentence":"Negligence claims for wrongfully causing conception, birth, or life Type of claim Description Damages Wrongful Parent's claim for birth of Cost of defective pregnancy/conception healthy but unwanted child procedure/device Pregnancy/delivery expenses Eg, negligently performed vasectomy, defective Cost of remedial measures contraceptive device Emotional distress Wrongful birth Parent's claim for birth of Majority view:","synonyms":["bad","faulty"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":70,"sourceQuestionPage":211,"sourceQuestionPages":[211],"sourceExplanationPages":[212,213],"sourceAnswerPages":[212,213],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-071","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A bright 12-year-old child attended a day-care center after school. The center was located near a man-made duck pond on the property of a corporation. During the winter, the pond was used for ice-skating when conditions were suitable. At a time when the pond was obviously only partially frozen, the child sneaked away from the center's property and walked out onto the ice over the pond. The ice gave way, and the child fell into the cold water. He suffered shock and would have drowned had he not been rescued by a passerby. At the time of the incident, the pond was clearly marked with numerous signs that stated, \"THIN ICE—KEEP OFF.\" When the child sneaked away from the day-care center, the center was staffed with a reasonable number of qualified employees, and the employees were exercising reasonable care to ensure that the children in their charge did not leave the premises. There had not been a previous instance of a child coming onto the corporation's property from the day-care center. The jurisdiction follows a rule of pure comparative negligence. In a suit brought on the child's behalf against the day-care center and based only on the facts above, who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The child, because he left the center while he was under the center's care.","B":"The child, because the day-care center is located near a pond.","C":"The day-care center, because it was not negligent.","D":"The day-care center, because the child was a trespasser."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Special relationships\n\nimposing duty to protect others\n\nParent/child Hospital/patient Employer/employees Shopkeeper/business invitees Common carrier/passengers Custodian/person in custody Innkeeper/guests Mnemonic: Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries\n\nNegligence requires proof of all of the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. The duty element is satisfied if (1) the defendant's conduct created a foreseeable risk of harm to others or (2) the defendant had a special relationship with the plaintiff (eg, custodian of a child). To prove breach, the plaintiff must introduce evidence that the defendant failed to use reasonable care. If such evidence is lacking—or if the defendant introduces sufficient counterevidence—then the defendant will prevail. Here, the 12-year-old child sneaked away from the day-care center and suffered shock after falling into a nearby duck pond. The center had a duty to protect the child since he was under its care (Choice A). And this duty may have required extra vigilance since the center was located near a pond (Choice B). But there is no evidence that the center breached this duty by failing to use reasonable care. Instead, the following facts tend to show that the center was not negligent: The center was staffed with a reasonable number of qualified employees.\n\nThe employees were exercising reasonable care to ensure that the children in their charge did not leave the premises.\n\nNo child had previously come onto the corporation's property from the center.\n\nTherefore, the day-care center will likely prevail. (Choice D) The duck pond is located on the property of the corporation—not the day-care center. As a result, the child's status as a trespasser would only be relevant had he sued the corporation instead of the center.\n\nEducational objective: For a negligence claim, the duty element is satisfied if (1) the defendant's conduct created a foreseeable risk of harm to others or (2) the defendant had a special relationship with the plaintiff (eg, custodian of a child). That duty is breached if the defendant failed to use reasonable care.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 314A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that certain special relationships give rise to a duty to aid or protect).","educationalObjective":"For a negligence claim, the duty element is satisfied if (1) the defendant's conduct created a foreseeable risk of harm to others or (2) the defendant had a special relationship with the plaintiff (eg, custodian of a child). That duty is breached if the defendant failed to use reasonable care.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 314A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that certain special relationships give rise to a duty to aid or protect).","rule":"For a negligence claim, the duty element is satisfied if (1) the defendant's conduct created a foreseeable risk of harm to others or (2) the defendant had a special relationship with the plaintiff (eg, custodian of a child). That duty is breached if the defendant failed to use reasonable care.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit brought on the child's behalf against the day-care center and based only on the facts above, who is likely to prevail?","rule":"For a negligence claim, the duty element is satisfied if (1) the defendant's conduct created a foreseeable risk of harm to others or (2) the defendant had a special relationship with the plaintiff (eg, custodian of a child). That duty is breached if the defendant failed to use reasonable care.","analysis":"Special relationships\n\nimposing duty to protect others\n\nParent/child Hospital/patient Employer/employees Shopkeeper/business invitees Common carrier/passengers Custodian/person in custody Innkeeper/guests Mnemonic: Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries\n\nNegligence requires proof of all of the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. The duty element is satisfied if (1) the defendant's conduct created a foreseeable risk of harm to others or (2) the defendant had a special relationship with the plaintiff (eg, custodian of a child). To prove breach, the plaintiff must introduce evidence that the defendant failed to use reasonable care. If such evidence is lacking—or if the defendant introduces sufficient counterevidence—then the defendant will prevail. Here, the 12-year-old child sneaked away from the day-care center and suffered shock after falling into a nearby duck pond. The center had a duty to protect the child since he was under its care (Choice A). And this duty may have required extra vigilance since the center was located near a pond (Choice B). But there is no evidence that the center breached this duty by failing to use reasonable care. Instead, the following facts tend to show that the center was not negligent: The center was staffed with a reasonable number of qualified employees.\n\nThe employees were exercising reasonable care to ensure that the children in their charge did not leave the premises.\n\nNo child had previously come onto the corporation's property from the center.\n\nTherefore, the day-care center will likely prevail. (Choice D) The duck pond is located on the property of the corporation—not the day-care center. As a result, the child's status as a trespasser would only be relevant had he sued the corporation instead of the center.","conclusion":"Answer C: The day-care center, because it was not negligent."},"questionZh":"一名聪明的12岁儿童在放学后前往一家日托中心。\n\n该中心位于某公司场地内的一个人工鸭池附近。\n\n冬季条件适宜时，该池塘可供滑冰。\n\n在池塘明显仅部分结冰的情况下，这名儿童偷偷溜出中心场地，走到池塘冰面上。\n\n冰层破裂，儿童落入冰冷的水中。\n\n他遭受了休克，若非被路人救起，恐已溺亡。\n\n事发时，池塘边有许多明显的标志，上面写着“冰层薄——禁止进入”。\n\n当该儿童偷偷溜出日托中心时，中心配备了数量合理的合格员工，且员工已尽到合理注意义务，以确保受其照管的儿童不离开场地。\n\n此前从未发生过儿童从日托中心进入该公司场地的情况。\n\n该司法管辖区实行“纯粹比较过失”规则。\n\n若仅基于上述事实，代表该儿童针对日托中心提起的诉讼中，哪一方更有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"儿童胜诉，因为他在受中心照管期间离开了中心。","B":"儿童胜诉，因为日托中心位于池塘附近。","C":"日托中心胜诉，因为它没有过失。","D":"日托中心胜诉，因为该儿童属于侵入者。"},"explanationZh":"特殊关系 产生保护他人义务的情形 父母与子女、医院与患者、雇主与雇员、店主与受邀顾客、公共承运人与乘客、监管人与被监管人、旅馆经营者与住客。记忆口诀：Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries（请协助消除导致伤害的安全隐患）。\n\n认定过失侵权需要证明以下所有要素：注意义务（duty）、违反义务（breach）、因果关系（causation）和损害后果（damages）。\n\n若满足以下任一条件，即视为存在注意义务：(1) 被告的行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；或 (2) 被告与原告之间存在特殊关系（例如，儿童的监管人）。\n\n为证明被告违反了义务，原告必须提供证据表明被告未尽到合理的注意义务。若缺乏此类证据——或者被告提供了充分的反证——则被告将胜诉。\n\n在本案中，一名12岁儿童从日托中心溜走，并因跌入附近的鸭塘而受到惊吓。\n\n由于该儿童处于日托中心的照管之下，中心对其负有保护义务（选项A）。\n\n鉴于中心毗邻池塘，该义务可能要求中心保持额外的警惕（选项B）。\n\n然而，并无证据表明中心因未尽合理注意义务而违反了该义务。\n\n相反，以下事实倾向于证明中心并无过失：中心配备了数量合理的合格员工； 员工尽到了合理的注意义务，以确保其照管的儿童不会离开场所； 此前从未有儿童从该中心进入过涉事公司的场地。\n\n因此，日托中心很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项D）鸭塘位于涉事公司的土地上，而非日托中心的土地上。\n\n因此，该儿童作为“侵入者”的身份，仅在其起诉涉事公司（而非日托中心）时才具有相关性。\n\n教育目标：对于过失侵权索赔，若满足以下任一条件，即视为存在注意义务：(1) 被告的行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；或 (2) 被告与原告之间存在特殊关系（例如，儿童的监管人）。\n\n若被告未尽到合理的注意义务，即视为违反了该义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第314A条（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明某些特殊关系会产生提供援助或保护的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对于过失索赔，若满足以下条件之一，则“注意义务”这一要素即告成立：(1) 被告的行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；或 (2) 被告与原告之间存在特殊关系（例如，作为儿童的监护人）。\n\n若被告未能尽到合理注意义务，即构成违反该义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"若仅基于上述事实，代表该儿童针对日托中心提起的诉讼中，哪一方更有可能胜诉？","rule":"对于过失索赔，若满足以下条件之一，则“注意义务”这一要素即告成立：(1) 被告的行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；或 (2) 被告与原告之间存在特殊关系（例如，作为儿童的监护人）。\n\n若被告未能尽到合理注意义务，即构成违反该义务。","analysis":"特殊关系 产生保护他人义务的情形 父母与子女、医院与患者、雇主与雇员、店主与受邀顾客、公共承运人与乘客、监管人与被监管人、旅馆经营者与住客。记忆口诀：Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries（请协助消除导致伤害的安全隐患）。\n\n认定过失侵权需要证明以下所有要素：注意义务（duty）、违反义务（breach）、因果关系（causation）和损害后果（damages）。\n\n若满足以下任一条件，即视为存在注意义务：(1) 被告的行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；或 (2) 被告与原告之间存在特殊关系（例如，儿童的监管人）。\n\n为证明被告违反了义务，原告必须提供证据表明被告未尽到合理的注意义务。若缺乏此类证据——或者被告提供了充分的反证——则被告将胜诉。\n\n在本案中，一名12岁儿童从日托中心溜走，并因跌入附近的鸭塘而受到惊吓。\n\n由于该儿童处于日托中心的照管之下，中心对其负有保护义务（选项A）。\n\n鉴于中心毗邻池塘，该义务可能要求中心保持额外的警惕（选项B）。\n\n然而，并无证据表明中心因未尽合理注意义务而违反了该义务。\n\n相反，以下事实倾向于证明中心并无过失：中心配备了数量合理的合格员工； 员工尽到了合理的注意义务，以确保其照管的儿童不会离开场所； 此前从未有儿童从该中心进入过涉事公司的场地。\n\n因此，日托中心很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项D）鸭塘位于涉事公司的土地上，而非日托中心的土地上。\n\n因此，该儿童作为“侵入者”的身份，仅在其起诉涉事公司（而非日托中心）时才具有相关性。\n\n教育目标：对于过失侵权索赔，若满足以下任一条件，即视为存在注意义务：(1) 被告的行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；或 (2) 被告与原告之间存在特殊关系（例如，儿童的监管人）。\n\n若被告未尽到合理的注意义务，即视为违反了该义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第314A条（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明某些特殊关系会产生提供援助或保护的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 C：日托中心胜诉，因为它没有过失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":71,"sourceQuestionPage":214,"sourceQuestionPages":[214],"sourceExplanationPages":[215,216],"sourceAnswerPages":[215,216],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-072","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A man decided to cut back a tree on his property. The man borrowed his neighbor's chainsaw and, as an expression of gratitude, offered to also cut down a tree on her property. The man cut back his tree according to the chainsaw instructions, which stated that the chainsaw should not run continuously for more than five minutes at a time. The man then attempted to cut down the neighbor's tree, which took considerably longer due to the thickness of the tree trunk. At one point, the man ran the chainsaw for seven minutes in a row, causing the motor on the chainsaw to overheat and break. The man returned the broken chainsaw to the neighbor. When the neighbor demanded that he pay for a new one, the man refused. If the neighbor files a negligence action against the man, will the neighbor likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man used the chainsaw for the neighbor's benefit.","B":"No, because the man used the chainsaw in a reasonable manner when he cut back his own tree.","C":"Yes, because the man failed to use reasonable care when cutting down his neighbor's tree.","D":"Yes, because the man owed the neighbor a duty of extraordinary care and breached that duty while cutting both trees."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Bailment occurs when one party (the bailor) temporarily entrusts his/her personal property to another (the bailee) without transferring ownership of the property. The bailee's duty of care with respect to that property depends on who benefits from the bailment transaction: Bailor receives the sole benefit – the bailee has a lesser duty to care for the property and is liable for harm to the property only if the bailee has been grossly negligent\n\nBailee receives the sole benefit – the bailee must exercise extraordinary care for the property and is liable for harm to the property even if the bailee has been only slightly negligent\n\nBailor and bailee both benefit – the bailee must take reasonable care of the property and is liable for harm to the property under ordinary negligence principles\n\nHere, the bailment was a single transaction that benefited both the neighbor (bailor) and the man (bailee) because the man borrowed the chainsaw to cut back his own tree and to cut down the neighbor's tree. Consequently, the man had a duty to take reasonable care of the chainsaw (Choice D). He breached that duty by running the chainsaw for seven minutes in a row—when the instructions stated that it should not run continuously for more than five minutes—while cutting the neighbor's tree. Therefore, the neighbor will likely prevail (Choice A). (Choice B) The man may have used the chainsaw in a reasonable manner when he cut back his own tree. But he failed to use reasonable care when he cut down his neighbor's tree by running the chainsaw for more than five continuous minutes.\n\nEducational objective: A bailee's duty of care with respect to bailed property depends on who benefits from the bailment transaction: (1) if only the bailor benefits, the bailee has a lesser duty of care; (2) if only the bailee benefits, the bailee must exercise extraordinary care; and (3) if both parties benefit, the bailee must exercise reasonable care.\n\nReferences\n8A Am. Jur. 2d Bailments §§ 7–9 (2020) (classification of bailments).","educationalObjective":"A bailee's duty of care with respect to bailed property depends on who benefits from the bailment transaction: (1) if only the bailor benefits, the bailee has a lesser duty of care; (2) if only the bailee benefits, the bailee must exercise extraordinary care; and (3) if both parties benefit, the bailee must exercise reasonable care.","references":"8A Am. Jur. 2d Bailments §§ 7–9 (2020) (classification of bailments).","rule":"A bailee's duty of care with respect to bailed property depends on who benefits from the bailment transaction: (1) if only the bailor benefits, the bailee has a lesser duty of care; (2) if only the bailee benefits, the bailee must exercise extraordinary care; and (3) if both parties benefit, the bailee must exercise reasonable care.","irac":{"issue":"If the neighbor files a negligence action against the man, will the neighbor likely prevail?","rule":"A bailee's duty of care with respect to bailed property depends on who benefits from the bailment transaction: (1) if only the bailor benefits, the bailee has a lesser duty of care; (2) if only the bailee benefits, the bailee must exercise extraordinary care; and (3) if both parties benefit, the bailee must exercise reasonable care.","analysis":"Bailment occurs when one party (the bailor) temporarily entrusts his/her personal property to another (the bailee) without transferring ownership of the property. The bailee's duty of care with respect to that property depends on who benefits from the bailment transaction: Bailor receives the sole benefit – the bailee has a lesser duty to care for the property and is liable for harm to the property only if the bailee has been grossly negligent\n\nBailee receives the sole benefit – the bailee must exercise extraordinary care for the property and is liable for harm to the property even if the bailee has been only slightly negligent\n\nBailor and bailee both benefit – the bailee must take reasonable care of the property and is liable for harm to the property under ordinary negligence principles\n\nHere, the bailment was a single transaction that benefited both the neighbor (bailor) and the man (bailee) because the man borrowed the chainsaw to cut back his own tree and to cut down the neighbor's tree. Consequently, the man had a duty to take reasonable care of the chainsaw (Choice D). He breached that duty by running the chainsaw for seven minutes in a row—when the instructions stated that it should not run continuously for more than five minutes—while cutting the neighbor's tree. Therefore, the neighbor will likely prevail (Choice A). (Choice B) The man may have used the chainsaw in a reasonable manner when he cut back his own tree. But he failed to use reasonable care when he cut down his neighbor's tree by running the chainsaw for more than five continuous minutes.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the man failed to use reasonable care when cutting down his neighbor's tree."},"questionZh":"一名男子决定修剪自家院子里的一棵树。\n\n他向邻居借了电锯，并出于感激，主动提出也帮邻居砍掉其院子里的一棵树。\n\n在修剪自家树木时，该男子遵循了电锯的使用说明，即电锯不应连续运转超过五分钟。\n\n随后，他着手砍伐邻居家的树木；由于树干较粗，这项工作耗时更长。\n\n在此过程中，他曾让电锯连续运转了七分钟，导致电机过热并损坏。\n\n该男子将损坏的电锯归还给了邻居。\n\n当邻居要求他赔偿一台新电锯时，他予以拒绝。\n\n如果邻居针对该男子提起过失诉讼，邻居胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该男子使用电锯是为了邻居的利益。","B":"不会，因为该男子在修剪自家树木时是以合理的方式使用电锯的。","C":"会，因为该男子在砍伐邻居树木时未尽到合理的注意义务。","D":"会，因为该男子对邻居负有极高注意义务，并在砍伐两棵树木的过程中违反了该义务。"},"explanationZh":"寄托关系（Bailment）是指一方（寄托人/bailor）在不转移所有权的情况下，将其个人财产暂时委托给另一方（受托人/bailee）的情形。\n\n受托人对该财产的注意义务取决于谁从该寄托交易中获益：若仅寄托人获益，受托人的注意义务较低，仅在存在重大过失导致财产受损时才承担责任； 若仅受托人获益，受托人必须尽到极高程度的注意义务，即使仅有轻微过失导致财产受损，也需承担责任； 若双方均获益，受托人必须尽到合理的注意义务，并根据一般过失原则对财产受损承担责任。 本案中，该寄托关系是一项使邻居（寄托人）和该男子（受托人）双方均获益的单一交易，因为该男子借用链锯既是为了修剪自家的树木，也是为了砍伐邻居的树木。\n\n因此，该男子有义务对链锯尽到合理的注意义务（选项 D）。\n\n他在砍伐邻居树木时，连续运转链锯长达七分钟——而说明书规定其连续运转时间不得超过五分钟——从而违反了该义务。\n\n因此，邻居很可能会胜诉（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）该男子在修剪自家树木时，其使用链锯的方式可能是合理的；但在砍伐邻居树木时，他因连续运转链锯超过五分钟而未能尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n教育目标：受托人对寄托财产的注意义务取决于谁从寄托交易中获益：(1) 若仅寄托人获益，受托人的注意义务较低；(2) 若仅受托人获益，受托人必须尽到极高程度的注意义务；(3) 若双方均获益，受托人必须尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n参考文献 8A Am. Jur. 2d Bailments §§ 7–9 (2020)（寄托关系的分类）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"保管人对受托保管财产的注意义务取决于谁从保管交易中获益：(1) 若仅寄托人获益，保管人的注意义务较低；(2) 若仅保管人获益，保管人必须尽到极高的注意义务；(3) 若双方均获益，保管人必须尽到合理的注意义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果邻居针对该男子提起过失诉讼，邻居胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"保管人对受托保管财产的注意义务取决于谁从保管交易中获益：(1) 若仅寄托人获益，保管人的注意义务较低；(2) 若仅保管人获益，保管人必须尽到极高的注意义务；(3) 若双方均获益，保管人必须尽到合理的注意义务。","analysis":"寄托关系（Bailment）是指一方（寄托人/bailor）在不转移所有权的情况下，将其个人财产暂时委托给另一方（受托人/bailee）的情形。\n\n受托人对该财产的注意义务取决于谁从该寄托交易中获益：若仅寄托人获益，受托人的注意义务较低，仅在存在重大过失导致财产受损时才承担责任； 若仅受托人获益，受托人必须尽到极高程度的注意义务，即使仅有轻微过失导致财产受损，也需承担责任； 若双方均获益，受托人必须尽到合理的注意义务，并根据一般过失原则对财产受损承担责任。 本案中，该寄托关系是一项使邻居（寄托人）和该男子（受托人）双方均获益的单一交易，因为该男子借用链锯既是为了修剪自家的树木，也是为了砍伐邻居的树木。\n\n因此，该男子有义务对链锯尽到合理的注意义务（选项 D）。\n\n他在砍伐邻居树木时，连续运转链锯长达七分钟——而说明书规定其连续运转时间不得超过五分钟——从而违反了该义务。\n\n因此，邻居很可能会胜诉（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）该男子在修剪自家树木时，其使用链锯的方式可能是合理的；但在砍伐邻居树木时，他因连续运转链锯超过五分钟而未能尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n教育目标：受托人对寄托财产的注意义务取决于谁从寄托交易中获益：(1) 若仅寄托人获益，受托人的注意义务较低；(2) 若仅受托人获益，受托人必须尽到极高程度的注意义务；(3) 若双方均获益，受托人必须尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n参考文献 8A Am. Jur. 2d Bailments §§ 7–9 (2020)（寄托关系的分类）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，因为该男子在砍伐邻居树木时未尽到合理的注意义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-3899e26c67fb4450"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":72,"sourceQuestionPage":217,"sourceQuestionPages":[217],"sourceExplanationPages":[218,219],"sourceAnswerPages":[218,219],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-073","subject":"Torts","topic":"Actual & Proximate Cause","question":"A homeowner and a group of his friends are fanatical basketball fans who regularly meet at each other's homes to watch basketball games on television. Some of the group are fans of team A, and others are fans of team B. When the group has watched televised games between these two teams, fights sometimes have broken out among the group. Despite this fact, the homeowner invited the group to his home to watch a championship game between teams A and B. During the game, the homeowner's guests became rowdy and antagonistic. Fearing that they would begin to fight and that a fight would damage his possessions, the homeowner asked his guests to leave. They refused to go and soon began to fight. The homeowner called the police, and an officer was sent to the homeowner's home. The officer sustained a broken nose in his efforts to stop the fighting. The officer brought an action against the homeowner alleging that he was negligent in inviting the group to his house to watch this championship game. The homeowner has moved to dismiss the complaint. What is the best argument in support of this motion?","choices":{"A":"A police officer is not entitled to a recovery based upon the negligent conduct that created the need for the officer's professional intervention.","B":"A rescuer injured while attempting to avert a danger cannot recover damages from the endangered person.","C":"As a matter of law, the homeowner's conduct was not the proximate cause of the police officer's injury.","D":"The homeowner did not owe the police officer a duty to use reasonable care, because the officer was a mere licensee on the homeowner's property."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Rescue doctrine\n\nGeneral rule Rescuers can recover for injuries sustained while attempting to rescue another if that person's peril was caused by defendant's negligence\n\nException Professional rescuers cannot recover for injuries attributable to special dangers of their job—eg: (firefighter's rule) firefighter entering burning building police officer arresting suspect paramedic/lifeguard attempting risky rescue Under the rescue doctrine, persons who negligently endanger themselves or others are liable for injuries sustained by rescuers. But the firefighter's rule is a limitation on the rescue doctrine that applies to all professional rescuers (eg, police officers). This rule bars professional rescuers from recovering for harm that resulted from the special dangers of their jobs—eg, the negligent conduct that created the need for their professional intervention. Here, the police officer was injured in his efforts to stop the fighting among the homeowner's guests. The officer then sued the homeowner, alleging that he was negligent in inviting the group (who were prone to fighting) to his house to watch the game. But since the officer cannot recover based upon the negligent conduct that created the need for his professional intervention, this is the best argument for dismissing his complaint. (Choice B) An injured rescuer can recover damages from an endangered person if that person's own negligence created the need for his/her rescue. But the firefighter's rule still bars the police officer's recovery in this instance. (Choice C) The homeowner's conduct was a proximate cause of the police officer's injury since harm to rescuers is always a foreseeable consequence of negligence. (Choice D) Police officers are mere licensees when they enter a person's land. But land possessors (eg, the homeowner) owe licensees a duty to use reasonable care in conducting activities (eg, parties) on the land.\n\nEducational objective: The firefighter's rule bars firefighters and other professional rescuers from recovering for harm that resulted from the special dangers of their jobs—eg, the negligent conduct that created the need for their professional intervention.\n\nReferences\n62 Am. Jur. 2d Premises Liability § 421 (2019) (explaining that the firefighter's rule prevents professional rescuers from recovering for injuries arising out of the special risks of their professions).","educationalObjective":"The firefighter's rule bars firefighters and other professional rescuers from recovering for harm that resulted from the special dangers of their jobs—eg, the negligent conduct that created the need for their professional intervention.","references":"62 Am. Jur. 2d Premises Liability § 421 (2019) (explaining that the firefighter's rule prevents professional rescuers from recovering for injuries arising out of the special risks of their professions).","rule":"The firefighter's rule bars firefighters and other professional rescuers from recovering for harm that resulted from the special dangers of their jobs—eg, the negligent conduct that created the need for their professional intervention.","irac":{"issue":"What is the best argument in support of this motion?","rule":"The firefighter's rule bars firefighters and other professional rescuers from recovering for harm that resulted from the special dangers of their jobs—eg, the negligent conduct that created the need for their professional intervention.","analysis":"Rescue doctrine\n\nGeneral rule Rescuers can recover for injuries sustained while attempting to rescue another if that person's peril was caused by defendant's negligence\n\nException Professional rescuers cannot recover for injuries attributable to special dangers of their job—eg: (firefighter's rule) firefighter entering burning building police officer arresting suspect paramedic/lifeguard attempting risky rescue Under the rescue doctrine, persons who negligently endanger themselves or others are liable for injuries sustained by rescuers. But the firefighter's rule is a limitation on the rescue doctrine that applies to all professional rescuers (eg, police officers). This rule bars professional rescuers from recovering for harm that resulted from the special dangers of their jobs—eg, the negligent conduct that created the need for their professional intervention. Here, the police officer was injured in his efforts to stop the fighting among the homeowner's guests. The officer then sued the homeowner, alleging that he was negligent in inviting the group (who were prone to fighting) to his house to watch the game. But since the officer cannot recover based upon the negligent conduct that created the need for his professional intervention, this is the best argument for dismissing his complaint. (Choice B) An injured rescuer can recover damages from an endangered person if that person's own negligence created the need for his/her rescue. But the firefighter's rule still bars the police officer's recovery in this instance. (Choice C) The homeowner's conduct was a proximate cause of the police officer's injury since harm to rescuers is always a foreseeable consequence of negligence. (Choice D) Police officers are mere licensees when they enter a person's land. But land possessors (eg, the homeowner) owe licensees a duty to use reasonable care in conducting activities (eg, parties) on the land.","conclusion":"Answer A: A police officer is not entitled to a recovery based upon the negligent conduct that created the need for the officer's professional intervention."},"questionZh":"一位房主和一群朋友都是狂热的篮球迷，经常聚在彼此家中通过电视观看篮球比赛。\n\n这群人中，有的支持A队，有的支持B队。当他们聚在一起观看这两支球队的电视转播比赛时，有时会发生争执甚至打斗。\n\n尽管如此，房主还是邀请这群朋友到家中观看A队与B队之间的冠军赛。比赛期间，房主的客人们变得喧闹且充满敌意。\n\n房主担心他们会打起来并损坏自己的财物，于是要求客人们离开。\n\n客人们拒绝离开，并很快发生了打斗。\n\n房主报了警，一名警官随后赶到房主家中。\n\n该警官在试图制止打斗时鼻梁骨折。\n\n警官对房主提起诉讼，指控房主邀请这群人到家中观看冠军赛的行为存在疏忽。\n\n房主已提出动议，请求驳回该诉讼。\n\n支持这一动议的最佳论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"如果某种疏忽行为导致了警官进行专业干预的必要性，则警官无权就该行为造成的后果获得赔偿。","B":"救援人员在试图化解危险时受伤，不能向处于危险中的人索赔。","C":"从法律角度看，房主的行为并非导致警官受伤的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）。","D":"房主对警官不负有尽合理注意义务的责任，因为该警官仅属于房主物业上的“被许可人”（licensee）。"},"explanationZh":"“救援原则”（Rescue doctrine） 一般规则：如果某人的险境是由被告的过失造成的，那么试图营救该人而受伤的救援者可以获得赔偿。 例外情况：职业救援者若因其工作特有的危险而受伤，则无法获得赔偿——例如（“消防员规则”/firefighter's rule）：消防员进入燃烧的建筑物、警察逮捕嫌疑人、医护人员或救生员进行高风险救援。\n\n根据救援原则，因过失使自己或他人陷入险境的人，需对救援者受到的伤害承担责任。但“消防员规则”是对救援原则的一种限制，适用于所有职业救援者（如警察）。\n\n该规则禁止职业救援者就其工作特有危险导致的伤害获得赔偿——例如，导致需要其进行专业干预的过失行为。\n\n在本案中，警察在制止房主客人的打斗时受伤。\n\n随后，该警察起诉房主，指控房主在邀请这群（容易引发打斗的）人来家中看比赛时存在过失。\n\n然而，由于警察不能基于导致其需要进行专业干预的过失行为获得赔偿，这是驳回其诉讼请求的最有力论据。\n\n（选项 B）如果处于险境的人因自身过失导致需要救援，受伤的救援者可以向该人索赔。\n\n但在本案中，“消防员规则”仍然阻碍了警察获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）房主的行为是警察受伤的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause），因为对救援者的伤害总是过失行为可预见的后果。\n\n（选项 D）警察进入他人土地时仅被视为“被许可人”（licensee）。\n\n但土地占有者（如房主）对被许可人负有义务，即在土地上开展活动（如聚会）时须尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n教育目标：“消防员规则”禁止消防员及其他职业救援者就其工作特有危险导致的伤害获得赔偿——例如，导致需要其进行专业干预的过失行为。\n\n参考文献 62 Am. Jur. 2d Premises Liability § 421 (2019)（解释了“消防员规则”如何阻止职业救援者就其职业特有风险引起的伤害获得赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“消防员规则”（firefighter's rule）禁止消防员及其他专业救援人员就因其职业特殊危险（例如导致其进行专业干预的疏忽行为）而遭受的伤害获得赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"支持这一动议的最佳论点是什么？","rule":"“消防员规则”（firefighter's rule）禁止消防员及其他专业救援人员就因其职业特殊危险（例如导致其进行专业干预的疏忽行为）而遭受的伤害获得赔偿。","analysis":"“救援原则”（Rescue doctrine） 一般规则：如果某人的险境是由被告的过失造成的，那么试图营救该人而受伤的救援者可以获得赔偿。 例外情况：职业救援者若因其工作特有的危险而受伤，则无法获得赔偿——例如（“消防员规则”/firefighter's rule）：消防员进入燃烧的建筑物、警察逮捕嫌疑人、医护人员或救生员进行高风险救援。\n\n根据救援原则，因过失使自己或他人陷入险境的人，需对救援者受到的伤害承担责任。但“消防员规则”是对救援原则的一种限制，适用于所有职业救援者（如警察）。\n\n该规则禁止职业救援者就其工作特有危险导致的伤害获得赔偿——例如，导致需要其进行专业干预的过失行为。\n\n在本案中，警察在制止房主客人的打斗时受伤。\n\n随后，该警察起诉房主，指控房主在邀请这群（容易引发打斗的）人来家中看比赛时存在过失。\n\n然而，由于警察不能基于导致其需要进行专业干预的过失行为获得赔偿，这是驳回其诉讼请求的最有力论据。\n\n（选项 B）如果处于险境的人因自身过失导致需要救援，受伤的救援者可以向该人索赔。\n\n但在本案中，“消防员规则”仍然阻碍了警察获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）房主的行为是警察受伤的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause），因为对救援者的伤害总是过失行为可预见的后果。\n\n（选项 D）警察进入他人土地时仅被视为“被许可人”（licensee）。\n\n但土地占有者（如房主）对被许可人负有义务，即在土地上开展活动（如聚会）时须尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n教育目标：“消防员规则”禁止消防员及其他职业救援者就其工作特有危险导致的伤害获得赔偿——例如，导致需要其进行专业干预的过失行为。\n\n参考文献 62 Am. Jur. 2d Premises Liability § 421 (2019)（解释了“消防员规则”如何阻止职业救援者就其职业特有风险引起的伤害获得赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 A：如果某种疏忽行为导致了警官进行专业干预的必要性，则警官无权就该行为造成的后果获得赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":73,"sourceQuestionPage":220,"sourceQuestionPages":[220],"sourceExplanationPages":[221,222],"sourceAnswerPages":[221,222],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-074","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"An adult gave a pack of cigarettes to a minor whom the adult incorrectly believed to be an adult. The minor took the cigarettes to a nearby property where he climbed on top of a stack of fence posts, lit a cigarette, and began smoking it. Shortly thereafter, the minor unintentionally dropped the lit cigarette into the stack of fence posts. The minor tried to retrieve the cigarette so that he could extinguish it, but he was unable to do so. Then the minor wandered away. The lit cigarette subsequently set fire to the fence posts, resulting in the destruction of the posts as well as an adjacent garage and its contents. The jurisdiction has a strict liability statute that makes it a misdemeanor to provide a minor with cigarettes. Violation of the statute is punishable by a $200 fine. The owner of the destroyed property initiated a negligence lawsuit against the adult. Which of the following is the adult's WEAKEST defense to this action?","choices":{"A":"The adult did not know that the minor was underage when he gave the cigarettes to him.","B":"The destruction of the property is not a type of harm against which the statute was intended to protect.","C":"The destruction of the property was not proximately caused by the adult's violation of the statute.","D":"The property owner is not in the class of people intended to be protected by the statute."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Negligence requires proof of the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. Duty and breach are presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se when the following facts are proved: The defendant violated a statute or ordinance. The plaintiff suffered a type of harm that the statute or ordinance was intended to protect against. The plaintiff was in the class of persons intended to be protected by the statute or ordinance. Here, the jurisdiction's statute makes it a strict liability offense to provide a minor with cigarettes. This means that liability can be imposed without proof of fault. Therefore, the fact that the adult did not know that the minor was underage when he gave the cigarettes to him is no defense to the adult's violation of the statute—or to this negligence per se action. However, the adult may be able to successfully defend this action on one or more of the following grounds: The harm that the statute was intended to protect against is harm to minors from smoking—not property destruction caused by cigarettes (Choice B).\n\nThe class of persons that the statute was intended to protect is minors—not property owners who suffer property destruction caused by cigarettes (Choice D).\n\nWhile the adult's violation of the statute was the actual (\"but-for\") cause of the property destruction, this violation was not a proximate (legal) cause of the property destruction since this type of harm was not a risk that made the adult's conduct tortious (Choice C).\n\nEducational objective: Duty and breach are presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se when (1) the defendant violated a statute or ordinance, (2) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that the statute or ordinance was intended to protect against, and (3) the plaintiff was in the class of persons intended to be protected by the statute or ordinance.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 14 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence per se).","educationalObjective":"Duty and breach are presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se when (1) the defendant violated a statute or ordinance, (2) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that the statute or ordinance was intended to protect against, and (3) the plaintiff was in the class of persons intended to be protected by the statute or ordinance.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 14 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence per se).","rule":"Duty and breach are presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se when (1) the defendant violated a statute or ordinance, (2) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that the statute or ordinance was intended to protect against, and (3) the plaintiff was in the class of persons intended to be protected by the statute or ordinance.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the adult's WEAKEST defense to this action?","rule":"Duty and breach are presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se when (1) the defendant violated a statute or ordinance, (2) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that the statute or ordinance was intended to protect against, and (3) the plaintiff was in the class of persons intended to be protected by the statute or ordinance.","analysis":"Negligence requires proof of the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. Duty and breach are presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se when the following facts are proved: The defendant violated a statute or ordinance. The plaintiff suffered a type of harm that the statute or ordinance was intended to protect against. The plaintiff was in the class of persons intended to be protected by the statute or ordinance. Here, the jurisdiction's statute makes it a strict liability offense to provide a minor with cigarettes. This means that liability can be imposed without proof of fault. Therefore, the fact that the adult did not know that the minor was underage when he gave the cigarettes to him is no defense to the adult's violation of the statute—or to this negligence per se action. However, the adult may be able to successfully defend this action on one or more of the following grounds: The harm that the statute was intended to protect against is harm to minors from smoking—not property destruction caused by cigarettes (Choice B).\n\nThe class of persons that the statute was intended to protect is minors—not property owners who suffer property destruction caused by cigarettes (Choice D).\n\nWhile the adult's violation of the statute was the actual (\"but-for\") cause of the property destruction, this violation was not a proximate (legal) cause of the property destruction since this type of harm was not a risk that made the adult's conduct tortious (Choice C).","conclusion":"Answer A: The adult did not know that the minor was underage when he gave the cigarettes to him."},"questionZh":"一名成年人将一包香烟递给了一名未成年人，但他误以为对方是成年人。\n\n这名未成年人带着香烟来到附近一处场地，爬上一堆围栏立柱，点燃并开始吸烟。\n\n不久后，他不慎将点燃的香烟掉进了立柱堆里。\n\n他试图取回香烟以便将其熄灭，但未能成功，随后便离开了现场。\n\n这支点燃的香烟随后引燃了围栏立柱，导致立柱以及邻近的车库及其内部物品被烧毁。\n\n该司法管辖区有一项严格责任法规，规定向未成年人提供香烟构成轻罪，违者处以200美元罚款。\n\n受损财产的所有者针对该成年人提起了过失诉讼。\n\n以下哪项是该成年人针对此诉讼提出的**最薄弱**的抗辩理由？","choicesZh":{"A":"该成年人在递给未成年人香烟时，并不知道对方未成年。","B":"财产损毁并非该法规旨在防范的损害类型。","C":"财产损毁并非由该成年人违反法规的行为直接导致（即不构成法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause））。","D":"财产所有者不属于该法规旨在保护的人群类别。"},"explanationZh":"认定过失责任需要证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系以及损害后果。\n\n根据“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则，若证实以下事实，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的情形：被告违反了某项法规或条例；原告遭受了该法规或条例旨在防范的损害；原告属于该法规或条例旨在保护的人群。\n\n在本案中，该司法管辖区的法规规定，向未成年人提供香烟构成严格责任违法行为。\n\n这意味着无需证明过错即可追究责任。\n\n因此，该成年人在提供香烟时不知晓对方未成年，这一事实不能作为其违反法规的抗辩理由，也不能作为针对该“本身过失”诉讼的抗辩理由。\n\n然而，该成年人或许能基于以下一项或多项理由成功抗辩：该法规旨在防范的损害是未成年人因吸烟而受到的伤害，而非香烟引发的财产损毁（选项 B）。\n\n该法规旨在保护的人群是未成年人，而非因香烟引发财产损毁的财产所有者（选项 D）。\n\n尽管该成年人违反法规的行为是导致财产损毁的事实因果（即“若无该行为便不会发生损害”意义上的原因），但该违规行为并非财产损毁的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果），因为此类损害并非导致该成年人行为构成侵权的风险所在（选项 C）。\n\n教育目标：根据“本身过失”原则，若满足以下条件，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务：(1) 被告违反了法规或条例；(2) 原告遭受了该法规或条例旨在防范的损害；(3) 原告属于该法规或条例旨在保护的人群。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与情感伤害责任》第 14 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于“本身过失”原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则，若满足以下条件，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的行为：(1) 被告违反了法规或条例；(2) 原告遭受了该法规或条例旨在防范的损害；(3) 原告属于该法规或条例旨在保护的人群类别。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项是该成年人针对此诉讼提出的**最薄弱**的抗辩理由？","rule":"根据“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则，若满足以下条件，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的行为：(1) 被告违反了法规或条例；(2) 原告遭受了该法规或条例旨在防范的损害；(3) 原告属于该法规或条例旨在保护的人群类别。","analysis":"认定过失责任需要证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系以及损害后果。\n\n根据“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则，若证实以下事实，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的情形：被告违反了某项法规或条例；原告遭受了该法规或条例旨在防范的损害；原告属于该法规或条例旨在保护的人群。\n\n在本案中，该司法管辖区的法规规定，向未成年人提供香烟构成严格责任违法行为。\n\n这意味着无需证明过错即可追究责任。\n\n因此，该成年人在提供香烟时不知晓对方未成年，这一事实不能作为其违反法规的抗辩理由，也不能作为针对该“本身过失”诉讼的抗辩理由。\n\n然而，该成年人或许能基于以下一项或多项理由成功抗辩：该法规旨在防范的损害是未成年人因吸烟而受到的伤害，而非香烟引发的财产损毁（选项 B）。\n\n该法规旨在保护的人群是未成年人，而非因香烟引发财产损毁的财产所有者（选项 D）。\n\n尽管该成年人违反法规的行为是导致财产损毁的事实因果（即“若无该行为便不会发生损害”意义上的原因），但该违规行为并非财产损毁的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果），因为此类损害并非导致该成年人行为构成侵权的风险所在（选项 C）。\n\n教育目标：根据“本身过失”原则，若满足以下条件，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务：(1) 被告违反了法规或条例；(2) 原告遭受了该法规或条例旨在防范的损害；(3) 原告属于该法规或条例旨在保护的人群。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与情感伤害责任》第 14 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于“本身过失”原则）。","conclusion":"答案 A：该成年人在递给未成年人香烟时，并不知道对方未成年。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"proximately","ipa":"/'prɒksɪmeɪtlɪ/","syllables":"prox·i·mately","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"adv. 近似地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“proximately”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The destruction of the property was not proximately caused by the adult's violation of the statute.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-aee661d9028053d8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":74,"sourceQuestionPage":223,"sourceQuestionPages":[223],"sourceExplanationPages":[224,225],"sourceAnswerPages":[224,225],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-075","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A schizophrenic patient who was institutionalized in a psychiatric facility pushed a nurse down a stairwell at the facility. The nurse, a paid employee of the facility who was trained to care for schizophrenic patients, was injured. The patient is an indigent whose care is paid for by the government. The jurisdiction generally follows the rule that a person with a mental deficiency is held to the standard of a reasonable person. In a negligence action brought by the nurse against the patient, the patient's lawyer will argue that the patient should not be held responsible for the nurse's injury. Which of the following facts will be LEAST helpful to the patient's lawyer's argument?","choices":{"A":"At the time she pushed the nurse, the patient thought she was being attacked by an elephant.","B":"The nurse was a professional caregiver.","C":"The nurse was trained to care for patients with schizophrenia.","D":"The patient is an indigent whose care is paid for by the government."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Assumption of the risk\n\n(voluntary exposure to known risk of harm)\n\nTraditional contributory Bars plaintiff's recovery negligence\n\nPure comparative Majority view: Reduces recovery by plaintiff's negligence* proportionate share of fault Minority view: Bars plaintiff's recovery *Default rule on MBE.\n\nIn this jurisdiction, persons with mental deficiencies (eg, schizophrenia) are held to the standard of care of a reasonable person, which means that mental deficiency is not a defense to negligence. But in pure comparative negligence jurisdictions (default rule on the MBE), assumption of the risk is a defense that can either reduce (majority view) or bar (minority view) the plaintiff's recovery. This defense applies if the plaintiff: knew about the risk of harm (eg, based on prior training or experience) and voluntarily accepted that risk (eg, by agreeing to confront the risk for compensation). Here, the schizophrenic patient pushed the nurse down a stairwell, and the nurse sued the patient for negligence. But the nurse should have known about this risk since he was a professional caregiver trained to care for patients with schizophrenia (Choices B & C). And by agreeing to work at a psychiatric facility, the nurse voluntarily accepted the risks posed by hallucinating patients (Choice A). Therefore, these arguments are helpful to establishing assumption of the risk, which would reduce or negate the patient's responsibility for the nurse's injury. However, inability to pay is never a defense to tort liability. As a result, the patient's least helpful argument is that she is an indigent whose care is paid for by the government.\n\nEducational objective: Assumption of the risk is a defense to negligence that either reduces or bars recovery if the plaintiff knew about and voluntarily accepted the risk that caused that plaintiff's harm. But inability to pay is never a defense to tort liability.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 496C (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a plaintiff cannot recover for negligence when he/she fully understood and voluntarily confronted a risk).","educationalObjective":"Assumption of the risk is a defense to negligence that either reduces or bars recovery if the plaintiff knew about and voluntarily accepted the risk that caused that plaintiff's harm. But inability to pay is never a defense to tort liability.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 496C (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a plaintiff cannot recover for negligence when he/she fully understood and voluntarily confronted a risk).","rule":"Assumption of the risk is a defense to negligence that either reduces or bars recovery if the plaintiff knew about and voluntarily accepted the risk that caused that plaintiff's harm. But inability to pay is never a defense to tort liability.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following facts will be LEAST helpful to the patient's lawyer's argument?","rule":"Assumption of the risk is a defense to negligence that either reduces or bars recovery if the plaintiff knew about and voluntarily accepted the risk that caused that plaintiff's harm. But inability to pay is never a defense to tort liability.","analysis":"Assumption of the risk\n\n(voluntary exposure to known risk of harm)\n\nTraditional contributory Bars plaintiff's recovery negligence\n\nPure comparative Majority view: Reduces recovery by plaintiff's negligence* proportionate share of fault Minority view: Bars plaintiff's recovery *Default rule on MBE.\n\nIn this jurisdiction, persons with mental deficiencies (eg, schizophrenia) are held to the standard of care of a reasonable person, which means that mental deficiency is not a defense to negligence. But in pure comparative negligence jurisdictions (default rule on the MBE), assumption of the risk is a defense that can either reduce (majority view) or bar (minority view) the plaintiff's recovery. This defense applies if the plaintiff: knew about the risk of harm (eg, based on prior training or experience) and voluntarily accepted that risk (eg, by agreeing to confront the risk for compensation). Here, the schizophrenic patient pushed the nurse down a stairwell, and the nurse sued the patient for negligence. But the nurse should have known about this risk since he was a professional caregiver trained to care for patients with schizophrenia (Choices B & C). And by agreeing to work at a psychiatric facility, the nurse voluntarily accepted the risks posed by hallucinating patients (Choice A). Therefore, these arguments are helpful to establishing assumption of the risk, which would reduce or negate the patient's responsibility for the nurse's injury. However, inability to pay is never a defense to tort liability. As a result, the patient's least helpful argument is that she is an indigent whose care is paid for by the government.","conclusion":"Answer D: The patient is an indigent whose care is paid for by the government."},"questionZh":"一名在精神病院接受收治的精神分裂症患者将一名护士推下了楼梯。\n\n该护士是该机构的受薪员工，且受过护理精神分裂症患者的专业训练，她在事件中受伤。\n\n该患者属于贫困人士，其护理费用由政府承担。\n\n该司法管辖区通常遵循以下规则：精神障碍者在法律上需达到“理性人”的行为标准。\n\n在护士针对该患者提起的过失诉讼中，患者的律师将主张患者不应为护士的受伤承担责任。\n\n下列哪项事实对患者律师的辩护论点最无助益？","choicesZh":{"A":"在推倒护士时，患者误以为自己正遭到一头大象的使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）。","B":"该护士是一名专业护理人员。","C":"该护士受过护理精神分裂症患者的专业训练。","D":"该患者属于贫困人士，其护理费用由政府承担。"},"explanationZh":"风险自担原则 （自愿暴露于已知的伤害风险之中） 传统的“与有过失（contributory negligence）”原则：禁止原告获得赔偿。 纯粹的“比较过失”原则：多数观点认为，根据原告过失所占的过错比例相应减少赔偿额；少数观点认为，禁止原告获得赔偿（注：这是MBE考试中的默认规则）。\n\n在该司法管辖区，患有精神障碍（如精神分裂症）的人员仍需遵守“理性人”的注意标准，这意味着精神障碍不能作为过失侵权责任的抗辩理由。\n\n但在实行纯粹比较过失原则的司法管辖区（MBE考试的默认规则），“风险自担”是一项抗辩理由，既可以减少（多数观点）也可以完全排除（少数观点）原告的获赔权利。\n\n如果原告已知晓伤害风险（例如基于既往培训或经验）并自愿接受该风险（例如同意为获取报酬而面对该风险），则该抗辩理由适用。\n\n在本案中，患有精神分裂症的患者将护士推下楼梯，护士起诉患者存在过失。\n\n然而，作为受过专门培训、负责照料精神分裂症患者的专业护理人员，该护士理应知晓此类风险（选项B和C）。\n\n此外，通过同意在精神病医疗机构工作，该护士已自愿接受了由产生幻觉的患者所带来的风险（选项A）。\n\n因此，上述论点有助于确立“风险自担”这一抗辩理由，从而减轻或免除患者对护士所受伤害应承担的责任。\n\n然而，无力支付赔偿金绝不能作为侵权责任的抗辩理由。\n\n因此，对患者而言，最无助于其抗辩的理由是：她是一名贫困人士，其护理费用由政府支付。\n\n教育目标：“风险自担”是针对过失侵权的一种抗辩理由；如果原告已知晓并自愿接受导致其受害的风险，该原则将减少或完全排除其获赔权利。\n\n但无力支付赔偿金绝不能作为侵权责任的抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第496C条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明若原告完全理解并自愿面对某种风险，则无法就该风险引发的过失获得赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“自担风险”（assumption of the risk）是针对过失侵权的一种抗辩理由；如果原告明知并自愿承担导致其受损的风险，该抗辩可减轻或排除被告的赔偿责任。\n\n然而，无力支付赔偿金绝非侵权责任的抗辩理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"下列哪项事实对患者律师的辩护论点最无助益？","rule":"“自担风险”（assumption of the risk）是针对过失侵权的一种抗辩理由；如果原告明知并自愿承担导致其受损的风险，该抗辩可减轻或排除被告的赔偿责任。\n\n然而，无力支付赔偿金绝非侵权责任的抗辩理由。","analysis":"风险自担原则 （自愿暴露于已知的伤害风险之中） 传统的“与有过失（contributory negligence）”原则：禁止原告获得赔偿。 纯粹的“比较过失”原则：多数观点认为，根据原告过失所占的过错比例相应减少赔偿额；少数观点认为，禁止原告获得赔偿（注：这是MBE考试中的默认规则）。\n\n在该司法管辖区，患有精神障碍（如精神分裂症）的人员仍需遵守“理性人”的注意标准，这意味着精神障碍不能作为过失侵权责任的抗辩理由。\n\n但在实行纯粹比较过失原则的司法管辖区（MBE考试的默认规则），“风险自担”是一项抗辩理由，既可以减少（多数观点）也可以完全排除（少数观点）原告的获赔权利。\n\n如果原告已知晓伤害风险（例如基于既往培训或经验）并自愿接受该风险（例如同意为获取报酬而面对该风险），则该抗辩理由适用。\n\n在本案中，患有精神分裂症的患者将护士推下楼梯，护士起诉患者存在过失。\n\n然而，作为受过专门培训、负责照料精神分裂症患者的专业护理人员，该护士理应知晓此类风险（选项B和C）。\n\n此外，通过同意在精神病医疗机构工作，该护士已自愿接受了由产生幻觉的患者所带来的风险（选项A）。\n\n因此，上述论点有助于确立“风险自担”这一抗辩理由，从而减轻或免除患者对护士所受伤害应承担的责任。\n\n然而，无力支付赔偿金绝不能作为侵权责任的抗辩理由。\n\n因此，对患者而言，最无助于其抗辩的理由是：她是一名贫困人士，其护理费用由政府支付。\n\n教育目标：“风险自担”是针对过失侵权的一种抗辩理由；如果原告已知晓并自愿接受导致其受害的风险，该原则将减少或完全排除其获赔权利。\n\n但无力支付赔偿金绝不能作为侵权责任的抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第496C条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明若原告完全理解并自愿面对某种风险，则无法就该风险引发的过失获得赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 D：该患者属于贫困人士，其护理费用由政府承担。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":75,"sourceQuestionPage":226,"sourceQuestionPages":[226],"sourceExplanationPages":[227,228],"sourceAnswerPages":[227,228],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-076","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A man's dog ran into the street in front of his home and began chasing cars. The man had seen the dog behave similarly on several prior occasions. A ride-share driver, who was driving a car on the street, swerved to avoid hitting the dog, struck a telephone pole, and was injured. There is no statute or ordinance requiring dogs to be leashed on a public street. If the driver asserts a claim against the man, will the driver prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a dog is a domestic animal.","B":"No, because no statute or ordinance made it unlawful for the owner to allow a dog to be unleashed on a public street.","C":"Yes, because the man is strictly liable for injuries caused by his dog.","D":"Yes, because the man knew his dog had a propensity to chase cars and did not restrain it."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Elements of negligence\n\nDuty – defendant owed duty of reasonable care because his/her conduct created foreseeable risk of harm to others Breach – defendant breached duty by failing to use reasonable care Causation – defendant's breach actually & proximately caused plaintiff's harm Damages – plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, bodily injury or property damage) Negligence is the typical tort claim for unintentional harms (eg, car-accident injuries). To prevail on a negligence claim, the plaintiff must prove all of the following elements by a preponderance of the evidence: Duty – the defendant owed a duty of reasonable care because his/her conduct created a foreseeable risk of harm to others\n\nBreach – the defendant breached that duty by failing to use reasonable care\n\nCausation – the defendant's breach actually and proximately caused the plaintiff's harm\n\nDamages – the plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, bodily injury or property damage)\n\nA defendant fails to use reasonable care when he/she does not take reasonable steps to reduce the risk posed by his/her conduct. Here, the man had a duty to use reasonable care since he knew that his dog had a propensity to chase cars. But he breached that duty when he failed to take reasonable steps to prevent the dog from running into the street (eg, by restraining it). And since this caused the driver to crash and sustain injuries, the driver will likely prevail. (Choices A & C) The owner of a domestic animal is not strictly liable for injuries caused by that animal unless the owner knew, or should have known, that the animal had propensities that are more dangerous than normal for the species. So here, the dog's mere propensity to chase cars would not subject the man to strict liability. But the man is still subject to negligence liability for injuries caused by his dog. (Choice B) Had the man violated a statute or ordinance requiring dogs to be leashed on public streets, his negligent conduct could be presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se. But the man's negligent conduct can still be proved since he knew of his dog's propensity to chase cars and did not restrain it.\n\nEducational objective: Persons owe a duty of reasonable care when their conduct poses a foreseeable risk of harm to others. Therefore, persons who fail to take reasonable steps to mitigate that risk and cause another physical harm are liable for negligence.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 281 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that conduct is negligent when it tends to subject another to an unreasonable risk of harm).","educationalObjective":"Persons owe a duty of reasonable care when their conduct poses a foreseeable risk of harm to others. Therefore, persons who fail to take reasonable steps to mitigate that risk and cause another physical harm are liable for negligence.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 281 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that conduct is negligent when it tends to subject another to an unreasonable risk of harm).","rule":"Persons owe a duty of reasonable care when their conduct poses a foreseeable risk of harm to others. Therefore, persons who fail to take reasonable steps to mitigate that risk and cause another physical harm are liable for negligence.","irac":{"issue":"If the driver asserts a claim against the man, will the driver prevail?","rule":"Persons owe a duty of reasonable care when their conduct poses a foreseeable risk of harm to others. Therefore, persons who fail to take reasonable steps to mitigate that risk and cause another physical harm are liable for negligence.","analysis":"Elements of negligence\n\nDuty – defendant owed duty of reasonable care because his/her conduct created foreseeable risk of harm to others Breach – defendant breached duty by failing to use reasonable care Causation – defendant's breach actually & proximately caused plaintiff's harm Damages – plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, bodily injury or property damage) Negligence is the typical tort claim for unintentional harms (eg, car-accident injuries). To prevail on a negligence claim, the plaintiff must prove all of the following elements by a preponderance of the evidence: Duty – the defendant owed a duty of reasonable care because his/her conduct created a foreseeable risk of harm to others\n\nBreach – the defendant breached that duty by failing to use reasonable care\n\nCausation – the defendant's breach actually and proximately caused the plaintiff's harm\n\nDamages – the plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, bodily injury or property damage)\n\nA defendant fails to use reasonable care when he/she does not take reasonable steps to reduce the risk posed by his/her conduct. Here, the man had a duty to use reasonable care since he knew that his dog had a propensity to chase cars. But he breached that duty when he failed to take reasonable steps to prevent the dog from running into the street (eg, by restraining it). And since this caused the driver to crash and sustain injuries, the driver will likely prevail. (Choices A & C) The owner of a domestic animal is not strictly liable for injuries caused by that animal unless the owner knew, or should have known, that the animal had propensities that are more dangerous than normal for the species. So here, the dog's mere propensity to chase cars would not subject the man to strict liability. But the man is still subject to negligence liability for injuries caused by his dog. (Choice B) Had the man violated a statute or ordinance requiring dogs to be leashed on public streets, his negligent conduct could be presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se. But the man's negligent conduct can still be proved since he knew of his dog's propensity to chase cars and did not restrain it.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the man knew his dog had a propensity to chase cars and did not restrain it."},"questionZh":"一名男子的狗跑到了他家门前的街道上，开始追逐车辆。\n\n该男子此前曾多次目睹这只狗有类似行为。\n\n一名正在该街道上驾驶车辆的网约车司机为避让这只狗而急转弯，结果撞上了电线杆并受伤。\n\n当地并无要求狗在公共街道上必须拴绳的成文法或地方法规。\n\n如果该司机向该男子提出索赔，他能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为狗属于家养动物。","B":"不能，因为没有成文法或地方法规规定狗主人让狗在公共街道上不拴绳属于违法行为。","C":"能，因为该男子应对其狗造成的伤害承担严格责任。","D":"能，因为该男子明知其狗有追逐车辆的习性，却未对其加以约束。"},"explanationZh":"过失侵权的构成要素 注意义务（Duty）——被告负有合理的注意义务，因为其行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；违反义务（Breach）——被告因未尽到合理的注意义务而违反了该义务；因果关系（Causation）——被告违反义务的行为实际上且在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）上导致了原告的伤害；损害（Damages）——原告遭受了实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。\n\n过失侵权是针对非故意伤害（例如车祸造成的伤害）的典型侵权诉讼。若要在过失侵权诉讼中胜诉，原告必须根据“优势证据”原则证明以下所有要素：注意义务——被告负有合理的注意义务，因为其行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险； 违反义务——被告因未尽到合理的注意义务而违反了该义务； 因果关系——被告违反义务的行为实际上且在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）上导致了原告的伤害； 损害——原告遭受了实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。 当被告未采取合理措施降低其行为所带来的风险时，即视为未尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n在本案中，该男子负有合理的注意义务，因为他明知其狗有追逐车辆的习性。\n\n但他未能采取合理措施（例如约束狗）以防止狗冲入街道，从而违反了该义务。\n\n由于这一行为导致司机发生车祸并受伤，该司机很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A 和 C）除非饲养人明知或应知其家养动物具有超出该物种正常危险程度的习性，否则饲养人不对该动物造成的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，在本案中，仅凭狗有追逐车辆的习性，并不足以使该男子承担严格责任。\n\n但该男子仍需对其狗造成的伤害承担过失侵权责任。\n\n（选项 B）如果该男子违反了要求在公共街道上必须拴狗的法规或条例，则根据“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则，可推定其行为构成过失。\n\n即便如此，鉴于该男子明知其狗有追逐车辆的习性却未加约束，仍可证明其行为构成过失。\n\n教育目标：当个人的行为给他人造成可预见的伤害风险时，该个人负有合理的注意义务。\n\n因此，未采取合理措施降低该风险并导致他人遭受人身伤害的人，须承担过失责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第281条注释e（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明若某行为倾向于使他人面临不合理的伤害风险，则该行为构成过失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当个人的行为对他人的安全构成可预见的伤害风险时，该个人负有尽到合理注意义务的责任。\n\n因此，若未能采取合理措施降低该风险并导致他人遭受人身伤害，则须承担过失责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该司机向该男子提出索赔，他能胜诉吗？","rule":"当个人的行为对他人的安全构成可预见的伤害风险时，该个人负有尽到合理注意义务的责任。\n\n因此，若未能采取合理措施降低该风险并导致他人遭受人身伤害，则须承担过失责任。","analysis":"过失侵权的构成要素 注意义务（Duty）——被告负有合理的注意义务，因为其行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；违反义务（Breach）——被告因未尽到合理的注意义务而违反了该义务；因果关系（Causation）——被告违反义务的行为实际上且在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）上导致了原告的伤害；损害（Damages）——原告遭受了实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。\n\n过失侵权是针对非故意伤害（例如车祸造成的伤害）的典型侵权诉讼。若要在过失侵权诉讼中胜诉，原告必须根据“优势证据”原则证明以下所有要素：注意义务——被告负有合理的注意义务，因为其行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险； 违反义务——被告因未尽到合理的注意义务而违反了该义务； 因果关系——被告违反义务的行为实际上且在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）上导致了原告的伤害； 损害——原告遭受了实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。 当被告未采取合理措施降低其行为所带来的风险时，即视为未尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n在本案中，该男子负有合理的注意义务，因为他明知其狗有追逐车辆的习性。\n\n但他未能采取合理措施（例如约束狗）以防止狗冲入街道，从而违反了该义务。\n\n由于这一行为导致司机发生车祸并受伤，该司机很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A 和 C）除非饲养人明知或应知其家养动物具有超出该物种正常危险程度的习性，否则饲养人不对该动物造成的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，在本案中，仅凭狗有追逐车辆的习性，并不足以使该男子承担严格责任。\n\n但该男子仍需对其狗造成的伤害承担过失侵权责任。\n\n（选项 B）如果该男子违反了要求在公共街道上必须拴狗的法规或条例，则根据“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则，可推定其行为构成过失。\n\n即便如此，鉴于该男子明知其狗有追逐车辆的习性却未加约束，仍可证明其行为构成过失。\n\n教育目标：当个人的行为给他人造成可预见的伤害风险时，该个人负有合理的注意义务。\n\n因此，未采取合理措施降低该风险并导致他人遭受人身伤害的人，须承担过失责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第281条注释e（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明若某行为倾向于使他人面临不合理的伤害风险，则该行为构成过失）。","conclusion":"答案 D：能，因为该男子明知其狗有追逐车辆的习性，却未对其加以约束。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"domestic animal","ipa":"/dʌmˈɛstɪk ˈænəməl/","syllables":"do·mes·tic an·i·mal","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"家养动物：主人知道异常危险倾向时可能承担严格责任","contextualMeaning":"家养动物：主人知道异常危险倾向时可能承担严格责任","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because a dog is a domestic animal.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":76,"sourceQuestionPage":229,"sourceQuestionPages":[229],"sourceExplanationPages":[230,231],"sourceAnswerPages":[230,231],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-077","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A man rented a car from a car rental agency. Unbeknownst to the rental agency, the car had a bomb hidden in it at the time of the rental. The bomb exploded an hour later, injuring the man. Immediately prior to renting the car to the man, the rental agency had carefully inspected the car to be sure it was in sound operating condition. The rental agency did not inspect for hidden explosive devices, but such an inspection would have revealed the bomb. There had been no previous incidents of persons hiding bombs in rental cars. In a negligence action by the man against the car rental agency, is the man likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the rental agency could not have reasonably foreseen the likelihood of someone placing a bomb in the car it was about to rent to the man.","B":"No, because the rental agency did not hide the bomb in the car.","C":"Yes, because an inspection for explosive devices would have revealed the bomb.","D":"Yes, because the bomb made the car abnormally dangerous."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Relevance of foreseeability for negligence\n\nDuty Defendant's duty only extends to foreseeable risks of his/her conduct\n\nBreach Defendant fails to act like reasonable person under similar circumstances by not mitigating foreseeable risks Causation Actual (but for) cause – foreseeability irrelevant Proximate cause – plaintiff's harm must have been foreseeable consequence of defendant's breach Damages Foreseeability irrelevant (ie, eggshell-skull rule)\n\nNegligence has four elements, three of which require proof of foreseeability: duty, breach, and causation. The duty element can only be satisfied if the defendant's conduct created a reasonably foreseeable risk of harm to others. This occurs when the defendant knows or has reason to know of the risk—eg, from past occurrences leading to harm. And since foreseeability is needed to establish duty, as well as breach and proximate cause, plaintiffs harmed by an unforeseeable risk cannot prevail. Here, the man was injured when a hidden bomb exploded in his rental car. But the rental agency did not know about the bomb. And it could not have reasonably foreseen such a risk since there had been no previous incidents of persons hiding bombs in rental cars. Therefore, the rental agency had no duty to inspect for explosive devices, and the man likely will not prevail. (Choice B) Although the rental agency did not hide the bomb in the car, it could have been liable for failing to inspect for explosive devices had it been reasonably foreseeable that one might be planted in a rental car—eg, because this was a common occurrence in the area. (Choice C) An inspection for explosive devices would have revealed the bomb and prevented the man's harm, so the rental agency's failure to conduct such an inspection was an actual cause of that harm. But the man still cannot prevail since the agency had no duty to inspect for explosive devices. (Choice D) A person is strictly liable for harm caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity. And though storing explosives can be abnormally dangerous, the rental agency did not store (or even know about) the bomb.\n\nEducational objective: Every person has a duty to mitigate the reasonably foreseeable risks of his/her conduct—ie, risks that the person knows or should know about. But no such duty exists for unforeseeable risks.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 281 cmt. f (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a defendant is not liable for negligence when the plaintiff's harm was not a recognizable risk of the defendant's conduct).","educationalObjective":"Every person has a duty to mitigate the reasonably foreseeable risks of his/her conduct—ie, risks that the person knows or should know about. But no such duty exists for unforeseeable risks.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 281 cmt. f (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a defendant is not liable for negligence when the plaintiff's harm was not a recognizable risk of the defendant's conduct).","rule":"Every person has a duty to mitigate the reasonably foreseeable risks of his/her conduct—ie, risks that the person knows or should know about. But no such duty exists for unforeseeable risks.","irac":{"issue":"In a negligence action by the man against the car rental agency, is the man likely to prevail?","rule":"Every person has a duty to mitigate the reasonably foreseeable risks of his/her conduct—ie, risks that the person knows or should know about. But no such duty exists for unforeseeable risks.","analysis":"Relevance of foreseeability for negligence\n\nDuty Defendant's duty only extends to foreseeable risks of his/her conduct\n\nBreach Defendant fails to act like reasonable person under similar circumstances by not mitigating foreseeable risks Causation Actual (but for) cause – foreseeability irrelevant Proximate cause – plaintiff's harm must have been foreseeable consequence of defendant's breach Damages Foreseeability irrelevant (ie, eggshell-skull rule)\n\nNegligence has four elements, three of which require proof of foreseeability: duty, breach, and causation. The duty element can only be satisfied if the defendant's conduct created a reasonably foreseeable risk of harm to others. This occurs when the defendant knows or has reason to know of the risk—eg, from past occurrences leading to harm. And since foreseeability is needed to establish duty, as well as breach and proximate cause, plaintiffs harmed by an unforeseeable risk cannot prevail. Here, the man was injured when a hidden bomb exploded in his rental car. But the rental agency did not know about the bomb. And it could not have reasonably foreseen such a risk since there had been no previous incidents of persons hiding bombs in rental cars. Therefore, the rental agency had no duty to inspect for explosive devices, and the man likely will not prevail. (Choice B) Although the rental agency did not hide the bomb in the car, it could have been liable for failing to inspect for explosive devices had it been reasonably foreseeable that one might be planted in a rental car—eg, because this was a common occurrence in the area. (Choice C) An inspection for explosive devices would have revealed the bomb and prevented the man's harm, so the rental agency's failure to conduct such an inspection was an actual cause of that harm. But the man still cannot prevail since the agency had no duty to inspect for explosive devices. (Choice D) A person is strictly liable for harm caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity. And though storing explosives can be abnormally dangerous, the rental agency did not store (or even know about) the bomb.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the rental agency could not have reasonably foreseen the likelihood of someone placing a bomb in the car it was about to rent to the man."},"questionZh":"一名男子从租车公司租了一辆车。\n\n在出租时，租车公司并不知道车内藏有炸弹。\n\n一小时后炸弹爆炸，导致该男子受伤。\n\n在将车租给该男子之前，租车公司曾仔细检查车辆，以确保其处于良好的运行状态。\n\n租车公司并未检查是否存在隐藏的爆炸装置，但如果进行了此类检查，就能发现炸弹。\n\n此前从未发生过有人在租赁车辆中藏匿炸弹的事件。\n\n在该男子针对租车公司提起的过失诉讼中，该男子胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为租车公司无法合理预见到有人会在即将租给该男子的车内放置炸弹的可能性。","B":"不大，因为炸弹并非由租车公司藏在车内的。","C":"大，因为针对爆炸装置的检查本可以发现炸弹。","D":"大，因为炸弹使该车辆变得异常危险。"},"explanationZh":"过失侵权中“可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）”的相关性 注意义务：被告的注意义务仅限于其行为所带来的可预见风险。 违反义务：被告未能在类似情况下像“理性人”那样行事，未能减轻可预见风险。 因果关系：事实因果关系（“若无……则不……”测试）——与可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）无关；法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）——原告遭受的损害必须是被告违反义务的可预见后果。 损害赔偿：与可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）无关（例如，“薄弱头骨规则”/Eggshell-skull rule）。\n\n过失侵权包含四个要素，其中三个要素（注意义务、违反义务和因果关系）需要证明存在可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）。\n\n只有当被告的行为给他人造成了“合理可预见”的伤害风险时，注意义务这一要素才成立。\n\n这种情况通常发生在被告已知或有理由知道该风险时——例如，基于过往导致伤害的事件。由于确立注意义务、违反义务及法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）都需要可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability），因此因不可预见风险而受害的原告无法胜诉。\n\n在本案中，该男子因其租赁车辆内隐藏的炸弹爆炸而受伤。\n\n但租赁公司并不知晓炸弹的存在。\n\n此外，由于此前从未发生过有人在租赁车辆内藏匿炸弹的事件，公司也无法合理预见此类风险。\n\n因此，租赁公司没有义务检查爆炸装置，该男子很可能无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）尽管租赁公司并未在车内藏匿炸弹，但如果当时合理预见到租赁车辆内可能被安放炸弹（例如，因为该地区此类事件频发），公司本应因未检查爆炸装置而承担责任。\n\n（选项 C）检查爆炸装置本可以发现炸弹并防止该男子受害，因此租赁公司未进行此类检查构成了该伤害的事实因果关系。\n\n但该男子仍无法胜诉，因为公司没有义务检查爆炸装置。\n\n（选项 D）对于因从事异常危险活动而造成的损害，行为人承担严格责任。\n\n虽然储存爆炸物可能属于异常危险活动，但租赁公司并未储存（甚至不知晓）该炸弹。\n\n教学目标：每个人都有义务减轻其行为带来的合理可预见风险——即行为人已知或应知的风险。\n\n但对于不可预见风险，则不存在此类义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第281条注释f（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明若原告所受损害并非被告行为所带来的可预见风险，则被告不承担过失责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"每个人都有义务减轻其行为带来的可合理预见的风险——即该人已知或应知的风险。\n\n但对于不可预见的风险，则不存在此类义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"在该男子针对租车公司提起的过失诉讼中，该男子胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"每个人都有义务减轻其行为带来的可合理预见的风险——即该人已知或应知的风险。\n\n但对于不可预见的风险，则不存在此类义务。","analysis":"过失侵权中“可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）”的相关性 注意义务：被告的注意义务仅限于其行为所带来的可预见风险。 违反义务：被告未能在类似情况下像“理性人”那样行事，未能减轻可预见风险。 因果关系：事实因果关系（“若无……则不……”测试）——与可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）无关；法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）——原告遭受的损害必须是被告违反义务的可预见后果。 损害赔偿：与可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）无关（例如，“薄弱头骨规则”/Eggshell-skull rule）。\n\n过失侵权包含四个要素，其中三个要素（注意义务、违反义务和因果关系）需要证明存在可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）。\n\n只有当被告的行为给他人造成了“合理可预见”的伤害风险时，注意义务这一要素才成立。\n\n这种情况通常发生在被告已知或有理由知道该风险时——例如，基于过往导致伤害的事件。由于确立注意义务、违反义务及法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）都需要可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability），因此因不可预见风险而受害的原告无法胜诉。\n\n在本案中，该男子因其租赁车辆内隐藏的炸弹爆炸而受伤。\n\n但租赁公司并不知晓炸弹的存在。\n\n此外，由于此前从未发生过有人在租赁车辆内藏匿炸弹的事件，公司也无法合理预见此类风险。\n\n因此，租赁公司没有义务检查爆炸装置，该男子很可能无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）尽管租赁公司并未在车内藏匿炸弹，但如果当时合理预见到租赁车辆内可能被安放炸弹（例如，因为该地区此类事件频发），公司本应因未检查爆炸装置而承担责任。\n\n（选项 C）检查爆炸装置本可以发现炸弹并防止该男子受害，因此租赁公司未进行此类检查构成了该伤害的事实因果关系。\n\n但该男子仍无法胜诉，因为公司没有义务检查爆炸装置。\n\n（选项 D）对于因从事异常危险活动而造成的损害，行为人承担严格责任。\n\n虽然储存爆炸物可能属于异常危险活动，但租赁公司并未储存（甚至不知晓）该炸弹。\n\n教学目标：每个人都有义务减轻其行为带来的合理可预见风险——即行为人已知或应知的风险。\n\n但对于不可预见风险，则不存在此类义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第281条注释f（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明若原告所受损害并非被告行为所带来的可预见风险，则被告不承担过失责任）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不大，因为租车公司无法合理预见到有人会在即将租给该男子的车内放置炸弹的可能性。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"abnormally dangerous activity","ipa":"/æbnˈɔɹməli dˈeɪndʒɝəs æktˈɪvəti/","syllables":"ab·nor·mally dan·ger·ous ac·tiv·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"异常危险活动：即使合理谨慎仍有高度重大风险的活动","contextualMeaning":"异常危险活动：即使合理谨慎仍有高度重大风险的活动","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) A person is strictly liable for harm caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":77,"sourceQuestionPage":232,"sourceQuestionPages":[232],"sourceExplanationPages":[233,234],"sourceAnswerPages":[233,234],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-078","subject":"Torts","topic":"Actual & Proximate Cause","question":"A driver, returning home from a long work shift at a factory, fell asleep at the wheel and lost control of his car. As a result, his car collided with a police car driven by an officer who was returning to the station after having responded to an emergency. The officer was injured in the accident and later sued the driver in negligence for her injuries. The driver has moved for summary judgment, arguing that the common law firefighter's rule bars the suit. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the firefighter's rule does not apply to police officers.","B":"No, because the police officer's injuries were not related to any special dangers of her job.","C":"Yes, because the accident would not have occurred but for the emergency.","D":"Yes, because the police officer was injured on the job."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Rescue doctrine\n\nGeneral rule Rescuers can recover for injuries sustained while attempting to rescue another if that person's peril was caused by defendant's negligence\n\nException Professional rescuers cannot recover for injuries attributable to special dangers of their job—eg: (firefighter's rule) firefighter entering burning building police officer arresting suspect paramedic/lifeguard attempting risky rescue When a person's conduct creates a risk of harm, he/she owes a duty to all foreseeable plaintiffs. Under the rescue doctrine, foreseeable plaintiffs include rescuers who might be harmed while attempting to aid victims of the risky conduct. But the rescue doctrine is limited by the common law firefighter's rule, which applies to all professional rescuers (eg, police officers) (Choice A). This rule bars recovery in negligence if the professional rescuer's harm resulted from a special danger of his/her job. Here, the officer was injured in a car accident and later sued the driver in negligence for her injuries. The officer's injuries were not related to any special dangers of her job since the risk of being struck by a car in normal traffic applies to everyone on the road. Therefore, the firefighter's rule does not bar the officer's recovery, and the court should deny the driver's motion for summary judgment. (Choice C) The emergency—from which the officer was returning—was an actual cause of the accident since it would not have occurred but for the emergency. But this does not justify summary judgment for the driver since his conduct (ie, falling asleep at the wheel) was also an actual cause of the accident. (Choice D) Although the police officer was injured on the job, her injury did not result from any special danger of police work. Therefore, summary judgment for the driver would be improper.\n\nEducational objective: The firefighter's rule is a limitation on the rescue doctrine that bars firefighters and other professional rescuers from recovering in negligence for harm that resulted from the special dangers of their jobs. But they can still recover for injuries that arose from risks applicable to everyone.\n\nReferences\n62 Am. Jur. 2d Premises Liability § 421 (2019) (explaining that the firefighter's rule prevents professional rescuers from recovering for injuries arising out of the special risks of their professions).\n\nVasquez v. N. Cty. Transit Dist., 292 F.3d 1049, 1054–55 (9th Cir. 2002) (explaining that the firefighter's rule extends to police officers).","educationalObjective":"The firefighter's rule is a limitation on the rescue doctrine that bars firefighters and other professional rescuers from recovering in negligence for harm that resulted from the special dangers of their jobs. But they can still recover for injuries that arose from risks applicable to everyone.","references":"62 Am. Jur. 2d Premises Liability § 421 (2019) (explaining that the firefighter's rule prevents professional rescuers from recovering for injuries arising out of the special risks of their professions).\n\nVasquez v. N. Cty. Transit Dist., 292 F.3d 1049, 1054–55 (9th Cir. 2002) (explaining that the firefighter's rule extends to police officers).","rule":"The firefighter's rule is a limitation on the rescue doctrine that bars firefighters and other professional rescuers from recovering in negligence for harm that resulted from the special dangers of their jobs. But they can still recover for injuries that arose from risks applicable to everyone.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"The firefighter's rule is a limitation on the rescue doctrine that bars firefighters and other professional rescuers from recovering in negligence for harm that resulted from the special dangers of their jobs. But they can still recover for injuries that arose from risks applicable to everyone.","analysis":"Rescue doctrine\n\nGeneral rule Rescuers can recover for injuries sustained while attempting to rescue another if that person's peril was caused by defendant's negligence\n\nException Professional rescuers cannot recover for injuries attributable to special dangers of their job—eg: (firefighter's rule) firefighter entering burning building police officer arresting suspect paramedic/lifeguard attempting risky rescue When a person's conduct creates a risk of harm, he/she owes a duty to all foreseeable plaintiffs. Under the rescue doctrine, foreseeable plaintiffs include rescuers who might be harmed while attempting to aid victims of the risky conduct. But the rescue doctrine is limited by the common law firefighter's rule, which applies to all professional rescuers (eg, police officers) (Choice A). This rule bars recovery in negligence if the professional rescuer's harm resulted from a special danger of his/her job. Here, the officer was injured in a car accident and later sued the driver in negligence for her injuries. The officer's injuries were not related to any special dangers of her job since the risk of being struck by a car in normal traffic applies to everyone on the road. Therefore, the firefighter's rule does not bar the officer's recovery, and the court should deny the driver's motion for summary judgment. (Choice C) The emergency—from which the officer was returning—was an actual cause of the accident since it would not have occurred but for the emergency. But this does not justify summary judgment for the driver since his conduct (ie, falling asleep at the wheel) was also an actual cause of the accident. (Choice D) Although the police officer was injured on the job, her injury did not result from any special danger of police work. Therefore, summary judgment for the driver would be improper.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the police officer's injuries were not related to any special dangers of her job."},"questionZh":"一名司机在工厂长时间轮班后驾车回家途中，因在驾驶时睡着而导致车辆失控。\n\n结果，他的车撞上了一辆警车；该警车由一名刚处理完紧急情况正返回警局的警官驾驶。\n\n这名警官在事故中受伤，随后以过失为由起诉了该司机。\n\n司机提出简易判决动议，主张普通法中的“消防员规则”（firefighter's rule）禁止此类诉讼。\n\n法院应批准该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应批准，因为消防员规则不适用于警官。","B":"不应批准，因为警官的受伤与她工作中的任何特殊危险无关。","C":"应批准，因为若非那起紧急情况，事故就不会发生。","D":"应批准，因为警官是在工作中受伤的。"},"explanationZh":"“救援原则”（Rescue doctrine） 一般规则：如果某人的险境是由被告的过失造成的，那么试图营救该人而受伤的救援者可以获得损害赔偿。 例外情况：职业救援者若因其工作特有的危险而受伤，则无法获得赔偿——例如（“消防员规则”/firefighter's rule）：消防员进入燃烧的建筑物、警察逮捕嫌疑人、急救人员/救生员进行高风险救援。\n\n当某人的行为制造了伤害风险时，该人对所有可预见原告负有注意义务。根据救援原则，可预见原告包括那些在试图援助因上述高风险行为而受害的人时可能受伤的救援者。\n\n但救援原则受到普通法中“消防员规则”的限制，该规则适用于所有职业救援者（例如警察）（选项 A）。\n\n如果职业救援者受到的伤害源于其工作特有的危险，该规则禁止其基于过失主张赔偿。\n\n在本案中，该警官在车祸中受伤，随后起诉司机过失导致其受伤。\n\n该警官的受伤与任何工作特有危险无关，因为在正常交通状况下被汽车撞击的风险适用于道路上的所有人。\n\n因此，“消防员规则”并不妨碍该警官获得赔偿，法院应驳回司机提出的即决判决（summary judgment）动议。\n\n（选项 C）警官当时正从处理紧急情况的现场返回，该紧急情况是事故的“事实因果关系”（actual cause）来源，因为若无该紧急情况，事故便不会发生。\n\n但这并不能成为支持司机获得即决判决的理由，因为司机的行为（即开车时睡着）也是事故的事实因果关系来源。\n\n（选项 D）尽管该警官是在执行公务时受伤，但其受伤并非源于警察工作的任何特有危险。\n\n因此，支持司机获得即决判决是不恰当的。\n\n教育目标：“消防员规则”是对救援原则的一种限制，禁止消防员和其他职业救援者因其工作特有危险导致的伤害而主张过失赔偿。\n\n但如果伤害源于适用于所有人的风险，他们仍可获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 62 Am. Jur. 2d Premises Liability § 421 (2019)（解释了“消防员规则”禁止职业救援者因其职业特有风险引起的伤害而获得赔偿）。\n\nVasquez 诉 N. Cty. Transit Dist. 案，292 F.3d 1049, 1054–55 (9th Cir. 2002)（阐明“消防员规则”同样适用于警务人员）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"消防员规则是对“救援原则”（rescue doctrine）的一种限制，它禁止消防员及其他专业救援人员因工作中特有危险所致的伤害而提起过失诉讼并获得赔偿。\n\n但对于因普通人同样面临的风险而造成的伤害，他们仍可获得赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应批准该动议吗？","rule":"消防员规则是对“救援原则”（rescue doctrine）的一种限制，它禁止消防员及其他专业救援人员因工作中特有危险所致的伤害而提起过失诉讼并获得赔偿。\n\n但对于因普通人同样面临的风险而造成的伤害，他们仍可获得赔偿。","analysis":"“救援原则”（Rescue doctrine） 一般规则：如果某人的险境是由被告的过失造成的，那么试图营救该人而受伤的救援者可以获得损害赔偿。 例外情况：职业救援者若因其工作特有的危险而受伤，则无法获得赔偿——例如（“消防员规则”/firefighter's rule）：消防员进入燃烧的建筑物、警察逮捕嫌疑人、急救人员/救生员进行高风险救援。\n\n当某人的行为制造了伤害风险时，该人对所有可预见原告负有注意义务。根据救援原则，可预见原告包括那些在试图援助因上述高风险行为而受害的人时可能受伤的救援者。\n\n但救援原则受到普通法中“消防员规则”的限制，该规则适用于所有职业救援者（例如警察）（选项 A）。\n\n如果职业救援者受到的伤害源于其工作特有的危险，该规则禁止其基于过失主张赔偿。\n\n在本案中，该警官在车祸中受伤，随后起诉司机过失导致其受伤。\n\n该警官的受伤与任何工作特有危险无关，因为在正常交通状况下被汽车撞击的风险适用于道路上的所有人。\n\n因此，“消防员规则”并不妨碍该警官获得赔偿，法院应驳回司机提出的即决判决（summary judgment）动议。\n\n（选项 C）警官当时正从处理紧急情况的现场返回，该紧急情况是事故的“事实因果关系”（actual cause）来源，因为若无该紧急情况，事故便不会发生。\n\n但这并不能成为支持司机获得即决判决的理由，因为司机的行为（即开车时睡着）也是事故的事实因果关系来源。\n\n（选项 D）尽管该警官是在执行公务时受伤，但其受伤并非源于警察工作的任何特有危险。\n\n因此，支持司机获得即决判决是不恰当的。\n\n教育目标：“消防员规则”是对救援原则的一种限制，禁止消防员和其他职业救援者因其工作特有危险导致的伤害而主张过失赔偿。\n\n但如果伤害源于适用于所有人的风险，他们仍可获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 62 Am. Jur. 2d Premises Liability § 421 (2019)（解释了“消防员规则”禁止职业救援者因其职业特有风险引起的伤害而获得赔偿）。\n\nVasquez 诉 N. Cty. Transit Dist. 案，292 F.3d 1049, 1054–55 (9th Cir. 2002)（阐明“消防员规则”同样适用于警务人员）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不应批准，因为警官的受伤与她工作中的任何特殊危险无关。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":78,"sourceQuestionPage":235,"sourceQuestionPages":[235],"sourceExplanationPages":[236,237],"sourceAnswerPages":[236,237],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-079","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A car owner washed her car while it was parked on a public street, in violation of a local ordinance that prohibits the washing of vehicles on public streets during specified hours. The ordinance was enacted only to expedite the flow of automobile traffic. Due to sudden and unexpected cold weather, the car owner's wastewater formed a puddle that froze in a crosswalk. A pedestrian slipped on the frozen puddle and broke her leg. The pedestrian sued the car owner to recover for her injury. At trial, the only evidence the pedestrian offered as to negligence was the car owner's admission that she had violated the ordinance. At the conclusion of the evidence, both parties moved for a directed verdict. How should the trial judge proceed?","choices":{"A":"Deny both motions and submit the case to the jury, because, on the facts, the jury may infer that the car owner was negligent.","B":"Deny both motions and submit the case to the jury, because the jury may consider the ordinance violation as evidence that the car owner was negligent.","C":"Grant the car owner's motion, because the pedestrian has failed to offer adequate evidence that the car owner was negligent.","D":"Grant the pedestrian's motion, because of the car owner's admitted ordinance violation."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Establishing breach of duty\n\nDoctrine Standard Effect\n\nDefault Defendant failed to act in same manner as Proof of breach reasonably prudent person in similar circumstances Custom / Defendant failed to act according to local Evidence (but not industry custom or industry standards proof) of breach standards\n\nNegligence Defendant violated law Breach presumed unless Law was intended to prevent plaintiff's type rebutted per se of harm Plaintiff is in class of persons law intended to protect Res ipsa loquitur Plaintiff suffered injury usually caused by Allows (but does not negligence of someone in defendant's require) inference of position breach Evidence tends to eliminate other causes of injury To prevail on a negligence claim, the plaintiff must prove the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. The elements of duty and breach can be presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se if the plaintiff proves that: the defendant violated a statute or ordinance that statute or ordinance was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff and the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute or ordinance was intended to protect. If the plaintiff fails to present legally sufficient evidence to support all the elements of negligence at trial—ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor—the court should grant the defendant's motion for a directed verdict. Here, the pedestrian slipped on a frozen puddle and broke her leg. The puddle had been left by the car owner, who washed her car on a public street in violation of a local ordinance. But that ordinance was enacted to expedite traffic flow—not to protect pedestrians from slipping. Therefore, negligence per se does not apply. And since the pedestrian offered no other evidence supporting her negligence claim, the judge should grant the car owner's motion for a directed verdict (Choices A, B, & D).\n\nEducational objective: The requirements for negligence per se are that (1) the defendant violated a statute or ordinance, (2) the statute or ordinance was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff, and (3) the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 328B (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that the trial judge determines whether the evidence is adequate to raise a factual dispute in a negligence action).","educationalObjective":"The requirements for negligence per se are that (1) the defendant violated a statute or ordinance, (2) the statute or ordinance was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff, and (3) the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328B (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that the trial judge determines whether the evidence is adequate to raise a factual dispute in a negligence action).","rule":"The requirements for negligence per se are that (1) the defendant violated a statute or ordinance, (2) the statute or ordinance was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff, and (3) the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect.","irac":{"issue":"How should the trial judge proceed?","rule":"The requirements for negligence per se are that (1) the defendant violated a statute or ordinance, (2) the statute or ordinance was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff, and (3) the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect.","analysis":"Establishing breach of duty\n\nDoctrine Standard Effect\n\nDefault Defendant failed to act in same manner as Proof of breach reasonably prudent person in similar circumstances Custom / Defendant failed to act according to local Evidence (but not industry custom or industry standards proof) of breach standards\n\nNegligence Defendant violated law Breach presumed unless Law was intended to prevent plaintiff's type rebutted per se of harm Plaintiff is in class of persons law intended to protect Res ipsa loquitur Plaintiff suffered injury usually caused by Allows (but does not negligence of someone in defendant's require) inference of position breach Evidence tends to eliminate other causes of injury To prevail on a negligence claim, the plaintiff must prove the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. The elements of duty and breach can be presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se if the plaintiff proves that: the defendant violated a statute or ordinance that statute or ordinance was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff and the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute or ordinance was intended to protect. If the plaintiff fails to present legally sufficient evidence to support all the elements of negligence at trial—ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor—the court should grant the defendant's motion for a directed verdict. Here, the pedestrian slipped on a frozen puddle and broke her leg. The puddle had been left by the car owner, who washed her car on a public street in violation of a local ordinance. But that ordinance was enacted to expedite traffic flow—not to protect pedestrians from slipping. Therefore, negligence per se does not apply. And since the pedestrian offered no other evidence supporting her negligence claim, the judge should grant the car owner's motion for a directed verdict (Choices A, B, & D).","conclusion":"Answer C: Grant the car owner's motion, because the pedestrian has failed to offer adequate evidence that the car owner was negligent."},"questionZh":"一位车主在公共街道上停车洗车，违反了当地一项禁止在特定时段于公共街道上洗车的法规。\n\n该法规制定的唯一目的是为了加快汽车交通的通行速度。\n\n由于突发且意料之外的寒冷天气，车主排出的废水在人行横道上形成了一滩积水并结了冰。\n\n一名行人在这片结冰的积水上滑倒，导致腿部骨折。\n\n该行人起诉车主，要求赔偿其伤害。\n\n在庭审中，行人就过失问题提供的唯一证据是车主承认自己违反了该法规。\n\n在举证阶段结束后，双方均申请法官作出指示裁决（即要求法官直接判决，无需交由陪审团审议）。\n\n主审法官应如何处理？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回双方的申请，并将案件提交给陪审团审议，因为根据案件事实，陪审团可以推断车主存在过失。","B":"驳回双方的申请，并将案件提交给陪审团审议，因为陪审团可以将违反法规这一事实视为车主存在过失的证据。","C":"支持车主的申请，因为行人未能提供充分证据证明车主存在过失。","D":"支持行人的申请，因为车主承认违反了法规。"},"explanationZh":"确立违反注意义务 **原则 / 标准 / 效力** **一般标准**：被告未像类似情况下的理性谨慎之人那样行事 → 证明违反义务 **行业惯例/标准**：被告未按当地或行业惯例/标准行事 → 证明违反义务 **“本身过失”原则 (Negligence *per se*)**：被告违反法律 → 除非被反驳，否则推定存在过失（前提：法律旨在防止原告所受的此类伤害；原告属于法律旨在保护的人群） **“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”原则 (*Res ipsa loquitur*)**：原告遭受的伤害通常由处于被告位置的人的过失引起；证据倾向于排除其他致伤原因 → 允许（但不强制）推断存在违反义务的行为 若要在过失索赔中胜诉，原告必须证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系和损害后果。\n\n如果原告能证明以下几点，则可根据“本身过失”原则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的情形：被告违反了某项法规或条例；该法规或条例旨在防止原告所受的此类伤害；且原告属于该法规或条例旨在保护的人群。\n\n如果在庭审中，原告未能提供足以在法律上支持过失索赔所有要素的证据（即没有理性的陪审团会做出有利于原告的裁决），法院应批准被告提出的指示裁决（directed verdict）动议。\n\n在本案中，行人因踩到结冰的水坑滑倒并摔断了腿。\n\n该水坑由车主留下，车主违反当地条例在公共街道上洗车。\n\n然而，该条例的制定旨在加快交通流量，而非防止行人滑倒。\n\n因此，“本身过失”原则不适用。\n\n鉴于该行人未提供其他证据支持其过失索赔，法官应批准车主提出的指示裁决动议（选项 A、B 和 D）。\n\n**教育目标**：“本身过失”原则的适用要求包括：(1) 被告违反了法规或条例；(2) 该法规或条例旨在防止原告所受的此类伤害；以及 (3) 原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第328B条（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明在过失诉讼中，由初审法官判定证据是否足以引发事实争议）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"构成“本身过失”（negligence per se）的要件包括：(1) 被告违反了某项成文法或法规；(2) 该成文法或法规旨在防止原告所遭受的那类损害；以及 (3) 原告属于该成文法旨在保护的人群范围。","iracZh":{"issue":"主审法官应如何处理？","rule":"构成“本身过失”（negligence per se）的要件包括：(1) 被告违反了某项成文法或法规；(2) 该成文法或法规旨在防止原告所遭受的那类损害；以及 (3) 原告属于该成文法旨在保护的人群范围。","analysis":"确立违反注意义务 **原则 / 标准 / 效力** **一般标准**：被告未像类似情况下的理性谨慎之人那样行事 → 证明违反义务 **行业惯例/标准**：被告未按当地或行业惯例/标准行事 → 证明违反义务 **“本身过失”原则 (Negligence *per se*)**：被告违反法律 → 除非被反驳，否则推定存在过失（前提：法律旨在防止原告所受的此类伤害；原告属于法律旨在保护的人群） **“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”原则 (*Res ipsa loquitur*)**：原告遭受的伤害通常由处于被告位置的人的过失引起；证据倾向于排除其他致伤原因 → 允许（但不强制）推断存在违反义务的行为 若要在过失索赔中胜诉，原告必须证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系和损害后果。\n\n如果原告能证明以下几点，则可根据“本身过失”原则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的情形：被告违反了某项法规或条例；该法规或条例旨在防止原告所受的此类伤害；且原告属于该法规或条例旨在保护的人群。\n\n如果在庭审中，原告未能提供足以在法律上支持过失索赔所有要素的证据（即没有理性的陪审团会做出有利于原告的裁决），法院应批准被告提出的指示裁决（directed verdict）动议。\n\n在本案中，行人因踩到结冰的水坑滑倒并摔断了腿。\n\n该水坑由车主留下，车主违反当地条例在公共街道上洗车。\n\n然而，该条例的制定旨在加快交通流量，而非防止行人滑倒。\n\n因此，“本身过失”原则不适用。\n\n鉴于该行人未提供其他证据支持其过失索赔，法官应批准车主提出的指示裁决动议（选项 A、B 和 D）。\n\n**教育目标**：“本身过失”原则的适用要求包括：(1) 被告违反了法规或条例；(2) 该法规或条例旨在防止原告所受的此类伤害；以及 (3) 原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第328B条（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明在过失诉讼中，由初审法官判定证据是否足以引发事实争议）。","conclusion":"答案 C：支持车主的申请，因为行人未能提供充分证据证明车主存在过失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":79,"sourceQuestionPage":238,"sourceQuestionPages":[238],"sourceExplanationPages":[239,240],"sourceAnswerPages":[239,240],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-080","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"During a comprehensive evaluation of an adult patient's psychiatric condition, a psychiatrist failed to diagnose her patient's suicidal state. One day after the misdiagnosis, the patient committed suicide. The patient's father, immediately after having been told of his son's suicide, suffered severe emotional distress, which resulted in a stroke. The patient's father was not present at the patient's appointment with the psychiatrist, nor did he witness the suicide. The father has brought an action against the psychiatrist to recover for his severe emotional distress and the resulting stroke. Is the father likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the father did not sustain a physical impact.","B":"No, because the psychiatrist's professional duty did not extend to the harms suffered by the patient's father.","C":"Yes, because the father was a member of the patient's immediate family.","D":"Yes, because the psychiatrist reasonably could have foreseen that a misdiagnosis would result in the patient's suicide and the resulting emotional distress of the patient's father."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Mental-health professional's\n\nduty to control patient's conduct\n\nMust use reasonable care to:\n\ndetermine whether patient poses risk of harm to others and mitigate that risk—eg: treat patient warn potential victims notify law enforcement seek patient's commitment For negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED), the plaintiff must prove that the defendant acted negligently by breaching a duty owed to the plaintiff. Mental-health professionals owe their patients a duty to use reasonable care. And because they have a special relationship with their patients, mental-health professionals also owe others a duty to use reasonable care to: identify patients who pose a foreseeable risk of physical harm to others and take steps to mitigate that risk. Here, the psychiatrist may be liable to the patient's estate for failing to diagnose his suicidal state. However, the psychiatrist is not liable to the father because the patient only posed a threat of physical harm to himself—not to his father. Therefore, the psychiatrist did not breach her duty to the father, and the father will not recover for his severe emotional distress or resulting stroke. (Choice A) The zone-of-danger theory of NIED requires proof that the plaintiff was in danger of physical harm—not actually harmed by a physical impact. Therefore, had the psychiatrist breached her duty to the father by placing him in a zone of physical danger, then the father may have prevailed despite his lack of physical impact. (Choice C) The father's relation to the patient is relevant under the bystander theory of NIED, which applies when a plaintiff suffers emotional distress upon witnessing bodily harm to a close relative. But since the father did not witness the patient's suicide, this theory does not apply. (Choice D) The psychiatrist's ability to foresee that her misdiagnosis would result in the patient's suicide shows that she breached her duty to the patient—not the father. And though the father's resulting emotional distress may have been foreseeable, the psychiatrist's duty to the father only extended to foreseeable risks of physical harm.\n\nEducational objective: Mental-health professionals have a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate any foreseeable risk of physical harm that their patients pose to others.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 41 cmt. g (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (duty of mental-health professionals to control patients).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 47 cmt. i (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (duty arising from a special relationship is limited to foreseeable physical harm).","educationalObjective":"Mental-health professionals have a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate any foreseeable risk of physical harm that their patients pose to others.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 41 cmt. g (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (duty of mental-health professionals to control patients).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 47 cmt. i (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (duty arising from a special relationship is limited to foreseeable physical harm).","rule":"Mental-health professionals have a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate any foreseeable risk of physical harm that their patients pose to others.","irac":{"issue":"Is the father likely to prevail?","rule":"Mental-health professionals have a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate any foreseeable risk of physical harm that their patients pose to others.","analysis":"Mental-health professional's\n\nduty to control patient's conduct\n\nMust use reasonable care to:\n\ndetermine whether patient poses risk of harm to others and mitigate that risk—eg: treat patient warn potential victims notify law enforcement seek patient's commitment For negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED), the plaintiff must prove that the defendant acted negligently by breaching a duty owed to the plaintiff. Mental-health professionals owe their patients a duty to use reasonable care. And because they have a special relationship with their patients, mental-health professionals also owe others a duty to use reasonable care to: identify patients who pose a foreseeable risk of physical harm to others and take steps to mitigate that risk. Here, the psychiatrist may be liable to the patient's estate for failing to diagnose his suicidal state. However, the psychiatrist is not liable to the father because the patient only posed a threat of physical harm to himself—not to his father. Therefore, the psychiatrist did not breach her duty to the father, and the father will not recover for his severe emotional distress or resulting stroke. (Choice A) The zone-of-danger theory of NIED requires proof that the plaintiff was in danger of physical harm—not actually harmed by a physical impact. Therefore, had the psychiatrist breached her duty to the father by placing him in a zone of physical danger, then the father may have prevailed despite his lack of physical impact. (Choice C) The father's relation to the patient is relevant under the bystander theory of NIED, which applies when a plaintiff suffers emotional distress upon witnessing bodily harm to a close relative. But since the father did not witness the patient's suicide, this theory does not apply. (Choice D) The psychiatrist's ability to foresee that her misdiagnosis would result in the patient's suicide shows that she breached her duty to the patient—not the father. And though the father's resulting emotional distress may have been foreseeable, the psychiatrist's duty to the father only extended to foreseeable risks of physical harm.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the psychiatrist's professional duty did not extend to the harms suffered by the patient's father."},"questionZh":"在对一名成年患者的精神状况进行全面评估期间，精神科医生未能诊断出患者的自杀倾向。\n\n误诊发生一天后，患者自杀身亡。\n\n患者的父亲在得知儿子自杀的消息后，遭受了严重的精神痛苦，并因此引发了中风。\n\n患者父亲既未陪同患者就诊，也未目睹自杀过程。\n\n这位父亲针对精神科医生提起诉讼，要求赔偿其遭受的严重精神痛苦及由此引发的中风。\n\n这位父亲胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为这位父亲未遭受身体上的物理冲击。","B":"不大，因为精神科医生的职业义务并不涵盖患者父亲所遭受的损害。","C":"很大，因为这位父亲是患者的直系亲属。","D":"很大，因为精神科医生本可以合理预见到，误诊会导致患者自杀，进而给患者父亲带来精神痛苦。"},"explanationZh":"心理健康专业人员 控制患者行为的义务 必须尽到合理注意义务，以： 确定患者是否对他人的安全构成风险并降低该风险——例如：治疗患者、警告潜在受害者、通知执法部门、寻求将患者送医强制治疗。关于过失导致精神痛苦（NIED）的诉讼，原告必须证明被告因违反了对原告负有的义务而存在过失行为。\n\n心理健康专业人员对患者负有尽到合理注意的义务。\n\n此外，由于与患者存在特殊关系，心理健康专业人员对他人也负有尽到合理注意的义务，具体包括：识别那些对他人的身体安全构成可预见风险的患者，并采取措施降低该风险。\n\n在本案中，精神科医生可能因未能诊断出患者的自杀倾向而需向患者的遗产承担责任。\n\n然而，精神科医生无需向父亲承担责任，因为患者仅对自己构成身体伤害的威胁，并未威胁到其父亲的身体安全。\n\n因此，精神科医生并未违反其对父亲的义务，父亲也无法就其遭受的严重精神痛苦或由此引发的中风获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）NIED 诉讼中的“危险区域”理论要求证明原告处于遭受身体伤害的危险之中，而非必须遭受实际的身体接触伤害。\n\n因此，如果精神科医生因将父亲置于身体危险区域而违反了对父亲的义务，那么即使父亲未遭受身体接触伤害，也可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）父亲与患者的关系在 NIED 的“旁观者”理论下具有相关性；该理论适用于原告因目睹近亲遭受身体伤害而遭受精神痛苦的情形。\n\n但由于父亲并未目睹患者自杀，该理论不适用。\n\n（选项 D）精神科医生能够预见到其误诊会导致患者自杀，这表明她违反了对患者（而非父亲）的义务。\n\n尽管父亲由此产生的精神痛苦可能是可预见的，但精神科医生对父亲的义务仅限于防范可预见的身体伤害风险。\n\n教育目标：心理健康专业人员有义务尽到合理注意，识别并降低患者对他人的任何可预见的身体伤害风险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 41 条及评注\n\ng (美国法律协会 2012)（心理健康专业人员控制患者的义务）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第47条注释i（美国法律协会 2012）（源于特殊关系的义务仅限于可预见的人身伤害）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"心理健康专业人员有义务尽到合理的注意，以识别并减轻患者可能对他人的身体伤害构成的任何可预见风险。","iracZh":{"issue":"这位父亲胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"心理健康专业人员有义务尽到合理的注意，以识别并减轻患者可能对他人的身体伤害构成的任何可预见风险。","analysis":"心理健康专业人员 控制患者行为的义务 必须尽到合理注意义务，以： 确定患者是否对他人的安全构成风险并降低该风险——例如：治疗患者、警告潜在受害者、通知执法部门、寻求将患者送医强制治疗。关于过失导致精神痛苦（NIED）的诉讼，原告必须证明被告因违反了对原告负有的义务而存在过失行为。\n\n心理健康专业人员对患者负有尽到合理注意的义务。\n\n此外，由于与患者存在特殊关系，心理健康专业人员对他人也负有尽到合理注意的义务，具体包括：识别那些对他人的身体安全构成可预见风险的患者，并采取措施降低该风险。\n\n在本案中，精神科医生可能因未能诊断出患者的自杀倾向而需向患者的遗产承担责任。\n\n然而，精神科医生无需向父亲承担责任，因为患者仅对自己构成身体伤害的威胁，并未威胁到其父亲的身体安全。\n\n因此，精神科医生并未违反其对父亲的义务，父亲也无法就其遭受的严重精神痛苦或由此引发的中风获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）NIED 诉讼中的“危险区域”理论要求证明原告处于遭受身体伤害的危险之中，而非必须遭受实际的身体接触伤害。\n\n因此，如果精神科医生因将父亲置于身体危险区域而违反了对父亲的义务，那么即使父亲未遭受身体接触伤害，也可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）父亲与患者的关系在 NIED 的“旁观者”理论下具有相关性；该理论适用于原告因目睹近亲遭受身体伤害而遭受精神痛苦的情形。\n\n但由于父亲并未目睹患者自杀，该理论不适用。\n\n（选项 D）精神科医生能够预见到其误诊会导致患者自杀，这表明她违反了对患者（而非父亲）的义务。\n\n尽管父亲由此产生的精神痛苦可能是可预见的，但精神科医生对父亲的义务仅限于防范可预见的身体伤害风险。\n\n教育目标：心理健康专业人员有义务尽到合理注意，识别并降低患者对他人的任何可预见的身体伤害风险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 41 条及评注\n\ng (美国法律协会 2012)（心理健康专业人员控制患者的义务）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第47条注释i（美国法律协会 2012）（源于特殊关系的义务仅限于可预见的人身伤害）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不大，因为精神科医生的职业义务并不涵盖患者父亲所遭受的损害。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":80,"sourceQuestionPage":241,"sourceQuestionPages":[241],"sourceExplanationPages":[242,243],"sourceAnswerPages":[242,243],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-081","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"Two coworkers agreed to carpool to work each day. The driver agreed to pick the passenger up each morning and return her home after work. In exchange, the passenger agreed to pay the driver a monthly fee. One morning, while on their way to work, the driver and the passenger drove down a city street. As they approached a busy intersection, the driver's cell phone made a beeping sound, indicating that she had received a text message. The driver glanced over at her cell phone, which was attached to her dashboard, to read the message. As a result, the driver failed to notice that she was driving through a red light. As the driver's vehicle entered the intersection, a car that had the right-of-way struck the vehicle on the passenger's side. The driver was not injured, but the passenger sustained a broken arm and bruised ribs. The passenger subsequently filed a negligence action against the driver. What standard of care should the court apply to determine if the driver breached her duty to the passenger?","choices":{"A":"Duty to refrain from gross or wanton and willful misconduct.","B":"Extraordinary care.","C":"Ordinary care.","D":"That of a reasonable person of similar age, intelligence, and experience."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nIn negligence actions, the standard most often applied to determine if the defendant breached a duty to the plaintiff is one of ordinary care. Ordinary care is the care that a reasonably prudent person would use under the circumstances. In most jurisdictions, automobile drivers owe a duty of ordinary care to their passengers (those who pay money for the ride) as well as their guests (those who ride for free). A few jurisdictions have enacted \"guest statutes,\" under which an automobile driver's only duty to guests is to refrain from gross or wanton and willful misconduct. However, there is no indication that this jurisdiction has enacted such a statute. And even if it had, guest statutes do not apply to passengers (Choice A). Therefore, the court should apply the ordinary-care standard in this passenger's negligence action against the driver. (Choice B) A duty of extraordinary care imposes liability on a defendant who is slightly negligent. This standard of care has been imposed on common carriers, innkeepers,* and bailees—but not automobile drivers. *Most modern courts hold innkeepers (ie, hotel operators) to an ordinary standard of care. (Choice D) The standard of care generally imposed upon a child, age five or older, is that of a reasonable child of similar age, intelligence, and experience. However, a child who engages in a high-risk activity usually undertaken by adults (eg, driving a car) is held to the same ordinary standard of care as an adult.\n\nEducational objective: In most jurisdictions, automobile drivers owe a duty of ordinary care to both their guests (who ride for free) and passengers (who pay money for the ride).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 7 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (duty).","educationalObjective":"In most jurisdictions, automobile drivers owe a duty of ordinary care to both their guests (who ride for free) and passengers (who pay money for the ride).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 7 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (duty).","rule":"In most jurisdictions, automobile drivers owe a duty of ordinary care to both their guests (who ride for free) and passengers (who pay money for the ride).","irac":{"issue":"What standard of care should the court apply to determine if the driver breached her duty to the passenger?","rule":"In most jurisdictions, automobile drivers owe a duty of ordinary care to both their guests (who ride for free) and passengers (who pay money for the ride).","analysis":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nIn negligence actions, the standard most often applied to determine if the defendant breached a duty to the plaintiff is one of ordinary care. Ordinary care is the care that a reasonably prudent person would use under the circumstances. In most jurisdictions, automobile drivers owe a duty of ordinary care to their passengers (those who pay money for the ride) as well as their guests (those who ride for free). A few jurisdictions have enacted \"guest statutes,\" under which an automobile driver's only duty to guests is to refrain from gross or wanton and willful misconduct. However, there is no indication that this jurisdiction has enacted such a statute. And even if it had, guest statutes do not apply to passengers (Choice A). Therefore, the court should apply the ordinary-care standard in this passenger's negligence action against the driver. (Choice B) A duty of extraordinary care imposes liability on a defendant who is slightly negligent. This standard of care has been imposed on common carriers, innkeepers,* and bailees—but not automobile drivers. *Most modern courts hold innkeepers (ie, hotel operators) to an ordinary standard of care. (Choice D) The standard of care generally imposed upon a child, age five or older, is that of a reasonable child of similar age, intelligence, and experience. However, a child who engages in a high-risk activity usually undertaken by adults (eg, driving a car) is held to the same ordinary standard of care as an adult.","conclusion":"Answer C: Ordinary care."},"questionZh":"两名同事约定每天拼车上班。\n\n驾驶员同意每天早上接载乘客，并在下班后送其回家；作为交换，乘客同意每月向驾驶员支付费用。\n\n一天早上，两人在上班途中驾车行驶在城市街道上。\n\n当车辆接近一个繁忙的十字路口时，驾驶员的手机发出提示音，表明收到了一条短信。\n\n驾驶员瞥了一眼固定在仪表盘上的手机以查看信息，结果未能注意到自己正闯红灯驶入路口。\n\n当驾驶员的车辆进入路口时，一辆拥有优先通行权的汽车撞上了该车的乘客一侧。\n\n驾驶员未受伤，但乘客手臂骨折并伴有肋骨挫伤。\n\n随后，乘客针对驾驶员提起了过失诉讼。\n\n法院应适用何种注意标准来判定驾驶员是否违反了对乘客应尽的义务？","choicesZh":{"A":"避免重大过失或肆意妄为及故意不当行为的义务。","B":"极度谨慎（特殊注意）标准。","C":"普通注意标准。","D":"具有相似年龄、智力和经验的理性人的注意标准。"},"explanationZh":"过失侵权中的注意标准 成年人：必须像处于类似情况下的普通、理性的成年人那样行事。 标准会针对身体残疾、卓越技能与知识以及非自愿醉酒状态进行调整；但不会针对精神与情感障碍或自愿醉酒状态进行调整。专业人士：必须展现出与同类社区中其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和注意程度。 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。儿童：必须像具有相同年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 从事危险成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准；五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意标准。\n\n在过失侵权诉讼中，判断被告是否违反了对原告应尽义务时，最常适用的标准是“普通注意标准”（ordinary care）。\n\n普通注意是指一个理性的谨慎之人在特定情况下会采取的注意程度。\n\n在大多数司法管辖区，汽车驾驶员对乘客（付费乘车者）和搭乘者（免费乘车者，即“guest”）均负有普通注意义务。\n\n少数司法管辖区制定了“搭乘者法规”（guest statutes），根据该法规，驾驶员对搭乘者仅负有避免重大过失或肆意妄为（故意或鲁莽）不当行为的义务。\n\n然而，没有迹象表明本司法管辖区制定了此类法规。\n\n即使制定了此类法规，它们也不适用于付费乘客（选项 A）。\n\n因此，法院应针对该乘客针对驾驶员提起的过失诉讼适用普通注意标准。\n\n（选项 B）“极高注意标准”（extraordinary care）意味着被告即使只有轻微过失也要承担责任。\n\n这种注意标准适用于公共承运人、旅馆经营者*和保管人（bailees），但不适用于汽车驾驶员。\n\n*大多数现代法院对旅馆经营者（即酒店经营者）适用普通注意标准。\n\n（选项 D）对于五岁或以上的儿童，通常适用的注意标准是具有相似年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童的标准。\n\n然而，如果儿童从事通常由成年人进行的、具有高风险的活动（例如驾驶汽车），则适用与成年人相同的普通注意标准。\n\n教育目标：在大多数司法管辖区，汽车驾驶员对搭乘者（免费乘车者）和乘客（付费乘车者）均负有普通注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》\n\n针对身体与情感伤害 § 7 (美国法律学会 2010)（注意义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在大多数司法管辖区，汽车驾驶员对搭乘者（免费搭乘）和乘客（付费搭乘）均负有普通注意义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应适用何种注意标准来判定驾驶员是否违反了对乘客应尽的义务？","rule":"在大多数司法管辖区，汽车驾驶员对搭乘者（免费搭乘）和乘客（付费搭乘）均负有普通注意义务。","analysis":"过失侵权中的注意标准 成年人：必须像处于类似情况下的普通、理性的成年人那样行事。 标准会针对身体残疾、卓越技能与知识以及非自愿醉酒状态进行调整；但不会针对精神与情感障碍或自愿醉酒状态进行调整。专业人士：必须展现出与同类社区中其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和注意程度。 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。儿童：必须像具有相同年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 从事危险成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准；五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意标准。\n\n在过失侵权诉讼中，判断被告是否违反了对原告应尽义务时，最常适用的标准是“普通注意标准”（ordinary care）。\n\n普通注意是指一个理性的谨慎之人在特定情况下会采取的注意程度。\n\n在大多数司法管辖区，汽车驾驶员对乘客（付费乘车者）和搭乘者（免费乘车者，即“guest”）均负有普通注意义务。\n\n少数司法管辖区制定了“搭乘者法规”（guest statutes），根据该法规，驾驶员对搭乘者仅负有避免重大过失或肆意妄为（故意或鲁莽）不当行为的义务。\n\n然而，没有迹象表明本司法管辖区制定了此类法规。\n\n即使制定了此类法规，它们也不适用于付费乘客（选项 A）。\n\n因此，法院应针对该乘客针对驾驶员提起的过失诉讼适用普通注意标准。\n\n（选项 B）“极高注意标准”（extraordinary care）意味着被告即使只有轻微过失也要承担责任。\n\n这种注意标准适用于公共承运人、旅馆经营者*和保管人（bailees），但不适用于汽车驾驶员。\n\n*大多数现代法院对旅馆经营者（即酒店经营者）适用普通注意标准。\n\n（选项 D）对于五岁或以上的儿童，通常适用的注意标准是具有相似年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童的标准。\n\n然而，如果儿童从事通常由成年人进行的、具有高风险的活动（例如驾驶汽车），则适用与成年人相同的普通注意标准。\n\n教育目标：在大多数司法管辖区，汽车驾驶员对搭乘者（免费乘车者）和乘客（付费乘车者）均负有普通注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》\n\n针对身体与情感伤害 § 7 (美国法律学会 2010)（注意义务）。","conclusion":"答案 C：普通注意标准。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":81,"sourceQuestionPage":244,"sourceQuestionPages":[244],"sourceExplanationPages":[245,246],"sourceAnswerPages":[245,246],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-082","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A farmer owns a large farm on which he allows his friends to hunt during quail-hunting season. He does not provide his friends with any instructions about gun safety. The neighbor who owns property adjacent to the farm knows of the friends' use of the property during the hunting season. One day during the hunting season, without the farmer's knowledge or permission, the neighbor took a shortcut across the farm to visit an acquaintance. The neighbor was wounded by a shot fired by one of the farmer's friends, who was shooting at quail and carelessly failed to see the neighbor. Traditional rules of landowners' and occupiers' liability apply. In an action by the neighbor against the farmer to recover for the injuries, will the neighbor be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the farmer is not responsible for his friends' conduct.","B":"No, because the neighbor was trespassing.","C":"Yes, because the careless friend was permitted to hunt without safety training.","D":"Yes, because the use of firearms is an abnormally dangerous activity."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Land possessor's duty to land entrants\n\n(traditional approach)*\n\nStatus Characteristics Duty\n\nTrespasser Intentionally enters land Known or frequent trespassers – warn of without permission known artificial dangers & use reasonable care in active operations Unknown or unanticipated trespassers – no duty Licensee Enters land with permission Warn of known latent dangers & use (eg, social guest) reasonable care in active operations Invitee Enters land open to public Inspect for unknown dangers (eg, churchgoer) Make premises safe or provide adequate Enters land for business warnings purpose (eg, store customer) Prevent harm from active operations *In jurisdictions that follow the modern approach, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants, except flagrant trespassers.\n\nUnder the traditional approach, persons who occupy and control land (ie, land possessors) owe foreseeable land entrants a duty to exercise reasonable care. But a land possessor owes no duty to trespassers—ie, those who intentionally enter another's land without permission—unless the land possessor knows of, or can reasonably anticipate, their presence. The land possessor is then liable for negligence if he/she breaches this duty and causes the trespasser physical harm. Here, the neighbor was a trespasser since the neighbor intentionally entered the farmer's land without permission. And the farmer did not know that the neighbor was on the farmer's land. Nor is there any indication that the farmer had reason to anticipate the neighbor's presence (eg, from frequently witnessing the neighbor cut across the farm). As a result, the farmer did not owe his neighbor a duty of care, and the neighbor is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) Although the farmer is not responsible (ie, vicariously liable) for his friends' conduct, the farmer could have been liable for his own negligent conduct had he owed his neighbor a duty (not seen here). (Choice C) Had the neighbor been a foreseeable land entrant, the farmer would have owed the neighbor a duty to use reasonable care in active operations on the farm—eg, by\n\nensuring that his friends had gun-safety training. But since the neighbor was an unknown and unanticipated trespasser, the farmer owed the neighbor no duty. (Choice D) Using firearms in rural areas during hunting season is not an abnormally dangerous activity that subjects a defendant to strict liability—ie, liability imposed regardless of fault—since this is a common activity whose risks can be mitigated by reasonable care.\n\nEducational objective: A land possessor only owes a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants—not unforeseeable ones (eg, unknown and unanticipated trespassers).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 333 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (no duty to unknown trespassers).","educationalObjective":"A land possessor only owes a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants—not unforeseeable ones (eg, unknown and unanticipated trespassers).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 333 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (no duty to unknown trespassers).","rule":"A land possessor only owes a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants—not unforeseeable ones (eg, unknown and unanticipated trespassers).","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the neighbor against the farmer to recover for the injuries, will the neighbor be likely to prevail?","rule":"A land possessor only owes a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants—not unforeseeable ones (eg, unknown and unanticipated trespassers).","analysis":"Land possessor's duty to land entrants\n\n(traditional approach)*\n\nStatus Characteristics Duty\n\nTrespasser Intentionally enters land Known or frequent trespassers – warn of without permission known artificial dangers & use reasonable care in active operations Unknown or unanticipated trespassers – no duty Licensee Enters land with permission Warn of known latent dangers & use (eg, social guest) reasonable care in active operations Invitee Enters land open to public Inspect for unknown dangers (eg, churchgoer) Make premises safe or provide adequate Enters land for business warnings purpose (eg, store customer) Prevent harm from active operations *In jurisdictions that follow the modern approach, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants, except flagrant trespassers.\n\nUnder the traditional approach, persons who occupy and control land (ie, land possessors) owe foreseeable land entrants a duty to exercise reasonable care. But a land possessor owes no duty to trespassers—ie, those who intentionally enter another's land without permission—unless the land possessor knows of, or can reasonably anticipate, their presence. The land possessor is then liable for negligence if he/she breaches this duty and causes the trespasser physical harm. Here, the neighbor was a trespasser since the neighbor intentionally entered the farmer's land without permission. And the farmer did not know that the neighbor was on the farmer's land. Nor is there any indication that the farmer had reason to anticipate the neighbor's presence (eg, from frequently witnessing the neighbor cut across the farm). As a result, the farmer did not owe his neighbor a duty of care, and the neighbor is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) Although the farmer is not responsible (ie, vicariously liable) for his friends' conduct, the farmer could have been liable for his own negligent conduct had he owed his neighbor a duty (not seen here). (Choice C) Had the neighbor been a foreseeable land entrant, the farmer would have owed the neighbor a duty to use reasonable care in active operations on the farm—eg, by\n\nensuring that his friends had gun-safety training. But since the neighbor was an unknown and unanticipated trespasser, the farmer owed the neighbor no duty. (Choice D) Using firearms in rural areas during hunting season is not an abnormally dangerous activity that subjects a defendant to strict liability—ie, liability imposed regardless of fault—since this is a common activity whose risks can be mitigated by reasonable care.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the neighbor was trespassing."},"questionZh":"一位农民拥有一处大型农场，并允许朋友们在鹌鹑狩猎季期间在此狩猎。\n\n他并未向朋友们提供任何关于枪支安全的指导。\n\n农场隔壁的邻居知道这些朋友在狩猎季期间使用该农场。\n\n狩猎季期间的某一天，邻居在未告知农民也未获得其许可的情况下，为了去拜访一位熟人，抄近路穿过了该农场。\n\n邻居被农民的一位朋友开枪击伤；该朋友当时正在射击鹌鹑，因疏忽大意而未发现邻居。\n\n本案适用关于土地所有者和占有者责任的传统规则。\n\n在邻居针对农民提起的损害赔偿诉讼中，邻居胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为农民不对其朋友的行为负责。","B":"不会，因为邻居当时属于非法侵入。","C":"会，因为那位疏忽大意的朋友在未接受安全培训的情况下获准狩猎。","D":"会，因为使用枪支属于异常危险的活动。"},"explanationZh":"土地占有者对进入土地者的义务 （传统做法）* 身份 特征 义务 侵入者 未经许可故意进入土地 已知或频繁出现的侵入者——须警告已知的、隐蔽的人造危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务；未知或未预料到的侵入者——无义务 获准进入者 经许可进入土地（如社交访客） 须警告已知的隐蔽危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务 受邀进入者 进入向公众开放的土地（如去教堂的人）或为商业目的进入土地（如商店顾客） 检查是否存在未知危险；确保场所安全或提供充分警告；防止因活动造成伤害 *在遵循现代做法的司法管辖区，土地占有者对所有进入土地者（恶劣侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n根据传统做法，占有并控制土地的人（即土地占有者）对可预见的进入土地者负有尽到合理注意义务的责任。\n\n但土地占有者对侵入者（即未经许可故意进入他人土地的人）不负有义务，除非土地占有者知道或有理由预料到他们的存在。\n\n如果土地占有者违反了这一义务并导致侵入者身体受伤，则需承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，邻居属于侵入者，因为他未经许可故意进入了农场主的土地。\n\n农场主并不知道邻居在自己的土地上，也没有迹象表明农场主有理由预料到邻居的存在（例如，经常看到邻居穿过农场）。\n\n因此，农场主对邻居不负有注意义务，邻居胜诉的可能性不大。\n\n（选项 A）虽然农场主不对其朋友的行为负责（即不承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）），但如果他对邻居负有义务，他本可能因自己的过失行为而承担责任（本案中不存在这种情况）。\n\n（选项 C）如果邻居属于可预见的进入土地者，农场主就有义务在农场进行活动时尽到合理注意义务——例如， 确保其朋友接受过枪支安全培训。\n\n但由于邻居属于未知且未被预料到的侵入者，农场主对邻居不负有义务。\n\n(选项 D) 在狩猎季节于农村地区使用枪支，并不属于那种会使被告承担“严格责任”（即无论有无过错均须承担的责任）的“异常危险活动”；因为这是一种常见活动，且其风险可以通过采取合理的谨慎措施加以降低。\n\n教育目标：土地占有者仅对可预见的进入者负有合理谨慎义务，而不对不可预见的进入者（例如未知且未被预料到的侵入者）负有此义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 333 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（针对未知侵入者不负有义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地占有者仅对可预见的进入土地者负有合理注意义务，而不对不可预见的进入者（例如，未知的、未被预料到的非法侵入者）负有此义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"在邻居针对农民提起的损害赔偿诉讼中，邻居胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"土地占有者仅对可预见的进入土地者负有合理注意义务，而不对不可预见的进入者（例如，未知的、未被预料到的非法侵入者）负有此义务。","analysis":"土地占有者对进入土地者的义务 （传统做法）* 身份 特征 义务 侵入者 未经许可故意进入土地 已知或频繁出现的侵入者——须警告已知的、隐蔽的人造危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务；未知或未预料到的侵入者——无义务 获准进入者 经许可进入土地（如社交访客） 须警告已知的隐蔽危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务 受邀进入者 进入向公众开放的土地（如去教堂的人）或为商业目的进入土地（如商店顾客） 检查是否存在未知危险；确保场所安全或提供充分警告；防止因活动造成伤害 *在遵循现代做法的司法管辖区，土地占有者对所有进入土地者（恶劣侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n根据传统做法，占有并控制土地的人（即土地占有者）对可预见的进入土地者负有尽到合理注意义务的责任。\n\n但土地占有者对侵入者（即未经许可故意进入他人土地的人）不负有义务，除非土地占有者知道或有理由预料到他们的存在。\n\n如果土地占有者违反了这一义务并导致侵入者身体受伤，则需承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，邻居属于侵入者，因为他未经许可故意进入了农场主的土地。\n\n农场主并不知道邻居在自己的土地上，也没有迹象表明农场主有理由预料到邻居的存在（例如，经常看到邻居穿过农场）。\n\n因此，农场主对邻居不负有注意义务，邻居胜诉的可能性不大。\n\n（选项 A）虽然农场主不对其朋友的行为负责（即不承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）），但如果他对邻居负有义务，他本可能因自己的过失行为而承担责任（本案中不存在这种情况）。\n\n（选项 C）如果邻居属于可预见的进入土地者，农场主就有义务在农场进行活动时尽到合理注意义务——例如， 确保其朋友接受过枪支安全培训。\n\n但由于邻居属于未知且未被预料到的侵入者，农场主对邻居不负有义务。\n\n(选项 D) 在狩猎季节于农村地区使用枪支，并不属于那种会使被告承担“严格责任”（即无论有无过错均须承担的责任）的“异常危险活动”；因为这是一种常见活动，且其风险可以通过采取合理的谨慎措施加以降低。\n\n教育目标：土地占有者仅对可预见的进入者负有合理谨慎义务，而不对不可预见的进入者（例如未知且未被预料到的侵入者）负有此义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 333 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（针对未知侵入者不负有义务）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为邻居当时属于非法侵入。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":82,"sourceQuestionPage":247,"sourceQuestionPages":[247],"sourceExplanationPages":[248,249],"sourceAnswerPages":[248,249],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-083","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A man suffered from a serious, though not immediately life-threatening, impairment of his circulatory system. The man's cardiologist recommended a cardiac bypass operation and referred the man to a cardiothoracic surgeon. The surgeon did not inform the man of the 2% risk of death associated with this operation. The surgeon defended this decision based on his best personal judgment that \"the man was a worrier and it would have significantly lessened his chances of survival to be worried about the nonsurvival rate.\" After obtaining the patient's consent, the surgeon successfully performed the bypass operation and the man made a good recovery. However, when the man learned of the 2% risk of death associated with the operation, he was furious that the surgeon had failed to disclose this information to him. The man would have refused the operation had he been informed of the risk. If the man asserts a claim against the surgeon based on negligence, will the man prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the operation was successful, and the man suffered no harm.","B":"No, because the surgeon used his best personal judgment in shielding the man from the risk statistic.","C":"Yes, because a patient must be told the risk factor associated with a surgical procedure to give an informed consent.","D":"Yes, because the man would have refused the operation had he been informed of the risk."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Physicians have a duty to disclose the risks of a medical treatment or procedure to a patient so that the patient can give informed consent to the treatment or procedure. In most jurisdictions, the required level of disclosure of risks is governed by custom among physicians.* Failure to make the required disclosure subjects the physician to negligence liability** when: the failure to disclose caused the patient to consent to the treatment or procedure (ie, the patient would not have done so had the risk been disclosed) and\n\nthe undisclosed risk materialized and caused the plaintiff physical harm.\n\nHere, the surgeon failed to inform the man of the 2% risk of death associated with the operation before performing it. The man likely needed to be told of this risk to give informed consent, and he would have refused the operation had he been so informed. But since the surgery was successful and the man suffered no harm, he will not prevail in a negligence suit against the surgeon (Choices C & D). *A significant minority holds that the physician must disclose \"material risks.\" A material risk is one that a reasonable person in the patient's position would consider when deciding whether to undergo the treatment or procedure. **In contrast, failure to obtain the patient's consent to a treatment or procedure may subject the physician to battery liability. (Choice B) A physician has no duty to disclose a risk when the physician reasonably believes that disclosure would threaten a patient's well-being. However, that belief must be based on the physician's sound medical judgment—not the physician's best personal judgment (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Under the informed-consent doctrine, a physician who fails to disclose the risks of a medical treatment or procedure to a patient is liable for negligence if (1) the failure to disclose caused the patient to consent and (2) the undisclosed risk materialized and resulted in physical harm.\n\nReferences\nCanterbury v. Spence, 464 F.2d 772, 790 (D.C. Cir. 1972) (establishing that negligence based on informed-consent doctrine requires that the patient suffer physical harm).\n\n61 Am. Jur. 2d Physicians, Surgeons, and Other Healers § 167 (2020) (discussing the informed-consent doctrine).","educationalObjective":"Under the informed-consent doctrine, a physician who fails to disclose the risks of a medical treatment or procedure to a patient is liable for negligence if (1) the failure to disclose caused the patient to consent and (2) the undisclosed risk materialized and resulted in physical harm.","references":"Canterbury v. Spence, 464 F.2d 772, 790 (D.C. Cir. 1972) (establishing that negligence based on informed-consent doctrine requires that the patient suffer physical harm).\n\n61 Am. Jur. 2d Physicians, Surgeons, and Other Healers § 167 (2020) (discussing the informed-consent doctrine).","rule":"Under the informed-consent doctrine, a physician who fails to disclose the risks of a medical treatment or procedure to a patient is liable for negligence if (1) the failure to disclose caused the patient to consent and (2) the undisclosed risk materialized and resulted in physical harm.","irac":{"issue":"If the man asserts a claim against the surgeon based on negligence, will the man prevail?","rule":"Under the informed-consent doctrine, a physician who fails to disclose the risks of a medical treatment or procedure to a patient is liable for negligence if (1) the failure to disclose caused the patient to consent and (2) the undisclosed risk materialized and resulted in physical harm.","analysis":"Physicians have a duty to disclose the risks of a medical treatment or procedure to a patient so that the patient can give informed consent to the treatment or procedure. In most jurisdictions, the required level of disclosure of risks is governed by custom among physicians.* Failure to make the required disclosure subjects the physician to negligence liability** when: the failure to disclose caused the patient to consent to the treatment or procedure (ie, the patient would not have done so had the risk been disclosed) and\n\nthe undisclosed risk materialized and caused the plaintiff physical harm.\n\nHere, the surgeon failed to inform the man of the 2% risk of death associated with the operation before performing it. The man likely needed to be told of this risk to give informed consent, and he would have refused the operation had he been so informed. But since the surgery was successful and the man suffered no harm, he will not prevail in a negligence suit against the surgeon (Choices C & D). *A significant minority holds that the physician must disclose \"material risks.\" A material risk is one that a reasonable person in the patient's position would consider when deciding whether to undergo the treatment or procedure. **In contrast, failure to obtain the patient's consent to a treatment or procedure may subject the physician to battery liability. (Choice B) A physician has no duty to disclose a risk when the physician reasonably believes that disclosure would threaten a patient's well-being. However, that belief must be based on the physician's sound medical judgment—not the physician's best personal judgment (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the operation was successful, and the man suffered no harm."},"questionZh":"一名男子患有严重的循环系统疾病，但并非立即危及生命。\n\n他的心脏病专家建议进行心脏搭桥手术，并将他转诊至心胸外科医生处。\n\n这位外科医生并未告知该男子手术存在2%的死亡风险。\n\n外科医生辩称，他基于个人最佳判断认为“该男子是个爱操心的人，如果他担心死亡风险，反而会大大降低他的生存几率”。\n\n在获得患者同意后，外科医生成功实施了搭桥手术，该男子术后恢复良好。\n\n然而，当该男子得知手术存在2%的死亡风险时，他非常愤怒，认为外科医生没有事先告知他这一信息。\n\n如果事先告知风险，他会拒绝手术。\n\n如果该男子以医疗过失为由起诉外科医生，他能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为手术成功，该男子并未受到任何伤害。","B":"不，因为外科医生已尽其所能，运用个人判断力，使该男子免受风险统计数据的影响。","C":"是，因为患者必须被告知手术相关的风险因素才能做出知情同意。","D":"是，因为如果该男子被告知风险，他就会拒绝手术。"},"explanationZh":"医生有义务向患者披露医疗或手术的风险，以便患者能就该医疗或手术作出知情同意。\n\n在大多数司法管辖区，风险披露的必要程度取决于医生群体的惯例。* 如果医生未按要求进行披露，且出现以下情况，则需承担过失责任**：未披露风险的行为导致患者同意接受该医疗或手术（即：若风险已披露，患者本不会同意）；以及 未披露的风险实际发生，并对原告造成了身体伤害。\n\n在本案中，外科医生在实施手术前未告知该男子手术存在 2% 的死亡风险。\n\n该男子在作出知情同意前理应获知此风险，且若获知该风险，他本会拒绝手术。\n\n然而，由于手术成功且该男子未受伤害，他在针对该医生的过失诉讼中将无法胜诉（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n* 相当一部分少数派观点认为，医生必须披露“重大风险”（material risks）。\n\n所谓重大风险，是指处于患者处境的理性人在决定是否接受该医疗或手术时会予以考量的风险。\n\n** 相比之下，若未获得患者对医疗或手术的同意，医生可能需承担侵犯人身权（battery）的责任。\n\n（选项 B）当医生有合理理由认为披露风险会威胁患者的身心健康时，则无义务披露该风险。\n\n然而，这种判断必须基于医生合理的医学判断，而非医生个人的主观判断（如本案所示）。\n\n教育目标：根据知情同意原则，若医生未向患者披露医疗或手术的风险，且 (1) 未披露风险的行为导致患者同意接受治疗/手术，以及 (2) 未披露的风险实际发生并造成了身体伤害，则该医生需承担过失责任。\n\n参考文献 Canterbury v. Spence, 464 F.2d 772, 790 (D.C. Cir. 1972)（确立了基于知情同意原则的过失责任认定要求患者遭受身体伤害）。\n\n61 Am. Jur. 2d Physicians, Surgeons, and Other Healers § 167 (2020)（探讨知情同意原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据知情同意原则，如果医生未能向患者披露医疗或手术的风险，且 (1) 未披露风险导致患者同意接受手术，并且 (2) 未披露的风险发生并造成身体伤害，则医生应承担过失责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该男子以过失为由对外科医生提起诉讼，他能否胜诉？","rule":"根据知情同意原则，如果医生未能向患者披露医疗或手术的风险，且 (1) 未披露风险导致患者同意接受手术，并且 (2) 未披露的风险发生并造成身体伤害，则医生应承担过失责任。","analysis":"医生有义务向患者披露医疗或手术的风险，以便患者能就该医疗或手术作出知情同意。\n\n在大多数司法管辖区，风险披露的必要程度取决于医生群体的惯例。* 如果医生未按要求进行披露，且出现以下情况，则需承担过失责任**：未披露风险的行为导致患者同意接受该医疗或手术（即：若风险已披露，患者本不会同意）；以及 未披露的风险实际发生，并对原告造成了身体伤害。\n\n在本案中，外科医生在实施手术前未告知该男子手术存在 2% 的死亡风险。\n\n该男子在作出知情同意前理应获知此风险，且若获知该风险，他本会拒绝手术。\n\n然而，由于手术成功且该男子未受伤害，他在针对该医生的过失诉讼中将无法胜诉（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n* 相当一部分少数派观点认为，医生必须披露“重大风险”（material risks）。\n\n所谓重大风险，是指处于患者处境的理性人在决定是否接受该医疗或手术时会予以考量的风险。\n\n** 相比之下，若未获得患者对医疗或手术的同意，医生可能需承担侵犯人身权（battery）的责任。\n\n（选项 B）当医生有合理理由认为披露风险会威胁患者的身心健康时，则无义务披露该风险。\n\n然而，这种判断必须基于医生合理的医学判断，而非医生个人的主观判断（如本案所示）。\n\n教育目标：根据知情同意原则，若医生未向患者披露医疗或手术的风险，且 (1) 未披露风险的行为导致患者同意接受治疗/手术，以及 (2) 未披露的风险实际发生并造成了身体伤害，则该医生需承担过失责任。\n\n参考文献 Canterbury v. Spence, 464 F.2d 772, 790 (D.C. Cir. 1972)（确立了基于知情同意原则的过失责任认定要求患者遭受身体伤害）。\n\n61 Am. Jur. 2d Physicians, Surgeons, and Other Healers § 167 (2020)（探讨知情同意原则）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不能，因为手术成功，该男子并未受到任何伤害。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-38f9f1c2a9e9d2d8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":83,"sourceQuestionPage":250,"sourceQuestionPages":[250],"sourceExplanationPages":[251,252],"sourceAnswerPages":[251,252],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-084","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A woman signed up for a bowling class. Before allowing the woman to bowl, the instructor required her to sign a waiver explicitly stating that she assumed all risk of injuries that she might suffer in connection with the class, including injuries due to negligence or any other fault. After she signed the waiver, the woman was injured when the instructor negligently dropped a bowling ball on the woman's foot. The woman brought a negligence action against the instructor. The instructor has filed a motion for summary judgment based on the waiver. What is the woman's best argument in opposition to the instructor's motion?","choices":{"A":"Bowling is an inherently dangerous activity.","B":"In circumstances like these, it is against public policy to enforce agreements that insulate people from the consequences of their own negligence.","C":"It was unreasonable to require the woman to sign the waiver before she was allowed to bowl.","D":"When she signed the form, the woman could not foresee that the instructor would drop a bowling ball on her foot."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Assumption of risk\n\n(tort defense)\n\nApplicability Public policy limitations\n\nExpress Plaintiff assented to liability waiver intended to Not a defense when cover type of conduct that caused plaintiff's harm defendant:\n\nis plaintiff's employer is a hotel or common carrier is a public servant/service has substantially more bargaining power Implied Plaintiff voluntarily accepted known risk of harm Not a defense when plaintiff:\n\nsuffered intentional harm is member of statutorily protected class A person can assume the risk of another's tortious conduct by expressly waiving the right to sue. Such waivers are generally valid if the plaintiff assented (eg, by signing the waiver) AND the waiver was intended to cover the type of conduct that caused the plaintiff's harm. But liability waivers are invalid and cannot justify summary judgment if they are against public policy—ie, when the defendant (1) is the plaintiff's employer, (2) is a hotel or common carrier, (3) is a public servant or service, or (4) has substantially more bargaining power. Here, the woman signed a waiver assuming all risk of injuries that she might suffer in connection with a bowling class. Since this waiver included injuries due to negligence or any other fault, it was intended to cover the instructor's negligence in dropping a ball on the woman's foot. But the woman can still oppose the instructor's motion for summary judgment by arguing that it is against public policy to enforce the waiver in circumstances like these—eg, due to a substantial imbalance in bargaining power. (Choice A) Bowling is not inherently dangerous since it rarely results in serious injuries. And even if bowling were inherently dangerous, this would be irrelevant to whether the waiver violates public policy.\n\n(Choice C) In determining the validity of liability waivers, courts will consider whether the defendant had significantly more bargaining power than the plaintiff—but not the reasonableness of requiring the plaintiff to sign the waiver. (Choice D) The woman assumed all risk of injuries, including unforeseeable risks. Therefore, the validity of the waiver does not depend on whether the woman could foresee that the instructor would drop a ball on her foot.\n\nEducational objective: A person can expressly waive the right to sue over another person's tortious conduct. Such waivers are generally valid unless they violate public policy—ie, when the defendant (1) is the plaintiff's employer, (2) is a hotel or common carrier, (3) is a public servant or service, or (4) has substantially more bargaining power.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 496B cmts. c–j (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a person can expressly assume the risk of another's negligence unless the waiver is against public policy).","educationalObjective":"A person can expressly waive the right to sue over another person's tortious conduct. Such waivers are generally valid unless they violate public policy—ie, when the defendant (1) is the plaintiff's employer, (2) is a hotel or common carrier, (3) is a public servant or service, or (4) has substantially more bargaining power.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 496B cmts. c–j (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a person can expressly assume the risk of another's negligence unless the waiver is against public policy).","rule":"A person can expressly waive the right to sue over another person's tortious conduct. Such waivers are generally valid unless they violate public policy—ie, when the defendant (1) is the plaintiff's employer, (2) is a hotel or common carrier, (3) is a public servant or service, or (4) has substantially more bargaining power.","irac":{"issue":"What is the woman's best argument in opposition to the instructor's motion?","rule":"A person can expressly waive the right to sue over another person's tortious conduct. Such waivers are generally valid unless they violate public policy—ie, when the defendant (1) is the plaintiff's employer, (2) is a hotel or common carrier, (3) is a public servant or service, or (4) has substantially more bargaining power.","analysis":"Assumption of risk\n\n(tort defense)\n\nApplicability Public policy limitations\n\nExpress Plaintiff assented to liability waiver intended to Not a defense when cover type of conduct that caused plaintiff's harm defendant:\n\nis plaintiff's employer is a hotel or common carrier is a public servant/service has substantially more bargaining power Implied Plaintiff voluntarily accepted known risk of harm Not a defense when plaintiff:\n\nsuffered intentional harm is member of statutorily protected class A person can assume the risk of another's tortious conduct by expressly waiving the right to sue. Such waivers are generally valid if the plaintiff assented (eg, by signing the waiver) AND the waiver was intended to cover the type of conduct that caused the plaintiff's harm. But liability waivers are invalid and cannot justify summary judgment if they are against public policy—ie, when the defendant (1) is the plaintiff's employer, (2) is a hotel or common carrier, (3) is a public servant or service, or (4) has substantially more bargaining power. Here, the woman signed a waiver assuming all risk of injuries that she might suffer in connection with a bowling class. Since this waiver included injuries due to negligence or any other fault, it was intended to cover the instructor's negligence in dropping a ball on the woman's foot. But the woman can still oppose the instructor's motion for summary judgment by arguing that it is against public policy to enforce the waiver in circumstances like these—eg, due to a substantial imbalance in bargaining power. (Choice A) Bowling is not inherently dangerous since it rarely results in serious injuries. And even if bowling were inherently dangerous, this would be irrelevant to whether the waiver violates public policy.\n\n(Choice C) In determining the validity of liability waivers, courts will consider whether the defendant had significantly more bargaining power than the plaintiff—but not the reasonableness of requiring the plaintiff to sign the waiver. (Choice D) The woman assumed all risk of injuries, including unforeseeable risks. Therefore, the validity of the waiver does not depend on whether the woman could foresee that the instructor would drop a ball on her foot.","conclusion":"Answer B: In circumstances like these, it is against public policy to enforce agreements that insulate people from the consequences of their own negligence."},"questionZh":"一名女子报名参加了保龄球课程。\n\n在允许该女子开始打球之前，教练要求她签署一份弃权声明，明确规定她承担因参加该课程而可能遭受的所有伤害风险，包括因疏忽或任何其他过错造成的伤害。\n\n签署该声明后，由于教练疏忽大意，将保龄球掉落在该女子的脚上，导致其受伤。\n\n该女子针对教练提起了疏忽诉讼。\n\n教练依据该弃权声明提出了即决判决动议。\n\n该女子反对教练动议的最有力论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"保龄球是一项具有固有危险性的活动。","B":"在此类情况下，强制执行那些使人免于承担自身疏忽后果的协议，违反了公共政策。","C":"在允许该女子打球之前要求她签署弃权声明是不合理的。","D":"在签署该表格时，该女子无法预见到教练会将保龄球掉落在她的脚上。"},"explanationZh":"风险自担 （侵权法抗辩） 适用性及公共政策限制 明示：原告同意放弃追究责任的权利，且该放弃涵盖了导致其受损的行为类型。若被告属于以下情形，则该抗辩无效： 被告是原告的雇主；被告是酒店或公共承运人；被告是公职人员/公共服务机构；被告拥有显著更强的议价能力。默示：原告自愿承担已知的伤害风险。若原告属于以下情形，则该抗辩无效： 遭受了故意伤害；属于受法律特别保护的群体。个人可以通过明示放弃诉讼权的方式，承担他人侵权行为带来的风险。\n\n此类放弃权利的协议通常有效，前提是原告表示同意（例如通过签署弃权书），且该协议涵盖了导致原告受损的行为类型。\n\n然而，如果此类责任豁免协议违反公共政策，则其无效，也不能作为支持简易判决（summary judgment）的依据——即当被告属于以下情形时：（1）是原告的雇主，（2）是酒店或公共承运人，（3）是公职人员或公共服务机构，或（4）拥有显著更强的议价能力。\n\n在本案中，该女子签署了一份弃权书，承担了因参加保龄球课程可能遭受的所有伤害风险。\n\n由于该弃权书涵盖了因疏忽或任何其他过错造成的伤害，其范围包括了教练因失手将球掉落在该女子脚上而造成的疏忽行为。\n\n尽管如此，该女子仍可反对教练提出的简易判决动议，理由是在此类情况下执行该弃权书违反公共政策——例如，由于双方议价能力存在显著失衡。\n\n（选项 A）保龄球运动并非本质上具有危险性，因为它很少导致严重伤害。\n\n即便保龄球本质上具有危险性，这也与该弃权书是否违反公共政策无关。\n\n（选项 C）在判定责任豁免协议的有效性时，法院会考量被告是否拥有显著强于原告的议价能力，而不会考量要求原告签署该协议是否合理。\n\n（选项 D）该女子承担了所有伤害风险，包括不可预见的风险。\n\n因此，弃权书的有效性并不取决于该女子是否能预见到教练会将球掉落在她的脚上。\n\n教育目标：个人可以明示放弃就他人侵权行为提起诉讼的权利。\n\n此类弃权声明通常有效，除非其违反公共政策——即当被告属于以下情形时：(1) 是原告的雇主；(2) 是酒店或公共承运人；(3) 是公务员或公共服务提供者；或 (4) 拥有显著更强的议价能力。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 496B 条及注释 c–j（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明个人可明示承担因他人过失而产生的风险，除非该弃权声明违反公共政策）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一个人可以明确放弃因他人的侵权行为而提起诉讼的权利。\n\n此类弃权声明通常有效，除非它们违反了公共政策——即被告属于以下情形之一：(1) 是原告的雇主；(2) 是酒店或公共承运人；(3) 是公务员或公共服务机构；或 (4) 拥有显著更强的议价能力。","iracZh":{"issue":"该女子反对教练动议的最有力论点是什么？","rule":"一个人可以明确放弃因他人的侵权行为而提起诉讼的权利。\n\n此类弃权声明通常有效，除非它们违反了公共政策——即被告属于以下情形之一：(1) 是原告的雇主；(2) 是酒店或公共承运人；(3) 是公务员或公共服务机构；或 (4) 拥有显著更强的议价能力。","analysis":"风险自担 （侵权法抗辩） 适用性及公共政策限制 明示：原告同意放弃追究责任的权利，且该放弃涵盖了导致其受损的行为类型。若被告属于以下情形，则该抗辩无效： 被告是原告的雇主；被告是酒店或公共承运人；被告是公职人员/公共服务机构；被告拥有显著更强的议价能力。默示：原告自愿承担已知的伤害风险。若原告属于以下情形，则该抗辩无效： 遭受了故意伤害；属于受法律特别保护的群体。个人可以通过明示放弃诉讼权的方式，承担他人侵权行为带来的风险。\n\n此类放弃权利的协议通常有效，前提是原告表示同意（例如通过签署弃权书），且该协议涵盖了导致原告受损的行为类型。\n\n然而，如果此类责任豁免协议违反公共政策，则其无效，也不能作为支持简易判决（summary judgment）的依据——即当被告属于以下情形时：（1）是原告的雇主，（2）是酒店或公共承运人，（3）是公职人员或公共服务机构，或（4）拥有显著更强的议价能力。\n\n在本案中，该女子签署了一份弃权书，承担了因参加保龄球课程可能遭受的所有伤害风险。\n\n由于该弃权书涵盖了因疏忽或任何其他过错造成的伤害，其范围包括了教练因失手将球掉落在该女子脚上而造成的疏忽行为。\n\n尽管如此，该女子仍可反对教练提出的简易判决动议，理由是在此类情况下执行该弃权书违反公共政策——例如，由于双方议价能力存在显著失衡。\n\n（选项 A）保龄球运动并非本质上具有危险性，因为它很少导致严重伤害。\n\n即便保龄球本质上具有危险性，这也与该弃权书是否违反公共政策无关。\n\n（选项 C）在判定责任豁免协议的有效性时，法院会考量被告是否拥有显著强于原告的议价能力，而不会考量要求原告签署该协议是否合理。\n\n（选项 D）该女子承担了所有伤害风险，包括不可预见的风险。\n\n因此，弃权书的有效性并不取决于该女子是否能预见到教练会将球掉落在她的脚上。\n\n教育目标：个人可以明示放弃就他人侵权行为提起诉讼的权利。\n\n此类弃权声明通常有效，除非其违反公共政策——即当被告属于以下情形时：(1) 是原告的雇主；(2) 是酒店或公共承运人；(3) 是公务员或公共服务提供者；或 (4) 拥有显著更强的议价能力。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 496B 条及注释 c–j（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明个人可明示承担因他人过失而产生的风险，除非该弃权声明违反公共政策）。","conclusion":"答案 B：在此类情况下，强制执行那些使人免于承担自身疏忽后果的协议，违反了公共政策。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":84,"sourceQuestionPage":253,"sourceQuestionPages":[253],"sourceExplanationPages":[254,255],"sourceAnswerPages":[254,255],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-085","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"While walking on a public sidewalk, a pedestrian was struck by a piece of lumber that fell from the roof of a homeowner's house. The homeowner had hired a roofer to make repairs to his roof, and the lumber fell through negligence on the roofer's part. If the pedestrian brings an action against the homeowner to recover damages for the injury caused to him by the roofer's negligence, will the pedestrian prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the roofer was an independent contractor rather than the homeowner's servant.","B":"No, if the homeowner exercised reasonable care in hiring the roofer to do the repair work.","C":"Yes, because the roofer's act was a breach of a nondelegable duty owed by the homeowner to the pedestrian.","D":"Yes, under the res ipsa loquitur doctrine."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Nondelegable duties\n\n(duties that cannot be assigned to another to avoid liability)\n\nMaintain safe conditions on premises open to public (eg, store, restaurant) Safely perform activities that: are abnormally or highly dangerous infringe on private property right (eg, nuisance, trespass) are regulated by law or are conducted in public place A principal is generally not vicariously liable for torts (eg, negligence) committed by his/her independent contractor. But vicarious liability can be imposed when the independent contractor's work involves a nondelegable duty—ie, a duty of care that cannot be assigned to a third party to avoid liability. This includes a land possessor's duty to safely conduct activities on the land that pose foreseeable risks of harm to others (eg, construction, repairs). Here, the homeowner (land possessor) hired the roofer (independent contractor) to repair the homeowner's roof. Since repairing a roof poses a foreseeable risk of harm to others, the homeowner had a nondelegable duty to ensure that the repairs were conducted safely. And since the roofer's act (negligently allowing lumber to fall from the roof) breached that duty, the homeowner is vicariously liable for the resulting harm (the pedestrian's injury). (Choice A) Employers are vicariously liable for torts committed by their employees (ie, servants) while acting within the scope of employment. But the fact that the roofer was an independent contractor—not an employee—does not defeat the pedestrian's claim since the roofer's work involved a nondelegable duty. (Choice B) The fact that the homeowner exercised reasonable care in hiring the roofer would show that the homeowner is not directly liable for negligently hiring an incompetent contractor. But the homeowner can still be vicariously liable for the roofer's negligence since the roofer's work involved a nondelegable duty. (Choice D) When there is no direct evidence of negligence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence if (1) the event that caused the plaintiff's harm would not ordinarily occur absent negligence by someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm. But this doctrine does not apply since the roofer's negligence is stated.\n\nEducational objective: A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability can be imposed when the principal owed a nondelegable duty of care—eg, a land possessor's duty to safely conduct activities on the land that pose a foreseeable risk of harm to others.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 57 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (general rule: no vicarious liability for independent contractors' torts).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm §§ 54, 62 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (exception: land possessor's nondelegable duty to safely conduct activities on the land).","educationalObjective":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability can be imposed when the principal owed a nondelegable duty of care—eg, a land possessor's duty to safely conduct activities on the land that pose a foreseeable risk of harm to others.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 57 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (general rule: no vicarious liability for independent contractors' torts).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm §§ 54, 62 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (exception: land possessor's nondelegable duty to safely conduct activities on the land).","rule":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability can be imposed when the principal owed a nondelegable duty of care—eg, a land possessor's duty to safely conduct activities on the land that pose a foreseeable risk of harm to others.","irac":{"issue":"If the pedestrian brings an action against the homeowner to recover damages for the injury caused to him by the roofer's negligence, will the pedestrian prevail?","rule":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability can be imposed when the principal owed a nondelegable duty of care—eg, a land possessor's duty to safely conduct activities on the land that pose a foreseeable risk of harm to others.","analysis":"Nondelegable duties\n\n(duties that cannot be assigned to another to avoid liability)\n\nMaintain safe conditions on premises open to public (eg, store, restaurant) Safely perform activities that: are abnormally or highly dangerous infringe on private property right (eg, nuisance, trespass) are regulated by law or are conducted in public place A principal is generally not vicariously liable for torts (eg, negligence) committed by his/her independent contractor. But vicarious liability can be imposed when the independent contractor's work involves a nondelegable duty—ie, a duty of care that cannot be assigned to a third party to avoid liability. This includes a land possessor's duty to safely conduct activities on the land that pose foreseeable risks of harm to others (eg, construction, repairs). Here, the homeowner (land possessor) hired the roofer (independent contractor) to repair the homeowner's roof. Since repairing a roof poses a foreseeable risk of harm to others, the homeowner had a nondelegable duty to ensure that the repairs were conducted safely. And since the roofer's act (negligently allowing lumber to fall from the roof) breached that duty, the homeowner is vicariously liable for the resulting harm (the pedestrian's injury). (Choice A) Employers are vicariously liable for torts committed by their employees (ie, servants) while acting within the scope of employment. But the fact that the roofer was an independent contractor—not an employee—does not defeat the pedestrian's claim since the roofer's work involved a nondelegable duty. (Choice B) The fact that the homeowner exercised reasonable care in hiring the roofer would show that the homeowner is not directly liable for negligently hiring an incompetent contractor. But the homeowner can still be vicariously liable for the roofer's negligence since the roofer's work involved a nondelegable duty. (Choice D) When there is no direct evidence of negligence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence if (1) the event that caused the plaintiff's harm would not ordinarily occur absent negligence by someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm. But this doctrine does not apply since the roofer's negligence is stated.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the roofer's act was a breach of a nondelegable duty owed by the homeowner to the pedestrian."},"questionZh":"一名行人在公共人行道上行走时，被一块从房主屋顶掉落的木料击中。\n\n房主曾聘请一名屋顶工进行维修，而木料的掉落是由于该屋顶工的疏忽造成的。\n\n如果该行人起诉房主，要求赔偿因屋顶工的疏忽而遭受的伤害，该行人能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为屋顶工是独立承包商，而非房主的雇员。","B":"不能，如果房主在聘请屋顶工进行维修工作时尽到了合理的注意义务。","C":"能，因为屋顶工的行为违反了房主对行人负有的不可转嫁义务。","D":"能，依据“事实本身说明问题”（res ipsa loquitur）原则。"},"explanationZh":"不可转嫁的义务 （指不能通过委托他人来规避责任的义务） 在向公众开放的场所（如商店、餐馆）维持安全状况；安全地进行以下活动：具有异常危险性或高度危险性的活动；侵犯私人财产权的活动（如构成妨害或非法侵入）；受法律监管的活动或在公共场所进行的活动。通常情况下，委托人不对其独立承包商实施的侵权行为（如过失行为）承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n但在独立承包商的工作涉及“不可转嫁的义务”——即不能通过委托第三方来免除责任的注意义务——时，委托人可能需承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n这包括土地占有人在土地上进行对他人构成可预见伤害风险的活动（如建筑、维修）时所负有的安全义务。\n\n在本案中，房主（土地占有人）聘请了屋顶工（独立承包商）来修理屋顶。\n\n由于屋顶维修活动对他人构成可预见的伤害风险，房主负有确保维修工作安全进行的不可转嫁义务。\n\n鉴于屋顶工的行为（因过失导致木材从屋顶坠落）违反了该义务，房主须对由此造成的损害（行人受伤）承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 A）雇主对其雇员（即受雇人）在受雇范围内实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n但屋顶工属于独立承包商而非雇员这一事实，并不妨碍行人提出索赔，因为屋顶工的工作涉及不可转嫁的义务。\n\n（选项 B）房主在聘请屋顶工时尽到了合理注意，这一事实表明房主不因过失聘请不称职的承包商而承担直接责任。\n\n但由于屋顶工的工作涉及不可转嫁的义务，房主仍可能需对屋顶工的过失行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 D）在缺乏过失直接证据的情况下，若满足以下条件，根据“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则”（res ipsa loquitur），可依据间接证据推断存在过失：(1) 导致原告受损的事件，若非处于被告位置的人员存在过失，通常不会发生；(2) 证据倾向于排除导致该损害的其他潜在原因。\n\n但鉴于本案已明确指出屋顶工存在过失，该原则不适用。\n\n教学目标：委托人通常不对其独立承包商实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，若委托人负有不可转让的注意义务（例如，土地占有人有义务确保在土地上进行活动时安全无虞，以免对他方造成可预见的伤害风险），则可能需承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第57条（美国法律协会，2012年）（一般规则：不对独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第54、62条（美国法律协会，2012年）（例外情况：土地占有人在土地上进行活动时负有安全保障方面的不可转让义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"委托人通常不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当委托人负有不可转嫁的注意义务时，可能会被追究替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）——例如，土地占有者有义务确保在土地上进行可能对他人构成可预见伤害风险的活动时保持安全。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该行人起诉房主，要求赔偿因屋顶工的疏忽而遭受的伤害，该行人能否胜诉？","rule":"委托人通常不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当委托人负有不可转嫁的注意义务时，可能会被追究替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）——例如，土地占有者有义务确保在土地上进行可能对他人构成可预见伤害风险的活动时保持安全。","analysis":"不可转嫁的义务 （指不能通过委托他人来规避责任的义务） 在向公众开放的场所（如商店、餐馆）维持安全状况；安全地进行以下活动：具有异常危险性或高度危险性的活动；侵犯私人财产权的活动（如构成妨害或非法侵入）；受法律监管的活动或在公共场所进行的活动。通常情况下，委托人不对其独立承包商实施的侵权行为（如过失行为）承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n但在独立承包商的工作涉及“不可转嫁的义务”——即不能通过委托第三方来免除责任的注意义务——时，委托人可能需承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n这包括土地占有人在土地上进行对他人构成可预见伤害风险的活动（如建筑、维修）时所负有的安全义务。\n\n在本案中，房主（土地占有人）聘请了屋顶工（独立承包商）来修理屋顶。\n\n由于屋顶维修活动对他人构成可预见的伤害风险，房主负有确保维修工作安全进行的不可转嫁义务。\n\n鉴于屋顶工的行为（因过失导致木材从屋顶坠落）违反了该义务，房主须对由此造成的损害（行人受伤）承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 A）雇主对其雇员（即受雇人）在受雇范围内实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n但屋顶工属于独立承包商而非雇员这一事实，并不妨碍行人提出索赔，因为屋顶工的工作涉及不可转嫁的义务。\n\n（选项 B）房主在聘请屋顶工时尽到了合理注意，这一事实表明房主不因过失聘请不称职的承包商而承担直接责任。\n\n但由于屋顶工的工作涉及不可转嫁的义务，房主仍可能需对屋顶工的过失行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 D）在缺乏过失直接证据的情况下，若满足以下条件，根据“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则”（res ipsa loquitur），可依据间接证据推断存在过失：(1) 导致原告受损的事件，若非处于被告位置的人员存在过失，通常不会发生；(2) 证据倾向于排除导致该损害的其他潜在原因。\n\n但鉴于本案已明确指出屋顶工存在过失，该原则不适用。\n\n教学目标：委托人通常不对其独立承包商实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，若委托人负有不可转让的注意义务（例如，土地占有人有义务确保在土地上进行活动时安全无虞，以免对他方造成可预见的伤害风险），则可能需承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第57条（美国法律协会，2012年）（一般规则：不对独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第54、62条（美国法律协会，2012年）（例外情况：土地占有人在土地上进行活动时负有安全保障方面的不可转让义务）。","conclusion":"答案 C：能，因为屋顶工的行为违反了房主对行人负有的不可转嫁义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"vicarious liability","ipa":"/vaɪkˈɛɹiəs lˌaɪʌbˈɪlɪti/","syllables":"vic·ar·i·ous li·a·bil·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"替代责任：一人因特定关系对另一人的侵权承担责任","contextualMeaning":"替代责任：一人因特定关系对另一人的侵权承担责任","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"However, vicarious liability can be imposed when the principal owed a nondelegable duty of care—eg, a land possessor's duty to safely conduct activities on the land that pose a foreseeable risk of harm to others.","synonyms":["imputed liability","derivative liability"]},{"term":"scope of employment","ipa":"/skˈoʊp ˈəv ɛmplˈɔɪmənt/","syllables":"scope of em·ploy·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"职务范围：行为与受雇工作有关并至少部分服务雇主目的","contextualMeaning":"职务范围：行为与受雇工作有关并至少部分服务雇主目的","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) Employers are vicariously liable for torts committed by their employees (ie, servants) while acting within the scope of employment.","synonyms":["course and scope of employment"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":85,"sourceQuestionPage":256,"sourceQuestionPages":[256],"sourceExplanationPages":[257,258],"sourceAnswerPages":[257,258],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-086","subject":"Torts","topic":"Damages & Joint Liability","question":"A four-year-old child sustained serious injuries when a playmate pushed him from between two parked cars into the street, where he was struck by a car. The child, by his representative, sued the driver of the car, the playmate's parents, and his own parents. At trial, the child's total damages were determined to be $100,000. The playmate's parents were determined to be 20% at fault because they had failed to adequately supervise her. The driver was found to be 50% at fault. The child's own parents were determined to be 30% at fault for failure to adequately supervise him. The court has adopted the pure comparative negligence doctrine, with joint and several liability, in place of the common law rules relating to plaintiff's fault. In addition, the common law doctrines relating to intra-family liability have been abrogated. What is the maximum amount, if anything, that the child's representative can recover from the driver?","choices":{"A":"$30,000.","B":"$50,000.","C":"$100,000.","D":"Nothing."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Liability of multiple tortfeasors for indivisible harm\n\nJoint & several Any tortfeasor can be held liable for plaintiff's total amount of damages liability* Tortfeasors can sue each other for contribution Several liability Each tortfeasor is liable for portion of damages corresponding to his/her proportionate fault (eg, 15% of damages for 15% fault) *Default rule on MBE.\n\nThis jurisdiction has adopted the following: Pure comparative negligence (default rule on MBE) – when the plaintiff's own negligence contributes to his/her harm, the plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault\n\nJoint and several liability – when multiple defendants cause the plaintiff indivisible harm, the plaintiff can recover the total amount of damages from any defendant (who can then sue the others for contribution)\n\nAdditionally, this jurisdiction has abandoned the common law rules of intra-family liability. As a result, the fault of a child's parents will not be imputed to the child or vice versa. Here, the court determined that multiple defendants were at fault for causing the child's injuries: the playmate's parents (20%), the driver (50%), and the child's parents (30%). The child was not at fault. And since his parents' fault will not be imputed to him, his recovery will not be reduced for comparative negligence. Therefore, the child can recover his total damages ($100,000) from any of the defendants—including the driver. (Choices A & B) If liability were only several (not seen here), each tortfeasor would only be liable for the portion of damages that corresponds to his/her proportionate fault. In that case, the child could only recover $30,000 from his parents (30% of total damages) and $50,000 from the driver (50% of total damages). (Choice D) If traditional common law rules regarding intra-family liability and contributory negligence applied, the fault of the child's parents would be imputed to the child and bar all recovery. But these rules have been abandoned or replaced in this jurisdiction.\n\nEducational objective: Under the pure comparative negligence doctrine, a negligent plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault. And under joint and several liability, any one of\n\nmultiple tortious defendants who caused the plaintiff indivisible harm can be held liable for the plaintiff's total amount of damages.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 488 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a parent's negligence is no longer imputed to his/her child and a child's negligence will not be imputed to his/her parent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 875 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (explaining that multiple tortfeasors who cause an indivisible harm can be held liable individually for the entire harm).","educationalObjective":"Under the pure comparative negligence doctrine, a negligent plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault. And under joint and several liability, any one of\n\nmultiple tortious defendants who caused the plaintiff indivisible harm can be held liable for the plaintiff's total amount of damages.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 488 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a parent's negligence is no longer imputed to his/her child and a child's negligence will not be imputed to his/her parent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 875 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (explaining that multiple tortfeasors who cause an indivisible harm can be held liable individually for the entire harm).","rule":"Under the pure comparative negligence doctrine, a negligent plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault. And under joint and several liability, any one of\n\nmultiple tortious defendants who caused the plaintiff indivisible harm can be held liable for the plaintiff's total amount of damages.","irac":{"issue":"What is the maximum amount, if anything, that the child's representative can recover from the driver?","rule":"Under the pure comparative negligence doctrine, a negligent plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault. And under joint and several liability, any one of\n\nmultiple tortious defendants who caused the plaintiff indivisible harm can be held liable for the plaintiff's total amount of damages.","analysis":"Liability of multiple tortfeasors for indivisible harm\n\nJoint & several Any tortfeasor can be held liable for plaintiff's total amount of damages liability* Tortfeasors can sue each other for contribution Several liability Each tortfeasor is liable for portion of damages corresponding to his/her proportionate fault (eg, 15% of damages for 15% fault) *Default rule on MBE.\n\nThis jurisdiction has adopted the following: Pure comparative negligence (default rule on MBE) – when the plaintiff's own negligence contributes to his/her harm, the plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault\n\nJoint and several liability – when multiple defendants cause the plaintiff indivisible harm, the plaintiff can recover the total amount of damages from any defendant (who can then sue the others for contribution)\n\nAdditionally, this jurisdiction has abandoned the common law rules of intra-family liability. As a result, the fault of a child's parents will not be imputed to the child or vice versa. Here, the court determined that multiple defendants were at fault for causing the child's injuries: the playmate's parents (20%), the driver (50%), and the child's parents (30%). The child was not at fault. And since his parents' fault will not be imputed to him, his recovery will not be reduced for comparative negligence. Therefore, the child can recover his total damages ($100,000) from any of the defendants—including the driver. (Choices A & B) If liability were only several (not seen here), each tortfeasor would only be liable for the portion of damages that corresponds to his/her proportionate fault. In that case, the child could only recover $30,000 from his parents (30% of total damages) and $50,000 from the driver (50% of total damages). (Choice D) If traditional common law rules regarding intra-family liability and contributory negligence applied, the fault of the child's parents would be imputed to the child and bar all recovery. But these rules have been abandoned or replaced in this jurisdiction.","conclusion":"Answer C: $100,000."},"questionZh":"一名四岁儿童在玩耍时，被玩伴从两辆停放的汽车之间推入街道，随后被一辆汽车撞伤。\n\n该儿童通过其代理人提起诉讼，被告包括肇事司机、玩伴的父母以及他自己的父母。\n\n庭审认定该儿童遭受的损害总额为10万美元。\n\n玩伴的父母因未能尽到充分监管责任而被认定承担20%的过错责任；司机被认定承担50%的过错责任；该儿童的父母因未能尽到充分监管责任而被认定承担30%的过错责任。\n\n法院已采用“纯粹比较过失”原则及“连带责任”制度，以取代关于原告过失的普通法规则；此外，有关家庭成员间责任的普通法原则也已被废除。\n\n该儿童的代理人最多能从司机处获得多少赔偿（如有）？","choicesZh":{"A":"3万美元。","B":"5万美元。","C":"10万美元。","D":"无法获得赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"多名侵权行为人对不可分割损害的责任 连带责任（Joint & several liability）：任何侵权行为人都可能被要求对原告的全部损害赔偿额承担责任*；侵权行为人之间可相互追偿（要求分担责任）。按份责任（Several liability）：每位侵权行为人仅对其过错比例相应的损害部分承担责任（例如，过错比例为15%则承担15%的损害赔偿）*这是MBE（多州律师资格考试）中的默认规则。\n\n本司法管辖区采用了以下规则：纯粹比较过失原则（MBE默认规则）——当原告自身的过失导致了其受到的损害时，原告获得的赔偿额将根据其过错比例相应扣减。 连带责任——当多名被告共同导致原告遭受不可分割的损害时，原告可以向任一被告追索全部损害赔偿额（该被告随后可向其他被告追偿/要求分担责任）。 此外，本司法管辖区已废除了关于家庭成员间责任的普通法规则。\n\n因此，父母的过失不会归责于子女，反之亦然。\n\n在本案中，法院认定多名被告对导致该儿童受伤负有过错：玩伴的父母（20%）、司机（50%）以及该儿童的父母（30%）。\n\n该儿童本身无过错。\n\n由于其父母的过失不会归责于他，其获赔金额不会因比较过失原则而被扣减。\n\n因此，该儿童可以向任一被告（包括司机）追索全部损害赔偿额（10万美元）。\n\n（选项A和B）如果适用按份责任（本案不适用），则每位侵权行为人仅需承担与其过错比例相应的损害赔偿部分。\n\n在此情况下，该儿童只能从其父母处获得3万美元（总赔偿额的30%），并从司机处获得5万美元（总赔偿额的50%）。\n\n（选项D）如果适用关于家庭成员间责任和与有过失（contributory negligence）（contributory negligence）的传统普通法规则，父母的过失将被归责于子女，从而导致其无法获得任何赔偿。\n\n但这些规则在本司法管辖区已被废除或取代。\n\n教学目标：在“纯粹比较过失”原则下，有过失的原告所获赔偿额将根据其过失比例相应扣减；而在“连带责任”原则下，若多名侵权被告共同导致了原告遭受不可分割的损害，则其中任何一名被告均可被判承担原告全部损害赔偿责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第488条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明父母的过失不再归责于子女，子女的过失亦不归责于父母）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第875条（美国法律协会，1979年）（阐明导致不可分割损害的多名侵权行为人，可就全部损害承担单独责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在“纯粹比较过失”原则下，有过失的原告所获赔偿额将扣除其过失比例对应的份额。\n\n而在“连带责任”制度下，导致原告遭受不可分割损害的多个侵权被告中，任何一方均可被判承担原告损害赔偿总额的责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"该儿童的代理人最多能从司机处获得多少赔偿（如有）？","rule":"在“纯粹比较过失”原则下，有过失的原告所获赔偿额将扣除其过失比例对应的份额。\n\n而在“连带责任”制度下，导致原告遭受不可分割损害的多个侵权被告中，任何一方均可被判承担原告损害赔偿总额的责任。","analysis":"多名侵权行为人对不可分割损害的责任 连带责任（Joint & several liability）：任何侵权行为人都可能被要求对原告的全部损害赔偿额承担责任*；侵权行为人之间可相互追偿（要求分担责任）。按份责任（Several liability）：每位侵权行为人仅对其过错比例相应的损害部分承担责任（例如，过错比例为15%则承担15%的损害赔偿）*这是MBE（多州律师资格考试）中的默认规则。\n\n本司法管辖区采用了以下规则：纯粹比较过失原则（MBE默认规则）——当原告自身的过失导致了其受到的损害时，原告获得的赔偿额将根据其过错比例相应扣减。 连带责任——当多名被告共同导致原告遭受不可分割的损害时，原告可以向任一被告追索全部损害赔偿额（该被告随后可向其他被告追偿/要求分担责任）。 此外，本司法管辖区已废除了关于家庭成员间责任的普通法规则。\n\n因此，父母的过失不会归责于子女，反之亦然。\n\n在本案中，法院认定多名被告对导致该儿童受伤负有过错：玩伴的父母（20%）、司机（50%）以及该儿童的父母（30%）。\n\n该儿童本身无过错。\n\n由于其父母的过失不会归责于他，其获赔金额不会因比较过失原则而被扣减。\n\n因此，该儿童可以向任一被告（包括司机）追索全部损害赔偿额（10万美元）。\n\n（选项A和B）如果适用按份责任（本案不适用），则每位侵权行为人仅需承担与其过错比例相应的损害赔偿部分。\n\n在此情况下，该儿童只能从其父母处获得3万美元（总赔偿额的30%），并从司机处获得5万美元（总赔偿额的50%）。\n\n（选项D）如果适用关于家庭成员间责任和与有过失（contributory negligence）（contributory negligence）的传统普通法规则，父母的过失将被归责于子女，从而导致其无法获得任何赔偿。\n\n但这些规则在本司法管辖区已被废除或取代。\n\n教学目标：在“纯粹比较过失”原则下，有过失的原告所获赔偿额将根据其过失比例相应扣减；而在“连带责任”原则下，若多名侵权被告共同导致了原告遭受不可分割的损害，则其中任何一名被告均可被判承担原告全部损害赔偿责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第488条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明父母的过失不再归责于子女，子女的过失亦不归责于父母）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第875条（美国法律协会，1979年）（阐明导致不可分割损害的多名侵权行为人，可就全部损害承担单独责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：10万美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":86,"sourceQuestionPage":259,"sourceQuestionPages":[259],"sourceExplanationPages":[260,261],"sourceAnswerPages":[260,261],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-087","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A politician and a businessman were passengers sitting in adjoining seats on an airline's flight. There were many empty seats on the aircraft. During the flight, a flight attendant served the businessman eight drinks. As the businessman became more and more obviously intoxicated and attempted to engage the politician in a conversation, the politician chose to ignore the businessman. This angered the businessman, who suddenly struck the politician in the face, giving her a black eye. If the politician asserts a claim for damages against the airline based on battery, will she prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the businessman was not acting as an agent or employee of the airline.","B":"No, unless she can establish some permanent injury from the contact.","C":"Yes, because she suffered an intentionally inflicted harmful or offensive contact.","D":"Yes, if the flight attendant acted recklessly in continuing to serve liquor to the businessman."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Vicarious liability\n\n(liability for tortious acts of another)\n\nRespondeat Employee's conduct occurred within scope of employment—ie, under superior employer's control or while performing assigned work\n\nNondelegable Independent contractor's work involved abnormally or inherently duty dangerous activity, condition on land open to general public, or activity regulated by law\n\nParent Child committed tort while acting as parent's agent\n\nBusiness partner Business partner's conduct occurred within scope of business\n\nAutomobile Family member drove car with permission (family-car doctrine) or owner any person drove car with permission under owner-liability statute\n\nDram shop* & Alcohol sold (dram shop) or served at noncommercial setting (social social host liability) to visibly intoxicated person or minor\n\n*Only applies when question provides dram-shop statute.\n\nVicarious liability is a type of strict liability in which a principal is liable for the tortious act (e.g., battery) of his/her agent if the act was committed within the scope of an agency relationship. An agency relationship exists when the parties mutually agree that the agent will act on the principal's behalf and subject to the principal's control (e.g., employer– employee). Here, the businessman intentionally struck the politician in the face (battery) on the airline's flight. But since the businessman was not an employee of the airline or otherwise acting on its behalf (no agency relationship), the airline is not vicariously liable for the businessman's battery. Therefore, the politician will not prevail in her battery claim against the airline. (Choice B) Battery merely requires proof of harmful or offensive contact—not permanent injury. Therefore, the politician's ability to show permanent injury will not affect the success of her claim against the airline. Instead, her claim will fail because the airline is not vicariously liable for the businessman's conduct. (Choice C) The fact that the politician suffered an intentionally inflicted harmful or offensive contact when the businessman struck her face would support a battery claim against the businessman. But since the businessman was not the airline's agent, this fact will not allow the politician to prevail against the airline.\n\n(Choice D) The airline could be vicariously liable for the flight attendant's (i.e., an employee's) negligence or recklessness in continuing to serve liquor to the businessman. But since the politician's battery claim against the airline is based on the businessman's conduct—not the flight attendant's negligence—the politician's claim will fail.\n\nEducational objective: A principal is vicariously liable for a tort committed by his/her agent if the tort was committed within the scope of an agency relationship. An agency relationship exists when both parties agree that the agent will act on the principal's behalf and subject to the principal's control (e.g., employer–employee).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Agency § 7.03 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (principal's liability for acts of its agents).","educationalObjective":"A principal is vicariously liable for a tort committed by his/her agent if the tort was committed within the scope of an agency relationship. An agency relationship exists when both parties agree that the agent will act on the principal's behalf and subject to the principal's control (e.g., employer–employee).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Agency § 7.03 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (principal's liability for acts of its agents).","rule":"A principal is vicariously liable for a tort committed by his/her agent if the tort was committed within the scope of an agency relationship. An agency relationship exists when both parties agree that the agent will act on the principal's behalf and subject to the principal's control (e.g., employer–employee).","irac":{"issue":"If the politician asserts a claim for damages against the airline based on battery, will she prevail?","rule":"A principal is vicariously liable for a tort committed by his/her agent if the tort was committed within the scope of an agency relationship. An agency relationship exists when both parties agree that the agent will act on the principal's behalf and subject to the principal's control (e.g., employer–employee).","analysis":"Vicarious liability\n\n(liability for tortious acts of another)\n\nRespondeat Employee's conduct occurred within scope of employment—ie, under superior employer's control or while performing assigned work\n\nNondelegable Independent contractor's work involved abnormally or inherently duty dangerous activity, condition on land open to general public, or activity regulated by law\n\nParent Child committed tort while acting as parent's agent\n\nBusiness partner Business partner's conduct occurred within scope of business\n\nAutomobile Family member drove car with permission (family-car doctrine) or owner any person drove car with permission under owner-liability statute\n\nDram shop* & Alcohol sold (dram shop) or served at noncommercial setting (social social host liability) to visibly intoxicated person or minor\n\n*Only applies when question provides dram-shop statute.\n\nVicarious liability is a type of strict liability in which a principal is liable for the tortious act (e.g., battery) of his/her agent if the act was committed within the scope of an agency relationship. An agency relationship exists when the parties mutually agree that the agent will act on the principal's behalf and subject to the principal's control (e.g., employer– employee). Here, the businessman intentionally struck the politician in the face (battery) on the airline's flight. But since the businessman was not an employee of the airline or otherwise acting on its behalf (no agency relationship), the airline is not vicariously liable for the businessman's battery. Therefore, the politician will not prevail in her battery claim against the airline. (Choice B) Battery merely requires proof of harmful or offensive contact—not permanent injury. Therefore, the politician's ability to show permanent injury will not affect the success of her claim against the airline. Instead, her claim will fail because the airline is not vicariously liable for the businessman's conduct. (Choice C) The fact that the politician suffered an intentionally inflicted harmful or offensive contact when the businessman struck her face would support a battery claim against the businessman. But since the businessman was not the airline's agent, this fact will not allow the politician to prevail against the airline.\n\n(Choice D) The airline could be vicariously liable for the flight attendant's (i.e., an employee's) negligence or recklessness in continuing to serve liquor to the businessman. But since the politician's battery claim against the airline is based on the businessman's conduct—not the flight attendant's negligence—the politician's claim will fail.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the businessman was not acting as an agent or employee of the airline."},"questionZh":"一位政治家和一位商人乘坐同一航班，座位相邻。\n\n机上有许多空位。\n\n飞行途中，空乘人员为该商人提供了八杯酒。\n\n随着商人醉意渐浓并试图与政治家搭话，政治家选择不予理睬。\n\n这激怒了商人，他突然打了政治家一拳，导致其眼部受伤（出现“黑眼圈”）。\n\n如果政治家基于“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）这一侵权行为向航空公司提出损害赔偿请求，她能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为该商人并非作为航空公司的代理人或雇员行事。","B":"不能，除非她能证明该接触造成了某种永久性伤害。","C":"能，因为她遭受了蓄意造成的有害或冒犯性接触。","D":"能，如果空乘人员在继续向该商人提供酒水时表现出鲁莽行为。"},"explanationZh":"替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability） （对他人的侵权行为承担责任） 雇主责任（Respondeat Superior） 雇员的行为发生在雇佣范围内——即受雇主控制或在执行指派工作期间 不可转让义务（Non-delegable duty） 独立承包商的工作涉及异常危险或固有危险的活动、向公众开放的土地状况，或受法律监管的活动 父母责任 子女在作为父母代理人行事时实施了侵权行为 商业合伙人责任 合伙人的行为发生在业务范围内 汽车责任 家庭成员经许可驾驶汽车（家庭用车原则），或根据车主责任法规，任何人经车主许可驾驶汽车 酒馆责任（Dram shop）与社交东道主责任 向明显醉酒者或未成年人出售（酒馆责任）或提供（社交东道主责任）酒精饮料 *仅在题目提供酒馆责任法规时适用。\n\n替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）是一种严格责任，即如果代理人在代理关系范围内实施了侵权行为（例如故意侵害身体（battery）），委托人需为此承担责任。\n\n当双方达成一致，由代理人代表委托人行事并受委托人控制时（例如雇主与雇员关系），即存在代理关系。\n\n在本案中，该商人是在航空公司的航班上故意击打政治家的面部（构成故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n但由于该商人并非航空公司的雇员，也未代表航空公司行事（不存在代理关系），因此航空公司无需为该商人的故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n因此，该政治家针对航空公司提起的故意侵害身体（battery）索赔将无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）故意侵害身体（battery）行为仅需证明存在有害或冒犯性的接触，而无需证明造成了永久性伤害。\n\n因此，该政治家能否证明存在永久性伤害，并不影响其针对航空公司索赔的成败。\n\n相反，她的索赔将因航空公司无需为该商人的行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）而失败。\n\n（选项 C）该政治家在被商人击打面部时遭受了蓄意造成的有害或冒犯性接触，这一事实支持其针对商人提起故意侵害身体（battery）索赔。\n\n但由于该商人并非航空公司的代理人，这一事实无法使该政治家在针对航空公司的索赔中胜诉。\n\n(选项 D) 航空公司可能需为乘务员（即雇员）继续向该商人提供酒精饮料时的疏忽或鲁莽行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，由于该政客针对航空公司提出的故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）索赔是基于该商人的行为——而非乘务员的疏忽——因此该政客的索赔将无法成立。\n\n教学目标：如果代理人（或雇员）在代理关系范围内实施了侵权行为，则本人（或雇主）须为此承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n当双方同意由代理人代表本人行事并受本人控制时（例如雇主与雇员的关系），即存在代理关系。\n\n参考文献 《代理法重述（第三版）》第 7.03 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（关于本人对其代理人行为应承担的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果代理人实施了侵权行为，且该行为是在代理关系范围内实施的，则委托人须对该侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n当双方同意由代理人代表委托人行事并受委托人控制时（例如雇主与雇员关系），即存在代理关系。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果政治家基于“故意侵害身体（battery）”这一侵权行为向航空公司提出损害赔偿请求，她能胜诉吗？","rule":"如果代理人实施了侵权行为，且该行为是在代理关系范围内实施的，则委托人须对该侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n当双方同意由代理人代表委托人行事并受委托人控制时（例如雇主与雇员关系），即存在代理关系。","analysis":"替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability） （对他人的侵权行为承担责任） 雇主责任（Respondeat Superior） 雇员的行为发生在雇佣范围内——即受雇主控制或在执行指派工作期间 不可转让义务（Non-delegable duty） 独立承包商的工作涉及异常危险或固有危险的活动、向公众开放的土地状况，或受法律监管的活动 父母责任 子女在作为父母代理人行事时实施了侵权行为 商业合伙人责任 合伙人的行为发生在业务范围内 汽车责任 家庭成员经许可驾驶汽车（家庭用车原则），或根据车主责任法规，任何人经车主许可驾驶汽车 酒馆责任（Dram shop）与社交东道主责任 向明显醉酒者或未成年人出售（酒馆责任）或提供（社交东道主责任）酒精饮料 *仅在题目提供酒馆责任法规时适用。\n\n替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）是一种严格责任，即如果代理人在代理关系范围内实施了侵权行为（例如故意侵害身体（battery）），委托人需为此承担责任。\n\n当双方达成一致，由代理人代表委托人行事并受委托人控制时（例如雇主与雇员关系），即存在代理关系。\n\n在本案中，该商人是在航空公司的航班上故意击打政治家的面部（构成故意侵害身体（battery））。\n\n但由于该商人并非航空公司的雇员，也未代表航空公司行事（不存在代理关系），因此航空公司无需为该商人的故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n因此，该政治家针对航空公司提起的故意侵害身体（battery）索赔将无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）故意侵害身体（battery）行为仅需证明存在有害或冒犯性的接触，而无需证明造成了永久性伤害。\n\n因此，该政治家能否证明存在永久性伤害，并不影响其针对航空公司索赔的成败。\n\n相反，她的索赔将因航空公司无需为该商人的行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）而失败。\n\n（选项 C）该政治家在被商人击打面部时遭受了蓄意造成的有害或冒犯性接触，这一事实支持其针对商人提起故意侵害身体（battery）索赔。\n\n但由于该商人并非航空公司的代理人，这一事实无法使该政治家在针对航空公司的索赔中胜诉。\n\n(选项 D) 航空公司可能需为乘务员（即雇员）继续向该商人提供酒精饮料时的疏忽或鲁莽行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，由于该政客针对航空公司提出的故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）索赔是基于该商人的行为——而非乘务员的疏忽——因此该政客的索赔将无法成立。\n\n教学目标：如果代理人（或雇员）在代理关系范围内实施了侵权行为，则本人（或雇主）须为此承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n当双方同意由代理人代表本人行事并受本人控制时（例如雇主与雇员的关系），即存在代理关系。\n\n参考文献 《代理法重述（第三版）》第 7.03 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（关于本人对其代理人行为应承担的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不能，因为该商人并非作为航空公司的代理人或雇员行事。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":87,"sourceQuestionPage":262,"sourceQuestionPages":[262],"sourceExplanationPages":[263,264],"sourceAnswerPages":[263,264],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-088","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A seven-year-old child regularly visited a neighboring farm to play and to visit the neighbor's horses. The neighbor told the child that he was welcome at the farm anytime. However, the neighbor warned the child that the horses had to remain in their stable or in their paddock unless the neighbor was present. One day while the neighbor was away, the child went to visit the horses. The child wanted to watch the horses run, so he let them out of their paddock and into an adjoining pasture. One horse escaped from the pasture and ran across a busy road. A driver who swerved to avoid hitting the horse lost control of her car, sustaining serious injuries to herself and damage to the car. This jurisdiction has a statute that states, in part: \"Any person who knowingly releases or causes the release of a domesticated animal is liable in negligence for damage caused by that animal.\" The driver of the car sued the child and the child's parents for her injuries and the damage to her car, alleging negligence per se. Which of the following would likely be the child's best defense?","choices":{"A":"Neither the child nor the child's parents had ownership or control of the horse.","B":"The child exercised the care that a child of his age and maturity would have exercised.","C":"The driver's harm was not of the type that the statute was designed to prevent.","D":"The driver's harm was not proximately caused by the child's actions."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Defenses to negligence per se\n\nViolation of law excused by:\n\nuse of reasonable care incapacity need to avoid greater risk of harm impossibility of complying vagueness of law reasonable ignorance (rare) Negligence requires proof of duty, breach, causation, and damages. Duty and breach are presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se when the following facts are proved: The defendant violated a statute or ordinance. The plaintiff suffered a type of harm that the statute or ordinance was intended to protect against. The plaintiff was in the class of persons intended to be protected by the statute or ordinance. However, the defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused if the violation was reasonable in light of the defendant's physical disability or incapacity OR because the defendant is a child. This means that a child's violation will be excused, and negligence per se will not apply, if the child acted with the same level of care as other children of similar age and maturity. Therefore, this would likely be the child's best defense. (Choice A) The statute applies to any person who releases a domestic animal; it is not limited to persons who own or control the animal. (Choice C) While the statute does not specify the exact type(s) of harm it was designed to prevent, it seems likely that traffic accidents caused by escaped domestic animals is one of them. (Choice D) Proximate (legal) cause requires that the plaintiff suffer a foreseeable harm that is not too remote from, and is within the risk created by, the defendant's conduct. Here, it is foreseeable that letting a horse out of its enclosure could result in the horse entering a nearby roadway and causing a traffic hazard.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused, and negligence per se will not apply, if the violation was reasonable in light of the defendant's childhood, physical disability, or physical incapacitation.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (discussing excused violations of a statute).","educationalObjective":"A defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused, and negligence per se will not apply, if the violation was reasonable in light of the defendant's childhood, physical disability, or physical incapacitation.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (discussing excused violations of a statute).","rule":"A defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused, and negligence per se will not apply, if the violation was reasonable in light of the defendant's childhood, physical disability, or physical incapacitation.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would likely be the child's best defense?","rule":"A defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused, and negligence per se will not apply, if the violation was reasonable in light of the defendant's childhood, physical disability, or physical incapacitation.","analysis":"Defenses to negligence per se\n\nViolation of law excused by:\n\nuse of reasonable care incapacity need to avoid greater risk of harm impossibility of complying vagueness of law reasonable ignorance (rare) Negligence requires proof of duty, breach, causation, and damages. Duty and breach are presumed under the doctrine of negligence per se when the following facts are proved: The defendant violated a statute or ordinance. The plaintiff suffered a type of harm that the statute or ordinance was intended to protect against. The plaintiff was in the class of persons intended to be protected by the statute or ordinance. However, the defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused if the violation was reasonable in light of the defendant's physical disability or incapacity OR because the defendant is a child. This means that a child's violation will be excused, and negligence per se will not apply, if the child acted with the same level of care as other children of similar age and maturity. Therefore, this would likely be the child's best defense. (Choice A) The statute applies to any person who releases a domestic animal; it is not limited to persons who own or control the animal. (Choice C) While the statute does not specify the exact type(s) of harm it was designed to prevent, it seems likely that traffic accidents caused by escaped domestic animals is one of them. (Choice D) Proximate (legal) cause requires that the plaintiff suffer a foreseeable harm that is not too remote from, and is within the risk created by, the defendant's conduct. Here, it is foreseeable that letting a horse out of its enclosure could result in the horse entering a nearby roadway and causing a traffic hazard.","conclusion":"Answer B: The child exercised the care that a child of his age and maturity would have exercised."},"questionZh":"一名七岁儿童经常去邻近的农场玩耍并看望邻居家的马。\n\n邻居告诉这名儿童，他随时欢迎来农场。\n\n然而，邻居也告诫过他，除非邻居在场，否则马匹必须留在马厩或围场内。\n\n有一天，趁邻居不在，这名儿童去看了马。\n\n他想看马奔跑，于是把马从围场放到了相邻的牧场里。\n\n其中一匹马逃出了牧场，跑过了一条繁忙的道路。\n\n一名司机为了避让这匹马而急打方向盘，导致车辆失控，司机本人受重伤，车辆也受损。\n\n该司法管辖区有一项法规，其中规定：“任何明知而释放或导致释放家养动物的人，均须对该动物造成的损害承担过失责任。”这名司机起诉了该儿童及其父母，就其受伤及车辆受损索赔，并主张“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则。\n\n以下哪项最可能是该儿童的最佳抗辩理由？","choicesZh":{"A":"该儿童及其父母均不拥有该马匹，也未对其进行控制。","B":"该儿童尽到了与其年龄和成熟度相符的注意义务。","C":"司机所受的伤害不属于该法规旨在防止的损害类型。","D":"司机所受的伤害并非由该儿童的行为直接导致（即缺乏法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause））。"},"explanationZh":"“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则的抗辩理由 违反法律行为可获豁免的情形包括： 尽到了合理注意义务；丧失行为能力；为避免更大的伤害风险；无法遵守法律；法律规定含糊不清；合理的不知情（罕见）。\n\n认定过失需要证明存在注意义务、违反义务、因果关系及损害后果。\n\n根据“本身过失”原则，若证实以下事实，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的情形：被告违反了某项成文法或地方法规；原告遭受了该法规旨在防范的某种损害；原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群范围。\n\n然而，如果被告因身体残疾或丧失行为能力，或者因其为儿童，导致违反适用法规的行为在当时情况下是合理的，则该违规行为可获豁免。\n\n这意味着，如果儿童的行为符合同等年龄和成熟度儿童应有的注意程度，其违规行为将获豁免，且不适用“本身过失”原则。\n\n因此，这很可能是该儿童的最佳抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 A）该法规适用于任何释放家畜的人；其适用范围不限于家畜的所有者或控制者。\n\n（选项 C）虽然该法规未明确列出其旨在防范的具体损害类型，但家畜逃逸引发的交通事故很可能属于其中之一。\n\n（选项 D）法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律上的因果关系）要求原告遭受的损害是可预见的，且该损害与被告行为之间不存在过远的因果链条，并处于被告行为所引发的风险范围之内。\n\n在本案中，放任马匹离开围栏可能导致马匹进入附近道路并引发交通隐患，这种后果是可预见的。\n\n教育目标：如果被告因处于儿童期、身体残疾或丧失身体行为能力而导致违反适用法规的行为在当时情况下是合理的，则该违规行为可获豁免，且不适用“本身过失”原则。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第15条（美国法律协会，2010年）（讨论违反法规行为的豁免情形）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果被告违反适用法规或条例的行为，鉴于其儿童身份、身体残疾或身体丧失行为能力的情况，被视为是合理的，则该违规行为可获免责，且不适用“本身过失”原则。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项最可能是该儿童的最佳抗辩理由？","rule":"如果被告违反适用法规或条例的行为，鉴于其儿童身份、身体残疾或身体丧失行为能力的情况，被视为是合理的，则该违规行为可获免责，且不适用“本身过失”原则。","analysis":"“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则的抗辩理由 违反法律行为可获豁免的情形包括： 尽到了合理注意义务；丧失行为能力；为避免更大的伤害风险；无法遵守法律；法律规定含糊不清；合理的不知情（罕见）。\n\n认定过失需要证明存在注意义务、违反义务、因果关系及损害后果。\n\n根据“本身过失”原则，若证实以下事实，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的情形：被告违反了某项成文法或地方法规；原告遭受了该法规旨在防范的某种损害；原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群范围。\n\n然而，如果被告因身体残疾或丧失行为能力，或者因其为儿童，导致违反适用法规的行为在当时情况下是合理的，则该违规行为可获豁免。\n\n这意味着，如果儿童的行为符合同等年龄和成熟度儿童应有的注意程度，其违规行为将获豁免，且不适用“本身过失”原则。\n\n因此，这很可能是该儿童的最佳抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 A）该法规适用于任何释放家畜的人；其适用范围不限于家畜的所有者或控制者。\n\n（选项 C）虽然该法规未明确列出其旨在防范的具体损害类型，但家畜逃逸引发的交通事故很可能属于其中之一。\n\n（选项 D）法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律上的因果关系）要求原告遭受的损害是可预见的，且该损害与被告行为之间不存在过远的因果链条，并处于被告行为所引发的风险范围之内。\n\n在本案中，放任马匹离开围栏可能导致马匹进入附近道路并引发交通隐患，这种后果是可预见的。\n\n教育目标：如果被告因处于儿童期、身体残疾或丧失身体行为能力而导致违反适用法规的行为在当时情况下是合理的，则该违规行为可获豁免，且不适用“本身过失”原则。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第15条（美国法律协会，2010年）（讨论违反法规行为的豁免情形）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该儿童尽到了与其年龄和成熟度相符的注意义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":88,"sourceQuestionPage":265,"sourceQuestionPages":[265],"sourceExplanationPages":[266,267],"sourceAnswerPages":[266,267],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-089","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A shopper was riding on an escalator in a department store when the escalator stopped abruptly. The shopper lost her balance and fell down the escalator steps, sustaining injuries. Although the escalator had been regularly maintained by an independent contractor, the store's obligation to provide safe conditions for its invitees was nondelegable. The shopper has brought an action against the store for damages, and the above facts are the only facts in evidence. The store has moved for a directed verdict. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the finder of fact could infer that the escalator malfunction was due to negligence.","B":"No, because the store is strictly liable for the shopper's injuries.","C":"Yes, because an independent contractor maintained the escalator.","D":"Yes, because the shopper has not produced evidence of negligence."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A court should grant a defendant's motion for a directed verdict if the plaintiff fails to present legally sufficient evidence to support any theory of recovery. A plaintiff can recover for negligence under theories of: direct liability (for the defendant's own conduct) – requires proof that the defendant's failure to exercise reasonable care caused the plaintiff harm or\n\nvicarious liability (for an independent contractor's conduct) – requires proof that a contractor's work involved a nondelegable duty owed by the defendant—a duty that cannot be assigned to a third party to avoid liability—and the contractor's breach of that duty (ie, negligence) caused the plaintiff harm.\n\nSince businesses have a duty to provide safe conditions for their invitees (eg, customers), the store could be directly liable if the shopper can show that her injuries were caused by the store's negligence. And since that duty is nondelegable, the store could also be vicariously liable for its independent contractor's negligence (Choice C). And though there is no direct evidence of the store's or the contractor's negligence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence when: the type of accident that caused the plaintiff's harm would not ordinarily occur absent negligence by someone in the defendant's (or the contractor's) position and\n\nthe evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.\n\nHere, an escalator would not ordinarily malfunction in the absence of negligence by someone operating or maintaining the escalator, and there is no evidence of other possible causes of the shopper's harm. The fact finder could therefore infer that the malfunction was due to either the store's or the contractor's negligence and that this negligence caused the shopper's injuries (Choice D). As a result, the evidence is legally sufficient to support the shopper's recovery under either theory, and the court should deny the store's motion for a directed verdict. (Choice B) Strict liability applies to strict products liability, certain animals, and abnormally dangerous activities. But here, the shopper was not harmed by a product sold by the store (no strict products liability), an animal, or an abnormally dangerous activity.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant can be (1) directly liable for his/her own negligence or (2) vicariously liable for an independent contractor's negligence if the contractor's work involved a nondelegable duty owed by the defendant—eg, a business's duty to maintain safe premises for its invitees.\n\nReferences\nFed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (judgment as a matter of law).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 6 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (direct liability for negligence).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 62 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (vicarious liability for independent contractor's negligence).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (res ipsa loquitur).","educationalObjective":"A defendant can be (1) directly liable for his/her own negligence or (2) vicariously liable for an independent contractor's negligence if the contractor's work involved a nondelegable duty owed by the defendant—eg, a business's duty to maintain safe premises for its invitees.","references":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (judgment as a matter of law).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 6 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (direct liability for negligence).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 62 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (vicarious liability for independent contractor's negligence).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (res ipsa loquitur).","rule":"A defendant can be (1) directly liable for his/her own negligence or (2) vicariously liable for an independent contractor's negligence if the contractor's work involved a nondelegable duty owed by the defendant—eg, a business's duty to maintain safe premises for its invitees.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"A defendant can be (1) directly liable for his/her own negligence or (2) vicariously liable for an independent contractor's negligence if the contractor's work involved a nondelegable duty owed by the defendant—eg, a business's duty to maintain safe premises for its invitees.","analysis":"A court should grant a defendant's motion for a directed verdict if the plaintiff fails to present legally sufficient evidence to support any theory of recovery. A plaintiff can recover for negligence under theories of: direct liability (for the defendant's own conduct) – requires proof that the defendant's failure to exercise reasonable care caused the plaintiff harm or\n\nvicarious liability (for an independent contractor's conduct) – requires proof that a contractor's work involved a nondelegable duty owed by the defendant—a duty that cannot be assigned to a third party to avoid liability—and the contractor's breach of that duty (ie, negligence) caused the plaintiff harm.\n\nSince businesses have a duty to provide safe conditions for their invitees (eg, customers), the store could be directly liable if the shopper can show that her injuries were caused by the store's negligence. And since that duty is nondelegable, the store could also be vicariously liable for its independent contractor's negligence (Choice C). And though there is no direct evidence of the store's or the contractor's negligence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence when: the type of accident that caused the plaintiff's harm would not ordinarily occur absent negligence by someone in the defendant's (or the contractor's) position and\n\nthe evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.\n\nHere, an escalator would not ordinarily malfunction in the absence of negligence by someone operating or maintaining the escalator, and there is no evidence of other possible causes of the shopper's harm. The fact finder could therefore infer that the malfunction was due to either the store's or the contractor's negligence and that this negligence caused the shopper's injuries (Choice D). As a result, the evidence is legally sufficient to support the shopper's recovery under either theory, and the court should deny the store's motion for a directed verdict. (Choice B) Strict liability applies to strict products liability, certain animals, and abnormally dangerous activities. But here, the shopper was not harmed by a product sold by the store (no strict products liability), an animal, or an abnormally dangerous activity.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the finder of fact could infer that the escalator malfunction was due to negligence."},"questionZh":"一名顾客在百货商店乘坐自动扶梯时，扶梯突然停止。\n\n顾客失去平衡，从扶梯台阶上摔下，受伤。\n\n虽然扶梯一直由独立承包商定期维护，但商店为顾客提供安全环境的义务是不可推卸的。\n\n该顾客已就此对商店提起损害赔偿诉讼，以上事实是仅有的证据。\n\n商店已提出指示性判决动议。\n\n法院是否应准许该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为事实认定者可以推断扶梯故障是由于商店的疏忽造成的。","B":"否，因为商店对顾客的受伤负有严格责任。","C":"是，因为扶梯是由独立承包商维护的。","D":"是，因为顾客没有提供任何关于商店疏忽的证据。"},"explanationZh":"如果原告未能提出在法律上足以支持任何索赔主张的证据，法院应准予被告提出的指示裁决（directed verdict）动议。\n\n原告可基于以下理论就过失主张赔偿：直接责任（针对被告自身的行为）——要求证明被告未尽到合理注意义务，从而导致原告受到伤害；或 替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（针对独立承包商的行为）——要求证明承包商的工作涉及被告负有的不可转让义务（即不能通过将义务委派给第三方来免除责任的义务），且承包商违反了该义务（即存在过失），从而导致原告受到伤害。\n\n鉴于企业有义务为其受邀者（如顾客）提供安全的环境，如果购物者能证明其受伤是由商店的过失造成的，商店可能承担直接责任。\n\n此外，由于该义务不可转让，商店也可能因其独立承包商的过失而承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（选项 C）。\n\n尽管没有关于商店或承包商过失的直接证据，但“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”原则（res ipsa loquitur）允许根据间接证据推断存在过失，前提是：导致原告受伤的事故类型，若非处于被告（或承包商）地位的人员存在过失，通常不会发生；以及 证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因。\n\n在本案中，若无操作或维护自动扶梯的人员存在过失，自动扶梯通常不会发生故障，且没有证据表明购物者受伤存在其他可能原因。\n\n因此，事实认定者可以推断，故障是由商店或承包商的过失造成的，且该过失导致了购物者的受伤（选项 D）。\n\n因此，现有证据在法律上足以支持购物者基于上述任一理论获得赔偿，法院应驳回商店提出的指示裁决动议。\n\n（选项 B）严格责任适用于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）、特定动物相关情形以及异常危险活动。\n\n但在本案中，购物者并非因商店销售的产品（不涉及严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））、动物或异常危险活动而受伤。\n\n教学目标：被告可能因（1）其自身的过失而承担直接责任，或（2）因独立承包商的过失而承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（若该承包商的工作涉及被告负有的不可转让义务——例如，企业有义务为其受邀者维护场所安全）。\n\n参考文献 《联邦民事诉讼规则》第50(a)条（法律问题判决）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第6条（美国法律协会，2010年）（过失的直接责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第62条（美国法律协会，2012年）（独立承包商过失的替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第17条（美国法律协会，2010年）（“事实本身说明问题”原则/res ipsa loquitur）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"被告可能承担以下责任：(1) 直接因其自身过失而承担责任；或 (2) 因独立承包商的过失而承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），前提是该承包商的工作涉及被告不可转让的义务——例如，企业有义务为受邀者维护场所安全。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应准许该动议？","rule":"被告可能承担以下责任：(1) 直接因其自身过失而承担责任；或 (2) 因独立承包商的过失而承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），前提是该承包商的工作涉及被告不可转让的义务——例如，企业有义务为受邀者维护场所安全。","analysis":"如果原告未能提出在法律上足以支持任何索赔主张的证据，法院应准予被告提出的指示裁决（directed verdict）动议。\n\n原告可基于以下理论就过失主张赔偿：直接责任（针对被告自身的行为）——要求证明被告未尽到合理注意义务，从而导致原告受到伤害；或 替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（针对独立承包商的行为）——要求证明承包商的工作涉及被告负有的不可转让义务（即不能通过将义务委派给第三方来免除责任的义务），且承包商违反了该义务（即存在过失），从而导致原告受到伤害。\n\n鉴于企业有义务为其受邀者（如顾客）提供安全的环境，如果购物者能证明其受伤是由商店的过失造成的，商店可能承担直接责任。\n\n此外，由于该义务不可转让，商店也可能因其独立承包商的过失而承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（选项 C）。\n\n尽管没有关于商店或承包商过失的直接证据，但“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”原则（res ipsa loquitur）允许根据间接证据推断存在过失，前提是：导致原告受伤的事故类型，若非处于被告（或承包商）地位的人员存在过失，通常不会发生；以及 证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因。\n\n在本案中，若无操作或维护自动扶梯的人员存在过失，自动扶梯通常不会发生故障，且没有证据表明购物者受伤存在其他可能原因。\n\n因此，事实认定者可以推断，故障是由商店或承包商的过失造成的，且该过失导致了购物者的受伤（选项 D）。\n\n因此，现有证据在法律上足以支持购物者基于上述任一理论获得赔偿，法院应驳回商店提出的指示裁决动议。\n\n（选项 B）严格责任适用于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）、特定动物相关情形以及异常危险活动。\n\n但在本案中，购物者并非因商店销售的产品（不涉及严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））、动物或异常危险活动而受伤。\n\n教学目标：被告可能因（1）其自身的过失而承担直接责任，或（2）因独立承包商的过失而承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（若该承包商的工作涉及被告负有的不可转让义务——例如，企业有义务为其受邀者维护场所安全）。\n\n参考文献 《联邦民事诉讼规则》第50(a)条（法律问题判决）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第6条（美国法律协会，2010年）（过失的直接责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第62条（美国法律协会，2012年）（独立承包商过失的替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第17条（美国法律协会，2010年）（“事实本身说明问题”原则/res ipsa loquitur）。","conclusion":"答案 A：否，因为事实认定者可以推断扶梯故障是由于商店的疏忽造成的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"strict products liability","ipa":"/stɹˈɪkt pɹˈɑdəkts lˌaɪʌbˈɪlɪti/","syllables":"strict prod·ucts li·a·bil·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"严格产品责任：商业供应链被告对缺陷产品造成的损害承担无过错责任","contextualMeaning":"严格产品责任：商业供应链被告对缺陷产品造成的损害承担无过错责任","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) Strict liability applies to strict products liability, certain animals, and abnormally dangerous activities.","synonyms":["strict product liability"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-1a7701a2bfedb2a2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":89,"sourceQuestionPage":268,"sourceQuestionPages":[268],"sourceExplanationPages":[269,270],"sourceAnswerPages":[269,270],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-090","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A customer bought a can of corn at a grocery store. While eating the corn later that evening, the customer was injured by a small piece of glass in the corn. The customer sued the canning company that had processed and canned the corn. At trial, the customer presented evidence that neither the customer nor any third party had done anything after the can of corn was opened that would account for the presence of the glass. Without any other evidence, is the customer likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because it is possible that someone tampered with the can before the customer bought it.","B":"No, because the customer has not shown any direct evidence that the canning company acted negligently.","C":"Yes, because a jury may reasonably infer that the canning company acted negligently.","D":"Yes, because the grocery store could not have discovered the piece of glass by reasonable inspection."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A plaintiff harmed by a defective product can prevail on a negligence claim against a commercial or noncommercial seller when the plaintiff can prove that the seller's failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff harm. But when there is no direct evidence of the seller's failure to use reasonable care, the seller's negligence will be inferred under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur if: the plaintiff was harmed by a type of accident that is usually caused by negligence of a seller in the defendant's position (eg, manufacturer) and\n\nthe evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of the plaintiff's harm.\n\nHere, the customer was injured by a piece of glass inside a can of corn—an accident usually caused by a canner's negligence. And by presenting evidence that neither he nor a third party was responsible for the presence of the glass, the customer eliminated other potential causes of the harmful glass's presence. Therefore, the canning company's negligence can be inferred under res ipsa loquitur, and the customer is likely to prevail. (Choice A) It is possible that someone tampered with the can, but there is no evidence to support that contention. Instead, the canning company's negligence is the most likely explanation for the glass's presence. (Choice B) Direct evidence of the canning company's negligence is unnecessary because its negligence can be inferred from indirect evidence under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur. (Choice D) Although the grocery store had a duty to use reasonable care to discover obvious defects in its canned corn, that duty does not eliminate the canning company's prior negligence in sealing the glass in the can.\n\nEducational objective: Res ipsa loquitur allows the defendant's negligence to be inferred when (1) the plaintiff's harm resulted from a type of accident that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of the plaintiff's harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 6 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence liability).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (res ipsa loquitur).","educationalObjective":"Res ipsa loquitur allows the defendant's negligence to be inferred when (1) the plaintiff's harm resulted from a type of accident that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of the plaintiff's harm.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 6 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence liability).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (res ipsa loquitur).","rule":"Res ipsa loquitur allows the defendant's negligence to be inferred when (1) the plaintiff's harm resulted from a type of accident that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of the plaintiff's harm.","irac":{"issue":"Without any other evidence, is the customer likely to prevail?","rule":"Res ipsa loquitur allows the defendant's negligence to be inferred when (1) the plaintiff's harm resulted from a type of accident that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of the plaintiff's harm.","analysis":"A plaintiff harmed by a defective product can prevail on a negligence claim against a commercial or noncommercial seller when the plaintiff can prove that the seller's failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff harm. But when there is no direct evidence of the seller's failure to use reasonable care, the seller's negligence will be inferred under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur if: the plaintiff was harmed by a type of accident that is usually caused by negligence of a seller in the defendant's position (eg, manufacturer) and\n\nthe evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of the plaintiff's harm.\n\nHere, the customer was injured by a piece of glass inside a can of corn—an accident usually caused by a canner's negligence. And by presenting evidence that neither he nor a third party was responsible for the presence of the glass, the customer eliminated other potential causes of the harmful glass's presence. Therefore, the canning company's negligence can be inferred under res ipsa loquitur, and the customer is likely to prevail. (Choice A) It is possible that someone tampered with the can, but there is no evidence to support that contention. Instead, the canning company's negligence is the most likely explanation for the glass's presence. (Choice B) Direct evidence of the canning company's negligence is unnecessary because its negligence can be inferred from indirect evidence under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur. (Choice D) Although the grocery store had a duty to use reasonable care to discover obvious defects in its canned corn, that duty does not eliminate the canning company's prior negligence in sealing the glass in the can.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because a jury may reasonably infer that the canning company acted negligently."},"questionZh":"一位顾客在杂货店买了一罐玉米。\n\n当晚晚些时候，顾客在吃玉米时被玉米罐头里的一小块玻璃划伤。\n\n顾客起诉了加工并罐装这罐玉米的罐头公司。\n\n庭审中，顾客提供的证据表明，罐头打开后，无论是顾客本人还是任何第三方都没有做任何可能导致玻璃碎片出现的事情。\n\n在没有其他证据的情况下，顾客胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为有可能有人在顾客购买之前动过手脚。","B":"不可能，因为顾客没有提供任何直接证据证明罐头公司存在过失。","C":"可能，因为陪审团可以合理推断罐头公司存在过失。","D":"可能，因为杂货店通过合理的检查不可能发现这块玻璃碎片。"},"explanationZh":"因缺陷产品而受害的原告，若能证明销售商（无论是商业性还是非商业性销售商）因未尽合理注意义务而导致其受损，便可在针对该销售商提起的过失索赔诉讼中胜诉。\n\n然而，在缺乏销售商未尽合理注意义务的直接证据时，若满足以下条件，则可根据“事实本身说明一切”（res ipsa loquitur）原则推断销售商存在过失：原告所受伤害源于某种通常由处于被告地位者（如制造商）的过失所引发的事故；且 证据倾向于排除导致原告受害的其他潜在原因。\n\n在本案中，顾客因玉米罐头内的一块玻璃而受伤——此类事故通常由罐头生产商的过失引起。\n\n此外，通过提供证据证明其本人及第三方均未导致玻璃的出现，该顾客排除了导致有害玻璃存在的其他潜在原因。\n\n因此，根据“事实本身说明一切”原则，可以推断罐头公司存在过失，且该顾客很可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）尽管可能有人对罐头进行了篡改，但并无证据支持这一主张。\n\n相比之下，罐头公司的过失是导致玻璃出现的最合理解释。\n\n（选项 B）无需提供罐头公司过失的直接证据，因为根据“事实本身说明一切”原则，可从间接证据中推断出其过失。\n\n（选项 D）尽管杂货店有义务尽合理注意以发现其罐装玉米中的明显缺陷，但该义务并不免除罐头公司在将玻璃密封于罐内时所犯的先前过失。\n\n教学目标：“事实本身说明一切”（res ipsa loquitur）原则允许在以下情况下推断被告存在过失：(1) 原告受到的伤害源于某种通常由处于被告地位者的过失所引发的事故；以及 (2) 证据倾向于排除导致原告受害的其他潜在原因。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 6 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（过失责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》\n\n身体和情感伤害第 17 条（Am. Law Inst. 2010）（res ipsa loquitur）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“事实本身说明问题”原则允许在以下情况下推定被告存在过失：(1) 原告的损害是由通常由被告这种身份的人的过失造成的事故类型所致；(2) 证据倾向于排除其他可能导致原告损害的原因。","iracZh":{"issue":"在没有任何其他证据的情况下，客户是否有可能胜诉？","rule":"“事实本身说明问题”原则允许在以下情况下推定被告存在过失：(1) 原告的损害是由通常由被告这种身份的人的过失造成的事故类型所致；(2) 证据倾向于排除其他可能导致原告损害的原因。","analysis":"因缺陷产品而受害的原告，若能证明销售商（无论是商业性还是非商业性销售商）因未尽合理注意义务而导致其受损，便可在针对该销售商提起的过失索赔诉讼中胜诉。\n\n然而，在缺乏销售商未尽合理注意义务的直接证据时，若满足以下条件，则可根据“事实本身说明一切”（res ipsa loquitur）原则推断销售商存在过失：原告所受伤害源于某种通常由处于被告地位者（如制造商）的过失所引发的事故；且 证据倾向于排除导致原告受害的其他潜在原因。\n\n在本案中，顾客因玉米罐头内的一块玻璃而受伤——此类事故通常由罐头生产商的过失引起。\n\n此外，通过提供证据证明其本人及第三方均未导致玻璃的出现，该顾客排除了导致有害玻璃存在的其他潜在原因。\n\n因此，根据“事实本身说明一切”原则，可以推断罐头公司存在过失，且该顾客很可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）尽管可能有人对罐头进行了篡改，但并无证据支持这一主张。\n\n相比之下，罐头公司的过失是导致玻璃出现的最合理解释。\n\n（选项 B）无需提供罐头公司过失的直接证据，因为根据“事实本身说明一切”原则，可从间接证据中推断出其过失。\n\n（选项 D）尽管杂货店有义务尽合理注意以发现其罐装玉米中的明显缺陷，但该义务并不免除罐头公司在将玻璃密封于罐内时所犯的先前过失。\n\n教学目标：“事实本身说明一切”（res ipsa loquitur）原则允许在以下情况下推断被告存在过失：(1) 原告受到的伤害源于某种通常由处于被告地位者的过失所引发的事故；以及 (2) 证据倾向于排除导致原告受害的其他潜在原因。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 6 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（过失责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》\n\n身体和情感伤害第 17 条（Am. Law Inst. 2010）（res ipsa loquitur）。","conclusion":"答案 C：可能，因为陪审团可以合理推断罐头公司存在过失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"commercial seller","ipa":"/kʌmˈɝʃəl sˈɛlɝ/","syllables":"com·mer·cial seller","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"商业卖方：日常经营中销售或分销相关产品的人","contextualMeaning":"商业卖方：日常经营中销售或分销相关产品的人","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A plaintiff harmed by a defective product can prevail on a negligence claim against a commercial or noncommercial seller when the plaintiff can prove that the seller's failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff harm.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"manufacturer","ipa":"/mˌænjʌfˈæktʃɝɝ/","syllables":"man·u·fac·turer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 制造业者, 厂商；[经] 制造人, 制造商, 制造厂","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“manufacturer”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:manufacturers","sourceSentence":"But when there is no direct evidence of the seller's failure to use reasonable care, the seller's negligence will be inferred under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur if: the plaintiff was harmed by a type of accident that is usually caused by negligence of a seller in the defendant's position (eg, manufacturer) and the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of the plaintiff's harm.","synonyms":["maker","manufacturing business","producer"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-62019772e0bdbfce"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":90,"sourceQuestionPage":271,"sourceQuestionPages":[271],"sourceExplanationPages":[272,273],"sourceAnswerPages":[272,273],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-091","subject":"Torts","topic":"Actual & Proximate Cause","question":"A man and a woman had been dating for two years and decided to go to a baseball game for their anniversary. Before the game started, the woman decided to listen to music on her headphones while the man went to get snacks. Another fan, bored before the game, found a baseball in the stands and tossed it as hard as he could into the air. As the ball came back down, despite the fan's attempt to get the woman's attention, the ball hit her in the head, causing her to collapse. When the man returned, he administered CPR to resuscitate the woman while waiting on an ambulance. However, the man did not know CPR and broke the woman's ribs in the process. The paramedics then transported the woman to the hospital, where a triage nurse did a head-to-toe examination and determined that the woman did not need immediate medical attention. While waiting to see the doctor, the woman suffered a brain hemorrhage. Which of the woman's injuries will the fan be liable for causing?","choices":{"A":"None, because the woman was contributorily negligent by wearing headphones at the ballpark.","B":"Head injury, because the man's negligent administration of CPR was a superseding cause that cut off the fan's liability.","C":"Head injury and broken ribs, because the triage nurse's specialized training imposed a higher duty of care that, when breached, cut off the fan's liability.","D":"Head injury, broken ribs, and brain hemorrhage, because all of the injuries were a foreseeable result of the fan's actions."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Intervening causes\n\nForeseeable Foreseeable intervening act or force that contributes to plaintiff's harm cause Defendant remains liable Superseding Unforeseeable intervening act or force that breaks chain of causation cause between defendant's tortious conduct & plaintiff's harm Defendant is not liable Negligence requires proof of the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. To prove causation, the plaintiff (the woman) must show that the defendant (the fan) was the actual and proximate cause of the plaintiff's injuries: Actual (factual) cause – the plaintiff's harm would not have occurred \"but for\" the defendant's actions. Here, the head injury would not have occurred if the fan had not thrown the ball in the air.\n\nProximate (legal) cause – the plaintiff's harm was a reasonably foreseeable consequence of the defendant's conduct.* Here, it was reasonably foreseeable that throwing a ball in the air could result in the ball injuring someone nearby.\n\nHowever, the chain of proximate causation can be broken by a superseding cause—ie, an unforeseeable, intervening act or force that occurred after the defendant's negligence and contributed to the plaintiff's harm. But negligent intervening acts (eg, medical malpractice) are typically regarded as foreseeable and therefore do not break the chain of proximate causation. Here, the man may have been negligent when he administered CPR since he did not know how to do so (Choice B). And the triage nurse may have committed medical malpractice when she incorrectly assessed the woman's need for medical attention (Choice C). But since their negligence is considered foreseeable, it does not break the chain of proximate causation. Therefore, the fan will be liable for causing all of the woman's injuries. *This is the majority rule for proximate causation that should be applied unless the question states otherwise. (Choice A) In a pure comparative-fault jurisdiction (default rule on the MBE), a plaintiff's contributory negligence is not a complete bar to recovery. Instead, the plaintiff's full damages are calculated by the trier of fact and then reduced by the proportion that the plaintiff's fault bears to the total harm.\n\nEducational objective: Superseding causes—ie, unforeseeable, intervening acts that occur after the defendant's negligence and contribute to the plaintiff's harm—break the chain of proximate causation. But negligent intervening acts (eg, medical malpractice) are typically regarded as foreseeable and therefore do not cut off the defendant's liability.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 447 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (negligence of intervening acts).","educationalObjective":"Superseding causes—ie, unforeseeable, intervening acts that occur after the defendant's negligence and contribute to the plaintiff's harm—break the chain of proximate causation. But negligent intervening acts (eg, medical malpractice) are typically regarded as foreseeable and therefore do not cut off the defendant's liability.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 447 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (negligence of intervening acts).","rule":"Superseding causes—ie, unforeseeable, intervening acts that occur after the defendant's negligence and contribute to the plaintiff's harm—break the chain of proximate causation. But negligent intervening acts (eg, medical malpractice) are typically regarded as foreseeable and therefore do not cut off the defendant's liability.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the woman's injuries will the fan be liable for causing?","rule":"Superseding causes—ie, unforeseeable, intervening acts that occur after the defendant's negligence and contribute to the plaintiff's harm—break the chain of proximate causation. But negligent intervening acts (eg, medical malpractice) are typically regarded as foreseeable and therefore do not cut off the defendant's liability.","analysis":"Intervening causes\n\nForeseeable Foreseeable intervening act or force that contributes to plaintiff's harm cause Defendant remains liable Superseding Unforeseeable intervening act or force that breaks chain of causation cause between defendant's tortious conduct & plaintiff's harm Defendant is not liable Negligence requires proof of the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. To prove causation, the plaintiff (the woman) must show that the defendant (the fan) was the actual and proximate cause of the plaintiff's injuries: Actual (factual) cause – the plaintiff's harm would not have occurred \"but for\" the defendant's actions. Here, the head injury would not have occurred if the fan had not thrown the ball in the air.\n\nProximate (legal) cause – the plaintiff's harm was a reasonably foreseeable consequence of the defendant's conduct.* Here, it was reasonably foreseeable that throwing a ball in the air could result in the ball injuring someone nearby.\n\nHowever, the chain of proximate causation can be broken by a superseding cause—ie, an unforeseeable, intervening act or force that occurred after the defendant's negligence and contributed to the plaintiff's harm. But negligent intervening acts (eg, medical malpractice) are typically regarded as foreseeable and therefore do not break the chain of proximate causation. Here, the man may have been negligent when he administered CPR since he did not know how to do so (Choice B). And the triage nurse may have committed medical malpractice when she incorrectly assessed the woman's need for medical attention (Choice C). But since their negligence is considered foreseeable, it does not break the chain of proximate causation. Therefore, the fan will be liable for causing all of the woman's injuries. *This is the majority rule for proximate causation that should be applied unless the question states otherwise. (Choice A) In a pure comparative-fault jurisdiction (default rule on the MBE), a plaintiff's contributory negligence is not a complete bar to recovery. Instead, the plaintiff's full damages are calculated by the trier of fact and then reduced by the proportion that the plaintiff's fault bears to the total harm.","conclusion":"Answer D: Head injury, broken ribs, and brain hemorrhage, because all of the injuries were a foreseeable result of the fan's actions."},"questionZh":"一对男女交往了两年，决定在纪念日去观看一场棒球比赛。\n\n比赛开始前，当男子去买零食时，女子戴上耳机听起了音乐。\n\n另一位球迷因赛前无聊，在看台上捡到一颗棒球，并用力将其抛向空中。\n\n当球落下时，尽管该球迷试图引起女子的注意，球还是击中了她的头部，导致她昏倒。\n\n男子回来后，在等待救护车期间对女子实施了心肺复苏术（CPR）以进行急救。\n\n然而，该男子并不懂心肺复苏术，操作过程中导致女子肋骨骨折。\n\n随后，急救人员将女子送往医院，分诊护士对其进行了全身检查，判定她无需立即接受医疗救治。\n\n在等待就诊期间，女子发生了脑出血。\n\n该球迷需对女子的哪些伤害承担责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"无需承担任何责任，因为女子在棒球场戴耳机的行为构成了与有过失（contributory negligence）（contributory negligence）。","B":"仅需对头部受伤承担责任，因为男子实施心肺复苏术时的过失行为构成了一种“阻断性原因”（superseding cause），从而切断了该球迷的责任链条。","C":"需对头部受伤和肋骨骨折承担责任，因为分诊护士受过专业训练，负有更高的注意义务；一旦违反该义务，便会切断该球迷的责任链条。","D":"需对头部受伤、肋骨骨折和脑出血承担责任，因为所有这些伤害都是该球迷行为可预见的后果。"},"explanationZh":"介入原因 可预见的介入原因：导致原告受害的可预见的介入行为或力量；被告仍需承担责任。阻断性原因（Superseding cause）：切断被告侵权行为与原告伤害之间因果关系链条的不可预见的介入行为或力量；被告无需承担责任。构成过失侵权需证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系及损害后果。\n\n为证明因果关系，原告（该女子）必须证明被告（该球迷）是导致其受伤的实际原因和法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）：实际原因（事实因果关系）——若无被告的行为，原告的伤害便不会发生。\n\n在本案中，若该球迷未将球抛向空中，头部受伤事件便不会发生。\n\n法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）——原告受到的伤害是被告行为可合理预见的后果。* 在本案中，将球抛向空中可能导致球击伤附近人员，这一后果是可合理预见的。\n\n然而，法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）链条可能被“阻断性原因”切断——即发生在被告过失行为之后、促成原告伤害的不可预见的介入行为或力量。\n\n但过失性的介入行为（例如医疗事故）通常被视为可预见的，因此不会切断法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）链条。\n\n在本案中，该男子在实施心肺复苏（CPR）时可能存在过失，因为他并不掌握相关操作方法（选项 B）；分诊护士在错误评估该女子医疗需求时也可能存在医疗过失（选项 C）。\n\n但由于这些过失被视为可预见的，它们并未切断法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）链条。\n\n因此，该球迷须对该女子遭受的所有伤害承担责任。\n\n* 除非题目另有说明，否则应适用关于法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）的多数派规则。\n\n（选项 A）在纯粹比较过失管辖区（MBE 考试中的默认规则），原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）（contributory negligence）并不完全阻碍其获得赔偿。\n\n相反，事实认定者会计算原告遭受的全部损害赔偿额，然后根据原告过失在总损害中所占的比例扣减相应金额。\n\n教学目标：阻断因果关系的中断事由（即发生在被告过失行为之后、促成原告损害且不可预见的介入行为）会切断法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）链条。\n\n然而，具有过失性质的介入行为（例如医疗事故）通常被视为可预见的，因此不会免除被告的责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第447条（美国法律协会，1965年）（关于介入行为的过失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“阻断性原因”（即发生在被告过失行为之后、且促成原告受损的不可预见的介入行为）会切断法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（proximate causation）链条。\n\n然而，过失性的介入行为（例如医疗事故）通常被视为可预见的，因此不会切断被告的责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"该球迷需对女子的哪些伤害承担责任？","rule":"“阻断性原因”（即发生在被告过失行为之后、且促成原告受损的不可预见的介入行为）会切断法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（proximate causation）链条。\n\n然而，过失性的介入行为（例如医疗事故）通常被视为可预见的，因此不会切断被告的责任。","analysis":"介入原因 可预见的介入原因：导致原告受害的可预见的介入行为或力量；被告仍需承担责任。阻断性原因（Superseding cause）：切断被告侵权行为与原告伤害之间因果关系链条的不可预见的介入行为或力量；被告无需承担责任。构成过失侵权需证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系及损害后果。\n\n为证明因果关系，原告（该女子）必须证明被告（该球迷）是导致其受伤的实际原因和法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）：实际原因（事实因果关系）——若无被告的行为，原告的伤害便不会发生。\n\n在本案中，若该球迷未将球抛向空中，头部受伤事件便不会发生。\n\n法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）——原告受到的伤害是被告行为可合理预见的后果。* 在本案中，将球抛向空中可能导致球击伤附近人员，这一后果是可合理预见的。\n\n然而，法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）链条可能被“阻断性原因”切断——即发生在被告过失行为之后、促成原告伤害的不可预见的介入行为或力量。\n\n但过失性的介入行为（例如医疗事故）通常被视为可预见的，因此不会切断法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）链条。\n\n在本案中，该男子在实施心肺复苏（CPR）时可能存在过失，因为他并不掌握相关操作方法（选项 B）；分诊护士在错误评估该女子医疗需求时也可能存在医疗过失（选项 C）。\n\n但由于这些过失被视为可预见的，它们并未切断法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）链条。\n\n因此，该球迷须对该女子遭受的所有伤害承担责任。\n\n* 除非题目另有说明，否则应适用关于法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）的多数派规则。\n\n（选项 A）在纯粹比较过失管辖区（MBE 考试中的默认规则），原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）（contributory negligence）并不完全阻碍其获得赔偿。\n\n相反，事实认定者会计算原告遭受的全部损害赔偿额，然后根据原告过失在总损害中所占的比例扣减相应金额。\n\n教学目标：阻断因果关系的中断事由（即发生在被告过失行为之后、促成原告损害且不可预见的介入行为）会切断法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）链条。\n\n然而，具有过失性质的介入行为（例如医疗事故）通常被视为可预见的，因此不会免除被告的责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第447条（美国法律协会，1965年）（关于介入行为的过失）。","conclusion":"答案 D：需对头部受伤、肋骨骨折和脑出血承担责任，因为所有这些伤害都是该球迷行为可预见的后果。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"foreseeable intervening act","ipa":"/fɔɹsˈiəbəl ˌɪntɝvˈinɪŋ ˈækt/","syllables":"fore·see·able in·ter·ven·ing act","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"可预见介入行为：通常不切断法律近因的后续行为","contextualMeaning":"可预见介入行为：通常不切断法律近因的后续行为","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Intervening causes Foreseeable Foreseeable intervening act or force that contributes to plaintiff's harm cause Defendant remains liable Superseding Unforeseeable intervening act or force that breaks chain of causation cause between defendant's tortious conduct & plaintiff's harm Defendant is not liable Negligence requires proof of the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"intervening cause","ipa":"/ˌɪntɝvˈinɪŋ kˈɑz/","syllables":"in·ter·ven·ing cause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"介入原因：发生在被告行为之后并参与造成损害的事件","contextualMeaning":"介入原因：发生在被告行为之后并参与造成损害的事件","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Intervening causes Foreseeable Foreseeable intervening act or force that contributes to plaintiff's harm cause Defendant remains liable Superseding Unforeseeable intervening act or force that breaks chain of causation cause between defendant's tortious conduct & plaintiff's harm Defendant is not liable Negligence requires proof of the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":91,"sourceQuestionPage":274,"sourceQuestionPages":[274],"sourceExplanationPages":[275,276],"sourceAnswerPages":[275,276],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-092","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A grandmother had her grandson and his friend, both eight years old, over to visit at her house. The grandson and his friend explored her home and discovered a hunting rifle in an unlocked gun cabinet. They removed it from the cabinet and were examining it when the rifle, while in the grandson's hands, somehow discharged. The bullet struck and injured his friend. The gun cabinet was normally locked, but the grandmother had opened it for dusting several days before the boys' visit and had then forgotten to relock it. She was not aware that it was unlocked when the boys arrived. The friend brought a suit against the grandmother to recover for his injuries. The friend introduced evidence supporting all the facts listed above. At the end of the friend's case-in- chief, the grandmother moved for a directed verdict. The jurisdiction has abolished traditional rules of landowner liability. How should the court rule on the grandmother's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because a jury could find that the grandmother breached a duty of care she owed to the friend.","B":"Deny the motion, because keeping a firearm in one's home is an abnormally dangerous activity.","C":"Grant the motion, because the grandmother did not recall that the gun cabinet was unlocked.","D":"Grant the motion, because the grandmother is not legally responsible for the acts of her grandson."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A court should grant a defendant's motion for a directed verdict if the plaintiff fails to present legally sufficient evidence to support every element of his/her claim—ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor. In a negligence claim—the typical claim for unintended physical harm—the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant owed the plaintiff a duty. The defendant breached that duty. The defendant's breach caused the plaintiff's harm. The plaintiff is entitled to damages (ie, suffered physical harm). Traditionally, the duty owed to land entrants depends on their status on the land (eg, licensee). But under the modern approach—adopted in this jurisdiction and about half the others—land possessors owe all land entrants (except flagrant trespassers) a duty of reasonable care. Here, the grandmother (land possessor) owed a duty of reasonable care to her grandson's friend because he was visiting her home (land entrant). A reasonable jury could find that she breached that duty by failing to lock the gun cabinet, which ultimately caused the friend to get shot and suffer damages. As a result, the court should deny her motion for a directed verdict and allow the case to proceed. (Choice B) Keeping a firearm in one's home is not an abnormally dangerous activity that subjects the actor to strict liability (ie, liability regardless of fault). That is because it is a common activity whose risks can be mitigated by reasonable care. (Choice C) The fact that the grandmother did not recall that the gun cabinet was unlocked does not shield her from liability for negligence since she owed the friend a duty to ensure that it was locked. (Choice D) The grandmother is not legally responsible for her grandson's acts because they do not share a special relationship (eg, parent-child). But she is responsible for her own failure to lock the gun cabinet.\n\nEducational objective: Under the modern approach to land-possessor liability, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants (except flagrant trespassers).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 51 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (general duty of land possessors).","educationalObjective":"Under the modern approach to land-possessor liability, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants (except flagrant trespassers).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts § 51 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (general duty of land possessors).","rule":"Under the modern approach to land-possessor liability, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants (except flagrant trespassers).","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule on the grandmother's motion?","rule":"Under the modern approach to land-possessor liability, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants (except flagrant trespassers).","analysis":"A court should grant a defendant's motion for a directed verdict if the plaintiff fails to present legally sufficient evidence to support every element of his/her claim—ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor. In a negligence claim—the typical claim for unintended physical harm—the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant owed the plaintiff a duty. The defendant breached that duty. The defendant's breach caused the plaintiff's harm. The plaintiff is entitled to damages (ie, suffered physical harm). Traditionally, the duty owed to land entrants depends on their status on the land (eg, licensee). But under the modern approach—adopted in this jurisdiction and about half the others—land possessors owe all land entrants (except flagrant trespassers) a duty of reasonable care. Here, the grandmother (land possessor) owed a duty of reasonable care to her grandson's friend because he was visiting her home (land entrant). A reasonable jury could find that she breached that duty by failing to lock the gun cabinet, which ultimately caused the friend to get shot and suffer damages. As a result, the court should deny her motion for a directed verdict and allow the case to proceed. (Choice B) Keeping a firearm in one's home is not an abnormally dangerous activity that subjects the actor to strict liability (ie, liability regardless of fault). That is because it is a common activity whose risks can be mitigated by reasonable care. (Choice C) The fact that the grandmother did not recall that the gun cabinet was unlocked does not shield her from liability for negligence since she owed the friend a duty to ensure that it was locked. (Choice D) The grandmother is not legally responsible for her grandson's acts because they do not share a special relationship (eg, parent-child). But she is responsible for her own failure to lock the gun cabinet.","conclusion":"Answer A: Deny the motion, because a jury could find that the grandmother breached a duty of care she owed to the friend."},"questionZh":"一位祖母邀请了她八岁的孙子及其朋友来家中做客。\n\n两个孩子在屋里玩耍时，发现了一个未上锁的枪柜，里面放着一支猎枪。\n\n他们把枪取出来查看，结果枪在孙子手中意外走火，子弹击伤了那位朋友。\n\n该枪柜平时是上锁的，但祖母在孩子们来访前几天曾打开柜子除尘，之后忘了重新上锁；孩子们到达时，她并不知道柜子处于未上锁状态。\n\n受伤的朋友对祖母提起诉讼，要求赔偿损失，并提交了证据证实上述所有事实。\n\n在原告（即那位朋友）完成举证后，祖母提出了“指示裁决”（directed verdict）的动议。\n\n该司法管辖区已废除了关于土地所有者责任的传统规则。\n\n法院应如何裁决祖母的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为陪审团可能认定祖母违反了她对该朋友应尽的注意义务。","B":"驳回动议，因为在家中存放枪支属于异常危险的活动。","C":"批准动议，因为祖母不记得枪柜未上锁。","D":"批准动议，因为祖母无需对其孙子的行为承担法律责任。"},"explanationZh":"如果原告未能提供足以在法律上支持其主张各项构成要件的证据——即没有理性的陪审团会做出有利于原告的裁决——法院应准予被告提出的指示裁决（directed verdict）动议。\n\n在过失索赔（即针对非故意造成的人身伤害的典型索赔）中，原告必须证明以下所有事项：被告对原告负有注意义务；被告违反了该义务；被告的违约行为导致了原告的伤害；原告有权获得损害赔偿（即遭受了人身伤害）。\n\n传统上，对进入土地者所负的义务取决于其在土地上的身份（例如，被许可人）。\n\n但在现代做法下——本司法管辖区及其他约半数司法管辖区均采用此做法——土地占有者对所有进入土地者（公然的非法侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n在本案中，祖母（土地占有者）对其孙子的朋友（进入土地者）负有合理注意义务，因为该朋友当时正在造访她的住所。\n\n理性的陪审团可能会认定，她因未锁好枪柜而违反了该义务，并最终导致该朋友中枪受伤。\n\n因此，法院应驳回她关于指示裁决的动议，并允许案件继续审理。\n\n（选项 B）在家中存放枪支并非一种会导致行为人承担严格责任（即无论有无过错均须承担责任）的异常危险活动。\n\n这是因为这是一种常见活动，其风险可以通过合理的注意措施加以降低。\n\n（选项 C）祖母不记得枪柜未上锁这一事实，并不能免除其过失责任，因为她对该朋友负有确保枪柜上锁的义务。\n\n（选项 D）祖母不对其孙子的行为承担法律责任，因为两人之间不存在特殊关系（例如，亲子关系）。\n\n但她必须对其自身未锁好枪柜的行为承担责任。\n\n教育目标：根据关于土地占有者责任的现代做法，土地占有者对所有进入土地者（公然的非法侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 51 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（土地占有者的一般义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据关于土地占有者责任的现代法律原则，土地占有者对所有进入土地的人（公然的非法入侵者除外）均负有合理的注意义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应如何裁决祖母的动议？","rule":"根据关于土地占有者责任的现代法律原则，土地占有者对所有进入土地的人（公然的非法入侵者除外）均负有合理的注意义务。","analysis":"如果原告未能提供足以在法律上支持其主张各项构成要件的证据——即没有理性的陪审团会做出有利于原告的裁决——法院应准予被告提出的指示裁决（directed verdict）动议。\n\n在过失索赔（即针对非故意造成的人身伤害的典型索赔）中，原告必须证明以下所有事项：被告对原告负有注意义务；被告违反了该义务；被告的违约行为导致了原告的伤害；原告有权获得损害赔偿（即遭受了人身伤害）。\n\n传统上，对进入土地者所负的义务取决于其在土地上的身份（例如，被许可人）。\n\n但在现代做法下——本司法管辖区及其他约半数司法管辖区均采用此做法——土地占有者对所有进入土地者（公然的非法侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n在本案中，祖母（土地占有者）对其孙子的朋友（进入土地者）负有合理注意义务，因为该朋友当时正在造访她的住所。\n\n理性的陪审团可能会认定，她因未锁好枪柜而违反了该义务，并最终导致该朋友中枪受伤。\n\n因此，法院应驳回她关于指示裁决的动议，并允许案件继续审理。\n\n（选项 B）在家中存放枪支并非一种会导致行为人承担严格责任（即无论有无过错均须承担责任）的异常危险活动。\n\n这是因为这是一种常见活动，其风险可以通过合理的注意措施加以降低。\n\n（选项 C）祖母不记得枪柜未上锁这一事实，并不能免除其过失责任，因为她对该朋友负有确保枪柜上锁的义务。\n\n（选项 D）祖母不对其孙子的行为承担法律责任，因为两人之间不存在特殊关系（例如，亲子关系）。\n\n但她必须对其自身未锁好枪柜的行为承担责任。\n\n教育目标：根据关于土地占有者责任的现代做法，土地占有者对所有进入土地者（公然的非法侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 51 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（土地占有者的一般义务）。","conclusion":"答案 A：驳回动议，因为陪审团可能认定祖母违反了她对该朋友应尽的注意义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-908e346bd886c771"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":92,"sourceQuestionPage":277,"sourceQuestionPages":[277],"sourceExplanationPages":[278,279],"sourceAnswerPages":[278,279],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-093","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Breach & Proof","question":"A hiker sustained a head injury when he was struck by a limb that fell from a tree. At the time of his injury, the hiker was walking through a forest on private property without the property owner's knowledge or permission. It was determined that the limb fell because the tree was infested with termites. In an action by the hiker against the property owner to recover for his head injury, will the hiker likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the property owner breached no duty to the hiker, who was a trespasser.","B":"No, because the property owner could not foresee that anyone would be injured.","C":"Yes, because the property owner had a duty to prevent the trees on his property from becoming dangerous.","D":"Yes, because the property owner is liable for hidden dangers on his property."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Land possessor's duty to land entrants\n\n(traditional approach)*\n\nStatus Characteristics Duty\n\nTrespasser Intentionally enters land Known or frequent trespassers – warn of without permission known artificial dangers & use reasonable care in active operations Unknown or unanticipated trespassers – no duty Licensee Enters land with permission Warn of known latent dangers & use (eg, social guest) reasonable care in active operations Invitee Enters land open to public Inspect for unknown dangers (eg, churchgoer) Make premises safe or provide adequate Enters land for business warnings purpose (eg, store customer) Prevent harm from active operations *In jurisdictions that follow the modern approach, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants, except flagrant trespassers.\n\nLand possessors are in the best position to prevent harm to those who enter their land. As a result, they owe a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants. But no such duty is owed to trespassers—ie, persons who intentionally enter another's land without permission—unless the land possessor knows of, or has reason to anticipate, their presence on the land. Here, the hiker was injured by a falling tree limb while walking through a forest on private property. The hiker was a trespasser because he intentionally entered the forest without the property owner's permission. And since there is no evidence that the owner knew of— or had reason to anticipate—the hiker's presence, he was an unforeseeable trespasser. As a result, the owner owed no duty to the hiker and the hiker will not recover for his injury. (Choice B) Had the hiker been an invitee, the owner would have owed him a duty to make reasonable inspections of the property. In that case, the owner likely would have discovered the weak tree limbs and foreseen that they could injure someone. But the owner owed no such duty to the hiker since he was a trespasser. (Choice C) The owner would have owed the hiker a duty to prevent the trees on his property from becoming dangerous (or to provide adequate warnings) had the hiker been an invitee—not a trespasser.\n\n(Choice D) Land possessors must warn discovered or anticipated trespassers about hidden, artificial (ie, man-made) dangers on the land of which the land possessor is aware. But even if the hiker had been such a trespasser, the owner would not be liable since he had no knowledge of this hidden, natural danger.\n\nEducational objective: A land possessor owes a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants. But no such duty is owed to unforeseeable land entrants (eg, undiscovered or unanticipated trespassers).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 333 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (no duty to unknown trespassers).","educationalObjective":"A land possessor owes a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants. But no such duty is owed to unforeseeable land entrants (eg, undiscovered or unanticipated trespassers).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 333 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (no duty to unknown trespassers).","rule":"A land possessor owes a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants. But no such duty is owed to unforeseeable land entrants (eg, undiscovered or unanticipated trespassers).","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the hiker against the property owner to recover for his head injury, will the hiker likely prevail?","rule":"A land possessor owes a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants. But no such duty is owed to unforeseeable land entrants (eg, undiscovered or unanticipated trespassers).","analysis":"Land possessor's duty to land entrants\n\n(traditional approach)*\n\nStatus Characteristics Duty\n\nTrespasser Intentionally enters land Known or frequent trespassers – warn of without permission known artificial dangers & use reasonable care in active operations Unknown or unanticipated trespassers – no duty Licensee Enters land with permission Warn of known latent dangers & use (eg, social guest) reasonable care in active operations Invitee Enters land open to public Inspect for unknown dangers (eg, churchgoer) Make premises safe or provide adequate Enters land for business warnings purpose (eg, store customer) Prevent harm from active operations *In jurisdictions that follow the modern approach, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants, except flagrant trespassers.\n\nLand possessors are in the best position to prevent harm to those who enter their land. As a result, they owe a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants. But no such duty is owed to trespassers—ie, persons who intentionally enter another's land without permission—unless the land possessor knows of, or has reason to anticipate, their presence on the land. Here, the hiker was injured by a falling tree limb while walking through a forest on private property. The hiker was a trespasser because he intentionally entered the forest without the property owner's permission. And since there is no evidence that the owner knew of— or had reason to anticipate—the hiker's presence, he was an unforeseeable trespasser. As a result, the owner owed no duty to the hiker and the hiker will not recover for his injury. (Choice B) Had the hiker been an invitee, the owner would have owed him a duty to make reasonable inspections of the property. In that case, the owner likely would have discovered the weak tree limbs and foreseen that they could injure someone. But the owner owed no such duty to the hiker since he was a trespasser. (Choice C) The owner would have owed the hiker a duty to prevent the trees on his property from becoming dangerous (or to provide adequate warnings) had the hiker been an invitee—not a trespasser.\n\n(Choice D) Land possessors must warn discovered or anticipated trespassers about hidden, artificial (ie, man-made) dangers on the land of which the land possessor is aware. But even if the hiker had been such a trespasser, the owner would not be liable since he had no knowledge of this hidden, natural danger.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the property owner breached no duty to the hiker, who was a trespasser."},"questionZh":"一名徒步旅行者被从树上掉落的树枝击中头部受伤。\n\n受伤时，该徒步旅行者未经土地所有者知情或许可，擅自进入私人土地上的森林。\n\n经查明，树枝掉落是因为树木遭受白蚁侵蚀。\n\n如果该徒步旅行者起诉土地所有者，要求赔偿头部受伤造成的损失，他胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为土地所有者并未对该擅闯私人土地的徒步旅行者违反任何义务。","B":"否，因为土地所有者无法预见任何人会受伤。","C":"是，因为土地所有者有义务防止其土地上的树木变得危险。","D":"是，因为土地所有者对其土地上的隐蔽危险负有责任。"},"explanationZh":"土地占有者对进入土地者的义务 （传统做法）* 身份特征 义务 侵入者 未经许可故意进入土地 已知或频繁出现的侵入者——须告知已知的、隐蔽的人造危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务；未知或未预料到的侵入者——无义务 获准进入者 经许可进入土地（如社交访客） 须告知已知的隐蔽危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务 受邀进入者 进入向公众开放的土地（如去教堂的人） 检查是否存在未知危险；确保场所安全或提供充分警告；防止因活动造成伤害；进入土地从事商业活动（如商店顾客） *在采用现代做法的司法管辖区，土地占有者对所有进入土地者（恶劣侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n土地占有者最能有效防止进入其土地的人员受到伤害。\n\n因此，他们对可预见的进入者负有合理注意义务。\n\n然而，对于侵入者（即未经许可故意进入他人土地的人），占有者通常不负此义务，除非占有者已知晓或有理由预见到其在土地上的存在。\n\n在本案中，徒步旅行者在穿过属于私人财产的森林时，被掉落的树枝砸伤。\n\n该徒步旅行者属于侵入者，因为他在未获业主许可的情况下故意进入了森林。\n\n由于没有证据表明业主知晓或有理由预见到该徒步旅行者的存在，他属于不可预见的侵入者。\n\n因此，业主对该徒步旅行者不负义务，后者无法就其受伤获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）如果该徒步旅行者是受邀进入者，业主则有义务对财产进行合理检查。\n\n在这种情况下，业主很可能会发现脆弱的树枝并预见到它们可能伤人。\n\n但由于该徒步旅行者是侵入者，业主对其不负此义务。\n\n（选项 C）如果该徒步旅行者是受邀进入者（而非侵入者），业主则有义务防止其财产上的树木变得危险（或提供充分警告）。\n\n(选项 D) 土地占有人有义务告知已被发现或可预见的侵入者，关于其知晓的土地上存在的隐蔽性、人造（即人工造成的）危险。\n\n但即使该徒步旅行者属于此类侵入者，土地所有者也不承担责任，因为他对这种隐蔽的自然危险并不知情。\n\n教育目标：土地占有人对可预见的进入者负有合理注意义务；但对于不可预见的进入者（例如未被发现或未被预料到的侵入者），则不负有此项义务。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 333 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（对未知的侵入者不负有义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地所有者对可预见的土地进入者负有合理的注意义务。\n\n但对于不可预见的土地进入者（例如，未被发现或意外闯入者），业主不承担此类义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果徒步旅行者起诉土地所有者，要求赔偿其头部受伤造成的损失，徒步旅行者胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"土地所有者对可预见的土地进入者负有合理的注意义务。\n\n但对于不可预见的土地进入者（例如，未被发现或意外闯入者），业主不承担此类义务。","analysis":"土地占有者对进入土地者的义务 （传统做法）* 身份特征 义务 侵入者 未经许可故意进入土地 已知或频繁出现的侵入者——须告知已知的、隐蔽的人造危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务；未知或未预料到的侵入者——无义务 获准进入者 经许可进入土地（如社交访客） 须告知已知的隐蔽危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务 受邀进入者 进入向公众开放的土地（如去教堂的人） 检查是否存在未知危险；确保场所安全或提供充分警告；防止因活动造成伤害；进入土地从事商业活动（如商店顾客） *在采用现代做法的司法管辖区，土地占有者对所有进入土地者（恶劣侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n土地占有者最能有效防止进入其土地的人员受到伤害。\n\n因此，他们对可预见的进入者负有合理注意义务。\n\n然而，对于侵入者（即未经许可故意进入他人土地的人），占有者通常不负此义务，除非占有者已知晓或有理由预见到其在土地上的存在。\n\n在本案中，徒步旅行者在穿过属于私人财产的森林时，被掉落的树枝砸伤。\n\n该徒步旅行者属于侵入者，因为他在未获业主许可的情况下故意进入了森林。\n\n由于没有证据表明业主知晓或有理由预见到该徒步旅行者的存在，他属于不可预见的侵入者。\n\n因此，业主对该徒步旅行者不负义务，后者无法就其受伤获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）如果该徒步旅行者是受邀进入者，业主则有义务对财产进行合理检查。\n\n在这种情况下，业主很可能会发现脆弱的树枝并预见到它们可能伤人。\n\n但由于该徒步旅行者是侵入者，业主对其不负此义务。\n\n（选项 C）如果该徒步旅行者是受邀进入者（而非侵入者），业主则有义务防止其财产上的树木变得危险（或提供充分警告）。\n\n(选项 D) 土地占有人有义务告知已被发现或可预见的侵入者，关于其知晓的土地上存在的隐蔽性、人造（即人工造成的）危险。\n\n但即使该徒步旅行者属于此类侵入者，土地所有者也不承担责任，因为他对这种隐蔽的自然危险并不知情。\n\n教育目标：土地占有人对可预见的进入者负有合理注意义务；但对于不可预见的进入者（例如未被发现或未被预料到的侵入者），则不负有此项义务。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 333 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（对未知的侵入者不负有义务）。","conclusion":"答案 A：否，因为土地所有者并未对该擅闯私人土地的徒步旅行者违反任何义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":93,"sourceQuestionPage":280,"sourceQuestionPages":[280],"sourceExplanationPages":[281,282],"sourceAnswerPages":[281,282],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-094","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A lawyer and an accountant were passengers sitting in adjoining seats on an airline's flight. There were many empty seats on the aircraft. During the flight, a flight attendant served the accountant nine drinks. As the accountant became more and more obviously intoxicated and attempted to engage the lawyer in a conversation, the lawyer chose to ignore the accountant. This angered the accountant, who suddenly struck the lawyer in the face, giving her a black eye. If the lawyer asserts a claim for damages against the airline based on negligence, will the lawyer prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a person is not required by law to come to the assistance of another who is imperiled by a third party.","B":"No, because the lawyer could easily have moved to another seat.","C":"Yes, because a common carrier is strictly liable for injuries suffered by a passenger while aboard the carrier.","D":"Yes, because the flight attendant should have perceived the accountant's condition and acted to protect the lawyer before the blow was struck."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Affirmative duty to act\n\nAssumption of Defendant who voluntarily aids or rescues another has duty to use duty reasonable care when rendering aid or performing rescue\n\nCreation of Defendant whose conduct creates foreseeable risk of harm or places risk another in peril has duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent further harm by rendering care or aid\n\nBy contract Duty to use care when performing contractual obligations\n\nBy authority Duty to exercise reasonable control over third party whom defendant has actual ability & authority to control\n\nExamples: parent over child, custodian over person in custody, employer over employee, mental-health professional over patient By Duty to protect, aid, or assist plaintiff with whom defendant shares relationship unique relationship\n\nExamples: business proprietor with patron, common carrier with passenger, innkeeper with guest, employer with employee, parent with child By statute Statute imposes obligation to act for another's protection\n\nLand Duty to mitigate risks posed by natural or artificial conditions on land possessor\n\nAn actor has a duty to use reasonable care to protect a victim if the actor knows (or reasonably should know) that his/her conduct has exposed the victim to an unreasonable risk of harm from third parties. The actor may either be: directly liable for negligence if his/her breach of that duty caused the victim harm or\n\nvicariously liable (under the doctrine of respondeat superior) for his/her employee's negligence if it occurred within the scope of employment.\n\nHere, the accountant struck the lawyer after a flight attendant served him nine drinks on a flight. The flight attendant had a duty to use reasonable care to protect the other passengers since the flight attendant should have known that the accountant was becoming intoxicated (creating an unreasonable risk of harm). The flight attendant then breached that duty by failing to protect the lawyer before the blow was struck. And since serving\n\ndrinks fell within the scope of the flight attendant's employment, the airline is vicariously liable for negligence. (Choice A) A person generally is not required to come to the assistance of another who is imperiled by a third party. But a person does have a duty to assist another when the person's conduct has exposed the other to an unreasonable risk of harm from a third party (as seen here). (Choice B) In a comparative negligence jurisdiction (default rule on the MBE), a plaintiff's recovery for negligence is reduced—not barred—if he/she failed to use reasonable care for his/her own protection. Therefore, the fact that the lawyer remained in her seat when she could have easily moved would not defeat her claim. (Choice C) Strict liability applies to strict products liability, certain animals, and abnormally dangerous activities. Here, the lawyer was not harmed by a product sold by the airline (no strict products liability), an animal, or an abnormally dangerous activity.\n\nEducational objective: An actor has a duty to use reasonable care to protect others if the actor knows, or reasonably should know, that his/her conduct has exposed others to an unreasonable risk of harm from third parties.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 6 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence liability).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Agency §§ 2.04, 7.07 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (vicarious liability of employer).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 19 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (duty when conduct exposes others to an unreasonable risk of harm from a third party).","educationalObjective":"An actor has a duty to use reasonable care to protect others if the actor knows, or reasonably should know, that his/her conduct has exposed others to an unreasonable risk of harm from third parties.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 6 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence liability).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Agency §§ 2.04, 7.07 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (vicarious liability of employer).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 19 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (duty when conduct exposes others to an unreasonable risk of harm from a third party).","rule":"An actor has a duty to use reasonable care to protect others if the actor knows, or reasonably should know, that his/her conduct has exposed others to an unreasonable risk of harm from third parties.","irac":{"issue":"If the lawyer asserts a claim for damages against the airline based on negligence, will the lawyer prevail?","rule":"An actor has a duty to use reasonable care to protect others if the actor knows, or reasonably should know, that his/her conduct has exposed others to an unreasonable risk of harm from third parties.","analysis":"Affirmative duty to act\n\nAssumption of Defendant who voluntarily aids or rescues another has duty to use duty reasonable care when rendering aid or performing rescue\n\nCreation of Defendant whose conduct creates foreseeable risk of harm or places risk another in peril has duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent further harm by rendering care or aid\n\nBy contract Duty to use care when performing contractual obligations\n\nBy authority Duty to exercise reasonable control over third party whom defendant has actual ability & authority to control\n\nExamples: parent over child, custodian over person in custody, employer over employee, mental-health professional over patient By Duty to protect, aid, or assist plaintiff with whom defendant shares relationship unique relationship\n\nExamples: business proprietor with patron, common carrier with passenger, innkeeper with guest, employer with employee, parent with child By statute Statute imposes obligation to act for another's protection\n\nLand Duty to mitigate risks posed by natural or artificial conditions on land possessor\n\nAn actor has a duty to use reasonable care to protect a victim if the actor knows (or reasonably should know) that his/her conduct has exposed the victim to an unreasonable risk of harm from third parties. The actor may either be: directly liable for negligence if his/her breach of that duty caused the victim harm or\n\nvicariously liable (under the doctrine of respondeat superior) for his/her employee's negligence if it occurred within the scope of employment.\n\nHere, the accountant struck the lawyer after a flight attendant served him nine drinks on a flight. The flight attendant had a duty to use reasonable care to protect the other passengers since the flight attendant should have known that the accountant was becoming intoxicated (creating an unreasonable risk of harm). The flight attendant then breached that duty by failing to protect the lawyer before the blow was struck. And since serving\n\ndrinks fell within the scope of the flight attendant's employment, the airline is vicariously liable for negligence. (Choice A) A person generally is not required to come to the assistance of another who is imperiled by a third party. But a person does have a duty to assist another when the person's conduct has exposed the other to an unreasonable risk of harm from a third party (as seen here). (Choice B) In a comparative negligence jurisdiction (default rule on the MBE), a plaintiff's recovery for negligence is reduced—not barred—if he/she failed to use reasonable care for his/her own protection. Therefore, the fact that the lawyer remained in her seat when she could have easily moved would not defeat her claim. (Choice C) Strict liability applies to strict products liability, certain animals, and abnormally dangerous activities. Here, the lawyer was not harmed by a product sold by the airline (no strict products liability), an animal, or an abnormally dangerous activity.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the flight attendant should have perceived the accountant's condition and acted to protect the lawyer before the blow was struck."},"questionZh":"一位律师和一位会计师乘坐同一航班，座位相邻。\n\n机上有许多空位。\n\n飞行途中，空乘人员为会计师提供了九杯酒。\n\n随着会计师醉意渐浓并试图与律师搭话，律师选择不予理睬。\n\n这激怒了会计师，他突然打了律师的脸，导致其眼部受伤（出现“黑眼圈”）。\n\n如果律师基于过失向航空公司提出损害赔偿请求，她能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为法律并不要求一个人必须去援助那些正遭受第三方威胁的人。","B":"不能，因为律师本可以轻易地换到其他座位。","C":"能，因为公共承运人对于乘客在乘坐期间遭受的伤害承担严格责任。","D":"能，因为空乘人员本应察觉到会计师的状态，并应在袭击发生前采取行动保护律师。"},"explanationZh":"作为义务（Affirmative duty to act） 自愿承担（Assumption）：自愿协助或营救他人的被告，在提供协助或进行营救时有义务尽到合理的注意。 因行为引发（Creation）：被告的行为若造成了可预见的伤害风险，或使他人陷入危险境地，则有义务通过提供照料或协助来尽到合理的注意，以防止进一步的伤害。 基于合同：在履行合同义务时有义务尽到合理的注意。 基于权力：对于被告有实际能力及权力进行控制的第三方，被告有义务对其行使合理的控制。 例子：父母对子女、监护人对被监护人、雇主对雇员、心理健康专业人员对患者。基于保护、协助或救助义务：针对与被告存在特殊关系的原告。 例子：商家与顾客、公共承运人与乘客、旅馆经营者与住客、雇主与雇员、父母与子女。基于制定法：法律规定了为保护他人而采取行动的义务。 土地：土地占有人有义务减轻因土地上的自然或人工状况而产生的风险。 如果行为人知道（或理应知道）其行为使受害者面临来自第三方的、不合理的伤害风险，则该行为人有义务尽到合理的注意以保护受害者。\n\n行为人可能面临以下责任：若其违反该义务导致受害者受到伤害，则直接承担过失责任；或者 若其雇员在受雇范围内发生过失，则（根据“雇主责任原则”/respondeat superior）承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n本案中，会计师在航班上被空乘人员连续提供了九杯酒后，故意侵害身体（battery）了律师。\n\n空乘人员有义务尽到合理的注意来保护其他乘客，因为空乘人员理应察觉到会计师正变得醉酒（从而产生了不合理的伤害风险）。\n\n空乘人员在故意侵害身体（battery）发生前未能保护律师，从而违反了该义务。\n\n由于提供 酒水属于空乘人员的受雇职责范围，航空公司需对该过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 A）通常情况下，一个人没有义务去救助因第三方行为而陷入危险的他人。\n\n但如果某人的行为使他人面临来自第三方的、不合理的伤害风险（如本案所示），则该人有义务协助他人。\n\n（选项 B）在适用“比较过失”原则的司法管辖区（这是 MBE 考试中的默认规则），如果原告未能采取合理的注意措施来保护自己，其因过失而获得的赔偿金额会相应减少，但其索赔权不会因此丧失。\n\n因此，尽管该律师本可轻易移开却仍留在座位上，这一事实并不会导致其索赔主张不成立。\n\n（选项 C）严格责任适用于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）、特定动物相关责任以及异常危险活动。\n\n在本案中，该律师并未因航空公司销售的产品（不涉及严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））、动物或异常危险活动而受到伤害。\n\n教育目标：如果行为人知道或理应知道其行为使他人面临来自第三方的、不合理的伤害风险，则该行为人有义务采取合理的注意措施来保护他人。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 6 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（过失责任）。\n\n《代理法重述（第三次）》第 2.04 条、第 7.07 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（雇主的替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第19条（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于当行为使他人面临来自第三方的、不合理的伤害风险时所负有的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果行为人知道或理应知道其行为使他人面临来自第三方的、不合理的伤害风险，则该行为人有义务采取合理的谨慎措施来保护他人。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果律师基于过失向航空公司提出损害赔偿请求，她能胜诉吗？","rule":"如果行为人知道或理应知道其行为使他人面临来自第三方的、不合理的伤害风险，则该行为人有义务采取合理的谨慎措施来保护他人。","analysis":"作为义务（Affirmative duty to act） 自愿承担（Assumption）：自愿协助或营救他人的被告，在提供协助或进行营救时有义务尽到合理的注意。 因行为引发（Creation）：被告的行为若造成了可预见的伤害风险，或使他人陷入危险境地，则有义务通过提供照料或协助来尽到合理的注意，以防止进一步的伤害。 基于合同：在履行合同义务时有义务尽到合理的注意。 基于权力：对于被告有实际能力及权力进行控制的第三方，被告有义务对其行使合理的控制。 例子：父母对子女、监护人对被监护人、雇主对雇员、心理健康专业人员对患者。基于保护、协助或救助义务：针对与被告存在特殊关系的原告。 例子：商家与顾客、公共承运人与乘客、旅馆经营者与住客、雇主与雇员、父母与子女。基于制定法：法律规定了为保护他人而采取行动的义务。 土地：土地占有人有义务减轻因土地上的自然或人工状况而产生的风险。 如果行为人知道（或理应知道）其行为使受害者面临来自第三方的、不合理的伤害风险，则该行为人有义务尽到合理的注意以保护受害者。\n\n行为人可能面临以下责任：若其违反该义务导致受害者受到伤害，则直接承担过失责任；或者 若其雇员在受雇范围内发生过失，则（根据“雇主责任原则”/respondeat superior）承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n本案中，会计师在航班上被空乘人员连续提供了九杯酒后，故意侵害身体（battery）了律师。\n\n空乘人员有义务尽到合理的注意来保护其他乘客，因为空乘人员理应察觉到会计师正变得醉酒（从而产生了不合理的伤害风险）。\n\n空乘人员在故意侵害身体（battery）发生前未能保护律师，从而违反了该义务。\n\n由于提供 酒水属于空乘人员的受雇职责范围，航空公司需对该过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 A）通常情况下，一个人没有义务去救助因第三方行为而陷入危险的他人。\n\n但如果某人的行为使他人面临来自第三方的、不合理的伤害风险（如本案所示），则该人有义务协助他人。\n\n（选项 B）在适用“比较过失”原则的司法管辖区（这是 MBE 考试中的默认规则），如果原告未能采取合理的注意措施来保护自己，其因过失而获得的赔偿金额会相应减少，但其索赔权不会因此丧失。\n\n因此，尽管该律师本可轻易移开却仍留在座位上，这一事实并不会导致其索赔主张不成立。\n\n（选项 C）严格责任适用于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）、特定动物相关责任以及异常危险活动。\n\n在本案中，该律师并未因航空公司销售的产品（不涉及严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））、动物或异常危险活动而受到伤害。\n\n教育目标：如果行为人知道或理应知道其行为使他人面临来自第三方的、不合理的伤害风险，则该行为人有义务采取合理的注意措施来保护他人。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 6 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（过失责任）。\n\n《代理法重述（第三次）》第 2.04 条、第 7.07 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（雇主的替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第19条（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于当行为使他人面临来自第三方的、不合理的伤害风险时所负有的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 D：能，因为空乘人员本应察觉到会计师的状态，并应在袭击发生前采取行动保护律师。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":94,"sourceQuestionPage":283,"sourceQuestionPages":[283],"sourceExplanationPages":[284,285],"sourceAnswerPages":[284,285],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-095","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A 14-year-old girl of low intelligence received her parents' permission to drive their car. She had very little experience driving a car and did not have a driver's license. Although she did the best she could, she lost control of the car and hit a pedestrian. The pedestrian has brought a negligence action against the girl. Is the pedestrian likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because only the girl's parents are subject to liability.","B":"No, because the girl was acting reasonably for a 14-year-old of low intelligence and little driving experience.","C":"Yes, because the girl was engaging in an adult activity.","D":"Yes, because the girl was not old enough to obtain a driver's license."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nChildren are generally required to act like a reasonable child of the same age, intelligence, and experience. But children who engage in a dangerous activity that is normally undertaken by adults are held to an adult standard of care. This means that the child's conduct will be measured against that of an ordinary, reasonable adult—irrespective of individual shortcomings like low intelligence and lack of experience. A child whose conduct falls below this standard of care and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for negligence. Here, the 14-year-old girl engaged in a dangerous adult activity when she drove her parents' car. As a result, the adult standard of care applies, and it will not be adjusted to reflect the girl's low intelligence or limited driving experience (Choice B). And since the girl's conduct fell below that standard of care when she lost control of the car and hit the pedestrian, the pedestrian will likely prevail in his negligence action against her. (Choice A) The girl's parents are likely subject to liability for negligently entrusting their car to her. But this does not mean that the girl cannot be held liable for her own negligence. (Choice D) The mere fact that the girl was not old enough to obtain a driver's license does not establish that her conduct fell below the adult standard of care at the time of the accident.\n\nEducational objective: Children are generally required to act in the same manner as a reasonable child of the same age, intelligence, and experience. But a child who engages in a dangerous adult activity will be held to an adult standard of care, under which the child's conduct will be measured against that of an ordinary, reasonable adult.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 10 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (standard of care for children).","educationalObjective":"Children are generally required to act in the same manner as a reasonable child of the same age, intelligence, and experience. But a child who engages in a dangerous adult activity will be held to an adult standard of care, under which the child's conduct will be measured against that of an ordinary, reasonable adult.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts § 10 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (standard of care for children).","rule":"Children are generally required to act in the same manner as a reasonable child of the same age, intelligence, and experience. But a child who engages in a dangerous adult activity will be held to an adult standard of care, under which the child's conduct will be measured against that of an ordinary, reasonable adult.","irac":{"issue":"Is the pedestrian likely to prevail?","rule":"Children are generally required to act in the same manner as a reasonable child of the same age, intelligence, and experience. But a child who engages in a dangerous adult activity will be held to an adult standard of care, under which the child's conduct will be measured against that of an ordinary, reasonable adult.","analysis":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nChildren are generally required to act like a reasonable child of the same age, intelligence, and experience. But children who engage in a dangerous activity that is normally undertaken by adults are held to an adult standard of care. This means that the child's conduct will be measured against that of an ordinary, reasonable adult—irrespective of individual shortcomings like low intelligence and lack of experience. A child whose conduct falls below this standard of care and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for negligence. Here, the 14-year-old girl engaged in a dangerous adult activity when she drove her parents' car. As a result, the adult standard of care applies, and it will not be adjusted to reflect the girl's low intelligence or limited driving experience (Choice B). And since the girl's conduct fell below that standard of care when she lost control of the car and hit the pedestrian, the pedestrian will likely prevail in his negligence action against her. (Choice A) The girl's parents are likely subject to liability for negligently entrusting their car to her. But this does not mean that the girl cannot be held liable for her own negligence. (Choice D) The mere fact that the girl was not old enough to obtain a driver's license does not establish that her conduct fell below the adult standard of care at the time of the accident.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the girl was engaging in an adult activity."},"questionZh":"一名智力较低的14岁女孩获父母许可驾驶他们的汽车。\n\n她几乎没有驾驶经验，也没有驾驶执照。\n\n尽管她已尽力而为，但仍失去了对车辆的控制并撞倒了一名行人。\n\n该行人已针对这名女孩提起了过失诉讼。\n\n该行人胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为只有女孩的父母才承担责任。","B":"不会，因为考虑到女孩智力较低且缺乏驾驶经验，她的行为对于一名14岁少年而言是合理的。","C":"会，因为该女孩从事的是一项成人活动。","D":"会，因为该女孩的年龄未达到考取驾驶执照的标准。"},"explanationZh":"过失侵权中的注意义务标准 成年人：必须像类似情况下的普通、理性成年人那样行事。 标准调整情形：身体残疾、具备卓越技能与知识、非自愿醉酒状态。标准不调整情形：精神与情绪障碍、自愿醉酒状态。专业人士：必须展现出与同一社区内其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和注意义务。 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。儿童：必须像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 从事危险性成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准。五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意义务标准。\n\n通常要求儿童像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。\n\n但如果儿童从事通常由成年人进行的危险活动，则适用成年人注意义务标准。\n\n这意味着儿童的行为将参照普通、理性成年人的行为来衡量——而不考虑其智力低下或缺乏经验等个人缺陷。\n\n如果儿童的行为低于此注意义务标准并导致原告身体伤害，则需承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，这名14岁女孩驾驶父母的汽车，属于从事危险性成人活动。\n\n因此，适用成年人注意义务标准，且不会因女孩智力低下或驾驶经验不足而进行调整（选项B）。\n\n由于女孩在失去车辆控制并撞倒行人时其行为低于该注意义务标准，行人很可能在针对她的过失诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项A）女孩的父母可能因疏忽将车交给她驾驶而承担责任。\n\n但这并不意味着女孩无需为自己的过失承担责任。\n\n（选项D）仅凭女孩未达到考取驾照的法定年龄这一事实，并不能确立其在事故发生时的行为低于成年人注意义务标准。\n\n教育目标：通常要求儿童像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。\n\n但从事危险性成人活动的儿童将适用成年人注意义务标准，即参照普通、理性成年人的行为来衡量该儿童的行为。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）》第10条（美国法律学会，2010年）（针对儿童的注意标准）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"通常情况下，儿童的行为需符合同等年龄、智力和经验的“理性儿童”的标准。\n\n然而，如果儿童从事危险的成人活动，则适用成人的注意义务标准，即以普通理性成人的行为标准来衡量该儿童的行为。","iracZh":{"issue":"该行人胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"通常情况下，儿童的行为需符合同等年龄、智力和经验的“理性儿童”的标准。\n\n然而，如果儿童从事危险的成人活动，则适用成人的注意义务标准，即以普通理性成人的行为标准来衡量该儿童的行为。","analysis":"过失侵权中的注意义务标准 成年人：必须像类似情况下的普通、理性成年人那样行事。 标准调整情形：身体残疾、具备卓越技能与知识、非自愿醉酒状态。标准不调整情形：精神与情绪障碍、自愿醉酒状态。专业人士：必须展现出与同一社区内其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和注意义务。 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。儿童：必须像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 从事危险性成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准。五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意义务标准。\n\n通常要求儿童像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。\n\n但如果儿童从事通常由成年人进行的危险活动，则适用成年人注意义务标准。\n\n这意味着儿童的行为将参照普通、理性成年人的行为来衡量——而不考虑其智力低下或缺乏经验等个人缺陷。\n\n如果儿童的行为低于此注意义务标准并导致原告身体伤害，则需承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，这名14岁女孩驾驶父母的汽车，属于从事危险性成人活动。\n\n因此，适用成年人注意义务标准，且不会因女孩智力低下或驾驶经验不足而进行调整（选项B）。\n\n由于女孩在失去车辆控制并撞倒行人时其行为低于该注意义务标准，行人很可能在针对她的过失诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项A）女孩的父母可能因疏忽将车交给她驾驶而承担责任。\n\n但这并不意味着女孩无需为自己的过失承担责任。\n\n（选项D）仅凭女孩未达到考取驾照的法定年龄这一事实，并不能确立其在事故发生时的行为低于成年人注意义务标准。\n\n教育目标：通常要求儿童像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。\n\n但从事危险性成人活动的儿童将适用成年人注意义务标准，即参照普通、理性成年人的行为来衡量该儿童的行为。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）》第10条（美国法律学会，2010年）（针对儿童的注意标准）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，因为该女孩从事的是一项成人活动。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":95,"sourceQuestionPage":286,"sourceQuestionPages":[286],"sourceExplanationPages":[287,288],"sourceAnswerPages":[287,288],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-096","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A young woman who attended a rock concert at a nightclub was injured when the band opened its performance with illegal fireworks that ignited foam insulation in the club's ceiling and walls. The young woman sued the radio station that sponsored the performance. The radio station has moved for summary judgment, claiming that it owed no duty to audience members. The evidence has established the following facts: The station advertised its sponsorship on the radio and in print, distributed free tickets to the concert, staffed the event with the station's interns to assist with crowd control, and provided a station disc jockey to serve as master of ceremonies. The master of ceremonies had the authority to stop or delay the performance at any time on the basis of any safety concern. The station knew or should have known that the band routinely used unlicensed, illegal fireworks in its performances. Should the court grant the radio station's motion for summary judgment?","choices":{"A":"No, because there is sufficient evidence of knowledge and control on the part of the station to impose on it a duty of care to audience members.","B":"No, because under respondeat superior, the radio station is vicariously liable for the negligent actions of the band.","C":"Yes, because it is the band and the nightclub owners who owed audience members a duty of care.","D":"Yes, because the conduct of the band in setting off illegal fireworks was criminal and was a superseding cause as a matter of law."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A defendant's motion for summary judgment should be granted when there is insufficient evidence to support one or more elements of the plaintiff's claim. Therefore, summary judgment for the defendant is warranted on a negligence claim when there is no evidence that the defendant: owed the plaintiff a duty to use reasonable care breached that duty or caused the plaintiff harm. When the defendant is a land possessor (ie, one who occupies and controls the land), the defendant owes a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate any foreseeable risk of harm to land entrants. Here, the radio station was a land possessor because it staffed the event (occupation) and provided a disc jockey who had the authority to stop the concert for any safety concern (control). And since the station knew or should have known that the band routinely used illegal fireworks in its performances, the risk of a fire was foreseeable. Therefore, there is sufficient evidence that the station owed audience members a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate that risk, so the station's motion for summary judgment should be denied. (Choice B) The doctrine of respondeat superior holds employers vicariously liable for their employees' torts. Here, the band was not the radio station's employee, so this doctrine does not apply. (Choice C) Although the band and nightclub owners likely owed the audience a duty of care, that does not eliminate the radio station's concurrent duty of care. (Choice D) A superseding cause is an unforeseeable force or act that negates a defendant's tort liability. Here, the band's illegal use of fireworks was reasonably foreseeable, so its conduct was not a superseding cause and does not absolve the station of liability.\n\nEducational objective: Land possessors—persons who occupy and control the land—owe land entrants a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate foreseeable risks of harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm §§ 49, 51 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (duty owed by land possessors).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 56(a) (motion for summary judgment).","educationalObjective":"Land possessors—persons who occupy and control the land—owe land entrants a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate foreseeable risks of harm.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm §§ 49, 51 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (duty owed by land possessors).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 56(a) (motion for summary judgment).","rule":"Land possessors—persons who occupy and control the land—owe land entrants a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate foreseeable risks of harm.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the radio station's motion for summary judgment?","rule":"Land possessors—persons who occupy and control the land—owe land entrants a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate foreseeable risks of harm.","analysis":"A defendant's motion for summary judgment should be granted when there is insufficient evidence to support one or more elements of the plaintiff's claim. Therefore, summary judgment for the defendant is warranted on a negligence claim when there is no evidence that the defendant: owed the plaintiff a duty to use reasonable care breached that duty or caused the plaintiff harm. When the defendant is a land possessor (ie, one who occupies and controls the land), the defendant owes a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate any foreseeable risk of harm to land entrants. Here, the radio station was a land possessor because it staffed the event (occupation) and provided a disc jockey who had the authority to stop the concert for any safety concern (control). And since the station knew or should have known that the band routinely used illegal fireworks in its performances, the risk of a fire was foreseeable. Therefore, there is sufficient evidence that the station owed audience members a duty to use reasonable care to identify and mitigate that risk, so the station's motion for summary judgment should be denied. (Choice B) The doctrine of respondeat superior holds employers vicariously liable for their employees' torts. Here, the band was not the radio station's employee, so this doctrine does not apply. (Choice C) Although the band and nightclub owners likely owed the audience a duty of care, that does not eliminate the radio station's concurrent duty of care. (Choice D) A superseding cause is an unforeseeable force or act that negates a defendant's tort liability. Here, the band's illegal use of fireworks was reasonably foreseeable, so its conduct was not a superseding cause and does not absolve the station of liability.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because there is sufficient evidence of knowledge and control on the part of the station to impose on it a duty of care to audience members."},"questionZh":"一名年轻女子在夜总会观看摇滚音乐会时受伤，起因是乐队在开场表演时使用了非法烟花，引燃了夜总会天花板和墙壁上的泡沫隔热材料。\n\n该女子起诉了赞助此次演出的广播电台。\n\n广播电台提出简易判决动议，声称其对观众不负有注意义务。\n\n证据确立了以下事实：该电台通过广播和印刷媒体宣传其赞助活动，分发免费音乐会门票，指派实习生协助维持现场秩序，并安排了一名电台DJ担任司仪。\n\n司仪有权基于任何安全顾虑随时叫停或推迟演出。\n\n电台知道或应当知道该乐队在演出中惯常使用未经许可的非法烟花。\n\n法院是否应批准广播电台的简易判决动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应批准，因为有充分证据表明电台具备相关知情权与控制权，从而使其对观众负有注意义务。","B":"不应批准，因为根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），广播电台须对乐队的疏忽行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","C":"应予批准，因为对观众负有注意义务的是乐队和夜总会经营者。","D":"应予批准，因为乐队燃放非法烟花的行为属于犯罪行为，且在法律上构成导致损害结果的“阻断因果关系的中断原因”（superseding cause）。"},"explanationZh":"当缺乏足够证据支持原告主张的一个或多个构成要件时，法院应准予被告提出的简易判决动议。\n\n因此，在针对过失索赔的诉讼中，如果缺乏证据表明被告对原告负有尽合理注意义务、违反了该义务或导致了原告受损，则支持被告的简易判决是正当的。\n\n当被告属于土地占有者（即实际占用并控制土地的人）时，其对进入土地的人员负有尽合理注意义务，以识别并减轻任何可预见的伤害风险。\n\n在本案中，广播电台属于土地占有者，因为它为活动配备了工作人员（体现“占用”），并指派了一名有权因安全顾虑叫停音乐会的唱片骑师（体现“控制”）。\n\n鉴于该电台已知或应知该乐队在演出中惯常使用非法烟花，发生火灾的风险是可预见的。\n\n因此，现有充分证据表明该电台对观众负有尽合理注意义务以识别并减轻该风险，故应驳回该电台提出的简易判决动议。\n\n（选项 B）“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior）规定，雇主须对其雇员的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n在本案中，乐队并非广播电台的雇员，因此该原则不适用。\n\n（选项 C）尽管乐队和夜店所有者可能对观众负有注意义务，但这并不免除广播电台同时负有的注意义务。\n\n（选项 D）“阻断因果关系的介入原因”（superseding cause）是指一种不可预见的力量或行为，它能排除被告的侵权责任。\n\n在本案中，乐队非法使用烟花的行为是合理可预见的，因此其行为不构成阻断因果关系的介入原因，也不能免除电台的责任。\n\n教育目标：土地占有者（即占用并控制土地的人）对进入土地的人员负有尽合理注意义务，以识别并减轻可预见的伤害风险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 49、51 条（美国法律协会，2012 年）（关于土地占有者义务的规定）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 56(a) 条（关于简易判决动议的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地占有者——即占用并控制土地的人——对进入土地者负有义务，须尽合理注意以识别并减轻可预见的伤害风险。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应批准广播电台的简易判决动议？","rule":"土地占有者——即占用并控制土地的人——对进入土地者负有义务，须尽合理注意以识别并减轻可预见的伤害风险。","analysis":"当缺乏足够证据支持原告主张的一个或多个构成要件时，法院应准予被告提出的简易判决动议。\n\n因此，在针对过失索赔的诉讼中，如果缺乏证据表明被告对原告负有尽合理注意义务、违反了该义务或导致了原告受损，则支持被告的简易判决是正当的。\n\n当被告属于土地占有者（即实际占用并控制土地的人）时，其对进入土地的人员负有尽合理注意义务，以识别并减轻任何可预见的伤害风险。\n\n在本案中，广播电台属于土地占有者，因为它为活动配备了工作人员（体现“占用”），并指派了一名有权因安全顾虑叫停音乐会的唱片骑师（体现“控制”）。\n\n鉴于该电台已知或应知该乐队在演出中惯常使用非法烟花，发生火灾的风险是可预见的。\n\n因此，现有充分证据表明该电台对观众负有尽合理注意义务以识别并减轻该风险，故应驳回该电台提出的简易判决动议。\n\n（选项 B）“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior）规定，雇主须对其雇员的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n在本案中，乐队并非广播电台的雇员，因此该原则不适用。\n\n（选项 C）尽管乐队和夜店所有者可能对观众负有注意义务，但这并不免除广播电台同时负有的注意义务。\n\n（选项 D）“阻断因果关系的介入原因”（superseding cause）是指一种不可预见的力量或行为，它能排除被告的侵权责任。\n\n在本案中，乐队非法使用烟花的行为是合理可预见的，因此其行为不构成阻断因果关系的介入原因，也不能免除电台的责任。\n\n教育目标：土地占有者（即占用并控制土地的人）对进入土地的人员负有尽合理注意义务，以识别并减轻可预见的伤害风险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 49、51 条（美国法律协会，2012 年）（关于土地占有者义务的规定）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 56(a) 条（关于简易判决动议的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不应批准，因为有充分证据表明电台具备相关知情权与控制权，从而使其对观众负有注意义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"distributed","ipa":"/dɪstɹˈɪbjətəd/","syllables":"dis·trib·uted","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 分布式的；[计] 分散对齐, 分布式处理","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“distributed”","wordForm":"形容词；原形 distribute；词形 0:distribute/1:dp/d:distributed/p:distributed","sourceSentence":"The evidence has established the following facts: The station advertised its sponsorship on the radio and in print, distributed free tickets to the concert, staffed the event with the station's interns to assist with crowd control, and provided a station disc jockey to serve as master of ceremonies.","synonyms":["circulate","circularize","circularise","disseminate"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-946b0d0454efc179"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":96,"sourceQuestionPage":289,"sourceQuestionPages":[289],"sourceExplanationPages":[290,291],"sourceAnswerPages":[290,291],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-097","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Breach & Proof","question":"A customer fell and injured himself when he slipped on a banana peel while shopping at a grocery store. The banana peel was fresh and unblemished except for a mark made by the heel of the customer's shoe. In an action brought by the customer against the store, these are the only facts in evidence. Should the trial judge permit the case to go to the jury?","choices":{"A":"No, because the customer had an obligation to watch where he stepped.","B":"No, because there is not a reasonable basis for inferring that the store knew or should have known of the banana peel.","C":"Yes, because it is more likely than not that the peel came from a banana offered for sale by the store.","D":"Yes, because the store could foresee that a customer might slip on a banana peel."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Land possessor's duty to land entrants\n\n(traditional approach)*\n\nStatus Characteristics Duty\n\nTrespasser Intentionally enters land Known or frequent trespassers – warn of without permission known artificial dangers & use reasonable care in active operations Unknown or unanticipated trespassers – no duty Licensee Enters land with permission Warn of known latent dangers & use (eg, social guest) reasonable care in active operations Invitee Enters land open to public Inspect for unknown dangers (eg, churchgoer) Make premises safe or provide adequate Enters land for business warnings purpose (eg, store customer) Prevent harm from active operations *In jurisdictions that follow the modern approach, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants, except flagrant trespassers.\n\nWhen an invitee (eg, customer) is harmed by a dangerous condition on the premises, the land possessor (eg, grocery store) is liable for negligence if it: knew or should have known about the condition and failed to use reasonable care to protect the invitee from it. In the absence of direct evidence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence. This doctrine may be applied to a defect in the building or its fixtures (eg, escalator malfunction). But it does not apply to a temporary danger that could have arisen immediately before the plaintiff was harmed (eg, spill). Here, the customer injured himself when he slipped on a banana peel at the grocery store. But there is no basis for inferring that the store knew or should have known of this temporary danger since the fresh and unblemished peel could have been dropped immediately before the accident. Therefore, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur does not apply, and the trial judge should not permit the case to go to the jury. (Choice A) The customer had an obligation to use reasonable care for his own protection— eg, by watching where he stepped. But the customer's contributory negligence would not bar his claim.\n\n(Choice C) The grocery store would be strictly liable had (1) the customer been injured by a defective product and (2) the store been the likely seller of that product. But strict liability is inappropriate because the customer was harmed by a discarded banana peel— not a defective product. (Choice D) The store had a duty to inspect and clean the floor periodically since it could foresee that a customer might slip on debris (eg, banana peel). But the banana peel was fresh and unblemished, indicating that it had not been on the floor for an unreasonably long time.\n\nEducational objective: The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur does not permit an inference of negligence when the plaintiff was harmed by a temporary danger that could have arisen immediately before the injury occurred (eg, spill).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a defendant's negligence can be inferred based on the type of accident that injured the plaintiff).\n\nCox v. Wal-Mart Stores East, L.P., 350 F. App'x 741, 744 (3d Cir. 2009) (explaining that res ipsa loquitur applies to defects in buildings and fixtures, but not to transitory dangers that cause slip-and-falls).","educationalObjective":"The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur does not permit an inference of negligence when the plaintiff was harmed by a temporary danger that could have arisen immediately before the injury occurred (eg, spill).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a defendant's negligence can be inferred based on the type of accident that injured the plaintiff).\n\nCox v. Wal-Mart Stores East, L.P., 350 F. App'x 741, 744 (3d Cir. 2009) (explaining that res ipsa loquitur applies to defects in buildings and fixtures, but not to transitory dangers that cause slip-and-falls).","rule":"The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur does not permit an inference of negligence when the plaintiff was harmed by a temporary danger that could have arisen immediately before the injury occurred (eg, spill).","irac":{"issue":"Should the trial judge permit the case to go to the jury?","rule":"The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur does not permit an inference of negligence when the plaintiff was harmed by a temporary danger that could have arisen immediately before the injury occurred (eg, spill).","analysis":"Land possessor's duty to land entrants\n\n(traditional approach)*\n\nStatus Characteristics Duty\n\nTrespasser Intentionally enters land Known or frequent trespassers – warn of without permission known artificial dangers & use reasonable care in active operations Unknown or unanticipated trespassers – no duty Licensee Enters land with permission Warn of known latent dangers & use (eg, social guest) reasonable care in active operations Invitee Enters land open to public Inspect for unknown dangers (eg, churchgoer) Make premises safe or provide adequate Enters land for business warnings purpose (eg, store customer) Prevent harm from active operations *In jurisdictions that follow the modern approach, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants, except flagrant trespassers.\n\nWhen an invitee (eg, customer) is harmed by a dangerous condition on the premises, the land possessor (eg, grocery store) is liable for negligence if it: knew or should have known about the condition and failed to use reasonable care to protect the invitee from it. In the absence of direct evidence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence. This doctrine may be applied to a defect in the building or its fixtures (eg, escalator malfunction). But it does not apply to a temporary danger that could have arisen immediately before the plaintiff was harmed (eg, spill). Here, the customer injured himself when he slipped on a banana peel at the grocery store. But there is no basis for inferring that the store knew or should have known of this temporary danger since the fresh and unblemished peel could have been dropped immediately before the accident. Therefore, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur does not apply, and the trial judge should not permit the case to go to the jury. (Choice A) The customer had an obligation to use reasonable care for his own protection— eg, by watching where he stepped. But the customer's contributory negligence would not bar his claim.\n\n(Choice C) The grocery store would be strictly liable had (1) the customer been injured by a defective product and (2) the store been the likely seller of that product. But strict liability is inappropriate because the customer was harmed by a discarded banana peel— not a defective product. (Choice D) The store had a duty to inspect and clean the floor periodically since it could foresee that a customer might slip on debris (eg, banana peel). But the banana peel was fresh and unblemished, indicating that it had not been on the floor for an unreasonably long time.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because there is not a reasonable basis for inferring that the store knew or should have known of the banana peel."},"questionZh":"一位顾客在杂货店购物时，因踩到香蕉皮滑倒受伤。\n\n香蕉皮新鲜完好，只有顾客鞋跟留下的痕迹。\n\n顾客起诉该商店，以上是仅有的证据。\n\n审判法官是否应允许此案提交陪审团审理？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为顾客有义务注意脚下。","B":"否，因为没有合理的理由推断商店知道或应该知道香蕉皮的存在。","C":"是，因为香蕉皮很可能来自商店出售的香蕉。","D":"是，因为商店可以预见顾客可能会踩到香蕉皮滑倒。"},"explanationZh":"土地占有者对进入土地者的义务 （传统做法）* **身份** | **特征** | **义务** **侵入者** | 未经许可故意进入土地 | **已知或频繁出现的侵入者** – 须警示已知的“人造”危险，并在进行活动（如施工、操作设备）时尽到合理注意义务；**未知或未预料到的侵入者** – 无义务 **被许可人** | 经许可进入土地（如社交访客） | 须警示已知的隐蔽危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务 **受邀者** | 进入向公众开放的场所（如去教堂的人）；为商业目的进入土地（如商店顾客） | 须检查是否存在未知危险；确保场所安全或提供充分警示；防止因进行活动而造成伤害 *在采用现代做法的司法管辖区，土地占有者对所有进入土地者（恶劣侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n当受邀者（如顾客）因场所内的危险状况而受到伤害时，如果土地占有者（如杂货店）已知或理应知道该状况，却未尽到合理注意义务以保护受邀者免受伤害，则须承担过失责任。\n\n在缺乏直接证据的情况下，“事实本身说明问题”原则（*res ipsa loquitur*）允许根据间接证据推断存在过失。\n\n该原则可适用于建筑物或其固定装置的缺陷（如自动扶梯故障）。\n\n但它不适用于可能在原告受害前瞬间才出现的临时性危险（如液体泼洒）。\n\n本案中，顾客在杂货店因踩到香蕉皮滑倒受伤。\n\n但没有依据推断商店已知或理应知道这一临时性危险，因为那块新鲜且完好无损的香蕉皮可能是在事故发生前瞬间掉落的。\n\n因此，不适用“事实本身说明问题”原则，初审法官不应允许该案提交陪审团审理。\n\n（选项 A）顾客有义务采取合理注意措施保护自身安全——例如，留意脚下。\n\n但顾客的与有过失（contributory negligence）（contributory negligence）并不会阻碍其提出索赔。\n\n（选项 C）如果 (1) 顾客因缺陷产品受伤，且 (2) 商店很可能是该产品的销售者，则杂货店将承担严格责任。\n\n但在此不适用严格责任，因为顾客是因丢弃的香蕉皮——而非缺陷产品——而受伤的。\n\n(选项 D) 商店有义务定期检查并清理地面，因为商店能够预见到顾客可能会因地面杂物（如香蕉皮）而滑倒。\n\n然而，该香蕉皮新鲜且完好无损，这表明它在地面上停留的时间并不长（未达到“不合理时长”的标准）。\n\n教育目标：“事实本身即说明问题”原则（*res ipsa loquitur*）并不允许在原告因某种可能在伤害发生前一刻才出现的暂时性危险（例如液体泼洒）而受损时，据此推断被告存在过失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 328D 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐述了在何种情况下可根据导致原告受伤的事故类型推断被告存在过失）。\n\n*Cox v. Wal-Mart Stores East, L.P.*, 350 F. App'x 741, 744 (3d Cir. 2009)（阐明“事实本身即说明问题”原则适用于建筑物及固定设施的缺陷，但不适用于导致滑倒事故的暂时性危险）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“事实本身说明问题”原则不允许在原告因可能在伤害发生前立即出现的暂时性危险（例如，液体泄漏）而受到伤害时，推定被告存在过失。","iracZh":{"issue":"审判法官是否应允许案件提交陪审团审理？","rule":"“事实本身说明问题”原则不允许在原告因可能在伤害发生前立即出现的暂时性危险（例如，液体泄漏）而受到伤害时，推定被告存在过失。","analysis":"土地占有者对进入土地者的义务 （传统做法）* **身份** | **特征** | **义务** **侵入者** | 未经许可故意进入土地 | **已知或频繁出现的侵入者** – 须警示已知的“人造”危险，并在进行活动（如施工、操作设备）时尽到合理注意义务；**未知或未预料到的侵入者** – 无义务 **被许可人** | 经许可进入土地（如社交访客） | 须警示已知的隐蔽危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务 **受邀者** | 进入向公众开放的场所（如去教堂的人）；为商业目的进入土地（如商店顾客） | 须检查是否存在未知危险；确保场所安全或提供充分警示；防止因进行活动而造成伤害 *在采用现代做法的司法管辖区，土地占有者对所有进入土地者（恶劣侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n当受邀者（如顾客）因场所内的危险状况而受到伤害时，如果土地占有者（如杂货店）已知或理应知道该状况，却未尽到合理注意义务以保护受邀者免受伤害，则须承担过失责任。\n\n在缺乏直接证据的情况下，“事实本身说明问题”原则（*res ipsa loquitur*）允许根据间接证据推断存在过失。\n\n该原则可适用于建筑物或其固定装置的缺陷（如自动扶梯故障）。\n\n但它不适用于可能在原告受害前瞬间才出现的临时性危险（如液体泼洒）。\n\n本案中，顾客在杂货店因踩到香蕉皮滑倒受伤。\n\n但没有依据推断商店已知或理应知道这一临时性危险，因为那块新鲜且完好无损的香蕉皮可能是在事故发生前瞬间掉落的。\n\n因此，不适用“事实本身说明问题”原则，初审法官不应允许该案提交陪审团审理。\n\n（选项 A）顾客有义务采取合理注意措施保护自身安全——例如，留意脚下。\n\n但顾客的与有过失（contributory negligence）（contributory negligence）并不会阻碍其提出索赔。\n\n（选项 C）如果 (1) 顾客因缺陷产品受伤，且 (2) 商店很可能是该产品的销售者，则杂货店将承担严格责任。\n\n但在此不适用严格责任，因为顾客是因丢弃的香蕉皮——而非缺陷产品——而受伤的。\n\n(选项 D) 商店有义务定期检查并清理地面，因为商店能够预见到顾客可能会因地面杂物（如香蕉皮）而滑倒。\n\n然而，该香蕉皮新鲜且完好无损，这表明它在地面上停留的时间并不长（未达到“不合理时长”的标准）。\n\n教育目标：“事实本身即说明问题”原则（*res ipsa loquitur*）并不允许在原告因某种可能在伤害发生前一刻才出现的暂时性危险（例如液体泼洒）而受损时，据此推断被告存在过失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 328D 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐述了在何种情况下可根据导致原告受伤的事故类型推断被告存在过失）。\n\n*Cox v. Wal-Mart Stores East, L.P.*, 350 F. App'x 741, 744 (3d Cir. 2009)（阐明“事实本身即说明问题”原则适用于建筑物及固定设施的缺陷，但不适用于导致滑倒事故的暂时性危险）。","conclusion":"答案 B：否，因为没有合理的理由推断商店知道或应该知道香蕉皮的存在。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":97,"sourceQuestionPage":292,"sourceQuestionPages":[292],"sourceExplanationPages":[293,294],"sourceAnswerPages":[293,294],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-098","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A real estate developer purchased a large tract of land intending to construct residential housing on it. The developer hired a pool company to build a large in-ground swimming pool on the tract. The contract provided that the pool company would carry out blasting operations that were necessary to create an excavation large enough for the pool. The blasting caused cracks to form in the walls of a man's home in a nearby residential neighborhood. Will the man prevail on an action for damages against the real estate developer?","choices":{"A":"No, if the pool company used reasonable care in conducting the blasting operations.","B":"No, if the real estate developer used reasonable care to hire a competent contractor.","C":"Yes, because the blasting that the pool company was hired to perform damaged the man's home.","D":"Yes, but only if the real estate developer retained the right to direct and control the pool company's construction of the pool."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for torts committed by its independent contractor. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the independent contractor's work involves a nondelegable duty—ie, a duty retained by the defendant even when the performance of that duty is assigned to an independent contractor. That type of work includes abnormally dangerous activities—uncommon activities in the community that present a foreseeable and highly significant risk of harm that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. Here, the real estate developer hired the pool company (independent contractor) to build an in-ground swimming pool. Although the developer normally would not be vicariously liable for the pool company's torts, the pool company had to carry out blasting operations in a residential area (abnormally dangerous activity) to build the pool. And since that work involved a nondelegable duty, the developer is vicariously liable for damages caused by the blasting (cracks in the man's nearby home). (Choice A) Strict liability (liability without proof of fault) is imposed for abnormally dangerous activities since the risk of harm cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. And since reasonable care could not have reduced the risk of harm from blasting (abnormally dangerous activity), the man can prevail against the real estate developer. (Choice B) A principal can be directly liable for his/her own negligence in hiring an incompetent independent contractor or vicariously liable for the independent contractor's torts if they involved a nondelegable duty. Therefore, even if the real estate developer used reasonable care in hiring a competent contractor (no direct liability), the developer is still vicariously liable for the damage caused by the pool company's blasting. (Choice D) A principal who retains the right to control any part of an independent contractor's work may be liable for failing to use reasonable care when exercising that control. But if the work involves an abnormally dangerous activity (as seen here), the principal will be vicariously liable for the plaintiff's harm (eg, damage to home)—regardless of whether the principal retained the right to control the work.\n\nEducational objective: A principal generally is not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the principal had a nondelegable duty of care—eg, when the contractor's work involved an abnormally dangerous activity.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 57 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (general rule – no vicarious liability for independent contractors).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 58 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (exception – vicarious liability for independent contractor's abnormally dangerous activities).","educationalObjective":"A principal generally is not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the principal had a nondelegable duty of care—eg, when the contractor's work involved an abnormally dangerous activity.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 57 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (general rule – no vicarious liability for independent contractors).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 58 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (exception – vicarious liability for independent contractor's abnormally dangerous activities).","rule":"A principal generally is not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the principal had a nondelegable duty of care—eg, when the contractor's work involved an abnormally dangerous activity.","irac":{"issue":"Will the man prevail on an action for damages against the real estate developer?","rule":"A principal generally is not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the principal had a nondelegable duty of care—eg, when the contractor's work involved an abnormally dangerous activity.","analysis":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for torts committed by its independent contractor. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the independent contractor's work involves a nondelegable duty—ie, a duty retained by the defendant even when the performance of that duty is assigned to an independent contractor. That type of work includes abnormally dangerous activities—uncommon activities in the community that present a foreseeable and highly significant risk of harm that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. Here, the real estate developer hired the pool company (independent contractor) to build an in-ground swimming pool. Although the developer normally would not be vicariously liable for the pool company's torts, the pool company had to carry out blasting operations in a residential area (abnormally dangerous activity) to build the pool. And since that work involved a nondelegable duty, the developer is vicariously liable for damages caused by the blasting (cracks in the man's nearby home). (Choice A) Strict liability (liability without proof of fault) is imposed for abnormally dangerous activities since the risk of harm cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. And since reasonable care could not have reduced the risk of harm from blasting (abnormally dangerous activity), the man can prevail against the real estate developer. (Choice B) A principal can be directly liable for his/her own negligence in hiring an incompetent independent contractor or vicariously liable for the independent contractor's torts if they involved a nondelegable duty. Therefore, even if the real estate developer used reasonable care in hiring a competent contractor (no direct liability), the developer is still vicariously liable for the damage caused by the pool company's blasting. (Choice D) A principal who retains the right to control any part of an independent contractor's work may be liable for failing to use reasonable care when exercising that control. But if the work involves an abnormally dangerous activity (as seen here), the principal will be vicariously liable for the plaintiff's harm (eg, damage to home)—regardless of whether the principal retained the right to control the work.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the blasting that the pool company was hired to perform damaged the man's home."},"questionZh":"一家房地产开发商购入一大片土地，计划在其上建造住宅。\n\n开发商聘请了一家泳池公司，负责在该地块上建造一个大型地下游泳池。\n\n合同规定，泳池公司需进行必要的爆破作业，以便挖掘出足以容纳该泳池的坑洞。\n\n爆破导致附近居民区的一位居民家中墙壁出现裂缝。\n\n该居民针对房地产开发商提起的损害赔偿诉讼能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，如果泳池公司在进行爆破作业时尽到了合理的注意义务。","B":"不能，如果房地产开发商在聘请具备相应能力的承包商时尽到了合理的注意义务。","C":"能，因为泳池公司受雇进行的爆破作业损坏了该居民的房屋。","D":"能，但前提是房地产开发商保留了对泳池公司建造泳池过程的指挥和控制权。"},"explanationZh":"通常情况下，委托人不对其独立承包商实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当独立承包商的工作涉及“不可转让义务”（nondelegable duty）时，委托人须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）；所谓不可转让义务，是指即便将履行职责的任务委派给独立承包商，被告仍保留的义务。\n\n此类工作包括“异常危险活动”——即社区中不常见的活动，其带来的伤害风险既可预见又极高，且无法通过合理的谨慎措施加以降低。\n\n在本案中，房地产开发商聘请了泳池公司（独立承包商）建造嵌入式游泳池。\n\n尽管开发商通常不对泳池公司的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），但泳池公司为建造泳池必须在居民区进行爆破作业（属于异常危险活动）。\n\n由于该工作涉及不可转让义务，开发商须对爆破造成的损害（即该男子附近房屋出现的裂缝）承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 A）针对异常危险活动适用严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担责任），因为此类活动带来的伤害风险无法通过合理的谨慎措施降低。\n\n鉴于合理的谨慎措施无法降低爆破（异常危险活动）带来的伤害风险，该男子可以成功向房地产开发商主张权利。\n\n（选项 B）委托人可能因聘请不称职的独立承包商时的自身过失而承担直接责任，也可能因独立承包商的侵权行为涉及不可转让义务而承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n因此，即使房地产开发商在聘请称职承包商时尽到了合理的谨慎义务（即不承担直接责任），其仍须对泳池公司爆破造成的损害承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 D）若委托人保留了对独立承包商工作任何部分的控制权，则可能因在行使该控制权时未尽到合理的谨慎义务而承担责任。\n\n但如果工作涉及异常危险活动（如本案所示），无论委托人是否保留了对工作的控制权，其均须对原告遭受的损害（例如房屋受损）承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n教育目标：通常情况下，委托人不对其独立承包商实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，若委托人负有不可转让的注意义务（例如，承包商的工作涉及异常危险活动），则须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第57条（美国法律协会，2012年）（一般规则——对独立承包商不承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第58条（美国法律协会，2012年）（例外情况——对独立承包商的异常危险活动承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"委托人通常不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当委托人负有不可转让的注意义务时（例如承包商的工作涉及异常危险的活动），则须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","iracZh":{"issue":"该居民针对房地产开发商提起的损害赔偿诉讼能否胜诉？","rule":"委托人通常不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当委托人负有不可转让的注意义务时（例如承包商的工作涉及异常危险的活动），则须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","analysis":"通常情况下，委托人不对其独立承包商实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当独立承包商的工作涉及“不可转让义务”（nondelegable duty）时，委托人须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）；所谓不可转让义务，是指即便将履行职责的任务委派给独立承包商，被告仍保留的义务。\n\n此类工作包括“异常危险活动”——即社区中不常见的活动，其带来的伤害风险既可预见又极高，且无法通过合理的谨慎措施加以降低。\n\n在本案中，房地产开发商聘请了泳池公司（独立承包商）建造嵌入式游泳池。\n\n尽管开发商通常不对泳池公司的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），但泳池公司为建造泳池必须在居民区进行爆破作业（属于异常危险活动）。\n\n由于该工作涉及不可转让义务，开发商须对爆破造成的损害（即该男子附近房屋出现的裂缝）承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 A）针对异常危险活动适用严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担责任），因为此类活动带来的伤害风险无法通过合理的谨慎措施降低。\n\n鉴于合理的谨慎措施无法降低爆破（异常危险活动）带来的伤害风险，该男子可以成功向房地产开发商主张权利。\n\n（选项 B）委托人可能因聘请不称职的独立承包商时的自身过失而承担直接责任，也可能因独立承包商的侵权行为涉及不可转让义务而承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n因此，即使房地产开发商在聘请称职承包商时尽到了合理的谨慎义务（即不承担直接责任），其仍须对泳池公司爆破造成的损害承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 D）若委托人保留了对独立承包商工作任何部分的控制权，则可能因在行使该控制权时未尽到合理的谨慎义务而承担责任。\n\n但如果工作涉及异常危险活动（如本案所示），无论委托人是否保留了对工作的控制权，其均须对原告遭受的损害（例如房屋受损）承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n教育目标：通常情况下，委托人不对其独立承包商实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，若委托人负有不可转让的注意义务（例如，承包商的工作涉及异常危险活动），则须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第57条（美国法律协会，2012年）（一般规则——对独立承包商不承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第58条（美国法律协会，2012年）（例外情况——对独立承包商的异常危险活动承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。","conclusion":"答案 C：能，因为泳池公司受雇进行的爆破作业损坏了该居民的房屋。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-6b9606187544aae8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":98,"sourceQuestionPage":295,"sourceQuestionPages":[295],"sourceExplanationPages":[296,297],"sourceAnswerPages":[296,297],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-099","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A pedestrian was walking on a crowded public path when she was struck by a speeding bicyclist who swerved wildly to avoid a jogger. As a result, the pedestrian suffered a dislocated vertebra in her spine that would have required several months of treatment to completely heal. The jogger, who witnessed the accident, had no medical training but believed in good faith that it was medically necessary to lift the pedestrian's head and slip his jacket underneath. This unnecessary act resulted in additional damage to the pedestrian's spine that left the pedestrian permanently unable to walk. This jurisdiction has enacted the following Good Samaritan statute: \"Any person who in good faith renders gratuitous emergency medical care or assistance shall not be liable for civil damages for any act or omission, not amounting to gross negligence, in rendering such aid.\" In a suit against both the bicyclist and the jogger for negligence, is the pedestrian likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the jogger's act is protected by the statute and is a superseding cause that cut off the bicyclist's liability.","B":"Yes, but only against the bicyclist because the jogger's conduct was a foreseeable intervening cause.","C":"Yes, but only against the jogger because his gross negligence actually caused the pedestrian's enhanced injury.","D":"Yes, since both the bicyclist and the jogger are jointly and severally liable for the pedestrian's injury."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nA plaintiff will prevail on a negligence claim if four elements are established: duty, breach, causation, and damages. Duty refers to the obligation to protect another against unreasonable risk of injury. The duty of care imposed on a defendant is that of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances—ie, one in the defendant's same position, with the same information and level of competence. Here, the bicyclist breached the duty of care by speeding and swerving wildly to avoid a jogger since a reasonably prudent person would ride in a controlled manner. Since that breach caused the bicyclist to hit and injure the pedestrian, the bicyclist is liable for negligence. The jogger's attempted aid did not negate the bicyclist's liability because it was not a superseding cause—ie, an unforeseeable intervening event that breaks the chain of proximate causation between the defendant's tortious act and the plaintiff's harm. Instead, the jogger's intervention was foreseeable and occurred after the plaintiff's injury (Choice A). This foreseeable intervention would make the jogger jointly and severally liable to the plaintiff if his actions were negligent. But less may be expected of a reasonably prudent person forced to act in an emergency such as this one—so long as the defendant's conduct did not cause the emergency.*\n\nHere, the jogger acted as a reasonably prudent person during this emergency by rendering aid to the injured pedestrian. Therefore, the jogger did not breach a duty of care—and is not liable for negligence—even though his aid exacerbated the pedestrian's injuries. Even if the jogger had been negligent, he would have been shielded by the jurisdiction's Good Samaritan statute since his actions did not amount to gross negligence. As a result, the pedestrian will not prevail against the jogger (Choices C & D). *But if the defendant's conduct created the emergency, then the defendant may be liable for failing to rescue the plaintiff from that situation even if a reasonably prudent person would not have been expected to do so.\n\nEducational objective: The duty of care imposed on a defendant is that of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances—ie, one in the defendant's same position, with the same information and level of competence. Therefore, less may be expected of a reasonably prudent person acting in an emergency.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 283 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (reasonable-man standard).","educationalObjective":"The duty of care imposed on a defendant is that of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances—ie, one in the defendant's same position, with the same information and level of competence. Therefore, less may be expected of a reasonably prudent person acting in an emergency.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 283 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (reasonable-man standard).","rule":"The duty of care imposed on a defendant is that of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances—ie, one in the defendant's same position, with the same information and level of competence. Therefore, less may be expected of a reasonably prudent person acting in an emergency.","irac":{"issue":"A pedestrian was walking on a crowded public path when she was struck by a speeding bicyclist who swerved wildly to avoid a jogger. As a result, the pedestrian suffered a dislocated vertebra in her spine that would have required several months of treatment to completely heal. The jogger, who witnessed the accident, had no medical training but believed in good faith that it was medically necessary to lift the pedestrian's head and slip his jacket underneath. This unnecessary act resulted in additional damage to the pedestrian's spine that left the pedestrian permanently unable to walk. This jurisdiction has enacted the following Good Samaritan statute: \"Any person who in good faith renders gratuitous emergency medical care or assistance shall not be liable for civil damages for any act or omission, not amounting to gross negligence, in rendering such aid.\" In a suit against both the bicyclist and the jogger for negligence, is the pedestrian likely to prevail?","rule":"The duty of care imposed on a defendant is that of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances—ie, one in the defendant's same position, with the same information and level of competence. Therefore, less may be expected of a reasonably prudent person acting in an emergency.","analysis":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nA plaintiff will prevail on a negligence claim if four elements are established: duty, breach, causation, and damages. Duty refers to the obligation to protect another against unreasonable risk of injury. The duty of care imposed on a defendant is that of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances—ie, one in the defendant's same position, with the same information and level of competence. Here, the bicyclist breached the duty of care by speeding and swerving wildly to avoid a jogger since a reasonably prudent person would ride in a controlled manner. Since that breach caused the bicyclist to hit and injure the pedestrian, the bicyclist is liable for negligence. The jogger's attempted aid did not negate the bicyclist's liability because it was not a superseding cause—ie, an unforeseeable intervening event that breaks the chain of proximate causation between the defendant's tortious act and the plaintiff's harm. Instead, the jogger's intervention was foreseeable and occurred after the plaintiff's injury (Choice A). This foreseeable intervention would make the jogger jointly and severally liable to the plaintiff if his actions were negligent. But less may be expected of a reasonably prudent person forced to act in an emergency such as this one—so long as the defendant's conduct did not cause the emergency.*\n\nHere, the jogger acted as a reasonably prudent person during this emergency by rendering aid to the injured pedestrian. Therefore, the jogger did not breach a duty of care—and is not liable for negligence—even though his aid exacerbated the pedestrian's injuries. Even if the jogger had been negligent, he would have been shielded by the jurisdiction's Good Samaritan statute since his actions did not amount to gross negligence. As a result, the pedestrian will not prevail against the jogger (Choices C & D). *But if the defendant's conduct created the emergency, then the defendant may be liable for failing to rescue the plaintiff from that situation even if a reasonably prudent person would not have been expected to do so.","conclusion":"Answer B: Yes, but only against the bicyclist because the jogger's conduct was a foreseeable intervening cause."},"questionZh":"一名行人正在拥挤的公共步道上行走，被一名为避让慢跑者而急剧转向的超速骑行者撞倒。\n\n事故导致该行人脊椎脱位，原本需要数月治疗才能完全康复。\n\n目睹事故的慢跑者虽无医学背景，但出于善意认为有必要抬起行人的头部并将自己的夹克垫在下面。\n\n这一不必要的举动导致行人脊椎受到额外伤害，使其永久丧失行走能力。\n\n该司法管辖区制定了如下“好撒玛利亚人法”（Good Samaritan statute）：“任何出于善意提供无偿紧急医疗救护或援助的人，对于在提供此类援助过程中发生的行为或不作为（只要不构成重大过失），均不承担民事赔偿责任。”若该行人起诉骑行者和慢跑者存在过失，她胜诉的可能性如何？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会胜诉，因为慢跑者的行为受该法规保护，且构成切断骑行者责任的“阻断性原因”（superseding cause）。","B":"会胜诉，但仅针对骑行者，因为慢跑者的行为属于可预见的介入原因（foreseeable intervening cause）。","C":"会胜诉，但仅针对慢跑者，因为他的重大过失实际上导致了行人伤势的加重。","D":"会胜诉，因为骑行者和慢跑者对行人的伤害承担连带责任。"},"explanationZh":"过失侵权中的注意标准 **成年人：** 必须像处于类似情况下的普通、理性的成年人那样行事。 * 标准会针对身体残疾、卓越技能与知识以及非自愿醉酒状态进行调整；但不会针对精神与情感障碍或自愿醉酒状态进行调整。 **专业人士：** 必须展现出与同类社区中其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和注意程度。 * 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。 **儿童：** 必须像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 * 从事危险成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准；五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意标准。\n\n如果原告能确立以下四个要素，其过失侵权主张即可成立：注意义务（duty）、违反义务（breach）、因果关系（causation）和损害后果（damages）。\n\n注意义务是指保护他人免受不合理伤害风险的义务。\n\n被告被要求的注意标准是“在特定情况下一个理性谨慎的人”的标准——即处于被告相同处境、拥有相同信息和能力水平的人。\n\n在本案中，骑自行车的人因超速行驶并为避让慢跑者而剧烈转向，从而违反了注意义务，因为一个理性谨慎的人会以可控的方式骑行。\n\n由于这种违规行为导致骑车人撞伤行人，骑车人须承担过失责任。\n\n慢跑者试图施救的行为并未免除骑车人的责任，因为该行为不构成“阻断因果关系的原因”（superseding cause）——即一种不可预见的介入事件，它会切断被告侵权行为与原告伤害之间的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）果关系链。\n\n相反，慢跑者的介入是可预见的，且发生在原告受伤之后（选项 A）。\n\n如果慢跑者的行为存在过失，这种可预见的介入将使其对原告承担连带责任。\n\n但对于被迫在此类紧急情况下采取行动的理性谨慎之人，其行为标准可能会有所降低——前提是被告的行为并未引发该紧急情况。* 在本案中，慢跑者在紧急情况下向受伤行人提供救助，其行为符合理性谨慎之人的标准。\n\n因此，尽管他的救助行为加重了行人的伤势，但他并未违反注意义务，也不承担过失责任。\n\n即使该慢跑者存在疏忽，由于其行为未达到“重大过失”的程度，他仍会受到该司法管辖区“好撒马利亚人法”（Good Samaritan statute）的保护。\n\n因此，行人无法在针对慢跑者的诉讼中胜诉（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n*然而，如果被告的行为引发了紧急情况，那么即使在通常情况下不要求一个“理性谨慎的人”采取救援行动，被告也可能因未能将原告从该处境中解救出来而承担责任。\n\n教育目标：法律对被告施加的注意义务标准是“在特定情境下理性谨慎的人”的标准——即处于被告相同处境、拥有相同信息及能力水平的人的标准。\n\n因此，对于处于紧急情况下的理性谨慎之人，法律对其行为的要求可能会相应降低。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 283 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（“理性人”标准）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"法律对被告施加的注意义务标准是“在特定情况下一个理性谨慎的人”的标准——即处于被告相同处境、拥有相同信息和能力水平的人。\n\n因此，对于在紧急情况下行事的理性谨慎之人，其行为标准可能会相应降低。","iracZh":{"issue":"一名行人正在拥挤的公共步道上行走，被一名为避让慢跑者而急剧转向的超速骑行者撞倒。\n\n事故导致该行人脊椎脱位，原本需要数月治疗才能完全康复。\n\n目睹事故的慢跑者虽无医学背景，但出于善意认为有必要抬起行人的头部并将自己的夹克垫在下面。\n\n这一不必要的行为导致该行人脊柱遭受额外损伤，使其永久丧失行走能力。\n\n该司法管辖区制定了如下“好撒马利亚人法”（Good Samaritan statute）：“任何出于善意提供无偿紧急医疗救治或援助的人，对于在提供此类援助过程中所实施的任何作为或不作为（若未构成重大过失），均不承担民事赔偿责任。”若该行人针对骑行者和慢跑者提起过失诉讼，其胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"法律对被告施加的注意义务标准是“在特定情况下一个理性谨慎的人”的标准——即处于被告相同处境、拥有相同信息和能力水平的人。\n\n因此，对于在紧急情况下行事的理性谨慎之人，其行为标准可能会相应降低。","analysis":"过失侵权中的注意标准 **成年人：** 必须像处于类似情况下的普通、理性的成年人那样行事。 * 标准会针对身体残疾、卓越技能与知识以及非自愿醉酒状态进行调整；但不会针对精神与情感障碍或自愿醉酒状态进行调整。 **专业人士：** 必须展现出与同类社区中其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和注意程度。 * 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。 **儿童：** 必须像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 * 从事危险成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准；五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意标准。\n\n如果原告能确立以下四个要素，其过失侵权主张即可成立：注意义务（duty）、违反义务（breach）、因果关系（causation）和损害后果（damages）。\n\n注意义务是指保护他人免受不合理伤害风险的义务。\n\n被告被要求的注意标准是“在特定情况下一个理性谨慎的人”的标准——即处于被告相同处境、拥有相同信息和能力水平的人。\n\n在本案中，骑自行车的人因超速行驶并为避让慢跑者而剧烈转向，从而违反了注意义务，因为一个理性谨慎的人会以可控的方式骑行。\n\n由于这种违规行为导致骑车人撞伤行人，骑车人须承担过失责任。\n\n慢跑者试图施救的行为并未免除骑车人的责任，因为该行为不构成“阻断因果关系的原因”（superseding cause）——即一种不可预见的介入事件，它会切断被告侵权行为与原告伤害之间的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）果关系链。\n\n相反，慢跑者的介入是可预见的，且发生在原告受伤之后（选项 A）。\n\n如果慢跑者的行为存在过失，这种可预见的介入将使其对原告承担连带责任。\n\n但对于被迫在此类紧急情况下采取行动的理性谨慎之人，其行为标准可能会有所降低——前提是被告的行为并未引发该紧急情况。* 在本案中，慢跑者在紧急情况下向受伤行人提供救助，其行为符合理性谨慎之人的标准。\n\n因此，尽管他的救助行为加重了行人的伤势，但他并未违反注意义务，也不承担过失责任。\n\n即使该慢跑者存在疏忽，由于其行为未达到“重大过失”的程度，他仍会受到该司法管辖区“好撒马利亚人法”（Good Samaritan statute）的保护。\n\n因此，行人无法在针对慢跑者的诉讼中胜诉（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n*然而，如果被告的行为引发了紧急情况，那么即使在通常情况下不要求一个“理性谨慎的人”采取救援行动，被告也可能因未能将原告从该处境中解救出来而承担责任。\n\n教育目标：法律对被告施加的注意义务标准是“在特定情境下理性谨慎的人”的标准——即处于被告相同处境、拥有相同信息及能力水平的人的标准。\n\n因此，对于处于紧急情况下的理性谨慎之人，法律对其行为的要求可能会相应降低。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 283 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（“理性人”标准）。","conclusion":"答案 B：会胜诉，但仅针对骑行者，因为慢跑者的行为属于可预见的介入原因（foreseeable intervening cause）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":99,"sourceQuestionPage":298,"sourceQuestionPages":[298],"sourceExplanationPages":[299,300],"sourceAnswerPages":[299,300],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-100","subject":"Torts","topic":"Privileges & Defenses","question":"A male attendant who worked at a hospital had sexual relations with a severely mentally incompetent patient in her room at the hospital. In a tort action brought on the patient's behalf against the hospital, will the patient prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, if the male attendant's actions were outside the scope of his employment.","B":"No, if the patient initiated the relationship with the male attendant and encouraged his actions.","C":"Yes, if the hospital failed to use reasonable care to protect the patient from such conduct.","D":"Yes, if the male attendant was an employee of the hospital."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Special relationships\n\nimposing duty to protect others\n\nParent/child Hospital/patient Employer/employees Shopkeeper/business invitees Common carrier/passengers Custodian/person in custody Innkeeper/guests Mnemonic: Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries\n\nA defendant generally has no duty to protect a plaintiff from harm. But when the defendant has a special relationship with the plaintiff (eg, hospitals and their patients), the defendant has a duty to exercise reasonable care to protect the plaintiff from harm arising within the scope of that relationship. A defendant is liable for negligence if it breaches this duty and causes the plaintiff harm. Here, the hospital had a duty to use reasonable care to protect the patient from harm arising within the scope of the hospital-patient relationship. Since the male attendant had sexual relations with the patient in her hospital room, her harm occurred within the scope of that relationship. Therefore, the patient will prevail in her action against the hospital if it failed to use reasonable care to protect her from the male attendant's conduct. (Choice B) Consent to another's conduct is a complete bar to recovery for the resulting harm. But a person can only consent if he/she has the capacity to do so—ie, the ability to understand the nature and effect of his/her actions. And since the patient was severely mentally incompetent (no capacity), the fact that she initiated the relationship with the male attendant or encouraged his actions would not defeat her claim. (Choices A & D) Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for torts committed by its employees while acting within the scope of employment— ie, while (1) performing assigned work or (2) engaging in conduct subject to the employer's control. But even if the male attendant was not an employee or was acting outside the scope of employment (no vicarious liability), the hospital will still be directly liable for its own negligence.\n\nEducational objective: A hospital owes a duty to exercise reasonable care to protect its patients from harm arising within the scope of the hospital-patient relationship.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 282 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (definition of negligence).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 314 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (no duty to act to protect or aid another).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 314A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (duty to protect or aid based on special relationship).","educationalObjective":"A hospital owes a duty to exercise reasonable care to protect its patients from harm arising within the scope of the hospital-patient relationship.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 282 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (definition of negligence).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 314 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (no duty to act to protect or aid another).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 314A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (duty to protect or aid based on special relationship).","rule":"A hospital owes a duty to exercise reasonable care to protect its patients from harm arising within the scope of the hospital-patient relationship.","irac":{"issue":"In a tort action brought on the patient's behalf against the hospital, will the patient prevail?","rule":"A hospital owes a duty to exercise reasonable care to protect its patients from harm arising within the scope of the hospital-patient relationship.","analysis":"Special relationships\n\nimposing duty to protect others\n\nParent/child Hospital/patient Employer/employees Shopkeeper/business invitees Common carrier/passengers Custodian/person in custody Innkeeper/guests Mnemonic: Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries\n\nA defendant generally has no duty to protect a plaintiff from harm. But when the defendant has a special relationship with the plaintiff (eg, hospitals and their patients), the defendant has a duty to exercise reasonable care to protect the plaintiff from harm arising within the scope of that relationship. A defendant is liable for negligence if it breaches this duty and causes the plaintiff harm. Here, the hospital had a duty to use reasonable care to protect the patient from harm arising within the scope of the hospital-patient relationship. Since the male attendant had sexual relations with the patient in her hospital room, her harm occurred within the scope of that relationship. Therefore, the patient will prevail in her action against the hospital if it failed to use reasonable care to protect her from the male attendant's conduct. (Choice B) Consent to another's conduct is a complete bar to recovery for the resulting harm. But a person can only consent if he/she has the capacity to do so—ie, the ability to understand the nature and effect of his/her actions. And since the patient was severely mentally incompetent (no capacity), the fact that she initiated the relationship with the male attendant or encouraged his actions would not defeat her claim. (Choices A & D) Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for torts committed by its employees while acting within the scope of employment— ie, while (1) performing assigned work or (2) engaging in conduct subject to the employer's control. But even if the male attendant was not an employee or was acting outside the scope of employment (no vicarious liability), the hospital will still be directly liable for its own negligence.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, if the hospital failed to use reasonable care to protect the patient from such conduct."},"questionZh":"一名在医院工作的男护工在病房内与一名重度精神障碍患者发生了性关系。\n\n如果患者以自身名义对医院提起侵权诉讼，患者能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，如果该男护工的行为超出其工作范围。","B":"否，如果患者主动与该男护工发生关系并鼓励其行为。","C":"是，如果医院未能尽到合理的注意义务保护患者免受此类行为的伤害。","D":"是，如果该男护工是医院的雇员。"},"explanationZh":"特殊关系 产生保护他人义务的情形 父母与子女；医院与患者；雇主与雇员；店主与受邀顾客；公共承运人与乘客；监管人与被监管人；旅馆经营者与住客。（记忆口诀：Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries —— 请协助消除导致伤害的安全隐患） 被告通常没有义务保护原告免受伤害。\n\n但当被告与原告存在特殊关系（例如医院与患者）时，被告有义务尽到合理注意，保护原告免受该关系范围内产生的伤害。\n\n如果被告违反此义务并导致原告受到伤害，则需承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，医院有义务尽到合理注意，保护患者免受医院-患者关系范围内产生的伤害。\n\n由于男性护理人员在病房内与患者发生了性关系，患者所受的伤害发生在这一关系范围内。\n\n因此，如果医院未能尽到合理注意以保护患者免受该男性护理人员行为的伤害，患者针对医院提起的诉讼将会胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）对他人的行为表示同意，是针对由此产生的伤害寻求赔偿的完全抗辩事由（即导致无法获得赔偿）。\n\n但一个人只有在具备相应行为能力的情况下才能表示同意——即有能力理解其行为的性质和后果。\n\n鉴于该患者存在严重的精神行为能力缺陷（无行为能力），即便她是与该男性护理人员建立关系的发起者或鼓励了其行为，也不足以否定她的索赔主张。\n\n（选项 A 和 D）根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），雇主须对其雇员在受雇范围内实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）——即雇员在 (1) 执行指派工作或 (2) 从事受雇主控制的行为时所实施的侵权行为。\n\n但即使该男性护理人员并非雇员，或其行为超出了受雇范围（不承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）），医院仍需对其自身的过失承担直接责任。\n\n教育目标：医院有义务尽到合理注意，保护患者免受医院-患者关系范围内产生的伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 282 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（过失的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第314条（美国法律协会，1979年）（无采取行动保护或救助他人的义务）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第314A条（美国法律协会，1979年）（基于特殊关系的保护或救助义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"医院有义务尽到合理的注意义务，保护患者免受在医患关系范围内发生的伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果患者以自身名义对医院提起侵权诉讼，患者能否胜诉？","rule":"医院有义务尽到合理的注意义务，保护患者免受在医患关系范围内发生的伤害。","analysis":"特殊关系 产生保护他人义务的情形 父母与子女；医院与患者；雇主与雇员；店主与受邀顾客；公共承运人与乘客；监管人与被监管人；旅馆经营者与住客。（记忆口诀：Please Help Eliminate Safety Concerns Causing Injuries —— 请协助消除导致伤害的安全隐患） 被告通常没有义务保护原告免受伤害。\n\n但当被告与原告存在特殊关系（例如医院与患者）时，被告有义务尽到合理注意，保护原告免受该关系范围内产生的伤害。\n\n如果被告违反此义务并导致原告受到伤害，则需承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，医院有义务尽到合理注意，保护患者免受医院-患者关系范围内产生的伤害。\n\n由于男性护理人员在病房内与患者发生了性关系，患者所受的伤害发生在这一关系范围内。\n\n因此，如果医院未能尽到合理注意以保护患者免受该男性护理人员行为的伤害，患者针对医院提起的诉讼将会胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）对他人的行为表示同意，是针对由此产生的伤害寻求赔偿的完全抗辩事由（即导致无法获得赔偿）。\n\n但一个人只有在具备相应行为能力的情况下才能表示同意——即有能力理解其行为的性质和后果。\n\n鉴于该患者存在严重的精神行为能力缺陷（无行为能力），即便她是与该男性护理人员建立关系的发起者或鼓励了其行为，也不足以否定她的索赔主张。\n\n（选项 A 和 D）根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），雇主须对其雇员在受雇范围内实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）——即雇员在 (1) 执行指派工作或 (2) 从事受雇主控制的行为时所实施的侵权行为。\n\n但即使该男性护理人员并非雇员，或其行为超出了受雇范围（不承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）），医院仍需对其自身的过失承担直接责任。\n\n教育目标：医院有义务尽到合理注意，保护患者免受医院-患者关系范围内产生的伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 282 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（过失的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第314条（美国法律协会，1979年）（无采取行动保护或救助他人的义务）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第314A条（美国法律协会，1979年）（基于特殊关系的保护或救助义务）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是，如果医院未能尽到合理的注意义务保护患者免受此类行为的伤害。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":100,"sourceQuestionPage":301,"sourceQuestionPages":[301],"sourceExplanationPages":[302,303],"sourceAnswerPages":[302,303],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-101","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"While a businesswoman was leaving an elevator, it suddenly dropped several inches, causing her to fall. An investigation of the accident revealed that the elevator dropped because it had been negligently maintained by an elevator company. The elevator company had a contract with the owner of the building to inspect and maintain the elevator. The businesswoman's fall severely aggravated a preexisting physical disability. If the businesswoman sues the elevator company for damages for her injuries, what should she recover?","choices":{"A":"Damages for the full amount of her disability, because a tortfeasor must take its victim as it finds her.","B":"Damages for the injury caused by the falling elevator, including the aggravation of her preexisting disability.","C":"Nothing, if the accident would not have caused significant harm to an ordinarily prudent elevator passenger.","D":"Nothing, if the elevator company could not reasonably have been expected to foresee the extent of the harm that the businesswoman suffered as a result of the accident."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Relevance of foreseeability for negligence\n\nDuty Defendant's duty only extends to foreseeable risks of his/her conduct\n\nBreach Defendant fails to act like reasonable person under similar circumstances by not mitigating foreseeable risks Causation Actual (but for) cause – foreseeability irrelevant Proximate cause – plaintiff's harm must have been foreseeable consequence of defendant's breach Damages Foreseeability irrelevant (ie, eggshell-skull rule)\n\nTo recovery under a theory of negligence, the plaintiff must prove duty, breach, causation, and damages. Under the eggshell-skull rule, damages are available for all of the harm stemming from the defendant's negligence. This is true even if the extent of that harm was unforeseeable and unusually severe—eg, due to the plaintiff's preexisting vulnerability or medical condition. Here, the elevator company's negligent maintenance caused the elevator to suddenly drop several inches while the businesswoman was leaving it. Since this caused the businesswoman to fall, she can recover damages for her injury, including the aggravation of her preexisting disability. This is true even if the elevator company could not have reasonably foreseen the extent of the businesswoman's harm (Choice D). (Choice A) Under the eggshell-skull rule, a tortfeasor \"takes its victim as it finds her.\" This means that the elevator company must pay for the full extent of the businesswoman's injuries caused by its negligence (eg, the aggravation of her disability). But it need not pay for any injury that was not caused by its negligence (eg, her entire disability). (Choice C) The extent and severity of the plaintiff's harm never has to be foreseeable, so the fact that the accident would not have caused an ordinary elevator passenger significant harm is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: Under the eggshell-skull rule, a tortfeasor is liable for all of the harm resulting from his/her tortious conduct—even if the extent of that harm was unforeseeable and unusually severe (eg, due to the victim's preexisting vulnerability).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 435 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a tortfeasor can be liable for harm that he/she neither foresaw nor should have foreseen).","educationalObjective":"Under the eggshell-skull rule, a tortfeasor is liable for all of the harm resulting from his/her tortious conduct—even if the extent of that harm was unforeseeable and unusually severe (eg, due to the victim's preexisting vulnerability).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 435 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a tortfeasor can be liable for harm that he/she neither foresaw nor should have foreseen).","rule":"Under the eggshell-skull rule, a tortfeasor is liable for all of the harm resulting from his/her tortious conduct—even if the extent of that harm was unforeseeable and unusually severe (eg, due to the victim's preexisting vulnerability).","irac":{"issue":"If the businesswoman sues the elevator company for damages for her injuries, what should she recover?","rule":"Under the eggshell-skull rule, a tortfeasor is liable for all of the harm resulting from his/her tortious conduct—even if the extent of that harm was unforeseeable and unusually severe (eg, due to the victim's preexisting vulnerability).","analysis":"Relevance of foreseeability for negligence\n\nDuty Defendant's duty only extends to foreseeable risks of his/her conduct\n\nBreach Defendant fails to act like reasonable person under similar circumstances by not mitigating foreseeable risks Causation Actual (but for) cause – foreseeability irrelevant Proximate cause – plaintiff's harm must have been foreseeable consequence of defendant's breach Damages Foreseeability irrelevant (ie, eggshell-skull rule)\n\nTo recovery under a theory of negligence, the plaintiff must prove duty, breach, causation, and damages. Under the eggshell-skull rule, damages are available for all of the harm stemming from the defendant's negligence. This is true even if the extent of that harm was unforeseeable and unusually severe—eg, due to the plaintiff's preexisting vulnerability or medical condition. Here, the elevator company's negligent maintenance caused the elevator to suddenly drop several inches while the businesswoman was leaving it. Since this caused the businesswoman to fall, she can recover damages for her injury, including the aggravation of her preexisting disability. This is true even if the elevator company could not have reasonably foreseen the extent of the businesswoman's harm (Choice D). (Choice A) Under the eggshell-skull rule, a tortfeasor \"takes its victim as it finds her.\" This means that the elevator company must pay for the full extent of the businesswoman's injuries caused by its negligence (eg, the aggravation of her disability). But it need not pay for any injury that was not caused by its negligence (eg, her entire disability). (Choice C) The extent and severity of the plaintiff's harm never has to be foreseeable, so the fact that the accident would not have caused an ordinary elevator passenger significant harm is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer B: Damages for the injury caused by the falling elevator, including the aggravation of her preexisting disability."},"questionZh":"一位女商人在走出电梯时，电梯突然下坠了几英寸，导致她摔倒。\n\n事故调查显示，电梯下坠是由于某电梯公司的疏忽维护造成的。\n\n该电梯公司与大楼业主签有合同，负责电梯的检查与维护工作。\n\n女商人的摔倒严重加重了她原有的身体残疾。\n\n如果女商人就其受伤向电梯公司提起损害赔偿诉讼，她应当获得什么赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"针对其残疾状况的全部损害赔偿，因为侵权行为人必须接受受害人当时的身体状况（即“必须接受受害人本来的样子”）。","B":"针对电梯下坠所致伤害的损害赔偿，包括其原有残疾加重部分的赔偿。","C":"不予赔偿，如果该事故对于一个通常谨慎的电梯乘客而言不会造成严重伤害。","D":"不予赔偿，如果电梯公司无法合理预见到女商人因该事故所受伤害的严重程度。"},"explanationZh":"过失侵权中“可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）”的相关性 注意义务（Duty）：被告的注意义务仅限于其行为所带来的可预见风险。 违反义务（Breach）：被告未能在类似情况下像“理性人”那样行事，即未能减轻可预见风险。 因果关系（Causation）：事实因果关系（“若无……则不……”测试）——与可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）无关；法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（Proximate cause）——原告遭受的损害必须是被告违反义务行为的可预见后果。 损害赔偿（Damages）：与可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）无关（即适用“薄弱颅骨规则”/egg-shell skull rule）。\n\n若要基于过失理论获得赔偿，原告必须证明存在注意义务、违反义务行为、因果关系及损害后果。\n\n根据“薄弱颅骨规则”，被告须对因其过失行为导致的所有损害承担赔偿责任。即使损害程度不可预见且异常严重（例如，由于原告既有的身体脆弱性或既往病史），该规则依然适用。\n\n在本案中，电梯公司的过失维护导致电梯在女商人离开时突然下坠数英寸。\n\n由于这导致女商人摔倒，她有权就其受伤（包括既有残疾状况的加重）获得赔偿。\n\n即使电梯公司无法合理预见女商人受损的严重程度，这一结论依然成立（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 A）根据“薄弱颅骨规则”，侵权行为人必须“按受害人的实际状况承担责任”（即“接受受害人原本的样子”）。\n\n这意味着电梯公司必须赔偿因其过失导致的女商人全部伤害（例如，其残疾状况的加重）。\n\n但对于并非由其过失造成的伤害（例如，其残疾的全部状况），则无需赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）原告受损的程度和严重性无需具有可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability），因此，该事故本不会对普通电梯乘客造成严重伤害这一事实，与本案责任认定无关。\n\n教育目标：根据“薄弱颅骨规则”，侵权行为人须对其侵权行为导致的所有损害承担责任——即使损害程度不可预见且异常严重（例如，由于受害人既有的身体脆弱性）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 435 条 (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 435) (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明侵权行为人可能需对其既未预见也无法合理预见的损害承担责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“薄壳颅骨规则”（egg-shell skull rule），侵权行为人须对其侵权行为造成的所有损害承担责任——即使损害程度是不可预见的且异常严重（例如，由于受害人原有的脆弱体质所致）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果女商人就其受伤向电梯公司提起损害赔偿诉讼，她应当获得什么赔偿？","rule":"根据“薄壳颅骨规则”（egg-shell skull rule），侵权行为人须对其侵权行为造成的所有损害承担责任——即使损害程度是不可预见的且异常严重（例如，由于受害人原有的脆弱体质所致）。","analysis":"过失侵权中“可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）”的相关性 注意义务（Duty）：被告的注意义务仅限于其行为所带来的可预见风险。 违反义务（Breach）：被告未能在类似情况下像“理性人”那样行事，即未能减轻可预见风险。 因果关系（Causation）：事实因果关系（“若无……则不……”测试）——与可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）无关；法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（Proximate cause）——原告遭受的损害必须是被告违反义务行为的可预见后果。 损害赔偿（Damages）：与可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）无关（即适用“薄弱颅骨规则”/egg-shell skull rule）。\n\n若要基于过失理论获得赔偿，原告必须证明存在注意义务、违反义务行为、因果关系及损害后果。\n\n根据“薄弱颅骨规则”，被告须对因其过失行为导致的所有损害承担赔偿责任。即使损害程度不可预见且异常严重（例如，由于原告既有的身体脆弱性或既往病史），该规则依然适用。\n\n在本案中，电梯公司的过失维护导致电梯在女商人离开时突然下坠数英寸。\n\n由于这导致女商人摔倒，她有权就其受伤（包括既有残疾状况的加重）获得赔偿。\n\n即使电梯公司无法合理预见女商人受损的严重程度，这一结论依然成立（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 A）根据“薄弱颅骨规则”，侵权行为人必须“按受害人的实际状况承担责任”（即“接受受害人原本的样子”）。\n\n这意味着电梯公司必须赔偿因其过失导致的女商人全部伤害（例如，其残疾状况的加重）。\n\n但对于并非由其过失造成的伤害（例如，其残疾的全部状况），则无需赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）原告受损的程度和严重性无需具有可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability），因此，该事故本不会对普通电梯乘客造成严重伤害这一事实，与本案责任认定无关。\n\n教育目标：根据“薄弱颅骨规则”，侵权行为人须对其侵权行为导致的所有损害承担责任——即使损害程度不可预见且异常严重（例如，由于受害人既有的身体脆弱性）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 435 条 (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 435) (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明侵权行为人可能需对其既未预见也无法合理预见的损害承担责任）。","conclusion":"答案 B：针对电梯下坠所致伤害的损害赔偿，包括其原有残疾加重部分的赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":101,"sourceQuestionPage":304,"sourceQuestionPages":[304],"sourceExplanationPages":[305,306],"sourceAnswerPages":[305,306],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-102","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A municipal builder constructed a strip mall in an area designated as a flood zone, which had a one percent annual chance of flooding. The municipal builder negligently failed to adhere to a local building code that set forth flood-resistant construction requirements on structures built within the zone and imposed penalties for failing to meet those requirements. Upon completing construction, the municipal builder leased storefronts to various local businesses. The following year, one of the storefronts flooded, destroying all of the merchandise therein. Even if the builder had adhered to the local building code, however, the storefront would have suffered the same damage due to the unusual severity of the flood. The tenant of the flooded storefront sued the municipal builder in a negligence action for the value of the merchandise destroyed by the flood. The jurisdiction continues to follow traditional rules regarding municipal tort immunity. Will the tenant be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the municipal builder enjoys immunity from tort liability.","B":"No, because the storefront would have suffered the same damage even if the municipal builder had adhered to the local building code.","C":"Yes, because the municipal builder's failure to adhere to the local building code was negligence per se.","D":"Yes, because the risk of damage to the merchandise was the type of risk that the local building code was intended to prevent."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Government immunity from tort liability\n\nFederal Immunity governed by Federal Tort Claims Act government U.S. government waives immunity in tort actions except for: certain enumerated torts (not including negligence) discretionary functions government contractors in products liability cases certain traditional government activities State Immunity governed by state tort-claims acts (which vary greatly) governments\n\nMunicipalities Immunity governed by state tort-claims acts Traditional rule – immunity for performance of traditional government functions but not proprietary functions Public-duty rule – no liability to one citizen for failure to fulfill duty owed to public at large unless special relationship exists Government Immunity for discretionary acts done without malice or improper officials purpose but not ministerial acts (eg, driving while on government business) Traditionally, the immunity of a municipal actor depended on the act giving rise to the tort claim.* Immunity attached to the performance of traditional government functions (eg, police, court systems) but not to the performance of a proprietary function that is often performed by a private company (eg, utilities, parking lots). Building a shopping mall falls under the latter category, so the municipal builder here is not immune from tort liability (Choice A). However, the tenant will only prevail in this negligence action if the tenant proves that the builder's negligent failure to adhere to the local building code actually and proximately caused the tenant's harm. Actual causation exists when, but for the defendant's tortious conduct, the plaintiff's harm would not have occurred. Since the storefront would have suffered the same damage even if the builder had adhered to the building code, the builder's negligence was not the actual cause of that harm. Therefore, the tenant is unlikely to prevail. *Today, municipal tort liability is usually governed by the provisions of state tort-claims acts. (Choice C) Violating a statute (eg, building code) can constitute negligence per se (ie, negligence as a matter of law). But unless that negligence actually and proximately caused the plaintiff's injuries, there can be no liability.\n\n(Choice D) While the risk of flood damage (eg, destruction of merchandise) was the very risk that the local building code was imposed to address, compliance with the code would not have prevented the damage to the storefront here.\n\nEducational objective: Traditionally, municipal tort immunity attached to the performance of traditional government functions but not to the performance of proprietary functions that are usually performed by a private company.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 895C cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (governmental and proprietary functions).","educationalObjective":"Traditionally, municipal tort immunity attached to the performance of traditional government functions but not to the performance of proprietary functions that are usually performed by a private company.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 895C cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (governmental and proprietary functions).","rule":"Traditionally, municipal tort immunity attached to the performance of traditional government functions but not to the performance of proprietary functions that are usually performed by a private company.","irac":{"issue":"Will the tenant be likely to prevail?","rule":"Traditionally, municipal tort immunity attached to the performance of traditional government functions but not to the performance of proprietary functions that are usually performed by a private company.","analysis":"Government immunity from tort liability\n\nFederal Immunity governed by Federal Tort Claims Act government U.S. government waives immunity in tort actions except for: certain enumerated torts (not including negligence) discretionary functions government contractors in products liability cases certain traditional government activities State Immunity governed by state tort-claims acts (which vary greatly) governments\n\nMunicipalities Immunity governed by state tort-claims acts Traditional rule – immunity for performance of traditional government functions but not proprietary functions Public-duty rule – no liability to one citizen for failure to fulfill duty owed to public at large unless special relationship exists Government Immunity for discretionary acts done without malice or improper officials purpose but not ministerial acts (eg, driving while on government business) Traditionally, the immunity of a municipal actor depended on the act giving rise to the tort claim.* Immunity attached to the performance of traditional government functions (eg, police, court systems) but not to the performance of a proprietary function that is often performed by a private company (eg, utilities, parking lots). Building a shopping mall falls under the latter category, so the municipal builder here is not immune from tort liability (Choice A). However, the tenant will only prevail in this negligence action if the tenant proves that the builder's negligent failure to adhere to the local building code actually and proximately caused the tenant's harm. Actual causation exists when, but for the defendant's tortious conduct, the plaintiff's harm would not have occurred. Since the storefront would have suffered the same damage even if the builder had adhered to the building code, the builder's negligence was not the actual cause of that harm. Therefore, the tenant is unlikely to prevail. *Today, municipal tort liability is usually governed by the provisions of state tort-claims acts. (Choice C) Violating a statute (eg, building code) can constitute negligence per se (ie, negligence as a matter of law). But unless that negligence actually and proximately caused the plaintiff's injuries, there can be no liability.\n\n(Choice D) While the risk of flood damage (eg, destruction of merchandise) was the very risk that the local building code was imposed to address, compliance with the code would not have prevented the damage to the storefront here.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the storefront would have suffered the same damage even if the municipal builder had adhered to the local building code."},"questionZh":"一家市政建筑商在被划定为洪水区（即每年有1%几率发生洪水的区域）的地点建造了一个带状商业中心。\n\n该市政建筑商因疏忽未遵守当地建筑规范；该规范针对该区域内的建筑物规定了防洪施工要求，并对未达到这些要求的行为设定了处罚措施。\n\n工程完工后，市政建筑商将店面出租给了当地的各类商家。\n\n次年，其中一间店面遭遇洪水，导致店内所有商品损毁。\n\n然而，即便建筑商遵守了当地建筑规范，由于此次洪水的严重程度非同寻常，该店面仍会遭受同样的损失。\n\n受灾店面的承租人就洪水损毁商品的价值，以过失为由起诉了市政建筑商。\n\n该司法管辖区继续沿用关于市政侵权豁免的传统规则。\n\n承租人胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为市政建筑商享有侵权责任豁免权。","B":"不大，因为即使市政建筑商遵守了当地建筑规范，该店面仍会遭受同样的损失。","C":"大，因为市政建筑商未遵守当地建筑规范的行为构成“本身过失”（negligence per se）。","D":"大，因为商品受损的风险正是当地建筑规范旨在防范的那类风险。"},"explanationZh":"政府侵权责任豁免 联邦豁免：受《联邦侵权索赔法》（Federal Tort Claims Act）管辖。美国政府在侵权诉讼中放弃豁免权，但以下情况除外：特定列举的侵权行为（不包括过失）、自由裁量行为、产品责任案件中的政府承包商，以及某些传统的政府活动。州豁免：受各州侵权索赔法管辖（各州法律差异很大）。 市政当局豁免：受州侵权索赔法管辖。传统规则——履行传统政府职能时享有豁免权，但履行“营利性/私有性质职能”（proprietary functions）时不享有豁免权。公共义务规则——除非存在特殊关系，否则政府不对因未履行对公众整体的义务而给个别公民造成的损害承担责任。政府对无恶意或不正当目的的自由裁量行为享有豁免权，但对执行性行为（ministerial acts，如执行公务时的驾驶行为）不享有豁免权。传统上，市政主体的豁免权取决于引发侵权索赔的具体行为。* 履行传统政府职能（如警察、法院系统）时享有豁免权，但履行通常由私营公司承担的营利性职能（如公用事业、停车场）时不享有豁免权。\n\n建造购物中心属于后者，因此作为建造者的市政当局不享有侵权责任豁免（选项 A）。\n\n然而，租户若要在该过失诉讼中胜诉，必须证明建造者因过失未遵守当地建筑规范，且该过失实际上并法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）性地（actually and proximately）导致了租户的损害。\n\n实际因果关系（actual causation）是指：若无被告的侵权行为，原告的损害便不会发生。\n\n由于即使建造者遵守了建筑规范，店面仍会遭受同样的损害，因此建造者的过失并非该损害的实际原因。\n\n因此，租户不太可能胜诉。\n\n* 如今，市政当局的侵权责任通常受州侵权索赔法条款的管辖。\n\n（选项 C）违反法规（如建筑规范）可构成“本身过失”（negligence per se，即法律意义上的过失）。\n\n但除非该过失实际上并法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）性地导致了原告的伤害，否则不承担责任。\n\n（选项 D）尽管洪水损害（如商品损毁）正是当地建筑规范旨在防范的风险，但遵守该规范并不能防止本案中店面遭受的损害。\n\n教育目标：传统上，市政侵权豁免权适用于政府履行传统职能的情形，但不适用于履行通常由私营公司承担的“营利性职能”（proprietary functions）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第895C条注释e（美国法律学会，1981年）（关于政府职能与营利性职能）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"传统上，市政侵权豁免适用于履行传统政府职能的情形，但不适用于履行通常由私营公司执行的“专有职能”（proprietary functions，即经营性或商业性职能）。","iracZh":{"issue":"承租人胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"传统上，市政侵权豁免适用于履行传统政府职能的情形，但不适用于履行通常由私营公司执行的“专有职能”（proprietary functions，即经营性或商业性职能）。","analysis":"政府侵权责任豁免 联邦豁免：受《联邦侵权索赔法》（Federal Tort Claims Act）管辖。美国政府在侵权诉讼中放弃豁免权，但以下情况除外：特定列举的侵权行为（不包括过失）、自由裁量行为、产品责任案件中的政府承包商，以及某些传统的政府活动。州豁免：受各州侵权索赔法管辖（各州法律差异很大）。 市政当局豁免：受州侵权索赔法管辖。传统规则——履行传统政府职能时享有豁免权，但履行“营利性/私有性质职能”（proprietary functions）时不享有豁免权。公共义务规则——除非存在特殊关系，否则政府不对因未履行对公众整体的义务而给个别公民造成的损害承担责任。政府对无恶意或不正当目的的自由裁量行为享有豁免权，但对执行性行为（ministerial acts，如执行公务时的驾驶行为）不享有豁免权。传统上，市政主体的豁免权取决于引发侵权索赔的具体行为。* 履行传统政府职能（如警察、法院系统）时享有豁免权，但履行通常由私营公司承担的营利性职能（如公用事业、停车场）时不享有豁免权。\n\n建造购物中心属于后者，因此作为建造者的市政当局不享有侵权责任豁免（选项 A）。\n\n然而，租户若要在该过失诉讼中胜诉，必须证明建造者因过失未遵守当地建筑规范，且该过失实际上并法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）性地（actually and proximately）导致了租户的损害。\n\n实际因果关系（actual causation）是指：若无被告的侵权行为，原告的损害便不会发生。\n\n由于即使建造者遵守了建筑规范，店面仍会遭受同样的损害，因此建造者的过失并非该损害的实际原因。\n\n因此，租户不太可能胜诉。\n\n* 如今，市政当局的侵权责任通常受州侵权索赔法条款的管辖。\n\n（选项 C）违反法规（如建筑规范）可构成“本身过失”（negligence per se，即法律意义上的过失）。\n\n但除非该过失实际上并法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）性地导致了原告的伤害，否则不承担责任。\n\n（选项 D）尽管洪水损害（如商品损毁）正是当地建筑规范旨在防范的风险，但遵守该规范并不能防止本案中店面遭受的损害。\n\n教育目标：传统上，市政侵权豁免权适用于政府履行传统职能的情形，但不适用于履行通常由私营公司承担的“营利性职能”（proprietary functions）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第895C条注释e（美国法律学会，1981年）（关于政府职能与营利性职能）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不大，因为即使市政建筑商遵守了当地建筑规范，该店面仍会遭受同样的损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":102,"sourceQuestionPage":307,"sourceQuestionPages":[307],"sourceExplanationPages":[308,309],"sourceAnswerPages":[308,309],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-103","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A 13-year-old girl was operating a high-speed motorboat. The boat was towing a 9-year-old boy in an inner tube tied to the rear of the motorboat by a rope. The rope became tangled around the boy's foot, causing him to suffer severe injuries. In a suit brought on the boy's behalf against the girl, the boy has introduced uncontroverted evidence that the girl drove carelessly in such a way as to entangle the boy in the rope. Is the boy likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the boy assumed the risk.","B":"No, because the girl was too young to be expected to appreciate and avoid the risk to which she exposed the boy.","C":"Yes, because children of the girl's age should have the capacity to operate motorboats.","D":"Yes, because the girl will be held to an adult standard of care."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nChildren are generally expected to conform to a child's standard of care, which requires them to act like a reasonable child of the same age, intelligence, and experience. But a child who engages in a dangerous activity that is normally undertaken by adults (eg, driving a car) will be held to an adult standard of care. The child's conduct will then be measured against that of an ordinary, reasonable adult under the circumstances. And if the child breaches this duty of care and causes the plaintiff physical harm, the child is liable for negligence. Here, the 13-year-old girl may have been too young to appreciate and avoid the risk to which she exposed the boy by towing him in an inner tube behind the motorboat. But she will be held to an adult standard of care because she was engaged in a dangerous adult activity (Choice B). And since the girl breached that duty of care by driving carelessly and caused the boy to suffer severe injuries, the boy will likely prevail in his suit against her. (Choice A) In most jurisdictions, a plaintiff's voluntary assumption of the risk reduces— but does not bar—recovery. So even if the boy assumed the risk by choosing to ride in the inner tube, his recovery will not be barred. (Choice C) Children of the girl's age should not be expected to have the capacity to operate motorboats. Instead, the boy's claim will likely succeed because the girl will be held to an adult standard of care.\n\nEducational objective: Children who engage in a dangerous adult activity (eg, driving a motor vehicle) will be held to an adult standard of care, which requires them to conform their conduct to that of an ordinary, reasonable adult.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 10 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (standard of care for children).","educationalObjective":"Children who engage in a dangerous adult activity (eg, driving a motor vehicle) will be held to an adult standard of care, which requires them to conform their conduct to that of an ordinary, reasonable adult.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts § 10 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (standard of care for children).","rule":"Children who engage in a dangerous adult activity (eg, driving a motor vehicle) will be held to an adult standard of care, which requires them to conform their conduct to that of an ordinary, reasonable adult.","irac":{"issue":"Is the boy likely to prevail?","rule":"Children who engage in a dangerous adult activity (eg, driving a motor vehicle) will be held to an adult standard of care, which requires them to conform their conduct to that of an ordinary, reasonable adult.","analysis":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nChildren are generally expected to conform to a child's standard of care, which requires them to act like a reasonable child of the same age, intelligence, and experience. But a child who engages in a dangerous activity that is normally undertaken by adults (eg, driving a car) will be held to an adult standard of care. The child's conduct will then be measured against that of an ordinary, reasonable adult under the circumstances. And if the child breaches this duty of care and causes the plaintiff physical harm, the child is liable for negligence. Here, the 13-year-old girl may have been too young to appreciate and avoid the risk to which she exposed the boy by towing him in an inner tube behind the motorboat. But she will be held to an adult standard of care because she was engaged in a dangerous adult activity (Choice B). And since the girl breached that duty of care by driving carelessly and caused the boy to suffer severe injuries, the boy will likely prevail in his suit against her. (Choice A) In most jurisdictions, a plaintiff's voluntary assumption of the risk reduces— but does not bar—recovery. So even if the boy assumed the risk by choosing to ride in the inner tube, his recovery will not be barred. (Choice C) Children of the girl's age should not be expected to have the capacity to operate motorboats. Instead, the boy's claim will likely succeed because the girl will be held to an adult standard of care.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the girl will be held to an adult standard of care."},"questionZh":"一名13岁女孩正在驾驶一艘高速摩托艇。\n\n该艇拖着一名9岁男孩，男孩坐在系于艇尾的充气浮圈（inner tube）上。\n\n拖绳缠住了男孩的脚，导致其受重伤。\n\n在代表该男孩针对女孩提起的诉讼中，男孩提交了无可争议的证据，证明女孩驾驶疏忽，导致男孩被绳索缠住。\n\n男孩胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为男孩承担了风险（assumed the risk）。","B":"不大，因为女孩年龄尚小，不能指望她能意识到并避免其行为给男孩带来的风险。","C":"大，因为该年龄段的儿童应当具备驾驶摩托艇的能力。","D":"大，因为女孩将被适用成人的注意标准（adult standard of care）。"},"explanationZh":"过失侵权中的注意义务标准 成年人：必须像处于类似情况下的普通、理性的成年人那样行事。 标准调整情形：身体残疾、具备卓越技能与知识、非自愿醉酒状态。标准不调整情形：精神与情绪障碍、自愿醉酒状态。专业人士：必须展现出与同一社区内其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和注意程度。 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。儿童：必须像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 从事危险性成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准；五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意义务标准。\n\n通常要求儿童遵守儿童注意义务标准，即要求其行为符合同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童的标准。\n\n但如果儿童从事通常由成年人进行的危险活动（例如驾驶汽车），则适用成年人注意义务标准。\n\n此时，将根据该情况下普通、理性成年人的行为来衡量该儿童的行为。\n\n如果儿童违反了此项注意义务并导致原告身体受损，则该儿童须承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，这名13岁女孩可能因年龄尚小而无法充分认识并规避因驾驶摩托艇拖拽坐在充气内胎上的男孩而产生的风险。\n\n但由于她从事的是危险的成人活动，她仍须遵守成年人注意义务标准（选项B）。\n\n鉴于该女孩因驾驶不当违反了注意义务并导致男孩受重伤，男孩针对她的诉讼很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项A）在大多数司法管辖区，原告自愿承担风险的行为会减少——但不会完全排除——其获得赔偿的权利。\n\n因此，即使男孩选择乘坐充气内胎即意味着承担了风险，他获得赔偿的权利也不会因此被剥夺。\n\n（选项C）不应期望该年龄段的儿童具备驾驶摩托艇的能力。\n\n相反，男孩的索赔很可能会成功，因为该女孩将被适用成年人注意义务标准。\n\n教育目标：从事危险性成人活动（例如驾驶机动车辆）的儿童，须遵守针对成人的注意标准；这意味着其行为必须符合普通、理性的成人的行为标准。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）》第10条（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于儿童注意标准的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"从事危险性成人活动（例如驾驶机动车辆）的儿童，将被适用成人的注意标准；该标准要求其行为符合普通、理性的成人的行为规范。","iracZh":{"issue":"男孩胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"从事危险性成人活动（例如驾驶机动车辆）的儿童，将被适用成人的注意标准；该标准要求其行为符合普通、理性的成人的行为规范。","analysis":"过失侵权中的注意义务标准 成年人：必须像处于类似情况下的普通、理性的成年人那样行事。 标准调整情形：身体残疾、具备卓越技能与知识、非自愿醉酒状态。标准不调整情形：精神与情绪障碍、自愿醉酒状态。专业人士：必须展现出与同一社区内其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和注意程度。 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。儿童：必须像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 从事危险性成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准；五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意义务标准。\n\n通常要求儿童遵守儿童注意义务标准，即要求其行为符合同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童的标准。\n\n但如果儿童从事通常由成年人进行的危险活动（例如驾驶汽车），则适用成年人注意义务标准。\n\n此时，将根据该情况下普通、理性成年人的行为来衡量该儿童的行为。\n\n如果儿童违反了此项注意义务并导致原告身体受损，则该儿童须承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，这名13岁女孩可能因年龄尚小而无法充分认识并规避因驾驶摩托艇拖拽坐在充气内胎上的男孩而产生的风险。\n\n但由于她从事的是危险的成人活动，她仍须遵守成年人注意义务标准（选项B）。\n\n鉴于该女孩因驾驶不当违反了注意义务并导致男孩受重伤，男孩针对她的诉讼很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项A）在大多数司法管辖区，原告自愿承担风险的行为会减少——但不会完全排除——其获得赔偿的权利。\n\n因此，即使男孩选择乘坐充气内胎即意味着承担了风险，他获得赔偿的权利也不会因此被剥夺。\n\n（选项C）不应期望该年龄段的儿童具备驾驶摩托艇的能力。\n\n相反，男孩的索赔很可能会成功，因为该女孩将被适用成年人注意义务标准。\n\n教育目标：从事危险性成人活动（例如驾驶机动车辆）的儿童，须遵守针对成人的注意标准；这意味着其行为必须符合普通、理性的成人的行为标准。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）》第10条（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于儿童注意标准的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 D：大，因为女孩将被适用成人的注意标准（adult standard of care）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":103,"sourceQuestionPage":310,"sourceQuestionPages":[310],"sourceExplanationPages":[311,312],"sourceAnswerPages":[311,312],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-104","subject":"Torts","topic":"Damages & Joint Liability","question":"A private school held a bake sale to raise money. Three parents separately made chocolate chip cookies at their own homes, following their own recipes. The parents brought their cookies to school on the designated day, and a volunteer divided the cookies among small plates for sale. Each small plate contained at least one cookie from each of the three parents. Several people who purchased treats from the bake sale became ill and were diagnosed with food poisoning. After an investigation, the Health Department determined that the chocolate chip cookies caused the outbreak, and that someone who made the cookies did not follow appropriate food-safety measures. A student who purchased a plate of the chocolate chip cookies became so ill that she required hospitalization. She sued the three parents who made the chocolate chip cookies for negligence, claiming that the parents are jointly and severally liable for her harm. The student was able to conclusively establish that the chocolate chip cookies were the cause of her illness, but not which specific parent made the contaminated cookies. Is the student likely to succeed in her negligence suit?","choices":{"A":"No, because the student cannot sue multiple defendants for the same harm.","B":"No, because there is no evidence that each of the parents was negligent.","C":"Yes, because the parents have the burden to prove that they did not cause the student's harm.","D":"Yes, because the parents were acting in concert to raise money through the bake sale."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Joint and several liability arises when two or more defendants were negligent and any one of them could have caused the plaintiff's indivisible harm. This allows the plaintiff to recover the full amount of damages from any of the negligent defendants, even if it is impossible to prove which one actually caused the harm. However, the plaintiff must first prove that each defendant was negligent. Here, the parents were not acting in concert—ie, pursuant to a common plan or design— because they each made the cookies in their own homes, following their own recipes (Choice D). And there is no evidence that each parent was negligent because the Health Department merely determined that someone (not everyone) failed to follow appropriate food-safety measures. This means that the parents are not jointly and severally liable for the student's illness. Therefore, the student is unlikely to succeed in her negligence suit. (Choice A) A plaintiff can sue multiple defendants for the same harm based on joint and several liability. However, the plaintiff must still prove that each defendant acted negligently. (Choice C) In a joint and several liability action, the burden shifts to each defendant to prove that his/her negligence did not cause the plaintiff's harm only after the plaintiff proves that each defendant was negligent (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: If multiple defendants were negligent and any one of them could have caused the plaintiff's harm, joint and several liability allows the plaintiff to recover from any of them even if it is impossible to prove which defendant actually caused the harm. But the plaintiff must first show that each of the defendants was negligent.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 875 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (explaining that multiple tortfeasors who cause an indivisible harm can be held liable individually for the entire harm).","educationalObjective":"If multiple defendants were negligent and any one of them could have caused the plaintiff's harm, joint and several liability allows the plaintiff to recover from any of them even if it is impossible to prove which defendant actually caused the harm. But the plaintiff must first show that each of the defendants was negligent.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 875 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (explaining that multiple tortfeasors who cause an indivisible harm can be held liable individually for the entire harm).","rule":"If multiple defendants were negligent and any one of them could have caused the plaintiff's harm, joint and several liability allows the plaintiff to recover from any of them even if it is impossible to prove which defendant actually caused the harm. But the plaintiff must first show that each of the defendants was negligent.","irac":{"issue":"Is the student likely to succeed in her negligence suit?","rule":"If multiple defendants were negligent and any one of them could have caused the plaintiff's harm, joint and several liability allows the plaintiff to recover from any of them even if it is impossible to prove which defendant actually caused the harm. But the plaintiff must first show that each of the defendants was negligent.","analysis":"Joint and several liability arises when two or more defendants were negligent and any one of them could have caused the plaintiff's indivisible harm. This allows the plaintiff to recover the full amount of damages from any of the negligent defendants, even if it is impossible to prove which one actually caused the harm. However, the plaintiff must first prove that each defendant was negligent. Here, the parents were not acting in concert—ie, pursuant to a common plan or design— because they each made the cookies in their own homes, following their own recipes (Choice D). And there is no evidence that each parent was negligent because the Health Department merely determined that someone (not everyone) failed to follow appropriate food-safety measures. This means that the parents are not jointly and severally liable for the student's illness. Therefore, the student is unlikely to succeed in her negligence suit. (Choice A) A plaintiff can sue multiple defendants for the same harm based on joint and several liability. However, the plaintiff must still prove that each defendant acted negligently. (Choice C) In a joint and several liability action, the burden shifts to each defendant to prove that his/her negligence did not cause the plaintiff's harm only after the plaintiff proves that each defendant was negligent (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because there is no evidence that each of the parents was negligent."},"questionZh":"一所私立学校举办了一场烘焙义卖活动以筹集资金。\n\n三位家长各自在家中按自己的配方制作了巧克力豆饼干。\n\n他们在指定日期将饼干带到学校，由一名志愿者将饼干分装在小盘子里进行售卖。\n\n每个小盘子里都至少包含来自这三位家长中每人制作的一块饼干。\n\n几位购买了义卖食品的人随后患病，并被诊断为食物中毒。\n\n经调查，卫生部门认定巧克力豆饼干是导致此次疫情的源头，且其中一位制作饼干的家长未遵守适当的食品安全措施。\n\n一名购买了一盘巧克力豆饼干的学生病情严重，不得不入院治疗。\n\n她以疏忽为由起诉了制作这些饼干的三位家长，主张他们应对其受到的伤害承担连带责任。\n\n该学生能够确凿地证明巧克力豆饼干是其致病原因，但无法确定具体是哪位家长制作了受污染的饼干。\n\n该学生在疏忽诉讼中胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为学生不能因同一伤害起诉多名被告。","B":"不大，因为没有证据表明每位家长都存在疏忽行为。","C":"大，因为家长们有责任证明他们并未造成该学生的伤害。","D":"大，因为家长们是协同行动，共同通过烘焙义卖筹集资金。"},"explanationZh":"当两名或多名被告存在过失，且其中任何一方都可能导致了原告遭受的不可分割的损害时，便会产生连带责任。\n\n这意味着原告可以向任何一名有过失的被告追索全部损害赔偿金，即使无法证明具体是哪一方造成了损害。\n\n然而，原告必须首先证明每名被告都存在过失。\n\n在本案中，父母双方并非共同行动（即并非基于共同计划或意图），因为他们各自在自己家中、按照各自的食谱制作饼干（选项 D）。\n\n此外，也没有证据表明每位家长都存在过失，因为卫生部门仅认定有人（而非所有人）未遵守适当的食品安全措施。\n\n这意味着家长们无需为该学生的患病承担连带责任。\n\n因此，该学生不太可能在过失诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）原告可以基于连带责任就同一损害起诉多名被告。\n\n然而，原告仍须证明每名被告的行为存在过失。\n\n（选项 C）在连带责任诉讼中，只有在原告证明每名被告均存在过失之后（本案未出现此情况），举证责任才会转移给各被告，由其证明自身的过失并未导致原告的损害。\n\n教育目标：如果多名被告存在过失，且其中任何一方都可能导致了原告的损害，那么连带责任允许原告向其中任何一方追索赔偿，即使无法证明具体是哪名被告造成了损害。\n\n但原告必须首先证明每名被告都存在过失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 875 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（阐明了导致不可分割损害的多名侵权行为人可就全部损害承担个人责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果多名被告均存在疏忽行为，且其中任何一人都可能造成了原告的伤害，那么连带责任原则允许原告向其中任何一人寻求赔偿，即使无法证明具体是哪位被告造成了伤害。\n\n但原告必须首先证明每位被告都存在疏忽行为。","iracZh":{"issue":"该学生在疏忽诉讼中胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"如果多名被告均存在疏忽行为，且其中任何一人都可能造成了原告的伤害，那么连带责任原则允许原告向其中任何一人寻求赔偿，即使无法证明具体是哪位被告造成了伤害。\n\n但原告必须首先证明每位被告都存在疏忽行为。","analysis":"当两名或多名被告存在过失，且其中任何一方都可能导致了原告遭受的不可分割的损害时，便会产生连带责任。\n\n这意味着原告可以向任何一名有过失的被告追索全部损害赔偿金，即使无法证明具体是哪一方造成了损害。\n\n然而，原告必须首先证明每名被告都存在过失。\n\n在本案中，父母双方并非共同行动（即并非基于共同计划或意图），因为他们各自在自己家中、按照各自的食谱制作饼干（选项 D）。\n\n此外，也没有证据表明每位家长都存在过失，因为卫生部门仅认定有人（而非所有人）未遵守适当的食品安全措施。\n\n这意味着家长们无需为该学生的患病承担连带责任。\n\n因此，该学生不太可能在过失诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）原告可以基于连带责任就同一损害起诉多名被告。\n\n然而，原告仍须证明每名被告的行为存在过失。\n\n（选项 C）在连带责任诉讼中，只有在原告证明每名被告均存在过失之后（本案未出现此情况），举证责任才会转移给各被告，由其证明自身的过失并未导致原告的损害。\n\n教育目标：如果多名被告存在过失，且其中任何一方都可能导致了原告的损害，那么连带责任允许原告向其中任何一方追索赔偿，即使无法证明具体是哪名被告造成了损害。\n\n但原告必须首先证明每名被告都存在过失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 875 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（阐明了导致不可分割损害的多名侵权行为人可就全部损害承担个人责任）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不大，因为没有证据表明每位家长都存在疏忽行为。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":104,"sourceQuestionPage":313,"sourceQuestionPages":[313],"sourceExplanationPages":[314],"sourceAnswerPages":[314],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-105","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A homeowner was injured when he slipped and fell in a puddle of water on his sunroom floor; the water had accumulated on the floor during a rainstorm because of leaks in the roof. The roof's manufacturer had supplied nondefective materials to the installer, who was a franchisee (and not an employee) of the manufacturer. The leaks resulted from the carelessness of the installer during the installation of the roof. The installer's truck, which had been parked in front of the homeowner's house during the roof installation, bore the manufacturer's logo. The manufacturer was aware that the truck and the literature supplied by the installer both displayed the manufacturer's logo. Is there any basis for a claim by the homeowner against the manufacturer?","choices":{"A":"No, because a franchisor has no duty to supervise the conduct of a franchisee.","B":"No, under the rule that a manufacturer is liable only for defects in a product that existed at the time the product left the hands of the manufacturer.","C":"Yes, because the installer was a franchisee of the manufacturer.","D":"Yes, under the rule of apparent agency."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A defendant may be vicariously liable for a third party's torts if an agency relationship existed between them—ie, when the third party agreed to act on the defendant's behalf and subject to the defendant's control. Since a franchisor does not control a franchisee's day-to- day operations (no agency relationship), the franchisor is generally not vicariously liable for the franchisee's torts. But under the rule of apparent agency (ie, apparent authority), vicarious liability can be imposed if: the plaintiff reasonably believed that the third party was acting within the scope of an agency relationship with the defendant and\n\nthat belief was traceable to the defendant's manifestations (ie, words or conduct).\n\nHere, the manufacturer (franchisor) would generally not be vicariously liable for the installer's (franchisee's) conduct (Choice C). But the homeowner could have reasonably believed that the installer was acting as the manufacturer's agent since the installer's truck and literature displayed the manufacturer's logo. And since the manufacturer knew about this and permitted the installer to do so, that belief was traceable to the manufacturer's manifestations. Therefore, the homeowner has a basis for a claim against the manufacturer under the rule of apparent agency. (Choice A) Although a franchisor is not directly liable for a franchisee's conduct (since the franchisor has no duty to supervise the franchisee), a franchisor can still be vicariously liable for a franchisee's torts under the rule of apparent agency. (Choice B) Under strict products liability, a manufacturer is only liable for product defects that existed when the product left the manufacturer's hands. Here, the manufacturer supplied nondefective materials to the installer so there is no basis for a strict products liability claim.\n\nEducational objective: Under the rule of apparent agency, a defendant may be vicariously liable for a third party's torts if (1) the plaintiff reasonably believed that the third party was acting within the scope of an agency relationship with the defendant and (2) that belief was traceable to the defendant's manifestations.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Agency § 1.01 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (definition of agency).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Agency § 2.03 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (apparent agency).","educationalObjective":"Under the rule of apparent agency, a defendant may be vicariously liable for a third party's torts if (1) the plaintiff reasonably believed that the third party was acting within the scope of an agency relationship with the defendant and (2) that belief was traceable to the defendant's manifestations.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Agency § 1.01 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (definition of agency).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Agency § 2.03 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (apparent agency).","rule":"Under the rule of apparent agency, a defendant may be vicariously liable for a third party's torts if (1) the plaintiff reasonably believed that the third party was acting within the scope of an agency relationship with the defendant and (2) that belief was traceable to the defendant's manifestations.","irac":{"issue":"Is there any basis for a claim by the homeowner against the manufacturer?","rule":"Under the rule of apparent agency, a defendant may be vicariously liable for a third party's torts if (1) the plaintiff reasonably believed that the third party was acting within the scope of an agency relationship with the defendant and (2) that belief was traceable to the defendant's manifestations.","analysis":"A defendant may be vicariously liable for a third party's torts if an agency relationship existed between them—ie, when the third party agreed to act on the defendant's behalf and subject to the defendant's control. Since a franchisor does not control a franchisee's day-to- day operations (no agency relationship), the franchisor is generally not vicariously liable for the franchisee's torts. But under the rule of apparent agency (ie, apparent authority), vicarious liability can be imposed if: the plaintiff reasonably believed that the third party was acting within the scope of an agency relationship with the defendant and\n\nthat belief was traceable to the defendant's manifestations (ie, words or conduct).\n\nHere, the manufacturer (franchisor) would generally not be vicariously liable for the installer's (franchisee's) conduct (Choice C). But the homeowner could have reasonably believed that the installer was acting as the manufacturer's agent since the installer's truck and literature displayed the manufacturer's logo. And since the manufacturer knew about this and permitted the installer to do so, that belief was traceable to the manufacturer's manifestations. Therefore, the homeowner has a basis for a claim against the manufacturer under the rule of apparent agency. (Choice A) Although a franchisor is not directly liable for a franchisee's conduct (since the franchisor has no duty to supervise the franchisee), a franchisor can still be vicariously liable for a franchisee's torts under the rule of apparent agency. (Choice B) Under strict products liability, a manufacturer is only liable for product defects that existed when the product left the manufacturer's hands. Here, the manufacturer supplied nondefective materials to the installer so there is no basis for a strict products liability claim.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, under the rule of apparent agency."},"questionZh":"一位房主因在阳光房地板的积水中滑倒而受伤；这些积水是暴雨期间因屋顶漏水而形成的。\n\n屋顶制造商向安装人员提供了无缺陷的材料，而该安装人员是制造商的特许经营商（而非雇员）。\n\n漏水是由于安装人员在安装屋顶时的疏忽造成的。\n\n安装人员的卡车在安装期间停在房主家门前，车身上印有制造商的标志。\n\n制造商知晓该卡车及安装人员提供的宣传资料上均印有其标志。\n\n房主针对制造商提出索赔是否有依据？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为特许人没有义务监督特许经营商的行为。","B":"没有，根据制造商仅对产品离开其控制时已存在的缺陷承担责任的规则。","C":"有，因为安装人员是制造商的特许经营商。","D":"有，根据表见代理（apparent agency）规则。"},"explanationZh":"如果被告与第三方之间存在代理关系——即第三方同意代表被告行事并受被告控制——被告可能需对第三方的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n由于特许人（franchisor）并不控制被特许人（franchisee）的日常运营（即不存在代理关系），特许人通常无需对被特许人的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n但在表见代理（apparent agency，即表见授权/apparent authority）规则下，若满足以下条件，则可能产生替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）：原告合理地相信第三方是在与被告的代理关系范围内行事，且 该种相信源于被告的表象行为（即言语或行为）。\n\n在本案中，制造商（特许人）通常无需对安装人员（被特许人）的行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（选项 C）。\n\n但房主完全有理由相信安装人员是作为制造商的代理人行事，因为安装人员的卡车和宣传资料上都展示了制造商的标识。\n\n此外，由于制造商知晓并允许安装人员这样做，这种相信便源于制造商的表象行为。\n\n因此，根据表见代理规则，房主有依据向制造商提出索赔。\n\n（选项 A）尽管特许人不对被特许人的行为承担直接责任（因为特许人没有监督被特许人的义务），但特许人仍可能根据表见代理规则对被特许人的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 B）根据严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）原则，制造商仅对产品离开其控制时已存在的产品缺陷承担责任。\n\n在本案中，制造商向安装人员提供了无缺陷的材料，因此不存在提出严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔的依据。\n\n教育目标：根据表见代理规则，如果 (1) 原告合理地相信第三方是在与被告的代理关系范围内行事，且 (2) 这种相信源于被告的表象行为，则被告可能需对第三方的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n参考文献 《代理法重述（第三版）》第 1.01 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（代理的定义）。\n\n《代理法重述（第三次）》第2.03条（美国法律协会，2006年）（表见代理）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据表见代理规则，如果 (1) 原告合理地相信第三方是在与被告的代理关系范围内行事，且 (2) 这种相信源于被告的表示（manifestations），则被告可能需对第三方的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","iracZh":{"issue":"房主针对制造商提出索赔是否有依据？","rule":"根据表见代理规则，如果 (1) 原告合理地相信第三方是在与被告的代理关系范围内行事，且 (2) 这种相信源于被告的表示（manifestations），则被告可能需对第三方的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","analysis":"如果被告与第三方之间存在代理关系——即第三方同意代表被告行事并受被告控制——被告可能需对第三方的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n由于特许人（franchisor）并不控制被特许人（franchisee）的日常运营（即不存在代理关系），特许人通常无需对被特许人的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n但在表见代理（apparent agency，即表见授权/apparent authority）规则下，若满足以下条件，则可能产生替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）：原告合理地相信第三方是在与被告的代理关系范围内行事，且 该种相信源于被告的表象行为（即言语或行为）。\n\n在本案中，制造商（特许人）通常无需对安装人员（被特许人）的行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（选项 C）。\n\n但房主完全有理由相信安装人员是作为制造商的代理人行事，因为安装人员的卡车和宣传资料上都展示了制造商的标识。\n\n此外，由于制造商知晓并允许安装人员这样做，这种相信便源于制造商的表象行为。\n\n因此，根据表见代理规则，房主有依据向制造商提出索赔。\n\n（选项 A）尽管特许人不对被特许人的行为承担直接责任（因为特许人没有监督被特许人的义务），但特许人仍可能根据表见代理规则对被特许人的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 B）根据严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）原则，制造商仅对产品离开其控制时已存在的产品缺陷承担责任。\n\n在本案中，制造商向安装人员提供了无缺陷的材料，因此不存在提出严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔的依据。\n\n教育目标：根据表见代理规则，如果 (1) 原告合理地相信第三方是在与被告的代理关系范围内行事，且 (2) 这种相信源于被告的表象行为，则被告可能需对第三方的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n参考文献 《代理法重述（第三版）》第 1.01 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（代理的定义）。\n\n《代理法重述（第三次）》第2.03条（美国法律协会，2006年）（表见代理）。","conclusion":"答案 D：有，根据表见代理（apparent agency）规则。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-bef4528975b78923"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":105,"sourceQuestionPage":315,"sourceQuestionPages":[315],"sourceExplanationPages":[316,317],"sourceAnswerPages":[316,317],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-106","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A student had weekly sessions with a psychotherapist. During every session, the student would tell the psychotherapist that he was convinced that his roommate was impersonating him and slowly trying to take over his life. For the past several weeks, the student had become more and more agitated when speaking about the roommate, and at their most recent session, the student told the psychotherapist about his plans to kill the roommate. The student explained to her that he had not quite figured out how he was going to carry out his plan, but that he planned to kill the roommate before the upcoming winter break. A statute in the jurisdiction imposes an affirmative duty to act \"for the protection of another whom the individual has found in peril.\" Is the psychotherapist under a legal duty to warn the roommate?","choices":{"A":"No, because the psychotherapist has no affirmative duty to act.","B":"No, because the student's threats of future harm do not put the roommate in imminent danger.","C":"Yes, because the student has made credible threats of physical violence against the roommate.","D":"Yes, because under the statute, the psychotherapist has an affirmative duty to act."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Affirmative duty to act\n\nAssumption of Defendant who voluntarily aids or rescues another has duty to use duty reasonable care when rendering aid or performing rescue\n\nCreation of Defendant whose conduct creates foreseeable risk of harm or places risk another in peril has duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent further harm by rendering care or aid\n\nBy contract Duty to use care when performing contractual obligations\n\nBy authority Duty to exercise reasonable control over third party whom defendant has actual ability & authority to control\n\nExamples: parent over child, custodian over person in custody, employer over employee, mental-health professional over patient By Duty to protect, aid, or assist plaintiff with whom defendant shares relationship unique relationship\n\nExamples: business proprietor with patron, common carrier with passenger, innkeeper with guest, employer with employee, parent with child By statute Statute imposes obligation to act for another's protection\n\nLand Duty to mitigate risks posed by natural or artificial conditions on land possessor\n\nA person generally has no duty to control another unless the parties share a special relationship. Mental-health professionals share a special relationship with their patients, so they have a duty to use reasonable care to identify dangerous patients and mitigate the risks they pose to others. So when a patient makes a credible threat of physical violence against an ascertainable victim, the mental-health professional has a duty to warn the would-be victim or take other steps to mitigate the risk posed by the patient (eg, notify law enforcement).* Here, the student told the psychotherapist that he planned to kill his roommate before the upcoming winter break. This was a credible threat of physical violence, especially since the student had become increasingly agitated when speaking to the psychotherapist about the roommate over the past several weeks. And because of the psychotherapist's special relationship with the student, she has a legal duty to warn the roommate of the student's plan (Choice A).\n\n*Whether a threat of physical violence is credible is determined by the objective standard of a reasonable mental-health professional in the same circumstance. (Choice B) Threats of future harm do not put others in apprehension of imminent danger and therefore cannot support a claim for assault. But imminence is irrelevant in determining whether a mental-health professional's duty to warn has attached. (Choice D) The psychotherapist's duty to warn the roommate arises from the psychotherapist's special relationship with the student who poses a risk of harm to the roommate—not from the jurisdiction's statute. Additionally, it is unclear whether the statute would even apply here since the psychotherapist did not necessarily \"find the roommate in peril.\"\n\nEducational objective: A special relationship exists between mental-health professionals and their patients. Therefore, if a patient makes a credible threat of physical violence against an ascertainable victim, the mental-health professional must warn the would-be victim of the risk posed by the patient or take other steps to mitigate that risk.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 41 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (duty to third parties based on special relationship with person posing risks).","educationalObjective":"A special relationship exists between mental-health professionals and their patients. Therefore, if a patient makes a credible threat of physical violence against an ascertainable victim, the mental-health professional must warn the would-be victim of the risk posed by the patient or take other steps to mitigate that risk.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 41 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (duty to third parties based on special relationship with person posing risks).","rule":"A special relationship exists between mental-health professionals and their patients. Therefore, if a patient makes a credible threat of physical violence against an ascertainable victim, the mental-health professional must warn the would-be victim of the risk posed by the patient or take other steps to mitigate that risk.","irac":{"issue":"A student had weekly sessions with a psychotherapist. During every session, the student would tell the psychotherapist that he was convinced that his roommate was impersonating him and slowly trying to take over his life. For the past several weeks, the student had become more and more agitated when speaking about the roommate, and at their most recent session, the student told the psychotherapist about his plans to kill the roommate. The student explained to her that he had not quite figured out how he was going to carry out his plan, but that he planned to kill the roommate before the upcoming winter break. A statute in the jurisdiction imposes an affirmative duty to act \"for the protection of another whom the individual has found in peril.\" Is the psychotherapist under a legal duty to warn the roommate?","rule":"A special relationship exists between mental-health professionals and their patients. Therefore, if a patient makes a credible threat of physical violence against an ascertainable victim, the mental-health professional must warn the would-be victim of the risk posed by the patient or take other steps to mitigate that risk.","analysis":"Affirmative duty to act\n\nAssumption of Defendant who voluntarily aids or rescues another has duty to use duty reasonable care when rendering aid or performing rescue\n\nCreation of Defendant whose conduct creates foreseeable risk of harm or places risk another in peril has duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent further harm by rendering care or aid\n\nBy contract Duty to use care when performing contractual obligations\n\nBy authority Duty to exercise reasonable control over third party whom defendant has actual ability & authority to control\n\nExamples: parent over child, custodian over person in custody, employer over employee, mental-health professional over patient By Duty to protect, aid, or assist plaintiff with whom defendant shares relationship unique relationship\n\nExamples: business proprietor with patron, common carrier with passenger, innkeeper with guest, employer with employee, parent with child By statute Statute imposes obligation to act for another's protection\n\nLand Duty to mitigate risks posed by natural or artificial conditions on land possessor\n\nA person generally has no duty to control another unless the parties share a special relationship. Mental-health professionals share a special relationship with their patients, so they have a duty to use reasonable care to identify dangerous patients and mitigate the risks they pose to others. So when a patient makes a credible threat of physical violence against an ascertainable victim, the mental-health professional has a duty to warn the would-be victim or take other steps to mitigate the risk posed by the patient (eg, notify law enforcement).* Here, the student told the psychotherapist that he planned to kill his roommate before the upcoming winter break. This was a credible threat of physical violence, especially since the student had become increasingly agitated when speaking to the psychotherapist about the roommate over the past several weeks. And because of the psychotherapist's special relationship with the student, she has a legal duty to warn the roommate of the student's plan (Choice A).\n\n*Whether a threat of physical violence is credible is determined by the objective standard of a reasonable mental-health professional in the same circumstance. (Choice B) Threats of future harm do not put others in apprehension of imminent danger and therefore cannot support a claim for assault. But imminence is irrelevant in determining whether a mental-health professional's duty to warn has attached. (Choice D) The psychotherapist's duty to warn the roommate arises from the psychotherapist's special relationship with the student who poses a risk of harm to the roommate—not from the jurisdiction's statute. Additionally, it is unclear whether the statute would even apply here since the psychotherapist did not necessarily \"find the roommate in peril.\"","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the student has made credible threats of physical violence against the roommate."},"questionZh":"一名学生每周与心理治疗师进行一次会谈。\n\n在每次会谈中，该学生都会告诉心理治疗师，他确信自己的室友正在冒充他，并试图慢慢接管他的生活。\n\n过去几周里，每当谈及室友时，该学生的情绪都变得越来越激动；在最近一次会谈中，他向心理治疗师透露了杀害室友的计划。\n\n他向治疗师解释说，虽然尚未完全确定如何实施该计划，但他打算在即将到来的寒假前杀掉室友。\n\n该司法管辖区的一项法规规定，当个人发现他人处于危险之中时，有义务采取行动“以保护该他人”。\n\n心理治疗师是否有法律义务向该室友发出警告？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为心理治疗师没有采取行动的积极义务。","B":"没有，因为该学生关于未来伤害的威胁并未使室友处于迫在眉睫的危险之中。","C":"有，因为该学生针对室友发出了可信的身体暴力威胁。","D":"有，因为根据该法规，心理治疗师有采取行动的积极义务。"},"explanationZh":"作为义务（Affirmative duty to act） 自愿承担（Assumption）：自愿向他人提供援助或进行营救的被告，在提供援助或实施营救时有义务尽到合理的注意。 因行为引发（Creation）：被告的行为若造成了可预见的伤害风险，或使他人陷入危险境地，则有义务通过提供照料或援助来尽到合理的注意，以防止进一步的伤害。 基于合同：在履行合同义务时有义务尽到合理的注意。 基于权力：对于被告有实际能力及权力进行控制的第三方，被告有义务行使合理的控制。 例子：父母对子女、监护人对被监护人、雇主对雇员、心理健康专业人员对患者。基于保护、援助或协助原告的义务（双方存在特殊关系）。 例子：商家与顾客、公共承运人与乘客、旅馆经营者与住客、雇主与雇员、父母与子女。基于制定法：法律规定了为保护他人而采取行动的义务。 土地：土地占有人有义务减轻因土地上的自然或人工状况而产生的风险。 通常情况下，除非双方存在特殊关系，否则一个人没有义务控制他人。\n\n心理健康专业人员与患者之间存在特殊关系，因此他们有义务尽到合理的注意，识别危险患者并减轻这些患者对他人的风险。\n\n因此，当患者针对特定受害者发出可信的身体暴力威胁时，心理健康专业人员有义务警告潜在受害者或采取其他措施以减轻患者构成的风险（例如，通知执法部门）。* 在本案中，该学生告诉心理治疗师，他计划在即将到来的寒假前杀害室友。\n\n这是一个可信的身体暴力威胁，尤其是考虑到该学生在过去几周与心理治疗师谈论室友时情绪日益激动。\n\n鉴于心理治疗师与该学生之间的特殊关系，她有法律义务就该学生的计划向室友发出警告（选项 A）。\n\n*身体暴力威胁是否可信，取决于处于相同情况下的“理性心理健康专业人员”这一客观标准。\n\n（选项 B）关于未来伤害的威胁不会使他人产生迫在眉睫的危险感，因此无法支持“袭击”（assault）的诉讼主张。\n\n但在确定心理健康专业人员的警告义务是否产生时，“迫在眉睫性”（imminence）这一因素无关紧要。\n\n(选项 D) 心理治疗师向室友发出警告的义务，源于其与那位对室友构成伤害风险的学生之间的特殊关系，而非源于该司法管辖区的成文法。\n\n此外，鉴于心理治疗师未必“发现室友处于危难之中”，该成文法是否适用于本案尚不明确。\n\n教育目标：心理健康专业人员与其患者之间存在特殊关系。\n\n因此，如果患者针对特定受害者发出可信的身体暴力威胁，心理健康专业人员必须向潜在受害者发出警告，告知其面临的由该患者构成的风险，或采取其他措施以降低该风险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 41 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（基于与构成风险者的特殊关系而产生的对第三方的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"心理健康专业人员与其患者之间存在一种特殊关系。\n\n因此，如果患者针对一名可确定的受害者发出可信的身体暴力威胁，心理健康专业人员必须警告该潜在受害者注意患者构成的风险，或采取其他措施以降低该风险。","iracZh":{"issue":"一名学生每周与心理治疗师进行一次会谈。\n\n在每次会谈中，该学生都会告诉心理治疗师，他确信自己的室友正在冒充他，并试图慢慢接管他的生活。\n\n过去几周里，每当谈及室友时，该学生的情绪都变得越来越激动；在最近一次会谈中，他向心理治疗师透露了杀害室友的计划。\n\n他向治疗师解释说，虽然尚未完全确定如何实施该计划，但他打算在即将到来的寒假前杀掉室友。\n\n该司法管辖区的一项法规规定，当个人发现他人处于危险之中时，有义务采取行动“以保护该他人”。\n\n心理治疗师是否有法律义务向该室友发出警告？","rule":"心理健康专业人员与其患者之间存在一种特殊关系。\n\n因此，如果患者针对一名可确定的受害者发出可信的身体暴力威胁，心理健康专业人员必须警告该潜在受害者注意患者构成的风险，或采取其他措施以降低该风险。","analysis":"作为义务（Affirmative duty to act） 自愿承担（Assumption）：自愿向他人提供援助或进行营救的被告，在提供援助或实施营救时有义务尽到合理的注意。 因行为引发（Creation）：被告的行为若造成了可预见的伤害风险，或使他人陷入危险境地，则有义务通过提供照料或援助来尽到合理的注意，以防止进一步的伤害。 基于合同：在履行合同义务时有义务尽到合理的注意。 基于权力：对于被告有实际能力及权力进行控制的第三方，被告有义务行使合理的控制。 例子：父母对子女、监护人对被监护人、雇主对雇员、心理健康专业人员对患者。基于保护、援助或协助原告的义务（双方存在特殊关系）。 例子：商家与顾客、公共承运人与乘客、旅馆经营者与住客、雇主与雇员、父母与子女。基于制定法：法律规定了为保护他人而采取行动的义务。 土地：土地占有人有义务减轻因土地上的自然或人工状况而产生的风险。 通常情况下，除非双方存在特殊关系，否则一个人没有义务控制他人。\n\n心理健康专业人员与患者之间存在特殊关系，因此他们有义务尽到合理的注意，识别危险患者并减轻这些患者对他人的风险。\n\n因此，当患者针对特定受害者发出可信的身体暴力威胁时，心理健康专业人员有义务警告潜在受害者或采取其他措施以减轻患者构成的风险（例如，通知执法部门）。* 在本案中，该学生告诉心理治疗师，他计划在即将到来的寒假前杀害室友。\n\n这是一个可信的身体暴力威胁，尤其是考虑到该学生在过去几周与心理治疗师谈论室友时情绪日益激动。\n\n鉴于心理治疗师与该学生之间的特殊关系，她有法律义务就该学生的计划向室友发出警告（选项 A）。\n\n*身体暴力威胁是否可信，取决于处于相同情况下的“理性心理健康专业人员”这一客观标准。\n\n（选项 B）关于未来伤害的威胁不会使他人产生迫在眉睫的危险感，因此无法支持“袭击”（assault）的诉讼主张。\n\n但在确定心理健康专业人员的警告义务是否产生时，“迫在眉睫性”（imminence）这一因素无关紧要。\n\n(选项 D) 心理治疗师向室友发出警告的义务，源于其与那位对室友构成伤害风险的学生之间的特殊关系，而非源于该司法管辖区的成文法。\n\n此外，鉴于心理治疗师未必“发现室友处于危难之中”，该成文法是否适用于本案尚不明确。\n\n教育目标：心理健康专业人员与其患者之间存在特殊关系。\n\n因此，如果患者针对特定受害者发出可信的身体暴力威胁，心理健康专业人员必须向潜在受害者发出警告，告知其面临的由该患者构成的风险，或采取其他措施以降低该风险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 41 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（基于与构成风险者的特殊关系而产生的对第三方的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 C：有，因为该学生针对室友发出了可信的身体暴力威胁。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":106,"sourceQuestionPage":318,"sourceQuestionPages":[318],"sourceExplanationPages":[319,320],"sourceAnswerPages":[319,320],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-107","subject":"Torts","topic":"Damages & Joint Liability","question":"An elderly neighbor hired a 17-year-old boy with a reputation for reckless driving to drive the neighbor on errands once a week. One day the teenager, driving the neighbor's car, took the neighbor to the grocery store. While the neighbor was in the store, the teenager drove out of the parking lot and headed for a party on the other side of town. While on his way to the party, the teenager negligently turned in front of a moving car and caused a collision. The other driver was injured in the collision. The injured driver has brought an action for damages against the neighbor, based on negligent entrustment, and against the teenager. The jury has found that the injured driver's damages were $100,000, that the injured driver was 10% at fault, that the teenager was 60% at fault, and that the neighbor was 30% at fault for entrusting his car to the teenager. Based on these damage and responsibility amounts, what is the maximum that the injured driver could recover from the neighbor?","choices":{"A":"$100,000.","B":"$90,000.","C":"$60,000.","D":"$30,000."},"answer":"B","explanation":"To determine the amount of compensatory damages a plaintiff may recover in a negligence action, the following rules apply (unless the question otherwise indicates): Pure comparative negligence – when the plaintiff's own negligence contributes to his/her harm, the plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault\n\nJoint and several liability – when multiple defendants cause the plaintiff indivisible harm, the plaintiff can recover the total amount of damages from any defendant (who can then sue the others for contribution)\n\nHere, the jury determined that the injured driver sustained $100,000 in damages and apportioned liability as follows: the driver (10%), the neighbor (30%), and the teenager (60%). The driver's recovery will be reduced by his proportionate share of fault ($100,000 - $10,000 = $90,000) (Choice A). The driver may then recover the $90,000 from any of the defendants, including the neighbor.* *The neighbor could then bring a contribution action against the teenager for $60,000—the amount attributable to the teenager's fault. (Choices C & D) Had the jurisdiction adopted several liability (not seen here), each defendant would have been liable only for his/her proportionate share of fault. This would mean that the driver could only recover $30,000 from the neighbor for negligently entrusting his car and $60,000 from the teenager for his negligent driving (as opposed to recovering the full $90,000 from either defendant).\n\nEducational objective: Under pure comparative negligence, a negligent plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault. And if multiple defendants cause the plaintiff indivisible harm, joint and several liability allows the plaintiff to recover the total amount of recoverable damages from any defendant.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liab. § 7 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (pure comparative negligence).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liab. § 10 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (joint and several liability).","educationalObjective":"Under pure comparative negligence, a negligent plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault. And if multiple defendants cause the plaintiff indivisible harm, joint and several liability allows the plaintiff to recover the total amount of recoverable damages from any defendant.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liab. § 7 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (pure comparative negligence).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liab. § 10 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (joint and several liability).","rule":"Under pure comparative negligence, a negligent plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault. And if multiple defendants cause the plaintiff indivisible harm, joint and several liability allows the plaintiff to recover the total amount of recoverable damages from any defendant.","irac":{"issue":"Based on these damage and responsibility amounts, what is the maximum that the injured driver could recover from the neighbor?","rule":"Under pure comparative negligence, a negligent plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault. And if multiple defendants cause the plaintiff indivisible harm, joint and several liability allows the plaintiff to recover the total amount of recoverable damages from any defendant.","analysis":"To determine the amount of compensatory damages a plaintiff may recover in a negligence action, the following rules apply (unless the question otherwise indicates): Pure comparative negligence – when the plaintiff's own negligence contributes to his/her harm, the plaintiff's recovery is reduced by his/her proportionate share of fault\n\nJoint and several liability – when multiple defendants cause the plaintiff indivisible harm, the plaintiff can recover the total amount of damages from any defendant (who can then sue the others for contribution)\n\nHere, the jury determined that the injured driver sustained $100,000 in damages and apportioned liability as follows: the driver (10%), the neighbor (30%), and the teenager (60%). The driver's recovery will be reduced by his proportionate share of fault ($100,000 - $10,000 = $90,000) (Choice A). The driver may then recover the $90,000 from any of the defendants, including the neighbor.* *The neighbor could then bring a contribution action against the teenager for $60,000—the amount attributable to the teenager's fault. (Choices C & D) Had the jurisdiction adopted several liability (not seen here), each defendant would have been liable only for his/her proportionate share of fault. This would mean that the driver could only recover $30,000 from the neighbor for negligently entrusting his car and $60,000 from the teenager for his negligent driving (as opposed to recovering the full $90,000 from either defendant).","conclusion":"Answer B: $90,000."},"questionZh":"一位年长的邻居雇了一名以鲁莽驾驶著称的17岁少年，每周开车带他去办事。\n\n一天，这名少年驾驶邻居的车带他去杂货店。\n\n当邻居在店里时，少年把车开出停车场，前往城另一头的一个聚会。\n\n在去聚会的路上，少年因疏忽大意，在行驶中的车辆前方转弯，引发了碰撞事故。\n\n另一名司机在碰撞中受伤。\n\n受伤司机针对邻居（基于“过失委托”原则）和那名少年提起了损害赔偿诉讼。\n\n陪审团认定：受伤司机的损失总额为100,000美元；受伤司机本人承担10%的过失责任；少年承担60%的过失责任；邻居因将车委托给该少年而承担30%的过失责任。\n\n根据这些损失金额和责任比例，受伤司机最多能从邻居那里获得多少赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"100,000美元。","B":"90,000美元。","C":"60,000美元。","D":"30,000美元。"},"explanationZh":"在确定原告于过失诉讼中可获得的补偿性损害赔偿金额时，适用以下规则（除非题目另有说明）：纯粹比较过失原则（pure comparative negligence）——当原告自身的过失导致了其受到的损害时，原告的获赔金额将扣除其过失所占的比例份额； 连带责任原则（joint and several liability）——当多名被告共同导致原告遭受不可分割的损害时，原告可向任一被告追索全部损害赔偿金（该被告随后可向其他被告追偿相应的分担份额）。 在本案中，陪审团认定受伤驾驶员遭受了10万美元的损害，并将责任分配如下：驾驶员（10%）、邻居（30%）和青少年（60%）。\n\n驾驶员的获赔金额将扣除其过失比例份额（10万美元 - 1万美元 = 9万美元）（选项A）。\n\n随后，驾驶员可向任一被告（包括邻居）追索这9万美元。* *邻居随后可针对青少年提起追偿诉讼，要求其承担6万美元的责任——即对应于青少年过失行为的份额。\n\n（选项C和D）如果该司法管辖区采用的是按份责任制（本案未采用），则每位被告仅需对其过失比例份额承担责任。\n\n这意味着驾驶员只能就邻居的“过失交付车辆”行为向其追索3万美元，并就青少年的“过失驾驶”行为向其追索6万美元（而不是向任一被告追索全部9万美元）。\n\n教学目标：在纯粹比较过失原则下，有过失的原告其获赔金额会扣除其过失比例份额。\n\n此外，若多名被告导致原告遭受不可分割的损害，连带责任原则允许原告向任一被告追索全部可获赔的损害赔偿金。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任分担》第7条（美国法律协会，2000年）（纯粹比较过失原则）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任分担》第10条（美国法律协会，2000年）（连带责任原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在“纯粹比较过失”原则下，有过失的原告所获赔偿会按其过失比例相应扣减。\n\n此外，如果多名被告共同造成了原告不可分割的损害，根据“连带责任”原则，原告可以向任一被告追索全部可获赔偿的金额。","iracZh":{"issue":"根据这些损失金额和责任比例，受伤司机最多能从邻居那里获得多少赔偿？","rule":"在“纯粹比较过失”原则下，有过失的原告所获赔偿会按其过失比例相应扣减。\n\n此外，如果多名被告共同造成了原告不可分割的损害，根据“连带责任”原则，原告可以向任一被告追索全部可获赔偿的金额。","analysis":"在确定原告于过失诉讼中可获得的补偿性损害赔偿金额时，适用以下规则（除非题目另有说明）：纯粹比较过失原则（pure comparative negligence）——当原告自身的过失导致了其受到的损害时，原告的获赔金额将扣除其过失所占的比例份额； 连带责任原则（joint and several liability）——当多名被告共同导致原告遭受不可分割的损害时，原告可向任一被告追索全部损害赔偿金（该被告随后可向其他被告追偿相应的分担份额）。 在本案中，陪审团认定受伤驾驶员遭受了10万美元的损害，并将责任分配如下：驾驶员（10%）、邻居（30%）和青少年（60%）。\n\n驾驶员的获赔金额将扣除其过失比例份额（10万美元 - 1万美元 = 9万美元）（选项A）。\n\n随后，驾驶员可向任一被告（包括邻居）追索这9万美元。* *邻居随后可针对青少年提起追偿诉讼，要求其承担6万美元的责任——即对应于青少年过失行为的份额。\n\n（选项C和D）如果该司法管辖区采用的是按份责任制（本案未采用），则每位被告仅需对其过失比例份额承担责任。\n\n这意味着驾驶员只能就邻居的“过失交付车辆”行为向其追索3万美元，并就青少年的“过失驾驶”行为向其追索6万美元（而不是向任一被告追索全部9万美元）。\n\n教学目标：在纯粹比较过失原则下，有过失的原告其获赔金额会扣除其过失比例份额。\n\n此外，若多名被告导致原告遭受不可分割的损害，连带责任原则允许原告向任一被告追索全部可获赔的损害赔偿金。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任分担》第7条（美国法律协会，2000年）（纯粹比较过失原则）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任分担》第10条（美国法律协会，2000年）（连带责任原则）。","conclusion":"答案 B：90,000美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-b1000a62ed9b15cd"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":107,"sourceQuestionPage":321,"sourceQuestionPages":[321],"sourceExplanationPages":[322,323],"sourceAnswerPages":[322,323],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-108","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"While visiting at his son's home, a grandfather tripped on a toy left on the floor by his four- year-old grandson. The grandfather fell and was severely injured. The grandfather regularly visited his son's home and was aware that the grandson routinely left toys scattered about the house. The son had never warned the grandfather to look out for toys. The grandfather brought an action against his son to recover for his injuries. At trial, after the close of evidence, both the grandfather and the son have moved for judgment as a matter of law as to liability. The jurisdiction has abolished intra-family immunity and applies the traditional rules of landowner liability. What action should the court take?","choices":{"A":"Deny both motions and submit the case to the jury based on negligence.","B":"Deny both motions and submit the case to the jury based on strict liability.","C":"Grant the grandfather's motion, because the son is liable as a matter of law for failing to warn about the risk of toys being left on the floor.","D":"Grant the son's motion, because the son had no duty to warn that the grandson might leave toys on the floor."},"answer":"D","explanation":"After the parties have presented their evidence to the jury, any party may move for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL). This motion asks the judge to bypass the jury and render a decision in favor of the moving party because no reasonable jury could find in favor of the opposing party. Therefore, a judge should grant a defendant's motion for JMOL if there is legally insufficient evidence to support any element of the plaintiff's claim. In a negligence claim, the plaintiff must first prove that the defendant owed a duty to the plaintiff. When the defendant is a land possessor, the duty owed to the plaintiff under the traditional rules of landowner liability depends on the plaintiff's status on the land. Many land entrants are licensees—ie, persons who enter another's land with permission (eg, social guests). And a land possessor has a duty to warn licensees about latent (ie, hidden) dangers of which the land possessor is, or should be, aware. Here, the grandfather (plaintiff) was injured when he tripped on his grandson's toy. The grandfather was a licensee because he was visiting his son's (defendant's) home at the time. But since the grandfather already knew that his grandson routinely left toys scattered about the house, the son had no duty to warn the grandfather that toys might be on the floor. And since the grandfather cannot establish this element of negligence, the court should grant the son's motion (and deny the grandfather's motion) for JMOL (Choices A & C). (Choice B) Strict liability (ie, liability regardless of fault) is appropriate only in limited situations—none of which apply here.\n\nEducational objective: A land possessor traditionally owes licensees (eg, social guests) a duty to warn about latent dangers of which the land possessor is, or should be, aware.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 342 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (duty to licensees).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (judgment as a matter of law).","educationalObjective":"A land possessor traditionally owes licensees (eg, social guests) a duty to warn about latent dangers of which the land possessor is, or should be, aware.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 342 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (duty to licensees).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (judgment as a matter of law).","rule":"A land possessor traditionally owes licensees (eg, social guests) a duty to warn about latent dangers of which the land possessor is, or should be, aware.","irac":{"issue":"What action should the court take?","rule":"A land possessor traditionally owes licensees (eg, social guests) a duty to warn about latent dangers of which the land possessor is, or should be, aware.","analysis":"After the parties have presented their evidence to the jury, any party may move for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL). This motion asks the judge to bypass the jury and render a decision in favor of the moving party because no reasonable jury could find in favor of the opposing party. Therefore, a judge should grant a defendant's motion for JMOL if there is legally insufficient evidence to support any element of the plaintiff's claim. In a negligence claim, the plaintiff must first prove that the defendant owed a duty to the plaintiff. When the defendant is a land possessor, the duty owed to the plaintiff under the traditional rules of landowner liability depends on the plaintiff's status on the land. Many land entrants are licensees—ie, persons who enter another's land with permission (eg, social guests). And a land possessor has a duty to warn licensees about latent (ie, hidden) dangers of which the land possessor is, or should be, aware. Here, the grandfather (plaintiff) was injured when he tripped on his grandson's toy. The grandfather was a licensee because he was visiting his son's (defendant's) home at the time. But since the grandfather already knew that his grandson routinely left toys scattered about the house, the son had no duty to warn the grandfather that toys might be on the floor. And since the grandfather cannot establish this element of negligence, the court should grant the son's motion (and deny the grandfather's motion) for JMOL (Choices A & C). (Choice B) Strict liability (ie, liability regardless of fault) is appropriate only in limited situations—none of which apply here.","conclusion":"Answer D: Grant the son's motion, because the son had no duty to warn that the grandson might leave toys on the floor."},"questionZh":"一位祖父在探访儿子家时，被四岁孙子留在地板上的玩具绊倒。\n\n祖父摔倒并受了重伤。\n\n祖父经常去儿子家，也知道孙子习惯把玩具散落在屋里各处。\n\n儿子从未提醒过祖父要留意这些玩具。\n\n祖父针对儿子提起诉讼，要求赔偿其受伤损失。\n\n在庭审中，证据提交阶段结束后，祖父和儿子双方均就责任问题提出了“法律问题判决”（即要求法院直接作出判决）的动议。\n\n该司法管辖区已废除家庭成员间免责原则（intra-family immunity），并适用传统的土地占有者责任规则。\n\n法院应采取什么行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回双方动议，并将案件基于过失责任原则提交给陪审团审理。","B":"驳回双方动议，并将案件基于严格责任原则提交给陪审团审理。","C":"支持祖父的动议，因为儿子因未就地板上遗留玩具的风险发出警告，在法律上应承担责任。","D":"支持儿子的动议，因为儿子没有义务警告他人孙子可能会把玩具留在地板上。"},"explanationZh":"当事各方向陪审团提交证据后，任何一方均可提出“法律问题判决”（JMOL）的动议。\n\n该动议请求法官绕过陪审团，直接作出有利于动议方的裁决，理由是没有任何理性的陪审团会作出有利于对方的裁定。\n\n因此，如果支持原告主张之任何构成要件的证据在法律上不足，法官应准予被告提出的JMOL动议。\n\n在过失索赔案中，原告必须首先证明被告对原告负有注意义务。\n\n当被告为土地占有者时，根据传统的土地所有者责任规则，其对原告负有的义务取决于原告在土地上的身份。\n\n许多进入土地的人员属于“被许可人”（licensees）——即经许可进入他人土地的人（例如社交访客）。\n\n土地占有者有义务向被许可人提示其已知或应知的潜在（即隐蔽的）危险。\n\n在本案中，祖父（原告）因被孙子的玩具绊倒而受伤。\n\n祖父属于被许可人，因为他当时正在造访儿子（被告）的住所。\n\n然而，鉴于祖父已知悉孙子习惯将玩具散落在屋内，儿子并无义务提醒祖父地板上可能有玩具。\n\n由于祖父无法确立过失索赔的这一构成要件，法院应准予儿子提出的JMOL动议（并驳回祖父的动议）（选项A和C）。\n\n（选项B）严格责任（即不考虑过错的责任）仅适用于有限的情形——而本案并不属于这些情形。\n\n教育目标：根据传统规则，土地占有者对被许可人（如社交访客）负有义务，须提示其已知或应知的潜在危险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第342条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于对被许可人的义务）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第50(a)条（法律问题判决）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据传统规则，土地占有者对被许可人（例如社交访客）负有警告义务，即须告知其已知或应知的潜在危险。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应采取什么行动？","rule":"根据传统规则，土地占有者对被许可人（例如社交访客）负有警告义务，即须告知其已知或应知的潜在危险。","analysis":"当事各方向陪审团提交证据后，任何一方均可提出“法律问题判决”（JMOL）的动议。\n\n该动议请求法官绕过陪审团，直接作出有利于动议方的裁决，理由是没有任何理性的陪审团会作出有利于对方的裁定。\n\n因此，如果支持原告主张之任何构成要件的证据在法律上不足，法官应准予被告提出的JMOL动议。\n\n在过失索赔案中，原告必须首先证明被告对原告负有注意义务。\n\n当被告为土地占有者时，根据传统的土地所有者责任规则，其对原告负有的义务取决于原告在土地上的身份。\n\n许多进入土地的人员属于“被许可人”（licensees）——即经许可进入他人土地的人（例如社交访客）。\n\n土地占有者有义务向被许可人提示其已知或应知的潜在（即隐蔽的）危险。\n\n在本案中，祖父（原告）因被孙子的玩具绊倒而受伤。\n\n祖父属于被许可人，因为他当时正在造访儿子（被告）的住所。\n\n然而，鉴于祖父已知悉孙子习惯将玩具散落在屋内，儿子并无义务提醒祖父地板上可能有玩具。\n\n由于祖父无法确立过失索赔的这一构成要件，法院应准予儿子提出的JMOL动议（并驳回祖父的动议）（选项A和C）。\n\n（选项B）严格责任（即不考虑过错的责任）仅适用于有限的情形——而本案并不属于这些情形。\n\n教育目标：根据传统规则，土地占有者对被许可人（如社交访客）负有义务，须提示其已知或应知的潜在危险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第342条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（关于对被许可人的义务）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第50(a)条（法律问题判决）。","conclusion":"答案 D：支持儿子的动议，因为儿子没有义务警告他人孙子可能会把玩具留在地板上。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-b8e8dc2dea04944b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":108,"sourceQuestionPage":324,"sourceQuestionPages":[324],"sourceExplanationPages":[325,326],"sourceAnswerPages":[325,326],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-109","subject":"Torts","topic":"Privileges & Defenses","question":"A woman went hunting in a remote forest at the beginning of winter, when no other hunters were in the area. Tired after a long day and fearing a possible snowstorm, the woman decided to stay at her brother-in-law's cottage rather than drive home. The cottage was located near several identical cottages, all of which were unoccupied. The woman entered a cottage that she thought was owned by her brother-in-law but actually belonged to someone else. The owner of the cottage knew that uninvited hunters used the cottage during the summer and that one of those hunters had damaged the fireplace, rendering it hazardous to use. However, because the owner had posted no warning about the defective fireplace, the woman had no idea that it was defective and started the fireplace. Due to the defect in the fireplace, the cottage went up in flames, causing her to sustain third-degree burns on her arms and legs. The woman sued the owner of the cottage to recover for her injuries. Is the woman likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the owner had no duty to warn trespassers of the defective fireplace.","B":"No, because the woman was an unknown and unanticipated trespasser.","C":"Yes, because it was reasonably necessary for the woman to take shelter in the cottage and start the fireplace for warmth.","D":"Yes, because the owner knew that the fireplace was defective and had an affirmative duty to repair it."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Land possessor's duty to land entrants\n\n(traditional approach)*\n\nStatus Characteristics Duty\n\nTrespasser Intentionally enters land Known or frequent trespassers – warn of without permission known artificial dangers & use reasonable care in active operations Unknown or unanticipated trespassers – no duty Licensee Enters land with permission Warn of known latent dangers & use (eg, social guest) reasonable care in active operations Invitee Enters land open to public Inspect for unknown dangers (eg, churchgoer) Make premises safe or provide adequate Enters land for business warnings purpose (eg, store customer) Prevent harm from active operations *In jurisdictions that follow the modern approach, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants, except flagrant trespassers.\n\nA land possessor owes a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable plaintiffs who enter the land. But a land possessor owes no duty to trespassers—ie, persons who intentionally enter another's land without permission—unless the land possessor knows of or has reason to anticipate their presence. A land possessor who breaches this duty and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for negligence. Here, the woman was a trespasser because she intentionally entered the cottage without the owner's permission, thinking that it belonged to her brother-in-law. Her presence was unforeseeable because the owner (1) did not know that the woman was present and (2) had no reason to anticipate her presence since no other hunters were in the area during winter. The owner therefore did not owe the woman a duty of care, and she likely will not prevail. (Choice A) A land possessor has a duty to warn known or anticipated trespassers (here, the summer hunters) about known, artificial (ie, man-made) dangers. But since the woman was an unknown and unanticipated trespasser, the owner owed the woman no such duty. (Choice C) Despite the woman's fear of a possible snowstorm, it was not necessary for her to stay at the cottage because she could have driven home (ie, no private necessity). But this fact is irrelevant because the owner's duty depends on whether the woman's presence was foreseeable.\n\n(Choice D) Land possessors generally have a duty to use reasonable care to prevent harm to persons on their land (eg, to repair known defects). But this duty does not extend to unanticipated trespassers such as the woman.\n\nEducational objective: A land possessor owes no duty of reasonable care to unforeseeable plaintiffs (eg, undiscovered or unanticipated trespassers).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 7 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (duty of reasonable care).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 333 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (no duty to unknown trespassers).","educationalObjective":"A land possessor owes no duty of reasonable care to unforeseeable plaintiffs (eg, undiscovered or unanticipated trespassers).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts § 7 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (duty of reasonable care).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 333 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (no duty to unknown trespassers).","rule":"A land possessor owes no duty of reasonable care to unforeseeable plaintiffs (eg, undiscovered or unanticipated trespassers).","irac":{"issue":"Is the woman likely to prevail?","rule":"A land possessor owes no duty of reasonable care to unforeseeable plaintiffs (eg, undiscovered or unanticipated trespassers).","analysis":"Land possessor's duty to land entrants\n\n(traditional approach)*\n\nStatus Characteristics Duty\n\nTrespasser Intentionally enters land Known or frequent trespassers – warn of without permission known artificial dangers & use reasonable care in active operations Unknown or unanticipated trespassers – no duty Licensee Enters land with permission Warn of known latent dangers & use (eg, social guest) reasonable care in active operations Invitee Enters land open to public Inspect for unknown dangers (eg, churchgoer) Make premises safe or provide adequate Enters land for business warnings purpose (eg, store customer) Prevent harm from active operations *In jurisdictions that follow the modern approach, land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants, except flagrant trespassers.\n\nA land possessor owes a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable plaintiffs who enter the land. But a land possessor owes no duty to trespassers—ie, persons who intentionally enter another's land without permission—unless the land possessor knows of or has reason to anticipate their presence. A land possessor who breaches this duty and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for negligence. Here, the woman was a trespasser because she intentionally entered the cottage without the owner's permission, thinking that it belonged to her brother-in-law. Her presence was unforeseeable because the owner (1) did not know that the woman was present and (2) had no reason to anticipate her presence since no other hunters were in the area during winter. The owner therefore did not owe the woman a duty of care, and she likely will not prevail. (Choice A) A land possessor has a duty to warn known or anticipated trespassers (here, the summer hunters) about known, artificial (ie, man-made) dangers. But since the woman was an unknown and unanticipated trespasser, the owner owed the woman no such duty. (Choice C) Despite the woman's fear of a possible snowstorm, it was not necessary for her to stay at the cottage because she could have driven home (ie, no private necessity). But this fact is irrelevant because the owner's duty depends on whether the woman's presence was foreseeable.\n\n(Choice D) Land possessors generally have a duty to use reasonable care to prevent harm to persons on their land (eg, to repair known defects). But this duty does not extend to unanticipated trespassers such as the woman.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the woman was an unknown and unanticipated trespasser."},"questionZh":"初冬时节，一位女性前往偏远森林打猎，当时该区域并无其他猎人。\n\n经过一整天的劳累，加上担心可能出现暴风雪，她决定在姐夫（或妹夫）的小屋过夜，而不开车回家。\n\n这间小屋位于几间外观相同的小屋群中，且当时均无人居住。\n\n该女子走进了一间她误以为属于其姐夫（或妹夫）的小屋，但实际上该小屋属于他人。\n\n小屋主人知道，夏季常有不请自来的猎人使用该小屋，且其中一名猎人曾损坏壁炉，导致其存在使用隐患。\n\n然而，由于主人未就壁炉的缺陷设置任何警示标志，该女子在不知情的情况下点燃了壁炉。\n\n因壁炉存在缺陷，小屋起火，导致她手臂和腿部遭受三度烧伤。\n\n该女子起诉小屋主人，要求赔偿其所受伤害。\n\n该女子胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为主人没有义务向侵入者就壁炉的缺陷发出警告。","B":"不大，因为该女子属于未被发现且未被预料到的侵入者。","C":"大，因为该女子进入小屋避难并点燃壁炉取暖是合理必要的。","D":"大，因为主人知道壁炉存在缺陷，并负有修复它的积极义务。"},"explanationZh":"土地占有者对进入土地者的义务 （传统做法）* 身份 特征 义务 **侵入者** 未经许可故意进入土地 **已知或频繁出现的侵入者** – 告知已知的非自然（人为）危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务；**未知或未预料到的侵入者** – 无义务 **被许可人** 经许可进入土地（如社交访客） 告知已知的隐蔽危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务 **受邀者** 进入向公众开放的土地（如去教堂的人）；为商业目的进入土地（如商店顾客） 检查是否存在未知危险；确保场所安全或提供充分警示；防止因活动造成伤害 *在采用现代做法的司法管辖区，土地占有者对所有进入土地者（恶劣侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n土地占有者对进入其土地的可预见原告负有合理注意义务。但土地占有者对侵入者（即未经许可故意进入他人土地的人）不负此义务，除非占有者知道或有理由预料到他们的存在。\n\n若土地占有者违反此义务并导致原告身体伤害，则须承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，该女子属于侵入者，因为她在未经房主许可的情况下故意进入小屋，误以为那是她姐夫/妹夫的房产。\n\n她的出现是不可预见的，因为房主：(1) 不知道该女子的存在；(2) 没有理由预料到她的出现，因为冬季该地区没有其他猎人。\n\n因此，房主对该女子不负注意义务，她很可能无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）土地占有者有义务向已知或可预见的侵入者（此处指夏季猎人）告知已知的非自然（即人为）危险。\n\n但由于该女子属于未知且未被预料到的侵入者，房主对她不负此义务。\n\n（选项 C）尽管该女子担心可能发生暴风雪，但她并无必要留在小屋内，因为她本可以开车回家（即不构成“私法上的紧急避险”）。\n\n但这并非关键事实，因为房主的义务取决于该女子的出现是否可预见。\n\n（选项 D）土地占有人通常有义务尽合理注意，以防止其土地上的人员受到伤害（例如，修复已知的缺陷）。\n\n但该义务并不适用于像这名女子那样未被预料到的侵入者。\n\n教育目标：土地占有人对不可预见原告（例如，未被发现或未被预料到的侵入者）不负有合理注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 7 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（合理注意义务）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 333 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（对未知的侵入者不负义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地占有者对不可预见原告（例如未被发现或未被预料到的侵入者）不负有合理注意义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"该女子胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"土地占有者对不可预见原告（例如未被发现或未被预料到的侵入者）不负有合理注意义务。","analysis":"土地占有者对进入土地者的义务 （传统做法）* 身份 特征 义务 **侵入者** 未经许可故意进入土地 **已知或频繁出现的侵入者** – 告知已知的非自然（人为）危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务；**未知或未预料到的侵入者** – 无义务 **被许可人** 经许可进入土地（如社交访客） 告知已知的隐蔽危险，并在进行活动时尽到合理注意义务 **受邀者** 进入向公众开放的土地（如去教堂的人）；为商业目的进入土地（如商店顾客） 检查是否存在未知危险；确保场所安全或提供充分警示；防止因活动造成伤害 *在采用现代做法的司法管辖区，土地占有者对所有进入土地者（恶劣侵入者除外）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n土地占有者对进入其土地的可预见原告负有合理注意义务。但土地占有者对侵入者（即未经许可故意进入他人土地的人）不负此义务，除非占有者知道或有理由预料到他们的存在。\n\n若土地占有者违反此义务并导致原告身体伤害，则须承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，该女子属于侵入者，因为她在未经房主许可的情况下故意进入小屋，误以为那是她姐夫/妹夫的房产。\n\n她的出现是不可预见的，因为房主：(1) 不知道该女子的存在；(2) 没有理由预料到她的出现，因为冬季该地区没有其他猎人。\n\n因此，房主对该女子不负注意义务，她很可能无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）土地占有者有义务向已知或可预见的侵入者（此处指夏季猎人）告知已知的非自然（即人为）危险。\n\n但由于该女子属于未知且未被预料到的侵入者，房主对她不负此义务。\n\n（选项 C）尽管该女子担心可能发生暴风雪，但她并无必要留在小屋内，因为她本可以开车回家（即不构成“私法上的紧急避险”）。\n\n但这并非关键事实，因为房主的义务取决于该女子的出现是否可预见。\n\n（选项 D）土地占有人通常有义务尽合理注意，以防止其土地上的人员受到伤害（例如，修复已知的缺陷）。\n\n但该义务并不适用于像这名女子那样未被预料到的侵入者。\n\n教育目标：土地占有人对不可预见原告（例如，未被发现或未被预料到的侵入者）不负有合理注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第 7 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（合理注意义务）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 333 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（对未知的侵入者不负义务）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不大，因为该女子属于未被发现且未被预料到的侵入者。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":109,"sourceQuestionPage":327,"sourceQuestionPages":[327],"sourceExplanationPages":[328,329],"sourceAnswerPages":[328,329],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-110","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Breach & Proof","question":"A patient in a hospital was placed in a wheelchair with his broken leg extended straight out in front of him. As a nurse employed by the hospital was pushing the wheelchair through a set of automatic doors at a normal pace, the doors closed on the patient's foot, injuring it. The nurse attempted to pull the wheelchair back through the doors. This action caused the doors to close more tightly on the patient's foot, injuring it further. The patient sued the hospital, alleging improper maintenance of the doors. The patient has produced no evidence of specific conduct or neglect on the part of the hospital that would have caused the automatic doors to malfunction. The hospital has moved for summary judgment. Should the court grant the hospital's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because a jury could find that there was a latent defect in the doors.","B":"No, because a jury could find the hospital liable for negligence based on res ipsa loquitur.","C":"Yes, because proof of an accident, by itself, does not establish that an injured person was a victim of negligence.","D":"Yes, because the nurse's action was a superseding cause of the injury."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Negligence doctrines\n\nApplicability Effect\n\nRes ipsa Plaintiff suffered type of harm usually Permissible inference that loquitur caused by someone in defendant's defendant was negligent position Evidence eliminates other potential causes of harm Negligence Defendant violated statute Rebuttable presumption that per se Statute was intended to prevent plaintiff's defendant was negligent type of harm Plaintiff is within class of persons statute was intended to protect Lawsuits alleging improper maintenance of equipment are often based on negligence, which generally requires direct evidence that the defendant was negligent. But in the absence of such evidence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows the fact finder (eg, jury) to infer that the defendant was negligent if: the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm. Here, the hospital's automatic doors closed on the patient's foot, injuring it. Although there is no direct evidence of the hospital's negligence, automatic doors should not malfunction when properly maintained. And though the nurse's action caused the doors to close more tightly on the patient's foot, injuring it further, the nurse was pushing his wheelchair at a normal pace when the initial injury occurred—eliminating other potential causes of harm. Therefore, a jury could infer the hospital's negligence, and the court should deny its motion for summary judgment. (Choice A) Product defects can also be inferred under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur. But that inference is inappropriate here since (1) the patient sued the hospital—not a seller of automatic doors—and (2) the patient alleged improper maintenance—not a latent defect. (Choice C) Proof of an accident, by itself, does not establish that an injured person was a victim of negligence. But here, the hospital's negligence can be inferred from the nature and circumstances of the patient's accident. (Choice D) The nurse's action was not a superseding cause of the patient's injury since it was foreseeable that a nurse would (1) push patients in wheelchairs through the hospital's doors and (2) attempt to help a patient who had been injured by a hospital door.\n\nEducational objective: Negligence can be inferred under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a defendant's negligence can be inferred based on the type of accident that injured the plaintiff).","educationalObjective":"Negligence can be inferred under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a defendant's negligence can be inferred based on the type of accident that injured the plaintiff).","rule":"Negligence can be inferred under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the hospital's motion?","rule":"Negligence can be inferred under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","analysis":"Negligence doctrines\n\nApplicability Effect\n\nRes ipsa Plaintiff suffered type of harm usually Permissible inference that loquitur caused by someone in defendant's defendant was negligent position Evidence eliminates other potential causes of harm Negligence Defendant violated statute Rebuttable presumption that per se Statute was intended to prevent plaintiff's defendant was negligent type of harm Plaintiff is within class of persons statute was intended to protect Lawsuits alleging improper maintenance of equipment are often based on negligence, which generally requires direct evidence that the defendant was negligent. But in the absence of such evidence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows the fact finder (eg, jury) to infer that the defendant was negligent if: the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm. Here, the hospital's automatic doors closed on the patient's foot, injuring it. Although there is no direct evidence of the hospital's negligence, automatic doors should not malfunction when properly maintained. And though the nurse's action caused the doors to close more tightly on the patient's foot, injuring it further, the nurse was pushing his wheelchair at a normal pace when the initial injury occurred—eliminating other potential causes of harm. Therefore, a jury could infer the hospital's negligence, and the court should deny its motion for summary judgment. (Choice A) Product defects can also be inferred under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur. But that inference is inappropriate here since (1) the patient sued the hospital—not a seller of automatic doors—and (2) the patient alleged improper maintenance—not a latent defect. (Choice C) Proof of an accident, by itself, does not establish that an injured person was a victim of negligence. But here, the hospital's negligence can be inferred from the nature and circumstances of the patient's accident. (Choice D) The nurse's action was not a superseding cause of the patient's injury since it was foreseeable that a nurse would (1) push patients in wheelchairs through the hospital's doors and (2) attempt to help a patient who had been injured by a hospital door.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because a jury could find the hospital liable for negligence based on res ipsa loquitur."},"questionZh":"一位病人被安置在医院的轮椅上，他骨折的腿笔直地伸在身前。\n\n当医院的一名护士以正常速度推着轮椅穿过自动门时，门突然关上，夹住了病人的脚，导致其受伤。\n\n护士试图将轮椅拉回，但这一举动反而使门关得更紧，进一步加重了病人的伤势。\n\n病人起诉医院，指控医院对自动门维护不当。\n\n病人没有提供任何证据证明医院存在导致自动门故障的具体行为或疏忽。\n\n医院已提出简易判决动议。\n\n法院是否应该批准医院的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应该，因为陪审团可能会认定自动门存在潜在缺陷。","B":"不应该，因为陪审团可能会基于“事实本身说明问题”原则认定医院存在过失。","C":"是的，因为仅凭事故发生本身并不能证明受伤者是过失的受害者。","D":"是的，因为护士的行为是造成伤害的直接原因。"},"explanationZh":"过失法理 法理名称 | 适用情形 | 法律效力 **事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则** (Res ipsa loquitur) | 原告遭受的损害通常由处于被告位置的人的过失引起；证据排除了其他潜在致害原因 | 允许推断被告存在过失 **违反法规即构成过失** (Negligence per se) | 被告违反了法规；法规旨在防止原告所受的那类损害；原告属于法规旨在保护的人群 | 可推翻的推定：被告存在过失 指控设备维护不当的诉讼通常基于过失，这一般需要直接证据证明被告存在过失。\n\n但在缺乏此类证据的情况下，“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则”（res ipsa loquitur）允许事实认定者（如陪审团）推断被告存在过失，前提是：原告遭受的损害通常由处于被告位置的人的过失引起，且证据倾向于排除该损害的其他潜在成因。\n\n在本案中，医院的自动门夹住了病人的脚并造成伤害。\n\n尽管没有直接证据表明医院存在过失，但维护得当的自动门不应发生故障。\n\n虽然护士的动作导致门更紧地夹住病人的脚并加重了伤害，但在最初受伤发生时，护士正以正常速度推着轮椅——这排除了其他潜在的致害原因。\n\n因此，陪审团可以推断医院存在过失，法院应驳回医院提出的即决判决（summary judgment）动议。\n\n（选项 A）根据事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则，也可以推断存在产品缺陷。\n\n但在此案中，这种推断并不恰当，因为：(1) 病人起诉的是医院，而非自动门销售商；(2) 病人主张的是维护不当，而非存在潜在缺陷。\n\n（选项 C）仅凭发生事故这一事实，并不能确立受害人是过失行为的受害者。\n\n但在此案中，可以根据病人事故的性质和情况推断出医院存在过失。\n\n（选项 D）护士的动作并非病人受伤的“阻断性原因”（superseding cause），因为护士 (1) 推着坐轮椅的病人通过医院大门，以及 (2) 试图帮助被医院大门夹伤的病人，这些行为都是可预见的。\n\n教育目标：根据“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”（res ipsa loquitur）原则，若满足以下条件，即可推断存在过失：(1) 原告所受伤害的类型通常是由处于被告地位的人的过失造成的；以及 (2) 证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 328D 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（阐述了在何种情况下，可根据导致原告受伤的事故类型推断被告存在过失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“事实本身说明问题”原则，如果满足以下条件，则可以推断存在过失：(1) 原告遭受的损害通常是由被告这种身份的人的过失造成的；(2) 证据倾向于排除其他可能导致该损害的原因。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应该批准医院的动议？","rule":"根据“事实本身说明问题”原则，如果满足以下条件，则可以推断存在过失：(1) 原告遭受的损害通常是由被告这种身份的人的过失造成的；(2) 证据倾向于排除其他可能导致该损害的原因。","analysis":"过失法理 法理名称 | 适用情形 | 法律效力 **事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则** (Res ipsa loquitur) | 原告遭受的损害通常由处于被告位置的人的过失引起；证据排除了其他潜在致害原因 | 允许推断被告存在过失 **违反法规即构成过失** (Negligence per se) | 被告违反了法规；法规旨在防止原告所受的那类损害；原告属于法规旨在保护的人群 | 可推翻的推定：被告存在过失 指控设备维护不当的诉讼通常基于过失，这一般需要直接证据证明被告存在过失。\n\n但在缺乏此类证据的情况下，“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则”（res ipsa loquitur）允许事实认定者（如陪审团）推断被告存在过失，前提是：原告遭受的损害通常由处于被告位置的人的过失引起，且证据倾向于排除该损害的其他潜在成因。\n\n在本案中，医院的自动门夹住了病人的脚并造成伤害。\n\n尽管没有直接证据表明医院存在过失，但维护得当的自动门不应发生故障。\n\n虽然护士的动作导致门更紧地夹住病人的脚并加重了伤害，但在最初受伤发生时，护士正以正常速度推着轮椅——这排除了其他潜在的致害原因。\n\n因此，陪审团可以推断医院存在过失，法院应驳回医院提出的即决判决（summary judgment）动议。\n\n（选项 A）根据事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则，也可以推断存在产品缺陷。\n\n但在此案中，这种推断并不恰当，因为：(1) 病人起诉的是医院，而非自动门销售商；(2) 病人主张的是维护不当，而非存在潜在缺陷。\n\n（选项 C）仅凭发生事故这一事实，并不能确立受害人是过失行为的受害者。\n\n但在此案中，可以根据病人事故的性质和情况推断出医院存在过失。\n\n（选项 D）护士的动作并非病人受伤的“阻断性原因”（superseding cause），因为护士 (1) 推着坐轮椅的病人通过医院大门，以及 (2) 试图帮助被医院大门夹伤的病人，这些行为都是可预见的。\n\n教育目标：根据“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”（res ipsa loquitur）原则，若满足以下条件，即可推断存在过失：(1) 原告所受伤害的类型通常是由处于被告地位的人的过失造成的；以及 (2) 证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 328D 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（阐述了在何种情况下，可根据导致原告受伤的事故类型推断被告存在过失）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不应该，因为陪审团可能会基于“事实本身说明问题”原则认定医院存在过失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":110,"sourceQuestionPage":330,"sourceQuestionPages":[330],"sourceExplanationPages":[331,332],"sourceAnswerPages":[331,332],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-111","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Breach & Proof","question":"Under the Federal Tort Claims Act, with certain exceptions not relevant here, the federal government is liable only for negligence. A federally owned and operated nuclear reactor emitted substantial quantities of radioactive matter that settled on a nearby dairy farm, killing the dairy herd and contaminating the soil. At the trial of an action brought against the federal government by the farm's owner, the trier of fact found the following: (1) the nuclear plant had a sound design, but a valve made by an engineering company had malfunctioned and allowed the radioactive matter to escape; (2) the engineering company was universally regarded as a quality manufacturer of components for nuclear plants; and (3) there was no way the federal government could have anticipated or prevented the emission of the radioactive matter. If there is no other applicable statute, for which party should the court enter judgment?","choices":{"A":"The farm owner, on the ground that one who allows dangerous material to escape to the property of another is liable for the damage done.","B":"The farm owner, on the ground that the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur applies.","C":"The government, on the ground that a case under the Federal Tort Claims Act has not been proved.","D":"The government, on the ground that the engineering company is the proximate cause of the farm owner's damage."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Elements of negligence\n\nDuty – defendant owed duty of reasonable care because his/her conduct created foreseeable risk of harm to others Breach – defendant breached duty by failing to use reasonable care Causation – defendant's breach actually & proximately caused plaintiff's harm Damages – plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, bodily injury or property damage) Land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care when they know or should know that an activity on the land poses an unreasonable risk of physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage) to others. A land possessor who breaches this duty and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for negligence. Here, the federally owned and operated nuclear reactor emitted radioactive matter that killed the farm owner's dairy herd and contaminated his soil. But since the federal government could not have anticipated the emission, it could not have known that the reactor posed an unreasonable risk of harm. Therefore, the court should enter judgment for the government on the ground that negligence cannot be proved. (Choice A) Strict liability—ie, liability without proof of fault—is imposed for harm caused by abnormally dangerous activities. Operating a nuclear reactor may be abnormally dangerous. But under the Federal Tort Claims Act, the federal government is liable only for negligence. (Choice B) The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur would not permit an inference of negligence by the federal government here. That is because the nuclear reactor is complex machinery that could have malfunctioned for reasons other than negligence by the federal government—eg, negligence by the engineering company that made its parts. (Choice D) More than one defendant may be a proximate cause of the plaintiff's harm. Therefore, the fact that the engineering company proximately caused the farm owner's damage would not eliminate the federal government's liability had that damage also been a foreseeable consequence of the government's conduct.\n\nEducational objective: Land possessors have a duty of reasonable care when they know or should know that an activity on the land poses an unreasonable risk of physical harm to others (ie, when such a risk is foreseeable).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 371 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that land possessors are subject to liability for harm caused by activities on their land that pose an unreasonable risk of harm to others).","educationalObjective":"Land possessors have a duty of reasonable care when they know or should know that an activity on the land poses an unreasonable risk of physical harm to others (ie, when such a risk is foreseeable).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 371 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that land possessors are subject to liability for harm caused by activities on their land that pose an unreasonable risk of harm to others).","rule":"Land possessors have a duty of reasonable care when they know or should know that an activity on the land poses an unreasonable risk of physical harm to others (ie, when such a risk is foreseeable).","irac":{"issue":"If there is no other applicable statute, for which party should the court enter judgment?","rule":"Land possessors have a duty of reasonable care when they know or should know that an activity on the land poses an unreasonable risk of physical harm to others (ie, when such a risk is foreseeable).","analysis":"Elements of negligence\n\nDuty – defendant owed duty of reasonable care because his/her conduct created foreseeable risk of harm to others Breach – defendant breached duty by failing to use reasonable care Causation – defendant's breach actually & proximately caused plaintiff's harm Damages – plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, bodily injury or property damage) Land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care when they know or should know that an activity on the land poses an unreasonable risk of physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage) to others. A land possessor who breaches this duty and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for negligence. Here, the federally owned and operated nuclear reactor emitted radioactive matter that killed the farm owner's dairy herd and contaminated his soil. But since the federal government could not have anticipated the emission, it could not have known that the reactor posed an unreasonable risk of harm. Therefore, the court should enter judgment for the government on the ground that negligence cannot be proved. (Choice A) Strict liability—ie, liability without proof of fault—is imposed for harm caused by abnormally dangerous activities. Operating a nuclear reactor may be abnormally dangerous. But under the Federal Tort Claims Act, the federal government is liable only for negligence. (Choice B) The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur would not permit an inference of negligence by the federal government here. That is because the nuclear reactor is complex machinery that could have malfunctioned for reasons other than negligence by the federal government—eg, negligence by the engineering company that made its parts. (Choice D) More than one defendant may be a proximate cause of the plaintiff's harm. Therefore, the fact that the engineering company proximately caused the farm owner's damage would not eliminate the federal government's liability had that damage also been a foreseeable consequence of the government's conduct.","conclusion":"Answer C: The government, on the ground that a case under the Federal Tort Claims Act has not been proved."},"questionZh":"根据《联邦侵权索赔法》，除某些与本案无关的例外情况外，联邦政府仅对过失行为承担责任。\n\n一座联邦政府所有并运营的核反应堆排放了大量放射性物质，这些物质沉降到附近的一家奶牛场，导致奶牛死亡并污染了土壤。\n\n在农场主对联邦政府提起的诉讼中，事实审理者认定：（1）核电站设计合理，但一家工程公司制造的阀门发生故障，导致放射性物质泄漏；（2）该工程公司被普遍认为是核电站部件的优质制造商；（3）联邦政府无法预见或阻止放射性物质的泄漏。\n\n如果没有其他适用的法律，法院应判决哪一方胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"农场主胜诉，理由是允许危险物质泄漏到他人财产的人应对造成的损害承担责任。","B":"农场主，理由是“事实本身说明问题”原则适用。","C":"政府，理由是《联邦侵权索赔法》下的案件尚未得到证实。","D":"政府，理由是工程公司是造成农场主损害的直接原因。"},"explanationZh":"过失侵权的构成要素 注意义务（Duty）——被告负有合理注意义务，因为其行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；违反义务（Breach）——被告因未尽合理注意义务而违反了该义务；因果关系（Causation）——被告违反义务的行为实际上且在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）上导致了原告的损害；损害后果（Damages）——原告遭受了实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。当土地占有者知道或应当知道其土地上的某项活动给他人造成了不合理的实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）风险时，即负有合理注意义务。\n\n违反此义务并导致原告遭受实质性损害的土地占有者，须承担过失侵权责任。\n\n在本案中，由联邦政府拥有并运营的核反应堆释放了放射性物质，导致农场主的奶牛群死亡并污染了其土壤。\n\n然而，由于联邦政府无法预见此次泄漏，因此也就无法知晓该反应堆构成了不合理的伤害风险。\n\n因此，法院应判决政府胜诉，理由是无法证明存在过失。\n\n（选项 A）对于因“异常危险活动”造成的损害，适用严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担责任）原则。\n\n运营核反应堆可能属于异常危险活动。\n\n但根据《联邦侵权索赔法》（Federal Tort Claims Act），联邦政府仅对过失行为承担责任。\n\n（选项 B）在本案中，“事实本身说明过失”（res ipsa loquitur）原则不足以推断联邦政府存在过失。\n\n这是因为核反应堆属于复杂机械设备，其发生故障的原因可能并非联邦政府的过失所致——例如，可能是由制造其零部件的工程公司的过失造成的。\n\n（选项 D）导致原告损害的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）可能涉及多个被告。\n\n因此，即使工程公司是导致农场主损害的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause），如果该损害同时也是联邦政府行为的可预见后果，联邦政府仍须承担责任。\n\n教育目标：当土地占有者知道或应当知道其土地上的某项活动给他人造成了不合理的实质性损害风险（即该风险是可预见的）时，即负有合理注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第371条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明土地占有人须对其土地上构成对他人不合理伤害风险的活动所造成的损害承担责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地占有者在明知或应当知道其土地上的活动会对他人造成不合理的身体伤害风险时（即，当这种风险是可预见的），负有合理的注意义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果没有其他适用的法律，法院应判决哪一方胜诉？","rule":"土地占有者在明知或应当知道其土地上的活动会对他人造成不合理的身体伤害风险时（即，当这种风险是可预见的），负有合理的注意义务。","analysis":"过失侵权的构成要素 注意义务（Duty）——被告负有合理注意义务，因为其行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；违反义务（Breach）——被告因未尽合理注意义务而违反了该义务；因果关系（Causation）——被告违反义务的行为实际上且在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）上导致了原告的损害；损害后果（Damages）——原告遭受了实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。当土地占有者知道或应当知道其土地上的某项活动给他人造成了不合理的实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）风险时，即负有合理注意义务。\n\n违反此义务并导致原告遭受实质性损害的土地占有者，须承担过失侵权责任。\n\n在本案中，由联邦政府拥有并运营的核反应堆释放了放射性物质，导致农场主的奶牛群死亡并污染了其土壤。\n\n然而，由于联邦政府无法预见此次泄漏，因此也就无法知晓该反应堆构成了不合理的伤害风险。\n\n因此，法院应判决政府胜诉，理由是无法证明存在过失。\n\n（选项 A）对于因“异常危险活动”造成的损害，适用严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担责任）原则。\n\n运营核反应堆可能属于异常危险活动。\n\n但根据《联邦侵权索赔法》（Federal Tort Claims Act），联邦政府仅对过失行为承担责任。\n\n（选项 B）在本案中，“事实本身说明过失”（res ipsa loquitur）原则不足以推断联邦政府存在过失。\n\n这是因为核反应堆属于复杂机械设备，其发生故障的原因可能并非联邦政府的过失所致——例如，可能是由制造其零部件的工程公司的过失造成的。\n\n（选项 D）导致原告损害的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）可能涉及多个被告。\n\n因此，即使工程公司是导致农场主损害的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause），如果该损害同时也是联邦政府行为的可预见后果，联邦政府仍须承担责任。\n\n教育目标：当土地占有者知道或应当知道其土地上的某项活动给他人造成了不合理的实质性损害风险（即该风险是可预见的）时，即负有合理注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第371条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明土地占有人须对其土地上构成对他人不合理伤害风险的活动所造成的损害承担责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：政府，理由是《联邦侵权索赔法》下的案件尚未得到证实。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":111,"sourceQuestionPage":333,"sourceQuestionPages":[333],"sourceExplanationPages":[334,335],"sourceAnswerPages":[334,335],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-112","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"In a civil action, a plaintiff sued a decedent's estate to recover damages for injuries she suffered in a collision between her car and one driven by the decedent. At trial, the plaintiff introduced undisputed evidence that the decedent's car had swerved across the centerline of the highway into oncoming traffic, where it had collided with the plaintiff's car. The decedent's estate introduced undisputed evidence that, before he swerved across the centerline, the decedent had suffered a fatal heart attack which he had no reason to foresee and that, just prior to the heart attack, the decedent had been driving at a reasonable speed and in a reasonable manner. A statute makes it a traffic offense to cross the centerline of a highway. In this case, which party is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The decedent's estate, because its rebuttal evidence is undisputed.","B":"The decedent's estate, because the plaintiff has not established a prima facie case of liability.","C":"The plaintiff, because the accident was of a type that does not ordinarily happen in the absence of negligence on the actor's part.","D":"The plaintiff, because the decedent crossed the centerline in violation of the statute."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Defenses to negligence per se\n\nViolation of law excused by:\n\nuse of reasonable care incapacity need to avoid greater risk of harm impossibility of complying vagueness of law reasonable ignorance (rare) To recover damages for accidental injuries, a plaintiff must establish a prima facie negligence case by proving the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. Under the doctrine of negligence per se, the elements of duty and breach are presumed if: the defendant violated a statute the statute was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff and the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect. However, the defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused if the defendant presents rebuttal evidence that he/she was using reasonable care or was incapacitated while attempting to comply with the statute or ordinance. If the plaintiff fails to dispute this excuse with counterevidence, then the defendant will prevail. Here, the plaintiff showed that she was injured when the decedent's car swerved across the centerline of the highway in violation of a statute designed to protect drivers like her from head-on collisions. Therefore, the plaintiff established a prima facie negligence case (Choice B). But the decedent's estate presented rebuttal evidence that the decedent was driving with reasonable care when he was incapacitated by an unforeseeable heart attack. Since that evidence is undisputed, the decedent's estate will likely prevail (Choice D). (Choice C) When an accident was of a type that does not ordinarily happen in the absence of negligence on the actor's part, the actor's negligence can be presumed under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur. But this presumption can be rebutted by evidence that the defendant was using reasonable care (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused, and negligence per se will not apply, if the defendant presents evidence that he/she was using reasonable care or was incapacitated while attempting to comply with the statute or ordinance.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (discussing excused violations of a statute).","educationalObjective":"A defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused, and negligence per se will not apply, if the defendant presents evidence that he/she was using reasonable care or was incapacitated while attempting to comply with the statute or ordinance.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (discussing excused violations of a statute).","rule":"A defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused, and negligence per se will not apply, if the defendant presents evidence that he/she was using reasonable care or was incapacitated while attempting to comply with the statute or ordinance.","irac":{"issue":"In this case, which party is likely to prevail?","rule":"A defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused, and negligence per se will not apply, if the defendant presents evidence that he/she was using reasonable care or was incapacitated while attempting to comply with the statute or ordinance.","analysis":"Defenses to negligence per se\n\nViolation of law excused by:\n\nuse of reasonable care incapacity need to avoid greater risk of harm impossibility of complying vagueness of law reasonable ignorance (rare) To recover damages for accidental injuries, a plaintiff must establish a prima facie negligence case by proving the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. Under the doctrine of negligence per se, the elements of duty and breach are presumed if: the defendant violated a statute the statute was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff and the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect. However, the defendant's violation of an applicable statute or ordinance will be excused if the defendant presents rebuttal evidence that he/she was using reasonable care or was incapacitated while attempting to comply with the statute or ordinance. If the plaintiff fails to dispute this excuse with counterevidence, then the defendant will prevail. Here, the plaintiff showed that she was injured when the decedent's car swerved across the centerline of the highway in violation of a statute designed to protect drivers like her from head-on collisions. Therefore, the plaintiff established a prima facie negligence case (Choice B). But the decedent's estate presented rebuttal evidence that the decedent was driving with reasonable care when he was incapacitated by an unforeseeable heart attack. Since that evidence is undisputed, the decedent's estate will likely prevail (Choice D). (Choice C) When an accident was of a type that does not ordinarily happen in the absence of negligence on the actor's part, the actor's negligence can be presumed under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur. But this presumption can be rebutted by evidence that the defendant was using reasonable care (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: The decedent's estate, because its rebuttal evidence is undisputed."},"questionZh":"在一项民事诉讼中，原告起诉死者遗产，就其与死者驾驶的车辆发生碰撞所受的伤害寻求损害赔偿。\n\n在庭审中，原告提交了无可争议的证据，证明死者的车辆偏离车道、越过公路中心线驶入对向车道，并与原告的车辆相撞。\n\n死者遗产方提交了无可争议的证据，证明死者在越过中心线之前突发致命心脏病（该病发作无法预见），且在病发前，死者是以合理的速度和方式驾驶车辆的。\n\n某项法规规定，越过公路中心线属于交通违法行为。\n\n在本案中，哪一方更有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"死者遗产方，因为其反驳证据是无可争议的。","B":"死者遗产方，因为原告未能确立关于责任的初步案情（prima facie case）。","C":"原告，因为该事故属于那种若非行为人存在过失通常不会发生的类型。","D":"原告，因为死者越过中心线违反了相关法规。"},"explanationZh":"“本身构成过失”（negligence per se）的抗辩理由 违反法律的行为可因以下情形获豁免： 尽到了合理注意义务；丧失行为能力；为避免更大的伤害风险；无法遵守法律；法律规定含糊不清；合理的不知情（罕见）。若要就意外伤害获得损害赔偿，原告必须确立过失侵权的初步案情（prima facie case），即证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系及损害后果。\n\n根据“本身构成过失”原则，若出现以下情况，则推定存在“注意义务”和“违反义务”这两个要素：被告违反了某项法规；该法规旨在防止原告所遭受的那类伤害；且原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群。\n\n然而，如果被告能提出反驳证据，证明其在试图遵守该法规或条例时已尽到合理注意义务，或因丧失行为能力而无法遵守，则其违规行为可获豁免。\n\n若原告未能提出反证来驳斥这一豁免理由，被告将胜诉。\n\n在本案中，原告证明了其受伤是因死者的车辆越过公路中心线所致，而该中心线法规旨在保护像她这样的驾驶员免受迎面相撞事故的伤害。\n\n因此，原告确立了过失侵权的初步案情（选项 B）。\n\n但死者遗产方提出了反驳证据，证明死者在驾驶时已尽到合理注意义务，只是因突发不可预见的心脏病发作而丧失了行为能力。\n\n鉴于该证据未受质疑，死者遗产方很可能胜诉（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 C）当事故属于那种若行为人无过失通常不会发生的类型时，根据“事实本身说明过失”原则（res ipsa loquitur），可推定行为人存在过失。\n\n但如果证据显示被告已尽到合理注意义务（如本案所示），则该推定可被推翻。\n\n教育目标：如果被告能提出证据证明其在试图遵守相关法规或条例时已尽到合理注意义务，或因丧失行为能力而无法遵守，则其违规行为将获豁免，且不适用“本身构成过失”原则。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 15 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（探讨法规违规行为的豁免情形）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果被告能提供证据证明其在试图遵守法规或条例时已尽到合理注意义务，或者因丧失行为能力而无法遵守，则被告违反适用法规或条例的行为可获免责，且不适用“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则。","iracZh":{"issue":"在本案中，哪一方更有可能胜诉？","rule":"如果被告能提供证据证明其在试图遵守法规或条例时已尽到合理注意义务，或者因丧失行为能力而无法遵守，则被告违反适用法规或条例的行为可获免责，且不适用“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则。","analysis":"“本身构成过失”（negligence per se）的抗辩理由 违反法律的行为可因以下情形获豁免： 尽到了合理注意义务；丧失行为能力；为避免更大的伤害风险；无法遵守法律；法律规定含糊不清；合理的不知情（罕见）。若要就意外伤害获得损害赔偿，原告必须确立过失侵权的初步案情（prima facie case），即证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系及损害后果。\n\n根据“本身构成过失”原则，若出现以下情况，则推定存在“注意义务”和“违反义务”这两个要素：被告违反了某项法规；该法规旨在防止原告所遭受的那类伤害；且原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群。\n\n然而，如果被告能提出反驳证据，证明其在试图遵守该法规或条例时已尽到合理注意义务，或因丧失行为能力而无法遵守，则其违规行为可获豁免。\n\n若原告未能提出反证来驳斥这一豁免理由，被告将胜诉。\n\n在本案中，原告证明了其受伤是因死者的车辆越过公路中心线所致，而该中心线法规旨在保护像她这样的驾驶员免受迎面相撞事故的伤害。\n\n因此，原告确立了过失侵权的初步案情（选项 B）。\n\n但死者遗产方提出了反驳证据，证明死者在驾驶时已尽到合理注意义务，只是因突发不可预见的心脏病发作而丧失了行为能力。\n\n鉴于该证据未受质疑，死者遗产方很可能胜诉（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 C）当事故属于那种若行为人无过失通常不会发生的类型时，根据“事实本身说明过失”原则（res ipsa loquitur），可推定行为人存在过失。\n\n但如果证据显示被告已尽到合理注意义务（如本案所示），则该推定可被推翻。\n\n教育目标：如果被告能提出证据证明其在试图遵守相关法规或条例时已尽到合理注意义务，或因丧失行为能力而无法遵守，则其违规行为将获豁免，且不适用“本身构成过失”原则。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 15 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（探讨法规违规行为的豁免情形）。","conclusion":"答案 A：死者遗产方，因为其反驳证据是无可争议的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":112,"sourceQuestionPage":336,"sourceQuestionPages":[336],"sourceExplanationPages":[337,338],"sourceAnswerPages":[337,338],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-113","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"An airport-shuttle company employs drivers to operate vans that carry multiple passengers to and from the airport along a regular route. The company exercises reasonable care in hiring and supervising all of its drivers. One day, one of the drivers was distracted by a conversation he was having with a passenger and failed to make a correct turn. Rather than immediately turning around, the driver decided to continue to deviate from the regular route and take an alternate route. While the van was on this alternate route, a sinkhole unexpectedly opened under the van and swallowed it. The van's passengers were injured. If the passengers file a negligence action against the airport-shuttle company, will they be likely to succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the company exercised reasonable care in selecting and supervising the driver.","B":"No, because there was no causal link between the driver's negligence and the passengers' injuries.","C":"Yes, because an airport-shuttle company is a common carrier and therefore subject to a heightened duty of care.","D":"Yes, because but for the driver's negligence, the van would not have been at the place where the sinkhole appeared."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Tort causation\n\nActual (factual) Single cause – but for defendant's conduct, plaintiff would not have cause been harmed Multiple causes: Second Restatement (majority rule) – defendant's conduct was substantial factor causing plaintiff's harm Third Restatement (minority rule) – defendant's conduct regarded as factual cause of plaintiff's harm Proximate (legal) Plaintiff's harm was reasonably foreseeable consequence of cause defendant's conduct\n\nTo succeed in a negligence claim, the plaintiff must prove that there was a causal link (ie, connection) between the defendant's tortious conduct and the plaintiff's harm. This requires proof that the defendant's conduct was both the actual and proximate cause of that harm: Actual cause – but for the defendant's conduct, the plaintiff would not have been harmed Proximate cause – the plaintiff's harm was a reasonably foreseeable consequence of the defendant's conduct, meaning that the conduct was of a type that generally increases the risk of that harm Here, the van would not have been where the sinkhole appeared but for the driver's missing a turn and taking an alternate route (actual cause). But since the driver's conduct was not of a type that generally increases the risk of being swallowed by a sinkhole, the passengers' injuries were not a foreseeable consequence of that conduct (no proximate cause).* Therefore, the passengers are unlikely to succeed in their negligence action because there was no causal link between the driver's conduct and the passengers' injuries (Choice D). *Even if the driver's conduct were treated as the proximate cause of the passengers' injuries, the sinkhole, as an extraordinary act of nature, constitutes an unforeseeable intervening cause that would break the chain of causation. (Choice A) A company that used reasonable care in its hiring and supervisory activities is not liable in negligence for its own conduct (eg, for negligent hiring). But this would not preclude the company from being vicariously liable for its employee's negligent conduct while acting within the scope of employment. (Choice C) At common law and in most jurisdictions, a common carrier (eg, plane, train, bus) has a heightened duty of care to its passengers. This means that a common carrier can be held liable for even \"slight negligence.\" But here, even if the shuttle company is treated\n\nas a common carrier, the driver's conduct was not the proximate cause of the passengers' injuries (no liability).\n\nEducational objective: To succeed in a negligence claim, the plaintiff must establish a causal link between the defendant's tortious conduct and the plaintiff's harm. Stated differently, the defendant's conduct must have been the actual and proximate cause of that harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm §§ 29–30 (proximate cause).","educationalObjective":"To succeed in a negligence claim, the plaintiff must establish a causal link between the defendant's tortious conduct and the plaintiff's harm. Stated differently, the defendant's conduct must have been the actual and proximate cause of that harm.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm §§ 29–30 (proximate cause).","rule":"To succeed in a negligence claim, the plaintiff must establish a causal link between the defendant's tortious conduct and the plaintiff's harm. Stated differently, the defendant's conduct must have been the actual and proximate cause of that harm.","irac":{"issue":"If the passengers file a negligence action against the airport-shuttle company, will they be likely to succeed?","rule":"To succeed in a negligence claim, the plaintiff must establish a causal link between the defendant's tortious conduct and the plaintiff's harm. Stated differently, the defendant's conduct must have been the actual and proximate cause of that harm.","analysis":"Tort causation\n\nActual (factual) Single cause – but for defendant's conduct, plaintiff would not have cause been harmed Multiple causes: Second Restatement (majority rule) – defendant's conduct was substantial factor causing plaintiff's harm Third Restatement (minority rule) – defendant's conduct regarded as factual cause of plaintiff's harm Proximate (legal) Plaintiff's harm was reasonably foreseeable consequence of cause defendant's conduct\n\nTo succeed in a negligence claim, the plaintiff must prove that there was a causal link (ie, connection) between the defendant's tortious conduct and the plaintiff's harm. This requires proof that the defendant's conduct was both the actual and proximate cause of that harm: Actual cause – but for the defendant's conduct, the plaintiff would not have been harmed Proximate cause – the plaintiff's harm was a reasonably foreseeable consequence of the defendant's conduct, meaning that the conduct was of a type that generally increases the risk of that harm Here, the van would not have been where the sinkhole appeared but for the driver's missing a turn and taking an alternate route (actual cause). But since the driver's conduct was not of a type that generally increases the risk of being swallowed by a sinkhole, the passengers' injuries were not a foreseeable consequence of that conduct (no proximate cause).* Therefore, the passengers are unlikely to succeed in their negligence action because there was no causal link between the driver's conduct and the passengers' injuries (Choice D). *Even if the driver's conduct were treated as the proximate cause of the passengers' injuries, the sinkhole, as an extraordinary act of nature, constitutes an unforeseeable intervening cause that would break the chain of causation. (Choice A) A company that used reasonable care in its hiring and supervisory activities is not liable in negligence for its own conduct (eg, for negligent hiring). But this would not preclude the company from being vicariously liable for its employee's negligent conduct while acting within the scope of employment. (Choice C) At common law and in most jurisdictions, a common carrier (eg, plane, train, bus) has a heightened duty of care to its passengers. This means that a common carrier can be held liable for even \"slight negligence.\" But here, even if the shuttle company is treated\n\nas a common carrier, the driver's conduct was not the proximate cause of the passengers' injuries (no liability).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because there was no causal link between the driver's negligence and the passengers' injuries."},"questionZh":"一家机场接送公司雇佣司机驾驶面包车，沿固定路线接送多名乘客往返机场。\n\n该公司在招聘和监督所有司机方面都尽到了合理的注意义务。\n\n一天，其中一名司机因与乘客交谈而分神，未能正确转弯。\n\n该司机没有立即掉头，而是决定继续偏离常规路线，选择另一条路线。\n\n面包车行驶在这条替代路线上时，车底突然出现一个塌陷坑，面包车被吞没。\n\n车上的乘客受伤。\n\n如果乘客对机场接送公司提起过失诉讼，他们胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为该公司在选择和监督司机方面尽到了合理的注意义务。","B":"不可能，因为司机的过失与乘客的受伤之间没有因果关系。","C":"是的，因为机场班车公司属于公共承运人，因此负有更高的注意义务。","D":"是的，因为如果不是司机疏忽，这辆班车就不会出现在塌陷坑出现的地方。"},"explanationZh":"侵权行为中的因果关系 实际（事实）因果关系：单一原因——若无被告的行为，原告便不会受到伤害；多重原因：第二次《侵权法重述》（多数规则）——被告的行为是导致原告受害的实质性因素；第三次《侵权法重述》（少数规则）——被告的行为被视为原告受害的事实原因。法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）：原告受到的伤害是被告行为可合理预见的后果。 若要在过失索赔诉讼中胜诉，原告必须证明被告的侵权行为与原告受到的伤害之间存在因果联系（即关联性）。\n\n这要求证明被告的行为既是该伤害的实际原因，也是法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）：实际原因——若无被告的行为，原告便不会受到伤害；法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）——原告受到的伤害是被告行为可合理预见的后果，即该行为属于通常会增加此类伤害风险的行为。在本案中，若非司机错过了转弯并改走另一条路线，那辆货车就不会出现在天坑出现的位置（实际原因）。\n\n然而，由于司机的行为并不属于通常会增加被天坑吞没风险的行为，乘客受伤并非该行为可预见的后果（不存在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause））。* 因此，乘客不太可能在过失诉讼中胜诉，因为司机的行为与乘客受伤之间不存在因果联系（选项 D）。\n\n* 即使将司机的行为视为乘客受伤的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause），天坑作为一种异常的自然现象，也构成了一个不可预见的介入原因，从而切断了因果关系链。\n\n（选项 A）一家在招聘和监督活动中尽到合理注意义务的公司，不因其自身行为（例如过失招聘）而承担过失责任。\n\n但这并不妨碍公司对其雇员在受雇范围内实施的过失行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 C）根据普通法及大多数司法管辖区的法律，公共承运人（如飞机、火车、巴士）对其乘客负有更高的注意义务。\n\n这意味着，即使是“轻微过失”，公共承运人也可能需要承担责任。\n\n但在本案中，即使将该班车公司视为“公共承运人”，司机的行为也并非导致乘客受伤的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（即不承担责任）。\n\n教学目标：若要在过失索赔诉讼中胜诉，原告必须证明被告的侵权行为与原告所受损害之间存在因果关系。\n\n换言之，被告的行为必须是导致该损害的实际原因及法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第29–30条（关于法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要成功提起过失侵权诉讼，原告必须证明被告的侵权行为与原告的损害之间存在因果关系。\n\n换句话说，被告的行为必须是造成该损害的实际且直接的原因。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果乘客对机场班车公司提起过失侵权诉讼，他们胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"要成功提起过失侵权诉讼，原告必须证明被告的侵权行为与原告的损害之间存在因果关系。\n\n换句话说，被告的行为必须是造成该损害的实际且直接的原因。","analysis":"侵权行为中的因果关系 实际（事实）因果关系：单一原因——若无被告的行为，原告便不会受到伤害；多重原因：第二次《侵权法重述》（多数规则）——被告的行为是导致原告受害的实质性因素；第三次《侵权法重述》（少数规则）——被告的行为被视为原告受害的事实原因。法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）：原告受到的伤害是被告行为可合理预见的后果。 若要在过失索赔诉讼中胜诉，原告必须证明被告的侵权行为与原告受到的伤害之间存在因果联系（即关联性）。\n\n这要求证明被告的行为既是该伤害的实际原因，也是法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）：实际原因——若无被告的行为，原告便不会受到伤害；法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）——原告受到的伤害是被告行为可合理预见的后果，即该行为属于通常会增加此类伤害风险的行为。在本案中，若非司机错过了转弯并改走另一条路线，那辆货车就不会出现在天坑出现的位置（实际原因）。\n\n然而，由于司机的行为并不属于通常会增加被天坑吞没风险的行为，乘客受伤并非该行为可预见的后果（不存在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause））。* 因此，乘客不太可能在过失诉讼中胜诉，因为司机的行为与乘客受伤之间不存在因果联系（选项 D）。\n\n* 即使将司机的行为视为乘客受伤的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause），天坑作为一种异常的自然现象，也构成了一个不可预见的介入原因，从而切断了因果关系链。\n\n（选项 A）一家在招聘和监督活动中尽到合理注意义务的公司，不因其自身行为（例如过失招聘）而承担过失责任。\n\n但这并不妨碍公司对其雇员在受雇范围内实施的过失行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 C）根据普通法及大多数司法管辖区的法律，公共承运人（如飞机、火车、巴士）对其乘客负有更高的注意义务。\n\n这意味着，即使是“轻微过失”，公共承运人也可能需要承担责任。\n\n但在本案中，即使将该班车公司视为“公共承运人”，司机的行为也并非导致乘客受伤的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（即不承担责任）。\n\n教学目标：若要在过失索赔诉讼中胜诉，原告必须证明被告的侵权行为与原告所受损害之间存在因果关系。\n\n换言之，被告的行为必须是导致该损害的实际原因及法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第29–30条（关于法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可能，因为司机的过失与乘客的受伤之间没有因果关系。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"substantial factor","ipa":"/sʌbstˈænʃəl fˈæktɝ/","syllables":"sub·stan·tial fac·tor","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"实质因素：在多重充分原因中对损害具有实质作用的原因","contextualMeaning":"实质因素：在多重充分原因中对损害具有实质作用的原因","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Tort causation Actual (factual) Single cause – but for defendant's conduct, plaintiff would not have cause been harmed Multiple causes: Second Restatement (majority rule) – defendant's conduct was substantial factor causing plaintiff's harm Third Restatement (minority rule) – defendant's conduct regarded as factual cause of plaintiff's harm Proximate (legal) Plaintiff's harm was reasonably foreseeable consequence of ","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":113,"sourceQuestionPage":339,"sourceQuestionPages":[339],"sourceExplanationPages":[340,341],"sourceAnswerPages":[340,341],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-114","subject":"Torts","topic":"Damages & Joint Liability","question":"A man decided to have lunch at a new restaurant. While the man was eating his lunch, an accidental fire broke out in the kitchen, and the dining room quickly filled with smoke. The other restaurant patrons experienced mild discomfort, which did not require medical treatment. However, the man had been born with an acute and discerning sense of smell, and he had a job as a senior perfumer at a fragrance corporation. He subsequently sued the restaurant in negligence for the injury to the olfactory sensory cells in his nasal cavity that left him unable to work. His annual salary had been $250,000 before the accident. Assuming that the man can otherwise establish the elements of negligence, can he recover damages for the injury to his olfactory sensory cells as well as for his loss of employment?","choices":{"A":"No, because economic damages due to loss of employment are not recoverable in a negligence action.","B":"No, because the man's acute and discerning sense of smell was a preexisting condition.","C":"Yes, because the man can recover even if the extent of his injuries was not reasonably foreseeable.","D":"Yes, because the man's damages were severe and exceeded those of the other restaurant patrons."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A successful negligence claim requires proof that the plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, personal injury or property damage) as a result of the defendant's negligent conduct. Assuming all other elements are proved, the plaintiff can then recover compensatory damages for his/her initial physical harm AND any subsequent harm (physical, economic, or emotional) that is traceable to that initial harm. This is true even if the extent of the harm was unusual or unforeseeable because \"the defendant takes the victim as found.\"* Here, the man suffered injuries to his olfactory sensory cells (initial physical harm) as a result of the fire. This left the man unable to perform his job as a senior perfumer (economic harm traceable to initial harm). Therefore, the man can recover compensatory damages for the injury to his olfactory sensory cells as well as for his loss of employment— even if the extent of his injuries was not reasonably foreseeable. *This is known colloquially as the \"thin-skull\" or \"eggshell plaintiff\" rule. (Choice A) A plaintiff who suffers economic damages (eg, due to loss of employment) without any related personal injury or property damage cannot recover in a negligence action. But economic damages stemming from physical harm (as seen here) are recoverable. (Choice B) The physical harm required for negligence includes the exacerbation of a preexisting condition, so the fact that the man's acute and discerning sense of smell was a preexisting condition does not defeat his claim. (Choice D) While a negligence plaintiff must prove actual damages, the damages need not be severe or exceed those suffered by others who did not have a preexisting condition.\n\nEducational objective: A successful negligence plaintiff can recover compensatory damages for his/her initial physical harm as well as any subsequent harm (physical, economic, or emotional) that stems from that initial harm. This is so even if the extent of the harm was unusual or unforeseeable.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 31 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (preexisting conditions and unforeseeable harm).","educationalObjective":"A successful negligence plaintiff can recover compensatory damages for his/her initial physical harm as well as any subsequent harm (physical, economic, or emotional) that stems from that initial harm. This is so even if the extent of the harm was unusual or unforeseeable.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 31 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (preexisting conditions and unforeseeable harm).","rule":"A successful negligence plaintiff can recover compensatory damages for his/her initial physical harm as well as any subsequent harm (physical, economic, or emotional) that stems from that initial harm. This is so even if the extent of the harm was unusual or unforeseeable.","irac":{"issue":"Assuming that the man can otherwise establish the elements of negligence, can he recover damages for the injury to his olfactory sensory cells as well as for his loss of employment?","rule":"A successful negligence plaintiff can recover compensatory damages for his/her initial physical harm as well as any subsequent harm (physical, economic, or emotional) that stems from that initial harm. This is so even if the extent of the harm was unusual or unforeseeable.","analysis":"A successful negligence claim requires proof that the plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, personal injury or property damage) as a result of the defendant's negligent conduct. Assuming all other elements are proved, the plaintiff can then recover compensatory damages for his/her initial physical harm AND any subsequent harm (physical, economic, or emotional) that is traceable to that initial harm. This is true even if the extent of the harm was unusual or unforeseeable because \"the defendant takes the victim as found.\"* Here, the man suffered injuries to his olfactory sensory cells (initial physical harm) as a result of the fire. This left the man unable to perform his job as a senior perfumer (economic harm traceable to initial harm). Therefore, the man can recover compensatory damages for the injury to his olfactory sensory cells as well as for his loss of employment— even if the extent of his injuries was not reasonably foreseeable. *This is known colloquially as the \"thin-skull\" or \"eggshell plaintiff\" rule. (Choice A) A plaintiff who suffers economic damages (eg, due to loss of employment) without any related personal injury or property damage cannot recover in a negligence action. But economic damages stemming from physical harm (as seen here) are recoverable. (Choice B) The physical harm required for negligence includes the exacerbation of a preexisting condition, so the fact that the man's acute and discerning sense of smell was a preexisting condition does not defeat his claim. (Choice D) While a negligence plaintiff must prove actual damages, the damages need not be severe or exceed those suffered by others who did not have a preexisting condition.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the man can recover even if the extent of his injuries was not reasonably foreseeable."},"questionZh":"一名男子决定去一家新餐厅吃午饭。\n\n用餐期间，厨房突发火灾，餐厅很快被浓烟笼罩。\n\n其他顾客仅感到轻微不适，无需就医。\n\n然而，这名男子天生嗅觉灵敏，在一家香水公司担任高级调香师。\n\n事后，他以餐厅疏忽大意为由起诉该餐厅，称其鼻腔嗅觉细胞受损，导致他无法工作。\n\n事故发生前，他的年薪为25万美元。\n\n假设该男子能够证明餐厅存在过失，他能否就嗅觉细胞受损以及失业获得赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为在过失诉讼中，因失业造成的经济损失是无法获得赔偿的。","B":"不，因为该男子敏锐的嗅觉是其既有的。","C":"是，因为即使该男子所受伤害的程度无法合理预见，他仍然可以获得赔偿。","D":"是，因为该男子的损失严重，超过了其他餐厅顾客的损失。"},"explanationZh":"要成功提出过失索赔，必须证明原告因被告的过失行为遭受了身体伤害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。\n\n在证明了所有其他必要要素的前提下，原告不仅可以就其最初遭受的身体伤害获得补偿性损害赔偿，还可以就因该最初伤害而引发的任何后续伤害（无论是身体、经济还是情感方面的伤害）获得赔偿。\n\n即使伤害程度异常或不可预见，这一原则依然适用，因为法律遵循“被告必须接受受害者原本的身体状况”（即“被告须按受害者的现状承担责任”）这一准则*。在本案中，该男子因火灾导致嗅觉感觉细胞受损（即最初的身体伤害）。\n\n这导致他无法继续从事高级调香师的工作（即由最初伤害引发的经济损失）。\n\n因此，该男子既可以就其嗅觉感觉细胞受损获得补偿性损害赔偿，也可以就其失去工作获得赔偿——即使其受伤程度在合理预见范围之外。\n\n*这在通俗说法中被称为“薄颅骨”规则或“蛋壳脑袋原告”规则（即“脆弱受害者”规则）。\n\n(选项 A) 如果原告仅遭受经济损失（例如因失业）而未伴随相关的人身伤害或财产损失，则无法在过失诉讼中获得赔偿。\n\n但源于身体伤害的经济损失（如本案所示）是可以获得赔偿的。\n\n(选项 B) 过失诉讼中要求的身体伤害包括既有病症（或身体状况）的恶化，因此，该男子敏锐的嗅觉属于其既有身体状况这一事实，并不会导致其索赔失败。\n\n(选项 D) 虽然过失诉讼的原告必须证明存在实际损害，但该损害无需达到严重程度，也不必超过未患有既有病症的人所遭受的损害。\n\n教育目标：过失诉讼的胜诉原告不仅可以就其最初遭受的身体伤害获得补偿性损害赔偿，还可以就因该最初伤害而引发的任何后续伤害（身体、经济或情感方面的伤害）获得赔偿。\n\n即使伤害程度异常或不可预见，这一原则依然适用。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与情感伤害责任》第 31 条 (美国法律协会，2010 年)（关于既有身体状况与不可预见伤害的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"胜诉的过失侵权原告可以就其最初的身体伤害以及由此产生的任何后续伤害（包括身体、经济或精神方面的伤害）获得补偿性赔偿。\n\n即使伤害的程度异常或无法预见，也是如此。","iracZh":{"issue":"假设该男子能够证明过失侵权的其他构成要件，他是否可以就其嗅觉细胞的损伤以及失业获得赔偿？","rule":"胜诉的过失侵权原告可以就其最初的身体伤害以及由此产生的任何后续伤害（包括身体、经济或精神方面的伤害）获得补偿性赔偿。\n\n即使伤害的程度异常或无法预见，也是如此。","analysis":"要成功提出过失索赔，必须证明原告因被告的过失行为遭受了身体伤害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。\n\n在证明了所有其他必要要素的前提下，原告不仅可以就其最初遭受的身体伤害获得补偿性损害赔偿，还可以就因该最初伤害而引发的任何后续伤害（无论是身体、经济还是情感方面的伤害）获得赔偿。\n\n即使伤害程度异常或不可预见，这一原则依然适用，因为法律遵循“被告必须接受受害者原本的身体状况”（即“被告须按受害者的现状承担责任”）这一准则*。在本案中，该男子因火灾导致嗅觉感觉细胞受损（即最初的身体伤害）。\n\n这导致他无法继续从事高级调香师的工作（即由最初伤害引发的经济损失）。\n\n因此，该男子既可以就其嗅觉感觉细胞受损获得补偿性损害赔偿，也可以就其失去工作获得赔偿——即使其受伤程度在合理预见范围之外。\n\n*这在通俗说法中被称为“薄颅骨”规则或“蛋壳脑袋原告”规则（即“脆弱受害者”规则）。\n\n(选项 A) 如果原告仅遭受经济损失（例如因失业）而未伴随相关的人身伤害或财产损失，则无法在过失诉讼中获得赔偿。\n\n但源于身体伤害的经济损失（如本案所示）是可以获得赔偿的。\n\n(选项 B) 过失诉讼中要求的身体伤害包括既有病症（或身体状况）的恶化，因此，该男子敏锐的嗅觉属于其既有身体状况这一事实，并不会导致其索赔失败。\n\n(选项 D) 虽然过失诉讼的原告必须证明存在实际损害，但该损害无需达到严重程度，也不必超过未患有既有病症的人所遭受的损害。\n\n教育目标：过失诉讼的胜诉原告不仅可以就其最初遭受的身体伤害获得补偿性损害赔偿，还可以就因该最初伤害而引发的任何后续伤害（身体、经济或情感方面的伤害）获得赔偿。\n\n即使伤害程度异常或不可预见，这一原则依然适用。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与情感伤害责任》第 31 条 (美国法律协会，2010 年)（关于既有身体状况与不可预见伤害的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是，因为即使该男子所受伤害的程度无法合理预见，他仍然可以获得赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-282bbf14df3a1f77"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":114,"sourceQuestionPage":342,"sourceQuestionPages":[342],"sourceExplanationPages":[343,344],"sourceAnswerPages":[343,344],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-115","subject":"Torts","topic":"Actual & Proximate Cause","question":"When a tire of a motorist's car suffered a blowout, the car rolled over and the motorist was badly injured. Vehicles made by the manufacturer of the motorist's car have been found to be negligently designed, making them dangerously prone to rolling over when they suffer blowouts. A truck driver who was driving behind the motorist when the accident occurred stopped to help. Rescue vehicles promptly arrived, and the truck driver walked along the side of the road to return to his truck. As he approached his truck, he was struck and injured by a speeding car. The truck driver has sued the manufacturer of the injured motorist's car. Is the truck driver likely to prevail in a suit against the car manufacturer?","choices":{"A":"No, because the car manufacturer's negligence was not the proximate cause of the truck driver's injuries.","B":"No, because the truck driver assumed the risk of injury when he undertook to help the motorist.","C":"Yes, because it is foreseeable that injuries can result from rollovers.","D":"Yes, because the car manufacturer's negligence caused the dangerous situation that invited the rescue by the truck driver."},"answer":"D","explanation":"In a negligence suit, the plaintiff must prove actual and proximate cause. Proximate cause is satisfied when the plaintiff's harm was a foreseeable consequence of the defendant's negligence. And when a defendant negligently endangers him/herself or others, the law deems it foreseeable that someone will be injured in a rescue attempt. As a result, a plaintiff can recover for negligence under the rescue doctrine if: the plaintiff was injured while attempting to rescue another and that person's peril was caused by the defendant's negligence. Here, the motorist was badly injured when his car suffered a blowout and rolled over. The car manufacturer's negligent design made the car dangerously prone to this type of accident—thereby causing the motorist's peril and inviting rescue by another. The truck driver, who had stopped to help the motorist, was then struck and injured by a speeding car. Since injury to rescuers is sufficiently foreseeable to satisfy proximate cause, the truck driver will likely prevail in a suit against the manufacturer (Choice A). (Choice B) A rescuer does not assume the risk of injury by undertaking a rescue attempt unless he/she (1) knew the rescue attempt was dangerous and (2) could have proceeded in a reasonable alternative manner. Here, there is no indication that the truck driver had a reasonable alternative. And even if he did, this would merely reduce (not bar) his recovery in most jurisdictions. (Choice C) Although it is foreseeable that injuries can result from rollovers, the truck driver cannot prevail unless he also shows that the defendant's negligence caused the motorist's peril.\n\nEducational objective: Injuries to rescuers are sufficiently foreseeable to satisfy proximate cause. As a result, a rescuer can recover for negligence if (1) he/she was injured while attempting to rescue another and (2) that person's peril was caused by the defendant's negligence.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 294 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a defendant's conduct may be negligent to a potential rescuer even though that conduct poses no direct risk to the rescuer).","educationalObjective":"Injuries to rescuers are sufficiently foreseeable to satisfy proximate cause. As a result, a rescuer can recover for negligence if (1) he/she was injured while attempting to rescue another and (2) that person's peril was caused by the defendant's negligence.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 294 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a defendant's conduct may be negligent to a potential rescuer even though that conduct poses no direct risk to the rescuer).","rule":"Injuries to rescuers are sufficiently foreseeable to satisfy proximate cause. As a result, a rescuer can recover for negligence if (1) he/she was injured while attempting to rescue another and (2) that person's peril was caused by the defendant's negligence.","irac":{"issue":"Is the truck driver likely to prevail in a suit against the car manufacturer?","rule":"Injuries to rescuers are sufficiently foreseeable to satisfy proximate cause. As a result, a rescuer can recover for negligence if (1) he/she was injured while attempting to rescue another and (2) that person's peril was caused by the defendant's negligence.","analysis":"In a negligence suit, the plaintiff must prove actual and proximate cause. Proximate cause is satisfied when the plaintiff's harm was a foreseeable consequence of the defendant's negligence. And when a defendant negligently endangers him/herself or others, the law deems it foreseeable that someone will be injured in a rescue attempt. As a result, a plaintiff can recover for negligence under the rescue doctrine if: the plaintiff was injured while attempting to rescue another and that person's peril was caused by the defendant's negligence. Here, the motorist was badly injured when his car suffered a blowout and rolled over. The car manufacturer's negligent design made the car dangerously prone to this type of accident—thereby causing the motorist's peril and inviting rescue by another. The truck driver, who had stopped to help the motorist, was then struck and injured by a speeding car. Since injury to rescuers is sufficiently foreseeable to satisfy proximate cause, the truck driver will likely prevail in a suit against the manufacturer (Choice A). (Choice B) A rescuer does not assume the risk of injury by undertaking a rescue attempt unless he/she (1) knew the rescue attempt was dangerous and (2) could have proceeded in a reasonable alternative manner. Here, there is no indication that the truck driver had a reasonable alternative. And even if he did, this would merely reduce (not bar) his recovery in most jurisdictions. (Choice C) Although it is foreseeable that injuries can result from rollovers, the truck driver cannot prevail unless he also shows that the defendant's negligence caused the motorist's peril.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the car manufacturer's negligence caused the dangerous situation that invited the rescue by the truck driver."},"questionZh":"一名驾驶员的汽车发生爆胎，导致车辆侧翻，驾驶员身受重伤。\n\n经查明，该汽车制造商生产的车辆存在设计疏忽，导致车辆在发生爆胎时极易发生危险的侧翻事故。\n\n事故发生时，一名卡车司机正行驶在该车后方，他停下车提供协助。\n\n救援车辆迅速赶到，该卡车司机沿路边步行返回自己的卡车。\n\n在靠近卡车时，他被一辆超速行驶的汽车撞伤。\n\n该卡车司机起诉了受伤驾驶员所驾驶车辆的制造商。\n\n这名卡车司机起诉汽车制造商胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为汽车制造商的疏忽并非导致卡车司机受伤的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（proximate cause）。","B":"不大，因为卡车司机在决定协助该驾驶员时，已承担了受伤的风险。","C":"很大，因为车辆侧翻可能导致人员受伤是可预见的。","D":"很大，因为汽车制造商的疏忽引发了危险状况，从而促使了卡车司机的救援行为。"},"explanationZh":"在过失诉讼中，原告必须证明存在实际因果关系和法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（proximate cause）。\n\n当原告遭受的伤害是被告过失行为的可预见后果时，即满足法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）要件。\n\n当被告因过失使自己或他人陷入危险时，法律认定有人在试图救援时受伤是可预见的。\n\n因此，根据“救援原则”（rescue doctrine），如果原告在试图救援他人时受伤，且该他人的险境是由被告的过失造成的，原告便可就过失提出索赔。\n\n本案中，一名驾驶员因车辆爆胎翻车而受重伤。\n\n汽车制造商的设计过失使得该车型极易发生此类事故，从而导致了驾驶员的险境并引发了他人的救援行动。\n\n一名停下来提供帮助的卡车司机随后被一辆超速行驶的汽车撞伤。\n\n由于救援人员受伤属于足以满足法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）要件的可预见后果，该卡车司机很可能在针对制造商的诉讼中胜诉（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）救援人员在实施救援时并不承担受伤风险，除非他/她 (1) 明知救援行动具有危险性，且 (2) 本可以采取合理的替代方式进行救援。\n\n本案中，没有迹象表明该卡车司机有合理的替代方案。\n\n即使有，在大多数司法管辖区，这也只会减少（而非完全排除）其获赔金额。\n\n（选项 C）尽管翻车事故可能导致人员受伤是可预见的，但卡车司机若要胜诉，还必须证明被告的过失导致了该驾驶员的险境。\n\n教育目标：救援人员受伤属于足以满足法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）要件的可预见后果。\n\n因此，如果救援人员 (1) 在试图救援他人时受伤，且 (2) 该他人的险境由被告的过失造成，则该救援人员可就过失提出索赔。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 294 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明被告的行为即使未对潜在救援人员构成直接风险，也可能被视为对该救援人员存在过失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"救援人员受伤的情况被视为具有足够的可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability），从而满足法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）的要求。\n\n因此，如果救援人员 (1) 在试图营救他人时受伤，且 (2) 被救者的险境是由被告的疏忽造成的，则该救援人员可就疏忽行为获得赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"这名卡车司机起诉汽车制造商胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"救援人员受伤的情况被视为具有足够的可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability），从而满足法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）的要求。\n\n因此，如果救援人员 (1) 在试图营救他人时受伤，且 (2) 被救者的险境是由被告的疏忽造成的，则该救援人员可就疏忽行为获得赔偿。","analysis":"在过失诉讼中，原告必须证明存在实际因果关系和法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（proximate cause）。\n\n当原告遭受的伤害是被告过失行为的可预见后果时，即满足法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）要件。\n\n当被告因过失使自己或他人陷入危险时，法律认定有人在试图救援时受伤是可预见的。\n\n因此，根据“救援原则”（rescue doctrine），如果原告在试图救援他人时受伤，且该他人的险境是由被告的过失造成的，原告便可就过失提出索赔。\n\n本案中，一名驾驶员因车辆爆胎翻车而受重伤。\n\n汽车制造商的设计过失使得该车型极易发生此类事故，从而导致了驾驶员的险境并引发了他人的救援行动。\n\n一名停下来提供帮助的卡车司机随后被一辆超速行驶的汽车撞伤。\n\n由于救援人员受伤属于足以满足法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）要件的可预见后果，该卡车司机很可能在针对制造商的诉讼中胜诉（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）救援人员在实施救援时并不承担受伤风险，除非他/她 (1) 明知救援行动具有危险性，且 (2) 本可以采取合理的替代方式进行救援。\n\n本案中，没有迹象表明该卡车司机有合理的替代方案。\n\n即使有，在大多数司法管辖区，这也只会减少（而非完全排除）其获赔金额。\n\n（选项 C）尽管翻车事故可能导致人员受伤是可预见的，但卡车司机若要胜诉，还必须证明被告的过失导致了该驾驶员的险境。\n\n教育目标：救援人员受伤属于足以满足法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）要件的可预见后果。\n\n因此，如果救援人员 (1) 在试图救援他人时受伤，且 (2) 该他人的险境由被告的过失造成，则该救援人员可就过失提出索赔。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 294 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明被告的行为即使未对潜在救援人员构成直接风险，也可能被视为对该救援人员存在过失）。","conclusion":"答案 D：很大，因为汽车制造商的疏忽引发了危险状况，从而促使了卡车司机的救援行为。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-4757739a11129dec"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":115,"sourceQuestionPage":345,"sourceQuestionPages":[345],"sourceExplanationPages":[346,347],"sourceAnswerPages":[346,347],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-116","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Breach & Proof","question":"A longshoreman fell to his death through an open hatch on the deck of a ship. The longshoreman was an employee of a company that had contracted with the ship's owner to load and unload the ship. The fall occurred at night, when loading work was over for the day, and there was no reason for the longshoreman to have been near the hatch. A negligence action was filed against the ship's owner for the death of the longshoreman. In that action, the owner has moved for summary judgment and has provided unrebutted evidence that it is customary for the crews of ships to open the hatches for ventilation after the longshoremen have left the ships. How should the court respond to the motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion and submit the case to the jury with instructions that the custom is relevant but not conclusive on the issue of negligence.","B":"Deny the motion and submit the case to the jury with instructions that the ship's owner should win if the longshoreman was improperly near the hatch.","C":"Deny the motion, because the probability of serious injury caused by falling down an open hatch clearly outweighs the burden of keeping the hatch closed.","D":"Grant the motion, because the custom should be considered conclusive on the issue of negligence."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Elements of negligence\n\nDuty – defendant owed duty of reasonable care because his/her conduct created foreseeable risk of harm to others Breach – defendant breached duty by failing to use reasonable care Causation – defendant's breach actually & proximately caused plaintiff's harm Damages – plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, bodily injury or property damage) To determine whether a defendant was negligent, the trier of fact (eg, the jury) can consider all relevant factors—including whether the defendant complied with community or industry custom. But custom is not conclusive on the issue of negligence since, among other things, the entire community or industry may be negligent. Therefore, this court should deny the ship owner's summary judgment motion and instruct the jury that the custom of opening the hatches after the longshoremen have left the ship is relevant—but not conclusive (Choice D). (Choice B) The fact that the longshoreman was improperly near the hatch could show that he was contributorily negligent—ie, failed to use reasonable care for his own protection. This would completely bar his recovery if the traditional common law rule applied. But under the comparative negligence rule (default rule on the MBE), contributory negligence only reduces recovery. Therefore, the ship's owner cannot win on this basis. (Choice C) In determining negligence issues, the jury can consider whether the probability of harm outweighed the burden of avoiding that harm (ie, the Hand formula). But here, there is no indication that the probability of serious injury caused by falling down an open hatch outweighs the burden of keeping the hatch closed. This is particularly true since it is customary to only open the hatches after the longshoremen have left the ship.\n\nEducational objective: Evidence that the defendant complied with (or deviated from) community or industry custom is relevant—but not conclusive—on the issue of negligence.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 295A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that the customs of the community are relevant but not conclusive on the issue of negligence).","educationalObjective":"Evidence that the defendant complied with (or deviated from) community or industry custom is relevant—but not conclusive—on the issue of negligence.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 295A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that the customs of the community are relevant but not conclusive on the issue of negligence).","rule":"Evidence that the defendant complied with (or deviated from) community or industry custom is relevant—but not conclusive—on the issue of negligence.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court respond to the motion?","rule":"Evidence that the defendant complied with (or deviated from) community or industry custom is relevant—but not conclusive—on the issue of negligence.","analysis":"Elements of negligence\n\nDuty – defendant owed duty of reasonable care because his/her conduct created foreseeable risk of harm to others Breach – defendant breached duty by failing to use reasonable care Causation – defendant's breach actually & proximately caused plaintiff's harm Damages – plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, bodily injury or property damage) To determine whether a defendant was negligent, the trier of fact (eg, the jury) can consider all relevant factors—including whether the defendant complied with community or industry custom. But custom is not conclusive on the issue of negligence since, among other things, the entire community or industry may be negligent. Therefore, this court should deny the ship owner's summary judgment motion and instruct the jury that the custom of opening the hatches after the longshoremen have left the ship is relevant—but not conclusive (Choice D). (Choice B) The fact that the longshoreman was improperly near the hatch could show that he was contributorily negligent—ie, failed to use reasonable care for his own protection. This would completely bar his recovery if the traditional common law rule applied. But under the comparative negligence rule (default rule on the MBE), contributory negligence only reduces recovery. Therefore, the ship's owner cannot win on this basis. (Choice C) In determining negligence issues, the jury can consider whether the probability of harm outweighed the burden of avoiding that harm (ie, the Hand formula). But here, there is no indication that the probability of serious injury caused by falling down an open hatch outweighs the burden of keeping the hatch closed. This is particularly true since it is customary to only open the hatches after the longshoremen have left the ship.","conclusion":"Answer A: Deny the motion and submit the case to the jury with instructions that the custom is relevant but not conclusive on the issue of negligence."},"questionZh":"一名码头工人从船甲板上一个敞开的舱口坠落身亡。\n\n该码头工人受雇于一家与船东签订装卸合同的公司。\n\n坠落发生在夜间，当时装卸工作已经结束，码头工人没有理由靠近舱口。\n\n码头工人的死亡引发了针对船东的过失诉讼。\n\n在诉讼中，船东提出简易判决动议，并提供了未被反驳的证据，证明船员在码头工人离开船只后打开舱口通风是惯例。\n\n法院应如何回应该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回该动议，并将案件提交陪审团，指示陪审团该惯例与过失问题相关，但并非决定性因素。","B":"驳回该动议，并将案件提交陪审团，指示陪审团，如果码头工人当时不当靠近舱口，则船东应胜诉。","C":"驳回该动议，因为从敞开的舱口跌落造成严重伤害的可能性明显大于保持舱口关闭的必要性。","D":"准予该动议，因为该惯例应被视为过失认定的决定性因素。"},"explanationZh":"过失的构成要素 注意义务（Duty）——被告负有合理的注意义务，因为其行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；违反义务（Breach）——被告因未尽到合理注意义务而构成违约；因果关系（Causation）——被告的违约行为实际上且在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）上导致了原告的损害；损害（Damages）——原告遭受了实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。在判定被告是否存在过失时，事实认定者（如陪审团）可以考虑所有相关因素——包括被告是否遵守了社会惯例或行业惯例。\n\n但惯例对于过失的认定并非决定性因素，因为整个社会或行业本身也可能存在过失。\n\n因此，法院应驳回船主的即决判决动议，并指示陪审团：在码头工人离开船舶后才开启舱口的惯例虽属相关考量因素，但并非决定性因素（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 B）码头工人在舱口附近的不当位置这一事实，可能表明其存在“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）——即未尽到保护自身安全的合理注意义务。\n\n若适用传统的普通法规则，这将完全阻却其获得赔偿；但在“比较过失”（comparative negligence）规则下（这是 MBE 考试中的默认规则），与有过失（contributory negligence）仅会减少赔偿金额。\n\n因此，船主无法仅凭此点胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）在判定过失问题时，陪审团可以考量发生损害的概率是否超过了避免该损害所需的负担（即“汉德公式”/Hand formula）。\n\n然而，本案并无迹象表明，因跌入敞开舱口而导致严重伤害的概率超过了保持舱口关闭所需的负担。\n\n尤其是考虑到惯例通常是在码头工人离开船舶后才开启舱口。\n\n教育目标：关于被告遵守（或背离）社会惯例或行业惯例的证据，对于过失的认定具有相关性，但并非决定性因素。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 295A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明社会惯例对于过失认定具有相关性，但非决定性因素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"被告遵守（或违反）社区或行业惯例的证据与过失认定相关，但并非决定性因素。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应如何回应该动议？","rule":"被告遵守（或违反）社区或行业惯例的证据与过失认定相关，但并非决定性因素。","analysis":"过失的构成要素 注意义务（Duty）——被告负有合理的注意义务，因为其行为给他人造成了可预见的伤害风险；违反义务（Breach）——被告因未尽到合理注意义务而构成违约；因果关系（Causation）——被告的违约行为实际上且在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）上导致了原告的损害；损害（Damages）——原告遭受了实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。在判定被告是否存在过失时，事实认定者（如陪审团）可以考虑所有相关因素——包括被告是否遵守了社会惯例或行业惯例。\n\n但惯例对于过失的认定并非决定性因素，因为整个社会或行业本身也可能存在过失。\n\n因此，法院应驳回船主的即决判决动议，并指示陪审团：在码头工人离开船舶后才开启舱口的惯例虽属相关考量因素，但并非决定性因素（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 B）码头工人在舱口附近的不当位置这一事实，可能表明其存在“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）——即未尽到保护自身安全的合理注意义务。\n\n若适用传统的普通法规则，这将完全阻却其获得赔偿；但在“比较过失”（comparative negligence）规则下（这是 MBE 考试中的默认规则），与有过失（contributory negligence）仅会减少赔偿金额。\n\n因此，船主无法仅凭此点胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）在判定过失问题时，陪审团可以考量发生损害的概率是否超过了避免该损害所需的负担（即“汉德公式”/Hand formula）。\n\n然而，本案并无迹象表明，因跌入敞开舱口而导致严重伤害的概率超过了保持舱口关闭所需的负担。\n\n尤其是考虑到惯例通常是在码头工人离开船舶后才开启舱口。\n\n教育目标：关于被告遵守（或背离）社会惯例或行业惯例的证据，对于过失的认定具有相关性，但并非决定性因素。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 295A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明社会惯例对于过失认定具有相关性，但非决定性因素）。","conclusion":"答案 A：驳回该动议，并将案件提交陪审团，指示陪审团该惯例与过失问题相关，但并非决定性因素。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":116,"sourceQuestionPage":348,"sourceQuestionPages":[348],"sourceExplanationPages":[349],"sourceAnswerPages":[349],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-117","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A rancher owns a herd of cattle that are permitted to freely roam his ranch. The cattle's blood is more alkaline than normal because their diet consists primarily of the natural grasses that grow in the ranch's slightly alkaline soil. The primary source of water for the ranch is a stream that runs through it. Two upstream landowners use water from the stream to operate mills. Recently, each landowner has negligently increased the alkaline nature of the water. As a result, several of the rancher's cattle have died from alkalosis, a disease caused by an animal's bodily fluids being too alkaline, and the rancher has had to treat the rest of his herd to prevent their deaths. The alkali added to the water by each landowner was a substantial factor in causing the injuries to the rancher's cattle. Although each alkali addition alone would have been a factual cause of the injuries to the cattle, neither landowner added enough alkali to the water to have caused the injuries in the absence of the naturally occurring alkali in the stream. The rancher has brought a negligence action against the two landowners for the harm suffered by his herd. Is each landowner's conduct a cause in fact of the damage suffered by the rancher?","choices":{"A":"No, because neither landowner's conduct as the sole source of alkali in the stream would have caused the damage.","B":"No, because neither landowner's conduct was the \"but for\" cause of the damage since it would have been caused by the action of the other landowner.","C":"Yes, because each landowner's conduct in adding additional alkali to the stream was a substantial factor in causing the damage.","D":"Yes, because it was foreseeable that each landowner's negligence in adding alkali to the stream could cause alkalosis."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Tort causation\n\nActual (factual) Single cause – but for defendant's conduct, plaintiff would not have cause been harmed Multiple causes: Second Restatement (majority rule) – defendant's conduct was substantial factor causing plaintiff's harm Third Restatement (minority rule) – defendant's conduct regarded as factual cause of plaintiff's harm Proximate (legal) Plaintiff's harm was reasonably foreseeable consequence of cause defendant's conduct\n\nA negligence action requires proof that the defendant's conduct was a cause in fact (ie, actual cause) of the plaintiff's injury. The predominant test for actual causation is the but- for test, which is satisfied if the plaintiff's injury would not have occurred without the defendant's conduct. However, this test will not work if multiple forces combine to cause the plaintiff's injury and any one alone would have been sufficient to cause the injury. In that case, the test is whether the defendant's conduct was a substantial factor in causing the injury. Here, each landowner negligently added alkali to the stream, and each addition was sufficient to cause the injuries to the rancher's cattle when combined with the naturally occurring alkali in the stream. As a result, the substantial-factor test (not the but-for test) applies. And since the facts stipulate that each landowner's conduct was a substantial factor in causing the injuries to the rancher's cattle, each landowner's conduct is a cause in fact of the damage suffered by the rancher (Choices A & B). (Choice D) The foreseeability of the plaintiff's harm is relevant to proximate cause, which is considered only after a defendant's conduct is determined to be a cause in fact of that harm.\n\nEducational objective: Where multiple forces combine to cause the plaintiff's injury and any one alone would have been sufficient to cause the injury, the test for actual causation is whether the defendant's conduct was a substantial factor in causing the injury.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 432(2) (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (substantial-factor test for multiple causes).","educationalObjective":"Where multiple forces combine to cause the plaintiff's injury and any one alone would have been sufficient to cause the injury, the test for actual causation is whether the defendant's conduct was a substantial factor in causing the injury.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 432(2) (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (substantial-factor test for multiple causes).","rule":"Where multiple forces combine to cause the plaintiff's injury and any one alone would have been sufficient to cause the injury, the test for actual causation is whether the defendant's conduct was a substantial factor in causing the injury.","irac":{"issue":"Is each landowner's conduct a cause in fact of the damage suffered by the rancher?","rule":"Where multiple forces combine to cause the plaintiff's injury and any one alone would have been sufficient to cause the injury, the test for actual causation is whether the defendant's conduct was a substantial factor in causing the injury.","analysis":"Tort causation\n\nActual (factual) Single cause – but for defendant's conduct, plaintiff would not have cause been harmed Multiple causes: Second Restatement (majority rule) – defendant's conduct was substantial factor causing plaintiff's harm Third Restatement (minority rule) – defendant's conduct regarded as factual cause of plaintiff's harm Proximate (legal) Plaintiff's harm was reasonably foreseeable consequence of cause defendant's conduct\n\nA negligence action requires proof that the defendant's conduct was a cause in fact (ie, actual cause) of the plaintiff's injury. The predominant test for actual causation is the but- for test, which is satisfied if the plaintiff's injury would not have occurred without the defendant's conduct. However, this test will not work if multiple forces combine to cause the plaintiff's injury and any one alone would have been sufficient to cause the injury. In that case, the test is whether the defendant's conduct was a substantial factor in causing the injury. Here, each landowner negligently added alkali to the stream, and each addition was sufficient to cause the injuries to the rancher's cattle when combined with the naturally occurring alkali in the stream. As a result, the substantial-factor test (not the but-for test) applies. And since the facts stipulate that each landowner's conduct was a substantial factor in causing the injuries to the rancher's cattle, each landowner's conduct is a cause in fact of the damage suffered by the rancher (Choices A & B). (Choice D) The foreseeability of the plaintiff's harm is relevant to proximate cause, which is considered only after a defendant's conduct is determined to be a cause in fact of that harm.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because each landowner's conduct in adding additional alkali to the stream was a substantial factor in causing the damage."},"questionZh":"一位牧场主拥有一群可以在其牧场上自由漫步的牛。\n\n这些牛的血液呈碱性，程度高于正常水平，因为它们的饮食主要由生长在牧场微碱性土壤中的天然牧草构成。\n\n该牧场的主要水源是一条流经牧场的溪流。\n\n上游的两名土地所有者利用这条溪流的水来运营磨坊。\n\n最近，这两名土地所有者均因疏忽大意而增加了水中的碱性成分。\n\n结果，牧场主的一些牛死于碱中毒（一种因动物体液碱性过高而引发的疾病），牧场主也不得不对其余的牛群进行治疗以防止它们死亡。\n\n每位土地所有者向水中添加的碱性物质，都是导致牧场主的牛群遭受损害的一个实质性因素。\n\n尽管每位土地所有者单独添加的碱性物质都足以构成导致牛群受损的事实原因，但若没有溪流中天然存在的碱性物质，任何一方单独添加的碱性量都不足以导致这种损害。\n\n牧场主就其牛群遭受的损害对这两名土地所有者提起了过失诉讼。\n\n每位土地所有者的行为是否构成了牧场主所受损害的事实原因？","choicesZh":{"A":"不构成，因为如果任何一方是溪流中碱性物质的唯一来源，其行为都不会导致这种损害。","B":"不构成，因为任何一方的行为都不是导致损害的“若无其事则无其果”（but-for）的原因，即即便没有该方的行为，另一方的行为也会导致同样的损害。","C":"构成，因为每位土地所有者向溪流中添加额外碱性物质的行为，都是导致损害的一个实质性因素。","D":"构成，因为每位土地所有者因疏忽向溪流中添加碱性物质可能导致碱中毒，这一后果是可预见的。"},"explanationZh":"侵权行为中的因果关系 实际（事实）因果关系：单一原因——若无被告的行为，原告便不会受到伤害；多重原因：第二次《侵权法重述》（多数规则）——被告的行为是导致原告伤害的实质性因素；第三次《侵权法重述》（少数规则）——被告的行为被视为导致原告伤害的事实原因。法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）：原告受到的伤害是被告行为可合理预见的后果。 过失诉讼要求证明被告的行为是原告受伤的事实原因（即实际原因）。\n\n判定实际因果关系的主要标准是“若无……则不……”（but-for）测试；如果原告的伤害在没有被告行为的情况下不会发生，则该测试成立。\n\n然而，当多种力量共同导致原告受伤，且其中任何一种力量单独作用都足以导致该伤害时，该测试便不再适用。\n\n在此类情况下，判定标准在于被告的行为是否为导致伤害的实质性因素。\n\n本案中，各土地所有者均过失地向溪流中添加了碱性物质；当这些添加物与溪流中天然存在的碱性物质结合时，任何一方的添加行为都足以导致牧场主的牲畜受损。\n\n因此，应适用“实质性因素”测试（而非“若无……则不……”测试）。\n\n鉴于案情已确定每位土地所有者的行为都是导致牧场主牲畜受损的实质性因素，因此每位土地所有者的行为均构成牧场主所受损害的事实原因（选项 A 和 B）。\n\n（选项 D）原告伤害的可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）与法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）相关，而法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）的考量仅在确定被告行为是该伤害的事实原因之后才会进行。\n\n教学目标：当多种力量共同导致原告受伤，且其中任何一种力量单独作用都足以导致该伤害时，判定实际因果关系的标准在于被告的行为是否为导致伤害的实质性因素。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 432(2) 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（针对多重原因的实质性因素测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当多种力量共同导致原告受损，且其中任何一种力量单独作用都足以导致该损害时，判定事实因果关系的标准在于被告的行为是否为导致损害的实质性因素。","iracZh":{"issue":"每位土地所有者的行为是否构成了牧场主所受损害的事实原因？","rule":"当多种力量共同导致原告受损，且其中任何一种力量单独作用都足以导致该损害时，判定事实因果关系的标准在于被告的行为是否为导致损害的实质性因素。","analysis":"侵权行为中的因果关系 实际（事实）因果关系：单一原因——若无被告的行为，原告便不会受到伤害；多重原因：第二次《侵权法重述》（多数规则）——被告的行为是导致原告伤害的实质性因素；第三次《侵权法重述》（少数规则）——被告的行为被视为导致原告伤害的事实原因。法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）：原告受到的伤害是被告行为可合理预见的后果。 过失诉讼要求证明被告的行为是原告受伤的事实原因（即实际原因）。\n\n判定实际因果关系的主要标准是“若无……则不……”（but-for）测试；如果原告的伤害在没有被告行为的情况下不会发生，则该测试成立。\n\n然而，当多种力量共同导致原告受伤，且其中任何一种力量单独作用都足以导致该伤害时，该测试便不再适用。\n\n在此类情况下，判定标准在于被告的行为是否为导致伤害的实质性因素。\n\n本案中，各土地所有者均过失地向溪流中添加了碱性物质；当这些添加物与溪流中天然存在的碱性物质结合时，任何一方的添加行为都足以导致牧场主的牲畜受损。\n\n因此，应适用“实质性因素”测试（而非“若无……则不……”测试）。\n\n鉴于案情已确定每位土地所有者的行为都是导致牧场主牲畜受损的实质性因素，因此每位土地所有者的行为均构成牧场主所受损害的事实原因（选项 A 和 B）。\n\n（选项 D）原告伤害的可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）与法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）相关，而法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）的考量仅在确定被告行为是该伤害的事实原因之后才会进行。\n\n教学目标：当多种力量共同导致原告受伤，且其中任何一种力量单独作用都足以导致该伤害时，判定实际因果关系的标准在于被告的行为是否为导致伤害的实质性因素。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 432(2) 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（针对多重原因的实质性因素测试）。","conclusion":"答案 C：构成，因为每位土地所有者向溪流中添加额外碱性物质的行为，都是导致损害的一个实质性因素。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":117,"sourceQuestionPage":350,"sourceQuestionPages":[350],"sourceExplanationPages":[351,352],"sourceAnswerPages":[351,352],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-118","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"A man was admitted to a hospital after complaining of persistent severe headaches. While he was there, hospital staff failed to diagnose his condition, and he was discharged. Two days later, the man died of a massive brain hemorrhage due to a congenital defect in an artery. The man's wife has brought a wrongful death action against the hospital. The wife offers expert testimony that the man would have had a \"reasonable chance\" (not greater than 50%) of surviving the hemorrhage if he had been given appropriate medical care at the hospital. In what type of jurisdiction would the wife's suit most likely be successful?","choices":{"A":"A jurisdiction that allows recovery based on strict liability.","B":"A jurisdiction that allows recovery for the loss of the chance of survival.","C":"A jurisdiction that applies traditional common law rules concerning burden of proof.","D":"A jurisdiction that recognizes loss of spousal consortium."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Actual causation\n\n(in wrongful death claims based on medical malpractice)\n\nTraditional common Decedent probably would have survived (ie, greater than 50% law rule chance) but for defendant's malpractice\n\nModern rule Decedent's chance of survival was reduced by defendant's malpractice (eg, 45% chance reduced to 20%) (loss-of-chance doctrine)\n\nWrongful death statutes allow a decedent's spouse, next of kin, or personal representative to sue a defendant for wrongfully causing the decedent's death. These lawsuits are often based on medical malpractice (eg, failure to diagnose—as seen here), which requires the plaintiff to prove actual causation by a preponderance of the evidence. Traditionally, this standard is met if it is more likely than not (ie, greater than 50% chance) that the decedent would have survived but for the defendant's malpractice. Here, the wife offered expert testimony that the man had a reasonable chance—not greater than 50 percent—of survival had he received appropriate medical care. As a result, her suit would not be successful in a jurisdiction that applies traditional common law rules concerning burden of proof (Choice C). However, most jurisdictions recognize the loss-of- chance doctrine, which allows the plaintiff to recover if the malpractice caused any loss of the chance of survival. Therefore, the wife's suit is most likely to be successful in such a jurisdiction. (Choice A) Strict liability subjects a defendant to liability regardless of the defendant's level of fault. But it would still require proof of actual causation, which cannot be proved here unless this jurisdiction allows recovery for loss of chance. (Choice D) In a wrongful death action, most jurisdictions allow recovery for loss of spousal consortium (ie, spousal support, companionship, and sexual relations). But even if this jurisdiction does so, the wife cannot establish actual causation or succeed in her claim unless this is a loss-of-chance jurisdiction.\n\nEducational objective: The loss-of-chance doctrine allows a plaintiff to recover in a wrongful death action based on medical malpractice if the defendant's malpractice caused any reduction in the decedent's chance of survival.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm, § 26 cmt. n (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (explaining that many courts recognize a lost chance for cure of a medical condition as a legally cognizable harm).","educationalObjective":"The loss-of-chance doctrine allows a plaintiff to recover in a wrongful death action based on medical malpractice if the defendant's malpractice caused any reduction in the decedent's chance of survival.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm, § 26 cmt. n (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (explaining that many courts recognize a lost chance for cure of a medical condition as a legally cognizable harm).","rule":"The loss-of-chance doctrine allows a plaintiff to recover in a wrongful death action based on medical malpractice if the defendant's malpractice caused any reduction in the decedent's chance of survival.","irac":{"issue":"In what type of jurisdiction would the wife's suit most likely be successful?","rule":"The loss-of-chance doctrine allows a plaintiff to recover in a wrongful death action based on medical malpractice if the defendant's malpractice caused any reduction in the decedent's chance of survival.","analysis":"Actual causation\n\n(in wrongful death claims based on medical malpractice)\n\nTraditional common Decedent probably would have survived (ie, greater than 50% law rule chance) but for defendant's malpractice\n\nModern rule Decedent's chance of survival was reduced by defendant's malpractice (eg, 45% chance reduced to 20%) (loss-of-chance doctrine)\n\nWrongful death statutes allow a decedent's spouse, next of kin, or personal representative to sue a defendant for wrongfully causing the decedent's death. These lawsuits are often based on medical malpractice (eg, failure to diagnose—as seen here), which requires the plaintiff to prove actual causation by a preponderance of the evidence. Traditionally, this standard is met if it is more likely than not (ie, greater than 50% chance) that the decedent would have survived but for the defendant's malpractice. Here, the wife offered expert testimony that the man had a reasonable chance—not greater than 50 percent—of survival had he received appropriate medical care. As a result, her suit would not be successful in a jurisdiction that applies traditional common law rules concerning burden of proof (Choice C). However, most jurisdictions recognize the loss-of- chance doctrine, which allows the plaintiff to recover if the malpractice caused any loss of the chance of survival. Therefore, the wife's suit is most likely to be successful in such a jurisdiction. (Choice A) Strict liability subjects a defendant to liability regardless of the defendant's level of fault. But it would still require proof of actual causation, which cannot be proved here unless this jurisdiction allows recovery for loss of chance. (Choice D) In a wrongful death action, most jurisdictions allow recovery for loss of spousal consortium (ie, spousal support, companionship, and sexual relations). But even if this jurisdiction does so, the wife cannot establish actual causation or succeed in her claim unless this is a loss-of-chance jurisdiction.","conclusion":"Answer B: A jurisdiction that allows recovery for the loss of the chance of survival."},"questionZh":"一名男子因持续剧烈头痛入院。\n\n在院期间，医务人员未能诊断出其病因，随后该男子出院。\n\n两天后，他因动脉先天性缺陷引发的大面积脑出血而死亡。\n\n该男子的妻子针对医院提起了不当致死诉讼。\n\n妻子提供的专家证词指出，如果该男子在医院接受了适当的医疗护理，他将有“合理机会”（虽不超过50%）在脑出血事件中幸存。\n\n在何种类型的司法管辖区，该妻子的诉讼最有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"允许基于严格责任原则获得赔偿的司法管辖区。","B":"允许就“丧失生存机会”获得赔偿的司法管辖区。","C":"适用关于举证责任的传统普通法规则的司法管辖区。","D":"承认“丧失配偶伴侣权”（loss of spousal consortium）的司法管辖区。"},"explanationZh":"实际因果关系 （基于医疗事故的不当致死索赔） 传统普通法规则：若无被告的医疗过失，死者本可能存活（即存活几率超过 50%）。 现代规则：被告的医疗过失降低了死者的存活几率（例如，从 45% 降至 20%）（即“丧失机会”原则）。\n\n不当致死诉讼允许死者的配偶、近亲属或遗产代理人起诉被告，指控其不当行为导致了死者的死亡。此类诉讼通常基于医疗事故（例如本案中的漏诊），要求原告以“优势证据”标准证明存在实际因果关系。\n\n按照传统标准，若能证明“若无被告的医疗过失，死者存活的可能性大于不存活的可能性”（即存活几率超过 50%），则满足该要求。\n\n在本案中，妻子提供的专家证词显示，如果该男子接受了适当的医疗护理，他有合理的存活机会——但该几率不超过 50%。\n\n因此，在适用传统普通法举证责任规则的司法管辖区，她的诉讼将无法胜诉（选项 C）。\n\n然而，大多数司法管辖区承认“丧失机会”原则；根据该原则，如果医疗过失导致死者的存活机会受到任何程度的减损，原告即可获得赔偿。\n\n因此，在适用该原则的司法管辖区，妻子的诉讼最有可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）严格责任意味着无论被告过错程度如何，均须承担责任。\n\n但这仍要求证明存在实际因果关系；除非该司法管辖区允许就“丧失机会”获得赔偿，否则本案无法证明这一点。\n\n（选项 D）在不当致死诉讼中，大多数司法管辖区允许就丧失配偶伴侣权益（即配偶间的支持、陪伴和性关系）获得赔偿。\n\n但即使该司法管辖区允许此类赔偿，除非属于承认“丧失机会”原则的司法管辖区，否则妻子仍无法确立实际因果关系或赢得索赔。\n\n教育目标：“丧失机会”原则规定，在基于医疗事故的不当致死诉讼中，如果被告的医疗过失导致死者的存活几率出现任何程度的降低，原告即可获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》（Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical & Emotional Harm），第 26 条及评注。\n\nn (美国法律协会 2010)（阐明许多法院承认，丧失治愈某种病症的机会构成法律上可予认可的损害）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“丧失机会”原则（loss-of-chance doctrine）允许原告在基于医疗事故提起的“不当致死”诉讼中获得赔偿，前提是被告的医疗过失导致死者的生存机会有所降低。","iracZh":{"issue":"在何种类型的司法管辖区，该妻子的诉讼最有可能胜诉？","rule":"“丧失机会”原则（loss-of-chance doctrine）允许原告在基于医疗事故提起的“不当致死”诉讼中获得赔偿，前提是被告的医疗过失导致死者的生存机会有所降低。","analysis":"实际因果关系 （基于医疗事故的不当致死索赔） 传统普通法规则：若无被告的医疗过失，死者本可能存活（即存活几率超过 50%）。 现代规则：被告的医疗过失降低了死者的存活几率（例如，从 45% 降至 20%）（即“丧失机会”原则）。\n\n不当致死诉讼允许死者的配偶、近亲属或遗产代理人起诉被告，指控其不当行为导致了死者的死亡。此类诉讼通常基于医疗事故（例如本案中的漏诊），要求原告以“优势证据”标准证明存在实际因果关系。\n\n按照传统标准，若能证明“若无被告的医疗过失，死者存活的可能性大于不存活的可能性”（即存活几率超过 50%），则满足该要求。\n\n在本案中，妻子提供的专家证词显示，如果该男子接受了适当的医疗护理，他有合理的存活机会——但该几率不超过 50%。\n\n因此，在适用传统普通法举证责任规则的司法管辖区，她的诉讼将无法胜诉（选项 C）。\n\n然而，大多数司法管辖区承认“丧失机会”原则；根据该原则，如果医疗过失导致死者的存活机会受到任何程度的减损，原告即可获得赔偿。\n\n因此，在适用该原则的司法管辖区，妻子的诉讼最有可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）严格责任意味着无论被告过错程度如何，均须承担责任。\n\n但这仍要求证明存在实际因果关系；除非该司法管辖区允许就“丧失机会”获得赔偿，否则本案无法证明这一点。\n\n（选项 D）在不当致死诉讼中，大多数司法管辖区允许就丧失配偶伴侣权益（即配偶间的支持、陪伴和性关系）获得赔偿。\n\n但即使该司法管辖区允许此类赔偿，除非属于承认“丧失机会”原则的司法管辖区，否则妻子仍无法确立实际因果关系或赢得索赔。\n\n教育目标：“丧失机会”原则规定，在基于医疗事故的不当致死诉讼中，如果被告的医疗过失导致死者的存活几率出现任何程度的降低，原告即可获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》（Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical & Emotional Harm），第 26 条及评注。\n\nn (美国法律协会 2010)（阐明许多法院承认，丧失治愈某种病症的机会构成法律上可予认可的损害）。","conclusion":"答案 B：允许就“丧失生存机会”获得赔偿的司法管辖区。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":118,"sourceQuestionPage":353,"sourceQuestionPages":[353],"sourceExplanationPages":[354,355],"sourceAnswerPages":[354,355],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-119","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"A man and a woman were competing in an illegal drag race. Both of them were driving over the speed limit but were otherwise driving very carefully. However, when a tire on the woman's car suddenly blew out, she lost control of her car and crashed, injuring a pedestrian. The pedestrian later sued the man, because the woman had no insurance or assets. Will the pedestrian be likely to prevail in that action?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man did not cause the injury.","B":"No, because the man was driving very carefully.","C":"Yes, because the man and the woman were acting in concert in a dangerous activity.","D":"Yes, because the man was exceeding the speed limit."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Liability of multiple negligent actors\n\nIndivisible injuries Separable injuries\n\nActing independently Joint & several liability* Several liability only\n\nActing in concert Joint & several liability\n\n*Default rule on MBE.\n\nTort claims for personal injury generally require proof that the defendant's tortious conduct actually and proximately caused the plaintiff's harm. However, when two or more tortfeasors act pursuant to a common plan or design (ie, act in concert) AND one or more of them cause the plaintiff's harm, all of the tortfeasors are jointly and severally liable for 100% of the harm. Here, the man and woman were competing in an illegal drag race (acting in concert) when the woman crashed her car and injured the pedestrian. As a result, the man and woman are jointly and severally liable for the pedestrian's injury, and the pedestrian will prevail against the man—regardless of whether he also caused the injury (Choice A). (Choices B & D) Even if the man was driving carefully, he was likely negligent per se since he was speeding in an illegal drag race in violation of traffic laws designed to protect persons like the pedestrian from harm. But this alone does not subject the man to liability since it does not prove that his tortious conduct caused the pedestrian's injury. Instead, the man is liable because he was acting in concert with the woman who caused the injury.\n\nEducational objective: If multiple tortfeasors act pursuant to a common plan or design (ie, act in concert) and at least one of them causes the plaintiff's harm, then all of the tortfeasors are jointly and severally liable for the harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 876 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a defendant is subject to liability if the defendant commits a tortious act in concert with others or pursuant to a common design).","educationalObjective":"If multiple tortfeasors act pursuant to a common plan or design (ie, act in concert) and at least one of them causes the plaintiff's harm, then all of the tortfeasors are jointly and severally liable for the harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 876 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a defendant is subject to liability if the defendant commits a tortious act in concert with others or pursuant to a common design).","rule":"If multiple tortfeasors act pursuant to a common plan or design (ie, act in concert) and at least one of them causes the plaintiff's harm, then all of the tortfeasors are jointly and severally liable for the harm.","irac":{"issue":"Will the pedestrian be likely to prevail in that action?","rule":"If multiple tortfeasors act pursuant to a common plan or design (ie, act in concert) and at least one of them causes the plaintiff's harm, then all of the tortfeasors are jointly and severally liable for the harm.","analysis":"Liability of multiple negligent actors\n\nIndivisible injuries Separable injuries\n\nActing independently Joint & several liability* Several liability only\n\nActing in concert Joint & several liability\n\n*Default rule on MBE.\n\nTort claims for personal injury generally require proof that the defendant's tortious conduct actually and proximately caused the plaintiff's harm. However, when two or more tortfeasors act pursuant to a common plan or design (ie, act in concert) AND one or more of them cause the plaintiff's harm, all of the tortfeasors are jointly and severally liable for 100% of the harm. Here, the man and woman were competing in an illegal drag race (acting in concert) when the woman crashed her car and injured the pedestrian. As a result, the man and woman are jointly and severally liable for the pedestrian's injury, and the pedestrian will prevail against the man—regardless of whether he also caused the injury (Choice A). (Choices B & D) Even if the man was driving carefully, he was likely negligent per se since he was speeding in an illegal drag race in violation of traffic laws designed to protect persons like the pedestrian from harm. But this alone does not subject the man to liability since it does not prove that his tortious conduct caused the pedestrian's injury. Instead, the man is liable because he was acting in concert with the woman who caused the injury.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the man and the woman were acting in concert in a dangerous activity."},"questionZh":"一男一女正在进行非法飙车比赛。\n\n两人都超速行驶，但除此之外，他们驾驶得非常谨慎。\n\n然而，当女车的轮胎突然爆胎时，她失去了对车辆的控制，撞车并撞伤了一名行人。\n\n行人后来起诉了男车主，因为女车主没有保险或资产。\n\n行人有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为男车主并非造成伤害的直接原因。","B":"不可能，因为男车主驾驶得非常谨慎。","C":"可能，因为男女双方共同从事了危险活动。","D":"可能，因为男车主超速行驶。"},"explanationZh":"多名有过失行为人的责任 不可分割的伤害 可分割的伤害 独立行动 连带责任（Joint & several liability）* 仅承担各自份额责任（Several liability only） 协同行动（Acting in concert） 连带责任 *MBE（多州律师资格考试）中的默认规则。\n\n针对人身伤害的侵权索赔通常要求证明被告的侵权行为实际上且在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）上（proximately）导致了原告的伤害。\n\n然而，当两名或多名侵权行为人根据共同计划或意图行事（即“协同行动”），且其中一人或多人导致了原告的伤害时，所有侵权行为人均需对伤害承担 100% 的连带责任。\n\n在本案中，该男子和女子正在进行非法的直线加速赛车（属于协同行动），期间女子驾车发生事故并导致行人受伤。\n\n因此，该男子和女子对行人的伤害承担连带责任，行人针对该男子的索赔将获得胜诉——无论该男子是否也直接导致了伤害（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）即使该男子驾驶时很小心，他也可能构成“本身过失”（negligence per se），因为他在非法赛车中超速行驶，违反了旨在保护行人免受伤害的交通法规。\n\n但这本身并不足以让该男子承担责任，因为它并未证明其侵权行为导致了行人的伤害。\n\n相反，该男子之所以承担责任，是因为他与导致伤害的女子存在协同行动关系。\n\n教育目标：如果多名侵权行为人根据共同计划或意图行事（即协同行动），且其中至少一人导致了原告的伤害，则所有侵权行为人均需对该伤害承担连带责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 876 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明若被告与他人协同或根据共同意图实施侵权行为，则须承担责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果多个侵权行为人按照共同计划或意图行事（即共同实施），并且其中至少一人造成了原告的损害，则所有侵权行为人均须对该损害承担连带责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"行人提起诉讼是否有可能胜诉？","rule":"如果多个侵权行为人按照共同计划或意图行事（即共同实施），并且其中至少一人造成了原告的损害，则所有侵权行为人均须对该损害承担连带责任。","analysis":"多名有过失行为人的责任 不可分割的伤害 可分割的伤害 独立行动 连带责任（Joint & several liability）* 仅承担各自份额责任（Several liability only） 协同行动（Acting in concert） 连带责任 *MBE（多州律师资格考试）中的默认规则。\n\n针对人身伤害的侵权索赔通常要求证明被告的侵权行为实际上且在法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）上（proximately）导致了原告的伤害。\n\n然而，当两名或多名侵权行为人根据共同计划或意图行事（即“协同行动”），且其中一人或多人导致了原告的伤害时，所有侵权行为人均需对伤害承担 100% 的连带责任。\n\n在本案中，该男子和女子正在进行非法的直线加速赛车（属于协同行动），期间女子驾车发生事故并导致行人受伤。\n\n因此，该男子和女子对行人的伤害承担连带责任，行人针对该男子的索赔将获得胜诉——无论该男子是否也直接导致了伤害（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）即使该男子驾驶时很小心，他也可能构成“本身过失”（negligence per se），因为他在非法赛车中超速行驶，违反了旨在保护行人免受伤害的交通法规。\n\n但这本身并不足以让该男子承担责任，因为它并未证明其侵权行为导致了行人的伤害。\n\n相反，该男子之所以承担责任，是因为他与导致伤害的女子存在协同行动关系。\n\n教育目标：如果多名侵权行为人根据共同计划或意图行事（即协同行动），且其中至少一人导致了原告的伤害，则所有侵权行为人均需对该伤害承担连带责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 876 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明若被告与他人协同或根据共同意图实施侵权行为，则须承担责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：可能，因为男女双方共同从事了危险活动。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":119,"sourceQuestionPage":356,"sourceQuestionPages":[356],"sourceExplanationPages":[357],"sourceAnswerPages":[357],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-120","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"The owner of a shopping mall hired a construction company to design and construct a new entryway to the mall. The construction company negligently selected an unusually slippery material for the floor covering. A week after the entryway was completed, a customer who had come to the mall to buy cosmetics slipped on the floor of the entryway, sustaining injuries. The customer sued the mall owner for the construction company's negligent design of the mall's entryway. Will the injured customer be likely to recover damages?","choices":{"A":"No, because no other customers had previously slipped on the floor.","B":"No, because the construction company will likely be considered an independent contractor.","C":"Yes, because the customer intended to make a purchase at the mall.","D":"Yes, because the mall owner's duty to maintain safe conditions was nondelegable."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Nondelegable duties\n\n(duties that cannot be assigned to another to avoid liability)\n\nMaintain safe conditions on premises open to public (eg, store, restaurant) Safely perform activities that: are abnormally or highly dangerous infringe on private property right (eg, nuisance, trespass) are regulated by law or are conducted in public place A principal is generally not vicariously liable for torts committed by its independent contractor—one who performs work for another outside an employment relationship. However, vicarious liability will be imposed if the independent contractor's work breaches a nondelegable duty owed by the principal—a duty of care that cannot be assigned to a third party to avoid liability. This includes the duty of a land possessor who holds his/her land open to the public to keep the premises safe for business visitors. Here, the construction company was likely an independent contractor since it was hired for a one-time job—to design and construct a new entryway to the mall. And though the mall owner ordinarily would not be liable for an independent contractor's torts, the mall owner had a nondelegable duty to maintain safe conditions for its customers (Choice B). Since the construction company's work—selecting a slippery material for the floor—breached that duty, the mall owner is vicariously liable for the customer's resulting injuries. (Choice A) Proof of prior accidents could help show that the entryway was negligently designed. But such proof is not required here since (1) land possessors have a nondelegable duty to use reasonable care to discover dangerous conditions on the land before an injury occurs and (2) the construction company's negligence is stipulated above. (Choice C) Since the mall was held open to the public, the mall owner had a nondelegable duty to maintain safe premises for all business visitors (eg, window shoppers)—not just those intending to make a purchase. Therefore, the customer's intention to make a purchase is irrelevant to her recovery.\n\nEducational objective: A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the independent contractor breaches a nondelegable duty of care owed by the principal—eg, a land possessor's duty to keep the premises safe for business visitors.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 57 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (no vicarious liability generally for independent contractors).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 344 (nondelegable duty to keep business premises safe).","educationalObjective":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the independent contractor breaches a nondelegable duty of care owed by the principal—eg, a land possessor's duty to keep the premises safe for business visitors.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 57 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (no vicarious liability generally for independent contractors).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 344 (nondelegable duty to keep business premises safe).","rule":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the independent contractor breaches a nondelegable duty of care owed by the principal—eg, a land possessor's duty to keep the premises safe for business visitors.","irac":{"issue":"Will the injured customer be likely to recover damages?","rule":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the independent contractor breaches a nondelegable duty of care owed by the principal—eg, a land possessor's duty to keep the premises safe for business visitors.","analysis":"Nondelegable duties\n\n(duties that cannot be assigned to another to avoid liability)\n\nMaintain safe conditions on premises open to public (eg, store, restaurant) Safely perform activities that: are abnormally or highly dangerous infringe on private property right (eg, nuisance, trespass) are regulated by law or are conducted in public place A principal is generally not vicariously liable for torts committed by its independent contractor—one who performs work for another outside an employment relationship. However, vicarious liability will be imposed if the independent contractor's work breaches a nondelegable duty owed by the principal—a duty of care that cannot be assigned to a third party to avoid liability. This includes the duty of a land possessor who holds his/her land open to the public to keep the premises safe for business visitors. Here, the construction company was likely an independent contractor since it was hired for a one-time job—to design and construct a new entryway to the mall. And though the mall owner ordinarily would not be liable for an independent contractor's torts, the mall owner had a nondelegable duty to maintain safe conditions for its customers (Choice B). Since the construction company's work—selecting a slippery material for the floor—breached that duty, the mall owner is vicariously liable for the customer's resulting injuries. (Choice A) Proof of prior accidents could help show that the entryway was negligently designed. But such proof is not required here since (1) land possessors have a nondelegable duty to use reasonable care to discover dangerous conditions on the land before an injury occurs and (2) the construction company's negligence is stipulated above. (Choice C) Since the mall was held open to the public, the mall owner had a nondelegable duty to maintain safe premises for all business visitors (eg, window shoppers)—not just those intending to make a purchase. Therefore, the customer's intention to make a purchase is irrelevant to her recovery.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the mall owner's duty to maintain safe conditions was nondelegable."},"questionZh":"某购物中心业主聘请了一家建筑公司来设计并建造新的入口。\n\n该建筑公司在选择地面铺设材料时存在疏忽，选用了异常湿滑的材料。\n\n入口完工一周后，一位前来购买化妆品的顾客在入口地面上滑倒并受伤。\n\n该顾客起诉购物中心业主，理由是建筑公司对入口的设计存在疏忽。\n\n受伤顾客是否很可能获得损害赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为此前没有其他顾客在该地面上滑倒过。","B":"不会，因为该建筑公司很可能被视为独立承包商。","C":"会，因为该顾客有意在购物中心进行消费。","D":"会，因为购物中心业主维持安全环境的义务是不可转让的。"},"explanationZh":"不可转嫁的义务 （指不能通过委托他人来规避责任的义务） 维持向公众开放场所（如商店、餐馆）的安全状况；安全地进行以下活动：具有异常或高度危险性的活动、侵犯私人财产权的活动（如构成妨害或侵入）、受法律监管的活动或在公共场所进行的活动。委托人通常不对其独立承包商（即在雇佣关系之外为他人工作的人）实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，如果独立承包商的工作违反了委托人负有的不可转嫁义务（即不能通过委托第三方来规避的注意义务），则委托人需承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n这包括土地占有者向公众开放其土地时，有义务确保场所对商业访客安全。\n\n在本案中，建筑公司很可能属于独立承包商，因为它是受雇执行一项一次性任务——即设计并建造商场的新入口。\n\n尽管商场业主通常不对独立承包商的侵权行为负责，但商场业主负有为顾客维持安全状况的不可转嫁义务（选项 B）。\n\n由于建筑公司的施工行为（选用易滑的地面材料）违反了这一义务，商场业主需对顾客因此遭受的伤害承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 A）证明此前曾发生过事故有助于表明入口设计存在疏忽。\n\n但本案中无需此类证据，因为 (1) 土地占有者负有不可转嫁的义务，即在伤害发生前尽合理注意以发现土地上的危险状况；(2) 上文已认定建筑公司存在疏忽。\n\n（选项 C）由于商场向公众开放，商场业主有义务为所有商业访客（例如仅浏览橱窗而不打算购物的人）维持场所安全，而不仅仅是针对那些打算购物的人。\n\n因此，顾客是否有意购物与其能否获得赔偿无关。\n\n教育目标：委托人通常不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当独立承包商违反了委托人负有的不可转嫁注意义务时，委托人需承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）——例如，土地占有者有义务确保场所对商业访客安全。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第57条（美国法律学会，2012年）（一般而言，对独立承包商不承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第344条（维持营业场所安全属于不可转让义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"委托人通常不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，如果独立承包商违反了委托人所负有的不可转让的注意义务（例如，土地占有人有义务确保场所对商业访客而言是安全的），则委托人须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","iracZh":{"issue":"受伤顾客是否很可能获得损害赔偿？","rule":"委托人通常不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，如果独立承包商违反了委托人所负有的不可转让的注意义务（例如，土地占有人有义务确保场所对商业访客而言是安全的），则委托人须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","analysis":"不可转嫁的义务 （指不能通过委托他人来规避责任的义务） 维持向公众开放场所（如商店、餐馆）的安全状况；安全地进行以下活动：具有异常或高度危险性的活动、侵犯私人财产权的活动（如构成妨害或侵入）、受法律监管的活动或在公共场所进行的活动。委托人通常不对其独立承包商（即在雇佣关系之外为他人工作的人）实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，如果独立承包商的工作违反了委托人负有的不可转嫁义务（即不能通过委托第三方来规避的注意义务），则委托人需承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n这包括土地占有者向公众开放其土地时，有义务确保场所对商业访客安全。\n\n在本案中，建筑公司很可能属于独立承包商，因为它是受雇执行一项一次性任务——即设计并建造商场的新入口。\n\n尽管商场业主通常不对独立承包商的侵权行为负责，但商场业主负有为顾客维持安全状况的不可转嫁义务（选项 B）。\n\n由于建筑公司的施工行为（选用易滑的地面材料）违反了这一义务，商场业主需对顾客因此遭受的伤害承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 A）证明此前曾发生过事故有助于表明入口设计存在疏忽。\n\n但本案中无需此类证据，因为 (1) 土地占有者负有不可转嫁的义务，即在伤害发生前尽合理注意以发现土地上的危险状况；(2) 上文已认定建筑公司存在疏忽。\n\n（选项 C）由于商场向公众开放，商场业主有义务为所有商业访客（例如仅浏览橱窗而不打算购物的人）维持场所安全，而不仅仅是针对那些打算购物的人。\n\n因此，顾客是否有意购物与其能否获得赔偿无关。\n\n教育目标：委托人通常不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当独立承包商违反了委托人负有的不可转嫁注意义务时，委托人需承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）——例如，土地占有者有义务确保场所对商业访客安全。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第57条（美国法律学会，2012年）（一般而言，对独立承包商不承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第344条（维持营业场所安全属于不可转让义务）。","conclusion":"答案 D：会，因为购物中心业主维持安全环境的义务是不可转让的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"intention","ipa":"/ˌɪntˈɛntʃən/","syllables":"in·ten·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 意图, 目的, 含义；[医] 愈合, 意向","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“intention”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:intentions","sourceSentence":"Therefore, the customer's intention to make a purchase is irrelevant to her recovery.","synonyms":["purpose","intent","aim","design"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":120,"sourceQuestionPage":358,"sourceQuestionPages":[358],"sourceExplanationPages":[359,360],"sourceAnswerPages":[359,360],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-121","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"A pedestrian was crossing a street in a crosswalk when a woman walking just ahead of him was hit by a truck. The pedestrian, who had jumped out of the way of the truck, administered CPR to the woman, who was a stranger. The woman bled profusely, and the pedestrian was covered in blood. The woman died in the ambulance on the way to the hospital. The pedestrian became very depressed immediately after the incident and developed physical symptoms as a result of his emotional distress. The pedestrian has brought an action against the driver of the truck for negligent infliction of emotional distress. In her defense, the driver asserts that she should not be held liable, because the pedestrian's emotional distress and resulting physical symptoms are not compensable. What is the strongest argument that the pedestrian can make in response to the driver's defense?","choices":{"A":"The pedestrian saw the driver hit the woman.","B":"The pedestrian was acting as a Good Samaritan.","C":"The pedestrian was covered in the woman's blood and developed physical symptoms as a result of his emotional distress.","D":"The pedestrian was in the zone of danger."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A plaintiff can establish negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) under the zone- of-danger theory when: the defendant's negligent conduct placed the plaintiff in danger of immediate bodily harm and that danger caused the plaintiff serious emotional distress. Here, the truck driver's negligence forced the pedestrian to jump out of the truck's way to avoid being run over in the crosswalk (danger of immediate bodily harm). And since this danger likely caused the pedestrian's depression and resulting physical symptoms (serious emotional harm), the pedestrian's strongest argument for recovery is that he was in the zone of danger. (Choice A) Under the bystander theory for NIED, the plaintiff must suffer serious emotional distress upon witnessing the defendant negligently causing serious physical harm to the plaintiff's close relative. Here, although the pedestrian saw the driver hit the woman, she was a stranger—not a close relative. Therefore, the pedestrian cannot recover under this theory. (Choice B) \"Good Samaritan\" refers to the rescue doctrine, which holds a defendant liable for any physical harm the plaintiff sustains during an attempt to aid a person that the defendant placed in harm's way. Here, the pedestrian's rescue attempt caused him emotional harm that later resulted in physical symptoms—not direct physical harm. Therefore, he cannot recover under this doctrine. (Choice C) The fact that the pedestrian was covered in the woman's blood shows that he came to the woman's aid—not that he had been placed in the zone of danger.\n\nEducational objective: Negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) liability arises under the zone-of-danger theory when (1) the defendant negligently placed the plaintiff at risk of immediate bodily injury and (2) that risk caused the plaintiff serious emotional harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 47 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (negligent infliction of emotional distress).","educationalObjective":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) liability arises under the zone-of-danger theory when (1) the defendant negligently placed the plaintiff at risk of immediate bodily injury and (2) that risk caused the plaintiff serious emotional harm.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 47 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (negligent infliction of emotional distress).","rule":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) liability arises under the zone-of-danger theory when (1) the defendant negligently placed the plaintiff at risk of immediate bodily injury and (2) that risk caused the plaintiff serious emotional harm.","irac":{"issue":"What is the strongest argument that the pedestrian can make in response to the driver's defense?","rule":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) liability arises under the zone-of-danger theory when (1) the defendant negligently placed the plaintiff at risk of immediate bodily injury and (2) that risk caused the plaintiff serious emotional harm.","analysis":"A plaintiff can establish negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) under the zone- of-danger theory when: the defendant's negligent conduct placed the plaintiff in danger of immediate bodily harm and that danger caused the plaintiff serious emotional distress. Here, the truck driver's negligence forced the pedestrian to jump out of the truck's way to avoid being run over in the crosswalk (danger of immediate bodily harm). And since this danger likely caused the pedestrian's depression and resulting physical symptoms (serious emotional harm), the pedestrian's strongest argument for recovery is that he was in the zone of danger. (Choice A) Under the bystander theory for NIED, the plaintiff must suffer serious emotional distress upon witnessing the defendant negligently causing serious physical harm to the plaintiff's close relative. Here, although the pedestrian saw the driver hit the woman, she was a stranger—not a close relative. Therefore, the pedestrian cannot recover under this theory. (Choice B) \"Good Samaritan\" refers to the rescue doctrine, which holds a defendant liable for any physical harm the plaintiff sustains during an attempt to aid a person that the defendant placed in harm's way. Here, the pedestrian's rescue attempt caused him emotional harm that later resulted in physical symptoms—not direct physical harm. Therefore, he cannot recover under this doctrine. (Choice C) The fact that the pedestrian was covered in the woman's blood shows that he came to the woman's aid—not that he had been placed in the zone of danger.","conclusion":"Answer D: The pedestrian was in the zone of danger."},"questionZh":"一名行人正在人行横道上过马路时，走在他前方不远处的一名女子被一辆卡车撞倒。\n\n这名行人及时跳开躲过了卡车，并对该女子（一名陌生人）实施了心肺复苏术。\n\n该女子流血严重，导致行人身上也沾满了血迹。\n\n她在送往医院的救护车上去世。\n\n事发后，该行人立即陷入极度抑郁，并因情绪痛苦而出现了身体症状。\n\n该行人针对卡车司机提起了“过失导致精神痛苦”（negligent infliction of emotional distress）的诉讼。\n\n司机在抗辩中主张自己不应承担责任，理由是行人的精神痛苦及其引发的身体症状不属于可获赔偿的损害。\n\n针对司机的抗辩，该行人能提出的最有力的论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"行人目睹了司机撞倒该女子的过程。","B":"行人的行为属于“好撒马利亚人”式的善意救助。","C":"行人身上沾满了该女子的血迹，并因情绪痛苦而出现了身体症状。","D":"行人处于“危险区域”（zone of danger）内。"},"explanationZh":"根据“危险区域”理论（zone-of-danger theory），若被告的过失行为使原告面临遭受即时身体伤害的危险，且该危险导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦，则原告可主张被告承担过失造成精神痛苦（NIED）的责任。\n\n在本案中，卡车司机的过失迫使行人为了避免在人行横道上被碾压而跳开躲避（即面临即时身体伤害的危险）。\n\n鉴于这种危险很可能导致了行人的抑郁症及其随后的身体症状（即严重的精神伤害），行人主张获得赔偿的最有力依据便是他当时处于危险区域内。\n\n（选项 A）根据 NIED 的“旁观者”理论（bystander theory），原告必须在目睹被告因过失导致其近亲属遭受严重身体伤害时，自身遭受严重精神痛苦，方可主张赔偿。\n\n在本案中，尽管行人目睹了司机撞倒那名女子，但该女子是陌生人，而非其近亲属。\n\n因此，行人无法依据该理论获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）“好撒玛利亚人”（Good Samaritan）概念涉及“救援原则”（rescue doctrine）；该原则规定，若被告将某人置于危险境地，且原告在试图救助该人时遭受身体伤害，则被告须为此承担责任。\n\n在本案中，行人的救援行为导致其遭受精神伤害，进而引发身体症状，而非直接遭受身体伤害。\n\n因此，他无法依据该原则获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）行人身上沾满该女子血迹这一事实，表明他曾上前救助该女子，但这并不意味着他曾处于危险区域之中。\n\n教育目标：根据“危险区域”理论，当 (1) 被告过失地使原告面临即时身体伤害的风险，且 (2) 该风险导致原告遭受严重精神伤害时，即产生过失造成精神痛苦（NIED）的责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 47 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（过失造成精神痛苦）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“危险区域”理论，若满足以下条件，即产生“过失导致精神痛苦”（NIED）的责任：(1) 被告的过失行为使原告面临遭受即时身体伤害的风险；(2) 该风险导致原告遭受了严重的情绪伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"针对司机的抗辩，该行人能提出的最有力的论点是什么？","rule":"根据“危险区域”理论，若满足以下条件，即产生“过失导致精神痛苦”（NIED）的责任：(1) 被告的过失行为使原告面临遭受即时身体伤害的风险；(2) 该风险导致原告遭受了严重的情绪伤害。","analysis":"根据“危险区域”理论（zone-of-danger theory），若被告的过失行为使原告面临遭受即时身体伤害的危险，且该危险导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦，则原告可主张被告承担过失造成精神痛苦（NIED）的责任。\n\n在本案中，卡车司机的过失迫使行人为了避免在人行横道上被碾压而跳开躲避（即面临即时身体伤害的危险）。\n\n鉴于这种危险很可能导致了行人的抑郁症及其随后的身体症状（即严重的精神伤害），行人主张获得赔偿的最有力依据便是他当时处于危险区域内。\n\n（选项 A）根据 NIED 的“旁观者”理论（bystander theory），原告必须在目睹被告因过失导致其近亲属遭受严重身体伤害时，自身遭受严重精神痛苦，方可主张赔偿。\n\n在本案中，尽管行人目睹了司机撞倒那名女子，但该女子是陌生人，而非其近亲属。\n\n因此，行人无法依据该理论获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）“好撒玛利亚人”（Good Samaritan）概念涉及“救援原则”（rescue doctrine）；该原则规定，若被告将某人置于危险境地，且原告在试图救助该人时遭受身体伤害，则被告须为此承担责任。\n\n在本案中，行人的救援行为导致其遭受精神伤害，进而引发身体症状，而非直接遭受身体伤害。\n\n因此，他无法依据该原则获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）行人身上沾满该女子血迹这一事实，表明他曾上前救助该女子，但这并不意味着他曾处于危险区域之中。\n\n教育目标：根据“危险区域”理论，当 (1) 被告过失地使原告面临即时身体伤害的风险，且 (2) 该风险导致原告遭受严重精神伤害时，即产生过失造成精神痛苦（NIED）的责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 47 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（过失造成精神痛苦）。","conclusion":"答案 D：行人处于“危险区域”（zone of danger）内。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-900261402ca368a0"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":121,"sourceQuestionPage":361,"sourceQuestionPages":[361],"sourceExplanationPages":[362,363],"sourceAnswerPages":[362,363],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-122","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A bright 12-year-old child attended a day-care center after school. The day-care center was located near a man-made duck pond on the property of a corporation. During the winter, the pond was used for ice-skating when conditions were suitable. At a time when the pond was obviously only partially frozen, the child sneaked away from the center's property and walked out onto the ice over the pond. The ice gave way, and the child fell into the cold water. He suffered shock and would have drowned had he not been rescued by a passerby. At the time of the incident, the pond was clearly marked with numerous signs that stated, \"THIN ICE—KEEP OFF.\" When the child sneaked away from the day-care center, the center was staffed with a reasonable number of qualified employees, and the employees were exercising reasonable care to ensure that the children in their charge did not leave the premises. There had not been a previous instance of a child coming onto the corporation's property from the day-care center. The jurisdiction follows a rule of pure comparative negligence. In a suit brought on the child's behalf against the corporation and based only on the facts above, who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The child, because the corporation owes a duty to keep its premises free of dangerous conditions.","B":"The child, because the pond was an attractive nuisance.","C":"The corporation, because the danger of thin ice may reasonably be expected to be understood by a 12-year-old child.","D":"The corporation, because the day-care center had a duty to keep the child off the ice."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Attractive nuisance doctrine\n\n(land possessor's duty to child trespassers)\n\nDuty to protect child trespassers from artificial condition on land when:\n\ncondition exists where land possessor knows or should know children are likely to trespass land possessor knows or should know condition poses unreasonable risk of death or serious bodily harm to children children of trespasser's age cannot reasonably discover or appreciate risk and risk outweighs condition's utility and burden of eliminating risk Land possessors owe only limited duties to trespassers. But most jurisdictions apply a special rule to child trespassers since they lack the same experience and judgment as adults. This rule, known as the attractive nuisance doctrine, gives land possessors the duty to protect child trespassers from artificial (ie, man-made) conditions on the land when: the condition exists where the land possessor knows or should know that children are likely to trespass\n\nthe land possessor knows or should know that the condition poses an unreasonable risk of serious bodily harm or death to children\n\nchildren of the trespasser's age cannot reasonably discover or appreciate the risk and\n\nthe risk outweighs the condition's utility and the burden of eliminating the risk.\n\nA land possessor is liable for negligence if he/she breaches this duty (ie, fails to exercise reasonable care) and causes the child physical harm. Here, the 12-year-old child was injured when he trespassed on the corporation's property and fell through obviously thin ice on its man-made pond (artificial condition). But a 12- year-old child may reasonably be expected to understand the danger of thin ice—especially since the pond was clearly marked with numerous warning signs. As a result, the pond was not an attractive nuisance and the corporation will likely prevail (Choice B). (Choice A) The corporation owed no duty to the child since the attractive nuisance doctrine does not apply. But even if it did, that duty would be to exercise reasonable care to protect him from the artificial condition—not to keep the premises free of dangerous conditions. (Choice D) The day-care center's duty to protect the child (eg, by keeping him off the ice) would not negate the corporation's liability had the attractive nuisance doctrine applied.\n\nEducational objective: The attractive nuisance doctrine governs the duty of land possessors to protect child trespassers from artificial conditions on their land. But this doctrine does not apply when children of the trespasser's age can reasonably discover or appreciate the risk presented by the condition.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 339 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (attractive nuisance doctrine).","educationalObjective":"The attractive nuisance doctrine governs the duty of land possessors to protect child trespassers from artificial conditions on their land. But this doctrine does not apply when children of the trespasser's age can reasonably discover or appreciate the risk presented by the condition.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 339 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (attractive nuisance doctrine).","rule":"The attractive nuisance doctrine governs the duty of land possessors to protect child trespassers from artificial conditions on their land. But this doctrine does not apply when children of the trespasser's age can reasonably discover or appreciate the risk presented by the condition.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit brought on the child's behalf against the corporation and based only on the facts above, who is likely to prevail?","rule":"The attractive nuisance doctrine governs the duty of land possessors to protect child trespassers from artificial conditions on their land. But this doctrine does not apply when children of the trespasser's age can reasonably discover or appreciate the risk presented by the condition.","analysis":"Attractive nuisance doctrine\n\n(land possessor's duty to child trespassers)\n\nDuty to protect child trespassers from artificial condition on land when:\n\ncondition exists where land possessor knows or should know children are likely to trespass land possessor knows or should know condition poses unreasonable risk of death or serious bodily harm to children children of trespasser's age cannot reasonably discover or appreciate risk and risk outweighs condition's utility and burden of eliminating risk Land possessors owe only limited duties to trespassers. But most jurisdictions apply a special rule to child trespassers since they lack the same experience and judgment as adults. This rule, known as the attractive nuisance doctrine, gives land possessors the duty to protect child trespassers from artificial (ie, man-made) conditions on the land when: the condition exists where the land possessor knows or should know that children are likely to trespass\n\nthe land possessor knows or should know that the condition poses an unreasonable risk of serious bodily harm or death to children\n\nchildren of the trespasser's age cannot reasonably discover or appreciate the risk and\n\nthe risk outweighs the condition's utility and the burden of eliminating the risk.\n\nA land possessor is liable for negligence if he/she breaches this duty (ie, fails to exercise reasonable care) and causes the child physical harm. Here, the 12-year-old child was injured when he trespassed on the corporation's property and fell through obviously thin ice on its man-made pond (artificial condition). But a 12- year-old child may reasonably be expected to understand the danger of thin ice—especially since the pond was clearly marked with numerous warning signs. As a result, the pond was not an attractive nuisance and the corporation will likely prevail (Choice B). (Choice A) The corporation owed no duty to the child since the attractive nuisance doctrine does not apply. But even if it did, that duty would be to exercise reasonable care to protect him from the artificial condition—not to keep the premises free of dangerous conditions. (Choice D) The day-care center's duty to protect the child (eg, by keeping him off the ice) would not negate the corporation's liability had the attractive nuisance doctrine applied.","conclusion":"Answer C: The corporation, because the danger of thin ice may reasonably be expected to be understood by a 12-year-old child."},"questionZh":"一名聪明的12岁儿童在放学后前往一家日托中心。\n\n该日托中心位于某公司所属地块内的一处人工鸭池附近。\n\n冬季条件适宜时，该池塘会被用作滑冰场。\n\n在池塘明显仅部分结冰的情况下，该儿童偷偷溜出日托中心，走到了池塘的冰面上。\n\n冰面破裂，儿童落入冰冷的水中。\n\n他遭受了休克，若非被路人救起，恐已溺亡。\n\n事发时，池塘边设有多个醒目的警示牌，标明“冰层薄——禁止进入”。\n\n当该儿童偷偷溜出日托中心时，中心配备了数量合理的合格员工，且员工已尽到合理注意义务，确保受其照管的儿童不离开场所。\n\n此前从未发生过儿童从日托中心进入该公司所属地块的先例。\n\n该司法管辖区适用“纯粹比较过失”规则。\n\n若仅依据上述事实，代表该儿童向该公司提起诉讼，谁更有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"儿童胜诉，因为公司有义务确保其场所内不存在危险状况。","B":"儿童胜诉，因为该池塘属于“诱惑性危险物”（attractive nuisance）。","C":"公司胜诉，因为可以合理预期一名12岁的儿童能够理解冰层薄所带来的危险。","D":"公司胜诉，因为日托中心有义务阻止该儿童进入冰面。"},"explanationZh":"“诱人危险物”原则（Attractive Nuisance Doctrine） （土地占有者对儿童侵入者所负的义务） 当存在以下情况时，土地占有者有义务保护儿童侵入者免受土地上的人造状况（artificial condition）的伤害： 该状况存在于土地占有者明知或应知儿童可能侵入的区域；土地占有者明知或应知该状况对儿童构成死亡或严重身体伤害的不合理风险；处于该侵入儿童年龄段的儿童无法合理发现或意识到该风险；且该风险超过了该状况的效用以及消除该风险所需的负担。\n\n土地占有者对侵入者通常只负有限的义务。但大多数司法管辖区对儿童侵入者适用一项特殊规则，因为他们缺乏成年人所具备的经验和判断力。\n\n这项被称为“诱人危险物”原则的规则规定，在以下情况下，土地占有者有义务保护儿童侵入者免受土地上人造（即人为制造的）状况的伤害：该状况存在于土地占有者明知或应知儿童可能侵入的区域； 土地占有者明知或应知该状况对儿童构成严重身体伤害或死亡的不合理风险； 处于该侵入儿童年龄段的儿童无法合理发现或意识到该风险；以及 该风险超过了该状况的效用以及消除该风险所需的负担。\n\n如果土地占有者违反了这一义务（即未尽到合理的注意义务）并导致儿童身体受伤，则需承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，这名12岁儿童在侵入公司物业时，因跌入公司人造池塘（即人造状况）上明显很薄的冰层而受伤。\n\n然而，人们有理由期望一名12岁的儿童能够理解薄冰的危险——尤其是考虑到该池塘已清楚地竖立了许多警告标志。\n\n因此，该池塘不构成“诱人危险物”，公司很可能会胜诉（选项B）。\n\n（选项A）由于“诱人危险物”原则不适用，公司对该儿童不负有义务。\n\n但即使该原则适用，其义务也仅限于尽到合理的注意义务以保护其免受该人造状况的伤害，而非确保场所内完全不存在危险状况。\n\n（选项D）即使“诱人危险物”原则适用，日托中心保护儿童的义务（例如，阻止其踏上冰面）也不会免除公司的责任。\n\n教育目标：“诱惑性妨害”（attractive nuisance）原则规范了土地占有者的一项义务，即保护儿童侵入者免受其土地上的人造状况所带来的危险。\n\n然而，如果处于侵入者相应年龄段的儿童能够合理地发现或意识到该状况所构成的风险，则该原则不适用。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第339条（美国法律协会，1965年）（诱惑性妨害原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“诱惑性危险物”原则规范了土地占有人保护儿童侵入者免受其土地上人造危险状况伤害的义务。\n\n然而，当处于该侵入者年龄段的儿童能够合理发现或意识到该状况所带来的风险时，该原则不适用。","iracZh":{"issue":"若仅依据上述事实，代表该儿童向该公司提起诉讼，谁更有可能胜诉？","rule":"“诱惑性危险物”原则规范了土地占有人保护儿童侵入者免受其土地上人造危险状况伤害的义务。\n\n然而，当处于该侵入者年龄段的儿童能够合理发现或意识到该状况所带来的风险时，该原则不适用。","analysis":"“诱人危险物”原则（Attractive Nuisance Doctrine） （土地占有者对儿童侵入者所负的义务） 当存在以下情况时，土地占有者有义务保护儿童侵入者免受土地上的人造状况（artificial condition）的伤害： 该状况存在于土地占有者明知或应知儿童可能侵入的区域；土地占有者明知或应知该状况对儿童构成死亡或严重身体伤害的不合理风险；处于该侵入儿童年龄段的儿童无法合理发现或意识到该风险；且该风险超过了该状况的效用以及消除该风险所需的负担。\n\n土地占有者对侵入者通常只负有限的义务。但大多数司法管辖区对儿童侵入者适用一项特殊规则，因为他们缺乏成年人所具备的经验和判断力。\n\n这项被称为“诱人危险物”原则的规则规定，在以下情况下，土地占有者有义务保护儿童侵入者免受土地上人造（即人为制造的）状况的伤害：该状况存在于土地占有者明知或应知儿童可能侵入的区域； 土地占有者明知或应知该状况对儿童构成严重身体伤害或死亡的不合理风险； 处于该侵入儿童年龄段的儿童无法合理发现或意识到该风险；以及 该风险超过了该状况的效用以及消除该风险所需的负担。\n\n如果土地占有者违反了这一义务（即未尽到合理的注意义务）并导致儿童身体受伤，则需承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，这名12岁儿童在侵入公司物业时，因跌入公司人造池塘（即人造状况）上明显很薄的冰层而受伤。\n\n然而，人们有理由期望一名12岁的儿童能够理解薄冰的危险——尤其是考虑到该池塘已清楚地竖立了许多警告标志。\n\n因此，该池塘不构成“诱人危险物”，公司很可能会胜诉（选项B）。\n\n（选项A）由于“诱人危险物”原则不适用，公司对该儿童不负有义务。\n\n但即使该原则适用，其义务也仅限于尽到合理的注意义务以保护其免受该人造状况的伤害，而非确保场所内完全不存在危险状况。\n\n（选项D）即使“诱人危险物”原则适用，日托中心保护儿童的义务（例如，阻止其踏上冰面）也不会免除公司的责任。\n\n教育目标：“诱惑性妨害”（attractive nuisance）原则规范了土地占有者的一项义务，即保护儿童侵入者免受其土地上的人造状况所带来的危险。\n\n然而，如果处于侵入者相应年龄段的儿童能够合理地发现或意识到该状况所构成的风险，则该原则不适用。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第339条（美国法律协会，1965年）（诱惑性妨害原则）。","conclusion":"答案 C：公司胜诉，因为可以合理预期一名12岁的儿童能够理解冰层薄所带来的危险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":122,"sourceQuestionPage":364,"sourceQuestionPages":[364],"sourceExplanationPages":[365,366],"sourceAnswerPages":[365,366],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-123","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A patient who had suffered a severe fracture of her leg was treated by an orthopedist, who set the patient's leg and put it in a cast. When the leg continued to bother the patient six months later, she consulted a second orthopedist in the same town. The second orthopedist surgically inserted a pin to facilitate healing. The patient brought a malpractice action against the first orthopedist, claiming that he should have surgically inserted a pin at the time of initial treatment. The only evidence that the patient offered in support of her malpractice claim was the testimony of the second orthopedist, as follows: In response to the question \"Would you have inserted a pin initially?\" the second orthopedist testified, \"I personally would not have been satisfied that the leg would heal properly without a pin.\" At the close of the patient's evidence, the first orthopedist moved for judgment as a matter of law. Should the motion be granted?","choices":{"A":"No, because the patient has introduced evidence that the first orthopedist failed to give the care that the second orthopedist would have provided.","B":"No, because the second orthopedist practices in the same town and field of specialty as the first orthopedist.","C":"Yes, because the patient has failed to introduce evidence that the first orthopedist's care fell below the professional standard of care.","D":"Yes, because the second orthopedist also treated the patient and is thus not sufficiently objective to offer expert testimony."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Elements of professional malpractice\n\nDuty All professionals (eg, physicians, lawyers, nurses) owe clients professional standard of care\n\nBreach Professional failed to exercise same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Causation Breach actually & proximately caused plaintiff's harm\n\nDamages Plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, personal injury or property damage)\n\n*A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nAll professionals owe their clients a professional standard of care. For most professionals, this means that they must demonstrate the same knowledge, skill, and care as a normal member of the profession in a similar community. However, medical specialists (eg, orthopedists) are held to a national standard of care. Any professional whose conduct falls below the applicable standard of care and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for malpractice (a specialized negligence claim). Here, the first orthopedist set the patient's fractured leg and put it in a cast without inserting a pin. In the patient's malpractice action, a second orthopedist testified that he personally would not have been satisfied that the leg would heal without a pin. However, this testimony does not establish that the first orthopedist failed to provide the same level of care as normal orthopedists nationwide (Choice A). And since the patient has offered no other evidence to support her claim, the court should grant the first orthopedist's motion for judgment as a matter of law. (Choice B) The fact that both orthopedists practice in the same town and field shows that they are comparable professionals in similar communities—but a national standard of care applies here. And the second orthopedist's testimony as to his personal standard of care does not prove that the first orthopedist breached a national (or community) standard of care. (Choice D) The fact that the second orthopedist also treated the patient does not bar his expert testimony establishing breach of the applicable standard of care. However, his testimony failed to do so.\n\nEducational objective: Most professionals are required to demonstrate the same knowledge, skill, and care as a normal member of the profession in a similar community. But medical specialists (eg, orthopedists) are held to a national standard.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 299A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining the professional standard of care in malpractice cases).\n\nO'Neil v. Great Plains Women's Clinic, Inc., 759 F.2d 787, 789–91 (10th Cir. 1985) (applying a national standard of care in a malpractice action against a specialist).","educationalObjective":"Most professionals are required to demonstrate the same knowledge, skill, and care as a normal member of the profession in a similar community. But medical specialists (eg, orthopedists) are held to a national standard.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 299A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining the professional standard of care in malpractice cases).\n\nO'Neil v. Great Plains Women's Clinic, Inc., 759 F.2d 787, 789–91 (10th Cir. 1985) (applying a national standard of care in a malpractice action against a specialist).","rule":"Most professionals are required to demonstrate the same knowledge, skill, and care as a normal member of the profession in a similar community. But medical specialists (eg, orthopedists) are held to a national standard.","irac":{"issue":"Should the motion be granted?","rule":"Most professionals are required to demonstrate the same knowledge, skill, and care as a normal member of the profession in a similar community. But medical specialists (eg, orthopedists) are held to a national standard.","analysis":"Elements of professional malpractice\n\nDuty All professionals (eg, physicians, lawyers, nurses) owe clients professional standard of care\n\nBreach Professional failed to exercise same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Causation Breach actually & proximately caused plaintiff's harm\n\nDamages Plaintiff suffered physical harm (ie, personal injury or property damage)\n\n*A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nAll professionals owe their clients a professional standard of care. For most professionals, this means that they must demonstrate the same knowledge, skill, and care as a normal member of the profession in a similar community. However, medical specialists (eg, orthopedists) are held to a national standard of care. Any professional whose conduct falls below the applicable standard of care and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for malpractice (a specialized negligence claim). Here, the first orthopedist set the patient's fractured leg and put it in a cast without inserting a pin. In the patient's malpractice action, a second orthopedist testified that he personally would not have been satisfied that the leg would heal without a pin. However, this testimony does not establish that the first orthopedist failed to provide the same level of care as normal orthopedists nationwide (Choice A). And since the patient has offered no other evidence to support her claim, the court should grant the first orthopedist's motion for judgment as a matter of law. (Choice B) The fact that both orthopedists practice in the same town and field shows that they are comparable professionals in similar communities—but a national standard of care applies here. And the second orthopedist's testimony as to his personal standard of care does not prove that the first orthopedist breached a national (or community) standard of care. (Choice D) The fact that the second orthopedist also treated the patient does not bar his expert testimony establishing breach of the applicable standard of care. However, his testimony failed to do so.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the patient has failed to introduce evidence that the first orthopedist's care fell below the professional standard of care."},"questionZh":"一位腿部严重骨折的患者接受了骨科医生的治疗，医生为她复位并打了石膏。\n\n六个月后，患者的腿部仍然疼痛，于是她咨询了同一城镇的另一位骨科医生。\n\n第二位骨科医生通过手术植入了一根钢钉以促进愈合。\n\n患者对第一位骨科医生提起医疗事故诉讼，声称他本应在初次治疗时就进行钢钉植入。\n\n患者提供的唯一证据是第二位骨科医生的证词，如下：当被问及“您会在最初就植入钢钉吗？”时，第二位骨科医生作证说：“我个人认为，如果没有钢钉，腿部无法正常愈合。”在患者举证完毕后，第一位骨科医生提出法律判决动议。\n\n该动议是否应予准许？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为患者已提供证据证明第一位骨科医生未能提供第二位骨科医生本应提供的医疗服务。","B":"否，因为第二位骨科医生与第一位骨科医生在同一城镇执业，且专业领域相同。","C":"是，因为患者未能提供证据证明第一位骨科医生的医疗服务低于专业标准。","D":"是，因为第二位骨科医生也曾为该患者诊治，因此其客观性不足以提供专家证词。"},"explanationZh":"专业执业不当行为（医疗事故等）的构成要素 注意义务：所有专业人员（如医生、律师、护士）均对客户负有专业护理标准（或称执业标准）的义务。 违反义务：专业人员未能展现出与同类社区中其他专业人员相当的知识、技能和谨慎程度。 适用标准：医学专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。 因果关系：违反义务的行为实际导致并直接导致了原告受到的损害。 损害后果：原告遭受了实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。\n\n*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性护理标准。 所有专业人员都对客户负有专业护理标准的义务。\n\n对于大多数专业人员而言，这意味着他们必须展现出与同类社区中该行业普通从业者相当的知识、技能和谨慎程度。\n\n然而，医学专科医生（如骨科医生）适用全国性护理标准。\n\n任何专业人员，若其行为低于适用的护理标准并导致原告遭受实质性损害，即构成执业不当行为（一种特殊的过失索赔）。\n\n在本案中，第一位骨科医生在处理患者腿部骨折时，进行了复位并打上石膏，但未植入固定钢针。\n\n在患者提起的执业不当行为诉讼中，第二位骨科医生作证称，若不植入钢针，他个人无法确信腿部能愈合。\n\n然而，该证词并不能证明第一位骨科医生未能达到全国范围内普通骨科医生的护理水平（选项 A）。\n\n鉴于患者未提供其他证据支持其主张，法院应支持第一位骨科医生提出的“法律问题判决”动议（即无需陪审团审议直接判决）（选项 B）。\n\n尽管两位骨科医生在同一城镇执业且属于同一专业领域，表明他们属于同类社区中的可比专业人员，但本案适用的是全国性护理标准。\n\n此外，第二位骨科医生关于其个人护理标准的证词，并不能证明第一位骨科医生违反了全国性（或社区性）护理标准（选项 D）。\n\n第二位骨科医生曾治疗过该患者这一事实，并不妨碍他作为专家证人就违反适用护理标准的问题作证；然而，他的证词未能证实这一点。\n\n教育目标：大多数专业人员被要求展现出与同类社区中该行业普通从业者相当的知识、技能和谨慎程度。\n\n但医学专科医生（如骨科医生）适用全国性标准。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第299A条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐述了医疗事故案件中专业人员的注意义务标准）。\n\n*O'Neil v. Great Plains Women's Clinic, Inc.*, 759 F.2d 787, 789–91 (10th Cir. 1985)（在针对专科医生的医疗事故诉讼中适用全国性注意义务标准）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"大多数专业人士只需展现与同行业其他专业人士在类似社区中相同的知识、技能和专业素养。\n\n但医学专家（例如骨科医生）则需遵守国家标准。","iracZh":{"issue":"该动议是否应予批准？","rule":"大多数专业人士只需展现与同行业其他专业人士在类似社区中相同的知识、技能和专业素养。\n\n但医学专家（例如骨科医生）则需遵守国家标准。","analysis":"专业执业不当行为（医疗事故等）的构成要素 注意义务：所有专业人员（如医生、律师、护士）均对客户负有专业护理标准（或称执业标准）的义务。 违反义务：专业人员未能展现出与同类社区中其他专业人员相当的知识、技能和谨慎程度。 适用标准：医学专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。 因果关系：违反义务的行为实际导致并直接导致了原告受到的损害。 损害后果：原告遭受了实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）。\n\n*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性护理标准。 所有专业人员都对客户负有专业护理标准的义务。\n\n对于大多数专业人员而言，这意味着他们必须展现出与同类社区中该行业普通从业者相当的知识、技能和谨慎程度。\n\n然而，医学专科医生（如骨科医生）适用全国性护理标准。\n\n任何专业人员，若其行为低于适用的护理标准并导致原告遭受实质性损害，即构成执业不当行为（一种特殊的过失索赔）。\n\n在本案中，第一位骨科医生在处理患者腿部骨折时，进行了复位并打上石膏，但未植入固定钢针。\n\n在患者提起的执业不当行为诉讼中，第二位骨科医生作证称，若不植入钢针，他个人无法确信腿部能愈合。\n\n然而，该证词并不能证明第一位骨科医生未能达到全国范围内普通骨科医生的护理水平（选项 A）。\n\n鉴于患者未提供其他证据支持其主张，法院应支持第一位骨科医生提出的“法律问题判决”动议（即无需陪审团审议直接判决）（选项 B）。\n\n尽管两位骨科医生在同一城镇执业且属于同一专业领域，表明他们属于同类社区中的可比专业人员，但本案适用的是全国性护理标准。\n\n此外，第二位骨科医生关于其个人护理标准的证词，并不能证明第一位骨科医生违反了全国性（或社区性）护理标准（选项 D）。\n\n第二位骨科医生曾治疗过该患者这一事实，并不妨碍他作为专家证人就违反适用护理标准的问题作证；然而，他的证词未能证实这一点。\n\n教育目标：大多数专业人员被要求展现出与同类社区中该行业普通从业者相当的知识、技能和谨慎程度。\n\n但医学专科医生（如骨科医生）适用全国性标准。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第299A条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐述了医疗事故案件中专业人员的注意义务标准）。\n\n*O'Neil v. Great Plains Women's Clinic, Inc.*, 759 F.2d 787, 789–91 (10th Cir. 1985)（在针对专科医生的医疗事故诉讼中适用全国性注意义务标准）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是，因为患者未能提供证据证明第一位骨科医生的医疗服务低于专业标准。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":123,"sourceQuestionPage":367,"sourceQuestionPages":[367],"sourceExplanationPages":[368,369],"sourceAnswerPages":[368,369],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-124","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A socialite attended a cocktail party where the host provided large amounts of alcohol to the guests. The host noticed that the socialite had become visibly intoxicated during the party but did not prevent her from consuming more alcohol. After the party ended, the socialite drove to a bar, where the bartender initially refused to serve her because she was exhibiting outward signs of intoxication. However, the socialite eventually persuaded the bartender to serve her one last alcoholic drink. After consuming her drink, the socialite left the bar and drove home. On her way home, the socialite accidentally struck and seriously injured a pedestrian. The pedestrian sued the host of the cocktail party for all of his injuries. The jurisdiction has enacted a dram-shop law that imposes negligence liability on sellers of intoxicating beverages, but otherwise the jurisdiction follows the common law. Is the pedestrian likely to prevail in his action?","choices":{"A":"No, because the bar's subsequent tortious conduct was a superseding cause that cut off the host's liability.","B":"No, because the host of the cocktail party is not liable for serving alcohol to the socialite, despite the socialite's visible intoxication.","C":"Yes, because as a social host, the host of the cocktail party is liable under the jurisdiction's dram-shop law.","D":"Yes, because the pedestrian's injuries were proximately caused by the socialite's state of intoxication."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Vicarious liability\n\n(liability for tortious acts of another)\n\nRespondeat Employee's conduct occurred within scope of employment—ie, under superior employer's control or while performing assigned work\n\nNondelegable Independent contractor's work involved abnormally or inherently duty dangerous activity, condition on land open to general public, or activity regulated by law\n\nParent Child committed tort while acting as parent's agent\n\nBusiness partner Business partner's conduct occurred within scope of business\n\nAutomobile Family member drove car with permission (family-car doctrine) or owner any person drove car with permission under owner-liability statute\n\nDram shop* & Alcohol sold (dram shop) or served at noncommercial setting (social social host liability) to visibly intoxicated person or minor\n\n*Only applies when question provides dram-shop statute.\n\nMany jurisdictions have enacted dram-shop laws, which allow a plaintiff injured as a result of another's intoxication to sue the business (eg, bar, restaurant) that provided the alcohol to the intoxicated person.* To sustain a cause of action under a dram-shop law, the plaintiff generally must prove the following: The business sold alcohol to a minor or someone who was visibly intoxicated at the time of the sale. The plaintiff's injuries were a proximate result (foreseeable consequence) of that person's intoxication. Several jurisdictions have also extended liability to social hosts who serve alcohol to visibly intoxicated guests. But social-host liability is not recognized by the common law, which considers the plaintiff's consumption—not the host's provision—of alcohol to be the proximate cause of the plaintiff's injury. Here, the host of the cocktail party continued to serve the socialite alcohol despite her visible intoxication. But the host is not liable for the pedestrian's injuries because (1) the host is not a commercial seller subject to the jurisdiction's dram-shop law and (2) the jurisdiction otherwise follows the common law, which does not recognize social-host liability (Choice C). Therefore, the pedestrian is not likely to prevail in his action against the host.\n\n*Dram-shop laws exist to circumvent the common-law rule that sellers of alcohol are not liable for injuries resulting from a buyer's intoxication. (Choice A) Had the host been liable for the socialite's conduct, the bar's subsequent tortious conduct—serving the socialite despite her visible intoxication—likely would not have been a superseding cause. That is because subsequent tortious conduct (other than intentional tortious conduct) is considered foreseeable. (Choice D) The pedestrian's injuries likely were proximately caused by (ie, were a foreseeable consequence of) the socialite's intoxication, and the pedestrian could likely prevail in an action against the socialite. But since this jurisdiction does not recognize social-host liability, the pedestrian cannot prevail in his action against the host.\n\nEducational objective: In a dram-shop action, the plaintiff generally must prove that (1) a business sold alcohol to a minor or someone who was visibly intoxicated at the time and (2) the plaintiff's injuries were a proximate result of that person's intoxication. Some jurisdictions—but not the common law—extend liability to social hosts who serve visibly intoxicated guests.\n\nReferences\n45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 443 (2020) (common law on providing alcohol to social guests).\n\n45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 455 (2020) (elements of dram-shop action).\n\n45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 468 (2020) (proximate cause in dram-shop action).","educationalObjective":"In a dram-shop action, the plaintiff generally must prove that (1) a business sold alcohol to a minor or someone who was visibly intoxicated at the time and (2) the plaintiff's injuries were a proximate result of that person's intoxication. Some jurisdictions—but not the common law—extend liability to social hosts who serve visibly intoxicated guests.","references":"45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 443 (2020) (common law on providing alcohol to social guests).\n\n45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 455 (2020) (elements of dram-shop action).\n\n45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 468 (2020) (proximate cause in dram-shop action).","rule":"In a dram-shop action, the plaintiff generally must prove that (1) a business sold alcohol to a minor or someone who was visibly intoxicated at the time and (2) the plaintiff's injuries were a proximate result of that person's intoxication. Some jurisdictions—but not the common law—extend liability to social hosts who serve visibly intoxicated guests.","irac":{"issue":"Is the pedestrian likely to prevail in his action?","rule":"In a dram-shop action, the plaintiff generally must prove that (1) a business sold alcohol to a minor or someone who was visibly intoxicated at the time and (2) the plaintiff's injuries were a proximate result of that person's intoxication. Some jurisdictions—but not the common law—extend liability to social hosts who serve visibly intoxicated guests.","analysis":"Vicarious liability\n\n(liability for tortious acts of another)\n\nRespondeat Employee's conduct occurred within scope of employment—ie, under superior employer's control or while performing assigned work\n\nNondelegable Independent contractor's work involved abnormally or inherently duty dangerous activity, condition on land open to general public, or activity regulated by law\n\nParent Child committed tort while acting as parent's agent\n\nBusiness partner Business partner's conduct occurred within scope of business\n\nAutomobile Family member drove car with permission (family-car doctrine) or owner any person drove car with permission under owner-liability statute\n\nDram shop* & Alcohol sold (dram shop) or served at noncommercial setting (social social host liability) to visibly intoxicated person or minor\n\n*Only applies when question provides dram-shop statute.\n\nMany jurisdictions have enacted dram-shop laws, which allow a plaintiff injured as a result of another's intoxication to sue the business (eg, bar, restaurant) that provided the alcohol to the intoxicated person.* To sustain a cause of action under a dram-shop law, the plaintiff generally must prove the following: The business sold alcohol to a minor or someone who was visibly intoxicated at the time of the sale. The plaintiff's injuries were a proximate result (foreseeable consequence) of that person's intoxication. Several jurisdictions have also extended liability to social hosts who serve alcohol to visibly intoxicated guests. But social-host liability is not recognized by the common law, which considers the plaintiff's consumption—not the host's provision—of alcohol to be the proximate cause of the plaintiff's injury. Here, the host of the cocktail party continued to serve the socialite alcohol despite her visible intoxication. But the host is not liable for the pedestrian's injuries because (1) the host is not a commercial seller subject to the jurisdiction's dram-shop law and (2) the jurisdiction otherwise follows the common law, which does not recognize social-host liability (Choice C). Therefore, the pedestrian is not likely to prevail in his action against the host.\n\n*Dram-shop laws exist to circumvent the common-law rule that sellers of alcohol are not liable for injuries resulting from a buyer's intoxication. (Choice A) Had the host been liable for the socialite's conduct, the bar's subsequent tortious conduct—serving the socialite despite her visible intoxication—likely would not have been a superseding cause. That is because subsequent tortious conduct (other than intentional tortious conduct) is considered foreseeable. (Choice D) The pedestrian's injuries likely were proximately caused by (ie, were a foreseeable consequence of) the socialite's intoxication, and the pedestrian could likely prevail in an action against the socialite. But since this jurisdiction does not recognize social-host liability, the pedestrian cannot prevail in his action against the host.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the host of the cocktail party is not liable for serving alcohol to the socialite, despite the socialite's visible intoxication."},"questionZh":"一位社交名流参加了一场鸡尾酒会，主人向宾客提供了大量酒精饮料。\n\n主人注意到该名流在聚会期间已明显醉酒，但并未阻止她继续饮酒。\n\n聚会结束后，该名流驾车前往一家酒吧；尽管她表现出明显的醉酒迹象，酒吧调酒师起初拒绝向她供酒，但她最终说服调酒师为她提供了最后一杯含酒精饮料。\n\n饮酒后，该名流离开酒吧驾车回家。\n\n在回家途中，她不慎撞倒一名行人并致其重伤。\n\n该行人起诉鸡尾酒会的主人，要求赔偿其所有伤害损失。\n\n该司法管辖区已制定“酒馆责任法”（dram-shop law），规定向他人出售含酒精饮料的商家须承担过失责任；除此规定外，该司法管辖区遵循普通法原则。\n\n该行人胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为酒吧随后的侵权行为构成了一种阻断因果关系的“介入原因”（superseding cause），从而切断了主人的责任。","B":"不大，因为尽管该名流已明显醉酒，但鸡尾酒会的主人并不因向其提供酒精饮料而承担责任。","C":"大，因为作为社交聚会的主人，该鸡尾酒会的主人须根据该司法管辖区的“酒馆责任法”承担责任。","D":"大，因为行人的受伤是该名流醉酒状态的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（proximate cause）所致。"},"explanationZh":"替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability） （对他人的侵权行为承担责任） 雇主责任（Respondeat Superior） 雇员的行为发生在雇佣范围内——即受雇主控制或在执行指派工作期间 不可转授的义务 独立承包商的工作涉及异常危险或固有危险的活动、向公众开放的土地上的状况，或受法律监管的活动 父母责任 子女作为父母的代理人行事时实施了侵权行为 商业合伙人责任 合伙人的行为发生在业务范围内 汽车责任 家庭成员经许可驾驶汽车（家庭用车原则），或车主责任法规下经车主许可驾驶汽车的任何人 酒馆责任（Dram shop）* 与社交东道主责任 向明显醉酒者或未成年人出售（酒馆责任）或提供（社交东道主责任）酒精饮料 *仅适用于题目中提供了酒馆责任法规的情况。\n\n许多司法管辖区制定了酒馆责任法（dram-shop laws），允许因他人醉酒而受伤的原告起诉向该醉酒者提供酒精饮料的企业（如酒吧、餐馆）。* 要依据酒馆责任法确立诉因，原告通常必须证明以下几点：该企业向未成年人或在销售时已明显醉酒的人出售了酒精饮料；原告的伤害是该人醉酒的直接结果（可预见的后果）。\n\n一些司法管辖区还将责任范围扩大到了向明显醉酒的客人提供酒精饮料的社交东道主（social hosts）。\n\n但普通法并不承认社交东道主责任，因为普通法认为原告对酒精的“消费”——而非东道主的“提供”——才是导致原告受伤的直接原因。\n\n在本案中，鸡尾酒会的主办方尽管看到那位名媛已明显醉酒，仍继续向其提供酒精饮料。\n\n但主办方不对行人的伤害承担责任，因为：(1) 主办方并非受该司法管辖区酒馆责任法管辖的商业销售商；(2) 该司法管辖区遵循普通法原则，即不承认社交东道主责任（选项 C）。\n\n因此，行人针对主办方提起的诉讼不太可能胜诉。\n\n*制定酒馆责任法是为了规避普通法中的一项规则，即酒精销售商不对因买方醉酒而造成的伤害承担责任。\n\n（选项 A）如果聚会主人需对该名社交名流的行为承担责任，那么酒吧随后的侵权行为——即在明知该名流已明显醉酒的情况下仍向其提供酒水——很可能不会构成“阻断因果关系的中断事由”（superseding cause）。\n\n这是因为随后的侵权行为（故意侵权行为除外）通常被视为可预见的。\n\n（选项 D）行人的受伤很可能是由该名流的醉酒状态直接导致的（即醉酒的可预见后果），因此该行人若起诉该名流，很可能会胜诉。\n\n然而，由于该司法管辖区不承认“社交聚会主人责任”（social-host liability），该行人无法在针对聚会主人的诉讼中胜诉。\n\n教学目标：在涉及酒类销售责任（dram-shop action）的诉讼中，原告通常必须证明：(1) 商家向未成年人或当时已明显醉酒的人员出售了酒水；以及 (2) 原告的伤害是该人员醉酒状态的直接后果。\n\n某些司法管辖区（普通法原则除外）将责任范围扩大到了向明显醉酒的宾客提供酒水的社交聚会主人。\n\n参考文献 45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 443 (2020)（关于向社交聚会宾客提供酒水的普通法原则）。\n\n45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 455 (2020)（酒类销售责任诉讼的构成要件）。\n\n45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 468 (2020)（酒馆责任诉讼中的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在涉及“酒馆责任法”的诉讼中，原告通常必须证明：(1) 商业经营者向未成年人或当时已明显醉酒的人出售了酒精饮料；以及 (2) 原告的伤害是该人醉酒状态的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）结果。\n\n某些司法管辖区（但普通法不包括此类规定）将责任范围扩大到了向明显醉酒的宾客提供酒精饮料的社交聚会主人。","iracZh":{"issue":"该行人胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"在涉及“酒馆责任法”的诉讼中，原告通常必须证明：(1) 商业经营者向未成年人或当时已明显醉酒的人出售了酒精饮料；以及 (2) 原告的伤害是该人醉酒状态的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）结果。\n\n某些司法管辖区（但普通法不包括此类规定）将责任范围扩大到了向明显醉酒的宾客提供酒精饮料的社交聚会主人。","analysis":"替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability） （对他人的侵权行为承担责任） 雇主责任（Respondeat Superior） 雇员的行为发生在雇佣范围内——即受雇主控制或在执行指派工作期间 不可转授的义务 独立承包商的工作涉及异常危险或固有危险的活动、向公众开放的土地上的状况，或受法律监管的活动 父母责任 子女作为父母的代理人行事时实施了侵权行为 商业合伙人责任 合伙人的行为发生在业务范围内 汽车责任 家庭成员经许可驾驶汽车（家庭用车原则），或车主责任法规下经车主许可驾驶汽车的任何人 酒馆责任（Dram shop）* 与社交东道主责任 向明显醉酒者或未成年人出售（酒馆责任）或提供（社交东道主责任）酒精饮料 *仅适用于题目中提供了酒馆责任法规的情况。\n\n许多司法管辖区制定了酒馆责任法（dram-shop laws），允许因他人醉酒而受伤的原告起诉向该醉酒者提供酒精饮料的企业（如酒吧、餐馆）。* 要依据酒馆责任法确立诉因，原告通常必须证明以下几点：该企业向未成年人或在销售时已明显醉酒的人出售了酒精饮料；原告的伤害是该人醉酒的直接结果（可预见的后果）。\n\n一些司法管辖区还将责任范围扩大到了向明显醉酒的客人提供酒精饮料的社交东道主（social hosts）。\n\n但普通法并不承认社交东道主责任，因为普通法认为原告对酒精的“消费”——而非东道主的“提供”——才是导致原告受伤的直接原因。\n\n在本案中，鸡尾酒会的主办方尽管看到那位名媛已明显醉酒，仍继续向其提供酒精饮料。\n\n但主办方不对行人的伤害承担责任，因为：(1) 主办方并非受该司法管辖区酒馆责任法管辖的商业销售商；(2) 该司法管辖区遵循普通法原则，即不承认社交东道主责任（选项 C）。\n\n因此，行人针对主办方提起的诉讼不太可能胜诉。\n\n*制定酒馆责任法是为了规避普通法中的一项规则，即酒精销售商不对因买方醉酒而造成的伤害承担责任。\n\n（选项 A）如果聚会主人需对该名社交名流的行为承担责任，那么酒吧随后的侵权行为——即在明知该名流已明显醉酒的情况下仍向其提供酒水——很可能不会构成“阻断因果关系的中断事由”（superseding cause）。\n\n这是因为随后的侵权行为（故意侵权行为除外）通常被视为可预见的。\n\n（选项 D）行人的受伤很可能是由该名流的醉酒状态直接导致的（即醉酒的可预见后果），因此该行人若起诉该名流，很可能会胜诉。\n\n然而，由于该司法管辖区不承认“社交聚会主人责任”（social-host liability），该行人无法在针对聚会主人的诉讼中胜诉。\n\n教学目标：在涉及酒类销售责任（dram-shop action）的诉讼中，原告通常必须证明：(1) 商家向未成年人或当时已明显醉酒的人员出售了酒水；以及 (2) 原告的伤害是该人员醉酒状态的直接后果。\n\n某些司法管辖区（普通法原则除外）将责任范围扩大到了向明显醉酒的宾客提供酒水的社交聚会主人。\n\n参考文献 45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 443 (2020)（关于向社交聚会宾客提供酒水的普通法原则）。\n\n45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 455 (2020)（酒类销售责任诉讼的构成要件）。\n\n45 Am. Jur. 2d Intoxicating Liquors § 468 (2020)（酒馆责任诉讼中的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause））。","conclusion":"答案 B：不大，因为尽管该名流已明显醉酒，但鸡尾酒会的主人并不因向其提供酒精饮料而承担责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":124,"sourceQuestionPage":370,"sourceQuestionPages":[370],"sourceExplanationPages":[371,372],"sourceAnswerPages":[371,372],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-125","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A security guard, dressed in plain clothes, was working for a discount store when a customer got into a heated argument with a cashier over the store's refund policy. Without identifying himself as a security guard, the security guard suddenly grabbed the customer's arm. The customer attempted to push the security guard away, and the security guard knocked the customer to the floor, causing him injuries. The customer sued the store for battery on a theory of vicarious liability for the injuries caused by the security guard. The store filed an answer to the customer's complaint, asserting the affirmative defense of contributory negligence. The customer has moved to strike the affirmative defense. Traditional rules of contributory negligence apply. Should the trial court grant the customer's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because contributory negligence is an affirmative defense to a cause of action based on vicarious liability.","B":"No, because the customer should have known that his argument with the cashier might provoke an action by a security guard.","C":"Yes, because contributory negligence is not a defense to battery.","D":"Yes, because the customer did not know that he was pushing away someone who was employed as a security guard."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Contributory negligence is an affirmative defense that completely bars the plaintiff's recovery when his/her conduct falls below the standard of care persons should use for their own protection (ie, the plaintiff's own negligence contributed to the harm). However, under the traditional rules of contributory negligence, contributory negligence serves only as a defense to negligence claims—not intentional tort claims like battery. Since the customer sued the store for battery—not negligence—contributory negligence is not a defense. Therefore, the court should grant the customer's motion to strike that defense. (Choice A) A principal can only assert contributory negligence as a defense to a negligence cause of action based on a theory of vicarious liability. Here, the customer alleged that the store was vicariously liable for the guard's battery. Because the guard could not assert contributory negligence as a defense to battery, neither can the store. (Choices B & D) If the store could assert the defense of contributory negligence, then the court would consider whether the customer should have known that a heated argument with the cashier might provoke the guard or did not know that he was pushing away a guard. But since contributory negligence is not a defense to battery, these factual issues do not matter.\n\nEducational objective: Under the traditional rules of contributory negligence, contributory negligence completely bars the plaintiff's recovery when his/her own negligence was a contributing cause of the harm. This affirmative defense only applies to negligence claims.\n\nReferences\nFed. R. Civ. P. 12(f) (motion to strike).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 463 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of contributory negligence).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 467 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (effect of contributory negligence).","educationalObjective":"Under the traditional rules of contributory negligence, contributory negligence completely bars the plaintiff's recovery when his/her own negligence was a contributing cause of the harm. This affirmative defense only applies to negligence claims.","references":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(f) (motion to strike).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 463 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (definition of contributory negligence).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 467 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (effect of contributory negligence).","rule":"Under the traditional rules of contributory negligence, contributory negligence completely bars the plaintiff's recovery when his/her own negligence was a contributing cause of the harm. This affirmative defense only applies to negligence claims.","irac":{"issue":"Should the trial court grant the customer's motion?","rule":"Under the traditional rules of contributory negligence, contributory negligence completely bars the plaintiff's recovery when his/her own negligence was a contributing cause of the harm. This affirmative defense only applies to negligence claims.","analysis":"Contributory negligence is an affirmative defense that completely bars the plaintiff's recovery when his/her conduct falls below the standard of care persons should use for their own protection (ie, the plaintiff's own negligence contributed to the harm). However, under the traditional rules of contributory negligence, contributory negligence serves only as a defense to negligence claims—not intentional tort claims like battery. Since the customer sued the store for battery—not negligence—contributory negligence is not a defense. Therefore, the court should grant the customer's motion to strike that defense. (Choice A) A principal can only assert contributory negligence as a defense to a negligence cause of action based on a theory of vicarious liability. Here, the customer alleged that the store was vicariously liable for the guard's battery. Because the guard could not assert contributory negligence as a defense to battery, neither can the store. (Choices B & D) If the store could assert the defense of contributory negligence, then the court would consider whether the customer should have known that a heated argument with the cashier might provoke the guard or did not know that he was pushing away a guard. But since contributory negligence is not a defense to battery, these factual issues do not matter.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because contributory negligence is not a defense to battery."},"questionZh":"一名身穿便服的保安在一家折扣店工作时，一位顾客因退款政策与收银员发生了激烈争执。\n\n该保安在未表明身份的情况下，突然抓住了顾客的手臂。\n\n顾客试图推开保安，保安随即将顾客推倒在地，导致其受伤。\n\n顾客以“替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）”（vicarious liability）为由，起诉该店对其遭受的伤害承担责任，指控该店构成故意侵害身体（battery）（battery）侵权。\n\n商店提交了答辩状，主张“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）这一积极抗辩。\n\n顾客已提出动议，要求驳回该积极抗辩。\n\n本案适用传统的与有过失（contributory negligence）规则。\n\n初审法院是否应批准顾客的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应批准，因为与有过失（contributory negligence）是针对基于替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）的诉因的一种积极抗辩。","B":"不应批准，因为顾客本应预见到他与收银员的争执可能会引发保安采取行动。","C":"应予批准，因为与有过失（contributory negligence）不能作为故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的抗辩理由。","D":"应予批准，因为顾客当时并不知道他推开的人是一名受雇的保安。"},"explanationZh":"“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）是一种肯定性抗辩（affirmative defense），当原告的行为未达到人们为保护自身安全应有的注意标准时（即原告自身的过失促成了损害的发生），该抗辩将完全阻却原告获得赔偿。\n\n然而，根据与有过失（contributory negligence）的传统规则，与有过失（contributory negligence）仅适用于针对过失侵权（negligence claims）的抗辩，而不适用于针对故意侵权（如故意侵害身体（battery）/battery）的抗辩。\n\n由于该顾客起诉商店的理由是故意侵害身体（battery）而非过失，因此与有过失（contributory negligence）不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n故法院应准许顾客提出的驳回该抗辩的动议。\n\n（选项 A）委托人（或雇主）仅在基于替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）理论的过失诉因中，才能主张与有过失（contributory negligence）作为抗辩。\n\n本案中，顾客主张商店应对保安实施的故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n既然保安本人不能以与有过失（contributory negligence）作为针对故意侵害身体（battery）指控的抗辩，商店也就不能主张该抗辩。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）如果商店能够主张与有过失（contributory negligence）抗辩，法院则会考量顾客是否应当预见到与收银员的激烈争执可能会激怒保安，或者考量顾客是否未意识到自己正在推开一名保安。\n\n但鉴于与有过失（contributory negligence）不能作为故意侵害身体（battery）指控的抗辩，这些事实问题便无关紧要了。\n\n教育目标：根据与有过失（contributory negligence）的传统规则，当原告自身的过失促成了损害发生时，与有过失（contributory negligence）将完全阻却原告获得赔偿。\n\n这种肯定性抗辩仅适用于过失侵权诉讼。\n\n参考文献 《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 12(f) 条（关于驳回抗辩的动议）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 463 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（与有过失（contributory negligence）的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 467 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（与有过失（contributory negligence）的法律后果）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据传统的与有过失（contributory negligence）规则，如果原告自身的过失是导致损害的原因之一，则与有过失（contributory negligence）将完全阻却原告获得赔偿。\n\n该积极抗辩仅适用于过失侵权诉讼。","iracZh":{"issue":"初审法院是否应批准顾客的动议？","rule":"根据传统的与有过失（contributory negligence）规则，如果原告自身的过失是导致损害的原因之一，则与有过失（contributory negligence）将完全阻却原告获得赔偿。\n\n该积极抗辩仅适用于过失侵权诉讼。","analysis":"“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）是一种肯定性抗辩（affirmative defense），当原告的行为未达到人们为保护自身安全应有的注意标准时（即原告自身的过失促成了损害的发生），该抗辩将完全阻却原告获得赔偿。\n\n然而，根据与有过失（contributory negligence）的传统规则，与有过失（contributory negligence）仅适用于针对过失侵权（negligence claims）的抗辩，而不适用于针对故意侵权（如故意侵害身体（battery）/battery）的抗辩。\n\n由于该顾客起诉商店的理由是故意侵害身体（battery）而非过失，因此与有过失（contributory negligence）不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n故法院应准许顾客提出的驳回该抗辩的动议。\n\n（选项 A）委托人（或雇主）仅在基于替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）理论的过失诉因中，才能主张与有过失（contributory negligence）作为抗辩。\n\n本案中，顾客主张商店应对保安实施的故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n既然保安本人不能以与有过失（contributory negligence）作为针对故意侵害身体（battery）指控的抗辩，商店也就不能主张该抗辩。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）如果商店能够主张与有过失（contributory negligence）抗辩，法院则会考量顾客是否应当预见到与收银员的激烈争执可能会激怒保安，或者考量顾客是否未意识到自己正在推开一名保安。\n\n但鉴于与有过失（contributory negligence）不能作为故意侵害身体（battery）指控的抗辩，这些事实问题便无关紧要了。\n\n教育目标：根据与有过失（contributory negligence）的传统规则，当原告自身的过失促成了损害发生时，与有过失（contributory negligence）将完全阻却原告获得赔偿。\n\n这种肯定性抗辩仅适用于过失侵权诉讼。\n\n参考文献 《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 12(f) 条（关于驳回抗辩的动议）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 463 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（与有过失（contributory negligence）的定义）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 467 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（与有过失（contributory negligence）的法律后果）。","conclusion":"答案 C：应予批准，因为与有过失（contributory negligence）不能作为故意侵害身体（battery）侵权的抗辩理由。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-8a62ab4dc8b72d25"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":125,"sourceQuestionPage":373,"sourceQuestionPages":[373],"sourceExplanationPages":[374,375],"sourceAnswerPages":[374,375],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-126","subject":"Torts","topic":"Actual & Proximate Cause","question":"A pedestrian was injured when hit by a chair that was thrown from an upper-story hotel window. The pedestrian sued the occupants of all the rooms from which the chair might have been thrown. At trial, the pedestrian has been unable to offer any evidence as to the exact room from which the chair was thrown. The defendants have filed a motion for a directed verdict. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because it is unreasonable to expect the pedestrian to prove which of the defendants caused the harm.","B":"No, because of the doctrine of alternative liability.","C":"Yes, because a plaintiff always has the burden to prove that a particular defendant's conduct was the factual cause of the plaintiff's physical harm.","D":"Yes, because the pedestrian has failed to offer evidence that the defendants jointly engaged in tortious conduct."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Liability of multiple negligent actors\n\nIndivisible injuries Separable injuries\n\nActing independently Joint & several liability* Several liability only\n\nActing in concert Joint & several liability\n\n*Default rule on MBE.\n\nTort claims for personal injury generally require proof that a particular defendant's tortious conduct was the actual and proximate cause of the plaintiff's harm. But when two or more defendants jointly engage in tortious conduct (ie, act in concert)—and at least one of them causes the plaintiff's harm—all of the defendants are jointly and severally liable for the harm (Choice C). Here, the pedestrian was injured by a chair that was thrown from a hotel window. She then sued the occupants of all the rooms from which it might have been thrown. Since she offered no evidence as to the exact room from which the chair was thrown, she cannot prove which defendant(s) caused her injury. And since she also offered no evidence of joint tortious conduct, the defendants' motion for a directed verdict should be granted. (Choices A & B) The doctrine of alternative liability applies when (1) there were multiple tortfeasors, (2) at least one of them caused the plaintiff's harm, and (3) it is impossible to tell which one of them did. The burden then shifts to each defendant to prove that he/she did not cause the harm. Here, it may be unreasonable to expect the pedestrian to prove which defendant caused her harm. But alternative liability only applies if each defendant's tortious conduct is proved (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: When multiple defendants engage in joint tortious conduct and one or more of them causes the plaintiff's harm, all of the defendants are jointly and severally liable for the harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 876 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a defendant is subject to liability if he/she does a tortious act in concert with others or pursuant to a common design).","educationalObjective":"When multiple defendants engage in joint tortious conduct and one or more of them causes the plaintiff's harm, all of the defendants are jointly and severally liable for the harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 876 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a defendant is subject to liability if he/she does a tortious act in concert with others or pursuant to a common design).","rule":"When multiple defendants engage in joint tortious conduct and one or more of them causes the plaintiff's harm, all of the defendants are jointly and severally liable for the harm.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"When multiple defendants engage in joint tortious conduct and one or more of them causes the plaintiff's harm, all of the defendants are jointly and severally liable for the harm.","analysis":"Liability of multiple negligent actors\n\nIndivisible injuries Separable injuries\n\nActing independently Joint & several liability* Several liability only\n\nActing in concert Joint & several liability\n\n*Default rule on MBE.\n\nTort claims for personal injury generally require proof that a particular defendant's tortious conduct was the actual and proximate cause of the plaintiff's harm. But when two or more defendants jointly engage in tortious conduct (ie, act in concert)—and at least one of them causes the plaintiff's harm—all of the defendants are jointly and severally liable for the harm (Choice C). Here, the pedestrian was injured by a chair that was thrown from a hotel window. She then sued the occupants of all the rooms from which it might have been thrown. Since she offered no evidence as to the exact room from which the chair was thrown, she cannot prove which defendant(s) caused her injury. And since she also offered no evidence of joint tortious conduct, the defendants' motion for a directed verdict should be granted. (Choices A & B) The doctrine of alternative liability applies when (1) there were multiple tortfeasors, (2) at least one of them caused the plaintiff's harm, and (3) it is impossible to tell which one of them did. The burden then shifts to each defendant to prove that he/she did not cause the harm. Here, it may be unreasonable to expect the pedestrian to prove which defendant caused her harm. But alternative liability only applies if each defendant's tortious conduct is proved (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the pedestrian has failed to offer evidence that the defendants jointly engaged in tortious conduct."},"questionZh":"一名行人被从高层酒店窗户扔出的椅子砸伤。\n\n该行人起诉了所有可能扔出椅子的房间的住户。\n\n庭审中，该行人未能提供任何证据证明椅子是从哪个房间扔出的。\n\n被告已提出指示性判决动议。\n\n法院是否应准许该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应准许，因为要求行人证明是哪位被告造成了损害是不合理的。","B":"不应准许，因为存在替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）原则。","C":"应准许，因为原告始终负有举证责任，证明特定被告的行为是造成原告人身伤害的实际原因。","D":"应准许，因为该行人未能提供证据证明被告共同实施了侵权行为。"},"explanationZh":"多名有过失行为人的责任 不可分割的伤害 | 可分割的伤害 独立行为 | 连带责任* | 仅承担各自份额的责任（按份责任） 共同行为 | 连带责任 *MBE（多州律师资格考试）中的默认规则。\n\n针对人身伤害的侵权诉讼通常要求证明特定被告的侵权行为是原告所受伤害的实际原因（事实因果关系）和法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）。\n\n然而，当两名或多名被告共同实施侵权行为（即“共同行为”）——且其中至少一人造成了原告的伤害——时，所有被告均须对该伤害承担连带责任（选项 C）。\n\n本案中，一名行人被从酒店窗户扔出的椅子砸伤。\n\n随后，她起诉了所有可能扔出椅子的房间的住户。\n\n由于她未能提供证据证明椅子具体是从哪个房间扔出的，她无法证明是哪位（或哪些）被告造成了她的伤害。\n\n此外，由于她也未能提供存在共同侵权行为的证据，法院应支持被告提出的指示裁决（directed verdict）动议。\n\n（选项 A 和 B）“择一责任”（alternative liability）原则适用于以下情形：(1) 存在多名侵权行为人；(2) 其中至少一人造成了原告的伤害；(3) 无法确定具体是哪一人造成了伤害。\n\n此时，举证责任转移至每位被告，由其证明自己未造成该伤害。\n\n本案中，要求行人证明具体是哪位被告造成了伤害可能不尽合理。\n\n但“择一责任”原则的适用前提是必须先证明每位被告均实施了侵权行为（本案中未见此类证明）。\n\n教学目标：当多名被告实施共同侵权行为，且其中一人或多人造成了原告的伤害时，所有被告均须对该伤害承担连带责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 876 条 (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 876) (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明若被告与他人共同实施侵权行为，或依据共同意图实施侵权行为，则须承担责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当多名被告共同实施侵权行为，且其中一名或多名被告造成原告损害时，所有被告均须对该损害承担连带责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应准许该动议？","rule":"当多名被告共同实施侵权行为，且其中一名或多名被告造成原告损害时，所有被告均须对该损害承担连带责任。","analysis":"多名有过失行为人的责任 不可分割的伤害 | 可分割的伤害 独立行为 | 连带责任* | 仅承担各自份额的责任（按份责任） 共同行为 | 连带责任 *MBE（多州律师资格考试）中的默认规则。\n\n针对人身伤害的侵权诉讼通常要求证明特定被告的侵权行为是原告所受伤害的实际原因（事实因果关系）和法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）（法律因果关系）。\n\n然而，当两名或多名被告共同实施侵权行为（即“共同行为”）——且其中至少一人造成了原告的伤害——时，所有被告均须对该伤害承担连带责任（选项 C）。\n\n本案中，一名行人被从酒店窗户扔出的椅子砸伤。\n\n随后，她起诉了所有可能扔出椅子的房间的住户。\n\n由于她未能提供证据证明椅子具体是从哪个房间扔出的，她无法证明是哪位（或哪些）被告造成了她的伤害。\n\n此外，由于她也未能提供存在共同侵权行为的证据，法院应支持被告提出的指示裁决（directed verdict）动议。\n\n（选项 A 和 B）“择一责任”（alternative liability）原则适用于以下情形：(1) 存在多名侵权行为人；(2) 其中至少一人造成了原告的伤害；(3) 无法确定具体是哪一人造成了伤害。\n\n此时，举证责任转移至每位被告，由其证明自己未造成该伤害。\n\n本案中，要求行人证明具体是哪位被告造成了伤害可能不尽合理。\n\n但“择一责任”原则的适用前提是必须先证明每位被告均实施了侵权行为（本案中未见此类证明）。\n\n教学目标：当多名被告实施共同侵权行为，且其中一人或多人造成了原告的伤害时，所有被告均须对该伤害承担连带责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 876 条 (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 876) (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明若被告与他人共同实施侵权行为，或依据共同意图实施侵权行为，则须承担责任）。","conclusion":"答案 D：应准许，因为该行人未能提供证据证明被告共同实施了侵权行为。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":126,"sourceQuestionPage":376,"sourceQuestionPages":[376],"sourceExplanationPages":[377,378],"sourceAnswerPages":[377,378],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-127","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A fire that started in the defendant's warehouse spread to the plaintiff's adjacent warehouse. The defendant did not intentionally start the fire, and the plaintiff can produce no evidence as to how the fire started. However, the defendant had failed to install a sprinkler system, which was required by a criminal statute. The plaintiff can produce evidence that had the sprinkler system been installed, it could have extinguished the fire before it spread. In an action by the plaintiff against the defendant to recover for the fire damage, is it possible for the plaintiff to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because there is no evidence that the defendant negligently caused the fire to start.","B":"No, because the statute provides only for criminal penalties.","C":"Yes, because a landowner is strictly liable for harm to others caused by the spread of fire from his premises under the doctrine of Rylands v. Fletcher.","D":"Yes, because the plaintiff was harmed as a result of the defendant's violation of a statute that was meant to protect against this type of occurrence."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Elements of negligence per se\n\nDefendant violated statute or ordinance Plaintiff suffered type of harm statute or ordinance was intended to prevent Plaintiff is in class of persons statute or ordinance was intended to protect Civil actions for accidental fire damage are typically based on negligence. To prevail on a negligence claim, the plaintiff must prove the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. And under the doctrine of negligence per se, the elements of duty and breach are presumed if: the defendant violated a statute that statute was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff and the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect. Here, the defendant violated a statute designed to protect buildings and people from fire by failing to install a sprinkler system. As a result, the defendant's duty and breach will be presumed. And though there is no evidence that he negligently caused the fire to start, there is evidence that a sprinkler system could have prevented the fire from spreading to the plaintiff's warehouse (Choice A). As a result, there is evidence that the defendant's failure to install a sprinkler system caused the plaintiff's loss—making it possible for the plaintiff to prevail. (Choice B) The fact that a statute provides only for criminal penalties does not preclude it from establishing a duty of care for a civil negligence claim. (Choice C) Under the modern interpretation of Rylands v. Fletcher, a landowner is strictly liable for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity on his/her land. Since there is no indication that the defendant was engaging in any such activity in his warehouse here, this precedent does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of negligence per se, duty and breach are presumed if (1) the defendant violated a statute, (2) that statute was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff, and (3) the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 288B (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that it is negligent per se to violate a statute that establishes a duty of care).\n\nRylands v. Fletcher [1868] UKHL 1, [1868] LR 3 (HL) 330 (appeal taken from Eng.) (holding the defendant strictly liable for storing water that flooded the plaintiff's adjacent land).","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of negligence per se, duty and breach are presumed if (1) the defendant violated a statute, (2) that statute was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff, and (3) the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 288B (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that it is negligent per se to violate a statute that establishes a duty of care).\n\nRylands v. Fletcher [1868] UKHL 1, [1868] LR 3 (HL) 330 (appeal taken from Eng.) (holding the defendant strictly liable for storing water that flooded the plaintiff's adjacent land).","rule":"Under the doctrine of negligence per se, duty and breach are presumed if (1) the defendant violated a statute, (2) that statute was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff, and (3) the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the plaintiff against the defendant to recover for the fire damage, is it possible for the plaintiff to prevail?","rule":"Under the doctrine of negligence per se, duty and breach are presumed if (1) the defendant violated a statute, (2) that statute was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff, and (3) the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect.","analysis":"Elements of negligence per se\n\nDefendant violated statute or ordinance Plaintiff suffered type of harm statute or ordinance was intended to prevent Plaintiff is in class of persons statute or ordinance was intended to protect Civil actions for accidental fire damage are typically based on negligence. To prevail on a negligence claim, the plaintiff must prove the following elements: duty, breach, causation, and damages. And under the doctrine of negligence per se, the elements of duty and breach are presumed if: the defendant violated a statute that statute was intended to prevent the type of harm suffered by the plaintiff and the plaintiff is within a class of persons that the statute was intended to protect. Here, the defendant violated a statute designed to protect buildings and people from fire by failing to install a sprinkler system. As a result, the defendant's duty and breach will be presumed. And though there is no evidence that he negligently caused the fire to start, there is evidence that a sprinkler system could have prevented the fire from spreading to the plaintiff's warehouse (Choice A). As a result, there is evidence that the defendant's failure to install a sprinkler system caused the plaintiff's loss—making it possible for the plaintiff to prevail. (Choice B) The fact that a statute provides only for criminal penalties does not preclude it from establishing a duty of care for a civil negligence claim. (Choice C) Under the modern interpretation of Rylands v. Fletcher, a landowner is strictly liable for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity on his/her land. Since there is no indication that the defendant was engaging in any such activity in his warehouse here, this precedent does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the plaintiff was harmed as a result of the defendant's violation of a statute that was meant to protect against this type of occurrence."},"questionZh":"被告仓库起火，火势蔓延至原告相邻的仓库。\n\n被告并非蓄意纵火，原告也无法提供关于起火原因的证据。\n\n然而，被告未安装某项刑事法规所要求的自动喷淋系统。\n\n原告能够提供证据证明，若安装了该喷淋系统，便可在火势蔓延前将其扑灭。\n\n在原告针对被告提起的火灾损失索赔诉讼中，原告有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为没有证据表明被告因疏忽导致了火灾的发生。","B":"不能，因为该法规仅规定了刑事处罚。","C":"能，因为根据 *Rylands v. Fletcher* 案确立的原则，土地所有者对其场所内蔓延的火灾给他人造成的损害承担严格责任。","D":"能，因为原告遭受的损害源于被告违反了旨在防止此类事件发生的法规。"},"explanationZh":"“本身过失”（negligence per se）的构成要素 被告违反了法规或条例；原告遭受了该法规或条例旨在防止的损害类型；原告属于该法规或条例旨在保护的人群类别。因意外火灾造成的损失而提起的民事诉讼通常基于过失侵权。\n\n要在过失索赔中胜诉，原告必须证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系和损害后果。\n\n根据“本身过失”原则，如果出现以下情况，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的情形：被告违反了某项法规；该法规旨在防止原告所遭受的损害类型；且原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群类别。\n\n在本案中，被告因未安装自动喷水灭火系统，违反了旨在保护建筑物和人员免受火灾侵害的法规。\n\n因此，将推定被告存在注意义务及违反义务的行为。\n\n尽管没有证据表明被告因过失引发了火灾，但有证据显示，安装自动喷水灭火系统本可以阻止火势蔓延至原告的仓库（选项 A）。\n\n因此，有证据表明被告未安装自动喷水灭火系统的行为导致了原告的损失——这使得原告有可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）某项法规仅规定刑事处罚这一事实，并不妨碍其确立民事过失索赔中的注意义务。\n\n（选项 C）根据对 *Rylands v. Fletcher* 案的现代解读，土地所有者对其土地上进行的异常危险活动所造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n由于本案中没有迹象表明被告在其仓库内从事此类活动，因此该先例不适用。\n\n教育目标：根据“本身过失”原则，若满足以下条件，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的情形：(1) 被告违反了法规；(2) 该法规旨在防止原告所遭受的损害类型；(3) 原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群类别。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 288B 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明违反确立注意义务的法规即构成“本身过失”）。\n\nRylands 诉 Fletcher 案 [1868] UKHL 1, [1868] LR 3 (HL) 330（源自英格兰的上诉案件）（法院裁定，被告因蓄水导致洪水淹没原告相邻土地而须承担严格责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则，若满足以下条件，即推定存在注意义务及违反义务的行为：(1) 被告违反了某项法规；(2) 该法规旨在防止原告所遭受的那类损害；以及 (3) 原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群范围。","iracZh":{"issue":"在原告针对被告提起的火灾损失索赔诉讼中，原告有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"根据“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则，若满足以下条件，即推定存在注意义务及违反义务的行为：(1) 被告违反了某项法规；(2) 该法规旨在防止原告所遭受的那类损害；以及 (3) 原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群范围。","analysis":"“本身过失”（negligence per se）的构成要素 被告违反了法规或条例；原告遭受了该法规或条例旨在防止的损害类型；原告属于该法规或条例旨在保护的人群类别。因意外火灾造成的损失而提起的民事诉讼通常基于过失侵权。\n\n要在过失索赔中胜诉，原告必须证明以下要素：注意义务、违反义务、因果关系和损害后果。\n\n根据“本身过失”原则，如果出现以下情况，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的情形：被告违反了某项法规；该法规旨在防止原告所遭受的损害类型；且原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群类别。\n\n在本案中，被告因未安装自动喷水灭火系统，违反了旨在保护建筑物和人员免受火灾侵害的法规。\n\n因此，将推定被告存在注意义务及违反义务的行为。\n\n尽管没有证据表明被告因过失引发了火灾，但有证据显示，安装自动喷水灭火系统本可以阻止火势蔓延至原告的仓库（选项 A）。\n\n因此，有证据表明被告未安装自动喷水灭火系统的行为导致了原告的损失——这使得原告有可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）某项法规仅规定刑事处罚这一事实，并不妨碍其确立民事过失索赔中的注意义务。\n\n（选项 C）根据对 *Rylands v. Fletcher* 案的现代解读，土地所有者对其土地上进行的异常危险活动所造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n由于本案中没有迹象表明被告在其仓库内从事此类活动，因此该先例不适用。\n\n教育目标：根据“本身过失”原则，若满足以下条件，则推定存在注意义务及违反义务的情形：(1) 被告违反了法规；(2) 该法规旨在防止原告所遭受的损害类型；(3) 原告属于该法规旨在保护的人群类别。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 288B 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明违反确立注意义务的法规即构成“本身过失”）。\n\nRylands 诉 Fletcher 案 [1868] UKHL 1, [1868] LR 3 (HL) 330（源自英格兰的上诉案件）（法院裁定，被告因蓄水导致洪水淹没原告相邻土地而须承担严格责任）。","conclusion":"答案 D：能，因为原告遭受的损害源于被告违反了旨在防止此类事件发生的法规。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":127,"sourceQuestionPage":379,"sourceQuestionPages":[379],"sourceExplanationPages":[380,381],"sourceAnswerPages":[380,381],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-128","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"The day after a man completed the sale of his house and moved out, one of the slates flew off the roof during a windstorm. The slate struck the man's former neighbor, who was on the public sidewalk. The neighbor was seriously injured. Both the man and the neighbor were aware that the roof is old and had lost several slates in ordinary windstorms on other occasions. If the neighbor sues the man to recover damages for his injuries, will the neighbor likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man was neither the owner nor the occupier of the house when the neighbor was injured.","B":"No, because the neighbor knew that in the past slates had blown off the roof during windstorms.","C":"Yes, because the man was aware of the condition of the roof and should have realized that it was dangerous to persons outside the premises.","D":"Yes, because the roof was defective when the man sold the house."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Sellers of real property\n\n(negligence liability arising from dangerous condition on land)\n\nInjured Seller's liability Duration of liability party\n\nPerson Liable for physical harm caused by If seller created or actively concealed natural or artificial condition if: condition, liable until buyer discovers on land condition & has reasonable opportunity condition existed at time of sale to remedy it seller knew or should have known Otherwise, liable until buyer has condition existed & posed reasonable opportunity to discover & unreasonable risk to persons on remedy condition land buyer neither knew nor should have known of condition or risk and seller had reason to believe buyer would not discover condition or realize risk Person Liable for physical harm caused by artificial condition if: off land condition existed at time of sale and seller knew or should have known condition existed & posed unreasonable risk to persons off land Obligations associated with real property are generally owed by whoever occupies and controls the land—ie, the current land possessor. But a seller (previous land possessor) remains liable for negligence to persons off the land for physical harm caused by an artificial condition on the land that: existed at the time of the sale and\n\nthe seller knew, or should have known, existed and posed an unreasonable risk of harm to such persons.\n\nThat liability continues at least until the buyer has had a reasonable opportunity to discover and remedy the condition.* Here, the day after the man sold his home and moved out, a slate flew off the roof (artificial condition) during a windstorm and struck his former neighbor on the public sidewalk. The man knew that the roof was old and had lost several slates in previous windstorms, so he should have realized that it was dangerous to persons outside the premises. And since the new owner had not yet had a reasonable opportunity to discover and remedy this danger, the neighbor will likely prevail against the man (Choice A). *If the seller created or actively concealed the condition, the seller's liability continues until the buyer has discovered and had a reasonable opportunity to remedy the condition. (Choice B) In comparative negligence jurisdictions (default rule on the MBE), a plaintiff's recovery is reduced (not barred) if the plaintiff failed to exercise reasonable care. Therefore, the fact that the neighbor knew that slates had blown off the roof during windstorms and walked on the sidewalk anyway does not bar his claim. (Choice D) Although the roof was defective when the man sold the house, he is only liable for negligence if he knew or should have known that the defect existed and was dangerous.\n\nEducational objective: A seller remains liable to persons off the land for physical harm caused by an artificial condition on the land that (1) existed at the time of sale and (2) the seller knew, or should have known, existed and posed an unreasonable risk of harm to such persons.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 373 (duty of sellers to those off the land).","educationalObjective":"A seller remains liable to persons off the land for physical harm caused by an artificial condition on the land that (1) existed at the time of sale and (2) the seller knew, or should have known, existed and posed an unreasonable risk of harm to such persons.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 373 (duty of sellers to those off the land).","rule":"A seller remains liable to persons off the land for physical harm caused by an artificial condition on the land that (1) existed at the time of sale and (2) the seller knew, or should have known, existed and posed an unreasonable risk of harm to such persons.","irac":{"issue":"If the neighbor sues the man to recover damages for his injuries, will the neighbor likely prevail?","rule":"A seller remains liable to persons off the land for physical harm caused by an artificial condition on the land that (1) existed at the time of sale and (2) the seller knew, or should have known, existed and posed an unreasonable risk of harm to such persons.","analysis":"Sellers of real property\n\n(negligence liability arising from dangerous condition on land)\n\nInjured Seller's liability Duration of liability party\n\nPerson Liable for physical harm caused by If seller created or actively concealed natural or artificial condition if: condition, liable until buyer discovers on land condition & has reasonable opportunity condition existed at time of sale to remedy it seller knew or should have known Otherwise, liable until buyer has condition existed & posed reasonable opportunity to discover & unreasonable risk to persons on remedy condition land buyer neither knew nor should have known of condition or risk and seller had reason to believe buyer would not discover condition or realize risk Person Liable for physical harm caused by artificial condition if: off land condition existed at time of sale and seller knew or should have known condition existed & posed unreasonable risk to persons off land Obligations associated with real property are generally owed by whoever occupies and controls the land—ie, the current land possessor. But a seller (previous land possessor) remains liable for negligence to persons off the land for physical harm caused by an artificial condition on the land that: existed at the time of the sale and\n\nthe seller knew, or should have known, existed and posed an unreasonable risk of harm to such persons.\n\nThat liability continues at least until the buyer has had a reasonable opportunity to discover and remedy the condition.* Here, the day after the man sold his home and moved out, a slate flew off the roof (artificial condition) during a windstorm and struck his former neighbor on the public sidewalk. The man knew that the roof was old and had lost several slates in previous windstorms, so he should have realized that it was dangerous to persons outside the premises. And since the new owner had not yet had a reasonable opportunity to discover and remedy this danger, the neighbor will likely prevail against the man (Choice A). *If the seller created or actively concealed the condition, the seller's liability continues until the buyer has discovered and had a reasonable opportunity to remedy the condition. (Choice B) In comparative negligence jurisdictions (default rule on the MBE), a plaintiff's recovery is reduced (not barred) if the plaintiff failed to exercise reasonable care. Therefore, the fact that the neighbor knew that slates had blown off the roof during windstorms and walked on the sidewalk anyway does not bar his claim. (Choice D) Although the roof was defective when the man sold the house, he is only liable for negligence if he knew or should have known that the defect existed and was dangerous.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the man was aware of the condition of the roof and should have realized that it was dangerous to persons outside the premises."},"questionZh":"一名男子卖掉房子搬出去的第二天，一场暴风雨吹落了屋顶的一块瓦片。\n\n瓦片击中了站在人行道上的前邻居，导致邻居严重受伤。\n\n该男子和邻居都知道屋顶老旧，以前也曾在普通的暴风雨中掉落过几块瓦片。\n\n如果邻居起诉该男子要求赔偿损失，邻居胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为邻居受伤时，该男子既不是房屋的所有者也不是居住者。","B":"否，因为邻居知道以前暴风雨中屋顶瓦片被吹落过。","C":"是，因为该男子了解屋顶的状况，应该意识到这对房屋外的人构成危险。","D":"是，因为该男子卖房时屋顶就存在缺陷。"},"explanationZh":"不动产出卖人 （因土地上的危险状况而产生的过失责任） **责任主体** | **导致人身伤害的状况** | **责任持续时间** | **责任成立条件** --- | --- | --- | --- 出卖人 | 土地上的状况（自然或人工形成） | 直至买受人发现该状况并有合理机会予以修缮 | 出卖人造成或积极隐瞒了该状况；且出卖人知道或应当知道该状况存在并对土地上的人员构成不合理风险；且买受人既不知道也不应当知道该状况或风险，而出卖人有理由相信买受人不会发现该状况或意识到风险 出卖人 | 土地之外的状况（人工形成） | （未明确规定，通常直至买受人有合理机会发现并修缮该状况） | 出卖人造成或积极隐瞒了该状况；且出卖人知道或应当知道该状况存在并对土地外的人员构成不合理风险 与不动产相关的义务通常由占有并控制土地的人（即当前的土地占有者）承担。\n\n但出卖人（前土地占有者）仍需对土地之外的人员因土地上的人工状况造成的人身伤害承担过失责任，前提是该状况：在出售时已存在，且 出卖人知道或应当知道该状况存在，并对该类人员构成不合理伤害风险。\n\n该责任至少持续到买受人有合理机会发现并修缮该状况为止。* 在本案中，该男子售房搬出后的第二天，一场大风导致屋顶的一块石板（人工状况）脱落，击中了公共人行道上的前邻居。\n\n该男子知道屋顶年久失修，且此前大风中曾有石板脱落，因此他应当意识到这对房产外的人员构成危险。\n\n由于新业主尚未有合理机会发现并消除这一危险，邻居很可能胜诉（选项 A）。\n\n*如果出卖人造成或积极隐瞒了该状况，则出卖人的责任持续至买受人发现该状况并有合理机会予以修缮为止。\n\n（选项 B）在适用比较过失原则的司法管辖区（MBE 考试的默认规则）中，如果原告未尽到合理注意义务，其获赔金额会减少（而非丧失索赔权）。\n\n因此，邻居明知大风曾导致屋顶石板脱落却仍走在人行道上，这一事实并不妨碍其提出索赔。\n\n(选项 D) 尽管该男子在出售房屋时屋顶存在缺陷，但只有在他已知或理应知道该缺陷存在且具有危险性的情况下，他才需承担过失责任。\n\n学习目标：对于因土地上的人造状况而对土地以外的人员造成的人身伤害，卖方仍需承担责任，前提是该状况：(1) 在出售时已存在；且 (2) 卖方已知或理应知道其存在，并意识到其对上述人员构成不合理的伤害风险。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 373 条（卖方对土地以外人员的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果土地上存在人为因素，且该因素在出售时已存在，并且卖方明知或应知该因素存在，且对土地外的人身伤害构成不合理的风险，则卖方仍需对土地外的人身伤害承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果邻居起诉该男子要求赔偿其所受伤害，邻居胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"如果土地上存在人为因素，且该因素在出售时已存在，并且卖方明知或应知该因素存在，且对土地外的人身伤害构成不合理的风险，则卖方仍需对土地外的人身伤害承担责任。","analysis":"不动产出卖人 （因土地上的危险状况而产生的过失责任） **责任主体** | **导致人身伤害的状况** | **责任持续时间** | **责任成立条件** --- | --- | --- | --- 出卖人 | 土地上的状况（自然或人工形成） | 直至买受人发现该状况并有合理机会予以修缮 | 出卖人造成或积极隐瞒了该状况；且出卖人知道或应当知道该状况存在并对土地上的人员构成不合理风险；且买受人既不知道也不应当知道该状况或风险，而出卖人有理由相信买受人不会发现该状况或意识到风险 出卖人 | 土地之外的状况（人工形成） | （未明确规定，通常直至买受人有合理机会发现并修缮该状况） | 出卖人造成或积极隐瞒了该状况；且出卖人知道或应当知道该状况存在并对土地外的人员构成不合理风险 与不动产相关的义务通常由占有并控制土地的人（即当前的土地占有者）承担。\n\n但出卖人（前土地占有者）仍需对土地之外的人员因土地上的人工状况造成的人身伤害承担过失责任，前提是该状况：在出售时已存在，且 出卖人知道或应当知道该状况存在，并对该类人员构成不合理伤害风险。\n\n该责任至少持续到买受人有合理机会发现并修缮该状况为止。* 在本案中，该男子售房搬出后的第二天，一场大风导致屋顶的一块石板（人工状况）脱落，击中了公共人行道上的前邻居。\n\n该男子知道屋顶年久失修，且此前大风中曾有石板脱落，因此他应当意识到这对房产外的人员构成危险。\n\n由于新业主尚未有合理机会发现并消除这一危险，邻居很可能胜诉（选项 A）。\n\n*如果出卖人造成或积极隐瞒了该状况，则出卖人的责任持续至买受人发现该状况并有合理机会予以修缮为止。\n\n（选项 B）在适用比较过失原则的司法管辖区（MBE 考试的默认规则）中，如果原告未尽到合理注意义务，其获赔金额会减少（而非丧失索赔权）。\n\n因此，邻居明知大风曾导致屋顶石板脱落却仍走在人行道上，这一事实并不妨碍其提出索赔。\n\n(选项 D) 尽管该男子在出售房屋时屋顶存在缺陷，但只有在他已知或理应知道该缺陷存在且具有危险性的情况下，他才需承担过失责任。\n\n学习目标：对于因土地上的人造状况而对土地以外的人员造成的人身伤害，卖方仍需承担责任，前提是该状况：(1) 在出售时已存在；且 (2) 卖方已知或理应知道其存在，并意识到其对上述人员构成不合理的伤害风险。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 373 条（卖方对土地以外人员的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是，因为该男子了解屋顶的状况，应该意识到这对房屋外的人构成危险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":128,"sourceQuestionPage":382,"sourceQuestionPages":[382],"sourceExplanationPages":[383,384],"sourceAnswerPages":[383,384],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-129","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"During a professional baseball game, a pitcher was continuously heckled by some spectators seated behind a wire mesh fence in the stands above the dugout. On several occasions, the pitcher turned and looked directly at the hecklers with a scowl on his face, but the heckling continued. The pitcher then wound up as though he was preparing to pitch in the direction of his catcher but threw a fastball toward the hecklers instead. The ball passed through the wire mesh fence and struck a woman who was one of the hecklers. The woman brought an action against the pitcher and the team to recover damages for battery. The trial court entered judgment as a matter of law in favor of the defendants on the battery claim. The woman has appealed the judgment, contending that the trial court erred in entering judgment as a matter of law in favor of the defendants. The appellate court has held that the battery claim presents a jury issue. How should the court rule on the judgment as a matter of law entered in favor of the team?","choices":{"A":"Affirm, because an employer is not vicariously liable for a servant's battery.","B":"Affirm, if the pitcher's act was a knowing violation of team rules.","C":"Reverse and remand, because a jury could find the team vicariously liable for a battery committed by the pitcher in the course of his employment.","D":"Reverse and remand, if a jury could find negligence on the part of the team's management."},"answer":"C","explanation":"An employer is vicariously liable under the doctrine of respondeat superior for any tort committed by its employee while acting within the scope of the employment relationship—ie, when: the employee's conduct is inherent in the employment position (eg, a security guard batters a thieving customer) or\n\nthe employee acts to further the employer's business (eg, a waiter batters a customer who is complaining about food quality to protect the restaurant's integrity).\n\nHere, a reasonable jury could find that the pitcher committed the battery within the course of his employment—eg, to further the team's success by quieting the hecklers. Therefore, the appellate court should reverse the judgment as a matter of law and remand the case for a jury to ultimately determine the team's vicarious liability (Choice A). (Choice B) An employer (master) may be vicariously liable for a tort committed by its employee (servant) acting within the scope of the employment relationship, even if the employee knowingly violated employment guidelines or rules. (Choice D) Vicarious liability holds the employer liable for torts committed by its employee within the scope of employment based solely on the relationship between the two parties— not based on the employer's negligent supervision or control of its employee. Therefore, it is irrelevant whether the team's management acted negligently.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for its employee's torts committed within the scope of the employment relationship. Torts are committed within the scope of employment when the employee's conduct is (1) inherent to the job or (2) in furtherance of the employer's business.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Agency §§ 2.04, 7.07 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (doctrine of respondeat superior).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (judgment as a matter of law).\n\nManning v. Grimsley, 643 F.2d 20 (1st Cir. 1981).","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for its employee's torts committed within the scope of the employment relationship. Torts are committed within the scope of employment when the employee's conduct is (1) inherent to the job or (2) in furtherance of the employer's business.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Agency §§ 2.04, 7.07 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (doctrine of respondeat superior).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (judgment as a matter of law).\n\nManning v. Grimsley, 643 F.2d 20 (1st Cir. 1981).","rule":"Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for its employee's torts committed within the scope of the employment relationship. Torts are committed within the scope of employment when the employee's conduct is (1) inherent to the job or (2) in furtherance of the employer's business.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule on the judgment as a matter of law entered in favor of the team?","rule":"Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for its employee's torts committed within the scope of the employment relationship. Torts are committed within the scope of employment when the employee's conduct is (1) inherent to the job or (2) in furtherance of the employer's business.","analysis":"An employer is vicariously liable under the doctrine of respondeat superior for any tort committed by its employee while acting within the scope of the employment relationship—ie, when: the employee's conduct is inherent in the employment position (eg, a security guard batters a thieving customer) or\n\nthe employee acts to further the employer's business (eg, a waiter batters a customer who is complaining about food quality to protect the restaurant's integrity).\n\nHere, a reasonable jury could find that the pitcher committed the battery within the course of his employment—eg, to further the team's success by quieting the hecklers. Therefore, the appellate court should reverse the judgment as a matter of law and remand the case for a jury to ultimately determine the team's vicarious liability (Choice A). (Choice B) An employer (master) may be vicariously liable for a tort committed by its employee (servant) acting within the scope of the employment relationship, even if the employee knowingly violated employment guidelines or rules. (Choice D) Vicarious liability holds the employer liable for torts committed by its employee within the scope of employment based solely on the relationship between the two parties— not based on the employer's negligent supervision or control of its employee. Therefore, it is irrelevant whether the team's management acted negligently.","conclusion":"Answer C: Reverse and remand, because a jury could find the team vicariously liable for a battery committed by the pitcher in the course of his employment."},"questionZh":"在一场职业棒球比赛中，一名投手不断受到看台上（位于球员休息区上方、铁丝网围栏后方）几名观众的起哄和嘲讽。\n\n投手曾数次怒目回头看向这些起哄者，但对方并未停止。\n\n随后，投手做出了准备向接球手投球的动作，却转而向起哄者投出了一记快速球。\n\n球穿过铁丝网围栏，击中了一名起哄的女性。\n\n该女子对投手及球队提起诉讼，就“故意侵害身体（battery）”（battery）侵权行为寻求损害赔偿。\n\n初审法院针对故意侵害身体（battery）指控作出了有利于被告的“法律问题判决”（judgment as a matter of law）。\n\n该女子提出上诉，主张初审法院作出有利于被告的法律问题判决存在错误。\n\n上诉法院裁定，该故意侵害身体（battery）指控涉及应由陪审团裁决的事实问题。\n\n法院应如何处理针对球队作出的这项法律问题判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"维持原判，因为雇主不对雇员实施的故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","B":"维持原判，如果投手的行为属于明知故犯地违反球队规则。","C":"撤销原判并发回重审，因为陪审团可能认定球队应对投手在受雇期间实施的故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","D":"撤销原判并发回重审，如果陪审团可能认定球队管理层存在过失。"},"explanationZh":"根据“上级责任原则”（respondeat superior），如果雇员在雇佣关系范围内实施了侵权行为，雇主即须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。所谓在雇佣关系范围内，是指：雇员的行为是其职位的固有组成部分（例如，保安故意侵害身体（battery）行窃的顾客），或者 雇员的行为旨在促进雇主的业务（例如，服务员故意侵害身体（battery）抱怨食物质量的顾客，以维护餐厅的声誉）。\n\n在本案中，合理的陪审团可能会认定该投手是在履行职务期间实施了故意侵害身体（battery）行为——例如，为了让起哄者安静下来从而促进球队的成功。\n\n因此，上诉法院应推翻原判（即撤销关于无需承担责任的判决），并将案件发回重审，由陪审团最终裁定球队是否应承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）即使雇员（仆人）明知违反了雇佣准则或规则，只要其行为处于雇佣关系范围内，雇主（主人）仍可能须对其侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 D）替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）的依据仅在于双方之间的关系，而非雇主对雇员的监督或控制是否存在疏忽；只要雇员在雇佣范围内实施了侵权行为，雇主即须承担责任。\n\n因此，球队管理层是否存在疏忽行为在法律上无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：根据“上级责任原则”，雇主须对其雇员在雇佣关系范围内实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n当雇员的行为属于 (1) 职位固有职责或 (2) 旨在促进雇主业务时，该行为即被视为在雇佣关系范围内。\n\n参考文献 《代理法重述（第三版）》第 2.04、7.07 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（上级责任原则）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 50(a) 条（法律问题判决/即决判决）。\n\nManning v. Grimsley, 643 F.2d 20 (1st Cir. 1981)。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“上级责任原则”（respondeat superior），雇主应对其雇员在雇佣关系范围内实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n当雇员的行为（1）属于工作本身固有的内容，或（2）旨在促进雇主业务时，该行为即被视为在雇佣范围内实施。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应如何处理针对球队作出的这项法律问题判决？","rule":"根据“上级责任原则”（respondeat superior），雇主应对其雇员在雇佣关系范围内实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n当雇员的行为（1）属于工作本身固有的内容，或（2）旨在促进雇主业务时，该行为即被视为在雇佣范围内实施。","analysis":"根据“上级责任原则”（respondeat superior），如果雇员在雇佣关系范围内实施了侵权行为，雇主即须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。所谓在雇佣关系范围内，是指：雇员的行为是其职位的固有组成部分（例如，保安故意侵害身体（battery）行窃的顾客），或者 雇员的行为旨在促进雇主的业务（例如，服务员故意侵害身体（battery）抱怨食物质量的顾客，以维护餐厅的声誉）。\n\n在本案中，合理的陪审团可能会认定该投手是在履行职务期间实施了故意侵害身体（battery）行为——例如，为了让起哄者安静下来从而促进球队的成功。\n\n因此，上诉法院应推翻原判（即撤销关于无需承担责任的判决），并将案件发回重审，由陪审团最终裁定球队是否应承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）即使雇员（仆人）明知违反了雇佣准则或规则，只要其行为处于雇佣关系范围内，雇主（主人）仍可能须对其侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 D）替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）的依据仅在于双方之间的关系，而非雇主对雇员的监督或控制是否存在疏忽；只要雇员在雇佣范围内实施了侵权行为，雇主即须承担责任。\n\n因此，球队管理层是否存在疏忽行为在法律上无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：根据“上级责任原则”，雇主须对其雇员在雇佣关系范围内实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n当雇员的行为属于 (1) 职位固有职责或 (2) 旨在促进雇主业务时，该行为即被视为在雇佣关系范围内。\n\n参考文献 《代理法重述（第三版）》第 2.04、7.07 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（上级责任原则）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 50(a) 条（法律问题判决/即决判决）。\n\nManning v. Grimsley, 643 F.2d 20 (1st Cir. 1981)。","conclusion":"答案 C：撤销原判并发回重审，因为陪审团可能认定球队应对投手在受雇期间实施的故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-540e7512ddb3e420"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":129,"sourceQuestionPage":385,"sourceQuestionPages":[385],"sourceExplanationPages":[386,387],"sourceAnswerPages":[386,387],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-130","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Breach & Proof","question":"As a shopper was leaving a supermarket, an automatic door that should have opened outward opened inward, striking and breaking the shopper's nose. The owner of the building had installed the automatic door. The lease, pursuant to which the supermarket occupied the building, provided that the supermarket was responsible for all maintenance of the premises. The shopper sued the supermarket. At trial, neither the shopper nor the supermarket offered any testimony, expert or otherwise, as to why the door had opened inward. At the close of evidence, both the shopper and the supermarket moved for judgment as a matter of law. How should the trial judge rule?","choices":{"A":"Grant judgment for the shopper, because it is undisputed that the door malfunctioned.","B":"Grant judgment for the supermarket, because the shopper failed to join the owner of the building as a defendant.","C":"Grant judgment for the supermarket, because the shopper failed to offer proof of the supermarket's negligence.","D":"Submit the case to the jury, because on these facts negligence may be inferred."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Land possessors (eg, the supermarket) have a nondelegable duty to keep their premises safe for invitees (eg, the shopper). A land possessor who breaches this duty and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for negligence. And if there is no direct evidence of negligence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur permits—but does not require—an inference of negligence when: the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and\n\nthe evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm (eg, the instrumentality that inflicted the harm was under the defendant's exclusive control).\n\nIf reasonable juries could reach differing conclusions on the issue of negligence, the court should deny both parties' motions for judgment as a matter of law and submit the case to the jury. Here, an automatic door in the supermarket opened in the wrong direction and broke the shopper's nose. A reasonable jury could conclude that the supermarket was not negligent— eg, if the injury was caused by the building owner's installation. But a reasonable jury could also infer negligence because (1) an automatic door would not usually malfunction unless negligently maintained and (2) the supermarket had exclusive control of maintaining the door (Choice C). Therefore, the court should deny both motions and submit the case to the jury. (Choice A) The undisputed fact that the door malfunctioned does not prove the supermarket's negligence. And strict liability is inappropriate here since there was no commercial sale of a defective product. (Choice B) The shopper is not required to sue all possibly negligent persons. Therefore, the shopper's failure to join the building owner as a defendant does not justify judgment as a matter of law for the supermarket.\n\nEducational objective: The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur permits an inference of negligence if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a defendant's negligence can be inferred based on the type of accident that injured the plaintiff).","educationalObjective":"The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur permits an inference of negligence if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a defendant's negligence can be inferred based on the type of accident that injured the plaintiff).","rule":"The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur permits an inference of negligence if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","irac":{"issue":"How should the trial judge rule?","rule":"The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur permits an inference of negligence if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","analysis":"Land possessors (eg, the supermarket) have a nondelegable duty to keep their premises safe for invitees (eg, the shopper). A land possessor who breaches this duty and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for negligence. And if there is no direct evidence of negligence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur permits—but does not require—an inference of negligence when: the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and\n\nthe evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm (eg, the instrumentality that inflicted the harm was under the defendant's exclusive control).\n\nIf reasonable juries could reach differing conclusions on the issue of negligence, the court should deny both parties' motions for judgment as a matter of law and submit the case to the jury. Here, an automatic door in the supermarket opened in the wrong direction and broke the shopper's nose. A reasonable jury could conclude that the supermarket was not negligent— eg, if the injury was caused by the building owner's installation. But a reasonable jury could also infer negligence because (1) an automatic door would not usually malfunction unless negligently maintained and (2) the supermarket had exclusive control of maintaining the door (Choice C). Therefore, the court should deny both motions and submit the case to the jury. (Choice A) The undisputed fact that the door malfunctioned does not prove the supermarket's negligence. And strict liability is inappropriate here since there was no commercial sale of a defective product. (Choice B) The shopper is not required to sue all possibly negligent persons. Therefore, the shopper's failure to join the building owner as a defendant does not justify judgment as a matter of law for the supermarket.","conclusion":"Answer D: Submit the case to the jury, because on these facts negligence may be inferred."},"questionZh":"一名顾客在离开超市时，本应向外开启的自动门却向内开启，撞伤并导致该顾客鼻梁骨折。\n\n该自动门是由建筑物业主安装的。\n\n超市承租该建筑物的租赁合同规定，超市负责场所的所有维护工作。\n\n该顾客起诉了超市。\n\n在庭审中，无论是顾客还是超市，均未提供任何证词（包括专家证词或其他证词）来解释为何该门会向内开启。\n\n在举证阶段结束后，顾客和超市双方均申请法院直接作出法律裁决（即请求法院不将案件交由陪审团审议而直接判决）。\n\n初审法官应如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"判决顾客胜诉，因为门发生故障这一事实并无争议。","B":"判决超市胜诉，因为顾客未将建筑物业主列为共同被告。","C":"判决超市胜诉，因为顾客未能提供证据证明超市存在过失。","D":"将案件提交给陪审团审理，因为根据这些事实，可以推断出存在过失。"},"explanationZh":"土地占有者（例如超市）有义务确保其场所对受邀者（例如购物者）的安全，且该义务不可转让。\n\n若土地占有者违反此义务并导致原告身体伤害，则须承担过失责任。\n\n若无直接证据证明存在过失，依据“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则”（res ipsa loquitur），在以下情况下，法院可以（但非必须）推断存在过失：原告所受伤害通常由处于被告地位的人员的过失所致；且证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因（例如，致害物处于被告的完全控制之下）。\n\n如果合理的陪审团可能就过失问题得出不同结论，法院应驳回双方关于“法律问题判决”（judgment as a matter of law）的动议，并将案件提交给陪审团审理。\n\n本案中，超市的一扇自动门开启方向错误，导致购物者鼻梁骨折。\n\n合理的陪审团可能认定超市并无过失——例如，若伤害是由建筑物业主的安装问题引起的。\n\n但合理的陪审团也可能推断存在过失，理由是：(1) 自动门通常不会发生故障，除非维护不当；(2) 超市对该门的维护拥有完全控制权（选项 C）。\n\n因此，法院应驳回双方动议，将案件交由陪审团审理。\n\n（选项 A）门发生故障这一无争议事实并不能证明超市存在过失。\n\n此外，由于本案不涉及缺陷产品的商业销售，适用严格责任原则并不恰当。\n\n（选项 B）购物者无需起诉所有可能存在过失的人员。\n\n因此，购物者未将建筑物业主列为被告，并不构成超市获得“法律问题判决”的充分理由。\n\n教育目标：“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则”允许推断存在过失，前提是：(1) 原告所受伤害通常由处于被告地位的人员的过失所致；(2) 证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 328D 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐述了在何种情况下可根据导致原告受伤的事故类型推断被告存在过失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“事实本身说明问题”原则（res ipsa loquitur）允许在以下情况下推断存在过失：(1) 原告遭受的损害类型通常是由处于被告地位的人的过失造成的；以及 (2) 证据倾向于排除导致该损害的其他潜在原因。","iracZh":{"issue":"初审法官应如何裁决？","rule":"“事实本身说明问题”原则（res ipsa loquitur）允许在以下情况下推断存在过失：(1) 原告遭受的损害类型通常是由处于被告地位的人的过失造成的；以及 (2) 证据倾向于排除导致该损害的其他潜在原因。","analysis":"土地占有者（例如超市）有义务确保其场所对受邀者（例如购物者）的安全，且该义务不可转让。\n\n若土地占有者违反此义务并导致原告身体伤害，则须承担过失责任。\n\n若无直接证据证明存在过失，依据“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则”（res ipsa loquitur），在以下情况下，法院可以（但非必须）推断存在过失：原告所受伤害通常由处于被告地位的人员的过失所致；且证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因（例如，致害物处于被告的完全控制之下）。\n\n如果合理的陪审团可能就过失问题得出不同结论，法院应驳回双方关于“法律问题判决”（judgment as a matter of law）的动议，并将案件提交给陪审团审理。\n\n本案中，超市的一扇自动门开启方向错误，导致购物者鼻梁骨折。\n\n合理的陪审团可能认定超市并无过失——例如，若伤害是由建筑物业主的安装问题引起的。\n\n但合理的陪审团也可能推断存在过失，理由是：(1) 自动门通常不会发生故障，除非维护不当；(2) 超市对该门的维护拥有完全控制权（选项 C）。\n\n因此，法院应驳回双方动议，将案件交由陪审团审理。\n\n（选项 A）门发生故障这一无争议事实并不能证明超市存在过失。\n\n此外，由于本案不涉及缺陷产品的商业销售，适用严格责任原则并不恰当。\n\n（选项 B）购物者无需起诉所有可能存在过失的人员。\n\n因此，购物者未将建筑物业主列为被告，并不构成超市获得“法律问题判决”的充分理由。\n\n教育目标：“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则”允许推断存在过失，前提是：(1) 原告所受伤害通常由处于被告地位的人员的过失所致；(2) 证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 328D 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐述了在何种情况下可根据导致原告受伤的事故类型推断被告存在过失）。","conclusion":"答案 D：将案件提交给陪审团审理，因为根据这些事实，可以推断出存在过失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-c6c9134f57a34849"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":130,"sourceQuestionPage":388,"sourceQuestionPages":[388],"sourceExplanationPages":[389,390],"sourceAnswerPages":[389,390],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-131","subject":"Torts","topic":"Intentional Torts","question":"A philanthropist operates a residential rehabilitation center for emotionally disturbed and ungovernable children who have been committed to his custody by their parents or by juvenile authorities. The center's purpose is to modify the behavior of the children through a teaching program carried out in a family-like environment. Though the children are not permitted to leave the center without his permission, there are no bars or guards to prevent them from doing so. It has been held in the state where the center is located that persons having custody of children have the same duties and responsibilities that they would have if they were the parents of the children. An orphan, aged 12, who had been in the philanthropist's custody for six months while exhibiting no signs of violence, left the center without permission. The philanthropist became aware of the orphan's absence almost immediately, but made no attempt to locate him or secure his return, though reports reached him that the orphan had been seen in the vicinity. Thirty-six hours after the orphan left the center, he committed a brutal assault upon a five-year-old girl, causing her to suffer extensive permanent injury. If an action is brought against the philanthropist on behalf of the girl to recover damages for her injuries, is the girl likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because custodians are not personally liable for the intentional torts of those in custody.","B":"No, because the philanthropist did not know or have reason to know that the orphan had a propensity to attack younger children.","C":"Yes, because the orphan was in the philanthropist's custody.","D":"Yes, because the orphan was old enough to be liable for battery."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Defendants generally have no duty to control the conduct of others absent some special relationship. Custodians have a special relationship with persons in their custody, so custodians owe a duty to use reasonable care to mitigate any foreseeable risks of harm that those persons pose to others (eg, where a custodian knows of a prisoner's threat or propensity for violence). If a custodian breaches that duty and causes the plaintiff physical harm, the custodian will be liable for negligence. Here, the orphan was in the philanthropist's custody (special relationship). But since the orphan exhibited no signs of violence, the philanthropist neither knew nor had reason to know of the orphan's propensity for attacking younger children (no foreseeable risk of harm). Therefore, the philanthropist did not breach a duty of care owed to the girl, and the girl is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) A custodian is personally liable (ie, directly liable) for the intentional tort of a person in his/her custody if the custodian encouraged the person to commit that tort. (Choice C) Although the philanthropist's custody of the orphan imposed a duty of reasonable care, that duty only required the philanthropist to mitigate foreseeable risks of harm. Since any risk of harm by the orphan was unforeseeable, the philanthropist did not breach that duty. (Choice D) The fact that the orphan was old enough to be liable for battery is insufficient to establish the philanthropist's liability. The philanthropist would be liable only if the orphan posed a foreseeable risk of harm (liability for negligent failure to control) or acted on the philanthropist's behalf (direct liability for battery).\n\nEducational objective: Because of their special relationship with persons in their custody, custodians have a duty to use reasonable care to mitigate any foreseeable risks of harm that those persons pose to others. Therefore, a custodian may be liable for negligence if he/she breaches that duty and a person in his/her custody harms the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 41 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (duty to control another's foreseeable risk of harm).","educationalObjective":"Because of their special relationship with persons in their custody, custodians have a duty to use reasonable care to mitigate any foreseeable risks of harm that those persons pose to others. Therefore, a custodian may be liable for negligence if he/she breaches that duty and a person in his/her custody harms the plaintiff.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 41 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (duty to control another's foreseeable risk of harm).","rule":"Because of their special relationship with persons in their custody, custodians have a duty to use reasonable care to mitigate any foreseeable risks of harm that those persons pose to others. Therefore, a custodian may be liable for negligence if he/she breaches that duty and a person in his/her custody harms the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"If an action is brought against the philanthropist on behalf of the girl to recover damages for her injuries, is the girl likely to prevail?","rule":"Because of their special relationship with persons in their custody, custodians have a duty to use reasonable care to mitigate any foreseeable risks of harm that those persons pose to others. Therefore, a custodian may be liable for negligence if he/she breaches that duty and a person in his/her custody harms the plaintiff.","analysis":"Defendants generally have no duty to control the conduct of others absent some special relationship. Custodians have a special relationship with persons in their custody, so custodians owe a duty to use reasonable care to mitigate any foreseeable risks of harm that those persons pose to others (eg, where a custodian knows of a prisoner's threat or propensity for violence). If a custodian breaches that duty and causes the plaintiff physical harm, the custodian will be liable for negligence. Here, the orphan was in the philanthropist's custody (special relationship). But since the orphan exhibited no signs of violence, the philanthropist neither knew nor had reason to know of the orphan's propensity for attacking younger children (no foreseeable risk of harm). Therefore, the philanthropist did not breach a duty of care owed to the girl, and the girl is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) A custodian is personally liable (ie, directly liable) for the intentional tort of a person in his/her custody if the custodian encouraged the person to commit that tort. (Choice C) Although the philanthropist's custody of the orphan imposed a duty of reasonable care, that duty only required the philanthropist to mitigate foreseeable risks of harm. Since any risk of harm by the orphan was unforeseeable, the philanthropist did not breach that duty. (Choice D) The fact that the orphan was old enough to be liable for battery is insufficient to establish the philanthropist's liability. The philanthropist would be liable only if the orphan posed a foreseeable risk of harm (liability for negligent failure to control) or acted on the philanthropist's behalf (direct liability for battery).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the philanthropist did not know or have reason to know that the orphan had a propensity to attack younger children."},"questionZh":"一位慈善家经营着一家寄宿式康复中心，专门收容那些由父母或少年司法部门移交其监护的情绪不稳定且难以管教的儿童。\n\n该中心的宗旨是在家庭般的氛围中通过教育项目来矫正儿童的行为。\n\n尽管未经许可儿童不得离开中心，但中心并未设置栅栏或警卫来阻止他们离开。\n\n在该中心所在的州，法律规定，拥有儿童监护权的人承担着与儿童父母相同的义务和责任。\n\n一名12岁的孤儿在接受该慈善家监护的六个月期间未表现出任何暴力倾向，后未经许可离开了中心。\n\n慈善家几乎立即察觉了该孤儿的失踪，尽管有报告称有人在附近看到了这名孤儿，但他并未试图寻找或将其带回。\n\n在离开中心36小时后，该孤儿对一名五岁女童实施了残忍的袭击，导致其遭受了严重的永久性伤害。\n\n如果代表该女童对慈善家提起诉讼以寻求损害赔偿，该女童胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为监护人不对被监护人实施的故意侵权行为承担个人责任。","B":"不会，因为慈善家并不知道也不应知道该孤儿有使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）年幼儿童的倾向。","C":"会，因为该孤儿处于慈善家的监护之下。","D":"会，因为该孤儿已达到足以对故意侵害身体（battery）行为承担责任的年龄。"},"explanationZh":"通常情况下，除非存在某种特殊关系，否则被告没有义务控制他人的行为。\n\n监管人与其监管对象之间存在特殊关系，因此监管人有义务尽合理注意，以减轻监管对象对他人的任何可预见的伤害风险（例如，当监管人知晓囚犯发出的威胁或其暴力倾向时）。\n\n如果监管人违反了该义务并导致原告遭受人身伤害，则监管人须承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，孤儿处于慈善家的监管之下（构成特殊关系）。\n\n但由于该孤儿未表现出暴力迹象，慈善家既不知晓也不应知晓该孤儿有使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）年幼儿童的倾向（不存在可预见的伤害风险）。\n\n因此，慈善家并未违反其对该女孩应尽的注意义务，该女孩胜诉的可能性不大。\n\n（选项 A）如果监管人怂恿其监管对象实施故意侵权行为，则监管人须对该对象的故意侵权行为承担个人责任（即直接责任）。\n\n（选项 C）尽管慈善家对孤儿的监管使其负有合理注意义务，但该义务仅要求慈善家减轻可预见的伤害风险。\n\n由于该孤儿造成的任何伤害风险均属不可预见，慈善家并未违反该义务。\n\n（选项 D）仅凭该孤儿已达到足以对故意侵害身体（battery）行为（battery）承担责任的年龄，不足以确立慈善家的责任。\n\n慈善家须承担责任的前提是：该孤儿构成了可预见的伤害风险（因未能控制而产生的过失责任），或者该孤儿是代表慈善家行事（因故意侵害身体（battery）行为而产生的直接责任）。\n\n教育目标：由于监管人与其监管对象之间存在特殊关系，监管人有义务尽合理注意，以减轻监管对象对他人的任何可预见的伤害风险。\n\n因此，如果监管人违反了该义务，且其监管对象伤害了原告，则监管人可能须承担过失责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 41 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（关于控制他人可预见伤害风险的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"由于与被监护人之间存在特殊关系，监护人有义务尽合理注意，以减轻被监护人可能对他人造成的任何可预见的伤害风险。\n\n因此，如果监护人违反了这一义务，且其被监护人伤害了原告，监护人可能需承担过失责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果代表该女童对慈善家提起诉讼以寻求损害赔偿，该女童胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"由于与被监护人之间存在特殊关系，监护人有义务尽合理注意，以减轻被监护人可能对他人造成的任何可预见的伤害风险。\n\n因此，如果监护人违反了这一义务，且其被监护人伤害了原告，监护人可能需承担过失责任。","analysis":"通常情况下，除非存在某种特殊关系，否则被告没有义务控制他人的行为。\n\n监管人与其监管对象之间存在特殊关系，因此监管人有义务尽合理注意，以减轻监管对象对他人的任何可预见的伤害风险（例如，当监管人知晓囚犯发出的威胁或其暴力倾向时）。\n\n如果监管人违反了该义务并导致原告遭受人身伤害，则监管人须承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，孤儿处于慈善家的监管之下（构成特殊关系）。\n\n但由于该孤儿未表现出暴力迹象，慈善家既不知晓也不应知晓该孤儿有使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）年幼儿童的倾向（不存在可预见的伤害风险）。\n\n因此，慈善家并未违反其对该女孩应尽的注意义务，该女孩胜诉的可能性不大。\n\n（选项 A）如果监管人怂恿其监管对象实施故意侵权行为，则监管人须对该对象的故意侵权行为承担个人责任（即直接责任）。\n\n（选项 C）尽管慈善家对孤儿的监管使其负有合理注意义务，但该义务仅要求慈善家减轻可预见的伤害风险。\n\n由于该孤儿造成的任何伤害风险均属不可预见，慈善家并未违反该义务。\n\n（选项 D）仅凭该孤儿已达到足以对故意侵害身体（battery）行为（battery）承担责任的年龄，不足以确立慈善家的责任。\n\n慈善家须承担责任的前提是：该孤儿构成了可预见的伤害风险（因未能控制而产生的过失责任），或者该孤儿是代表慈善家行事（因故意侵害身体（battery）行为而产生的直接责任）。\n\n教育目标：由于监管人与其监管对象之间存在特殊关系，监管人有义务尽合理注意，以减轻监管对象对他人的任何可预见的伤害风险。\n\n因此，如果监管人违反了该义务，且其监管对象伤害了原告，则监管人可能须承担过失责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 41 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2012)（关于控制他人可预见伤害风险的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为慈善家并不知道也不应知道该孤儿有使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）年幼儿童的倾向。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-0884fbc3b0e51190"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":131,"sourceQuestionPage":391,"sourceQuestionPages":[391],"sourceExplanationPages":[392,393],"sourceAnswerPages":[392,393],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-132","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"An employer retained a doctor to conduct physical examinations of prospective employees. After reviewing one applicant's test results, the doctor informed the employer and the applicant that the applicant suffered from AIDS. The applicant was shocked by this news and suffered a heart attack as a result. One month later, the applicant learned that she in fact did not have AIDS and that the doctor had negligently confused her file with that of another prospective employee. If the applicant sues the doctor to recover damages, under which cause(s) of action is she most likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"Invasion of privacy.","B":"Invasion of privacy and negligent misrepresentation.","C":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress.","D":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress and negligent misrepresentation."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress\n\nTheories of Required elements recovery\n\nZone of danger Defendant’s negligence put plaintiff in danger of immediate bodily harm Danger caused plaintiff serious emotional distress Bystander Defendant negligently injured plaintiff’s close relative Plaintiff contemporaneously perceived that event Event caused plaintiff serious emotional distress Special situations Defendant negligently: delivered erroneous announcement of death or illness mishandled corpse or bodily remains contaminated food with repulsive foreign object Caused plaintiff serious emotional distress A plaintiff can recover for negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) in three situations: (1) zone of danger, (2) bystander, or (3) special situations. Liability under the special-situations theory arises when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently: delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness mishandled a corpse or bodily remains or contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object. Here, the doctor negligently misdiagnosed the applicant with AIDS because he confused her file with that of another prospective employee. The erroneous announcement of this illness caused the applicant serious emotional distress—she went into shock and suffered a heart attack upon being informed of this diagnosis. As a result, she will most likely prevail on her cause of action against the doctor for NIED. (Choices A & B) There are four theories of recovery for invasion of privacy—none of which apply here. That is because the doctor did not (1) conduct the physical examination of the applicant without her authorization (ie, no intrusion upon seclusion), (2) publicize her private information, (3) publicize the misdiagnosis to cast her in a false light, or (4) use her name or likeness for personal benefit. (Choices B & D) Negligent misrepresentation occurs in a commercial setting when (1) the defendant negligently provides the plaintiff with false information and (2) the plaintiff reasonably relies on that information and suffers pecuniary loss. Here, the doctor provided\n\nthe applicant with false information, but she never relied on his misdiagnosis. Therefore, negligent misrepresentation does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: Negligent infliction of emotional distress occurs under the special-situations theory when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently (1) delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness, (2) mishandled a corpse or bodily remains, or (3) contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 47(b) cmt. f (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (special-situations theory of negligent infliction of emotional distress).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 652A–652E (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (invasion of privacy).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 552 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (negligent misrepresentation).","educationalObjective":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress occurs under the special-situations theory when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently (1) delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness, (2) mishandled a corpse or bodily remains, or (3) contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 47(b) cmt. f (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (special-situations theory of negligent infliction of emotional distress).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 652A–652E (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (invasion of privacy).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 552 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (negligent misrepresentation).","rule":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress occurs under the special-situations theory when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently (1) delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness, (2) mishandled a corpse or bodily remains, or (3) contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object.","irac":{"issue":"If the applicant sues the doctor to recover damages, under which cause(s) of action is she most likely to prevail?","rule":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress occurs under the special-situations theory when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently (1) delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness, (2) mishandled a corpse or bodily remains, or (3) contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object.","analysis":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress\n\nTheories of Required elements recovery\n\nZone of danger Defendant’s negligence put plaintiff in danger of immediate bodily harm Danger caused plaintiff serious emotional distress Bystander Defendant negligently injured plaintiff’s close relative Plaintiff contemporaneously perceived that event Event caused plaintiff serious emotional distress Special situations Defendant negligently: delivered erroneous announcement of death or illness mishandled corpse or bodily remains contaminated food with repulsive foreign object Caused plaintiff serious emotional distress A plaintiff can recover for negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) in three situations: (1) zone of danger, (2) bystander, or (3) special situations. Liability under the special-situations theory arises when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently: delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness mishandled a corpse or bodily remains or contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object. Here, the doctor negligently misdiagnosed the applicant with AIDS because he confused her file with that of another prospective employee. The erroneous announcement of this illness caused the applicant serious emotional distress—she went into shock and suffered a heart attack upon being informed of this diagnosis. As a result, she will most likely prevail on her cause of action against the doctor for NIED. (Choices A & B) There are four theories of recovery for invasion of privacy—none of which apply here. That is because the doctor did not (1) conduct the physical examination of the applicant without her authorization (ie, no intrusion upon seclusion), (2) publicize her private information, (3) publicize the misdiagnosis to cast her in a false light, or (4) use her name or likeness for personal benefit. (Choices B & D) Negligent misrepresentation occurs in a commercial setting when (1) the defendant negligently provides the plaintiff with false information and (2) the plaintiff reasonably relies on that information and suffers pecuniary loss. Here, the doctor provided\n\nthe applicant with false information, but she never relied on his misdiagnosis. Therefore, negligent misrepresentation does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer C: Negligent infliction of emotional distress."},"questionZh":"某雇主聘请了一位医生对求职者进行体检。\n\n在查看了某位申请人的检查结果后，医生告知雇主和该申请人，称其患有艾滋病。\n\n申请人对这一消息感到震惊，并因此引发了心脏病发作。\n\n一个月后，申请人得知自己实际上并未患艾滋病，而是医生因疏忽将她的档案与另一位求职者的档案弄混了。\n\n如果申请人起诉医生要求赔偿，她最有可能基于何种诉由胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"侵犯隐私权。","B":"侵犯隐私权与过失虚假陈述。","C":"过失导致精神痛苦。","D":"过失导致精神痛苦与过失虚假陈述。"},"explanationZh":"因过失导致精神痛苦（NIED） 获赔的理论依据/构成要件 “危险区域”理论：被告的过失使原告处于遭受即时身体伤害的危险之中；该危险导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。“旁观者”理论：被告过失伤害了原告的近亲属；原告同时目睹了该事件；该事件导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。“特殊情形”理论：被告过失（1）发布了关于死亡或疾病的错误通知，（2）不当处理尸体或遗骸，或（3）在食物中混入令人作呕的异物；从而导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。原告可在以下三种情形下就“因过失导致精神痛苦”（NIED）主张赔偿：（1）危险区域，（2）旁观者，或（3）特殊情形。\n\n在“特殊情形”理论下，若原告因被告的过失行为——即发布死亡或疾病的错误通知、不当处理尸体或遗骸、或在食物中混入令人作呕的异物——而遭受严重精神痛苦，则产生赔偿责任。\n\n本案中，医生因将申请人的档案与另一位求职者的档案混淆，过失地误诊申请人患有艾滋病。\n\n这一错误的疾病通知导致申请人遭受严重精神痛苦——她在获悉诊断结果后陷入休克并突发心脏病。\n\n因此，她极有可能在针对医生提起的NIED诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项A和B）关于侵犯隐私权的索赔有四种理论依据，但本案均不适用。\n\n这是因为医生并未：（1）未经授权对申请人进行体检（即不构成“侵扰私密空间”），（2）公开其私人信息，（3）公开误诊情况从而将其置于“虚假形象”之下，或（4）为个人利益使用其姓名或肖像。\n\n（选项B和D）“过失虚假陈述”发生在商业环境中，需满足以下条件：（1）被告过失向原告提供虚假信息，且（2）原告合理信赖该信息并遭受了金钱损失。\n\n本案中，医生虽然向 申请人提供了虚假信息，但她并未信赖该误诊结论。\n\n因此，“过失虚假陈述”不适用。\n\n教育目标：根据“特殊情形理论”（special-situations theory），若被告因疏忽而（1）发布关于死亡或疾病的错误通知，（2）不当处置尸体或遗骸，或（3）使食物受到令人作呕的异物污染，导致原告遭受严重的情绪困扰，即构成过失导致情绪困扰（negligent infliction of emotional distress）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与情绪伤害责任》第47(b)条注释f（美国法律协会，2012年）（关于过失导致情绪困扰的特殊情形理论）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第652A–652E条（美国法律协会，1977年）（关于侵犯隐私）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第552条（美国法律协会，1977年）（关于过失虚假陈述）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“特殊情形”理论，当原告因被告的过失行为而遭受严重精神痛苦时，即构成“过失导致精神痛苦”；这些过失行为包括：(1) 传达了关于死亡或疾病的错误通知；(2) 不当处理尸体或遗骸；或 (3) 在食物中混入令人作呕的异物。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果申请人起诉医生要求赔偿，她最有可能基于何种诉由胜诉？","rule":"根据“特殊情形”理论，当原告因被告的过失行为而遭受严重精神痛苦时，即构成“过失导致精神痛苦”；这些过失行为包括：(1) 传达了关于死亡或疾病的错误通知；(2) 不当处理尸体或遗骸；或 (3) 在食物中混入令人作呕的异物。","analysis":"因过失导致精神痛苦（NIED） 获赔的理论依据/构成要件 “危险区域”理论：被告的过失使原告处于遭受即时身体伤害的危险之中；该危险导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。“旁观者”理论：被告过失伤害了原告的近亲属；原告同时目睹了该事件；该事件导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。“特殊情形”理论：被告过失（1）发布了关于死亡或疾病的错误通知，（2）不当处理尸体或遗骸，或（3）在食物中混入令人作呕的异物；从而导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。原告可在以下三种情形下就“因过失导致精神痛苦”（NIED）主张赔偿：（1）危险区域，（2）旁观者，或（3）特殊情形。\n\n在“特殊情形”理论下，若原告因被告的过失行为——即发布死亡或疾病的错误通知、不当处理尸体或遗骸、或在食物中混入令人作呕的异物——而遭受严重精神痛苦，则产生赔偿责任。\n\n本案中，医生因将申请人的档案与另一位求职者的档案混淆，过失地误诊申请人患有艾滋病。\n\n这一错误的疾病通知导致申请人遭受严重精神痛苦——她在获悉诊断结果后陷入休克并突发心脏病。\n\n因此，她极有可能在针对医生提起的NIED诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项A和B）关于侵犯隐私权的索赔有四种理论依据，但本案均不适用。\n\n这是因为医生并未：（1）未经授权对申请人进行体检（即不构成“侵扰私密空间”），（2）公开其私人信息，（3）公开误诊情况从而将其置于“虚假形象”之下，或（4）为个人利益使用其姓名或肖像。\n\n（选项B和D）“过失虚假陈述”发生在商业环境中，需满足以下条件：（1）被告过失向原告提供虚假信息，且（2）原告合理信赖该信息并遭受了金钱损失。\n\n本案中，医生虽然向 申请人提供了虚假信息，但她并未信赖该误诊结论。\n\n因此，“过失虚假陈述”不适用。\n\n教育目标：根据“特殊情形理论”（special-situations theory），若被告因疏忽而（1）发布关于死亡或疾病的错误通知，（2）不当处置尸体或遗骸，或（3）使食物受到令人作呕的异物污染，导致原告遭受严重的情绪困扰，即构成过失导致情绪困扰（negligent infliction of emotional distress）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与情绪伤害责任》第47(b)条注释f（美国法律协会，2012年）（关于过失导致情绪困扰的特殊情形理论）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第652A–652E条（美国法律协会，1977年）（关于侵犯隐私）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第552条（美国法律协会，1977年）（关于过失虚假陈述）。","conclusion":"答案 C：过失导致精神痛苦。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"negligent misrepresentation","ipa":"/nˈɛɡlədʒənt mɪsɹˌɛpɹɪzɛntˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"neg·li·gent mis·rep·re·sen·ta·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"过失性虚假陈述：在特定信息关系中未合理核实信息","contextualMeaning":"过失性虚假陈述：在特定信息关系中未合理核实信息","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Invasion of privacy and negligent misrepresentation.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"intrusion upon seclusion","ipa":"/ˌɪntɹˈuʒən ʌpˈɑn sɪklˈuʒən/","syllables":"in·tru·sion upon seclu·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"侵扰私人安宁：以高度冒犯方式故意侵入合理隐私领域","contextualMeaning":"侵扰私人安宁：以高度冒犯方式故意侵入合理隐私领域","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"That is because the doctor did not (1) conduct the physical examination of the applicant without her authorization (ie, no intrusion upon seclusion), (2) publicize her private information, (3) publicize the misdiagnosis to cast her in a false light, or (4) use her name or likeness for personal benefit.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"false light","ipa":"/fˈɔls lˈaɪt/","syllables":"false light","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"虚假形象：以高度冒犯方式广泛制造关于原告的虚假印象","contextualMeaning":"虚假形象：以高度冒犯方式广泛制造关于原告的虚假印象","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"That is because the doctor did not (1) conduct the physical examination of the applicant without her authorization (ie, no intrusion upon seclusion), (2) publicize her private information, (3) publicize the misdiagnosis to cast her in a false light, or (4) use her name or likeness for personal benefit.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":132,"sourceQuestionPage":394,"sourceQuestionPages":[394],"sourceExplanationPages":[395,396],"sourceAnswerPages":[395,396],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-133","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A man has a small trampoline in his backyard which, as he knows, is commonly used by neighbor children as well as his own. The trampoline is in good condition, is not defective in any way, and normally is surrounded by mats to prevent injury if a user should fall off. Prior to leaving with his family for the day, the man leaned the trampoline up against the side of the house and placed the mats in the garage. While the family was away, a new boy in the neighborhood, aged 11, wandered into the man's yard and saw the trampoline. The boy had not previously been aware of its presence, but having frequently used a trampoline before, he decided to set it up and started to jump. He lost his balance on one jump and took a hard fall on the bare ground, suffering a serious injury that would have been prevented by the mats. An action has been brought against the man on the boy's behalf to recover damages for the injuries the boy sustained from his fall. In this jurisdiction, the traditional common law rules pertaining to contributory negligence have been replaced by a pure comparative negligence rule. If the boy's suit fails, what will be the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"An 11-year old child who has frequently used a trampoline should reasonably understand the danger of using a trampoline without mats.","B":"The boy did not know about the trampoline before entering the man's yard and thus was not \"lured\" onto the premises.","C":"The boy entered the man's yard and used the trampoline without the man's permission.","D":"The boy failed to exercise reasonable care commensurate with his age, intelligence, and experience."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the attractive nuisance doctrine, land possessors have a duty to exercise reasonable care to protect child trespassers from artificial (ie, man-made) conditions on their land when: the condition is located where the land possessor knows or should know that children are likely to trespass\n\nthe land possessor knows or should know that the condition poses an unreasonable risk of serious bodily harm or death to children\n\nchildren of the trespasser's age cannot reasonably discover or appreciate the risk and\n\nthe risk outweighs the condition's utility and the burden of eliminating the risk.\n\nA land possessor who breaches this duty and causes the child trespasser physical harm is liable for negligence. Here, the 11-year-old boy was a trespasser because he wandered into the man's yard without permission. The boy then suffered a serious injury when he fell off the man's trampoline (artificial condition). But an 11-year old child—especially one who has frequently used a trampoline—should reasonably understand the risk of using a trampoline without mats. Therefore, the attractive nuisance doctrine does not apply, and the boy's suit will likely fail. (Choice B) Traditionally, the attractive nuisance doctrine only applied when the child was attracted (ie, lured) onto the property by the artificial condition. But since this is no longer required, the fact that the boy did not know about the trampoline before entering the man's yard is irrelevant. (Choice C) Although the boy trespassed by entering the man's yard without permission, the boy could have recovered had the attractive nuisance doctrine applied. (Choice D) The boy's failure to exercise reasonable care commensurate with his age, intelligence, and experience would completely bar his recovery if traditional contributory negligence rules applied. But this jurisdiction has adopted a pure comparative negligence rule, which would only reduce his recovery.\n\nEducational objective: Under the attractive nuisance doctrine, land possessors have a duty to protect child trespassers from artificial conditions on their land under certain circumstances. But this doctrine does not apply when children of the trespasser's age could reasonably discover or appreciate the risk presented by the condition.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 339 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (attractive nuisance doctrine).","educationalObjective":"Under the attractive nuisance doctrine, land possessors have a duty to protect child trespassers from artificial conditions on their land under certain circumstances. But this doctrine does not apply when children of the trespasser's age could reasonably discover or appreciate the risk presented by the condition.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 339 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (attractive nuisance doctrine).","rule":"Under the attractive nuisance doctrine, land possessors have a duty to protect child trespassers from artificial conditions on their land under certain circumstances. But this doctrine does not apply when children of the trespasser's age could reasonably discover or appreciate the risk presented by the condition.","irac":{"issue":"If the boy's suit fails, what will be the most likely reason?","rule":"Under the attractive nuisance doctrine, land possessors have a duty to protect child trespassers from artificial conditions on their land under certain circumstances. But this doctrine does not apply when children of the trespasser's age could reasonably discover or appreciate the risk presented by the condition.","analysis":"Under the attractive nuisance doctrine, land possessors have a duty to exercise reasonable care to protect child trespassers from artificial (ie, man-made) conditions on their land when: the condition is located where the land possessor knows or should know that children are likely to trespass\n\nthe land possessor knows or should know that the condition poses an unreasonable risk of serious bodily harm or death to children\n\nchildren of the trespasser's age cannot reasonably discover or appreciate the risk and\n\nthe risk outweighs the condition's utility and the burden of eliminating the risk.\n\nA land possessor who breaches this duty and causes the child trespasser physical harm is liable for negligence. Here, the 11-year-old boy was a trespasser because he wandered into the man's yard without permission. The boy then suffered a serious injury when he fell off the man's trampoline (artificial condition). But an 11-year old child—especially one who has frequently used a trampoline—should reasonably understand the risk of using a trampoline without mats. Therefore, the attractive nuisance doctrine does not apply, and the boy's suit will likely fail. (Choice B) Traditionally, the attractive nuisance doctrine only applied when the child was attracted (ie, lured) onto the property by the artificial condition. But since this is no longer required, the fact that the boy did not know about the trampoline before entering the man's yard is irrelevant. (Choice C) Although the boy trespassed by entering the man's yard without permission, the boy could have recovered had the attractive nuisance doctrine applied. (Choice D) The boy's failure to exercise reasonable care commensurate with his age, intelligence, and experience would completely bar his recovery if traditional contributory negligence rules applied. But this jurisdiction has adopted a pure comparative negligence rule, which would only reduce his recovery.","conclusion":"Answer A: An 11-year old child who has frequently used a trampoline should reasonably understand the danger of using a trampoline without mats."},"questionZh":"一名男子在后院放着一个小蹦床，他知道邻居家的小孩和自家的孩子经常使用它。\n\n该蹦床状况良好，没有任何缺陷，且通常周围都铺有垫子，以防使用者跌落受伤。\n\n在全家外出之前，该男子将蹦床斜靠在房屋侧墙上，并将垫子收进了车库。\n\n全家外出期间，邻居家一名11岁的男孩误入该男子的院子，看到了这个蹦床。\n\n这名男孩此前并不知道这里有蹦床，但由于他以前经常玩蹦床，便决定将其支好并开始跳跃。\n\n他在一次跳跃中失去平衡，重重地摔在裸露的地面上，导致了严重的伤害——而如果当时有垫子，这种伤害本可避免。\n\n现代表该男孩向该男子提起诉讼，要求赔偿其因跌落而遭受的伤害。\n\n在该司法管辖区，有关“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）的传统普通法规则已被“纯粹比较过失”（pure comparative negligence）规则所取代。\n\n如果男孩的诉讼败诉，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"一名经常使用蹦床的11岁儿童，理应理解在没有垫子的情况下使用蹦床的危险性。","B":"男孩在进入该男子院子之前并不知道蹦床的存在，因此并未受到“诱导”而进入该场所。","C":"男孩是在未经该男子许可的情况下进入其院子并使用蹦床的。","D":"男孩未能尽到与其年龄、智力和经验相称的合理注意义务。"},"explanationZh":"根据“诱人妨害”（attractive nuisance）原则，土地占有者有义务尽合理注意，保护儿童侵入者免受其土地上的人造（即人工建造的）状况造成的伤害，前提是：该状况位于土地占有者已知或应知儿童可能侵入的地点； 土地占有者已知或应知该状况对儿童构成严重人身伤害或死亡的不合理风险； 处于侵入者该年龄段的儿童无法合理发现或意识到该风险；以及 该风险超过了该状况的效用及消除该风险所需的负担。\n\n若土地占有者违反此义务并导致儿童侵入者遭受身体伤害，则须承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，这名11岁男孩属于侵入者，因为他未经许可擅自进入了该男子的院子。\n\n随后，他在从该男子的蹦床（属于人造状况）上跌落时受了重伤。\n\n然而，一名11岁的儿童——尤其是经常使用蹦床的儿童——理应理解在没有防护垫的情况下使用蹦床所带来的风险。\n\n因此，“诱人妨害”原则不适用，该男孩的诉讼很可能会败诉。\n\n（选项 B）传统上，“诱人妨害”原则仅适用于儿童因受人造状况吸引（即被诱导）而进入该物业的情况。\n\n但由于现已不再有此要求，该男孩在进入院子前不知道蹦床存在这一事实便无关紧要。\n\n（选项 C）尽管该男孩因未经许可进入院子而构成侵入，但如果“诱人妨害”原则适用，他本可以获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）如果适用传统的“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）规则，该男孩未能尽到与其年龄、智力和经验相称的合理注意义务，将完全阻碍其获得赔偿。\n\n但该司法管辖区采用了“纯粹比较过失”（pure comparative negligence）规则，这意味着只会减少其获赔金额。\n\n教育目标：根据“诱人妨害”原则，在特定情况下，土地占有者有义务保护儿童侵入者免受其土地上人造状况的伤害。\n\n但如果处于侵入者该年龄段的儿童能够合理发现或意识到该状况所带来的风险，则该原则不适用。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第339条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（诱人妨害原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“诱人危险物”原则（attractive nuisance doctrine），在特定情况下，土地占有者有义务保护儿童侵入者免受其土地上的人造设施或状况的伤害。\n\n但如果该年龄段的儿童能够合理地发现或意识到该状况所带来的风险，则该原则不适用。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果男孩的诉讼败诉，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"根据“诱人危险物”原则（attractive nuisance doctrine），在特定情况下，土地占有者有义务保护儿童侵入者免受其土地上的人造设施或状况的伤害。\n\n但如果该年龄段的儿童能够合理地发现或意识到该状况所带来的风险，则该原则不适用。","analysis":"根据“诱人妨害”（attractive nuisance）原则，土地占有者有义务尽合理注意，保护儿童侵入者免受其土地上的人造（即人工建造的）状况造成的伤害，前提是：该状况位于土地占有者已知或应知儿童可能侵入的地点； 土地占有者已知或应知该状况对儿童构成严重人身伤害或死亡的不合理风险； 处于侵入者该年龄段的儿童无法合理发现或意识到该风险；以及 该风险超过了该状况的效用及消除该风险所需的负担。\n\n若土地占有者违反此义务并导致儿童侵入者遭受身体伤害，则须承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，这名11岁男孩属于侵入者，因为他未经许可擅自进入了该男子的院子。\n\n随后，他在从该男子的蹦床（属于人造状况）上跌落时受了重伤。\n\n然而，一名11岁的儿童——尤其是经常使用蹦床的儿童——理应理解在没有防护垫的情况下使用蹦床所带来的风险。\n\n因此，“诱人妨害”原则不适用，该男孩的诉讼很可能会败诉。\n\n（选项 B）传统上，“诱人妨害”原则仅适用于儿童因受人造状况吸引（即被诱导）而进入该物业的情况。\n\n但由于现已不再有此要求，该男孩在进入院子前不知道蹦床存在这一事实便无关紧要。\n\n（选项 C）尽管该男孩因未经许可进入院子而构成侵入，但如果“诱人妨害”原则适用，他本可以获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）如果适用传统的“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）规则，该男孩未能尽到与其年龄、智力和经验相称的合理注意义务，将完全阻碍其获得赔偿。\n\n但该司法管辖区采用了“纯粹比较过失”（pure comparative negligence）规则，这意味着只会减少其获赔金额。\n\n教育目标：根据“诱人妨害”原则，在特定情况下，土地占有者有义务保护儿童侵入者免受其土地上人造状况的伤害。\n\n但如果处于侵入者该年龄段的儿童能够合理发现或意识到该状况所带来的风险，则该原则不适用。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第339条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（诱人妨害原则）。","conclusion":"答案 A：一名经常使用蹦床的11岁儿童，理应理解在没有垫子的情况下使用蹦床的危险性。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-db8761ad9fc432c1"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":133,"sourceQuestionPage":397,"sourceQuestionPages":[397],"sourceExplanationPages":[398,399],"sourceAnswerPages":[398,399],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-134","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A man entered a drug store to make some purchases. As he was searching the aisles for various items, he noticed a display card containing automatic pencils. The display card was on a high shelf behind a cashier's counter. The man saw a sign on the counter that read, \"No Admittance, Employees Only.\" Seeing no clerks in the vicinity to help him, the man went behind the counter to get a pencil. A clerk then appeared behind the counter and asked whether she could help him. He said he just wanted a pencil and that he could reach the display card himself. The clerk said nothing further. While reaching for the display card, the man stepped sideways into a hidden, open shaft and fell to the basement, ten feet below. The clerk knew of the presence of the open shaft but assumed incorrectly that the man had noticed it. The man sued the drug store to recover damages for the injuries he sustained in the fall. The jurisdiction has adopted a rule of pure comparative negligence, and it follows traditional common law rules governing the duties of a land possessor. Will the man likely recover a judgment against the drug store?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man was a trespasser.","B":"No, because the man's injuries did not result from the defendant's willful or wanton misconduct.","C":"Yes, because the clerk had reason to believe that the man was unaware of the open shaft.","D":"Yes, because the premises were defective with respect to a public invitee."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the traditional common law approach,* land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants—including invitees. But an invitee is treated as a trespasser if that person intentionally enters an area without permission. And if that person is a known or anticipated trespasser, the landowner owes a limited duty to: warn the trespasser about hidden, artificial (ie, man-made) dangers that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by the trespasser and use reasonable care in active operations (ie, activities conducted on the land). A land possessor who breaches this duty and causes the trespasser physical harm is liable for negligence. And if the land possessor is an employer, the doctrine of respondeat superior holds the land possessor vicariously liable for its employees' negligence while on the job. Here, the man was an invitee when he entered the drug store to shop. But he became a trespasser when he intentionally went behind the cashier's counter without permission (Choice D). Nevertheless, the clerk owed him a duty to warn since she knew he was there and had reason to believe that he was unaware of the hidden, open shaft (Choice A). And since her failure to warn the man caused him to fall into the shaft and suffer harm, the man will likely recover a judgment from the drug store (employer) for the clerk's (employee's) negligence. *The traditional approach is followed in about half the jurisdictions. The other half follow the modern approach, under which land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants (except flagrant trespassers). (Choice B) Although the man's injuries did not result from the drug store's willful or wanton (ie, intentional or reckless) misconduct, the store is still vicariously liable for its employee's negligence under the doctrine of respondeat superior.\n\nEducational objective: A land possessor owes a duty to known or anticipated trespassers to (1) warn them about hidden, artificial dangers that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by trespassers and (2) use reasonable care in active operations.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 337 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (duty to known trespassers).","educationalObjective":"A land possessor owes a duty to known or anticipated trespassers to (1) warn them about hidden, artificial dangers that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by trespassers and (2) use reasonable care in active operations.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 337 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (duty to known trespassers).","rule":"A land possessor owes a duty to known or anticipated trespassers to (1) warn them about hidden, artificial dangers that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by trespassers and (2) use reasonable care in active operations.","irac":{"issue":"Will the man likely recover a judgment against the drug store?","rule":"A land possessor owes a duty to known or anticipated trespassers to (1) warn them about hidden, artificial dangers that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by trespassers and (2) use reasonable care in active operations.","analysis":"Under the traditional common law approach,* land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to foreseeable land entrants—including invitees. But an invitee is treated as a trespasser if that person intentionally enters an area without permission. And if that person is a known or anticipated trespasser, the landowner owes a limited duty to: warn the trespasser about hidden, artificial (ie, man-made) dangers that are known to the land possessor but unlikely to be discovered by the trespasser and use reasonable care in active operations (ie, activities conducted on the land). A land possessor who breaches this duty and causes the trespasser physical harm is liable for negligence. And if the land possessor is an employer, the doctrine of respondeat superior holds the land possessor vicariously liable for its employees' negligence while on the job. Here, the man was an invitee when he entered the drug store to shop. But he became a trespasser when he intentionally went behind the cashier's counter without permission (Choice D). Nevertheless, the clerk owed him a duty to warn since she knew he was there and had reason to believe that he was unaware of the hidden, open shaft (Choice A). And since her failure to warn the man caused him to fall into the shaft and suffer harm, the man will likely recover a judgment from the drug store (employer) for the clerk's (employee's) negligence. *The traditional approach is followed in about half the jurisdictions. The other half follow the modern approach, under which land possessors owe a duty of reasonable care to all land entrants (except flagrant trespassers). (Choice B) Although the man's injuries did not result from the drug store's willful or wanton (ie, intentional or reckless) misconduct, the store is still vicariously liable for its employee's negligence under the doctrine of respondeat superior.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the clerk had reason to believe that the man was unaware of the open shaft."},"questionZh":"一名男子进入一家药店购物。\n\n当他在货架间寻找商品时，注意到一张陈列着自动铅笔的展示卡。\n\n该展示卡位于收银台后方的高处货架上。\n\n男子看到柜台上写着“非员工禁止入内”的标示。\n\n由于附近没有店员提供协助，该男子便走到柜台后面去拿铅笔。\n\n此时，一名店员出现在柜台后，询问是否需要帮忙。\n\n男子表示他只是想拿一支铅笔，而且自己够得着那张展示卡。\n\n店员未再多言。\n\n在伸手去拿展示卡时，男子向侧面迈步，不慎踏入一个隐蔽的敞开式竖井口，并跌落至十英尺（约3米）深的地下室。\n\n店员明知该敞开式竖井的存在，却错误地以为男子也注意到了它。\n\n该男子起诉药店，要求赔偿因跌落而遭受的伤害。\n\n该司法管辖区采用“纯粹比较过失”规则，并遵循关于土地占有者义务的传统普通法规则。\n\n该男子胜诉（获得法院判决支持其赔偿请求）的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该男子属于侵入者。","B":"不会，因为该男子的伤害并非由被告的故意或肆意不当行为所致。","C":"会，因为店员有理由相信该男子并未察觉那个敞开的竖井。","D":"会，因为对于“公众受邀者”而言，该场所存在缺陷。"},"explanationZh":"根据传统的普通法原则*，土地占有者对可预见的进入者（包括受邀者）负有合理的注意义务。\n\n然而，如果受邀者未经许可故意进入某区域，则会被视为侵入者。\n\n对于已知或可预见的侵入者，土地占有者负有有限的义务：即针对那些土地占有者已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽的人造（即人为造成的）危险发出警告，并在进行主动操作（即在土地上开展的活动）时尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n如果土地占有者违反此义务并导致侵入者身体受伤，则需承担过失责任。\n\n此外，如果土地占有者是雇主，根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），该占有者还需对其雇员在工作期间的过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n在本案中，该男子进入药店购物时属于受邀者；但他未经许可故意走到收银台后面时，便转而成为侵入者（选项 D）。\n\n尽管如此，店员仍对他负有警告义务，因为她知道他在那里，并且有理由相信他并未察觉那个隐蔽的敞开式竖井（选项 A）。\n\n由于她未向该男子发出警告，导致其跌入竖井并受伤，该男子很可能因店员（雇员）的过失而获得针对药店（雇主）的胜诉判决。\n\n*约半数司法管辖区遵循上述传统原则。\n\n其余半数则遵循现代原则，即土地占有者对所有进入者（恶劣的侵入者除外）均负有合理的注意义务。\n\n（选项 B）尽管该男子的伤害并非源于药店故意或肆意（即蓄意或鲁莽）的不当行为，但根据“雇主责任原则”，药店仍需对其雇员的过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n教育目标：土地占有者对已知或可预见的侵入者负有以下义务：(1) 针对那些土地占有者已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽的人造危险发出警告；(2) 在进行主动操作时尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第337条（美国法律学会，1965年）（针对已知侵入者的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地占有者对已知或可预见的侵入者负有以下义务：(1) 针对土地占有者已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽人造危险发出警告；以及 (2) 在进行积极作业时尽到合理的注意义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"该男子胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"土地占有者对已知或可预见的侵入者负有以下义务：(1) 针对土地占有者已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽人造危险发出警告；以及 (2) 在进行积极作业时尽到合理的注意义务。","analysis":"根据传统的普通法原则*，土地占有者对可预见的进入者（包括受邀者）负有合理的注意义务。\n\n然而，如果受邀者未经许可故意进入某区域，则会被视为侵入者。\n\n对于已知或可预见的侵入者，土地占有者负有有限的义务：即针对那些土地占有者已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽的人造（即人为造成的）危险发出警告，并在进行主动操作（即在土地上开展的活动）时尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n如果土地占有者违反此义务并导致侵入者身体受伤，则需承担过失责任。\n\n此外，如果土地占有者是雇主，根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），该占有者还需对其雇员在工作期间的过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n在本案中，该男子进入药店购物时属于受邀者；但他未经许可故意走到收银台后面时，便转而成为侵入者（选项 D）。\n\n尽管如此，店员仍对他负有警告义务，因为她知道他在那里，并且有理由相信他并未察觉那个隐蔽的敞开式竖井（选项 A）。\n\n由于她未向该男子发出警告，导致其跌入竖井并受伤，该男子很可能因店员（雇员）的过失而获得针对药店（雇主）的胜诉判决。\n\n*约半数司法管辖区遵循上述传统原则。\n\n其余半数则遵循现代原则，即土地占有者对所有进入者（恶劣的侵入者除外）均负有合理的注意义务。\n\n（选项 B）尽管该男子的伤害并非源于药店故意或肆意（即蓄意或鲁莽）的不当行为，但根据“雇主责任原则”，药店仍需对其雇员的过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n教育目标：土地占有者对已知或可预见的侵入者负有以下义务：(1) 针对那些土地占有者已知但侵入者不太可能发现的隐蔽的人造危险发出警告；(2) 在进行主动操作时尽到合理的注意义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第337条（美国法律学会，1965年）（针对已知侵入者的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，因为店员有理由相信该男子并未察觉那个敞开的竖井。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"failure to warn","ipa":"/fˈeɪljɝ tˈu wˈɔɹn/","syllables":"fail·ure to warn","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"警示不足：未对不可避免风险提供充分指示或警告","contextualMeaning":"警示不足：未对不可避免风险提供充分指示或警告","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"And since her failure to warn the man caused him to fall into the shaft and suffer harm, the man will likely recover a judgment from the drug store (employer) for the clerk's (employee's) negligence.","synonyms":["warning defect","inadequate warning"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-62f825ae654bfa61"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":134,"sourceQuestionPage":400,"sourceQuestionPages":[400],"sourceExplanationPages":[401,402],"sourceAnswerPages":[401,402],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-135","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A hotel employed a carefully selected independent contractor to rebuild its swimming pool. The hotel continued to operate while the pool was being rebuilt. The contract between the hotel and the contractor required the contractor to indemnify the hotel for any liability arising from the contractor's negligent acts. A guest of the hotel fell into the excavation, which the contractor had negligently left unguarded. In an action by the guest against the hotel to recover for his injuries, what would be the most likely outcome?","choices":{"A":"Liability, because the contract between the hotel and the contractor required the contractor to indemnify the hotel for any liability arising from the contractor's negligent acts.","B":"Liability, because the hotel had a nondelegable duty to the guest to keep a safe premises.","C":"No liability, because the contractor was the actively negligent party.","D":"No liability, because the hotel exercised reasonable care in employing the contractor."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for torts committed by its independent contractor. However, vicarious liability will be imposed if the independent contractor's work breaches a nondelegable duty owed by the principal—ie, a duty retained by the principal even when the performance of that duty is assigned to an independent contractor. This includes the duty of a land possessor that holds its land open to the public to keep the premises safe for business visitors. Here, the hotel hired an independent contractor to rebuild its swimming pool. While performing that work, the contractor negligently left the excavation unguarded. A guest then fell into the excavation and sustained injuries. And since the hotel had a nondelegable duty to keep the premises safe for its guests, the hotel is vicariously liable for the guest's injuries caused by the contractor's negligence. (Choice A) The contractor's agreement to indemnify (ie, reimburse) the hotel for any liability arising from the contractor's negligent acts does not affect the hotel's liability to its guests. Instead, the indemnity agreement provides a basis for the hotel to seek reimbursement from the contractor if the guest's suit is successful. (Choice C) Active negligence is negligence resulting from an affirmative act (eg, rebuilding the swimming pool), while passive negligence is negligence resulting from inaction (eg, failing to maintain safe premises). However, these distinctions are outdated and, even if they applied, merely address the apportionment of damages between multiple defendants—not one defendant's liability to the plaintiff. (Choice D) The fact that the hotel exercised reasonable care in employing the contractor shows that the hotel is not directly liable for its own negligence in hiring an incompetent worker. But the hotel is still vicariously liable for the contractor's negligence since the hotel owed its guests a nondelegable duty to maintain safe premises.\n\nEducational objective: A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the independent contractor breaches a nondelegable duty of care owed by the principal—eg, a land possessor's duty to keep the premises safe for business visitors.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 57 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (no vicarious liability generally for independent contractor's torts).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 344 (nondelegable duty to keep business premises safe).","educationalObjective":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the independent contractor breaches a nondelegable duty of care owed by the principal—eg, a land possessor's duty to keep the premises safe for business visitors.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 57 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (no vicarious liability generally for independent contractor's torts).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 344 (nondelegable duty to keep business premises safe).","rule":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the independent contractor breaches a nondelegable duty of care owed by the principal—eg, a land possessor's duty to keep the premises safe for business visitors.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the guest against the hotel to recover for his injuries, what would be the most likely outcome?","rule":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for its independent contractor's torts. However, vicarious liability will be imposed when the independent contractor breaches a nondelegable duty of care owed by the principal—eg, a land possessor's duty to keep the premises safe for business visitors.","analysis":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for torts committed by its independent contractor. However, vicarious liability will be imposed if the independent contractor's work breaches a nondelegable duty owed by the principal—ie, a duty retained by the principal even when the performance of that duty is assigned to an independent contractor. This includes the duty of a land possessor that holds its land open to the public to keep the premises safe for business visitors. Here, the hotel hired an independent contractor to rebuild its swimming pool. While performing that work, the contractor negligently left the excavation unguarded. A guest then fell into the excavation and sustained injuries. And since the hotel had a nondelegable duty to keep the premises safe for its guests, the hotel is vicariously liable for the guest's injuries caused by the contractor's negligence. (Choice A) The contractor's agreement to indemnify (ie, reimburse) the hotel for any liability arising from the contractor's negligent acts does not affect the hotel's liability to its guests. Instead, the indemnity agreement provides a basis for the hotel to seek reimbursement from the contractor if the guest's suit is successful. (Choice C) Active negligence is negligence resulting from an affirmative act (eg, rebuilding the swimming pool), while passive negligence is negligence resulting from inaction (eg, failing to maintain safe premises). However, these distinctions are outdated and, even if they applied, merely address the apportionment of damages between multiple defendants—not one defendant's liability to the plaintiff. (Choice D) The fact that the hotel exercised reasonable care in employing the contractor shows that the hotel is not directly liable for its own negligence in hiring an incompetent worker. But the hotel is still vicariously liable for the contractor's negligence since the hotel owed its guests a nondelegable duty to maintain safe premises.","conclusion":"Answer B: Liability, because the hotel had a nondelegable duty to the guest to keep a safe premises."},"questionZh":"一家酒店聘请了一位经过慎重挑选的独立承包商来重建其游泳池。\n\n在泳池重建期间，酒店继续营业。\n\n酒店与承包商之间的合同规定，对于因承包商的疏忽行为而产生的任何责任，承包商须向酒店承担赔偿责任。\n\n一名酒店住客跌入了挖掘坑中，而该坑是承包商因疏忽大意而未加防护的。\n\n在该住客针对酒店提起的损害赔偿诉讼中，最可能的结果是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"承担责任，因为酒店与承包商之间的合同规定，对于因承包商的疏忽行为而产生的任何责任，承包商须向酒店承担赔偿责任。","B":"承担责任，因为酒店对住客负有维持场所安全的不可转让义务。","C":"不承担责任，因为承包商是实施疏忽行为的一方。","D":"不承担责任，因为酒店在聘请承包商时已尽到了合理的注意义务。"},"explanationZh":"通常情况下，委托人不对其独立承包商实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，如果独立承包商的工作违反了委托人负有的“不可转让义务”（即即便将履行该义务的工作委派给独立承包商，委托人仍须保留的义务），则委托人须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n这包括向公众开放其土地的土地占有者有义务确保场所对商业访客而言是安全的。\n\n在本案中，酒店聘请了一家独立承包商来重建游泳池。\n\n在施工过程中，承包商疏忽大意，未对挖掘坑洞采取防护措施。\n\n随后，一名客人跌入坑洞并受伤。\n\n由于酒店对客人负有确保场所安全的不可转让义务，因此酒店须对承包商因疏忽导致客人受伤的行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 A）承包商同意就其疏忽行为引起的任何责任向酒店进行赔偿（即补偿）的协议，并不影响酒店对客人应负的责任。\n\n相反，该赔偿协议为酒店在客人胜诉后向承包商追偿提供了依据。\n\n（选项 C）“积极过失”是指因主动行为（例如重建游泳池）而导致的过失，而“消极过失”是指因不作为（例如未能维护场所安全）而导致的过失。\n\n然而，这些区分已过时；即便适用这些区分，它们也仅涉及多个被告之间的损害赔偿分摊问题，而非单一被告对原告应负的责任问题。\n\n（选项 D）酒店在聘请承包商时尽到了合理的注意义务，这一事实表明，酒店无需因聘用不称职人员的自身过失而承担直接责任。\n\n但由于酒店对客人负有维护场所安全的不可转让义务，它仍须对承包商的过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n教育目标：通常情况下，委托人不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当独立承包商违反了委托人负有的不可转让注意义务时（例如土地占有者有义务确保场所对商业访客安全），委托人须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与情感伤害》第57条（美国法律协会，2012年）（一般而言，不对独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第344条（维持营业场所安全的不可转让义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"委托人通常不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当独立承包商违反了委托人所负有的不可转让的注意义务时（例如，土地占有人有义务确保场所对商业访客而言是安全的），委托人须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","iracZh":{"issue":"在该住客针对酒店提起的损害赔偿诉讼中，最可能的结果是什么？","rule":"委托人通常不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当独立承包商违反了委托人所负有的不可转让的注意义务时（例如，土地占有人有义务确保场所对商业访客而言是安全的），委托人须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","analysis":"通常情况下，委托人不对其独立承包商实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，如果独立承包商的工作违反了委托人负有的“不可转让义务”（即即便将履行该义务的工作委派给独立承包商，委托人仍须保留的义务），则委托人须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n这包括向公众开放其土地的土地占有者有义务确保场所对商业访客而言是安全的。\n\n在本案中，酒店聘请了一家独立承包商来重建游泳池。\n\n在施工过程中，承包商疏忽大意，未对挖掘坑洞采取防护措施。\n\n随后，一名客人跌入坑洞并受伤。\n\n由于酒店对客人负有确保场所安全的不可转让义务，因此酒店须对承包商因疏忽导致客人受伤的行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 A）承包商同意就其疏忽行为引起的任何责任向酒店进行赔偿（即补偿）的协议，并不影响酒店对客人应负的责任。\n\n相反，该赔偿协议为酒店在客人胜诉后向承包商追偿提供了依据。\n\n（选项 C）“积极过失”是指因主动行为（例如重建游泳池）而导致的过失，而“消极过失”是指因不作为（例如未能维护场所安全）而导致的过失。\n\n然而，这些区分已过时；即便适用这些区分，它们也仅涉及多个被告之间的损害赔偿分摊问题，而非单一被告对原告应负的责任问题。\n\n（选项 D）酒店在聘请承包商时尽到了合理的注意义务，这一事实表明，酒店无需因聘用不称职人员的自身过失而承担直接责任。\n\n但由于酒店对客人负有维护场所安全的不可转让义务，它仍须对承包商的过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n教育目标：通常情况下，委托人不对其独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n然而，当独立承包商违反了委托人负有的不可转让注意义务时（例如土地占有者有义务确保场所对商业访客安全），委托人须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与情感伤害》第57条（美国法律协会，2012年）（一般而言，不对独立承包商的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第344条（维持营业场所安全的不可转让义务）。","conclusion":"答案 B：承担责任，因为酒店对住客负有维持场所安全的不可转让义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"indemnity","ipa":"/ˌɪndˈɛmnəti/","syllables":"in·dem·nity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 赔偿, 保障, 补偿, 赦免, 保护；[经] 赔偿, 补偿, 赔偿金","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“indemnity”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:indemnities","sourceSentence":"Instead, the indemnity agreement provides a basis for the hotel to seek reimbursement from the contractor if the guest's suit is successful.","synonyms":["damages","amends","indemnification","restitution"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-58352992d6d8c5ad"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":135,"sourceQuestionPage":403,"sourceQuestionPages":[403],"sourceExplanationPages":[404,405],"sourceAnswerPages":[404,405],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-136","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"In an action by a man against a pharmacy, the man offered only the following evidence: The man took a clearly written prescription to a pharmacy. The pharmacy's employee filled the prescription by providing pills with 30 milligrams of the active ingredient instead of 20 milligrams, as was prescribed. Shortly after taking the pills as directed, the man, who had no previous history of heart problems, suffered a heart attack. Overdoses of the active ingredient had previously been associated with heart problems. Does the man have a valid claim against the pharmacy?","choices":{"A":"No, because pharmacies are not strictly liable for injuries caused by incorrectly filled prescriptions.","B":"No, because the man offered no specific proof as to the pharmacy's negligence.","C":"Yes, because a jury could reasonably conclude that the man would not have suffered a heart attack had the pharmacist provided the correct dosage.","D":"Yes, because by providing the 30-milligram pills rather than the 20-milligram pills, the pharmacy sold the man a defective product."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Vicarious liability\n\n(liability for tortious acts of another)\n\nRespondeat Employee's conduct occurred within scope of employment—ie, under superior employer's control or while performing assigned work\n\nNondelegable Independent contractor's work involved abnormally or inherently duty dangerous activity, condition on land open to general public, or activity regulated by law\n\nParent Child committed tort while acting as parent's agent\n\nBusiness partner Business partner's conduct occurred within scope of business\n\nAutomobile Family member drove car with permission (family-car doctrine) or owner any person drove car with permission under owner-liability statute\n\nDram shop* & Alcohol sold (dram shop) or served at noncommercial setting (social social host liability) to visibly intoxicated person or minor\n\n*Only applies when question provides dram-shop statute.\n\nA valid negligence claim requires proof that another's failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff harm. Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for its employee's negligence if the negligent act occurred within the scope of the employment relationship—ie, while (1) performing assigned work or (2) engaging in conduct subject to the employer's control. Here, the pharmacist (employee) filled the man's prescription (performing assigned work) with 30 milligrams of the active ingredient instead of 20 milligrams, as prescribed. That ingredient had been associated with heart problems. Since the man had no history of heart problems, he can likely prove that he would not have suffered a heart attack had the pharmacist used reasonable care to provide the correct dosage. And since the pharmacy (employer) is vicariously liable for the pharmacist's negligence, the man has a valid negligence claim against the pharmacy. (Choice A) Pharmacies are generally not strictly liable (liable without proof of fault) for harm caused by incorrectly filled prescriptions since they are considered service providers—not commercial sellers. But the man still has a valid negligence claim against the pharmacy under vicarious liability principles. (Choice B) An employer can be directly liable for its own negligence (eg, hiring an incompetent employee) or vicariously liable for its employee's negligence. Therefore, the\n\nman does not need to offer specific proof as to the pharmacy's negligence since he has a valid claim against the pharmacy based on its pharmacist's negligence. (Choice D) A product is defective when it contains a manufacturing defect, design defect, or inadequate warning/instruction at the time of sale or distribution. But proof that a product is defective is only necessary for a products liability claim—not a negligence claim. And since pharmacies (service providers) are generally not subject to products liability, the man will not prevail on this basis.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for torts committed by its employees while acting within the scope of employment—ie, performing assigned work or engaging in conduct subject to the employer's control.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Agency § 2.04 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (respondeat superior).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Agency § 7.07 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (scope of employment).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 282 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (definition of negligence).","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for torts committed by its employees while acting within the scope of employment—ie, performing assigned work or engaging in conduct subject to the employer's control.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Agency § 2.04 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (respondeat superior).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Agency § 7.07 (Am. Law Inst. 2006) (scope of employment).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 282 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (definition of negligence).","rule":"Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for torts committed by its employees while acting within the scope of employment—ie, performing assigned work or engaging in conduct subject to the employer's control.","irac":{"issue":"Does the man have a valid claim against the pharmacy?","rule":"Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for torts committed by its employees while acting within the scope of employment—ie, performing assigned work or engaging in conduct subject to the employer's control.","analysis":"Vicarious liability\n\n(liability for tortious acts of another)\n\nRespondeat Employee's conduct occurred within scope of employment—ie, under superior employer's control or while performing assigned work\n\nNondelegable Independent contractor's work involved abnormally or inherently duty dangerous activity, condition on land open to general public, or activity regulated by law\n\nParent Child committed tort while acting as parent's agent\n\nBusiness partner Business partner's conduct occurred within scope of business\n\nAutomobile Family member drove car with permission (family-car doctrine) or owner any person drove car with permission under owner-liability statute\n\nDram shop* & Alcohol sold (dram shop) or served at noncommercial setting (social social host liability) to visibly intoxicated person or minor\n\n*Only applies when question provides dram-shop statute.\n\nA valid negligence claim requires proof that another's failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff harm. Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, an employer is vicariously liable for its employee's negligence if the negligent act occurred within the scope of the employment relationship—ie, while (1) performing assigned work or (2) engaging in conduct subject to the employer's control. Here, the pharmacist (employee) filled the man's prescription (performing assigned work) with 30 milligrams of the active ingredient instead of 20 milligrams, as prescribed. That ingredient had been associated with heart problems. Since the man had no history of heart problems, he can likely prove that he would not have suffered a heart attack had the pharmacist used reasonable care to provide the correct dosage. And since the pharmacy (employer) is vicariously liable for the pharmacist's negligence, the man has a valid negligence claim against the pharmacy. (Choice A) Pharmacies are generally not strictly liable (liable without proof of fault) for harm caused by incorrectly filled prescriptions since they are considered service providers—not commercial sellers. But the man still has a valid negligence claim against the pharmacy under vicarious liability principles. (Choice B) An employer can be directly liable for its own negligence (eg, hiring an incompetent employee) or vicariously liable for its employee's negligence. Therefore, the\n\nman does not need to offer specific proof as to the pharmacy's negligence since he has a valid claim against the pharmacy based on its pharmacist's negligence. (Choice D) A product is defective when it contains a manufacturing defect, design defect, or inadequate warning/instruction at the time of sale or distribution. But proof that a product is defective is only necessary for a products liability claim—not a negligence claim. And since pharmacies (service providers) are generally not subject to products liability, the man will not prevail on this basis.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because a jury could reasonably conclude that the man would not have suffered a heart attack had the pharmacist provided the correct dosage."},"questionZh":"一名男子起诉一家药房，他提供的证据只有以下几点：该男子拿着一张清晰的处方去药房取药。\n\n药房员工配药时，误将有效成分含量为20毫克的药片配成了30毫克。\n\n该男子此前并无心脏病史，服药后不久便突发心脏病。\n\n此前已有研究表明，过量服用该有效成分会导致心脏病。\n\n该男子是否可以对药房提起诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为药房对因配药错误造成的伤害不承担严格责任。","B":"否，因为该男子没有提供任何关于药房存在过失的具体证据。","C":"是，因为陪审团可以合理地得出结论：如果药剂师提供了正确的剂量，该男子就不会心脏病发作。","D":"是的，因为药房向该男子提供了 30 毫克的药片而非 20 毫克的药片，所以药房出售的是有缺陷的产品。"},"explanationZh":"替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability） （对他人的侵权行为承担责任） 雇主责任（Respondeat Superior） 雇员的行为发生在雇佣范围内——即在雇主的控制之下，或在执行指派工作期间 不可转授的义务 独立承包商的工作涉及异常危险或固有危险的活动、向公众开放的土地上的状况，或受法律监管的活动 父母与子女 子女在作为父母代理人行事时实施了侵权行为 商业合伙人 合伙人的行为发生在业务范围内 汽车 家庭成员经许可驾驶汽车（家庭用车原则），或根据车主责任法规，任何人经车主许可驾驶汽车 酒馆责任（Dram shop）* 与社交东道主责任 向明显醉酒者或未成年人出售（酒馆责任）或提供（社交东道主责任）酒精饮料 *仅在题目提供酒馆责任法规时适用。\n\n有效的过失索赔需要证明他人未尽合理注意义务，从而导致原告受到伤害。\n\n根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），如果雇员的过失行为发生在雇佣关系范围内——即在（1）执行指派工作或（2）从事受雇主控制的行为期间——雇主须对雇员的过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n在本案中，药剂师（雇员）在配药时（执行指派工作），将处方要求的20毫克活性成分配成了30毫克。\n\n该成分与心脏问题有关。\n\n由于该男子没有心脏病史，他很可能能够证明，如果药剂师尽到合理注意义务并提供了正确剂量，他就不会遭受心脏病发作。\n\n鉴于药房（雇主）对药剂师的过失负有替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），该男子针对药房拥有有效的过失索赔权。\n\n（选项 A）药房通常不对因错误配药造成的伤害承担严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担责任），因为它们被视为服务提供者，而非商业销售商。\n\n但根据替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）原则，该男子仍可针对药房提出有效的过失索赔。\n\n（选项 B）雇主既可能因自身过失（例如聘用不称职的雇员）而承担直接责任，也可能因雇员的过失而承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n因此， 该男子无需提供关于药房自身存在过失的具体证据，因为基于其药剂师的过失，他已对该药房拥有有效的索赔依据。\n\n（选项 D）当产品在销售或分销时存在制造缺陷、设计缺陷或警示/说明不充分的情况，即被视为有缺陷。\n\n然而，证明产品存在缺陷仅在产品责任索赔中是必要的，而非过失索赔。\n\n鉴于药房（作为服务提供者）通常不承担产品责任，该男子无法基于此理由胜诉。\n\n教育目标：根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），如果雇员在履行职务范围内（即执行指派工作或从事受雇主控制的行为）实施了侵权行为，雇主须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n参考文献 《代理法重述（第三版）》第 2.04 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（雇主责任原则）。\n\n《代理法重述（第三版）》第 7.07 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（职务范围）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 282 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（过失的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据雇主责任原则，雇主对其雇员在受雇范围内（即履行指派的工作或从事受雇主控制的行为）实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","iracZh":{"issue":"该男子是否可以对药房提出有效的索赔？","rule":"根据雇主责任原则，雇主对其雇员在受雇范围内（即履行指派的工作或从事受雇主控制的行为）实施的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","analysis":"替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability） （对他人的侵权行为承担责任） 雇主责任（Respondeat Superior） 雇员的行为发生在雇佣范围内——即在雇主的控制之下，或在执行指派工作期间 不可转授的义务 独立承包商的工作涉及异常危险或固有危险的活动、向公众开放的土地上的状况，或受法律监管的活动 父母与子女 子女在作为父母代理人行事时实施了侵权行为 商业合伙人 合伙人的行为发生在业务范围内 汽车 家庭成员经许可驾驶汽车（家庭用车原则），或根据车主责任法规，任何人经车主许可驾驶汽车 酒馆责任（Dram shop）* 与社交东道主责任 向明显醉酒者或未成年人出售（酒馆责任）或提供（社交东道主责任）酒精饮料 *仅在题目提供酒馆责任法规时适用。\n\n有效的过失索赔需要证明他人未尽合理注意义务，从而导致原告受到伤害。\n\n根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），如果雇员的过失行为发生在雇佣关系范围内——即在（1）执行指派工作或（2）从事受雇主控制的行为期间——雇主须对雇员的过失承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n在本案中，药剂师（雇员）在配药时（执行指派工作），将处方要求的20毫克活性成分配成了30毫克。\n\n该成分与心脏问题有关。\n\n由于该男子没有心脏病史，他很可能能够证明，如果药剂师尽到合理注意义务并提供了正确剂量，他就不会遭受心脏病发作。\n\n鉴于药房（雇主）对药剂师的过失负有替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），该男子针对药房拥有有效的过失索赔权。\n\n（选项 A）药房通常不对因错误配药造成的伤害承担严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担责任），因为它们被视为服务提供者，而非商业销售商。\n\n但根据替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）原则，该男子仍可针对药房提出有效的过失索赔。\n\n（选项 B）雇主既可能因自身过失（例如聘用不称职的雇员）而承担直接责任，也可能因雇员的过失而承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n因此， 该男子无需提供关于药房自身存在过失的具体证据，因为基于其药剂师的过失，他已对该药房拥有有效的索赔依据。\n\n（选项 D）当产品在销售或分销时存在制造缺陷、设计缺陷或警示/说明不充分的情况，即被视为有缺陷。\n\n然而，证明产品存在缺陷仅在产品责任索赔中是必要的，而非过失索赔。\n\n鉴于药房（作为服务提供者）通常不承担产品责任，该男子无法基于此理由胜诉。\n\n教育目标：根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），如果雇员在履行职务范围内（即执行指派工作或从事受雇主控制的行为）实施了侵权行为，雇主须承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n参考文献 《代理法重述（第三版）》第 2.04 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（雇主责任原则）。\n\n《代理法重述（第三版）》第 7.07 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2006)（职务范围）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 282 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1979)（过失的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是，因为陪审团可以合理地得出结论：如果药剂师提供了正确的剂量，该男子就不会心脏病发作。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"manufacturing defect","ipa":"/mˌænjʌfˈæktʃɝɪŋ dˈifɛkt/","syllables":"man·u·fac·tur·ing de·fect","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"制造缺陷：产品偏离其设计或同批正常产品","contextualMeaning":"制造缺陷：产品偏离其设计或同批正常产品","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) A product is defective when it contains a manufacturing defect, design defect, or inadequate warning/instruction at the time of sale or distribution.","synonyms":["production defect"]},{"term":"design defect","ipa":"/dɪzˈaɪn dˈifɛkt/","syllables":"de·sign de·fect","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"设计缺陷：产品设计本身造成不合理危险","contextualMeaning":"设计缺陷：产品设计本身造成不合理危险","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) A product is defective when it contains a manufacturing defect, design defect, or inadequate warning/instruction at the time of sale or distribution.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"distribution","ipa":"/dˌɪstɹʌbjˈuʃən/","syllables":"dis·tri·b·u·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 分配；[计] 分布","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“distribution”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:distributions","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) A product is defective when it contains a manufacturing defect, design defect, or inadequate warning/instruction at the time of sale or distribution.","synonyms":["dispersion","statistical distribution"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":136,"sourceQuestionPage":406,"sourceQuestionPages":[406],"sourceExplanationPages":[407,408],"sourceAnswerPages":[407,408],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-137","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A quarterback and a linebacker were competing against one another in a professional football game. At one point during the game, the quarterback attempted to throw the football to one of his teammates. At the same time, the linebacker was chasing the quarterback in order to tackle him before the ball could be thrown. Just as the linebacker was about to tackle the quarterback, the referee blew her whistle, indicating that the play was over and that the players should stop what they were doing. Nevertheless, the linebacker tackled the quarterback two seconds after the whistle was blown and broke the quarterback's arm. If the quarterback files a negligence action against the linebacker, will the quarterback be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the linebacker did not intend to break the quarterback's arm.","B":"No, because the quarterback assumed the risk of breaking his arm by playing football.","C":"Yes, because the linebacker breached the duty of care he owed to the quarterback.","D":"Yes, because the linebacker tackled the quarterback after the referee blew the whistle."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Assumption of risk\n\n(tort defense)\n\nApplicability Public policy limitations\n\nExpress Plaintiff assented to liability waiver intended to Not a defense when cover type of conduct that caused plaintiff's harm defendant:\n\nis plaintiff's employer is a hotel or common carrier is a public servant/service has substantially more bargaining power Implied Plaintiff voluntarily accepted known risk of harm Not a defense when plaintiff:\n\nsuffered intentional harm is member of statutorily protected class A defendant is liable for negligence if he/she breaches a duty of care owed to the plaintiff and causes the plaintiff harm. However, assumption of the risk is a complete defense to negligence liability. Implied assumption of the risk arises when the plaintiff voluntarily engages in an activity with the knowledge that it carries inherent risks. For example, an athlete who participates in a sport impliedly assumes the inherent risks of that sport.* Here, the linebacker may have breached the duty of care he owed to the quarterback by tackling him after the referee blew the whistle. However, this is the type of risk that is inherent to the sport of football, especially since the linebacker tackled the quarterback only two seconds after the whistle was blown. Therefore, the quarterback assumed the risk of being injured by a tackle while playing football, and he is unlikely to prevail in a negligence action (Choices C & D). *Spectators similarly assume certain risks inherent in watching the sport (eg, the risk of being struck by a game ball). (Choice A) Intent is irrelevant to negligence, which merely requires proof that the defendant's conduct fell below the applicable standard of care.\n\nEducational objective: Implied assumption of the risk is a complete defense to negligence liability when the plaintiff voluntarily engages in an activity (eg, football) with the knowledge that it carries inherent risks (eg, risk of being injured during a tackle).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 496A-496C (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (assumption of risk principles).","educationalObjective":"Implied assumption of the risk is a complete defense to negligence liability when the plaintiff voluntarily engages in an activity (eg, football) with the knowledge that it carries inherent risks (eg, risk of being injured during a tackle).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 496A-496C (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (assumption of risk principles).","rule":"Implied assumption of the risk is a complete defense to negligence liability when the plaintiff voluntarily engages in an activity (eg, football) with the knowledge that it carries inherent risks (eg, risk of being injured during a tackle).","irac":{"issue":"If the quarterback files a negligence action against the linebacker, will the quarterback be likely to prevail?","rule":"Implied assumption of the risk is a complete defense to negligence liability when the plaintiff voluntarily engages in an activity (eg, football) with the knowledge that it carries inherent risks (eg, risk of being injured during a tackle).","analysis":"Assumption of risk\n\n(tort defense)\n\nApplicability Public policy limitations\n\nExpress Plaintiff assented to liability waiver intended to Not a defense when cover type of conduct that caused plaintiff's harm defendant:\n\nis plaintiff's employer is a hotel or common carrier is a public servant/service has substantially more bargaining power Implied Plaintiff voluntarily accepted known risk of harm Not a defense when plaintiff:\n\nsuffered intentional harm is member of statutorily protected class A defendant is liable for negligence if he/she breaches a duty of care owed to the plaintiff and causes the plaintiff harm. However, assumption of the risk is a complete defense to negligence liability. Implied assumption of the risk arises when the plaintiff voluntarily engages in an activity with the knowledge that it carries inherent risks. For example, an athlete who participates in a sport impliedly assumes the inherent risks of that sport.* Here, the linebacker may have breached the duty of care he owed to the quarterback by tackling him after the referee blew the whistle. However, this is the type of risk that is inherent to the sport of football, especially since the linebacker tackled the quarterback only two seconds after the whistle was blown. Therefore, the quarterback assumed the risk of being injured by a tackle while playing football, and he is unlikely to prevail in a negligence action (Choices C & D). *Spectators similarly assume certain risks inherent in watching the sport (eg, the risk of being struck by a game ball). (Choice A) Intent is irrelevant to negligence, which merely requires proof that the defendant's conduct fell below the applicable standard of care.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the quarterback assumed the risk of breaking his arm by playing football."},"questionZh":"一名四分卫和一名线卫正在参加一场职业橄榄球比赛。\n\n比赛中，四分卫试图将球传给队友。\n\n与此同时，线卫正在追赶四分卫，准备在他出手前将其擒抱。\n\n就在线卫即将擒抱四分卫时，裁判吹响了哨子，示意比赛结束，球员们应该停止比赛。\n\n然而，哨声响起两秒钟后，线卫仍然擒抱了四分卫，导致四分卫手臂骨折。\n\n如果四分卫对线卫提起过失诉讼，他胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为线卫并非故意要弄断四分卫的手臂。","B":"不可能，因为四分卫在参加橄榄球比赛时就承担了手臂骨折的风险。","C":"是的，因为线卫违反了他对四分卫应尽的注意义务。","D":"是的，因为线卫在裁判吹哨后仍然擒抱了四分卫。"},"explanationZh":"风险自担 （侵权法抗辩） 适用性及公共政策限制 明示：原告同意放弃追究责任（针对导致其受损的行为类型）。若被告属于以下情形，则该抗辩无效： 原告的雇主；酒店或公共承运人；公职人员/公共服务提供者；拥有显著更强的议价能力。默示：原告自愿承担已知的伤害风险。若原告属于以下情形，则该抗辩无效： 遭受故意伤害；属于受法律特别保护的群体。若被告违反了对原告负有的注意义务并导致原告受损，则须承担过失责任。\n\n然而，“风险自担”是针对过失责任的完全抗辩。\n\n当原告在明知某项活动存在固有风险的情况下自愿参与该活动时，即产生默示风险自担。\n\n例如，参与某项运动的运动员默示承担了该运动固有的风险。* 在本案中，线卫（linebacker）在裁判鸣哨后仍擒抱四分卫（quarterback），这可能违反了他对四分卫负有的注意义务。\n\n然而，这种风险属于美式足球运动固有的风险，尤其是考虑到线卫是在鸣哨后仅两秒钟进行的擒抱。\n\n因此，四分卫在参与足球运动时已承担了被擒抱受伤的风险，他在过失诉讼中胜诉的可能性不大（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n* 观众在观看体育比赛时也同样承担了某些固有风险（例如，被比赛用球击中的风险）。\n\n（选项 A）意图与过失责任无关；过失责任仅要求证明被告的行为未达到适用的注意标准。\n\n教育目标：当原告在明知某项活动（如美式足球）存在固有风险（如被擒抱受伤的风险）的情况下自愿参与该活动时，默示风险自担构成针对过失责任的完全抗辩。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 496A-496C 条（美国法律协会，1981 年）（风险自担原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当原告明知某项活动（例如橄榄球）存在固有风险（例如在擒抱中受伤）而自愿参与时，默示承担风险是过失责任的完全抗辩理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果四分卫对线卫提起过失诉讼，他胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"当原告明知某项活动（例如橄榄球）存在固有风险（例如在擒抱中受伤）而自愿参与时，默示承担风险是过失责任的完全抗辩理由。","analysis":"风险自担 （侵权法抗辩） 适用性及公共政策限制 明示：原告同意放弃追究责任（针对导致其受损的行为类型）。若被告属于以下情形，则该抗辩无效： 原告的雇主；酒店或公共承运人；公职人员/公共服务提供者；拥有显著更强的议价能力。默示：原告自愿承担已知的伤害风险。若原告属于以下情形，则该抗辩无效： 遭受故意伤害；属于受法律特别保护的群体。若被告违反了对原告负有的注意义务并导致原告受损，则须承担过失责任。\n\n然而，“风险自担”是针对过失责任的完全抗辩。\n\n当原告在明知某项活动存在固有风险的情况下自愿参与该活动时，即产生默示风险自担。\n\n例如，参与某项运动的运动员默示承担了该运动固有的风险。* 在本案中，线卫（linebacker）在裁判鸣哨后仍擒抱四分卫（quarterback），这可能违反了他对四分卫负有的注意义务。\n\n然而，这种风险属于美式足球运动固有的风险，尤其是考虑到线卫是在鸣哨后仅两秒钟进行的擒抱。\n\n因此，四分卫在参与足球运动时已承担了被擒抱受伤的风险，他在过失诉讼中胜诉的可能性不大（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n* 观众在观看体育比赛时也同样承担了某些固有风险（例如，被比赛用球击中的风险）。\n\n（选项 A）意图与过失责任无关；过失责任仅要求证明被告的行为未达到适用的注意标准。\n\n教育目标：当原告在明知某项活动（如美式足球）存在固有风险（如被擒抱受伤的风险）的情况下自愿参与该活动时，默示风险自担构成针对过失责任的完全抗辩。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 496A-496C 条（美国法律协会，1981 年）（风险自担原则）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可能，因为四分卫在参加橄榄球比赛时就承担了手臂骨折的风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":137,"sourceQuestionPage":409,"sourceQuestionPages":[409],"sourceExplanationPages":[410,411],"sourceAnswerPages":[410,411],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-138","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"After her husband died in a hospital, a widow directed the hospital to send her husband's body to a funeral home for burial. The hospital negligently misidentified the husband's body and sent it to be cremated. When she was informed of the hospital's mistake, the widow suffered serious emotional distress. She has sued the hospital. Is the hospital likely to be held liable to the widow?","choices":{"A":"No, because the widow did not witness the cremation.","B":"No, because the widow was never in any danger of bodily harm.","C":"Yes, because hospitals are strictly liable if they do not properly dispose of corpses.","D":"Yes, because the negligent handling of the husband's body was especially likely to cause his widow serious emotional distress."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress\n\nTheories of Required elements recovery\n\nZone of danger Defendant’s negligence put plaintiff in danger of immediate bodily harm Danger caused plaintiff serious emotional distress Bystander Defendant negligently injured plaintiff’s close relative Plaintiff contemporaneously perceived that event Event caused plaintiff serious emotional distress Special situations Defendant negligently: delivered erroneous announcement of death or illness mishandled corpse or bodily remains contaminated food with repulsive foreign object Caused plaintiff serious emotional distress A plaintiff can recover for negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) under one of three theories: (1) zone of danger, (2) bystander, or (3) special situations. The special- situations theory applies to three scenarios where the defendant's negligence is especially likely to cause the plaintiff serious emotional harm. Liability arises under this theory when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently: delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness mishandled a corpse or bodily remains or contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object (eg, a used condom in a soda bottle). Here, the hospital negligently mishandled the husband's corpse by mistakenly cremating it. And since the widow suffered serious emotional distress upon learning of that mistake, the hospital will likely be liable to the widow for NIED. (Choice A) A plaintiff must contemporaneously perceive an event to recover under the bystander theory for NIED. Since the widow did not witness the cremation, she cannot recover under that theory. However, she can still prevail under the special-situations theory, which does not require contemporaneous perception. (Choice B) A plaintiff must be placed in danger of immediate bodily harm to recover under the zone-of-danger theory for NIED. The widow cannot recover under this theory since she was never in danger of bodily harm. However, she can still prevail under the special- situations theory, which does not require imminent physical danger.\n\n(Choice C) A defendant may be strictly liable (ie, liable without a finding of fault) for physical harm caused by his/her participation in an abnormally dangerous activity (eg, storing explosives in a thickly populated area). Although the mishandling of corpses will likely cause serious emotional distress, it is not considered an abnormally dangerous activity. Therefore, the hospital is not strictly liable for improperly disposing of the husband's corpse.\n\nEducational objective: Negligent infliction of emotional distress occurs under the special-situations theory when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently (1) delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness, (2) mishandled a corpse or bodily remains, or (3) contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 47(b), cmt. f (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (special-situations theory of negligent infliction of emotional distress).","educationalObjective":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress occurs under the special-situations theory when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently (1) delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness, (2) mishandled a corpse or bodily remains, or (3) contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 47(b), cmt. f (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (special-situations theory of negligent infliction of emotional distress).","rule":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress occurs under the special-situations theory when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently (1) delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness, (2) mishandled a corpse or bodily remains, or (3) contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object.","irac":{"issue":"Is the hospital likely to be held liable to the widow?","rule":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress occurs under the special-situations theory when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently (1) delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness, (2) mishandled a corpse or bodily remains, or (3) contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object.","analysis":"Negligent infliction of emotional distress\n\nTheories of Required elements recovery\n\nZone of danger Defendant’s negligence put plaintiff in danger of immediate bodily harm Danger caused plaintiff serious emotional distress Bystander Defendant negligently injured plaintiff’s close relative Plaintiff contemporaneously perceived that event Event caused plaintiff serious emotional distress Special situations Defendant negligently: delivered erroneous announcement of death or illness mishandled corpse or bodily remains contaminated food with repulsive foreign object Caused plaintiff serious emotional distress A plaintiff can recover for negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED) under one of three theories: (1) zone of danger, (2) bystander, or (3) special situations. The special- situations theory applies to three scenarios where the defendant's negligence is especially likely to cause the plaintiff serious emotional harm. Liability arises under this theory when the plaintiff suffers serious emotional distress because the defendant negligently: delivered an erroneous announcement of death or illness mishandled a corpse or bodily remains or contaminated food with a repulsive foreign object (eg, a used condom in a soda bottle). Here, the hospital negligently mishandled the husband's corpse by mistakenly cremating it. And since the widow suffered serious emotional distress upon learning of that mistake, the hospital will likely be liable to the widow for NIED. (Choice A) A plaintiff must contemporaneously perceive an event to recover under the bystander theory for NIED. Since the widow did not witness the cremation, she cannot recover under that theory. However, she can still prevail under the special-situations theory, which does not require contemporaneous perception. (Choice B) A plaintiff must be placed in danger of immediate bodily harm to recover under the zone-of-danger theory for NIED. The widow cannot recover under this theory since she was never in danger of bodily harm. However, she can still prevail under the special- situations theory, which does not require imminent physical danger.\n\n(Choice C) A defendant may be strictly liable (ie, liable without a finding of fault) for physical harm caused by his/her participation in an abnormally dangerous activity (eg, storing explosives in a thickly populated area). Although the mishandling of corpses will likely cause serious emotional distress, it is not considered an abnormally dangerous activity. Therefore, the hospital is not strictly liable for improperly disposing of the husband's corpse.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the negligent handling of the husband's body was especially likely to cause his widow serious emotional distress."},"questionZh":"丈夫在医院去世后，遗孀指示医院将其丈夫的遗体送往殡仪馆进行土葬。\n\n医院因疏忽大意，误认了其丈夫的遗体并将其送去火化。\n\n得知医院的这一错误后，该遗孀遭受了严重的精神痛苦，并起诉了医院。\n\n医院是否会被认定须向该遗孀承担责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该遗孀并未目睹火化过程。","B":"不会，因为该遗孀从未面临遭受身体伤害的危险。","C":"会，因为如果医院未能妥善处置遗体，须承担严格责任。","D":"会，因为疏忽处理其丈夫遗体的行为极有可能导致遗孀遭受严重的精神痛苦。"},"explanationZh":"因过失导致精神痛苦（NIED） 索赔的理论依据： “危险区域”理论：被告的过失使原告面临即刻遭受身体伤害的危险，且该危险导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。 “旁观者”理论：被告过失伤害了原告的近亲属，原告同时目睹了该事件，且该事件导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。 “特殊情形”理论：被告因过失（如错误发布死亡或患病通知、不当处理尸体或遗骸、或在食物中混入令人作呕的异物）导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n原告可依据以下三种理论之一就“因过失导致精神痛苦”（NIED）主张赔偿：(1) 危险区域理论，(2) 旁观者理论，或 (3) 特殊情形理论。\n\n“特殊情形”理论适用于被告的过失极有可能给原告造成严重精神伤害的三种情形。若原告因被告的过失行为——即错误发布死亡或患病通知、不当处理尸体或遗骸、或在食物中混入令人作呕的异物（例如汽水瓶中出现使用过的避孕套）——而遭受严重精神痛苦，则被告须承担责任。\n\n在本案中，医院因过失错误地火化了丈夫的尸体，处理不当；由于遗孀在得知这一错误后遭受了严重精神痛苦，医院很可能需就 NIED 向遗孀承担责任。\n\n（选项 A）依据 NIED 的“旁观者”理论索赔，原告必须同时目睹相关事件。\n\n由于遗孀未目睹火化过程，她无法依据该理论索赔。\n\n然而，她仍可依据“特殊情形”理论胜诉，因为该理论不要求同时目睹事件。\n\n（选项 B）依据 NIED 的“危险区域”理论索赔，原告必须处于面临即刻身体伤害的危险之中。\n\n由于遗孀从未面临身体伤害的危险，她无法依据该理论索赔。\n\n然而，她仍可依据“特殊情形”理论胜诉，因为该理论不要求存在迫在眉睫的身体危险。\n\n（选项 C）若被告从事异常危险的活动（例如在人口稠密地区储存爆炸物）并造成身体伤害，则可能需承担严格责任（即无需认定过错即承担责任）。\n\n尽管不当处理尸体很可能导致严重精神痛苦，但这并不被视为异常危险活动。\n\n因此，医院无需就不当处置丈夫尸体一事承担严格责任。\n\n教育目标：根据“特殊情形理论”（special-situations theory），若被告因疏忽而导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦，即构成过失致人精神痛苦（negligent infliction of emotional distress）。\n\n此类情形包括被告疏忽地：(1) 传达了关于死亡或疾病的错误通知；(2) 不当处置尸体或遗骸；或 (3) 在食物中混入令人作呕的异物。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 47(b) 条及注释 f（美国法律协会，2012年）（关于过失致人精神痛苦的特殊情形理论）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“特殊情形”理论，当原告因被告的疏忽行为而遭受严重精神痛苦时，即构成“疏忽导致精神痛苦”的侵权行为；这些疏忽行为包括：(1) 传达了关于死亡或疾病的错误通知；(2) 处置遗体或遗骸不当；或 (3) 在食物中混入令人作呕的异物。","iracZh":{"issue":"医院是否会被认定须向该遗孀承担责任？","rule":"根据“特殊情形”理论，当原告因被告的疏忽行为而遭受严重精神痛苦时，即构成“疏忽导致精神痛苦”的侵权行为；这些疏忽行为包括：(1) 传达了关于死亡或疾病的错误通知；(2) 处置遗体或遗骸不当；或 (3) 在食物中混入令人作呕的异物。","analysis":"因过失导致精神痛苦（NIED） 索赔的理论依据： “危险区域”理论：被告的过失使原告面临即刻遭受身体伤害的危险，且该危险导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。 “旁观者”理论：被告过失伤害了原告的近亲属，原告同时目睹了该事件，且该事件导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。 “特殊情形”理论：被告因过失（如错误发布死亡或患病通知、不当处理尸体或遗骸、或在食物中混入令人作呕的异物）导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦。\n\n原告可依据以下三种理论之一就“因过失导致精神痛苦”（NIED）主张赔偿：(1) 危险区域理论，(2) 旁观者理论，或 (3) 特殊情形理论。\n\n“特殊情形”理论适用于被告的过失极有可能给原告造成严重精神伤害的三种情形。若原告因被告的过失行为——即错误发布死亡或患病通知、不当处理尸体或遗骸、或在食物中混入令人作呕的异物（例如汽水瓶中出现使用过的避孕套）——而遭受严重精神痛苦，则被告须承担责任。\n\n在本案中，医院因过失错误地火化了丈夫的尸体，处理不当；由于遗孀在得知这一错误后遭受了严重精神痛苦，医院很可能需就 NIED 向遗孀承担责任。\n\n（选项 A）依据 NIED 的“旁观者”理论索赔，原告必须同时目睹相关事件。\n\n由于遗孀未目睹火化过程，她无法依据该理论索赔。\n\n然而，她仍可依据“特殊情形”理论胜诉，因为该理论不要求同时目睹事件。\n\n（选项 B）依据 NIED 的“危险区域”理论索赔，原告必须处于面临即刻身体伤害的危险之中。\n\n由于遗孀从未面临身体伤害的危险，她无法依据该理论索赔。\n\n然而，她仍可依据“特殊情形”理论胜诉，因为该理论不要求存在迫在眉睫的身体危险。\n\n（选项 C）若被告从事异常危险的活动（例如在人口稠密地区储存爆炸物）并造成身体伤害，则可能需承担严格责任（即无需认定过错即承担责任）。\n\n尽管不当处理尸体很可能导致严重精神痛苦，但这并不被视为异常危险活动。\n\n因此，医院无需就不当处置丈夫尸体一事承担严格责任。\n\n教育目标：根据“特殊情形理论”（special-situations theory），若被告因疏忽而导致原告遭受严重精神痛苦，即构成过失致人精神痛苦（negligent infliction of emotional distress）。\n\n此类情形包括被告疏忽地：(1) 传达了关于死亡或疾病的错误通知；(2) 不当处置尸体或遗骸；或 (3) 在食物中混入令人作呕的异物。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身伤害与精神损害责任》第 47(b) 条及注释 f（美国法律协会，2012年）（关于过失致人精神痛苦的特殊情形理论）。","conclusion":"答案 D：会，因为疏忽处理其丈夫遗体的行为极有可能导致遗孀遭受严重的精神痛苦。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":138,"sourceQuestionPage":412,"sourceQuestionPages":[412],"sourceExplanationPages":[413,414],"sourceAnswerPages":[413,414],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-139","subject":"Torts","topic":"Actual & Proximate Cause","question":"A driver negligently ran into a pedestrian who was walking along a road. The pedestrian sustained an injury to his knee, causing it to buckle from time to time. Several months later, the pedestrian sustained an injury to his shoulder when his knee buckled, causing him to fall down a flight of stairs. The pedestrian then brought an action against the driver for the injuries to his knee and shoulder. In his action against the driver, for which of his injuries may the pedestrian recover damages?","choices":{"A":"For the injuries to his knee and shoulder, because the driver takes the victim as he finds him.","B":"For the injuries to his knee and shoulder, if the jury finds that the pedestrian's fall down a flight of stairs was a normal consequence of his original injury.","C":"For the injury to his knee only, because the injury to the pedestrian's shoulder is separable.","D":"For the injury to his knee only, if the jury finds that the driver could not have foreseen that his negligent driving would cause the pedestrian to fall down a flight of stairs."},"answer":"B","explanation":"To prevail on a negligence claim, the plaintiff must prove each element by a preponderance of the evidence. Once the plaintiff has done so, he/she is entitled to recover compensatory damages for (1) his/her initial physical harm (ie, personal injury, property damage) and (2) any subsequent harm—physical, economic, or emotional—that occurs as a normal consequence of that initial harm. Here, the pedestrian suffered a knee injury when he was struck by the driver. Several months later, the pedestrian's knee buckled, causing him to fall and injure his shoulder. The pedestrian can recover compensatory damages for his knee injury because it was caused by the driver's negligence. And if the jury finds that the pedestrian's fall was a normal consequence of his original knee injury, then he can also recover compensatory damages for his shoulder injury. (Choice A) Under the eggshell-skull rule, a tortfeasor \"takes the victim as he finds him.\" This means that a tortfeasor is liable for the full extent of the victim's harm, even if the victim was unusually vulnerable. But here, there is no indication that the pedestrian was unusually vulnerable to either injury when the driver hit him. (Choice C) When multiple tortfeasors cause the plaintiff to sustain separable (ie, distinct) injuries, each tortfeasor is only liable for the specific injury he/she caused—ie, no joint and several liability. But here, the separability of the pedestrian's injuries is irrelevant since they were both caused by a single tortfeasor. (Choice D) Foreseeability applies to duty and proximate cause—not damages. Therefore, the driver could be liable for both of the pedestrian's injuries even if the driver could not have foreseen that his negligent driving would cause the pedestrian to fall down a flight of stairs.\n\nEducational objective: A successful plaintiff for negligence and other tort claims can recover compensatory damages for (1) the initial harm caused by the defendant's tort and (2) any subsequent harm that was a normal consequence of that initial harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 460 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a negligent defendant is liable for subsequent harm when it was a normal consequence of the initial harm).","educationalObjective":"A successful plaintiff for negligence and other tort claims can recover compensatory damages for (1) the initial harm caused by the defendant's tort and (2) any subsequent harm that was a normal consequence of that initial harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 460 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a negligent defendant is liable for subsequent harm when it was a normal consequence of the initial harm).","rule":"A successful plaintiff for negligence and other tort claims can recover compensatory damages for (1) the initial harm caused by the defendant's tort and (2) any subsequent harm that was a normal consequence of that initial harm.","irac":{"issue":"In his action against the driver, for which of his injuries may the pedestrian recover damages?","rule":"A successful plaintiff for negligence and other tort claims can recover compensatory damages for (1) the initial harm caused by the defendant's tort and (2) any subsequent harm that was a normal consequence of that initial harm.","analysis":"To prevail on a negligence claim, the plaintiff must prove each element by a preponderance of the evidence. Once the plaintiff has done so, he/she is entitled to recover compensatory damages for (1) his/her initial physical harm (ie, personal injury, property damage) and (2) any subsequent harm—physical, economic, or emotional—that occurs as a normal consequence of that initial harm. Here, the pedestrian suffered a knee injury when he was struck by the driver. Several months later, the pedestrian's knee buckled, causing him to fall and injure his shoulder. The pedestrian can recover compensatory damages for his knee injury because it was caused by the driver's negligence. And if the jury finds that the pedestrian's fall was a normal consequence of his original knee injury, then he can also recover compensatory damages for his shoulder injury. (Choice A) Under the eggshell-skull rule, a tortfeasor \"takes the victim as he finds him.\" This means that a tortfeasor is liable for the full extent of the victim's harm, even if the victim was unusually vulnerable. But here, there is no indication that the pedestrian was unusually vulnerable to either injury when the driver hit him. (Choice C) When multiple tortfeasors cause the plaintiff to sustain separable (ie, distinct) injuries, each tortfeasor is only liable for the specific injury he/she caused—ie, no joint and several liability. But here, the separability of the pedestrian's injuries is irrelevant since they were both caused by a single tortfeasor. (Choice D) Foreseeability applies to duty and proximate cause—not damages. Therefore, the driver could be liable for both of the pedestrian's injuries even if the driver could not have foreseen that his negligent driving would cause the pedestrian to fall down a flight of stairs.","conclusion":"Answer B: For the injuries to his knee and shoulder, if the jury finds that the pedestrian's fall down a flight of stairs was a normal consequence of his original injury."},"questionZh":"一名司机因疏忽撞倒了一名沿路行走的行人。\n\n行人膝盖受伤，导致膝盖不时扭伤。\n\n几个月后，行人膝盖再次扭伤，导致其从楼梯上摔下，肩部受伤。\n\n行人随后就膝盖和肩部的伤势起诉了该司机。\n\n在对司机的诉讼中，行人可以就哪些伤势获得赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"膝盖和肩部的伤势，因为司机承担了受害者受伤后的所有责任。","B":"膝盖和肩部的伤势，如果陪审团认定行人从楼梯上摔下是其最初受伤的必然结果。","C":"仅膝盖的伤势，因为肩部的伤势是可以分离的。","D":"仅就其膝盖受伤而言，如果陪审团认定驾驶员无法预见其疏忽驾驶会导致行人从楼梯上摔下，则可获得赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"若要在过失侵权诉讼中胜诉，原告必须以“优势证据”证明构成该侵权的每一要素。\n\n一旦原告完成举证，便有权就以下两方面获得补偿性损害赔偿：(1) 最初遭受的身体伤害（即人身伤害或财产损失）；以及 (2) 作为该初始伤害的正常后果而发生的任何后续伤害（无论是身体、经济还是情感方面的伤害）。\n\n在本案中，行人被司机撞倒时膝盖受伤。\n\n数月后，该行人的膝盖突然无力（打软腿），导致其摔倒并伤及肩膀。\n\n行人可就其膝盖伤害获得补偿性损害赔偿，因为该伤害是由司机的过失造成的。\n\n此外，如果陪审团认定行人的摔倒是其最初膝盖伤害的正常后果，那么他也可以就其肩膀伤害获得补偿性损害赔偿。\n\n(选项 A) 根据“薄壳颅骨规则”（eggshell-skull rule），侵权行为人必须“按受害人的实际状况承担责任”（即“照单全收”）。\n\n这意味着，即使受害人身体状况异常脆弱，侵权行为人仍需对受害人遭受的全部损害承担责任。\n\n但在本案中，并无迹象表明行人在被司机撞击时，对于上述任一伤害存在异常脆弱的体质。\n\n(选项 C) 当多名侵权行为人导致原告遭受可区分（即截然不同）的伤害时，每位侵权行为人仅对其造成的特定伤害承担责任——即不承担连带责任。\n\n但在本案中，行人所受伤害是否可区分并不重要，因为这两处伤害均由同一名侵权行为人造成。\n\n(选项 D) 可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）原则适用于“注意义务”和“法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）”的认定，而不适用于损害赔偿范围的确定。\n\n因此，即使司机无法预见到其过失驾驶行为会导致行人从楼梯上摔落，他仍可能需要对行人的两处伤害承担责任。\n\n教育目标：在过失侵权及其他侵权诉讼中，胜诉原告有权就以下两方面获得补偿性损害赔偿：(1) 由被告侵权行为造成的初始伤害；以及 (2) 作为该初始伤害正常后果而发生的任何后续伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第460条（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明若后续损害系初始损害的通常后果，则有过失的被告须对该后续损害承担责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"过失侵权及其他侵权行为的胜诉原告可就以下两项获得补偿性损害赔偿：(1) 被告侵权行为造成的初始损害；(2) 该初始损害的正常后果所导致的任何后续损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"在针对驾驶员的诉讼中，行人可就其哪些伤害获得赔偿？","rule":"过失侵权及其他侵权行为的胜诉原告可就以下两项获得补偿性损害赔偿：(1) 被告侵权行为造成的初始损害；(2) 该初始损害的正常后果所导致的任何后续损害。","analysis":"若要在过失侵权诉讼中胜诉，原告必须以“优势证据”证明构成该侵权的每一要素。\n\n一旦原告完成举证，便有权就以下两方面获得补偿性损害赔偿：(1) 最初遭受的身体伤害（即人身伤害或财产损失）；以及 (2) 作为该初始伤害的正常后果而发生的任何后续伤害（无论是身体、经济还是情感方面的伤害）。\n\n在本案中，行人被司机撞倒时膝盖受伤。\n\n数月后，该行人的膝盖突然无力（打软腿），导致其摔倒并伤及肩膀。\n\n行人可就其膝盖伤害获得补偿性损害赔偿，因为该伤害是由司机的过失造成的。\n\n此外，如果陪审团认定行人的摔倒是其最初膝盖伤害的正常后果，那么他也可以就其肩膀伤害获得补偿性损害赔偿。\n\n(选项 A) 根据“薄壳颅骨规则”（eggshell-skull rule），侵权行为人必须“按受害人的实际状况承担责任”（即“照单全收”）。\n\n这意味着，即使受害人身体状况异常脆弱，侵权行为人仍需对受害人遭受的全部损害承担责任。\n\n但在本案中，并无迹象表明行人在被司机撞击时，对于上述任一伤害存在异常脆弱的体质。\n\n(选项 C) 当多名侵权行为人导致原告遭受可区分（即截然不同）的伤害时，每位侵权行为人仅对其造成的特定伤害承担责任——即不承担连带责任。\n\n但在本案中，行人所受伤害是否可区分并不重要，因为这两处伤害均由同一名侵权行为人造成。\n\n(选项 D) 可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability）原则适用于“注意义务”和“法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）”的认定，而不适用于损害赔偿范围的确定。\n\n因此，即使司机无法预见到其过失驾驶行为会导致行人从楼梯上摔落，他仍可能需要对行人的两处伤害承担责任。\n\n教育目标：在过失侵权及其他侵权诉讼中，胜诉原告有权就以下两方面获得补偿性损害赔偿：(1) 由被告侵权行为造成的初始伤害；以及 (2) 作为该初始伤害正常后果而发生的任何后续伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第460条（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明若后续损害系初始损害的通常后果，则有过失的被告须对该后续损害承担责任）。","conclusion":"答案 B：膝盖和肩部的伤势，如果陪审团认定行人从楼梯上摔下是其最初受伤的必然结果。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-282bbf14df3a1f77"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":139,"sourceQuestionPage":415,"sourceQuestionPages":[415],"sourceExplanationPages":[416,417],"sourceAnswerPages":[416,417],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-140","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A mother purchased an expensive television from an appliance store for her adult son. Two years after the purchase, a fire started in the son's living room in the middle of the night. The fire department concluded that the fire had started in the television. No other facts are known. The son sued the appliance store for negligence. The store has moved for summary judgment. Should the court grant the store's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because televisions do not catch fire in the absence of negligence.","B":"No, because the store sold the television.","C":"Yes, because the son is not in privity with the store.","D":"Yes, because there is no evidence of negligence on the part of the store."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Products liability claims\n\nClaims Required elements\n\nStrict products Commercial seller (ie, manufacturer, distributor, retailer) liability produced or sold defective product Defect caused plaintiff harm Negligence Seller (commercial or noncommercial) failed to use reasonable care in producing or inspecting defective product Product caused plaintiff harm Intentional tort Seller (commercial or noncommercial) purposefully or knowingly produced or sold defective product Product caused plaintiff harm Misrepresentation* Commercial seller misrepresented material fact about product Consumer justifiably relied on misrepresentation when purchasing product Product caused plaintiff harm Warranty Merchantability (implied) Commercial seller warranted product is generally acceptable & reasonably fit for its ordinary purpose Fitness for particular purpose (implied) Seller (commercial or noncommercial) warranted product is fit for particular purpose Purchaser relied on seller's judgment or skill to purchase product Express Seller (commercial or noncommercial) made affirmation of fact or promise that is part of basis of bargain *Commercial sellers are strictly liable for the misrepresentations they make regarding their products—regardless of whether those products are defective.\n\nTo prevail on a products liability claim based on negligence, the plaintiff generally must prove that the defendant's failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff harm. But in the absence of direct evidence of negligence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows the defendant's negligence to be inferred when: the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and\n\nthe evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.\n\nIf the plaintiff fails to provide sufficient evidence to support such an inference of negligence (ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor), the court should grant the defendant's motion for summary judgment. Here, the fire in the son's living room started in his television, which had been purchased from an appliance store two years earlier. But there is no direct evidence of the store's negligence. And even if televisions do not usually catch fire in the absence of a store's negligence, the evidence does not eliminate other potential causes of the fire—eg, manufacturing defect, improper repair (Choice A). Therefore, there is insufficient evidence to infer that the store was negligent, and the court should grant the store's motion for summary judgment. (Choice B) Commercial sellers (ie, manufacturers, wholesalers, and retailers) are strictly liable for harm caused by defective products that they sell. But here, the son sued the store for negligence—not strict products liability. (Choice C) Negligence does not require the plaintiff to be in privity (ie, in a contractual relationship) with the defendant. Instead, any foreseeable plaintiff can recover for injuries caused by the defendant's negligence.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur, the defendant's negligence can be inferred when (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a defendant's negligence can be inferred based on the type of accident that injured the plaintiff).","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur, the defendant's negligence can be inferred when (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining when a defendant's negligence can be inferred based on the type of accident that injured the plaintiff).","rule":"Under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur, the defendant's negligence can be inferred when (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the store's motion?","rule":"Under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur, the defendant's negligence can be inferred when (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","analysis":"Products liability claims\n\nClaims Required elements\n\nStrict products Commercial seller (ie, manufacturer, distributor, retailer) liability produced or sold defective product Defect caused plaintiff harm Negligence Seller (commercial or noncommercial) failed to use reasonable care in producing or inspecting defective product Product caused plaintiff harm Intentional tort Seller (commercial or noncommercial) purposefully or knowingly produced or sold defective product Product caused plaintiff harm Misrepresentation* Commercial seller misrepresented material fact about product Consumer justifiably relied on misrepresentation when purchasing product Product caused plaintiff harm Warranty Merchantability (implied) Commercial seller warranted product is generally acceptable & reasonably fit for its ordinary purpose Fitness for particular purpose (implied) Seller (commercial or noncommercial) warranted product is fit for particular purpose Purchaser relied on seller's judgment or skill to purchase product Express Seller (commercial or noncommercial) made affirmation of fact or promise that is part of basis of bargain *Commercial sellers are strictly liable for the misrepresentations they make regarding their products—regardless of whether those products are defective.\n\nTo prevail on a products liability claim based on negligence, the plaintiff generally must prove that the defendant's failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff harm. But in the absence of direct evidence of negligence, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows the defendant's negligence to be inferred when: the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and\n\nthe evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.\n\nIf the plaintiff fails to provide sufficient evidence to support such an inference of negligence (ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor), the court should grant the defendant's motion for summary judgment. Here, the fire in the son's living room started in his television, which had been purchased from an appliance store two years earlier. But there is no direct evidence of the store's negligence. And even if televisions do not usually catch fire in the absence of a store's negligence, the evidence does not eliminate other potential causes of the fire—eg, manufacturing defect, improper repair (Choice A). Therefore, there is insufficient evidence to infer that the store was negligent, and the court should grant the store's motion for summary judgment. (Choice B) Commercial sellers (ie, manufacturers, wholesalers, and retailers) are strictly liable for harm caused by defective products that they sell. But here, the son sued the store for negligence—not strict products liability. (Choice C) Negligence does not require the plaintiff to be in privity (ie, in a contractual relationship) with the defendant. Instead, any foreseeable plaintiff can recover for injuries caused by the defendant's negligence.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because there is no evidence of negligence on the part of the store."},"questionZh":"一位母亲从电器商店为她成年的儿子购买了一台昂贵的电视机。\n\n两年后，一天半夜，儿子家的客厅发生火灾。\n\n消防部门认定火灾是由电视机引起的。\n\n除此之外，没有其他事实。\n\n儿子以疏忽为由起诉了电器商店。\n\n商店已提出简易判决动议。\n\n法院是否应该批准商店的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应该，因为电视机不会在没有疏忽的情况下起火。","B":"不应该，因为电视机是商店出售的。","C":"应该，因为儿子与商店之间不存在直接或间接的合同关系。","D":"应该，因为没有证据表明商店存在疏忽。"},"explanationZh":"产品责任索赔 索赔类型 | 必备要件 严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability） | 商业销售商（即制造商、分销商、零售商）生产或销售了有缺陷的产品；该缺陷导致原告受到损害。 过失 | 销售商（商业或非商业）在生产或检查有缺陷产品时未尽到合理注意义务；该产品导致原告受到损害。 故意侵权 | 销售商（商业或非商业）蓄意或明知地生产或销售了有缺陷的产品；该产品导致原告受到损害。 虚假陈述* | 商业销售商对产品的重要事实作出了虚假陈述；消费者在购买产品时合理信赖了该虚假陈述；该产品导致原告受到损害。 保证 | 适销性（默示） | 商业销售商保证产品通常可被接受，且合理适用于其通常用途。 | 特定用途适用性（默示） | 销售商（商业或非商业）保证产品适用于特定用途；买方在购买产品时信赖了销售商的判断或技能。 | 明示 | 销售商（商业或非商业）作出了构成交易基础的事实确认或承诺。 | *对于就其产品作出的虚假陈述，商业销售商承担严格责任——无论产品是否存在缺陷。\n\n若要基于过失主张产品责任索赔并胜诉，原告通常必须证明被告未尽到合理注意义务，从而导致原告受到损害。\n\n但在缺乏过失直接证据的情况下，“事实本身说明问题”（res ipsa loquitur）原则允许在以下情形下推断被告存在过失：原告遭受的损害类型通常是由处于被告地位的人员的过失造成的；且 证据倾向于排除导致该损害的其他潜在原因。\n\n如果原告未能提供充分证据支持此类过失推断（即没有理性的陪审团会作出有利于原告的裁决），法院应准予被告提出的简易判决动议。\n\n在本案中，儿子客厅发生的火灾源于其两年前从一家电器商店购买的电视机。\n\n然而，并无直接证据表明该商店存在过失。\n\n即便电视机通常不会在商店无过失的情况下起火，现有证据也未能排除导致火灾的其他潜在原因——例如制造缺陷或不当维修（选项 A）。\n\n因此，证据不足以推断该商店存在过失，法院应准予该商店提出的简易判决动议。\n\n（选项 B）商业销售者（即制造商、批发商和零售商）对其销售的有缺陷产品所造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n但在本案中，儿子起诉商店的理由是过失，而非产品责任中的严格责任。\n\n（选项 C）过失侵权诉讼并不要求原告与被告之间存在“契约关系”（即合同关系）。\n\n相反，任何处于可预见范围内的原告，若因被告的过失而遭受伤害，均可获得赔偿。\n\n教学目标：根据“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”（res ipsa loquitur）原则，在以下情况下可推断被告存在过失：（1）原告遭受的损害类型通常是由处于被告地位的人的过失造成的；以及（2）证据倾向于排除导致该损害的其他潜在原因。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 328D 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（阐述了如何根据导致原告受伤的事故类型来推断被告的过失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据“事实本身说明问题”原则，当满足以下条件时，可以推定被告存在过失：(1) 原告遭受的损害通常由被告这种身份的人的过失造成；(2) 证据倾向于排除其他可能导致该损害的原因。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应准许商店的动议？","rule":"根据“事实本身说明问题”原则，当满足以下条件时，可以推定被告存在过失：(1) 原告遭受的损害通常由被告这种身份的人的过失造成；(2) 证据倾向于排除其他可能导致该损害的原因。","analysis":"产品责任索赔 索赔类型 | 必备要件 严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability） | 商业销售商（即制造商、分销商、零售商）生产或销售了有缺陷的产品；该缺陷导致原告受到损害。 过失 | 销售商（商业或非商业）在生产或检查有缺陷产品时未尽到合理注意义务；该产品导致原告受到损害。 故意侵权 | 销售商（商业或非商业）蓄意或明知地生产或销售了有缺陷的产品；该产品导致原告受到损害。 虚假陈述* | 商业销售商对产品的重要事实作出了虚假陈述；消费者在购买产品时合理信赖了该虚假陈述；该产品导致原告受到损害。 保证 | 适销性（默示） | 商业销售商保证产品通常可被接受，且合理适用于其通常用途。 | 特定用途适用性（默示） | 销售商（商业或非商业）保证产品适用于特定用途；买方在购买产品时信赖了销售商的判断或技能。 | 明示 | 销售商（商业或非商业）作出了构成交易基础的事实确认或承诺。 | *对于就其产品作出的虚假陈述，商业销售商承担严格责任——无论产品是否存在缺陷。\n\n若要基于过失主张产品责任索赔并胜诉，原告通常必须证明被告未尽到合理注意义务，从而导致原告受到损害。\n\n但在缺乏过失直接证据的情况下，“事实本身说明问题”（res ipsa loquitur）原则允许在以下情形下推断被告存在过失：原告遭受的损害类型通常是由处于被告地位的人员的过失造成的；且 证据倾向于排除导致该损害的其他潜在原因。\n\n如果原告未能提供充分证据支持此类过失推断（即没有理性的陪审团会作出有利于原告的裁决），法院应准予被告提出的简易判决动议。\n\n在本案中，儿子客厅发生的火灾源于其两年前从一家电器商店购买的电视机。\n\n然而，并无直接证据表明该商店存在过失。\n\n即便电视机通常不会在商店无过失的情况下起火，现有证据也未能排除导致火灾的其他潜在原因——例如制造缺陷或不当维修（选项 A）。\n\n因此，证据不足以推断该商店存在过失，法院应准予该商店提出的简易判决动议。\n\n（选项 B）商业销售者（即制造商、批发商和零售商）对其销售的有缺陷产品所造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n但在本案中，儿子起诉商店的理由是过失，而非产品责任中的严格责任。\n\n（选项 C）过失侵权诉讼并不要求原告与被告之间存在“契约关系”（即合同关系）。\n\n相反，任何处于可预见范围内的原告，若因被告的过失而遭受伤害，均可获得赔偿。\n\n教学目标：根据“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”（res ipsa loquitur）原则，在以下情况下可推断被告存在过失：（1）原告遭受的损害类型通常是由处于被告地位的人的过失造成的；以及（2）证据倾向于排除导致该损害的其他潜在原因。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 328D 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（阐述了如何根据导致原告受伤的事故类型来推断被告的过失）。","conclusion":"答案 D：应该，因为没有证据表明商店存在疏忽。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"distributor","ipa":"/dɪstɹˈɪbjətɝ/","syllables":"dis·trib·u·tor","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 分发者, 分布者, 散布者, 分配者, 销售者, 分配器, 配电盘, 自动拆版机；[计] 分配器","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“distributor”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:distributors","sourceSentence":"Products liability claims Claims Required elements Strict products Commercial seller (ie, manufacturer, distributor, retailer) liability produced or sold defective product Defect caused plaintiff harm Negligence Seller (commercial or noncommercial) failed to use reasonable care in producing or inspecting defective product Product caused plaintiff harm Intentional tort Seller (commercial or noncommercial) purposefully","synonyms":["distributer","electrical distributor","allocator"]},{"term":"consumer","ipa":"/kʌnsˈumɝ/","syllables":"con·sumer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 消费者；[经] 消费者, 用户","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“consumer”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:consumers","sourceSentence":"Products liability claims Claims Required elements Strict products Commercial seller (ie, manufacturer, distributor, retailer) liability produced or sold defective product Defect caused plaintiff harm Negligence Seller (commercial or noncommercial) failed to use reasonable care in producing or inspecting defective product Product caused plaintiff harm Intentional tort Seller (commercial or noncommercial) purposefully","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":140,"sourceQuestionPage":418,"sourceQuestionPages":[418],"sourceExplanationPages":[419,420],"sourceAnswerPages":[419,420],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-141","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A child was bitten by a dog while playing in a fenced-in common area of an apartment complex owned by a landlord. The child was the guest of a tenant living in the complex, and the dog was owned by another tenant. The owner of the dog knew that the dog had a propensity to bite, but the landlord did not have any notice of the dog's vicious propensities. In an action by the child against the landlord, will the child be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a landlord owes no duty to a tenant's guests.","B":"No, because the landlord did not have notice of the dog's vicious propensities.","C":"Yes, because a landlord's duty to protect a tenant's guests from dangerous conditions is nondelegable.","D":"Yes, because in these circumstances a landlord is strictly liable."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Dangerous conditions on land\n\n(landlord's duties)\n\nWarn tenant of known latent (hidden) defects on land Safely perform repairs Ensure common areas are in safe condition Maintain areas necessary for safe use of land (eg, walls, roof) Keep land in good repair (lease provision required) Protect entrants from dangerous condition on land leased for public purpose Landlords are generally not liable for harm that occurs on the leased premises. This is because they give up possession and control of the premises to their tenants and are unlikely to be aware of subsequent dangers. However, landlords owe tenants and other foreseeable persons on the premises (eg, their guests) a duty to use reasonable care to ensure the safety of common areas that remain under the landlord's control (Choice A). A landlord who breaches this duty and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for negligence. Here, the child's presence in the apartment complex was foreseeable since he was a tenant's guest. The child was then bitten by another tenant's dog in a common area of the apartment complex. As a result, the landlord owed the child a duty of reasonable care. But since the landlord had no reason to know that the dog posed a risk to those on his property, his failure to take precautions against that risk did not breach this duty of care. Therefore, the child is unlikely to prevail in an action against the landlord. (Choice C) Landlords owe tenants and their guests a nondelegable duty to protect them from dangerous physical conditions on the property that remain under the landlord's control (eg, broken elevators). But a vicious dog is not such a condition. Additionally, it does not matter whether the landlord's duty was nondelegable here since there is no evidence that he assigned its performance to a third party whose negligence caused the child's injuries. (Choice D) Strict liability for harm caused by a dog (or other animal) with vicious propensities extends to the dog's owners or possessors who know or should know of that propensity. But since the landlord neither owned nor possessed the dog, strict liability is inapplicable.\n\nEducational objective: Landlords owe foreseeable persons on the leased premises (eg, tenants and their guests) a duty to use reasonable care to ensure the safety of common areas that remain under the landlord's control.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 53 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (lessor's duties).","educationalObjective":"Landlords owe foreseeable persons on the leased premises (eg, tenants and their guests) a duty to use reasonable care to ensure the safety of common areas that remain under the landlord's control.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 53 (Am. Law Inst. 2012) (lessor's duties).","rule":"Landlords owe foreseeable persons on the leased premises (eg, tenants and their guests) a duty to use reasonable care to ensure the safety of common areas that remain under the landlord's control.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the child against the landlord, will the child be likely to prevail?","rule":"Landlords owe foreseeable persons on the leased premises (eg, tenants and their guests) a duty to use reasonable care to ensure the safety of common areas that remain under the landlord's control.","analysis":"Dangerous conditions on land\n\n(landlord's duties)\n\nWarn tenant of known latent (hidden) defects on land Safely perform repairs Ensure common areas are in safe condition Maintain areas necessary for safe use of land (eg, walls, roof) Keep land in good repair (lease provision required) Protect entrants from dangerous condition on land leased for public purpose Landlords are generally not liable for harm that occurs on the leased premises. This is because they give up possession and control of the premises to their tenants and are unlikely to be aware of subsequent dangers. However, landlords owe tenants and other foreseeable persons on the premises (eg, their guests) a duty to use reasonable care to ensure the safety of common areas that remain under the landlord's control (Choice A). A landlord who breaches this duty and causes the plaintiff physical harm is liable for negligence. Here, the child's presence in the apartment complex was foreseeable since he was a tenant's guest. The child was then bitten by another tenant's dog in a common area of the apartment complex. As a result, the landlord owed the child a duty of reasonable care. But since the landlord had no reason to know that the dog posed a risk to those on his property, his failure to take precautions against that risk did not breach this duty of care. Therefore, the child is unlikely to prevail in an action against the landlord. (Choice C) Landlords owe tenants and their guests a nondelegable duty to protect them from dangerous physical conditions on the property that remain under the landlord's control (eg, broken elevators). But a vicious dog is not such a condition. Additionally, it does not matter whether the landlord's duty was nondelegable here since there is no evidence that he assigned its performance to a third party whose negligence caused the child's injuries. (Choice D) Strict liability for harm caused by a dog (or other animal) with vicious propensities extends to the dog's owners or possessors who know or should know of that propensity. But since the landlord neither owned nor possessed the dog, strict liability is inapplicable.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the landlord did not have notice of the dog's vicious propensities."},"questionZh":"一名儿童在房东拥有的公寓楼的公共围栏区域玩耍时被狗咬伤。\n\n该儿童是公寓楼内一位租户的客人，而狗的主人是另一位租户。\n\n狗主人知道这只狗有咬人的倾向，但房东对此毫不知情。\n\n如果儿童起诉房东，儿童胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为房东对租户的客人没有义务。","B":"否，因为房东对狗的使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）性毫不知情。","C":"是，因为房东保护租户客人免受危险状况侵害的义务不可转让。","D":"是，因为在这种情况下，房东承担严格责任。"},"explanationZh":"土地上的危险状况 （房东的义务） 告知租户土地上已知的潜在（隐蔽）缺陷；安全地进行维修；确保公共区域处于安全状态；维护安全使用土地所必需的区域（例如墙壁、屋顶）；保持土地处于良好维修状态（视租赁条款而定）；保护进入者免受用于公共目的的租赁土地上的危险状况伤害。房东通常不对租赁场所内发生的伤害承担责任。\n\n这是因为他们已将场所的占有权和控制权移交给租户，且不太可能知晓随后出现的危险。\n\n然而，对于仍由其控制的公共区域，房东有义务尽合理注意以确保其安全，这一义务适用于租户及场所内其他可预见的人员（例如租户的访客）（选项 A）。\n\n如果房东违反此义务并导致原告身体伤害，则需承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，该儿童作为租户的访客出现在公寓大楼内，其出现是可预见的。\n\n随后，该儿童在公寓大楼的公共区域被另一位租户的狗咬伤。\n\n因此，房东对该儿童负有合理注意义务。\n\n但由于房东没有理由知道该狗对其物业上的人员构成风险，因此他未针对该风险采取预防措施的行为并不构成违反注意义务。\n\n因此，该儿童针对房东提起的诉讼不太可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）房东对租户及其访客负有一项不可转让的义务，即保护他们免受仍由房东控制的物业上的危险物理状况（例如损坏的电梯）的伤害。\n\n但凶猛的狗不属于此类状况。\n\n此外，在本案中，房东的义务是否不可转让并不重要，因为没有证据表明他将义务履行委托给了第三方，且该第三方的过失导致了儿童受伤。\n\n（选项 D）对于由具有凶猛倾向的狗（或其他动物）造成的伤害，严格责任适用于知晓或应当知晓该倾向的狗的主人或占有人。\n\n但由于房东既非该狗的主人也非占有人，因此严格责任不适用。\n\n教育目标：房东对租赁场所内可预见的人员（例如租户及其访客）负有义务，需尽合理注意以确保仍由其控制的公共区域的安全。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第53条（美国法律学会，2012年）（出租人的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"房东对租赁房屋内可预见的人员（例如，租户及其访客）负有合理注意义务，以确保其控制下的公共区域的安全。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果儿童起诉房东，儿童胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"房东对租赁房屋内可预见的人员（例如，租户及其访客）负有合理注意义务，以确保其控制下的公共区域的安全。","analysis":"土地上的危险状况 （房东的义务） 告知租户土地上已知的潜在（隐蔽）缺陷；安全地进行维修；确保公共区域处于安全状态；维护安全使用土地所必需的区域（例如墙壁、屋顶）；保持土地处于良好维修状态（视租赁条款而定）；保护进入者免受用于公共目的的租赁土地上的危险状况伤害。房东通常不对租赁场所内发生的伤害承担责任。\n\n这是因为他们已将场所的占有权和控制权移交给租户，且不太可能知晓随后出现的危险。\n\n然而，对于仍由其控制的公共区域，房东有义务尽合理注意以确保其安全，这一义务适用于租户及场所内其他可预见的人员（例如租户的访客）（选项 A）。\n\n如果房东违反此义务并导致原告身体伤害，则需承担过失责任。\n\n在本案中，该儿童作为租户的访客出现在公寓大楼内，其出现是可预见的。\n\n随后，该儿童在公寓大楼的公共区域被另一位租户的狗咬伤。\n\n因此，房东对该儿童负有合理注意义务。\n\n但由于房东没有理由知道该狗对其物业上的人员构成风险，因此他未针对该风险采取预防措施的行为并不构成违反注意义务。\n\n因此，该儿童针对房东提起的诉讼不太可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）房东对租户及其访客负有一项不可转让的义务，即保护他们免受仍由房东控制的物业上的危险物理状况（例如损坏的电梯）的伤害。\n\n但凶猛的狗不属于此类状况。\n\n此外，在本案中，房东的义务是否不可转让并不重要，因为没有证据表明他将义务履行委托给了第三方，且该第三方的过失导致了儿童受伤。\n\n（选项 D）对于由具有凶猛倾向的狗（或其他动物）造成的伤害，严格责任适用于知晓或应当知晓该倾向的狗的主人或占有人。\n\n但由于房东既非该狗的主人也非占有人，因此严格责任不适用。\n\n教育目标：房东对租赁场所内可预见的人员（例如租户及其访客）负有义务，需尽合理注意以确保仍由其控制的公共区域的安全。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第53条（美国法律学会，2012年）（出租人的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 B：否，因为房东对狗的使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）性毫不知情。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":141,"sourceQuestionPage":421,"sourceQuestionPages":[421],"sourceExplanationPages":[422,423],"sourceAnswerPages":[422,423],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-142","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A customer went to a restaurant for lunch. The customer told the server that he had a severe peanut allergy and requested that the chef be extra cautious about not putting peanuts in his food. The server conveyed this information to the head chef. The head chef then asked a line cook to prepare the customer's meal and told the line cook not to put any peanuts in it because the customer was allergic to them. The line cook, who had a mental disability, did not think that the dish he was preparing for the customer would taste good without peanuts. He chopped up a few peanuts and added them to the dish, thinking that a few peanuts would not harm the customer. The customer took a bite of his meal, went into anaphylactic shock, and was rushed to a nearby hospital. If the customer files a negligence action against the line cook, is the customer likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the line cook did not act in bad faith when he decided to put peanuts in the customer's food.","B":"No, because the line cook's mental disability prevented him from understanding the effect of putting peanuts in the customer's food.","C":"Yes, because a person with a mental disability is held to the same standard as someone of ordinary intelligence and knowledge.","D":"Yes, because the line cook intentionally put peanuts into the customer's food."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nIn negligence cases, the standard of care most often imposed is that of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances. Under this standard, a defendant is presumed to have the same mental abilities and knowledge as an average member of the community. A defendant's own mental or emotional disability is not a factor in determining whether the defendant was negligent (unless the defendant is a child). Therefore, a mentally incompetent person is held to the same standard as someone of ordinary intelligence and knowledge. Here, a person of ordinary intelligence and knowledge who knew that the customer had a severe peanut allergy would not have put peanuts in the customer's food. The line cook will be held to that standard, even if his mental disability prevented him from understanding the ramifications of putting peanuts in the customer's food (Choice B). And since the line cook failed to conform to this standard (ie, breached his duty of care) and caused the customer's injuries, the customer is likely to prevail. (Choice A) The standard of care for negligence is an objective one; it is measured by what a reasonably prudent person would have done rather than whether a particular defendant acted in good faith or used his/her best efforts. Therefore, the fact that the line cook did not act in bad faith when he decided to put peanuts in the customer's food will not be considered in determining whether he breached his duty of care.\n\n(Choice D) It is not enough that the line cook intentionally put peanuts in the customer's food. For negligence liability, the line cook also must have breached a duty that he owed to the customer (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: In negligence cases, a mentally incompetent person is held to the same standard as a reasonably prudent person with ordinary intelligence and knowledge.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts § 11 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that an adult's mental or emotional disability is not a factor in determining whether he/she was negligent).","educationalObjective":"In negligence cases, a mentally incompetent person is held to the same standard as a reasonably prudent person with ordinary intelligence and knowledge.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts § 11 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that an adult's mental or emotional disability is not a factor in determining whether he/she was negligent).","rule":"In negligence cases, a mentally incompetent person is held to the same standard as a reasonably prudent person with ordinary intelligence and knowledge.","irac":{"issue":"If the customer files a negligence action against the line cook, is the customer likely to prevail?","rule":"In negligence cases, a mentally incompetent person is held to the same standard as a reasonably prudent person with ordinary intelligence and knowledge.","analysis":"Standards of care for negligence\n\nAdult Must act in same manner as ordinary, reasonable adult in similar circumstances\n\nStandard adjusted for physical disabilities, superior skill & knowledge, involuntary intoxication Standard not adjusted for mental & emotional disabilities, voluntary intoxication Professional Must demonstrate same knowledge, skill & care as another professional in similar community\n\nNational (not community) standard applied to medical specialists* Child Must act in same manner as reasonable child of same age, intelligence & experience\n\nAdult standard applied to children engaged in dangerous adult activity Children under five years old incapable of negligence *A modern trend applies a national standard of care to all physicians.\n\nIn negligence cases, the standard of care most often imposed is that of a reasonably prudent person under the circumstances. Under this standard, a defendant is presumed to have the same mental abilities and knowledge as an average member of the community. A defendant's own mental or emotional disability is not a factor in determining whether the defendant was negligent (unless the defendant is a child). Therefore, a mentally incompetent person is held to the same standard as someone of ordinary intelligence and knowledge. Here, a person of ordinary intelligence and knowledge who knew that the customer had a severe peanut allergy would not have put peanuts in the customer's food. The line cook will be held to that standard, even if his mental disability prevented him from understanding the ramifications of putting peanuts in the customer's food (Choice B). And since the line cook failed to conform to this standard (ie, breached his duty of care) and caused the customer's injuries, the customer is likely to prevail. (Choice A) The standard of care for negligence is an objective one; it is measured by what a reasonably prudent person would have done rather than whether a particular defendant acted in good faith or used his/her best efforts. Therefore, the fact that the line cook did not act in bad faith when he decided to put peanuts in the customer's food will not be considered in determining whether he breached his duty of care.\n\n(Choice D) It is not enough that the line cook intentionally put peanuts in the customer's food. For negligence liability, the line cook also must have breached a duty that he owed to the customer (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because a person with a mental disability is held to the same standard as someone of ordinary intelligence and knowledge."},"questionZh":"一位顾客去餐厅吃午餐。\n\n该顾客告诉服务员自己对花生严重过敏，并要求厨师务必小心，不要在食物中加入花生。\n\n服务员将这一情况转达给了主厨。\n\n主厨随后指派一名负责烹饪的厨师（line cook）准备这道菜，并叮嘱他不要放花生，因为顾客对此过敏。\n\n这名患有智力障碍的厨师认为，如果不放花生，这道菜的味道会不好。\n\n他切碎了一些花生并加到了菜里，心想少许花生不会对顾客造成伤害。\n\n顾客吃了一口后发生了过敏性休克，随即被紧急送往附近医院。\n\n如果该顾客针对这名厨师提起过失诉讼，他胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该厨师在决定向食物中加入花生时并无恶意。","B":"不会，因为该厨师的智力障碍使他无法理解在食物中加入花生会产生什么后果。","C":"会，因为智力障碍人士所适用的行为标准与智力和知识水平正常的人相同。","D":"会，因为该厨师是有意将花生加入顾客食物中的。"},"explanationZh":"过失侵权中的注意义务标准 成年人：必须像类似情况下的普通、理性成年人那样行事。 标准调整情形：身体残疾、具备卓越技能与知识、非自愿醉酒状态。标准不调整情形：精神或情绪障碍、自愿醉酒状态。专业人士：必须展现出与同类社区中其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和谨慎程度。 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。儿童：必须像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 从事危险成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准；五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。\n\n*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意义务标准。 在过失侵权案件中，最常适用的注意义务标准是“在特定情况下理性审慎之人的标准”。\n\n根据该标准，被告被推定具备与社区普通成员相当的智力能力和知识水平。\n\n被告自身的精神或情绪障碍不作为判定其是否存在过失的考量因素（除非被告是儿童）。\n\n因此，精神障碍者须遵守与智力和知识水平正常者相同的标准。\n\n在此案中，一个智力和知识水平正常且知晓顾客有严重花生过敏症的人，绝不会在顾客的食物中加入花生。\n\n该厨师必须遵守这一标准，即使其精神障碍导致他无法理解在顾客食物中加入花生可能带来的后果（选项 B）。\n\n鉴于该厨师未能达到这一标准（即违反了注意义务）并导致顾客受伤，顾客很可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）过失侵权中的注意义务标准是客观的；其衡量依据是理性审慎之人会如何行事，而非特定被告是否出于善意或已尽最大努力。\n\n因此，在判定该厨师是否违反注意义务时，不会考虑他在决定向顾客食物中加入花生时并无恶意这一事实。\n\n（选项 D）仅凭厨师故意在顾客食物中加入花生这一事实是不够的。\n\n要构成过失责任，该厨师还必须违反了其对顾客应尽的义务（本案中即存在此种违反义务的情形）。\n\n教育目标：在过失案件中，对于精神无行为能力人，适用与具有普通智识和知识的理性审慎之人相同的行为标准。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第11条评注e（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明成年人的精神或情感障碍并非判定其是否存在过失的考量因素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在过失侵权案件中，智力障碍人士所适用的行为标准与具备正常智力和知识水平的理性审慎之人相同。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该顾客针对这名厨师提起过失诉讼，他胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"在过失侵权案件中，智力障碍人士所适用的行为标准与具备正常智力和知识水平的理性审慎之人相同。","analysis":"过失侵权中的注意义务标准 成年人：必须像类似情况下的普通、理性成年人那样行事。 标准调整情形：身体残疾、具备卓越技能与知识、非自愿醉酒状态。标准不调整情形：精神或情绪障碍、自愿醉酒状态。专业人士：必须展现出与同类社区中其他专业人士相当的知识、技能和谨慎程度。 医疗专科医生适用全国性（而非社区性）标准*。儿童：必须像同等年龄、智力和经验的理性儿童那样行事。 从事危险成人活动的儿童适用成年人标准；五岁以下儿童不承担过失责任。\n\n*现代趋势是对所有医生适用全国性注意义务标准。 在过失侵权案件中，最常适用的注意义务标准是“在特定情况下理性审慎之人的标准”。\n\n根据该标准，被告被推定具备与社区普通成员相当的智力能力和知识水平。\n\n被告自身的精神或情绪障碍不作为判定其是否存在过失的考量因素（除非被告是儿童）。\n\n因此，精神障碍者须遵守与智力和知识水平正常者相同的标准。\n\n在此案中，一个智力和知识水平正常且知晓顾客有严重花生过敏症的人，绝不会在顾客的食物中加入花生。\n\n该厨师必须遵守这一标准，即使其精神障碍导致他无法理解在顾客食物中加入花生可能带来的后果（选项 B）。\n\n鉴于该厨师未能达到这一标准（即违反了注意义务）并导致顾客受伤，顾客很可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）过失侵权中的注意义务标准是客观的；其衡量依据是理性审慎之人会如何行事，而非特定被告是否出于善意或已尽最大努力。\n\n因此，在判定该厨师是否违反注意义务时，不会考虑他在决定向顾客食物中加入花生时并无恶意这一事实。\n\n（选项 D）仅凭厨师故意在顾客食物中加入花生这一事实是不够的。\n\n要构成过失责任，该厨师还必须违反了其对顾客应尽的义务（本案中即存在此种违反义务的情形）。\n\n教育目标：在过失案件中，对于精神无行为能力人，适用与具有普通智识和知识的理性审慎之人相同的行为标准。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）》第11条评注e（美国法律学会，1965年）（阐明成年人的精神或情感障碍并非判定其是否存在过失的考量因素）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，因为智力障碍人士所适用的行为标准与智力和知识水平正常的人相同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":142,"sourceQuestionPage":424,"sourceQuestionPages":[424],"sourceExplanationPages":[425,426],"sourceAnswerPages":[425,426],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-143","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Breach & Proof","question":"A complaint filed on behalf of a woman against a nursing home and an ambulance service included the following allegations: The woman, who was 86 years old and unable to speak after suffering a stroke, was picked up from her daughter's house by the ambulance service and taken to the nursing home to stay while her daughter was out of town. When the woman's daughter returned a few days later, the ambulance service picked up the woman from the nursing home and returned her to the daughter's house. The daughter was shocked to discover that the woman had a broken leg; her leg had been uninjured when she left for the nursing home. A physician's report attached to the complaint stated that the woman's leg injury would not have occurred in the absence of negligence. The complaint further alleged that the woman was under the control, successively, of the ambulance service and the nursing home during the time when she must have sustained the injury, and that either the ambulance service or the nursing home must have negligently moved or handled the woman, causing the injury to her leg. Both defendants have argued that the allegations in the complaint are inadequate to support a negligence claim. What is the best response to the defendants' argument?","choices":{"A":"Both defendants owed a duty to the woman.","B":"One of the two defendants probably caused the injury, and the circumstances of the injury are primarily within the knowledge and control of the defendants rather than the woman or her representative.","C":"The defendants are concurrent tortfeasors, so each is vicariously liable for any tortious act committed by the other.","D":"There are grounds for the fact finder to infer that both defendants were negligent."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The ambulance service and nursing home argued that the allegations in the woman's complaint are inadequate to support her negligence claim. This is likely because the woman cannot identify the direct cause of her injury—the ambulance service's or nursing home's negligence. In such a situation, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows a defendant's (or, in this case, defendants') negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence when: the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position (as stated in the physician's report) and\n\nthe evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm (eg, evidence that the injury did not exist before the woman left her daughter's house).\n\nThis suggests that evidence regarding the cause of the plaintiff's injury is primarily within the defendants' knowledge and control and allows the fact finder to infer that one of the defendants caused the injury. Therefore, res ipsa loquitur is the woman's best response to support her negligence claim. (Choice A) Both defendants owed the woman a duty of reasonable care because she was entrusted to their control. But this is not her best response since it does not address the other elements of her negligence claim—namely, the cause of her injury. (Choice C) Even if the defendants are concurrent tortfeasors, they are not vicariously liable for each other's actions since they do not share an agency relationship (eg, employer- employee). (Choice D) The woman alleged grounds for the fact finder (eg, jury) to infer that one (not both) of the defendants was negligent.\n\nEducational objective: The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows a defendant's negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (defining the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur).\n\nCollins v. Superior Air-Ground Ambulance Serv., Inc., 789 N.E.2d 394, 402 (Ill. App. Ct. 2003) (explaining how res ipsa loquitur can be alleged in an action involving multiple defendants).","educationalObjective":"The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows a defendant's negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (defining the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur).\n\nCollins v. Superior Air-Ground Ambulance Serv., Inc., 789 N.E.2d 394, 402 (Ill. App. Ct. 2003) (explaining how res ipsa loquitur can be alleged in an action involving multiple defendants).","rule":"The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows a defendant's negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","irac":{"issue":"What is the best response to the defendants' argument?","rule":"The doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows a defendant's negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence if (1) the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position and (2) the evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm.","analysis":"The ambulance service and nursing home argued that the allegations in the woman's complaint are inadequate to support her negligence claim. This is likely because the woman cannot identify the direct cause of her injury—the ambulance service's or nursing home's negligence. In such a situation, the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur allows a defendant's (or, in this case, defendants') negligence to be inferred from circumstantial evidence when: the plaintiff suffered a type of harm that is usually caused by negligence of someone in the defendant's position (as stated in the physician's report) and\n\nthe evidence tends to eliminate other potential causes of that harm (eg, evidence that the injury did not exist before the woman left her daughter's house).\n\nThis suggests that evidence regarding the cause of the plaintiff's injury is primarily within the defendants' knowledge and control and allows the fact finder to infer that one of the defendants caused the injury. Therefore, res ipsa loquitur is the woman's best response to support her negligence claim. (Choice A) Both defendants owed the woman a duty of reasonable care because she was entrusted to their control. But this is not her best response since it does not address the other elements of her negligence claim—namely, the cause of her injury. (Choice C) Even if the defendants are concurrent tortfeasors, they are not vicariously liable for each other's actions since they do not share an agency relationship (eg, employer- employee). (Choice D) The woman alleged grounds for the fact finder (eg, jury) to infer that one (not both) of the defendants was negligent.","conclusion":"Answer B: One of the two defendants probably caused the injury, and the circumstances of the injury are primarily within the knowledge and control of the defendants rather than the woman or her representative."},"questionZh":"一份代表某位女性针对一家疗养院和一家救护车服务机构提起的诉讼包含以下指控：该女性时年86岁，因中风而无法说话；在其女儿外出期间，她由救护车服务机构从女儿家中接走，送往疗养院暂住。\n\n几天后其女儿返回时，救护车服务机构又将她从疗养院接回女儿家中。\n\n女儿震惊地发现该女性腿部骨折；而在她前往疗养院时，其腿部并无伤情。\n\n诉讼附带的一份医生报告指出，若无过失行为，该女性的腿部损伤本不会发生。\n\n诉讼进一步指控称，在该女性必然遭受损伤的期间，她先后处于救护车服务机构和疗养院的控制之下；救护车服务机构或疗养院在移动或处置该女性时存在过失，从而导致了其腿部受伤。\n\n两名被告均辩称，诉讼中的指控不足以支持过失索赔。\n\n针对被告的辩解，最佳回应是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"两名被告均对该女性负有注意义务。","B":"两名被告中的一方很可能造成了该损伤，且有关损伤发生的情况主要处于被告的知情与控制之下，而非该女性或其代理人的知情与控制之下。","C":"被告属于共同侵权行为人，因此每一方均需对另一方实施的任何侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","D":"事实认定者有理由推断两名被告均存在过失。"},"explanationZh":"救护车服务机构和疗养院辩称，该女子投诉中的指控不足以支持其过失索赔。\n\n这很可能是因为该女子无法确定其受伤的直接原因——即究竟是救护车服务机构还是疗养院的过失所致。\n\n在此类情况下，“事实不言自明”原则（res ipsa loquitur）允许根据间接证据推断被告（本案中为多名被告）存在过失，前提是：原告遭受的伤害类型通常是由处于被告位置的人员的过失造成的（如医生报告所述），且 证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因（例如，证据显示该女子离开其女儿家之前并未受伤）。\n\n这表明，关于原告受伤原因的证据主要处于被告的知情和控制范围内，并允许事实认定者推断其中一名被告造成了该伤害。\n\n因此，“事实不言自明”原则是该女子支持其过失索赔的最佳论据。\n\n（选项 A）两名被告均对该女子负有合理注意义务，因为她被交由他们照管。\n\n但这并非最佳论据，因为它未能解决其过失索赔中的其他要素——即受伤原因。\n\n（选项 C）即使被告属于共同侵权行为人，他们也不对彼此的行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），因为他们之间不存在代理关系（例如雇主与雇员关系）。\n\n（选项 D）该女子提出的理由旨在让事实认定者（如陪审团）推断其中一名（而非两名）被告存在过失。\n\n教学目标：“事实不言自明”原则允许根据间接证据推断被告存在过失，条件是：(1) 原告遭受的伤害类型通常是由处于被告位置的人员的过失造成的；(2) 证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 328D 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（定义了“事实不言自明”原则）。\n\nCollins 诉 Superior Air-Ground Ambulance Serv., Inc. 案，789 N.E.2d 394, 402 (Ill. App. Ct. 2003)（阐述了在涉及多名被告的诉讼中如何主张“事实本身即说明问题”原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”（res ipsa loquitur）原则允许根据间接证据推断被告存在过失，前提是：(1) 原告遭受的损害类型通常是由处于被告地位的人员的过失造成的；以及 (2) 证据倾向于排除导致该损害的其他潜在原因。","iracZh":{"issue":"针对被告的辩解，最佳回应是什么？","rule":"“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）”（res ipsa loquitur）原则允许根据间接证据推断被告存在过失，前提是：(1) 原告遭受的损害类型通常是由处于被告地位的人员的过失造成的；以及 (2) 证据倾向于排除导致该损害的其他潜在原因。","analysis":"救护车服务机构和疗养院辩称，该女子投诉中的指控不足以支持其过失索赔。\n\n这很可能是因为该女子无法确定其受伤的直接原因——即究竟是救护车服务机构还是疗养院的过失所致。\n\n在此类情况下，“事实不言自明”原则（res ipsa loquitur）允许根据间接证据推断被告（本案中为多名被告）存在过失，前提是：原告遭受的伤害类型通常是由处于被告位置的人员的过失造成的（如医生报告所述），且 证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因（例如，证据显示该女子离开其女儿家之前并未受伤）。\n\n这表明，关于原告受伤原因的证据主要处于被告的知情和控制范围内，并允许事实认定者推断其中一名被告造成了该伤害。\n\n因此，“事实不言自明”原则是该女子支持其过失索赔的最佳论据。\n\n（选项 A）两名被告均对该女子负有合理注意义务，因为她被交由他们照管。\n\n但这并非最佳论据，因为它未能解决其过失索赔中的其他要素——即受伤原因。\n\n（选项 C）即使被告属于共同侵权行为人，他们也不对彼此的行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），因为他们之间不存在代理关系（例如雇主与雇员关系）。\n\n（选项 D）该女子提出的理由旨在让事实认定者（如陪审团）推断其中一名（而非两名）被告存在过失。\n\n教学目标：“事实不言自明”原则允许根据间接证据推断被告存在过失，条件是：(1) 原告遭受的伤害类型通常是由处于被告位置的人员的过失造成的；(2) 证据倾向于排除导致该伤害的其他潜在原因。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 328D 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（定义了“事实不言自明”原则）。\n\nCollins 诉 Superior Air-Ground Ambulance Serv., Inc. 案，789 N.E.2d 394, 402 (Ill. App. Ct. 2003)（阐述了在涉及多名被告的诉讼中如何主张“事实本身即说明问题”原则）。","conclusion":"答案 B：两名被告中的一方很可能造成了该损伤，且有关损伤发生的情况主要处于被告的知情与控制之下，而非该女性或其代理人的知情与控制之下。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-c6c9134f57a34849"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":143,"sourceQuestionPage":427,"sourceQuestionPages":[427],"sourceExplanationPages":[428,429],"sourceAnswerPages":[428,429],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-144","subject":"Torts","topic":"Damages & Joint Liability","question":"A homeowner resides downhill from a metal fabrication facility. She has sued both the owner of the facility and the supplier of a solvent used at the facility. She contends that contaminants, consisting mostly of the solvent, were released into the ground at the facility and have migrated and continue to migrate to her property, contaminating the soil, the groundwater, and her well. She alleges various acts of negligence on the part of the facility owner in causing the release of the contaminants into the ground. She also alleges that employees of the solvent supplier were negligent in frequently spilling some of the solvent onto the ground while filling a rooftop tank at the facility. The solvent supplier has moved for summary judgment, arguing that if there was any contamination, the facility owner and the supplier independently contributed indeterminate amounts to the contamination and that therefore the homeowner cannot show how much damage each has inflicted on her. There is no evidence that the facility owner and the solvent supplier acted in concert. Should the court grant the summary judgment motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because concurrent tortfeasors are jointly and severally liable for an indivisible injury.","B":"No, because the solvent supplier is vicariously liable for damage inflicted by the facility owner.","C":"Yes, because there is no basis for allocating damages against the solvent supplier.","D":"Yes, because there is no evidence that the facility owner and the solvent supplier acted in concert."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Liability of multiple negligent actors\n\nIndivisible injuries Separable injuries\n\nActing independently Joint & several liability* Several liability only\n\nActing in concert Joint & several liability\n\n*Default rule on MBE.\n\nA movant is entitled to summary judgment when there is no genuine dispute of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law—eg, when the nonmovant fails to prove his/her claim. A claim for negligence requires proof that a tortfeasor's conduct actually and proximately caused the alleged harm. If the harm was caused by multiple tortfeasors, then those tortfeasors can be held: severally liable – when each tortfeasor is liable for a proportionate amount of damages because his/her independent actions caused separable injuries or\n\njointly and severally liable – when any tortfeasor can be held liable for the total amount of damages because his/her independent or concerted actions concurrently caused an indivisible injury.*\n\nHere, the solvent supplier's motion for summary judgment alleges that both parties contributed indeterminate amounts to the contamination. This allegation shows that the homeowner suffered an indivisible injury, so either the solvent supplier or the facility owner can be held jointly and severally liable for all of her harm—even though they did not act in concert. As a result, the solvent supplier is not entitled to judgment as a matter of law, and the court should deny its summary judgment motion (Choice C). *When a plaintiff recovers the full amount of damages from any one of the tortfeasors, that tortfeasor can then sue the others for indemnity or contribution. (Choice B) Under vicarious liability, a principal is strictly liable for its agent's tortious acts committed within the scope of an agency relationship (eg, employer-employee). Here, the solvent supplier is vicariously liable for the damage caused by its employees—but not for the damage caused by the facility owner. (Choice D) Joint and several liability applies if the concerted or independent acts of multiple tortfeasors concurrently caused an indivisible injury. Therefore, evidence that the facility owner and the solvent supplier acted in concert is not required.\n\nEducational objective: When multiple tortfeasors caused the plaintiff harm, each will be liable for a proportionate amount of damages if his/her independent actions caused separable injuries (ie, several liability) OR for the total amount if their independent or concerted actions concurrently caused an indivisible injury (ie, joint and several liability).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liab. § A18 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (joint & several liability).","educationalObjective":"When multiple tortfeasors caused the plaintiff harm, each will be liable for a proportionate amount of damages if his/her independent actions caused separable injuries (ie, several liability) OR for the total amount if their independent or concerted actions concurrently caused an indivisible injury (ie, joint and several liability).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liab. § A18 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (joint & several liability).","rule":"When multiple tortfeasors caused the plaintiff harm, each will be liable for a proportionate amount of damages if his/her independent actions caused separable injuries (ie, several liability) OR for the total amount if their independent or concerted actions concurrently caused an indivisible injury (ie, joint and several liability).","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the summary judgment motion?","rule":"When multiple tortfeasors caused the plaintiff harm, each will be liable for a proportionate amount of damages if his/her independent actions caused separable injuries (ie, several liability) OR for the total amount if their independent or concerted actions concurrently caused an indivisible injury (ie, joint and several liability).","analysis":"Liability of multiple negligent actors\n\nIndivisible injuries Separable injuries\n\nActing independently Joint & several liability* Several liability only\n\nActing in concert Joint & several liability\n\n*Default rule on MBE.\n\nA movant is entitled to summary judgment when there is no genuine dispute of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law—eg, when the nonmovant fails to prove his/her claim. A claim for negligence requires proof that a tortfeasor's conduct actually and proximately caused the alleged harm. If the harm was caused by multiple tortfeasors, then those tortfeasors can be held: severally liable – when each tortfeasor is liable for a proportionate amount of damages because his/her independent actions caused separable injuries or\n\njointly and severally liable – when any tortfeasor can be held liable for the total amount of damages because his/her independent or concerted actions concurrently caused an indivisible injury.*\n\nHere, the solvent supplier's motion for summary judgment alleges that both parties contributed indeterminate amounts to the contamination. This allegation shows that the homeowner suffered an indivisible injury, so either the solvent supplier or the facility owner can be held jointly and severally liable for all of her harm—even though they did not act in concert. As a result, the solvent supplier is not entitled to judgment as a matter of law, and the court should deny its summary judgment motion (Choice C). *When a plaintiff recovers the full amount of damages from any one of the tortfeasors, that tortfeasor can then sue the others for indemnity or contribution. (Choice B) Under vicarious liability, a principal is strictly liable for its agent's tortious acts committed within the scope of an agency relationship (eg, employer-employee). Here, the solvent supplier is vicariously liable for the damage caused by its employees—but not for the damage caused by the facility owner. (Choice D) Joint and several liability applies if the concerted or independent acts of multiple tortfeasors concurrently caused an indivisible injury. Therefore, evidence that the facility owner and the solvent supplier acted in concert is not required.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because concurrent tortfeasors are jointly and severally liable for an indivisible injury."},"questionZh":"一位房主居住在一家金属加工厂下坡处。\n\n她起诉了该工厂的所有者以及该工厂所用溶剂的供应商。\n\n她主张，污染物（主要成分为该溶剂）从工厂释放到地下，并已迁移（且仍在继续迁移）至她的房产，从而污染了土壤、地下水及她的水井。\n\n她指控工厂所有者在导致污染物释放至地下的过程中存在多种疏忽行为。\n\n她还指控溶剂供应商的员工在为工厂屋顶储罐加注溶剂时存在疏忽，频繁将溶剂洒落在地面上。\n\n溶剂供应商已申请简易判决，理由是：即便存在污染，工厂所有者和供应商也是各自独立地造成了无法确切定量的污染，因此房主无法证明各方分别给她造成了多大程度的损害。\n\n目前没有证据表明工厂所有者与溶剂供应商存在共同行动。\n\n法院是否应批准该简易判决申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应批准，因为对于不可分割的损害，竞合侵权行为人（concurrent tortfeasors）需承担连带责任。","B":"不应批准，因为溶剂供应商需对工厂所有者造成的损害承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","C":"应批准，因为没有依据向溶剂供应商分摊损害赔偿责任。","D":"应批准，因为没有证据表明工厂所有者与溶剂供应商存在共同行动。"},"explanationZh":"多名有过失行为人的责任 不可分割的损害 vs. 可分割的损害 独立行为：连带责任（Joint & several liability）* 或 仅承担各自份额责任（Several liability only） 共同行为：连带责任 *MBE（多州律师资格考试）中的默认规则。\n\n当不存在实质性事实争议且申请人有权依法获得胜诉判决时（例如，当被申请人未能证明其主张时），申请人有权获得简易判决。\n\n过失索赔要求证明侵权行为人的行为实际且法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）性地（actually and proximately）导致了所称的损害。\n\n如果损害由多名侵权行为人造成，则这些侵权行为人可能承担：各自份额责任（severally liable）——即每位侵权行为人仅对其独立行为导致的可分割损害承担相应比例的赔偿责任；或 连带责任（jointly and severally liable）——即任何一位侵权行为人都可能需对全部损害金额承担责任，因为其独立或共同行为同时导致了不可分割的损害。* 本案中，溶剂供应商提出的简易判决申请主张，双方均对污染造成了无法确切量化的影响。\n\n该主张表明房主遭受了不可分割的损害，因此溶剂供应商或设施所有者均可能需对房主的全部损害承担连带责任——即使他们并未采取共同行动。\n\n因此，溶剂供应商无权依法获得胜诉判决，法院应驳回其简易判决申请（选项 C）。\n\n*当原告从任一侵权行为人处获得全额赔偿后，该侵权行为人可向其他侵权行为人追偿（indemnity）或要求分摊（contribution）。\n\n（选项 B）根据替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）原则，委托人（principal）对其代理人（agent）在代理关系范围内（例如雇主与雇员之间）实施的侵权行为承担严格责任。\n\n本案中，溶剂供应商对其雇员造成的损害承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），但不对设施所有者造成的损害承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 D）如果多名侵权行为人的共同行为或独立行为同时导致了不可分割的损害，则适用连带责任。\n\n因此，无需证明设施所有者与溶剂供应商采取了共同行动。\n\n教学目标：当多名侵权行为人共同导致原告受损时，若各侵权行为人的独立行为造成了可分损害，则各自仅就其行为造成的损害份额承担赔偿责任（即“按份责任”）；若各侵权行为人的独立行为或共同行为同时导致了不可分损害，则须就全部损害承担赔偿责任（即“连带责任”）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：责任分担》第 A18 条（美国法律协会，2000年）（连带责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当多名侵权行为人对原告造成损害时：若其各自独立的行动导致了可区分的损害，则各方仅需按比例承担赔偿责任（即按份责任）；若其独立或共同行动同时导致了不可分割的损害，则各方需对损害总额承担责任（即连带责任）。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应批准该简易判决申请？","rule":"当多名侵权行为人对原告造成损害时：若其各自独立的行动导致了可区分的损害，则各方仅需按比例承担赔偿责任（即按份责任）；若其独立或共同行动同时导致了不可分割的损害，则各方需对损害总额承担责任（即连带责任）。","analysis":"多名有过失行为人的责任 不可分割的损害 vs. 可分割的损害 独立行为：连带责任（Joint & several liability）* 或 仅承担各自份额责任（Several liability only） 共同行为：连带责任 *MBE（多州律师资格考试）中的默认规则。\n\n当不存在实质性事实争议且申请人有权依法获得胜诉判决时（例如，当被申请人未能证明其主张时），申请人有权获得简易判决。\n\n过失索赔要求证明侵权行为人的行为实际且法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）性地（actually and proximately）导致了所称的损害。\n\n如果损害由多名侵权行为人造成，则这些侵权行为人可能承担：各自份额责任（severally liable）——即每位侵权行为人仅对其独立行为导致的可分割损害承担相应比例的赔偿责任；或 连带责任（jointly and severally liable）——即任何一位侵权行为人都可能需对全部损害金额承担责任，因为其独立或共同行为同时导致了不可分割的损害。* 本案中，溶剂供应商提出的简易判决申请主张，双方均对污染造成了无法确切量化的影响。\n\n该主张表明房主遭受了不可分割的损害，因此溶剂供应商或设施所有者均可能需对房主的全部损害承担连带责任——即使他们并未采取共同行动。\n\n因此，溶剂供应商无权依法获得胜诉判决，法院应驳回其简易判决申请（选项 C）。\n\n*当原告从任一侵权行为人处获得全额赔偿后，该侵权行为人可向其他侵权行为人追偿（indemnity）或要求分摊（contribution）。\n\n（选项 B）根据替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）原则，委托人（principal）对其代理人（agent）在代理关系范围内（例如雇主与雇员之间）实施的侵权行为承担严格责任。\n\n本案中，溶剂供应商对其雇员造成的损害承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability），但不对设施所有者造成的损害承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n（选项 D）如果多名侵权行为人的共同行为或独立行为同时导致了不可分割的损害，则适用连带责任。\n\n因此，无需证明设施所有者与溶剂供应商采取了共同行动。\n\n教学目标：当多名侵权行为人共同导致原告受损时，若各侵权行为人的独立行为造成了可分损害，则各自仅就其行为造成的损害份额承担赔偿责任（即“按份责任”）；若各侵权行为人的独立行为或共同行为同时导致了不可分损害，则须就全部损害承担赔偿责任（即“连带责任”）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：责任分担》第 A18 条（美国法律协会，2000年）（连带责任）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不应批准，因为对于不可分割的损害，竞合侵权行为人（concurrent tortfeasors）需承担连带责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":144,"sourceQuestionPage":430,"sourceQuestionPages":[430],"sourceExplanationPages":[431,432],"sourceAnswerPages":[431,432],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-145","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A seller sold his boat to a buyer. During negotiations, the buyer said that he planned to sail the boat on the open seas. The seller told the buyer that the boat was seaworthy and had never sustained any significant damage. In fact, the hull of the boat had been badly damaged when the seller had run the boat aground. The seller had then done a cosmetic repair to the hull rather than a structural repair. The buyer relied on the seller's representations and paid a fair price for a boat in good repair, only to discover after the sale was completed that the hull was in fact badly damaged and in a dangerous condition. The seller has refused to refund any of the buyer's money, and the buyer is contemplating suing the seller. Under what theory would the buyer be most likely to recover?","choices":{"A":"Fraud.","B":"Intentional endangerment.","C":"Negligent misrepresentation.","D":"Strict products liability."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Misrepresentation\n\n(fraudulent v. negligent)\n\nConduct Setting Harm\n\nFraudulent Knowingly or recklessly Any setting Justifiable misrepresented material fact to reliance induce reliance caused\n\npecuniary loss Negligent Negligently provided plaintiff Commercial setting or Justifiable false information setting with risk of reliance physical harm caused\n\npecuniary loss or physical harm To recover for fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation or deceit), the plaintiff must show that: the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresented a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and\n\nthe plaintiff justifiably relied on the defendant's misrepresentation and suffered pecuniary loss (ie, monetary loss).\n\nHere, the seller knowingly misrepresented that the boat had never sustained significant damage with the intent to persuade the buyer to purchase it. Since the seller had cosmetically repaired the boat's damaged hull, the buyer's reliance on that misrepresentation was reasonable. The buyer then suffered a pecuniary loss when he paid a fair price for a boat in good repair but received a badly damaged one. Therefore, the buyer will likely recover for fraud. (Choice B) Intentional endangerment (conduct that is likely to cause death or serious bodily harm to another) is a theory of criminal liability—not civil liability. (Choice C) Negligent misrepresentation occurs when a defendant negligently provides the plaintiff with false information in a transaction where the defendant has a pecuniary interest (ie, a commercial setting). Although the seller had a pecuniary interest in the boat transaction, his misrepresentation was made knowingly—not merely negligently. As a result, negligent misrepresentation is not the best theory of recovery.\n\n(Choice D) Strict products liability is only imposed on a commercial supplier (eg, manufacturer, distributor, retailer)—not a casual seller (eg, an individual selling a car to a friend). Since there is no evidence that the seller is a commercial supplier, the buyer is unlikely to recover on this basis.\n\nEducational objective: Fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation or deceit) liability arises when (1) the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresents a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and (2) the plaintiff reasonably relies on that misrepresentation and suffers pecuniary loss.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 525 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (fraudulent misrepresentation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 552 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (negligent misrepresentation).","educationalObjective":"Fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation or deceit) liability arises when (1) the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresents a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and (2) the plaintiff reasonably relies on that misrepresentation and suffers pecuniary loss.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 525 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (fraudulent misrepresentation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 552 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (negligent misrepresentation).","rule":"Fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation or deceit) liability arises when (1) the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresents a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and (2) the plaintiff reasonably relies on that misrepresentation and suffers pecuniary loss.","irac":{"issue":"Under what theory would the buyer be most likely to recover?","rule":"Fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation or deceit) liability arises when (1) the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresents a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and (2) the plaintiff reasonably relies on that misrepresentation and suffers pecuniary loss.","analysis":"Misrepresentation\n\n(fraudulent v. negligent)\n\nConduct Setting Harm\n\nFraudulent Knowingly or recklessly Any setting Justifiable misrepresented material fact to reliance induce reliance caused\n\npecuniary loss Negligent Negligently provided plaintiff Commercial setting or Justifiable false information setting with risk of reliance physical harm caused\n\npecuniary loss or physical harm To recover for fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation or deceit), the plaintiff must show that: the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresented a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and\n\nthe plaintiff justifiably relied on the defendant's misrepresentation and suffered pecuniary loss (ie, monetary loss).\n\nHere, the seller knowingly misrepresented that the boat had never sustained significant damage with the intent to persuade the buyer to purchase it. Since the seller had cosmetically repaired the boat's damaged hull, the buyer's reliance on that misrepresentation was reasonable. The buyer then suffered a pecuniary loss when he paid a fair price for a boat in good repair but received a badly damaged one. Therefore, the buyer will likely recover for fraud. (Choice B) Intentional endangerment (conduct that is likely to cause death or serious bodily harm to another) is a theory of criminal liability—not civil liability. (Choice C) Negligent misrepresentation occurs when a defendant negligently provides the plaintiff with false information in a transaction where the defendant has a pecuniary interest (ie, a commercial setting). Although the seller had a pecuniary interest in the boat transaction, his misrepresentation was made knowingly—not merely negligently. As a result, negligent misrepresentation is not the best theory of recovery.\n\n(Choice D) Strict products liability is only imposed on a commercial supplier (eg, manufacturer, distributor, retailer)—not a casual seller (eg, an individual selling a car to a friend). Since there is no evidence that the seller is a commercial supplier, the buyer is unlikely to recover on this basis.","conclusion":"Answer A: Fraud."},"questionZh":"卖方将船只出售给了买方。\n\n在谈判过程中，买方表示计划驾驶该船在公海航行。\n\n卖方告知买方，该船适航且从未遭受过重大损坏。\n\n事实上，卖方曾因搁浅事故导致船体严重受损，随后仅对船体进行了外观上的修补，而未进行结构性修复。\n\n买方信赖卖方的陈述，并按状况良好的船只应有的合理价格支付了款项；然而交易完成后，买方才发现船体实际上严重受损，处于危险状态。\n\n卖方拒绝退还任何款项，买方正考虑起诉卖方。\n\n买方最有可能依据何种法律理论获得赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"欺诈。","B":"故意危害行为。","C":"过失虚假陈述。","D":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。"},"explanationZh":"虚假陈述 （欺诈性与过失性） **行为** | **主观心态** | **情境** | **导致后果** **欺诈性** | 明知或鲁莽 | 任何情境 | 诱导原告产生合理信赖，导致金钱损失 **过失性** | 过失提供虚假信息 | 商业情境或存在信赖风险的情境 | 导致金钱损失或人身伤害 若要就欺诈（即故意虚假陈述或欺骗）主张赔偿，原告必须证明：被告明知或鲁莽地对重要事实作出了虚假陈述，意图诱导原告产生信赖；且 原告合理地信赖了被告的虚假陈述，并遭受了金钱损失（即金钱上的损失）。\n\n在本案中，卖方明知该船只曾遭受严重损坏，却虚假陈述称其从未受过重大损坏，意图以此说服买方购买。\n\n由于卖方对受损船体进行了外观修复，买方信赖该虚假陈述是合理的。\n\n买方支付了相当于一艘状况良好的船只的合理价格，却收到了一艘严重受损的船只，从而遭受了金钱损失。\n\n因此，买方很可能因欺诈获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）故意危害行为（即可能导致他人死亡或严重身体伤害的行为）属于刑事责任的范畴，而非民事责任。\n\n（选项 C）过失性虚假陈述是指被告在自身拥有金钱利益的交易中（即商业情境），过失地向原告提供虚假信息。\n\n尽管卖方在该船只交易中拥有金钱利益，但其虚假陈述是明知故犯的，而不仅仅是出于过失。\n\n因此，过失性虚假陈述并非最佳的索赔依据。\n\n（选项 D）严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）仅适用于商业供应商（例如制造商、分销商、零售商），而不适用于偶尔出售物品的卖方（例如向朋友出售汽车的个人）。\n\n由于没有证据表明该卖方是商业供应商，买方不太可能基于此理由获得赔偿。\n\n**教育目标**：当满足以下条件时，即产生欺诈（即故意虚假陈述或欺骗）责任：(1) 被告明知或鲁莽地对重要事实作出虚假陈述，意图诱导原告产生信赖；(2) 原告合理地信赖了该虚假陈述并遭受了金钱损失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第525条（美国法律学会，1977年）（欺诈性虚假陈述）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第552条（美国法律学会，1977年）（过失性虚假陈述）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"欺诈（即故意虚假陈述或欺骗）责任的构成要件包括：(1) 被告明知或罔顾事实（鲁莽地）虚假陈述某项重要事实，意图诱使原告产生信赖；以及 (2) 原告合理信赖了该虚假陈述并遭受了金钱损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"买方最有可能依据何种法律理论获得赔偿？","rule":"欺诈（即故意虚假陈述或欺骗）责任的构成要件包括：(1) 被告明知或罔顾事实（鲁莽地）虚假陈述某项重要事实，意图诱使原告产生信赖；以及 (2) 原告合理信赖了该虚假陈述并遭受了金钱损失。","analysis":"虚假陈述 （欺诈性与过失性） **行为** | **主观心态** | **情境** | **导致后果** **欺诈性** | 明知或鲁莽 | 任何情境 | 诱导原告产生合理信赖，导致金钱损失 **过失性** | 过失提供虚假信息 | 商业情境或存在信赖风险的情境 | 导致金钱损失或人身伤害 若要就欺诈（即故意虚假陈述或欺骗）主张赔偿，原告必须证明：被告明知或鲁莽地对重要事实作出了虚假陈述，意图诱导原告产生信赖；且 原告合理地信赖了被告的虚假陈述，并遭受了金钱损失（即金钱上的损失）。\n\n在本案中，卖方明知该船只曾遭受严重损坏，却虚假陈述称其从未受过重大损坏，意图以此说服买方购买。\n\n由于卖方对受损船体进行了外观修复，买方信赖该虚假陈述是合理的。\n\n买方支付了相当于一艘状况良好的船只的合理价格，却收到了一艘严重受损的船只，从而遭受了金钱损失。\n\n因此，买方很可能因欺诈获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）故意危害行为（即可能导致他人死亡或严重身体伤害的行为）属于刑事责任的范畴，而非民事责任。\n\n（选项 C）过失性虚假陈述是指被告在自身拥有金钱利益的交易中（即商业情境），过失地向原告提供虚假信息。\n\n尽管卖方在该船只交易中拥有金钱利益，但其虚假陈述是明知故犯的，而不仅仅是出于过失。\n\n因此，过失性虚假陈述并非最佳的索赔依据。\n\n（选项 D）严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）仅适用于商业供应商（例如制造商、分销商、零售商），而不适用于偶尔出售物品的卖方（例如向朋友出售汽车的个人）。\n\n由于没有证据表明该卖方是商业供应商，买方不太可能基于此理由获得赔偿。\n\n**教育目标**：当满足以下条件时，即产生欺诈（即故意虚假陈述或欺骗）责任：(1) 被告明知或鲁莽地对重要事实作出虚假陈述，意图诱导原告产生信赖；(2) 原告合理地信赖了该虚假陈述并遭受了金钱损失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第525条（美国法律学会，1977年）（欺诈性虚假陈述）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第552条（美国法律学会，1977年）（过失性虚假陈述）。","conclusion":"答案 A：欺诈。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":145,"sourceQuestionPage":433,"sourceQuestionPages":[433],"sourceExplanationPages":[434,435],"sourceAnswerPages":[434,435],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-146","subject":"Torts","topic":"Nuisance","question":"A homeowner owns a house on a lake. A neighbor owns a house across a driveway from the homeowner's property. The neighbor's house sits on a hill and the neighbor can see the lake from his living room window. The homeowner and the neighbor got into an argument and the homeowner erected a large spotlight on his property that automatically comes on at dusk and goes off at sunrise. The only reason the homeowner installed the light was to annoy the neighbor. The glare from the light severely detracts from the neighbor's view of the lake. In a suit by the neighbor against the homeowner, will the neighbor prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the neighbor's view of the lake is not always obstructed.","B":"No, if the spotlight provides added security to the homeowner's property.","C":"Yes, because the homeowner installed the light solely to annoy the neighbor.","D":"Yes, if, and only if, the neighbor's property value is adversely affected."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Unreasonable interferences as a matter of law\n\nMalicious or indecent Defendant's sole purpose is to harm plaintiff (eg, fence built to conduct block view) or Defendant's conduct is indecent (eg, obscene gestures) Severe harm Plaintiff's harm is severe (eg, physical harm)\n\nAvoidable harm Defendant could avoid plaintiff's harm without undue hardship Conduct unsuited Defendant's conduct is unsuited to location (eg, tavern in residential area) and to location Plaintiff's use & enjoyment of property is suited to location When a person interferes with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property, the plaintiff can sue for private nuisance. To prevail in that suit, the plaintiff must prove that the interference was both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. An interference is unreasonable as a matter of law if the defendant's sole purpose was to harm the plaintiff because a harmful interference has no legal utility (eg, no social value). Here, the homeowner substantially interfered with the neighbor's use and enjoyment of his property by installing a spotlight that severely detracted from his view of the lake each night. And since the spotlight was only installed to annoy the neighbor, that interference was unreasonable as a matter of law (no utility). Therefore, the neighbor will likely prevail in a private nuisance claim. (Choice A) While evidence that an interference is continuous may prove that the interference is substantial and unreasonable, such evidence is not always required. Therefore, though his view of the lake was not always obstructed, the neighbor can prevail since the spotlight was a substantial and unreasonable interference. (Choice B) Although the spotlight may add security to the homeowner's property (utility), the homeowner installed it for the sole purpose of annoying the neighbor—not for added security. Therefore, the interference was unreasonable as a matter of law and the neighbor will prevail. (Choice D) Private nuisance does not require proof that the plaintiff's property value decreased. Instead, the neighbor will prevail because the spotlight substantially interfered\n\nwith his use and enjoyment of his property and the homeowner installed it solely to annoy him.\n\nEducational objective: Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is unreasonable if the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct—eg, when the defendant's sole purpose was to harm the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 821D, 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (liability for private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonableness requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 829 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (malicious or indecent conduct).","educationalObjective":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is unreasonable if the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct—eg, when the defendant's sole purpose was to harm the plaintiff.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 821D, 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (liability for private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonableness requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 829 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (malicious or indecent conduct).","rule":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is unreasonable if the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct—eg, when the defendant's sole purpose was to harm the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit by the neighbor against the homeowner, will the neighbor prevail?","rule":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is unreasonable if the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct—eg, when the defendant's sole purpose was to harm the plaintiff.","analysis":"Unreasonable interferences as a matter of law\n\nMalicious or indecent Defendant's sole purpose is to harm plaintiff (eg, fence built to conduct block view) or Defendant's conduct is indecent (eg, obscene gestures) Severe harm Plaintiff's harm is severe (eg, physical harm)\n\nAvoidable harm Defendant could avoid plaintiff's harm without undue hardship Conduct unsuited Defendant's conduct is unsuited to location (eg, tavern in residential area) and to location Plaintiff's use & enjoyment of property is suited to location When a person interferes with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property, the plaintiff can sue for private nuisance. To prevail in that suit, the plaintiff must prove that the interference was both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. An interference is unreasonable as a matter of law if the defendant's sole purpose was to harm the plaintiff because a harmful interference has no legal utility (eg, no social value). Here, the homeowner substantially interfered with the neighbor's use and enjoyment of his property by installing a spotlight that severely detracted from his view of the lake each night. And since the spotlight was only installed to annoy the neighbor, that interference was unreasonable as a matter of law (no utility). Therefore, the neighbor will likely prevail in a private nuisance claim. (Choice A) While evidence that an interference is continuous may prove that the interference is substantial and unreasonable, such evidence is not always required. Therefore, though his view of the lake was not always obstructed, the neighbor can prevail since the spotlight was a substantial and unreasonable interference. (Choice B) Although the spotlight may add security to the homeowner's property (utility), the homeowner installed it for the sole purpose of annoying the neighbor—not for added security. Therefore, the interference was unreasonable as a matter of law and the neighbor will prevail. (Choice D) Private nuisance does not require proof that the plaintiff's property value decreased. Instead, the neighbor will prevail because the spotlight substantially interfered\n\nwith his use and enjoyment of his property and the homeowner installed it solely to annoy him.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the homeowner installed the light solely to annoy the neighbor."},"questionZh":"一位房主在湖边拥有一处房产。\n\n邻居的房子位于房主房产的另一侧，两者之间隔着一条车道。\n\n邻居的房子建在山坡上，从客厅窗户可以看到湖景。\n\n房主与邻居发生了争执，随后在自家地块上安装了一盏大型聚光灯，该灯会在黄昏时自动开启，并在日出时自动关闭。\n\n房主安装这盏灯的唯一目的是为了骚扰邻居。\n\n灯光的强光严重影响了邻居欣赏湖景。\n\n在邻居针对房主提起的诉讼中，邻居会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为邻居的湖景视野并未一直受到阻挡。","B":"不会，如果该聚光灯为房主的房产提供了额外的安保作用。","C":"会，因为房主安装该灯的唯一目的是为了骚扰邻居。","D":"会，但前提是邻居的房产价值因此受到不利影响。"},"explanationZh":"法律认定为“不合理干扰”的情形 恶意或不当行为：被告的唯一目的是伤害原告（例如，建造围栏以遮挡视线），或被告的行为不当（例如，做出猥亵手势）。严重损害：原告遭受的损害严重（例如，身体伤害）。 可避免的损害：被告本可在不造成过度困难的情况下避免给原告造成损害。行为不适宜：被告的行为与所在地点不符（例如，在住宅区开设酒馆），而原告对财产的使用和享受则是适宜该地点的。当某人干扰原告对其财产的使用和享受时，原告可以提起私人妨害（private nuisance）诉讼。\n\n若要胜诉，原告必须证明该干扰既是“实质性的”（即对社区中的普通人而言是令人反感、恼火或无法忍受的），又是“不合理的”（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用）。\n\n如果被告的唯一目的是伤害原告，则该干扰在法律上被认定为不合理，因为这种有害干扰不具备法律效用（例如，没有社会价值）。\n\n在本案中，房主安装了一盏聚光灯，每晚严重阻碍了邻居欣赏湖景，从而实质性地干扰了邻居对其财产的使用和享受。\n\n鉴于安装该聚光灯的唯一目的是激怒邻居，这种干扰在法律上即被视为不合理（缺乏效用）。\n\n因此，邻居很可能在私人妨害诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）虽然干扰具有持续性的证据可以证明其属于实质性且不合理的干扰，但此类证据并非必要条件。\n\n因此，即使邻居的湖景并未时刻受阻，只要聚光灯构成了实质性且不合理的干扰，邻居仍可胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）尽管聚光灯可能增强了房主财产的安全性（即具备效用），但房主安装它的唯一目的是激怒邻居，而非为了增加安全性。\n\n因此，该干扰在法律上是不合理的，邻居将会胜诉。\n\n（选项 D）私人妨害诉讼并不要求证明原告的财产价值有所下降。\n\n相反，邻居之所以能胜诉，是因为聚光灯实质性地干扰了 他对财产的使用和享受，且房主安装该灯的唯一目的是激怒他。\n\n教学目标：私害（private nuisance）是指对原告使用和享用其财产进行的实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用（例如，被告的唯一目的是伤害原告），则该干扰即被视为不合理。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D、822 条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于私害的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 826 条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于不合理性的要求）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 829 条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于恶意或不当行为）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"私人妨害（private nuisance）是指对原告使用和享用其房产的实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用（例如，被告的唯一目的是伤害原告），则该干扰被视为不合理。","iracZh":{"issue":"在邻居针对房主提起的诉讼中，邻居会胜诉吗？","rule":"私人妨害（private nuisance）是指对原告使用和享用其房产的实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用（例如，被告的唯一目的是伤害原告），则该干扰被视为不合理。","analysis":"法律认定为“不合理干扰”的情形 恶意或不当行为：被告的唯一目的是伤害原告（例如，建造围栏以遮挡视线），或被告的行为不当（例如，做出猥亵手势）。严重损害：原告遭受的损害严重（例如，身体伤害）。 可避免的损害：被告本可在不造成过度困难的情况下避免给原告造成损害。行为不适宜：被告的行为与所在地点不符（例如，在住宅区开设酒馆），而原告对财产的使用和享受则是适宜该地点的。当某人干扰原告对其财产的使用和享受时，原告可以提起私人妨害（private nuisance）诉讼。\n\n若要胜诉，原告必须证明该干扰既是“实质性的”（即对社区中的普通人而言是令人反感、恼火或无法忍受的），又是“不合理的”（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用）。\n\n如果被告的唯一目的是伤害原告，则该干扰在法律上被认定为不合理，因为这种有害干扰不具备法律效用（例如，没有社会价值）。\n\n在本案中，房主安装了一盏聚光灯，每晚严重阻碍了邻居欣赏湖景，从而实质性地干扰了邻居对其财产的使用和享受。\n\n鉴于安装该聚光灯的唯一目的是激怒邻居，这种干扰在法律上即被视为不合理（缺乏效用）。\n\n因此，邻居很可能在私人妨害诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）虽然干扰具有持续性的证据可以证明其属于实质性且不合理的干扰，但此类证据并非必要条件。\n\n因此，即使邻居的湖景并未时刻受阻，只要聚光灯构成了实质性且不合理的干扰，邻居仍可胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）尽管聚光灯可能增强了房主财产的安全性（即具备效用），但房主安装它的唯一目的是激怒邻居，而非为了增加安全性。\n\n因此，该干扰在法律上是不合理的，邻居将会胜诉。\n\n（选项 D）私人妨害诉讼并不要求证明原告的财产价值有所下降。\n\n相反，邻居之所以能胜诉，是因为聚光灯实质性地干扰了 他对财产的使用和享受，且房主安装该灯的唯一目的是激怒他。\n\n教学目标：私害（private nuisance）是指对原告使用和享用其财产进行的实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用（例如，被告的唯一目的是伤害原告），则该干扰即被视为不合理。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D、822 条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于私害的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 826 条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于不合理性的要求）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 829 条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于恶意或不当行为）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，因为房主安装该灯的唯一目的是为了骚扰邻居。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":146,"sourceQuestionPage":436,"sourceQuestionPages":[436],"sourceExplanationPages":[437,438],"sourceAnswerPages":[437,438],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-147","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A woman and a man, who were professional rivals, were attending a computer industry dinner where each was to receive an award for achievement in the field of data processing. The man engaged the woman in conversation and expressed the opinion that if they joined forces, they could do even better. The woman replied that she would not consider him as a business partner. When the man demanded to know why, the woman attempted to offend him by stating that he was incompetent. The exchange was overheard by a guest who attended the dinner. The man suffered emotional distress but no pecuniary loss. If the man asserts a claim against the woman based on defamation, will he prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man suffered no pecuniary loss.","B":"No, because the woman's statement was made to the man and not to the guest.","C":"Yes, because the woman intended to cause the man emotional distress.","D":"Yes, because the woman should have foreseen that her statement would be overheard by another person."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Intentional v. Negligent communication\n\nIntentional Knowledge or substantial certainty that statement will be communication communicated to third party—eg:\n\nspeaking directly to third person sending message in group chat screaming near crowd Negligent Foreseeable risk that statement will be communicated to third communication party—eg:\n\nleaving document on desk in open area speaking loudly in public Defamation can be either libel (written statements) or slander (spoken statements—as seen here). To establish a defamation claim based on slander, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or should have known that the statement was false.\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party.\n\nThat statement caused the plaintiff special harm or was slanderous per se—eg, accused the plaintiff of conduct adversely affecting the plaintiff's occupation.\n\nNegligent communication (ie, publication) occurs when it is reasonably foreseeable that a third party might hear the defendant's statement. Here, the woman stated that the man was incompetent in his field of data processing (slander per se) during a dinner. Since the man received an achievement award at the dinner, the woman should have known that her statement was false. And though she directed the statement at the man, it was reasonably foreseeable that another dinner guest would overhear her (negligent communication) (Choice B). Therefore, the man will prevail on his defamation claim. (Choice A) Slander generally requires proof of special harm (pecuniary/monetary loss). But since the woman accused the man of professional incompetence (slander per se), his harm is presumed and no proof of special harm is required. (Choice C) Intent to cause emotional distress is not an element of defamation, so the fact that the woman intended to cause the man emotional distress by offending him has no\n\neffect on the viability of his claim. However, such intent would be required had the man sued for intentional infliction of emotional distress.\n\nEducational objective: Defamation requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party. Negligent communication occurs when it is reasonably foreseeable that a third party might hear the defendant's statement.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 577 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (intentional or negligent communication).","educationalObjective":"Defamation requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party. Negligent communication occurs when it is reasonably foreseeable that a third party might hear the defendant's statement.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 577 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (intentional or negligent communication).","rule":"Defamation requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party. Negligent communication occurs when it is reasonably foreseeable that a third party might hear the defendant's statement.","irac":{"issue":"If the man asserts a claim against the woman based on defamation, will he prevail?","rule":"Defamation requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party. Negligent communication occurs when it is reasonably foreseeable that a third party might hear the defendant's statement.","analysis":"Intentional v. Negligent communication\n\nIntentional Knowledge or substantial certainty that statement will be communication communicated to third party—eg:\n\nspeaking directly to third person sending message in group chat screaming near crowd Negligent Foreseeable risk that statement will be communicated to third communication party—eg:\n\nleaving document on desk in open area speaking loudly in public Defamation can be either libel (written statements) or slander (spoken statements—as seen here). To establish a defamation claim based on slander, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or should have known that the statement was false.\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party.\n\nThat statement caused the plaintiff special harm or was slanderous per se—eg, accused the plaintiff of conduct adversely affecting the plaintiff's occupation.\n\nNegligent communication (ie, publication) occurs when it is reasonably foreseeable that a third party might hear the defendant's statement. Here, the woman stated that the man was incompetent in his field of data processing (slander per se) during a dinner. Since the man received an achievement award at the dinner, the woman should have known that her statement was false. And though she directed the statement at the man, it was reasonably foreseeable that another dinner guest would overhear her (negligent communication) (Choice B). Therefore, the man will prevail on his defamation claim. (Choice A) Slander generally requires proof of special harm (pecuniary/monetary loss). But since the woman accused the man of professional incompetence (slander per se), his harm is presumed and no proof of special harm is required. (Choice C) Intent to cause emotional distress is not an element of defamation, so the fact that the woman intended to cause the man emotional distress by offending him has no\n\neffect on the viability of his claim. However, such intent would be required had the man sued for intentional infliction of emotional distress.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the woman should have foreseen that her statement would be overheard by another person."},"questionZh":"一男一女是职场上的竞争对手，两人一同出席了一场计算机行业的晚宴，并双双因在数据处理领域的成就而获颁奖项。\n\n席间，该男子主动与女子攀谈，并提出若两人联手合作，定能取得更卓越的成就。\n\n女子当即表示不愿与他建立商业合作伙伴关系。\n\n当男子追问原因时，女子为了冒犯他，直言他能力不足。\n\n这一对话被在场的一位宾客无意中听到。\n\n该男子因此遭受了精神上的痛苦，但并未产生任何经济损失。\n\n若该男子以诽谤为由向该女子提出索赔，他能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为该男子未遭受经济损失。","B":"不能，因为该女子的言论是针对该男子本人发表的，而非针对那位宾客。","C":"能，因为该女子有意导致该男子遭受精神痛苦。","D":"能，因为该女子本应预见到其言论会被他人听到。"},"explanationZh":"故意传播与过失传播 故意传播：明知或确信某项陈述会被传达给第三方——例如： 直接向第三人说话、在群聊中发送信息、在人群附近大喊大叫。过失传播：存在可预见的风险，即某项陈述会被传达给第三方——例如： 将文件留在开放区域的桌面上、在公共场合大声说话。诽谤既可以是书面诽谤（libel，即书面陈述），也可以是口头诽谤（slander，即口头陈述——本案即属此类）。\n\n要确立基于口头诽谤的索赔，原告必须证明以下所有要点：被告明知关于原告的陈述是虚假的，或者本应知道该陈述是虚假的。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或过失地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n该陈述给原告造成了特殊损害（special harm），或者属于“本身构成诽谤”（slander per se）的情形——例如，指控原告有对其职业产生不利影响的行为。\n\n过失传播（即“发布”行为）发生于下述情况：即被告的陈述被第三方听到的可能性是合理可预见的。\n\n在本案中，该女子在晚宴上声称该男子在其数据处理领域能力不足（这属于“本身构成诽谤”）。\n\n鉴于该男子在晚宴上获得了成就奖，该女子本应知道其陈述是虚假的。\n\n尽管她是针对该男子发表该陈述的，但其他晚宴宾客听到该陈述的可能性是合理可预见的（属于过失传播）（选项 B）。\n\n因此，该男子的诽谤索赔将获得胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）口头诽谤通常需要证明存在特殊损害（即金钱/经济损失）。\n\n但由于该女子指控该男子职业能力不足（属于“本身构成诽谤”），其受到的损害被视为已确立，无需证明存在特殊损害。\n\n（选项 C）意图造成精神痛苦并非诽谤构成的要素，因此，该女子意图通过冒犯该男子来使其遭受精神痛苦这一事实，对其索赔主张的有效性 没有影响。\n\n然而，如果该男子起诉的是“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（intentional infliction of emotional distress），则需要具备这种意图。\n\n教育目标：诽谤索赔要求证明被告故意或过失地向第三方传达了关于原告的虚假陈述。\n\n当被告的陈述被第三方听到的可能性是合理可预见的时，即构成过失传播。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第577条（美国法律学会，1977年）（故意或过失传播）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"构成诽谤须证明被告故意或过失地向第三方传达了关于原告的不实陈述。\n\n若被告在发表言论时，可合理预见第三方可能会听到该言论，即构成过失传达。","iracZh":{"issue":"若该男子以诽谤为由向该女子提出索赔，他能胜诉吗？","rule":"构成诽谤须证明被告故意或过失地向第三方传达了关于原告的不实陈述。\n\n若被告在发表言论时，可合理预见第三方可能会听到该言论，即构成过失传达。","analysis":"故意传播与过失传播 故意传播：明知或确信某项陈述会被传达给第三方——例如： 直接向第三人说话、在群聊中发送信息、在人群附近大喊大叫。过失传播：存在可预见的风险，即某项陈述会被传达给第三方——例如： 将文件留在开放区域的桌面上、在公共场合大声说话。诽谤既可以是书面诽谤（libel，即书面陈述），也可以是口头诽谤（slander，即口头陈述——本案即属此类）。\n\n要确立基于口头诽谤的索赔，原告必须证明以下所有要点：被告明知关于原告的陈述是虚假的，或者本应知道该陈述是虚假的。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或过失地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n该陈述给原告造成了特殊损害（special harm），或者属于“本身构成诽谤”（slander per se）的情形——例如，指控原告有对其职业产生不利影响的行为。\n\n过失传播（即“发布”行为）发生于下述情况：即被告的陈述被第三方听到的可能性是合理可预见的。\n\n在本案中，该女子在晚宴上声称该男子在其数据处理领域能力不足（这属于“本身构成诽谤”）。\n\n鉴于该男子在晚宴上获得了成就奖，该女子本应知道其陈述是虚假的。\n\n尽管她是针对该男子发表该陈述的，但其他晚宴宾客听到该陈述的可能性是合理可预见的（属于过失传播）（选项 B）。\n\n因此，该男子的诽谤索赔将获得胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）口头诽谤通常需要证明存在特殊损害（即金钱/经济损失）。\n\n但由于该女子指控该男子职业能力不足（属于“本身构成诽谤”），其受到的损害被视为已确立，无需证明存在特殊损害。\n\n（选项 C）意图造成精神痛苦并非诽谤构成的要素，因此，该女子意图通过冒犯该男子来使其遭受精神痛苦这一事实，对其索赔主张的有效性 没有影响。\n\n然而，如果该男子起诉的是“故意造成严重精神损害（IIED）”（intentional infliction of emotional distress），则需要具备这种意图。\n\n教育目标：诽谤索赔要求证明被告故意或过失地向第三方传达了关于原告的虚假陈述。\n\n当被告的陈述被第三方听到的可能性是合理可预见的时，即构成过失传播。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第577条（美国法律学会，1977年）（故意或过失传播）。","conclusion":"答案 D：能，因为该女子本应预见到其言论会被他人听到。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"slander","ipa":"/slˈændɝ/","syllables":"slan·der","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 中伤, 诽谤；vt. 中伤, 诽谤","contextualMeaning":"口头诽谤：以口头或较短暂形式实施的诽谤","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:slanders/i:slandering/d:slandered/p:slandered/3:slanders","sourceSentence":"Negligent communication Intentional Knowledge or substantial certainty that statement will be communication communicated to third party—eg: speaking directly to third person sending message in group chat screaming near crowd Negligent Foreseeable risk that statement will be communicated to third communication party—eg: leaving document on desk in open area speaking loudly in public Defamation can be either libel (wri","synonyms":["aspersion","calumny","defamation","denigration"]},{"term":"libel","ipa":"/lˈaɪbəl/","syllables":"li·bel","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 以文字损害名誉, 诽谤罪, 侮辱；v. 诽谤, 中伤, 损害名誉","contextualMeaning":"书面诽谤：以书写、印刷或类似较持久形式实施的诽谤","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:libelled/s:libels/p:libelled/3:libels/i:libelling","sourceSentence":"Negligent communication Intentional Knowledge or substantial certainty that statement will be communication communicated to third party—eg: speaking directly to third person sending message in group chat screaming near crowd Negligent Foreseeable risk that statement will be communicated to third communication party—eg: leaving document on desk in open area speaking loudly in public Defamation can be either libel (wri","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"processing","ipa":"/pɹˈɑsɛsɪŋ/","syllables":"pro·cess·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[计] 处理；[化] 加工","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“processing”","wordForm":"名词；词形 0:process/1:i/i:processing","sourceSentence":"A woman and a man, who were professional rivals, were attending a computer industry dinner where each was to receive an award for achievement in the field of data processing.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"slanderous","ipa":"/slˈændɝəs/","syllables":"slan·der·ous","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 中伤性的, 中伤的, 挖苦人的；[法] 诽谤的, 造谣中伤的, 诋毁的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“slanderous”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"That statement caused the plaintiff special harm or was slanderous per se—eg, accused the plaintiff of conduct adversely affecting the plaintiff's occupation.","synonyms":["calumniatory","calumnious","defamatory","denigrative"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":147,"sourceQuestionPage":439,"sourceQuestionPages":[439],"sourceExplanationPages":[440,441],"sourceAnswerPages":[440,441],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-148","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A lawyer conducted a free consultation with a businessman who wanted advice on a tax matter for which he sought a refund from the IRS. The lawyer declined to represent the businessman, but an eager intern who had overheard their conversation stopped the businessman as he was leaving the office. The intern told the businessman that she was a law student and wanted to share her opinion with him. She then told the businessman that she believed that the applicable statute of limitations for claiming a refund had more than a year to run and that he had plenty of time in which to act before his legal rights would expire. Although the intern believed this statement to be accurate, the statute of limitations for claiming a refund had only days to run at the time of the consultation. Relying on the intern's statement, the businessman delayed in contacting another attorney to assist him with the tax refund claim. As a result, his refund claim was barred by the statute of limitations. The businessman has sued the intern for negligent misrepresentation, seeking damages for financial loss resulting from his inability to timely file for a refund. The intern has filed a motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim. How should the court rule on the intern's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny it, because the businessman is seeking to recover only pecuniary damages and not damages for physical harm.","B":"Deny it, because the intern should have known that her statement about the statute of limitations was false.","C":"Grant it, because the intern believed that the statute of limitations had more than a year to run when she made that statement.","D":"Grant it, because the intern was expressing a lay opinion that the businessman should not have relied upon."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Misrepresentation\n\n(fraudulent v. negligent)\n\nConduct Setting Harm\n\nFraudulent Knowingly or recklessly Any setting Justifiable misrepresented material fact to reliance induce reliance caused\n\npecuniary loss Negligent Negligently provided plaintiff Commercial setting or Justifiable false information setting with risk of reliance physical harm caused\n\npecuniary loss or physical harm Negligent misrepresentation is based upon a breach of the duty to supply correct information and often arises in the context of accountants and other suppliers of commercial information (eg, attorneys). This tort requires proof of the following elements: The defendant negligently provided false information during the course of his/her business or profession.\n\nThe plaintiff justifiably relied upon the false information and suffered pecuniary (ie, financial) loss as a result (Choice A).\n\nThe plaintiff was in a contractual relationship with the defendant or was a third party known by the defendant as one for whose benefit the information was supplied.\n\nA plaintiff's reliance was not justified if the information was obviously false or if the defendant was clearly stating a lay opinion. Here, the intern provided the businessman with false information regarding the statute of limitations. However, she was clearly expressing a lay opinion since she told the businessman that she was still a law student and merely offering an opinion. As a result, the businessman cannot establish that he was justified in relying upon the information provided, and the intern's motion to dismiss should be granted (Choice B).* *Additionally, there was no contractual relationship between the businessman and the intern, so the businessman also cannot establish that element of negligent misrepresentation.\n\n(Choice C) Negligent misrepresentation requires that the defendant act negligently. Negligence will be found if the defendant failed to exercise the same level of care and competence as a reasonable person in his/her business or professional position. The standard is an objective one, so the intern's subjective belief that her statement was true is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: One of the elements of negligent misrepresentation is that the plaintiff's reliance on the false information provided was justifiable. Reliance was not justified if the information was obviously false or the defendant was clearly stating a lay opinion.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 552 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (negligent misrepresentation).","educationalObjective":"One of the elements of negligent misrepresentation is that the plaintiff's reliance on the false information provided was justifiable. Reliance was not justified if the information was obviously false or the defendant was clearly stating a lay opinion.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 552 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (negligent misrepresentation).","rule":"One of the elements of negligent misrepresentation is that the plaintiff's reliance on the false information provided was justifiable. Reliance was not justified if the information was obviously false or the defendant was clearly stating a lay opinion.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule on the intern's motion?","rule":"One of the elements of negligent misrepresentation is that the plaintiff's reliance on the false information provided was justifiable. Reliance was not justified if the information was obviously false or the defendant was clearly stating a lay opinion.","analysis":"Misrepresentation\n\n(fraudulent v. negligent)\n\nConduct Setting Harm\n\nFraudulent Knowingly or recklessly Any setting Justifiable misrepresented material fact to reliance induce reliance caused\n\npecuniary loss Negligent Negligently provided plaintiff Commercial setting or Justifiable false information setting with risk of reliance physical harm caused\n\npecuniary loss or physical harm Negligent misrepresentation is based upon a breach of the duty to supply correct information and often arises in the context of accountants and other suppliers of commercial information (eg, attorneys). This tort requires proof of the following elements: The defendant negligently provided false information during the course of his/her business or profession.\n\nThe plaintiff justifiably relied upon the false information and suffered pecuniary (ie, financial) loss as a result (Choice A).\n\nThe plaintiff was in a contractual relationship with the defendant or was a third party known by the defendant as one for whose benefit the information was supplied.\n\nA plaintiff's reliance was not justified if the information was obviously false or if the defendant was clearly stating a lay opinion. Here, the intern provided the businessman with false information regarding the statute of limitations. However, she was clearly expressing a lay opinion since she told the businessman that she was still a law student and merely offering an opinion. As a result, the businessman cannot establish that he was justified in relying upon the information provided, and the intern's motion to dismiss should be granted (Choice B).* *Additionally, there was no contractual relationship between the businessman and the intern, so the businessman also cannot establish that element of negligent misrepresentation.\n\n(Choice C) Negligent misrepresentation requires that the defendant act negligently. Negligence will be found if the defendant failed to exercise the same level of care and competence as a reasonable person in his/her business or professional position. The standard is an objective one, so the intern's subjective belief that her statement was true is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer D: Grant it, because the intern was expressing a lay opinion that the businessman should not have relied upon."},"questionZh":"一位律师为一名商人提供了免费咨询，该商人就一项向美国国税局（IRS）申请退税的税务事宜寻求建议。\n\n律师拒绝代理该商人，但一名无意中听到他们谈话的热心实习生在商人离开办公室时拦住了他。\n\n实习生告诉商人，她是一名法学院学生，想分享一下自己的看法。\n\n随后，她告诉商人，她认为申请退税的适用诉讼时效还有一年多的时间，他在合法权利失效前有充裕的时间采取行动。\n\n尽管实习生自认为该说法准确无误，但实际上在咨询时，申请退税的诉讼时效仅剩几天。\n\n由于信赖了实习生的说法，商人推迟了联系其他律师协助处理退税申请。\n\n结果，他的退税申请因超过诉讼时效而被驳回。\n\n商人起诉该实习生构成“过失虚假陈述”（negligent misrepresentation），要求赔偿因未能及时申请退税而造成的经济损失。\n\n实习生提出了驳回起诉的动议，理由是原告未能提出有效的诉讼主张（即“未提出可获支持的诉讼请求”）。\n\n法院应如何裁决实习生的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为商人寻求的仅是金钱损失赔偿，而非人身伤害赔偿。","B":"驳回动议，因为实习生本应知道她关于诉讼时效的说法是错误的。","C":"准予动议，因为实习生在发表该言论时，确实相信诉讼时效还有一年多的时间。","D":"准予动议，因为实习生表达的是一种非专业人士的意见（lay opinion），商人本不应信赖该意见。"},"explanationZh":"虚假陈述 （欺诈性与过失性） **虚假陈述** | **行为** | **适用场景** | **导致后果** | **关键要素** **欺诈性** | 明知或鲁莽地 | 任何场景 | 导致金钱损失 | 虚假陈述了实质性事实，旨在诱导他人信赖，且信赖是合理的 **过失性** | 过失提供虚假信息 | 商业场景或存在信赖风险的场景 | 导致金钱损失或人身伤害 | 过失提供虚假信息，且信赖是合理的 过失性虚假陈述基于违反提供正确信息的义务，常见于会计师及其他商业信息提供者（如律师）的执业活动中。\n\n该侵权行为的成立需证明以下要素：被告在从事业务或职业活动期间过失提供了虚假信息。 原告合理信赖了该虚假信息，并因此遭受了金钱（即财务）损失（选项 A）。\n\n原告与被告之间存在合同关系，或者原告属于被告已知晓的、旨在从该信息中获益的第三方。\n\n如果信息明显虚假，或者被告明确表示其陈述仅为非专业人士的个人意见，则原告的信赖不被视为合理。\n\n在本案中，实习生向商人提供了关于诉讼时效的虚假信息。\n\n然而，由于她明确告知商人自己仍是一名法学院学生且仅提供个人意见，因此她显然是在表达非专业人士的观点。\n\n结果，商人无法证明其信赖该信息是合理的，法院应支持实习生提出的驳回起诉动议（选项 B）。* *此外，商人与实习生之间不存在合同关系，因此商人也无法满足过失性虚假陈述的这一构成要件。\n\n（选项 C）过失性虚假陈述要求被告的行为存在过失。\n\n如果被告未能尽到与其业务或职业身份相称的、理性人应有的注意义务和专业能力，即构成过失。\n\n该标准为客观标准，因此实习生主观上认为其陈述属实这一事实无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：过失性虚假陈述的构成要件之一是原告对所提供虚假信息的信赖必须是合理的。\n\n如果信息明显虚假，或者被告明确表示其陈述仅为非专业人士的个人意见，则信赖不被视为合理。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 552 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（过失性虚假陈述）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"过失虚假陈述的构成要件之一是原告对所提供虚假信息的信赖必须是合理的（justifiable）。\n\n如果信息明显虚假，或者被告明确表示其表达的是非专业人士的意见，则这种信赖不被视为合理。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应如何裁决实习生的动议？","rule":"过失虚假陈述的构成要件之一是原告对所提供虚假信息的信赖必须是合理的（justifiable）。\n\n如果信息明显虚假，或者被告明确表示其表达的是非专业人士的意见，则这种信赖不被视为合理。","analysis":"虚假陈述 （欺诈性与过失性） **虚假陈述** | **行为** | **适用场景** | **导致后果** | **关键要素** **欺诈性** | 明知或鲁莽地 | 任何场景 | 导致金钱损失 | 虚假陈述了实质性事实，旨在诱导他人信赖，且信赖是合理的 **过失性** | 过失提供虚假信息 | 商业场景或存在信赖风险的场景 | 导致金钱损失或人身伤害 | 过失提供虚假信息，且信赖是合理的 过失性虚假陈述基于违反提供正确信息的义务，常见于会计师及其他商业信息提供者（如律师）的执业活动中。\n\n该侵权行为的成立需证明以下要素：被告在从事业务或职业活动期间过失提供了虚假信息。 原告合理信赖了该虚假信息，并因此遭受了金钱（即财务）损失（选项 A）。\n\n原告与被告之间存在合同关系，或者原告属于被告已知晓的、旨在从该信息中获益的第三方。\n\n如果信息明显虚假，或者被告明确表示其陈述仅为非专业人士的个人意见，则原告的信赖不被视为合理。\n\n在本案中，实习生向商人提供了关于诉讼时效的虚假信息。\n\n然而，由于她明确告知商人自己仍是一名法学院学生且仅提供个人意见，因此她显然是在表达非专业人士的观点。\n\n结果，商人无法证明其信赖该信息是合理的，法院应支持实习生提出的驳回起诉动议（选项 B）。* *此外，商人与实习生之间不存在合同关系，因此商人也无法满足过失性虚假陈述的这一构成要件。\n\n（选项 C）过失性虚假陈述要求被告的行为存在过失。\n\n如果被告未能尽到与其业务或职业身份相称的、理性人应有的注意义务和专业能力，即构成过失。\n\n该标准为客观标准，因此实习生主观上认为其陈述属实这一事实无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：过失性虚假陈述的构成要件之一是原告对所提供虚假信息的信赖必须是合理的。\n\n如果信息明显虚假，或者被告明确表示其陈述仅为非专业人士的个人意见，则信赖不被视为合理。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 552 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（过失性虚假陈述）。","conclusion":"答案 D：准予动议，因为实习生表达的是一种非专业人士的意见（lay opinion），商人本不应信赖该意见。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":148,"sourceQuestionPage":442,"sourceQuestionPages":[442],"sourceExplanationPages":[443,444],"sourceAnswerPages":[443,444],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-149","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A newspaper published an editorial in which an editor asserted that a candidate for high political office was a user of illegal drugs. The accusation was untrue. The editor acted unreasonably in not investigating the accusation before publishing it; however, the editor honestly believed that the accusation was true. The candidate sued the editor for defamation. Is the candidate entitled to recover?","choices":{"A":"No, because the accusation appeared in an editorial and was therefore merely an opinion.","B":"No, because the editor honestly believed that the accusation was true.","C":"Yes, because calling someone an illegal drug user is defamatory per se.","D":"Yes, because the accusation was false and injurious to the candidate's reputation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Defamation can be either slander (spoken statements) OR libel (written statements—as seen here). A defamation claim based on libel ordinarily requires proof that: the defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity\n\nthat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation and\n\nthe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party.\n\nBut when the plaintiff is a public figure or official (eg, celebrity, governor), he/she must prove that the false statement was made with actual malice. Actual malice exists when the defendant knew that the statement was false or recklessly disregarded its falsity (ie, had serious doubts about its truthfulness). Therefore, proof of negligence—failure to use reasonable care to determine a statement's falsity—is not enough. Here, the editor falsely accused the candidate of using illegal drugs in a published editorial. Since the candidate is a public figure, he can only recover for defamation if the editor acted with actual malice. And since the editor honestly believed that the accusation was true (no actual malice), the candidate cannot recover for defamation. (Choice A) Defamation can stem from a statement alleging a fact or an opinion that appears to be based on specific facts. Here, the editorial asserted that the candidate used illegal drugs (factual allegation). But a stated belief about the candidate's drug use (opinion) would still be actionable if it implied knowledge of specific facts. (Choice C) Statements that are presumptively harmful to the plaintiff's reputation are considered defamatory per se. Though accusing someone of a criminal act (illegal drug use) is defamatory per se, the candidate will not prevail because the editor believed that the accusation was true (no actual malice). (Choice D) Although the editor's accusation was false and likely injurious to the candidate's reputation, the candidate will not prevail because the editor did not act with actual malice.\n\nEducational objective: A public figure or public official can only recover for defamation if he/she proves that the defendant made a false statement with actual malice—ie, with knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 568 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (libel and slander distinguished).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 580A (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defamation of public official or public figure).","educationalObjective":"A public figure or public official can only recover for defamation if he/she proves that the defendant made a false statement with actual malice—ie, with knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 568 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (libel and slander distinguished).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 580A (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defamation of public official or public figure).","rule":"A public figure or public official can only recover for defamation if he/she proves that the defendant made a false statement with actual malice—ie, with knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity.","irac":{"issue":"Is the candidate entitled to recover?","rule":"A public figure or public official can only recover for defamation if he/she proves that the defendant made a false statement with actual malice—ie, with knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity.","analysis":"Defamation can be either slander (spoken statements) OR libel (written statements—as seen here). A defamation claim based on libel ordinarily requires proof that: the defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity\n\nthat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation and\n\nthe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party.\n\nBut when the plaintiff is a public figure or official (eg, celebrity, governor), he/she must prove that the false statement was made with actual malice. Actual malice exists when the defendant knew that the statement was false or recklessly disregarded its falsity (ie, had serious doubts about its truthfulness). Therefore, proof of negligence—failure to use reasonable care to determine a statement's falsity—is not enough. Here, the editor falsely accused the candidate of using illegal drugs in a published editorial. Since the candidate is a public figure, he can only recover for defamation if the editor acted with actual malice. And since the editor honestly believed that the accusation was true (no actual malice), the candidate cannot recover for defamation. (Choice A) Defamation can stem from a statement alleging a fact or an opinion that appears to be based on specific facts. Here, the editorial asserted that the candidate used illegal drugs (factual allegation). But a stated belief about the candidate's drug use (opinion) would still be actionable if it implied knowledge of specific facts. (Choice C) Statements that are presumptively harmful to the plaintiff's reputation are considered defamatory per se. Though accusing someone of a criminal act (illegal drug use) is defamatory per se, the candidate will not prevail because the editor believed that the accusation was true (no actual malice). (Choice D) Although the editor's accusation was false and likely injurious to the candidate's reputation, the candidate will not prevail because the editor did not act with actual malice.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the editor honestly believed that the accusation was true."},"questionZh":"一家报纸发表了一篇社论，其中一位编辑声称一位高级政治候选人吸食毒品。\n\n该指控不实。\n\n编辑在发表社论前未进行调查，其行为不合理；然而，编辑真诚地相信该指控属实。\n\n该候选人以诽谤罪起诉了该编辑。\n\n该候选人是否有权获得赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为该指控出现在社论中，因此仅仅是编辑的观点。","B":"否，因为编辑真诚地相信该指控属实。","C":"是，因为称某人吸食毒品本身就构成诽谤。","D":"是，因为该指控不实且损害了候选人的名誉。"},"explanationZh":"诽谤既可以是口头诽谤（slander，即口头陈述），也可以是书面诽谤（libel，即书面陈述——本案即属此类）。\n\n基于书面诽谤的索赔通常要求证明以下几点：被告明知故犯地就原告发表了虚假陈述，或者因疏忽而未能核实其虚假性； 该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉；以及 被告故意或因疏忽将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n但当原告属于公众人物或公职人员（如名人、州长）时，其必须证明该虚假陈述是出于“实际恶意”（actual malice）作出的。\n\n所谓“实际恶意”，是指被告明知陈述虚假，或罔顾其虚假性（即对陈述的真实性抱有严重怀疑）。\n\n因此，仅证明存在疏忽——即未尽合理注意义务去核实陈述的虚假性——是不够的。\n\n在本案中，编辑在发表的社论中错误地指控候选人使用非法药物。\n\n由于该候选人是公众人物，他只有在证明编辑具有“实际恶意”的情况下，才能就诽谤获得赔偿。\n\n而鉴于编辑真诚地相信该指控属实（即不存在实际恶意），该候选人无法就诽谤获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）诽谤可能源于陈述事实，也可能源于看似基于特定事实的意见表达。\n\n本案中，社论断言候选人使用了非法药物（属于事实性指控）。\n\n但即便只是表达关于候选人吸毒的看法（属于意见），如果其中暗示了对特定事实的了解，仍可能构成诽谤诉讼的依据。\n\n（选项 C）被推定会损害原告名誉的陈述被视为“本身构成诽谤”（defamatory per se）。\n\n尽管指控某人实施犯罪行为（如使用非法药物）属于本身构成诽谤，但候选人无法胜诉，因为编辑相信该指控属实（即不存在实际恶意）。\n\n（选项 D）尽管编辑的指控不实且可能损害候选人的名誉，但候选人无法胜诉，因为编辑的行为不具备“实际恶意”。\n\n教学目标：公众人物或公职人员若要就诽谤获得赔偿，必须证明被告出于“实际恶意”作出了虚假陈述——即明知陈述虚假或罔顾其虚假性。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 558 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（诽谤的构成要素）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第568条（美国法律协会，1977年）（区分书面诽谤与口头诽谤）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第580A条（美国法律协会，1977年）（针对公职人员或公众人物的诽谤）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"公众人物或公职人员只有在证明被告出于恶意（即明知或罔顾事实）发表虚假陈述的情况下，才能就诽谤获得赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"候选人是否有权获得赔偿？","rule":"公众人物或公职人员只有在证明被告出于恶意（即明知或罔顾事实）发表虚假陈述的情况下，才能就诽谤获得赔偿。","analysis":"诽谤既可以是口头诽谤（slander，即口头陈述），也可以是书面诽谤（libel，即书面陈述——本案即属此类）。\n\n基于书面诽谤的索赔通常要求证明以下几点：被告明知故犯地就原告发表了虚假陈述，或者因疏忽而未能核实其虚假性； 该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉；以及 被告故意或因疏忽将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n但当原告属于公众人物或公职人员（如名人、州长）时，其必须证明该虚假陈述是出于“实际恶意”（actual malice）作出的。\n\n所谓“实际恶意”，是指被告明知陈述虚假，或罔顾其虚假性（即对陈述的真实性抱有严重怀疑）。\n\n因此，仅证明存在疏忽——即未尽合理注意义务去核实陈述的虚假性——是不够的。\n\n在本案中，编辑在发表的社论中错误地指控候选人使用非法药物。\n\n由于该候选人是公众人物，他只有在证明编辑具有“实际恶意”的情况下，才能就诽谤获得赔偿。\n\n而鉴于编辑真诚地相信该指控属实（即不存在实际恶意），该候选人无法就诽谤获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）诽谤可能源于陈述事实，也可能源于看似基于特定事实的意见表达。\n\n本案中，社论断言候选人使用了非法药物（属于事实性指控）。\n\n但即便只是表达关于候选人吸毒的看法（属于意见），如果其中暗示了对特定事实的了解，仍可能构成诽谤诉讼的依据。\n\n（选项 C）被推定会损害原告名誉的陈述被视为“本身构成诽谤”（defamatory per se）。\n\n尽管指控某人实施犯罪行为（如使用非法药物）属于本身构成诽谤，但候选人无法胜诉，因为编辑相信该指控属实（即不存在实际恶意）。\n\n（选项 D）尽管编辑的指控不实且可能损害候选人的名誉，但候选人无法胜诉，因为编辑的行为不具备“实际恶意”。\n\n教学目标：公众人物或公职人员若要就诽谤获得赔偿，必须证明被告出于“实际恶意”作出了虚假陈述——即明知陈述虚假或罔顾其虚假性。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 558 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（诽谤的构成要素）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第568条（美国法律协会，1977年）（区分书面诽谤与口头诽谤）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第580A条（美国法律协会，1977年）（针对公职人员或公众人物的诽谤）。","conclusion":"答案 B：否，因为编辑真诚地相信该指控属实。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"falsity","ipa":"/fˈɔlsəti/","syllables":"fal·sity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 虚伪, 错误, 不忠实, 谎言；[经] 伪造, 篡改","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“falsity”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:falsities","sourceSentence":"A public figure or public official can only recover for defamation if he/she proves that the defendant made a false statement with actual malice—ie, with knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity.","synonyms":["falsehood","untruth","falseness"]},{"term":"publishing","ipa":"/pˈəblɪʃɪŋ/","syllables":"pub·lish·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 出版, 刊印, 发行","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“publishing”","wordForm":"词；词形 0:publish/1:i/i:publishing","sourceSentence":"The editor acted unreasonably in not investigating the accusation before publishing it; however, the editor honestly believed that the accusation was true.","synonyms":["publication"]},{"term":"injurious","ipa":"/ˌɪndʒˈʊɹiəs/","syllables":"in·ju·ri·ous","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 有害的, 不公正的；[法] 伤害的, 有害的, 不公平的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“injurious”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the accusation was false and injurious to the candidate's reputation.","synonyms":["deleterious","hurtful"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-06c8a04b0b9a1fd2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":149,"sourceQuestionPage":445,"sourceQuestionPages":[445],"sourceExplanationPages":[446,447],"sourceAnswerPages":[446,447],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-150","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"An assistant to a famous writer surreptitiously observed the writer as the writer typed her private password into her personal computer in order to access her email. On several subsequent occasions in the writer's absence, the assistant read the writer's email messages and printed out selections from them. The assistant later quit his job and earned a considerable amount of money by leaking information to the media that he had learned from reading the writer's email messages. All of the information published about the writer as a result of the assistant's conduct was true and concerned matters of public interest. The writer's secretary had seen the assistant reading the writer's emails and printing out selections, and she has told the writer what she saw. The writer now wishes to sue the assistant for damages. At trial, the writer can show that the media leaks could have come only from someone reading her email. Can the writer recover damages from the assistant?","choices":{"A":"No, because the assistant was an invitee on the premises.","B":"No, because the published information resulting from the assistant's conduct was true and concerned matters of public interest.","C":"Yes, because the assistant invaded the writer's privacy.","D":"Yes, because the published information resulting from the assistant's conduct constituted publication of private facts concerning the writer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Invasion of privacy\n\nTheories of recovery Required elements\n\nIntrusion upon seclusion Intentional intrusion (physical or otherwise) on plaintiff's solitude, seclusion, or private affairs Intrusion would be highly offensive to reasonable person Appropriation of name Unauthorized use of plaintiff's name or likeness for personal or likeness benefit (typically for commercial purpose)\n\nPublic disclosure of Publicity given to plaintiff's private matter private facts Matter publicized would be both: highly offensive to reasonable person not of legitimate public concern Results in damages Publicity in a false light Publicity given to false information that places plaintiff in false light False light would be highly offensive to reasonable person Publication is made with actual malice Results in damages Intrusion upon seclusion is an invasion of privacy that occurs when the defendant intentionally intrudes on the plaintiff's private affairs in a manner that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person—eg, tapping telephone wires, examining private bank account, opening personal mail. Here, the assistant opened and read the writer's private, password-protected emails. Since a reasonable person would be highly offended by that intrusion, the writer can likely recover damages from the assistant for invasion of privacy (intrusion upon seclusion). (Choice A) The fact that the assistant was an invitee shows that he had permission to enter the writer's premises, but does not show that he had permission to open and read the writer's private, password-protected emails. Therefore, the assistant's status as an invitee does not shield him from liability. (Choice B) Truth is generally not a defense to invasion of privacy. And though public interest in the disclosed material may preclude an invasion of privacy claim based on public disclosure of private facts—publicizing a private matter that is highly offensive and not of legitimate public concern—it is irrelevant to intrusion upon seclusion. (Choice D) A privacy claim for the public disclosure of private facts requires proof that the publicized matter would be highly offensive to a reasonable person. Since there is no\n\nevidence that the writer's emails would be highly offensive, the writer cannot prevail on that claim.\n\nEducational objective: Intrusion upon seclusion (a type of invasion of privacy) occurs when the defendant intentionally intrudes on the plaintiff's private affairs in a manner that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 652B (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (intrusion upon seclusion).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 652D (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (publicity given to private life).","educationalObjective":"Intrusion upon seclusion (a type of invasion of privacy) occurs when the defendant intentionally intrudes on the plaintiff's private affairs in a manner that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 652B (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (intrusion upon seclusion).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 652D (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (publicity given to private life).","rule":"Intrusion upon seclusion (a type of invasion of privacy) occurs when the defendant intentionally intrudes on the plaintiff's private affairs in a manner that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person.","irac":{"issue":"Can the writer recover damages from the assistant?","rule":"Intrusion upon seclusion (a type of invasion of privacy) occurs when the defendant intentionally intrudes on the plaintiff's private affairs in a manner that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person.","analysis":"Invasion of privacy\n\nTheories of recovery Required elements\n\nIntrusion upon seclusion Intentional intrusion (physical or otherwise) on plaintiff's solitude, seclusion, or private affairs Intrusion would be highly offensive to reasonable person Appropriation of name Unauthorized use of plaintiff's name or likeness for personal or likeness benefit (typically for commercial purpose)\n\nPublic disclosure of Publicity given to plaintiff's private matter private facts Matter publicized would be both: highly offensive to reasonable person not of legitimate public concern Results in damages Publicity in a false light Publicity given to false information that places plaintiff in false light False light would be highly offensive to reasonable person Publication is made with actual malice Results in damages Intrusion upon seclusion is an invasion of privacy that occurs when the defendant intentionally intrudes on the plaintiff's private affairs in a manner that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person—eg, tapping telephone wires, examining private bank account, opening personal mail. Here, the assistant opened and read the writer's private, password-protected emails. Since a reasonable person would be highly offended by that intrusion, the writer can likely recover damages from the assistant for invasion of privacy (intrusion upon seclusion). (Choice A) The fact that the assistant was an invitee shows that he had permission to enter the writer's premises, but does not show that he had permission to open and read the writer's private, password-protected emails. Therefore, the assistant's status as an invitee does not shield him from liability. (Choice B) Truth is generally not a defense to invasion of privacy. And though public interest in the disclosed material may preclude an invasion of privacy claim based on public disclosure of private facts—publicizing a private matter that is highly offensive and not of legitimate public concern—it is irrelevant to intrusion upon seclusion. (Choice D) A privacy claim for the public disclosure of private facts requires proof that the publicized matter would be highly offensive to a reasonable person. Since there is no\n\nevidence that the writer's emails would be highly offensive, the writer cannot prevail on that claim.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the assistant invaded the writer's privacy."},"questionZh":"一位著名作家的助手在作家于个人电脑上输入私人密码以访问电子邮件时，偷偷观察并记下了密码。\n\n此后，该助手曾多次利用作家不在场的机会，阅读其电子邮件并打印出部分内容。\n\n该助手后来辞职，并将从阅读邮件中获悉的信息泄露给媒体，从而赚取了可观的收入。\n\n因该助手的行为而向公众披露的关于该作家的所有信息均属实，且涉及公共利益。\n\n作家的秘书曾目睹助手阅读邮件及打印内容，并将所见情况告知了作家。\n\n现作家欲起诉该助手并要求赔偿损失。\n\n在庭审中，作家能够证明媒体披露的信息只能源于有人阅读了她的电子邮件。\n\n作家能否从该助手处获得损害赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为该助手是受邀进入该场所的人员。","B":"不能，因为因该助手行为而披露的信息属实且涉及公共利益。","C":"能，因为该助手侵犯了作家的隐私。","D":"能，因为因该助手行为而披露的信息构成了对作家私人事实的公开。"},"explanationZh":"侵犯隐私权 索赔依据（诉因） 构成要件 侵入私密领域（Intrusion upon seclusion） 故意侵入（身体或其他形式）原告的独处状态、隐秘空间或私人事务；该侵入行为会被理性人视为极具冒犯性。 擅用姓名或肖像（Appropriation of name or likeness） 未经许可使用原告的姓名或肖像以谋取个人利益（通常为商业目的）。 公开私人事实（Public disclosure of private facts） 向公众披露原告的私人事项；披露的事项必须同时满足：会被理性人视为极具冒犯性，且不属于正当的公众关注事项；并导致损害后果。 歪曲事实的公开（Publicity in a false light） 公开虚假信息，使原告处于一种不实的形象中；该不实形象会被理性人视为极具冒犯性；发布行为出于“实际恶意”（actual malice）；并导致损害后果。\n\n“侵入私密领域”是一种侵犯隐私权的行为，指被告故意以某种会被理性人视为极具冒犯性的方式侵入原告的私人事务——例如窃听电话线、查阅私人银行账户或拆阅私人信件。\n\n在本案中，助手打开并阅读了作者受密码保护的私人电子邮件。由于这种侵入行为会被理性人视为极具冒犯性，作者很可能因隐私权受侵犯（侵入私密领域）而向助手索赔。\n\n（选项 A）助手作为“受邀者”（invitee）这一事实表明他获准进入作者的场所，但这并不意味着他获准打开并阅读作者受密码保护的私人电子邮件。\n\n因此，助手作为受邀者的身份并不能免除其法律责任。\n\n（选项 B）“事实属实”通常不能作为侵犯隐私权指控的抗辩理由。\n\n虽然公众对披露内容的兴趣可能会排除基于“公开私人事实”提出的侵权索赔（即公开那些极具冒犯性且不属于正当公众关注事项的私人事务），但这与“侵入私密领域”的索赔无关。\n\n（选项 D）基于“公开私人事实”提出的隐私权索赔，要求证明所披露的事项会被理性人视为极具冒犯性。\n\n由于没有 证据表明作者的电子邮件内容会被视为极具冒犯性，作者无法基于该理由胜诉。\n\n教育目标：“侵入私密领域”（一种侵犯隐私权的形式）是指被告故意以某种会被理性人视为极具冒犯性的方式侵入原告的私人事务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第652B条（美国法律学会，1977年）（侵扰私密领域）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第652D条（美国法律学会，1977年）（公开私人生活）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“侵入私密领域”（一种侵犯隐私权的行为）是指被告故意侵入原告的私密事务，且其侵入方式会令理性人感到极度冒犯。","iracZh":{"issue":"作家能否从该助手处获得损害赔偿？","rule":"“侵入私密领域”（一种侵犯隐私权的行为）是指被告故意侵入原告的私密事务，且其侵入方式会令理性人感到极度冒犯。","analysis":"侵犯隐私权 索赔依据（诉因） 构成要件 侵入私密领域（Intrusion upon seclusion） 故意侵入（身体或其他形式）原告的独处状态、隐秘空间或私人事务；该侵入行为会被理性人视为极具冒犯性。 擅用姓名或肖像（Appropriation of name or likeness） 未经许可使用原告的姓名或肖像以谋取个人利益（通常为商业目的）。 公开私人事实（Public disclosure of private facts） 向公众披露原告的私人事项；披露的事项必须同时满足：会被理性人视为极具冒犯性，且不属于正当的公众关注事项；并导致损害后果。 歪曲事实的公开（Publicity in a false light） 公开虚假信息，使原告处于一种不实的形象中；该不实形象会被理性人视为极具冒犯性；发布行为出于“实际恶意”（actual malice）；并导致损害后果。\n\n“侵入私密领域”是一种侵犯隐私权的行为，指被告故意以某种会被理性人视为极具冒犯性的方式侵入原告的私人事务——例如窃听电话线、查阅私人银行账户或拆阅私人信件。\n\n在本案中，助手打开并阅读了作者受密码保护的私人电子邮件。由于这种侵入行为会被理性人视为极具冒犯性，作者很可能因隐私权受侵犯（侵入私密领域）而向助手索赔。\n\n（选项 A）助手作为“受邀者”（invitee）这一事实表明他获准进入作者的场所，但这并不意味着他获准打开并阅读作者受密码保护的私人电子邮件。\n\n因此，助手作为受邀者的身份并不能免除其法律责任。\n\n（选项 B）“事实属实”通常不能作为侵犯隐私权指控的抗辩理由。\n\n虽然公众对披露内容的兴趣可能会排除基于“公开私人事实”提出的侵权索赔（即公开那些极具冒犯性且不属于正当公众关注事项的私人事务），但这与“侵入私密领域”的索赔无关。\n\n（选项 D）基于“公开私人事实”提出的隐私权索赔，要求证明所披露的事项会被理性人视为极具冒犯性。\n\n由于没有 证据表明作者的电子邮件内容会被视为极具冒犯性，作者无法基于该理由胜诉。\n\n教育目标：“侵入私密领域”（一种侵犯隐私权的形式）是指被告故意以某种会被理性人视为极具冒犯性的方式侵入原告的私人事务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第652B条（美国法律学会，1977年）（侵扰私密领域）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第652D条（美国法律学会，1977年）（公开私人生活）。","conclusion":"答案 C：能，因为该助手侵犯了作家的隐私。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"public disclosure of private facts","ipa":"/pˈəblɪk dɪsklˈoʊʒɝ ˈəv pɹˈaɪvət fˈækts/","syllables":"pub·lic dis·clo·sure of pri·vate facts","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"公开私人事实：广泛披露非公众关切且高度冒犯的真实私密事实","contextualMeaning":"公开私人事实：广泛披露非公众关切且高度冒犯的真实私密事实","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"And though public interest in the disclosed material may preclude an invasion of privacy claim based on public disclosure of private facts—publicizing a private matter that is highly offensive and not of legitimate public concern—it is irrelevant to intrusion upon seclusion.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":150,"sourceQuestionPage":448,"sourceQuestionPages":[448],"sourceExplanationPages":[449,450],"sourceAnswerPages":[449,450],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-151","subject":"Torts","topic":"Nuisance","question":"A manufacturing company constructed a factory near a small town. Five years later, a woman purchased a home one mile away from the factory. Her home is the only residence within five miles of the factory. During this time, the company had steady business but produced very little smoke. Two years after the woman moved into her home, the company secured several large contracts and expanded its factory to add several large smokestacks that emit smoke on a continual basis. Other factories commonly use the same number of smokestacks in their operations, and such use complies with government regulations. The woman's home is downwind from the factory, so the smoke constantly blows toward her home. The woman has suffered breathing difficulties, constant headaches, and other sicknesses ever since the factory expanded. The value of her property has also severely declined. The woman sued the manufacturing company for private nuisance. Will the woman be likely to prevail as a matter of law?","choices":{"A":"No, because the company used the same number of smokestacks in its operations as other companies and complied with government regulations.","B":"No, because the woman knowingly purchased a home one mile away from a factory that was in operation at the time of purchase.","C":"Yes, because the smoke caused the value of the woman's property to severely decline.","D":"Yes, because the smoke unreasonably interfered with the woman's use and enjoyment of her property."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A private nuisance claim requires proof that the defendant's interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property was both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. Severe harm (eg, physical harm to those on the property) always outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. As a result, an interference that causes severe harm is unreasonable as a matter of law. Here, the smoke from the factory interfered with the woman's use and enjoyment of her property by causing her physical harm and reducing the value of her property. The interference was substantial since a normal homeowner would find it annoying or intolerable. It was also unreasonable because the smoke caused the woman severe harm— including difficulty breathing and constant headaches. Therefore, she will likely prevail on this claim. (Choice A) The company's interference is unreasonable as a matter of law because it caused severe harm. Therefore, evidence of the company's reasonableness—ie, operating with the same number of smokestacks as other companies and complying with government regulations—is no defense. (Choice B) Whether a plaintiff \"came to the nuisance\" because the nuisance existed before the plaintiff moved in is merely one factor that may be considered in determining whether an interference is actionable. It is not a defense. (Choice C) The woman could recover damages for the reduction of her property's value caused by the smoke, but this is not required for her to prevail. Instead, she will prevail because the smoke unreasonably interfered with her use and enjoyment of her property.\n\nEducational objective: Private nuisance is an interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property that is both substantial (ie, offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community) and unreasonable (ie, severity of the harm outweighs the utility of the conduct). Severe harm is unreasonable as a matter of law.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 167, 821D (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference required).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 829A cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (severe harm).","educationalObjective":"Private nuisance is an interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property that is both substantial (ie, offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community) and unreasonable (ie, severity of the harm outweighs the utility of the conduct). Severe harm is unreasonable as a matter of law.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 167, 821D (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference required).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 829A cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (severe harm).","rule":"Private nuisance is an interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property that is both substantial (ie, offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community) and unreasonable (ie, severity of the harm outweighs the utility of the conduct). Severe harm is unreasonable as a matter of law.","irac":{"issue":"Will the woman be likely to prevail as a matter of law?","rule":"Private nuisance is an interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property that is both substantial (ie, offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community) and unreasonable (ie, severity of the harm outweighs the utility of the conduct). Severe harm is unreasonable as a matter of law.","analysis":"A private nuisance claim requires proof that the defendant's interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property was both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. Severe harm (eg, physical harm to those on the property) always outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. As a result, an interference that causes severe harm is unreasonable as a matter of law. Here, the smoke from the factory interfered with the woman's use and enjoyment of her property by causing her physical harm and reducing the value of her property. The interference was substantial since a normal homeowner would find it annoying or intolerable. It was also unreasonable because the smoke caused the woman severe harm— including difficulty breathing and constant headaches. Therefore, she will likely prevail on this claim. (Choice A) The company's interference is unreasonable as a matter of law because it caused severe harm. Therefore, evidence of the company's reasonableness—ie, operating with the same number of smokestacks as other companies and complying with government regulations—is no defense. (Choice B) Whether a plaintiff \"came to the nuisance\" because the nuisance existed before the plaintiff moved in is merely one factor that may be considered in determining whether an interference is actionable. It is not a defense. (Choice C) The woman could recover damages for the reduction of her property's value caused by the smoke, but this is not required for her to prevail. Instead, she will prevail because the smoke unreasonably interfered with her use and enjoyment of her property.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the smoke unreasonably interfered with the woman's use and enjoyment of her property."},"questionZh":"一家制造公司在某小镇附近建了一座工厂。\n\n五年后，一位女士在距离工厂一英里的地方买了一处房产。\n\n她的住所是工厂方圆五英里内唯一的住宅。\n\n在此期间，该公司业务稳定，但排放的烟雾很少。\n\n该女士入住两年后，公司获得了几份大订单并扩建了工厂，增设了数根持续排放烟雾的大烟囱。\n\n其他工厂在运营中通常也使用同样数量的烟囱，且这种使用方式符合政府法规。\n\n该女士的住所位于工厂的下风向，因此烟雾不断吹向她的家。\n\n自工厂扩建以来，该女士一直遭受呼吸困难、持续头痛及其他病痛的困扰。\n\n她的房产价值也大幅下跌。\n\n该女士以“私人妨害”（private nuisance）为由起诉了这家制造公司。\n\n从法律角度看，该女士胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为该公司在运营中使用的烟囱数量与其他公司相同，且符合政府法规。","B":"不大，因为该女士在购房时明知该工厂正在运营，却仍选择在距离工厂一英里的地方买房。","C":"大，因为烟雾导致该女士的房产价值大幅下跌。","D":"大，因为烟雾不合理地干扰了该女士对其房产的使用和享用。"},"explanationZh":"提出私人妨害（private nuisance）索赔要求证明被告对原告使用和享用其财产的干扰同时具备以下两个特征：实质性（即对于社区中的普通人而言，这种干扰是令人反感、恼人或无法忍受的）和不合理性（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用）。\n\n严重的损害（例如，对财产上人员造成的人身伤害）总是超过被告行为的效用。\n\n因此，造成严重损害的干扰在法律上被视为不合理。\n\n在本案中，工厂排放的烟雾造成了该女子人身伤害并降低了其房产价值，从而干扰了她对财产的使用和享用。\n\n这种干扰具有实质性，因为普通房主会认为其令人恼火或无法忍受。\n\n它也具有不合理性，因为烟雾给该女子造成了严重损害——包括呼吸困难和持续性头痛。\n\n因此，她很可能在这一索赔中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）该公司的干扰在法律上是不合理的，因为它造成了严重损害。\n\n因此，关于该公司行为合理的证据——即烟囱数量与其他公司相同且符合政府法规——并不能构成抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 B）原告是否“迁入妨害源所在地”（即妨害在原告迁入前已存在）仅仅是判定干扰是否构成可诉侵权时可能考虑的一个因素，它本身并不构成抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 C）该女子可以就烟雾造成的房产价值贬值获得损害赔偿，但这并非她胜诉的必要条件。\n\n相反，她胜诉的原因在于烟雾不合理地干扰了她对财产的使用和享用。\n\n教育目标：私人妨害是指对原告使用和享用其财产的干扰，这种干扰必须同时具备实质性（即对于社区中的普通人而言，这种干扰是令人反感、恼人或无法忍受的）和不合理性（即损害的严重程度超过了行为的效用）。\n\n严重的损害在法律上被视为不合理。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 167 条、第 821D 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律学会，1979年）（要求存在不合理的干扰）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第829A条注释b（美国法律学会，1979年）（严重损害）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"私人妨害是指对原告使用和享用其房产的干扰，这种干扰既是实质性的（即对社区中的普通人而言是令人反感、恼人或无法忍受的），也是不合理的（即损害的严重程度超过了该行为的效用）。\n\n严重的损害在法律上被视为不合理。","iracZh":{"issue":"从法律角度看，该女士胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"私人妨害是指对原告使用和享用其房产的干扰，这种干扰既是实质性的（即对社区中的普通人而言是令人反感、恼人或无法忍受的），也是不合理的（即损害的严重程度超过了该行为的效用）。\n\n严重的损害在法律上被视为不合理。","analysis":"提出私人妨害（private nuisance）索赔要求证明被告对原告使用和享用其财产的干扰同时具备以下两个特征：实质性（即对于社区中的普通人而言，这种干扰是令人反感、恼人或无法忍受的）和不合理性（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用）。\n\n严重的损害（例如，对财产上人员造成的人身伤害）总是超过被告行为的效用。\n\n因此，造成严重损害的干扰在法律上被视为不合理。\n\n在本案中，工厂排放的烟雾造成了该女子人身伤害并降低了其房产价值，从而干扰了她对财产的使用和享用。\n\n这种干扰具有实质性，因为普通房主会认为其令人恼火或无法忍受。\n\n它也具有不合理性，因为烟雾给该女子造成了严重损害——包括呼吸困难和持续性头痛。\n\n因此，她很可能在这一索赔中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）该公司的干扰在法律上是不合理的，因为它造成了严重损害。\n\n因此，关于该公司行为合理的证据——即烟囱数量与其他公司相同且符合政府法规——并不能构成抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 B）原告是否“迁入妨害源所在地”（即妨害在原告迁入前已存在）仅仅是判定干扰是否构成可诉侵权时可能考虑的一个因素，它本身并不构成抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 C）该女子可以就烟雾造成的房产价值贬值获得损害赔偿，但这并非她胜诉的必要条件。\n\n相反，她胜诉的原因在于烟雾不合理地干扰了她对财产的使用和享用。\n\n教育目标：私人妨害是指对原告使用和享用其财产的干扰，这种干扰必须同时具备实质性（即对于社区中的普通人而言，这种干扰是令人反感、恼人或无法忍受的）和不合理性（即损害的严重程度超过了行为的效用）。\n\n严重的损害在法律上被视为不合理。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 167 条、第 821D 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律学会，1979年）（要求存在不合理的干扰）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第829A条注释b（美国法律学会，1979年）（严重损害）。","conclusion":"答案 D：大，因为烟雾不合理地干扰了该女士对其房产的使用和享用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-2f455d41a8d6c034"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":151,"sourceQuestionPage":451,"sourceQuestionPages":[451],"sourceExplanationPages":[452,453],"sourceAnswerPages":[452,453],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-152","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A man lived in a lakefront home. The man suspected that his neighbor was emptying her septic tank into the lake. Although he did not have any tangible proof, the man had seen the neighbor putting a large hose that led from an area in the neighbor's backyard where the septic tank was located into the lake late at night. Based upon this observation, the man identified the neighbor by name and made the following statement to a local reporter: \"She is polluting the lake and ruining its ecosystem by dumping the contents of her septic tank into it every night!\" The reporter published the statement in the newspaper. Upon reading it, the neighbor was horrified because in actuality she had simply been pumping water from the lake into her backyard. If the neighbor brings a defamation action against the man, will the neighbor be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because pollution of the lake is a matter of public concern.","B":"No, because the man did not act with actual malice.","C":"Yes, because the man acted negligently when he spoke to the reporter about the neighbor.","D":"Yes, because the reporter published the man's defamatory statement about the neighbor."},"answer":"C","explanation":"At common law, defamation liability could be strict. This means that the plaintiff was not required to prove that the defendant was at fault (eg, knew that the defamatory statement was false). However, a number of Supreme Court decisions based on the First Amendment have since imposed the following fault requirements: If the plaintiff is a public official or figure, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant acted with actual malice—ie, knowledge that the defamatory statement was false or reckless disregard for its falsity. If the plaintiff is a private person and the defamatory statement involves a matter of public concern, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant was at least negligent as to the falsity of the statement.* Here, the man falsely told the reporter that the neighbor was dumping the contents of her septic tank into the lake. The neighbor is a private person and the man's statement involves a matter of public concern—the potential pollution and destruction of the lake's ecosystem. This means that the neighbor is constitutionally required to prove that the man was negligent. The neighbor can likely do so since the man made the statement to the reporter despite having no tangible proof that it was true. Therefore, the neighbor is likely to prevail (Choices A & B). *If the plaintiff is a private person and the defamatory statement involves a matter of private concern, then the constitutional requirements do not apply. However, most states today require at least negligence by the defendant for all defamation actions. (Choice D) Defamation requires proof that the defendant published the defamatory statement by intentionally or negligently communicating it to a third party. And here, publication occurred when the man spoke to the reporter. However, the reporter may also be subject to defamation liability for republishing the man's statement.\n\nEducational objective: A defamation plaintiff is constitutionally required to prove that the defendant acted with fault if the plaintiff is a public official or figure (actual malice required) OR a matter of public concern is involved (at least negligence required).\n\nReferences\nNew York Times v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254, 283–84 (1964) (holding that the First Amendment affects the plaintiff's right to recover under the common law tort of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 580A, 580B (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (fault requirement for defamation of public or private person).","educationalObjective":"A defamation plaintiff is constitutionally required to prove that the defendant acted with fault if the plaintiff is a public official or figure (actual malice required) OR a matter of public concern is involved (at least negligence required).","references":"New York Times v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254, 283–84 (1964) (holding that the First Amendment affects the plaintiff's right to recover under the common law tort of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 580A, 580B (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (fault requirement for defamation of public or private person).","rule":"A defamation plaintiff is constitutionally required to prove that the defendant acted with fault if the plaintiff is a public official or figure (actual malice required) OR a matter of public concern is involved (at least negligence required).","irac":{"issue":"If the neighbor brings a defamation action against the man, will the neighbor be likely to prevail?","rule":"A defamation plaintiff is constitutionally required to prove that the defendant acted with fault if the plaintiff is a public official or figure (actual malice required) OR a matter of public concern is involved (at least negligence required).","analysis":"At common law, defamation liability could be strict. This means that the plaintiff was not required to prove that the defendant was at fault (eg, knew that the defamatory statement was false). However, a number of Supreme Court decisions based on the First Amendment have since imposed the following fault requirements: If the plaintiff is a public official or figure, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant acted with actual malice—ie, knowledge that the defamatory statement was false or reckless disregard for its falsity. If the plaintiff is a private person and the defamatory statement involves a matter of public concern, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant was at least negligent as to the falsity of the statement.* Here, the man falsely told the reporter that the neighbor was dumping the contents of her septic tank into the lake. The neighbor is a private person and the man's statement involves a matter of public concern—the potential pollution and destruction of the lake's ecosystem. This means that the neighbor is constitutionally required to prove that the man was negligent. The neighbor can likely do so since the man made the statement to the reporter despite having no tangible proof that it was true. Therefore, the neighbor is likely to prevail (Choices A & B). *If the plaintiff is a private person and the defamatory statement involves a matter of private concern, then the constitutional requirements do not apply. However, most states today require at least negligence by the defendant for all defamation actions. (Choice D) Defamation requires proof that the defendant published the defamatory statement by intentionally or negligently communicating it to a third party. And here, publication occurred when the man spoke to the reporter. However, the reporter may also be subject to defamation liability for republishing the man's statement.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the man acted negligently when he spoke to the reporter about the neighbor."},"questionZh":"一名男子住在一栋临湖的房子里。\n\n他怀疑邻居将化粪池里的污物排入湖中。\n\n尽管没有确凿证据，但他曾目睹邻居在深夜将一根大软管伸入湖中，而软管的另一端连接着邻居后院的化粪池所在区域。\n\n基于这一观察，该男子指名道姓地向当地记者发表了以下言论：“她每晚都把化粪池里的东西排进湖里，这正在污染湖泊并破坏其生态系统！”记者将这番言论刊登在了报纸上。\n\n邻居读到这篇报道后感到震惊，因为实际上她当时只是在把湖水抽到自己的后院里。\n\n如果该邻居对这名男子提起诽谤诉讼，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为湖泊污染属于公众关注的议题。","B":"不会，因为该男子的行为不构成“实际恶意”（actual malice）。","C":"会，因为该男子在向记者谈论邻居时存在疏忽大意（过失）的行为。","D":"会，因为记者刊登了该男子关于邻居的诽谤性言论。"},"explanationZh":"在普通法下，诽谤责任可能属于严格责任。\n\n这意味着原告无需证明被告存在过错（例如，明知诽谤性陈述不实）。\n\n然而，最高法院基于宪法第一修正案作出的一系列判决确立了以下过错要求：如果原告是公职人员或公众人物，则必须证明被告存在“实际恶意”（actual malice）——即明知陈述不实或罔顾其不实性。\n\n如果原告是普通公民（私人），且诽谤性陈述涉及公共关注事项，则原告必须证明被告在陈述不实的问题上至少存在过失。* 在本案中，该男子向记者谎称邻居将化粪池内的污物排入湖中。\n\n该邻居属于普通公民，且该男子的陈述涉及公共关注事项——即湖泊生态系统可能遭受的污染与破坏。\n\n这意味着根据宪法要求，该邻居必须证明该男子存在过失。\n\n该邻居很可能能够做到这一点，因为该男子在没有任何确凿证据的情况下向记者作出了上述陈述。\n\n因此，该邻居很可能胜诉（选项 A 和 B）。\n\n* 如果原告是普通公民，且诽谤性陈述涉及私人事务，则不适用上述宪法要求。\n\n然而，如今大多数州在所有诽谤诉讼中都要求被告至少存在过失。\n\n（选项 D）构成诽谤需要证明被告通过故意或过失向第三方传达诽谤性陈述的方式“发布”了该陈述。\n\n在本案中，当该男子向记者讲述时，发布行为即已发生。\n\n不过，记者也可能因转载该男子的陈述而承担诽谤责任。\n\n教育目标：如果诽谤诉讼的原告是公职人员或公众人物（需证明存在“实际恶意”），或者案件涉及公共关注事项（需证明被告至少存在过失），则根据宪法要求，原告必须证明被告存在过错。\n\n参考文献 New York Times v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254, 283–84 (1964)（裁定宪法第一修正案影响原告依据普通法诽谤侵权行为获得赔偿的权利）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第580A、580B条（美国法律学会，1981年）（针对诽谤公众人物或私人时的过错要件）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据宪法规定，诽谤诉讼的原告若为公职人员或公众人物（需证明存在“实际恶意”），或者诉讼涉及公众关注的议题（至少需证明存在过失），则必须证明被告的行为存在过错。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该邻居对这名男子提起诽谤诉讼，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"根据宪法规定，诽谤诉讼的原告若为公职人员或公众人物（需证明存在“实际恶意”），或者诉讼涉及公众关注的议题（至少需证明存在过失），则必须证明被告的行为存在过错。","analysis":"在普通法下，诽谤责任可能属于严格责任。\n\n这意味着原告无需证明被告存在过错（例如，明知诽谤性陈述不实）。\n\n然而，最高法院基于宪法第一修正案作出的一系列判决确立了以下过错要求：如果原告是公职人员或公众人物，则必须证明被告存在“实际恶意”（actual malice）——即明知陈述不实或罔顾其不实性。\n\n如果原告是普通公民（私人），且诽谤性陈述涉及公共关注事项，则原告必须证明被告在陈述不实的问题上至少存在过失。* 在本案中，该男子向记者谎称邻居将化粪池内的污物排入湖中。\n\n该邻居属于普通公民，且该男子的陈述涉及公共关注事项——即湖泊生态系统可能遭受的污染与破坏。\n\n这意味着根据宪法要求，该邻居必须证明该男子存在过失。\n\n该邻居很可能能够做到这一点，因为该男子在没有任何确凿证据的情况下向记者作出了上述陈述。\n\n因此，该邻居很可能胜诉（选项 A 和 B）。\n\n* 如果原告是普通公民，且诽谤性陈述涉及私人事务，则不适用上述宪法要求。\n\n然而，如今大多数州在所有诽谤诉讼中都要求被告至少存在过失。\n\n（选项 D）构成诽谤需要证明被告通过故意或过失向第三方传达诽谤性陈述的方式“发布”了该陈述。\n\n在本案中，当该男子向记者讲述时，发布行为即已发生。\n\n不过，记者也可能因转载该男子的陈述而承担诽谤责任。\n\n教育目标：如果诽谤诉讼的原告是公职人员或公众人物（需证明存在“实际恶意”），或者案件涉及公共关注事项（需证明被告至少存在过失），则根据宪法要求，原告必须证明被告存在过错。\n\n参考文献 New York Times v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254, 283–84 (1964)（裁定宪法第一修正案影响原告依据普通法诽谤侵权行为获得赔偿的权利）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第580A、580B条（美国法律学会，1981年）（针对诽谤公众人物或私人时的过错要件）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，因为该男子在向记者谈论邻居时存在疏忽大意（过失）的行为。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-06c8a04b0b9a1fd2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":152,"sourceQuestionPage":454,"sourceQuestionPages":[454],"sourceExplanationPages":[455,456],"sourceAnswerPages":[455,456],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-153","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A college junior applied for membership in a college honor society. One of the current members of the society, an academic rival of the junior, sent an email to the other members of the society claiming that the junior was an alcoholic. Although some of the members believed the email, most did not. Consequently, the junior was admitted to the society. The junior subsequently sued her rival for libel. At trial, the junior established that she had never consumed alcohol and that her rival knew this. The rival offered proof that the junior was a user of illegal drugs at the time of the email. Is the junior likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because she failed to prove that a third party acted adversely toward her.","B":"No, because she was a user of illegal drugs.","C":"Yes, because of the doctrine of libel per quod.","D":"Yes, because she is entitled to general damages for reputational harm."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Libel is defamation in words written, printed, or otherwise recorded in permanent form. Most courts addressing the issue have held that email messages are categorized as libel. A libel claim requires proof of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity. The statement was defamatory, meaning that the statement would have a general tendency to harm the plaintiff's reputation. The defendant intentionally or negligently communicated the statement to a third party. Once proved, the plaintiff can recover general damages, which are any damages that compensate the plaintiff for harm to the plaintiff's reputation. Reputational harm can be presumed for libel because it is more permanent and more easily spread than slander (ie, spoken defamation). Here, the rival's email claimed that the junior was an alcoholic even though the rival knew that the junior had never consumed alcohol. That statement would have a general tendency to harm the junior's reputation. And since the rival intentionally communicated the statement to third parties by emailing the other members of the honor society, the junior is entitled to recover general damages for reputational harm. Therefore, the junior will likely prevail. (Choice A) Special damages are a prerequisite to recovery for slander and require the plaintiff to prove that a third party acted adversely to the plaintiff as a result of the defendant's defamatory statement. But since the junior sued for libel, the fact that she faced no adverse action because she was admitted to the honor society is irrelevant. (Choice B) Even if the junior was a user of illegal drugs at the time of the email, she was not an alcoholic, so the rival's statement was false. (Choice C) The doctrine of libel per quod applies when reference to extrinsic facts is needed to show that the statement is defamatory (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Libel is defamation by words written, printed, or otherwise recorded in permanent form (eg, emails). A libel plaintiff can recover general damages to compensate the plaintiff for the presumed harm to his/her reputation.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 568 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (distinguishing libel and slander).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 621 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (explaining that general damages are imposed to compensate the plaintiff for reputational harm).","educationalObjective":"Libel is defamation by words written, printed, or otherwise recorded in permanent form (eg, emails). A libel plaintiff can recover general damages to compensate the plaintiff for the presumed harm to his/her reputation.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 568 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (distinguishing libel and slander).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 621 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (explaining that general damages are imposed to compensate the plaintiff for reputational harm).","rule":"Libel is defamation by words written, printed, or otherwise recorded in permanent form (eg, emails). A libel plaintiff can recover general damages to compensate the plaintiff for the presumed harm to his/her reputation.","irac":{"issue":"Is the junior likely to prevail?","rule":"Libel is defamation by words written, printed, or otherwise recorded in permanent form (eg, emails). A libel plaintiff can recover general damages to compensate the plaintiff for the presumed harm to his/her reputation.","analysis":"Libel is defamation in words written, printed, or otherwise recorded in permanent form. Most courts addressing the issue have held that email messages are categorized as libel. A libel claim requires proof of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity. The statement was defamatory, meaning that the statement would have a general tendency to harm the plaintiff's reputation. The defendant intentionally or negligently communicated the statement to a third party. Once proved, the plaintiff can recover general damages, which are any damages that compensate the plaintiff for harm to the plaintiff's reputation. Reputational harm can be presumed for libel because it is more permanent and more easily spread than slander (ie, spoken defamation). Here, the rival's email claimed that the junior was an alcoholic even though the rival knew that the junior had never consumed alcohol. That statement would have a general tendency to harm the junior's reputation. And since the rival intentionally communicated the statement to third parties by emailing the other members of the honor society, the junior is entitled to recover general damages for reputational harm. Therefore, the junior will likely prevail. (Choice A) Special damages are a prerequisite to recovery for slander and require the plaintiff to prove that a third party acted adversely to the plaintiff as a result of the defendant's defamatory statement. But since the junior sued for libel, the fact that she faced no adverse action because she was admitted to the honor society is irrelevant. (Choice B) Even if the junior was a user of illegal drugs at the time of the email, she was not an alcoholic, so the rival's statement was false. (Choice C) The doctrine of libel per quod applies when reference to extrinsic facts is needed to show that the statement is defamatory (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because she is entitled to general damages for reputational harm."},"questionZh":"一名大三学生申请加入某大学荣誉学会。\n\n该学会的一名现任成员（也是这名大三学生的学业竞争对手）向学会其他成员发送了一封电子邮件，声称该学生酗酒。\n\n尽管部分成员相信了这封邮件，但大多数人并未相信。\n\n最终，该学生获准加入学会。\n\n随后，她起诉了那名竞争对手，指控其构成书面诽谤（libel）。\n\n在庭审中，该学生证实自己从未饮酒，且竞争对手对此知情。\n\n竞争对手则提供了证据，证明该学生在邮件发送时有使用非法药物的行为。\n\n该学生胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为她未能证明有第三方因该邮件而对她采取了不利行动。","B":"不大，因为她曾使用非法药物。","C":"大，因为适用“需证明特殊损害的书面诽谤”（libel per quod）原则。","D":"大，因为她有权就名誉受损获得一般性损害赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"“Libel”（书面诽谤）是指以书写、印刷或以其他永久形式记录的言辞所构成的诽谤。\n\n大多数审理此类案件的法院都认定电子邮件属于书面诽谤。\n\n提出书面诽谤索赔需要证明以下几点：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实，或因疏忽而未核实其不实性；该陈述具有诽谤性质，即该陈述通常会损害原告的名誉；被告故意或疏忽地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n一旦证明了这些要点，原告即可获得“一般损害赔偿”（general damages），即旨在补偿原告名誉受损的赔偿。\n\n对于书面诽谤，法律可推定存在名誉损害，因为与口头诽谤（slander）相比，书面诽谤更具持久性且更易传播。\n\n在本案中，竞争对手发送的电子邮件声称该名低年级学生酗酒，尽管该对手明知该学生从未饮酒。\n\n该陈述通常会损害该学生的名誉。\n\n此外，由于该对手通过向荣誉社团的其他成员发送电子邮件，故意将该陈述传达给了第三方，因此该学生有权就名誉受损获得一般损害赔偿。\n\n因此，该学生很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）“特殊损害赔偿”（special damages）是针对口头诽谤索赔的先决条件，要求原告证明第三方因被告的诽谤性陈述而对原告采取了不利行动。\n\n但由于该学生提起的是书面诽谤诉讼，她因被荣誉社团录取而未遭受任何不利行动这一事实与本案无关。\n\n（选项 B）即使该学生在发送电子邮件时使用非法药物，她也并非酗酒者，因此竞争对手的陈述仍属不实。\n\n（选项 C）“Libel per quod”（需结合外部事实方构成诽谤的书面诽谤）原则适用于需要借助外部事实才能证明陈述具有诽谤性的情况（本案不属于此类情况）。\n\n教育目标：书面诽谤（Libel）是指以书写、印刷或以其他永久形式（如电子邮件）记录的言辞所构成的诽谤。\n\n书面诽谤案件的原告可以获得一般损害赔偿，以补偿其名誉受到的推定损害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 568 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（区分了书面诽谤与口头诽谤）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第621条（美国法律学会，1977年）（阐明一般损害赔偿旨在补偿原告所受的名誉损害）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"书面诽谤（Libel）是指通过书写、印刷或以其他永久形式（例如电子邮件）记录的言辞所构成的诽谤。\n\n书面诽谤案件的原告可以获得一般性损害赔偿，以补偿其名誉受到的推定损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"该学生胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"书面诽谤（Libel）是指通过书写、印刷或以其他永久形式（例如电子邮件）记录的言辞所构成的诽谤。\n\n书面诽谤案件的原告可以获得一般性损害赔偿，以补偿其名誉受到的推定损害。","analysis":"“Libel”（书面诽谤）是指以书写、印刷或以其他永久形式记录的言辞所构成的诽谤。\n\n大多数审理此类案件的法院都认定电子邮件属于书面诽谤。\n\n提出书面诽谤索赔需要证明以下几点：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实，或因疏忽而未核实其不实性；该陈述具有诽谤性质，即该陈述通常会损害原告的名誉；被告故意或疏忽地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n一旦证明了这些要点，原告即可获得“一般损害赔偿”（general damages），即旨在补偿原告名誉受损的赔偿。\n\n对于书面诽谤，法律可推定存在名誉损害，因为与口头诽谤（slander）相比，书面诽谤更具持久性且更易传播。\n\n在本案中，竞争对手发送的电子邮件声称该名低年级学生酗酒，尽管该对手明知该学生从未饮酒。\n\n该陈述通常会损害该学生的名誉。\n\n此外，由于该对手通过向荣誉社团的其他成员发送电子邮件，故意将该陈述传达给了第三方，因此该学生有权就名誉受损获得一般损害赔偿。\n\n因此，该学生很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）“特殊损害赔偿”（special damages）是针对口头诽谤索赔的先决条件，要求原告证明第三方因被告的诽谤性陈述而对原告采取了不利行动。\n\n但由于该学生提起的是书面诽谤诉讼，她因被荣誉社团录取而未遭受任何不利行动这一事实与本案无关。\n\n（选项 B）即使该学生在发送电子邮件时使用非法药物，她也并非酗酒者，因此竞争对手的陈述仍属不实。\n\n（选项 C）“Libel per quod”（需结合外部事实方构成诽谤的书面诽谤）原则适用于需要借助外部事实才能证明陈述具有诽谤性的情况（本案不属于此类情况）。\n\n教育目标：书面诽谤（Libel）是指以书写、印刷或以其他永久形式（如电子邮件）记录的言辞所构成的诽谤。\n\n书面诽谤案件的原告可以获得一般损害赔偿，以补偿其名誉受到的推定损害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 568 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（区分了书面诽谤与口头诽谤）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第621条（美国法律学会，1977年）（阐明一般损害赔偿旨在补偿原告所受的名誉损害）。","conclusion":"答案 D：大，因为她有权就名誉受损获得一般性损害赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-097114fcabf90e4c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":153,"sourceQuestionPage":457,"sourceQuestionPages":[457],"sourceExplanationPages":[458,459],"sourceAnswerPages":[458,459],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-154","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A factory manager and one of her employees speak fluent Spanish. No other employees at the factory speak or understand Spanish. One day, the manager accused the employee, in Spanish and in front of other employees, of stealing a valuable item from the manager's office. Shortly after making this accusation, the manager discovered that she had accidently failed to see the item on her desk and apologized to the employee. The employee sued the manager for defamation. Is the employee likely to prevail in his suit?","choices":{"A":"No, because none of the other employees understood Spanish.","B":"No, because the manager mistakenly believed that the employee stole the item.","C":"Yes, because the manager made the defamatory statement in front of other employees.","D":"Yes, because the manager's accusation amounted to slander per se."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Defamation can be either libel (written statements) OR slander (spoken statements—as seen here). For defamation based on slander, the plaintiff must prove that: the defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff OR negligently failed to determine its falsity\n\nthat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation\n\nthe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated (ie, published) that statement to a third party who understood the statement's message and\n\nthat statement caused the plaintiff special damage (monetary loss) OR constituted slander per se (eg, accused the plaintiff of theft or other serious crime).\n\nHere, the manager falsely stated that the employee stole a valuable item from her office in front of other employees. But since that statement was made in Spanish, none of the other employees understood. Therefore, the employee will likely not prevail on his defamation claim (Choice C). (Choice B) A plaintiff who is a public figure or official must prove that the defendant made the defamatory statement with actual malice—ie, knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity. But since the employee is a private individual, he need only prove that the manager negligently failed to determine falsity. (Choice D) Although the manager's accusation that the employee committed theft (a serious crime) constituted slander per se, the employee will not prevail because none of the other employees understood the accusation.\n\nEducational objective: Defamation requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party who understood its meaning.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 558–59, 568, 570, 577, 580B (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 570–74 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (slander per se).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 577 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (communication/publication requirement).","educationalObjective":"Defamation requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party who understood its meaning.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 558–59, 568, 570, 577, 580B (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 570–74 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (slander per se).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 577 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (communication/publication requirement).","rule":"Defamation requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party who understood its meaning.","irac":{"issue":"Is the employee likely to prevail in his suit?","rule":"Defamation requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party who understood its meaning.","analysis":"Defamation can be either libel (written statements) OR slander (spoken statements—as seen here). For defamation based on slander, the plaintiff must prove that: the defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff OR negligently failed to determine its falsity\n\nthat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation\n\nthe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated (ie, published) that statement to a third party who understood the statement's message and\n\nthat statement caused the plaintiff special damage (monetary loss) OR constituted slander per se (eg, accused the plaintiff of theft or other serious crime).\n\nHere, the manager falsely stated that the employee stole a valuable item from her office in front of other employees. But since that statement was made in Spanish, none of the other employees understood. Therefore, the employee will likely not prevail on his defamation claim (Choice C). (Choice B) A plaintiff who is a public figure or official must prove that the defendant made the defamatory statement with actual malice—ie, knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity. But since the employee is a private individual, he need only prove that the manager negligently failed to determine falsity. (Choice D) Although the manager's accusation that the employee committed theft (a serious crime) constituted slander per se, the employee will not prevail because none of the other employees understood the accusation.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because none of the other employees understood Spanish."},"questionZh":"一家工厂的经理和她的一名员工都能说一口流利的西班牙语。\n\n工厂里其他员工都不会说或听懂西班牙语。\n\n一天，经理当着其他员工的面，用西班牙语指责这名员工从经理办公室偷了一件贵重物品。\n\n不久之后，经理发现自己不小心没看到放在桌上的那件物品，并向员工道歉。\n\n这名员工以诽谤罪起诉了经理。\n\n这名员工胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为其他员工都听不懂西班牙语。","B":"不可能，因为经理误以为这名员工偷了东西。","C":"可能，因为经理当着其他员工的面发表了诽谤性言论。","D":"可能，因为经理的指控本身就构成诽谤。"},"explanationZh":"诽谤既可以是书面形式（libel，即书面陈述），也可以是口头形式（slander，即口头陈述——本案即属此类）。\n\n对于基于口头诽谤（slander）的诉讼，原告必须证明：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实，或因疏忽而未核实其不实性； 该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉； 被告故意或疏忽地向第三方传达（即发布）了该陈述，且该第三方理解了陈述的内容；以及 该陈述给原告造成了特别损害（金钱损失），或构成了“本身即构成诽谤”（slander per se，例如指控原告犯有盗窃罪或其他严重罪行）。\n\n在本案中，经理当着其他员工的面，错误地声称该员工从其办公室偷走了一件贵重物品。\n\n但由于该陈述是用西班牙语作出的，其他员工均未听懂。\n\n因此，该员工很可能无法在诽谤诉讼中胜诉（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 B）如果原告是公众人物或公职人员，则必须证明被告在发表诽谤性陈述时具有“实际恶意”（actual malice）——即明知陈述不实或罔顾其不实性。\n\n但由于该员工是普通公民，他只需证明经理因疏忽而未核实陈述的不实性即可。\n\n（选项 D）尽管经理指控该员工犯有盗窃罪（一种严重罪行）构成了“本身即构成诽谤”，但该员工仍无法胜诉，因为其他员工并未听懂该指控。\n\n教育目标：构成诽谤需要证明被告故意或疏忽地向理解其含义的第三方传达了关于原告的不实陈述。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 558–59、568、570、577、580B 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（诽谤的构成要素）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 570–74 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（本身即构成诽谤）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 577 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（传达/发布要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"诽谤需要证明被告故意或过失地向理解其含义的第三方传达了关于原告的虚假陈述。","iracZh":{"issue":"该雇员的诉讼胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"诽谤需要证明被告故意或过失地向理解其含义的第三方传达了关于原告的虚假陈述。","analysis":"诽谤既可以是书面形式（libel，即书面陈述），也可以是口头形式（slander，即口头陈述——本案即属此类）。\n\n对于基于口头诽谤（slander）的诉讼，原告必须证明：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实，或因疏忽而未核实其不实性； 该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉； 被告故意或疏忽地向第三方传达（即发布）了该陈述，且该第三方理解了陈述的内容；以及 该陈述给原告造成了特别损害（金钱损失），或构成了“本身即构成诽谤”（slander per se，例如指控原告犯有盗窃罪或其他严重罪行）。\n\n在本案中，经理当着其他员工的面，错误地声称该员工从其办公室偷走了一件贵重物品。\n\n但由于该陈述是用西班牙语作出的，其他员工均未听懂。\n\n因此，该员工很可能无法在诽谤诉讼中胜诉（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 B）如果原告是公众人物或公职人员，则必须证明被告在发表诽谤性陈述时具有“实际恶意”（actual malice）——即明知陈述不实或罔顾其不实性。\n\n但由于该员工是普通公民，他只需证明经理因疏忽而未核实陈述的不实性即可。\n\n（选项 D）尽管经理指控该员工犯有盗窃罪（一种严重罪行）构成了“本身即构成诽谤”，但该员工仍无法胜诉，因为其他员工并未听懂该指控。\n\n教育目标：构成诽谤需要证明被告故意或疏忽地向理解其含义的第三方传达了关于原告的不实陈述。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 558–59、568、570、577、580B 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（诽谤的构成要素）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 570–74 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（本身即构成诽谤）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 577 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（传达/发布要求）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可能，因为其他员工都听不懂西班牙语。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-5531c2931e73e79e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":154,"sourceQuestionPage":460,"sourceQuestionPages":[460],"sourceExplanationPages":[461,462],"sourceAnswerPages":[461,462],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-155","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"The owner of a truck leasing company asked one of his employees to deliver $1,000 to the dealership's main office. The following week, as a result of the owner's belief that the money had not been delivered, the owner instructed the employee to come to the office to submit to a lie detector test. When the employee reported to the owner's office for the test, it was not administered. Instead, without hearing the employee's story, the owner shouted at him, \"You're a thief!\" and fired him. The owner's shout was overheard by several other employees who were in another office, which was separated from the owner's office by a thin partition. The next day, the employee accepted another job at a higher salary. Several weeks later, upon discovering that the money had not been stolen, the owner offered to rehire the employee. In a suit for slander by the employee against the owner, will the employee prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the statement was made to the employee alone and intended for his ears only.","B":"No, because the owner made the charge in good faith, believing it to be true.","C":"Yes, because the employee was fraudulently induced to go to the office for a lie detector test, which was not, in fact, given.","D":"Yes, because the owner should have foreseen that the statement would be overheard by other employees."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Slander is defamation through speech, gesture, or any other form that is not libel. To prevail in a suit for slander, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party (ie, should have foreseen that it would be heard by a third party).\n\nThat statement caused the plaintiff special harm (ie, pecuniary/monetary loss).\n\nHowever, no proof of harm is required when the defamatory statement constitutes slander per se—ie, the statement accused the plaintiff of any of the following: a serious crime (eg, theft, murder, rape) conduct that adversely affects the plaintiff's occupation (eg, performing surgeries while intoxicated) serious sexual misconduct (eg, infidelity) having a loathsome disease (eg, sexually transmitted infection). Here, the owner falsely accused the employee of theft (slander per se). Since the owner made that statement without listening to the employee's story, the owner was likely negligent in failing to determine that his statement was false. Additionally, though the accusation may have been intended for the employee's ears only, the owner should have foreseen that other employees would overhear him shouting through the thin partition (negligent communication) (Choice A). Therefore, the employee will likely prevail on his slander claim. (Choice B) Slander requires proof that the defendant knew or negligently failed to determine that his/her statement was false. Here, although the owner believed in good faith that his accusation was true (no knowledge of falsity), he could still be liable for slander if he negligently failed to determine that his accusation was false. (Choice C) Fraudulent inducement is an element of intentional misrepresentation, which requires proof that the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresented a material fact to induce the plaintiff's reliance. But since the employee only sued for slander, his fraudulent inducement is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: Slander (ie, spoken defamation) requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party. Negligent\n\ncommunication occurs when the defendant should have foreseen that his/her statement would be heard by a third party.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 577 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (intentional or negligent communication).","educationalObjective":"Slander (ie, spoken defamation) requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party. Negligent\n\ncommunication occurs when the defendant should have foreseen that his/her statement would be heard by a third party.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 577 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (intentional or negligent communication).","rule":"Slander (ie, spoken defamation) requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party. Negligent\n\ncommunication occurs when the defendant should have foreseen that his/her statement would be heard by a third party.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit for slander by the employee against the owner, will the employee prevail?","rule":"Slander (ie, spoken defamation) requires proof that the defendant intentionally or negligently communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party. Negligent\n\ncommunication occurs when the defendant should have foreseen that his/her statement would be heard by a third party.","analysis":"Slander is defamation through speech, gesture, or any other form that is not libel. To prevail in a suit for slander, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party (ie, should have foreseen that it would be heard by a third party).\n\nThat statement caused the plaintiff special harm (ie, pecuniary/monetary loss).\n\nHowever, no proof of harm is required when the defamatory statement constitutes slander per se—ie, the statement accused the plaintiff of any of the following: a serious crime (eg, theft, murder, rape) conduct that adversely affects the plaintiff's occupation (eg, performing surgeries while intoxicated) serious sexual misconduct (eg, infidelity) having a loathsome disease (eg, sexually transmitted infection). Here, the owner falsely accused the employee of theft (slander per se). Since the owner made that statement without listening to the employee's story, the owner was likely negligent in failing to determine that his statement was false. Additionally, though the accusation may have been intended for the employee's ears only, the owner should have foreseen that other employees would overhear him shouting through the thin partition (negligent communication) (Choice A). Therefore, the employee will likely prevail on his slander claim. (Choice B) Slander requires proof that the defendant knew or negligently failed to determine that his/her statement was false. Here, although the owner believed in good faith that his accusation was true (no knowledge of falsity), he could still be liable for slander if he negligently failed to determine that his accusation was false. (Choice C) Fraudulent inducement is an element of intentional misrepresentation, which requires proof that the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresented a material fact to induce the plaintiff's reliance. But since the employee only sued for slander, his fraudulent inducement is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the owner should have foreseen that the statement would be overheard by other employees."},"questionZh":"一家卡车租赁公司的老板让一名员工把1000美元送到公司的总部办公室。\n\n次周，由于老板认为这笔钱并未送达，便指示该员工到办公室接受测谎。\n\n当该员工来到老板办公室准备接受测试时，测试并未进行。\n\n相反，老板在未听取员工解释的情况下，对他大喊：“你是个小偷！”并将其解雇。\n\n老板的喊叫声被身处另一间办公室的几名员工听到了，这两间办公室之间仅隔着一道薄隔板。\n\n第二天，该员工接受了另一份薪水更高的工作。\n\n几周后，老板发现那笔钱并未失窃，便提出重新聘用该员工。\n\n在该员工针对老板提起的口头诽谤（slander）诉讼中，该员工会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该言论是单独对该员工发表的，且仅意在让他一人听到。","B":"不会，因为老板是在善意（good faith）的情况下提出指控的，即他当时相信该指控属实。","C":"会，因为该员工是被欺骗诱导去办公室接受测谎的，而实际上并未进行该测试。","D":"会，因为老板本应预见到该言论会被其他员工听到。"},"explanationZh":"口头诽谤（Slander）是指通过言语、手势或除书面诽谤（libel）以外的任何形式进行的诽谤。\n\n原告若要在口头诽谤诉讼中胜诉，必须证明以下所有要件：被告明知关于原告的陈述为虚假，或因疏忽而未核实其虚假性。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或因疏忽将该陈述传达给了第三方（即，被告本应预见到该陈述会被第三方听到）。\n\n该陈述给原告造成了特殊损害（即金钱/经济损失）。\n\n然而，若诽谤性陈述构成“本身即构成诽谤”（slander per se），则无需证明损害——即该陈述指控原告存在以下情形之一：犯有严重罪行（如盗窃、谋杀、强奸）；有损原告职业声誉的行为（如酒后进行手术）；严重的性不端行为（如不忠）；患有令人厌恶的疾病（如性传播疾病）。\n\n在本案中，店主错误地指控员工盗窃（构成“本身即构成诽谤”）。\n\n由于店主在未听取员工陈述的情况下发表了该言论，他很可能因未核实言论的虚假性而存在疏忽。\n\n此外，尽管该指控本意可能仅针对该员工，但店主本应预见到其他员工会隔着薄隔断听到他的大声指责（属于疏忽传达）（选项 A）。\n\n因此，该员工很可能在口头诽谤诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）口头诽谤要求证明被告明知或因疏忽未核实其陈述为虚假。\n\n在本案中，尽管店主真诚地相信其指控属实（即并无明知虚假的主观意图），但如果他因疏忽而未核实指控的虚假性，仍可能需承担口头诽谤的责任。\n\n（选项 C）欺诈性诱导（fraudulent inducement）属于故意虚假陈述（intentional misrepresentation）的构成要素，要求证明被告明知或鲁莽地对重要事实进行虚假陈述，以诱使原告产生信赖。\n\n但由于该员工仅就口头诽谤提起诉讼，欺诈性诱导的问题与本案无关。\n\n教育目标：口头诽谤（即言语形式的诽谤）要求证明被告故意或因疏忽将关于原告的虚假陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n疏忽 传达是指被告本应预见到其陈述会被第三方听到。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第577条（美国法律学会，1977年）（故意或过失传播）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"口头诽谤（即口头形式的诽谤）要求证明被告故意或过失地向第三方传达了关于原告的不实言论。\n\n过失 传达是指被告本应预见到其言论会被第三方听到。","iracZh":{"issue":"在该员工针对老板提起的口头诽谤诉讼中，该员工会胜诉吗？","rule":"口头诽谤（即口头形式的诽谤）要求证明被告故意或过失地向第三方传达了关于原告的不实言论。\n\n过失 传达是指被告本应预见到其言论会被第三方听到。","analysis":"口头诽谤（Slander）是指通过言语、手势或除书面诽谤（libel）以外的任何形式进行的诽谤。\n\n原告若要在口头诽谤诉讼中胜诉，必须证明以下所有要件：被告明知关于原告的陈述为虚假，或因疏忽而未核实其虚假性。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或因疏忽将该陈述传达给了第三方（即，被告本应预见到该陈述会被第三方听到）。\n\n该陈述给原告造成了特殊损害（即金钱/经济损失）。\n\n然而，若诽谤性陈述构成“本身即构成诽谤”（slander per se），则无需证明损害——即该陈述指控原告存在以下情形之一：犯有严重罪行（如盗窃、谋杀、强奸）；有损原告职业声誉的行为（如酒后进行手术）；严重的性不端行为（如不忠）；患有令人厌恶的疾病（如性传播疾病）。\n\n在本案中，店主错误地指控员工盗窃（构成“本身即构成诽谤”）。\n\n由于店主在未听取员工陈述的情况下发表了该言论，他很可能因未核实言论的虚假性而存在疏忽。\n\n此外，尽管该指控本意可能仅针对该员工，但店主本应预见到其他员工会隔着薄隔断听到他的大声指责（属于疏忽传达）（选项 A）。\n\n因此，该员工很可能在口头诽谤诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）口头诽谤要求证明被告明知或因疏忽未核实其陈述为虚假。\n\n在本案中，尽管店主真诚地相信其指控属实（即并无明知虚假的主观意图），但如果他因疏忽而未核实指控的虚假性，仍可能需承担口头诽谤的责任。\n\n（选项 C）欺诈性诱导（fraudulent inducement）属于故意虚假陈述（intentional misrepresentation）的构成要素，要求证明被告明知或鲁莽地对重要事实进行虚假陈述，以诱使原告产生信赖。\n\n但由于该员工仅就口头诽谤提起诉讼，欺诈性诱导的问题与本案无关。\n\n教育目标：口头诽谤（即言语形式的诽谤）要求证明被告故意或因疏忽将关于原告的虚假陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n疏忽 传达是指被告本应预见到其陈述会被第三方听到。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第577条（美国法律学会，1977年）（故意或过失传播）。","conclusion":"答案 D：会，因为老板本应预见到该言论会被其他员工听到。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-26b60b9778980ded"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":155,"sourceQuestionPage":463,"sourceQuestionPages":[463],"sourceExplanationPages":[464,465],"sourceAnswerPages":[464,465],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-156","subject":"Torts","topic":"Nuisance","question":"A recently established law school constructed its building in a quiet residential neighborhood. The law school had obtained all the necessary municipal permits for the construction of the building, which included a large clock tower whose clock chimed every hour. The chimes disturbed only one homeowner in the neighborhood, who had purchased her house prior to the construction of the building. The homeowner was abnormally sensitive to ringing sounds, such as bells and sirens, and found the chimes to be extremely annoying. In a nuisance action by the homeowner against the law school, will the homeowner be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the chimes do not disturb the other residents of the neighborhood.","B":"No, because the law school had obtained the requisite municipal permits to erect the clock tower.","C":"Yes, because the chimes interfere with the homeowner's use and enjoyment of her property.","D":"Yes, because the homeowner purchased her house prior to the construction of the building."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Nuisance can be either public (interfering with a right common to the general public) OR private (interfering with a private property right—as alleged here). Liability for private nuisance arises when the defendant's interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and\n\nunreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct.\n\nAn interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's abnormal sensitivity causes him/her to find the interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not. Here, the law school interfered with the homeowner's use and enjoyment of her property by constructing a large clock tower whose clock chimed every hour. But since the homeowner was abnormally sensitive to ringing sounds, she was the only one who found the chimes extremely annoying when no other residents were disturbed. Therefore, the interference was not substantial and the homeowner's nuisance action will likely fail (Choice C). (Choice B) Although compliance with zoning regulations (eg, obtaining municipal permits) helps show that the defendant's interference was not unreasonable, it is not a defense to nuisance. Instead, the homeowner's claim will fail because no other residents were disturbed by the chimes (no substantial interference). (Choice D) Courts may consider priority in time (ie, whether the plaintiff came to the nuisance) when determining if a nuisance is actionable. Here, the homeowner's nuisance claim is actionable since she purchased her house before the clock tower was built. However, she will only prevail on this claim if she proves that the law school's interference was both substantial and unreasonable.\n\nEducational objective: An interference with a plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is not substantial (and will not support a nuisance claim) if the plaintiff's abnormal sensitivity causes him/her to find that interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821D (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (substantial interference required).","educationalObjective":"An interference with a plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is not substantial (and will not support a nuisance claim) if the plaintiff's abnormal sensitivity causes him/her to find that interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 821D (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (substantial interference required).","rule":"An interference with a plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is not substantial (and will not support a nuisance claim) if the plaintiff's abnormal sensitivity causes him/her to find that interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not.","irac":{"issue":"In a nuisance action by the homeowner against the law school, will the homeowner be likely to prevail?","rule":"An interference with a plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is not substantial (and will not support a nuisance claim) if the plaintiff's abnormal sensitivity causes him/her to find that interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not.","analysis":"Nuisance can be either public (interfering with a right common to the general public) OR private (interfering with a private property right—as alleged here). Liability for private nuisance arises when the defendant's interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and\n\nunreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct.\n\nAn interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's abnormal sensitivity causes him/her to find the interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not. Here, the law school interfered with the homeowner's use and enjoyment of her property by constructing a large clock tower whose clock chimed every hour. But since the homeowner was abnormally sensitive to ringing sounds, she was the only one who found the chimes extremely annoying when no other residents were disturbed. Therefore, the interference was not substantial and the homeowner's nuisance action will likely fail (Choice C). (Choice B) Although compliance with zoning regulations (eg, obtaining municipal permits) helps show that the defendant's interference was not unreasonable, it is not a defense to nuisance. Instead, the homeowner's claim will fail because no other residents were disturbed by the chimes (no substantial interference). (Choice D) Courts may consider priority in time (ie, whether the plaintiff came to the nuisance) when determining if a nuisance is actionable. Here, the homeowner's nuisance claim is actionable since she purchased her house before the clock tower was built. However, she will only prevail on this claim if she proves that the law school's interference was both substantial and unreasonable.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the chimes do not disturb the other residents of the neighborhood."},"questionZh":"一所新成立的法学院在安静的居民区建造了校舍。\n\n该法学院已获得所有必要的市政许可，其中包括一座大型钟楼，钟楼每小时都会鸣响。\n\n钟声只打扰到附近一位居民，这位居民在法学院建造之前就购买了房屋。\n\n她对铃声、警笛声等声响异常敏感，觉得钟声极其烦人。\n\n如果这位居民起诉法学院扰民，她胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为钟声不会打扰到附近的其他居民。","B":"否，因为法学院已获得建造钟楼所需的市政许可。","C":"是，因为钟声干扰了这位居民对其房产的使用和享受。","D":"是，因为这位居民在法学院建造之前就购买了房屋。"},"explanationZh":"妨害行为（Nuisance）可分为公共妨害（干扰公众共有的权利）或私人妨害（干扰私人财产权——本案即属此类）。\n\n当被告对原告使用和享用其财产的干扰同时满足以下两个条件时，即构成私人妨害的责任：实质性——即干扰行为令社区中的普通人感到冒犯、恼人或无法忍受；以及 不合理性——即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用。\n\n如果原告因异常敏感而感到干扰行为冒犯、恼人或无法忍受，但社区中的普通人并无此感，则该干扰不被视为实质性干扰。\n\n在本案中，法学院建造了一座每小时报时的大钟楼，从而干扰了房主对财产的使用和享用。\n\n然而，由于该房主对钟声异常敏感，她是唯一感到钟声极度恼人的人，而其他居民并未受到干扰。\n\n因此，该干扰不构成实质性干扰，房主提起的妨害诉讼很可能会败诉（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 B）尽管遵守分区法规（例如获得市政许可）有助于证明被告的干扰行为并非不合理，但这并不能作为针对妨害指控的抗辩理由。\n\n房主的诉求之所以会失败，是因为其他居民并未受到钟声干扰（即不存在实质性干扰）。\n\n（选项 D）法院在判定妨害行为是否构成诉讼事由时，可能会考虑时间先后顺序（即原告是否在妨害行为存在后才迁入该地）。\n\n在本案中，房主的妨害诉讼具有可诉性，因为她在钟楼建成之前就购买了房屋。\n\n然而，她若要胜诉，必须证明法学院的干扰行为既具有实质性，又具有不合理性。\n\n教育目标：如果原告因异常敏感而感到干扰行为冒犯、恼人或无法忍受，但社区中的普通人并无此感，则该干扰不被视为实质性干扰（因此无法支持妨害诉讼）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821F条（美国法律学会，1977年）（要求构成实质性干扰）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果原告异常敏感，认为其对财产的使用和享用受到的干扰令人反感、烦扰或无法忍受，而社区中的普通人却不会有这种感觉，那么这种干扰就不构成实质性干扰（不足以支持妨害诉讼）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果房主起诉法学院妨害诉讼，房主胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"如果原告异常敏感，认为其对财产的使用和享用受到的干扰令人反感、烦扰或无法忍受，而社区中的普通人却不会有这种感觉，那么这种干扰就不构成实质性干扰（不足以支持妨害诉讼）。","analysis":"妨害行为（Nuisance）可分为公共妨害（干扰公众共有的权利）或私人妨害（干扰私人财产权——本案即属此类）。\n\n当被告对原告使用和享用其财产的干扰同时满足以下两个条件时，即构成私人妨害的责任：实质性——即干扰行为令社区中的普通人感到冒犯、恼人或无法忍受；以及 不合理性——即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用。\n\n如果原告因异常敏感而感到干扰行为冒犯、恼人或无法忍受，但社区中的普通人并无此感，则该干扰不被视为实质性干扰。\n\n在本案中，法学院建造了一座每小时报时的大钟楼，从而干扰了房主对财产的使用和享用。\n\n然而，由于该房主对钟声异常敏感，她是唯一感到钟声极度恼人的人，而其他居民并未受到干扰。\n\n因此，该干扰不构成实质性干扰，房主提起的妨害诉讼很可能会败诉（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 B）尽管遵守分区法规（例如获得市政许可）有助于证明被告的干扰行为并非不合理，但这并不能作为针对妨害指控的抗辩理由。\n\n房主的诉求之所以会失败，是因为其他居民并未受到钟声干扰（即不存在实质性干扰）。\n\n（选项 D）法院在判定妨害行为是否构成诉讼事由时，可能会考虑时间先后顺序（即原告是否在妨害行为存在后才迁入该地）。\n\n在本案中，房主的妨害诉讼具有可诉性，因为她在钟楼建成之前就购买了房屋。\n\n然而，她若要胜诉，必须证明法学院的干扰行为既具有实质性，又具有不合理性。\n\n教育目标：如果原告因异常敏感而感到干扰行为冒犯、恼人或无法忍受，但社区中的普通人并无此感，则该干扰不被视为实质性干扰（因此无法支持妨害诉讼）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821F条（美国法律学会，1977年）（要求构成实质性干扰）。","conclusion":"答案 A：否，因为钟声不会打扰到附近的其他居民。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-2f455d41a8d6c034"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":156,"sourceQuestionPage":466,"sourceQuestionPages":[466],"sourceExplanationPages":[467,468],"sourceAnswerPages":[467,468],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-157","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"Upon the recommendation of her child's pediatrician, a mother purchased a vaporizer for her child, who had been suffering from respiratory congestion. The vaporizer consisted of a gallon-size glass jar, which held water to be heated until it became steam, and a metal heating unit into which the jar fit. The jar was covered by a plastic cap with an opening to allow the steam to escape. At the time the vaporizer was manufactured and sold, there was no safer alternative design. The booklet that accompanied the vaporizer read: \"This product is safe, spillproof, and practically foolproof. It shuts off automatically when the water is gone.\" The booklet had a picture of a vaporizer sending steam over a baby's crib. The mother used the vaporizer whenever the child was suffering from congestion. She placed the vaporizer on the floor near the child's bed. One night, the child got out of bed to get a drink of water and tripped over the cord of the vaporizer as she crossed the room. The top of the vaporizer separated from the base, and boiling water from the jar spilled on the child when the vaporizer tipped over. The child suffered serious burns as a consequence. The child's representative brought an action for damages against the manufacturer of the vaporizer. The manufacturer moved to dismiss after the representative presented the evidence above. Should the manufacturer's motion be granted?","choices":{"A":"No, because a jury could find that the manufacturer expressly represented that the vaporizer was spillproof.","B":"No, because the vaporizer caused a serious injury to the child.","C":"Yes, because it should have been obvious to the mother that the water in the jar would become boiling hot.","D":"Yes, because there was no safer alternative design."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A court should grant a defendant's motion to dismiss if the plaintiff fails to present legally sufficient evidence to support every element of his/her claim (ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor). Therefore, a court should grant a motion to dismiss a products liability action based on misrepresentation if there is insufficient evidence to support any of the following elements: The defendant (a commercial seller) made a misrepresentation of material fact about a product to the public.\n\nA consumer (not necessarily the plaintiff) justifiably relied on this misrepresentation when purchasing that product.\n\nThat product caused the plaintiff harm.\n\nHere, the manufacturer sold the vaporizer with a booklet that expressly stated that the vaporizer was spillproof and included a picture that implied that the vaporizer could be safely used beside a child's crib (misrepresentation). Because a reasonable jury could conclude that the mother justifiably relied on these assertions when she purchased the vaporizer and that the vaporizer caused her child harm, the manufacturer's motion to dismiss should be denied. (Choice B) The fact that the vaporizer seriously injured the child provides evidence of harm. However, the child cannot recover for that harm unless there also is evidence that the manufacturer falsely represented that the vaporizer was spillproof. Since there is such evidence, the court should deny the manufacturer's motion. (Choice C) While it should have been obvious to the mother that the water in the vaporizer would become extremely hot, it was still reasonable for her to rely on the manufacturer's assertion that the product was spillproof. (Choice D) Even if a reasonable jury could find that the manufacturer did not defectively design the vaporizer because there was no safer alternative design, the manufacturer may still be liable for misrepresenting the vaporizer's safety.\n\nEducational objective: Products liability based on misrepresentation arises when (1) the defendant (a commercial supplier) misrepresents a material fact about a product, (2) a consumer justifiably relied on that misrepresentation, and (3) the product caused the plaintiff harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 9 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (products liability by misrepresentation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 402B (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (strict products liability by misrepresentation).","educationalObjective":"Products liability based on misrepresentation arises when (1) the defendant (a commercial supplier) misrepresents a material fact about a product, (2) a consumer justifiably relied on that misrepresentation, and (3) the product caused the plaintiff harm.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 9 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (products liability by misrepresentation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 402B (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (strict products liability by misrepresentation).","rule":"Products liability based on misrepresentation arises when (1) the defendant (a commercial supplier) misrepresents a material fact about a product, (2) a consumer justifiably relied on that misrepresentation, and (3) the product caused the plaintiff harm.","irac":{"issue":"Should the manufacturer's motion be granted?","rule":"Products liability based on misrepresentation arises when (1) the defendant (a commercial supplier) misrepresents a material fact about a product, (2) a consumer justifiably relied on that misrepresentation, and (3) the product caused the plaintiff harm.","analysis":"A court should grant a defendant's motion to dismiss if the plaintiff fails to present legally sufficient evidence to support every element of his/her claim (ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor). Therefore, a court should grant a motion to dismiss a products liability action based on misrepresentation if there is insufficient evidence to support any of the following elements: The defendant (a commercial seller) made a misrepresentation of material fact about a product to the public.\n\nA consumer (not necessarily the plaintiff) justifiably relied on this misrepresentation when purchasing that product.\n\nThat product caused the plaintiff harm.\n\nHere, the manufacturer sold the vaporizer with a booklet that expressly stated that the vaporizer was spillproof and included a picture that implied that the vaporizer could be safely used beside a child's crib (misrepresentation). Because a reasonable jury could conclude that the mother justifiably relied on these assertions when she purchased the vaporizer and that the vaporizer caused her child harm, the manufacturer's motion to dismiss should be denied. (Choice B) The fact that the vaporizer seriously injured the child provides evidence of harm. However, the child cannot recover for that harm unless there also is evidence that the manufacturer falsely represented that the vaporizer was spillproof. Since there is such evidence, the court should deny the manufacturer's motion. (Choice C) While it should have been obvious to the mother that the water in the vaporizer would become extremely hot, it was still reasonable for her to rely on the manufacturer's assertion that the product was spillproof. (Choice D) Even if a reasonable jury could find that the manufacturer did not defectively design the vaporizer because there was no safer alternative design, the manufacturer may still be liable for misrepresenting the vaporizer's safety.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because a jury could find that the manufacturer expressly represented that the vaporizer was spillproof."},"questionZh":"一位母亲听从儿科医生的建议，为患有呼吸道充血的孩子购买了一台蒸汽吸入器。\n\n这台吸入器由一个一加仑大小的玻璃罐和一个金属加热装置组成，玻璃罐装在罐子里加热至蒸汽。\n\n罐子上盖着一个塑料盖，盖子上有一个开口，可以让蒸汽逸出。\n\n在蒸汽吸入器生产和销售的年代，还没有更安全的替代设计。\n\n吸入器的说明书上写着：“本产品安全、防漏、几乎不会出错。水用完后会自动关闭。” 说明书上还配有一张蒸汽吸入器向婴儿床上方喷洒蒸汽的图片。\n\n每当孩子呼吸道充血时，这位母亲都会使用蒸汽吸入器。\n\n她把吸入器放在孩子床边的地板上。\n\n一天晚上，孩子下床喝水时，在穿过房间时被蒸汽吸入器的电线绊倒了。\n\n蒸汽吸入器的顶部与底座分离，蒸汽吸入器倾倒时，罐子里的沸水溅到孩子身上，导致孩子严重烧伤。\n\n孩子的代理人对蒸汽吸入器的制造商提起损害赔偿诉讼。\n\n在代理人提交上述证据后，制造商提出驳回诉讼的动议。\n\n制造商的动议是否应该被准许？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应该，因为陪审团可以认定制造商明确声称该蒸汽吸入器是防溢的。","B":"不应该，因为该蒸汽吸入器对孩子造成了严重伤害。","C":"应该，因为母亲应该清楚地意识到罐子里的水会变得滚烫。","D":"应该，因为没有更安全的替代设计。"},"explanationZh":"如果原告未能提供充分的法律证据来支持其诉讼请求的每一项要素（即，任何合理的陪审团都不可能做出有利于原告的裁决），法院应当准许被告的驳回动议。\n\n因此，如果缺乏足够的证据支持以下任何一项要素，法院应当准许基于虚假陈述的产品责任诉讼的驳回动议：被告（商业销售商）向公众就产品的重要事实作出了虚假陈述。\n\n消费者（不一定是原告）在购买该产品时有理由信赖了该虚假陈述。\n\n该产品对原告造成了损害。\n\n在本案中，制造商销售的电子烟附带的小册子明确指出该电子烟防漏，并配有一张图片，暗示该电子烟可以安全地放在婴儿床边使用（虚假陈述）。\n\n因为一个理性的陪审团可以认定，母亲在购买电子烟时有理由信赖这些说法，并且电子烟对她的孩子造成了伤害，所以制造商的驳回动议应该被驳回。\n\n（选项 B）电子烟严重伤害了孩子这一事实本身就构成了伤害的证据。\n\n然而，除非还有证据表明制造商虚假宣传电子烟防漏，否则孩子无法就该伤害获得赔偿。\n\n由于存在这样的证据，法院应该驳回制造商的动议。\n\n（选项 C）虽然母亲理应知道电子烟中的水会变得非常热，但她信赖制造商关于产品防漏的说法仍然是合理的。\n\n（选项 D）即使一个理性的陪审团可以认定，由于没有更安全的替代设计，制造商并未在电子烟的设计上存在缺陷，但制造商仍然可能因虚假宣传电子烟的安全性而承担责任。\n\n教育目标：基于虚假陈述的产品责任是指：(1)被告（商业供应商）对产品的重要事实作出虚假陈述；(2)消费者有理由信赖该虚假陈述；(3)该产品给原告造成了损害。\n\n参考文献： 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第9条（美国法律协会，1998年）（虚假陈述的产品责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第402B条（美国法律协会，1965年）（虚假陈述的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于虚假陈述的产品责任是指：(1)被告（商业供应商）对产品的重要事实作出虚假陈述；(2)消费者有理由信赖该虚假陈述；(3)该产品给原告造成了损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"制造商的动议是否应予准许？","rule":"基于虚假陈述的产品责任是指：(1)被告（商业供应商）对产品的重要事实作出虚假陈述；(2)消费者有理由信赖该虚假陈述；(3)该产品给原告造成了损害。","analysis":"如果原告未能提供充分的法律证据来支持其诉讼请求的每一项要素（即，任何合理的陪审团都不可能做出有利于原告的裁决），法院应当准许被告的驳回动议。\n\n因此，如果缺乏足够的证据支持以下任何一项要素，法院应当准许基于虚假陈述的产品责任诉讼的驳回动议：被告（商业销售商）向公众就产品的重要事实作出了虚假陈述。\n\n消费者（不一定是原告）在购买该产品时有理由信赖了该虚假陈述。\n\n该产品对原告造成了损害。\n\n在本案中，制造商销售的电子烟附带的小册子明确指出该电子烟防漏，并配有一张图片，暗示该电子烟可以安全地放在婴儿床边使用（虚假陈述）。\n\n因为一个理性的陪审团可以认定，母亲在购买电子烟时有理由信赖这些说法，并且电子烟对她的孩子造成了伤害，所以制造商的驳回动议应该被驳回。\n\n（选项 B）电子烟严重伤害了孩子这一事实本身就构成了伤害的证据。\n\n然而，除非还有证据表明制造商虚假宣传电子烟防漏，否则孩子无法就该伤害获得赔偿。\n\n由于存在这样的证据，法院应该驳回制造商的动议。\n\n（选项 C）虽然母亲理应知道电子烟中的水会变得非常热，但她信赖制造商关于产品防漏的说法仍然是合理的。\n\n（选项 D）即使一个理性的陪审团可以认定，由于没有更安全的替代设计，制造商并未在电子烟的设计上存在缺陷，但制造商仍然可能因虚假宣传电子烟的安全性而承担责任。\n\n教育目标：基于虚假陈述的产品责任是指：(1)被告（商业供应商）对产品的重要事实作出虚假陈述；(2)消费者有理由信赖该虚假陈述；(3)该产品给原告造成了损害。\n\n参考文献： 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第9条（美国法律协会，1998年）（虚假陈述的产品责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第402B条（美国法律协会，1965年）（虚假陈述的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））。","conclusion":"答案 A：不应该，因为陪审团可以认定制造商明确声称该蒸汽吸入器是防溢的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-bcc344c2069cd0ab"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":157,"sourceQuestionPage":469,"sourceQuestionPages":[469],"sourceExplanationPages":[470,471],"sourceAnswerPages":[470,471],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-158","subject":"Torts","topic":"Nuisance","question":"A company operates a factory that requires the use of very high voltage electricity. A man owns property adjacent to the company's plant where he has attempted to carry on a business that requires the use of sensitive electronic equipment. The effectiveness of the man's electronic equipment is impaired by electrical interference arising from the high voltage currents used in the company's plant. The man has complained to the company several times, with no result. There is no way that the company, by taking reasonable precautions, can avoid the interference with the man's operation that arises from the high voltage currents necessary to the company's operation. In the man's action against the company to recover damages for the economic loss caused to him by the electrical interference, will the man prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man's harm was purely economic and did not arise from physical harm to his person or property.","B":"No, unless the company caused a substantial and unreasonable interference with the man's business.","C":"Yes, because the company's activity is abnormally dangerous.","D":"Yes, for loss suffered by the man after the company was made aware of the harm its activity was causing to the man."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A plaintiff can recover damages for economic loss and other injuries related to a private nuisance. To recover such damages, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant's interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property was both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. An interference is not substantial if harm only results from the plaintiff's hypersensitivity (eg, abnormal aversion to the normal ringing of nearby church bells) or abnormal use of his/her own property (eg, sensitive photographic film harmed by heat from nearby factory). Here, the company interfered with the use and enjoyment of the man's property through high-voltage electricity that impaired the sensitive electronic equipment required for his business (an abnormal use of the man's property). Therefore, the man will not recover economic damages for the company's interference with his business unless he can show that the interference was substantial—eg, that the high-voltage electricity interfered with the normal use of his property—and unreasonable. (Choice A) Negligence requires proof of physical harm before a plaintiff can recover for economic loss, but nuisance allows a plaintiff to recover for pure economic loss (ie, economic loss not arising from physical harm). Therefore, the man's lack of physical harm does not preclude his recovery for economic loss based on nuisance. (Choice C) The use of high-voltage electricity may be an abnormally dangerous activity—ie, an uncommon activity in the community that creates a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm that cannot be eliminated by reasonable care. But the company would not be liable for engaging in that activity since the man's harm was caused by electrical interference—not the abnormal danger of that activity (eg, electric shock). (Choice D) Private nuisance does not require proof that the defendant was aware of the harm caused by his/her activity. Therefore, if the company's interference was substantial and unreasonable, the man could recover for losses suffered before and after the company was made aware of his harm.\n\nEducational objective: Private nuisance requires proof that the defendant substantially and unreasonably interfered with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's harm results from his/her hypersensitivity or abnormal use of the property.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance liability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (substantial harm requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference requirement).\n\nPostal Tel.-Cable Co. v. Pac. Gas & Elec. Co., 202 Cal. 382, 385 (1927) (using sensitive electrical equipment is an abnormal use of property).","educationalObjective":"Private nuisance requires proof that the defendant substantially and unreasonably interfered with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's harm results from his/her hypersensitivity or abnormal use of the property.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance liability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (substantial harm requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference requirement).\n\nPostal Tel.-Cable Co. v. Pac. Gas & Elec. Co., 202 Cal. 382, 385 (1927) (using sensitive electrical equipment is an abnormal use of property).","rule":"Private nuisance requires proof that the defendant substantially and unreasonably interfered with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's harm results from his/her hypersensitivity or abnormal use of the property.","irac":{"issue":"In the man's action against the company to recover damages for the economic loss caused to him by the electrical interference, will the man prevail?","rule":"Private nuisance requires proof that the defendant substantially and unreasonably interfered with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's harm results from his/her hypersensitivity or abnormal use of the property.","analysis":"A plaintiff can recover damages for economic loss and other injuries related to a private nuisance. To recover such damages, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant's interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property was both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. An interference is not substantial if harm only results from the plaintiff's hypersensitivity (eg, abnormal aversion to the normal ringing of nearby church bells) or abnormal use of his/her own property (eg, sensitive photographic film harmed by heat from nearby factory). Here, the company interfered with the use and enjoyment of the man's property through high-voltage electricity that impaired the sensitive electronic equipment required for his business (an abnormal use of the man's property). Therefore, the man will not recover economic damages for the company's interference with his business unless he can show that the interference was substantial—eg, that the high-voltage electricity interfered with the normal use of his property—and unreasonable. (Choice A) Negligence requires proof of physical harm before a plaintiff can recover for economic loss, but nuisance allows a plaintiff to recover for pure economic loss (ie, economic loss not arising from physical harm). Therefore, the man's lack of physical harm does not preclude his recovery for economic loss based on nuisance. (Choice C) The use of high-voltage electricity may be an abnormally dangerous activity—ie, an uncommon activity in the community that creates a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm that cannot be eliminated by reasonable care. But the company would not be liable for engaging in that activity since the man's harm was caused by electrical interference—not the abnormal danger of that activity (eg, electric shock). (Choice D) Private nuisance does not require proof that the defendant was aware of the harm caused by his/her activity. Therefore, if the company's interference was substantial and unreasonable, the man could recover for losses suffered before and after the company was made aware of his harm.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, unless the company caused a substantial and unreasonable interference with the man's business."},"questionZh":"某公司运营着一家需要使用超高压电力的工厂。\n\n一名男子在紧邻该公司工厂的地块上拥有一处房产，并试图在那里经营一项需要使用敏感电子设备的业务。\n\n由于该公司工厂使用高压电流而产生的电气干扰，该男子的电子设备效能受到了损害。\n\n该男子曾多次向该公司投诉，但未获解决。\n\n对于该公司而言，即便采取合理的预防措施，也无法避免因其生产经营所需的高压电流而对该男子的业务活动造成的干扰。\n\n在该男子针对该公司提起的、旨在就电气干扰造成的经济损失寻求损害赔偿的诉讼中，该男子能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为该男子遭受的损害纯属经济损失，并非源于对其人身或财产造成的物理损害。","B":"不能，除非该公司对其业务活动造成了实质性且不合理的干扰。","C":"能，因为该公司的活动属于异常危险活动。","D":"能，但仅限于该公司获悉其活动对该男子造成损害之后，该男子所遭受的损失。"},"explanationZh":"原告可就与私人妨害（private nuisance）相关的经济损失及其他损害获得赔偿。\n\n若要获得此类赔偿，原告必须证明被告对其财产使用与享用的干扰同时具备以下特征：实质性（即对于社区中的普通人而言，该干扰令人反感、烦恼或无法忍受）以及不合理性（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的社会效用）。\n\n如果损害仅源于原告的过度敏感（例如，对附近教堂正常钟声的异常厌恶）或其对自身财产的异常使用（例如，感光胶片因附近工厂的热量而受损），则该干扰不被视为实质性干扰。\n\n在本案中，该公司的干扰行为涉及高压电，损害了该男子经营业务所需的敏感电子设备（这属于对该男子财产的一种异常使用）。\n\n因此，除非该男子能证明这种干扰既是实质性的（例如，高压电干扰了其财产的正常使用），又是不合理的，否则他无法就公司对其业务的干扰获得经济损失赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）在过失侵权诉讼中，原告须证明存在人身伤害或财产实物损害（physical harm）方可就经济损失获得赔偿；而在妨害诉讼中，原告可就纯粹经济损失（即非由人身伤害或财产实物损害引起的经济损失）获得赔偿。\n\n因此，即便该男子未遭受人身伤害或财产实物损害，也不妨碍其基于妨害诉讼主张经济损失赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）使用高压电可能构成“异常危险活动”（即社区中不常见的活动，且会产生可预见的、极高的人身伤害风险，而这种风险无法通过合理的谨慎措施消除）。\n\n然而，该公司无需为此活动承担责任，因为该男子遭受的损害是由电气干扰引起的，而非由该活动的异常危险性（例如触电）直接导致。\n\n（选项 D）私人妨害诉讼不要求证明被告明知其活动会造成损害。\n\n因此，如果公司的干扰行为具有实质性和不合理性，该男子便可就公司获悉其损害之前及之后所遭受的损失获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：私人妨害诉讼要求证明被告实质性且不合理地干扰了原告对其财产的使用与享用。\n\n如果原告所受的损害源于其自身的过度敏感或对财产的异常使用，则该干扰不构成“实质性”干扰。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第822条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于私人妨害的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821F条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于实质性损害的要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于不合理干扰的要件）。\n\n*Postal Tel.-Cable Co. v. Pac. Gas & Elec. Co.*, 202 Cal. 382, 385 (1927)（使用敏感电气设备属于对财产的异常使用）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"构成“私人妨害”（private nuisance）需要证明被告实质性且不合理地干扰了原告对其财产的使用和享用。\n\n如果原告遭受的损害源于其自身的过度敏感或对财产的异常使用，则该干扰不被视为实质性干扰。","iracZh":{"issue":"在该男子针对该公司提起的、旨在就电气干扰造成的经济损失寻求损害赔偿的诉讼中，该男子能否胜诉？","rule":"构成“私人妨害”（private nuisance）需要证明被告实质性且不合理地干扰了原告对其财产的使用和享用。\n\n如果原告遭受的损害源于其自身的过度敏感或对财产的异常使用，则该干扰不被视为实质性干扰。","analysis":"原告可就与私人妨害（private nuisance）相关的经济损失及其他损害获得赔偿。\n\n若要获得此类赔偿，原告必须证明被告对其财产使用与享用的干扰同时具备以下特征：实质性（即对于社区中的普通人而言，该干扰令人反感、烦恼或无法忍受）以及不合理性（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的社会效用）。\n\n如果损害仅源于原告的过度敏感（例如，对附近教堂正常钟声的异常厌恶）或其对自身财产的异常使用（例如，感光胶片因附近工厂的热量而受损），则该干扰不被视为实质性干扰。\n\n在本案中，该公司的干扰行为涉及高压电，损害了该男子经营业务所需的敏感电子设备（这属于对该男子财产的一种异常使用）。\n\n因此，除非该男子能证明这种干扰既是实质性的（例如，高压电干扰了其财产的正常使用），又是不合理的，否则他无法就公司对其业务的干扰获得经济损失赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）在过失侵权诉讼中，原告须证明存在人身伤害或财产实物损害（physical harm）方可就经济损失获得赔偿；而在妨害诉讼中，原告可就纯粹经济损失（即非由人身伤害或财产实物损害引起的经济损失）获得赔偿。\n\n因此，即便该男子未遭受人身伤害或财产实物损害，也不妨碍其基于妨害诉讼主张经济损失赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）使用高压电可能构成“异常危险活动”（即社区中不常见的活动，且会产生可预见的、极高的人身伤害风险，而这种风险无法通过合理的谨慎措施消除）。\n\n然而，该公司无需为此活动承担责任，因为该男子遭受的损害是由电气干扰引起的，而非由该活动的异常危险性（例如触电）直接导致。\n\n（选项 D）私人妨害诉讼不要求证明被告明知其活动会造成损害。\n\n因此，如果公司的干扰行为具有实质性和不合理性，该男子便可就公司获悉其损害之前及之后所遭受的损失获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：私人妨害诉讼要求证明被告实质性且不合理地干扰了原告对其财产的使用与享用。\n\n如果原告所受的损害源于其自身的过度敏感或对财产的异常使用，则该干扰不构成“实质性”干扰。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第822条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于私人妨害的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821F条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于实质性损害的要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于不合理干扰的要件）。\n\n*Postal Tel.-Cable Co. v. Pac. Gas & Elec. Co.*, 202 Cal. 382, 385 (1927)（使用敏感电气设备属于对财产的异常使用）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不能，除非该公司对其业务活动造成了实质性且不合理的干扰。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-806db4b749b939f6"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":158,"sourceQuestionPage":472,"sourceQuestionPages":[472],"sourceExplanationPages":[473,474],"sourceAnswerPages":[473,474],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-159","subject":"Torts","topic":"Nuisance","question":"A manufacturing plant emitted a faint noise even though the owner had installed state-of- the-art sound dampeners. The plant operated only on weekdays and only during daylight hours. A homeowner who lived near the plant worked a night shift and could not sleep when he arrived home because of the noise from the plant. The other residents in the area did not notice the noise. Does the homeowner have a viable nuisance claim against the owner of the plant?","choices":{"A":"No, because the homeowner is unusually sensitive to noise during the day.","B":"No, because the plant operates only during the day.","C":"Yes, because the noise is heard beyond the boundaries of the plant.","D":"Yes, because the operation of the plant interferes with the homeowner's quiet use and enjoyment of his property."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Private nuisance v. Public nuisance\n\n(action brought by private plaintiff)\n\nInterference Unreasonable Harm\n\nPublic Public right—eg, Interference is Special harm—harm different health, safety, peace, significant or violated from that of public at large* property law\n\nPrivate Use & enjoyment of Severity of harm Substantial interference— property outweighs utility of offensive, annoying, or interference intolerable to normal person\n\n*Proof of harm not required when plaintiff is public entity or government actor.\n\nNuisance can be either public (interfering with a right common to the general public) OR private (interfering with a private property right—as seen here). Liability for private nuisance arises when the defendant's tortious interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff has an unusual sensitivity that causes him/her to find it offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not. Here, the manufacturing plant interfered with the homeowner's use and enjoyment of his property by emitting a noise that kept him awake during the day (Choice D). But since the homeowner worked a night shift, he was unusually sensitive to the noise that other residents in the community did not notice. Therefore, the interference was not substantial and the homeowner does not have a viable nuisance claim. (Choice B) Although the plant only operates during the day, the homeowner could still recover if the noise was offensive, annoying, or intolerable to normal people in the community (eg, had the other residents found the noise too loud for daytime). (Choice C) The noise was heard beyond the boundaries of the plant—but only by the homeowner. Since the homeowner was disturbed by that noise when none of the other residents noticed it (ie, he was unusually sensitive), his nuisance claim will fail.\n\nEducational objective: Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's unusual sensitivity causes him/her to find it offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821D (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (significant harm requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (liability for nuisance).","educationalObjective":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's unusual sensitivity causes him/her to find it offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 821D (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (significant harm requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (liability for nuisance).","rule":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's unusual sensitivity causes him/her to find it offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not.","irac":{"issue":"Does the homeowner have a viable nuisance claim against the owner of the plant?","rule":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's unusual sensitivity causes him/her to find it offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not.","analysis":"Private nuisance v. Public nuisance\n\n(action brought by private plaintiff)\n\nInterference Unreasonable Harm\n\nPublic Public right—eg, Interference is Special harm—harm different health, safety, peace, significant or violated from that of public at large* property law\n\nPrivate Use & enjoyment of Severity of harm Substantial interference— property outweighs utility of offensive, annoying, or interference intolerable to normal person\n\n*Proof of harm not required when plaintiff is public entity or government actor.\n\nNuisance can be either public (interfering with a right common to the general public) OR private (interfering with a private property right—as seen here). Liability for private nuisance arises when the defendant's tortious interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff has an unusual sensitivity that causes him/her to find it offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a normal person in the community would not. Here, the manufacturing plant interfered with the homeowner's use and enjoyment of his property by emitting a noise that kept him awake during the day (Choice D). But since the homeowner worked a night shift, he was unusually sensitive to the noise that other residents in the community did not notice. Therefore, the interference was not substantial and the homeowner does not have a viable nuisance claim. (Choice B) Although the plant only operates during the day, the homeowner could still recover if the noise was offensive, annoying, or intolerable to normal people in the community (eg, had the other residents found the noise too loud for daytime). (Choice C) The noise was heard beyond the boundaries of the plant—but only by the homeowner. Since the homeowner was disturbed by that noise when none of the other residents noticed it (ie, he was unusually sensitive), his nuisance claim will fail.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the homeowner is unusually sensitive to noise during the day."},"questionZh":"尽管工厂主安装了最先进的隔音装置，该制造厂仍会发出微弱的噪音。\n\n该工厂仅在工作日的日间时段运营。\n\n一位住在工厂附近的房主上夜班，由于工厂的噪音，他下班回家后无法入睡。\n\n该地区的其他居民并未察觉到这种噪音。\n\n该房主针对工厂主提出的妨害（nuisance）索赔是否成立？","choicesZh":{"A":"不成立，因为该房主对日间噪音异常敏感。","B":"不成立，因为该工厂仅在日间运营。","C":"成立，因为在工厂边界之外也能听到该噪音。","D":"成立，因为工厂的运营干扰了房主对其房产的安宁使用与享用。"},"explanationZh":"私害（Private nuisance）与公害（Public nuisance） （由私人原告提起的诉讼） **干扰对象** | **干扰性质** | **损害程度** | **关键要素** **公害** | 公共权利——例如健康、安全、安宁、财产 | 严重干扰 | 特殊损害——损害不同于公众所受的损害* **私害** | 财产的使用与享用 | 损害严重性超过干扰行为的效用 | 实质性干扰——对普通人而言具有冒犯性、令人烦恼或无法忍受 *若原告为公共实体或政府机构，则无需证明存在损害。\n\n妨害行为（Nuisance）既可以是公害（干扰公众共有的权利），也可以是私害（干扰私人财产权——本案即属此类）。当被告对原告财产的使用和享用构成侵权性干扰，且该干扰同时满足以下两个条件时，即产生私害责任：实质性（对社区中的普通人而言具有冒犯性、令人烦恼或无法忍受）以及不合理性（原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用）。\n\n如果原告因异常敏感而感到干扰具有冒犯性、令人烦恼或无法忍受，但社区中的普通人并无此感受，则该干扰不构成实质性干扰。\n\n在本案中，制造厂排放的噪音导致房主在白天无法入睡，从而干扰了其对财产的使用和享用（选项 D）。\n\n但由于房主上夜班，他对噪音异常敏感，而社区其他居民并未察觉该噪音。\n\n因此，该干扰不构成实质性干扰，房主无法提出有效的私害索赔。\n\n（选项 B）尽管工厂仅在白天运营，但如果噪音对社区普通人而言具有冒犯性、令人烦恼或无法忍受（例如，其他居民也认为该噪音在白天过大），房主仍可获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）噪音传播到了工厂边界之外——但只有房主听到了。\n\n由于房主受到噪音干扰而其他居民未察觉（即他异常敏感），他的私害索赔将无法成立。\n\n教学目标：私害是指对原告财产的使用和享用构成的实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果原告因异常敏感而感到干扰具有冒犯性、令人烦恼或无法忍受，但社区中的普通人并无此感受，则该干扰不构成实质性干扰。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821D条（美国法律学会，1979年）（私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821F条（美国法律学会，1979年）（重大损害要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第822条（美国法律学会，1979年）（妨害责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"私人妨害是指对原告使用和享用其房产进行的实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果干扰之所以被原告视为令人反感、恼人或无法忍受，仅仅是因为原告异常敏感（而社区中的普通人并不会有此感受），则该干扰不构成实质性干扰。","iracZh":{"issue":"该房主针对工厂主提出的妨害索赔是否成立？","rule":"私人妨害是指对原告使用和享用其房产进行的实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果干扰之所以被原告视为令人反感、恼人或无法忍受，仅仅是因为原告异常敏感（而社区中的普通人并不会有此感受），则该干扰不构成实质性干扰。","analysis":"私害（Private nuisance）与公害（Public nuisance） （由私人原告提起的诉讼） **干扰对象** | **干扰性质** | **损害程度** | **关键要素** **公害** | 公共权利——例如健康、安全、安宁、财产 | 严重干扰 | 特殊损害——损害不同于公众所受的损害* **私害** | 财产的使用与享用 | 损害严重性超过干扰行为的效用 | 实质性干扰——对普通人而言具有冒犯性、令人烦恼或无法忍受 *若原告为公共实体或政府机构，则无需证明存在损害。\n\n妨害行为（Nuisance）既可以是公害（干扰公众共有的权利），也可以是私害（干扰私人财产权——本案即属此类）。当被告对原告财产的使用和享用构成侵权性干扰，且该干扰同时满足以下两个条件时，即产生私害责任：实质性（对社区中的普通人而言具有冒犯性、令人烦恼或无法忍受）以及不合理性（原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用）。\n\n如果原告因异常敏感而感到干扰具有冒犯性、令人烦恼或无法忍受，但社区中的普通人并无此感受，则该干扰不构成实质性干扰。\n\n在本案中，制造厂排放的噪音导致房主在白天无法入睡，从而干扰了其对财产的使用和享用（选项 D）。\n\n但由于房主上夜班，他对噪音异常敏感，而社区其他居民并未察觉该噪音。\n\n因此，该干扰不构成实质性干扰，房主无法提出有效的私害索赔。\n\n（选项 B）尽管工厂仅在白天运营，但如果噪音对社区普通人而言具有冒犯性、令人烦恼或无法忍受（例如，其他居民也认为该噪音在白天过大），房主仍可获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）噪音传播到了工厂边界之外——但只有房主听到了。\n\n由于房主受到噪音干扰而其他居民未察觉（即他异常敏感），他的私害索赔将无法成立。\n\n教学目标：私害是指对原告财产的使用和享用构成的实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果原告因异常敏感而感到干扰具有冒犯性、令人烦恼或无法忍受，但社区中的普通人并无此感受，则该干扰不构成实质性干扰。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821D条（美国法律学会，1979年）（私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821F条（美国法律学会，1979年）（重大损害要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第822条（美国法律学会，1979年）（妨害责任）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不成立，因为该房主对日间噪音异常敏感。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":159,"sourceQuestionPage":475,"sourceQuestionPages":[475],"sourceExplanationPages":[476,477],"sourceAnswerPages":[476,477],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-160","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A chef wrote a cookbook that contained the recipes for the most requested dishes that she cooked at her popular restaurant. After the chef died, a sous chef who worked at the restaurant began telling the restaurant's customers that the chef was a fraud and that a dishwasher at the restaurant had actually created all of the recipes that were contained in the cookbook. However, when asked by a customer, the dishwasher denied having written any of the recipes in the cookbook and said that the sous chef probably lied because he and the chef had hated each other. The chef's estate learned of the sous chef's statements to the restaurant's customers. Concerned about the effect the statements might have had on the cookbook's sales, the chef's estate filed a defamation suit against the sous chef. Will the chef's estate prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the chef is deceased.","B":"No, because there was no publication.","C":"Yes, because the statements damaged the chef's reputation.","D":"Yes, because the statements likely impacted cookbook sales."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Defamation can be either libel (written statements) OR slander (spoken statements—as seen here). A plaintiff has an action for defamation based on slander if all of the following elements are met: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff (or negligently failed to determine its falsity).\n\nThe statement was defamatory, meaning that the type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated the statement to a third party who understood it.\n\nThe statement caused the plaintiff special damage (usually monetary loss) or was slanderous per se.\n\nHowever, defamation of a deceased person is not actionable.* Therefore, the chef's estate will not prevail in a defamation suit against the sous chef. *A statement about a dead person may amount to defamation of a living person. Additionally, whether an action for defamation of a living person survives the person's death depends on the state's survival statute. (Choice B) The publication (ie, communication) element for defamation was met when the sous chef intentionally told the restaurant's customers that the chef was a fraud and that a dishwasher had created the recipes. (Choices C & D) Calling the chef a fraud was harmful to her reputation and may have impacted cookbook sales (special damage). But defamation of a deceased person is not actionable.\n\nEducational objective: A defamation claim based on libel (written statements) or slander (spoken statements) of a deceased person is not actionable.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 560 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (defamation of deceased persons).","educationalObjective":"A defamation claim based on libel (written statements) or slander (spoken statements) of a deceased person is not actionable.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 560 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (defamation of deceased persons).","rule":"A defamation claim based on libel (written statements) or slander (spoken statements) of a deceased person is not actionable.","irac":{"issue":"Will the chef's estate prevail?","rule":"A defamation claim based on libel (written statements) or slander (spoken statements) of a deceased person is not actionable.","analysis":"Defamation can be either libel (written statements) OR slander (spoken statements—as seen here). A plaintiff has an action for defamation based on slander if all of the following elements are met: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff (or negligently failed to determine its falsity).\n\nThe statement was defamatory, meaning that the type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated the statement to a third party who understood it.\n\nThe statement caused the plaintiff special damage (usually monetary loss) or was slanderous per se.\n\nHowever, defamation of a deceased person is not actionable.* Therefore, the chef's estate will not prevail in a defamation suit against the sous chef. *A statement about a dead person may amount to defamation of a living person. Additionally, whether an action for defamation of a living person survives the person's death depends on the state's survival statute. (Choice B) The publication (ie, communication) element for defamation was met when the sous chef intentionally told the restaurant's customers that the chef was a fraud and that a dishwasher had created the recipes. (Choices C & D) Calling the chef a fraud was harmful to her reputation and may have impacted cookbook sales (special damage). But defamation of a deceased person is not actionable.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the chef is deceased."},"questionZh":"一位厨师写了一本食谱，收录了她在自己颇受欢迎的餐厅里最受欢迎的菜肴的配方。\n\n厨师去世后，一位曾在该餐厅工作的副厨开始向顾客散布谣言，说厨师是个骗子，食谱里的所有菜谱实际上都是餐厅的一名洗碗工创作的。\n\n然而，当一位顾客询问时，洗碗工否认自己写过食谱里的任何菜谱，并说副厨很可能是在撒谎，因为他和厨师之间有过节。\n\n厨师的遗产管理人得知了副厨的这些言论。\n\n由于担心这些言论会影响食谱的销量，遗产管理人对副厨提起了诽谤诉讼。\n\n厨师的遗产管理人会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为厨师已经去世。","B":"不会，因为这些言论没有被公开发表。","C":"会，因为这些言论损害了厨师的名誉。","D":"是的，因为这些言论很可能影响了烹饪书的销量。"},"explanationZh":"诽谤既可以是书面形式（libel，即书面陈述），也可以是口头形式（slander，即口头陈述——本案即属此类）。\n\n若满足以下所有要件，原告即可就口头诽谤提起诉讼：被告明知故犯地作出了关于原告的虚假陈述（或因疏忽而未核实其虚假性）。\n\n该陈述具有诽谤性质，即该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或过失地将该陈述传达给了能够理解该陈述内容的第三方。\n\n该陈述给原告造成了“特别损害”（通常指金钱损失），或者属于“本身即构成诽谤”（slander per se）的情形。\n\n然而，针对已故之人的诽谤行为不可诉（即无法提起法律诉讼）。* 因此，主厨的遗产继承人无法在针对副主厨的诽谤诉讼中胜诉。\n\n*关于死者的陈述有时可能构成对在世之人的诽谤。\n\n此外，针对在世之人的诽谤诉讼是否因当事人死亡而终止，取决于各州关于诉讼存续的法规（survival statute）。\n\n(选项 B) 当副主厨故意向餐厅顾客宣称主厨是个骗子、且食谱是由洗碗工创作时，诽谤构成要件中的“发布”（即传播）要素即已满足。\n\n(选项 C 和 D) 称主厨为骗子损害了她的名誉，并可能影响了食谱书的销量（构成特别损害）。\n\n但针对已故之人的诽谤行为不可诉。\n\n教育目标：针对已故之人的书面诽谤（libel）或口头诽谤（slander）提出的索赔主张不可诉。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 560 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（关于已故之人的诽谤）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于诽谤（书面陈述）或诋毁（口头陈述）已故人士的诽谤诉讼不成立。","iracZh":{"issue":"厨师的遗产继承人会胜诉吗？","rule":"基于诽谤（书面陈述）或诋毁（口头陈述）已故人士的诽谤诉讼不成立。","analysis":"诽谤既可以是书面形式（libel，即书面陈述），也可以是口头形式（slander，即口头陈述——本案即属此类）。\n\n若满足以下所有要件，原告即可就口头诽谤提起诉讼：被告明知故犯地作出了关于原告的虚假陈述（或因疏忽而未核实其虚假性）。\n\n该陈述具有诽谤性质，即该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或过失地将该陈述传达给了能够理解该陈述内容的第三方。\n\n该陈述给原告造成了“特别损害”（通常指金钱损失），或者属于“本身即构成诽谤”（slander per se）的情形。\n\n然而，针对已故之人的诽谤行为不可诉（即无法提起法律诉讼）。* 因此，主厨的遗产继承人无法在针对副主厨的诽谤诉讼中胜诉。\n\n*关于死者的陈述有时可能构成对在世之人的诽谤。\n\n此外，针对在世之人的诽谤诉讼是否因当事人死亡而终止，取决于各州关于诉讼存续的法规（survival statute）。\n\n(选项 B) 当副主厨故意向餐厅顾客宣称主厨是个骗子、且食谱是由洗碗工创作时，诽谤构成要件中的“发布”（即传播）要素即已满足。\n\n(选项 C 和 D) 称主厨为骗子损害了她的名誉，并可能影响了食谱书的销量（构成特别损害）。\n\n但针对已故之人的诽谤行为不可诉。\n\n教育目标：针对已故之人的书面诽谤（libel）或口头诽谤（slander）提出的索赔主张不可诉。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 560 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（关于已故之人的诽谤）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为厨师已经去世。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-28e81e847a555ac7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":160,"sourceQuestionPage":478,"sourceQuestionPages":[478],"sourceExplanationPages":[479,480],"sourceAnswerPages":[479,480],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-161","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A statistics professor told a friend that one of the professor's graduate students had failed an introductory statistics course in the second quarter of his sophomore year in college. The friend repeated this statement to an economics professor, but the friend prefaced it with the proviso that she had heard the information from the statistics professor and had no personal knowledge of whether it was true. The student later learned of the statement and initiated a lawsuit against the friend for slander. At trial, the student proved that he had failed the introductory statistics course in the first quarter, not the second quarter, of his sophomore year in college. Can the student likely recover?","choices":{"A":"No, because the friend merely repeated what she had heard from the statistics professor.","B":"No, because the statement is substantially true.","C":"Yes, because the friend was negligent in failing to verify the statement before repeating it.","D":"Yes, because the friend's statement was inaccurate."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Defamation defenses\n\n(libel, slander, slander per se)\n\nAbsolute Truth Truthful statements are not defamatory privileges Consent Actual – plaintiff agreed to publication Apparent – defendant reasonably believed plaintiff agreed to publication Other Statement made during:\n\nJudicial, administrative, or legislative proceeding Communication to spouse Publication required by law (eg, emergency alert system) Conditional Statement made: privileges about an important public interest (lost if abused) to protect defendant's interests, family members, or third party owed a legal duty (eg, business partner) to person authorized to act in public's interest (eg, 911 call to police) in report of official proceeding or public meeting Defamation can be either libel (ie, written statements) or slander (ie, spoken statements— as seen here). To establish a defamation claim based on slander, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm a person's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated the statement to a third party.\n\nThe statement caused the plaintiff special damage (ie, monetary loss).\n\nBut since defamation requires proof that the defendant made a false statement, truth is a complete defense. A statement that contains slight inaccuracies may nevertheless be considered true and therefore not defamatory. Here, the friend told the economics professor that the student failed an introductory statistics course in the second quarter of his sophomore year. Although the friend was merely repeating what she had heard from the statistics professor, she may have been negligent in failing to verify the statement before doing so (Choices A & C). But even\n\nthough the statement was slightly inaccurate—the student failed the class in the first quarter, not the second quarter—the statement was substantially true (Choice D). Therefore, the student likely cannot recover for slander.\n\nEducational objective: Proof of a statement's falsity is essential to all defamation (ie, libel and slander) claims, so truth serves as a complete defense. A statement that contains slight inaccuracies may nevertheless be considered true and therefore not defamatory.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defining the elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 581A (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (explaining that truth is a defense to defamation).","educationalObjective":"Proof of a statement's falsity is essential to all defamation (ie, libel and slander) claims, so truth serves as a complete defense. A statement that contains slight inaccuracies may nevertheless be considered true and therefore not defamatory.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defining the elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 581A (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (explaining that truth is a defense to defamation).","rule":"Proof of a statement's falsity is essential to all defamation (ie, libel and slander) claims, so truth serves as a complete defense. A statement that contains slight inaccuracies may nevertheless be considered true and therefore not defamatory.","irac":{"issue":"Can the student likely recover?","rule":"Proof of a statement's falsity is essential to all defamation (ie, libel and slander) claims, so truth serves as a complete defense. A statement that contains slight inaccuracies may nevertheless be considered true and therefore not defamatory.","analysis":"Defamation defenses\n\n(libel, slander, slander per se)\n\nAbsolute Truth Truthful statements are not defamatory privileges Consent Actual – plaintiff agreed to publication Apparent – defendant reasonably believed plaintiff agreed to publication Other Statement made during:\n\nJudicial, administrative, or legislative proceeding Communication to spouse Publication required by law (eg, emergency alert system) Conditional Statement made: privileges about an important public interest (lost if abused) to protect defendant's interests, family members, or third party owed a legal duty (eg, business partner) to person authorized to act in public's interest (eg, 911 call to police) in report of official proceeding or public meeting Defamation can be either libel (ie, written statements) or slander (ie, spoken statements— as seen here). To establish a defamation claim based on slander, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm a person's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated the statement to a third party.\n\nThe statement caused the plaintiff special damage (ie, monetary loss).\n\nBut since defamation requires proof that the defendant made a false statement, truth is a complete defense. A statement that contains slight inaccuracies may nevertheless be considered true and therefore not defamatory. Here, the friend told the economics professor that the student failed an introductory statistics course in the second quarter of his sophomore year. Although the friend was merely repeating what she had heard from the statistics professor, she may have been negligent in failing to verify the statement before doing so (Choices A & C). But even\n\nthough the statement was slightly inaccurate—the student failed the class in the first quarter, not the second quarter—the statement was substantially true (Choice D). Therefore, the student likely cannot recover for slander.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the statement is substantially true."},"questionZh":"一位统计学教授告诉一位朋友，他的一位研究生在大二第二学期挂科了一门统计学入门课程。\n\n这位朋友将此事告诉了一位经济学教授，但她事先声明，她是从那位统计学教授那里听说的，自己并不清楚真假。\n\n后来，这位学生得知此事后，以诽谤罪起诉了这位朋友。\n\n庭审中，这位学生证明，他挂科的统计学入门课程是在大二第一学期，而不是第二学期。\n\n这位学生有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为这位朋友只是复述了她从统计学教授那里听到的内容。","B":"不可能，因为该陈述基本属实。","C":"可能，因为这位朋友在复述之前没有核实该陈述，属于疏忽。","D":"可能，因为这位朋友的陈述不准确。"},"explanationZh":"诽谤抗辩 （书面诽谤 [libel]、口头诽谤 [slander]、本身构成诽谤的口头诽谤 [slander per se]） 绝对特权（Absolute Privileges）：真实陈述不构成诽谤；同意（包括实际同意——原告同意发布；表见同意——被告合理相信原告同意发布）；其他情形：司法、行政或立法程序中的陈述；向配偶进行的沟通；法律要求的发布（如紧急警报系统）。有条件特权（Conditional Privileges）：涉及重大公共利益的陈述（滥用则丧失特权）；为保护被告自身利益、家庭成员或负有法律义务的第三方（如商业伙伴）利益而作的陈述；向获授权代表公众利益行事的人（如拨打911报警）作出的陈述；关于官方程序或公开会议的报告。\n\n诽谤既可以是书面诽谤（即书面陈述），也可以是口头诽谤（即口头陈述——本案即属此类）。要确立口头诽谤的诉讼主张，原告必须证明以下所有要件：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实，或因疏忽而未核实其不实性。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害某人的名誉。\n\n被告故意或疏忽地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n该陈述给原告造成了特殊损害（即金钱损失）。\n\n但由于诽谤诉讼要求证明被告作出了不实陈述，因此“真实性”构成完全抗辩。\n\n包含细微不准确之处的陈述仍可能被视为真实，从而不构成诽谤。\n\n在本案中，朋友告诉经济学教授，该学生在大二第二学期未通过一门统计学入门课程。\n\n尽管该朋友只是复述了从统计学教授那里听来的话，但她在复述前未核实该陈述，可能存在疏忽（选项A和C）。\n\n然而， 即使该陈述存在细微不准确之处——学生是在第一学期而非第二学期挂科——该陈述在实质上仍属真实（选项D）。\n\n因此，该学生很可能无法就口头诽谤获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：证明陈述不实是所有诽谤（即书面诽谤和口头诽谤）诉讼主张的必要条件，因此真实性构成完全抗辩。\n\n包含细微不准确之处的陈述仍可能被视为真实，从而不构成诽谤。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第558条（美国法律协会，1977年）（定义了诽谤的构成要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第581A条（美国法律学会，1977年）（阐明真实性是针对诽谤指控的一种抗辩理由）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"证明陈述不实是所有诽谤（即书面诽谤和口头诋毁）诉讼的必要条件，因此，真实性本身就是完全的抗辩理由。\n\n即使陈述中存在轻微的不准确之处，也可能被认定为真实，从而不构成诽谤。","iracZh":{"issue":"学生是否有可能获得赔偿？","rule":"证明陈述不实是所有诽谤（即书面诽谤和口头诋毁）诉讼的必要条件，因此，真实性本身就是完全的抗辩理由。\n\n即使陈述中存在轻微的不准确之处，也可能被认定为真实，从而不构成诽谤。","analysis":"诽谤抗辩 （书面诽谤 [libel]、口头诽谤 [slander]、本身构成诽谤的口头诽谤 [slander per se]） 绝对特权（Absolute Privileges）：真实陈述不构成诽谤；同意（包括实际同意——原告同意发布；表见同意——被告合理相信原告同意发布）；其他情形：司法、行政或立法程序中的陈述；向配偶进行的沟通；法律要求的发布（如紧急警报系统）。有条件特权（Conditional Privileges）：涉及重大公共利益的陈述（滥用则丧失特权）；为保护被告自身利益、家庭成员或负有法律义务的第三方（如商业伙伴）利益而作的陈述；向获授权代表公众利益行事的人（如拨打911报警）作出的陈述；关于官方程序或公开会议的报告。\n\n诽谤既可以是书面诽谤（即书面陈述），也可以是口头诽谤（即口头陈述——本案即属此类）。要确立口头诽谤的诉讼主张，原告必须证明以下所有要件：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实，或因疏忽而未核实其不实性。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害某人的名誉。\n\n被告故意或疏忽地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n该陈述给原告造成了特殊损害（即金钱损失）。\n\n但由于诽谤诉讼要求证明被告作出了不实陈述，因此“真实性”构成完全抗辩。\n\n包含细微不准确之处的陈述仍可能被视为真实，从而不构成诽谤。\n\n在本案中，朋友告诉经济学教授，该学生在大二第二学期未通过一门统计学入门课程。\n\n尽管该朋友只是复述了从统计学教授那里听来的话，但她在复述前未核实该陈述，可能存在疏忽（选项A和C）。\n\n然而， 即使该陈述存在细微不准确之处——学生是在第一学期而非第二学期挂科——该陈述在实质上仍属真实（选项D）。\n\n因此，该学生很可能无法就口头诽谤获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：证明陈述不实是所有诽谤（即书面诽谤和口头诽谤）诉讼主张的必要条件，因此真实性构成完全抗辩。\n\n包含细微不准确之处的陈述仍可能被视为真实，从而不构成诽谤。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第558条（美国法律协会，1977年）（定义了诽谤的构成要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第581A条（美国法律学会，1977年）（阐明真实性是针对诽谤指控的一种抗辩理由）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可能，因为该陈述基本属实。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":161,"sourceQuestionPage":481,"sourceQuestionPages":[481],"sourceExplanationPages":[482,483],"sourceAnswerPages":[482,483],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-162","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A cigarette maker created and published a magazine advertisement that featured a person dressed as a race-car driver with a helmet on standing in front of a distinctive race car. In fact, the car looked almost exactly like the very unusually marked car driven by a famous and popular driver. The driver in the ad was not identified, and his face was not shown. The cigarette maker published the advertisement without obtaining the famous driver's permission. The famous race-car driver sued the cigarette maker for economic loss only, based on common law misappropriation of the right of publicity. The cigarette maker moved to dismiss the complaint. Will the cigarette maker's motion to dismiss the complaint be granted?","choices":{"A":"No, because there are sufficient indicia of the famous driver's identity to support a verdict of liability.","B":"Yes, because the driver is a public figure.","C":"Yes, because the famous driver did not claim any emotional or dignitary loss.","D":"Yes, because there was no mention of the famous driver's name in the ad."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A defendant's motion to dismiss will be granted if the plaintiff's complaint fails to allege facts sufficient to support his/her claim. For a misappropriation of the right to publicity (ie, appropriation of name or likeness) claim, the plaintiff must allege that the defendant: used the plaintiff's identity (ie, name, likeness, or item closely associated with the plaintiff) without authorization\n\nto receive a benefit (typically for a commercial purpose—eg, using the plaintiff's picture to advertise a product).\n\nHere, though the cigarette maker did not use the famous driver's name or picture, it did feature a distinctive race car that was almost identical to his unusually marked car (item closely associated with the driver) in its advertisement (commercial purpose). Therefore, that advertisement contained sufficient indicia of the driver's identity to support his complaint and the court will likely deny the cigarette maker's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) Public figures (persons who achieve pervasive fame/notoriety or who voluntarily involve themselves in a public controversy) have the same right to control the use of their identity as everyone else. Therefore, the driver's status as a public figure will not prevent him from recovering for misappropriation of his right to publicity. (Choice C) Misappropriation of the right to publicity allows the plaintiff to recover for emotional distress or personal humiliation, but neither is required. Therefore, the driver's complaint did not need to allege any emotional or dignitary loss. (Choice D) Although the cigarette maker's ad did not mention the famous driver by name, its use of his unusually marked race car was sufficient to depict or identify him.\n\nEducational objective: Misappropriation of the right to publicity is the unauthorized use of the plaintiff's identity— ie, name, likeness, or item closely associated with the plaintiff—for the defendant's benefit (typically for a commercial purpose).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 652C (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (appropriation of name or likeness).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 12(b)(6) (motion to dismiss).","educationalObjective":"Misappropriation of the right to publicity is the unauthorized use of the plaintiff's identity— ie, name, likeness, or item closely associated with the plaintiff—for the defendant's benefit (typically for a commercial purpose).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 652C (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (appropriation of name or likeness).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 12(b)(6) (motion to dismiss).","rule":"Misappropriation of the right to publicity is the unauthorized use of the plaintiff's identity— ie, name, likeness, or item closely associated with the plaintiff—for the defendant's benefit (typically for a commercial purpose).","irac":{"issue":"Will the cigarette maker's motion to dismiss the complaint be granted?","rule":"Misappropriation of the right to publicity is the unauthorized use of the plaintiff's identity— ie, name, likeness, or item closely associated with the plaintiff—for the defendant's benefit (typically for a commercial purpose).","analysis":"A defendant's motion to dismiss will be granted if the plaintiff's complaint fails to allege facts sufficient to support his/her claim. For a misappropriation of the right to publicity (ie, appropriation of name or likeness) claim, the plaintiff must allege that the defendant: used the plaintiff's identity (ie, name, likeness, or item closely associated with the plaintiff) without authorization\n\nto receive a benefit (typically for a commercial purpose—eg, using the plaintiff's picture to advertise a product).\n\nHere, though the cigarette maker did not use the famous driver's name or picture, it did feature a distinctive race car that was almost identical to his unusually marked car (item closely associated with the driver) in its advertisement (commercial purpose). Therefore, that advertisement contained sufficient indicia of the driver's identity to support his complaint and the court will likely deny the cigarette maker's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) Public figures (persons who achieve pervasive fame/notoriety or who voluntarily involve themselves in a public controversy) have the same right to control the use of their identity as everyone else. Therefore, the driver's status as a public figure will not prevent him from recovering for misappropriation of his right to publicity. (Choice C) Misappropriation of the right to publicity allows the plaintiff to recover for emotional distress or personal humiliation, but neither is required. Therefore, the driver's complaint did not need to allege any emotional or dignitary loss. (Choice D) Although the cigarette maker's ad did not mention the famous driver by name, its use of his unusually marked race car was sufficient to depict or identify him.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because there are sufficient indicia of the famous driver's identity to support a verdict of liability."},"questionZh":"一家卷烟制造商制作并发布了一则杂志广告，画面中展示了一位身穿赛车手服装、头戴头盔的人，站在一辆极具特色的赛车前。\n\n事实上，这辆车看起来与一位著名且广受欢迎的车手所驾驶的那辆涂装独特的赛车几乎一模一样。\n\n广告中的车手身份未被明确标示，其面部也未露出。\n\n卷烟制造商在未获得该著名车手许可的情况下发布了这则广告。\n\n这位著名赛车手依据普通法中关于“擅自挪用公开权”（misappropriation of the right of publicity）的侵权理论，起诉卷烟制造商，仅就其遭受的经济损失主张赔偿。\n\n卷烟制造商提出动议，请求驳回该诉讼。\n\n卷烟制造商驳回诉讼的动议会被法院批准吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为广告中包含了足以表明该著名车手身份的特征，足以支持认定被告承担责任的裁决。","B":"会，因为该车手属于公众人物。","C":"会，因为该著名车手并未主张任何精神或人格尊严方面的损失。","D":"会，因为广告中未提及该著名车手的姓名。"},"explanationZh":"如果原告的起诉状未能主张足以支持其诉求的事实，法院将批准被告提出的驳回起诉动议。\n\n对于“擅自利用公开权”（即擅自使用姓名或肖像）的诉求，原告必须主张被告存在以下行为：未经授权使用原告的身份特征（即姓名、肖像或与原告密切相关的物品） 以获取利益（通常出于商业目的——例如，使用原告的照片来宣传产品）。\n\n在本案中，尽管该卷烟制造商没有使用这位著名赛车手的姓名或照片，但其广告（出于商业目的）中展示了一辆独特的赛车，该赛车与车手那辆具有独特涂装的赛车（即与车手密切相关的物品）几乎一模一样。\n\n因此，该广告包含了足以表明车手身份特征的要素，足以支持其诉求，法院很可能会驳回卷烟制造商提出的驳回起诉动议。\n\n（选项 B）公众人物（即获得广泛知名度或恶名，或自愿卷入公共争议的人）与其他人一样，拥有控制他人使用其身份特征的权利。\n\n因此，该车手的公众人物身份并不妨碍他就其公开权被擅自利用一事寻求救济。\n\n（选项 C）在擅自利用公开权的诉讼中，原告可以就精神痛苦或个人受辱寻求赔偿，但这并非必要条件。\n\n因此，该车手的起诉状无需主张存在任何情感或人格尊严方面的损失。\n\n（选项 D）尽管卷烟制造商的广告未提及这位著名赛车手的姓名，但其使用车手那辆具有独特涂装的赛车，已足以描绘或识别出该车手。\n\n教育目标：擅自利用公开权是指为被告利益（通常出于商业目的）而未经授权使用原告身份特征（即姓名、肖像或与原告密切相关的物品）的行为。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 652C 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（擅自使用姓名或肖像）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 12(b)(6) 条（驳回起诉动议）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“擅自挪用公开权”是指为被告自身利益（通常为商业目的）而未经许可使用原告身份特征的行为——即使用原告的姓名、肖像或与原告密切相关的特定事物。","iracZh":{"issue":"卷烟制造商驳回诉讼的动议会被法院批准吗？","rule":"“擅自挪用公开权”是指为被告自身利益（通常为商业目的）而未经许可使用原告身份特征的行为——即使用原告的姓名、肖像或与原告密切相关的特定事物。","analysis":"如果原告的起诉状未能主张足以支持其诉求的事实，法院将批准被告提出的驳回起诉动议。\n\n对于“擅自利用公开权”（即擅自使用姓名或肖像）的诉求，原告必须主张被告存在以下行为：未经授权使用原告的身份特征（即姓名、肖像或与原告密切相关的物品） 以获取利益（通常出于商业目的——例如，使用原告的照片来宣传产品）。\n\n在本案中，尽管该卷烟制造商没有使用这位著名赛车手的姓名或照片，但其广告（出于商业目的）中展示了一辆独特的赛车，该赛车与车手那辆具有独特涂装的赛车（即与车手密切相关的物品）几乎一模一样。\n\n因此，该广告包含了足以表明车手身份特征的要素，足以支持其诉求，法院很可能会驳回卷烟制造商提出的驳回起诉动议。\n\n（选项 B）公众人物（即获得广泛知名度或恶名，或自愿卷入公共争议的人）与其他人一样，拥有控制他人使用其身份特征的权利。\n\n因此，该车手的公众人物身份并不妨碍他就其公开权被擅自利用一事寻求救济。\n\n（选项 C）在擅自利用公开权的诉讼中，原告可以就精神痛苦或个人受辱寻求赔偿，但这并非必要条件。\n\n因此，该车手的起诉状无需主张存在任何情感或人格尊严方面的损失。\n\n（选项 D）尽管卷烟制造商的广告未提及这位著名赛车手的姓名，但其使用车手那辆具有独特涂装的赛车，已足以描绘或识别出该车手。\n\n教育目标：擅自利用公开权是指为被告利益（通常出于商业目的）而未经授权使用原告身份特征（即姓名、肖像或与原告密切相关的物品）的行为。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 652C 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（擅自使用姓名或肖像）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 12(b)(6) 条（驳回起诉动议）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为广告中包含了足以表明该著名车手身份的特征，足以支持认定被告承担责任的裁决。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-b6721a1baa3aee8b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":162,"sourceQuestionPage":484,"sourceQuestionPages":[484],"sourceExplanationPages":[485,486],"sourceAnswerPages":[485,486],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-163","subject":"Torts","topic":"Economic & Dignitary Torts","question":"A real estate developer was trying to purchase land on which he intended to build a large commercial development. An elderly widow had rejected all of the developer's offers to buy her ancestral home, where she had lived all her life and which was located in the middle of the developer's planned development. Finally, the developer offered her $250,000. He told her that it was his last offer and that if she rejected it, state law authorized him to have her property condemned. The widow then consulted her nephew, a law student, who researched the question and advised her that the developer had no power of condemnation under state law. The widow had been frightened by the developer's threat and was outraged when she learned that the developer had lied to her. If the widow asserts a claim based on misrepresentation against the developer, will she prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the widow suffered no pecuniary loss.","B":"No, if the developer's offer of $250,000 equaled or exceeded the market value of the widow's property.","C":"Yes, if the developer knew he had no legal power of condemnation.","D":"Yes, if the developer tried to take unfair advantage of a gross difference between himself and the widow in commercial knowledge and experience."},"answer":"A","explanation":"To establish a prima facie case of intentional misrepresentation (ie, fraud or deceit), the plaintiff must show that: the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresented a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and\n\nthe plaintiff reasonably relied on the misrepresentation and suffered pecuniary loss (ie, monetary loss) as a result.\n\nHere, the developer may have knowingly or recklessly misrepresented state law with the intent to persuade the widow to sell her home. But since the widow did not rely on the developer's statement (eg, by selling her home) and suffered no pecuniary loss (eg, by selling her home for less than its worth), she will not prevail on a misrepresentation claim. (Choice B) The widow did not sell her home in reliance on the developer's statement (and therefore could not have suffered pecuniary loss), so it does not matter whether the developer's offer equaled or exceeded the market value of her home. (Choice C) Even if the developer knew that he had no legal power of condemnation (ie, he knowingly misrepresented a material fact), the widow will not prevail because she did not rely on his misrepresentation or suffer a pecuniary loss. (Choice D) The developer's superior commercial knowledge and his experience go to whether the widow could have reasonably relied on his misrepresentation. But since the widow did not rely on his statement, his knowledge and experience are irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: Liability for intentional misrepresentation arises when (1) the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresents a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and (2) the plaintiff reasonably relies on the misrepresentation and suffers pecuniary loss as a result.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 525 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (fraudulent misrepresentation).","educationalObjective":"Liability for intentional misrepresentation arises when (1) the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresents a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and (2) the plaintiff reasonably relies on the misrepresentation and suffers pecuniary loss as a result.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 525 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (fraudulent misrepresentation).","rule":"Liability for intentional misrepresentation arises when (1) the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresents a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and (2) the plaintiff reasonably relies on the misrepresentation and suffers pecuniary loss as a result.","irac":{"issue":"If the widow asserts a claim based on misrepresentation against the developer, will she prevail?","rule":"Liability for intentional misrepresentation arises when (1) the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresents a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and (2) the plaintiff reasonably relies on the misrepresentation and suffers pecuniary loss as a result.","analysis":"To establish a prima facie case of intentional misrepresentation (ie, fraud or deceit), the plaintiff must show that: the defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresented a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance and\n\nthe plaintiff reasonably relied on the misrepresentation and suffered pecuniary loss (ie, monetary loss) as a result.\n\nHere, the developer may have knowingly or recklessly misrepresented state law with the intent to persuade the widow to sell her home. But since the widow did not rely on the developer's statement (eg, by selling her home) and suffered no pecuniary loss (eg, by selling her home for less than its worth), she will not prevail on a misrepresentation claim. (Choice B) The widow did not sell her home in reliance on the developer's statement (and therefore could not have suffered pecuniary loss), so it does not matter whether the developer's offer equaled or exceeded the market value of her home. (Choice C) Even if the developer knew that he had no legal power of condemnation (ie, he knowingly misrepresented a material fact), the widow will not prevail because she did not rely on his misrepresentation or suffer a pecuniary loss. (Choice D) The developer's superior commercial knowledge and his experience go to whether the widow could have reasonably relied on his misrepresentation. But since the widow did not rely on his statement, his knowledge and experience are irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the widow suffered no pecuniary loss."},"questionZh":"一位房地产开发商正试图购入土地，计划开发一个大型商业项目。\n\n一位年迈的寡妇拒绝了开发商提出的所有收购其祖宅的报价；这处房产位于开发商规划项目的中心地带，也是她生活了一辈子的地方。\n\n最终，开发商向她开出了25万美元的报价。\n\n他告诉她，这是最后一次报价，如果她拒绝，根据州法律，他有权申请征收（强制收购）她的房产。\n\n随后，这位寡妇咨询了身为法学院学生的侄子；侄子研究了相关法律问题，告知她开发商并无州法律赋予的征收权。\n\n寡妇此前曾因开发商的威胁而感到恐惧，在得知开发商对自己撒谎后，她感到非常愤怒。\n\n如果这位寡妇针对开发商提出基于虚假陈述的索赔主张，她能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为这位寡妇并未遭受金钱损失。","B":"不能，如果开发商提出的25万美元报价等于或超过了该寡妇房产的市场价值。","C":"能，如果开发商明知自己没有合法的征收权。","D":"能，如果开发商试图利用自己与寡妇在商业知识和经验上的巨大差距，占取不当优势。"},"explanationZh":"要确立故意失实陈述（即欺诈或欺骗）的初步案情（prima facie case），原告必须证明：被告明知或罔顾事实地对重要事实作出了失实陈述，且意图诱使原告产生信赖；以及 原告合理地信赖了该失实陈述，并因此遭受了金钱损失（即经济损失）。\n\n在本案中，开发商可能确实明知或罔顾事实地就州法律作出了失实陈述，意图说服那位寡妇出售其房屋。\n\n但由于该寡妇并未信赖开发商的陈述（例如，并未因此出售房屋），也未遭受金钱损失（例如，并未因低价出售房屋而受损），因此她在失实陈述的索赔主张上将无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）该寡妇并未因信赖开发商的陈述而出售房屋（因此也不可能遭受金钱损失），所以开发商的报价是否等于或高于其房屋的市场价值并不重要。\n\n（选项 C）即使开发商明知自己无权进行征收（即他明知故犯地对重要事实作出了失实陈述），该寡妇仍无法胜诉，因为她并未信赖该失实陈述，也未遭受金钱损失。\n\n（选项 D）开发商在商业方面的丰富知识和经验，涉及该寡妇是否能够合理信赖其失实陈述的问题。\n\n但由于该寡妇并未信赖其陈述，因此他的知识和经验与本案无关。\n\n教学目标：当满足以下条件时，即产生故意失实陈述的法律责任：(1) 被告明知或罔顾事实地对重要事实作出失实陈述，意图诱使原告产生信赖；以及 (2) 原告合理信赖该失实陈述，并因此遭受金钱损失。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 525 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（欺诈性失实陈述）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"故意虚假陈述的责任产生于以下情况：(1) 被告明知或鲁莽地对重要事实作虚假陈述，意图诱使原告产生信赖；以及 (2) 原告合理地信赖了该虚假陈述，并因此遭受了金钱损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果这位寡妇针对开发商提出基于虚假陈述的索赔主张，她能胜诉吗？","rule":"故意虚假陈述的责任产生于以下情况：(1) 被告明知或鲁莽地对重要事实作虚假陈述，意图诱使原告产生信赖；以及 (2) 原告合理地信赖了该虚假陈述，并因此遭受了金钱损失。","analysis":"要确立故意失实陈述（即欺诈或欺骗）的初步案情（prima facie case），原告必须证明：被告明知或罔顾事实地对重要事实作出了失实陈述，且意图诱使原告产生信赖；以及 原告合理地信赖了该失实陈述，并因此遭受了金钱损失（即经济损失）。\n\n在本案中，开发商可能确实明知或罔顾事实地就州法律作出了失实陈述，意图说服那位寡妇出售其房屋。\n\n但由于该寡妇并未信赖开发商的陈述（例如，并未因此出售房屋），也未遭受金钱损失（例如，并未因低价出售房屋而受损），因此她在失实陈述的索赔主张上将无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）该寡妇并未因信赖开发商的陈述而出售房屋（因此也不可能遭受金钱损失），所以开发商的报价是否等于或高于其房屋的市场价值并不重要。\n\n（选项 C）即使开发商明知自己无权进行征收（即他明知故犯地对重要事实作出了失实陈述），该寡妇仍无法胜诉，因为她并未信赖该失实陈述，也未遭受金钱损失。\n\n（选项 D）开发商在商业方面的丰富知识和经验，涉及该寡妇是否能够合理信赖其失实陈述的问题。\n\n但由于该寡妇并未信赖其陈述，因此他的知识和经验与本案无关。\n\n教学目标：当满足以下条件时，即产生故意失实陈述的法律责任：(1) 被告明知或罔顾事实地对重要事实作出失实陈述，意图诱使原告产生信赖；以及 (2) 原告合理信赖该失实陈述，并因此遭受金钱损失。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 525 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（欺诈性失实陈述）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不能，因为这位寡妇并未遭受金钱损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-fdd9f36c92825f1b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":163,"sourceQuestionPage":487,"sourceQuestionPages":[487],"sourceExplanationPages":[488,489,490],"sourceAnswerPages":[488,489,490],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-164","subject":"Torts","topic":"Nuisance","question":"A businesswoman owns a tract of land in fee simple, and an environmentalist owns the adjoining tract of land in fee simple. The businesswoman has kept the lawns and trees on her land trimmed and neat. The environmentalist \"lets nature take its course\" on his land. The result on the environmentalist's land is a tangle of underbrush, fallen trees, and standing trees that are in danger of losing limbs. Many of the trees on the environmentalist's land are near the businesswoman's property line. In the past, debris and large limbs have been blown from the environmentalist's land onto the businesswoman's land. By local standards the environmentalist's land is an eyesore that depresses market values of real property in the vicinity, but the condition of the land violates no applicable laws or ordinances. The businesswoman has demanded that the environmentalist keep the trees near her land trimmed. The environmentalist has refused. The businesswoman brought an appropriate action against the environmentalist to require him to abate what she alleges to be a nuisance. In the lawsuit, the only issue is whether the condition of the environmentalist's land constitutes a nuisance. What is the businesswoman's strongest argument?","choices":{"A":"The condition of the environmentalist's land cannot otherwise be challenged under any law or ordinance.","B":"The condition of the environmentalist's land has an adverse impact on real estate values.","C":"The condition of the environmentalist's land poses a danger to the occupants of her land.","D":"The condition of the environmentalist's land violates community aesthetic standards."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property. An interference arising from a natural condition on land (eg, encroaching trees or plants) is generally not a nuisance. However, some courts have held that a natural condition on land may be a nuisance if it caused—or poses an imminent danger of—actual harm to adjoining property or those on it. Here, the environmentalist has kept his land in its natural condition. As a result, debris and large tree limbs from his land have blown onto the businesswoman's property in the past. And since many of the environmentalist's trees are near the businesswoman's property line and in danger of losing limbs, her strongest argument for why the environmentalist's land constitutes a nuisance is that the condition of his land poses an imminent danger to the occupants of her land. (Choice A) Compliance with the law is one factor that weighs in favor of a finding that the interference was not unreasonable. Therefore, the fact that the condition of the environmentalist's land cannot be challenged under any law or ordinance favors the environmentalist—not the businesswoman. (Choices B & D) A natural condition on land can only support a private nuisance claim if the condition caused—or poses an imminent danger of—actual harm to adjoining property or those on it. Adverse impacts on real estate values and violations of community aesthetic standards do not cause—or pose an imminent danger of—actual harm to adjoining property or those on it. Therefore, these are not the businesswoman's strongest arguments.\n\nEducational objective: An interference arising from a natural condition on land is generally not a nuisance. However, some courts have held that a natural condition may be a nuisance if it caused, or poses an imminent danger of, actual harm to adjoining property or those on it.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821D (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 840 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (failure to abate harmful natural condition on land).","educationalObjective":"An interference arising from a natural condition on land is generally not a nuisance. However, some courts have held that a natural condition may be a nuisance if it caused, or poses an imminent danger of, actual harm to adjoining property or those on it.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 821D (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 840 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (failure to abate harmful natural condition on land).","rule":"An interference arising from a natural condition on land is generally not a nuisance. However, some courts have held that a natural condition may be a nuisance if it caused, or poses an imminent danger of, actual harm to adjoining property or those on it.","irac":{"issue":"What is the businesswoman's strongest argument?","rule":"An interference arising from a natural condition on land is generally not a nuisance. However, some courts have held that a natural condition may be a nuisance if it caused, or poses an imminent danger of, actual harm to adjoining property or those on it.","analysis":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property. An interference arising from a natural condition on land (eg, encroaching trees or plants) is generally not a nuisance. However, some courts have held that a natural condition on land may be a nuisance if it caused—or poses an imminent danger of—actual harm to adjoining property or those on it. Here, the environmentalist has kept his land in its natural condition. As a result, debris and large tree limbs from his land have blown onto the businesswoman's property in the past. And since many of the environmentalist's trees are near the businesswoman's property line and in danger of losing limbs, her strongest argument for why the environmentalist's land constitutes a nuisance is that the condition of his land poses an imminent danger to the occupants of her land. (Choice A) Compliance with the law is one factor that weighs in favor of a finding that the interference was not unreasonable. Therefore, the fact that the condition of the environmentalist's land cannot be challenged under any law or ordinance favors the environmentalist—not the businesswoman. (Choices B & D) A natural condition on land can only support a private nuisance claim if the condition caused—or poses an imminent danger of—actual harm to adjoining property or those on it. Adverse impacts on real estate values and violations of community aesthetic standards do not cause—or pose an imminent danger of—actual harm to adjoining property or those on it. Therefore, these are not the businesswoman's strongest arguments.","conclusion":"Answer C: The condition of the environmentalist's land poses a danger to the occupants of her land."},"questionZh":"一位女商人拥有一块完全所有权（fee simple）的土地，一位环保主义者拥有毗邻的另一块完全所有权土地。\n\n女商人一直将自己土地上的草坪和树木修剪得整洁有序；而环保主义者则任由其土地“顺其自然”。\n\n结果，环保主义者的土地上杂草灌木丛生，既有倒伏的树木，也有随时可能断枝坠落的树木。\n\n环保主义者土地上的许多树木都靠近女商人的地界线。\n\n过去，曾有杂物和大树枝从环保主义者的土地被风吹落到女商人的土地上。\n\n按当地标准衡量，环保主义者的土地显得杂乱难看，拉低了周边房地产的市场价值，但该土地的状况并未违反任何现行法律或法规。\n\n女商人曾要求环保主义者修剪靠近她土地的树木，但遭到拒绝。\n\n女商人针对环保主义者提起诉讼，要求其消除她所主张的“妨害”（nuisance）。\n\n本案的唯一争议焦点在于：环保主义者土地的状况是否构成妨害。\n\n女商人最有力的论据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"环保主义者土地的状况无法依据任何法律或法规受到质疑。","B":"环保主义者土地的状况对房地产价值产生了不利影响。","C":"环保主义者土地的状况对其土地上的人员构成危险。","D":"环保主义者土地的状况违反了社区的审美标准。"},"explanationZh":"私害（private nuisance）是指对原告财产的使用和享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n源于土地自然状况（例如，侵入邻地的树木或植物）的干扰通常不构成私害。\n\n然而，一些法院裁定，如果土地上的自然状况导致了——或构成了导致——邻近财产或其上人员遭受实际损害的紧迫危险，则该自然状况可能构成私害。\n\n在本案中，环保人士保持了其土地的自然状态。\n\n因此，过去曾有来自其土地的碎片和大树枝被吹落到女商人的财产上。\n\n鉴于环保人士的许多树木靠近女商人的地界线且存在断枝风险，女商人主张环保人士的土地构成私害的最有力论据是：其土地状况对女商人土地上的居住者构成了紧迫危险。\n\n（选项 A）遵守法律是认定干扰并非不合理的一个考量因素。\n\n因此，环保人士的土地状况未违反任何法律或条例这一事实，有利于环保人士，而非女商人。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）只有当土地上的自然状况导致了——或构成了导致——邻近财产或其上人员遭受实际损害的紧迫危险时，该状况才能支持私害索赔。\n\n对房地产价值的不利影响以及违反社区美学标准，并不会导致——或构成导致——邻近财产或其上人员遭受实际损害的紧迫危险。\n\n因此，这些并非女商人最有力的论据。\n\n教育目标：源于土地自然状况的干扰通常不构成私害。\n\n然而，一些法院裁定，如果自然状况导致了——或构成了导致——邻近财产或其上人员遭受实际损害的紧迫危险，则该状况可能构成私害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（私害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 840 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（未消除土地上有害的自然状况）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"源于土地自然状况的干扰通常不构成妨害。\n\n然而，一些法院裁定，如果某种自然状况导致了对毗邻财产或其上人员的实际损害，或者构成了此类损害的紧迫危险，则该自然状况可能构成妨害。","iracZh":{"issue":"女商人最有力的论据是什么？","rule":"源于土地自然状况的干扰通常不构成妨害。\n\n然而，一些法院裁定，如果某种自然状况导致了对毗邻财产或其上人员的实际损害，或者构成了此类损害的紧迫危险，则该自然状况可能构成妨害。","analysis":"私害（private nuisance）是指对原告财产的使用和享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n源于土地自然状况（例如，侵入邻地的树木或植物）的干扰通常不构成私害。\n\n然而，一些法院裁定，如果土地上的自然状况导致了——或构成了导致——邻近财产或其上人员遭受实际损害的紧迫危险，则该自然状况可能构成私害。\n\n在本案中，环保人士保持了其土地的自然状态。\n\n因此，过去曾有来自其土地的碎片和大树枝被吹落到女商人的财产上。\n\n鉴于环保人士的许多树木靠近女商人的地界线且存在断枝风险，女商人主张环保人士的土地构成私害的最有力论据是：其土地状况对女商人土地上的居住者构成了紧迫危险。\n\n（选项 A）遵守法律是认定干扰并非不合理的一个考量因素。\n\n因此，环保人士的土地状况未违反任何法律或条例这一事实，有利于环保人士，而非女商人。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）只有当土地上的自然状况导致了——或构成了导致——邻近财产或其上人员遭受实际损害的紧迫危险时，该状况才能支持私害索赔。\n\n对房地产价值的不利影响以及违反社区美学标准，并不会导致——或构成导致——邻近财产或其上人员遭受实际损害的紧迫危险。\n\n因此，这些并非女商人最有力的论据。\n\n教育目标：源于土地自然状况的干扰通常不构成私害。\n\n然而，一些法院裁定，如果自然状况导致了——或构成了导致——邻近财产或其上人员遭受实际损害的紧迫危险，则该状况可能构成私害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（私害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 840 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（未消除土地上有害的自然状况）。","conclusion":"答案 C：环保主义者土地的状况对其土地上的人员构成危险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-66c121ada5f9c5c4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":164,"sourceQuestionPage":491,"sourceQuestionPages":[491],"sourceExplanationPages":[492,493],"sourceAnswerPages":[492,493],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-165","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A man sued his neighbor for defamation based on the following facts: The neighbor told a friend that the man had set fire to a house in the neighborhood. The friend, who knew the man well, did not believe the neighbor's allegation, and the neighbor in fact knew that it was false. The friend told the man about the neighbor's allegation. The man was very upset by the allegation, but neither the man nor the neighbor nor the friend communicated the allegation to anyone else. Should the man prevail in his lawsuit?","choices":{"A":"No, because the friend did not believe what the neighbor had said.","B":"No, because the man cannot prove that he suffered pecuniary loss.","C":"Yes, because the man was very upset at hearing what the neighbor had said.","D":"Yes, because the neighbor communicated to the friend the false accusation that the man had committed a serious crime."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Defamation can be either libel (written statements) OR slander (spoken statements—as seen here). To establish a defamation claim based on slander, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff (or negligently failed to determine its falsity).\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party.\n\nThat statement caused the plaintiff special damage (ie, pecuniary/monetary loss).\n\nHowever, some spoken statements are slanderous per se because they are so harmful to the plaintiff's reputation that damages are presumed. Therefore, proof of special damage is unnecessary when a statement falsely accused the plaintiff of any of the following: committing a serious crime (eg, arson, murder, rape, theft) engaging in conduct that adversely affects the plaintiff's occupation (eg, butcher sells rotten meats) committing serious sexual misconduct (eg, adultery) having a loathsome disease (eg, sexually transmitted infection) Here, the neighbor falsely told the friend that the man had set fire to a house in the neighborhood (serious crime). Since that type of statement constitutes slander per se, the man's harm is presumed. Therefore, the man should prevail in his defamation lawsuit even if he cannot prove pecuniary loss (Choice B). (Choice A) Defamation requires proof that the defendant's false statement was communicated to a third party—not that the third party believed it. Therefore, the man can prevail even if the friend did not believe the neighbor's allegation. (Choice C) Defamation does not require proof of emotional distress, so the fact that the man was very upset after hearing the neighbor's allegation has no effect on the viability of the man's claim. However, proof of emotional distress would be required in an intentional or negligent infliction of emotional distress claim.\n\nEducational objective: Slander generally requires proof of special damage. However, damage is presumed when the statement was slanderous per se—ie, it accused the plaintiff of (1) a serious crime, (2) conduct that adversely affects his/her occupation, (3) serious sexual misconduct, or (4) having a loathsome disease.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defamation elements).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 568 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (libel and slander distinguished).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 570–74 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (slander per se).","educationalObjective":"Slander generally requires proof of special damage. However, damage is presumed when the statement was slanderous per se—ie, it accused the plaintiff of (1) a serious crime, (2) conduct that adversely affects his/her occupation, (3) serious sexual misconduct, or (4) having a loathsome disease.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defamation elements).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 568 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (libel and slander distinguished).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 570–74 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (slander per se).","rule":"Slander generally requires proof of special damage. However, damage is presumed when the statement was slanderous per se—ie, it accused the plaintiff of (1) a serious crime, (2) conduct that adversely affects his/her occupation, (3) serious sexual misconduct, or (4) having a loathsome disease.","irac":{"issue":"Should the man prevail in his lawsuit?","rule":"Slander generally requires proof of special damage. However, damage is presumed when the statement was slanderous per se—ie, it accused the plaintiff of (1) a serious crime, (2) conduct that adversely affects his/her occupation, (3) serious sexual misconduct, or (4) having a loathsome disease.","analysis":"Defamation can be either libel (written statements) OR slander (spoken statements—as seen here). To establish a defamation claim based on slander, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff (or negligently failed to determine its falsity).\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party.\n\nThat statement caused the plaintiff special damage (ie, pecuniary/monetary loss).\n\nHowever, some spoken statements are slanderous per se because they are so harmful to the plaintiff's reputation that damages are presumed. Therefore, proof of special damage is unnecessary when a statement falsely accused the plaintiff of any of the following: committing a serious crime (eg, arson, murder, rape, theft) engaging in conduct that adversely affects the plaintiff's occupation (eg, butcher sells rotten meats) committing serious sexual misconduct (eg, adultery) having a loathsome disease (eg, sexually transmitted infection) Here, the neighbor falsely told the friend that the man had set fire to a house in the neighborhood (serious crime). Since that type of statement constitutes slander per se, the man's harm is presumed. Therefore, the man should prevail in his defamation lawsuit even if he cannot prove pecuniary loss (Choice B). (Choice A) Defamation requires proof that the defendant's false statement was communicated to a third party—not that the third party believed it. Therefore, the man can prevail even if the friend did not believe the neighbor's allegation. (Choice C) Defamation does not require proof of emotional distress, so the fact that the man was very upset after hearing the neighbor's allegation has no effect on the viability of the man's claim. However, proof of emotional distress would be required in an intentional or negligent infliction of emotional distress claim.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the neighbor communicated to the friend the false accusation that the man had committed a serious crime."},"questionZh":"一名男子因邻居的言论起诉其诽谤，案情如下：邻居向一位朋友声称该男子在社区内纵火烧毁了一所房屋。\n\n这位朋友非常了解该男子，因此并不相信邻居的指控；事实上，邻居也明知该指控不实。\n\n朋友将邻居的指控告诉了该男子。\n\n该男子对这一指控感到非常气愤，但该男子、邻居和朋友均未向其他人透露过这一指控。\n\n该男子能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为朋友并不相信邻居所说的话。","B":"不能，因为该男子无法证明自己遭受了金钱损失。","C":"能，因为该男子在听到邻居的话后感到非常气愤。","D":"能，因为邻居向朋友传达了关于该男子犯下严重罪行的虚假指控。"},"explanationZh":"诽谤既可以是书面形式（libel，即“文字诽谤”），也可以是口头形式（slander，即“口头诽谤”——本案即属此类）。\n\n若要确立基于口头诽谤的索赔，原告必须证明以下所有要件：被告明知故犯地作出了关于原告的虚假陈述（或因疏忽而未核实其虚假性）。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或疏忽地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n该陈述给原告造成了“特别损害”（即金钱/经济损失）。\n\n然而，某些口头陈述属于“本身构成诽谤”（slander per se），因为它们对原告名誉的损害极大，以至于法律推定存在损害。\n\n因此，如果陈述虚假指控原告存在以下任一情形，则无需证明特别损害：犯有严重罪行（如纵火、谋杀、强奸、盗窃）；从事对其职业产生不利影响的行为（如屠夫出售腐烂肉类）；犯有严重性不当行为（如通奸）；患有令人厌恶的疾病（如性传播感染）。在本案中，邻居向朋友谎称该男子在社区内纵火（属于严重罪行）。\n\n由于此类陈述构成“本身诽谤”，法律推定该男子受到了损害。\n\n因此，即使该男子无法证明存在金钱损失，他在诽谤诉讼中也应胜诉（选项 B）。\n\n（选项 A）诽谤索赔要求证明被告的虚假陈述被传达给了第三方，但不要求第三方相信该陈述。\n\n因此，即使朋友不相信邻居的指控，该男子仍可胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）诽谤索赔不要求证明存在精神痛苦，因此该男子在听到邻居指控后感到非常难过这一事实，并不影响其索赔主张的成立。\n\n不过，在“故意或疏忽造成精神痛苦”的索赔中，则需要证明存在精神痛苦。\n\n教育目标：口头诽谤通常要求证明存在特别损害。\n\n然而，当陈述属于“本身构成诽谤”时，法律推定存在损害——即陈述指控原告：(1) 犯有严重罪行；(2) 从事对其职业产生不利影响的行为；(3) 犯有严重性不当行为；或 (4) 患有令人厌恶的疾病。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第558条（美国法律协会，1977年）（诽谤的构成要素）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第568条（美国法律协会，1977年）（书面诽谤与口头诽谤的区分）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第570–574条（美国法律协会，1977年）（本身构成诽谤的口头诽谤）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"口头诽谤（slander）通常需要证明存在“特殊损害”（即金钱损失）。\n\n然而，如果言论属于“本身即构成诽谤”（slander per se），则推定存在损害——即指控原告：(1) 犯有严重罪行；(2) 有对其职业产生不利影响的行为；(3) 有严重的性不端行为；或 (4) 患有令人厌恶的疾病。","iracZh":{"issue":"该男子能胜诉吗？","rule":"口头诽谤（slander）通常需要证明存在“特殊损害”（即金钱损失）。\n\n然而，如果言论属于“本身即构成诽谤”（slander per se），则推定存在损害——即指控原告：(1) 犯有严重罪行；(2) 有对其职业产生不利影响的行为；(3) 有严重的性不端行为；或 (4) 患有令人厌恶的疾病。","analysis":"诽谤既可以是书面形式（libel，即“文字诽谤”），也可以是口头形式（slander，即“口头诽谤”——本案即属此类）。\n\n若要确立基于口头诽谤的索赔，原告必须证明以下所有要件：被告明知故犯地作出了关于原告的虚假陈述（或因疏忽而未核实其虚假性）。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或疏忽地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n该陈述给原告造成了“特别损害”（即金钱/经济损失）。\n\n然而，某些口头陈述属于“本身构成诽谤”（slander per se），因为它们对原告名誉的损害极大，以至于法律推定存在损害。\n\n因此，如果陈述虚假指控原告存在以下任一情形，则无需证明特别损害：犯有严重罪行（如纵火、谋杀、强奸、盗窃）；从事对其职业产生不利影响的行为（如屠夫出售腐烂肉类）；犯有严重性不当行为（如通奸）；患有令人厌恶的疾病（如性传播感染）。在本案中，邻居向朋友谎称该男子在社区内纵火（属于严重罪行）。\n\n由于此类陈述构成“本身诽谤”，法律推定该男子受到了损害。\n\n因此，即使该男子无法证明存在金钱损失，他在诽谤诉讼中也应胜诉（选项 B）。\n\n（选项 A）诽谤索赔要求证明被告的虚假陈述被传达给了第三方，但不要求第三方相信该陈述。\n\n因此，即使朋友不相信邻居的指控，该男子仍可胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）诽谤索赔不要求证明存在精神痛苦，因此该男子在听到邻居指控后感到非常难过这一事实，并不影响其索赔主张的成立。\n\n不过，在“故意或疏忽造成精神痛苦”的索赔中，则需要证明存在精神痛苦。\n\n教育目标：口头诽谤通常要求证明存在特别损害。\n\n然而，当陈述属于“本身构成诽谤”时，法律推定存在损害——即陈述指控原告：(1) 犯有严重罪行；(2) 从事对其职业产生不利影响的行为；(3) 犯有严重性不当行为；或 (4) 患有令人厌恶的疾病。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第558条（美国法律协会，1977年）（诽谤的构成要素）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第568条（美国法律协会，1977年）（书面诽谤与口头诽谤的区分）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第570–574条（美国法律协会，1977年）（本身构成诽谤的口头诽谤）。","conclusion":"答案 D：能，因为邻居向朋友传达了关于该男子犯下严重罪行的虚假指控。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-881da389828d82c4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":165,"sourceQuestionPage":494,"sourceQuestionPages":[494],"sourceExplanationPages":[495,496],"sourceAnswerPages":[495,496],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-166","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A gas company built a large refining facility that conformed to zoning requirements on land near a landowner's property. The landowner had his own home and a mini-golf business on his property. In a nuisance action against the gas company, the landowner established that the refinery emitted fumes that made many people feel quite sick when they were outside on his property for longer than a few minutes. The landowner's mini-golf business had greatly declined as a consequence, and the value of his property had gone down markedly. Is the landowner likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the landowner has offered no evidence demonstrating that the gas company was negligent.","B":"No, because the refinery conforms to the zoning requirements.","C":"Yes, because the refinery has substantially and unreasonably interfered with the landowner's use and enjoyment of his property.","D":"Yes, because the value of the landowner's property has declined."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A nuisance can be either public (interfering with a public right—eg, blocking a public highway) or private (interfering with a private property right—as seen here). Liability for private nuisance arises when the defendant's interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property is both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the gravity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. Severe harm (eg, bodily harm to persons on the property) will always outweigh the utility of the defendant's conduct. Therefore, an interference that causes severe harm is unreasonable as a matter of law. Here, the gas company's refinery substantially interfered with the use and enjoyment of the landowner's property (home and business) by emitting fumes that made people feel quite sick when they were on the property for more than a few minutes. And since the fumes caused physical illness (severe harm), that interference was unreasonable as a matter of law. Therefore, the landowner will likely prevail on his nuisance claim. (Choice A) For private nuisance, the defendant's conduct must be tortious—but not necessarily negligent (ie, creating an unreasonable risk of interference). Therefore, the landowner can prevail even if he offers no evidence demonstrating that the gas company was negligent. (Choice B) Compliance with zoning requirements—although not a complete defense to private nuisance—can help show that the defendant's interference was not unreasonable. But here, since the gas company's interference was unreasonable as a matter of law (because the company's fumes caused severe harm), the company's regulatory compliance is inconsequential. (Choice D) Private nuisance allows the plaintiff to recover damages for a reduction in property value, but a reduction in property value is not required to prevail. Therefore, the landowner will likely prevail because the refinery substantially and unreasonably interfered with the use and enjoyment of his property—not because the value of the property has declined.\n\nEducational objective: Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property. An interference is unreasonable as a matter of law when the plaintiff's harm is severe.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance liability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (substantial harm requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 829A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (severe harm is unreasonable).","educationalObjective":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property. An interference is unreasonable as a matter of law when the plaintiff's harm is severe.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance liability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (substantial harm requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 829A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (severe harm is unreasonable).","rule":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property. An interference is unreasonable as a matter of law when the plaintiff's harm is severe.","irac":{"issue":"Is the landowner likely to prevail?","rule":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property. An interference is unreasonable as a matter of law when the plaintiff's harm is severe.","analysis":"A nuisance can be either public (interfering with a public right—eg, blocking a public highway) or private (interfering with a private property right—as seen here). Liability for private nuisance arises when the defendant's interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property is both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the gravity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. Severe harm (eg, bodily harm to persons on the property) will always outweigh the utility of the defendant's conduct. Therefore, an interference that causes severe harm is unreasonable as a matter of law. Here, the gas company's refinery substantially interfered with the use and enjoyment of the landowner's property (home and business) by emitting fumes that made people feel quite sick when they were on the property for more than a few minutes. And since the fumes caused physical illness (severe harm), that interference was unreasonable as a matter of law. Therefore, the landowner will likely prevail on his nuisance claim. (Choice A) For private nuisance, the defendant's conduct must be tortious—but not necessarily negligent (ie, creating an unreasonable risk of interference). Therefore, the landowner can prevail even if he offers no evidence demonstrating that the gas company was negligent. (Choice B) Compliance with zoning requirements—although not a complete defense to private nuisance—can help show that the defendant's interference was not unreasonable. But here, since the gas company's interference was unreasonable as a matter of law (because the company's fumes caused severe harm), the company's regulatory compliance is inconsequential. (Choice D) Private nuisance allows the plaintiff to recover damages for a reduction in property value, but a reduction in property value is not required to prevail. Therefore, the landowner will likely prevail because the refinery substantially and unreasonably interfered with the use and enjoyment of his property—not because the value of the property has declined.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the refinery has substantially and unreasonably interfered with the landowner's use and enjoyment of his property."},"questionZh":"一家天然气公司在某位土地所有者的地产附近建造了一座大型炼油设施，该设施符合土地分区规划要求。\n\n该土地所有者在自己的地产上拥有住所并经营着一项迷你高尔夫业务。\n\n在针对该天然气公司提起的妨害（nuisance）诉讼中，土地所有者证实，炼油厂排放的废气导致许多人在其地产户外停留超过几分钟后便感到严重不适。\n\n受此影响，土地所有者的迷你高尔夫业务大幅衰退，其地产价值也显著下降。\n\n该土地所有者胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为土地所有者未提供证据证明天然气公司存在过失。","B":"不大，因为炼油厂符合土地分区规划要求。","C":"很大，因为炼油厂实质性且不合理地干扰了土地所有者对其地产的使用与享用。","D":"很大，因为土地所有者的地产价值下降了。"},"explanationZh":"妨害行为（nuisance）可分为公害（干扰公共权利，例如阻塞公共公路）和私害（干扰私人财产权，本案即属此类）。\n\n当被告对原告财产的使用和享用构成以下两种性质的干扰时，即产生私害责任：一是“实质性”干扰——即对社区中的普通人而言，该干扰令人反感、烦恼或无法忍受；二是“不合理”干扰——即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用。\n\n严重的损害（例如对财产所在地人员造成人身伤害）总是会超过被告行为的效用。\n\n因此，造成严重损害的干扰在法律上即被视为不合理。\n\n在本案中，天然气公司的炼油厂排放的废气导致人们在涉案财产（住宅及营业场所）停留短短几分钟后便感到严重不适，从而实质性地干扰了土地所有者对该财产的使用和享用。\n\n鉴于这些废气造成了身体疾病（即严重损害），该干扰在法律上即构成不合理。\n\n因此，土地所有者很可能在妨害行为的诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）对于私害而言，被告的行为必须构成侵权，但不一定非得涉及过失（即制造了不合理的干扰风险）。\n\n因此，即使土地所有者未能提供证据证明天然气公司存在过失，他仍可胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）遵守土地分区规划要求虽不能作为私害诉讼的完全抗辩理由，但有助于证明被告的干扰并非不合理。\n\n然而在本案中，由于天然气公司的干扰在法律上已被认定为不合理（因其废气造成了严重损害），该公司遵守监管规定的事实便无关紧要。\n\n（选项 D）在私害诉讼中，原告虽可就财产价值贬值主张损害赔偿，但财产价值贬值并非胜诉的必要条件。\n\n因此，土地所有者胜诉的原因在于炼油厂实质性且不合理地干扰了他对财产的使用和享用，而非在于财产价值的下降。\n\n教育目标：私害是指对原告财产的使用和享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n当原告遭受的损害严重时，该干扰在法律上即被视为不合理。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第822条（美国法律学会，1979年）（私人妨害责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821F条（美国法律学会，1979年）（实质性损害要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律学会，1979年）（不合理干扰要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第829A条（美国法律学会，1979年）（严重损害即为不合理）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"私害（Private nuisance）是指对原告地产的使用与享用进行的实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n当原告遭受的损害严重时，该干扰在法律上即被视为不合理。","iracZh":{"issue":"该土地所有者胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"私害（Private nuisance）是指对原告地产的使用与享用进行的实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n当原告遭受的损害严重时，该干扰在法律上即被视为不合理。","analysis":"妨害行为（nuisance）可分为公害（干扰公共权利，例如阻塞公共公路）和私害（干扰私人财产权，本案即属此类）。\n\n当被告对原告财产的使用和享用构成以下两种性质的干扰时，即产生私害责任：一是“实质性”干扰——即对社区中的普通人而言，该干扰令人反感、烦恼或无法忍受；二是“不合理”干扰——即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用。\n\n严重的损害（例如对财产所在地人员造成人身伤害）总是会超过被告行为的效用。\n\n因此，造成严重损害的干扰在法律上即被视为不合理。\n\n在本案中，天然气公司的炼油厂排放的废气导致人们在涉案财产（住宅及营业场所）停留短短几分钟后便感到严重不适，从而实质性地干扰了土地所有者对该财产的使用和享用。\n\n鉴于这些废气造成了身体疾病（即严重损害），该干扰在法律上即构成不合理。\n\n因此，土地所有者很可能在妨害行为的诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）对于私害而言，被告的行为必须构成侵权，但不一定非得涉及过失（即制造了不合理的干扰风险）。\n\n因此，即使土地所有者未能提供证据证明天然气公司存在过失，他仍可胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）遵守土地分区规划要求虽不能作为私害诉讼的完全抗辩理由，但有助于证明被告的干扰并非不合理。\n\n然而在本案中，由于天然气公司的干扰在法律上已被认定为不合理（因其废气造成了严重损害），该公司遵守监管规定的事实便无关紧要。\n\n（选项 D）在私害诉讼中，原告虽可就财产价值贬值主张损害赔偿，但财产价值贬值并非胜诉的必要条件。\n\n因此，土地所有者胜诉的原因在于炼油厂实质性且不合理地干扰了他对财产的使用和享用，而非在于财产价值的下降。\n\n教育目标：私害是指对原告财产的使用和享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n当原告遭受的损害严重时，该干扰在法律上即被视为不合理。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第822条（美国法律学会，1979年）（私人妨害责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821F条（美国法律学会，1979年）（实质性损害要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律学会，1979年）（不合理干扰要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第829A条（美国法律学会，1979年）（严重损害即为不合理）。","conclusion":"答案 C：很大，因为炼油厂实质性且不合理地干扰了土地所有者对其地产的使用与享用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-94e031420069e8c3"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":166,"sourceQuestionPage":497,"sourceQuestionPages":[497],"sourceExplanationPages":[498,499],"sourceAnswerPages":[498,499],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-167","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"At a country auction, a collector acquired an antique cabinet that he recognized as an extremely rare and valuable collector's item. Unfortunately, the cabinet had several coats of varnish and paint over the original finish. Its potential value could only be realized if these layers could be removed without damaging the original finish. Much of the value of the cabinet depended on the condition of a unique oil finish, the secret of which died with the cabinet's inventor. A professional restorer of antique furniture recommended that the collector use a certain paint-stripping product to remove the paint and varnish from the cabinet. The collector obtained and read a sales brochure published by the manufacturer of the paint-stripping product. The brochure stated: \"This product will renew all antique furniture. Will not damage original oil finishes.\" The collector purchased this paint-stripping product and used it on his cabinet, being very careful to follow the accompanying instructions exactly. Despite the collector's care, the cabinet's original finish was irreparably damaged. When finally refinished, the cabinet was worth less than 20% of what it would have been worth if its original finish had been preserved. If the collector sues the manufacturer of the paint-stripping product to recover the loss he has suffered as a result of the destruction of the cabinet's original finish, will the collector likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, if the product was not dangerous to persons.","B":"No, unless the product was defective when sold by the manufacturer.","C":"Yes, if the loss would not have occurred had the statement in the brochure been true.","D":"Yes, unless no other known removal technique would have preserved the cabinet's original finish."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Products liability claims\n\nClaims Required elements\n\nStrict products Commercial seller (ie, manufacturer, distributor, retailer) liability produced or sold defective product Defect caused plaintiff harm Negligence Seller (commercial or noncommercial) failed to use reasonable care in producing or inspecting defective product Product caused plaintiff harm Intentional tort Seller (commercial or noncommercial) purposefully or knowingly produced or sold defective product Product caused plaintiff harm Misrepresentation* Commercial seller misrepresented material fact about product Consumer justifiably relied on misrepresentation when purchasing product Product caused plaintiff harm Warranty Merchantability (implied) Commercial seller warranted product is generally acceptable & reasonably fit for its ordinary purpose Fitness for particular purpose (implied) Seller (commercial or noncommercial) warranted product is fit for particular purpose Purchaser relied on seller's judgment or skill to purchase product Express Seller (commercial or noncommercial) made affirmation of fact or promise that is part of basis of bargain *Commercial sellers are strictly liable for the misrepresentations they make regarding their products—regardless of whether those products are defective.\n\nA commercial seller (ie, manufacturer, distributor, retailer) is subject to strict liability for harm caused by defective products. In addition, a commercial seller can be strictly liable for misrepresentations regarding any of its products—regardless of whether that product is defective. To recover based on misrepresentation, the plaintiff must show that: the commercial seller misrepresented a material fact about a product—ie, a fact that would be important to a normal purchaser\n\nthe plaintiff justifiably relied on that misrepresentation when purchasing the product and\n\nthe product caused the plaintiff physical harm—ie, harm that would not have occurred had the seller's representation been true.\n\nHere, the manufacturer's brochure falsely stated that its paint-stripping product \"[w]ill not damage original oil finishes\" (misrepresentation). The collector read the brochure before purchasing the product to use on his antique cabinet (justifiable reliance). And since the destruction of the cabinet's original finish would not have occurred had the statement in the brochure been true (causation), the collector will likely prevail. (Choice A) Products liability based on misrepresentation applies to any type of physical harm (ie, personal injury or property damage), so the fact that this product was not dangerous to persons would not absolve the manufacturer of liability. (Choice B) A commercial seller is strictly liable for harm caused by its misrepresentations about any of its products—not just defective ones. (Choice D) Products liability can be based on a commercial seller's misrepresentation about a product even if no other product can satisfy that representation.\n\nEducational objective: Products liability based on misrepresentation arises when (1) a commercial seller misrepresented a material fact about a product, (2) the plaintiff justifiably relied on that misrepresentation, and (3) the product caused the plaintiff physical harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 402B (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a commercial seller is subject to strict liability for physical harm caused by its misrepresentations regarding its products).","educationalObjective":"Products liability based on misrepresentation arises when (1) a commercial seller misrepresented a material fact about a product, (2) the plaintiff justifiably relied on that misrepresentation, and (3) the product caused the plaintiff physical harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 402B (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that a commercial seller is subject to strict liability for physical harm caused by its misrepresentations regarding its products).","rule":"Products liability based on misrepresentation arises when (1) a commercial seller misrepresented a material fact about a product, (2) the plaintiff justifiably relied on that misrepresentation, and (3) the product caused the plaintiff physical harm.","irac":{"issue":"If the collector sues the manufacturer of the paint-stripping product to recover the loss he has suffered as a result of the destruction of the cabinet's original finish, will the collector likely prevail?","rule":"Products liability based on misrepresentation arises when (1) a commercial seller misrepresented a material fact about a product, (2) the plaintiff justifiably relied on that misrepresentation, and (3) the product caused the plaintiff physical harm.","analysis":"Products liability claims\n\nClaims Required elements\n\nStrict products Commercial seller (ie, manufacturer, distributor, retailer) liability produced or sold defective product Defect caused plaintiff harm Negligence Seller (commercial or noncommercial) failed to use reasonable care in producing or inspecting defective product Product caused plaintiff harm Intentional tort Seller (commercial or noncommercial) purposefully or knowingly produced or sold defective product Product caused plaintiff harm Misrepresentation* Commercial seller misrepresented material fact about product Consumer justifiably relied on misrepresentation when purchasing product Product caused plaintiff harm Warranty Merchantability (implied) Commercial seller warranted product is generally acceptable & reasonably fit for its ordinary purpose Fitness for particular purpose (implied) Seller (commercial or noncommercial) warranted product is fit for particular purpose Purchaser relied on seller's judgment or skill to purchase product Express Seller (commercial or noncommercial) made affirmation of fact or promise that is part of basis of bargain *Commercial sellers are strictly liable for the misrepresentations they make regarding their products—regardless of whether those products are defective.\n\nA commercial seller (ie, manufacturer, distributor, retailer) is subject to strict liability for harm caused by defective products. In addition, a commercial seller can be strictly liable for misrepresentations regarding any of its products—regardless of whether that product is defective. To recover based on misrepresentation, the plaintiff must show that: the commercial seller misrepresented a material fact about a product—ie, a fact that would be important to a normal purchaser\n\nthe plaintiff justifiably relied on that misrepresentation when purchasing the product and\n\nthe product caused the plaintiff physical harm—ie, harm that would not have occurred had the seller's representation been true.\n\nHere, the manufacturer's brochure falsely stated that its paint-stripping product \"[w]ill not damage original oil finishes\" (misrepresentation). The collector read the brochure before purchasing the product to use on his antique cabinet (justifiable reliance). And since the destruction of the cabinet's original finish would not have occurred had the statement in the brochure been true (causation), the collector will likely prevail. (Choice A) Products liability based on misrepresentation applies to any type of physical harm (ie, personal injury or property damage), so the fact that this product was not dangerous to persons would not absolve the manufacturer of liability. (Choice B) A commercial seller is strictly liable for harm caused by its misrepresentations about any of its products—not just defective ones. (Choice D) Products liability can be based on a commercial seller's misrepresentation about a product even if no other product can satisfy that representation.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, if the loss would not have occurred had the statement in the brochure been true."},"questionZh":"在一次乡村拍卖会上，一位收藏家购得一个古董柜，他认定这是一个极其罕见且极具价值的藏品。\n\n遗憾的是，该柜体原有的饰面之上覆盖了数层清漆和油漆。\n\n只有在不损坏原有饰面的前提下将其去除，才能实现其潜在价值。\n\n该柜的大部分价值取决于其独特的油性饰面状况，而这种饰面的制作秘方已随发明者的离世而失传。\n\n一位古董家具修复专家建议该收藏家使用某种特定的脱漆产品来去除柜体上的油漆和清漆。\n\n收藏家获取并阅读了该脱漆产品制造商发布的宣传册。\n\n宣传册上写道：“本产品能让所有古董家具焕然一新，且不会损坏原有的油性饰面。”收藏家购买了该脱漆产品并将其用于柜体，且严格遵循了随附的使用说明。\n\n尽管收藏家操作谨慎，柜体的原有饰面仍遭到了不可修复的损坏。\n\n最终重新修整后，该柜的价值不足其原有饰面得以保留时价值的20%。\n\n如果收藏家起诉脱漆产品制造商，要求赔偿因柜体原有饰面损毁而遭受的损失，他胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，如果该产品对人身没有危险的话。","B":"不大，除非该产品在制造商销售时存在缺陷。","C":"大，如果宣传册上的陈述属实就不会发生这种损失的话。","D":"大，除非没有其他已知的去除技术能够保留柜体的原有饰面。"},"explanationZh":"产品责任索赔 索赔类型 | 必备要件 **严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）** | 商业销售商（即制造商、分销商、零售商）生产或销售了有缺陷的产品；该缺陷导致原告受到损害。 **过失** | 销售商（商业或非商业）在生产或检查有缺陷产品时未尽到合理注意义务；该产品导致原告受到损害。 **故意侵权** | 销售商（商业或非商业）蓄意或明知地生产或销售有缺陷的产品；该产品导致原告受到损害。 **虚假陈述*** | 商业销售商对产品作出了关于重大事实的虚假陈述；消费者在购买产品时合理信赖了该虚假陈述；该产品导致原告受到损害。 **保证** | **适销性（默示）**：商业销售商保证产品通常可被接受，且合理适用于其通常用途。**特定用途适用性（默示）**：销售商（商业或非商业）保证产品适用于特定用途；买方在购买产品时信赖了销售商的判断或技能。**明示保证**：销售商（商业或非商业）作出了构成交易基础的事实确认或承诺。*商业销售商对其产品作出的虚假陈述承担严格责任——无论产品本身是否存在缺陷。\n\n商业销售商（即制造商、分销商、零售商）因有缺陷产品造成的损害而承担严格责任。\n\n此外，商业销售商还可能对其产品的虚假陈述承担严格责任——无论该产品是否存在缺陷。\n\n若要基于虚假陈述获得赔偿，原告必须证明：商业销售商对产品作出了关于重大事实的虚假陈述（即对普通买方而言重要的事实）； 原告在购买产品时合理信赖了该虚假陈述；以及 该产品导致原告遭受了人身或财产上的实际损害（即如果销售商的陈述属实，该损害本不会发生）。\n\n在本案中，制造商的宣传册虚假声称其脱漆产品“不会损坏原始油性饰面”（虚假陈述）。\n\n收藏者在购买该产品用于其古董柜之前阅读了宣传册（合理信赖）。\n\n由于如果宣传册中的陈述属实，柜子原始饰面的损坏本不会发生（因果关系），因此该收藏者很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）基于虚假陈述的产品责任适用于任何类型的实际损害（即人身伤害或财产损失），因此，该产品对人体无害这一事实并不能免除制造商的责任。\n\n（选项 B）对于因产品（不仅限于有缺陷的产品）相关虚假陈述而造成的损害，商业销售者须承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）即使没有任何其他产品能够符合该虚假陈述所描述的特性，商业销售者仍可能因其关于产品的虚假陈述而承担产品责任。\n\n教育目标：基于虚假陈述的产品责任产生于以下情形：(1) 商业销售者就产品作出了关于重要事实的虚假陈述；(2) 原告合理地信赖了该虚假陈述；以及 (3) 该产品对原告造成了人身伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 402B 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明商业销售者须对其产品相关虚假陈述所致的人身伤害承担严格责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于虚假陈述的产品责任产生于以下情况：(1) 商业销售者对产品作出了关于重要事实的虚假陈述；(2) 原告合理地信赖了该虚假陈述；以及 (3) 该产品对原告造成了人身伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果收藏家起诉脱漆产品制造商，要求赔偿因柜体原有饰面损毁而遭受的损失，他胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"基于虚假陈述的产品责任产生于以下情况：(1) 商业销售者对产品作出了关于重要事实的虚假陈述；(2) 原告合理地信赖了该虚假陈述；以及 (3) 该产品对原告造成了人身伤害。","analysis":"产品责任索赔 索赔类型 | 必备要件 **严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）** | 商业销售商（即制造商、分销商、零售商）生产或销售了有缺陷的产品；该缺陷导致原告受到损害。 **过失** | 销售商（商业或非商业）在生产或检查有缺陷产品时未尽到合理注意义务；该产品导致原告受到损害。 **故意侵权** | 销售商（商业或非商业）蓄意或明知地生产或销售有缺陷的产品；该产品导致原告受到损害。 **虚假陈述*** | 商业销售商对产品作出了关于重大事实的虚假陈述；消费者在购买产品时合理信赖了该虚假陈述；该产品导致原告受到损害。 **保证** | **适销性（默示）**：商业销售商保证产品通常可被接受，且合理适用于其通常用途。**特定用途适用性（默示）**：销售商（商业或非商业）保证产品适用于特定用途；买方在购买产品时信赖了销售商的判断或技能。**明示保证**：销售商（商业或非商业）作出了构成交易基础的事实确认或承诺。*商业销售商对其产品作出的虚假陈述承担严格责任——无论产品本身是否存在缺陷。\n\n商业销售商（即制造商、分销商、零售商）因有缺陷产品造成的损害而承担严格责任。\n\n此外，商业销售商还可能对其产品的虚假陈述承担严格责任——无论该产品是否存在缺陷。\n\n若要基于虚假陈述获得赔偿，原告必须证明：商业销售商对产品作出了关于重大事实的虚假陈述（即对普通买方而言重要的事实）； 原告在购买产品时合理信赖了该虚假陈述；以及 该产品导致原告遭受了人身或财产上的实际损害（即如果销售商的陈述属实，该损害本不会发生）。\n\n在本案中，制造商的宣传册虚假声称其脱漆产品“不会损坏原始油性饰面”（虚假陈述）。\n\n收藏者在购买该产品用于其古董柜之前阅读了宣传册（合理信赖）。\n\n由于如果宣传册中的陈述属实，柜子原始饰面的损坏本不会发生（因果关系），因此该收藏者很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）基于虚假陈述的产品责任适用于任何类型的实际损害（即人身伤害或财产损失），因此，该产品对人体无害这一事实并不能免除制造商的责任。\n\n（选项 B）对于因产品（不仅限于有缺陷的产品）相关虚假陈述而造成的损害，商业销售者须承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）即使没有任何其他产品能够符合该虚假陈述所描述的特性，商业销售者仍可能因其关于产品的虚假陈述而承担产品责任。\n\n教育目标：基于虚假陈述的产品责任产生于以下情形：(1) 商业销售者就产品作出了关于重要事实的虚假陈述；(2) 原告合理地信赖了该虚假陈述；以及 (3) 该产品对原告造成了人身伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 402B 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（阐明商业销售者须对其产品相关虚假陈述所致的人身伤害承担严格责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：大，如果宣传册上的陈述属实就不会发生这种损失的话。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":167,"sourceQuestionPage":500,"sourceQuestionPages":[500],"sourceExplanationPages":[501,502],"sourceAnswerPages":[501,502],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-168","subject":"Torts","topic":"Economic & Dignitary Torts","question":"A woman was touring a house that was \"for sale by owner.\" She noticed water stains on the living-room ceiling and asked the seller about them. The seller responded that the roof did not leak even though he knew otherwise. The woman entered into a contract with the seller to purchase the house. The contract had a provision that conditioned the woman's obligation to purchase the house on the receipt of a favorable inspection report by an inspector of her choosing. The inspection report prepared by the woman's inspector indicated that the roof substantially leaked. However, the woman really wanted the home and purchased it in reliance on the seller's statement regarding the roof's condition. One month later, during the first heavy rain after the purchase, the roof leaked and caused extensive damage to the house. If the woman sues the seller for fraud, who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The seller, because the woman did not justifiably rely on the seller's statement that the roof did not leak.","B":"The seller, because the woman had a contractual obligation to perform an inspection of the house.","C":"The woman, because the roof's condition was a material fact in her decision to purchase the home.","D":"The woman, because the seller told her that the roof did not leak even though he knew otherwise."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Misrepresentation\n\n(fraudulent v. negligent)\n\nConduct Setting Harm\n\nFraudulent Knowingly or recklessly Any setting Justifiable misrepresented material fact to reliance induce reliance caused\n\npecuniary loss Negligent Negligently provided plaintiff Commercial setting or Justifiable false information setting with risk of reliance physical harm caused\n\npecuniary loss or physical harm A prima facie case of fraud (also called \"intentional misrepresentation\" or \"deceit\") is established by proof of the following elements: The defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresented a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance.\n\nThe plaintiff justifiably relied on the misrepresentation and suffered pecuniary (ie, financial) loss as a result.\n\nJustifiable reliance does not require the plaintiff to investigate the truth or falsity of the defendant's statement. But reliance is not justifiable if the statement is obviously false OR the defendant is stating a lay opinion. Here, the seller knowingly misrepresented a material fact when he told the woman that the roof did not leak. The seller presumably intended to induce the woman to purchase the home, and the woman did purchase it in reliance on the seller's statement. But that statement was obviously false in light of the water stains and the report by the woman's own inspector that the roof substantially leaked. Therefore, the woman's reliance was not justifiable and the seller will likely prevail (Choices C & D). (Choice B) The contract did not obligate the woman to have the house inspected. Instead, it gave her the right to obtain a favorable inspection report before concluding the sale. The woman received an unfavorable report yet chose to rely (unjustifiably) on the seller's statement instead. For this reason, the seller will prevail in the fraud suit.\n\nEducational objective: Fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation or deceit) requires proof that the plaintiff's reliance on the defendant's misrepresentation was justifiable. Reliance was not justifiable if the representation was obviously false or the defendant was stating a lay opinion.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 525 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (fraudulent misrepresentation).","educationalObjective":"Fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation or deceit) requires proof that the plaintiff's reliance on the defendant's misrepresentation was justifiable. Reliance was not justifiable if the representation was obviously false or the defendant was stating a lay opinion.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 525 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (fraudulent misrepresentation).","rule":"Fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation or deceit) requires proof that the plaintiff's reliance on the defendant's misrepresentation was justifiable. Reliance was not justifiable if the representation was obviously false or the defendant was stating a lay opinion.","irac":{"issue":"If the woman sues the seller for fraud, who is likely to prevail?","rule":"Fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation or deceit) requires proof that the plaintiff's reliance on the defendant's misrepresentation was justifiable. Reliance was not justifiable if the representation was obviously false or the defendant was stating a lay opinion.","analysis":"Misrepresentation\n\n(fraudulent v. negligent)\n\nConduct Setting Harm\n\nFraudulent Knowingly or recklessly Any setting Justifiable misrepresented material fact to reliance induce reliance caused\n\npecuniary loss Negligent Negligently provided plaintiff Commercial setting or Justifiable false information setting with risk of reliance physical harm caused\n\npecuniary loss or physical harm A prima facie case of fraud (also called \"intentional misrepresentation\" or \"deceit\") is established by proof of the following elements: The defendant knowingly or recklessly misrepresented a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance.\n\nThe plaintiff justifiably relied on the misrepresentation and suffered pecuniary (ie, financial) loss as a result.\n\nJustifiable reliance does not require the plaintiff to investigate the truth or falsity of the defendant's statement. But reliance is not justifiable if the statement is obviously false OR the defendant is stating a lay opinion. Here, the seller knowingly misrepresented a material fact when he told the woman that the roof did not leak. The seller presumably intended to induce the woman to purchase the home, and the woman did purchase it in reliance on the seller's statement. But that statement was obviously false in light of the water stains and the report by the woman's own inspector that the roof substantially leaked. Therefore, the woman's reliance was not justifiable and the seller will likely prevail (Choices C & D). (Choice B) The contract did not obligate the woman to have the house inspected. Instead, it gave her the right to obtain a favorable inspection report before concluding the sale. The woman received an unfavorable report yet chose to rely (unjustifiably) on the seller's statement instead. For this reason, the seller will prevail in the fraud suit.","conclusion":"Answer A: The seller, because the woman did not justifiably rely on the seller's statement that the roof did not leak."},"questionZh":"一位女士正在参观一处“由业主直接出售”的房产。\n\n她注意到客厅天花板上有水渍，便向卖方询问情况。\n\n尽管卖方明知屋顶漏水，却声称屋顶不漏水。\n\n该女士与卖方签订了购房合同，合同中规定，她是否有义务购买该房产，取决于她指定的检查员是否出具一份满意的检查报告。\n\n该女士指定的检查员出具的报告显示，屋顶存在严重漏水问题。\n\n然而，由于非常中意这所房子，该女士在听信卖方关于屋顶状况的陈述后，还是买下了它。\n\n一个月后，购房后的首场大雨导致屋顶漏水，并对房屋造成了严重损坏。\n\n如果该女士因欺诈起诉卖方，谁更有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"卖方胜诉，因为该女士对卖方关于屋顶不漏水的陈述所进行的信赖是不合理的。","B":"卖方胜诉，因为该女士有合同义务对房屋进行检查。","C":"女士胜诉，因为屋顶状况是她决定购买该房屋时的一个重要事实。","D":"女士胜诉，因为卖方在明知屋顶漏水的情况下，却告诉她屋顶不漏水。"},"explanationZh":"虚假陈述 （欺诈性与过失性） 行为 | 适用情境 | 导致后果 | 关键要素 欺诈性 | 明知或鲁莽地 | 任何情境 | 导致金钱损失 | 虚假陈述了实质性事实，意图诱导（并实际导致）原告产生合理的信赖 过失性 | 过失地提供虚假信息 | 商业情境或存在信赖风险的情境 | 导致金钱损失或人身伤害 | 过失提供虚假信息，导致原告产生合理的信赖 欺诈（也称为“故意虚假陈述”或“欺骗”）的初步案情（prima facie case）需通过证明以下要素来确立：被告明知或鲁莽地虚假陈述了实质性事实，意图诱导原告产生信赖。\n\n原告合理地信赖了该虚假陈述，并因此遭受了金钱（即财务）损失。 “合理信赖”并不要求原告必须去核实被告陈述的真伪。\n\n但如果陈述明显虚假，或者被告表达的仅是外行意见，则信赖不被视为合理。\n\n在本案中，卖方在告诉该女子屋顶不漏水时，明知这是对实质性事实的虚假陈述。\n\n卖方显然意图诱导该女子购买房屋，而该女子也确实基于卖方的陈述购买了房屋。\n\n然而，鉴于存在水渍以及该女子聘请的检查员出具的屋顶严重漏水的报告，该陈述显然是虚假的。\n\n因此，该女子的信赖不属于合理信赖，卖方很可能会胜诉（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n（选项 B）合同并未强制要求该女子对房屋进行检查，而是赋予了她在完成交易前获取有利检查报告的权利。\n\n该女子收到了不利的报告，却选择（不合理地）信赖卖方的陈述。\n\n正因如此，卖方将在欺诈诉讼中胜诉。\n\n教育目标：欺诈（即故意虚假陈述或欺骗）要求证明原告对被告虚假陈述的信赖是合理的。\n\n如果陈述明显虚假或被告表达的是外行意见，则信赖不被视为合理。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 525 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（欺诈性虚假陈述）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"欺诈（即故意虚假陈述或欺骗）要求证明原告对被告虚假陈述的信赖是合理的。\n\n如果陈述明显虚假，或者被告表达的仅是非专业人士的意见，那么这种信赖便不被视为合理。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该女士因欺诈起诉卖方，谁更有可能胜诉？","rule":"欺诈（即故意虚假陈述或欺骗）要求证明原告对被告虚假陈述的信赖是合理的。\n\n如果陈述明显虚假，或者被告表达的仅是非专业人士的意见，那么这种信赖便不被视为合理。","analysis":"虚假陈述 （欺诈性与过失性） 行为 | 适用情境 | 导致后果 | 关键要素 欺诈性 | 明知或鲁莽地 | 任何情境 | 导致金钱损失 | 虚假陈述了实质性事实，意图诱导（并实际导致）原告产生合理的信赖 过失性 | 过失地提供虚假信息 | 商业情境或存在信赖风险的情境 | 导致金钱损失或人身伤害 | 过失提供虚假信息，导致原告产生合理的信赖 欺诈（也称为“故意虚假陈述”或“欺骗”）的初步案情（prima facie case）需通过证明以下要素来确立：被告明知或鲁莽地虚假陈述了实质性事实，意图诱导原告产生信赖。\n\n原告合理地信赖了该虚假陈述，并因此遭受了金钱（即财务）损失。 “合理信赖”并不要求原告必须去核实被告陈述的真伪。\n\n但如果陈述明显虚假，或者被告表达的仅是外行意见，则信赖不被视为合理。\n\n在本案中，卖方在告诉该女子屋顶不漏水时，明知这是对实质性事实的虚假陈述。\n\n卖方显然意图诱导该女子购买房屋，而该女子也确实基于卖方的陈述购买了房屋。\n\n然而，鉴于存在水渍以及该女子聘请的检查员出具的屋顶严重漏水的报告，该陈述显然是虚假的。\n\n因此，该女子的信赖不属于合理信赖，卖方很可能会胜诉（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n（选项 B）合同并未强制要求该女子对房屋进行检查，而是赋予了她在完成交易前获取有利检查报告的权利。\n\n该女子收到了不利的报告，却选择（不合理地）信赖卖方的陈述。\n\n正因如此，卖方将在欺诈诉讼中胜诉。\n\n教育目标：欺诈（即故意虚假陈述或欺骗）要求证明原告对被告虚假陈述的信赖是合理的。\n\n如果陈述明显虚假或被告表达的是外行意见，则信赖不被视为合理。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 525 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（欺诈性虚假陈述）。","conclusion":"答案 A：卖方胜诉，因为该女士对卖方关于屋顶不漏水的陈述所进行的信赖是不合理的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":168,"sourceQuestionPage":503,"sourceQuestionPages":[503],"sourceExplanationPages":[504,505],"sourceAnswerPages":[504,505],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-169","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A man and a woman were law school classmates who had competed for the position of editor of the law review. The man had the higher grade point average, but the woman was elected editor, largely in recognition of a long and important note that had appeared in the review with the woman credited as the author. During the following placement interview season, the man was interviewed by a representative of a nationally prominent law firm. In response to the interviewer's request for information about the authorship of the law review note, the man said that he believed that the note attributed to the woman was largely the work of another student. The firm told the woman that it would not interview her because of doubts about the authorship of the note. This greatly distressed the woman. In fact, the note had been prepared by the woman without assistance from anyone else. If the woman asserts a claim against the man based on defamation, will she recover?","choices":{"A":"No, because the statement was made by the man only after the interviewer inquired about the authorship of the note.","B":"No, unless the woman proves pecuniary loss.","C":"Yes, because the man's statement was false.","D":"Yes, if the man had substantial doubts about the accuracy of the information he gave the interviewer."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Defamation (libel or slander) occurs when a defendant communicates (ie, publishes) a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party and harms the plaintiff's reputation. But the defendant is not liable for defamation if that communication is privileged. One such privilege arises when the defendant reasonably believes that: the statement affects an important interest of the third party and\n\ncommunicating that statement to the third party is socially acceptable—eg, the statement is made in response to a request rather than volunteered.\n\nHowever, this is a conditional privilege that is lost when abused. Abuse occurs when the defendant knows that the statement is false or recklessly disregards that possibility (ie, has substantial doubts about the statement's accuracy). Here, the man falsely told the interviewer that the woman's law review note was largely the work of another student (defamation). This communication was privileged since (1) the man could reasonably believe that his statement would affect the interviewer's hiring decision (important interest) and (2) the statement was made in response to the interviewer's inquiry about the note (socially acceptable) (Choice A). But if the man had substantial doubts about the accuracy of his statement, then the privilege will be lost and the woman will recover for defamation. (Choice B) Slander (spoken defamation) ordinarily requires proof of special harm (pecuniary loss). But such proof is not required when the defendant's statement constitutes slander per se—eg, a statement accusing the plaintiff of conduct that adversely affects his/her occupation. Here, since an accusation of plagiarism would harm a law student's job prospects (slander per se), the woman can recover without proving pecuniary loss. (Choice C) Since defamation requires proof of falsity, the fact that the man's statement was false supports the woman's claim. But since the man's statement was privileged, the woman will only recover if this privilege was abused—ie, the man knew or recklessly disregarded the possibility that his statement was false.\n\nEducational objective: A defamatory statement communicated to a third party is privileged when the defendant reasonably believes that (1) the statement affects an important interest of the third party and (2) the communication is socially acceptable. This privilege is lost if the defendant knows that the statement is false or recklessly disregards that possibility.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 595 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (statements made to protect third party's interest).","educationalObjective":"A defamatory statement communicated to a third party is privileged when the defendant reasonably believes that (1) the statement affects an important interest of the third party and (2) the communication is socially acceptable. This privilege is lost if the defendant knows that the statement is false or recklessly disregards that possibility.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 595 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (statements made to protect third party's interest).","rule":"A defamatory statement communicated to a third party is privileged when the defendant reasonably believes that (1) the statement affects an important interest of the third party and (2) the communication is socially acceptable. This privilege is lost if the defendant knows that the statement is false or recklessly disregards that possibility.","irac":{"issue":"If the woman asserts a claim against the man based on defamation, will she recover?","rule":"A defamatory statement communicated to a third party is privileged when the defendant reasonably believes that (1) the statement affects an important interest of the third party and (2) the communication is socially acceptable. This privilege is lost if the defendant knows that the statement is false or recklessly disregards that possibility.","analysis":"Defamation (libel or slander) occurs when a defendant communicates (ie, publishes) a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party and harms the plaintiff's reputation. But the defendant is not liable for defamation if that communication is privileged. One such privilege arises when the defendant reasonably believes that: the statement affects an important interest of the third party and\n\ncommunicating that statement to the third party is socially acceptable—eg, the statement is made in response to a request rather than volunteered.\n\nHowever, this is a conditional privilege that is lost when abused. Abuse occurs when the defendant knows that the statement is false or recklessly disregards that possibility (ie, has substantial doubts about the statement's accuracy). Here, the man falsely told the interviewer that the woman's law review note was largely the work of another student (defamation). This communication was privileged since (1) the man could reasonably believe that his statement would affect the interviewer's hiring decision (important interest) and (2) the statement was made in response to the interviewer's inquiry about the note (socially acceptable) (Choice A). But if the man had substantial doubts about the accuracy of his statement, then the privilege will be lost and the woman will recover for defamation. (Choice B) Slander (spoken defamation) ordinarily requires proof of special harm (pecuniary loss). But such proof is not required when the defendant's statement constitutes slander per se—eg, a statement accusing the plaintiff of conduct that adversely affects his/her occupation. Here, since an accusation of plagiarism would harm a law student's job prospects (slander per se), the woman can recover without proving pecuniary loss. (Choice C) Since defamation requires proof of falsity, the fact that the man's statement was false supports the woman's claim. But since the man's statement was privileged, the woman will only recover if this privilege was abused—ie, the man knew or recklessly disregarded the possibility that his statement was false.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, if the man had substantial doubts about the accuracy of the information he gave the interviewer."},"questionZh":"一男一女是法学院同学，曾竞争法学评论（law review）编辑一职。\n\n男方的平均绩点（GPA）较高，但女方最终当选为编辑，这在很大程度上归功于该评论刊登的一篇篇幅较长且重要的文章（note），而署名作者正是该女子。\n\n在随后的求职面试季，该男子接受了一家全国知名律师事务所代表的面试。\n\n当面试官询问有关该法学评论文章的作者身份信息时，该男子表示，他认为那篇署名为该女子的文章实际上主要出自另一位学生之手。\n\n该律所告知该女子，由于对其文章的作者身份存疑，律所将不予面试。\n\n这令该女子深感苦恼。\n\n事实上，该文章完全由该女子独立撰写，未获他人协助。\n\n如果该女子基于诽谤向该男子提出索赔，她能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为该男子的陈述是在面试官询问文章作者身份后才作出的。","B":"不能，除非该女子能证明存在金钱损失。","C":"能，因为该男子的陈述是不实的。","D":"能，如果该男子对其提供给面试官的信息的准确性存有重大怀疑。"},"explanationZh":"当被告向第三方传达（即发布）关于原告的虚假陈述并损害原告名誉时，即构成诽谤（书面诽谤或口头诽谤）。\n\n但如果该传达行为享有特权（免责权），被告则无需承担诽谤责任。\n\n其中一种特权产生于被告有合理理由相信：该陈述涉及第三方的重大利益，且 向第三方传达该陈述在社会观念上是可接受的——例如，该陈述是应要求作出的，而非主动提出的。\n\n然而，这是一种有条件的特权，一旦被滥用即告丧失。\n\n滥用特权的情形包括：被告明知陈述虚假，或罔顾其虚假的可能性（即对陈述的准确性存有重大怀疑）。\n\n在本案中，该男子向面试官谎称该女子的法学评论文章（law review note）主要由另一名学生完成（构成诽谤）。\n\n该传达行为享有特权，因为：(1) 该男子有合理理由相信其陈述会影响面试官的录用决定（涉及重大利益）；(2) 该陈述是应面试官关于该文章的询问而作出的（社会观念上可接受）（选项 A）。\n\n但如果该男子对其陈述的准确性存有重大怀疑，则特权丧失，该女子可就诽谤获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）口头诽谤（slander）通常需要证明存在“特殊损害”（即金钱损失）。\n\n但在被告的陈述构成“本身即构成诽谤”（slander per se）的情形下，则无需此类证明——例如，指控原告有对其职业产生不利影响的行为。\n\n在本案中，由于指控抄袭会损害法学院学生的就业前景（属于本身即构成诽谤），该女子无需证明金钱损失即可获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）由于诽谤诉讼要求证明陈述虚假，该男子陈述虚假这一事实支持了该女子的主张。\n\n但鉴于该男子的陈述享有特权，该女子仅在特权被滥用的情况下才能获得赔偿——即该男子明知其陈述虚假，或罔顾其陈述可能虚假的事实。\n\n教育目标：当被告有合理理由相信 (1) 陈述涉及第三方的重大利益，且 (2) 传达该陈述在社会观念上是可接受的时，向第三方作出的诽谤性陈述即享有特权。\n\n如果被告明知该陈述为虚假，或罔顾其可能为虚假的事实，则该特权即告丧失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第558条（美国法律协会，1977年）（诽谤的构成要素）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第595条（美国法律协会，1977年）（为保护第三方利益而作出的陈述）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当被告合理地认为 (1) 该陈述涉及第三方的重大利益，且 (2) 这种信息传达在社会上是可接受的时，向第三方传达的诽谤性陈述享有特权（免责权）。\n\n如果被告明知该陈述不实或鲁莽地忽视了这种可能性，则该特权丧失。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该女子基于诽谤向该男子提出索赔，她能胜诉吗？","rule":"当被告合理地认为 (1) 该陈述涉及第三方的重大利益，且 (2) 这种信息传达在社会上是可接受的时，向第三方传达的诽谤性陈述享有特权（免责权）。\n\n如果被告明知该陈述不实或鲁莽地忽视了这种可能性，则该特权丧失。","analysis":"当被告向第三方传达（即发布）关于原告的虚假陈述并损害原告名誉时，即构成诽谤（书面诽谤或口头诽谤）。\n\n但如果该传达行为享有特权（免责权），被告则无需承担诽谤责任。\n\n其中一种特权产生于被告有合理理由相信：该陈述涉及第三方的重大利益，且 向第三方传达该陈述在社会观念上是可接受的——例如，该陈述是应要求作出的，而非主动提出的。\n\n然而，这是一种有条件的特权，一旦被滥用即告丧失。\n\n滥用特权的情形包括：被告明知陈述虚假，或罔顾其虚假的可能性（即对陈述的准确性存有重大怀疑）。\n\n在本案中，该男子向面试官谎称该女子的法学评论文章（law review note）主要由另一名学生完成（构成诽谤）。\n\n该传达行为享有特权，因为：(1) 该男子有合理理由相信其陈述会影响面试官的录用决定（涉及重大利益）；(2) 该陈述是应面试官关于该文章的询问而作出的（社会观念上可接受）（选项 A）。\n\n但如果该男子对其陈述的准确性存有重大怀疑，则特权丧失，该女子可就诽谤获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）口头诽谤（slander）通常需要证明存在“特殊损害”（即金钱损失）。\n\n但在被告的陈述构成“本身即构成诽谤”（slander per se）的情形下，则无需此类证明——例如，指控原告有对其职业产生不利影响的行为。\n\n在本案中，由于指控抄袭会损害法学院学生的就业前景（属于本身即构成诽谤），该女子无需证明金钱损失即可获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 C）由于诽谤诉讼要求证明陈述虚假，该男子陈述虚假这一事实支持了该女子的主张。\n\n但鉴于该男子的陈述享有特权，该女子仅在特权被滥用的情况下才能获得赔偿——即该男子明知其陈述虚假，或罔顾其陈述可能虚假的事实。\n\n教育目标：当被告有合理理由相信 (1) 陈述涉及第三方的重大利益，且 (2) 传达该陈述在社会观念上是可接受的时，向第三方作出的诽谤性陈述即享有特权。\n\n如果被告明知该陈述为虚假，或罔顾其可能为虚假的事实，则该特权即告丧失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第558条（美国法律协会，1977年）（诽谤的构成要素）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第595条（美国法律协会，1977年）（为保护第三方利益而作出的陈述）。","conclusion":"答案 D：能，如果该男子对其提供给面试官的信息的准确性存有重大怀疑。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-211de4a94a78dde9"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":169,"sourceQuestionPage":506,"sourceQuestionPages":[506],"sourceExplanationPages":[507,508],"sourceAnswerPages":[507,508],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-170","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A newspaper printed an article that stated: \"The popular restaurant on the town square has closed its doors. The restaurant's employees have said that the closing resulted from the owner's belief that the restaurant's general manager has embezzled thousands of dollars from the restaurant over the last several years. A decision on reopening the restaurant will be made after the completion of an audit of the restaurant's books.\" The restaurant's general manager brought a libel action against the newspaper based on the publication of this article. The parties stipulated that the general manager never embezzled any funds from the restaurant. The case went to trial before a jury. The newspaper filed a motion for a directed verdict at the close of the evidence. Why should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"The evidence is not clear and convincing that the newspaper published the article with \"actual malice.\"","B":"The record contains no evidence that the general manager suffered special harm as a result of the publication.","C":"The record contains no evidence that the newspaper was negligent as to the truth or falsity of the charge of embezzlement.","D":"The record contains uncontradicted evidence that the article accurately reported what the employees told the newspaper."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Libel is defamation that appears in written or other physical form. To prevail in a suit for libel, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff OR negligently failed to determine its falsity (ie, failed to use reasonable care).\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party.\n\nIf the plaintiff fails to present legally sufficient evidence to support all of these elements at trial (ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor), the court should grant the defendant's motion for a directed verdict. Here, the newspaper falsely reported that the general manager had embezzled thousands of dollars in a published article. But there is no evidence that the newspaper knew or negligently failed to determine that the embezzlement charge was false since it used reasonable care by consulting with former restaurant employees about that charge. As a result, the court should grant the newspaper's motion for a directed verdict. (Choice A) A libel claim brought by a public figure or official (eg, celebrity, politician) requires clear and convincing evidence that the defendant made a statement with actual malice—knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity. But a claim brought by a private person (eg, the general manager) only requires proof by a preponderance of the evidence that the defendant knew or negligently failed to determine the statement's truth. (Choice B) Libel (written defamation) does not require proof of special harm (ie, monetary loss), so the fact that the general manager suffered no special harm does not defeat his libel claim. In contrast, special harm must be proven for slander (spoken defamation). (Choice D) A defamation claim can be brought against any person who communicates a false statement to a third party (ie, a person who repeats a defamatory statement is just as liable as the original defamer). Therefore, the newspaper could be liable for defamation even if its article merely reported the employees' comments.\n\nEducational objective: Libel requires proof that the defendant (1) knowingly communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party or (2) negligently failed to determine the statement's falsity (ie, no reasonable care used to determine its truth).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 580B (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (knowing or negligent statement of falsity).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (judgment as a matter of law).","educationalObjective":"Libel requires proof that the defendant (1) knowingly communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party or (2) negligently failed to determine the statement's falsity (ie, no reasonable care used to determine its truth).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 580B (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (knowing or negligent statement of falsity).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (judgment as a matter of law).","rule":"Libel requires proof that the defendant (1) knowingly communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party or (2) negligently failed to determine the statement's falsity (ie, no reasonable care used to determine its truth).","irac":{"issue":"Why should the court grant the motion?","rule":"Libel requires proof that the defendant (1) knowingly communicated a false statement about the plaintiff to a third party or (2) negligently failed to determine the statement's falsity (ie, no reasonable care used to determine its truth).","analysis":"Libel is defamation that appears in written or other physical form. To prevail in a suit for libel, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff OR negligently failed to determine its falsity (ie, failed to use reasonable care).\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party.\n\nIf the plaintiff fails to present legally sufficient evidence to support all of these elements at trial (ie, no reasonable jury could find in the plaintiff's favor), the court should grant the defendant's motion for a directed verdict. Here, the newspaper falsely reported that the general manager had embezzled thousands of dollars in a published article. But there is no evidence that the newspaper knew or negligently failed to determine that the embezzlement charge was false since it used reasonable care by consulting with former restaurant employees about that charge. As a result, the court should grant the newspaper's motion for a directed verdict. (Choice A) A libel claim brought by a public figure or official (eg, celebrity, politician) requires clear and convincing evidence that the defendant made a statement with actual malice—knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity. But a claim brought by a private person (eg, the general manager) only requires proof by a preponderance of the evidence that the defendant knew or negligently failed to determine the statement's truth. (Choice B) Libel (written defamation) does not require proof of special harm (ie, monetary loss), so the fact that the general manager suffered no special harm does not defeat his libel claim. In contrast, special harm must be proven for slander (spoken defamation). (Choice D) A defamation claim can be brought against any person who communicates a false statement to a third party (ie, a person who repeats a defamatory statement is just as liable as the original defamer). Therefore, the newspaper could be liable for defamation even if its article merely reported the employees' comments.","conclusion":"Answer C: The record contains no evidence that the newspaper was negligent as to the truth or falsity of the charge of embezzlement."},"questionZh":"某报纸刊登了一篇报道，称：“位于镇广场的那家热门餐厅已停业。据餐厅员工所述，停业原因是店主认为该餐厅总经理在过去几年中挪用了数千美元公款。关于是否重新开业，将待餐厅账目审计完成后再作决定。”该餐厅总经理因这篇报道对报纸提起了诽谤诉讼。\n\n双方当事人均确认，该总经理从未挪用过餐厅的任何资金。\n\n案件进入陪审团审理阶段。\n\n在举证环节结束后，报纸方面提出了指示裁决（directed verdict）的动议。\n\n法院为何应当批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有清晰且令人信服的证据表明报纸在发表该文章时存在“实际恶意”（actual malice）。","B":"案卷记录中没有任何证据表明该总经理因文章发表而遭受了“特别损害”（special harm）。","C":"案卷记录中没有任何证据表明报纸在核实挪用公款指控的真伪方面存在过失。","D":"案卷记录中存在未受反驳的证据，表明该文章准确报道了员工向报纸陈述的内容。"},"explanationZh":"书面诽谤（libel）是指以书面或其他有形形式呈现的诽谤。\n\n原告若要在书面诽谤诉讼中胜诉，必须证明以下所有要件：被告明知故犯地就原告发表了虚假陈述，或者因疏忽而未能核实其虚假性（即未尽到合理注意义务）。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或因疏忽将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n如果原告在庭审中未能提供足以支持所有这些要件的法律证据（即没有理性的陪审团会做出有利于原告的裁决），法院应批准被告提出的指示裁决（directed verdict）动议。\n\n在本案中，该报纸在一篇已发表的文章中虚假报道称总经理挪用了数千美元公款。\n\n然而，没有证据表明该报纸明知或因疏忽未核实该挪用公款指控的虚假性，因为该报纸已尽到合理注意义务，就该指控咨询了前餐厅员工。\n\n因此，法院应批准该报纸提出的指示裁决动议。\n\n（选项 A）由公众人物或公职人员（如名人、政治家）提起的书面诽谤诉讼，要求提供清晰且令人信服的证据，证明被告发表陈述时具有“实际恶意”（actual malice）——即明知陈述虚假或罔顾其虚假性。\n\n但由普通私人（如本案中的总经理）提起的诉讼，仅需通过“优势证据”（preponderance of the evidence）证明被告明知或因疏忽未核实陈述的真实性即可。\n\n（选项 B）书面诽谤（以书面形式进行的诽谤）不要求证明“特殊损害”（即金钱损失），因此总经理未遭受特殊损害这一事实并不能否定其书面诽谤索赔。\n\n相比之下，口头诽谤（slander）则必须证明存在特殊损害。\n\n（选项 D）针对任何向第三方传达虚假陈述的人均可提起诽谤诉讼（即重复诽谤性陈述的人与最初的诽谤者承担同等责任）。\n\n因此，即使报纸的文章只是报道了员工的言论，该报纸仍可能因诽谤承担责任。\n\n教育目标：书面诽谤要求证明被告 (1) 明知故犯地向第三方传达了关于原告的虚假陈述，或 (2) 因疏忽而未能核实陈述的虚假性（即未尽到合理注意义务来核实其真实性）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第558条（美国法律协会，1977年）（诽谤的构成要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第580B条（美国法律协会，1977年）（明知虚假或过失作出虚假陈述）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第50(a)条（法律问题判决）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"构成诽谤需要证明被告：(1) 明知关于原告的陈述不实而将其传达给第三方；或 (2) 因过失未能查明该陈述的不实之处（即未尽合理注意义务去核实其真实性）。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院为何应当批准该动议？","rule":"构成诽谤需要证明被告：(1) 明知关于原告的陈述不实而将其传达给第三方；或 (2) 因过失未能查明该陈述的不实之处（即未尽合理注意义务去核实其真实性）。","analysis":"书面诽谤（libel）是指以书面或其他有形形式呈现的诽谤。\n\n原告若要在书面诽谤诉讼中胜诉，必须证明以下所有要件：被告明知故犯地就原告发表了虚假陈述，或者因疏忽而未能核实其虚假性（即未尽到合理注意义务）。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或因疏忽将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n如果原告在庭审中未能提供足以支持所有这些要件的法律证据（即没有理性的陪审团会做出有利于原告的裁决），法院应批准被告提出的指示裁决（directed verdict）动议。\n\n在本案中，该报纸在一篇已发表的文章中虚假报道称总经理挪用了数千美元公款。\n\n然而，没有证据表明该报纸明知或因疏忽未核实该挪用公款指控的虚假性，因为该报纸已尽到合理注意义务，就该指控咨询了前餐厅员工。\n\n因此，法院应批准该报纸提出的指示裁决动议。\n\n（选项 A）由公众人物或公职人员（如名人、政治家）提起的书面诽谤诉讼，要求提供清晰且令人信服的证据，证明被告发表陈述时具有“实际恶意”（actual malice）——即明知陈述虚假或罔顾其虚假性。\n\n但由普通私人（如本案中的总经理）提起的诉讼，仅需通过“优势证据”（preponderance of the evidence）证明被告明知或因疏忽未核实陈述的真实性即可。\n\n（选项 B）书面诽谤（以书面形式进行的诽谤）不要求证明“特殊损害”（即金钱损失），因此总经理未遭受特殊损害这一事实并不能否定其书面诽谤索赔。\n\n相比之下，口头诽谤（slander）则必须证明存在特殊损害。\n\n（选项 D）针对任何向第三方传达虚假陈述的人均可提起诽谤诉讼（即重复诽谤性陈述的人与最初的诽谤者承担同等责任）。\n\n因此，即使报纸的文章只是报道了员工的言论，该报纸仍可能因诽谤承担责任。\n\n教育目标：书面诽谤要求证明被告 (1) 明知故犯地向第三方传达了关于原告的虚假陈述，或 (2) 因疏忽而未能核实陈述的虚假性（即未尽到合理注意义务来核实其真实性）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第558条（美国法律协会，1977年）（诽谤的构成要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第580B条（美国法律协会，1977年）（明知虚假或过失作出虚假陈述）。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第50(a)条（法律问题判决）。","conclusion":"答案 C：案卷记录中没有任何证据表明报纸在核实挪用公款指控的真伪方面存在过失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-aedfbe3e1c63d4ed"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":170,"sourceQuestionPage":509,"sourceQuestionPages":[509],"sourceExplanationPages":[510,511],"sourceAnswerPages":[510,511],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-171","subject":"Torts","topic":"Privileges & Defenses","question":"A hotel company owns and operates a beachfront hotel. Under a contract with the city to restore a public beach, a dredging company placed a large and unavoidably dangerous stone-crushing machine on the city's land near the hotel. The machine creates a continuous and intense noise that is so disturbing to the hotel guests that they have canceled their hotel reservations in large numbers, resulting in a substantial loss to the hotel company. The hotel company's best chance to recover damages for its financial losses from the dredging company is under the theory that the operation of the stone-crushing machine constitutes which of the following?","choices":{"A":"A private nuisance.","B":"A trespass.","C":"An abnormally dangerous activity.","D":"Negligence."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Damages for private nuisance\n\nCompensatory Property damage (eg, repair cost, depreciation) Personal injury (eg, sickness, loss of consortium) Emotional distress (requires personal injury in some jurisdictions) Deprivation of use & enjoyment of property (ie, personal discomfort, inconvenience, annoyance) Financial loss (eg, lost business) Punitive Available when interference was intentional, willful, or malicious\n\nLiability for private nuisance arises when the defendant's interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property is both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. Once these elements are met, the plaintiff can recover damages for any type of harm caused by the interference—including financial loss (eg, lost business). Here, the dredging company interfered with the use and enjoyment of the hotel company's property when it placed a stone-crushing machine that created a continuous and intense noise nearby. The interference was likely substantial since it caused many hotel guests to cancel their reservations. And if that interference was also unreasonable—ie, if the hotel company's financial losses outweighed the utility of restoring the public beach—then the company could recover damages for its financial losses under the theory of private nuisance. (Choice B) Trespass requires proof that the defendant intentionally entered the plaintiff's property without his/her consent. Here, since the dredging company's interference was caused by noise (not by an entry onto the hotel company's property), the dredging company is not liable for trespass. (Choice C) A defendant who engages in an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable (ie, liable regardless of fault) for physical harm caused by a hazard that made the activity abnormally dangerous. Here, though the machine's operation was unavoidably (and possibly abnormally) dangerous, the hotel company cannot recover under this theory since it only suffered financial loss—not physical harm (eg, property damage, bodily harm). (Choice D) The economic loss rule eliminates negligence liability for financial harm unaccompanied by physical harm. And since the hotel company is only seeking damages for financial harm—not physical harm such as property damage—it cannot recover under this theory.\n\nEducational objective: Private nuisance—a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property—allows the plaintiff to recover damages for any type of harm, including financial loss.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance liability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (substantial harm requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference requirement).","educationalObjective":"Private nuisance—a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property—allows the plaintiff to recover damages for any type of harm, including financial loss.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance liability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (substantial harm requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference requirement).","rule":"Private nuisance—a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property—allows the plaintiff to recover damages for any type of harm, including financial loss.","irac":{"issue":"The hotel company's best chance to recover damages for its financial losses from the dredging company is under the theory that the operation of the stone-crushing machine constitutes which of the following?","rule":"Private nuisance—a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property—allows the plaintiff to recover damages for any type of harm, including financial loss.","analysis":"Damages for private nuisance\n\nCompensatory Property damage (eg, repair cost, depreciation) Personal injury (eg, sickness, loss of consortium) Emotional distress (requires personal injury in some jurisdictions) Deprivation of use & enjoyment of property (ie, personal discomfort, inconvenience, annoyance) Financial loss (eg, lost business) Punitive Available when interference was intentional, willful, or malicious\n\nLiability for private nuisance arises when the defendant's interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property is both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. Once these elements are met, the plaintiff can recover damages for any type of harm caused by the interference—including financial loss (eg, lost business). Here, the dredging company interfered with the use and enjoyment of the hotel company's property when it placed a stone-crushing machine that created a continuous and intense noise nearby. The interference was likely substantial since it caused many hotel guests to cancel their reservations. And if that interference was also unreasonable—ie, if the hotel company's financial losses outweighed the utility of restoring the public beach—then the company could recover damages for its financial losses under the theory of private nuisance. (Choice B) Trespass requires proof that the defendant intentionally entered the plaintiff's property without his/her consent. Here, since the dredging company's interference was caused by noise (not by an entry onto the hotel company's property), the dredging company is not liable for trespass. (Choice C) A defendant who engages in an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable (ie, liable regardless of fault) for physical harm caused by a hazard that made the activity abnormally dangerous. Here, though the machine's operation was unavoidably (and possibly abnormally) dangerous, the hotel company cannot recover under this theory since it only suffered financial loss—not physical harm (eg, property damage, bodily harm). (Choice D) The economic loss rule eliminates negligence liability for financial harm unaccompanied by physical harm. And since the hotel company is only seeking damages for financial harm—not physical harm such as property damage—it cannot recover under this theory.","conclusion":"Answer A: A private nuisance."},"questionZh":"一家酒店公司拥有并经营着一家海滨酒店。\n\n根据与市政府签订的修复公共海滩的合同，一家疏浚公司在酒店附近属于市政府的土地上放置了一台大型且具有不可避免危险性的碎石机。\n\n该机器产生的持续且强烈的噪音严重干扰了酒店住客，导致大量住客取消预订，从而给酒店公司造成了重大经济损失。\n\n酒店公司若想就其经济损失向疏浚公司索赔，最有利的法律依据是主张该碎石机的操作构成了下列哪种侵权行为？","choicesZh":{"A":"私害（Private nuisance）。","B":"侵入（Trespass）。","C":"异常危险活动（Abnormally dangerous activity）。","D":"过失（Negligence）。"},"explanationZh":"针对私人妨害（private nuisance）的损害赔偿 补偿性赔偿：包括财产损失（如维修费、贬值）、人身伤害（如疾病、丧失伴侣陪伴权）、精神痛苦（某些司法管辖区要求必须伴有人身伤害）、剥夺对财产的使用与享用权（即个人不适、不便、烦扰）以及经济损失（如业务损失）。惩罚性赔偿：当干扰行为属于故意、蓄意或恶意时适用。 当被告对原告财产的使用与享用构成干扰，且该干扰同时满足以下两个条件时，即产生私人妨害的责任：实质性（即对社区中的普通人而言，该干扰令人反感、烦扰或无法忍受）以及不合理性（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的社会效用）。\n\n一旦满足这些要素，原告即可就干扰行为造成的任何类型的损害（包括经济损失，如业务损失）获得赔偿。\n\n在本案中，疏浚公司在附近放置了一台产生持续且强烈噪音的碎石机，从而干扰了酒店公司对其财产的使用与享用。\n\n这种干扰很可能被视为实质性的，因为它导致许多酒店客人取消了预订。\n\n如果该干扰同时被认定为不合理——即酒店公司的经济损失超过了修复公共海滩的社会效用——那么该公司便可依据私人妨害的法理就其经济损失获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）侵入（Trespass）要求证明被告在未经原告同意的情况下故意进入了原告的财产范围。\n\n在本案中，由于疏浚公司的干扰是由噪音引起的（而非通过进入酒店公司的财产范围），因此疏浚公司不承担侵入责任。\n\n（选项 C）从事异常危险活动的被告，若该活动因某种特定危险源而具有异常危险性，则需对该危险源造成的人身或财产损害承担严格责任（即无论有无过错均须承担责任）。\n\n在本案中，尽管机器的运行具有不可避免的（甚至可能是异常的）危险性，但酒店公司无法依据此理论获得赔偿，因为它仅遭受了经济损失，而未遭受实质性的人身或财产损害。\n\n（选项 D）经济损失规则（economic loss rule）排除了针对不伴随人身或财产损害的纯粹经济损失的过失侵权责任。\n\n由于酒店公司仅就经济损失（而非财产损害等人身或财产损害）寻求赔偿，因此无法依据此理论获得赔偿。\n\n教学目标：私人妨害（即对原告财产的使用与享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰）允许原告就任何类型的损害（包括经济损失）获得损害赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第822条（美国法律协会，1979年）（私人妨害责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821F条（美国法律协会，1979年）（实质性损害要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律协会，1979年）（不合理干扰要件）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"私害——即对原告财产的使用和享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰——允许原告就任何类型的损害（包括经济损失）获得赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"酒店公司若想就其经济损失向疏浚公司索赔，最有利的法律依据是主张该碎石机的操作构成了下列哪种侵权行为？","rule":"私害——即对原告财产的使用和享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰——允许原告就任何类型的损害（包括经济损失）获得赔偿。","analysis":"针对私人妨害（private nuisance）的损害赔偿 补偿性赔偿：包括财产损失（如维修费、贬值）、人身伤害（如疾病、丧失伴侣陪伴权）、精神痛苦（某些司法管辖区要求必须伴有人身伤害）、剥夺对财产的使用与享用权（即个人不适、不便、烦扰）以及经济损失（如业务损失）。惩罚性赔偿：当干扰行为属于故意、蓄意或恶意时适用。 当被告对原告财产的使用与享用构成干扰，且该干扰同时满足以下两个条件时，即产生私人妨害的责任：实质性（即对社区中的普通人而言，该干扰令人反感、烦扰或无法忍受）以及不合理性（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的社会效用）。\n\n一旦满足这些要素，原告即可就干扰行为造成的任何类型的损害（包括经济损失，如业务损失）获得赔偿。\n\n在本案中，疏浚公司在附近放置了一台产生持续且强烈噪音的碎石机，从而干扰了酒店公司对其财产的使用与享用。\n\n这种干扰很可能被视为实质性的，因为它导致许多酒店客人取消了预订。\n\n如果该干扰同时被认定为不合理——即酒店公司的经济损失超过了修复公共海滩的社会效用——那么该公司便可依据私人妨害的法理就其经济损失获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）侵入（Trespass）要求证明被告在未经原告同意的情况下故意进入了原告的财产范围。\n\n在本案中，由于疏浚公司的干扰是由噪音引起的（而非通过进入酒店公司的财产范围），因此疏浚公司不承担侵入责任。\n\n（选项 C）从事异常危险活动的被告，若该活动因某种特定危险源而具有异常危险性，则需对该危险源造成的人身或财产损害承担严格责任（即无论有无过错均须承担责任）。\n\n在本案中，尽管机器的运行具有不可避免的（甚至可能是异常的）危险性，但酒店公司无法依据此理论获得赔偿，因为它仅遭受了经济损失，而未遭受实质性的人身或财产损害。\n\n（选项 D）经济损失规则（economic loss rule）排除了针对不伴随人身或财产损害的纯粹经济损失的过失侵权责任。\n\n由于酒店公司仅就经济损失（而非财产损害等人身或财产损害）寻求赔偿，因此无法依据此理论获得赔偿。\n\n教学目标：私人妨害（即对原告财产的使用与享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰）允许原告就任何类型的损害（包括经济损失）获得损害赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第822条（美国法律协会，1979年）（私人妨害责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第821F条（美国法律协会，1979年）（实质性损害要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律协会，1979年）（不合理干扰要件）。","conclusion":"答案 A：私害（Private nuisance）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":171,"sourceQuestionPage":512,"sourceQuestionPages":[512],"sourceExplanationPages":[513,514],"sourceAnswerPages":[513,514],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-172","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Breach & Proof","question":"A retired couple had lived in their home in a residential neighborhood for 20 years when a large family moved into the house next door and built a swimming pool in the backyard. The family's four young children frequently played in the pool after school. They often were joined by other neighborhood children. The retired couple was in the habit of reading and listening to classical music in the afternoons. Sometimes the couple took naps. The boisterous sounds of the children playing in the pool disturbed the couple's customary enjoyment of quiet afternoons. In a nuisance action for damages against the family, is the couple likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the children's interest in healthy play has priority over the couple's interest in peace and quiet.","B":"No, unless the noise constituted a substantial and unreasonable disturbance to persons of normal sensibilities.","C":"Yes, because the couple's interest in the quiet enjoyment of their home takes precedence in time over the family's interests.","D":"Yes, if the children's noise constituted a substantial interference with the couple's use and enjoyment of their home."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A plaintiff can sue for private nuisance when a person interferes with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. To prevail in that suit, the plaintiff must show that the interference was both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighed the utility of the defendant's conduct. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's unusual sensitivity causes him/her to regard the interference as offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a person with normal sensibilities would not. Here, the boisterous sounds of the family's children playing next door interfered with the couple's enjoyment of quiet afternoons at home. But the couple will not prevail on their nuisance claim unless that interference was both substantial (eg, residents with normal sensibilities would be annoyed) and unreasonable (ie, the severity of the couple's harm outweighs the utility of the children's conduct) (Choice D). (Choice A) The children's interest in healthy play (utility) does not necessarily have priority over the couple's interest in peace and quiet (harm) since courts weigh multiple factors to determine whether an interference was unreasonable. And even if the interference was unreasonable, the couple will prevail only if the interference was also substantial. (Choice C) Courts may consider precedence in time (ie, whether the plaintiff came to the nuisance) when determining if a nuisance is actionable. Therefore, the fact that the couple's interest in the quiet enjoyment of their home takes precedence in time over the family's interests helps show that the couple's claim is actionable. But they will only prevail if the children's interference was substantial and unreasonable.\n\nEducational objective: An interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is not substantial—and will not support a private nuisance claim—unless a person with normal sensibilities would regard the interference as offensive, annoying, or intolerable.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 821D, 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (substantial harm requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference requirement).","educationalObjective":"An interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is not substantial—and will not support a private nuisance claim—unless a person with normal sensibilities would regard the interference as offensive, annoying, or intolerable.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 821D, 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (substantial harm requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference requirement).","rule":"An interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is not substantial—and will not support a private nuisance claim—unless a person with normal sensibilities would regard the interference as offensive, annoying, or intolerable.","irac":{"issue":"In a nuisance action for damages against the family, is the couple likely to prevail?","rule":"An interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property is not substantial—and will not support a private nuisance claim—unless a person with normal sensibilities would regard the interference as offensive, annoying, or intolerable.","analysis":"A plaintiff can sue for private nuisance when a person interferes with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property. To prevail in that suit, the plaintiff must show that the interference was both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighed the utility of the defendant's conduct. An interference is not substantial if the plaintiff's unusual sensitivity causes him/her to regard the interference as offensive, annoying, or intolerable when a person with normal sensibilities would not. Here, the boisterous sounds of the family's children playing next door interfered with the couple's enjoyment of quiet afternoons at home. But the couple will not prevail on their nuisance claim unless that interference was both substantial (eg, residents with normal sensibilities would be annoyed) and unreasonable (ie, the severity of the couple's harm outweighs the utility of the children's conduct) (Choice D). (Choice A) The children's interest in healthy play (utility) does not necessarily have priority over the couple's interest in peace and quiet (harm) since courts weigh multiple factors to determine whether an interference was unreasonable. And even if the interference was unreasonable, the couple will prevail only if the interference was also substantial. (Choice C) Courts may consider precedence in time (ie, whether the plaintiff came to the nuisance) when determining if a nuisance is actionable. Therefore, the fact that the couple's interest in the quiet enjoyment of their home takes precedence in time over the family's interests helps show that the couple's claim is actionable. But they will only prevail if the children's interference was substantial and unreasonable.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, unless the noise constituted a substantial and unreasonable disturbance to persons of normal sensibilities."},"questionZh":"一对退休夫妇在某住宅区的家中居住了20年，后来隔壁搬来了一个大家庭，并在后院建了一个游泳池。\n\n这家人四个年幼的孩子放学后经常在泳池里玩耍，邻居家其他的孩子也常来加入。\n\n这对退休夫妇习惯在午后阅读和聆听古典音乐，有时也会小睡一会儿。\n\n孩子们在泳池中嬉戏的喧闹声打扰了夫妇俩享受宁静午后的习惯。\n\n在这起针对该家庭提起的妨害安宁（nuisance）损害赔偿诉讼中，这对夫妇胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为孩子们进行健康玩耍的利益优先于夫妇俩享受宁静的利益。","B":"不会，除非这种噪音对具有正常感官的人构成了实质性且不合理的干扰。","C":"会，因为夫妇俩安静享受其住所的利益在时间上优先于该家庭的利益。","D":"会，如果孩子们的噪音构成了对夫妇俩使用和享受其住所的实质性干扰。"},"explanationZh":"当他人干扰原告对其财产的使用和享用时，原告可提起私人妨害（private nuisance）之诉。\n\n若要胜诉，原告必须证明该干扰同时具备以下两个特征：实质性（即对于社区中的普通人而言，该干扰是令人反感、恼人或无法忍受的）以及不合理性（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用/价值）。\n\n如果干扰之所以被原告视为令人反感、恼人或无法忍受，仅仅是因为原告具有异常敏感的特质（而具有正常感知能力的人不会有此感受），则该干扰不被视为具有实质性。\n\n在本案中，隔壁一家孩子玩耍时的喧闹声干扰了这对夫妇在家享受宁静午后的时光。\n\n然而，这对夫妇若要赢得妨害之诉，必须证明该干扰既具有实质性（例如，具有正常感知能力的居民会感到恼火），又具有不合理性（即夫妇所受损害的严重程度超过了孩子们行为的效用）（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 A）孩子们进行健康玩耍的利益（效用）并不必然优先于夫妇享受宁静安详的利益（损害），因为法院在判定干扰是否不合理时会权衡多种因素。\n\n即便干扰被认定为不合理，夫妇若要胜诉，仍需证明该干扰同时也具有实质性。\n\n（选项 C）法院在判定妨害行为是否构成诉因时，可能会考虑时间上的先后顺序（即原告是否在妨害行为存在后才迁入该处，即“明知有妨害而迁入”）。\n\n因此，夫妇对安宁居住环境的享用利益在时间上先于邻居一家的活动利益，这一事实有助于支持其诉讼主张；但他们最终能否胜诉，仍取决于孩子们造成的干扰是否同时具备实质性和不合理性。\n\n教育目标：若要构成私人妨害之诉的依据，对原告财产使用和享用的干扰必须具有实质性；而“实质性”的判定标准在于，具有正常感知能力的人是否会认为该干扰是令人反感、恼人或无法忍受的。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D、822 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821F 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于实质性损害的要求）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于不合理干扰的要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对原告使用和享受其财产的干扰，若不被具有正常感官的人视为令人反感、恼人或无法忍受，则不构成实质性干扰，也无法支持私人妨害安宁的诉讼主张。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这起针对该家庭提起的妨害安宁损害赔偿诉讼中，这对夫妇胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"对原告使用和享受其财产的干扰，若不被具有正常感官的人视为令人反感、恼人或无法忍受，则不构成实质性干扰，也无法支持私人妨害安宁的诉讼主张。","analysis":"当他人干扰原告对其财产的使用和享用时，原告可提起私人妨害（private nuisance）之诉。\n\n若要胜诉，原告必须证明该干扰同时具备以下两个特征：实质性（即对于社区中的普通人而言，该干扰是令人反感、恼人或无法忍受的）以及不合理性（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用/价值）。\n\n如果干扰之所以被原告视为令人反感、恼人或无法忍受，仅仅是因为原告具有异常敏感的特质（而具有正常感知能力的人不会有此感受），则该干扰不被视为具有实质性。\n\n在本案中，隔壁一家孩子玩耍时的喧闹声干扰了这对夫妇在家享受宁静午后的时光。\n\n然而，这对夫妇若要赢得妨害之诉，必须证明该干扰既具有实质性（例如，具有正常感知能力的居民会感到恼火），又具有不合理性（即夫妇所受损害的严重程度超过了孩子们行为的效用）（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 A）孩子们进行健康玩耍的利益（效用）并不必然优先于夫妇享受宁静安详的利益（损害），因为法院在判定干扰是否不合理时会权衡多种因素。\n\n即便干扰被认定为不合理，夫妇若要胜诉，仍需证明该干扰同时也具有实质性。\n\n（选项 C）法院在判定妨害行为是否构成诉因时，可能会考虑时间上的先后顺序（即原告是否在妨害行为存在后才迁入该处，即“明知有妨害而迁入”）。\n\n因此，夫妇对安宁居住环境的享用利益在时间上先于邻居一家的活动利益，这一事实有助于支持其诉讼主张；但他们最终能否胜诉，仍取决于孩子们造成的干扰是否同时具备实质性和不合理性。\n\n教育目标：若要构成私人妨害之诉的依据，对原告财产使用和享用的干扰必须具有实质性；而“实质性”的判定标准在于，具有正常感知能力的人是否会认为该干扰是令人反感、恼人或无法忍受的。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D、822 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821F 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于实质性损害的要求）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于不合理干扰的要求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，除非这种噪音对具有正常感官的人构成了实质性且不合理的干扰。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-66c121ada5f9c5c4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":172,"sourceQuestionPage":515,"sourceQuestionPages":[515],"sourceExplanationPages":[516,517],"sourceAnswerPages":[516,517],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-173","subject":"Torts","topic":"Negligence: Duty & Standard of Care","question":"A buyer bought a large, nicely kept house near a university campus. She was able to buy the house for a very good price because it was located directly across the street from a fraternity house known for its frequent late-night parties. The buyer knew of the fraternity's reputation before she bought the house. The reputation was well deserved, and the buyer found the noise from the parties extremely unpleasant and disruptive. The buyer has asked an attorney for legal advice regarding a possible nuisance claim against the fraternity. Which of the following responses would best express the applicable law?","choices":{"A":"\"You have no nuisance claim, because the fraternity members have the right to use their property as they please.\"","B":"\"You have no nuisance claim, because you came to the nuisance.\"","C":"\"You might have a nuisance claim, but the fact that you bought the house fully aware of the fraternity's habitual late-night activities will count against your claim and could help defeat it.\"","D":"\"You will be able to recover damages in a nuisance action, because the late-night activities of the fraternity members violate your right to the quiet enjoyment of your property.\""},"answer":"C","explanation":"Liability for private nuisance arises when the defendant's interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property was both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. But if the plaintiff came to the nuisance (ie, acquired or improved property next to a known nuisance), then the court will consider this factor in determining whether the nuisance is actionable (ie, whether the plaintiff can recover for the nuisance). Here, the buyer might have a private nuisance claim since the fraternity's late-night parties were extremely unpleasant and disruptive to her at home (substantial and unreasonable interference). But since she bought her home knowing the fraternity's reputation for late- night parties (ie, she came to the nuisance), this fact will count against her claim and could help defeat it. (Choice A) Landowners have the right to use their property in any reasonable way—not in any way they please. Therefore, the buyer could prevail on her nuisance claim if the fraternity's activities were unreasonable and substantially interfered with the use and enjoyment of her home. (Choice B) The fact that the buyer came to the nuisance is a factor in determining whether the nuisance is actionable but does not bar her claim. (Choice D) Even if the fraternity's activities violate the buyer's right to enjoy her property, the fact that she knew the fraternity's reputation (ie, she came to the nuisance) could help defeat her claim.\n\nEducational objective: Coming to the nuisance—ie, acquiring or improving property next to a known nuisance— does not bar a nuisance claim. However, the court will consider this factor in determining whether the plaintiff can recover.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821D (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 840D (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (coming to the nuisance).","educationalObjective":"Coming to the nuisance—ie, acquiring or improving property next to a known nuisance— does not bar a nuisance claim. However, the court will consider this factor in determining whether the plaintiff can recover.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 821D (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 840D (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (coming to the nuisance).","rule":"Coming to the nuisance—ie, acquiring or improving property next to a known nuisance— does not bar a nuisance claim. However, the court will consider this factor in determining whether the plaintiff can recover.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following responses would best express the applicable law?","rule":"Coming to the nuisance—ie, acquiring or improving property next to a known nuisance— does not bar a nuisance claim. However, the court will consider this factor in determining whether the plaintiff can recover.","analysis":"Liability for private nuisance arises when the defendant's interference with the plaintiff's use and enjoyment of his/her property was both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. But if the plaintiff came to the nuisance (ie, acquired or improved property next to a known nuisance), then the court will consider this factor in determining whether the nuisance is actionable (ie, whether the plaintiff can recover for the nuisance). Here, the buyer might have a private nuisance claim since the fraternity's late-night parties were extremely unpleasant and disruptive to her at home (substantial and unreasonable interference). But since she bought her home knowing the fraternity's reputation for late- night parties (ie, she came to the nuisance), this fact will count against her claim and could help defeat it. (Choice A) Landowners have the right to use their property in any reasonable way—not in any way they please. Therefore, the buyer could prevail on her nuisance claim if the fraternity's activities were unreasonable and substantially interfered with the use and enjoyment of her home. (Choice B) The fact that the buyer came to the nuisance is a factor in determining whether the nuisance is actionable but does not bar her claim. (Choice D) Even if the fraternity's activities violate the buyer's right to enjoy her property, the fact that she knew the fraternity's reputation (ie, she came to the nuisance) could help defeat her claim.","conclusion":"Answer C: \"You might have a nuisance claim, but the fact that you bought the house fully aware of the fraternity's habitual late-night activities will count against your claim and could help defeat it.\""},"questionZh":"一位买家在大学校园附近买了一栋宽敞且维护良好的房子。\n\n她之所以能以非常优惠的价格买到这栋房子，是因为它正对面就是一个以频繁举办深夜派对而闻名的兄弟会会所。\n\n买家在买房前就知道这个兄弟会的名声。\n\n事实证明，这个名声名不虚传，买家发现派对噪音极其令人不快，严重影响了她的生活。\n\n买家就可能针对该兄弟会提起妨害诉讼一事咨询了律师。\n\n以下哪项回复最能体现适用的法律？","choicesZh":{"A":"“你无权提起妨害诉讼，因为兄弟会成员有权随意使用他们的房产。”","B":"“你无权提起妨害诉讼，因为你是主动选择忍受这种妨害。”","C":"“您或许可以提起妨害诉讼，但您在购买房屋时完全了解该兄弟会成员经常深夜活动的事实，将对您的诉讼不利，并可能导致诉讼败诉。”","D":"“您可以通过妨害诉讼获得损害赔偿，因为兄弟会成员的深夜活动侵犯了您对房产的安静享用权。”"},"explanationZh":"当被告对原告使用和享用其财产的干扰同时满足以下两个条件时，即产生私人妨害（private nuisance）的法律责任：一是“实质性”（substantial）——即对社区中的普通人而言，这种干扰令人反感、恼火或无法忍受；二是“不合理性”（unreasonable）——即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的社会效用。\n\n然而，如果原告属于“主动迁入妨害源所在地”（即在已知存在妨害源的地点附近购置或改良房产），法院在判定该妨害是否构成可诉侵权（即原告能否就此获得救济）时，会将这一因素纳入考量。\n\n在本案中，买方或许可以提出私人妨害索赔，因为该兄弟会深夜举办的派对令她在自家住所感到极度不适并受到严重干扰（构成了实质性且不合理的干扰）。\n\n但鉴于她在购房时已知晓该兄弟会深夜举办派对的惯例（即她属于“主动迁入妨害源所在地”），这一事实将对她的索赔主张不利，甚至可能导致其败诉。\n\n（选项 A）土地所有者有权以任何合理的方式使用其财产，但并非可以随心所欲地使用。\n\n因此，如果兄弟会的活动不合理且实质性地干扰了买方对其住所的使用和享用，买方在妨害索赔诉讼中便可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）买方“主动迁入妨害源所在地”这一事实是判定妨害是否可诉的考量因素之一，但并不构成对其索赔权的绝对阻却。\n\n（选项 D）即使兄弟会的活动侵犯了买方享用其财产的权利，她已知晓该兄弟会相关惯例（即“主动迁入妨害源所在地”）这一事实，仍可能导致其索赔主张被驳回。\n\n教育目标：“主动迁入妨害源所在地”（即在已知存在妨害源的地点附近购置或改良房产）并不构成妨害索赔的绝对障碍。\n\n然而，法院在判定原告能否获得救济时，会将这一因素纳入考量。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 840D 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于“主动迁入妨害源所在地”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"购买或改善毗邻已知妨害场所的房产并不妨碍您提起妨害诉讼。\n\n但是，法院在决定原告是否可以获得赔偿时会考虑这一因素。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项回答最能体现适用的法律？","rule":"购买或改善毗邻已知妨害场所的房产并不妨碍您提起妨害诉讼。\n\n但是，法院在决定原告是否可以获得赔偿时会考虑这一因素。","analysis":"当被告对原告使用和享用其财产的干扰同时满足以下两个条件时，即产生私人妨害（private nuisance）的法律责任：一是“实质性”（substantial）——即对社区中的普通人而言，这种干扰令人反感、恼火或无法忍受；二是“不合理性”（unreasonable）——即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的社会效用。\n\n然而，如果原告属于“主动迁入妨害源所在地”（即在已知存在妨害源的地点附近购置或改良房产），法院在判定该妨害是否构成可诉侵权（即原告能否就此获得救济）时，会将这一因素纳入考量。\n\n在本案中，买方或许可以提出私人妨害索赔，因为该兄弟会深夜举办的派对令她在自家住所感到极度不适并受到严重干扰（构成了实质性且不合理的干扰）。\n\n但鉴于她在购房时已知晓该兄弟会深夜举办派对的惯例（即她属于“主动迁入妨害源所在地”），这一事实将对她的索赔主张不利，甚至可能导致其败诉。\n\n（选项 A）土地所有者有权以任何合理的方式使用其财产，但并非可以随心所欲地使用。\n\n因此，如果兄弟会的活动不合理且实质性地干扰了买方对其住所的使用和享用，买方在妨害索赔诉讼中便可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）买方“主动迁入妨害源所在地”这一事实是判定妨害是否可诉的考量因素之一，但并不构成对其索赔权的绝对阻却。\n\n（选项 D）即使兄弟会的活动侵犯了买方享用其财产的权利，她已知晓该兄弟会相关惯例（即“主动迁入妨害源所在地”）这一事实，仍可能导致其索赔主张被驳回。\n\n教育目标：“主动迁入妨害源所在地”（即在已知存在妨害源的地点附近购置或改良房产）并不构成妨害索赔的绝对障碍。\n\n然而，法院在判定原告能否获得救济时，会将这一因素纳入考量。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 840D 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于“主动迁入妨害源所在地”）。","conclusion":"答案 C：“您或许可以提起妨害诉讼，但您在购买房屋时完全了解该兄弟会成员经常深夜活动的事实，将对您的诉讼不利，并可能导致诉讼败诉。”"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-198c1d223d93369b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":173,"sourceQuestionPage":518,"sourceQuestionPages":[518],"sourceExplanationPages":[519,520],"sourceAnswerPages":[519,520],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-174","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A female quarterback was the only female on an otherwise all-male college football team. During training one day, one of the team's offensive linemen saw the female quarterback hidden behind the bleachers, injecting herself with a needle. The lineman immediately told the football team's coach that he had just seen the female quarterback injecting herself with steroids. The female quarterback was diabetic and had actually been injecting insulin into her arm when the lineman saw her behind the bleachers. However, not knowing this, the coach suspended the female quarterback from the team. If the female quarterback files a defamation suit against the lineman, who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The female quarterback, because she is a private person and this is not a matter of public concern.","B":"The female quarterback, because the statement was false and the lineman was negligent in making it.","C":"The lineman, because he was not acting with actual malice.","D":"The lineman, because there was no publication."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Because defamation consists of speech, the Supreme Court has decided that the First Amendment imposes a fault requirement in cases involving public officials, public figures, and matters of public concern. But if the plaintiff is a private person and the defamatory statement involves a matter of private concern (as seen here), the constitutional requirements do not apply (Choice A). However, most states require at least negligence by the defendant for all defamation actions. Accordingly, the following elements must generally be proved in all actions for defamation of a private person: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity. The statement was defamatory, meaning that the type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation. The defendant published the statement by intentionally or negligently communicating it to a third party. The statement caused the plaintiff special damage* OR amounted to libel or slander per se. Here, the lineman falsely told the coach (publication) that the female quarterback was injecting herself with steroids (defamatory statement), and she was suspended from the team as a result (special damage). Since the lineman made no effort to check the accuracy of his statement first, he was likely negligent in making it. Therefore, the female quarterback is likely to prevail in her defamation suit. *Special damage requires proof that a third party heard the defendant's defamatory comment and then acted adversely to the plaintiff. Most often, special damage involves an economic loss to the plaintiff, but it would also include such things as the coach suspending the female quarterback from the team. (Choice C) In defamation actions by public officials or public figures (not seen here), the plaintiff is constitutionally required to prove that the defendant acted with actual malice. Actual malice is the knowledge of, or reckless disregard for, the falsity of the defamatory statement. (Choice D) Publication can be any form of communication to a third party. Therefore, publication occurred here when the lineman told the coach that he had seen the female quarterback injecting herself with steroids.\n\nEducational objective: In defamation actions by private persons, the level of fault required is that the defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nReferences\nNew York Times v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254, 283–84 (1964) (holding that the First Amendment affects the plaintiff's right to recover under the common law tort of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 580A, 580B (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining the fault requirement for defamation of public or private person).","educationalObjective":"In defamation actions by private persons, the level of fault required is that the defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.","references":"New York Times v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254, 283–84 (1964) (holding that the First Amendment affects the plaintiff's right to recover under the common law tort of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 580A, 580B (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining the fault requirement for defamation of public or private person).","rule":"In defamation actions by private persons, the level of fault required is that the defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.","irac":{"issue":"If the female quarterback files a defamation suit against the lineman, who is likely to prevail?","rule":"In defamation actions by private persons, the level of fault required is that the defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.","analysis":"Because defamation consists of speech, the Supreme Court has decided that the First Amendment imposes a fault requirement in cases involving public officials, public figures, and matters of public concern. But if the plaintiff is a private person and the defamatory statement involves a matter of private concern (as seen here), the constitutional requirements do not apply (Choice A). However, most states require at least negligence by the defendant for all defamation actions. Accordingly, the following elements must generally be proved in all actions for defamation of a private person: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity. The statement was defamatory, meaning that the type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation. The defendant published the statement by intentionally or negligently communicating it to a third party. The statement caused the plaintiff special damage* OR amounted to libel or slander per se. Here, the lineman falsely told the coach (publication) that the female quarterback was injecting herself with steroids (defamatory statement), and she was suspended from the team as a result (special damage). Since the lineman made no effort to check the accuracy of his statement first, he was likely negligent in making it. Therefore, the female quarterback is likely to prevail in her defamation suit. *Special damage requires proof that a third party heard the defendant's defamatory comment and then acted adversely to the plaintiff. Most often, special damage involves an economic loss to the plaintiff, but it would also include such things as the coach suspending the female quarterback from the team. (Choice C) In defamation actions by public officials or public figures (not seen here), the plaintiff is constitutionally required to prove that the defendant acted with actual malice. Actual malice is the knowledge of, or reckless disregard for, the falsity of the defamatory statement. (Choice D) Publication can be any form of communication to a third party. Therefore, publication occurred here when the lineman told the coach that he had seen the female quarterback injecting herself with steroids.","conclusion":"Answer B: The female quarterback, because the statement was false and the lineman was negligent in making it."},"questionZh":"一名女四分卫是这支全是男生的大学橄榄球队中唯一的女性。\n\n一天训练时，球队的一名进攻锋线队员看到这名女四分卫躲在看台后面给自己注射药物。\n\n这名锋线队员立即告诉球队教练，他刚才看到这名女四分卫注射的是类固醇。\n\n事实上，这名女四分卫患有糖尿病，当这名锋线队员看到她躲在看台后面时，她正在给自己注射胰岛素。\n\n然而，教练并不知情，便将这名女四分卫停赛。\n\n如果这名女四分卫起诉这名锋线队员诽谤，谁更有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"女四分卫，因为她是个人，此事不属于公共事务。","B":"女四分卫，因为该陈述不实，且锋线队员在作出陈述时存在过失。","C":"锋线队员，因为他并非出于恶意。","D":"因为没有公开发表，所以是边锋。"},"explanationZh":"由于诽谤涉及言论，最高法院裁定，在涉及公职人员、公众人物及公共关注事项的案件中，宪法第一修正案要求证明被告存在过错。\n\n但如果原告是普通公民（私人），且诽谤性陈述涉及私人关注事项（如本案所示），则不适用上述宪法要求（选项 A）。\n\n不过，大多数州在所有诽谤诉讼中都要求被告至少存在过失。\n\n因此，在针对普通公民的诽谤诉讼中，通常必须证明以下要素：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实而发表该陈述，或因过失未核实其不实性；该陈述具有诽谤性，即该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉；被告通过故意或过失的方式向第三方传达该陈述，从而发布了该陈述；该陈述导致原告遭受“特别损害”*，或构成“本身即构成诽谤”（libel or slander per se）的情形。\n\n在本案中，该锋线球员向教练谎称（发布行为）这名女性四分卫给自己注射类固醇（诽谤性陈述），导致她被停赛（特别损害）。\n\n由于该锋线球员在发表言论前未核实其准确性，他在发表该言论时很可能存在过失。\n\n因此，这名女性四分卫很可能在诽谤诉讼中胜诉。\n\n*“特别损害”要求证明第三方听到了被告的诽谤性言论，并据此采取了对原告不利的行动。\n\n特别损害通常涉及原告的经济损失，但也包括诸如教练将该女性四分卫停赛之类的情况。\n\n（选项 C）在由公职人员或公众人物提起的诽谤诉讼中（本案不涉及此类情况），根据宪法要求，原告必须证明被告具有“实际恶意”。\n\n实际恶意是指明知诽谤性陈述不实，或罔顾其不实性（即鲁莽地不顾事实）。\n\n（选项 D）“发布”可以是向第三方传达信息的任何形式。\n\n因此，当该锋线球员告诉教练他看到这名女性四分卫给自己注射类固醇时，即构成了发布行为。\n\n教育目标：在由普通公民提起的诽谤诉讼中，要求的过错程度为：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实而发表该陈述，或因过失未核实其不实性。\n\n参考文献 《纽约时报诉沙利文案》（New York Times v. Sullivan），376 U.S. 254, 283–84 (1964)（裁定第一修正案影响原告依据普通法诽谤侵权行为主张赔偿的权利）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第580A、580B条（美国法律学会，1981年）（阐述针对公职人员或私人进行诽谤时的过错要件）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在私人提起的诽谤诉讼中，被告的过错程度要求是：被告明知故犯地对原告作出了虚假陈述，或者因疏忽未能辨别其虚假性。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果女四分卫起诉边锋诽谤，谁更有可能胜诉？","rule":"在私人提起的诽谤诉讼中，被告的过错程度要求是：被告明知故犯地对原告作出了虚假陈述，或者因疏忽未能辨别其虚假性。","analysis":"由于诽谤涉及言论，最高法院裁定，在涉及公职人员、公众人物及公共关注事项的案件中，宪法第一修正案要求证明被告存在过错。\n\n但如果原告是普通公民（私人），且诽谤性陈述涉及私人关注事项（如本案所示），则不适用上述宪法要求（选项 A）。\n\n不过，大多数州在所有诽谤诉讼中都要求被告至少存在过失。\n\n因此，在针对普通公民的诽谤诉讼中，通常必须证明以下要素：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实而发表该陈述，或因过失未核实其不实性；该陈述具有诽谤性，即该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉；被告通过故意或过失的方式向第三方传达该陈述，从而发布了该陈述；该陈述导致原告遭受“特别损害”*，或构成“本身即构成诽谤”（libel or slander per se）的情形。\n\n在本案中，该锋线球员向教练谎称（发布行为）这名女性四分卫给自己注射类固醇（诽谤性陈述），导致她被停赛（特别损害）。\n\n由于该锋线球员在发表言论前未核实其准确性，他在发表该言论时很可能存在过失。\n\n因此，这名女性四分卫很可能在诽谤诉讼中胜诉。\n\n*“特别损害”要求证明第三方听到了被告的诽谤性言论，并据此采取了对原告不利的行动。\n\n特别损害通常涉及原告的经济损失，但也包括诸如教练将该女性四分卫停赛之类的情况。\n\n（选项 C）在由公职人员或公众人物提起的诽谤诉讼中（本案不涉及此类情况），根据宪法要求，原告必须证明被告具有“实际恶意”。\n\n实际恶意是指明知诽谤性陈述不实，或罔顾其不实性（即鲁莽地不顾事实）。\n\n（选项 D）“发布”可以是向第三方传达信息的任何形式。\n\n因此，当该锋线球员告诉教练他看到这名女性四分卫给自己注射类固醇时，即构成了发布行为。\n\n教育目标：在由普通公民提起的诽谤诉讼中，要求的过错程度为：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实而发表该陈述，或因过失未核实其不实性。\n\n参考文献 《纽约时报诉沙利文案》（New York Times v. Sullivan），376 U.S. 254, 283–84 (1964)（裁定第一修正案影响原告依据普通法诽谤侵权行为主张赔偿的权利）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第580A、580B条（美国法律学会，1981年）（阐述针对公职人员或私人进行诽谤时的过错要件）。","conclusion":"答案 B：女四分卫，因为该陈述不实，且锋线队员在作出陈述时存在过失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-06c8a04b0b9a1fd2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":174,"sourceQuestionPage":521,"sourceQuestionPages":[521],"sourceExplanationPages":[522,523],"sourceAnswerPages":[522,523],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-175","subject":"Torts","topic":"Economic & Dignitary Torts","question":"A venture capitalist read through a publicly held corporation's most recent financial statement, which was available on the corporation's website. The corporation's financial statement disclosed that the book value of the corporation's stock was $10 per share, but its actual value is $50 per share. The venture capitalist also noticed that the corporation had invested heavily to develop a new social media platform over the previous year. After doing more research, she became convinced that this platform would become extremely popular and would, in turn, drive up the price of the corporation's stock. The venture capitalist approached one of the corporation's shareholders whom she had never met. She offered to buy his 500 shares at $15 a share. The shareholder said that he thought that the shares were worth more. The venture capitalist did not respond. Eventually, the shareholder agreed to the venture capitalist's price for the 500 shares. Two months after this sale, the shareholder learned about the actual value of the stock and the corporation's investment in the new social media platform. The shareholder asserted a misrepresentation claim against the venture capitalist. Will the shareholder likely prevail on his claim?","choices":{"A":"No, because the shareholder had access to the corporation's most recent financial statement at the time of the sale.","B":"No, because the venture capitalist had no duty to disclose the shares' actual value.","C":"Yes, because the venture capitalist had a duty to disclose the shares' actual value when she made the offer.","D":"Yes, because the venture capitalist had a duty to disclose the shares' actual value when the shareholder commented about their actual worth."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Liability for misrepresentation generally arises when a defendant actively misrepresents a material fact. However, nondisclosure of a material fact will also subject a defendant to liability if the defendant had a duty to disclose it. This duty arises when the defendant is engaged in a business transaction with a plaintiff who is: in a fiduciary relationship with the defendant\n\nlikely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements or\n\nmistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and can reasonably be expected to disclose.\n\nHere, the venture capitalist knew that her offer to buy the shareholder's shares of the corporation's stock was less than their actual value (material fact) but did not disclose that information to the shareholder. However, she had no duty to disclose it because: the parties had just met (no fiduciary relationship)\n\nshe only offered to purchase his shares and made no misleading statement before or after his comment about their actual worth (Choice D) and\n\nshe could not reasonably be expected to reveal that her offer was low because people routinely try to buy stocks for as little as possible to increase the value of the purchase in the long run (Choice C).\n\nTherefore, the shareholder likely will not prevail on his misrepresentation claim. (Choice A) The fact that the shareholder had access to the corporation's financial statement would not have defeated his misrepresentation claim had the venture capitalist owed him a duty to disclose that information—eg, had the venture capitalist been his financial advisor (fiduciary relationship).\n\nEducational objective: A defendant must disclose material facts during a business transaction with a plaintiff who is (1) in a fiduciary relationship with the defendant, (2) likely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements, or (3) mistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and can reasonably be expected to disclose.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 551 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (liability for nondisclosure).","educationalObjective":"A defendant must disclose material facts during a business transaction with a plaintiff who is (1) in a fiduciary relationship with the defendant, (2) likely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements, or (3) mistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and can reasonably be expected to disclose.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 551 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (liability for nondisclosure).","rule":"A defendant must disclose material facts during a business transaction with a plaintiff who is (1) in a fiduciary relationship with the defendant, (2) likely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements, or (3) mistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and can reasonably be expected to disclose.","irac":{"issue":"Will the shareholder likely prevail on his claim?","rule":"A defendant must disclose material facts during a business transaction with a plaintiff who is (1) in a fiduciary relationship with the defendant, (2) likely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements, or (3) mistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and can reasonably be expected to disclose.","analysis":"Liability for misrepresentation generally arises when a defendant actively misrepresents a material fact. However, nondisclosure of a material fact will also subject a defendant to liability if the defendant had a duty to disclose it. This duty arises when the defendant is engaged in a business transaction with a plaintiff who is: in a fiduciary relationship with the defendant\n\nlikely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements or\n\nmistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and can reasonably be expected to disclose.\n\nHere, the venture capitalist knew that her offer to buy the shareholder's shares of the corporation's stock was less than their actual value (material fact) but did not disclose that information to the shareholder. However, she had no duty to disclose it because: the parties had just met (no fiduciary relationship)\n\nshe only offered to purchase his shares and made no misleading statement before or after his comment about their actual worth (Choice D) and\n\nshe could not reasonably be expected to reveal that her offer was low because people routinely try to buy stocks for as little as possible to increase the value of the purchase in the long run (Choice C).\n\nTherefore, the shareholder likely will not prevail on his misrepresentation claim. (Choice A) The fact that the shareholder had access to the corporation's financial statement would not have defeated his misrepresentation claim had the venture capitalist owed him a duty to disclose that information—eg, had the venture capitalist been his financial advisor (fiduciary relationship).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the venture capitalist had no duty to disclose the shares' actual value."},"questionZh":"一位风险投资家查阅了一家上市公司在其官网上公布的最新财务报表。\n\n该报表显示，公司股票的账面价值为每股10美元，但其实际价值为每股50美元。\n\n这位风险投资家还注意到，该公司在过去一年里投入巨资开发了一个新的社交媒体平台。\n\n经过进一步研究，她确信该平台将大受欢迎，进而推高公司股价。\n\n风险投资家联系了该公司的一位她素不相识的股东，提出以每股15美元的价格收购其持有的500股股票。\n\n该股东表示，他认为这些股票的价值更高。\n\n风险投资家未予置评。\n\n最终，该股东接受了风险投资家提出的价格，出售了这500股股票。\n\n交易完成两个月后，该股东获悉了股票的实际价值以及公司对新社交媒体平台的投资情况。\n\n该股东针对风险投资家提起了关于虚假陈述的索赔主张。\n\n该股东的索赔主张胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为在出售时，该股东完全可以获取该公司的最新财务报表。","B":"不大，因为风险投资家没有义务披露股票的实际价值。","C":"大，因为风险投资家在提出收购要约时有义务披露股票的实际价值。","D":"大，因为当股东谈及股票的实际价值时，风险投资家有义务披露其实际价值。"},"explanationZh":"因虚假陈述而产生的法律责任通常源于被告积极地对重大事实进行不实陈述。\n\n然而，如果被告有义务披露某项重大事实，那么未披露该事实也会导致其承担责任。\n\n这种披露义务产生于被告与原告进行商业交易，且原告处于以下情形之一时：与被告存在信义关系（fiduciary relationship）； 很可能因被告先前的陈述而受到误导；或 对交易的某项基本事实存在误解，而被告已知晓该事实且按理应予披露。\n\n在本案中，风险投资家明知其收购该股东所持公司股份的报价低于其实际价值（即重大事实），却未向该股东披露这一信息。\n\n然而，她并无披露义务，原因如下：双方初次见面（不存在信义关系）； 她仅提出了收购股份的要约，且在该股东就股份实际价值发表评论的前后，她均未作出任何误导性陈述（选项 D）；以及 不能合理要求她披露其报价偏低这一事实，因为人们通常会试图以尽可能低的价格买入股票，以增加长期投资价值（选项 C）。\n\n因此，该股东关于虚假陈述的索赔主张很可能无法获得支持。\n\n（选项 A）如果风险投资家对该股东负有披露信息的义务——例如，若风险投资家是其财务顾问（存在信义关系）——那么即便该股东能够查阅公司的财务报表，也不会因此丧失提出虚假陈述索赔的权利。\n\n教学目标：在与原告进行商业交易时，被告必须披露重大事实，前提是原告：(1) 与被告存在信义关系；(2) 很可能因被告先前的陈述而受到误导；或 (3) 对交易的某项基本事实存在误解，而被告已知晓该事实且按理应予披露。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 551 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于未披露事实的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在与原告进行商业交易时，被告必须披露重大事实，如果原告：（1）与被告存在信义关系；（2）可能因被告先前的陈述而受到误导；或（3）对交易的基本事实存在误解，而被告了解该事实且有理由被预期应当披露该事实。","iracZh":{"issue":"该股东的索赔主张胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"在与原告进行商业交易时，被告必须披露重大事实，如果原告：（1）与被告存在信义关系；（2）可能因被告先前的陈述而受到误导；或（3）对交易的基本事实存在误解，而被告了解该事实且有理由被预期应当披露该事实。","analysis":"因虚假陈述而产生的法律责任通常源于被告积极地对重大事实进行不实陈述。\n\n然而，如果被告有义务披露某项重大事实，那么未披露该事实也会导致其承担责任。\n\n这种披露义务产生于被告与原告进行商业交易，且原告处于以下情形之一时：与被告存在信义关系（fiduciary relationship）； 很可能因被告先前的陈述而受到误导；或 对交易的某项基本事实存在误解，而被告已知晓该事实且按理应予披露。\n\n在本案中，风险投资家明知其收购该股东所持公司股份的报价低于其实际价值（即重大事实），却未向该股东披露这一信息。\n\n然而，她并无披露义务，原因如下：双方初次见面（不存在信义关系）； 她仅提出了收购股份的要约，且在该股东就股份实际价值发表评论的前后，她均未作出任何误导性陈述（选项 D）；以及 不能合理要求她披露其报价偏低这一事实，因为人们通常会试图以尽可能低的价格买入股票，以增加长期投资价值（选项 C）。\n\n因此，该股东关于虚假陈述的索赔主张很可能无法获得支持。\n\n（选项 A）如果风险投资家对该股东负有披露信息的义务——例如，若风险投资家是其财务顾问（存在信义关系）——那么即便该股东能够查阅公司的财务报表，也不会因此丧失提出虚假陈述索赔的权利。\n\n教学目标：在与原告进行商业交易时，被告必须披露重大事实，前提是原告：(1) 与被告存在信义关系；(2) 很可能因被告先前的陈述而受到误导；或 (3) 对交易的某项基本事实存在误解，而被告已知晓该事实且按理应予披露。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 551 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于未披露事实的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不大，因为风险投资家没有义务披露股票的实际价值。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-a9d0bfdeed73cbc4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":175,"sourceQuestionPage":524,"sourceQuestionPages":[524],"sourceExplanationPages":[525,526],"sourceAnswerPages":[525,526],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-176","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A man lives in a small community where there is only one judge. The man recently lost a highly publicized court case that had been adjudicated by her, and he was convinced that she had issued an unfair judgment. As the man left the courthouse, he made this statement to a reporter: \"Judges are crooks, and they will accept bribes if you pay them enough!\" The statement was subsequently printed in the local newspaper. The judge had never accepted a bribe, and after reading the statement in the newspaper, she filed a defamation action against the man. Will the judge be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the defamatory language applied to judges in general.","B":"No, because the man's statement did not cause the judge actual harm.","C":"Yes, because a reasonable person would conclude that the man's statement referred to the judge.","D":"Yes, because the statement was published in the local newspaper."},"answer":"C","explanation":"When a defendant's defamatory statement applies to a group of people, the general rule is that no member of the group can recover for defamation. This is because a reasonable person who hears or reads the statement would not conclude that it refers to a specific member of the group. However, an individual member can maintain a defamation action if: the group is so small that a reasonable person would conclude that the statement refers to that member or\n\nthe circumstances of publication would cause a reasonable person to conclude that the statement refers to the member.\n\nHere, the man told the reporter that \"judges\" are crooks who accept bribes. Although this defamatory statement did not identify the judge by name, it was made in a community with one judge as the man was leaving the courthouse after a highly publicized trial. As a result, the circumstances of publication reasonably give rise to the conclusion that the statement was a reference to this particular judge and not judges in general (Choice A). For this reason, the judge will likely prevail in her defamation action. (Choice B) Defamation generally requires proof that the statement caused the plaintiff special damage, typically monetary loss. However, harm is presumed when the statement is slanderous per se—eg, accuses the plaintiff of engaging in conduct that adversely affects the plaintiff's occupation (as seen here). (Choice D) Publication occurs when a defamatory statement is transmitted to a third party. Therefore, publication occurred here when the man made the statement to a reporter—not when it was published in the newspaper.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant who makes a defamatory statement about a group of people is liable to an individual member of that group only if (1) the group is so small that a reasonable person would conclude that the statement refers to that member or (2) the circumstances of publication would cause a reasonable person to reach that same conclusion.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 564A (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (defamation of a group or class).","educationalObjective":"A defendant who makes a defamatory statement about a group of people is liable to an individual member of that group only if (1) the group is so small that a reasonable person would conclude that the statement refers to that member or (2) the circumstances of publication would cause a reasonable person to reach that same conclusion.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 564A (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (defamation of a group or class).","rule":"A defendant who makes a defamatory statement about a group of people is liable to an individual member of that group only if (1) the group is so small that a reasonable person would conclude that the statement refers to that member or (2) the circumstances of publication would cause a reasonable person to reach that same conclusion.","irac":{"issue":"Will the judge be likely to prevail?","rule":"A defendant who makes a defamatory statement about a group of people is liable to an individual member of that group only if (1) the group is so small that a reasonable person would conclude that the statement refers to that member or (2) the circumstances of publication would cause a reasonable person to reach that same conclusion.","analysis":"When a defendant's defamatory statement applies to a group of people, the general rule is that no member of the group can recover for defamation. This is because a reasonable person who hears or reads the statement would not conclude that it refers to a specific member of the group. However, an individual member can maintain a defamation action if: the group is so small that a reasonable person would conclude that the statement refers to that member or\n\nthe circumstances of publication would cause a reasonable person to conclude that the statement refers to the member.\n\nHere, the man told the reporter that \"judges\" are crooks who accept bribes. Although this defamatory statement did not identify the judge by name, it was made in a community with one judge as the man was leaving the courthouse after a highly publicized trial. As a result, the circumstances of publication reasonably give rise to the conclusion that the statement was a reference to this particular judge and not judges in general (Choice A). For this reason, the judge will likely prevail in her defamation action. (Choice B) Defamation generally requires proof that the statement caused the plaintiff special damage, typically monetary loss. However, harm is presumed when the statement is slanderous per se—eg, accuses the plaintiff of engaging in conduct that adversely affects the plaintiff's occupation (as seen here). (Choice D) Publication occurs when a defamatory statement is transmitted to a third party. Therefore, publication occurred here when the man made the statement to a reporter—not when it was published in the newspaper.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because a reasonable person would conclude that the man's statement referred to the judge."},"questionZh":"一个男人住在只有一位法官的小社区。\n\n他最近输掉了一场备受瞩目的官司，而这场官司正是由这位法官审理的。他坚信法官的判决不公。\n\n离开法院时，他向记者说道：“法官都是骗子，只要给够钱，他们什么都收！” 这句话随后被当地报纸刊登。\n\n这位法官从未收受过贿赂，在报纸上看到这句话后，她对该男子提起了诽谤诉讼。\n\n这位法官胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为诽谤性言论针对的是所有法官。","B":"不可能，因为该男子的言论并未对法官造成实际损害。","C":"可能，因为一个理性的人会认为该男子的言论指的是这位法官。","D":"可能，因为该言论被刊登在当地报纸上。"},"explanationZh":"当被告发表针对某一群体的诽谤性言论时，一般规则是该群体的任何成员均无法就诽谤提起诉讼并获得赔偿。\n\n这是因为听到或读到该言论的理性人不会认为其指向该群体中的特定成员。\n\n然而，如果出现以下情况，群体中的个人成员可以提起诽谤诉讼：该群体规模极小，以至于理性人会认为该言论指向该成员；或者 发表该言论时的具体情境会使理性人认为该言论指向该成员。\n\n在本案中，该男子告诉记者“法官”都是收受贿赂的骗子。\n\n尽管这一诽谤性言论未指名道姓，但该言论是在一个仅有一名法官的社区发表的，且发表时机正值该男子结束一场备受瞩目的庭审离开法院之际。\n\n因此，根据发表言论时的情境，有理由得出结论：该言论指向的是这名特定法官，而非泛指所有法官（选项 A）。\n\n正因如此，该法官很可能在诽谤诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）诽谤诉讼通常要求证明该言论给原告造成了“特别损害”（special damage），即通常意义上的金钱损失。\n\n然而，若言论构成“本身诽谤”（slander per se）——例如指控原告从事了对其职业产生不利影响的行为（本案即属此类）——则推定存在损害。\n\n（选项 D）当诽谤性言论被传达给第三方时，即构成“发表”（publication）。\n\n因此，本案中的发表行为发生在该男子向记者陈述该言论之时，而非该言论见诸报端之时。\n\n教学目标：被告若发表针对某一群体的诽谤性言论，仅在以下两种情况下需对该群体中的个人成员承担责任：(1) 该群体规模极小，以至于理性人会认为该言论指向该成员；或 (2) 发表言论时的情境会使理性人得出同样的结论。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 564A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（针对群体或类别的诽谤）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"被告对一群人发表诽谤性言论，仅在以下情况下才对该群体中的个别成员承担责任：(1) 该群体规模极小，以至于任何理性的人都会认为该言论指的是该成员；或 (2) 言论的发布情况足以使任何理性的人得出同样的结论。","iracZh":{"issue":"法官是否有可能胜诉？","rule":"被告对一群人发表诽谤性言论，仅在以下情况下才对该群体中的个别成员承担责任：(1) 该群体规模极小，以至于任何理性的人都会认为该言论指的是该成员；或 (2) 言论的发布情况足以使任何理性的人得出同样的结论。","analysis":"当被告发表针对某一群体的诽谤性言论时，一般规则是该群体的任何成员均无法就诽谤提起诉讼并获得赔偿。\n\n这是因为听到或读到该言论的理性人不会认为其指向该群体中的特定成员。\n\n然而，如果出现以下情况，群体中的个人成员可以提起诽谤诉讼：该群体规模极小，以至于理性人会认为该言论指向该成员；或者 发表该言论时的具体情境会使理性人认为该言论指向该成员。\n\n在本案中，该男子告诉记者“法官”都是收受贿赂的骗子。\n\n尽管这一诽谤性言论未指名道姓，但该言论是在一个仅有一名法官的社区发表的，且发表时机正值该男子结束一场备受瞩目的庭审离开法院之际。\n\n因此，根据发表言论时的情境，有理由得出结论：该言论指向的是这名特定法官，而非泛指所有法官（选项 A）。\n\n正因如此，该法官很可能在诽谤诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）诽谤诉讼通常要求证明该言论给原告造成了“特别损害”（special damage），即通常意义上的金钱损失。\n\n然而，若言论构成“本身诽谤”（slander per se）——例如指控原告从事了对其职业产生不利影响的行为（本案即属此类）——则推定存在损害。\n\n（选项 D）当诽谤性言论被传达给第三方时，即构成“发表”（publication）。\n\n因此，本案中的发表行为发生在该男子向记者陈述该言论之时，而非该言论见诸报端之时。\n\n教学目标：被告若发表针对某一群体的诽谤性言论，仅在以下两种情况下需对该群体中的个人成员承担责任：(1) 该群体规模极小，以至于理性人会认为该言论指向该成员；或 (2) 发表言论时的情境会使理性人得出同样的结论。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 564A 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（针对群体或类别的诽谤）。","conclusion":"答案 C：可能，因为一个理性的人会认为该男子的言论指的是这位法官。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-926f84c7053b46f5"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":176,"sourceQuestionPage":527,"sourceQuestionPages":[527],"sourceExplanationPages":[528,529],"sourceAnswerPages":[528,529],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-177","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A popular sports magazine published an investigative article detailing the prohibited tactics used by a university football program to recruit and retain top players. The article stated that at least five players had received postgame cash from their coaches based on performance during the game. One of those players, the quarterback, was quoted as saying that he lived \"rent-free at the ranch of a booster club member\" who had told him, \"I'll pay you $400 a week to shoe horses and shovel manure.\" The quarterback was further quoted as saying, \"I never even touched a horse [at the ranch].\" Soon after the article was published, the booster club member was asked to leave the club because of the public outrage created by the article. The booster club member now seeks to file an action against the magazine, alleging that the quarterback did odd jobs to earn his keep and that the magazine had published the article with reckless disregard for its truthfulness. Under which theory would the booster club member be most likely to recover?","choices":{"A":"Intrusion upon seclusion.","B":"Public disclosure of private facts.","C":"Publicity in a false light.","D":"Slander."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Invasion of privacy\n\nIntrusion upon Highly offensive & intentional intrusion on plaintiff's solitude, seclusion seclusion, or private affairs\n\nAppropriation of Unauthorized use of plaintiff's name or likeness for personal name or likeness benefit—eg, commercial advantage\n\nPublic disclosure of Publicity given to highly offensive & private matter concerning private facts plaintiff that is not of legitimate public concern & results in damages\n\nPublicity in a false Publicity given to false information about plaintiff with actual light malice that places him/her in highly offensive & false light & results in damages\n\nInvasion of privacy based on publicity in a false light (recognized in most jurisdictions) requires the following elements: The defendant publicized false information that placed the plaintiff in a false light (eg, the magazine published an article falsely stating that the quarterback lived rent-free at the booster club member's ranch, which falsely portrayed the member as someone willing to engage in prohibited recruiting tactics).\n\nThe false light would be highly offensive to a reasonable person (eg, the public was outraged by the booster club member's engagement in prohibited tactics).\n\nThe publication was made with actual malice (eg, the magazine published the article with reckless disregard for its truthfulness).\n\nThe plaintiff suffered damages (eg, the booster club member was asked to leave the club).\n\nHere, all of the elements of publicity in a false light have been satisfied. Therefore, the booster club member is most likely to recover under a false-light theory. (Choice A) Intrusion upon seclusion requires a highly offensive intrusion on the plaintiff's solitude, seclusion, or private affairs. Here, the magazine interviewed the quarterback, who voluntarily spoke about his own housing arrangement. This means there was no intrusion upon the private affairs of the booster club member. (Choice B) Public disclosure of private facts is grounded in the disclosure of truthful information about the plaintiff. Here, the booster club member is alleging that the magazine article was false. Therefore, he is unlikely to recover for public disclosure of private facts.\n\n(Choice D) Defamation by words written, printed, or otherwise recorded in permanent form is libel. In contrast, defamation by spoken word, gesture, or any form other than libel is slander. Since the publication was made in a magazine article (printed word), it may constitute libel—but not slander.\n\nEducational objective: Invasion of privacy based on publicity in a false light requires proof that (1) the defendant publicized false information that placed the plaintiff in a false light, (2) the false light would be highly offensive to a reasonable person, (3) the publication was made with actual malice, and (4) the plaintiff suffered damages.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 652E (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (publicity placing person in false light).\n\nNew York Times Co. v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254, 279–280 (1964) (standard for actual malice).","educationalObjective":"Invasion of privacy based on publicity in a false light requires proof that (1) the defendant publicized false information that placed the plaintiff in a false light, (2) the false light would be highly offensive to a reasonable person, (3) the publication was made with actual malice, and (4) the plaintiff suffered damages.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 652E (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (publicity placing person in false light).\n\nNew York Times Co. v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254, 279–280 (1964) (standard for actual malice).","rule":"Invasion of privacy based on publicity in a false light requires proof that (1) the defendant publicized false information that placed the plaintiff in a false light, (2) the false light would be highly offensive to a reasonable person, (3) the publication was made with actual malice, and (4) the plaintiff suffered damages.","irac":{"issue":"Under which theory would the booster club member be most likely to recover?","rule":"Invasion of privacy based on publicity in a false light requires proof that (1) the defendant publicized false information that placed the plaintiff in a false light, (2) the false light would be highly offensive to a reasonable person, (3) the publication was made with actual malice, and (4) the plaintiff suffered damages.","analysis":"Invasion of privacy\n\nIntrusion upon Highly offensive & intentional intrusion on plaintiff's solitude, seclusion seclusion, or private affairs\n\nAppropriation of Unauthorized use of plaintiff's name or likeness for personal name or likeness benefit—eg, commercial advantage\n\nPublic disclosure of Publicity given to highly offensive & private matter concerning private facts plaintiff that is not of legitimate public concern & results in damages\n\nPublicity in a false Publicity given to false information about plaintiff with actual light malice that places him/her in highly offensive & false light & results in damages\n\nInvasion of privacy based on publicity in a false light (recognized in most jurisdictions) requires the following elements: The defendant publicized false information that placed the plaintiff in a false light (eg, the magazine published an article falsely stating that the quarterback lived rent-free at the booster club member's ranch, which falsely portrayed the member as someone willing to engage in prohibited recruiting tactics).\n\nThe false light would be highly offensive to a reasonable person (eg, the public was outraged by the booster club member's engagement in prohibited tactics).\n\nThe publication was made with actual malice (eg, the magazine published the article with reckless disregard for its truthfulness).\n\nThe plaintiff suffered damages (eg, the booster club member was asked to leave the club).\n\nHere, all of the elements of publicity in a false light have been satisfied. Therefore, the booster club member is most likely to recover under a false-light theory. (Choice A) Intrusion upon seclusion requires a highly offensive intrusion on the plaintiff's solitude, seclusion, or private affairs. Here, the magazine interviewed the quarterback, who voluntarily spoke about his own housing arrangement. This means there was no intrusion upon the private affairs of the booster club member. (Choice B) Public disclosure of private facts is grounded in the disclosure of truthful information about the plaintiff. Here, the booster club member is alleging that the magazine article was false. Therefore, he is unlikely to recover for public disclosure of private facts.\n\n(Choice D) Defamation by words written, printed, or otherwise recorded in permanent form is libel. In contrast, defamation by spoken word, gesture, or any form other than libel is slander. Since the publication was made in a magazine article (printed word), it may constitute libel—but not slander.","conclusion":"Answer C: Publicity in a false light."},"questionZh":"一家颇受欢迎的体育杂志刊登了一篇调查报道，详细披露了某大学橄榄球队在招募和留住顶尖球员时使用的违规手段。\n\n文章指出，至少有五名球员根据比赛表现从教练那里获得了赛后现金奖励。\n\n其中一名球员（四分卫）在受访时表示，他“免费寄宿在一位支持者俱乐部成员的牧场里”，该成员曾对他说：“我每周付你400美元，让你负责给马钉掌和清理粪便。”该四分卫还补充道：“我（在牧场）甚至连马都没碰过。”文章发表后引发了公众的强烈愤慨，该支持者俱乐部成员随即被要求退出俱乐部。\n\n该成员现打算起诉该杂志，声称该四分卫确实从事零工以换取食宿，而杂志在发表文章时罔顾事实真相，存在鲁莽不顾后果的行为。\n\n该支持者俱乐部成员最有可能依据哪种法律理论胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"侵入私人生活（Intrusion upon seclusion）。","B":"公开披露私人事实。","C":"虚假形象公开（Publicity in a false light）。","D":"口头诽谤（Slander）。"},"explanationZh":"侵犯隐私权 侵扰（Intrusion upon seclusion）：对原告的独处、隐居状态或私事进行的、具有高度冒犯性且蓄意的侵扰。 盗用（Appropriation）：未经授权使用原告的姓名或肖像以谋取个人利益（例如，获取商业优势）。 公开披露私人事实（Public disclosure of private facts）：公开披露关于原告的高度冒犯性私人事实，且该事实不属于正当的公众关注范畴，并导致了损害后果。 虚假形象公开（Publicity in a false light）：在具有“实际恶意”的情况下，公开披露关于原告的虚假信息，使其处于一种高度冒犯性且不实的形象之中，并导致了损害后果。 基于“虚假形象公开”的隐私侵权主张（大多数司法管辖区均予认可）需满足以下要件：被告公开了虚假信息，使原告处于虚假形象之中（例如，某杂志刊登文章，虚假声称某四分卫免费居住在支持者俱乐部成员的牧场里，从而将该成员描绘成愿意采取违规招募手段的人）。\n\n该虚假形象对理性人而言具有高度冒犯性（例如，公众对该支持者俱乐部成员采取违规手段的行为感到愤怒）。\n\n发布行为出于“实际恶意”（例如，杂志在发布文章时罔顾事实真伪，表现出鲁莽的漠视态度）。\n\n原告遭受了损害（例如，该支持者俱乐部成员被要求退出俱乐部）。\n\n在本案中，“虚假形象公开”的所有要件均已满足。\n\n因此，该支持者俱乐部成员极有可能依据“虚假形象公开”理论获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）“侵扰隐居状态”要求对原告的独处、隐居状态或私事进行高度冒犯性的侵扰。\n\n在本案中，杂志采访了四分卫，而该四分卫是自愿谈论其居住安排的。\n\n这意味着并未发生对该支持者俱乐部成员私事的侵扰。\n\n（选项 B）“公开披露私人事实”的依据是披露关于原告的真实信息。\n\n在本案中，该支持者俱乐部成员主张杂志文章内容不实。\n\n因此，他不太可能依据“公开披露私人事实”获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）以书写、印刷或其他永久形式记录的言辞构成的诽谤属于“书面诽谤”（libel）。\n\n相比之下，通过口头言辞、手势或除书面诽谤以外的任何形式构成的诽谤则属于“口头诽谤”（slander）。\n\n由于该内容是通过杂志文章（印刷文字）发布的，因此可能构成“书面诽谤”，但不构成“口头诽谤”。\n\n教学目标：基于“虚假形象”（false light）公开而构成的侵犯隐私权，须证明：(1) 被告公开了虚假信息，使原告呈现出一种虚假形象；(2) 这种虚假形象会使理性人感到极度反感；(3) 该公开行为出于“实际恶意”（actual malice）；以及 (4) 原告遭受了损害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 652E 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于使人呈现虚假形象的公开行为）。\n\n*New York Times Co. v. Sullivan* 案，376 U.S. 254, 279–280 (1964)（关于“实际恶意”的认定标准）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于“虚假形象公开”的侵犯隐私权行为，需要证明以下几点：(1) 被告公开了虚假信息，使原告呈现出一种虚假形象；(2) 这种虚假形象会令理性人感到极度反感；(3) 发布行为出于“实际恶意”（actual malice）；以及 (4) 原告遭受了损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"该支持者俱乐部成员最有可能依据哪种法律理论胜诉？","rule":"基于“虚假形象公开”的侵犯隐私权行为，需要证明以下几点：(1) 被告公开了虚假信息，使原告呈现出一种虚假形象；(2) 这种虚假形象会令理性人感到极度反感；(3) 发布行为出于“实际恶意”（actual malice）；以及 (4) 原告遭受了损害。","analysis":"侵犯隐私权 侵扰（Intrusion upon seclusion）：对原告的独处、隐居状态或私事进行的、具有高度冒犯性且蓄意的侵扰。 盗用（Appropriation）：未经授权使用原告的姓名或肖像以谋取个人利益（例如，获取商业优势）。 公开披露私人事实（Public disclosure of private facts）：公开披露关于原告的高度冒犯性私人事实，且该事实不属于正当的公众关注范畴，并导致了损害后果。 虚假形象公开（Publicity in a false light）：在具有“实际恶意”的情况下，公开披露关于原告的虚假信息，使其处于一种高度冒犯性且不实的形象之中，并导致了损害后果。 基于“虚假形象公开”的隐私侵权主张（大多数司法管辖区均予认可）需满足以下要件：被告公开了虚假信息，使原告处于虚假形象之中（例如，某杂志刊登文章，虚假声称某四分卫免费居住在支持者俱乐部成员的牧场里，从而将该成员描绘成愿意采取违规招募手段的人）。\n\n该虚假形象对理性人而言具有高度冒犯性（例如，公众对该支持者俱乐部成员采取违规手段的行为感到愤怒）。\n\n发布行为出于“实际恶意”（例如，杂志在发布文章时罔顾事实真伪，表现出鲁莽的漠视态度）。\n\n原告遭受了损害（例如，该支持者俱乐部成员被要求退出俱乐部）。\n\n在本案中，“虚假形象公开”的所有要件均已满足。\n\n因此，该支持者俱乐部成员极有可能依据“虚假形象公开”理论获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）“侵扰隐居状态”要求对原告的独处、隐居状态或私事进行高度冒犯性的侵扰。\n\n在本案中，杂志采访了四分卫，而该四分卫是自愿谈论其居住安排的。\n\n这意味着并未发生对该支持者俱乐部成员私事的侵扰。\n\n（选项 B）“公开披露私人事实”的依据是披露关于原告的真实信息。\n\n在本案中，该支持者俱乐部成员主张杂志文章内容不实。\n\n因此，他不太可能依据“公开披露私人事实”获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）以书写、印刷或其他永久形式记录的言辞构成的诽谤属于“书面诽谤”（libel）。\n\n相比之下，通过口头言辞、手势或除书面诽谤以外的任何形式构成的诽谤则属于“口头诽谤”（slander）。\n\n由于该内容是通过杂志文章（印刷文字）发布的，因此可能构成“书面诽谤”，但不构成“口头诽谤”。\n\n教学目标：基于“虚假形象”（false light）公开而构成的侵犯隐私权，须证明：(1) 被告公开了虚假信息，使原告呈现出一种虚假形象；(2) 这种虚假形象会使理性人感到极度反感；(3) 该公开行为出于“实际恶意”（actual malice）；以及 (4) 原告遭受了损害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 652E 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于使人呈现虚假形象的公开行为）。\n\n*New York Times Co. v. Sullivan* 案，376 U.S. 254, 279–280 (1964)（关于“实际恶意”的认定标准）。","conclusion":"答案 C：虚假形象公开（Publicity in a false light）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":177,"sourceQuestionPage":530,"sourceQuestionPages":[530],"sourceExplanationPages":[531,532],"sourceAnswerPages":[531,532],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-178","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A disgruntled science teacher had recently been fired from her job at the local high school. She told a parent, who was the communications chairperson for the high school's parent-teacher organization, that the principal was having an affair with the married assistant principal. The parent immediately sent a group email to all of the parents at the high school that included the following statement: \"I'm not sure if this is true or not, but the science teacher said that the principal is having an affair with the assistant principal!\" The principal, who was not having an affair with the assistant principal, subsequently sued the parent for libel. Is the principal likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the parent expressed a lack of knowledge as to the truthfulness of the science teacher's statement.","B":"No, because the parent identified the science teacher as the originator of the statement.","C":"Yes, because the original statement made by the science teacher was false.","D":"Yes, because the parent repeated a defamatory statement."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Libel is defamation in words that are written, printed, or otherwise recorded in permanent form. Most courts addressing the issue have held that email messages are categorized as libel. A libel claim requires proof of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nThe statement was defamatory, meaning that the statement would have a general tendency to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant published the statement by intentionally or negligently communicating it to a third party.\n\nA third party who repeats (ie, republishes) a defamatory statement to someone else is independently liable for defamation. This is true even if the republisher identifies the originator of the statement and expresses a lack of knowledge as to its truthfulness.* Here, the science teacher's statement to the parent, whether knowingly or negligently made, was false and harmful to the principal's reputation at the school. The parent then repeated the defamatory statement to all of the parents at the high school via email. Although the parent identified the science teacher as the statement's originator and expressed a lack of knowledge as to its truthfulness, the republication makes the parent liable for defamation. Therefore, the principal is likely to prevail in his libel suit (Choices A, B & C). *Note that secondary publishers who merely disseminate material published by others (eg, bookstores, newspaper vendors) are liable only if they know or should know of the defamatory statements in the material.\n\nEducational objective: A third party who repeats (ie, republishes) a defamatory statement to someone else is liable for defamation even if the republisher identifies the originator of the statement and expresses a lack of knowledge as to its truthfulness.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 578 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (republisher's liability for defamation).","educationalObjective":"A third party who repeats (ie, republishes) a defamatory statement to someone else is liable for defamation even if the republisher identifies the originator of the statement and expresses a lack of knowledge as to its truthfulness.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 578 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (republisher's liability for defamation).","rule":"A third party who repeats (ie, republishes) a defamatory statement to someone else is liable for defamation even if the republisher identifies the originator of the statement and expresses a lack of knowledge as to its truthfulness.","irac":{"issue":"Is the principal likely to prevail?","rule":"A third party who repeats (ie, republishes) a defamatory statement to someone else is liable for defamation even if the republisher identifies the originator of the statement and expresses a lack of knowledge as to its truthfulness.","analysis":"Libel is defamation in words that are written, printed, or otherwise recorded in permanent form. Most courts addressing the issue have held that email messages are categorized as libel. A libel claim requires proof of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nThe statement was defamatory, meaning that the statement would have a general tendency to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant published the statement by intentionally or negligently communicating it to a third party.\n\nA third party who repeats (ie, republishes) a defamatory statement to someone else is independently liable for defamation. This is true even if the republisher identifies the originator of the statement and expresses a lack of knowledge as to its truthfulness.* Here, the science teacher's statement to the parent, whether knowingly or negligently made, was false and harmful to the principal's reputation at the school. The parent then repeated the defamatory statement to all of the parents at the high school via email. Although the parent identified the science teacher as the statement's originator and expressed a lack of knowledge as to its truthfulness, the republication makes the parent liable for defamation. Therefore, the principal is likely to prevail in his libel suit (Choices A, B & C). *Note that secondary publishers who merely disseminate material published by others (eg, bookstores, newspaper vendors) are liable only if they know or should know of the defamatory statements in the material.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the parent repeated a defamatory statement."},"questionZh":"一位心怀不满的科学老师最近被当地高中解雇了。\n\n她告诉一位家长（该家长是学校家长教师协会的联络主席），校长与已婚的副校长有染。\n\n这位家长立即向全校家长群发了一封邮件，邮件中包含以下声明：“我不确定这是不是真的，但那位科学老师说校长和副校长有染！” 校长并没有与副校长有染，随后他以诽谤罪起诉了这位家长。\n\n校长有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为这位家长表示她并不了解科学老师所说的话是否属实。","B":"不可能，因为这位家长指认科学老师是该声明的始作俑者。","C":"可能胜诉，因为科学老师最初的说法是错误的。","D":"是的，因为家长重复了诽谤性言论。"},"explanationZh":"书面诽谤（Libel）是指以书写、印刷或其他永久形式记录下来的诽谤性言论。\n\n大多数审理此类案件的法院都认定电子邮件属于书面诽谤。\n\n提出书面诽谤索赔需要证明以下几点：被告明知故犯地针对原告发表了虚假陈述，或者因疏忽大意而未能核实其虚假性。\n\n该陈述具有诽谤性质，即该陈述通常会损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告通过故意或疏忽的方式将该陈述传达给第三方，从而发布了该陈述。\n\n第三方若向他人重复（即再次发布）诽谤性陈述，须就该诽谤行为承担独立责任。\n\n即使再次发布者指明了陈述的原始发布者，并声明自己不了解陈述内容的真实性，这一原则依然适用。* 在本案中，科学老师向家长所作的陈述——无论是有意还是疏忽——均属虚假，且损害了校长在学校的名誉。\n\n随后，该家长通过电子邮件向该高中的所有家长重复了这一诽谤性陈述。\n\n尽管该家长指明了科学老师是陈述的原始发布者，并声明不了解其真实性，但这种再次发布行为仍使该家长须承担诽谤责任。\n\n因此，校长很可能在书面诽谤诉讼中胜诉（选项 A、B 和 C）。\n\n*注意：仅传播他人已发布材料的次级发布者（例如书店、报摊），只有在明知或应知材料中包含诽谤性陈述的情况下，才须承担责任。\n\n教育目标：第三方若向他人重复（即再次发布）诽谤性陈述，须就该诽谤行为承担责任；即使再次发布者指明了陈述的原始发布者，并声明不了解其真实性，这一责任依然成立。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 578 条（美国法律协会，1981 年）（关于再次发布者诽谤责任的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第三方重复（即转载）诽谤性言论给他人，即使转载者指明了言论的来源并表示不了解其真实性，也应承担诽谤责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"委托人胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"第三方重复（即转载）诽谤性言论给他人，即使转载者指明了言论的来源并表示不了解其真实性，也应承担诽谤责任。","analysis":"书面诽谤（Libel）是指以书写、印刷或其他永久形式记录下来的诽谤性言论。\n\n大多数审理此类案件的法院都认定电子邮件属于书面诽谤。\n\n提出书面诽谤索赔需要证明以下几点：被告明知故犯地针对原告发表了虚假陈述，或者因疏忽大意而未能核实其虚假性。\n\n该陈述具有诽谤性质，即该陈述通常会损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告通过故意或疏忽的方式将该陈述传达给第三方，从而发布了该陈述。\n\n第三方若向他人重复（即再次发布）诽谤性陈述，须就该诽谤行为承担独立责任。\n\n即使再次发布者指明了陈述的原始发布者，并声明自己不了解陈述内容的真实性，这一原则依然适用。* 在本案中，科学老师向家长所作的陈述——无论是有意还是疏忽——均属虚假，且损害了校长在学校的名誉。\n\n随后，该家长通过电子邮件向该高中的所有家长重复了这一诽谤性陈述。\n\n尽管该家长指明了科学老师是陈述的原始发布者，并声明不了解其真实性，但这种再次发布行为仍使该家长须承担诽谤责任。\n\n因此，校长很可能在书面诽谤诉讼中胜诉（选项 A、B 和 C）。\n\n*注意：仅传播他人已发布材料的次级发布者（例如书店、报摊），只有在明知或应知材料中包含诽谤性陈述的情况下，才须承担责任。\n\n教育目标：第三方若向他人重复（即再次发布）诽谤性陈述，须就该诽谤行为承担责任；即使再次发布者指明了陈述的原始发布者，并声明不了解其真实性，这一责任依然成立。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 578 条（美国法律协会，1981 年）（关于再次发布者诽谤责任的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为家长重复了诽谤性言论。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"publishers","ipa":"/pˈəblɪʃɝz/","syllables":"pub·lish·ers","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 出版商, 出版公司；出版社（publisher的复数）","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“publishers”","wordForm":"词；原形 publisher；词形 0:publisher/1:s","sourceSentence":"*Note that secondary publishers who merely disseminate material published by others (eg, bookstores, newspaper vendors) are liable only if they know or should know of the defamatory statements in the material.","synonyms":["publishing house","publishing firm","publishing company","newspaper publisher"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-28e81e847a555ac7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":178,"sourceQuestionPage":533,"sourceQuestionPages":[533],"sourceExplanationPages":[534,535],"sourceAnswerPages":[534,535],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-179","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"The governor of State A signed a death warrant for a convicted murderer. At a demonstration protesting the execution of the murderer, two opponents of the death penalty carried large signs that stated, \"The governor is a murderer!\" A television station broadcasted news coverage of the demonstration, including pictures of the signs carried by the two protestors. The broadcast caused the governor severe emotional distress. If the governor asserts a defamation claim against the television station, will he prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, if the only reasonable interpretation of the signs was that the term \"murderer\" was intended as a characterization of one who would sign a death warrant.","B":"No, unless the governor proves he suffered pecuniary loss resulting from harm to his reputation proximately caused by the protestors' signs.","C":"Yes, because the signs would cause persons to hold the governor in lower esteem.","D":"Yes, if the governor proves that the television station showed the signs with knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard of the truth that the governor had not committed homicide."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Defamatory statements\n\nStatements that would tend to harm plaintiff's reputation by either:\n\nlowering plaintiff's standing in community (eg, allegations challenging plaintiff's integrity or financial reputation) or deterring third parties from associating or dealing with plaintiff (eg, allegations that plaintiff is insane or diseased) Defamation can be either slander (spoken statements) OR libel (statements made in physical form or that appear on radio or television—as seen here). When a defamation claim based on libel is brought by a public official or public figure, he/she must prove all of the following: The defendant made a false statement about the plaintiff with actual malice—knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity.\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party.\n\nA statement would only tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation if a third party's reasonable interpretation of that statement would tend to do so. Here, the television station broadcasted pictures of protestors' signs that stated, \"The governor is a murderer!\" But those signs would not tend to harm the governor's reputation if they could only be reasonably interpreted to mean that a person who signs a death warrant should be characterized as a murderer—not that the governor actually committed murder. In that case, the governor's defamation claim would fail. (Choice B) Libel does not require proof of pecuniary loss (ie, financial harm). Instead, the governor's claim would fail if a reasonable interpretation of the protestors' signs would not harm his reputation. (Choice C) Even if the signs would cause persons to hold the governor in lower esteem (ie, harm his reputation), the governor (public official) can only prevail if he also proves that the television station acted with actual malice. (Choice D) Assuming that the governor proves that the television station knew that the signs were false or recklessly disregarded their falsity (actual malice), he would still have to prove that a third party's reasonable interpretation of those signs would tend to harm his reputation.\n\nEducational objective: A plaintiff can only recover for libel (defamation in physical form or that appears on radio or television) if a third party's reasonable interpretation of the defendant's statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 568 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (libel and slander distinguished).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 580A (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defamation of public official or public figure).","educationalObjective":"A plaintiff can only recover for libel (defamation in physical form or that appears on radio or television) if a third party's reasonable interpretation of the defendant's statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 568 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (libel and slander distinguished).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 580A (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defamation of public official or public figure).","rule":"A plaintiff can only recover for libel (defamation in physical form or that appears on radio or television) if a third party's reasonable interpretation of the defendant's statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.","irac":{"issue":"If the governor asserts a defamation claim against the television station, will he prevail?","rule":"A plaintiff can only recover for libel (defamation in physical form or that appears on radio or television) if a third party's reasonable interpretation of the defendant's statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.","analysis":"Defamatory statements\n\nStatements that would tend to harm plaintiff's reputation by either:\n\nlowering plaintiff's standing in community (eg, allegations challenging plaintiff's integrity or financial reputation) or deterring third parties from associating or dealing with plaintiff (eg, allegations that plaintiff is insane or diseased) Defamation can be either slander (spoken statements) OR libel (statements made in physical form or that appear on radio or television—as seen here). When a defamation claim based on libel is brought by a public official or public figure, he/she must prove all of the following: The defendant made a false statement about the plaintiff with actual malice—knowledge or reckless disregard of the statement's falsity.\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party.\n\nA statement would only tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation if a third party's reasonable interpretation of that statement would tend to do so. Here, the television station broadcasted pictures of protestors' signs that stated, \"The governor is a murderer!\" But those signs would not tend to harm the governor's reputation if they could only be reasonably interpreted to mean that a person who signs a death warrant should be characterized as a murderer—not that the governor actually committed murder. In that case, the governor's defamation claim would fail. (Choice B) Libel does not require proof of pecuniary loss (ie, financial harm). Instead, the governor's claim would fail if a reasonable interpretation of the protestors' signs would not harm his reputation. (Choice C) Even if the signs would cause persons to hold the governor in lower esteem (ie, harm his reputation), the governor (public official) can only prevail if he also proves that the television station acted with actual malice. (Choice D) Assuming that the governor proves that the television station knew that the signs were false or recklessly disregarded their falsity (actual malice), he would still have to prove that a third party's reasonable interpretation of those signs would tend to harm his reputation.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, if the only reasonable interpretation of the signs was that the term \"murderer\" was intended as a characterization of one who would sign a death warrant."},"questionZh":"A州州长签署了一份对一名已定罪谋杀犯的死刑执行令。\n\n在一次抗议处决该谋杀犯的示威活动中，两名反对死刑的人举着写有“州长是杀人犯！”的大牌子。\n\n一家电视台对示威活动进行了新闻报道，其中包括两名抗议者举着的牌子的照片。\n\n该报道给州长造成了严重的精神创伤。\n\n如果州长对电视台提起诽谤诉讼，他会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，如果对牌子的唯一合理解释是“杀人犯”一词意在描述签署死刑执行令的人。","B":"不会，除非州长能够证明，抗议者的牌子直接损害了他的名誉，导致他遭受了经济损失。","C":"会，因为这些牌子会降低人们对州长的评价。","D":"是的，如果州长能够证明电视台明知其言论虚假或罔顾事实，仍然播放州长未犯谋杀罪的画面。"},"explanationZh":"诽谤性陈述 此类陈述往往会损害原告的声誉，具体表现为： 降低原告在社区中的地位（例如，质疑原告诚信或财务信誉的指控），或阻碍第三方与原告交往或进行交易（例如，声称原告精神失常或患有疾病）。诽谤既可以是口头诽谤（slander，即口头陈述），也可以是书面诽谤（libel，即以实物形式呈现或在广播、电视上播出的陈述——本案即属此类）。\n\n当公职人员或公众人物提起书面诽谤诉讼时，必须证明以下所有要件：被告出于“实际恶意”（actual malice）——即明知陈述不实或罔顾陈述不实的事实——作出了关于原告的不实陈述。\n\n该类陈述往往会损害原告的声誉。\n\n被告故意或过失地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n只有当第三方对该陈述的合理理解倾向于损害原告声誉时，该陈述才会被视为具有损害原告声誉的倾向。\n\n在本案中，电视台播放了抗议者标语的画面，标语上写着“州长是杀人犯！”。\n\n然而，如果这些标语的合理理解仅限于“签署死刑执行令的人应被定性为杀人犯”——而非指控州长本人实施了谋杀行为——那么这些标语便不会损害州长的声誉。\n\n在这种情况下，州长的诽谤诉讼将无法成立。\n\n（选项 B）书面诽谤不要求证明金钱损失（即经济上的损害）。\n\n相反，如果对抗议者标语的合理理解不会损害州长的声誉，则其诉讼将无法成立。\n\n（选项 C）即使标语会导致人们降低对州长的评价（即损害其声誉），作为公职人员的州长也只有在证明电视台存在“实际恶意”的情况下，才能胜诉。\n\n（选项 D）假设州长证明了电视台明知标语内容不实或罔顾其不实性（即存在实际恶意），他仍需证明第三方对这些标语的合理理解会损害其声誉。\n\n教学目标：原告仅在第三方对被告陈述的合理理解倾向于损害原告名誉的情况下，方可就书面诽谤（即以有形形式呈现，或在广播、电视上播出的诽谤）获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第558条（美国法律协会，1977年）（诽谤的构成要素）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第568条（美国法律协会，1977年）（书面诽谤与口头诽谤的区分）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第580A条（美国法律协会，1977年）（针对公职人员或公众人物的诽谤）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只有当第三方对被告言论的合理解读会损害原告名誉时，原告才能就诽谤（以书面形式或通过广播电视传播的诽谤）获得赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果州长对电视台提起诽谤诉讼，他会胜诉吗？","rule":"只有当第三方对被告言论的合理解读会损害原告名誉时，原告才能就诽谤（以书面形式或通过广播电视传播的诽谤）获得赔偿。","analysis":"诽谤性陈述 此类陈述往往会损害原告的声誉，具体表现为： 降低原告在社区中的地位（例如，质疑原告诚信或财务信誉的指控），或阻碍第三方与原告交往或进行交易（例如，声称原告精神失常或患有疾病）。诽谤既可以是口头诽谤（slander，即口头陈述），也可以是书面诽谤（libel，即以实物形式呈现或在广播、电视上播出的陈述——本案即属此类）。\n\n当公职人员或公众人物提起书面诽谤诉讼时，必须证明以下所有要件：被告出于“实际恶意”（actual malice）——即明知陈述不实或罔顾陈述不实的事实——作出了关于原告的不实陈述。\n\n该类陈述往往会损害原告的声誉。\n\n被告故意或过失地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n只有当第三方对该陈述的合理理解倾向于损害原告声誉时，该陈述才会被视为具有损害原告声誉的倾向。\n\n在本案中，电视台播放了抗议者标语的画面，标语上写着“州长是杀人犯！”。\n\n然而，如果这些标语的合理理解仅限于“签署死刑执行令的人应被定性为杀人犯”——而非指控州长本人实施了谋杀行为——那么这些标语便不会损害州长的声誉。\n\n在这种情况下，州长的诽谤诉讼将无法成立。\n\n（选项 B）书面诽谤不要求证明金钱损失（即经济上的损害）。\n\n相反，如果对抗议者标语的合理理解不会损害州长的声誉，则其诉讼将无法成立。\n\n（选项 C）即使标语会导致人们降低对州长的评价（即损害其声誉），作为公职人员的州长也只有在证明电视台存在“实际恶意”的情况下，才能胜诉。\n\n（选项 D）假设州长证明了电视台明知标语内容不实或罔顾其不实性（即存在实际恶意），他仍需证明第三方对这些标语的合理理解会损害其声誉。\n\n教学目标：原告仅在第三方对被告陈述的合理理解倾向于损害原告名誉的情况下，方可就书面诽谤（即以有形形式呈现，或在广播、电视上播出的诽谤）获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第558条（美国法律协会，1977年）（诽谤的构成要素）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第568条（美国法律协会，1977年）（书面诽谤与口头诽谤的区分）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第580A条（美国法律协会，1977年）（针对公职人员或公众人物的诽谤）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，如果对牌子的唯一合理解释是“杀人犯”一词意在描述签署死刑执行令的人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":179,"sourceQuestionPage":536,"sourceQuestionPages":[536],"sourceExplanationPages":[537,538],"sourceAnswerPages":[537,538],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-180","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A well-known movie star was drinking a vineyard's wine at a nightclub. A bottle of the vineyard's wine, with its label plainly showing, was on the table in front of the movie star. An amateur photographer asked the movie star if he could take his picture and the movie star said, \"Yes.\" Subsequently, the photographer sold the photo to the vineyard. The vineyard, without the movie star's consent, used the photo in a wine advertisement in a nationally circulated magazine. The caption below the photo stated, \"A movie star enjoys the vineyard's wine.\" If the movie star sues the vineyard to recover damages as a result of the vineyard's use of the photograph, will the movie star prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the movie star consented to being photographed.","B":"No, because the movie star is a public figure.","C":"Yes, because the vineyard made commercial use of the photograph.","D":"Yes, unless the movie star did, in fact, enjoy the vineyard's wine."},"answer":"C","explanation":"To recover damages for an invasion of privacy claim based on appropriation of name or likeness (ie, misappropriation of the right to publicity), the plaintiff must prove that the defendant: used the plaintiff's picture, name, or likeness without authorization and obtained a benefit (typically a commercial advantage—eg, advertising) from that use. Here, the vineyard used the movie star's photo in an advertisement in a nationally circulated magazine. Although the movie star consented to being photographed, he did not authorize the vineyard to use his photo (Choice A). And since the vineyard made commercial use of that photo (wine advertisement) without authorization, the movie star will recover damages from the vineyard. (Choice B) Public figures have the same right to control the use of their identities as anyone else, so the fact that the movie star is a public figure does not preclude his recovery. Instead, this fact strengthens his claim by establishing the commercial value (ie, benefit) of using his identity. (Choice D) Truth is a defense to an invasion of privacy claim based on publicity in false light, which requires proof that the defendant published false information about the plaintiff. But truth is no defense here since falsity is not an element of appropriation of name or likeness. Therefore, the movie star will recover even if the photo caption truthfully claimed that he enjoyed the vineyard's wine.\n\nEducational objective: Invasion of privacy based on appropriation of name or likeness requires proof that the defendant (1) used the plaintiff's name, picture, or likeness without his/her authorization and (2) obtained a benefit—typically a commercial advantage.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 652C (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (appropriation of name or likeness).","educationalObjective":"Invasion of privacy based on appropriation of name or likeness requires proof that the defendant (1) used the plaintiff's name, picture, or likeness without his/her authorization and (2) obtained a benefit—typically a commercial advantage.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 652C (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (appropriation of name or likeness).","rule":"Invasion of privacy based on appropriation of name or likeness requires proof that the defendant (1) used the plaintiff's name, picture, or likeness without his/her authorization and (2) obtained a benefit—typically a commercial advantage.","irac":{"issue":"A well-known movie star was drinking a vineyard's wine at a nightclub. A bottle of the vineyard's wine, with its label plainly showing, was on the table in front of the movie star. An amateur photographer asked the movie star if he could take his picture and the movie star said, \"Yes.\" Subsequently, the photographer sold the photo to the vineyard. The vineyard, without the movie star's consent, used the photo in a wine advertisement in a nationally circulated magazine. The caption below the photo stated, \"A movie star enjoys the vineyard's wine.\" If the movie star sues the vineyard to recover damages as a result of the vineyard's use of the photograph, will the movie star prevail?","rule":"Invasion of privacy based on appropriation of name or likeness requires proof that the defendant (1) used the plaintiff's name, picture, or likeness without his/her authorization and (2) obtained a benefit—typically a commercial advantage.","analysis":"To recover damages for an invasion of privacy claim based on appropriation of name or likeness (ie, misappropriation of the right to publicity), the plaintiff must prove that the defendant: used the plaintiff's picture, name, or likeness without authorization and obtained a benefit (typically a commercial advantage—eg, advertising) from that use. Here, the vineyard used the movie star's photo in an advertisement in a nationally circulated magazine. Although the movie star consented to being photographed, he did not authorize the vineyard to use his photo (Choice A). And since the vineyard made commercial use of that photo (wine advertisement) without authorization, the movie star will recover damages from the vineyard. (Choice B) Public figures have the same right to control the use of their identities as anyone else, so the fact that the movie star is a public figure does not preclude his recovery. Instead, this fact strengthens his claim by establishing the commercial value (ie, benefit) of using his identity. (Choice D) Truth is a defense to an invasion of privacy claim based on publicity in false light, which requires proof that the defendant published false information about the plaintiff. But truth is no defense here since falsity is not an element of appropriation of name or likeness. Therefore, the movie star will recover even if the photo caption truthfully claimed that he enjoyed the vineyard's wine.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the vineyard made commercial use of the photograph."},"questionZh":"一位知名影星在夜总会饮用某酒庄的葡萄酒。\n\n影星面前的桌上放着一瓶该酒庄的葡萄酒，酒标清晰可见。\n\n一位业余摄影师询问是否可以为影星拍照，影星表示同意。\n\n随后，摄影师将照片卖给了该酒庄。\n\n酒庄在未经影星同意的情况下，将该照片用于一份全国性杂志上的葡萄酒广告。\n\n照片下方的说明文字写道：“一位影星正在享用该酒庄的葡萄酒。”如果影星因酒庄使用该照片而起诉索赔，他能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为影星同意了被拍照。","B":"不能，因为影星是公众人物。","C":"能，因为酒庄对照片进行了商业性使用。","D":"能，除非影星实际上确实享用了该酒庄的葡萄酒。"},"explanationZh":"若要就“擅自使用姓名或肖像”（即“滥用公开权”）这一侵犯隐私权主张获得损害赔偿，原告必须证明被告：未经授权使用了原告的照片、姓名或肖像，并从中获得了利益（通常为商业利益，例如用于广告）。\n\n在本案中，该葡萄园在全国性杂志的广告中使用了这位电影明星的照片。\n\n尽管该电影明星同意拍摄照片，但他并未授权葡萄园使用该照片（选项 A）。\n\n由于葡萄园未经授权对该照片进行了商业使用（即用于葡萄酒广告），该电影明星将有权从葡萄园获得损害赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）公众人物与普通人一样，拥有控制其身份信息被使用的权利；因此，该电影明星作为公众人物这一事实并不妨碍其获得赔偿。\n\n相反，这一事实通过确立使用其身份所带来的商业价值（即利益），反而增强了其主张的力度。\n\n（选项 D）“事实真实性”是针对“将原告置于虚假形象之下”（false light）这一侵犯隐私权主张的抗辩理由，而该类主张要求证明被告发布了关于原告的虚假信息。\n\n但在本案中，事实真实性不能作为抗辩理由，因为“虚假性”并非构成“擅自使用姓名或肖像”这一侵权行为的必要要件。\n\n因此，即使照片说明文字如实声称该电影明星喜爱该葡萄园的葡萄酒，他仍有权获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：“擅自使用姓名或肖像”类侵犯隐私权行为要求证明被告：(1) 未经授权使用了原告的姓名、照片或肖像；以及 (2) 获得了利益——通常为商业利益。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 652C 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于擅自使用姓名或肖像的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于擅自使用姓名或肖像而构成的侵犯隐私权，要求证明被告：(1) 未经原告授权使用了其姓名、照片或肖像；以及 (2) 获得了某种利益——通常是商业利益。","iracZh":{"issue":"一位知名影星在夜总会饮用某酒庄的葡萄酒。\n\n影星面前的桌上放着一瓶该酒庄的葡萄酒，酒标清晰可见。\n\n一位业余摄影师询问是否可以为影星拍照，影星表示同意。\n\n随后，摄影师将照片卖给了该酒庄。\n\n酒庄在未经影星同意的情况下，将该照片用于一份全国性杂志上的葡萄酒广告。\n\n照片下方的说明文字写道：“一位影星正在享用该酒庄的葡萄酒。”如果影星因酒庄使用该照片而起诉索赔，他能胜诉吗？","rule":"基于擅自使用姓名或肖像而构成的侵犯隐私权，要求证明被告：(1) 未经原告授权使用了其姓名、照片或肖像；以及 (2) 获得了某种利益——通常是商业利益。","analysis":"若要就“擅自使用姓名或肖像”（即“滥用公开权”）这一侵犯隐私权主张获得损害赔偿，原告必须证明被告：未经授权使用了原告的照片、姓名或肖像，并从中获得了利益（通常为商业利益，例如用于广告）。\n\n在本案中，该葡萄园在全国性杂志的广告中使用了这位电影明星的照片。\n\n尽管该电影明星同意拍摄照片，但他并未授权葡萄园使用该照片（选项 A）。\n\n由于葡萄园未经授权对该照片进行了商业使用（即用于葡萄酒广告），该电影明星将有权从葡萄园获得损害赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）公众人物与普通人一样，拥有控制其身份信息被使用的权利；因此，该电影明星作为公众人物这一事实并不妨碍其获得赔偿。\n\n相反，这一事实通过确立使用其身份所带来的商业价值（即利益），反而增强了其主张的力度。\n\n（选项 D）“事实真实性”是针对“将原告置于虚假形象之下”（false light）这一侵犯隐私权主张的抗辩理由，而该类主张要求证明被告发布了关于原告的虚假信息。\n\n但在本案中，事实真实性不能作为抗辩理由，因为“虚假性”并非构成“擅自使用姓名或肖像”这一侵权行为的必要要件。\n\n因此，即使照片说明文字如实声称该电影明星喜爱该葡萄园的葡萄酒，他仍有权获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：“擅自使用姓名或肖像”类侵犯隐私权行为要求证明被告：(1) 未经授权使用了原告的姓名、照片或肖像；以及 (2) 获得了利益——通常为商业利益。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 652C 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于擅自使用姓名或肖像的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 C：能，因为酒庄对照片进行了商业性使用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-ef2a43cf62c60d26"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":180,"sourceQuestionPage":539,"sourceQuestionPages":[539],"sourceExplanationPages":[540,541],"sourceAnswerPages":[540,541],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-181","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A 15-year-old boy was killed during a gang fight. Two days after his funeral, the boy's mother saw a television program about gang violence and was shocked to see video of herself weeping over the boy's body. The video had been shot by the television reporting team while the boy's body was still lying on a public street. The mother suffered severe emotional distress as a result of seeing the video. If the mother sues the television station for invasion of her privacy and that of her son, will the mother be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a person has no right to privacy after his or her death.","B":"No, because the street was open to the public and the subject was newsworthy.","C":"Yes, because the mother did not give permission to have the video used in the program.","D":"Yes, because the mother suffered severe emotional distress as a result of viewing the video."},"answer":"B","explanation":"There are four theories of recovery for invasion of privacy: intrusion upon seclusion – an intentional intrusion (physical or otherwise) on the plaintiff's solitude, seclusion, or private affairs that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person\n\npublic disclosure of private facts – publicity given to the plaintiff's private matter (1) that does not address a legitimate public concern, (2) would be highly offensive to a reasonable person and (3) causes damages\n\nappropriation of name or likeness – the unauthorized use of the plaintiff's name, picture, or likeness for a personal benefit (e.g., a monetary advantage)\n\npublicity in a false light – publicity given to false information about the plaintiff with actual malice that places him/her in a false light that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person and causes damages\n\nHere, the woman's invasion of privacy claim will likely fail because the video was taken when the woman and son were on a public street, so it did not intrude upon their private affairs. Additionally, the video was used to present a newsworthy topic and address a legitimate public concern—not for personal benefit or to present false information. (Choice A) A person has a right to privacy against the appropriation of his/her name or likeness—even after death. But since the television station did not use her son's likeness for personal benefit (e.g., for an advertisement), the mother, suing on her dead son's behalf, will not prevail. (Choice C) Although the mother did not give the television station permission to use the video in its program, her consent was unnecessary because she was in a public place and the video addressed a newsworthy issue. (Choice D) Evidence of emotional distress alone is insufficient to prove invasion of privacy. The mother must also show some type of intrusion, publicity, or unauthorized use.\n\nEducational objective: The four theories of recovery for invasion of privacy are: (1) intrusion upon seclusion, (2) appropriation of name or likeness, (3) public disclosure of private facts, and (4) publicity in a false light.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 652A–652E (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (invasion of privacy).","educationalObjective":"The four theories of recovery for invasion of privacy are: (1) intrusion upon seclusion, (2) appropriation of name or likeness, (3) public disclosure of private facts, and (4) publicity in a false light.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 652A–652E (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (invasion of privacy).","rule":"The four theories of recovery for invasion of privacy are: (1) intrusion upon seclusion, (2) appropriation of name or likeness, (3) public disclosure of private facts, and (4) publicity in a false light.","irac":{"issue":"If the mother sues the television station for invasion of her privacy and that of her son, will the mother be likely to prevail?","rule":"The four theories of recovery for invasion of privacy are: (1) intrusion upon seclusion, (2) appropriation of name or likeness, (3) public disclosure of private facts, and (4) publicity in a false light.","analysis":"There are four theories of recovery for invasion of privacy: intrusion upon seclusion – an intentional intrusion (physical or otherwise) on the plaintiff's solitude, seclusion, or private affairs that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person\n\npublic disclosure of private facts – publicity given to the plaintiff's private matter (1) that does not address a legitimate public concern, (2) would be highly offensive to a reasonable person and (3) causes damages\n\nappropriation of name or likeness – the unauthorized use of the plaintiff's name, picture, or likeness for a personal benefit (e.g., a monetary advantage)\n\npublicity in a false light – publicity given to false information about the plaintiff with actual malice that places him/her in a false light that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person and causes damages\n\nHere, the woman's invasion of privacy claim will likely fail because the video was taken when the woman and son were on a public street, so it did not intrude upon their private affairs. Additionally, the video was used to present a newsworthy topic and address a legitimate public concern—not for personal benefit or to present false information. (Choice A) A person has a right to privacy against the appropriation of his/her name or likeness—even after death. But since the television station did not use her son's likeness for personal benefit (e.g., for an advertisement), the mother, suing on her dead son's behalf, will not prevail. (Choice C) Although the mother did not give the television station permission to use the video in its program, her consent was unnecessary because she was in a public place and the video addressed a newsworthy issue. (Choice D) Evidence of emotional distress alone is insufficient to prove invasion of privacy. The mother must also show some type of intrusion, publicity, or unauthorized use.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the street was open to the public and the subject was newsworthy."},"questionZh":"一名15岁男孩在帮派冲突中丧生。\n\n在男孩的葬礼结束两天后，其母亲看到了一档关于帮派暴力的电视节目，并震惊地发现节目中播放了她在男孩遗体旁痛哭的画面。\n\n这段视频是电视报道团队在男孩遗体仍停放在公共街道上时拍摄的。\n\n看到这段视频后，这位母亲遭受了严重的精神痛苦。\n\n如果这位母亲起诉电视台侵犯了她及其儿子的隐私权，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会胜诉，因为人死后不再享有隐私权。","B":"不会胜诉，因为事发地点是向公众开放的街道，且该事件具有新闻价值。","C":"会胜诉，因为这位母亲并未许可在节目中使用该视频。","D":"会胜诉，因为这位母亲因观看该视频而遭受了严重的精神痛苦。"},"explanationZh":"针对侵犯隐私权的索赔，主要有四种法律理论依据：侵入私人领域（intrusion upon seclusion）——指对他人的独处、隐居状态或私事进行蓄意侵扰（无论是身体上的还是其他形式的），且这种侵扰会令理性人感到极度反感； 公开披露私人事实（public disclosure of private facts）——指公开披露原告的私事，且该披露：(1) 不涉及正当的公众关切事项，(2) 会令理性人感到极度反感，以及 (3) 造成了损害； 盗用姓名或肖像（appropriation of name or likeness）——指未经授权使用原告的姓名、照片或肖像以谋取个人利益（例如金钱收益）； 歪曲事实的公开宣传（publicity in a false light）——指怀有“实际恶意”（actual malice）地公开传播关于原告的虚假信息，从而将原告置于一种会令理性人感到极度反感的虚假形象之中，并造成了损害。 在本案中，该女子提出的侵犯隐私权索赔很可能会败诉，因为视频拍摄时她和儿子身处公共街道，并未侵犯其私事。\n\n此外，该视频的使用是为了呈现具有新闻价值的话题并探讨正当的公众关切事项，而非为了谋取个人利益或传播虚假信息。\n\n(选项 A) 个人享有防止其姓名或肖像被盗用的隐私权——即便在死后亦然。\n\n但由于电视台并未利用其儿子的肖像谋取个人利益（例如用于广告），作为已故儿子代理人的母亲将无法胜诉。\n\n(选项 C) 尽管母亲未授权电视台在节目中使用该视频，但这种同意并非必要，因为当时她身处公共场所，且视频内容涉及具有新闻价值的议题。\n\n(选项 D) 仅凭精神痛苦的证据不足以证明存在侵犯隐私权的行为。\n\n母亲还必须证明存在某种形式的侵入、公开披露或未经授权的使用行为。\n\n教育目标：侵犯隐私权的四种索赔理论依据为：(1) 侵入私人领域，(2) 盗用姓名或肖像，(3) 公开披露私人事实，以及 (4) 歪曲事实的公开宣传。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 652A–652E 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于侵犯隐私权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"构成侵犯隐私权的四种诉因（或理论）包括：(1) 侵入私人领域（或干扰独处），(2) 擅自使用姓名或肖像，(3) 公开披露私人事实，以及 (4) 虚假描绘（或造成误导性印象）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果这位母亲起诉电视台侵犯了她及其儿子的隐私权，她胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"构成侵犯隐私权的四种诉因（或理论）包括：(1) 侵入私人领域（或干扰独处），(2) 擅自使用姓名或肖像，(3) 公开披露私人事实，以及 (4) 虚假描绘（或造成误导性印象）。","analysis":"针对侵犯隐私权的索赔，主要有四种法律理论依据：侵入私人领域（intrusion upon seclusion）——指对他人的独处、隐居状态或私事进行蓄意侵扰（无论是身体上的还是其他形式的），且这种侵扰会令理性人感到极度反感； 公开披露私人事实（public disclosure of private facts）——指公开披露原告的私事，且该披露：(1) 不涉及正当的公众关切事项，(2) 会令理性人感到极度反感，以及 (3) 造成了损害； 盗用姓名或肖像（appropriation of name or likeness）——指未经授权使用原告的姓名、照片或肖像以谋取个人利益（例如金钱收益）； 歪曲事实的公开宣传（publicity in a false light）——指怀有“实际恶意”（actual malice）地公开传播关于原告的虚假信息，从而将原告置于一种会令理性人感到极度反感的虚假形象之中，并造成了损害。 在本案中，该女子提出的侵犯隐私权索赔很可能会败诉，因为视频拍摄时她和儿子身处公共街道，并未侵犯其私事。\n\n此外，该视频的使用是为了呈现具有新闻价值的话题并探讨正当的公众关切事项，而非为了谋取个人利益或传播虚假信息。\n\n(选项 A) 个人享有防止其姓名或肖像被盗用的隐私权——即便在死后亦然。\n\n但由于电视台并未利用其儿子的肖像谋取个人利益（例如用于广告），作为已故儿子代理人的母亲将无法胜诉。\n\n(选项 C) 尽管母亲未授权电视台在节目中使用该视频，但这种同意并非必要，因为当时她身处公共场所，且视频内容涉及具有新闻价值的议题。\n\n(选项 D) 仅凭精神痛苦的证据不足以证明存在侵犯隐私权的行为。\n\n母亲还必须证明存在某种形式的侵入、公开披露或未经授权的使用行为。\n\n教育目标：侵犯隐私权的四种索赔理论依据为：(1) 侵入私人领域，(2) 盗用姓名或肖像，(3) 公开披露私人事实，以及 (4) 歪曲事实的公开宣传。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 652A–652E 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于侵犯隐私权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会胜诉，因为事发地点是向公众开放的街道，且该事件具有新闻价值。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":181,"sourceQuestionPage":542,"sourceQuestionPages":[542],"sourceExplanationPages":[543,544],"sourceAnswerPages":[543,544],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-182","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A board-certified vascular surgeon applied for privileges at a newly built hospital to expand his practice. The chief of vascular surgery at the hospital decided to sabotage the surgeon's application in order to maintain exclusive control over the vascular surgery department. The chief told the hospital's board members, who were responsible for reviewing the surgeon's application, that she had received disturbing data about his practice's unusually high patient-mortality rates. She then presented the board with data that she had fabricated. The board was disturbed by the fabricated data and therefore decided to reject the surgeon's application. As a result of the board's decision to reject his application, the surgeon is unable to perform surgeries at the newly built hospital. The surgeon claims that his practice has suffered pecuniary losses in the form of lost revenue. He has sought the advice of his attorney regarding which action against the chief of vascular surgery is most likely to succeed. What sort of action against the chief of vascular surgery should the attorney recommend?","choices":{"A":"Intentional interference with an existing contract.","B":"Intentional misrepresentation of the mortality rates of the surgeon's practice.","C":"Slander of title regarding the ownership of the surgeon's practice.","D":"Trade libel for a false statement regarding the surgeon's practice."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Injurious falsehoods\n\nDefamation False & derogatory statements that harm plaintiff's personal reputation\n\nSlander of False & derogatory statements that harm or call into question plaintiff's title ownership of real property\n\nTrade libel False & derogatory statements that harm plaintiff's business or products\n\nTrade libel imposes tort liability for statements (oral or written) that injure a plaintiff's business or products. To recover for trade libel, the plaintiff must prove the following: The defendant made a false and derogatory statement about the quality of the plaintiff's business or products (eg, the chief stated that the surgeon's practice had unusually high patient-mortality rates, which reflected poorly on the medical services provided by the surgeon).\n\nThe statement was made with actual malice, meaning that the defendant knew of or recklessly disregarded its falsity (eg, the chief fabricated the patient-mortality data).\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated the statement to a third party, knowing that this would likely cause the plaintiff to suffer pecuniary loss (eg, the chief presented the data to the hospital's board members to sabotage the surgeon's application).\n\nThe plaintiff suffered pecuniary loss as a result (eg, the board rejected the surgeon's application, and the surgeon's practice lost revenue as a result).\n\nTherefore, the surgeon's attorney should recommend an action for trade libel against the chief. (Choice A) A defendant can be liable in tort for intentionally interfering with an existing contract between the plaintiff and a third party. But here, the surgeon and the hospital only had a prospective business relationship, not a valid contract. (Choice B) Intentional misrepresentation requires proof that the defendant knowingly misrepresented a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance. But here, the chief's misrepresentation was intended to induce the board's (not the surgeon's) reliance, so the surgeon should not assert this claim against the chief. (Choice C) Slander of title protects against false statements that harm or call into question the plaintiff's ownership of real property (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A defendant may be liable for trade libel if he/she makes a false and derogatory statement about the quality of the plaintiff's business or products with actual malice and communicates the statement to a third party knowing that it will likely cause (and does cause) the plaintiff to suffer pecuniary loss.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 623A (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (injurious falsehoods).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 626 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (trade libel).","educationalObjective":"A defendant may be liable for trade libel if he/she makes a false and derogatory statement about the quality of the plaintiff's business or products with actual malice and communicates the statement to a third party knowing that it will likely cause (and does cause) the plaintiff to suffer pecuniary loss.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 623A (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (injurious falsehoods).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 626 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (trade libel).","rule":"A defendant may be liable for trade libel if he/she makes a false and derogatory statement about the quality of the plaintiff's business or products with actual malice and communicates the statement to a third party knowing that it will likely cause (and does cause) the plaintiff to suffer pecuniary loss.","irac":{"issue":"What sort of action against the chief of vascular surgery should the attorney recommend?","rule":"A defendant may be liable for trade libel if he/she makes a false and derogatory statement about the quality of the plaintiff's business or products with actual malice and communicates the statement to a third party knowing that it will likely cause (and does cause) the plaintiff to suffer pecuniary loss.","analysis":"Injurious falsehoods\n\nDefamation False & derogatory statements that harm plaintiff's personal reputation\n\nSlander of False & derogatory statements that harm or call into question plaintiff's title ownership of real property\n\nTrade libel False & derogatory statements that harm plaintiff's business or products\n\nTrade libel imposes tort liability for statements (oral or written) that injure a plaintiff's business or products. To recover for trade libel, the plaintiff must prove the following: The defendant made a false and derogatory statement about the quality of the plaintiff's business or products (eg, the chief stated that the surgeon's practice had unusually high patient-mortality rates, which reflected poorly on the medical services provided by the surgeon).\n\nThe statement was made with actual malice, meaning that the defendant knew of or recklessly disregarded its falsity (eg, the chief fabricated the patient-mortality data).\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated the statement to a third party, knowing that this would likely cause the plaintiff to suffer pecuniary loss (eg, the chief presented the data to the hospital's board members to sabotage the surgeon's application).\n\nThe plaintiff suffered pecuniary loss as a result (eg, the board rejected the surgeon's application, and the surgeon's practice lost revenue as a result).\n\nTherefore, the surgeon's attorney should recommend an action for trade libel against the chief. (Choice A) A defendant can be liable in tort for intentionally interfering with an existing contract between the plaintiff and a third party. But here, the surgeon and the hospital only had a prospective business relationship, not a valid contract. (Choice B) Intentional misrepresentation requires proof that the defendant knowingly misrepresented a material fact with the intent to induce the plaintiff's reliance. But here, the chief's misrepresentation was intended to induce the board's (not the surgeon's) reliance, so the surgeon should not assert this claim against the chief. (Choice C) Slander of title protects against false statements that harm or call into question the plaintiff's ownership of real property (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Trade libel for a false statement regarding the surgeon's practice."},"questionZh":"一位获得认证的血管外科医生向一家新建医院申请执业资格，以扩大其业务范围。\n\n该院血管外科主任为了保持对血管外科部门的独家控制权，决定阻挠该医生的申请。\n\n主任告诉负责审核申请的医院董事会成员，她收到了一些令人不安的数据，显示该医生的执业过程中患者死亡率异常高。\n\n随后，她向董事会提交了自己伪造的数据。\n\n董事会对这些伪造的数据感到不安，因此决定拒绝该医生的申请。\n\n由于董事会拒绝了申请，该医生无法在这家新建医院进行手术。\n\n该医生声称其业务遭受了金钱损失（即收入损失）。\n\n他就针对血管外科主任采取何种法律行动胜算最大一事咨询了律师。\n\n律师应建议针对该血管外科主任采取何种行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"故意干扰现有合同。","B":"故意虚假陈述该医生执业过程中的患者死亡率。","C":"针对该医生业务所有权的权利诽谤（slander of title）。","D":"针对该医生业务的虚假陈述而提起的商业诽谤（trade libel）。"},"explanationZh":"损害性虚假陈述 诽谤（Defamation）：损害原告个人声誉的虚假且贬损性的陈述。 产权诽谤（Slander of title）：损害或质疑原告对不动产所有权的虚假且贬损性的陈述。 商业诽谤（Trade libel）：损害原告业务或产品的虚假且贬损性的陈述。\n\n商业诽谤针对的是损害原告业务或产品的陈述（口头或书面），并由此产生侵权责任。若要就商业诽谤获得赔偿，原告必须证明以下几点：被告就原告业务或产品的质量作出了虚假且贬损性的陈述（例如，科室主任声称某外科医生的执业过程中患者死亡率异常高，这对其提供的医疗服务造成了负面影响）。\n\n该陈述是在具有“实际恶意”的情况下作出的，即被告明知其虚假性或罔顾其虚假性（例如，科室主任捏造了患者死亡率数据）。\n\n被告故意或过失地将该陈述传达给第三方，并明知此举可能导致原告遭受金钱损失（例如，科室主任向医院董事会成员展示这些数据，意图破坏该外科医生的申请）。\n\n原告因此遭受了金钱损失（例如，董事会拒绝了该外科医生的申请，导致其执业收入受损）。\n\n因此，该外科医生的律师应建议针对科室主任提起商业诽谤诉讼。\n\n（选项 A）被告若故意干涉原告与第三方之间现有的合同，可能需承担侵权责任。\n\n但在此案中，该外科医生与医院之间仅存在预期的业务关系，而非有效的合同关系。\n\n（选项 B）“故意虚假陈述”要求证明被告明知事实不符却进行虚假陈述，且意图诱导原告产生信赖。\n\n但在此案中，科室主任的虚假陈述旨在诱导董事会（而非该外科医生）产生信赖，因此该外科医生不应针对科室主任提出此项主张。\n\n（选项 C）“产权诽谤”旨在防止损害或质疑原告对不动产所有权的虚假陈述（本案不涉及此类情况）。\n\n教学目标：如果被告出于“实际恶意”（actual malice），就原告的业务或产品质量发表虚假且贬损性的陈述，并明知该陈述可能导致（且确实导致）原告遭受金钱损失而将其传达给第三方，则被告可能须承担商业诽谤（trade libel）的法律责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第623A条（美国法律学会，1977年）（关于“有害虚假陈述”）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第626条（美国法律学会，1977年）（关于“商业诽谤”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果被告出于实际恶意（actual malice），就原告业务或产品的质量发表虚假且贬损性的陈述，并明知该陈述可能导致（且确实导致）原告遭受金钱损失而将其传达给第三方，则被告可能需承担商业诽谤的责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师应建议针对该血管外科主任采取何种行动？","rule":"如果被告出于实际恶意（actual malice），就原告业务或产品的质量发表虚假且贬损性的陈述，并明知该陈述可能导致（且确实导致）原告遭受金钱损失而将其传达给第三方，则被告可能需承担商业诽谤的责任。","analysis":"损害性虚假陈述 诽谤（Defamation）：损害原告个人声誉的虚假且贬损性的陈述。 产权诽谤（Slander of title）：损害或质疑原告对不动产所有权的虚假且贬损性的陈述。 商业诽谤（Trade libel）：损害原告业务或产品的虚假且贬损性的陈述。\n\n商业诽谤针对的是损害原告业务或产品的陈述（口头或书面），并由此产生侵权责任。若要就商业诽谤获得赔偿，原告必须证明以下几点：被告就原告业务或产品的质量作出了虚假且贬损性的陈述（例如，科室主任声称某外科医生的执业过程中患者死亡率异常高，这对其提供的医疗服务造成了负面影响）。\n\n该陈述是在具有“实际恶意”的情况下作出的，即被告明知其虚假性或罔顾其虚假性（例如，科室主任捏造了患者死亡率数据）。\n\n被告故意或过失地将该陈述传达给第三方，并明知此举可能导致原告遭受金钱损失（例如，科室主任向医院董事会成员展示这些数据，意图破坏该外科医生的申请）。\n\n原告因此遭受了金钱损失（例如，董事会拒绝了该外科医生的申请，导致其执业收入受损）。\n\n因此，该外科医生的律师应建议针对科室主任提起商业诽谤诉讼。\n\n（选项 A）被告若故意干涉原告与第三方之间现有的合同，可能需承担侵权责任。\n\n但在此案中，该外科医生与医院之间仅存在预期的业务关系，而非有效的合同关系。\n\n（选项 B）“故意虚假陈述”要求证明被告明知事实不符却进行虚假陈述，且意图诱导原告产生信赖。\n\n但在此案中，科室主任的虚假陈述旨在诱导董事会（而非该外科医生）产生信赖，因此该外科医生不应针对科室主任提出此项主张。\n\n（选项 C）“产权诽谤”旨在防止损害或质疑原告对不动产所有权的虚假陈述（本案不涉及此类情况）。\n\n教学目标：如果被告出于“实际恶意”（actual malice），就原告的业务或产品质量发表虚假且贬损性的陈述，并明知该陈述可能导致（且确实导致）原告遭受金钱损失而将其传达给第三方，则被告可能须承担商业诽谤（trade libel）的法律责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第623A条（美国法律学会，1977年）（关于“有害虚假陈述”）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第626条（美国法律学会，1977年）（关于“商业诽谤”）。","conclusion":"答案 D：针对该医生业务的虚假陈述而提起的商业诽谤（trade libel）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"falsehoods","ipa":"/fˈælshˌʊdz/","syllables":"false·hoods","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. <正> 谎言, 假话, 虚假( falsehood的复数形式 )","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“falsehoods”","wordForm":"名词；原形 falsehood；词形 0:falsehood/1:s","sourceSentence":"Injurious falsehoods Defamation False & derogatory statements that harm plaintiff's personal reputation Slander of False & derogatory statements that harm or call into question plaintiff's title ownership of real property Trade libel False & derogatory statements that harm plaintiff's business or products Trade libel imposes tort liability for statements (oral or written) that injure a plaintiff's business or product","synonyms":["falsification","falsity","untruth"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":182,"sourceQuestionPage":545,"sourceQuestionPages":[545],"sourceExplanationPages":[546,547],"sourceAnswerPages":[546,547],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-183","subject":"Torts","topic":"Nuisance","question":"For ten years, a woman and her neighbor have owned summer vacation homes on adjoining lots. A stream flows through both lots. As a result of a childhood swimming accident, the woman is afraid of water and has never gone close to the stream. The neighbor built a dam on his property that has completely stopped the flow of the stream to the woman's property. In a suit by the woman against the neighbor, will the woman prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the woman made no use of the stream.","B":"No, if the dam was built in conformity with all applicable laws.","C":"Yes, if the damming unreasonably interferes with the use and enjoyment of the woman's property.","D":"Yes, if the neighbor intended to affect the woman's property."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Liability for private nuisance arises when a defendant's interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property is both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. Therefore, a plaintiff who is not bothered by an interference with the use and enjoyment of his/her property can still prevail as long as a normal person in the community would find the interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable (ie, the interference is still substantial). Here, the neighbor's dam interfered with the use and enjoyment of the woman's property by stopping the flow of a stream to her property. Although the woman was afraid of water and made no use of the stream, the interference was still substantial since a normal person in her community of summer vacation homes would likely be annoyed by the damming of the stream (Choice A). Therefore, the woman will prevail if the interference was also unreasonable. (Choice B) Although a defendant's compliance with the law is a factor that weighs in favor of a finding that an interference was not unreasonable, compliance with the law is not a complete defense to private nuisance. Therefore, though the dam's conformity with applicable laws weighs in the neighbor's favor, it does not—by itself—defeat the woman's claim. (Choice D) Private nuisance requires proof that the defendant's conduct was tortious—but not necessarily intentional. So even if the neighbor did not intend to affect the woman's property, the woman could still prevail if the neighbor's conduct was negligent, reckless, or abnormally dangerous.\n\nEducational objective: Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property. An interference is substantial if a normal person in the community would find the interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable—even if the plaintiff is not personally bothered by it.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 821D, 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (substantial interference requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference requirement).","educationalObjective":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property. An interference is substantial if a normal person in the community would find the interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable—even if the plaintiff is not personally bothered by it.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 821D, 822 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (private nuisance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 821F (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (substantial interference requirement).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 826 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (unreasonable interference requirement).","rule":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property. An interference is substantial if a normal person in the community would find the interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable—even if the plaintiff is not personally bothered by it.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit by the woman against the neighbor, will the woman prevail?","rule":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property. An interference is substantial if a normal person in the community would find the interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable—even if the plaintiff is not personally bothered by it.","analysis":"Liability for private nuisance arises when a defendant's interference with the use and enjoyment of the plaintiff's property is both: substantial – offensive, annoying, or intolerable to a normal person in the community and unreasonable – the severity of the plaintiff's harm outweighs the utility of the defendant's conduct. Therefore, a plaintiff who is not bothered by an interference with the use and enjoyment of his/her property can still prevail as long as a normal person in the community would find the interference offensive, annoying, or intolerable (ie, the interference is still substantial). Here, the neighbor's dam interfered with the use and enjoyment of the woman's property by stopping the flow of a stream to her property. Although the woman was afraid of water and made no use of the stream, the interference was still substantial since a normal person in her community of summer vacation homes would likely be annoyed by the damming of the stream (Choice A). Therefore, the woman will prevail if the interference was also unreasonable. (Choice B) Although a defendant's compliance with the law is a factor that weighs in favor of a finding that an interference was not unreasonable, compliance with the law is not a complete defense to private nuisance. Therefore, though the dam's conformity with applicable laws weighs in the neighbor's favor, it does not—by itself—defeat the woman's claim. (Choice D) Private nuisance requires proof that the defendant's conduct was tortious—but not necessarily intentional. So even if the neighbor did not intend to affect the woman's property, the woman could still prevail if the neighbor's conduct was negligent, reckless, or abnormally dangerous.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, if the damming unreasonably interferes with the use and enjoyment of the woman's property."},"questionZh":"十年来，一位女士和她的邻居在相邻的地块上各自拥有避暑度假屋。\n\n一条溪流穿过这两个地块。\n\n由于童年时期的一次溺水事故，该女士非常怕水，从未靠近过这条溪流。\n\n邻居在其地块上修建了一道水坝，完全阻断了流向该女士地块的溪流。\n\n在该女士起诉邻居的案件中，她会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该女士并未利用这条溪流。","B":"不会，如果该水坝的修建符合所有相关法律规定。","C":"会，如果筑坝行为不合理地干扰了该女士对其财产的使用与享用。","D":"会，如果邻居有意影响该女士的财产。"},"explanationZh":"当被告对原告财产的使用和享用构成以下两种性质的干扰时，即产生私人妨害（private nuisance）的责任：实质性干扰（即对于社区中的普通人而言，该干扰令人反感、烦恼或无法忍受）以及不合理的干扰（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用）。\n\n因此，即使原告本人并未因其财产使用和享用受到的干扰而感到困扰，只要社区中的普通人认为该干扰令人反感、烦恼或无法忍受（即干扰仍属实质性），原告仍可胜诉。\n\n在本案中，邻居修建的堤坝阻断了流向该女子房产的溪流，从而干扰了她对该房产的使用和享用。\n\n尽管该女子怕水且并未利用该溪流，但这种干扰仍被视为实质性干扰，因为在该避暑别墅区，普通人很可能会对溪流被拦截感到不满（选项 A）。\n\n因此，如果该干扰同时被认定为不合理，该女子即可胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）虽然被告遵守法律这一事实有利于认定干扰并非不合理，但遵守法律并不能作为针对私人妨害指控的绝对抗辩理由。\n\n因此，尽管该堤坝符合相关法律规定这一事实对邻居有利，但这本身并不能否定该女子的诉讼主张。\n\n（选项 D）私人妨害的认定要求证明被告的行为构成侵权，但不要求该行为必须是故意的。\n\n因此，即使邻居无意影响该女子的房产，只要其行为构成过失、鲁莽或属于异常危险活动，该女子仍可胜诉。\n\n教育目标：私人妨害是指对原告财产的使用和享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果社区中的普通人认为某种干扰令人反感、烦恼或无法忍受，则该干扰即构成实质性干扰——即使原告本人并未因此感到困扰。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D、822 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821F 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于实质性干扰的要求）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于不合理干扰的要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"私害（private nuisance）是指对原告财产的使用与享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果社区中的普通人会认为这种干扰令人反感、恼火或无法忍受，那么该干扰即被视为实质性的——即使原告本人并未因此感到困扰。","iracZh":{"issue":"在该女士起诉邻居的案件中，她会胜诉吗？","rule":"私害（private nuisance）是指对原告财产的使用与享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果社区中的普通人会认为这种干扰令人反感、恼火或无法忍受，那么该干扰即被视为实质性的——即使原告本人并未因此感到困扰。","analysis":"当被告对原告财产的使用和享用构成以下两种性质的干扰时，即产生私人妨害（private nuisance）的责任：实质性干扰（即对于社区中的普通人而言，该干扰令人反感、烦恼或无法忍受）以及不合理的干扰（即原告所受损害的严重程度超过了被告行为的效用）。\n\n因此，即使原告本人并未因其财产使用和享用受到的干扰而感到困扰，只要社区中的普通人认为该干扰令人反感、烦恼或无法忍受（即干扰仍属实质性），原告仍可胜诉。\n\n在本案中，邻居修建的堤坝阻断了流向该女子房产的溪流，从而干扰了她对该房产的使用和享用。\n\n尽管该女子怕水且并未利用该溪流，但这种干扰仍被视为实质性干扰，因为在该避暑别墅区，普通人很可能会对溪流被拦截感到不满（选项 A）。\n\n因此，如果该干扰同时被认定为不合理，该女子即可胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）虽然被告遵守法律这一事实有利于认定干扰并非不合理，但遵守法律并不能作为针对私人妨害指控的绝对抗辩理由。\n\n因此，尽管该堤坝符合相关法律规定这一事实对邻居有利，但这本身并不能否定该女子的诉讼主张。\n\n（选项 D）私人妨害的认定要求证明被告的行为构成侵权，但不要求该行为必须是故意的。\n\n因此，即使邻居无意影响该女子的房产，只要其行为构成过失、鲁莽或属于异常危险活动，该女子仍可胜诉。\n\n教育目标：私人妨害是指对原告财产的使用和享用构成实质性且不合理的干扰。\n\n如果社区中的普通人认为某种干扰令人反感、烦恼或无法忍受，则该干扰即构成实质性干扰——即使原告本人并未因此感到困扰。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D、822 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于私人妨害）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821F 条（美国法律协会，1979 年）（关于实质性干扰的要求）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第826条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于不合理干扰的要求）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，如果筑坝行为不合理地干扰了该女士对其财产的使用与享用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"conformity","ipa":"/kʌnfˈɔɹməti/","syllables":"con·for·mity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 遵照, 适合, 一致, 相似；[计] 符合度","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“conformity”","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"No, if the dam was built in conformity with all applicable laws.","synonyms":["conformation","compliance","abidance","conformance"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-2f455d41a8d6c034"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":183,"sourceQuestionPage":548,"sourceQuestionPages":[548],"sourceExplanationPages":[549,550],"sourceAnswerPages":[549,550],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-184","subject":"Torts","topic":"Economic & Dignitary Torts","question":"A toy collector attended a flea market at which a variety of second-hand goods were sold. A seller who specialized in dolls also displayed a mechanical bank for sale at a price of $50. Although the collector had no knowledge of the seller, the collector was an expert in such banks and knew that this particular bank was actually worth $10,000. He paid the seller her asking price without any discussion regarding the bank or its value. If the seller, upon learning of the bank's true value, asserts a tort claim against the collector based on fraud, will the seller be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because fraud requires an active misrepresentation.","B":"No, because the collector had no duty to disclose the worth of the bank to the seller.","C":"Yes, because the bank's true value was 200 times the price paid by the collector.","D":"Yes, because the collector was aware of the seller's mistake."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation) can occur when a defendant actively misrepresents or conceals a material fact. It can also occur when a defendant fails to disclose a material fact—but only if the defendant had a duty to disclose it. There is an affirmative duty to disclose a material fact in three limited circumstances: The plaintiff has a fiduciary relationship with the defendant.\n\nThe plaintiff is likely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements.\n\nThe plaintiff is mistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and should disclose (in a minority of jurisdictions).\n\nHere, the collector did not know the seller (no fiduciary relationship) and did not discuss the bank or its value with the seller before purchasing it (no prior misleading statements). And though the collector was aware of the seller's mistake about the bank's worth, he would not be expected to correct it—even in a minority jurisdiction—since people are generally entitled to use their superior knowledge or expertise while bargaining (Choice D). Therefore, the collector had no duty to disclose the bank's worth to the seller, and the seller is unlikely to prevail.* *The outcome would likely be the same even if the collector did have a duty to disclose the bank's true value to the seller because fraud also requires proof that the plaintiff relied on the defendant's misrepresentation. Here, there was no reliance since the seller set the price for the bank before the collector approached her. (Choice A) Fraud can occur absent an active misrepresentation if the defendant has an affirmative duty to disclose a material fact to the plaintiff (not seen here) but fails to do so. (Choice C) The difference between the price paid by the collector and the bank's true value has no bearing on the collector's duty of disclosure (or lack thereof).\n\nEducational objective: An affirmative duty to disclose a material fact exists if the plaintiff is (1) in a fiduciary relationship with the defendant, (2) likely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements, or (3) mistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and should disclose (minority of jurisdictions).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 551 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (liability for nondisclosure).","educationalObjective":"An affirmative duty to disclose a material fact exists if the plaintiff is (1) in a fiduciary relationship with the defendant, (2) likely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements, or (3) mistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and should disclose (minority of jurisdictions).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 551 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (liability for nondisclosure).","rule":"An affirmative duty to disclose a material fact exists if the plaintiff is (1) in a fiduciary relationship with the defendant, (2) likely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements, or (3) mistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and should disclose (minority of jurisdictions).","irac":{"issue":"If the seller, upon learning of the bank's true value, asserts a tort claim against the collector based on fraud, will the seller be likely to prevail?","rule":"An affirmative duty to disclose a material fact exists if the plaintiff is (1) in a fiduciary relationship with the defendant, (2) likely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements, or (3) mistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and should disclose (minority of jurisdictions).","analysis":"Fraud (ie, intentional misrepresentation) can occur when a defendant actively misrepresents or conceals a material fact. It can also occur when a defendant fails to disclose a material fact—but only if the defendant had a duty to disclose it. There is an affirmative duty to disclose a material fact in three limited circumstances: The plaintiff has a fiduciary relationship with the defendant.\n\nThe plaintiff is likely to be misled by the defendant's prior statements.\n\nThe plaintiff is mistaken about a basic fact of the transaction that the defendant is aware of and should disclose (in a minority of jurisdictions).\n\nHere, the collector did not know the seller (no fiduciary relationship) and did not discuss the bank or its value with the seller before purchasing it (no prior misleading statements). And though the collector was aware of the seller's mistake about the bank's worth, he would not be expected to correct it—even in a minority jurisdiction—since people are generally entitled to use their superior knowledge or expertise while bargaining (Choice D). Therefore, the collector had no duty to disclose the bank's worth to the seller, and the seller is unlikely to prevail.* *The outcome would likely be the same even if the collector did have a duty to disclose the bank's true value to the seller because fraud also requires proof that the plaintiff relied on the defendant's misrepresentation. Here, there was no reliance since the seller set the price for the bank before the collector approached her. (Choice A) Fraud can occur absent an active misrepresentation if the defendant has an affirmative duty to disclose a material fact to the plaintiff (not seen here) but fails to do so. (Choice C) The difference between the price paid by the collector and the bank's true value has no bearing on the collector's duty of disclosure (or lack thereof).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the collector had no duty to disclose the worth of the bank to the seller."},"questionZh":"一位玩具收藏家去了一个跳蚤市场，那里出售各种各样的二手商品。\n\n一位专营娃娃的卖家还展示了一个机械存钱罐，标价 50 美元。\n\n虽然这位收藏家并不认识这位卖家，但他对这类存钱罐很了解，知道这个存钱罐的实际价值是 10,000 美元。\n\n他没有和卖家讨论存钱罐的真实价值，就按卖家的要价付了款。\n\n如果卖家在得知存钱罐的真实价值后，以欺诈为由对收藏家提起侵权诉讼，卖家胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为欺诈需要主动的虚假陈述。","B":"不，因为收藏家没有义务向卖家披露存钱罐的真实价值。","C":"是，因为存钱罐的真实价值是收藏家支付价格的 200 倍。","D":"是，因为收藏家知道卖家的错误。"},"explanationZh":"欺诈（即蓄意虚假陈述）可能发生在被告积极地虚假陈述或隐瞒重大事实的情况下。\n\n如果被告有义务披露某项重大事实却未予披露，也可能构成欺诈。\n\n在以下三种有限的情况下，被告负有披露重大事实的积极义务：原告与被告之间存在信义关系。\n\n原告很可能因被告先前的陈述而受到误导。\n\n原告对交易的基本事实存在误解，而被告了解该事实且应当予以披露（仅适用于少数司法管辖区）。\n\n在本案中，收藏家并不认识卖家（不存在信义关系），且在购买前未与卖家讨论过该存钱罐或其价值（不存在先前的误导性陈述）。\n\n尽管收藏家意识到卖家对存钱罐价值的误解，但他并不被要求予以纠正——即使在少数司法管辖区也是如此——因为人们通常有权在讨价还价时利用其更胜一筹的知识或专业技能（选项 D）。\n\n因此，收藏家没有义务向卖家披露存钱罐的价值，卖家胜诉的可能性不大。* *即使收藏家确实有义务向卖家披露存钱罐的真实价值，结果也可能相同，因为欺诈的认定还需要证明原告依赖了被告的虚假陈述。\n\n在本案中，不存在这种依赖关系，因为卖家在收藏家接触她之前就已经设定了存钱罐的价格。\n\n（选项 A）如果被告对原告负有披露重大事实的积极义务（本案中不存在此情况）却未履行，即使没有积极的虚假陈述，也可能构成欺诈。\n\n（选项 C）收藏家支付的价格与存钱罐真实价值之间的差额，与收藏家是否负有披露义务无关。\n\n教育目标：如果原告 (1) 与被告存在信义关系，(2) 很可能因被告先前的陈述而受到误导，或 (3) 对交易的基本事实存在误解（且被告了解该事实并应予披露，仅适用于少数司法管辖区），则存在披露重大事实的积极义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第551条（美国法律学会，1981年）（关于不披露信息的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果原告符合以下情况，则被告负有披露重大事实的积极义务：(1) 与被告存在信托关系；(2) 可能被被告先前的陈述误导；或 (3) 对被告知晓且应当披露的交易基本事实存在误解（少数司法管辖区适用）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果卖方在得知银行的真实价值后，以欺诈为由对收款人提起侵权诉讼，卖方是否有可能胜诉？","rule":"如果原告符合以下情况，则被告负有披露重大事实的积极义务：(1) 与被告存在信托关系；(2) 可能被被告先前的陈述误导；或 (3) 对被告知晓且应当披露的交易基本事实存在误解（少数司法管辖区适用）。","analysis":"欺诈（即蓄意虚假陈述）可能发生在被告积极地虚假陈述或隐瞒重大事实的情况下。\n\n如果被告有义务披露某项重大事实却未予披露，也可能构成欺诈。\n\n在以下三种有限的情况下，被告负有披露重大事实的积极义务：原告与被告之间存在信义关系。\n\n原告很可能因被告先前的陈述而受到误导。\n\n原告对交易的基本事实存在误解，而被告了解该事实且应当予以披露（仅适用于少数司法管辖区）。\n\n在本案中，收藏家并不认识卖家（不存在信义关系），且在购买前未与卖家讨论过该存钱罐或其价值（不存在先前的误导性陈述）。\n\n尽管收藏家意识到卖家对存钱罐价值的误解，但他并不被要求予以纠正——即使在少数司法管辖区也是如此——因为人们通常有权在讨价还价时利用其更胜一筹的知识或专业技能（选项 D）。\n\n因此，收藏家没有义务向卖家披露存钱罐的价值，卖家胜诉的可能性不大。* *即使收藏家确实有义务向卖家披露存钱罐的真实价值，结果也可能相同，因为欺诈的认定还需要证明原告依赖了被告的虚假陈述。\n\n在本案中，不存在这种依赖关系，因为卖家在收藏家接触她之前就已经设定了存钱罐的价格。\n\n（选项 A）如果被告对原告负有披露重大事实的积极义务（本案中不存在此情况）却未履行，即使没有积极的虚假陈述，也可能构成欺诈。\n\n（选项 C）收藏家支付的价格与存钱罐真实价值之间的差额，与收藏家是否负有披露义务无关。\n\n教育目标：如果原告 (1) 与被告存在信义关系，(2) 很可能因被告先前的陈述而受到误导，或 (3) 对交易的基本事实存在误解（且被告了解该事实并应予披露，仅适用于少数司法管辖区），则存在披露重大事实的积极义务。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第551条（美国法律学会，1981年）（关于不披露信息的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为收藏家没有义务向卖家披露存钱罐的真实价值。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-a9d0bfdeed73cbc4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":184,"sourceQuestionPage":551,"sourceQuestionPages":[551],"sourceExplanationPages":[552,553],"sourceAnswerPages":[552,553],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-185","subject":"Torts","topic":"Economic & Dignitary Torts","question":"The personnel director of an investment company told a job applicant during an interview that the company was worth millions of dollars and that the company's portfolio would triple in the next several months. The applicant was very excited about the company's prospects and accepted an offer to work for the company. Two days later, the applicant read in the newspaper that the investment company had filed for bankruptcy reorganization. As a result of reading this news, the applicant suffered severe emotional distress, but he immediately found another comparable position. Is the applicant likely to prevail in an action for negligent misrepresentation?","choices":{"A":"No, because the applicant did not suffer any physical injury or pecuniary loss.","B":"No, because the personnel director's statement was purely speculative.","C":"Yes, because the applicant relied on the personnel director's misrepresentations about the investment company.","D":"Yes, because the personnel director should have foreseen that his misrepresentations would cause the applicant to be distressed."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Liability for negligent misrepresentation arises when: the defendant negligently provides the plaintiff with false information in a commercial setting (eg, in the course of the defendant's business or profession) and\n\nthe plaintiff reasonably relies on that information and suffers pecuniary loss (ie, monetary loss)\n\nOR\n\nthe defendant negligently provides the plaintiff with false information under circumstances posing a risk of physical harm and\n\nthe plaintiff reasonably relies on that information and suffers physical harm.\n\nHere, the personnel director may have negligently misrepresented the company's worth and prospects to the applicant. And the applicant may have reasonably relied on those misrepresentations by accepting an offer to work for the company (Choice C). But since the applicant immediately found a comparable position (no pecuniary loss) and faced no risk of—and did not suffer—physical harm, his claim will likely fail. (Choice B) Reliance on purely speculative information is generally unreasonable. But since the personnel director's statement appeared to include factual information (eg, that the company was worth millions of dollars), the applicant's reliance may have been reasonable. Regardless, the applicant will not prevail because he did not suffer the requisite harm. (Choice D) A victim of negligent misrepresentation can only recover damages for pecuniary loss or physical harm—not any foreseeable harm (eg, stand-alone emotional distress). Therefore, whether the personnel director should have foreseen that his misrepresentations would cause the applicant emotional distress is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: Liability for negligent misrepresentation arises when a defendant negligently provides false information to a plaintiff who reasonably relies on that information (1) in a commercial setting and suffers pecuniary loss or (2) under circumstances posing a risk of physical harm and suffers physical harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 552 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (negligent misrepresentation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 311 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (negligent misrepresentation involving risk of physical harm).","educationalObjective":"Liability for negligent misrepresentation arises when a defendant negligently provides false information to a plaintiff who reasonably relies on that information (1) in a commercial setting and suffers pecuniary loss or (2) under circumstances posing a risk of physical harm and suffers physical harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 552 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (negligent misrepresentation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 311 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (negligent misrepresentation involving risk of physical harm).","rule":"Liability for negligent misrepresentation arises when a defendant negligently provides false information to a plaintiff who reasonably relies on that information (1) in a commercial setting and suffers pecuniary loss or (2) under circumstances posing a risk of physical harm and suffers physical harm.","irac":{"issue":"Is the applicant likely to prevail in an action for negligent misrepresentation?","rule":"Liability for negligent misrepresentation arises when a defendant negligently provides false information to a plaintiff who reasonably relies on that information (1) in a commercial setting and suffers pecuniary loss or (2) under circumstances posing a risk of physical harm and suffers physical harm.","analysis":"Liability for negligent misrepresentation arises when: the defendant negligently provides the plaintiff with false information in a commercial setting (eg, in the course of the defendant's business or profession) and\n\nthe plaintiff reasonably relies on that information and suffers pecuniary loss (ie, monetary loss)\n\nOR\n\nthe defendant negligently provides the plaintiff with false information under circumstances posing a risk of physical harm and\n\nthe plaintiff reasonably relies on that information and suffers physical harm.\n\nHere, the personnel director may have negligently misrepresented the company's worth and prospects to the applicant. And the applicant may have reasonably relied on those misrepresentations by accepting an offer to work for the company (Choice C). But since the applicant immediately found a comparable position (no pecuniary loss) and faced no risk of—and did not suffer—physical harm, his claim will likely fail. (Choice B) Reliance on purely speculative information is generally unreasonable. But since the personnel director's statement appeared to include factual information (eg, that the company was worth millions of dollars), the applicant's reliance may have been reasonable. Regardless, the applicant will not prevail because he did not suffer the requisite harm. (Choice D) A victim of negligent misrepresentation can only recover damages for pecuniary loss or physical harm—not any foreseeable harm (eg, stand-alone emotional distress). Therefore, whether the personnel director should have foreseen that his misrepresentations would cause the applicant emotional distress is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the applicant did not suffer any physical injury or pecuniary loss."},"questionZh":"一家投资公司的人事主管在面试中告诉求职者，公司价值数百万美元，并且其投资组合将在未来几个月内增长三倍。\n\n求职者对公司的前景非常兴奋，并接受了公司的聘用。\n\n两天后，求职者在报纸上看到该投资公司已申请破产重组。\n\n由于看到这条新闻，求职者遭受了严重的精神创伤，但他立即找到了另一份类似的工作。\n\n求职者是否有可能在过失虚假陈述诉讼中胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为求职者没有遭受任何身体伤害或经济损失。","B":"否，因为人事主管的陈述纯属推测。","C":"是，因为求职者信赖了人事主管关于投资公司的虚假陈述。","D":"是，因为人事主管应该预见到他的虚假陈述会导致求职者遭受精神创伤。"},"explanationZh":"因过失虚假陈述（negligent misrepresentation）而产生的责任，通常在以下情形中成立：被告在商业环境（例如，在其业务或职业活动中）中过失地向原告提供虚假信息，且原告合理地依赖该信息并遭受了金钱损失（即经济损失）； 或者 被告在存在人身伤害风险的情况下过失地向原告提供虚假信息，且原告合理地依赖该信息并遭受了人身伤害。\n\n在本案中，人事主管可能过失地向求职者虚假陈述了公司的价值和前景。\n\n求职者也可能因合理依赖这些虚假陈述而接受了该公司的聘用（选项 C）。\n\n但由于求职者随即找到了同等条件的职位（未遭受金钱损失），且未面临也不曾遭受人身伤害风险，因此他的索赔很可能会失败。\n\n（选项 B）对纯粹推测性信息的依赖通常是不合理的。\n\n但鉴于人事主管的陈述似乎包含事实性信息（例如，公司价值数百万美元），求职者的依赖可能是合理的。\n\n无论如何，求职者都无法胜诉，因为他未遭受法定的损害。\n\n（选项 D）过失虚假陈述的受害者仅能就金钱损失或人身伤害获得损害赔偿，而不能就任何可预见的损害（例如，单纯的情绪困扰）获得赔偿。\n\n因此，人事主管是否应当预见到其虚假陈述会导致求职者情绪困扰，这一点无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：当被告过失地向原告提供虚假信息，且原告合理依赖该信息时，即产生过失虚假陈述责任，具体情形包括：(1) 在商业环境中依赖该信息并遭受金钱损失；或 (2) 在存在人身伤害风险的情况下依赖该信息并遭受人身伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 552 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（过失虚假陈述）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 311 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（涉及人身伤害风险的过失虚假陈述）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"过失虚假陈述责任是指被告在以下情况下向合理信赖该信息的原告提供虚假信息：(1) 在商业环境中，且原告因此遭受经济损失；或 (2) 在存在人身伤害风险的情况下，且原告因此遭受人身伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"申请人提起过失虚假陈述诉讼，胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"过失虚假陈述责任是指被告在以下情况下向合理信赖该信息的原告提供虚假信息：(1) 在商业环境中，且原告因此遭受经济损失；或 (2) 在存在人身伤害风险的情况下，且原告因此遭受人身伤害。","analysis":"因过失虚假陈述（negligent misrepresentation）而产生的责任，通常在以下情形中成立：被告在商业环境（例如，在其业务或职业活动中）中过失地向原告提供虚假信息，且原告合理地依赖该信息并遭受了金钱损失（即经济损失）； 或者 被告在存在人身伤害风险的情况下过失地向原告提供虚假信息，且原告合理地依赖该信息并遭受了人身伤害。\n\n在本案中，人事主管可能过失地向求职者虚假陈述了公司的价值和前景。\n\n求职者也可能因合理依赖这些虚假陈述而接受了该公司的聘用（选项 C）。\n\n但由于求职者随即找到了同等条件的职位（未遭受金钱损失），且未面临也不曾遭受人身伤害风险，因此他的索赔很可能会失败。\n\n（选项 B）对纯粹推测性信息的依赖通常是不合理的。\n\n但鉴于人事主管的陈述似乎包含事实性信息（例如，公司价值数百万美元），求职者的依赖可能是合理的。\n\n无论如何，求职者都无法胜诉，因为他未遭受法定的损害。\n\n（选项 D）过失虚假陈述的受害者仅能就金钱损失或人身伤害获得损害赔偿，而不能就任何可预见的损害（例如，单纯的情绪困扰）获得赔偿。\n\n因此，人事主管是否应当预见到其虚假陈述会导致求职者情绪困扰，这一点无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：当被告过失地向原告提供虚假信息，且原告合理依赖该信息时，即产生过失虚假陈述责任，具体情形包括：(1) 在商业环境中依赖该信息并遭受金钱损失；或 (2) 在存在人身伤害风险的情况下依赖该信息并遭受人身伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 552 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（过失虚假陈述）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 311 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1965)（涉及人身伤害风险的过失虚假陈述）。","conclusion":"答案 A：否，因为求职者没有遭受任何身体伤害或经济损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-fa5e36c8cd2ffebc"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":185,"sourceQuestionPage":554,"sourceQuestionPages":[554],"sourceExplanationPages":[555,556],"sourceAnswerPages":[555,556],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-186","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"An associate professor in the pediatrics department of a local medical school was denied tenure. He asked a national education lobbying organization to represent him in his efforts to have the tenure decision reversed. In response to a letter from the organization on the professor's behalf, the dean of the medical school wrote to the organization explaining truthfully that the professor had been denied tenure because of reports that he had abused two of his former patients. Several months later, after a thorough investigation, the allegations were proven false, and the professor was granted tenure. He had remained working at the medical school at full pay during the tenure decision review process and thus suffered no pecuniary harm. In a suit for libel by the professor against the dean of the medical school, will the professor be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the professor invited the libel.","B":"No, because the professor suffered no pecuniary loss.","C":"Yes, because the dean had a duty to investigate the rumor before repeating it.","D":"Yes, because the dean's defamatory statement was in the form of a writing."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Libel is a defamatory statement that appears in written or other physical form. To prevail in a suit for libel, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party (the key to a defamation claim).\n\nHowever, consent (apparent, actual, or implied) is a complete defense to defamation. Apparent consent exists when the plaintiff's words or conduct were reasonably understood by the defendant as consent to the defamatory communication. Here, the dean's written response to the organization may have been libelous since it contained false allegations that the professor had abused patients. But since the professor had authorized the organization to investigate his case, it was reasonable for the dean to understand that the professor had invited a response to the organization's inquiry (apparent consent). Therefore, the dean has a valid defense and the professor's claim will likely fail. (Choice B) Although pecuniary loss is an element of slander (spoken defamation), it is not required for libel (written defamation) since harm is presumed. Therefore, the fact that the professor suffered no pecuniary loss would not defeat his libel claim. (Choice C) Even if the dean had a duty to investigate the rumor before repeating it (and breached that duty by negligently failing to determine its falsity), the professor will not prevail because the dean has a valid defense to libel (apparent consent). (Choice D) Although the dean's defamatory statement appeared in physical form (libel) as a written response to the organization's inquiry, the professor will not prevail because he gave the dean apparent consent to respond to that inquiry.\n\nEducational objective: Apparent consent is a defense to defamation when the plaintiff's words or conduct were reasonably understood by the defendant to indicate consent to the defamatory communication.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 568 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (libel and slander distinguished).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 583 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (consent as a defense to defamation).","educationalObjective":"Apparent consent is a defense to defamation when the plaintiff's words or conduct were reasonably understood by the defendant to indicate consent to the defamatory communication.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 558 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (elements of defamation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 568 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (libel and slander distinguished).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 583 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (consent as a defense to defamation).","rule":"Apparent consent is a defense to defamation when the plaintiff's words or conduct were reasonably understood by the defendant to indicate consent to the defamatory communication.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit for libel by the professor against the dean of the medical school, will the professor be likely to prevail?","rule":"Apparent consent is a defense to defamation when the plaintiff's words or conduct were reasonably understood by the defendant to indicate consent to the defamatory communication.","analysis":"Libel is a defamatory statement that appears in written or other physical form. To prevail in a suit for libel, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nThat type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated that statement to a third party (the key to a defamation claim).\n\nHowever, consent (apparent, actual, or implied) is a complete defense to defamation. Apparent consent exists when the plaintiff's words or conduct were reasonably understood by the defendant as consent to the defamatory communication. Here, the dean's written response to the organization may have been libelous since it contained false allegations that the professor had abused patients. But since the professor had authorized the organization to investigate his case, it was reasonable for the dean to understand that the professor had invited a response to the organization's inquiry (apparent consent). Therefore, the dean has a valid defense and the professor's claim will likely fail. (Choice B) Although pecuniary loss is an element of slander (spoken defamation), it is not required for libel (written defamation) since harm is presumed. Therefore, the fact that the professor suffered no pecuniary loss would not defeat his libel claim. (Choice C) Even if the dean had a duty to investigate the rumor before repeating it (and breached that duty by negligently failing to determine its falsity), the professor will not prevail because the dean has a valid defense to libel (apparent consent). (Choice D) Although the dean's defamatory statement appeared in physical form (libel) as a written response to the organization's inquiry, the professor will not prevail because he gave the dean apparent consent to respond to that inquiry.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the professor invited the libel."},"questionZh":"当地一所医学院儿科系的一位副教授被拒绝授予终身教职。\n\n他请求一家全国性的教育游说组织代表他争取推翻这一决定。\n\n该组织代表教授致函医学院院长，院长随后回信该组织，如实解释说，教授被拒绝授予终身教职是因为有报告称他曾虐待过两名前患者。\n\n几个月后，经过彻底调查，这些指控被证实不实，教授获得了终身教职。\n\n在终身教职决定审查期间，他一直留在医学院工作并领取全额工资，因此没有遭受任何经济损失。\n\n如果这位教授起诉医学院院长诽谤，他是否有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为教授招致了诽谤。","B":"否，因为教授没有遭受任何经济损失。","C":"是，因为院长有义务在传播谣言之前进行调查。","D":"是的，因为院长的诽谤性言论是以书面形式发表的。"},"explanationZh":"书面诽谤（Libel）是指以书面或其他有形形式呈现的诽谤性陈述。\n\n原告若要在书面诽谤诉讼中胜诉，必须证明以下所有要件：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实，或因疏忽而未核实其不实性。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或因疏忽将该陈述传达给第三方（这是构成诽谤索赔的关键）。\n\n然而，同意（包括表面同意、实际同意或默示同意）是针对诽谤指控的有效抗辩。\n\n当原告的言行被被告合理理解为对诽谤性陈述的同意时，即构成“表面同意”。\n\n在本案中，院长给该组织的书面回复可能构成书面诽谤，因为它包含了关于该教授虐待病人的不实指控。\n\n但由于该教授已授权该组织调查其案件，院长有理由认为该教授已同意针对该组织的询问作出回应（即表面同意）。\n\n因此，院长拥有有效的抗辩理由，该教授的索赔很可能无法成立。\n\n（选项 B）尽管金钱损失是口头诽谤（slander）的构成要件，但对于书面诽谤（libel）而言并非必要，因为法律推定其造成了损害。\n\n因此，该教授未遭受金钱损失这一事实并不能否定其书面诽谤索赔。\n\n（选项 C）即使院长有义务在复述传言前进行调查（并因疏忽未核实其不实性而违反了该义务），该教授仍无法胜诉，因为院长针对书面诽谤拥有有效的抗辩理由（表面同意）。\n\n（选项 D）尽管院长的诽谤性陈述是以书面回复该组织询问的形式（即书面诽谤）作出的，但该教授无法胜诉，因为他已给予院长针对该询问作出回应的表面同意。\n\n教育目标：当原告的言行被被告合理理解为表示同意进行诽谤性陈述时，表面同意即构成针对诽谤指控的抗辩。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 558 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（诽谤的构成要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 568 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（书面诽谤与口头诽谤的区别）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第583条（美国法律学会，1977年）（同意作为诽谤侵权的抗辩事由）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当被告合理地理解原告的言语或行为表明其同意诽谤性言论时，表见同意可以作为诽谤罪的抗辩理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果教授起诉医学院院长诽谤，教授胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"当被告合理地理解原告的言语或行为表明其同意诽谤性言论时，表见同意可以作为诽谤罪的抗辩理由。","analysis":"书面诽谤（Libel）是指以书面或其他有形形式呈现的诽谤性陈述。\n\n原告若要在书面诽谤诉讼中胜诉，必须证明以下所有要件：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实，或因疏忽而未核实其不实性。\n\n该类陈述倾向于损害原告的名誉。\n\n被告故意或因疏忽将该陈述传达给第三方（这是构成诽谤索赔的关键）。\n\n然而，同意（包括表面同意、实际同意或默示同意）是针对诽谤指控的有效抗辩。\n\n当原告的言行被被告合理理解为对诽谤性陈述的同意时，即构成“表面同意”。\n\n在本案中，院长给该组织的书面回复可能构成书面诽谤，因为它包含了关于该教授虐待病人的不实指控。\n\n但由于该教授已授权该组织调查其案件，院长有理由认为该教授已同意针对该组织的询问作出回应（即表面同意）。\n\n因此，院长拥有有效的抗辩理由，该教授的索赔很可能无法成立。\n\n（选项 B）尽管金钱损失是口头诽谤（slander）的构成要件，但对于书面诽谤（libel）而言并非必要，因为法律推定其造成了损害。\n\n因此，该教授未遭受金钱损失这一事实并不能否定其书面诽谤索赔。\n\n（选项 C）即使院长有义务在复述传言前进行调查（并因疏忽未核实其不实性而违反了该义务），该教授仍无法胜诉，因为院长针对书面诽谤拥有有效的抗辩理由（表面同意）。\n\n（选项 D）尽管院长的诽谤性陈述是以书面回复该组织询问的形式（即书面诽谤）作出的，但该教授无法胜诉，因为他已给予院长针对该询问作出回应的表面同意。\n\n教育目标：当原告的言行被被告合理理解为表示同意进行诽谤性陈述时，表面同意即构成针对诽谤指控的抗辩。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 558 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（诽谤的构成要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 568 条（美国法律协会，1977 年）（书面诽谤与口头诽谤的区别）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第583条（美国法律学会，1977年）（同意作为诽谤侵权的抗辩事由）。","conclusion":"答案 A：否，因为教授招致了诽谤。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"libelous","ipa":"/lˈaɪbələs/","syllables":"li·belous","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 诽谤的, 败坏名誉的, 爱诽谤的, 诽谤性的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“libelous”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Here, the dean's written response to the organization may have been libelous since it contained false allegations that the professor had abused patients.","synonyms":["calumniatory","calumnious","defamatory","denigrative"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-85c347a7a1ab9501"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":186,"sourceQuestionPage":557,"sourceQuestionPages":[557],"sourceExplanationPages":[558,559],"sourceAnswerPages":[558,559],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-187","subject":"Torts","topic":"Economic & Dignitary Torts","question":"A food company contracted with a delivery service to supply food to remote areas around the world. The contract between the food company and the delivery service was terminable at will. The delivery service then entered into a contract with an airline to provide an airplane to deliver the food. The contract between the delivery service and the airline was also terminable at will. The food company was displeased with the airline because of a previous business dispute between them. Upon learning of the delivery service's contract with the airline, the food company terminated its contract with the delivery service in order to cause the airline to lose the business. After the food company terminated the delivery service's contract, the delivery service had no choice but to terminate the airline contract. If the airline sues the delivery service for tortious interference with contract, will the airline prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the airline and the delivery service were the parties to the contract.","B":"No, because the airline was not in privity with the food company.","C":"Yes, because the delivery service did not terminate the contract because of poor performance.","D":"Yes, because the delivery service's termination of the contract made it a party to the food company's acts."},"answer":"A","explanation":"To prevail on a tortious interference with contract claim, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: A valid contract existed between the plaintiff and a third party.\n\nThe defendant intentionally and improperly interfered with the contract's performance—eg, using physical violence or economic pressure to prevent or hinder performance of the contract.\n\nThe interference caused the plaintiff pecuniary (monetary) loss.\n\nTherefore, the plaintiff can only prevail if the defendant interfered with a contract between the plaintiff and a third party—not a contract between the plaintiff and the defendant. Here, the delivery service (defendant) entered into a contract with the airline (plaintiff). Although the delivery service terminated that contract, the delivery service is not liable for tortious interference with contract because the delivery service interfered with its own contract with the airline—not a contract between the airline and a third party (eg, the food company). Therefore, the airline will not prevail. (Choice B) The airline's claim for tortious interference with contract will fail because the contract at issue involves the airline (plaintiff) and the delivery service (defendant)—not the airline and a third party. Therefore, the airline's lack of privity (ie, contractual relationship) with the food company (third party) is inconsequential. (Choice C) Since the airline's suit stems from the airline's contract with the delivery service, the claim against the delivery service should be based on that contract (eg, breach of contract)—not tort (eg, tortious interference with contract). And since the contract was terminable at will, the contract could be terminated by either party for any reason—not just poor performance. (Choice D) The food company's acts forced the delivery service to terminate its contract with the airline, but did not make the delivery service a party to (or liable for) the food company's acts.\n\nEducational objective: Tortious interference with contract requires proof that (1) a valid contract existed between the plaintiff and a third party, (2) the defendant intentionally and improperly interfered with the contract's performance, and (3) that interference caused the plaintiff pecuniary loss.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 766, 766A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (intentional interference with contract).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 767 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (improper interference requirement).","educationalObjective":"Tortious interference with contract requires proof that (1) a valid contract existed between the plaintiff and a third party, (2) the defendant intentionally and improperly interfered with the contract's performance, and (3) that interference caused the plaintiff pecuniary loss.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 766, 766A (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (intentional interference with contract).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 767 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (improper interference requirement).","rule":"Tortious interference with contract requires proof that (1) a valid contract existed between the plaintiff and a third party, (2) the defendant intentionally and improperly interfered with the contract's performance, and (3) that interference caused the plaintiff pecuniary loss.","irac":{"issue":"If the airline sues the delivery service for tortious interference with contract, will the airline prevail?","rule":"Tortious interference with contract requires proof that (1) a valid contract existed between the plaintiff and a third party, (2) the defendant intentionally and improperly interfered with the contract's performance, and (3) that interference caused the plaintiff pecuniary loss.","analysis":"To prevail on a tortious interference with contract claim, the plaintiff must prove all of the following: A valid contract existed between the plaintiff and a third party.\n\nThe defendant intentionally and improperly interfered with the contract's performance—eg, using physical violence or economic pressure to prevent or hinder performance of the contract.\n\nThe interference caused the plaintiff pecuniary (monetary) loss.\n\nTherefore, the plaintiff can only prevail if the defendant interfered with a contract between the plaintiff and a third party—not a contract between the plaintiff and the defendant. Here, the delivery service (defendant) entered into a contract with the airline (plaintiff). Although the delivery service terminated that contract, the delivery service is not liable for tortious interference with contract because the delivery service interfered with its own contract with the airline—not a contract between the airline and a third party (eg, the food company). Therefore, the airline will not prevail. (Choice B) The airline's claim for tortious interference with contract will fail because the contract at issue involves the airline (plaintiff) and the delivery service (defendant)—not the airline and a third party. Therefore, the airline's lack of privity (ie, contractual relationship) with the food company (third party) is inconsequential. (Choice C) Since the airline's suit stems from the airline's contract with the delivery service, the claim against the delivery service should be based on that contract (eg, breach of contract)—not tort (eg, tortious interference with contract). And since the contract was terminable at will, the contract could be terminated by either party for any reason—not just poor performance. (Choice D) The food company's acts forced the delivery service to terminate its contract with the airline, but did not make the delivery service a party to (or liable for) the food company's acts.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the airline and the delivery service were the parties to the contract."},"questionZh":"一家食品公司与一家配送服务公司签订合同，向世界各地的偏远地区供应食品。\n\n该食品公司与配送服务公司之间的合同为可随时终止合同。\n\n之后，该配送服务公司又与一家航空公司签订合同，由航空公司提供飞机运送食品。\n\n该配送服务公司与航空公司之间的合同也为可随时终止合同。\n\n由于之前与该航空公司存在商业纠纷，食品公司对航空公司不满。\n\n得知配送服务公司与航空公司签订了合同后，食品公司终止了与配送服务公司的合同，意图使航空公司失去这笔业务。\n\n食品公司终止与配送服务公司的合同后，配送服务公司别无选择，只能终止与航空公司的合同。\n\n如果航空公司以侵权干预合同为由起诉配送服务公司，航空公司能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为航空公司和配送服务公司是合同的当事人。","B":"不能，因为航空公司与食品公司之间不存在合同关系。","C":"是的，因为外卖服务公司并非因服务质量不佳而终止合同。","D":"是的，因为外卖服务公司终止合同的行为使其成为食品公司行为的当事方。"},"explanationZh":"若要在“侵权性干扰合同”的诉讼中胜诉，原告必须证明以下所有要件：原告与第三方之间存在有效的合同。\n\n被告故意且不当地干扰了合同的履行——例如，使用暴力或经济压力来阻止或阻碍合同的履行。\n\n该干扰行为给原告造成了金钱（经济）损失。\n\n因此，原告只有在被告干扰了原告与第三方之间的合同（而非原告与被告之间的合同）时，才能胜诉。\n\n在本案中，配送服务商（被告）与航空公司（原告）签订了合同。\n\n尽管配送服务商终止了该合同，但其不承担侵权性干扰合同的责任，因为其干扰的是自身与航空公司之间的合同，而非航空公司与第三方（例如食品公司）之间的合同。\n\n因此，航空公司无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）航空公司关于侵权性干扰合同的诉讼主张将不成立，因为涉案合同涉及的是航空公司（原告）与配送服务商（被告），而非航空公司与第三方。\n\n因此，航空公司与食品公司（第三方）之间缺乏合同关系（即合同当事人关系）这一事实无关紧要。\n\n（选项 C）由于航空公司的诉讼源于其与配送服务商之间的合同，因此针对配送服务商的索赔应基于该合同（例如违约），而非侵权行为（例如侵权性干扰合同）。\n\n此外，由于该合同属于可随时终止的合同，任何一方均可因任何理由（而不仅仅是履约不善）终止合同。\n\n（选项 D）食品公司的行为迫使配送服务商终止了与航空公司的合同，但这并未使配送服务商成为食品公司行为的当事人（或为此承担责任）。\n\n教育目标：侵权性干扰合同的诉讼要求证明：(1) 原告与第三方之间存在有效合同；(2) 被告故意且不当地干扰了合同的履行；(3) 该干扰行为给原告造成了金钱损失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第766条、第766A条（美国法律协会，1979年）（故意干扰合同）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第767条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于“不当干扰”要件的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"侵权干预合同需要证明：(1) 原告与第三方之间存在有效合同；(2) 被告故意且不正当地干预了合同的履行；(3) 该干预给原告造成了经济损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果航空公司以侵权干预合同为由起诉外卖服务公司，航空公司会胜诉吗？","rule":"侵权干预合同需要证明：(1) 原告与第三方之间存在有效合同；(2) 被告故意且不正当地干预了合同的履行；(3) 该干预给原告造成了经济损失。","analysis":"若要在“侵权性干扰合同”的诉讼中胜诉，原告必须证明以下所有要件：原告与第三方之间存在有效的合同。\n\n被告故意且不当地干扰了合同的履行——例如，使用暴力或经济压力来阻止或阻碍合同的履行。\n\n该干扰行为给原告造成了金钱（经济）损失。\n\n因此，原告只有在被告干扰了原告与第三方之间的合同（而非原告与被告之间的合同）时，才能胜诉。\n\n在本案中，配送服务商（被告）与航空公司（原告）签订了合同。\n\n尽管配送服务商终止了该合同，但其不承担侵权性干扰合同的责任，因为其干扰的是自身与航空公司之间的合同，而非航空公司与第三方（例如食品公司）之间的合同。\n\n因此，航空公司无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）航空公司关于侵权性干扰合同的诉讼主张将不成立，因为涉案合同涉及的是航空公司（原告）与配送服务商（被告），而非航空公司与第三方。\n\n因此，航空公司与食品公司（第三方）之间缺乏合同关系（即合同当事人关系）这一事实无关紧要。\n\n（选项 C）由于航空公司的诉讼源于其与配送服务商之间的合同，因此针对配送服务商的索赔应基于该合同（例如违约），而非侵权行为（例如侵权性干扰合同）。\n\n此外，由于该合同属于可随时终止的合同，任何一方均可因任何理由（而不仅仅是履约不善）终止合同。\n\n（选项 D）食品公司的行为迫使配送服务商终止了与航空公司的合同，但这并未使配送服务商成为食品公司行为的当事人（或为此承担责任）。\n\n教育目标：侵权性干扰合同的诉讼要求证明：(1) 原告与第三方之间存在有效合同；(2) 被告故意且不当地干扰了合同的履行；(3) 该干扰行为给原告造成了金钱损失。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第766条、第766A条（美国法律协会，1979年）（故意干扰合同）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第767条（美国法律协会，1979年）（关于“不当干扰”要件的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不能，因为航空公司和配送服务公司是合同的当事人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-cd70260ca19396f9"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":187,"sourceQuestionPage":560,"sourceQuestionPages":[560],"sourceExplanationPages":[561,562],"sourceAnswerPages":[561,562],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-188","subject":"Torts","topic":"Nuisance","question":"An elderly man has resided in a particular neighborhood throughout his life. As has always been his habit, the man brings stale bread to a nearby public park each day to feed the pigeons. There is no state law or local ordinance prohibiting him from doing so. However, in addition to the pigeon droppings and feathers generated by his actions, the food attracts rats. Several mothers who recently started frequenting the park with their children have brought a private nuisance action against the man to enjoin him from feeding the pigeons. The mothers contend that the man's actions have created an unsafe environment for themselves and their children, interfered with their enjoyment of the park, and defaced park property. Which of the following is the man's best defense to this action?","choices":{"A":"The man's habit of feeding the pigeons predates the mothers' use of the park.","B":"The mothers do not have a possessory interest in the park.","C":"The mothers have not alleged an injury different in kind from the general public.","D":"There is no state law or local ordinance that prohibits feeding pigeons in public parks."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Private nuisance v. Public nuisance\n\n(action brought by private plaintiff)\n\nInterference Unreasonable Harm\n\nPublic Public right—eg, Interference is Special harm—harm different health, safety, peace, significant or violated from that of public at large* property law\n\nPrivate Use & enjoyment of Severity of harm Substantial interference— property outweighs utility of offensive, annoying, or interference intolerable to normal person\n\n*Proof of harm not required when plaintiff is public entity or government actor.\n\nA private nuisance is a thing or activity that substantially and unreasonably interferes with another individual's use or enjoyment of real property. To have standing to sue for private nuisance, the plaintiff must have some possessory interest in that property (eg, ownership, lease). Here, the mothers do not have a possessory interest in a public park. Since the mothers have not alleged harm to their own real property interests—eg, that rats attracted to the park are invading their homes—they lack standing to bring a private nuisance action against the man. Therefore, that is the man's best defense. (Choice A) A plaintiff \"comes to the nuisance\" by purchasing (not merely using) property near the defendant's premises, knowing of the nuisance operated by the defendant. This is one factor that the trier of fact may consider in deciding whether the plaintiff can recover for the nuisance, but it is not an automatic defense. (Choice C) Private citizens (eg, the mothers) have a claim for public nuisance only if they suffer a different kind of injury than the general public. But this limitation does not apply to private nuisance actions. (Choice D) The defendant's compliance with a statute, ordinance, or administrative regulation may be admitted in a nuisance action as evidence that the defendant's actions were reasonable. But it is not a complete defense.\n\nEducational objective: A person with a possessory interest in real property (eg, ownership, lease) may bring a private nuisance action if the defendant substantially and unreasonably interfered with the person's use and enjoyment of that property.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 821D, 821E (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (recovery for private nuisance).","educationalObjective":"A person with a possessory interest in real property (eg, ownership, lease) may bring a private nuisance action if the defendant substantially and unreasonably interfered with the person's use and enjoyment of that property.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 821D, 821E (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (recovery for private nuisance).","rule":"A person with a possessory interest in real property (eg, ownership, lease) may bring a private nuisance action if the defendant substantially and unreasonably interfered with the person's use and enjoyment of that property.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the man's best defense to this action?","rule":"A person with a possessory interest in real property (eg, ownership, lease) may bring a private nuisance action if the defendant substantially and unreasonably interfered with the person's use and enjoyment of that property.","analysis":"Private nuisance v. Public nuisance\n\n(action brought by private plaintiff)\n\nInterference Unreasonable Harm\n\nPublic Public right—eg, Interference is Special harm—harm different health, safety, peace, significant or violated from that of public at large* property law\n\nPrivate Use & enjoyment of Severity of harm Substantial interference— property outweighs utility of offensive, annoying, or interference intolerable to normal person\n\n*Proof of harm not required when plaintiff is public entity or government actor.\n\nA private nuisance is a thing or activity that substantially and unreasonably interferes with another individual's use or enjoyment of real property. To have standing to sue for private nuisance, the plaintiff must have some possessory interest in that property (eg, ownership, lease). Here, the mothers do not have a possessory interest in a public park. Since the mothers have not alleged harm to their own real property interests—eg, that rats attracted to the park are invading their homes—they lack standing to bring a private nuisance action against the man. Therefore, that is the man's best defense. (Choice A) A plaintiff \"comes to the nuisance\" by purchasing (not merely using) property near the defendant's premises, knowing of the nuisance operated by the defendant. This is one factor that the trier of fact may consider in deciding whether the plaintiff can recover for the nuisance, but it is not an automatic defense. (Choice C) Private citizens (eg, the mothers) have a claim for public nuisance only if they suffer a different kind of injury than the general public. But this limitation does not apply to private nuisance actions. (Choice D) The defendant's compliance with a statute, ordinance, or administrative regulation may be admitted in a nuisance action as evidence that the defendant's actions were reasonable. But it is not a complete defense.","conclusion":"Answer B: The mothers do not have a possessory interest in the park."},"questionZh":"一位老人在同一个社区生活了一辈子。\n\n他一直有个习惯，就是每天带着陈面包去附近的公园喂鸽子。\n\n没有任何州法律或地方法规禁止他这样做。\n\n然而，他的行为不仅留下了鸽子粪便和羽毛，还引来了老鼠。\n\n几位最近开始带孩子去该公园的母亲对这名老人提起了私人妨害（private nuisance）诉讼，试图禁止他继续喂鸽子。\n\n这些母亲主张，老人的行为给她们和孩子造成了不安全的环境，妨碍了她们对公园的正常使用与享受，并损毁了公园的设施。\n\n针对这起诉讼，以下哪项是该老人最有力的抗辩理由？","choicesZh":{"A":"老人喂鸽子的习惯早于这些母亲开始使用该公园的时间。","B":"这些母亲对该公园不享有占有权（possessory interest）。","C":"这些母亲未主张其遭受了与普通公众性质不同的损害。","D":"没有任何州法律或地方法规禁止在公园内喂鸽子。"},"explanationZh":"私害（Private nuisance）与公害（Public nuisance） （由私人原告提起的诉讼） **类型** | **受干扰对象** | **损害性质** | **损害程度** **公害** | 公众权利——例如健康、安全、安宁、财产 | 干扰重大或违反法律 | 特殊损害——即损害不同于公众所受的损害* **私害** | 对财产的使用与享用 | 损害严重性超过干扰行为的效用 | 实质性干扰——即令普通人感到冒犯、烦扰或无法忍受 *若原告为公共实体或政府主体，则无需证明存在损害。\n\n私害是指实质性且不合理地干扰他人对不动产的使用或享用的事物或活动。若要具备提起私害诉讼的资格（即原告适格），原告必须对该财产拥有某种占有权益（例如所有权、租赁权）。\n\n在本案中，这些母亲对公共公园并不拥有占有权益。\n\n由于这些母亲并未主张其自身的不动产权益受到损害（例如，公园引来的老鼠侵入其家中），她们缺乏针对该男子提起私害诉讼的资格。\n\n因此，这是该男子最有力的抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 A）所谓原告“主动迎向妨害”（comes to the nuisance），是指原告在明知被告存在妨害行为的情况下，购买（而不仅仅是使用）了被告场所附近的财产。\n\n这是事实认定者在决定原告能否就妨害获得救济时可能考虑的一个因素，但它并不构成自动抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 C）私人公民（例如本案中的母亲们）仅在遭受了不同于一般公众的损害时，才有权就公害提出索赔。\n\n但这一限制不适用于私害诉讼。\n\n（选项 D）在妨害诉讼中，被告遵守成文法、地方法规或行政规章的事实可被采纳为证据，以证明其行为是合理的。\n\n但这并不构成完全抗辩理由。\n\n教育目标：若被告实质性且不合理地干扰了某人对不动产的使用和享用，则该不动产的占有权益人（例如所有权人、承租人）可提起私害诉讼。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D、821E 条（美国法律协会，1981 年）（关于私害的救济）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果被告实质性且不合理地妨碍了某人对不动产的使用和享受，则该不动产的占有权人（例如所有权人或承租人）可以提起私人妨害诉讼。","iracZh":{"issue":"针对这起诉讼，以下哪项是该老人最有力的抗辩理由？","rule":"如果被告实质性且不合理地妨碍了某人对不动产的使用和享受，则该不动产的占有权人（例如所有权人或承租人）可以提起私人妨害诉讼。","analysis":"私害（Private nuisance）与公害（Public nuisance） （由私人原告提起的诉讼） **类型** | **受干扰对象** | **损害性质** | **损害程度** **公害** | 公众权利——例如健康、安全、安宁、财产 | 干扰重大或违反法律 | 特殊损害——即损害不同于公众所受的损害* **私害** | 对财产的使用与享用 | 损害严重性超过干扰行为的效用 | 实质性干扰——即令普通人感到冒犯、烦扰或无法忍受 *若原告为公共实体或政府主体，则无需证明存在损害。\n\n私害是指实质性且不合理地干扰他人对不动产的使用或享用的事物或活动。若要具备提起私害诉讼的资格（即原告适格），原告必须对该财产拥有某种占有权益（例如所有权、租赁权）。\n\n在本案中，这些母亲对公共公园并不拥有占有权益。\n\n由于这些母亲并未主张其自身的不动产权益受到损害（例如，公园引来的老鼠侵入其家中），她们缺乏针对该男子提起私害诉讼的资格。\n\n因此，这是该男子最有力的抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 A）所谓原告“主动迎向妨害”（comes to the nuisance），是指原告在明知被告存在妨害行为的情况下，购买（而不仅仅是使用）了被告场所附近的财产。\n\n这是事实认定者在决定原告能否就妨害获得救济时可能考虑的一个因素，但它并不构成自动抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 C）私人公民（例如本案中的母亲们）仅在遭受了不同于一般公众的损害时，才有权就公害提出索赔。\n\n但这一限制不适用于私害诉讼。\n\n（选项 D）在妨害诉讼中，被告遵守成文法、地方法规或行政规章的事实可被采纳为证据，以证明其行为是合理的。\n\n但这并不构成完全抗辩理由。\n\n教育目标：若被告实质性且不合理地干扰了某人对不动产的使用和享用，则该不动产的占有权益人（例如所有权人、承租人）可提起私害诉讼。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 821D、821E 条（美国法律协会，1981 年）（关于私害的救济）。","conclusion":"答案 B：这些母亲对该公园不享有占有权（possessory interest）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":188,"sourceQuestionPage":563,"sourceQuestionPages":[563],"sourceExplanationPages":[564,565],"sourceAnswerPages":[564,565],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-189","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"A senior member of the high school choir sued his music teacher for defamation based on the following facts: The director of a local choir called the teacher about the senior. The teacher, accidentally confusing the senior with another choir member with the same last name, stated that the senior was a bass. The senior was actually a tenor. The director did not contact the senior about joining the local choir because it needed more tenors, not basses. Should the senior prevail in his lawsuit?","choices":{"A":"No, because describing a person as a bass instead of a tenor is not a derogatory statement.","B":"No, because the senior cannot prove special damages in connection with the false statement.","C":"Yes, because the statement was false and the teacher was negligent in making it.","D":"Yes, because the teacher's false statement cost the senior a position in the local choir."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Defamation can be either libel (written statements) or slander (spoken statements—as seen here). A defamation claim based on slander requires proof of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nThe statement was defamatory, meaning that the type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated the statement to a third party.\n\nThe statement caused the plaintiff to suffer special damages or amounted to slander per se.\n\nA statement is harmful to the plaintiff's reputation if it either diminishes respect, esteem, or goodwill toward the plaintiff or deters others from associating with the plaintiff—eg, had the teacher told the director that the senior had a poor work ethic. But since the teacher's statement that the senior was a bass instead of a tenor was not derogatory or harmful to his reputation in any way, the senior should not prevail in his defamation lawsuit. (Choices B & D) Special damages require the plaintiff to prove that a third party heard the defendant's defamatory comments and acted adversely to the plaintiff. Most often, special damages involve an economic loss to the plaintiff, but they also would include such things as the director not contacting the senior about joining the local choir. In spite of this, the senior will not prevail because the teacher's statement was not defamatory. (Choice C) The fact that the statement was false and that the teacher was negligent (ie, failed to use reasonable care) in making it is not a reason for the senior to prevail since the statement was not defamatory.\n\nEducational objective: A statement is defamatory if the type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation by diminishing respect, esteem, or goodwill toward the plaintiff OR deterring others from associating with the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 559 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defamatory statement defined).","educationalObjective":"A statement is defamatory if the type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation by diminishing respect, esteem, or goodwill toward the plaintiff OR deterring others from associating with the plaintiff.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 559 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (defamatory statement defined).","rule":"A statement is defamatory if the type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation by diminishing respect, esteem, or goodwill toward the plaintiff OR deterring others from associating with the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"Should the senior prevail in his lawsuit?","rule":"A statement is defamatory if the type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation by diminishing respect, esteem, or goodwill toward the plaintiff OR deterring others from associating with the plaintiff.","analysis":"Defamation can be either libel (written statements) or slander (spoken statements—as seen here). A defamation claim based on slander requires proof of the following: The defendant knowingly made a false statement about the plaintiff or negligently failed to determine its falsity.\n\nThe statement was defamatory, meaning that the type of statement would tend to harm the plaintiff's reputation.\n\nThe defendant intentionally or negligently communicated the statement to a third party.\n\nThe statement caused the plaintiff to suffer special damages or amounted to slander per se.\n\nA statement is harmful to the plaintiff's reputation if it either diminishes respect, esteem, or goodwill toward the plaintiff or deters others from associating with the plaintiff—eg, had the teacher told the director that the senior had a poor work ethic. But since the teacher's statement that the senior was a bass instead of a tenor was not derogatory or harmful to his reputation in any way, the senior should not prevail in his defamation lawsuit. (Choices B & D) Special damages require the plaintiff to prove that a third party heard the defendant's defamatory comments and acted adversely to the plaintiff. Most often, special damages involve an economic loss to the plaintiff, but they also would include such things as the director not contacting the senior about joining the local choir. In spite of this, the senior will not prevail because the teacher's statement was not defamatory. (Choice C) The fact that the statement was false and that the teacher was negligent (ie, failed to use reasonable care) in making it is not a reason for the senior to prevail since the statement was not defamatory.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because describing a person as a bass instead of a tenor is not a derogatory statement."},"questionZh":"一位高中合唱团的高年级成员以诽谤罪起诉了他的音乐老师，事实如下：当地合唱团的指挥打电话给这位老师，询问这位高年级成员的情况。\n\n老师误将这位高年级成员与另一位同姓的合唱团成员混淆，谎称这位高年级成员是男低音。\n\n而事实上，这位高年级成员是男高音。\n\n指挥没有联系这位高年级成员邀请他加入当地合唱团，因为合唱团需要更多男高音，而不是男低音。\n\n这位高年级成员的诉讼是否应该胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应该，因为将一个人描述为男低音而不是男高音并不构成贬损。","B":"不应该，因为这位高年级成员无法证明因老师的虚假陈述而遭受了特殊损失。","C":"应该，因为老师的陈述是虚假的，而且老师在陈述过程中存在过失。","D":"应该，因为老师的虚假陈述导致这位高年级成员失去了在当地合唱团的席位。"},"explanationZh":"诽谤（Defamation）既可以是书面形式的“诽谤”（libel），也可以是口头形式的“诋毁”（slander，即本案中的情况）。\n\n基于口头诋毁提出的索赔要求证明以下几点：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实，或因疏忽而未能核实其不实性。\n\n该陈述具有诽谤性质，即该类陈述倾向于损害原告的声誉。\n\n被告故意或疏忽地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n该陈述导致原告遭受了“特别损害”（special damages），或者构成了“本身即构成诋毁”（slander per se）的情形。\n\n如果某陈述降低了他人对原告的尊重、敬意或好感，或者阻碍了他人与原告交往，则该陈述被视为损害了原告的声誉——例如，如果老师告诉主管该高年级学生缺乏职业道德，便属于此类情况。\n\n然而，在本案中，老师关于该学生是男低音（bass）而非男高音（tenor）的陈述，既无贬义，也未以任何方式损害其声誉，因此该学生不应在诽谤诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）“特别损害”要求原告证明第三方听到了被告的诋毁性言论，并因此对原告采取了不利行动。\n\n特别损害通常涉及原告的经济损失，但也包括诸如主管因该言论而未联系该学生加入当地合唱团等情况。\n\n尽管如此，该学生仍无法胜诉，因为老师的陈述并不构成诽谤。\n\n（选项 C）尽管该陈述不实且老师在发表时存在疏忽（即未尽到合理注意义务），但这并不构成该学生胜诉的理由，因为该陈述本身并不具有诽谤性质。\n\n教育目标：如果某类陈述倾向于通过降低他人对原告的尊重、敬意或好感，或者阻碍他人与原告交往来损害原告的声誉，则该陈述构成诽谤。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 559 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于诽谤性陈述的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果某项陈述会损害原告的名誉，例如降低他人对原告的尊重、敬意或好感，或阻止他人与原告交往，则该陈述构成诽谤。","iracZh":{"issue":"这位老人应该在诉讼中胜诉吗？","rule":"如果某项陈述会损害原告的名誉，例如降低他人对原告的尊重、敬意或好感，或阻止他人与原告交往，则该陈述构成诽谤。","analysis":"诽谤（Defamation）既可以是书面形式的“诽谤”（libel），也可以是口头形式的“诋毁”（slander，即本案中的情况）。\n\n基于口头诋毁提出的索赔要求证明以下几点：被告明知关于原告的陈述不实，或因疏忽而未能核实其不实性。\n\n该陈述具有诽谤性质，即该类陈述倾向于损害原告的声誉。\n\n被告故意或疏忽地将该陈述传达给了第三方。\n\n该陈述导致原告遭受了“特别损害”（special damages），或者构成了“本身即构成诋毁”（slander per se）的情形。\n\n如果某陈述降低了他人对原告的尊重、敬意或好感，或者阻碍了他人与原告交往，则该陈述被视为损害了原告的声誉——例如，如果老师告诉主管该高年级学生缺乏职业道德，便属于此类情况。\n\n然而，在本案中，老师关于该学生是男低音（bass）而非男高音（tenor）的陈述，既无贬义，也未以任何方式损害其声誉，因此该学生不应在诽谤诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 B 和 D）“特别损害”要求原告证明第三方听到了被告的诋毁性言论，并因此对原告采取了不利行动。\n\n特别损害通常涉及原告的经济损失，但也包括诸如主管因该言论而未联系该学生加入当地合唱团等情况。\n\n尽管如此，该学生仍无法胜诉，因为老师的陈述并不构成诽谤。\n\n（选项 C）尽管该陈述不实且老师在发表时存在疏忽（即未尽到合理注意义务），但这并不构成该学生胜诉的理由，因为该陈述本身并不具有诽谤性质。\n\n教育目标：如果某类陈述倾向于通过降低他人对原告的尊重、敬意或好感，或者阻碍他人与原告交往来损害原告的声誉，则该陈述构成诽谤。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 559 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于诽谤性陈述的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不应该，因为将一个人描述为男低音而不是男高音并不构成贬损。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-6107dff726b73f69"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":189,"sourceQuestionPage":566,"sourceQuestionPages":[566],"sourceExplanationPages":[567,568],"sourceAnswerPages":[567,568],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-190","subject":"Torts","topic":"Defamation & Privacy","question":"When two parents were told that their son should repeat second grade, they sought to have him evaluated by a psychologist. The psychologist, who charged $300, determined that the son had a learning disability. Based upon the report, the school board placed the son in special classes. At an open meeting of the school board about school expenditures, the parents asked that the $300 they had paid to the psychologist be reimbursed by the school district. A reporter attending the meeting wrote a newspaper article about this request, mentioning the son by name. In a privacy action brought by the son's legal representative against the newspaper, will the plaintiff recover?","choices":{"A":"No, if the parents knew that the reporter was present.","B":"No, if the story is a fair and accurate report of what transpired at the meeting.","C":"Yes, because the son is under the age of consent.","D":"Yes, because the story is not newsworthy."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Invasion-of-privacy privileges\n\nAbsolute privileges Consent to publication Publication by participant in judicial or legislative proceeding Publication by executive or administrative officer Publication to spouse Publication required by law Conditional Publication serves important public or private interest privileges Publication to someone with common interest (eg, business partner) (lost if abused) Publication by inferior state officer Report of official proceeding or public meeting Liability for invasion of privacy based on public disclosure of private facts arises when: the defendant publicly disclosed facts about the plaintiff's private life that disclosure would be highly offensive to a reasonable person the disclosed facts are not of legitimate public interest and the disclosure caused the plaintiff harm. However, the defendant is privileged to disclose such facts if (1) the disclosure was in a fair and accurate report of a public meeting and (2) the meeting dealt with matters of legitimate public interest (ie, newsworthy topics). Here, the reporter publicized the son's psychological evaluation (private fact)—a disclosure that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person and is not of legitimate public interest. However, the parents requested reimbursement for that evaluation at an open school board meeting about a matter of public interest—school expenditures. Therefore, the reporter's disclosure would be privileged if it was a fair and accurate report of that meeting, and the son's privacy action would fail. (Choice A) A defendant's privilege to publicize a fair and accurate report of a public meeting does not depend on anyone's knowledge of the defendant's presence. Therefore, it does not matter whether the parents knew that the reporter was present. (Choice C) Although consent is a defense to a privacy claim based on public disclosure of private facts, it does not apply here since the son was under the age of consent. Nevertheless, the reporter may have been privileged to publicize the son's psychological evaluation since it was discussed at a public meeting regarding a matter of public interest. In that case, the son would not recover.\n\n(Choice D) The story is newsworthy because a school board's consideration of expenditures (eg, reimbursing the parents for the son's psychological evaluation) is a matter of public interest.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant is privileged to publicly disclose facts about the plaintiff's private life if (1) the disclosure is in a fair and accurate report of a public meeting and (2) that meeting dealt with matters of legitimate public interest.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 652D (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (public disclosure of private facts).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 611, 652G (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (privilege to report public meeting).","educationalObjective":"A defendant is privileged to publicly disclose facts about the plaintiff's private life if (1) the disclosure is in a fair and accurate report of a public meeting and (2) that meeting dealt with matters of legitimate public interest.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 652D (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (public disclosure of private facts).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 611, 652G (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (privilege to report public meeting).","rule":"A defendant is privileged to publicly disclose facts about the plaintiff's private life if (1) the disclosure is in a fair and accurate report of a public meeting and (2) that meeting dealt with matters of legitimate public interest.","irac":{"issue":"In a privacy action brought by the son's legal representative against the newspaper, will the plaintiff recover?","rule":"A defendant is privileged to publicly disclose facts about the plaintiff's private life if (1) the disclosure is in a fair and accurate report of a public meeting and (2) that meeting dealt with matters of legitimate public interest.","analysis":"Invasion-of-privacy privileges\n\nAbsolute privileges Consent to publication Publication by participant in judicial or legislative proceeding Publication by executive or administrative officer Publication to spouse Publication required by law Conditional Publication serves important public or private interest privileges Publication to someone with common interest (eg, business partner) (lost if abused) Publication by inferior state officer Report of official proceeding or public meeting Liability for invasion of privacy based on public disclosure of private facts arises when: the defendant publicly disclosed facts about the plaintiff's private life that disclosure would be highly offensive to a reasonable person the disclosed facts are not of legitimate public interest and the disclosure caused the plaintiff harm. However, the defendant is privileged to disclose such facts if (1) the disclosure was in a fair and accurate report of a public meeting and (2) the meeting dealt with matters of legitimate public interest (ie, newsworthy topics). Here, the reporter publicized the son's psychological evaluation (private fact)—a disclosure that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person and is not of legitimate public interest. However, the parents requested reimbursement for that evaluation at an open school board meeting about a matter of public interest—school expenditures. Therefore, the reporter's disclosure would be privileged if it was a fair and accurate report of that meeting, and the son's privacy action would fail. (Choice A) A defendant's privilege to publicize a fair and accurate report of a public meeting does not depend on anyone's knowledge of the defendant's presence. Therefore, it does not matter whether the parents knew that the reporter was present. (Choice C) Although consent is a defense to a privacy claim based on public disclosure of private facts, it does not apply here since the son was under the age of consent. Nevertheless, the reporter may have been privileged to publicize the son's psychological evaluation since it was discussed at a public meeting regarding a matter of public interest. In that case, the son would not recover.\n\n(Choice D) The story is newsworthy because a school board's consideration of expenditures (eg, reimbursing the parents for the son's psychological evaluation) is a matter of public interest.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, if the story is a fair and accurate report of what transpired at the meeting."},"questionZh":"当一对父母被告知他们的儿子需要留级二年级时，他们决定带他去接受心理学家的评估。\n\n这位心理学家收取了300美元的费用，并认定儿子有学习障碍。\n\n根据这份报告，校董会将儿子安排到特殊班级。\n\n在一次关于学校支出的公开会议上，这对父母要求学区报销他们支付给心理学家的300美元。\n\n一位出席会议的记者就此请求撰写了一篇报纸文章，并在文章中提到了儿子的名字。\n\n如果儿子的法律代表以侵犯隐私为由起诉该报社，原告能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，如果父母知道记者在场。","B":"不能，如果报道内容是对会议事实的公正准确陈述。","C":"可以，因为儿子未成年。","D":"可以，因为该报道不具有新闻价值。"},"explanationZh":"侵犯隐私权的抗辩事由（特权） 绝对特权：经同意发布；司法或立法程序参与者的发布；行政或管理官员的发布；向配偶发布；法律要求的发布。有条件特权（若滥用则丧失）：发布涉及重要公共或私人利益的事项；向具有共同利益者（如商业伙伴）发布；下级州官员的发布；关于官方程序或公开会议的报道。基于公开披露私人事实而产生的侵犯隐私权责任，其构成要件包括：被告公开披露了关于原告私生活的事实；该披露对理性人而言具有高度冒犯性；披露的事实不涉及正当的公共利益；且该披露给原告造成了损害。\n\n然而，如果满足以下条件，被告披露此类事实即享有特权：(1) 披露内容是对公开会议的公正准确报道；(2) 会议涉及正当的公共利益事项（即具有新闻价值的话题）。\n\n在本案中，记者公开了儿子的心理评估结果（属于私人事实）——这种披露对理性人而言具有高度冒犯性，且不涉及正当的公共利益。\n\n然而，父母是在一次关于公共利益事项（即学校开支）的公开校董会会议上，请求报销该评估费用的。\n\n因此，如果记者的报道是对该会议的公正准确报道，则其披露行为享有特权，儿子的侵犯隐私权诉讼将无法成立。\n\n（选项 A）被告发布关于公开会议的公正准确报道的特权，并不取决于他人是否知晓被告在场。\n\n因此，父母是否知道记者在场并不重要。\n\n（选项 C）尽管“同意”是针对基于公开披露私人事实的隐私权索赔的一种抗辩理由，但本案不适用该理由，因为儿子未达到法定同意年龄。\n\n不过，记者公开儿子心理评估结果的行为可能享有特权，因为该结果是在涉及公共利益事项的公开会议上讨论的。\n\n若属此种情况，儿子将无法获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）该报道具有新闻价值，因为校董会对开支（例如，向父母报销儿子的心理评估费用）的审议属于公共利益事项。\n\n教育目标：若满足以下条件，被告即享有公开披露原告私人生活事实的特权：（1）该披露属于对公开会议的公正且准确的报道；（2）该会议涉及正当的公共利益事项。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第652D条（美国法律协会，1977年）（公开披露私人事实）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第611条、第652G条（美国法律协会，1977年）（报道公开会议的特权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果符合以下条件，被告有权公开披露有关原告私生活的事实：(1) 该披露是对公开会议的公正准确的报道；(2) 该会议讨论的是具有合法公共利益的事项。","iracZh":{"issue":"在儿子的法律代表对报社提起的隐私诉讼中，原告能否胜诉？","rule":"如果符合以下条件，被告有权公开披露有关原告私生活的事实：(1) 该披露是对公开会议的公正准确的报道；(2) 该会议讨论的是具有合法公共利益的事项。","analysis":"侵犯隐私权的抗辩事由（特权） 绝对特权：经同意发布；司法或立法程序参与者的发布；行政或管理官员的发布；向配偶发布；法律要求的发布。有条件特权（若滥用则丧失）：发布涉及重要公共或私人利益的事项；向具有共同利益者（如商业伙伴）发布；下级州官员的发布；关于官方程序或公开会议的报道。基于公开披露私人事实而产生的侵犯隐私权责任，其构成要件包括：被告公开披露了关于原告私生活的事实；该披露对理性人而言具有高度冒犯性；披露的事实不涉及正当的公共利益；且该披露给原告造成了损害。\n\n然而，如果满足以下条件，被告披露此类事实即享有特权：(1) 披露内容是对公开会议的公正准确报道；(2) 会议涉及正当的公共利益事项（即具有新闻价值的话题）。\n\n在本案中，记者公开了儿子的心理评估结果（属于私人事实）——这种披露对理性人而言具有高度冒犯性，且不涉及正当的公共利益。\n\n然而，父母是在一次关于公共利益事项（即学校开支）的公开校董会会议上，请求报销该评估费用的。\n\n因此，如果记者的报道是对该会议的公正准确报道，则其披露行为享有特权，儿子的侵犯隐私权诉讼将无法成立。\n\n（选项 A）被告发布关于公开会议的公正准确报道的特权，并不取决于他人是否知晓被告在场。\n\n因此，父母是否知道记者在场并不重要。\n\n（选项 C）尽管“同意”是针对基于公开披露私人事实的隐私权索赔的一种抗辩理由，但本案不适用该理由，因为儿子未达到法定同意年龄。\n\n不过，记者公开儿子心理评估结果的行为可能享有特权，因为该结果是在涉及公共利益事项的公开会议上讨论的。\n\n若属此种情况，儿子将无法获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）该报道具有新闻价值，因为校董会对开支（例如，向父母报销儿子的心理评估费用）的审议属于公共利益事项。\n\n教育目标：若满足以下条件，被告即享有公开披露原告私人生活事实的特权：（1）该披露属于对公开会议的公正且准确的报道；（2）该会议涉及正当的公共利益事项。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第652D条（美国法律协会，1977年）（公开披露私人事实）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第611条、第652G条（美国法律协会，1977年）（报道公开会议的特权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不能，如果报道内容是对会议事实的公正准确陈述。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"absolute privilege","ipa":"/ˈæbsʌlˌut pɹˈɪvlədʒ/","syllables":"ab·solute priv·i·lege","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"绝对特权：特定司法、立法等场合免负诽谤责任","contextualMeaning":"绝对特权：特定司法、立法等场合免负诽谤责任","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Invasion-of-privacy privileges Absolute privileges Consent to publication Publication by participant in judicial or legislative proceeding Publication by executive or administrative officer Publication to spouse Publication required by law Conditional Publication serves important public or private interest privileges Publication to someone with common interest (eg, business partner) (lost if abused) Publication by in","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":190,"sourceQuestionPage":569,"sourceQuestionPages":[569],"sourceExplanationPages":[570,571],"sourceAnswerPages":[570,571],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-191","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A construction worker was working at the construction site of a new building. An open elevator, which had been installed in the building by the elevator manufacturer, was used to haul workers and building materials between floors. While the worker was riding the elevator, it stalled between floors due to a manufacturing defect in the elevator. The worker called for assistance and was in no danger, but after waiting 15 minutes for help, he became anxious and jumped 12 feet to get out. He severely injured his back when he landed. In an action by the worker against the elevator manufacturer to recover for his back injury, is the worker likely to obtain a judgment for 100% of his damages?","choices":{"A":"No, because such risks are inherent in construction work.","B":"No, because the worker was not in danger while on the stalled elevator.","C":"Yes, because the elevator stalled due to a manufacturing defect.","D":"Yes, because the worker was falsely imprisoned in the stalled elevator."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Contributory negligence\n\n(defense to strict products liability)\n\nTraditional contributory Plaintiff's negligence is no defense (no bar & no reduction in negligence jurisdiction recovery)\n\nPure comparative Plaintiff's negligence reduces recovery by plaintiff's negligence jurisdiction* proportionate fault (eg, plaintiff 90% at fault can recover 10% of damages)\n\n*Default rule on MBE\n\nCommercial suppliers are subject to strict products liability when their defective product caused the plaintiff harm. But if the plaintiff contributed to that harm by failing to use the level of reasonable care that should have been used for his/her own protection (contributory negligence), then recovery will be proportionally reduced according to the plaintiff's share of fault in a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction (default rule). Here, the elevator stalled between floors due to a manufacturing defect and caused the worker to jump out of the elevator and injure his back (Choice C). But since the stalled elevator did not place the worker in any danger, it was unreasonable for him to jump after waiting only 15 minutes for help (contributory negligence). Therefore, the worker's damages will likely be reduced by an amount proportionate to his share of fault. (Choice A) The risk of an elevator stalling may be inherent in constructing a new building, but it is not a basis for denying or reducing the worker's recovery. Instead, his recovery will be reduced because he created an additional risk not inherent to construction work by jumping out of the elevator when he was in no danger. (Choice D) False imprisonment requires proof that (1) the defendant intentionally confined the plaintiff within a fixed space and (2) the plaintiff was aware of such confinement or harmed by it. Here, there is no evidence that the elevator manufacturer intended to confine the worker, so it is not liable for false imprisonment.\n\nEducational objective: In a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction, a plaintiff's recovery for strict products liability is reduced in proportion to the plaintiff's contributory negligence (failure to use reasonable care for his/her own protection).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(a) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (strict products liability for manufacturing defects).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (apportionment of liability).","educationalObjective":"In a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction, a plaintiff's recovery for strict products liability is reduced in proportion to the plaintiff's contributory negligence (failure to use reasonable care for his/her own protection).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(a) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (strict products liability for manufacturing defects).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (apportionment of liability).","rule":"In a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction, a plaintiff's recovery for strict products liability is reduced in proportion to the plaintiff's contributory negligence (failure to use reasonable care for his/her own protection).","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the worker against the elevator manufacturer to recover for his back injury, is the worker likely to obtain a judgment for 100% of his damages?","rule":"In a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction, a plaintiff's recovery for strict products liability is reduced in proportion to the plaintiff's contributory negligence (failure to use reasonable care for his/her own protection).","analysis":"Contributory negligence\n\n(defense to strict products liability)\n\nTraditional contributory Plaintiff's negligence is no defense (no bar & no reduction in negligence jurisdiction recovery)\n\nPure comparative Plaintiff's negligence reduces recovery by plaintiff's negligence jurisdiction* proportionate fault (eg, plaintiff 90% at fault can recover 10% of damages)\n\n*Default rule on MBE\n\nCommercial suppliers are subject to strict products liability when their defective product caused the plaintiff harm. But if the plaintiff contributed to that harm by failing to use the level of reasonable care that should have been used for his/her own protection (contributory negligence), then recovery will be proportionally reduced according to the plaintiff's share of fault in a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction (default rule). Here, the elevator stalled between floors due to a manufacturing defect and caused the worker to jump out of the elevator and injure his back (Choice C). But since the stalled elevator did not place the worker in any danger, it was unreasonable for him to jump after waiting only 15 minutes for help (contributory negligence). Therefore, the worker's damages will likely be reduced by an amount proportionate to his share of fault. (Choice A) The risk of an elevator stalling may be inherent in constructing a new building, but it is not a basis for denying or reducing the worker's recovery. Instead, his recovery will be reduced because he created an additional risk not inherent to construction work by jumping out of the elevator when he was in no danger. (Choice D) False imprisonment requires proof that (1) the defendant intentionally confined the plaintiff within a fixed space and (2) the plaintiff was aware of such confinement or harmed by it. Here, there is no evidence that the elevator manufacturer intended to confine the worker, so it is not liable for false imprisonment.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the worker was not in danger while on the stalled elevator."},"questionZh":"一名建筑工人在新建建筑工地工作。\n\n该建筑安装了一部由电梯制造商安装的开放式电梯，用于在楼层间运送工人和建筑材料。\n\n工人在乘坐电梯时，由于电梯存在制造缺陷，电梯在楼层间发生故障。\n\n工人呼救后并未处于危险之中，但在等待15分钟后，他感到焦虑，于是跳下12英尺（约3.7米）试图逃生。\n\n落地时，他的背部严重受伤。\n\n如果该工人起诉电梯制造商，要求赔偿背部受伤造成的损失，他是否有可能获得100%的赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为此类风险是建筑工作固有的。","B":"否，因为工人在故障电梯内并未处于危险之中。","C":"是，因为电梯故障是由于制造缺陷造成的。","D":"是，因为工人被错误地困在故障电梯内。"},"explanationZh":"与有过失（contributory negligence） （针对严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的抗辩） 传统与有过失（contributory negligence）：原告的过失不构成抗辩（既不阻却索赔，也不减少获赔金额）。 纯粹比较过失：原告的过失会按其过失比例减少获赔金额（例如，若原告承担90%的过失责任，则可获得10%的损害赔偿）。\n\n*MBE（多州律师资格考试）的默认规则 当商业供应商的缺陷产品导致原告受损时，该供应商须承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。但在“纯粹比较过失”司法管辖区（默认规则）内，如果原告因未尽到保护自身安全所需的合理注意义务而促成了该损害（即存在与有过失（contributory negligence）），则其获赔金额将根据其过失比例相应减少。\n\n本案中，电梯因制造缺陷停在楼层之间，导致工人跳出电梯并伤及背部（选项C）。\n\n然而，由于电梯停运并未使工人处于危险之中，他在仅等待救援15分钟后便跳出电梯的行为是不合理的（构成与有过失（contributory negligence））。\n\n因此，该工人的获赔金额很可能会按其过失比例被扣减。\n\n（选项A）电梯停运的风险可能是新建建筑施工过程中固有的风险，但这并非拒绝或减少工人获赔金额的理由。\n\n相反，其获赔金额之所以会被扣减，是因为他在未处危险境地时跳出电梯，从而制造了施工工作中本不存在的额外风险。\n\n（选项D）非法拘禁的认定需要证明：(1) 被告故意将原告限制在特定空间内；以及 (2) 原告意识到这种限制或因此受到伤害。\n\n本案中，没有证据表明电梯制造商有意限制该工人的行动，因此制造商不承担非法拘禁的责任。\n\n教学目标：在纯粹比较过失司法管辖区，原告在严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中的获赔金额，会根据其与有过失（contributory negligence）（即未尽到保护自身安全所需的合理注意义务）的比例进行扣减。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第1条、第2(a)条（美国法律协会，1998年）（关于制造缺陷的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第17条（美国法律学会，1998年）（责任分担）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在纯粹的比较过失管辖区，原告因严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）而获得的赔偿金额，会根据原告的过失程度（即未能采取合理的谨慎措施保护自身安全）按比例减少。","iracZh":{"issue":"在工人起诉电梯制造商要求赔偿其背部受伤的案件中，该工人是否有可能获得100%的损害赔偿？","rule":"在纯粹的比较过失管辖区，原告因严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）而获得的赔偿金额，会根据原告的过失程度（即未能采取合理的谨慎措施保护自身安全）按比例减少。","analysis":"与有过失（contributory negligence） （针对严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的抗辩） 传统与有过失（contributory negligence）：原告的过失不构成抗辩（既不阻却索赔，也不减少获赔金额）。 纯粹比较过失：原告的过失会按其过失比例减少获赔金额（例如，若原告承担90%的过失责任，则可获得10%的损害赔偿）。\n\n*MBE（多州律师资格考试）的默认规则 当商业供应商的缺陷产品导致原告受损时，该供应商须承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。但在“纯粹比较过失”司法管辖区（默认规则）内，如果原告因未尽到保护自身安全所需的合理注意义务而促成了该损害（即存在与有过失（contributory negligence）），则其获赔金额将根据其过失比例相应减少。\n\n本案中，电梯因制造缺陷停在楼层之间，导致工人跳出电梯并伤及背部（选项C）。\n\n然而，由于电梯停运并未使工人处于危险之中，他在仅等待救援15分钟后便跳出电梯的行为是不合理的（构成与有过失（contributory negligence））。\n\n因此，该工人的获赔金额很可能会按其过失比例被扣减。\n\n（选项A）电梯停运的风险可能是新建建筑施工过程中固有的风险，但这并非拒绝或减少工人获赔金额的理由。\n\n相反，其获赔金额之所以会被扣减，是因为他在未处危险境地时跳出电梯，从而制造了施工工作中本不存在的额外风险。\n\n（选项D）非法拘禁的认定需要证明：(1) 被告故意将原告限制在特定空间内；以及 (2) 原告意识到这种限制或因此受到伤害。\n\n本案中，没有证据表明电梯制造商有意限制该工人的行动，因此制造商不承担非法拘禁的责任。\n\n教学目标：在纯粹比较过失司法管辖区，原告在严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中的获赔金额，会根据其与有过失（contributory negligence）（即未尽到保护自身安全所需的合理注意义务）的比例进行扣减。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第1条、第2(a)条（美国法律协会，1998年）（关于制造缺陷的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第17条（美国法律学会，1998年）（责任分担）。","conclusion":"答案 B：否，因为工人在故障电梯内并未处于危险之中。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":191,"sourceQuestionPage":572,"sourceQuestionPages":[572],"sourceExplanationPages":[573,574],"sourceAnswerPages":[573,574],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-192","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"A manufacturing plant located near a busy highway uses and stores highly volatile explosives. The owner of the plant has imposed strict safety measures to prevent an explosion at the plant. During an unusually heavy windstorm, a large tile was blown off the roof of the plant and crashed into a passing car, damaging the hood and the windshield. The driver of the car brought a strict liability action against the owner of the plant to recover for the damage to his car. Is the driver likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the damage to the car did not result from the abnormally dangerous aspect of the plant's activity.","B":"No, because the severity of the windstorm was unusual.","C":"Yes, because the plant's activity was abnormally dangerous.","D":"Yes, because the plant's location near a busy highway was abnormally dangerous."},"answer":"A","explanation":"An abnormally dangerous activity is an uncommon activity that poses a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm—even when reasonable care is used. A defendant is strictly liable (liable without proof of fault) for bodily harm or property damage resulting from the abnormal dangers of that activity. Here, the manufacturing plant stored highly volatile explosives (uncommon activity) near a busy highway, which created a foreseeable and highly significant risk of a harmful explosion that could not be mitigated by the exercise of reasonable care. However, the damage to the driver's car was caused by a windstorm—a hazard unrelated to the abnormal danger of storing explosives. Therefore, even though the plant engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity (Choice C), the driver will not prevail on a strict liability claim. (Choice B) The unusual severity of the windstorm is irrelevant to the driver's strict liability claim. Instead, the driver's claim will fail because the windstorm was unrelated to the danger of storing explosives. (Choice D) The plant's location near a busy highway increased the abnormal danger of the stored explosives. However, the driver will not prevail because his property damage was not caused by a risk associated with storing explosives.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant is strictly liable for bodily harm or property damage caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity—not for harm caused by a risk unrelated to the abnormal dangers of the activity.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 29 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (limitations on strict liability).","educationalObjective":"A defendant is strictly liable for bodily harm or property damage caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity—not for harm caused by a risk unrelated to the abnormal dangers of the activity.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 29 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (limitations on strict liability).","rule":"A defendant is strictly liable for bodily harm or property damage caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity—not for harm caused by a risk unrelated to the abnormal dangers of the activity.","irac":{"issue":"Is the driver likely to prevail?","rule":"A defendant is strictly liable for bodily harm or property damage caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity—not for harm caused by a risk unrelated to the abnormal dangers of the activity.","analysis":"An abnormally dangerous activity is an uncommon activity that poses a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm—even when reasonable care is used. A defendant is strictly liable (liable without proof of fault) for bodily harm or property damage resulting from the abnormal dangers of that activity. Here, the manufacturing plant stored highly volatile explosives (uncommon activity) near a busy highway, which created a foreseeable and highly significant risk of a harmful explosion that could not be mitigated by the exercise of reasonable care. However, the damage to the driver's car was caused by a windstorm—a hazard unrelated to the abnormal danger of storing explosives. Therefore, even though the plant engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity (Choice C), the driver will not prevail on a strict liability claim. (Choice B) The unusual severity of the windstorm is irrelevant to the driver's strict liability claim. Instead, the driver's claim will fail because the windstorm was unrelated to the danger of storing explosives. (Choice D) The plant's location near a busy highway increased the abnormal danger of the stored explosives. However, the driver will not prevail because his property damage was not caused by a risk associated with storing explosives.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the damage to the car did not result from the abnormally dangerous aspect of the plant's activity."},"questionZh":"一家位于繁忙高速公路附近的制造厂使用并储存着高度易爆的爆炸物。\n\n工厂所有者已采取严格的安全措施，以防止工厂发生爆炸。\n\n在一次异常强烈的风暴中，一块大瓦片从厂房顶部被吹落，砸中了一辆过往车辆，导致其引擎盖和挡风玻璃受损。\n\n该车司机针对工厂所有者提起了严格责任诉讼，要求赔偿车辆损失。\n\n该司机胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为车辆受损并非由该工厂活动的“异常危险”特性所导致。","B":"不大，因为此次风暴的强度异常。","C":"大，因为该工厂的活动属于异常危险活动。","D":"大，因为该工厂位于繁忙高速公路附近这一选址属于异常危险行为。"},"explanationZh":"“异常危险活动”是指一种不常见的活动，即使采取了合理的注意措施，仍会带来可预见的且极大的身体伤害风险。\n\n对于因该活动的异常危险性而导致的人身伤害或财产损失，被告须承担严格责任（即无需证明存在过错即承担责任）。\n\n在本案中，该制造厂在繁忙公路附近储存了极易挥发的爆炸物（属于不常见活动），这构成了可预见且极大的有害爆炸风险，且这种风险无法通过采取合理的注意措施来消除。\n\n然而，导致驾驶员车辆受损的原因是狂风——这是一种与储存爆炸物所带来的异常危险无关的危害。\n\n因此，尽管该工厂从事了异常危险活动（选项 C），但驾驶员无法在严格责任索赔中胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）狂风的异常强烈程度与驾驶员的严格责任索赔无关。\n\n相反，驾驶员的索赔将无法成立，因为狂风与储存爆炸物带来的危险并无关联。\n\n（选项 D）工厂靠近繁忙公路的位置确实增加了储存爆炸物所带来的异常危险。\n\n然而，驾驶员无法胜诉，因为其财产损失并非由与储存爆炸物相关的风险所引起。\n\n教育目标：被告须对其异常危险活动引起的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格责任，但不对由与该活动的异常危险性无关的风险所造成的损害承担责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 20 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于异常危险活动的严格责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 29 条评注 d (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于严格责任的限制）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"被告仅对其异常危险活动所造成的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格责任，而不对由与该活动的异常危险性无关的风险所造成的损害承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"该司机胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"被告仅对其异常危险活动所造成的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格责任，而不对由与该活动的异常危险性无关的风险所造成的损害承担责任。","analysis":"“异常危险活动”是指一种不常见的活动，即使采取了合理的注意措施，仍会带来可预见的且极大的身体伤害风险。\n\n对于因该活动的异常危险性而导致的人身伤害或财产损失，被告须承担严格责任（即无需证明存在过错即承担责任）。\n\n在本案中，该制造厂在繁忙公路附近储存了极易挥发的爆炸物（属于不常见活动），这构成了可预见且极大的有害爆炸风险，且这种风险无法通过采取合理的注意措施来消除。\n\n然而，导致驾驶员车辆受损的原因是狂风——这是一种与储存爆炸物所带来的异常危险无关的危害。\n\n因此，尽管该工厂从事了异常危险活动（选项 C），但驾驶员无法在严格责任索赔中胜诉。\n\n（选项 B）狂风的异常强烈程度与驾驶员的严格责任索赔无关。\n\n相反，驾驶员的索赔将无法成立，因为狂风与储存爆炸物带来的危险并无关联。\n\n（选项 D）工厂靠近繁忙公路的位置确实增加了储存爆炸物所带来的异常危险。\n\n然而，驾驶员无法胜诉，因为其财产损失并非由与储存爆炸物相关的风险所引起。\n\n教育目标：被告须对其异常危险活动引起的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格责任，但不对由与该活动的异常危险性无关的风险所造成的损害承担责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 20 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于异常危险活动的严格责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 29 条评注 d (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于严格责任的限制）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不大，因为车辆受损并非由该工厂活动的“异常危险”特性所导致。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-3150b556e9578f50"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":192,"sourceQuestionPage":575,"sourceQuestionPages":[575],"sourceExplanationPages":[576,577],"sourceAnswerPages":[576,577],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-193","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A homeowner purchased an over-the-range microwave and planned to hire someone to install it. The microwave's manual, which the homeowner read, contained a warning that the microwave should not be operated on a kitchen countertop because the underside does not provide a stable surface, hot liquids could spill and cause serious injury, and over time, the position could cause excessive wear on the power cord. Before the homeowner was able to have the microwave installed, he used it while it was resting on his kitchen countertop to heat a cup of coffee. Due to an improperly manufactured thermal fuse in the microwave, the microwave caught fire while heating the coffee and caused extensive damage to the home. The homeowner has filed a complaint based on strict products liability against the microwave's manufacturer. Will the homeowner be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the homeowner assumed the risk by ignoring the manufacturer's warning.","B":"No, because the homeowner was negligent in using the microwave before it was installed.","C":"Yes, because the homeowner's misuse of the microwave while it was unmounted was foreseeable.","D":"Yes, because the microwave contained a manufacturing defect that caused it to catch fire."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A plaintiff can recover in a strict products liability action against a manufacturer, retailer, or other distributor (ie, commercial seller) if the following facts are proved: The product was defective (in manufacture, design, or failure to warn).\n\nThe defect existed when the product left the defendant's control.\n\nThe defect caused the plaintiff's injuries when the product was used in an intended or reasonably foreseeable way.\n\nA product has a manufacturing defect if it deviates from the manufacturer's intended design (eg, due to incorrect assembly). Here, the microwave contained an improperly manufactured thermal fuse when the microwave left the manufacturer's control. This manufacturing defect caused the microwave to catch fire and damage the home when the homeowner used it to heat a cup of coffee. As a result, the homeowner will likely prevail in a strict products liability action against the manufacturer. (Choice A) Assumption of the risk is a defense that reduces the plaintiff's recovery if the plaintiff voluntarily exposed him/herself to a known risk of harm. Here, the homeowner knew the risk associated with using the microwave while it was unmounted—but not the risk stemming from the improperly manufactured thermal fuse that caused his harm. Therefore, this defense does not apply. (Choice B) The homeowner's negligence in using the microwave before installing it is irrelevant because his injury was caused by the defective fuse—not by using the microwave without mounting it. (Choice C) Using a product in an unintended and unforeseeable manner may negate liability if the misuse leads to the plaintiff's harm. Here, the homeowner's misuse was foreseeable because the manufacturer's manual specifically addressed it. But the misuse did not lead to the microwave catching fire (the defective fuse did), so the homeowner will not prevail on this basis.\n\nEducational objective: An action based on strict products liability requires proof that the product was defective (eg, in manufacture) when it left the defendant's control AND that the defect caused the plaintiff's injuries when the product was used in an intended or reasonably foreseeable way.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prod. Liab. § 2 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (categories of product defect).","educationalObjective":"An action based on strict products liability requires proof that the product was defective (eg, in manufacture) when it left the defendant's control AND that the defect caused the plaintiff's injuries when the product was used in an intended or reasonably foreseeable way.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prod. Liab. § 2 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (categories of product defect).","rule":"An action based on strict products liability requires proof that the product was defective (eg, in manufacture) when it left the defendant's control AND that the defect caused the plaintiff's injuries when the product was used in an intended or reasonably foreseeable way.","irac":{"issue":"Will the homeowner be likely to prevail?","rule":"An action based on strict products liability requires proof that the product was defective (eg, in manufacture) when it left the defendant's control AND that the defect caused the plaintiff's injuries when the product was used in an intended or reasonably foreseeable way.","analysis":"A plaintiff can recover in a strict products liability action against a manufacturer, retailer, or other distributor (ie, commercial seller) if the following facts are proved: The product was defective (in manufacture, design, or failure to warn).\n\nThe defect existed when the product left the defendant's control.\n\nThe defect caused the plaintiff's injuries when the product was used in an intended or reasonably foreseeable way.\n\nA product has a manufacturing defect if it deviates from the manufacturer's intended design (eg, due to incorrect assembly). Here, the microwave contained an improperly manufactured thermal fuse when the microwave left the manufacturer's control. This manufacturing defect caused the microwave to catch fire and damage the home when the homeowner used it to heat a cup of coffee. As a result, the homeowner will likely prevail in a strict products liability action against the manufacturer. (Choice A) Assumption of the risk is a defense that reduces the plaintiff's recovery if the plaintiff voluntarily exposed him/herself to a known risk of harm. Here, the homeowner knew the risk associated with using the microwave while it was unmounted—but not the risk stemming from the improperly manufactured thermal fuse that caused his harm. Therefore, this defense does not apply. (Choice B) The homeowner's negligence in using the microwave before installing it is irrelevant because his injury was caused by the defective fuse—not by using the microwave without mounting it. (Choice C) Using a product in an unintended and unforeseeable manner may negate liability if the misuse leads to the plaintiff's harm. Here, the homeowner's misuse was foreseeable because the manufacturer's manual specifically addressed it. But the misuse did not lead to the microwave catching fire (the defective fuse did), so the homeowner will not prevail on this basis.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the microwave contained a manufacturing defect that caused it to catch fire."},"questionZh":"一位房主购买了一台灶台上方安装式微波炉（over-the-range microwave），并计划聘请他人进行安装。\n\n房主阅读了微波炉的使用说明书，其中包含一项警告：不得将该微波炉置于厨房台面上使用，因为其底部无法提供稳固的支撑面，热液体可能溢出导致严重伤害，且长期如此放置会导致电源线过度磨损。\n\n在尚未安装微波炉之前，房主将其放置在厨房台面上加热了一杯咖啡。\n\n由于微波炉内的一个热熔断器制造不当，微波炉在加热咖啡时起火，导致房屋遭受严重损坏。\n\n房主已针对微波炉制造商提起基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的诉讼。\n\n房主胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为房主无视制造商的警告，从而承担了相应的风险。","B":"不大，因为房主在微波炉尚未安装到位的情况下使用它，存在疏忽行为。","C":"很大，因为房主在微波炉未安装状态下对其进行不当使用的情况是可预见的。","D":"很大，因为该微波炉存在导致起火的制造缺陷。"},"explanationZh":"在针对制造商、零售商或其他分销商（即商业销售商）提起的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中，如果原告能证明以下事实，即可获得赔偿：产品存在缺陷（涉及制造、设计或未尽警示义务）。\n\n该缺陷在产品脱离被告控制时即已存在。\n\n当产品按预期或合理可预见的方式使用时，该缺陷导致了原告的伤害。\n\n如果产品偏离了制造商预期的设计（例如，由于组装不当），则该产品存在制造缺陷。\n\n在本案中，当微波炉脱离制造商控制时，其内部的温度保险丝已存在制造缺陷。\n\n正是这一制造缺陷导致微波炉在房主加热咖啡时起火并损坏了房屋。\n\n因此，房主很可能在针对制造商的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”（assumption of the risk）是一项抗辩理由，如果原告自愿置身于已知的伤害风险之中，该理由可减少原告获得的赔偿。\n\n在本案中，房主虽然知道在未固定安装的情况下使用微波炉存在风险，但他并不知道导致其受损的那个制造不当的温度保险丝所带来的风险。\n\n因此，该抗辩理由不适用。\n\n（选项 B）房主在未安装微波炉前使用它的疏忽行为与本案无关，因为导致其受损的是有缺陷的保险丝，而不是未固定安装微波炉这一行为。\n\n（选项 C）如果原告因误用产品而受损，且该误用方式属于非预期且不可预见的，则可能免除责任。\n\n在本案中，房主的误用行为是可预见的，因为制造商的说明书中专门提到了这一点。\n\n但该误用行为并未导致微波炉起火（起火原因是保险丝缺陷），因此房主不能以此为由胜诉。\n\n教学目标：基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的诉讼要求证明：产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷（例如制造缺陷），且当产品按预期或合理可预见的方式使用时，该缺陷导致了原告的伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 2 条 (Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prod. Liab. § 2) (美国法律协会，2010 年)（产品缺陷的分类）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的诉讼要求证明：产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷（例如制造缺陷），且当产品按预期或合理可预见的方式使用时，该缺陷导致了原告的损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"房主胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的诉讼要求证明：产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷（例如制造缺陷），且当产品按预期或合理可预见的方式使用时，该缺陷导致了原告的损害。","analysis":"在针对制造商、零售商或其他分销商（即商业销售商）提起的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中，如果原告能证明以下事实，即可获得赔偿：产品存在缺陷（涉及制造、设计或未尽警示义务）。\n\n该缺陷在产品脱离被告控制时即已存在。\n\n当产品按预期或合理可预见的方式使用时，该缺陷导致了原告的伤害。\n\n如果产品偏离了制造商预期的设计（例如，由于组装不当），则该产品存在制造缺陷。\n\n在本案中，当微波炉脱离制造商控制时，其内部的温度保险丝已存在制造缺陷。\n\n正是这一制造缺陷导致微波炉在房主加热咖啡时起火并损坏了房屋。\n\n因此，房主很可能在针对制造商的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）“自担风险”（assumption of the risk）是一项抗辩理由，如果原告自愿置身于已知的伤害风险之中，该理由可减少原告获得的赔偿。\n\n在本案中，房主虽然知道在未固定安装的情况下使用微波炉存在风险，但他并不知道导致其受损的那个制造不当的温度保险丝所带来的风险。\n\n因此，该抗辩理由不适用。\n\n（选项 B）房主在未安装微波炉前使用它的疏忽行为与本案无关，因为导致其受损的是有缺陷的保险丝，而不是未固定安装微波炉这一行为。\n\n（选项 C）如果原告因误用产品而受损，且该误用方式属于非预期且不可预见的，则可能免除责任。\n\n在本案中，房主的误用行为是可预见的，因为制造商的说明书中专门提到了这一点。\n\n但该误用行为并未导致微波炉起火（起火原因是保险丝缺陷），因此房主不能以此为由胜诉。\n\n教学目标：基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的诉讼要求证明：产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷（例如制造缺陷），且当产品按预期或合理可预见的方式使用时，该缺陷导致了原告的伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 2 条 (Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prod. Liab. § 2) (美国法律协会，2010 年)（产品缺陷的分类）。","conclusion":"答案 D：很大，因为该微波炉存在导致起火的制造缺陷。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-6b9bd86c1ec551e2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":193,"sourceQuestionPage":578,"sourceQuestionPages":[578],"sourceExplanationPages":[579,580,581],"sourceAnswerPages":[579,580,581],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-194","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A company manufactured metal stamping presses that were usually sold with an installed safety device that made it impossible for a press to close on a worker's hands. The company strongly recommended that its presses be purchased with the safety device installed, but would sell a press without the safety device at a slightly reduced price. Rejecting the company's advice, a worker's employer purchased a stamping press without the safety device. The press closed on the worker's hand, crushing it. If the worker were to sue the company, would the worker be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the company strongly recommended that the worker's employer purchase the press with the safety device.","B":"No, because the failure of the worker's employer to purchase the press with a safety device was a superseding cause of the worker's injury.","C":"Yes, because the company sold the press to the worker's employer without an installed safety device.","D":"Yes, because the company's press was the cause in fact of the worker's injury."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Types of product defects\n\nManufacturing Deviation from intended design (eg, incorrect assembly) defect\n\nDesign defect Foreseeable risk of harm that could have been mitigated by feasible alternative design (ie, reasonable in cost)\n\nInadequate No reasonable instructions or warnings about risks of harm warning\n\nStrict products liability is imposed on a commercial seller (eg, manufacturer, wholesaler, retailer) when its defective product causes the plaintiff harm. A product is defective by design when: the design creates a foreseeable risk of physical harm and that risk could have been mitigated by a reasonable alternative design (eg, a modification that reduces the risk for a reasonable cost). Here, the company's metal stamping presses posed a foreseeable risk of closing on a worker's hands. As a result, the company usually sold its presses with a pre-installed safety device that eliminated the risk of harm for a slightly increased cost (reasonable alternative design). But since the company still sold presses without the safety device (defective design) and that defect caused the press to crush the worker's hand, the company is strictly liable for the worker's harm. (Choice A) When a manufacturer has a reasonable alternative design, it cannot avoid strict liability by merely warning the purchaser of the product's danger. Therefore, the company's recommendation that customers purchase presses with the safety device installed does not extinguish its strict liability for selling presses without the safety device. (Choice B) A superseding cause is an unforeseeable intervening act or force that breaks the chain of causation between the defendant's tortious conduct and the plaintiff's harm. Here, since presses were sold without the safety device, it was foreseeable that the worker's employer might purchase one without the device. Therefore, the company's purchase was not a superseding cause. (Choice D) Although a plaintiff must prove that a product was the cause of his/her injury to prevail on a strict products liability claim, the plaintiff must also prove that the product was defective.\n\nEducational objective: Strict products liability is imposed on a commercial seller whose defective product caused the plaintiff harm. A product is defective by design if (1) the design creates a foreseeable risk of physical harm and (2) that risk could have been mitigated by a reasonable alternative design (a safer design available at a reasonable cost).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability for defectively designed products).","educationalObjective":"Strict products liability is imposed on a commercial seller whose defective product caused the plaintiff harm. A product is defective by design if (1) the design creates a foreseeable risk of physical harm and (2) that risk could have been mitigated by a reasonable alternative design (a safer design available at a reasonable cost).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability for defectively designed products).","rule":"Strict products liability is imposed on a commercial seller whose defective product caused the plaintiff harm. A product is defective by design if (1) the design creates a foreseeable risk of physical harm and (2) that risk could have been mitigated by a reasonable alternative design (a safer design available at a reasonable cost).","irac":{"issue":"If the worker were to sue the company, would the worker be likely to prevail?","rule":"Strict products liability is imposed on a commercial seller whose defective product caused the plaintiff harm. A product is defective by design if (1) the design creates a foreseeable risk of physical harm and (2) that risk could have been mitigated by a reasonable alternative design (a safer design available at a reasonable cost).","analysis":"Types of product defects\n\nManufacturing Deviation from intended design (eg, incorrect assembly) defect\n\nDesign defect Foreseeable risk of harm that could have been mitigated by feasible alternative design (ie, reasonable in cost)\n\nInadequate No reasonable instructions or warnings about risks of harm warning\n\nStrict products liability is imposed on a commercial seller (eg, manufacturer, wholesaler, retailer) when its defective product causes the plaintiff harm. A product is defective by design when: the design creates a foreseeable risk of physical harm and that risk could have been mitigated by a reasonable alternative design (eg, a modification that reduces the risk for a reasonable cost). Here, the company's metal stamping presses posed a foreseeable risk of closing on a worker's hands. As a result, the company usually sold its presses with a pre-installed safety device that eliminated the risk of harm for a slightly increased cost (reasonable alternative design). But since the company still sold presses without the safety device (defective design) and that defect caused the press to crush the worker's hand, the company is strictly liable for the worker's harm. (Choice A) When a manufacturer has a reasonable alternative design, it cannot avoid strict liability by merely warning the purchaser of the product's danger. Therefore, the company's recommendation that customers purchase presses with the safety device installed does not extinguish its strict liability for selling presses without the safety device. (Choice B) A superseding cause is an unforeseeable intervening act or force that breaks the chain of causation between the defendant's tortious conduct and the plaintiff's harm. Here, since presses were sold without the safety device, it was foreseeable that the worker's employer might purchase one without the device. Therefore, the company's purchase was not a superseding cause. (Choice D) Although a plaintiff must prove that a product was the cause of his/her injury to prevail on a strict products liability claim, the plaintiff must also prove that the product was defective.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the company sold the press to the worker's employer without an installed safety device."},"questionZh":"一家公司生产的金属冲压机通常都配有安全装置，可以防止冲压机夹伤工人的手。\n\n该公司强烈建议客户购买时安装安全装置，但如果没有安全装置，则会以略低的价格出售。\n\n一位工人的雇主没有听从公司的建议，购买了一台没有安全装置的冲压机。\n\n结果，冲压机夹伤了工人的手，导致其受伤。\n\n如果该工人起诉公司，他胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为公司强烈建议该工人的雇主购买带有安全装置的冲压机。","B":"否，因为该工人的雇主没有购买带有安全装置的冲压机是导致工人受伤的直接原因。","C":"是，因为公司将没有安装安全装置的冲压机卖给了该工人的雇主。","D":"是的，因为该公司的印刷机确实是造成工人受伤的直接原因。"},"explanationZh":"产品缺陷的类型 制造缺陷：偏离预期设计（例如，组装错误）。 设计缺陷：存在可预见的伤害风险，而该风险本可通过可行的替代设计（即成本合理的方案）加以减轻。 警示不足：未就伤害风险提供合理的说明或警告。 当商业销售商（如制造商、批发商、零售商）的缺陷产品导致原告受到伤害时，该销售商须承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n若产品设计产生可预见的身体伤害风险，且该风险本可通过合理的替代设计（例如，以合理成本降低风险的改进方案）加以减轻，则该产品存在设计缺陷。\n\n在本案中，该公司的金属冲压机存在一种可预见的风险，即机器闭合时可能夹伤工人的手。\n\n因此，该公司通常在销售冲压机时预装安全装置，以消除伤害风险，且成本仅略有增加（这属于合理的替代设计）。\n\n然而，由于该公司也销售未配备该安全装置的冲压机（构成设计缺陷），且该缺陷导致工人的手被冲压机压伤，因此公司须对工人的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）当制造商拥有合理的替代设计方案时，仅向买方提示产品危险并不能免除其严格责任。\n\n因此，尽管公司建议客户购买安装了安全装置的冲压机，但这并不能免除其因销售未配备安全装置的冲压机而应承担的严格责任。\n\n（选项 B）“阻断性原因”（superseding cause）是指一种不可预见的介入行为或力量，它切断了被告侵权行为与原告伤害之间的因果链条。\n\n在本案中，由于公司销售了未配备安全装置的冲压机，工人的雇主购买未配备该装置的机器是可预见的。\n\n因此，雇主的购买行为不构成阻断性原因。\n\n（选项 D）虽然原告在主张严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）时必须证明产品是导致其受伤的原因，但原告同时也必须证明该产品存在缺陷。\n\n教育目标：当商业销售商的缺陷产品导致原告受到伤害时，该销售商须承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n若产品设计满足以下条件，则被视为存在设计缺陷：(1) 设计产生了可预见的身体伤害风险；(2) 该风险本可通过合理的替代设计（即成本合理且更安全的设计方案）加以减轻。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第1条、第2(b)条（美国法律学会，1998年）（关于设计缺陷产品的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）是指商业销售商因其缺陷产品造成原告损害而承担的责任。\n\n如果产品存在设计缺陷，则该产品属于设计缺陷产品，且 (1) 该设计会造成可预见的身体伤害风险，并且 (2) 该风险可以通过合理的替代设计（一种成本合理的更安全的设计）来降低。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果工人起诉该公司，他/她胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）是指商业销售商因其缺陷产品造成原告损害而承担的责任。\n\n如果产品存在设计缺陷，则该产品属于设计缺陷产品，且 (1) 该设计会造成可预见的身体伤害风险，并且 (2) 该风险可以通过合理的替代设计（一种成本合理的更安全的设计）来降低。","analysis":"产品缺陷的类型 制造缺陷：偏离预期设计（例如，组装错误）。 设计缺陷：存在可预见的伤害风险，而该风险本可通过可行的替代设计（即成本合理的方案）加以减轻。 警示不足：未就伤害风险提供合理的说明或警告。 当商业销售商（如制造商、批发商、零售商）的缺陷产品导致原告受到伤害时，该销售商须承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n若产品设计产生可预见的身体伤害风险，且该风险本可通过合理的替代设计（例如，以合理成本降低风险的改进方案）加以减轻，则该产品存在设计缺陷。\n\n在本案中，该公司的金属冲压机存在一种可预见的风险，即机器闭合时可能夹伤工人的手。\n\n因此，该公司通常在销售冲压机时预装安全装置，以消除伤害风险，且成本仅略有增加（这属于合理的替代设计）。\n\n然而，由于该公司也销售未配备该安全装置的冲压机（构成设计缺陷），且该缺陷导致工人的手被冲压机压伤，因此公司须对工人的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）当制造商拥有合理的替代设计方案时，仅向买方提示产品危险并不能免除其严格责任。\n\n因此，尽管公司建议客户购买安装了安全装置的冲压机，但这并不能免除其因销售未配备安全装置的冲压机而应承担的严格责任。\n\n（选项 B）“阻断性原因”（superseding cause）是指一种不可预见的介入行为或力量，它切断了被告侵权行为与原告伤害之间的因果链条。\n\n在本案中，由于公司销售了未配备安全装置的冲压机，工人的雇主购买未配备该装置的机器是可预见的。\n\n因此，雇主的购买行为不构成阻断性原因。\n\n（选项 D）虽然原告在主张严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）时必须证明产品是导致其受伤的原因，但原告同时也必须证明该产品存在缺陷。\n\n教育目标：当商业销售商的缺陷产品导致原告受到伤害时，该销售商须承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n若产品设计满足以下条件，则被视为存在设计缺陷：(1) 设计产生了可预见的身体伤害风险；(2) 该风险本可通过合理的替代设计（即成本合理且更安全的设计方案）加以减轻。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第1条、第2(b)条（美国法律学会，1998年）（关于设计缺陷产品的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是，因为公司将没有安装安全装置的冲压机卖给了该工人的雇主。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":194,"sourceQuestionPage":582,"sourceQuestionPages":[582],"sourceExplanationPages":[583,584],"sourceAnswerPages":[583,584],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-195","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A car enthusiast, who was 20 years old, purchased a new, high-powered sports car that was marketed with an intended and recognized appeal to youthful drivers. The car was designed with the capability to attain speeds in excess of 100 miles per hour. It was equipped with tires designed and tested only for a maximum safe speed of 85 miles per hour. The owner's manual that came with the car stated that \"continuous driving over 90 miles per hour requires high-speed-capability tires,\" but did not describe the speed capability of the tires sold with the car. The car enthusiast took his new car out for a spin on a straight, smooth country road where the posted speed limit was 55 miles per hour. Intending to test the car's power, he drove for a considerable distance at over 100 miles per hour. While he was doing so, the tread separated from the left rear tire, causing the car to leave the road and hit a tree. The car enthusiast sustained severe injuries. The car enthusiast has brought a strict products liability action in tort against the manufacturer of the car. Assume that pure comparative fault principles apply to this case. Will the car enthusiast prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the car enthusiast's driving at an excessive speed constituted a misuse of the car.","B":"No, because the car was not defective.","C":"Yes, if the statement in the manual concerning the tires did not adequately warn of the danger of high-speed driving on the tires mounted on the car.","D":"Yes, unless the car enthusiast's driving at a speed in excess of the posted speed limit was negligence per se that, by the law of the jurisdiction, was not excusable."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Adequacy of instructions/warnings\n\nContent & comprehensibility Unclear or ambiguous instructions Technical language Incomplete or excessive information Intensity of expression Text size Location (eg, manual v. product) Inconspicuous color Depiction of danger (eg, skull & crossbones) Expected users Children/adolescents Education/skill level Disability A commercial supplier is subject to strict products liability for harm caused by the commercial supplier's defective product. A product is defective for failure to warn if (1) the product posed a foreseeable and unobvious risk of harm and (2) that risk could have been reduced or eliminated by providing reasonable warnings or instructions. In assessing the reasonableness of a product's warnings or instructions, courts consider: the content and comprehensibility of the warnings or instructions the intensity (eg, prominence) of the warnings or instructions and the characteristics of the expected user groups. Here, while the 20-year-old car enthusiast was driving at over 100 miles per hour, the tread separated from a tire, causing him to crash into a tree and sustain injuries. The owner's manual warned that \"continuous driving over 90 miles per hour requires high-speed- capability tires.\" But it failed to warn expected users (youthful drivers prone to drive at high speed) that the car was not sold with high-speed-capability tires (inadequate content). That inadequate warning rendered the car defective, so the car enthusiast will prevail (Choice B). (Choice A) A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm resulting from the supplier's failure to provide adequate warnings or instructions regarding a foreseeable misuse of its product. Therefore, the foreseeability of high-speed driving by youthful drivers (misuse of the sports car) necessitates an adequate warning. (Choice D) Under the doctrine of negligence per se, an unexcused violation of a statute (eg, the car enthusiast's speeding violation) is automatically deemed negligent if the statute was designed to prevent the kind of accident that occurred. However, in a pure comparative fault jurisdiction (default rule), the car enthusiast's contributory negligence will merely reduce recovery by his share of fault—not bar his strict liability claim.\n\nEducational objective: A commercial supplier must provide reasonable instructions or warnings about any foreseeable and unobvious risk posed by its product. Reasonableness is determined by (1) the content and comprehensibility of the warnings or instructions, (2) the intensity of the warnings or instructions, and (3) the characteristics of the expected user groups.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(c) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (commercial sellers' liability for inadequate instructions or warnings).","educationalObjective":"A commercial supplier must provide reasonable instructions or warnings about any foreseeable and unobvious risk posed by its product. Reasonableness is determined by (1) the content and comprehensibility of the warnings or instructions, (2) the intensity of the warnings or instructions, and (3) the characteristics of the expected user groups.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(c) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (commercial sellers' liability for inadequate instructions or warnings).","rule":"A commercial supplier must provide reasonable instructions or warnings about any foreseeable and unobvious risk posed by its product. Reasonableness is determined by (1) the content and comprehensibility of the warnings or instructions, (2) the intensity of the warnings or instructions, and (3) the characteristics of the expected user groups.","irac":{"issue":"Will the car enthusiast prevail?","rule":"A commercial supplier must provide reasonable instructions or warnings about any foreseeable and unobvious risk posed by its product. Reasonableness is determined by (1) the content and comprehensibility of the warnings or instructions, (2) the intensity of the warnings or instructions, and (3) the characteristics of the expected user groups.","analysis":"Adequacy of instructions/warnings\n\nContent & comprehensibility Unclear or ambiguous instructions Technical language Incomplete or excessive information Intensity of expression Text size Location (eg, manual v. product) Inconspicuous color Depiction of danger (eg, skull & crossbones) Expected users Children/adolescents Education/skill level Disability A commercial supplier is subject to strict products liability for harm caused by the commercial supplier's defective product. A product is defective for failure to warn if (1) the product posed a foreseeable and unobvious risk of harm and (2) that risk could have been reduced or eliminated by providing reasonable warnings or instructions. In assessing the reasonableness of a product's warnings or instructions, courts consider: the content and comprehensibility of the warnings or instructions the intensity (eg, prominence) of the warnings or instructions and the characteristics of the expected user groups. Here, while the 20-year-old car enthusiast was driving at over 100 miles per hour, the tread separated from a tire, causing him to crash into a tree and sustain injuries. The owner's manual warned that \"continuous driving over 90 miles per hour requires high-speed- capability tires.\" But it failed to warn expected users (youthful drivers prone to drive at high speed) that the car was not sold with high-speed-capability tires (inadequate content). That inadequate warning rendered the car defective, so the car enthusiast will prevail (Choice B). (Choice A) A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm resulting from the supplier's failure to provide adequate warnings or instructions regarding a foreseeable misuse of its product. Therefore, the foreseeability of high-speed driving by youthful drivers (misuse of the sports car) necessitates an adequate warning. (Choice D) Under the doctrine of negligence per se, an unexcused violation of a statute (eg, the car enthusiast's speeding violation) is automatically deemed negligent if the statute was designed to prevent the kind of accident that occurred. However, in a pure comparative fault jurisdiction (default rule), the car enthusiast's contributory negligence will merely reduce recovery by his share of fault—not bar his strict liability claim.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, if the statement in the manual concerning the tires did not adequately warn of the danger of high-speed driving on the tires mounted on the car."},"questionZh":"一位20岁的汽车爱好者购买了一辆新款大马力跑车，该车在营销时明确针对年轻驾驶员群体，旨在吸引他们。\n\n该车的设计最高时速可超过每小时100英里，但配备的轮胎经设计和测试，其最高安全时速仅为每小时85英里。\n\n随车附带的用户手册指出“持续以超过每小时90英里的速度行驶需要具备高速性能的轮胎”，但并未说明原厂配备轮胎的具体速度极限。\n\n该爱好者驾驶新车在一条笔直平坦的乡村道路上行驶，该路段限速为每小时55英里。\n\n为了测试车辆的动力性能，他以超过每小时100英里的速度行驶了相当长的一段距离。\n\n在此过程中，左后轮胎胎面脱落，导致车辆冲出路面并撞上一棵树，该爱好者因此受重伤。\n\n该爱好者针对汽车制造商提起了严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）侵权诉讼。\n\n假设本案适用纯粹比较过错原则。\n\n该爱好者能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为该爱好者超速驾驶的行为构成了对车辆的滥用（misuse）。","B":"不能，因为该车辆不存在缺陷。","C":"能，如果手册中关于轮胎的说明未能充分警示使用原厂配备轮胎进行高速驾驶所带来的危险。","D":"能，除非该爱好者超过限速行驶的行为构成了“本身过失”（negligence per se），且根据该司法管辖区的法律，这种过失是不可免责的。"},"explanationZh":"说明书/警告的充分性 内容与易懂性：说明不清晰或含糊；使用技术术语；信息不完整或冗余。表达强度；字体大小；位置（例如：用户手册与产品本体）；颜色不醒目；危险标识（例如：骷髅交叉骨图案）。预期用户：儿童/青少年；教育/技能水平；残障状况。\n\n商业供应商对其缺陷产品造成的损害承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。如果产品存在（1）可预见且不明显的伤害风险，且（2）该风险本可通过提供合理的警告或说明来降低或消除，则该产品因未尽警告义务而被视为有缺陷。\n\n在评估产品警告或说明的合理性时，法院会考量：警告或说明的内容与易懂性；警告或说明的强度（例如：显著程度）；以及预期用户群体的特征。\n\n本案中，一名20岁的汽车爱好者在以超过每小时100英里的速度驾驶时，轮胎胎面脱落，导致其撞树受伤。\n\n车主手册中警告称：“持续以超过每小时90英里的速度行驶需要具备高速行驶能力的轮胎。”但手册未向预期用户（即倾向于高速驾驶的年轻驾驶员）明确告知该车出厂时并未配备具备高速行驶能力的轮胎（内容不充分）。\n\n这一不充分的警告使该车被认定为存在缺陷，因此该汽车爱好者将胜诉（选项B）。\n\n（选项A）对于因未就产品的可预见误用提供充分警告或说明而造成的损害，商业供应商承担严格责任。\n\n因此，鉴于年轻驾驶员高速驾驶（即对跑车的误用）属于可预见行为，供应商有必要提供充分的警告。\n\n（选项D）根据“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则，如果某项法规旨在预防所发生的此类事故，而当事人无正当理由违反了该法规（例如：该汽车爱好者超速行驶），则其行为自动被视为存在过失。\n\n然而，在纯粹比较过失（pure comparative fault）的司法管辖区（默认规则）内，该汽车爱好者自身的与有过失（contributory negligence）（contributory negligence）只会按其过失比例扣减赔偿金额，而不会导致其严格责任索赔请求被驳回。\n\n教育目标：商业供应商必须就其产品带来的任何可预见且不明显的风险，提供合理的说明或警告。\n\n合理性的判定取决于：(1) 警告或说明的内容及其易懂程度；(2) 警告或说明的强度；以及 (3) 预期用户群体的特征。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第1条、第2条(c)款（美国法律协会，1998年）（关于商业销售者因说明或警告不充分而承担的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商业供应商必须就其产品可能带来的任何可预见且非显而易见的风险，提供合理的说明或警示。\n\n合理性的判断标准包括：(1) 警示或说明的内容及易懂程度；(2) 警示或说明的强度；以及 (3) 预期用户群体的特征。","iracZh":{"issue":"该爱好者能胜诉吗？","rule":"商业供应商必须就其产品可能带来的任何可预见且非显而易见的风险，提供合理的说明或警示。\n\n合理性的判断标准包括：(1) 警示或说明的内容及易懂程度；(2) 警示或说明的强度；以及 (3) 预期用户群体的特征。","analysis":"说明书/警告的充分性 内容与易懂性：说明不清晰或含糊；使用技术术语；信息不完整或冗余。表达强度；字体大小；位置（例如：用户手册与产品本体）；颜色不醒目；危险标识（例如：骷髅交叉骨图案）。预期用户：儿童/青少年；教育/技能水平；残障状况。\n\n商业供应商对其缺陷产品造成的损害承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。如果产品存在（1）可预见且不明显的伤害风险，且（2）该风险本可通过提供合理的警告或说明来降低或消除，则该产品因未尽警告义务而被视为有缺陷。\n\n在评估产品警告或说明的合理性时，法院会考量：警告或说明的内容与易懂性；警告或说明的强度（例如：显著程度）；以及预期用户群体的特征。\n\n本案中，一名20岁的汽车爱好者在以超过每小时100英里的速度驾驶时，轮胎胎面脱落，导致其撞树受伤。\n\n车主手册中警告称：“持续以超过每小时90英里的速度行驶需要具备高速行驶能力的轮胎。”但手册未向预期用户（即倾向于高速驾驶的年轻驾驶员）明确告知该车出厂时并未配备具备高速行驶能力的轮胎（内容不充分）。\n\n这一不充分的警告使该车被认定为存在缺陷，因此该汽车爱好者将胜诉（选项B）。\n\n（选项A）对于因未就产品的可预见误用提供充分警告或说明而造成的损害，商业供应商承担严格责任。\n\n因此，鉴于年轻驾驶员高速驾驶（即对跑车的误用）属于可预见行为，供应商有必要提供充分的警告。\n\n（选项D）根据“本身过失”（negligence per se）原则，如果某项法规旨在预防所发生的此类事故，而当事人无正当理由违反了该法规（例如：该汽车爱好者超速行驶），则其行为自动被视为存在过失。\n\n然而，在纯粹比较过失（pure comparative fault）的司法管辖区（默认规则）内，该汽车爱好者自身的与有过失（contributory negligence）（contributory negligence）只会按其过失比例扣减赔偿金额，而不会导致其严格责任索赔请求被驳回。\n\n教育目标：商业供应商必须就其产品带来的任何可预见且不明显的风险，提供合理的说明或警告。\n\n合理性的判定取决于：(1) 警告或说明的内容及其易懂程度；(2) 警告或说明的强度；以及 (3) 预期用户群体的特征。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第1条、第2条(c)款（美国法律协会，1998年）（关于商业销售者因说明或警告不充分而承担的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：能，如果手册中关于轮胎的说明未能充分警示使用原厂配备轮胎进行高速驾驶所带来的危险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":195,"sourceQuestionPage":585,"sourceQuestionPages":[585],"sourceExplanationPages":[586,587],"sourceAnswerPages":[586,587],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-196","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A construction worker was using a nail gun to reshingle the roof of a residence. While the construction worker was near the edge of the roof, a manufacturing defect in the gun caused a nail to be discharged from the gun at an angle rather than directly downward. A 10-year- old child, who was playing in the yard below, was struck by the nail and seriously injured. The child's parents, on behalf of their child, have brought a strict products liability action against the construction company that employed the construction worker. The construction company can establish that, although the child was not negligent, her parents were negligent in allowing her to play near the construction work occurring on the roof. Can the construction company successfully defend against this action?","choices":{"A":"No, because the construction company is vicariously liable for the construction worker's conduct.","B":"No, because the nail gun used by the construction worker had a manufacturing defect.","C":"Yes, because the nail gun was used to provide a service.","D":"Yes, because the parents' contributory negligence will be imputed to the child to reduce the construction company's damages."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Strict products liability actions can only be maintained against commercial sellers who are in the business of selling or otherwise distributing the type of defective product that harmed the plaintiff. As a result, someone who merely used a defective product to provide a service is not subject to strict products liability for any harm to persons or property caused by the defect. Here, the child was injured because of a manufacturing defect in the nail gun. But since the construction worker—and vicariously the construction company—was using the nail gun to reshingle the roof, the defective product was merely used to provide a service. As a result, the construction company can successfully defend against this action for strict products liability. (Choice A) Employers can be held vicariously liable for an employee's tortious conduct under the doctrine of respondeat superior. But here, the construction worker (employee) is not strictly liable for the child's injury caused by the defective nail gun because the worker was not a commercial seller of the nail gun. As a result, the construction company (employer) is also not strictly liable. (Choice B) The nail gun did have a manufacturing defect, which could impose strict liability (ie, liability without proof of fault) on the manufacturer and other commercial sellers of the gun. But strict liability would not be imposed on someone who merely used the nail gun to provide a service (eg, reshingle a roof). (Choice D) Under the rule of pure comparative negligence (default rule on the MBE), a plaintiff's contributory negligence reduces the plaintiff's recovery by his/her proportionate share of fault. However, a parent's negligence will not be imputed (ie, assigned) to a child- plaintiff in a suit against a third party. And even if it were, this would merely reduce the child's damages—not serve as a complete defense to strict products liability.\n\nEducational objective: Someone who merely used a defective product to provide a service is not subject to strict products liability for harm to persons or property caused by the defect.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial suppliers).","educationalObjective":"Someone who merely used a defective product to provide a service is not subject to strict products liability for harm to persons or property caused by the defect.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial suppliers).","rule":"Someone who merely used a defective product to provide a service is not subject to strict products liability for harm to persons or property caused by the defect.","irac":{"issue":"Can the construction company successfully defend against this action?","rule":"Someone who merely used a defective product to provide a service is not subject to strict products liability for harm to persons or property caused by the defect.","analysis":"Strict products liability actions can only be maintained against commercial sellers who are in the business of selling or otherwise distributing the type of defective product that harmed the plaintiff. As a result, someone who merely used a defective product to provide a service is not subject to strict products liability for any harm to persons or property caused by the defect. Here, the child was injured because of a manufacturing defect in the nail gun. But since the construction worker—and vicariously the construction company—was using the nail gun to reshingle the roof, the defective product was merely used to provide a service. As a result, the construction company can successfully defend against this action for strict products liability. (Choice A) Employers can be held vicariously liable for an employee's tortious conduct under the doctrine of respondeat superior. But here, the construction worker (employee) is not strictly liable for the child's injury caused by the defective nail gun because the worker was not a commercial seller of the nail gun. As a result, the construction company (employer) is also not strictly liable. (Choice B) The nail gun did have a manufacturing defect, which could impose strict liability (ie, liability without proof of fault) on the manufacturer and other commercial sellers of the gun. But strict liability would not be imposed on someone who merely used the nail gun to provide a service (eg, reshingle a roof). (Choice D) Under the rule of pure comparative negligence (default rule on the MBE), a plaintiff's contributory negligence reduces the plaintiff's recovery by his/her proportionate share of fault. However, a parent's negligence will not be imputed (ie, assigned) to a child- plaintiff in a suit against a third party. And even if it were, this would merely reduce the child's damages—not serve as a complete defense to strict products liability.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the nail gun was used to provide a service."},"questionZh":"一名建筑工人正在使用射钉枪为一处住宅重新铺设屋顶瓦片。\n\n当该工人位于屋顶边缘附近时，射钉枪的制造缺陷导致射出的钉子呈一定角度飞出，而非直接向下射出。\n\n一名在下方院子里玩耍的10岁儿童被该钉子击中并受重伤。\n\n该儿童的父母代表其子女，向雇佣该工人的建筑公司提起了严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n建筑公司能够证明，尽管该儿童本人并无过失，但其父母在允许孩子于屋顶施工现场附近玩耍时存在过失。\n\n建筑公司能否成功抗辩该诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为建筑公司需对该建筑工人的行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。","B":"不能，因为该建筑工人使用的射钉枪存在制造缺陷。","C":"能，因为该射钉枪是用于提供服务的。","D":"能，因为父母的与有过失（contributory negligence）将被归责于该儿童，从而减轻建筑公司的损害赔偿责任。"},"explanationZh":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼只能针对从事销售或分销导致原告受损的那类缺陷产品的商业销售商提起。\n\n因此，仅使用缺陷产品来提供服务的人，不对该缺陷造成的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n在本案中，儿童因射钉枪的制造缺陷而受伤。\n\n但由于建筑工人（以及作为替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）承担者的建筑公司）是使用该射钉枪来重新铺设屋顶瓦片，该缺陷产品仅被用于提供服务。\n\n因此，建筑公司可以成功抗辩针对其提起的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n（选项 A）根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），雇主可能需对雇员的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n但在此案中，建筑工人（雇员）不对因缺陷射钉枪导致儿童受伤承担严格责任，因为该工人并非射钉枪的商业销售商。\n\n因此，建筑公司（雇主）也不承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 B）该射钉枪确实存在制造缺陷，这可能使制造商及其他商业销售商承担严格责任（即无需证明过错的责任）。\n\n但对于仅使用该射钉枪提供服务（例如重新铺设屋顶）的人，则不适用严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）根据纯粹比较过失规则（MBE 考试中的默认规则），原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）会按其过失比例扣减其获赔金额。\n\n然而，在针对第三方的诉讼中，父母的过失不会被归责于（即转嫁给）作为原告的儿童。\n\n即使发生归责，这也只会减少儿童的损害赔偿额，而不能作为严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼的完全抗辩理由。\n\n教育目标：仅使用缺陷产品提供服务的人，不对该缺陷造成的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1、20 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于商业供应商的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"仅因提供服务而使用缺陷产品的人，无需就该缺陷造成的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。","iracZh":{"issue":"建筑公司能否成功抗辩该诉讼？","rule":"仅因提供服务而使用缺陷产品的人，无需就该缺陷造成的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。","analysis":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼只能针对从事销售或分销导致原告受损的那类缺陷产品的商业销售商提起。\n\n因此，仅使用缺陷产品来提供服务的人，不对该缺陷造成的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n在本案中，儿童因射钉枪的制造缺陷而受伤。\n\n但由于建筑工人（以及作为替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）承担者的建筑公司）是使用该射钉枪来重新铺设屋顶瓦片，该缺陷产品仅被用于提供服务。\n\n因此，建筑公司可以成功抗辩针对其提起的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n（选项 A）根据“雇主责任原则”（respondeat superior），雇主可能需对雇员的侵权行为承担替代责任（vicarious liability）（vicarious liability）。\n\n但在此案中，建筑工人（雇员）不对因缺陷射钉枪导致儿童受伤承担严格责任，因为该工人并非射钉枪的商业销售商。\n\n因此，建筑公司（雇主）也不承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 B）该射钉枪确实存在制造缺陷，这可能使制造商及其他商业销售商承担严格责任（即无需证明过错的责任）。\n\n但对于仅使用该射钉枪提供服务（例如重新铺设屋顶）的人，则不适用严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）根据纯粹比较过失规则（MBE 考试中的默认规则），原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）会按其过失比例扣减其获赔金额。\n\n然而，在针对第三方的诉讼中，父母的过失不会被归责于（即转嫁给）作为原告的儿童。\n\n即使发生归责，这也只会减少儿童的损害赔偿额，而不能作为严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼的完全抗辩理由。\n\n教育目标：仅使用缺陷产品提供服务的人，不对该缺陷造成的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1、20 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于商业供应商的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：能，因为该射钉枪是用于提供服务的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-7c72a73b70797559"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":196,"sourceQuestionPage":588,"sourceQuestionPages":[588],"sourceExplanationPages":[589,590],"sourceAnswerPages":[589,590],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-197","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A homeowner owned a large poisonous snake which had been defanged and was kept in a cage. A storm damaged the homeowner's house and the snake's cage, allowing the snake to escape. During the cleanup after the storm, a volunteer worker came across the snake. The worker tried to run away from the snake and fell, breaking his arm. In a suit by the worker against the homeowner based on strict liability in tort to recover for his injury, will the worker prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the snake's escape was caused by a force of nature.","B":"No, because the worker should have anticipated an injury during his volunteer work.","C":"Yes, because the homeowner did not take adequate precautions to secure the snake.","D":"Yes, because the worker's injury was the result of his fear of the escaped snake."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Strict animal liability\n\nLivestock Wild animals Abnormally dangerous animals\n\nCattle Lions Rabid dogs/cats Goats Monkeys Aggressive birds Horses Coyotes Skittish horses Deer Zebras Dangerously playful dogs Rabbits Venomous snakes Strict liability (liability without proof of fault) is imposed for certain activities that create unusual risks for other people—eg, owning or possessing a wild animal. An animal is wild if that category of animal typically is undomesticated and likely to cause personal injury unless restrained. A defendant is strictly liable for physical harm caused by his/her wild animal, but only if that harm stemmed from the animal's abnormally dangerous characteristic. Here, although the homeowner's poisonous snake was defanged, it falls into a wild animal category because snakes typically are undomesticated and likely to cause personal injury (poisoning) unless restrained. And since the worker did not know that the snake was defanged and broke his arm while trying to run from its seemingly venomous bite, his harm stemmed from the dangerous characteristic of snakes. Therefore, the homeowner will be strictly liable for that harm. (Choice A) Because there is very little social utility in owning wild animals, strict liability applies even if a force of nature (whether foreseeable or unforeseeable) allows a wild animal to escape. (Choice B) In a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction (default rule), contributory negligence will reduce—but not bar—the plaintiff's recovery. As a result, even if the worker negligently contributed to his harm by failing to anticipate an injury, he would still prevail (but his recovery would be reduced by his share of fault). (Choice C) Strict liability does not require proof of fault. Therefore, the homeowner is strictly liable even if he took adequate precautions to secure the snake.\n\nEducational objective: A wild animal is a category of animal that typically is undomesticated and likely to cause personal injury unless restrained (eg, chimpanzee, tarantula, poisonous frog). The owner of a wild animal is strictly liable for physical harm stemming from the animal's abnormally dangerous characteristic.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (strict liability for wild animals).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 29 cmt. l (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (strict liability limited to wild animal's abnormally dangerous characteristic).","educationalObjective":"A wild animal is a category of animal that typically is undomesticated and likely to cause personal injury unless restrained (eg, chimpanzee, tarantula, poisonous frog). The owner of a wild animal is strictly liable for physical harm stemming from the animal's abnormally dangerous characteristic.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (strict liability for wild animals).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 29 cmt. l (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (strict liability limited to wild animal's abnormally dangerous characteristic).","rule":"A wild animal is a category of animal that typically is undomesticated and likely to cause personal injury unless restrained (eg, chimpanzee, tarantula, poisonous frog). The owner of a wild animal is strictly liable for physical harm stemming from the animal's abnormally dangerous characteristic.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit by the worker against the homeowner based on strict liability in tort to recover for his injury, will the worker prevail?","rule":"A wild animal is a category of animal that typically is undomesticated and likely to cause personal injury unless restrained (eg, chimpanzee, tarantula, poisonous frog). The owner of a wild animal is strictly liable for physical harm stemming from the animal's abnormally dangerous characteristic.","analysis":"Strict animal liability\n\nLivestock Wild animals Abnormally dangerous animals\n\nCattle Lions Rabid dogs/cats Goats Monkeys Aggressive birds Horses Coyotes Skittish horses Deer Zebras Dangerously playful dogs Rabbits Venomous snakes Strict liability (liability without proof of fault) is imposed for certain activities that create unusual risks for other people—eg, owning or possessing a wild animal. An animal is wild if that category of animal typically is undomesticated and likely to cause personal injury unless restrained. A defendant is strictly liable for physical harm caused by his/her wild animal, but only if that harm stemmed from the animal's abnormally dangerous characteristic. Here, although the homeowner's poisonous snake was defanged, it falls into a wild animal category because snakes typically are undomesticated and likely to cause personal injury (poisoning) unless restrained. And since the worker did not know that the snake was defanged and broke his arm while trying to run from its seemingly venomous bite, his harm stemmed from the dangerous characteristic of snakes. Therefore, the homeowner will be strictly liable for that harm. (Choice A) Because there is very little social utility in owning wild animals, strict liability applies even if a force of nature (whether foreseeable or unforeseeable) allows a wild animal to escape. (Choice B) In a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction (default rule), contributory negligence will reduce—but not bar—the plaintiff's recovery. As a result, even if the worker negligently contributed to his harm by failing to anticipate an injury, he would still prevail (but his recovery would be reduced by his share of fault). (Choice C) Strict liability does not require proof of fault. Therefore, the homeowner is strictly liable even if he took adequate precautions to secure the snake.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the worker's injury was the result of his fear of the escaped snake."},"questionZh":"一位房主养了一条大型毒蛇，该蛇已被拔除毒牙并关在笼子里。\n\n一场风暴损坏了房主的房屋和蛇笼，导致蛇逃脱。\n\n在风暴过后的清理工作中，一名志愿者偶遇了这条蛇。\n\n该志愿者试图逃离，结果摔倒并导致手臂骨折。\n\n如果该志愿者基于侵权法中的严格责任原则起诉房主以寻求赔偿，他能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为蛇的逃脱是由自然力造成的。","B":"不能，因为志愿者在从事志愿工作时本应预料到可能发生伤害。","C":"能，因为房主未采取充分的预防措施来控制这条蛇。","D":"能，因为志愿者的伤害是因其对逃脱的蛇感到恐惧而造成的。"},"explanationZh":"针对动物的严格责任 家畜 野生动物 具有异常危险性的动物 牛 狮子 患狂犬病的猫/狗 山羊 猴子 具有使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）性的鸟类 马 郊狼 易受惊的马 鹿 斑马 玩耍时具有危险性的狗 兔子 毒蛇 严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担责任）适用于某些给他人带来异常风险的活动——例如，拥有或占有野生动物。\n\n如果某类动物通常未被驯化，且若不加约束极易造成人身伤害，则该类动物被视为野生动物。\n\n被告对其野生动物造成的人身伤害承担严格责任，但前提是该伤害源于动物的异常危险特性。\n\n在本案中，尽管房主的毒蛇已被拔除毒牙，但它仍属于野生动物范畴，因为蛇类通常未被驯化，且若不加约束极易造成人身伤害（中毒）。\n\n此外，由于工人不知道蛇的毒牙已被拔除，在试图躲避看似有毒的咬伤时摔断了手臂，因此他所受的伤害源于蛇类固有的危险特性。\n\n因此，房主将对该伤害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）由于拥有野生动物的社会效用极低，即使因自然力（无论是否可预见）导致野生动物逃脱，严格责任原则依然适用。\n\n（选项 B）在纯粹比较过失管辖区（默认规则）内，原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）会减少其获赔金额，但不会完全排除其获赔权利。\n\n因此，即使工人因未能预见伤害而对自身受到的伤害负有一定过失责任，他仍能胜诉（但其获赔金额会根据其过失比例相应扣减）。\n\n（选项 C）严格责任不要求证明过失的存在。\n\n因此，即使房主已采取充分的预防措施来控制这条蛇，他仍需承担严格责任。\n\n教育目标：野生动物是指通常未被驯化且若不加约束极易造成人身伤害的动物类别（例如黑猩猩、捕鸟蛛、毒蛙）。\n\n野生动物的所有者对其动物因异常危险特性而造成的人身伤害承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 22 条（美国法律协会，2010 年）（关于野生动物的严格责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第29条评注l（美国法律协会，2010年）（严格责任仅限于野生动物异常危险的特性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"野生动物是指通常未被驯化且若不加约束便可能造成人身伤害的动物类别（例如：黑猩猩、捕鸟蛛、毒蛙）。\n\n野生动物的所有者对于因该动物异常危险的特性而造成的人身伤害，须承担严格责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该志愿者基于侵权法中的严格责任原则起诉房主以寻求赔偿，他能胜诉吗？","rule":"野生动物是指通常未被驯化且若不加约束便可能造成人身伤害的动物类别（例如：黑猩猩、捕鸟蛛、毒蛙）。\n\n野生动物的所有者对于因该动物异常危险的特性而造成的人身伤害，须承担严格责任。","analysis":"针对动物的严格责任 家畜 野生动物 具有异常危险性的动物 牛 狮子 患狂犬病的猫/狗 山羊 猴子 具有使人恐惧即将遭受侵害（assault）性的鸟类 马 郊狼 易受惊的马 鹿 斑马 玩耍时具有危险性的狗 兔子 毒蛇 严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担责任）适用于某些给他人带来异常风险的活动——例如，拥有或占有野生动物。\n\n如果某类动物通常未被驯化，且若不加约束极易造成人身伤害，则该类动物被视为野生动物。\n\n被告对其野生动物造成的人身伤害承担严格责任，但前提是该伤害源于动物的异常危险特性。\n\n在本案中，尽管房主的毒蛇已被拔除毒牙，但它仍属于野生动物范畴，因为蛇类通常未被驯化，且若不加约束极易造成人身伤害（中毒）。\n\n此外，由于工人不知道蛇的毒牙已被拔除，在试图躲避看似有毒的咬伤时摔断了手臂，因此他所受的伤害源于蛇类固有的危险特性。\n\n因此，房主将对该伤害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）由于拥有野生动物的社会效用极低，即使因自然力（无论是否可预见）导致野生动物逃脱，严格责任原则依然适用。\n\n（选项 B）在纯粹比较过失管辖区（默认规则）内，原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）会减少其获赔金额，但不会完全排除其获赔权利。\n\n因此，即使工人因未能预见伤害而对自身受到的伤害负有一定过失责任，他仍能胜诉（但其获赔金额会根据其过失比例相应扣减）。\n\n（选项 C）严格责任不要求证明过失的存在。\n\n因此，即使房主已采取充分的预防措施来控制这条蛇，他仍需承担严格责任。\n\n教育目标：野生动物是指通常未被驯化且若不加约束极易造成人身伤害的动物类别（例如黑猩猩、捕鸟蛛、毒蛙）。\n\n野生动物的所有者对其动物因异常危险特性而造成的人身伤害承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 22 条（美国法律协会，2010 年）（关于野生动物的严格责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第29条评注l（美国法律协会，2010年）（严格责任仅限于野生动物异常危险的特性）。","conclusion":"答案 D：能，因为志愿者的伤害是因其对逃脱的蛇感到恐惧而造成的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"wild animal","ipa":"/wˈaɪld ˈænəməl/","syllables":"wild an·i·mal","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"野生动物：饲养人通常对其危险倾向造成的损害承担严格责任","contextualMeaning":"野生动物：饲养人通常对其危险倾向造成的损害承担严格责任","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A wild animal is a category of animal that typically is undomesticated and likely to cause personal injury unless restrained (eg, chimpanzee, tarantula, poisonous frog).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":197,"sourceQuestionPage":591,"sourceQuestionPages":[591],"sourceExplanationPages":[592,593],"sourceAnswerPages":[592,593],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-198","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A homeowner was injured when an automatic cutoff switch failed to function on a snowblower he was using. The cutoff switch had functioned well for a year after he purchased the snowblower but failed after the machine had been improperly repaired by a mechanic. The snowblower's operating manual contained a clear and prominent warning against making the very alteration to the switch mechanism that was made by the mechanic. The mechanic, however, did not have a manual available when he repaired the snowblower. Does the homeowner have a viable claim against the manufacturer of the snowblower for damages?","choices":{"A":"No, because the homeowner was contributorily negligent in failing to furnish the snowblower's manual to the mechanic.","B":"No, because the injury resulted from a substantial alteration of the snowblower by a third party.","C":"Yes, because a defect in the snowblower caused the homeowner's injury.","D":"Yes, because the manufacturer should have made the manual available to repair personnel."},"answer":"B","explanation":"When a product poses a foreseeable risk of harm (eg, injury resulting from an unsafe repair), commercial suppliers must provide reasonable warnings or instructions if doing so would reduce that risk. Failure to provide adequate warnings or instructions renders the product defective and subjects commercial suppliers to strict products liability. Here, the homeowner's injury occurred when his snowblower's automatic cutoff switch failed due to the mechanic's improper repair (foreseeable risk). But since the manufacturer provided a clear and prominent warning about that very alteration in the operating manual (reasonable warning) and there is no evidence that the snowblower was otherwise defective, the manufacturer is not liable for the homeowner's damages (Choice C). (Choice A) Contributory negligence occurs when a plaintiff fails to use reasonable care for his/her own protection. Here, the homeowner was not contributorily negligent in failing to furnish the manual to the mechanic because the homeowner could reasonably rely on the mechanic's expertise. (Choice D) A commercial supplier is only required to provide reasonable warnings or instructions. Here, the manufacturer could not identify every repair person who might work on its snowblowers, so it would be unreasonable to impose strict liability on the manufacturer for failing to provide a manual to the mechanic.\n\nEducational objective: To avoid strict products liability, a commercial seller must provide reasonable warnings or instructions regarding any foreseeable risk of harm posed by its product if doing so will reduce that risk. Failure to provide adequate warnings or instructions renders the product defective.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 2 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (product defective due to the lack of reasonable warnings or instructions).","educationalObjective":"To avoid strict products liability, a commercial seller must provide reasonable warnings or instructions regarding any foreseeable risk of harm posed by its product if doing so will reduce that risk. Failure to provide adequate warnings or instructions renders the product defective.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 2 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (product defective due to the lack of reasonable warnings or instructions).","rule":"To avoid strict products liability, a commercial seller must provide reasonable warnings or instructions regarding any foreseeable risk of harm posed by its product if doing so will reduce that risk. Failure to provide adequate warnings or instructions renders the product defective.","irac":{"issue":"Does the homeowner have a viable claim against the manufacturer of the snowblower for damages?","rule":"To avoid strict products liability, a commercial seller must provide reasonable warnings or instructions regarding any foreseeable risk of harm posed by its product if doing so will reduce that risk. Failure to provide adequate warnings or instructions renders the product defective.","analysis":"When a product poses a foreseeable risk of harm (eg, injury resulting from an unsafe repair), commercial suppliers must provide reasonable warnings or instructions if doing so would reduce that risk. Failure to provide adequate warnings or instructions renders the product defective and subjects commercial suppliers to strict products liability. Here, the homeowner's injury occurred when his snowblower's automatic cutoff switch failed due to the mechanic's improper repair (foreseeable risk). But since the manufacturer provided a clear and prominent warning about that very alteration in the operating manual (reasonable warning) and there is no evidence that the snowblower was otherwise defective, the manufacturer is not liable for the homeowner's damages (Choice C). (Choice A) Contributory negligence occurs when a plaintiff fails to use reasonable care for his/her own protection. Here, the homeowner was not contributorily negligent in failing to furnish the manual to the mechanic because the homeowner could reasonably rely on the mechanic's expertise. (Choice D) A commercial supplier is only required to provide reasonable warnings or instructions. Here, the manufacturer could not identify every repair person who might work on its snowblowers, so it would be unreasonable to impose strict liability on the manufacturer for failing to provide a manual to the mechanic.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the injury resulted from a substantial alteration of the snowblower by a third party."},"questionZh":"一位房主在使用除雪机时，因机器上的自动切断开关失灵而受伤。\n\n该切断开关在房主购机后的一年内运作正常，但在经由一名技工不当维修后失效。\n\n除雪机的操作手册中包含一项清晰且醒目的警告，明确禁止对开关机构进行技工所实施的那种改动。\n\n然而，技工在维修除雪机时并未查阅该手册。\n\n房主是否有正当理由就损害赔偿向除雪机制造商提出索赔？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为房主未能向技工提供除雪机手册，存在促成损害发生的过失（contributory negligence）。","B":"没有，因为伤害是由第三方对除雪机进行的重大改动造成的。","C":"有，因为除雪机的缺陷导致了房主的伤害。","D":"有，因为制造商本应确保维修人员能够获取该手册。"},"explanationZh":"当产品存在可预见的伤害风险（例如，因不安全维修导致的伤害）时，如果提供合理的警告或说明能够降低该风险，商业供应商必须提供此类警告或说明。\n\n若未能提供充分的警告或说明，产品即被视为存在缺陷，商业供应商须承担严格的产品责任。\n\n在本案中，房主的受伤是由于维修工的不当维修导致除雪机的自动切断开关失效所致（属于可预见的风险）。\n\n然而，由于制造商已在操作手册中针对此类改动提供了清晰且醒目的警告（属于合理的警告），且无证据表明除雪机存在其他缺陷，因此制造商无需对房主的损失承担责任（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 A）“与有过失（contributory negligence）”是指原告未能采取合理的注意措施来保护自身安全。\n\n在本案中，房主未向维修工提供操作手册并不构成与有过失（contributory negligence），因为房主有理由信赖维修工的专业技能。\n\n（选项 D）商业供应商仅需提供合理的警告或说明。\n\n在本案中，制造商无法确定所有可能维修其除雪机的人员，因此，若因制造商未向维修工提供手册而对其施加严格责任，则是不合理的。\n\n教育目标：为避免承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），商业销售商必须针对其产品可能带来的任何可预见伤害风险提供合理的警告或说明（前提是此举能降低该风险）。\n\n未能提供充分的警告或说明会导致产品被认定为存在缺陷。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 2 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（因缺乏合理的警告或说明而导致产品存在缺陷）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为避免承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），商业销售商必须针对其产品可能造成的任何可预见伤害风险，提供合理的警告或说明（前提是此举能降低该风险）。\n\n未能提供充分的警告或说明将导致产品被视为存在缺陷。","iracZh":{"issue":"房主是否有正当理由就损害赔偿向除雪机制造商提出索赔？","rule":"为避免承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），商业销售商必须针对其产品可能造成的任何可预见伤害风险，提供合理的警告或说明（前提是此举能降低该风险）。\n\n未能提供充分的警告或说明将导致产品被视为存在缺陷。","analysis":"当产品存在可预见的伤害风险（例如，因不安全维修导致的伤害）时，如果提供合理的警告或说明能够降低该风险，商业供应商必须提供此类警告或说明。\n\n若未能提供充分的警告或说明，产品即被视为存在缺陷，商业供应商须承担严格的产品责任。\n\n在本案中，房主的受伤是由于维修工的不当维修导致除雪机的自动切断开关失效所致（属于可预见的风险）。\n\n然而，由于制造商已在操作手册中针对此类改动提供了清晰且醒目的警告（属于合理的警告），且无证据表明除雪机存在其他缺陷，因此制造商无需对房主的损失承担责任（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 A）“与有过失（contributory negligence）”是指原告未能采取合理的注意措施来保护自身安全。\n\n在本案中，房主未向维修工提供操作手册并不构成与有过失（contributory negligence），因为房主有理由信赖维修工的专业技能。\n\n（选项 D）商业供应商仅需提供合理的警告或说明。\n\n在本案中，制造商无法确定所有可能维修其除雪机的人员，因此，若因制造商未向维修工提供手册而对其施加严格责任，则是不合理的。\n\n教育目标：为避免承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），商业销售商必须针对其产品可能带来的任何可预见伤害风险提供合理的警告或说明（前提是此举能降低该风险）。\n\n未能提供充分的警告或说明会导致产品被认定为存在缺陷。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 2 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（因缺乏合理的警告或说明而导致产品存在缺陷）。","conclusion":"答案 B：没有，因为伤害是由第三方对除雪机进行的重大改动造成的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-3eac218a9ce62000"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":198,"sourceQuestionPage":594,"sourceQuestionPages":[594],"sourceExplanationPages":[595,596],"sourceAnswerPages":[595,596],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-199","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A consumer bought a kitchen blender from a manufacturer. Soon after the purchase, the consumer was using the blender in an appropriate way when the blender jar shattered, throwing a piece of glass into the consumer's eye. The consumer brought an action against the manufacturer based solely on strict products liability. The consumer's expert testified that the blender was defectively designed. However, because the blender jar had been destroyed in the accident, the expert could not determine whether the accident had been caused by the design defect or a manufacturing defect. The manufacturer's expert testified that the blender was not defective. If, at the conclusion of the evidence, both parties move for directed verdicts, how should the trial judge rule?","choices":{"A":"Deny both motions and send the case to the jury, because a jury reasonably could conclude that the harm was caused by a defect present in the product when it was sold.","B":"Direct a verdict for the consumer, because the blender was new when the jar shattered, and thus was undeniably defective.","C":"Direct a verdict for the manufacturer, because the consumer's action was brought solely on a strict liability theory.","D":"Direct a verdict for the manufacturer, because the consumer's expert was unable to specify the nature of the defect."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A motion for a directed verdict should be granted if no reasonable jury could find in the nonmovant's favor. For example, a defendant's motion should be granted if there is insufficient evidence to support any element of the plaintiff's claim. Conversely, a plaintiff's motion should be granted if every element of the claim has been established beyond dispute. A strict products liability claim requires proof of the following elements: The defendant was a commercial seller (eg, manufacturer—as seen here).\n\nThe product was defective at the time it left the defendant's control.\n\nThe defect caused the plaintiff physical harm (eg, bodily injury—as seen here).\n\nThe trier of fact can infer that the product was defective and caused the plaintiff's harm if that harm (1) is of a kind that ordinarily results from a product defect and (2) was not solely due to other causes. This is true even when the plaintiff lacks direct evidence on these last two elements. Here, the consumer's expert witness could not specify the nature of the blender's defect. But the jury may infer that the blender was defective when purchased from the manufacturer. That is because (1) a new blender jar typically shatters because of a defect and (2) the consumer's appropriate use soon after the purchase negates other causes. As a result, the judge should deny the manufacturer's motion (Choices C & D). However, the fact that the blender was new when the jar shattered does not require the jury to conclude that the blender was undeniably defective. The jury may infer that the blender was defective OR rely on the manufacturer's expert testimony and conclude that it was not. Therefore, the judge should deny both motions and send the case to the jury (Choice B).\n\nEducational objective: In a strict products liability action, it can be inferred that a product was defective and caused the plaintiff's harm if that harm is of a kind that ordinarily results from a product defect AND was not solely due to other causes.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (requirements for strict products liability).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 3 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (inference of product defect).","educationalObjective":"In a strict products liability action, it can be inferred that a product was defective and caused the plaintiff's harm if that harm is of a kind that ordinarily results from a product defect AND was not solely due to other causes.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (requirements for strict products liability).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 3 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (inference of product defect).","rule":"In a strict products liability action, it can be inferred that a product was defective and caused the plaintiff's harm if that harm is of a kind that ordinarily results from a product defect AND was not solely due to other causes.","irac":{"issue":"If, at the conclusion of the evidence, both parties move for directed verdicts, how should the trial judge rule?","rule":"In a strict products liability action, it can be inferred that a product was defective and caused the plaintiff's harm if that harm is of a kind that ordinarily results from a product defect AND was not solely due to other causes.","analysis":"A motion for a directed verdict should be granted if no reasonable jury could find in the nonmovant's favor. For example, a defendant's motion should be granted if there is insufficient evidence to support any element of the plaintiff's claim. Conversely, a plaintiff's motion should be granted if every element of the claim has been established beyond dispute. A strict products liability claim requires proof of the following elements: The defendant was a commercial seller (eg, manufacturer—as seen here).\n\nThe product was defective at the time it left the defendant's control.\n\nThe defect caused the plaintiff physical harm (eg, bodily injury—as seen here).\n\nThe trier of fact can infer that the product was defective and caused the plaintiff's harm if that harm (1) is of a kind that ordinarily results from a product defect and (2) was not solely due to other causes. This is true even when the plaintiff lacks direct evidence on these last two elements. Here, the consumer's expert witness could not specify the nature of the blender's defect. But the jury may infer that the blender was defective when purchased from the manufacturer. That is because (1) a new blender jar typically shatters because of a defect and (2) the consumer's appropriate use soon after the purchase negates other causes. As a result, the judge should deny the manufacturer's motion (Choices C & D). However, the fact that the blender was new when the jar shattered does not require the jury to conclude that the blender was undeniably defective. The jury may infer that the blender was defective OR rely on the manufacturer's expert testimony and conclude that it was not. Therefore, the judge should deny both motions and send the case to the jury (Choice B).","conclusion":"Answer A: Deny both motions and send the case to the jury, because a jury reasonably could conclude that the harm was caused by a defect present in the product when it was sold."},"questionZh":"一位消费者从某制造商处购买了一台厨房搅拌机。\n\n购买后不久，该消费者在使用搅拌机时，搅拌杯突然破碎，玻璃碎片飞入其眼中。\n\n该消费者仅依据严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）对制造商提起诉讼。\n\n消费者的专家证词表明，该搅拌机存在设计缺陷。\n\n然而，由于搅拌杯在事故中损毁，专家无法确定事故是由设计缺陷还是制造缺陷造成的。\n\n制造商的专家证词则表明该搅拌机不存在缺陷。\n\n如果双方在证据提交完毕后均提出指示性判决动议，审判法官应如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回双方的动议，并将案件提交陪审团审理，因为陪审团有理由认定损害是由产品在售出时存在的缺陷造成的。","B":"指示性判决支持消费者，因为搅拌机在搅拌杯破碎时是全新的，因此毫无疑问存在缺陷。","C":"指示性判决支持制造商，因为消费者的诉讼完全基于严格责任理论。","D":"指示性判决支持制造商，因为消费者的专家无法具体说明缺陷的性质。"},"explanationZh":"如果没有任何理性的陪审团能够做出有利于非动议方（即未提出动议的一方）的裁决，则应准予关于指示裁决（directed verdict）的动议。\n\n例如，如果证据不足以支持原告主张的任何构成要件，则应准予被告提出的动议。\n\n反之，如果原告主张的所有构成要件均已确立且无争议，则应准予原告提出的动议。\n\n严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）之诉要求证明以下要件：被告属于商业销售者（例如本案中的制造商）。\n\n产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷。\n\n该缺陷导致原告遭受人身损害（例如本案中的身体伤害）。\n\n如果损害 (1) 属于通常由产品缺陷引起的类型，且 (2) 并非完全由其他原因造成，则事实认定者（陪审团）可以推断该产品存在缺陷并导致了原告的损害。\n\n即使原告缺乏关于后两个要件的直接证据，此原则依然适用。\n\n在本案中，消费者的专家证人无法具体说明搅拌机缺陷的性质。\n\n但陪审团可以推断，该搅拌机在从制造商处购得时即存在缺陷。\n\n这是因为：(1) 新搅拌机杯体破碎通常是由于缺陷造成的；(2) 消费者在购买后不久的正确使用排除了其他原因。\n\n因此，法官应驳回制造商的动议（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n然而，杯体破碎时搅拌机尚属全新这一事实，并不强制要求陪审团得出搅拌机确有缺陷的结论。\n\n陪审团既可以推断搅拌机存在缺陷，也可以采信制造商的专家证词并得出其不存在缺陷的结论。\n\n因此，法官应驳回双方的动议，并将案件交由陪审团审理（选项 B）。\n\n教学目标：在严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中，如果损害属于通常由产品缺陷引起的类型，且并非完全由其他原因造成，则可推断产品存在缺陷并导致了原告的损害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的构成要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》\n\n§ 3 (美国法律协会 1998年)（关于产品缺陷的推断）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中，如果损害通常是由产品缺陷造成的，并且并非完全由其他原因造成，则可以推断产品存在缺陷并导致了原告的损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果在证据提交完毕后，双方都提出指示性判决动议，审判法官应如何裁决？","rule":"在严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中，如果损害通常是由产品缺陷造成的，并且并非完全由其他原因造成，则可以推断产品存在缺陷并导致了原告的损害。","analysis":"如果没有任何理性的陪审团能够做出有利于非动议方（即未提出动议的一方）的裁决，则应准予关于指示裁决（directed verdict）的动议。\n\n例如，如果证据不足以支持原告主张的任何构成要件，则应准予被告提出的动议。\n\n反之，如果原告主张的所有构成要件均已确立且无争议，则应准予原告提出的动议。\n\n严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）之诉要求证明以下要件：被告属于商业销售者（例如本案中的制造商）。\n\n产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷。\n\n该缺陷导致原告遭受人身损害（例如本案中的身体伤害）。\n\n如果损害 (1) 属于通常由产品缺陷引起的类型，且 (2) 并非完全由其他原因造成，则事实认定者（陪审团）可以推断该产品存在缺陷并导致了原告的损害。\n\n即使原告缺乏关于后两个要件的直接证据，此原则依然适用。\n\n在本案中，消费者的专家证人无法具体说明搅拌机缺陷的性质。\n\n但陪审团可以推断，该搅拌机在从制造商处购得时即存在缺陷。\n\n这是因为：(1) 新搅拌机杯体破碎通常是由于缺陷造成的；(2) 消费者在购买后不久的正确使用排除了其他原因。\n\n因此，法官应驳回制造商的动议（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n然而，杯体破碎时搅拌机尚属全新这一事实，并不强制要求陪审团得出搅拌机确有缺陷的结论。\n\n陪审团既可以推断搅拌机存在缺陷，也可以采信制造商的专家证词并得出其不存在缺陷的结论。\n\n因此，法官应驳回双方的动议，并将案件交由陪审团审理（选项 B）。\n\n教学目标：在严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中，如果损害属于通常由产品缺陷引起的类型，且并非完全由其他原因造成，则可推断产品存在缺陷并导致了原告的损害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的构成要件）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》\n\n§ 3 (美国法律协会 1998年)（关于产品缺陷的推断）。","conclusion":"答案 A：驳回双方的动议，并将案件提交陪审团审理，因为陪审团有理由认定损害是由产品在售出时存在的缺陷造成的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-c50f0b5fe933495a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":199,"sourceQuestionPage":597,"sourceQuestionPages":[597],"sourceExplanationPages":[598,599],"sourceAnswerPages":[598,599],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-200","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A farmer owns a small farm with several head of cattle, which are kept in a fenced grazing area. One day the cattle were frightened by a thunderstorm, an occasional occurrence in the area. The cattle broke through the fence, entered onto a neighbor's property, and severely damaged the neighbor's crops. Under the law of the state, landowners are not required to erect fences to prevent the intrusion of livestock. If the neighbor sues the farmer to recover for the damage done to his crops, will the neighbor prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the thunderstorm was a force of nature.","B":"No, unless the fence was negligently maintained by the farmer.","C":"Yes, because the farmer's cattle caused the damage to the neighbor's crops.","D":"Yes, if the farmer's cattle had panicked during previous thunderstorms."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Imposition of strict liability\n\n(liability imposed regardless of fault)\n\nAbnormally Uncommon activity in community that poses foreseeable & highly dangerous activities significant risk of physical harm Risk cannot be minimized by reasonable care and Physical harm caused by abnormal danger Intrusion by Foreseeable intrusion of livestock onto land of another and livestock Intrusion causes physical harm typical of livestock (eg, consumption of grass) Wild animals Category of typically undomesticated animal likely to cause personal injury unless restrained (eg, poisonous snake) and Physical harm caused by animal's abnormal danger Abnormally Animal with dangerous tendencies other animals in that category dangerous animals typically lack (eg, rabid dog) Owner/possessor knows or should know of danger and Physical harm results from that danger Strict products Harm caused by commercial supplier's defective product and liability Defect existed when product left commercial supplier's control Strict liability (liability without proof of fault) is imposed when livestock or other animals (not dogs or cats) enter another's land and cause bodily harm or property damage typical of livestock (eg, consumption of grass). But since the cause of the intrusion must be foreseeable, strict liability is not imposed when the intrusion is caused by an unforeseeable event or force—eg, stranger opens cattle gate, unpredictable tornado destroys fence. Here, the farmer's cattle broke through his fence because they were frightened by a thunderstorm—an occasional (and therefore foreseeable) occurrence in the area. And since the cattle intruded onto the neighbor's land and damaged his crops, the farmer is strictly liable for that damage. (Choice A) Because the thunderstorm was a foreseeable force of nature, it does not relieve the farmer of strict liability. (Choice B) Although the farmer kept his cattle in a fenced area, he had no duty to erect a fence to prevent the cattle from escaping under state law. And since strict liability is imposed without proof of fault, the farmer's negligent maintenance of the fence is irrelevant. (Choice D) While the intervening event that caused the intrusion must have been foreseeable to impose strict liability, the effect of that event need not have been. Therefore,\n\nthe farmer would be strictly liable even if his cattle had never panicked during a previous thunderstorm.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant is strictly liable when his/her livestock foreseeably enter onto another's land and cause physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage) typical of livestock. However, a defendant is not strictly liable for a livestock intrusion caused by an unforeseeable act or force—eg, an unprecedented earthquake.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (strict liability for livestock intrusion).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 34 reporter's note to cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (foreseeable force of nature does not negate strict liability).","educationalObjective":"A defendant is strictly liable when his/her livestock foreseeably enter onto another's land and cause physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage) typical of livestock. However, a defendant is not strictly liable for a livestock intrusion caused by an unforeseeable act or force—eg, an unprecedented earthquake.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (strict liability for livestock intrusion).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 34 reporter's note to cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (foreseeable force of nature does not negate strict liability).","rule":"A defendant is strictly liable when his/her livestock foreseeably enter onto another's land and cause physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage) typical of livestock. However, a defendant is not strictly liable for a livestock intrusion caused by an unforeseeable act or force—eg, an unprecedented earthquake.","irac":{"issue":"If the neighbor sues the farmer to recover for the damage done to his crops, will the neighbor prevail?","rule":"A defendant is strictly liable when his/her livestock foreseeably enter onto another's land and cause physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage) typical of livestock. However, a defendant is not strictly liable for a livestock intrusion caused by an unforeseeable act or force—eg, an unprecedented earthquake.","analysis":"Imposition of strict liability\n\n(liability imposed regardless of fault)\n\nAbnormally Uncommon activity in community that poses foreseeable & highly dangerous activities significant risk of physical harm Risk cannot be minimized by reasonable care and Physical harm caused by abnormal danger Intrusion by Foreseeable intrusion of livestock onto land of another and livestock Intrusion causes physical harm typical of livestock (eg, consumption of grass) Wild animals Category of typically undomesticated animal likely to cause personal injury unless restrained (eg, poisonous snake) and Physical harm caused by animal's abnormal danger Abnormally Animal with dangerous tendencies other animals in that category dangerous animals typically lack (eg, rabid dog) Owner/possessor knows or should know of danger and Physical harm results from that danger Strict products Harm caused by commercial supplier's defective product and liability Defect existed when product left commercial supplier's control Strict liability (liability without proof of fault) is imposed when livestock or other animals (not dogs or cats) enter another's land and cause bodily harm or property damage typical of livestock (eg, consumption of grass). But since the cause of the intrusion must be foreseeable, strict liability is not imposed when the intrusion is caused by an unforeseeable event or force—eg, stranger opens cattle gate, unpredictable tornado destroys fence. Here, the farmer's cattle broke through his fence because they were frightened by a thunderstorm—an occasional (and therefore foreseeable) occurrence in the area. And since the cattle intruded onto the neighbor's land and damaged his crops, the farmer is strictly liable for that damage. (Choice A) Because the thunderstorm was a foreseeable force of nature, it does not relieve the farmer of strict liability. (Choice B) Although the farmer kept his cattle in a fenced area, he had no duty to erect a fence to prevent the cattle from escaping under state law. And since strict liability is imposed without proof of fault, the farmer's negligent maintenance of the fence is irrelevant. (Choice D) While the intervening event that caused the intrusion must have been foreseeable to impose strict liability, the effect of that event need not have been. Therefore,\n\nthe farmer would be strictly liable even if his cattle had never panicked during a previous thunderstorm.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the farmer's cattle caused the damage to the neighbor's crops."},"questionZh":"一位农民拥有一座小型农场，养了几头牛，这些牛被圈养在设有围栏的放牧区内。\n\n一天，牛群因雷暴（该地区偶尔会发生此类天气）而受惊。\n\n牛群冲破围栏，闯入邻居的土地，并严重损毁了邻居的农作物。\n\n根据该州法律，土地所有者没有义务建造围栏以防止牲畜入侵。\n\n如果邻居起诉该农民，要求赔偿其农作物遭受的损失，邻居会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为雷暴属于自然力。","B":"不会，除非该农民对围栏的维护存在疏忽。","C":"会，因为是该农民的牛造成了邻居农作物的损失。","D":"会，如果该农民的牛在以往的雷暴中也曾受惊失控。"},"explanationZh":"严格责任的适用 （无论有无过错均须承担的责任） 异常危险活动：在社区中极不寻常、构成可预见的重大身体伤害风险且无法通过合理注意将风险降至最低的活动；由该异常危险造成的身体伤害。牲畜侵入：牲畜可预见地侵入他人土地，并造成了牲畜特有的损害（如啃食草木）。野生动物：通常未被驯化、若不加约束极易造成人身伤害的动物类别（如毒蛇）；由动物的异常危险特性造成的身体伤害。异常危险动物：具有该类动物通常不具备的危险倾向的动物（如患狂犬病的狗）；所有者/占有者已知或应知该危险，且该危险导致了身体伤害。产品严格责任：由商业供应商提供的缺陷产品造成的损害；缺陷在产品脱离商业供应商控制时即已存在。当牲畜或其他动物（不包括猫狗）进入他人土地并造成人身伤害或牲畜特有的财产损失（如啃食草木）时，适用严格责任（即无需证明过错的责任）。\n\n但由于侵入的原因必须是可预见的，若侵入是由不可预见的事件或外力引起的（例如陌生人打开牛栏门，或不可预测的龙卷风摧毁围栏），则不适用严格责任。\n\n在本案中，农民的牛群因受雷暴惊吓而冲破围栏——雷暴在该地区属于偶发（因而是可预见）的事件。\n\n由于牛群侵入了邻居的土地并损坏了庄稼，该农民须对这些损害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）由于雷暴是可预见的自然力，它不能免除农民的严格责任。\n\n（选项 B）尽管农民将牛群圈养在围栏内，但根据州法律，他并无义务必须建造围栏以防止牛群逃逸。\n\n且由于严格责任的适用无需证明过错，农民维护围栏不当的事实与责任认定无关。\n\n（选项 D）虽然导致侵入的介入事件必须是可预见的方可适用严格责任，但该事件的具体后果无需是可预见的。\n\n因此， 即使该农民的牛群此前从未在雷暴中受惊，他也仍须承担严格责任。\n\n教育目标：当被告的牲畜可预见地进入他人土地并造成牲畜特有的身体伤害（即人身伤害或财产损失）时，被告须承担严格责任。\n\n然而，如果牲畜的侵入是由不可预见的作为或外力（例如史无前例的地震）引起的，则被告无需承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第21条（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于牲畜侵入的严格责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第34条，关于评注d的报告人说明（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于可预见的自然力不排除严格责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当被告的牲畜可预见地进入他人土地并造成牲畜特有的实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）时，被告须承担严格责任。\n\n然而，如果牲畜的入侵是由不可预见的事件或力量（例如前所未有的地震）引起的，则被告无需承担严格责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果邻居起诉该农民，要求赔偿其农作物遭受的损失，邻居会胜诉吗？","rule":"当被告的牲畜可预见地进入他人土地并造成牲畜特有的实质性损害（即人身伤害或财产损失）时，被告须承担严格责任。\n\n然而，如果牲畜的入侵是由不可预见的事件或力量（例如前所未有的地震）引起的，则被告无需承担严格责任。","analysis":"严格责任的适用 （无论有无过错均须承担的责任） 异常危险活动：在社区中极不寻常、构成可预见的重大身体伤害风险且无法通过合理注意将风险降至最低的活动；由该异常危险造成的身体伤害。牲畜侵入：牲畜可预见地侵入他人土地，并造成了牲畜特有的损害（如啃食草木）。野生动物：通常未被驯化、若不加约束极易造成人身伤害的动物类别（如毒蛇）；由动物的异常危险特性造成的身体伤害。异常危险动物：具有该类动物通常不具备的危险倾向的动物（如患狂犬病的狗）；所有者/占有者已知或应知该危险，且该危险导致了身体伤害。产品严格责任：由商业供应商提供的缺陷产品造成的损害；缺陷在产品脱离商业供应商控制时即已存在。当牲畜或其他动物（不包括猫狗）进入他人土地并造成人身伤害或牲畜特有的财产损失（如啃食草木）时，适用严格责任（即无需证明过错的责任）。\n\n但由于侵入的原因必须是可预见的，若侵入是由不可预见的事件或外力引起的（例如陌生人打开牛栏门，或不可预测的龙卷风摧毁围栏），则不适用严格责任。\n\n在本案中，农民的牛群因受雷暴惊吓而冲破围栏——雷暴在该地区属于偶发（因而是可预见）的事件。\n\n由于牛群侵入了邻居的土地并损坏了庄稼，该农民须对这些损害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）由于雷暴是可预见的自然力，它不能免除农民的严格责任。\n\n（选项 B）尽管农民将牛群圈养在围栏内，但根据州法律，他并无义务必须建造围栏以防止牛群逃逸。\n\n且由于严格责任的适用无需证明过错，农民维护围栏不当的事实与责任认定无关。\n\n（选项 D）虽然导致侵入的介入事件必须是可预见的方可适用严格责任，但该事件的具体后果无需是可预见的。\n\n因此， 即使该农民的牛群此前从未在雷暴中受惊，他也仍须承担严格责任。\n\n教育目标：当被告的牲畜可预见地进入他人土地并造成牲畜特有的身体伤害（即人身伤害或财产损失）时，被告须承担严格责任。\n\n然而，如果牲畜的侵入是由不可预见的作为或外力（例如史无前例的地震）引起的，则被告无需承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第21条（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于牲畜侵入的严格责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神伤害责任》第34条，关于评注d的报告人说明（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于可预见的自然力不排除严格责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，因为是该农民的牛造成了邻居农作物的损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":200,"sourceQuestionPage":600,"sourceQuestionPages":[600],"sourceExplanationPages":[601,602],"sourceAnswerPages":[601,602],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-201","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A manufacturer of baby gear designed a baby bouncer that allowed infants to jump up and down without parental assistance. The baby bouncer could be attached to a doorframe with a plastic clamp, which was connected to the baby seat via a spring and safety cord. The manufacturer designed the baby bouncer to safely withstand a maximum weight of 20 pounds, but if an infant exceeded that weight, there was a risk that the clamp could slip off of the doorframe and severely injure the infant. For a slightly higher cost, the manufacturer could have used a metal clamp that withstood a maximum weight of 40 pounds, but the manufacturer opted for the cheaper plastic clamp so that it could undercut its competitors. The manufacturer attached a warning label to the baby bouncer stating the weight limit and that if the limit was exceeded, the clamp could slip off the doorframe and injure an infant. A mother purchased the manufacturer's baby bouncer for her nine-month-old infant who weighed 25 pounds. She read the warning label but chose to ignore the weight limit. After a few uses, the clamp slipped off the doorframe, and the infant suffered a concussion and broken leg from the fall. The mother brought a strict products liability action against the manufacturer in a jurisdiction that applies traditional contributory negligence. Who will likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"The manufacturer, because it put a warning label on the baby bouncer regarding the weight limit.","B":"The manufacturer, because the mother chose to ignore the weight limit on the warning label.","C":"The mother, because the baby bouncer was defectively designed.","D":"The mother, because the weight limit was the result of a manufacturing defect."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Assumption of the risk\n\n(defense to strict liability)\n\nTraditional contributory Bars recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction him/herself to known risk\n\nPure comparative Reduces recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction* him/herself to known risk Narrow exception: Bars recovery when plaintiff received benefit (eg, financial gain, employment) from his/her exposure to risk * Default rule on MBE.\n\nA plaintiff can prevail on a strict products liability claim if the defendant is a commercial supplier (eg, manufacturer) and its defective product caused the plaintiff harm. Under the risk-utility test, a product is defective by design when: the design creates a foreseeable risk of harm and\n\nthat risk could have been mitigated by a reasonable alternative design (eg, a modification that reduces the risk for a reasonable cost).\n\nHowever, in jurisdictions that apply the traditional rules of contributory negligence (as seen here), assumption of the risk completely bars a plaintiff's recovery for strict products liability when the plaintiff (1) knows of the product's risk of harm and (2) voluntarily uses the product despite that risk. Here, the manufacturer's baby bouncer used a plastic clamp that could only withstand a maximum weight of 20 pounds. This created a foreseeable risk that the clamp could slip off and severely injure an infant. And this risk of harm could have been mitigated by using a metal clamp for a slightly higher cost. As a result, the bouncer was defectively designed (Choice C). But the mother assumed this risk because she read the warning label describing the risk (knowledge of risk) and chose to ignore it (voluntary use). Therefore, the manufacturer will likely prevail. (Choice A) The fact that the manufacturer put a warning label on the baby bouncer regarding the weight limit is relevant to establishing that the mother knew about the risk of harm. But this fact alone does not absolve the manufacturer from strict products liability. (Choice D) A product has a manufacturing defect if it deviates from the manufacturer's intended design (eg, due to incorrect assembly). Here, the baby bouncer was manufactured\n\naccording to the manufacturer's design, so its weight limit was not the result of a manufacturing defect.\n\nEducational objective: In a traditional contributory-negligence jurisdiction, the plaintiff's assumption of the risk— ie, voluntary use of a product despite its known risk of harm—is a complete bar to recovery for strict products liability.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (defining strict products liability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts: § 523 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (explaining strict liability and assumption of the risk).","educationalObjective":"In a traditional contributory-negligence jurisdiction, the plaintiff's assumption of the risk— ie, voluntary use of a product despite its known risk of harm—is a complete bar to recovery for strict products liability.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (defining strict products liability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts: § 523 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (explaining strict liability and assumption of the risk).","rule":"In a traditional contributory-negligence jurisdiction, the plaintiff's assumption of the risk— ie, voluntary use of a product despite its known risk of harm—is a complete bar to recovery for strict products liability.","irac":{"issue":"Who will likely prevail?","rule":"In a traditional contributory-negligence jurisdiction, the plaintiff's assumption of the risk— ie, voluntary use of a product despite its known risk of harm—is a complete bar to recovery for strict products liability.","analysis":"Assumption of the risk\n\n(defense to strict liability)\n\nTraditional contributory Bars recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction him/herself to known risk\n\nPure comparative Reduces recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction* him/herself to known risk Narrow exception: Bars recovery when plaintiff received benefit (eg, financial gain, employment) from his/her exposure to risk * Default rule on MBE.\n\nA plaintiff can prevail on a strict products liability claim if the defendant is a commercial supplier (eg, manufacturer) and its defective product caused the plaintiff harm. Under the risk-utility test, a product is defective by design when: the design creates a foreseeable risk of harm and\n\nthat risk could have been mitigated by a reasonable alternative design (eg, a modification that reduces the risk for a reasonable cost).\n\nHowever, in jurisdictions that apply the traditional rules of contributory negligence (as seen here), assumption of the risk completely bars a plaintiff's recovery for strict products liability when the plaintiff (1) knows of the product's risk of harm and (2) voluntarily uses the product despite that risk. Here, the manufacturer's baby bouncer used a plastic clamp that could only withstand a maximum weight of 20 pounds. This created a foreseeable risk that the clamp could slip off and severely injure an infant. And this risk of harm could have been mitigated by using a metal clamp for a slightly higher cost. As a result, the bouncer was defectively designed (Choice C). But the mother assumed this risk because she read the warning label describing the risk (knowledge of risk) and chose to ignore it (voluntary use). Therefore, the manufacturer will likely prevail. (Choice A) The fact that the manufacturer put a warning label on the baby bouncer regarding the weight limit is relevant to establishing that the mother knew about the risk of harm. But this fact alone does not absolve the manufacturer from strict products liability. (Choice D) A product has a manufacturing defect if it deviates from the manufacturer's intended design (eg, due to incorrect assembly). Here, the baby bouncer was manufactured\n\naccording to the manufacturer's design, so its weight limit was not the result of a manufacturing defect.","conclusion":"Answer B: The manufacturer, because the mother chose to ignore the weight limit on the warning label."},"questionZh":"一家婴儿用品制造商设计了一款婴儿弹跳椅，允许婴儿在没有父母协助的情况下上下跳跃。\n\n该弹跳椅通过一个塑料夹固定在门框上，夹子与婴儿座椅之间由弹簧和安全绳连接。\n\n制造商设计的弹跳椅安全承重上限为20磅；若婴儿体重超过此限，夹子便有从门框滑落并导致婴儿严重受伤的风险。\n\n制造商本可选择成本稍高、承重上限达40磅的金属夹，但为了在价格上压倒竞争对手，最终选择了成本较低的塑料夹。\n\n制造商在弹跳椅上贴了警告标签，标明了重量限制，并说明若超过该限制，夹子可能会从门框滑落并伤害婴儿。\n\n一位母亲为自己九个月大、体重25磅的婴儿购买了这款弹跳椅。\n\n她阅读了警告标签，但选择无视其中的重量限制。\n\n使用几次后，夹子从门框滑落，导致婴儿摔伤，造成脑震荡和腿部骨折。\n\n该母亲在一个适用传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区，针对制造商提起了严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n谁更有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"制造商胜诉，因为它在弹跳椅上贴了关于重量限制的警告标签。","B":"制造商胜诉，因为母亲选择无视警告标签上的重量限制。","C":"母亲胜诉，因为该弹跳椅存在设计缺陷。","D":"母亲胜诉，因为重量限制问题源于制造缺陷。"},"explanationZh":"自担风险原则 （针对严格责任的抗辩） 传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”法域：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则丧失获得赔偿的权利。 纯粹“比较过失”法域：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则赔偿金额会相应减少。狭义例外：若原告因置身于该风险中而获得了某种利益（如经济收益、就业机会），则丧失获得赔偿的权利。\n\n（注：这是MBE考试中的默认规则。） 若被告属于商业供应商（如制造商），且其缺陷产品导致原告受到伤害，原告即可在严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中胜诉。\n\n根据“风险-效用”测试（risk-utility test），若产品设计存在以下情况，则视为存在设计缺陷：该设计产生了可预见的伤害风险，且 通过合理的替代设计（例如，以合理成本降低风险的改进方案）本可减轻该风险。\n\n然而，在适用传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”规则的法域（如本案情形），若原告 (1) 知晓产品存在伤害风险，且 (2) 明知该风险仍自愿使用该产品，则“自担风险”原则将完全排除原告基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）获得赔偿的权利。\n\n本案中，制造商生产的婴儿弹跳椅使用了一种最大承重仅为20磅的塑料夹。\n\n这造成了一种可预见的风险，即夹子可能滑脱并导致婴儿严重受伤。\n\n而通过使用成本稍高的金属夹，本可减轻这种伤害风险。\n\n因此，该弹跳椅存在设计缺陷（选项C）。\n\n但这位母亲承担了这一风险，因为她阅读了说明风险的警告标签（知晓风险），并选择无视该警告（自愿使用）。\n\n因此，制造商很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项A）制造商在婴儿弹跳椅上贴有关于承重限制的警告标签，这一事实对于认定母亲知晓伤害风险具有相关性。\n\n但仅凭这一事实，并不能免除制造商的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n（选项D）如果产品偏离了制造商预期的设计（例如，由于组装错误），则该产品存在制造缺陷。\n\n本案中，该婴儿弹跳椅是 按照制造商的设计制造的，因此其承重限制并非制造缺陷所致。\n\n教学目标：在实行传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区，原告承担风险的行为——即明知产品存在致害风险仍自愿使用该产品——构成针对严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔的绝对抗辩事由（即完全阻却原告获得赔偿）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第1条（美国法律协会，1998年）（定义了严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第523条（美国法律协会，1977年）（阐述了严格责任与承担风险原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在适用传统与有过失（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区，原告的“风险自担”（即明知产品存在伤害风险仍自愿使用）行为，构成对严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔的完全抗辩（即导致原告无法获得赔偿）。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁更有可能胜诉？","rule":"在适用传统与有过失（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区，原告的“风险自担”（即明知产品存在伤害风险仍自愿使用）行为，构成对严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔的完全抗辩（即导致原告无法获得赔偿）。","analysis":"自担风险原则 （针对严格责任的抗辩） 传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”法域：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则丧失获得赔偿的权利。 纯粹“比较过失”法域：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则赔偿金额会相应减少。狭义例外：若原告因置身于该风险中而获得了某种利益（如经济收益、就业机会），则丧失获得赔偿的权利。\n\n（注：这是MBE考试中的默认规则。） 若被告属于商业供应商（如制造商），且其缺陷产品导致原告受到伤害，原告即可在严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中胜诉。\n\n根据“风险-效用”测试（risk-utility test），若产品设计存在以下情况，则视为存在设计缺陷：该设计产生了可预见的伤害风险，且 通过合理的替代设计（例如，以合理成本降低风险的改进方案）本可减轻该风险。\n\n然而，在适用传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”规则的法域（如本案情形），若原告 (1) 知晓产品存在伤害风险，且 (2) 明知该风险仍自愿使用该产品，则“自担风险”原则将完全排除原告基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）获得赔偿的权利。\n\n本案中，制造商生产的婴儿弹跳椅使用了一种最大承重仅为20磅的塑料夹。\n\n这造成了一种可预见的风险，即夹子可能滑脱并导致婴儿严重受伤。\n\n而通过使用成本稍高的金属夹，本可减轻这种伤害风险。\n\n因此，该弹跳椅存在设计缺陷（选项C）。\n\n但这位母亲承担了这一风险，因为她阅读了说明风险的警告标签（知晓风险），并选择无视该警告（自愿使用）。\n\n因此，制造商很可能会胜诉。\n\n（选项A）制造商在婴儿弹跳椅上贴有关于承重限制的警告标签，这一事实对于认定母亲知晓伤害风险具有相关性。\n\n但仅凭这一事实，并不能免除制造商的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n（选项D）如果产品偏离了制造商预期的设计（例如，由于组装错误），则该产品存在制造缺陷。\n\n本案中，该婴儿弹跳椅是 按照制造商的设计制造的，因此其承重限制并非制造缺陷所致。\n\n教学目标：在实行传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区，原告承担风险的行为——即明知产品存在致害风险仍自愿使用该产品——构成针对严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔的绝对抗辩事由（即完全阻却原告获得赔偿）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第1条（美国法律协会，1998年）（定义了严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第523条（美国法律协会，1977年）（阐述了严格责任与承担风险原则）。","conclusion":"答案 B：制造商胜诉，因为母亲选择无视警告标签上的重量限制。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":201,"sourceQuestionPage":603,"sourceQuestionPages":[603],"sourceExplanationPages":[604,605],"sourceAnswerPages":[604,605],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-202","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A tanker truck filled with a flammable gas properly displayed the warning signs required by the U.S. Department of Transportation. As the truck driver was driving on an interstate highway, he failed to signal when changing lanes as required by a state statute. As a result, an automobile driver traveling in the next lane had to swerve sharply onto the shoulder of the road. Due to debris on the shoulder and the fact that he was speeding, the driver lost control of the automobile. In the resulting accident, the automobile driver suffered serious injuries. The automobile driver brought a strict liability action against the truck driver to recover personal-injury damages. In a contributory negligence jurisdiction, is the automobile driver likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the accident was not caused by the transportation of the hazardous chemicals.","B":"No, because the automobile driver's own negligence bars his recovery.","C":"Yes, because the transportation of hazardous chemicals is an abnormally dangerous activity.","D":"Yes, because the truck driver violated a statute by failing to signal when changing lanes."},"answer":"A","explanation":"An abnormally dangerous activity is an uncommon activity that creates a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm (ie, bodily injury or property damage) even when reasonable care is used. A defendant can be held strictly liable for an abnormally dangerous activity (eg, transporting hazardous chemicals) if the plaintiff's harm results from the risk that made the activity abnormally dangerous in the first place. Here, the truck driver was transporting a flammable gas (hazardous chemical), which is traditionally considered abnormally dangerous because of the highly significant risk of fire or explosion that it poses.* However, the automobile driver swerved and lost control of his car because the truck driver unexpectedly changed lanes without signaling—a hazard unrelated to the abnormal dangers of transporting hazardous chemicals. Therefore, the automobile driver is unlikely to prevail in his strict liability action (Choice C). *Some modern courts have found that transportation of hazardous chemicals does not qualify as an abnormally dangerous activity because of its current frequency. (Choice B) In contributory negligence jurisdictions (including this one), the plaintiff's own negligence is a complete bar to recovery in a negligence action. But it is no defense to strict liability (ie, it does not bar recovery).** Therefore, the fact that the automobile driver's speeding contributed to the accident does not bar his recovery. **In comparative negligence jurisdictions, courts are divided as to whether the plaintiff's own negligence will reduce the plaintiff's recovery by his/her proportionate share of fault in a strict liability action. (Choice D) The truck driver did violate a statute by failing to signal when changing lanes. This was negligent per se because the statute was designed to protect persons like the automobile driver from traffic accidents. But proof of negligence is irrelevant to this strict liability action.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant may be held strictly liable for an abnormally dangerous activity if the plaintiff's harm results from the risk that made the activity abnormally dangerous in the first place.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical and Emotional Harm § 20 (strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities).","educationalObjective":"A defendant may be held strictly liable for an abnormally dangerous activity if the plaintiff's harm results from the risk that made the activity abnormally dangerous in the first place.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical and Emotional Harm § 20 (strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities).","rule":"A defendant may be held strictly liable for an abnormally dangerous activity if the plaintiff's harm results from the risk that made the activity abnormally dangerous in the first place.","irac":{"issue":"In a contributory negligence jurisdiction, is the automobile driver likely to prevail?","rule":"A defendant may be held strictly liable for an abnormally dangerous activity if the plaintiff's harm results from the risk that made the activity abnormally dangerous in the first place.","analysis":"An abnormally dangerous activity is an uncommon activity that creates a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm (ie, bodily injury or property damage) even when reasonable care is used. A defendant can be held strictly liable for an abnormally dangerous activity (eg, transporting hazardous chemicals) if the plaintiff's harm results from the risk that made the activity abnormally dangerous in the first place. Here, the truck driver was transporting a flammable gas (hazardous chemical), which is traditionally considered abnormally dangerous because of the highly significant risk of fire or explosion that it poses.* However, the automobile driver swerved and lost control of his car because the truck driver unexpectedly changed lanes without signaling—a hazard unrelated to the abnormal dangers of transporting hazardous chemicals. Therefore, the automobile driver is unlikely to prevail in his strict liability action (Choice C). *Some modern courts have found that transportation of hazardous chemicals does not qualify as an abnormally dangerous activity because of its current frequency. (Choice B) In contributory negligence jurisdictions (including this one), the plaintiff's own negligence is a complete bar to recovery in a negligence action. But it is no defense to strict liability (ie, it does not bar recovery).** Therefore, the fact that the automobile driver's speeding contributed to the accident does not bar his recovery. **In comparative negligence jurisdictions, courts are divided as to whether the plaintiff's own negligence will reduce the plaintiff's recovery by his/her proportionate share of fault in a strict liability action. (Choice D) The truck driver did violate a statute by failing to signal when changing lanes. This was negligent per se because the statute was designed to protect persons like the automobile driver from traffic accidents. But proof of negligence is irrelevant to this strict liability action.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the accident was not caused by the transportation of the hazardous chemicals."},"questionZh":"一辆装载易燃气体的油罐车按规定展示了美国交通部要求的警示标志。\n\n卡车司机在州际公路上行驶时，变道未按州法律规定打转向灯。\n\n结果，在相邻车道行驶的一名轿车司机被迫急打方向盘，驶向路肩。\n\n由于路肩上有杂物且该司机当时超速行驶，导致车辆失控。\n\n在随后的事故中，轿车司机受了重伤。\n\n该轿车司机针对卡车司机提起了严格责任诉讼，要求赔偿人身伤害损失。\n\n在适用“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）原则的司法管辖区，该轿车司机胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为事故并非由运输危险化学品这一行为引起。","B":"不会，因为轿车司机自身的过失阻碍了其获得赔偿。","C":"会，因为运输危险化学品属于异常危险活动。","D":"会，因为卡车司机在变道时未打转向灯，违反了法律规定。"},"explanationZh":"“异常危险活动”是指一种不常见的活动，即使采取了合理的注意措施，仍会产生可预见的、极高的人身伤害（即身体伤害或财产损失）风险。\n\n如果原告遭受的损害是由导致该活动被定性为“异常危险”的特定风险所引起的，则被告可能需对该异常危险活动（例如运输危险化学品）承担严格责任。\n\n在本案中，卡车司机正在运输易燃气体（属于危险化学品），这种活动传统上被视为异常危险，因为它存在引发火灾或爆炸的极高风险。然而，轿车司机转向并失控，是因为卡车司机在未打转向灯的情况下突然变道——这种危险与运输危险化学品本身固有的异常危险无关。\n\n因此，轿车司机不太可能在严格责任诉讼中胜诉（选项 C）。\n\n*一些现代法院认定，鉴于运输危险化学品在当今社会已十分频繁，它不再被归类为异常危险活动。\n\n（选项 B）在适用“与有过失（contributory negligence）”原则的司法管辖区（包括本案所属辖区），原告自身的过失会完全阻却其在过失侵权诉讼中获得赔偿。\n\n但在严格责任诉讼中，原告过失不能作为抗辩理由（即不阻却赔偿）。**因此，轿车司机超速驾驶促成了事故发生这一事实，并不妨碍其获得赔偿。\n\n**在适用“比较过失”原则的司法管辖区，对于在严格责任诉讼中是否应根据原告过失所占的责任比例来扣减其赔偿金额，法院的观点存在分歧。\n\n（选项 D）卡车司机在变道时未打转向灯，确实违反了相关法规。\n\n这构成了“本身过失”（negligence per se），因为该法规旨在保护像轿车司机这样的人免受交通事故伤害。\n\n然而，证明存在过失与本案的严格责任诉讼无关。\n\n教育目标：如果原告遭受的损害是由导致该活动被定性为“异常危险”的特定风险所引起的，则被告可能需对该异常危险活动承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 20 条（关于异常危险活动的严格责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果原告遭受的损害是由导致某项活动被定性为“异常危险活动”的特定风险所引起的，则被告可能需对该活动承担严格责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"在适用“与有过失（contributory negligence）”原则的司法管辖区，该轿车司机胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"如果原告遭受的损害是由导致某项活动被定性为“异常危险活动”的特定风险所引起的，则被告可能需对该活动承担严格责任。","analysis":"“异常危险活动”是指一种不常见的活动，即使采取了合理的注意措施，仍会产生可预见的、极高的人身伤害（即身体伤害或财产损失）风险。\n\n如果原告遭受的损害是由导致该活动被定性为“异常危险”的特定风险所引起的，则被告可能需对该异常危险活动（例如运输危险化学品）承担严格责任。\n\n在本案中，卡车司机正在运输易燃气体（属于危险化学品），这种活动传统上被视为异常危险，因为它存在引发火灾或爆炸的极高风险。然而，轿车司机转向并失控，是因为卡车司机在未打转向灯的情况下突然变道——这种危险与运输危险化学品本身固有的异常危险无关。\n\n因此，轿车司机不太可能在严格责任诉讼中胜诉（选项 C）。\n\n*一些现代法院认定，鉴于运输危险化学品在当今社会已十分频繁，它不再被归类为异常危险活动。\n\n（选项 B）在适用“与有过失（contributory negligence）”原则的司法管辖区（包括本案所属辖区），原告自身的过失会完全阻却其在过失侵权诉讼中获得赔偿。\n\n但在严格责任诉讼中，原告过失不能作为抗辩理由（即不阻却赔偿）。**因此，轿车司机超速驾驶促成了事故发生这一事实，并不妨碍其获得赔偿。\n\n**在适用“比较过失”原则的司法管辖区，对于在严格责任诉讼中是否应根据原告过失所占的责任比例来扣减其赔偿金额，法院的观点存在分歧。\n\n（选项 D）卡车司机在变道时未打转向灯，确实违反了相关法规。\n\n这构成了“本身过失”（negligence per se），因为该法规旨在保护像轿车司机这样的人免受交通事故伤害。\n\n然而，证明存在过失与本案的严格责任诉讼无关。\n\n教育目标：如果原告遭受的损害是由导致该活动被定性为“异常危险”的特定风险所引起的，则被告可能需对该异常危险活动承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 20 条（关于异常危险活动的严格责任）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为事故并非由运输危险化学品这一行为引起。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-3150b556e9578f50"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":202,"sourceQuestionPage":606,"sourceQuestionPages":[606],"sourceExplanationPages":[607,608],"sourceAnswerPages":[607,608],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-203","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"The driver of a newly purchased automobile encountered problems with its power steering. Due to a manufacturing defect in the steering system that was present when the automobile left the manufacturer, the driver was unable to control the automobile. Fortunately, the driver was able to bring the automobile to a stop, but not before hitting and destroying a parked bicycle. Seeking to recover the cost of her bicycle, the bicyclist has sued the automobile manufacturer in a breach-of-warranty action. Is the bicyclist likely to succeed in a jurisdiction that follows the majority approach to breach-of-warranty actions?","choices":{"A":"No, because the bicyclist did not suffer any personal injuries due to the defective steering system.","B":"No, because the bicyclist was not in privity with the automobile manufacturer.","C":"Yes, because the defect in the automobile's steering system that destroyed the bicycle existed at the time the automobile left the manufacturer's control.","D":"Yes, because the property damage suffered by the bicyclist was a foreseeable result of an automobile with a faulty steering system."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Products liability actions based on breach of warranty can be asserted against a variety of defendants (eg, retailers, distributors, manufacturers). However, the plaintiff must establish privity with the defendant to have standing to sue for breach of warranty. This becomes an issue when the plaintiff is someone other than the purchaser. Under the majority approach, the purchaser's privity extends only to members of the purchaser's family or household—but they can only recover for personal injury. Here, the driver (not the bicyclist) was the purchaser of the automobile, and the bicyclist is not a member of the driver's family or household. This means that the bicyclist was not in privity with the automobile manufacturer under the majority approach and has no standing to sue. As a result, her breach-of-warranty action against the manufacturer will not succeed.* *Had this jurisdiction followed a minority approach—under which privity extends to any person reasonably expected to use, consume, or be affected by the product—the bicyclist would have had standing and could have recovered for any injury. (Choice A) Whether the bicyclist suffered any personal injuries would matter only if she were in privity with the manufacturer and had standing to sue. But even if she had standing, the bicyclist could not recover because she only sustained property damage—not personal injury. (Choice C) Because the steering system defect existed when the automobile left the manufacturer's control, the manufacturer is a proper defendant. But the bicyclist is not a proper plaintiff because she is not in privity with the manufacturer and therefore lacks standing to sue. (Choice D) A strict products liability action can be brought by anyone foreseeably injured by a defective product or whose property is harmed by the product. But the foreseeability of the property damage suffered by the bicyclist is not a reason for her to prevail in this breach-of-warranty action.\n\nEducational objective: Under the majority approach, only a purchaser or members of a purchaser's family or household have standing to sue for breach of a product warranty, but they can only recover for personal injury.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-318 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (extended privity for breach-of- warranty actions).","educationalObjective":"Under the majority approach, only a purchaser or members of a purchaser's family or household have standing to sue for breach of a product warranty, but they can only recover for personal injury.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-318 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (extended privity for breach-of- warranty actions).","rule":"Under the majority approach, only a purchaser or members of a purchaser's family or household have standing to sue for breach of a product warranty, but they can only recover for personal injury.","irac":{"issue":"Is the bicyclist likely to succeed in a jurisdiction that follows the majority approach to breach-of-warranty actions?","rule":"Under the majority approach, only a purchaser or members of a purchaser's family or household have standing to sue for breach of a product warranty, but they can only recover for personal injury.","analysis":"Products liability actions based on breach of warranty can be asserted against a variety of defendants (eg, retailers, distributors, manufacturers). However, the plaintiff must establish privity with the defendant to have standing to sue for breach of warranty. This becomes an issue when the plaintiff is someone other than the purchaser. Under the majority approach, the purchaser's privity extends only to members of the purchaser's family or household—but they can only recover for personal injury. Here, the driver (not the bicyclist) was the purchaser of the automobile, and the bicyclist is not a member of the driver's family or household. This means that the bicyclist was not in privity with the automobile manufacturer under the majority approach and has no standing to sue. As a result, her breach-of-warranty action against the manufacturer will not succeed.* *Had this jurisdiction followed a minority approach—under which privity extends to any person reasonably expected to use, consume, or be affected by the product—the bicyclist would have had standing and could have recovered for any injury. (Choice A) Whether the bicyclist suffered any personal injuries would matter only if she were in privity with the manufacturer and had standing to sue. But even if she had standing, the bicyclist could not recover because she only sustained property damage—not personal injury. (Choice C) Because the steering system defect existed when the automobile left the manufacturer's control, the manufacturer is a proper defendant. But the bicyclist is not a proper plaintiff because she is not in privity with the manufacturer and therefore lacks standing to sue. (Choice D) A strict products liability action can be brought by anyone foreseeably injured by a defective product or whose property is harmed by the product. But the foreseeability of the property damage suffered by the bicyclist is not a reason for her to prevail in this breach-of-warranty action.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the bicyclist was not in privity with the automobile manufacturer."},"questionZh":"一位新购汽车的驾驶员遇到了动力转向系统故障。\n\n由于汽车出厂时转向系统存在制造缺陷，驾驶员无法控制车辆。\n\n幸运的是，驾驶员最终将车停了下来，但在此之前撞毁了一辆停放的自行车。\n\n为了赔偿自行车的损失，骑车人以违反保修条款为由起诉了汽车制造商。\n\n在遵循大多数司法管辖区对违反保修条款诉讼的处理方式的情况下，骑车人胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为骑车人并未因转向系统缺陷而遭受人身伤害。","B":"否，因为骑车人与汽车制造商之间不存在合同关系。","C":"是，因为导致自行车损毁的汽车转向系统缺陷在汽车出厂时就已存在。","D":"是的，因为骑车人遭受的财产损失是汽车转向系统故障造成的可预见后果。"},"explanationZh":"基于违反保证（breach of warranty）的产品责任诉讼可以针对多种被告（如零售商、分销商、制造商）提起。\n\n然而，原告必须与被告之间存在“合同关系”（privity），才具备因违反保证而提起诉讼的资格（诉讼主体资格）。\n\n当原告并非购买者本人时，这就成了一个问题。\n\n根据多数派观点，购买者的合同关系仅延伸至其家庭成员或同住人员——且这些人仅能就人身伤害获得赔偿。\n\n在本案中，汽车的购买者是司机（而非骑自行车的人），而骑自行车的人并非司机的家庭成员或同住人员。\n\n这意味着根据多数派观点，骑自行车的人与汽车制造商之间不存在合同关系，因此不具备诉讼资格。\n\n结果，她针对制造商提起的违反保证之诉将无法胜诉。* *如果该司法管辖区采用少数派观点——即合同关系延伸至任何被合理预期会使用、消费该产品或受其影响的人——那么骑自行车的人将具备诉讼资格，并可就任何伤害获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）骑自行车的人是否遭受人身伤害，仅在她与制造商存在合同关系并具备诉讼资格的情况下才有意义。\n\n但即使她具备诉讼资格，也无法获得赔偿，因为她仅遭受了财产损失，而未遭受人身伤害。\n\n（选项 C）由于转向系统的缺陷在汽车脱离制造商控制时就已经存在，因此制造商是适格被告。\n\n但骑自行车的人并非适格原告，因为她与制造商之间不存在合同关系，从而缺乏诉讼资格。\n\n（选项 D）任何因缺陷产品而遭受可预见伤害或财产受损的人，均可提起严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n但骑自行车的人所遭受的财产损失是否具有可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability），并非她在本案违反保证之诉中胜诉的理由。\n\n教育目标：根据多数派观点，仅购买者或其家庭成员/同住人员具备因违反产品保证而提起诉讼的资格，且他们仅能就人身伤害获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《统一商法典》（U.C.C.）\n\n§ 2-318 (美国法律学会与统一州法委员会 2020年)（针对违反保证之诉扩大了合同相对性范围）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据多数派观点，只有购买者或其家庭成员才有资格就产品保修违约提起诉讼，但他们只能就人身伤害获得赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"在遵循多数派观点处理保修违约诉讼的司法管辖区，骑车人胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"根据多数派观点，只有购买者或其家庭成员才有资格就产品保修违约提起诉讼，但他们只能就人身伤害获得赔偿。","analysis":"基于违反保证（breach of warranty）的产品责任诉讼可以针对多种被告（如零售商、分销商、制造商）提起。\n\n然而，原告必须与被告之间存在“合同关系”（privity），才具备因违反保证而提起诉讼的资格（诉讼主体资格）。\n\n当原告并非购买者本人时，这就成了一个问题。\n\n根据多数派观点，购买者的合同关系仅延伸至其家庭成员或同住人员——且这些人仅能就人身伤害获得赔偿。\n\n在本案中，汽车的购买者是司机（而非骑自行车的人），而骑自行车的人并非司机的家庭成员或同住人员。\n\n这意味着根据多数派观点，骑自行车的人与汽车制造商之间不存在合同关系，因此不具备诉讼资格。\n\n结果，她针对制造商提起的违反保证之诉将无法胜诉。* *如果该司法管辖区采用少数派观点——即合同关系延伸至任何被合理预期会使用、消费该产品或受其影响的人——那么骑自行车的人将具备诉讼资格，并可就任何伤害获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）骑自行车的人是否遭受人身伤害，仅在她与制造商存在合同关系并具备诉讼资格的情况下才有意义。\n\n但即使她具备诉讼资格，也无法获得赔偿，因为她仅遭受了财产损失，而未遭受人身伤害。\n\n（选项 C）由于转向系统的缺陷在汽车脱离制造商控制时就已经存在，因此制造商是适格被告。\n\n但骑自行车的人并非适格原告，因为她与制造商之间不存在合同关系，从而缺乏诉讼资格。\n\n（选项 D）任何因缺陷产品而遭受可预见伤害或财产受损的人，均可提起严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n但骑自行车的人所遭受的财产损失是否具有可预见性（foreseeability）（foreseeability），并非她在本案违反保证之诉中胜诉的理由。\n\n教育目标：根据多数派观点，仅购买者或其家庭成员/同住人员具备因违反产品保证而提起诉讼的资格，且他们仅能就人身伤害获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献 《统一商法典》（U.C.C.）\n\n§ 2-318 (美国法律学会与统一州法委员会 2020年)（针对违反保证之诉扩大了合同相对性范围）。","conclusion":"答案 B：否，因为骑车人与汽车制造商之间不存在合同关系。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-727b149f15178326"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":203,"sourceQuestionPage":609,"sourceQuestionPages":[609],"sourceExplanationPages":[610,611],"sourceAnswerPages":[610,611],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-204","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A retired man who had been a sales representative for a drill manufacturer listed a few of his tools manufactured by his former employer on an online garage-sale website. A woman purchased one of the man's listed power drills to use in her home-remodeling business. The power drill had never been used and was still sealed in its original box. The drill manufacturer had used all available techniques to inspect the drill for defects. The drill manufacturer intended the power drill to be used on wood only and therefore included on the drill's box a warning against using the drill on metal or masonry. One week later, the woman was using the power drill to put holes in a brick wall when the drill bit flew off the drill due to a defective component and caused her to sustain a severe eye injury. Traditional common-law rules regarding contributory negligence apply in this jurisdiction. If the woman asserts a strict products liability claim against the man, is she likely to recover?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man was not a commercial supplier.","B":"No, because the woman used the drill in an unintended manner.","C":"Yes, because the drill had a defective component part.","D":"Yes, because the man was a retailer of the power drill."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Strict products liability applies to commercial suppliers—ie, persons in the business of manufacturing, distributing, or selling the type of defective product that caused the plaintiff harm. However, it does not apply to casual sellers—ie, persons conducting occasional sales outside their regular course of business. Here, the man is a casual seller (not a commercial supplier) because he sold the power drill at an online garage sale—not in the regular course of business. Therefore, although the man sold the defective drill to the woman, he is not subject to strict products liability, and the woman is unlikely to recover. (Choice B) Misuse is a defense to strict products liability when a product was used in an unintended, unforeseeable manner. Here, the woman used the drill in an unintended manner, despite the manufacturer's warning against using the drill on masonry (ie, brick). But that warning shows that her misuse was foreseeable. (Choice C) It is irrelevant in this case that the drill had a defective component part. The man is only a casual seller, so he cannot be held strictly liable for the defective drill. (Choice D) Any commercial supplier along the chain of distribution—including retailers— may be subject to strict products liability if the product contained a defect when it left the supplier's control. While the man likely would have been considered a retailer in his former job, he is only a casual seller in this case.\n\nEducational objective: Strict products liability is only imposed on commercial suppliers (persons who manufacture, distribute, or sell products as part of their business)—not casual sellers (persons conducting occasional sales outside their regular course of business).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1 cmt. c, 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (defining commercial supplier).","educationalObjective":"Strict products liability is only imposed on commercial suppliers (persons who manufacture, distribute, or sell products as part of their business)—not casual sellers (persons conducting occasional sales outside their regular course of business).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1 cmt. c, 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (defining commercial supplier).","rule":"Strict products liability is only imposed on commercial suppliers (persons who manufacture, distribute, or sell products as part of their business)—not casual sellers (persons conducting occasional sales outside their regular course of business).","irac":{"issue":"If the woman asserts a strict products liability claim against the man, is she likely to recover?","rule":"Strict products liability is only imposed on commercial suppliers (persons who manufacture, distribute, or sell products as part of their business)—not casual sellers (persons conducting occasional sales outside their regular course of business).","analysis":"Strict products liability applies to commercial suppliers—ie, persons in the business of manufacturing, distributing, or selling the type of defective product that caused the plaintiff harm. However, it does not apply to casual sellers—ie, persons conducting occasional sales outside their regular course of business. Here, the man is a casual seller (not a commercial supplier) because he sold the power drill at an online garage sale—not in the regular course of business. Therefore, although the man sold the defective drill to the woman, he is not subject to strict products liability, and the woman is unlikely to recover. (Choice B) Misuse is a defense to strict products liability when a product was used in an unintended, unforeseeable manner. Here, the woman used the drill in an unintended manner, despite the manufacturer's warning against using the drill on masonry (ie, brick). But that warning shows that her misuse was foreseeable. (Choice C) It is irrelevant in this case that the drill had a defective component part. The man is only a casual seller, so he cannot be held strictly liable for the defective drill. (Choice D) Any commercial supplier along the chain of distribution—including retailers— may be subject to strict products liability if the product contained a defect when it left the supplier's control. While the man likely would have been considered a retailer in his former job, he is only a casual seller in this case.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the man was not a commercial supplier."},"questionZh":"一位曾任钻孔机制造商销售代表的退休男子，在一家在线二手物品交易网站上挂牌出售了几件由其前雇主生产的工具。\n\n一位女士买下了其中一把电动钻孔机，打算用于她的房屋改造业务。\n\n这把电动钻孔机从未被使用过，且仍处于原包装密封状态。\n\n制造商此前已采用所有可用技术对该钻孔机进行了缺陷检测。\n\n制造商将该钻孔机设计为仅用于木材加工，因此在包装盒上印有警告，禁止将其用于金属或砖石材料。\n\n一周后，该女士在用这把钻孔机于砖墙上钻孔时，因部件缺陷导致钻头脱落飞出，致使其眼睛严重受伤。\n\n该司法管辖区适用关于“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）的传统普通法规则。\n\n如果该女士针对该男子提出严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔，她胜诉（获得赔偿）的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为该男子并非商业供应商。","B":"不大，因为该女士以非预定的方式使用了该钻孔机。","C":"大，因为该钻孔机存在缺陷部件。","D":"大，因为该男子是该电动钻孔机的零售商。"},"explanationZh":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）适用于商业供应商——即从事制造、分销或销售导致原告受损的那类缺陷产品业务的人员。\n\n然而，它不适用于偶然卖家——即在常规业务活动之外进行偶尔销售的人员。\n\n在本案中，该男子属于偶然卖家（而非商业供应商），因为他在网上“车库甩卖”（garage sale）中出售了该电钻，而非在常规业务活动中进行销售。\n\n因此，尽管该男子将有缺陷的电钻卖给了该女子，但他不承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），该女子很可能无法获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）当产品以非预期或不可预见的方式使用时，“不当使用”（misuse）可作为严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的抗辩理由。\n\n在本案中，尽管制造商已发出警告不得在砖石（如砖块）上使用该电钻，但该女子仍以非预期的方式使用了它。\n\n然而，该警告恰恰表明她的不当使用是可预见的。\n\n（选项 C）电钻含有缺陷部件这一事实在本案中无关紧要。\n\n该男子仅为偶然卖家，因此不能因该电钻存在缺陷而被追究严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n（选项 D）在分销链上的任何商业供应商（包括零售商）都可能承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），前提是产品在离开其控制时已存在缺陷。\n\n虽然该男子在之前的工作中可能被视为零售商，但在本案中，他仅属于偶然卖家。\n\n教育目标：严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）仅适用于商业供应商（即作为其业务一部分制造、分销或销售产品的人员），而不适用于偶然卖家（即在常规业务活动之外进行偶尔销售的人员）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条注释 c 及第 20 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于商业供应商的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）仅适用于商业供应商（即在业务过程中制造、分销或销售产品的人员），而不适用于偶然卖家（即在常规业务活动之外偶尔进行销售的人员）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该女士针对该男子提出严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔，她胜诉（获得赔偿）的可能性大吗？","rule":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）仅适用于商业供应商（即在业务过程中制造、分销或销售产品的人员），而不适用于偶然卖家（即在常规业务活动之外偶尔进行销售的人员）。","analysis":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）适用于商业供应商——即从事制造、分销或销售导致原告受损的那类缺陷产品业务的人员。\n\n然而，它不适用于偶然卖家——即在常规业务活动之外进行偶尔销售的人员。\n\n在本案中，该男子属于偶然卖家（而非商业供应商），因为他在网上“车库甩卖”（garage sale）中出售了该电钻，而非在常规业务活动中进行销售。\n\n因此，尽管该男子将有缺陷的电钻卖给了该女子，但他不承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），该女子很可能无法获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 B）当产品以非预期或不可预见的方式使用时，“不当使用”（misuse）可作为严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的抗辩理由。\n\n在本案中，尽管制造商已发出警告不得在砖石（如砖块）上使用该电钻，但该女子仍以非预期的方式使用了它。\n\n然而，该警告恰恰表明她的不当使用是可预见的。\n\n（选项 C）电钻含有缺陷部件这一事实在本案中无关紧要。\n\n该男子仅为偶然卖家，因此不能因该电钻存在缺陷而被追究严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n（选项 D）在分销链上的任何商业供应商（包括零售商）都可能承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），前提是产品在离开其控制时已存在缺陷。\n\n虽然该男子在之前的工作中可能被视为零售商，但在本案中，他仅属于偶然卖家。\n\n教育目标：严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）仅适用于商业供应商（即作为其业务一部分制造、分销或销售产品的人员），而不适用于偶然卖家（即在常规业务活动之外进行偶尔销售的人员）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条注释 c 及第 20 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于商业供应商的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不大，因为该男子并非商业供应商。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-62019772e0bdbfce"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":204,"sourceQuestionPage":612,"sourceQuestionPages":[612],"sourceExplanationPages":[613,614],"sourceAnswerPages":[613,614],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-205","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A bartender was removing the restraining wire from a bottle of champagne produced and bottled by a wine company when the plastic stopper suddenly shot out of the bottle. The stopper struck and injured the bartender's eye. The bartender had opened other bottles of champagne, and occasionally the stoppers had shot out with great force, but he had not been injured. The bartender brought an action against the wine company, and the jury found that the bottle that caused his injury was defective and unreasonably dangerous because its label did not warn that the stopper might suddenly shoot out during opening. The state has merged contributory negligence and unreasonable assumption of risk into a pure comparative fault system that is applied in strict products liability actions. Will the bartender recover a judgment in his favor?","choices":{"A":"No, if a reasonable bartender would have realized that a stopper could eject from the bottle and hit his eye.","B":"No, if the jury finds that a legally sufficient warning would not have prevented the bartender's injury.","C":"Yes, with damages reduced by the percentage of any contributory fault on the bartender's part.","D":"Yes, with no reduction in damages, because foreseeable lack of caution is the reason for requiring a warning."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Under products liability law, commercial suppliers are required to provide adequate warnings (or instructions) when: their product poses a foreseeable and unobvious risk of physical harm and reasonable warnings could reduce that risk. Failure to provide adequate warnings renders a product defective and subjects a commercial supplier to strict liability when that defect causes the plaintiff harm. To prove that the defect caused his/her harm, the plaintiff must show that the harm would not have occurred had a legally sufficient warning been provided. Here, the bartender was injured by a champagne bottle stopper. The jury found that the bottle was defective due to its lack of warning about the stopper. But if the jury also finds that a legally sufficient warning would not have prevented the bartender's injury—eg, that even with a warning the bartender would have opened the bottle the same way he had in the past—then the bottle's absent warning did not cause his injury and he will not prevail. (Choice A) If a reasonable bartender would have realized the stopper's risk of harm, then the bartender may have contributed to his harm by failing to protect himself against that risk (contributory negligence). However, the bartender's contributory negligence would merely reduce—not bar—his recovery in this pure comparative fault jurisdiction. (Choice C) If the bartender could obtain a judgment in his favor, his recovery would be reduced by his percentage of contributory fault in this pure comparative fault system—eg, bartender 60% at fault would still recover 40% of his damages. But if the jury finds that a sufficient warning would not have prevented his injury (eg, because he would have ignored the warning), then none of his harm was caused by the absent warning and he will recover nothing. (Choice D) The fact that a user's lack of caution may have been foreseeable would support the jury's finding that the bottle was defective. But to recover, the bartender must also prove that a legally sufficient warning would have prevented his injury.\n\nEducational objective: A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a defective product that lacks adequate warnings if legally sufficient warnings would have prevented the plaintiff's harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(c) cmt. i (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (strict products liability for inadequate warnings or instructions).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 26 cmt. g (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (harm caused by inadequate warnings).","educationalObjective":"A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a defective product that lacks adequate warnings if legally sufficient warnings would have prevented the plaintiff's harm.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(c) cmt. i (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (strict products liability for inadequate warnings or instructions).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 26 cmt. g (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (harm caused by inadequate warnings).","rule":"A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a defective product that lacks adequate warnings if legally sufficient warnings would have prevented the plaintiff's harm.","irac":{"issue":"Will the bartender recover a judgment in his favor?","rule":"A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a defective product that lacks adequate warnings if legally sufficient warnings would have prevented the plaintiff's harm.","analysis":"Under products liability law, commercial suppliers are required to provide adequate warnings (or instructions) when: their product poses a foreseeable and unobvious risk of physical harm and reasonable warnings could reduce that risk. Failure to provide adequate warnings renders a product defective and subjects a commercial supplier to strict liability when that defect causes the plaintiff harm. To prove that the defect caused his/her harm, the plaintiff must show that the harm would not have occurred had a legally sufficient warning been provided. Here, the bartender was injured by a champagne bottle stopper. The jury found that the bottle was defective due to its lack of warning about the stopper. But if the jury also finds that a legally sufficient warning would not have prevented the bartender's injury—eg, that even with a warning the bartender would have opened the bottle the same way he had in the past—then the bottle's absent warning did not cause his injury and he will not prevail. (Choice A) If a reasonable bartender would have realized the stopper's risk of harm, then the bartender may have contributed to his harm by failing to protect himself against that risk (contributory negligence). However, the bartender's contributory negligence would merely reduce—not bar—his recovery in this pure comparative fault jurisdiction. (Choice C) If the bartender could obtain a judgment in his favor, his recovery would be reduced by his percentage of contributory fault in this pure comparative fault system—eg, bartender 60% at fault would still recover 40% of his damages. But if the jury finds that a sufficient warning would not have prevented his injury (eg, because he would have ignored the warning), then none of his harm was caused by the absent warning and he will recover nothing. (Choice D) The fact that a user's lack of caution may have been foreseeable would support the jury's finding that the bottle was defective. But to recover, the bartender must also prove that a legally sufficient warning would have prevented his injury.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, if the jury finds that a legally sufficient warning would not have prevented the bartender's injury."},"questionZh":"一名酒保在处理某葡萄酒公司生产并装瓶的香槟时，正试图拆除瓶口的固定铁丝，此时塑料瓶塞突然从瓶中弹出，击伤了酒保的眼睛。\n\n该酒保此前曾开启过其他香槟，虽然偶尔也会遇到瓶塞强力弹出的情况，但他从未因此受伤。\n\n酒保针对该葡萄酒公司提起诉讼，陪审团认定致伤的香槟瓶存在缺陷且具有不合理的危险，因为其标签未就开瓶时瓶塞可能突然弹出发出警告。\n\n该州已将“与有过失（contributory negligence）”与“不合理承担风险”合并为一种纯粹的“比较过错”制度，并将其适用于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n酒保能否获得胜诉判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，如果一个理性的酒保本应意识到瓶塞可能会从瓶中弹出并击中眼睛。","B":"不能，如果陪审团认定，即使有充分的法律警告，也无法避免酒保受伤。","C":"能，但赔偿金额需扣除酒保自身共同过错所占的比例。","D":"能，且不扣除赔偿金额，因为“可预见的疏忽”正是要求提供警告的原因。"},"explanationZh":"根据产品责任法，当产品存在可预见且不明显的身体伤害风险，且合理的警告能够降低该风险时，商业供应商有义务提供充分的警告（或说明）。\n\n若未能提供充分警告，产品即被视为存在缺陷；当该缺陷导致原告受损时，商业供应商须承担严格责任。\n\n为证明该缺陷导致了损害，原告必须证明：若提供了法律上充分的警告，该损害本不会发生。\n\n本案中，调酒师被香槟瓶塞击伤。\n\n陪审团认定，由于缺乏关于瓶塞风险的警告，该瓶子存在缺陷。\n\n但如果陪审团同时认定，即使提供了法律上充分的警告也无法避免调酒师受伤（例如，即便有警告，调酒师仍会像过去那样开启瓶子），那么缺乏警告便未导致其受伤，他也将无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）如果一个理性的调酒师本应意识到瓶塞带来的伤害风险，那么调酒师因未针对该风险采取自我保护措施，可能对损害的发生负有一定责任（即“与有过失（contributory negligence）”）。\n\n然而，在这个适用“纯粹比较过失”原则的司法管辖区，调酒师的与有过失（contributory negligence）只会减少、而不会完全排除其获得赔偿的权利。\n\n（选项 C）如果调酒师胜诉，其获赔金额将根据其与有过失（contributory negligence）的比例进行扣减（例如，若调酒师负有 60% 的过失责任，仍可获得 40% 的损害赔偿）。\n\n但如果陪审团认定充分的警告无法避免其受伤（例如，因为他会无视警告），那么其所受损害便并非由缺乏警告所致，他也将无法获得任何赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）用户可能缺乏谨慎这一事实，可支持陪审团关于瓶子存在缺陷的认定。\n\n但若要获得赔偿，调酒师还必须证明，法律上充分的警告本可避免其受伤。\n\n教育目标：若法律上充分的警告本可避免原告受损，则商业供应商须对因缺乏充分警告的缺陷产品所造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 2(c) 条及相关评注。\n\ni (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（因警告或说明不充分而承担的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第26条评注g (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（因警告不充分造成的损害）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果充分的法律警告本可避免原告受到的伤害，那么对于因缺乏充分警告的缺陷产品所造成的伤害，商业供应商须承担严格责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"酒保能否获得胜诉判决？","rule":"如果充分的法律警告本可避免原告受到的伤害，那么对于因缺乏充分警告的缺陷产品所造成的伤害，商业供应商须承担严格责任。","analysis":"根据产品责任法，当产品存在可预见且不明显的身体伤害风险，且合理的警告能够降低该风险时，商业供应商有义务提供充分的警告（或说明）。\n\n若未能提供充分警告，产品即被视为存在缺陷；当该缺陷导致原告受损时，商业供应商须承担严格责任。\n\n为证明该缺陷导致了损害，原告必须证明：若提供了法律上充分的警告，该损害本不会发生。\n\n本案中，调酒师被香槟瓶塞击伤。\n\n陪审团认定，由于缺乏关于瓶塞风险的警告，该瓶子存在缺陷。\n\n但如果陪审团同时认定，即使提供了法律上充分的警告也无法避免调酒师受伤（例如，即便有警告，调酒师仍会像过去那样开启瓶子），那么缺乏警告便未导致其受伤，他也将无法胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）如果一个理性的调酒师本应意识到瓶塞带来的伤害风险，那么调酒师因未针对该风险采取自我保护措施，可能对损害的发生负有一定责任（即“与有过失（contributory negligence）”）。\n\n然而，在这个适用“纯粹比较过失”原则的司法管辖区，调酒师的与有过失（contributory negligence）只会减少、而不会完全排除其获得赔偿的权利。\n\n（选项 C）如果调酒师胜诉，其获赔金额将根据其与有过失（contributory negligence）的比例进行扣减（例如，若调酒师负有 60% 的过失责任，仍可获得 40% 的损害赔偿）。\n\n但如果陪审团认定充分的警告无法避免其受伤（例如，因为他会无视警告），那么其所受损害便并非由缺乏警告所致，他也将无法获得任何赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）用户可能缺乏谨慎这一事实，可支持陪审团关于瓶子存在缺陷的认定。\n\n但若要获得赔偿，调酒师还必须证明，法律上充分的警告本可避免其受伤。\n\n教育目标：若法律上充分的警告本可避免原告受损，则商业供应商须对因缺乏充分警告的缺陷产品所造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 2(c) 条及相关评注。\n\ni (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（因警告或说明不充分而承担的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第26条评注g (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（因警告不充分造成的损害）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不能，如果陪审团认定，即使有充分的法律警告，也无法避免酒保受伤。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-aa139f9682534b62"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":205,"sourceQuestionPage":615,"sourceQuestionPages":[615],"sourceExplanationPages":[616,617],"sourceAnswerPages":[616,617],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-206","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A 16-year-old student purchased an educational chemistry set manufactured by a chemical company. The student invited his classmate to assist him in a chemistry project. Referring to a website on explosives and finding that the chemistry set contained all of the necessary chemicals, the student and classmate agreed to make a bomb. During the course of the project, the student carelessly knocked a lighted Bunsen burner into a bowl of chemicals from the chemistry set. The chemicals burst into flames, injuring the classmate. In a suit by the classmate against the chemical company based on strict liability, will the classmate likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the student's negligence was the cause in fact of the classmate's injury.","B":"No, if the chemistry set was as safe as possible, consistent with its educational purposes, and its benefits exceeded its risks.","C":"Yes, because manufacturers of chemistry sets are engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity.","D":"Yes, if the chemistry set did not contain a warning that its contents could be combined to form dangerous explosives."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A commercial supplier is subject to strict products liability for harm caused by its defective product. A product is defective by design if: it poses a foreseeable risk of harm and\n\nthat risk could have been reduced by a feasible alternative design (ie, the risk-utility test— comparing the risks and benefits of the original design to those of an alternative design).\n\nHere, it may have been foreseeable that the chemistry set would be used in a harmful manner (to make a bomb). But if the chemistry set was as safe as possible, consistent with its educational purposes, and its benefits exceeded its risks, then the benefits of its original design would surpass those of an alternative design. In that case, the chemistry set would not be defective and the chemical company would not be strictly liable. (Choice A) A \"cause in fact\" is an event that plays such a substantial role in causing the plaintiff's injury that the injury would not have occurred but for that event. But here, the classmate cannot prevail—regardless of whether the student's negligence was a cause in fact of the classmate's injury—because the chemistry set was not defective. (Choice C) Strict liability may be imposed for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity. Although it may be abnormally dangerous to manufacture chemistry sets, the classmate was harmed by his attempt to make a bomb with a chemistry set—not by the chemical company's earlier manufacturing of that set. Therefore, the company is not liable under this theory. (Choice D) Commercial suppliers may be strictly liable for not providing adequate warnings about a product's risk of harm—but only if those warnings would have prevented the plaintiff's harm. Here, the student and the classmate already knew (from the website) that the chemicals could be combined to make explosives and knowingly ignored that risk. Therefore, a warning would have been ineffective and a failure to warn claim would likely fail.\n\nEducational objective: A product is defectively designed if (1) it poses a foreseeable risk of harm and (2) that risk could have been reduced by a feasible alternative design, as determined by the risk-utility test (comparing the risks and benefits of the original and alternative designs).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (strict liability for defectively designed products).","educationalObjective":"A product is defectively designed if (1) it poses a foreseeable risk of harm and (2) that risk could have been reduced by a feasible alternative design, as determined by the risk-utility test (comparing the risks and benefits of the original and alternative designs).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (strict liability for defectively designed products).","rule":"A product is defectively designed if (1) it poses a foreseeable risk of harm and (2) that risk could have been reduced by a feasible alternative design, as determined by the risk-utility test (comparing the risks and benefits of the original and alternative designs).","irac":{"issue":"In a suit by the classmate against the chemical company based on strict liability, will the classmate likely prevail?","rule":"A product is defectively designed if (1) it poses a foreseeable risk of harm and (2) that risk could have been reduced by a feasible alternative design, as determined by the risk-utility test (comparing the risks and benefits of the original and alternative designs).","analysis":"A commercial supplier is subject to strict products liability for harm caused by its defective product. A product is defective by design if: it poses a foreseeable risk of harm and\n\nthat risk could have been reduced by a feasible alternative design (ie, the risk-utility test— comparing the risks and benefits of the original design to those of an alternative design).\n\nHere, it may have been foreseeable that the chemistry set would be used in a harmful manner (to make a bomb). But if the chemistry set was as safe as possible, consistent with its educational purposes, and its benefits exceeded its risks, then the benefits of its original design would surpass those of an alternative design. In that case, the chemistry set would not be defective and the chemical company would not be strictly liable. (Choice A) A \"cause in fact\" is an event that plays such a substantial role in causing the plaintiff's injury that the injury would not have occurred but for that event. But here, the classmate cannot prevail—regardless of whether the student's negligence was a cause in fact of the classmate's injury—because the chemistry set was not defective. (Choice C) Strict liability may be imposed for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity. Although it may be abnormally dangerous to manufacture chemistry sets, the classmate was harmed by his attempt to make a bomb with a chemistry set—not by the chemical company's earlier manufacturing of that set. Therefore, the company is not liable under this theory. (Choice D) Commercial suppliers may be strictly liable for not providing adequate warnings about a product's risk of harm—but only if those warnings would have prevented the plaintiff's harm. Here, the student and the classmate already knew (from the website) that the chemicals could be combined to make explosives and knowingly ignored that risk. Therefore, a warning would have been ineffective and a failure to warn claim would likely fail.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, if the chemistry set was as safe as possible, consistent with its educational purposes, and its benefits exceeded its risks."},"questionZh":"一名16岁的学生购买了一套化学实验套装，该套装由一家化学公司生产。\n\n这名学生邀请他的同学协助他完成一个化学实验项目。\n\n他参考了一个关于爆炸物的网站，发现这套化学实验套装包含了所有必要的化学试剂，于是他和同学决定制作一枚炸弹。\n\n在实验过程中，这名学生不小心将点燃的本生灯碰进了装有化学试剂的碗里。\n\n化学试剂爆炸起火，导致他的同学受伤。\n\n如果这位同学以严格责任为由起诉这家化学公司，他胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为学生的疏忽是造成同学受伤的直接原因。","B":"不可能，如果这套化学实验套装已经尽可能安全，符合其教育目的，并且其益处大于风险。","C":"可能，因为化学实验套装的制造商从事的是一项异常危险的活动。","D":"是的，如果化学实验套装没有警告说明其内容物混合后可能形成危险爆炸物。"},"explanationZh":"商业供应商须对其缺陷产品造成的损害承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n如果产品存在以下情况，则被视为设计有缺陷：它构成了可预见的损害风险，且 该风险本可通过一种可行的替代设计加以降低（即“风险-效用测试”——比较原始设计与替代设计的风险和收益）。\n\n在本案中，该化学实验套装可能被用于有害目的（制造炸弹）这一情况或许是可预见的。\n\n但如果该化学实验套装在符合其教育目的的前提下已尽可能安全，且其收益超过了风险，那么其原始设计的收益将优于替代设计。\n\n在这种情况下，该化学实验套装不属于有缺陷产品，化学公司也无需承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）“事实因果关系”（cause in fact）是指在导致原告受伤的过程中起实质性作用的事件，若无该事件，伤害便不会发生。\n\n但在此案中，即便该学生的疏忽是导致同学受伤的事实原因，同学也无法胜诉，因为该化学实验套装本身并无缺陷。\n\n（选项 C）从事异常危险活动造成的损害可能引发严格责任。\n\n尽管制造化学实验套装本身可能属于异常危险活动，但该同学受到的伤害源于其试图利用该套装制造炸弹的行为，而非源于化学公司早先制造该套装的行为。\n\n因此，根据该理论，公司不承担责任。\n\n（选项 D）商业供应商若未就产品的损害风险提供充分警示，可能须承担严格责任——但这仅限于警示本能防止原告受到伤害的情形。\n\n在本案中，该学生和同学已（通过网站）获知这些化学品混合后可制造爆炸物，并明知故犯地忽视了这一风险。\n\n因此，警示将无法发挥作用，关于“未尽警示义务”的索赔主张很可能无法成立。\n\n教育目标：若产品满足以下条件，则被视为设计有缺陷：(1) 构成可预见的损害风险；(2) 该风险本可通过可行的替代设计加以降低（依据风险-效用测试判定，即比较原始设计与替代设计的风险和收益）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》\n\n第1、2(b)条（美国法律协会，1998年）（针对设计缺陷产品的严格责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果产品 (1) 存在可预见的危害风险，并且 (2) 通过风险效用检验（比较原设计和替代设计的风险和收益）确定，该风险可以通过可行的替代设计来降低，则该产品的设计存在缺陷。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果同学基于严格责任起诉这家化学公司，同学胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"如果产品 (1) 存在可预见的危害风险，并且 (2) 通过风险效用检验（比较原设计和替代设计的风险和收益）确定，该风险可以通过可行的替代设计来降低，则该产品的设计存在缺陷。","analysis":"商业供应商须对其缺陷产品造成的损害承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n如果产品存在以下情况，则被视为设计有缺陷：它构成了可预见的损害风险，且 该风险本可通过一种可行的替代设计加以降低（即“风险-效用测试”——比较原始设计与替代设计的风险和收益）。\n\n在本案中，该化学实验套装可能被用于有害目的（制造炸弹）这一情况或许是可预见的。\n\n但如果该化学实验套装在符合其教育目的的前提下已尽可能安全，且其收益超过了风险，那么其原始设计的收益将优于替代设计。\n\n在这种情况下，该化学实验套装不属于有缺陷产品，化学公司也无需承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）“事实因果关系”（cause in fact）是指在导致原告受伤的过程中起实质性作用的事件，若无该事件，伤害便不会发生。\n\n但在此案中，即便该学生的疏忽是导致同学受伤的事实原因，同学也无法胜诉，因为该化学实验套装本身并无缺陷。\n\n（选项 C）从事异常危险活动造成的损害可能引发严格责任。\n\n尽管制造化学实验套装本身可能属于异常危险活动，但该同学受到的伤害源于其试图利用该套装制造炸弹的行为，而非源于化学公司早先制造该套装的行为。\n\n因此，根据该理论，公司不承担责任。\n\n（选项 D）商业供应商若未就产品的损害风险提供充分警示，可能须承担严格责任——但这仅限于警示本能防止原告受到伤害的情形。\n\n在本案中，该学生和同学已（通过网站）获知这些化学品混合后可制造爆炸物，并明知故犯地忽视了这一风险。\n\n因此，警示将无法发挥作用，关于“未尽警示义务”的索赔主张很可能无法成立。\n\n教育目标：若产品满足以下条件，则被视为设计有缺陷：(1) 构成可预见的损害风险；(2) 该风险本可通过可行的替代设计加以降低（依据风险-效用测试判定，即比较原始设计与替代设计的风险和收益）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》\n\n第1、2(b)条（美国法律协会，1998年）（针对设计缺陷产品的严格责任）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可能，如果这套化学实验套装已经尽可能安全，符合其教育目的，并且其益处大于风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-f4135effd6e629da"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":206,"sourceQuestionPage":618,"sourceQuestionPages":[618],"sourceExplanationPages":[619,620],"sourceAnswerPages":[619,620],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-207","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"One weekend, a retired man conducted a garage sale at his home. A woman came to the garage sale in search of a power drill to use in her home-remodeling business. The man happened to have a power drill for sale that fit the woman's needs. The power drill had never been used and was still sealed in its original box. The power drill was manufactured by a drill company, which uses all available techniques to inspect its products for defects. The drill company only intended the power drill to be used on plywood. One week later, the woman suffered a severe eye injury while using the power drill to put holes in a thick metal sheet when the drill bit flew off the drill due to a defective component and caused her eye injury. Traditional common law rules regarding contributory negligence apply in this jurisdiction. If the woman asserts a claim based on strict products liability against the drill company, which of the following facts will most likely negate the drill company's liability?","choices":{"A":"The drill company used every available means to inspect the drill for defects.","B":"The drill was being put to an improper use.","C":"The woman did not directly purchase the drill from the drill company.","D":"The woman was contributorily negligent in using the drill on a thick sheet of metal."},"answer":"B","explanation":"When a commercial supplier's defective product causes harm to the plaintiff, the supplier is subject to strict products liability absent some defense. One defense that negates a commercial supplier's strict liability is the plaintiff's improper use of a product—ie, using the product in an unintended and unforeseeable manner. Here, the woman used the drill in an unintended manner because the drill was intended to be used only on plywood—not metal. So if it is established that the woman's use of the drill was also unforeseeable, then her improper use of the drill would negate the company's strict liability. (Choice A) A commercial supplier's use of every available means to inspect a product would be relevant to a negligence action, which considers the defendant's use of reasonable care. But the drill company's reasonable care is irrelevant to the woman's strict liability action, which imposes liability without proof of culpability. (Choice C) A commercial supplier is strictly liable when its defective product causes harm to any foreseeable plaintiff—not just direct purchasers. Therefore, the fact that the woman did not purchase the drill directly from the drill company is irrelevant. (Choice D) Contributory negligence refers to the plaintiff's failure to use the standard of care that a person should use for his/her own protection. This is no defense to strict products liability in this jurisdiction, which follows the traditional common law rules of contributory negligence.\n\nEducational objective: A plaintiff's improper use of a defective product—ie, using the product in an unintended and unforeseeable fashion—negates a commercial supplier's strict liability.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (misuse of a product).","educationalObjective":"A plaintiff's improper use of a defective product—ie, using the product in an unintended and unforeseeable fashion—negates a commercial supplier's strict liability.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (misuse of a product).","rule":"A plaintiff's improper use of a defective product—ie, using the product in an unintended and unforeseeable fashion—negates a commercial supplier's strict liability.","irac":{"issue":"If the woman asserts a claim based on strict products liability against the drill company, which of the following facts will most likely negate the drill company's liability?","rule":"A plaintiff's improper use of a defective product—ie, using the product in an unintended and unforeseeable fashion—negates a commercial supplier's strict liability.","analysis":"When a commercial supplier's defective product causes harm to the plaintiff, the supplier is subject to strict products liability absent some defense. One defense that negates a commercial supplier's strict liability is the plaintiff's improper use of a product—ie, using the product in an unintended and unforeseeable manner. Here, the woman used the drill in an unintended manner because the drill was intended to be used only on plywood—not metal. So if it is established that the woman's use of the drill was also unforeseeable, then her improper use of the drill would negate the company's strict liability. (Choice A) A commercial supplier's use of every available means to inspect a product would be relevant to a negligence action, which considers the defendant's use of reasonable care. But the drill company's reasonable care is irrelevant to the woman's strict liability action, which imposes liability without proof of culpability. (Choice C) A commercial supplier is strictly liable when its defective product causes harm to any foreseeable plaintiff—not just direct purchasers. Therefore, the fact that the woman did not purchase the drill directly from the drill company is irrelevant. (Choice D) Contributory negligence refers to the plaintiff's failure to use the standard of care that a person should use for his/her own protection. This is no defense to strict products liability in this jurisdiction, which follows the traditional common law rules of contributory negligence.","conclusion":"Answer B: The drill was being put to an improper use."},"questionZh":"一个周末，一位退休男子在家中举办了车库旧货出售活动。\n\n一位从事房屋改造业务的女士来到现场，想找一把电钻。\n\n该男子恰好有一把符合她需求的电钻待售。\n\n这把电钻从未被使用过，且仍密封在原包装盒内。\n\n该电钻由一家钻具公司生产，该公司利用一切现有技术手段检查产品是否存在缺陷。\n\n该钻具公司原本仅将该电钻设计用于在胶合板上钻孔。\n\n一周后，该女士在使用这把电钻于厚金属板上钻孔时，因部件缺陷导致钻头从电钻上飞出，致使其眼睛严重受伤。\n\n该司法管辖区适用关于“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）的传统普通法规则。\n\n如果该女士针对该钻具公司提出基于“产品严格责任”的索赔，下列哪项事实最有可能免除该钻具公司的责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"该钻具公司利用一切现有手段检查了电钻是否存在缺陷。","B":"该电钻被用于不当用途。","C":"该女士并非直接从该钻具公司购买的电钻。","D":"该女士在厚金属板上使用电钻的行为构成与有过失（contributory negligence）。"},"explanationZh":"当商业供应商的缺陷产品对原告造成伤害时，若无抗辩事由，该供应商须承担严格的产品责任。\n\n能够排除商业供应商严格责任的一种抗辩事由是原告对产品的不当使用——即以非预期的、不可预见的方式使用产品。\n\n在本案中，该女子以非预期的方式使用了电钻，因为该电钻仅设计用于胶合板，而非金属。\n\n因此，如果能证实该女子使用电钻的方式也是不可预见的，那么她的不当使用行为将排除该公司的严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）商业供应商采取一切可用手段检验产品的行为，与过失诉讼（该诉讼考量被告是否尽到了合理的注意义务）相关。\n\n然而，电钻公司是否尽到合理注意义务，与该女子提起的严格责任诉讼无关，因为严格责任的认定无需证明被告存在过错。\n\n（选项 C）当商业供应商的缺陷产品对任何可预见原告（而不仅仅是直接购买者）造成伤害时，该供应商须承担严格责任。\n\n因此，该女子并非直接从电钻公司购买该电钻这一事实，与本案无关。\n\n（选项 D）“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）是指原告未能尽到保护自身安全所需的注意义务。\n\n在该司法管辖区（遵循传统的普通法与有过失（contributory negligence）规则），这不能作为针对严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的抗辩理由。\n\n教育目标：原告对缺陷产品的不当使用——即以非预期的、不可预见的方式使用产品——会排除商业供应商的严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 15 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（产品滥用）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"原告对有缺陷产品的不当使用——即以非预期且不可预见的方式使用该产品——会免除商业供应商的严格责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该女士针对该钻具公司提出基于“产品严格责任”的索赔，下列哪项事实最有可能免除该钻具公司的责任？","rule":"原告对有缺陷产品的不当使用——即以非预期且不可预见的方式使用该产品——会免除商业供应商的严格责任。","analysis":"当商业供应商的缺陷产品对原告造成伤害时，若无抗辩事由，该供应商须承担严格的产品责任。\n\n能够排除商业供应商严格责任的一种抗辩事由是原告对产品的不当使用——即以非预期的、不可预见的方式使用产品。\n\n在本案中，该女子以非预期的方式使用了电钻，因为该电钻仅设计用于胶合板，而非金属。\n\n因此，如果能证实该女子使用电钻的方式也是不可预见的，那么她的不当使用行为将排除该公司的严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）商业供应商采取一切可用手段检验产品的行为，与过失诉讼（该诉讼考量被告是否尽到了合理的注意义务）相关。\n\n然而，电钻公司是否尽到合理注意义务，与该女子提起的严格责任诉讼无关，因为严格责任的认定无需证明被告存在过错。\n\n（选项 C）当商业供应商的缺陷产品对任何可预见原告（而不仅仅是直接购买者）造成伤害时，该供应商须承担严格责任。\n\n因此，该女子并非直接从电钻公司购买该电钻这一事实，与本案无关。\n\n（选项 D）“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）是指原告未能尽到保护自身安全所需的注意义务。\n\n在该司法管辖区（遵循传统的普通法与有过失（contributory negligence）规则），这不能作为针对严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的抗辩理由。\n\n教育目标：原告对缺陷产品的不当使用——即以非预期的、不可预见的方式使用产品——会排除商业供应商的严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 15 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（产品滥用）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该电钻被用于不当用途。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-3086cf5ad5529083"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":207,"sourceQuestionPage":621,"sourceQuestionPages":[621],"sourceExplanationPages":[622,623],"sourceAnswerPages":[622,623],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-208","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A window manufacturer made windows for a commercial skyscraper. The manufacturer made the windows in strict accordance with the specifications provided by the general contractor for the building and used reasonable care in making the windows. Nevertheless, a defect in the design of the windows resulted in one of the windows suddenly shattering and injuring an office worker who sat next to the window in the completed building. Is the office worker likely to recover damages in an action against the manufacturer based solely on strict products liability?","choices":{"A":"No, because the manufacturer strictly adhered to the specifications provided.","B":"No, because the windows' defective design was attributable to the general contractor's negligence.","C":"Yes, because the manufacturer was in privity with the general contractor.","D":"Yes, because the office worker was injured by the defective window."},"answer":"D","explanation":"To recover in an action based on strict products liability, the plaintiff must prove the following elements: The defendant was a commercial seller (eg, manufacturer—as seen here).* The product was defective when it left the defendant's control. The defect caused the plaintiff physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage). Here, there was a defect in the design of the windows, which necessarily existed when the windows left the manufacturer's control. That defect resulted in one of the windows suddenly shattering and injuring the office worker. Therefore, the office worker is likely to recover damages in an action against the manufacturer based on strict products liability. *A commercial seller does not include someone who makes casual or occasional sales that are not part of his/her regular business (eg, an individual car owner who sells a car to his neighbor). (Choices A & B) The manufacturer's strict adherence to the specifications provided by the general contractor shows that the windows' defective design was attributable to the general contractor. But strict products liability is imposed on commercial sellers without proof of fault (eg, negligence)—even if the seller did not create the defect—so long as the defect existed at the time it left the seller's control (as seen with the manufacturer here). (Choice C) Although the manufacturer and the general contractor were in privity, privity is not a requirement in a strict liability action. Additionally, even if privity were a requirement, it would be based on the relationship of the manufacturer (as defendant) and the office worker (as plaintiff)—not the manufacturer and the general contractor.\n\nEducational objective: A strict products liability claim requires proof that (1) the defendant was a commercial seller, (2) the product was defective when it left the defendant's control, and (3) the defect caused the plaintiff physical harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability for products that are defective in design).","educationalObjective":"A strict products liability claim requires proof that (1) the defendant was a commercial seller, (2) the product was defective when it left the defendant's control, and (3) the defect caused the plaintiff physical harm.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability for products that are defective in design).","rule":"A strict products liability claim requires proof that (1) the defendant was a commercial seller, (2) the product was defective when it left the defendant's control, and (3) the defect caused the plaintiff physical harm.","irac":{"issue":"Is the office worker likely to recover damages in an action against the manufacturer based solely on strict products liability?","rule":"A strict products liability claim requires proof that (1) the defendant was a commercial seller, (2) the product was defective when it left the defendant's control, and (3) the defect caused the plaintiff physical harm.","analysis":"To recover in an action based on strict products liability, the plaintiff must prove the following elements: The defendant was a commercial seller (eg, manufacturer—as seen here).* The product was defective when it left the defendant's control. The defect caused the plaintiff physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage). Here, there was a defect in the design of the windows, which necessarily existed when the windows left the manufacturer's control. That defect resulted in one of the windows suddenly shattering and injuring the office worker. Therefore, the office worker is likely to recover damages in an action against the manufacturer based on strict products liability. *A commercial seller does not include someone who makes casual or occasional sales that are not part of his/her regular business (eg, an individual car owner who sells a car to his neighbor). (Choices A & B) The manufacturer's strict adherence to the specifications provided by the general contractor shows that the windows' defective design was attributable to the general contractor. But strict products liability is imposed on commercial sellers without proof of fault (eg, negligence)—even if the seller did not create the defect—so long as the defect existed at the time it left the seller's control (as seen with the manufacturer here). (Choice C) Although the manufacturer and the general contractor were in privity, privity is not a requirement in a strict liability action. Additionally, even if privity were a requirement, it would be based on the relationship of the manufacturer (as defendant) and the office worker (as plaintiff)—not the manufacturer and the general contractor.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the office worker was injured by the defective window."},"questionZh":"一家窗户制造商为一座商业摩天大楼生产窗户。\n\n该制造商严格按照总承包商提供的建筑规范生产窗户，并在生产过程中尽到了合理的注意义务。\n\n然而，窗户设计上的缺陷导致其中一扇窗户突然破碎，砸伤了坐在已竣工大楼窗户旁的一名办公室工作人员。\n\n该工作人员能否仅依据严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）向制造商提起诉讼并获得赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为制造商严格遵守了提供的规范。","B":"否，因为窗户的设计缺陷是由于总承包商的过失造成的。","C":"是，因为制造商与总承包商存在合同关系。","D":"是，因为办公室工作人员因缺陷窗户而受伤。"},"explanationZh":"若要基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）（strict products liability）提起诉讼并获得赔偿，原告必须证明以下要素：被告属于商业销售者（例如本案中的制造商）*；产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷；该缺陷导致原告遭受人身伤害（即身体伤害或财产损失）。\n\n在本案中，窗户存在设计缺陷，而该缺陷必然在窗户脱离制造商控制时就已经存在。\n\n该缺陷导致其中一扇窗户突然破碎，并致使该办公室职员受伤。\n\n因此，该办公室职员很可能在针对制造商提起的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中获得赔偿。\n\n*“商业销售者”不包括那些进行非经常性或偶然性销售的人员（即销售行为不属于其常规业务范围的人员，例如将汽车卖给邻居的个人车主）。\n\n（选项 A 和 B）尽管制造商严格遵守了总承包商提供的规格说明，表明窗户的设计缺陷应归咎于总承包商，但严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）适用于商业销售者，且无需证明其存在过错（例如疏忽）——即使销售者并未制造该缺陷，只要缺陷在产品脱离其控制时已存在（如本案中的制造商），即应承担责任。\n\n（选项 C）尽管制造商与总承包商之间存在合同关系（privity），但在严格责任诉讼中，并不要求存在这种合同关系。\n\n此外，即使要求存在合同关系，其考量对象也应是制造商（作为被告）与办公室职员（作为原告）之间的关系，而非制造商与总承包商之间的关系。\n\n教学目标：严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔要求证明：(1) 被告为商业销售者；(2) 产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷；(3) 该缺陷导致原告遭受人身伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 2(b) 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（关于设计缺陷产品的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔需要证明以下三点：(1)被告是商业销售商；(2)产品在离开被告控制时存在缺陷；(3)该缺陷导致原告遭受人身伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"仅基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），该办公室职员是否有可能在针对制造商的诉讼中获得赔偿？","rule":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔需要证明以下三点：(1)被告是商业销售商；(2)产品在离开被告控制时存在缺陷；(3)该缺陷导致原告遭受人身伤害。","analysis":"若要基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）（strict products liability）提起诉讼并获得赔偿，原告必须证明以下要素：被告属于商业销售者（例如本案中的制造商）*；产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷；该缺陷导致原告遭受人身伤害（即身体伤害或财产损失）。\n\n在本案中，窗户存在设计缺陷，而该缺陷必然在窗户脱离制造商控制时就已经存在。\n\n该缺陷导致其中一扇窗户突然破碎，并致使该办公室职员受伤。\n\n因此，该办公室职员很可能在针对制造商提起的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中获得赔偿。\n\n*“商业销售者”不包括那些进行非经常性或偶然性销售的人员（即销售行为不属于其常规业务范围的人员，例如将汽车卖给邻居的个人车主）。\n\n（选项 A 和 B）尽管制造商严格遵守了总承包商提供的规格说明，表明窗户的设计缺陷应归咎于总承包商，但严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）适用于商业销售者，且无需证明其存在过错（例如疏忽）——即使销售者并未制造该缺陷，只要缺陷在产品脱离其控制时已存在（如本案中的制造商），即应承担责任。\n\n（选项 C）尽管制造商与总承包商之间存在合同关系（privity），但在严格责任诉讼中，并不要求存在这种合同关系。\n\n此外，即使要求存在合同关系，其考量对象也应是制造商（作为被告）与办公室职员（作为原告）之间的关系，而非制造商与总承包商之间的关系。\n\n教学目标：严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔要求证明：(1) 被告为商业销售者；(2) 产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷；(3) 该缺陷导致原告遭受人身伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 2(b) 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（关于设计缺陷产品的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是，因为办公室工作人员因缺陷窗户而受伤。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-6b9bd86c1ec551e2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":208,"sourceQuestionPage":624,"sourceQuestionPages":[624],"sourceExplanationPages":[625,626],"sourceAnswerPages":[625,626],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-209","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"In preparation for a mountain-climbing expedition, a climber purchased the necessary climbing equipment from a retailer in sporting goods. A week later, the climber fell from a rock face when a safety device he had purchased from the retailer malfunctioned because of a defect in its manufacture. Thereafter, a paramedic was severely injured when he tried to reach and give assistance to the climber on the ledge to which the climber had fallen. The paramedic's injury was not caused by any fault on his own part. This jurisdiction does not recognize the firefighter's rule in products liability actions. If the paramedic brings an action against the retailer to recover damages for his injuries, will the paramedic prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the paramedic did not rely on the representation of safety implied from the sale of the safety device by the retailer.","B":"No, unless the retailer could have discovered the defect by a reasonable inspection of the safety device.","C":"Yes, because injury to a person in the paramedic's position was foreseeable if the safety device failed.","D":"Yes, unless the climber was negligent in failing to test the safety device."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Under products liability law, a commercial seller is strictly liable when (1) it produces or sells a defective product and (2) the product's defect harms a foreseeable plaintiff—ie, a person who is reasonably likely to suffer harm because of the defect. A rescuer is a foreseeable plaintiff because a rescuer is likely to risk injury by aiding a person endangered by the defective product.* Here, the retailer sold a defective safety device that caused the climber to fall. The paramedic then tried to assist the fallen climber and was severely injured in the process. Since it was reasonably foreseeable that the paramedic would risk injury by attempting to aid a climber who was injured by the defective safety device, the paramedic will likely prevail. *In jurisdictions that recognize the firefighter's rule in products liability actions (not seen here), professional rescuers are generally precluded from recovering for injuries sustained in attempting to rescue an individual harmed by the defective product. (Choice A) Products sold by a commercial seller have an implied warranty of merchantability—a seller's implied representation that the product is safe and suitable for its ordinary purpose. However, a plaintiff's reliance on a seller's implied warranty is irrelevant to a strict products liability claim since reliance is not an element of that claim. (Choice B) Since strict liability is imposed without proof of the defendant's culpability, the retailer would be strictly liable regardless of whether it could have discovered the defect by a reasonable inspection of the safety device. (Choice D) A consumer has no duty to inspect a product for manufacturing defects. Therefore, the climber could not have been negligent in failing to test the safety device.\n\nEducational objective: A commercial seller (eg, manufacturer, wholesaler, retailer) is subject to strict products liability when (1) it sells a defective product and (2) the product's defect harms a foreseeable plaintiff (eg, purchaser, rescuer).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(a) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability for products that contain manufacturing defects).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical &; Emotional Harm § 32 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (liability to rescuers).","educationalObjective":"A commercial seller (eg, manufacturer, wholesaler, retailer) is subject to strict products liability when (1) it sells a defective product and (2) the product's defect harms a foreseeable plaintiff (eg, purchaser, rescuer).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(a) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability for products that contain manufacturing defects).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical &; Emotional Harm § 32 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (liability to rescuers).","rule":"A commercial seller (eg, manufacturer, wholesaler, retailer) is subject to strict products liability when (1) it sells a defective product and (2) the product's defect harms a foreseeable plaintiff (eg, purchaser, rescuer).","irac":{"issue":"If the paramedic brings an action against the retailer to recover damages for his injuries, will the paramedic prevail?","rule":"A commercial seller (eg, manufacturer, wholesaler, retailer) is subject to strict products liability when (1) it sells a defective product and (2) the product's defect harms a foreseeable plaintiff (eg, purchaser, rescuer).","analysis":"Under products liability law, a commercial seller is strictly liable when (1) it produces or sells a defective product and (2) the product's defect harms a foreseeable plaintiff—ie, a person who is reasonably likely to suffer harm because of the defect. A rescuer is a foreseeable plaintiff because a rescuer is likely to risk injury by aiding a person endangered by the defective product.* Here, the retailer sold a defective safety device that caused the climber to fall. The paramedic then tried to assist the fallen climber and was severely injured in the process. Since it was reasonably foreseeable that the paramedic would risk injury by attempting to aid a climber who was injured by the defective safety device, the paramedic will likely prevail. *In jurisdictions that recognize the firefighter's rule in products liability actions (not seen here), professional rescuers are generally precluded from recovering for injuries sustained in attempting to rescue an individual harmed by the defective product. (Choice A) Products sold by a commercial seller have an implied warranty of merchantability—a seller's implied representation that the product is safe and suitable for its ordinary purpose. However, a plaintiff's reliance on a seller's implied warranty is irrelevant to a strict products liability claim since reliance is not an element of that claim. (Choice B) Since strict liability is imposed without proof of the defendant's culpability, the retailer would be strictly liable regardless of whether it could have discovered the defect by a reasonable inspection of the safety device. (Choice D) A consumer has no duty to inspect a product for manufacturing defects. Therefore, the climber could not have been negligent in failing to test the safety device.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because injury to a person in the paramedic's position was foreseeable if the safety device failed."},"questionZh":"为了准备一次登山探险，一名登山者从一家体育用品零售商处购买了必要的登山装备。\n\n一周后，这名登山者从岩壁上坠落，原因是其从该零售商处购买的安全装置因制造缺陷而发生故障。\n\n随后，一名医护人员试图前往登山者坠落的岩架处施救，结果严重受伤。\n\n该医护人员的受伤并非由其自身过错造成。\n\n本司法管辖区在产品责任诉讼中不承认“消防员规则”。\n\n如果该医护人员起诉零售商，要求赔偿其受伤损失，他能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为该医护人员并未依赖零售商销售安全装置时所隐含的安全保证。","B":"不能，除非零售商可以通过对安全装置的合理检查发现该缺陷。","C":"是的，因为如果安全装置失效，医护人员受伤是可以预见的。","D":"是的，除非攀岩者疏忽大意，没有测试安全装置。"},"explanationZh":"根据产品责任法，商业销售者在以下情况下承担严格责任：(1) 其生产或销售了有缺陷的产品；(2) 该产品的缺陷伤害了“可预见原告”——即因该缺陷而合理地可能遭受伤害的人。\n\n救援人员属于可预见原告，因为他们在协助因缺陷产品而陷入危险的人时，很可能会面临受伤风险。* 在本案中，零售商销售了导致攀岩者坠落的缺陷安全装置。\n\n随后，急救人员试图协助坠落的攀岩者，并在过程中受了重伤。\n\n由于急救人员在试图救助因缺陷安全装置受伤的攀岩者时面临受伤风险是合理可预见的，因此该急救人员很可能会胜诉。\n\n* 在承认产品责任诉讼中适用“消防员规则”（本案不适用）的司法管辖区，专业救援人员通常不得就因试图营救受缺陷产品伤害的人员而遭受的伤害获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）商业销售者销售的产品附带“适销性默示保证”——即销售者默示保证产品安全且适合其通常用途。\n\n然而，原告是否信赖销售者的默示保证与严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔无关，因为“信赖”并非该类索赔的构成要件。\n\n（选项 B）由于严格责任的适用无需证明被告存在过错，因此无论零售商是否本可以通过对安全装置进行合理检查来发现缺陷，其均须承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）消费者没有义务检查产品是否存在制造缺陷。\n\n因此，攀岩者未测试安全装置的行为不构成过失。\n\n教育目标：商业销售者（例如制造商、批发商、零售商）在以下情况下须承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）：(1) 其销售了有缺陷的产品；(2) 该产品的缺陷伤害了可预见原告（例如购买者、救援人员）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 2(a) 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（关于存在制造缺陷的产品的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 32 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于对救援人员的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商业销售商（例如，制造商、批发商、零售商）在以下情况下承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）：(1) 销售有缺陷的产品；(2) 该产品的缺陷对可预见的受害者（例如，购买者、救援人员）造成了损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果医护人员起诉零售商，要求赔偿其受伤损失，医护人员会胜诉吗？","rule":"商业销售商（例如，制造商、批发商、零售商）在以下情况下承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）：(1) 销售有缺陷的产品；(2) 该产品的缺陷对可预见的受害者（例如，购买者、救援人员）造成了损害。","analysis":"根据产品责任法，商业销售者在以下情况下承担严格责任：(1) 其生产或销售了有缺陷的产品；(2) 该产品的缺陷伤害了“可预见原告”——即因该缺陷而合理地可能遭受伤害的人。\n\n救援人员属于可预见原告，因为他们在协助因缺陷产品而陷入危险的人时，很可能会面临受伤风险。* 在本案中，零售商销售了导致攀岩者坠落的缺陷安全装置。\n\n随后，急救人员试图协助坠落的攀岩者，并在过程中受了重伤。\n\n由于急救人员在试图救助因缺陷安全装置受伤的攀岩者时面临受伤风险是合理可预见的，因此该急救人员很可能会胜诉。\n\n* 在承认产品责任诉讼中适用“消防员规则”（本案不适用）的司法管辖区，专业救援人员通常不得就因试图营救受缺陷产品伤害的人员而遭受的伤害获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 A）商业销售者销售的产品附带“适销性默示保证”——即销售者默示保证产品安全且适合其通常用途。\n\n然而，原告是否信赖销售者的默示保证与严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔无关，因为“信赖”并非该类索赔的构成要件。\n\n（选项 B）由于严格责任的适用无需证明被告存在过错，因此无论零售商是否本可以通过对安全装置进行合理检查来发现缺陷，其均须承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）消费者没有义务检查产品是否存在制造缺陷。\n\n因此，攀岩者未测试安全装置的行为不构成过失。\n\n教育目标：商业销售者（例如制造商、批发商、零售商）在以下情况下须承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）：(1) 其销售了有缺陷的产品；(2) 该产品的缺陷伤害了可预见原告（例如购买者、救援人员）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 2(a) 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（关于存在制造缺陷的产品的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 32 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于对救援人员的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为如果安全装置失效，医护人员受伤是可以预见的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-e1a0165fceec36ad"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":209,"sourceQuestionPage":627,"sourceQuestionPages":[627],"sourceExplanationPages":[628,629],"sourceAnswerPages":[628,629],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-210","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A man was driving his new car along a dark road with the car's high-beam headlights on to illuminate the road. When he saw the headlights of another car appear in the distance, he reached to turn the high beams off. Instead of turning from high-beam to low-beam, the headlights on the car turned off completely. The man tried repeatedly to turn the lights on again but could not do so. He collided with the other car and suffered injuries. The man has brought an action against the manufacturer of the headlight controls in his car, the manufacturer of his car, and the retailer who sold him the car. If the man can establish that a defect in the controls caused the accident, from whom can he recover?","choices":{"A":"Only the manufacturer of the car.","B":"Only the manufacturer of the car and the retailer of the car.","C":"Only the manufacturer of the headlight controls and the manufacturer of the car.","D":"All three defendants."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution (eg, manufacturer, distributor, retailer) may be subject to strict products liability if (1) the product contained a defect when it left that commercial supplier's control and (2) the defect caused the plaintiff's harm. Strict liability (liability without proof of fault) is imposed even when the commercial supplier did not directly cause—or was unaware of—the product defect. Here, a defect in the man's headlight controls caused him to collide with another car and suffer injuries. That defect existed at the time the headlight manufacturer sold the headlight controls to the car manufacturer. And the defect continued to exist when the car manufacturer sold the car (with the defective headlight controls) to the retailer and when the retailer sold that car to the man. As a result, all three defendants are strictly liable for the man's injuries (Choices A, B, & C).\n\nEducational objective: Any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution may be strictly liable for a defective product that harms the plaintiff if the defect existed at the time the product left the commercial supplier's control.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (commercial supplier liability).","educationalObjective":"Any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution may be strictly liable for a defective product that harms the plaintiff if the defect existed at the time the product left the commercial supplier's control.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (commercial supplier liability).","rule":"Any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution may be strictly liable for a defective product that harms the plaintiff if the defect existed at the time the product left the commercial supplier's control.","irac":{"issue":"If the man can establish that a defect in the controls caused the accident, from whom can he recover?","rule":"Any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution may be strictly liable for a defective product that harms the plaintiff if the defect existed at the time the product left the commercial supplier's control.","analysis":"Any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution (eg, manufacturer, distributor, retailer) may be subject to strict products liability if (1) the product contained a defect when it left that commercial supplier's control and (2) the defect caused the plaintiff's harm. Strict liability (liability without proof of fault) is imposed even when the commercial supplier did not directly cause—or was unaware of—the product defect. Here, a defect in the man's headlight controls caused him to collide with another car and suffer injuries. That defect existed at the time the headlight manufacturer sold the headlight controls to the car manufacturer. And the defect continued to exist when the car manufacturer sold the car (with the defective headlight controls) to the retailer and when the retailer sold that car to the man. As a result, all three defendants are strictly liable for the man's injuries (Choices A, B, & C).","conclusion":"Answer D: All three defendants."},"questionZh":"一名男子驾驶着他的新车行驶在一条漆黑的道路上，为了照亮道路，他打开了远光灯。\n\n当他看到远处出现另一辆车的车灯时，他伸手去关远光灯。\n\n然而，远光灯并没有切换到近光灯，而是完全熄灭了。\n\n该男子多次尝试重新打开车灯，但都失败了。\n\n他与另一辆车相撞，并因此受伤。\n\n该男子已对车灯控制系统的制造商、汽车制造商以及向他出售汽车的零售商提起诉讼。\n\n如果该男子能够证明是控制系统的缺陷导致了事故，他可以向谁索赔？","choicesZh":{"A":"仅汽车制造商。","B":"仅汽车制造商和汽车零售商。","C":"仅车灯控制系统的制造商和汽车制造商。","D":"所有三名被告。"},"explanationZh":"分销链中的任何商业供应商（例如制造商、分销商、零售商）都可能承担严格的产品责任，前提是：(1) 产品在离开该商业供应商的控制范围时已存在缺陷；以及 (2) 该缺陷导致了原告受损。\n\n即使商业供应商并未直接导致产品缺陷，或对缺陷毫不知情，也需承担严格责任（即无需证明存在过错的责任）。\n\n在本案中，该男子车辆前大灯控制装置的缺陷导致其与另一辆车相撞并受伤。\n\n该缺陷在“前大灯控制装置制造商”将该装置出售给“汽车制造商”时便已存在；且在“汽车制造商”将装有该缺陷装置的汽车出售给“零售商”，以及“零售商”将该车出售给该男子时，该缺陷依然存在。\n\n因此，这三方被告均需对该男子的伤害承担严格责任（选项 A、B 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：如果缺陷在产品离开商业供应商控制范围时已存在，则分销链中的任何商业供应商都可能因导致原告受损的缺陷产品而承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第 1 条注释 e (Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 cmt. e) (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（关于商业供应商责任的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果缺陷产品在离开分销链时已存在，且该缺陷产品对原告造成损害，则分销链中的任何商业供应商都可能承担严格责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果原告能够证明是控制环节的缺陷导致了事故，那么他可以向谁索赔？","rule":"如果缺陷产品在离开分销链时已存在，且该缺陷产品对原告造成损害，则分销链中的任何商业供应商都可能承担严格责任。","analysis":"分销链中的任何商业供应商（例如制造商、分销商、零售商）都可能承担严格的产品责任，前提是：(1) 产品在离开该商业供应商的控制范围时已存在缺陷；以及 (2) 该缺陷导致了原告受损。\n\n即使商业供应商并未直接导致产品缺陷，或对缺陷毫不知情，也需承担严格责任（即无需证明存在过错的责任）。\n\n在本案中，该男子车辆前大灯控制装置的缺陷导致其与另一辆车相撞并受伤。\n\n该缺陷在“前大灯控制装置制造商”将该装置出售给“汽车制造商”时便已存在；且在“汽车制造商”将装有该缺陷装置的汽车出售给“零售商”，以及“零售商”将该车出售给该男子时，该缺陷依然存在。\n\n因此，这三方被告均需对该男子的伤害承担严格责任（选项 A、B 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：如果缺陷在产品离开商业供应商控制范围时已存在，则分销链中的任何商业供应商都可能因导致原告受损的缺陷产品而承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第 1 条注释 e (Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 cmt. e) (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（关于商业供应商责任的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 D：所有三名被告。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-1b24b14ad72709f3"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":210,"sourceQuestionPage":630,"sourceQuestionPages":[630],"sourceExplanationPages":[631,632],"sourceAnswerPages":[631,632],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-211","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"An environmentalist purchased a newly released electric car from a car manufacturer. The car possessed many innovative features, including the ability to drive and maneuver itself through traffic with minimal assistance from the driver. One day, the environmentalist entered an address into the car's computer system and followed all protocol regarding the amount of assistance she needed to give the car in order to reach her destination. However, as the car was driving her to the destination, the computer system malfunctioned. The car swerved violently into oncoming traffic, causing a car accident in which the environmentalist sustained a broken arm. The environmentalist subsequently filed a strict products liability action against the car manufacturer under a design-defect theory of recovery. At trial, the environmentalist introduced evidence that a reasonable alternative design for the computer system was available to the car manufacturer and that failure to use that design rendered the car not reasonably safe. In its defense, the car manufacturer introduced evidence that the car's computer system complied with all governmental safety standards for electric cars. In addition, the car manufacturer demonstrated that the car's computer system was \"state of the art\" by introducing as evidence the level of relevant technological knowledge existing and reasonably feasible at the time the car was distributed. Will the environmentalist likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the car's computer system complied with all governmental safety standards for electric cars.","B":"No, because the car's computer system was \"state of the art\" based upon the technological knowledge existing and reasonably feasible at the time the car was released.","C":"Yes, because the car manufacturer negligently designed the car's computer system since there was a reasonable alternative design available to it.","D":"Yes, because the car's computer system was defective and the environmentalist was injured as a result of the computer system's malfunction."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Strict liability may be imposed on a commercial seller (eg, manufacturer) that produces or sells a defective product that causes the plaintiff physical harm. A product is defective by design if: the product's design is unreasonably dangerous to consumers and\n\nthat danger could have been mitigated by a feasible alternative design—ie, a less dangerous and economically reasonable modification or alternative design.\n\nIn its defense, the commercial seller may introduce evidence that the product's design was \"state of the art.\" The \"state of the art\" standard is met if the design complied with the level of relevant scientific, technological, and safety knowledge existing and reasonably feasible at the time the product was distributed. However, compliance with this standard does NOT bar the plaintiff's recovery against the commercial seller as a matter of law.* Here, the environmentalist introduced evidence that a reasonable alternative design for the car's computer system was available to the car manufacturer and that failure to use that design rendered the car not reasonably safe (design defect). Although the manufacturer then introduced evidence that the car's computer system was \"state of the art,\" this does not bar recovery against the manufacturer (Choice B). Therefore, the environmentalist will likely prevail because the car's computer system was defective and she was injured as a result of its malfunction. *Some states have enacted statutes providing that compliance with the state-of-the-art standard is a total bar to recovery (not seen here). (Choice A) A product that fails to comply with governmental safety standards is deemed defective. But a product's compliance with such standards is not conclusive evidence that the product is free from defects. (Choice C) Whether the car manufacturer negligently designed the car's computer system is irrelevant in this strict liability action.\n\nEducational objective: In a strict products liability action brought under a defective-design theory of recovery, the manufacturer's evidence that the product's design was \"state of the art\" does not bar the plaintiff's recovery as a matter of law.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial seller for design defects).","educationalObjective":"In a strict products liability action brought under a defective-design theory of recovery, the manufacturer's evidence that the product's design was \"state of the art\" does not bar the plaintiff's recovery as a matter of law.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial seller for design defects).","rule":"In a strict products liability action brought under a defective-design theory of recovery, the manufacturer's evidence that the product's design was \"state of the art\" does not bar the plaintiff's recovery as a matter of law.","irac":{"issue":"Will the environmentalist likely prevail?","rule":"In a strict products liability action brought under a defective-design theory of recovery, the manufacturer's evidence that the product's design was \"state of the art\" does not bar the plaintiff's recovery as a matter of law.","analysis":"Strict liability may be imposed on a commercial seller (eg, manufacturer) that produces or sells a defective product that causes the plaintiff physical harm. A product is defective by design if: the product's design is unreasonably dangerous to consumers and\n\nthat danger could have been mitigated by a feasible alternative design—ie, a less dangerous and economically reasonable modification or alternative design.\n\nIn its defense, the commercial seller may introduce evidence that the product's design was \"state of the art.\" The \"state of the art\" standard is met if the design complied with the level of relevant scientific, technological, and safety knowledge existing and reasonably feasible at the time the product was distributed. However, compliance with this standard does NOT bar the plaintiff's recovery against the commercial seller as a matter of law.* Here, the environmentalist introduced evidence that a reasonable alternative design for the car's computer system was available to the car manufacturer and that failure to use that design rendered the car not reasonably safe (design defect). Although the manufacturer then introduced evidence that the car's computer system was \"state of the art,\" this does not bar recovery against the manufacturer (Choice B). Therefore, the environmentalist will likely prevail because the car's computer system was defective and she was injured as a result of its malfunction. *Some states have enacted statutes providing that compliance with the state-of-the-art standard is a total bar to recovery (not seen here). (Choice A) A product that fails to comply with governmental safety standards is deemed defective. But a product's compliance with such standards is not conclusive evidence that the product is free from defects. (Choice C) Whether the car manufacturer negligently designed the car's computer system is irrelevant in this strict liability action.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the car's computer system was defective and the environmentalist was injured as a result of the computer system's malfunction."},"questionZh":"一位环保人士从汽车制造商处购买了一辆新上市的电动汽车。\n\n该车具备许多创新功能，包括能够在驾驶员极少辅助的情况下在车流中自动驾驶和操控。\n\n一天，这位环保人士在车载计算机系统中输入了目的地地址，并遵循了所有关于辅助驾驶要求的操作规程。\n\n然而，在车辆驶向目的地的过程中，计算机系统发生了故障。\n\n车辆猛烈转向，冲入对向车道，引发交通事故，导致该环保人士手臂骨折。\n\n随后，该环保人士依据“设计缺陷”理论，对汽车制造商提起了严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n在庭审中，该环保人士提交证据表明，汽车制造商当时完全可以采用一种合理的替代设计，而未采用该设计导致车辆不具备合理的安全性。\n\n作为抗辩，汽车制造商提交证据证明，该车的计算机系统符合所有针对电动汽车的政府安全标准。\n\n此外，汽车制造商还通过提交关于车辆投放市场时已存在且在技术上合理可行的相关技术知识水平的证据，证明其计算机系统代表了当时“最先进的技术水平”（state of the art）。\n\n该环保人士胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为该车的计算机系统符合所有针对电动汽车的政府安全标准。","B":"不大，因为根据车辆投放市场时已存在且在技术上合理可行的技术知识，该车的计算机系统代表了当时“最先进的技术水平”。","C":"大，因为汽车制造商在设计车载计算机系统时存在过失，当时存在一种合理的替代设计可供其采用。","D":"大，因为该车的计算机系统存在缺陷，且该环保人士因计算机系统故障而受伤。"},"explanationZh":"如果商业销售者（例如制造商）生产或销售了导致原告身体伤害的缺陷产品，则可能被追究严格责任。\n\n若产品设计对消费者构成不合理的危险，且该危险本可通过一种可行的替代设计（即危险性较低且在经济上合理的改进或替代方案）来减轻，则该产品被视为存在设计缺陷。\n\n作为抗辩，商业销售者可以提交证据证明其产品设计符合“现有技术水平”（state of the art）。\n\n如果设计符合产品投放市场时已存在且在技术上切实可行的相关科学、技术及安全知识水平，即视为符合“现有技术水平”标准。\n\n然而，符合该标准并不能在法律上绝对排除原告向商业销售者寻求赔偿的权利。* 在本案中，环保人士提交了证据，证明汽车制造商当时有能力采用一种合理的替代设计方案来制造汽车计算机系统，而未采用该方案导致汽车不具备合理的安全性（即存在设计缺陷）。\n\n尽管制造商随后提交证据证明其汽车计算机系统符合“现有技术水平”，但这并不妨碍原告向制造商寻求赔偿（选项 B）。\n\n因此，该环保人士很可能胜诉，因为汽车计算机系统存在缺陷，且她因该系统的故障而受伤。\n\n* 某些州已制定法规，规定符合“现有技术水平”标准可完全排除赔偿责任（本案不涉及此类情况）。\n\n（选项 A）未能符合政府安全标准的产品被视为有缺陷。\n\n但产品符合此类标准并不构成该产品无缺陷的决定性证据。\n\n（选项 C）在本案的严格责任诉讼中，汽车制造商在设计汽车计算机系统时是否存在过失是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：在基于设计缺陷理论提起的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中，制造商提交的关于产品设计符合“现有技术水平”的证据，并不能在法律上排除原告获得赔偿的权利。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 2(b) 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于商业销售者对设计缺陷承担责任的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在依据“设计缺陷”理论提起的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中，制造商关于产品设计代表“最先进技术水平”的证据，并不在法律上构成阻碍原告获得赔偿的理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"该环保人士胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"在依据“设计缺陷”理论提起的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中，制造商关于产品设计代表“最先进技术水平”的证据，并不在法律上构成阻碍原告获得赔偿的理由。","analysis":"如果商业销售者（例如制造商）生产或销售了导致原告身体伤害的缺陷产品，则可能被追究严格责任。\n\n若产品设计对消费者构成不合理的危险，且该危险本可通过一种可行的替代设计（即危险性较低且在经济上合理的改进或替代方案）来减轻，则该产品被视为存在设计缺陷。\n\n作为抗辩，商业销售者可以提交证据证明其产品设计符合“现有技术水平”（state of the art）。\n\n如果设计符合产品投放市场时已存在且在技术上切实可行的相关科学、技术及安全知识水平，即视为符合“现有技术水平”标准。\n\n然而，符合该标准并不能在法律上绝对排除原告向商业销售者寻求赔偿的权利。* 在本案中，环保人士提交了证据，证明汽车制造商当时有能力采用一种合理的替代设计方案来制造汽车计算机系统，而未采用该方案导致汽车不具备合理的安全性（即存在设计缺陷）。\n\n尽管制造商随后提交证据证明其汽车计算机系统符合“现有技术水平”，但这并不妨碍原告向制造商寻求赔偿（选项 B）。\n\n因此，该环保人士很可能胜诉，因为汽车计算机系统存在缺陷，且她因该系统的故障而受伤。\n\n* 某些州已制定法规，规定符合“现有技术水平”标准可完全排除赔偿责任（本案不涉及此类情况）。\n\n（选项 A）未能符合政府安全标准的产品被视为有缺陷。\n\n但产品符合此类标准并不构成该产品无缺陷的决定性证据。\n\n（选项 C）在本案的严格责任诉讼中，汽车制造商在设计汽车计算机系统时是否存在过失是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：在基于设计缺陷理论提起的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼中，制造商提交的关于产品设计符合“现有技术水平”的证据，并不能在法律上排除原告获得赔偿的权利。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 2(b) 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于商业销售者对设计缺陷承担责任的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 D：大，因为该车的计算机系统存在缺陷，且该环保人士因计算机系统故障而受伤。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-6b9bd86c1ec551e2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":211,"sourceQuestionPage":633,"sourceQuestionPages":[633],"sourceExplanationPages":[634,635],"sourceAnswerPages":[634,635],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-212","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"A fumigation company was hired to eliminate pests in one of two buildings in a condominium complex that shared a common wall. The owners of the complex told the fumigation company that the common wall separating the infested building from the uninfested building was an impenetrable fire wall. The fumigation company did its own thorough inspection and determined that the buildings were indeed completely separated by the wall. Residents of the condominium units in the building that was to be sprayed were told to evacuate, but the residents of the uninfested building were told that they could remain while the other building was treated. During and shortly after the fumigation, in which a highly toxic chemical was used, many residents of the uninfested building became sick. It was determined that their illnesses were caused by the fumigation chemical. In fact, there was a hole in the fire wall separating the two buildings, but because it could only be observed from a specific position in the crawl space underneath the floor of the uninfested building, it had not been discovered by either the fumigation company or any previous building inspector. Are the residents of the uninfested building likely to prevail in a tort action against the fumigation company?","choices":{"A":"No, because the condominium complex owners were responsible for accurately conveying the condition of their buildings.","B":"No, because the fumigation company exercised a high level of care.","C":"Yes, because the fumigation company can be held strictly liable for its activity.","D":"Yes, because the fumigation company put a dangerous product into the stream of commerce."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Abnormally dangerous activity\n\nAn activity is abnormally dangerous when:\n\nit is uncommon in the community and it presents a foreseeable & highly significant risk of physical harm that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care A defendant is strictly liable (liable regardless of fault) for the plaintiff's harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity. An activity is abnormally dangerous when it (1) is uncommon in the community and (2) creates a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm to the plaintiff that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. Here, the use of highly toxic chemicals to fumigate a building (uncommon activity in the community) is an abnormally dangerous activity since it is highly probable that persons exposed to the chemicals will become sick (foreseeable and significant risk of harm). And since several residents became sick after their exposure to the chemicals, the fumigation company will likely be strictly liable for their harm. (Choice A) The significant risk of harm posed by an abnormally dangerous activity cannot be mitigated by anyone's use of reasonable care. Therefore, the condominium owners' failure to exercise reasonable care (by not accurately conveying the condition of their buildings) will not extinguish the fumigation company's strict liability. (Choice B) A defendant engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable for the plaintiff's harm regardless of the level of care used in carrying out the activity. Therefore, the fumigation company's exercise of a high level of care does not negate its strict liability to the sick residents. (Choice D) A defendant may be strictly liable for distributing a defective or dangerous product into the stream of commerce, but that is irrelevant here since the fumigation company provided a service—not a product.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant is strictly liable for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity—an uncommon activity that poses a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. Therefore, the use of reasonable care is no defense.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (abnormally dangerous activity).\n\nOld Island Fumigation, Inc. v. Barbee, 604 So. 2d 1246 (Fla. Dist. Ct. App. 1992).","educationalObjective":"A defendant is strictly liable for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity—an uncommon activity that poses a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. Therefore, the use of reasonable care is no defense.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (abnormally dangerous activity).\n\nOld Island Fumigation, Inc. v. Barbee, 604 So. 2d 1246 (Fla. Dist. Ct. App. 1992).","rule":"A defendant is strictly liable for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity—an uncommon activity that poses a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. Therefore, the use of reasonable care is no defense.","irac":{"issue":"Are the residents of the uninfested building likely to prevail in a tort action against the fumigation company?","rule":"A defendant is strictly liable for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity—an uncommon activity that poses a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. Therefore, the use of reasonable care is no defense.","analysis":"Abnormally dangerous activity\n\nAn activity is abnormally dangerous when:\n\nit is uncommon in the community and it presents a foreseeable & highly significant risk of physical harm that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care A defendant is strictly liable (liable regardless of fault) for the plaintiff's harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity. An activity is abnormally dangerous when it (1) is uncommon in the community and (2) creates a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm to the plaintiff that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. Here, the use of highly toxic chemicals to fumigate a building (uncommon activity in the community) is an abnormally dangerous activity since it is highly probable that persons exposed to the chemicals will become sick (foreseeable and significant risk of harm). And since several residents became sick after their exposure to the chemicals, the fumigation company will likely be strictly liable for their harm. (Choice A) The significant risk of harm posed by an abnormally dangerous activity cannot be mitigated by anyone's use of reasonable care. Therefore, the condominium owners' failure to exercise reasonable care (by not accurately conveying the condition of their buildings) will not extinguish the fumigation company's strict liability. (Choice B) A defendant engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable for the plaintiff's harm regardless of the level of care used in carrying out the activity. Therefore, the fumigation company's exercise of a high level of care does not negate its strict liability to the sick residents. (Choice D) A defendant may be strictly liable for distributing a defective or dangerous product into the stream of commerce, but that is irrelevant here since the fumigation company provided a service—not a product.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the fumigation company can be held strictly liable for its activity."},"questionZh":"一家熏蒸公司受雇对某公寓大楼群中的一栋建筑进行虫害治理；该大楼群由两栋共用一堵墙的建筑组成。\n\n大楼业主告知熏蒸公司，分隔受虫害影响建筑与未受影响建筑的那堵共用墙是一堵严密无缝的防火墙。\n\n熏蒸公司进行了彻底的自行检查，确认这两栋建筑确实被该墙体完全隔开。\n\n拟进行熏蒸作业的大楼内的住户被要求撤离，而未受影响大楼的住户则被告知在另一栋楼进行处理期间可以留在家中。\n\n在使用了剧毒化学品的熏蒸作业期间及之后不久，未受影响大楼的许多住户出现了身体不适。\n\n经查明，他们的病症是由熏蒸化学品引起的。\n\n事实上，分隔两栋建筑的防火墙上存在一个孔洞，但由于该孔洞只能从未受影响大楼地板下方的狭窄空间（crawl space）内的特定位置才能看到，因此无论是熏蒸公司还是此前的任何建筑检查员都未曾发现它。\n\n未受影响大楼的住户针对熏蒸公司提起的侵权诉讼，胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不大，因为准确告知建筑状况的责任在于公寓大楼的业主。","B":"不大，因为熏蒸公司尽到了高度的注意义务。","C":"大，因为熏蒸公司可能需对其活动承担严格责任。","D":"大，因为熏蒸公司将一种危险产品投入了流通领域。"},"explanationZh":"异常危险活动 当某项活动具备以下特征时，即被视为异常危险活动： 该活动在当地社区不常见，且会带来可预见的、极高的人身伤害风险，而这种风险无法通过采取合理的注意措施来降低。对于因异常危险活动给原告造成的伤害，被告承担严格责任（即无论有无过错均须承担责任）。\n\n一项活动若 (1) 在社区内不常见，且 (2) 给原告造成了可预见的、极高的人身伤害风险（且该风险无法通过合理注意措施降低），即构成异常危险活动。\n\n在本案中，使用剧毒化学品对建筑物进行熏蒸（属于社区内不常见的活动）构成异常危险活动，因为接触这些化学品的人极有可能患病（即存在可预见且重大的伤害风险）。\n\n鉴于数名居民在接触化学品后患病，熏蒸公司很可能需对这些伤害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）异常危险活动所带来的重大伤害风险，无法通过任何人的合理注意措施加以降低。\n\n因此，即使公寓业主未尽到合理注意义务（例如未准确传达建筑物状况），也不能免除熏蒸公司的严格责任。\n\n（选项 B）从事异常危险活动的被告，无论在实施活动时采取了何种程度的注意措施，均须对原告遭受的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，熏蒸公司采取了高度注意措施这一事实，并不能免除其对患病居民应承担的严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）被告若将有缺陷或危险的产品投入流通领域，可能需承担严格责任；但这与本案无关，因为熏蒸公司提供的是服务，而非产品。\n\n教育目标：对于因异常危险活动造成的伤害，被告承担严格责任。所谓异常危险活动，是指在社区内不常见、且带来可预见的、极高的人身伤害风险且无法通过合理注意措施降低的活动。\n\n因此，采取合理注意措施不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 20 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于异常危险活动）。\n\nOld Island Fumigation, Inc. 诉 Barbee 案，604 So.\n\n2d 1246 (佛罗里达州上诉法院，1992年)。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对于因“异常危险活动”造成的损害，被告需承担严格责任；所谓异常危险活动，是指一种不常见的活动，其带来的身体伤害风险既可预见又极其严重，且无法通过合理的注意义务加以减轻。\n\n因此，尽到合理注意义务不能作为抗辩理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"未受影响大楼的住户针对熏蒸公司提起的侵权诉讼，胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"对于因“异常危险活动”造成的损害，被告需承担严格责任；所谓异常危险活动，是指一种不常见的活动，其带来的身体伤害风险既可预见又极其严重，且无法通过合理的注意义务加以减轻。\n\n因此，尽到合理注意义务不能作为抗辩理由。","analysis":"异常危险活动 当某项活动具备以下特征时，即被视为异常危险活动： 该活动在当地社区不常见，且会带来可预见的、极高的人身伤害风险，而这种风险无法通过采取合理的注意措施来降低。对于因异常危险活动给原告造成的伤害，被告承担严格责任（即无论有无过错均须承担责任）。\n\n一项活动若 (1) 在社区内不常见，且 (2) 给原告造成了可预见的、极高的人身伤害风险（且该风险无法通过合理注意措施降低），即构成异常危险活动。\n\n在本案中，使用剧毒化学品对建筑物进行熏蒸（属于社区内不常见的活动）构成异常危险活动，因为接触这些化学品的人极有可能患病（即存在可预见且重大的伤害风险）。\n\n鉴于数名居民在接触化学品后患病，熏蒸公司很可能需对这些伤害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A）异常危险活动所带来的重大伤害风险，无法通过任何人的合理注意措施加以降低。\n\n因此，即使公寓业主未尽到合理注意义务（例如未准确传达建筑物状况），也不能免除熏蒸公司的严格责任。\n\n（选项 B）从事异常危险活动的被告，无论在实施活动时采取了何种程度的注意措施，均须对原告遭受的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，熏蒸公司采取了高度注意措施这一事实，并不能免除其对患病居民应承担的严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）被告若将有缺陷或危险的产品投入流通领域，可能需承担严格责任；但这与本案无关，因为熏蒸公司提供的是服务，而非产品。\n\n教育目标：对于因异常危险活动造成的伤害，被告承担严格责任。所谓异常危险活动，是指在社区内不常见、且带来可预见的、极高的人身伤害风险且无法通过合理注意措施降低的活动。\n\n因此，采取合理注意措施不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 20 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于异常危险活动）。\n\nOld Island Fumigation, Inc. 诉 Barbee 案，604 So.\n\n2d 1246 (佛罗里达州上诉法院，1992年)。","conclusion":"答案 C：大，因为熏蒸公司可能需对其活动承担严格责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":212,"sourceQuestionPage":636,"sourceQuestionPages":[636],"sourceExplanationPages":[637,638],"sourceAnswerPages":[637,638],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-213","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A homeowner hired an arsonist to set fire to the homeowner's house so that the homeowner could collect the insurance proceeds from the fire. After pouring gasoline around the house, the arsonist lit the fire with his cigarette lighter and then put the lighter in his pocket. As the arsonist was standing back admiring his work, the lighter exploded in his pocket. The arsonist suffered severe burns to his leg. The arsonist brought an action against the manufacturer of the lighter based on strict products liability. Under applicable law, the rules of pure comparative fault apply in such actions. Will the arsonist prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the arsonist was injured in the course of committing a felony by the device used to perpetrate the felony.","B":"No, because the lighter was not being used for an intended or reasonably foreseeable purpose.","C":"Yes, if the arsonist can establish that the lighter was the proximate cause of his injury.","D":"Yes, if the lighter exploded because of a defect caused by a manufacturing error."},"answer":"D","explanation":"To prevail in an action based on strict products liability, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant is a commercial supplier—one engaged in the business of manufacturing, distributing, or selling products—who produced or sold a defective product that caused the plaintiff harm. A product is defective if a manufacturing error caused the product to deviate from its intended design despite the manufacturer's exercise of all possible care. Here, the cigarette lighter exploded in the arsonist's pocket and caused him severe burns. If that explosion resulted from a manufacturing error that made the lighter deviate from its intended design, then the manufacturer will be strictly liable for the arsonist's harm. (Choices A & B) A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a defective product used in a foreseeable manner. Therefore, a plaintiff's misuse of a product—using the product in an unintended and unforeseeable manner—negates a commercial supplier's strict liability. Here, the arsonist will prevail because the lighter exploded while he stored it in his pocket (foreseeable use)—not while he was using it to perpetrate a felony (misuse). (Choice C) Commercial suppliers are only strictly liable for harm proximately caused by the product's manufacturing error. Therefore, mere evidence that the lighter—not a manufacturing error in the lighter—proximately caused the arsonist's injury is insufficient to establish strict products liability.\n\nEducational objective: A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a manufacturing defect—a deviation from the product's intended design despite the manufacturer's exercise of all possible care.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial supplier).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 2(a) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (manufacturing defect).","educationalObjective":"A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a manufacturing defect—a deviation from the product's intended design despite the manufacturer's exercise of all possible care.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial supplier).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 2(a) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (manufacturing defect).","rule":"A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a manufacturing defect—a deviation from the product's intended design despite the manufacturer's exercise of all possible care.","irac":{"issue":"Will the arsonist prevail?","rule":"A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a manufacturing defect—a deviation from the product's intended design despite the manufacturer's exercise of all possible care.","analysis":"To prevail in an action based on strict products liability, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant is a commercial supplier—one engaged in the business of manufacturing, distributing, or selling products—who produced or sold a defective product that caused the plaintiff harm. A product is defective if a manufacturing error caused the product to deviate from its intended design despite the manufacturer's exercise of all possible care. Here, the cigarette lighter exploded in the arsonist's pocket and caused him severe burns. If that explosion resulted from a manufacturing error that made the lighter deviate from its intended design, then the manufacturer will be strictly liable for the arsonist's harm. (Choices A & B) A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a defective product used in a foreseeable manner. Therefore, a plaintiff's misuse of a product—using the product in an unintended and unforeseeable manner—negates a commercial supplier's strict liability. Here, the arsonist will prevail because the lighter exploded while he stored it in his pocket (foreseeable use)—not while he was using it to perpetrate a felony (misuse). (Choice C) Commercial suppliers are only strictly liable for harm proximately caused by the product's manufacturing error. Therefore, mere evidence that the lighter—not a manufacturing error in the lighter—proximately caused the arsonist's injury is insufficient to establish strict products liability.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, if the lighter exploded because of a defect caused by a manufacturing error."},"questionZh":"一位房主雇佣了一名纵火犯放火烧毁自己的房屋，以便获取火灾保险赔偿。\n\n纵火犯在房屋周围泼洒汽油后，用打火机点燃了火，随后将打火机放回口袋。\n\n正当他退后欣赏自己的“杰作”时，口袋里的打火机发生了爆炸，导致其腿部严重烧伤。\n\n纵火犯依据严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）原则，起诉了打火机制造商。\n\n根据相关法律，此类诉讼适用纯粹比较过失（pure comparative fault）规则。\n\n纵火犯能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为纵火犯是在实施重罪的过程中，因用于实施该重罪的装置而受伤。","B":"不能，因为该打火机的使用目的并非其预定用途或合理可预见的用途。","C":"能，如果纵火犯能证明该打火机是导致其受伤的法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）。","D":"能，如果打火机爆炸是由于制造错误导致的缺陷所致。"},"explanationZh":"若要在基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的诉讼中胜诉，原告必须证明被告属于商业供应商（即从事产品制造、分销或销售业务的主体），且该被告生产或销售了导致原告受损的缺陷产品。\n\n如果产品因制造错误而偏离了其预期设计（即便制造商已尽到一切可能的注意义务），则该产品即被视为存在缺陷。\n\n在本案中，打火机在纵火犯的口袋里发生爆炸，导致其严重烧伤。\n\n如果该爆炸源于制造错误，致使打火机偏离了预期设计，那么制造商即应对纵火犯所受的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A 和 B）商业供应商对因以可预见方式使用缺陷产品而造成的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，原告对产品的不当使用（即以非预期且不可预见的方式使用产品）会排除商业供应商的严格责任。\n\n在本案中，纵火犯将胜诉，因为打火机是在他将其放在口袋里（属于可预见的使用方式）时发生爆炸的，而非在他利用打火机实施重罪（属于不当使用）的过程中爆炸的。\n\n（选项 C）商业供应商仅对由产品制造错误直接导致（即具有法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）关系）的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，仅有证据表明是打火机（而非打火机中的制造错误）直接导致了纵火犯的伤害，并不足以确立严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n教育目标：商业供应商对因制造缺陷（即产品因制造错误而偏离预期设计，尽管制造商已尽到一切可能的注意义务）造成的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（商业供应商的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 2(a) 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（制造缺陷）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对于因制造缺陷——即尽管制造商已尽一切可能的注意义务，产品仍偏离了其预定设计——造成的损害，商业供应商须承担严格责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"纵火犯能胜诉吗？","rule":"对于因制造缺陷——即尽管制造商已尽一切可能的注意义务，产品仍偏离了其预定设计——造成的损害，商业供应商须承担严格责任。","analysis":"若要在基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的诉讼中胜诉，原告必须证明被告属于商业供应商（即从事产品制造、分销或销售业务的主体），且该被告生产或销售了导致原告受损的缺陷产品。\n\n如果产品因制造错误而偏离了其预期设计（即便制造商已尽到一切可能的注意义务），则该产品即被视为存在缺陷。\n\n在本案中，打火机在纵火犯的口袋里发生爆炸，导致其严重烧伤。\n\n如果该爆炸源于制造错误，致使打火机偏离了预期设计，那么制造商即应对纵火犯所受的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 A 和 B）商业供应商对因以可预见方式使用缺陷产品而造成的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，原告对产品的不当使用（即以非预期且不可预见的方式使用产品）会排除商业供应商的严格责任。\n\n在本案中，纵火犯将胜诉，因为打火机是在他将其放在口袋里（属于可预见的使用方式）时发生爆炸的，而非在他利用打火机实施重罪（属于不当使用）的过程中爆炸的。\n\n（选项 C）商业供应商仅对由产品制造错误直接导致（即具有法律法律近因（proximate cause）（proximate cause）关系）的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，仅有证据表明是打火机（而非打火机中的制造错误）直接导致了纵火犯的伤害，并不足以确立严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n教育目标：商业供应商对因制造缺陷（即产品因制造错误而偏离预期设计，尽管制造商已尽到一切可能的注意义务）造成的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（商业供应商的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 2(a) 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（制造缺陷）。","conclusion":"答案 D：能，如果打火机爆炸是由于制造错误导致的缺陷所致。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-b0690a9f76ce45e9"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":213,"sourceQuestionPage":639,"sourceQuestionPages":[639],"sourceExplanationPages":[640,641],"sourceAnswerPages":[640,641],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-214","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"A hot-air balloon touring company operated near a golf course. The company's property was separated from the golf course by a fence on which the company had posted signs warning people not to enter the property because of the dangers of balloons landing. A golfer on the golf course hit an errant shot onto the company's property, ignored the warning signs, and jumped over the fence to retrieve her golf ball. At about the same time, one of the company's balloons experienced mechanical problems and had to make an emergency landing to avoid crashing. The balloon, which was out of control when it landed, struck the golfer and injured her. The jurisdiction has decided that hot-air ballooning is an abnormally dangerous activity. In an action by the golfer against the company, does the company have any affirmative defenses?","choices":{"A":"No, because the balloon was out of control when it struck the golfer.","B":"No, because the company was engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity.","C":"Yes, because the balloon landed to avoid crashing.","D":"Yes, because the golfer assumed the risk by coming onto the company's property."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Generally, a defendant is strictly liable—liable regardless of fault—for physical harm caused to the plaintiff by an abnormally dangerous activity. However, assumption of the risk is an affirmative defense to a strict liability claim when the plaintiff (1) knows of the risk of harm and (2) voluntarily accepts that risk. Here, the company is strictly liable for the golfer's injury because one of its hot-air balloons (an abnormally dangerous activity in this jurisdiction) struck her as it landed. But since the golfer ignored the signs warning about the dangers of the balloons (knowledge) and entered the property (voluntary acceptance), the company can assert that the golfer assumed the risk as an affirmative defense (Choice B). (Choice A) Although the balloon was out of control when it struck the golfer, the company had posted signs warning people about the dangers of balloons landing. Because the golfer entered the property despite those warning signs, she assumed the risk of being harmed by a dangerous or out-of-control balloon landing. (Choice C) Since a defendant is strictly liable for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity (eg, hot-air ballooning), the use of reasonable care is no defense. Therefore, while it may have been reasonable to land the balloon to avoid a crash, the reasonableness of the company's conduct is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: Assumption of the risk is an affirmative defense to strict liability when the plaintiff (1) knows of the risk of harm and (2) voluntarily accepts that risk.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (abnormally dangerous activity).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 523 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (assumption of the risk as defense against abnormally dangerous activity).","educationalObjective":"Assumption of the risk is an affirmative defense to strict liability when the plaintiff (1) knows of the risk of harm and (2) voluntarily accepts that risk.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (abnormally dangerous activity).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 523 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (assumption of the risk as defense against abnormally dangerous activity).","rule":"Assumption of the risk is an affirmative defense to strict liability when the plaintiff (1) knows of the risk of harm and (2) voluntarily accepts that risk.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the golfer against the company, does the company have any affirmative defenses?","rule":"Assumption of the risk is an affirmative defense to strict liability when the plaintiff (1) knows of the risk of harm and (2) voluntarily accepts that risk.","analysis":"Generally, a defendant is strictly liable—liable regardless of fault—for physical harm caused to the plaintiff by an abnormally dangerous activity. However, assumption of the risk is an affirmative defense to a strict liability claim when the plaintiff (1) knows of the risk of harm and (2) voluntarily accepts that risk. Here, the company is strictly liable for the golfer's injury because one of its hot-air balloons (an abnormally dangerous activity in this jurisdiction) struck her as it landed. But since the golfer ignored the signs warning about the dangers of the balloons (knowledge) and entered the property (voluntary acceptance), the company can assert that the golfer assumed the risk as an affirmative defense (Choice B). (Choice A) Although the balloon was out of control when it struck the golfer, the company had posted signs warning people about the dangers of balloons landing. Because the golfer entered the property despite those warning signs, she assumed the risk of being harmed by a dangerous or out-of-control balloon landing. (Choice C) Since a defendant is strictly liable for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity (eg, hot-air ballooning), the use of reasonable care is no defense. Therefore, while it may have been reasonable to land the balloon to avoid a crash, the reasonableness of the company's conduct is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the golfer assumed the risk by coming onto the company's property."},"questionZh":"一家热气球观光公司在一家高尔夫球场附近运营。\n\n该公司场地与高尔夫球场之间隔着一道围栏，围栏上张贴着警告标志，提醒人们不要进入，因为热气球降落存在危险。\n\n一位高尔夫球手在球场上击球失误，球落入了该公司场地内。她无视警告标志，翻过围栏去捡球。\n\n几乎与此同时，该公司的一架热气球发生机械故障，不得不紧急降落以避免坠毁。\n\n降落时失控的热气球撞到了这位高尔夫球手，导致她受伤。\n\n当地法院认定热气球飞行是一项异常危险的活动。\n\n如果这位高尔夫球手起诉该公司，该公司是否有任何抗辩理由？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为热气球在撞击高尔夫球手时已经失控。","B":"没有，因为该公司从事的活动本身就属于异常危险的活动。","C":"有，因为热气球是为了避免坠毁而降落的。","D":"是的，因为高尔夫球手进入公司场地即承担了风险。"},"explanationZh":"通常情况下，如果被告从事异常危险活动并导致原告遭受人身伤害，被告需承担严格责任（即无论有无过错均须承担责任）。\n\n然而，针对严格责任索赔，如果原告 (1) 知晓伤害风险，且 (2) 自愿接受该风险，则“自担风险”可作为一项积极抗辩理由。\n\n在本案中，该公司需对高尔夫球手的受伤承担严格责任，因为其热气球（在该司法管辖区被视为异常危险活动）在着陆时撞到了该球手。\n\n但由于该球手无视了关于气球危险性的警示标志（即知晓风险），并进入了该场地（即自愿接受风险），因此该公司可以主张“自担风险”作为积极抗辩理由（选项 B）。\n\n（选项 A）尽管气球在撞击球手时处于失控状态，但该公司已设置标志警告人们注意气球着陆的危险。\n\n由于球手在无视这些警示标志的情况下进入场地，她即承担了因危险或失控气球着陆而受伤害的风险。\n\n（选项 C）由于被告对异常危险活动（如热气球飞行）造成的伤害承担严格责任，因此采取了合理的注意措施并不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n因此，尽管为了避免坠毁而着陆气球可能是合理的做法，但该公司行为的合理性与本案责任认定无关。\n\n教育目标：“自担风险”是针对严格责任的一项积极抗辩理由，前提是原告 (1) 知晓伤害风险，且 (2) 自愿接受该风险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 20 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于异常危险活动）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 523 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于针对异常危险活动的“自担风险”抗辩）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当原告 (1) 明知存在损害风险且 (2) 自愿接受该风险时，风险承担是针对严格责任的积极抗辩。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果高尔夫球手起诉公司，公司是否有任何积极抗辩？","rule":"当原告 (1) 明知存在损害风险且 (2) 自愿接受该风险时，风险承担是针对严格责任的积极抗辩。","analysis":"通常情况下，如果被告从事异常危险活动并导致原告遭受人身伤害，被告需承担严格责任（即无论有无过错均须承担责任）。\n\n然而，针对严格责任索赔，如果原告 (1) 知晓伤害风险，且 (2) 自愿接受该风险，则“自担风险”可作为一项积极抗辩理由。\n\n在本案中，该公司需对高尔夫球手的受伤承担严格责任，因为其热气球（在该司法管辖区被视为异常危险活动）在着陆时撞到了该球手。\n\n但由于该球手无视了关于气球危险性的警示标志（即知晓风险），并进入了该场地（即自愿接受风险），因此该公司可以主张“自担风险”作为积极抗辩理由（选项 B）。\n\n（选项 A）尽管气球在撞击球手时处于失控状态，但该公司已设置标志警告人们注意气球着陆的危险。\n\n由于球手在无视这些警示标志的情况下进入场地，她即承担了因危险或失控气球着陆而受伤害的风险。\n\n（选项 C）由于被告对异常危险活动（如热气球飞行）造成的伤害承担严格责任，因此采取了合理的注意措施并不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n因此，尽管为了避免坠毁而着陆气球可能是合理的做法，但该公司行为的合理性与本案责任认定无关。\n\n教育目标：“自担风险”是针对严格责任的一项积极抗辩理由，前提是原告 (1) 知晓伤害风险，且 (2) 自愿接受该风险。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 20 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于异常危险活动）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二版）》第 523 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（关于针对异常危险活动的“自担风险”抗辩）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为高尔夫球手进入公司场地即承担了风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-58854cdd6a3288f9"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":214,"sourceQuestionPage":642,"sourceQuestionPages":[642],"sourceExplanationPages":[643,644],"sourceAnswerPages":[643,644],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-215","subject":"Torts","topic":"Plaintiff Conduct","question":"A construction company was engaged in blasting operations to clear the way for a new road. The construction company had erected adequate barriers and posted adequate warning signs in the vicinity of the blasting. Although a hiker read and understood the signs, he entered the area to walk his dog. As a result of the blasting, the hiker was hit by a piece of rock and sustained head injuries. The jurisdiction follows the traditional common law rules governing the defenses of contributory negligence, assumption of risk, and last clear chance. In an action by the hiker against the construction company to recover damages for his injuries, will the hiker prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the construction company exercised reasonable care to protect the public from harm.","B":"No, because the hiker understood the signs and disregarded the warnings.","C":"Yes, because the hiker was harmed by the construction company's abnormally dangerous activity.","D":"Yes, unless the hiker failed to use reasonable care to protect himself from harm."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Assumption of the risk\n\n(defense to strict liability)\n\nTraditional contributory Bars recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction him/herself to known risk\n\nPure comparative Reduces recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction* him/herself to known risk Narrow exception: Bars recovery when plaintiff received benefit (eg, financial gain, employment) from his/her exposure to risk * Default rule on MBE.\n\nIndividuals who engage in abnormally dangerous activities (eg, blasting—as seen here) are generally subject to strict liability. To prevail on a strict liability claim, the plaintiff must show that he/she was harmed by the defendant's abnormally dangerous activity. But under the traditional common law rules, assumption of the risk is a complete defense to strict liability when the plaintiff voluntarily confronted a known risk of harm. Here, the construction company erected barriers and warning signs near its blasting operations. The hiker read and understood these signs, so he knew that entering the area was risky. And he voluntarily confronted that risk by disregarding the warnings and entering the area. Therefore, although the hiker was harmed by the company's abnormally dangerous activity, he assumed the risk and cannot prevail in this common law jurisdiction (Choice C). (Choice A) Strict liability claims do not require proof of fault. Therefore, the construction company could be liable even though it exercised reasonable care by erecting adequate barriers and posting adequate warning signs. (Choice D) Contributory negligence is not a defense to strict liability in common law jurisdictions. Therefore, the hiker's mere failure to use reasonable care for his own protection would not bar his recovery. Instead, the hiker's recovery is barred by his voluntary assumption of the risk.\n\nEducational objective: Individuals who engage in abnormally dangerous activities are generally subject to strict liability for any resulting harm. But in traditional common law jurisdictions, assumption of the risk is a complete defense to strict liability if the plaintiff voluntarily confronted a known risk of harm.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 524 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that contributory negligence is not a defense to strict liability unless the plaintiff's negligence amounts to assumption of the risk).","educationalObjective":"Individuals who engage in abnormally dangerous activities are generally subject to strict liability for any resulting harm. But in traditional common law jurisdictions, assumption of the risk is a complete defense to strict liability if the plaintiff voluntarily confronted a known risk of harm.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 524 (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (explaining that contributory negligence is not a defense to strict liability unless the plaintiff's negligence amounts to assumption of the risk).","rule":"Individuals who engage in abnormally dangerous activities are generally subject to strict liability for any resulting harm. But in traditional common law jurisdictions, assumption of the risk is a complete defense to strict liability if the plaintiff voluntarily confronted a known risk of harm.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the hiker against the construction company to recover damages for his injuries, will the hiker prevail?","rule":"Individuals who engage in abnormally dangerous activities are generally subject to strict liability for any resulting harm. But in traditional common law jurisdictions, assumption of the risk is a complete defense to strict liability if the plaintiff voluntarily confronted a known risk of harm.","analysis":"Assumption of the risk\n\n(defense to strict liability)\n\nTraditional contributory Bars recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction him/herself to known risk\n\nPure comparative Reduces recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction* him/herself to known risk Narrow exception: Bars recovery when plaintiff received benefit (eg, financial gain, employment) from his/her exposure to risk * Default rule on MBE.\n\nIndividuals who engage in abnormally dangerous activities (eg, blasting—as seen here) are generally subject to strict liability. To prevail on a strict liability claim, the plaintiff must show that he/she was harmed by the defendant's abnormally dangerous activity. But under the traditional common law rules, assumption of the risk is a complete defense to strict liability when the plaintiff voluntarily confronted a known risk of harm. Here, the construction company erected barriers and warning signs near its blasting operations. The hiker read and understood these signs, so he knew that entering the area was risky. And he voluntarily confronted that risk by disregarding the warnings and entering the area. Therefore, although the hiker was harmed by the company's abnormally dangerous activity, he assumed the risk and cannot prevail in this common law jurisdiction (Choice C). (Choice A) Strict liability claims do not require proof of fault. Therefore, the construction company could be liable even though it exercised reasonable care by erecting adequate barriers and posting adequate warning signs. (Choice D) Contributory negligence is not a defense to strict liability in common law jurisdictions. Therefore, the hiker's mere failure to use reasonable care for his own protection would not bar his recovery. Instead, the hiker's recovery is barred by his voluntary assumption of the risk.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the hiker understood the signs and disregarded the warnings."},"questionZh":"一家建筑公司正在进行爆破作业，以便为修建新道路清理场地。\n\n该公司已在爆破区域附近设置了适当的屏障，并张贴了充分的警示标志。\n\n尽管一名徒步者阅读并理解了这些标志，但他仍进入该区域遛狗。\n\n爆破过程中，该徒步者被一块飞石击中，导致头部受伤。\n\n该司法管辖区适用关于“与有过失（contributory negligence）”（contributory negligence）、“自甘风险”（assumption of risk）和“最后机会原则”（last clear chance）等抗辩理由的传统普通法规则。\n\n在该徒步者针对建筑公司提起的损害赔偿诉讼中，他能否胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为建筑公司已尽到合理注意义务，以保护公众免受伤害。","B":"不能，因为该徒步者理解警示标志的内容，却无视了其中的警告。","C":"能，因为该徒步者是因建筑公司从事“异常危险活动”而受到伤害的。","D":"能，除非该徒步者未能尽到合理注意义务以保护自己免受伤害。"},"explanationZh":"风险自担（Assumption of the risk） （针对严格责任的抗辩） 传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”规则（Contributory negligence）：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则丧失获赔权利。 “纯粹比较过失”规则（Pure comparative negligence）：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则获赔金额会相应减少。\n\n（狭义例外：若原告因承担该风险而获得利益——如经济收益或就业机会——则丧失获赔权利。*注：这是MBE考试中的默认规则。） 从事异常危险活动（如本案中的爆破作业）的人员通常须承担严格责任。\n\n原告若要在严格责任诉讼中胜诉，必须证明其受到了被告异常危险活动的伤害。\n\n但在传统普通法规则下，如果原告自愿面对已知的伤害风险，则“风险自担”构成针对严格责任的完全抗辩。\n\n本案中，建筑公司在爆破作业区附近设置了屏障和警示标志。\n\n徒步旅行者阅读并理解了这些标志，因此他知道进入该区域存在风险。\n\n但他无视警告并进入该区域，从而自愿承担了这一风险。\n\n因此，尽管该徒步旅行者受到了公司异常危险活动的伤害，但他属于“风险自担”，在该普通法司法管辖区内无法胜诉（选项C）。\n\n（选项A）严格责任索赔无需证明过错。\n\n因此，即使建筑公司通过设置充分的屏障和警示标志尽到了合理的注意义务，仍可能需要承担责任。\n\n（选项D）在普通法司法管辖区，与有过失（contributory negligence）并非针对严格责任的抗辩理由。\n\n因此，徒步旅行者仅仅未能采取合理措施保护自己，并不会导致其丧失获赔权利。\n\n相反，导致其无法获赔的原因是他自愿承担了风险。\n\n教育目标：从事异常危险活动的人员通常须对由此造成的任何伤害承担严格责任。\n\n但在传统普通法司法管辖区，如果原告自愿面对已知的伤害风险，则“风险自担”构成针对严格责任的完全抗辩。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第524条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明与有过失（contributory negligence）并非针对严格责任的抗辩，除非原告的过失构成了风险自担）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"从事异常危险活动的个人，通常须对由此造成的任何伤害承担严格责任。\n\n但在传统普通法司法管辖区，如果原告自愿面对已知的伤害风险，则“自甘风险”可作为针对严格责任的完全抗辩理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"在该徒步者针对建筑公司提起的损害赔偿诉讼中，他能否胜诉？","rule":"从事异常危险活动的个人，通常须对由此造成的任何伤害承担严格责任。\n\n但在传统普通法司法管辖区，如果原告自愿面对已知的伤害风险，则“自甘风险”可作为针对严格责任的完全抗辩理由。","analysis":"风险自担（Assumption of the risk） （针对严格责任的抗辩） 传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”规则（Contributory negligence）：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则丧失获赔权利。 “纯粹比较过失”规则（Pure comparative negligence）：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则获赔金额会相应减少。\n\n（狭义例外：若原告因承担该风险而获得利益——如经济收益或就业机会——则丧失获赔权利。*注：这是MBE考试中的默认规则。） 从事异常危险活动（如本案中的爆破作业）的人员通常须承担严格责任。\n\n原告若要在严格责任诉讼中胜诉，必须证明其受到了被告异常危险活动的伤害。\n\n但在传统普通法规则下，如果原告自愿面对已知的伤害风险，则“风险自担”构成针对严格责任的完全抗辩。\n\n本案中，建筑公司在爆破作业区附近设置了屏障和警示标志。\n\n徒步旅行者阅读并理解了这些标志，因此他知道进入该区域存在风险。\n\n但他无视警告并进入该区域，从而自愿承担了这一风险。\n\n因此，尽管该徒步旅行者受到了公司异常危险活动的伤害，但他属于“风险自担”，在该普通法司法管辖区内无法胜诉（选项C）。\n\n（选项A）严格责任索赔无需证明过错。\n\n因此，即使建筑公司通过设置充分的屏障和警示标志尽到了合理的注意义务，仍可能需要承担责任。\n\n（选项D）在普通法司法管辖区，与有过失（contributory negligence）并非针对严格责任的抗辩理由。\n\n因此，徒步旅行者仅仅未能采取合理措施保护自己，并不会导致其丧失获赔权利。\n\n相反，导致其无法获赔的原因是他自愿承担了风险。\n\n教育目标：从事异常危险活动的人员通常须对由此造成的任何伤害承担严格责任。\n\n但在传统普通法司法管辖区，如果原告自愿面对已知的伤害风险，则“风险自担”构成针对严格责任的完全抗辩。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第二次）》第524条（美国法律协会，1965年）（阐明与有过失（contributory negligence）并非针对严格责任的抗辩，除非原告的过失构成了风险自担）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不能，因为该徒步者理解警示标志的内容，却无视了其中的警告。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":215,"sourceQuestionPage":645,"sourceQuestionPages":[645],"sourceExplanationPages":[646,647],"sourceAnswerPages":[646,647],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-216","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A consumer became physically ill after drinking part of a bottle of soda that contained a large decomposed snail. The consumer sued the store from which she had bought the soda to recover damages for her injuries. The parties agreed that the snail had been put into the bottle during the bottling process, over which the store had no control. The parties also agreed that the snail would have been visible in the bottle before the consumer opened it. Will the consumer likely prevail in an action against the store?","choices":{"A":"No, because the consumer could have seen the snail in the bottle before she drank out of it.","B":"No, because the store was not responsible for the bottling process.","C":"Yes, because the consumer was injured by a defective product sold to her by the store.","D":"Yes, because the store had exclusive control over the bottle before selling it to the consumer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Commercial suppliers (eg, manufacturers, distributors, retailers) are strictly liable for harm caused by manufacturing defects in products that they produced or sold. Manufacturing defects include any deviation from a product's intended design that renders it more dangerous than a reasonable consumer would expect—eg, damaged or incorrectly assembled products. Here, the store (commercial supplier) sold a bottle of soda that contained a decomposed snail (deviation from intended design). And since this manufacturing defect caused the consumer to become physically ill (unreasonable danger to consumer's health), the consumer will likely prevail against the store. (Choice A) Under pure comparative fault (default rule), a plaintiff's contributory negligence may reduce his/her recovery of damages but will not absolve the defendant of strict liability. Therefore, the store is strictly liable even though the consumer could have avoided harm by inspecting the bottle before drinking. (Choice B) Every commercial supplier along the chain of distribution—even one that did not create the defect—is subject to strict liability if the defect was present when the product left the supplier's control. Here, although the store did not bottle the soda, it is strictly liable because the bottle contained a snail at the time of sale (when the bottle left the store's control). (Choice D) Exclusive control is relevant to a negligence claim based on res ipsa loquitur. However, strict products liability may be imposed on any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution, regardless of whether it had exclusive control over the product before it reached the consumer. Here, the store did not have exclusive control of the bottle. But even if it did, that control has no effect on the store's strict liability.\n\nEducational objective: A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a manufacturing defect—any deviation from a product's intended design that renders it more dangerous than a reasonable consumer would expect—in a product that it produced or sold.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial supplier).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 2(a) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (manufacturing defect).","educationalObjective":"A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a manufacturing defect—any deviation from a product's intended design that renders it more dangerous than a reasonable consumer would expect—in a product that it produced or sold.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial supplier).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 2(a) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (manufacturing defect).","rule":"A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a manufacturing defect—any deviation from a product's intended design that renders it more dangerous than a reasonable consumer would expect—in a product that it produced or sold.","irac":{"issue":"Will the consumer likely prevail in an action against the store?","rule":"A commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by a manufacturing defect—any deviation from a product's intended design that renders it more dangerous than a reasonable consumer would expect—in a product that it produced or sold.","analysis":"Commercial suppliers (eg, manufacturers, distributors, retailers) are strictly liable for harm caused by manufacturing defects in products that they produced or sold. Manufacturing defects include any deviation from a product's intended design that renders it more dangerous than a reasonable consumer would expect—eg, damaged or incorrectly assembled products. Here, the store (commercial supplier) sold a bottle of soda that contained a decomposed snail (deviation from intended design). And since this manufacturing defect caused the consumer to become physically ill (unreasonable danger to consumer's health), the consumer will likely prevail against the store. (Choice A) Under pure comparative fault (default rule), a plaintiff's contributory negligence may reduce his/her recovery of damages but will not absolve the defendant of strict liability. Therefore, the store is strictly liable even though the consumer could have avoided harm by inspecting the bottle before drinking. (Choice B) Every commercial supplier along the chain of distribution—even one that did not create the defect—is subject to strict liability if the defect was present when the product left the supplier's control. Here, although the store did not bottle the soda, it is strictly liable because the bottle contained a snail at the time of sale (when the bottle left the store's control). (Choice D) Exclusive control is relevant to a negligence claim based on res ipsa loquitur. However, strict products liability may be imposed on any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution, regardless of whether it had exclusive control over the product before it reached the consumer. Here, the store did not have exclusive control of the bottle. But even if it did, that control has no effect on the store's strict liability.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the consumer was injured by a defective product sold to her by the store."},"questionZh":"一名消费者喝了一瓶含有腐烂蜗牛的汽水后感到身体不适。\n\n该消费者起诉了购买汽水的商店，要求赔偿其所受伤害。\n\n双方一致认为，蜗牛是在汽水灌装过程中被放入瓶中的，而商店对此并无控制权。\n\n双方还一致认为，在消费者打开瓶盖之前，蜗牛应该就在瓶中。\n\n该消费者起诉商店的胜诉可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可能，因为消费者在饮用前本可以看到瓶中的蜗牛。","B":"不可能，因为商店对汽水灌装过程不负有责任。","C":"可能，因为消费者因商店出售的缺陷产品而受伤。","D":"可能，因为商店在将汽水出售给消费者之前对瓶子拥有完全的控制权。"},"explanationZh":"商业供应商（如制造商、分销商、零售商）对其生产或销售的产品因制造缺陷造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n制造缺陷包括任何偏离产品预期设计的状况，导致产品危险程度超过理性消费者的预期——例如产品受损或组装错误。\n\n在本案中，商店（商业供应商）销售了一瓶含有腐烂蜗牛的汽水（偏离了预期设计）。\n\n由于该制造缺陷导致消费者身体不适（对消费者健康构成不合理的危险），消费者很可能在针对商店的诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）在纯粹比较过错原则（默认规则）下，原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）可能会减少其获得的损害赔偿额，但不会免除被告的严格责任。\n\n因此，即使消费者本可以通过在饮用前检查瓶子来避免伤害，商店仍需承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 B）分销链上的每一位商业供应商——即使未造成该缺陷——只要产品在离开其控制时已存在该缺陷，均须承担严格责任。\n\n在本案中，尽管商店并未灌装该汽水，但由于销售时（即瓶子离开商店控制时）瓶内已含有蜗牛，商店仍须承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）“排他性控制”与基于“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则”（res ipsa loquitur）的过失索赔有关。\n\n然而，严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）可适用于分销链上的任何商业供应商，无论其在产品到达消费者手中之前是否拥有排他性控制权。\n\n在本案中，商店并未对瓶子拥有排他性控制权。\n\n但即使拥有，这种控制权也不影响商店应承担的严格责任。\n\n教育目标：商业供应商对其生产或销售的产品因制造缺陷（即偏离产品预期设计，导致危险程度超过理性消费者预期的状况）造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第1条（美国法律协会，1998年）（商业供应商的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第2(a)条（美国法律协会，1998年）（制造缺陷）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商业供应商对其生产或销售的产品中因制造缺陷（即产品偏离预期设计，导致其危险性超出合理消费者预期）造成的损害承担严格责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"消费者起诉商店后，胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"商业供应商对其生产或销售的产品中因制造缺陷（即产品偏离预期设计，导致其危险性超出合理消费者预期）造成的损害承担严格责任。","analysis":"商业供应商（如制造商、分销商、零售商）对其生产或销售的产品因制造缺陷造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n制造缺陷包括任何偏离产品预期设计的状况，导致产品危险程度超过理性消费者的预期——例如产品受损或组装错误。\n\n在本案中，商店（商业供应商）销售了一瓶含有腐烂蜗牛的汽水（偏离了预期设计）。\n\n由于该制造缺陷导致消费者身体不适（对消费者健康构成不合理的危险），消费者很可能在针对商店的诉讼中胜诉。\n\n（选项 A）在纯粹比较过错原则（默认规则）下，原告的与有过失（contributory negligence）可能会减少其获得的损害赔偿额，但不会免除被告的严格责任。\n\n因此，即使消费者本可以通过在饮用前检查瓶子来避免伤害，商店仍需承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 B）分销链上的每一位商业供应商——即使未造成该缺陷——只要产品在离开其控制时已存在该缺陷，均须承担严格责任。\n\n在本案中，尽管商店并未灌装该汽水，但由于销售时（即瓶子离开商店控制时）瓶内已含有蜗牛，商店仍须承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）“排他性控制”与基于“事实自证原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则（res ipsa loquitur）原则”（res ipsa loquitur）的过失索赔有关。\n\n然而，严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）可适用于分销链上的任何商业供应商，无论其在产品到达消费者手中之前是否拥有排他性控制权。\n\n在本案中，商店并未对瓶子拥有排他性控制权。\n\n但即使拥有，这种控制权也不影响商店应承担的严格责任。\n\n教育目标：商业供应商对其生产或销售的产品因制造缺陷（即偏离产品预期设计，导致危险程度超过理性消费者预期的状况）造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第1条（美国法律协会，1998年）（商业供应商的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第2(a)条（美国法律协会，1998年）（制造缺陷）。","conclusion":"答案 C：可能，因为消费者因商店出售的缺陷产品而受伤。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-80dfcc00952a9913"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":216,"sourceQuestionPage":648,"sourceQuestionPages":[648],"sourceExplanationPages":[649,650],"sourceAnswerPages":[649,650],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-217","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A cell phone manufacturer purchased batteries from a battery manufacturer for use in its cell phones. The batteries were defectively designed. The cell phone manufacturer was aware of the defect in the batteries but nevertheless sold the cell phones directly to consumers without any warning regarding this problem. The defect caused the cell phones to function only when plugged into a charger, greatly diminishing their use and value. However, no consumer was injured, and no property damage was sustained, as a result of the defective batteries. Consumers whose cell phones were adversely affected by the defect in the batteries brought a class action lawsuit against the cell phone manufacturer based in negligence. The consumers now seek replacement of their cell phone batteries. Assuming that the consumers can clear the procedural hurdles for bringing a class action lawsuit, are they likely to succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the batteries are defective component parts and, therefore, the battery manufacturer is the liable party.","B":"No, because the consumers cannot recover for a purely economic loss.","C":"Yes, because the batteries were defective.","D":"Yes, because the cell phone manufacturer failed to warn the consumers about the defective batteries despite being aware of the defect."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Products liability for a defective product can arise under several theories, including negligence. Under a negligence theory, the plaintiff can recover damages resulting from any personal injury or damage to property other than the defective product itself. However, a claim for purely economic loss is not allowed. In this context, purely economic loss includes any damage to the defective product itself.* Here, the consumers sued the cell phone manufacturer in negligence after the manufacturer sold them cell phones with defectively designed batteries. The defectively designed batteries adversely affected the cell phones themselves but caused no harm to persons or other property—eg, had they caused the cell phones to explode and destroy surrounding property. Therefore, the consumers are unlikely to succeed because they are seeking to recover damages for an unrecoverable, purely economic loss. *Other common examples of purely economic loss include lost profits, the diminished value of the defective product, and the cost of repairing the defective product. (Choice A) Although the battery manufacturer is liable for the defectively designed batteries, this does not preclude the consumers from suing the cell phone manufacturer that sold them the phones. That is because anyone who supplies a defective product to another owes a duty of care. (Choices C & D) The cell phone manufacturer was aware that the batteries were defective and nevertheless used them in its cell phones without warning consumers about the defect. While this conduct may show that the cell phone manufacturer was at least negligent (ie, failed to use reasonable care), the consumers cannot recover for harm to the cell phones alone through a negligence action.** **However, the consumers may be able to bring a contracts-based claim for breach of warranty to recover for this purely economic loss.\n\nEducational objective: In a products liability action based on negligence, the plaintiff can recover damages stemming from personal injury or damage to property other than the defective product itself. Claims for purely economic loss, which includes any damage to the defective product itself, are not allowed.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 4 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (claims for property damage).","educationalObjective":"In a products liability action based on negligence, the plaintiff can recover damages stemming from personal injury or damage to property other than the defective product itself. Claims for purely economic loss, which includes any damage to the defective product itself, are not allowed.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 4 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (claims for property damage).","rule":"In a products liability action based on negligence, the plaintiff can recover damages stemming from personal injury or damage to property other than the defective product itself. Claims for purely economic loss, which includes any damage to the defective product itself, are not allowed.","irac":{"issue":"Assuming that the consumers can clear the procedural hurdles for bringing a class action lawsuit, are they likely to succeed?","rule":"In a products liability action based on negligence, the plaintiff can recover damages stemming from personal injury or damage to property other than the defective product itself. Claims for purely economic loss, which includes any damage to the defective product itself, are not allowed.","analysis":"Products liability for a defective product can arise under several theories, including negligence. Under a negligence theory, the plaintiff can recover damages resulting from any personal injury or damage to property other than the defective product itself. However, a claim for purely economic loss is not allowed. In this context, purely economic loss includes any damage to the defective product itself.* Here, the consumers sued the cell phone manufacturer in negligence after the manufacturer sold them cell phones with defectively designed batteries. The defectively designed batteries adversely affected the cell phones themselves but caused no harm to persons or other property—eg, had they caused the cell phones to explode and destroy surrounding property. Therefore, the consumers are unlikely to succeed because they are seeking to recover damages for an unrecoverable, purely economic loss. *Other common examples of purely economic loss include lost profits, the diminished value of the defective product, and the cost of repairing the defective product. (Choice A) Although the battery manufacturer is liable for the defectively designed batteries, this does not preclude the consumers from suing the cell phone manufacturer that sold them the phones. That is because anyone who supplies a defective product to another owes a duty of care. (Choices C & D) The cell phone manufacturer was aware that the batteries were defective and nevertheless used them in its cell phones without warning consumers about the defect. While this conduct may show that the cell phone manufacturer was at least negligent (ie, failed to use reasonable care), the consumers cannot recover for harm to the cell phones alone through a negligence action.** **However, the consumers may be able to bring a contracts-based claim for breach of warranty to recover for this purely economic loss.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the consumers cannot recover for a purely economic loss."},"questionZh":"一家手机制造商从电池制造商处购入电池，用于其生产的手机。\n\n这些电池存在设计缺陷。\n\n手机制造商明知电池存在缺陷，却未就此问题发出任何警告，直接将手机销售给了消费者。\n\n该缺陷导致手机仅在连接充电器时才能正常工作，从而极大地降低了其使用效能与价值。\n\n然而，这些有缺陷的电池并未造成任何人员受伤或财产损失。\n\n受电池缺陷影响的消费者基于过失侵权理论，对手机制造商提起了集体诉讼。\n\n消费者目前要求更换手机电池。\n\n假设消费者能够克服提起集体诉讼的程序性障碍，他们胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会胜诉，因为电池属于有缺陷的零部件，因此应由电池制造商承担责任。","B":"不会胜诉，因为消费者无法就纯粹的经济损失获得赔偿。","C":"会胜诉，因为电池存在缺陷。","D":"会胜诉，因为手机制造商明知电池存在缺陷，却未向消费者发出警告。"},"explanationZh":"因缺陷产品引发的产品责任诉讼可基于多种法理（诉因）提起，其中包括过失（negligence）。\n\n在过失法理下，原告可就人身伤害或缺陷产品本身以外的财产损失获得赔偿；然而，针对纯粹经济损失（purely economic loss）的索赔不予支持。\n\n在此语境下，纯粹经济损失包括对缺陷产品本身造成的任何损害。* 本案中，消费者因手机制造商销售了电池设计有缺陷的手机而以过失为由提起诉讼。\n\n这些设计有缺陷的电池对手机本身造成了不利影响，但未对人员或其他财产造成损害（例如，未导致手机爆炸并损毁周边财产）。\n\n因此，消费者胜诉的可能性不大，因为他们寻求赔偿的是一种不可获赔的纯粹经济损失。\n\n* 纯粹经济损失的其他常见例子包括利润损失、缺陷产品价值贬损以及维修缺陷产品的费用。\n\n（选项 A）尽管电池制造商应对设计有缺陷的电池承担责任，但这并不妨碍消费者起诉向其销售手机的手机制造商。\n\n这是因为任何向他人提供缺陷产品的主体都负有注意义务。\n\n（选项 C 和 D）手机制造商明知电池存在缺陷，却仍将其用于手机生产，且未就该缺陷向消费者发出警告。\n\n虽然这种行为可能表明手机制造商至少存在过失（即未尽到合理的注意义务），但消费者无法仅凭过失之诉就手机本身的损害获得赔偿。** **不过，消费者或许可以基于合同法理提起违反保证（breach of warranty）的诉讼，以就此纯粹经济损失获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：在基于过失的产品责任诉讼中，原告可就人身伤害或缺陷产品本身以外的财产损害获得赔偿。\n\n针对纯粹经济损失（包括对缺陷产品本身造成的任何损害）的索赔不予支持。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第 4 条注释 e (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于财产损害的索赔）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在基于过失的產品責任訴訟中，原告可就人身伤害或缺陷产品本身以外的财产损失获得赔偿。\n\n针对纯粹经济损失（包括对缺陷产品本身的任何损害）的索赔不予支持。","iracZh":{"issue":"假设消费者能够克服提起集体诉讼的程序性障碍，他们胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"在基于过失的產品責任訴訟中，原告可就人身伤害或缺陷产品本身以外的财产损失获得赔偿。\n\n针对纯粹经济损失（包括对缺陷产品本身的任何损害）的索赔不予支持。","analysis":"因缺陷产品引发的产品责任诉讼可基于多种法理（诉因）提起，其中包括过失（negligence）。\n\n在过失法理下，原告可就人身伤害或缺陷产品本身以外的财产损失获得赔偿；然而，针对纯粹经济损失（purely economic loss）的索赔不予支持。\n\n在此语境下，纯粹经济损失包括对缺陷产品本身造成的任何损害。* 本案中，消费者因手机制造商销售了电池设计有缺陷的手机而以过失为由提起诉讼。\n\n这些设计有缺陷的电池对手机本身造成了不利影响，但未对人员或其他财产造成损害（例如，未导致手机爆炸并损毁周边财产）。\n\n因此，消费者胜诉的可能性不大，因为他们寻求赔偿的是一种不可获赔的纯粹经济损失。\n\n* 纯粹经济损失的其他常见例子包括利润损失、缺陷产品价值贬损以及维修缺陷产品的费用。\n\n（选项 A）尽管电池制造商应对设计有缺陷的电池承担责任，但这并不妨碍消费者起诉向其销售手机的手机制造商。\n\n这是因为任何向他人提供缺陷产品的主体都负有注意义务。\n\n（选项 C 和 D）手机制造商明知电池存在缺陷，却仍将其用于手机生产，且未就该缺陷向消费者发出警告。\n\n虽然这种行为可能表明手机制造商至少存在过失（即未尽到合理的注意义务），但消费者无法仅凭过失之诉就手机本身的损害获得赔偿。** **不过，消费者或许可以基于合同法理提起违反保证（breach of warranty）的诉讼，以就此纯粹经济损失获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：在基于过失的产品责任诉讼中，原告可就人身伤害或缺陷产品本身以外的财产损害获得赔偿。\n\n针对纯粹经济损失（包括对缺陷产品本身造成的任何损害）的索赔不予支持。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第 4 条注释 e (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于财产损害的索赔）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会胜诉，因为消费者无法就纯粹的经济损失获得赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-62019772e0bdbfce"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":217,"sourceQuestionPage":651,"sourceQuestionPages":[651],"sourceExplanationPages":[652,653],"sourceAnswerPages":[652,653],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-218","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A homeowner was using a six-foot stepladder to clean the furnace in his home. The homeowner broke his arm when he slipped and fell from the ladder. The furnace had no warnings or instructions on how it was to be cleaned. In a suit by the homeowner against the manufacturer of the furnace to recover for his injury, is the homeowner likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the danger of falling from a ladder is obvious.","B":"No, because the homeowner should have hired a professional to clean the furnace.","C":"Yes, because the furnace did not have a ladder attached to it for cleaning purposes.","D":"Yes, because the lack of warnings or instructions for how to clean the furnace made the furnace defective."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under products liability law, a commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by its defective product. A product is defective due to inadequate warnings or instructions when: the product poses a foreseeable risk of harm (eg, from improper maintenance) and reasonable instructions or warnings by the commercial supplier could have reduced that risk. However, a product is not defective for failing to warn or instruct about obvious risks (eg, the risk of cutting oneself with a sharp knife). Here, although it may have been foreseeable that a homeowner would use a stepladder to clean the furnace, there is nothing about a home furnace that adds to the obvious dangers associated with working on a stepladder. And since the risk of falling from a stepladder is obvious, the furnace was not defective in failing to provide warnings or instructions about that risk (Choice D). Therefore, the homeowner is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) If cleaning the home furnace was so dangerous as to require a professional, then the manufacturer would be strictly liable for failing to provide a warning or instruction about that danger. (Choice C) A manufacturer is strictly liable to a plaintiff harmed by its defectively designed product—a product that poses a risk of harm that could have been mitigated by a reasonable alternative design. Here, a stepladder is a common household tool, and there is no indication that attaching a ladder to the furnace would have reduced the homeowner's risk of falling (no design defect), so the manufacturer is not liable for failing to attach a ladder.\n\nEducational objective: If a product poses a foreseeable risk of physical harm, the manufacturer must mitigate that risk by providing adequate warnings or instructions. However, a manufacturer is not required to provide warnings or instructions about obvious risks.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(c) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability for failure to warn about a product's risks).","educationalObjective":"If a product poses a foreseeable risk of physical harm, the manufacturer must mitigate that risk by providing adequate warnings or instructions. However, a manufacturer is not required to provide warnings or instructions about obvious risks.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(c) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability for failure to warn about a product's risks).","rule":"If a product poses a foreseeable risk of physical harm, the manufacturer must mitigate that risk by providing adequate warnings or instructions. However, a manufacturer is not required to provide warnings or instructions about obvious risks.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit by the homeowner against the manufacturer of the furnace to recover for his injury, is the homeowner likely to prevail?","rule":"If a product poses a foreseeable risk of physical harm, the manufacturer must mitigate that risk by providing adequate warnings or instructions. However, a manufacturer is not required to provide warnings or instructions about obvious risks.","analysis":"Under products liability law, a commercial supplier is strictly liable for harm caused by its defective product. A product is defective due to inadequate warnings or instructions when: the product poses a foreseeable risk of harm (eg, from improper maintenance) and reasonable instructions or warnings by the commercial supplier could have reduced that risk. However, a product is not defective for failing to warn or instruct about obvious risks (eg, the risk of cutting oneself with a sharp knife). Here, although it may have been foreseeable that a homeowner would use a stepladder to clean the furnace, there is nothing about a home furnace that adds to the obvious dangers associated with working on a stepladder. And since the risk of falling from a stepladder is obvious, the furnace was not defective in failing to provide warnings or instructions about that risk (Choice D). Therefore, the homeowner is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) If cleaning the home furnace was so dangerous as to require a professional, then the manufacturer would be strictly liable for failing to provide a warning or instruction about that danger. (Choice C) A manufacturer is strictly liable to a plaintiff harmed by its defectively designed product—a product that poses a risk of harm that could have been mitigated by a reasonable alternative design. Here, a stepladder is a common household tool, and there is no indication that attaching a ladder to the furnace would have reduced the homeowner's risk of falling (no design defect), so the manufacturer is not liable for failing to attach a ladder.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the danger of falling from a ladder is obvious."},"questionZh":"一位房主使用六英尺高的梯子清洁家里的暖气炉。\n\n他不慎从梯子上滑落，摔断了胳膊。\n\n暖气炉上没有任何关于如何清洁的警告或说明。\n\n如果房主起诉暖气炉制造商，要求赔偿其受伤损失，房主胜诉的可能性有多大？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为从梯子上摔落的危险显而易见。","B":"否，因为房主应该聘请专业人员来清洁暖气炉。","C":"是，因为暖气炉本身并没有配备用于清洁的梯子。","D":"是，因为缺少关于如何清洁暖气炉的警告或说明，导致暖气炉存在缺陷。"},"explanationZh":"根据产品责任法，商业供应商对其缺陷产品造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n如果产品因警告或说明不足而被视为有缺陷，通常是因为：该产品存在可预见的伤害风险（例如，因维护不当引起的风险），而商业供应商提供的合理说明或警告本可以降低该风险。\n\n然而，如果未就明显风险（例如，被锋利刀具割伤的风险）发出警告或说明，产品并不因此被视为有缺陷。\n\n在本案中，尽管房主使用梯子清洁供暖炉的情况可能是可预见的，但家用供暖炉本身并不增加使用梯子作业时固有的明显危险。\n\n鉴于从梯子上跌落的风险是显而易见的，供暖炉未就该风险提供警告或说明并不构成产品缺陷（选项 D）。\n\n因此，房主胜诉的可能性不大。\n\n（选项 B）如果清洁家用供暖炉的危险性极高，以至于需要专业人员操作，那么制造商若未就该危险提供警告或说明，则需承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 C）对于因设计有缺陷的产品（即存在可通过合理的替代设计来降低伤害风险的产品）而受到伤害的原告，制造商需承担严格责任。\n\n在本案中，梯子是一种常见的家用工具，且没有迹象表明在供暖炉上加装梯子能降低房主跌落的风险（不存在设计缺陷），因此制造商无需因未加装梯子而承担责任。\n\n教育目标：如果产品存在可预见的人身伤害风险，制造商必须通过提供充分的警告或说明来降低该风险。\n\n然而，制造商无需就明显风险提供警告或说明。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 2(c) 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于未就产品风险发出警告的责任规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果产品存在可预见的身体伤害风险，制造商必须通过提供充分的警告或说明来降低该风险。\n\n但是，制造商没有义务就显而易见的风险提供警告或说明。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果房主起诉炉子制造商，要求赔偿其所受伤害，房主胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"如果产品存在可预见的身体伤害风险，制造商必须通过提供充分的警告或说明来降低该风险。\n\n但是，制造商没有义务就显而易见的风险提供警告或说明。","analysis":"根据产品责任法，商业供应商对其缺陷产品造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n如果产品因警告或说明不足而被视为有缺陷，通常是因为：该产品存在可预见的伤害风险（例如，因维护不当引起的风险），而商业供应商提供的合理说明或警告本可以降低该风险。\n\n然而，如果未就明显风险（例如，被锋利刀具割伤的风险）发出警告或说明，产品并不因此被视为有缺陷。\n\n在本案中，尽管房主使用梯子清洁供暖炉的情况可能是可预见的，但家用供暖炉本身并不增加使用梯子作业时固有的明显危险。\n\n鉴于从梯子上跌落的风险是显而易见的，供暖炉未就该风险提供警告或说明并不构成产品缺陷（选项 D）。\n\n因此，房主胜诉的可能性不大。\n\n（选项 B）如果清洁家用供暖炉的危险性极高，以至于需要专业人员操作，那么制造商若未就该危险提供警告或说明，则需承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 C）对于因设计有缺陷的产品（即存在可通过合理的替代设计来降低伤害风险的产品）而受到伤害的原告，制造商需承担严格责任。\n\n在本案中，梯子是一种常见的家用工具，且没有迹象表明在供暖炉上加装梯子能降低房主跌落的风险（不存在设计缺陷），因此制造商无需因未加装梯子而承担责任。\n\n教育目标：如果产品存在可预见的人身伤害风险，制造商必须通过提供充分的警告或说明来降低该风险。\n\n然而，制造商无需就明显风险提供警告或说明。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 2(c) 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于未就产品风险发出警告的责任规定）。","conclusion":"答案 A：否，因为从梯子上摔落的危险显而易见。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-e1978dbafb8eefd0"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":218,"sourceQuestionPage":654,"sourceQuestionPages":[654],"sourceExplanationPages":[655,656],"sourceAnswerPages":[655,656],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-219","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A pop star hired a seamstress to sew several leather outfits for his upcoming musical tour. The seamstress needed a new sewing machine for the project, so she went to a retail store that specialized in sewing machines. While she was browsing, a sales associate approached her. Although the sales associate did not know the particular purpose for which the seamstress was buying a sewing machine, he told her to buy a specific model, stating, \"This model is the best one on the market!\" Relying upon his opinion, the seamstress purchased the sewing machine. However, when the seamstress tried to sew the pieces of the leather outfits together, the sewing machine could not sew through the thick leather. As a result, the seamstress was unable to make the leather outfits in time for the pop star's musical tour, and she lost thousands of dollars in profit. The seamstress brought a products liability action against the retail store, alleging a breach of the implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose. For whom will the court likely decide?","choices":{"A":"The retail store, because the sales associate did not know the particular purpose for which the sewing machine was being purchased.","B":"The retail store, because the seamstress cannot recover for purely economic loss.","C":"The seamstress, because she relied on the sales associate's opinion when purchasing the sewing machine.","D":"The seamstress, because the sewing machine was not generally acceptable or reasonably fit for the purpose for which it had been sold."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Warranties for products liability actions\n\nImplied Merchantability Product is generally acceptable & reasonably fit for its ordinary purpose—if seller is merchant\n\nFitness for Product is fit for particular purpose—if seller has reason particular purpose to know purchaser is relying on seller's skill or judgment to furnish suitable product\n\nExpress Any affirmation of fact or promise made by seller regarding product that is part of basis of bargain\n\nA buyer may bring a products liability action against a seller of goods for breach of certain warranties, including the implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose. This implied warranty guarantees that a product is fit for a particular purpose, but only if: the seller knows the particular purpose for which the product is being purchased and the buyer relies on the seller's judgment or skill in selecting a product suitable for that purpose. The warranty is breached if a product fails to live up to the standards imposed by this warranty, and the buyer need not prove that the seller was at fault. The buyer can then recover damages for personal injury or property damage, as well as for purely economic loss (Choice B).* Here, the sales associate did not know the particular purpose for which the sewing machine was being purchased (ie, to sew leather), so there was no implied warranty that the sewing machine was fit for that purpose. This is true even though the seamstress relied on the sales associate's opinion when purchasing the sewing machine (Choice C). Therefore, the sewing machine's inability to sew through leather was not a breach of this warranty, and the court will likely decide for the retail store. *In most states, an injured party other than the buyer cannot recover for property damage or purely economic loss arising from a breach of warranty. (Choice D) The implied warranty of merchantability guarantees that a product is acceptable and reasonably fit for the ordinary purpose for which it is being sold—eg, a sewing machine can sew through ordinary fabrics. But here, the seamstress's claim is based solely on the implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose.\n\nEducational objective: The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose applies when (1) the seller knows the particular purpose for which the product is being purchased and (2) the buyer relies on\n\nthe seller's judgment or skill in selecting a product suitable for that purpose. Breach of this warranty can give rise to a products liability claim.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-315 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose).","educationalObjective":"The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose applies when (1) the seller knows the particular purpose for which the product is being purchased and (2) the buyer relies on\n\nthe seller's judgment or skill in selecting a product suitable for that purpose. Breach of this warranty can give rise to a products liability claim.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-315 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose).","rule":"The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose applies when (1) the seller knows the particular purpose for which the product is being purchased and (2) the buyer relies on\n\nthe seller's judgment or skill in selecting a product suitable for that purpose. Breach of this warranty can give rise to a products liability claim.","irac":{"issue":"For whom will the court likely decide?","rule":"The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose applies when (1) the seller knows the particular purpose for which the product is being purchased and (2) the buyer relies on\n\nthe seller's judgment or skill in selecting a product suitable for that purpose. Breach of this warranty can give rise to a products liability claim.","analysis":"Warranties for products liability actions\n\nImplied Merchantability Product is generally acceptable & reasonably fit for its ordinary purpose—if seller is merchant\n\nFitness for Product is fit for particular purpose—if seller has reason particular purpose to know purchaser is relying on seller's skill or judgment to furnish suitable product\n\nExpress Any affirmation of fact or promise made by seller regarding product that is part of basis of bargain\n\nA buyer may bring a products liability action against a seller of goods for breach of certain warranties, including the implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose. This implied warranty guarantees that a product is fit for a particular purpose, but only if: the seller knows the particular purpose for which the product is being purchased and the buyer relies on the seller's judgment or skill in selecting a product suitable for that purpose. The warranty is breached if a product fails to live up to the standards imposed by this warranty, and the buyer need not prove that the seller was at fault. The buyer can then recover damages for personal injury or property damage, as well as for purely economic loss (Choice B).* Here, the sales associate did not know the particular purpose for which the sewing machine was being purchased (ie, to sew leather), so there was no implied warranty that the sewing machine was fit for that purpose. This is true even though the seamstress relied on the sales associate's opinion when purchasing the sewing machine (Choice C). Therefore, the sewing machine's inability to sew through leather was not a breach of this warranty, and the court will likely decide for the retail store. *In most states, an injured party other than the buyer cannot recover for property damage or purely economic loss arising from a breach of warranty. (Choice D) The implied warranty of merchantability guarantees that a product is acceptable and reasonably fit for the ordinary purpose for which it is being sold—eg, a sewing machine can sew through ordinary fabrics. But here, the seamstress's claim is based solely on the implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose.","conclusion":"Answer A: The retail store, because the sales associate did not know the particular purpose for which the sewing machine was being purchased."},"questionZh":"一位流行歌星聘请了一位裁缝，为他即将到来的巡回演唱会制作几套皮质演出服。\n\n裁缝需要一台新缝纫机来完成这项工作，于是她去了一家专门销售缝纫机的零售店。\n\n在选购过程中，一位销售人员主动上前接待了她。\n\n尽管该销售人员并不了解裁缝购买缝纫机的具体用途，但他仍向她推荐了某款特定型号，并声称：“这是市面上最好的一款！”裁缝听信了他的建议，买下了这台缝纫机。\n\n然而，当裁缝试图缝合皮衣部件时，发现该缝纫机无法穿透厚实的皮革。\n\n结果，裁缝没能赶在流行歌星巡回演唱会开始前制作好皮衣，并因此损失了数千美元的利润。\n\n裁缝针对该零售店提起了产品责任诉讼，主张其违反了“适合特定用途的默示保证”（implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose）。\n\n法院很可能会判决哪一方胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"零售店胜诉，因为销售人员并不知道裁缝购买缝纫机的具体用途。","B":"零售店胜诉，因为裁缝无法就纯粹的经济损失获得赔偿。","C":"裁缝胜诉，因为她在购买缝纫机时依赖了销售人员的意见。","D":"裁缝胜诉，因为该缝纫机在市场上不被普遍接受，或者不适合其销售时预期的用途。"},"explanationZh":"产品责任诉讼中的保证 **适销性默示保证**：若卖方为商人（merchant），则保证产品通常可被接受，且合理适用于其一般用途。 **特定用途适用性保证**：若卖方有理由知道买方依赖卖方的技能或判断来提供合适的产品，则保证产品适用于该特定用途。 **明示保证**：卖方就产品作出的、构成交易基础的事实陈述或承诺。 买方可因卖方违反特定保证（包括特定用途适用性默示保证）而提起产品责任诉讼。\n\n该默示保证确保产品适用于特定用途，但前提是：卖方知晓购买该产品的特定用途，且买方在选择适用于该用途的产品时依赖了卖方的判断或技能。\n\n若产品未能达到该保证所要求的标准，即构成违约，且买方无需证明卖方存在过错。\n\n随后，买方可就人身伤害、财产损失以及纯粹经济损失获得赔偿（选项 B）。* 在本案中，销售人员并不知道购买缝纫机的特定用途（即缝制皮革），因此不存在该缝纫机适用于该用途的默示保证。\n\n即使裁缝在购买缝纫机时依赖了销售人员的意见，情况依然如此（选项 C）。\n\n因此，缝纫机无法缝透皮革并不构成对该保证的违反，法院很可能会判决零售店胜诉。\n\n* 在大多数州，除买方以外的受害方无法就因违反保证而产生的财产损失或纯粹经济损失获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）适销性默示保证确保产品可被接受，并合理适用于其销售时的一般用途——例如，缝纫机能缝透普通织物。\n\n但在此案中，裁缝的主张仅基于特定用途适用性默示保证。\n\n教育目标：特定用途适用性默示保证适用于以下情况：(1) 卖方知晓购买该产品的特定用途；以及 (2) 买方在选择适用于该用途的产品时依赖了 卖方的判断或技能。\n\n违反此项保证可能引发产品责任索赔。\n\n参考文献 《统一商法典》（U.C.C.）第2-315条（美国法律协会与统一州法委员会，2020年）（关于特定用途适用性的默示保证）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“适合特定用途的默示保证”适用于以下情况：(1) 卖方知晓买方购买产品的具体用途；以及 (2) 买方依赖 卖方在选择适合该用途产品方面的判断或技能。\n\n违反此保证可引发产品责任索赔。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院很可能会判决哪一方胜诉？","rule":"“适合特定用途的默示保证”适用于以下情况：(1) 卖方知晓买方购买产品的具体用途；以及 (2) 买方依赖 卖方在选择适合该用途产品方面的判断或技能。\n\n违反此保证可引发产品责任索赔。","analysis":"产品责任诉讼中的保证 **适销性默示保证**：若卖方为商人（merchant），则保证产品通常可被接受，且合理适用于其一般用途。 **特定用途适用性保证**：若卖方有理由知道买方依赖卖方的技能或判断来提供合适的产品，则保证产品适用于该特定用途。 **明示保证**：卖方就产品作出的、构成交易基础的事实陈述或承诺。 买方可因卖方违反特定保证（包括特定用途适用性默示保证）而提起产品责任诉讼。\n\n该默示保证确保产品适用于特定用途，但前提是：卖方知晓购买该产品的特定用途，且买方在选择适用于该用途的产品时依赖了卖方的判断或技能。\n\n若产品未能达到该保证所要求的标准，即构成违约，且买方无需证明卖方存在过错。\n\n随后，买方可就人身伤害、财产损失以及纯粹经济损失获得赔偿（选项 B）。* 在本案中，销售人员并不知道购买缝纫机的特定用途（即缝制皮革），因此不存在该缝纫机适用于该用途的默示保证。\n\n即使裁缝在购买缝纫机时依赖了销售人员的意见，情况依然如此（选项 C）。\n\n因此，缝纫机无法缝透皮革并不构成对该保证的违反，法院很可能会判决零售店胜诉。\n\n* 在大多数州，除买方以外的受害方无法就因违反保证而产生的财产损失或纯粹经济损失获得赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）适销性默示保证确保产品可被接受，并合理适用于其销售时的一般用途——例如，缝纫机能缝透普通织物。\n\n但在此案中，裁缝的主张仅基于特定用途适用性默示保证。\n\n教育目标：特定用途适用性默示保证适用于以下情况：(1) 卖方知晓购买该产品的特定用途；以及 (2) 买方在选择适用于该用途的产品时依赖了 卖方的判断或技能。\n\n违反此项保证可能引发产品责任索赔。\n\n参考文献 《统一商法典》（U.C.C.）第2-315条（美国法律协会与统一州法委员会，2020年）（关于特定用途适用性的默示保证）。","conclusion":"答案 A：零售店胜诉，因为销售人员并不知道裁缝购买缝纫机的具体用途。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"implied warranty of fitness","ipa":"/ˌɪmplˈaɪd wˈɔɹənti ˈəv fˈɪtnəs/","syllables":"im·plied war·ranty of fit·ness","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"特定用途适用性默示担保：卖方知悉特定用途及买方依赖其判断","contextualMeaning":"特定用途适用性默示担保：卖方知悉特定用途及买方依赖其判断","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The seamstress brought a products liability action against the retail store, alleging a breach of the implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":219,"sourceQuestionPage":657,"sourceQuestionPages":[657],"sourceExplanationPages":[658,659],"sourceAnswerPages":[658,659],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-220","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A mother went to a retail toy store to purchase a birthday gift for her eight-year-old daughter. Without inspecting it, a toy-store employee sold an electric toy oven to the mother. The toy oven could bake small batches of real food using heat generated from light bulbs located in the interior of the oven. The instructions that came with the toy oven clearly stated that adult supervision was required when operating the oven, so the mother helped the daughter use the oven to bake brownies. While the brownies were baking, a six- year-old boy who lived next door came over to play with the daughter. When the brownies were done baking, the mother allowed the boy to open the oven and remove them. As he was doing so, a broken light bulb inside of the oven suddenly caught on fire, causing second- degree burns on the boy's hands. The boy's father subsequently filed a negligence action against the manufacturer of the toy oven. At trial, it was established that had the manufacturer or the toy store exercised reasonable care in the inspection of the toy oven, the broken light bulb would have been discovered. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The boy's father, because the manufacturer breached its duty of reasonable care toward the boy.","B":"The boy's father, because the manufacturer is strictly liable for the toy oven's defect.","C":"The manufacturer, because it was not reasonably foreseeable that the boy would be injured by the daughter's defective toy oven.","D":"The manufacturer, because the toy store's negligent failure to inspect the toy oven before selling it to the mother is a superseding cause of the boy's injuries."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A commercial manufacturer, distributor, retailer, or seller of a product owes a duty of reasonable care to any foreseeable plaintiff (ie, purchaser, user, bystander). Failure to exercise reasonable care in the inspection or sale of a product constitutes a breach of that duty. If the breach causes the plaintiff physical harm (ie, personal injury or property damage), the plaintiff will prevail in a negligence action. Here, the manufacturer owed a duty of reasonable care to the boy as a user of the toy oven because it was foreseeable that other children might play with the daughter's toy (Choice C). Had the manufacturer exercised reasonable care in the inspection of the oven, it would have discovered the broken light bulb and the boy would not have suffered second-degree burns. Therefore, the boy's father will prevail because the manufacturer breached its duty of reasonable care toward the boy and caused his injuries. (Choice B) A strict products liability action requires proof that the product was defective, the defect existed when it left the defendant's control, and the defect caused the plaintiff's injuries. But here, the father filed a negligence action—not a strict products liability action. (Choice D) The toy store's failure to inspect the toy oven before selling it to the mother is not a superseding cause that would relieve the manufacturer of liability for the boy's harm. That is because the toy store's failure to inspect the toy oven was foreseeable.\n\nEducational objective: Commercial manufacturers, distributors, retailers, and sellers of a product owe a duty of reasonable care to any foreseeable plaintiff (ie, purchaser, user, or bystander). The failure to exercise reasonable care in the inspection or sale of a product constitutes a breach of that duty.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (failure to inspect product).","educationalObjective":"Commercial manufacturers, distributors, retailers, and sellers of a product owe a duty of reasonable care to any foreseeable plaintiff (ie, purchaser, user, or bystander). The failure to exercise reasonable care in the inspection or sale of a product constitutes a breach of that duty.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (failure to inspect product).","rule":"Commercial manufacturers, distributors, retailers, and sellers of a product owe a duty of reasonable care to any foreseeable plaintiff (ie, purchaser, user, or bystander). The failure to exercise reasonable care in the inspection or sale of a product constitutes a breach of that duty.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"Commercial manufacturers, distributors, retailers, and sellers of a product owe a duty of reasonable care to any foreseeable plaintiff (ie, purchaser, user, or bystander). The failure to exercise reasonable care in the inspection or sale of a product constitutes a breach of that duty.","analysis":"A commercial manufacturer, distributor, retailer, or seller of a product owes a duty of reasonable care to any foreseeable plaintiff (ie, purchaser, user, bystander). Failure to exercise reasonable care in the inspection or sale of a product constitutes a breach of that duty. If the breach causes the plaintiff physical harm (ie, personal injury or property damage), the plaintiff will prevail in a negligence action. Here, the manufacturer owed a duty of reasonable care to the boy as a user of the toy oven because it was foreseeable that other children might play with the daughter's toy (Choice C). Had the manufacturer exercised reasonable care in the inspection of the oven, it would have discovered the broken light bulb and the boy would not have suffered second-degree burns. Therefore, the boy's father will prevail because the manufacturer breached its duty of reasonable care toward the boy and caused his injuries. (Choice B) A strict products liability action requires proof that the product was defective, the defect existed when it left the defendant's control, and the defect caused the plaintiff's injuries. But here, the father filed a negligence action—not a strict products liability action. (Choice D) The toy store's failure to inspect the toy oven before selling it to the mother is not a superseding cause that would relieve the manufacturer of liability for the boy's harm. That is because the toy store's failure to inspect the toy oven was foreseeable.","conclusion":"Answer A: The boy's father, because the manufacturer breached its duty of reasonable care toward the boy."},"questionZh":"一位母亲去玩具店为她八岁的女儿购买生日礼物。\n\n玩具店员工在未进行检查的情况下，向这位母亲出售了一款电动玩具烤箱。\n\n该玩具烤箱利用内部灯泡产生的热量，可以烘焙少量真正的食物。\n\n烤箱附带的说明书明确指出，操作时需要成人监督，因此母亲协助女儿使用烤箱烘焙布朗尼蛋糕。\n\n在烘焙过程中，邻居家一名六岁的男孩过来找女儿玩。\n\n布朗尼烤好后，母亲允许男孩打开烤箱并将蛋糕取出。\n\n就在他操作时，烤箱内一个破损的灯泡突然起火，导致男孩双手遭受二级烧伤。\n\n随后，男孩的父亲针对玩具烤箱制造商提起了过失诉讼。\n\n庭审中查明，如果制造商或玩具店在检查该玩具烤箱时尽到了合理的注意义务，就能发现那个破损的灯泡。\n\n谁更有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"男孩的父亲，因为制造商违反了对该男孩应尽的合理注意义务。","B":"男孩的父亲，因为制造商应对玩具烤箱的缺陷承担严格责任。","C":"制造商，因为男孩因女儿那个有缺陷的玩具烤箱而受伤，这一情况在合理预见范围内并不存在。","D":"制造商，因为玩具店在向母亲出售玩具烤箱前疏于检查，构成了导致男孩受伤的阻断因果关系的中断原因（superseding cause）。"},"explanationZh":"产品的商业制造商、分销商、零售商或销售者对任何可预见原告（即购买者、使用者或旁观者）负有尽合理注意义务的责任。\n\n在检查或销售产品时未能尽到合理注意义务，即构成对该义务的违反。\n\n如果这种违反行为导致原告遭受身体伤害（即人身伤害或财产损失），原告将在过失诉讼中胜诉。\n\n在本案中，制造商对作为该玩具烤箱使用者的男孩负有尽合理注意义务的责任，因为可以预见其他儿童可能会玩耍那个女孩的玩具（选项 C）。\n\n如果制造商在检查烤箱时尽到了合理注意义务，它本会发现破损的灯泡，男孩也就不会遭受二度烧伤。\n\n因此，男孩的父亲将胜诉，因为制造商违反了对男孩应尽的合理注意义务，并导致了男孩受伤。\n\n（选项 B）严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼要求证明产品存在缺陷、缺陷在产品脱离被告控制时即已存在，且该缺陷导致了原告的伤害。\n\n但本案中，父亲提起的是过失诉讼，而非严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n（选项 D）玩具店在将玩具烤箱出售给母亲之前未对其进行检查，这一行为并不构成足以免除制造商对男孩所受伤害承担责任的“阻断因果关系事件”（superseding cause）。\n\n这是因为玩具店未检查玩具烤箱的情况是可预见的。\n\n教育目标：产品的商业制造商、分销商、零售商和销售者对任何可预见原告（即购买者、使用者或旁观者）负有尽合理注意义务的责任。\n\n在检查或销售产品时未能尽到合理注意义务，即构成对该义务的违反。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 15 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（未检查产品）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"产品的商业制造商、分销商、零售商及销售者，对任何可预见原告（即购买者、使用者或旁观者）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n在产品检查或销售过程中未能尽到合理注意义务，即构成对该义务的违反。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁更有可能胜诉？","rule":"产品的商业制造商、分销商、零售商及销售者，对任何可预见原告（即购买者、使用者或旁观者）均负有合理注意义务。\n\n在产品检查或销售过程中未能尽到合理注意义务，即构成对该义务的违反。","analysis":"产品的商业制造商、分销商、零售商或销售者对任何可预见原告（即购买者、使用者或旁观者）负有尽合理注意义务的责任。\n\n在检查或销售产品时未能尽到合理注意义务，即构成对该义务的违反。\n\n如果这种违反行为导致原告遭受身体伤害（即人身伤害或财产损失），原告将在过失诉讼中胜诉。\n\n在本案中，制造商对作为该玩具烤箱使用者的男孩负有尽合理注意义务的责任，因为可以预见其他儿童可能会玩耍那个女孩的玩具（选项 C）。\n\n如果制造商在检查烤箱时尽到了合理注意义务，它本会发现破损的灯泡，男孩也就不会遭受二度烧伤。\n\n因此，男孩的父亲将胜诉，因为制造商违反了对男孩应尽的合理注意义务，并导致了男孩受伤。\n\n（选项 B）严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼要求证明产品存在缺陷、缺陷在产品脱离被告控制时即已存在，且该缺陷导致了原告的伤害。\n\n但本案中，父亲提起的是过失诉讼，而非严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n（选项 D）玩具店在将玩具烤箱出售给母亲之前未对其进行检查，这一行为并不构成足以免除制造商对男孩所受伤害承担责任的“阻断因果关系事件”（superseding cause）。\n\n这是因为玩具店未检查玩具烤箱的情况是可预见的。\n\n教育目标：产品的商业制造商、分销商、零售商和销售者对任何可预见原告（即购买者、使用者或旁观者）负有尽合理注意义务的责任。\n\n在检查或销售产品时未能尽到合理注意义务，即构成对该义务的违反。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 15 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（未检查产品）。","conclusion":"答案 A：男孩的父亲，因为制造商违反了对该男孩应尽的合理注意义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-62019772e0bdbfce"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":220,"sourceQuestionPage":660,"sourceQuestionPages":[660],"sourceExplanationPages":[661,662],"sourceAnswerPages":[661,662],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-221","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A shop operates an aircraft supply business serving the needs of owners of private airplanes. A pilot contracted with the shop to replace the engine in his plane with a more powerful engine manufactured by an engine company. The shop purchased the replacement engine through a representative of the engine company and sold it to the pilot. A short time after it was put into use, the new engine failed, and the plane crashed into a warehouse owned by a woman, destroying the warehouse and its contents. The shop was guilty of no negligence in the procurement or inspection of the engine. The failure of the engine was caused by a manufacturing defect that would not be disclosed by inspection and testing procedures available to a supplier. There was also no negligence on the part of the pilot in the installation of the engine or the piloting of the plane. The woman recovered a judgment for damages from the shop for the destruction of her warehouse and its contents, and the shop has asserted a claim against the engine company to recover compensation on account of that liability. In that action, what amount of compensation will the shop most likely recover?","choices":{"A":"No compensation, because the shop sold the engine to the pilot.","B":"No compensation, because the woman's judgment established the shop's responsibility to the woman.","C":"Contribution only, because the shop and the engine company were equally innocent.","D":"Full compensation, because the engine was defective."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Any commercial supplier along the chain of distribution may be strictly liable for physical harm caused by a product's manufacturing defect that existed when the product left the supplier's control. However, commercial suppliers who did not negligently, recklessly, or intentionally fail to discover the product defect (ie, no independent culpability) can seek indemnity (ie, full compensation) from commercial suppliers earlier in the chain of distribution. Here, the woman recovered a judgment against the shop (retailer) because it sold the pilot a defective engine that caused his plane to crash into her warehouse (physical harm). But since the shop was not negligent in failing to discover the engine defect, it was not independently culpable. Therefore, the shop can recover full compensation from an earlier commercial supplier—the engine company (manufacturer). (Choice A) Although the shop is subject to strict products liability because it sold the defective engine to the pilot, it can still recover full compensation (indemnity) from the engine company because it was not independently culpable. (Choice B) The judgment establishing the shop's responsibility to the woman does not prevent the shop from recovering full compensation from the engine company since the judgment did not address whether the shop negligently, recklessly, or intentionally failed to discover the engine defect (independent culpability). But if the shop had been found independently culpable, the judgment would have precluded its indemnity claim. (Choice C) Contribution allows one independently culpable commercial seller to recover the proportional share of damages attributable to another independently culpable commercial seller (eg, if a negligent retailer pays 100% of an injured party's claim but is only 60% at fault, it can recover 40% from the negligent manufacturer). But since the shop was not independently culpable, it can recover full compensation (indemnity)—not just a share (contribution).\n\nEducational objective: Although any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution may be subject to strict products liability, suppliers who did not negligently, recklessly, or intentionally fail to discover the product defect (no independent culpability) can recover full compensation (indemnity) from suppliers earlier in the distribution chain.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liab. § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (indemnity).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(a) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (strict products liability for manufacturing defects).","educationalObjective":"Although any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution may be subject to strict products liability, suppliers who did not negligently, recklessly, or intentionally fail to discover the product defect (no independent culpability) can recover full compensation (indemnity) from suppliers earlier in the distribution chain.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liab. § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (indemnity).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 2(a) (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (strict products liability for manufacturing defects).","rule":"Although any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution may be subject to strict products liability, suppliers who did not negligently, recklessly, or intentionally fail to discover the product defect (no independent culpability) can recover full compensation (indemnity) from suppliers earlier in the distribution chain.","irac":{"issue":"In that action, what amount of compensation will the shop most likely recover?","rule":"Although any commercial supplier in the chain of distribution may be subject to strict products liability, suppliers who did not negligently, recklessly, or intentionally fail to discover the product defect (no independent culpability) can recover full compensation (indemnity) from suppliers earlier in the distribution chain.","analysis":"Any commercial supplier along the chain of distribution may be strictly liable for physical harm caused by a product's manufacturing defect that existed when the product left the supplier's control. However, commercial suppliers who did not negligently, recklessly, or intentionally fail to discover the product defect (ie, no independent culpability) can seek indemnity (ie, full compensation) from commercial suppliers earlier in the chain of distribution. Here, the woman recovered a judgment against the shop (retailer) because it sold the pilot a defective engine that caused his plane to crash into her warehouse (physical harm). But since the shop was not negligent in failing to discover the engine defect, it was not independently culpable. Therefore, the shop can recover full compensation from an earlier commercial supplier—the engine company (manufacturer). (Choice A) Although the shop is subject to strict products liability because it sold the defective engine to the pilot, it can still recover full compensation (indemnity) from the engine company because it was not independently culpable. (Choice B) The judgment establishing the shop's responsibility to the woman does not prevent the shop from recovering full compensation from the engine company since the judgment did not address whether the shop negligently, recklessly, or intentionally failed to discover the engine defect (independent culpability). But if the shop had been found independently culpable, the judgment would have precluded its indemnity claim. (Choice C) Contribution allows one independently culpable commercial seller to recover the proportional share of damages attributable to another independently culpable commercial seller (eg, if a negligent retailer pays 100% of an injured party's claim but is only 60% at fault, it can recover 40% from the negligent manufacturer). But since the shop was not independently culpable, it can recover full compensation (indemnity)—not just a share (contribution).","conclusion":"Answer D: Full compensation, because the engine was defective."},"questionZh":"某商店经营航空用品业务，服务于私人飞机机主。\n\n一位飞行员与该商店签订合同，将其飞机上的发动机更换为由某发动机公司制造的动力更强的发动机。\n\n商店通过该发动机公司的代表购入替换发动机，并将其出售给飞行员。\n\n新发动机投入使用后不久便发生故障，导致飞机撞向一位女士拥有的仓库，致使仓库及其内部物品损毁。\n\n商店在采购或检查该发动机的过程中并无过失。\n\n发动机故障是由制造缺陷引起的，而供应商可用的检查和测试程序无法发现该缺陷。\n\n飞行员在安装发动机或驾驶飞机的过程中也不存在过失。\n\n该女士就其仓库及内部物品的损毁获得了针对商店的损害赔偿判决，商店随后向发动机公司提出索赔，要求就该项责任获得补偿。\n\n在该诉讼中，商店最可能获得多少赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"无法获得赔偿，因为商店将发动机出售给了飞行员。","B":"无法获得赔偿，因为该女士获得的判决确立了商店对该女士的责任。","C":"仅能获得分摊赔偿（contribution），因为商店和发动机公司均无过错。","D":"获得全额赔偿，因为发动机存在缺陷。"},"explanationZh":"在分销链上的任何商业供应商，若产品在离开其控制时已存在制造缺陷，并由此造成人身伤害或财产损失（即“人身伤害”），则该供应商可能需承担严格的产品责任。\n\n然而，对于那些并非因疏忽、鲁莽或故意而未能发现产品缺陷的商业供应商（即无独立过错），他们可以向分销链上游的商业供应商寻求赔偿（即全额补偿）。\n\n在本案中，该女子针对商店（零售商）胜诉并获得判决，因为该商店向飞行员出售了有缺陷的发动机，导致飞机撞向她的仓库（造成了人身伤害）。\n\n但由于该商店在未能发现发动机缺陷方面并无疏忽，因此它不承担独立过错责任。\n\n据此，该商店可以从上游商业供应商——即发动机公司（制造商）——处获得全额补偿。\n\n（选项 A）尽管该商店因向飞行员出售有缺陷的发动机而须承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），但由于其自身无独立过错，它仍可从发动机公司获得全额补偿（赔偿）。\n\n（选项 B）确立该商店对该女子负有责任的判决，并不妨碍该商店向发动机公司追讨全额补偿，因为该判决并未涉及该商店是否存在疏忽、鲁莽或故意未能发现发动机缺陷的情形（即独立过错问题）。\n\n但如果认定该商店负有独立过错，该判决将排除其获得赔偿的权利。\n\n（选项 C）分担责任（contribution）允许一个负有独立过错的商业销售商，向另一个负有独立过错的商业销售商追讨其应承担的损害赔偿份额（例如，若一家有过失的零售商支付了受害方索赔额的 100%，但其过错责任仅占 60%，则它可以向同样有过失的制造商追讨 40% 的款项）。\n\n但由于该商店并无独立过错，它可以获得全额补偿（赔偿），而不仅仅是部分份额（分担责任）。\n\n教育目标：尽管分销链上的任何商业供应商都可能面临严格的产品责任，但对于那些并非因疏忽、鲁莽或故意而未能发现产品缺陷的供应商（无独立过错），他们可以向分销链上游的供应商追讨全额补偿（赔偿）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任分担》第 22 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（关于赔偿/indemnity 的规定）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第1条、第2(a)条（美国法律协会，1998年）（关于制造缺陷的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"尽管流通链条中的任何商业供应商都可能承担严格的产品责任，但对于那些并非因疏忽、鲁莽或故意而未能发现产品缺陷的供应商（即无独立过错），他们可以向流通链条中更上游的供应商追索全额赔偿（即获得补偿/追偿权）。","iracZh":{"issue":"在该诉讼中，商店最可能获得多少赔偿？","rule":"尽管流通链条中的任何商业供应商都可能承担严格的产品责任，但对于那些并非因疏忽、鲁莽或故意而未能发现产品缺陷的供应商（即无独立过错），他们可以向流通链条中更上游的供应商追索全额赔偿（即获得补偿/追偿权）。","analysis":"在分销链上的任何商业供应商，若产品在离开其控制时已存在制造缺陷，并由此造成人身伤害或财产损失（即“人身伤害”），则该供应商可能需承担严格的产品责任。\n\n然而，对于那些并非因疏忽、鲁莽或故意而未能发现产品缺陷的商业供应商（即无独立过错），他们可以向分销链上游的商业供应商寻求赔偿（即全额补偿）。\n\n在本案中，该女子针对商店（零售商）胜诉并获得判决，因为该商店向飞行员出售了有缺陷的发动机，导致飞机撞向她的仓库（造成了人身伤害）。\n\n但由于该商店在未能发现发动机缺陷方面并无疏忽，因此它不承担独立过错责任。\n\n据此，该商店可以从上游商业供应商——即发动机公司（制造商）——处获得全额补偿。\n\n（选项 A）尽管该商店因向飞行员出售有缺陷的发动机而须承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability），但由于其自身无独立过错，它仍可从发动机公司获得全额补偿（赔偿）。\n\n（选项 B）确立该商店对该女子负有责任的判决，并不妨碍该商店向发动机公司追讨全额补偿，因为该判决并未涉及该商店是否存在疏忽、鲁莽或故意未能发现发动机缺陷的情形（即独立过错问题）。\n\n但如果认定该商店负有独立过错，该判决将排除其获得赔偿的权利。\n\n（选项 C）分担责任（contribution）允许一个负有独立过错的商业销售商，向另一个负有独立过错的商业销售商追讨其应承担的损害赔偿份额（例如，若一家有过失的零售商支付了受害方索赔额的 100%，但其过错责任仅占 60%，则它可以向同样有过失的制造商追讨 40% 的款项）。\n\n但由于该商店并无独立过错，它可以获得全额补偿（赔偿），而不仅仅是部分份额（分担责任）。\n\n教育目标：尽管分销链上的任何商业供应商都可能面临严格的产品责任，但对于那些并非因疏忽、鲁莽或故意而未能发现产品缺陷的供应商（无独立过错），他们可以向分销链上游的供应商追讨全额补偿（赔偿）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任分担》第 22 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（关于赔偿/indemnity 的规定）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第1条、第2(a)条（美国法律协会，1998年）（关于制造缺陷的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability））。","conclusion":"答案 D：获得全额赔偿，因为发动机存在缺陷。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-ef0046792dc391f3"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":221,"sourceQuestionPage":663,"sourceQuestionPages":[663],"sourceExplanationPages":[664,665],"sourceAnswerPages":[664,665],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-222","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A member of a health club was exercising on an elliptical machine when the machine malfunctioned due to a defectively manufactured part, seriously injuring the member. The machine had not been modified since the health club purchased it from the manufacturer. The member had signed a waiver that absolved the health club from any liability, including strict liability, for injuries suffered by the member while at the health club. The member filed a complaint against the health club seeking to recover damages for her personal injuries on the basis of strict liability. The health club has moved to dismiss the complaint. How should the court rule on the health club's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the elliptical machine contained a manufacturing defect.","B":"Deny the motion, because the health club purchased the machine directly from the manufacturer.","C":"Grant the motion, because the health club is a service provider.","D":"Grant the motion, because the member exculpated the club's liability."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Strict products liability claims can only be brought against commercial suppliers or sellers. This means that the defendant must be in the business of selling or otherwise distributing the type of defective product that harmed the plaintiff. Accordingly, those in the business of providing services are not subject to strict products liability. Health clubs are in the business of providing fitness services to its members, including access to facilities and equipment—not selling exercise machines like the one that injured the member. As a result, the health club's motion to dismiss the member's complaint should be granted. (Choice A) Because the elliptical machine contained a manufacturing defect, commercial suppliers or sellers of the machine can be held strictly liable for the member's injuries caused by the defective machine. But service providers like the health club cannot. (Choice B) Any commercial supplier or seller in the chain of distribution (eg, wholesaler, retailer) can be sued for strict products liability if the product in question was defective when it left the supplier or seller's control. Had the health club been in the business of selling exercise machines to its members, it could have been liable for the member's injuries regardless of whether it purchased the machine directly from the manufacturer. (Choice D) An exculpatory agreement (eg, the waiver absolving the health club of liability) does not bar an otherwise valid strict products liability claim for personal injury or property damage.\n\nEducational objective: Strict products liability claims can only be brought against commercial suppliers or sellers—ie, those in the business of selling or otherwise distributing products of the type that harmed the plaintiff. Service providers are not subject to strict products liability.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial suppliers).","educationalObjective":"Strict products liability claims can only be brought against commercial suppliers or sellers—ie, those in the business of selling or otherwise distributing products of the type that harmed the plaintiff. Service providers are not subject to strict products liability.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial suppliers).","rule":"Strict products liability claims can only be brought against commercial suppliers or sellers—ie, those in the business of selling or otherwise distributing products of the type that harmed the plaintiff. Service providers are not subject to strict products liability.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule on the health club's motion?","rule":"Strict products liability claims can only be brought against commercial suppliers or sellers—ie, those in the business of selling or otherwise distributing products of the type that harmed the plaintiff. Service providers are not subject to strict products liability.","analysis":"Strict products liability claims can only be brought against commercial suppliers or sellers. This means that the defendant must be in the business of selling or otherwise distributing the type of defective product that harmed the plaintiff. Accordingly, those in the business of providing services are not subject to strict products liability. Health clubs are in the business of providing fitness services to its members, including access to facilities and equipment—not selling exercise machines like the one that injured the member. As a result, the health club's motion to dismiss the member's complaint should be granted. (Choice A) Because the elliptical machine contained a manufacturing defect, commercial suppliers or sellers of the machine can be held strictly liable for the member's injuries caused by the defective machine. But service providers like the health club cannot. (Choice B) Any commercial supplier or seller in the chain of distribution (eg, wholesaler, retailer) can be sued for strict products liability if the product in question was defective when it left the supplier or seller's control. Had the health club been in the business of selling exercise machines to its members, it could have been liable for the member's injuries regardless of whether it purchased the machine directly from the manufacturer. (Choice D) An exculpatory agreement (eg, the waiver absolving the health club of liability) does not bar an otherwise valid strict products liability claim for personal injury or property damage.","conclusion":"Answer C: Grant the motion, because the health club is a service provider."},"questionZh":"一名健身俱乐部会员正在使用椭圆机锻炼时，该机器因制造缺陷导致故障，致使该会员受重伤。\n\n自健身俱乐部从制造商处购入该机器以来，未对其进行过任何改装。\n\n该会员曾签署一份免责声明，免除健身俱乐部对其在俱乐部内所受伤害承担的任何责任（包括严格责任）。\n\n该会员针对健身俱乐部提起诉讼，主张基于严格责任原则就其人身伤害寻求损害赔偿。\n\n健身俱乐部已提出动议，请求驳回该诉讼。\n\n法院应如何裁决健身俱乐部的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为该椭圆机存在制造缺陷。","B":"驳回动议，因为健身俱乐部直接从制造商处购入了该机器。","C":"准予动议，因为健身俱乐部属于服务提供者。","D":"准予动议，因为该会员已免除了俱乐部的责任。"},"explanationZh":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔只能针对商业供应商或销售商提起。\n\n这意味着被告必须从事销售或以其他方式分销导致原告受损的那类缺陷产品的业务。\n\n因此，从事提供服务业务的主体不承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n健身俱乐部从事的是向会员提供健身服务的业务（包括使用设施和设备），而非销售导致会员受伤的那类健身器械。\n\n因此，法院应批准健身俱乐部驳回会员起诉的动议。\n\n（选项 A）由于椭圆机存在制造缺陷，该机器的商业供应商或销售商需对因该缺陷机器造成的会员伤害承担严格责任；但像健身俱乐部这样的服务提供商则无需承担此类责任。\n\n（选项 B）如果涉案产品在离开供应商或销售商的控制范围时已存在缺陷，则分销链中的任何商业供应商或销售商（例如批发商、零售商）均可能面临严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n如果健身俱乐部从事向会员销售健身器械的业务，无论其是否直接从制造商处购入该机器，均可能需对会员的伤害承担责任。\n\n（选项 D）免责协议（例如免除健身俱乐部责任的弃权声明）不能阻却针对人身伤害或财产损失提出的、在其他方面成立的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔。\n\n教育目标：严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔只能针对商业供应商或销售商提起——即从事销售或以其他方式分销导致原告受损的那类产品的经营者。\n\n服务提供商不承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 20 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于商业供应商责任的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"针对产品提起的严格责任索赔，仅能针对商业供应商或销售商——即从事销售或以其他方式分销导致原告受损的那类产品的经营者——提出。\n\n服务提供者不承担产品严格责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应如何裁决健身俱乐部的动议？","rule":"针对产品提起的严格责任索赔，仅能针对商业供应商或销售商——即从事销售或以其他方式分销导致原告受损的那类产品的经营者——提出。\n\n服务提供者不承担产品严格责任。","analysis":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔只能针对商业供应商或销售商提起。\n\n这意味着被告必须从事销售或以其他方式分销导致原告受损的那类缺陷产品的业务。\n\n因此，从事提供服务业务的主体不承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n健身俱乐部从事的是向会员提供健身服务的业务（包括使用设施和设备），而非销售导致会员受伤的那类健身器械。\n\n因此，法院应批准健身俱乐部驳回会员起诉的动议。\n\n（选项 A）由于椭圆机存在制造缺陷，该机器的商业供应商或销售商需对因该缺陷机器造成的会员伤害承担严格责任；但像健身俱乐部这样的服务提供商则无需承担此类责任。\n\n（选项 B）如果涉案产品在离开供应商或销售商的控制范围时已存在缺陷，则分销链中的任何商业供应商或销售商（例如批发商、零售商）均可能面临严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）诉讼。\n\n如果健身俱乐部从事向会员销售健身器械的业务，无论其是否直接从制造商处购入该机器，均可能需对会员的伤害承担责任。\n\n（选项 D）免责协议（例如免除健身俱乐部责任的弃权声明）不能阻却针对人身伤害或财产损失提出的、在其他方面成立的严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔。\n\n教育目标：严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔只能针对商业供应商或销售商提起——即从事销售或以其他方式分销导致原告受损的那类产品的经营者。\n\n服务提供商不承担严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第 1 条、第 20 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于商业供应商责任的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 C：准予动议，因为健身俱乐部属于服务提供者。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-add5dcb7677e0a38"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":222,"sourceQuestionPage":666,"sourceQuestionPages":[666],"sourceExplanationPages":[667,668],"sourceAnswerPages":[667,668],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-223","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A mining company that operated a copper mine in a remote location kept dynamite in a storage facility at the mine. The storage facility was designed and operated in conformity with state-of-the-art safety standards. In the jurisdiction, the storage of dynamite is deemed an abnormally dangerous activity. Dynamite that was stored in the mining company's storage facility and that had been manufactured by an explosives manufacturer exploded due to an unknown cause. The explosion injured a state employee who was at the mine performing a safety audit. The employee brought an action in strict liability against the mining company. What would be the mining company's best defense?","choices":{"A":"The mine was in a remote location.","B":"The mining company did not manufacture the dynamite.","C":"The state employee assumed the risk of injury inherent in the job.","D":"The storage facility conformed to state-of-the-art safety standards."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Assumption of the risk\n\n(defense to strict liability)\n\nTraditional contributory Bars recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction him/herself to known risk\n\nPure comparative Reduces recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction* him/herself to known risk Narrow exception: Bars recovery when plaintiff received benefit (eg, financial gain, employment) from his/her exposure to risk * Default rule on MBE.\n\nA defendant is strictly liable (liable without proof of fault) for bodily harm caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity. However, in a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction (default rule), assumption of the risk is a defense to strict liability that: reduces recovery when the plaintiff knowingly and voluntarily exposed him/herself to an abnormally dangerous activity and\n\nbars recovery when the plaintiff received a benefit from his/her exposure to that activity.\n\nHere, the mining company's storage of dynamite (abnormally dangerous activity) injured the state employee. But since the employee performed the safety audit as part of his employment, he assumed the risk of injury for a benefit and his recovery would likely be barred. Therefore, the mining company's best defense is that the state employee assumed the risk of injury inherent in his job. (Choice A) The location of an activity may be relevant to whether that activity is abnormally dangerous. But since this jurisdiction has already determined that storing dynamite is an abnormally dangerous activity, the location of the mine is irrelevant. (Choice B) Although the mining company did not manufacture the dynamite (no strict products liability), the company is still strictly liable for harm caused by storing the dynamite (abnormally dangerous activity). (Choice D) Because strict liability is imposed without proof of fault, the defendant's use of reasonable care is not a defense. Therefore, the storage facility's conformance with state-of- the-art safety standards (evidence of reasonable care) has no effect on the company's strict liability.\n\nEducational objective: Assumption of the risk is a defense to strict liability that (1) reduces recovery when the plaintiff knowingly and voluntarily exposed him/herself to a risk of harm (eg, abnormally dangerous activity) and (2) bars recovery when the plaintiff received a benefit from his/her exposure to that risk.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm §§ 20, 24, 25 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (assumption of the risk as a defense to strict liability).","educationalObjective":"Assumption of the risk is a defense to strict liability that (1) reduces recovery when the plaintiff knowingly and voluntarily exposed him/herself to a risk of harm (eg, abnormally dangerous activity) and (2) bars recovery when the plaintiff received a benefit from his/her exposure to that risk.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm §§ 20, 24, 25 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (assumption of the risk as a defense to strict liability).","rule":"Assumption of the risk is a defense to strict liability that (1) reduces recovery when the plaintiff knowingly and voluntarily exposed him/herself to a risk of harm (eg, abnormally dangerous activity) and (2) bars recovery when the plaintiff received a benefit from his/her exposure to that risk.","irac":{"issue":"What would be the mining company's best defense?","rule":"Assumption of the risk is a defense to strict liability that (1) reduces recovery when the plaintiff knowingly and voluntarily exposed him/herself to a risk of harm (eg, abnormally dangerous activity) and (2) bars recovery when the plaintiff received a benefit from his/her exposure to that risk.","analysis":"Assumption of the risk\n\n(defense to strict liability)\n\nTraditional contributory Bars recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction him/herself to known risk\n\nPure comparative Reduces recovery when plaintiff voluntarily exposed negligence jurisdiction* him/herself to known risk Narrow exception: Bars recovery when plaintiff received benefit (eg, financial gain, employment) from his/her exposure to risk * Default rule on MBE.\n\nA defendant is strictly liable (liable without proof of fault) for bodily harm caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity. However, in a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction (default rule), assumption of the risk is a defense to strict liability that: reduces recovery when the plaintiff knowingly and voluntarily exposed him/herself to an abnormally dangerous activity and\n\nbars recovery when the plaintiff received a benefit from his/her exposure to that activity.\n\nHere, the mining company's storage of dynamite (abnormally dangerous activity) injured the state employee. But since the employee performed the safety audit as part of his employment, he assumed the risk of injury for a benefit and his recovery would likely be barred. Therefore, the mining company's best defense is that the state employee assumed the risk of injury inherent in his job. (Choice A) The location of an activity may be relevant to whether that activity is abnormally dangerous. But since this jurisdiction has already determined that storing dynamite is an abnormally dangerous activity, the location of the mine is irrelevant. (Choice B) Although the mining company did not manufacture the dynamite (no strict products liability), the company is still strictly liable for harm caused by storing the dynamite (abnormally dangerous activity). (Choice D) Because strict liability is imposed without proof of fault, the defendant's use of reasonable care is not a defense. Therefore, the storage facility's conformance with state-of- the-art safety standards (evidence of reasonable care) has no effect on the company's strict liability.","conclusion":"Answer C: The state employee assumed the risk of injury inherent in the job."},"questionZh":"一家在偏远地区经营铜矿的矿业公司在矿区的储存设施内存放了炸药。\n\n该储存设施的设计与运营均符合当时最先进的安全标准。\n\n在该司法管辖区，储存炸药被视为一种“异常危险活动”（abnormally dangerous activity）。\n\n存放在该矿业公司设施内、由某炸药制造商生产的炸药因不明原因发生爆炸。\n\n爆炸导致一名正在矿区进行安全审计的州政府雇员受伤。\n\n该雇员针对矿业公司提起了严格责任（strict liability）诉讼。\n\n矿业公司最好的抗辩理由是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"矿区位于偏远地区。","B":"矿业公司并未生产该炸药。","C":"该州政府雇员承担了工作中固有的受伤风险。","D":"该储存设施符合当时最先进的安全标准。"},"explanationZh":"风险自担（Assumption of the risk） （针对严格责任的抗辩） 传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”法域：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则丧失获赔权。 纯粹“比较过失”法域（MBE默认规则）：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则获赔金额会减少。狭义例外：若原告因置身于该风险中而获得利益（如经济收益、就业机会），则丧失获赔权。\n\n被告因其“异常危险活动”造成的人身伤害需承担严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担责任）。\n\n然而，在纯粹比较过失法域（默认规则）中，“风险自担”可作为针对严格责任的抗辩理由：当原告明知并自愿将自己置于异常危险活动中时，其获赔金额会减少； 而当原告因置身于该活动中而获得利益时，则丧失获赔权。\n\n本案中，矿业公司储存炸药（属于异常危险活动）的行为导致该州政府雇员受伤。\n\n但由于该雇员是在履行职务时进行安全检查，他因获得利益而承担了受伤风险，因此其获赔权很可能被排除。\n\n因此，矿业公司最有力的抗辩理由是：该州政府雇员承担了其工作中固有的受伤风险。\n\n（选项 A）活动的地点可能与该活动是否属于“异常危险活动”有关。\n\n但既然该法域已认定储存炸药属于异常危险活动，矿山的具体位置便无关紧要。\n\n（选项 B）尽管矿业公司并未生产该炸药（不涉及严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）），但该公司仍需对其储存炸药（异常危险活动）所造成的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）由于严格责任的适用无需证明过错，被告采取了合理注意措施并不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n因此，储存设施符合最先进的安全标准（即采取合理注意的证据）这一事实，并不影响公司承担严格责任。\n\n教学目标：“风险自担”是针对严格责任的一种抗辩，其规则为：(1) 当原告明知并自愿将自己置于伤害风险（如异常危险活动）中时，获赔金额减少；(2) 当原告因置身于该风险中而获得利益时，丧失获赔权。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》\n\n针对身体与情感伤害 §§ 20, 24, 25 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（作为严格责任抗辩理由的“自甘冒险”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“风险自担”（assumption of the risk）是针对严格责任的一种抗辩理由：(1) 当原告明知并自愿将自己置于某种伤害风险（例如异常危险活动）之中时，该抗辩可减少其获赔金额；(2) 当原告因置身于该风险之中而获得某种利益时，该抗辩可排除其获赔权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"矿业公司最好的抗辩理由是什么？","rule":"“风险自担”（assumption of the risk）是针对严格责任的一种抗辩理由：(1) 当原告明知并自愿将自己置于某种伤害风险（例如异常危险活动）之中时，该抗辩可减少其获赔金额；(2) 当原告因置身于该风险之中而获得某种利益时，该抗辩可排除其获赔权利。","analysis":"风险自担（Assumption of the risk） （针对严格责任的抗辩） 传统“与有过失（contributory negligence）”法域：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则丧失获赔权。 纯粹“比较过失”法域（MBE默认规则）：若原告自愿将自己置于已知风险之中，则获赔金额会减少。狭义例外：若原告因置身于该风险中而获得利益（如经济收益、就业机会），则丧失获赔权。\n\n被告因其“异常危险活动”造成的人身伤害需承担严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担责任）。\n\n然而，在纯粹比较过失法域（默认规则）中，“风险自担”可作为针对严格责任的抗辩理由：当原告明知并自愿将自己置于异常危险活动中时，其获赔金额会减少； 而当原告因置身于该活动中而获得利益时，则丧失获赔权。\n\n本案中，矿业公司储存炸药（属于异常危险活动）的行为导致该州政府雇员受伤。\n\n但由于该雇员是在履行职务时进行安全检查，他因获得利益而承担了受伤风险，因此其获赔权很可能被排除。\n\n因此，矿业公司最有力的抗辩理由是：该州政府雇员承担了其工作中固有的受伤风险。\n\n（选项 A）活动的地点可能与该活动是否属于“异常危险活动”有关。\n\n但既然该法域已认定储存炸药属于异常危险活动，矿山的具体位置便无关紧要。\n\n（选项 B）尽管矿业公司并未生产该炸药（不涉及严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）），但该公司仍需对其储存炸药（异常危险活动）所造成的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n（选项 D）由于严格责任的适用无需证明过错，被告采取了合理注意措施并不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n因此，储存设施符合最先进的安全标准（即采取合理注意的证据）这一事实，并不影响公司承担严格责任。\n\n教学目标：“风险自担”是针对严格责任的一种抗辩，其规则为：(1) 当原告明知并自愿将自己置于伤害风险（如异常危险活动）中时，获赔金额减少；(2) 当原告因置身于该风险中而获得利益时，丧失获赔权。\n\n参考资料 《侵权法重述（第三版）：责任》\n\n针对身体与情感伤害 §§ 20, 24, 25 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（作为严格责任抗辩理由的“自甘冒险”）。","conclusion":"答案 C：该州政府雇员承担了工作中固有的受伤风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"conformance","ipa":"/kʌnfˈɔɹməns/","syllables":"con·for·mance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 顺应, 一致","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“conformance”","wordForm":"词；词形 s:conformances","sourceSentence":"Therefore, the storage facility's conformance with state-of- the-art safety standards (evidence of reasonable care) has no effect on the company's strict liability.","synonyms":["conformity"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":223,"sourceQuestionPage":669,"sourceQuestionPages":[669],"sourceExplanationPages":[670,671],"sourceAnswerPages":[670,671],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-224","subject":"Torts","topic":"Actual & Proximate Cause","question":"Toxic materials being transported by truck from a manufacturer's plant to a warehouse leaked from the truck onto the street a few miles from the plant. A driver lost control of his car when he hit the puddle of spilled toxic materials on the street, and he was injured when his car hit a stop sign. In an action for damages by the driver against the manufacturer based on strict liability, is the driver likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the driver's injury did not result from the toxicity of the materials.","B":"No, because the driver's loss of control was an intervening cause.","C":"Yes, because the leak occurred near the manufacturer's plant.","D":"Yes, because the manufacturer is strictly liable for leaks of its toxic materials."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Common examples of abnormally dangerous activities\n\nTransporting & storing hazardous materials & gasoline Fumigating & crop dusting with toxic chemicals Storing explosives & blasting Operating nuclear facility Burning fields A defendant is strictly liable—liable regardless of fault—for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity. An activity is abnormally dangerous if it is (1) uncommon in the community and (2) poses a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm to the plaintiff that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. However, the defendant is only strictly liable for harm that stems from the abnormally dangerous characteristic of that activity. Here, the transportation of toxic materials (uncommon activity in the community) is an abnormally dangerous activity since persons exposed to the materials have a high probability of becoming seriously ill (foreseeable and significant risk of harm). However, the manufacturer is not strictly liable to the driver since the driver's injury resulted from the puddle of spilled materials—not the materials' abnormally dangerous toxicity. Therefore, the driver's strict liability claim against the manufacturer will likely fail (Choice D). (Choice B) Only an unforeseeable intervening cause will negate the defendant's strict liability. Here, however, it was foreseeable that a person driving over spilled toxic materials would lose control of the vehicle, so the driver's loss of control will not negate the manufacturer's liability. (Choice C) A defendant is strictly liable for harm resulting from his/her abnormally dangerous activity regardless of where the harm occurs. Therefore, the location of the leak is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable for harm resulting from the abnormally dangerous characteristic of that activity—eg, illness caused by exposure to spilled toxic chemical, but not injury from a car accident caused by spilled chemicals.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (abnormally dangerous activity).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 29 cmt. l (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (limitations on tortious conduct).","educationalObjective":"A defendant engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable for harm resulting from the abnormally dangerous characteristic of that activity—eg, illness caused by exposure to spilled toxic chemical, but not injury from a car accident caused by spilled chemicals.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (abnormally dangerous activity).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 29 cmt. l (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (limitations on tortious conduct).","rule":"A defendant engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable for harm resulting from the abnormally dangerous characteristic of that activity—eg, illness caused by exposure to spilled toxic chemical, but not injury from a car accident caused by spilled chemicals.","irac":{"issue":"In an action for damages by the driver against the manufacturer based on strict liability, is the driver likely to prevail?","rule":"A defendant engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable for harm resulting from the abnormally dangerous characteristic of that activity—eg, illness caused by exposure to spilled toxic chemical, but not injury from a car accident caused by spilled chemicals.","analysis":"Common examples of abnormally dangerous activities\n\nTransporting & storing hazardous materials & gasoline Fumigating & crop dusting with toxic chemicals Storing explosives & blasting Operating nuclear facility Burning fields A defendant is strictly liable—liable regardless of fault—for harm caused by an abnormally dangerous activity. An activity is abnormally dangerous if it is (1) uncommon in the community and (2) poses a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm to the plaintiff that cannot be mitigated by reasonable care. However, the defendant is only strictly liable for harm that stems from the abnormally dangerous characteristic of that activity. Here, the transportation of toxic materials (uncommon activity in the community) is an abnormally dangerous activity since persons exposed to the materials have a high probability of becoming seriously ill (foreseeable and significant risk of harm). However, the manufacturer is not strictly liable to the driver since the driver's injury resulted from the puddle of spilled materials—not the materials' abnormally dangerous toxicity. Therefore, the driver's strict liability claim against the manufacturer will likely fail (Choice D). (Choice B) Only an unforeseeable intervening cause will negate the defendant's strict liability. Here, however, it was foreseeable that a person driving over spilled toxic materials would lose control of the vehicle, so the driver's loss of control will not negate the manufacturer's liability. (Choice C) A defendant is strictly liable for harm resulting from his/her abnormally dangerous activity regardless of where the harm occurs. Therefore, the location of the leak is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the driver's injury did not result from the toxicity of the materials."},"questionZh":"一辆卡车从制造商工厂向仓库运送有毒物质，途中在距离工厂几英里外的街道上发生泄漏。\n\n一名司机撞上街道上的泄漏有毒物质后失去控制，撞上停车标志后受伤。\n\n如果该司机基于严格责任原则起诉制造商，要求赔偿损失，他是否有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为司机的受伤并非由有毒物质本身的毒性造成。","B":"否，因为司机失去控制是导致事故发生的中间原因。","C":"是，因为泄漏发生在制造商工厂附近。","D":"是，因为制造商对其有毒物质的泄漏负有严格责任。"},"explanationZh":"异常危险活动的常见例子 运输和储存危险材料及汽油；使用有毒化学品进行熏蒸或农作物喷洒；储存炸药及进行爆破作业；运营核设施；焚烧田地。对于因异常危险活动造成的损害，被告承担严格责任（即无论有无过错均须承担责任）。\n\n若某项活动满足以下条件，即被视为异常危险活动：(1) 在该社区中不常见；(2) 对原告构成可预见的、极大的身体伤害风险，且该风险无法通过合理的谨慎措施加以缓解。\n\n然而，被告仅对源于该活动“异常危险”特性的损害承担严格责任。\n\n在本案中，运输有毒材料（属于社区中的不常见活动）属于异常危险活动，因为接触此类材料的人极有可能患上重病（存在可预见的重大伤害风险）。\n\n然而，制造商无需对司机承担严格责任，因为司机的受伤是由于车辆驶过泄漏材料形成的积洼所致，而非源于材料本身异常危险的毒性。\n\n因此，司机针对制造商提出的严格责任索赔很可能会败诉（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 B）只有不可预见的介入原因才会免除被告的严格责任。\n\n然而在本案中，驾驶车辆驶过泄漏的有毒材料导致车辆失控是可预见的，因此司机的失控行为并不能免除制造商的责任。\n\n（选项 C）无论损害发生在何处，被告均须对其异常危险活动造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，泄漏发生的地点无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：从事异常危险活动的被告，须对源于该活动“异常危险”特性的损害承担严格责任——例如，因接触泄漏的有毒化学品而患病，但不包括因泄漏化学品引发车祸而造成的伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与情感伤害责任》第 20 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于异常危险活动）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与情感伤害责任》第 29 条注释 l (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于侵权行为的限制）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"被告从事异常危险的活动，须对该活动异常危险性所造成的损害承担严格责任——例如，接触泄漏的有毒化学品导致的疾病，但不包括因化学品泄漏造成的交通事故而导致的伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"在驾驶员基于严格责任对制造商提起损害赔偿诉讼的情况下，驾驶员胜诉的可能性有多大？","rule":"被告从事异常危险的活动，须对该活动异常危险性所造成的损害承担严格责任——例如，接触泄漏的有毒化学品导致的疾病，但不包括因化学品泄漏造成的交通事故而导致的伤害。","analysis":"异常危险活动的常见例子 运输和储存危险材料及汽油；使用有毒化学品进行熏蒸或农作物喷洒；储存炸药及进行爆破作业；运营核设施；焚烧田地。对于因异常危险活动造成的损害，被告承担严格责任（即无论有无过错均须承担责任）。\n\n若某项活动满足以下条件，即被视为异常危险活动：(1) 在该社区中不常见；(2) 对原告构成可预见的、极大的身体伤害风险，且该风险无法通过合理的谨慎措施加以缓解。\n\n然而，被告仅对源于该活动“异常危险”特性的损害承担严格责任。\n\n在本案中，运输有毒材料（属于社区中的不常见活动）属于异常危险活动，因为接触此类材料的人极有可能患上重病（存在可预见的重大伤害风险）。\n\n然而，制造商无需对司机承担严格责任，因为司机的受伤是由于车辆驶过泄漏材料形成的积洼所致，而非源于材料本身异常危险的毒性。\n\n因此，司机针对制造商提出的严格责任索赔很可能会败诉（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 B）只有不可预见的介入原因才会免除被告的严格责任。\n\n然而在本案中，驾驶车辆驶过泄漏的有毒材料导致车辆失控是可预见的，因此司机的失控行为并不能免除制造商的责任。\n\n（选项 C）无论损害发生在何处，被告均须对其异常危险活动造成的损害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，泄漏发生的地点无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：从事异常危险活动的被告，须对源于该活动“异常危险”特性的损害承担严格责任——例如，因接触泄漏的有毒化学品而患病，但不包括因泄漏化学品引发车祸而造成的伤害。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与情感伤害责任》第 20 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于异常危险活动）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与情感伤害责任》第 29 条注释 l (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于侵权行为的限制）。","conclusion":"答案 A：否，因为司机的受伤并非由有毒物质本身的毒性造成。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":224,"sourceQuestionPage":672,"sourceQuestionPages":[672],"sourceExplanationPages":[673,674],"sourceAnswerPages":[673,674],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-225","subject":"Torts","topic":"Torts","question":"A chemical company's plant was located in a residential community. The manufacturing process used at the plant generated a toxic chemical as a by-product. The chemical was stored in a state-of-the-art tank on the site before being moved to an off-site disposal facility. The on-site storage arrangement conformed to the requirements of reasonable care and to the applicable government regulations. However, the storage of the toxic chemical created a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm even when reasonable care was exercised. Despite the chemical company's proper use and care of the storage tank, toxic fumes escaped from the tank and made residents of the area seriously ill. No state or federal statutes address the issue of the company's liability. In an action by one of the affected residents against the chemical company, will the resident be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the chemical company conformed to the requirements of reasonable care and to the applicable government regulations.","B":"No, because the chemical company used a state-of-the-art storage tank.","C":"Yes, because the chemical company is strictly liable in tort for any harm caused by the toxic chemicals it produced.","D":"Yes, because the storage of toxic chemicals in a residential community created a highly significant risk of physical harm even when reasonable care was exercised."},"answer":"D","explanation":"An abnormally dangerous activity is an uncommon activity that poses a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm that cannot be mitigated by the exercise of reasonable care. A defendant is strictly liable when (1) the defendant's abnormally dangerous activity causes physical harm and (2) that harm results from the hazards that make the activity abnormally dangerous. Here, the chemical company's storage of a toxic chemical (uncommon activity) was abnormally dangerous because it posed a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm despite the company's exercise of reasonable care. And since the resident's illness was caused by the chemical's toxicity (the hazard that made storing the chemical abnormally dangerous), the resident will likely prevail. (Choices A & B) Evidence that the company used a state-of-the-art storage tank, conformed to the requirements of reasonable care, and abided by applicable government regulations shows that the company exercised reasonable care. But these facts are irrelevant since the use of reasonable care is not a defense to strict liability (liability imposed without proof of fault). (Choice C) A defendant who engages in an abnormally dangerous activity is only strictly liable for harm that results from the abnormal dangers or hazards of that activity. Therefore, the company is strictly liable for harm resulting from the chemical's toxicity (eg, illness caused by exposure to toxic fumes), but not for harm unrelated to that toxicity (eg, car accident caused by toxic fumes that obscured a motorist's view).\n\nEducational objective: A defendant is strictly liable for physical harm caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity, as long as that harm resulted from the abnormal dangers or hazards of the activity. The use of reasonable care is no defense.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 29 cmt. l (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (limitations on strict liability).","educationalObjective":"A defendant is strictly liable for physical harm caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity, as long as that harm resulted from the abnormal dangers or hazards of the activity. The use of reasonable care is no defense.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 29 cmt. l (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (limitations on strict liability).","rule":"A defendant is strictly liable for physical harm caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity, as long as that harm resulted from the abnormal dangers or hazards of the activity. The use of reasonable care is no defense.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by one of the affected residents against the chemical company, will the resident be likely to prevail?","rule":"A defendant is strictly liable for physical harm caused by his/her abnormally dangerous activity, as long as that harm resulted from the abnormal dangers or hazards of the activity. The use of reasonable care is no defense.","analysis":"An abnormally dangerous activity is an uncommon activity that poses a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm that cannot be mitigated by the exercise of reasonable care. A defendant is strictly liable when (1) the defendant's abnormally dangerous activity causes physical harm and (2) that harm results from the hazards that make the activity abnormally dangerous. Here, the chemical company's storage of a toxic chemical (uncommon activity) was abnormally dangerous because it posed a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm despite the company's exercise of reasonable care. And since the resident's illness was caused by the chemical's toxicity (the hazard that made storing the chemical abnormally dangerous), the resident will likely prevail. (Choices A & B) Evidence that the company used a state-of-the-art storage tank, conformed to the requirements of reasonable care, and abided by applicable government regulations shows that the company exercised reasonable care. But these facts are irrelevant since the use of reasonable care is not a defense to strict liability (liability imposed without proof of fault). (Choice C) A defendant who engages in an abnormally dangerous activity is only strictly liable for harm that results from the abnormal dangers or hazards of that activity. Therefore, the company is strictly liable for harm resulting from the chemical's toxicity (eg, illness caused by exposure to toxic fumes), but not for harm unrelated to that toxicity (eg, car accident caused by toxic fumes that obscured a motorist's view).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the storage of toxic chemicals in a residential community created a highly significant risk of physical harm even when reasonable care was exercised."},"questionZh":"某家化工公司的工厂位于居民区内。\n\n该厂的生产工艺会产生一种有毒化学品作为副产品。\n\n在运往厂外处置设施之前，该化学品被储存在厂内一个技术最先进的储罐中。\n\n厂内的储存安排符合“合理注意”（reasonable care）的要求以及适用的政府法规。\n\n然而，即便已尽到合理注意，储存这种有毒化学品仍会带来可预见的、且极高的人身伤害风险。\n\n尽管该化工公司对储罐的使用和维护得当，有毒烟雾仍从储罐中泄漏，导致当地居民患上重病。\n\n目前尚无州或联邦法规针对该公司的责任问题作出规定。\n\n在受影响居民之一针对该化工公司提起的诉讼中，该居民胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该化工公司符合了合理注意的要求以及适用的政府法规。","B":"不会，因为该化工公司使用了技术最先进的储罐。","C":"会，因为对于其生产的有毒化学品所造成的任何伤害，该化工公司须承担侵权法上的严格责任。","D":"会，因为在居民区储存有毒化学品，即便已尽到合理注意，仍会带来极高的人身伤害风险。"},"explanationZh":"“异常危险活动”是指一种不常见的活动，它带来可预见的且极重大的身体伤害风险，且这种风险无法通过尽到合理注意义务来消除。\n\n当满足以下条件时，被告承担严格责任：(1) 被告的异常危险活动造成了身体伤害；(2) 该伤害源于使该活动具有异常危险性的那些特定危害。\n\n在本案中，该化学品公司储存有毒化学品（属于不常见活动）的行为构成异常危险活动，因为它带来了可预见且极重大的身体伤害风险，即便公司已尽到合理注意义务，该风险依然存在。\n\n由于居民患病是由该化学品的毒性（即导致储存该化学品具有异常危险性的危害因素）引起的，因此该居民很可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 A 和 B）关于公司使用了最先进的储罐、符合合理注意义务要求并遵守了相关政府法规的证据，确实表明公司尽到了合理注意义务。\n\n但这些事实与本案无关，因为尽到合理注意义务并不能作为免除严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担的责任）的抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 C）从事异常危险活动的被告，仅需对由该活动的异常危险或危害因素所造成的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，对于因化学品毒性造成的伤害（例如，因接触有毒烟雾而患病），公司需承担严格责任；但对于与该毒性无关的伤害（例如，因有毒烟雾遮挡驾驶员视线而引发的车祸），公司则无需承担严格责任。\n\n教育目标：只要身体伤害是由被告的异常危险活动引起的，且该伤害源于该活动的异常危险或危害因素，被告即须承担严格责任。\n\n尽到合理注意义务不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 20 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于异常危险活动的严格责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 29 条评注 l (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于严格责任的限制）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果人身伤害是由被告从事的“异常危险活动”所固有的异常危险或隐患造成的，则被告须对该伤害承担严格责任。\n\n采取合理注意并不能作为抗辩理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"在受影响居民之一针对该化工公司提起的诉讼中，该居民胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"如果人身伤害是由被告从事的“异常危险活动”所固有的异常危险或隐患造成的，则被告须对该伤害承担严格责任。\n\n采取合理注意并不能作为抗辩理由。","analysis":"“异常危险活动”是指一种不常见的活动，它带来可预见的且极重大的身体伤害风险，且这种风险无法通过尽到合理注意义务来消除。\n\n当满足以下条件时，被告承担严格责任：(1) 被告的异常危险活动造成了身体伤害；(2) 该伤害源于使该活动具有异常危险性的那些特定危害。\n\n在本案中，该化学品公司储存有毒化学品（属于不常见活动）的行为构成异常危险活动，因为它带来了可预见且极重大的身体伤害风险，即便公司已尽到合理注意义务，该风险依然存在。\n\n由于居民患病是由该化学品的毒性（即导致储存该化学品具有异常危险性的危害因素）引起的，因此该居民很可能胜诉。\n\n（选项 A 和 B）关于公司使用了最先进的储罐、符合合理注意义务要求并遵守了相关政府法规的证据，确实表明公司尽到了合理注意义务。\n\n但这些事实与本案无关，因为尽到合理注意义务并不能作为免除严格责任（即无需证明过错即承担的责任）的抗辩理由。\n\n（选项 C）从事异常危险活动的被告，仅需对由该活动的异常危险或危害因素所造成的伤害承担严格责任。\n\n因此，对于因化学品毒性造成的伤害（例如，因接触有毒烟雾而患病），公司需承担严格责任；但对于与该毒性无关的伤害（例如，因有毒烟雾遮挡驾驶员视线而引发的车祸），公司则无需承担严格责任。\n\n教育目标：只要身体伤害是由被告的异常危险活动引起的，且该伤害源于该活动的异常危险或危害因素，被告即须承担严格责任。\n\n尽到合理注意义务不能作为抗辩理由。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 20 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于异常危险活动的严格责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第三版）：身体与精神伤害责任》第 29 条评注 l (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（关于严格责任的限制）。","conclusion":"答案 D：会，因为在居民区储存有毒化学品，即便已尽到合理注意，仍会带来极高的人身伤害风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-9add3e671660f7b5"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":225,"sourceQuestionPage":675,"sourceQuestionPages":[675],"sourceExplanationPages":[676,677],"sourceAnswerPages":[676,677],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-226","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A dentist was anesthetizing a patient's jaw before pulling a tooth. Although the dentist used due care, the hypodermic needle broke off in the patient's gum tissue, causing injury. The needle broke because of a manufacturing defect that the dentist could not have detected. Is the patient likely to recover damages in an action against the dentist based on strict products liability and malpractice?","choices":{"A":"No, on neither basis.","B":"Yes, based on malpractice, but not on strict products liability.","C":"Yes, based on strict products liability, but not on malpractice.","D":"Yes, on both bases."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Strict products liability claims require proof that the defendant is a commercial supplier—one in the business of manufacturing or selling the type of defective product that harmed the plaintiff. Here, the patient was injured by a manufacturing defect in a hypodermic needle. But since the dentist is a service provider (not in the business of manufacturing or selling needles), the patient cannot recover under this claim (Choices C & D). Malpractice claims require proof that a professional (eg, dentist, doctor, lawyer) deviated from the standard of care that reasonable professionals in his/her field would have used under similar circumstances. Here, the dentist used due care and could not have detected the needle's defect. Since there is no evidence that the dentist deviated from professional standards, the patient is unlikely to recover on this basis (Choices B & D).\n\nEducational objective: Strict products liability arises when a commercial supplier produces or sells a defective product (not a service) that causes the plaintiff harm. Malpractice liability arises when a professional fails to use the standard of care that reasonable professionals in his/her field would have used under similar circumstances.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial suppliers).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 299A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (professional standard of care).","educationalObjective":"Strict products liability arises when a commercial supplier produces or sells a defective product (not a service) that causes the plaintiff harm. Malpractice liability arises when a professional fails to use the standard of care that reasonable professionals in his/her field would have used under similar circumstances.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. §§ 1, 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (liability of commercial suppliers).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Torts § 299A (Am. Law Inst. 1965) (professional standard of care).","rule":"Strict products liability arises when a commercial supplier produces or sells a defective product (not a service) that causes the plaintiff harm. Malpractice liability arises when a professional fails to use the standard of care that reasonable professionals in his/her field would have used under similar circumstances.","irac":{"issue":"Is the patient likely to recover damages in an action against the dentist based on strict products liability and malpractice?","rule":"Strict products liability arises when a commercial supplier produces or sells a defective product (not a service) that causes the plaintiff harm. Malpractice liability arises when a professional fails to use the standard of care that reasonable professionals in his/her field would have used under similar circumstances.","analysis":"Strict products liability claims require proof that the defendant is a commercial supplier—one in the business of manufacturing or selling the type of defective product that harmed the plaintiff. Here, the patient was injured by a manufacturing defect in a hypodermic needle. But since the dentist is a service provider (not in the business of manufacturing or selling needles), the patient cannot recover under this claim (Choices C & D). Malpractice claims require proof that a professional (eg, dentist, doctor, lawyer) deviated from the standard of care that reasonable professionals in his/her field would have used under similar circumstances. Here, the dentist used due care and could not have detected the needle's defect. Since there is no evidence that the dentist deviated from professional standards, the patient is unlikely to recover on this basis (Choices B & D).","conclusion":"Answer A: No, on neither basis."},"questionZh":"一位牙医在拔牙前为患者的下颌进行局部麻醉。\n\n尽管牙医操作谨慎，但注射针头在患者牙龈组织中折断，导致了伤害。\n\n针头折断是由于牙医无法察觉的制造缺陷造成的。\n\n如果患者基于“严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）”和“医疗事故责任”起诉该牙医，是否很可能获得损害赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，基于这两种理由均无法获得赔偿。","B":"是的，基于医疗事故责任可以获得赔偿，但基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）则不能。","C":"是的，基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）可以获得赔偿，但基于医疗事故责任则不能。","D":"是的，基于这两种理由均可获得赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的索赔要求证明被告属于商业供应商——即从事制造或销售导致原告受损的那类缺陷产品的业务。\n\n在本案中，患者因皮下注射针头的制造缺陷而受伤。\n\n但由于牙医属于服务提供者（并不从事制造或销售针头的业务），患者无法依据此项主张获得赔偿（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n医疗事故（malpractice）索赔要求证明专业人员（如牙医、医生、律师）未达到其所在领域的合理专业人员在类似情况下应遵循的注意标准（standard of care）。\n\n在本案中，牙医已尽到应有的注意义务，且无法察觉该针头的缺陷。\n\n由于没有证据表明牙医偏离了专业标准，患者不太可能据此获得赔偿（选项 B 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：当商业供应商生产或销售导致原告受损的缺陷产品（而非服务）时，即产生严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n当专业人员未达到其所在领域的合理专业人员在类似情况下应遵循的注意标准时，即产生医疗事故责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第 1、20 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于商业供应商的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 299A 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于专业人员的注意标准）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当商业供应商生产或销售了导致原告受损的缺陷产品（而非服务）时，即产生严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n当专业人员未能在类似情况下达到其所在领域内理性专业人员应有的注意标准时，即产生医疗事故责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果患者基于“严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）”和“医疗事故责任”起诉该牙医，是否很可能获得损害赔偿？","rule":"当商业供应商生产或销售了导致原告受损的缺陷产品（而非服务）时，即产生严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n当专业人员未能在类似情况下达到其所在领域内理性专业人员应有的注意标准时，即产生医疗事故责任。","analysis":"基于严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）（strict products liability）的索赔要求证明被告属于商业供应商——即从事制造或销售导致原告受损的那类缺陷产品的业务。\n\n在本案中，患者因皮下注射针头的制造缺陷而受伤。\n\n但由于牙医属于服务提供者（并不从事制造或销售针头的业务），患者无法依据此项主张获得赔偿（选项 C 和 D）。\n\n医疗事故（malpractice）索赔要求证明专业人员（如牙医、医生、律师）未达到其所在领域的合理专业人员在类似情况下应遵循的注意标准（standard of care）。\n\n在本案中，牙医已尽到应有的注意义务，且无法察觉该针头的缺陷。\n\n由于没有证据表明牙医偏离了专业标准，患者不太可能据此获得赔偿（选项 B 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：当商业供应商生产或销售导致原告受损的缺陷产品（而非服务）时，即产生严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）。\n\n当专业人员未达到其所在领域的合理专业人员在类似情况下应遵循的注意标准时，即产生医疗事故责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：产品责任》第 1、20 条（美国法律协会，1998 年）（关于商业供应商的责任）。\n\n《侵权法重述（第二次）》第 299A 条（美国法律协会，1965 年）（关于专业人员的注意标准）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，基于这两种理由均无法获得赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-9e420b0f92178a6f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":226,"sourceQuestionPage":678,"sourceQuestionPages":[678],"sourceExplanationPages":[679,680],"sourceAnswerPages":[679,680],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-227","subject":"Torts","topic":"Actual & Proximate Cause","question":"A cattle rancher owns property near the bottom of a steep mountain slope. A logging company recently clear-cut all of the standing timber on the slope and transported it to an out-of-state lumber processing plant. Soon thereafter, a heavy rainstorm triggered several landslides in the mountain range where the rancher's property is located. One of the landslides dumped masses of earth, rock, and debris from the cleared slope onto the rancher's property, destroying several structures and rendering much of the land unsuitable for grazing cattle. The rancher has brought an action in strict liability against the logging company. Which of the following facts, if true, best supports the logging company's defense to this action?","choices":{"A":"Commercial logging is prevalent in the area and requires specialized equipment, skills, and permits before a person can engage in this activity.","B":"New housing starts are on the rise in the community and the need for lumber is high.","C":"The heavy rainstorm triggered landslides in forested and unforested areas throughout the mountain range.","D":"The logging company conducted the work according to industry-wide safety standards."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Abnormally dangerous activity\n\nDefinition Activity is abnormally dangerous if it: is not commonly engaged in and presents foreseeable & highly significant risk of harm even when reasonable care is used Relevant Gravity of harm resulting from activity factors Inappropriateness of place where activity is conducted Limited value of activity to community A defendant engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity may be held strictly liable for personal injuries or property damage caused by the activity. Abnormally dangerous means the activity (1) is not commonly engaged in and (2) presents a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm even when reasonable care is exercised. In determining whether an activity meets these criteria, courts often consider these factors: The gravity of the harm resulting from the activity The inappropriateness of the place where the activity is being conducted The limited value of the activity to the community in which it is performed Here, although commercial logging is prevalent in the area, it can only be carried out by persons having specialized equipment, skills, and permits. This tends to show that logging is not carried out by a large percentage of the population (uncommon activity) (Choice A). And though the need for lumber is high in the community, the logging company sent all of its timber out-of-state (limited value to community) (Choice B). As a result, both of these facts weigh in favor of a finding that the logging company's activity was abnormally dangerous. However, even if that activity was abnormally dangerous, the logging company can successfully defend this action on the ground that it did not cause the rancher's property damage. The fact that the rainstorm triggered landslides in both forested and unforested areas tends to show that clear-cutting the slope was not the but-for cause of the rancher's property damage (ie, the damage would have occurred anyway). So this fact, if proved, best supports the logging company's defense to the rancher's action. (Choice D) Strict liability for an abnormally dangerous activity cannot be negated by the use of reasonable care, so the logging company's compliance with industry-wide safety standards would not affect the outcome of this action.\n\nEducational objective: In deciding whether an activity is abnormally dangerous, courts often consider (1) the gravity of the harm resulting from the activity, (2) the inappropriateness of the place where it is being conducted, and (3) its limited value to the community.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (abnormally dangerous activities).","educationalObjective":"In deciding whether an activity is abnormally dangerous, courts often consider (1) the gravity of the harm resulting from the activity, (2) the inappropriateness of the place where it is being conducted, and (3) its limited value to the community.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (abnormally dangerous activities).","rule":"In deciding whether an activity is abnormally dangerous, courts often consider (1) the gravity of the harm resulting from the activity, (2) the inappropriateness of the place where it is being conducted, and (3) its limited value to the community.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following facts, if true, best supports the logging company's defense to this action?","rule":"In deciding whether an activity is abnormally dangerous, courts often consider (1) the gravity of the harm resulting from the activity, (2) the inappropriateness of the place where it is being conducted, and (3) its limited value to the community.","analysis":"Abnormally dangerous activity\n\nDefinition Activity is abnormally dangerous if it: is not commonly engaged in and presents foreseeable & highly significant risk of harm even when reasonable care is used Relevant Gravity of harm resulting from activity factors Inappropriateness of place where activity is conducted Limited value of activity to community A defendant engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity may be held strictly liable for personal injuries or property damage caused by the activity. Abnormally dangerous means the activity (1) is not commonly engaged in and (2) presents a foreseeable and highly significant risk of physical harm even when reasonable care is exercised. In determining whether an activity meets these criteria, courts often consider these factors: The gravity of the harm resulting from the activity The inappropriateness of the place where the activity is being conducted The limited value of the activity to the community in which it is performed Here, although commercial logging is prevalent in the area, it can only be carried out by persons having specialized equipment, skills, and permits. This tends to show that logging is not carried out by a large percentage of the population (uncommon activity) (Choice A). And though the need for lumber is high in the community, the logging company sent all of its timber out-of-state (limited value to community) (Choice B). As a result, both of these facts weigh in favor of a finding that the logging company's activity was abnormally dangerous. However, even if that activity was abnormally dangerous, the logging company can successfully defend this action on the ground that it did not cause the rancher's property damage. The fact that the rainstorm triggered landslides in both forested and unforested areas tends to show that clear-cutting the slope was not the but-for cause of the rancher's property damage (ie, the damage would have occurred anyway). So this fact, if proved, best supports the logging company's defense to the rancher's action. (Choice D) Strict liability for an abnormally dangerous activity cannot be negated by the use of reasonable care, so the logging company's compliance with industry-wide safety standards would not affect the outcome of this action.","conclusion":"Answer C: The heavy rainstorm triggered landslides in forested and unforested areas throughout the mountain range."},"questionZh":"一位牧场主在陡峭山坡脚下拥有一块土地。\n\n一家伐木公司最近砍伐了山坡上的所有树木，并将其运往外州的一家木材加工厂。\n\n不久之后，一场暴雨引发了牧场主土地所在山脉的多起山体滑坡。\n\n其中一次滑坡将大量泥土、岩石和碎石从清理过的山坡倾泻到牧场主的土地上，摧毁了多处建筑物，并使大部分土地不再适合放牧。\n\n牧场主已对伐木公司提起严格责任诉讼。\n\n以下哪项事实（如果属实）最能支持伐木公司对此诉讼的辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"商业伐木在该地区很普遍，从事这项活动需要专门的设备、技能和许可证。","B":"当地新建房屋数量不断增加，对木材的需求量很大。","C":"暴雨引发了整个山脉林区和非林区发生山体滑坡。","D":"伐木公司按照行业通用的安全标准开展作业。"},"explanationZh":"异常危险活动 定义：如果某项活动（1）不属于普遍进行的活动，且（2）即使在采取合理谨慎措施的情况下仍存在可预见的、极大的伤害风险，则该活动被视为异常危险活动。相关考量因素包括：活动造成的伤害严重程度；活动进行地点的不适宜性；活动对社区的价值有限。从事异常危险活动的被告，可能需对该活动造成的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格责任。\n\n“异常危险”意味着该活动：（1）不属于普遍进行的活动；（2）即使已尽到合理注意义务，仍存在可预见的、极大的身体伤害风险。\n\n在判定某项活动是否符合这些标准时，法院通常会考虑以下因素：活动造成的伤害严重程度；活动进行地点的不适宜性；活动对其所在社区的价值有限。在本案中，尽管商业伐木在该地区很普遍，但只有具备专业设备、技能和许可证的人员才能进行此类活动。\n\n这表明伐木并非大多数人从事的活动（属于非普遍性活动）（选项 A）。\n\n此外，尽管社区对木材的需求量很大，但该伐木公司将其所有木材都运往了州外（对社区价值有限）（选项 B）。\n\n因此，这两个事实都有助于认定该伐木公司的活动属于异常危险活动。\n\n然而，即使该活动被认定为异常危险活动，伐木公司仍可成功抗辩，理由是其活动并未导致牧场主的财产损失。\n\n暴雨在有林区和无林区均引发了山体滑坡，这一事实表明，对山坡进行皆伐（彻底砍伐）并非导致牧场主财产损失的“必要原因”（即，即便不进行皆伐，损失仍会发生）。\n\n因此，如果这一事实得到证实，它将最有力地支持伐木公司针对牧场主诉讼所提出的抗辩。\n\n（选项 D）针对异常危险活动的严格责任不因采取了合理谨慎措施而被免除，因此，伐木公司遵守全行业安全标准的行为不会影响本案的判决结果。\n\n教学目标：在判定某项活动是否属于“异常危险活动”时，法院通常会考量以下因素：(1) 该活动所致损害的严重程度；(2) 活动开展地点的不适宜性；以及 (3) 该活动对社区的价值有限。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第20条（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于异常危险活动）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在判定某项活动是否异常危险时，法院通常会考虑以下因素：(1) 该活动造成的危害程度；(2) 活动地点是否适宜；(3) 该活动对社区的价值有限。","iracZh":{"issue":"下列哪项事实（如果属实）最能支持伐木公司对此案的辩护？","rule":"在判定某项活动是否异常危险时，法院通常会考虑以下因素：(1) 该活动造成的危害程度；(2) 活动地点是否适宜；(3) 该活动对社区的价值有限。","analysis":"异常危险活动 定义：如果某项活动（1）不属于普遍进行的活动，且（2）即使在采取合理谨慎措施的情况下仍存在可预见的、极大的伤害风险，则该活动被视为异常危险活动。相关考量因素包括：活动造成的伤害严重程度；活动进行地点的不适宜性；活动对社区的价值有限。从事异常危险活动的被告，可能需对该活动造成的人身伤害或财产损失承担严格责任。\n\n“异常危险”意味着该活动：（1）不属于普遍进行的活动；（2）即使已尽到合理注意义务，仍存在可预见的、极大的身体伤害风险。\n\n在判定某项活动是否符合这些标准时，法院通常会考虑以下因素：活动造成的伤害严重程度；活动进行地点的不适宜性；活动对其所在社区的价值有限。在本案中，尽管商业伐木在该地区很普遍，但只有具备专业设备、技能和许可证的人员才能进行此类活动。\n\n这表明伐木并非大多数人从事的活动（属于非普遍性活动）（选项 A）。\n\n此外，尽管社区对木材的需求量很大，但该伐木公司将其所有木材都运往了州外（对社区价值有限）（选项 B）。\n\n因此，这两个事实都有助于认定该伐木公司的活动属于异常危险活动。\n\n然而，即使该活动被认定为异常危险活动，伐木公司仍可成功抗辩，理由是其活动并未导致牧场主的财产损失。\n\n暴雨在有林区和无林区均引发了山体滑坡，这一事实表明，对山坡进行皆伐（彻底砍伐）并非导致牧场主财产损失的“必要原因”（即，即便不进行皆伐，损失仍会发生）。\n\n因此，如果这一事实得到证实，它将最有力地支持伐木公司针对牧场主诉讼所提出的抗辩。\n\n（选项 D）针对异常危险活动的严格责任不因采取了合理谨慎措施而被免除，因此，伐木公司遵守全行业安全标准的行为不会影响本案的判决结果。\n\n教学目标：在判定某项活动是否属于“异常危险活动”时，法院通常会考量以下因素：(1) 该活动所致损害的严重程度；(2) 活动开展地点的不适宜性；以及 (3) 该活动对社区的价值有限。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三次）：人身与精神损害责任》第20条（美国法律协会，2010年）（关于异常危险活动）。","conclusion":"答案 C：暴雨引发了整个山脉林区和非林区发生山体滑坡。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":227,"sourceQuestionPage":681,"sourceQuestionPages":[681],"sourceExplanationPages":[682,683],"sourceAnswerPages":[682,683],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-228","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"A movie theater brought an action against a manufacturer and a retailer based solely on strict products liability for damages suffered when the theater's $20,000 movie projector caught on fire. While the fire destroyed the projector, it caused no property damage to its surroundings and no bodily harm because the projectionist put out the fire before it could spread. The movie theater sought economic damages based on the cost of the projector, as well as lost profits for the week the theater was closed until a replacement projector could be obtained. At trial, the court ruled that because the projector had been destroyed, the theater was entitled to an inference that it was defective, which the defendants were free to rebut. The manufacturer presented evidence clearly showing that the only damage sustained by the theater was the destruction of the projector. The manufacturer and the retailer then filed a joint motion for a directed verdict. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No as to both defendants, because the court must infer that the projector was defective.","B":"No as to both defendants, because the theater is entitled to recover economic losses.","C":"Yes as to both defendants, because the theater suffered purely economic losses.","D":"Yes as to the retailer only, because the retailer was not involved in the manufacture of the projector."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A motion for a directed verdict should be granted if no reasonable jury could find in the nonmovant's favor. For example, a defendant's motion should be granted if there is insufficient evidence to support any element of the plaintiff's claim. A strict products liability claim requires proof of the following elements: The defendant(s) were commercial sellers (eg, manufacturer, retailer—as seen here). The product was defective at the time it left the defendant's control. The defect caused the plaintiff physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage). As a result, claims for purely economic loss are not allowed. In products liability cases, purely economic loss includes any harm to the defective product itself as well as consequential damages arising therefrom (eg, lost profits). Here, the movie theater is only seeking to recover damages based on the cost of the defective projector and lost profits—both of which are purely economic loss. Therefore, the theater has not suffered adequate damages to recover under its strict products liability claim, and the motion for a directed verdict should be granted as to both defendants. (Choice A) Although the theater was entitled to an inference that the projector was defective, the theater cannot recover for its purely economic losses. And since the theater failed to allege property damage or personal injury, the court should grant the defendants' motion for a directed verdict. (Choice B) The movie theater may have been entitled to recover economic losses had they stemmed from bodily harm or damage to property other than the defective product itself (not seen here). (Choice D) Commercial sellers are subject to strict liability for a defective product even if they were not responsible for the defect in any way. Accordingly, the retailer cannot escape liability on the basis that it was not involved in the manufacture of the projector.\n\nEducational objective: Strict products liability claims require proof of physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage). Claims for purely economic loss, including harm to the defective product itself and consequential damages arising therefrom, are not allowed.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (economic loss).","educationalObjective":"Strict products liability claims require proof of physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage). Claims for purely economic loss, including harm to the defective product itself and consequential damages arising therefrom, are not allowed.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Prods. Liab. § 21 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) (economic loss).","rule":"Strict products liability claims require proof of physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage). Claims for purely economic loss, including harm to the defective product itself and consequential damages arising therefrom, are not allowed.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"Strict products liability claims require proof of physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage). Claims for purely economic loss, including harm to the defective product itself and consequential damages arising therefrom, are not allowed.","analysis":"A motion for a directed verdict should be granted if no reasonable jury could find in the nonmovant's favor. For example, a defendant's motion should be granted if there is insufficient evidence to support any element of the plaintiff's claim. A strict products liability claim requires proof of the following elements: The defendant(s) were commercial sellers (eg, manufacturer, retailer—as seen here). The product was defective at the time it left the defendant's control. The defect caused the plaintiff physical harm (ie, bodily harm or property damage). As a result, claims for purely economic loss are not allowed. In products liability cases, purely economic loss includes any harm to the defective product itself as well as consequential damages arising therefrom (eg, lost profits). Here, the movie theater is only seeking to recover damages based on the cost of the defective projector and lost profits—both of which are purely economic loss. Therefore, the theater has not suffered adequate damages to recover under its strict products liability claim, and the motion for a directed verdict should be granted as to both defendants. (Choice A) Although the theater was entitled to an inference that the projector was defective, the theater cannot recover for its purely economic losses. And since the theater failed to allege property damage or personal injury, the court should grant the defendants' motion for a directed verdict. (Choice B) The movie theater may have been entitled to recover economic losses had they stemmed from bodily harm or damage to property other than the defective product itself (not seen here). (Choice D) Commercial sellers are subject to strict liability for a defective product even if they were not responsible for the defect in any way. Accordingly, the retailer cannot escape liability on the basis that it was not involved in the manufacture of the projector.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes as to both defendants, because the theater suffered purely economic losses."},"questionZh":"一家电影院以严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）为由，起诉了一家制造商和一家零售商，指控其价值 2 万美元的电影放映机起火，造成影院损失。\n\n虽然放映机被烧毁，但由于放映员及时扑灭了火势，并未对周围环境造成财产损失或人员伤亡。\n\n电影院要求赔偿经济损失，赔偿范围包括放映机的成本以及因停业一周而造成的利润损失（直至找到替代放映机）。\n\n庭审中，法院裁定，由于放映机已被烧毁，影院有权推定放映机存在缺陷，被告可以对此进行反驳。\n\n制造商提供了证据，清楚地表明影院遭受的唯一损失是放映机的损毁。\n\n随后，制造商和零售商共同提出动议，请求法院作出指示性判决。\n\n法院是否应该批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"对于两名被告而言，答案是否定的，因为法院必须推定放映机存在缺陷。","B":"对于两名被告，均不予受理，因为影院有权获得经济损失赔偿。","C":"对于两名被告，均予受理，因为影院遭受的损失纯粹是经济损失。","D":"仅对于零售商，均予受理，因为零售商并未参与投影机的生产。"},"explanationZh":"如果没有任何理性的陪审团能够做出有利于非动议方（即未提出动议的一方）的裁决，则应准予关于作出指示性裁决（directed verdict）的动议。\n\n例如，如果证据不足以支持原告主张的任何构成要件，则应准予被告提出的动议。\n\n严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔要求证明以下要件：被告属于商业销售者（例如制造商或零售商——本案即属此类）；产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷；该缺陷导致原告遭受人身损害（即身体伤害或财产损失）。\n\n因此，不允许就纯粹经济损失提出索赔。\n\n在产品责任案件中，纯粹经济损失包括对缺陷产品本身的损害以及由此产生的后果性损害（例如利润损失）。\n\n在本案中，电影院仅寻求就缺陷放映机的成本和利润损失获得赔偿——这两者均属于纯粹经济损失。\n\n因此，该电影院未遭受足以支持其严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔的损害，法院应针对两名被告准予作出指示性裁决的动议。\n\n（选项 A）尽管电影院有权基于推断认定放映机存在缺陷，但其无法就纯粹经济损失获得赔偿。\n\n且由于电影院未能主张存在财产损失或人身伤害，法院应准予被告提出的指示性裁决动议。\n\n（选项 B）如果经济损失源于身体伤害或对缺陷产品本身以外的财产造成的损害（本案未涉及此类情况），电影院或许有权获得经济损失赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）即使商业销售者对产品缺陷不负任何责任，仍须对缺陷产品承担严格责任。\n\n因此，零售商不能以其未参与放映机制造为由免除责任。\n\n教育目标：严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔要求证明存在人身损害（即身体伤害或财产损失）。\n\n不允许就纯粹经济损失（包括对缺陷产品本身的损害及由此产生的后果性损害）提出索赔。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 21 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（关于经济损失的规定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔需要证明存在人身伤害（即身体伤害或财产损失）。\n\n纯粹经济损失的索赔，包括缺陷产品本身的损害以及由此产生的间接损失，均不予受理。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应准许该动议？","rule":"严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔需要证明存在人身伤害（即身体伤害或财产损失）。\n\n纯粹经济损失的索赔，包括缺陷产品本身的损害以及由此产生的间接损失，均不予受理。","analysis":"如果没有任何理性的陪审团能够做出有利于非动议方（即未提出动议的一方）的裁决，则应准予关于作出指示性裁决（directed verdict）的动议。\n\n例如，如果证据不足以支持原告主张的任何构成要件，则应准予被告提出的动议。\n\n严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔要求证明以下要件：被告属于商业销售者（例如制造商或零售商——本案即属此类）；产品在脱离被告控制时存在缺陷；该缺陷导致原告遭受人身损害（即身体伤害或财产损失）。\n\n因此，不允许就纯粹经济损失提出索赔。\n\n在产品责任案件中，纯粹经济损失包括对缺陷产品本身的损害以及由此产生的后果性损害（例如利润损失）。\n\n在本案中，电影院仅寻求就缺陷放映机的成本和利润损失获得赔偿——这两者均属于纯粹经济损失。\n\n因此，该电影院未遭受足以支持其严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔的损害，法院应针对两名被告准予作出指示性裁决的动议。\n\n（选项 A）尽管电影院有权基于推断认定放映机存在缺陷，但其无法就纯粹经济损失获得赔偿。\n\n且由于电影院未能主张存在财产损失或人身伤害，法院应准予被告提出的指示性裁决动议。\n\n（选项 B）如果经济损失源于身体伤害或对缺陷产品本身以外的财产造成的损害（本案未涉及此类情况），电影院或许有权获得经济损失赔偿。\n\n（选项 D）即使商业销售者对产品缺陷不负任何责任，仍须对缺陷产品承担严格责任。\n\n因此，零售商不能以其未参与放映机制造为由免除责任。\n\n教育目标：严格产品责任（strict products liability）（strict products liability）索赔要求证明存在人身损害（即身体伤害或财产损失）。\n\n不允许就纯粹经济损失（包括对缺陷产品本身的损害及由此产生的后果性损害）提出索赔。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：产品责任》第 21 条 (Am. Law Inst. 1998)（关于经济损失的规定）。","conclusion":"答案 C：对于两名被告，均予受理，因为影院遭受的损失纯粹是经济损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-c50f0b5fe933495a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":228,"sourceQuestionPage":684,"sourceQuestionPages":[684],"sourceExplanationPages":[685,686],"sourceAnswerPages":[685,686],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-torts-229","subject":"Torts","topic":"Products Liability","question":"An amusement park purchased an oversized caricature mask from a manufacturer. One of the amusement park's employees was wearing the mask when she was accidentally tripped by a customer. The employee fell, injuring her neck from the strain caused by the weight of the mask. The employee filed a worker's compensation claim but only received limited compensation that did not cover the full cost of treating her injury. The employee brought a negligence suit against the manufacturer and has established that, in designing the mask, the manufacturer failed to include a safety harness that would protect the wearer's neck from strain caused by a fall. In its defense, the manufacturer established that the mask's design complied with all government standards for flammability and composition, and that those standards did not require the masks to have safety harnesses. Is the employee likely to be awarded damages from the manufacturer?","choices":{"A":"No, because the employee's injury was caused by an intervening negligent act.","B":"No, because the manufacturer's compliance with safety standards is conclusive evidence that it was not negligent.","C":"Yes, because negligent intervening acts are generally regarded as foreseeable.","D":"Yes, because the manufacturer was a commercial seller of the mask."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A plaintiff harmed by a defective product can prevail in a negligence suit against a manufacturer (or other seller) by proving that the manufacturer's failure to use reasonable care actually and proximately caused the plaintiff's harm. An intervening act or event that contributed to the plaintiff's harm will not break the chain of causation and relieve the manufacturer from liability if that act or event was reasonably foreseeable.* And, as a general rule, negligent intervening acts are regarded as foreseeable. Here, the employee sued the manufacturer of the mask after she fell and injured her neck from the strain caused by the weight of the mask. The manufacturer likely failed to use reasonable care in designing the mask without a safety harness to prevent this type of injury. And though a customer accidentally tripped the employee, causing her to fall, that intervening negligent act is regarded as foreseeable. Therefore, it did not cut off the manufacturer's liability, and the employee is likely to be awarded damages from the manufacturer (Choice A). *By contrast, intervening acts or events that were NOT reasonably foreseeable become superseding causes that break the chain of causation between the defendant's tortious conduct and the plaintiff's harm. (Choice B) Compliance with governmental safety standards is not conclusive evidence that a product was not defective or that the manufacturer was not negligent. However, a jury may consider whether a product complied with governmental safety standards when determining these issues. (Choice D) Unlike a products liability action based in strict liability, an action based in negligence can be brought against a commercial or noncommercial seller. Therefore, the fact that the manufacturer was a commercial seller of the mask is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: A products liability claim based in negligence requires proof that the defendant's (eg, manufacturer's) failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff's harm. An intervening act or event that contributed to the plaintiff's harm will NOT cut off the defendant's liablity if the act or event was reasonably foreseeable (eg, a negligent intervening act).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 6 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence liability).","educationalObjective":"A products liability claim based in negligence requires proof that the defendant's (eg, manufacturer's) failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff's harm. An intervening act or event that contributed to the plaintiff's harm will NOT cut off the defendant's liablity if the act or event was reasonably foreseeable (eg, a negligent intervening act).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liab. for Physical & Emotional Harm § 6 (Am. Law Inst. 2010) (negligence liability).","rule":"A products liability claim based in negligence requires proof that the defendant's (eg, manufacturer's) failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff's harm. An intervening act or event that contributed to the plaintiff's harm will NOT cut off the defendant's liablity if the act or event was reasonably foreseeable (eg, a negligent intervening act).","irac":{"issue":"Is the employee likely to be awarded damages from the manufacturer?","rule":"A products liability claim based in negligence requires proof that the defendant's (eg, manufacturer's) failure to use reasonable care caused the plaintiff's harm. An intervening act or event that contributed to the plaintiff's harm will NOT cut off the defendant's liablity if the act or event was reasonably foreseeable (eg, a negligent intervening act).","analysis":"A plaintiff harmed by a defective product can prevail in a negligence suit against a manufacturer (or other seller) by proving that the manufacturer's failure to use reasonable care actually and proximately caused the plaintiff's harm. An intervening act or event that contributed to the plaintiff's harm will not break the chain of causation and relieve the manufacturer from liability if that act or event was reasonably foreseeable.* And, as a general rule, negligent intervening acts are regarded as foreseeable. Here, the employee sued the manufacturer of the mask after she fell and injured her neck from the strain caused by the weight of the mask. The manufacturer likely failed to use reasonable care in designing the mask without a safety harness to prevent this type of injury. And though a customer accidentally tripped the employee, causing her to fall, that intervening negligent act is regarded as foreseeable. Therefore, it did not cut off the manufacturer's liability, and the employee is likely to be awarded damages from the manufacturer (Choice A). *By contrast, intervening acts or events that were NOT reasonably foreseeable become superseding causes that break the chain of causation between the defendant's tortious conduct and the plaintiff's harm. (Choice B) Compliance with governmental safety standards is not conclusive evidence that a product was not defective or that the manufacturer was not negligent. However, a jury may consider whether a product complied with governmental safety standards when determining these issues. (Choice D) Unlike a products liability action based in strict liability, an action based in negligence can be brought against a commercial or noncommercial seller. Therefore, the fact that the manufacturer was a commercial seller of the mask is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because negligent intervening acts are generally regarded as foreseeable."},"questionZh":"一家游乐园从制造商处购入了一个超大型的夸张造型面具。\n\n一名员工在佩戴该面具时，不慎被一名顾客绊倒。\n\n该员工摔倒，因面具重量造成的拉力导致颈部受伤。\n\n该员工提出了工伤赔偿申请，但仅获得了有限的赔偿，不足以支付全部治疗费用。\n\n该员工针对制造商提起了过失诉讼，并已证实：制造商在设计该面具时，未配备能保护佩戴者颈部免受跌倒时拉力伤害的安全固定装置。\n\n制造商在抗辩中指出，该面具的设计符合政府关于易燃性和材质成分的所有标准，且这些标准并未要求面具必须配备安全固定装置。\n\n该员工是否很可能获得制造商的损害赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该员工的伤害是由介入性过失行为造成的。","B":"不会，因为制造商符合安全标准这一事实是其不存在过失的确凿证据。","C":"会，因为介入性过失行为通常被视为可预见的。","D":"会，因为制造商是该面具的商业销售商。"},"explanationZh":"因缺陷产品而受害的原告，若能证明制造商（或其他销售商）未尽合理注意义务，且该疏忽行为实际上并直接导致了原告的伤害，便可在针对制造商的过失诉讼中胜诉。\n\n如果导致原告伤害的介入行为或事件属于“合理可预见”的范畴，则该行为或事件不会中断因果关系链，也不会免除制造商的责任*；且通常情况下，介入的过失行为被视为可预见的。\n\n在本案中，一名员工因佩戴口罩，其重量造成的拉力导致她摔倒并伤及颈部，随后她起诉了口罩制造商。\n\n制造商在设计口罩时未配备防止此类伤害的安全固定装置，这很可能构成未尽合理注意义务。\n\n尽管有一名顾客意外绊倒了该员工导致其摔倒，但这种介入的过失行为被视为可预见的。\n\n因此，该行为并未切断制造商的责任，该员工很可能获得制造商的损害赔偿（选项 A）。\n\n*相比之下，若介入行为或事件并非“合理可预见”，则会构成“阻断原因”（superseding cause），从而切断被告侵权行为与原告伤害之间的因果关系链。\n\n（选项 B）符合政府安全标准并不能作为产品无缺陷或制造商无过失的决定性证据。\n\n不过，陪审团在裁决这些问题时，可以考量产品是否符合政府安全标准。\n\n（选项 D）与基于严格责任的产品责任诉讼不同，基于过失的诉讼既可针对商业销售商，也可针对非商业销售商提起。\n\n因此，制造商作为口罩的商业销售商这一事实并不影响诉讼结果。\n\n教育目标：基于过失的产品责任索赔要求证明被告（如制造商）未尽合理注意义务，从而导致了原告的伤害。\n\n如果导致原告伤害的介入行为或事件属于合理可预见（例如介入的过失行为），则该行为或事件不会切断被告的责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 6 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（过失责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于过失的产品责任索赔要求证明被告（例如制造商）未尽合理注意义务，从而导致了原告的伤害。\n\n如果导致原告伤害的介入行为或事件是合理可预见的（例如介入性过失行为），则该行为或事件不会免除被告的责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"该员工是否很可能获得制造商的损害赔偿？","rule":"基于过失的产品责任索赔要求证明被告（例如制造商）未尽合理注意义务，从而导致了原告的伤害。\n\n如果导致原告伤害的介入行为或事件是合理可预见的（例如介入性过失行为），则该行为或事件不会免除被告的责任。","analysis":"因缺陷产品而受害的原告，若能证明制造商（或其他销售商）未尽合理注意义务，且该疏忽行为实际上并直接导致了原告的伤害，便可在针对制造商的过失诉讼中胜诉。\n\n如果导致原告伤害的介入行为或事件属于“合理可预见”的范畴，则该行为或事件不会中断因果关系链，也不会免除制造商的责任*；且通常情况下，介入的过失行为被视为可预见的。\n\n在本案中，一名员工因佩戴口罩，其重量造成的拉力导致她摔倒并伤及颈部，随后她起诉了口罩制造商。\n\n制造商在设计口罩时未配备防止此类伤害的安全固定装置，这很可能构成未尽合理注意义务。\n\n尽管有一名顾客意外绊倒了该员工导致其摔倒，但这种介入的过失行为被视为可预见的。\n\n因此，该行为并未切断制造商的责任，该员工很可能获得制造商的损害赔偿（选项 A）。\n\n*相比之下，若介入行为或事件并非“合理可预见”，则会构成“阻断原因”（superseding cause），从而切断被告侵权行为与原告伤害之间的因果关系链。\n\n（选项 B）符合政府安全标准并不能作为产品无缺陷或制造商无过失的决定性证据。\n\n不过，陪审团在裁决这些问题时，可以考量产品是否符合政府安全标准。\n\n（选项 D）与基于严格责任的产品责任诉讼不同，基于过失的诉讼既可针对商业销售商，也可针对非商业销售商提起。\n\n因此，制造商作为口罩的商业销售商这一事实并不影响诉讼结果。\n\n教育目标：基于过失的产品责任索赔要求证明被告（如制造商）未尽合理注意义务，从而导致了原告的伤害。\n\n如果导致原告伤害的介入行为或事件属于合理可预见（例如介入的过失行为），则该行为或事件不会切断被告的责任。\n\n参考文献 《侵权法重述（第三版）：人身与精神伤害责任》第 6 条 (Am. Law Inst. 2010)（过失责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：会，因为介入性过失行为通常被视为可预见的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Torts 考试范围复核普通法侵权规则、抗辩与损害赔偿框架；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Torts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"designing","ipa":"/dɪzˈaɪnɪŋ/","syllables":"de·sign·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 阴谋的, 诡诈的；n. 设计(工作), 计谋","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题侵权法语境中的“designing”","wordForm":"词；词形 0:design/1:i/i:designing","sourceSentence":"The employee brought a negligence suit against the manufacturer and has established that, in designing the mask, the manufacturer failed to include a safety harness that would protect the wearer's neck from strain caused by a fall.","synonyms":["design","scheming"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["torts-chart-62019772e0bdbfce"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Torts(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":229,"sourceQuestionPage":687,"sourceQuestionPages":[687],"sourceExplanationPages":[688,689],"sourceAnswerPages":[688,689],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-001","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A seller and a buyer signed a contract of sale for improved real property. The contract contained a financing contingency for a certain percentage of the purchase price. The buyer obtained the requisite financing from a bank. At the closing, the buyer executed a note to the seller for a portion of the purchase price, which note was not secured by a mortgage. The buyer then executed a second note, secured by a mortgage to the bank, applying the bank loan proceeds to the purchase price of the property. The bank had actual knowledge of the prior note to the seller. The bank promptly recorded its mortgage. The buyer is now in default on both notes. There is no applicable statute. Which party has priority?","choices":{"A":"The bank, because its loan satisfied the financing contingency in the contract of sale.","B":"The bank, because its note is secured by a purchase-money mortgage.","C":"The seller, because he retained a vendor's lien that was first in time.","D":"The seller, because the bank had actual knowledge of the seller's note."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The seller received a promissory note—ie, a legal document that creates or memorializes a debt—but did not take a mortgage or other lien to secure the debt. However, when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid (as seen here), an equitable vendor's lien is implied by law. Subsequently, the bank received a promissory note secured by a mortgage—ie, a lien against real property given to secure a debt. Since the mortgage was given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, it is a purchase-money mortgage (PMM). PMMs have super priority over all other liens (whether the PMM or those liens are recorded or not). Therefore, the bank's mortgage has priority. This is true regardless of whether it knew of the seller's lien (Choices C & D). (Choice A) The fact that the bank's loan satisfied the financing contingency in the contract of sale between the seller and buyer does not give the bank priority. Instead, it has priority because its note is secured by a PMM.\n\nEducational objective: Equitable vendor's liens are implied by law when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid. Purchase-money mortgages are created when a mortgage is given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, thereby giving them super priority over all other mortgages and liens.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (priority of purchase-money mortgages).","educationalObjective":"Equitable vendor's liens are implied by law when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid. Purchase-money mortgages are created when a mortgage is given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, thereby giving them super priority over all other mortgages and liens.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (priority of purchase-money mortgages).","rule":"Equitable vendor's liens are implied by law when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid. Purchase-money mortgages are created when a mortgage is given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, thereby giving them super priority over all other mortgages and liens.","irac":{"issue":"Which party has priority?","rule":"Equitable vendor's liens are implied by law when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid. Purchase-money mortgages are created when a mortgage is given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, thereby giving them super priority over all other mortgages and liens.","analysis":"The seller received a promissory note—ie, a legal document that creates or memorializes a debt—but did not take a mortgage or other lien to secure the debt. However, when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid (as seen here), an equitable vendor's lien is implied by law. Subsequently, the bank received a promissory note secured by a mortgage—ie, a lien against real property given to secure a debt. Since the mortgage was given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, it is a purchase-money mortgage (PMM). PMMs have super priority over all other liens (whether the PMM or those liens are recorded or not). Therefore, the bank's mortgage has priority. This is true regardless of whether it knew of the seller's lien (Choices C & D). (Choice A) The fact that the bank's loan satisfied the financing contingency in the contract of sale between the seller and buyer does not give the bank priority. Instead, it has priority because its note is secured by a PMM.","conclusion":"Answer B: The bank, because its note is secured by a purchase-money mortgage."},"questionZh":"卖方和买方签署了一份改善房地产的销售合同。该合同包含购买价格一定比例的融资意外费用。买方从银行获得了必要的融资。成交时，买方向卖方签署了一份票据，支付部分购买价格，该票据没有抵押担保。然后，买方签署了第二张票据，以银行抵押贷款为担保，将银行贷款收益用于购买房产的价格。银行实际知道之前给卖方的票据。银行立即记录了其抵押贷款。买方现在对两张票据都违约。没有适用的法规。哪一方有优先权？","choicesZh":{"A":"银行，因为其贷款满足了销售合同中的融资意外事件。","B":"银行，因为它的票据是由购房抵押贷款担保的。","C":"卖方，因为他及时保留了卖方留置权。","D":"卖方，因为银行实际了解卖方的票据。"},"explanationZh":"卖方收到了本票——即创造或纪念债务的法律文件——但没有采取抵押或其他留置权来担保债务。然而，当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价格的任何部分仍未支付时（如此处所示），法律暗示了衡平法上卖方的留置权。随后，银行收到了一张由抵押贷款担保的本票，即为担保债务而给予的不动产留置权。由于抵押贷款是为了保证抵押财产的购买价格，因此它是购房款抵押贷款（PMM）。 PMM 比所有其他留置权拥有超级优先权（无论 PMM 或这些留置权是否已记录）。因此，银行的抵押贷款具有优先权。无论其是否知道卖方的留置权（选择 C ​​和 D），情况都是如此。 （选择A）银行贷款满足了买卖双方销售合同中的融资意外事件的事实并不赋予银行优先权。相反，它具有优先权，因为它的票据受到 PMM 的保护。\n\n教育目标：当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价的任何部分仍未支付时，法律暗示了公平的卖方留置权。当提供抵押贷款以确保抵押财产的购买价格时，就创建了购房款抵押贷款，从而使它们比所有其他抵押贷款和留置权具有超级优先权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（购房款抵押贷款的优先权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价的任何部分仍未支付时，法律暗示了衡平法卖方的留置权。当提供抵押贷款以确保抵押财产的购买价格时，就创建了购房款抵押贷款，从而使它们比所有其他抵押贷款和留置权具有超级优先权。","iracZh":{"issue":"哪一方有优先权？","rule":"当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价的任何部分仍未支付时，法律暗示了衡平法卖方的留置权。当提供抵押贷款以确保抵押财产的购买价格时，就创建了购房款抵押贷款，从而使它们比所有其他抵押贷款和留置权具有超级优先权。","analysis":"卖方收到了本票——即创造或纪念债务的法律文件——但没有采取抵押或其他留置权来担保债务。然而，当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价格的任何部分仍未支付时（如此处所示），法律暗示了衡平法上卖方的留置权。随后，银行收到了一张由抵押贷款担保的本票，即为担保债务而给予的不动产留置权。由于抵押贷款是为了保证抵押财产的购买价格，因此它是购房款抵押贷款（PMM）。 PMM 比所有其他留置权拥有超级优先权（无论 PMM 或这些留置权是否已记录）。因此，银行的抵押贷款具有优先权。无论其是否知道卖方的留置权（选择 C ​​和 D），情况都是如此。 （选择A）银行贷款满足了买卖双方销售合同中的融资意外事件的事实并不赋予银行优先权。相反，它具有优先权，因为它的票据受到 PMM 的保护。\n\n教育目标：当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价的任何部分仍未支付时，法律暗示了公平的卖方留置权。当提供抵押贷款以确保抵押财产的购买价格时，就创建了购房款抵押贷款，从而使它们比所有其他抵押贷款和留置权具有超级优先权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（购房款抵押贷款的优先权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：银行，因为它的票据是由购房抵押贷款担保的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"equitable vendor's lien","ipa":"/ˈɛkwətəbəl vˈɛndɝz lˈin/","syllables":"eq·ui·table ven·dor's lien","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"卖方衡平留置权：卖方过户后未收清价款时法律默示的担保权益","contextualMeaning":"卖方衡平留置权：卖方过户后未收清价款时法律默示的担保权益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Equitable vendor's liens are implied by law when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"mortgage","ipa":"/mˈɔɹɡədʒ/","syllables":"mort·gage","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 抵押, 约束性义务, 抵押借款；vt. 抵押, 以...作担保, 把...许给","contextualMeaning":"抵押：以不动产担保债务履行的担保权益","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:mortgages/d:mortgaged/i:mortgaging/p:mortgaged/3:mortgages","sourceSentence":"At the closing, the buyer executed a note to the seller for a portion of the purchase price, which note was not secured by a mortgage.","synonyms":["security interest in land"]},{"term":"recorded","ipa":"/ɹʌkˈɔɹdɪd/","syllables":"recorded","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 记录的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“recorded”","wordForm":"形容词；原形 record；词形 0:record/1:dp/p:recorded/d:recorded","sourceSentence":"The bank promptly recorded its mortgage.","synonyms":["track record","phonograph record","phonograph recording","disk"]},{"term":"purchase","ipa":"/pˈɝtʃəs/","syllables":"pur·chase","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 购买, 购买品, 紧握, 绞辘；vt. 购买, 赢得, 努力取得, 用滑轮起(锚等)","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“purchase”","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:purchased/s:purchases/p:purchased/i:purchasing/3:purchases","sourceSentence":"The contract contained a financing contingency for a certain percentage of the purchase price.","synonyms":["leverage","buy"]},{"term":"priority","ipa":"/pɹaɪˈɔɹəti/","syllables":"pri·or·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 优先权, 优先；[计] 优先级","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“priority”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:priorities","sourceSentence":"Which party has priority?","synonyms":["antecedence","antecedency","anteriority","precedence"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-a5b820bdb2830699"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":1,"sourceQuestionPage":1,"sourceQuestionPages":[1],"sourceExplanationPages":[2,3],"sourceAnswerPages":[2,3],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-002","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man owned property that he used as his residence. The man received a loan, secured by a mortgage on the property, from a bank. Later, the man defaulted on the loan. The bank then brought an appropriate action to foreclose the mortgage, was the sole bidder at the judicial sale, and received title to the property as a result of the foreclosure sale. Shortly after the foreclosure sale, the man received a substantial inheritance. He approached the bank to repurchase the property, but the bank had decided to build a branch office on the property and declined to sell. If the man prevails in an appropriate action to recover title to the property, what will be the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"He had used the property as his residence.","B":"He timely exercised an equitable right of redemption.","C":"The court applied the doctrine of exoneration.","D":"The jurisdiction provides a statutory right of redemption."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Right of redemption\n\n(regaining title after default on loan)\n\nTiming Assertion of right\n\nEquitable At any time prior to foreclosure sale Pay full amount of debt due right (including interest) to lender\n\n(always exists)\n\nStatutory Statutory period after property has Pay foreclosure sale price to right been sold in foreclosure purchasing party\n\n(if statute exists)\n\nWhen a debtor defaults on mortgage payments, the creditor may initiate foreclosure proceedings to force the sale of the mortgaged property to satisfy the unpaid debt. Once the property is sold, jurisdictions with a statutory right of redemption allow the original debtor to reacquire ownership of the property by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchasing party—even if that party is the creditor—within a designated time. Therefore, the man will successfully redeem his property from the bank if the jurisdiction provides this statutory right. (Choice A) Unless otherwise noted, the statutory right of redemption applies to any real property—not just residential property. (Choice B) The equitable right of redemption allows a debtor to reclaim mortgaged property before foreclosure by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt. Therefore, the man did not timely exercise this right by attempting to repurchase the property after foreclosure. (Choice C) The doctrine of exoneration allows a will beneficiary to use the estate's general assets to pay off any encumbrances (eg, mortgages) on the devised property. But since the man did not receive devised mortgaged property (he placed the mortgage on the property himself), this doctrine does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: The statutory right of redemption allows a debtor to reacquire ownership of property after foreclosure by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchasing party within the designated statutory period.\n\nReferences\n30 Am. Jur. 2d Executions and Enforcement of Judgments § 291 (2018) (statutory right of redemption).","educationalObjective":"The statutory right of redemption allows a debtor to reacquire ownership of property after foreclosure by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchasing party within the designated statutory period.","references":"30 Am. Jur. 2d Executions and Enforcement of Judgments § 291 (2018) (statutory right of redemption).","rule":"The statutory right of redemption allows a debtor to reacquire ownership of property after foreclosure by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchasing party within the designated statutory period.","irac":{"issue":"If the man prevails in an appropriate action to recover title to the property, what will be the most likely reason?","rule":"The statutory right of redemption allows a debtor to reacquire ownership of property after foreclosure by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchasing party within the designated statutory period.","analysis":"Right of redemption\n\n(regaining title after default on loan)\n\nTiming Assertion of right\n\nEquitable At any time prior to foreclosure sale Pay full amount of debt due right (including interest) to lender\n\n(always exists)\n\nStatutory Statutory period after property has Pay foreclosure sale price to right been sold in foreclosure purchasing party\n\n(if statute exists)\n\nWhen a debtor defaults on mortgage payments, the creditor may initiate foreclosure proceedings to force the sale of the mortgaged property to satisfy the unpaid debt. Once the property is sold, jurisdictions with a statutory right of redemption allow the original debtor to reacquire ownership of the property by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchasing party—even if that party is the creditor—within a designated time. Therefore, the man will successfully redeem his property from the bank if the jurisdiction provides this statutory right. (Choice A) Unless otherwise noted, the statutory right of redemption applies to any real property—not just residential property. (Choice B) The equitable right of redemption allows a debtor to reclaim mortgaged property before foreclosure by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt. Therefore, the man did not timely exercise this right by attempting to repurchase the property after foreclosure. (Choice C) The doctrine of exoneration allows a will beneficiary to use the estate's general assets to pay off any encumbrances (eg, mortgages) on the devised property. But since the man did not receive devised mortgaged property (he placed the mortgage on the property himself), this doctrine does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer D: The jurisdiction provides a statutory right of redemption."},"questionZh":"一名男子拥有用作住所的财产。该男子从银行获得了一笔以房产抵押作为担保的贷款。后来，该男子拖欠贷款。随后，该银行采取了适当的行动，取消了抵押贷款的赎回权，成为司法出售中的唯一投标人，并因取消抵押品赎回权的出售而获得了该财产的所有权。丧失抵押品赎回权出售后不久，该男子就获得了一大笔遗产。他向银行提出回购该房产，但银行决定在该房产上建立分行，并拒绝出售。如果该男子在恢复财产所有权的适当诉讼中获胜，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"他已将该房产用作他的住所。","B":"他及时行使了公平的赎回权。","C":"法院适用了免责原则。","D":"司法管辖区提供法定的赎回权。"},"explanationZh":"赎回权\n\n（贷款违约后恢复所有权）\n\n权利主张的时机\n\n公平 在取消抵押品赎回权出售之前的任何时间 向贷款人支付全额债务到期权利（包括利息）\n\n（始终存在）\n\n法定期限 财产支付止赎出售价格以在止赎购买方出售权利后的法定期限\n\n（如果有法规的话）\n\n当债务人拖欠抵押付款时，债权人可以启动止赎程序，强制出售抵押财产以偿还未偿债务。一旦财产被出售，具有法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）的司法管辖区允许原始债务人在指定时间内向购买方（即使该方是债权人）支付止赎价，从而重新获得财产的所有权。因此，如果司法管辖区提供了这项法定权利，该男子将成功从银行赎回其财产。 （选项 A）除非另有说明，法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）适用于任何不动产，而不仅仅是住宅房产。 （选择B）衡平法上的赎回权允许债务人在丧失抵押品赎回权之前通过全额支付未偿债务来收回抵押财产。因此，该男子没有及时行使这项权利，试图在取消抵押品赎回权后回购房产。 （选择C） 免除原则允许遗嘱受益人使用遗产的一般资产来偿还所设计财产的任何产权负担（例如抵押）。但由于该男子并未收到设计抵押财产（他自己将该财产抵押），因此该原则不适用。\n\n教育目标：法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）允许债务人在丧失抵押品赎回权后通过在指定的法定期限内向购买方支付丧失抵押品赎回权的销售价格来重新获得财产的所有权。\n\n参考文献\n上午 30 点。尤尔。 2d 判决的执行和执行 § 291 (2018)（法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）允许债务人在丧失抵押品赎回权后通过在指定的法定期限内向购买方支付丧失抵押品赎回权的销售价格来重新获得财产的所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该男子在恢复财产所有权的适当诉讼中获胜，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）允许债务人在丧失抵押品赎回权后通过在指定的法定期限内向购买方支付丧失抵押品赎回权的销售价格来重新获得财产的所有权。","analysis":"赎回权\n\n（贷款违约后恢复所有权）\n\n权利主张的时机\n\n公平 在取消抵押品赎回权出售之前的任何时间 向贷款人支付全额债务到期权利（包括利息）\n\n（始终存在）\n\n法定期限 财产支付止赎出售价格以在止赎购买方出售权利后的法定期限\n\n（如果有法规的话）\n\n当债务人拖欠抵押付款时，债权人可以启动止赎程序，强制出售抵押财产以偿还未偿债务。一旦财产被出售，具有法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）的司法管辖区允许原始债务人在指定时间内向购买方（即使该方是债权人）支付止赎价，从而重新获得财产的所有权。因此，如果司法管辖区提供了这项法定权利，该男子将成功从银行赎回其财产。 （选项 A）除非另有说明，法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）适用于任何不动产，而不仅仅是住宅房产。 （选择B）衡平法上的赎回权允许债务人在丧失抵押品赎回权之前通过全额支付未偿债务来收回抵押财产。因此，该男子没有及时行使这项权利，试图在取消抵押品赎回权后回购房产。 （选择C） 免除原则允许遗嘱受益人使用遗产的一般资产来偿还所设计财产的任何产权负担（例如抵押）。但由于该男子并未收到设计抵押财产（他自己将该财产抵押），因此该原则不适用。\n\n教育目标：法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）允许债务人在丧失抵押品赎回权后通过在指定的法定期限内向购买方支付丧失抵押品赎回权的销售价格来重新获得财产的所有权。\n\n参考文献\n上午 30 点。尤尔。 2d 判决的执行和执行 § 291 (2018)（法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption））。","conclusion":"答案 D：司法管辖区提供法定的赎回权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"equitable right of redemption","ipa":"/ˈɛkwətəbəl ɹˈaɪt ˈəv ɹɪdˈɛmpʃən/","syllables":"eq·ui·table right of re·demp·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"衡平赎回权：止赎拍卖前清偿全部到期债务以保住土地的权利","contextualMeaning":"衡平赎回权：止赎拍卖前清偿全部到期债务以保住土地的权利","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"He timely exercised an equitable right of redemption.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"statutory right of redemption","ipa":"/stˈætʃʌtˌɔɹi ɹˈaɪt ˈəv ɹɪdˈɛmpʃən/","syllables":"statu·tory right of re·demp·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"法定赎回权：部分州允许债务人在止赎拍卖后于法定期限内赎回土地","contextualMeaning":"法定赎回权：部分州允许债务人在止赎拍卖后于法定期限内赎回土地","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The jurisdiction provides a statutory right of redemption.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"foreclosure sale","ipa":"/fɔɹklˈoʊʒɝ sˈeɪl/","syllables":"fore·clo·sure sale","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"止赎拍卖：为清偿抵押债务而依法出售抵押土地","contextualMeaning":"止赎拍卖：为清偿抵押债务而依法出售抵押土地","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The bank then brought an appropriate action to foreclose the mortgage, was the sole bidder at the judicial sale, and received title to the property as a result of the foreclosure sale.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"encumbrances","ipa":"/en'kʌmbrənsɪz/","syllables":"en·cum·brances","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 负担( encumbrance的复数形式 )； 累赘； 妨碍","contextualMeaning":"产权负担：抵押、地役权、契约等影响产权的权利","wordForm":"名词；原形 encumbrance；词形 0:encumbrance/1:s","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) The doctrine of exoneration allows a will beneficiary to use the estate's general assets to pay off any encumbrances (eg, mortgages) on the devised property.","synonyms":["title burden","property burden"]},{"term":"devised","ipa":"/dɪvˈaɪzd/","syllables":"de·vised","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 想出( devise的过去式和过去分词 )； 计划； 设计","contextualMeaning":"遗赠不动产：通过遗嘱转让土地权益","wordForm":"词；原形 devise；词形 0:devise/1:dp/p:devised","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) The doctrine of exoneration allows a will beneficiary to use the estate's general assets to pay off any encumbrances (eg, mortgages) on the devised property.","synonyms":["invent","contrive","excogitate","formulate"]},{"term":"redeem","ipa":"/ɹɪdˈim/","syllables":"re·deem","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 赎回, 挽回, 恢复, 偿还, 补偿；[经] 履行(契约), 买回, 赎回","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“redeem”","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:redeemed/p:redeemed/i:redeeming/3:redeems","sourceSentence":"Therefore, the man will successfully redeem his property from the bank if the jurisdiction provides this statutory right.","synonyms":["pay off","ransom","deliver","save"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":2,"sourceQuestionPage":4,"sourceQuestionPages":[4],"sourceExplanationPages":[5,6],"sourceAnswerPages":[5,6],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-003","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"Under the terms of his duly probated will, a testator devised his house to his \"grandchildren in fee simple\" and the residue of his estate to his brother. The testator had had two children, a son and a daughter, but only the daughter survived him. At the time of the testator's death, the daughter was 30 years old and had two minor children (grandchildren of the testator) who also survived the testator. A third grandchild of the testator, who was the child of the testator's predeceased son, had been alive when the testator executed the will, but he had predeceased the testator. Under the applicable intestate succession laws, the deceased grandchild's sole heir was his mother. A statute of the jurisdiction provides as follows: \"If a devisee, including a devisee of a class gift, who is a grandparent or a lineal descendant of a grandparent of the testator is dead at the time of execution of the will or fails to survive the testator, the issue of such deceased devisee shall take the deceased's share under the will, unless the will expressly provides that this statute shall not apply. For this purpose, words of survivorship, such as 'if he survives me,' are a sufficient expression that the statute shall not apply.\" Who now owns the house?","choices":{"A":"The testator's brother.","B":"The testator's two surviving grandchildren.","C":"The testator's two surviving grandchildren and all other grandchildren who are born to the testator's daughter.","D":"The testator's two surviving grandchildren and the deceased grandchild's mother."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A gift is a voluntary transfer of property without payment or consideration (eg, by devise). A class gift is created when the gift recipients (ie, donees) are unspecified but can be identified in the future (eg, upon the donor's death). Under the rule of convenience, a class closes once any member is entitled to immediate possession of the property, and anyone born after the class closes cannot claim an interest in that property.* However, if a donee receives a gift under a will and predeceases the testator, the gift will lapse and become invalid unless the will or a statute provides otherwise. An anti-lapse statute (as seen here) will save the gift from lapsing if the donee has a specified family relation to the testator AND the donee is survived by his/her own children or other lineal descendants (ie, issue). The surviving issue will then take the gift in place of the donee. Here, the testator devised his house to his \"grandchildren in fee simple.\" The testator had three grandchildren, one of whom predeceased the testator. This jurisdiction's anti-lapse statute applies to a testator's lineal descendants. But it does not save the deceased grandchild's gift because his sole heir was his mother—ie, he has no lineal descendants. As a result, the house is now owned by the testator's two surviving grandchildren (Choices C & D). *Those born after a class closes are excluded from the class, but those already in utero upon closing are included. (Choice A) The brother, as the recipient of the residuary estate, would have received the house had no grandchildren been eligible for the devise (eg, had they all predeceased the testator without lineal descendants).\n\nEducational objective: A gift conveyed under a will typically lapses if the recipient predeceases the testator. However, an anti-lapse statute will save the gift if the recipient (1) has a specified family relation to the testator and (2) is survived by his/her own children or other lineal descendants.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Wills and Other Donative Transfers §§ 1.2, 5.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1999) (lapse and anti-lapse statutes).","educationalObjective":"A gift conveyed under a will typically lapses if the recipient predeceases the testator. However, an anti-lapse statute will save the gift if the recipient (1) has a specified family relation to the testator and (2) is survived by his/her own children or other lineal descendants.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Wills and Other Donative Transfers §§ 1.2, 5.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1999) (lapse and anti-lapse statutes).","rule":"A gift conveyed under a will typically lapses if the recipient predeceases the testator. However, an anti-lapse statute will save the gift if the recipient (1) has a specified family relation to the testator and (2) is survived by his/her own children or other lineal descendants.","irac":{"issue":"Under the terms of his duly probated will, a testator devised his house to his \"grandchildren in fee simple\" and the residue of his estate to his brother. The testator had had two children, a son and a daughter, but only the daughter survived him. At the time of the testator's death, the daughter was 30 years old and had two minor children (grandchildren of the testator) who also survived the testator. A third grandchild of the testator, who was the child of the testator's predeceased son, had been alive when the testator executed the will, but he had predeceased the testator. Under the applicable intestate succession laws, the deceased grandchild's sole heir was his mother. A statute of the jurisdiction provides as follows: \"If a devisee, including a devisee of a class gift, who is a grandparent or a lineal descendant of a grandparent of the testator is dead at the time of execution of the will or fails to survive the testator, the issue of such deceased devisee shall take the deceased's share under the will, unless the will expressly provides that this statute shall not apply. For this purpose, words of survivorship, such as 'if he survives me,' are a sufficient expression that the statute shall not apply.\" Who now owns the house?","rule":"A gift conveyed under a will typically lapses if the recipient predeceases the testator. However, an anti-lapse statute will save the gift if the recipient (1) has a specified family relation to the testator and (2) is survived by his/her own children or other lineal descendants.","analysis":"A gift is a voluntary transfer of property without payment or consideration (eg, by devise). A class gift is created when the gift recipients (ie, donees) are unspecified but can be identified in the future (eg, upon the donor's death). Under the rule of convenience, a class closes once any member is entitled to immediate possession of the property, and anyone born after the class closes cannot claim an interest in that property.* However, if a donee receives a gift under a will and predeceases the testator, the gift will lapse and become invalid unless the will or a statute provides otherwise. An anti-lapse statute (as seen here) will save the gift from lapsing if the donee has a specified family relation to the testator AND the donee is survived by his/her own children or other lineal descendants (ie, issue). The surviving issue will then take the gift in place of the donee. Here, the testator devised his house to his \"grandchildren in fee simple.\" The testator had three grandchildren, one of whom predeceased the testator. This jurisdiction's anti-lapse statute applies to a testator's lineal descendants. But it does not save the deceased grandchild's gift because his sole heir was his mother—ie, he has no lineal descendants. As a result, the house is now owned by the testator's two surviving grandchildren (Choices C & D). *Those born after a class closes are excluded from the class, but those already in utero upon closing are included. (Choice A) The brother, as the recipient of the residuary estate, would have received the house had no grandchildren been eligible for the devise (eg, had they all predeceased the testator without lineal descendants).","conclusion":"Answer B: The testator's two surviving grandchildren."},"questionZh":"根据他经过正式检验的遗嘱条款，遗嘱人将他的房子设计给他的“简单的孙子们”，并将剩余的遗产留给他的兄弟。立遗嘱人有两个孩子，一个儿子和一个女儿，但只有女儿幸免于难。立遗嘱人去世时，女儿30岁，有两个未成年子女（立遗嘱人的孙子），他们也在立遗嘱人身后幸存。立遗嘱人的第三个孙子，即立遗嘱人已故儿子的孩子，在立遗嘱人执行遗嘱时还活着，但他先于立遗嘱人去世。根据适用的无遗嘱继承法，已故孙子的唯一继承人是他的母亲。司法管辖区的法规规定如下：“如果遗嘱人的祖父母或祖父母的直系后裔的受遗赠人，包括集体赠与的受遗赠人，在立遗嘱时死亡或遗嘱人未能生存，则该已故受遗赠人的后代应取得遗嘱中死者的份额，除非遗嘱明确规定不适用本法规。为此目的，幸存者的文字，例如‘如果他比我活得更久’这样的表述就足够了，该法规不适用。”房子现在归谁所有？","choicesZh":{"A":"立遗嘱人的兄弟。","B":"立遗嘱人的两个幸存的孙子。","C":"遗嘱人的两个幸存孙子以及遗嘱人女儿所生的所有其他孙子。","D":"立遗嘱人的两个幸存的孙子和已故孙子的母亲。"},"explanationZh":"礼物是一种自愿的财产转让，无需支付任何费用或报酬（例如，通过设计）。当礼物接受者（即受赠者）未指定但可以在将来（例如，捐赠者去世时）确定时，将创建类别礼物。根据便利规则，一旦任何成员有权立即占有该财产，集体就结束，集体结束后出生的任何人都不能主张该财产的权益。*但是，如果受赠人根据遗嘱收到礼物并先于立遗嘱人去世，则该礼物将失效，除非遗嘱或法规另有规定。如果受赠人与立遗嘱人有特定的家庭关系，并且受赠人有他/她自己的孩子或其他直系后裔（即后裔），则防失效法规（如此处所示）将避免赠与失效。然后，幸存的后代将代替受赠人继承该礼物。在这里，立遗嘱人为他的“简单的孙子们”设计了他的房子。遗嘱人有三个孙子，其中一名先于遗嘱人去世。该司法管辖区的反失效法规适用于立遗嘱人的直系后裔。但这并不能挽救已故孙子的礼物，因为他的唯一继承人是他的母亲——也就是说，他没有直系后裔。因此，该房屋现在由立遗嘱人的两个幸存孙子（选择 C ​​和 D）所有。 *课程结束后出生的学生不包括在课程中，但课程结束时已经在子宫内的学生包括在内。 （选择A）如果没有孙辈有资格获得该设计（例如，如果他们都先于立遗嘱人去世而没有直系后裔），作为剩余遗产接收人的兄弟将获得该房屋。\n\n教育目标：如果接受者先于立遗嘱人去世，则根据遗嘱传达的礼物通常会失效。然而，如果接受者 (1) 与立遗嘱人有特定的家庭关系，并且 (2) 他/她自己的孩子或其他直系后裔幸存，则防失效法规将保存该礼物。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转让 §§ 1.2、5.5（Am. Law Inst. 1999）（失效和反失效法规）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果接受者先于立遗嘱人去世，根据遗嘱传达的礼物通常会失效。然而，如果接受者 (1) 与立遗嘱人有特定的家庭关系，并且 (2) 他/她自己的孩子或其他直系后裔幸存，则防失效法规将保存该礼物。","iracZh":{"issue":"根据他经过正式检验的遗嘱条款，遗嘱人将他的房子设计给他的“简单的孙子们”，并将剩余的遗产留给他的兄弟。立遗嘱人有两个孩子，一个儿子和一个女儿，但只有女儿幸免于难。立遗嘱人去世时，女儿30岁，有两个未成年子女（立遗嘱人的孙子），他们也在立遗嘱人身后幸存。立遗嘱人的第三个孙子，即立遗嘱人已故儿子的孩子，在立遗嘱人执行遗嘱时还活着，但他先于立遗嘱人去世。根据适用的无遗嘱继承法，已故孙子的唯一继承人是他的母亲。司法管辖区的法规规定如下：“如果遗嘱人的祖父母或祖父母的直系后裔的受遗赠人，包括集体赠与的受遗赠人，在立遗嘱时死亡或遗嘱人未能生存，则该已故受遗赠人的后代应取得遗嘱中死者的份额，除非遗嘱明确规定不适用本法规。为此目的，幸存者的文字，例如‘如果他比我活得更久’这样的表述就足够了，该法规不适用。”房子现在归谁所有？","rule":"如果接受者先于立遗嘱人去世，根据遗嘱传达的礼物通常会失效。然而，如果接受者 (1) 与立遗嘱人有特定的家庭关系，并且 (2) 他/她自己的孩子或其他直系后裔幸存，则防失效法规将保存该礼物。","analysis":"礼物是一种自愿的财产转让，无需支付任何费用或报酬（例如，通过设计）。当礼物接受者（即受赠者）未指定但可以在将来（例如，捐赠者去世时）确定时，将创建类别礼物。根据便利规则，一旦任何成员有权立即占有该财产，集体就结束，集体结束后出生的任何人都不能主张该财产的权益。*但是，如果受赠人根据遗嘱收到礼物并先于立遗嘱人去世，则该礼物将失效，除非遗嘱或法规另有规定。如果受赠人与立遗嘱人有特定的家庭关系，并且受赠人有他/她自己的孩子或其他直系后裔（即后裔），则防失效法规（如此处所示）将避免赠与失效。然后，幸存的后代将代替受赠人继承该礼物。在这里，立遗嘱人为他的“简单的孙子们”设计了他的房子。遗嘱人有三个孙子，其中一名先于遗嘱人去世。该司法管辖区的反失效法规适用于立遗嘱人的直系后裔。但这并不能挽救已故孙子的礼物，因为他的唯一继承人是他的母亲——也就是说，他没有直系后裔。因此，该房屋现在由立遗嘱人的两个幸存孙子（选择 C ​​和 D）所有。 *课程结束后出生的学生不包括在课程中，但课程结束时已经在子宫内的学生包括在内。 （选择A）如果没有孙辈有资格获得该设计（例如，如果他们都先于立遗嘱人去世而没有直系后裔），作为剩余遗产接收人的兄弟将获得该房屋。\n\n教育目标：如果接受者先于立遗嘱人去世，则根据遗嘱传达的礼物通常会失效。然而，如果接受者 (1) 与立遗嘱人有特定的家庭关系，并且 (2) 他/她自己的孩子或其他直系后裔幸存，则防失效法规将保存该礼物。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转让 §§ 1.2、5.5（Am. Law Inst. 1999）（失效和反失效法规）。","conclusion":"答案 B：立遗嘱人的两个幸存的孙子。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"rule of convenience","ipa":"/ɹˈul ˈəv kʌnvˈinjəns/","syllables":"rule of con·ve·nience","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"便利规则：类别成员首次有权立即占有时通常关闭受赠类别","contextualMeaning":"便利规则：类别成员首次有权立即占有时通常关闭受赠类别","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Under the rule of convenience, a class closes once any member is entitled to immediate possession of the property, and anyone born after the class closes cannot claim an interest in that property.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"class gift","ipa":"/klˈæs ɡˈɪft/","syllables":"class gift","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"类别赠与：以群体描述确定受赠人的赠与","contextualMeaning":"类别赠与：以群体描述确定受赠人的赠与","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A statute of the jurisdiction provides as follows: \"If a devisee, including a devisee of a class gift, who is a grandparent or a lineal descendant of a grandparent of the testator is dead at the time of execution of the will or fails to survive the testator, the issue of such deceased devisee shall take the deceased's share under the will, unless the will expressly provides that this statute shall not apply.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"possession","ipa":"/pʌzˈɛʃən/","syllables":"pos·ses·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 拥有, 占有, 所有, 财产, 领土, 领地, 自制, 着迷；[经] 占有, 持有","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“possession”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:possessions","sourceSentence":"Under the rule of convenience, a class closes once any member is entitled to immediate possession of the property, and anyone born after the class closes cannot claim an interest in that property.","synonyms":["ownership","self-control","self-possession","willpower"]},{"term":"conveyed","ipa":"/kʌnvˈeɪd/","syllables":"con·veyed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 运输( convey的过去式和过去分词 )； 运送； 输送","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“conveyed”","wordForm":"词；原形 convey；词形 0:convey/1:dp","sourceSentence":"A gift conveyed under a will typically lapses if the recipient predeceases the testator.","synonyms":["carry","express","bring","get"]},{"term":"devisee","ipa":"/devi'zi:, divai'zi:/","syllables":"de·visee","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 受遗赠者；[经] 受遗赠者","contextualMeaning":"受遗赠人：根据遗嘱取得土地或其他财产的人","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"A statute of the jurisdiction provides as follows: \"If a devisee, including a devisee of a class gift, who is a grandparent or a lineal descendant of a grandparent of the testator is dead at the time of execution of the will or fails to survive the testator, the issue of such deceased devisee shall take the deceased's share under the will, unless the will expressly provides that this statute shall not apply.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"estate","ipa":"/ɪstˈeɪt/","syllables":"es·tate","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 不动产, (人生的)阶段, 阶层, 财产；[经] 房地产, 遗产, 财产","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“estate”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:estates","sourceSentence":"Under the terms of his duly probated will, a testator devised his house to his \"grandchildren in fee simple\" and the residue of his estate to his brother.","synonyms":["estate of the realm","the three estates","land","landed estate"]},{"term":"survivorship","ipa":"/sə'vaivəʃip/","syllables":"sur·vivor·ship","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 幸存, 残存, 未死, 生者对死者名下财产的享有权；[经] 生存者所有权","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“survivorship”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:survivorships","sourceSentence":"For this purpose, words of survivorship, such as 'if he survives me,' are a sufficient expression that the statute shall not apply.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"survive","ipa":"/sɝvˈaɪv/","syllables":"sur·vive","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 比...活得长, 生存, 生还, 幸免于；vi. 活下来, 幸存","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“survive”","wordForm":"动词；词形 p:survived/d:survived/3:survives/i:surviving","sourceSentence":"The testator had had two children, a son and a daughter, but only the daughter survived him.","synonyms":["exist","live","subsist","last"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-0f6d6373fc867c62"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":3,"sourceQuestionPage":7,"sourceQuestionPages":[7],"sourceExplanationPages":[8,9],"sourceAnswerPages":[8,9],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-004","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Adverse Possession","question":"A woman died, devising land that she owned in another state to her daughter, who was then 17 years old. A neighbor who owned the property immediately adjacent to the land wrongfully began to possess the land at that time. For 24 of the next 25 years, the neighbor planted and harvested crops on the land, hunted on it, and parked cars on it. However, in the sixth year after he first took possession of the land, the neighbor neither planted crops nor hunted nor parked cars on the land because he spent that entire year living in Europe. The neighbor built a small gardening shed on the land, but he never built a residence on it. When the daughter was 28, she was declared mentally incompetent and had a conservator appointed to oversee her affairs. Since then, she has continuously resided in a care facility. The applicable statute of limitations provides as follows: \"An ejectment action shall be brought within 21 years after the cause of action accrues, but if the person entitled to bring the cause of action is under age 18 or mentally incompetent at the time the cause of action accrues, it may be brought by such person within 10 years after attaining age 18 or after the person becomes competent.\" If the daughter's conservator wins an ejectment action against the neighbor, what will be the most likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"Because the daughter is mentally incompetent, the statute of limitations has been tolled.","B":"The daughter was age 17 when the neighbor first took possession of the land.","C":"The neighbor never built a residence on the land.","D":"The neighbor was not in continuous possession of the land for 21 years."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under the doctrine of adverse possession, a person who possesses land owned by another may acquire title to the land if his/her possession was: Open and notorious – apparent to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the land Non-permissive – hostile and adverse to the owner Here, the statutory period for adverse possession is 21 years. Although the neighbor first possessed the land 25 years ago, he left after 5 years and spent a year in Europe. When the neighbor returned from Europe and retook possession of the land, the statutory period started anew. Therefore, the neighbor only continuously possessed the land for 19 years— not 21 years—and he did not acquire title by adverse possession. (Choices A & B) The statute of limitations would have tolled (ie, paused) if the daughter had been under 18 or mentally incompetent at the time the claim accrued. Here, though the daughter was 17 when the neighbor first took possession of the land, she was 23 when the neighbor retook possession (and her claim accrued). And since she was declared mentally incompetent 5 years after her claim accrued, the statute of limitations was not tolled. (Choice C) A person does not have to build a residence on the land to adversely possess it, so the fact that the neighbor never built a residence on the daughter's land is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: A person can only acquire title to another's land through adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is continuous—ie, uninterrupted—for the entire statutory period.","educationalObjective":"A person can only acquire title to another's land through adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is continuous—ie, uninterrupted—for the entire statutory period.","references":"","rule":"A person can only acquire title to another's land through adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is continuous—ie, uninterrupted—for the entire statutory period.","irac":{"issue":"A woman died, devising land that she owned in another state to her daughter, who was then 17 years old. A neighbor who owned the property immediately adjacent to the land wrongfully began to possess the land at that time. For 24 of the next 25 years, the neighbor planted and harvested crops on the land, hunted on it, and parked cars on it. However, in the sixth year after he first took possession of the land, the neighbor neither planted crops nor hunted nor parked cars on the land because he spent that entire year living in Europe. The neighbor built a small gardening shed on the land, but he never built a residence on it. When the daughter was 28, she was declared mentally incompetent and had a conservator appointed to oversee her affairs. Since then, she has continuously resided in a care facility. The applicable statute of limitations provides as follows: \"An ejectment action shall be brought within 21 years after the cause of action accrues, but if the person entitled to bring the cause of action is under age 18 or mentally incompetent at the time the cause of action accrues, it may be brought by such person within 10 years after attaining age 18 or after the person becomes competent.\" If the daughter's conservator wins an ejectment action against the neighbor, what will be the most likely explanation?","rule":"A person can only acquire title to another's land through adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is continuous—ie, uninterrupted—for the entire statutory period.","analysis":"Under the doctrine of adverse possession, a person who possesses land owned by another may acquire title to the land if his/her possession was: Open and notorious – apparent to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the land Non-permissive – hostile and adverse to the owner Here, the statutory period for adverse possession is 21 years. Although the neighbor first possessed the land 25 years ago, he left after 5 years and spent a year in Europe. When the neighbor returned from Europe and retook possession of the land, the statutory period started anew. Therefore, the neighbor only continuously possessed the land for 19 years— not 21 years—and he did not acquire title by adverse possession. (Choices A & B) The statute of limitations would have tolled (ie, paused) if the daughter had been under 18 or mentally incompetent at the time the claim accrued. Here, though the daughter was 17 when the neighbor first took possession of the land, she was 23 when the neighbor retook possession (and her claim accrued). And since she was declared mentally incompetent 5 years after her claim accrued, the statute of limitations was not tolled. (Choice C) A person does not have to build a residence on the land to adversely possess it, so the fact that the neighbor never built a residence on the daughter's land is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer D: The neighbor was not in continuous possession of the land for 21 years."},"questionZh":"一名妇女在为当时 17 岁的女儿设计她在另一个州拥有的土地时去世。一位拥有紧邻该土地的房产的邻居当时开始错误地占有该土地。在接下来的 25 年中，有 24 年，邻居在这片土地上种植和收割庄稼、打猎、停放汽车。然而，在他占有这块土地的第六年，邻居在这块土地上既没有种植庄稼，也没有打猎，也没有停放汽车，因为他整年都生活在欧洲。邻居在这块土地上建了一个小园艺棚，但他从未在上面建造过住宅。当女儿 28 岁时，她被宣布精神上无行为能力，并指定了一名监护人来监督她的事务。此后，她一直住在护理机构。适用的诉讼时效规定如下：“驱逐诉讼应在诉讼事由产生后 21 年内提起，但如果有权提起诉讼事由的人未满 18 岁或在诉讼事由产生时精神上无行为能力，则该人可以在年满 18 岁后或具有行为能力后 10 年内提起诉讼。”如果女儿的管理员在针对邻居的驱逐诉讼中获胜，最可能的解释是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"由于女儿精神上无行为能力，已过了诉讼时效。","B":"邻居第一次占有这块土地时，女儿 17 岁。","C":"邻居从未在这块土地上建造过住宅。","D":"邻居已经 21 年没有连续占有这块土地了。"},"explanationZh":"根据逆权管有原则，拥有他人拥有的土地的人如果其占有符合以下条件，则可以获得该土地的所有权： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对合理的所有者来说是显而易见的 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 排他性 – 不与所有者共享 实际 – 实际存在于土地上 不允许 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 这里，逆权管有的法定期限为 21 年。尽管这位邻居25年前就首次拥有了这块土地，但5年后他就离开了，并在欧洲度过了一年。当邻居从欧洲回来并重新占有土地时，法定期限重新开始。因此，邻居只连续占有土地19年，而不是21年，并且他没有通过逆权占有获得所有权。 （选项 A 和 B）如果女儿在提出索赔时未满 18 岁或精神上无行为能力，则诉讼时效将会终止（即暂停）。在这里，虽然邻居第一次占有土地时女儿 17 岁，但邻居重新占有土地时她已经 23 岁了（她的索赔权也随之产生）。由于她在提出索赔五年后被宣布精神上无行为能力，因此诉讼时效并未终止。 （选项C） 一个人不必在土地上建造住宅才能对这块土地进行逆权占有，因此邻居从未在女儿的土地上建造住宅这一事实是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：只有在整个法定期限内，一个人对土地的占有是连续的（即不间断的），他/她才能通过逆权管有获得他人土地的所有权。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果一个人在整个法定期限内连续（即不间断）占有土地，则他/她只能通过逆权占有获得他人土地的所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"一名妇女在为当时 17 岁的女儿设计她在另一个州拥有的土地时去世。一位拥有紧邻该土地的房产的邻居当时开始错误地占有该土地。在接下来的 25 年中，有 24 年，邻居在这片土地上种植和收割庄稼、打猎、停放汽车。然而，在他占有这块土地的第六年，邻居在这块土地上既没有种植庄稼，也没有打猎，也没有停放汽车，因为他整年都生活在欧洲。邻居在这块土地上建了一个小园艺棚，但他从未在上面建造过住宅。当女儿 28 岁时，她被宣布精神上无行为能力，并指定了一名监护人来监督她的事务。此后，她一直住在护理机构。适用的诉讼时效规定如下：“驱逐诉讼应在诉讼事由产生后 21 年内提起，但如果有权提起诉讼事由的人未满 18 岁或在诉讼事由产生时精神上无行为能力，则该人可以在年满 18 岁后或具有行为能力后 10 年内提起诉讼。”如果女儿的管理员在针对邻居的驱逐诉讼中获胜，最可能的解释是什么？","rule":"如果一个人在整个法定期限内连续（即不间断）占有土地，则他/她只能通过逆权占有获得他人土地的所有权。","analysis":"根据逆权管有原则，拥有他人拥有的土地的人如果其占有符合以下条件，则可以获得该土地的所有权： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对合理的所有者来说是显而易见的 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 排他性 – 不与所有者共享 实际 – 实际存在于土地上 不允许 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 这里，逆权管有的法定期限为 21 年。尽管这位邻居25年前就首次拥有了这块土地，但5年后他就离开了，并在欧洲度过了一年。当邻居从欧洲回来并重新占有土地时，法定期限重新开始。因此，邻居只连续占有土地19年，而不是21年，并且他没有通过逆权占有获得所有权。 （选项 A 和 B）如果女儿在提出索赔时未满 18 岁或精神上无行为能力，则诉讼时效将会终止（即暂停）。在这里，虽然邻居第一次占有土地时女儿 17 岁，但邻居重新占有土地时她已经 23 岁了（她的索赔权也随之产生）。由于她在提出索赔五年后被宣布精神上无行为能力，因此诉讼时效并未终止。 （选项C） 一个人不必在土地上建造住宅才能对这块土地进行逆权占有，因此邻居从未在女儿的土地上建造住宅这一事实是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：只有在整个法定期限内，一个人对土地的占有是连续的（即不间断的），他/她才能通过逆权管有获得他人土地的所有权。","conclusion":"答案 D：邻居已经 21 年没有连续占有这块土地了。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"continuous possession","ipa":"/kʌntˈɪnjuəs pʌzˈɛʃən/","syllables":"con·tin·u·ous pos·ses·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"连续占有：按照该类土地通常使用方式持续达到法定期间","contextualMeaning":"连续占有：按照该类土地通常使用方式持续达到法定期间","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The neighbor was not in continuous possession of the land for 21 years.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"adverse possession","ipa":"/ædvˈɝs pʌzˈɛʃən/","syllables":"ad·verse pos·ses·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"逆权占有：实际、公开、敌意、排他且连续占有达到期限后取得产权","contextualMeaning":"逆权占有：实际、公开、敌意、排他且连续占有达到期限后取得产权","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A person can only acquire title to another's land through adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is continuous—ie, uninterrupted—for the entire statutory period.","synonyms":["prescriptive title"]},{"term":"open and notorious","ipa":"/ˈoʊpən ənd noʊtˈɔɹiəs/","syllables":"open and no·to·ri·ous","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"公开显著：占有足以使合理土地所有人知悉","contextualMeaning":"公开显著：占有足以使合理土地所有人知悉","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Under the doctrine of adverse possession, a person who possesses land owned by another may acquire title to the land if his/her possession was: Open and notorious – apparent to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the land Non-permissive – hostile and adverse to the owner Here, the statutory period for adverse posses","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"hostile","ipa":"/hˈɑstəl/","syllables":"hos·tile","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 敌人的, 怀敌意的, 敌对的；n. 敌对分子","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“hostile”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:hostiles","sourceSentence":"Under the doctrine of adverse possession, a person who possesses land owned by another may acquire title to the land if his/her possession was: Open and notorious – apparent to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the land Non-permissive – hostile and adverse to the owner Here, the statutory period for adverse posses","synonyms":["uncongenial","unfriendly"]},{"term":"continuously","ipa":"/kʌntˈɪnjuəsli/","syllables":"con·tin·u·ously","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 不断地, 连续地；[电] 连续地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“continuously”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Since then, she has continuously resided in a care facility.","synonyms":["endlessly","ceaselessly","incessantly","unceasingly"]},{"term":"adversely","ipa":"/ædvˈɝsli/","syllables":"ad·versely","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 不利, 有害, 相反, 逆, 敌对, 逆向","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“adversely”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) A person does not have to build a residence on the land to adversely possess it, so the fact that the neighbor never built a residence on the daughter's land is irrelevant.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-27af9ec8a6288559"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":4,"sourceQuestionPage":10,"sourceQuestionPages":[10],"sourceExplanationPages":[11],"sourceAnswerPages":[11],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-005","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"An individual purchased a residence for $200,000. The individual paid $25,000 of the purchase price from her own funds and the remaining $175,000 with the proceeds of a loan she obtained from a bank. She signed a promissory note payable to the bank in the principal amount of $175,000 and gave the bank a mortgage on the residence. The bank promptly and properly recorded this mortgage. Several years later, the individual borrowed $35,000 from a credit union to finance the construction of an addition to the residence. She signed a promissory note payable to the credit union in the principal amount of $35,000 and gave the credit union a mortgage on the residence. Because of a clerical oversight, the credit union failed to record this mortgage. Subsequently, the individual defaulted on the bank note. This note contained an acceleration clause, pursuant to which the full unpaid balance of the note became immediately due. The fair market value of the residence was slightly less than the amount owed on the note, so the bank offered to accept the deed to the residence in exchange for cancellation of the note. The individual agreed, and also agreed to waive her equitable right to redeem the property. Shortly after title to the residence was transferred to the bank, the bank learned of the credit union's mortgage. The bank has brought a foreclosure action to which the credit union was made a party. The credit union contests the bank's right to bring this action. If the court rules in favor of the bank, what is the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The jurisdiction recognizes a statutory right of redemption.","B":"The jurisdiction recognizes the principle of clogging the equity of redemption.","C":"The jurisdiction does not recognize the doctrine of caveat emptor.","D":"The jurisdiction does not recognize the doctrine of merger with regard to mortgages."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The doctrine of merger applies to mortgages when the same person acquires both: a mortgagee's (lender's) interest in real property and the mortgagor's (borrower's) interest in that same property. When this occurs, the mortgage merges into the fee estate and is thereby extinguished. Here, the bank held a mortgage on the residence when it acquired the individual's ownership interest in that residence. If the doctrine of merger applied, the mortgage would have been extinguished upon that transfer and the bank would have no right to bring this foreclosure action. So if the court rules in the bank's favor, it is likely because the jurisdiction does not recognize the doctrine of merger with regard to mortgages. (Choice A) The statutory right of redemption allows a mortgagor to regain ownership of real property after it has been sold in foreclosure. This right does not apply here because the credit union is contending that the bank no longer has a mortgage on the property needed to bring this foreclosure action. (Choice B) The principle of clogging the equity of redemption allows courts to reject a mortgagee's attempt to deny the mortgagor the ability to waive the right to redemption after the mortgage is executed—eg, by including a waiver clause in the mortgage. Here, the individual was able to waive her right to redeem the residence after the mortgage was executed, and that waiver is not at issue. Therefore, this principle does not apply. (Choice C) The common-law doctrine of caveat emptor (meaning \"let the buyer beware\") provides that a seller has no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer unless otherwise agreed. Since this doctrine is related to warranties, it has no effect on the bank's right to foreclose on a mortgage.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of merger, when the same person acquires a mortgagee's interest and the mortgagor's interest in the same property, the mortgage merges into the fee estate and is extinguished.","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of merger, when the same person acquires a mortgagee's interest and the mortgagor's interest in the same property, the mortgage merges into the fee estate and is extinguished.","references":"","rule":"Under the doctrine of merger, when the same person acquires a mortgagee's interest and the mortgagor's interest in the same property, the mortgage merges into the fee estate and is extinguished.","irac":{"issue":"If the court rules in favor of the bank, what is the most likely reason?","rule":"Under the doctrine of merger, when the same person acquires a mortgagee's interest and the mortgagor's interest in the same property, the mortgage merges into the fee estate and is extinguished.","analysis":"The doctrine of merger applies to mortgages when the same person acquires both: a mortgagee's (lender's) interest in real property and the mortgagor's (borrower's) interest in that same property. When this occurs, the mortgage merges into the fee estate and is thereby extinguished. Here, the bank held a mortgage on the residence when it acquired the individual's ownership interest in that residence. If the doctrine of merger applied, the mortgage would have been extinguished upon that transfer and the bank would have no right to bring this foreclosure action. So if the court rules in the bank's favor, it is likely because the jurisdiction does not recognize the doctrine of merger with regard to mortgages. (Choice A) The statutory right of redemption allows a mortgagor to regain ownership of real property after it has been sold in foreclosure. This right does not apply here because the credit union is contending that the bank no longer has a mortgage on the property needed to bring this foreclosure action. (Choice B) The principle of clogging the equity of redemption allows courts to reject a mortgagee's attempt to deny the mortgagor the ability to waive the right to redemption after the mortgage is executed—eg, by including a waiver clause in the mortgage. Here, the individual was able to waive her right to redeem the residence after the mortgage was executed, and that waiver is not at issue. Therefore, this principle does not apply. (Choice C) The common-law doctrine of caveat emptor (meaning \"let the buyer beware\") provides that a seller has no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer unless otherwise agreed. Since this doctrine is related to warranties, it has no effect on the bank's right to foreclose on a mortgage.","conclusion":"Answer D: The jurisdiction does not recognize the doctrine of merger with regard to mortgages."},"questionZh":"某人以 20 万美元购买了一套住宅。该个人用自己的资金支付了 25,000 美元的购买价，其余的 175,000 美元则用她从银行获得的贷款收益支付。她签署了一份应付给银行的本金为 175,000 美元的本票，并向银行提供了该住宅的抵押贷款。银行及时、妥善地记录了这笔抵押贷款。几年后，该人从一家信用合作社借了 35,000 美元，用于资助住宅的扩建。她签署了一份应付给信用社的本金为 35,000 美元的期票，并向信用社提供了该住宅的抵押贷款。由于文书疏忽，信用合作社未能记录这笔抵押贷款。随后，该人拖欠了该钞票。该票据包含一项加速条款，根据该条款，该票据的全部未付余额立即到期。该住宅的公平市场价值略低于票据上所欠的金额，因此银行提出接受该住宅的契约，以换取取消该票据。该人同意了，并同意放弃其赎回财产的公平权利。住宅所有权转移给银行后不久，银行就得知了信用合作社的抵押贷款。该银行已提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼，信用合作社是其中的一方。信用合作社对银行提起这一诉讼的权利提出质疑。如果法院做出有利于银行的裁决，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"司法管辖区承认法定的赎回权。","B":"司法管辖区承认堵塞股权赎回的原则。","C":"该司法管辖区不承认买者自负原则。","D":"该司法管辖区不承认抵押贷款方面的合并原则。"},"explanationZh":"当同一个人同时获得抵押权人（贷方）对不动产的权益和抵押人（借款人）对同一财产的权益时，合并原则适用于抵押。当这种情况发生时，抵押贷款并入费用财产，从而消失。在这里，银行在获得个人对该住宅的所有权权益时对该住宅持有抵押贷款。如果适用合并原则，抵押贷款将在转让后消失，银行将无权提起取消抵押品赎回权的诉讼。因此，如果法院做出对银行有利的裁决，很可能是因为该司法管辖区不承认抵押贷款方面的合并原则。 （选项A）法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）允许抵押人在取消抵押品赎回权后出售不动产后重新获得其所有权。这项权利在这里并不适用，因为信用合作社认为银行不再拥有提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼所需的财产抵押。 （选择B） 堵塞赎回权的原则允许法院驳回抵押权人试图剥夺抵押人在抵押执行后放弃赎回权的能力的行为——例如，通过在抵押中包含放弃条款。在这里，个人能够在抵押贷款执行后放弃赎回住宅的权利，并且这种放弃不存在问题。因此，该原则不适用。 （选项C） 普通法中的“买者自负”原则（意思是“让买方当心”）规定，卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷，除非另有约定。由于该原则与保证有关，因此它对银行取消抵押贷款赎回权的权利没有影响。\n\n教育目标：根据合并原则，当同一个人获得同一财产中抵押权人的权益和抵押人的权益时，抵押权合并到费用财产中并消灭。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据合并原则，当同一人获得同一财产中抵押权人的权益和抵押人的权益时，抵押权合并到费用财产中并消灭。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院做出有利于银行的裁决，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"根据合并原则，当同一人获得同一财产中抵押权人的权益和抵押人的权益时，抵押权合并到费用财产中并消灭。","analysis":"当同一个人同时获得抵押权人（贷方）对不动产的权益和抵押人（借款人）对同一财产的权益时，合并原则适用于抵押。当这种情况发生时，抵押贷款并入费用财产，从而消失。在这里，银行在获得个人对该住宅的所有权权益时对该住宅持有抵押贷款。如果适用合并原则，抵押贷款将在转让后消失，银行将无权提起取消抵押品赎回权的诉讼。因此，如果法院做出对银行有利的裁决，很可能是因为该司法管辖区不承认抵押贷款方面的合并原则。 （选项A）法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）允许抵押人在取消抵押品赎回权后出售不动产后重新获得其所有权。这项权利在这里并不适用，因为信用合作社认为银行不再拥有提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼所需的财产抵押。 （选择B） 堵塞赎回权的原则允许法院驳回抵押权人试图剥夺抵押人在抵押执行后放弃赎回权的能力的行为——例如，通过在抵押中包含放弃条款。在这里，个人能够在抵押贷款执行后放弃赎回住宅的权利，并且这种放弃不存在问题。因此，该原则不适用。 （选项C） 普通法中的“买者自负”原则（意思是“让买方当心”）规定，卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷，除非另有约定。由于该原则与保证有关，因此它对银行取消抵押贷款赎回权的权利没有影响。\n\n教育目标：根据合并原则，当同一个人获得同一财产中抵押权人的权益和抵押人的权益时，抵押权合并到费用财产中并消灭。","conclusion":"答案 D：该司法管辖区不承认抵押贷款方面的合并原则。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"acceleration clause","ipa":"/ˌæksˌɛlɝˈeɪʃən klˈɔz/","syllables":"ac·cel·er·a·tion clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"加速到期条款：违约后使全部贷款余额立即到期","contextualMeaning":"加速到期条款：违约后使全部贷款余额立即到期","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"This note contained an acceleration clause, pursuant to which the full unpaid balance of the note became immediately due.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"deed","ipa":"/dˈid/","syllables":"deed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 行为, 实行, 契约；vt. 立契转让","contextualMeaning":"契据：以书面形式转让不动产产权的法律文书","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:deeds","sourceSentence":"The fair market value of the residence was slightly less than the amount owed on the note, so the bank offered to accept the deed to the residence in exchange for cancellation of the note.","synonyms":["instrument of conveyance"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-e9bf5f58e21fa79e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":5,"sourceQuestionPage":12,"sourceQuestionPages":[12],"sourceExplanationPages":[13,14],"sourceAnswerPages":[13,14],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-006","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"An aspiring dancer purchased an apartment near the theater district from a local real estate broker. To complete the sale, the dancer signed a document entitled \"Contract for Deed.\" The document provided that the dancer was to pay the broker in monthly installments over a five-year period for the apartment, but it was silent as to remedies in the event of any default. Four years later, after never missing a payment, the dancer suffered a severe injury and was out of work for many months. When she missed a monthly payment, the broker notified her that he intended to retake possession of the apartment. The dancer refused to surrender possession unless the broker refunded some of the money that she paid him over the years. Which of the following outcomes is the court LEAST likely to impose?","choices":{"A":"Require the broker to initiate judicial foreclosure proceedings on the property.","B":"Require the broker to refund the dancer's payments to the extent that they exceed the broker's actual damages upon forfeiture.","C":"Require the dancer to indemnify the broker for the delinquent payments in order to retain possession.","D":"Require the dancer to pay the remaining contract balance to retain possession and obtain ownership."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Mortgage alternatives\n\nAbsolute deed Debtor gives deed to creditor with intent to secure loan (ie, equitable mortgage)\n\nDeed of trust Debtor gives deed of trust to third-party trustee as collateral for debt, & creditor can instruct trustee to foreclose upon default\n\nInstallment land Debtor agrees to buy land through installment payments & gets contract immediate possession, but seller keeps legal title until paid in full\n\nSale-leaseback Seller leases property from buyer immediately after sale, & seller's rental payments function as repayments on loan\n\nEquitable Seller finances buyer's purchase with equitable vendor's lien when vendor's lien seller transfers title to buyer but purchase price not fully paid\n\nA contract for deed (ie, installment land contract) is a type of mortgage alternative. Under this contract, the buyer may take immediate possession of the property, but the seller retains title until the buyer makes the final payment under an installment payment plan. Traditionally, a contract for deed allowed the seller to keep all installment payments and retake possession if the buyer failed to make a single payment—even if all but one payment was made. However, states now seek to assist a buyer in default by: treating an installment land contract as a mortgage and requiring the seller to foreclose on the property to gain clear title (Choice A)\n\noffering the buyer the equitable right of redemption by paying the remaining contract balance to retain possession and obtain ownership (Choice D) or\n\nallowing the seller to retain ownership of the property but requiring some form of restitution to the buyer—eg, refunding the contract payments to the extent that they exceed the damages caused to the seller by the buyer's default (Choice B).\n\nHowever, a buyer cannot regain possession by merely indemnifying (ie, reimbursing) the seller for the missed payments. Therefore, the court is least likely to impose this outcome.\n\nEducational objective: A contract for deed allows a buyer to take immediate possession of the property while the seller retains title until the final installment payment is made. The seller could traditionally keep all installment payments and retake possession upon default. But states now assist buyers in default by requiring a foreclosure, offering the equitable right of redemption, or granting the buyer restitution.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 3.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (contract for deed).","educationalObjective":"A contract for deed allows a buyer to take immediate possession of the property while the seller retains title until the final installment payment is made. The seller could traditionally keep all installment payments and retake possession upon default. But states now assist buyers in default by requiring a foreclosure, offering the equitable right of redemption, or granting the buyer restitution.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 3.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (contract for deed).","rule":"A contract for deed allows a buyer to take immediate possession of the property while the seller retains title until the final installment payment is made. The seller could traditionally keep all installment payments and retake possession upon default. But states now assist buyers in default by requiring a foreclosure, offering the equitable right of redemption, or granting the buyer restitution.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following outcomes is the court LEAST likely to impose?","rule":"A contract for deed allows a buyer to take immediate possession of the property while the seller retains title until the final installment payment is made. The seller could traditionally keep all installment payments and retake possession upon default. But states now assist buyers in default by requiring a foreclosure, offering the equitable right of redemption, or granting the buyer restitution.","analysis":"Mortgage alternatives\n\nAbsolute deed Debtor gives deed to creditor with intent to secure loan (ie, equitable mortgage)\n\nDeed of trust Debtor gives deed of trust to third-party trustee as collateral for debt, & creditor can instruct trustee to foreclose upon default\n\nInstallment land Debtor agrees to buy land through installment payments & gets contract immediate possession, but seller keeps legal title until paid in full\n\nSale-leaseback Seller leases property from buyer immediately after sale, & seller's rental payments function as repayments on loan\n\nEquitable Seller finances buyer's purchase with equitable vendor's lien when vendor's lien seller transfers title to buyer but purchase price not fully paid\n\nA contract for deed (ie, installment land contract) is a type of mortgage alternative. Under this contract, the buyer may take immediate possession of the property, but the seller retains title until the buyer makes the final payment under an installment payment plan. Traditionally, a contract for deed allowed the seller to keep all installment payments and retake possession if the buyer failed to make a single payment—even if all but one payment was made. However, states now seek to assist a buyer in default by: treating an installment land contract as a mortgage and requiring the seller to foreclose on the property to gain clear title (Choice A)\n\noffering the buyer the equitable right of redemption by paying the remaining contract balance to retain possession and obtain ownership (Choice D) or\n\nallowing the seller to retain ownership of the property but requiring some form of restitution to the buyer—eg, refunding the contract payments to the extent that they exceed the damages caused to the seller by the buyer's default (Choice B).\n\nHowever, a buyer cannot regain possession by merely indemnifying (ie, reimbursing) the seller for the missed payments. Therefore, the court is least likely to impose this outcome.","conclusion":"Answer C: Require the dancer to indemnify the broker for the delinquent payments in order to retain possession."},"questionZh":"一位有抱负的舞者从当地房地产经纪人那里购买了剧院区附近的一套公寓。为了完成销售，舞者签署了一份名为“契约合同”的文件。该文件规定，舞者将在五年内按月分期向经纪人支付公寓费用，但没有提及违约情况下的补救措施。四年后，在从未拖欠付款后，这位舞者受了重伤，失业了好几个月。当她错过每月付款时，经纪人通知她，他打算重新获得公寓的所有权。舞者拒绝交出财产，除非经纪人退还她多年来付给他的部分钱。法院最不可能施加以下哪种结果？","choicesZh":{"A":"要求经纪人对该财产启动司法止赎程序。","B":"要求经纪人退还舞者的款项，金额超过经纪人没收时的实际损失的部分。","C":"要求舞者赔偿经纪人拖欠的款项，以保留所有权。","D":"要求舞者支付剩余的合同余额以保留占有并获得所有权。"},"explanationZh":"抵押贷款替代方案\n\n绝对契约 债务人向债权人提供契约以获取贷款（即公平抵押贷款）\n\n信托契约债务人向第三方受托人提供信托契约作为债务抵押品，债权人可以指示受托人在违约时取消赎回权\n\n分期付款土地 债务人同意通过分期付款购买土地并立即获得合同占有，但卖方保留合法所有权，直至全额付款\n\n售后回租 卖方在出售后立即向买方出租房产，卖方的租金支付相当于贷款还款\n\n当卖方的留置权卖方将所有权转让给买方但购买价款未完全支付时，衡平法卖方利用衡平法卖方留置权为买方的购买提供资金\n\n契约合同（即分期付款土地合同）是一种抵押贷款替代方案。根据本合同，买方可以立即占有该财产，但卖方保留所有权，直到买方根据分期付款计划支付最后一笔款项。传统上，契约合同允许卖方保留所有分期付款，并在买方未能支付一笔款项的情况下重新获得所有权——即使除了一笔付款外，所有款项均已支付。然而，各州现在寻求通过以下方式协助违约买方： 将分期付款土地合同视为抵押，并要求卖方取消财产赎回权以获得明确的所有权（选择 A）\n\n通过支付剩余合同余额来向买方提供公平的赎回权，以保留占有权并获得所有权（选择 D）或\n\n允许卖方保留财产所有权，但要求买方以某种形式恢复原状，例如，退还合同付款，金额超出买方违约给卖方造成的损失（选择B）。\n\n然而，买方不能仅通过赔偿（即偿还）卖方的未付款项来重新获得所有权。因此，法院强制执行这一结果的可能性最小。\n\n教育目标：契约合同允许买方立即占有该财产，而卖方保留所有权，直到支付最后一笔分期付款。传统上，卖方可以保留所有分期付款，并在违约时重新收回所有权。但各州现在通过要求取消抵押品赎回权、提供公平的赎回权或给予买家赔偿来帮助违约买家。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 3.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（契据合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"契约合同允许买方立即占有该财产，而卖方保留所有权，直到支付最后一笔分期付款。传统上，卖方可以保留所有分期付款，并在违约时重新收回所有权。但各州现在通过要求取消抵押品赎回权、提供公平的赎回权或给予买家赔偿来帮助违约买家。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院最不可能施加以下哪种结果？","rule":"契约合同允许买方立即占有该财产，而卖方保留所有权，直到支付最后一笔分期付款。传统上，卖方可以保留所有分期付款，并在违约时重新收回所有权。但各州现在通过要求取消抵押品赎回权、提供公平的赎回权或给予买家赔偿来帮助违约买家。","analysis":"抵押贷款替代方案\n\n绝对契约 债务人向债权人提供契约以获取贷款（即公平抵押贷款）\n\n信托契约债务人向第三方受托人提供信托契约作为债务抵押品，债权人可以指示受托人在违约时取消赎回权\n\n分期付款土地 债务人同意通过分期付款购买土地并立即获得合同占有，但卖方保留合法所有权，直至全额付款\n\n售后回租 卖方在出售后立即向买方出租房产，卖方的租金支付相当于贷款还款\n\n当卖方的留置权卖方将所有权转让给买方但购买价款未完全支付时，衡平法卖方利用衡平法卖方留置权为买方的购买提供资金\n\n契约合同（即分期付款土地合同）是一种抵押贷款替代方案。根据本合同，买方可以立即占有该财产，但卖方保留所有权，直到买方根据分期付款计划支付最后一笔款项。传统上，契约合同允许卖方保留所有分期付款，并在买方未能支付一笔款项的情况下重新获得所有权——即使除了一笔付款外，所有款项均已支付。然而，各州现在寻求通过以下方式协助违约买方： 将分期付款土地合同视为抵押，并要求卖方取消财产赎回权以获得明确的所有权（选择 A）\n\n通过支付剩余合同余额来向买方提供公平的赎回权，以保留占有权并获得所有权（选择 D）或\n\n允许卖方保留财产所有权，但要求买方以某种形式恢复原状，例如，退还合同付款，金额超出买方违约给卖方造成的损失（选择B）。\n\n然而，买方不能仅通过赔偿（即偿还）卖方的未付款项来重新获得所有权。因此，法院强制执行这一结果的可能性最小。\n\n教育目标：契约合同允许买方立即占有该财产，而卖方保留所有权，直到支付最后一笔分期付款。传统上，卖方可以保留所有分期付款，并在违约时重新收回所有权。但各州现在通过要求取消抵押品赎回权、提供公平的赎回权或给予买家赔偿来帮助违约买家。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 3.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（契据合同）。","conclusion":"答案 C：要求舞者赔偿经纪人拖欠的款项，以保留所有权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"leases","ipa":"/lˈisɪz/","syllables":"leases","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 租赁；租约；租赁协定（lease的复数）","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“leases”","wordForm":"词；原形 lease；词形 0:lease/1:s3","sourceSentence":"Mortgage alternatives Absolute deed Debtor gives deed to creditor with intent to secure loan (ie, equitable mortgage) Deed of trust Debtor gives deed of trust to third-party trustee as collateral for debt, & creditor can instruct trustee to foreclose upon default Installment land Debtor agrees to buy land through installment payments & gets contract immediate possession, but seller keeps legal title until paid in ful","synonyms":["rental","letting","term of a contract","rent"]},{"term":"rental","ipa":"/ɹˈɛntəl/","syllables":"rental","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 租费, 租金收入；a. 租赁的, 收取租金的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“rental”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:rentals","sourceSentence":"Mortgage alternatives Absolute deed Debtor gives deed to creditor with intent to secure loan (ie, equitable mortgage) Deed of trust Debtor gives deed of trust to third-party trustee as collateral for debt, & creditor can instruct trustee to foreclose upon default Installment land Debtor agrees to buy land through installment payments & gets contract immediate possession, but seller keeps legal title until paid in ful","synonyms":["renting","lease","letting"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":6,"sourceQuestionPage":15,"sourceQuestionPages":[15],"sourceExplanationPages":[16,17],"sourceAnswerPages":[16,17],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-007","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A seller owned property in fee simple, as the land records showed, when he contracted to sell the property to a buyer. Two weeks later, the buyer paid the agreed price and received a warranty deed. A week thereafter, when neither the contract nor the deed had been recorded and while the seller remained in possession of the property, a creditor properly filed a money judgment against the seller. The creditor knew nothing of the buyer's interest. A statute in the jurisdiction provides: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for ten years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.\" The creditor brought an appropriate action to enforce her lien against the property in the buyer's hands. If the court decides for the buyer, what will be the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The buyer was a purchaser without notice.","B":"The doctrine of equitable conversion applies.","C":"The jurisdiction's recording act does not protect creditors.","D":"The seller's possession gave the creditor constructive notice of the buyer's interest."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Payment of value\n\n(bona fide purchaser)\n\nValue Release of existing debt Substantial amount not grossly inadequate in relation to property interest's value No Security for existing debt value Nominal payment (eg, $1) Recital of consideration in deed Gift, devise, or inheritance Recording acts determine the priority of competing property interests. A notice statute (as seen here) only protects subsequent purchasers for value who lack notice of an earlier property interest. Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for an existing debt—not payment of valuable consideration. Therefore, judgment creditors are not protected under the notice statute, and the creditor here cannot enforce her judgment lien. But even if the recording act did apply, the creditor could not enforce her judgment lien against the property. That is because a judgment creditor can only enforce an interest in property that is owned by the debtor. Here, the property had already been conveyed to the buyer (ie, the seller no longer owned it) when the creditor recorded her judgment lien. Therefore, the creditor never acquired an interest in the property. (Choice A) Whether the buyer lacked notice would only matter if the buyer were trying to establish priority over an earlier property interest. Since the buyer has the earlier interest here, this information is irrelevant. (Choice B) Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, the buyer receives equitable title after the parties enter a land sales contract. But this doctrine does not apply when, as here, the buyer acquired legal title—eg, by paying the purchase price and receiving the deed— before the judgment lien was obtained. (Choice D) Inquiry notice (a form of constructive notice) exists when a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate—eg, visible use of the property. But here, whether the creditor had notice is irrelevant since she is not a buyer for value and the recording act does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for an existing debt—not payment of valuable\n\nconsideration. Additionally, a judgment creditor can only enforce an interest in property that is owned by the debtor.\n\nReferences\n46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 352 (2018) (priority of judgment liens).","educationalObjective":"Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for an existing debt—not payment of valuable\n\nconsideration. Additionally, a judgment creditor can only enforce an interest in property that is owned by the debtor.","references":"46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 352 (2018) (priority of judgment liens).","rule":"Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for an existing debt—not payment of valuable\n\nconsideration. Additionally, a judgment creditor can only enforce an interest in property that is owned by the debtor.","irac":{"issue":"If the court decides for the buyer, what will be the most likely reason?","rule":"Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for an existing debt—not payment of valuable\n\nconsideration. Additionally, a judgment creditor can only enforce an interest in property that is owned by the debtor.","analysis":"Payment of value\n\n(bona fide purchaser)\n\nValue Release of existing debt Substantial amount not grossly inadequate in relation to property interest's value No Security for existing debt value Nominal payment (eg, $1) Recital of consideration in deed Gift, devise, or inheritance Recording acts determine the priority of competing property interests. A notice statute (as seen here) only protects subsequent purchasers for value who lack notice of an earlier property interest. Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for an existing debt—not payment of valuable consideration. Therefore, judgment creditors are not protected under the notice statute, and the creditor here cannot enforce her judgment lien. But even if the recording act did apply, the creditor could not enforce her judgment lien against the property. That is because a judgment creditor can only enforce an interest in property that is owned by the debtor. Here, the property had already been conveyed to the buyer (ie, the seller no longer owned it) when the creditor recorded her judgment lien. Therefore, the creditor never acquired an interest in the property. (Choice A) Whether the buyer lacked notice would only matter if the buyer were trying to establish priority over an earlier property interest. Since the buyer has the earlier interest here, this information is irrelevant. (Choice B) Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, the buyer receives equitable title after the parties enter a land sales contract. But this doctrine does not apply when, as here, the buyer acquired legal title—eg, by paying the purchase price and receiving the deed— before the judgment lien was obtained. (Choice D) Inquiry notice (a form of constructive notice) exists when a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate—eg, visible use of the property. But here, whether the creditor had notice is irrelevant since she is not a buyer for value and the recording act does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer C: The jurisdiction's recording act does not protect creditors."},"questionZh":"正如土地记录所示，当卖方签订将财产出售给买方的合同时，他仅以简单的费用拥有财产。两周后，买方支付了约定的价格并收到了保修契约。一周后，当合同和契约都没有被记录并且卖方仍然占有该财产时，债权人对卖方提出了适当的金钱判决。债权人对买方的利益一无所知。该司法管辖区的一项法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起十年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”该司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“除非依法记录，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者。”债权人提起适当诉讼，强制执行对买方手中财产的留置权。如果法院做出有利于买方的裁决，最可能的理由是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"买家是在没有通知的情况下购买的。","B":"适用衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则。","C":"司法管辖区的记录行为并不保护债权人。","D":"卖方的占有向债权人推定了买方的利益。"},"explanationZh":"支付价值\n\n（善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser））\n\n价值 现有债务的免除 相对于财产权益的价值而言，相当大的金额并非严重不足 现有债务价值无担保 名义付款（例如，1 美元） 契约中的对价记录 礼物、设计或继承 记录行为决定了竞争财产权益的优先顺序。通知法规（如此处所示）仅保护后续购买者的价值，这些购买者缺乏对早期财产权益的通知。判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是现有债务的担保，而不是对价的支付。因此，判决债权人不受通知法的保护，债权人不能强制执行其判决留置权。但即使记录法确实适用，债权人也无法对财产强制执行其判决留置权。这是因为判决债权人只能强制执行债务人拥有的财产权益。在这里，当债权人记录其判决留置权时，财产已经转让给买方（即卖方不再拥有该财产）。因此，债权人从未获得该财产的权益。 （选项 A）仅当买方试图优先于较早的财产权益时，买方是否缺乏通知才重要。由于买方在此拥有较早的兴趣，因此此信息无关紧要。 （选择B） 根据衡平转换原则，买方在双方签订土地销售合同后获得衡平法所有权。但当买方在获得判决留置权之前获得法定所有权（例如，通过支付购买价款并收到契约）时，该原则并不适用。 （选项 D）当买方知道或应该知道会促使理性人进行调查的情况（例如，财产的可见使用）时，就存在询问通知（推定通知的一种形式）。但在这里，债权人是否有通知并不重要，因为她不是价值买家，记录法也不适用。\n\n教育目标：判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是现有债务的担保，而不是有价值的支付\n\n考虑。此外，判决债权人只能强制执行债务人拥有的财产的权益。\n\n参考文献\n46 上午。尤尔。 2d 判决 § 352 (2018)（判决留置权的优先权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是现有债务的担保，而不是有价值的支付\n\n考虑。此外，判决债权人只能强制执行债务人拥有的财产的权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院做出有利于买方的裁决，最可能的理由是什么？","rule":"判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是现有债务的担保，而不是有价值的支付\n\n考虑。此外，判决债权人只能强制执行债务人拥有的财产的权益。","analysis":"支付价值\n\n（善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser））\n\n价值 现有债务的免除 相对于财产权益的价值而言，相当大的金额并非严重不足 现有债务价值无担保 名义付款（例如，1 美元） 契约中的对价记录 礼物、设计或继承 记录行为决定了竞争财产权益的优先顺序。通知法规（如此处所示）仅保护后续购买者的价值，这些购买者缺乏对早期财产权益的通知。判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是现有债务的担保，而不是对价的支付。因此，判决债权人不受通知法的保护，债权人不能强制执行其判决留置权。但即使记录法确实适用，债权人也无法对财产强制执行其判决留置权。这是因为判决债权人只能强制执行债务人拥有的财产权益。在这里，当债权人记录其判决留置权时，财产已经转让给买方（即卖方不再拥有该财产）。因此，债权人从未获得该财产的权益。 （选项 A）仅当买方试图优先于较早的财产权益时，买方是否缺乏通知才重要。由于买方在此拥有较早的兴趣，因此此信息无关紧要。 （选择B） 根据衡平转换原则，买方在双方签订土地销售合同后获得衡平法所有权。但当买方在获得判决留置权之前获得法定所有权（例如，通过支付购买价款并收到契约）时，该原则并不适用。 （选项 D）当买方知道或应该知道会促使理性人进行调查的情况（例如，财产的可见使用）时，就存在询问通知（推定通知的一种形式）。但在这里，债权人是否有通知并不重要，因为她不是价值买家，记录法也不适用。\n\n教育目标：判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是现有债务的担保，而不是有价值的支付\n\n考虑。此外，判决债权人只能强制执行债务人拥有的财产的权益。\n\n参考文献\n46 上午。尤尔。 2d 判决 § 352 (2018)（判决留置权的优先权）。","conclusion":"答案 C：司法管辖区的记录行为并不保护债权人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"equitable conversion","ipa":"/ˈɛkwətəbəl kʌnvˈɝʒən/","syllables":"eq·ui·table con·ver·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"衡平转换：合同签订后买方视为衡平所有人，卖方保留价款请求权","contextualMeaning":"衡平转换：合同签订后买方视为衡平所有人，卖方保留价款请求权","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The doctrine of equitable conversion applies.","synonyms":["equitable ownership conversion"]},{"term":"bona fide purchaser","ipa":"/bˈoʊnə fˈaɪd pˈɝtʃəsɝ/","syllables":"bona fide pur·chaser","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"善意有偿购买人：支付价值且无在先权益通知的后手购买人","contextualMeaning":"善意有偿购买人：支付价值且无在先权益通知的后手购买人","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Payment of value (bona fide purchaser) Value Release of existing debt Substantial amount not grossly inadequate in relation to property interest's value No Security for existing debt value Nominal payment (eg, $1) Recital of consideration in deed Gift, devise, or inheritance Recording acts determine the priority of competing property interests.","synonyms":["BFP","good-faith purchaser for value"]},{"term":"notice statute","ipa":"/nˈoʊtəs stˈætʃut/","syllables":"no·tice statute","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"通知型登记法：无在先权益通知的后手有偿购买人取得优先","contextualMeaning":"通知型登记法：无在先权益通知的后手有偿购买人取得优先","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A notice statute (as seen here) only protects subsequent purchasers for value who lack notice of an earlier property interest.","synonyms":["notice recording act"]},{"term":"inquiry notice","ipa":"/ˌɪnkwˈaɪɹˌi nˈoʊtəs/","syllables":"in·quiry no·tice","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"调查通知：可疑事实足以促使合理购买人进一步调查的通知","contextualMeaning":"调查通知：可疑事实足以促使合理购买人进一步调查的通知","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) Inquiry notice (a form of constructive notice) exists when a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate—eg, visible use of the property.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"recording act","ipa":"/ɹʌkˈɔɹdɪŋ ˈækt/","syllables":"record·ing act","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"登记法：决定未登记或相互冲突土地权益优先次序的州法","contextualMeaning":"登记法：决定未登记或相互冲突土地权益优先次序的州法","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"\" The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":7,"sourceQuestionPage":18,"sourceQuestionPages":[18],"sourceExplanationPages":[19,20],"sourceAnswerPages":[19,20],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-008","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"Seven years ago, a man conveyed vacant land by warranty deed to a woman, a bona fide purchaser for value. The woman did not record the warranty deed and did not enter into possession of the land. Five years ago, the man conveyed the same land to a neighbor, also a bona fide purchaser for value, by a quitclaim deed. The neighbor immediately recorded the quitclaim deed and went into possession of the land. Two years ago, the neighbor conveyed the land to a friend, who had notice of the prior conveyance from the man to the woman. The friend never recorded the deed, but he went into immediate possession of the land. The jurisdiction has a notice recording statute and a grantor-grantee index system. If the woman sues to eject the friend, will the woman be likely to succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the friend took possession of the land before the woman did.","B":"No, because the neighbor's title was superior to the woman's title.","C":"Yes, because the friend had notice of the conveyance from the man to the woman.","D":"Yes, because the woman, unlike the friend, took title under a warranty deed."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Recording acts are used to evaluate the priority of competing interests in the same property. Most recording acts protect bona fide purchasers (BFPs)—ie, persons who: pay value for an interest in property and lack notice of a prior interest. In a jurisdiction with a notice recording act (as seen here), a BFP's property interest is always superior to an earlier competing property interest. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is protected from prior interests by the recording act to the same extent as the BFP. Here, the man first conveyed the vacant land to the woman (BFP) and then conveyed it to the neighbor (BFP). Since the neighbor was a BFP, the neighbor's title was superior to the woman's earlier title under the notice statute. And though the friend knew about the earlier conveyance to the woman when the neighbor conveyed the land to him (not a BFP), he is sheltered by the neighbor's BFP status (Choice C). As a result, the woman will likely lose her suit to eject (ie, remove) the friend from the land. (Choice A) The priority of competing property interests is not determined by who took possession first. Therefore, the friend's prior possession is not the reason the woman will lose. Instead, she will lose because the neighbor was a BFP and the friend is protected by the neighbor's BFP status under the Shelter Rule. (Choice D) A warranty deed, unlike a quitclaim deed, contains assurances regarding the quality of title conveyed. But the type of deed is irrelevant in determining priority among competing property interests. Therefore, the fact that the woman took title under a warranty deed is not a basis for her to succeed in her suit to eject the friend.\n\nEducational objective: In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser (BFP) without notice of an earlier competing property interest has priority over that competing interest. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is protected from prior interests to the same extent as the BFP.","educationalObjective":"In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser (BFP) without notice of an earlier competing property interest has priority over that competing interest. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is protected from prior interests to the same extent as the BFP.","references":"","rule":"In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser (BFP) without notice of an earlier competing property interest has priority over that competing interest. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is protected from prior interests to the same extent as the BFP.","irac":{"issue":"If the woman sues to eject the friend, will the woman be likely to succeed?","rule":"In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser (BFP) without notice of an earlier competing property interest has priority over that competing interest. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is protected from prior interests to the same extent as the BFP.","analysis":"Recording acts are used to evaluate the priority of competing interests in the same property. Most recording acts protect bona fide purchasers (BFPs)—ie, persons who: pay value for an interest in property and lack notice of a prior interest. In a jurisdiction with a notice recording act (as seen here), a BFP's property interest is always superior to an earlier competing property interest. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is protected from prior interests by the recording act to the same extent as the BFP. Here, the man first conveyed the vacant land to the woman (BFP) and then conveyed it to the neighbor (BFP). Since the neighbor was a BFP, the neighbor's title was superior to the woman's earlier title under the notice statute. And though the friend knew about the earlier conveyance to the woman when the neighbor conveyed the land to him (not a BFP), he is sheltered by the neighbor's BFP status (Choice C). As a result, the woman will likely lose her suit to eject (ie, remove) the friend from the land. (Choice A) The priority of competing property interests is not determined by who took possession first. Therefore, the friend's prior possession is not the reason the woman will lose. Instead, she will lose because the neighbor was a BFP and the friend is protected by the neighbor's BFP status under the Shelter Rule. (Choice D) A warranty deed, unlike a quitclaim deed, contains assurances regarding the quality of title conveyed. But the type of deed is irrelevant in determining priority among competing property interests. Therefore, the fact that the woman took title under a warranty deed is not a basis for her to succeed in her suit to eject the friend.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the neighbor's title was superior to the woman's title."},"questionZh":"七年前，一名男子通过保修契约将空置土地转让给一名真正的价值购买者。该妇女没有记录保修契约，也没有占有该土地。五年前，该男子通过一份无偿所有权契约将同一块土地转让给了一位邻居，这位邻居也是一位善意的价值购买者。邻居立即记录了放弃所有权契约并占有了这块土地。两年前，邻居将土地转让给一位朋友，而朋友已经注意到男方先前将土地转让给了女方。这位朋友从未记录过该契约，但他立即拥有了这片土地。该司法管辖区有通知登记法（recording act）和授予者-受让人索引系统。如果女方起诉驱逐朋友，女方有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为朋友先于女人占有了土地。","B":"不，因为邻居的头衔比女人的头衔高。","C":"是的，因为朋友已经注意到男方向女方转让房产的情况。","D":"是的，因为与朋友不同，这位女士根据保修契约获得了所有权。"},"explanationZh":"记录行为用于评估同一财产中竞争利益的优先顺序。大多数记录行为都保护善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（BFP），即那些满足以下条件的人：为财产权益支付价值并且不知道先前的权益。在具有通知记录法的司法管辖区（如此处所示），BFP 的财产权益始终优于早期竞争的财产权益。根据庇护规则，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人将受到与 BFP 相同程度的记录行为的保护，免受先前利益的影响。在这里，男人首先将空置土地转让给女人（BFP），然后再转让给邻居（BFP）。由于邻居是 BFP，根据通知法规，邻居的头衔高于该妇女之前的头衔。尽管当邻居将土地转让给他（不是 BFP）时，该朋友知道了早些时候向该妇女转让的情况，但他受到邻居 BFP 身份的保护（选项 C）。结果，该妇女可能会失去将朋友逐出土地的诉讼。 （选项A） 竞争性财产利益的优先顺序并不取决于谁先占有。因此，朋友的先占有并不是女人会输的原因。相反，她会输，因为邻居是 BFP，并且根据庇护规则，该朋友受到邻居 BFP 身份的保护。 （选项 D） 与无偿索赔契约不同，保修契约包含有关所传达的所有权质量的保证。但契约的类型与确定相互竞争的财产利益之间的优先顺序无关。因此，该女子根据保修契约取得所有权这一事实并不能成为她驱逐朋友的诉讼成功的基础。\n\n教育目标：在通知管辖区，善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 在未通知较早的竞争性财产权益的情况下，优先于该竞争性权益。根据庇护规则，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人将受到与 BFP 相同程度的保护，免受先前利益的影响。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在通知管辖区，善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（BFP）在未通知较早的竞争性财产权益的情况下，优先于该竞争性权益。根据庇护规则，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人将受到与 BFP 相同程度的保护，免受先前利益的影响。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果女方起诉驱逐朋友，女方有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"在通知管辖区，善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（BFP）在未通知较早的竞争性财产权益的情况下，优先于该竞争性权益。根据庇护规则，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人将受到与 BFP 相同程度的保护，免受先前利益的影响。","analysis":"记录行为用于评估同一财产中竞争利益的优先顺序。大多数记录行为都保护善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（BFP），即那些满足以下条件的人：为财产权益支付价值并且不知道先前的权益。在具有通知记录法的司法管辖区（如此处所示），BFP 的财产权益始终优于早期竞争的财产权益。根据庇护规则，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人将受到与 BFP 相同程度的记录行为的保护，免受先前利益的影响。在这里，男人首先将空置土地转让给女人（BFP），然后再转让给邻居（BFP）。由于邻居是 BFP，根据通知法规，邻居的头衔高于该妇女之前的头衔。尽管当邻居将土地转让给他（不是 BFP）时，该朋友知道了早些时候向该妇女转让的情况，但他受到邻居 BFP 身份的保护（选项 C）。结果，该妇女可能会失去将朋友逐出土地的诉讼。 （选项A） 竞争性财产利益的优先顺序并不取决于谁先占有。因此，朋友的先占有并不是女人会输的原因。相反，她会输，因为邻居是 BFP，并且根据庇护规则，该朋友受到邻居 BFP 身份的保护。 （选项 D） 与无偿索赔契约不同，保修契约包含有关所传达的所有权质量的保证。但契约的类型与确定相互竞争的财产利益之间的优先顺序无关。因此，该女子根据保修契约取得所有权这一事实并不能成为她驱逐朋友的诉讼成功的基础。\n\n教育目标：在通知管辖区，善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 在未通知较早的竞争性财产权益的情况下，优先于该竞争性权益。根据庇护规则，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人将受到与 BFP 相同程度的保护，免受先前利益的影响。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为邻居的头衔比女人的头衔高。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"quitclaim deed","ipa":"/'kwitkleim dˈid/","syllables":"quit·claim deed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"权利放弃契据：仅转让出让人现有权益，不保证产权有效","contextualMeaning":"权利放弃契据：仅转让出让人现有权益，不保证产权有效","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Five years ago, the man conveyed the same land to a neighbor, also a bona fide purchaser for value, by a quitclaim deed.","synonyms":["release deed","nonwarranty deed"]},{"term":"shelter rule","ipa":"/ʃˈɛltɝ ɹˈul/","syllables":"shel·ter rule","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"庇护规则：从受保护善意购买人处受让者通常承继其优先地位","contextualMeaning":"庇护规则：从受保护善意购买人处受让者通常承继其优先地位","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is protected from prior interests to the same extent as the BFP.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-16bb3a363e9d6e33"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":8,"sourceQuestionPage":21,"sourceQuestionPages":[21],"sourceExplanationPages":[22,23],"sourceAnswerPages":[22,23],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-009","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A large corporation owned property in fee simple, as the real estate records showed. The corporation entered into a valid written contract to convey the property to a shareholder. At closing, the shareholder paid the price in full and received an instrument in the proper form of a deed, signed by duly authorized corporate officers on behalf of the corporation, purporting to convey the property to the shareholder. The shareholder did not then record the deed or take possession of the property. Next, a creditor (who had no knowledge of the contract or the deed) obtained a substantial money judgment against the corporation. Then, the shareholder recorded the deed from the corporation. Thereafter, the creditor properly filed the judgment against the corporation. A statute of the jurisdiction provides: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for ten years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" Afterward, the shareholder entered into a valid written contract to convey the property to a local businessman. The businessman objected to the shareholder's title and refused to close. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"Unless the same be recorded according to law, no conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice.\" The shareholder brought an appropriate action to require the businessman to complete the purchase contract. For whom should the court decide?","choices":{"A":"The businessman, because even though the corporation's deed to the shareholder prevented the creditor's judgment from being a lien on the property, the creditor's filed judgment poses a threat of litigation.","B":"The businessman, because the creditor's judgment was obtained before the shareholder recorded the deed from the corporation.","C":"The shareholder, because the shareholder received the deed from the corporation before the creditor filed his judgment.","D":"The shareholder, because the shareholder recorded her deed before the creditor filed his judgment."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A money judgment is a court order awarding the plaintiff (ie, creditor) a sum of money that the defendant (ie, debtor) must pay. A judgment lien is created when: the creditor obtains a money judgment against the debtor and the money judgment is properly filed in the land records (majority rule). Once a judgment lien is created, it attaches to the debtor's property and (1) prevents the owner from conveying marketable title and (2) remains with the property even if it is later conveyed to another. However, the lien can only attach to property owned by the debtor at the time the lien is filed. Here, the creditor filed his judgment against the corporation after the shareholder had received the deed from the corporation—ie, when the corporation no longer owned the property. As a result, the judgment lien did not attach to the property, and the shareholder can convey marketable title. The court should therefore find in her favor and require the businessman to specifically perform the purchase contract. (Choice A) A threat of litigation renders title unmarketable if a third party (eg, the creditor) could reasonably claim a superior interest in the buyer's property. Since the corporation's deed to the shareholder prevented the creditor's subsequent judgment from being a lien on the property, the creditor has no interest in the property. Therefore, though the creditor's filed judgment may pose a threat of litigation, the shareholder can convey marketable title. (Choice B) The creditor's judgment would have attached to the property only if it had been filed before the shareholder received her deed. The fact that it was obtained before she recorded her deed is immaterial. (Choice D) Recording is not required to transfer an interest in property. And since the property transferred to the shareholder when she received her deed (before the judgment was filed), she will prevail on that basis instead.\n\nEducational objective: A properly filed judgment lien attaches to the debtor's property. The lien prevents the owner from conveying marketable title and remains with the property even if it is later conveyed to another. But a judgment lien can only attach to property owned by the debtor at the time the judgment lien is filed.\n\nReferences\n46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 352 (2018) (priority of judgment liens).","educationalObjective":"A properly filed judgment lien attaches to the debtor's property. The lien prevents the owner from conveying marketable title and remains with the property even if it is later conveyed to another. But a judgment lien can only attach to property owned by the debtor at the time the judgment lien is filed.","references":"46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 352 (2018) (priority of judgment liens).","rule":"A properly filed judgment lien attaches to the debtor's property. The lien prevents the owner from conveying marketable title and remains with the property even if it is later conveyed to another. But a judgment lien can only attach to property owned by the debtor at the time the judgment lien is filed.","irac":{"issue":"For whom should the court decide?","rule":"A properly filed judgment lien attaches to the debtor's property. The lien prevents the owner from conveying marketable title and remains with the property even if it is later conveyed to another. But a judgment lien can only attach to property owned by the debtor at the time the judgment lien is filed.","analysis":"A money judgment is a court order awarding the plaintiff (ie, creditor) a sum of money that the defendant (ie, debtor) must pay. A judgment lien is created when: the creditor obtains a money judgment against the debtor and the money judgment is properly filed in the land records (majority rule). Once a judgment lien is created, it attaches to the debtor's property and (1) prevents the owner from conveying marketable title and (2) remains with the property even if it is later conveyed to another. However, the lien can only attach to property owned by the debtor at the time the lien is filed. Here, the creditor filed his judgment against the corporation after the shareholder had received the deed from the corporation—ie, when the corporation no longer owned the property. As a result, the judgment lien did not attach to the property, and the shareholder can convey marketable title. The court should therefore find in her favor and require the businessman to specifically perform the purchase contract. (Choice A) A threat of litigation renders title unmarketable if a third party (eg, the creditor) could reasonably claim a superior interest in the buyer's property. Since the corporation's deed to the shareholder prevented the creditor's subsequent judgment from being a lien on the property, the creditor has no interest in the property. Therefore, though the creditor's filed judgment may pose a threat of litigation, the shareholder can convey marketable title. (Choice B) The creditor's judgment would have attached to the property only if it had been filed before the shareholder received her deed. The fact that it was obtained before she recorded her deed is immaterial. (Choice D) Recording is not required to transfer an interest in property. And since the property transferred to the shareholder when she received her deed (before the judgment was filed), she will prevail on that basis instead.","conclusion":"Answer C: The shareholder, because the shareholder received the deed from the corporation before the creditor filed his judgment."},"questionZh":"正如房地产记录所示，一家大公司拥有的财产很简单。公司签订了有效的书面合同，将财产转让给股东。交割时，股东全额支付了价款，并收到了由正式授权的公司官员代表公司签署的适当契约形式的文书，旨在将财产转让给股东。股东随后没有记录契约或占有财产。接下来，债权人（不了解合同或契约）获得了针对该公司的巨额金钱判决。然后，股东记录了公司的契约。此后，债权人对公司作出了适当的判决。该司法管辖区的法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起十年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”随后，股东签订了有效的书面合同，将财产转让给当地商人。该商人对股东的头衔提出异议并拒绝关闭。司法管辖区的备案法规定：“除非依法备案，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者的价值。”股东提起适当诉讼，要求商人完成购买合同。法院应该为谁做出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"商人，因为即使公司对股东的契约阻止了债权人的判决成为财产留置权，但债权人提出的判决构成了诉讼的威胁。","B":"商人，因为债权人的判断是在股东记录公司契约之前获得的。","C":"股东，因为股东在债权人提出判决之前收到了公司的契约。","D":"股东，因为股东在债权人提交判决之前记录了她的行为。"},"explanationZh":"金钱判决是法院命令，判给原告（即债权人）被告（即债务人）必须支付的一笔金钱。在以下情况下，判决留置权产生： 债权人获得针对债务人的金钱判决，并且金钱判决已正确归档在土地记录中（多数规则）。判决留置权一旦设立，它就附着在债务人的财产上，并且 (1) 阻止所有者转让可出售的所有权，并且 (2) 即使后来将其转让给他人，该财产仍保留在该财产中。但是，留置权只能附加到提交留置权时债务人拥有的财产。在此，债权人在股东收到公司的契约后（即公司不再拥有该财产时）对公司提出判决。因此，判决留置权不附加在财产上，股东可以转让可出售的所有权。因此，法院应做出对她有利的判决，并要求商家具体履行购买合同。 （选择A）如果第三方（例如债权人）可以合理地主张买方财产的优先利益，则诉讼威胁会使所有权无法出售。由于公司对股东的契约阻止了债权人随后的判决成为对该财产的留置权，因此债权人对该财产不享有任何权益。因此，尽管债权人提交的判决可能会构成诉讼威胁，但股东可以转让可出售的所有权。 （选择B）只有在股东收到她的契约之前提交债权人的判决时，债权人的判决才会附加到该财产上。事实上，它是在她记录自己的行为之前获得的，这一事实并不重要。 （选择 D）转让财产权益不需要进行记录。由于财产在她收到契约时（在判决提交之前）转移给股东，因此她将以此为基础胜诉。\n\n教育目标：正确提交的判决留置权附加在债务人的财产上。留置权阻止所有者转让可出售的所有权，并且即使后来转让给他人，该财产仍保留在该财产中。但判决留置权只能附加到提交判决留置权时债务人拥有的财产。\n\n参考文献\n46 上午。尤尔。 2d 判决 § 352 (2018)（判决留置权的优先权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"正确提交的判决留置权附在债务人的财产上。留置权阻止所有者转让可出售的所有权，并且即使后来转让给他人，该财产仍保留在该财产中。但判决留置权只能附加到提交判决留置权时债务人拥有的财产。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该为谁做出裁决？","rule":"正确提交的判决留置权附在债务人的财产上。留置权阻止所有者转让可出售的所有权，并且即使后来转让给他人，该财产仍保留在该财产中。但判决留置权只能附加到提交判决留置权时债务人拥有的财产。","analysis":"金钱判决是法院命令，判给原告（即债权人）被告（即债务人）必须支付的一笔金钱。在以下情况下，判决留置权产生： 债权人获得针对债务人的金钱判决，并且金钱判决已正确归档在土地记录中（多数规则）。判决留置权一旦设立，它就附着在债务人的财产上，并且 (1) 阻止所有者转让可出售的所有权，并且 (2) 即使后来将其转让给他人，该财产仍保留在该财产中。但是，留置权只能附加到提交留置权时债务人拥有的财产。在此，债权人在股东收到公司的契约后（即公司不再拥有该财产时）对公司提出判决。因此，判决留置权不附加在财产上，股东可以转让可出售的所有权。因此，法院应做出对她有利的判决，并要求商家具体履行购买合同。 （选择A）如果第三方（例如债权人）可以合理地主张买方财产的优先利益，则诉讼威胁会使所有权无法出售。由于公司对股东的契约阻止了债权人随后的判决成为对该财产的留置权，因此债权人对该财产不享有任何权益。因此，尽管债权人提交的判决可能会构成诉讼威胁，但股东可以转让可出售的所有权。 （选择B）只有在股东收到她的契约之前提交债权人的判决时，债权人的判决才会附加到该财产上。事实上，它是在她记录自己的行为之前获得的，这一事实并不重要。 （选择 D）转让财产权益不需要进行记录。由于财产在她收到契约时（在判决提交之前）转移给股东，因此她将以此为基础胜诉。\n\n教育目标：正确提交的判决留置权附加在债务人的财产上。留置权阻止所有者转让可出售的所有权，并且即使后来转让给他人，该财产仍保留在该财产中。但判决留置权只能附加到提交判决留置权时债务人拥有的财产。\n\n参考文献\n46 上午。尤尔。 2d 判决 § 352 (2018)（判决留置权的优先权）。","conclusion":"答案 C：股东，因为股东在债权人提出判决之前收到了公司的契约。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"marketable title","ipa":"/mˈɑɹkətəbəl tˈaɪtəl/","syllables":"mar·ketable ti·tle","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"可交易产权：不存在合理诉讼风险或重大产权瑕疵的产权","contextualMeaning":"可交易产权：不存在合理诉讼风险或重大产权瑕疵的产权","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The lien prevents the owner from conveying marketable title and remains with the property even if it is later conveyed to another.","synonyms":["merchantable title"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-cbb9071d35ccc28e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":9,"sourceQuestionPage":24,"sourceQuestionPages":[24],"sourceExplanationPages":[25,26],"sourceAnswerPages":[25,26],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-010","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man borrowed money from a bank and executed a promissory note for the amount secured by a mortgage on an office building that he owned. Several years later, the man sold the building. As specified in the contract of sale, the deed to the buyer provided that the buyer agreed \"to assume the existing mortgage debt\" on the building. Subsequently, the buyer defaulted on the mortgage loan to the bank, and appropriate foreclosure proceedings were initiated. The foreclosure sale resulted in a deficiency. There is no applicable statute. Is the buyer liable for the deficiency?","choices":{"A":"No, because even if the buyer assumed the mortgage, the man is solely responsible for any deficiency.","B":"No, because the buyer did not sign a promissory note to the bank and therefore has no personal liability.","C":"Yes, because the buyer assumed the mortgage and therefore became personally liable for the mortgage loan and any deficiency.","D":"Yes, because the transfer of the mortgage debt to the buyer resulted in a novation of the original mortgage and loan and rendered the buyer solely responsible for any deficiency."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Mortgage documents are used to convey a lender (ie, mortgagee) an interest in real property to secure a debt. The debtor (ie, mortgagor) can freely transfer mortgaged property unless the parties agree otherwise. After the transfer, the mortgage remains attached to the property and the debtor remains personally liable for the mortgage debt. But the grantee's obligations depend on whether the grantee: took subject to the mortgage – in which case, the grantee does not agree to pay and is not personally liable for the debt or assumed the mortgage – in which case, the grantee expressly agrees to pay and becomes personally liable for the debt, while the debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. When a grantee assumes the mortgage (as seen here) and defaults on the mortgage loan, the grantee is primarily liable for any deficiency from the foreclosure sale (Choice B). Therefore, the buyer (grantee) is primarily liable for the deficiency here (Choice A). (Choice D) A novation is a separate agreement between a lender and debtor in which (1) the lender agrees to substitute a new party for the debtor and (2) the debtor is excused from liability for the debt. Therefore, merely transferring the mortgage debt to another does not create a novation.\n\nEducational objective: A party who assumes a mortgage expressly agrees to become personally liable for the mortgage debt. That means the party assuming the mortgage is primarily liable for paying any deficiency resulting from a foreclosure sale and the debtor is secondarily liable as a surety.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (assuming a mortgage debt).","educationalObjective":"A party who assumes a mortgage expressly agrees to become personally liable for the mortgage debt. That means the party assuming the mortgage is primarily liable for paying any deficiency resulting from a foreclosure sale and the debtor is secondarily liable as a surety.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (assuming a mortgage debt).","rule":"A party who assumes a mortgage expressly agrees to become personally liable for the mortgage debt. That means the party assuming the mortgage is primarily liable for paying any deficiency resulting from a foreclosure sale and the debtor is secondarily liable as a surety.","irac":{"issue":"Is the buyer liable for the deficiency?","rule":"A party who assumes a mortgage expressly agrees to become personally liable for the mortgage debt. That means the party assuming the mortgage is primarily liable for paying any deficiency resulting from a foreclosure sale and the debtor is secondarily liable as a surety.","analysis":"Mortgage documents are used to convey a lender (ie, mortgagee) an interest in real property to secure a debt. The debtor (ie, mortgagor) can freely transfer mortgaged property unless the parties agree otherwise. After the transfer, the mortgage remains attached to the property and the debtor remains personally liable for the mortgage debt. But the grantee's obligations depend on whether the grantee: took subject to the mortgage – in which case, the grantee does not agree to pay and is not personally liable for the debt or assumed the mortgage – in which case, the grantee expressly agrees to pay and becomes personally liable for the debt, while the debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. When a grantee assumes the mortgage (as seen here) and defaults on the mortgage loan, the grantee is primarily liable for any deficiency from the foreclosure sale (Choice B). Therefore, the buyer (grantee) is primarily liable for the deficiency here (Choice A). (Choice D) A novation is a separate agreement between a lender and debtor in which (1) the lender agrees to substitute a new party for the debtor and (2) the debtor is excused from liability for the debt. Therefore, merely transferring the mortgage debt to another does not create a novation.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the buyer assumed the mortgage and therefore became personally liable for the mortgage loan and any deficiency."},"questionZh":"一名男子从银行借了钱，并以他拥有的办公楼抵押贷款的金额签发了本票。几年后，该男子卖掉了这栋大楼。根据销售合同的规定，买方的契约规定买方同意“承担该建筑物的现有抵押债务”。随后，买方拖欠银行抵押贷款，并启动了适当的止赎程序。取消抵押品赎回权的出售导致了缺陷。没有适用的法规。买方是否需要对缺陷承担责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为即使买家承担了抵押贷款，该男子也应对任何不足承担全部责任。","B":"不会，因为买方没有向银行签署本票，因此不承担个人责任。","C":"是的，因为买方承担了抵押贷款，因此对抵押贷款和任何不足承担个人责任。","D":"是的，因为将抵押贷款债务转移给买方导致了原始抵押贷款和贷款的更新，并使买方对任何不足承担全部责任。"},"explanationZh":"抵押文件用于向贷款人（即抵押权人）传达不动产权益以担保债务。债务人（即抵押人）可以自由转让抵押财产，但当事人另有约定的除外。转让后，抵押仍然附着在财产上，债务人仍然对抵押债务承担个人责任。但受让人的义务取决于受让人是否： 承担抵押——在这种情况下，受让人不同意付款且不对债务承担个人责任或承担抵押——在这种情况下，受让人明确同意付款并对债务承担个人责任，而债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。当受让人承担抵押贷款（如此处所示）并拖欠抵押贷款时，受让人主要对止赎出售造成的任何缺陷承担责任（选择 B）。因此，买方（受让人）对这里的缺陷负有主要责任（选择A）。 （选项 D）更替是贷款人和债务人之间的单独协议，其中 (1) 贷款人同意用新的当事人替代债务人，并且 (2) 债务人免除债务责任。因此，仅仅将抵押债务转移给另一个人并不构成更新。\n\n教育目标：承担抵押贷款的一方明确同意对抵押贷款债务承担个人责任。这意味着承担抵押贷款的一方主要负责支付取消抵押品赎回权出售造成的任何不足，债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.1（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（假设有抵押贷款债务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"承担抵押的一方明确同意对抵押债务承担个人责任。这意味着承担抵押贷款的一方主要负责支付取消抵押品赎回权出售造成的任何不足，债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"买方是否需要对缺陷承担责任？","rule":"承担抵押的一方明确同意对抵押债务承担个人责任。这意味着承担抵押贷款的一方主要负责支付取消抵押品赎回权出售造成的任何不足，债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。","analysis":"抵押文件用于向贷款人（即抵押权人）传达不动产权益以担保债务。债务人（即抵押人）可以自由转让抵押财产，但当事人另有约定的除外。转让后，抵押仍然附着在财产上，债务人仍然对抵押债务承担个人责任。但受让人的义务取决于受让人是否： 承担抵押——在这种情况下，受让人不同意付款且不对债务承担个人责任或承担抵押——在这种情况下，受让人明确同意付款并对债务承担个人责任，而债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。当受让人承担抵押贷款（如此处所示）并拖欠抵押贷款时，受让人主要对止赎出售造成的任何缺陷承担责任（选择 B）。因此，买方（受让人）对这里的缺陷负有主要责任（选择A）。 （选项 D）更替是贷款人和债务人之间的单独协议，其中 (1) 贷款人同意用新的当事人替代债务人，并且 (2) 债务人免除债务责任。因此，仅仅将抵押债务转移给另一个人并不构成更新。\n\n教育目标：承担抵押贷款的一方明确同意对抵押贷款债务承担个人责任。这意味着承担抵押贷款的一方主要负责支付取消抵押品赎回权出售造成的任何不足，债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.1（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（假设有抵押贷款债务）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为买方承担了抵押贷款，因此对抵押贷款和任何不足承担个人责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"subject to the mortgage","ipa":"/sʌbdʒˈɛkt tˈu ðə mˈɔɹɡədʒ/","syllables":"sub·ject to the mort·gage","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"受抵押约束取得：受让土地但不对抵押债务承担个人责任","contextualMeaning":"受抵押约束取得：受让土地但不对抵押债务承担个人责任","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"But the grantee's obligations depend on whether the grantee: took subject to the mortgage – in which case, the grantee does not agree to pay and is not personally liable for the debt or assumed the mortgage – in which case, the grantee expressly agrees to pay and becomes personally liable for the debt, while the debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"grantee","ipa":"/græn'ti:/","syllables":"grantee","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 受让人, 被授予人；[经] 受让人, 被授与者","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“grantee”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:grantees","sourceSentence":"But the grantee's obligations depend on whether the grantee: took subject to the mortgage – in which case, the grantee does not agree to pay and is not personally liable for the debt or assumed the mortgage – in which case, the grantee expressly agrees to pay and becomes personally liable for the debt, while the debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety.","synonyms":["alienee"]},{"term":"deficiency","ipa":"/dɪfˈɪʃənsi/","syllables":"de·fi·ciency","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 缺乏, 不足；[医] 缺乏, 不足","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“deficiency”","wordForm":"词；词形 s:deficiencies","sourceSentence":"The foreclosure sale resulted in a deficiency.","synonyms":["insufficiency","inadequacy","lack","want"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-65d2c11ad335daa7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":10,"sourceQuestionPage":27,"sourceQuestionPages":[27],"sourceExplanationPages":[28,29],"sourceAnswerPages":[28,29],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-011","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Co-ownership","question":"Fifteen years ago, two men who were fishing buddies moved onto vacant rural land owned by a woman they didn't know and built a small fishing shack on it. Twelve years ago, the men replaced the shack with a fish processing plant and a commercial fishing boat dock. The men maintained their commercial fishery operation on the land until one of them died intestate last year, leaving a sole heir. The period of time to acquire title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 10 years. The woman has now become aware of the changes that have occurred on the land. In an action to determine title, for whom should the court decide?","choices":{"A":"The man who is still alive and the deceased man's heir, because the men acquired title as tenants in common.","B":"The man who is still alive, because he is the surviving adverse possessor.","C":"The woman, because the use was changed by the men while they were in possession.","D":"The woman, because title cannot be claimed by two adverse possessors simultaneously."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Types of concurrent estates\n\n(real property owned by two or more persons)\n\nCreation language Succession of interest\n\nTenancy in \"To A and B\" No right of survivorship common* \"To A and B as tenants in common\"\n\nJoint tenancy \"To A and B as joint tenants with the Right of survivorship right of survivorship\"\n\nTenancy by the \"To A and B as husband and wife\" Indestructible right of entirety survivorship \"To A and B as tenants by the entirety\"\n\n*Presumed unless creation language states otherwise\n\nAn adverse possessor can acquire title to land owned by another if his/her possession of the land is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the land Non-permissive – hostile and adverse to the owner When land is adversely possessed by two or more persons, the possessors acquire a concurrent ownership interest as tenants in common. Since a tenancy in common has no right of survivorship, a co-tenant's ownership interest will pass to his/her heirs if the co- tenant dies intestate (ie, without a will). Here, the two men moved onto the land without the woman's permission or participation, opened a fishing operation, and continuously remained on the property for the 10-year statutory period (Choice D). As a result, the men divested the woman of title through adverse possession and became co-tenants in common. Since one of the men later died intestate, his concurrent ownership interest passed to his sole heir. Therefore, the living man and the sole heir own the land as tenants in common. (Choice B) The living man would have acquired sole ownership of the property upon the other man's death if the men had a joint tenancy—a concurrent ownership interest with the right of survivorship. But since the men only acquired a tenancy in common (no right of\n\nsurvivorship) by adverse possession, the deceased man's interest passed to his sole heir— not the surviving adverse possessor. (Choice C) To satisfy the continuous-use requirement, an adverse possessor must (1) constantly remain on the property or (2) consistently use the property in the same manner a usual owner would—eg, seasonal use of a hunting cabin, intermittent use for grazing cattle. Here, the men constantly remained on the property, so it does not matter that their use of the property changed.\n\nEducational objective: When two or more persons adversely possess the land of another, they acquire a tenancy in common—a concurrent ownership interest with no right of survivorship.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (First) of Property § 65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (creation of tenancy in common).\n\nRestatement (First) of Property §§ 12–13 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (transferring and devising a property interest).","educationalObjective":"When two or more persons adversely possess the land of another, they acquire a tenancy in common—a concurrent ownership interest with no right of survivorship.","references":"Restatement (First) of Property § 65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (creation of tenancy in common).\n\nRestatement (First) of Property §§ 12–13 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (transferring and devising a property interest).","rule":"When two or more persons adversely possess the land of another, they acquire a tenancy in common—a concurrent ownership interest with no right of survivorship.","irac":{"issue":"In an action to determine title, for whom should the court decide?","rule":"When two or more persons adversely possess the land of another, they acquire a tenancy in common—a concurrent ownership interest with no right of survivorship.","analysis":"Types of concurrent estates\n\n(real property owned by two or more persons)\n\nCreation language Succession of interest\n\nTenancy in \"To A and B\" No right of survivorship common* \"To A and B as tenants in common\"\n\nJoint tenancy \"To A and B as joint tenants with the Right of survivorship right of survivorship\"\n\nTenancy by the \"To A and B as husband and wife\" Indestructible right of entirety survivorship \"To A and B as tenants by the entirety\"\n\n*Presumed unless creation language states otherwise\n\nAn adverse possessor can acquire title to land owned by another if his/her possession of the land is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the land Non-permissive – hostile and adverse to the owner When land is adversely possessed by two or more persons, the possessors acquire a concurrent ownership interest as tenants in common. Since a tenancy in common has no right of survivorship, a co-tenant's ownership interest will pass to his/her heirs if the co- tenant dies intestate (ie, without a will). Here, the two men moved onto the land without the woman's permission or participation, opened a fishing operation, and continuously remained on the property for the 10-year statutory period (Choice D). As a result, the men divested the woman of title through adverse possession and became co-tenants in common. Since one of the men later died intestate, his concurrent ownership interest passed to his sole heir. Therefore, the living man and the sole heir own the land as tenants in common. (Choice B) The living man would have acquired sole ownership of the property upon the other man's death if the men had a joint tenancy—a concurrent ownership interest with the right of survivorship. But since the men only acquired a tenancy in common (no right of\n\nsurvivorship) by adverse possession, the deceased man's interest passed to his sole heir— not the surviving adverse possessor. (Choice C) To satisfy the continuous-use requirement, an adverse possessor must (1) constantly remain on the property or (2) consistently use the property in the same manner a usual owner would—eg, seasonal use of a hunting cabin, intermittent use for grazing cattle. Here, the men constantly remained on the property, so it does not matter that their use of the property changed.","conclusion":"Answer A: The man who is still alive and the deceased man's heir, because the men acquired title as tenants in common."},"questionZh":"十五年前，两个打鱼好友搬到了一个他们不认识的女人拥有的空置农村土地上，并在上面建了一个小渔棚。十二年前，这些人用鱼类加工厂和商业渔船码头取代了棚屋。这些人在这片土地上维持着商业渔业经营，直到其中一人去年去世时没有留下遗嘱，留下了唯一的继承人。在管辖区内通过逆权占有取得所有权的期限为10年。这位女士现在已经意识到这片土地上发生的变化。在确定所有权的诉讼中，法院应该为谁做出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"仍然活着的人以及已故人的继承人，因为这些人获得了共同佃户的所有权。","B":"还活着的人，因为他是幸存的逆权占有人。","C":"女人，因为男人在拥有时改变了用途。","D":"女子，因为所有权不能同时被两个逆权占有人主张。"},"explanationZh":"共有遗产的类型\n\n（两人或两人以上拥有的不动产）\n\n创作语言 兴趣传承\n\n“A 和 B”中的租赁 无共同生存权* “A 和 B 作为按份共有人（tenant in common）”\n\n联权共有“A 和 B 作为联权共有人，享有生存权”\n\n“甲、乙为夫妻”的租赁权 不可剥夺的整体生存权“甲、乙为全体租户”\n\n*假设除非创作语言另有说明\n\n逆权占有者可以获得他人拥有的土地的所有权，如果他/她对土地的占有是： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者来说明显或可见 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 排他性 – 不与所有者共享 实际 – 实际存在于土地上 不允许 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 当土地被两个或两个以上的人逆权占有时，占有者作为按份共有人（tenant in common）获得共同的所有权权益。由于共同共有（joint tenancy）没有生存权，如果按份共有人（tenant in common）去世时没有留下遗嘱（即没有遗嘱），则按份共有人（tenant in common）的所有权权益将转移给他/她的继承人。在这里，两名男子在未经妇女许可或参与的情况下搬到这片土地上，开始捕鱼作业，并在 10 年法定期限内继续留在该土地上（选项 D）。结果，男人们通过逆权占有剥夺了女人的所有权，成为按份共有人（tenant in common）。由于其中一名男子后来去世时没有留下遗嘱，因此他的共同所有权权益转移给了他的唯一继承人。因此，活着的人和唯一的继承人作为共同佃户拥有土地。 （选择 B） 如果活着的人拥有共同共有权（即具有生存权的共同所有权权益），那么在世的人将在另一人去世后获得该财产的唯一所有权。但由于这些人只获得了一份共同共有（joint tenancy）权（无权\n\n通过逆权占有，死者的权益转移给他的唯一继承人，而不是幸存的逆权占有人。 （选择C）为了满足持续使用的要求，逆权占有人必须（1）持续留在该财产上，或（2）以与通常所有者相同的方式持续使用该财产，例如季节性使用狩猎小屋，间歇性地用于放牧牛。在这里，男人们一直留在这片土地上，所以他们对土地用途的改变并不重要。\n\n教育目标：当两个或两个以上的人反向占有另一个人的土地时，他们就获得了共同的租赁权——一种没有生存权的共同所有权权益。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 65（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（共同共有（joint tenancy）的设立）。\n\n财产重述（第一）§§ 12–13（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（转让和设计财产权益）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当两个或两个以上的人不利地占有另一个人的土地时，他们就获得了共同共有（joint tenancy）权——一种没有生存权的共同所有权权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"在确定所有权的诉讼中，法院应该为谁做出裁决？","rule":"当两个或两个以上的人不利地占有另一个人的土地时，他们就获得了共同共有（joint tenancy）权——一种没有生存权的共同所有权权益。","analysis":"共有遗产的类型\n\n（两人或两人以上拥有的不动产）\n\n创作语言 兴趣传承\n\n“A 和 B”中的租赁 无共同生存权* “A 和 B 作为按份共有人（tenant in common）”\n\n联权共有“A 和 B 作为联权共有人，享有生存权”\n\n“甲、乙为夫妻”的租赁权 不可剥夺的整体生存权“甲、乙为全体租户”\n\n*假设除非创作语言另有说明\n\n逆权占有者可以获得他人拥有的土地的所有权，如果他/她对土地的占有是： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者来说明显或可见 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 排他性 – 不与所有者共享 实际 – 实际存在于土地上 不允许 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 当土地被两个或两个以上的人逆权占有时，占有者作为按份共有人（tenant in common）获得共同的所有权权益。由于共同共有（joint tenancy）没有生存权，如果按份共有人（tenant in common）去世时没有留下遗嘱（即没有遗嘱），则按份共有人（tenant in common）的所有权权益将转移给他/她的继承人。在这里，两名男子在未经妇女许可或参与的情况下搬到这片土地上，开始捕鱼作业，并在 10 年法定期限内继续留在该土地上（选项 D）。结果，男人们通过逆权占有剥夺了女人的所有权，成为按份共有人（tenant in common）。由于其中一名男子后来去世时没有留下遗嘱，因此他的共同所有权权益转移给了他的唯一继承人。因此，活着的人和唯一的继承人作为共同佃户拥有土地。 （选择 B） 如果活着的人拥有共同共有权（即具有生存权的共同所有权权益），那么在世的人将在另一人去世后获得该财产的唯一所有权。但由于这些人只获得了一份共同共有（joint tenancy）权（无权\n\n通过逆权占有，死者的权益转移给他的唯一继承人，而不是幸存的逆权占有人。 （选择C）为了满足持续使用的要求，逆权占有人必须（1）持续留在该财产上，或（2）以与通常所有者相同的方式持续使用该财产，例如季节性使用狩猎小屋，间歇性地用于放牧牛。在这里，男人们一直留在这片土地上，所以他们对土地用途的改变并不重要。\n\n教育目标：当两个或两个以上的人反向占有另一个人的土地时，他们就获得了共同的租赁权——一种没有生存权的共同所有权权益。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 65（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（共同共有（joint tenancy）的设立）。\n\n财产重述（第一）§§ 12–13（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（转让和设计财产权益）。","conclusion":"答案 A：仍然活着的人以及已故人的继承人，因为这些人获得了共同佃户的所有权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"right of survivorship","ipa":"/ɹˈaɪt ˈəv sə'vaivəʃip/","syllables":"right of sur·vivor·ship","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"生存者取得权：一名共同共有人死亡时其份额自动归其余生存共有人","contextualMeaning":"生存者取得权：一名共同共有人死亡时其份额自动归其余生存共有人","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"When two or more persons adversely possess the land of another, they acquire a tenancy in common—a concurrent ownership interest with no right of survivorship.","synonyms":["survivorship right"]},{"term":"tenancy in common","ipa":"/tˈɛnənsi ɪn kˈɑmən/","syllables":"ten·ancy in com·mon","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"按份共有：各共有人有独立可转让份额且无生存者取得权","contextualMeaning":"按份共有：各共有人有独立可转让份额且无生存者取得权","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"When two or more persons adversely possess the land of another, they acquire a tenancy in common—a concurrent ownership interest with no right of survivorship.","synonyms":["co-ownership without survivorship"]},{"term":"joint tenancy","ipa":"/dʒˈɔɪnt tˈɛnənsi/","syllables":"joint ten·ancy","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"共同共有：共有人份额平等并带生存者取得权的共有形式","contextualMeaning":"共同共有：共有人份额平等并带生存者取得权的共有形式","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Types of concurrent estates (real property owned by two or more persons) Creation language Succession of interest Tenancy in \"To A and B\" No right of survivorship common* \"To A and B as tenants in common\" Joint tenancy \"To A and B as joint tenants with the Right of survivorship right of survivorship\" Tenancy by the \"To A and B as husband and wife\" Indestructible right of entirety survivorship \"To A and B as tenants b","synonyms":["joint ownership with survivorship"]},{"term":"tenant","ipa":"/tˈɛnənt/","syllables":"ten·ant","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 承租人, 房客, 居住者；vt. 租借；[计] 占据者","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“tenant”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:tenants/d:tenanted/p:tenanted/i:tenanting/3:tenants","sourceSentence":"The man who is still alive and the deceased man's heir, because the men acquired title as tenants in common.","synonyms":["renter"]},{"term":"possessor","ipa":"/pʌzˈɛsɝ/","syllables":"pos·ses·sor","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 持有人, 所有人；[法] 拥有的, 所有者, 持有人","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“possessor”","wordForm":"词；词形 s:possessors","sourceSentence":"The man who is still alive, because he is the surviving adverse possessor.","synonyms":["owner"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":11,"sourceQuestionPage":30,"sourceQuestionPages":[30],"sourceExplanationPages":[31,32],"sourceAnswerPages":[31,32],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-012","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man purchased a house that needed substantial repairs. The man financed the purchase of the house by borrowing funds from a bank. He signed a note to the bank and secured repayment of the loan with a mortgage. After purchasing the house, the man borrowed money from his credit union to make the repairs. The man signed a note to the credit union; this note was also secured by a mortgage on the house. Both mortgages were promptly recorded in the order granted. The man was personally liable on both loans. The man moved into the house and made the necessary repairs. He later defaulted on the debt to the bank, and the bank initiated judicial foreclosure proceedings, naming both the man and the credit union as parties to the action. An outside party acquired the house at the foreclosure sale. After the expenses of the sale and the balance due the bank have been paid, $5,000 remains in the sale proceeds. The outstanding balance of the credit union loan is $20,000. The man and the credit union both claim the $5,000. There is no applicable statute. Who is entitled to the $5,000?","choices":{"A":"The credit union, because the credit union has priority.","B":"The credit union, because the man is personally liable on the debt.","C":"The man, because of his equitable right of redemption.","D":"The man, because the outside party received title to the house subject to the second mortgage."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Foreclosure is the process in which the creditor forces the sale of the mortgaged property to satisfy the debt. After the foreclosure sale, proceeds are typically distributed in the following order of priority: Costs from the foreclosure sale (eg, attorneys fees, court costs) Mortgage being foreclosed Junior mortgages (or other junior liens), in the order of priority Debtor, if any surplus remains Here, the man mortgaged his house, first to the bank and then to the credit union. When the man defaulted on his debt to the bank, the bank foreclosed on his house. After the costs from the foreclosure sale and the bank's mortgage were paid, $5,000 remained. Since the credit union has the second (junior) mortgage and is still owed $20,000, it has priority over the man. Therefore, the credit union is entitled to the $5,000. (Choice B) The fact that the man is personally liable on the remainder of the debt to the credit union is irrelevant to determining who is entitled to the $5,000. Instead, the credit union is entitled to the $5,000 because it is a junior lienholder with priority. (Choice C) The equitable right of redemption allows a debtor who has defaulted on a mortgage to cure the default before the foreclosure sale by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt. But this right is irrelevant to determining who is entitled to the proceeds after the foreclosure sale. (Choice D) Since the credit union was a party to the bank's foreclosure action, the purchaser took the house free and clear of (not subject to) the second mortgage. But had the credit union not been a party, the purchaser would have taken the house subject to that mortgage.\n\nEducational objective: The proceeds of a foreclosure sale are distributed in the following order of priority: (1) the costs from the foreclosure sale, (2) the mortgage being foreclosed, (3) junior liens in order of priority, and (4) the debtor.\n\nReferences\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019) (distribution of foreclosure sale proceeds).","educationalObjective":"The proceeds of a foreclosure sale are distributed in the following order of priority: (1) the costs from the foreclosure sale, (2) the mortgage being foreclosed, (3) junior liens in order of priority, and (4) the debtor.","references":"55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019) (distribution of foreclosure sale proceeds).","rule":"The proceeds of a foreclosure sale are distributed in the following order of priority: (1) the costs from the foreclosure sale, (2) the mortgage being foreclosed, (3) junior liens in order of priority, and (4) the debtor.","irac":{"issue":"Who is entitled to the $5,000?","rule":"The proceeds of a foreclosure sale are distributed in the following order of priority: (1) the costs from the foreclosure sale, (2) the mortgage being foreclosed, (3) junior liens in order of priority, and (4) the debtor.","analysis":"Foreclosure is the process in which the creditor forces the sale of the mortgaged property to satisfy the debt. After the foreclosure sale, proceeds are typically distributed in the following order of priority: Costs from the foreclosure sale (eg, attorneys fees, court costs) Mortgage being foreclosed Junior mortgages (or other junior liens), in the order of priority Debtor, if any surplus remains Here, the man mortgaged his house, first to the bank and then to the credit union. When the man defaulted on his debt to the bank, the bank foreclosed on his house. After the costs from the foreclosure sale and the bank's mortgage were paid, $5,000 remained. Since the credit union has the second (junior) mortgage and is still owed $20,000, it has priority over the man. Therefore, the credit union is entitled to the $5,000. (Choice B) The fact that the man is personally liable on the remainder of the debt to the credit union is irrelevant to determining who is entitled to the $5,000. Instead, the credit union is entitled to the $5,000 because it is a junior lienholder with priority. (Choice C) The equitable right of redemption allows a debtor who has defaulted on a mortgage to cure the default before the foreclosure sale by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt. But this right is irrelevant to determining who is entitled to the proceeds after the foreclosure sale. (Choice D) Since the credit union was a party to the bank's foreclosure action, the purchaser took the house free and clear of (not subject to) the second mortgage. But had the credit union not been a party, the purchaser would have taken the house subject to that mortgage.","conclusion":"Answer A: The credit union, because the credit union has priority."},"questionZh":"一名男子购买了一座需要大量维修的房子。该男子通过向银行借款购买了这所房子。他向银行签署了一张票据，并以抵押贷款偿还贷款。买下房子后，该男子向信用合作社借钱进行修缮。该男子在给信用合作社的一张纸条上签了字；这张票据还得到了房屋抵押贷款的担保。两项抵押贷款均立即记录在授予的订单中。该男子对两笔贷款均负有个人责任。该男子搬进了房子并进行了必要的修缮。后来他拖欠了银行的债务，银行启动了司法止赎程序，将该男子和信用合作社列为诉讼当事人。外部人士在取消抵押品赎回权的拍卖中获得了该房屋。在支付销售费用和应付银行的余额后，销售收益中还剩下 5,000 美元。信用合作社贷款的未偿余额为 20,000 美元。该男子和信用合作社都领取了 5,000 美元。没有适用的法规。谁有权获得这 5,000 美元？","choicesZh":{"A":"信用社，因为信用社有优先权。","B":"信用合作社，因为该人对债务负有个人责任。","C":"这个人，因为他有公平的救赎权。","D":"该男子，因为外方获得了第二抵押贷款的房屋所有权。"},"explanationZh":"取消抵押品赎回权是债权人强制出售抵押财产以偿还债务的过程。取消抵押品赎回权出售后，收益通常按以下优先顺序分配： 取消抵押品赎回权出售产生的费用（例如律师费、法庭费用） 抵押贷款被取消抵押品赎回权 初级抵押贷款（或其他初级留置权），按优先顺序排列 债务人（如果还有剩余） 在此，该男子将其房屋抵押，首先抵押给银行，然后抵押给信用合作社。当该男子拖欠银行债务时，银行取消了他的房屋赎回权。在支付取消抵押品赎回权的出售费用和银行抵押贷款后，还剩下 5,000 美元。由于信用合作社拥有第二（初级）抵押贷款且仍欠 20,000 美元，因此它比该男子拥有优先权。因此，信用合作社有权获得 5,000 美元。 （选择 B） 该男子对信用合作社剩余债务负有个人责任，这一事实与确定谁有权获得 5,000 美元无关。相反，信用合作社有权获得 5,000 美元，因为它是优先留置权持有人。 （选择C）衡平法上的赎回权允许拖欠抵押贷款的债务人在取消抵押品赎回权出售之前通过全额支付未偿债务来解决违约问题。但这项权利与确定取消抵押品赎回权出售后谁有权获得收益无关。 （选项D） 由于信用合作社是银行取消抵押品赎回权行动的一方，购买者可以免费获得（不需承担）第二抵押贷款的房屋。但如果信用合作社不是一方，购买者就会按照抵押贷款购买房屋。\n\n教育目标： 止赎出售的收益按以下优先顺序分配：(1) 止赎出售的成本，(2) 被止赎的抵押贷款，(3) 按优先顺序排列的初级留置权，以及 (4) 债务人。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019)（止赎出售收益的分配）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"取消抵押品赎回权销售的收益按以下优先顺序分配：(1) 取消抵押品赎回权销售的成本，(2) 被取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款，(3) 按优先顺序排列的初级留置权，以及 (4) 债务人。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有权获得这 5,000 美元？","rule":"取消抵押品赎回权销售的收益按以下优先顺序分配：(1) 取消抵押品赎回权销售的成本，(2) 被取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款，(3) 按优先顺序排列的初级留置权，以及 (4) 债务人。","analysis":"取消抵押品赎回权是债权人强制出售抵押财产以偿还债务的过程。取消抵押品赎回权出售后，收益通常按以下优先顺序分配： 取消抵押品赎回权出售产生的费用（例如律师费、法庭费用） 抵押贷款被取消抵押品赎回权 初级抵押贷款（或其他初级留置权），按优先顺序排列 债务人（如果还有剩余） 在此，该男子将其房屋抵押，首先抵押给银行，然后抵押给信用合作社。当该男子拖欠银行债务时，银行取消了他的房屋赎回权。在支付取消抵押品赎回权的出售费用和银行抵押贷款后，还剩下 5,000 美元。由于信用合作社拥有第二（初级）抵押贷款且仍欠 20,000 美元，因此它比该男子拥有优先权。因此，信用合作社有权获得 5,000 美元。 （选择 B） 该男子对信用合作社剩余债务负有个人责任，这一事实与确定谁有权获得 5,000 美元无关。相反，信用合作社有权获得 5,000 美元，因为它是优先留置权持有人。 （选择C）衡平法上的赎回权允许拖欠抵押贷款的债务人在取消抵押品赎回权出售之前通过全额支付未偿债务来解决违约问题。但这项权利与确定取消抵押品赎回权出售后谁有权获得收益无关。 （选项D） 由于信用合作社是银行取消抵押品赎回权行动的一方，购买者可以免费获得（不需承担）第二抵押贷款的房屋。但如果信用合作社不是一方，购买者就会按照抵押贷款购买房屋。\n\n教育目标： 止赎出售的收益按以下优先顺序分配：(1) 止赎出售的成本，(2) 被止赎的抵押贷款，(3) 按优先顺序排列的初级留置权，以及 (4) 债务人。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019)（止赎出售收益的分配）。","conclusion":"答案 A：信用社，因为信用社有优先权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"junior mortgage","ipa":"/dʒˈunjɝ mˈɔɹɡədʒ/","syllables":"ju·nior mort·gage","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"次级抵押：顺位在后，仅能从优先债权受偿后的余额受偿","contextualMeaning":"次级抵押：顺位在后，仅能从优先债权受偿后的余额受偿","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"After the foreclosure sale, proceeds are typically distributed in the following order of priority: Costs from the foreclosure sale (eg, attorneys fees, court costs) Mortgage being foreclosed Junior mortgages (or other junior liens), in the order of priority Debtor, if any surplus remains Here, the man mortgaged his house, first to the bank and then to the credit union.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"remainder","ipa":"/ɹɪmˈeɪndɝ/","syllables":"re·main·der","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 剩余物, 其他人, 残余, 余数；v. 削价出售(图书)；a. 剩余的, 出售削价剩书的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“remainder”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:remainders/d:remaindered/i:remaindering/p:remaindered/3:remainders","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) The fact that the man is personally liable on the remainder of the debt to the credit union is irrelevant to determining who is entitled to the $5,000.","synonyms":["end","remnant","oddment","difference"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-c28c568058e9801b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":12,"sourceQuestionPage":33,"sourceQuestionPages":[33],"sourceExplanationPages":[34,35],"sourceAnswerPages":[34,35],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-013","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man owned land. He took out a loan that was secured by a mortgage on the land. The man had personal liability on the loan. The man sold the land to a purchaser, who expressly assumed the mortgage debt. The purchaser failed to make two installment payments. To prevent default and foreclosure, the man paid the overdue installments. After making the payments, the man sued the purchaser for reimbursement. Is the man likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man did not initiate foreclosure proceedings before seeking reimbursement.","B":"No, because the man was personally liable on the loan.","C":"Yes, because the law of suretyship permits the man to seek reimbursement.","D":"Yes, because the man's transfer of the land discharged him from any personal liability on the loan."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Suretyship rights\n\n(recourse for secondary obligor)\n\nSpecific Court order requiring primarily obligor to specifically perform all or performance part of his/her contractual duties\n\nReimbursement Repayment for secondary obligor fulfilling all or part of primary obligor's underlying contractual duties\n\nRestitution Repayment to secondary obligor to avoid unjust enrichment to primary obligor even when no duty exists\n\nSubrogation Secondary obligor given rights of primary obligor & is protected as though an assignment of rights occurred\n\nA mortgagor (ie, borrower) is generally free to transfer mortgaged property to another (ie, transferee). After the sale, the mortgage remains attached to the property and the mortgagor remains personally liable for the mortgage debt in the absence of a release by the mortgagee (eg, bank). However, the transferee's obligations with respect to that debt depend on whether the transferee: took subject to the mortgage – in which case, the transferee is not personally liable for the debt or agreed to assume the mortgage – in which case, the transferee becomes primarily liable for the debt and the mortgagor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. If a transferee defaults on an assumed mortgage and the mortgagor then pays that debt, the mortgagor can seek reimbursement from the transferee under the law of suretyship. Here, the purchaser expressly assumed the man's mortgage debt, so the purchaser became primarily liable for the debt and the man became secondarily liable as a surety (Choice D). The purchaser then defaulted by failing to make two installment payments. Since the man paid the overdue installments, the law of suretyship permits him to seek reimbursement from the purchaser. As a result, the man is likely to prevail. (Choice A) A foreclosure proceeding can be initiated only by the mortgagee or the current holder of the mortgagee's security interest—not the mortgagor (eg, the man). (Choice B) The right to seek reimbursement under the law of suretyship requires that the man be personally liable on the loan, which he was as a surety. Therefore, the man's personal liability provides a basis for him to prevail.\n\nEducational objective: When a mortgagor transfers mortgaged property to someone who assumes the mortgage, the transferee becomes primarily liable for the mortgage and the mortgagor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. So if the mortgagor pays the mortgage debt after the transferee defaults, the mortgagor can seek reimbursement.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (assumption of a mortgage).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Suretyship & Guaranty § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 1996) (surety's right to reimbursement).","educationalObjective":"When a mortgagor transfers mortgaged property to someone who assumes the mortgage, the transferee becomes primarily liable for the mortgage and the mortgagor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. So if the mortgagor pays the mortgage debt after the transferee defaults, the mortgagor can seek reimbursement.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (assumption of a mortgage).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Suretyship & Guaranty § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 1996) (surety's right to reimbursement).","rule":"When a mortgagor transfers mortgaged property to someone who assumes the mortgage, the transferee becomes primarily liable for the mortgage and the mortgagor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. So if the mortgagor pays the mortgage debt after the transferee defaults, the mortgagor can seek reimbursement.","irac":{"issue":"Is the man likely to prevail?","rule":"When a mortgagor transfers mortgaged property to someone who assumes the mortgage, the transferee becomes primarily liable for the mortgage and the mortgagor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. So if the mortgagor pays the mortgage debt after the transferee defaults, the mortgagor can seek reimbursement.","analysis":"Suretyship rights\n\n(recourse for secondary obligor)\n\nSpecific Court order requiring primarily obligor to specifically perform all or performance part of his/her contractual duties\n\nReimbursement Repayment for secondary obligor fulfilling all or part of primary obligor's underlying contractual duties\n\nRestitution Repayment to secondary obligor to avoid unjust enrichment to primary obligor even when no duty exists\n\nSubrogation Secondary obligor given rights of primary obligor & is protected as though an assignment of rights occurred\n\nA mortgagor (ie, borrower) is generally free to transfer mortgaged property to another (ie, transferee). After the sale, the mortgage remains attached to the property and the mortgagor remains personally liable for the mortgage debt in the absence of a release by the mortgagee (eg, bank). However, the transferee's obligations with respect to that debt depend on whether the transferee: took subject to the mortgage – in which case, the transferee is not personally liable for the debt or agreed to assume the mortgage – in which case, the transferee becomes primarily liable for the debt and the mortgagor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. If a transferee defaults on an assumed mortgage and the mortgagor then pays that debt, the mortgagor can seek reimbursement from the transferee under the law of suretyship. Here, the purchaser expressly assumed the man's mortgage debt, so the purchaser became primarily liable for the debt and the man became secondarily liable as a surety (Choice D). The purchaser then defaulted by failing to make two installment payments. Since the man paid the overdue installments, the law of suretyship permits him to seek reimbursement from the purchaser. As a result, the man is likely to prevail. (Choice A) A foreclosure proceeding can be initiated only by the mortgagee or the current holder of the mortgagee's security interest—not the mortgagor (eg, the man). (Choice B) The right to seek reimbursement under the law of suretyship requires that the man be personally liable on the loan, which he was as a surety. Therefore, the man's personal liability provides a basis for him to prevail.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the law of suretyship permits the man to seek reimbursement."},"questionZh":"男人拥有土地。他借了一笔以土地抵押作为担保的贷款。该男子对贷款负有个人责任。该男子将土地卖给了一位买家，买家明确承担了抵押债务。买方未能支付两笔分期付款。为了防止违约和丧失抵押品赎回权，该男子支付了逾期分期付款。付款后，该男子起诉买家要求赔偿。男人有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该男子在寻求报销之前并没有启动止赎程序。","B":"不，因为该男子个人对贷款负有责任。","C":"是的，因为担保法允许该男子寻求赔偿。","D":"是的，因为该男子转让土地后免除了他对贷款的任何个人责任。"},"explanationZh":"担保权\n\n（次要债务人的追索权）\n\n要求主要义务人具体履行其全部或部分合同义务的特定法院命令\n\n偿付 次要债务人履行全部或部分主要债务人的基本合同义务的偿付\n\n返还 向次要债务人偿还，以避免主要债务人不当得利，即使不存在任何义务\n\n代位权 次要债务人获得主要债务人的权利并受到保护，就像发生权利转让一样\n\n抵押人（即借款人）通常可以自由地将抵押财产转让给另一人（即受让人）。出售后，抵押贷款仍依附于该财产，并且在抵押权人（例如银行）未解除抵押债务的情况下，抵押人仍对抵押债务承担个人责任。然而，受让人对该债务的义务取决于受让人是否： 接受抵押——在这种情况下，受让人不对债务承担个人责任或同意承担抵押——在这种情况下，受让人对债务承担主要责任，抵押人作为担保人承担次要责任。如果受让人拖欠所承担的抵押贷款，并且抵押人偿还了该债务，抵押人可以根据担保法向受让人寻求偿还。在此，买方明确承担了该男子的抵押债务，因此买方对债务承担主要责任，而男子则作为担保人承担次要责任（选项D）。买方随后因未能支付两期分期付款而违约。由于该男子支付了逾期分期付款，担保法允许他向买方寻求赔偿。结果，该男子很可能获胜。 （选择A）止赎程序只能由抵押权人或抵押权人担保权益的当前持有人发起，而不能由抵押人（例如男子）发起。 （选项B）根据担保法要求偿还的权利要求该男子对他作为担保人的贷款承担个人责任。因此，男方的个人责任为他的胜诉提供了基础。\n\n教育目的：当抵押人将抵押财产转让给承担抵押的人时，受让人对抵押负主要责任，抵押人作为担保人承担次要责任。因此，如果抵押人在受让人违约后偿还了抵押债务，抵押人可以寻求偿还。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.3（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款的假设）。\n\n《保证和保证》第 22 条重述（第三次）（Am. Law Inst. 1996）（保证人获得赔偿的权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"抵押人将抵押财产转让给抵押人时，受让人承担抵押物的第一责任，抵押人作为保证人承担次要责任。因此，如果抵押人在受让人违约后偿还了抵押债务，抵押人可以寻求偿还。","iracZh":{"issue":"男人有可能获胜吗？","rule":"抵押人将抵押财产转让给抵押人时，受让人承担抵押物的第一责任，抵押人作为保证人承担次要责任。因此，如果抵押人在受让人违约后偿还了抵押债务，抵押人可以寻求偿还。","analysis":"担保权\n\n（次要债务人的追索权）\n\n要求主要义务人具体履行其全部或部分合同义务的特定法院命令\n\n偿付 次要债务人履行全部或部分主要债务人的基本合同义务的偿付\n\n返还 向次要债务人偿还，以避免主要债务人不当得利，即使不存在任何义务\n\n代位权 次要债务人获得主要债务人的权利并受到保护，就像发生权利转让一样\n\n抵押人（即借款人）通常可以自由地将抵押财产转让给另一人（即受让人）。出售后，抵押贷款仍依附于该财产，并且在抵押权人（例如银行）未解除抵押债务的情况下，抵押人仍对抵押债务承担个人责任。然而，受让人对该债务的义务取决于受让人是否： 接受抵押——在这种情况下，受让人不对债务承担个人责任或同意承担抵押——在这种情况下，受让人对债务承担主要责任，抵押人作为担保人承担次要责任。如果受让人拖欠所承担的抵押贷款，并且抵押人偿还了该债务，抵押人可以根据担保法向受让人寻求偿还。在此，买方明确承担了该男子的抵押债务，因此买方对债务承担主要责任，而男子则作为担保人承担次要责任（选项D）。买方随后因未能支付两期分期付款而违约。由于该男子支付了逾期分期付款，担保法允许他向买方寻求赔偿。结果，该男子很可能获胜。 （选择A）止赎程序只能由抵押权人或抵押权人担保权益的当前持有人发起，而不能由抵押人（例如男子）发起。 （选项B）根据担保法要求偿还的权利要求该男子对他作为担保人的贷款承担个人责任。因此，男方的个人责任为他的胜诉提供了基础。\n\n教育目的：当抵押人将抵押财产转让给承担抵押的人时，受让人对抵押负主要责任，抵押人作为担保人承担次要责任。因此，如果抵押人在受让人违约后偿还了抵押债务，抵押人可以寻求偿还。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.3（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款的假设）。\n\n《保证和保证》第 22 条重述（第三次）（Am. Law Inst. 1996）（保证人获得赔偿的权利）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为担保法允许该男子寻求赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":13,"sourceQuestionPage":36,"sourceQuestionPages":[36],"sourceExplanationPages":[37,38],"sourceAnswerPages":[37,38],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-014","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Twenty-five years ago, a seller conveyed a lot of land to a buyer by a warranty deed. The seller at that time also executed and delivered an instrument in the proper form of a deed, purporting to convey a second lot of land to the buyer. The seller thought she had title to the second lot but did not; therefore, no title passed by virtue of that deed. The second lot consisted of three acres of brushland adjoining the west boundary of the first lot. The buyer has occasionally hunted rabbits on the second lot, but less often than annually. No one else came on the second lot except occasional rabbit hunters. Twenty years ago, the buyer planted a row of evergreens in the vicinity of the opposite (east) boundary of the first lot and erected a fence just beyond the evergreens to the east. In fact, both the trees and the fence were placed on a neighboring lot, owned by a neighbor, which bordered the east boundary of the first lot. The buyer was unsure of the exact boundary, and placed the trees and the fence in order to establish his rights up to the fence. The fence is located 10 feet within the neighboring lot. Now, the buyer has had his property surveyed and the title checked and has learned the facts. The period of time to acquire title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 15 years. In an appropriate action, to which of the following would the buyer likely obtain title?","choices":{"A":"The second lot but not the 10-foot strip of the neighboring lot.","B":"The 10-foot strip of the neighboring lot but not the second lot.","C":"Both the second lot and the 10-foot strip of the neighboring lot.","D":"Neither the second lot nor the 10-foot strip of the neighboring lot."},"answer":"B","explanation":"To acquire title to another's land by adverse possession, a person's possession of the land must be: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the portion of the land to be adversely possessed Non-permissive – hostile and adverse to the owner Here, the buyer openly planted trees and built a fence 10 feet into a neighboring lot, which excluded the neighbor from that portion of his property. And since the trees and fence have continuously remained on the neighbor's property without his permission for over 15 years (the statutory period), the buyer likely obtained title to the 10-foot strip by adverse possession. (Choices A & C) The buyer did not acquire title to the second lot by deed because the seller had no interest to convey. Therefore, the buyer could only acquire title by adverse possession. But since the buyer merely used the second lot to hunt rabbits less often than annually (no continuous use), he did not adversely possess that lot. (Choice D) Although the buyer did not acquire title to the second lot, he did acquire title to the 10-foot strip of the neighboring lot by adverse possession.\n\nEducational objective: A person may acquire title to another's land by adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is open and notorious, continuous, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive (OCEAN). However, the adverse possessor can only acquire title to the portion of the land that he/she actually occupies.","educationalObjective":"A person may acquire title to another's land by adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is open and notorious, continuous, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive (OCEAN). However, the adverse possessor can only acquire title to the portion of the land that he/she actually occupies.","references":"","rule":"A person may acquire title to another's land by adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is open and notorious, continuous, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive (OCEAN). However, the adverse possessor can only acquire title to the portion of the land that he/she actually occupies.","irac":{"issue":"In an appropriate action, to which of the following would the buyer likely obtain title?","rule":"A person may acquire title to another's land by adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is open and notorious, continuous, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive (OCEAN). However, the adverse possessor can only acquire title to the portion of the land that he/she actually occupies.","analysis":"To acquire title to another's land by adverse possession, a person's possession of the land must be: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the portion of the land to be adversely possessed Non-permissive – hostile and adverse to the owner Here, the buyer openly planted trees and built a fence 10 feet into a neighboring lot, which excluded the neighbor from that portion of his property. And since the trees and fence have continuously remained on the neighbor's property without his permission for over 15 years (the statutory period), the buyer likely obtained title to the 10-foot strip by adverse possession. (Choices A & C) The buyer did not acquire title to the second lot by deed because the seller had no interest to convey. Therefore, the buyer could only acquire title by adverse possession. But since the buyer merely used the second lot to hunt rabbits less often than annually (no continuous use), he did not adversely possess that lot. (Choice D) Although the buyer did not acquire title to the second lot, he did acquire title to the 10-foot strip of the neighboring lot by adverse possession.","conclusion":"Answer B: The 10-foot strip of the neighboring lot but not the second lot."},"questionZh":"二十五年前，卖方通过保修契约将大量土地转让给买方。当时，卖方还以适当的契约形式签署并交付了一份文书，旨在将第二块土地转让给买方。卖家认为她拥有第二批地块的所有权，但事实并非如此；因此，没有任何所有权因该契约而转移。第二块土地由三英亩的灌木丛组成，毗邻第一块土地的西边界。买家偶尔会在第二块土地上猎杀兔子，但频率低于每年一次。除了偶尔有猎兔者之外，第二批没有其他人来。二十年前，买家在第一块地块的对面（东）边界附近种植了一排常青树，并在常青树东边竖起了栅栏。事实上，树木和栅栏都放置在邻居拥有的邻近地块上，该地块与第一块地块的东边界接壤。买家不确定确切的边界，并放置了树木和栅栏，以确立他对栅栏的权利。围栏位于邻近地块 10 英尺处。目前，买家已对其财产进行了勘察，并核实了产权证，并了解了事实。在管辖范围内通过逆权占有取得所有权的期限为 15 年。通过适当的行动，买方可能会获得以下哪一项的所有权？","choicesZh":{"A":"第二个地块，但不是邻近地块的 10 英尺地带。","B":"邻近地块的 10 英尺长地带，但不是第二块地块。","C":"第二个地块和相邻地块的 10 英尺长的地带。","D":"既不是第二个地块，也不是相邻地块的 10 英尺地带。"},"explanationZh":"要通过逆权侵占获得他人土地的所有权，一个人对土地的占有必须是： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者来说是显而易见的或可见的 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 排他 – 不与所有者共享 实际 – 实际存在于将被逆权侵占的土地部分 不允许 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 在这里，买方公开种植树木并在邻近地块 10 英尺处建造了栅栏，将邻居排除在其财产的该部分之外。由于树木和栅栏在未经邻居许可的情况下一直保留在邻居的财产上超过 15 年（法定期限），因此买方很可能通过逆权管有获得了这片 10 英尺长地带的所有权。 （选项 A 和 C） 买方没有通过契约获得第二批土地的所有权，因为卖方没有兴趣转让。因此，买方只能通过逆权占有来获得所有权。但由于买方仅使用第二批地来狩猎兔子，频率低于每年一次（没有连续使用），因此他并没有不利地占有该地块。 （选项D）尽管买方没有获得第二块地块的所有权，但他确实通过逆权管有获得了相邻地块10英尺长地带的所有权。\n\n教育目标：如果一个人对他人土地的占有是公开的、臭名昭著的、连续的、排他的、实际的和不允许的（OCEAN），则他/她可以通过逆权占有获得他人土地的所有权。然而，逆权占有人只能获得他/她实际占用的部分土地的所有权。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果一个人对他人土地的占有是开放的、臭名昭著的、连续的、排他的、实际的和不允许的（OCEAN），则他/她可以通过逆权占有获得他人土地的所有权。然而，逆权占有人只能获得他/她实际占用的部分土地的所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"通过适当的行动，买方可能会获得以下哪一项的所有权？","rule":"如果一个人对他人土地的占有是开放的、臭名昭著的、连续的、排他的、实际的和不允许的（OCEAN），则他/她可以通过逆权占有获得他人土地的所有权。然而，逆权占有人只能获得他/她实际占用的部分土地的所有权。","analysis":"要通过逆权侵占获得他人土地的所有权，一个人对土地的占有必须是： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者来说是显而易见的或可见的 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 排他 – 不与所有者共享 实际 – 实际存在于将被逆权侵占的土地部分 不允许 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 在这里，买方公开种植树木并在邻近地块 10 英尺处建造了栅栏，将邻居排除在其财产的该部分之外。由于树木和栅栏在未经邻居许可的情况下一直保留在邻居的财产上超过 15 年（法定期限），因此买方很可能通过逆权管有获得了这片 10 英尺长地带的所有权。 （选项 A 和 C） 买方没有通过契约获得第二批土地的所有权，因为卖方没有兴趣转让。因此，买方只能通过逆权占有来获得所有权。但由于买方仅使用第二批地来狩猎兔子，频率低于每年一次（没有连续使用），因此他并没有不利地占有该地块。 （选项D）尽管买方没有获得第二块地块的所有权，但他确实通过逆权管有获得了相邻地块10英尺长地带的所有权。\n\n教育目标：如果一个人对他人土地的占有是公开的、臭名昭著的、连续的、排他的、实际的和不允许的（OCEAN），则他/她可以通过逆权占有获得他人土地的所有权。然而，逆权占有人只能获得他/她实际占用的部分土地的所有权。","conclusion":"答案 B：邻近地块的 10 英尺长地带，但不是第二块地块。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-367dbaddaaf3aefa"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":14,"sourceQuestionPage":39,"sourceQuestionPages":[39],"sourceExplanationPages":[40,41],"sourceAnswerPages":[40,41],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-015","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A seller conveyed residential land to a buyer by a warranty deed that contained no exceptions and recited that the full consideration had been paid. To finance the purchase, the buyer borrowed 80% of the necessary funds from a bank. The seller agreed to finance 15% of the purchase price, and the buyer agreed to provide cash for the remaining 5%. At the closing, the buyer signed a promissory note to the seller for 15% of the purchase price but did not execute a mortgage. The bank knew of the loan made by the seller and of the promissory note executed by the buyer to the seller. The buyer also signed a note to the bank, secured by a mortgage, for the 80% advanced by the bank. The buyer has now defaulted on both loans. There are no applicable statutes. Which loan has priority?","choices":{"A":"The bank's loan, because it was secured by a purchase-money mortgage.","B":"The bank's loan, because the seller can finance a part of the purchase price only by use of an installment land contract.","C":"The seller's loan, because a promissory note to a seller has priority over a bank loan for residential property.","D":"The seller's loan, because the bank knew that the seller had an equitable vendor's lien."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The seller received a promissory note—ie, a legal document that creates or memorializes a debt—but no lien or mortgage to secure the debt. But when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid, an equitable vendor's lien may be implied by law. Therefore, the seller may have an equitable vendor's lien, but the deed recites that the full consideration was paid. The bank received a promissory note secured by a mortgage—ie, a lien against real property given to secure a debt. Since the mortgage was given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, it is a purchase-money mortgage (PMM). PMMs have super priority over all prior liens regardless of whether the PMM or those liens are recorded. Therefore, the bank's loan has priority—regardless of whether it knew of the seller's lien (Choices C & D). (Choice B) A seller can finance the purchase of land through several means—including a PMM, equitable vendor's lien, and installment land contract. An installment land contract is an agreement where the debtor promises to make regular installment payments until the full contract price is paid (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: An equitable vendor's lien is implied by law when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid. In contrast, a purchase-money mortgage is created when a mortgage is given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, thereby giving it super priority over all other mortgages and liens.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (priority of purchase-money mortgages)","educationalObjective":"An equitable vendor's lien is implied by law when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid. In contrast, a purchase-money mortgage is created when a mortgage is given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, thereby giving it super priority over all other mortgages and liens.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (priority of purchase-money mortgages)","rule":"An equitable vendor's lien is implied by law when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid. In contrast, a purchase-money mortgage is created when a mortgage is given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, thereby giving it super priority over all other mortgages and liens.","irac":{"issue":"Which loan has priority?","rule":"An equitable vendor's lien is implied by law when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid. In contrast, a purchase-money mortgage is created when a mortgage is given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, thereby giving it super priority over all other mortgages and liens.","analysis":"The seller received a promissory note—ie, a legal document that creates or memorializes a debt—but no lien or mortgage to secure the debt. But when a seller transfers title to a buyer and any portion of the purchase price remains unpaid, an equitable vendor's lien may be implied by law. Therefore, the seller may have an equitable vendor's lien, but the deed recites that the full consideration was paid. The bank received a promissory note secured by a mortgage—ie, a lien against real property given to secure a debt. Since the mortgage was given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property, it is a purchase-money mortgage (PMM). PMMs have super priority over all prior liens regardless of whether the PMM or those liens are recorded. Therefore, the bank's loan has priority—regardless of whether it knew of the seller's lien (Choices C & D). (Choice B) A seller can finance the purchase of land through several means—including a PMM, equitable vendor's lien, and installment land contract. An installment land contract is an agreement where the debtor promises to make regular installment payments until the full contract price is paid (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: The bank's loan, because it was secured by a purchase-money mortgage."},"questionZh":"卖方通过一份不包含任何例外的保证契约将住宅用地转让给买方，并表示已支付全部对价。为了筹集购买资金，买方从银行借了 80% 的必要资金。卖方同意支付购买价格的 15%，买方同意提供现金支付剩余的 5%。成交时，买方向卖方签署了一份金额为购买价格 15% 的期票，但没有执行抵押贷款。银行知道卖方提供的贷款以及买方向卖方签发的本票。买方还向银行签署了一份票据，以抵押贷款作为担保，支付银行预付的 80% 的款项。买方现已拖欠两项贷款。没有适用的法规。哪种贷款有优先权？","choicesZh":{"A":"银行的贷款，因为它是由购房款抵押贷款担保的。","B":"银行贷款，因为卖方只能通过分期付款土地合同来支付部分购买价格。","C":"卖方贷款，因为针对住宅物业，卖方的本票优先于银行贷款。","D":"卖方的贷款，因为银行知道卖方拥有公平的卖方留置权。"},"explanationZh":"卖方收到了本票——即产生或纪念债务的法律文件——但没有留置权或抵押来担保债务。但是，当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价款的任何部分仍未支付时，法律可能会暗示衡平法上卖方的留置权。因此，卖方可能拥有公平的卖方留置权，但契约规定已支付全部对价。银行收到了一张由抵押贷款担保的本票，即为担保债务而给予的不动产留置权。由于抵押贷款是为了保证抵押财产的购买价格，因此它是购房款抵押贷款（PMM）。 PMM 比所有先前的留置权拥有超级优先权，无论 PMM 或那些留置权是否被记录。因此，银行的贷款具有优先权——无论银行是否知道卖方的留置权（选择C和D）。 （选择 B）卖方可以通过多种方式为购买土地提供资金，包括 PMM、公平卖方留置权和分期土地合同。分期土地合同是一种协议，其中债务人承诺定期分期付款，直到支付全部合同价格（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价的任何部分仍未支付时，法律暗示了公平的卖方留置权。相比之下，当提供抵押贷款以确保抵押财产的购买价格时，就创建了购房款抵押贷款，从而赋予其高于所有其他抵押贷款和留置权的超级优先权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（购房款抵押贷款的优先权）","educationalObjectiveZh":"当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价的任何部分仍未支付时，法律暗示了衡平法卖方的留置权。相比之下，当提供抵押贷款以确保抵押财产的购买价格时，就创建了购房款抵押贷款，从而赋予其高于所有其他抵押贷款和留置权的超级优先权。","iracZh":{"issue":"哪种贷款有优先权？","rule":"当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价的任何部分仍未支付时，法律暗示了衡平法卖方的留置权。相比之下，当提供抵押贷款以确保抵押财产的购买价格时，就创建了购房款抵押贷款，从而赋予其高于所有其他抵押贷款和留置权的超级优先权。","analysis":"卖方收到了本票——即产生或纪念债务的法律文件——但没有留置权或抵押来担保债务。但是，当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价款的任何部分仍未支付时，法律可能会暗示衡平法上卖方的留置权。因此，卖方可能拥有公平的卖方留置权，但契约规定已支付全部对价。银行收到了一张由抵押贷款担保的本票，即为担保债务而给予的不动产留置权。由于抵押贷款是为了保证抵押财产的购买价格，因此它是购房款抵押贷款（PMM）。 PMM 比所有先前的留置权拥有超级优先权，无论 PMM 或那些留置权是否被记录。因此，银行的贷款具有优先权——无论银行是否知道卖方的留置权（选择C和D）。 （选择 B）卖方可以通过多种方式为购买土地提供资金，包括 PMM、公平卖方留置权和分期土地合同。分期土地合同是一种协议，其中债务人承诺定期分期付款，直到支付全部合同价格（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：当卖方将所有权转让给买方并且购买价的任何部分仍未支付时，法律暗示了公平的卖方留置权。相比之下，当提供抵押贷款以确保抵押财产的购买价格时，就创建了购房款抵押贷款，从而赋予其高于所有其他抵押贷款和留置权的超级优先权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（购房款抵押贷款的优先权）","conclusion":"答案 A：银行的贷款，因为它是由购房款抵押贷款担保的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-a5b820bdb2830699"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":15,"sourceQuestionPage":42,"sourceQuestionPages":[42],"sourceExplanationPages":[43,44],"sourceAnswerPages":[43,44],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-016","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Two friends planned to incorporate a business together and agreed that they would own all of the corporation's stock in equal proportion. A businesswoman conveyed land by a warranty deed to \"the corporation and its successors and assigns.\" The deed was recorded. Thereafter, the friends had a disagreement. No papers were ever filed to incorporate the business. There is no applicable statute. Who owns the land?","choices":{"A":"The businesswoman, because the deed was a warranty deed.","B":"The businesswoman, because the deed was void.","C":"The two friends as tenants in common, because they intended to own the corporation's stock in equal proportion.","D":"The two friends as tenants in common, because they were the intended sole shareholders."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Deed requirements\n\nRequirements Valid Invalid\n\nWritten & signed Any written instrument Oral (except permissible gift) by grantor signed by grantor Signed only by grantee Identifies grantor Separate grantor & grantee Ambiguous/unidentifiable grantee(s) & grantee (except concurrent estates) Nonexistent grantee Identifiable by name or description Identifies land Identifiable by reasonable Ambiguous description (but extrinsic certainty—eg: evidence admissible) Government survey Undefined part of larger parcel Metes & bounds Street, lot, house number Includes words of \"Convey\" \"Lease\" transfer \"Transfer\" \"Rent\" \"Grant\" \"License\" \"Sell\" A deed is a document that conveys an interest in real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee). But to be valid, a deed must: be in writing and signed by the grantor identify the grantor and the grantee contain words of transfer (eg, \"deed over,\" \"transfer,\" \"give\") and describe the property interest being transferred. If the deed refers to a nonexistent grantee (eg, a corporation that has not been legally formed), the deed is void and the property interest remains with the grantor. Here, two friends planned to incorporate a business together. But since that corporation was never legally formed, the warranty deed that purported to convey the businesswoman's land to that corporation (a nonexistent grantee) was void. Therefore, no title was conveyed and the businesswoman still owns the land. (Choice A) A deed to a nonexistent grantee (as seen here) is void. Therefore, the type of deed (ie, general warranty, special warranty, quitclaim) that was used in the failed conveyance is irrelevant.\n\n(Choices C & D) Since the corporation was never legally formed (nonexistent grantee), the deed was void and the businesswoman retained ownership of the land. But had the corporation been legally formed, the property would have been owned by the corporation—not the two friends as tenants in common. Therefore, the two friends' intent to own the corporation's stock in equal proportion and be the sole shareholders is immaterial.\n\nEducational objective: A deed must (1) be in writing and signed by the grantor, (2) identify the grantor and the grantee, (3) contain words of transfer, and (4) describe the property interest being transferred. Therefore, a deed to a nonexistent grantee (eg, a corporation that has not been legally formed) is void.","educationalObjective":"A deed must (1) be in writing and signed by the grantor, (2) identify the grantor and the grantee, (3) contain words of transfer, and (4) describe the property interest being transferred. Therefore, a deed to a nonexistent grantee (eg, a corporation that has not been legally formed) is void.","references":"","rule":"A deed must (1) be in writing and signed by the grantor, (2) identify the grantor and the grantee, (3) contain words of transfer, and (4) describe the property interest being transferred. Therefore, a deed to a nonexistent grantee (eg, a corporation that has not been legally formed) is void.","irac":{"issue":"Who owns the land?","rule":"A deed must (1) be in writing and signed by the grantor, (2) identify the grantor and the grantee, (3) contain words of transfer, and (4) describe the property interest being transferred. Therefore, a deed to a nonexistent grantee (eg, a corporation that has not been legally formed) is void.","analysis":"Deed requirements\n\nRequirements Valid Invalid\n\nWritten & signed Any written instrument Oral (except permissible gift) by grantor signed by grantor Signed only by grantee Identifies grantor Separate grantor & grantee Ambiguous/unidentifiable grantee(s) & grantee (except concurrent estates) Nonexistent grantee Identifiable by name or description Identifies land Identifiable by reasonable Ambiguous description (but extrinsic certainty—eg: evidence admissible) Government survey Undefined part of larger parcel Metes & bounds Street, lot, house number Includes words of \"Convey\" \"Lease\" transfer \"Transfer\" \"Rent\" \"Grant\" \"License\" \"Sell\" A deed is a document that conveys an interest in real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee). But to be valid, a deed must: be in writing and signed by the grantor identify the grantor and the grantee contain words of transfer (eg, \"deed over,\" \"transfer,\" \"give\") and describe the property interest being transferred. If the deed refers to a nonexistent grantee (eg, a corporation that has not been legally formed), the deed is void and the property interest remains with the grantor. Here, two friends planned to incorporate a business together. But since that corporation was never legally formed, the warranty deed that purported to convey the businesswoman's land to that corporation (a nonexistent grantee) was void. Therefore, no title was conveyed and the businesswoman still owns the land. (Choice A) A deed to a nonexistent grantee (as seen here) is void. Therefore, the type of deed (ie, general warranty, special warranty, quitclaim) that was used in the failed conveyance is irrelevant.\n\n(Choices C & D) Since the corporation was never legally formed (nonexistent grantee), the deed was void and the businesswoman retained ownership of the land. But had the corporation been legally formed, the property would have been owned by the corporation—not the two friends as tenants in common. Therefore, the two friends' intent to own the corporation's stock in equal proportion and be the sole shareholders is immaterial.","conclusion":"Answer B: The businesswoman, because the deed was void."},"questionZh":"两个朋友计划一起成立一家企业，并同意他们以同等比例拥有该公司的全部股票。一名女商人通过保修契约将土地转让给“公司及其继承人和受让人”。该行为被记录下来。此后，朋友们产生了分歧。从未提交过任何文件来合并该业务。没有适用的法规。谁拥有土地？","choicesZh":{"A":"女商人，因为该契约是保修契约。","B":"女商人，因为契约无效。","C":"这两个朋友作为按份共有人（tenant in common），因为他们打算以同等比例拥有该公司的股票。","D":"这两个朋友是按份共有人（tenant in common），因为他们是拟定的唯一股东。"},"explanationZh":"契约要求\n\n要求 有效 无效\n\n书面和签署 任何书面文件，由授予人口头签署（允许的赠与除外），由授予人签署 仅由受让人签署 确定授予人的身份 单独的授予人和受让人 模糊/无法识别的受让人和受让人（共同财产除外） 不存在的受让人 可通过姓名或描述识别 识别土地 可通过合理的描述识别 不明确（但外在确定性 — 例如：证据可接受） 政府调查 大地块的未定义部分 边界和边界街道、地段、门牌号 包括“转让”、“租赁”、转让、“转让”、“出租”、“授予”、“许可”、“出售”等词语 契约是一种将不动产权益从所有者（授予人）转让给另一人（受让人）的文件。但为了有效，契约必须： 采用书面形式并由设保人签署，标识设保人和受让人，并包含转让词语（例如“契约转让”、“转让”、“给予”）并描述所转让的财产权益。如果契约涉及不存在的受让人（例如，尚未合法成立的公司），则契约无效，财产权益仍归授予人所有。在这里，两个朋友计划一起创办一家企业。但由于该公司从未合法成立，因此旨在将女商人的土地转让给该公司（不存在的受让人）的保修契约无效。因此，没有转让所有权，女商人仍然拥有该土地。 （选项 A）对于不存在的受让人（如此处所示）的契约是无效的。因此，失败的运输中使用的契约类型（即一般保修、特殊保修、无偿索赔）是无关紧要的。\n\n（选项 C 和 D）由于该公司从未合法成立（不存在受让人），因此该契约无效，女商人保留了土地的所有权。但如果公司是合法成立的，那么财产将归公司所有，而不是作为按份共有人（tenant in common）的两个朋友所有。因此，两位朋友平均持有公司股票并成为唯一股东的意图并不重要。\n\n教育目标：契约必须 (1) 为书面形式并由授予人签署，(2) 确定授予人和受让人的身份，(3) 包含转让文字，以及 (4) 描述所转让的财产权益。因此，对于不存在的受让人（例如，尚未合法成立的公司）的契约是无效的。","educationalObjectiveZh":"契约必须 (1) 为书面形式并由授予人签署，(2) 确定授予人和受让人的身份，(3) 包含转让文字，以及 (4) 描述所转让的财产权益。因此，对于不存在的受让人（例如，尚未合法成立的公司）的契约是无效的。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁拥有土地？","rule":"契约必须 (1) 为书面形式并由授予人签署，(2) 确定授予人和受让人的身份，(3) 包含转让文字，以及 (4) 描述所转让的财产权益。因此，对于不存在的受让人（例如，尚未合法成立的公司）的契约是无效的。","analysis":"契约要求\n\n要求 有效 无效\n\n书面和签署 任何书面文件，由授予人口头签署（允许的赠与除外），由授予人签署 仅由受让人签署 确定授予人的身份 单独的授予人和受让人 模糊/无法识别的受让人和受让人（共同财产除外） 不存在的受让人 可通过姓名或描述识别 识别土地 可通过合理的描述识别 不明确（但外在确定性 — 例如：证据可接受） 政府调查 大地块的未定义部分 边界和边界街道、地段、门牌号 包括“转让”、“租赁”、转让、“转让”、“出租”、“授予”、“许可”、“出售”等词语 契约是一种将不动产权益从所有者（授予人）转让给另一人（受让人）的文件。但为了有效，契约必须： 采用书面形式并由设保人签署，标识设保人和受让人，并包含转让词语（例如“契约转让”、“转让”、“给予”）并描述所转让的财产权益。如果契约涉及不存在的受让人（例如，尚未合法成立的公司），则契约无效，财产权益仍归授予人所有。在这里，两个朋友计划一起创办一家企业。但由于该公司从未合法成立，因此旨在将女商人的土地转让给该公司（不存在的受让人）的保修契约无效。因此，没有转让所有权，女商人仍然拥有该土地。 （选项 A）对于不存在的受让人（如此处所示）的契约是无效的。因此，失败的运输中使用的契约类型（即一般保修、特殊保修、无偿索赔）是无关紧要的。\n\n（选项 C 和 D）由于该公司从未合法成立（不存在受让人），因此该契约无效，女商人保留了土地的所有权。但如果公司是合法成立的，那么财产将归公司所有，而不是作为按份共有人（tenant in common）的两个朋友所有。因此，两位朋友平均持有公司股票并成为唯一股东的意图并不重要。\n\n教育目标：契约必须 (1) 为书面形式并由授予人签署，(2) 确定授予人和受让人的身份，(3) 包含转让文字，以及 (4) 描述所转让的财产权益。因此，对于不存在的受让人（例如，尚未合法成立的公司）的契约是无效的。","conclusion":"答案 B：女商人，因为契约无效。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"grantor","ipa":"/ɡɹˈæntɝ/","syllables":"grantor","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 授予者, 让渡人；[经] 让与人, 授与者","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“grantor”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:grantors","sourceSentence":"A deed must (1) be in writing and signed by the grantor, (2) identify the grantor and the grantee, (3) contain words of transfer, and (4) describe the property interest being transferred.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"rent","ipa":"/ɹˈɛnt/","syllables":"rent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 租金, 房租, 出租物, 裂缝, 破裂处, 分裂；vt. 租用, 租出；vi. 出租","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“rent”","wordForm":"名词；原形 rend；词形 s:rents/i:renting/d:rented/p:rented/3:rents/0:rent/1:d","sourceSentence":"Deed requirements Requirements Valid Invalid Written & signed Any written instrument Oral (except permissible gift) by grantor signed by grantor Signed only by grantee Identifies grantor Separate grantor & grantee Ambiguous/unidentifiable grantee(s) & grantee (except concurrent estates) Nonexistent grantee Identifiable by name or description Identifies land Identifiable by reasonable Ambiguous description (but extrin","synonyms":["rip","rive","pull"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":16,"sourceQuestionPage":45,"sourceQuestionPages":[45],"sourceExplanationPages":[46,47],"sourceAnswerPages":[46,47],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-017","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man borrowed money from a lender and mortgaged land that he owned to secure repayment of the loan. Before he had completely repaid the loan, the man conveyed the land to an investor, who expressly assumed the loan. The note and mortgage did not contain a due-on-sale clause. After the investor had made several payments on the loan, she defaulted on two payments. The lender notified the man and the investor of its intention to accelerate the loan pursuant to the terms of the note and mortgage unless the default was cured within 60 days. When neither the man nor the investor made the required payment, the lender accelerated the loan and initiated foreclosure proceedings, naming both the man and the investor as party defendants. The foreclosure sale resulted in a deficiency. The lender has sought a deficiency judgment against only the man, because the investor has become insolvent in the meantime. Will the court likely find the man liable for the deficiency?","choices":{"A":"No, because the investor's express assumption of the loan released the man from liability.","B":"No, because the lender must first seek to obtain a deficiency judgment against the investor.","C":"Yes, because even after the assumption, the man remains liable as a surety of the investor in the absence of a release from the lender.","D":"Yes, because the note and mortgage did not contain a due-on-sale clause."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A mortgage is an interest in real property given to a lender (mortgagee) to secure a debt. The debtor (mortgagor) can freely transfer mortgaged property to a grantee unless the mortgage states otherwise. After the transfer, the mortgage remains attached to the property and the debtor remains personally liable for the mortgage debt. But the grantee will become liable for the debt only if the grantee expressly agrees to assume the mortgage. Assumption of a mortgage makes the grantee primarily liable for the debt and the debtor secondarily liable as a surety in the absence of a release from the lender (Choice A). This means a lender can sue the grantee, the debtor, or both for any deficiency resulting from a foreclosure sale (Choice B). If the debtor pays any amount of the deficiency, the debtor can seek reimbursement from the grantee. (Choice D) A due-on-sale clause allows a lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor sells the mortgaged property without the lender's permission. However, this type of clause (or the absence thereof) does not affect the debtor's liability to the lender for a deficiency judgment after a foreclosure sale.\n\nEducational objective: A grantee who assumes a mortgage is primarily responsible for a deficiency from a foreclosure sale, and the debtor is secondarily liable as a surety in the absence of a release from the lender. The lender can sue the grantee, the debtor, or both to recover the deficiency amount, and the debtor can seek reimbursement from the grantee.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (assumption of a mortgage).\n\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 967 (2019) (deficiency judgments).","educationalObjective":"A grantee who assumes a mortgage is primarily responsible for a deficiency from a foreclosure sale, and the debtor is secondarily liable as a surety in the absence of a release from the lender. The lender can sue the grantee, the debtor, or both to recover the deficiency amount, and the debtor can seek reimbursement from the grantee.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (assumption of a mortgage).\n\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 967 (2019) (deficiency judgments).","rule":"A grantee who assumes a mortgage is primarily responsible for a deficiency from a foreclosure sale, and the debtor is secondarily liable as a surety in the absence of a release from the lender. The lender can sue the grantee, the debtor, or both to recover the deficiency amount, and the debtor can seek reimbursement from the grantee.","irac":{"issue":"Will the court likely find the man liable for the deficiency?","rule":"A grantee who assumes a mortgage is primarily responsible for a deficiency from a foreclosure sale, and the debtor is secondarily liable as a surety in the absence of a release from the lender. The lender can sue the grantee, the debtor, or both to recover the deficiency amount, and the debtor can seek reimbursement from the grantee.","analysis":"A mortgage is an interest in real property given to a lender (mortgagee) to secure a debt. The debtor (mortgagor) can freely transfer mortgaged property to a grantee unless the mortgage states otherwise. After the transfer, the mortgage remains attached to the property and the debtor remains personally liable for the mortgage debt. But the grantee will become liable for the debt only if the grantee expressly agrees to assume the mortgage. Assumption of a mortgage makes the grantee primarily liable for the debt and the debtor secondarily liable as a surety in the absence of a release from the lender (Choice A). This means a lender can sue the grantee, the debtor, or both for any deficiency resulting from a foreclosure sale (Choice B). If the debtor pays any amount of the deficiency, the debtor can seek reimbursement from the grantee. (Choice D) A due-on-sale clause allows a lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor sells the mortgaged property without the lender's permission. However, this type of clause (or the absence thereof) does not affect the debtor's liability to the lender for a deficiency judgment after a foreclosure sale.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because even after the assumption, the man remains liable as a surety of the investor in the absence of a release from the lender."},"questionZh":"一名男子向贷款人借了钱，并抵押了他拥有的土地以确保偿还贷款。在他完全偿还贷款之前，该男子将土地转让给了一位投资者，而投资者明确承担了这笔贷款。该票据和抵押贷款不包含到期出售条款。在投资者多次偿还贷款后，她拖欠了两次付款。贷款人通知该男子和投资者，除非在 60 天内解决违约问题，否则其打算根据票据和抵押贷款的条款加速贷款。当该男子和投资者均未支付所需付款时，贷方加速了贷款并启动了止赎程序，将该男子和投资者都列为当事人被告。取消抵押品赎回权的出售导致了缺陷。贷方仅针对该男子寻求缺陷判决，因为投资者在此期间已资不抵债。法院可能会认定该男子对缺陷负有责任吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为投资者明确承担贷款，使该人免于承担责任。","B":"不可以，因为贷款人必须首先寻求获得针对投资者的缺陷判断。","C":"是的，因为即使在假设之后，在没有贷款人释放的情况下，该男子仍然作为投资者的担保人承担责任。","D":"是的，因为票据和抵押贷款不包含到期出售条款。"},"explanationZh":"抵押贷款是给予贷方（抵押权人）以担保债务的不动产权益。除非抵押另有规定，债务人（抵押人）可以自由地将抵押财产转让给受让人。转让后，抵押仍然附着在财产上，债务人仍然对抵押债务承担个人责任。但只有在受让人明确同意承担抵押权的情况下，受让人才承担债务责任。抵押贷款的承担使得受让人对债务承担主要责任，而在贷款人没有解除债务的情况下，债务人作为担保人承担次要责任（选择 A）。这意味着贷方可以就止赎出售造成的任何缺陷起诉受让人、债务人或两者（选择 B）。如果债务人支付了任何不足额，债务人可以向受让人寻求偿还。 （选项D）到期出售条款允许如果债务人未经贷款人许可出售抵押财产，贷款人可以要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。然而，此类条款（或没有此类条款）并不影响债务人在取消抵押品赎回权出售后因缺陷判断而对贷方承担的责任。\n\n教育目标：承担抵押贷款的受让人对丧失抵押品赎回权的出售造成的损失负有主要责任，而在贷款人未解除抵押品赎回权的情况下，债务人则作为担保人承担次要责任。贷款人可以起诉受让人、债务人或两者，以追回不足金额，而债务人可以向受让人寻求偿还。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.1（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款的假设）。\n\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 967 (2019)（缺陷判断）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"承担抵押贷款的受让人对丧失抵押品赎回权的出售造成的损失负有主要责任，而在贷款人未解除抵押品赎回权的情况下，债务人则作为担保人承担次要责任。贷款人可以起诉受让人、债务人或两者，以追回不足金额，而债务人可以向受让人寻求偿还。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会认定该男子对缺陷负有责任吗？","rule":"承担抵押贷款的受让人对丧失抵押品赎回权的出售造成的损失负有主要责任，而在贷款人未解除抵押品赎回权的情况下，债务人则作为担保人承担次要责任。贷款人可以起诉受让人、债务人或两者，以追回不足金额，而债务人可以向受让人寻求偿还。","analysis":"抵押贷款是给予贷方（抵押权人）以担保债务的不动产权益。除非抵押另有规定，债务人（抵押人）可以自由地将抵押财产转让给受让人。转让后，抵押仍然附着在财产上，债务人仍然对抵押债务承担个人责任。但只有在受让人明确同意承担抵押权的情况下，受让人才承担债务责任。抵押贷款的承担使得受让人对债务承担主要责任，而在贷款人没有解除债务的情况下，债务人作为担保人承担次要责任（选择 A）。这意味着贷方可以就止赎出售造成的任何缺陷起诉受让人、债务人或两者（选择 B）。如果债务人支付了任何不足额，债务人可以向受让人寻求偿还。 （选项D）到期出售条款允许如果债务人未经贷款人许可出售抵押财产，贷款人可以要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。然而，此类条款（或没有此类条款）并不影响债务人在取消抵押品赎回权出售后因缺陷判断而对贷方承担的责任。\n\n教育目标：承担抵押贷款的受让人对丧失抵押品赎回权的出售造成的损失负有主要责任，而在贷款人未解除抵押品赎回权的情况下，债务人则作为担保人承担次要责任。贷款人可以起诉受让人、债务人或两者，以追回不足金额，而债务人可以向受让人寻求偿还。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.1（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款的假设）。\n\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 967 (2019)（缺陷判断）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为即使在假设之后，在没有贷款人释放的情况下，该男子仍然作为投资者的担保人承担责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"deficiency judgment","ipa":"/dɪfˈɪʃənsi dʒˈədʒmənt/","syllables":"de·fi·ciency judg·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不足额判决：拍卖价不足清偿债务时对债务人作出的余额金钱判决","contextualMeaning":"不足额判决：拍卖价不足清偿债务时对债务人作出的余额金钱判决","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The lender has sought a deficiency judgment against only the man, because the investor has become insolvent in the meantime.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"accelerate","ipa":"/æksˈɛlɝˌeɪt/","syllables":"ac·cel·er·ate","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 加速, 促进","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“accelerate”","wordForm":"动词；词形 p:accelerated/d:accelerated/i:accelerating/3:accelerates","sourceSentence":"The lender notified the man and the investor of its intention to accelerate the loan pursuant to the terms of the note and mortgage unless the default was cured within 60 days.","synonyms":["speed up","speed","quicken"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-65d2c11ad335daa7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":17,"sourceQuestionPage":48,"sourceQuestionPages":[48],"sourceExplanationPages":[49,50],"sourceAnswerPages":[49,50],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-018","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"When a homeowner became ill, he properly executed a deed sufficient to convey his home to his nephew, who was then serving overseas in the military. Two persons signed as witnesses to qualify the deed for recording under an applicable statute. The homeowner handed the deed to his nephew's friend and said to her, \"I want [the nephew] to have my home. Please take this deed for him.\" Shortly thereafter, the nephew's friend learned that the homeowner's death was imminent. One day before the homeowner's death, the nephew's friend recorded the deed. The nephew returned home shortly after the homeowner's death, learned about the deed, and took possession of the home. The homeowner had died intestate, leaving a daughter as his sole heir. When she asserted ownership of the home, the nephew brought an appropriate action against her to determine title. The law of the jurisdiction requires only two witnesses for a will to be properly executed. If the court rules for the nephew and against the daughter, what will be the most likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"The deed was delivered when the homeowner handed it to the nephew's friend.","B":"The delivery of the deed was accomplished by the recording of the deed.","C":"The homeowner's death consummated a valid gift causa mortis to the nephew.","D":"The homeowner's properly executed deed was effective as a testamentary document."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A deed is a legal instrument that transfers ownership of real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee). But to be effective, the deed must be: delivered by the grantor – evidenced by the grantor's present intent to convey ownership to the grantee and accepted by the grantee – presumed if the transfer is beneficial to the grantee. Delivery is presumed when the deed has been recorded or is in the grantee's possession. Delivery also occurs when the deed is physically handed to the grantee's agent—ie, a third party acting on behalf of the grantee. Here, the homeowner properly executed a deed conveying his home to the nephew. Since that transfer benefited the nephew, acceptance is presumed. Therefore, if the court rules in favor of the nephew, the most likely explanation is that the deed was delivered when the homeowner handed it to the nephew's friend (agent) and told her to take it on the nephew's behalf. (Choice B) Recording a deed (ie, filing it in official land records) creates a presumption of delivery. But here, delivery occurred when the homeowner handed the deed to the nephew's friend, which happened before the deed was recorded. (Choice C) A gift causa mortis (ie, deathbed gift) is a gift of personal property made in expectation of the grantor's imminent death. Here, the homeowner gifted real property (his home) to the nephew. And this gift was not made in expectation of imminent death since the homeowner's death became imminent after he delivered the deed. (Choice D) A deed requires the grantor's present intent to convey ownership to the grantee—ie, the grantor must intend that title transfer immediately. And since a testamentary document (eg, a will or testament) only takes effect upon the testator's death (no intent to presently transfer), a deed cannot serve as a testamentary document.\n\nEducational objective: To effectively transfer an interest in real property, a deed must be (1) delivered by the grantor and (2) accepted by the grantee. The grantor can deliver the deed by handing it to the grantee's agent.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Donative Transfers § 32.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1992) (transfer relating to land).","educationalObjective":"To effectively transfer an interest in real property, a deed must be (1) delivered by the grantor and (2) accepted by the grantee. The grantor can deliver the deed by handing it to the grantee's agent.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property: Donative Transfers § 32.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1992) (transfer relating to land).","rule":"To effectively transfer an interest in real property, a deed must be (1) delivered by the grantor and (2) accepted by the grantee. The grantor can deliver the deed by handing it to the grantee's agent.","irac":{"issue":"If the court rules for the nephew and against the daughter, what will be the most likely explanation?","rule":"To effectively transfer an interest in real property, a deed must be (1) delivered by the grantor and (2) accepted by the grantee. The grantor can deliver the deed by handing it to the grantee's agent.","analysis":"A deed is a legal instrument that transfers ownership of real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee). But to be effective, the deed must be: delivered by the grantor – evidenced by the grantor's present intent to convey ownership to the grantee and accepted by the grantee – presumed if the transfer is beneficial to the grantee. Delivery is presumed when the deed has been recorded or is in the grantee's possession. Delivery also occurs when the deed is physically handed to the grantee's agent—ie, a third party acting on behalf of the grantee. Here, the homeowner properly executed a deed conveying his home to the nephew. Since that transfer benefited the nephew, acceptance is presumed. Therefore, if the court rules in favor of the nephew, the most likely explanation is that the deed was delivered when the homeowner handed it to the nephew's friend (agent) and told her to take it on the nephew's behalf. (Choice B) Recording a deed (ie, filing it in official land records) creates a presumption of delivery. But here, delivery occurred when the homeowner handed the deed to the nephew's friend, which happened before the deed was recorded. (Choice C) A gift causa mortis (ie, deathbed gift) is a gift of personal property made in expectation of the grantor's imminent death. Here, the homeowner gifted real property (his home) to the nephew. And this gift was not made in expectation of imminent death since the homeowner's death became imminent after he delivered the deed. (Choice D) A deed requires the grantor's present intent to convey ownership to the grantee—ie, the grantor must intend that title transfer immediately. And since a testamentary document (eg, a will or testament) only takes effect upon the testator's death (no intent to presently transfer), a deed cannot serve as a testamentary document.","conclusion":"Answer A: The deed was delivered when the homeowner handed it to the nephew's friend."},"questionZh":"当房主生病时，他妥善办理了一份足以将房屋转让给当时在海外服兵役的侄子的契约。两人作为见证人签字，以证明该契约有资格根据适用的法规进行记录。房主将房契交给了侄子的朋友，并对她说：“我想要[侄子]拥有我的家，请帮他拿走这张房契。”此后不久，侄子的朋友得知房主即将去世。房主去世前一天，侄子的朋友记录了这件事。房主去世后不久，侄子回到家中，得知了这件事，并接管了房子。房主去世时没有留下遗嘱，留下一个女儿作为他唯一的继承人。当她声称拥有该房屋的所有权时，侄子对她提起了适当的诉讼以确定所有权。司法管辖区的法律只需要两名证人即可正确执行遗嘱。如果法院做出有利于侄子而不利于女儿的裁决，最可能的解释是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"当房主将其交给侄子的朋友时，契约就已交付。","B":"契约的交付是通过契约的记录来完成的。","C":"房主的去世为侄子提供了一份有效的死亡礼物。","D":"房主正确签署的契约作为遗嘱文件是有效的。"},"explanationZh":"契约是将不动产所有权从所有者（授予人）转让给另一人（受让人）的法律文书。但为了有效，契约必须： 由设保人交付——由设保人目前将所有权转让给受让人的意图证明并被受让人接受——假定转让对受让人有利。当契约已被记录或为受让人占有时，即视为已交付。当契约实际交付给受让人的代理人（即代表受让人行事的第三方）时，也会发生交付。在这里，房主妥善履行了将其房屋转让给侄子的契约。由于该转让对侄子有利，因此假定接受。因此，如果法院做出有利于侄子的裁决，最可能的解释是房主将房产契交给侄子的朋友（代理人）并告诉她代表侄子领取时，房产证就已交付。 （选择 B）记录契约（即，将其归档到官方土地记录中）会产生交付的推定。但在这里，交付发生在房主将契约交给侄子的朋友时，这发生在契约被记录之前。 （选项C） 死因赠与（即临终赠与）是在赠与人即将死亡的情况下赠送的个人财产。在这里，房主将不动产（他的房子）赠予了侄子。而且这份礼物并不是在预期即将死亡的情况下赠送的，因为在房主交付契约后，他的死亡就迫在眉睫了。 （选择 D） 契约要求设保人目前有意将所有权转让给受让人，即设保人必须打算立即转让所有权。由于遗嘱文件（例如遗嘱或遗嘱）仅在立遗嘱人死亡（目前无意转让）时生效，因此契约不能作为遗嘱文件。\n\n教育目标：为了有效转让不动产权益，契约必须 (1) 由授予人交付，并且 (2) 被受让人接受。授予人可以将契约交给受让人的代理人来交付。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：捐赠转让 § 32.3（Am. Law Inst. 1992）（与土地有关的转让）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为了有效转让不动产权益，契约必须 (1) 由授予人交付，并且 (2) 被受让人接受。授予人可以将契约交给受让人的代理人来交付。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院做出有利于侄子而不利于女儿的裁决，最可能的解释是什么？","rule":"为了有效转让不动产权益，契约必须 (1) 由授予人交付，并且 (2) 被受让人接受。授予人可以将契约交给受让人的代理人来交付。","analysis":"契约是将不动产所有权从所有者（授予人）转让给另一人（受让人）的法律文书。但为了有效，契约必须： 由设保人交付——由设保人目前将所有权转让给受让人的意图证明并被受让人接受——假定转让对受让人有利。当契约已被记录或为受让人占有时，即视为已交付。当契约实际交付给受让人的代理人（即代表受让人行事的第三方）时，也会发生交付。在这里，房主妥善履行了将其房屋转让给侄子的契约。由于该转让对侄子有利，因此假定接受。因此，如果法院做出有利于侄子的裁决，最可能的解释是房主将房产契交给侄子的朋友（代理人）并告诉她代表侄子领取时，房产证就已交付。 （选择 B）记录契约（即，将其归档到官方土地记录中）会产生交付的推定。但在这里，交付发生在房主将契约交给侄子的朋友时，这发生在契约被记录之前。 （选项C） 死因赠与（即临终赠与）是在赠与人即将死亡的情况下赠送的个人财产。在这里，房主将不动产（他的房子）赠予了侄子。而且这份礼物并不是在预期即将死亡的情况下赠送的，因为在房主交付契约后，他的死亡就迫在眉睫了。 （选择 D） 契约要求设保人目前有意将所有权转让给受让人，即设保人必须打算立即转让所有权。由于遗嘱文件（例如遗嘱或遗嘱）仅在立遗嘱人死亡（目前无意转让）时生效，因此契约不能作为遗嘱文件。\n\n教育目标：为了有效转让不动产权益，契约必须 (1) 由授予人交付，并且 (2) 被受让人接受。授予人可以将契约交给受让人的代理人来交付。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：捐赠转让 § 32.3（Am. Law Inst. 1992）（与土地有关的转让）。","conclusion":"答案 A：当房主将其交给侄子的朋友时，契约就已交付。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-b2ee90cf5210c6f8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":18,"sourceQuestionPage":51,"sourceQuestionPages":[51],"sourceExplanationPages":[52,53],"sourceAnswerPages":[52,53],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-019","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A landowner mortgaged her land to a nationally chartered bank as security for a loan. The mortgage provided that the bank could, at its option, declare the entire loan due and payable if all or any part of the land, or an interest therein, was sold or transferred without the bank's prior written consent. Subsequently, the landowner wanted to sell the land to a neighbor by an installment land contract, but the bank refused to consent. The neighbor's credit was good, and all mortgage payments to the bank were fully current. The landowner and the neighbor consulted an attorney about their proposed transaction, their desire to complete it, and the bank's refusal to consent. What would the attorney's best advice be?","choices":{"A":"By making the transfer in land contract form, the landowner will prevent enforcement of the due-on-sale clause if the mortgage payments are kept current.","B":"Even if the landowner transfers to the neighbor by land contract, the bank may accelerate the debt and foreclose if the full amount is not paid.","C":"The due-on-sale clause is void as an illegal restraint on alienation of the fee simple, so they may proceed.","D":"The only effect of the due-on-sale clause is that the proposed transfer will automatically make the neighbor personally liable on the debt, whether or not the neighbor specifically agrees to assume it."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Common mortgage provisions\n\nAcceleration clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor defaults\n\nDue-on-sale clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor sells mortgaged property without permission\n\nDue-on- Allows creditor to accelerate mortgage obligation if debtor obtains encumbrance clause second mortgage or otherwise encumbers property\n\nDefeasance clause Requires creditor to give debtor legal title to property & release mortgage lien once all payments are made\n\nMortgage documents are used to convey a lender an interest in real property to secure debt. These documents may contain a due-on-sale clause, which allows the lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor transfers the mortgaged property without the lender's consent. If the debtor is unable to pay, the lender can bring a foreclosure action to force the sale of the property to satisfy any remaining debt. Here, the landowner seeks to transfer the mortgaged property to the neighbor through an installment land contract without the bank's consent. However, the method used to transfer the mortgaged property has no effect on the enforceability of a due-on-sale clause—even if mortgage payments are kept current (Choice A). The attorney should therefore advise the parties that the bank may accelerate the debt and foreclose if the full amount is not paid. (Choice C) Due-on-sale clauses are restraints on alienation because they restrict a debtor's ability to transfer the mortgaged property. However, these restraints are considered reasonable and can be challenged only if the lender engaged in unconscionable conduct when exercising the clause. (Choice D) Under the due-on-sale clause, the unauthorized transfer will make the landowner personally liable on the debt, but it would make the neighbor liable only if he specifically agreed to assume it.\n\nEducational objective: A due-on-sale clause allows a lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor transfers the mortgaged property without permission. If the debtor is unable to pay, the lender may bring a foreclosure action to force the sale of the property to satisfy any remaining debt.\n\nReferences\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 415 (2019) (due-on-sale clauses).\n\nRustic Hills Shopping Plaza, Inc. v. Columbia Sav. & Loan Ass'n, 661 P.2d 254, 256–57 (Colo. 1983) (holding that the unauthorized transfer of mortgaged property by an installment land contract triggered the due-on-sale clause).","educationalObjective":"A due-on-sale clause allows a lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor transfers the mortgaged property without permission. If the debtor is unable to pay, the lender may bring a foreclosure action to force the sale of the property to satisfy any remaining debt.","references":"55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 415 (2019) (due-on-sale clauses).\n\nRustic Hills Shopping Plaza, Inc. v. Columbia Sav. & Loan Ass'n, 661 P.2d 254, 256–57 (Colo. 1983) (holding that the unauthorized transfer of mortgaged property by an installment land contract triggered the due-on-sale clause).","rule":"A due-on-sale clause allows a lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor transfers the mortgaged property without permission. If the debtor is unable to pay, the lender may bring a foreclosure action to force the sale of the property to satisfy any remaining debt.","irac":{"issue":"What would the attorney's best advice be?","rule":"A due-on-sale clause allows a lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor transfers the mortgaged property without permission. If the debtor is unable to pay, the lender may bring a foreclosure action to force the sale of the property to satisfy any remaining debt.","analysis":"Common mortgage provisions\n\nAcceleration clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor defaults\n\nDue-on-sale clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor sells mortgaged property without permission\n\nDue-on- Allows creditor to accelerate mortgage obligation if debtor obtains encumbrance clause second mortgage or otherwise encumbers property\n\nDefeasance clause Requires creditor to give debtor legal title to property & release mortgage lien once all payments are made\n\nMortgage documents are used to convey a lender an interest in real property to secure debt. These documents may contain a due-on-sale clause, which allows the lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor transfers the mortgaged property without the lender's consent. If the debtor is unable to pay, the lender can bring a foreclosure action to force the sale of the property to satisfy any remaining debt. Here, the landowner seeks to transfer the mortgaged property to the neighbor through an installment land contract without the bank's consent. However, the method used to transfer the mortgaged property has no effect on the enforceability of a due-on-sale clause—even if mortgage payments are kept current (Choice A). The attorney should therefore advise the parties that the bank may accelerate the debt and foreclose if the full amount is not paid. (Choice C) Due-on-sale clauses are restraints on alienation because they restrict a debtor's ability to transfer the mortgaged property. However, these restraints are considered reasonable and can be challenged only if the lender engaged in unconscionable conduct when exercising the clause. (Choice D) Under the due-on-sale clause, the unauthorized transfer will make the landowner personally liable on the debt, but it would make the neighbor liable only if he specifically agreed to assume it.","conclusion":"Answer B: Even if the landowner transfers to the neighbor by land contract, the bank may accelerate the debt and foreclose if the full amount is not paid."},"questionZh":"一位土地所有者将她的土地抵押给一家国家特许银行作为贷款担保。抵押贷款规定，如果未经银行事先书面同意出售或转让全部或任何部分土地或其中的权益，银行可以选择宣布全部贷款到期并应付。随后，地主想以分期土地承包方式将土地卖给邻居，但银行不同意。邻居的信用良好，向银行支付的所有抵押贷款均已全额偿还。土地所有者和邻居就他们拟议的交易、完成交易的愿望以及银行拒绝同意的问题咨询了律师。律师的最佳建议是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"通过以土地合同形式进行转让，如果抵押付款保持最新状态，土地所有者将阻止执行到期销售条款。","B":"即使土地所有者通过土地合同转让给邻居，如果未全额支付，银行也可能加速债务并取消抵押品赎回权。","C":"到期出售条款作为对费用转让的非法限制而无效，因此他们可以继续进行。","D":"到期出售条款的唯一作用是，拟议的转让将自动使邻居对债务承担个人责任，无论邻居是否明确同意承担债务。"},"explanationZh":"常见抵押条款\n\n加速条款 如果债务人违约，债权人可以要求全额到期应付贷款\n\n到期出售条款 如果债务人未经许可出售抵押财产，债权人可以要求偿还全部到期应付贷款\n\n到期 - 如果债务人获得产权负担条款第二抵押贷款或以其他方式抵押财产，则允许债权人加速抵押贷款义务\n\n废止条款 要求债权人给予债务人财产的合法所有权并在支付所有款项后释放抵押留置权\n\n抵押文件用于向贷方传达不动产权益以担保债务。这些文件可能包含到期出售条款，如果债务人未经贷款人同意转让抵押财产，贷款人可以要求全额支付剩余抵押债务。如果债务人无法付款，贷款人可以提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼，强制出售财产以偿还任何剩余债务。这里，土地所有者在未经银行同意的情况下，试图通过分期土地合同将抵押财产转让给邻居。然而，用于转让抵押财产的方法对到期出售条款的可执行性没有影响——即使抵押贷款付款保持最新（选择 A）。因此，律师应告知当事人，如果未全额支付，银行可能会加速偿还债务并取消抵押品赎回权。 （选项C） 到期出售条款是对转让的限制，因为它们限制了债务人转让抵押财产的能力。然而，这些限制被认为是合理的，只有当贷款人在行使该条款时有不合理行为时，才能受到质疑。 （选项D）根据到期出售条款，未经授权的转让将使土地所有者对债务承担个人责任，但只有在他明确同意承担的情况下，邻居才会承担责任。\n\n教育目标：如果债务人未经许可转让抵押财产，到期出售条款允许贷款人要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。如果债务人无法付款，贷款人可以提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼，强制出售财产以偿还任何剩余债务。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d 抵押贷款 § 415 (2019)（到期销售条款）。\n\nRustic Hills Shopping Plaza, Inc. 诉 Columbia Sav. & Loan Ass'n, 661 P.2d 254, 256–57 (Colo. 1983)（认为通过分期付款土地合同未经授权转让抵押财产引发了到期出售条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"到期出售条款允许贷款人在债务人未经许可转让抵押财产的情况下要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。如果债务人无法付款，贷款人可以提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼，强制出售财产以偿还任何剩余债务。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师的最佳建议是什么？","rule":"到期出售条款允许贷款人在债务人未经许可转让抵押财产的情况下要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。如果债务人无法付款，贷款人可以提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼，强制出售财产以偿还任何剩余债务。","analysis":"常见抵押条款\n\n加速条款 如果债务人违约，债权人可以要求全额到期应付贷款\n\n到期出售条款 如果债务人未经许可出售抵押财产，债权人可以要求偿还全部到期应付贷款\n\n到期 - 如果债务人获得产权负担条款第二抵押贷款或以其他方式抵押财产，则允许债权人加速抵押贷款义务\n\n废止条款 要求债权人给予债务人财产的合法所有权并在支付所有款项后释放抵押留置权\n\n抵押文件用于向贷方传达不动产权益以担保债务。这些文件可能包含到期出售条款，如果债务人未经贷款人同意转让抵押财产，贷款人可以要求全额支付剩余抵押债务。如果债务人无法付款，贷款人可以提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼，强制出售财产以偿还任何剩余债务。这里，土地所有者在未经银行同意的情况下，试图通过分期土地合同将抵押财产转让给邻居。然而，用于转让抵押财产的方法对到期出售条款的可执行性没有影响——即使抵押贷款付款保持最新（选择 A）。因此，律师应告知当事人，如果未全额支付，银行可能会加速偿还债务并取消抵押品赎回权。 （选项C） 到期出售条款是对转让的限制，因为它们限制了债务人转让抵押财产的能力。然而，这些限制被认为是合理的，只有当贷款人在行使该条款时有不合理行为时，才能受到质疑。 （选项D）根据到期出售条款，未经授权的转让将使土地所有者对债务承担个人责任，但只有在他明确同意承担的情况下，邻居才会承担责任。\n\n教育目标：如果债务人未经许可转让抵押财产，到期出售条款允许贷款人要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。如果债务人无法付款，贷款人可以提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼，强制出售财产以偿还任何剩余债务。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d 抵押贷款 § 415 (2019)（到期销售条款）。\n\nRustic Hills Shopping Plaza, Inc. 诉 Columbia Sav. & Loan Ass'n, 661 P.2d 254, 256–57 (Colo. 1983)（认为通过分期付款土地合同未经授权转让抵押财产引发了到期出售条款）。","conclusion":"答案 B：即使土地所有者通过土地合同转让给邻居，如果未全额支付，银行也可能加速债务并取消抵押品赎回权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":19,"sourceQuestionPage":54,"sourceQuestionPages":[54],"sourceExplanationPages":[55,56],"sourceAnswerPages":[55,56],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-020","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man who owned property in fee simple mortgaged the property to a bank to secure a loan of $100,000. The mortgage was promptly and properly recorded. The man later mortgaged the property to his mother to secure a loan of $50,000. This mortgage was also promptly and properly recorded. Subsequently, the man conveyed the property to a purchaser. About a year later, the purchaser borrowed $100,000 from an elderly widow and gave her a mortgage on the property to secure repayment of the loan. The widow did not know about the mortgage held by the mother. The understanding between the purchaser and the widow was that the purchaser would use the $100,000 to pay off the mortgage held by the bank and that the widow would therefore have a first mortgage on the property. The widow's mortgage was promptly and properly recorded. The purchaser paid the $100,000 received from the widow to the bank and obtained and recorded a release of the bank's mortgage. The $50,000 debt secured by the mother's mortgage was not paid when it was due, and the mother brought an appropriate action to foreclose, joining the man, the purchaser, and the widow as defendants and alleging that the mother's mortgage was senior to the widow's mortgage on the property. If the court rules that the widow's mortgage is entitled to priority over the mother's mortgage, which of the following determinations are necessary to support that ruling?","choices":{"A":"The bank's mortgage was originally senior to the mother's mortgage.","B":"The widow is entitled to have the bank's mortgage revived for her benefit, and the widow is entitled to be subrogated to the bank's original position as senior mortgagee.","C":"There are no countervailing equities in favor of the mother.","D":"All of the above."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Lien priority is generally determined by who records first (ie, first in time, first in right). However, subrogation allows a third party who fully pays off a senior mortgage to take that senior mortgage's priority so long as the third party had no actual knowledge of junior interests.* This does not harm junior interests because they retain the same lien priority. Here, the bank's mortgage was recorded first, so it had priority over the mother's mortgage. The widow's mortgage was recorded last. However, the widow's loan was used to fully pay off the bank's senior mortgage, and the widow did not know about the mother's mortgage. As a result, the widow acquired the bank's senior mortgage through subrogation. Also, there are no equities in the mother's favor because she has retained her second-in-priority status (Choices A, B & C). *In a minority of jurisdictions, record notice is also sufficient to preclude subrogation.\n\nEducational objective: Subrogation allows a third party to take a senior mortgage's priority when the third party (1) fully pays off the senior mortgage and (2) has no actual knowledge of junior interests.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.6 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (doctrine of subrogation).","educationalObjective":"Subrogation allows a third party to take a senior mortgage's priority when the third party (1) fully pays off the senior mortgage and (2) has no actual knowledge of junior interests.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.6 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (doctrine of subrogation).","rule":"Subrogation allows a third party to take a senior mortgage's priority when the third party (1) fully pays off the senior mortgage and (2) has no actual knowledge of junior interests.","irac":{"issue":"If the court rules that the widow's mortgage is entitled to priority over the mother's mortgage, which of the following determinations are necessary to support that ruling?","rule":"Subrogation allows a third party to take a senior mortgage's priority when the third party (1) fully pays off the senior mortgage and (2) has no actual knowledge of junior interests.","analysis":"Lien priority is generally determined by who records first (ie, first in time, first in right). However, subrogation allows a third party who fully pays off a senior mortgage to take that senior mortgage's priority so long as the third party had no actual knowledge of junior interests.* This does not harm junior interests because they retain the same lien priority. Here, the bank's mortgage was recorded first, so it had priority over the mother's mortgage. The widow's mortgage was recorded last. However, the widow's loan was used to fully pay off the bank's senior mortgage, and the widow did not know about the mother's mortgage. As a result, the widow acquired the bank's senior mortgage through subrogation. Also, there are no equities in the mother's favor because she has retained her second-in-priority status (Choices A, B & C). *In a minority of jurisdictions, record notice is also sufficient to preclude subrogation.","conclusion":"Answer D: All of the above."},"questionZh":"一名拥有简单房产的男子将房产抵押给银行以获得 10 万美元的贷款。抵押贷款被及时、正确地记录。该男子后来将房产抵押给了他的母亲，以获得 5 万美元的贷款。这笔抵押贷款也被及时、正确地记录下来。随后，该男子将房产转让给买家。大约一年后，买家向一位年迈的寡妇借了10万美元，并以房产抵押给她以确保偿还贷款。寡妇不知道母亲持有的抵押贷款。买家与寡妇之间的谅解是，买家将用这10万元偿还银行持有的按揭，而寡妇因此将拥有该物业的第一按揭。寡妇的抵押贷款被及时、正确地记录下来。购买者将从寡妇处收到的 10 万美元支付给银行，并获得并记录了银行抵押贷款的释放。由母亲的抵押贷款担保的 50,000 美元债务到期时未偿还，母亲提起适当的取消赎回权诉讼，将男子、买家和寡妇列为被告，并声称母亲的抵押贷款优先于寡妇的财产抵押贷款。如果法院裁定寡妇的抵押贷款有权优先于母亲的抵押贷款，则需要以下哪些决定来支持该裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"银行的抵押贷款本来就比母亲的抵押贷款优先。","B":"寡妇有权要求银行为她的利益恢复抵押贷款，并且寡妇有权代位继承银行原来作为高级抵押权人的地位。","C":"不存在有利于母亲的抵消性股票。","D":"上述所有的。"},"explanationZh":"留置权优先权一般由谁先记录决定（即时间第一、权利第一）。然而，代位权允许完全偿还高级抵押贷款的第三方获得该高级抵押贷款的优先权，只要该第三方实际上不了解次级利益。*这不会损害次级利益，因为它们保留相同的留置权优先权。这里，银行的房贷是先入账的，所以优先于母亲的房贷。寡妇的抵押贷款最后记录。然而，寡妇的贷款被用来全额偿还银行的高级抵押贷款，而寡妇并不知道母亲的抵押贷款。结果，寡妇通过代位获得了银行的高级抵押贷款。此外，没有任何股权对母亲有利，因为她保留了第二优先地位（选择 A、B 和 C）。 *在少数司法管辖区，记录通知也足以排除代位求偿权。\n\n教育目标：当第三方 (1) 完全还清高级抵押贷款且 (2) 对次级权益没有实际了解时，代位权允许第三方获得高级抵押贷款的优先权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.6（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（代位权原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当第三方 (1) 完全清偿高级抵押贷款且 (2) 不实际了解次级权益时，代位权允许第三方取得高级抵押贷款的优先权。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院裁定寡妇的抵押贷款有权优先于母亲的抵押贷款，则需要以下哪些决定来支持该裁决？","rule":"当第三方 (1) 完全清偿高级抵押贷款且 (2) 不实际了解次级权益时，代位权允许第三方取得高级抵押贷款的优先权。","analysis":"留置权优先权一般由谁先记录决定（即时间第一、权利第一）。然而，代位权允许完全偿还高级抵押贷款的第三方获得该高级抵押贷款的优先权，只要该第三方实际上不了解次级利益。*这不会损害次级利益，因为它们保留相同的留置权优先权。这里，银行的房贷是先入账的，所以优先于母亲的房贷。寡妇的抵押贷款最后记录。然而，寡妇的贷款被用来全额偿还银行的高级抵押贷款，而寡妇并不知道母亲的抵押贷款。结果，寡妇通过代位获得了银行的高级抵押贷款。此外，没有任何股权对母亲有利，因为她保留了第二优先地位（选择 A、B 和 C）。 *在少数司法管辖区，记录通知也足以排除代位求偿权。\n\n教育目标：当第三方 (1) 完全还清高级抵押贷款且 (2) 对次级权益没有实际了解时，代位权允许第三方获得高级抵押贷款的优先权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.6（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（代位权原则）。","conclusion":"答案 D：上述所有的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"senior mortgage","ipa":"/sˈinjɝ mˈɔɹɡədʒ/","syllables":"se·nior mort·gage","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"优先抵押：顺位在前、先从止赎价款受偿的抵押","contextualMeaning":"优先抵押：顺位在前、先从止赎价款受偿的抵押","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The widow is entitled to have the bank's mortgage revived for her benefit, and the widow is entitled to be subrogated to the bank's original position as senior mortgagee.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"lien priority","ipa":"/lˈin pɹaɪˈɔɹəti/","syllables":"lien pri·or·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"留置权顺位：多个担保权益之间受偿的先后次序","contextualMeaning":"留置权顺位：多个担保权益之间受偿的先后次序","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Lien priority is generally determined by who records first (ie, first in time, first in right).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"record notice","ipa":"/ɹʌkˈɔɹd nˈoʊtəs/","syllables":"record no·tice","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"登记通知：产权档案中适当登记的文件依法推定购买人知悉","contextualMeaning":"登记通知：产权档案中适当登记的文件依法推定购买人知悉","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"*In a minority of jurisdictions, record notice is also sufficient to preclude subrogation.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"support","ipa":"/sʌpˈɔɹt/","syllables":"sup·port","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 支持, 支撑, 援助, 供养, 支撑物；vt. 支援, 支撑, 帮助, 支持, 忍受, 供养, 证实；[计] 后援","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“support”","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:supported/i:supporting/3:supports/p:supported/s:supports","sourceSentence":"If the court rules that the widow's mortgage is entitled to priority over the mother's mortgage, which of the following determinations are necessary to support that ruling?","synonyms":["documentation","reinforcement","reenforcement","supporting"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-dff99d06d24ac541"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":20,"sourceQuestionPage":57,"sourceQuestionPages":[57],"sourceExplanationPages":[58],"sourceAnswerPages":[58],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-021","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A mother executed a will devising vacant land to her son. The mother showed the will to her son. Thereafter, the son purported to convey the land to a friend by a warranty deed that contained no exceptions. The friend paid value for the land and promptly recorded the deed without having first conducted any title search. The friend never took possession of the land. The mother later died, and the will devising the land to her son was duly admitted to probate. Thereafter, the friend conducted a title search for the land and asked the son for a new deed. The son refused, because the value of the land had doubled, but he offered to refund the purchase price to the friend. The friend has sued to quiet title to the land. Is the friend likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the son had no interest in the land at the time of conveyance.","B":"No, because the son offered to refund the purchase price.","C":"Yes, because of the doctrine of estoppel by deed.","D":"Yes, because the deed was recorded."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The doctrine of estoppel by deed (ie, after-acquired title) applies when a grantor conveys property to a grantee by warranty deed—not quitclaim deed—before the grantor has acquired title to that property. And once the grantor receives title, this doctrine will cause it to automatically transfer to the grantee. The grantee can then accept that transfer or reject it (and sue for damages). Here, the son conveyed the land to a friend by a warranty deed before the son acquired title under his mother's will. But since the son received title when his mother later died, that title automatically transferred to the friend (Choice A). And since the friend accepted that transfer by suing to quiet title to the land, the friend will likely prevail because of the doctrine of estoppel by deed. (Choice B) Although the son offered to refund the purchase price, the friend was not required to accept it. Instead, the friend chose to accept title to the land by suing to quiet title—not to recover damages. (Choice D) Although the friend recorded the deed, this would only protect the friend from subsequent ownership claims. And since the son acquired title to the land before it automatically transferred to the friend, the recording would not protect against the son's earlier ownership claim.\n\nEducational objective: The doctrine of estoppel by deed applies when a grantor conveys property by warranty deed before acquiring title to that property. And once the grantor receives title, it will automatically transfer to the earlier grantee who can then accept or reject the transfer.\n\nReferences\n23 Am. Jur. 2d Deeds § 278 (2018) (estoppel by deed).","educationalObjective":"The doctrine of estoppel by deed applies when a grantor conveys property by warranty deed before acquiring title to that property. And once the grantor receives title, it will automatically transfer to the earlier grantee who can then accept or reject the transfer.","references":"23 Am. Jur. 2d Deeds § 278 (2018) (estoppel by deed).","rule":"The doctrine of estoppel by deed applies when a grantor conveys property by warranty deed before acquiring title to that property. And once the grantor receives title, it will automatically transfer to the earlier grantee who can then accept or reject the transfer.","irac":{"issue":"Is the friend likely to prevail?","rule":"The doctrine of estoppel by deed applies when a grantor conveys property by warranty deed before acquiring title to that property. And once the grantor receives title, it will automatically transfer to the earlier grantee who can then accept or reject the transfer.","analysis":"The doctrine of estoppel by deed (ie, after-acquired title) applies when a grantor conveys property to a grantee by warranty deed—not quitclaim deed—before the grantor has acquired title to that property. And once the grantor receives title, this doctrine will cause it to automatically transfer to the grantee. The grantee can then accept that transfer or reject it (and sue for damages). Here, the son conveyed the land to a friend by a warranty deed before the son acquired title under his mother's will. But since the son received title when his mother later died, that title automatically transferred to the friend (Choice A). And since the friend accepted that transfer by suing to quiet title to the land, the friend will likely prevail because of the doctrine of estoppel by deed. (Choice B) Although the son offered to refund the purchase price, the friend was not required to accept it. Instead, the friend chose to accept title to the land by suing to quiet title—not to recover damages. (Choice D) Although the friend recorded the deed, this would only protect the friend from subsequent ownership claims. And since the son acquired title to the land before it automatically transferred to the friend, the recording would not protect against the son's earlier ownership claim.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because of the doctrine of estoppel by deed."},"questionZh":"一位母亲立了一份遗嘱，为她的儿子设计了一块空地。母亲向儿子表明了自己的意愿。此后，儿子声称通过一份不包含任何例外的保修契约将土地转让给朋友。这位朋友支付了土地的价值，并立即记录了契约，而没有先进行任何产权搜索。这位朋友从未占有这片土地。母亲后来去世了，为她儿子设计土地的遗嘱被正式批准进行遗嘱认证。此后，朋友对这块土地进行了产权调查，并要求儿子提供新的契约。儿子拒绝了，因为土地的价值增加了​​一倍，但他提出将购买价退还给朋友。这位朋友已提起诉讼，要求获得该土地的安静所有权。这位朋友有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为儿子在转让时对土地没有兴趣。","B":"不，因为儿子提出退还货款。","C":"是的，因为禁止反悔的原则。","D":"是的，因为行为已被记录。"},"explanationZh":"当设保人在获得该财产的所有权之前，通过保证契据（而非无偿债权契据）将财产转让给受让人时，适用契约禁止反言原则（即事后获得的所有权）。一旦授予人获得所有权，该学说将使其自动转让给受让人。然后，受让人可以接受该转让或拒绝该转让（并起诉要求赔偿）。在这里，儿子在母亲的遗嘱下获得所有权之前，通过保修契约将土地转让给朋友。但由于儿子在母亲后来去世后获得了头衔，该头衔自动转移给朋友（选项 A）。由于朋友通过起诉获得土地的安静所有权来接受转让，因此由于行为禁止反言原则，朋友很可能会胜诉。 （选项B） 虽然儿子提出退还货款，但朋友并不一定要接受。相反，这位朋友选择通过提起静默所有权诉讼来接受土地所有权，而不是要求赔偿损失。 （选项D）虽然朋友记录了契约，但这只能保护朋友免受随后的所有权索赔。由于儿子在土地自动转让给朋友之前就获得了土地所有权，因此录音不能防止儿子先前的所有权主张。\n\n教育目标：当授予人在获得财产所有权之前通过保证契约转让财产时，契约禁止反言原则适用。一旦授予人收到所有权，它将自动转让给先前的受让人，后者可以接受或拒绝转让。\n\n参考文献\n23 上午。尤尔。 2d 契约 § 278 (2018)（契约禁止反言）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当设保人在获得财产所有权之前通过保证契约转让财产时，契约禁止反言原则适用。一旦授予人收到所有权，它将自动转让给先前的受让人，后者可以接受或拒绝转让。","iracZh":{"issue":"这位朋友有可能获胜吗？","rule":"当设保人在获得财产所有权之前通过保证契约转让财产时，契约禁止反言原则适用。一旦授予人收到所有权，它将自动转让给先前的受让人，后者可以接受或拒绝转让。","analysis":"当设保人在获得该财产的所有权之前，通过保证契据（而非无偿债权契据）将财产转让给受让人时，适用契约禁止反言原则（即事后获得的所有权）。一旦授予人获得所有权，该学说将使其自动转让给受让人。然后，受让人可以接受该转让或拒绝该转让（并起诉要求赔偿）。在这里，儿子在母亲的遗嘱下获得所有权之前，通过保修契约将土地转让给朋友。但由于儿子在母亲后来去世后获得了头衔，该头衔自动转移给朋友（选项 A）。由于朋友通过起诉获得土地的安静所有权来接受转让，因此由于行为禁止反言原则，朋友很可能会胜诉。 （选项B） 虽然儿子提出退还货款，但朋友并不一定要接受。相反，这位朋友选择通过提起静默所有权诉讼来接受土地所有权，而不是要求赔偿损失。 （选项D）虽然朋友记录了契约，但这只能保护朋友免受随后的所有权索赔。由于儿子在土地自动转让给朋友之前就获得了土地所有权，因此录音不能防止儿子先前的所有权主张。\n\n教育目标：当授予人在获得财产所有权之前通过保证契约转让财产时，契约禁止反言原则适用。一旦授予人收到所有权，它将自动转让给先前的受让人，后者可以接受或拒绝转让。\n\n参考文献\n23 上午。尤尔。 2d 契约 § 278 (2018)（契约禁止反言）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为禁止反悔的原则。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"estoppel by deed","ipa":"/i'stɒpl bˈaɪ dˈid/","syllables":"estop·pel by deed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"契据禁反言：无权转让人日后取得产权时，产权自动归于其先前受让人","contextualMeaning":"契据禁反言：无权转让人日后取得产权时，产权自动归于其先前受让人","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because of the doctrine of estoppel by deed.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-cdfb0f9735eeab74"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":21,"sourceQuestionPage":59,"sourceQuestionPages":[59],"sourceExplanationPages":[60,61],"sourceAnswerPages":[60,61],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-022","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A businessman executed a promissory note for $200,000 to a bank, secured by a mortgage on commercial real estate owned by the businessman. The promissory note stated that the businessman was not personally liable for the mortgage debt. One week later, a finance company obtained a judgment against the businessman for $50,000 and filed the judgment in the county where the real estate was located. At the time the judgment was filed, the finance company had no actual notice of the bank's mortgage. Two weeks after that filing, the bank recorded its mortgage on the businessman's real estate. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"Unless the same be recorded according to law, no conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice or against judgment creditors without notice.\" The finance company sued to enforce its judgment lien against the businessman's real estate. The bank intervened in the action, contending that the judgment lien was a second lien on the real estate and that its mortgage was a first lien. Is the bank's contention correct?","choices":{"A":"No, because the businessman was not personally liable for the mortgage debt, and the mortgage was therefore void.","B":"No, because the judgment lien was recorded before the mortgage, and the finance company had no actual notice of the mortgage.","C":"Yes, because a mortgage prior in time has priority over a subsequent judgment lien.","D":"Yes, because the recording of a mortgage relates back to the date of execution of the mortgage note."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The priority of mortgages and other liens is generally determined by their recording dates—not by the order in which those interests were created (Choices C & D). Purchase- money mortgages (PMMs), however, have super priority over all other liens—whether or not the PMM or those liens are recorded—because PMMs secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property. Here, the businessman mortgaged commercial real estate he already owned to the bank (not a PMM). One week later, the finance company filed a judgment lien against the real estate in a jurisdiction whose recording act expressly protects judgment creditors who lack notice.* Since the judgment lien was recorded before the mortgage, the finance company lacked constructive (ie, record or inquiry) notice. Therefore, the judgment lien has first priority because the finance company also lacked actual notice of the bank's prior mortgage. *Most recording acts protect only subsequent purchasers for value who lack notice of prior competing interests in the property—not judgment creditors since they do not pay value for an interest in the property. (Choice A) A mortgage is enforceable even if no one is personally liable for payment of the mortgage debt.\n\nEducational objective: The priority of mortgages (and other liens) is generally determined by the order of recording unless the mortgage enjoys super priority as a purchase-money mortgage.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.1 cmt. a (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (noting that recording acts may alter the priority of mortgages differently than their chronological creation).","educationalObjective":"The priority of mortgages (and other liens) is generally determined by the order of recording unless the mortgage enjoys super priority as a purchase-money mortgage.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.1 cmt. a (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (noting that recording acts may alter the priority of mortgages differently than their chronological creation).","rule":"The priority of mortgages (and other liens) is generally determined by the order of recording unless the mortgage enjoys super priority as a purchase-money mortgage.","irac":{"issue":"Is the bank's contention correct?","rule":"The priority of mortgages (and other liens) is generally determined by the order of recording unless the mortgage enjoys super priority as a purchase-money mortgage.","analysis":"The priority of mortgages and other liens is generally determined by their recording dates—not by the order in which those interests were created (Choices C & D). Purchase- money mortgages (PMMs), however, have super priority over all other liens—whether or not the PMM or those liens are recorded—because PMMs secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property. Here, the businessman mortgaged commercial real estate he already owned to the bank (not a PMM). One week later, the finance company filed a judgment lien against the real estate in a jurisdiction whose recording act expressly protects judgment creditors who lack notice.* Since the judgment lien was recorded before the mortgage, the finance company lacked constructive (ie, record or inquiry) notice. Therefore, the judgment lien has first priority because the finance company also lacked actual notice of the bank's prior mortgage. *Most recording acts protect only subsequent purchasers for value who lack notice of prior competing interests in the property—not judgment creditors since they do not pay value for an interest in the property. (Choice A) A mortgage is enforceable even if no one is personally liable for payment of the mortgage debt.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the judgment lien was recorded before the mortgage, and the finance company had no actual notice of the mortgage."},"questionZh":"一名商人向一家银行开立了一张 20 万美元的期票，以该商人拥有的商业房地产抵押贷款作为担保。期票指出，该商人个人不对抵押贷款债务承担责任。一周后，一家金融公司获得了对该商人赔偿5万美元的判决，并在该房产所在县提交了判决。在作出判决时，财务公司并没有收到银行抵押贷款的实际通知。提交文件两周后，银行记录了该商人房地产的抵押贷款。司法管辖区的备案法规定：“除非依法进行备案，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者或判决债权人有利。”金融公司提起诉讼，要求强制执行对该商人房地产的判决留置权。银行介入了诉讼，认为判决留置权是房地产的第二留置权，而其抵押贷款是第一留置权。银行的说法正确吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为商人个人不对抵押贷款债务承担责任，因此抵押贷款无效。","B":"不会，因为判决留置权是在抵押之前记录的，而财务公司并没有实际的抵押通知。","C":"是的，因为提前的抵押贷款优先于随后的判决留置权。","D":"是的，因为抵押贷款的记录与抵押票据的执行日期有关。"},"explanationZh":"抵押贷款和其他留置权的优先权通常由其记录日期决定，而不是由这些权益创建的顺序决定（选择 C ​​和 D）。然而，购房款抵押贷款 (PMM) 比所有其他留置权拥有超级优先权——无论 PMM 或那些留置权是否被记录——因为 PMM 保证了抵押财产的购买价格。在这里，商人将他已经拥有的商业房地产抵押给银行（不是 PMM）。一周后，财务公司在其记录法明确保护缺乏通知的判决债权人的司法管辖区针对房地产提出了判决留置权。*由于判决留置权是在抵押贷款之前记录的，因此财务公司缺乏推定（即记录或查询）通知。因此，判决留置权具有优先权，因为财务公司也缺乏对银行事先抵押的实际通知。 *大多数记录行为仅保护后续购买者的价值，他们不知道之前对该财产的竞争利益，而不是判决债权人，因为他们不为财产权益支付价值。 （选择 A）即使没有人个人负责偿还抵押贷款债务，抵押贷款也是可执行的。\n\n教育目标： 抵押贷款（和其他留置权）的优先权通常由记录顺序决定，除非抵押贷款作为购房款抵押贷款享有超级优先权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.1 cmt。 a（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（注意到记录行为可能会改变抵押贷款的优先权，这与按时间顺序创建不同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"抵押贷款（和其他留置权）的优先权一般按记录顺序确定，除非抵押贷款作为购房款抵押贷款享有超级优先权。","iracZh":{"issue":"银行的说法正确吗？","rule":"抵押贷款（和其他留置权）的优先权一般按记录顺序确定，除非抵押贷款作为购房款抵押贷款享有超级优先权。","analysis":"抵押贷款和其他留置权的优先权通常由其记录日期决定，而不是由这些权益创建的顺序决定（选择 C ​​和 D）。然而，购房款抵押贷款 (PMM) 比所有其他留置权拥有超级优先权——无论 PMM 或那些留置权是否被记录——因为 PMM 保证了抵押财产的购买价格。在这里，商人将他已经拥有的商业房地产抵押给银行（不是 PMM）。一周后，财务公司在其记录法明确保护缺乏通知的判决债权人的司法管辖区针对房地产提出了判决留置权。*由于判决留置权是在抵押贷款之前记录的，因此财务公司缺乏推定（即记录或查询）通知。因此，判决留置权具有优先权，因为财务公司也缺乏对银行事先抵押的实际通知。 *大多数记录行为仅保护后续购买者的价值，他们不知道之前对该财产的竞争利益，而不是判决债权人，因为他们不为财产权益支付价值。 （选择 A）即使没有人个人负责偿还抵押贷款债务，抵押贷款也是可执行的。\n\n教育目标： 抵押贷款（和其他留置权）的优先权通常由记录顺序决定，除非抵押贷款作为购房款抵押贷款享有超级优先权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.1 cmt。 a（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（注意到记录行为可能会改变抵押贷款的优先权，这与按时间顺序创建不同）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为判决留置权是在抵押之前记录的，而财务公司并没有实际的抵押通知。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"actual notice","ipa":"/ˈæktʃəwəl nˈoʊtəs/","syllables":"ac·tual no·tice","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"实际通知：购买人真实知道在先土地权益","contextualMeaning":"实际通知：购买人真实知道在先土地权益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"At the time the judgment was filed, the finance company had no actual notice of the bank's mortgage.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-614928657ec24590"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":22,"sourceQuestionPage":62,"sourceQuestionPages":[62],"sourceExplanationPages":[63,64,65],"sourceAnswerPages":[63,64,65],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-023","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"Eight years ago, a farmer, prior to moving to a distant city, conveyed his farm to his son by a quitclaim deed. The son paid no consideration. The son, who was 19 years old, without formal education, and without experience in business, took possession of the farm and operated the farm but neglected to record his deed. Subsequently, the farmer conveyed the farm to a friend by warranty deed. The friend, a substantial land and timber promoter, paid valuable consideration for the deed to him. He was unaware of the son's possession, his quitclaim deed, or his relationship to the farmer. The friend promptly and properly recorded his deed and began removing timber from the land. Immediately upon learning of the friend's actions, the son recorded his deed and brought an appropriate action to enjoin the friend from removing the timber and to quiet title in the son. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.\" In this action, should the friend prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the equities favor the son.","B":"No, because the son's possession charged the friend with notice.","C":"Yes, because a warranty deed for valuable consideration takes priority over a quitclaim deed without consideration.","D":"Yes, because the farmer's subsequent conveyance to the friend revoked the gift to the son."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Recording acts are used to evaluate the priority of competing property interests. In a notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier property interest if the BFP lacked notice. Here, the farmer gifted his farm to his son. The farmer then sold the farm to his friend. The friend was unaware of the son's prior interest when he purchased the farm (no actual notice), and the son had not recorded his deed at that time (no record notice). But since the son possessed and operated the farm, the friend was charged with inquiry notice of the son's prior interest. Therefore, the son's interest has priority and the friend will not prevail in the quiet title action. (Choice A) The equities (ie, fairness factors) may favor the son since he was only 19 years old, lacked a formal education, and had no experience in acquiring property. But this information is irrelevant in determining whether his interest or the friend's has priority. (Choice C) In a notice jurisdiction, a warranty deed for valuable consideration (eg, the friend's deed) only has priority over an earlier quitclaim deed without consideration (eg, the son's deed) if the recipient of the warranty deed lacked notice. Here, the friend had inquiry notice of the son's interest, so the friend will not prevail. (Choice D) A gift of land is effective once it has been delivered by the grantor and accepted by the grantee (eg, the son). The grantor's subsequent conveyance of the land to a third party (eg, the friend) does not revoke the prior gift. Therefore, the friend will not prevail on this basis.\n\nEducational objective: In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser without notice of an earlier property interest will prevail. Inquiry notice exists if a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property).","educationalObjective":"In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser without notice of an earlier property interest will prevail. Inquiry notice exists if a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property).","references":"","rule":"In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser without notice of an earlier property interest will prevail. Inquiry notice exists if a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property).","irac":{"issue":"Eight years ago, a farmer, prior to moving to a distant city, conveyed his farm to his son by a quitclaim deed. The son paid no consideration. The son, who was 19 years old, without formal education, and without experience in business, took possession of the farm and operated the farm but neglected to record his deed. Subsequently, the farmer conveyed the farm to a friend by warranty deed. The friend, a substantial land and timber promoter, paid valuable consideration for the deed to him. He was unaware of the son's possession, his quitclaim deed, or his relationship to the farmer. The friend promptly and properly recorded his deed and began removing timber from the land. Immediately upon learning of the friend's actions, the son recorded his deed and brought an appropriate action to enjoin the friend from removing the timber and to quiet title in the son. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.\" In this action, should the friend prevail?","rule":"In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser without notice of an earlier property interest will prevail. Inquiry notice exists if a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property).","analysis":"Recording acts are used to evaluate the priority of competing property interests. In a notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier property interest if the BFP lacked notice. Here, the farmer gifted his farm to his son. The farmer then sold the farm to his friend. The friend was unaware of the son's prior interest when he purchased the farm (no actual notice), and the son had not recorded his deed at that time (no record notice). But since the son possessed and operated the farm, the friend was charged with inquiry notice of the son's prior interest. Therefore, the son's interest has priority and the friend will not prevail in the quiet title action. (Choice A) The equities (ie, fairness factors) may favor the son since he was only 19 years old, lacked a formal education, and had no experience in acquiring property. But this information is irrelevant in determining whether his interest or the friend's has priority. (Choice C) In a notice jurisdiction, a warranty deed for valuable consideration (eg, the friend's deed) only has priority over an earlier quitclaim deed without consideration (eg, the son's deed) if the recipient of the warranty deed lacked notice. Here, the friend had inquiry notice of the son's interest, so the friend will not prevail. (Choice D) A gift of land is effective once it has been delivered by the grantor and accepted by the grantee (eg, the son). The grantor's subsequent conveyance of the land to a third party (eg, the friend) does not revoke the prior gift. Therefore, the friend will not prevail on this basis.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the son's possession charged the friend with notice."},"questionZh":"八年前，一位农民在搬到一个遥远的城市之前，通过一份无偿所有权契约将他的农场转让给了他的儿子。儿子没有考虑。儿子当时 19 岁，没有受过正规教育，也没有经商经验，他拥有农场并经营农场，但没有记录他的契约。随后，该农民通过保修契约将农场转让给朋友。这位朋友是一位重要的土地和木材发起人，为他的这份契约付出了宝贵的代价。他不知道儿子的财产、他的放弃权利契约，也不知道他与农民的关系。这位朋友立即正确地记录了他的行为，并开始从土地上清除木材。在得知朋友的行为后，儿子立即记录了自己的行为，并采取了适当的行动，禁止朋友拆除木材，并取消了儿子的所有权。该司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“除非依法记录，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者。”在这次行动中，朋友应该占上风吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为股票有利于儿子。","B":"不，因为儿子的财产需要朋友注意。","C":"是的，因为有价对价的保修契约优先于无对价的无偿索赔契约。","D":"是的，因为农民随后将财产转让给朋友，从而撤销了给儿子的礼物。"},"explanationZh":"记录行为用于评估竞争财产利益的优先顺序。在通知管辖区（如此处所示），如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 缺乏通知，则其财产权益优先于先前的财产权益。在这里，农民将他的农场赠予了他的儿子。农民随后将农场卖给了他的朋友。朋友在购买农场时并不知道儿子先前的权益（没有实际通知），而且儿子当时也没有记录他的契约（没有记录通知）。但由于儿子拥有并经营农场，因此该朋友被指控询问儿子先前的权益。因此，在平静的产权诉讼中，儿子的利益优先，朋友不会占上风。 （选项A） 股权（即公平因素）可能对儿子有利，因为他只有19岁，缺乏正规教育，也没有获得财产的经验。但此信息与确定他的兴趣还是朋友的兴趣优先无关。 （选择C）在通知司法管辖区，如果保证契约的接收人没有通知，则有价对价的保证契约（例如，朋友的契约）仅比较早的无对价的无偿债权契约（例如，儿子的契约）具有优先权。在此，朋友已询问过儿子的兴趣，所以朋友不予采纳。 （选项D） 土地赠与一旦由赠与人交付并被受赠人（例如儿子）接受后即生效。授予人随后将土地转让给第三方（例如朋友）并不撤销先前的赠与。所以，朋友不会以此为据而占上风。\n\n教育目标：在通知管辖区，未提前通知财产权益的善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）优先。如果买方知道或应该知道会促使理性人进行调查的情况（例如，对财产的明显使用），则存在询问通知。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在通知司法管辖区，未提前通知财产权益的善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）优先。如果买方知道或应该知道会促使理性人进行调查的情况（例如，对财产的明显使用），则存在询问通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"八年前，一位农民在搬到一个遥远的城市之前，通过一份无偿所有权契约将他的农场转让给了他的儿子。儿子没有考虑。儿子当时 19 岁，没有受过正规教育，也没有经商经验，他拥有农场并经营农场，但没有记录他的契约。随后，该农民通过保修契约将农场转让给朋友。这位朋友是一位重要的土地和木材发起人，为他的这份契约付出了宝贵的代价。他不知道儿子的财产、他的放弃权利契约，也不知道他与农民的关系。这位朋友立即正确地记录了他的行为，并开始从土地上清除木材。在得知朋友的行为后，儿子立即记录了自己的行为，并采取了适当的行动，禁止朋友拆除木材，并取消了儿子的所有权。该司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“除非依法记录，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者。”在这次行动中，朋友应该占上风吗？","rule":"在通知司法管辖区，未提前通知财产权益的善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）优先。如果买方知道或应该知道会促使理性人进行调查的情况（例如，对财产的明显使用），则存在询问通知。","analysis":"记录行为用于评估竞争财产利益的优先顺序。在通知管辖区（如此处所示），如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 缺乏通知，则其财产权益优先于先前的财产权益。在这里，农民将他的农场赠予了他的儿子。农民随后将农场卖给了他的朋友。朋友在购买农场时并不知道儿子先前的权益（没有实际通知），而且儿子当时也没有记录他的契约（没有记录通知）。但由于儿子拥有并经营农场，因此该朋友被指控询问儿子先前的权益。因此，在平静的产权诉讼中，儿子的利益优先，朋友不会占上风。 （选项A） 股权（即公平因素）可能对儿子有利，因为他只有19岁，缺乏正规教育，也没有获得财产的经验。但此信息与确定他的兴趣还是朋友的兴趣优先无关。 （选择C）在通知司法管辖区，如果保证契约的接收人没有通知，则有价对价的保证契约（例如，朋友的契约）仅比较早的无对价的无偿债权契约（例如，儿子的契约）具有优先权。在此，朋友已询问过儿子的兴趣，所以朋友不予采纳。 （选项D） 土地赠与一旦由赠与人交付并被受赠人（例如儿子）接受后即生效。授予人随后将土地转让给第三方（例如朋友）并不撤销先前的赠与。所以，朋友不会以此为据而占上风。\n\n教育目标：在通知管辖区，未提前通知财产权益的善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）优先。如果买方知道或应该知道会促使理性人进行调查的情况（例如，对财产的明显使用），则存在询问通知。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为儿子的财产需要朋友注意。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-cd5714736c236394"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":23,"sourceQuestionPage":66,"sourceQuestionPages":[66],"sourceExplanationPages":[67,68],"sourceAnswerPages":[67,68],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-024","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A brother and sister owned in fee simple a large piece of property as tenants in common, each owning an undivided one-half interest. The brother and sister joined in mortgaging the property to their cousin by a properly recorded mortgage that contained a general warranty clause. The brother became disenchanted with land-owning and notified the sister that he would no longer contribute to the payment of installments due to the cousin. After the mortgage was in default and the cousin made demand for payment of the entire amount of principal and interest due, the sister tendered to the cousin, and the cousin deposited, a check for one-half of the amount due to the cousin. The sister then demanded a release of her undivided one-half interest. The cousin refused to release any interest in the property. The sister promptly brought an action against the cousin to quiet title to an undivided one-half interest in the property. In such action, should the sister win or lose?","choices":{"A":"Lose, because the cousin's title had been warranted by an express provision of the mortgage.","B":"Lose, because there was no redemption from the mortgage.","C":"Win, because the co-tenancy of the mortgagors was in common and not joint.","D":"Win, because the sister is entitled to marshalling."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Right of redemption\n\n(regaining title after default on loan)\n\nTiming Assertion of right\n\nEquitable At any time prior to foreclosure sale Pay full amount of debt due right (including interest) to lender\n\n(always exists)\n\nStatutory Statutory period after property has Pay foreclosure sale price to right been sold in foreclosure purchasing party\n\n(if statute exists)\n\nTenants who jointly mortgage co-owned property are responsible for repaying that debt in proportion to their respective ownership interests (unless they agree otherwise). If the mortgage goes into default, any tenant can avoid foreclosure by exercising the equitable right of redemption. This right allows a tenant to cure the default by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt—including any amount owed by the other tenants. The paying tenant can then seek contribution (ie, reimbursement) from the other tenants for their share of the debt. Here, the brother and sister (tenants in common) jointly mortgaged their property to the cousin. When the brother stopped paying his share of the debt, the mortgage went into default. And though the sister tried to redeem her one-half interest in the property by paying half the amount due, she could only cure the default by paying the full amount due. Therefore, there was no redemption from the mortgage, and the sister's quiet title action will fail. (Choice A) A general warranty clause in a mortgage (as in a deed) promises that title is free of any encumbrances or defects. Although this guarantees that the cousin will acquire clear title upon foreclosure, it does not extinguish the sister's right to redeem the property. (Choice C) The sister had to pay the full amount of the debt to redeem the property, regardless of whether she was a tenant in common or joint tenant. (Choice D) Marshalling applies when (1) a senior creditor has a security interest in multiple assets and (2) a junior creditor has an interest in one of those assets. It requires the senior creditor to first foreclose on the asset against which it holds an exclusive security\n\ninterest to preserve the junior creditor's interest in the remaining asset. But since this case only involves one security interest (the mortgage), marshalling does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: The equitable right of redemption allows a debtor to avoid foreclosure by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt. This means that a tenant can only redeem co-owned property by paying the amount owed by all tenants.\n\nReferences\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 392 (2018) (common law right of redemption).","educationalObjective":"The equitable right of redemption allows a debtor to avoid foreclosure by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt. This means that a tenant can only redeem co-owned property by paying the amount owed by all tenants.","references":"55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 392 (2018) (common law right of redemption).","rule":"The equitable right of redemption allows a debtor to avoid foreclosure by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt. This means that a tenant can only redeem co-owned property by paying the amount owed by all tenants.","irac":{"issue":"In such action, should the sister win or lose?","rule":"The equitable right of redemption allows a debtor to avoid foreclosure by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt. This means that a tenant can only redeem co-owned property by paying the amount owed by all tenants.","analysis":"Right of redemption\n\n(regaining title after default on loan)\n\nTiming Assertion of right\n\nEquitable At any time prior to foreclosure sale Pay full amount of debt due right (including interest) to lender\n\n(always exists)\n\nStatutory Statutory period after property has Pay foreclosure sale price to right been sold in foreclosure purchasing party\n\n(if statute exists)\n\nTenants who jointly mortgage co-owned property are responsible for repaying that debt in proportion to their respective ownership interests (unless they agree otherwise). If the mortgage goes into default, any tenant can avoid foreclosure by exercising the equitable right of redemption. This right allows a tenant to cure the default by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt—including any amount owed by the other tenants. The paying tenant can then seek contribution (ie, reimbursement) from the other tenants for their share of the debt. Here, the brother and sister (tenants in common) jointly mortgaged their property to the cousin. When the brother stopped paying his share of the debt, the mortgage went into default. And though the sister tried to redeem her one-half interest in the property by paying half the amount due, she could only cure the default by paying the full amount due. Therefore, there was no redemption from the mortgage, and the sister's quiet title action will fail. (Choice A) A general warranty clause in a mortgage (as in a deed) promises that title is free of any encumbrances or defects. Although this guarantees that the cousin will acquire clear title upon foreclosure, it does not extinguish the sister's right to redeem the property. (Choice C) The sister had to pay the full amount of the debt to redeem the property, regardless of whether she was a tenant in common or joint tenant. (Choice D) Marshalling applies when (1) a senior creditor has a security interest in multiple assets and (2) a junior creditor has an interest in one of those assets. It requires the senior creditor to first foreclose on the asset against which it holds an exclusive security\n\ninterest to preserve the junior creditor's interest in the remaining asset. But since this case only involves one security interest (the mortgage), marshalling does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer B: Lose, because there was no redemption from the mortgage."},"questionZh":"一对兄弟姐妹以按份共有人（tenant in common）的身份拥有一大块财产，每人拥有不可分割的一半权益。兄妹俩通过一份包含一般保修条款的正确记录的抵押贷款，将财产抵押给了他们的表弟。哥哥对拥有土地不再抱有希望，并通知妹妹，他将不再支付表弟的分期付款。抵押贷款违约，表弟要求支付全部到期本金和利息后，姐姐向表弟付款，表弟存入了一张支票，金额为应付表弟金额的一半。姐姐随后要求释放她不可分割的二分之一权益。表弟拒绝透露该房产的任何权益。姐姐立即对表弟提起诉讼，要求其获得该财产不可分割的二分之一权益的所有权。这样的行动，姐姐到底应该赢还是输呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"输了，因为表弟的所有权已得到抵押贷款的明确规定的保证。","B":"输了，因为没有赎回抵押贷款。","C":"胜诉，因为抵押人的共同共有（joint tenancy）是共同的，而不是联合的。","D":"赢了，因为姐姐有权编组。"},"explanationZh":"赎回权\n\n（贷款违约后恢复所有权）\n\n权利主张的时机\n\n公平 在取消抵押品赎回权出售之前的任何时间 向贷款人支付全额债务到期权利（包括利息）\n\n（始终存在）\n\n法定期限 财产支付止赎出售价格以在止赎购买方出售权利后的法定期限\n\n（如果有法规的话）\n\n共同抵押共有财产的租户有责任按照各自所有权权益的比例偿还债务（除非他们另有约定）。如果抵押贷款违约，任何租户都可以通过行使公平的赎回权来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。这项权利允许租户通过全额支付未偿债务（包括其他租户所欠的任何金额）来解决违约问题。然后，付款的租户可以向其他租户寻求分担（即偿还）他们的债务份额。在这里，兄妹（共同房客）共同将财产抵押给表弟。当兄弟停止偿还他那部分债务时，抵押贷款就违约了。尽管姐姐试图通过支付一半的应付金额来赎回她在房产中的一半权益，但她只能通过支付全额应付金额来解决违约问题。因此，抵押贷款无法赎回，姐姐的不动产产权诉讼也将失败。 （选择 A）抵押贷款中的一般保证条款（如契约中的）承诺所有权不存在任何产权负担或缺陷。虽然这保证了表弟在丧失抵押品赎回权时将获得明确的所有权，但这并不能消除姐妹赎回财产的权利。 （选项C） 姐姐必须全额偿还债务才能赎回房产，无论她是按份共有人（tenant in common）还是联名租户。 （选择 D）编组适用于以下情况：(1) 高级债权人在多项资产中拥有担保权益，并且 (2) 初级债权人在其中一项资产中拥有权益。它要求高级债权人首先取消其持有专属担保的资产的赎回权\n\n保留次级债权人在剩余资产中的权益。但由于本案仅涉及一项担保权益（抵押），因此不适用编组。\n\n教育目标：公平的赎回权允许债务人通过全额支付未偿债务来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。这意味着租户只能通过支付所有租户所欠的金额来赎回共有财产。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 392 (2018)（普通法赎回权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"衡平法上的赎回权允许债务人通过全额支付未偿债务来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。这意味着租户只能通过支付所有租户所欠的金额来赎回共有财产。","iracZh":{"issue":"这样的行动，姐姐到底应该赢还是输呢？","rule":"衡平法上的赎回权允许债务人通过全额支付未偿债务来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。这意味着租户只能通过支付所有租户所欠的金额来赎回共有财产。","analysis":"赎回权\n\n（贷款违约后恢复所有权）\n\n权利主张的时机\n\n公平 在取消抵押品赎回权出售之前的任何时间 向贷款人支付全额债务到期权利（包括利息）\n\n（始终存在）\n\n法定期限 财产支付止赎出售价格以在止赎购买方出售权利后的法定期限\n\n（如果有法规的话）\n\n共同抵押共有财产的租户有责任按照各自所有权权益的比例偿还债务（除非他们另有约定）。如果抵押贷款违约，任何租户都可以通过行使公平的赎回权来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。这项权利允许租户通过全额支付未偿债务（包括其他租户所欠的任何金额）来解决违约问题。然后，付款的租户可以向其他租户寻求分担（即偿还）他们的债务份额。在这里，兄妹（共同房客）共同将财产抵押给表弟。当兄弟停止偿还他那部分债务时，抵押贷款就违约了。尽管姐姐试图通过支付一半的应付金额来赎回她在房产中的一半权益，但她只能通过支付全额应付金额来解决违约问题。因此，抵押贷款无法赎回，姐姐的不动产产权诉讼也将失败。 （选择 A）抵押贷款中的一般保证条款（如契约中的）承诺所有权不存在任何产权负担或缺陷。虽然这保证了表弟在丧失抵押品赎回权时将获得明确的所有权，但这并不能消除姐妹赎回财产的权利。 （选项C） 姐姐必须全额偿还债务才能赎回房产，无论她是按份共有人（tenant in common）还是联名租户。 （选择 D）编组适用于以下情况：(1) 高级债权人在多项资产中拥有担保权益，并且 (2) 初级债权人在其中一项资产中拥有权益。它要求高级债权人首先取消其持有专属担保的资产的赎回权\n\n保留次级债权人在剩余资产中的权益。但由于本案仅涉及一项担保权益（抵押），因此不适用编组。\n\n教育目标：公平的赎回权允许债务人通过全额支付未偿债务来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。这意味着租户只能通过支付所有租户所欠的金额来赎回共有财产。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 392 (2018)（普通法赎回权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：输了，因为没有赎回抵押贷款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"jointly","ipa":"/dʒˈɔɪntli/","syllables":"jointly","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 共同地, 连带地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“jointly”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Right of redemption (regaining title after default on loan) Timing Assertion of right Equitable At any time prior to foreclosure sale Pay full amount of debt due right (including interest) to lender (always exists) Statutory Statutory period after property has Pay foreclosure sale price to right been sold in foreclosure purchasing party (if statute exists) Tenants who jointly mortgage co-owned property are responsibl","synonyms":["collectively","conjointly","together with"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":24,"sourceQuestionPage":69,"sourceQuestionPages":[69],"sourceExplanationPages":[70,71],"sourceAnswerPages":[70,71],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-025","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Nuisance, Zoning & Support","question":"A man died testate. The man's estate consisted of a residence as well as significant personal property. By his duly probated will, the man devised the residence to a friend, who was specifically identified in the will. The residue of the estate was given to a stated charity. The man's friend, although alive at the time the man executed the will, had predeceased the man. The friend's wife and their child, who has a disability, both survived the man. The value of the residence has increased significantly because of recent zoning changes. There is credible extrinsic evidence that the man wanted his friend to own the residence after the man's death so that the friend and his wife could care for their child there. There is no applicable statute. If both the charity and the child claim the residence, to whom should the estate distribute the residence?","choices":{"A":"The charity, because the devise to the friend adeemed.","B":"The charity, because the devise to the friend lapsed.","C":"The child, because extrinsic evidence exists that the man's intent was to benefit the child.","D":"The child, because no conditions of survivorship were noted in the will."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Doctrines affecting conveyance by will\n\nLapse Causes devise to fail if beneficiary predeceases testator\n\nAdemption Causes devise to fail by either:\n\nextinction – specifically devised property not owned by testator (or destroyed or fundamentally changed) at death satisfaction – beneficiary received devised property (or other asset intended to satisfy devise) during testator's life Exoneration Allows beneficiary of specifically devised real property to use estate's remaining assets to pay off any encumbrances on that property\n\nAbatement Reduces devises that cannot be satisfied by assets remaining after testator's debts are paid Residuary devises abated first, followed by general & then specific devises The man (testator) died testate and left his residence to the friend (beneficiary) under a valid will. However, the doctrine of lapse will cause a devise to fail if the beneficiary dies before the testator (as seen here). In that case, neither the beneficiary nor his/her heirs will acquire the devised property. Instead, it will become part of the general estate and be distributed in accordance with the rest of the will. And if there are no other provisions in the will, the property will be distributed under intestacy laws. But since the man left the residue of his estate to the charity, the estate should distribute the residence to the charity—not the child. (Choice A) The doctrine of ademption will cause a specific devise to fail if the testator does not own the devised property upon death. But since the man owned the residence when he died, the devise to the friend did not adeem. (Choice C) Although extrinsic evidence exists that the man's intent was to benefit the child, the parol evidence rule prohibits a court from rewriting a will that lacks significant ambiguities. And since the man's will was clear and unambiguous, such evidence cannot be used to support the child's claim to the residence. (Choice D) Had the man's will included a condition of survivorship instructing that the residence go to the child in the event that the friend predeceased the man, the child would have received the residence. But since the man's will included no such condition, the residence should be distributed to the charity.\n\nEducational objective: The doctrine of lapse causes a devise to fail when the beneficiary predeceases the testator. The devised property will instead be distributed in accordance with other devises in the will or, if no others exist, in accordance with intestacy laws.\n\nReferences\n80 Am. Jur. 2d Wills § 1409 (2018) (doctrine of lapse).","educationalObjective":"The doctrine of lapse causes a devise to fail when the beneficiary predeceases the testator. The devised property will instead be distributed in accordance with other devises in the will or, if no others exist, in accordance with intestacy laws.","references":"80 Am. Jur. 2d Wills § 1409 (2018) (doctrine of lapse).","rule":"The doctrine of lapse causes a devise to fail when the beneficiary predeceases the testator. The devised property will instead be distributed in accordance with other devises in the will or, if no others exist, in accordance with intestacy laws.","irac":{"issue":"If both the charity and the child claim the residence, to whom should the estate distribute the residence?","rule":"The doctrine of lapse causes a devise to fail when the beneficiary predeceases the testator. The devised property will instead be distributed in accordance with other devises in the will or, if no others exist, in accordance with intestacy laws.","analysis":"Doctrines affecting conveyance by will\n\nLapse Causes devise to fail if beneficiary predeceases testator\n\nAdemption Causes devise to fail by either:\n\nextinction – specifically devised property not owned by testator (or destroyed or fundamentally changed) at death satisfaction – beneficiary received devised property (or other asset intended to satisfy devise) during testator's life Exoneration Allows beneficiary of specifically devised real property to use estate's remaining assets to pay off any encumbrances on that property\n\nAbatement Reduces devises that cannot be satisfied by assets remaining after testator's debts are paid Residuary devises abated first, followed by general & then specific devises The man (testator) died testate and left his residence to the friend (beneficiary) under a valid will. However, the doctrine of lapse will cause a devise to fail if the beneficiary dies before the testator (as seen here). In that case, neither the beneficiary nor his/her heirs will acquire the devised property. Instead, it will become part of the general estate and be distributed in accordance with the rest of the will. And if there are no other provisions in the will, the property will be distributed under intestacy laws. But since the man left the residue of his estate to the charity, the estate should distribute the residence to the charity—not the child. (Choice A) The doctrine of ademption will cause a specific devise to fail if the testator does not own the devised property upon death. But since the man owned the residence when he died, the devise to the friend did not adeem. (Choice C) Although extrinsic evidence exists that the man's intent was to benefit the child, the parol evidence rule prohibits a court from rewriting a will that lacks significant ambiguities. And since the man's will was clear and unambiguous, such evidence cannot be used to support the child's claim to the residence. (Choice D) Had the man's will included a condition of survivorship instructing that the residence go to the child in the event that the friend predeceased the man, the child would have received the residence. But since the man's will included no such condition, the residence should be distributed to the charity.","conclusion":"Answer B: The charity, because the devise to the friend lapsed."},"questionZh":"一名男子去世时留下遗嘱。该男子的遗产包括住宅和重要的个人财产。根据他的正式遗嘱，该男子为一位朋友设计了住所，遗嘱中特别指出了这位朋友的身份。遗产的剩余部分被捐给了一个指定的慈善机构。该男子的朋友虽然在该男子执行遗嘱时还活着，但已先于该男子去世。这位朋友的妻子和他们患有残疾的孩子都在这名男子的生命中幸存下来。由于最近的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变化，住宅的价值显着增加。有可靠的外在证据表明，该男子希望他的朋友在他死后拥有该住宅，以便朋友和他的妻子可以在那里照顾他们的孩子。没有适用的法规。如果慈善机构和孩子都主张该住宅，那么遗产应该将住宅分配给谁？","choicesZh":{"A":"慈善事业，是因为对朋友的认可。","B":"慈善事业，因为朋友的计划失效了。","C":"孩子，因为有外在证据表明该男子的意图是让孩子受益。","D":"孩子，因为遗嘱中没有注明生存条件。"},"explanationZh":"影响遗嘱转让的学说\n\n如果受益人先于遗嘱人去世，失效会导致设计失败\n\n赎回会因以下任一原因导致设计失败：\n\n消灭 – 在死亡时不属于立遗嘱人拥有的特别设计的财产（或被毁坏或根本改变） 满意 – 受益人在立遗嘱人的一生中收到设计的财产（或旨在满足设计的其他资产） 免除 允许特别设计的不动产的受益人使用遗产的剩余资产来偿还该财产的任何产权负担\n\n减免 减少立遗嘱人偿清债务后剩余资产无法满足的规定。首先减免剩余规定，然后是一般规定，然后是具体规定。男子（立遗嘱人）去世时留下遗嘱，并根据有效遗嘱将住所留给朋友（受益人）。然而，如果受益人先于遗嘱人去世（如此处所示），失效原则将导致设计失败。在这种情况下，受益人及其继承人都不会获得设计财产。相反，它将成为一般遗产的一部分，并根据遗嘱的其余部分进行分配。如果遗嘱中没有其他规定，财产将根据无遗嘱法进行分配。但由于该男子将剩余的遗产留给了慈善机构，因此遗产机构应该将住宅分配给慈善机构，而不是孩子。 （选项A）如果立遗嘱人在死亡时不拥有所设计的财产，则赎回原则将导致特定的设计失败。但由于该男子死后拥有该住宅，因此该朋友的设计不成立。 （选项C） 尽管有外在证据表明该男子的意图是为了让孩子受益，但口头证据规则禁止法院重写没有明显含糊之处的遗嘱。而且由于男方的遗嘱明确无误，因此这些证据不能用来支持孩子对住所的主张。 （选项D）如果该男子的遗嘱中包含了一项生存条件，指示如果该男子的朋友先于该男子去世，则该住宅将归该孩子所有，则该孩子将获得该住宅。但由于该男子的遗嘱中没有包含这样的条件，因此该住宅应该分配给慈善机构。\n\n教育目标：当受益人先于立遗嘱人去世时，失效原则会导致遗嘱失效。设计的财产将根据遗嘱中的其他设计进行分配，或者，如果没有其他设计，则根据无遗嘱法进行分配。\n\n参考文献\n上午 80 点。尤尔。 2d Wills § 1409 (2018)（失效原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当受益人先于立遗嘱人去世时，失效原则会导致遗嘱失效。设计的财产将根据遗嘱中的其他设计进行分配，或者，如果没有其他设计，则根据无遗嘱法进行分配。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果慈善机构和孩子都主张该住宅，那么遗产应该将住宅分配给谁？","rule":"当受益人先于立遗嘱人去世时，失效原则会导致遗嘱失效。设计的财产将根据遗嘱中的其他设计进行分配，或者，如果没有其他设计，则根据无遗嘱法进行分配。","analysis":"影响遗嘱转让的学说\n\n如果受益人先于遗嘱人去世，失效会导致设计失败\n\n赎回会因以下任一原因导致设计失败：\n\n消灭 – 在死亡时不属于立遗嘱人拥有的特别设计的财产（或被毁坏或根本改变） 满意 – 受益人在立遗嘱人的一生中收到设计的财产（或旨在满足设计的其他资产） 免除 允许特别设计的不动产的受益人使用遗产的剩余资产来偿还该财产的任何产权负担\n\n减免 减少立遗嘱人偿清债务后剩余资产无法满足的规定。首先减免剩余规定，然后是一般规定，然后是具体规定。男子（立遗嘱人）去世时留下遗嘱，并根据有效遗嘱将住所留给朋友（受益人）。然而，如果受益人先于遗嘱人去世（如此处所示），失效原则将导致设计失败。在这种情况下，受益人及其继承人都不会获得设计财产。相反，它将成为一般遗产的一部分，并根据遗嘱的其余部分进行分配。如果遗嘱中没有其他规定，财产将根据无遗嘱法进行分配。但由于该男子将剩余的遗产留给了慈善机构，因此遗产机构应该将住宅分配给慈善机构，而不是孩子。 （选项A）如果立遗嘱人在死亡时不拥有所设计的财产，则赎回原则将导致特定的设计失败。但由于该男子死后拥有该住宅，因此该朋友的设计不成立。 （选项C） 尽管有外在证据表明该男子的意图是为了让孩子受益，但口头证据规则禁止法院重写没有明显含糊之处的遗嘱。而且由于男方的遗嘱明确无误，因此这些证据不能用来支持孩子对住所的主张。 （选项D）如果该男子的遗嘱中包含了一项生存条件，指示如果该男子的朋友先于该男子去世，则该住宅将归该孩子所有，则该孩子将获得该住宅。但由于该男子的遗嘱中没有包含这样的条件，因此该住宅应该分配给慈善机构。\n\n教育目标：当受益人先于立遗嘱人去世时，失效原则会导致遗嘱失效。设计的财产将根据遗嘱中的其他设计进行分配，或者，如果没有其他设计，则根据无遗嘱法进行分配。\n\n参考文献\n上午 80 点。尤尔。 2d Wills § 1409 (2018)（失效原则）。","conclusion":"答案 B：慈善事业，因为朋友的计划失效了。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":25,"sourceQuestionPage":72,"sourceQuestionPages":[72],"sourceExplanationPages":[73,74],"sourceAnswerPages":[73,74],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-026","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A woman entered a hospital to undergo surgery and feared that she might not survive. She instructed her lawyer by telephone to prepare a deed conveying her property, a large tract of undeveloped land, as a gift to her nephew who lived in a distant state. Her instructions were followed, and, prior to her surgery, she executed a document in a form sufficient to constitute a deed of conveyance. The deed was recorded by the lawyer promptly and properly as she instructed him to do. The recorded deed was returned to the lawyer by the land record office. The woman, in fact, recovered from her surgery and the lawyer returned the recorded deed to her. Before the woman or the lawyer thought to inform the nephew of the conveyance, the nephew was killed in an auto accident. The nephew's will left all of his estate to a satanic religious cult. The woman was very upset at the prospect of the cult acquiring the property. The local taxing authority assessed the next real property tax bill on the property to the nephew's estate. The woman brought an appropriate action against the nephew's estate and the cult to set aside the conveyance to the nephew. If the woman loses, what will be the likely reason?","choices":{"A":"Delivery of the deed is presumed from the recording of the deed.","B":"Disappointing the nephew's devisee would violate the religious freedom provisions of the First Amendment to the Constitution.","C":"The gift of the property was inter vivos rather than causa mortis.","D":"The showing of the nephew's estate as the owner of the property on the tax rolls supplied what otherwise would be a missing essential element for a valid conveyance."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A deed is a document that transfers ownership of real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee) once it has been: delivered – presumed when the deed (1) has been recorded—ie, filed in the official land records—or (2) is in the grantee's physical possession and accepted – presumed when the transfer benefits the grantee. Once these requirements are met, the grantee owns the property and the transfer cannot be canceled—even if the land record office later returns the deed to the grantor. Here, delivery of the deed to the nephew is presumed since the woman's lawyer (agent) recorded it as instructed. And though the nephew never learned of the deed, his acceptance is presumed since the conveyance benefited him. As a result, ownership of the property transferred to the nephew and was unaffected by the land record office's return of the recorded deed. Therefore, the woman will likely lose her action to set aside the conveyance. (Choice B) The religious freedom provisions of the First Amendment protect persons from government action—not private individuals (as seen here). Therefore, disappointing the satanic cult by setting aside the conveyance would not violate protected religious freedoms—even if the woman had asked the court to do so because she disapproved of satanism. (Choice C) An inter vivos gift is an irrevocable gift of land or personal property given during the grantor's life. A causa mortis gift is a revocable gift of personal property given in expectations of the grantor's imminent death. Therefore, though the woman gifted the land when she feared that she might not survive surgery, that gift could only be inter vivos. And since that inter vivos gift only became irrevocable when it was delivered and accepted, the woman will lose because the deed was delivered (and presumably accepted) when it was recorded. (Choice D) A conveyance by deed is valid once there is delivery and acceptance. Whether the nephew's estate appeared as the owner of the property on the tax rolls is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: A deed transfers ownership of real property once it is delivered (presumed when it has been recorded) and accepted (presumed when the transfer benefits the grantee). After this occurs, the transfer is irrevocable.","educationalObjective":"A deed transfers ownership of real property once it is delivered (presumed when it has been recorded) and accepted (presumed when the transfer benefits the grantee). After this occurs, the transfer is irrevocable.","references":"","rule":"A deed transfers ownership of real property once it is delivered (presumed when it has been recorded) and accepted (presumed when the transfer benefits the grantee). After this occurs, the transfer is irrevocable.","irac":{"issue":"If the woman loses, what will be the likely reason?","rule":"A deed transfers ownership of real property once it is delivered (presumed when it has been recorded) and accepted (presumed when the transfer benefits the grantee). After this occurs, the transfer is irrevocable.","analysis":"A deed is a document that transfers ownership of real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee) once it has been: delivered – presumed when the deed (1) has been recorded—ie, filed in the official land records—or (2) is in the grantee's physical possession and accepted – presumed when the transfer benefits the grantee. Once these requirements are met, the grantee owns the property and the transfer cannot be canceled—even if the land record office later returns the deed to the grantor. Here, delivery of the deed to the nephew is presumed since the woman's lawyer (agent) recorded it as instructed. And though the nephew never learned of the deed, his acceptance is presumed since the conveyance benefited him. As a result, ownership of the property transferred to the nephew and was unaffected by the land record office's return of the recorded deed. Therefore, the woman will likely lose her action to set aside the conveyance. (Choice B) The religious freedom provisions of the First Amendment protect persons from government action—not private individuals (as seen here). Therefore, disappointing the satanic cult by setting aside the conveyance would not violate protected religious freedoms—even if the woman had asked the court to do so because she disapproved of satanism. (Choice C) An inter vivos gift is an irrevocable gift of land or personal property given during the grantor's life. A causa mortis gift is a revocable gift of personal property given in expectations of the grantor's imminent death. Therefore, though the woman gifted the land when she feared that she might not survive surgery, that gift could only be inter vivos. And since that inter vivos gift only became irrevocable when it was delivered and accepted, the woman will lose because the deed was delivered (and presumably accepted) when it was recorded. (Choice D) A conveyance by deed is valid once there is delivery and acceptance. Whether the nephew's estate appeared as the owner of the property on the tax rolls is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer A: Delivery of the deed is presumed from the recording of the deed."},"questionZh":"一名妇女进入医院接受手术，担心自己可能无法生存。她通过电话指示律师准备一份契约，将她的财产（一大片未开发的土地）转让给她住在遥远州的侄子。她遵循了她的指示，并且在手术之前，她签署了一份足以构成产权转让契约的文件。律师按照她的指示及时、正确地记录了该行为。土地记录办公室将记录的契约退还给律师。事实上，这名妇女已经从手术中康复，律师将记录的契约归还给她。在这位女士或律师想要通知侄子有关房产转让的信息之前，侄子在一场车祸中丧生。侄子的遗嘱将他的全部财产留给了一个邪恶的宗教邪教。这位女士对邪教收购该财产的前景感到非常不安。当地税务机关对侄子的遗产评估了下一份房产税。该女子对侄子的遗产和邪教提起了适当的诉讼，取消了给侄子的财产转让。如果女方输了，可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"契约的交付是根据契约的记录推定的。","B":"让侄子的受托人失望将违反宪法第一修正案的宗教自由条款。","C":"财产的赠与是生前的而不是死后的。","D":"在税单上将侄子的遗产显示为财产所有者，这提供了有效转让所缺少的基本要素。"},"explanationZh":"契约是一份将不动产所有权从所有者（授予人）转移至另一人（受让人）的文件：交付——在契约 (1) 已记录（即在官方土地记录中归档）时推定；或 (2) 为受让人实际占有并被接受——在转让使受让人受益时推定。一旦满足这些要求，受让人就拥有该财产，并且转让不能取消——即使土地记录办公室后来将契据退还给授予人。在此，由于该妇女的律师（代理人）按照指示记录了该契约，因此推定该契约已交付给该侄子。尽管侄子从未听说过该契约，但由于该转让对他有利，因此可以推定他接受了该契约。结果，财产的所有权转移给了侄子，并且不受土地记录办公室归还记录契约的影响。因此，该女子很可能会失去撤销房产权的诉讼权。 （选项 B）第一修正案的宗教自由条款保护个人免受政府行为的影响，而不是个人（如此处所示）。因此，通过搁置财产转让而让撒旦邪教失望，并不会侵犯受保护的宗教自由——即使该妇女因不赞成撒旦教而要求法院这样做。 （选项 C） 生前赠与是授予人生前赠与的不可撤销的土地或动产赠与。死因赠与是在赠与人即将死亡的情况下赠予的可撤销的个人财产赠与。因此，虽然女人在担心自己无法活过手术的情况下赠予了土地，但那份礼物只能是生前的。由于该生前礼物只有在交付和接受时才变得不可撤销，因此该妇女将遭受损失，因为该契约是在记录时交付（并且可能被接受）的。 （选项D）一旦交付并接受，契约转让即有效。侄子的遗产是否作为财产所有者出现在税单上并不重要。\n\n教育目标：一旦交付（假定已记录）并接受（假定转让使受让人受益），契约就转移了不动产的所有权。发生此情况后，转让不可撤销。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一旦交付（在已记录的情况下推定）并被接受（在转让使受让人受益时推定），契约就转移了不动产的所有权。发生此情况后，转让不可撤销。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果女方输了，可能的原因是什么？","rule":"一旦交付（在已记录的情况下推定）并被接受（在转让使受让人受益时推定），契约就转移了不动产的所有权。发生此情况后，转让不可撤销。","analysis":"契约是一份将不动产所有权从所有者（授予人）转移至另一人（受让人）的文件：交付——在契约 (1) 已记录（即在官方土地记录中归档）时推定；或 (2) 为受让人实际占有并被接受——在转让使受让人受益时推定。一旦满足这些要求，受让人就拥有该财产，并且转让不能取消——即使土地记录办公室后来将契据退还给授予人。在此，由于该妇女的律师（代理人）按照指示记录了该契约，因此推定该契约已交付给该侄子。尽管侄子从未听说过该契约，但由于该转让对他有利，因此可以推定他接受了该契约。结果，财产的所有权转移给了侄子，并且不受土地记录办公室归还记录契约的影响。因此，该女子很可能会失去撤销房产权的诉讼权。 （选项 B）第一修正案的宗教自由条款保护个人免受政府行为的影响，而不是个人（如此处所示）。因此，通过搁置财产转让而让撒旦邪教失望，并不会侵犯受保护的宗教自由——即使该妇女因不赞成撒旦教而要求法院这样做。 （选项 C） 生前赠与是授予人生前赠与的不可撤销的土地或动产赠与。死因赠与是在赠与人即将死亡的情况下赠予的可撤销的个人财产赠与。因此，虽然女人在担心自己无法活过手术的情况下赠予了土地，但那份礼物只能是生前的。由于该生前礼物只有在交付和接受时才变得不可撤销，因此该妇女将遭受损失，因为该契约是在记录时交付（并且可能被接受）的。 （选项D）一旦交付并接受，契约转让即有效。侄子的遗产是否作为财产所有者出现在税单上并不重要。\n\n教育目标：一旦交付（假定已记录）并接受（假定转让使受让人受益），契约就转移了不动产的所有权。发生此情况后，转让不可撤销。","conclusion":"答案 A：契约的交付是根据契约的记录推定的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-a945cb10313e963a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":26,"sourceQuestionPage":75,"sourceQuestionPages":[75],"sourceExplanationPages":[76,77],"sourceAnswerPages":[76,77],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-027","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman acquired title to a four-acre lot. Several years later, she executed a mortgage on the lot to a bank to secure repayment of a $100,000 loan. Subsequently, the woman executed a mortgage on the same four-acre lot to a finance company to secure repayment of a $50,000 loan. Both mortgages were promptly recorded. The woman recently defaulted on both loans. The bank promptly initiated foreclosure proceedings and sent proper notice to all necessary parties. The current fair market value of the four-acre lot is $250,000. The finance company has filed a timely motion in the foreclosure proceeding asking the court to require the bank to first foreclose on two of the four acres in the four-acre lot. The bank opposes this motion and insists that it has the right to subject the entire four-acre lot to the foreclosure sale. Will the court grant the finance company's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the bank holds a purchase-money mortgage.","B":"No, because the entire four-acre lot is subject to the bank's senior mortgage.","C":"Yes, because a pro rata foreclosure of the lot will not prejudice the rights of the bank.","D":"Yes, because of the \"two funds\" rule of marshalling."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The priority of mortgages and other liens in the foreclosure process is generally determined by their recording dates. However, a different rule applies to purchase- money mortgages (PMMs)—ie, mortgages granted to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property. PMMs have super-priority over all other mortgages or liens, whether or not recorded. Here, both the bank and the finance company have mortgages on the entire four-acre lot. The woman already owned the lot when she executed the mortgages, so neither mortgage is a PMM (Choice A). Therefore, the order of recording determines priority. And since the bank received its mortgage first and promptly recorded it, the bank has the senior mortgage and is entitled to foreclose on the entire four-acre lot. (Choice C) Mortgagees are entitled to foreclose on the entire property subject to the mortgage unless otherwise provided by law. Therefore, the bank has no obligation to foreclose on a smaller two-acre parcel. Additionally, such a pro rata foreclosure could prejudice the bank's rights since there is no guarantee that two acres would sell for an amount sufficient to satisfy the bank's interest. (Choice D) The \"two funds\" rule of marshalling applies when a senior creditor has two funds available to satisfy a debt (eg, a mortgage on multiple properties), and a junior creditor also has an interest in one of those funds. It requires the senior creditor to first look for satisfaction from the fund that is not shared so as to preserve the junior creditor's interest in the shared fund. But here, both mortgages are secured by a single fund—the four-acre lot.\n\nEducational objective: Priority of mortgages and other liens is generally based on the order in which they were recorded. Purchase-money mortgages, however, have super-priority over all other mortgages or liens, whether or not recorded.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (priority of purchase-money mortgages).","educationalObjective":"Priority of mortgages and other liens is generally based on the order in which they were recorded. Purchase-money mortgages, however, have super-priority over all other mortgages or liens, whether or not recorded.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (priority of purchase-money mortgages).","rule":"Priority of mortgages and other liens is generally based on the order in which they were recorded. Purchase-money mortgages, however, have super-priority over all other mortgages or liens, whether or not recorded.","irac":{"issue":"Will the court grant the finance company's motion?","rule":"Priority of mortgages and other liens is generally based on the order in which they were recorded. Purchase-money mortgages, however, have super-priority over all other mortgages or liens, whether or not recorded.","analysis":"The priority of mortgages and other liens in the foreclosure process is generally determined by their recording dates. However, a different rule applies to purchase- money mortgages (PMMs)—ie, mortgages granted to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property. PMMs have super-priority over all other mortgages or liens, whether or not recorded. Here, both the bank and the finance company have mortgages on the entire four-acre lot. The woman already owned the lot when she executed the mortgages, so neither mortgage is a PMM (Choice A). Therefore, the order of recording determines priority. And since the bank received its mortgage first and promptly recorded it, the bank has the senior mortgage and is entitled to foreclose on the entire four-acre lot. (Choice C) Mortgagees are entitled to foreclose on the entire property subject to the mortgage unless otherwise provided by law. Therefore, the bank has no obligation to foreclose on a smaller two-acre parcel. Additionally, such a pro rata foreclosure could prejudice the bank's rights since there is no guarantee that two acres would sell for an amount sufficient to satisfy the bank's interest. (Choice D) The \"two funds\" rule of marshalling applies when a senior creditor has two funds available to satisfy a debt (eg, a mortgage on multiple properties), and a junior creditor also has an interest in one of those funds. It requires the senior creditor to first look for satisfaction from the fund that is not shared so as to preserve the junior creditor's interest in the shared fund. But here, both mortgages are secured by a single fund—the four-acre lot.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the entire four-acre lot is subject to the bank's senior mortgage."},"questionZh":"一名妇女获得了一块四英亩土地的所有权。几年后，她将该地块抵押给一家银行，以偿还 10 万美元的贷款。随后，该女子将同一块四英亩土地抵押给一家金融公司，以确保偿还 5 万美元的贷款。两项抵押贷款都立即被记录。该女子最近拖欠了两笔贷款。该银行立即启动了止赎程序，并向所有必要方发出了适当的通知。该四英亩土地目前的公平市场价值为 250,000 美元。该金融公司已在取消抵押品赎回权诉讼中及时提出动议，要求法院要求银行首先取消该四英亩土地中四英亩土地中两英亩的抵押品赎回权。该银行反对这项动议，并坚持认为它有权将整个四英亩土地进行止赎出售。法院会批准财务公司的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为银行持有购房款抵押贷款。","B":"不可以，因为整个四英亩地块都须缴纳银行的高级抵押贷款。","C":"是的，因为按比例取消抵押品赎回权不会损害银行的权利。","D":"是的，因为“两基金”的编组规则。"},"explanationZh":"取消抵押品赎回权过程中抵押贷款和其他留置权的优先顺序通常由其记录日期决定。然而，不同的规则适用于购房抵押贷款（PMM），即为确保抵押财产的购买价格而发放的抵押贷款。 PMM 比所有其他抵押贷款或留置权拥有超级优先权，无论是否记录。在这里，银行和财务公司都对整个四英亩土地提供抵押贷款。该女士在执行抵押贷款时已经拥有该土地，因此两种抵押贷款都不是 PMM（选择 A）。因此，记录的顺序决定了优先级。由于银行首先收到抵押贷款并及时记录，因此银行拥有优先抵押贷款，并有权取消整个四英亩土地的赎回权。 （选择C） 除非法律另有规定，抵押权人有权取消抵押贷款下的全部财产的赎回权。因此，银行没有义务取消较小的两英亩地块的赎回权。此外，这种按比例取消抵押品赎回权可能会损害银行的权利，因为不能保证两英亩土地的售价足以满足银行的利息。 （选项D） 当高级债权人有两笔资金可用于偿还债务（例如，多处房产的抵押贷款），并且初级债权人也对其中一项资金拥有权益时，适用“两笔资金”组合规则。它要求高级债权人首先从未共享的基金中寻求满足，以维护初级债权人在共享基金中的利益。但在这里，两项抵押贷款均由单一基金（四英亩土地）担保。\n\n教育目标：抵押贷款和其他留置权的优先顺序通常基于其记录的顺序。然而，购房款抵押贷款比所有其他抵押贷款或留置权具有超级优先权，无论是否记录。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（购房款抵押贷款的优先权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"抵押贷款和其他留置权的优先权通常基于其记录的顺序。然而，购房款抵押贷款比所有其他抵押贷款或留置权具有超级优先权，无论是否记录。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院会批准财务公司的动议吗？","rule":"抵押贷款和其他留置权的优先权通常基于其记录的顺序。然而，购房款抵押贷款比所有其他抵押贷款或留置权具有超级优先权，无论是否记录。","analysis":"取消抵押品赎回权过程中抵押贷款和其他留置权的优先顺序通常由其记录日期决定。然而，不同的规则适用于购房抵押贷款（PMM），即为确保抵押财产的购买价格而发放的抵押贷款。 PMM 比所有其他抵押贷款或留置权拥有超级优先权，无论是否记录。在这里，银行和财务公司都对整个四英亩土地提供抵押贷款。该女士在执行抵押贷款时已经拥有该土地，因此两种抵押贷款都不是 PMM（选择 A）。因此，记录的顺序决定了优先级。由于银行首先收到抵押贷款并及时记录，因此银行拥有优先抵押贷款，并有权取消整个四英亩土地的赎回权。 （选择C） 除非法律另有规定，抵押权人有权取消抵押贷款下的全部财产的赎回权。因此，银行没有义务取消较小的两英亩地块的赎回权。此外，这种按比例取消抵押品赎回权可能会损害银行的权利，因为不能保证两英亩土地的售价足以满足银行的利息。 （选项D） 当高级债权人有两笔资金可用于偿还债务（例如，多处房产的抵押贷款），并且初级债权人也对其中一项资金拥有权益时，适用“两笔资金”组合规则。它要求高级债权人首先从未共享的基金中寻求满足，以维护初级债权人在共享基金中的利益。但在这里，两项抵押贷款均由单一基金（四英亩土地）担保。\n\n教育目标：抵押贷款和其他留置权的优先顺序通常基于其记录的顺序。然而，购房款抵押贷款比所有其他抵押贷款或留置权具有超级优先权，无论是否记录。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（购房款抵押贷款的优先权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为整个四英亩地块都须缴纳银行的高级抵押贷款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-614928657ec24590"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":27,"sourceQuestionPage":78,"sourceQuestionPages":[78],"sourceExplanationPages":[79,80,81],"sourceAnswerPages":[79,80,81],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-028","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"An uncle owned a valuable tract of land. The uncle executed a document in the form of a warranty deed of land, which was regular in all respects except that the only language designating the grantees in each of the granting and habendum clauses was: \"The leaders of all the Protestant churches in County A.\" The instrument was acknowledged as required by statute and promptly and properly recorded. The uncle told his lawyer, but no one else, that he had made the conveyance as he did because he abhorred sectarianism in the Protestant movement and because he thought that the leaders would devote the asset to lessening sectarianism. The uncle died suddenly and unexpectedly a week later, leaving a will that bequeathed and devised his entire estate to his niece. After probate of the will became final and the administration on the uncle's estate was closed, the niece instituted an appropriate action to quiet title to the land and properly served as defendant each Protestant church situated in the county. The only evidence introduced consisted of the chain of title under which the uncle held, the probated will, the recorded deed, the fact that no person knew about the deed except the uncle and his lawyer, and the conversation the uncle had with his lawyer described above. In the action to quiet title, who will prevail and why?","choices":{"A":"The defendants, because a deed is prima facie valid until rebutted.","B":"The defendants, because recording established prima facie delivery until rebutted.","C":"The niece, because the state of the evidence would not support a finding of delivery of the deed.","D":"The niece, because there is inadequate identification of grantees in the deed."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Deed requirements\n\nRequirements Valid Invalid\n\nWritten & signed Any written instrument Oral (except permissible gift) by grantor signed by grantor Signed only by grantee Identifies grantor Separate grantor & grantee Ambiguous/unidentifiable grantee(s) & grantee (except concurrent estates) Nonexistent grantee Identifiable by name or description Identifies land Identifiable by reasonable Ambiguous description (but extrinsic certainty—eg: evidence admissible) Government survey Undefined part of larger parcel Metes & bounds Street, lot, house number Includes words of \"Convey\" \"Lease\" transfer \"Transfer\" \"Rent\" \"Grant\" \"License\" \"Sell\" A deed is a legal instrument that transfers ownership of real property from the grantor (old owner) to the grantee (new owner). To be valid, a deed must: be in writing and signed by the grantor unambiguously identify the grantor and grantee unambiguously describe the land and include words of transfer. When a deed transfers ownership to a group of grantees, the identities of the group members must be ascertainable with reasonable certainty—eg, \"the present members of the United States Congress.\" Extrinsic evidence can be admitted to clarify ambiguities. But if the extrinsic evidence is also unclear, a court will not speculate as to the grantor's intent and the deed will be deemed void (ie, of no legal effect). Here, the uncle attempted to deed his land to \"the leaders of all the Protestant churches in County A.\" But that description was ambiguous because there is no way to identify the specific individuals belonging to that group. And though the uncle's conversation with his lawyer (extrinsic evidence) was introduced, it did not resolve this ambiguity. Therefore, the inadequate identification of the grantees rendered the uncle's deed void. And since the deed did not convey title to the defendants, the niece will prevail in her action to quiet title.\n\n(Choice A) A deed is not prima facie valid if it does not meet the above requirements. And since the deed here cannot satisfy all these requirements (due to the ambiguous description of the grantees), it is invalid. (Choices B & C) A deed is valid if it meets the above requirements, and the transfer of the described land is effective when the deed is delivered and accepted. While recording is prima facie evidence that the deed was delivered to the grantee, it does not establish the deed's validity.\n\nEducational objective: A valid deed (1) is in writing and signed by the grantor, (2) unambiguously identifies the grantor and grantee, (3) unambiguously describes the land, and (4) includes words of transfer. The identity of the parties and the land must be ascertainable with reasonable certainty, but extrinsic evidence can be used to resolve ambiguities.","educationalObjective":"A valid deed (1) is in writing and signed by the grantor, (2) unambiguously identifies the grantor and grantee, (3) unambiguously describes the land, and (4) includes words of transfer. The identity of the parties and the land must be ascertainable with reasonable certainty, but extrinsic evidence can be used to resolve ambiguities.","references":"","rule":"A valid deed (1) is in writing and signed by the grantor, (2) unambiguously identifies the grantor and grantee, (3) unambiguously describes the land, and (4) includes words of transfer. The identity of the parties and the land must be ascertainable with reasonable certainty, but extrinsic evidence can be used to resolve ambiguities.","irac":{"issue":"In the action to quiet title, who will prevail and why?","rule":"A valid deed (1) is in writing and signed by the grantor, (2) unambiguously identifies the grantor and grantee, (3) unambiguously describes the land, and (4) includes words of transfer. The identity of the parties and the land must be ascertainable with reasonable certainty, but extrinsic evidence can be used to resolve ambiguities.","analysis":"Deed requirements\n\nRequirements Valid Invalid\n\nWritten & signed Any written instrument Oral (except permissible gift) by grantor signed by grantor Signed only by grantee Identifies grantor Separate grantor & grantee Ambiguous/unidentifiable grantee(s) & grantee (except concurrent estates) Nonexistent grantee Identifiable by name or description Identifies land Identifiable by reasonable Ambiguous description (but extrinsic certainty—eg: evidence admissible) Government survey Undefined part of larger parcel Metes & bounds Street, lot, house number Includes words of \"Convey\" \"Lease\" transfer \"Transfer\" \"Rent\" \"Grant\" \"License\" \"Sell\" A deed is a legal instrument that transfers ownership of real property from the grantor (old owner) to the grantee (new owner). To be valid, a deed must: be in writing and signed by the grantor unambiguously identify the grantor and grantee unambiguously describe the land and include words of transfer. When a deed transfers ownership to a group of grantees, the identities of the group members must be ascertainable with reasonable certainty—eg, \"the present members of the United States Congress.\" Extrinsic evidence can be admitted to clarify ambiguities. But if the extrinsic evidence is also unclear, a court will not speculate as to the grantor's intent and the deed will be deemed void (ie, of no legal effect). Here, the uncle attempted to deed his land to \"the leaders of all the Protestant churches in County A.\" But that description was ambiguous because there is no way to identify the specific individuals belonging to that group. And though the uncle's conversation with his lawyer (extrinsic evidence) was introduced, it did not resolve this ambiguity. Therefore, the inadequate identification of the grantees rendered the uncle's deed void. And since the deed did not convey title to the defendants, the niece will prevail in her action to quiet title.\n\n(Choice A) A deed is not prima facie valid if it does not meet the above requirements. And since the deed here cannot satisfy all these requirements (due to the ambiguous description of the grantees), it is invalid. (Choices B & C) A deed is valid if it meets the above requirements, and the transfer of the described land is effective when the deed is delivered and accepted. While recording is prima facie evidence that the deed was delivered to the grantee, it does not establish the deed's validity.","conclusion":"Answer D: The niece, because there is inadequate identification of grantees in the deed."},"questionZh":"一位叔叔拥有一块宝贵的土地。叔叔以土地保证契约的形式签署了一份文件，该文件在各方面都很正常，只是在每项授予和责任条款中指定受赠者的唯一语言是：“A县所有新教教堂的领导人。”该文书已按法规要求得到认可，并被及时、正确地记录。叔叔告诉他的律师，但没有告诉其他人，他之所以做出转让，是因为他憎恶新教运动中的宗派主义，并且因为他认为领导人会将这笔资产用于减少宗派主义。一周后，叔叔突然出人意料地去世了，留下了一份遗嘱，将他的全部财产遗赠给了他的侄女。在遗嘱认证成为最终结果并且叔叔遗产的管理结束后，侄女采取了适当的行动来平息土地所有权，并适当地担任县内每个新教教堂的被告。提供的唯一证据包括叔叔持有的所有权链、遗嘱认证、记录的契约、除了叔叔和他的律师之外没有人知道该契约的事实，以及上述叔叔与律师的谈话。在这场平息争夺战中，谁会获胜，为什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"被告，因为契约在被反驳之前表面上是有效的。","B":"被告认为，录音已初步确定交付，直至反驳。","C":"侄女，因为证据状况不支持交付契约的结论。","D":"侄女，因为契约中的受赠人身份不充分。"},"explanationZh":"契约要求\n\n要求 有效 无效\n\n书面和签署 任何书面文件，由授予人口头签署（允许的赠与除外），由授予人签署 仅由受让人签署 确定授予人的身份 单独的授予人和受让人 模糊/无法识别的受让人和受让人（共同财产除外） 不存在的受让人 可通过姓名或描述识别 识别土地 可通过合理的描述识别 不明确（但外在确定性 — 例如：证据可接受） 政府调查 大地块的未定义部分 边界和边界街道、地段、门牌号 包括“转让”“租赁”转让“转让”“出租”“授予”“许可”“出售”等字样 契约是将不动产所有权从授予人（旧所有者）转移到受让人（新所有者）的法律文书。为了有效，契约必须： 以书面形式并由授予人签署 明确标识授予人和受让人 明确描述土地并包括转让字词。当契约将所有权转让给一组受让人时，必须以合理的确定性确定该组成员的身份，例如“美国国会现任议员”。可以接受外部证据来澄清歧义。但如果外在证据也不清楚，法院将不会推测设保人的意图，并且该契约将被视为无效（即不具有法律效力）。在这里，叔叔试图将他的土地转让给“A县所有新教教堂的领导人”。但这种描述并不明确，因为无法识别属于该群体的具体个人。尽管介绍了叔叔与律师的谈话（外在证据），但这并没有解决这种歧义。因此，由于受赠人身份不充分，叔叔的契约无效。由于该契约并未将所有权转让给被告，因此侄女将在其平息所有权诉讼中获胜。\n\n（选择A）如果不满足上述要求，契约表面上无效。由于这里的契约不能满足所有这些要求（由于受赠人的描述不明确），因此它是无效的。 （选项B和C）符合上述要求的契约有效，并且所述土地的转让在契约交付并接受时生效。虽然记录是契约已交付给受让人的表面证据，但它并不能确定契约的有效性。\n\n教育目标：有效契约 (1) 为书面形式并由授予人签署，(2) 明确标识授予人和受让人，(3) 明确描述土地，(4) 包括转让字词。当事人和土地的身份必须能够以合理的确定性确定，但可以使用外在证据来解决歧义。","educationalObjectiveZh":"有效契约 (1) 为书面形式并由授予人签署，(2) 明确标识授予人和受让人，(3) 明确描述土地，以及 (4) 包括转让字词。当事人和土地的身份必须能够以合理的确定性确定，但可以使用外在证据来解决歧义。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这场平息争夺战中，谁会获胜，为什么？","rule":"有效契约 (1) 为书面形式并由授予人签署，(2) 明确标识授予人和受让人，(3) 明确描述土地，以及 (4) 包括转让字词。当事人和土地的身份必须能够以合理的确定性确定，但可以使用外在证据来解决歧义。","analysis":"契约要求\n\n要求 有效 无效\n\n书面和签署 任何书面文件，由授予人口头签署（允许的赠与除外），由授予人签署 仅由受让人签署 确定授予人的身份 单独的授予人和受让人 模糊/无法识别的受让人和受让人（共同财产除外） 不存在的受让人 可通过姓名或描述识别 识别土地 可通过合理的描述识别 不明确（但外在确定性 — 例如：证据可接受） 政府调查 大地块的未定义部分 边界和边界街道、地段、门牌号 包括“转让”“租赁”转让“转让”“出租”“授予”“许可”“出售”等字样 契约是将不动产所有权从授予人（旧所有者）转移到受让人（新所有者）的法律文书。为了有效，契约必须： 以书面形式并由授予人签署 明确标识授予人和受让人 明确描述土地并包括转让字词。当契约将所有权转让给一组受让人时，必须以合理的确定性确定该组成员的身份，例如“美国国会现任议员”。可以接受外部证据来澄清歧义。但如果外在证据也不清楚，法院将不会推测设保人的意图，并且该契约将被视为无效（即不具有法律效力）。在这里，叔叔试图将他的土地转让给“A县所有新教教堂的领导人”。但这种描述并不明确，因为无法识别属于该群体的具体个人。尽管介绍了叔叔与律师的谈话（外在证据），但这并没有解决这种歧义。因此，由于受赠人身份不充分，叔叔的契约无效。由于该契约并未将所有权转让给被告，因此侄女将在其平息所有权诉讼中获胜。\n\n（选择A）如果不满足上述要求，契约表面上无效。由于这里的契约不能满足所有这些要求（由于受赠人的描述不明确），因此它是无效的。 （选项B和C）符合上述要求的契约有效，并且所述土地的转让在契约交付并接受时生效。虽然记录是契约已交付给受让人的表面证据，但它并不能确定契约的有效性。\n\n教育目标：有效契约 (1) 为书面形式并由授予人签署，(2) 明确标识授予人和受让人，(3) 明确描述土地，(4) 包括转让字词。当事人和土地的身份必须能够以合理的确定性确定，但可以使用外在证据来解决歧义。","conclusion":"答案 D：侄女，因为契约中的受赠人身份不充分。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"chain of title","ipa":"/tʃˈeɪn ˈəv tˈaɪtəl/","syllables":"chain of ti·tle","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"产权链：从共同来源至现权利人的连续登记转让记录","contextualMeaning":"产权链：从共同来源至现权利人的连续登记转让记录","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The only evidence introduced consisted of the chain of title under which the uncle held, the probated will, the recorded deed, the fact that no person knew about the deed except the uncle and his lawyer, and the conversation the uncle had with his lawyer described above.","synonyms":["record title chain"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":28,"sourceQuestionPage":82,"sourceQuestionPages":[82],"sourceExplanationPages":[83,84],"sourceAnswerPages":[83,84],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-029","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A businessman owned a hotel, subject to a mortgage securing a debt he owed to a bank. The businessman later acquired a nearby parking garage, financing part of the purchase price with a loan from a financing company, secured by a mortgage on the parking garage. Two years thereafter, the businessman defaulted on the loan owed to the bank, which caused the full amount of that loan to become immediately due and payable. The bank decided not to foreclose the mortgage on the hotel at that time, but instead properly sued for the full amount of the defaulted loan. The bank obtained and properly filed a judgment for that amount. A statute of the jurisdiction provides: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for 10 years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" There is no other applicable statute, except the statute providing for judicial foreclosure of mortgages, which places no restrictions on deficiency judgments. Shortly thereafter, the bank brought an appropriate action for judicial foreclosure of its mortgage on the hotel and of its judgment lien on the parking garage. The financing company was joined as a party defendant and appropriately sued for foreclosure of its mortgage on the parking garage, which was also in default. All procedures were properly followed, and the confirmed foreclosure sales resulted in the following: The net proceeds of the sale of the hotel to a third party were $200,000 less than the bank's mortgage balance. The net proceeds of the sale of the parking garage to a fourth party were $200,000 more than the financing company's mortgage balance. How should the $200,000 surplus arising from the sale of the parking garage be distributed?","choices":{"A":"It should be paid to the bank.","B":"It should be paid to the businessman.","C":"It should be paid to the financing company.","D":"It should be split equally between the bank and the financing company."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Proceeds from a foreclosure sale are typically distributed in the following order of priority: Expenses from the sale (eg, attorneys' fees, court costs) Mortgage being foreclosed Junior liens, in order of lien priority Debtor, if any surplus remains Here, the financing company had the senior lien on the garage since its mortgage was placed on the property first. The bank later acquired a judgment lien on all the businessman's property—including a junior lien on the garage—when the businessman defaulted on the bank's loan. And since the foreclosure sale of the garage satisfied the financing company's entire mortgage and resulted in a $200,000 surplus, the bank (as the junior lienholder) should receive the surplus to cover the balance still owed for its mortgage on the hotel (Choices B, C & D).\n\nEducational objective: The proceeds from a foreclosure sale are distributed in the following order of priority: (1) the expenses from the foreclosure sale, (2) the mortgage being foreclosed, (3) junior liens in order of priority, and (4) the debtor.\n\nReferences\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019) (distribution of foreclosure sale proceeds).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (effect of priority on distribution of foreclosure surplus).","educationalObjective":"The proceeds from a foreclosure sale are distributed in the following order of priority: (1) the expenses from the foreclosure sale, (2) the mortgage being foreclosed, (3) junior liens in order of priority, and (4) the debtor.","references":"55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019) (distribution of foreclosure sale proceeds).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (effect of priority on distribution of foreclosure surplus).","rule":"The proceeds from a foreclosure sale are distributed in the following order of priority: (1) the expenses from the foreclosure sale, (2) the mortgage being foreclosed, (3) junior liens in order of priority, and (4) the debtor.","irac":{"issue":"How should the $200,000 surplus arising from the sale of the parking garage be distributed?","rule":"The proceeds from a foreclosure sale are distributed in the following order of priority: (1) the expenses from the foreclosure sale, (2) the mortgage being foreclosed, (3) junior liens in order of priority, and (4) the debtor.","analysis":"Proceeds from a foreclosure sale are typically distributed in the following order of priority: Expenses from the sale (eg, attorneys' fees, court costs) Mortgage being foreclosed Junior liens, in order of lien priority Debtor, if any surplus remains Here, the financing company had the senior lien on the garage since its mortgage was placed on the property first. The bank later acquired a judgment lien on all the businessman's property—including a junior lien on the garage—when the businessman defaulted on the bank's loan. And since the foreclosure sale of the garage satisfied the financing company's entire mortgage and resulted in a $200,000 surplus, the bank (as the junior lienholder) should receive the surplus to cover the balance still owed for its mortgage on the hotel (Choices B, C & D).","conclusion":"Answer A: It should be paid to the bank."},"questionZh":"一位商人拥有一家酒店，并以抵押贷款担保他欠银行的债务。该商人后来购买了附近的一个停车场，通过一家融资公司的贷款融资了部分购买价格，并以停车场的抵押贷款为担保。两年后，该商人拖欠银行贷款，导致该笔贷款立即到期应付。银行当时决定不取消酒店抵押贷款的赎回权，而是适当起诉，要求全额偿还拖欠贷款。银行获得并妥善提交了该金额的判决。该管辖区的法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起 10 年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”除了规定司法取消抵押品赎回权的法规外，没有其他适用的法规，该法规对缺陷判决没有任何限制。此后不久，该银行提起了适当的诉讼，要求司法取消其酒店抵押贷款和停车场的判决留置权。该金融公司被列为当事人被告，并因取消其停车场抵押赎回权而被适当起诉，该停车场也已违约。所有程序均得到妥善执行，确认的止赎销售结果如下： 将酒店出售给第三方的净收益比银行的抵押贷款余额少 200,000 美元。将停车场出售给第四方的净收益比融资公司的抵押贷款余额多出 20 万美元。出售停车场的20万元盈余该如何分配？","choicesZh":{"A":"应该支付给银行。","B":"应该付给商人。","C":"应该支付给融资公司。","D":"它应该由银行和融资公司平均分配。"},"explanationZh":"取消抵押品赎回权销售的收益通常按以下优先顺序分配： 出售费用（例如律师费、法庭费用） 抵押贷款被取消抵押品赎回权 初级留置权，按留置权优先顺序排列 债务人（如果还有剩余） 这里，融资公司拥有车库的高级留置权，因为它的抵押首先放在房产上。当商人拖欠银行贷款时，银行后来获得了对该商人所有财产的判决留置权，包括车库的初级留置权。由于取消抵押品赎回权的车库出售满足了融资公司的全部抵押贷款并产生了 200,000 美元的盈余，因此银行（作为初级留置权人）应该收到盈余，以弥补酒店抵押贷款所欠的余额（选项 B、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标： 止赎出售的收益按以下优先顺序分配：(1) 止赎出售的费用，(2) 被止赎的抵押贷款，(3) 按优先顺序排列的初级留置权，以及 (4) 债务人。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019)（止赎出售收益的分配）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（优先权对止赎盈余分配的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"取消抵押品赎回权销售的收益按以下优先顺序分配：(1) 取消抵押品赎回权销售的费用，(2) 被取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款，(3) 按优先顺序排列的初级留置权，以及 (4) 债务人。","iracZh":{"issue":"出售停车场的20万元盈余该如何分配？","rule":"取消抵押品赎回权销售的收益按以下优先顺序分配：(1) 取消抵押品赎回权销售的费用，(2) 被取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款，(3) 按优先顺序排列的初级留置权，以及 (4) 债务人。","analysis":"取消抵押品赎回权销售的收益通常按以下优先顺序分配： 出售费用（例如律师费、法庭费用） 抵押贷款被取消抵押品赎回权 初级留置权，按留置权优先顺序排列 债务人（如果还有剩余） 这里，融资公司拥有车库的高级留置权，因为它的抵押首先放在房产上。当商人拖欠银行贷款时，银行后来获得了对该商人所有财产的判决留置权，包括车库的初级留置权。由于取消抵押品赎回权的车库出售满足了融资公司的全部抵押贷款并产生了 200,000 美元的盈余，因此银行（作为初级留置权人）应该收到盈余，以弥补酒店抵押贷款所欠的余额（选项 B、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标： 止赎出售的收益按以下优先顺序分配：(1) 止赎出售的费用，(2) 被止赎的抵押贷款，(3) 按优先顺序排列的初级留置权，以及 (4) 债务人。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019)（止赎出售收益的分配）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（优先权对止赎盈余分配的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 A：应该支付给银行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"appropriately","ipa":"/ʌpɹˈoʊpɹiɪtli/","syllables":"ap·pro·pri·ately","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 适当的, 恰如其分的, 恰当的, 合适的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“appropriately”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The financing company was joined as a party defendant and appropriately sued for foreclosure of its mortgage on the parking garage, which was also in default.","synonyms":["suitably","fittingly","befittingly","fitly"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-d28b331bf7e70ed0"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":29,"sourceQuestionPage":85,"sourceQuestionPages":[85],"sourceExplanationPages":[86,87],"sourceAnswerPages":[86,87],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-030","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"A woman owned a tract of land in fee simple. By warranty deed, she conveyed the land in fee simple to her brother for a recited consideration of \"$10 and other valuable consideration.\" The deed was promptly and properly recorded. One week later, the woman and her brother executed a written document that stated that the conveyance of the land was for the purpose of establishing a trust for the benefit of the woman's grandson. The brother expressly accepted the trust and signed the document with the woman. This written agreement was not authenticated to be eligible for recordation and there never was an attempt to record it. The brother entered into possession of the land and distributed the net income from the land to the grandson at appropriate intervals. Five years later, the brother conveyed the land in fee simple to an investor by warranty deed. The investor paid the fair market value of the land, had no knowledge of the written agreement between the woman and the brother, and entered into possession of the land. The grandson made demand upon the investor for distribution of income at the next usual time the brother would have distributed. The investor refused. The grandson brought an appropriate action against the investor for a decree requiring her to perform the trust the brother had theretofore recognized. Which party is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The grandson, because a successor in title to the trustee takes title subject to the grantor's trust.","B":"The grandson, because equitable interests are not subject to the recording act.","C":"The investor, because, as a bona fide purchaser, she took free of the trust encumbering the brother's title.","D":"The investor, because no trust was ever created since the woman had no title at the time of the purported creation."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Types of recording acts\n\nType Effect Statutory language\n\nRace Interest holder who records first will prevail \"First record\"\n\nNotice Subsequent BFP without notice of earlier property \"Without notice,\" \"In interest will prevail good faith\"\n\nRace- Subsequent BFP (1) without notice of earlier \"Without notice, who Notice property interest (2) who records first will prevail shall first record\"\n\nBFP = Bona fide purchaser.\n\nMany interests affecting property can be recorded, including trusts affecting land. When different persons own such interests, recording acts are used to evaluate priority of competing interests. Under most recording acts, a subsequent property interest has priority over an earlier interest if the subsequent interest-holder was a bona fide purchaser (BFP)—ie, a person who (1) paid value and (2) lacked notice of the prior interest. There are three types of notice: Actual – when a buyer has direct knowledge of a prior interest Constructive (ie, record) – when a document showing a prior interest was properly recorded in the land records and appears in the buyer's chain of title and Inquiry – when a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property by someone other than the seller). Here, the investor had no knowledge of the prior trust agreement when she purchased the land from the brother (no actual notice), and the brother's possession of the land gave her no reason to investigate further (no inquiry notice). And since the trust agreement was never recorded, the investor did not have constructive notice of the trust. Therefore, the investor was a BFP and took the land free of the trust. (Choice A) A successor in title to a trustee does not necessarily take title subject to the trust. And here, since the investor (successor in title) had no notice of the trust when she purchased the land from the brother (trustee), she is not bound by the trust. (Choice B) Equitable interests affecting property (eg, the grandson's trust) can be recorded and, when they are, give constructive notice to subsequent purchasers. (Choice D) Although the woman no longer had title to the land when the trust was created, the brother did. Therefore, the trust was valid and enforceable against the brother. But it is\n\nnot enforceable against the investor—a BFP who lacked notice of the trust when she purchased the land.\n\nEducational objective: Most recording acts protect bona fide purchasers who lacked notice—actual (direct knowledge), constructive (prior recording in chain of title), or inquiry (circumstances warrant investigation)—of a prior competing property interest.","educationalObjective":"Most recording acts protect bona fide purchasers who lacked notice—actual (direct knowledge), constructive (prior recording in chain of title), or inquiry (circumstances warrant investigation)—of a prior competing property interest.","references":"","rule":"Most recording acts protect bona fide purchasers who lacked notice—actual (direct knowledge), constructive (prior recording in chain of title), or inquiry (circumstances warrant investigation)—of a prior competing property interest.","irac":{"issue":"Which party is likely to prevail?","rule":"Most recording acts protect bona fide purchasers who lacked notice—actual (direct knowledge), constructive (prior recording in chain of title), or inquiry (circumstances warrant investigation)—of a prior competing property interest.","analysis":"Types of recording acts\n\nType Effect Statutory language\n\nRace Interest holder who records first will prevail \"First record\"\n\nNotice Subsequent BFP without notice of earlier property \"Without notice,\" \"In interest will prevail good faith\"\n\nRace- Subsequent BFP (1) without notice of earlier \"Without notice, who Notice property interest (2) who records first will prevail shall first record\"\n\nBFP = Bona fide purchaser.\n\nMany interests affecting property can be recorded, including trusts affecting land. When different persons own such interests, recording acts are used to evaluate priority of competing interests. Under most recording acts, a subsequent property interest has priority over an earlier interest if the subsequent interest-holder was a bona fide purchaser (BFP)—ie, a person who (1) paid value and (2) lacked notice of the prior interest. There are three types of notice: Actual – when a buyer has direct knowledge of a prior interest Constructive (ie, record) – when a document showing a prior interest was properly recorded in the land records and appears in the buyer's chain of title and Inquiry – when a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property by someone other than the seller). Here, the investor had no knowledge of the prior trust agreement when she purchased the land from the brother (no actual notice), and the brother's possession of the land gave her no reason to investigate further (no inquiry notice). And since the trust agreement was never recorded, the investor did not have constructive notice of the trust. Therefore, the investor was a BFP and took the land free of the trust. (Choice A) A successor in title to a trustee does not necessarily take title subject to the trust. And here, since the investor (successor in title) had no notice of the trust when she purchased the land from the brother (trustee), she is not bound by the trust. (Choice B) Equitable interests affecting property (eg, the grandson's trust) can be recorded and, when they are, give constructive notice to subsequent purchasers. (Choice D) Although the woman no longer had title to the land when the trust was created, the brother did. Therefore, the trust was valid and enforceable against the brother. But it is\n\nnot enforceable against the investor—a BFP who lacked notice of the trust when she purchased the land.","conclusion":"Answer C: The investor, because, as a bona fide purchaser, she took free of the trust encumbering the brother's title."},"questionZh":"一个女人拥有一块土地，费用很简单。根据保修契约，她以“10 美元和其他有价值的对价”为代价，以简单费用将土地转让给她的兄弟。该行为被及时、正确地记录下来。一周后，该妇女和她的兄弟签署了一份书面文件，表明转让土地的目的是为了该妇女的孙子的利益建立信托。兄弟明确接受了信任，与女子签署了文件。该书面协议未经认证符合备案资格，也从未尝试对其进行备案。兄弟取得了土地的所有权，并以适当的时间间隔将土地的净收入分配给孙子。五年后，兄弟通过保修契约将土地免费转让给投资者。投资者支付了土地的公平市场价值，在不知道该妇女与兄弟之间的书面协议的情况下，并占有了该土地。孙子要求投资者在兄弟下一次通常分配的时间分配收入。投资人拒绝了。孙子对投资者提起了适当的诉讼，要求她履行兄弟迄今为止所承认的信托。哪一方有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"孙子，因为受托人的所有权继承人取得的所有权受到设保人的信任。","B":"孙子，因为衡平法利益不受记录法约束。","C":"投资者，因为作为一个善意的购买者，她摆脱了阻碍兄弟所有权的信托。","D":"投资者，因为由于该妇女在所谓的创建时没有所有权，因此从未创建过任何信托。"},"explanationZh":"录音行为的类型\n\n类型 效果 法定语言\n\n最先记录的比赛兴趣持有人将优先“第一记录”\n\n通知后续 BFP，无需通知先前的财产“无需通知”，“利益优先，诚信为本”\n\n竞赛-后续BFP（1）无通知前“无通知，谁通知财产利益（2）谁先记录为准，以先记录为准”\n\nBFP = 善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）。\n\n许多影响财产的利益都可以记录，包括影响土地的信托。当不同的人拥有这些利益时，记录行为用于评估竞争利益的优先级。根据大多数记录法，如果随后的利益持有人是善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（BFP），即（1）支付了价值并且（2）没有通知先前的利益的人，则随后的财产利益优先于先前的利益。通知分为三种类型： 实际通知 – 当买方直接了解先前权益时 推定通知（即记录） – 当显示先前权益的文件已正确记录在土地记录中并出现在买方的所有权链和查询中时 – 当买方知道或应该知道会促使合理人士进行调查的情况时（例如，卖方以外的其他人明显使用该财产）。在这里，投资者在从兄弟那里购买土地时并不知道事先的信托协议（没有实际通知），而兄弟对土地的占有使她没有理由进一步调查（没有询问通知）。由于信托协议从未被记录，投资者并没有获得信托的推定通知。因此，投资者是 BFP，并从信托中获得了土地。 （选择 A）受托人的所有权继承人不一定取得受信托约束的所有权。在这里，由于投资者（所有权继承人）在从兄弟（受托人）那里购买土地时没有通知信托，因此她不受信托的约束。 （选择B）可以记录影响财产的衡平法利益（例如孙子的信托），并且在记录时向后续购买者发出推定通知。 （选项 D） 虽然信托成立时，该妇女不再拥有土地所有权，但兄弟拥有。因此，该信托对兄弟有效且可执行。但它是\n\n不适用于投资者——一名在购买土地时没有通知信托的 BFP。\n\n教育目标：大多数记录行为保护善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser），他们缺乏对先前竞争性财产权益的实际（直接了解）、推定（事先记录在所有权链中）或询问（需要调查的情况）的通知。","educationalObjectiveZh":"大多数记录行为保护善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser），他们缺乏对先前竞争财产权益的实际（直接了解）、推定（事先记录在所有权链中）或询问（需要调查的情况）的通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"哪一方有可能获胜？","rule":"大多数记录行为保护善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser），他们缺乏对先前竞争财产权益的实际（直接了解）、推定（事先记录在所有权链中）或询问（需要调查的情况）的通知。","analysis":"录音行为的类型\n\n类型 效果 法定语言\n\n最先记录的比赛兴趣持有人将优先“第一记录”\n\n通知后续 BFP，无需通知先前的财产“无需通知”，“利益优先，诚信为本”\n\n竞赛-后续BFP（1）无通知前“无通知，谁通知财产利益（2）谁先记录为准，以先记录为准”\n\nBFP = 善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）。\n\n许多影响财产的利益都可以记录，包括影响土地的信托。当不同的人拥有这些利益时，记录行为用于评估竞争利益的优先级。根据大多数记录法，如果随后的利益持有人是善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（BFP），即（1）支付了价值并且（2）没有通知先前的利益的人，则随后的财产利益优先于先前的利益。通知分为三种类型： 实际通知 – 当买方直接了解先前权益时 推定通知（即记录） – 当显示先前权益的文件已正确记录在土地记录中并出现在买方的所有权链和查询中时 – 当买方知道或应该知道会促使合理人士进行调查的情况时（例如，卖方以外的其他人明显使用该财产）。在这里，投资者在从兄弟那里购买土地时并不知道事先的信托协议（没有实际通知），而兄弟对土地的占有使她没有理由进一步调查（没有询问通知）。由于信托协议从未被记录，投资者并没有获得信托的推定通知。因此，投资者是 BFP，并从信托中获得了土地。 （选择 A）受托人的所有权继承人不一定取得受信托约束的所有权。在这里，由于投资者（所有权继承人）在从兄弟（受托人）那里购买土地时没有通知信托，因此她不受信托的约束。 （选择B）可以记录影响财产的衡平法利益（例如孙子的信托），并且在记录时向后续购买者发出推定通知。 （选项 D） 虽然信托成立时，该妇女不再拥有土地所有权，但兄弟拥有。因此，该信托对兄弟有效且可执行。但它是\n\n不适用于投资者——一名在购买土地时没有通知信托的 BFP。\n\n教育目标：大多数记录行为保护善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser），他们缺乏对先前竞争性财产权益的实际（直接了解）、推定（事先记录在所有权链中）或询问（需要调查的情况）的通知。","conclusion":"答案 C：投资者，因为作为一个善意的购买者，她摆脱了阻碍兄弟所有权的信托。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":30,"sourceQuestionPage":88,"sourceQuestionPages":[88],"sourceExplanationPages":[89,90],"sourceAnswerPages":[89,90],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-031","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man owned land in fee simple of record on January 10. On that day, a friend loaned the man $50,000 and the man mortgaged the land to her as security for the loan. The mortgage was recorded on January 18. The man conveyed the land to a buyer for valuable consideration on January 11. The friend did not know of this, nor did the buyer know of the mortgage to the friend, until both discovered the facts on January 23, the day on which the buyer recorded the man's deed. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No unrecorded conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value without notice, who shall first record.\" There is no provision for a period of grace and there is no other relevant statutory provision. The friend sued the buyer to establish that her mortgage was good against the land. For whom should the court decide?","choices":{"A":"The buyer, because he paid valuable consideration without notice before the friend recorded her mortgage.","B":"The buyer, because the friend's delay in recording means that she is estopped from asserting her priority in time.","C":"The friend, because after the mortgage to her, the man's deed to the buyer was necessarily subject to her mortgage.","D":"The friend, because the buyer did not record his deed before her mortgage was recorded."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Recording acts seek to promote certainty of title by creating a framework to determine the priority of competing property interests. In a race-notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest will have priority over an earlier property interest if the BFP: lacked notice of the earlier interest at the time the BFP acquired his/her interest and recorded his/her interest at any time before the earlier interest was recorded. Here, the buyer was unaware of the friend's prior mortgage when he purchased the land. But since he did not record his deed until after the friend had recorded her mortgage, the court should decide for the friend. (Choice A) Although the buyer paid valuable consideration without notice before the friend recorded her mortgage (ie, he was a BFP who lacked notice), he did not also record his deed before the friend recorded her mortgage. (Choice B) The friend's delay in recording does not estop (ie, preclude) her from asserting her priority in time since there is no deadline for recording. She only needed to record her mortgage first (ie, before the buyer recorded his deed) to prevail in this race-notice jurisdiction. And since she did so, the court should find for the friend. (Choice C) The man's deed to the buyer was not necessarily subject to the friend's mortgage. Instead, the friend will prevail because this jurisdiction has a race-notice statute and her mortgage was recorded first.\n\nEducational objective: In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier competing property interest if the BFP (1) lacked notice of the earlier interest and (2) recorded first.","educationalObjective":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier competing property interest if the BFP (1) lacked notice of the earlier interest and (2) recorded first.","references":"","rule":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier competing property interest if the BFP (1) lacked notice of the earlier interest and (2) recorded first.","irac":{"issue":"For whom should the court decide?","rule":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier competing property interest if the BFP (1) lacked notice of the earlier interest and (2) recorded first.","analysis":"Recording acts seek to promote certainty of title by creating a framework to determine the priority of competing property interests. In a race-notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest will have priority over an earlier property interest if the BFP: lacked notice of the earlier interest at the time the BFP acquired his/her interest and recorded his/her interest at any time before the earlier interest was recorded. Here, the buyer was unaware of the friend's prior mortgage when he purchased the land. But since he did not record his deed until after the friend had recorded her mortgage, the court should decide for the friend. (Choice A) Although the buyer paid valuable consideration without notice before the friend recorded her mortgage (ie, he was a BFP who lacked notice), he did not also record his deed before the friend recorded her mortgage. (Choice B) The friend's delay in recording does not estop (ie, preclude) her from asserting her priority in time since there is no deadline for recording. She only needed to record her mortgage first (ie, before the buyer recorded his deed) to prevail in this race-notice jurisdiction. And since she did so, the court should find for the friend. (Choice C) The man's deed to the buyer was not necessarily subject to the friend's mortgage. Instead, the friend will prevail because this jurisdiction has a race-notice statute and her mortgage was recorded first.","conclusion":"Answer D: The friend, because the buyer did not record his deed before her mortgage was recorded."},"questionZh":"一名男子于 1 月 10 日拥有土地，并有简单记录。当天，一位朋友借给该男子 5 万美元，该男子将土地抵押给她作为贷款担保。抵押贷款于1月18日登记。该男子于1月11日以有价值的对价将土地转让给买家。朋友并不知道这一点，买家也不知道抵押给朋友的事情，直到1月23日，即买家记录该男子的房产的那一天，双方才发现事实。该司法管辖区的备案法规定：“未经备案的不动产转让或抵押不得对后续购买者有利于未经通知的价值，后续购买者应首先进行记录。”没有宽限期的规定，也没有其他相关的法律规定。这位朋友起诉买方，要求证明她的抵押贷款对这块土地是有利的。法院应该为谁做出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"买家，因为他在朋友记录她的抵押贷款之前，在没有通知的情况下支付了宝贵的对价。","B":"买家，因为朋友的录音延迟意味着她无法及时主张自己的优先权。","C":"朋友，因为抵押给她之后，那个男人给买家的房产证，必然要受她抵押的约束。","D":"朋友，因为买家在记录她的抵押贷款之前没有记录他的契约。"},"explanationZh":"记录法旨在通过创建一个框架来确定竞争性财产利益的优先顺序，从而提高所有权的确定性。在竞赛通知管辖区（如此处所示），善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益将优先于先前的财产权益，如果 BFP： 在 BFP 获得他/她的权益时缺乏对先前权益的通知，并在记录先前权益之前的任何时间记录了他/她的权益。在这里，买家在购买土地时并不知道朋友之前有抵押贷款。但由于他直到朋友记录了抵押贷款之后才记录了自己的行为，因此法院应该为朋友做出裁决。 （选项A） 尽管买方在朋友登记她的抵押贷款之前，在没有通知的情况下支付了宝贵的对价（即，他是一个没有通知的BFP），但在朋友记录她的抵押贷款之前，他也没有记录他的契约。 （选择B） 朋友的录音延迟并不能阻止（即阻止）她及时主张她的优先权，因为录音没有最后期限。她只需要先记录她的抵押贷款（即在买方记录其契约之前）即可在这个竞赛通知管辖区获胜。既然她这样做了，法院就应该为这位朋友做出赔偿。 （选项C） 该男子向买方提供的房产契约不一定适用于该朋友的抵押贷款。相反，这位朋友会占上风，因为这个司法管辖区有种族通知法规，并且她的抵押贷款首先被记录。\n\n教育目标：在竞争通知司法管辖区，如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益 (BFP) (1) 缺乏对较早权益的通知且 (2) 首先记录，则该 BFP 的财产权益优先于较早竞争的财产权益。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在竞赛通知司法管辖区，如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益 (1) 未通知较早的权益且 (2) 首先记录，则该 BFP 的财产权益优先于较早的竞争性财产权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该为谁做出裁决？","rule":"在竞赛通知司法管辖区，如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益 (1) 未通知较早的权益且 (2) 首先记录，则该 BFP 的财产权益优先于较早的竞争性财产权益。","analysis":"记录法旨在通过创建一个框架来确定竞争性财产利益的优先顺序，从而提高所有权的确定性。在竞赛通知管辖区（如此处所示），善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益将优先于先前的财产权益，如果 BFP： 在 BFP 获得他/她的权益时缺乏对先前权益的通知，并在记录先前权益之前的任何时间记录了他/她的权益。在这里，买家在购买土地时并不知道朋友之前有抵押贷款。但由于他直到朋友记录了抵押贷款之后才记录了自己的行为，因此法院应该为朋友做出裁决。 （选项A） 尽管买方在朋友登记她的抵押贷款之前，在没有通知的情况下支付了宝贵的对价（即，他是一个没有通知的BFP），但在朋友记录她的抵押贷款之前，他也没有记录他的契约。 （选择B） 朋友的录音延迟并不能阻止（即阻止）她及时主张她的优先权，因为录音没有最后期限。她只需要先记录她的抵押贷款（即在买方记录其契约之前）即可在这个竞赛通知管辖区获胜。既然她这样做了，法院就应该为这位朋友做出赔偿。 （选项C） 该男子向买方提供的房产契约不一定适用于该朋友的抵押贷款。相反，这位朋友会占上风，因为这个司法管辖区有种族通知法规，并且她的抵押贷款首先被记录。\n\n教育目标：在竞争通知司法管辖区，如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益 (BFP) (1) 缺乏对较早权益的通知且 (2) 首先记录，则该 BFP 的财产权益优先于较早竞争的财产权益。","conclusion":"答案 D：朋友，因为买家在记录她的抵押贷款之前没有记录他的契约。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-da4315e0b49872a2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":31,"sourceQuestionPage":91,"sourceQuestionPages":[91],"sourceExplanationPages":[92],"sourceAnswerPages":[92],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-032","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"An environmentalist conveyed his land by quitclaim deed as a gift to his daughter, who did not then record her deed. Later, the environmentalist conveyed the land by warranty deed to a buyer, who paid valuable consideration, knew nothing of the daughter's claim, and promptly and properly recorded. Next, the daughter recorded her deed. Then the buyer conveyed the land by quitclaim deed to his son as a gift. When the possible conflict with the daughter was discovered, the son recorded his deed. The land at all relevant times has been vacant unoccupied land. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No unrecorded conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value without notice, who shall first record.\" No other statute is applicable. The son has sued the daughter to establish who owns the land. For whom will the court hold?","choices":{"A":"The daughter, because she recorded before the son.","B":"The daughter, because the son was a subsequent donee.","C":"The son, because the buyer's purchase cut off the daughter's rights.","D":"The son, because the daughter was a donee."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The priority of competing property interests is determined by the jurisdiction's recording act. In a race-notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a purchaser has priority over another's earlier property interest if the purchaser: is bona fide (ie, a BFP) because the purchaser obtained an interest in property without notice of the earlier interest and recorded first. Under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is entitled to the same protection under the recording act as the BFP—even if that person would not otherwise be protected by the recording act. Here, the buyer's purchase cut off the daughter's rights because the buyer knew nothing of the daughter's prior interest (BFP) and recorded his deed before the daughter recorded hers.* And though the son received the land as a gift from the buyer (not a BFP) and recorded his deed after the daughter recorded hers, the Shelter Rule entitles him to the buyer's protection under this jurisdiction's race-notice act. As a result, the court should rule for the son. *Had the buyer recorded his deed after the daughter recorded hers, the buyer would not have had priority under the recording act and the daughter would have prevailed. (Choices A & B) The recording act would not ordinarily protect the son from the daughter's prior claim since he was a donee (not a BFP) and the daughter recorded before him. But since he was gifted the land by a BFP (the buyer) who recorded before the daughter, the son is protected from her claim under the Shelter Rule. (Choice D) The daughter's status as a donee would only matter if she were seeking to establish priority over an earlier competing interest in the land. Since she has the earlier claim here, her status as a donee is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: In a race-notice jurisdiction, a purchaser who lacks notice of an earlier property interest (BFP) and records first will prevail. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is entitled to the same protection under the recording act as the BFP.","educationalObjective":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a purchaser who lacks notice of an earlier property interest (BFP) and records first will prevail. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is entitled to the same protection under the recording act as the BFP.","references":"","rule":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a purchaser who lacks notice of an earlier property interest (BFP) and records first will prevail. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is entitled to the same protection under the recording act as the BFP.","irac":{"issue":"For whom will the court hold?","rule":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a purchaser who lacks notice of an earlier property interest (BFP) and records first will prevail. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is entitled to the same protection under the recording act as the BFP.","analysis":"The priority of competing property interests is determined by the jurisdiction's recording act. In a race-notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a purchaser has priority over another's earlier property interest if the purchaser: is bona fide (ie, a BFP) because the purchaser obtained an interest in property without notice of the earlier interest and recorded first. Under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is entitled to the same protection under the recording act as the BFP—even if that person would not otherwise be protected by the recording act. Here, the buyer's purchase cut off the daughter's rights because the buyer knew nothing of the daughter's prior interest (BFP) and recorded his deed before the daughter recorded hers.* And though the son received the land as a gift from the buyer (not a BFP) and recorded his deed after the daughter recorded hers, the Shelter Rule entitles him to the buyer's protection under this jurisdiction's race-notice act. As a result, the court should rule for the son. *Had the buyer recorded his deed after the daughter recorded hers, the buyer would not have had priority under the recording act and the daughter would have prevailed. (Choices A & B) The recording act would not ordinarily protect the son from the daughter's prior claim since he was a donee (not a BFP) and the daughter recorded before him. But since he was gifted the land by a BFP (the buyer) who recorded before the daughter, the son is protected from her claim under the Shelter Rule. (Choice D) The daughter's status as a donee would only matter if she were seeking to establish priority over an earlier competing interest in the land. Since she has the earlier claim here, her status as a donee is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer C: The son, because the buyer's purchase cut off the daughter's rights."},"questionZh":"一位环保人士通过放弃所有权契约将他的土地作为礼物转让给他的女儿，但女儿随后没有记录她的契约。后来，这位环保人士通过保修契约将土地转让给了一位买家，买家支付了宝贵的对价，对女儿的主张一无所知，并及时正确地进行了记录。接下来，女儿记录了她的行为。然后，买方通过放弃所有权契据将土地转让给他的儿子作为礼物。当发现与女儿可能发生的冲突时，儿子记录了自己的行为。该土地在所有相关时间都是空置的未占用土地。该司法管辖区的备案法规定：“未经备案的不动产转让或抵押不得对后续购买者有利于未经通知的价值，后续购买者应首先进行记录。”没有其他适用的法规。儿子起诉女儿以确定土地所有权。法庭将为谁开庭？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿，因为她先于儿子录制。","B":"女儿，因为儿子是后来的受赠人。","C":"儿子，因为买家的购买切断了女儿的权利。","D":"儿子，因为女儿是受赠人。"},"explanationZh":"竞争财产利益的优先顺序由司法管辖区的记录法决定。在竞赛通知司法管辖区（如此处所示），如果购买者满足以下条件，则购买者优先于其他人的早期财产权益： 是善意的（即 BFP），因为购买者在没有通知早期权益的情况下获得了财产权益并首先记录。根据庇护规则，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人有权根据记录法获得与 BFP 相同的保护，即使该人不会受到记录法的保护。在此，买方的购买行为切断了女儿的权利，因为买方对女儿的优先权益 (BFP) 毫不知情，并且在女儿记录她的权益之前记录了自己的契约。*尽管儿子从买方（而非 BFP）那里收到了土地作为礼物，并在女儿记录了她的权益之后才记录了自己的契约，但《住房规则》使他有权根据本管辖区的竞赛通知法获得买方的保护。因此，法院应判决儿子胜诉。 *如果买方在女儿记录了她的行为之后记录了他的行为，则根据记录法，买方将不具有优先权，而女儿将占上风。 （选择 A 和 B）录音行为通常不会保护儿子免受女儿事先索赔的影响，因为他是受赠人（不是 BFP）并且女儿在他之前录音。但由于 BFP（买家）在女儿之前登记了土地，因此儿子根据《庇护所规则》受到保护，免受她的索赔。 （选项 D） 女儿作为受赠人的地位只有在她寻求优先于先前对土地的竞争利益时才重要。由于她在此拥有较早的索赔权，因此她作为受赠人的身份无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：在竞赛通知司法管辖区，缺乏早期财产权益通知 (BFP) 和记录的购买者优先。根据《庇护规则》，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人有权根据记录法获得与 BFP 相同的保护。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在竞赛通知司法管辖区，缺乏早期财产权益通知 (BFP) 和记录的购买者将优先。根据《庇护规则》，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人有权根据记录法获得与 BFP 相同的保护。","iracZh":{"issue":"法庭将为谁开庭？","rule":"在竞赛通知司法管辖区，缺乏早期财产权益通知 (BFP) 和记录的购买者将优先。根据《庇护规则》，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人有权根据记录法获得与 BFP 相同的保护。","analysis":"竞争财产利益的优先顺序由司法管辖区的记录法决定。在竞赛通知司法管辖区（如此处所示），如果购买者满足以下条件，则购买者优先于其他人的早期财产权益： 是善意的（即 BFP），因为购买者在没有通知早期权益的情况下获得了财产权益并首先记录。根据庇护规则，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人有权根据记录法获得与 BFP 相同的保护，即使该人不会受到记录法的保护。在此，买方的购买行为切断了女儿的权利，因为买方对女儿的优先权益 (BFP) 毫不知情，并且在女儿记录她的权益之前记录了自己的契约。*尽管儿子从买方（而非 BFP）那里收到了土地作为礼物，并在女儿记录了她的权益之后才记录了自己的契约，但《住房规则》使他有权根据本管辖区的竞赛通知法获得买方的保护。因此，法院应判决儿子胜诉。 *如果买方在女儿记录了她的行为之后记录了他的行为，则根据记录法，买方将不具有优先权，而女儿将占上风。 （选择 A 和 B）录音行为通常不会保护儿子免受女儿事先索赔的影响，因为他是受赠人（不是 BFP）并且女儿在他之前录音。但由于 BFP（买家）在女儿之前登记了土地，因此儿子根据《庇护所规则》受到保护，免受她的索赔。 （选项 D） 女儿作为受赠人的地位只有在她寻求优先于先前对土地的竞争利益时才重要。由于她在此拥有较早的索赔权，因此她作为受赠人的身份无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：在竞赛通知司法管辖区，缺乏早期财产权益通知 (BFP) 和记录的购买者优先。根据《庇护规则》，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人有权根据记录法获得与 BFP 相同的保护。","conclusion":"答案 C：儿子，因为买家的购买切断了女儿的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-84a2c2bf0814188e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":32,"sourceQuestionPage":93,"sourceQuestionPages":[93],"sourceExplanationPages":[94,95],"sourceAnswerPages":[94,95],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-033","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man wanted to downsize his home after his youngest child moved out to attend college. To finance the purchase, the man secured a loan from a bank and executed a promissory note in which he agreed to repay the bank's loan over a 30-year period. The loan was secured by a mortgage on the new home. The bank promptly and properly recorded the mortgage. A year later, the man missed several mortgage payments and abandoned the home shortly thereafter. The bank initiated judicial foreclosure proceedings once it learned of the man's abandonment. In a jurisdiction that follows the intermediate theory of mortgages, which of the following events first entitled the bank to take possession of the mortgaged property?","choices":{"A":"The man executing the mortgage.","B":"The man defaulting on the mortgage.","C":"The man abandoning the property.","D":"The bank foreclosing on the property."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A lender's (mortgagee's) right to take possession of mortgaged property depends on the mortgage theory followed in that jurisdiction: Lien theory (majority rule) – the mortgagor (borrower) is considered the owner of the mortgaged property, so the lender cannot take possession prior to foreclosure.\n\nTitle theory – the lender is considered the owner of the mortgaged property until the mortgage has been paid in full, so the lender may take possession at any time.*\n\nIntermediate theory – the mortgagor is considered the owner of the mortgaged property until default, so the lender obtains legal title (ie, the right of ownership and possession) to the property upon default.\n\nSince this jurisdiction follows the intermediate theory of mortgages, the man was entitled to possession of the home until he defaulted on the mortgage (Choice A). Therefore, the bank first acquired the right of ownership and possession of the home upon default. *However, mortgage agreements typically limit this right so that the lender may take possession only upon default. (Choice C) Under any mortgage theory, a lender may take possession of mortgaged property if it is abandoned by the mortgagor. But here, the man defaulted on the mortgage loan before he abandoned the property, so the bank was entitled to possession prior to his abandonment. (Choice D) The bank was first entitled to take possession of the home when the man defaulted on the mortgage, which occurred prior to foreclosure.\n\nEducational objective: Under the intermediate theory of mortgages, a mortgagor is entitled to possession until default, at which time the lender acquires legal title (ie, the right to own and possess) to the mortgaged property.","educationalObjective":"Under the intermediate theory of mortgages, a mortgagor is entitled to possession until default, at which time the lender acquires legal title (ie, the right to own and possess) to the mortgaged property.","references":"","rule":"Under the intermediate theory of mortgages, a mortgagor is entitled to possession until default, at which time the lender acquires legal title (ie, the right to own and possess) to the mortgaged property.","irac":{"issue":"In a jurisdiction that follows the intermediate theory of mortgages, which of the following events first entitled the bank to take possession of the mortgaged property?","rule":"Under the intermediate theory of mortgages, a mortgagor is entitled to possession until default, at which time the lender acquires legal title (ie, the right to own and possess) to the mortgaged property.","analysis":"A lender's (mortgagee's) right to take possession of mortgaged property depends on the mortgage theory followed in that jurisdiction: Lien theory (majority rule) – the mortgagor (borrower) is considered the owner of the mortgaged property, so the lender cannot take possession prior to foreclosure.\n\nTitle theory – the lender is considered the owner of the mortgaged property until the mortgage has been paid in full, so the lender may take possession at any time.*\n\nIntermediate theory – the mortgagor is considered the owner of the mortgaged property until default, so the lender obtains legal title (ie, the right of ownership and possession) to the property upon default.\n\nSince this jurisdiction follows the intermediate theory of mortgages, the man was entitled to possession of the home until he defaulted on the mortgage (Choice A). Therefore, the bank first acquired the right of ownership and possession of the home upon default. *However, mortgage agreements typically limit this right so that the lender may take possession only upon default. (Choice C) Under any mortgage theory, a lender may take possession of mortgaged property if it is abandoned by the mortgagor. But here, the man defaulted on the mortgage loan before he abandoned the property, so the bank was entitled to possession prior to his abandonment. (Choice D) The bank was first entitled to take possession of the home when the man defaulted on the mortgage, which occurred prior to foreclosure.","conclusion":"Answer B: The man defaulting on the mortgage."},"questionZh":"一名男子在他最小的孩子搬出去上大学后想要缩小自己的房子。为了筹集购买资金，该男子从银行获得了贷款，并签署了一份期票，同意在 30 年内偿还银行贷款。这笔贷款以新房抵押贷款为担保。银行及时、妥善地记录了抵押贷款。一年后，该男子错过了几笔抵押贷款付款，不久后就放弃了房子。该银行在得知该男子被遗弃后，启动了司法止赎程序。在遵循抵押中间理论的司法管辖区中，下列哪项事件首先赋予银行占有抵押财产的权利？","choicesZh":{"A":"执行抵押贷款的人。","B":"该男子拖欠抵押贷款。","C":"男子放弃财产。","D":"银行取消了该房产的赎回权。"},"explanationZh":"贷款人（抵押权人）占有抵押财产的权利取决于该管辖区遵循的抵押理论：留置权理论（多数规则）——抵押人（借款人）被视为抵押财产的所有者，因此贷款人不能在丧失抵押品赎回权之前占有抵押财产。\n\n所有权理论——在抵押贷款全额支付之前，贷款人被视为抵押财产的所有者，因此贷款人可以随时占有。*\n\n中间理论——在违约之前，抵押人被视为抵押财产的所有者，因此贷款人在违约时获得该财产的合法所有权（即所有权和占有权）。\n\n由于该司法管辖区遵循抵押贷款的中间理论，因此该男子有权占有该房屋，直到他拖欠抵押贷款（选择 A）。因此，银行在违约时首先获得了房屋的所有权和占有权。 *但是，抵押贷款协议通常会限制这一权利，以便贷款人只有在违约时才能占有。 （选项C）根据任何抵押理论，如果抵押财产被抵押人遗弃，贷款人可以占有抵押财产。但在这里，该男子在放弃该房产之前拖欠了抵押贷款，因此银行有权在他放弃之前占有该房产。 （选项 D） 当该男子拖欠抵押贷款（发生在丧失抵押品赎回权之前）时，银行首先有权接管该房屋。\n\n教育目标：根据抵押贷款的中间理论，抵押人有权占有直至违约，此时贷款人获得抵押财产的合法所有权（即拥有和占有权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据抵押贷款的中间理论，抵押人有权占有直至违约，此时贷款人获得抵押财产的合法所有权（即拥有和占有权）。","iracZh":{"issue":"在遵循抵押中间理论的司法管辖区中，下列哪项事件首先赋予银行占有抵押财产的权利？","rule":"根据抵押贷款的中间理论，抵押人有权占有直至违约，此时贷款人获得抵押财产的合法所有权（即拥有和占有权）。","analysis":"贷款人（抵押权人）占有抵押财产的权利取决于该管辖区遵循的抵押理论：留置权理论（多数规则）——抵押人（借款人）被视为抵押财产的所有者，因此贷款人不能在丧失抵押品赎回权之前占有抵押财产。\n\n所有权理论——在抵押贷款全额支付之前，贷款人被视为抵押财产的所有者，因此贷款人可以随时占有。*\n\n中间理论——在违约之前，抵押人被视为抵押财产的所有者，因此贷款人在违约时获得该财产的合法所有权（即所有权和占有权）。\n\n由于该司法管辖区遵循抵押贷款的中间理论，因此该男子有权占有该房屋，直到他拖欠抵押贷款（选择 A）。因此，银行在违约时首先获得了房屋的所有权和占有权。 *但是，抵押贷款协议通常会限制这一权利，以便贷款人只有在违约时才能占有。 （选项C）根据任何抵押理论，如果抵押财产被抵押人遗弃，贷款人可以占有抵押财产。但在这里，该男子在放弃该房产之前拖欠了抵押贷款，因此银行有权在他放弃之前占有该房产。 （选项 D） 当该男子拖欠抵押贷款（发生在丧失抵押品赎回权之前）时，银行首先有权接管该房屋。\n\n教育目标：根据抵押贷款的中间理论，抵押人有权占有直至违约，此时贷款人获得抵押财产的合法所有权（即拥有和占有权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该男子拖欠抵押贷款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-59bce4b12988d9ed"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":33,"sourceQuestionPage":96,"sourceQuestionPages":[96],"sourceExplanationPages":[97,98],"sourceAnswerPages":[97,98],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-034","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A land developer executed and delivered a promissory note and a mortgage securing the note to a bank. The bank was named as payee in the note and as mortgagee in the mortgage. The note also included a statement that the indebtedness evidenced by the note was \"subject to the terms of a contract between the maker and the payee of the note executed on the same day\" and that the note was \"secured by a mortgage of even date.\" The mortgage was promptly and properly recorded. Subsequently, the bank sold the land developer's note and mortgage to an investor and delivered to the investor a written assignment of the land developer's note and mortgage. The assignment was promptly and properly recorded. The bank retained possession of both the note and the mortgage in order to act as collecting agent. Later, being short of funds, the bank sold the note and mortgage to an asset management company at a substantial discount. The bank executed a written assignment of the note and mortgage to the asset management company and delivered to it the note, the mortgage, and the assignment. The asset management company paid value for the assignment without actual knowledge of the prior assignment to the investor and promptly and properly recorded the assignment. The principal of the note was not then due, and there had been no default in payment of either interest or principal. If the issue of ownership of the land developer's note and mortgage is subsequently raised in an appropriate action by the investor to foreclose, for whom should the court hold?","choices":{"A":"The asset management company as to both the note and the mortgage.","B":"The investor as to both the note and the mortgage.","C":"The asset management company as to the note and the investor as to the mortgage.","D":"The investor as to the note and the asset management company as to the mortgage."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Transfer of promissory note & mortgage\n\nTransfer of both documents Both documents are transferred unless:\n\nTransfer of note without mortgage expressly prohibited in note/mortgage forbidden by statute or public policy or Transfer of mortgage without note* increases mortgagor's duties, burdens, risks Transfer is void\n\n*Jurisdictions are split as to effect of transfer.\n\nA mortgage is a document that gives the mortgagee (eg, bank) an interest in real property as security for an obligation owed by the mortgagor (ie, borrower). The obligation is typically memorialized in a promissory note. The mortgagee is generally free to transfer the promissory note and/or the mortgage securing that note unless: the mortgage or note expressly states otherwise the transfer is forbidden by statute or public policy or the transfer will increase the duties, burdens, or risks on the mortgagor. The recipient of the transferred note/mortgage (ie, transferee) then acquires the right of foreclosure. However, the transferee can lose this right to a bona fide purchaser—ie, one who pays value for the property interest without notice of another's prior interest in the property. Here, the investor acquired the right of foreclosure when it purchased the note and mortgage from the bank. The investor promptly and properly recorded the assignment (ie, transfer), providing record notice to any subsequent purchasers of that note and mortgage—including the asset management company. Since the asset management company was not a bona fide purchaser, it did not acquire the investor's right of foreclosure. Therefore, the court should hold for the investor as to both the note and the mortgage (Choices A, C & D).\n\nEducational objective: The recipient of a promissory note and mortgage securing that note acquires the right of foreclosure. However, the recipient can lose this right if the note and mortgage are subsequently conveyed to a bona fide purchaser—ie, one who pays value without notice of another's prior interest.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (transfer of mortgages).","educationalObjective":"The recipient of a promissory note and mortgage securing that note acquires the right of foreclosure. However, the recipient can lose this right if the note and mortgage are subsequently conveyed to a bona fide purchaser—ie, one who pays value without notice of another's prior interest.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (transfer of mortgages).","rule":"The recipient of a promissory note and mortgage securing that note acquires the right of foreclosure. However, the recipient can lose this right if the note and mortgage are subsequently conveyed to a bona fide purchaser—ie, one who pays value without notice of another's prior interest.","irac":{"issue":"If the issue of ownership of the land developer's note and mortgage is subsequently raised in an appropriate action by the investor to foreclose, for whom should the court hold?","rule":"The recipient of a promissory note and mortgage securing that note acquires the right of foreclosure. However, the recipient can lose this right if the note and mortgage are subsequently conveyed to a bona fide purchaser—ie, one who pays value without notice of another's prior interest.","analysis":"Transfer of promissory note & mortgage\n\nTransfer of both documents Both documents are transferred unless:\n\nTransfer of note without mortgage expressly prohibited in note/mortgage forbidden by statute or public policy or Transfer of mortgage without note* increases mortgagor's duties, burdens, risks Transfer is void\n\n*Jurisdictions are split as to effect of transfer.\n\nA mortgage is a document that gives the mortgagee (eg, bank) an interest in real property as security for an obligation owed by the mortgagor (ie, borrower). The obligation is typically memorialized in a promissory note. The mortgagee is generally free to transfer the promissory note and/or the mortgage securing that note unless: the mortgage or note expressly states otherwise the transfer is forbidden by statute or public policy or the transfer will increase the duties, burdens, or risks on the mortgagor. The recipient of the transferred note/mortgage (ie, transferee) then acquires the right of foreclosure. However, the transferee can lose this right to a bona fide purchaser—ie, one who pays value for the property interest without notice of another's prior interest in the property. Here, the investor acquired the right of foreclosure when it purchased the note and mortgage from the bank. The investor promptly and properly recorded the assignment (ie, transfer), providing record notice to any subsequent purchasers of that note and mortgage—including the asset management company. Since the asset management company was not a bona fide purchaser, it did not acquire the investor's right of foreclosure. Therefore, the court should hold for the investor as to both the note and the mortgage (Choices A, C & D).","conclusion":"Answer B: The investor as to both the note and the mortgage."},"questionZh":"土地开发商签署并向银行交付了本票和担保该票据的抵押贷款。该银行在票据中被指定为收款人，在抵押贷款中被指定为抵押权人。该票据还包括一项声明，称该票据所证明的债务“受制票人和收款人于同一天签署的合同条款的约束”，并且该票据“由偶数日期的抵押担保”。抵押贷款被及时、正确地记录。随后，银行将土地开发商票据和抵押贷款出售给投资者，并向投资者交付土地开发商票据和抵押贷款的书面转让书。任务被及时、正确地记录下来。银行保留了票据和抵押贷款的所有权，以便充当收款代理。后来，由于资金短缺，银行将票据和抵押物以大幅折扣卖给了一家资产管理公司。银行向资产管理公司签署了票据和抵押的书面转让，并将票据、抵押和转让交付给资产管理公司。资产管理公司在实际不知情的情况下支付了转让价款，并及时、正确地记录了转让情况。该票据的本金当时尚未到期，并且没有拖欠利息或本金的情况。如果投资者随后在适当的取消赎回权诉讼中提出土地开发商票据和抵押的所有权问题，法院应为谁举行？","choicesZh":{"A":"资产管理公司对票据和抵押物均如此。","B":"票据和抵押贷款的投资者。","C":"对于票据，资产管理公司；对于抵押，投资者。","D":"票据是投资者，抵押是资产管理公司。"},"explanationZh":"本票及抵押的转让\n\n两份文件的转让 两份文件均被转让，除非：\n\n票据中明确禁止转让无抵押的票据/法律或公共政策禁止的抵押或转让无票据*的抵押会增加抵押人的义务、负担和风险 转让无效\n\n*司法管辖区因转让的影响而有所不同。\n\n抵押贷款是一种文件，向抵押权人（例如银行）提供不动产权益，作为抵押人（即借款人）所欠债务的担保。该义务通常以本票形式记录。抵押权人通常可以自由转让本票和/或担保该票据的抵押，除非：抵押或票据明确规定法律或公共政策禁止转让，或者转让将增加抵押人的义务、负担或风险。转让票据/抵押贷款的接收者（即受让人）随后获得取消抵押品赎回权的权利。然而，受让人可能会失去对善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）的这一权利，即为财产权益支付价值而未通知他人先前对该财产的权益的人。在这里，投资者在从银行购买票据和抵押贷款时获得了取消抵押品赎回权。投资者及时、正确地记录转让（即转让），向该票据和抵押贷款的任何后续购买者（包括资产管理公司）提供记录通知。由于资产管理公司并非善意购买人，因此并未取得投资者的止赎权。因此，法院应支持投资者对票据和抵押贷款的支持（选项 A、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：本票和担保该票据的抵押贷款的接收者获得取消抵押品赎回权。然而，如果票据和抵押随后被转让给善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（即在未通知他人先前利益的情况下支付价值的购买者），接收者可能会失去这项权利。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款转让）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"本票和担保该票据的抵押的接收者获得取消抵押品赎回权。然而，如果票据和抵押随后被转让给善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（即在未通知他人先前利益的情况下支付价值的购买者），接收者可能会失去这项权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果投资者随后在适当的取消赎回权诉讼中提出土地开发商票据和抵押的所有权问题，法院应为谁举行？","rule":"本票和担保该票据的抵押的接收者获得取消抵押品赎回权。然而，如果票据和抵押随后被转让给善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（即在未通知他人先前利益的情况下支付价值的购买者），接收者可能会失去这项权利。","analysis":"本票及抵押的转让\n\n两份文件的转让 两份文件均被转让，除非：\n\n票据中明确禁止转让无抵押的票据/法律或公共政策禁止的抵押或转让无票据*的抵押会增加抵押人的义务、负担和风险 转让无效\n\n*司法管辖区因转让的影响而有所不同。\n\n抵押贷款是一种文件，向抵押权人（例如银行）提供不动产权益，作为抵押人（即借款人）所欠债务的担保。该义务通常以本票形式记录。抵押权人通常可以自由转让本票和/或担保该票据的抵押，除非：抵押或票据明确规定法律或公共政策禁止转让，或者转让将增加抵押人的义务、负担或风险。转让票据/抵押贷款的接收者（即受让人）随后获得取消抵押品赎回权的权利。然而，受让人可能会失去对善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）的这一权利，即为财产权益支付价值而未通知他人先前对该财产的权益的人。在这里，投资者在从银行购买票据和抵押贷款时获得了取消抵押品赎回权。投资者及时、正确地记录转让（即转让），向该票据和抵押贷款的任何后续购买者（包括资产管理公司）提供记录通知。由于资产管理公司并非善意购买人，因此并未取得投资者的止赎权。因此，法院应支持投资者对票据和抵押贷款的支持（选项 A、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：本票和担保该票据的抵押贷款的接收者获得取消抵押品赎回权。然而，如果票据和抵押随后被转让给善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（即在未通知他人先前利益的情况下支付价值的购买者），接收者可能会失去这项权利。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款转让）。","conclusion":"答案 B：票据和抵押贷款的投资者。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":34,"sourceQuestionPage":99,"sourceQuestionPages":[99],"sourceExplanationPages":[100,101],"sourceAnswerPages":[100,101],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-035","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A landowner executed an instrument in the proper form of a deed, purporting to convey his land to a friend. The landowner handed the instrument to the friend, saying, \"This is yours, but please do not record it until after I am dead. Otherwise, it will cause me no end of trouble with my relatives.\" Two days later, the landowner asked the friend to return the deed to him because he had decided that he should devise the land to the friend by will rather than by deed. The friend said that he would destroy the deed and a day or so later falsely told the landowner that the deed had been destroyed. Six months ago, the landowner, who had never executed a will, died intestate, survived by a daughter as his sole heir. The day after the landowner's death, the friend recorded the deed from him. As soon as the daughter discovered this recording and the friend's claim to the land, she brought an appropriate action against the friend to quiet title to the land. For whom should the court hold?","choices":{"A":"The daughter, because the death of the landowner deprived the subsequent recording of any effect.","B":"The daughter, because the friend was dishonest in reporting that he had destroyed the deed.","C":"The friend, because the deed was delivered to him.","D":"The friend, because the deed was recorded by him."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A deed is a document that transfers ownership of real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee) once it has been: delivered – evidenced by the grantor's intent to presently convey ownership to the grantee and accepted – presumed when the transfer benefits the grantee. Delivery is presumed if the grantor hands the deed to the grantee. This presumption can generally be rebutted by evidence that the grantor did not intend to presently transfer ownership. But evidence that the grantor gave the deed to the grantee subject to an oral condition is inadmissible, so that condition cannot be enforced. Here, delivery is presumed since the landowner handed the deed to the friend. And that presumption is not rebuttable since evidence of the landowner's oral condition (\"please do not record it until after I am dead\") is inadmissible. Therefore, the condition is not enforceable, and the court should hold for the friend. (Choices A & D) Recording a deed (ie, filing it in the official land records) after the grantor's death creates a presumption of delivery. But this presumption can be rebutted by evidence that the grantor did not deliver the deed during his/her life. Here, though the deed was not recorded until after the landowner's death, it was delivered during his life (when he handed it to the friend). Therefore, the land was effectively conveyed to the friend. (Choice B) Once a deed has been delivered and accepted, the grantee owns the property and the transfer cannot be canceled (eg, by destroying the deed). Therefore, the friend's dishonesty in reporting that he had destroyed the deed is inconsequential.\n\nEducational objective: A deed is delivered when the grantor intends to presently transfer ownership to the grantee (presumed when the deed is in the grantee's possession). Evidence that the grantor gave the deed to the grantee subject to an oral condition is inadmissible to rebut this presumption, and the condition is unenforceable.","educationalObjective":"A deed is delivered when the grantor intends to presently transfer ownership to the grantee (presumed when the deed is in the grantee's possession). Evidence that the grantor gave the deed to the grantee subject to an oral condition is inadmissible to rebut this presumption, and the condition is unenforceable.","references":"","rule":"A deed is delivered when the grantor intends to presently transfer ownership to the grantee (presumed when the deed is in the grantee's possession). Evidence that the grantor gave the deed to the grantee subject to an oral condition is inadmissible to rebut this presumption, and the condition is unenforceable.","irac":{"issue":"For whom should the court hold?","rule":"A deed is delivered when the grantor intends to presently transfer ownership to the grantee (presumed when the deed is in the grantee's possession). Evidence that the grantor gave the deed to the grantee subject to an oral condition is inadmissible to rebut this presumption, and the condition is unenforceable.","analysis":"A deed is a document that transfers ownership of real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee) once it has been: delivered – evidenced by the grantor's intent to presently convey ownership to the grantee and accepted – presumed when the transfer benefits the grantee. Delivery is presumed if the grantor hands the deed to the grantee. This presumption can generally be rebutted by evidence that the grantor did not intend to presently transfer ownership. But evidence that the grantor gave the deed to the grantee subject to an oral condition is inadmissible, so that condition cannot be enforced. Here, delivery is presumed since the landowner handed the deed to the friend. And that presumption is not rebuttable since evidence of the landowner's oral condition (\"please do not record it until after I am dead\") is inadmissible. Therefore, the condition is not enforceable, and the court should hold for the friend. (Choices A & D) Recording a deed (ie, filing it in the official land records) after the grantor's death creates a presumption of delivery. But this presumption can be rebutted by evidence that the grantor did not deliver the deed during his/her life. Here, though the deed was not recorded until after the landowner's death, it was delivered during his life (when he handed it to the friend). Therefore, the land was effectively conveyed to the friend. (Choice B) Once a deed has been delivered and accepted, the grantee owns the property and the transfer cannot be canceled (eg, by destroying the deed). Therefore, the friend's dishonesty in reporting that he had destroyed the deed is inconsequential.","conclusion":"Answer C: The friend, because the deed was delivered to him."},"questionZh":"土地所有者以契约的适当形式签署了一份文书，旨在将其土地转让给朋友。地主把乐器递给朋友，说道：“这是你的，但请在我死后才可以录制，否则会给我的亲戚带来无尽的麻烦。”两天后，地主要求朋友将地契归还给他，因为他决定应该通过意愿而不是通过契约将土地设计给朋友。朋友说他将销毁该契约，大约一天后，他谎称该契约已被销毁。六个月前，这位从未立过遗嘱的地主去世时没有留下遗嘱，幸存下来的女儿是他唯一的继承人。地主去世后的第二天，朋友记录了他的契约。女儿一发现这段录音以及朋友对这块土地的所有权，她就对朋友提起适当的诉讼，以平息这块土地的所有权。法庭应该为谁开庭？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿，因为地主的去世使随后的录音失去了任何效果。","B":"女儿，因为朋友不诚实地报告说他毁掉了房产证。","C":"朋友，因为房产证是交给他的。","D":"朋友，因为事迹是他记录的。"},"explanationZh":"契约是一种将不动产所有权从所有者（授予人）转移至另一人（受让人）的文件： 已交付 – 由授予人目前将所有权转让给受让人的意图证明并被接受 – 当转让使受让人受益时推定。如果设保人将契据交给受让人，则视为已交付。通常可以通过设保人目前不打算转让所有权的证据来反驳这一推定。但设保人在口头条件下将契约授予受让人的证据是不可接受的，因此该条件无法执行。在这里，由于土地所有者将契约交给了朋友，因此假定已交付。而且这一推定是不可反驳的，因为土地所有者口腔状况的证据（“请在我死后不要记录它”）是不可接受的。因此，该条件不可强制执行，法院应支持该朋友。 （选项 A 和 D）在授予人死亡后记录契约（即，将其归档到官方土地记录中）会产生交付推定。但这一推定可以通过证据证明设保人在其生前并未交付契据来反驳。在这里，尽管地契是在地主去世后才被记录的，但它是在他生前交付的（当他将其交给朋友时）。因此，该土地已有效转让给该朋友。 （选择B）一旦契约被交付并被接受，受让人就拥有该财产，并且转让不能被取消（例如，通过销毁契约）。因此，这位朋友不诚实地报告他毁坏了该房产的行为是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：当授予人打算立即将所有权转让给受让人时（假定该契约由受让人占有），契约就被交付。设保人向受让人附有口头条件的契约的证据不能用来反驳这一推定，并且该条件不可执行。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当设保人打算立即将所有权转让给受让人时（假定契据由受让人占有），契据即被交付。设保人向受让人附有口头条件的契约的证据不能用来反驳这一推定，并且该条件不可执行。","iracZh":{"issue":"法庭应该为谁开庭？","rule":"当设保人打算立即将所有权转让给受让人时（假定契据由受让人占有），契据即被交付。设保人向受让人附有口头条件的契约的证据不能用来反驳这一推定，并且该条件不可执行。","analysis":"契约是一种将不动产所有权从所有者（授予人）转移至另一人（受让人）的文件： 已交付 – 由授予人目前将所有权转让给受让人的意图证明并被接受 – 当转让使受让人受益时推定。如果设保人将契据交给受让人，则视为已交付。通常可以通过设保人目前不打算转让所有权的证据来反驳这一推定。但设保人在口头条件下将契约授予受让人的证据是不可接受的，因此该条件无法执行。在这里，由于土地所有者将契约交给了朋友，因此假定已交付。而且这一推定是不可反驳的，因为土地所有者口腔状况的证据（“请在我死后不要记录它”）是不可接受的。因此，该条件不可强制执行，法院应支持该朋友。 （选项 A 和 D）在授予人死亡后记录契约（即，将其归档到官方土地记录中）会产生交付推定。但这一推定可以通过证据证明设保人在其生前并未交付契据来反驳。在这里，尽管地契是在地主去世后才被记录的，但它是在他生前交付的（当他将其交给朋友时）。因此，该土地已有效转让给该朋友。 （选择B）一旦契约被交付并被接受，受让人就拥有该财产，并且转让不能被取消（例如，通过销毁契约）。因此，这位朋友不诚实地报告他毁坏了该房产的行为是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：当授予人打算立即将所有权转让给受让人时（假定该契约由受让人占有），契约就被交付。设保人向受让人附有口头条件的契约的证据不能用来反驳这一推定，并且该条件不可执行。","conclusion":"答案 C：朋友，因为房产证是交给他的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-08d7e9f3100fd18e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":35,"sourceQuestionPage":102,"sourceQuestionPages":[102],"sourceExplanationPages":[103,104],"sourceAnswerPages":[103,104],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-036","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A landowner needed money. His land was fairly worth $100,000, so the landowner tried to borrow $60,000 from a lender on the security of the land. The lender agreed, but only if the landowner would convey the land to the lender outright by warranty deed, with the lender agreeing orally to reconvey to the owner once the loan was paid according to its terms. The landowner agreed, conveyed the land to the lender by warranty deed, and received $60,000 cash from the lender. The lender promptly and properly recorded the landowner's deed. Now, the landowner has defaulted on repayment with $55,000 still due on the loan. The landowner is still in possession. Which of the following best states the parties' rights in the land?","choices":{"A":"The attempted security arrangement is null as an unknown creature to the law, so the lender has only a personal right to $55,000 from the landowner.","B":"The landowner, having defaulted, has no further rights in the land, so the lender may obtain summary eviction.","C":"The lender may bring whatever foreclosure proceeding is appropriate under the laws of the jurisdiction.","D":"The lender's oral agreement to reconvey is invalid under the statute of frauds, so the lender owns the land outright."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Mortgage alternatives\n\nAbsolute deed Debtor gives deed to creditor with intent to secure loan (ie, equitable mortgage)\n\nDeed of trust Debtor gives deed of trust to third-party trustee as collateral for debt, & creditor can instruct trustee to foreclose upon default\n\nInstallment land Debtor agrees to buy land through installment payments & gets contract immediate possession, but seller keeps legal title until paid in full\n\nSale-leaseback Seller leases property from buyer immediately after sale, & seller's rental payments function as repayments on loan\n\nEquitable Seller finances buyer's purchase with equitable vendor's lien when vendor's lien seller transfers title to buyer but purchase price not fully paid\n\nA mortgage is a lien against real property that is given to secure a debt and is often memorialized in two documents: Mortgage deed – a writing that conveys an interest in the real property to secure the debt Promissory note – a formal \"IOU\" in which the borrower promises to repay the debt according to the listed terms (eg, interest rate, payment schedule, acceleration clause) But an equitable mortgage can be established by an absolute deed (or other alternative means) that was intended to secure a debt. As a result, the deed recipient (like any other lender) may bring a foreclosure action if the debtor defaults on repaying the loan. Here, the landowner conveyed his land to the lender outright by warranty deed. And since the lender agreed to reconvey the land once the loan was paid, the deed was intended to secure the landowner's debt. As a result, the deed will be treated as an equitable mortgage (ie, an absolute deed) (Choice A). This means that the lender can bring an appropriate foreclosure proceeding to recover the $55,000 still due on the loan. (Choice B) A summary eviction is a legal procedure that allows a landlord to quickly remove a tenant from leased property without the formalities of a full trial. But it does not allow a lender (mortgagee) to remove a borrower (mortgagor) from mortgaged land. (Choice D) A mortgage is subject to the statute of frauds, so it must be in writing, signed by the parties against whom it is to be enforced, and contain all essential terms. Therefore, the lender's oral agreement to reconvey the land is invalid under this statute. But the written warranty deed is valid under the statute, and extrinsic evidence (eg, the oral agreement) can be used to show the parties' intent to create a mortgage.\n\nEducational objective: A mortgage is typically evidenced by a mortgage deed and a promissory note. But an equitable mortgage can also be created when an absolute deed is made with the intent that it secure a debt.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Prop.: Mortgages § 3.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (absolute deed as mortgage).","educationalObjective":"A mortgage is typically evidenced by a mortgage deed and a promissory note. But an equitable mortgage can also be created when an absolute deed is made with the intent that it secure a debt.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Prop.: Mortgages § 3.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (absolute deed as mortgage).","rule":"A mortgage is typically evidenced by a mortgage deed and a promissory note. But an equitable mortgage can also be created when an absolute deed is made with the intent that it secure a debt.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best states the parties' rights in the land?","rule":"A mortgage is typically evidenced by a mortgage deed and a promissory note. But an equitable mortgage can also be created when an absolute deed is made with the intent that it secure a debt.","analysis":"Mortgage alternatives\n\nAbsolute deed Debtor gives deed to creditor with intent to secure loan (ie, equitable mortgage)\n\nDeed of trust Debtor gives deed of trust to third-party trustee as collateral for debt, & creditor can instruct trustee to foreclose upon default\n\nInstallment land Debtor agrees to buy land through installment payments & gets contract immediate possession, but seller keeps legal title until paid in full\n\nSale-leaseback Seller leases property from buyer immediately after sale, & seller's rental payments function as repayments on loan\n\nEquitable Seller finances buyer's purchase with equitable vendor's lien when vendor's lien seller transfers title to buyer but purchase price not fully paid\n\nA mortgage is a lien against real property that is given to secure a debt and is often memorialized in two documents: Mortgage deed – a writing that conveys an interest in the real property to secure the debt Promissory note – a formal \"IOU\" in which the borrower promises to repay the debt according to the listed terms (eg, interest rate, payment schedule, acceleration clause) But an equitable mortgage can be established by an absolute deed (or other alternative means) that was intended to secure a debt. As a result, the deed recipient (like any other lender) may bring a foreclosure action if the debtor defaults on repaying the loan. Here, the landowner conveyed his land to the lender outright by warranty deed. And since the lender agreed to reconvey the land once the loan was paid, the deed was intended to secure the landowner's debt. As a result, the deed will be treated as an equitable mortgage (ie, an absolute deed) (Choice A). This means that the lender can bring an appropriate foreclosure proceeding to recover the $55,000 still due on the loan. (Choice B) A summary eviction is a legal procedure that allows a landlord to quickly remove a tenant from leased property without the formalities of a full trial. But it does not allow a lender (mortgagee) to remove a borrower (mortgagor) from mortgaged land. (Choice D) A mortgage is subject to the statute of frauds, so it must be in writing, signed by the parties against whom it is to be enforced, and contain all essential terms. Therefore, the lender's oral agreement to reconvey the land is invalid under this statute. But the written warranty deed is valid under the statute, and extrinsic evidence (eg, the oral agreement) can be used to show the parties' intent to create a mortgage.","conclusion":"Answer C: The lender may bring whatever foreclosure proceeding is appropriate under the laws of the jurisdiction."},"questionZh":"地主需要钱。他的土地价值 10 万美元，因此地主试图以土地为抵押向贷款人借 6 万美元。贷款人同意，但前提是土地所有者通过保修契约将土地直接转让给贷款人，并且贷款人口头同意一旦根据其条款支付贷款，就将土地重新转让给所有者。土地所有者同意了，通过保修契约将土地转让给贷款人，并从贷款人那里收到了 60,000 美元现金。贷款人及时、正确地记录了土地所有者的契约。现在，土地所有者已拖欠还款，尚有 55,000 美元的贷款到期。土地所有者仍占有该土地。以下哪项最能说明各方对土地的权利？","choicesZh":{"A":"尝试的担保安排作为法律上的未知生物而无效，因此贷方仅拥有从土地所有者处获得 55,000 美元的个人权利。","B":"土地所有者违约后，对土地不再享有任何权利，因此贷款人可以获得即决驱逐。","C":"贷款人可以根据管辖区的法律提起任何适当的止赎程序。","D":"根据欺诈法，贷款人的口头返还协议无效，因此贷款人完全拥有土地。"},"explanationZh":"抵押贷款替代方案\n\n绝对契约 债务人向债权人提供契约以获取贷款（即公平抵押贷款）\n\n信托契约债务人向第三方受托人提供信托契约作为债务抵押品，债权人可以指示受托人在违约时取消赎回权\n\n分期付款土地 债务人同意通过分期付款购买土地并立即获得合同占有，但卖方保留合法所有权，直至全额付款\n\n售后回租 卖方在出售后立即向买方出租房产，卖方的租金支付相当于贷款还款\n\n当卖方的留置权卖方将所有权转让给买方但购买价款未完全支付时，衡平法卖方利用衡平法卖方留置权为买方的购买提供资金\n\n抵押贷款是为担保债务而给予的不动产留置权，通常记录在两份文件中： 抵押契据——一种表达不动产权益以担保债务的书面文件 本票——一种正式的“欠条”，其中借款人承诺根据列出的条款（例如，利率、还款计划、加速条款）偿还债务。但是，公平的抵押贷款可以通过旨在担保债务的绝对契据（或其他替代方式）建立。因此，如果债务人拖欠偿还贷款，契约接收者（像任何其他贷款人一样）可能会提起取消抵押品赎回权的诉讼。在这里，土地所有者通过保修契约将其土地直接转让给贷方。由于贷款人同意在贷款支付后归还土地，因此该契约旨在保护土地所有者的债务。因此，该契约将被视为衡平抵押贷款（即绝对契约）（选择 A）。这意味着贷方可以提起适当的止赎程序，以收回仍到期的 55,000 美元贷款。 （选择 B）即决驱逐是一种法律程序，允许房东快速将租户从租赁房产中驱逐，而无需经过全面审判的手续。但它不允许贷款人（抵押权人）将借款人（抵押人）从抵押土地上移除。 （选择 D）抵押贷款受欺诈法的约束，因此必须采用书面形式，由执行对象方签署，并包含所有基本条款。因此，根据本法，贷款人收回土地的口头协议无效。但根据法律规定，书面担保契约是有效的，并且可以使用外在证据（例如口头协议）来表明当事人设立抵押的意图。\n\n教育目标：抵押贷款通常由抵押契据和本票证明。但是，当为了担保债务而签订绝对契约时，也可以创建公平的抵押贷款。\n\n参考文献\n提案重述（第三次）：抵押贷款第 3.2 条（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（绝对契约作为抵押贷款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"抵押贷款通常由抵押契据和本票证明。但是，当为了担保债务而签订绝对契约时，也可以创建公平的抵押贷款。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项最能说明各方对土地的权利？","rule":"抵押贷款通常由抵押契据和本票证明。但是，当为了担保债务而签订绝对契约时，也可以创建公平的抵押贷款。","analysis":"抵押贷款替代方案\n\n绝对契约 债务人向债权人提供契约以获取贷款（即公平抵押贷款）\n\n信托契约债务人向第三方受托人提供信托契约作为债务抵押品，债权人可以指示受托人在违约时取消赎回权\n\n分期付款土地 债务人同意通过分期付款购买土地并立即获得合同占有，但卖方保留合法所有权，直至全额付款\n\n售后回租 卖方在出售后立即向买方出租房产，卖方的租金支付相当于贷款还款\n\n当卖方的留置权卖方将所有权转让给买方但购买价款未完全支付时，衡平法卖方利用衡平法卖方留置权为买方的购买提供资金\n\n抵押贷款是为担保债务而给予的不动产留置权，通常记录在两份文件中： 抵押契据——一种表达不动产权益以担保债务的书面文件 本票——一种正式的“欠条”，其中借款人承诺根据列出的条款（例如，利率、还款计划、加速条款）偿还债务。但是，公平的抵押贷款可以通过旨在担保债务的绝对契据（或其他替代方式）建立。因此，如果债务人拖欠偿还贷款，契约接收者（像任何其他贷款人一样）可能会提起取消抵押品赎回权的诉讼。在这里，土地所有者通过保修契约将其土地直接转让给贷方。由于贷款人同意在贷款支付后归还土地，因此该契约旨在保护土地所有者的债务。因此，该契约将被视为衡平抵押贷款（即绝对契约）（选择 A）。这意味着贷方可以提起适当的止赎程序，以收回仍到期的 55,000 美元贷款。 （选择 B）即决驱逐是一种法律程序，允许房东快速将租户从租赁房产中驱逐，而无需经过全面审判的手续。但它不允许贷款人（抵押权人）将借款人（抵押人）从抵押土地上移除。 （选择 D）抵押贷款受欺诈法的约束，因此必须采用书面形式，由执行对象方签署，并包含所有基本条款。因此，根据本法，贷款人收回土地的口头协议无效。但根据法律规定，书面担保契约是有效的，并且可以使用外在证据（例如口头协议）来表明当事人设立抵押的意图。\n\n教育目标：抵押贷款通常由抵押契据和本票证明。但是，当为了担保债务而签订绝对契约时，也可以创建公平的抵押贷款。\n\n参考文献\n提案重述（第三次）：抵押贷款第 3.2 条（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（绝对契约作为抵押贷款）。","conclusion":"答案 C：贷款人可以根据管辖区的法律提起任何适当的止赎程序。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"eviction","ipa":"/ɪvˈɪkʃən/","syllables":"evic·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 逐出, 赶出；[法] 收回财产, 收回租地, 没收","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“eviction”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:evictions","sourceSentence":"The landowner, having defaulted, has no further rights in the land, so the lender may obtain summary eviction.","synonyms":["dispossession","legal ouster","constructive eviction"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":36,"sourceQuestionPage":105,"sourceQuestionPages":[105],"sourceExplanationPages":[106,107],"sourceAnswerPages":[106,107],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-037","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man conveyed land by quitclaim deed as a gift to his cousin, who did not then record the deed or take possession of the land. Six months later, when the man was still in possession, he conveyed the land by quitclaim deed as a gift to a friend, who knew nothing of the deed to the cousin. The friend did not record his deed. The man then vacated the land, and the friend took possession. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides as follows: \"No unrecorded conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value without notice, who shall first record.\" Recently, the cousin learned about the friend's deed and possession, immediately recorded her deed, and sued the friend for possession and to quiet title. The friend then recorded his deed and raised all available defenses. For whom is the court likely to decide?","choices":{"A":"For the cousin, because she was first in time and the friend was not a purchaser.","B":"For the cousin, because the friend failed to first record.","C":"For the friend, because a subsequent good-faith donee has priority over a prior donee who fails to record.","D":"For the friend, because he was first in possession."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Priority of subsequent property interests\n\nRecording Race Interest holder who records first will prevail act Notice Subsequent bona fide purchaser (BFP) without notice of earlier interest will prevail\n\nRace- Subsequent BFP who lacks notice of earlier interest & records notice first will prevail\n\nCommon First in Earlier interest holder will prevail over subsequent interest law time holders\n\nA jurisdiction's recording act is typically used to determine priority among competing interests in the same property. But if the recording act does not apply, priority will be determined under the common law \"first-in-time\" rule, which gives earlier property interests priority over subsequent property interests. This jurisdiction's recording act (a race-notice statute) only protects subsequent purchasers for value who lack notice of a competing claim and record first. Since the man gifted his land to the cousin and then to the friend (ie, they were not purchasers), the recording act does not apply. Instead, the dispute will be resolved under the \"first-in-time\" rule. And the court will likely find for the cousin because she received her property interest before the friend received his. (Choice B) Had the friend been a purchaser for value, he would have needed to record his property interest first to protect it under the jurisdiction's race-notice act. But since the friend was a gift recipient, the recording act does not apply and the friend will not lose on this basis. (Choice C) An earlier donee's property interest always has priority over a subsequent donee's interest in a race-notice jurisdiction. The fact that the subsequent donee was a good-faith donee (ie, had no notice of the prior donee's interest) is immaterial. (Choice D) The common law \"first-in-time\" rule gives priority to whoever acquires the property interest first—not whoever takes possession first. So, the cousin (not the friend) will prevail because she was gifted the land first.\n\nEducational objective: Priority among competing interests in the same property is generally evaluated under the jurisdiction's recording act. But if the recording act does not apply, priority will be assessed under the common law \"first-in-time\" rule, which prioritizes earlier property interests over subsequent ones.","educationalObjective":"Priority among competing interests in the same property is generally evaluated under the jurisdiction's recording act. But if the recording act does not apply, priority will be assessed under the common law \"first-in-time\" rule, which prioritizes earlier property interests over subsequent ones.","references":"","rule":"Priority among competing interests in the same property is generally evaluated under the jurisdiction's recording act. But if the recording act does not apply, priority will be assessed under the common law \"first-in-time\" rule, which prioritizes earlier property interests over subsequent ones.","irac":{"issue":"For whom is the court likely to decide?","rule":"Priority among competing interests in the same property is generally evaluated under the jurisdiction's recording act. But if the recording act does not apply, priority will be assessed under the common law \"first-in-time\" rule, which prioritizes earlier property interests over subsequent ones.","analysis":"Priority of subsequent property interests\n\nRecording Race Interest holder who records first will prevail act Notice Subsequent bona fide purchaser (BFP) without notice of earlier interest will prevail\n\nRace- Subsequent BFP who lacks notice of earlier interest & records notice first will prevail\n\nCommon First in Earlier interest holder will prevail over subsequent interest law time holders\n\nA jurisdiction's recording act is typically used to determine priority among competing interests in the same property. But if the recording act does not apply, priority will be determined under the common law \"first-in-time\" rule, which gives earlier property interests priority over subsequent property interests. This jurisdiction's recording act (a race-notice statute) only protects subsequent purchasers for value who lack notice of a competing claim and record first. Since the man gifted his land to the cousin and then to the friend (ie, they were not purchasers), the recording act does not apply. Instead, the dispute will be resolved under the \"first-in-time\" rule. And the court will likely find for the cousin because she received her property interest before the friend received his. (Choice B) Had the friend been a purchaser for value, he would have needed to record his property interest first to protect it under the jurisdiction's race-notice act. But since the friend was a gift recipient, the recording act does not apply and the friend will not lose on this basis. (Choice C) An earlier donee's property interest always has priority over a subsequent donee's interest in a race-notice jurisdiction. The fact that the subsequent donee was a good-faith donee (ie, had no notice of the prior donee's interest) is immaterial. (Choice D) The common law \"first-in-time\" rule gives priority to whoever acquires the property interest first—not whoever takes possession first. So, the cousin (not the friend) will prevail because she was gifted the land first.","conclusion":"Answer A: For the cousin, because she was first in time and the friend was not a purchaser."},"questionZh":"一名男子通过无偿所有权契据将土地作为礼物转让给他的堂兄，但他的堂兄随后没有记录该契据或占有该土地。六个月后，当该男子仍然拥有土地时，他通过无偿所有权契据将这块土地作为礼物转让给一位朋友，而朋友对堂兄的这块土地一无所知。这位朋友没有记录他的行为。随后，该男子腾出土地，由朋友接管。该司法管辖区的备案法规定：“未经备案的不动产转让或抵押不得对后续购买者有利于未经通知的价值购买者，后续购买者应首先进行记录。”最近，表姐得知了朋友的行为和占有，立即记录了她的行为，并起诉朋友占有并要求安静的所有权。这位朋友随后记录了他的行为并提出了所有可用的防御措施。法院可能会为谁做出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"对于表姐来说，因为她是第一个来的，而朋友不是购买者。","B":"对于表弟来说，因为朋友没能先记录。","C":"对于朋友来说，因为后续的善意受赠人比之前未记录的受赠人具有优先权。","D":"对于朋友来说，因为他是第一个拥有球权的人。"},"explanationZh":"后续财产权益的优先顺序\n\n记录竞赛 兴趣持有人先记录者优先。 注意 后续善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 未提前通知兴趣者优先\n\n竞赛 - 后续 BFP 缺乏早期兴趣通知且首先记录通知的将优先\n\n早期利息持有人的共同优先优先于随后的利息法时间持有人\n\n司法管辖区的记录行为通常用于确定同一财产中竞争利益之间的优先权。但如果记录法不适用，则优先权将根据普通法的“在先”规则确定，即先行的财产权益优先于随后的财产权益。该司法管辖区的记录法（竞赛通知法规）仅保护后续购买者的价值，这些购买者缺乏对竞争索赔和记录的通知。由于该男子将其土地赠与他的表弟，然后又赠予朋友（即，他们不是购买者），因此记录法不适用。相反，争议将根据“先到先得”规则得到解决。法院很可能会为表弟做出判决，因为她在朋友收到财产权益之前就收到了财产权益。 （选择 B）如果这位朋友是价值购买者，他需要首先记录他的财产权益，以根据司法管辖区的竞赛通知法对其进行保护。但由于该朋友是礼物接收者，因此不适用录音行为，该朋友不会因此而遭受损失。 （选择 C）在竞赛通知管辖区，较早受赠人的财产权益始终优先于后续受赠人的权益。后续受赠人是善意受赠人（即没有注意到前一个受赠人的利益）这一事实并不重要。 （选项D） 普通法中的“先到先得”规则优先考虑谁先获得财产权益，而不是谁先占有。因此，表弟（而不是朋友）会占上风，因为她首先获得了土地。\n\n教育目标：同一财产中相互竞争的利益之间的优先权通常根据司法管辖区的记录法进行评估。但如果记录法不适用，优先权将根据普通法的“时间在先”规则进行评估，该规则优先考虑较早的财产权益而不是随后的财产权益。","educationalObjectiveZh":"同一财产中的竞争利益之间的优先权通常根据司法管辖区的记录法进行评估。但如果记录法不适用，优先权将根据普通法的“时间在先”规则进行评估，该规则优先考虑较早的财产权益而不是随后的财产权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会为谁做出裁决？","rule":"同一财产中的竞争利益之间的优先权通常根据司法管辖区的记录法进行评估。但如果记录法不适用，优先权将根据普通法的“时间在先”规则进行评估，该规则优先考虑较早的财产权益而不是随后的财产权益。","analysis":"后续财产权益的优先顺序\n\n记录竞赛 兴趣持有人先记录者优先。 注意 后续善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 未提前通知兴趣者优先\n\n竞赛 - 后续 BFP 缺乏早期兴趣通知且首先记录通知的将优先\n\n早期利息持有人的共同优先优先于随后的利息法时间持有人\n\n司法管辖区的记录行为通常用于确定同一财产中竞争利益之间的优先权。但如果记录法不适用，则优先权将根据普通法的“在先”规则确定，即先行的财产权益优先于随后的财产权益。该司法管辖区的记录法（竞赛通知法规）仅保护后续购买者的价值，这些购买者缺乏对竞争索赔和记录的通知。由于该男子将其土地赠与他的表弟，然后又赠予朋友（即，他们不是购买者），因此记录法不适用。相反，争议将根据“先到先得”规则得到解决。法院很可能会为表弟做出判决，因为她在朋友收到财产权益之前就收到了财产权益。 （选择 B）如果这位朋友是价值购买者，他需要首先记录他的财产权益，以根据司法管辖区的竞赛通知法对其进行保护。但由于该朋友是礼物接收者，因此不适用录音行为，该朋友不会因此而遭受损失。 （选择 C）在竞赛通知管辖区，较早受赠人的财产权益始终优先于后续受赠人的权益。后续受赠人是善意受赠人（即没有注意到前一个受赠人的利益）这一事实并不重要。 （选项D） 普通法中的“先到先得”规则优先考虑谁先获得财产权益，而不是谁先占有。因此，表弟（而不是朋友）会占上风，因为她首先获得了土地。\n\n教育目标：同一财产中相互竞争的利益之间的优先权通常根据司法管辖区的记录法进行评估。但如果记录法不适用，优先权将根据普通法的“时间在先”规则进行评估，该规则优先考虑较早的财产权益而不是随后的财产权益。","conclusion":"答案 A：对于表姐来说，因为她是第一个来的，而朋友不是购买者。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":37,"sourceQuestionPage":108,"sourceQuestionPages":[108],"sourceExplanationPages":[109,110],"sourceAnswerPages":[109,110],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-038","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"Ten years ago, a seller sold land to a buyer, who financed the purchase price with a loan from a bank that was secured by a mortgage on the land. The buyer purchased a title insurance policy running to both the buyer and the bank, showing no liens on the property other than the buyer's mortgage to the bank. Eight years ago, the buyer paid the mortgage in full. Seven years ago, the buyer sold the land to an investor by a full covenant and warranty deed without exceptions. Six years ago, the investor gave the land to a donee by a quitclaim deed. Last year, the donee discovered an outstanding mortgage on the land that predated all of these conveyances. As a result of a title examiner's negligence, this mortgage was not disclosed in the title insurance policy issued to the buyer and the bank. Following this discovery, the donee successfully sued the buyer to recover the amount of the outstanding mortgage. If the buyer sues the title insurance company to recover the amount he paid to the donee, is he likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer conveyed the land to an investor.","B":"No, because the title insurance policy lapsed when the buyer paid off the bank's mortgage.","C":"Yes, because the buyer is protected by the title insurance policy even though he no longer owns the land.","D":"Yes, because the buyer was successfully sued by a donee and not by a bona fide purchaser for value."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A title insurance policy is a contract where the insurer agrees to indemnify (ie, compensate) the insureds within policy limits for any harm caused by title defects (eg, outstanding mortgages) not disclosed in the policy. There are two types of title insurance policies: Owner's policy – issued to property owners and protects them from any reduction in the full market value of the property so long as it is (1) owned by the insureds and their heirs or (2) conveyed by warranty deed Lender's policy – issued to lenders and protects them from any reduction in the value or priority of their mortgage until the mortgage is paid in full or otherwise discharged Here, the buyer purchased a title insurance policy that was issued to the buyer (owner) and the bank (lender). The bank's policy lapsed once the buyer paid off the mortgage, but the buyer's policy remained in effect even after he conveyed the property by warranty deed (Choices A & B). The buyer's right to the full market value of the property was then reduced by the donee's successful suit for the cost of the undisclosed outstanding mortgage. And since the buyer's policy protects against such losses, he can likely recover that cost from the title insurance company. (Choice D) Since the buyer was protected by the title insurance policy, he could recover costs from a successful lawsuit. It is irrelevant that the buyer had been sued by a donee (ie, gift recipient) instead of a bona fide purchaser (ie, purchaser for value who lacks notice of a prior competing interest).\n\nEducational objective: There are two types of title insurance policies: (1) owner's policy, which remains in effect as long as the property is owned by the insured or conveyed by warranty deed and (2) lender's policy, which ends once the mortgage is discharged.\n\nReferences\n43 Am. Jur. 2d Insurance § 518 (2018) (title insurance policies).","educationalObjective":"There are two types of title insurance policies: (1) owner's policy, which remains in effect as long as the property is owned by the insured or conveyed by warranty deed and (2) lender's policy, which ends once the mortgage is discharged.","references":"43 Am. Jur. 2d Insurance § 518 (2018) (title insurance policies).","rule":"There are two types of title insurance policies: (1) owner's policy, which remains in effect as long as the property is owned by the insured or conveyed by warranty deed and (2) lender's policy, which ends once the mortgage is discharged.","irac":{"issue":"If the buyer sues the title insurance company to recover the amount he paid to the donee, is he likely to prevail?","rule":"There are two types of title insurance policies: (1) owner's policy, which remains in effect as long as the property is owned by the insured or conveyed by warranty deed and (2) lender's policy, which ends once the mortgage is discharged.","analysis":"A title insurance policy is a contract where the insurer agrees to indemnify (ie, compensate) the insureds within policy limits for any harm caused by title defects (eg, outstanding mortgages) not disclosed in the policy. There are two types of title insurance policies: Owner's policy – issued to property owners and protects them from any reduction in the full market value of the property so long as it is (1) owned by the insureds and their heirs or (2) conveyed by warranty deed Lender's policy – issued to lenders and protects them from any reduction in the value or priority of their mortgage until the mortgage is paid in full or otherwise discharged Here, the buyer purchased a title insurance policy that was issued to the buyer (owner) and the bank (lender). The bank's policy lapsed once the buyer paid off the mortgage, but the buyer's policy remained in effect even after he conveyed the property by warranty deed (Choices A & B). The buyer's right to the full market value of the property was then reduced by the donee's successful suit for the cost of the undisclosed outstanding mortgage. And since the buyer's policy protects against such losses, he can likely recover that cost from the title insurance company. (Choice D) Since the buyer was protected by the title insurance policy, he could recover costs from a successful lawsuit. It is irrelevant that the buyer had been sued by a donee (ie, gift recipient) instead of a bona fide purchaser (ie, purchaser for value who lacks notice of a prior competing interest).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the buyer is protected by the title insurance policy even though he no longer owns the land."},"questionZh":"十年前，卖方将土地出售给买方，买方通过以土地抵押贷款为担保的银行贷款来支付购买价格。买方购买了一份适用于买方和银行的产权保险单，除了买方向银行提供的抵押贷款外，该保险没有显示对该财产的留置权。八年前，买家全额支付了抵押贷款。七年前，买方通过完整的契约和保修契约将土地出售给投资者，无一例外。六年前，投资者通过无偿债权契据将土地转让给受赠人。去年，受赠人发现该土地上有一笔未偿还的抵押贷款，该抵押贷款早于所有这些转让。由于产权审查员的疏忽，该抵押贷款并未在向买方和银行签发的产权保险单中披露。在这一发现之后，受赠人成功起诉买方，收回未偿抵押贷款金额。如果买方起诉产权保险公司以追回他支付给受赠人的金额，他有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为买方将土地转让给了投资者。","B":"不可以，因为当买方还清银行抵押贷款时，产权保险单就失效了。","C":"是的，因为即使买家不再拥有土地，他仍受到产权保险单的保护。","D":"是的，因为买方是被受赠人而不是善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）成功起诉的。"},"explanationZh":"产权保险保单是一种合同，其中保险公司同意在保单限额内赔偿（即补偿）被保险人因保单中未披露的产权缺陷（例如，未偿还的抵押贷款）造成的任何损害。产权保险单有两种类型： 业主保单 – 签发给业主，保护他们免受财产全部市场价值的任何减少，只要该财产 (1) 由被保险人及其继承人拥有或 (2) 通过保修契约转让 贷款人保单 – 签发给贷款人，保护他们的抵押贷款价值或优先权免受任何减少，直至抵押贷款全额支付或以其他方式解除 在此，买方购买了签发给买方（所有者）的产权保险单，并且银行（贷方）。一旦买方还清抵押贷款，银行的保单就失效了，但即使在他通过保修契据转让财产之后，买方的保单仍然有效（选择A和B）。由于受赠人成功起诉未披露的未偿还抵押贷款费用，买方对财产全部市场价值的权利被减少。由于买方的保单可以防止此类损失，因此他很可能可以从产权保险公司收回这笔费用。 （选项D）由于买方受到产权保险单的保护，他可以通过成功的诉讼收回费用。买方被受赠人（即礼物接受者）而不是善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（即没有事先通知竞争利益的价值购买者）起诉是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：产权保险保单有两种类型：(1) 业主保单，只要财产归被保险人所有或通过保修契约转让，该保单就一直有效；(2) 贷方保单，一旦抵押贷款解除，该保单就终止。\n\n参考文献\n43 上午。尤尔。 2d 保险 § 518 (2018)（产权保险单）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"产权保险保单有两种类型：(1) 业主保单，只要财产归被保险人所有或通过保修契约转让，该保单就一直有效；(2) 贷方保单，一旦抵押解除，该保单就终止。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果买方起诉产权保险公司以追回他支付给受赠人的金额，他有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"产权保险保单有两种类型：(1) 业主保单，只要财产归被保险人所有或通过保修契约转让，该保单就一直有效；(2) 贷方保单，一旦抵押解除，该保单就终止。","analysis":"产权保险保单是一种合同，其中保险公司同意在保单限额内赔偿（即补偿）被保险人因保单中未披露的产权缺陷（例如，未偿还的抵押贷款）造成的任何损害。产权保险单有两种类型： 业主保单 – 签发给业主，保护他们免受财产全部市场价值的任何减少，只要该财产 (1) 由被保险人及其继承人拥有或 (2) 通过保修契约转让 贷款人保单 – 签发给贷款人，保护他们的抵押贷款价值或优先权免受任何减少，直至抵押贷款全额支付或以其他方式解除 在此，买方购买了签发给买方（所有者）的产权保险单，并且银行（贷方）。一旦买方还清抵押贷款，银行的保单就失效了，但即使在他通过保修契据转让财产之后，买方的保单仍然有效（选择A和B）。由于受赠人成功起诉未披露的未偿还抵押贷款费用，买方对财产全部市场价值的权利被减少。由于买方的保单可以防止此类损失，因此他很可能可以从产权保险公司收回这笔费用。 （选项D）由于买方受到产权保险单的保护，他可以通过成功的诉讼收回费用。买方被受赠人（即礼物接受者）而不是善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（即没有事先通知竞争利益的价值购买者）起诉是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：产权保险保单有两种类型：(1) 业主保单，只要财产归被保险人所有或通过保修契约转让，该保单就一直有效；(2) 贷方保单，一旦抵押贷款解除，该保单就终止。\n\n参考文献\n43 上午。尤尔。 2d 保险 § 518 (2018)（产权保险单）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为即使买家不再拥有土地，他仍受到产权保险单的保护。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"covenant","ipa":"/kˈəvənənt/","syllables":"covenant","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 契约, 盟约；v. 立契约, 缔结盟约","contextualMeaning":"土地契约：约束土地使用或相关义务的承诺","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:covenants/3:covenants/p:covenanted/d:covenanted/i:covenanting","sourceSentence":"Seven years ago, the buyer sold the land to an investor by a full covenant and warranty deed without exceptions.","synonyms":["compact","concordat"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-1ede83d5fa13a28a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":38,"sourceQuestionPage":111,"sourceQuestionPages":[111],"sourceExplanationPages":[112,113],"sourceAnswerPages":[112,113],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-039","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A farmer borrowed $100,000 from a bank and gave the bank a promissory note secured by a mortgage on the farm that she owned. The bank promptly recorded the mortgage, which contained an acceleration clause and a due-on-sale provision. A few years later, the farmer borrowed $5,000 from a second bank and gave that bank a promissory note secured by a mortgage on her farm. The bank promptly recorded the mortgage. Subsequently, the farmer defaulted on her obligation to the first bank, which then validly accelerated the debt and instituted nonjudicial foreclosure proceedings as permitted by the jurisdiction. The second bank received notice of the foreclosure sale but did not send a representative to the sale. At the foreclosure sale, a buyer who was not acting in collusion with the farmer outbid all other bidders and received a deed to the farm. Several months later, the original farmer repurchased her farm from the buyer, who executed a warranty deed transferring the farm to her. After the farmer promptly recorded that deed, the second bank commenced foreclosure proceedings on the farm. The farmer denied the validity of the second bank's mortgage. Does the second bank continue to have a valid mortgage on the farm?","choices":{"A":"No, because of the due-on-sale provision in the farmer's mortgage to the first bank.","B":"No, because the purchase at the foreclosure sale by the buyer under these facts eliminated the second bank's junior mortgage lien.","C":"Yes, because of the doctrine of estoppel by deed.","D":"Yes, because the original owner reacquired title to the farm."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Foreclosure methods\n\nJudicial sale Judicially supervised public sale of mortgaged property\n\n(all states)\n\nNonjudicial Privately conducted public sale of mortgaged property (permitted only if sale mortgage contains power-of-sale clause)\n\n(half of all states)\n\nStrict Foreclosure without sale of mortgaged property foreclosure\n\n(few states)\n\nAbout half of all states permit nonjudicial foreclosure proceedings—ie, a privately conducted public sale of the mortgaged property (as in this case). The foreclosure sale generally destroys all interests that are junior to the mortgage being foreclosed. As a result, junior interest holders are necessary parties who must be given notice of the foreclosure so that they can participate (or send a representative). Failure to notify a junior interest holder results in the preservation of that party's interest despite the foreclosure sale. Here, the first bank instituted nonjudicial foreclosure proceedings after the farmer defaulted on her mortgage. The second bank (junior interest holder) received notice of the foreclosure sale but did not participate in or send a representative to the sale. This means that when the buyer purchased the farm in the foreclosure sale, the second bank's junior mortgage lien was eliminated. Accordingly, the second bank does not have a valid mortgage on the farm. (Choice A) A due-on-sale provision allows the mortgagee to declare the entire loan balance due and payable if the mortgagor sells the property without the mortgagee's permission. This provision is not implicated here because the farmer never sold the farm. (Choice C) The doctrine of estoppel by deed (ie, after-acquired title) applies when a person conveys unowned property to another by warranty deed. If that person later acquires title to the property, it automatically passes to the grantee. But this doctrine is clearly inapplicable here since the farmer owned the farm when she executed both mortgages. (Choice D) The fact that the farmer reacquired title to the farm would matter only if the buyer and the farmer had been acting in collusion. In that case, the second bank could claim that the foreclosure sale was a fraud intended to eliminate its interest in the farm.\n\nEducational objective: A foreclosure sale destroys all interests that are junior to the foreclosing mortgage, so the junior interest holders are necessary parties who must be given notice of the foreclosure. Otherwise, their interests are preserved post-sale.\n\nReferences\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 592 (2019) (requirement to notify junior lienholders of foreclosure action).","educationalObjective":"A foreclosure sale destroys all interests that are junior to the foreclosing mortgage, so the junior interest holders are necessary parties who must be given notice of the foreclosure. Otherwise, their interests are preserved post-sale.","references":"55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 592 (2019) (requirement to notify junior lienholders of foreclosure action).","rule":"A foreclosure sale destroys all interests that are junior to the foreclosing mortgage, so the junior interest holders are necessary parties who must be given notice of the foreclosure. Otherwise, their interests are preserved post-sale.","irac":{"issue":"Does the second bank continue to have a valid mortgage on the farm?","rule":"A foreclosure sale destroys all interests that are junior to the foreclosing mortgage, so the junior interest holders are necessary parties who must be given notice of the foreclosure. Otherwise, their interests are preserved post-sale.","analysis":"Foreclosure methods\n\nJudicial sale Judicially supervised public sale of mortgaged property\n\n(all states)\n\nNonjudicial Privately conducted public sale of mortgaged property (permitted only if sale mortgage contains power-of-sale clause)\n\n(half of all states)\n\nStrict Foreclosure without sale of mortgaged property foreclosure\n\n(few states)\n\nAbout half of all states permit nonjudicial foreclosure proceedings—ie, a privately conducted public sale of the mortgaged property (as in this case). The foreclosure sale generally destroys all interests that are junior to the mortgage being foreclosed. As a result, junior interest holders are necessary parties who must be given notice of the foreclosure so that they can participate (or send a representative). Failure to notify a junior interest holder results in the preservation of that party's interest despite the foreclosure sale. Here, the first bank instituted nonjudicial foreclosure proceedings after the farmer defaulted on her mortgage. The second bank (junior interest holder) received notice of the foreclosure sale but did not participate in or send a representative to the sale. This means that when the buyer purchased the farm in the foreclosure sale, the second bank's junior mortgage lien was eliminated. Accordingly, the second bank does not have a valid mortgage on the farm. (Choice A) A due-on-sale provision allows the mortgagee to declare the entire loan balance due and payable if the mortgagor sells the property without the mortgagee's permission. This provision is not implicated here because the farmer never sold the farm. (Choice C) The doctrine of estoppel by deed (ie, after-acquired title) applies when a person conveys unowned property to another by warranty deed. If that person later acquires title to the property, it automatically passes to the grantee. But this doctrine is clearly inapplicable here since the farmer owned the farm when she executed both mortgages. (Choice D) The fact that the farmer reacquired title to the farm would matter only if the buyer and the farmer had been acting in collusion. In that case, the second bank could claim that the foreclosure sale was a fraud intended to eliminate its interest in the farm.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the purchase at the foreclosure sale by the buyer under these facts eliminated the second bank's junior mortgage lien."},"questionZh":"一位农民从银行借了 10 万美元，并向银行提供了一张以她拥有的农场抵押贷款为担保的期票。银行立即记录了抵押贷款，其中包含加速条款和到期出售条款。几年后，这位农民从第二家银行借了 5,000 美元，并向该银行提供了一张以她的农场抵押贷款为担保的期票。银行立即记录了抵押贷款。随后，该农民未能履行对第一家银行的义务，该银行随后有效地加速了债务，并在司法管辖区允许的情况下启动了非司法止赎程序。第二家银行收到了取消抵押品赎回权出售的通知，但没有派代表参与出售。在取消抵押品赎回权的拍卖中，一名未与农民串通的买家出价高于所有其他竞标者，并获得了农场契约。几个月后，原来的农民从买家手中买回了她的农场，买家签署了一份保修契约，将农场转让给她。在农场主立即记录该契约后，第二家银行开始对该农场进行止赎程序。农民否认第二家银行抵押贷款的有效性。第二家银行是否继续拥有该农场的有效抵押贷款？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为农民向第一家银行提供的抵押贷款中有到期出售条款。","B":"不会，因为买方根据这些事实在取消抵押品赎回权出售时购买的商品消除了第二家银行的初级抵押留置权。","C":"是的，因为禁止反悔的原则。","D":"是的，因为原主人重新获得了农场的所有权。"},"explanationZh":"止赎方法\n\n司法出售 司法监督下抵押财产的公开出售\n\n（所有州）\n\n非司法私下公开出售抵押财产（仅当出售抵押包含出售权条款时才允许）\n\n（占所有州的一半）\n\n严格止赎，不出售抵押财产止赎\n\n（少数州）\n\n大约一半的州允许非司法取消抵押品赎回权程序，即私人公开出售抵押财产（如本例）。取消抵押品赎回权的出售通常会破坏所有次于被取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款的权益。因此，初级利益持有人是必要的当事人，必须向他们发出取消抵押品赎回权的通知，以便他们能够参与（或派出代表）。尽管取消抵押品赎回权出售，但未能通知初级利益持有人会导致该方的利益得以保留。在这里，第一家银行在农民拖欠抵押贷款后启动了非司法取消抵押品赎回权程序。第二家银行（初级利益持有人）收到取消抵押品赎回权出售的通知，但没有参与或派代表参加出售。这意味着，当买方在止赎出售中购买农场时，第二家银行的初级抵押留置权被消除。因此，第二家银行对该农场没有有效的抵押。 （选择A）如果抵押人未经抵押权人许可出售房产，到期出售条款允许抵押权人申报全部贷款余额到期应付。此处不涉及该条款，因为农民从未出售过农场。 （选择C）当一个人通过保证契约将无主财产转让给另一个人时，契约禁止反言原则（即事后获得的所有权）适用。如果该人后来获得了该财产的所有权，该所有权将自动转移给受让人。但这一原则显然在这里不适用，因为农民在执行两项抵押贷款时拥有农场。 （选项 D）只有当买方和农民串通一气时，农民重新获得农场所有权这一事实才有意义。在这种情况下，第二家银行可以声称取消抵押品赎回权的出售是一场欺诈，旨在消除其在农场的权益。\n\n教育目标：取消抵押品赎回权的出售会破坏所有低于取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款的权益，因此初级权益持有人是必须收到取消抵押品赎回权通知的必要当事人。否则，他们的利益在售后得到保留。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 592 (2019)（要求将取消抵押品赎回权行动通知初级留置权人）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"取消抵押品赎回权出售会破坏所有低于取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款的权益，因此初级权益持有人是必须收到取消抵押品赎回权通知的必要当事人。否则，他们的利益在售后得到保留。","iracZh":{"issue":"第二家银行是否继续拥有该农场的有效抵押贷款？","rule":"取消抵押品赎回权出售会破坏所有低于取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款的权益，因此初级权益持有人是必须收到取消抵押品赎回权通知的必要当事人。否则，他们的利益在售后得到保留。","analysis":"止赎方法\n\n司法出售 司法监督下抵押财产的公开出售\n\n（所有州）\n\n非司法私下公开出售抵押财产（仅当出售抵押包含出售权条款时才允许）\n\n（占所有州的一半）\n\n严格止赎，不出售抵押财产止赎\n\n（少数州）\n\n大约一半的州允许非司法取消抵押品赎回权程序，即私人公开出售抵押财产（如本例）。取消抵押品赎回权的出售通常会破坏所有次于被取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款的权益。因此，初级利益持有人是必要的当事人，必须向他们发出取消抵押品赎回权的通知，以便他们能够参与（或派出代表）。尽管取消抵押品赎回权出售，但未能通知初级利益持有人会导致该方的利益得以保留。在这里，第一家银行在农民拖欠抵押贷款后启动了非司法取消抵押品赎回权程序。第二家银行（初级利益持有人）收到取消抵押品赎回权出售的通知，但没有参与或派代表参加出售。这意味着，当买方在止赎出售中购买农场时，第二家银行的初级抵押留置权被消除。因此，第二家银行对该农场没有有效的抵押。 （选择A）如果抵押人未经抵押权人许可出售房产，到期出售条款允许抵押权人申报全部贷款余额到期应付。此处不涉及该条款，因为农民从未出售过农场。 （选择C）当一个人通过保证契约将无主财产转让给另一个人时，契约禁止反言原则（即事后获得的所有权）适用。如果该人后来获得了该财产的所有权，该所有权将自动转移给受让人。但这一原则显然在这里不适用，因为农民在执行两项抵押贷款时拥有农场。 （选项 D）只有当买方和农民串通一气时，农民重新获得农场所有权这一事实才有意义。在这种情况下，第二家银行可以声称取消抵押品赎回权的出售是一场欺诈，旨在消除其在农场的权益。\n\n教育目标：取消抵押品赎回权的出售会破坏所有低于取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款的权益，因此初级权益持有人是必须收到取消抵押品赎回权通知的必要当事人。否则，他们的利益在售后得到保留。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 592 (2019)（要求将取消抵押品赎回权行动通知初级留置权人）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为买方根据这些事实在取消抵押品赎回权出售时购买的商品消除了第二家银行的初级抵押留置权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":39,"sourceQuestionPage":114,"sourceQuestionPages":[114],"sourceExplanationPages":[115,116],"sourceAnswerPages":[115,116],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-040","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A landowner in fee simple mortgaged the land to an investor to secure repayment of the loan she made to him. The loan was due at the end of the growing season of the year in which it was made. The landowner maintained and operated an orchard on the land, which was his sole source of income. Halfway through the growing season, the landowner experienced severe health and personal problems and, as a result, left the state; his whereabouts were unknown. The investor learned that no one was responsible for the cultivation and care of the orchard on the land. She undertook to provide, through employees, the care of the orchard and the harvest for the remainder of the growing season. The net profits were applied to the debt secured by the mortgage on the land. During the course of the harvest, the landowner's friend, a business invitee, was injured by reason of a fault in the equipment used. Under applicable tort case law, the owner of the premises would be liable for the friend's injuries. The friend brought an appropriate action against the investor to recover damages for the injuries suffered, relying on this aspect of tort law. In such lawsuit, for whom should the court render judgment?","choices":{"A":"The friend, because the investor was a mortgagee in possession.","B":"The friend, but only if the state is a title theory state, because in other jurisdictions a mortgagee has no title interest but only a lien.","C":"The investor, because she acted as agent of the landowner only to preserve her security interest.","D":"The investor, but only if the mortgage expressly provided for her taking possession in the event of danger to her security interest."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Mortgage theories\n\nLien theory Lender receives security interest in property. Mortgagor retains title & possession unless lender forecloses. (majority rule)\n\nTitle theory Lender receives legal title & mortgagor retains right of possession. Title reverts to mortgagor once debt is paid.\n\nIntermediate Mortgagor retains title & possession until default, then full title passes theory to lender without foreclosure.\n\nA mortgage is a lien against real property given to secure a debt. Under the lien theory of mortgages (adhered to by a majority of jurisdictions), a mortgage creates a security interest only and confers no right to possession of the mortgaged property. Regardless of the applicable mortgage theory, however, the mortgagee is entitled to take possession if the mortgagor abandons the mortgaged property (Choice B). A mortgagee who does so incurs liability as if he/she were the owner (eg, tort liability to anyone injured on the property). Here, the landowner mortgaged land to the investor. When the landowner abandoned the property due to severe health and personal problems, the investor rightfully took possession—regardless of whether the mortgage expressly permitted her to do so (Choice D). And since the facts stipulate that the owner of the premises would be liable for the friend's injuries, the investor is liable. The court should therefore render judgment for the friend. (Choice C) In lieu of taking possession, most mortgagees attempt to preserve their security interest by getting a court-appointed receiver (ie, agent) to manage the property. This shields the mortgagee from the liability risks of being a mortgagee in possession. But no receivership was created on these facts.\n\nEducational objective: The mortgagee can take possession of the mortgaged property if the mortgagor abandons it. Once in possession, the mortgagee incurs liability as if he/she were the owner.\n\nReferences\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 153 (2019) (specifying rights and liabilities of mortgagee in possession).\n\nEssex Cleaning Contractors, Inc. v. Amato, 317 A.2d 411, 412 (N.J. Super. 1974) (discussing tort liability of mortgagees in possession).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 4.1(c) (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (identifying mortgagee rights to take possession).","educationalObjective":"The mortgagee can take possession of the mortgaged property if the mortgagor abandons it. Once in possession, the mortgagee incurs liability as if he/she were the owner.","references":"55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 153 (2019) (specifying rights and liabilities of mortgagee in possession).\n\nEssex Cleaning Contractors, Inc. v. Amato, 317 A.2d 411, 412 (N.J. Super. 1974) (discussing tort liability of mortgagees in possession).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 4.1(c) (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (identifying mortgagee rights to take possession).","rule":"The mortgagee can take possession of the mortgaged property if the mortgagor abandons it. Once in possession, the mortgagee incurs liability as if he/she were the owner.","irac":{"issue":"In such lawsuit, for whom should the court render judgment?","rule":"The mortgagee can take possession of the mortgaged property if the mortgagor abandons it. Once in possession, the mortgagee incurs liability as if he/she were the owner.","analysis":"Mortgage theories\n\nLien theory Lender receives security interest in property. Mortgagor retains title & possession unless lender forecloses. (majority rule)\n\nTitle theory Lender receives legal title & mortgagor retains right of possession. Title reverts to mortgagor once debt is paid.\n\nIntermediate Mortgagor retains title & possession until default, then full title passes theory to lender without foreclosure.\n\nA mortgage is a lien against real property given to secure a debt. Under the lien theory of mortgages (adhered to by a majority of jurisdictions), a mortgage creates a security interest only and confers no right to possession of the mortgaged property. Regardless of the applicable mortgage theory, however, the mortgagee is entitled to take possession if the mortgagor abandons the mortgaged property (Choice B). A mortgagee who does so incurs liability as if he/she were the owner (eg, tort liability to anyone injured on the property). Here, the landowner mortgaged land to the investor. When the landowner abandoned the property due to severe health and personal problems, the investor rightfully took possession—regardless of whether the mortgage expressly permitted her to do so (Choice D). And since the facts stipulate that the owner of the premises would be liable for the friend's injuries, the investor is liable. The court should therefore render judgment for the friend. (Choice C) In lieu of taking possession, most mortgagees attempt to preserve their security interest by getting a court-appointed receiver (ie, agent) to manage the property. This shields the mortgagee from the liability risks of being a mortgagee in possession. But no receivership was created on these facts.","conclusion":"Answer A: The friend, because the investor was a mortgagee in possession."},"questionZh":"一名土地所有者将土地抵押给一名投资者，以确保偿还她向他提供的贷款。贷款应在发放当年的生长季结束时到期。土地所有者在土地上维护和经营一个果园，这是他唯一的收入来源。在生长季节进行到一半时，土地所有者出现了严重的健康和个人问题，因此离开了该州；他的下落不明。投资者了解到，没有人负责土地上果园的种植和护理。她承诺通过员工提供果园的照料和生长季节剩余时间的收获。净利润用于偿还以土地抵押担保的债务。在收割过程中，地主的朋友，一位受邀做生意的人，因使用的设备故障而受伤。根据适用的侵权判例法，房屋所有者应对朋友的伤害承担责任。这位朋友依据侵权法的这一方面，对投资者提起了适当的诉讼，要求赔偿所遭受的伤害。在这样的诉讼中，法院该判决谁呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"朋友，因为投资者是占有的抵押权人。","B":"朋友，但前提是该州是产权理论州，因为在其他司法管辖区，抵押权人没有产权权益，只有留置权。","C":"投资者，因为她充当土地所有者的代理人只是为了维护她的担保权益。","D":"投资者，但前提是抵押贷款明确规定她在其担保权益受到威胁时可以占有。"},"explanationZh":"抵押理论\n\n留置权理论 贷款人获得财产的担保权益。除非贷款人取消抵押品赎回权，否则抵押人保留所有权和占有权。 （多数决定）\n\n所有权理论 贷款人获得合法所有权，抵押人保留占有权。债务清偿后，所有权即恢复至抵押人。\n\n中级抵押人保留所有权和占有权直至违约，然后完整的所有权将理论上传递给贷款人而不会丧失抵押品赎回权。\n\n抵押贷款是为担保债务而给予不动产的留置权。根据抵押留置权理论（大多数司法管辖区都遵守），抵押仅产生担保权益，并不赋予对抵押财产的占有权。然而，无论适用的抵押理论如何，如果抵押人放弃抵押财产，抵押权人就有权占有抵押​​财产（选择 B）。这样做的抵押权人将承担责任，就像他/她是所有者一样（例如，对财产上受伤的任何人的侵权责任）。在这里，土地所有者将土地抵押给投资者。当土地所有者因严重的健康和个人问题而放弃该财产时，投资者合法地占有该财产，无论抵押贷款是否明确允许她这样做（选择 D）。而事实证明，该朋友的伤害是由业主承担的，因此投资者也应承担责任。因此，法院应作出有利于这位朋友的判决。 （选择C） 大多数抵押权人试图通过让法院指定的接管人（即代理人）来管理财产来保护其担保权益，而不是占有财产。这可以保护抵押权人免受占有抵押权人的责任风险。但并没有根据这些事实设立接管程序。\n\n教育目的：如果抵押人放弃抵押财产，抵押权人可以占有该抵押财产。一旦占有，抵押权人就如同所有者一样承担责任。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 153 (2019)（明确占有抵押权人的权利和责任）。\n\nEssex Cleaning Contractors, Inc. v. Amato, 317 A.2d 411, 412 (N.J. Super. 1974)（讨论占有抵押权人的侵权责任）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 4.1(c)（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（确定抵押权人占有的权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"抵押人放弃抵押财产的，抵押权人可以占有该抵押财产。一旦占有，抵押权人就如同所有者一样承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这样的诉讼中，法院该判决谁呢？","rule":"抵押人放弃抵押财产的，抵押权人可以占有该抵押财产。一旦占有，抵押权人就如同所有者一样承担责任。","analysis":"抵押理论\n\n留置权理论 贷款人获得财产的担保权益。除非贷款人取消抵押品赎回权，否则抵押人保留所有权和占有权。 （多数决定）\n\n所有权理论 贷款人获得合法所有权，抵押人保留占有权。债务清偿后，所有权即恢复至抵押人。\n\n中级抵押人保留所有权和占有权直至违约，然后完整的所有权将理论上传递给贷款人而不会丧失抵押品赎回权。\n\n抵押贷款是为担保债务而给予不动产的留置权。根据抵押留置权理论（大多数司法管辖区都遵守），抵押仅产生担保权益，并不赋予对抵押财产的占有权。然而，无论适用的抵押理论如何，如果抵押人放弃抵押财产，抵押权人就有权占有抵押​​财产（选择 B）。这样做的抵押权人将承担责任，就像他/她是所有者一样（例如，对财产上受伤的任何人的侵权责任）。在这里，土地所有者将土地抵押给投资者。当土地所有者因严重的健康和个人问题而放弃该财产时，投资者合法地占有该财产，无论抵押贷款是否明确允许她这样做（选择 D）。而事实证明，该朋友的伤害是由业主承担的，因此投资者也应承担责任。因此，法院应作出有利于这位朋友的判决。 （选择C） 大多数抵押权人试图通过让法院指定的接管人（即代理人）来管理财产来保护其担保权益，而不是占有财产。这可以保护抵押权人免受占有抵押权人的责任风险。但并没有根据这些事实设立接管程序。\n\n教育目的：如果抵押人放弃抵押财产，抵押权人可以占有该抵押财产。一旦占有，抵押权人就如同所有者一样承担责任。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 153 (2019)（明确占有抵押权人的权利和责任）。\n\nEssex Cleaning Contractors, Inc. v. Amato, 317 A.2d 411, 412 (N.J. Super. 1974)（讨论占有抵押权人的侵权责任）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 4.1(c)（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（确定抵押权人占有的权利）。","conclusion":"答案 A：朋友，因为投资者是占有的抵押权人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"profits","ipa":"/pɹˈɑfɪts/","syllables":"prof·its","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 收益, 红利；盈利（profit的复数）","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“profits”","wordForm":"词；原形 profit；词形 0:profit/1:s3","sourceSentence":"The net profits were applied to the debt secured by the mortgage on the land.","synonyms":["gain","net income","net","net profit"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":40,"sourceQuestionPage":117,"sourceQuestionPages":[117],"sourceExplanationPages":[118,119],"sourceAnswerPages":[118,119],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-041","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A baker borrowed funds from an investor to purchase a plot of land on which the baker planned to build a new bakery. In order to secure repayment of the loan, the baker signed a nonnegotiable promissory note and executed a mortgage to the investor. Five years later, the investor sold her promissory note to a credit union by endorsing the note and promptly delivering it to the credit union. Due to a downturn in business, the baker was unable to make payments on the mortgage. The credit union now seeks to foreclose on the baker's plot of land. If the court denies the credit union's foreclosure, what is the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The mortgage required a separate written assignment in order for the transferee- credit union to enforce the mortgage.","B":"The nonnegotiable promissory note required a separate document for assignment to the credit union, so neither the note nor the mortgage transferred.","C":"The transfer of the nonnegotiable promissory note modified the terms of the loan, resulting in a release of the baker's personal liability.","D":"The transfer of the nonnegotiable promissory note without the mortgage was void."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Transfer of promissory note & mortgage\n\nTransfer of both documents Both documents are transferred unless:\n\nTransfer of note without mortgage expressly prohibited in note/mortgage forbidden by statute or public policy or Transfer of mortgage without note* increases mortgagor's duties, burdens, risks Transfer is void\n\n*Jurisdictions are split as to effect of transfer.\n\nTo finance the purchase of real property, a borrower typically executes two documents that serve as evidence of the debt: Promissory note – a formal \"IOU\" that sets forth the terms of the loan. It is the primary evidence of the debt and is not recorded in the deed records.\n\nMortgage (or deed of trust) – a lien that secures the loan by attaching the debt to a real property interest and providing a means of enforcement (eg, foreclosure). It is recorded in the deed records to provide notice of an outstanding debt attached to the real property.\n\nA promissory note can be assigned to another (an assignee) independent of the mortgage (Choice D). The mortgage automatically transfers with the note once the note has been properly assigned (unless the parties agree otherwise) (Choice A). A negotiable promissory note can be assigned by simply endorsing and delivering the note to the assignee. However, a nonnegotiable promissory note requires a separate assignment document to transfer ownership. Here, the investor sought to assign her nonnegotiable promissory note by endorsing and delivering it to the credit union. However, this type of promissory note requires that a separate document be executed to assign this interest. The investor failed to execute such a document, so the note (and therefore the accompanying mortgage) did not transfer to the credit union. And since the credit union has no security interest in the land, it cannot initiate foreclosure proceedings against the baker. (Choice C) A transfer of the mortgagee's interest in the promissory note changes the party who receives payment on the note, but it does not change the terms of the mortgagor's obligation. Therefore, the transfer of the note did not modify the terms of the loan or release the baker's personal liability.\n\nEducational objective: Although a negotiable promissory note can be transferred by endorsing and delivering the note to another, a nonnegotiable promissory note requires that a separate document of assignment be executed to transfer ownership.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (transfer of mortgages).","educationalObjective":"Although a negotiable promissory note can be transferred by endorsing and delivering the note to another, a nonnegotiable promissory note requires that a separate document of assignment be executed to transfer ownership.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (transfer of mortgages).","rule":"Although a negotiable promissory note can be transferred by endorsing and delivering the note to another, a nonnegotiable promissory note requires that a separate document of assignment be executed to transfer ownership.","irac":{"issue":"If the court denies the credit union's foreclosure, what is the most likely reason?","rule":"Although a negotiable promissory note can be transferred by endorsing and delivering the note to another, a nonnegotiable promissory note requires that a separate document of assignment be executed to transfer ownership.","analysis":"Transfer of promissory note & mortgage\n\nTransfer of both documents Both documents are transferred unless:\n\nTransfer of note without mortgage expressly prohibited in note/mortgage forbidden by statute or public policy or Transfer of mortgage without note* increases mortgagor's duties, burdens, risks Transfer is void\n\n*Jurisdictions are split as to effect of transfer.\n\nTo finance the purchase of real property, a borrower typically executes two documents that serve as evidence of the debt: Promissory note – a formal \"IOU\" that sets forth the terms of the loan. It is the primary evidence of the debt and is not recorded in the deed records.\n\nMortgage (or deed of trust) – a lien that secures the loan by attaching the debt to a real property interest and providing a means of enforcement (eg, foreclosure). It is recorded in the deed records to provide notice of an outstanding debt attached to the real property.\n\nA promissory note can be assigned to another (an assignee) independent of the mortgage (Choice D). The mortgage automatically transfers with the note once the note has been properly assigned (unless the parties agree otherwise) (Choice A). A negotiable promissory note can be assigned by simply endorsing and delivering the note to the assignee. However, a nonnegotiable promissory note requires a separate assignment document to transfer ownership. Here, the investor sought to assign her nonnegotiable promissory note by endorsing and delivering it to the credit union. However, this type of promissory note requires that a separate document be executed to assign this interest. The investor failed to execute such a document, so the note (and therefore the accompanying mortgage) did not transfer to the credit union. And since the credit union has no security interest in the land, it cannot initiate foreclosure proceedings against the baker. (Choice C) A transfer of the mortgagee's interest in the promissory note changes the party who receives payment on the note, but it does not change the terms of the mortgagor's obligation. Therefore, the transfer of the note did not modify the terms of the loan or release the baker's personal liability.","conclusion":"Answer B: The nonnegotiable promissory note required a separate document for assignment to the credit union, so neither the note nor the mortgage transferred."},"questionZh":"一位面包师向一位投资者借钱购买了一块土地，计划在上面建造一家新面包店。为了确保偿还贷款，面包师签署了不可转让的本票并向投资者执行抵押。五年后，投资者通过背书并立即将其交付给信用社，将她的本票卖给了信用社。由于生意不景气，面包师无力偿还抵押贷款。信用合作社现在试图取消面包师土地的赎回权。如果法院否认信用合作社取消抵押品赎回权，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"抵押贷款需要一份单独的书面转让，以便受让人——信用合作社执行抵押贷款。","B":"不可转让本票需要一份单独的文件才能转让给信用合作社，因此票据和抵押贷款都没有转让。","C":"不可流通本票的转让修改了贷款条款，从而免除了面包师的个人责任。","D":"没有抵押的不可转让本票的转让无效。"},"explanationZh":"本票及抵押的转让\n\n两份文件的转让 两份文件均被转让，除非：\n\n票据中明确禁止转让无抵押的票据/法律或公共政策禁止的抵押或转让无票据*的抵押会增加抵押人的义务、负担和风险 转让无效\n\n*司法管辖区因转让的影响而有所不同。\n\n为了为购买不动产提供资金，借款人通常会签署两份文件作为债务证据： 本票 - 规定贷款条款的正式“借据”。它是债务的主要证据，不记录在契约记录中。\n\n抵押（或信托契约）——通过将债务附加到不动产权益并提供强制执行手段（例如取消抵押品赎回权）来确保贷款的留置权。它被记录在契约记录中，以提供与不动产相关的未偿债务的通知。\n\n本票可以转让给独立于抵押贷款的另一人（受让人）（选择 D）。一旦票据被正确转让（除非双方另有约定），抵押权将自动随票据一起转移（选择 A）。可转让本票只需背书并将票据交付给受让人即可转让。但是，不可转让本票需要单独的转让文件来转让所有权。在这里，投资者试图通过背书并将其交付给信用合作社来转让其不可流通的本票。然而，这种类型的本票需要签署一份单独的文件来转让该利息。投资者未能签署此类文件，因此票据（以及随附的抵押贷款）没有转移到信用合作社。由于信用合作社在土地上没有担保权益，因此它无法对面包师提起取消抵押品赎回权的诉讼。 （选择C） 抵押权人在本票中的权益的转让改变了票据付款的接收方，但不改变抵押人的义务条款。因此，票据的转让并没有修改贷款条款或免除面包师的个人责任。\n\n教育目标：虽然可转让本票可以通过背书并将票据交付给他人来转让，但不可转让本票需要签署单独的转让文件来转让所有权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款转让）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"虽然可转让本票可以通过背书并将票据交付给他人来转让，但不可转让本票需要签署单独的转让文件来转让所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院否认信用合作社取消抵押品赎回权，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"虽然可转让本票可以通过背书并将票据交付给他人来转让，但不可转让本票需要签署单独的转让文件来转让所有权。","analysis":"本票及抵押的转让\n\n两份文件的转让 两份文件均被转让，除非：\n\n票据中明确禁止转让无抵押的票据/法律或公共政策禁止的抵押或转让无票据*的抵押会增加抵押人的义务、负担和风险 转让无效\n\n*司法管辖区因转让的影响而有所不同。\n\n为了为购买不动产提供资金，借款人通常会签署两份文件作为债务证据： 本票 - 规定贷款条款的正式“借据”。它是债务的主要证据，不记录在契约记录中。\n\n抵押（或信托契约）——通过将债务附加到不动产权益并提供强制执行手段（例如取消抵押品赎回权）来确保贷款的留置权。它被记录在契约记录中，以提供与不动产相关的未偿债务的通知。\n\n本票可以转让给独立于抵押贷款的另一人（受让人）（选择 D）。一旦票据被正确转让（除非双方另有约定），抵押权将自动随票据一起转移（选择 A）。可转让本票只需背书并将票据交付给受让人即可转让。但是，不可转让本票需要单独的转让文件来转让所有权。在这里，投资者试图通过背书并将其交付给信用合作社来转让其不可流通的本票。然而，这种类型的本票需要签署一份单独的文件来转让该利息。投资者未能签署此类文件，因此票据（以及随附的抵押贷款）没有转移到信用合作社。由于信用合作社在土地上没有担保权益，因此它无法对面包师提起取消抵押品赎回权的诉讼。 （选择C） 抵押权人在本票中的权益的转让改变了票据付款的接收方，但不改变抵押人的义务条款。因此，票据的转让并没有修改贷款条款或免除面包师的个人责任。\n\n教育目标：虽然可转让本票可以通过背书并将票据交付给他人来转让，但不可转让本票需要签署单独的转让文件来转让所有权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款转让）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可转让本票需要一份单独的文件才能转让给信用合作社，因此票据和抵押贷款都没有转让。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":41,"sourceQuestionPage":120,"sourceQuestionPages":[120],"sourceExplanationPages":[121,122],"sourceAnswerPages":[121,122],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-042","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man decided to give a cabin he owned to his daughter at his death. To accomplish this goal, he delivered to his attorney a deed that fully complied with the applicable statute of frauds and told his attorney to record the deed when he died unless he later gave the attorney instructions to the contrary. Three weeks after dropping off the deed, the man properly drafted and executed his own will, which left all of his real property to his son. One year later, the man died, and the attorney immediately recorded the deed. At the time of the man's death, the cabin was titled in his name and he owned no other real property. The daughter and the son now disagree as to who is entitled to ownership of the cabin. Other than the jurisdiction's statute of frauds and statute of wills, there are no applicable statutes. Who is entitled to ownership of the cabin?","choices":{"A":"The daughter, because the attorney was, for gift-law purposes, a trustee for the daughter.","B":"The daughter, because the deed fully complied with the statute of frauds.","C":"The son, because the deed was not delivered to the daughter during the man's lifetime.","D":"The son, because the proper execution of the will revoked the earlier gift to the daughter."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Presumed delivery of deed\n\nPhysical delivery (eg, by hand or mail) Recording Unconditional delivery to agent Grantee obtains possession of deed A deed is a document that transfers ownership of real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee) once it has been: delivered – evidenced by the grantor's intent to presently convey ownership to the grantee (ie, the grantor must not retain a right to recover the deed) and accepted – presumed when the transfer benefits the grantee. Delivery is presumed if the deed is recorded (ie, filed in official land records). But this presumption can be rebutted by evidence that the grantor did not intend to presently transfer ownership. And if the deed is not delivered during the grantor's lifetime, it is void and conveys no interest. Here, the attorney recorded the deed (presumed delivery). But the man retained the right to recover the deed by instructing the attorney to record it upon the man's death unless he gave instructions to the contrary (presumption rebutted). Therefore, the deed was not delivered to the daughter during his lifetime. And since the deed is void, the son (as the beneficiary under the man's will) is entitled to ownership of the cabin. (Choice A) Since the man retained a right to recover the deed (no present intent to transfer), the son owns the property. But had the man relinquished that right when he gave the deed to the attorney (intending to presently transfer ownership), the attorney would have been the daughter's trustee—ie, her fiduciary who would have been legally required to hold the deed for her benefit until the man's death. (Choice B) A deed must comply with the statute of frauds—ie, be in writing and signed by the party to be bound. But to effectively transfer ownership of property, the deed must also be delivered during the grantor's lifetime (and accepted by the grantee). (Choice D) A properly executed will does not revoke an earlier gift that has been both delivered and accepted. That is because the testator (person who made the will) no longer owns the gifted property at death. Therefore, the son is not entitled to ownership of the cabin on this basis.\n\nEducational objective: To effectively transfer ownership of real property, a deed must be delivered during the grantor's lifetime (and then accepted by the grantee). There is no delivery if the grantor retains a right to recover the deed.","educationalObjective":"To effectively transfer ownership of real property, a deed must be delivered during the grantor's lifetime (and then accepted by the grantee). There is no delivery if the grantor retains a right to recover the deed.","references":"","rule":"To effectively transfer ownership of real property, a deed must be delivered during the grantor's lifetime (and then accepted by the grantee). There is no delivery if the grantor retains a right to recover the deed.","irac":{"issue":"Who is entitled to ownership of the cabin?","rule":"To effectively transfer ownership of real property, a deed must be delivered during the grantor's lifetime (and then accepted by the grantee). There is no delivery if the grantor retains a right to recover the deed.","analysis":"Presumed delivery of deed\n\nPhysical delivery (eg, by hand or mail) Recording Unconditional delivery to agent Grantee obtains possession of deed A deed is a document that transfers ownership of real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee) once it has been: delivered – evidenced by the grantor's intent to presently convey ownership to the grantee (ie, the grantor must not retain a right to recover the deed) and accepted – presumed when the transfer benefits the grantee. Delivery is presumed if the deed is recorded (ie, filed in official land records). But this presumption can be rebutted by evidence that the grantor did not intend to presently transfer ownership. And if the deed is not delivered during the grantor's lifetime, it is void and conveys no interest. Here, the attorney recorded the deed (presumed delivery). But the man retained the right to recover the deed by instructing the attorney to record it upon the man's death unless he gave instructions to the contrary (presumption rebutted). Therefore, the deed was not delivered to the daughter during his lifetime. And since the deed is void, the son (as the beneficiary under the man's will) is entitled to ownership of the cabin. (Choice A) Since the man retained a right to recover the deed (no present intent to transfer), the son owns the property. But had the man relinquished that right when he gave the deed to the attorney (intending to presently transfer ownership), the attorney would have been the daughter's trustee—ie, her fiduciary who would have been legally required to hold the deed for her benefit until the man's death. (Choice B) A deed must comply with the statute of frauds—ie, be in writing and signed by the party to be bound. But to effectively transfer ownership of property, the deed must also be delivered during the grantor's lifetime (and accepted by the grantee). (Choice D) A properly executed will does not revoke an earlier gift that has been both delivered and accepted. That is because the testator (person who made the will) no longer owns the gifted property at death. Therefore, the son is not entitled to ownership of the cabin on this basis.","conclusion":"Answer C: The son, because the deed was not delivered to the daughter during the man's lifetime."},"questionZh":"一名男子决定在去世后将自己拥有的一间小屋赠​​予女儿。为了实现这一目标，他向律师提交了一份完全符合适用的欺诈法规的契约，并告诉他的律师在他去世时记录该契约，除非他后来向律师发出相反的指示。交付契约三周后，该男子妥善起草并执行了自己的遗嘱，将所有不动产留给了儿子。一年后，该男子去世，律师立即记录了这件事。该男子去世时，该小屋的所有权属于他的名下，他没有其他不动产。女儿和儿子现在对于谁有权拥有小屋的所有权存在分歧。除管辖区的欺诈法规和遗嘱法规外，没有适用的法规。谁有权拥有小屋的所有权？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿，因为就赠与法而言，律师是女儿的受托人。","B":"女儿，因为该行为完全符合欺诈法。","C":"儿子，因为该男子生前并未将房产证交付给女儿。","D":"儿子，因为遗嘱的正确执行，撤销了之前给女儿的礼物。"},"explanationZh":"推定交付契据\n\n实物交付（例如，通过专人或邮寄） 记录 无条件交付给代理人 受让人获得契据的占有权 契据是一种将不动产所有权从所有者（设保人）转让给另一方（受让人）的文件，一旦它： 交付 – 以设保人目前将所有权转让给受让人的意图证明（即，设保人不得保留收回契据的权利）并接受 – 假定转让使受让人受益。如果契约被记录（即在官方土地记录中归档），则推定已交付。但这一推定可以通过设保人目前不打算转让所有权的证据来反驳。如果契约没有在设保人在世期间交付，则该契约无效并且不产生任何利息。在这里，律师记录了契约（假定交付）。但该男子保留通过指示律师在该男子死亡时记录该契约来收回该契约的权利，除非他发出相反的指示（推定被反驳）。因此，该房产证并未在他生前交付给女儿。由于契约无效，儿子（作为男子遗嘱的受益人）有权获得小屋的所有权。 （选择 A）由于该男子保留收回契约的权利（目前没有转让意图），因此儿子拥有该财产。但是，如果该男子在将契约交给律师时放弃了这一权利（打算目前转让所有权），那么律师将成为女儿的受托人，即她的受托人，法律上将要求他为她的利益持有契约，直到该男子去世为止。 （选择 B）契约必须符合欺诈法规，即采用书面形式并由受约束方签署。但为了有效地转让财产所有权，契约还必须在授予人的一生中交付（并被受让人接受）。 （选项 D）正确执行的遗嘱不会撤销先前已交付并接受的礼物。这是因为立遗嘱人（立遗嘱的人）死后不再拥有赠与财产。因此，儿子并不能据此获得小屋的所有权。\n\n教育目标：为了有效地转让不动产所有权，契约必须在授予人的一生中交付（然后由受让人接受）。如果设保人保留收回契约的权利，则不进行交付。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为了有效地转让不动产所有权，契约必须在授予人的一生中交付（然后由受让人接受）。如果设保人保留收回契约的权利，则不进行交付。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有权拥有小屋的所有权？","rule":"为了有效地转让不动产所有权，契约必须在授予人的一生中交付（然后由受让人接受）。如果设保人保留收回契约的权利，则不进行交付。","analysis":"推定交付契据\n\n实物交付（例如，通过专人或邮寄） 记录 无条件交付给代理人 受让人获得契据的占有权 契据是一种将不动产所有权从所有者（设保人）转让给另一方（受让人）的文件，一旦它： 交付 – 以设保人目前将所有权转让给受让人的意图证明（即，设保人不得保留收回契据的权利）并接受 – 假定转让使受让人受益。如果契约被记录（即在官方土地记录中归档），则推定已交付。但这一推定可以通过设保人目前不打算转让所有权的证据来反驳。如果契约没有在设保人在世期间交付，则该契约无效并且不产生任何利息。在这里，律师记录了契约（假定交付）。但该男子保留通过指示律师在该男子死亡时记录该契约来收回该契约的权利，除非他发出相反的指示（推定被反驳）。因此，该房产证并未在他生前交付给女儿。由于契约无效，儿子（作为男子遗嘱的受益人）有权获得小屋的所有权。 （选择 A）由于该男子保留收回契约的权利（目前没有转让意图），因此儿子拥有该财产。但是，如果该男子在将契约交给律师时放弃了这一权利（打算目前转让所有权），那么律师将成为女儿的受托人，即她的受托人，法律上将要求他为她的利益持有契约，直到该男子去世为止。 （选择 B）契约必须符合欺诈法规，即采用书面形式并由受约束方签署。但为了有效地转让财产所有权，契约还必须在授予人的一生中交付（并被受让人接受）。 （选项 D）正确执行的遗嘱不会撤销先前已交付并接受的礼物。这是因为立遗嘱人（立遗嘱的人）死后不再拥有赠与财产。因此，儿子并不能据此获得小屋的所有权。\n\n教育目标：为了有效地转让不动产所有权，契约必须在授予人的一生中交付（然后由受让人接受）。如果设保人保留收回契约的权利，则不进行交付。","conclusion":"答案 C：儿子，因为该男子生前并未将房产证交付给女儿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"delivery of deed","ipa":"/dɪlˈɪvɝi ˈəv dˈid/","syllables":"de·liv·ery of deed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"契据交付：出让人以当前转让产权的意思使契据发生效力","contextualMeaning":"契据交付：出让人以当前转让产权的意思使契据发生效力","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Presumed delivery of deed Physical delivery (eg, by hand or mail) Recording Unconditional delivery to agent Grantee obtains possession of deed A deed is a document that transfers ownership of real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee) once it has been: delivered – evidenced by the grantor's intent to presently convey ownership to the grantee (ie, the grantor must not retain a right to recover the dee","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":42,"sourceQuestionPage":123,"sourceQuestionPages":[123],"sourceExplanationPages":[124,125],"sourceAnswerPages":[124,125],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-043","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A woman who owned a house executed a deed purporting to convey the house to her son and his wife. The language of the deed was sufficient to create a common law joint tenancy with right of survivorship, which is unmodified by statute in the jurisdiction. The woman mailed the deed to the son with a letter saying: \"Because I intend you and your wife to have my house after my death, I am enclosing a deed to the house. However, I intend to live in the house for the rest of my life, so don't record the deed until I die. The deed will be effective at my death.\" The son put the deed in his desk. The wife discovered the deed and recorded it without the son's knowledge. Subsequently, the son and the wife separated, and the wife, without telling anyone, conveyed her interest in the house to a friend who immediately reconveyed it to the wife. The woman learned that the son and the wife had separated and also learned what had happened to the deed to the house. The woman then brought an appropriate action against the son and the wife to obtain a declaration that the woman was still the owner of the house and an order canceling of record the woman's deed and the subsequent deeds. If the court determines that the woman owns the house in fee simple, what will be the likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"The deed was not delivered.","B":"The wife's conduct entitles the woman to equitable relief.","C":"The woman expressly reserved a life estate.","D":"The woman received no consideration for her deed."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Presumed delivery of deed\n\nPhysical delivery (eg, by hand or mail) Recording Unconditional delivery to agent Grantee obtains possession of deed A deed is a legal instrument that transfers an interest in real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee) once it has been: delivered – evidenced by the grantor's intent to presently transfer an interest to the grantee, even if the interest itself is a future interest (ie, the possessory rights transfer later) and accepted – presumed if the transfer benefits the grantee. Recording a deed (ie, depositing it in official land records) creates a presumption of delivery. But this presumption can be rebutted by evidence that the grantor did not intend to presently transfer an interest to the grantee—eg, if the transfer was contingent upon a future event. Here, delivery is presumed since the wife recorded the deed. But that presumption can be rebutted since the woman mailed the deed with a letter explaining that she only wanted the son and wife to have her house after her death (transfer contingent upon future event). Therefore, the court will likely determine that the woman owns the house in fee simple because the deed was not delivered. (Choice B) A court may grant an injured party equitable relief (eg, rescinding or reforming a deed) to prevent unjust enrichment of a wrongdoer when other remedies are insufficient. Here, the wife was likely a wrongdoer since she recorded and conveyed the deed without the son's knowledge. But the court can hold that the woman owns the property since the deed was not delivered (sufficient remedy), so equitable relief is not needed. (Choice C) A life estate is a possessory interest in land that is limited in duration by a life (eg, \"to A for life\"). Here, the woman attempted to reserve a life estate in her letter—not in the deed. But had she reserved a life estate in the deed, she would not own the home in fee simple since her ownership interest would terminate upon her death. (Choice D) Consideration (eg, monetary payment) is not needed to create a valid deed or to effectively transfer a property interest to another.\n\nEducational objective: To transfer an interest in real property, a deed must be delivered (and accepted). Delivery occurs when the grantor intends to presently transfer an interest to the grantee. Therefore, delivery is ineffective if the transfer is contingent upon a future event.","educationalObjective":"To transfer an interest in real property, a deed must be delivered (and accepted). Delivery occurs when the grantor intends to presently transfer an interest to the grantee. Therefore, delivery is ineffective if the transfer is contingent upon a future event.","references":"","rule":"To transfer an interest in real property, a deed must be delivered (and accepted). Delivery occurs when the grantor intends to presently transfer an interest to the grantee. Therefore, delivery is ineffective if the transfer is contingent upon a future event.","irac":{"issue":"If the court determines that the woman owns the house in fee simple, what will be the likely explanation?","rule":"To transfer an interest in real property, a deed must be delivered (and accepted). Delivery occurs when the grantor intends to presently transfer an interest to the grantee. Therefore, delivery is ineffective if the transfer is contingent upon a future event.","analysis":"Presumed delivery of deed\n\nPhysical delivery (eg, by hand or mail) Recording Unconditional delivery to agent Grantee obtains possession of deed A deed is a legal instrument that transfers an interest in real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee) once it has been: delivered – evidenced by the grantor's intent to presently transfer an interest to the grantee, even if the interest itself is a future interest (ie, the possessory rights transfer later) and accepted – presumed if the transfer benefits the grantee. Recording a deed (ie, depositing it in official land records) creates a presumption of delivery. But this presumption can be rebutted by evidence that the grantor did not intend to presently transfer an interest to the grantee—eg, if the transfer was contingent upon a future event. Here, delivery is presumed since the wife recorded the deed. But that presumption can be rebutted since the woman mailed the deed with a letter explaining that she only wanted the son and wife to have her house after her death (transfer contingent upon future event). Therefore, the court will likely determine that the woman owns the house in fee simple because the deed was not delivered. (Choice B) A court may grant an injured party equitable relief (eg, rescinding or reforming a deed) to prevent unjust enrichment of a wrongdoer when other remedies are insufficient. Here, the wife was likely a wrongdoer since she recorded and conveyed the deed without the son's knowledge. But the court can hold that the woman owns the property since the deed was not delivered (sufficient remedy), so equitable relief is not needed. (Choice C) A life estate is a possessory interest in land that is limited in duration by a life (eg, \"to A for life\"). Here, the woman attempted to reserve a life estate in her letter—not in the deed. But had she reserved a life estate in the deed, she would not own the home in fee simple since her ownership interest would terminate upon her death. (Choice D) Consideration (eg, monetary payment) is not needed to create a valid deed or to effectively transfer a property interest to another.","conclusion":"Answer A: The deed was not delivered."},"questionZh":"一名拥有房屋的妇女签署了一份契约，旨在将房屋转让给她的儿子和他的妻子。契约的措辞足以建立具有生存权的普通法联合共有权，且该权利未经司法管辖区的法规修改。该妇女将房产契邮寄给儿子，并附上一封信，上面写着：“因为我想让你和你的妻子在我死后拥有我的房子，所以我附上一份房子的房产证。不过，我打算余生都住在这所房子里，所以在我死之前不要记录房产证。房产证将在我死后生效。”儿子把契约放在办公桌上。妻子发现了这一行为，并在儿子不知情的情况下将其记录下来。随后，儿子和妻子分居了，妻子在没有告诉任何人的情况下，将她对这所房子的兴趣转达给了一位朋友，朋友立即将其重新转给了妻子。这名女子得知儿子和妻子已经分居，也得知了房屋契约的情况。然后，该妇女对儿子和妻子提起适当的诉讼，以获得一份声明，表明该妇女仍然是房屋的所有者，并下令取消该妇女的契约和随后的契约记录。如果法院判定该妇女拥有该房屋的所有权，可能的解释是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"契约没有交付。","B":"妻子的行为使妇女有权获得公平的救济。","C":"该女子明确保留了终身财产。","D":"该女子的行为没有得到任何考虑。"},"explanationZh":"推定交付契据\n\n实物交付（例如，通过专人或邮寄） 记录 无条件交付给代理人 受让人获得契据的占有权 契据是一种法律文书，将不动产权益从所有者（设保人）转让给另一方（受让人），一旦它： 交付 – 由设保人目前向受让人转让权益的意图证明，即使该权益本身是未来权益（即以后的占有权转让）并且被接受 – 假定转让对受让人有利。记录契约（即，将其存入官方土地记录）会产生交付的推定。但这一推定可以通过以下证据来反驳：设保人目前并不打算将权益转让给受让人——例如，如果转让取决于未来的事件。在这里，由于妻子记录了该行为，因此推定分娩。但这一推定可以被反驳，因为该妇女在邮寄契约时附上了一封信，解释说她只想让儿子和妻子在她死后拥有她的房子（根据未来事件的转移而定）。因此，法院可能会因为契约未交付而判定该妇女拥有该房屋的所有权。 （选择B）当其他补救措施不充分时，法院可以给予受害方公平救济（例如，撤销或修改契约），以防止不法行为者获得不公正的利益。在这里，妻子很可能是违法者，因为她在儿子不知情的情况下记录并传达了这一行为。但法院可以认为该妇女拥有该财产，因为契约没有交付（充分的补救措施），因此不需要公平救济。 （选项C） 终身财产是对土地的占有权益，其期限受终生限制（例如，“终身归A”）。在这里，这位女士试图在她的信中而不是在契约中保留终身财产。但是，如果她在契约中保留了终身财产，她将不会简单地拥有该房屋，因为她的所有权权益将在她去世时终止。 （选择D） 订立有效契约或将财产权益有效转让给他人不需要考虑（例如金钱支付）。\n\n教育目标：要转让不动产权益，必须交付（并接受）契约。当授予人打算立即将权益转让给受让人时，即发生交付。因此，如果转移取决于未来事件，则交付无效。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要转让不动产权益，必须交付（并接受）契约。当授予人打算立即将权益转让给受让人时，即发生交付。因此，如果转移取决于未来事件，则交付无效。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院判定该妇女拥有该房屋的所有权，可能的解释是什么？","rule":"要转让不动产权益，必须交付（并接受）契约。当授予人打算立即将权益转让给受让人时，即发生交付。因此，如果转移取决于未来事件，则交付无效。","analysis":"推定交付契据\n\n实物交付（例如，通过专人或邮寄） 记录 无条件交付给代理人 受让人获得契据的占有权 契据是一种法律文书，将不动产权益从所有者（设保人）转让给另一方（受让人），一旦它： 交付 – 由设保人目前向受让人转让权益的意图证明，即使该权益本身是未来权益（即以后的占有权转让）并且被接受 – 假定转让对受让人有利。记录契约（即，将其存入官方土地记录）会产生交付的推定。但这一推定可以通过以下证据来反驳：设保人目前并不打算将权益转让给受让人——例如，如果转让取决于未来的事件。在这里，由于妻子记录了该行为，因此推定分娩。但这一推定可以被反驳，因为该妇女在邮寄契约时附上了一封信，解释说她只想让儿子和妻子在她死后拥有她的房子（根据未来事件的转移而定）。因此，法院可能会因为契约未交付而判定该妇女拥有该房屋的所有权。 （选择B）当其他补救措施不充分时，法院可以给予受害方公平救济（例如，撤销或修改契约），以防止不法行为者获得不公正的利益。在这里，妻子很可能是违法者，因为她在儿子不知情的情况下记录并传达了这一行为。但法院可以认为该妇女拥有该财产，因为契约没有交付（充分的补救措施），因此不需要公平救济。 （选项C） 终身财产是对土地的占有权益，其期限受终生限制（例如，“终身归A”）。在这里，这位女士试图在她的信中而不是在契约中保留终身财产。但是，如果她在契约中保留了终身财产，她将不会简单地拥有该房屋，因为她的所有权权益将在她去世时终止。 （选择D） 订立有效契约或将财产权益有效转让给他人不需要考虑（例如金钱支付）。\n\n教育目标：要转让不动产权益，必须交付（并接受）契约。当授予人打算立即将权益转让给受让人时，即发生交付。因此，如果转移取决于未来事件，则交付无效。","conclusion":"答案 A：契约没有交付。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"life estate","ipa":"/lˈaɪf ɪstˈeɪt/","syllables":"life es·tate","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"终身地产：以特定人生存期间为期限的占有性土地权益","contextualMeaning":"终身地产：以特定人生存期间为期限的占有性土地权益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The woman expressly reserved a life estate.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"contingent","ipa":"/kʌntˈɪndʒənt/","syllables":"con·tin·gent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 或许发生的, 附随的, 偶然发生的；n. 偶然事件, 分遣队","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“contingent”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:contingents","sourceSentence":"Therefore, delivery is ineffective if the transfer is contingent upon a future event.","synonyms":["detail","contingent on","contingent upon","dependent on"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":43,"sourceQuestionPage":126,"sourceQuestionPages":[126],"sourceExplanationPages":[127,128],"sourceAnswerPages":[127,128],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-044","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man decided to give his farm to his nephew. The man took a deed to his attorney and told the attorney to deliver the deed to the nephew upon the man's death. The man also told the attorney to return the deed to him if he asked. None of these instructions to the attorney were in writing, and the deed was not recorded. The man then e-mailed the nephew informing him of the arrangement. Shortly thereafter, the nephew died testate. In his will, he devised the farm to his daughter. Several years later, the man died intestate, survived by two sons. The nephew's daughter immediately claimed ownership of the farm and demanded that the attorney deliver the deed to her. Must the attorney deliver the deed to the nephew's daughter?","choices":{"A":"No, because a gratuitous death escrow is void unless supported by a written contract.","B":"No, because the man never placed the deed beyond his control.","C":"Yes, because the death of the nephew rendered the gratuitous death escrow irrevocable by the man.","D":"Yes, because the deed to the nephew was legally delivered when the man took it to his attorney."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A deed effectively transfers ownership of a landowner's (grantor's) real property to another (grantee) when it is delivered to, and accepted by, the grantee. To deliver a deed through a valid death escrow, the grantor must: give the deed to an escrow agent (eg, attorney) with instructions to transfer it to the grantee upon the grantor's death and relinquish the right to take back the deed (ie, by placing the deed beyond his/her control). Here, the man attempted to deliver a deed to the farm through a death escrow. Though he gave his attorney the deed with instructions to deliver it to the nephew upon the man's death, the man explicitly retained the right to have the deed returned to him if he asked. Since the man never placed the deed beyond his control, the death escrow was invalid (no delivery). As a result, the nephew never acquired ownership of the farm and could not devise it to his daughter. And since she has no interest in the farm, the attorney need not deliver the deed to her. (Choice A) Since most death escrows are gratuitous, a contract (written or oral) is not required. Instead, the death escrow was void because the man retained the right to take back the deed. (Choice C) A gratuitous death escrow becomes irrevocable once the grantor delivers the deed—not when the grantee dies. (Choice D) Delivery is presumed when a deed is given to the grantee directly. But when the deed is given to an escrow agent, there is no delivery unless the grantor relinquishes his/her right to recover it. And since the man retained the right to have the deed returned, giving it to his attorney (escrow agent) did not constitute delivery.\n\nEducational objective: A deed is delivered through a valid death escrow when (1) the deed is given to an escrow agent with instructions to transfer it to the grantee upon the grantor's death, and (2) the grantor relinquishes the right to take back the deed.","educationalObjective":"A deed is delivered through a valid death escrow when (1) the deed is given to an escrow agent with instructions to transfer it to the grantee upon the grantor's death, and (2) the grantor relinquishes the right to take back the deed.","references":"","rule":"A deed is delivered through a valid death escrow when (1) the deed is given to an escrow agent with instructions to transfer it to the grantee upon the grantor's death, and (2) the grantor relinquishes the right to take back the deed.","irac":{"issue":"Must the attorney deliver the deed to the nephew's daughter?","rule":"A deed is delivered through a valid death escrow when (1) the deed is given to an escrow agent with instructions to transfer it to the grantee upon the grantor's death, and (2) the grantor relinquishes the right to take back the deed.","analysis":"A deed effectively transfers ownership of a landowner's (grantor's) real property to another (grantee) when it is delivered to, and accepted by, the grantee. To deliver a deed through a valid death escrow, the grantor must: give the deed to an escrow agent (eg, attorney) with instructions to transfer it to the grantee upon the grantor's death and relinquish the right to take back the deed (ie, by placing the deed beyond his/her control). Here, the man attempted to deliver a deed to the farm through a death escrow. Though he gave his attorney the deed with instructions to deliver it to the nephew upon the man's death, the man explicitly retained the right to have the deed returned to him if he asked. Since the man never placed the deed beyond his control, the death escrow was invalid (no delivery). As a result, the nephew never acquired ownership of the farm and could not devise it to his daughter. And since she has no interest in the farm, the attorney need not deliver the deed to her. (Choice A) Since most death escrows are gratuitous, a contract (written or oral) is not required. Instead, the death escrow was void because the man retained the right to take back the deed. (Choice C) A gratuitous death escrow becomes irrevocable once the grantor delivers the deed—not when the grantee dies. (Choice D) Delivery is presumed when a deed is given to the grantee directly. But when the deed is given to an escrow agent, there is no delivery unless the grantor relinquishes his/her right to recover it. And since the man retained the right to have the deed returned, giving it to his attorney (escrow agent) did not constitute delivery.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the man never placed the deed beyond his control."},"questionZh":"一个男人决定把他的农场交给他的侄子。该男子将一份契约交给了他的律师，并告诉律师在该男子去世后将契约交给他的侄子。该男子还告诉律师，如果他要求的话，就把房产证归还给他。这些给律师的指示都不是书面的，也没有记录该契约。该男子随后给侄子发了电子邮件，告知他这一安排。此后不久，侄子去世时留下了遗嘱。在他的遗嘱中，他为女儿设计了这座农场。几年后，该男子去世时没有立下遗嘱，留下了两个儿子。侄子的女儿立即声称拥有农场的所有权，并要求律师将地契交给她。律师必须将契约交给侄子的女儿吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为除非有书面合同支持，否则无偿死亡托管无效。","B":"不，因为这个人从来没有让这件事超出他的控制范围。","C":"是的，因为侄子的死亡使得该男子无法撤销无偿死亡托管。","D":"是的，因为当该男子将契约交给他的律师时，该契约已合法交付给侄子。"},"explanationZh":"当契约交付给受让人并被受让人接受时，它就有效地将土地所有者（授予人）的不动产所有权转让给另一个人（受让人）。要通过有效的死亡托管交付契据，设保人必须： 将契据交给托管代理人（例如律师），并指示在设保人死亡后将契据转让给受让人，并放弃收回契据的权利（即，将契据置于其控制范围之外）。在这里，该男子试图通过死亡托管将一份契约交付给农场。尽管他向律师提供了该契约，并指示在该男子去世后将其交给侄子，但该男子明​​确保留如果他提出要求，可以将契约归还给他的权利。由于该男子从未将契约置于他的控制之外，因此死亡托管无效（没有交付）。结果，侄子从未获得农场的所有权，也无法将其设计给他的女儿。由于她对农场没有兴趣，律师不需要将契约交给她。 （选择 A）由于大多数死亡托管是无偿的，因此不需要合同（书面或口头）。相反，死亡托管无效，因为该男子保留收回契约的权利。 （选择 C）一旦授予人交付契约，无偿死亡托管就变得不可撤销，而不是在受让人死亡时。 （选项 D）当契约直接交给受让人时，即推定交付。但是，当契约被交给托管代理人时，除非授予人放弃其收回契约的权利，否则不会进行交付。由于该男子保留要求归还契约的权利，因此将契约交给他的律师（托管代理人）并不构成交付。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，契约通过有效的死亡托管交付：(1) 将契约交给托管代理人，并指示在授予人死亡后将其转让给受让人，并且 (2) 授予人放弃收回契约的权利。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在以下情况下，契约通过有效的死亡托管交付：(1) 将契约交给托管代理人，并指示在授予人死亡后将其转让给受让人，并且 (2) 授予人放弃收回契约的权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师必须将契约交给侄子的女儿吗？","rule":"在以下情况下，契约通过有效的死亡托管交付：(1) 将契约交给托管代理人，并指示在授予人死亡后将其转让给受让人，并且 (2) 授予人放弃收回契约的权利。","analysis":"当契约交付给受让人并被受让人接受时，它就有效地将土地所有者（授予人）的不动产所有权转让给另一个人（受让人）。要通过有效的死亡托管交付契据，设保人必须： 将契据交给托管代理人（例如律师），并指示在设保人死亡后将契据转让给受让人，并放弃收回契据的权利（即，将契据置于其控制范围之外）。在这里，该男子试图通过死亡托管将一份契约交付给农场。尽管他向律师提供了该契约，并指示在该男子去世后将其交给侄子，但该男子明​​确保留如果他提出要求，可以将契约归还给他的权利。由于该男子从未将契约置于他的控制之外，因此死亡托管无效（没有交付）。结果，侄子从未获得农场的所有权，也无法将其设计给他的女儿。由于她对农场没有兴趣，律师不需要将契约交给她。 （选择 A）由于大多数死亡托管是无偿的，因此不需要合同（书面或口头）。相反，死亡托管无效，因为该男子保留收回契约的权利。 （选择 C）一旦授予人交付契约，无偿死亡托管就变得不可撤销，而不是在受让人死亡时。 （选项 D）当契约直接交给受让人时，即推定交付。但是，当契约被交给托管代理人时，除非授予人放弃其收回契约的权利，否则不会进行交付。由于该男子保留要求归还契约的权利，因此将契约交给他的律师（托管代理人）并不构成交付。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，契约通过有效的死亡托管交付：(1) 将契约交给托管代理人，并指示在授予人死亡后将其转让给受让人，并且 (2) 授予人放弃收回契约的权利。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为这个人从来没有让这件事超出他的控制范围。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-d874bf91afb80a81"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":44,"sourceQuestionPage":129,"sourceQuestionPages":[129],"sourceExplanationPages":[130,131],"sourceAnswerPages":[130,131],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-045","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man defaulted on a loan that was secured by a mortgage on his home. The lender brought an appropriate action to foreclose the mortgage and, at the judicial foreclosure sale, the home was sold to a buyer. Shortly thereafter, the man inherited sufficient funds to enable him to reacquire the home from the buyer, but the buyer declined to sell. Which of the following would provide the man with legal justification for pursuing an action for specific performance to compel the buyer to transfer ownership of the home to the man?","choices":{"A":"The jurisdiction adheres to the doctrine of equitable redemption.","B":"The jurisdiction does not follow the doctrine of merger.","C":"The jurisdiction has enacted a statute that permits redemption.","D":"The jurisdiction recognizes the lien theory of mortgages."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Right of redemption\n\n(regaining title after default on loan)\n\nTiming Assertion of right\n\nEquitable At any time prior to foreclosure sale Pay full amount of debt due right (including interest) to lender\n\n(always exists)\n\nStatutory Statutory period after property has Pay foreclosure sale price to right been sold in foreclosure purchasing party\n\n(if statute exists)\n\nWhen a debtor (here, the man) defaults on mortgage payments, the creditor can initiate foreclosure proceedings to force the sale of the mortgaged property to satisfy the unpaid debt. Once the property has been sold, jurisdictions with a statutory right of redemption allow the debtor to reclaim the property by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchaser within a fixed period of time (typically between three months and two years). Therefore, if the jurisdiction has enacted a statute that provides this right, the man would have legal justification to pursue an action for specific performance to compel the buyer to transfer ownership of the home to the man. (Choice A) The doctrine of equitable redemption allows a debtor to reclaim mortgaged property at any time before a foreclosure sale by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt, plus any accrued interest. But a debtor can only reclaim mortgaged property after a foreclosure sale if the jurisdiction has enacted a statute providing this right. (Choice B) The doctrine of merger applies in the mortgage context when the same person acquires ownership of both the mortgage and the mortgaged property. When this occurs, the mortgage merges into fee simple ownership of the property. But the fact that the jurisdiction does not follow this doctrine would not affect the outcome of this dispute. (Choice D) Regardless of which mortgage theory the jurisdiction recognizes, the man will have the right to redeem his home from the buyer only if the jurisdiction has a statutory right of redemption.\n\nEducational objective: In jurisdictions with a statutory right of redemption, a debtor can reclaim property after a foreclosure sale by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchaser within a fixed period of time.\n\nReferences\n30 Am. Jur. 2d Executions and Enforcement of Judgments § 291 (2020) (statutory right of redemption).","educationalObjective":"In jurisdictions with a statutory right of redemption, a debtor can reclaim property after a foreclosure sale by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchaser within a fixed period of time.","references":"30 Am. Jur. 2d Executions and Enforcement of Judgments § 291 (2020) (statutory right of redemption).","rule":"In jurisdictions with a statutory right of redemption, a debtor can reclaim property after a foreclosure sale by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchaser within a fixed period of time.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would provide the man with legal justification for pursuing an action for specific performance to compel the buyer to transfer ownership of the home to the man?","rule":"In jurisdictions with a statutory right of redemption, a debtor can reclaim property after a foreclosure sale by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchaser within a fixed period of time.","analysis":"Right of redemption\n\n(regaining title after default on loan)\n\nTiming Assertion of right\n\nEquitable At any time prior to foreclosure sale Pay full amount of debt due right (including interest) to lender\n\n(always exists)\n\nStatutory Statutory period after property has Pay foreclosure sale price to right been sold in foreclosure purchasing party\n\n(if statute exists)\n\nWhen a debtor (here, the man) defaults on mortgage payments, the creditor can initiate foreclosure proceedings to force the sale of the mortgaged property to satisfy the unpaid debt. Once the property has been sold, jurisdictions with a statutory right of redemption allow the debtor to reclaim the property by paying the foreclosure sale price to the purchaser within a fixed period of time (typically between three months and two years). Therefore, if the jurisdiction has enacted a statute that provides this right, the man would have legal justification to pursue an action for specific performance to compel the buyer to transfer ownership of the home to the man. (Choice A) The doctrine of equitable redemption allows a debtor to reclaim mortgaged property at any time before a foreclosure sale by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt, plus any accrued interest. But a debtor can only reclaim mortgaged property after a foreclosure sale if the jurisdiction has enacted a statute providing this right. (Choice B) The doctrine of merger applies in the mortgage context when the same person acquires ownership of both the mortgage and the mortgaged property. When this occurs, the mortgage merges into fee simple ownership of the property. But the fact that the jurisdiction does not follow this doctrine would not affect the outcome of this dispute. (Choice D) Regardless of which mortgage theory the jurisdiction recognizes, the man will have the right to redeem his home from the buyer only if the jurisdiction has a statutory right of redemption.","conclusion":"Answer C: The jurisdiction has enacted a statute that permits redemption."},"questionZh":"一名男子拖欠了以房屋抵押贷款为担保的贷款。贷方采取了适当的行动取消了抵押贷款的赎回权，并在司法取消赎回权出售中将房屋出售给了买家。此后不久，该男子继承了足够的资金，使他能够从买家手中重新购买房屋，但买家拒绝出售。以下哪项可以为该男子提供法律依据，以采取具体履行行动，迫使买方将房屋所有权转让给该男子？","choicesZh":{"A":"司法管辖区遵循公平赎回的原则。","B":"管辖权不遵循合并原则。","C":"该司法管辖区已颁布一项允许赎回的法规。","D":"该司法管辖区承认抵押贷款的留置权理论。"},"explanationZh":"赎回权\n\n（贷款违约后恢复所有权）\n\n权利主张的时机\n\n公平 在取消抵押品赎回权出售之前的任何时间 向贷款人支付全额债务到期权利（包括利息）\n\n（始终存在）\n\n法定期限 财产支付止赎出售价格以在止赎购买方出售权利后的法定期限\n\n（如果有法规的话）\n\n当债务人（此处为男子）拖欠抵押贷款时，债权人可以启动止赎程序，强制出售抵押财产以偿还未偿债务。一旦财产被出售，具有法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）的司法管辖区允许债务人通过在固定期限内（通常在三个月到两年之间）向购买者支付取消抵押品赎回权的销售价格来收回财产。因此，如果司法管辖区颁布了规定这项权利的法规，该男子就有法律理由提起具体履行诉讼，迫使买方将房屋所有权转让给该男子。 （选择A）公平赎回原则允许债务人在取消抵押品赎回权出售之前的任何时间通过支付全部未偿债务以及任何应计利息来收回抵押财产。但债务人只有在司法管辖区颁布了提供此项权利的法规的情况下，才能在取消抵押品赎回权出售后收回抵押财产。 （选择B）当同一人同时获得抵押权和抵押财产的所有权时，合并原则适用于抵押权情况。当这种情况发生时，抵押贷款就合并为财产的简单所有权。但管辖权不遵循该原则的事实并不影响本案争议的结果。 （选项D）无论司法管辖区承认哪种抵押理论，只有在司法管辖区具有法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）的情况下，该男子才有权从买方手中赎回其房屋。\n\n教育目标：在具有法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）的司法管辖区，债务人可以通过在固定期限内向购买者支付止赎出售价格来收回止赎出售后的财产。\n\n参考文献\n上午 30 点。尤尔。 2d 判决的执行和执行 § 291 (2020)（法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在具有法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）的司法管辖区，债务人可以在取消抵押品赎回权出售后通过在固定期限内向购买者支付取消抵押品赎回权的销售价格来收回财产。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项可以为该男子提供法律依据，以采取具体履行行动，迫使买方将房屋所有权转让给该男子？","rule":"在具有法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）的司法管辖区，债务人可以在取消抵押品赎回权出售后通过在固定期限内向购买者支付取消抵押品赎回权的销售价格来收回财产。","analysis":"赎回权\n\n（贷款违约后恢复所有权）\n\n权利主张的时机\n\n公平 在取消抵押品赎回权出售之前的任何时间 向贷款人支付全额债务到期权利（包括利息）\n\n（始终存在）\n\n法定期限 财产支付止赎出售价格以在止赎购买方出售权利后的法定期限\n\n（如果有法规的话）\n\n当债务人（此处为男子）拖欠抵押贷款时，债权人可以启动止赎程序，强制出售抵押财产以偿还未偿债务。一旦财产被出售，具有法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）的司法管辖区允许债务人通过在固定期限内（通常在三个月到两年之间）向购买者支付取消抵押品赎回权的销售价格来收回财产。因此，如果司法管辖区颁布了规定这项权利的法规，该男子就有法律理由提起具体履行诉讼，迫使买方将房屋所有权转让给该男子。 （选择A）公平赎回原则允许债务人在取消抵押品赎回权出售之前的任何时间通过支付全部未偿债务以及任何应计利息来收回抵押财产。但债务人只有在司法管辖区颁布了提供此项权利的法规的情况下，才能在取消抵押品赎回权出售后收回抵押财产。 （选择B）当同一人同时获得抵押权和抵押财产的所有权时，合并原则适用于抵押权情况。当这种情况发生时，抵押贷款就合并为财产的简单所有权。但管辖权不遵循该原则的事实并不影响本案争议的结果。 （选项D）无论司法管辖区承认哪种抵押理论，只有在司法管辖区具有法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）的情况下，该男子才有权从买方手中赎回其房屋。\n\n教育目标：在具有法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption）的司法管辖区，债务人可以通过在固定期限内向购买者支付止赎出售价格来收回止赎出售后的财产。\n\n参考文献\n上午 30 点。尤尔。 2d 判决的执行和执行 § 291 (2020)（法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption））。","conclusion":"答案 C：该司法管辖区已颁布一项允许赎回的法规。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":45,"sourceQuestionPage":132,"sourceQuestionPages":[132],"sourceExplanationPages":[133,134],"sourceAnswerPages":[133,134],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-046","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A builder sold a new house to a buyer for use as the buyer's residence. The buyer paid 10% of the purchase price and financed the rest by executing a promissory note and purchase- money mortgage to the builder. A year later, the buyer missed several mortgage payments to the builder and became unable to make payments. During that year, property values in the neighborhood declined substantially. The builder suggested that the buyer deed the house back to the builder to settle all claims and avoid the costs and other disadvantages of foreclosure. The buyer deeded the house back to the builder. Does the builder now own fee simple title to the house?","choices":{"A":"No, because the deed back to the builder constitutes a disguised mortgage.","B":"No, because the owner of a personal residence cannot waive the right to foreclosure.","C":"Yes, because of the doctrine of equitable redemption.","D":"Yes, because the transaction was reasonable and fair under the circumstances."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Ways to avoid foreclosure\n\nEquitable Mortgagor pays full amount of outstanding debt (as increased by redemption* acceleration clause) plus any accrued interest\n\nDeed in lieu of Mortgagor conveys all interest in mortgaged property to foreclosure mortgagee\n\nRenegotiating debt Parties renegotiate terms of promissory note & mortgage\n\n*Many states also recognize a statutory right of redemption that permits a mortgagor to reclaim the property after a foreclosure sale.\n\nIn lieu of foreclosure, the mortgagor may convey title to the mortgaged property to the mortgagee (\"deed in lieu of foreclosure\"). This allows the grantee-mortgagee to take immediate possession of the property without the formalities of a foreclosure sale. Deed-in-lieu transactions are generally valid so long as they are fair to the grantor- mortgagor. However, they run the risk of being labeled as an equitable or \"disguised\" mortgage (as opposed to a title conveyance) if it appears that the deed was intended only as security for the original mortgage. Here, the buyer deeded the house back to the builder to avoid foreclosure. That transaction was reasonable and fair under the circumstances—particularly since property values had declined so much that any foreclosure sale might have resulted in a deficiency that the buyer would have been obligated to pay out of personal assets. And since there is no indication that the deed was intended only as security for the original mortgage (eg, had the buyer remained indebted to the builder or retained possession of the house), the builder owns the house in fee simple (Choice A). (Choice B) The right to foreclose is held by the mortgagee (eg, builder)—not the mortgagor (eg, buyer)—and the mortgagee can waive this right by accepting a deed in lieu of foreclosure. (Choice C) The doctrine of equitable redemption allows a mortgagor to prevent foreclosure by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt (as increased by an acceleration clause) plus interest. Had this doctrine been invoked, however, the buyer (not the builder) would own the home.\n\nEducational objective: A mortgagor can avoid foreclosure by conveying title to the mortgaged property to the mortgagee (\"deed in lieu of foreclosure\"). Deed-in-lieu transactions are generally valid so\n\nlong as they are fair to the grantor-mortgagor and not simply a means of continuing the original mortgage (ie, equitable or \"disguised\" mortgage).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages & 8.5 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (discussing deeds in lieu of foreclosure).","educationalObjective":"A mortgagor can avoid foreclosure by conveying title to the mortgaged property to the mortgagee (\"deed in lieu of foreclosure\"). Deed-in-lieu transactions are generally valid so\n\nlong as they are fair to the grantor-mortgagor and not simply a means of continuing the original mortgage (ie, equitable or \"disguised\" mortgage).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages & 8.5 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (discussing deeds in lieu of foreclosure).","rule":"A mortgagor can avoid foreclosure by conveying title to the mortgaged property to the mortgagee (\"deed in lieu of foreclosure\"). Deed-in-lieu transactions are generally valid so\n\nlong as they are fair to the grantor-mortgagor and not simply a means of continuing the original mortgage (ie, equitable or \"disguised\" mortgage).","irac":{"issue":"Does the builder now own fee simple title to the house?","rule":"A mortgagor can avoid foreclosure by conveying title to the mortgaged property to the mortgagee (\"deed in lieu of foreclosure\"). Deed-in-lieu transactions are generally valid so\n\nlong as they are fair to the grantor-mortgagor and not simply a means of continuing the original mortgage (ie, equitable or \"disguised\" mortgage).","analysis":"Ways to avoid foreclosure\n\nEquitable Mortgagor pays full amount of outstanding debt (as increased by redemption* acceleration clause) plus any accrued interest\n\nDeed in lieu of Mortgagor conveys all interest in mortgaged property to foreclosure mortgagee\n\nRenegotiating debt Parties renegotiate terms of promissory note & mortgage\n\n*Many states also recognize a statutory right of redemption that permits a mortgagor to reclaim the property after a foreclosure sale.\n\nIn lieu of foreclosure, the mortgagor may convey title to the mortgaged property to the mortgagee (\"deed in lieu of foreclosure\"). This allows the grantee-mortgagee to take immediate possession of the property without the formalities of a foreclosure sale. Deed-in-lieu transactions are generally valid so long as they are fair to the grantor- mortgagor. However, they run the risk of being labeled as an equitable or \"disguised\" mortgage (as opposed to a title conveyance) if it appears that the deed was intended only as security for the original mortgage. Here, the buyer deeded the house back to the builder to avoid foreclosure. That transaction was reasonable and fair under the circumstances—particularly since property values had declined so much that any foreclosure sale might have resulted in a deficiency that the buyer would have been obligated to pay out of personal assets. And since there is no indication that the deed was intended only as security for the original mortgage (eg, had the buyer remained indebted to the builder or retained possession of the house), the builder owns the house in fee simple (Choice A). (Choice B) The right to foreclose is held by the mortgagee (eg, builder)—not the mortgagor (eg, buyer)—and the mortgagee can waive this right by accepting a deed in lieu of foreclosure. (Choice C) The doctrine of equitable redemption allows a mortgagor to prevent foreclosure by paying the full amount of the outstanding debt (as increased by an acceleration clause) plus interest. Had this doctrine been invoked, however, the buyer (not the builder) would own the home.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the transaction was reasonable and fair under the circumstances."},"questionZh":"一名建筑商将一套新房子出售给买家，用作买家的住所。买方支付了购买价格的 10%，并通过向建筑商签发本票和购房款抵押来支付其余部分。一年后，买家错过了向建筑商支付的几笔抵押贷款，并无法付款。那一年，附近的房产价值大幅下降。建筑商建议买家将房屋归还给建筑商，以解决所有索赔并避免取消抵押品赎回权的费用和其他缺点。买家将房子转还给建筑商。建筑商现在是否拥有房屋的简单所有权？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为回给建筑商的契约构成了变相的抵押贷款。","B":"不可以，因为个人住宅的所有者不能放弃取消抵押品赎回权的权利。","C":"是的，因为公平赎回的原则。","D":"是的，因为在当时的情况下，交易是合理、公平的。"},"explanationZh":"避免丧失抵押品赎回权的方法\n\n公平抵押人全额支付未偿债务（因赎回*加速条款而增加）加上任何应计利息\n\n代替抵押人的契约将抵押财产的所有权益转让给丧失抵押品赎回权的抵押权人\n\n重新谈判债务 双方重新谈判本票和抵押贷款的条款\n\n*许多州还承认法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption），允许抵押人在取消抵押品赎回权出售后收回财产。\n\n抵押人可以将抵押财产的所有权转让给抵押权人，以代替丧失抵押品赎回权（“代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约”）。这使得受让人-抵押权人可以立即占有该财产，而无需办理丧失抵押品赎回权的出售手续。只要对设保人-抵押人公平，替代契约交易一般都是有效的。然而，如果契约似乎只是作为原始抵押贷款的担保，那么它们就面临着被标记为公平或“变相”抵押贷款（而不是所有权转让）的风险。在这里，买家将房子转还给建筑商以避免丧失抵押品赎回权。在当时的情况下，这笔交易是合理和公平的——特别是考虑到房产价值已经大幅下跌，任何止赎出售都可能导致买家有义务用个人资产来支付资金短缺。并且由于没有迹象表明该契约仅旨在作为原始抵押贷款的担保（例如，如果买方仍然欠建筑商或保留对房屋的所有权），则建筑商以简单费用拥有该房屋（选择 A）。 （选择B）取消抵押品赎回权的权利由抵押权人（例如建筑商）持有，而不是抵押人（例如买方），并且抵押权人可以通过接受代替取消抵押品赎回权的契据来放弃这项权利。 （选择 C） 公平赎回原则允许抵押人通过全额支付未偿债务（通过加速条款增加）加利息来防止丧失抵押品赎回权。然而，如果援引这一原则，买家（而不是建筑商）将拥有该房屋。\n\n教育目标：抵押人可以通过将抵押财产的所有权转让给抵押权人（“代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约”）来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。代替契约交易通常是有效的，因此\n\n只要它们对设保人-抵押人公平，而不仅仅是继续原始抵押的一种手段（即公平或“变相”抵押）。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款和 8.5 cmt。 b（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（讨论代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"抵押人可以通过将抵押财产的所有权转让给抵押权人（“代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约”）来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。代替契约交易通常是有效的，因此\n\n只要它们对设保人-抵押人公平，而不仅仅是继续原始抵押的一种手段（即公平或“变相”抵押）。","iracZh":{"issue":"建筑商现在是否拥有房屋的简单所有权？","rule":"抵押人可以通过将抵押财产的所有权转让给抵押权人（“代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约”）来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。代替契约交易通常是有效的，因此\n\n只要它们对设保人-抵押人公平，而不仅仅是继续原始抵押的一种手段（即公平或“变相”抵押）。","analysis":"避免丧失抵押品赎回权的方法\n\n公平抵押人全额支付未偿债务（因赎回*加速条款而增加）加上任何应计利息\n\n代替抵押人的契约将抵押财产的所有权益转让给丧失抵押品赎回权的抵押权人\n\n重新谈判债务 双方重新谈判本票和抵押贷款的条款\n\n*许多州还承认法定赎回权（statutory right of redemption）（statutory right of redemption），允许抵押人在取消抵押品赎回权出售后收回财产。\n\n抵押人可以将抵押财产的所有权转让给抵押权人，以代替丧失抵押品赎回权（“代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约”）。这使得受让人-抵押权人可以立即占有该财产，而无需办理丧失抵押品赎回权的出售手续。只要对设保人-抵押人公平，替代契约交易一般都是有效的。然而，如果契约似乎只是作为原始抵押贷款的担保，那么它们就面临着被标记为公平或“变相”抵押贷款（而不是所有权转让）的风险。在这里，买家将房子转还给建筑商以避免丧失抵押品赎回权。在当时的情况下，这笔交易是合理和公平的——特别是考虑到房产价值已经大幅下跌，任何止赎出售都可能导致买家有义务用个人资产来支付资金短缺。并且由于没有迹象表明该契约仅旨在作为原始抵押贷款的担保（例如，如果买方仍然欠建筑商或保留对房屋的所有权），则建筑商以简单费用拥有该房屋（选择 A）。 （选择B）取消抵押品赎回权的权利由抵押权人（例如建筑商）持有，而不是抵押人（例如买方），并且抵押权人可以通过接受代替取消抵押品赎回权的契据来放弃这项权利。 （选择 C） 公平赎回原则允许抵押人通过全额支付未偿债务（通过加速条款增加）加利息来防止丧失抵押品赎回权。然而，如果援引这一原则，买家（而不是建筑商）将拥有该房屋。\n\n教育目标：抵押人可以通过将抵押财产的所有权转让给抵押权人（“代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约”）来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。代替契约交易通常是有效的，因此\n\n只要它们对设保人-抵押人公平，而不仅仅是继续原始抵押的一种手段（即公平或“变相”抵押）。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款和 8.5 cmt。 b（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（讨论代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为在当时的情况下，交易是合理、公平的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"deed in lieu of foreclosure","ipa":"/dˈid ɪn lˈu ˈəv fɔɹklˈoʊʒɝ/","syllables":"deed in lieu of fore·clo·sure","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"代止赎契据：债务人以交付土地契据代替正式止赎程序","contextualMeaning":"代止赎契据：债务人以交付土地契据代替正式止赎程序","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A mortgagor can avoid foreclosure by conveying title to the mortgaged property to the mortgagee (\"deed in lieu of foreclosure\").","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":46,"sourceQuestionPage":135,"sourceQuestionPages":[135],"sourceExplanationPages":[136,137],"sourceAnswerPages":[136,137],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-047","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A landowner borrowed $100,000 from a lender and executed a valid mortgage on a commercial tract of land to secure the debt. The lender promptly recorded the mortgage. A year later, the landowner conveyed the same tract to a developer by a deed that expressly stated that the conveyance was subject to the mortgage to the lender and that the grantee expressly assumed and agreed to pay the mortgage obligation as part of the consideration for the purchase. The mortgage was properly described in the deed, and the deed was properly executed by the landowner; however, because there was no provision or place in the deed for the developer to sign, he did not do so. The developer promptly recorded the deed. The developer made the monthly mortgage payments of principal and interest for six payments but then stopped payments and defaulted on the mortgage obligation. The lender properly instituted foreclosure procedures in accordance with the governing law. After the foreclosure sale, there was a $10,000 deficiency due to the lender. Both the landowner and the developer had sufficient assets to pay the deficiency. There is no applicable statute in the jurisdiction other than the statute relating to foreclosure proceedings. At the appropriate stage of the foreclosure action, which party will the court decide is responsible for payment of the deficiency?","choices":{"A":"The developer, because he accepted delivery of the deed from the landowner and in so doing accepted the terms and conditions of the deed.","B":"The developer, because he is estopped by his having made six monthly payments to the lender.","C":"The landowner, because he was the maker of the note and the mortgage, and at most the developer is liable only as a guarantor of the landowner's obligation.","D":"The landowner, because the developer was not a signatory to the deed."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Transfer of mortgaged property\n\nEffect on buyer/transferee Effecton seller\n\nSubject to mortgage Does not agree to pay loan & is not Remains solely liable for liable for debt debt\n\nAssumption of Agrees to pay loan & is primarily Becomes secondarily liable mortgage liable for debt as surety\n\nA mortgage is an interest in real property given to secure a debt. The debtor may freely transfer mortgaged property (unless otherwise agreed), but the mortgage will remain attached to that property even after it is transferred to another. The grantee's (eg, buyer's) obligations with respect to that mortgage depend on whether he/she either: took subject to the mortgage – did not agree to pay and is not personally liable for the debt or assumed the mortgage – expressly agreed to pay and becomes primarily liable for the debt, while the original debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. Here, the landowner's deed to the developer stated that the developer expressly assumed and agreed to pay the mortgage. And though the developer did not sign the deed, he assumed the mortgage by accepting delivery of that deed (Choice D). Therefore, the developer is primarily liable for the lender's $10,000 deficiency. And since the developer has sufficient assets to pay the deficiency judgment, the landowner need not pay anything. (Choice B) Because the developer assumed the mortgage once he accepted the landowner's transfer of the deed, he is liable for the deficiency judgment regardless of whether, or for how long, he had paid the mortgage. (Choice C) Although the landowner was the maker of the note and mortgage, the landowner (not the developer) became secondarily liable as a guarantor (ie, surety) when the developer assumed primary liability for that mortgage.\n\nEducational objective: A grantee assumes a mortgage when he/she expressly agrees to pay the debt. The grantee then becomes primarily liable for that debt, while the original debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Prop.: Mortgages § 5.1 (assumption of mortgage).","educationalObjective":"A grantee assumes a mortgage when he/she expressly agrees to pay the debt. The grantee then becomes primarily liable for that debt, while the original debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Prop.: Mortgages § 5.1 (assumption of mortgage).","rule":"A grantee assumes a mortgage when he/she expressly agrees to pay the debt. The grantee then becomes primarily liable for that debt, while the original debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety.","irac":{"issue":"At the appropriate stage of the foreclosure action, which party will the court decide is responsible for payment of the deficiency?","rule":"A grantee assumes a mortgage when he/she expressly agrees to pay the debt. The grantee then becomes primarily liable for that debt, while the original debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety.","analysis":"Transfer of mortgaged property\n\nEffect on buyer/transferee Effecton seller\n\nSubject to mortgage Does not agree to pay loan & is not Remains solely liable for liable for debt debt\n\nAssumption of Agrees to pay loan & is primarily Becomes secondarily liable mortgage liable for debt as surety\n\nA mortgage is an interest in real property given to secure a debt. The debtor may freely transfer mortgaged property (unless otherwise agreed), but the mortgage will remain attached to that property even after it is transferred to another. The grantee's (eg, buyer's) obligations with respect to that mortgage depend on whether he/she either: took subject to the mortgage – did not agree to pay and is not personally liable for the debt or assumed the mortgage – expressly agreed to pay and becomes primarily liable for the debt, while the original debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. Here, the landowner's deed to the developer stated that the developer expressly assumed and agreed to pay the mortgage. And though the developer did not sign the deed, he assumed the mortgage by accepting delivery of that deed (Choice D). Therefore, the developer is primarily liable for the lender's $10,000 deficiency. And since the developer has sufficient assets to pay the deficiency judgment, the landowner need not pay anything. (Choice B) Because the developer assumed the mortgage once he accepted the landowner's transfer of the deed, he is liable for the deficiency judgment regardless of whether, or for how long, he had paid the mortgage. (Choice C) Although the landowner was the maker of the note and mortgage, the landowner (not the developer) became secondarily liable as a guarantor (ie, surety) when the developer assumed primary liability for that mortgage.","conclusion":"Answer A: The developer, because he accepted delivery of the deed from the landowner and in so doing accepted the terms and conditions of the deed."},"questionZh":"一位土地所有者向贷方借了 10 万美元，并在一块商业土地上执行了有效的抵押贷款以担保债务。贷方立即记录了抵押贷款。一年后，土地所有者通过契约将同一块土地转让给开发商，该契约明确规定该转让权须抵押给贷款人，并且受让人明确承担并同意支付抵押债务，作为购买对价的一部分。契约中正确描述了抵押，并且契约由土地所有者正确执行；然而，由于契约中没有规定或地方可供开发商签署，因此他没有这样做。开发商立即记录了该行为。开发商每月按揭还款本金和利息共六期，但随后停止付款并拖欠抵押贷款义务。贷款人根据适用法律正确启动了止赎程序。取消抵押品赎回权出售后，由于贷款人的原因，出现了 10,000 美元的短缺。土地所有者和开发商都有足够的资产来弥补不足。除与止赎程序有关的法规外，该司法管辖区没有适用的法规。在取消抵押品赎回权诉讼的适当阶段，法院将决定哪一方负责支付不足额？","choicesZh":{"A":"开发商，因为他接受了土地所有者交付的地契，并因此接受了地契的条款和条件。","B":"开发商，因为他因每月向贷方付款六个月而被阻止。","C":"土地所有者，因为他是票据和抵押的制作人，而开发商最多只作为土地所有者义务的担保人承担责任。","D":"土地所有者，因为开发商不是契约的签署人。"},"explanationZh":"抵押财产转让\n\n对买方/受让人的影响 对卖方的影响\n\n有抵押 不同意偿还贷款且不承担全部债务责任\n\n假设同意支付贷款，并且主要成为次要责任抵押贷款，作为担保人对债务承担责任\n\n抵押贷款是为担保债务而提供的不动产权益。债务人可以自由转让抵押财产（除非另有约定），但即使该财产转让给他人，抵押仍将依附于该财产。受让人（例如买方）对该抵押贷款的义务取决于他/她是否： 接受抵押贷款 – 不同意付款且不对债务承担个人责任或承担抵押贷款 – 明确同意付款并成为该债务的主要责任，而原始债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。这里，土地所有者给开发商的契约表明开发商明确承担并同意支付抵押贷款。尽管开发商没有签署该契约，但他通过接受该契约的交付而承担了抵押贷款（选择D）。因此，开发商对贷方10,000美元的不足负有主要责任。由于开发商有足够的资产来支付缺陷判决，土地所有者无需支付任何费用。 （选项B）由于开发商在接受土地所有者转让契据后就承担了抵押权，因此无论其是否支付抵押贷款或支付了多久，他都应对不足判断承担责任。 （选择C）虽然土地所有者是票据和抵押贷款的制作人，但当开发商对该抵押贷款承担主要责任时，土地所有者（而不是开发商）作为担保人（即保证人）承担次要责任。\n\n教育目标：当受资助者明确同意偿还债务时，他/她就承担了抵押贷款。然后，受让人对该债务承担主要责任，而原始债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。\n\n参考文献\n提案重述（第三）：抵押贷款第 5.1 条（假设抵押贷款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当受让人明确同意偿还债务时，他/她就承担了抵押。然后，受让人对该债务承担主要责任，而原始债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"在取消抵押品赎回权诉讼的适当阶段，法院将决定哪一方负责支付不足额？","rule":"当受让人明确同意偿还债务时，他/她就承担了抵押。然后，受让人对该债务承担主要责任，而原始债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。","analysis":"抵押财产转让\n\n对买方/受让人的影响 对卖方的影响\n\n有抵押 不同意偿还贷款且不承担全部债务责任\n\n假设同意支付贷款，并且主要成为次要责任抵押贷款，作为担保人对债务承担责任\n\n抵押贷款是为担保债务而提供的不动产权益。债务人可以自由转让抵押财产（除非另有约定），但即使该财产转让给他人，抵押仍将依附于该财产。受让人（例如买方）对该抵押贷款的义务取决于他/她是否： 接受抵押贷款 – 不同意付款且不对债务承担个人责任或承担抵押贷款 – 明确同意付款并成为该债务的主要责任，而原始债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。这里，土地所有者给开发商的契约表明开发商明确承担并同意支付抵押贷款。尽管开发商没有签署该契约，但他通过接受该契约的交付而承担了抵押贷款（选择D）。因此，开发商对贷方10,000美元的不足负有主要责任。由于开发商有足够的资产来支付缺陷判决，土地所有者无需支付任何费用。 （选项B）由于开发商在接受土地所有者转让契据后就承担了抵押权，因此无论其是否支付抵押贷款或支付了多久，他都应对不足判断承担责任。 （选择C）虽然土地所有者是票据和抵押贷款的制作人，但当开发商对该抵押贷款承担主要责任时，土地所有者（而不是开发商）作为担保人（即保证人）承担次要责任。\n\n教育目标：当受资助者明确同意偿还债务时，他/她就承担了抵押贷款。然后，受让人对该债务承担主要责任，而原始债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。\n\n参考文献\n提案重述（第三）：抵押贷款第 5.1 条（假设抵押贷款）。","conclusion":"答案 A：开发商，因为他接受了土地所有者交付的地契，并因此接受了地契的条款和条件。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":47,"sourceQuestionPage":138,"sourceQuestionPages":[138],"sourceExplanationPages":[139,140],"sourceAnswerPages":[139,140],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-048","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A woman duly executed a will under which she devised her farm to her nephew and bequeathed the residue of her estate to her niece. For 12 years after executing her will, the woman lived on her farm. Then she sold it and used the sale proceeds to purchase a home in the city, in which she lived until she died, never having changed her will. Following the admission of the will to probate, both the nephew and the niece claimed ownership of the home. There is no applicable statute. Who is the owner of the home?","choices":{"A":"The nephew, because of the doctrine of ademption.","B":"The nephew, because of the doctrine of equitable estoppel.","C":"The niece, because of the doctrine of lapse.","D":"The niece, because she is the residuary legatee."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Classification of devises\n\n(conveyance by will)\n\nType Definition Examples\n\nSpecific Devise of specifically identified asset \"my farm to my nephew\"\n\nGeneral Devise (often of money) payable from general \"$2,000 to my daughter\" assets of estate \"one-half of my estate to my son\" Demonstrative Devise primarily payable from designated \"$2,000 to my daughter source, but secondarily payable from general out of my bank account\" assets\n\nResiduary Devise of all property not disposed of by \"all of the remainder of specific, general, or demonstrative devise my estate to my niece\"\n\nThe woman (testator) specifically devised her farm to the nephew (devisee). She later sold that farm and used the proceeds to purchase a home. But the nephew is not entitled to that home because of the doctrine of ademption, which causes a devise to fail in two circumstances: Ademption by extinction – when the specifically devised asset is not owned by the testator (or is destroyed or fundamentally changed) at the time of death (as seen here) Ademption by satisfaction – when the devisee received the devised asset (or some other asset intended to satisfy the devise) during the testator's life (not applicable here) As a result, the devisee takes nothing unless the will expressly states otherwise. So, when a specifically devised asset (eg, the farm) is sold during the testator's lifetime, the sale proceeds—and any assets later acquired with those proceeds (eg, the home)—become part of the general estate. The general estate is then distributed in accordance with the remainder of the will. And since the woman's will bequeathed the residue of her estate to the niece, the niece owns the home as the residuary legatee (Choice A). (Choice B) The doctrine of equitable estoppel bars a party from asserting a material fact that is inconsistent with a prior representation upon which another party detrimentally relied. But this doctrine does not apply here since no party has asserted contradictory facts. (Choice C) The doctrine of lapse causes any type of devise to fail when the devisee dies before the testator. But since the nephew and niece (devisees) survived the woman (testator), neither devise will fail under this doctrine.\n\nEducational objective: Specific devises are adeemed by extinction when the devised asset is not owned by the testator (or is destroyed or fundamentally changed) upon death. And any type of devise is adeemed by satisfaction when the devised asset or a substituted asset is given during the testator's life. The devised asset will then join the general estate.\n\nReferences\n80 Am. Jur. 2d Wills § 1444 (2018) (doctrine of ademption).","educationalObjective":"Specific devises are adeemed by extinction when the devised asset is not owned by the testator (or is destroyed or fundamentally changed) upon death. And any type of devise is adeemed by satisfaction when the devised asset or a substituted asset is given during the testator's life. The devised asset will then join the general estate.","references":"80 Am. Jur. 2d Wills § 1444 (2018) (doctrine of ademption).","rule":"Specific devises are adeemed by extinction when the devised asset is not owned by the testator (or is destroyed or fundamentally changed) upon death. And any type of devise is adeemed by satisfaction when the devised asset or a substituted asset is given during the testator's life. The devised asset will then join the general estate.","irac":{"issue":"Who is the owner of the home?","rule":"Specific devises are adeemed by extinction when the devised asset is not owned by the testator (or is destroyed or fundamentally changed) upon death. And any type of devise is adeemed by satisfaction when the devised asset or a substituted asset is given during the testator's life. The devised asset will then join the general estate.","analysis":"Classification of devises\n\n(conveyance by will)\n\nType Definition Examples\n\nSpecific Devise of specifically identified asset \"my farm to my nephew\"\n\nGeneral Devise (often of money) payable from general \"$2,000 to my daughter\" assets of estate \"one-half of my estate to my son\" Demonstrative Devise primarily payable from designated \"$2,000 to my daughter source, but secondarily payable from general out of my bank account\" assets\n\nResiduary Devise of all property not disposed of by \"all of the remainder of specific, general, or demonstrative devise my estate to my niece\"\n\nThe woman (testator) specifically devised her farm to the nephew (devisee). She later sold that farm and used the proceeds to purchase a home. But the nephew is not entitled to that home because of the doctrine of ademption, which causes a devise to fail in two circumstances: Ademption by extinction – when the specifically devised asset is not owned by the testator (or is destroyed or fundamentally changed) at the time of death (as seen here) Ademption by satisfaction – when the devisee received the devised asset (or some other asset intended to satisfy the devise) during the testator's life (not applicable here) As a result, the devisee takes nothing unless the will expressly states otherwise. So, when a specifically devised asset (eg, the farm) is sold during the testator's lifetime, the sale proceeds—and any assets later acquired with those proceeds (eg, the home)—become part of the general estate. The general estate is then distributed in accordance with the remainder of the will. And since the woman's will bequeathed the residue of her estate to the niece, the niece owns the home as the residuary legatee (Choice A). (Choice B) The doctrine of equitable estoppel bars a party from asserting a material fact that is inconsistent with a prior representation upon which another party detrimentally relied. But this doctrine does not apply here since no party has asserted contradictory facts. (Choice C) The doctrine of lapse causes any type of devise to fail when the devisee dies before the testator. But since the nephew and niece (devisees) survived the woman (testator), neither devise will fail under this doctrine.","conclusion":"Answer D: The niece, because she is the residuary legatee."},"questionZh":"一名妇女正式签署了一份遗嘱，根据遗嘱，她将自己的农场划给了她的侄子，并将剩余的遗产遗赠给了她的侄女。在执行遗嘱后的 12 年里，这位妇女一直住在自己的农场里。然后她卖掉了它，并用出售所得在城里购买了一套房子，她一直住在那里，直到去世，从未改变过她的遗嘱。在接受遗嘱认证后，侄子和侄女都声称拥有该房屋的所有权。没有适用的法规。房子的主人是谁？","choicesZh":{"A":"侄子，因赎罪之教义。","B":"侄子，因为衡平法上禁止反言的原则。","C":"侄女，因教义失效。","D":"侄女，因为她是剩余的受遗赠人。"},"explanationZh":"装置分类\n\n（遗嘱转让）\n\n类型定义示例\n\n特定资产的具体设计“我的农场给我的侄子”\n\n一般设计（通常是金钱）从一般“2,000 美元给我女儿”的遗产资产支付“我一半的遗产给我儿子” 示范性设计主要从指定的“2,000 美元给我女儿来源，但其次从我的银行账户的一般资产支付”\n\n剩余财产 未处置的所有财产“所有剩余的具体、一般或示范性财产均归我的侄女”\n\n女人（立遗嘱人）专门为侄子（受遗嘱人）设计了她的农场。后来她卖掉了那个农场，并用所得收益购买了一套房子。但由于赎回原则，侄子无权获得该房屋，这会导致设计在两种情况下失败： 消灭赎回 – 当特别设计的资产在死亡时不为遗嘱人所有（或被毁坏或根本改变）时（如此处所示） 满足赎回 – 当受遗赠人在立遗嘱人的一生中收到设计的资产（或旨在满足设计的其他资产）时（此处不适用）结果，除非遗嘱另有明确规定，否则受遗嘱人不会得到任何东西。因此，当立遗嘱人在世时出售专门设计的资产（例如农场）时，出售收益以及后来用这些收益获得的任何资产（例如房屋）都成为一般遗产的一部分。然后，一般遗产将根据遗嘱的其余部分进行分配。由于女方的遗嘱将她剩余的遗产遗赠给了侄女，因此侄女作为剩余受遗赠人拥有该房屋（选项 A）。 （选择 B） 衡平法禁止反言原则禁止一方当事人主张与另一方不利地依赖的先前陈述不一致的重要事实。但这一原则在这里并不适用，因为没有一方声称有矛盾的事实。 （选项C）当受遗嘱人先于立遗嘱人去世时，失效原则会导致任何类型的遗嘱失效。但由于侄子和侄女（受遗嘱人）比女人（立遗嘱人）幸存，因此根据这一学说，这两个遗嘱都不会失败。\n\n教育目标：当立遗嘱人死亡时所设计的资产不属于遗嘱人所有（或被毁坏或根本改变）时，特定的设计被视为消灭。当遗嘱人在世期间给予所设计的资产或替代资产时，任何类型的设计都被视为满足。然后设计的资产将加入一般遗产。\n\n参考文献\n上午 80 点。尤尔。 2d Wills § 1444 (2018)（赎回原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当所设计的资产在死亡时不属于立遗嘱人所有（或被毁坏或根本改变）时，特定的设计被视为消灭。当遗嘱人在世期间给予所设计的资产或替代资产时，任何类型的设计都被视为满足。然后设计的资产将加入一般遗产。","iracZh":{"issue":"房子的主人是谁？","rule":"当所设计的资产在死亡时不属于立遗嘱人所有（或被毁坏或根本改变）时，特定的设计被视为消灭。当遗嘱人在世期间给予所设计的资产或替代资产时，任何类型的设计都被视为满足。然后设计的资产将加入一般遗产。","analysis":"装置分类\n\n（遗嘱转让）\n\n类型定义示例\n\n特定资产的具体设计“我的农场给我的侄子”\n\n一般设计（通常是金钱）从一般“2,000 美元给我女儿”的遗产资产支付“我一半的遗产给我儿子” 示范性设计主要从指定的“2,000 美元给我女儿来源，但其次从我的银行账户的一般资产支付”\n\n剩余财产 未处置的所有财产“所有剩余的具体、一般或示范性财产均归我的侄女”\n\n女人（立遗嘱人）专门为侄子（受遗嘱人）设计了她的农场。后来她卖掉了那个农场，并用所得收益购买了一套房子。但由于赎回原则，侄子无权获得该房屋，这会导致设计在两种情况下失败： 消灭赎回 – 当特别设计的资产在死亡时不为遗嘱人所有（或被毁坏或根本改变）时（如此处所示） 满足赎回 – 当受遗赠人在立遗嘱人的一生中收到设计的资产（或旨在满足设计的其他资产）时（此处不适用）结果，除非遗嘱另有明确规定，否则受遗嘱人不会得到任何东西。因此，当立遗嘱人在世时出售专门设计的资产（例如农场）时，出售收益以及后来用这些收益获得的任何资产（例如房屋）都成为一般遗产的一部分。然后，一般遗产将根据遗嘱的其余部分进行分配。由于女方的遗嘱将她剩余的遗产遗赠给了侄女，因此侄女作为剩余受遗赠人拥有该房屋（选项 A）。 （选择 B） 衡平法禁止反言原则禁止一方当事人主张与另一方不利地依赖的先前陈述不一致的重要事实。但这一原则在这里并不适用，因为没有一方声称有矛盾的事实。 （选项C）当受遗嘱人先于立遗嘱人去世时，失效原则会导致任何类型的遗嘱失效。但由于侄子和侄女（受遗嘱人）比女人（立遗嘱人）幸存，因此根据这一学说，这两个遗嘱都不会失败。\n\n教育目标：当立遗嘱人死亡时所设计的资产不属于遗嘱人所有（或被毁坏或根本改变）时，特定的设计被视为消灭。当遗嘱人在世期间给予所设计的资产或替代资产时，任何类型的设计都被视为满足。然后设计的资产将加入一般遗产。\n\n参考文献\n上午 80 点。尤尔。 2d Wills § 1444 (2018)（赎回原则）。","conclusion":"答案 D：侄女，因为她是剩余的受遗赠人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":48,"sourceQuestionPage":141,"sourceQuestionPages":[141],"sourceExplanationPages":[142,143],"sourceAnswerPages":[142,143],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-049","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman borrowed $100,000 from a bank and executed a promissory note to the bank in that amount. As security for repayment of the loan, the woman's brother gave the bank a mortgage on a tract of land solely owned by him. The brother did not sign the promissory note. The woman subsequently defaulted on the loan, and after acceleration, the bank instituted foreclosure proceedings on the brother's land. The brother filed a timely objection to the foreclosure. Will the bank succeed in foreclosing on the tract of land?","choices":{"A":"No, because a mortgage from the brother is invalid without a mortgage debt owed by him.","B":"No, because the bank has an equitable mortgage rather than a legal mortgage.","C":"Yes, because the bank has a valid mortgage.","D":"Yes, because the bank is a surety for the brother's mortgage."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Mortgage documents\n\nMortgage deed A writing that conveys an interest in collateral to secure a debt\n\nPromissory A formal \"IOU\" in which the borrower promises to repay the debt note according to the listed terms\n\nA mortgage is a lien against real property given to secure repayment of a debt. If the debt (evidenced by a promissory note) belongs to someone other than the mortgagor, then the mortgagor is not personally liable for repaying that debt.* However, the mortgagee can foreclose on the mortgaged property if the debtor defaults. Here, the woman received a $100,000 loan from the bank, which was evidenced by a promissory note. The brother did not sign the promissory note, so he is not personally liable for the debt. But since he used his tract of land to secure repayment of the woman's debt, the bank has a valid mortgage on that land. Therefore, the bank will succeed in foreclosing on the brother's land after the woman defaulted on the loan. *The mortgagor would be personally liable for the debt if the mortgage itself contained language to that effect. (Choice A) The fact that the mortgage debt is owed by someone else does not render the mortgage from the brother invalid. It merely means that the brother is not personally liable for repaying the mortgage debt. (Choice B) An equitable mortgage is a mortgage alternative that arises when a party takes possession of the original deed to property with the intent that the property serve as security for a debt (not seen here). (Choice D) A surety is a party who guarantees to pay another's debt in the event that person fails to do so. Here, the bank has not agreed to pay the debt for the woman if she defaults, so the bank is not a surety.\n\nEducational objective: A mortgage can be given to secure repayment of a debt owed by someone other than the mortgagor, and the mortgagee can foreclose on the mortgaged property if the debtor defaults.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 1.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (mortgages securing obligations of nonmortgagors).","educationalObjective":"A mortgage can be given to secure repayment of a debt owed by someone other than the mortgagor, and the mortgagee can foreclose on the mortgaged property if the debtor defaults.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 1.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (mortgages securing obligations of nonmortgagors).","rule":"A mortgage can be given to secure repayment of a debt owed by someone other than the mortgagor, and the mortgagee can foreclose on the mortgaged property if the debtor defaults.","irac":{"issue":"Will the bank succeed in foreclosing on the tract of land?","rule":"A mortgage can be given to secure repayment of a debt owed by someone other than the mortgagor, and the mortgagee can foreclose on the mortgaged property if the debtor defaults.","analysis":"Mortgage documents\n\nMortgage deed A writing that conveys an interest in collateral to secure a debt\n\nPromissory A formal \"IOU\" in which the borrower promises to repay the debt note according to the listed terms\n\nA mortgage is a lien against real property given to secure repayment of a debt. If the debt (evidenced by a promissory note) belongs to someone other than the mortgagor, then the mortgagor is not personally liable for repaying that debt.* However, the mortgagee can foreclose on the mortgaged property if the debtor defaults. Here, the woman received a $100,000 loan from the bank, which was evidenced by a promissory note. The brother did not sign the promissory note, so he is not personally liable for the debt. But since he used his tract of land to secure repayment of the woman's debt, the bank has a valid mortgage on that land. Therefore, the bank will succeed in foreclosing on the brother's land after the woman defaulted on the loan. *The mortgagor would be personally liable for the debt if the mortgage itself contained language to that effect. (Choice A) The fact that the mortgage debt is owed by someone else does not render the mortgage from the brother invalid. It merely means that the brother is not personally liable for repaying the mortgage debt. (Choice B) An equitable mortgage is a mortgage alternative that arises when a party takes possession of the original deed to property with the intent that the property serve as security for a debt (not seen here). (Choice D) A surety is a party who guarantees to pay another's debt in the event that person fails to do so. Here, the bank has not agreed to pay the debt for the woman if she defaults, so the bank is not a surety.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the bank has a valid mortgage."},"questionZh":"一名妇女从银行借了 10 万美元，并向银行开立了一张等额本票。作为偿还贷款的担保，该女子的兄弟将自己拥有的一块土地抵押给了银行。弟弟没有在期票上签字。该女子随后拖欠贷款，加速后，银行对兄弟的土地提起了取消抵押品赎回权的程序。兄弟及时对取消抵押品赎回权提出了反对。银行能否成功取消这块土地的赎回权？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为兄弟的抵押贷款如果没有他所欠的抵押债务就无效。","B":"不，因为银行拥有的是公平抵押贷款，而不是合法抵押贷款。","C":"是的，因为银行有有效的抵押贷款。","D":"是的，因为银行是弟弟抵押贷款的担保人。"},"explanationZh":"抵押文件\n\n抵押契据 表达抵押品权益以担保债务的书面文件\n\n本票 借款人承诺按照所列条款偿还债务票据的正式“借条”\n\n抵押贷款是为确保债务偿还而给予不动产的留置权。如果债务（由本票证明）属于抵押人以外的其他人，则抵押人个人不承担偿还该债务的责任。*但是，如果债务人违约，抵押权人可以取消抵押财产的赎回权。在这里，这位女士从银行获得了10万美元的贷款，并有一张期票作为证明。兄弟没有签署本票，因此他个人不承担债务。但由于他用自己的一块土地来偿还这位女士的债务，银行对该土地拥有有效的抵押。因此，在该女子拖欠贷款后，银行将成功取消其兄弟土地的赎回权。 *如果抵押贷款本身包含这样的语言，抵押人将对债务承担个人责任。 （选项A）抵押贷款债务是由其他人所欠的事实并不意味着兄弟的抵押贷款无效。这仅仅意味着兄弟个人不承担偿还抵押贷款债务的责任。 （选择 B）衡平抵押贷款是一种抵押贷款替代方案，当一方占有原始财产契约并意图将该财产作为债务担保（此处未看到）时，就会出现这种抵押贷款替代方案。 （选项D） 担保人是保证在他人不履行债务的情况下偿还他人债务的一方。在这里，银行不同意在该妇女违约的情况下为她偿还债务，因此银行不是担保人。\n\n教育目标：可以提供抵押贷款来确保偿还抵押人以外的其他人所欠的债务，如果债务人违约，抵押权人可以取消抵押财产的赎回权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 1.3（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（保证非抵押人义务的抵押贷款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"可以提供抵押贷款以确保偿还抵押人以外的其他人所欠的债务，如果债务人违约，抵押权人可以取消抵押财产的赎回权。","iracZh":{"issue":"银行能否成功取消这块土地的赎回权？","rule":"可以提供抵押贷款以确保偿还抵押人以外的其他人所欠的债务，如果债务人违约，抵押权人可以取消抵押财产的赎回权。","analysis":"抵押文件\n\n抵押契据 表达抵押品权益以担保债务的书面文件\n\n本票 借款人承诺按照所列条款偿还债务票据的正式“借条”\n\n抵押贷款是为确保债务偿还而给予不动产的留置权。如果债务（由本票证明）属于抵押人以外的其他人，则抵押人个人不承担偿还该债务的责任。*但是，如果债务人违约，抵押权人可以取消抵押财产的赎回权。在这里，这位女士从银行获得了10万美元的贷款，并有一张期票作为证明。兄弟没有签署本票，因此他个人不承担债务。但由于他用自己的一块土地来偿还这位女士的债务，银行对该土地拥有有效的抵押。因此，在该女子拖欠贷款后，银行将成功取消其兄弟土地的赎回权。 *如果抵押贷款本身包含这样的语言，抵押人将对债务承担个人责任。 （选项A）抵押贷款债务是由其他人所欠的事实并不意味着兄弟的抵押贷款无效。这仅仅意味着兄弟个人不承担偿还抵押贷款债务的责任。 （选择 B）衡平抵押贷款是一种抵押贷款替代方案，当一方占有原始财产契约并意图将该财产作为债务担保（此处未看到）时，就会出现这种抵押贷款替代方案。 （选项D） 担保人是保证在他人不履行债务的情况下偿还他人债务的一方。在这里，银行不同意在该妇女违约的情况下为她偿还债务，因此银行不是担保人。\n\n教育目标：可以提供抵押贷款来确保偿还抵押人以外的其他人所欠的债务，如果债务人违约，抵押权人可以取消抵押财产的赎回权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 1.3（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（保证非抵押人义务的抵押贷款）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为银行有有效的抵押贷款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":49,"sourceQuestionPage":144,"sourceQuestionPages":[144],"sourceExplanationPages":[145,146],"sourceAnswerPages":[145,146],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-050","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman owned several vacant lots in a subdivision. She obtained a $500,000 loan from a bank and executed and delivered to the bank a promissory note and mortgage describing Lots 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5. The mortgage was promptly and properly recorded. Upon payment of $100,000, the woman obtained a release of Lot 2 duly executed by the bank. She altered the instrument of release to include Lot 5 as well as Lot 2 and recorded it. The woman thereafter sold Lot 5 to a man, an innocent purchaser, for value. The bank discovered that the instrument of release had been altered and brought an appropriate action against the woman and the man to set aside the release as it applied to Lot 5. The woman did not defend against the action, but the man did. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No unrecorded conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value without notice, who shall first record.\" Who should the court rule for?","choices":{"A":"The bank, because the alteration of the release was ineffective.","B":"The bank, because the man could have discovered the alteration by reasonable inquiry.","C":"The man, because he was entitled to rely on the recorded release.","D":"The man, because the bank was negligent in failing to check the recordation of the release."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Void v. Voidable contracts\n\nVoid Forgery Illegality (invalid upon creation) Fraud in the factum (assent by trick) Duress by physical compulsion Voidable Lack of capacity Mistake (valid unless set aside) Misrepresentation Duress by threat Undue influence Fraud in the inducement Recording acts determine the priority of competing interests in the same land. In a race- notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) subsequent property interest has priority over an earlier property interest if the BFP (1) lacked notice and (2) recorded first. But a BFP cannot rely on a forged or altered document to establish priority under the recording act since that document is void. Here, the woman sold Lot 5 to the man, an innocent purchaser for value (BFP), after altering the mortgage release for Lot 2 to include Lot 5. But since the alteration of the release was ineffective (ie, void), the man cannot rely on the release to establish his priority under the recording act (Choice C). Therefore, the court should rule for the bank and set aside the altered release. (Choice B) The man would not have been a BFP if he could have discovered the alteration of the release by reasonable inquiry—eg, had a different recorded instrument explained that Lot 5 was not released. But since there is no evidence that he could have reasonably discovered the alteration, he was a BFP. Nevertheless, the bank will prevail because the altered release was ineffective. (Choice D) The man cannot rely on the altered release to establish his priority because the recording act does not protect BFPs from forged or altered documents. As a result, the bank's negligence (ie, failure to use reasonable care) in failing to check the recordation of the release is immaterial.\n\nEducational objective: In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser who (1) lacks notice and (2) records first will prevail. But a BFP cannot rely on a forged or altered document to establish priority since that document is void.","educationalObjective":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser who (1) lacks notice and (2) records first will prevail. But a BFP cannot rely on a forged or altered document to establish priority since that document is void.","references":"","rule":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser who (1) lacks notice and (2) records first will prevail. But a BFP cannot rely on a forged or altered document to establish priority since that document is void.","irac":{"issue":"A woman owned several vacant lots in a subdivision. She obtained a $500,000 loan from a bank and executed and delivered to the bank a promissory note and mortgage describing Lots 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5. The mortgage was promptly and properly recorded. Upon payment of $100,000, the woman obtained a release of Lot 2 duly executed by the bank. She altered the instrument of release to include Lot 5 as well as Lot 2 and recorded it. The woman thereafter sold Lot 5 to a man, an innocent purchaser, for value. The bank discovered that the instrument of release had been altered and brought an appropriate action against the woman and the man to set aside the release as it applied to Lot 5. The woman did not defend against the action, but the man did. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No unrecorded conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value without notice, who shall first record.\" Who should the court rule for?","rule":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser who (1) lacks notice and (2) records first will prevail. But a BFP cannot rely on a forged or altered document to establish priority since that document is void.","analysis":"Void v. Voidable contracts\n\nVoid Forgery Illegality (invalid upon creation) Fraud in the factum (assent by trick) Duress by physical compulsion Voidable Lack of capacity Mistake (valid unless set aside) Misrepresentation Duress by threat Undue influence Fraud in the inducement Recording acts determine the priority of competing interests in the same land. In a race- notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) subsequent property interest has priority over an earlier property interest if the BFP (1) lacked notice and (2) recorded first. But a BFP cannot rely on a forged or altered document to establish priority under the recording act since that document is void. Here, the woman sold Lot 5 to the man, an innocent purchaser for value (BFP), after altering the mortgage release for Lot 2 to include Lot 5. But since the alteration of the release was ineffective (ie, void), the man cannot rely on the release to establish his priority under the recording act (Choice C). Therefore, the court should rule for the bank and set aside the altered release. (Choice B) The man would not have been a BFP if he could have discovered the alteration of the release by reasonable inquiry—eg, had a different recorded instrument explained that Lot 5 was not released. But since there is no evidence that he could have reasonably discovered the alteration, he was a BFP. Nevertheless, the bank will prevail because the altered release was ineffective. (Choice D) The man cannot rely on the altered release to establish his priority because the recording act does not protect BFPs from forged or altered documents. As a result, the bank's negligence (ie, failure to use reasonable care) in failing to check the recordation of the release is immaterial.","conclusion":"Answer A: The bank, because the alteration of the release was ineffective."},"questionZh":"一名妇女在一个小区拥有几块空地。她从一家银行获得了 500,000 美元的贷款，并签署并向银行交付了一份本票和抵押贷款，描述了 1、2、3、4 和 5 号地块。抵押贷款得到了及时、正确的记录。支付 100,000 美元后，该女子获得了银行正式签署的第 2 批商品的授权书。她更改了发布工具，将 Lot 5 和 Lot 2 包括在内并进行了录制。此后，该女子将第 5 号拍品卖给了一名无辜的男子，以换取价值。银行发现免责文书已被更改，并对这名女子和男子提起适当的诉讼，以撤销适用于第 5 号批次的免责令。该女子没有对该诉讼进行辩护，但该男子却进行了辩护。该司法管辖区的备案法规定：“未经备案的不动产转让或抵押不得对后续购买者有利于未经通知的价值，后续购买者应首先进行记录。”法院应该为谁作出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"银行，因为变更发布无效。","B":"银行，因为该男子可以通过合理询问发现更改。","C":"这个人，因为他有权依赖录音释放。","D":"该男子，因为银行疏忽，没有检查释放记录。"},"explanationZh":"无效诉可撤销合同\n\n无效 伪造 非法（创建时无效） 事实欺诈（以诡计同意） 身体强迫胁迫 无效 缺乏行为能力 错误（除非搁置，否则有效） 失实陈述 威胁胁迫 不当影响 诱导欺诈 记录行为决定同一土地上竞争利益的优先顺序。在竞赛通知司法管辖区（如此处所示），如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) (BFP) (1) 缺乏通知且 (2) 首先记录，则其后续财产权益优先于先前的财产权益。但 BFP 不能依靠伪造或更改的文件来确定记录法下的优先权，因为该文件无效。在此，女子将拍品 2 的抵押免责书更改为包括拍品 5 后，将拍品 5 卖给了一名无罪价值购买者 (BFP)。但由于对拍品的更改无效（即无效），因此该男子不能依靠该免责声明来确定其在记录法下的优先权（选择 C）。因此，法院应作出有利于银行的裁决，并撤销变更后的解除。 （选项 B）如果该男子能够通过合理询问发现授权书的变更，例如，如果有不同的记录仪器解释了批次 5 未发布，则该男子就不会成为 BFP。但由于没有证据表明他能够合理地发现这种改变，因此他是一名 BFP。尽管如此，银行还是会获胜，因为更改后的发布无效。 （选项 D）该男子不能依靠更改的释放来确定其优先权，因为记录行为不能保护 BFP 免受伪造或更改文件的侵害。因此，银行未能检查释放记录的疏忽（即未能采取合理的谨慎措施）并不重要。\n\n教育目标：在竞赛通知司法管辖区，（1）缺乏通知和（2）先记录的善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）将获胜。但 BFP 不能依靠伪造或更改的文件来确定优先权，因为该文件无效。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在比赛通知司法管辖区，（1）缺乏通知和（2）首先记录的善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）将优先。但 BFP 不能依靠伪造或更改的文件来确定优先权，因为该文件无效。","iracZh":{"issue":"一名妇女在一个小区拥有几块空地。她从一家银行获得了 500,000 美元的贷款，并签署并向银行交付了一份本票和抵押贷款，描述了 1、2、3、4 和 5 号地块。抵押贷款得到了及时、正确的记录。支付 100,000 美元后，该女子获得了银行正式签署的第 2 批商品的授权书。她更改了发布工具，将 Lot 5 和 Lot 2 包括在内并进行了录制。此后，该女子将第 5 号拍品卖给了一名无辜的男子，以换取价值。银行发现免责文书已被更改，并对这名女子和男子提起适当的诉讼，以撤销适用于第 5 号批次的免责令。该女子没有对该诉讼进行辩护，但该男子却进行了辩护。该司法管辖区的备案法规定：“未经备案的不动产转让或抵押不得对后续购买者有利于未经通知的价值，后续购买者应首先进行记录。”法院应该为谁作出裁决？","rule":"在比赛通知司法管辖区，（1）缺乏通知和（2）首先记录的善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）将优先。但 BFP 不能依靠伪造或更改的文件来确定优先权，因为该文件无效。","analysis":"无效诉可撤销合同\n\n无效 伪造 非法（创建时无效） 事实欺诈（以诡计同意） 身体强迫胁迫 无效 缺乏行为能力 错误（除非搁置，否则有效） 失实陈述 威胁胁迫 不当影响 诱导欺诈 记录行为决定同一土地上竞争利益的优先顺序。在竞赛通知司法管辖区（如此处所示），如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) (BFP) (1) 缺乏通知且 (2) 首先记录，则其后续财产权益优先于先前的财产权益。但 BFP 不能依靠伪造或更改的文件来确定记录法下的优先权，因为该文件无效。在此，女子将拍品 2 的抵押免责书更改为包括拍品 5 后，将拍品 5 卖给了一名无罪价值购买者 (BFP)。但由于对拍品的更改无效（即无效），因此该男子不能依靠该免责声明来确定其在记录法下的优先权（选择 C）。因此，法院应作出有利于银行的裁决，并撤销变更后的解除。 （选项 B）如果该男子能够通过合理询问发现授权书的变更，例如，如果有不同的记录仪器解释了批次 5 未发布，则该男子就不会成为 BFP。但由于没有证据表明他能够合理地发现这种改变，因此他是一名 BFP。尽管如此，银行还是会获胜，因为更改后的发布无效。 （选项 D）该男子不能依靠更改的释放来确定其优先权，因为记录行为不能保护 BFP 免受伪造或更改文件的侵害。因此，银行未能检查释放记录的疏忽（即未能采取合理的谨慎措施）并不重要。\n\n教育目标：在竞赛通知司法管辖区，（1）缺乏通知和（2）先记录的善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）将获胜。但 BFP 不能依靠伪造或更改的文件来确定优先权，因为该文件无效。","conclusion":"答案 A：银行，因为变更发布无效。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":50,"sourceQuestionPage":147,"sourceQuestionPages":[147],"sourceExplanationPages":[148,149],"sourceAnswerPages":[148,149],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-051","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman borrowed money from her local credit union to purchase an undeveloped lot on which to build her dream home. She executed a promissory note agreeing to repay the loan over a 10-year period. The loan was secured by a deed of trust that contained a power-of- sale clause. The deed of trust was promptly recorded. After the woman missed five loan payments, the credit union accelerated the debt and instructed the trustee to initiate nonjudicial foreclosure proceedings as permitted by the jurisdiction. The trustee followed the jurisdiction's notice requirements and procedures. At the foreclosure sale, the credit union was the sole bidder. The winning bid was equal to the remaining obligation on the note, amounting to only 60% of the fair market value of the property. The woman immediately filed an action against the credit union to set aside the foreclosure sale. Is the woman likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a court cannot overturn a nonjudicial foreclosure on a deed of trust.","B":"No, because there is no indication that the procedure or price would shock the conscience of the court.","C":"Yes, because a deed of trust can be foreclosed upon only by judicial foreclosure.","D":"Yes, because a lender may not purchase the mortgaged property at a nonjudicial foreclosure."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Foreclosure methods\n\nJudicial sale Judicially supervised public sale of mortgaged property\n\n(all states)\n\nNonjudicial Privately conducted public sale of mortgaged property (permitted only if sale mortgage contains power-of-sale clause)\n\n(half of all states)\n\nStrict Foreclosure without sale of mortgaged property foreclosure\n\n(few states)\n\nA debtor may give his/her interest in real property to a lender to secure a debt—usually through a mortgage or a deed of trust. A deed of trust is created when the debtor conveys the property interest to an impartial third party, as trustee, who holds title to the property for the lender's benefit. If the debtor pays the debt in full, then the trustee must reconvey the property to the debtor. But if the debtor defaults on loan payments, the trustee can initiate foreclosure proceedings (see table above). The trustee can initiate nonjudicial foreclosure in about half of all states if the mortgage or deed of trust contains a power-of-sale clause—as seen here (Choice C). This allows the trustee to sell the property at a public auction without requesting permission from the court. However, the debtor can ask a court to overturn the foreclosure if the auction or sales process violated due process or the purchase price was grossly inadequate* (Choice A). Courts have consistently been unwilling to impose a \"fair market value\" standard on the purchase price, so the lender can credit bid up to the amount of the outstanding debt without paying additional cash (Choice D). Therefore, the winning bid is often below fair market value. Here, there was no due process violation because the jurisdiction's notice requirements and procedures were followed. And though the credit union's winning bid was only 60% of the property's fair market value, it was equal to the remaining obligation on the note. Therefore, this credit bid was not grossly inadequate. Because neither the procedure nor the price would shock the conscience of the court, the woman is not likely to prevail in her action to set aside the foreclosure sale.\n\n*Purchase prices have been deemed grossly inadequate if they are below 20% of the fair market value.\n\nEducational objective: Nonjudicial foreclosures are allowed in most states if the mortgage or deed of trust contains a power-of-sale clause. However, the court can overturn the foreclosure if the auction or sales process violated due process or the purchase price was grossly inadequate.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages §8.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (adequacy of foreclosure sale price).","educationalObjective":"Nonjudicial foreclosures are allowed in most states if the mortgage or deed of trust contains a power-of-sale clause. However, the court can overturn the foreclosure if the auction or sales process violated due process or the purchase price was grossly inadequate.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages §8.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (adequacy of foreclosure sale price).","rule":"Nonjudicial foreclosures are allowed in most states if the mortgage or deed of trust contains a power-of-sale clause. However, the court can overturn the foreclosure if the auction or sales process violated due process or the purchase price was grossly inadequate.","irac":{"issue":"Is the woman likely to prevail?","rule":"Nonjudicial foreclosures are allowed in most states if the mortgage or deed of trust contains a power-of-sale clause. However, the court can overturn the foreclosure if the auction or sales process violated due process or the purchase price was grossly inadequate.","analysis":"Foreclosure methods\n\nJudicial sale Judicially supervised public sale of mortgaged property\n\n(all states)\n\nNonjudicial Privately conducted public sale of mortgaged property (permitted only if sale mortgage contains power-of-sale clause)\n\n(half of all states)\n\nStrict Foreclosure without sale of mortgaged property foreclosure\n\n(few states)\n\nA debtor may give his/her interest in real property to a lender to secure a debt—usually through a mortgage or a deed of trust. A deed of trust is created when the debtor conveys the property interest to an impartial third party, as trustee, who holds title to the property for the lender's benefit. If the debtor pays the debt in full, then the trustee must reconvey the property to the debtor. But if the debtor defaults on loan payments, the trustee can initiate foreclosure proceedings (see table above). The trustee can initiate nonjudicial foreclosure in about half of all states if the mortgage or deed of trust contains a power-of-sale clause—as seen here (Choice C). This allows the trustee to sell the property at a public auction without requesting permission from the court. However, the debtor can ask a court to overturn the foreclosure if the auction or sales process violated due process or the purchase price was grossly inadequate* (Choice A). Courts have consistently been unwilling to impose a \"fair market value\" standard on the purchase price, so the lender can credit bid up to the amount of the outstanding debt without paying additional cash (Choice D). Therefore, the winning bid is often below fair market value. Here, there was no due process violation because the jurisdiction's notice requirements and procedures were followed. And though the credit union's winning bid was only 60% of the property's fair market value, it was equal to the remaining obligation on the note. Therefore, this credit bid was not grossly inadequate. Because neither the procedure nor the price would shock the conscience of the court, the woman is not likely to prevail in her action to set aside the foreclosure sale.\n\n*Purchase prices have been deemed grossly inadequate if they are below 20% of the fair market value.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because there is no indication that the procedure or price would shock the conscience of the court."},"questionZh":"一名妇女从当地信用社借钱购买了一块未开发的土地，并在上面建造了她的梦想家园。她签署了一份期票，同意在 10 年内偿还贷款。该贷款由包含出售权条款的信托契约担保。信托契约立即被记录下来。在该妇女未能支付五笔贷款后，信用合作社加速了债务偿还，并指示受托人在司法管辖区允许的情况下启动非司法止赎程序。受托人遵循司法管辖区的通知要求和程序。在止赎拍卖中，信用合作社是唯一的投标人。中标金额等于票据上的剩余债务，仅相当于该财产公平市场价值的 60%。该女子立即对信用合作社提起诉讼，要求搁置止赎出售。女人有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为法院不能推翻基于信托契约的非司法丧失抵押品赎回权。","B":"不会，因为没有迹象表明该程序或价格会震撼法院的良心。","C":"是的，因为信托契约只能通过司法取消抵押品赎回权来取消抵押品赎回权。","D":"是的，因为贷款人不得以非司法丧失抵押品赎回权的方式购买抵押财产。"},"explanationZh":"止赎方法\n\n司法出售 司法监督下抵押财产的公开出售\n\n（所有州）\n\n非司法私下公开出售抵押财产（仅当出售抵押包含出售权条款时才允许）\n\n（占所有州的一半）\n\n严格止赎，不出售抵押财产止赎\n\n（少数州）\n\n债务人可以将其在不动产中的权益交给贷方以担保债务——通常通过抵押贷款或信托契约。当债务人将财产权益转让给公正的第三方（作为受托人）时，信托契约就成立了，受托人为了贷款人的利益而持有财产的所有权。如果债务人全额偿还债务，则受托人必须将财产返还给债务人。但如果债务人拖欠贷款，受托人可以启动止赎程序（见上表）。如果抵押贷款或信托契约包含出售权条款，受托人可以在大约一半的州启动非司法取消抵押品赎回权——如此处所示（选项 C）。这使得受托人可以在公开拍卖中出售财产，而无需请求法院许可。然而，如果拍卖或销售过程违反正当程序或购买价格严重不足*（选择 A），债务人可以请求法院推翻取消抵押品赎回权。法院一直不愿意对购买价格强加“公平市场价值”标准，因此贷款人可以在不支付额外现金的情况下对未偿债务金额进行信用竞价（选择D）。因此，中标价格往往低于公平市场价值。在此，不存在违反正当程序的情况，因为遵循了司法管辖区的通知要求和程序。尽管信用合作社的中标价格仅为该房产公平市场价值的 60%，但它与票据上的剩余义务相等。因此，本次信用投标并不算严重不足。由于程序和价格都不会震撼法院的良心，因此该妇女在撤销止赎拍卖的诉讼中不太可能获胜。\n\n*如果购买价格低于公平市场价值的 20%，则被视为严重不足。\n\n教育目标：如果抵押贷款或信托契约包含出售权条款，大多数州都允许非司法取消抵押品赎回权。然而，如果拍卖或销售过程违反正当程序或购买价格严重不足，法院可以推翻取消抵押品赎回权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 §8.3（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（取消抵押品赎回权销售价格的充分性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果抵押贷款或信托契约包含出售权条款，大多数州都允许非司法取消抵押品赎回权。然而，如果拍卖或销售过程违反正当程序或购买价格严重不足，法院可以推翻取消抵押品赎回权。","iracZh":{"issue":"女人有可能获胜吗？","rule":"如果抵押贷款或信托契约包含出售权条款，大多数州都允许非司法取消抵押品赎回权。然而，如果拍卖或销售过程违反正当程序或购买价格严重不足，法院可以推翻取消抵押品赎回权。","analysis":"止赎方法\n\n司法出售 司法监督下抵押财产的公开出售\n\n（所有州）\n\n非司法私下公开出售抵押财产（仅当出售抵押包含出售权条款时才允许）\n\n（占所有州的一半）\n\n严格止赎，不出售抵押财产止赎\n\n（少数州）\n\n债务人可以将其在不动产中的权益交给贷方以担保债务——通常通过抵押贷款或信托契约。当债务人将财产权益转让给公正的第三方（作为受托人）时，信托契约就成立了，受托人为了贷款人的利益而持有财产的所有权。如果债务人全额偿还债务，则受托人必须将财产返还给债务人。但如果债务人拖欠贷款，受托人可以启动止赎程序（见上表）。如果抵押贷款或信托契约包含出售权条款，受托人可以在大约一半的州启动非司法取消抵押品赎回权——如此处所示（选项 C）。这使得受托人可以在公开拍卖中出售财产，而无需请求法院许可。然而，如果拍卖或销售过程违反正当程序或购买价格严重不足*（选择 A），债务人可以请求法院推翻取消抵押品赎回权。法院一直不愿意对购买价格强加“公平市场价值”标准，因此贷款人可以在不支付额外现金的情况下对未偿债务金额进行信用竞价（选择D）。因此，中标价格往往低于公平市场价值。在此，不存在违反正当程序的情况，因为遵循了司法管辖区的通知要求和程序。尽管信用合作社的中标价格仅为该房产公平市场价值的 60%，但它与票据上的剩余义务相等。因此，本次信用投标并不算严重不足。由于程序和价格都不会震撼法院的良心，因此该妇女在撤销止赎拍卖的诉讼中不太可能获胜。\n\n*如果购买价格低于公平市场价值的 20%，则被视为严重不足。\n\n教育目标：如果抵押贷款或信托契约包含出售权条款，大多数州都允许非司法取消抵押品赎回权。然而，如果拍卖或销售过程违反正当程序或购买价格严重不足，法院可以推翻取消抵押品赎回权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 §8.3（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（取消抵押品赎回权销售价格的充分性）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为没有迹象表明该程序或价格会震撼法院的良心。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":51,"sourceQuestionPage":150,"sourceQuestionPages":[150],"sourceExplanationPages":[151,152],"sourceAnswerPages":[151,152],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-052","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A developer entered into a valid written contract to purchase a large tract of land from a contractor for its fair market value of $500,000. The contract was assignable by the developer. The developer duly notified the contractor to convey title to her and her business partner whom the developer had not seen for many years. When the developer learned that the business partner would have to sign certain documents in connection with the closing, she prevailed upon her brother to attend the closing and pretend to be the business partner. The developer and the brother attended the closing, and the contractor executed an instrument in the proper form of a deed purporting to convey the land to the developer and the business partner as tenants in common. The brother pretended that he was the business partner, and he signed the business partner's name to all the required documents. The developer provided the entire $500,000 consideration for the transaction. The deed was promptly and properly recorded. Unknown to the developer or her brother, the business partner had died several months before the closing. The business partner's will, which was duly probated, devised \"All my real estate to my nephew\" and the residue of his estate to the developer. The developer and nephew have been unable to agree as to the status or disposition of the land. The nephew brought an appropriate action against the contractor and the developer to quiet legal title to an undivided one-half interest in the land. In whom should the court hold that legal title to the land has vested?","choices":{"A":"All in the contractor.","B":"All in the developer.","C":"One-half in the developer and one-half in the contractor.","D":"One-half in the developer and one-half in the nephew."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Deed requirements\n\nRequirements Valid Invalid\n\nWritten & signed Any written instrument Oral (except permissible gift) by grantor signed by grantor Signed only by grantee Identifies grantor Separate grantor & grantee Ambiguous/unidentifiable grantee(s) & grantee (except concurrent estates) Nonexistent grantee Identifiable by name or description Identifies land Identifiable by reasonable Ambiguous description (but extrinsic certainty—eg: evidence admissible) Government survey Undefined part of larger parcel Metes & bounds Street, lot, house number Includes words of \"Convey\" \"Lease\" transfer \"Transfer\" \"Rent\" \"Grant\" \"License\" \"Sell\" A tenancy in common is a concurrent ownership interest in land where each co-tenant has a separate share of the property but an equal right to possess the whole property (ie, separate but undivided interests). Co-tenants can receive this interest under a deed, but that deed is only valid if it: is in writing and signed by the grantor unambiguously identifies the grantor and grantee unambiguously describes the land and includes words of transfer. A deed that names a nonexistent co-tenant is void as to that co-tenant. Therefore, the grantor retains the nonexistent co-tenant's interest and becomes tenants in common with the other co-tenant(s) named in the deed. Here, the contractor attempted to deed land to the business partner and developer as tenants in common. But since the business partner had died several months earlier (and was therefore a nonexistent co-tenant), the portion of the deed naming him as grantee is void. Therefore, the court should hold that the contractor (the grantor) and the developer (other co-tenant) each have one-half interests in the land as tenants in common (Choices A & B).\n\n(Choice D) Although the business partner devised his property to the nephew, the business partner had already died when the contractor attempted to deed him the land. Therefore, the deed did not convey the business partner an interest in the land that could pass to the nephew.\n\nEducational objective: When a tenancy in common is deeded to a nonexistent (eg, deceased) co-tenant, the deed is void as to that co-tenant and the grantor retains the co-tenant's interest. As a result, a tenancy in common forms between the grantor and the other co-tenant(s) named in the deed.","educationalObjective":"When a tenancy in common is deeded to a nonexistent (eg, deceased) co-tenant, the deed is void as to that co-tenant and the grantor retains the co-tenant's interest. As a result, a tenancy in common forms between the grantor and the other co-tenant(s) named in the deed.","references":"","rule":"When a tenancy in common is deeded to a nonexistent (eg, deceased) co-tenant, the deed is void as to that co-tenant and the grantor retains the co-tenant's interest. As a result, a tenancy in common forms between the grantor and the other co-tenant(s) named in the deed.","irac":{"issue":"In whom should the court hold that legal title to the land has vested?","rule":"When a tenancy in common is deeded to a nonexistent (eg, deceased) co-tenant, the deed is void as to that co-tenant and the grantor retains the co-tenant's interest. As a result, a tenancy in common forms between the grantor and the other co-tenant(s) named in the deed.","analysis":"Deed requirements\n\nRequirements Valid Invalid\n\nWritten & signed Any written instrument Oral (except permissible gift) by grantor signed by grantor Signed only by grantee Identifies grantor Separate grantor & grantee Ambiguous/unidentifiable grantee(s) & grantee (except concurrent estates) Nonexistent grantee Identifiable by name or description Identifies land Identifiable by reasonable Ambiguous description (but extrinsic certainty—eg: evidence admissible) Government survey Undefined part of larger parcel Metes & bounds Street, lot, house number Includes words of \"Convey\" \"Lease\" transfer \"Transfer\" \"Rent\" \"Grant\" \"License\" \"Sell\" A tenancy in common is a concurrent ownership interest in land where each co-tenant has a separate share of the property but an equal right to possess the whole property (ie, separate but undivided interests). Co-tenants can receive this interest under a deed, but that deed is only valid if it: is in writing and signed by the grantor unambiguously identifies the grantor and grantee unambiguously describes the land and includes words of transfer. A deed that names a nonexistent co-tenant is void as to that co-tenant. Therefore, the grantor retains the nonexistent co-tenant's interest and becomes tenants in common with the other co-tenant(s) named in the deed. Here, the contractor attempted to deed land to the business partner and developer as tenants in common. But since the business partner had died several months earlier (and was therefore a nonexistent co-tenant), the portion of the deed naming him as grantee is void. Therefore, the court should hold that the contractor (the grantor) and the developer (other co-tenant) each have one-half interests in the land as tenants in common (Choices A & B).\n\n(Choice D) Although the business partner devised his property to the nephew, the business partner had already died when the contractor attempted to deed him the land. Therefore, the deed did not convey the business partner an interest in the land that could pass to the nephew.","conclusion":"Answer C: One-half in the developer and one-half in the contractor."},"questionZh":"开发商签订了一份有效的书面合同，以 50 万美元的公平市场价值从承包商手中购买大片土地。合同可由开发商转让。开发商及时通知承包商将产权转让给她和她多年未见的商业伙伴。当开发商得知业务合作伙伴必须签署与交割有关的某些文件时，她说服她的兄弟参加交割并假装是业务合作伙伴。开发商和兄弟出席了交割仪式，承包商以适当的契约形式签署了一份文书，旨在将土地转让给开发商和商业伙伴作为按份共有人（tenant in common）。这位兄弟假装自己是商业伙伴，并在所有必需的文件上签下了商业伙伴的名字。开发商为此次交易支付了全部 50 万美元的对价。该行为被及时、正确地记录下来。开发商和她的兄弟都不知道，这位商业伙伴在交割前几个月就去世了。商业伙伴的遗嘱经过正式验证，规定“我所有的房地产归我的侄子”，而他的剩余房地产则归开发商。开发商和侄子未能就土地的状况或处置达成一致。侄子对承包商和开发商提起了适当的诉讼，以平息对该土地不可分割的二分之一权益的合法所有权。法院应该认定土地的合法所有权归属于谁？","choicesZh":{"A":"一切都在承包商身上。","B":"一切尽在开发商手中。","C":"一半是开发商，一半是承包商。","D":"开发商一半，侄子一半。"},"explanationZh":"契约要求\n\n要求 有效 无效\n\n书面和签署 任何书面文件，由授予人口头签署（允许的赠与除外），由授予人签署 仅由受让人签署 确定授予人的身份 单独的授予人和受让人 模糊/无法识别的受让人和受让人（共同财产除外） 不存在的受让人 可通过姓名或描述识别 识别土地 可通过合理的描述识别 不明确（但外在确定性 — 例如：证据可接受） 政府调查 大地块的未定义部分 边界和边界街道、地段、门牌号码 包括“转让”“租赁”转让“转让”“出租”“授予”“许可”“出售”等词语 共同共有（joint tenancy）是土地的共同所有权权益，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）拥有单独的财产份额，但拥有拥有整个财产的平等权利（即单独但不可分割的权益）。按份共有人（tenant in common）可以根据契约获得该权益，但该契约只有在以下情况下才有效： 以书面形式并由授予人明确签署 明确标识授予人和受让人 明确描述土地并包括转让字词。指定不存在的按份共有人（tenant in common）的契约对于该按份共有人（tenant in common）来说是无效的。因此，设保人保留不存在的按份共有人（tenant in common）的权益，并与契约中指定的其他按份共有人（tenant in common）成为按份共有人（tenant in common）。在这里，承包商试图将土地契约给商业伙伴和开发商作为按份共有人（tenant in common）。但由于商业伙伴在几个月前去世（因此是不存在的按份共有人（tenant in common）），契约中将他命名为受让人的部分是无效的。因此，法院应认定承包商（设保人）和开发商（其他按份共有人（tenant in common））作为按份共有人（tenant in common），各自拥有该土地二分之一的权益（选项A和B）。\n\n（选项D）虽然商业伙伴将其财产设计给了侄子，但当承包商试图将土地转让给他时，商业伙伴已经去世。因此，该契约并未将商业伙伴对土地的权益转让给侄子。\n\n教育目标：当共同共有（joint tenancy）被转让给不存在的（例如已故的）按份共有人（tenant in common）时，该契约对该按份共有人（tenant in common）而言无效，且授予人保留该按份共有人（tenant in common）的权益。因此，授予人和契约中指定的其他按份共有人（tenant in common）之间形成共同形式的租赁。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当共同共有（joint tenancy）被转让给不存在的（例如已故的）按份共有人（tenant in common）时，该契约对该按份共有人（tenant in common）而言无效，并且授予人保留该按份共有人（tenant in common）的权益。因此，授予人和契约中指定的其他按份共有人（tenant in common）之间形成共同形式的租赁。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该认定土地的合法所有权归属于谁？","rule":"当共同共有（joint tenancy）被转让给不存在的（例如已故的）按份共有人（tenant in common）时，该契约对该按份共有人（tenant in common）而言无效，并且授予人保留该按份共有人（tenant in common）的权益。因此，授予人和契约中指定的其他按份共有人（tenant in common）之间形成共同形式的租赁。","analysis":"契约要求\n\n要求 有效 无效\n\n书面和签署 任何书面文件，由授予人口头签署（允许的赠与除外），由授予人签署 仅由受让人签署 确定授予人的身份 单独的授予人和受让人 模糊/无法识别的受让人和受让人（共同财产除外） 不存在的受让人 可通过姓名或描述识别 识别土地 可通过合理的描述识别 不明确（但外在确定性 — 例如：证据可接受） 政府调查 大地块的未定义部分 边界和边界街道、地段、门牌号码 包括“转让”“租赁”转让“转让”“出租”“授予”“许可”“出售”等词语 共同共有（joint tenancy）是土地的共同所有权权益，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）拥有单独的财产份额，但拥有拥有整个财产的平等权利（即单独但不可分割的权益）。按份共有人（tenant in common）可以根据契约获得该权益，但该契约只有在以下情况下才有效： 以书面形式并由授予人明确签署 明确标识授予人和受让人 明确描述土地并包括转让字词。指定不存在的按份共有人（tenant in common）的契约对于该按份共有人（tenant in common）来说是无效的。因此，设保人保留不存在的按份共有人（tenant in common）的权益，并与契约中指定的其他按份共有人（tenant in common）成为按份共有人（tenant in common）。在这里，承包商试图将土地契约给商业伙伴和开发商作为按份共有人（tenant in common）。但由于商业伙伴在几个月前去世（因此是不存在的按份共有人（tenant in common）），契约中将他命名为受让人的部分是无效的。因此，法院应认定承包商（设保人）和开发商（其他按份共有人（tenant in common））作为按份共有人（tenant in common），各自拥有该土地二分之一的权益（选项A和B）。\n\n（选项D）虽然商业伙伴将其财产设计给了侄子，但当承包商试图将土地转让给他时，商业伙伴已经去世。因此，该契约并未将商业伙伴对土地的权益转让给侄子。\n\n教育目标：当共同共有（joint tenancy）被转让给不存在的（例如已故的）按份共有人（tenant in common）时，该契约对该按份共有人（tenant in common）而言无效，且授予人保留该按份共有人（tenant in common）的权益。因此，授予人和契约中指定的其他按份共有人（tenant in common）之间形成共同形式的租赁。","conclusion":"答案 C：一半是开发商，一半是承包商。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":52,"sourceQuestionPage":153,"sourceQuestionPages":[153],"sourceExplanationPages":[154,155],"sourceAnswerPages":[154,155],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-053","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A franchisee owned a hotel, subject to a mortgage securing a debt that the franchisee owed to a bank. The franchisee later acquired a nearby parking garage, financing a part of the purchase price by a loan from a credit union, secured by a mortgage on the parking garage. Two years thereafter, the franchisee defaulted on the loan owed to the bank, which caused the full amount of that loan to become immediately due and payable. The bank decided not to foreclose the mortgage on the franchisee's hotel at that time but instead brought an action, appropriate under the laws of the jurisdiction and authorized by the mortgage loan documents, for the full amount of the defaulted loan. The bank obtained and properly filed a judgment for that amount. A statute of the jurisdiction provides: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for ten years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" There is no other applicable statute, except the statute providing for judicial foreclosure of mortgages, which places no restriction on deficiency judgments. The bank later brought an appropriate action for judicial foreclosure of its first mortgage on the hotel and of its judgment lien on the parking garage. The credit union was joined as a party defendant, and appropriately counterclaimed for foreclosure of its mortgage on the parking garage, which was also in default. All procedures were properly followed, and the confirmed foreclosure sales resulted as follows: The bank purchased the hotel for $100,000 less than its mortgage balance. The bank purchased the parking garage for an amount that is $200,000 in excess of the credit union's mortgage balance. How should the $200,000 surplus arising from the bid paid by the bank for the parking garage be paid?","choices":{"A":"$100,000 to the bank and $100,000 to the credit union.","B":"$100,000 to the bank and $100,000 to the franchisee.","C":"$100,000 to the credit union and $100,000 to the franchisee.","D":"$200,000 to the franchisee."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Proceeds from a foreclosure sale are typically distributed in the following order of priority: Expenses from the sale (eg, attorneys' fees, court costs) Mortgage being foreclosed Junior liens, in order of lien priority Debtor, if any surplus remains Here, the credit union had the senior lien on the parking garage since its mortgage was placed on the property first. The bank later acquired a judgment lien on all the franchisee's property—including a junior lien on the garage—when the franchisee defaulted on the bank's loan. And since the foreclosure sale of the garage satisfied the credit union's entire mortgage and created a $200,000 surplus, the bank (as the junior lienholder) should receive $100,000 to cover the balance still owed under its mortgage on the hotel. The remaining surplus ($100,000) should then be distributed to the franchisee (Choices A, C & D).\n\nEducational objective: Proceeds from a foreclosure sale are typically distributed in the following order: expenses, foreclosed mortgage, junior liens, then the debtor.\n\nReferences\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019) (distribution of foreclosure sale proceeds).","educationalObjective":"Proceeds from a foreclosure sale are typically distributed in the following order: expenses, foreclosed mortgage, junior liens, then the debtor.","references":"55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019) (distribution of foreclosure sale proceeds).","rule":"Proceeds from a foreclosure sale are typically distributed in the following order: expenses, foreclosed mortgage, junior liens, then the debtor.","irac":{"issue":"How should the $200,000 surplus arising from the bid paid by the bank for the parking garage be paid?","rule":"Proceeds from a foreclosure sale are typically distributed in the following order: expenses, foreclosed mortgage, junior liens, then the debtor.","analysis":"Proceeds from a foreclosure sale are typically distributed in the following order of priority: Expenses from the sale (eg, attorneys' fees, court costs) Mortgage being foreclosed Junior liens, in order of lien priority Debtor, if any surplus remains Here, the credit union had the senior lien on the parking garage since its mortgage was placed on the property first. The bank later acquired a judgment lien on all the franchisee's property—including a junior lien on the garage—when the franchisee defaulted on the bank's loan. And since the foreclosure sale of the garage satisfied the credit union's entire mortgage and created a $200,000 surplus, the bank (as the junior lienholder) should receive $100,000 to cover the balance still owed under its mortgage on the hotel. The remaining surplus ($100,000) should then be distributed to the franchisee (Choices A, C & D).","conclusion":"Answer B: $100,000 to the bank and $100,000 to the franchisee."},"questionZh":"特许经营商拥有一家酒店，并需要抵押贷款来担保特许经营商欠银行的债务。特许经营商后来购买了附近的一个停车场，通过信用合作社的贷款筹集了部分购买价格，并以停车场的抵押贷款为担保。两年后，加盟商拖欠银行贷款，导致该贷款全额立即到期应付。银行当时决定不取消特许经营商酒店的抵押赎回权，而是根据司法管辖区的法律并经抵押贷款文件授权，对拖欠贷款的全额提起诉讼。银行获得并妥善提交了该金额的判决。该司法管辖区的法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起十年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”除了规定司法取消抵押品赎回权的法规外，没有其他适用的法规，该法规对缺陷判决没有限制。该银行后来提起了适当的诉讼，要求司法取消其对酒店的第一笔抵押贷款和对停车场的判决留置权。信用合作社被列为一方被告，并因其停车场抵押贷款丧失赎回权而被适当反诉，该停车场也违约了。所有程序均得到妥善执行，确认的取消抵押品赎回权销售结果如下：银行以比抵押贷款余额少 10 万美元的价格购买了该酒店。银行以超出信用合作社抵押贷款余额 20 万美元的价格购买了停车场。银行竞投停车场所产生的20万元盈余该如何支付？","choicesZh":{"A":"银行 100,000 美元，信用合作社 100,000 美元。","B":"银行 100,000 美元，加盟商 100,000 美元。","C":"100,000 美元给信用合作社，100,000 美元给特许经营商。","D":"200,000 美元给特许经营商。"},"explanationZh":"取消抵押品赎回权销售的收益通常按以下优先顺序分配： 出售费用（例如律师费、法庭费用） 抵押品被取消抵押品赎回权 初级留置权，按留置权优先顺序排列 债务人（如果还有剩余） 这里，信用合作社拥有停车场的高级留置权，因为它的抵押贷款首先放置在该房产上。当特许经营者拖欠银行贷款时，银行后来获得了特许经营者所有财产的判决留置权，包括车库的初级留置权。由于取消抵押品赎回权的车库出售满足了信用合作社的全部抵押贷款并创造了 20 万美元的盈余，因此银行（作为初级留置权人）应获得 10 万美元，以弥补其酒店抵押贷款下仍欠的余额。剩余的盈余（100,000 美元）应分配给特许经营商（选项 A、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：取消抵押品赎回权出售的收益通常按以下顺序分配：费用、取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款、初级留置权，然后是债务人。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019)（止赎出售收益的分配）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"取消抵押品赎回权出售的收益通常按以下顺序分配：费用、取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款、初级留置权，然后是债务人。","iracZh":{"issue":"银行竞投停车场所产生的20万元盈余该如何支付？","rule":"取消抵押品赎回权出售的收益通常按以下顺序分配：费用、取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款、初级留置权，然后是债务人。","analysis":"取消抵押品赎回权销售的收益通常按以下优先顺序分配： 出售费用（例如律师费、法庭费用） 抵押品被取消抵押品赎回权 初级留置权，按留置权优先顺序排列 债务人（如果还有剩余） 这里，信用合作社拥有停车场的高级留置权，因为它的抵押贷款首先放置在该房产上。当特许经营者拖欠银行贷款时，银行后来获得了特许经营者所有财产的判决留置权，包括车库的初级留置权。由于取消抵押品赎回权的车库出售满足了信用合作社的全部抵押贷款并创造了 20 万美元的盈余，因此银行（作为初级留置权人）应获得 10 万美元，以弥补其酒店抵押贷款下仍欠的余额。剩余的盈余（100,000 美元）应分配给特许经营商（选项 A、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：取消抵押品赎回权出售的收益通常按以下顺序分配：费用、取消抵押品赎回权的抵押贷款、初级留置权，然后是债务人。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 705 (2019)（止赎出售收益的分配）。","conclusion":"答案 B：银行 100,000 美元，加盟商 100,000 美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-d28b331bf7e70ed0"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":53,"sourceQuestionPage":156,"sourceQuestionPages":[156],"sourceExplanationPages":[157,158],"sourceAnswerPages":[157,158],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-054","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman borrowed money from a bank in order to build a new barn on her 20-acre farm. The loan was secured by a 10-year mortgage on the farm. The mortgage contained an acceleration clause. After three years, the woman defaulted on the loan. The bank sent a certified letter to the woman notifying her of its intention to accelerate the loan unless she cured the default within 75 days. The woman was unable to make the required payment by the 75-day deadline to cure the default. As a result, the bank accelerated the loan and initiated foreclosure proceedings against the woman. The day after being served with a summons and complaint, the woman informed the bank that she was able to make the two outstanding mortgage payments, including late fees and interest. The woman then filed a motion to dismiss the foreclosure proceedings. Should the motion to dismiss be granted?","choices":{"A":"No, because the bank initiated the foreclosure, which terminated the woman's right to redemption.","B":"No, because the woman does not have enough money to force the bank to accept her payment.","C":"Yes, because the bank is clogging the equity of redemption by failing to accept her payment.","D":"Yes, because the woman has properly exercised the statutory right of redemption."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Common mortgage provisions\n\nAcceleration clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor defaults\n\nDue-on-sale clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor sells mortgaged property without permission\n\nDue-on- Allows creditor to accelerate mortgage obligation if debtor obtains encumbrance clause second mortgage or otherwise encumbers property\n\nDefeasance clause Requires creditor to give debtor legal title to property & release mortgage lien once all payments are made\n\nMortgage documents often contain an acceleration clause, which allows the lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt plus any accrued interest if the debtor (mortgagor) defaults on the mortgage loan. If the debtor is unable to pay, then the lender can move forward with foreclosure proceedings to recover any remaining debt. However, the doctrine of equitable redemption gives the debtor an opportunity to retain the property and avoid foreclosure by paying the amount of the loan obligation currently owed at any time prior to the foreclosure sale. If the mortgage documents contain an acceleration clause, then the debtor must pay the full amount of the unpaid loan plus interest to redeem the property. Here, the woman was unable to cure her default by the bank's 75-day deadline, so the bank initiated foreclosure proceedings. Although the woman was able to make the two outstanding mortgage payments plus late fees and interest, the acceleration clause required that she pay the full amount of the unpaid loan plus interest to redeem the property. Since the woman does not have enough money to do so, her motion to dismiss the foreclosure proceedings should be denied. (Choice A) The foreclosure sale—not the initiation of the foreclosure proceedings— terminated the equitable right of redemption. (Choice C) The principle of clogging the equity of redemption refers to a lender's attempt to deny the debtor the right to redemption after the mortgage is executed—eg, by including a waiver clause in the mortgage. Since no waiver occurred here, this principle does not apply. (Choice D) Many states recognize a statutory right of redemption that allows a debtor to reclaim the property after a foreclosure sale—not before the sale, as the woman seeks to do here.\n\nEducational objective: An acceleration clause allows the lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt plus any accrued interest if the debtor defaults on the mortgage loan. If the debtor is unable to pay, then the lender can move forward with foreclosure proceedings.","educationalObjective":"An acceleration clause allows the lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt plus any accrued interest if the debtor defaults on the mortgage loan. If the debtor is unable to pay, then the lender can move forward with foreclosure proceedings.","references":"","rule":"An acceleration clause allows the lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt plus any accrued interest if the debtor defaults on the mortgage loan. If the debtor is unable to pay, then the lender can move forward with foreclosure proceedings.","irac":{"issue":"Should the motion to dismiss be granted?","rule":"An acceleration clause allows the lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt plus any accrued interest if the debtor defaults on the mortgage loan. If the debtor is unable to pay, then the lender can move forward with foreclosure proceedings.","analysis":"Common mortgage provisions\n\nAcceleration clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor defaults\n\nDue-on-sale clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor sells mortgaged property without permission\n\nDue-on- Allows creditor to accelerate mortgage obligation if debtor obtains encumbrance clause second mortgage or otherwise encumbers property\n\nDefeasance clause Requires creditor to give debtor legal title to property & release mortgage lien once all payments are made\n\nMortgage documents often contain an acceleration clause, which allows the lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt plus any accrued interest if the debtor (mortgagor) defaults on the mortgage loan. If the debtor is unable to pay, then the lender can move forward with foreclosure proceedings to recover any remaining debt. However, the doctrine of equitable redemption gives the debtor an opportunity to retain the property and avoid foreclosure by paying the amount of the loan obligation currently owed at any time prior to the foreclosure sale. If the mortgage documents contain an acceleration clause, then the debtor must pay the full amount of the unpaid loan plus interest to redeem the property. Here, the woman was unable to cure her default by the bank's 75-day deadline, so the bank initiated foreclosure proceedings. Although the woman was able to make the two outstanding mortgage payments plus late fees and interest, the acceleration clause required that she pay the full amount of the unpaid loan plus interest to redeem the property. Since the woman does not have enough money to do so, her motion to dismiss the foreclosure proceedings should be denied. (Choice A) The foreclosure sale—not the initiation of the foreclosure proceedings— terminated the equitable right of redemption. (Choice C) The principle of clogging the equity of redemption refers to a lender's attempt to deny the debtor the right to redemption after the mortgage is executed—eg, by including a waiver clause in the mortgage. Since no waiver occurred here, this principle does not apply. (Choice D) Many states recognize a statutory right of redemption that allows a debtor to reclaim the property after a foreclosure sale—not before the sale, as the woman seeks to do here.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the woman does not have enough money to force the bank to accept her payment."},"questionZh":"一名妇女向银行借钱，以便在她 20 英亩的农场上建造一个新谷仓。这笔贷款以农场十年期抵押贷款作为担保。抵押贷款包含加速条款。三年后，该女子拖欠贷款。银行向该女士发送了一封经过认证的信函，通知她除非她在 75 天内解决违约问题，否则银行打算加速贷款。该妇女无法在 75 天的期限内支付所需的款项以解决违约问题。结果，银行加快了贷款速度，并对这名女子启动了止赎程序。在收到传票和投诉后的第二天，该妇女通知银行，她能够支付两笔未偿还的抵押贷款，包括滞纳金和利息。该女子随后提出动议，要求驳回止赎程序。驳回动议是否应获批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为银行发起了止赎，从而终止了该女子的赎回权。","B":"不，因为该妇女没有足够的钱来迫使银行接受她的付款。","C":"是的，因为银行因不接受她的付款而阻碍了赎回权益。","D":"是的，因为女方已经适当行使了法定的赎回权。"},"explanationZh":"常见抵押条款\n\n加速条款 如果债务人违约，债权人可以要求全额到期应付贷款\n\n到期出售条款 如果债务人未经许可出售抵押财产，债权人可以要求偿还全部到期应付贷款\n\n到期 - 如果债务人获得产权负担条款第二抵押贷款或以其他方式抵押财产，则允许债权人加速抵押贷款义务\n\n废止条款 要求债权人给予债务人财产的合法所有权并在支付所有款项后释放抵押留置权\n\n抵押文件通常包含加速条款，如果债务人（抵押人）拖欠抵押贷款，贷款人可以要求全额支付剩余抵押债务以及任何应计利息。如果债务人无法付款，则贷款人可以继续进行止赎程序以收回任何剩余债务。然而，公平赎回原则使债务人有机会保留财产并通过在丧失抵押品赎回权出售之前的任何时间支付当前所欠的贷款义务来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。如果抵押文件包含加速条款，则债务人必须全额支付未付贷款加上利息才能赎回财产。在此，该妇女无法在银行 75 天的期限内解决她的违约问题，因此银行启动了止赎程序。尽管该女子能够支付两笔未偿还的抵押贷款以及滞纳金和利息，但加速条款要求她全额支付未付贷款和利息以赎回房产。由于该妇女没有足够的钱来这样做，因此她驳回止赎程序的动议应被驳回。 （选项A）止赎出售——而非止赎程序的启动——终止了公平的赎回权。 （选项C） 堵塞赎回权原则是指贷款人在抵押贷款执行后试图剥夺债务人的赎回权，例如在抵押贷款中加入放弃条款。由于此处没有发生弃权，因此该原则不适用。 （选项 D）许多州承认法定的赎回权，允许债务人在取消抵押品赎回权出售后收回财产，而不是像这位女士在这里寻求的那样，在出售之前收回财产。\n\n教育目标：如果债务人拖欠抵押贷款，加速条款允许贷方要求全额支付剩余抵押贷款债务以及任何应计利息。如果债务人无法付款，则贷款人可以继续进行止赎程序。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果债务人拖欠抵押贷款，加速条款允许贷款人要求全额支付剩余抵押贷款债务以及任何应计利息。如果债务人无法付款，则贷款人可以继续进行止赎程序。","iracZh":{"issue":"驳回动议是否应获批准？","rule":"如果债务人拖欠抵押贷款，加速条款允许贷款人要求全额支付剩余抵押贷款债务以及任何应计利息。如果债务人无法付款，则贷款人可以继续进行止赎程序。","analysis":"常见抵押条款\n\n加速条款 如果债务人违约，债权人可以要求全额到期应付贷款\n\n到期出售条款 如果债务人未经许可出售抵押财产，债权人可以要求偿还全部到期应付贷款\n\n到期 - 如果债务人获得产权负担条款第二抵押贷款或以其他方式抵押财产，则允许债权人加速抵押贷款义务\n\n废止条款 要求债权人给予债务人财产的合法所有权并在支付所有款项后释放抵押留置权\n\n抵押文件通常包含加速条款，如果债务人（抵押人）拖欠抵押贷款，贷款人可以要求全额支付剩余抵押债务以及任何应计利息。如果债务人无法付款，则贷款人可以继续进行止赎程序以收回任何剩余债务。然而，公平赎回原则使债务人有机会保留财产并通过在丧失抵押品赎回权出售之前的任何时间支付当前所欠的贷款义务来避免丧失抵押品赎回权。如果抵押文件包含加速条款，则债务人必须全额支付未付贷款加上利息才能赎回财产。在此，该妇女无法在银行 75 天的期限内解决她的违约问题，因此银行启动了止赎程序。尽管该女子能够支付两笔未偿还的抵押贷款以及滞纳金和利息，但加速条款要求她全额支付未付贷款和利息以赎回房产。由于该妇女没有足够的钱来这样做，因此她驳回止赎程序的动议应被驳回。 （选项A）止赎出售——而非止赎程序的启动——终止了公平的赎回权。 （选项C） 堵塞赎回权原则是指贷款人在抵押贷款执行后试图剥夺债务人的赎回权，例如在抵押贷款中加入放弃条款。由于此处没有发生弃权，因此该原则不适用。 （选项 D）许多州承认法定的赎回权，允许债务人在取消抵押品赎回权出售后收回财产，而不是像这位女士在这里寻求的那样，在出售之前收回财产。\n\n教育目标：如果债务人拖欠抵押贷款，加速条款允许贷方要求全额支付剩余抵押贷款债务以及任何应计利息。如果债务人无法付款，则贷款人可以继续进行止赎程序。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为该妇女没有足够的钱来迫使银行接受她的付款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":54,"sourceQuestionPage":159,"sourceQuestionPages":[159],"sourceExplanationPages":[160,161],"sourceAnswerPages":[160,161],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-055","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A seller owned a tract of land in fee simple. The seller executed an instrument in the proper form of a deed, purporting to convey the land to a buyer in fee simple. The instrument recited that the conveyance was in consideration of \"$5 cash in hand paid and for other good and valuable consideration.\" The seller handed the instrument to the buyer and the buyer promptly and properly recorded it. Two months later, the seller brought an appropriate action against the buyer to cancel the instrument and to quiet title. In support, the seller proved that no money in fact had been paid by the buyer, notwithstanding the recitation, and that no other consideration of any kind had been supplied by the buyer. In such action, should the seller prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because any remedy the seller might have had was lost when the instrument was recorded.","B":"No, because the validity of conveyance of land does not depend upon consideration being paid, whether recited or not.","C":"Yes, because recordation does not make a void instrument effective.","D":"Yes, because the recitation of consideration paid may be contradicted by parol evidence."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Transfer by deed\n\nEssential deed components Writing signed by grantor Identity of grantor & grantee Description of land Words of transfer Delivery Grantor's present intent to transfer title Presumed upon: physical delivery of deed recording unconditional delivery to agent grantee's possession of property/deed Acceptance Presumed when transfer benefits grantee\n\nA plaintiff can bring a quiet title action to establish his/her title to land above all others (eg, by disputing the validity of a deed). A deed is an instrument used to convey an interest in real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee). The conveyance is effective once the deed is: delivered by the grantor – presumed when the deed has been recorded or is in the grantee's possession and accepted by the grantee – presumed when the conveyance is beneficial to the grantee. The validity of the conveyance does not depend upon consideration (eg, payment for the property)—even if the deed states that consideration has been paid. However, the grantee may be liable for nonpayment. Here, the seller properly executed a deed purporting to convey a tract of land to the buyer. The seller handed the deed to the buyer (delivery), who accepted and recorded it. And though the buyer never paid the \"$5 cash in hand\" or other consideration recited in the deed, the validity of the conveyance did not depend upon consideration being paid. Therefore, the transfer to the buyer was effective, and the seller should not prevail in his quiet title action. (Choice A) Recording a deed (ie, depositing it into the official land records) does not cure any defects in the conveyance. Therefore, no remedy the seller might have had was lost when the deed was recorded. (Choice C) Recording a void deed—a legally invalid deed that cannot prove title (eg, a forged deed)—does not make the deed effective. But here, the deed was not void since it was properly executed, delivered, and accepted.\n\n(Choice D) Parol evidence—extrinsic evidence offered to contradict or modify a document—is admissible to show that the consideration recited in a deed has not been paid. But while that evidence is relevant to the grantee's liability for nonpayment, it does not affect the validity of the conveyance since consideration is not required. Therefore, the seller will not prevail by contradicting the consideration recited in the deed.\n\nEducational objective: A deed effectively transfers an interest in real property when the deed is (1) delivered by the grantor and (2) accepted by the grantee. Consideration is not required, even when the deed states that consideration has been paid.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Prop.: Wills & Other Donative Transfers § 6.3 (Am. Law Inst. 2003) (gifts of land).","educationalObjective":"A deed effectively transfers an interest in real property when the deed is (1) delivered by the grantor and (2) accepted by the grantee. Consideration is not required, even when the deed states that consideration has been paid.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Prop.: Wills & Other Donative Transfers § 6.3 (Am. Law Inst. 2003) (gifts of land).","rule":"A deed effectively transfers an interest in real property when the deed is (1) delivered by the grantor and (2) accepted by the grantee. Consideration is not required, even when the deed states that consideration has been paid.","irac":{"issue":"In such action, should the seller prevail?","rule":"A deed effectively transfers an interest in real property when the deed is (1) delivered by the grantor and (2) accepted by the grantee. Consideration is not required, even when the deed states that consideration has been paid.","analysis":"Transfer by deed\n\nEssential deed components Writing signed by grantor Identity of grantor & grantee Description of land Words of transfer Delivery Grantor's present intent to transfer title Presumed upon: physical delivery of deed recording unconditional delivery to agent grantee's possession of property/deed Acceptance Presumed when transfer benefits grantee\n\nA plaintiff can bring a quiet title action to establish his/her title to land above all others (eg, by disputing the validity of a deed). A deed is an instrument used to convey an interest in real property from the owner (grantor) to another (grantee). The conveyance is effective once the deed is: delivered by the grantor – presumed when the deed has been recorded or is in the grantee's possession and accepted by the grantee – presumed when the conveyance is beneficial to the grantee. The validity of the conveyance does not depend upon consideration (eg, payment for the property)—even if the deed states that consideration has been paid. However, the grantee may be liable for nonpayment. Here, the seller properly executed a deed purporting to convey a tract of land to the buyer. The seller handed the deed to the buyer (delivery), who accepted and recorded it. And though the buyer never paid the \"$5 cash in hand\" or other consideration recited in the deed, the validity of the conveyance did not depend upon consideration being paid. Therefore, the transfer to the buyer was effective, and the seller should not prevail in his quiet title action. (Choice A) Recording a deed (ie, depositing it into the official land records) does not cure any defects in the conveyance. Therefore, no remedy the seller might have had was lost when the deed was recorded. (Choice C) Recording a void deed—a legally invalid deed that cannot prove title (eg, a forged deed)—does not make the deed effective. But here, the deed was not void since it was properly executed, delivered, and accepted.\n\n(Choice D) Parol evidence—extrinsic evidence offered to contradict or modify a document—is admissible to show that the consideration recited in a deed has not been paid. But while that evidence is relevant to the grantee's liability for nonpayment, it does not affect the validity of the conveyance since consideration is not required. Therefore, the seller will not prevail by contradicting the consideration recited in the deed.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the validity of conveyance of land does not depend upon consideration being paid, whether recited or not."},"questionZh":"卖方以简单的费用拥有一块土地。卖方以适当的契约形式签署了一份文书，旨在以简单的费用将土地转让给买方。该文件指出，该转让是考虑到“已支付的 5 美元现金以及其他良好且有价值的对价”。卖方将票据交给买方，买方及时并妥善记录。两个月后，卖方对买方提起适当诉讼，取消该票据并平息所有权。作为支持，卖方证明，尽管如此，买方实际上并未支付任何款项，并且买方也没有提供任何其他对价。在这种情况下，卖家应该占上风吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为当乐器被记录时，卖方可能拥有的任何补救措施都消失了。","B":"不，因为土地转让的有效性并不取决于是否支付对价，无论是否记载。","C":"是的，因为备案并不会使无效文书生效。","D":"是的，因为所支付的对价的叙述可能与口头证据相矛盾。"},"explanationZh":"通过契约转让\n\n契约的基本组成部分 授予人签署的书面文件 授予人和受让人的身份 土地描述 转让文字 交付 授予人目前转让所有权的意图 推定依据： 实际交付契约，记录无条件交付给代理人 受让人对财产/契约的占有 接受 当转让使受让人受益时推定\n\n原告可以提起静默的所有权诉讼，以确立他/她的土地所有权高于所有其他人（例如，通过质疑契约的有效性）。契约是用于将不动产权益从所有者（授予人）转让给另一人（受让人）的工具。一旦契据满足以下条件，转让即生效： 由授予人交付 – 当契据已被记录或为受让人占有并被受让人接受时推定 – 当转让对受让人有利时推定。转让的有效性并不取决于对价（例如，财产的付款）——即使契约表明对价已经支付。然而，受让人可能要承担不付款的责任。在此，卖方正确地签署了一份契约，旨在将一块土地转让给买方。卖方将契约交给买方（交货），买方接受并记录。尽管买方从未支付过“手中的 5 美元现金”或契约中所述的其他对价，但转让的有效性并不取决于对价的支付。因此，向买方的转让是有效的，卖方不应在其安静的所有权诉讼中获胜。 （选择 A）记录契约（即，将其存入官方土地记录）并不能解决运输工具中的任何缺陷。因此，在契约登记时，卖方可能拥有的补救措施并没有丧失。 （选择C）记录无效契约——无法证明所有权的法律上无效的契约（例如伪造的契约）——并不会使该契约生效。但在这里，契约并不无效，因为它是正确执行、交付和接受的。\n\n（选择 D） 口头证据（为反驳或修改文件而提供的外在证据）可以用来表明契约中所述的对价尚未支付。但是，虽然该证据与受让人的不付款责任相关，但它并不影响转让的有效性，因为不需要对价。因此，卖方不会因与契约中所述的对价相矛盾而获胜。\n\n教育目标：当契约 (1) 由授予人交付且 (2) 被受让人接受时，契约就有效地转让了不动产权益。即使契约表明已支付对价，也不需要对价。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转移 § 6.3（Am. Law Inst. 2003）（土地赠与）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当契约 (1) 由授予人交付且 (2) 被受让人接受时，契约就有效地转让了不动产权益。即使契约表明已支付对价，也不需要对价。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这种情况下，卖家应该占上风吗？","rule":"当契约 (1) 由授予人交付且 (2) 被受让人接受时，契约就有效地转让了不动产权益。即使契约表明已支付对价，也不需要对价。","analysis":"通过契约转让\n\n契约的基本组成部分 授予人签署的书面文件 授予人和受让人的身份 土地描述 转让文字 交付 授予人目前转让所有权的意图 推定依据： 实际交付契约，记录无条件交付给代理人 受让人对财产/契约的占有 接受 当转让使受让人受益时推定\n\n原告可以提起静默的所有权诉讼，以确立他/她的土地所有权高于所有其他人（例如，通过质疑契约的有效性）。契约是用于将不动产权益从所有者（授予人）转让给另一人（受让人）的工具。一旦契据满足以下条件，转让即生效： 由授予人交付 – 当契据已被记录或为受让人占有并被受让人接受时推定 – 当转让对受让人有利时推定。转让的有效性并不取决于对价（例如，财产的付款）——即使契约表明对价已经支付。然而，受让人可能要承担不付款的责任。在此，卖方正确地签署了一份契约，旨在将一块土地转让给买方。卖方将契约交给买方（交货），买方接受并记录。尽管买方从未支付过“手中的 5 美元现金”或契约中所述的其他对价，但转让的有效性并不取决于对价的支付。因此，向买方的转让是有效的，卖方不应在其安静的所有权诉讼中获胜。 （选择 A）记录契约（即，将其存入官方土地记录）并不能解决运输工具中的任何缺陷。因此，在契约登记时，卖方可能拥有的补救措施并没有丧失。 （选择C）记录无效契约——无法证明所有权的法律上无效的契约（例如伪造的契约）——并不会使该契约生效。但在这里，契约并不无效，因为它是正确执行、交付和接受的。\n\n（选择 D） 口头证据（为反驳或修改文件而提供的外在证据）可以用来表明契约中所述的对价尚未支付。但是，虽然该证据与受让人的不付款责任相关，但它并不影响转让的有效性，因为不需要对价。因此，卖方不会因与契约中所述的对价相矛盾而获胜。\n\n教育目标：当契约 (1) 由授予人交付且 (2) 被受让人接受时，契约就有效地转让了不动产权益。即使契约表明已支付对价，也不需要对价。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转移 § 6.3（Am. Law Inst. 2003）（土地赠与）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为土地转让的有效性并不取决于是否支付对价，无论是否记载。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":55,"sourceQuestionPage":162,"sourceQuestionPages":[162],"sourceExplanationPages":[163,164],"sourceAnswerPages":[163,164],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-056","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A homebuyer financed the purchase of a new home by executing a promissory note and purchase-money mortgage to his credit union. The mortgage was promptly recorded. The next year, the home developed foundation problems. The homebuyer was forced to take out a second mortgage with a bank to finance the repairs. The mortgage was properly recorded, and file-stamped copies were sent to the homebuyer and the credit union. After making consistent payments for five years, the homebuyer defaulted on both mortgages. The homebuyer agreed to execute a deed in lieu of foreclosure to the credit union in satisfaction of the debt. The deed was signed, delivered, and properly recorded. The credit union then initiated foreclosure proceedings on its first mortgage in order to eliminate the second mortgage held by the bank. Is the credit union likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the credit union had actual knowledge of the second mortgage when it accepted the deed in lieu of foreclosure.","B":"No, because the deed in lieu of foreclosure automatically eliminated the second mortgage under the doctrine of merger.","C":"Yes, because the credit union impliedly retained the right to foreclose on the mortgage to eliminate junior liens.","D":"Yes, because the doctrine of merger does not apply to mortgages and the lender retains the right to foreclose."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The homebuyer (mortgagor) executed a deed in lieu of foreclosure, under which he conveyed all interest in the mortgaged property to the credit union (mortgagee) to avoid foreclosure. This gave the credit union the right to take immediate possession of the property without the formalities of a foreclosure sale. However, by accepting the deed, the credit union may have lost the right to foreclose on its mortgage to eliminate the bank's junior mortgage, depending on the jurisdiction. A minority of jurisdictions apply the doctrine of merger when the same person (here, the credit union) acquires the mortgagee's interest in real property and the mortgagor's interest in that same property (Choice D). When this occurs, the mortgage merges into the fee estate and is thereby extinguished. As a result, the mortgagee has no lien on which to foreclose and any junior liens are promoted in status. However, most jurisdictions have adopted the Restatement's position that the merger doctrine does not apply to mortgages (default rule on the MBE). Instead, a mortgagee that receives a deed in lieu of foreclosure is treated as having impliedly retained the right to foreclose on its mortgage—unless it had actual knowledge of a junior lien. If so, then the mortgagee cannot foreclose on its mortgage to eliminate that junior lien (Choices C & D). Here, the credit union received a file-stamped copy of the bank's mortgage, so the credit union had actual knowledge of this junior lien when it received the homebuyer's deed in lieu of foreclosure. As a result, the credit union is not likely to prevail in foreclosure proceedings on its first mortgage to eliminate the bank's junior mortgage. (Choice B) Had the minority rule regarding merger applied, the deed in lieu of foreclosure would have automatically eliminated the credit union's first mortgage—not the bank's second mortgage.\n\nEducational objective: A mortgagee that receives a deed in lieu of foreclosure generally retains the right to foreclose on its mortgage. But if the mortgagee had actual knowledge of a junior lien, then it cannot foreclose on its mortgage to eliminate that lien.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 8.5 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (discussing deeds in lieu of foreclosure).","educationalObjective":"A mortgagee that receives a deed in lieu of foreclosure generally retains the right to foreclose on its mortgage. But if the mortgagee had actual knowledge of a junior lien, then it cannot foreclose on its mortgage to eliminate that lien.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 8.5 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (discussing deeds in lieu of foreclosure).","rule":"A mortgagee that receives a deed in lieu of foreclosure generally retains the right to foreclose on its mortgage. But if the mortgagee had actual knowledge of a junior lien, then it cannot foreclose on its mortgage to eliminate that lien.","irac":{"issue":"Is the credit union likely to prevail?","rule":"A mortgagee that receives a deed in lieu of foreclosure generally retains the right to foreclose on its mortgage. But if the mortgagee had actual knowledge of a junior lien, then it cannot foreclose on its mortgage to eliminate that lien.","analysis":"The homebuyer (mortgagor) executed a deed in lieu of foreclosure, under which he conveyed all interest in the mortgaged property to the credit union (mortgagee) to avoid foreclosure. This gave the credit union the right to take immediate possession of the property without the formalities of a foreclosure sale. However, by accepting the deed, the credit union may have lost the right to foreclose on its mortgage to eliminate the bank's junior mortgage, depending on the jurisdiction. A minority of jurisdictions apply the doctrine of merger when the same person (here, the credit union) acquires the mortgagee's interest in real property and the mortgagor's interest in that same property (Choice D). When this occurs, the mortgage merges into the fee estate and is thereby extinguished. As a result, the mortgagee has no lien on which to foreclose and any junior liens are promoted in status. However, most jurisdictions have adopted the Restatement's position that the merger doctrine does not apply to mortgages (default rule on the MBE). Instead, a mortgagee that receives a deed in lieu of foreclosure is treated as having impliedly retained the right to foreclose on its mortgage—unless it had actual knowledge of a junior lien. If so, then the mortgagee cannot foreclose on its mortgage to eliminate that junior lien (Choices C & D). Here, the credit union received a file-stamped copy of the bank's mortgage, so the credit union had actual knowledge of this junior lien when it received the homebuyer's deed in lieu of foreclosure. As a result, the credit union is not likely to prevail in foreclosure proceedings on its first mortgage to eliminate the bank's junior mortgage. (Choice B) Had the minority rule regarding merger applied, the deed in lieu of foreclosure would have automatically eliminated the credit union's first mortgage—not the bank's second mortgage.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the credit union had actual knowledge of the second mortgage when it accepted the deed in lieu of foreclosure."},"questionZh":"一位购房者通过向其信用合作社签发期票和购房款抵押贷款来为购买新房提供资金。抵押贷款立即被记录。第二年，家里的地基出现了问题。购房者被迫向银行申请第二笔抵押贷款来支付维修费用。抵押贷款被正确记录，加盖文件的副本被发送给购房者和信用合作社。在持续支付五年后，购房者拖欠了两项抵押贷款。购房者同意向信用合作社签署一份契约，以代替取消抵押品赎回权，以清偿债务。契约已签署、交付并正确记录。随后，信用合作社对其第一抵押贷款启动了取消抵押品赎回权的程序，以取消银行持有的第二抵押贷款。信用合作社有可能占上风吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为信用合作社在接受代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约时实际上了解第二抵押贷款。","B":"不会，因为代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约自动消除了合并原则下的第二抵押权。","C":"是的，因为信用合作社默示保留取消抵押品赎回权以消除初级留置权的权利。","D":"是的，因为合并原则不适用于抵押贷款，并且贷款人保留取消抵押品赎回权的权利。"},"explanationZh":"购房者（抵押人）签署了一份代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约，根据该契约，他将抵押财产的所有权益转让给信用合作社（抵押权人）以避免丧失抵押品赎回权。这使得信用合作社有权立即占有该财产，而无需办理取消抵押品赎回权的出售手续。然而，通过接受契约，信用合作社可能失去取消其抵押贷款赎回权以消除银行初级抵押贷款的权利，具体取决于司法管辖区。当同一个人（此处为信用合作社）收购抵押权人在不动产中的权益以及抵押人在同一财产中的权益时，少数司法管辖区适用合并原则（选择 D）。当这种情况发生时，抵押贷款并入费用财产，从而消失。因此，抵押权人没有可以取消抵押品赎回权的留置权，并且任何初级留置权的地位都会得到提升。然而，大多数司法管辖区都采纳了重述的立场，即合并原则不适用于抵押贷款（MBE 的默认规则）。相反，收到代替取消抵押品赎回权的契约的抵押权人被视为默示保留取消抵押品赎回权的权利，除非其实际了解初级留置权。如果是这样，那么抵押权人就不能取消其抵押贷款的赎回权以消除该初级留置权（选择 C ​​和 D）。在这里，信用合作社收到了一份加盖文件印章的银行抵押贷款副本，因此，当信用合作社收到购房者代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约时，它实际上了解了该初级留置权。因此，信用合作社不太可能在其第一抵押贷款的取消抵押品赎回权诉讼中获胜，以取消银行的初级抵押贷款。 （选择B）如果适用关于合并的少数规则，代替取消抵押品赎回权的契约将自动消除信用合作社的第一抵押贷款，而不是银行的第二抵押贷款。\n\n教育目标：收到代替取消抵押品赎回权的契约的抵押权人通常保留取消抵押品赎回权的权利。但如果抵押权人实际了解初级留置权，那么它就不能取消其抵押贷款的赎回权以消除该留置权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 8.5 cmt。 b（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（讨论代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"收到代替取消抵押品赎回权的契约的抵押权人通常保留取消抵押品赎回权的权利。但如果抵押权人实际了解初级留置权，那么它就不能取消其抵押贷款的赎回权以消除该留置权。","iracZh":{"issue":"信用合作社有可能占上风吗？","rule":"收到代替取消抵押品赎回权的契约的抵押权人通常保留取消抵押品赎回权的权利。但如果抵押权人实际了解初级留置权，那么它就不能取消其抵押贷款的赎回权以消除该留置权。","analysis":"购房者（抵押人）签署了一份代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约，根据该契约，他将抵押财产的所有权益转让给信用合作社（抵押权人）以避免丧失抵押品赎回权。这使得信用合作社有权立即占有该财产，而无需办理取消抵押品赎回权的出售手续。然而，通过接受契约，信用合作社可能失去取消其抵押贷款赎回权以消除银行初级抵押贷款的权利，具体取决于司法管辖区。当同一个人（此处为信用合作社）收购抵押权人在不动产中的权益以及抵押人在同一财产中的权益时，少数司法管辖区适用合并原则（选择 D）。当这种情况发生时，抵押贷款并入费用财产，从而消失。因此，抵押权人没有可以取消抵押品赎回权的留置权，并且任何初级留置权的地位都会得到提升。然而，大多数司法管辖区都采纳了重述的立场，即合并原则不适用于抵押贷款（MBE 的默认规则）。相反，收到代替取消抵押品赎回权的契约的抵押权人被视为默示保留取消抵押品赎回权的权利，除非其实际了解初级留置权。如果是这样，那么抵押权人就不能取消其抵押贷款的赎回权以消除该初级留置权（选择 C ​​和 D）。在这里，信用合作社收到了一份加盖文件印章的银行抵押贷款副本，因此，当信用合作社收到购房者代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约时，它实际上了解了该初级留置权。因此，信用合作社不太可能在其第一抵押贷款的取消抵押品赎回权诉讼中获胜，以取消银行的初级抵押贷款。 （选择B）如果适用关于合并的少数规则，代替取消抵押品赎回权的契约将自动消除信用合作社的第一抵押贷款，而不是银行的第二抵押贷款。\n\n教育目标：收到代替取消抵押品赎回权的契约的抵押权人通常保留取消抵押品赎回权的权利。但如果抵押权人实际了解初级留置权，那么它就不能取消其抵押贷款的赎回权以消除该留置权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 8.5 cmt。 b（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（讨论代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为信用合作社在接受代替丧失抵押品赎回权的契约时实际上了解第二抵押贷款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"merger doctrine","ipa":"/mˈɝdʒɝ dˈɑktɹən/","syllables":"merger doc·trine","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合并原则：交割后土地买卖合同条款通常并入契据，救济以契据为准","contextualMeaning":"合并原则：交割后土地买卖合同条款通常并入契据，救济以契据为准","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"However, most jurisdictions have adopted the Restatement's position that the merger doctrine does not apply to mortgages (default rule on the MBE).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-1a0fc80c2fc248d2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":56,"sourceQuestionPage":165,"sourceQuestionPages":[165],"sourceExplanationPages":[166,167],"sourceAnswerPages":[166,167],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-057","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A carpenter decided to start a business with his friend. In order to fund the business, the carpenter took out a loan from a local bank. The carpenter signed a 30-year promissory note to the bank and secured the note with a mortgage on his cabin, which served as his primary residence. The mortgage included a due-on-sale clause and was promptly recorded by the bank. After running a successful business together for 10 years, the carpenter wanted to reward his friend for all of his hard work. As a result, the carpenter conveyed the cabin by quitclaim deed as a gift to the friend. When the bank received notice that the cabin had been transferred to the friend, the bank demanded full payment of the remaining mortgage debt. When neither the carpenter nor the friend made the required payment, the bank initiated foreclosure proceedings. Will the bank likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a due-on-sale clause can only be enforced when the mortgaged property is sold to another.","B":"No, because a due-on-sale clause cannot be enforced against a mortgage on a primary residence.","C":"Yes, because the due-on-sale clause made the friend personally liable for the mortgage debt.","D":"Yes, because the transfer of the mortgaged property triggered the due-on-sale clause."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Common mortgage provisions\n\nAcceleration clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor defaults\n\nDue-on-sale clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor sells mortgaged property without permission\n\nDue-on- Allows creditor to accelerate mortgage obligation if debtor obtains encumbrance clause second mortgage or otherwise encumbers property\n\nDefeasance clause Requires creditor to give debtor legal title to property & release mortgage lien once all payments are made\n\nMortgage documents are used to convey a lender (mortgagee) an interest in real property to secure repayment of a debt. These documents may contain a due-on-sale clause, which allows the lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor (mortgagor) transfers the mortgaged property without the lender's consent (absent limited exceptions). If the debtor is unable to pay, then the lender can initiate foreclosure proceedings to recover any remaining debt. Here, the carpenter gave the bank a mortgage on his residence, the cabin, to secure a loan. The mortgage contained a due-on-sale clause, which was triggered when the carpenter transferred the cabin to the friend by quitclaim deed and allowed the bank to demand full payment of the remaining loan balance. The fact that the cabin was gifted (not sold) has no impact on the bank's ability to enforce this clause (Choice A). And since the carpenter did not make the required payment, the bank will prevail in its foreclosure proceedings. (Choice B) A due-on-sale clause can be enforced on a primary residence or any other real property used to secure repayment of a debt. (Choice C) The due-on-sale clause made the carpenter (mortgagor) personally liable on the debt if the mortgaged property was conveyed without the bank's consent. The friend would be personally liable only if he agreed to assume the mortgage debt (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A due-on-sale clause allows a lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor transfers the mortgaged property without the lender's permission. Although labeled a \"due on sale\" clause, the clause is triggered by most transfers—including gifts.\n\nReferences\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 415 (2019) (due-on-sale clauses).","educationalObjective":"A due-on-sale clause allows a lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor transfers the mortgaged property without the lender's permission. Although labeled a \"due on sale\" clause, the clause is triggered by most transfers—including gifts.","references":"55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 415 (2019) (due-on-sale clauses).","rule":"A due-on-sale clause allows a lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor transfers the mortgaged property without the lender's permission. Although labeled a \"due on sale\" clause, the clause is triggered by most transfers—including gifts.","irac":{"issue":"Will the bank likely prevail?","rule":"A due-on-sale clause allows a lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor transfers the mortgaged property without the lender's permission. Although labeled a \"due on sale\" clause, the clause is triggered by most transfers—including gifts.","analysis":"Common mortgage provisions\n\nAcceleration clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor defaults\n\nDue-on-sale clause Allows creditor to demand entire loan due & payable if debtor sells mortgaged property without permission\n\nDue-on- Allows creditor to accelerate mortgage obligation if debtor obtains encumbrance clause second mortgage or otherwise encumbers property\n\nDefeasance clause Requires creditor to give debtor legal title to property & release mortgage lien once all payments are made\n\nMortgage documents are used to convey a lender (mortgagee) an interest in real property to secure repayment of a debt. These documents may contain a due-on-sale clause, which allows the lender to demand full payment of the remaining mortgage debt if the debtor (mortgagor) transfers the mortgaged property without the lender's consent (absent limited exceptions). If the debtor is unable to pay, then the lender can initiate foreclosure proceedings to recover any remaining debt. Here, the carpenter gave the bank a mortgage on his residence, the cabin, to secure a loan. The mortgage contained a due-on-sale clause, which was triggered when the carpenter transferred the cabin to the friend by quitclaim deed and allowed the bank to demand full payment of the remaining loan balance. The fact that the cabin was gifted (not sold) has no impact on the bank's ability to enforce this clause (Choice A). And since the carpenter did not make the required payment, the bank will prevail in its foreclosure proceedings. (Choice B) A due-on-sale clause can be enforced on a primary residence or any other real property used to secure repayment of a debt. (Choice C) The due-on-sale clause made the carpenter (mortgagor) personally liable on the debt if the mortgaged property was conveyed without the bank's consent. The friend would be personally liable only if he agreed to assume the mortgage debt (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the transfer of the mortgaged property triggered the due-on-sale clause."},"questionZh":"一位木匠决定和他的朋友一起创业。为了资助生意，木匠从当地一家银行贷款。木匠向银行签署了一张30年期的本票，并用他的小屋（他的主要住所）作为抵押来担保这张票据。该抵押贷款包含到期出售条款，并立即由银行记录。一起经营一家成功的企业十年后，木匠想奖励他的朋友的辛勤工作。结果，木匠通过无偿所有权契据将小屋转让给了朋友。当银行收到小屋已转让给朋友的通知后，银行要求全额支付剩余的抵押贷款债务。当木匠和朋友都没有支付所需付款时，银行启动了止赎程序。银行可能会占上风吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为到期出售条款只有在抵押财产出售给他人时才能执行。","B":"不可以，因为到期销售条款不能针对主要住宅的抵押贷款执行。","C":"是的，因为到期出售条款使朋友个人对抵押贷款债务承担责任。","D":"是的，因为抵押财产的转让触发了到期出售条款。"},"explanationZh":"常见抵押条款\n\n加速条款 如果债务人违约，债权人可以要求全额到期应付贷款\n\n到期出售条款 如果债务人未经许可出售抵押财产，债权人可以要求偿还全部到期应付贷款\n\n到期 - 如果债务人获得产权负担条款第二抵押贷款或以其他方式抵押财产，则允许债权人加速抵押贷款义务\n\n废止条款 要求债权人给予债务人财产的合法所有权并在支付所有款项后释放抵押留置权\n\n抵押文件用于向贷款人（抵押权人）传达不动产权益，以确保债务偿还。这些文件可能包含到期出售条款，如果债务人（抵押人）未经贷款人同意（无有限例外）转让抵押财产，则允许贷款人要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。如果债务人无法付款，则贷款人可以启动止赎程序以追回任何剩余债务。在这里，木匠向银行抵押了他的住所小屋，以获得贷款。该抵押贷款包含到期出售条款，当木匠通过无偿债权契据将小屋转让给朋友时，该条款被触发，并允许银行要求全额支付剩余贷款余额。小屋是赠予（而非出售）这一事实并不影响银行执行本条款的能力（选择 A）。由于木匠没有支付所需的款项，银行将在其止赎程序中胜诉。 （选择 B）可以对主要住宅或用于确保偿还债务的任何其他不动产执行到期出售条款。 （选项C） 到期出售条款规定，如果未经银行同意而转让抵押财产，则木匠（抵押人）应对债务承担个人责任。只有当朋友同意承担抵押贷款债务（此处未显示）时，他才会承担个人责任。\n\n教育目标：如果债务人未经贷款人许可转让抵押财产，到期出售条款允许贷款人要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。尽管标有“到期出售”条款，但该条款是由大多数转让（包括礼物）触发的。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d 抵押贷款 § 415 (2019)（到期销售条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果债务人未经贷款人许可转让抵押财产，到期出售条款允许贷款人要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。尽管标有“到期出售”条款，但该条款是由大多数转让（包括礼物）触发的。","iracZh":{"issue":"银行可能会占上风吗？","rule":"如果债务人未经贷款人许可转让抵押财产，到期出售条款允许贷款人要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。尽管标有“到期出售”条款，但该条款是由大多数转让（包括礼物）触发的。","analysis":"常见抵押条款\n\n加速条款 如果债务人违约，债权人可以要求全额到期应付贷款\n\n到期出售条款 如果债务人未经许可出售抵押财产，债权人可以要求偿还全部到期应付贷款\n\n到期 - 如果债务人获得产权负担条款第二抵押贷款或以其他方式抵押财产，则允许债权人加速抵押贷款义务\n\n废止条款 要求债权人给予债务人财产的合法所有权并在支付所有款项后释放抵押留置权\n\n抵押文件用于向贷款人（抵押权人）传达不动产权益，以确保债务偿还。这些文件可能包含到期出售条款，如果债务人（抵押人）未经贷款人同意（无有限例外）转让抵押财产，则允许贷款人要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。如果债务人无法付款，则贷款人可以启动止赎程序以追回任何剩余债务。在这里，木匠向银行抵押了他的住所小屋，以获得贷款。该抵押贷款包含到期出售条款，当木匠通过无偿债权契据将小屋转让给朋友时，该条款被触发，并允许银行要求全额支付剩余贷款余额。小屋是赠予（而非出售）这一事实并不影响银行执行本条款的能力（选择 A）。由于木匠没有支付所需的款项，银行将在其止赎程序中胜诉。 （选择 B）可以对主要住宅或用于确保偿还债务的任何其他不动产执行到期出售条款。 （选项C） 到期出售条款规定，如果未经银行同意而转让抵押财产，则木匠（抵押人）应对债务承担个人责任。只有当朋友同意承担抵押贷款债务（此处未显示）时，他才会承担个人责任。\n\n教育目标：如果债务人未经贷款人许可转让抵押财产，到期出售条款允许贷款人要求全额偿还剩余抵押债务。尽管标有“到期出售”条款，但该条款是由大多数转让（包括礼物）触发的。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d 抵押贷款 § 415 (2019)（到期销售条款）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为抵押财产的转让触发了到期出售条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":57,"sourceQuestionPage":168,"sourceQuestionPages":[168],"sourceExplanationPages":[169,170],"sourceAnswerPages":[169,170],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-058","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man who owned a farm in fee simple conveyed it to a friend by warranty deed. An adjoining neighbor asserted title to the farm and brought an appropriate action against the friend to quiet title to the farm. The friend demanded that the man defend her title under the deed's covenant of warranty, but the man refused. The friend then successfully defended at her own expense. The friend brought an appropriate action against the man to recover her expenses incurred in defending against the adjoining neighbor's action to quiet title to the farm. In this action, will the friend prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the adjoining neighbor could have elected to sue either the man or the friend.","B":"No, because the title the man conveyed was not defective.","C":"Yes, because in effect it was the man's title that was challenged.","D":"Yes, because the man's deed to her included the covenant of warranty."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Covenants of title\n\n(warranty & special warranty deeds)\n\nPresent covenants Seisin Grantor owns interest being conveyed\n\n(breached at time of Right to convey Grantor has legal right to convey interest conveyance) Against No encumbrances (eg, easements, encumbrances mortgages) against interest conveyed\n\nFuture covenants Warranty Grantor will defend & compensate grantee for lawful claims made against grantee's (breached upon title interference with possession) Quiet enjoyment Grantee's possession & enjoyment will not be disturbed by another's lawful claim of title\n\nFurther Grantor will take any actions reasonably assurances necessary to perfect grantee's title\n\nA warranty deed conveys a grantor's real property to a grantee with assurances that there are no defects in title—ie, no one else owns the land and it is free from encumbrances. The covenant of warranty is an assurance that the grantor will defend and compensate the grantee for lawful claims made against the grantee's title—eg, when a third party prevails against the grantee in a quiet title action. Here, the man conveyed his farm to a friend by warranty deed. The friend demanded that the man defend her under the covenant of warranty after an adjoining neighbor asserted title to the farm through a quiet title action (Choice D). But the man was not required to do so because the neighbor did not have a lawful claim (as evidenced by the friend's successful defense). Therefore, the friend will not recover the expenses she incurred in defending that claim. (Choice A) In a quiet title action, the plaintiff can sue all persons claiming an interest in the property to establish superior title. Here, the neighbor could not have elected to sue the man since he no longer claimed an interest in the farm (he had conveyed his interest to the friend). But had the neighbor prevailed against the friend (lawful claim), the friend could recover her expenses from the man under the covenant of warranty. (Choice C) The neighbor's action against the friend was, in effect, challenging the man's title since the neighbor would only prevail if the man had conveyed defective title to the\n\nfriend. But the man was not required to defend that suit under the covenant of warranty (or compensate the friend) since he had not conveyed defective title.\n\nEducational objective: Under the covenant of warranty, the grantor of a warranty deed must defend and compensate the grantee for any lawful claims made against the grantee's title—eg, when the grantee is defeated in a third party's quiet title action.","educationalObjective":"Under the covenant of warranty, the grantor of a warranty deed must defend and compensate the grantee for any lawful claims made against the grantee's title—eg, when the grantee is defeated in a third party's quiet title action.","references":"","rule":"Under the covenant of warranty, the grantor of a warranty deed must defend and compensate the grantee for any lawful claims made against the grantee's title—eg, when the grantee is defeated in a third party's quiet title action.","irac":{"issue":"In this action, will the friend prevail?","rule":"Under the covenant of warranty, the grantor of a warranty deed must defend and compensate the grantee for any lawful claims made against the grantee's title—eg, when the grantee is defeated in a third party's quiet title action.","analysis":"Covenants of title\n\n(warranty & special warranty deeds)\n\nPresent covenants Seisin Grantor owns interest being conveyed\n\n(breached at time of Right to convey Grantor has legal right to convey interest conveyance) Against No encumbrances (eg, easements, encumbrances mortgages) against interest conveyed\n\nFuture covenants Warranty Grantor will defend & compensate grantee for lawful claims made against grantee's (breached upon title interference with possession) Quiet enjoyment Grantee's possession & enjoyment will not be disturbed by another's lawful claim of title\n\nFurther Grantor will take any actions reasonably assurances necessary to perfect grantee's title\n\nA warranty deed conveys a grantor's real property to a grantee with assurances that there are no defects in title—ie, no one else owns the land and it is free from encumbrances. The covenant of warranty is an assurance that the grantor will defend and compensate the grantee for lawful claims made against the grantee's title—eg, when a third party prevails against the grantee in a quiet title action. Here, the man conveyed his farm to a friend by warranty deed. The friend demanded that the man defend her under the covenant of warranty after an adjoining neighbor asserted title to the farm through a quiet title action (Choice D). But the man was not required to do so because the neighbor did not have a lawful claim (as evidenced by the friend's successful defense). Therefore, the friend will not recover the expenses she incurred in defending that claim. (Choice A) In a quiet title action, the plaintiff can sue all persons claiming an interest in the property to establish superior title. Here, the neighbor could not have elected to sue the man since he no longer claimed an interest in the farm (he had conveyed his interest to the friend). But had the neighbor prevailed against the friend (lawful claim), the friend could recover her expenses from the man under the covenant of warranty. (Choice C) The neighbor's action against the friend was, in effect, challenging the man's title since the neighbor would only prevail if the man had conveyed defective title to the\n\nfriend. But the man was not required to defend that suit under the covenant of warranty (or compensate the friend) since he had not conveyed defective title.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the title the man conveyed was not defective."},"questionZh":"一名拥有一座农场的男子通过保修契约将其转让给朋友。毗邻的邻居主张对该农场的所有权，并对这位朋友提起适当的诉讼，以平息对该农场的所有权。朋友要求该男子根据契约的保证契约捍卫她的所有权，但该男子拒绝了。朋友随后自费辩护成功。这位朋友对该男子提起了适当的诉讼，以追回她因抗辩邻近邻居要求平息农场所有权而产生的费用。在这次行动中，朋友会占上风吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为隔壁邻居可以选择起诉该男子或该朋友。","B":"不，因为这个人所传达的头衔并没有缺陷。","C":"是的，因为实际上是这个人的头衔受到了挑战。","D":"是的，因为那个男人给她的契约包括保修契约。"},"explanationZh":"所有权契约\n\n（保修和特殊保修契约）\n\n目前的契约 Seisin Grantor 拥有正在转让的权益\n\n（在转让权时违反 授予人有转让利息的合法权利） 所转让的利息无产权负担（例如地役权、抵押贷款）\n\n未来契约 保证 授予人将为受让人针对受让人提出的合法索赔进行辩护并赔偿（因所有权干扰占有而违反） 安静的享受 受让人的占有和享受不会受到他人合法所有权主张的干扰\n\n此外，授予人将采取任何必要的行动合理保证完善受让人的所有权\n\n担保契约将授予人的不动产转让给受让人，并保证所有权不存在任何缺陷，即没有其他人拥有该土地并且不存在产权负担。保证契约保证授予人将为受让人辩护并赔偿针对受让人所有权的合法索赔，例如，当第三方在安静的所有权诉讼中胜诉受让人时。在这里，该男子通过保修契约将他的农场转让给朋友。在一位邻居通过一项安静的产权诉讼主张农场所有权后，这位朋友要求该男子根据保修契约为她辩护（选择 D）。但该男子没有被要求这样做，因为邻居没有合法的主张（正如朋友成功辩护所证明的那样）。因此，该朋友将不会收回她为辩护该索赔而产生的费用。 （选择 A） 在安静的所有权诉讼中，原告可以起诉所有声称对该财产拥有权益的人，以建立优先所有权。在这里，邻居不能选择起诉该男子，因为他不再声称拥有农场的权益（他已将其权益传达给朋友）。但是，如果邻居胜诉了朋友（合法索赔），朋友可以根据保修契约向该男子追回她的费用。 （选项 C） 邻居对朋友的行为实际上是在挑战该男子的所有权，因为只有当该男子将有缺陷的所有权转让给邻居时，邻居才会获胜。\n\n朋友。但该男子无需根据保证契约对该诉讼进行辩护（或赔偿朋友），因为他没有转让有缺陷的所有权。\n\n教育目标：根据保证契约，保证契约的授予人必须为受让人辩护并赔偿针对受让人所有权的任何合法索赔——例如，当受让人在第三方的静默所有权诉讼中被击败时。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据保证契约，保证契约的授予人必须为受让人辩护并赔偿针对受让人所有权的任何合法索赔——例如，当受让人在第三方的静默所有权诉讼中被击败时。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这次行动中，朋友会占上风吗？","rule":"根据保证契约，保证契约的授予人必须为受让人辩护并赔偿针对受让人所有权的任何合法索赔——例如，当受让人在第三方的静默所有权诉讼中被击败时。","analysis":"所有权契约\n\n（保修和特殊保修契约）\n\n目前的契约 Seisin Grantor 拥有正在转让的权益\n\n（在转让权时违反 授予人有转让利息的合法权利） 所转让的利息无产权负担（例如地役权、抵押贷款）\n\n未来契约 保证 授予人将为受让人针对受让人提出的合法索赔进行辩护并赔偿（因所有权干扰占有而违反） 安静的享受 受让人的占有和享受不会受到他人合法所有权主张的干扰\n\n此外，授予人将采取任何必要的行动合理保证完善受让人的所有权\n\n担保契约将授予人的不动产转让给受让人，并保证所有权不存在任何缺陷，即没有其他人拥有该土地并且不存在产权负担。保证契约保证授予人将为受让人辩护并赔偿针对受让人所有权的合法索赔，例如，当第三方在安静的所有权诉讼中胜诉受让人时。在这里，该男子通过保修契约将他的农场转让给朋友。在一位邻居通过一项安静的产权诉讼主张农场所有权后，这位朋友要求该男子根据保修契约为她辩护（选择 D）。但该男子没有被要求这样做，因为邻居没有合法的主张（正如朋友成功辩护所证明的那样）。因此，该朋友将不会收回她为辩护该索赔而产生的费用。 （选择 A） 在安静的所有权诉讼中，原告可以起诉所有声称对该财产拥有权益的人，以建立优先所有权。在这里，邻居不能选择起诉该男子，因为他不再声称拥有农场的权益（他已将其权益传达给朋友）。但是，如果邻居胜诉了朋友（合法索赔），朋友可以根据保修契约向该男子追回她的费用。 （选项 C） 邻居对朋友的行为实际上是在挑战该男子的所有权，因为只有当该男子将有缺陷的所有权转让给邻居时，邻居才会获胜。\n\n朋友。但该男子无需根据保证契约对该诉讼进行辩护（或赔偿朋友），因为他没有转让有缺陷的所有权。\n\n教育目标：根据保证契约，保证契约的授予人必须为受让人辩护并赔偿针对受让人所有权的任何合法索赔——例如，当受让人在第三方的静默所有权诉讼中被击败时。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为这个人所传达的头衔并没有缺陷。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"special warranty deed","ipa":"/spˈɛʃəl wˈɔɹənti dˈid/","syllables":"spe·cial war·ranty deed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"特别担保契据：出让人仅担保其持有期间未造成产权瑕疵","contextualMeaning":"特别担保契据：出让人仅担保其持有期间未造成产权瑕疵","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Covenants of title (warranty & special warranty deeds) Present covenants Seisin Grantor owns interest being conveyed (breached at time of Right to convey Grantor has legal right to convey interest conveyance) Against No encumbrances (eg, easements, encumbrances mortgages) against interest conveyed Future covenants Warranty Grantor will defend & compensate grantee for lawful claims made against grantee's (breached upo","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"covenant of warranty","ipa":"/kˈəvənənt ˈəv wˈɔɹənti/","syllables":"covenant of war·ranty","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"产权保证担保：出让人承诺在有效产权主张出现时为受让人抗辩","contextualMeaning":"产权保证担保：出让人承诺在有效产权主张出现时为受让人抗辩","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The friend demanded that the man defend her title under the deed's covenant of warranty, but the man refused.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"seisin","ipa":"/'si:zin/","syllables":"seisin","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"[律]依法占有(财产)","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“seisin”","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Covenants of title (warranty & special warranty deeds) Present covenants Seisin Grantor owns interest being conveyed (breached at time of Right to convey Grantor has legal right to convey interest conveyance) Against No encumbrances (eg, easements, encumbrances mortgages) against interest conveyed Future covenants Warranty Grantor will defend & compensate grantee for lawful claims made against grantee's (breached upo","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":58,"sourceQuestionPage":171,"sourceQuestionPages":[171],"sourceExplanationPages":[172,173],"sourceAnswerPages":[172,173],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-059","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman owned a lot, which was improved with a dwelling. Her neighbor owned an adjoining unimproved lot suitable for constructing a dwelling. The neighbor executed and delivered a deed granting to the woman an easement over the westerly 15 feet of her lot for convenient ingress and egress to a public street, since the woman's lot only abutted a dirt road. The woman did not then record the neighbor's deed. After the woman constructed and started using a driveway within the described 15-foot strip in a clearly visible manner, the neighbor borrowed $100,000 cash from the bank and gave the bank a mortgage on her lot. The mortgage was promptly and properly recorded. The woman then recorded the neighbor's deed granting the easement. The neighbor subsequently defaulted on her loan payments to the bank. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.\" In an appropriate foreclosure action as to the neighbor's lot, brought against the woman and the neighbor, the bank seeks, among other things, to have the woman's easement declared subordinate to the bank's mortgage, so that the easement will be terminated by completion of the foreclosure. If the woman's easement is NOT terminated, what will be the reason?","choices":{"A":"The easement provides access from the woman's lot to a public street.","B":"The recording of the deed granting the easement prior to the foreclosure action protects the woman's rights.","C":"The woman's easement is appurtenant to her lot and thus cannot be separated from her lot.","D":"Visible use of the easement by the woman put the bank on notice of the easement."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Recording acts are used to determine priority among competing property interests. In a notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser (BFP) has priority over an earlier property interest if the BFP took without notice of that interest. There are three types of notice: Actual – a buyer has direct knowledge of a prior interest in land Record – a document showing a prior interest is properly recorded in the land records and appears in the buyer's chain of title Inquiry – a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property, reference to unrecorded transaction in recorded instrument) Here, the foreclosure would only terminate the woman's prior easement over the neighbor's lot if the bank had no notice of the easement when it obtained a mortgage from the neighbor. The bank had no direct knowledge of the easement (no actual notice), and it was recorded after the mortgage was conveyed (no record notice). But if visible use of the easement put the bank on inquiry notice, the easement would not be terminated by the foreclosure. (Choice A) An easement by necessity allows an owner of a landlocked parcel to cross another's land to access a public road and only terminates when the necessity ends. Although the woman's easement allowed her to access a public street, her lot was not landlocked since it abutted another street (no necessity). (Choice B) Recording the deed granting the easement prior to the mortgage (not the foreclosure action) would have protected the woman's rights. But since she recorded the deed after the mortgage was conveyed, the recording did not protect her (ie, no record notice). (Choice C) An easement appurtenant—an easement that benefits the easement holder's land by granting limited use or control of another's land—cannot be separated (ie, conveyed separately) from the easement holder's land. But it is only binding on subsequent purchasers (eg, the bank) who take with notice of the easement.\n\nEducational objective: In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser who acquires a property interest without notice (actual, record, or inquiry) of an earlier property interest will prevail. A buyer has inquiry notice when he/she knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property).","educationalObjective":"In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser who acquires a property interest without notice (actual, record, or inquiry) of an earlier property interest will prevail. A buyer has inquiry notice when he/she knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property).","references":"","rule":"In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser who acquires a property interest without notice (actual, record, or inquiry) of an earlier property interest will prevail. A buyer has inquiry notice when he/she knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property).","irac":{"issue":"If the woman's easement is NOT terminated, what will be the reason?","rule":"In a notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser who acquires a property interest without notice (actual, record, or inquiry) of an earlier property interest will prevail. A buyer has inquiry notice when he/she knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property).","analysis":"Recording acts are used to determine priority among competing property interests. In a notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser (BFP) has priority over an earlier property interest if the BFP took without notice of that interest. There are three types of notice: Actual – a buyer has direct knowledge of a prior interest in land Record – a document showing a prior interest is properly recorded in the land records and appears in the buyer's chain of title Inquiry – a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property, reference to unrecorded transaction in recorded instrument) Here, the foreclosure would only terminate the woman's prior easement over the neighbor's lot if the bank had no notice of the easement when it obtained a mortgage from the neighbor. The bank had no direct knowledge of the easement (no actual notice), and it was recorded after the mortgage was conveyed (no record notice). But if visible use of the easement put the bank on inquiry notice, the easement would not be terminated by the foreclosure. (Choice A) An easement by necessity allows an owner of a landlocked parcel to cross another's land to access a public road and only terminates when the necessity ends. Although the woman's easement allowed her to access a public street, her lot was not landlocked since it abutted another street (no necessity). (Choice B) Recording the deed granting the easement prior to the mortgage (not the foreclosure action) would have protected the woman's rights. But since she recorded the deed after the mortgage was conveyed, the recording did not protect her (ie, no record notice). (Choice C) An easement appurtenant—an easement that benefits the easement holder's land by granting limited use or control of another's land—cannot be separated (ie, conveyed separately) from the easement holder's land. But it is only binding on subsequent purchasers (eg, the bank) who take with notice of the easement.","conclusion":"Answer D: Visible use of the easement by the woman put the bank on notice of the easement."},"questionZh":"一个女人拥有很多土地，并通过住宅加以改善。她的邻居拥有毗邻的一块未开发的土地，适合建造住宅。邻居签署并交付了一份契约，授予该妇女其地块西侧 15 英尺的地役权，以便方便地进出公共街道，因为该妇女的地块仅毗邻一条土路。该女子随后没有记录邻居的行为。当这名女子在所描述的 15 英尺长的地带内以清晰可见的方式建造并开始使用车道后，邻居向银行借了 10 万美元现金，并以她的土地向银行提供了抵押贷款。抵押贷款被及时、正确地记录。然后，该妇女记录了邻居授予地役权的行为。邻居随后拖欠银行贷款。该司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“除非依法记录，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者。”在对妇女和邻居提起的针对邻居土地的适当取消抵押品赎回权诉讼中，银行除其他外，寻求宣布妇女的地役权从属于银行抵押贷款，以便在取消抵押品赎回权完成时终止地役权。如果妇女的地役权没有终止，原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"地役权提供了从该妇女的地块到公共街道的通道。","B":"在取消抵押品赎回权之前记录授予地役权的契约可以保护妇女的权利。","C":"妇女的地役权是从属于她的命运的，因此不能与她的命运分开。","D":"该妇女对地役权的明显使用使银行注意到了地役权。"},"explanationZh":"记录行为用于确定相互竞争的财产利益之间的优先顺序。在通知司法管辖区（如此处所示），如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 在未通知的情况下取得了先前的财产权益，则该善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 拥有对该权益的优先权。通知分为三种类型： 实际 - 买方直接了解土地的先前权益 记录 - 显示先前权益的文件已正确记录在土地记录中，并出现在买方的所有权链中 查询 - 买方知道或应该知道会促使合理人员进行调查的情况（例如，财产的明显使用、在记录文书中提及未记录的交易） 在这里，如果银行没有通知，取消抵押品赎回权只会终止妇女对邻居地块的先前地役权当它从邻居那里获得抵押贷款时，就获得了地役权。银行对地役权没有直接了解（没有实际通知），是在抵押权转让后记录的（没有记录通知）。但如果地役权的明显使用使银行收到询问通知，则地役权不会因丧失抵押品赎回权而终止。 （选项 A） 必要时的地役权允许内陆地块的所有者穿过另一块土地进入公共道路，并且仅在必要性结束时终止。尽管该妇女的地役权允许她进入公共街道，但她的地块并不内陆，因为它毗邻另一条街道（没有必要）。 （选择 B）在抵押贷款（而不是取消抵押品赎回权行动）之前记录授予地役权的契约会保护妇女的权利。但由于她在抵押权转让后记录了契约，因此记录并不能保护她（即没有记录通知）。 （选项C）地役权附属物——通过授予他人土地的有限使用或控制权而使地役权持有人的土地受益的地役权——不能与地役权持有人的土地分离（即单独转让）。但它仅对通知地役权的后续购买者（例如银行）具有约束力。\n\n教育目标：在通知司法管辖区，善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）在未通知（实际、记录或查询）先前财产权益的情况下购买财产权益的行为优先。当买方知道或应该知道会促使理性人进行调查的情况（例如，对该财产的明显使用）时，他/她就会收到询问通知。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在通知司法管辖区，善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）在未通知（实际、记录或查询）先前财产权益的情况下购买财产权益的，将优先适用。当买方知道或应该知道会促使理性人进行调查的情况（例如，对该财产的明显使用）时，他/她就会收到询问通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果妇女的地役权没有终止，原因是什么？","rule":"在通知司法管辖区，善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）在未通知（实际、记录或查询）先前财产权益的情况下购买财产权益的，将优先适用。当买方知道或应该知道会促使理性人进行调查的情况（例如，对该财产的明显使用）时，他/她就会收到询问通知。","analysis":"记录行为用于确定相互竞争的财产利益之间的优先顺序。在通知司法管辖区（如此处所示），如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 在未通知的情况下取得了先前的财产权益，则该善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 拥有对该权益的优先权。通知分为三种类型： 实际 - 买方直接了解土地的先前权益 记录 - 显示先前权益的文件已正确记录在土地记录中，并出现在买方的所有权链中 查询 - 买方知道或应该知道会促使合理人员进行调查的情况（例如，财产的明显使用、在记录文书中提及未记录的交易） 在这里，如果银行没有通知，取消抵押品赎回权只会终止妇女对邻居地块的先前地役权当它从邻居那里获得抵押贷款时，就获得了地役权。银行对地役权没有直接了解（没有实际通知），是在抵押权转让后记录的（没有记录通知）。但如果地役权的明显使用使银行收到询问通知，则地役权不会因丧失抵押品赎回权而终止。 （选项 A） 必要时的地役权允许内陆地块的所有者穿过另一块土地进入公共道路，并且仅在必要性结束时终止。尽管该妇女的地役权允许她进入公共街道，但她的地块并不内陆，因为它毗邻另一条街道（没有必要）。 （选择 B）在抵押贷款（而不是取消抵押品赎回权行动）之前记录授予地役权的契约会保护妇女的权利。但由于她在抵押权转让后记录了契约，因此记录并不能保护她（即没有记录通知）。 （选项C）地役权附属物——通过授予他人土地的有限使用或控制权而使地役权持有人的土地受益的地役权——不能与地役权持有人的土地分离（即单独转让）。但它仅对通知地役权的后续购买者（例如银行）具有约束力。\n\n教育目标：在通知司法管辖区，善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）在未通知（实际、记录或查询）先前财产权益的情况下购买财产权益的行为优先。当买方知道或应该知道会促使理性人进行调查的情况（例如，对该财产的明显使用）时，他/她就会收到询问通知。","conclusion":"答案 D：该妇女对地役权的明显使用使银行注意到了地役权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"easement by necessity","ipa":"/ˈizmənt bˈaɪ nʌsˈɛsəti/","syllables":"ease·ment by ne·ces·sity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"必要地役权：原统一土地分割后某地块无合理出入通道时产生","contextualMeaning":"必要地役权：原统一土地分割后某地块无合理出入通道时产生","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) An easement by necessity allows an owner of a landlocked parcel to cross another's land to access a public road and only terminates when the necessity ends.","synonyms":["way of necessity"]},{"term":"easement appurtenant","ipa":"/ˈizmənt ə'pə:tinənt/","syllables":"ease·ment ap·pur·tenant","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"附属地役权：利益归属于特定优势地并随该土地转让","contextualMeaning":"附属地役权：利益归属于特定优势地并随该土地转让","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) An easement appurtenant—an easement that benefits the easement holder's land by granting limited use or control of another's land—cannot be separated (ie, conveyed separately) from the easement holder's land.","synonyms":["appurtenant easement"]},{"term":"landlocked","ipa":"/lˈændlˌɑkt/","syllables":"land·locked","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 被陆地围住的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“landlocked”","wordForm":"词；原形 landlock；词形 p:landlocked/0:landlocked/1:p","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) An easement by necessity allows an owner of a landlocked parcel to cross another's land to access a public road and only terminates when the necessity ends.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-cd5714736c236394"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":59,"sourceQuestionPage":174,"sourceQuestionPages":[174],"sourceExplanationPages":[175,176],"sourceAnswerPages":[175,176],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-060","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man decided to purchase an apartment building. To finance this purchase, the man borrowed funds from a bank. The man executed a negotiable promissory note and a mortgage to the bank in order to secure repayment of the loan. Two years later, the bank sold its promissory note to a mortgage company by endorsing and promptly delivering the note to the mortgage company. Shortly thereafter, the man stopped making payments on the mortgage, and the mortgage company initiated judicial foreclosure proceedings against him. The man has moved to dismiss the suit. Should the motion to dismiss be granted?","choices":{"A":"No, because the mortgage was not assigned and is unenforceable by anyone except the bank.","B":"No, because the mortgage will be deemed to have been transferred with the promissory note.","C":"Yes, because the promissory note could not be assigned independent of the mortgage.","D":"Yes, because the promissory note required a separate document for assignment to the mortgage company."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Transfer of promissory note & mortgage\n\nTransfer of both documents Both documents are transferred unless:\n\nTransfer of note without mortgage expressly prohibited in note/mortgage forbidden by statute or public policy or Transfer of mortgage without note* increases mortgagor's duties, burdens, risks Transfer is void\n\n*Jurisdictions are split as to effect of transfer.\n\nTo finance the purchase of real property, a borrower typically executes two documents that serve as evidence of the debt: Promissory note – a formal \"IOU\" that sets forth the terms of the loan. It is the primary evidence of the debt and is not recorded in the deed records. Mortgage (or deed of trust) – a lien that secures the loan by attaching the debt to a real property interest and providing a means of enforcement (eg, foreclosure). It is recorded in the deed records to provide notice of an outstanding debt attached to the real property. If a lender decides to sell its security interest, the lender can assign the promissory note independent of the mortgage to an assignee (Choice C). A nonnegotiable promissory note requires a separate assignment document to transfer ownership (Choice D). However, a negotiable promissory note can be transferred by merely endorsing and delivering the note to the assignee. Once the promissory note is properly transferred, the mortgage automatically transfers with the note (unless the parties agreed otherwise) (Choice A). Here, the negotiable promissory note was properly transferred to the mortgage company upon its endorsement and delivery. The mortgage will be deemed to have automatically transferred to the mortgage company, so the mortgage company can enforce the mortgage through foreclosure. Therefore, the man's motion to dismiss this foreclosure proceeding should be denied.\n\nEducational objective: A nonnegotiable promissory note must be transferred by a separate document, while a negotiable promissory note can be transferred by endorsing and delivering the note. Either note can be transferred independent of the mortgage, and the mortgage will automatically transfer with the note (unless otherwise agreed).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (transfer of mortgages).","educationalObjective":"A nonnegotiable promissory note must be transferred by a separate document, while a negotiable promissory note can be transferred by endorsing and delivering the note. Either note can be transferred independent of the mortgage, and the mortgage will automatically transfer with the note (unless otherwise agreed).","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (transfer of mortgages).","rule":"A nonnegotiable promissory note must be transferred by a separate document, while a negotiable promissory note can be transferred by endorsing and delivering the note. Either note can be transferred independent of the mortgage, and the mortgage will automatically transfer with the note (unless otherwise agreed).","irac":{"issue":"Should the motion to dismiss be granted?","rule":"A nonnegotiable promissory note must be transferred by a separate document, while a negotiable promissory note can be transferred by endorsing and delivering the note. Either note can be transferred independent of the mortgage, and the mortgage will automatically transfer with the note (unless otherwise agreed).","analysis":"Transfer of promissory note & mortgage\n\nTransfer of both documents Both documents are transferred unless:\n\nTransfer of note without mortgage expressly prohibited in note/mortgage forbidden by statute or public policy or Transfer of mortgage without note* increases mortgagor's duties, burdens, risks Transfer is void\n\n*Jurisdictions are split as to effect of transfer.\n\nTo finance the purchase of real property, a borrower typically executes two documents that serve as evidence of the debt: Promissory note – a formal \"IOU\" that sets forth the terms of the loan. It is the primary evidence of the debt and is not recorded in the deed records. Mortgage (or deed of trust) – a lien that secures the loan by attaching the debt to a real property interest and providing a means of enforcement (eg, foreclosure). It is recorded in the deed records to provide notice of an outstanding debt attached to the real property. If a lender decides to sell its security interest, the lender can assign the promissory note independent of the mortgage to an assignee (Choice C). A nonnegotiable promissory note requires a separate assignment document to transfer ownership (Choice D). However, a negotiable promissory note can be transferred by merely endorsing and delivering the note to the assignee. Once the promissory note is properly transferred, the mortgage automatically transfers with the note (unless the parties agreed otherwise) (Choice A). Here, the negotiable promissory note was properly transferred to the mortgage company upon its endorsement and delivery. The mortgage will be deemed to have automatically transferred to the mortgage company, so the mortgage company can enforce the mortgage through foreclosure. Therefore, the man's motion to dismiss this foreclosure proceeding should be denied.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the mortgage will be deemed to have been transferred with the promissory note."},"questionZh":"一名男子决定购买一栋公寓楼。为了资助这次购买，该男子从银行借了资金。该男子向银行签发了可转让本票和抵押贷款，以确保偿还贷款。两年后，银行通过背书并立即将票据交付给抵押贷款公司，将其出售给抵押贷款公司。此后不久，该男子停止支付抵押贷款，抵押贷款公司对他发起了司法取消抵押品赎回权诉讼。该男子已提出驳回诉讼。驳回动议是否应获批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为抵押贷款尚未转让，除银行外任何人都无法执行。","B":"不可以，因为抵押贷款将被视为已随本票一起转让。","C":"是的，因为本票不能独立于抵押贷款而转让。","D":"是的，因为本票需要一份单独的文件才能转让给抵押贷款公司。"},"explanationZh":"本票及抵押的转让\n\n两份文件的转让 两份文件均被转让，除非：\n\n票据中明确禁止转让无抵押的票据/法律或公共政策禁止的抵押或转让无票据*的抵押会增加抵押人的义务、负担和风险 转让无效\n\n*司法管辖区因转让的影响而有所不同。\n\n为了为购买不动产提供资金，借款人通常会签署两份文件作为债务证据： 本票 - 规定贷款条款的正式“借据”。它是债务的主要证据，不记录在契约记录中。抵押（或信托契约）——通过将债务附加到不动产权益并提供强制执行手段（例如取消抵押品赎回权）来确保贷款的留置权。它被记录在契约记录中，以提供与不动产相关的未偿债务的通知。如果贷款人决定出售其担保权益，则贷款人可以将独立于抵押贷款的本票转让给受让人（选择 C）。不可转让本票需要单独的转让文件来转让所有权（选择 D）。然而，可转让本票只需背书并将票据交付给受让人即可转让。一旦本票妥善转让，抵押权自动随本票转让（除非当事人另有约定）（选择A）。在此，可转让本票在背书并交付后已妥善转让给抵押公司。抵押贷款将被视为已自动转移至抵押公司，因此抵押公司可以通过取消抵押品赎回权来强制执行抵押贷款。因此，该男子提出的驳回取消抵押品赎回权诉讼的动议应予驳回。\n\n教育目标：不可转让本票必须通过单独的单据转让，而可转让本票可以通过背书并交付票据来转让。任何一张票据都可以独立于抵押而转让，抵押将自动随票据一起转让（除非另有约定）。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款转让）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"不可转让本票必须以单独的单据转让，而可转让本票可以通过背书并交付票据来转让。任何一张票据都可以独立于抵押而转让，抵押将自动随票据一起转让（除非另有约定）。","iracZh":{"issue":"驳回动议是否应获批准？","rule":"不可转让本票必须以单独的单据转让，而可转让本票可以通过背书并交付票据来转让。任何一张票据都可以独立于抵押而转让，抵押将自动随票据一起转让（除非另有约定）。","analysis":"本票及抵押的转让\n\n两份文件的转让 两份文件均被转让，除非：\n\n票据中明确禁止转让无抵押的票据/法律或公共政策禁止的抵押或转让无票据*的抵押会增加抵押人的义务、负担和风险 转让无效\n\n*司法管辖区因转让的影响而有所不同。\n\n为了为购买不动产提供资金，借款人通常会签署两份文件作为债务证据： 本票 - 规定贷款条款的正式“借据”。它是债务的主要证据，不记录在契约记录中。抵押（或信托契约）——通过将债务附加到不动产权益并提供强制执行手段（例如取消抵押品赎回权）来确保贷款的留置权。它被记录在契约记录中，以提供与不动产相关的未偿债务的通知。如果贷款人决定出售其担保权益，则贷款人可以将独立于抵押贷款的本票转让给受让人（选择 C）。不可转让本票需要单独的转让文件来转让所有权（选择 D）。然而，可转让本票只需背书并将票据交付给受让人即可转让。一旦本票妥善转让，抵押权自动随本票转让（除非当事人另有约定）（选择A）。在此，可转让本票在背书并交付后已妥善转让给抵押公司。抵押贷款将被视为已自动转移至抵押公司，因此抵押公司可以通过取消抵押品赎回权来强制执行抵押贷款。因此，该男子提出的驳回取消抵押品赎回权诉讼的动议应予驳回。\n\n教育目标：不可转让本票必须通过单独的单据转让，而可转让本票可以通过背书并交付票据来转让。任何一张票据都可以独立于抵押而转让，抵押将自动随票据一起转让（除非另有约定）。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款转让）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为抵押贷款将被视为已随本票一起转让。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":60,"sourceQuestionPage":177,"sourceQuestionPages":[177],"sourceExplanationPages":[178,179],"sourceAnswerPages":[178,179],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-061","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A newly married couple, anxious to start a life together, decided to build a new home. They financed the home by executing a 15-year promissory note and purchase-money mortgage to the bank. The mortgage, which contained a power-of-sale clause, was promptly recorded. Seven years later, the couple legally separated. Neither could afford the mortgage payments on their individual salary, so they abandoned the home and stopped making mortgage payments. After the couple missed two payments, the bank decided to initiate a foreclosure proceeding. The jurisdiction follows the title theory of mortgages and allows for nonjudicial foreclosures. Which of the following actions poses the most financial risk to the bank?","choices":{"A":"Accepting a deed in lieu of foreclosure.","B":"Entering the property to make repairs prior to foreclosure.","C":"Foreclosing on the mortgage through a privately conducted public sale.","D":"Leasing the home to a tenant."},"answer":"D","explanation":"This jurisdiction follows the title theory of mortgages, so a lender is considered the owner of the mortgaged property until the mortgage debt is fully satisfied. This gives the lender the right to take possession of the property at any time (unless the mortgage states otherwise). If the lender takes possession, then it assumes the duty to take reasonable care of the property until foreclosure proceedings end. The duty of reasonable care includes the duty not to commit waste that would impair the value of the property. As a result, the lender is liable to the borrower if the value of the property is reduced because of the lender's actions during foreclosure proceedings. Here, if the bank chooses to lease the home, it runs the risk that the tenant will damage the property. Damage to the property reduces any equity that the borrower (here, the couple) may have in the home. Such damage would amount to waste, for which the bank would have to account to the couple during the foreclosure proceedings. Therefore, leasing the home to a tenant poses the most financial risk to the bank. (Choice A) A borrower may deed all interest in the property to the lender in lieu of foreclosure. This poses minimal financial risk to the lender because it allows the lender to take immediate possession of the property without any further legal formalities and to generally reserve the right to pursue a deficiency against the debtor. (Choice B) The bank may enter the property to make repairs prior to foreclosure because this would likely increase the value of the property—not impair it. (Choice C) In jurisdictions that permit nonjudicial foreclosure, a lender may foreclose on a mortgage through a privately conducted public sale when the mortgage contains a power- of-sale clause (as seen here). This allows the bank to foreclose without court intervention.\n\nEducational objective: A lender has the right to take possession of mortgaged property but is liable to the borrower for any waste that the lender commits during foreclosure proceedings.","educationalObjective":"A lender has the right to take possession of mortgaged property but is liable to the borrower for any waste that the lender commits during foreclosure proceedings.","references":"","rule":"A lender has the right to take possession of mortgaged property but is liable to the borrower for any waste that the lender commits during foreclosure proceedings.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following actions poses the most financial risk to the bank?","rule":"A lender has the right to take possession of mortgaged property but is liable to the borrower for any waste that the lender commits during foreclosure proceedings.","analysis":"This jurisdiction follows the title theory of mortgages, so a lender is considered the owner of the mortgaged property until the mortgage debt is fully satisfied. This gives the lender the right to take possession of the property at any time (unless the mortgage states otherwise). If the lender takes possession, then it assumes the duty to take reasonable care of the property until foreclosure proceedings end. The duty of reasonable care includes the duty not to commit waste that would impair the value of the property. As a result, the lender is liable to the borrower if the value of the property is reduced because of the lender's actions during foreclosure proceedings. Here, if the bank chooses to lease the home, it runs the risk that the tenant will damage the property. Damage to the property reduces any equity that the borrower (here, the couple) may have in the home. Such damage would amount to waste, for which the bank would have to account to the couple during the foreclosure proceedings. Therefore, leasing the home to a tenant poses the most financial risk to the bank. (Choice A) A borrower may deed all interest in the property to the lender in lieu of foreclosure. This poses minimal financial risk to the lender because it allows the lender to take immediate possession of the property without any further legal formalities and to generally reserve the right to pursue a deficiency against the debtor. (Choice B) The bank may enter the property to make repairs prior to foreclosure because this would likely increase the value of the property—not impair it. (Choice C) In jurisdictions that permit nonjudicial foreclosure, a lender may foreclose on a mortgage through a privately conducted public sale when the mortgage contains a power- of-sale clause (as seen here). This allows the bank to foreclose without court intervention.","conclusion":"Answer D: Leasing the home to a tenant."},"questionZh":"一对新婚夫妇渴望共同开始新的生活，决定建造一个新家。他们通过向银行签发 15 年期本票和购房款抵押贷款来为房屋融资。包含销售权条款的抵押贷款立即被记录下来。七年后，这对夫妇合法分居。两人都无法以个人工资支付抵押贷款，因此他们放弃了房屋并停止支付抵押贷款。在这对夫妇错过两次付款后，银行决定启动止赎程序。该司法管辖区遵循抵押权理论，并允许非司法取消抵押品赎回权。以下哪项行为给银行带来的财务风险最大？","choicesZh":{"A":"接受契约代替丧失抵押品赎回权。","B":"在丧失抵押品赎回权之前进入该房产进行维修。","C":"通过私人公开出售取消抵押贷款的赎回权。","D":"将房屋出租给租户。"},"explanationZh":"该司法管辖区遵循抵押权理论，因此在抵押债务完全清偿之前，贷款人被视为抵押财产的所有者。这使贷方有权随时占有该财产（除非抵押贷款另有规定）。如果贷款人占有该财产，则其有责任合理保管该财产，直至止赎程序结束。合理注意的义务包括不造成损害财产价值的浪费的义务。因此，如果由于贷款人在止赎程序期间的行为导致财产价值减少，贷款人应对借款人承担责任。在这里，如果银行选择出租房屋，就会面临租户损坏财产的风险。财产损坏会减少借款人（此处为夫妇）在房屋中可能拥有的任何净值。这种损害相当于浪费，银行必须在取消抵押品赎回权的诉讼过程中向这对夫妇负责。因此，将房屋出租给租户对银行构成的财务风险最大。 （选择 A）借款人可以将财产的所有权益转让给贷款人以代替丧失抵押品赎回权。这对贷方造成的财务风险很小，因为它允许贷方立即占有该财产，而无需任何进一步的法律手续，并且通常保留向债务人追索欠款的权利。 （选择 B）银行可能会在丧失抵押品赎回权之前进入该房产进行维修，因为这可能会增加该房产的价值，而不是损害它。 （选择 C）在允许非司法取消抵押品赎回权的司法管辖区，当抵押贷款包含出售权条款（如此处所示）时，贷款人可以通过私下进行的公开出售取消抵押贷款的抵押品赎回权。这使得银行可以在没有法院干预的情况下取消抵押品赎回权。\n\n教育目标：贷款人有权占有抵押​​财产，但要对贷款人在止赎程序中造成的任何浪费承担责任。","educationalObjectiveZh":"贷款人有权占有抵押财产，但要对贷款人在止赎程序中造成的任何浪费承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项行为给银行带来的财务风险最大？","rule":"贷款人有权占有抵押财产，但要对贷款人在止赎程序中造成的任何浪费承担责任。","analysis":"该司法管辖区遵循抵押权理论，因此在抵押债务完全清偿之前，贷款人被视为抵押财产的所有者。这使贷方有权随时占有该财产（除非抵押贷款另有规定）。如果贷款人占有该财产，则其有责任合理保管该财产，直至止赎程序结束。合理注意的义务包括不造成损害财产价值的浪费的义务。因此，如果由于贷款人在止赎程序期间的行为导致财产价值减少，贷款人应对借款人承担责任。在这里，如果银行选择出租房屋，就会面临租户损坏财产的风险。财产损坏会减少借款人（此处为夫妇）在房屋中可能拥有的任何净值。这种损害相当于浪费，银行必须在取消抵押品赎回权的诉讼过程中向这对夫妇负责。因此，将房屋出租给租户对银行构成的财务风险最大。 （选择 A）借款人可以将财产的所有权益转让给贷款人以代替丧失抵押品赎回权。这对贷方造成的财务风险很小，因为它允许贷方立即占有该财产，而无需任何进一步的法律手续，并且通常保留向债务人追索欠款的权利。 （选择 B）银行可能会在丧失抵押品赎回权之前进入该房产进行维修，因为这可能会增加该房产的价值，而不是损害它。 （选择 C）在允许非司法取消抵押品赎回权的司法管辖区，当抵押贷款包含出售权条款（如此处所示）时，贷款人可以通过私下进行的公开出售取消抵押贷款的抵押品赎回权。这使得银行可以在没有法院干预的情况下取消抵押品赎回权。\n\n教育目标：贷款人有权占有抵押​​财产，但要对贷款人在止赎程序中造成的任何浪费承担责任。","conclusion":"答案 D：将房屋出租给租户。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"waste","ipa":"/wˈeɪst/","syllables":"waste","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 浪费, 废物, 损耗, 消耗, 荒地, 垃圾, 地面风化物；a. 废弃的, 荒芜的, 多余的；vt. 浪费, 消耗, 使荒芜","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“waste”","wordForm":"名词；词形 i:wasting/d:wasted/s:wastes/p:wasted/3:wastes","sourceSentence":"A lender has the right to take possession of mortgaged property but is liable to the borrower for any waste that the lender commits during foreclosure proceedings.","synonyms":["wastefulness","dissipation","permissive waste","thriftlessness"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":61,"sourceQuestionPage":180,"sourceQuestionPages":[180],"sourceExplanationPages":[181],"sourceAnswerPages":[181],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-062","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"At the time of his death last week, a man owned a small farm. By his duly probated will, drawn five years ago, the man did the following: 1. He devised the farm \"to my farmhand for the life of my wife, then to my cousin.\" 2. He gave \"all the rest, residue and remainder of my estate, both real and personal, to my friend.\" At his death, the man was survived by a son (his sole heir), the farmhand, the cousin, and the friend. The wife had died a week before the man. Who has title to the farm?","choices":{"A":"The cousin, in fee simple.","B":"The farmhand for life, remainder to the cousin.","C":"The friend, in fee simple.","D":"The son, in fee simple."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Doctrines affecting conveyance by will\n\nLapse Causes devise to fail if beneficiary predeceases testator\n\nAdemption Causes devise to fail by either:\n\nextinction – specifically devised property not owned by testator (or destroyed or fundamentally changed) at death satisfaction – beneficiary received devised property (or other asset intended to satisfy devise) during testator's life Exoneration Allows beneficiary of specifically devised real property to use estate's remaining assets to pay off any encumbrances on that property\n\nAbatement Reduces devises that cannot be satisfied by assets remaining after testator's debts are paid Residuary devises abated first, followed by general & then specific devises Lapse (ie, the failure of a gift of property under a will) normally occurs when the beneficiary dies before the testator dies. But when a will gives a life estate pur autre vie— ie, a life estate measured by the life of someone other than the life tenant—lapse occurs when either the life tenant or the measuring life predeceases the testator. The life tenant's (or other measuring life's) predecease affects only the life tenant's interest, however, so any remainder interest is not defeated. Here, the man's will devised a life estate pur autre vie in the farm to the farmhand (measured by the wife's life) and a remainder to the cousin. Because the wife died before the man, the doctrine of lapse caused the devise to the farmhand to fail. But since the cousin's remainder interest was not defeated by the wife's death, the cousin has title to the farm in fee simple (Choices B, C & D).\n\nEducational objective: When a will gives a life estate pur autre vie, the devise will lapse (ie, fail) if the life tenant or the measuring life dies before the testator. But the remainder interest, if any, will survive.\n\nReferences\n80 Am. Jur. 2d Wills § 1415 (2019) (death of life tenant before death of testator).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Donative Transfers § 34.6 (Am. Law Inst. 1992) (donee dies before donor).","educationalObjective":"When a will gives a life estate pur autre vie, the devise will lapse (ie, fail) if the life tenant or the measuring life dies before the testator. But the remainder interest, if any, will survive.","references":"80 Am. Jur. 2d Wills § 1415 (2019) (death of life tenant before death of testator).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Donative Transfers § 34.6 (Am. Law Inst. 1992) (donee dies before donor).","rule":"When a will gives a life estate pur autre vie, the devise will lapse (ie, fail) if the life tenant or the measuring life dies before the testator. But the remainder interest, if any, will survive.","irac":{"issue":"Who has title to the farm?","rule":"When a will gives a life estate pur autre vie, the devise will lapse (ie, fail) if the life tenant or the measuring life dies before the testator. But the remainder interest, if any, will survive.","analysis":"Doctrines affecting conveyance by will\n\nLapse Causes devise to fail if beneficiary predeceases testator\n\nAdemption Causes devise to fail by either:\n\nextinction – specifically devised property not owned by testator (or destroyed or fundamentally changed) at death satisfaction – beneficiary received devised property (or other asset intended to satisfy devise) during testator's life Exoneration Allows beneficiary of specifically devised real property to use estate's remaining assets to pay off any encumbrances on that property\n\nAbatement Reduces devises that cannot be satisfied by assets remaining after testator's debts are paid Residuary devises abated first, followed by general & then specific devises Lapse (ie, the failure of a gift of property under a will) normally occurs when the beneficiary dies before the testator dies. But when a will gives a life estate pur autre vie— ie, a life estate measured by the life of someone other than the life tenant—lapse occurs when either the life tenant or the measuring life predeceases the testator. The life tenant's (or other measuring life's) predecease affects only the life tenant's interest, however, so any remainder interest is not defeated. Here, the man's will devised a life estate pur autre vie in the farm to the farmhand (measured by the wife's life) and a remainder to the cousin. Because the wife died before the man, the doctrine of lapse caused the devise to the farmhand to fail. But since the cousin's remainder interest was not defeated by the wife's death, the cousin has title to the farm in fee simple (Choices B, C & D).","conclusion":"Answer A: The cousin, in fee simple."},"questionZh":"上周去世时，一名男子拥有一个小农场。根据五年前经过正式认证的遗嘱，该男子做了以下事情： 1. 他“为我的农场工人设计了这个农场，让我妻子过上好日子，然后又给我的表弟”。 2. 他把“我的财产的所有剩余部分，无论是真实的还是个人的，都给了我的朋友”。在他死后，他留下了一个儿子（他的唯一继承人）、农场工人、表弟和朋友。妻子比男人早一周去世。谁拥有农场的所有权？","choicesZh":{"A":"表弟，其实很简单。","B":"终生当农场主，其余的归表弟。","C":"这位朋友，其实很简单。","D":"儿子，其实很简单。"},"explanationZh":"影响遗嘱转让的学说\n\n如果受益人先于遗嘱人去世，失效会导致设计失败\n\n赎回会因以下任一原因导致设计失败：\n\n消灭 – 在死亡时不属于立遗嘱人拥有的特别设计的财产（或被毁坏或根本改变） 满意 – 受益人在立遗嘱人的一生中收到设计的财产（或旨在满足设计的其他资产） 免除 允许特别设计的不动产的受益人使用遗产的剩余资产来偿还该财产的任何产权负担\n\n减免 减少遗嘱人债务清偿后剩余资产无法满足的规定。首先减免剩余规定，然后是一般规定，然后是具体规定。失效（即，遗嘱中财产赠与的失败）通常发生在受益人在立遗嘱人去世之前死亡时。但是，如果遗嘱给出了“纯粹的终身财产”（pur autre vie），即以终身承租人以外的其他人的寿命来衡量的终身财产，则当终身承租人或衡量寿命先于立遗嘱人去世时，就会发生失效。然而，终身承租人（或其他衡量寿命的人）的去世仅影响终身承租人的利益，因此任何剩余利益都不会受到影响。在这里，男人的遗嘱将农场中的终身财产划给了农场工人（以妻子的寿命来衡量），其余的则划给了表弟。由于妻子先于丈夫去世，所以“失效”理论导致农场工人的计划失败了。但由于表弟的剩余权益并未因妻子的去世而消失，因此表弟拥有农场的所有权（选择 B、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：当遗嘱赋予终身财产时，如果终身承租人或计量终身的人在立遗嘱人之前去世，则该计划将失效（即失败）。但剩余的利息（如果有的话）将继续存在。\n\n参考文献\n上午 80 点。尤尔。 2d Wills § 1415 (2019)（立遗嘱人死亡前终身承租人死亡）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：捐赠转让 § 34.6（Am. Law Inst. 1992）（受赠人先于捐赠人去世）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当遗嘱赋予终身财产时，如果终身承租人或测量生命先于立遗嘱人去世，则该设计将失效（即失败）。但剩余的利息（如果有的话）将继续存在。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁拥有农场的所有权？","rule":"当遗嘱赋予终身财产时，如果终身承租人或测量生命先于立遗嘱人去世，则该设计将失效（即失败）。但剩余的利息（如果有的话）将继续存在。","analysis":"影响遗嘱转让的学说\n\n如果受益人先于遗嘱人去世，失效会导致设计失败\n\n赎回会因以下任一原因导致设计失败：\n\n消灭 – 在死亡时不属于立遗嘱人拥有的特别设计的财产（或被毁坏或根本改变） 满意 – 受益人在立遗嘱人的一生中收到设计的财产（或旨在满足设计的其他资产） 免除 允许特别设计的不动产的受益人使用遗产的剩余资产来偿还该财产的任何产权负担\n\n减免 减少遗嘱人债务清偿后剩余资产无法满足的规定。首先减免剩余规定，然后是一般规定，然后是具体规定。失效（即，遗嘱中财产赠与的失败）通常发生在受益人在立遗嘱人去世之前死亡时。但是，如果遗嘱给出了“纯粹的终身财产”（pur autre vie），即以终身承租人以外的其他人的寿命来衡量的终身财产，则当终身承租人或衡量寿命先于立遗嘱人去世时，就会发生失效。然而，终身承租人（或其他衡量寿命的人）的去世仅影响终身承租人的利益，因此任何剩余利益都不会受到影响。在这里，男人的遗嘱将农场中的终身财产划给了农场工人（以妻子的寿命来衡量），其余的则划给了表弟。由于妻子先于丈夫去世，所以“失效”理论导致农场工人的计划失败了。但由于表弟的剩余权益并未因妻子的去世而消失，因此表弟拥有农场的所有权（选择 B、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：当遗嘱赋予终身财产时，如果终身承租人或计量终身的人在立遗嘱人之前去世，则该计划将失效（即失败）。但剩余的利息（如果有的话）将继续存在。\n\n参考文献\n上午 80 点。尤尔。 2d Wills § 1415 (2019)（立遗嘱人死亡前终身承租人死亡）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：捐赠转让 § 34.6（Am. Law Inst. 1992）（受赠人先于捐赠人去世）。","conclusion":"答案 A：表弟，其实很简单。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":62,"sourceQuestionPage":182,"sourceQuestionPages":[182],"sourceExplanationPages":[183,184],"sourceAnswerPages":[183,184],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-063","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man borrowed $500,000 from a bank, securing the loan with a mortgage on a commercial building he owned. The mortgage provided as follows: \"No prepayment may be made on this loan during the first two years after the date of this mortgage. Thereafter, prepayment may be made in any amount at any time but only if accompanied by a prepayment fee of 5% of the amount prepaid.\" One year later, the man received an unexpected cash gift of $1 million and wished to pay off the $495,000 principal balance still owed on the loan. Concerned that the bank might refuse prepayment—despite a rise in market interest rates in the year since the loan was made— or at least insist on the 5% prepayment fee, the man consulted an attorney concerning the enforceability of the above-quoted clause. There is no applicable statute. What is the attorney likely to say?","choices":{"A":"The entire clause is unenforceable, because it violates a public policy favoring the prompt and early repayment of debt.","B":"The entire clause is unenforceable, because the rise in interest rates will allow the bank to reloan the funds without loss.","C":"The two-year prepayment prohibition and the prepayment fee provision are both valid and enforceable.","D":"The two-year prepayment prohibition is unenforceable, but the prepayment fee provision is enforceable."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Right to prepay mortgage\n\nCommon law Prepayment barred unless mortgage documents provide otherwise\n\nMajority rule Prepayment allowed unless mortgage documents provide otherwise\n\nUnder the common law \"perfect tender in time\" rule, a mortgagor (debtor) has no right to prepay a mortgage debt unless the mortgage documents provide otherwise. However, the prevailing modern trend reverses this presumption and allows prepayment unless the mortgage documents provide otherwise. Prepayment prohibitions or penalties contained in a mortgage are generally valid and enforceable.* They do not violate public policy because they serve the beneficial purpose of: allowing the mortgagee to maintain the financial yield intended under the mortgage agreement and compensating the mortgagee for any losses suffered because of a prepayment (Choice A). As a result, the two-year prepayment prohibition and prepayment fee provision in the man's mortgage are valid and enforceable, even if the bank could reloan the funds to another without loss (Choices B & D). *Roughly half of all states prohibit or restrict prepayment penalties on mortgaged residential property (eg, by limiting the penalty rate or time in which the penalty can be imposed).\n\nEducational objective: A debtor has the right to prepay a mortgage debt unless the mortgage provides otherwise. Prepayment prohibitions or penalties in a mortgage are generally valid and enforceable.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages §§ 6.1–6.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (right to prepay mortgage).","educationalObjective":"A debtor has the right to prepay a mortgage debt unless the mortgage provides otherwise. Prepayment prohibitions or penalties in a mortgage are generally valid and enforceable.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages §§ 6.1–6.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (right to prepay mortgage).","rule":"A debtor has the right to prepay a mortgage debt unless the mortgage provides otherwise. Prepayment prohibitions or penalties in a mortgage are generally valid and enforceable.","irac":{"issue":"What is the attorney likely to say?","rule":"A debtor has the right to prepay a mortgage debt unless the mortgage provides otherwise. Prepayment prohibitions or penalties in a mortgage are generally valid and enforceable.","analysis":"Right to prepay mortgage\n\nCommon law Prepayment barred unless mortgage documents provide otherwise\n\nMajority rule Prepayment allowed unless mortgage documents provide otherwise\n\nUnder the common law \"perfect tender in time\" rule, a mortgagor (debtor) has no right to prepay a mortgage debt unless the mortgage documents provide otherwise. However, the prevailing modern trend reverses this presumption and allows prepayment unless the mortgage documents provide otherwise. Prepayment prohibitions or penalties contained in a mortgage are generally valid and enforceable.* They do not violate public policy because they serve the beneficial purpose of: allowing the mortgagee to maintain the financial yield intended under the mortgage agreement and compensating the mortgagee for any losses suffered because of a prepayment (Choice A). As a result, the two-year prepayment prohibition and prepayment fee provision in the man's mortgage are valid and enforceable, even if the bank could reloan the funds to another without loss (Choices B & D). *Roughly half of all states prohibit or restrict prepayment penalties on mortgaged residential property (eg, by limiting the penalty rate or time in which the penalty can be imposed).","conclusion":"Answer C: The two-year prepayment prohibition and the prepayment fee provision are both valid and enforceable."},"questionZh":"一名男子向银行借了 50 万美元，并以他拥有的一栋商业建筑作为抵押来获得贷款。抵押贷款规定如下：“在抵押贷款日期后的头两年内，不得提前还款。此后，可以随时预付任何金额，但必须缴纳预付金额5%的预付款费。”一年后，该男子意外收到了 100 万美元的现金礼物，并希望偿还尚欠的 495,000 美元的贷款本金余额。由于担心银行可能拒绝提前还款——尽管贷款当年市场利率有所上升——或者至少坚持收取5%的提前还款费，该男子就上述条款的可执行性咨询了律师。没有适用的法规。律师可能会说什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"整个条款无法执行，因为它违反了有利于及时和提前偿还债务的公共政策。","B":"整个条款是无法执行的，因为利率上升将使银行可以在不损失资金的情况下重新贷款。","C":"两年预付款禁令和预付款费用规定均有效且可执行。","D":"两年预付款禁令不可执行，但预付款费用条款是可执行的。"},"explanationZh":"提前偿还抵押贷款的权利\n\n普通法禁止预付款，除非抵押文件另有规定\n\n多数决定允许预付款，除非抵押文件另有规定\n\n根据普通法“及时交付”规则，除非抵押文件另有规定，抵押人（债务人）无权提前偿还抵押债务。然而，流行的现代趋势推翻了这一假设，并允许提前还款，除非抵押贷款文件另有规定。抵押贷款中包含的提前还款禁令或处罚通常是有效且可执行的。*它们不违反公共政策，因为它们的有益目的是：允许抵押权人维持抵押贷款协议规定的财务收益，并补偿抵押权人因提前还款而遭受的任何损失（选择 A）。因此，该男子抵押贷款中的两年提前还款禁止和提前还款费用规定是有效且可执行的，即使银行可以将资金再贷给另一人而不会造成损失（Choices B & D）。 *大约一半的州禁止或限制对抵押住宅房产的提前还款处罚（例如，通过限制处罚率或处罚的时间）。\n\n教育目标：债务人有权提前偿还抵押贷款债务，除非抵押贷款另有规定。抵押贷款中的提前还款禁令或处罚通常是有效且可执行的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款第 §§ 6.1–6.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（预付抵押贷款的权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非抵押贷款另有规定，债务人有权提前偿还抵押贷款债务。抵押贷款中的提前还款禁令或处罚通常是有效且可执行的。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师可能会说什么？","rule":"除非抵押贷款另有规定，债务人有权提前偿还抵押贷款债务。抵押贷款中的提前还款禁令或处罚通常是有效且可执行的。","analysis":"提前偿还抵押贷款的权利\n\n普通法禁止预付款，除非抵押文件另有规定\n\n多数决定允许预付款，除非抵押文件另有规定\n\n根据普通法“及时交付”规则，除非抵押文件另有规定，抵押人（债务人）无权提前偿还抵押债务。然而，流行的现代趋势推翻了这一假设，并允许提前还款，除非抵押贷款文件另有规定。抵押贷款中包含的提前还款禁令或处罚通常是有效且可执行的。*它们不违反公共政策，因为它们的有益目的是：允许抵押权人维持抵押贷款协议规定的财务收益，并补偿抵押权人因提前还款而遭受的任何损失（选择 A）。因此，该男子抵押贷款中的两年提前还款禁止和提前还款费用规定是有效且可执行的，即使银行可以将资金再贷给另一人而不会造成损失（Choices B & D）。 *大约一半的州禁止或限制对抵押住宅房产的提前还款处罚（例如，通过限制处罚率或处罚的时间）。\n\n教育目标：债务人有权提前偿还抵押贷款债务，除非抵押贷款另有规定。抵押贷款中的提前还款禁令或处罚通常是有效且可执行的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款第 §§ 6.1–6.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（预付抵押贷款的权利）。","conclusion":"答案 C：两年预付款禁令和预付款费用规定均有效且可执行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":63,"sourceQuestionPage":185,"sourceQuestionPages":[185],"sourceExplanationPages":[186],"sourceAnswerPages":[186],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-064","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A creditor received a valid judgment against a debtor and promptly filed the judgment in the county land records. Two years later, the debtor purchased land in the county and promptly recorded the warranty deed to the land. Subsequently, the debtor borrowed $30,000 from his aunt, signed a promissory note for that amount, and secured the note with a mortgage on the land. The mortgage was promptly recorded. The aunt failed to make a title search before making the loan. The debtor made no payment to the creditor and defaulted on the mortgage loan from his aunt. A valid judicial foreclosure proceeding was held, in which the creditor, the aunt, and the debtor were named parties. A dispute arose as to which lien had priority. A statute of the jurisdiction provides: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for 10 years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" A second statute of the jurisdiction provides: \"No unrecorded conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value without notice, who shall first record.\" Who has the prior lien?","choices":{"A":"The aunt, because a judgment lien is subordinate to a mortgage lien.","B":"The aunt, because she is a mortgagee under a purchase-money mortgage.","C":"The creditor, because its judgment was filed first.","D":"The creditor, because the aunt had a duty to make a title search of the property."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Notice of competing property interests\n\nActual Buyer has direct knowledge of prior interest in land notice\n\nRecord Document showing prior interest in land is properly recorded & appears in notice buyer's chain of title\n\nInquiry Buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt reasonable notice person to investigate (eg, visible use of property, reference to unrecorded transaction in recorded instrument)\n\nThe priority of competing property interests is evaluated under the jurisdiction's recording act. Under a race-notice statute (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) subsequent property interest has priority over an earlier competing property interest if the BFP: lacked notice of the earlier interest at the time the BFP acquired his/her interest and recorded his/her interest at any time before the earlier interest was recorded. And a purchaser had constructive notice (ie, record notice) of an earlier property interest if a document showing that interest was properly recorded (ie, filed in the official land records) and appeared in the purchaser's chain of title. Here, the creditor has the prior lien because it filed its judgment against the debtor in the county land records first. As a result, she had constructive notice of the creditor's prior interest when she acquired a mortgage lien on that property. Therefore, the aunt is not a BFP and the creditor has priority. (Choices A & B) A judgment lien is not necessarily subordinate (inferior) to a mortgage. However, a judgment lien is subordinate to a purchase-money mortgage (PMM)—a mortgage that allows the buyer to purchase or improve the property. Here, the aunt's mortgage was not used by the debtor to purchase or improve the land (not a PMM). And since the creditor filed its judgment before the aunt recorded her mortgage, the creditor has the prior lien. (Choice D) A purchaser (eg, the aunt) has no duty to conduct a title search before he/she acquires a property interest. But a purchaser who fails to do so cannot claim that he/she lacked notice of an earlier property interest that was properly recorded and in the purchaser's chain of title.\n\nEducational objective: In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier interest if the BFP (1) lacked notice of that interest and (2) recorded first. But a BFP has constructive notice of the earlier interest if a document showing that interest was properly recorded and appeared in the purchaser's chain of title.","educationalObjective":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier interest if the BFP (1) lacked notice of that interest and (2) recorded first. But a BFP has constructive notice of the earlier interest if a document showing that interest was properly recorded and appeared in the purchaser's chain of title.","references":"","rule":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier interest if the BFP (1) lacked notice of that interest and (2) recorded first. But a BFP has constructive notice of the earlier interest if a document showing that interest was properly recorded and appeared in the purchaser's chain of title.","irac":{"issue":"A creditor received a valid judgment against a debtor and promptly filed the judgment in the county land records. Two years later, the debtor purchased land in the county and promptly recorded the warranty deed to the land. Subsequently, the debtor borrowed $30,000 from his aunt, signed a promissory note for that amount, and secured the note with a mortgage on the land. The mortgage was promptly recorded. The aunt failed to make a title search before making the loan. The debtor made no payment to the creditor and defaulted on the mortgage loan from his aunt. A valid judicial foreclosure proceeding was held, in which the creditor, the aunt, and the debtor were named parties. A dispute arose as to which lien had priority. A statute of the jurisdiction provides: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for 10 years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" A second statute of the jurisdiction provides: \"No unrecorded conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value without notice, who shall first record.\" Who has the prior lien?","rule":"In a race-notice jurisdiction, a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier interest if the BFP (1) lacked notice of that interest and (2) recorded first. But a BFP has constructive notice of the earlier interest if a document showing that interest was properly recorded and appeared in the purchaser's chain of title.","analysis":"Notice of competing property interests\n\nActual Buyer has direct knowledge of prior interest in land notice\n\nRecord Document showing prior interest in land is properly recorded & appears in notice buyer's chain of title\n\nInquiry Buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt reasonable notice person to investigate (eg, visible use of property, reference to unrecorded transaction in recorded instrument)\n\nThe priority of competing property interests is evaluated under the jurisdiction's recording act. Under a race-notice statute (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) subsequent property interest has priority over an earlier competing property interest if the BFP: lacked notice of the earlier interest at the time the BFP acquired his/her interest and recorded his/her interest at any time before the earlier interest was recorded. And a purchaser had constructive notice (ie, record notice) of an earlier property interest if a document showing that interest was properly recorded (ie, filed in the official land records) and appeared in the purchaser's chain of title. Here, the creditor has the prior lien because it filed its judgment against the debtor in the county land records first. As a result, she had constructive notice of the creditor's prior interest when she acquired a mortgage lien on that property. Therefore, the aunt is not a BFP and the creditor has priority. (Choices A & B) A judgment lien is not necessarily subordinate (inferior) to a mortgage. However, a judgment lien is subordinate to a purchase-money mortgage (PMM)—a mortgage that allows the buyer to purchase or improve the property. Here, the aunt's mortgage was not used by the debtor to purchase or improve the land (not a PMM). And since the creditor filed its judgment before the aunt recorded her mortgage, the creditor has the prior lien. (Choice D) A purchaser (eg, the aunt) has no duty to conduct a title search before he/she acquires a property interest. But a purchaser who fails to do so cannot claim that he/she lacked notice of an earlier property interest that was properly recorded and in the purchaser's chain of title.","conclusion":"Answer C: The creditor, because its judgment was filed first."},"questionZh":"债权人收到针对债务人的有效判决，并立即将判决归档到县土地记录中。两年后，债务人在县城购买了土地，并及时登记了该土地的保修契约。随后，债务人向他的姨妈借了3万美元，签署了一份这笔金额的期票，并用土地抵押了该票据。抵押贷款立即被记录。阿姨在贷款前没有进行产权查询。债务人没有向债权人付款，也拖欠了他姨妈的抵押贷款。进行了有效的司法止赎程序，其中债权人、阿姨和债务人被指定为当事人。关于哪一个留置权具有优先权，出现了争议。该管辖区的法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起 10 年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”该司法管辖区的第二条法规规定：“未经通知的情况下，任何未记录的不动产转让或抵押均不得对后续购买者有利，后续购买者应首先进行记录。”谁拥有优先留置权？","choicesZh":{"A":"阿姨，因为判决留置权从属于抵押留置权。","B":"阿姨，因为她是购房款抵押贷款下的抵押权人。","C":"债权人，因为其判决是先提交的。","D":"债权人，因为阿姨有义务对财产进行产权调查。"},"explanationZh":"竞争财产利益的通知\n\n实际买家直接了解土地通知中的优先权益\n\n显示先前对土地的兴趣的记录文件已正确记录并出现在通知买方的所有权链中\n\n询问 买方知道或应该知道会促使合理通知人调查的情况（例如，财产的明显使用、提及记录文书中未记录的交易）\n\n竞争财产利益的优先权根据司法管辖区的记录法进行评估。根据竞赛通知法规（如下所示），善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的后续财产权益优先于先前的竞争性财产权益，如果 BFP： 在 BFP 获得其权益时缺乏对先前权益的通知，并在记录先前权益之前的任何时间记录了他/她的权益。如果有文件表明该权益已正确记录（即在官方土地记录中归档）并出现在购买者的所有权链中，则购买者就拥有先前财产权益的推定通知（即记录通知）。在这里，债权人拥有优先留置权，因为它首先在县土地记录中提交了针对债务人的判决。因此，当她获得该财产的抵押留置权时，她就已经推定知道债权人的优先权益。因此，阿姨不是BFP，债权人有优先权。 （选项 A 和 B） 判决留置权不一定从属于（次于）抵押贷款。然而，判决留置权从属于购房抵押贷款 (PMM)，即允许买方购买或改善房产的抵押贷款。在这里，阿姨的抵押贷款并没有被债务人用来购买或改善土地（不是PMM）。由于债权人在阿姨登记抵押贷款之前提交了判决，因此债权人拥有优先留置权。 （选项D） 购买者（例如阿姨）在获得房产权益之前没有义务进行产权调查。但未能这样做的购买者不能声称他/她没有注意到先前已正确记录并在购买者所有权链中的财产权益。\n\n教育目标：在竞赛通知辖区，如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益 (1) 没有通知该权益并且 (2) 首先记录，则该 BFP 的财产权益优先于先前的权益。但是，如果一份显示该利息的文件已正确记录并出现在购买者的所有权链中，则 BFP 对先前的利息具有推定通知。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在竞赛通知司法管辖区，如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益 (1) 没有通知该权益并且 (2) 首先记录，则该权益优先于先前的权益。但是，如果一份显示该利息的文件已正确记录并出现在购买者的所有权链中，则 BFP 对先前的利息具有推定通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"债权人收到针对债务人的有效判决，并立即将判决归档到县土地记录中。两年后，债务人在县城购买了土地，并及时登记了该土地的保修契约。随后，债务人向他的姨妈借了3万美元，签署了一份这笔金额的期票，并用土地抵押了该票据。抵押贷款立即被记录。阿姨在贷款前没有进行产权查询。债务人没有向债权人付款，也拖欠了他姨妈的抵押贷款。进行了有效的司法止赎程序，其中债权人、阿姨和债务人被指定为当事人。关于哪一个留置权具有优先权，出现了争议。该管辖区的法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起 10 年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”该司法管辖区的第二条法规规定：“未经通知的情况下，任何未记录的不动产转让或抵押均不得对后续购买者有利，后续购买者应首先进行记录。”谁拥有优先留置权？","rule":"在竞赛通知司法管辖区，如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益 (1) 没有通知该权益并且 (2) 首先记录，则该权益优先于先前的权益。但是，如果一份显示该利息的文件已正确记录并出现在购买者的所有权链中，则 BFP 对先前的利息具有推定通知。","analysis":"竞争财产利益的通知\n\n实际买家直接了解土地通知中的优先权益\n\n显示先前对土地的兴趣的记录文件已正确记录并出现在通知买方的所有权链中\n\n询问 买方知道或应该知道会促使合理通知人调查的情况（例如，财产的明显使用、提及记录文书中未记录的交易）\n\n竞争财产利益的优先权根据司法管辖区的记录法进行评估。根据竞赛通知法规（如下所示），善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的后续财产权益优先于先前的竞争性财产权益，如果 BFP： 在 BFP 获得其权益时缺乏对先前权益的通知，并在记录先前权益之前的任何时间记录了他/她的权益。如果有文件表明该权益已正确记录（即在官方土地记录中归档）并出现在购买者的所有权链中，则购买者就拥有先前财产权益的推定通知（即记录通知）。在这里，债权人拥有优先留置权，因为它首先在县土地记录中提交了针对债务人的判决。因此，当她获得该财产的抵押留置权时，她就已经推定知道债权人的优先权益。因此，阿姨不是BFP，债权人有优先权。 （选项 A 和 B） 判决留置权不一定从属于（次于）抵押贷款。然而，判决留置权从属于购房抵押贷款 (PMM)，即允许买方购买或改善房产的抵押贷款。在这里，阿姨的抵押贷款并没有被债务人用来购买或改善土地（不是PMM）。由于债权人在阿姨登记抵押贷款之前提交了判决，因此债权人拥有优先留置权。 （选项D） 购买者（例如阿姨）在获得房产权益之前没有义务进行产权调查。但未能这样做的购买者不能声称他/她没有注意到先前已正确记录并在购买者所有权链中的财产权益。\n\n教育目标：在竞赛通知辖区，如果善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益 (1) 没有通知该权益并且 (2) 首先记录，则该 BFP 的财产权益优先于先前的权益。但是，如果一份显示该利息的文件已正确记录并出现在购买者的所有权链中，则 BFP 对先前的利息具有推定通知。","conclusion":"答案 C：债权人，因为其判决是先提交的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":64,"sourceQuestionPage":187,"sourceQuestionPages":[187],"sourceExplanationPages":[188,189],"sourceAnswerPages":[188,189],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-065","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"An uncle was the record title holder of a vacant tract of land. He often told friends that he would leave the land to his nephew in his will. The nephew knew of these conversations. Prior to the uncle's death, the nephew conveyed the land by warranty deed to a woman for $100,000. She did not conduct a title search of the land before she accepted the deed from the nephew. She promptly and properly recorded her deed. Last month, the uncle died, leaving the land to the nephew in his duly probated will. Both the nephew and the woman now claim ownership of the land. The nephew has offered to return the $100,000 to the woman. Who has title to the land?","choices":{"A":"The nephew, because at the time of the deed to the woman, the uncle was the owner of record.","B":"The nephew, because the woman did not conduct a title search.","C":"The woman, because of the doctrine of estoppel by deed.","D":"The woman, because she recorded her deed prior to the uncle's death."},"answer":"C","explanation":"In general, a grantor can only convey title to property that he/she owns. But when unowned property is conveyed by warranty deed, that conveyance will become effective if the grantor later acquires title due to the doctrine of estoppel by deed (ie, after-acquired title). This doctrine causes the grantor's after-acquired title to automatically transfer to the grantee. The grantee can then choose to accept that transfer or reject it (and sue for damages). Here, the nephew conveyed the land to the woman for $100,000 by warranty deed. Since the uncle (not the nephew) owned the land at that time, the conveyance was ineffective. But it became effective—and title automatically transferred to the woman— when the nephew later acquired title under the uncle's will (Choice A). And since the woman accepted that transfer by claiming ownership, she has title to the land. (Choice B) A grantee has no obligation to conduct a title search before accepting a deed or claiming ownership of property. Therefore, the woman's failure to search the land records is immaterial. (Choice D) Recording protects property owners from subsequent ownership claims. Here, the woman recorded the deed before the uncle's death and before the nephew acquired title to the land under the uncle's will. But since she acquired title after the nephew, the recording would not protect her from his earlier ownership claim.\n\nEducational objective: The doctrine of estoppel by deed (ie, after-acquired title) effectuates a conveyance of unowned property by warranty deed when the grantor later acquires ownership of that property. The grantor's ownership interest automatically transfers to the grantee, who can then accept or reject the transfer.\n\nReferences\n23 Am. Jur. 2d Deeds § 278 (2018) (estoppel by deed).","educationalObjective":"The doctrine of estoppel by deed (ie, after-acquired title) effectuates a conveyance of unowned property by warranty deed when the grantor later acquires ownership of that property. The grantor's ownership interest automatically transfers to the grantee, who can then accept or reject the transfer.","references":"23 Am. Jur. 2d Deeds § 278 (2018) (estoppel by deed).","rule":"The doctrine of estoppel by deed (ie, after-acquired title) effectuates a conveyance of unowned property by warranty deed when the grantor later acquires ownership of that property. The grantor's ownership interest automatically transfers to the grantee, who can then accept or reject the transfer.","irac":{"issue":"Who has title to the land?","rule":"The doctrine of estoppel by deed (ie, after-acquired title) effectuates a conveyance of unowned property by warranty deed when the grantor later acquires ownership of that property. The grantor's ownership interest automatically transfers to the grantee, who can then accept or reject the transfer.","analysis":"In general, a grantor can only convey title to property that he/she owns. But when unowned property is conveyed by warranty deed, that conveyance will become effective if the grantor later acquires title due to the doctrine of estoppel by deed (ie, after-acquired title). This doctrine causes the grantor's after-acquired title to automatically transfer to the grantee. The grantee can then choose to accept that transfer or reject it (and sue for damages). Here, the nephew conveyed the land to the woman for $100,000 by warranty deed. Since the uncle (not the nephew) owned the land at that time, the conveyance was ineffective. But it became effective—and title automatically transferred to the woman— when the nephew later acquired title under the uncle's will (Choice A). And since the woman accepted that transfer by claiming ownership, she has title to the land. (Choice B) A grantee has no obligation to conduct a title search before accepting a deed or claiming ownership of property. Therefore, the woman's failure to search the land records is immaterial. (Choice D) Recording protects property owners from subsequent ownership claims. Here, the woman recorded the deed before the uncle's death and before the nephew acquired title to the land under the uncle's will. But since she acquired title after the nephew, the recording would not protect her from his earlier ownership claim.","conclusion":"Answer C: The woman, because of the doctrine of estoppel by deed."},"questionZh":"一位叔叔是一块空地的记录所有权保持者。他经常告诉朋友，他将在遗嘱中将土地留给他的侄子。侄子知道这些谈话。在叔叔去世之前，侄子以 10 万美元的价格通过担保契据将土地转让给一名妇女。在接受侄子的地契之前，她没有对土地进行产权调查。她迅速并正确地记录了自己的行为。上个月，叔叔去世了，在他经过正式认证的遗嘱中将土地留给了侄子。侄子和女人现在都声称拥有这片土地。侄子提出将这 10 万美元退还给这位女士。谁拥有土地所有权？","choicesZh":{"A":"侄子，因为在该妇女的契约发生时，叔叔是记录的所有者。","B":"侄子，因为该女子没有进行头衔搜索。","C":"女人，因为行为禁止反悔的原则。","D":"那个女人，因为她在叔叔去世前记录了自己的行为。"},"explanationZh":"一般来说，设保人只能转让他/她拥有的财产的所有权。但是，当通过保证契约转让无主财产时，如果授予人后来由于契约禁止反言原则（即事后获得的所有权）而获得了所有权，则该转让将生效。该原则导致设保人事后获得的所有权自动转移给受让人。然后，受让人可以选择接受该转让或拒绝该转让（并起诉要求赔偿）。在这里，侄子通过保修契约以 10 万美元的价格将土地转让给该妇女。由于当时土地归叔叔（而非侄子）所有，因此转让无效。但当侄子后来根据叔叔的遗嘱获得了所有权（选择 A）时，它就生效了，所有权自动转移给了女人。由于该妇女通过声称拥有所有权来接受转让，因此她拥有该土地的所有权。 （选择 B）受让人在接受契约或主张财产所有权之前没有义务进行产权调查。因此，该妇女未搜查土地记录并不重要。 （选择 D）记录可以保护业主免受后续所有权索赔的影响。在这里，该妇女记录了叔叔去世之前以及侄子根据叔叔的遗嘱获得土地所有权之前的契约。但由于她在侄子之后获得了所有权，因此该录音并不能保护她免受他先前的所有权主张的影响。\n\n教育目标：契约禁止反言原则（即事后获得的所有权）在授予人后来获得该财产的所有权时，通过保证契约实现了该财产的转让。授予人的所有权权益自动转移给受让人，然后受让人可以接受或拒绝转让。\n\n参考文献\n23 上午。尤尔。 2d 契约 § 278 (2018)（契约禁止反言）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当设保人后来获得该财产的所有权时，契约禁止反言原则（即事后获得的所有权）通过保证契约实现了该财产的转让。授予人的所有权权益自动转移给受让人，然后受让人可以接受或拒绝转让。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁拥有土地所有权？","rule":"当设保人后来获得该财产的所有权时，契约禁止反言原则（即事后获得的所有权）通过保证契约实现了该财产的转让。授予人的所有权权益自动转移给受让人，然后受让人可以接受或拒绝转让。","analysis":"一般来说，设保人只能转让他/她拥有的财产的所有权。但是，当通过保证契约转让无主财产时，如果授予人后来由于契约禁止反言原则（即事后获得的所有权）而获得了所有权，则该转让将生效。该原则导致设保人事后获得的所有权自动转移给受让人。然后，受让人可以选择接受该转让或拒绝该转让（并起诉要求赔偿）。在这里，侄子通过保修契约以 10 万美元的价格将土地转让给该妇女。由于当时土地归叔叔（而非侄子）所有，因此转让无效。但当侄子后来根据叔叔的遗嘱获得了所有权（选择 A）时，它就生效了，所有权自动转移给了女人。由于该妇女通过声称拥有所有权来接受转让，因此她拥有该土地的所有权。 （选择 B）受让人在接受契约或主张财产所有权之前没有义务进行产权调查。因此，该妇女未搜查土地记录并不重要。 （选择 D）记录可以保护业主免受后续所有权索赔的影响。在这里，该妇女记录了叔叔去世之前以及侄子根据叔叔的遗嘱获得土地所有权之前的契约。但由于她在侄子之后获得了所有权，因此该录音并不能保护她免受他先前的所有权主张的影响。\n\n教育目标：契约禁止反言原则（即事后获得的所有权）在授予人后来获得该财产的所有权时，通过保证契约实现了该财产的转让。授予人的所有权权益自动转移给受让人，然后受让人可以接受或拒绝转让。\n\n参考文献\n23 上午。尤尔。 2d 契约 § 278 (2018)（契约禁止反言）。","conclusion":"答案 C：女人，因为行为禁止反悔的原则。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-7d3cd42273b3e713"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":65,"sourceQuestionPage":190,"sourceQuestionPages":[190],"sourceExplanationPages":[191,192],"sourceAnswerPages":[191,192],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-066","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Twenty years ago, a man who owned a 20-acre ranch agreed to sell all of his mineral rights to his neighbor. The man executed a warranty deed conveying the mineral estate to the neighbor, who failed to record the deed. The following year, a woman moved her mobile home onto an undeveloped five-acre portion of the man's ranch. After the woman had lived on the property for 10 years, a local drilling company began operations on a nearby tract to drill a natural gas well. Believing that the woman owned the property, the drilling company approached the woman about leasing the mineral rights on her property and requested that the woman sign a lease of her mineral rights. The woman signed the lease as requested, and it was promptly and properly recorded. The drilling operations were successful, and the drilling company prepared to distribute profits from royalties. However, a dispute arose between the neighbor and the woman, as both parties claim ownership of the minerals. The period of time to acquire title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 10 years. In an action to determine title, is the court likely to award title to the mineral estate to the woman?","choices":{"A":"No, because the woman actually possessed only the surface estate that had previously been severed from the mineral estate.","B":"No, because the woman did not actually possess the mineral estate until she signed the lease of the mineral rights.","C":"Yes, because the neighbor failed to record the warranty deed conveying the mineral estate.","D":"Yes, because the woman adversely possessed both the surface estate and the mineral estate for the statutory period."},"answer":"A","explanation":"An adverse possessor can acquire title to land owned by another if his/her possession of the land is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the land and Nonpermissive – hostile and adverse to the owner. If the surface and mineral estates are owned by the same party, then the adverse possessor will acquire title to both estates—even if only one estate is actually possessed. But if the mineral estate has been severed from the surface estate (ie, the surface and mineral estates are owned by different parties), then the adverse possessor will only acquire title to the estate that is actually possessed. The mineral estate is actually possessed when the adverse possessor mines or drills wells on the land. Here, the neighbor purchased the mineral estate from the man, thereby severing the mineral estate from the surface estate. And since the woman merely lived on the property for the 10-year statutory period—she did not attempt to mine or drill a well on the mineral estate—she actually possessed only the surface estate during that time (Choice D). This means that the woman did not adversely possess the mineral estate, and the court is not likely to award her title to that estate. (Choice B) Adverse possession of a mineral estate requires the commencement of drilling or mining operations. Merely signing a lease of the mineral rights is not enough. (Choice C) A deed need not be recorded to be valid, so the neighbor's failure to record has no impact on whether the woman adversely possessed the mineral estate.\n\nEducational objective: If a mineral estate has previously been severed from the surface estate (ie, surface and minerals owned by different persons), then an adverse possessor can only acquire title to the mineral estate by actually possessing the minerals (eg, by mining or drilling wells).","educationalObjective":"If a mineral estate has previously been severed from the surface estate (ie, surface and minerals owned by different persons), then an adverse possessor can only acquire title to the mineral estate by actually possessing the minerals (eg, by mining or drilling wells).","references":"","rule":"If a mineral estate has previously been severed from the surface estate (ie, surface and minerals owned by different persons), then an adverse possessor can only acquire title to the mineral estate by actually possessing the minerals (eg, by mining or drilling wells).","irac":{"issue":"In an action to determine title, is the court likely to award title to the mineral estate to the woman?","rule":"If a mineral estate has previously been severed from the surface estate (ie, surface and minerals owned by different persons), then an adverse possessor can only acquire title to the mineral estate by actually possessing the minerals (eg, by mining or drilling wells).","analysis":"An adverse possessor can acquire title to land owned by another if his/her possession of the land is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the land and Nonpermissive – hostile and adverse to the owner. If the surface and mineral estates are owned by the same party, then the adverse possessor will acquire title to both estates—even if only one estate is actually possessed. But if the mineral estate has been severed from the surface estate (ie, the surface and mineral estates are owned by different parties), then the adverse possessor will only acquire title to the estate that is actually possessed. The mineral estate is actually possessed when the adverse possessor mines or drills wells on the land. Here, the neighbor purchased the mineral estate from the man, thereby severing the mineral estate from the surface estate. And since the woman merely lived on the property for the 10-year statutory period—she did not attempt to mine or drill a well on the mineral estate—she actually possessed only the surface estate during that time (Choice D). This means that the woman did not adversely possess the mineral estate, and the court is not likely to award her title to that estate. (Choice B) Adverse possession of a mineral estate requires the commencement of drilling or mining operations. Merely signing a lease of the mineral rights is not enough. (Choice C) A deed need not be recorded to be valid, so the neighbor's failure to record has no impact on whether the woman adversely possessed the mineral estate.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the woman actually possessed only the surface estate that had previously been severed from the mineral estate."},"questionZh":"二十年前，一名拥有 20 英亩牧场的男子同意将其所有采矿权出售给邻居。该男子签署了一份保修契约，将矿产转让给邻居，但邻居没有记录该契约。第二年，一名妇女将她的活动房屋搬到了该男子牧场的一块未开发的五英亩土地上。这名妇女在该房产上生活了 10 年后，当地一家钻井公司开始在附近的一块土地上钻探天然气井。钻探公司认为该妇女拥有该房产，因此就租赁其房产的采矿权事宜与该妇女接洽，并要求该妇女签署一份采矿权租赁协议。该妇女按照要求签署了租约，并及时、正确地记录下来。钻探作业取得了成功，钻探公司准备分配特许权使用费利润。然而，邻居和女子之间发生了争执，因为双方都声称对这些矿产拥有所有权。在管辖区内通过逆权占有取得所有权的期限为10年。在确定所有权的诉讼中，法院是否可能将矿产所有权授予该妇女？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为这个女人实际上只拥有先前与矿产区分离的地表产区。","B":"不，因为在签署矿权租约之前，该妇女并不真正拥有矿产。","C":"是的，因为邻居没有记录转让矿产的保修契约。","D":"是的，因为该妇女在法定期限内对地表财产和矿产财产享有逆权占有（adverse possession）。"},"explanationZh":"如果逆权占有者对土地的占有符合以下条件，则他/她可以获得他人拥有的土地的所有权： 公开且臭名昭著 - 对于合理的所有者来说明显或可见 连续 - 在法定期限内不间断 排他性 - 不与所有者共享 实际 - 在土地上实际存在和 不允许 - 对所有者怀有敌意和不利。如果地表资产和矿产资产由同一方所有，那么逆权所有人将获得两块资产的所有权——即使实际上只拥有一处资产。但如果矿产已与地表矿产分离（即地表矿产和矿产矿产由不同方所有），则逆权占有人将仅获得实际占有的矿产的所有权。当逆权占有（adverse possession）者在土地上采矿或打井时，矿产实际上被占有。在这里，邻居从该男子手中购买了矿产区，从而将矿产区与地表产区割裂开来。由于该妇女仅在 10 年法定期限内居住在该财产上（她没有尝试在矿产区采矿或打井），因此在此期间她实际上只拥有地表财产（选项 D）。这意味着该妇女没有不利地占有该矿产，法院不太可能授予她对该矿产的所有权。 （选择 B） 逆权占有矿产需要开始钻探或采矿作业。仅仅签署矿权租赁合同是不够的。 （选项C） 契约无需记录即可有效，因此邻居未记录对妇女是否对矿产拥有逆权占有（adverse possession）没有影响。\n\n教育目标：如果矿产资源先前已与地表矿产（即不同人拥有的地表和矿物）分离，则逆权占有者只能通过实际占有矿物（例如，通过采矿或钻井）来获得矿产资源的所有权。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果矿产资源先前已与地表矿产（即不同人拥有的地表和矿物）分离，则逆权占有人只能通过实际占有矿物（例如，通过采矿或钻井）来获得矿产资源的所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"在确定所有权的诉讼中，法院是否可能将矿产所有权授予该妇女？","rule":"如果矿产资源先前已与地表矿产（即不同人拥有的地表和矿物）分离，则逆权占有人只能通过实际占有矿物（例如，通过采矿或钻井）来获得矿产资源的所有权。","analysis":"如果逆权占有者对土地的占有符合以下条件，则他/她可以获得他人拥有的土地的所有权： 公开且臭名昭著 - 对于合理的所有者来说明显或可见 连续 - 在法定期限内不间断 排他性 - 不与所有者共享 实际 - 在土地上实际存在和 不允许 - 对所有者怀有敌意和不利。如果地表资产和矿产资产由同一方所有，那么逆权所有人将获得两块资产的所有权——即使实际上只拥有一处资产。但如果矿产已与地表矿产分离（即地表矿产和矿产矿产由不同方所有），则逆权占有人将仅获得实际占有的矿产的所有权。当逆权占有（adverse possession）者在土地上采矿或打井时，矿产实际上被占有。在这里，邻居从该男子手中购买了矿产区，从而将矿产区与地表产区割裂开来。由于该妇女仅在 10 年法定期限内居住在该财产上（她没有尝试在矿产区采矿或打井），因此在此期间她实际上只拥有地表财产（选项 D）。这意味着该妇女没有不利地占有该矿产，法院不太可能授予她对该矿产的所有权。 （选择 B） 逆权占有矿产需要开始钻探或采矿作业。仅仅签署矿权租赁合同是不够的。 （选项C） 契约无需记录即可有效，因此邻居未记录对妇女是否对矿产拥有逆权占有（adverse possession）没有影响。\n\n教育目标：如果矿产资源先前已与地表矿产（即不同人拥有的地表和矿物）分离，则逆权占有者只能通过实际占有矿物（例如，通过采矿或钻井）来获得矿产资源的所有权。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为这个女人实际上只拥有先前与矿产区分离的地表产区。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"severing","ipa":"/sˈɛvɝɪŋ/","syllables":"sev·er·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 切断, 断绝( sever的现在分词 )； 断, 裂","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“severing”","wordForm":"词；词形 0:sever/1:i","sourceSentence":"Here, the neighbor purchased the mineral estate from the man, thereby severing the mineral estate from the surface estate.","synonyms":["severance"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-70d815fbaed16e16"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":66,"sourceQuestionPage":193,"sourceQuestionPages":[193],"sourceExplanationPages":[194,195],"sourceAnswerPages":[194,195],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-067","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Adverse Possession","question":"A man owned a tract of undeveloped land. The man's land abuts a tract of land owned by the state's governmental energy agency. The agency has operated a waste-to-electricity recycling facility on its land for 12 years. Both the man's land and the agency's land are in a remote area, and the agency's land is the only developed parcel of real estate within a 10- mile radius. The boundary line between the man's land and the agency's land had never been surveyed or marked on the face of the earth. During the past 12 years, some of the trucks bringing waste to the agency facility have dumped their loads so that the piles of waste extend onto a portion of the man's land. However, prior to the four-week period during each calendar year when the agency facility is closed for inspection and repairs, the waste piles are reduced to minimal levels so that during each of the four-week closures no waste was, in fact, piled on the man's land. Neither the man nor any representative of the agency knew the facts about the relation of the boundary line to the waste piles. The time for acquiring title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 10 years. Last year, the man died, and his son succeeded him as the owner of the man's land. The son became aware of the facts, demanded that the agency stop using the man's land for the piling of waste, and, when the agency refused his demand, brought an appropriate action to enjoin any such use of the man's land in the future. If the agency prevails in that action, what is the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The facts constitute adverse possession, and title to the portion of the man's land concerned has vested in the agency.","B":"The interest of the public in the conversion of waste to energy overrides the son's entitlement to equitable remedies.","C":"The man's failure to keep himself informed as to the agency's use of his land and his failure to object constituted implied consent to the continuation of that use.","D":"The power of eminent domain of the state makes the son's claim moot."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A person may acquire title to another's land by adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – without substantial interruption for the statutory period (daily/weekly use is not required) Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the portion of the land to be adversely possessed Nonpermissive – hostile and adverse to the owner Here, the agency openly and continuously dumped waste on a portion of the man's land for more than 10 years (the statutory period) without his participation or permission. The four-week period each year during which waste was not being dumped did not invalidate the agency's continuous use of the land. Therefore, title to that portion of the man's land has likely vested in the agency by adverse possession. (Choice B) Although converting waste to energy has social value, the agency could have reasonably achieved that interest without infringing on the son's ownership rights (eg, by dumping waste on other land in the remote area). As a result, the utility of the agency's activity likely does not override the son's entitlement to equitable remedies. (Choice C) The man's failure to notice or object to the agency's use of his land did not constitute implied consent to the continuation of that use. Instead, his failure to notice or object within the 10-year statutory period allowed the agency to acquire title to a portion of his land through adverse possession. (Choice D) Eminent domain prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. Since there is no evidence that the governmental agency compensated the man or his son for its use of the land, there is a live controversy (ie, the son's claim is not moot).\n\nEducational objective: Adverse possession allows one to acquire title to any portion of another's land that he/she possesses in an open and notorious, continuous, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive manner (OCEAN).","educationalObjective":"Adverse possession allows one to acquire title to any portion of another's land that he/she possesses in an open and notorious, continuous, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive manner (OCEAN).","references":"","rule":"Adverse possession allows one to acquire title to any portion of another's land that he/she possesses in an open and notorious, continuous, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive manner (OCEAN).","irac":{"issue":"If the agency prevails in that action, what is the most likely reason?","rule":"Adverse possession allows one to acquire title to any portion of another's land that he/she possesses in an open and notorious, continuous, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive manner (OCEAN).","analysis":"A person may acquire title to another's land by adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – without substantial interruption for the statutory period (daily/weekly use is not required) Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the portion of the land to be adversely possessed Nonpermissive – hostile and adverse to the owner Here, the agency openly and continuously dumped waste on a portion of the man's land for more than 10 years (the statutory period) without his participation or permission. The four-week period each year during which waste was not being dumped did not invalidate the agency's continuous use of the land. Therefore, title to that portion of the man's land has likely vested in the agency by adverse possession. (Choice B) Although converting waste to energy has social value, the agency could have reasonably achieved that interest without infringing on the son's ownership rights (eg, by dumping waste on other land in the remote area). As a result, the utility of the agency's activity likely does not override the son's entitlement to equitable remedies. (Choice C) The man's failure to notice or object to the agency's use of his land did not constitute implied consent to the continuation of that use. Instead, his failure to notice or object within the 10-year statutory period allowed the agency to acquire title to a portion of his land through adverse possession. (Choice D) Eminent domain prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. Since there is no evidence that the governmental agency compensated the man or his son for its use of the land, there is a live controversy (ie, the son's claim is not moot).","conclusion":"Answer A: The facts constitute adverse possession, and title to the portion of the man's land concerned has vested in the agency."},"questionZh":"一个人拥有一块未开发的土地。该男子的土地毗邻该州政府能源机构拥有的一块土地。该机构已在其土地上运营废物发电回收设施 12 年。该男子的土地和该机构的土地都位于偏远地区，而该机构的土地是方圆10英里内唯一已开发的房地产地块。该人的土地与该机构的土地之间的边界线从未被测量过或在地球表面上标记。在过去的 12 年里，一些向该机构设施运送垃圾的卡车倾倒了货物，使得垃圾堆延伸到了该男子的部分土地上。然而，在每年该机构设施关闭进行检查和维修的四个星期之前，废物堆已减少到最低水平，因此在每四个星期的关闭期间，实际上没有废物堆积在该人的土地上。该男子和该机构的任何代表都不知道边界线与废物堆的关系的事实。在管辖区内通过逆权占有取得所有权的期限为10年。去年，这个人去世了，他的儿子继承了他的土地，成为了他的土地的所有者。儿子了解了事实，要求该机构停止使用该男子的土地来堆放废物，当该机构拒绝他的要求时，他采取了适当的行动，禁止今后再使用该男子的土地。如果该机构在该行动中获胜，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该事实构成逆权占有，该男子的部分土地的所有权已归属该机构。","B":"公众对将废物转化为能源的兴趣凌驾于儿子获得公平补救措施的权利之上。","C":"该男子未能及时告知该机构对其土地的使用情况，并且他没有提出反对，这构成了对继续使用该土地的默示同意。","D":"国家征用权使儿子的主张毫无意义。"},"explanationZh":"一个人可以通过逆权侵占获得他人土地的所有权，如果他/她对土地的占有是： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者来说是显而易见的或可见的 连续 – 在法定期限内没有实质性中断（不需要每天/每周使用） 排他性 – 不与所有者共享 实际 – 实际存在于将被逆权侵占的土地部分 不允许 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 这里，该机构公开且持续地将废物倾倒在该人的一部分土地上超过10年（法定期限），未经他的参与或许可。每年有四个星期不倾倒废物的时间并不会使该机构对土地的连续使用无效。因此，该男子那部分土地的所有权很可能通过逆权占有而归属于该机构。 （选择B）虽然将废物转化为能源具有社会价值，但该机构可以在不侵犯儿子所有权的情况下合理地实现该利益（例如，通过将废物倾倒在偏远地区的其他土地上）。因此，该机构活动的效用可能不会凌驾于儿子获得公平补救措施的权利之上。 （选项 C） 该男子未注意到或反对该机构使用其土地并不构成默示同意继续使用该土地。相反，他未能在 10 年法定期限内发出通知或提出反对，从而使该机构通过逆权管有获得了他部分土地的所有权。 （选项D）征用权禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下将私有财产用于公共用途。由于没有证据表明政府机构因使用土地而对该男子或其儿子进行了补偿，因此存在着激烈的争议（即儿子的主张没有实际意义）。\n\n教育目标：逆权占有允许一个人以公开的、臭名昭著的、连续的、排他的、实际的和非许可的方式（OCEAN）获得他/她拥有的另一人土地的任何部分的所有权。","educationalObjectiveZh":"逆权占有允许一个人以公开的、臭名昭著的、连续的、排他的、实际的和非许可的方式（OCEAN）获得他/她拥有的另一人土地的任何部分的所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该机构在该行动中获胜，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"逆权占有允许一个人以公开的、臭名昭著的、连续的、排他的、实际的和非许可的方式（OCEAN）获得他/她拥有的另一人土地的任何部分的所有权。","analysis":"一个人可以通过逆权侵占获得他人土地的所有权，如果他/她对土地的占有是： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者来说是显而易见的或可见的 连续 – 在法定期限内没有实质性中断（不需要每天/每周使用） 排他性 – 不与所有者共享 实际 – 实际存在于将被逆权侵占的土地部分 不允许 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 这里，该机构公开且持续地将废物倾倒在该人的一部分土地上超过10年（法定期限），未经他的参与或许可。每年有四个星期不倾倒废物的时间并不会使该机构对土地的连续使用无效。因此，该男子那部分土地的所有权很可能通过逆权占有而归属于该机构。 （选择B）虽然将废物转化为能源具有社会价值，但该机构可以在不侵犯儿子所有权的情况下合理地实现该利益（例如，通过将废物倾倒在偏远地区的其他土地上）。因此，该机构活动的效用可能不会凌驾于儿子获得公平补救措施的权利之上。 （选项 C） 该男子未注意到或反对该机构使用其土地并不构成默示同意继续使用该土地。相反，他未能在 10 年法定期限内发出通知或提出反对，从而使该机构通过逆权管有获得了他部分土地的所有权。 （选项D）征用权禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下将私有财产用于公共用途。由于没有证据表明政府机构因使用土地而对该男子或其儿子进行了补偿，因此存在着激烈的争议（即儿子的主张没有实际意义）。\n\n教育目标：逆权占有允许一个人以公开的、臭名昭著的、连续的、排他的、实际的和非许可的方式（OCEAN）获得他/她拥有的另一人土地的任何部分的所有权。","conclusion":"答案 A：该事实构成逆权占有，该男子的部分土地的所有权已归属该机构。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-d7ec862013cd68bc"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":67,"sourceQuestionPage":196,"sourceQuestionPages":[196],"sourceExplanationPages":[197,198],"sourceAnswerPages":[197,198],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-068","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man conveyed a large tract of land to a buyer by a warranty deed. The buyer recorded the deed four days later. After the conveyance but prior to the buyer's recording of the deed, a creditor properly filed a judgment against the man. A statute in the jurisdiction provides: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for ten years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.\" The recording act has no provision for a grace period. The creditor joined both the man and the buyer in an appropriate action to foreclose her judgment lien against the land. If the creditor is unsuccessful, what will be the reason?","choices":{"A":"Any deed is superior to a judgment lien.","B":"Four days is not an unreasonable delay in recording a deed.","C":"The creditor is not a purchaser for value.","D":"The man's warranty of title to the buyer defeats the creditor's claim."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Payment of value\n\n(bona fide purchaser)\n\nValue Release of existing debt Substantial amount not grossly inadequate in relation to property interest's value No Security for existing debt value Nominal payment (eg, $1) Recital of consideration in deed Gift, devise, or inheritance Recording acts are used to evaluate the priority of competing property interests. A notice act (as seen here) protects only subsequent purchasers for value who lack notice of prior interests in land. Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for a preexisting debt (ie, no new value has been paid). Therefore, judgment creditors are not protected under the notice act,* and the creditor here will be unsuccessful in her foreclosure action. *However, some recording acts now expressly protect judgment creditors by their terms. (Choice A) It is untrue that any deed is superior to a judgment lien. Had the creditor's judgment lien been filed before the man conveyed the land to the buyer, it would have been superior to the buyer's deed. (Choice B) When, as here, a recording act does not have a grace period, a purchaser must record immediately to prevent a subsequent purchaser from obtaining a superior interest in the property. Whether a delay in recording is reasonable is not taken into consideration. (Choice D) A warranty deed does not defeat a competing interest in land, such as that held by a judgment creditor. Instead, a warranty deed merely allows a grantee to sue the grantor in the event that the competing interest results in a grantor's breach of a warranty under the deed.\n\nEducational objective: Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for a preexisting debt—not payment of value.","educationalObjective":"Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for a preexisting debt—not payment of value.","references":"","rule":"Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for a preexisting debt—not payment of value.","irac":{"issue":"If the creditor is unsuccessful, what will be the reason?","rule":"Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for a preexisting debt—not payment of value.","analysis":"Payment of value\n\n(bona fide purchaser)\n\nValue Release of existing debt Substantial amount not grossly inadequate in relation to property interest's value No Security for existing debt value Nominal payment (eg, $1) Recital of consideration in deed Gift, devise, or inheritance Recording acts are used to evaluate the priority of competing property interests. A notice act (as seen here) protects only subsequent purchasers for value who lack notice of prior interests in land. Judgment creditors are not purchasers for value since the attachment of a judgment lien to a debtor's property is merely security for a preexisting debt (ie, no new value has been paid). Therefore, judgment creditors are not protected under the notice act,* and the creditor here will be unsuccessful in her foreclosure action. *However, some recording acts now expressly protect judgment creditors by their terms. (Choice A) It is untrue that any deed is superior to a judgment lien. Had the creditor's judgment lien been filed before the man conveyed the land to the buyer, it would have been superior to the buyer's deed. (Choice B) When, as here, a recording act does not have a grace period, a purchaser must record immediately to prevent a subsequent purchaser from obtaining a superior interest in the property. Whether a delay in recording is reasonable is not taken into consideration. (Choice D) A warranty deed does not defeat a competing interest in land, such as that held by a judgment creditor. Instead, a warranty deed merely allows a grantee to sue the grantor in the event that the competing interest results in a grantor's breach of a warranty under the deed.","conclusion":"Answer C: The creditor is not a purchaser for value."},"questionZh":"一名男子通过保修契约将一大片土地转让给买家。四天后，买家记录了该契约。在转让之后但在买方记录契约之前，债权人对该男子做出了适当的判决。该司法管辖区的一项法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起十年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”该司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“除非依法记录，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者。”录音法没有规定宽限期。债权人与该人和买方一起采取了适当的行动，取消了她对土地的判决留置权。如果债权人不成功，原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"任何行为均优于判决留置权。","B":"四天的记录延迟并不是无理的。","C":"债权人不是价值购买者。","D":"该男子对买方的所有权保证推翻了债权人的主张。"},"explanationZh":"支付价值\n\n（善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser））\n\n价值 现有债务的免除 相对于财产权益的价值而言，大额金额并非严重不足 现有债务价值无担保 名义付款（例如，1 美元） 契约中的对价记录 礼物、设计或继承 记录行为用于评估竞争性财产权益的优先顺序。通知法（如此处所示）仅保护后续购买者的价值，这些购买者缺乏对先前土地权益的通知。判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是先前存在的债务的担保（即没有支付新的价值）。因此，判决债权人不受通知法的保护，*并且这里的债权人的止赎诉讼将不会成功。 *然而，一些记录法案现在通过其条款明确保护判决债权人。 （选项 A） 任何行为都优于判决留置权的说法是不正确的。如果债权人的判决留置权是在该人将土地转让给买方之前提出的，那么它会优于买方的契约。 （选择B）当记录行为没有宽限期时（如此处所示），购买者必须立即记录，以防止后续购买者获得该房产的优先权益。不考虑录制延迟是否合理。 （选项 D）保证契约不会击败土地上的竞争利益，例如判定债权人持有的土地利益。相反，保证契约仅允许受让人在竞争利益导致授予人违反契约下的保证的情况下起诉授予人。\n\n教育目标：判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是先前存在的债务的担保，而不是价值的支付。","educationalObjectiveZh":"判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是先前存在的债务的担保，而不是价值的支付。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果债权人不成功，原因是什么？","rule":"判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是先前存在的债务的担保，而不是价值的支付。","analysis":"支付价值\n\n（善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser））\n\n价值 现有债务的免除 相对于财产权益的价值而言，大额金额并非严重不足 现有债务价值无担保 名义付款（例如，1 美元） 契约中的对价记录 礼物、设计或继承 记录行为用于评估竞争性财产权益的优先顺序。通知法（如此处所示）仅保护后续购买者的价值，这些购买者缺乏对先前土地权益的通知。判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是先前存在的债务的担保（即没有支付新的价值）。因此，判决债权人不受通知法的保护，*并且这里的债权人的止赎诉讼将不会成功。 *然而，一些记录法案现在通过其条款明确保护判决债权人。 （选项 A） 任何行为都优于判决留置权的说法是不正确的。如果债权人的判决留置权是在该人将土地转让给买方之前提出的，那么它会优于买方的契约。 （选择B）当记录行为没有宽限期时（如此处所示），购买者必须立即记录，以防止后续购买者获得该房产的优先权益。不考虑录制延迟是否合理。 （选项 D）保证契约不会击败土地上的竞争利益，例如判定债权人持有的土地利益。相反，保证契约仅允许受让人在竞争利益导致授予人违反契约下的保证的情况下起诉授予人。\n\n教育目标：判决债权人不是价值购买者，因为判决留置权对债务人财产的附加仅仅是先前存在的债务的担保，而不是价值的支付。","conclusion":"答案 C：债权人不是价值购买者。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"warranty of title","ipa":"/wˈɔɹənti ˈəv tˈaɪtəl/","syllables":"war·ranty of ti·tle","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"权利担保：卖方保证所有权良好且转让合法","contextualMeaning":"权利担保：卖方保证所有权良好且转让合法","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The man's warranty of title to the buyer defeats the creditor's claim.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":68,"sourceQuestionPage":199,"sourceQuestionPages":[199],"sourceExplanationPages":[200,201],"sourceAnswerPages":[200,201],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-069","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"An aunt owned a large parcel of vacant land in fee simple and executed a deed purporting to convey the land to her nephew. She told her nephew, who was then 19, about the deed and said that she would give it to him when he reached 21 and had received his undergraduate college degree. Shortly afterward the nephew searched his aunt's desk, found and removed the deed, and recorded it. A month later, the nephew executed an instrument in the proper form of a warranty deed purporting to convey the land to his fiancée. He delivered the deed to his fiancée, pointing out that the deed recited that it was given in exchange for \"$1 and other good and valuable consideration,\" and that to make it valid his fiancée must pay him $1. The fiancée, impressed and grateful, did so. Together, they went to the recording office and recorded the deed. The fiancée assumed that the nephew had owned the land and knew nothing about the nephew's dealing with his aunt. Neither the aunt's deed to the nephew nor the nephew's deed to the fiancée said anything about any conditions. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.\" Two years passed. The nephew turned 21, then graduated from college. At the graduation party, the aunt was chatting with the fiancée and for the first time learned the foregoing facts. The age of majority in the jurisdiction is 18 years. If the aunt brings an appropriate action against the fiancée to quiet title to the land, who will prevail?","choices":{"A":"The aunt, because her deed to the nephew was not delivered.","B":"The aunt, because her nephew's deed to his fiancée before the nephew satisfied the aunt's conditions was void, as the fiancée had paid only nominal consideration.","C":"The fiancée, because the deed to her was recorded.","D":"The fiancée, because the nephew has satisfied the aunt's oral conditions."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A deed is a legal instrument that transfers ownership of real property from the grantor (old owner) to a grantee (new owner) once it has been: delivered – evidenced by the grantor's intent to presently convey ownership to the grantee (ie, conditional deliveries are ineffective) and accepted – presumed when the transfer is beneficial to the grantee. Delivery is presumed if the deed is recorded (ie, filed in official land records). But this presumption can be rebutted by evidence that the grantor did not intend to presently transfer ownership. And if a deed is not delivered (and accepted), it is void and conveys no interest—not even to a bona fide purchaser. Here, the nephew and fiancée recorded the deed (presumed delivery). But that presumption is rebuttable since the aunt had placed the deed in a drawer and told the nephew it would be his when he reached 21 and graduated (conditional delivery). Since she did not intend to presently transfer ownership to the nephew, the deed was not delivered and conveyed no interest to the nephew. Therefore, his subsequent conveyance to the fiancée was ineffective (even though she was a bona fide purchaser), and the aunt will prevail. (Choice B) The deed is void because it was never delivered. But had the aunt delivered the deed, the nephew could have conveyed his interest in the property before it became possessory—ie, before he satisfied the aunt's conditions. And the fiancée's payment of only nominal consideration ($1) would have been inconsequential since consideration is not required to convey property through a deed. (Choice C) Although the deed from the nephew to the fiancée was recorded, he had no property interest to convey to her since the aunt's prior deed had not been delivered to him. Therefore, ownership never transferred to the nephew or the fiancée. (Choice D) Although the nephew met the aunt's oral conditions (he turned 21 and graduated from college), the deed was never delivered. Therefore, the nephew had no property interest to give the fiancée.\n\nEducational objective: A deed transfers ownership of real property once it is delivered (when the grantor has an intent to presently convey ownership) and accepted (presumed when the transfer is beneficial to the grantee). An undelivered or unaccepted deed is void and conveys no property interest to the grantee.","educationalObjective":"A deed transfers ownership of real property once it is delivered (when the grantor has an intent to presently convey ownership) and accepted (presumed when the transfer is beneficial to the grantee). An undelivered or unaccepted deed is void and conveys no property interest to the grantee.","references":"","rule":"A deed transfers ownership of real property once it is delivered (when the grantor has an intent to presently convey ownership) and accepted (presumed when the transfer is beneficial to the grantee). An undelivered or unaccepted deed is void and conveys no property interest to the grantee.","irac":{"issue":"If the aunt brings an appropriate action against the fiancée to quiet title to the land, who will prevail?","rule":"A deed transfers ownership of real property once it is delivered (when the grantor has an intent to presently convey ownership) and accepted (presumed when the transfer is beneficial to the grantee). An undelivered or unaccepted deed is void and conveys no property interest to the grantee.","analysis":"A deed is a legal instrument that transfers ownership of real property from the grantor (old owner) to a grantee (new owner) once it has been: delivered – evidenced by the grantor's intent to presently convey ownership to the grantee (ie, conditional deliveries are ineffective) and accepted – presumed when the transfer is beneficial to the grantee. Delivery is presumed if the deed is recorded (ie, filed in official land records). But this presumption can be rebutted by evidence that the grantor did not intend to presently transfer ownership. And if a deed is not delivered (and accepted), it is void and conveys no interest—not even to a bona fide purchaser. Here, the nephew and fiancée recorded the deed (presumed delivery). But that presumption is rebuttable since the aunt had placed the deed in a drawer and told the nephew it would be his when he reached 21 and graduated (conditional delivery). Since she did not intend to presently transfer ownership to the nephew, the deed was not delivered and conveyed no interest to the nephew. Therefore, his subsequent conveyance to the fiancée was ineffective (even though she was a bona fide purchaser), and the aunt will prevail. (Choice B) The deed is void because it was never delivered. But had the aunt delivered the deed, the nephew could have conveyed his interest in the property before it became possessory—ie, before he satisfied the aunt's conditions. And the fiancée's payment of only nominal consideration ($1) would have been inconsequential since consideration is not required to convey property through a deed. (Choice C) Although the deed from the nephew to the fiancée was recorded, he had no property interest to convey to her since the aunt's prior deed had not been delivered to him. Therefore, ownership never transferred to the nephew or the fiancée. (Choice D) Although the nephew met the aunt's oral conditions (he turned 21 and graduated from college), the deed was never delivered. Therefore, the nephew had no property interest to give the fiancée.","conclusion":"Answer A: The aunt, because her deed to the nephew was not delivered."},"questionZh":"一位阿姨免费拥有一大块空置土地，并签署了一份契约，旨在将土地转让给她的侄子。她把这件事告诉了当时 19 岁的侄子，并表示等他 21 岁并获得本科学位时，她会把它交给他。不久之后，侄子搜查了姨妈的办公桌，找到并移走了契约，并记录下来。一个月后，侄子以担保契约的适当形式签署了一份文书，旨在将土地转让给他的未婚妻。他将契约交给了他的未婚妻，并指出契约上注明，契约是为了换取“1 美元和其他良好且有价值的对价”，为了使契约有效，他的未婚妻必须向他支付 1 美元。未婚妻既感动又感激，就这么做了。他们一起去了记录办公室并记录了该行为。未婚妻以为侄子拥有这块土地，并且对侄子与姨妈的交易一无所知。无论是姨妈给侄子的契约，还是侄子给未婚妻的契约，都没有提及任何条件。该司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“除非依法记录，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者。”两年过去了。侄子21岁了，大学毕业了。毕业晚会上，阿姨正在和未婚妻聊天，第一次得知了上述事实。该司法管辖区的法定成年年龄为 18 岁。如果阿姨对未婚妻提起适当的诉讼，以平息土地所有权，谁会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"姨妈，因为她给侄子的房产证没有交付。","B":"姨妈，因为在侄子满足姨妈的条件之前，她的侄子与未婚妻的契约无效，因为未婚妻只支付了名义上的对价。","C":"未婚妻，因为她的契约被记录下来了。","D":"未婚妻，因为侄子已经满足了阿姨的口头条件。"},"explanationZh":"契约是一种法律文书，在以下情况下将不动产所有权从设保人（旧业主）转移至受让人（新业主）： 交付 – 以设保人目前将所有权转让给受让人的意图为证（即，有条件交付无效）并接受 – 假定转让对受让人有利。如果契约被记录（即在官方土地记录中归档），则推定已交付。但这一推定可以通过设保人目前不打算转让所有权的证据来反驳。如果契约没有交付（并被接受），那么它就是无效的，并且不会产生任何利益——即使对于真正的购买者来说也是如此。在这里，侄子和未婚妻记录了契约（推定交付）。但这一推定是可以反驳的，因为姨妈已将房产契放在抽屉里，并告诉侄子，等他 21 岁毕业（有条件交付）时，房产证将归他所有。由于她目前不打算将所有权转让给侄子，因此契约没有交付，也没有给侄子带来任何利益。因此，他后来向未婚妻的转让是无效的（尽管她是善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）），姑姑获胜。 （选择B）该契约无效，因为它从未交付。但是，如果阿姨交付了契约，侄子就可以在该财产成为占有之前，即在他满足阿姨的条件之前，转让他对该财产的兴趣。而且未婚妻只支付象征性的对价（1 美元）是无关紧要的，因为通过契约转让财产不需要对价。 （选项C） 虽然侄子给未婚妻的房产证已被记录，但由于姨妈之前的房产证尚未交付给他，因此他没有财产权益可转让给她。因此，所有权从未转移给侄子或未婚妻。 （选项D）虽然侄子符合阿姨的口头条件（年满21岁，大学毕业），但房产契始终没有交付。因此，侄子没有财产权益可以给予未婚妻。\n\n教育目标：一旦交付（当授予人有意立即转让所有权）并被接受（假定转让对受让人有利时），契约即转移不动产的所有权。未交付或未接受的契约是无效的，并且不会向受让人转让任何财产权益。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一旦交付（当设保人有意立即转让所有权）并被接受（假定转让对受让人有利时），契约即转移不动产的所有权。未交付或未接受的契约是无效的，并且不会向受让人转让任何财产权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果阿姨对未婚妻提起适当的诉讼，以平息土地所有权，谁会获胜？","rule":"一旦交付（当设保人有意立即转让所有权）并被接受（假定转让对受让人有利时），契约即转移不动产的所有权。未交付或未接受的契约是无效的，并且不会向受让人转让任何财产权益。","analysis":"契约是一种法律文书，在以下情况下将不动产所有权从设保人（旧业主）转移至受让人（新业主）： 交付 – 以设保人目前将所有权转让给受让人的意图为证（即，有条件交付无效）并接受 – 假定转让对受让人有利。如果契约被记录（即在官方土地记录中归档），则推定已交付。但这一推定可以通过设保人目前不打算转让所有权的证据来反驳。如果契约没有交付（并被接受），那么它就是无效的，并且不会产生任何利益——即使对于真正的购买者来说也是如此。在这里，侄子和未婚妻记录了契约（推定交付）。但这一推定是可以反驳的，因为姨妈已将房产契放在抽屉里，并告诉侄子，等他 21 岁毕业（有条件交付）时，房产证将归他所有。由于她目前不打算将所有权转让给侄子，因此契约没有交付，也没有给侄子带来任何利益。因此，他后来向未婚妻的转让是无效的（尽管她是善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）），姑姑获胜。 （选择B）该契约无效，因为它从未交付。但是，如果阿姨交付了契约，侄子就可以在该财产成为占有之前，即在他满足阿姨的条件之前，转让他对该财产的兴趣。而且未婚妻只支付象征性的对价（1 美元）是无关紧要的，因为通过契约转让财产不需要对价。 （选项C） 虽然侄子给未婚妻的房产证已被记录，但由于姨妈之前的房产证尚未交付给他，因此他没有财产权益可转让给她。因此，所有权从未转移给侄子或未婚妻。 （选项D）虽然侄子符合阿姨的口头条件（年满21岁，大学毕业），但房产契始终没有交付。因此，侄子没有财产权益可以给予未婚妻。\n\n教育目标：一旦交付（当授予人有意立即转让所有权）并被接受（假定转让对受让人有利时），契约即转移不动产的所有权。未交付或未接受的契约是无效的，并且不会向受让人转让任何财产权益。","conclusion":"答案 A：姨妈，因为她给侄子的房产证没有交付。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-9b7226734130ccde"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":69,"sourceQuestionPage":202,"sourceQuestionPages":[202],"sourceExplanationPages":[203,204],"sourceAnswerPages":[203,204],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-070","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman borrowed $500,000 from a bank. She signed a note to the bank and secured repayment of the loan with a mortgage on a commercial tract that she owned. The note and mortgage did not contain a due-on-sale clause. Several months later, the woman conveyed the commercial tract to her business partner \"subject to a mortgage to the bank, which the grantee assumes and agrees to pay.\" The business partner then conveyed the tract to a buyer \"subject to an existing mortgage to the bank.\" Each grantee received a copy of the note and the mortgage. After the buyer made several timely payments, no further payments were made by any party. The bank initiated a foreclosure action, naming the woman, the business partner, and the buyer as defendants. The foreclosure sale resulted in a deficiency. There are no applicable statutes. Against whom is the bank entitled to collect a deficiency judgment?","choices":{"A":"The business partner and the buyer.","B":"The woman.","C":"The woman and the business partner.","D":"The woman, the business partner, and the buyer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A debtor is free to sell mortgaged property unless the mortgage agreement states otherwise. After the sale, the mortgage remains attached to the property and the debtor remains personally liable for the debt secured by the mortgage. But the buyer's obligations with respect to the mortgage depend on whether he/she: took the property subject to the mortgage – in which case the buyer does not agree to pay, and is not personally liable for, the debt (even if the buyer makes payments on the loan) or\n\nassumed the mortgage – in which case the buyer expressly agrees to pay and becomes primarily liable for the debt, while the debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety.\n\nTherefore, if a creditor forecloses on mortgaged property and the sale results in a deficiency—ie, the proceeds are insufficient to satisfy the debt—the creditor can obtain a deficiency judgment against the original debtor and/or any party who has assumed the mortgage. Here, the woman mortgaged the commercial tract to a bank and then conveyed the tract to her business partner, who assumed the mortgage. The business partner then conveyed the tract to the buyer, who took it subject to the mortgage. Although the buyer made payments on the loan, this did not constitute an assumption of liability for the debt. As a result, the bank is entitled to collect a deficiency judgment only against the woman and the business partner (Choices A, B & D).\n\nEducational objective: When a foreclosure sale results in a deficiency, the creditor can obtain a deficiency judgment against (1) the original debtor and/or (2) any party who has assumed the mortgage.\n\nReferences\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 967 (2019) (deficiency judgments).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (assuming a mortgage debt).","educationalObjective":"When a foreclosure sale results in a deficiency, the creditor can obtain a deficiency judgment against (1) the original debtor and/or (2) any party who has assumed the mortgage.","references":"55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 967 (2019) (deficiency judgments).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (assuming a mortgage debt).","rule":"When a foreclosure sale results in a deficiency, the creditor can obtain a deficiency judgment against (1) the original debtor and/or (2) any party who has assumed the mortgage.","irac":{"issue":"Against whom is the bank entitled to collect a deficiency judgment?","rule":"When a foreclosure sale results in a deficiency, the creditor can obtain a deficiency judgment against (1) the original debtor and/or (2) any party who has assumed the mortgage.","analysis":"A debtor is free to sell mortgaged property unless the mortgage agreement states otherwise. After the sale, the mortgage remains attached to the property and the debtor remains personally liable for the debt secured by the mortgage. But the buyer's obligations with respect to the mortgage depend on whether he/she: took the property subject to the mortgage – in which case the buyer does not agree to pay, and is not personally liable for, the debt (even if the buyer makes payments on the loan) or\n\nassumed the mortgage – in which case the buyer expressly agrees to pay and becomes primarily liable for the debt, while the debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety.\n\nTherefore, if a creditor forecloses on mortgaged property and the sale results in a deficiency—ie, the proceeds are insufficient to satisfy the debt—the creditor can obtain a deficiency judgment against the original debtor and/or any party who has assumed the mortgage. Here, the woman mortgaged the commercial tract to a bank and then conveyed the tract to her business partner, who assumed the mortgage. The business partner then conveyed the tract to the buyer, who took it subject to the mortgage. Although the buyer made payments on the loan, this did not constitute an assumption of liability for the debt. As a result, the bank is entitled to collect a deficiency judgment only against the woman and the business partner (Choices A, B & D).","conclusion":"Answer C: The woman and the business partner."},"questionZh":"一名妇女向银行借了 50 万美元。她向银行签署了一张票据，并以她拥有的商业土地为抵押担保偿还贷款。该票据和抵押贷款不包含到期出售条款。几个月后，该妇女将商业单据转让给她的商业伙伴，“但须向银行提供抵押贷款，受让人承担并同意支付这笔抵押贷款”。然后，商业伙伴将这张单张转交给买家，“但须遵守银行现有的抵押贷款”。每个受赠者都会收到票据和抵押贷款的副本。买方多次按时付款后，任何一方均未再支付任何款项。该银行发起了取消抵押品赎回权的诉讼，将这名女子、商业伙伴和买家列为被告。取消抵押品赎回权的出售导致了缺陷。没有适用的法规。银行有权向谁收取缺陷判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"商业伙伴和买家。","B":"那个女人。","C":"女人和商业伙伴。","D":"女人、商业伙伴和买家。"},"explanationZh":"除非抵押协议另有规定，债务人可以自由出售抵押财产。出售后，抵押贷款仍依附于该财产，债务人仍对抵押贷款担保的债务承担个人责任。但买方对抵押贷款的义务取决于他/她是否： 取得了抵押贷款 – 在这种情况下，买方不同意支付债务，且不承担个人责任（即使买方支付了贷款）或\n\n承担抵押贷款——在这种情况下，买方明确同意付款并承担债务的主要责任，而债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。\n\n因此，如果债权人取消抵押财产的赎回权，并且出售导致短缺（即收益不足以清偿债务），则债权人可以获得针对原债务人和/或承担抵押的任何一方的短缺判决。在这里，这位女士将商业票据抵押给银行，然后将商业票据转交给她的商业伙伴，后者承担了抵押贷款。然后，商业伙伴将单张转交给买方，买方将其作为抵押贷款。尽管买方支付了贷款，但这并不构成对债务的责任承担。因此，银行有权仅针对该妇女和商业伙伴收取缺陷判决（选项 A、B 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：当取消抵押品赎回权出售导致缺陷时，债权人可以获得针对（1）原始债务人和/或（2）承担抵押贷款的任何一方的缺陷判决。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 967 (2019)（缺陷判断）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.1（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（假设有抵押贷款债务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当取消抵押品赎回权出售导致缺陷时，债权人可以获得针对（1）原始债务人和/或（2）承担抵押的任何一方的缺陷判决。","iracZh":{"issue":"银行有权向谁收取缺陷判决？","rule":"当取消抵押品赎回权出售导致缺陷时，债权人可以获得针对（1）原始债务人和/或（2）承担抵押的任何一方的缺陷判决。","analysis":"除非抵押协议另有规定，债务人可以自由出售抵押财产。出售后，抵押贷款仍依附于该财产，债务人仍对抵押贷款担保的债务承担个人责任。但买方对抵押贷款的义务取决于他/她是否： 取得了抵押贷款 – 在这种情况下，买方不同意支付债务，且不承担个人责任（即使买方支付了贷款）或\n\n承担抵押贷款——在这种情况下，买方明确同意付款并承担债务的主要责任，而债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。\n\n因此，如果债权人取消抵押财产的赎回权，并且出售导致短缺（即收益不足以清偿债务），则债权人可以获得针对原债务人和/或承担抵押的任何一方的短缺判决。在这里，这位女士将商业票据抵押给银行，然后将商业票据转交给她的商业伙伴，后者承担了抵押贷款。然后，商业伙伴将单张转交给买方，买方将其作为抵押贷款。尽管买方支付了贷款，但这并不构成对债务的责任承担。因此，银行有权仅针对该妇女和商业伙伴收取缺陷判决（选项 A、B 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：当取消抵押品赎回权出售导致缺陷时，债权人可以获得针对（1）原始债务人和/或（2）承担抵押贷款的任何一方的缺陷判决。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 967 (2019)（缺陷判断）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.1（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（假设有抵押贷款债务）。","conclusion":"答案 C：女人和商业伙伴。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-42c81a37841f7170"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":70,"sourceQuestionPage":205,"sourceQuestionPages":[205],"sourceExplanationPages":[206,207],"sourceAnswerPages":[206,207],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-071","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man obtained a bank loan secured by a mortgage on an office building that he owned. After several years, the man conveyed the office building to a woman, who took title subject to the mortgage. The deed to the woman was not recorded. The woman took immediate possession of the building and made the mortgage payments for several years. Subsequently, the woman stopped making payments on the mortgage loan, and the bank eventually commenced foreclosure proceedings in which the man and the woman were both named parties. At the foreclosure sale, a third party purchased the building for less than the outstanding balance on the mortgage loan. The bank then sought to collect the deficiency from the woman. Is the bank entitled to collect the deficiency from the woman?","choices":{"A":"No, because the woman did not record the deed from the man.","B":"No, because the woman is not personally liable on the loan.","C":"Yes, because the woman took immediate possession of the building when she bought it from the man.","D":"Yes, because the woman was a party to the foreclosure proceeding."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A mortgage is an interest in real property given to a lender (ie, mortgagee) to secure a debt. The debtor (ie, mortgagor) can freely transfer the mortgaged property to a grantee unless the mortgage states otherwise. After the conveyance, the mortgage remains attached to the property and the debtor remains personally liable for the debt secured by the mortgage. But the grantee's obligations depend on whether the grantee: took subject to the mortgage – in which case, the grantee does not agree to pay and is not personally liable for the debt or assumed the mortgage – in which case, the grantee agrees to pay and becomes primarily liable for the debt, and the debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. Therefore, if a lender forecloses and the sale results in a deficiency—ie, the proceeds are insufficient to satisfy the debt—the lender cannot collect that deficiency from a grantee who took the property subject to the mortgage. Here, the man (debtor) mortgaged his office building to a bank (lender). He then conveyed the office building to a woman, who took title subject to the man's mortgage. As a result, the woman did not assume personal liability for the man's mortgage debt. This means that the bank cannot collect the deficiency from the foreclosure sale from the woman—regardless of whether she took possession of the property or was a party to the foreclosure proceeding (Choices C & D). (Choice A) Recording a deed in the land records puts others on record notice of a person's interest in the land. But it has no impact on a lender's right to foreclosure or ability to collect a deficiency.\n\nEducational objective: A grantee who acquires property subject to a mortgage is not personally liable for the mortgage debt and therefore is not liable for any deficiency following a foreclosure sale.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (receipt of property subject to mortgage).","educationalObjective":"A grantee who acquires property subject to a mortgage is not personally liable for the mortgage debt and therefore is not liable for any deficiency following a foreclosure sale.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (receipt of property subject to mortgage).","rule":"A grantee who acquires property subject to a mortgage is not personally liable for the mortgage debt and therefore is not liable for any deficiency following a foreclosure sale.","irac":{"issue":"Is the bank entitled to collect the deficiency from the woman?","rule":"A grantee who acquires property subject to a mortgage is not personally liable for the mortgage debt and therefore is not liable for any deficiency following a foreclosure sale.","analysis":"A mortgage is an interest in real property given to a lender (ie, mortgagee) to secure a debt. The debtor (ie, mortgagor) can freely transfer the mortgaged property to a grantee unless the mortgage states otherwise. After the conveyance, the mortgage remains attached to the property and the debtor remains personally liable for the debt secured by the mortgage. But the grantee's obligations depend on whether the grantee: took subject to the mortgage – in which case, the grantee does not agree to pay and is not personally liable for the debt or assumed the mortgage – in which case, the grantee agrees to pay and becomes primarily liable for the debt, and the debtor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. Therefore, if a lender forecloses and the sale results in a deficiency—ie, the proceeds are insufficient to satisfy the debt—the lender cannot collect that deficiency from a grantee who took the property subject to the mortgage. Here, the man (debtor) mortgaged his office building to a bank (lender). He then conveyed the office building to a woman, who took title subject to the man's mortgage. As a result, the woman did not assume personal liability for the man's mortgage debt. This means that the bank cannot collect the deficiency from the foreclosure sale from the woman—regardless of whether she took possession of the property or was a party to the foreclosure proceeding (Choices C & D). (Choice A) Recording a deed in the land records puts others on record notice of a person's interest in the land. But it has no impact on a lender's right to foreclosure or ability to collect a deficiency.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the woman is not personally liable on the loan."},"questionZh":"一名男子以他拥有的一栋办公楼的抵押贷款获得了银行贷款。几年后，男方将办公楼转让给女方，女方通过抵押获得了所有权。该女子的行为没有被记录。该女子立即拥有了这栋大楼，并支付了几年的抵押贷款。随后，该女子停止支付抵押贷款，银行最终启动了止赎程序，该男子和该女子均被指定为当事人。在取消抵押品赎回权的出售中，第三方以低于抵押贷款未偿余额的价格购买了该建筑物。随后，银行试图向这名女子追回不足的金额。银行有权向该女子追讨不足的款项吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为女人没有记录男人的行为。","B":"不，因为该妇女个人不对贷款承担责任。","C":"是的，因为当女方从男方手中购买该建筑时，她立即拥有了该建筑。","D":"是的，因为该女子是止赎诉讼的一方。"},"explanationZh":"抵押贷款是给予贷款人（即抵押权人）以担保债务的不动产权益。除非抵押另有规定，债务人（即抵押人）可以自由地将抵押财产转让给受让人。转让后，抵押仍然附着在财产上，债务人仍然对抵押担保的债务承担个人责任。但受让人的义务取决于受让人是否： 接受抵押——在这种情况下，受让人不同意付款且不对债务承担个人责任或承担抵押——在这种情况下，受让人同意付款并对债务承担主要责任，债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。因此，如果贷款人取消抵押品赎回权并且出售导致短缺，即收益不足以偿还债务，则贷款人无法从取得抵押财产的受让人那里收回该短缺。在这里，该男子（债务人）将自己的办公楼抵押给了银行（贷方）。然后，他将办公楼转让给一名女士，该女士根据该男子的抵押贷款取得了所有权。因此，女方不对男方的抵押债务承担个人责任。这意味着银行无法从该妇女那里收取止赎出售中的不足额，无论她是否拥有该财产或是止赎诉讼的一方（选择 C ​​和 D）。 （选择 A）在土地记录中记录契约可以将某人对土地的权益记录在案。但它对贷款人取消抵押品赎回权的权利或收取不足额的能力没有影响。\n\n教育目标：获得抵押财产的受赠人个人不对抵押债务承担责任，因此不对取消抵押品赎回权出售后的任何缺陷承担责任。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押财产的收据）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"获得抵押财产的受让人个人不对抵押债务承担责任，因此不对取消抵押品赎回权出售后的任何缺陷承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"银行有权向该女子追讨不足的款项吗？","rule":"获得抵押财产的受让人个人不对抵押债务承担责任，因此不对取消抵押品赎回权出售后的任何缺陷承担责任。","analysis":"抵押贷款是给予贷款人（即抵押权人）以担保债务的不动产权益。除非抵押另有规定，债务人（即抵押人）可以自由地将抵押财产转让给受让人。转让后，抵押仍然附着在财产上，债务人仍然对抵押担保的债务承担个人责任。但受让人的义务取决于受让人是否： 接受抵押——在这种情况下，受让人不同意付款且不对债务承担个人责任或承担抵押——在这种情况下，受让人同意付款并对债务承担主要责任，债务人作为担保人承担次要责任。因此，如果贷款人取消抵押品赎回权并且出售导致短缺，即收益不足以偿还债务，则贷款人无法从取得抵押财产的受让人那里收回该短缺。在这里，该男子（债务人）将自己的办公楼抵押给了银行（贷方）。然后，他将办公楼转让给一名女士，该女士根据该男子的抵押贷款取得了所有权。因此，女方不对男方的抵押债务承担个人责任。这意味着银行无法从该妇女那里收取止赎出售中的不足额，无论她是否拥有该财产或是止赎诉讼的一方（选择 C ​​和 D）。 （选择 A）在土地记录中记录契约可以将某人对土地的权益记录在案。但它对贷款人取消抵押品赎回权的权利或收取不足额的能力没有影响。\n\n教育目标：获得抵押财产的受赠人个人不对抵押债务承担责任，因此不对取消抵押品赎回权出售后的任何缺陷承担责任。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押财产的收据）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为该妇女个人不对贷款承担责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-65d2c11ad335daa7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":71,"sourceQuestionPage":208,"sourceQuestionPages":[208],"sourceExplanationPages":[209,210],"sourceAnswerPages":[209,210],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-072","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man borrowed $150,000 from a bank to remodel his home and executed a promissory note agreeing to repay the loan over a 10-year period. The loan was secured by a mortgage on the home. The bank promptly recorded the mortgage, which was the only lien on the home at that time. Several months later, the man borrowed $40,000 from his mother in order to purchase a new truck and gave his mother a mortgage on the home to secure repayment of the debt. The mother promptly recorded the mortgage. The man later lost his job and was struggling to make loan payments to both the bank and his mother. To accommodate the man's financial situation, the bank extended the amortization period of its loan to substantially reduce the amount of each monthly payment. The bank did not alter the interest rate or increase the principal amount of the loan. At the time of the modification of the bank loan, the man was not in default in his payments to his mother. Neither the bank nor the man informed the mother of the modification of the bank loan. After the man later missed five payments to his mother, she commenced a foreclosure action. While the action was pending, the mother learned of the bank's loan modification. The mother asserted that her mortgage had become a first lien on the home and that the bank's mortgage had become a second lien. Is the mother's assertion correct?","choices":{"A":"No, because the bank's loan modification was not detrimental to the rights of the mother.","B":"No, because the man was not in default in his payments to his mother at the time of the bank's loan modification.","C":"Yes, because the bank's loan modification was made without the mother's prior consent.","D":"Yes, because the bank's loan modification was material."},"answer":"A","explanation":"When a senior mortgage (ie, first in time) is modified by the parties, it will retain its priority against junior interests so long as the modification does not materially prejudice the holders of the junior interests. There is a strong presumption that a time extension on repayment is not materially prejudicial because the debtor is less likely to default on the senior mortgage and force a foreclosure. Here, the bank's mortgage was recorded first, so it is senior to the mother's mortgage. The bank later modified the mortgage by extending the amortization period of its loan. But since it is presumed that this time extension did not materially prejudice the mother's junior interest, the bank's mortgage retained its senior priority (Choice D). As a result, the mother's assertion that her mortgage had become a first lien on the home is incorrect. (Choice B) Whether the man was in default on the mother's loan at the time of the modification is irrelevant. The default would merely give the mother the right to foreclose on her junior mortgage, but the foreclosure would not extinguish the bank's senior mortgage. (Choice C) A junior interest holder's consent is not needed to modify a senior mortgage.\n\nEducational objective: A modified senior mortgage will retain its priority against junior interests so long as the modification does not materially prejudice the junior interest holders. It is strongly presumed that a time extension is not materially prejudicial.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.3(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (effect of modifying senior mortgages on junior interests).","educationalObjective":"A modified senior mortgage will retain its priority against junior interests so long as the modification does not materially prejudice the junior interest holders. It is strongly presumed that a time extension is not materially prejudicial.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.3(b) (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (effect of modifying senior mortgages on junior interests).","rule":"A modified senior mortgage will retain its priority against junior interests so long as the modification does not materially prejudice the junior interest holders. It is strongly presumed that a time extension is not materially prejudicial.","irac":{"issue":"Is the mother's assertion correct?","rule":"A modified senior mortgage will retain its priority against junior interests so long as the modification does not materially prejudice the junior interest holders. It is strongly presumed that a time extension is not materially prejudicial.","analysis":"When a senior mortgage (ie, first in time) is modified by the parties, it will retain its priority against junior interests so long as the modification does not materially prejudice the holders of the junior interests. There is a strong presumption that a time extension on repayment is not materially prejudicial because the debtor is less likely to default on the senior mortgage and force a foreclosure. Here, the bank's mortgage was recorded first, so it is senior to the mother's mortgage. The bank later modified the mortgage by extending the amortization period of its loan. But since it is presumed that this time extension did not materially prejudice the mother's junior interest, the bank's mortgage retained its senior priority (Choice D). As a result, the mother's assertion that her mortgage had become a first lien on the home is incorrect. (Choice B) Whether the man was in default on the mother's loan at the time of the modification is irrelevant. The default would merely give the mother the right to foreclose on her junior mortgage, but the foreclosure would not extinguish the bank's senior mortgage. (Choice C) A junior interest holder's consent is not needed to modify a senior mortgage.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the bank's loan modification was not detrimental to the rights of the mother."},"questionZh":"一名男子从银行借了 15 万美元来改造自己的房屋，并签署了一份期票，同意在 10 年内偿还贷款。该贷款由房屋抵押贷款担保。银行立即记录了抵押贷款，这是当时房屋的唯一留置权。几个月后，该男子向母亲借了 4 万美元购买一辆新卡车，并向母亲提供了房屋抵押贷款以确保偿还债务。母亲立即记录了抵押贷款。该男子后来失业了，并且正在努力向银行和他的母亲偿还贷款。为了适应该男子的财务状况，银行延长了贷款的摊还期限，大幅减少了每月还款额。银行没有改变利率或增加贷款本金。在修改银行贷款时，该男子并未拖欠其母亲的付款。银行和男子都没有将银行贷款的修改告知母亲。在该男子后来拖欠了五笔款项后，他的母亲开始了取消抵押品赎回权的诉讼。在诉讼等待期间，母亲得知银行修改了贷款。这位母亲声称，她的抵押贷款已成为房屋的第一留置权，而银行的抵押贷款已成为第二留置权。妈妈的说法正确吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为银行的贷款修改并没有损害母亲的权利。","B":"不会，因为在银行修改贷款时，该男子并未拖欠其母亲的付款。","C":"是的，因为银行的贷款修改是在没有经过母亲事先同意的情况下进行的。","D":"是的，因为银行的贷款修改很重大。"},"explanationZh":"当优先抵押贷款（即时间上最先的）被当事人修改时，只要修改不会对次级权益的持有人造成重大损害，它将保留其相对于次级权益的优先权。有一个强有力的假设是，延长还款时间不会造成重大损害，因为债务人拖欠高级抵押贷款并强制取消抵押品赎回权的可能性较小。在这里，银行的抵押贷款是先记录的，所以它比母亲的抵押贷款优先。银行后来修改了抵押贷款，延长了贷款的摊销期限。但由于假定这次延期不会对母亲的次要利益造成重大损害，因此银行抵押贷款保留其优先权（选项D）。因此，母亲关于她的抵押贷款已成为房屋第一留置权的说法是不正确的。 （选择B） 修改时该男子是否拖欠母亲的贷款无关紧要。违约只会赋予母亲取消其初级抵押贷款的权利，但取消抵押品赎回权不会取消银行的高级抵押贷款。 （选择C） 修改高级抵押贷款不需要初级权益持有人的同意。\n\n教育目标：只要修改不会对初级权益持有人造成重大损害，修改后的高级抵押贷款将保留其相对初级权益的优先权。强烈认为延长时间不会造成实质性损害。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.3(b)（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（修改高级抵押贷款对初级权益的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只要修改不会对初级权益持有人造成重大损害，修改后的高级抵押贷款将保留其相对次级权益的优先权。强烈认为延长时间不会造成实质性损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"妈妈的说法正确吗？","rule":"只要修改不会对初级权益持有人造成重大损害，修改后的高级抵押贷款将保留其相对次级权益的优先权。强烈认为延长时间不会造成实质性损害。","analysis":"当优先抵押贷款（即时间上最先的）被当事人修改时，只要修改不会对次级权益的持有人造成重大损害，它将保留其相对于次级权益的优先权。有一个强有力的假设是，延长还款时间不会造成重大损害，因为债务人拖欠高级抵押贷款并强制取消抵押品赎回权的可能性较小。在这里，银行的抵押贷款是先记录的，所以它比母亲的抵押贷款优先。银行后来修改了抵押贷款，延长了贷款的摊销期限。但由于假定这次延期不会对母亲的次要利益造成重大损害，因此银行抵押贷款保留其优先权（选项D）。因此，母亲关于她的抵押贷款已成为房屋第一留置权的说法是不正确的。 （选择B） 修改时该男子是否拖欠母亲的贷款无关紧要。违约只会赋予母亲取消其初级抵押贷款的权利，但取消抵押品赎回权不会取消银行的高级抵押贷款。 （选择C） 修改高级抵押贷款不需要初级权益持有人的同意。\n\n教育目标：只要修改不会对初级权益持有人造成重大损害，修改后的高级抵押贷款将保留其相对初级权益的优先权。强烈认为延长时间不会造成实质性损害。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.3(b)（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（修改高级抵押贷款对初级权益的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为银行的贷款修改并没有损害母亲的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-492f35c0b93034f7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":72,"sourceQuestionPage":211,"sourceQuestionPages":[211],"sourceExplanationPages":[212,213],"sourceAnswerPages":[212,213],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-073","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Fifteen years ago, a father and daughter lived on a large 15-acre tract of land owned by the father. The land consists of three five-acre sections that are generally known as the home, the barn, and the hunting cabin. Due to the father's deteriorating health, he decided to move into an assisted living facility. The daughter soon realized that she could not perform all of the duties required to maintain the 15-acre tract. When she asked the father for permission to sell the land, he refused. Against the father's will, she proceeded to list the 15-acre tract for sale in the local newspaper. A couple who was new to the area contacted the daughter with a cash offer to buy the tract. The daughter prepared a warranty deed conveying the tract to the couple and forged her father's signature on the deed. For the last 14 years, the couple has lived in the home and maintained the surrounding five acres. During each hunting season, the couple occasionally spends time at the hunting cabin and hunts on that five-acre portion of the property. However, the couple has never used the five acres surrounding the barn. Recently, the couple learned that the signature on the deed was a forgery and filed suit to quiet title based on adverse possession. The period of time to acquire title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 10 years. In an action to determine title, what portion of the 15-acre tract will the court likely award the couple?","choices":{"A":"None, because the couple's possession of the farm was not hostile to the father.","B":"The five acres surrounding the home, because that was the only portion of the land continuously used by the couple.","C":"The 10 acres surrounding the home and the cabin, because those were the only portions of the land actually possessed by the couple.","D":"All 15 acres, because the deed described the entire 15-acre tract."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The deed conveying the 15-acre tract to the couple was invalid because the father's signature was forged. However, the couple may still have acquired title to the tract through adverse possession during the 10-year statutory period. An adverse possessor may acquire title to the portion of the land that was actually and continuously possessed. To satisfy the continuous-use requirement, an adverse possessor must either: constantly remain on the property or consistently use the property in the same manner that a usual owner would. Here, the couple actually and continuously possessed the five acres surrounding the home because they lived in the home and maintained that land for the past 14 years. And they actually and continuously possessed the five acres surrounding the hunting cabin by consistently using it on a seasonal basis to hunt—just as a usual owner would (Choice B). However, the couple never used the barn. Nevertheless, the couple acquired title to the entire property through constructive adverse possession (Choice C). This arises when an adverse possessor (1) enters the property under color of title—eg, a facially valid deed that describes the entire property—and (2) actually possesses a reasonable portion of the property for the statutory period. Here, the couple entered the property under color of title because they had a facially valid deed that described the entire 15 acres of the tract. And since they actually possessed two- thirds of the property (reasonable portion) for the 10-year statutory period, they acquired title through constructive adverse possession. Therefore, the court will likely award the couple title to all 15 acres. (Choice A) Since the couple possessed the tract without the father's permission, their possession was hostile (ie, nonpermissive). Additionally, in a majority of jurisdictions, the possessor need not purposefully seek to defeat the owner's title for the possession to be \"hostile.\"\n\nEducational objective: If a person enters property under color of title and actually possesses a reasonable portion of the property for the statutory period, then constructive adverse possession gives the person title to the entire property.","educationalObjective":"If a person enters property under color of title and actually possesses a reasonable portion of the property for the statutory period, then constructive adverse possession gives the person title to the entire property.","references":"","rule":"If a person enters property under color of title and actually possesses a reasonable portion of the property for the statutory period, then constructive adverse possession gives the person title to the entire property.","irac":{"issue":"In an action to determine title, what portion of the 15-acre tract will the court likely award the couple?","rule":"If a person enters property under color of title and actually possesses a reasonable portion of the property for the statutory period, then constructive adverse possession gives the person title to the entire property.","analysis":"The deed conveying the 15-acre tract to the couple was invalid because the father's signature was forged. However, the couple may still have acquired title to the tract through adverse possession during the 10-year statutory period. An adverse possessor may acquire title to the portion of the land that was actually and continuously possessed. To satisfy the continuous-use requirement, an adverse possessor must either: constantly remain on the property or consistently use the property in the same manner that a usual owner would. Here, the couple actually and continuously possessed the five acres surrounding the home because they lived in the home and maintained that land for the past 14 years. And they actually and continuously possessed the five acres surrounding the hunting cabin by consistently using it on a seasonal basis to hunt—just as a usual owner would (Choice B). However, the couple never used the barn. Nevertheless, the couple acquired title to the entire property through constructive adverse possession (Choice C). This arises when an adverse possessor (1) enters the property under color of title—eg, a facially valid deed that describes the entire property—and (2) actually possesses a reasonable portion of the property for the statutory period. Here, the couple entered the property under color of title because they had a facially valid deed that described the entire 15 acres of the tract. And since they actually possessed two- thirds of the property (reasonable portion) for the 10-year statutory period, they acquired title through constructive adverse possession. Therefore, the court will likely award the couple title to all 15 acres. (Choice A) Since the couple possessed the tract without the father's permission, their possession was hostile (ie, nonpermissive). Additionally, in a majority of jurisdictions, the possessor need not purposefully seek to defeat the owner's title for the possession to be \"hostile.\"","conclusion":"Answer D: All 15 acres, because the deed described the entire 15-acre tract."},"questionZh":"十五年前，一对父女生活在父亲拥有的一大片 15 英亩的土地上。这片土地由三个五英亩的部分组成，通常被称为住宅、谷仓和狩猎小屋。由于父亲的健康状况不断恶化，他决定搬进辅助生活设施。女儿很快意识到她无法履行维护 15 英亩土地所需的所有职责。当她请求父亲允许出售土地时，他拒绝了。她违背父亲的意愿，在当地报纸上列出了出售这块 15 英亩土地的清单。一对刚到该地区的夫妇联系了女儿，并提出现金购买这片土地的提议。女儿准备了一份保修契约，将单张转交给这对夫妇，并在契约上伪造了她父亲的签名。在过去的 14 年里，这对夫妇一直住在这所房子里，并维护着周围的 5 英亩土地。在每个狩猎季节，这对夫妇偶尔会在狩猎小屋度过一段时间，并在这片五英亩的土地上狩猎。然而，这对夫妇从未使用过谷仓周围的五英亩土地。最近，这对夫妇得知契约上的签名是伪造的，并提起诉讼，要求基于逆权占有的权利安静。在管辖区内通过逆权占有取得所有权的期限为10年。在确定所有权的诉讼中，法院可能会将 15 英亩土地的哪一部分判给这对夫妇？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为这对夫妇对农场的占有并不对父亲有敌意。","B":"房子周围的五英亩土地，因为这是这对夫妇持续使用的唯一一部分土地。","C":"房屋和小屋周围的 10 英亩土地，因为这是这对夫妇真正拥有的唯一部分土地。","D":"全部 15 英亩，因为契约描述了整个 15 英亩的土地。"},"explanationZh":"将15英亩土地转让给这对夫妇的契约无效，因为父亲的签名是伪造的。然而，这对夫妇可能仍然在 10 年法定期限内通过逆权占有获得了该土地的所有权。逆权占有人可以获得实际并持续占有的那部分土地的所有权。为了满足持续使用的要求，逆权占有人必须：持续留在该财产上，或者始终以与通常所有者相同的方式使用该财产。在这里，这对夫妇实际上并持续拥有房屋周围的 5 英亩土地，因为他们在过去 14 年里一直居住在房屋中并维护着这片土地。他们实际上并持续地占有狩猎小屋周围的五英亩土地，并定期使用它来狩猎——就像通常的主人一样（选择 B）。然而，这对夫妇从未使用过谷仓。尽管如此，这对夫妇还是通过推定逆权占有获得了整个财产的所有权（选择C）。当逆权占有人 (1) 以所有权名义进入财产（例如，描述整个财产的表面有效的契约）并且 (2) 在法定期限内实际拥有财产的合理部分时，就会出现这种情况。在这里，这对夫妇以所有权的名义进入了这处房产，因为他们拥有一份表面有效的契约，描述了整个 15 英亩的土地。由于他们在 10 年法定期限内实际上拥有该财产的三分之二（合理部分），因此他们通过推定逆权占有获得了所有权。因此，法院可能会授予这对夫妇所有 15 英亩土地的所有权。 （选项A） 由于这对夫妇在未经父亲许可的情况下占有该单张，因此他们的占有是敌对的（即，非许可的）。此外，在大多数司法管辖区，占有人无需故意寻求推翻所有者的所有权，从而使占有成为“敌意”。\n\n教育目标：如果一个人以所有权名义进入财产，并且在法定期限内实际拥有该财产的合理部分，则推定逆权占有赋予该人对整个财产的所有权。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果一个人以所有权名义进入财产，并且在法定期限内实际拥有该财产的合理部分，则推定逆权占有赋予该人对整个财产的所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"在确定所有权的诉讼中，法院可能会将 15 英亩土地的哪一部分判给这对夫妇？","rule":"如果一个人以所有权名义进入财产，并且在法定期限内实际拥有该财产的合理部分，则推定逆权占有赋予该人对整个财产的所有权。","analysis":"将15英亩土地转让给这对夫妇的契约无效，因为父亲的签名是伪造的。然而，这对夫妇可能仍然在 10 年法定期限内通过逆权占有获得了该土地的所有权。逆权占有人可以获得实际并持续占有的那部分土地的所有权。为了满足持续使用的要求，逆权占有人必须：持续留在该财产上，或者始终以与通常所有者相同的方式使用该财产。在这里，这对夫妇实际上并持续拥有房屋周围的 5 英亩土地，因为他们在过去 14 年里一直居住在房屋中并维护着这片土地。他们实际上并持续地占有狩猎小屋周围的五英亩土地，并定期使用它来狩猎——就像通常的主人一样（选择 B）。然而，这对夫妇从未使用过谷仓。尽管如此，这对夫妇还是通过推定逆权占有获得了整个财产的所有权（选择C）。当逆权占有人 (1) 以所有权名义进入财产（例如，描述整个财产的表面有效的契约）并且 (2) 在法定期限内实际拥有财产的合理部分时，就会出现这种情况。在这里，这对夫妇以所有权的名义进入了这处房产，因为他们拥有一份表面有效的契约，描述了整个 15 英亩的土地。由于他们在 10 年法定期限内实际上拥有该财产的三分之二（合理部分），因此他们通过推定逆权占有获得了所有权。因此，法院可能会授予这对夫妇所有 15 英亩土地的所有权。 （选项A） 由于这对夫妇在未经父亲许可的情况下占有该单张，因此他们的占有是敌对的（即，非许可的）。此外，在大多数司法管辖区，占有人无需故意寻求推翻所有者的所有权，从而使占有成为“敌意”。\n\n教育目标：如果一个人以所有权名义进入财产，并且在法定期限内实际拥有该财产的合理部分，则推定逆权占有赋予该人对整个财产的所有权。","conclusion":"答案 D：全部 15 英亩，因为契约描述了整个 15 英亩的土地。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"color of title","ipa":"/kˈəlɝ ˈəv tˈaɪtəl/","syllables":"color of ti·tle","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"表面权源：看似有效但实际有缺陷的书面产权文件","contextualMeaning":"表面权源：看似有效但实际有缺陷的书面产权文件","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"If a person enters property under color of title and actually possesses a reasonable portion of the property for the statutory period, then constructive adverse possession gives the person title to the entire property.","synonyms":["apparent title"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-163a254d414ac53a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":73,"sourceQuestionPage":214,"sourceQuestionPages":[214],"sourceExplanationPages":[215,216],"sourceAnswerPages":[215,216],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-074","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman owned her home. Her daughter lived with her and always referred to the home as \"my property.\" Two years ago, the daughter, for a valuable consideration, executed and delivered to her ex-husband an instrument in the proper form of a warranty deed purporting to convey the home to him in fee simple, reserving to herself an estate for two years in the home. The ex-husband promptly and properly recorded his deed. One year ago, the woman died and by will, duly admitted to probate, left her entire estate to her daughter. One month ago, the daughter, for a valuable consideration, executed and delivered to her only son an instrument in the proper form of a warranty deed purporting to convey the home to the son, who promptly and properly recorded the deed. The daughter was then in possession of the home and her son had no actual knowledge of the deed to the ex-husband. Immediately thereafter, the daughter gave possession to her son. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.\" Last week, the daughter fled the jurisdiction. Upon learning the facts, the son brought an appropriate action against the ex-husband to quiet title to the home. If the son wins, what will be the reason?","choices":{"A":"The daughter had nothing to convey to the ex-husband two years ago.","B":"The daughter's deed to the ex-husband was not in the son's chain of title.","C":"The daughter's deed to the ex-husband was not to take effect until after the daughter's deed to the son.","D":"The son was first in possession."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Recording acts determine the priority of competing property interests. In a notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier property interest of which the BFP lacked notice. There are three types of notice: Actual – a buyer has direct knowledge of another's prior interest in land Record – a document showing a prior interest is properly recorded and appears in the buyer's chain of title Inquiry – a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property) In most jurisdictions, a prematurely recorded deed—a deed recorded by a buyer before the seller owns the property—falls outside a later buyer's chain of title and therefore does not provide record notice. That is because a buyer is only required to search the recording index for the seller's name as far back as the date on which the seller acquired the property. Here, the son knew nothing of the ex-husband's prior claim when the son bought the home from the daughter (no actual notice), and the daughter was then in possession of the home (no inquiry notice). And since the ex-husband had recorded his deed from the daughter before she owned the home, that prematurely recorded deed likely fell outside the son's chain of title (no record notice). For that reason, the son (BFP) will likely win his quiet title action. (Choice A) When a grantor (eg, the daughter) conveys title that he/she does not have but later acquires, the doctrine of after-acquired title applies and title automatically vests in the grantee (eg, the ex-husband). But here, the son's subsequent interest has priority since the ex-husband's deed fell outside the son's chain of title. (Choices C & D) Although the ex-husband's deed did not give him an immediate right to possess the home (and the son took possession first), the deed still gave the ex-husband an ownership interest in the home that took effect before the son's. But since the ex-husband's prior deed was not in the son's chain of title, the son likely has priority.\n\nEducational objective: In most jurisdictions, a prematurely recorded deed—a deed recorded by a buyer before the seller owns the property—falls outside a later buyer's chain of title and fails to provide record notice.","educationalObjective":"In most jurisdictions, a prematurely recorded deed—a deed recorded by a buyer before the seller owns the property—falls outside a later buyer's chain of title and fails to provide record notice.","references":"","rule":"In most jurisdictions, a prematurely recorded deed—a deed recorded by a buyer before the seller owns the property—falls outside a later buyer's chain of title and fails to provide record notice.","irac":{"issue":"If the son wins, what will be the reason?","rule":"In most jurisdictions, a prematurely recorded deed—a deed recorded by a buyer before the seller owns the property—falls outside a later buyer's chain of title and fails to provide record notice.","analysis":"Recording acts determine the priority of competing property interests. In a notice jurisdiction (as seen here), a bona fide purchaser's (BFP's) property interest has priority over an earlier property interest of which the BFP lacked notice. There are three types of notice: Actual – a buyer has direct knowledge of another's prior interest in land Record – a document showing a prior interest is properly recorded and appears in the buyer's chain of title Inquiry – a buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt a reasonable person to investigate (eg, visible use of the property) In most jurisdictions, a prematurely recorded deed—a deed recorded by a buyer before the seller owns the property—falls outside a later buyer's chain of title and therefore does not provide record notice. That is because a buyer is only required to search the recording index for the seller's name as far back as the date on which the seller acquired the property. Here, the son knew nothing of the ex-husband's prior claim when the son bought the home from the daughter (no actual notice), and the daughter was then in possession of the home (no inquiry notice). And since the ex-husband had recorded his deed from the daughter before she owned the home, that prematurely recorded deed likely fell outside the son's chain of title (no record notice). For that reason, the son (BFP) will likely win his quiet title action. (Choice A) When a grantor (eg, the daughter) conveys title that he/she does not have but later acquires, the doctrine of after-acquired title applies and title automatically vests in the grantee (eg, the ex-husband). But here, the son's subsequent interest has priority since the ex-husband's deed fell outside the son's chain of title. (Choices C & D) Although the ex-husband's deed did not give him an immediate right to possess the home (and the son took possession first), the deed still gave the ex-husband an ownership interest in the home that took effect before the son's. But since the ex-husband's prior deed was not in the son's chain of title, the son likely has priority.","conclusion":"Answer B: The daughter's deed to the ex-husband was not in the son's chain of title."},"questionZh":"一个女人拥有她的房子。她的女儿和她住在一起，总是称这个家为“我的财产”。两年前，女儿出于有价值的考虑，以保修契约的适当形式签署并向前夫交付了一份文书，旨在以简单的费用将房屋转让给他，并为自己保留了该房屋两年的财产。前夫迅速并妥善记录了他的行为。一年前，这位女士去世了，并根据遗嘱，将她的全部遗产留给了她的女儿。一个月前，女儿出于有价值的考虑，以担保契约的适当形式签署并向她唯一的儿子交付了一份文书，旨在将房屋转让给儿子，儿子迅速正确地记录了该契约。当时，女儿拥有该房屋，而她的儿子并不真正了解前夫的房产契约。此后，女儿立即将财产交给了儿子。该司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“除非依法记录，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者。”上周，女儿逃离了司法管辖区。得知事实后，儿子对前夫提起适当诉讼，以平息房屋所有权。如果儿子赢了，原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"两年前，女儿对前夫没什么可传达的。","B":"女儿对前夫的房产契并不在儿子的产权链中。","C":"女儿对前夫的契约要等到女儿对儿子的契约之后才生效。","D":"儿子是第一个占有者。"},"explanationZh":"记录行为决定了相互竞争的财产利益的优先顺序。在通知管辖区（如此处所示），善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益优先于 BFP 未通知的早期财产权益。通知分为三种类型： 实际 - 买方直接了解他人先前对土地的权益 记录 - 显示先前权益的文件已正确记录并出现在买方的所有权链中 查询 - 买方知道或应该知道会促使合理人员进行调查的情况（例如，财产的可见使用） 在大多数司法管辖区，过早记录的契约 - 买方在卖方拥有该财产之前记录的契约 - 不属于后来买方的所有权链，因此不属于后续买方的所有权链。提供备案通知。这是因为买方只需要在记录索引中搜索最早追溯到卖方购买房产的日期的卖方姓名。在这里，当儿子从女儿那里购买房屋时，儿子对前夫先前的主张一无所知（没有实际通知），而当时女儿拥有该房屋（没有询问通知）。由于前夫在女儿拥有房屋之前就记录了女儿的契约，因此过早记录的契约很可能不属于儿子的所有权链（无记录通知）。因此，儿子（BFP）很可能会赢得安静的冠军争夺战。 （选择A）当设保人（例如，女儿）转让他/她没有但后来获得的所有权时，适用后获得所有权原则，所有权自动归属于受让人（例如，前夫）。但在这里，儿子的后续利益具有优先权，因为前夫的行为不属于儿子的所有权链。 （选项C和D）虽然前夫的契约没有赋予他立即拥有房屋的权利（儿子先占有），但契约仍然赋予前夫房屋的所有权权益，该权益在儿子的所有权之前生效。但由于前夫之前的契约并不在儿子的产权链中，因此儿子可能拥有优先权。\n\n教育目标：在大多数司法管辖区，过早记录的契约（买方在卖方拥有财产之前记录的契约）不属于后来买方的所有权链，并且无法提供记录通知。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在大多数司法管辖区，过早记录的契约（买方在卖方拥有财产之前记录的契约）不属于后来买方的所有权链，并且无法提供记录通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果儿子赢了，原因是什么？","rule":"在大多数司法管辖区，过早记录的契约（买方在卖方拥有财产之前记录的契约）不属于后来买方的所有权链，并且无法提供记录通知。","analysis":"记录行为决定了相互竞争的财产利益的优先顺序。在通知管辖区（如此处所示），善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 的财产权益优先于 BFP 未通知的早期财产权益。通知分为三种类型： 实际 - 买方直接了解他人先前对土地的权益 记录 - 显示先前权益的文件已正确记录并出现在买方的所有权链中 查询 - 买方知道或应该知道会促使合理人员进行调查的情况（例如，财产的可见使用） 在大多数司法管辖区，过早记录的契约 - 买方在卖方拥有该财产之前记录的契约 - 不属于后来买方的所有权链，因此不属于后续买方的所有权链。提供备案通知。这是因为买方只需要在记录索引中搜索最早追溯到卖方购买房产的日期的卖方姓名。在这里，当儿子从女儿那里购买房屋时，儿子对前夫先前的主张一无所知（没有实际通知），而当时女儿拥有该房屋（没有询问通知）。由于前夫在女儿拥有房屋之前就记录了女儿的契约，因此过早记录的契约很可能不属于儿子的所有权链（无记录通知）。因此，儿子（BFP）很可能会赢得安静的冠军争夺战。 （选择A）当设保人（例如，女儿）转让他/她没有但后来获得的所有权时，适用后获得所有权原则，所有权自动归属于受让人（例如，前夫）。但在这里，儿子的后续利益具有优先权，因为前夫的行为不属于儿子的所有权链。 （选项C和D）虽然前夫的契约没有赋予他立即拥有房屋的权利（儿子先占有），但契约仍然赋予前夫房屋的所有权权益，该权益在儿子的所有权之前生效。但由于前夫之前的契约并不在儿子的产权链中，因此儿子可能拥有优先权。\n\n教育目标：在大多数司法管辖区，过早记录的契约（买方在卖方拥有财产之前记录的契约）不属于后来买方的所有权链，并且无法提供记录通知。","conclusion":"答案 B：女儿对前夫的房产契并不在儿子的产权链中。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-37025346015d8175"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":74,"sourceQuestionPage":217,"sourceQuestionPages":[217],"sourceExplanationPages":[218,219],"sourceAnswerPages":[218,219],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-075","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"An investor purchased a tract of commercial land, financing a large part of the purchase price with a loan from a business partner that was secured by a mortgage. The investor made the installment payments on the mortgage regularly for several years. Then the investor persuaded a neighbor to buy the land, subject to the mortgage to his partner. They expressly agreed that the neighbor would not assume and agree to pay the investor's debt to the partner. The investor's mortgage to the partner contained a due-on-sale clause stating, \"If Mortgagor transfers his or her interest without the written consent of Mortgagee first obtained, then at Mortgagee's option the entire principal balance of the debt secured by this Mortgage shall become immediately due and payable.\" However, without seeking his partner's consent, the investor conveyed the land to the neighbor, the deed stating that it was \"subject to a mortgage to [the partner]\" and giving details and recording data related to the mortgage. The neighbor took possession of the land and made several mortgage payments, which the partner accepted. Now, however, neither the neighbor nor the investor has made the last three mortgage payments. The partner has sued the neighbor for the amount of the delinquent payments. In this action, for whom should the court render judgment?","choices":{"A":"The neighbor, because she did not assume and agree to pay the investor's mortgage debt.","B":"The neighbor, because she is not in privity of estate with the partner.","C":"The partner, because the investor's deed to the neighbor violated the due-on-sale clause.","D":"The partner, because the neighbor is in privity of estate with the partner."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Transfer of mortgaged property\n\nEffect on buyer/transferee Effecton seller\n\nSubject to mortgage Does not agree to pay loan & is not Remains solely liable for liable for debt debt\n\nAssumption of Agrees to pay loan & is primarily Becomes secondarily liable mortgage liable for debt as surety\n\nA mortgagor (typically a landowner) is generally free to transfer mortgaged property to another (eg, a buyer). However, the mortgage will remain attached to the property and the mortgagor will remain solely liable for the debt unless the buyer expressly agrees to assume the mortgage. If that occurs, the buyer becomes primarily liable for the debt and the mortgagor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. Here, the investor's mortgage to the partner contained a due-on-sale clause that was breached when the investor sold the mortgaged land to the neighbor without the partner's consent. But since the neighbor did not agree to assume the investor's mortgage, the neighbor is not liable for the investor's delinquent mortgage payments (Choice C). Therefore, in the partner's action against the neighbor, the court should render judgment for the neighbor. (Choices B & D) Privity of estate is a shared interest in property (eg, co-tenants or landlord/tenant). Here, the partner and the neighbor lack privity of estate because the neighbor's ownership interest is distinct from the partner's lien on the property. But the existence or lack of such privity has no bearing on the success of the partner's action.\n\nEducational objective: If a mortgagor transfers mortgaged property to another, the mortgage will remain attached to the property and the mortgagor will remain solely liable for the debt unless the buyer expressly agrees to assume the mortgage.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (assumption of a mortgage).","educationalObjective":"If a mortgagor transfers mortgaged property to another, the mortgage will remain attached to the property and the mortgagor will remain solely liable for the debt unless the buyer expressly agrees to assume the mortgage.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 5.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (assumption of a mortgage).","rule":"If a mortgagor transfers mortgaged property to another, the mortgage will remain attached to the property and the mortgagor will remain solely liable for the debt unless the buyer expressly agrees to assume the mortgage.","irac":{"issue":"In this action, for whom should the court render judgment?","rule":"If a mortgagor transfers mortgaged property to another, the mortgage will remain attached to the property and the mortgagor will remain solely liable for the debt unless the buyer expressly agrees to assume the mortgage.","analysis":"Transfer of mortgaged property\n\nEffect on buyer/transferee Effecton seller\n\nSubject to mortgage Does not agree to pay loan & is not Remains solely liable for liable for debt debt\n\nAssumption of Agrees to pay loan & is primarily Becomes secondarily liable mortgage liable for debt as surety\n\nA mortgagor (typically a landowner) is generally free to transfer mortgaged property to another (eg, a buyer). However, the mortgage will remain attached to the property and the mortgagor will remain solely liable for the debt unless the buyer expressly agrees to assume the mortgage. If that occurs, the buyer becomes primarily liable for the debt and the mortgagor becomes secondarily liable as a surety. Here, the investor's mortgage to the partner contained a due-on-sale clause that was breached when the investor sold the mortgaged land to the neighbor without the partner's consent. But since the neighbor did not agree to assume the investor's mortgage, the neighbor is not liable for the investor's delinquent mortgage payments (Choice C). Therefore, in the partner's action against the neighbor, the court should render judgment for the neighbor. (Choices B & D) Privity of estate is a shared interest in property (eg, co-tenants or landlord/tenant). Here, the partner and the neighbor lack privity of estate because the neighbor's ownership interest is distinct from the partner's lien on the property. But the existence or lack of such privity has no bearing on the success of the partner's action.","conclusion":"Answer A: The neighbor, because she did not assume and agree to pay the investor's mortgage debt."},"questionZh":"一位投资者购买了一块商业用地，并通过以抵押贷款为担保的商业伙伴提供的贷款为购买价格提供了很大一部分资金。投资者多年来定期分期支付抵押贷款。然后，投资者说服邻居购买土地，并抵押给他的合伙人。他们明确同意邻居不会承担并同意偿还投资者对合作伙伴的债务。投资者向合伙人提供的抵押贷款包含到期出售条款，规定：“如果抵押人在未经抵押权人事先书面同意的情况下转让其权益，则抵押权人可以选择由该抵押权担保的债务的全部本金余额应立即到期应付。”然而，在未征得其合伙人同意的情况下，投资者将土地转让给了邻居，契据上注明该土地“已抵押给[合伙人]”，并提供了与抵押相关的详细信息和记录数据。邻居占有了这块土地并支付了几笔抵押贷款，合伙人接受了。然而现在，邻居和投资者都没有支付最后三笔抵押贷款。该合作伙伴已就拖欠付款金额起诉邻居。在本案中，法院该判决给谁呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"邻居，因为她没有承担并同意支付投资者的抵押贷款债务。","B":"邻居，因为她与伴侣没有共同财产。","C":"合伙人，因为投资者对邻居的契约违反了到期出售条款。","D":"合伙人，因为邻居与合伙人拥有共同财产。"},"explanationZh":"抵押财产转让\n\n对买方/受让人的影响 对卖方的影响\n\n有抵押 不同意偿还贷款且不承担全部债务责任\n\n假设同意支付贷款，并且主要成为次要责任抵押贷款，作为担保人对债务承担责任\n\n抵押人（通常是土地所有者）通常可以自由地将抵押财产转让给另一个人（例如买方）。然而，除非买方明确同意承担抵押贷款，否则抵押贷款仍将依附于房产，抵押人仍将独自承担债务责任。如果发生这种情况，买方将对债务承担主要责任，抵押人作为担保人承担次要责任。在此，投资者向合伙人提供的抵押贷款包含到期出售条款，当投资者未经合伙人同意将抵押土地出售给邻居时，该条款就被违反。但由于邻居不同意承担投资者的抵押贷款，因此邻居不对投资者拖欠的抵押贷款承担责任（选择C）。因此，在合伙人对邻居提起诉讼时，法院应作出有利于邻居的判决。 （选择 B 和 D） 遗产相关性是指财产的共同利益（例如，按份共有人（tenant in common）或房东/租户）。在这里，合伙人和邻居缺乏财产相关性，因为邻居的所有权权益不同于合伙人对财产的留置权。但这种关联性的存在与否并不影响合作伙伴行动的成功。\n\n教育目的：如果抵押人将抵押财产转让给另一人，则抵押仍将附着在该财产上，并且抵押人仍将独自承担债务，除非买方明确同意承担抵押。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款的假设）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果抵押人将抵押财产转让给另一人，则抵押仍将附着在该财产上，并且抵押人仍将独自承担债务，除非买方明确同意承担抵押。","iracZh":{"issue":"在本案中，法院该判决给谁呢？","rule":"如果抵押人将抵押财产转让给另一人，则抵押仍将附着在该财产上，并且抵押人仍将独自承担债务，除非买方明确同意承担抵押。","analysis":"抵押财产转让\n\n对买方/受让人的影响 对卖方的影响\n\n有抵押 不同意偿还贷款且不承担全部债务责任\n\n假设同意支付贷款，并且主要成为次要责任抵押贷款，作为担保人对债务承担责任\n\n抵押人（通常是土地所有者）通常可以自由地将抵押财产转让给另一个人（例如买方）。然而，除非买方明确同意承担抵押贷款，否则抵押贷款仍将依附于房产，抵押人仍将独自承担债务责任。如果发生这种情况，买方将对债务承担主要责任，抵押人作为担保人承担次要责任。在此，投资者向合伙人提供的抵押贷款包含到期出售条款，当投资者未经合伙人同意将抵押土地出售给邻居时，该条款就被违反。但由于邻居不同意承担投资者的抵押贷款，因此邻居不对投资者拖欠的抵押贷款承担责任（选择C）。因此，在合伙人对邻居提起诉讼时，法院应作出有利于邻居的判决。 （选择 B 和 D） 遗产相关性是指财产的共同利益（例如，按份共有人（tenant in common）或房东/租户）。在这里，合伙人和邻居缺乏财产相关性，因为邻居的所有权权益不同于合伙人对财产的留置权。但这种关联性的存在与否并不影响合作伙伴行动的成功。\n\n教育目的：如果抵押人将抵押财产转让给另一人，则抵押仍将附着在该财产上，并且抵押人仍将独自承担债务，除非买方明确同意承担抵押。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 5.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（抵押贷款的假设）。","conclusion":"答案 A：邻居，因为她没有承担并同意支付投资者的抵押贷款债务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"privity of estate","ipa":"/'priviti ˈəv ɪstˈeɪt/","syllables":"priv·ity of es·tate","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"地产关系：因持有相互关联土地权益而产生的法律关系","contextualMeaning":"地产关系：因持有相互关联土地权益而产生的法律关系","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The neighbor, because she is not in privity of estate with the partner.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":75,"sourceQuestionPage":220,"sourceQuestionPages":[220],"sourceExplanationPages":[221],"sourceAnswerPages":[221],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-076","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"Two years ago, a developer conveyed title to a lot to a woman by warranty deed. The woman purchased an owner's policy of title insurance before the closing. Because of an error by the title insurance company, the title commitment and title policy failed to list a county tax lien encumbering the lot as an exception to coverage. Last year, the woman conveyed the lot for a bargain price by quitclaim deed to a friend, who paid cash, did not conduct a title search, and did not purchase title insurance. Subsequently, the county began proceedings to foreclose the tax lien, and the friend filed a claim with the woman's title insurance company, demanding that the company reimburse her for the tax lien amount. Is the title insurance company obligated to pay the friend's claim?","choices":{"A":"No, because the friend is not a named insured on the title insurance policy.","B":"No, because the title insurance policy did not expire when the woman conveyed the lot to the friend.","C":"Yes, because the tax lien was not listed as an exception on the title insurance policy.","D":"Yes, because the woman is liable to the friend for breach of the covenant against encumbrances, and the title policy should cover the friend's potential loss."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Title insurance policies\n\n(protect against undisclosed title defects)\n\nLosses covered Duration of coverage\n\nOwner's Reduction in full market While insured owns property AND after policy value of property insured conveys by warranty deed\n\nLender's Reduction in value or Ends once mortgage is paid in full or policy priority of mortgage otherwise discharged\n\nBoth owner's and lender's title insurance policies protect insureds—named persons covered by the policy—from undisclosed title defects by requiring the insurer to indemnify (ie, compensate) the insureds for any resulting losses. This means that an insurer is only responsible for title defects that: are not disclosed in the insurance policy (the county tax lien) and affect the named insureds (the woman). Therefore, the insurance company is not obligated to pay the friend's claim since she was not named in the insurance policy (Choice C). (Choice B) An owner's title insurance policy remains in effect so long as the named insured (or his/her heirs) owns the property or conveys it by warranty deed. As a result, the woman's policy did expire when she conveyed the property by quitclaim deed. (Choice D) A covenant against encumbrances promises that there are no outstanding claims on, or interests in, the property (eg, mortgages, tax liens) other than those disclosed in the deed. But since this covenant only appears in warranty deeds, it did not appear in the quitclaim deed that the woman gave the friend. Therefore, the woman cannot be held liable under this covenant.\n\nEducational objective: Title insurance policies only protect named insureds who are affected by an undisclosed title defect. Therefore, insurance companies are not required to indemnify persons whose names do not appear on the policy.\n\nReferences\n43 Am. Jur. 2d Insurance § 518 (2018) (title insurance).","educationalObjective":"Title insurance policies only protect named insureds who are affected by an undisclosed title defect. Therefore, insurance companies are not required to indemnify persons whose names do not appear on the policy.","references":"43 Am. Jur. 2d Insurance § 518 (2018) (title insurance).","rule":"Title insurance policies only protect named insureds who are affected by an undisclosed title defect. Therefore, insurance companies are not required to indemnify persons whose names do not appear on the policy.","irac":{"issue":"Is the title insurance company obligated to pay the friend's claim?","rule":"Title insurance policies only protect named insureds who are affected by an undisclosed title defect. Therefore, insurance companies are not required to indemnify persons whose names do not appear on the policy.","analysis":"Title insurance policies\n\n(protect against undisclosed title defects)\n\nLosses covered Duration of coverage\n\nOwner's Reduction in full market While insured owns property AND after policy value of property insured conveys by warranty deed\n\nLender's Reduction in value or Ends once mortgage is paid in full or policy priority of mortgage otherwise discharged\n\nBoth owner's and lender's title insurance policies protect insureds—named persons covered by the policy—from undisclosed title defects by requiring the insurer to indemnify (ie, compensate) the insureds for any resulting losses. This means that an insurer is only responsible for title defects that: are not disclosed in the insurance policy (the county tax lien) and affect the named insureds (the woman). Therefore, the insurance company is not obligated to pay the friend's claim since she was not named in the insurance policy (Choice C). (Choice B) An owner's title insurance policy remains in effect so long as the named insured (or his/her heirs) owns the property or conveys it by warranty deed. As a result, the woman's policy did expire when she conveyed the property by quitclaim deed. (Choice D) A covenant against encumbrances promises that there are no outstanding claims on, or interests in, the property (eg, mortgages, tax liens) other than those disclosed in the deed. But since this covenant only appears in warranty deeds, it did not appear in the quitclaim deed that the woman gave the friend. Therefore, the woman cannot be held liable under this covenant.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the friend is not a named insured on the title insurance policy."},"questionZh":"两年前，一名开发商通过保修契约将一块地块的所有权转让给了一位女士。该妇女在交割前购买了业主的产权保险单。由于产权保险公司的错误，产权承诺和产权保单未能将阻碍该地块的县税收留置权列为保险范围的例外情况。去年，该女子通过无偿债权契约以便宜的价格将该地块转让给一位朋友，该朋友支付了现金，没有进行产权搜索，也没有购买产权保险。随后，该县启动了取消税收留置权的程序，这位朋友向该女子的产权保险公司提出索赔，要求该公司偿还她的税收留置权金额。产权保险公司有义务支付朋友的索赔吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该朋友不是产权保险单上的指定被保险人。","B":"不会，因为当该妇女将土地转让给朋友时，产权保险单尚未到期。","C":"是的，因为税收留置权没有被列为产权保险单上的例外情况。","D":"是的，因为该妇女要因违反产权负担契约而对朋友承担责任，而产权保单应涵盖朋友的潜在损失。"},"explanationZh":"产权保险单\n\n（防止未公开的所有权缺陷）\n\n承保损失 承保期限\n\n业主在整个市场中的减免 当被保险人拥有财产时并且在被保险财产的保单价值通过保修契约转让后\n\n贷款人的价值减少或一旦抵押贷款全额支付或抵押贷款的保单优先权终止，则以其他方式解除\n\n业主和贷方的产权保险保单都要求保险公司赔偿（即补偿）被保险人所造成的任何损失，从而保护被保险人（保险单所涵盖的指定人员）免受未公开的产权缺陷的影响。这意味着保险公司仅对以下所有权缺陷负责： 未在保险单中披露（县税收留置权）并影响指定的被保险人（女性）。因此，保险公司没有义务支付这位朋友的索赔，因为保单上没有她的名字（选项C）。 （选择 B）只要指定的被保险人（或其继承人）拥有该财产或通过保修契约转让该财产，业主的产权保险保单就一直有效。结果，当这位女士通过无偿债权契据转让财产时，她的保单就到期了。 （选择 D） 产权负担契约承诺除契约中披露的内容外，不存在对财产的未决索赔或权益（例如抵押、税收留置权）。但由于该契约只出现在保修契约中，因此并没有出现在该女子给朋友的无偿索赔契约中。因此，该妇女不能根据该公约承担责任。\n\n教育目标：产权保险保单仅保护受未公开产权缺陷影响的指定被保险人。因此，保险公司无需对保单上未出现姓名的人员进行赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n43 上午。尤尔。 2d 保险 § 518 (2018)（产权保险）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"产权保险保单仅保护受未公开产权缺陷影响的指定被保险人。因此，保险公司无需对保单上未出现姓名的人员进行赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"产权保险公司有义务支付朋友的索赔吗？","rule":"产权保险保单仅保护受未公开产权缺陷影响的指定被保险人。因此，保险公司无需对保单上未出现姓名的人员进行赔偿。","analysis":"产权保险单\n\n（防止未公开的所有权缺陷）\n\n承保损失 承保期限\n\n业主在整个市场中的减免 当被保险人拥有财产时并且在被保险财产的保单价值通过保修契约转让后\n\n贷款人的价值减少或一旦抵押贷款全额支付或抵押贷款的保单优先权终止，则以其他方式解除\n\n业主和贷方的产权保险保单都要求保险公司赔偿（即补偿）被保险人所造成的任何损失，从而保护被保险人（保险单所涵盖的指定人员）免受未公开的产权缺陷的影响。这意味着保险公司仅对以下所有权缺陷负责： 未在保险单中披露（县税收留置权）并影响指定的被保险人（女性）。因此，保险公司没有义务支付这位朋友的索赔，因为保单上没有她的名字（选项C）。 （选择 B）只要指定的被保险人（或其继承人）拥有该财产或通过保修契约转让该财产，业主的产权保险保单就一直有效。结果，当这位女士通过无偿债权契据转让财产时，她的保单就到期了。 （选择 D） 产权负担契约承诺除契约中披露的内容外，不存在对财产的未决索赔或权益（例如抵押、税收留置权）。但由于该契约只出现在保修契约中，因此并没有出现在该女子给朋友的无偿索赔契约中。因此，该妇女不能根据该公约承担责任。\n\n教育目标：产权保险保单仅保护受未公开产权缺陷影响的指定被保险人。因此，保险公司无需对保单上未出现姓名的人员进行赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n43 上午。尤尔。 2d 保险 § 518 (2018)（产权保险）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为该朋友不是产权保险单上的指定被保险人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"covenant against encumbrances","ipa":"/kˈəvənənt ʌɡˈɛnst en'kʌmbrənsɪz/","syllables":"covenant against en·cum·brances","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"无负担担保：出让人保证土地不存在未披露抵押、地役权等负担","contextualMeaning":"无负担担保：出让人保证土地不存在未披露抵押、地役权等负担","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the woman is liable to the friend for breach of the covenant against encumbrances, and the title policy should cover the friend's potential loss.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":76,"sourceQuestionPage":222,"sourceQuestionPages":[222],"sourceExplanationPages":[223,224],"sourceAnswerPages":[223,224],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-077","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A credit card company obtained and properly filed a judgment against a man after he failed to pay a $10,000 debt. A statute in the jurisdiction provides as follows: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for 10 years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" Two years later, the man purchased land for $200,000. He made a down payment of $20,000 and borrowed the remaining $180,000 from a bank. The bank loan was secured by a mortgage on the land. Immediately after the closing, the deed to the man was recorded first, and the bank's mortgage was recorded second. Five months later, the man defaulted on the mortgage loan and the bank initiated judicial foreclosure proceedings. After receiving notice of the proceedings, the credit card company filed a motion to have its judgment lien declared to be the first lien on the land. Is the credit card company's motion likely to be granted?","choices":{"A":"No, because the bank's mortgage secured a loan used to purchase the land.","B":"No, because the man's down payment exceeded the amount of his debt to the credit card company.","C":"Yes, because the bank had constructive notice of the judgment lien.","D":"Yes, because the bank is a third-party lender and not the seller of the land."},"answer":"A","explanation":"In foreclosure proceedings, lien priority is generally determined by the date a lien is recorded (ie, first in time, first in right). But purchase-money mortgages (PMMs) are an exception to this rule. These mortgages have super priority over all other liens— regardless of whether the PMM or those liens are recorded—because PMMs are granted to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property. Here, the credit card company acquired a judgment lien to secure repayment of the man's $10,000 debt. Two years later, the bank acquired a PMM because it received a mortgage to secure the $180,000 loan used to purchase the land. Since that PMM has super priority over all other liens—including a judgment lien that was first in time—the court will likely deny the credit card company's motion to have its lien declared first on the land. (Choice B) Whether the man could have paid off the judgment lien with the money that he used for the down payment is irrelevant to the bank's priority over the lien. (Choice C) The bank likely had constructive (ie, record or inquiry) notice of the credit card company's judgment lien because that lien was properly filed. But this has no effect on the super priority of the bank's PMM. (Choice D) PMMs can be given to third-party lenders (ie, third-party PMMs) and sellers of the land (ie, vendor PMMs). But any PMM executed at the same time as the purchase of the mortgaged real property has priority over any other claim, including the credit card company's previously filed judgment lien.\n\nEducational objective: A purchase-money mortgage (PMM) has super priority over all other liens because it is granted to secure the purchase price of mortgaged property. As a result, PMMs trump the \"first in time, first in right\" rule that sets priority based on the date a lien is recorded.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (purchase-money mortgages).","educationalObjective":"A purchase-money mortgage (PMM) has super priority over all other liens because it is granted to secure the purchase price of mortgaged property. As a result, PMMs trump the \"first in time, first in right\" rule that sets priority based on the date a lien is recorded.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 7.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (purchase-money mortgages).","rule":"A purchase-money mortgage (PMM) has super priority over all other liens because it is granted to secure the purchase price of mortgaged property. As a result, PMMs trump the \"first in time, first in right\" rule that sets priority based on the date a lien is recorded.","irac":{"issue":"Is the credit card company's motion likely to be granted?","rule":"A purchase-money mortgage (PMM) has super priority over all other liens because it is granted to secure the purchase price of mortgaged property. As a result, PMMs trump the \"first in time, first in right\" rule that sets priority based on the date a lien is recorded.","analysis":"In foreclosure proceedings, lien priority is generally determined by the date a lien is recorded (ie, first in time, first in right). But purchase-money mortgages (PMMs) are an exception to this rule. These mortgages have super priority over all other liens— regardless of whether the PMM or those liens are recorded—because PMMs are granted to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property. Here, the credit card company acquired a judgment lien to secure repayment of the man's $10,000 debt. Two years later, the bank acquired a PMM because it received a mortgage to secure the $180,000 loan used to purchase the land. Since that PMM has super priority over all other liens—including a judgment lien that was first in time—the court will likely deny the credit card company's motion to have its lien declared first on the land. (Choice B) Whether the man could have paid off the judgment lien with the money that he used for the down payment is irrelevant to the bank's priority over the lien. (Choice C) The bank likely had constructive (ie, record or inquiry) notice of the credit card company's judgment lien because that lien was properly filed. But this has no effect on the super priority of the bank's PMM. (Choice D) PMMs can be given to third-party lenders (ie, third-party PMMs) and sellers of the land (ie, vendor PMMs). But any PMM executed at the same time as the purchase of the mortgaged real property has priority over any other claim, including the credit card company's previously filed judgment lien.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the bank's mortgage secured a loan used to purchase the land."},"questionZh":"一名男子未能偿还 10,000 美元的债务后，一家信用卡公司获得了该男子的判决，并对其做出了适当的判决。该司法管辖区的一项法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起 10 年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”两年后，该男子以 20 万美元购买了土地。他支付了 20,000 美元的首付，并向银行借了剩余的 180,000 美元。银行贷款以土地抵押作为担保。交割后，首先记录的是该男子的契约，其次记录的是银行的抵押贷款。五个月后，该男子拖欠抵押贷款，银行启动了司法止赎程序。收到诉讼通知后，信用卡公司提出动议，要求将其判决留置权宣布为土地上的第一留置权。信用卡公司的动议有可能获得批准吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为银行的抵押贷款担保了用于购买土地的贷款。","B":"不，因为该男子的首付超过了他欠信用卡公司的债务金额。","C":"是的，因为银行有判决留置权的推定通知。","D":"是的，因为银行是第三方贷款人，而不是土地卖方。"},"explanationZh":"在止赎程序中，留置权优先权通常由留置权记录日期确定（即时间第一、权利第一）。但购房抵押贷款 (PMM) 是该规则的一个例外。这些抵押贷款比所有其他留置权拥有超级优先权（无论是否记录了 PMM 或这些留置权），因为授予 PMM 是为了确保抵押财产的购买价格。在此，信用卡公司获得了判决留置权，以确保该男子偿还 10,000 美元的债务。两年后，该银行收购了一家 PMM，因为它收到了抵押贷款，以确保用于购买土地的 180,000 美元贷款。由于 PMM 比所有其他留置权（包括当时最先的判决留置权）具有超级优先权，因此法院可能会拒绝信用卡公司要求首先宣布其土地留置权的动议。 （选项B）该男子是否可以用他用于首付的钱清偿判决留置权，与银行对留置权的优先权无关。 （选择C） 银行可能拥有信用卡公司判决留置权的推定（即记录或查询）通知，因为该留置权已正确提交。但这对银行PMM的超级优先级没有影响。 （选择 D）PMM 可以提供给第三方贷方（即第三方 PMM）和土地卖家（即供应商 PMM）。但与购买抵押不动产同时执行的任何 PMM 均优先于任何其他索赔，包括信用卡公司之前提交的判决留置权。\n\n教育目标： 购房抵押贷款 (PMM) 比所有其他留置权具有超级优先权，因为授予它是为了确保抵押财产的购买价格。因此，PMM 胜过“时间在先、权利在先”的规则，该规则根据留置权的记录日期设置优先级。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（购房抵押贷款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"购房款抵押贷款 (PMM) 比所有其他留置权具有超级优先权，因为它的授予是为了确保抵押财产的购买价格。因此，PMM 胜过“时间在先、权利在先”的规则，该规则根据留置权的记录日期设置优先级。","iracZh":{"issue":"信用卡公司的动议有可能获得批准吗？","rule":"购房款抵押贷款 (PMM) 比所有其他留置权具有超级优先权，因为它的授予是为了确保抵押财产的购买价格。因此，PMM 胜过“时间在先、权利在先”的规则，该规则根据留置权的记录日期设置优先级。","analysis":"在止赎程序中，留置权优先权通常由留置权记录日期确定（即时间第一、权利第一）。但购房抵押贷款 (PMM) 是该规则的一个例外。这些抵押贷款比所有其他留置权拥有超级优先权（无论是否记录了 PMM 或这些留置权），因为授予 PMM 是为了确保抵押财产的购买价格。在此，信用卡公司获得了判决留置权，以确保该男子偿还 10,000 美元的债务。两年后，该银行收购了一家 PMM，因为它收到了抵押贷款，以确保用于购买土地的 180,000 美元贷款。由于 PMM 比所有其他留置权（包括当时最先的判决留置权）具有超级优先权，因此法院可能会拒绝信用卡公司要求首先宣布其土地留置权的动议。 （选项B）该男子是否可以用他用于首付的钱清偿判决留置权，与银行对留置权的优先权无关。 （选择C） 银行可能拥有信用卡公司判决留置权的推定（即记录或查询）通知，因为该留置权已正确提交。但这对银行PMM的超级优先级没有影响。 （选择 D）PMM 可以提供给第三方贷方（即第三方 PMM）和土地卖家（即供应商 PMM）。但与购买抵押不动产同时执行的任何 PMM 均优先于任何其他索赔，包括信用卡公司之前提交的判决留置权。\n\n教育目标： 购房抵押贷款 (PMM) 比所有其他留置权具有超级优先权，因为授予它是为了确保抵押财产的购买价格。因此，PMM 胜过“时间在先、权利在先”的规则，该规则根据留置权的记录日期设置优先级。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 7.2（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（购房抵押贷款）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为银行的抵押贷款担保了用于购买土地的贷款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-a5b820bdb2830699"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":77,"sourceQuestionPage":225,"sourceQuestionPages":[225],"sourceExplanationPages":[226,227],"sourceAnswerPages":[226,227],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-078","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"The owner of a residence entered into a contract of sale with a couple. After a down payment, the remaining purchase price of the residence was $500,000. The couple was only able to secure a mortgage loan from a bank for $400,000, so the owner-seller agreed to advance the couple the difference in exchange for a mortgage on the residence. At closing, the couple paid the owner-seller $400,000 from the proceeds of their bank loan. The couple also signed notes that obligated them to pay $400,000 to the bank and $100,000 to the owner-seller, and to give the bank and the owner-seller mortgages on the residence to secure those notes. The owner-seller, agreeing to the bank's demand, subordinated her mortgage to the bank's mortgage. The bank recorded its mortgage one day after the owner- seller recorded her mortgage. Several years later, the couple stopped paying on both notes at a time when the principal balances of their obligations were $350,000 to the bank and $85,000 to the owner- seller. The owner-seller has filed a complaint for foreclosure on her mortgage. The recording act of the applicable jurisdiction reads, \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice who shall first record.\" Is the owner-seller required to make the bank a party to this action?","choices":{"A":"No, because the owner-seller subordinated her mortgage to the bank's mortgage.","B":"No, because the remaining principal amount of the couple's obligation to the bank exceeds that of the owner-seller.","C":"Yes, because the owner-seller recorded her mortgage before the bank recorded its mortgage.","D":"Yes, because the owner-seller's purchase-money mortgage has priority over the bank's mortgage."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The owner-seller has filed a complaint for foreclosure on her mortgage, thereby seeking a foreclosure conducted by a judicially supervised sale. Under this method of foreclosure, the foreclosing mortgagee (ie, lender): must give notice to the holders of any junior interests in the property so that they can participate or send a representative—otherwise, the junior-interest holder's interest will remain after the sale\n\nmay, but need not, join others who have an interest in the property (eg, senior-mortgage holder) or who are liable on the debt (eg, guarantor) as proper, but not necessary, parties*\n\nIn foreclosure proceedings, lien priority is determined by the \"first in time, first in right\" rule unless the jurisdiction has a recording act—eg, a race-notice act (as seen here). Additionally, mortgages given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property (ie, purchase-money mortgages) have super priority over all other liens. However, a senior-mortgage holder can always agree to subordinate his/her interest to that of a junior-mortgage holder, in which case the junior mortgage receives priority. Here, the owner-seller and the bank acquired purchase-money mortgages because their loan proceeds were used to purchase the residence. Although the facts do not indicate which mortgage was created first, a seller's purchase-money mortgage generally has priority over a lender's purchase-money mortgage—regardless of who recorded first. But since the owner-seller agreed to subordinate her mortgage, the bank has the senior mortgage (Choices C & D). Therefore, the bank need not be made a party to this foreclosure action. *That is because a valid foreclosure only terminates interests in the foreclosed property that are junior to the interest being foreclosed. It has no effect on any other interests. (Choice B) The size of the obligation secured by a mortgage does not determine its priority with regard to other mortgages on the same property.\n\nEducational objective: For a judicially supervised foreclosure sale, the foreclosing mortgagee must give notice to the holders of any junior interests in the property. Any others who have an interest in the property (eg, senior-mortgage holder) or who are liable on the debt (eg, guarantor) may be joined as proper but unnecessary parties.\n\nReferences\n55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 592 (2019) (discussing the requirement to notify junior lienholders of the foreclosure action).","educationalObjective":"For a judicially supervised foreclosure sale, the foreclosing mortgagee must give notice to the holders of any junior interests in the property. Any others who have an interest in the property (eg, senior-mortgage holder) or who are liable on the debt (eg, guarantor) may be joined as proper but unnecessary parties.","references":"55 Am. Jur. 2d Mortgages § 592 (2019) (discussing the requirement to notify junior lienholders of the foreclosure action).","rule":"For a judicially supervised foreclosure sale, the foreclosing mortgagee must give notice to the holders of any junior interests in the property. Any others who have an interest in the property (eg, senior-mortgage holder) or who are liable on the debt (eg, guarantor) may be joined as proper but unnecessary parties.","irac":{"issue":"The owner of a residence entered into a contract of sale with a couple. After a down payment, the remaining purchase price of the residence was $500,000. The couple was only able to secure a mortgage loan from a bank for $400,000, so the owner-seller agreed to advance the couple the difference in exchange for a mortgage on the residence. At closing, the couple paid the owner-seller $400,000 from the proceeds of their bank loan. The couple also signed notes that obligated them to pay $400,000 to the bank and $100,000 to the owner-seller, and to give the bank and the owner-seller mortgages on the residence to secure those notes. The owner-seller, agreeing to the bank's demand, subordinated her mortgage to the bank's mortgage. The bank recorded its mortgage one day after the owner- seller recorded her mortgage. Several years later, the couple stopped paying on both notes at a time when the principal balances of their obligations were $350,000 to the bank and $85,000 to the owner- seller. The owner-seller has filed a complaint for foreclosure on her mortgage. The recording act of the applicable jurisdiction reads, \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice who shall first record.\" Is the owner-seller required to make the bank a party to this action?","rule":"For a judicially supervised foreclosure sale, the foreclosing mortgagee must give notice to the holders of any junior interests in the property. Any others who have an interest in the property (eg, senior-mortgage holder) or who are liable on the debt (eg, guarantor) may be joined as proper but unnecessary parties.","analysis":"The owner-seller has filed a complaint for foreclosure on her mortgage, thereby seeking a foreclosure conducted by a judicially supervised sale. Under this method of foreclosure, the foreclosing mortgagee (ie, lender): must give notice to the holders of any junior interests in the property so that they can participate or send a representative—otherwise, the junior-interest holder's interest will remain after the sale\n\nmay, but need not, join others who have an interest in the property (eg, senior-mortgage holder) or who are liable on the debt (eg, guarantor) as proper, but not necessary, parties*\n\nIn foreclosure proceedings, lien priority is determined by the \"first in time, first in right\" rule unless the jurisdiction has a recording act—eg, a race-notice act (as seen here). Additionally, mortgages given to secure the purchase price of the mortgaged property (ie, purchase-money mortgages) have super priority over all other liens. However, a senior-mortgage holder can always agree to subordinate his/her interest to that of a junior-mortgage holder, in which case the junior mortgage receives priority. Here, the owner-seller and the bank acquired purchase-money mortgages because their loan proceeds were used to purchase the residence. Although the facts do not indicate which mortgage was created first, a seller's purchase-money mortgage generally has priority over a lender's purchase-money mortgage—regardless of who recorded first. But since the owner-seller agreed to subordinate her mortgage, the bank has the senior mortgage (Choices C & D). Therefore, the bank need not be made a party to this foreclosure action. *That is because a valid foreclosure only terminates interests in the foreclosed property that are junior to the interest being foreclosed. It has no effect on any other interests. (Choice B) The size of the obligation secured by a mortgage does not determine its priority with regard to other mortgages on the same property.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the owner-seller subordinated her mortgage to the bank's mortgage."},"questionZh":"住宅的业主与一对夫妇签订了一份销售合同。支付首付后，该住宅的剩余购买价格为 50 万美元。这对夫妇只能从银行获得 40 万美元的抵押贷款，因此业主卖家同意向这对夫妇预付差额，以换取该住宅的抵押贷款。成交时，这对夫妇从银行贷款中向业主兼卖家支付了 40 万美元。这对夫妇还签署了票据，要求他们向银行支付 40 万美元，向业主卖家支付 10 万美元，并向银行和业主卖家提供住宅抵押贷款，以确保这些票据的安全。业主兼卖家同意银行的要求，将她的抵押贷款置于银行的抵押贷款之下。业主卖家记录抵押贷款一天后，银行记录了抵押贷款。几年后，这对夫妇停止支付两张票据，当时他们对银行的债务本金余额为 350,000 美元，对业主/卖家的债务本金余额为 85,000 美元。业主兼卖家已就其抵押贷款取消抵押品赎回权提出申诉。适用司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“任何不动产转让或抵押均不得有利于后续购买者的价值，且无需通知谁应首先记录。”业主/卖家是否需要让银行成为该行动的一方？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为业主兼卖方将她的抵押贷款从属于银行的抵押贷款。","B":"不会，因为这对夫妇对银行的债务的剩余本金超过了业主-卖方的债务。","C":"是的，因为业主/卖家在银行记录抵押贷款之前记录了她的抵押贷款。","D":"是的，因为业主-卖家的购房款抵押贷款比银行的抵押贷款具有优先权。"},"explanationZh":"业主兼卖方已就其抵押贷款取消抵押品赎回权提出申诉，从而寻求通过司法监督的销售进行取消抵押品赎回权。在这种取消抵押品赎回权的方法下，取消抵押品赎回权的抵押权人（即贷款人）： 必须向财产中任何次级权益的持有人发出通知，以便他们能够参与或派出代表，否则，次级权益持有人的权益将在出售后保留\n\n可以（但不必）加入对财产有兴趣的其他人（例如高级抵押贷款持有人）或对债务负有责任的人（例如担保人）作为适当但非必要的当事人*\n\n在止赎程序中，留置权优先权由“时间在先、权利在先”规则确定，除非司法管辖区有记录行为，例如竞赛通知行为（如此处所示）。此外，为确保抵押财产的购买价格而提供的抵押（即购房款抵押）比所有其他留置权具有超级优先权。然而，高级抵押贷款持有人始终可以同意将其利益置于次级抵押贷款持有人的利益之上，在这种情况下，初级抵押贷款获得优先权。在这里，业主兼卖方和银行获得了购房款抵押贷款，因为他们的贷款收益用于购买住宅。尽管事实并没有表明哪个抵押贷款最先设立，但卖方的购房款抵押贷款通常优先于贷款人的购房款抵押贷款——无论谁先记录。但由于业主兼卖方同意将她的抵押贷款从属，银行拥有高级抵押贷款（选择 C ​​和 D）。因此，银行不必成为该止赎行动的一方。 *这是因为有效的止赎仅终止止赎财产中比被止赎权益次要的权益。它不会影响任何其他利益。 （选择 B） 抵押贷款担保债务的大小并不决定其相对于同一财产的其他抵押贷款的优先权。\n\n教育目标：对于受司法监督的丧失抵押品赎回权的出售，丧失抵押品赎回权的抵押权人必须向财产中任何次级权益的持有人发出通知。任何其他对财产有兴趣的人（例如高级抵押贷款持有人）或对债务负有责任的人（例如担保人）可以作为适当但不必要的当事人加入。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 592 (2019)（讨论将取消抵押品赎回权行动通知初级留置权人的要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对于受司法监督的丧失抵押品赎回权的出售，丧失抵押品赎回权的抵押权人必须向财产中任何次级权益的持有人发出通知。任何其他对财产有兴趣的人（例如高级抵押贷款持有人）或对债务负有责任的人（例如担保人）可以作为适当但不必要的当事人加入。","iracZh":{"issue":"住宅的业主与一对夫妇签订了一份销售合同。支付首付后，该住宅的剩余购买价格为 50 万美元。这对夫妇只能从银行获得 40 万美元的抵押贷款，因此业主卖家同意向这对夫妇预付差额，以换取该住宅的抵押贷款。成交时，这对夫妇从银行贷款中向业主兼卖家支付了 40 万美元。这对夫妇还签署了票据，要求他们向银行支付 40 万美元，向业主卖家支付 10 万美元，并向银行和业主卖家提供住宅抵押贷款，以确保这些票据的安全。业主兼卖家同意银行的要求，将她的抵押贷款置于银行的抵押贷款之下。业主卖家记录抵押贷款一天后，银行记录了抵押贷款。几年后，这对夫妇停止支付两张票据，当时他们对银行的债务本金余额为 350,000 美元，对业主/卖家的债务本金余额为 85,000 美元。业主兼卖家已就其抵押贷款取消抵押品赎回权提出申诉。适用司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“任何不动产转让或抵押均不得有利于后续购买者的价值，且无需通知谁应首先记录。”业主/卖家是否需要让银行成为该行动的一方？","rule":"对于受司法监督的丧失抵押品赎回权的出售，丧失抵押品赎回权的抵押权人必须向财产中任何次级权益的持有人发出通知。任何其他对财产有兴趣的人（例如高级抵押贷款持有人）或对债务负有责任的人（例如担保人）可以作为适当但不必要的当事人加入。","analysis":"业主兼卖方已就其抵押贷款取消抵押品赎回权提出申诉，从而寻求通过司法监督的销售进行取消抵押品赎回权。在这种取消抵押品赎回权的方法下，取消抵押品赎回权的抵押权人（即贷款人）： 必须向财产中任何次级权益的持有人发出通知，以便他们能够参与或派出代表，否则，次级权益持有人的权益将在出售后保留\n\n可以（但不必）加入对财产有兴趣的其他人（例如高级抵押贷款持有人）或对债务负有责任的人（例如担保人）作为适当但非必要的当事人*\n\n在止赎程序中，留置权优先权由“时间在先、权利在先”规则确定，除非司法管辖区有记录行为，例如竞赛通知行为（如此处所示）。此外，为确保抵押财产的购买价格而提供的抵押（即购房款抵押）比所有其他留置权具有超级优先权。然而，高级抵押贷款持有人始终可以同意将其利益置于次级抵押贷款持有人的利益之上，在这种情况下，初级抵押贷款获得优先权。在这里，业主兼卖方和银行获得了购房款抵押贷款，因为他们的贷款收益用于购买住宅。尽管事实并没有表明哪个抵押贷款最先设立，但卖方的购房款抵押贷款通常优先于贷款人的购房款抵押贷款——无论谁先记录。但由于业主兼卖方同意将她的抵押贷款从属，银行拥有高级抵押贷款（选择 C ​​和 D）。因此，银行不必成为该止赎行动的一方。 *这是因为有效的止赎仅终止止赎财产中比被止赎权益次要的权益。它不会影响任何其他利益。 （选择 B） 抵押贷款担保债务的大小并不决定其相对于同一财产的其他抵押贷款的优先权。\n\n教育目标：对于受司法监督的丧失抵押品赎回权的出售，丧失抵押品赎回权的抵押权人必须向财产中任何次级权益的持有人发出通知。任何其他对财产有兴趣的人（例如高级抵押贷款持有人）或对债务负有责任的人（例如担保人）可以作为适当但不必要的当事人加入。\n\n参考文献\n上午 55 点。尤尔。 2d Mortgages § 592 (2019)（讨论将取消抵押品赎回权行动通知初级留置权人的要求）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为业主兼卖方将她的抵押贷款从属于银行的抵押贷款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-0e479142618dd937"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":78,"sourceQuestionPage":228,"sourceQuestionPages":[228],"sourceExplanationPages":[229,230],"sourceAnswerPages":[229,230],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-079","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"An owner owned a single-family residence. Fifteen years ago, the owner conveyed a life estate in the house to a landlord. Fourteen years ago, the landlord, who had taken possession of the house, leased the house to a tenant for a term of 15 years at the monthly rental of $500. Eleven years ago, the landlord died intestate leaving her son as her sole heir. The tenant regularly paid rent to the landlord and, after the landlord's death, to her son until last month. The period in which to acquire title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 10 years. In an appropriate action, the tenant, the owner, and the landlord's son each asserted ownership of the house. Who now owns the house?","choices":{"A":"The landlord's son, because the landlord asserted a claim adverse to the owner when she executed a lease to the tenant.","B":"The landlord's son, because the tenant's occupation was attributable to the son and the landlord died 11 years ago.","C":"The owner, because the owner held a reversion and the landlord has died.","D":"The tenant, because of his physical occupancy and because his term ended with the landlord's death."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A person may acquire title to another's land by adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is: Open and notorious – apparent to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence by the adverse possessor or another whose possession is attributable to the adverse possessor (as seen here) Non-permissive – hostile and adverse Here, the owner conveyed a life estate in the house to the landlord, who leased it to the tenant. Ownership of the house reverted to the owner when the landlord died. But since the tenant remained in the house and paid rent to the landlord's son, the tenant's possession was attributable to the son. And because the house was visibly and exclusively occupied by the tenant for the 10-year statutory period without the owner's permission, the son acquired title to the house by adverse possession. (Choice A) Life estates are alienable, which means that the life tenant can freely convey all or part of his/her interest to another (eg, by conveying a right of possession through a lease). Therefore, since the landlord (life tenant) was entitled to lease the house to the tenant during her life tenancy, she did not assert a claim adverse to the owner by doing so. (Choice C) A reversion is a future interest retained by the grantor. Although the owner had a reversionary interest in the house (which became possessory when the landlord died), the owner lost that interest due to the son's adverse possession. (Choice D) Although the tenant continued to occupy the house after the landlord's death for 11 years, he paid rent to the landlord's son. As a result, the tenant's possession was attributable to the son—not himself.\n\nEducational objective: To acquire title by adverse possession, a person's possession of another's land must be open and notorious, continuous for the statutory period, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive (OCEAN). Actual presence can occur through another so long as their presence on the land is attributable to the adverse possessor.","educationalObjective":"To acquire title by adverse possession, a person's possession of another's land must be open and notorious, continuous for the statutory period, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive (OCEAN). Actual presence can occur through another so long as their presence on the land is attributable to the adverse possessor.","references":"","rule":"To acquire title by adverse possession, a person's possession of another's land must be open and notorious, continuous for the statutory period, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive (OCEAN). Actual presence can occur through another so long as their presence on the land is attributable to the adverse possessor.","irac":{"issue":"Who now owns the house?","rule":"To acquire title by adverse possession, a person's possession of another's land must be open and notorious, continuous for the statutory period, exclusive, actual, and non-permissive (OCEAN). Actual presence can occur through another so long as their presence on the land is attributable to the adverse possessor.","analysis":"A person may acquire title to another's land by adverse possession if his/her possession of the land is: Open and notorious – apparent to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence by the adverse possessor or another whose possession is attributable to the adverse possessor (as seen here) Non-permissive – hostile and adverse Here, the owner conveyed a life estate in the house to the landlord, who leased it to the tenant. Ownership of the house reverted to the owner when the landlord died. But since the tenant remained in the house and paid rent to the landlord's son, the tenant's possession was attributable to the son. And because the house was visibly and exclusively occupied by the tenant for the 10-year statutory period without the owner's permission, the son acquired title to the house by adverse possession. (Choice A) Life estates are alienable, which means that the life tenant can freely convey all or part of his/her interest to another (eg, by conveying a right of possession through a lease). Therefore, since the landlord (life tenant) was entitled to lease the house to the tenant during her life tenancy, she did not assert a claim adverse to the owner by doing so. (Choice C) A reversion is a future interest retained by the grantor. Although the owner had a reversionary interest in the house (which became possessory when the landlord died), the owner lost that interest due to the son's adverse possession. (Choice D) Although the tenant continued to occupy the house after the landlord's death for 11 years, he paid rent to the landlord's son. As a result, the tenant's possession was attributable to the son—not himself.","conclusion":"Answer B: The landlord's son, because the tenant's occupation was attributable to the son and the landlord died 11 years ago."},"questionZh":"一位业主拥有一栋单户住宅。十五年前，房主将房子里的终身财产转让给了房东。十四年前，拥有该房屋的房东将房屋出租给一名租户，租期为 15 年，月租为 500 美元。十一年前，房东去世时没有留下遗嘱，留下她的儿子作为她唯一的继承人。租客定期向房东支付租金，并在房东去世后向她的儿子支付租金，直到上个月。在管辖区内通过逆权占有取得所有权的期限为10年。在适当的行动中，租户、业主和房东的儿子各自主张对房屋的所有权。房子现在归谁所有？","choicesZh":{"A":"房东的儿子，因为房东在向房客执行租约时提出了对业主不利的索赔。","B":"房东的儿子，因为租客的职业归属于儿子，而房东已于11年前去世。","C":"业主，因为业主举行了归还，并且房东已经去世。","D":"租客，因为他的实际占用以及他的租期随着房东的去世而结束。"},"explanationZh":"一个人可以通过逆权占有获得他人土地的所有权，如果他/她对土地的占有符合以下条件： 公开且臭名昭著 - 对合理的所有者来说是明显的 连续的 - 在法定期限内不间断 排他性 - 不与所有者共享 实际 - 逆权占有者或可归因于逆权占有者的占有的其他人实际存在（如此处所示） 不允许 - 敌对和不利 这里，所有者将房屋中的终身财产转让给房东，房东将其出租给租户。房东去世后，房子的所有权就归还给房主。但由于租客仍留在房子里并向房东的儿子支付租金，因此租客的财产归属于儿子。由于该房屋在未经业主许可的情况下，在10年的法定期限内明显由租户独占，儿子通过逆权管有取得了该房屋的所有权。 （选项A）终身财产是可转让的，这意味着终身承租人可以自由地将其全部或部分权益转让给他人（例如，通过租赁转让占有权）。因此，由于房东（终身租客）有权在租客的终身租约期间将房屋出租给租客，因此她并没有这样做对业主提出不利的索赔。 （选择 C） 归还是授予人保留的未来权益。尽管房主对房屋拥有复归权益（房东去世后房屋成为占有），但由于儿子的逆权占有，房主失去了该权益。 （选项D）尽管房客在房东去世后仍继续占用该房屋11年，但他向房东的儿子支付租金。结果，租客的财产归属于儿子，而不是他自己。\n\n教育目标：要通过逆权占有获得所有权，一个人对他人土地的占有必须是公开的、臭名昭著的、在法定期限内持续的、排他的、实际的和不允许的（OCEAN）。实际存在可以通过他人发生，只要他们在土地上的存在可归因于逆权占有者。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要通过逆权占有获得所有权，一个人对他人土地的占有必须是公开的、臭名昭著的、在法定期限内持续的、排他的、实际的和不允许的（OCEAN）。实际存在可以通过他人发生，只要他们在土地上的存在可归因于逆权占有者。","iracZh":{"issue":"房子现在归谁所有？","rule":"要通过逆权占有获得所有权，一个人对他人土地的占有必须是公开的、臭名昭著的、在法定期限内持续的、排他的、实际的和不允许的（OCEAN）。实际存在可以通过他人发生，只要他们在土地上的存在可归因于逆权占有者。","analysis":"一个人可以通过逆权占有获得他人土地的所有权，如果他/她对土地的占有符合以下条件： 公开且臭名昭著 - 对合理的所有者来说是明显的 连续的 - 在法定期限内不间断 排他性 - 不与所有者共享 实际 - 逆权占有者或可归因于逆权占有者的占有的其他人实际存在（如此处所示） 不允许 - 敌对和不利 这里，所有者将房屋中的终身财产转让给房东，房东将其出租给租户。房东去世后，房子的所有权就归还给房主。但由于租客仍留在房子里并向房东的儿子支付租金，因此租客的财产归属于儿子。由于该房屋在未经业主许可的情况下，在10年的法定期限内明显由租户独占，儿子通过逆权管有取得了该房屋的所有权。 （选项A）终身财产是可转让的，这意味着终身承租人可以自由地将其全部或部分权益转让给他人（例如，通过租赁转让占有权）。因此，由于房东（终身租客）有权在租客的终身租约期间将房屋出租给租客，因此她并没有这样做对业主提出不利的索赔。 （选择 C） 归还是授予人保留的未来权益。尽管房主对房屋拥有复归权益（房东去世后房屋成为占有），但由于儿子的逆权占有，房主失去了该权益。 （选项D）尽管房客在房东去世后仍继续占用该房屋11年，但他向房东的儿子支付租金。结果，租客的财产归属于儿子，而不是他自己。\n\n教育目标：要通过逆权占有获得所有权，一个人对他人土地的占有必须是公开的、臭名昭著的、在法定期限内持续的、排他的、实际的和不允许的（OCEAN）。实际存在可以通过他人发生，只要他们在土地上的存在可归因于逆权占有者。","conclusion":"答案 B：房东的儿子，因为租客的职业归属于儿子，而房东已于11年前去世。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"reversion","ipa":"/ɹɪvˈɝʒən/","syllables":"re·ver·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 回复, 复原, 归还, 继承权；[化] 返硫","contextualMeaning":"复归权：较短地产终止后由出让人保留的未来权益","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:reversions","sourceSentence":"The owner, because the owner held a reversion and the landlord has died.","synonyms":["backsliding","lapse","lapsing","relapse"]},{"term":"exclusively","ipa":"/ɪksklˈusɪvli/","syllables":"ex·clu·sively","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 排他地, 仅仅, 专门, 只, 仅, 唯一地, 专有地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“exclusively”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"And because the house was visibly and exclusively occupied by the tenant for the 10-year statutory period without the owner's permission, the son acquired title to the house by adverse possession.","synonyms":["entirely","solely","alone","only"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-dbfd75fc19886cdd"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":79,"sourceQuestionPage":231,"sourceQuestionPages":[231],"sourceExplanationPages":[232,233],"sourceAnswerPages":[232,233],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-080","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"Five years ago, an investor who owned a vacant lot in a residential area borrowed $25,000 from a friend and gave the friend a note for $25,000 due in five years, secured by a mortgage on the lot. The friend neglected to record the mortgage. The fair market value of the lot was then $25,000. Three years ago, the investor discovered that the friend had not recorded his mortgage and in consideration of $50,000 conveyed the lot to a buyer. The fair market value of the lot was then $50,000. The buyer knew nothing of the friend's mortgage. One month thereafter, the friend discovered the sale to the buyer, recorded his $25,000 mortgage, and notified the buyer that he held a $25,000 mortgage on the lot. Two years ago, the buyer needed funds. Although she told her bank about the mortgage claimed by the investor's friend, the bank loaned her $15,000, and she gave the bank a note for $15,000 due in two years secured by a mortgage on the lot. The bank promptly recorded the mortgage. At that time, the fair market value of the lot was $75,000. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.\" Both notes are now due, and both the investor and the buyer have refused to pay. The lot is now worth only $50,000. What are the rights of the investor's friend and the bank in the lot?","choices":{"A":"Both mortgages are enforceable liens, and the friend's has priority because it was first recorded.","B":"Both mortgages are enforceable liens, but the bank's has priority because the buyer was an innocent purchaser for value.","C":"Only the bank's mortgage is an enforceable lien, because the buyer was an innocent purchaser for value.","D":"Only the friend's mortgage is an enforceable lien, because the bank had actual and constructive notice of the investor's fraud."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Recording acts determine the priority of competing interests in the same property. Most recording acts protect bona fide purchasers (BFPs or innocent purchasers for value)—ie, persons or entities who: pay value for an interest in property and lack notice of a prior interest in the property. In a jurisdiction with a notice recording act (as seen here), a BFP's property interest is always superior to an earlier competing property interest of which the BFP lacked notice. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is entitled to the same protection under the recording act as the BFP. This ultimately protects the BFP by ensuring that the BFP receives the full benefit of the purchase and is able to convey an interest in the property. Here, the buyer was a BFP because she knew nothing of the friend's prior unrecorded mortgage when the investor sold her the vacant lot. The buyer therefore had priority under the jurisdiction's notice act and took the lot free of the friend's mortgage. The buyer then mortgaged the lot to the bank. And although the buyer told the bank about the friend's prior mortgage (ie, the bank was not a BFP), the Shelter Rule entitles the bank to the buyer's protection under the recording act. As a result, only the bank has an enforceable mortgage on the lot (Choices A, B & D).\n\nEducational objective: Under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a bona fide purchaser (BFP) is entitled to the same protection under the jurisdiction's recording act as the BFP.","educationalObjective":"Under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a bona fide purchaser (BFP) is entitled to the same protection under the jurisdiction's recording act as the BFP.","references":"","rule":"Under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a bona fide purchaser (BFP) is entitled to the same protection under the jurisdiction's recording act as the BFP.","irac":{"issue":"What are the rights of the investor's friend and the bank in the lot?","rule":"Under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a bona fide purchaser (BFP) is entitled to the same protection under the jurisdiction's recording act as the BFP.","analysis":"Recording acts determine the priority of competing interests in the same property. Most recording acts protect bona fide purchasers (BFPs or innocent purchasers for value)—ie, persons or entities who: pay value for an interest in property and lack notice of a prior interest in the property. In a jurisdiction with a notice recording act (as seen here), a BFP's property interest is always superior to an earlier competing property interest of which the BFP lacked notice. And under the Shelter Rule, a person who receives a property interest from a BFP is entitled to the same protection under the recording act as the BFP. This ultimately protects the BFP by ensuring that the BFP receives the full benefit of the purchase and is able to convey an interest in the property. Here, the buyer was a BFP because she knew nothing of the friend's prior unrecorded mortgage when the investor sold her the vacant lot. The buyer therefore had priority under the jurisdiction's notice act and took the lot free of the friend's mortgage. The buyer then mortgaged the lot to the bank. And although the buyer told the bank about the friend's prior mortgage (ie, the bank was not a BFP), the Shelter Rule entitles the bank to the buyer's protection under the recording act. As a result, only the bank has an enforceable mortgage on the lot (Choices A, B & D).","conclusion":"Answer C: Only the bank's mortgage is an enforceable lien, because the buyer was an innocent purchaser for value."},"questionZh":"五年前，一位在住宅区拥有一块空地的投资者向一位朋友借了 25,000 美元，并给了朋友一张 25,000 美元的票据，五年后到期，并以该地块的抵押作为担保。这位朋友忘记记录抵押贷款。当时该拍品的公平市场价值为 25,000 美元。三年前，投资者发现朋友没有记录他的抵押贷款，于是以5万美元的价格将拍卖品转让给了一位买家。当时该拍品的公平市场价值为 50,000 美元。买家对朋友的抵押贷款一无所知。一个月后，这位朋友发现买家有这笔交易，记录了他的 25,000 美元抵押贷款，并通知买家他持有该地块 25,000 美元的抵押贷款。两年前，买家需要资金。尽管她向银行告知了投资者朋友申请的抵押贷款，但银行还是贷给了她 15,000 美元，而她则向银行提供了一张两年内到期的 15,000 美元票据，并以该地块的抵押贷款作担保。银行立即记录了抵押贷款。当时，该拍品的公平市场价值为 75,000 美元。该司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“除非依法记录，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者。”两张票据均已到期，投资者和买方均拒绝付款。该地块现仅值 50,000 美元。投资者的朋友和银行在该地块上有哪些权利？","choicesZh":{"A":"两项抵押贷款都是可执行的留置权，朋友的拥有优先权，因为它是最先记录的。","B":"两种抵押贷款都是可执行的留置权，但银行的留置权具有优先权，因为买方是价值的无辜购买者。","C":"只有银行的抵押贷款才是可执行的留置权，因为买方是无辜的价值购买者。","D":"只有朋友的抵押贷款才是可执行的留置权，因为银行已经实际且推定地通知了投资者的欺诈行为。"},"explanationZh":"记录行为决定了同一财产中竞争利益的优先顺序。大多数记录行为保护善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（BFP 或无辜的价值购买者），即以下个人或实体： 为财产权益支付价值并且事先不知道该财产的权益。在具有通知记录法的司法管辖区（如此处所示），BFP 的财产权益始终优于 BFP 未通知的早期竞争财产权益。根据《庇护规则》，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人有权根据记录法获得与 BFP 相同的保护。这最终可以确保 BFP 获得购买的全部利益并能够表达对该房产的兴趣，从而保护 BFP。在这里，买家是 BFP，因为当投资者向她出售空地时，她对朋友之前未记录的抵押贷款一无所知。因此，根据该司法管辖区的通知法，买方拥有优先权，并且无​​需承担朋友的抵押贷款即可获得该地块。买家随后将该地块抵押给银行。尽管买家向银行告知了朋友之前的抵押贷款（即银行不是 BFP），但《庇护规则》使银行有权根据记录法获得买家的保护。因此，只有银行对该地块拥有可执行的抵押贷款（选项 A、B 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：根据庇护规则，从善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 处获得财产权益的人有权根据管辖区的记录法获得与 BFP 相同的保护。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据庇护规则，从善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 处获得财产权益的人有权根据司法管辖区的记录法案获得与 BFP 相同的保护。","iracZh":{"issue":"投资者的朋友和银行在该地块上有哪些权利？","rule":"根据庇护规则，从善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 处获得财产权益的人有权根据司法管辖区的记录法案获得与 BFP 相同的保护。","analysis":"记录行为决定了同一财产中竞争利益的优先顺序。大多数记录行为保护善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）（BFP 或无辜的价值购买者），即以下个人或实体： 为财产权益支付价值并且事先不知道该财产的权益。在具有通知记录法的司法管辖区（如此处所示），BFP 的财产权益始终优于 BFP 未通知的早期竞争财产权益。根据《庇护规则》，从 BFP 处获得财产权益的人有权根据记录法获得与 BFP 相同的保护。这最终可以确保 BFP 获得购买的全部利益并能够表达对该房产的兴趣，从而保护 BFP。在这里，买家是 BFP，因为当投资者向她出售空地时，她对朋友之前未记录的抵押贷款一无所知。因此，根据该司法管辖区的通知法，买方拥有优先权，并且无​​需承担朋友的抵押贷款即可获得该地块。买家随后将该地块抵押给银行。尽管买家向银行告知了朋友之前的抵押贷款（即银行不是 BFP），但《庇护规则》使银行有权根据记录法获得买家的保护。因此，只有银行对该地块拥有可执行的抵押贷款（选项 A、B 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：根据庇护规则，从善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser） (BFP) 处获得财产权益的人有权根据管辖区的记录法获得与 BFP 相同的保护。","conclusion":"答案 C：只有银行的抵押贷款才是可执行的留置权，因为买方是无辜的价值购买者。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-c87b50b271757e7c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":80,"sourceQuestionPage":234,"sourceQuestionPages":[234],"sourceExplanationPages":[235],"sourceAnswerPages":[235],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-081","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A brother and sister acquired title in fee simple to property, as equal tenants in common, by inheritance from their aunt. During the last 15 years of her lifetime, the aunt allowed the brother to occupy an apartment in the house on the property, to rent the other apartment in the house to various tenants, and to retain the rent. The brother made no payments to the aunt, and since the aunt's death 7 years ago he has made no payments to the sister. For those 22 years, the brother has paid the real estate taxes on the property, kept the building on the property insured, and maintained the building. At all times, the sister has lived in a distant city and has never had anything to do with the aunt, the brother, or the property. Recently, the sister needed money for the operation of her business and demanded that the brother join her in selling the property. The brother refused. The period of time to acquire title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 10 years. There is no other applicable statute. The sister brought an appropriate action against the brother for partition. The brother asserted all available defenses and counterclaims. How should the court rule on the sister's action for partition?","choices":{"A":"Deny the partition and find that title has vested in the brother by adverse possession.","B":"Deny the partition, confirm the tenancy in common, but require an accounting to determine if either the brother or sister is indebted to the other on account of the rental payment, taxes, insurance premiums, and maintenance costs.","C":"Grant the partition and require, as an adjustment, an accounting to determine if either the brother or sister is indebted to the other on account of the rental payments, taxes, insurance premiums, and maintenance costs.","D":"Grant the partition to the brother and sister as equal owners, but without an accounting."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Rights & duties of cotenants\n\nRights Possess & enjoy entire property Receive proportionate shares of net profits from removal of natural resources Receive pro rata shares of rent from third parties Duties Pay proportionate shares of expenses that may give rise to lien (eg, mortgage, property tax) Contribute to repair or improvement costs, but only upon agreement Tenancy in common is a type of concurrent estate in which each co-tenant has the right to possess and enjoy the entire property (ie, separate but undivided interests). A court must grant a co-tenant's request to partition (ie, divide) the co-tenancy (Choice B) and may require an accounting if one co-tenant appears to be indebted to the other. Indebtedness may occur if the co-tenants fail to proportionally share their rights (eg, receiving rental income) and duties (eg, paying property taxes, mortgage, agreed improvement costs). Here, the brother and sister inherited a tenancy in common 7 years ago, when their aunt died. Since that time, the brother has kept the rental income from the property and paid all the property taxes, insurance costs, and maintenance fees. Because the income and expenses were never shared, one co-tenant is likely indebted to the other. Therefore, the court should require an accounting upon granting the sister's request for partition (Choice D). (Choice A) To acquire title by adverse possession, a person must have exclusive possession of the property. Exclusive possession of a tenancy in common is only achieved when one co-tenant commits ouster by preventing other co-tenants from accessing the property (eg, changing the locks). Here, though the sister never lived on the property, there is no evidence that the brother ever prevented her from accessing it. Therefore, he did not commit adverse possession.\n\nEducational objective: Co-tenants in common must proportionately share their rights (eg, receiving rent) and duties (eg, paying property taxes, mortgage, agreed improvement costs). A court must grant a co-tenant's request to partition (ie, divide) the co-tenancy and may require an accounting when one co-tenant seems to be indebted to the other.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (First) of Property § 65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (creation of tenancy in common).","educationalObjective":"Co-tenants in common must proportionately share their rights (eg, receiving rent) and duties (eg, paying property taxes, mortgage, agreed improvement costs). A court must grant a co-tenant's request to partition (ie, divide) the co-tenancy and may require an accounting when one co-tenant seems to be indebted to the other.","references":"Restatement (First) of Property § 65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (creation of tenancy in common).","rule":"Co-tenants in common must proportionately share their rights (eg, receiving rent) and duties (eg, paying property taxes, mortgage, agreed improvement costs). A court must grant a co-tenant's request to partition (ie, divide) the co-tenancy and may require an accounting when one co-tenant seems to be indebted to the other.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule on the sister's action for partition?","rule":"Co-tenants in common must proportionately share their rights (eg, receiving rent) and duties (eg, paying property taxes, mortgage, agreed improvement costs). A court must grant a co-tenant's request to partition (ie, divide) the co-tenancy and may require an accounting when one co-tenant seems to be indebted to the other.","analysis":"Rights & duties of cotenants\n\nRights Possess & enjoy entire property Receive proportionate shares of net profits from removal of natural resources Receive pro rata shares of rent from third parties Duties Pay proportionate shares of expenses that may give rise to lien (eg, mortgage, property tax) Contribute to repair or improvement costs, but only upon agreement Tenancy in common is a type of concurrent estate in which each co-tenant has the right to possess and enjoy the entire property (ie, separate but undivided interests). A court must grant a co-tenant's request to partition (ie, divide) the co-tenancy (Choice B) and may require an accounting if one co-tenant appears to be indebted to the other. Indebtedness may occur if the co-tenants fail to proportionally share their rights (eg, receiving rental income) and duties (eg, paying property taxes, mortgage, agreed improvement costs). Here, the brother and sister inherited a tenancy in common 7 years ago, when their aunt died. Since that time, the brother has kept the rental income from the property and paid all the property taxes, insurance costs, and maintenance fees. Because the income and expenses were never shared, one co-tenant is likely indebted to the other. Therefore, the court should require an accounting upon granting the sister's request for partition (Choice D). (Choice A) To acquire title by adverse possession, a person must have exclusive possession of the property. Exclusive possession of a tenancy in common is only achieved when one co-tenant commits ouster by preventing other co-tenants from accessing the property (eg, changing the locks). Here, though the sister never lived on the property, there is no evidence that the brother ever prevented her from accessing it. Therefore, he did not commit adverse possession.","conclusion":"Answer C: Grant the partition and require, as an adjustment, an accounting to determine if either the brother or sister is indebted to the other on account of the rental payments, taxes, insurance premiums, and maintenance costs."},"questionZh":"一对兄弟姐妹作为平等的共有佃户，通过从姨妈那里继承遗产，获得了财产所有权。在她生命的最后15年里，姨妈允许哥哥占用该房产中的一套公寓，并将该房产中的另一套公寓出租给各个租户，并保留租金。兄弟没有向阿姨付款，自从七年前阿姨去世后，他也没有向姐姐付款。在这 22 年里，兄弟一直为该房产缴纳房地产税，为该房产上的建筑物投保，并维护该建筑物。一直以来，姐姐都住在一个遥远的城市，与姑姑、哥哥、财产从来没有任何关系。最近，姐姐经营生意需要资金，要求哥哥和她一起出售房产。哥哥拒绝了。在管辖区内通过逆权占有取得所有权的期限为10年。没有其他适用的法规。姐姐对哥哥提出了适当的分割诉讼。兄弟提出了所有可用的抗辩和反诉。对于姐姐的分割诉讼，法院该如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"否认分割并发现所有权已通过逆权占有而归属于兄弟。","B":"否认分割，确认共同共有（joint tenancy），但要求进行会计核算以确定兄弟或姐妹是否因租金、税金、保险费和维护费用而欠另一方债务。","C":"批准分割，并要求进行会计调整，以确定兄弟或姐妹是否因租金、税款、保险费和维护费用而欠另一方债务。","D":"将土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）授予兄弟姐妹作为平等所有者，但不进行会计处理。"},"explanationZh":"租户的权利和义务\n\n权利 拥有和享受全部财产 获得按比例份额的自然资源开采净利润 按比例份额从第三方收取租金 义务 支付按比例份额的可能产生留置权的费用（例如抵押贷款、财产税） 贡献维修或改善费用，但必须经协议 共同共有（joint tenancy）是一种并行财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）都有权占有和享有整个财产（即单独但不可分割的权益）。法院必须批准按份共有人（tenant in common）分割（即分割）共同共有（joint tenancy）（选择 B）的请求，并且如果一个按份共有人（tenant in common）似乎欠另一方债务，则法院可能要求进行会计处理。如果按份共有人（tenant in common）未能按比例分享其权利（例如，收取租金收入）和义务（例如，缴纳财产税、抵押贷款、商定的改善成本），则可能会产生债务。七年前，他们的姨妈去世后，兄妹俩继承了一份共同共有（joint tenancy）权。从那时起，哥哥就保留了房产的租金收入，并支付了所有的房产税、保险费和维护费。由于收入和支出从未分享，因此一个按份共有人（tenant in common）可能欠另一方债务。因此，法院在批准姐姐的分割请求时应要求进行会计处理（选项D）。 （选择 A） 要通过逆权占有获得所有权，一个人必须拥有该财产的排他占有。只有当一个按份共有人（tenant in common）通过阻止其他按份共有人（tenant in common）访问该财产（例如换锁）而进行驱逐时，才能实现对共同共有（joint tenancy）的独占占有。在这里，尽管妹妹从未居住在该房产上，但没有证据表明哥哥曾阻止她进入该房产。因此，他并无逆权管有。\n\n教育目标：按份共有人（tenant in common）必须按比例分享他们的权利（例如，收取租金）和义务（例如，缴纳财产税、抵押贷款、商定的改善费用）。法院必须批准按份共有人（tenant in common）分割（即划分）共同共有（joint tenancy）的请求，并且当一个按份共有人（tenant in common）似乎欠另一方的债务时，法院可能要求进行会计处理。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 65（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（共同共有（joint tenancy）的设立）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"按份共有人（tenant in common）必须按比例分享他们的权利（例如，收取租金）和义务（例如，缴纳财产税、抵押贷款、商定的改善费用）。法院必须批准按份共有人（tenant in common）分割（即划分）共同共有（joint tenancy）的请求，并且当一个按份共有人（tenant in common）似乎欠另一方的债务时，法院可能要求进行会计处理。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于姐姐的分割诉讼，法院该如何判决？","rule":"按份共有人（tenant in common）必须按比例分享他们的权利（例如，收取租金）和义务（例如，缴纳财产税、抵押贷款、商定的改善费用）。法院必须批准按份共有人（tenant in common）分割（即划分）共同共有（joint tenancy）的请求，并且当一个按份共有人（tenant in common）似乎欠另一方的债务时，法院可能要求进行会计处理。","analysis":"租户的权利和义务\n\n权利 拥有和享受全部财产 获得按比例份额的自然资源开采净利润 按比例份额从第三方收取租金 义务 支付按比例份额的可能产生留置权的费用（例如抵押贷款、财产税） 贡献维修或改善费用，但必须经协议 共同共有（joint tenancy）是一种并行财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）都有权占有和享有整个财产（即单独但不可分割的权益）。法院必须批准按份共有人（tenant in common）分割（即分割）共同共有（joint tenancy）（选择 B）的请求，并且如果一个按份共有人（tenant in common）似乎欠另一方债务，则法院可能要求进行会计处理。如果按份共有人（tenant in common）未能按比例分享其权利（例如，收取租金收入）和义务（例如，缴纳财产税、抵押贷款、商定的改善成本），则可能会产生债务。七年前，他们的姨妈去世后，兄妹俩继承了一份共同共有（joint tenancy）权。从那时起，哥哥就保留了房产的租金收入，并支付了所有的房产税、保险费和维护费。由于收入和支出从未分享，因此一个按份共有人（tenant in common）可能欠另一方债务。因此，法院在批准姐姐的分割请求时应要求进行会计处理（选项D）。 （选择 A） 要通过逆权占有获得所有权，一个人必须拥有该财产的排他占有。只有当一个按份共有人（tenant in common）通过阻止其他按份共有人（tenant in common）访问该财产（例如换锁）而进行驱逐时，才能实现对共同共有（joint tenancy）的独占占有。在这里，尽管妹妹从未居住在该房产上，但没有证据表明哥哥曾阻止她进入该房产。因此，他并无逆权管有。\n\n教育目标：按份共有人（tenant in common）必须按比例分享他们的权利（例如，收取租金）和义务（例如，缴纳财产税、抵押贷款、商定的改善费用）。法院必须批准按份共有人（tenant in common）分割（即划分）共同共有（joint tenancy）的请求，并且当一个按份共有人（tenant in common）似乎欠另一方的债务时，法院可能要求进行会计处理。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 65（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（共同共有（joint tenancy）的设立）。","conclusion":"答案 C：批准分割，并要求进行会计调整，以确定兄弟或姐妹是否因租金、税款、保险费和维护费用而欠另一方债务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"exclusive possession","ipa":"/ɪksklˈusɪv pʌzˈɛʃən/","syllables":"ex·clu·sive pos·ses·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"排他占有：不与产权人或公众共同占有土地","contextualMeaning":"排他占有：不与产权人或公众共同占有土地","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) To acquire title by adverse possession, a person must have exclusive possession of the property.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"cotenant","ipa":"/'kəu'tenənt/","syllables":"co·tenant","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 共同租地人, 合租人, 共同佃户","contextualMeaning":"共有人：与他人共同拥有同一土地占有权的人","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Rights & duties of cotenants Rights Possess & enjoy entire property Receive proportionate shares of net profits from removal of natural resources Receive pro rata shares of rent from third parties Duties Pay proportionate shares of expenses that may give rise to lien (eg, mortgage, property tax) Contribute to repair or improvement costs, but only upon agreement Tenancy in common is a type of concurrent estate in whic","synonyms":["co-owner"]},{"term":"ouster","ipa":"/ˈaʊstɝ/","syllables":"ouster","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 驱逐, 撵走, 剥夺；[法] 驱逐, 撵走, 剥夺","contextualMeaning":"排除占有：共有人明确拒绝另一共有人进入或使用共有土地","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:ousters","sourceSentence":"Exclusive possession of a tenancy in common is only achieved when one co-tenant commits ouster by preventing other co-tenants from accessing the property (eg, changing the locks).","synonyms":["ousting","ejector"]},{"term":"partition","ipa":"/pɑɹtˈɪʃən/","syllables":"par·ti·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 分割, 隔离物, 隔墙, 财产分割；vt. 区分, 瓜分, 隔开, 分割；[计] 分区","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“partition”","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:partitioned/s:partitions/i:partitioning/3:partitions/p:partitioned","sourceSentence":"The sister brought an appropriate action against the brother for partition.","synonyms":["division","partitioning","segmentation","sectionalization"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":81,"sourceQuestionPage":236,"sourceQuestionPages":[236],"sourceExplanationPages":[237,238],"sourceAnswerPages":[237,238],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-082","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A borrower, who is a citizen of State A, defaults on a loan secured by a mortgage on undeveloped land located in State B. The borrower executed the loan in State B and regularly transacts business there. The lender, who is a citizen of State B, sues the borrower in a state court in State A for the outstanding loan obligation before seeking to foreclose on the property. Under the laws of State A, a lender may sue the borrower directly on the debt before foreclosing on mortgaged property. Under the laws of State B, a lender must foreclose on the property before suing the borrower. The court in State A determines that the conflict-of-laws rules of its own state should apply and that, under those rules, State B has the most significant relationship to the loan, the lender, and the borrower. Should the court apply State A law or State B law regarding the lender's right to sue the borrower directly on the outstanding debt?","choices":{"A":"State A law, because the borrower is a citizen of State A.","B":"State A law, because the court is located in State A.","C":"State B law, because State B has the most significant relationship to this matter.","D":"State B law, because the loan was originated in State B."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Conflict-of-laws rule for real property issues\n\nGeneral In cases involving real property, law applied by forum court should be rule determined by conflict-of-laws rule that would be applied by state where property is located\n\nExceptions Document specifies application of specific jurisdiction's laws Issues regarding effect of marriage on interest in land Collateral issues (eg, whether transferor perpetrated fraud) Issues regarding mortgage note (eg, validity of note) Foreclosure-related issues that do not affect interest in land (eg, mortgagee's right to sue mortgagor for underlying debt before foreclosure) Conflict-of-laws issues arise when significant aspects of a legal action are divided between two or more states, creating a conflict as to which state's law applies (as seen here). If the conflict involves real property, the general rule is that the law applied by the forum court should be determined by the conflict-of-laws rule that would be applied by the courts of the situs state—ie, the state where the property is located. Usually, the conflict-of-laws rule of the situs state calls for the application of its own local law on that issue (ie, the law of the situs). However, this general rule does not apply to issues that are related to foreclosure but do not affect an interest in land—eg, a lender's right to sue a borrower on the outstanding debt before foreclosure (as seen here). Instead, such issues are determined by the law that governs the debt for which the mortgage was given, which is the law of the state that has the most significant relationship to the transaction and the parties (here, State B). (Choice A) The borrower is a citizen of State A, so the State A court has personal jurisdiction over the borrower. But this does not mean that the State A court should apply State A law regarding the lender's right to sue the borrower before foreclosing on the mortgaged property. (Choices B & D) Although the court is located in State A and the loan was originated in State B, the dispositive factor is which state has the most significant relationship to the issue at hand.\n\nEducational objective: Issues that relate to foreclosure but do not affect an interest in land—eg, a lender's right to sue the borrower before foreclosing on the mortgaged property—are determined by the law of the state with the most significant relationship to the transaction and the parties.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Conflict of Laws § 223, cmt. i (Am. Law Inst. 1971) (validity and effect of conveyance of interest in land).","educationalObjective":"Issues that relate to foreclosure but do not affect an interest in land—eg, a lender's right to sue the borrower before foreclosing on the mortgaged property—are determined by the law of the state with the most significant relationship to the transaction and the parties.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Conflict of Laws § 223, cmt. i (Am. Law Inst. 1971) (validity and effect of conveyance of interest in land).","rule":"Issues that relate to foreclosure but do not affect an interest in land—eg, a lender's right to sue the borrower before foreclosing on the mortgaged property—are determined by the law of the state with the most significant relationship to the transaction and the parties.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court apply State A law or State B law regarding the lender's right to sue the borrower directly on the outstanding debt?","rule":"Issues that relate to foreclosure but do not affect an interest in land—eg, a lender's right to sue the borrower before foreclosing on the mortgaged property—are determined by the law of the state with the most significant relationship to the transaction and the parties.","analysis":"Conflict-of-laws rule for real property issues\n\nGeneral In cases involving real property, law applied by forum court should be rule determined by conflict-of-laws rule that would be applied by state where property is located\n\nExceptions Document specifies application of specific jurisdiction's laws Issues regarding effect of marriage on interest in land Collateral issues (eg, whether transferor perpetrated fraud) Issues regarding mortgage note (eg, validity of note) Foreclosure-related issues that do not affect interest in land (eg, mortgagee's right to sue mortgagor for underlying debt before foreclosure) Conflict-of-laws issues arise when significant aspects of a legal action are divided between two or more states, creating a conflict as to which state's law applies (as seen here). If the conflict involves real property, the general rule is that the law applied by the forum court should be determined by the conflict-of-laws rule that would be applied by the courts of the situs state—ie, the state where the property is located. Usually, the conflict-of-laws rule of the situs state calls for the application of its own local law on that issue (ie, the law of the situs). However, this general rule does not apply to issues that are related to foreclosure but do not affect an interest in land—eg, a lender's right to sue a borrower on the outstanding debt before foreclosure (as seen here). Instead, such issues are determined by the law that governs the debt for which the mortgage was given, which is the law of the state that has the most significant relationship to the transaction and the parties (here, State B). (Choice A) The borrower is a citizen of State A, so the State A court has personal jurisdiction over the borrower. But this does not mean that the State A court should apply State A law regarding the lender's right to sue the borrower before foreclosing on the mortgaged property. (Choices B & D) Although the court is located in State A and the loan was originated in State B, the dispositive factor is which state has the most significant relationship to the issue at hand.","conclusion":"Answer C: State B law, because State B has the most significant relationship to this matter."},"questionZh":"借款人是 A 国公民，他拖欠了以 B 国未开发土地抵押担保的贷款。借款人在 B 国执行贷款并定期在那里开展业务。贷款人是 B 国的公民，在寻求取消财产赎回权之前，在 A 国的州法院就未偿还的贷款义务起诉借款人。根据 A 州法律，贷款人可以在取消抵押财产赎回权之前直接就债务起诉借款人。根据 B 州法律，贷款人在起诉借款人之前必须取消财产的赎回权。 A 国法院裁定应适用其本国的法律冲突规则，并且根据这些规则，B 国与贷款、贷款人和借款人具有最重要的关系。关于贷款人就未偿债务直接起诉借款人的权利，法院应该适用 A 州法律还是 B 州法律？","choicesZh":{"A":"A 州法律，因为借款人是 A 州公民。","B":"A 州法律，因为法院位于 A 州。","C":"B 州法律，因为 B 州与此事有最重要的关系。","D":"B 州法律，因为贷款源自 B 州。"},"explanationZh":"不动产问题的法律冲突规则\n\n一般性 在涉及不动产的案件中，法院适用的法律应由财产所在州适用的法律冲突规则确定。\n\n例外文件规定了具体司法管辖区法律的适用 婚姻对土地权益影响的问题 抵押问题（例如，转让人是否实施了欺诈） 有关抵押票据的问题（例如，票据的有效性） 不影响土地权益的止赎相关问题（例如，抵押权人在止赎之前就基础债务起诉抵押人的权利） 当法律诉讼的重要方面在两个或多个州之间划分时，就会出现法律冲突问题，从而产生冲突适用哪个州的法律（如此处所示）。如果冲突涉及不动产，一般规则是法院地法院适用的法律应由所在地国（即财产所在州）法院适用的法律冲突规则确定。通常，所在地国的法律冲突规则要求在该问题上适用其本国的当地法律（即所在地法）。然而，这一一般规则不适用于与取消抵押品赎回权相关但不影响土地权益的问题，例如，贷款人在取消抵押品赎回权之前就未偿债务起诉借款人的权利（如此处所示）。相反，此类问题由管辖抵押贷款债务的法律决定，即与交易和当事人关系最密切的州（此处为 B 州）的法律。 （选项A）借款人是A国公民，因此A国法院对借款人拥有属人管辖权。但这并不意味着A州法院应该适用A州法律，规定贷款人在取消抵押财产赎回权之前起诉借款人的权利。 （选择 B 和 D）虽然法院位于 A 州且贷款源自 B 州，但决定性因素是哪个州与当前问题最重要的关系。\n\n教育目标：与取消抵押品赎回权相关但不影响土地权益的问题（例如，贷款人在取消抵押财产赎回权之前起诉借款人的权利）由与交易和当事人关系最密切的州的法律确定。\n\n参考文献\n法律冲突重述（第二）§ 223，cmt。 i（Am. Law Inst. 1971）（土地权益转让的有效性和效果）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"与取消抵押品赎回权相关但不影响土地权益的问题（例如，贷款人在取消抵押财产赎回权之前起诉借款人的权利）由与交易和当事人关系最密切的州的法律决定。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于贷款人就未偿债务直接起诉借款人的权利，法院应该适用 A 州法律还是 B 州法律？","rule":"与取消抵押品赎回权相关但不影响土地权益的问题（例如，贷款人在取消抵押财产赎回权之前起诉借款人的权利）由与交易和当事人关系最密切的州的法律决定。","analysis":"不动产问题的法律冲突规则\n\n一般性 在涉及不动产的案件中，法院适用的法律应由财产所在州适用的法律冲突规则确定。\n\n例外文件规定了具体司法管辖区法律的适用 婚姻对土地权益影响的问题 抵押问题（例如，转让人是否实施了欺诈） 有关抵押票据的问题（例如，票据的有效性） 不影响土地权益的止赎相关问题（例如，抵押权人在止赎之前就基础债务起诉抵押人的权利） 当法律诉讼的重要方面在两个或多个州之间划分时，就会出现法律冲突问题，从而产生冲突适用哪个州的法律（如此处所示）。如果冲突涉及不动产，一般规则是法院地法院适用的法律应由所在地国（即财产所在州）法院适用的法律冲突规则确定。通常，所在地国的法律冲突规则要求在该问题上适用其本国的当地法律（即所在地法）。然而，这一一般规则不适用于与取消抵押品赎回权相关但不影响土地权益的问题，例如，贷款人在取消抵押品赎回权之前就未偿债务起诉借款人的权利（如此处所示）。相反，此类问题由管辖抵押贷款债务的法律决定，即与交易和当事人关系最密切的州（此处为 B 州）的法律。 （选项A）借款人是A国公民，因此A国法院对借款人拥有属人管辖权。但这并不意味着A州法院应该适用A州法律，规定贷款人在取消抵押财产赎回权之前起诉借款人的权利。 （选择 B 和 D）虽然法院位于 A 州且贷款源自 B 州，但决定性因素是哪个州与当前问题最重要的关系。\n\n教育目标：与取消抵押品赎回权相关但不影响土地权益的问题（例如，贷款人在取消抵押财产赎回权之前起诉借款人的权利）由与交易和当事人关系最密切的州的法律确定。\n\n参考文献\n法律冲突重述（第二）§ 223，cmt。 i（Am. Law Inst. 1971）（土地权益转让的有效性和效果）。","conclusion":"答案 C：B 州法律，因为 B 州与此事有最重要的关系。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":82,"sourceQuestionPage":239,"sourceQuestionPages":[239],"sourceExplanationPages":[240,241],"sourceAnswerPages":[240,241],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-083","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"An individual entered into an oral agreement with the owner of a residence to rent the residence for one year. They agreed that the individual was to pay a total of $12,000 in rent, payable in monthly installments of $1,000. The individual occupied the residence for one year but failed to pay the final $1,000 installment. At the end of the one-year period, the individual moved out of the residence without giving the owner any notice of his intent to do so. Has the individual's tenancy been terminated?","choices":{"A":"No, because the individual failed to give the owner notice of his intent to vacate the residence.","B":"No, because the individual failed to make the final $1,000 installment payment.","C":"Yes, because the agreement was oral.","D":"Yes, because the individual vacated the premises."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Types of tenancies\n\n(leases on land)\n\nType Creation Duration Termination\n\nTerm of Agreement (ie, lease) Fixed period (eg, End of term years one month, three (automatic) years) Breach of certain covenants (eg, rent, habitability) Periodic Agreement Periodically End of period (proper tenancy Presumed when lease has renews until notice) no specified termination terminated Breach of certain date covenants Operation of law (eg, holdover tenant) Agreement Indefinite period At any time Tenancy at Implied when person (reasonable notice) will allowed to possess premises without paying rent Tenancy at Implied when tenant keeps Continues until Landlord evicts sufferance possession after lease terminated Tenant vacates expires (ie, holdover Landlord accepts rent tenancy) (forms periodic tenancy) A tenancy for years is an estate measured by a fixed and ascertainable amount of time. This type of tenancy is created by an agreement between the landlord and the tenant and may be for any length of time (eg, one week, six months, five years). Termination occurs automatically upon the expiration of the term, with no notice required from either party. As a result, a tenant who remains on the premises after the term expires becomes a tenant at sufferance (ie, holdover tenant). Here, the individual and the owner entered into a lease that created a tenancy for years with a one-year term. This tenancy terminated automatically at the end of the one-year term (Choice A). And since the individual vacated the premises, no tenancy at sufferance was created. Therefore, the individual's tenancy has been terminated.\n\n(Choice B) The individual's failure to make the final $1,000 installment payment is not a reason for the tenancy to continue. Instead, it was a breach of the lease agreement that gave the owner the right to take legal action to terminate the tenancy early. But the owner did not do so, and the tenancy terminated automatically at the end of the one-year period. However, the owner can still sue the individual to recover the unpaid rent. (Choice C) The statute of frauds applies to a tenancy for years that is longer than one year (not seen here) and requires the lease agreement to be in writing. However, a lease subject to the statute of frauds that does not comply with it may be enforceable as a periodic tenancy when, as here, the tenant occupies the premises with the landlord's permission and the landlord accepts rent from the tenant.* *If the tenant also makes substantial improvements to the premises and thereby indicates that the parties contemplated a lease for more than a year, then the noncomplying lease is given full effect.\n\nEducational objective: A tenancy for years (ie, an estate for a fixed and ascertainable term) terminates automatically upon the expiration of the term without notice from either party.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 1.4 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (termination of tenancy for years).","educationalObjective":"A tenancy for years (ie, an estate for a fixed and ascertainable term) terminates automatically upon the expiration of the term without notice from either party.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 1.4 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (termination of tenancy for years).","rule":"A tenancy for years (ie, an estate for a fixed and ascertainable term) terminates automatically upon the expiration of the term without notice from either party.","irac":{"issue":"Has the individual's tenancy been terminated?","rule":"A tenancy for years (ie, an estate for a fixed and ascertainable term) terminates automatically upon the expiration of the term without notice from either party.","analysis":"Types of tenancies\n\n(leases on land)\n\nType Creation Duration Termination\n\nTerm of Agreement (ie, lease) Fixed period (eg, End of term years one month, three (automatic) years) Breach of certain covenants (eg, rent, habitability) Periodic Agreement Periodically End of period (proper tenancy Presumed when lease has renews until notice) no specified termination terminated Breach of certain date covenants Operation of law (eg, holdover tenant) Agreement Indefinite period At any time Tenancy at Implied when person (reasonable notice) will allowed to possess premises without paying rent Tenancy at Implied when tenant keeps Continues until Landlord evicts sufferance possession after lease terminated Tenant vacates expires (ie, holdover Landlord accepts rent tenancy) (forms periodic tenancy) A tenancy for years is an estate measured by a fixed and ascertainable amount of time. This type of tenancy is created by an agreement between the landlord and the tenant and may be for any length of time (eg, one week, six months, five years). Termination occurs automatically upon the expiration of the term, with no notice required from either party. As a result, a tenant who remains on the premises after the term expires becomes a tenant at sufferance (ie, holdover tenant). Here, the individual and the owner entered into a lease that created a tenancy for years with a one-year term. This tenancy terminated automatically at the end of the one-year term (Choice A). And since the individual vacated the premises, no tenancy at sufferance was created. Therefore, the individual's tenancy has been terminated.\n\n(Choice B) The individual's failure to make the final $1,000 installment payment is not a reason for the tenancy to continue. Instead, it was a breach of the lease agreement that gave the owner the right to take legal action to terminate the tenancy early. But the owner did not do so, and the tenancy terminated automatically at the end of the one-year period. However, the owner can still sue the individual to recover the unpaid rent. (Choice C) The statute of frauds applies to a tenancy for years that is longer than one year (not seen here) and requires the lease agreement to be in writing. However, a lease subject to the statute of frauds that does not comply with it may be enforceable as a periodic tenancy when, as here, the tenant occupies the premises with the landlord's permission and the landlord accepts rent from the tenant.* *If the tenant also makes substantial improvements to the premises and thereby indicates that the parties contemplated a lease for more than a year, then the noncomplying lease is given full effect.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the individual vacated the premises."},"questionZh":"个人与住宅业主签订口头协议，租用该住宅一年。他们同意该个人总共支付 12,000 美元的租金，每月分期付款 1,000 美元。该人占用该住宅一年，但未能支付最后 1,000 美元的分期付款。一年期结束后，该人搬出住所，但未向业主发出任何通知表明其打算这样做。个人的租约是否已终止？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该人没有向业主告知他打算搬出住宅。","B":"不可以，因为该个人未能支付最后 1,000 美元的分期付款。","C":"是的，因为协议是口头的。","D":"是的，因为该人已搬出该处所。"},"explanationZh":"租赁类型\n\n（土地租赁）\n\n类型创建持续时间终止\n\n协议期限（即租赁） 固定期限（例如，期限结束年 一个月、三年（自动）） 违反某些契约（例如，租金、可居住性） 定期协议 定期协议 期限结束（在租赁续签直至收到通知时推定为正当租赁） 无指定终止终止 违反特定日期契约 法律运作（例如，滞留租户） 协议 无限期 任何时间 隐含的租赁 允许个人（合理通知）拥有房屋时不支付租金 租客保留时隐含的租赁权 继续直至房东在租约终止后驱逐出暂缓占有权 租客腾出到期（即滞留 房东接受租金租赁）（形成定期租赁） 多年租赁是指以固定且可确定的时间量衡量的房地产。这种类型的租赁是由房东和租户之间的协议创建的，并且期限可以是任意长度（例如，一周、六个月、五年）。期限届满后自动终止，无需任何一方通知。因此，在租期届满后仍留在该处所的租户将成为“忍受租户”（即滞留租户）。在这里，个人和业主签订了一份租约，签订了为期一年的多年租赁合同。该租赁在一年期限结束时自动终止（选择 A）。由于该人腾出了该处所，因此没有创建宽容租赁。因此，该人的租约已被终止。\n\n（选择 B） 个人未能支付最后 1,000 美元的分期付款并不是继续租赁的理由。相反，由于违反了租赁协议，业主有权采取法律行动提前终止租赁。但业主没有这样做，租约在一年期结束时自动终止。然而，业主仍然可以起诉个人，以追回未付的租金。 （选项 C）欺诈法适用于超过一年的租赁（此处未显示），并且要求租赁协议采用书面形式。然而，如果租客经房东许可占用房屋并且房东接受租客的租金，则受欺诈法约束的租约如果不符合该租约，则可以作为定期租赁强制执行。* *如果租客还对房屋进行了实质性改进，从而表明双方考虑租赁超过一年，则不符合规定的租约将完全生效。\n\n教育目标：多年租赁（即固定且可确定期限的遗产）在期限届满时自动终止，无需任何一方通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 1.4 cmt。 e（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（终止租赁多年）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"多年租赁（即固定且可确定期限的遗产）在期限届满时自动终止，无需任何一方通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"个人的租约是否已终止？","rule":"多年租赁（即固定且可确定期限的遗产）在期限届满时自动终止，无需任何一方通知。","analysis":"租赁类型\n\n（土地租赁）\n\n类型创建持续时间终止\n\n协议期限（即租赁） 固定期限（例如，期限结束年 一个月、三年（自动）） 违反某些契约（例如，租金、可居住性） 定期协议 定期协议 期限结束（在租赁续签直至收到通知时推定为正当租赁） 无指定终止终止 违反特定日期契约 法律运作（例如，滞留租户） 协议 无限期 任何时间 隐含的租赁 允许个人（合理通知）拥有房屋时不支付租金 租客保留时隐含的租赁权 继续直至房东在租约终止后驱逐出暂缓占有权 租客腾出到期（即滞留 房东接受租金租赁）（形成定期租赁） 多年租赁是指以固定且可确定的时间量衡量的房地产。这种类型的租赁是由房东和租户之间的协议创建的，并且期限可以是任意长度（例如，一周、六个月、五年）。期限届满后自动终止，无需任何一方通知。因此，在租期届满后仍留在该处所的租户将成为“忍受租户”（即滞留租户）。在这里，个人和业主签订了一份租约，签订了为期一年的多年租赁合同。该租赁在一年期限结束时自动终止（选择 A）。由于该人腾出了该处所，因此没有创建宽容租赁。因此，该人的租约已被终止。\n\n（选择 B） 个人未能支付最后 1,000 美元的分期付款并不是继续租赁的理由。相反，由于违反了租赁协议，业主有权采取法律行动提前终止租赁。但业主没有这样做，租约在一年期结束时自动终止。然而，业主仍然可以起诉个人，以追回未付的租金。 （选项 C）欺诈法适用于超过一年的租赁（此处未显示），并且要求租赁协议采用书面形式。然而，如果租客经房东许可占用房屋并且房东接受租客的租金，则受欺诈法约束的租约如果不符合该租约，则可以作为定期租赁强制执行。* *如果租客还对房屋进行了实质性改进，从而表明双方考虑租赁超过一年，则不符合规定的租约将完全生效。\n\n教育目标：多年租赁（即固定且可确定期限的遗产）在期限届满时自动终止，无需任何一方通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 1.4 cmt。 e（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（终止租赁多年）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该人已搬出该处所。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"tenancy at sufferance","ipa":"/tˈɛnənsi ˈæt 'sʌfərəns/","syllables":"ten·ancy at suf·fer·ance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"容忍租赁：租期届满后承租人无权继续占有形成的状态","contextualMeaning":"容忍租赁：租期届满后承租人无权继续占有形成的状态","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"And since the individual vacated the premises, no tenancy at sufferance was created.","synonyms":["holdover tenancy"]},{"term":"tenancy for years","ipa":"/tˈɛnənsi fˈɔɹ jˈɪɹz/","syllables":"ten·ancy for years","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"定期租赁：有确定开始和终止日期的租赁地产","contextualMeaning":"定期租赁：有确定开始和终止日期的租赁地产","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A tenancy for years (ie, an estate for a fixed and ascertainable term) terminates automatically upon the expiration of the term without notice from either party.","synonyms":["estate for years","term tenancy"]},{"term":"periodic tenancy","ipa":"/pˌɪɹiˈɑdɪk tˈɛnənsi/","syllables":"pe·ri·odic ten·ancy","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"周期租赁：按固定周期自动续期，直至适当通知终止","contextualMeaning":"周期租赁：按固定周期自动续期，直至适当通知终止","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Types of tenancies (leases on land) Type Creation Duration Termination Term of Agreement (ie, lease) Fixed period (eg, End of term years one month, three (automatic) years) Breach of certain covenants (eg, rent, habitability) Periodic Agreement Periodically End of period (proper tenancy Presumed when lease has renews until notice) no specified termination terminated Breach of certain date covenants Operation of law (","synonyms":["period-to-period tenancy"]},{"term":"habitability","ipa":"/,hæbitə'biləti/","syllables":"hab·it·abil·ity","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 可居住性, 适于居住","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“habitability”","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Types of tenancies (leases on land) Type Creation Duration Termination Term of Agreement (ie, lease) Fixed period (eg, End of term years one month, three (automatic) years) Breach of certain covenants (eg, rent, habitability) Periodic Agreement Periodically End of period (proper tenancy Presumed when lease has renews until notice) no specified termination terminated Breach of certain date covenants Operation of law (","synonyms":["habitableness"]},{"term":"evicts","ipa":"/ɪvˈɪkt/","syllables":"evicts","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. （依法从房屋里或土地上）驱逐, 赶出( evict的第三人称单数 )","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“evicts”","wordForm":"词；原形 evict；词形 0:evict/1:3","sourceSentence":"Types of tenancies (leases on land) Type Creation Duration Termination Term of Agreement (ie, lease) Fixed period (eg, End of term years one month, three (automatic) years) Breach of certain covenants (eg, rent, habitability) Periodic Agreement Periodically End of period (proper tenancy Presumed when lease has renews until notice) no specified termination terminated Breach of certain date covenants Operation of law (","synonyms":["force out"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":83,"sourceQuestionPage":242,"sourceQuestionPages":[242],"sourceExplanationPages":[243,244],"sourceAnswerPages":[243,244],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-084","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"An elderly woman owned a large manor in fee simple. Because of her advanced age, she no longer wished to deal with maintenance and other obligations associated with homeownership. She therefore conveyed the manor to her eldest son for life, remainder to her youngest son. The youngest son took ill and died three years later, leaving his entire estate to a friend under the terms of his duly executed will. The youngest son was survived by his daughter, his sole heir. The eldest son died six months later, survived by the elderly woman, the friend, and the daughter. The jurisdiction in which the manor is situated recognizes traditional common-law rules regarding the destructibility of contingent remainders. In an appropriate action between the elderly woman, the friend, and the daughter to determine title to the manor, who will prevail?","choices":{"A":"The daughter, because she is the youngest son's heir.","B":"The elderly woman, because she had a reversion that entitled her to possession of the manor immediately upon the eldest son's death.","C":"The elderly woman, because the youngest son's contingent remainder was destroyed when he predeceased the eldest son.","D":"The friend, because the youngest son's vested remainder was transferred by his will."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A life estate is a present possessory interest in real property that terminates on the death of an individual. The future interest that follows a life estate is either a reversion (if held by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in a grantee—as seen here). Remainders are either: vested – not subject to any condition precedent AND held by an identifiable living person (eg, \"remainder to my youngest son\") or\n\ncontingent – subject to a condition precedent OR held by an unknown or unborn person (eg, \"remainder to my youngest son, but only if…\").\n\nBoth types of remainders are fully transferable during the grantee's lifetime,* devisable by will, and inheritable through intestate succession. Here, the elderly woman conveyed the manor to her eldest son for life, remainder to her youngest son. Since the manor would go to the youngest son unconditionally when the eldest son died, the youngest son had a vested remainder. That remainder was devisable, so it transferred to his friend under the terms of his duly executed will. And since the eldest son has since died, the friend has title to the manor in fee simple. *At common law and in a minority of jurisdictions, contingent remainders are not transferable during the grantee's lifetime. (Choice A) The daughter has no interest in the manor because the youngest son devised his vested remainder to the friend. (Choice B) The elderly woman (grantor) did not have a reversion that entitled her to possession immediately upon the eldest son's death. Instead, the friend was entitled to possession of the manor at that time because he had the youngest son's remainder interest. (Choice C) At common law and in this jurisdiction, contingent remainders are destroyed if they have not vested by the time the prior estate terminates. But this rule does not affect the youngest son's vested remainder.\n\nEducational objective: Remainders—whether they are vested or contingent—are fully transferable during the grantee's lifetime, devisable by will, and inheritable through intestate succession.\n\nReferences\nMoore v. McKinley, 69 N.W.2d 73, 80 (Iowa 1955) (describing the difference between vested and contingent remainders).","educationalObjective":"Remainders—whether they are vested or contingent—are fully transferable during the grantee's lifetime, devisable by will, and inheritable through intestate succession.","references":"Moore v. McKinley, 69 N.W.2d 73, 80 (Iowa 1955) (describing the difference between vested and contingent remainders).","rule":"Remainders—whether they are vested or contingent—are fully transferable during the grantee's lifetime, devisable by will, and inheritable through intestate succession.","irac":{"issue":"In an appropriate action between the elderly woman, the friend, and the daughter to determine title to the manor, who will prevail?","rule":"Remainders—whether they are vested or contingent—are fully transferable during the grantee's lifetime, devisable by will, and inheritable through intestate succession.","analysis":"A life estate is a present possessory interest in real property that terminates on the death of an individual. The future interest that follows a life estate is either a reversion (if held by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in a grantee—as seen here). Remainders are either: vested – not subject to any condition precedent AND held by an identifiable living person (eg, \"remainder to my youngest son\") or\n\ncontingent – subject to a condition precedent OR held by an unknown or unborn person (eg, \"remainder to my youngest son, but only if…\").\n\nBoth types of remainders are fully transferable during the grantee's lifetime,* devisable by will, and inheritable through intestate succession. Here, the elderly woman conveyed the manor to her eldest son for life, remainder to her youngest son. Since the manor would go to the youngest son unconditionally when the eldest son died, the youngest son had a vested remainder. That remainder was devisable, so it transferred to his friend under the terms of his duly executed will. And since the eldest son has since died, the friend has title to the manor in fee simple. *At common law and in a minority of jurisdictions, contingent remainders are not transferable during the grantee's lifetime. (Choice A) The daughter has no interest in the manor because the youngest son devised his vested remainder to the friend. (Choice B) The elderly woman (grantor) did not have a reversion that entitled her to possession immediately upon the eldest son's death. Instead, the friend was entitled to possession of the manor at that time because he had the youngest son's remainder interest. (Choice C) At common law and in this jurisdiction, contingent remainders are destroyed if they have not vested by the time the prior estate terminates. But this rule does not affect the youngest son's vested remainder.","conclusion":"Answer D: The friend, because the youngest son's vested remainder was transferred by his will."},"questionZh":"一位老妇人拥有一座简单的大庄园。由于她年事已高，她不再希望承担与房屋所有权相关的维护和其他义务。因此，她将庄园终身转让给长子，其余部分则留给小儿子。三年后，最小的儿子生病去世，根据他正式执行的遗嘱，将他的全部财产留给了一位朋友。最小的儿子留下了他的女儿，他的唯一继承人。六个月后，长子去世，留下老妇人、朋友和女儿。庄园所在的司法管辖区承认有关或有剩余物可销毁性的传统普通法规则。在老妇人、朋友和女儿之间决定庄园所有权的适当行动中，谁会占上风？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿，因为她是最小儿子的继承人。","B":"老妇人，因为她有一份回归权，有权在长子死后立即拥有庄园。","C":"老妇人，因为小儿子先于长子去世后，他的剩余财产就被毁掉了。","D":"朋友，因为小儿子的既得剩余是按照他的遗嘱转移的。"},"explanationZh":"终身财产是对不动产的现有占有权益，在个人死亡时终止。终身财产之后的未来利息要么是归还（如果由授予人持有），要么是剩余权益（如果由受让人创建——如此处所示）。剩余部分是： 既得 – 不受任何先决条件的约束并且由可识别的在世人士持有（例如，“剩余部分归我最小的儿子”）或\n\n或有的 – 受先决条件限制或由未知或未出生的人持有（例如，“剩余的给我最小的儿子，但前提是……”）。\n\n两种类型的剩余部分在受让人的一生中都可以完全转让，*可以通过遗嘱设计，并且可以通过无遗嘱继承来继承。在这里，这位老妇人将庄园终身转让给她的大儿子，其余的则交给她的小儿子。由于长子去世后，庄园将无条件归最小的儿子所有，因此最小的儿子拥有既得的剩余遗产。剩下的部分是可以设计的，因此根据他正式执行的遗嘱条款将其转移给他的朋友。由于长子已经去世，这位朋友就拥有了庄园的所有权。 *根据普通法和少数司法管辖区，或有余额在受让人的一生中不可转让。 （选项A） 女儿对庄园没有兴趣，因为最小的儿子将其既得剩余部分分配给了朋友。 （选项 B） 老妇人（授予人）没有归还权，使她有权在长子去世后立即拥有财产。相反，这位朋友当时有权拥有该庄园，因为他拥有最小儿子的剩余权益。 （选择 C）根据普通法和本司法管辖区，如果在先前遗产终止时尚未归属，则或有剩余物将被销毁。但这条规则并不影响最小儿子的既得剩余财产。\n\n教育目标：剩余财产——无论是既得的还是偶然的——在受赠人的一生中可以完全转让，可以通过遗嘱设计，并且可以通过无遗嘱继承来继承。\n\n参考文献\nMoore v. McKinley, 69 N.W.2d 73, 80 (Iowa 1955)（描述既定剩余和或有剩余之间的区别）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"剩余物——无论是既得的还是偶然的——在受让人的一生中可以完全转让，可以通过遗嘱设计，并且可以通过无遗嘱继承来继承。","iracZh":{"issue":"在老妇人、朋友和女儿之间决定庄园所有权的适当行动中，谁会占上风？","rule":"剩余物——无论是既得的还是偶然的——在受让人的一生中可以完全转让，可以通过遗嘱设计，并且可以通过无遗嘱继承来继承。","analysis":"终身财产是对不动产的现有占有权益，在个人死亡时终止。终身财产之后的未来利息要么是归还（如果由授予人持有），要么是剩余权益（如果由受让人创建——如此处所示）。剩余部分是： 既得 – 不受任何先决条件的约束并且由可识别的在世人士持有（例如，“剩余部分归我最小的儿子”）或\n\n或有的 – 受先决条件限制或由未知或未出生的人持有（例如，“剩余的给我最小的儿子，但前提是……”）。\n\n两种类型的剩余部分在受让人的一生中都可以完全转让，*可以通过遗嘱设计，并且可以通过无遗嘱继承来继承。在这里，这位老妇人将庄园终身转让给她的大儿子，其余的则交给她的小儿子。由于长子去世后，庄园将无条件归最小的儿子所有，因此最小的儿子拥有既得的剩余遗产。剩下的部分是可以设计的，因此根据他正式执行的遗嘱条款将其转移给他的朋友。由于长子已经去世，这位朋友就拥有了庄园的所有权。 *根据普通法和少数司法管辖区，或有余额在受让人的一生中不可转让。 （选项A） 女儿对庄园没有兴趣，因为最小的儿子将其既得剩余部分分配给了朋友。 （选项 B） 老妇人（授予人）没有归还权，使她有权在长子去世后立即拥有财产。相反，这位朋友当时有权拥有该庄园，因为他拥有最小儿子的剩余权益。 （选择 C）根据普通法和本司法管辖区，如果在先前遗产终止时尚未归属，则或有剩余物将被销毁。但这条规则并不影响最小儿子的既得剩余财产。\n\n教育目标：剩余财产——无论是既得的还是偶然的——在受赠人的一生中可以完全转让，可以通过遗嘱设计，并且可以通过无遗嘱继承来继承。\n\n参考文献\nMoore v. McKinley, 69 N.W.2d 73, 80 (Iowa 1955)（描述既定剩余和或有剩余之间的区别）。","conclusion":"答案 D：朋友，因为小儿子的既得剩余是按照他的遗嘱转移的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"contingent remainder","ipa":"/kʌntˈɪndʒənt ɹɪmˈeɪndɝ/","syllables":"con·tin·gent re·main·der","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"或有剩余权：受让人未确定或权益受先决条件限制的剩余权益","contextualMeaning":"或有剩余权：受让人未确定或权益受先决条件限制的剩余权益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The jurisdiction in which the manor is situated recognizes traditional common-law rules regarding the destructibility of contingent remainders.","synonyms":["conditional remainder"]},{"term":"vested remainder","ipa":"/vˈɛstɪd ɹɪmˈeɪndɝ/","syllables":"vested re·main·der","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"既得剩余权：给予已确定受让人且不存在先决条件的剩余权益","contextualMeaning":"既得剩余权：给予已确定受让人且不存在先决条件的剩余权益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The friend, because the youngest son's vested remainder was transferred by his will.","synonyms":["indefeasibly vested remainder"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-4151a2242082c9ea"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":84,"sourceQuestionPage":245,"sourceQuestionPages":[245],"sourceExplanationPages":[246,247],"sourceAnswerPages":[246,247],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-085","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"The owner of a tract of land executed a deed purporting to convey the land \"to my brother for life, then to my nephew in fee simple.\" The brother began to manage the land, which is valuable income-producing real estate. The brother collected all rents and paid all expenses, including real estate taxes. The nephew did not object, and this state of affairs continued until the brother executed a deed purporting to convey the land to his girlfriend five years later. The nephew asserted his right to ownership of the land. The girlfriend also asserted her ownership and said that if the nephew had any rights, he was obligated to pay real estate taxes even though the brother had paid them in the past. Income from the land is ample to cover expenses, including real estate taxes. In an appropriate action to determine the rights of the parties, what should the court decide?","choices":{"A":"The brother's purported deed forfeited his life estate, so the nephew owns the land in fee simple.","B":"The girlfriend owns an estate for her life, is entitled to all income, and must pay real estate taxes; the nephew owns the remainder interest.","C":"The girlfriend owns an estate for the life of the brother and is entitled to all income; the nephew owns the remainder interest and must pay real estate taxes.","D":"The girlfriend owns an estate for the life of the brother, is entitled to all income, and must pay real estate taxes; the nephew owns the remainder interest."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A life estate is a present possessory interest that terminates on the death of an individual. A life estate is freely transferable but becomes a life estate pur autre vie since it is measured by the life of someone other than the current life tenant. The future interest that follows a life estate is either a reversion (if held by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in a grantee). It will become a possessory interest when the life estate terminates. Here, the owner conveyed a life estate to the brother (\"to my brother for life\") and the nephew received a remainder in fee simple (\"then to my nephew\") (Choice A). The brother then conveyed his present possessory interest to the girlfriend, who received a life estate pur autre vie measured by the brother's life (Choice B). As the current life tenant, the girlfriend has a right to all income received from the property. But she also has a duty to pay current charges that become due during the tenancy—including real estate taxes. As a result, the court should decide the rights of the parties accordingly (Choice C).\n\nEducational objective: A life estate is a freely transferable present interest in land that terminates on the death of an individual and is followed by a reversionary or remainder interest. The life tenant is entitled to all income received from the land but must pay current charges on the land (eg, real estate taxes).\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property §§ 129–130 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (duty to pay current charges).","educationalObjective":"A life estate is a freely transferable present interest in land that terminates on the death of an individual and is followed by a reversionary or remainder interest. The life tenant is entitled to all income received from the land but must pay current charges on the land (eg, real estate taxes).","references":"Restatement of Property §§ 129–130 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (duty to pay current charges).","rule":"A life estate is a freely transferable present interest in land that terminates on the death of an individual and is followed by a reversionary or remainder interest. The life tenant is entitled to all income received from the land but must pay current charges on the land (eg, real estate taxes).","irac":{"issue":"In an appropriate action to determine the rights of the parties, what should the court decide?","rule":"A life estate is a freely transferable present interest in land that terminates on the death of an individual and is followed by a reversionary or remainder interest. The life tenant is entitled to all income received from the land but must pay current charges on the land (eg, real estate taxes).","analysis":"A life estate is a present possessory interest that terminates on the death of an individual. A life estate is freely transferable but becomes a life estate pur autre vie since it is measured by the life of someone other than the current life tenant. The future interest that follows a life estate is either a reversion (if held by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in a grantee). It will become a possessory interest when the life estate terminates. Here, the owner conveyed a life estate to the brother (\"to my brother for life\") and the nephew received a remainder in fee simple (\"then to my nephew\") (Choice A). The brother then conveyed his present possessory interest to the girlfriend, who received a life estate pur autre vie measured by the brother's life (Choice B). As the current life tenant, the girlfriend has a right to all income received from the property. But she also has a duty to pay current charges that become due during the tenancy—including real estate taxes. As a result, the court should decide the rights of the parties accordingly (Choice C).","conclusion":"Answer D: The girlfriend owns an estate for the life of the brother, is entitled to all income, and must pay real estate taxes; the nephew owns the remainder interest."},"questionZh":"一块土地的所有者签署了一份契约，旨在将土地“终身转让给我的兄弟，然后简单地转让给我的侄子”。兄弟开始管理土地，这是宝贵的创收房地产。哥哥收取了所有租金并支付了所有费用，包括房地产税。侄子没有反对，这种情况一直持续到五年后，兄弟签署了一份旨在将土地转让给女友的契约。侄子主张他对土地的所有权。女友还坚称自己拥有房产，并表示如果侄子有任何权利，他就有义务缴纳房产税，尽管哥哥过去已经缴纳过房产税。土地收入足以支付费用，包括房地产税。在确定当事人权利的适当诉讼中，法院应做出什么决定？","choicesZh":{"A":"兄弟的据称行为丧失了他的终身财产，因此侄子免费拥有土地。","B":"女友终身拥有房产，有权获得全部收入，必须缴纳房产税；侄子拥有剩余权益。","C":"女友拥有哥哥一生的遗产，并有权获得全部收入；侄子拥有剩余的利息，并且必须缴纳房地产税。","D":"女友拥有兄弟终生的房产，有权获得全部收入，并且必须缴纳房产税；侄子拥有剩余权益。"},"explanationZh":"终身财产是目前的占有权益，在个人死亡时终止。终身财产可以自由转让，但由于它是根据当前终身租户以外的其他人的生活来衡量的，因此成为“纯粹的终身财产”。终身财产之后的未来利息要么是归还（如果由授予人持有），要么是剩余权益（如果由受让人创建）。当终身财产终止时，它将成为占有权益。在此，业主将终身财产转让给兄弟（“终身转让给我的兄弟”），而侄子收到剩余的简单费用（“然后转让给我的侄子”）（选项A）。然后，兄弟将他目前的所有权权益转让给女友，女友收到了按照兄弟生命衡量的终身财产（选项B）。作为当前的终身租户，女友有权获得从房产中获得的所有收入。但她还有义务支付租赁期间到期的当前费用，包括房地产税。因此，法院应据此判决当事人的权利（选项C）。\n\n教育目标：终身财产是可自由转让的现有土地权益，在个人死亡时终止，随后是归还权益或剩余权益。终身租客有权获得从土地获得的所有收入，但必须支付土地的现行费用（例如房地产税）。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 129–130（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（支付当前费用的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"终身财产是可自由转让的现有土地权益，在个人死亡时终止，随后是归还权益或剩余权益。终身租客有权获得从土地获得的所有收入，但必须支付土地的现行费用（例如房地产税）。","iracZh":{"issue":"在确定当事人权利的适当诉讼中，法院应做出什么决定？","rule":"终身财产是可自由转让的现有土地权益，在个人死亡时终止，随后是归还权益或剩余权益。终身租客有权获得从土地获得的所有收入，但必须支付土地的现行费用（例如房地产税）。","analysis":"终身财产是目前的占有权益，在个人死亡时终止。终身财产可以自由转让，但由于它是根据当前终身租户以外的其他人的生活来衡量的，因此成为“纯粹的终身财产”。终身财产之后的未来利息要么是归还（如果由授予人持有），要么是剩余权益（如果由受让人创建）。当终身财产终止时，它将成为占有权益。在此，业主将终身财产转让给兄弟（“终身转让给我的兄弟”），而侄子收到剩余的简单费用（“然后转让给我的侄子”）（选项A）。然后，兄弟将他目前的所有权权益转让给女友，女友收到了按照兄弟生命衡量的终身财产（选项B）。作为当前的终身租户，女友有权获得从房产中获得的所有收入。但她还有义务支付租赁期间到期的当前费用，包括房地产税。因此，法院应据此判决当事人的权利（选项C）。\n\n教育目标：终身财产是可自由转让的现有土地权益，在个人死亡时终止，随后是归还权益或剩余权益。终身租客有权获得从土地获得的所有收入，但必须支付土地的现行费用（例如房地产税）。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 129–130（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（支付当前费用的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 D：女友拥有兄弟终生的房产，有权获得全部收入，并且必须缴纳房产税；侄子拥有剩余权益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"rents","ipa":"/ɹˈɛnts/","syllables":"rents","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 租金（rent的复数）","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“rents”","wordForm":"词；原形 rent；词形 0:rent/1:s3","sourceSentence":"The brother collected all rents and paid all expenses, including real estate taxes.","synonyms":["rip","split","snag","tear"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-ecbc2b47e7136e92"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":85,"sourceQuestionPage":248,"sourceQuestionPages":[248],"sourceExplanationPages":[249],"sourceAnswerPages":[249],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-086","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A woman conveyed a vacant rural lot by a warranty deed that stated: \"to my daughter for life, and upon her death, to my heirs.\" The next year, the woman executed a quitclaim deed for the same lot to the daughter with the expressed intent \"to make absolute\" the interest granted to her. Four years later, the daughter and her newborn child were involved in a car accident and sustained serious injuries. To pay for their medical expenses, the daughter sold the lot to a real estate developer. The daughter died several months later, leaving the child as her sole heir. The child's legal representative has asked a lawyer for advice regarding ownership of the lot. There is no applicable statute. Who should the lawyer say is the owner of the lot?","choices":{"A":"The child, because the child is the woman's sole heir.","B":"The child, because the child received a remainder following the daughter's life estate.","C":"The developer, because the daughter acquired fee simple title upon the execution of the quitclaim deed.","D":"The developer, because the warranty deed conveyed fee simple title to the daughter."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The doctrine of worthier title is triggered when a grantor conveys property inter vivos to another and, in the same instrument, conveys a future interest to the grantor's heirs. When this occurs, the future interest is rendered void and the grantor is deemed to hold a reversion. Most states have abolished this doctrine by statute, but there is no applicable statute in this jurisdiction. Therefore, the woman's warranty deed conveyed a life estate to the daughter and a reversion to the woman—not a remainder to her heirs (Choices B & D). The woman then conveyed her reversion to the daughter via quitclaim deed—leaving no other interest in the lot for the woman's heirs (Choice A). This means that the daughter held both a present interest (life estate) and future interest (the woman's reversion) in the lot with no intervening interest. As a result, the daughter's two interests merged into a fee simple absolute under the doctrine of merger. And since the daughter subsequently conveyed that interest to the developer, the lawyer should say that the developer owns the lot.\n\nEducational objective: The doctrine of worthier title applies when a grantor makes an inter vivos conveyance to another with a future interest to the grantor's heirs. The future interest is then deemed to create a reversion in the grantor—not a remainder in the grantor's heirs.","educationalObjective":"The doctrine of worthier title applies when a grantor makes an inter vivos conveyance to another with a future interest to the grantor's heirs. The future interest is then deemed to create a reversion in the grantor—not a remainder in the grantor's heirs.","references":"","rule":"The doctrine of worthier title applies when a grantor makes an inter vivos conveyance to another with a future interest to the grantor's heirs. The future interest is then deemed to create a reversion in the grantor—not a remainder in the grantor's heirs.","irac":{"issue":"Who should the lawyer say is the owner of the lot?","rule":"The doctrine of worthier title applies when a grantor makes an inter vivos conveyance to another with a future interest to the grantor's heirs. The future interest is then deemed to create a reversion in the grantor—not a remainder in the grantor's heirs.","analysis":"The doctrine of worthier title is triggered when a grantor conveys property inter vivos to another and, in the same instrument, conveys a future interest to the grantor's heirs. When this occurs, the future interest is rendered void and the grantor is deemed to hold a reversion. Most states have abolished this doctrine by statute, but there is no applicable statute in this jurisdiction. Therefore, the woman's warranty deed conveyed a life estate to the daughter and a reversion to the woman—not a remainder to her heirs (Choices B & D). The woman then conveyed her reversion to the daughter via quitclaim deed—leaving no other interest in the lot for the woman's heirs (Choice A). This means that the daughter held both a present interest (life estate) and future interest (the woman's reversion) in the lot with no intervening interest. As a result, the daughter's two interests merged into a fee simple absolute under the doctrine of merger. And since the daughter subsequently conveyed that interest to the developer, the lawyer should say that the developer owns the lot.","conclusion":"Answer C: The developer, because the daughter acquired fee simple title upon the execution of the quitclaim deed."},"questionZh":"一名妇女通过一份保证契约转让了一块空置的农村土地，契约上写着：“终身归我女儿，她死后，归我的继承人。”第二年，该妇女向女儿签署了同一批土地的无偿债权契约，并明确表示要“绝对”授予她的权益。四年后，女儿和刚出生的孩子遭遇车祸，受重伤。为了支付医疗费用，女儿将土地卖给了房地产开发商。几个月后，女儿去世，孩子成为她唯一的继承人。孩子的法律代表已向律师寻求有关该土地所有权的建议。没有适用的法规。律师应该说谁是该地块的所有者？","choicesZh":{"A":"孩子，因为孩子是女人唯一的继承人。","B":"孩子，因为孩子收到了女儿终身遗产的剩余部分。","C":"开发商，因为女儿在执行无偿债权契约后获得了简单的产权。","D":"开发商，因为保修契约将简单的所有权转让给了女儿。"},"explanationZh":"当设保人将生前财产转让给另一人，并在同一文书中将未来权益转让给设保人的继承人时，就会触发更有价值的所有权原则。当这种情况发生时，未来利息无效，并且授予人被视为持有归还权。大多数州已通过法规废除了这一原则，但该管辖区没有适用的法规。因此，该妇女的保证契约将终身财产转移给了女儿，并将财产归还给了妇女，而不是将剩余财产转移给了她的继承人（Choices B & D）。然后，该妇女通过放弃所有权契约将她的归还给了女儿——没有为该妇女的继承人留下对该地块的其他权益（选择A）。这意味着女儿对该地段同时拥有当前权益（终生财产）和未来权益（妇女的归还），没有任何干预权益。结果，女儿的两项权益根据合并原则合并为简单绝对权益。由于女儿随后将该权益转达给开发商，律师应该说开发商拥有该地块。\n\n教育目标：当设保人向另一名设保人的继承人拥有未来利益的人进行生前转让时，适用更有价值的所有权原则。未来的利息将被视为对设保人进行归还，而不是对设保人的继承人进行剩余。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当设保人向设保人的继承人拥有未来利益的另一人进行生前转让时，适用更有价值的所有权原则。未来的利息将被视为对设保人进行归还，而不是对设保人的继承人进行剩余。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师应该说谁是该地块的所有者？","rule":"当设保人向设保人的继承人拥有未来利益的另一人进行生前转让时，适用更有价值的所有权原则。未来的利息将被视为对设保人进行归还，而不是对设保人的继承人进行剩余。","analysis":"当设保人将生前财产转让给另一人，并在同一文书中将未来权益转让给设保人的继承人时，就会触发更有价值的所有权原则。当这种情况发生时，未来利息无效，并且授予人被视为持有归还权。大多数州已通过法规废除了这一原则，但该管辖区没有适用的法规。因此，该妇女的保证契约将终身财产转移给了女儿，并将财产归还给了妇女，而不是将剩余财产转移给了她的继承人（Choices B & D）。然后，该妇女通过放弃所有权契约将她的归还给了女儿——没有为该妇女的继承人留下对该地块的其他权益（选择A）。这意味着女儿对该地段同时拥有当前权益（终生财产）和未来权益（妇女的归还），没有任何干预权益。结果，女儿的两项权益根据合并原则合并为简单绝对权益。由于女儿随后将该权益转达给开发商，律师应该说开发商拥有该地块。\n\n教育目标：当设保人向另一名设保人的继承人拥有未来利益的人进行生前转让时，适用更有价值的所有权原则。未来的利息将被视为对设保人进行归还，而不是对设保人的继承人进行剩余。","conclusion":"答案 C：开发商，因为女儿在执行无偿债权契约后获得了简单的产权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"fee simple absolute","ipa":"/fˈi sˈɪmpəl ˈæbsʌlˌut/","syllables":"fee sim·ple ab·solute","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"绝对完全所有权：期限无限且不附终止条件的最大土地权益","contextualMeaning":"绝对完全所有权：期限无限且不附终止条件的最大土地权益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"As a result, the daughter's two interests merged into a fee simple absolute under the doctrine of merger.","synonyms":["absolute fee"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-59e41e9c027a8c20"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":86,"sourceQuestionPage":250,"sourceQuestionPages":[250],"sourceExplanationPages":[251,252],"sourceAnswerPages":[251,252],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-087","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A man's estate plan included a revocable trust established 35 years ago with a bank as trustee. The principal asset of the trust has always been a very profitable, debt-free office building. The trust instrument instructs the trustee to pay the net income to the man for life; and, after the death of the man, to pay the net income to his wife for life; and, after her death, \"to distribute the net trust estate as she may appoint by will, or in default of her exercise of this power of appointment, to my son (her stepson).\" The man died 30 years ago, survived by his wife and son. The man had not revoked or amended the trust agreement. A few years after the man's death, his wife remarried. She then had a daughter, was widowed a second time, and died last year. Her will contained only one dispositive provision: \"I give my entire estate to my daughter, and I intentionally make no provision for my stepson.\" The daughter is now 22 years old. The common-law Rule Against Perpetuities is unmodified by statute in the jurisdiction. There are no other applicable statutes. The son brought an appropriate action against the daughter to determine who was entitled to the net trust estate and thus to the office building. If the court rules for the daughter, what will be the reason?","choices":{"A":"The jurisdiction deems \"entire estate\" to be a reference to the office building or to the wife's power of appointment.","B":"The Rule Against Perpetuities does not apply to powers of appointment.","C":"The wife intended that the son should not benefit by reason of her death.","D":"The wife's life estate and power of appointment merge into complete ownership in the wife."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The man's trust instrument conveys the net income of the trust estate to the man for life, then to the wife for life with a power of appointment subject to the son's future interest. The powerholder has the right to designate the recipients of a beneficial property interest (eg, trust). This power can be either: general – if there are no limitations on the disposition of the property or\n\nnon-general – if there are limitations on the disposition of the property (eg, restrictions on the recipients, time, or manner of the disposition)\n\nHere, the wife received a non-general power of appointment since she may dispose of the net trust estate only \"by will\" (ie, devise). If she fails to do so, then the property will go to the designated remainderman (here, the son). But since the wife devised her entire estate to her daughter, the reason for the daughter prevailing would be that this jurisdiction considers \"entire estate\" to be a reference to the office building or the wife's power of appointment. (Choice B) The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) does apply to powers of appointment, so the exercise of this power is valid only if a trust would vest or fail to vest by the end of the life in being plus 21 years. Here, the appointment would have vested or failed immediately upon the wife's death, so RAP was not violated. (Choice C) To exercise the power of appointment, the wife had to designate the recipient of the net trust estate. The fact that the wife identified the stepson as a nonrecipient is irrelevant. (Choice D) A life estate and power of appointment do not merge into complete ownership (ie, a fee estate). However, the designated recipient will receive complete ownership of the property.\n\nEducational objective: The power of appointment gives a life tenant the right to designate recipients of a beneficial property interest. If the life tenant does not exercise this power, then the property will go to the designated remainderman.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Donative Transfers § 12.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1986) (power of appointment).","educationalObjective":"The power of appointment gives a life tenant the right to designate recipients of a beneficial property interest. If the life tenant does not exercise this power, then the property will go to the designated remainderman.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property: Donative Transfers § 12.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1986) (power of appointment).","rule":"The power of appointment gives a life tenant the right to designate recipients of a beneficial property interest. If the life tenant does not exercise this power, then the property will go to the designated remainderman.","irac":{"issue":"If the court rules for the daughter, what will be the reason?","rule":"The power of appointment gives a life tenant the right to designate recipients of a beneficial property interest. If the life tenant does not exercise this power, then the property will go to the designated remainderman.","analysis":"The man's trust instrument conveys the net income of the trust estate to the man for life, then to the wife for life with a power of appointment subject to the son's future interest. The powerholder has the right to designate the recipients of a beneficial property interest (eg, trust). This power can be either: general – if there are no limitations on the disposition of the property or\n\nnon-general – if there are limitations on the disposition of the property (eg, restrictions on the recipients, time, or manner of the disposition)\n\nHere, the wife received a non-general power of appointment since she may dispose of the net trust estate only \"by will\" (ie, devise). If she fails to do so, then the property will go to the designated remainderman (here, the son). But since the wife devised her entire estate to her daughter, the reason for the daughter prevailing would be that this jurisdiction considers \"entire estate\" to be a reference to the office building or the wife's power of appointment. (Choice B) The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) does apply to powers of appointment, so the exercise of this power is valid only if a trust would vest or fail to vest by the end of the life in being plus 21 years. Here, the appointment would have vested or failed immediately upon the wife's death, so RAP was not violated. (Choice C) To exercise the power of appointment, the wife had to designate the recipient of the net trust estate. The fact that the wife identified the stepson as a nonrecipient is irrelevant. (Choice D) A life estate and power of appointment do not merge into complete ownership (ie, a fee estate). However, the designated recipient will receive complete ownership of the property.","conclusion":"Answer A: The jurisdiction deems \"entire estate\" to be a reference to the office building or to the wife's power of appointment."},"questionZh":"一名男子的遗产计划包括 35 年前设立的可撤销信托，并由一家银行作为受托人。该信托的主要资产一直是一座利润丰厚、无债务的办公楼。信托文书指示受托人将净收入终身支付给该男子；并在男方去世后，将净收入支付给其妻子终身；并且，在她去世后，“将她可能通过遗嘱指定的净信托财产分配给我的儿子（她的继子），或者如果她没有行使这种指定权的话。”该男子已于 30 年前去世，身后留下妻子和儿子。该男子并未撤销或修改信托协议。该男子去世几年后，他的妻子再婚。随后她生了一个女儿，第二次守寡，并于去年去世。她的遗嘱只包含一项决定性条款：“我将全部财产交给我的女儿，并且我故意不为我的继子做任何准备。”女儿现年22岁。普通法中的“反对永续规则”未经司法管辖区的法规修改。没有其他适用的法规。儿子对女儿提起适当诉讼，以确定谁有权获得净信托财产，从而有权获得办公楼。如果法院判决女儿胜诉，理由是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"司法管辖区认为“整个遗产”是指办公楼或妻子的任命权。","B":"反对永久规则不适用于任命权。","C":"妻子的意思是儿子不应该因为她的死而受益。","D":"妻子的终身财产和任命权合并为妻子的完全所有权。"},"explanationZh":"男方的信托工具将信托财产的净收入终身转让给男方，然后终身转让给妻子，并根据儿子的未来利息授予指定权。权力持有者有权指定受益财产权益（例如信托）的接收者。该权力可以是： 一般权力——如果对财产的处置没有限制，或者\n\n非一般性——如果对财产的处置有限制（例如，对接收者、时间或处置方式的限制）\n\n在这里，妻子获得了非一般性的指定权力，因为她只能“按意愿”（即设计）处置净信托财产。如果她不这样做，那么财产将归指定的剩余人（此处为儿子）所有。但由于妻子将其全部遗产分配给了女儿，因此女儿胜诉的原因是该司法管辖区认为“全部遗产”是指办公楼或妻子的指定权。 （选择 B） 反对永续规则 (RAP) 确实适用于指定权，因此只有当信托在 21 年以上的生命周期结束时归属或未能归属时，该权力的行使才有效。在这里，任命将在妻子去世后立即生效或失败，因此 RAP 没有被违反。 （选项C） 为了行使指定权，妻子必须指定净信托财产的接收人。妻子认定继子为非受助人这一事实无关紧要。 （选项D）终身财产和指定权不合并为完全所有权（即费用财产）。然而，指定的接收者将获得该财产的完整所有权。\n\n教育目标：指定权赋予终身租户指定受益财产权益接收人的权利。如果终身租户不行使此权力，则财产将归指定的剩余人所有。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：捐赠转让 § 12.1（Am. Law Inst. 1986）（任命权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"指定权赋予终身租户指定受益财产权益接收人的权利。如果终身租户不行使此权力，则财产将归指定的剩余人所有。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院判决女儿胜诉，理由是什么？","rule":"指定权赋予终身租户指定受益财产权益接收人的权利。如果终身租户不行使此权力，则财产将归指定的剩余人所有。","analysis":"男方的信托工具将信托财产的净收入终身转让给男方，然后终身转让给妻子，并根据儿子的未来利息授予指定权。权力持有者有权指定受益财产权益（例如信托）的接收者。该权力可以是： 一般权力——如果对财产的处置没有限制，或者\n\n非一般性——如果对财产的处置有限制（例如，对接收者、时间或处置方式的限制）\n\n在这里，妻子获得了非一般性的指定权力，因为她只能“按意愿”（即设计）处置净信托财产。如果她不这样做，那么财产将归指定的剩余人（此处为儿子）所有。但由于妻子将其全部遗产分配给了女儿，因此女儿胜诉的原因是该司法管辖区认为“全部遗产”是指办公楼或妻子的指定权。 （选择 B） 反对永续规则 (RAP) 确实适用于指定权，因此只有当信托在 21 年以上的生命周期结束时归属或未能归属时，该权力的行使才有效。在这里，任命将在妻子去世后立即生效或失败，因此 RAP 没有被违反。 （选项C） 为了行使指定权，妻子必须指定净信托财产的接收人。妻子认定继子为非受助人这一事实无关紧要。 （选项D）终身财产和指定权不合并为完全所有权（即费用财产）。然而，指定的接收者将获得该财产的完整所有权。\n\n教育目标：指定权赋予终身租户指定受益财产权益接收人的权利。如果终身租户不行使此权力，则财产将归指定的剩余人所有。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：捐赠转让 § 12.1（Am. Law Inst. 1986）（任命权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：司法管辖区认为“整个遗产”是指办公楼或妻子的任命权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"rule against perpetuities","ipa":"/ɹˈul ʌɡˈɛnst pˌɝpɪtjˈuɪti/","syllables":"rule against per·pe·tu·ities","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"反永久权规则：特定或有未来权益必须在法定存续期内确定归属","contextualMeaning":"反永久权规则：特定或有未来权益必须在法定存续期内确定归属","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The common-law Rule Against Perpetuities is unmodified by statute in the jurisdiction.","synonyms":["RAP","common-law perpetuities rule"]},{"term":"profitable","ipa":"/pɹˈɑfətəbəl/","syllables":"prof·itable","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 有利润的, 有利益的, 赚钱的；[经] 有利(可图)的, 合算的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“profitable”","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"The principal asset of the trust has always been a very profitable, debt-free office building.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-fbdfa33ac4ec3e34"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":87,"sourceQuestionPage":253,"sourceQuestionPages":[253],"sourceExplanationPages":[254,255],"sourceAnswerPages":[254,255],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-088","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A landlord owns a building that contains six living units. The landlord lives in one of the units and rents out the other five. When a tenant with a two-year-old child moved out of one of the units, the landlord decided that he would not rent to a tenant with a child under the age of 10. However, when he advertised that the unit was available for rent, he did not include this limitation in the ad and instead indicated that all families were welcome. A couple with a six-year-old child contacted the landlord about renting the unit, and he informed them of his policy regarding children. The couple claimed that the landlord's policy violated the federal Fair Housing Act. Rather than dispute this with the couple, the landlord decided to take the unit off the market and allow it to remain empty for the foreseeable future. Has the landlord violated the federal Fair Housing Act?","choices":{"A":"No, because he lived in one of the units in the building.","B":"No, because he took the unit off the market and has not rented it to anyone.","C":"Yes, because he discriminated against the couple based on familial status.","D":"Yes, because his advertisement falsely represented his policy regarding children."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Fair Housing Act\n\nApplicability Prohibits discriminatory actions in sale/renting of dwelling based on race, color, religion, sex, handicap, national origin, or familial status—including:\n\nrefusing bona fide offer discriminating in terms, conditions, privileges, or services of purchase/rental advertising preference for or discrimination against buyer/renter falsely representing dwelling's availability for inspection, sale, or rental inducing sale/renting with discriminatory representations about neighborhood denying reasonable modifications to accommodate handicap at occupant's expense Exceptions Act does not apply to:\n\nreligious organizations private clubs that incidentally provide lodging to members familial status for senior housing owner of ≤ 3 single-family dwellings* owner-occupied dwellings with ≤ 4 units* sale/rental of single-family home by private owner *These exceptions do not apply to discriminatory advertisements or when owner is assisted by a real estate agent, broker, or salesman.\n\nThe Fair Housing Act (FHA) prohibits discrimination in the sale or rental of housing on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, sex, disability, or familial status. Protected familial status includes having children under the age of 18 and being pregnant. Prohibited discrimination can take various forms (see above table), such as refusing to rent or sell housing or making housing unavailable. Here, the landlord attempted to circumvent the FHA by taking the unit off the market and not renting it to anyone. But since the landlord made the unit unavailable to avoid renting to the couple with the six-year-old child, he discriminated against the couple on the basis of familial status in violation of the FHA (Choice B). (Choice A) Owner-occupied buildings with no more than four living units (including the owner's unit) are generally exempted from the FHA. Since the landlord's building contains six living units, his occupation of one of the units does not qualify him for this exemption.\n\n(Choice D) Advertising a preference or limitation based on protected characteristics is a violation of the FHA. However, the landlord's failure to advertise that he would not rent to a tenant with a child under the age of 10 is not a violation.\n\nEducational objective: The Fair Housing Act prohibits discrimination in the sale or rental of housing (eg, making housing unavailable) on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, sex, disability, or familial status. Protected familial status includes having children under the age of 18 and being pregnant.\n\nReferences\nFair Housing Act, 42 U.S.C. §§ 3601 et seq.","educationalObjective":"The Fair Housing Act prohibits discrimination in the sale or rental of housing (eg, making housing unavailable) on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, sex, disability, or familial status. Protected familial status includes having children under the age of 18 and being pregnant.","references":"Fair Housing Act, 42 U.S.C. §§ 3601 et seq.","rule":"The Fair Housing Act prohibits discrimination in the sale or rental of housing (eg, making housing unavailable) on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, sex, disability, or familial status. Protected familial status includes having children under the age of 18 and being pregnant.","irac":{"issue":"Has the landlord violated the federal Fair Housing Act?","rule":"The Fair Housing Act prohibits discrimination in the sale or rental of housing (eg, making housing unavailable) on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, sex, disability, or familial status. Protected familial status includes having children under the age of 18 and being pregnant.","analysis":"Fair Housing Act\n\nApplicability Prohibits discriminatory actions in sale/renting of dwelling based on race, color, religion, sex, handicap, national origin, or familial status—including:\n\nrefusing bona fide offer discriminating in terms, conditions, privileges, or services of purchase/rental advertising preference for or discrimination against buyer/renter falsely representing dwelling's availability for inspection, sale, or rental inducing sale/renting with discriminatory representations about neighborhood denying reasonable modifications to accommodate handicap at occupant's expense Exceptions Act does not apply to:\n\nreligious organizations private clubs that incidentally provide lodging to members familial status for senior housing owner of ≤ 3 single-family dwellings* owner-occupied dwellings with ≤ 4 units* sale/rental of single-family home by private owner *These exceptions do not apply to discriminatory advertisements or when owner is assisted by a real estate agent, broker, or salesman.\n\nThe Fair Housing Act (FHA) prohibits discrimination in the sale or rental of housing on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, sex, disability, or familial status. Protected familial status includes having children under the age of 18 and being pregnant. Prohibited discrimination can take various forms (see above table), such as refusing to rent or sell housing or making housing unavailable. Here, the landlord attempted to circumvent the FHA by taking the unit off the market and not renting it to anyone. But since the landlord made the unit unavailable to avoid renting to the couple with the six-year-old child, he discriminated against the couple on the basis of familial status in violation of the FHA (Choice B). (Choice A) Owner-occupied buildings with no more than four living units (including the owner's unit) are generally exempted from the FHA. Since the landlord's building contains six living units, his occupation of one of the units does not qualify him for this exemption.\n\n(Choice D) Advertising a preference or limitation based on protected characteristics is a violation of the FHA. However, the landlord's failure to advertise that he would not rent to a tenant with a child under the age of 10 is not a violation.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because he discriminated against the couple based on familial status."},"questionZh":"房东拥有一栋包含六个居住单元的建筑物。房东住在其中一套单元中，并出租了另外五套单元。当一名带着两岁孩子的租客搬出其中一间单位时，房东决定不会租给有十岁以下孩子的租客。然而，当他在广告中宣传该单位可供出租时，他并没有在广告中注明这一限制，而是表示欢迎所有家庭。一对带着六岁孩子的夫妇联系房东询问租用该单元的事宜，房东向他们告知了有关儿童的政策。这对夫妇声称房东的政策违反了联邦公平住房法。房东没有与这对夫妇争论这一点，而是决定将该单位从市场上撤下，并在可预见的未来让它保持空置状态。房东是否违反了联邦公平住房法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为他住在大楼的一个单元里。","B":"不，因为他将该单位从市场上撤下，并且没有租给任何人。","C":"是的，因为他基于家庭地位歧视这对夫妇。","D":"是的，因为他的广告错误地体现了他关于儿童的政策。"},"explanationZh":"公平住房法\n\n适用性 禁止在出售/租赁住宅时出现基于种族、肤色、宗教、性别、残障、国籍或家庭状况的歧视行为，包括：\n\n拒绝善意报价 在购买/租赁的条款、条件、特权或服务方面存在歧视 对买方/承租人的偏好或歧视 虚假陈述住宅可供检查、出售或出租 诱导出售/租赁 对社区进行歧视性陈述 拒绝合理改造以适应残障情况，费用由居住者承担 例外法不适用于：\n\n宗教组织 附带向会员提供住宿的私人俱乐部 ≤ 3 套单户住宅的老年住房所有者* 拥有 ≤ 4 套单元的自住住宅* 私人业主出售/出租单户住宅 *这些例外情况不适用于歧视性广告或业主得到房地产经纪人、经纪人或推销员协助的情况。\n\n《公平住房法》(FHA) 禁止在住房销售或租赁中存在基于种族、肤色、宗教、国籍、性别、残疾或家庭状况的歧视。受保护的家庭状况包括有 18 岁以下的孩子和怀孕。禁止的歧视可以采取多种形式（见上表），例如拒绝出租或出售住房或无法提供住房。在这里，房东试图通过将单位从市场上撤下并且不出租给任何人来规避联邦住房管理局 (FHA)。但由于房东为了避免租给这对带着六岁孩子的夫妇而拒绝提供该单位，因此他基于家庭状况歧视了这对夫妇，违反了联邦住房管理局的规定（选择 B）。 （选择 A） 居住单元不超过 4 个的自住建筑（包括业主的单元）通常免于缴纳 FHA。由于房东的建筑物包含六个居住单元，因此他占用其中一个单元并不符合此项豁免的资格。\n\n（选项 D）宣传基于受保护特征的偏好或限制违反了联邦住房管理局 (FHA) 的规定。然而，房东未在广告中表明他不会出租给有 10 岁以下儿童的租户，这并不构成违规。\n\n教育目标：《公平住房法》禁止在出售或租赁住房时基于种族、肤色、宗教、国籍、性别、残疾或家庭状况的歧视（例如，无法提供住房）。受保护的家庭状况包括有 18 岁以下的孩子和怀孕。\n\n参考文献\n《公平住房法》，42 U.S.C. §§ 3601 及以下。","educationalObjectiveZh":"《公平住房法》禁止在出售或租赁住房时基于种族、肤色、宗教、国籍、性别、残疾或家庭状况的歧视（例如，无法获得住房）。受保护的家庭状况包括有 18 岁以下的孩子和怀孕。","iracZh":{"issue":"房东是否违反了联邦公平住房法？","rule":"《公平住房法》禁止在出售或租赁住房时基于种族、肤色、宗教、国籍、性别、残疾或家庭状况的歧视（例如，无法获得住房）。受保护的家庭状况包括有 18 岁以下的孩子和怀孕。","analysis":"公平住房法\n\n适用性 禁止在出售/租赁住宅时出现基于种族、肤色、宗教、性别、残障、国籍或家庭状况的歧视行为，包括：\n\n拒绝善意报价 在购买/租赁的条款、条件、特权或服务方面存在歧视 对买方/承租人的偏好或歧视 虚假陈述住宅可供检查、出售或出租 诱导出售/租赁 对社区进行歧视性陈述 拒绝合理改造以适应残障情况，费用由居住者承担 例外法不适用于：\n\n宗教组织 附带向会员提供住宿的私人俱乐部 ≤ 3 套单户住宅的老年住房所有者* 拥有 ≤ 4 套单元的自住住宅* 私人业主出售/出租单户住宅 *这些例外情况不适用于歧视性广告或业主得到房地产经纪人、经纪人或推销员协助的情况。\n\n《公平住房法》(FHA) 禁止在住房销售或租赁中存在基于种族、肤色、宗教、国籍、性别、残疾或家庭状况的歧视。受保护的家庭状况包括有 18 岁以下的孩子和怀孕。禁止的歧视可以采取多种形式（见上表），例如拒绝出租或出售住房或无法提供住房。在这里，房东试图通过将单位从市场上撤下并且不出租给任何人来规避联邦住房管理局 (FHA)。但由于房东为了避免租给这对带着六岁孩子的夫妇而拒绝提供该单位，因此他基于家庭状况歧视了这对夫妇，违反了联邦住房管理局的规定（选择 B）。 （选择 A） 居住单元不超过 4 个的自住建筑（包括业主的单元）通常免于缴纳 FHA。由于房东的建筑物包含六个居住单元，因此他占用其中一个单元并不符合此项豁免的资格。\n\n（选项 D）宣传基于受保护特征的偏好或限制违反了联邦住房管理局 (FHA) 的规定。然而，房东未在广告中表明他不会出租给有 10 岁以下儿童的租户，这并不构成违规。\n\n教育目标：《公平住房法》禁止在出售或租赁住房时基于种族、肤色、宗教、国籍、性别、残疾或家庭状况的歧视（例如，无法提供住房）。受保护的家庭状况包括有 18 岁以下的孩子和怀孕。\n\n参考文献\n《公平住房法》，42 U.S.C. §§ 3601 及以下。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为他基于家庭地位歧视这对夫妇。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"incidentally","ipa":"/ˌɪnsɪdˈɛntəli/","syllables":"in·ci·den·tally","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 伴随地, 偶然, 顺便","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“incidentally”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Fair Housing Act Applicability Prohibits discriminatory actions in sale/renting of dwelling based on race, color, religion, sex, handicap, national origin, or familial status—including: refusing bona fide offer discriminating in terms, conditions, privileges, or services of purchase/rental advertising preference for or discrimination against buyer/renter falsely representing dwelling's availability for inspection, sa","synonyms":["by the way","by the bye","apropos","accidentally"]},{"term":"renting","ipa":"/ɹˈɛntɪŋ/","syllables":"rent·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 租用；出租（rent的ing形式）","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“renting”","wordForm":"词；词形 0:rent/1:i/i:renting","sourceSentence":"A couple with a six-year-old child contacted the landlord about renting the unit, and he informed them of his policy regarding children.","synonyms":["rental"]},{"term":"renter","ipa":"/ɹˈɛntɝ/","syllables":"renter","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 承租人, 佃户, 出租人；[经] 租赁人, 租户, 出租人","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“renter”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:renters","sourceSentence":"Fair Housing Act Applicability Prohibits discriminatory actions in sale/renting of dwelling based on race, color, religion, sex, handicap, national origin, or familial status—including: refusing bona fide offer discriminating in terms, conditions, privileges, or services of purchase/rental advertising preference for or discrimination against buyer/renter falsely representing dwelling's availability for inspection, sa","synonyms":["tenant"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":88,"sourceQuestionPage":256,"sourceQuestionPages":[256],"sourceExplanationPages":[257,258],"sourceAnswerPages":[257,258],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-089","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"An entrepreneur owned a ranch. He entered into a written three-year lease of the ranch with a rancher. Among other provisions, the lease prohibited the rancher from \"assigning this lease, in whole or in part, and from subletting the ranch, in whole or in part.\" In addition to a house, a barn, and a one-car garage, the ranch's 30 acres included several fields where first the entrepreneur, and now the rancher, grazed sheep. During the following months, by a written agreement, the rancher allowed his neighbor, a car enthusiast, exclusive use of the garage for storage, under lock and key, of his antique automobile for two years, charging him $240. The rancher told his other neighbor, a golfer, that she could use the fields to practice golf as long as she did not disturb his sheep. Which, if any, of the rancher's actions constituted a violation of the lease?","choices":{"A":"Only allowing the car enthusiast exclusive use of the garage for storage.","B":"Only allowing the golfer to use the fields to practice her golf.","C":"Both actions.","D":"Neither action."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Unless the lease expressly provides otherwise, a tenant is free to transfer his/her interest under the lease to a third party through either: assignment – a transfer of all, or part of, the tenant's interest under the lease to a third party for the remainder of the tenant's lease term or subletting – a transfer of all, or part of, the tenant's interest under the lease to a third party for less than the remainder of the tenant's lease term. Here, the entrepreneur leased his ranch to the rancher for three years. The lease expressly prohibited the rancher from assigning or subletting the ranch, in whole or in part. The rancher then entered into a written agreement that gave the car enthusiast exclusive possession of the ranch's garage (part of the rancher's lease interest) for two years (less than the remainder of the rancher's lease term) in exchange for $240. Therefore, this written agreement was a sublease and violated the entrepreneur and rancher's lease (Choices B & D). A tenant also is generally free to grant a license—a non-possessory (and usually non- exclusive) right to enter and use the property for a particular purpose—to a third party unless the lease states otherwise. Here, the lease only prohibited assigning and subletting. Therefore, the rancher's license to the golfer—granting her a non-exclusive right to enter and use the property to practice golf—did not violate the lease (Choices B & C).\n\nEducational objective: A tenant generally has the right to (1) transfer his/her interest under a lease for the remainder (assignment) or part (sublease) of the lease term or (2) grant a non-possessory right to use the property for a particular purpose to another (license). However, these rights can be restricted by an express lease provision.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant §§ 15.1–15.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (assignments and subleases).\n\nRestatement of Prop. § 512 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (licenses).","educationalObjective":"A tenant generally has the right to (1) transfer his/her interest under a lease for the remainder (assignment) or part (sublease) of the lease term or (2) grant a non-possessory right to use the property for a particular purpose to another (license). However, these rights can be restricted by an express lease provision.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant §§ 15.1–15.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (assignments and subleases).\n\nRestatement of Prop. § 512 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (licenses).","rule":"A tenant generally has the right to (1) transfer his/her interest under a lease for the remainder (assignment) or part (sublease) of the lease term or (2) grant a non-possessory right to use the property for a particular purpose to another (license). However, these rights can be restricted by an express lease provision.","irac":{"issue":"Which, if any, of the rancher's actions constituted a violation of the lease?","rule":"A tenant generally has the right to (1) transfer his/her interest under a lease for the remainder (assignment) or part (sublease) of the lease term or (2) grant a non-possessory right to use the property for a particular purpose to another (license). However, these rights can be restricted by an express lease provision.","analysis":"Unless the lease expressly provides otherwise, a tenant is free to transfer his/her interest under the lease to a third party through either: assignment – a transfer of all, or part of, the tenant's interest under the lease to a third party for the remainder of the tenant's lease term or subletting – a transfer of all, or part of, the tenant's interest under the lease to a third party for less than the remainder of the tenant's lease term. Here, the entrepreneur leased his ranch to the rancher for three years. The lease expressly prohibited the rancher from assigning or subletting the ranch, in whole or in part. The rancher then entered into a written agreement that gave the car enthusiast exclusive possession of the ranch's garage (part of the rancher's lease interest) for two years (less than the remainder of the rancher's lease term) in exchange for $240. Therefore, this written agreement was a sublease and violated the entrepreneur and rancher's lease (Choices B & D). A tenant also is generally free to grant a license—a non-possessory (and usually non- exclusive) right to enter and use the property for a particular purpose—to a third party unless the lease states otherwise. Here, the lease only prohibited assigning and subletting. Therefore, the rancher's license to the golfer—granting her a non-exclusive right to enter and use the property to practice golf—did not violate the lease (Choices B & C).","conclusion":"Answer A: Only allowing the car enthusiast exclusive use of the garage for storage."},"questionZh":"一位企业家拥有一座牧场。他与一位牧场主签订了一份为期三年的书面牧场租约。除其他条款外，租约禁止牧场主“全部或部分转让该租约，以及全部或部分转租牧场”。除了一栋房子、一个谷仓和一个可容纳一辆车的车库外，牧场占地 30 英亩，还包括几块田地，最初是企业家，现在是牧场主，在那里放牧羊群。在接下来的几个月里，牧场主通过一份书面协议，允许他的邻居（一位汽车爱好者）独家使用车库来存放他的古董汽车，并锁上钥匙，为期两年，收费为 240 美元。牧场主告诉他的另一个邻居，一位高尔夫球手，只要不打扰他的羊，她就可以用这些田地练习高尔夫球。牧场主的哪些行为（如果有）构成了违反租约的行为？","choicesZh":{"A":"只允许汽车爱好者专用车库进行存储。","B":"只允许高尔夫球手使用场地练习高尔夫球。","C":"两个动作。","D":"既没有动作。"},"explanationZh":"除非租约另有明确规定，承租人可以通过以下任一方式自由将其在租约下的权益转让给第三方：转让 - 在承租人剩余租赁期限内将承租人在租约下的全部或部分权益转让给第三方；或转租 - 在少于承租人剩余租赁期限的时间内将承租人在租约下的全部或部分权益转让给第三方。在这里，企业家将自己的牧场租给牧场主三年。租约明确禁止牧场主全部或部分转让或转租牧场。然后，牧场主签订了一份书面协议，给予汽车爱好者两年（少于牧场主剩余租赁期限）独家拥有牧场车库（牧场主租赁权益的一部分）的权利，以换取 240 美元。因此，这份书面协议属于转租，违反了企业家和牧场主的租约（Choices B & D）。除非租约另有规定，租户通常还可以自由地向第三方授予许可证，即出于特定目的进入和使用该财产的非占有（通常是非排他性）权利。在这里，租约仅禁止转让和转租。因此，牧场主向高尔夫球手颁发的许可证——授予她进入并使用该房产练习高尔夫球的非排他性权利——并不违反租约（选择 B 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：租户通常有权 (1) 在租赁期的剩余时间（转让）或部分时间（转租）内转让其在租赁下的权益，或 (2) 向他人授予将财产用于特定目的的非占有性权利（许可）。然而，这些权利可能受到明确租赁条款的限制。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户 §§ 15.1–15.2（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（转让和转租）。\n\n第 512 条提案重述（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（许可证）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"租户通常有权 (1) 在租赁期的剩余时间（转让）或部分（转租）内转让其在租赁下的权益，或 (2) 向他人授予将财产用于特定目的的非占有性权利（许可）。然而，这些权利可能受到明确租赁条款的限制。","iracZh":{"issue":"牧场主的哪些行为（如果有）构成了违反租约的行为？","rule":"租户通常有权 (1) 在租赁期的剩余时间（转让）或部分（转租）内转让其在租赁下的权益，或 (2) 向他人授予将财产用于特定目的的非占有性权利（许可）。然而，这些权利可能受到明确租赁条款的限制。","analysis":"除非租约另有明确规定，承租人可以通过以下任一方式自由将其在租约下的权益转让给第三方：转让 - 在承租人剩余租赁期限内将承租人在租约下的全部或部分权益转让给第三方；或转租 - 在少于承租人剩余租赁期限的时间内将承租人在租约下的全部或部分权益转让给第三方。在这里，企业家将自己的牧场租给牧场主三年。租约明确禁止牧场主全部或部分转让或转租牧场。然后，牧场主签订了一份书面协议，给予汽车爱好者两年（少于牧场主剩余租赁期限）独家拥有牧场车库（牧场主租赁权益的一部分）的权利，以换取 240 美元。因此，这份书面协议属于转租，违反了企业家和牧场主的租约（Choices B & D）。除非租约另有规定，租户通常还可以自由地向第三方授予许可证，即出于特定目的进入和使用该财产的非占有（通常是非排他性）权利。在这里，租约仅禁止转让和转租。因此，牧场主向高尔夫球手颁发的许可证——授予她进入并使用该房产练习高尔夫球的非排他性权利——并不违反租约（选择 B 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：租户通常有权 (1) 在租赁期的剩余时间（转让）或部分时间（转租）内转让其在租赁下的权益，或 (2) 向他人授予将财产用于特定目的的非占有性权利（许可）。然而，这些权利可能受到明确租赁条款的限制。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户 §§ 15.1–15.2（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（转让和转租）。\n\n第 512 条提案重述（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（许可证）。","conclusion":"答案 A：只允许汽车爱好者专用车库进行存储。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"sublease","ipa":"/sˈʌblˌis/","syllables":"sub·lease","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 转租；vt. 转租, 分租","contextualMeaning":"转租：承租人转让少于全部剩余租期并保留复归权益","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:subleases/p:subleased/d:subleased/i:subleasing/3:subleases","sourceSentence":"A tenant generally has the right to (1) transfer his/her interest under a lease for the remainder (assignment) or part (sublease) of the lease term or (2) grant a non-possessory right to use the property for a particular purpose to another (license).","synonyms":["sublet"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-250fe2d8cb876499"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":89,"sourceQuestionPage":259,"sourceQuestionPages":[259],"sourceExplanationPages":[260,261],"sourceAnswerPages":[260,261],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-090","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A woman owned a four-unit apartment building and lived in one of the units. When one of her tenants vacated his apartment, the woman placed an advertisement in the local paper that read as follows: \"Large two-bedroom apartment available for rent. White male preferred.\" The woman's preference was motivated by the fact that she liked to have a mix of tenants of both genders and from various racial and ethnic backgrounds in her building, and of the remaining rented units, one was rented to an African American man and the other to a Pacific Islander woman. Based upon these facts, which of the following statements is true?","choices":{"A":"The federal Fair Housing Act makes it illegal for the woman to refuse to rent her units to prospective tenants because of their race or gender.","B":"The woman's motive absolves her from any liability under the federal Fair Housing Act.","C":"There are no violations of any federal laws under these facts.","D":"Under the federal Fair Housing Act, the woman was not permitted to state a racial or gender preference in the advertisement."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Fair Housing Act\n\nApplicability Prohibits discriminatory actions in sale/renting of dwelling based on race, color, religion, sex, handicap, national origin, or familial status—including:\n\nrefusing bona fide offer discriminating in terms, conditions, privileges, or services of purchase/rental advertising preference for or discrimination against buyer/renter falsely representing dwelling's availability for inspection, sale, or rental inducing sale/renting with discriminatory representations about neighborhood denying reasonable modifications to accommodate handicap at occupant's expense Exceptions Act does not apply to:\n\nreligious organizations private clubs that incidentally provide lodging to members familial status for senior housing owner of ≤ 3 single-family dwellings* owner-occupied dwellings with ≤ 4 units* sale/rental of single-family home by private owner *These exceptions do not apply to discriminatory advertisements or when owner is assisted by a real estate agent, broker, or salesman.\n\nThe federal Fair Housing Act (FHA) was enacted to prohibit discrimination in the sale or rental of a dwelling on the basis of race, color, religion, sex/gender, handicap, national origin, or familial status. Under this act, the following discriminatory actions are prohibited: refusing to sell or rent a dwelling after receipt of a bona fide offer discriminating in the terms, conditions, privileges, or services of sale or rental of a dwelling advertising preference or discrimination with respect to buyers or renters falsely representing that a dwelling is unavailable for inspection, sale, or rental inducing, for profit, the sale or rental of a dwelling with discriminatory representations about the neighborhood refusing to permit, at the occupant's expense, reasonable modifications to accommodate a handicap Although owner-occupied dwellings with up to four units (as seen here) are generally excepted from the FHA, this exception does not apply to advertisements stating a\n\npreference for a protected class. The woman's advertised preference for a \"white male\" renter constitutes race-based and gender-based discrimination. Therefore, the advertisement is not permitted under the FHA (Choice C). (Choice A) The FHA makes it generally illegal to refuse to rent to a prospective tenant based on race, sex/gender, or other protected class. But since the woman is renting an owner-occupied dwelling with four units, she need not comply with this provision. (Choice B) The FHA applies when a person's actions have a discriminatory intent or discriminatory effect. Therefore, the woman's positive motive does not absolve her of liability under the FHA because her actions have a discriminatory effect.\n\nEducational objective: The Fair Housing Act prohibits discrimination in the sale or renting of a dwelling on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, handicap, national origin, or familial status. This includes advertisements that indicate any preference or discrimination with respect to buyers or renters.\n\nReferences\nFair Housing Act, 42 U.S.C. §§ 3601 et seq.","educationalObjective":"The Fair Housing Act prohibits discrimination in the sale or renting of a dwelling on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, handicap, national origin, or familial status. This includes advertisements that indicate any preference or discrimination with respect to buyers or renters.","references":"Fair Housing Act, 42 U.S.C. §§ 3601 et seq.","rule":"The Fair Housing Act prohibits discrimination in the sale or renting of a dwelling on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, handicap, national origin, or familial status. This includes advertisements that indicate any preference or discrimination with respect to buyers or renters.","irac":{"issue":"Based upon these facts, which of the following statements is true?","rule":"The Fair Housing Act prohibits discrimination in the sale or renting of a dwelling on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, handicap, national origin, or familial status. This includes advertisements that indicate any preference or discrimination with respect to buyers or renters.","analysis":"Fair Housing Act\n\nApplicability Prohibits discriminatory actions in sale/renting of dwelling based on race, color, religion, sex, handicap, national origin, or familial status—including:\n\nrefusing bona fide offer discriminating in terms, conditions, privileges, or services of purchase/rental advertising preference for or discrimination against buyer/renter falsely representing dwelling's availability for inspection, sale, or rental inducing sale/renting with discriminatory representations about neighborhood denying reasonable modifications to accommodate handicap at occupant's expense Exceptions Act does not apply to:\n\nreligious organizations private clubs that incidentally provide lodging to members familial status for senior housing owner of ≤ 3 single-family dwellings* owner-occupied dwellings with ≤ 4 units* sale/rental of single-family home by private owner *These exceptions do not apply to discriminatory advertisements or when owner is assisted by a real estate agent, broker, or salesman.\n\nThe federal Fair Housing Act (FHA) was enacted to prohibit discrimination in the sale or rental of a dwelling on the basis of race, color, religion, sex/gender, handicap, national origin, or familial status. Under this act, the following discriminatory actions are prohibited: refusing to sell or rent a dwelling after receipt of a bona fide offer discriminating in the terms, conditions, privileges, or services of sale or rental of a dwelling advertising preference or discrimination with respect to buyers or renters falsely representing that a dwelling is unavailable for inspection, sale, or rental inducing, for profit, the sale or rental of a dwelling with discriminatory representations about the neighborhood refusing to permit, at the occupant's expense, reasonable modifications to accommodate a handicap Although owner-occupied dwellings with up to four units (as seen here) are generally excepted from the FHA, this exception does not apply to advertisements stating a\n\npreference for a protected class. The woman's advertised preference for a \"white male\" renter constitutes race-based and gender-based discrimination. Therefore, the advertisement is not permitted under the FHA (Choice C). (Choice A) The FHA makes it generally illegal to refuse to rent to a prospective tenant based on race, sex/gender, or other protected class. But since the woman is renting an owner-occupied dwelling with four units, she need not comply with this provision. (Choice B) The FHA applies when a person's actions have a discriminatory intent or discriminatory effect. Therefore, the woman's positive motive does not absolve her of liability under the FHA because her actions have a discriminatory effect.","conclusion":"Answer D: Under the federal Fair Housing Act, the woman was not permitted to state a racial or gender preference in the advertisement."},"questionZh":"一名妇女拥有一栋四单元公寓楼，并住在其中一个单元中。当她的一名房客腾出公寓时，该女子在当地报纸上刊登了一则广告，内容如下：“大型两居室公寓可供出租。白人男性首选。”这位女士的偏好是因为她喜欢在她的大楼里拥有不同性别、不同种族和民族背景的租户，而在剩余的出租单元中，一套租给了一名非裔美国男子，另一套租给了一名太平洋岛民妇女。根据这些事实，以下哪项陈述是正确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"《联邦公平住房法》规定，女性因种族或性别而拒绝将其单位出租给潜在租户的行为属于违法行为。","B":"根据联邦公平住房法，该女子的动机免除了她的任何责任。","C":"根据这些事实，不存在违反任何联邦法律的情况。","D":"根据联邦公平住房法，该妇女不得在广告中陈述种族或性别偏好。"},"explanationZh":"公平住房法\n\n适用性 禁止在出售/租赁住宅时出现基于种族、肤色、宗教、性别、残障、国籍或家庭状况的歧视行为，包括：\n\n拒绝善意报价 在购买/租赁的条款、条件、特权或服务方面存在歧视 对买方/承租人的偏好或歧视 虚假陈述住宅可供检查、出售或出租 诱导出售/租赁 对社区进行歧视性陈述 拒绝合理改造以适应残障情况，费用由居住者承担 例外法不适用于：\n\n宗教组织 附带向会员提供住宿的私人俱乐部 ≤ 3 套单户住宅的老年住房所有者* 拥有 ≤ 4 套单元的自住住宅* 私人业主出售/出租单户住宅 *这些例外情况不适用于歧视性广告或业主得到房地产经纪人、经纪人或推销员协助的情况。\n\n联邦公平住房法 (FHA) 的颁布旨在禁止在住宅销售或租赁中基于种族、肤色、宗教、性别、残障、国籍或家庭状况的歧视。根据该法案，禁止以下歧视行为： 在收到善意要约后拒绝出售或出租住宅 在住宅销售或租赁的条款、条件、特权或服务方面存在歧视 广告对买家或承租人的偏好或歧视 虚假地表示住宅无法进行检查、出售或出租 诱导以营利为目的出售或出租住宅，并以歧视性的方式出售或出租住宅，并拒绝允许在居住者的费用、为适应残障而进行的合理改造 虽然拥有最多四个单元的自住住宅（如此处所示）通常不受联邦住房管理局 (FHA) 的约束，但此例外不适用于声明以下内容的广告：\n\n对受保护阶层的偏好。该妇女所宣称的对“白人男性”租客的偏好构成了基于种族和性别的歧视。因此，FHA 不允许该广告（选项 C）。 （选项 A）联邦住房管理局规定，基于种族、性别或其他受保护类别拒绝向潜在租户出租的行为通常是非法的。但由于该妇女租的是一套有四个单元的自住住宅，因此她不需要遵守这一规定。 （选择 B）当一个人的行为具有歧视意图或歧视效果时，FHA 适用。因此，该妇女的积极动机并不能免除她根据联邦住房管理局的责任，因为她的行为具有歧视性效果。\n\n教育目标：《公平住房法》禁止在出售或租赁住宅时出现基于种族、肤色、宗教、性别、残障、国籍或家庭状况的歧视。这包括表明对买家或租户的任何偏好或歧视的广告。\n\n参考文献\n《公平住房法》，42 U.S.C. §§ 3601 及以下。","educationalObjectiveZh":"《公平住房法》禁止在出售或租赁住宅时出现基于种族、肤色、宗教、性别、残障、国籍或家庭状况的歧视。这包括表明对买家或租户的任何偏好或歧视的广告。","iracZh":{"issue":"根据这些事实，以下哪项陈述是正确的？","rule":"《公平住房法》禁止在出售或租赁住宅时出现基于种族、肤色、宗教、性别、残障、国籍或家庭状况的歧视。这包括表明对买家或租户的任何偏好或歧视的广告。","analysis":"公平住房法\n\n适用性 禁止在出售/租赁住宅时出现基于种族、肤色、宗教、性别、残障、国籍或家庭状况的歧视行为，包括：\n\n拒绝善意报价 在购买/租赁的条款、条件、特权或服务方面存在歧视 对买方/承租人的偏好或歧视 虚假陈述住宅可供检查、出售或出租 诱导出售/租赁 对社区进行歧视性陈述 拒绝合理改造以适应残障情况，费用由居住者承担 例外法不适用于：\n\n宗教组织 附带向会员提供住宿的私人俱乐部 ≤ 3 套单户住宅的老年住房所有者* 拥有 ≤ 4 套单元的自住住宅* 私人业主出售/出租单户住宅 *这些例外情况不适用于歧视性广告或业主得到房地产经纪人、经纪人或推销员协助的情况。\n\n联邦公平住房法 (FHA) 的颁布旨在禁止在住宅销售或租赁中基于种族、肤色、宗教、性别、残障、国籍或家庭状况的歧视。根据该法案，禁止以下歧视行为： 在收到善意要约后拒绝出售或出租住宅 在住宅销售或租赁的条款、条件、特权或服务方面存在歧视 广告对买家或承租人的偏好或歧视 虚假地表示住宅无法进行检查、出售或出租 诱导以营利为目的出售或出租住宅，并以歧视性的方式出售或出租住宅，并拒绝允许在居住者的费用、为适应残障而进行的合理改造 虽然拥有最多四个单元的自住住宅（如此处所示）通常不受联邦住房管理局 (FHA) 的约束，但此例外不适用于声明以下内容的广告：\n\n对受保护阶层的偏好。该妇女所宣称的对“白人男性”租客的偏好构成了基于种族和性别的歧视。因此，FHA 不允许该广告（选项 C）。 （选项 A）联邦住房管理局规定，基于种族、性别或其他受保护类别拒绝向潜在租户出租的行为通常是非法的。但由于该妇女租的是一套有四个单元的自住住宅，因此她不需要遵守这一规定。 （选择 B）当一个人的行为具有歧视意图或歧视效果时，FHA 适用。因此，该妇女的积极动机并不能免除她根据联邦住房管理局的责任，因为她的行为具有歧视性效果。\n\n教育目标：《公平住房法》禁止在出售或租赁住宅时出现基于种族、肤色、宗教、性别、残障、国籍或家庭状况的歧视。这包括表明对买家或租户的任何偏好或歧视的广告。\n\n参考文献\n《公平住房法》，42 U.S.C. §§ 3601 及以下。","conclusion":"答案 D：根据联邦公平住房法，该妇女不得在广告中陈述种族或性别偏好。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":90,"sourceQuestionPage":262,"sourceQuestionPages":[262],"sourceExplanationPages":[263,264],"sourceAnswerPages":[263,264],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-091","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A man devised land \"to my brother and his heirs until my daughter reaches the age of 25, and then to my daughter and her heirs.\" The daughter was three years old at the time of her father's death. The applicable jurisdiction continues to adhere to the common law Rule Against Perpetuities. Which of the following is the most accurate statement of the daughter's interest in the land?","choices":{"A":"She has a possibility of reverter.","B":"She has a remainder interest subject to complete divestment.","C":"She has a shifting executory interest.","D":"She has no interest in the land because of the Rule Against Perpetuities."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nA defeasible fee has the potential to last forever but can be cut short if a specified event or condition occurs. One type of defeasible fee is a fee simple determinable (FSD), which is created with durational language (eg, \"to my brother and his heirs until my daughter reaches the age of 25\"). The future interest that follows an FSD is either: a possibility of reverter – in which case, the estate automatically reverts back to the grantor when the specified event occurs (presumed if not expressly stated) or an executory interest – in which case, the estate automatically passes to a third party when the stated event occurs (eg, \"then to my daughter and her heirs\").* Therefore, pursuant to the man's devise, the daughter has an executory interest (Choice A). There are two types of executory interests: Shifting – cuts short (or divests) an estate held by a grantee, such that the estate shifts from the grantee to the executory-interest holder Springing – cuts short an estate held by a grantor, such that the estate springs from the grantor to the executory-interest holder Since the daughter's interest will cut short the brother's (grantee's) interest in the land, she has a shifting executory interest. *An FSD followed by an executory limitation is sometimes referred to as a fee simple determinable subject to an executory limitation.\n\n(Choice B) A remainder is a future interest created in a grantee that is capable of becoming a present estate upon the natural expiration of a life estate, fee tail, or term of years (not seen here). The remainder is subject to complete divestment if the occurrence of a condition will eliminate the interest (eg, \"then to A, but if A has no children then to B\"). (Choice D) Under the Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP), certain future interests are void if they could vest more than 21 years after the death of some relevant person alive when the interest was created. But here, the daughter's executory interest does not violate RAP because she will turn 25 (or not) within her own lifetime.\n\nEducational objective: A third-party grantee's executory interest is shifting if it divests another grantee's estate and springing if it divests the grantor's estate.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (First) of Property § 44 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (fee simple determinable).","educationalObjective":"A third-party grantee's executory interest is shifting if it divests another grantee's estate and springing if it divests the grantor's estate.","references":"Restatement (First) of Property § 44 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (fee simple determinable).","rule":"A third-party grantee's executory interest is shifting if it divests another grantee's estate and springing if it divests the grantor's estate.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the most accurate statement of the daughter's interest in the land?","rule":"A third-party grantee's executory interest is shifting if it divests another grantee's estate and springing if it divests the grantor's estate.","analysis":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nA defeasible fee has the potential to last forever but can be cut short if a specified event or condition occurs. One type of defeasible fee is a fee simple determinable (FSD), which is created with durational language (eg, \"to my brother and his heirs until my daughter reaches the age of 25\"). The future interest that follows an FSD is either: a possibility of reverter – in which case, the estate automatically reverts back to the grantor when the specified event occurs (presumed if not expressly stated) or an executory interest – in which case, the estate automatically passes to a third party when the stated event occurs (eg, \"then to my daughter and her heirs\").* Therefore, pursuant to the man's devise, the daughter has an executory interest (Choice A). There are two types of executory interests: Shifting – cuts short (or divests) an estate held by a grantee, such that the estate shifts from the grantee to the executory-interest holder Springing – cuts short an estate held by a grantor, such that the estate springs from the grantor to the executory-interest holder Since the daughter's interest will cut short the brother's (grantee's) interest in the land, she has a shifting executory interest. *An FSD followed by an executory limitation is sometimes referred to as a fee simple determinable subject to an executory limitation.\n\n(Choice B) A remainder is a future interest created in a grantee that is capable of becoming a present estate upon the natural expiration of a life estate, fee tail, or term of years (not seen here). The remainder is subject to complete divestment if the occurrence of a condition will eliminate the interest (eg, \"then to A, but if A has no children then to B\"). (Choice D) Under the Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP), certain future interests are void if they could vest more than 21 years after the death of some relevant person alive when the interest was created. But here, the daughter's executory interest does not violate RAP because she will turn 25 (or not) within her own lifetime.","conclusion":"Answer C: She has a shifting executory interest."},"questionZh":"一名男子设计了土地“给我的兄弟和他的继承人，直到我女儿 25 岁，然后给我的女儿和她的继承人”。父亲去世时，女儿三岁。适用的司法管辖区继续遵守普通法的反永续规则。以下哪项最准确地表述了女儿对土地的权益？","choicesZh":{"A":"她有回归的可能。","B":"她拥有剩余权益，但须完全撤资。","C":"她的执行兴趣不断变化。","D":"由于永久规则，她对这片土地没有兴趣。"},"explanationZh":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n可废止的费用有可能永远持续下去，但如果发生特定的事件或情况，可以缩短。一种可废止的费用是简单可确定费用 (FSD)，它是用持续语言创建的（例如，“给我的兄弟和他的继承人，直到我女儿年满 25 岁”）。 FSD 后的未来权益是： 归还的可能性 – 在这种情况下，当指定事件发生时（假设没有明确说明），遗产自动归还给设保人；或执行权益 – 在这种情况下，当所述事件发生时，遗产自动转移给第三方（例如，“然后转移给我的女儿和她的继承人”）。* 因此，根据男子的设计，女儿拥有执行权益（选择） A).执行权益有两种类型： 转移 – 缩短（或剥离）受让人持有的遗产，使遗产从受让人转移到执行权益持有人 突然 – 缩短设保人持有的遗产，使遗产从设保人转移到执行权益持有人 由于女儿的权益将缩短兄弟（受让人）在土地上的权益，因此她有转移的执行权益。 *带有执行限制的 FSD 有时被称为可根据执行限制简单确定的费用。\n\n（选择 B）剩余部分是在受让人中创建的未来权益，能够在终身遗产、费用尾部或年限（此处未显示）自然到期时成为现有遗产。如果出现某种情况将消除利息（例如，“则归 A，但如果 A 没有子女，则归 B”），则剩余部分将完全撤资。 （选项 D） 根据反对永续规则 (RAP)，某些未来权益如果在权益创建时相关人员去世 21 年后才能归属，则该权益无效。但在这里，女儿的执行权益并不违反 RAP，因为她将在自己的一生中年满 25 岁（或不到 25 岁）。\n\n教育目标：如果第三方受让人剥离另一受让人的遗产，则其执行权益就会发生变化；如果它剥离设保人的遗产，则其执行权益就会发生变化。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 44（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（费用可简单确定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果第三方受让人剥离另一受让人的遗产，其执行权益就会发生变化；如果它剥离设保人的遗产，其执行权益就会发生变化。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项最准确地表述了女儿对土地的权益？","rule":"如果第三方受让人剥离另一受让人的遗产，其执行权益就会发生变化；如果它剥离设保人的遗产，其执行权益就会发生变化。","analysis":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n可废止的费用有可能永远持续下去，但如果发生特定的事件或情况，可以缩短。一种可废止的费用是简单可确定费用 (FSD)，它是用持续语言创建的（例如，“给我的兄弟和他的继承人，直到我女儿年满 25 岁”）。 FSD 后的未来权益是： 归还的可能性 – 在这种情况下，当指定事件发生时（假设没有明确说明），遗产自动归还给设保人；或执行权益 – 在这种情况下，当所述事件发生时，遗产自动转移给第三方（例如，“然后转移给我的女儿和她的继承人”）。* 因此，根据男子的设计，女儿拥有执行权益（选择） A).执行权益有两种类型： 转移 – 缩短（或剥离）受让人持有的遗产，使遗产从受让人转移到执行权益持有人 突然 – 缩短设保人持有的遗产，使遗产从设保人转移到执行权益持有人 由于女儿的权益将缩短兄弟（受让人）在土地上的权益，因此她有转移的执行权益。 *带有执行限制的 FSD 有时被称为可根据执行限制简单确定的费用。\n\n（选择 B）剩余部分是在受让人中创建的未来权益，能够在终身遗产、费用尾部或年限（此处未显示）自然到期时成为现有遗产。如果出现某种情况将消除利息（例如，“则归 A，但如果 A 没有子女，则归 B”），则剩余部分将完全撤资。 （选项 D） 根据反对永续规则 (RAP)，某些未来权益如果在权益创建时相关人员去世 21 年后才能归属，则该权益无效。但在这里，女儿的执行权益并不违反 RAP，因为她将在自己的一生中年满 25 岁（或不到 25 岁）。\n\n教育目标：如果第三方受让人剥离另一受让人的遗产，则其执行权益就会发生变化；如果它剥离设保人的遗产，则其执行权益就会发生变化。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 44（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（费用可简单确定）。","conclusion":"答案 C：她的执行兴趣不断变化。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"fee simple determinable","ipa":"/fˈi sˈɪmpəl dɪtˈɝmənəbəl/","syllables":"fee sim·ple de·ter·minable","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"可终止完全所有权：限定事件发生时自动终止并回复出让人","contextualMeaning":"可终止完全所有权：限定事件发生时自动终止并回复出让人","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Fee simple estates Type Language Future interest Indefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's Fee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry A d","synonyms":["determinable fee"]},{"term":"executory interest","ipa":"/e^'zekjutəri ˈɪntɹəst/","syllables":"ex·ecu·tory in·ter·est","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"执行性权益：将来切断他人权益或在出让人之后生效的第三人未来权益","contextualMeaning":"执行性权益：将来切断他人权益或在出让人之后生效的第三人未来权益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"She has a shifting executory interest.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"right of entry","ipa":"/ɹˈaɪt ˈəv ˈɛntɹi/","syllables":"right of en·try","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"收回权：附解除条件完全所有权违约后出让人选择收回的权利","contextualMeaning":"收回权：附解除条件完全所有权违约后出让人选择收回的权利","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Fee simple estates Type Language Future interest Indefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's Fee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry A d","synonyms":["power of termination"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":91,"sourceQuestionPage":265,"sourceQuestionPages":[265],"sourceExplanationPages":[266,267],"sourceAnswerPages":[266,267],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-092","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A retiree owned a condo, subject to a mortgage securing a debt that he owed to a lender. The retiree conveyed the condo to \"my son for life, remainder to my granddaughter, subject to the lender's mortgage thereon.\" At the time of the conveyance, there was an unpaid balance on the mortgage of $25,000. The mortgage required monthly payments covering the interest to date and a portion of the principal. For the past six months, the son has been living alone in the condo and paying the full monthly payment due to the lender. The son has since asked his lawyer for advice regarding the parties' obligations with respect to the monthly payment. What should the lawyer tell him?","choices":{"A":"The granddaughter and the son must pay based on the present value of their respective interests.","B":"The granddaughter must pay the full monthly payment.","C":"The son must pay the full monthly payment.","D":"The son must pay the principal, but the granddaughter must pay the interest to the lender."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Payment of preexisting mortgage obligation\n\nPeriodic payment of Life tenant is responsible for interest payments interest only Future-interest holder is responsible for principal payments due at later date Periodic payment of Life tenant & future-interest holder are responsible for interest & principal payments equal to present value of each interest in property\n\nA life estate is a present possessory interest in real property that terminates on the death of an individual and is followed by a reversion or a remainder. Payments due on an obligation secured by a mortgage of the property that was incurred before the creation of the life estate are subject to allocation between the holder of the life estate (ie, life tenant) and the future-interest holder (ie, reversioner or remainderman). The allocation can vary depending on the type of obligation: If the obligation requires the periodic payment of interest until the principal amount is due, then the life tenant is responsible for interest payments and the future-interest holder is responsible for principal payments due at a later date.\n\nIf the obligation requires the periodic payment of both interest andthe principal amount (in whole or in part), then the obligation is allocated between the life tenant and the future-interest holder based on the present value of each interest.\n\nHere, the retiree conveyed a life estate in the condo to his son and a remainder to his granddaughter, subject to the lender's mortgage on the condo. The mortgage required monthly payments covering the interest to date and a portion of the principal. Therefore, the lawyer should tell the son that both he (life tenant) and the granddaughter (remainderman) are obligated to pay the monthly mortgage payments based on the present values of their respective interests (Choices B, C & D).\n\nEducational objective: If a preexisting mortgage obligation requires the periodic payment of interest only, then the life tenant pays the interest and the future-interest holder pays the later-due principal. But if periodic payment of interest and principal is required, then both payments must be allocated between the parties based on the present value of each interest.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 132 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing life tenant's obligation to contribute to a preexisting mortgage obligation when the principal amount has become due).","educationalObjective":"If a preexisting mortgage obligation requires the periodic payment of interest only, then the life tenant pays the interest and the future-interest holder pays the later-due principal. But if periodic payment of interest and principal is required, then both payments must be allocated between the parties based on the present value of each interest.","references":"Restatement of Property § 132 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing life tenant's obligation to contribute to a preexisting mortgage obligation when the principal amount has become due).","rule":"If a preexisting mortgage obligation requires the periodic payment of interest only, then the life tenant pays the interest and the future-interest holder pays the later-due principal. But if periodic payment of interest and principal is required, then both payments must be allocated between the parties based on the present value of each interest.","irac":{"issue":"What should the lawyer tell him?","rule":"If a preexisting mortgage obligation requires the periodic payment of interest only, then the life tenant pays the interest and the future-interest holder pays the later-due principal. But if periodic payment of interest and principal is required, then both payments must be allocated between the parties based on the present value of each interest.","analysis":"Payment of preexisting mortgage obligation\n\nPeriodic payment of Life tenant is responsible for interest payments interest only Future-interest holder is responsible for principal payments due at later date Periodic payment of Life tenant & future-interest holder are responsible for interest & principal payments equal to present value of each interest in property\n\nA life estate is a present possessory interest in real property that terminates on the death of an individual and is followed by a reversion or a remainder. Payments due on an obligation secured by a mortgage of the property that was incurred before the creation of the life estate are subject to allocation between the holder of the life estate (ie, life tenant) and the future-interest holder (ie, reversioner or remainderman). The allocation can vary depending on the type of obligation: If the obligation requires the periodic payment of interest until the principal amount is due, then the life tenant is responsible for interest payments and the future-interest holder is responsible for principal payments due at a later date.\n\nIf the obligation requires the periodic payment of both interest andthe principal amount (in whole or in part), then the obligation is allocated between the life tenant and the future-interest holder based on the present value of each interest.\n\nHere, the retiree conveyed a life estate in the condo to his son and a remainder to his granddaughter, subject to the lender's mortgage on the condo. The mortgage required monthly payments covering the interest to date and a portion of the principal. Therefore, the lawyer should tell the son that both he (life tenant) and the granddaughter (remainderman) are obligated to pay the monthly mortgage payments based on the present values of their respective interests (Choices B, C & D).","conclusion":"Answer A: The granddaughter and the son must pay based on the present value of their respective interests."},"questionZh":"一名退休人员拥有一套公寓，并以抵押贷款担保他欠贷方的债务。这位退休人员将公寓转让给“我的儿子终身，其余的交给我的孙女，但须遵守贷款人的抵押贷款。”转让时，抵押贷款尚有 25,000 美元的未付余额。抵押贷款要求每月还款，包括迄今为止的利息和部分本金。在过去的六个月里，儿子一直独自住在公寓里，并每月向贷款人支付全额付款。此后，儿子向律师征求有关双方每月付款义务的建议。律师应该告诉他什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"孙女和儿子必须根据各自权益的现值支付。","B":"孙女必须每月全额付款。","C":"儿子必须每月全额付款。","D":"儿子必须支付本金，但孙女必须向贷款人支付利息。"},"explanationZh":"支付先前存在的抵押债务\n\n定期付款 终身租户仅负责支付利息 未来利息持有人负责支付稍后到期的本金 定期付款 终身租户和未来利息持有人负责支付相当于每项财产权益现值的利息和本金\n\n终身财产是不动产的现有占有权益，在个人死亡时终止，随后归还或剩余。在终身财产创建之前产生的财产抵押担保债务的到期付款，须在终身财产持有人（即终身租户）和未来权益持有人（即归还者或剩余人）之间进行分配。分配可能因债务类型而异：如果债务要求定期支付利息直至本金到期，则终身租户负责支付利息，而未来利息持有人则负责支付稍后到期的本金。\n\n如果该义务要求定期支付利息和本金（全部或部分），则该义务将根据每项利息的现值在终身租户和未来利息持有人之间分配。\n\n在这里，退休人员将公寓中的终身财产转让给他的儿子，剩余的财产转让给他的孙女，但须遵守贷款人对公寓的抵押贷款。抵押贷款要求每月还款，包括迄今为止的利息和部分本金。因此，律师应该告诉儿子，他（终身租户）和孙女（剩余人）都有义务根据各自权益的现值每月支付抵押贷款（选项B、C和D）。\n\n教育目标：如果先前存在的抵押贷款义务仅需要定期支付利息，则终身租户支付利息，未来利息持有人支付逾期本金。但如果需要定期支付利息和本金，则必须根据每项利息的现值在双方之间分配。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 132（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论当本金到期时终身租户有义务为先前存在的抵押贷款义务供款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果先前存在的抵押贷款义务仅要求定期支付利息，则终身租户支付利息，未来利息持有人支付逾期本金。但如果需要定期支付利息和本金，则必须根据每项利息的现值在双方之间分配。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师应该告诉他什么？","rule":"如果先前存在的抵押贷款义务仅要求定期支付利息，则终身租户支付利息，未来利息持有人支付逾期本金。但如果需要定期支付利息和本金，则必须根据每项利息的现值在双方之间分配。","analysis":"支付先前存在的抵押债务\n\n定期付款 终身租户仅负责支付利息 未来利息持有人负责支付稍后到期的本金 定期付款 终身租户和未来利息持有人负责支付相当于每项财产权益现值的利息和本金\n\n终身财产是不动产的现有占有权益，在个人死亡时终止，随后归还或剩余。在终身财产创建之前产生的财产抵押担保债务的到期付款，须在终身财产持有人（即终身租户）和未来权益持有人（即归还者或剩余人）之间进行分配。分配可能因债务类型而异：如果债务要求定期支付利息直至本金到期，则终身租户负责支付利息，而未来利息持有人则负责支付稍后到期的本金。\n\n如果该义务要求定期支付利息和本金（全部或部分），则该义务将根据每项利息的现值在终身租户和未来利息持有人之间分配。\n\n在这里，退休人员将公寓中的终身财产转让给他的儿子，剩余的财产转让给他的孙女，但须遵守贷款人对公寓的抵押贷款。抵押贷款要求每月还款，包括迄今为止的利息和部分本金。因此，律师应该告诉儿子，他（终身租户）和孙女（剩余人）都有义务根据各自权益的现值每月支付抵押贷款（选项B、C和D）。\n\n教育目标：如果先前存在的抵押贷款义务仅需要定期支付利息，则终身租户支付利息，未来利息持有人支付逾期本金。但如果需要定期支付利息和本金，则必须根据每项利息的现值在双方之间分配。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 132（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论当本金到期时终身租户有义务为先前存在的抵押贷款义务供款）。","conclusion":"答案 A：孙女和儿子必须根据各自权益的现值支付。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"reversioner","ipa":"/ri'v\\\\\\\\:ʃənə(r);-ʒən-/","syllables":"re·ver·sioner","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 复归权者, 继承权者","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“reversioner”","wordForm":"词；词形 s:reversioners","sourceSentence":"Payments due on an obligation secured by a mortgage of the property that was incurred before the creation of the life estate are subject to allocation between the holder of the life estate (ie, life tenant) and the future-interest holder (ie, reversioner or remainderman).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":92,"sourceQuestionPage":268,"sourceQuestionPages":[268],"sourceExplanationPages":[269,270],"sourceAnswerPages":[269,270],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-093","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman convinced her younger friend to purchase undeveloped land with her. The friend contributed 30 percent of the purchase price, and the woman contributed 70 percent. They took title to the property based on the percentage of their contributions. Subsequently, the friend, purporting to own the property outright, obtained a loan and gave a mortgage on the property to the lender. When the friend defaulted on the loan, the bank initiated a foreclosure action on the property. The jurisdiction follows the lien theory of mortgages. To what interest, if any, would the purchaser of the property at the foreclosure sale be entitled?","choices":{"A":"The entire property, because the friend, as a co-owner, is deemed to be the woman's agent.","B":"A 30 percent interest, because that is the friend's ownership interest in the property.","C":"A 30 percent interest, because the applicable jurisdiction follows the lien theory of mortgages.","D":"No interest, because a co-owner of property needs the consent of the other co-owners to mortgage it."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Consent between cotenants in common\n\n(common examples)\n\nConsent required Convey entire co-owned property Obtain exclusive possession over part of property Bind other cotenants' interests in contract with third party No consent Sell, lease, or devise personal ownership interest required Possess entire co-owned property Extract minerals from co-owned property (unless committing waste) Tenancy in common is a type of concurrent estate (ie, cotenancy) where each cotenant has a possibly unequal ownership interest in the property, an equal right to possess the whole property, but no right of survivorship. A tenant in common is free to bind his/her individual interest in the property (eg, by mortgaging it) without the other cotenant(s)' consent. However, a tenant in common has no right to bind other cotenant(s)' interests in the property. Any attempt to do so will not affect the other cotenant(s)' interests.* Here, the woman and the friend shared the property as tenants in common, as evidenced by their unequal interests in the property. Although the friend was free to bind her 30 percent interest in the property, she had no right to bind the woman's interest in the property. As a result, the friend's attempt to give the lender a mortgage on the entire property was ineffective as to the woman's interest. This means that the purchaser at the foreclosure sale would only be entitled to the friend's 30 percent interest in the property. *This is true unless the other cotenant(s) authorize or later ratify the transaction (not seen here). (Choice A) The mere existence of a cotenancy does not create an agency relationship between the cotenants, so the friend is not deemed to be the woman's agent simply because the friend co-owns the property. (Choice C) The friend was free to mortgage her 30 percent interest in the property regardless of which mortgage theory the jurisdiction follows. (Choice D) In a tenancy by the entirety, neither spouse can mortgage the property without the other spouse's consent. But here, the woman and her friend were not spouses who shared the property as tenants by the entirety. Instead, they shared the property as tenants in common, and each was free to mortgage her interest in the property without the other's consent.\n\nEducational objective: A tenant in common can bind his/her own interest in the co-owned property without the other cotenants(s)' consent but has no right to bind the other cotenant(s)' interests in the property.\n\nReferences\n20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 93 (2020) (ability to bind other cotenants).","educationalObjective":"A tenant in common can bind his/her own interest in the co-owned property without the other cotenants(s)' consent but has no right to bind the other cotenant(s)' interests in the property.","references":"20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 93 (2020) (ability to bind other cotenants).","rule":"A tenant in common can bind his/her own interest in the co-owned property without the other cotenants(s)' consent but has no right to bind the other cotenant(s)' interests in the property.","irac":{"issue":"To what interest, if any, would the purchaser of the property at the foreclosure sale be entitled?","rule":"A tenant in common can bind his/her own interest in the co-owned property without the other cotenants(s)' consent but has no right to bind the other cotenant(s)' interests in the property.","analysis":"Consent between cotenants in common\n\n(common examples)\n\nConsent required Convey entire co-owned property Obtain exclusive possession over part of property Bind other cotenants' interests in contract with third party No consent Sell, lease, or devise personal ownership interest required Possess entire co-owned property Extract minerals from co-owned property (unless committing waste) Tenancy in common is a type of concurrent estate (ie, cotenancy) where each cotenant has a possibly unequal ownership interest in the property, an equal right to possess the whole property, but no right of survivorship. A tenant in common is free to bind his/her individual interest in the property (eg, by mortgaging it) without the other cotenant(s)' consent. However, a tenant in common has no right to bind other cotenant(s)' interests in the property. Any attempt to do so will not affect the other cotenant(s)' interests.* Here, the woman and the friend shared the property as tenants in common, as evidenced by their unequal interests in the property. Although the friend was free to bind her 30 percent interest in the property, she had no right to bind the woman's interest in the property. As a result, the friend's attempt to give the lender a mortgage on the entire property was ineffective as to the woman's interest. This means that the purchaser at the foreclosure sale would only be entitled to the friend's 30 percent interest in the property. *This is true unless the other cotenant(s) authorize or later ratify the transaction (not seen here). (Choice A) The mere existence of a cotenancy does not create an agency relationship between the cotenants, so the friend is not deemed to be the woman's agent simply because the friend co-owns the property. (Choice C) The friend was free to mortgage her 30 percent interest in the property regardless of which mortgage theory the jurisdiction follows. (Choice D) In a tenancy by the entirety, neither spouse can mortgage the property without the other spouse's consent. But here, the woman and her friend were not spouses who shared the property as tenants by the entirety. Instead, they shared the property as tenants in common, and each was free to mortgage her interest in the property without the other's consent.","conclusion":"Answer B: A 30 percent interest, because that is the friend's ownership interest in the property."},"questionZh":"一位女士说服她的年轻朋友和她一起购买未开发的土地。朋友出资30%，女士出资70%。他们根据贡献的百分比获得了财产的所有权。随后，这位朋友声称完全拥有该房产，获得了一笔贷款，并将该房产抵押给贷方。当这位朋友拖欠贷款时，银行对该房产发起了止赎行动。管辖权遵循抵押留置权理论。取消抵押品赎回权出售时房产的购买者有权获得什么利息（如果有）？","choicesZh":{"A":"整个财产，因为朋友作为共同所有人，被视为该女子的代理人。","B":"30% 的权益，因为这是朋友对该房产的所有权权益。","C":"30% 的利息，因为适用的司法管辖区遵循抵押贷款的留置权理论。","D":"没有利息，因为财产共有人需要征得其他共有人的同意才能抵押。"},"explanationZh":"共同参与者之间的同意\n\n（常见例子）\n\n需要同意 转让全部共有财产 获得对部分财产的排他性占有 通过与第三方签订合同约束其他共有人的利益 不同意 需要出售、租赁或设计个人所有权权益 拥有全部共有财产 从共有财产中提取矿物（除非造成浪费） 共同共有（joint tenancy）是一种共有财产（即共同共有（joint tenancy）），其中每个共有人在财产中可能拥有不平等的所有权权益，拥有拥有整个财产的平等权利，但没有权利生存。共同承租人可以自由地约束他/她在财产中的个人利益（例如，通过抵押），而无需其他共同承租人的同意。然而，共同承租人无权约束其他共同承租人在该财产中的利益。任何这样做的尝试都不会影响其他承租人的利益。*在这里，这位女士和这位朋友作为按份共有人（tenant in common）共享该房产，这从他们在该房产中的不平等利益就可以看出。虽然朋友可以自由地约束她在该房产中 30% 的权益，但她无权约束该妇女在该房产中的权益。结果，朋友试图向贷款人提供整个财产的抵押贷款，但对妇女的利益来说是无效的。这意味着取消抵押品赎回权出售时的购买者只能获得朋友的房产 30% 的权益。 *除非其他租户授权或稍后批准交易（此处未见），否则情况属实。 （选项A） 仅仅存在共同共有并不会在共同共有人之间建立代理关系，因此朋友不会仅仅因为朋友共同拥有房产而被视为女方的代理人。 （选项 C）无论司法管辖区遵循哪种抵押理论，朋友都可以自由抵押其 30% 的房产权益。 （选项D） 在整体租赁中，未经另一方同意，配偶双方均不得抵押财产。但在这里，这位女士和她的朋友并不是以租户身份共同分享房产的配偶。相反，他们以按份共有人（tenant in common）的身份共享该房产，并且双方都可以在未经对方同意的情况下自由抵押自己在该房产中的权益。\n\n教育目标：按份共有人（tenant in common）可以在未经其他合租人同意的情况下约束自己在共有财产中的利益，但无权约束其他合租人在该财产中的利益。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 93 (2020)（约束其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的能力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"按份共有人（tenant in common）可以在未经其他合租人同意的情况下约束自己在共有财产中的权益，但无权约束其他合租人在该财产中的权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"取消抵押品赎回权出售时的房产购买者有权获得什么利息（如果有）？","rule":"按份共有人（tenant in common）可以在未经其他合租人同意的情况下约束自己在共有财产中的权益，但无权约束其他合租人在该财产中的权益。","analysis":"共同参与者之间的同意\n\n（常见例子）\n\n需要同意 转让全部共有财产 获得对部分财产的排他性占有 通过与第三方签订合同约束其他共有人的利益 不同意 需要出售、租赁或设计个人所有权权益 拥有全部共有财产 从共有财产中提取矿物（除非造成浪费） 共同共有（joint tenancy）是一种共有财产（即共同共有（joint tenancy）），其中每个共有人在财产中可能拥有不平等的所有权权益，拥有拥有整个财产的平等权利，但没有权利生存。共同承租人可以自由地约束他/她在财产中的个人利益（例如，通过抵押），而无需其他共同承租人的同意。然而，共同承租人无权约束其他共同承租人在该财产中的利益。任何这样做的尝试都不会影响其他承租人的利益。*在这里，这位女士和这位朋友作为按份共有人（tenant in common）共享该房产，这从他们在该房产中的不平等利益就可以看出。虽然朋友可以自由地约束她在该房产中 30% 的权益，但她无权约束该妇女在该房产中的权益。结果，朋友试图向贷款人提供整个财产的抵押贷款，但对妇女的利益来说是无效的。这意味着取消抵押品赎回权出售时的购买者只能获得朋友的房产 30% 的权益。 *除非其他租户授权或稍后批准交易（此处未见），否则情况属实。 （选项A） 仅仅存在共同共有并不会在共同共有人之间建立代理关系，因此朋友不会仅仅因为朋友共同拥有房产而被视为女方的代理人。 （选项 C）无论司法管辖区遵循哪种抵押理论，朋友都可以自由抵押其 30% 的房产权益。 （选项D） 在整体租赁中，未经另一方同意，配偶双方均不得抵押财产。但在这里，这位女士和她的朋友并不是以租户身份共同分享房产的配偶。相反，他们以按份共有人（tenant in common）的身份共享该房产，并且双方都可以在未经对方同意的情况下自由抵押自己在该房产中的权益。\n\n教育目标：按份共有人（tenant in common）可以在未经其他合租人同意的情况下约束自己在共有财产中的利益，但无权约束其他合租人在该财产中的利益。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 93 (2020)（约束其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的能力）。","conclusion":"答案 B：30% 的权益，因为这是朋友对该房产的所有权权益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"tenancy by the entirety","ipa":"/tˈɛnənsi bˈaɪ ðə ɪntˈaɪɝti/","syllables":"ten·ancy by the en·tirety","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"夫妻整体共有：仅配偶可持有、带生存权且通常不得单方处分","contextualMeaning":"夫妻整体共有：仅配偶可持有、带生存权且通常不得单方处分","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) In a tenancy by the entirety, neither spouse can mortgage the property without the other spouse's consent.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":93,"sourceQuestionPage":271,"sourceQuestionPages":[271],"sourceExplanationPages":[272,273],"sourceAnswerPages":[272,273],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-094","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A landowner conveyed his land by quitclaim deed to his daughter and son \"as joint tenants in fee simple.\" The language of the deed was sufficient to create a common law joint tenancy with right of survivorship, which is unmodified by statute. The daughter then duly executed a will devising her interest in the land to a friend. Then the son duly executed a will devising his interest in the land to a cousin. The son died, and later the daughter died. Neither had ever married. The daughter's friend and the cousin survived. After both wills have been duly probated, who owns what interest in the land?","choices":{"A":"The cousin owns the fee simple.","B":"The daughter's friend and the cousin own equal shares as joint tenants with right of survivorship.","C":"The daughter's friend and the cousin own equal shares as tenants in common.","D":"The daughter's friend owns the fee simple."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A joint tenancy is a type of concurrent estate in which each co-tenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship. The right of survivorship means that a joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it. Therefore, a joint tenant has no interest to devise upon death. Here, the daughter and the son were joint tenants in fee simple. Although they executed wills attempting to devise their joint tenancy interests to others, they could not do so because of their right of survivorship. But the joint tenancy disappeared when the son died, and the daughter automatically acquired the entire property in fee simple. As a result, her fee simple interest passed to the friend under her will when she died. (Choice A) Since the son was a joint tenant and died first, his property interest was acquired by the daughter. As a result, the son had no interest to devise to his cousin under a will. (Choice B) Under the common law and in most jurisdictions, a joint tenancy with right of survivorship entails four unities. This includes unity of title, which requires joint tenants to receive their interests in the same instrument of conveyance. Since the friend and the cousin received their purported interests in separate wills, they could not acquire joint tenancy interests. (Choice C) A lifetime transfer of a joint tenant's interest severs that interest from the joint tenancy, and it becomes a tenancy-in-common ownership interest. Therefore, the friend and cousin would have owned equal shares of the property as tenants in common had the daughter and son conveyed their interests when they were alive instead of attempting to devise them at death.\n\nEducational objective: A joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it (ie, right of survivorship). Therefore, a joint tenancy interest cannot be devised.","educationalObjective":"A joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it (ie, right of survivorship). Therefore, a joint tenancy interest cannot be devised.","references":"","rule":"A joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it (ie, right of survivorship). Therefore, a joint tenancy interest cannot be devised.","irac":{"issue":"After both wills have been duly probated, who owns what interest in the land?","rule":"A joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it (ie, right of survivorship). Therefore, a joint tenancy interest cannot be devised.","analysis":"A joint tenancy is a type of concurrent estate in which each co-tenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship. The right of survivorship means that a joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it. Therefore, a joint tenant has no interest to devise upon death. Here, the daughter and the son were joint tenants in fee simple. Although they executed wills attempting to devise their joint tenancy interests to others, they could not do so because of their right of survivorship. But the joint tenancy disappeared when the son died, and the daughter automatically acquired the entire property in fee simple. As a result, her fee simple interest passed to the friend under her will when she died. (Choice A) Since the son was a joint tenant and died first, his property interest was acquired by the daughter. As a result, the son had no interest to devise to his cousin under a will. (Choice B) Under the common law and in most jurisdictions, a joint tenancy with right of survivorship entails four unities. This includes unity of title, which requires joint tenants to receive their interests in the same instrument of conveyance. Since the friend and the cousin received their purported interests in separate wills, they could not acquire joint tenancy interests. (Choice C) A lifetime transfer of a joint tenant's interest severs that interest from the joint tenancy, and it becomes a tenancy-in-common ownership interest. Therefore, the friend and cousin would have owned equal shares of the property as tenants in common had the daughter and son conveyed their interests when they were alive instead of attempting to devise them at death.","conclusion":"Answer D: The daughter's friend owns the fee simple."},"questionZh":"一位土地所有者通过无偿所有权契约将其土地转让给他的女儿和儿子，“作为按份共有人（tenant in common），费用简单”。契约的措辞足以建立具有生存权的普通法联合共有权，且不受法规的修改。随后，女儿正式签署了一份遗嘱，向一位朋友规定了她对这片土地的兴趣。然后，儿子正式签署了一份遗嘱，将他在这片土地上的权益授予了他的表弟。儿子死了，后来女儿也死了。两人都没有结过婚。女儿的朋友和表弟幸存下来。两份遗嘱均经过正式验证后，谁拥有该土地的哪些权益？","choicesZh":{"A":"表弟拥有简单的费用。","B":"女儿的朋友和表弟作为联权共有人拥有平等的份额，并享有生存权。","C":"女儿的朋友和表弟作为按份共有人（tenant in common）拥有平等的份额。","D":"女儿的朋友拥有简单的费用。"},"explanationZh":"联合租赁是一种共有财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益，并享有生存权。生存权是指联权共有人的权益在他/她去世后消失，剩余的联权共有人的权益自动扩大以吸收该权益。因此，联权共有人在死亡时没有利益。在这里，女儿和儿子是简单的按份共有人（tenant in common）。尽管他们执行了遗嘱，试图将自己的共同共有（joint tenancy）权益设计给他人，但由于他们的生存权，他们无法这样做。但儿子去世后，联权共有权就消失了，女儿自动获得了全部财产。因此，当她去世时，她的单利利息根据她的遗嘱转移给了她的朋友。 （选项A） 由于儿子是联权共有人且先去世，所以他的财产权益由女儿获得。结果，儿子没有兴趣在遗嘱下为他的表弟设计。 （选择 B） 根据普通法和大多数司法管辖区，具有生存权的联合共有需要四个单位。这包括所有权的统一，即要求联权共有人以同一转让文书获得其权益。由于朋友和表兄弟在各自的遗嘱中获得了所谓的权益，因此他们无法获得联合租赁权益。 （选择 C） 联权共有人的权益的终身转让会将该权益与联权共有权分割开来，并成为共同共有权的所有权权益。因此，如果女儿和儿子在生时转让了他们的利益，而不是试图在死后设计这些利益，那么朋友和表兄弟将作为按份共有人（tenant in common）拥有平等的财产份额。\n\n教育目标：联权共有人的利益在他/她去世后消失，剩余的联权共有人的利益自动扩大以吸收它（即生存权）。因此，不能制定联合租赁权益。","educationalObjectiveZh":"联权共有人的权益在其死亡后消失，其余联权共有人的权益自动扩大以吸收该权益（即生存权）。因此，不能制定联合租赁权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"两份遗嘱均经过正式验证后，谁拥有该土地的哪些权益？","rule":"联权共有人的权益在其死亡后消失，其余联权共有人的权益自动扩大以吸收该权益（即生存权）。因此，不能制定联合租赁权益。","analysis":"联合租赁是一种共有财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益，并享有生存权。生存权是指联权共有人的权益在他/她去世后消失，剩余的联权共有人的权益自动扩大以吸收该权益。因此，联权共有人在死亡时没有利益。在这里，女儿和儿子是简单的按份共有人（tenant in common）。尽管他们执行了遗嘱，试图将自己的共同共有（joint tenancy）权益设计给他人，但由于他们的生存权，他们无法这样做。但儿子去世后，联权共有权就消失了，女儿自动获得了全部财产。因此，当她去世时，她的单利利息根据她的遗嘱转移给了她的朋友。 （选项A） 由于儿子是联权共有人且先去世，所以他的财产权益由女儿获得。结果，儿子没有兴趣在遗嘱下为他的表弟设计。 （选择 B） 根据普通法和大多数司法管辖区，具有生存权的联合共有需要四个单位。这包括所有权的统一，即要求联权共有人以同一转让文书获得其权益。由于朋友和表兄弟在各自的遗嘱中获得了所谓的权益，因此他们无法获得联合租赁权益。 （选择 C） 联权共有人的权益的终身转让会将该权益与联权共有权分割开来，并成为共同共有权的所有权权益。因此，如果女儿和儿子在生时转让了他们的利益，而不是试图在死后设计这些利益，那么朋友和表兄弟将作为按份共有人（tenant in common）拥有平等的财产份额。\n\n教育目标：联权共有人的利益在他/她去世后消失，剩余的联权共有人的利益自动扩大以吸收它（即生存权）。因此，不能制定联合租赁权益。","conclusion":"答案 D：女儿的朋友拥有简单的费用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"four unities","ipa":"/fˈɔɹ jˈunəti/","syllables":"four uni·ties","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"四统一：共同共有传统要求时间、权源、权益和占有统一","contextualMeaning":"四统一：共同共有传统要求时间、权源、权益和占有统一","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) Under the common law and in most jurisdictions, a joint tenancy with right of survivorship entails four unities.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-4f1764cdf092e20d"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":94,"sourceQuestionPage":274,"sourceQuestionPages":[274],"sourceExplanationPages":[275,276],"sourceAnswerPages":[275,276],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-095","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A landlord leased a building to a tenant for a 10-year term. Two years after the term began, the tenant subleased the building to a sublessee for a 5-year term. Under the terms of the sublease, the sublessee agreed to make monthly rent payments to the tenant. Although the sublessee made timely rent payments to the tenant, the tenant did not forward four of those payments to the landlord. The tenant has left the jurisdiction and cannot be found. The landlord has sued the sublessee for the unpaid rent. There is no applicable statute. If the court rules that the sublessee is not liable to the landlord for the unpaid rent, what will be the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"A sublessee is responsible to the landlord only as a surety for unpaid rent owed by the tenant.","B":"The sublease constitutes a novation of the original lease.","C":"The sublessee is not in privity of estate or contract with the landlord.","D":"The sublessee's rent payments to the tenant fully discharged the sublessee's obligation to pay rent to the landlord."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A landlord and tenant have a legal relationship based on both: privity of contract – their shared interest in the lease agreement and\n\nprivity of estate – their successive right to possess the property (ie, the tenant's current possessory right immediately precedes the landlord's future possessory right).\n\nEither party can freely transfer his/her interest unless the lease prohibits it. For example, a tenant can sublease part of his/her interest to a third party (sublessee) for less than the remainder of the tenant's lease term. Despite that transfer, a sublessee is not liable to the landlord for a breach of the lease (eg, unpaid rent) because: no lease agreement exists between the landlord and the sublessee (no privity of contract) and the tenant will regain possession of the property before the landlord does (no privity of estate). Instead, the tenant remains liable to the landlord for any such breach. (Choice A) A surety is a person who assumes direct liability for another's obligation (eg, debt). Here, the sublessee is not a surety because there is no indication that the sublessee agreed to assume the tenant's contractual liability under the lease. Therefore, the sublessee is not liable for the tenant's failure to pay rent. (Choice B) A novation is a separate agreement based on an existing contract that (1) substitutes a new party for an original contracting party and (2) excuses the original party from further performance under the original contract. But since a sublease does neither, it is not a novation. (Choice D) A sublessee has no obligation to pay rent to a landlord.\n\nEducational objective: Since a sublessee and landlord lack privity of contract and privity of estate, the sublessee is not liable to the landlord for a breach of the tenant's lease (eg, unpaid rent). Instead, the tenant remains liable to the landlord for any such breach.\n\nReferences\n49 Am. Jur. 2d Landlord and Tenant § 970 (2018).","educationalObjective":"Since a sublessee and landlord lack privity of contract and privity of estate, the sublessee is not liable to the landlord for a breach of the tenant's lease (eg, unpaid rent). Instead, the tenant remains liable to the landlord for any such breach.","references":"49 Am. Jur. 2d Landlord and Tenant § 970 (2018).","rule":"Since a sublessee and landlord lack privity of contract and privity of estate, the sublessee is not liable to the landlord for a breach of the tenant's lease (eg, unpaid rent). Instead, the tenant remains liable to the landlord for any such breach.","irac":{"issue":"If the court rules that the sublessee is not liable to the landlord for the unpaid rent, what will be the most likely reason?","rule":"Since a sublessee and landlord lack privity of contract and privity of estate, the sublessee is not liable to the landlord for a breach of the tenant's lease (eg, unpaid rent). Instead, the tenant remains liable to the landlord for any such breach.","analysis":"A landlord and tenant have a legal relationship based on both: privity of contract – their shared interest in the lease agreement and\n\nprivity of estate – their successive right to possess the property (ie, the tenant's current possessory right immediately precedes the landlord's future possessory right).\n\nEither party can freely transfer his/her interest unless the lease prohibits it. For example, a tenant can sublease part of his/her interest to a third party (sublessee) for less than the remainder of the tenant's lease term. Despite that transfer, a sublessee is not liable to the landlord for a breach of the lease (eg, unpaid rent) because: no lease agreement exists between the landlord and the sublessee (no privity of contract) and the tenant will regain possession of the property before the landlord does (no privity of estate). Instead, the tenant remains liable to the landlord for any such breach. (Choice A) A surety is a person who assumes direct liability for another's obligation (eg, debt). Here, the sublessee is not a surety because there is no indication that the sublessee agreed to assume the tenant's contractual liability under the lease. Therefore, the sublessee is not liable for the tenant's failure to pay rent. (Choice B) A novation is a separate agreement based on an existing contract that (1) substitutes a new party for an original contracting party and (2) excuses the original party from further performance under the original contract. But since a sublease does neither, it is not a novation. (Choice D) A sublessee has no obligation to pay rent to a landlord.","conclusion":"Answer C: The sublessee is not in privity of estate or contract with the landlord."},"questionZh":"房东将一栋建筑物出租给租户，租期为 10 年。租期开始两年后，租户将建筑物转租给转租人，租期为 5 年。根据转租条款，转租人同意每月向租户支付租金。尽管转租人及时向租户支付了租金，但租户并未将其中四笔款项转发给房东。租客已离开辖区，无法找到。房东已起诉转租人，要求其支付未付租金。没有适用的法规。如果法院裁定转租人无需就未付租金向房东承担责任，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"转租人仅作为承租人所欠未付租金的担保人对房东负责。","B":"转租构成原始租赁的更新。","C":"转租人与房东不存在财产关系或合同关系。","D":"转承租人向租户支付的租金完全解除了转承租人向房东支付租金的义务。"},"explanationZh":"房东和租客之间的法律关系基于以下两个方面： 合同关系 – 他们在租赁协议中的共同利益；\n\n财产的相对性——他们对财产的继承权（即，租户当前的占有权紧接着房东未来的占有权）。\n\n除非租约禁止，任何一方都可以自由转让其利益。例如，承租人可以将其部分权益转租给第三方（转承租人），租期少于承租人剩余的租赁期限。尽管进行了转让，转承租人并不因违反租约（例如未付租金）而对房东承担责任，因为： 房东和转租人之间不存在租赁协议（无合同关系），并且承租人将在房东之前重新获得财产的占有权（无遗产关系）。相反，承租人仍需就任何此类违规行为向房东承担责任。 （选择A） 担保人是对他人的义务（例如债务）承担直接责任的人。在这里，转承租人不是担保人，因为没有迹象表明转承租人同意承担承租人在租赁下的合同责任。因此，转租人对于承租人未缴纳租金不承担责任。 （选择 B） 更替是基于现有合同的单独协议，它 (1) 用新的一方替代原缔约方，并且 (2) 免除原方进一步履行原合同的义务。但由于转租不做这两件事，所以它不是更新。 （选项 D） 转租人没有义务向房东支付租金。\n\n教育目标：由于转租人和房东缺乏合同关系和财产关系，因此转租人无需因违反租客租约（例如未付租金）而对房东承担责任。相反，承租人仍需就任何此类违规行为向房东承担责任。\n\n参考文献\n49 上午。尤尔。 2d 房东和租户 § 970 (2018)。","educationalObjectiveZh":"由于转租人和房东缺乏合同关系和财产关系，因此转租人无需因违反租客租约（例如未付租金）而对房东承担责任。相反，承租人仍需就任何此类违规行为向房东承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院裁定转租人无需就未付租金向房东承担责任，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"由于转租人和房东缺乏合同关系和财产关系，因此转租人无需因违反租客租约（例如未付租金）而对房东承担责任。相反，承租人仍需就任何此类违规行为向房东承担责任。","analysis":"房东和租客之间的法律关系基于以下两个方面： 合同关系 – 他们在租赁协议中的共同利益；\n\n财产的相对性——他们对财产的继承权（即，租户当前的占有权紧接着房东未来的占有权）。\n\n除非租约禁止，任何一方都可以自由转让其利益。例如，承租人可以将其部分权益转租给第三方（转承租人），租期少于承租人剩余的租赁期限。尽管进行了转让，转承租人并不因违反租约（例如未付租金）而对房东承担责任，因为： 房东和转租人之间不存在租赁协议（无合同关系），并且承租人将在房东之前重新获得财产的占有权（无遗产关系）。相反，承租人仍需就任何此类违规行为向房东承担责任。 （选择A） 担保人是对他人的义务（例如债务）承担直接责任的人。在这里，转承租人不是担保人，因为没有迹象表明转承租人同意承担承租人在租赁下的合同责任。因此，转租人对于承租人未缴纳租金不承担责任。 （选择 B） 更替是基于现有合同的单独协议，它 (1) 用新的一方替代原缔约方，并且 (2) 免除原方进一步履行原合同的义务。但由于转租不做这两件事，所以它不是更新。 （选项 D） 转租人没有义务向房东支付租金。\n\n教育目标：由于转租人和房东缺乏合同关系和财产关系，因此转租人无需因违反租客租约（例如未付租金）而对房东承担责任。相反，承租人仍需就任何此类违规行为向房东承担责任。\n\n参考文献\n49 上午。尤尔。 2d 房东和租户 § 970 (2018)。","conclusion":"答案 C：转租人与房东不存在财产关系或合同关系。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"privity of contract","ipa":"/'priviti ˈəv kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"priv·ity of con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合同关系：原合同当事人之间持续存在的合同义务联系","contextualMeaning":"合同关系：原合同当事人之间持续存在的合同义务联系","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Since a sublessee and landlord lack privity of contract and privity of estate, the sublessee is not liable to the landlord for a breach of the tenant's lease (eg, unpaid rent).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-8eab4dcd32e27549"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":95,"sourceQuestionPage":277,"sourceQuestionPages":[277],"sourceExplanationPages":[278,279],"sourceAnswerPages":[278,279],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-096","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A landlord leased an apartment to a tenant on a month-to-month basis beginning on the first of the month, with rent payable monthly. Five months into the lease, the tenant failed to pay rent. The landlord then sued the tenant for possession for nonpayment of rent. The tenant successfully defended this suit on the ground that the apartment was uninhabitable and that, accordingly, no rent was properly payable. On May 7, immediately following the court order in the tenant's favor, the landlord served the tenant with a written but not notarized notice to terminate the tenancy and to vacate the apartment on or before June 30. On July 1, the tenant was still in possession, nothing had changed, and the landlord brought an appropriate action for possession. The tenant prevailed. What is the most likely reason for the court's decision?","choices":{"A":"A six-month notice was required to terminate this periodic tenancy.","B":"The landlord did not give the tenant a notarized notice of termination.","C":"The landlord's notice to terminate was improper, because it was given to the tenant more than one month before the required termination date.","D":"The landlord was attempting to terminate the tenancy as a reaction to the tenant's defense in the previous lawsuit."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Prohibited landlord conduct\n\nRetaliation Increasing rent or terminating lease because tenant exercised legal right—eg:\n\nreporting housing code violation organizing tenants’ association Discrimination Refusing to rent or negotiate lease based on tenant's race, ethnicity, religion, national origin, gender, familial status, or disability (Fair Housing Act)\n\nA periodic tenancy automatically continues for successive periods of time (eg, month to month) until either the landlord or tenant gives notice to terminate the tenancy. However, most jurisdictions prohibit a landlord from terminating a residential lease when: the landlord is in the business of renting residential property\n\nthe tenant has complied with his/her lease obligations and\n\nthe landlord is retaliating against the tenant (eg, increasing rent, terminating a lease) for exercising a legal right in good faith and with reasonable cause.\n\nHere, the landlord leased an apartment to the tenant (business of renting residential property) and later sued the tenant for unpaid rent. The tenant successfully defended that suit by challenging the apartment's habitability (exercise of a legal right). As a result, no rent was owed (compliance with lease obligations). The landlord then attempted to terminate the tenant's lease (retaliation). Therefore, the tenant likely prevailed in the landlord's action for possession because the termination was a retaliatory reaction to the tenant's defense in the previous lawsuit. (Choice A) Six months' notice is required to terminate a year-to-year periodic tenancy. But a periodic tenancy with successive periods of less than one year (as seen here) can be terminated at the end of a period if notice is given before the period starts—eg, terminating a month-to-month periodic tenancy on June 30 requires notice by May 31. (Choice B) Notice to terminate a periodic tenancy generally must be in writing but does not need to be notarized. (Choice C) A month-to-month periodic tenancy can be terminated at the end of a month through notice given any time before the start of that month. Therefore, notice given on May 7 to vacate the premises by June 30 would have been proper had the termination not been retaliatory.\n\nEducational objective: Most jurisdictions prohibit a landlord from terminating a residential lease when (1) the landlord is in the business of renting residential property, (2) the tenant has complied with his/her lease obligations, and (3) the landlord is retaliating against the tenant for exercising a legal right in good faith and with reasonable cause.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant §§ 14.8–14.9 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (listing remedies for retaliatory action by landlord).","educationalObjective":"Most jurisdictions prohibit a landlord from terminating a residential lease when (1) the landlord is in the business of renting residential property, (2) the tenant has complied with his/her lease obligations, and (3) the landlord is retaliating against the tenant for exercising a legal right in good faith and with reasonable cause.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant §§ 14.8–14.9 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (listing remedies for retaliatory action by landlord).","rule":"Most jurisdictions prohibit a landlord from terminating a residential lease when (1) the landlord is in the business of renting residential property, (2) the tenant has complied with his/her lease obligations, and (3) the landlord is retaliating against the tenant for exercising a legal right in good faith and with reasonable cause.","irac":{"issue":"What is the most likely reason for the court's decision?","rule":"Most jurisdictions prohibit a landlord from terminating a residential lease when (1) the landlord is in the business of renting residential property, (2) the tenant has complied with his/her lease obligations, and (3) the landlord is retaliating against the tenant for exercising a legal right in good faith and with reasonable cause.","analysis":"Prohibited landlord conduct\n\nRetaliation Increasing rent or terminating lease because tenant exercised legal right—eg:\n\nreporting housing code violation organizing tenants’ association Discrimination Refusing to rent or negotiate lease based on tenant's race, ethnicity, religion, national origin, gender, familial status, or disability (Fair Housing Act)\n\nA periodic tenancy automatically continues for successive periods of time (eg, month to month) until either the landlord or tenant gives notice to terminate the tenancy. However, most jurisdictions prohibit a landlord from terminating a residential lease when: the landlord is in the business of renting residential property\n\nthe tenant has complied with his/her lease obligations and\n\nthe landlord is retaliating against the tenant (eg, increasing rent, terminating a lease) for exercising a legal right in good faith and with reasonable cause.\n\nHere, the landlord leased an apartment to the tenant (business of renting residential property) and later sued the tenant for unpaid rent. The tenant successfully defended that suit by challenging the apartment's habitability (exercise of a legal right). As a result, no rent was owed (compliance with lease obligations). The landlord then attempted to terminate the tenant's lease (retaliation). Therefore, the tenant likely prevailed in the landlord's action for possession because the termination was a retaliatory reaction to the tenant's defense in the previous lawsuit. (Choice A) Six months' notice is required to terminate a year-to-year periodic tenancy. But a periodic tenancy with successive periods of less than one year (as seen here) can be terminated at the end of a period if notice is given before the period starts—eg, terminating a month-to-month periodic tenancy on June 30 requires notice by May 31. (Choice B) Notice to terminate a periodic tenancy generally must be in writing but does not need to be notarized. (Choice C) A month-to-month periodic tenancy can be terminated at the end of a month through notice given any time before the start of that month. Therefore, notice given on May 7 to vacate the premises by June 30 would have been proper had the termination not been retaliatory.","conclusion":"Answer D: The landlord was attempting to terminate the tenancy as a reaction to the tenant's defense in the previous lawsuit."},"questionZh":"房东从每月一号开始按月向租户出租公寓，每月支付租金。租约五个月后，租户未能支付租金。房东随后以未支付租金为由起诉房客占有。租客成功地抗辩了该诉讼，理由是该公寓无法居住，因此无需支付租金。 5 月 7 日，在法院作出对租户有利的命令后，房东立即向租户发出了一份书面但未经公证的通知，要求其终止租赁并在 6 月 30 日或之前搬出公寓。7 月 1 日，租户仍占有公寓，没有任何变化，房东提起了适当的占有诉讼。租客占了上风。法院做出决定的最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"终止此定期租赁需要提前六个月发出通知。","B":"房东没有向租客提供经过公证的终止通知。","C":"房东的终止通知是不恰当的，因为该通知是在规定的终止日期之前一个多月向租户发出的。","D":"房东试图终止租约，作为对租户在上一次诉讼中辩护的回应。"},"explanationZh":"房东禁止的行为\n\n报复 由于租户行使合法权利而增加租金或终止租约，例如：\n\n举报违反住房法规的行为 组织租户协会 歧视 基于租户的种族、民族、宗教、国籍、性别、家庭状况或残疾而拒绝出租或协商租赁（《公平住房法》）\n\n定期租赁会自动持续一段时间（例如，逐月），直到房东或租户发出终止租赁的通知。然而，大多数司法管辖区禁止房东在以下情况下终止住宅租赁： 房东从事出租住宅物业的业务\n\n承租人已履行其租赁义务，并且\n\n房东对善意并有合理理由行使合法权利的租户进行报复（例如，增加租金、终止租约）。\n\n在此，房东将一套公寓出租给租客（出租住宅物业的业务），后来起诉租客拖欠租金。租户通过质疑公寓的可居住性（行使合法权利）成功地在该诉讼中进行了辩护。因此，没有欠租金（遵守租赁义务）。房东随后试图终止租户的租约（报复）。因此，租户很可能在房东的占有诉讼中获胜，因为终止合同是对租户在之前诉讼中辩护的报复性反应。 （选择 A） 终止每年的定期租赁需要提前六个月发出通知。但是，如果在期限开始之前发出通知，连续期限少于一年的定期租赁（如此处所示）可以在期限结束时终止，例如，在 6 月 30 日终止按月定期租赁需要在 5 月 31 日之前发出通知。（选择 B）终止定期租赁的通知通常必须采用书面形式，但不需要经过公证。 （选择 C）按月定期租赁可以通过在该月月初之前的任何时间发出通知在月底终止。因此，如果终止协议不是报复性的，5 月 7 日发出的要求在 6 月 30 日之前搬出该场所的通知是适当的。\n\n教育目标：大多数司法管辖区禁止房东在下列情况下终止住宅租赁：(1) 房东从事住宅物业租赁业务；(2) 租户已履行其租赁义务；(3) 房东对善意并有合理理由行使合法权利的租户进行报复。\n\n参考文献\n提案重述（第二次）：房东与租户 §§ 14.8–14.9（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（列出针对房东报复行为的补救措施）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"大多数司法管辖区禁止房东在下列情况下终止住宅租赁：(1) 房东从事住宅物业租赁业务；(2) 租户已履行其租赁义务；(3) 房东对善意并有合理理由行使合法权利的租户进行报复。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院做出决定的最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"大多数司法管辖区禁止房东在下列情况下终止住宅租赁：(1) 房东从事住宅物业租赁业务；(2) 租户已履行其租赁义务；(3) 房东对善意并有合理理由行使合法权利的租户进行报复。","analysis":"房东禁止的行为\n\n报复 由于租户行使合法权利而增加租金或终止租约，例如：\n\n举报违反住房法规的行为 组织租户协会 歧视 基于租户的种族、民族、宗教、国籍、性别、家庭状况或残疾而拒绝出租或协商租赁（《公平住房法》）\n\n定期租赁会自动持续一段时间（例如，逐月），直到房东或租户发出终止租赁的通知。然而，大多数司法管辖区禁止房东在以下情况下终止住宅租赁： 房东从事出租住宅物业的业务\n\n承租人已履行其租赁义务，并且\n\n房东对善意并有合理理由行使合法权利的租户进行报复（例如，增加租金、终止租约）。\n\n在此，房东将一套公寓出租给租客（出租住宅物业的业务），后来起诉租客拖欠租金。租户通过质疑公寓的可居住性（行使合法权利）成功地在该诉讼中进行了辩护。因此，没有欠租金（遵守租赁义务）。房东随后试图终止租户的租约（报复）。因此，租户很可能在房东的占有诉讼中获胜，因为终止合同是对租户在之前诉讼中辩护的报复性反应。 （选择 A） 终止每年的定期租赁需要提前六个月发出通知。但是，如果在期限开始之前发出通知，连续期限少于一年的定期租赁（如此处所示）可以在期限结束时终止，例如，在 6 月 30 日终止按月定期租赁需要在 5 月 31 日之前发出通知。（选择 B）终止定期租赁的通知通常必须采用书面形式，但不需要经过公证。 （选择 C）按月定期租赁可以通过在该月月初之前的任何时间发出通知在月底终止。因此，如果终止协议不是报复性的，5 月 7 日发出的要求在 6 月 30 日之前搬出该场所的通知是适当的。\n\n教育目标：大多数司法管辖区禁止房东在下列情况下终止住宅租赁：(1) 房东从事住宅物业租赁业务；(2) 租户已履行其租赁义务；(3) 房东对善意并有合理理由行使合法权利的租户进行报复。\n\n参考文献\n提案重述（第二次）：房东与租户 §§ 14.8–14.9（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（列出针对房东报复行为的补救措施）。","conclusion":"答案 D：房东试图终止租约，作为对租户在上一次诉讼中辩护的回应。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":96,"sourceQuestionPage":280,"sourceQuestionPages":[280],"sourceExplanationPages":[281,282],"sourceAnswerPages":[281,282],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-097","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A woman owned land in fee simple absolute. The woman conveyed the land to a friend \"for life,\" and when the friend died the land was to go to the woman's neighbor \"and her heirs.\" The neighbor died and in her duly probated will devised her entire estate to a local charity. If she had died intestate, her daughter would have been her only heir. One year after the neighbor died, her daughter executed a quitclaim deed conveying any interest she might have in the land to the woman's friend. The common law Rule Against Perpetuities is unmodified in the jurisdiction. There are no other applicable statutes. Who has what interest in the land?","choices":{"A":"The friend has a fee simple absolute, because his life estate merged with the remainder conveyed to him by the daughter.","B":"The friend has a life estate and the charity has a vested remainder, because the neighbor's interest was devisable.","C":"The friend has a life estate and the daughter has a vested remainder, because the deed from the woman created an interest in the neighbor's heirs.","D":"The friend has a life estate and the woman has a reversion, because the neighbor's remainder was void under the Rule Against Perpetuities."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A life estate is a present possessory interest in real property that terminates upon the death of an individual. The future-interest holder acquires possession of the estate when that death occurs. If the future interest is held by someone other than the grantor, it is called a remainder. Remainders are fully transferable inter vivos, devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance. This is true in most jurisdictions* regardless of whether the remainder is: vested – not subject to any condition precedent AND held by an identifiable living person (eg, \"then to my neighbor and her heirs\") or\n\ncontingent – subject to some condition precedent (other than the natural termination of the prior estate) OR held by an unknown or unborn person (eg, \"then to my neighbor, but only if…\").\n\nHere, the woman conveyed a life estate in the land to her friend, which would go to her neighbor unconditionally when the friend died. The neighbor's vested remainder was devisable, so it passed to the charity under the terms of her duly probated will. Therefore, the friend (who is still living) has a life estate, and the charity has a vested remainder that will become possessory when the friend dies. *At common law and in a minority of jurisdictions, contingent remainders cannot be transferred inter vivos. (Choices A & C) The woman's deed conveyed a remainder interest to the neighbor \"and her heirs.\" These words of limitation indicate that the neighbor's remainder interest is a fee simple absolute (FSA). But they do not create an ownership interest in the neighbor's heirs. Therefore, the daughter (neighbor's only heir) did not have any interest in the land to convey to the friend, and he never acquired an FSA. (Choice D) The Rule Against Perpetuities applies only to future interests that may vest more than 21 years after the death of some relevant person alive when the interest was created—not to this vested remainder.\n\nEducational objective: Remainders are either vested (ie, not subject to conditions precedent and held by an identifiable living person) OR contingent (ie, subject to conditions precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person). Both are transferrable, devisable, and descendible.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Wills & Other Donative Transfers § 25.2 cmt. f (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (alienability, divisability, and inheritability of future interests).","educationalObjective":"Remainders are either vested (ie, not subject to conditions precedent and held by an identifiable living person) OR contingent (ie, subject to conditions precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person). Both are transferrable, devisable, and descendible.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Wills & Other Donative Transfers § 25.2 cmt. f (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (alienability, divisability, and inheritability of future interests).","rule":"Remainders are either vested (ie, not subject to conditions precedent and held by an identifiable living person) OR contingent (ie, subject to conditions precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person). Both are transferrable, devisable, and descendible.","irac":{"issue":"Who has what interest in the land?","rule":"Remainders are either vested (ie, not subject to conditions precedent and held by an identifiable living person) OR contingent (ie, subject to conditions precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person). Both are transferrable, devisable, and descendible.","analysis":"A life estate is a present possessory interest in real property that terminates upon the death of an individual. The future-interest holder acquires possession of the estate when that death occurs. If the future interest is held by someone other than the grantor, it is called a remainder. Remainders are fully transferable inter vivos, devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance. This is true in most jurisdictions* regardless of whether the remainder is: vested – not subject to any condition precedent AND held by an identifiable living person (eg, \"then to my neighbor and her heirs\") or\n\ncontingent – subject to some condition precedent (other than the natural termination of the prior estate) OR held by an unknown or unborn person (eg, \"then to my neighbor, but only if…\").\n\nHere, the woman conveyed a life estate in the land to her friend, which would go to her neighbor unconditionally when the friend died. The neighbor's vested remainder was devisable, so it passed to the charity under the terms of her duly probated will. Therefore, the friend (who is still living) has a life estate, and the charity has a vested remainder that will become possessory when the friend dies. *At common law and in a minority of jurisdictions, contingent remainders cannot be transferred inter vivos. (Choices A & C) The woman's deed conveyed a remainder interest to the neighbor \"and her heirs.\" These words of limitation indicate that the neighbor's remainder interest is a fee simple absolute (FSA). But they do not create an ownership interest in the neighbor's heirs. Therefore, the daughter (neighbor's only heir) did not have any interest in the land to convey to the friend, and he never acquired an FSA. (Choice D) The Rule Against Perpetuities applies only to future interests that may vest more than 21 years after the death of some relevant person alive when the interest was created—not to this vested remainder.","conclusion":"Answer B: The friend has a life estate and the charity has a vested remainder, because the neighbor's interest was devisable."},"questionZh":"女人拥有土地绝对是免费的。该妇女将土地“终身”转让给一位朋友，当朋友去世后，土地将归该妇女的邻居“及其继承人”所有。邻居去世了，在她经过正式认证的遗嘱中，她将她的全部财产捐赠给了当地的一家慈善机构。如果她去世时没有留下遗嘱，她的女儿将是她唯一的继承人。邻居去世一年后，她的女儿签署了一份无偿所有权契约，将她对这片土地可能拥有的任何权益转让给该妇女的朋友。普通法中的反永续规则在管辖范围内未作修改。没有其他适用的法规。谁对土地有什么兴趣？","choicesZh":{"A":"这位朋友拥有绝对的权利，因为他的终身财产与女儿转让给他的剩余财产合并在一起。","B":"朋友拥有终身财产，而慈善机构则拥有既得剩余财产，因为邻居的利益是可以设计的。","C":"朋友拥有终生财产，女儿拥有既得剩余财产，因为该妇女的契约创造了邻居继承人的利益。","D":"朋友拥有终身财产，而女人拥有归还财产，因为根据反对永久规则，邻居的剩余财产无效。"},"explanationZh":"终身财产是对不动产的现有占有权益，在个人死亡时终止。当死亡发生时，未来权益持有人获得遗产的占有。如果未来权益由授予人以外的其他人持有，则称为剩余权益。剩余部分可以在生前完全转移，可以通过意志设计，也可以通过继承传承。在大多数司法管辖区*都是如此，无论剩余部分是否：既得——不受任何先决条件的约束并且由可识别的在世人持有（例如，“然后归我的邻居和她的继承人”）或\n\n或有的 – 受某些先决条件限制（先前财产的自然终止除外）或由未知或未出生的人持有（例如，“然后给我的邻居，但前提是……”）。\n\n在这里，这位妇女将土地上的终身财产转让给她的朋友，当朋友去世时，这笔财产将无条件归她的邻居所有。邻居的既得剩余部分是可以设计的，因此根据她经过正式认证的遗嘱条款将其转交给慈善机构。因此，朋友（仍然活着）拥有终身财产，慈善机构拥有既得的剩余部分，当朋友去世时，该剩余部分将成为占有者。 *根据普通法和少数司法管辖区，或有余额不能在生前转移。 （选项 A 和 C）该妇女的行为向邻居“及其继承人”传达了剩余权益。这些限制性词语表明邻居的剩余利息是简单绝对利息（FSA）。但他们不会在邻居的继承人中产生所有权权益。因此，女儿（邻居的唯一继承人）对土地没有任何权益可以转让给朋友，而他也从未获得 FSA。 （选项 D）反对永续权规则仅适用于在权益创建时相关人员去世 21 年后可能归属的未来权益，而不适用于该既定剩余权益。\n\n教育目标：剩余部分要么是既定的（即不受先决条件限制并由可识别的活着的人持有），要么是偶然的（即受先决条件限制或由未知或未出生的人持有）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转移 § 25.2 cmt。 f（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（未来利益的可转让性、可分割性和可继承性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"剩余部分要么是既定的（即不受先决条件限制并由可识别的在世人士持有），要么是偶然的（即受先决条件限制或由未知或未出生的人持有）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁对土地有什么兴趣？","rule":"剩余部分要么是既定的（即不受先决条件限制并由可识别的在世人士持有），要么是偶然的（即受先决条件限制或由未知或未出生的人持有）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。","analysis":"终身财产是对不动产的现有占有权益，在个人死亡时终止。当死亡发生时，未来权益持有人获得遗产的占有。如果未来权益由授予人以外的其他人持有，则称为剩余权益。剩余部分可以在生前完全转移，可以通过意志设计，也可以通过继承传承。在大多数司法管辖区*都是如此，无论剩余部分是否：既得——不受任何先决条件的约束并且由可识别的在世人持有（例如，“然后归我的邻居和她的继承人”）或\n\n或有的 – 受某些先决条件限制（先前财产的自然终止除外）或由未知或未出生的人持有（例如，“然后给我的邻居，但前提是……”）。\n\n在这里，这位妇女将土地上的终身财产转让给她的朋友，当朋友去世时，这笔财产将无条件归她的邻居所有。邻居的既得剩余部分是可以设计的，因此根据她经过正式认证的遗嘱条款将其转交给慈善机构。因此，朋友（仍然活着）拥有终身财产，慈善机构拥有既得的剩余部分，当朋友去世时，该剩余部分将成为占有者。 *根据普通法和少数司法管辖区，或有余额不能在生前转移。 （选项 A 和 C）该妇女的行为向邻居“及其继承人”传达了剩余权益。这些限制性词语表明邻居的剩余利息是简单绝对利息（FSA）。但他们不会在邻居的继承人中产生所有权权益。因此，女儿（邻居的唯一继承人）对土地没有任何权益可以转让给朋友，而他也从未获得 FSA。 （选项 D）反对永续权规则仅适用于在权益创建时相关人员去世 21 年后可能归属的未来权益，而不适用于该既定剩余权益。\n\n教育目标：剩余部分要么是既定的（即不受先决条件限制并由可识别的活着的人持有），要么是偶然的（即受先决条件限制或由未知或未出生的人持有）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转移 § 25.2 cmt。 f（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（未来利益的可转让性、可分割性和可继承性）。","conclusion":"答案 B：朋友拥有终身财产，而慈善机构则拥有既得剩余财产，因为邻居的利益是可以设计的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-5df1a39a5673766a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":97,"sourceQuestionPage":283,"sourceQuestionPages":[283],"sourceExplanationPages":[284,285],"sourceAnswerPages":[284,285],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-098","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"An organic farm was conveyed to a married couple by a deed that created a cotenancy in equal shares with the right of survivorship. The husband later informed the wife that he intended to convey his \"undivided one-half interest in the farm\" to his sister. The wife objected to the conveyance, but the husband nevertheless conveyed his interest in the farm to the sister by deed. The husband has since died. In an action between the sister and the wife to determine title to the farm, who will prevail?","choices":{"A":"The sister, because the husband and the wife had a tenancy in common.","B":"The sister, because the wife knew of the conveyance prior to the husband's death.","C":"The wife, because she is the sole owner of the farm.","D":"The wife, but only if the jurisdiction recognizes tenancies by the entirety."},"answer":"D","explanation":"There are three types of concurrent estates (ie, cotenancies): Tenancy in common – a tenancy in which each cotenant has a separate and possibly unequal interest in the property with no right of survivorship\n\nJoint tenancy – a tenancy in which each cotenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship\n\nTenancy by the entirety – a joint tenancy between married persons\n\nHere, the conveyance to the husband and the wife created a cotenancy in equal shares with the right of survivorship. Therefore, they received either a joint tenancy or a tenancy by the entirety—not a tenancy in common (Choice A). Joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety are not devisable (by will) or inheritable (by intestate succession). And only a joint tenancy interest can be conveyed during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent. As a result, the outcome of this dispute depends on which tenancy the husband and the wife received: joint tenancy – in which case the husband's lifetime conveyance to the sister would have severed the joint tenancy and created a tenancy in common between the sister and the wife or\n\ntenancy by the entirety* – in which case the conveyance to the sister would have failed, and the wife would have acquired complete ownership of the farm upon the husband's death\n\nThis means that the wife would be the sole owner of the farm, and therefore prevail, only if the husband and the wife had a tenancy by the entirety (Choice C). Otherwise, the sister will prevail. *A tenancy by the entirety is recognized in about half of the jurisdictions. (Choice B) The wife's knowledge has no effect on the validity of the husband's conveyance. But the wife's consent would matter if the husband and wife had received a tenancy by the entirety, since neither tenant could convey the property without the other's consent.\n\nEducational objective: Both joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety have survivorship rights, so they are not devisable or inheritable. But joint tenancies—unlike tenancies by the entirety—are transferrable during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent.","educationalObjective":"Both joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety have survivorship rights, so they are not devisable or inheritable. But joint tenancies—unlike tenancies by the entirety—are transferrable during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent.","references":"","rule":"Both joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety have survivorship rights, so they are not devisable or inheritable. But joint tenancies—unlike tenancies by the entirety—are transferrable during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent.","irac":{"issue":"In an action between the sister and the wife to determine title to the farm, who will prevail?","rule":"Both joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety have survivorship rights, so they are not devisable or inheritable. But joint tenancies—unlike tenancies by the entirety—are transferrable during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent.","analysis":"There are three types of concurrent estates (ie, cotenancies): Tenancy in common – a tenancy in which each cotenant has a separate and possibly unequal interest in the property with no right of survivorship\n\nJoint tenancy – a tenancy in which each cotenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship\n\nTenancy by the entirety – a joint tenancy between married persons\n\nHere, the conveyance to the husband and the wife created a cotenancy in equal shares with the right of survivorship. Therefore, they received either a joint tenancy or a tenancy by the entirety—not a tenancy in common (Choice A). Joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety are not devisable (by will) or inheritable (by intestate succession). And only a joint tenancy interest can be conveyed during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent. As a result, the outcome of this dispute depends on which tenancy the husband and the wife received: joint tenancy – in which case the husband's lifetime conveyance to the sister would have severed the joint tenancy and created a tenancy in common between the sister and the wife or\n\ntenancy by the entirety* – in which case the conveyance to the sister would have failed, and the wife would have acquired complete ownership of the farm upon the husband's death\n\nThis means that the wife would be the sole owner of the farm, and therefore prevail, only if the husband and the wife had a tenancy by the entirety (Choice C). Otherwise, the sister will prevail. *A tenancy by the entirety is recognized in about half of the jurisdictions. (Choice B) The wife's knowledge has no effect on the validity of the husband's conveyance. But the wife's consent would matter if the husband and wife had received a tenancy by the entirety, since neither tenant could convey the property without the other's consent.","conclusion":"Answer D: The wife, but only if the jurisdiction recognizes tenancies by the entirety."},"questionZh":"有机农场通过一份契约转让给一对已婚夫妇，该契约创造了与幸存者权利均等的共同共有权。丈夫后来告诉妻子，他打算将“农场中不可分割的二分之一的权益”转让给他的妹妹。妻子反对转让，但丈夫仍然通过契约将其对农场的权益转让给了妹妹。丈夫已经去世了。在姐姐和妻子之间争夺农场所有权的诉讼中，谁会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"姐姐，因为丈夫和妻子有共同的租赁权。","B":"妹妹，因为妻子在丈夫去世前就知道财产转让事宜。","C":"妻子，因为她是农场的唯一所有者。","D":"妻子，但前提是司法管辖区承认全部租赁。"},"explanationZh":"共有三种类型的共同财产（即共同共有（joint tenancy））： 共同共有（joint tenancy） – 每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有单独且可能不平等的权益且没有生存权的租赁\n\n联名共有 – 每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益并享有生存权的租赁\n\n整体租赁 – 已婚人士之间的联合租赁\n\n在这里，丈夫和妻子的财产转让建立了平等分享的共同共有权和生存权。因此，他们要么获得联合租赁，要么获得整体租赁——而不是共同共有（joint tenancy）（选择 A）。联合租赁和整体租赁不可设计（通过遗嘱）或可继承（通过无遗嘱继承）。而且，在一个租户的一生中，只有联合租赁权益才能在未经其他租户同意的情况下转让。因此，这场纠纷的结果取决于丈夫和妻子获得的租赁权： 联合租赁——在这种情况下，丈夫的终身财产转让给姐妹将切断联合租赁权，并在姐妹和妻子之间建立共同共有（joint tenancy）权，或者\n\n全部租赁* – 在这种情况下，向姐妹的转让将失败，妻子将在丈夫去世后获得农场的完全所有权\n\n这意味着妻子将是农场的唯一所有者，因此只有在丈夫和妻子拥有全部租赁权的情况下才占上风（选项 C）。不然的话，姐姐就占上风了。 *大约一半的司法管辖区承认整体租赁。 （选项B） 妻子的知情权对丈夫财产转让的有效性没有影响。但如果丈夫和妻子获得了全部租赁权，妻子的同意就很重要，因为未经对方同意，任何一个租户都不能转让财产。\n\n教育目标： 联合租赁和整体租赁都具有幸存者权利，因此不可设计或继承。但联合租赁（与整体租赁不同）可以在租户的一生中转让，无需其他租户的同意。","educationalObjectiveZh":"联合租赁和整体租赁都具有幸存者权利，因此不可设计或继承。但联合租赁（与整体租赁不同）可以在租户的一生中转让，无需其他租户的同意。","iracZh":{"issue":"在姐姐和妻子之间争夺农场所有权的诉讼中，谁会获胜？","rule":"联合租赁和整体租赁都具有幸存者权利，因此不可设计或继承。但联合租赁（与整体租赁不同）可以在租户的一生中转让，无需其他租户的同意。","analysis":"共有三种类型的共同财产（即共同共有（joint tenancy））： 共同共有（joint tenancy） – 每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有单独且可能不平等的权益且没有生存权的租赁\n\n联名共有 – 每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益并享有生存权的租赁\n\n整体租赁 – 已婚人士之间的联合租赁\n\n在这里，丈夫和妻子的财产转让建立了平等分享的共同共有权和生存权。因此，他们要么获得联合租赁，要么获得整体租赁——而不是共同共有（joint tenancy）（选择 A）。联合租赁和整体租赁不可设计（通过遗嘱）或可继承（通过无遗嘱继承）。而且，在一个租户的一生中，只有联合租赁权益才能在未经其他租户同意的情况下转让。因此，这场纠纷的结果取决于丈夫和妻子获得的租赁权： 联合租赁——在这种情况下，丈夫的终身财产转让给姐妹将切断联合租赁权，并在姐妹和妻子之间建立共同共有（joint tenancy）权，或者\n\n全部租赁* – 在这种情况下，向姐妹的转让将失败，妻子将在丈夫去世后获得农场的完全所有权\n\n这意味着妻子将是农场的唯一所有者，因此只有在丈夫和妻子拥有全部租赁权的情况下才占上风（选项 C）。不然的话，姐姐就占上风了。 *大约一半的司法管辖区承认整体租赁。 （选项B） 妻子的知情权对丈夫财产转让的有效性没有影响。但如果丈夫和妻子获得了全部租赁权，妻子的同意就很重要，因为未经对方同意，任何一个租户都不能转让财产。\n\n教育目标： 联合租赁和整体租赁都具有幸存者权利，因此不可设计或继承。但联合租赁（与整体租赁不同）可以在租户的一生中转让，无需其他租户的同意。","conclusion":"答案 D：妻子，但前提是司法管辖区承认全部租赁。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-6d30fb910f85f985"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":98,"sourceQuestionPage":286,"sourceQuestionPages":[286],"sourceExplanationPages":[287,288],"sourceAnswerPages":[287,288],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-099","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A dairy farmer and his cousin owned a large farm in fee simple as tenants in common, each owning an undivided one-half interest. For five years, the farmer occupied the farm and conducted farming operations. The farmer never accounted to his cousin for any income but did pay all real estate taxes when they were due and kept the buildings on the farm insured against loss from fire, storm, and flood. The cousin lived in a distant city and was interested only in realizing a profit from the sale of the land when market conditions produced the price he wanted. The farmer died intestate survived by his daughter, who was his sole heir. Thereafter, the daughter occupied the farm but was inexperienced in farming operations. The result was a financial disaster. The daughter failed to pay real estate taxes for two years. The appropriate governmental authority held a tax sale to recover the taxes due. At the sale, the cousin was the only bidder and obtained a conveyance from the appropriate governmental authority upon payment of an amount sufficient to discharge the amounts due for taxes, plus interest and penalties, and the costs of holding the tax sale. The amount paid was one- third of the reasonable market value of the farm. Thereafter, the cousin instituted an appropriate action against the daughter to quiet title in and recover possession of the farm. The daughter asserted all defenses available to her. Except for the statutes related to real estate taxes and tax sales, there is no applicable statute. In this lawsuit, is the cousin entitled to a decree quieting title so that he is the sole owner in fee simple of the farm?","choices":{"A":"No, if the daughter pays the cousin one-half of the amount he paid for the tax deed.","B":"No, if the daughter pays the cousin one-half of the reasonable market value of the farm.","C":"Yes, because the cousin survived the farmer.","D":"Yes, because the daughter defaulted in the obligations undertaken by the farmer."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A tenant who buys back co-owned property at a tax sale or foreclosure sale takes the property subject to the other co-tenants' interests. The nonpaying co-tenants then have a reasonable time to contribute their share of the purchase price (as determined by their proportionate ownership interests). A co-tenant who fails to do so forfeits his/her interest in the property to the purchasing co-tenant. Here, the dairy farmer and his cousin each owned a one-half interest in the farm as tenants in common. When the farmer died, his daughter acquired his one-half interest in the farm. She failed to pay real estate taxes for two years, and a tax sale was held. At that sale, the cousin bought back the farm. But he will only be the sole owner of the farm if the daughter fails to contribute her one-half share of the purchase price. (Choice B) The daughter need only pay the cousin half of the amount he paid at the tax sale to retain her one-half interest in the farm. The farm's reasonable market value is irrelevant. (Choice C) The fact that the cousin survived the farmer would have entitled him to sole ownership of the farm had they been joint tenants. That is because a joint tenant's interest disappears upon death and the remaining tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it (ie, right of survivorship). But tenants in common have no such right. (Choice D) The daughter's default on tax obligations did not terminate her interest in the farm. Instead, her interest in the farm (or forfeiture thereof) depends on whether she pays the cousin her due contribution—half of the purchase price.\n\nEducational objective: A tenant who buys back co-owned property at a tax or foreclosure sale takes the property subject to the other co-tenants' interests. But the other co-tenants must contribute their proportionate share of the purchase price within a reasonable time to avoid forfeiting their interests to the purchasing co-tenant.","educationalObjective":"A tenant who buys back co-owned property at a tax or foreclosure sale takes the property subject to the other co-tenants' interests. But the other co-tenants must contribute their proportionate share of the purchase price within a reasonable time to avoid forfeiting their interests to the purchasing co-tenant.","references":"","rule":"A tenant who buys back co-owned property at a tax or foreclosure sale takes the property subject to the other co-tenants' interests. But the other co-tenants must contribute their proportionate share of the purchase price within a reasonable time to avoid forfeiting their interests to the purchasing co-tenant.","irac":{"issue":"In this lawsuit, is the cousin entitled to a decree quieting title so that he is the sole owner in fee simple of the farm?","rule":"A tenant who buys back co-owned property at a tax or foreclosure sale takes the property subject to the other co-tenants' interests. But the other co-tenants must contribute their proportionate share of the purchase price within a reasonable time to avoid forfeiting their interests to the purchasing co-tenant.","analysis":"A tenant who buys back co-owned property at a tax sale or foreclosure sale takes the property subject to the other co-tenants' interests. The nonpaying co-tenants then have a reasonable time to contribute their share of the purchase price (as determined by their proportionate ownership interests). A co-tenant who fails to do so forfeits his/her interest in the property to the purchasing co-tenant. Here, the dairy farmer and his cousin each owned a one-half interest in the farm as tenants in common. When the farmer died, his daughter acquired his one-half interest in the farm. She failed to pay real estate taxes for two years, and a tax sale was held. At that sale, the cousin bought back the farm. But he will only be the sole owner of the farm if the daughter fails to contribute her one-half share of the purchase price. (Choice B) The daughter need only pay the cousin half of the amount he paid at the tax sale to retain her one-half interest in the farm. The farm's reasonable market value is irrelevant. (Choice C) The fact that the cousin survived the farmer would have entitled him to sole ownership of the farm had they been joint tenants. That is because a joint tenant's interest disappears upon death and the remaining tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it (ie, right of survivorship). But tenants in common have no such right. (Choice D) The daughter's default on tax obligations did not terminate her interest in the farm. Instead, her interest in the farm (or forfeiture thereof) depends on whether she pays the cousin her due contribution—half of the purchase price.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, if the daughter pays the cousin one-half of the amount he paid for the tax deed."},"questionZh":"一位奶农和他的表弟以共同佃户的形式拥有一座大农场，每人拥有不可分割的一半权益。五年来，农民占据农场并进行耕作。这位农民从未向他的表弟算过任何收入，但确实按时缴纳了所有房地产税，并为农场的建筑物投了保险，以防火灾、风暴和洪水造成的损失。表弟住在一个遥远的城市，他只关心当市场条件达到他想要的价格时从出售土地中获得利润。这名农民去世时没有留下遗嘱，他的女儿是他唯一的继承人。此后，女儿占领了农场，但缺乏农耕经验。结果是一场金融灾难。女儿已经两年没有缴纳房产税了。有关政府机构进行了税收出售以收回应缴税款。在拍卖会上，表弟是唯一的投标人，并在支付了足以清偿应缴税款、利息和罚款以及举行税务拍卖的费用后，从适当的政府当局获得了转让书。支付的金额是农场合理市场价值的三分之一。此后，表弟对女儿提起适当的诉讼，以平息所有权并恢复对农场的占有。女儿提出了她可以利用的所有辩护理由。除与房地产税和销售税相关的法规外，没有适用的法规。在这起诉讼中，表弟是否有权获得法令平静所有权，以便他成为农场的唯一所有者？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，如果女儿向表弟支付他缴纳税契金额的一半。","B":"不，如果女儿向表弟支付农场合理市场价值的一半。","C":"是的，因为表弟比农夫活了下来。","D":"是的，因为女儿没有履行农民承担的义务。"},"explanationZh":"通过税收出售或取消抵押品赎回权出售买回共同拥有的房产的租户，该房产将受到其他按份共有人（tenant in common）利益的影响。然后，不付费的按份共有人（tenant in common）有合理的时间贡献其购买价格的份额（由其比例所有权权益决定）。如果按份共有人（tenant in common）不这样做，他/她将把自己在房产中的权益转让给购买的按份共有人（tenant in common）。在这里，奶农和他的表弟作为按份共有人（tenant in common），各拥有农场一半的权益。农夫去世后，他的女儿获得了他一半的农场权益。她连续两年没有缴纳房地产税，并举行了一次税收拍卖。在那次拍卖中，表弟买回了农场。但如果女儿未能贡献出一半的购买价，他就只能成为农场的唯一所有者。 （选择B） 女儿只需向表弟支付他在售税时支付的一半金额即可保留她在农场的二分之一权益。农场的合理市场价值无关紧要。 （选项 C）表弟幸存下来，如果他们是联名佃农，那么他将有权获得农场的单独所有权。这是因为联权共有人的利益在死亡后消失，而其余共有人的利益自动扩大以吸收它（即生存权）。但共有租户没有这样的权利。 （选项D） 女儿拖欠纳税义务并没有终止她对农场的兴趣。相反，她对农场的兴趣（或没收）取决于她是否向表弟支付了应得的捐款——购买价格的一半。\n\n教育目标：通过税收或取消抵押品赎回权出售买回共同拥有的财产的租户，将根据其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的利益获得该财产。但其他按份共有人（tenant in common）必须在合理时间内按比例缴纳购买价格，以避免将其利益让给购买按份共有人（tenant in common）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"通过税收或取消抵押品赎回权出售买回共同拥有的房产的租户，该房产将受到其他按份共有人（tenant in common）利益的影响。但其他按份共有人（tenant in common）必须在合理时间内按比例缴纳购买价格，以避免将其利益让给购买按份共有人（tenant in common）。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这起诉讼中，表弟是否有权获得法令平静所有权，以便他成为农场的唯一所有者？","rule":"通过税收或取消抵押品赎回权出售买回共同拥有的房产的租户，该房产将受到其他按份共有人（tenant in common）利益的影响。但其他按份共有人（tenant in common）必须在合理时间内按比例缴纳购买价格，以避免将其利益让给购买按份共有人（tenant in common）。","analysis":"通过税收出售或取消抵押品赎回权出售买回共同拥有的房产的租户，该房产将受到其他按份共有人（tenant in common）利益的影响。然后，不付费的按份共有人（tenant in common）有合理的时间贡献其购买价格的份额（由其比例所有权权益决定）。如果按份共有人（tenant in common）不这样做，他/她将把自己在房产中的权益转让给购买的按份共有人（tenant in common）。在这里，奶农和他的表弟作为按份共有人（tenant in common），各拥有农场一半的权益。农夫去世后，他的女儿获得了他一半的农场权益。她连续两年没有缴纳房地产税，并举行了一次税收拍卖。在那次拍卖中，表弟买回了农场。但如果女儿未能贡献出一半的购买价，他就只能成为农场的唯一所有者。 （选择B） 女儿只需向表弟支付他在售税时支付的一半金额即可保留她在农场的二分之一权益。农场的合理市场价值无关紧要。 （选项 C）表弟幸存下来，如果他们是联名佃农，那么他将有权获得农场的单独所有权。这是因为联权共有人的利益在死亡后消失，而其余共有人的利益自动扩大以吸收它（即生存权）。但共有租户没有这样的权利。 （选项D） 女儿拖欠纳税义务并没有终止她对农场的兴趣。相反，她对农场的兴趣（或没收）取决于她是否向表弟支付了应得的捐款——购买价格的一半。\n\n教育目标：通过税收或取消抵押品赎回权出售买回共同拥有的财产的租户，将根据其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的利益获得该财产。但其他按份共有人（tenant in common）必须在合理时间内按比例缴纳购买价格，以避免将其利益让给购买按份共有人（tenant in common）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，如果女儿向表弟支付他缴纳税契金额的一半。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-dda7767246ab2fa6"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":99,"sourceQuestionPage":289,"sourceQuestionPages":[289],"sourceExplanationPages":[290],"sourceAnswerPages":[290],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-100","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Six years ago, the owner of an estate in fee simple executed and delivered to his nephew an instrument in the proper form of a warranty deed, purporting to convey his estate to \"my nephew and his heirs.\" At that time, the nephew was a widower who had one child, a daughter. Three years ago, the nephew executed and delivered to his neighbor an instrument in the proper form of a warranty deed, purporting to convey the estate to \"my neighbor.\" The nephew's daughter did not join in the deed. The neighbor was, and still is, unmarried and childless. The only possibly applicable statute in the jurisdiction states that any deed will be construed to convey the grantor's entire estate, unless expressly limited. Last month, the nephew died, never having remarried. His daughter is his only heir. Who is the owner of the estate?","choices":{"A":"The daughter and neighbor as joint tenants, because both survived the nephew.","B":"The daughter and neighbor as tenants in common of equal shares.","C":"The daughter, because the nephew's death ended the neighbor's life estate pur autre vie.","D":"The neighbor in fee simple pursuant to the nephew's deed."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Ownership in real property is transferred from a grantor (current owner) to a grantee (new owner) through a conveying instrument like a will or deed. A conveying instrument usually contains both: words of purchase – identifying the new owner (eg, \"to my nephew\") and words of limitation – identifying the type of estate being conveyed (eg, \"and his heirs\"). \"And his/her heirs\" are words of limitation traditionally used to transfer a fee simple absolute (FSA or fee simple)—the most common and broadest form of property ownership. However, these words of limitation are not required since an FSA is presumed by law when no such words are used. And since an FSA conveys title without any conditions, limitations, or accompanying future interests, no ownership interest is created in the grantee's heirs. Here, the owner of an FSA conveyed his interest to his nephew by warranty deed. Since the deed used the words of limitation \"and his heirs,\" it conveyed an FSA to the nephew (but created no ownership interest in the daughter/heir). The nephew then conveyed his interest to his neighbor by a warranty deed that lacked words of limitation. Therefore, the neighbor received an FSA and owns the estate without any conditions or limitations. (Choices A & B) Joint tenancy and tenancy in common are two types of concurrent estates. Concurrent estates are created only when a conveying instrument identifies two or more persons as grantees (not seen here). (Choice C) A life estate pur autre vie is an ownership interest that lasts for the duration of the life of someone other than the grantee. It is transferred when a conveying instrument states \"to A\" (words of purchase) \"for the life of B\" (words of limitation). Since the nephew's warranty deed to the neighbor contained no such language, the neighbor did not receive a life estate pur autre vie.\n\nEducational objective: A fee simple absolute is the broadest form of property ownership and is transferred when the conveying instrument's words of limitation state \"and his/her heirs\" OR when no words of limitation are used.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (First) of Property § 14 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (types of fee simple estates).\n\nRestatement (First) of Property § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (estate in fee simple absolute).\n\nRestatement (First) of Property § 39 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (presumption of fee simple absolute).","educationalObjective":"A fee simple absolute is the broadest form of property ownership and is transferred when the conveying instrument's words of limitation state \"and his/her heirs\" OR when no words of limitation are used.","references":"Restatement (First) of Property § 14 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (types of fee simple estates).\n\nRestatement (First) of Property § 15 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (estate in fee simple absolute).\n\nRestatement (First) of Property § 39 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (presumption of fee simple absolute).","rule":"A fee simple absolute is the broadest form of property ownership and is transferred when the conveying instrument's words of limitation state \"and his/her heirs\" OR when no words of limitation are used.","irac":{"issue":"Who is the owner of the estate?","rule":"A fee simple absolute is the broadest form of property ownership and is transferred when the conveying instrument's words of limitation state \"and his/her heirs\" OR when no words of limitation are used.","analysis":"Ownership in real property is transferred from a grantor (current owner) to a grantee (new owner) through a conveying instrument like a will or deed. A conveying instrument usually contains both: words of purchase – identifying the new owner (eg, \"to my nephew\") and words of limitation – identifying the type of estate being conveyed (eg, \"and his heirs\"). \"And his/her heirs\" are words of limitation traditionally used to transfer a fee simple absolute (FSA or fee simple)—the most common and broadest form of property ownership. However, these words of limitation are not required since an FSA is presumed by law when no such words are used. And since an FSA conveys title without any conditions, limitations, or accompanying future interests, no ownership interest is created in the grantee's heirs. Here, the owner of an FSA conveyed his interest to his nephew by warranty deed. Since the deed used the words of limitation \"and his heirs,\" it conveyed an FSA to the nephew (but created no ownership interest in the daughter/heir). The nephew then conveyed his interest to his neighbor by a warranty deed that lacked words of limitation. Therefore, the neighbor received an FSA and owns the estate without any conditions or limitations. (Choices A & B) Joint tenancy and tenancy in common are two types of concurrent estates. Concurrent estates are created only when a conveying instrument identifies two or more persons as grantees (not seen here). (Choice C) A life estate pur autre vie is an ownership interest that lasts for the duration of the life of someone other than the grantee. It is transferred when a conveying instrument states \"to A\" (words of purchase) \"for the life of B\" (words of limitation). Since the nephew's warranty deed to the neighbor contained no such language, the neighbor did not receive a life estate pur autre vie.","conclusion":"Answer D: The neighbor in fee simple pursuant to the nephew's deed."},"questionZh":"六年前，一处遗产的所有者以担保契约的适当形式签署了一份文书并将其交付给他的侄子，旨在将他的遗产转让给“我的侄子和他的继承人”。当时，侄子是一位鳏夫，有一个孩子，一个女儿。三年前，侄子以保修契约的适当形式签署并交付给他的邻居一份文书，旨在将遗产转让给“我的邻居”。侄子的女儿没有参与行动。这位邻居过去是，现在仍然是未婚且无子女的人。该司法管辖区唯一可能适用的法规规定，除非明确限制，否则任何契约都将被解释为转让设保人的全部财产。上个月，侄子去世了，从未再婚。他的女儿是他唯一的继承人。庄园的所有者是谁？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿和邻居成为联权共有人，因为侄子都幸存下来。","B":"女儿和邻居作为按份共有人（tenant in common），平分财产。","C":"女儿，因为侄子的死结束了邻居的终身财产。","D":"邻居根据侄子的行为简单收费。"},"explanationZh":"不动产的所有权通过遗嘱或契约等转让文书从授予人（当前所有者）转移到受让人（新所有者）。转让文书通常包含：购买字词 - 识别新所有者（例如，“给我的侄子”）和限制字词 - 识别所转让的遗产类型（例如，“及其继承人”）。 “及其继承人”是传统上用于转让简单绝对财产（FSA 或简单费用）的限制用语，这是最常见和最广泛的财产所有权形式。然而，这些限制性词语并不是必需的，因为当不使用此类词语时，法律就会推定 FSA。由于 FSA 转让所有权时没有任何条件、限制或附带的未来利益，因此不会在受让人的继承人中产生所有权权益。在这里，FSA 的所有者通过保修契约向他的侄子表达了他的兴趣。由于该契约使用了“及其继承人”的限制字样，因此它向侄子转让了 FSA（但没有在女儿/继承人中产生所有权权益）。然后，侄子通过一份缺乏限制字样的保修契约将他的利益转达给他的邻居。因此，邻居收到了 FSA 并无任何条件或限制地拥有该房产。 （选项 A 和 B） 联权共有和共有共有两种共有财产。仅当转让文书将两个或两个以上的人确定为受让人（此处未看到）时，才会创建并发遗产。 （选项 C） 终身财产是指在受赠人以外的其他人的一生中持续存在的所有权权益。当转让文书注明“至 A”（购买字词）“为 B 的生命周期”（限制字词）时，该转让即被转让。由于侄子给邻居的保修契约中没有包含此类语言，因此邻居没有获得 pur autre vie 的终身财产。\n\n教育目标：简单绝对产权是最广泛的财产所有权形式，当转让文书的限制字样注明“及其继承人”或未使用任何限制字样时，产权即被转让。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 14（Am. Law Inst. 1979）（收费简单遗产的类型）。\n\n财产重述（第一）§ 15（Am. Law Inst. 1979）（简单绝对遗产）。\n\n财产重述（第一）§ 39（Am. Law Inst. 1979）（绝对完全所有权（fee simple absolute）推定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"简单绝对产权是最广泛的财产所有权形式，当转让文书的限制字样注明“及其继承人”或未使用限制字样时，产权转让即被转让。","iracZh":{"issue":"庄园的所有者是谁？","rule":"简单绝对产权是最广泛的财产所有权形式，当转让文书的限制字样注明“及其继承人”或未使用限制字样时，产权转让即被转让。","analysis":"不动产的所有权通过遗嘱或契约等转让文书从授予人（当前所有者）转移到受让人（新所有者）。转让文书通常包含：购买字词 - 识别新所有者（例如，“给我的侄子”）和限制字词 - 识别所转让的遗产类型（例如，“及其继承人”）。 “及其继承人”是传统上用于转让简单绝对财产（FSA 或简单费用）的限制用语，这是最常见和最广泛的财产所有权形式。然而，这些限制性词语并不是必需的，因为当不使用此类词语时，法律就会推定 FSA。由于 FSA 转让所有权时没有任何条件、限制或附带的未来利益，因此不会在受让人的继承人中产生所有权权益。在这里，FSA 的所有者通过保修契约向他的侄子表达了他的兴趣。由于该契约使用了“及其继承人”的限制字样，因此它向侄子转让了 FSA（但没有在女儿/继承人中产生所有权权益）。然后，侄子通过一份缺乏限制字样的保修契约将他的利益转达给他的邻居。因此，邻居收到了 FSA 并无任何条件或限制地拥有该房产。 （选项 A 和 B） 联权共有和共有共有两种共有财产。仅当转让文书将两个或两个以上的人确定为受让人（此处未看到）时，才会创建并发遗产。 （选项 C） 终身财产是指在受赠人以外的其他人的一生中持续存在的所有权权益。当转让文书注明“至 A”（购买字词）“为 B 的生命周期”（限制字词）时，该转让即被转让。由于侄子给邻居的保修契约中没有包含此类语言，因此邻居没有获得 pur autre vie 的终身财产。\n\n教育目标：简单绝对产权是最广泛的财产所有权形式，当转让文书的限制字样注明“及其继承人”或未使用任何限制字样时，产权即被转让。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 14（Am. Law Inst. 1979）（收费简单遗产的类型）。\n\n财产重述（第一）§ 15（Am. Law Inst. 1979）（简单绝对遗产）。\n\n财产重述（第一）§ 39（Am. Law Inst. 1979）（绝对完全所有权（fee simple absolute）推定）。","conclusion":"答案 D：邻居根据侄子的行为简单收费。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-aa0127749b637f64"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":100,"sourceQuestionPage":291,"sourceQuestionPages":[291],"sourceExplanationPages":[292,293,294,295],"sourceAnswerPages":[292,293,294,295],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-101","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man owned a tract of land in fee simple. Five years ago, he executed and delivered to his sister an instrument in the proper form of a warranty deed that conveyed the land to the sister \"for and during the term of her natural life.\" No other estate or interest or person taking an interest was mentioned. The sister took possession of the land and has remained in possession. Fifteen months ago, the man died, leaving a will that has been duly admitted to probate. The will, inter alia, had the following provision: \"I devise the land to my wife for her natural life and from and after my wife's death to my son, his heirs and assigns, forever.\" Administration of the man's estate has been completed. The man's wife claims the immediate right to possession of the land. His son also asserts a right to immediate possession. In an appropriate lawsuit to which the sister, the wife, and the son are parties, who should be adjudged to have the right to immediate possession?","choices":{"A":"The sister, because no subsequent act of the man would affect her life estate.","B":"The son, because the sister's estate terminated with the man's death and all that the man had was the right to transfer his reversion in fee simple.","C":"The wife, because the man's will was the final and definitive expression of his intent.","D":"The wife, because the sister's estate terminated with the death of the man."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Types of life estates\n\nWords of Words of Defining characteristic purchase limitation\n\nLife \"To A\" \"for life\" Ownership for duration of life of grantee estate\n\nLife \"To A\" \"for the life of Ownership for duration of life of person estate B\" other than grantee\n\npur autre vie\n\nA life estate is a present possessory estate that terminates on the death of an individual. The future interest that follows a life estate is either a remainder (if created in a grantee) or a reversion (if retained by the grantor). If a grantor conveys a life estate without specifying who holds the future interest, then it is presumed that the grantor retains a reversion. This interest is fully transferable during the grantor's lifetime, devisable by will, and inheritable through intestate succession. Here, the man conveyed a life estate in the land to his sister that would terminate when she died. The conveyance mentioned no other person or interest, so it is presumed that the man retained a reversion entitling him to possession of the land once the sister died. Until that time, however, the most the man could convey was his reversionary interest. Therefore, the man could do nothing that would affect the sister's life estate, and she should be adjudged to have the right to immediate possession of the land (Choices B, C & D).\n\nEducational objective: If a grantor conveys a life estate without specifying who holds the future interest, the grantor presumptively retains a reversion.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 107 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing life estates).","educationalObjective":"If a grantor conveys a life estate without specifying who holds the future interest, the grantor presumptively retains a reversion.","references":"Restatement of Property § 107 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing life estates).","rule":"If a grantor conveys a life estate without specifying who holds the future interest, the grantor presumptively retains a reversion.","irac":{"issue":"In an appropriate lawsuit to which the sister, the wife, and the son are parties, who should be adjudged to have the right to immediate possession?","rule":"If a grantor conveys a life estate without specifying who holds the future interest, the grantor presumptively retains a reversion.","analysis":"Types of life estates\n\nWords of Words of Defining characteristic purchase limitation\n\nLife \"To A\" \"for life\" Ownership for duration of life of grantee estate\n\nLife \"To A\" \"for the life of Ownership for duration of life of person estate B\" other than grantee\n\npur autre vie\n\nA life estate is a present possessory estate that terminates on the death of an individual. The future interest that follows a life estate is either a remainder (if created in a grantee) or a reversion (if retained by the grantor). If a grantor conveys a life estate without specifying who holds the future interest, then it is presumed that the grantor retains a reversion. This interest is fully transferable during the grantor's lifetime, devisable by will, and inheritable through intestate succession. Here, the man conveyed a life estate in the land to his sister that would terminate when she died. The conveyance mentioned no other person or interest, so it is presumed that the man retained a reversion entitling him to possession of the land once the sister died. Until that time, however, the most the man could convey was his reversionary interest. Therefore, the man could do nothing that would affect the sister's life estate, and she should be adjudged to have the right to immediate possession of the land (Choices B, C & D).","conclusion":"Answer A: The sister, because no subsequent act of the man would affect her life estate."},"questionZh":"一个人简单地拥有一块土地。五年前，他以担保契约的适当形式签署了一份文书并将其交付给他的妹妹，该文书将土地“在她的自然寿命期内”转让给了她的妹妹。没有提及其他财产或利益或感兴趣的人。姐姐占有了这块土地，并且一直占有这块土地。十五个月前，该男子去世，留下一份已正式接受遗嘱认证的遗嘱。遗嘱除其他外，有以下条款：“我将土地分配给我的妻子，供她自然生活，并在我妻子去世后，分配给我的儿子，他的继承人和受让人，永远。”该男子的遗产管理工作已经完成。该男子的妻子声称拥有该土地的直接占有权。他的儿子还主张立即占有的权利。在姐妹、妻子和儿子作为当事人的适当诉讼中，谁应该被判定拥有立即占有权？","choicesZh":{"A":"妹妹，因为该男子的后续行为不会影响她的终身财产。","B":"儿子，因为姐姐的遗产随着该男子的死亡而终止，而该男子所拥有的只是简单地转让其归还财产的权利。","C":"妻子，因为男人的遗嘱是他意图的最终且明确的表达。","D":"妻子，因为姐姐的遗产随着男人的去世而终止。"},"explanationZh":"终身财产的类型\n\n定义特征购买限制的词语\n\nLife “To A” “for life” 受赠人遗产存续期间的所有权\n\n“对A”的生命周期“在所有权的生命周期内，在个人财产B的生命周期内”，受让人除外\n\n纯粹的生活\n\n终身财产是目前的占有财产，在个人死亡时终止。终身财产后的未来利息要么是剩余权益（如果由受让人创建），要么是归还利息（如果由授予人保留）。如果设保人转让终身财产而未指定谁持有未来权益，则假定设保人保留归还权。该权益在授予人的一生中可以完全转让，可以通过遗嘱设计，并且可以通过无遗嘱继承来继承。在这里，该男子将土地上的终身财产转让给了他的妹妹，该财产将在她去世时终止。该转让书没有提及其他人或利益，因此推测该男子保留了归还权，使他有权在姐姐去世后拥有该土地。然而，在那之前，这个人最多能表达的是他的回归兴趣。因此，男方不能做任何影响姐姐终身财产的事情，应判决她拥有立即占有土地的权利（选项B、C、D）。\n\n教育目标：如果授予人转让终身财产而未指定谁持有未来权益，则假定授予人保留归还权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 107（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论终身财产）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果设保人转让终身财产而未指定谁持有未来权益，则设保人推定保留归还权。","iracZh":{"issue":"在姐妹、妻子和儿子作为当事人的适当诉讼中，谁应该被判定拥有立即占有权？","rule":"如果设保人转让终身财产而未指定谁持有未来权益，则设保人推定保留归还权。","analysis":"终身财产的类型\n\n定义特征购买限制的词语\n\nLife “To A” “for life” 受赠人遗产存续期间的所有权\n\n“对A”的生命周期“在所有权的生命周期内，在个人财产B的生命周期内”，受让人除外\n\n纯粹的生活\n\n终身财产是目前的占有财产，在个人死亡时终止。终身财产后的未来利息要么是剩余权益（如果由受让人创建），要么是归还利息（如果由授予人保留）。如果设保人转让终身财产而未指定谁持有未来权益，则假定设保人保留归还权。该权益在授予人的一生中可以完全转让，可以通过遗嘱设计，并且可以通过无遗嘱继承来继承。在这里，该男子将土地上的终身财产转让给了他的妹妹，该财产将在她去世时终止。该转让书没有提及其他人或利益，因此推测该男子保留了归还权，使他有权在姐姐去世后拥有该土地。然而，在那之前，这个人最多能表达的是他的回归兴趣。因此，男方不能做任何影响姐姐终身财产的事情，应判决她拥有立即占有土地的权利（选项B、C、D）。\n\n教育目标：如果授予人转让终身财产而未指定谁持有未来权益，则假定授予人保留归还权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 107（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论终身财产）。","conclusion":"答案 A：妹妹，因为该男子的后续行为不会影响她的终身财产。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":101,"sourceQuestionPage":296,"sourceQuestionPages":[296],"sourceExplanationPages":[297],"sourceAnswerPages":[297],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-102","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A landlord and a tenant orally agreed to a commercial tenancy for a term of six months beginning on July 1. Rent was to be paid by the first day of each month, and the tenant paid the first month's rent at the time of the agreement. When the tenant arrived at the leased premises on July 1, he learned that the previous tenant had not vacated the premises at the end of her lease term on May 31 and did not intend to vacate. The tenant then successfully sued the previous tenant for possession. The tenant did not inform the landlord of the eviction action until after the tenant received possession. The tenant then sued the landlord, claiming damages for that portion of the lease period during which the tenant was not in possession. If the court finds for the landlord, what will be the most likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"By suing the previous tenant for possession, the tenant elected that remedy in lieu of a suit against the landlord.","B":"The landlord had delivered the legal right of possession to the tenant.","C":"The tenant failed to timely vacate as required to sue for constructive eviction.","D":"The tenant had not notified the landlord before bringing the eviction action."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Landlord's duty to deliver possession\n\nRequirements Remedies for breach\n\nEnglish Deliver actual possession to tenant—ie, remove Withhold rent to rule third party who wrongfully possesses property acquire possession Seek damages for period (majority) not in possession American Deliver legal possession to tenant—ie, ensure that Terminate lease rule no third party has right to possess property\n\n(minority)\n\nA landlord must generally deliver actual possession of leased premises to the tenant at the start of the lease term—ie, by removing any third party who wrongfully possesses the property (eg, a holdover tenant). If the landlord fails to do so, the tenant can recover damages from the landlord for the time when the tenant was not in possession. However, a minority of jurisdictions only require a landlord to deliver legal possession—ie, by ensuring that no third party has a right to possess the property when the lease term begins. Here, the landlord leased property to the tenant for six months beginning on July 1. When the tenant arrived at the leased premises on July 1, he learned that the previous tenant had not vacated the premises (ie, the landlord failed to deliver actual possession). And since the previous tenant's lease ended on May 31, she was a holdover tenant who had no right to possess the property on July 1. Therefore, the most likely reason for a finding in the landlord's favor is that this court follows the minority rule and the landlord had delivered legal possession to the tenant. (Choices A & D) A tenant does not waive any claim against the landlord by (1) suing a third party (eg, the previous tenant) for possession of the premises or (2) failing to notify the landlord before evicting the third party. As a result, the court will not find for the landlord on either basis. (Choice C) To sue a landlord for constructive eviction—a landlord's substantial interference with a tenant's permissible use and enjoyment of the property—the tenant must notify the landlord and then vacate the premises within a reasonable time. But a failure to vacate does not prevent a tenant from suing based on the landlord's failure to deliver possession, so the court likely would not find in the landlord's favor on this basis.\n\nEducational objective: Although a landlord must generally deliver actual possession of the leased premises to the tenant at the beginning of the lease term, a minority of jurisdictions only require the landlord to deliver legal possession.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 6.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (landlord's duty to deliver possession).","educationalObjective":"Although a landlord must generally deliver actual possession of the leased premises to the tenant at the beginning of the lease term, a minority of jurisdictions only require the landlord to deliver legal possession.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 6.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (landlord's duty to deliver possession).","rule":"Although a landlord must generally deliver actual possession of the leased premises to the tenant at the beginning of the lease term, a minority of jurisdictions only require the landlord to deliver legal possession.","irac":{"issue":"If the court finds for the landlord, what will be the most likely explanation?","rule":"Although a landlord must generally deliver actual possession of the leased premises to the tenant at the beginning of the lease term, a minority of jurisdictions only require the landlord to deliver legal possession.","analysis":"Landlord's duty to deliver possession\n\nRequirements Remedies for breach\n\nEnglish Deliver actual possession to tenant—ie, remove Withhold rent to rule third party who wrongfully possesses property acquire possession Seek damages for period (majority) not in possession American Deliver legal possession to tenant—ie, ensure that Terminate lease rule no third party has right to possess property\n\n(minority)\n\nA landlord must generally deliver actual possession of leased premises to the tenant at the start of the lease term—ie, by removing any third party who wrongfully possesses the property (eg, a holdover tenant). If the landlord fails to do so, the tenant can recover damages from the landlord for the time when the tenant was not in possession. However, a minority of jurisdictions only require a landlord to deliver legal possession—ie, by ensuring that no third party has a right to possess the property when the lease term begins. Here, the landlord leased property to the tenant for six months beginning on July 1. When the tenant arrived at the leased premises on July 1, he learned that the previous tenant had not vacated the premises (ie, the landlord failed to deliver actual possession). And since the previous tenant's lease ended on May 31, she was a holdover tenant who had no right to possess the property on July 1. Therefore, the most likely reason for a finding in the landlord's favor is that this court follows the minority rule and the landlord had delivered legal possession to the tenant. (Choices A & D) A tenant does not waive any claim against the landlord by (1) suing a third party (eg, the previous tenant) for possession of the premises or (2) failing to notify the landlord before evicting the third party. As a result, the court will not find for the landlord on either basis. (Choice C) To sue a landlord for constructive eviction—a landlord's substantial interference with a tenant's permissible use and enjoyment of the property—the tenant must notify the landlord and then vacate the premises within a reasonable time. But a failure to vacate does not prevent a tenant from suing based on the landlord's failure to deliver possession, so the court likely would not find in the landlord's favor on this basis.","conclusion":"Answer B: The landlord had delivered the legal right of possession to the tenant."},"questionZh":"房东和租客口头同意商业租赁，租期为6个月，从7月1日开始。租金应在每个月的第一天支付，租客在达成协议时支付第一个月的租金。当租客7月1日抵达租赁房屋时，得知前租客在5月31日租期结束时尚未搬出该房屋，也无意搬出。随后，该租客成功起诉前租客，要求占有该房屋。直到租客获得占有后，租客才将驱逐行动通知房东。租客随后起诉房东，要求赔偿租客未占有的租赁期内的损失。如果法院做出有利于房东的裁决，最可能的解释是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"通过起诉前任租户要求占有，租户选择了这种补救措施来代替对房东的诉讼。","B":"房东已将合法的占有权移交给租户。","C":"租户未能按要求及时搬出，起诉推定驱逐。","D":"租户在提出驱逐行动之前没有通知房东。"},"explanationZh":"房东交付财产的义务\n\n要求 违规补救措施\n\n英语 将实际占有权交付给租户，即删除 扣留租金，以规则错误占有财产的第三方获得占有 寻求对不占有财产的期间（大部分）的损害赔偿 美式 将合法占有权交付给租户，即确保终止租赁规则，任何第三方都无权占有财产\n\n（少数）\n\n房东通常必须在租赁期开始时将租赁房屋的实际占有权交付给租户，即通过移除任何错误占有该财产的第三方（例如，滞留租户）。如果房东不这样做，租客可以向房东追偿租客不占有期间的损失。然而，少数司法管辖区仅要求房东交付合法占有，即确保在租赁期开始时没有第三方有权占有该财产。本案中，房东从7月1日起将房产租赁给租户，为期六个月。当租户于7月1日抵达租赁房屋时，得知前任租户尚未腾出该房屋（即房东未交付实际占有）。由于前任租户的租约于5月31日结束，她是滞留租户，在7月1日无权占有该房产。因此，最有可能做出对房东有利的裁决的理由是，本法院遵循少数人规则，并且房东已将合法占有权移交给租户。 （选项 A 和 D）如果 (1) 因占有房屋而起诉第三方（例如前任租户）或 (2) 在驱逐第三方之前未通知房东，租客并不放弃对房东提出的任何索赔。因此，法院不会根据这两种理由为房东做出有利于房东的判决。 （选择 C）要起诉房东推定驱逐（房东严重干扰租户对房产的允许使用和享受），租户必须通知房东，然后在合理时间内搬出房屋。但未能搬出并不能阻止房客因房东未能交付占有权而提起诉讼，因此法院可能不会据此做出对房东有利的裁决。\n\n教育目标：虽然房东通常必须在租赁期开始时将租赁房屋的实际所有权交付给租户，但少数司法管辖区仅要求房东交付合法所有权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租客第 6.2 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（房东交付占有的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"尽管房东通常必须在租赁期开始时将租赁房屋的实际所有权交付给租户，但少数司法管辖区仅要求房东交付合法所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院做出有利于房东的裁决，最可能的解释是什么？","rule":"尽管房东通常必须在租赁期开始时将租赁房屋的实际所有权交付给租户，但少数司法管辖区仅要求房东交付合法所有权。","analysis":"房东交付财产的义务\n\n要求 违规补救措施\n\n英语 将实际占有权交付给租户，即删除 扣留租金，以规则错误占有财产的第三方获得占有 寻求对不占有财产的期间（大部分）的损害赔偿 美式 将合法占有权交付给租户，即确保终止租赁规则，任何第三方都无权占有财产\n\n（少数）\n\n房东通常必须在租赁期开始时将租赁房屋的实际占有权交付给租户，即通过移除任何错误占有该财产的第三方（例如，滞留租户）。如果房东不这样做，租客可以向房东追偿租客不占有期间的损失。然而，少数司法管辖区仅要求房东交付合法占有，即确保在租赁期开始时没有第三方有权占有该财产。本案中，房东从7月1日起将房产租赁给租户，为期六个月。当租户于7月1日抵达租赁房屋时，得知前任租户尚未腾出该房屋（即房东未交付实际占有）。由于前任租户的租约于5月31日结束，她是滞留租户，在7月1日无权占有该房产。因此，最有可能做出对房东有利的裁决的理由是，本法院遵循少数人规则，并且房东已将合法占有权移交给租户。 （选项 A 和 D）如果 (1) 因占有房屋而起诉第三方（例如前任租户）或 (2) 在驱逐第三方之前未通知房东，租客并不放弃对房东提出的任何索赔。因此，法院不会根据这两种理由为房东做出有利于房东的判决。 （选择 C）要起诉房东推定驱逐（房东严重干扰租户对房产的允许使用和享受），租户必须通知房东，然后在合理时间内搬出房屋。但未能搬出并不能阻止房客因房东未能交付占有权而提起诉讼，因此法院可能不会据此做出对房东有利的裁决。\n\n教育目标：虽然房东通常必须在租赁期开始时将租赁房屋的实际所有权交付给租户，但少数司法管辖区仅要求房东交付合法所有权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租客第 6.2 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（房东交付占有的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 B：房东已将合法的占有权移交给租户。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"constructive eviction","ipa":"/kʌnstɹˈəktɪv ɪvˈɪkʃən/","syllables":"con·struc·tive evic·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"推定驱逐：房东重大干扰使房屋无法使用且承租人及时搬离","contextualMeaning":"推定驱逐：房东重大干扰使房屋无法使用且承租人及时搬离","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The tenant failed to timely vacate as required to sue for constructive eviction.","synonyms":["de facto eviction"]},{"term":"actual possession","ipa":"/ˈæktʃəwəl pʌzˈɛʃən/","syllables":"ac·tual pos·ses·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"实际占有：以土地性质相适应的方式真实控制或使用土地","contextualMeaning":"实际占有：以土地性质相适应的方式真实控制或使用土地","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Although a landlord must generally deliver actual possession of the leased premises to the tenant at the beginning of the lease term, a minority of jurisdictions only require the landlord to deliver legal possession.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":102,"sourceQuestionPage":298,"sourceQuestionPages":[298],"sourceExplanationPages":[299,300],"sourceAnswerPages":[299,300],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-103","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A husband and wife acquired land as common law joint tenants with right of survivorship. One year later, without his wife's knowledge, the husband executed a will devising his interest in the land to his best friend. The husband subsequently died. Is the wife now the sole owner of the land?","choices":{"A":"No, because a joint tenant has the unilateral right to end a joint tenancy without the consent of the other joint tenant.","B":"No, because the wife's interest in the husband's undivided 50% ownership in the land adeemed.","C":"Yes, because of the doctrine of after-acquired title, or estoppel by deed.","D":"Yes, because the devise to the friend did not sever the joint tenancy."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A joint tenancy is a type of concurrent estate in which each co-tenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship. The right of survivorship means that a joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it. As a result, although a joint tenant can sever the joint tenancy by conveying his/her interest to another during life (thereby creating a tenancy in common), a joint tenant cannot devise his/her interest at death. Here, the husband and wife were joint tenants with the right of survivorship. Therefore, the husband could not sever the joint tenancy by devising his interest to his best friend. And since the husband and wife's joint tenancy remained intact, the wife absorbed his interest and became the sole owner of the land upon his death. (Choice A) A tenant can unilaterally end a joint tenancy without the other tenant's consent. But this only occurs when a joint tenancy interest is conveyed during a tenant's lifetime, since a joint tenancy interest cannot be devised upon death. (Choice B) Ademption is the failure of a gift under a will, often because the testator no longer owns that gift when he/she dies. Here, the best friend's interest in the land (not the wife's) adeemed because the husband's joint tenancy interest disappeared upon his death. Therefore, the husband had no interest to devise to his friend. (Choice C) The doctrine of after-acquired title (ie, estoppel by deed) applies when a testator acquires title to devised property after executing a will. In that case, the property passes under the will so long as the testator still owns the property when he/she dies. This doctrine does not apply here since the husband (testator) owned the land before he attempted to devise it to the best friend.\n\nEducational objective: A joint tenancy includes the right of survivorship, so a joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it. As a result, a joint tenancy interest cannot be devised.\n\nReferences\n20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 22 (2018) (severance of joint tenancy).","educationalObjective":"A joint tenancy includes the right of survivorship, so a joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it. As a result, a joint tenancy interest cannot be devised.","references":"20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 22 (2018) (severance of joint tenancy).","rule":"A joint tenancy includes the right of survivorship, so a joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it. As a result, a joint tenancy interest cannot be devised.","irac":{"issue":"Is the wife now the sole owner of the land?","rule":"A joint tenancy includes the right of survivorship, so a joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it. As a result, a joint tenancy interest cannot be devised.","analysis":"A joint tenancy is a type of concurrent estate in which each co-tenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship. The right of survivorship means that a joint tenant's interest disappears upon his/her death and the remaining joint tenants' interests automatically expand to absorb it. As a result, although a joint tenant can sever the joint tenancy by conveying his/her interest to another during life (thereby creating a tenancy in common), a joint tenant cannot devise his/her interest at death. Here, the husband and wife were joint tenants with the right of survivorship. Therefore, the husband could not sever the joint tenancy by devising his interest to his best friend. And since the husband and wife's joint tenancy remained intact, the wife absorbed his interest and became the sole owner of the land upon his death. (Choice A) A tenant can unilaterally end a joint tenancy without the other tenant's consent. But this only occurs when a joint tenancy interest is conveyed during a tenant's lifetime, since a joint tenancy interest cannot be devised upon death. (Choice B) Ademption is the failure of a gift under a will, often because the testator no longer owns that gift when he/she dies. Here, the best friend's interest in the land (not the wife's) adeemed because the husband's joint tenancy interest disappeared upon his death. Therefore, the husband had no interest to devise to his friend. (Choice C) The doctrine of after-acquired title (ie, estoppel by deed) applies when a testator acquires title to devised property after executing a will. In that case, the property passes under the will so long as the testator still owns the property when he/she dies. This doctrine does not apply here since the husband (testator) owned the land before he attempted to devise it to the best friend.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the devise to the friend did not sever the joint tenancy."},"questionZh":"一对丈夫和妻子以普通法共同共有者的身份获得了土地，并享有生存权。一年后，在妻子不知情的情况下，丈夫签署了一份遗嘱，向他最好的朋友规定了他对这片土地的兴趣。丈夫随后去世。妻子现在是土地的唯一所有者吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为联权共有人有权在未经其他联权共有人同意的情况下单方面终止联权共有权。","B":"不可以，因为妻子对丈夫拥有不可分割的 50% 土地所有权拥有权益。","C":"是的，因为事后获得所有权或契约禁止反言的原则。","D":"是的，因为给朋友的设计并没有切断联权共有权。"},"explanationZh":"联合租赁是一种共有财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益，并享有生存权。生存权是指联权共有人的权益在他/她去世后消失，剩余的联权共有人的权益自动扩大以吸收该权益。因此，虽然联权共有人可以通过在生前将其利益转让给他人（从而形成共同共有权）来终止联权共有权，但联权共有人不能在死亡时制定其利益。在这里，丈夫和妻子是拥有生存权的联权共有人。因此，丈夫不能通过将自己的利益转让给他最好的朋友来终止联权共有权。由于丈夫和妻子的共同共有权保持不变，妻子吸收了他的权益，并在他死后成为土地的唯一所有者。 （选择 A） 租户可以单方面终止联权租赁，无需其他租户同意。但这仅发生在租户生前转让联权租赁权益的情况下，因为联权租赁权益在租户死亡后无法制定。 （选择 B） 赎回是遗嘱下的礼物的失败，通常是因为立遗嘱人死后不再拥有该礼物。在这里，最好的朋友对土地的权益（而不是妻子的）被视为是因为丈夫的共同共有（joint tenancy）权益在他去世后消失了。因此，丈夫没有兴趣为他的朋友设计。 （选项C） 当立遗嘱人在立遗嘱后获得设计财产的所有权时，适用事后所有权原则（即禁止反悔）。在这种情况下，只要立遗嘱人去世时仍然拥有该财产，该财产就会根据遗嘱进行转移。这一原则在这里并不适用，因为丈夫（立遗嘱人）在试图将土地转让给最好的朋友之前就拥有土地。\n\n教育目标： 联权共有包括生存权，因此联权共有人的权益在其死亡后消失，而其余联权共有人的权益自动扩大以吸收其权益。因此，不能设计联合租赁权益。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 22 (2018)（联合租赁的分割）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"联权共有权包括生存权，因此联权共有人的权益在他/她去世后消失，其余联权共有人的权益自动扩大以吸收其权益。因此，不能设计联合租赁权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"妻子现在是土地的唯一所有者吗？","rule":"联权共有权包括生存权，因此联权共有人的权益在他/她去世后消失，其余联权共有人的权益自动扩大以吸收其权益。因此，不能设计联合租赁权益。","analysis":"联合租赁是一种共有财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益，并享有生存权。生存权是指联权共有人的权益在他/她去世后消失，剩余的联权共有人的权益自动扩大以吸收该权益。因此，虽然联权共有人可以通过在生前将其利益转让给他人（从而形成共同共有权）来终止联权共有权，但联权共有人不能在死亡时制定其利益。在这里，丈夫和妻子是拥有生存权的联权共有人。因此，丈夫不能通过将自己的利益转让给他最好的朋友来终止联权共有权。由于丈夫和妻子的共同共有权保持不变，妻子吸收了他的权益，并在他死后成为土地的唯一所有者。 （选择 A） 租户可以单方面终止联权租赁，无需其他租户同意。但这仅发生在租户生前转让联权租赁权益的情况下，因为联权租赁权益在租户死亡后无法制定。 （选择 B） 赎回是遗嘱下的礼物的失败，通常是因为立遗嘱人死后不再拥有该礼物。在这里，最好的朋友对土地的权益（而不是妻子的）被视为是因为丈夫的共同共有（joint tenancy）权益在他去世后消失了。因此，丈夫没有兴趣为他的朋友设计。 （选项C） 当立遗嘱人在立遗嘱后获得设计财产的所有权时，适用事后所有权原则（即禁止反悔）。在这种情况下，只要立遗嘱人去世时仍然拥有该财产，该财产就会根据遗嘱进行转移。这一原则在这里并不适用，因为丈夫（立遗嘱人）在试图将土地转让给最好的朋友之前就拥有土地。\n\n教育目标： 联权共有包括生存权，因此联权共有人的权益在其死亡后消失，而其余联权共有人的权益自动扩大以吸收其权益。因此，不能设计联合租赁权益。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 22 (2018)（联合租赁的分割）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为给朋友的设计并没有切断联权共有权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-4f1764cdf092e20d"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":103,"sourceQuestionPage":301,"sourceQuestionPages":[301],"sourceExplanationPages":[302,303],"sourceAnswerPages":[302,303],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-104","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A brother and sister owned a large tract of land in fee simple as joint tenants with rights of survivorship. While the sister was on an extended safari in Kenya, the brother learned that there were very valuable coal deposits within the land, but he made no attempt to inform the sister. Thereupon, the brother conveyed his interest in the land to his wife, who immediately reconveyed that interest back to him. The common law joint tenancy is unmodified by statute. Shortly thereafter, the brother was killed in an automobile accident. His will, which was duly probated, specifically devised his one-half interest in the land to his wife. The sister then returned from Kenya and learned what had happened. The sister brought an appropriate action against the wife, who claimed a one-half interest in the land, seeking a declaratory judgment that she, the sister, was the sole owner of the land. In this action, who should prevail?","choices":{"A":"The sister, because the brother breached his fiduciary duty as joint tenant.","B":"The sister, because the joint tenancy was reestablished by the wife's reconveyance to the brother.","C":"The wife, because the brother and sister were tenants in common at the time of the brother's death.","D":"The wife, because the brother's will severed the joint tenancy."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A joint tenancy is a type of concurrent estate in which each co-tenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship. Under the common law (and in most jurisdictions), the creation and continuation of a joint tenancy relies on the coexistence of four unities (PITT): Possession – co-tenants share an equal right to possess or use the property Interest – co-tenants each have an equal interest in the property Time – property interests simultaneously vest in all co-tenants Title – property interests received in same instrument of conveyance If any of these unities is lost, then the noncomplying interest is severed from the joint tenancy and becomes a tenancy in common. Here, a brother and sister owned land as joint tenants. When the brother conveyed his interest to his wife, the joint tenancy lost the unities of time (interests vested at different times) and title (interests received in different instruments). Therefore, the conveyed interest was severed from the joint tenancy and converted into a tenancy in common (Choice D). The wife's immediate reconveyance to the brother did not reestablish the joint tenancy since the unities of time and title were still lost (Choice B). Instead, the brother became tenants in common with his sister. And since the brother's interest was devised to his wife upon his death, the wife should prevail. (Choice A) A fiduciary duty is a legal obligation to act in another's best interest. Joint tenants owe no such duty to each other.\n\nEducational objective: Under the common law, a joint tenancy must satisfy four unities: possession, interest, time, and title (PITT). If any of these unities is broken (eg, when a joint tenant conveys his/her interest), then the noncomplying interest is severed from the joint tenancy and converted to a tenancy in common.\n\nReferences\n20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 5 (2020) (four unities of a joint tenancy).","educationalObjective":"Under the common law, a joint tenancy must satisfy four unities: possession, interest, time, and title (PITT). If any of these unities is broken (eg, when a joint tenant conveys his/her interest), then the noncomplying interest is severed from the joint tenancy and converted to a tenancy in common.","references":"20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 5 (2020) (four unities of a joint tenancy).","rule":"Under the common law, a joint tenancy must satisfy four unities: possession, interest, time, and title (PITT). If any of these unities is broken (eg, when a joint tenant conveys his/her interest), then the noncomplying interest is severed from the joint tenancy and converted to a tenancy in common.","irac":{"issue":"In this action, who should prevail?","rule":"Under the common law, a joint tenancy must satisfy four unities: possession, interest, time, and title (PITT). If any of these unities is broken (eg, when a joint tenant conveys his/her interest), then the noncomplying interest is severed from the joint tenancy and converted to a tenancy in common.","analysis":"A joint tenancy is a type of concurrent estate in which each co-tenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship. Under the common law (and in most jurisdictions), the creation and continuation of a joint tenancy relies on the coexistence of four unities (PITT): Possession – co-tenants share an equal right to possess or use the property Interest – co-tenants each have an equal interest in the property Time – property interests simultaneously vest in all co-tenants Title – property interests received in same instrument of conveyance If any of these unities is lost, then the noncomplying interest is severed from the joint tenancy and becomes a tenancy in common. Here, a brother and sister owned land as joint tenants. When the brother conveyed his interest to his wife, the joint tenancy lost the unities of time (interests vested at different times) and title (interests received in different instruments). Therefore, the conveyed interest was severed from the joint tenancy and converted into a tenancy in common (Choice D). The wife's immediate reconveyance to the brother did not reestablish the joint tenancy since the unities of time and title were still lost (Choice B). Instead, the brother became tenants in common with his sister. And since the brother's interest was devised to his wife upon his death, the wife should prevail. (Choice A) A fiduciary duty is a legal obligation to act in another's best interest. Joint tenants owe no such duty to each other.","conclusion":"Answer C: The wife, because the brother and sister were tenants in common at the time of the brother's death."},"questionZh":"一对兄妹拥有一大片土地，作为联权共有者，享有生存权。当姐姐在肯尼亚进行长途旅行时，哥哥得知这片土地上蕴藏着非常宝贵的煤炭资源，但他并没有试图告知姐姐。于是，兄弟将他对这片土地的兴趣转达给了他的妻子，妻子立即将这种兴趣转达给了他。普通法联权共有权不受法规的修改。此后不久，弟弟在一场车祸中丧生。他的遗嘱经过正式验证，专门将他在这片土地上的一半权益划给了他的妻子。后来姐姐从肯尼亚回来，才知道发生了什么事。姐姐对声称拥有该土地一半权益的妻子提起适当诉讼，寻求宣告性判决，确认她（妹妹）是该土地的唯一所有者。在这场行动中，谁应该占上风？","choicesZh":{"A":"姐姐，因为哥哥违反了作为联权共有人的信托义务。","B":"姐妹，因为妻子将财产重新转让给兄弟，从而重新建立了联权共有权。","C":"妻子，因为兄弟姐妹在兄弟去世时是共同房客。","D":"妻子因兄弟的遗嘱终止了联权共有。"},"explanationZh":"联合租赁是一种共有财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益，并享有生存权。根据普通法（以及在大多数司法管辖区），联权租赁的建立和延续依赖于四个单位的共存 (PITT)： 占有 – 按份共有人（tenant in common）享有占有或使用财产的平等权利 利益 – 按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有平等的权益 时间 – 财产权益同时归属于所有按份共有人（tenant in common） 所有权 – 在同一转让文书中获得的财产权益 如果这些单位中的任何一个丧失，则不符合规定的权益将从联名中分离出来租赁并成为共有租赁。在这里，一对兄妹作为联名租户拥有土地。当兄弟将其权益转让给妻子时，联权共有权就失去了时间（在不同时间归属的权益）和所有权（以不同文书获得的权益）的统一性。因此，所转让的权益与联合租赁分离并转换为共同共有（joint tenancy）（选择D）。妻子立即将财产转让给兄弟并没有重新建立联名共有权，因为时间和所有权的统一性仍然丢失（选择B）。相反，哥哥和妹妹成为了按份共有人（tenant in common）。由于兄弟的利益是在他死后归给他的妻子的，所以妻子应该占上风。 （选择 A） 信托义务是一种以他人最佳利益行事的法律义务。联名共有人彼此之间不承担此类义务。\n\n教育目标：根据普通法，联合共有必须满足四个统一条件：占有、利息、时间和所有权 (PITT)。如果这些统一体中的任何一个被破坏（例如，当联权共有人转让他/她的权益时），则不符合规定的权益将从联权共有权中分离出来并转换为共同共有权。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 5 (2020)（共同共有（joint tenancy）的四个单位）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据普通法，联合共有必须满足四个统一条件：占有权、利息、时间和所有权 (PITT)。如果这些统一体中的任何一个被破坏（例如，当联权共有人转让他/她的权益时），则不符合规定的权益将从联权共有权中分离出来并转换为共同共有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这场行动中，谁应该占上风？","rule":"根据普通法，联合共有必须满足四个统一条件：占有权、利息、时间和所有权 (PITT)。如果这些统一体中的任何一个被破坏（例如，当联权共有人转让他/她的权益时），则不符合规定的权益将从联权共有权中分离出来并转换为共同共有权。","analysis":"联合租赁是一种共有财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益，并享有生存权。根据普通法（以及在大多数司法管辖区），联权租赁的建立和延续依赖于四个单位的共存 (PITT)： 占有 – 按份共有人（tenant in common）享有占有或使用财产的平等权利 利益 – 按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有平等的权益 时间 – 财产权益同时归属于所有按份共有人（tenant in common） 所有权 – 在同一转让文书中获得的财产权益 如果这些单位中的任何一个丧失，则不符合规定的权益将从联名中分离出来租赁并成为共有租赁。在这里，一对兄妹作为联名租户拥有土地。当兄弟将其权益转让给妻子时，联权共有权就失去了时间（在不同时间归属的权益）和所有权（以不同文书获得的权益）的统一性。因此，所转让的权益与联合租赁分离并转换为共同共有（joint tenancy）（选择D）。妻子立即将财产转让给兄弟并没有重新建立联名共有权，因为时间和所有权的统一性仍然丢失（选择B）。相反，哥哥和妹妹成为了按份共有人（tenant in common）。由于兄弟的利益是在他死后归给他的妻子的，所以妻子应该占上风。 （选择 A） 信托义务是一种以他人最佳利益行事的法律义务。联名共有人彼此之间不承担此类义务。\n\n教育目标：根据普通法，联合共有必须满足四个统一条件：占有、利息、时间和所有权 (PITT)。如果这些统一体中的任何一个被破坏（例如，当联权共有人转让他/她的权益时），则不符合规定的权益将从联权共有权中分离出来并转换为共同共有权。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 5 (2020)（共同共有（joint tenancy）的四个单位）。","conclusion":"答案 C：妻子，因为兄弟姐妹在兄弟去世时是共同房客。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-1345083e450f2073"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":104,"sourceQuestionPage":304,"sourceQuestionPages":[304],"sourceExplanationPages":[305,306],"sourceAnswerPages":[305,306],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-105","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A brother and sister owned property as joint tenants. A two-family house was situated on the property. The brother lived in one of the two apartments and rented the other apartment to a friend. The brother got in a fight with the friend and injured him. The friend obtained and properly filed a judgment for $10,000 against the brother. The statute in the jurisdiction reads: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for ten years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" The sister, who lived in a distant city, knew nothing of the friend's judgment. Before the friend took any further action, the brother died. The common law joint tenancy is unmodified by statute. The sister then learned the facts and brought an appropriate action against the friend to quiet title to the property. Does the friend have an enforceable judgment lien on the property?","choices":{"A":"No, because the brother's death terminated the interest to which the friend's lien attached.","B":"No, because the sister had no actual notice of the friend's judgment until after the brother's death.","C":"Yes, because the friend was a tenant of both the brother and sister at the time of the judgment.","D":"Yes, but only against the brother's undivided one-half interest in the property, because the friend's judgment was filed prior to the brother's death."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A joint tenancy is a type of concurrent estate in which each tenant has an equal and undivided interest in the property with the right of survivorship. Under the common law (and in most jurisdictions), the creation and continuation of a joint tenancy depends on the coexistence of four unities (PITT): Possession – tenants share an equal right to possess or use the property Interest – tenants each have an equal interest in the property Time – property interests simultaneously vest in all tenants Title – property interests received in the same instrument of conveyance A judgment lien on a joint tenant's interest does not disrupt the four unities because it merely secures payment of a money judgment (ie, the tenancy remains intact). But a forced sale of the encumbered property (eg, foreclosure) does sever the tenancy—and creates a tenancy in common—since the sale disrupts the unities of time and title. However, a joint tenant's property interest disappears upon his/her death and is absorbed by the surviving tenants due to the right of survivorship. Therefore, a judgment lien on the deceased tenant's property interest also disappears (as seen here). (Choice B) The sister's notice of the friend's judgment has no bearing on the enforceability of his judgment lien on the brother's property interest. (Choice C) Although the friend rented the apartment from the brother, he was a tenant of both siblings. But his judgment lien only encumbered the brother's property interest and disappeared upon the brother's death. (Choice D) To create an enforceable judgment lien on a debtor's property, the judgment must be filed while the debtor owns that property. Therefore, the friend obtained an enforceable lien on the brother's one-half interest by filing the judgment before the brother's death. But the lien disappeared when the brother died.\n\nEducational objective: Under a joint tenancy, a deceased tenant's property interest disappears and is absorbed by the surviving tenants due to the right of survivorship. As a result, a judgment lien on the deceased tenant's interest also disappears.","educationalObjective":"Under a joint tenancy, a deceased tenant's property interest disappears and is absorbed by the surviving tenants due to the right of survivorship. As a result, a judgment lien on the deceased tenant's interest also disappears.","references":"","rule":"Under a joint tenancy, a deceased tenant's property interest disappears and is absorbed by the surviving tenants due to the right of survivorship. As a result, a judgment lien on the deceased tenant's interest also disappears.","irac":{"issue":"Does the friend have an enforceable judgment lien on the property?","rule":"Under a joint tenancy, a deceased tenant's property interest disappears and is absorbed by the surviving tenants due to the right of survivorship. As a result, a judgment lien on the deceased tenant's interest also disappears.","analysis":"A joint tenancy is a type of concurrent estate in which each tenant has an equal and undivided interest in the property with the right of survivorship. Under the common law (and in most jurisdictions), the creation and continuation of a joint tenancy depends on the coexistence of four unities (PITT): Possession – tenants share an equal right to possess or use the property Interest – tenants each have an equal interest in the property Time – property interests simultaneously vest in all tenants Title – property interests received in the same instrument of conveyance A judgment lien on a joint tenant's interest does not disrupt the four unities because it merely secures payment of a money judgment (ie, the tenancy remains intact). But a forced sale of the encumbered property (eg, foreclosure) does sever the tenancy—and creates a tenancy in common—since the sale disrupts the unities of time and title. However, a joint tenant's property interest disappears upon his/her death and is absorbed by the surviving tenants due to the right of survivorship. Therefore, a judgment lien on the deceased tenant's property interest also disappears (as seen here). (Choice B) The sister's notice of the friend's judgment has no bearing on the enforceability of his judgment lien on the brother's property interest. (Choice C) Although the friend rented the apartment from the brother, he was a tenant of both siblings. But his judgment lien only encumbered the brother's property interest and disappeared upon the brother's death. (Choice D) To create an enforceable judgment lien on a debtor's property, the judgment must be filed while the debtor owns that property. Therefore, the friend obtained an enforceable lien on the brother's one-half interest by filing the judgment before the brother's death. But the lien disappeared when the brother died.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the brother's death terminated the interest to which the friend's lien attached."},"questionZh":"一对兄妹作为联权共有人拥有房产。该地产上有一栋两户住宅。弟弟住在两套公寓中的一套，另一套租给了朋友。弟弟与朋友打架，把他打伤了。朋友获得并正确提交了针对兄弟赔偿 10,000 美元的判决。该司法管辖区的法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起十年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”住在遥远城市的姐姐对朋友的判断一无所知。在朋友采取进一步行动之前，兄弟就去世了。普通法联权共有权不受法规的修改。姐姐随后了解了事实，并对朋友提起适当的诉讼，以平息该财产的所有权。朋友对该财产是否拥有可执行的判决留置权？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为兄弟的去世终止了朋友留置权所附的权益。","B":"不，因为直到哥哥去世后，姐姐才真正注意到朋友的判断。","C":"是的，因为在判决时，这位朋友是这对兄妹的房客。","D":"可以，但仅限于兄弟在财产中不可分割的二分之一权益，因为朋友的判决是在兄弟去世之前提交的。"},"explanationZh":"联合租赁是一种共同财产，其中每个租户对财产拥有平等且不可分割的权益，并享有生存权。根据普通法（以及在大多数司法管辖区），联权共有权的建立和延续取决于四个统一体的共存 (PITT)： 占有 – 租户享有占有或使用财产的平等权利 利息 – 每个租户对财产拥有平等的权益 时间 – 财产权益同时归属于所有租户 所有权 – 通过同一转让文书获得的财产权益 对联权共有人权益的判决留置权不会破坏四个统一体，因为它只是确保付款判决（即租赁保持不变）。但强制出售担保财产（例如取消抵押品赎回权）确实会切断租赁关系，并产生共同共有（joint tenancy）关系，因为出售破坏了时间和所有权的统一。然而，联权共有人的财产权益在他/她去世后消失，并根据生存权由幸存的共有人吸收。因此，对已故租户财产权益的判决留置权也消失了（如此处所示）。 （选择B） 姐妹对朋友判决的通知与他对兄弟财产权益的判决留置权的可执行性没有影响。 （选项C）虽然朋友从兄弟那里租了公寓，但他是兄弟姐妹的租户。但他的判决留置权仅妨碍了兄弟的财产权益，并在兄弟去世后消失了。 （选择 D）要对债务人的财产设立可执行的判决留置权，判决必须在债务人拥有该财产时提交。因此，朋友通过在兄弟去世前提交判决，获得了对兄弟一半权益的可执行留置权。但当兄弟去世后，留置权就消失了。\n\n教育目标：在联权共有下，已故租户的财产权益消失，并因生存权而被幸存租户吸收。因此，对已故租户利益的判决留置权也消失了。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在联权共有下，已故租户的财产权益消失，并因生存权而由幸存租户吸收。因此，对已故租户利益的判决留置权也消失了。","iracZh":{"issue":"朋友对该财产是否拥有可执行的判决留置权？","rule":"在联权共有下，已故租户的财产权益消失，并因生存权而由幸存租户吸收。因此，对已故租户利益的判决留置权也消失了。","analysis":"联合租赁是一种共同财产，其中每个租户对财产拥有平等且不可分割的权益，并享有生存权。根据普通法（以及在大多数司法管辖区），联权共有权的建立和延续取决于四个统一体的共存 (PITT)： 占有 – 租户享有占有或使用财产的平等权利 利息 – 每个租户对财产拥有平等的权益 时间 – 财产权益同时归属于所有租户 所有权 – 通过同一转让文书获得的财产权益 对联权共有人权益的判决留置权不会破坏四个统一体，因为它只是确保付款判决（即租赁保持不变）。但强制出售担保财产（例如取消抵押品赎回权）确实会切断租赁关系，并产生共同共有（joint tenancy）关系，因为出售破坏了时间和所有权的统一。然而，联权共有人的财产权益在他/她去世后消失，并根据生存权由幸存的共有人吸收。因此，对已故租户财产权益的判决留置权也消失了（如此处所示）。 （选择B） 姐妹对朋友判决的通知与他对兄弟财产权益的判决留置权的可执行性没有影响。 （选项C）虽然朋友从兄弟那里租了公寓，但他是兄弟姐妹的租户。但他的判决留置权仅妨碍了兄弟的财产权益，并在兄弟去世后消失了。 （选择 D）要对债务人的财产设立可执行的判决留置权，判决必须在债务人拥有该财产时提交。因此，朋友通过在兄弟去世前提交判决，获得了对兄弟一半权益的可执行留置权。但当兄弟去世后，留置权就消失了。\n\n教育目标：在联权共有下，已故租户的财产权益消失，并因生存权而被幸存租户吸收。因此，对已故租户利益的判决留置权也消失了。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为兄弟的去世终止了朋友留置权所附的权益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-cde3ec8cb59c1d49"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":105,"sourceQuestionPage":307,"sourceQuestionPages":[307],"sourceExplanationPages":[308,309],"sourceAnswerPages":[308,309],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-106","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man owned adjoining lots (Lot 1 and Lot 2) in fee simple. He built a house in which he took up residence on Lot 1. Thereafter, he built a house on Lot 2, which he sold, house and lot, to his brother. Consistent with the contract of sale and purchase, a valid deed conveying Lot 2 from the man to the brother contained the following clause: \"In the event my brother, his heirs or assigns, decide to sell the property hereby conveyed and obtain a purchaser ready, willing, and able to purchase Lot 2 and the improvements thereon on terms and conditions acceptable to my brother, said Lot 2 and improvements shall be offered to me, my heirs or assigns, on the same terms and conditions. I, my heirs or assigns, as the case may be, shall have ten days from said offer to accept said offer and thereby to exercise said option.\" Three years after delivery and recording of the deed and payment of the purchase price, the brother became ill and moved to a climate more compatible with his health. The brother's daughter orally offered to purchase the premises from him at its then fair market value. The brother declined his daughter's offer but instead deeded Lot 2 to his daughter as a gift. Immediately thereafter, the daughter sold Lot 2 to her friend at the then fair market value of Lot 2. The sale was completed by the delivery of deed and payment of the purchase price. At no time did the brother or his daughter offer to sell Lot 2 to the man. The man learned of the conveyance to the daughter and the sale to the friend one week after the conveyance of Lot 2 from the daughter to the friend. The man promptly brought an appropriate action against the friend to enforce rights created in him by the deed of the man to the brother. The man tendered the amount paid by the friend into the court for whatever disposition the court deemed proper. The common-law Rule Against Perpetuities is unmodified by statute. Which of the following will determine whether the man will prevail?","choices":{"A":"The parol evidence rule only.","B":"The parol evidence rule and the Rule Against Perpetuities.","C":"The Rule Against Perpetuities only.","D":"The statute of frauds and the type of recording statute of the jurisdiction in question."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) applies only to contingent future interests—ie, future interests that are held by unknown/unborn persons or subject to a condition precedent. A right of first refusal is a contractual right to purchase property before any other person if the owner later decides to sell, so it must comply with RAP. Under the common-law RAP, a contingent future interest is void ab initio (ie, from the beginning) if there is any possibility that it could vest more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest. Therefore, a right of first refusal almost always violates RAP if the conveying instrument does not provide a termination date* that falls within the perpetuities period. Here, there is no termination date for the man's right of first refusal. As a result, it is possible that the right of first refusal may not vest until after the perpetuities period—ie, more than 21 years after the man and brother have died. Since this RAP violation renders the man's right of first refusal invalid, RAP determines whether he will prevail in this action. *A termination date can be set by a temporal limitation (eg, calendar date) or by reference to a life in being (eg, within 21 years of grantee's death). (Choices A & B) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior agreement to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement. But here, the man is seeking to enforce not a prior agreement but the purchase option specified in the deed. (Choice D) The statute of frauds renders certain contracts, including contracts for the sale of land, unenforceable unless they are in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. Additionally, recording statutes protect earlier-recorded property interests against subsequent purchasers. But neither will protect the man's contingent future interest from being invalidated by RAP.\n\nEducational objective: The Rule Against Perpetuities applies to contingent future interests, including a right of first refusal. This contractual right violates RAP if it is not set to terminate within 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.\n\nReferences\n61 Am. Jur. 2d Perpetuities and Restraints on Alienation § 5 (2020) (Rule Against Perpetuities).","educationalObjective":"The Rule Against Perpetuities applies to contingent future interests, including a right of first refusal. This contractual right violates RAP if it is not set to terminate within 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.","references":"61 Am. Jur. 2d Perpetuities and Restraints on Alienation § 5 (2020) (Rule Against Perpetuities).","rule":"The Rule Against Perpetuities applies to contingent future interests, including a right of first refusal. This contractual right violates RAP if it is not set to terminate within 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following will determine whether the man will prevail?","rule":"The Rule Against Perpetuities applies to contingent future interests, including a right of first refusal. This contractual right violates RAP if it is not set to terminate within 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.","analysis":"The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) applies only to contingent future interests—ie, future interests that are held by unknown/unborn persons or subject to a condition precedent. A right of first refusal is a contractual right to purchase property before any other person if the owner later decides to sell, so it must comply with RAP. Under the common-law RAP, a contingent future interest is void ab initio (ie, from the beginning) if there is any possibility that it could vest more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest. Therefore, a right of first refusal almost always violates RAP if the conveying instrument does not provide a termination date* that falls within the perpetuities period. Here, there is no termination date for the man's right of first refusal. As a result, it is possible that the right of first refusal may not vest until after the perpetuities period—ie, more than 21 years after the man and brother have died. Since this RAP violation renders the man's right of first refusal invalid, RAP determines whether he will prevail in this action. *A termination date can be set by a temporal limitation (eg, calendar date) or by reference to a life in being (eg, within 21 years of grantee's death). (Choices A & B) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior agreement to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement. But here, the man is seeking to enforce not a prior agreement but the purchase option specified in the deed. (Choice D) The statute of frauds renders certain contracts, including contracts for the sale of land, unenforceable unless they are in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. Additionally, recording statutes protect earlier-recorded property interests against subsequent purchasers. But neither will protect the man's contingent future interest from being invalidated by RAP.","conclusion":"Answer C: The Rule Against Perpetuities only."},"questionZh":"一名男子以低廉的价格拥有相邻的地块（地块 1 和地块 2）。他在 1 号地块上建造了一所房子并居住。此后，他在 2 号地块上建造了一座房子，并将其连同房屋和地块一起卖给了他的兄弟。根据买卖合同，将第 2 号地块从该男子转让给兄弟的有效契约包含以下条款：“如果我的兄弟、他的继承人或受让人决定出售特此转让的财产，并让购买者准备好、愿意并能够按照我兄弟可接受的条款和条件购买第 2 号地块及其改进部分，则应按照相同的条款和条件向我、我的继承人或受让人提供第 2 号地块及其改进部分。我，我的继承人或受让人（视情况而定）应在上述要约后十天内接受上述要约，从而行使上述选择权。”在交付并记录契约和支付购买价格三年后，兄弟生病了，并搬到了一个更适合他健康的气候。兄弟的女儿口头提出以当时的公平市场价值向他购买房产。兄弟拒绝了女儿的提议，而是将第 2 号地块作为礼物送给了女儿。此后，女儿立即以当时 2 号地块的公平市场价值将 2 号地块出售给她的朋友。通过交付契据并支付购买价款完成了销售。兄弟或他的女儿从未提出将 2 号地段出售给该男子。该男子在女儿将第 2 号拍品转让给朋友一周后才得知将其转让给女儿并出售给朋友。该男子立即对这位朋友提起适当的诉讼，以强制执行该男子对兄弟的行为所赋予他的权利。该男子将朋友支付的金额提交给法庭，以进行法庭认为适当的处置。普通法中的“反对永续规则”未经成文法修改。以下哪一项将决定这个人是否会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"仅口头证据规则。","B":"口头证据规则和反对永久规则。","C":"仅反对永续规则。","D":"有关司法管辖区的欺诈法规和登记法（recording act）类型。"},"explanationZh":"反对永续规则 (RAP) 仅适用于或有未来权益，即由未知/未出生的人持有或受先决条件约束的未来权益。优先购买权是一种合同权利，如果业主后来决定出售，则可以在任何其他人之前购买财产，因此必须遵守 RAP。根据普通法RAP，如果或有未来权益有可能在权益产生时的相关寿命超过21年后归属，则该权益从一开始（即从一开始）就无效。因此，如果转让文书未提供位于永久期限内的终止日期*，则优先购买权几乎总是违反 RAP。在此，男方的优先购买权没有终止日期。因此，优先购买权可能要等到永久期限之后（即该男子和兄弟去世超过 21 年之后）才能授予。由于这种 RAP 违规行为使得该男子的优先拒绝权无效，因此 RAP 决定他是否会在这次诉讼中获胜。 *终止日期可以通过时间限制（例如，日历日期）或参考生命（例如，受赠者死亡后 21 年内）来设定。 （选项 A 和 B）口头证据规则通常禁止提供先前同意修改或反驳最终书面协议条款的证据。但在这里，该男子寻求执行的不是事先的协议，而是契约中指定的购买选择权。 （选项 D）欺诈法规定某些合同（包括土地销售合同）不可执行，除非这些合同采用书面形式并由被执行方签署。此外，登记法（recording act）保护先前记录的财产权益免受后续购买者的侵害。但两者都不能保护该人的或有未来利益不被 RAP 宣告无效。\n\n教育目标：反对永久规则适用于或有的未来利益，包括优先购买权。如果该合同权利未在权益创建时的相关寿命后 21 年内终止，则该合同权利违反了 RAP。\n\n参考文献\n上午 61 点。尤尔。 2d 永久权和转让限制§ 5 (2020)（反对永久权的规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"反对永续规则适用于或有未来利益，包括优先购买权。如果该合同权利未在权益创建时的相关寿命后 21 年内终止，则该合同权利违反了 RAP。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项将决定这个人是否会获胜？","rule":"反对永续规则适用于或有未来利益，包括优先购买权。如果该合同权利未在权益创建时的相关寿命后 21 年内终止，则该合同权利违反了 RAP。","analysis":"反对永续规则 (RAP) 仅适用于或有未来权益，即由未知/未出生的人持有或受先决条件约束的未来权益。优先购买权是一种合同权利，如果业主后来决定出售，则可以在任何其他人之前购买财产，因此必须遵守 RAP。根据普通法RAP，如果或有未来权益有可能在权益产生时的相关寿命超过21年后归属，则该权益从一开始（即从一开始）就无效。因此，如果转让文书未提供位于永久期限内的终止日期*，则优先购买权几乎总是违反 RAP。在此，男方的优先购买权没有终止日期。因此，优先购买权可能要等到永久期限之后（即该男子和兄弟去世超过 21 年之后）才能授予。由于这种 RAP 违规行为使得该男子的优先拒绝权无效，因此 RAP 决定他是否会在这次诉讼中获胜。 *终止日期可以通过时间限制（例如，日历日期）或参考生命（例如，受赠者死亡后 21 年内）来设定。 （选项 A 和 B）口头证据规则通常禁止提供先前同意修改或反驳最终书面协议条款的证据。但在这里，该男子寻求执行的不是事先的协议，而是契约中指定的购买选择权。 （选项 D）欺诈法规定某些合同（包括土地销售合同）不可执行，除非这些合同采用书面形式并由被执行方签署。此外，登记法（recording act）保护先前记录的财产权益免受后续购买者的侵害。但两者都不能保护该人的或有未来利益不被 RAP 宣告无效。\n\n教育目标：反对永久规则适用于或有的未来利益，包括优先购买权。如果该合同权利未在权益创建时的相关寿命后 21 年内终止，则该合同权利违反了 RAP。\n\n参考文献\n上午 61 点。尤尔。 2d 永久权和转让限制§ 5 (2020)（反对永久权的规则）。","conclusion":"答案 C：仅反对永续规则。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-d8b11b789c24ad2e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":106,"sourceQuestionPage":310,"sourceQuestionPages":[310],"sourceExplanationPages":[311,312],"sourceAnswerPages":[311,312],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-107","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"By warranty deed, a woman conveyed her property to her two closest friends \"as joint tenants with right of survivorship.\" The two friends are not related. The first friend conveyed all her interest to her son by warranty deed and subsequently died intestate. Thereafter, the second friend conveyed all her interest to her husband by warranty deed. There is no applicable statute, and the jurisdiction recognizes the common law joint tenancy. Who owns title to the property?","choices":{"A":"The husband.","B":"The husband and the heirs of the first friend.","C":"The husband and the son.","D":"The woman."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Concurrent estates\n\n(property shared by two or more persons)\n\nType Attributes Transferability\n\nTenancy in Separate & possibly unequal shares Alienable during life common of property Devisable Equal right to possess entire Inheritable property No right of survivorship Joint tenancy Undivided & equal shares of Alienable during life (but severs property* tenancy) Equal right to possess entire property Right of survivorship Tenancy by Joint tenancy between married Alienable with spouse's consent entirety persons\n\n*Some jurisdictions permit joint tenants to own unequal interests.\n\nA joint tenancy is a type of concurrent estate in which each co-tenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship. A lifetime transfer of a joint tenant's interest severs that interest from the joint tenancy relationship, and it becomes a tenancy-in-common ownership interest. Here, the two friends owned the property as joint tenants with the right of survivorship. The first friend conveyed her property interest to her son during her lifetime. This converted the joint tenancy into a tenancy in common between the son and the second friend. The second friend then conveyed her property interest (tenancy in common) to her husband. Therefore, the husband and the son own title as tenants in common (Choices A & D). (Choice B) Since the first friend conveyed her property interest to her son, her heirs had no property interest to inherit upon her death. But even if that conveyance had not been made (ie, had the joint tenancy continued), the first friend's property interest would have gone to the second friend—the surviving co-tenant. And the second friend's subsequent conveyance to her husband would have given him exclusive ownership of the property.\n\nEducational objective: A lifetime transfer of a joint tenant's interest severs that interest from the joint tenancy relationship, and it becomes a tenancy-in-common ownership interest.\n\nReferences\n20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 22 (2018) (severance of joint tenancy).","educationalObjective":"A lifetime transfer of a joint tenant's interest severs that interest from the joint tenancy relationship, and it becomes a tenancy-in-common ownership interest.","references":"20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 22 (2018) (severance of joint tenancy).","rule":"A lifetime transfer of a joint tenant's interest severs that interest from the joint tenancy relationship, and it becomes a tenancy-in-common ownership interest.","irac":{"issue":"Who owns title to the property?","rule":"A lifetime transfer of a joint tenant's interest severs that interest from the joint tenancy relationship, and it becomes a tenancy-in-common ownership interest.","analysis":"Concurrent estates\n\n(property shared by two or more persons)\n\nType Attributes Transferability\n\nTenancy in Separate & possibly unequal shares Alienable during life common of property Devisable Equal right to possess entire Inheritable property No right of survivorship Joint tenancy Undivided & equal shares of Alienable during life (but severs property* tenancy) Equal right to possess entire property Right of survivorship Tenancy by Joint tenancy between married Alienable with spouse's consent entirety persons\n\n*Some jurisdictions permit joint tenants to own unequal interests.\n\nA joint tenancy is a type of concurrent estate in which each co-tenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship. A lifetime transfer of a joint tenant's interest severs that interest from the joint tenancy relationship, and it becomes a tenancy-in-common ownership interest. Here, the two friends owned the property as joint tenants with the right of survivorship. The first friend conveyed her property interest to her son during her lifetime. This converted the joint tenancy into a tenancy in common between the son and the second friend. The second friend then conveyed her property interest (tenancy in common) to her husband. Therefore, the husband and the son own title as tenants in common (Choices A & D). (Choice B) Since the first friend conveyed her property interest to her son, her heirs had no property interest to inherit upon her death. But even if that conveyance had not been made (ie, had the joint tenancy continued), the first friend's property interest would have gone to the second friend—the surviving co-tenant. And the second friend's subsequent conveyance to her husband would have given him exclusive ownership of the property.","conclusion":"Answer C: The husband and the son."},"questionZh":"根据担保契约，一名妇女将她的财产转让给她的两个最亲密的朋友，“作为具有生存权的联权共有人”。这两个朋友没有血缘关系。第一个朋友通过担保契约将她所有的权益转嫁给了她的儿子，随后去世时没有留下遗嘱。此后，第二位朋友通过担保契据将她所有的权益转交给她的丈夫。没有适用的法规，司法管辖区承认普通法联权共有。谁拥有该财产的所有权？","choicesZh":{"A":"丈夫。","B":"第一个朋友的丈夫和继承人。","C":"丈夫和儿子。","D":"那个女人。"},"explanationZh":"并发地产\n\n（两人或多人共有的财产）\n\n类型属性可转移性\n\n分权和可能不等份的租赁 在生前可转让 共有财产 可转让 拥有全部财产的平等权利 可继承财产 无生存权 联名共有 生前不可分割且均等份额 可转让（但分割财产*租赁） 拥有全部财产的平等权利 生存权 已婚者之间的联名共有租赁 可在配偶同意的情况下全体转让\n\n*某些司法管辖区允许联权共有人拥有不平等的权益。\n\n联合租赁是一种共有财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益，并享有生存权。联权共有人的权益的终身转让会将该权益与联权租赁关系分离，并成为共同共有（joint tenancy）所有权权益。在这里，两个朋友作为联权共有人拥有该房产，并享有生存权。第一个朋友在生前将她的财产权益转让给了她的儿子。这将联权共有权转变为儿子和第二个朋友之间的共同共有权。然后，第二个朋友将她的财产权益（共同共有（joint tenancy））转让给她的丈夫。因此，丈夫和儿子拥有按份共有人（tenant in common）的所有权（选择 A 和 D）。 （选项B） 由于第一个朋友将她的财产权益转让给了她的儿子，她的继承人在她去世后没有财产权益可以继承。但即使没有进行转让（即，如果联名共有权继续存在），第一个朋友的财产权益也会归第二个朋友——幸存的按份共有人（tenant in common）所有。第二个朋友随后将财产转让给她的丈夫，这将使他获得该财产的独家所有权。\n\n教育目标： 联权共有人的权益的终身转让将该权益与联权共有关系分割开来，并成为共同共有的所有权权益。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 22 (2018)（联合租赁的分割）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"联权共有人的权益的终身转让会将该权益与联权租赁关系分离，并成为共同共有（joint tenancy）所有权权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁拥有该财产的所有权？","rule":"联权共有人的权益的终身转让会将该权益与联权租赁关系分离，并成为共同共有（joint tenancy）所有权权益。","analysis":"并发地产\n\n（两人或多人共有的财产）\n\n类型属性可转移性\n\n分权和可能不等份的租赁 在生前可转让 共有财产 可转让 拥有全部财产的平等权利 可继承财产 无生存权 联名共有 生前不可分割且均等份额 可转让（但分割财产*租赁） 拥有全部财产的平等权利 生存权 已婚者之间的联名共有租赁 可在配偶同意的情况下全体转让\n\n*某些司法管辖区允许联权共有人拥有不平等的权益。\n\n联合租赁是一种共有财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益，并享有生存权。联权共有人的权益的终身转让会将该权益与联权租赁关系分离，并成为共同共有（joint tenancy）所有权权益。在这里，两个朋友作为联权共有人拥有该房产，并享有生存权。第一个朋友在生前将她的财产权益转让给了她的儿子。这将联权共有权转变为儿子和第二个朋友之间的共同共有权。然后，第二个朋友将她的财产权益（共同共有（joint tenancy））转让给她的丈夫。因此，丈夫和儿子拥有按份共有人（tenant in common）的所有权（选择 A 和 D）。 （选项B） 由于第一个朋友将她的财产权益转让给了她的儿子，她的继承人在她去世后没有财产权益可以继承。但即使没有进行转让（即，如果联名共有权继续存在），第一个朋友的财产权益也会归第二个朋友——幸存的按份共有人（tenant in common）所有。第二个朋友随后将财产转让给她的丈夫，这将使他获得该财产的独家所有权。\n\n教育目标： 联权共有人的权益的终身转让将该权益与联权共有关系分割开来，并成为共同共有的所有权权益。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 22 (2018)（联合租赁的分割）。","conclusion":"答案 C：丈夫和儿子。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":107,"sourceQuestionPage":313,"sourceQuestionPages":[313],"sourceExplanationPages":[314,315],"sourceAnswerPages":[314,315],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-108","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"An organic farm was conveyed to a married couple, a chef and a restaurant manager, by a deed that created a co-tenancy in equal shares with the right of survivorship. The chef later informed the restaurant manager that he intended to convey his \"undivided one-half interest in the farm\" to his sister. The restaurant manager objected to the conveyance but the chef nevertheless conveyed his interest in the farm to the sister by deed. The chef has since died. In an action between the sister and the restaurant manager to determine title to the farm, who will prevail?","choices":{"A":"The restaurant manager, because she is the sole owner of the farm.","B":"The restaurant manager, but only if the jurisdiction recognizes tenancies by the entirety.","C":"The sister, because the chef and the restaurant manager had a tenancy in common.","D":"The sister, because the restaurant manager knew of the conveyance prior to the chef's death."},"answer":"B","explanation":"There are three types of concurrent estates (ie, cotenancies): Tenancy in common – a tenancy in which each co-tenant has a separate and possibly unequal interest in the property with no right of survivorship\n\nJoint tenancy – a tenancy in which each co-tenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship\n\nTenancy by the entirety – a joint tenancy between married persons\n\nHere, the conveyance to the chef and restaurant manager created a co-tenancy in equal shares with the right of survivorship. Therefore, they received either a joint tenancy or a tenancy by the entirety—not a tenancy in common (Choice C). Joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety are not devisable (by will) or inheritable (by intestate succession). And only a joint tenancy interest can be conveyed during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent. As a result, the outcome of this dispute depends on which tenancy the chef and restaurant manager received: joint tenancy – in which case the chef's lifetime conveyance to the sister would have severed the joint tenancy and created a tenancy in common between the sister and restaurant manager or\n\ntenancy by the entirety* – in which case the conveyance to the sister would have failed, and the restaurant manager would have acquired complete ownership of the farm upon the chef's death\n\nThis means that the restaurant manager would be the sole owner of the farm, and therefore prevail, only if the chef and restaurant manager had a tenancy by the entirety (Choice A). *A tenancy by the entirety is recognized in about half of the jurisdictions. (Choice D) The restaurant manager's knowledge has no effect on the validity of the chef's conveyance. But the restaurant manager's consent would matter if the chef and restaurant manager had received a tenancy by the entirety, since neither tenant could convey the property without the other's consent.\n\nEducational objective: Both joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety have survivorship rights, so they are not devisable or inheritable. But joint tenancies—unlike tenancies by the entirety—are transferrable during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent.","educationalObjective":"Both joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety have survivorship rights, so they are not devisable or inheritable. But joint tenancies—unlike tenancies by the entirety—are transferrable during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent.","references":"","rule":"Both joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety have survivorship rights, so they are not devisable or inheritable. But joint tenancies—unlike tenancies by the entirety—are transferrable during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent.","irac":{"issue":"In an action between the sister and the restaurant manager to determine title to the farm, who will prevail?","rule":"Both joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety have survivorship rights, so they are not devisable or inheritable. But joint tenancies—unlike tenancies by the entirety—are transferrable during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent.","analysis":"There are three types of concurrent estates (ie, cotenancies): Tenancy in common – a tenancy in which each co-tenant has a separate and possibly unequal interest in the property with no right of survivorship\n\nJoint tenancy – a tenancy in which each co-tenant has an undivided and equal interest in the property with the right of survivorship\n\nTenancy by the entirety – a joint tenancy between married persons\n\nHere, the conveyance to the chef and restaurant manager created a co-tenancy in equal shares with the right of survivorship. Therefore, they received either a joint tenancy or a tenancy by the entirety—not a tenancy in common (Choice C). Joint tenancies and tenancies by the entirety are not devisable (by will) or inheritable (by intestate succession). And only a joint tenancy interest can be conveyed during a tenant's lifetime without the other tenant's consent. As a result, the outcome of this dispute depends on which tenancy the chef and restaurant manager received: joint tenancy – in which case the chef's lifetime conveyance to the sister would have severed the joint tenancy and created a tenancy in common between the sister and restaurant manager or\n\ntenancy by the entirety* – in which case the conveyance to the sister would have failed, and the restaurant manager would have acquired complete ownership of the farm upon the chef's death\n\nThis means that the restaurant manager would be the sole owner of the farm, and therefore prevail, only if the chef and restaurant manager had a tenancy by the entirety (Choice A). *A tenancy by the entirety is recognized in about half of the jurisdictions. (Choice D) The restaurant manager's knowledge has no effect on the validity of the chef's conveyance. But the restaurant manager's consent would matter if the chef and restaurant manager had received a tenancy by the entirety, since neither tenant could convey the property without the other's consent.","conclusion":"Answer B: The restaurant manager, but only if the jurisdiction recognizes tenancies by the entirety."},"questionZh":"一个有机农场通过一份契约转让给了一对已婚夫妇、一名厨师和一名餐厅经理，该契约创造了一份具有生存权的平等共同共有（joint tenancy）权。厨师后来告诉餐厅经理，他打算将他“对农场不可分割的二分之一的权益”转让给他的妹妹。餐厅经理反对转让，但厨师仍然通过契约向妹妹传达了他对农场的兴趣。厨师现已去世。在姐姐和餐厅经理之间争夺农场所有权的诉讼中，谁会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"餐厅经理，因为她是农场的唯一所有者。","B":"餐厅经理，但前提是司法管辖区承认整体租赁。","C":"姐姐，因为厨师和餐厅经理有共同的租约。","D":"姐姐，因为餐厅经理在厨师去世前就知道了这件事。"},"explanationZh":"共存财产分为三种类型（即共同共有（joint tenancy））： 共同共有（joint tenancy） – 每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有单独且可能不平等的权益且没有生存权的租赁\n\n联合租赁 – 每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益并享有生存权的租赁\n\n整体租赁 – 已婚人士之间的联合租赁\n\n在这里，厨师和餐厅经理的产权转让创造了一种平等分享的共同共有（joint tenancy）和生存权。因此，他们要么获得联合租赁，要么获得整体租赁，而不是共同共有（joint tenancy）（选择 C）。联合租赁和整体租赁不可设计（通过遗嘱）或可继承（通过无遗嘱继承）。而且，在一个租户的一生中，只有联合租赁权益才能在未经其他租户同意的情况下转让。因此，这场纠纷的结果取决于厨师和餐厅经理获得的租赁权： 联合租赁 – 在这种情况下，厨师的终身产权转让给姐妹将切断联合租赁权，并在姐妹和餐厅经理之间建立共同共有（joint tenancy）权或\n\n全部租赁*——在这种情况下，向妹妹的转让将会失败，餐厅经理将在厨师去世后获得农场的完全所有权\n\n这意味着餐厅经理将是农场的唯一所有者，因此只有在厨师和餐厅经理拥有全部租赁权的情况下（选择 A），餐厅经理才会占上风。 *大约一半的司法管辖区承认整体租赁。 （选项D）餐厅经理的知识对厨师传达的有效性没有影响。但如果厨师和餐厅经理获得了全部租约，餐厅经理的同意就很重要，因为未经对方同意，任何租户都不能转让财产。\n\n教育目标： 联合租赁和整体租赁都具有幸存者权利，因此不可设计或继承。但联合租赁（与整体租赁不同）可以在租户的一生中转让，无需其他租户的同意。","educationalObjectiveZh":"联合租赁和整体租赁都具有幸存者权利，因此不可设计或继承。但联合租赁（与整体租赁不同）可以在租户的一生中转让，无需其他租户的同意。","iracZh":{"issue":"在姐姐和餐厅经理之间争夺农场所有权的诉讼中，谁会获胜？","rule":"联合租赁和整体租赁都具有幸存者权利，因此不可设计或继承。但联合租赁（与整体租赁不同）可以在租户的一生中转让，无需其他租户的同意。","analysis":"共存财产分为三种类型（即共同共有（joint tenancy））： 共同共有（joint tenancy） – 每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有单独且可能不平等的权益且没有生存权的租赁\n\n联合租赁 – 每个按份共有人（tenant in common）对财产拥有不可分割的平等权益并享有生存权的租赁\n\n整体租赁 – 已婚人士之间的联合租赁\n\n在这里，厨师和餐厅经理的产权转让创造了一种平等分享的共同共有（joint tenancy）和生存权。因此，他们要么获得联合租赁，要么获得整体租赁，而不是共同共有（joint tenancy）（选择 C）。联合租赁和整体租赁不可设计（通过遗嘱）或可继承（通过无遗嘱继承）。而且，在一个租户的一生中，只有联合租赁权益才能在未经其他租户同意的情况下转让。因此，这场纠纷的结果取决于厨师和餐厅经理获得的租赁权： 联合租赁 – 在这种情况下，厨师的终身产权转让给姐妹将切断联合租赁权，并在姐妹和餐厅经理之间建立共同共有（joint tenancy）权或\n\n全部租赁*——在这种情况下，向妹妹的转让将会失败，餐厅经理将在厨师去世后获得农场的完全所有权\n\n这意味着餐厅经理将是农场的唯一所有者，因此只有在厨师和餐厅经理拥有全部租赁权的情况下（选择 A），餐厅经理才会占上风。 *大约一半的司法管辖区承认整体租赁。 （选项D）餐厅经理的知识对厨师传达的有效性没有影响。但如果厨师和餐厅经理获得了全部租约，餐厅经理的同意就很重要，因为未经对方同意，任何租户都不能转让财产。\n\n教育目标： 联合租赁和整体租赁都具有幸存者权利，因此不可设计或继承。但联合租赁（与整体租赁不同）可以在租户的一生中转让，无需其他租户的同意。","conclusion":"答案 B：餐厅经理，但前提是司法管辖区承认整体租赁。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-6d30fb910f85f985"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":108,"sourceQuestionPage":316,"sourceQuestionPages":[316],"sourceExplanationPages":[317,318],"sourceAnswerPages":[317,318],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-109","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A landlord leased an apartment to a tenant by written lease for two years ending on the last day of a recent month. The lease provided for $700 in monthly rent. The tenant occupied the apartment and paid the rent for the first 15 months of the lease term, until he moved to another city to take a new job. Without consulting the landlord, the tenant moved a friend into the apartment and signed an informal writing transferring to the friend his \"lease rights\" for the remaining nine months of the lease. The friend made the next four monthly $700 rent payments to the landlord. For the final five months of the lease term, no rent was paid by anyone, and the friend moved out with three months left of the lease term. The landlord was on an extended trip abroad, and did not learn of the default and the vacancy until the end of the lease term. The landlord has sued the tenant and the friend, jointly and severally, for $3,500 for the last five months' rent. What is the likely outcome of the lawsuit?","choices":{"A":"Both the tenant and the friend are liable for the full $3,500, because the tenant is liable based on privity of contract and the friend is liable based on privity of estate as an assignee.","B":"The friend is liable for $1,400 based on privity of estate, which lasted only until he vacated, and the tenant is liable for $2,100 based on privity of contract and estate for the period after the friend vacated.","C":"The tenant is liable for $3,500 based on privity of contract, and the friend is not liable, because a sublessee does not have personal liability to the original landlord.","D":"The friend is liable for $3,500 based on privity of estate, and the tenant is not liable, because the landlord's failure to object to the friend's payment of rent relieved the tenant of liability."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A landlord and tenant have a legal relationship based on both: privity of contract – their shared interest in the lease agreement and\n\nprivity of estate – their successive right to possess the property (ie, the tenant's current right of possession is immediately followed by the landlord's future right of possession).\n\nUnless the lease states otherwise, either party can freely transfer his/her interest under the lease. Assignment is a complete transfer of a tenant's interest to a third party (assignee) for the remainder of the tenant's lease term (as seen here), so: the original tenant retains privity of contract due to the lease and\n\nthe assignee gains privity of estate since the landlord's right of possession now follows the assignee's.\n\nBecause of this privity, the original tenant and the assignee are jointly and severally liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease (eg, failure to pay rent). Therefore, the tenant and the friend are liable for the landlord's entire harm ($3,500). (Choice B) The friend's privity of estate did not terminate when he vacated the property— it lasted until the end of the lease term since that was the friend's assigned interest. And though the tenant lacked privity of estate, he retained privity of contract with the landlord—even after the friend vacated. Therefore, both are liable for the full $3,500. (Choice C) A sublease is a partial transfer of a tenant's interest to a third party (sublessee) for less than the remainder of the lease term. A sublessee is not personally liable to the landlord since (1) no lease agreement exists between them (no privity of contract) and (2) the tenant will regain possession before the landlord (no privity of estate). But since the friend was an assignee, he is jointly and severally liable to the landlord. (Choice D) A tenant is relieved of liability following assignment when the landlord expressly releases the tenant from the lease (eg, letter stating that tenant has no lease obligations). An implied release (eg, the landlord's failure to object to the friend's payment of rent) is insufficient.\n\nEducational objective: Assignment is a transfer of a tenant's entire interest to a third party (assignee) for the remainder of the lease term. The tenant (through privity of contract) and the assignee (through privity of estate) are jointly and severally liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 15.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (right to transfer lease interest).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 16.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (joint and several liability based on privity of contract and privity of estate).","educationalObjective":"Assignment is a transfer of a tenant's entire interest to a third party (assignee) for the remainder of the lease term. The tenant (through privity of contract) and the assignee (through privity of estate) are jointly and severally liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 15.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (right to transfer lease interest).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 16.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (joint and several liability based on privity of contract and privity of estate).","rule":"Assignment is a transfer of a tenant's entire interest to a third party (assignee) for the remainder of the lease term. The tenant (through privity of contract) and the assignee (through privity of estate) are jointly and severally liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease.","irac":{"issue":"What is the likely outcome of the lawsuit?","rule":"Assignment is a transfer of a tenant's entire interest to a third party (assignee) for the remainder of the lease term. The tenant (through privity of contract) and the assignee (through privity of estate) are jointly and severally liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease.","analysis":"A landlord and tenant have a legal relationship based on both: privity of contract – their shared interest in the lease agreement and\n\nprivity of estate – their successive right to possess the property (ie, the tenant's current right of possession is immediately followed by the landlord's future right of possession).\n\nUnless the lease states otherwise, either party can freely transfer his/her interest under the lease. Assignment is a complete transfer of a tenant's interest to a third party (assignee) for the remainder of the tenant's lease term (as seen here), so: the original tenant retains privity of contract due to the lease and\n\nthe assignee gains privity of estate since the landlord's right of possession now follows the assignee's.\n\nBecause of this privity, the original tenant and the assignee are jointly and severally liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease (eg, failure to pay rent). Therefore, the tenant and the friend are liable for the landlord's entire harm ($3,500). (Choice B) The friend's privity of estate did not terminate when he vacated the property— it lasted until the end of the lease term since that was the friend's assigned interest. And though the tenant lacked privity of estate, he retained privity of contract with the landlord—even after the friend vacated. Therefore, both are liable for the full $3,500. (Choice C) A sublease is a partial transfer of a tenant's interest to a third party (sublessee) for less than the remainder of the lease term. A sublessee is not personally liable to the landlord since (1) no lease agreement exists between them (no privity of contract) and (2) the tenant will regain possession before the landlord (no privity of estate). But since the friend was an assignee, he is jointly and severally liable to the landlord. (Choice D) A tenant is relieved of liability following assignment when the landlord expressly releases the tenant from the lease (eg, letter stating that tenant has no lease obligations). An implied release (eg, the landlord's failure to object to the friend's payment of rent) is insufficient.","conclusion":"Answer A: Both the tenant and the friend are liable for the full $3,500, because the tenant is liable based on privity of contract and the friend is liable based on privity of estate as an assignee."},"questionZh":"房东通过书面租约将一套公寓租给租户，租期为两年，截至最近一个月的最后一天。租约规定每月租金为 700 美元。租客占用了该公寓并支付了租赁期前 15 个月的租金，直到他搬到另一个城市找到新工作为止。租户在没有咨询房东的情况下，将一位朋友搬进了公寓，并签署了一份非正式书面文件，将其剩余九个月租约的“租赁权”转让给该朋友。接下来的四个月，这位朋友向房东支付了 700 美元的租金。在租期的最后五个月里，没有人支付任何租金，朋友在租期还剩三个月时就搬走了。房东长期出国旅行，直到租期结束才得知违约和空置的情况。房东已连带和分别起诉房客和朋友，要求赔偿最近五个月的租金 3,500 美元。诉讼的可能结果是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"租户和朋友均需承担 3,500 美元的全部责任，因为租户根据合同的相互关系承担责任，而朋友则根据作为受让人的遗产的相互关系承担责任。","B":"朋友根据遗产相关性承担 1,400 美元的责任，该责任仅持续到他搬出为止；而租户则根据朋友搬出后期间的合同和遗产的相关性承担 2,100 美元的责任。","C":"根据合同关系，租户需承担 3,500 美元的责任，而朋友则无需承担责任，因为转租人对原房东不承担个人责任。","D":"根据遗产相关性，朋友需承担 3,500 美元的责任，而租户则无需承担责任，因为房东未反对朋友支付租金，从而免除了租户的责任。"},"explanationZh":"房东和租客之间的法律关系基于以下两个方面： 合同关系 – 他们在租赁协议中的共同利益；\n\n财产的相互关系——他们对财产的继承权（即，租户当前的占有权紧随房东未来的占有权）。\n\n除非租约另有规定，任何一方都可以自由转让其在租约下的权益。转让是将租户的剩余租赁期限（如此处所示）的权益完全转让给第三方（受让人），因此：原租户因租赁而保留合同关系，并且\n\n受让人获得了财产的优先权，因为现在房东的占有权追随受让人的占有权。\n\n由于这种相关性，原租户和受让人对房东因违反租约（例如不支付租金）而造成的全部损害承担连带责任。因此，房客和朋友应对房东的全部损失承担责任（$3,500）。 （选择 B） 当朋友腾出房产时，他的遗产所有权并没有终止——它一直持续到租赁期结束，因为那是朋友的指定权益。尽管租客缺乏财产相关性，但他仍与房东保持合同相关性——即使在朋友搬出之后也是如此。因此，两人均需承担全额 3,500 美元。 （选择 C） 转租是指将租户的权益部分转让给第三方（转承租人），期限少于剩余租赁期限。转租人不对房东承担个人责任，因为 (1) 他们之间不存在租赁协议（不存在合同关系），并且 (2) 承租人将在房东之前重新获得占有权（不存在遗产关系）。但由于该朋友是受让人，他对房东负连带责任。 （选择 D）如果房东明确解除租客的租约（例如，声明租客没有租赁义务的信函），则租客在转让后的责任将被免除。默示的免除（例如，房东没有反对朋友支付租金）是不够的。\n\n教育目标：转让是将租户在剩余租赁期内的全部权益转让给第三方（受让人）。租户（通过合同关系）和受让人（通过遗产关系）对房东因违反租约而造成的全部损害承担连带责任。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户第 15.1 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（转让租赁权益的权利）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 16.1 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（基于合同相互关系和遗产相互关系的连带责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"转让是将租户在剩余租赁期内的全部权益转让给第三方（受让人）。租户（通过合同关系）和受让人（通过遗产关系）对房东因违反租约而造成的全部损害承担连带责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"诉讼的可能结果是什么？","rule":"转让是将租户在剩余租赁期内的全部权益转让给第三方（受让人）。租户（通过合同关系）和受让人（通过遗产关系）对房东因违反租约而造成的全部损害承担连带责任。","analysis":"房东和租客之间的法律关系基于以下两个方面： 合同关系 – 他们在租赁协议中的共同利益；\n\n财产的相互关系——他们对财产的继承权（即，租户当前的占有权紧随房东未来的占有权）。\n\n除非租约另有规定，任何一方都可以自由转让其在租约下的权益。转让是将租户的剩余租赁期限（如此处所示）的权益完全转让给第三方（受让人），因此：原租户因租赁而保留合同关系，并且\n\n受让人获得了财产的优先权，因为现在房东的占有权追随受让人的占有权。\n\n由于这种相关性，原租户和受让人对房东因违反租约（例如不支付租金）而造成的全部损害承担连带责任。因此，房客和朋友应对房东的全部损失承担责任（$3,500）。 （选择 B） 当朋友腾出房产时，他的遗产所有权并没有终止——它一直持续到租赁期结束，因为那是朋友的指定权益。尽管租客缺乏财产相关性，但他仍与房东保持合同相关性——即使在朋友搬出之后也是如此。因此，两人均需承担全额 3,500 美元。 （选择 C） 转租是指将租户的权益部分转让给第三方（转承租人），期限少于剩余租赁期限。转租人不对房东承担个人责任，因为 (1) 他们之间不存在租赁协议（不存在合同关系），并且 (2) 承租人将在房东之前重新获得占有权（不存在遗产关系）。但由于该朋友是受让人，他对房东负连带责任。 （选择 D）如果房东明确解除租客的租约（例如，声明租客没有租赁义务的信函），则租客在转让后的责任将被免除。默示的免除（例如，房东没有反对朋友支付租金）是不够的。\n\n教育目标：转让是将租户在剩余租赁期内的全部权益转让给第三方（受让人）。租户（通过合同关系）和受让人（通过遗产关系）对房东因违反租约而造成的全部损害承担连带责任。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户第 15.1 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（转让租赁权益的权利）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 16.1 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（基于合同相互关系和遗产相互关系的连带责任）。","conclusion":"答案 A：租户和朋友均需承担 3,500 美元的全部责任，因为租户根据合同的相互关系承担责任，而朋友则根据作为受让人的遗产的相互关系承担责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"severally","ipa":"/sˈɛvɹəli/","syllables":"sev·er·ally","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 各自地, 各个地, 各别地；[法] 分别地, 个别地, 有连带责任地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“severally”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The landlord has sued the tenant and the friend, jointly and severally, for $3,500 for the last five months' rent.","synonyms":["respectively","individually","separately","singly"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-22c3240ab4a4efb6"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":109,"sourceQuestionPage":319,"sourceQuestionPages":[319],"sourceExplanationPages":[320,321],"sourceAnswerPages":[320,321],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-110","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man owned a tract of land in fee simple. By warranty deed he conveyed the land to his brother for life \"and from and after the death of my brother to my sister, her heirs and assigns.\" Subsequently the sister died, devising all of her estate to a friend. The sister was survived by a daughter, her sole heir-at-law. Shortly thereafter the brother died, survived by the man, the friend, and the daughter. Title to the land now is in whom?","choices":{"A":"Either the man or the daughter, depending upon whether the destructibility of contingent remainders is recognized in the applicable jurisdiction.","B":"The daughter, because she is the sister's heir.","C":"The friend, because the vested remainder in the sister was transmitted by her will.","D":"The man, because the contingent remainder never vested and the man's reversion was entitled to possession immediately upon the brother's death."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A life estate is a present possessory estate that terminates upon the death of an individual. This estate is followed by either a reversion (if retained by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in a grantee). Remainders are fully transferable inter vivos, devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance. This is true in most jurisdictions* regardless of whether the remainder is: vested – not subject to any conditions precedent AND held by an identifiable living person (eg, \"then to my sister, her heirs and assigns\") or\n\ncontingent – is subject to an express condition precedent (other than the natural termination of the prior estate) OR held by an unknown or unborn person (eg, \"then to my neighbor, but only if…\").\n\nHere, the man conveyed his fee simple estate to his brother for life, which then passed to his sister unconditionally when the brother died. The sister's vested remainder was devisable, so it passed to the friend after the sister died—leaving no interest in the property for the daughter to inherit (Choice B). Therefore, title to the land is now in the friend. *At common law and in a minority of jurisdictions, contingent remainders cannot be transferred inter vivos. (Choice A) The doctrine of destructibility of contingent remainders provides that a remainder will be destroyed if it has not vested at the time the preceding interest ends. Here, the sister's remainder was vested, so this doctrine does not apply. (Choice D) Had the sister received a contingent remainder that had not vested by the time the preceding estate (brother's life estate) had terminated, the grantor's (man's) reversion would have become immediately possessory upon the brother's death.\n\nEducational objective: Remainders are either vested (ie, not subject to conditions precedent and held by an identifiable living person) OR contingent (ie, subject to conditions precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person). Both are transferrable, devisable, and descendible.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property §§ 162, 164–65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (transferability of remainders).","educationalObjective":"Remainders are either vested (ie, not subject to conditions precedent and held by an identifiable living person) OR contingent (ie, subject to conditions precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person). Both are transferrable, devisable, and descendible.","references":"Restatement of Property §§ 162, 164–65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (transferability of remainders).","rule":"Remainders are either vested (ie, not subject to conditions precedent and held by an identifiable living person) OR contingent (ie, subject to conditions precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person). Both are transferrable, devisable, and descendible.","irac":{"issue":"Title to the land now is in whom?","rule":"Remainders are either vested (ie, not subject to conditions precedent and held by an identifiable living person) OR contingent (ie, subject to conditions precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person). Both are transferrable, devisable, and descendible.","analysis":"A life estate is a present possessory estate that terminates upon the death of an individual. This estate is followed by either a reversion (if retained by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in a grantee). Remainders are fully transferable inter vivos, devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance. This is true in most jurisdictions* regardless of whether the remainder is: vested – not subject to any conditions precedent AND held by an identifiable living person (eg, \"then to my sister, her heirs and assigns\") or\n\ncontingent – is subject to an express condition precedent (other than the natural termination of the prior estate) OR held by an unknown or unborn person (eg, \"then to my neighbor, but only if…\").\n\nHere, the man conveyed his fee simple estate to his brother for life, which then passed to his sister unconditionally when the brother died. The sister's vested remainder was devisable, so it passed to the friend after the sister died—leaving no interest in the property for the daughter to inherit (Choice B). Therefore, title to the land is now in the friend. *At common law and in a minority of jurisdictions, contingent remainders cannot be transferred inter vivos. (Choice A) The doctrine of destructibility of contingent remainders provides that a remainder will be destroyed if it has not vested at the time the preceding interest ends. Here, the sister's remainder was vested, so this doctrine does not apply. (Choice D) Had the sister received a contingent remainder that had not vested by the time the preceding estate (brother's life estate) had terminated, the grantor's (man's) reversion would have become immediately possessory upon the brother's death.","conclusion":"Answer C: The friend, because the vested remainder in the sister was transmitted by her will."},"questionZh":"一个人简单地拥有一块土地。通过担保契据，他将土地终身转让给他的兄弟，“从我兄弟死后起，转让给我的妹妹，她的继承人和受让人”。随后，姐姐去世了，她把所有的财产都捐给了一位朋友。姐姐身后留下了一个女儿，她是她唯一的继承人。不久之后，兄弟去世，留下了男人、朋友和女儿。现在土地所有权属于谁？","choicesZh":{"A":"男人或女儿，取决于或有剩余物的可破坏性是否在适用的司法管辖区得到认可。","B":"女儿，因为她是姐姐的继承人。","C":"朋友，因为姐姐的既得剩余是由她的遗嘱继承的。","D":"男人，因为或有剩余从未归属，而男人的归还有权在兄弟去世后立即占有。"},"explanationZh":"终身财产是目前的占有财产，在个人死亡时终止。该遗产之后是归还（如果由授予人保留）或剩余部分（如果由受让人创建）。剩余部分可以在生前完全转移，可以通过意志设计，也可以通过继承传承。在大多数司法管辖区*都是如此，无论剩余部分是否：既得 – 不受任何先决条件的约束并且由可识别的在世人士持有（例如，“然后归我的姐妹、她的继承人和受让人”）或\n\n或有 – 受明确的先决条件限制（先前财产的自然终止除外）或由未知或未出生的人持有（例如，“然后给我的邻居，但前提是……”）。\n\n在这里，该男子将他的简单财产终身转让给他的兄弟，然后在兄弟去世后无条件地转移给他的妹妹。姐姐的既得剩余部分是可以设计的，因此在姐姐去世后，它就转移给了朋友——没有留下任何财产权益可供女儿继承（选择B）。因此，土地所有权现在归朋友所有。 *根据普通法和少数司法管辖区，或有余额不能在生前转移。 （选项A）或有剩余物可销毁性原则规定，如果在前一利息结束时剩余物尚未归属，则剩余物将被销毁。在此，姐妹的剩余部分已归属，因此该学说不适用。 （选项D）如果妹妹收到了在前一个遗产（兄弟的终身遗产）终止时尚未归属的或有剩余部分，则设保人（男子）的归还权将在兄弟去世后立即成为占有人。\n\n教育目标：剩余部分要么是既定的（即不受先决条件限制并由可识别的活着的人持有），要么是偶然的（即受先决条件限制或由未知或未出生的人持有）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 162, 164–65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936)（剩余部分的可转让性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"剩余部分要么是既定的（即不受先决条件限制并由可识别的在世人士持有），要么是偶然的（即受先决条件限制或由未知或未出生的人持有）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。","iracZh":{"issue":"现在土地所有权属于谁？","rule":"剩余部分要么是既定的（即不受先决条件限制并由可识别的在世人士持有），要么是偶然的（即受先决条件限制或由未知或未出生的人持有）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。","analysis":"终身财产是目前的占有财产，在个人死亡时终止。该遗产之后是归还（如果由授予人保留）或剩余部分（如果由受让人创建）。剩余部分可以在生前完全转移，可以通过意志设计，也可以通过继承传承。在大多数司法管辖区*都是如此，无论剩余部分是否：既得 – 不受任何先决条件的约束并且由可识别的在世人士持有（例如，“然后归我的姐妹、她的继承人和受让人”）或\n\n或有 – 受明确的先决条件限制（先前财产的自然终止除外）或由未知或未出生的人持有（例如，“然后给我的邻居，但前提是……”）。\n\n在这里，该男子将他的简单财产终身转让给他的兄弟，然后在兄弟去世后无条件地转移给他的妹妹。姐姐的既得剩余部分是可以设计的，因此在姐姐去世后，它就转移给了朋友——没有留下任何财产权益可供女儿继承（选择B）。因此，土地所有权现在归朋友所有。 *根据普通法和少数司法管辖区，或有余额不能在生前转移。 （选项A）或有剩余物可销毁性原则规定，如果在前一利息结束时剩余物尚未归属，则剩余物将被销毁。在此，姐妹的剩余部分已归属，因此该学说不适用。 （选项D）如果妹妹收到了在前一个遗产（兄弟的终身遗产）终止时尚未归属的或有剩余部分，则设保人（男子）的归还权将在兄弟去世后立即成为占有人。\n\n教育目标：剩余部分要么是既定的（即不受先决条件限制并由可识别的活着的人持有），要么是偶然的（即受先决条件限制或由未知或未出生的人持有）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 162, 164–65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936)（剩余部分的可转让性）。","conclusion":"答案 C：朋友，因为姐姐的既得剩余是由她的遗嘱继承的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-ab216bc7ad351337"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":110,"sourceQuestionPage":322,"sourceQuestionPages":[322],"sourceExplanationPages":[323,324],"sourceAnswerPages":[323,324],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-111","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"In the most recent deed in the chain of title to a tract of land, a man conveyed the land as follows: \"To my niece and her heirs and assigns in fee simple until my niece's daughter marries, and then to my niece's daughter and her heirs and assigns in fee simple.\" There is no applicable statute, and the common law Rule Against Perpetuities has not been modified in the jurisdiction. Which of the following is the most accurate statement concerning the title to the land?","choices":{"A":"The niece has a defeasible fee simple determinable and the daughter has an executory interest.","B":"The niece has a fee simple and the daughter has no interest, because after the grant of a fee simple there can be no gift over.","C":"The niece has a fee simple and the daughter has no interest, because she might not marry within 21 years after the date of the deed.","D":"The niece has a life estate and the daughter has a contingent remainder."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nDefeasible fees are fee simple estates that may be terminated by the occurrence of a stated event. One type of defeasible fee is a fee simple determinable (FSD), which is created with durational language (eg, \"to my niece until my niece's daughter marries\"). The future interest that follows an FSD is either: a possibility of reverter – in which case, the estate automatically reverts back to the grantor when the stated event occurs (presumed if not expressly stated) or\n\nan executory interest – in which case, the estate automatically passes to a third party (grantee) when the stated event occurs (eg, \"then to my niece's daughter\").\n\nTherefore, pursuant to the man's conveyance, the niece has a defeasible fee simple determinable and the daughter holds an executory interest (Choices B & D). (Choice C) The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) renders a future interest void if there is any possibility that it could vest more than 21 years after the death of some relevant person alive when the interest was created. But here, the daughter's executory interest does not violate RAP because she will marry (or not) within her own lifetime.\n\nEducational objective: A fee simple determinable (FSD) is a type of defeasible fee that is created with durational language. FSDs can be followed by a grantor's possibility of reverter OR a third party's executory interest.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property §§ 44, 47 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing the fee simple determinable and fee simple determinable subject to executory limitation).","educationalObjective":"A fee simple determinable (FSD) is a type of defeasible fee that is created with durational language. FSDs can be followed by a grantor's possibility of reverter OR a third party's executory interest.","references":"Restatement of Property §§ 44, 47 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing the fee simple determinable and fee simple determinable subject to executory limitation).","rule":"A fee simple determinable (FSD) is a type of defeasible fee that is created with durational language. FSDs can be followed by a grantor's possibility of reverter OR a third party's executory interest.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the most accurate statement concerning the title to the land?","rule":"A fee simple determinable (FSD) is a type of defeasible fee that is created with durational language. FSDs can be followed by a grantor's possibility of reverter OR a third party's executory interest.","analysis":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nDefeasible fees are fee simple estates that may be terminated by the occurrence of a stated event. One type of defeasible fee is a fee simple determinable (FSD), which is created with durational language (eg, \"to my niece until my niece's daughter marries\"). The future interest that follows an FSD is either: a possibility of reverter – in which case, the estate automatically reverts back to the grantor when the stated event occurs (presumed if not expressly stated) or\n\nan executory interest – in which case, the estate automatically passes to a third party (grantee) when the stated event occurs (eg, \"then to my niece's daughter\").\n\nTherefore, pursuant to the man's conveyance, the niece has a defeasible fee simple determinable and the daughter holds an executory interest (Choices B & D). (Choice C) The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) renders a future interest void if there is any possibility that it could vest more than 21 years after the death of some relevant person alive when the interest was created. But here, the daughter's executory interest does not violate RAP because she will marry (or not) within her own lifetime.","conclusion":"Answer A: The niece has a defeasible fee simple determinable and the daughter has an executory interest."},"questionZh":"在一块土地所有权链的最新契约中，一名男子将土地转让如下：“给我的侄女和她的继承人，并以简单的费用转让，直到我侄女的女儿结婚为止，然后给我的侄女的女儿和她的继承人，并以简单的费用转让。”没有适用的法规，普通法的反永续规则在管辖范围内也没有修改。关于土地所有权，以下哪项描述最准确？","choicesZh":{"A":"侄女有可简单确定的可废止费用，女儿有执行权益。","B":"侄女有一笔简单的费用，而女儿没有利息，因为在授予一笔简单的费用后，就不能再有任何礼物了。","C":"侄女有简单的费用，女儿没有利息，因为她可能不会在契约日期后 21 年内结婚。","D":"侄女拥有终身财产，女儿拥有或有剩余财产。"},"explanationZh":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n可废止的费用是可因规定事件的发生而终止的简单费用遗产。一种可废止的费用是简单可确定费用（FSD），它是用持续语言创建的（例如，“给我的侄女，直到我侄女的女儿结婚”）。 FSD 后的未来利息是： 归还的可能性 - 在这种情况下，当所述事件发生时，遗产自动归还给授予人（如果没有明确说明，则假定）或\n\n执行权益——在这种情况下，当所述事件发生时，遗产自动转移给第三方（受让人）（例如，“然后转移给我侄女的女儿”）。\n\n因此，根据该男子的转让，侄女具有可简单确定的可废止费用，并且女儿拥有执行权益（选择B＆D）。 （选项 C）如果在利息创建时某个活着的相关人员死亡超过 21 年后，未来利息有可能归属，则反永续规则 (RAP) 将使未来利息无效。但在这里，女儿的执行权益并不违反 RAP，因为她将在自己的有生之年结婚（或不结婚）。\n\n教育目标：费用简单确定（FSD）是一种用持续语言创建的可废止费用。 FSD 后可以是设保人恢复或第三方执行权益的可能性。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 44, 47 (Am. Law Inst. 1936)（讨论简单确定的费用和受执行限制的简单确定的费用）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"可终止完全所有权（fee simple determinable）（FSD）是一种使用持续语言创建的可废止费用。 FSD 后可以是设保人恢复或第三方执行权益的可能性。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于土地所有权，以下哪项描述最准确？","rule":"可终止完全所有权（fee simple determinable）（FSD）是一种使用持续语言创建的可废止费用。 FSD 后可以是设保人恢复或第三方执行权益的可能性。","analysis":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n可废止的费用是可因规定事件的发生而终止的简单费用遗产。一种可废止的费用是简单可确定费用（FSD），它是用持续语言创建的（例如，“给我的侄女，直到我侄女的女儿结婚”）。 FSD 后的未来利息是： 归还的可能性 - 在这种情况下，当所述事件发生时，遗产自动归还给授予人（如果没有明确说明，则假定）或\n\n执行权益——在这种情况下，当所述事件发生时，遗产自动转移给第三方（受让人）（例如，“然后转移给我侄女的女儿”）。\n\n因此，根据该男子的转让，侄女具有可简单确定的可废止费用，并且女儿拥有执行权益（选择B＆D）。 （选项 C）如果在利息创建时某个活着的相关人员死亡超过 21 年后，未来利息有可能归属，则反永续规则 (RAP) 将使未来利息无效。但在这里，女儿的执行权益并不违反 RAP，因为她将在自己的有生之年结婚（或不结婚）。\n\n教育目标：费用简单确定（FSD）是一种用持续语言创建的可废止费用。 FSD 后可以是设保人恢复或第三方执行权益的可能性。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 44, 47 (Am. Law Inst. 1936)（讨论简单确定的费用和受执行限制的简单确定的费用）。","conclusion":"答案 A：侄女有可简单确定的可废止费用，女儿有执行权益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":111,"sourceQuestionPage":325,"sourceQuestionPages":[325],"sourceExplanationPages":[326,327],"sourceAnswerPages":[326,327],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-112","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A landlord and a tenant entered into a valid written agreement to lease a single-family residence for a period of one year at $1,200 per month. Among the provisions in the agreement was a waiver of the implied warranty of habitability. Two months into the lease, the electricity stopped working through no fault of the tenant. The tenant notified the landlord, but after one week, the landlord had done nothing to remedy the problem. When the next month's rent came due, the tenant refused to pay it because the landlord had still not fixed the electricity. The landlord has filed an action for breach of contract against the tenant for failure to pay rent. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The landlord, because the tenant waived the implied warranty of habitability in the lease.","B":"The landlord, because there is no duty to repair under a residential lease.","C":"The tenant, because the landlord's failure to repair the electricity violated the implied covenant of quiet enjoyment.","D":"The tenant, because the tenant notified the landlord of the problem and gave him a reasonable opportunity to correct it."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Landlord's duty to repair leased premises\n\nType of Common law Majority rule lease\n\nResidential No duty to repair unless Duty to repair substantial defects under statute or contract requires implied warranty of habitability\n\nCommercial No duty to make ordinary repairs unless statute or contract requires\n\nIn most jurisdictions, landlords of leased residential properties have a duty to repair substantial defects that are not caused by the tenant. Failure to make such repairs violates the implied warranty of habitability, which ensures that residential properties satisfy basic safety and living conditions. If the warranty is violated, the tenant may: refuse to pay rent until the necessary repairs are made remedy the defect and offset those costs against the rent or defend against eviction (eg, by asserting constructive eviction). However, the tenant must notify the landlord of the problem AND give the landlord a reasonable opportunity to correct it before exercising these remedies. Here, the electricity in the leased residence stopped working two months into the lease through no fault of the tenant. The tenant notified the landlord of this defect and gave him one week, a reasonable opportunity, to correct the problem. The landlord did nothing. Therefore, the tenant was entitled to withhold rent and is likely to prevail in this breach-of-contract action. (Choice A) The implied warranty of habitability cannot be waived by a tenant, either by express language in the lease or by taking possession of the property with knowledge of the conditions. (Choice B) At common law, a landlord had no implied duty to make repairs under a residential lease. However, landlords do have an implied duty to repair in most jurisdictions today. (Choice C) The implied covenant of quiet enjoyment is a promise by the landlord not to interfere with the tenant's possession of the leased premises. Here, the landlord did not violate this covenant since the failure to repair the electricity did not interfere with the tenant's right to possess the leased residence.\n\nEducational objective: In most jurisdictions, a residential landlord must repair substantial defects not caused by the tenant. Breach of this duty violates the implied warranty of habitability and allows the\n\ntenant to withhold rent, remedy the defect and offset the costs, or defend against eviction— if the landlord had a reasonable opportunity to correct the defect after notice.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 5.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (landlord's duty to repair).","educationalObjective":"In most jurisdictions, a residential landlord must repair substantial defects not caused by the tenant. Breach of this duty violates the implied warranty of habitability and allows the\n\ntenant to withhold rent, remedy the defect and offset the costs, or defend against eviction— if the landlord had a reasonable opportunity to correct the defect after notice.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 5.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (landlord's duty to repair).","rule":"In most jurisdictions, a residential landlord must repair substantial defects not caused by the tenant. Breach of this duty violates the implied warranty of habitability and allows the\n\ntenant to withhold rent, remedy the defect and offset the costs, or defend against eviction— if the landlord had a reasonable opportunity to correct the defect after notice.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"In most jurisdictions, a residential landlord must repair substantial defects not caused by the tenant. Breach of this duty violates the implied warranty of habitability and allows the\n\ntenant to withhold rent, remedy the defect and offset the costs, or defend against eviction— if the landlord had a reasonable opportunity to correct the defect after notice.","analysis":"Landlord's duty to repair leased premises\n\nType of Common law Majority rule lease\n\nResidential No duty to repair unless Duty to repair substantial defects under statute or contract requires implied warranty of habitability\n\nCommercial No duty to make ordinary repairs unless statute or contract requires\n\nIn most jurisdictions, landlords of leased residential properties have a duty to repair substantial defects that are not caused by the tenant. Failure to make such repairs violates the implied warranty of habitability, which ensures that residential properties satisfy basic safety and living conditions. If the warranty is violated, the tenant may: refuse to pay rent until the necessary repairs are made remedy the defect and offset those costs against the rent or defend against eviction (eg, by asserting constructive eviction). However, the tenant must notify the landlord of the problem AND give the landlord a reasonable opportunity to correct it before exercising these remedies. Here, the electricity in the leased residence stopped working two months into the lease through no fault of the tenant. The tenant notified the landlord of this defect and gave him one week, a reasonable opportunity, to correct the problem. The landlord did nothing. Therefore, the tenant was entitled to withhold rent and is likely to prevail in this breach-of-contract action. (Choice A) The implied warranty of habitability cannot be waived by a tenant, either by express language in the lease or by taking possession of the property with knowledge of the conditions. (Choice B) At common law, a landlord had no implied duty to make repairs under a residential lease. However, landlords do have an implied duty to repair in most jurisdictions today. (Choice C) The implied covenant of quiet enjoyment is a promise by the landlord not to interfere with the tenant's possession of the leased premises. Here, the landlord did not violate this covenant since the failure to repair the electricity did not interfere with the tenant's right to possess the leased residence.","conclusion":"Answer D: The tenant, because the tenant notified the landlord of the problem and gave him a reasonable opportunity to correct it."},"questionZh":"房东和租户签订了一份有效的书面协议，以每月 1,200 美元的价格租赁一套单户住宅，为期一年。协议中的条款之一是放弃对可居住性的默示保证。租约两个月后，停电并非租户的过错。租客通知了房东，但一周后，房东没有采取任何措施来解决问题。下个月的房租到期时，房客拒绝支付，因为房东还没有修好电费。房东因未支付租金而对租户提起违约诉讼。谁有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"房东，因为租客放弃了租约中对可居住性的默示保证。","B":"房东，因为根据住宅租赁合同，没有维修义务。","C":"租客，因为房东未能修复电力，违反了安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）的默示契约。","D":"租客，因为租客将问题告知了房东，并给了他合理的纠正机会。"},"explanationZh":"房东修复租赁房屋的责任\n\n普通法类型 多数规则租赁\n\n住宅 没有维修义务，除非法律或合同规定的重大缺陷维修义务需要暗示可居住性保证\n\n商业 除非法规或合同要求，否则没有义务进行普通维修\n\n在大多数司法管辖区，租赁住宅物业的房东有责任修复非租户造成的重大缺陷。不进行此类维修就违反了可居住性的默示保证，该保证可确保住宅物业满足基本的安全和居住条件。如果违反保证，承租人可以： 拒绝支付租金，直到进行必要的维修为止 补救缺陷并用租金抵消这些费用或针对驱逐进行辩护（例如，通过主张推定驱逐）。但是，租户必须将问题通知房东，并在采取这些补救措施之前给房东一个合理的机会来纠正问题。在这里，租赁住宅的电力在租约两个月后就停止了，这并不是租户的过错。租客将这一缺陷通知了房东，并给了他一周的合理机会来纠正问题。楼主什么都没做。因此，租户有权扣留租金，并且很可能在本次违约诉讼中胜诉。 （选择 A） 租户不能通过租约中的明确语言或在了解条件的情况下占有该财产来放弃对可居住性的默示保证。 （选择 B） 根据普通法，房东没有隐含的根据住宅租约进行维修的义务。然而，在当今的大多数司法管辖区，房东确实有隐含的维修义务。 （选项C） 隐含的安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）契约是房东不干涉租户占有租赁房屋的承诺。在这里，房东并没有违反这一约定，因为未能修复电力并不影响租户占有租赁房屋的权利。\n\n教育目标：在大多数司法管辖区，住宅房东必须修复非租户造成的重大缺陷。违反此义务即违反了可居住性的默示保证，并允许\n\n如果房东在收到通知后有合理的机会纠正缺陷，则租户可以扣留租金、纠正缺陷并抵消费用，或针对驱逐进行辩护。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户第 5.5 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（房东的修理责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在大多数司法管辖区，住宅房东必须修复非租户造成的重大缺陷。违反此义务即违反了可居住性的默示保证，并允许\n\n如果房东在收到通知后有合理的机会纠正缺陷，则租户可以扣留租金、纠正缺陷并抵消费用，或针对驱逐进行辩护。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"在大多数司法管辖区，住宅房东必须修复非租户造成的重大缺陷。违反此义务即违反了可居住性的默示保证，并允许\n\n如果房东在收到通知后有合理的机会纠正缺陷，则租户可以扣留租金、纠正缺陷并抵消费用，或针对驱逐进行辩护。","analysis":"房东修复租赁房屋的责任\n\n普通法类型 多数规则租赁\n\n住宅 没有维修义务，除非法律或合同规定的重大缺陷维修义务需要暗示可居住性保证\n\n商业 除非法规或合同要求，否则没有义务进行普通维修\n\n在大多数司法管辖区，租赁住宅物业的房东有责任修复非租户造成的重大缺陷。不进行此类维修就违反了可居住性的默示保证，该保证可确保住宅物业满足基本的安全和居住条件。如果违反保证，承租人可以： 拒绝支付租金，直到进行必要的维修为止 补救缺陷并用租金抵消这些费用或针对驱逐进行辩护（例如，通过主张推定驱逐）。但是，租户必须将问题通知房东，并在采取这些补救措施之前给房东一个合理的机会来纠正问题。在这里，租赁住宅的电力在租约两个月后就停止了，这并不是租户的过错。租客将这一缺陷通知了房东，并给了他一周的合理机会来纠正问题。楼主什么都没做。因此，租户有权扣留租金，并且很可能在本次违约诉讼中胜诉。 （选择 A） 租户不能通过租约中的明确语言或在了解条件的情况下占有该财产来放弃对可居住性的默示保证。 （选择 B） 根据普通法，房东没有隐含的根据住宅租约进行维修的义务。然而，在当今的大多数司法管辖区，房东确实有隐含的维修义务。 （选项C） 隐含的安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）契约是房东不干涉租户占有租赁房屋的承诺。在这里，房东并没有违反这一约定，因为未能修复电力并不影响租户占有租赁房屋的权利。\n\n教育目标：在大多数司法管辖区，住宅房东必须修复非租户造成的重大缺陷。违反此义务即违反了可居住性的默示保证，并允许\n\n如果房东在收到通知后有合理的机会纠正缺陷，则租户可以扣留租金、纠正缺陷并抵消费用，或针对驱逐进行辩护。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户第 5.5 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（房东的修理责任）。","conclusion":"答案 D：租客，因为租客将问题告知了房东，并给了他合理的纠正机会。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"implied warranty of habitability","ipa":"/ˌɪmplˈaɪd wˈɔɹənti ˈəv ,hæbitə'biləti/","syllables":"im·plied war·ranty of hab·it·abil·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"默示适居担保：住宅房东必须维持符合基本居住条件的房屋","contextualMeaning":"默示适居担保：住宅房东必须维持符合基本居住条件的房屋","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Among the provisions in the agreement was a waiver of the implied warranty of habitability.","synonyms":["habitability warranty"]},{"term":"covenant of quiet enjoyment","ipa":"/kˈəvənənt ˈəv kwˈaɪət ˌɛndʒˈɔɪmənt/","syllables":"covenant of quiet en·joy·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"安宁享有担保：受让人不因优先产权主张而被逐出或受重大干扰","contextualMeaning":"安宁享有担保：受让人不因优先产权主张而被逐出或受重大干扰","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The tenant, because the landlord's failure to repair the electricity violated the implied covenant of quiet enjoyment.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":112,"sourceQuestionPage":328,"sourceQuestionPages":[328],"sourceExplanationPages":[329,330],"sourceAnswerPages":[329,330],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-113","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man owned in fee simple Lots 1 and 2 in an urban subdivision. The lots were vacant and unproductive. They were held as a speculation that their value would increase. The man died and, by his duly probated will, devised the residue of his estate (of which Lots 1 and 2 were part) to his wife for life with remainder in fee simple to his sister. The man's executor distributed the estate under appropriate court order and notified the wife that future real estate taxes on Lots 1 and 2 were her responsibility to pay. Except for the statutes relating to probate and those relating to real estate taxes, there is no applicable statute. The wife left the lots vacant and did not pay the real estate taxes due for Lots 1 and 2. To prevent a tax sale of the fee simple, the sister paid the taxes and demanded that the wife reimburse her for same. When the wife refused, the sister brought an appropriate action against the wife to recover the amount paid. In such action, what should the sister recover?","choices":{"A":"Nothing, because the wife never received any income from the lots.","B":"Nothing, because the wife's sole possession gave the right to decide whether or not taxes should be paid.","C":"The amount paid, because a life tenant has the duty to pay current charges.","D":"The present value of the interest that the amount paid would earn during the wife's lifetime."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Life tenant's duties\n\nDuty to pay Life tenant must pay all current charges due during life tenancy (eg, current charges property taxes, mortgage interest) up to financial benefit received from property\n\nDuty to prevent Life tenant must prevent affirmative waste (ie, voluntary waste), waste permissive waste & ameliorative waste\n\nDuty to make Life tenant must make reasonable repairs to preserve property ordinary repairs\n\nThe holder of a life estate (ie, the life tenant) has a duty to pay current charges on the land that become due during the life tenancy (eg, property taxes, mortgage interest). But this duty is limited to the financial benefit that the life tenant receives from the property. Therefore, there is no duty to pay these charges if the life tenant is not in possession of the property and does not receive rent or other income from the property. Here, the man's wife received a life estate (\"to his wife for life\") and the remainder was owned by his sister (\"remainder in fee simple to his sister\"). Although a life tenant generally has the duty to pay real estate taxes, the lots were left vacant and unproductive. As a result, the wife received no financial benefit from the lots and had no duty to pay the real estate taxes. The sister should therefore recover nothing. (Choice B) The wife's duty to pay taxes depends on the financial benefit she received from the land, not merely her possession of it. (Choices C & D) Had the wife been obligated to pay the real estate taxes, the sister could have recovered the amount paid and possibly the interest that accrued during the repayment period (but not during the wife's lifetime).\n\nEducational objective: A life tenant's duty to pay current charges on the land is limited to the financial benefit that the life tenant receives from the property. Therefore, this duty is not owed if the life tenant is not in possession of and receives no income from the property.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (First) of Property §§ 129–130 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (duty to pay current charges).","educationalObjective":"A life tenant's duty to pay current charges on the land is limited to the financial benefit that the life tenant receives from the property. Therefore, this duty is not owed if the life tenant is not in possession of and receives no income from the property.","references":"Restatement (First) of Property §§ 129–130 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (duty to pay current charges).","rule":"A life tenant's duty to pay current charges on the land is limited to the financial benefit that the life tenant receives from the property. Therefore, this duty is not owed if the life tenant is not in possession of and receives no income from the property.","irac":{"issue":"In such action, what should the sister recover?","rule":"A life tenant's duty to pay current charges on the land is limited to the financial benefit that the life tenant receives from the property. Therefore, this duty is not owed if the life tenant is not in possession of and receives no income from the property.","analysis":"Life tenant's duties\n\nDuty to pay Life tenant must pay all current charges due during life tenancy (eg, current charges property taxes, mortgage interest) up to financial benefit received from property\n\nDuty to prevent Life tenant must prevent affirmative waste (ie, voluntary waste), waste permissive waste & ameliorative waste\n\nDuty to make Life tenant must make reasonable repairs to preserve property ordinary repairs\n\nThe holder of a life estate (ie, the life tenant) has a duty to pay current charges on the land that become due during the life tenancy (eg, property taxes, mortgage interest). But this duty is limited to the financial benefit that the life tenant receives from the property. Therefore, there is no duty to pay these charges if the life tenant is not in possession of the property and does not receive rent or other income from the property. Here, the man's wife received a life estate (\"to his wife for life\") and the remainder was owned by his sister (\"remainder in fee simple to his sister\"). Although a life tenant generally has the duty to pay real estate taxes, the lots were left vacant and unproductive. As a result, the wife received no financial benefit from the lots and had no duty to pay the real estate taxes. The sister should therefore recover nothing. (Choice B) The wife's duty to pay taxes depends on the financial benefit she received from the land, not merely her possession of it. (Choices C & D) Had the wife been obligated to pay the real estate taxes, the sister could have recovered the amount paid and possibly the interest that accrued during the repayment period (but not during the wife's lifetime).","conclusion":"Answer A: Nothing, because the wife never received any income from the lots."},"questionZh":"一名男子拥有城市土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）的 1 号和 2 号地块。这些地块空置且毫无生产力。人们持有它们是为了猜测它们的价值会增加。该男子去世后，根据其经过正式遗嘱认证的遗嘱，将他的剩余遗产（其中第 1 号和第 2 号地块属于其中的一部分）留给了他的妻子终身，剩余的遗产则归他的妹妹所有。该男子的遗嘱执行人根据适当的法院命令分配了遗产，并通知妻子，未来 1 号和 2 号地段的房地产税由她负责支付。除与遗嘱认证有关的法规和与房地产税有关的法规外，没有适用的法规。妻子将这些地块空置，并且没有缴纳第 1 号和第 2 号地块应缴纳的房地产税。为了防止出售费用，姐姐缴纳了税款，并要求妻子偿还同样的税款。当妻子拒绝后，姐姐对妻子提起适当诉讼，要求收回所支付的款项。这样的举动，姐姐该挽回什么呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"什么也没有，因为妻子从未从这些土地上获得任何收入。","B":"没什么，因为妻子的唯一财产赋予她决定是否纳税的权利。","C":"支付的金额，因为终身租户有义务支付当前费用。","D":"所支付的金额在妻子一生中将赚取的利息的现值。"},"explanationZh":"终身租客的职责\n\n支付义务 终身租客必须支付终身租赁期间应付的所有当前费用（例如，当前费用、财产税、抵押贷款利息），直至从财产获得的经济利益\n\n防止的责任 终身租户必须防止肯定性浪费（即自愿性浪费）、允许性浪费和改善性浪费\n\n终身租客必须进行合理维修以保护财产的责任 普通维修\n\n终身财产的持有人（即终身租户）有义务支付终身租赁期间到期的土地费用（例如财产税、抵押贷款利息）。但这项义务仅限于终身租户从房产中获得的经济利益。因此，如果终身租客不拥有该房产并且没有从该房产获得租金或其他收入，则没有义务支付这些费用。在这里，该男子的妻子获得了终身财产（“终身归他的妻子”），其余部分归他的妹妹所有（“剩余的费用简单归他的妹妹所有”）。尽管终身租客通常有义务缴纳房地产税，但这些地块却空置且闲置。结果，妻子没有从这些土地中获得任何经济利益，也没有义务缴纳房地产税。因此，这位姊妹应该一无所获。 （选择B） 妻子的纳税义务取决于她从土地中获得的经济利益，而不仅仅是她对土地的占有。 （选项C和D）如果妻子有义务缴纳房地产税，姐姐可以收回所支付的金额，并可能收回还款期间（但不是妻子在世期间）产生的利息。\n\n教育目标：终身租客支付土地现行费用的义务仅限于终身租客从房产中获得的经济利益。因此，如果终身租客不拥有该财产并且没有从该财产中获得任何收入，则不承担这项义务。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§§ 129–130（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（支付当前费用的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"终身租客支付土地现行费用的义务仅限于终身租客从财产中获得的经济利益。因此，如果终身租客不拥有该财产并且没有从该财产中获得任何收入，则不承担这项义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"这样的举动，姐姐该挽回什么呢？","rule":"终身租客支付土地现行费用的义务仅限于终身租客从财产中获得的经济利益。因此，如果终身租客不拥有该财产并且没有从该财产中获得任何收入，则不承担这项义务。","analysis":"终身租客的职责\n\n支付义务 终身租客必须支付终身租赁期间应付的所有当前费用（例如，当前费用、财产税、抵押贷款利息），直至从财产获得的经济利益\n\n防止的责任 终身租户必须防止肯定性浪费（即自愿性浪费）、允许性浪费和改善性浪费\n\n终身租客必须进行合理维修以保护财产的责任 普通维修\n\n终身财产的持有人（即终身租户）有义务支付终身租赁期间到期的土地费用（例如财产税、抵押贷款利息）。但这项义务仅限于终身租户从房产中获得的经济利益。因此，如果终身租客不拥有该房产并且没有从该房产获得租金或其他收入，则没有义务支付这些费用。在这里，该男子的妻子获得了终身财产（“终身归他的妻子”），其余部分归他的妹妹所有（“剩余的费用简单归他的妹妹所有”）。尽管终身租客通常有义务缴纳房地产税，但这些地块却空置且闲置。结果，妻子没有从这些土地中获得任何经济利益，也没有义务缴纳房地产税。因此，这位姊妹应该一无所获。 （选择B） 妻子的纳税义务取决于她从土地中获得的经济利益，而不仅仅是她对土地的占有。 （选项C和D）如果妻子有义务缴纳房地产税，姐姐可以收回所支付的金额，并可能收回还款期间（但不是妻子在世期间）产生的利息。\n\n教育目标：终身租客支付土地现行费用的义务仅限于终身租客从房产中获得的经济利益。因此，如果终身租客不拥有该财产并且没有从该财产中获得任何收入，则不承担这项义务。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§§ 129–130（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（支付当前费用的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 A：什么也没有，因为妻子从未从这些土地上获得任何收入。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"ameliorative waste","ipa":"/ə'mi:ljərətiv wˈeɪst/","syllables":"ame·lio·ra·tive waste","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"改善性浪费：虽提高价值但实质改变土地性质或用途的行为","contextualMeaning":"改善性浪费：虽提高价值但实质改变土地性质或用途的行为","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Life tenant's duties Duty to pay Life tenant must pay all current charges due during life tenancy (eg, current charges property taxes, mortgage interest) up to financial benefit received from property Duty to prevent Life tenant must prevent affirmative waste (ie, voluntary waste), waste permissive waste & ameliorative waste Duty to make Life tenant must make reasonable repairs to preserve property ordinary repairs T","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"affirmative waste","ipa":"/ʌfˈɝmətɪv wˈeɪst/","syllables":"af·fir·ma·tive waste","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"积极浪费：终身权人主动造成土地重大损害或资源耗损","contextualMeaning":"积极浪费：终身权人主动造成土地重大损害或资源耗损","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Life tenant's duties Duty to pay Life tenant must pay all current charges due during life tenancy (eg, current charges property taxes, mortgage interest) up to financial benefit received from property Duty to prevent Life tenant must prevent affirmative waste (ie, voluntary waste), waste permissive waste & ameliorative waste Duty to make Life tenant must make reasonable repairs to preserve property ordinary repairs T","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"permissive waste","ipa":"/pɝmˈɪsɪv wˈeɪst/","syllables":"per·mis·sive waste","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"消极浪费：终身权人未合理维护、修缮或缴税而使土地受损","contextualMeaning":"消极浪费：终身权人未合理维护、修缮或缴税而使土地受损","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Life tenant's duties Duty to pay Life tenant must pay all current charges due during life tenancy (eg, current charges property taxes, mortgage interest) up to financial benefit received from property Duty to prevent Life tenant must prevent affirmative waste (ie, voluntary waste), waste permissive waste & ameliorative waste Duty to make Life tenant must make reasonable repairs to preserve property ordinary repairs T","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":113,"sourceQuestionPage":331,"sourceQuestionPages":[331],"sourceExplanationPages":[332,333],"sourceAnswerPages":[332,333],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-114","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A worker took a temporary job of unknown duration in a distant state. The worker entered into a written agreement to rent a room in that state on a weekly basis, with the weekly period to start on Sunday and a weekly rent of $350, reflecting a per-day rental rate of $50, to be paid at the end of the week on Saturday. The worker occupied the room and paid the rent for several months before learning on a Tuesday that his job would be finished the following day. That same Tuesday evening, the worker gave an oral notice of termination to the landlord. There is no applicable statute. Assuming that the landlord is unable to find someone else to rent the room, how much rent is the worker obligated to pay?","choices":{"A":"$150, because the worker's notice would terminate the lease immediately.","B":"$350, because the worker's notice would terminate the lease at the end of the current period, on Saturday.","C":"$500, because the worker's notice would terminate the lease seven days after notice was provided, on the following Tuesday.","D":"$700, because the worker's notice would terminate the lease at the end of the next full period, on the following Saturday."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Types of tenancies\n\n(leases on land)\n\nType Creation Duration Termination\n\nTerm of Agreement (ie, lease) Fixed period (eg, End of term years one month, three (automatic) years) Breach of certain covenants (eg, rent, habitability) Periodic Agreement Periodically End of period (proper tenancy Presumed when lease has renews until notice) no specified termination terminated Breach of certain date covenants Operation of law (eg, holdover tenant) Tenancy at Agreement Indefinite period At any time will Implied when person (reasonable notice) allowed to possess premises without paying rent Tenancy at Implied when tenant keeps Continues until Landlord evicts sufferance possession after lease terminated Tenant vacates expires (ie, holdover Landlord accepts rent tenancy) (forms periodic tenancy) A periodic tenancy lasts for a set period of time and then automatically renews at the end of each period (eg, on a weekly basis). Either party can terminate this tenancy at the end of a full period by giving the other party notice before that period begins. This means that notice given during the current period is effective to terminate the tenancy on the last day of the following period. Here, the worker rented the room on a weekly basis (periodic tenancy), with the weekly period to start on Sunday. The worker gave an oral notice of termination to the landlord on a Tuesday evening, which was effective to terminate the worker's tenancy at the end of the next full period—the Saturday of the following week.* As a result, the worker is obligated to pay rent for the current week and the following week ($350 + $350), or a total of $700.\n\n*An oral notice of termination is sufficient under the common law to terminate a periodic tenancy. However, most jurisdictions have enacted statutes that require some form of written notice (not seen here). (Choice A) $150 represents the amount of rent that the worker would have been obligated to pay if his termination notice had ended the tenancy immediately. That is because the worker had occupied the room for the first three days of the week (Sunday through Tuesday), and the per-day portion of the weekly rent is $50. (Choice B) $350 represents the amount of rent the worker would have been obligated to pay if his termination notice had been effective at the end of the current term. (Choice C) $500 represents the amount of rent that the worker would have owed if his termination notice had ended the tenancy after the length of a period. This amount includes the $150 ($50 × 3) for Sunday through Tuesday and $350 for the seven days after notice was given.\n\nEducational objective: Either party can terminate a periodic (eg, week-to-week) tenancy at the end of a full period by giving the other party notice before the period begins. This means that notice given during the current period is effective to terminate the tenancy on the last day of the following period.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (periodic tenancy).","educationalObjective":"Either party can terminate a periodic (eg, week-to-week) tenancy at the end of a full period by giving the other party notice before the period begins. This means that notice given during the current period is effective to terminate the tenancy on the last day of the following period.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (periodic tenancy).","rule":"Either party can terminate a periodic (eg, week-to-week) tenancy at the end of a full period by giving the other party notice before the period begins. This means that notice given during the current period is effective to terminate the tenancy on the last day of the following period.","irac":{"issue":"Assuming that the landlord is unable to find someone else to rent the room, how much rent is the worker obligated to pay?","rule":"Either party can terminate a periodic (eg, week-to-week) tenancy at the end of a full period by giving the other party notice before the period begins. This means that notice given during the current period is effective to terminate the tenancy on the last day of the following period.","analysis":"Types of tenancies\n\n(leases on land)\n\nType Creation Duration Termination\n\nTerm of Agreement (ie, lease) Fixed period (eg, End of term years one month, three (automatic) years) Breach of certain covenants (eg, rent, habitability) Periodic Agreement Periodically End of period (proper tenancy Presumed when lease has renews until notice) no specified termination terminated Breach of certain date covenants Operation of law (eg, holdover tenant) Tenancy at Agreement Indefinite period At any time will Implied when person (reasonable notice) allowed to possess premises without paying rent Tenancy at Implied when tenant keeps Continues until Landlord evicts sufferance possession after lease terminated Tenant vacates expires (ie, holdover Landlord accepts rent tenancy) (forms periodic tenancy) A periodic tenancy lasts for a set period of time and then automatically renews at the end of each period (eg, on a weekly basis). Either party can terminate this tenancy at the end of a full period by giving the other party notice before that period begins. This means that notice given during the current period is effective to terminate the tenancy on the last day of the following period. Here, the worker rented the room on a weekly basis (periodic tenancy), with the weekly period to start on Sunday. The worker gave an oral notice of termination to the landlord on a Tuesday evening, which was effective to terminate the worker's tenancy at the end of the next full period—the Saturday of the following week.* As a result, the worker is obligated to pay rent for the current week and the following week ($350 + $350), or a total of $700.\n\n*An oral notice of termination is sufficient under the common law to terminate a periodic tenancy. However, most jurisdictions have enacted statutes that require some form of written notice (not seen here). (Choice A) $150 represents the amount of rent that the worker would have been obligated to pay if his termination notice had ended the tenancy immediately. That is because the worker had occupied the room for the first three days of the week (Sunday through Tuesday), and the per-day portion of the weekly rent is $50. (Choice B) $350 represents the amount of rent the worker would have been obligated to pay if his termination notice had been effective at the end of the current term. (Choice C) $500 represents the amount of rent that the worker would have owed if his termination notice had ended the tenancy after the length of a period. This amount includes the $150 ($50 × 3) for Sunday through Tuesday and $350 for the seven days after notice was given.","conclusion":"Answer D: $700, because the worker's notice would terminate the lease at the end of the next full period, on the following Saturday."},"questionZh":"一名工人在遥远的州份从事了一份时间不详的临时工作。该工人签订了一份书面协议，在该州按周租用一间房间，每周租期从周日开始，周租金为 350 美元，即日租金为 50 美元，并在周六周末支付。该工人占用了这个房间并支付了几个月的租金，然后在周二得知他的工作将于第二天完成。同周二晚上，这名工人向房东发出了口头解雇通知。没有适用的法规。假设房东无法找到其他人出租房间，工人需要支付多少租金？","choicesZh":{"A":"150 美元，因为工人的通知将立即终止租约。","B":"350 美元，因为工人的通知将在本期结束时即周六终止租约。","C":"500 美元，因为工人的通知将在发出通知后 7 天（下一个星期二）终止租约。","D":"700 美元，因为工人的通知将在下一个完整期限结束时（即下一个星期六）终止租约。"},"explanationZh":"租赁类型\n\n（土地租赁）\n\n类型创建持续时间终止\n\n协议期限（即租赁） 固定期限（例如，期限结束年 一个月、三年（自动）） 违反某些契约（例如，租金、可居住性） 定期协议 定期协议 期限结束（在租赁续签直至收到通知时推定为正当租赁） 无指定终止终止 违反特定日期契约 法律运作（例如，滞留租户） 按协议租赁 无限期 任何时候都暗示当人（合理通知）被允许拥有房产时不支付租金 租客保留时隐含的租赁 继续，直至房东在租约终止后驱逐 忍受占有权 租客腾出到期（即，延期 房东接受租金租赁）（形成定期租赁） 定期租赁持续一段设定的时间，然后在每个期间结束时自动续订（例如，每周）。任何一方都可以在整个期限结束时终止本租赁，并在该期限开始之前通知另一方。这意味着当前期间发出的通知可在下一期间的最后一天终止租赁。在这里，工人按周租用房间（定期租赁），每周租期从周日开始。工人在周二晚上向房东发出口头终止通知，该通知在下一个完整期限（下周的星期六）结束时有效地终止工人的租约。*因此，工人有义务支付本周和下周的租金（350 美元 + 350 美元），即总计 700 美元。\n\n*根据普通法，口头终止通知足以终止定期租赁。然而，大多数司法管辖区已颁布法规，要求某种形式的书面通知（此处未见）。 （选项 A）150 美元代表如果终止通知立即终止租约，工人有义务支付的租金金额。这是因为该工人在一周的前三天（周日至周二）一直占用该房间，而每周租金的每日部分为 50 美元。 （选项 B） 350 美元代表如果终止通知在本期结束时生效，工人有义务支付的租金金额。 （选项 C） 500 美元代表如果工人在一段期限后收到终止通知而终止租约，他将需要支付的租金金额。该金额包括周日至周二的 150 美元（50 美元 × 3）以及发出通知后 7 天内的 350 美元。\n\n教育目标：任何一方都可以在整个租期结束时终止定期（例如，每周）租赁，方法是在租期开始之前向另一方发出通知。这意味着当前期间发出的通知可在下一期间的最后一天终止租赁。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（定期租赁）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"任何一方都可以在整个周期结束时终止定期（例如，每周）租赁，方法是在该周期开始之前向另一方发出通知。这意味着当前期间发出的通知可在下一期间的最后一天终止租赁。","iracZh":{"issue":"假设房东无法找到其他人出租房间，工人需要支付多少租金？","rule":"任何一方都可以在整个周期结束时终止定期（例如，每周）租赁，方法是在该周期开始之前向另一方发出通知。这意味着当前期间发出的通知可在下一期间的最后一天终止租赁。","analysis":"租赁类型\n\n（土地租赁）\n\n类型创建持续时间终止\n\n协议期限（即租赁） 固定期限（例如，期限结束年 一个月、三年（自动）） 违反某些契约（例如，租金、可居住性） 定期协议 定期协议 期限结束（在租赁续签直至收到通知时推定为正当租赁） 无指定终止终止 违反特定日期契约 法律运作（例如，滞留租户） 按协议租赁 无限期 任何时候都暗示当人（合理通知）被允许拥有房产时不支付租金 租客保留时隐含的租赁 继续，直至房东在租约终止后驱逐 忍受占有权 租客腾出到期（即，延期 房东接受租金租赁）（形成定期租赁） 定期租赁持续一段设定的时间，然后在每个期间结束时自动续订（例如，每周）。任何一方都可以在整个期限结束时终止本租赁，并在该期限开始之前通知另一方。这意味着当前期间发出的通知可在下一期间的最后一天终止租赁。在这里，工人按周租用房间（定期租赁），每周租期从周日开始。工人在周二晚上向房东发出口头终止通知，该通知在下一个完整期限（下周的星期六）结束时有效地终止工人的租约。*因此，工人有义务支付本周和下周的租金（350 美元 + 350 美元），即总计 700 美元。\n\n*根据普通法，口头终止通知足以终止定期租赁。然而，大多数司法管辖区已颁布法规，要求某种形式的书面通知（此处未见）。 （选项 A）150 美元代表如果终止通知立即终止租约，工人有义务支付的租金金额。这是因为该工人在一周的前三天（周日至周二）一直占用该房间，而每周租金的每日部分为 50 美元。 （选项 B） 350 美元代表如果终止通知在本期结束时生效，工人有义务支付的租金金额。 （选项 C） 500 美元代表如果工人在一段期限后收到终止通知而终止租约，他将需要支付的租金金额。该金额包括周日至周二的 150 美元（50 美元 × 3）以及发出通知后 7 天内的 350 美元。\n\n教育目标：任何一方都可以在整个租期结束时终止定期（例如，每周）租赁，方法是在租期开始之前向另一方发出通知。这意味着当前期间发出的通知可在下一期间的最后一天终止租赁。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（定期租赁）。","conclusion":"答案 D：700 美元，因为工人的通知将在下一个完整期限结束时（即下一个星期六）终止租约。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":114,"sourceQuestionPage":334,"sourceQuestionPages":[334],"sourceExplanationPages":[335,336],"sourceAnswerPages":[335,336],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-115","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A widow created a testamentary trust to which she devised real property that was rented under a long-term lease. The trust was to pay the income from the trust to the widow's son for life and then accumulate the income from the trust until the son's first-born child reached 30 years of age, at which time the trust property was to be distributed to that child. Neither the will nor the trust contained any specific provision for distribution of the trust property if the son's first-born child were to die before reaching the age of 30. At the time of the widow's death, the widow's son had no children. Subsequently, the son had a child. The son has recently died. His only child is now five years old. The trustee has sought a judicial ruling as to what to do with the trust property. The applicable jurisdiction continues to follow the common law Rule Against Perpetuities but modifies it in accord with the cy pres doctrine. If the court determines that the trust violates the Rule Against Perpetuities, what should the court order the trustee to do?","choices":{"A":"Maintain the trust under its existing terms, because the cy pres doctrine allows the trust to continue under its existing terms despite violating the Rule Against Perpetuities.","B":"Maintain the trust until the child either lives for another 21 years or dies, because the cy pres doctrine allows the trust to be reformed as close as possible to the grantor's intent.","C":"Terminate the trust, because the child's interest in the trust violates the Rule Against Perpetuities and cannot be saved under the cy pres doctrine.","D":"Terminate the trust, because the child's interest in the trust violates the Rule Against Perpetuities and the cy pres doctrine applies only to charitable gifts."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP)\n\nTraditional Renders contingent future interests void if they could vest more than common law rule 21 years after some life in being when interest was created: Contingent remainders Executory interests Vested remainders subject to open Powers of appointment Rights of first refusal Options Reforms \"Wait & see\" stance (most jurisdictions) – otherwise invalid interest is valid if it vests within 90 years of its creation Cy pres doctrine (few jurisdictions) – otherwise invalid interest will be reformed to come \"as close as possible\" to grantor's intent without violating RAP The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) applies to contingent future interests in grantees, where the grantee is unascertained or there is a condition precedent to the interest becoming vested. RAP renders these interests void if they could vest more than 21 years after the end of some life in being when the interest was created. Here, the child's future interest in the trust is subject to RAP because it is contingent upon the child's reaching 30 years of age. And it violates RAP because the 5-year-old child cannot turn 30 within 21 years of the son's death. However, this jurisdiction applies the cy pres doctrine to interests that violate RAP. This doctrine allows a court to make changes to a conveyance to come \"as close as possible\" to the grantor's actual intent without violating RAP (Choice C). Here, the widow did not provide for an alternative taker in the event that the child's interest failed, so her intent was to save the child's interest in the trust if possible. Therefore, the court should change the age contingency and order the trustee to maintain the trust until the child lives for another 21 years (or dies). (Choice A) The cy pres doctrine allows courts to reform a conveyance to prevent it from violating RAP, but it does not allow a trust that violated RAP to continue under its existing terms. (Choice D) While the cy pres doctrine was created by courts to save charitable gifts, it can also be applied when RAP would void a contingent future interest in a noncharitable grantee (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: In a few jurisdictions, the cy pres doctrine allows a court to reform a conveyance to come as close as possible to the grantor's intent while staying within the bounds of the Rule Against Perpetuities—eg, by reducing the time limit for a future interest to vest to 21 years after the end of the last life in being when the interest was created.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Donative Transfers § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1983) (applying cy pres principles to reform interests that would otherwise fail under the Rule Against Perpetuities).","educationalObjective":"In a few jurisdictions, the cy pres doctrine allows a court to reform a conveyance to come as close as possible to the grantor's intent while staying within the bounds of the Rule Against Perpetuities—eg, by reducing the time limit for a future interest to vest to 21 years after the end of the last life in being when the interest was created.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property: Donative Transfers § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1983) (applying cy pres principles to reform interests that would otherwise fail under the Rule Against Perpetuities).","rule":"In a few jurisdictions, the cy pres doctrine allows a court to reform a conveyance to come as close as possible to the grantor's intent while staying within the bounds of the Rule Against Perpetuities—eg, by reducing the time limit for a future interest to vest to 21 years after the end of the last life in being when the interest was created.","irac":{"issue":"If the court determines that the trust violates the Rule Against Perpetuities, what should the court order the trustee to do?","rule":"In a few jurisdictions, the cy pres doctrine allows a court to reform a conveyance to come as close as possible to the grantor's intent while staying within the bounds of the Rule Against Perpetuities—eg, by reducing the time limit for a future interest to vest to 21 years after the end of the last life in being when the interest was created.","analysis":"Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP)\n\nTraditional Renders contingent future interests void if they could vest more than common law rule 21 years after some life in being when interest was created: Contingent remainders Executory interests Vested remainders subject to open Powers of appointment Rights of first refusal Options Reforms \"Wait & see\" stance (most jurisdictions) – otherwise invalid interest is valid if it vests within 90 years of its creation Cy pres doctrine (few jurisdictions) – otherwise invalid interest will be reformed to come \"as close as possible\" to grantor's intent without violating RAP The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) applies to contingent future interests in grantees, where the grantee is unascertained or there is a condition precedent to the interest becoming vested. RAP renders these interests void if they could vest more than 21 years after the end of some life in being when the interest was created. Here, the child's future interest in the trust is subject to RAP because it is contingent upon the child's reaching 30 years of age. And it violates RAP because the 5-year-old child cannot turn 30 within 21 years of the son's death. However, this jurisdiction applies the cy pres doctrine to interests that violate RAP. This doctrine allows a court to make changes to a conveyance to come \"as close as possible\" to the grantor's actual intent without violating RAP (Choice C). Here, the widow did not provide for an alternative taker in the event that the child's interest failed, so her intent was to save the child's interest in the trust if possible. Therefore, the court should change the age contingency and order the trustee to maintain the trust until the child lives for another 21 years (or dies). (Choice A) The cy pres doctrine allows courts to reform a conveyance to prevent it from violating RAP, but it does not allow a trust that violated RAP to continue under its existing terms. (Choice D) While the cy pres doctrine was created by courts to save charitable gifts, it can also be applied when RAP would void a contingent future interest in a noncharitable grantee (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: Maintain the trust until the child either lives for another 21 years or dies, because the cy pres doctrine allows the trust to be reformed as close as possible to the grantor's intent."},"questionZh":"一位寡妇创建了一个遗嘱信托，她为该信托设计了根据长期租约出租的不动产。信托是将信托收入终身支付给寡妇的儿子，然后累积信托收入，直到儿子的第一个孩子年满30岁，届时信托财产将分配给该孩子。如果儿子的第一个孩子在 30 岁之前去世，遗嘱和信托都没有包含任何关于信托财产分配的具体规定。在寡妇去世时，寡妇的儿子没有孩子。后来，儿子有了孩子。儿子最近去世了。他唯一的孩子现在五岁了。受托人已就如何处理信托财产寻求司法裁决。适用的司法管辖区继续遵循普通法的反永久规则，但根据赛普拉斯原则对其进行了修改。如果法院判定信托违反了“反永续规则”，法院应命令受托人做什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"根据现有条款维持信托，因为赛普斯原则允许信托继续根据其现有条款，尽管违反了永久规则。","B":"维持信托直到孩子再活 21 年或去世，因为赛普斯原则允许信托的改革尽可能接近授予人的意图。","C":"终止信托，因为孩子在信托中的权益违反了“反对永久规则”，并且不能根据赛普斯原则得到保存。","D":"终止信托，因为孩子在信托中的权益违反了“反对永久规则”，并且“赛普斯原则”仅适用于慈善捐赠。"},"explanationZh":"反对永续规则 (RAP)\n\n传统 如果未来权益在利息产生后的某个寿命后 21 年内能够归属超过普通法规则，则或有未来权益无效： 或有剩余权益 执行权益 既得剩余权益，受公开任命权 优先购买权 期权改革 “观望”立场（大多数司法管辖区） – 否则，如果权益在其产生后 90 年内归属，则无效权益有效 赛前原则（少数司法管辖区） – 否则无效权益将在未来进行改革在不违反 RAP 的情况下“尽可能接近”授予人的意图 永久收益规则 (RAP) 适用于受让人的或有未来权益，如果受让人不确定或存在权益归属的先决条件。如果这些权益在权益创建时的生命周期结束后超过 21 年才能归属，则 RAP 会使这些权益无效。在这里，孩子在信托中的未来权益受 RAP 约束，因为这取决于孩子是否年满 30 岁。而且它违反了 RAP，因为 5 岁的孩子在儿子去世后 21 年内不能满 30 岁。然而，该司法管辖区将赛普斯原则适用于违反 RAP 的利益。该原则允许法院在不违反 RAP 的情况下对产权转让进行更改，以“尽可能接近”设保人的实际意图（选择 C）。在这里，寡妇没有在孩子的利益失败的情况下提供替代接受者，因此她的目的是在可能的情况下将孩子的利益保留在信托中。因此，法院应更改年龄意外情况，并命令受托人维持信托，直至孩子再活 21 年（或死亡）。 （选项 A） cy pres 原则允许法院改革产权转让以防止其违反 RAP，但不允许违反 RAP 的信托继续按照其现有条款继续存在。 （选项 D）虽然 cy pres 原则是由法院创建的，旨在保存慈善捐赠，但当 RAP 将使非慈善受赠人的或有未来权益无效时，也可以应用该原则（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：在一些司法管辖区，cy​​ pres原则允许法院对产权转让进行改革，以尽可能接近设保人的意图，同时保持在反对永久规则的范围内，例如，通过将未来权益归属的期限缩短至权益创建时最后生命结束后的21年。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二）：捐赠性转让 § 1.5（Am. Law Inst. 1983）（将 cy pres 原则应用于改革利益，否则根据反对永续规则将失败）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在一些司法管辖区，赛普斯原则允许法院改革产权转让，使其尽可能接近设保人的意图，同时又不超出“反对永续规则”的范围，例如，将未来权益归属的期限缩短至权益创建时最后生命周期结束后的 21 年。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院判定信托违反了“反永续规则”，法院应命令受托人做什么？","rule":"在一些司法管辖区，赛普斯原则允许法院改革产权转让，使其尽可能接近设保人的意图，同时又不超出“反对永续规则”的范围，例如，将未来权益归属的期限缩短至权益创建时最后生命周期结束后的 21 年。","analysis":"反对永续规则 (RAP)\n\n传统 如果未来权益在利息产生后的某个寿命后 21 年内能够归属超过普通法规则，则或有未来权益无效： 或有剩余权益 执行权益 既得剩余权益，受公开任命权 优先购买权 期权改革 “观望”立场（大多数司法管辖区） – 否则，如果权益在其产生后 90 年内归属，则无效权益有效 赛前原则（少数司法管辖区） – 否则无效权益将在未来进行改革在不违反 RAP 的情况下“尽可能接近”授予人的意图 永久收益规则 (RAP) 适用于受让人的或有未来权益，如果受让人不确定或存在权益归属的先决条件。如果这些权益在权益创建时的生命周期结束后超过 21 年才能归属，则 RAP 会使这些权益无效。在这里，孩子在信托中的未来权益受 RAP 约束，因为这取决于孩子是否年满 30 岁。而且它违反了 RAP，因为 5 岁的孩子在儿子去世后 21 年内不能满 30 岁。然而，该司法管辖区将赛普斯原则适用于违反 RAP 的利益。该原则允许法院在不违反 RAP 的情况下对产权转让进行更改，以“尽可能接近”设保人的实际意图（选择 C）。在这里，寡妇没有在孩子的利益失败的情况下提供替代接受者，因此她的目的是在可能的情况下将孩子的利益保留在信托中。因此，法院应更改年龄意外情况，并命令受托人维持信托，直至孩子再活 21 年（或死亡）。 （选项 A） cy pres 原则允许法院改革产权转让以防止其违反 RAP，但不允许违反 RAP 的信托继续按照其现有条款继续存在。 （选项 D）虽然 cy pres 原则是由法院创建的，旨在保存慈善捐赠，但当 RAP 将使非慈善受赠人的或有未来权益无效时，也可以应用该原则（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：在一些司法管辖区，cy​​ pres原则允许法院对产权转让进行改革，以尽可能接近设保人的意图，同时保持在反对永久规则的范围内，例如，通过将未来权益归属的期限缩短至权益创建时最后生命结束后的21年。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二）：捐赠性转让 § 1.5（Am. Law Inst. 1983）（将 cy pres 原则应用于改革利益，否则根据反对永续规则将失败）。","conclusion":"答案 B：维持信托直到孩子再活 21 年或去世，因为赛普斯原则允许信托的改革尽可能接近授予人的意图。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":115,"sourceQuestionPage":337,"sourceQuestionPages":[337],"sourceExplanationPages":[338,339],"sourceAnswerPages":[338,339],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-116","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman died testate. In her will, she devised a farm she owned to her husband for life, remainder to her niece. Her will did not specify the duties of the husband and the niece with regard to maintenance and expenses related to the farm. The husband took sole possession of the farm, did not farm the land, and did not rent the land to a third person, although the fair rental value was substantial. For two years in a row after the woman died, the county assessor sent the tax bills to the niece. However, the niece did not pay the bills, because she and the husband could not agree on who should pay them. Finally, the niece paid the taxes to avoid a tax foreclosure sale. The niece then sued the husband for reimbursement for the two years' worth of property taxes. There is no applicable statute. Is the niece likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because remaindermen are solely responsible for the payment of property taxes.","B":"No, because the county assessor sent the bills to the niece.","C":"No, because the woman's will was silent on responsibility for payment of property taxes.","D":"Yes, because the niece paid an obligation that was the sole responsibility of the husband."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Life tenant's duties\n\nDuty to pay Life tenant must pay all current charges due during life tenancy (eg, current charges property taxes, mortgage interest) up to financial benefit received from property\n\nDuty to prevent Life tenant must prevent affirmative waste (ie, voluntary waste), waste permissive waste & ameliorative waste\n\nDuty to make Life tenant must make reasonable repairs to preserve property ordinary repairs\n\nThe holder of a life estate (ie, the life tenant) has a duty to pay current charges—eg, property taxes and mortgage interest—unless the document creating the life estate provides otherwise. This duty is limited to the amount of income that can be generated from the land. If the holder of the remainder (ie, the remainderman) pays the current property charges instead, then the remainderman is entitled to reimbursement from the life tenant. Here, the woman's will granted the husband a life estate but was silent regarding the duty to pay current charges. Therefore, the husband (life tenant) had a duty to pay those charges—including the property taxes—up to the amount of income the farm could generate. Because the farm's fair rental value was substantial, the husband was obligated to pay all the property taxes. But since the niece (remainderman) ultimately paid those taxes, she is entitled to reimbursement from the husband. (Choices A & C) Life tenants—not remaindermen—are solely responsible for all current charges unless the document creating the life estate provides otherwise. Here, the woman's will was silent on the matter, so the property taxes were the husband's sole responsibility. (Choice B) The fact that the county assessor sent the tax bills to the niece has no effect on the husband's obligation to pay or reimburse the property taxes.\n\nEducational objective: Unless the document creating the life estate provides otherwise, a life tenant has the duty to pay all current charges (eg, property taxes, mortgage interest) up to the amount of income that can be generated from the land.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (First) of Property §§ 129–130 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (life tenant's duty to pay current charges).","educationalObjective":"Unless the document creating the life estate provides otherwise, a life tenant has the duty to pay all current charges (eg, property taxes, mortgage interest) up to the amount of income that can be generated from the land.","references":"Restatement (First) of Property §§ 129–130 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (life tenant's duty to pay current charges).","rule":"Unless the document creating the life estate provides otherwise, a life tenant has the duty to pay all current charges (eg, property taxes, mortgage interest) up to the amount of income that can be generated from the land.","irac":{"issue":"Is the niece likely to prevail?","rule":"Unless the document creating the life estate provides otherwise, a life tenant has the duty to pay all current charges (eg, property taxes, mortgage interest) up to the amount of income that can be generated from the land.","analysis":"Life tenant's duties\n\nDuty to pay Life tenant must pay all current charges due during life tenancy (eg, current charges property taxes, mortgage interest) up to financial benefit received from property\n\nDuty to prevent Life tenant must prevent affirmative waste (ie, voluntary waste), waste permissive waste & ameliorative waste\n\nDuty to make Life tenant must make reasonable repairs to preserve property ordinary repairs\n\nThe holder of a life estate (ie, the life tenant) has a duty to pay current charges—eg, property taxes and mortgage interest—unless the document creating the life estate provides otherwise. This duty is limited to the amount of income that can be generated from the land. If the holder of the remainder (ie, the remainderman) pays the current property charges instead, then the remainderman is entitled to reimbursement from the life tenant. Here, the woman's will granted the husband a life estate but was silent regarding the duty to pay current charges. Therefore, the husband (life tenant) had a duty to pay those charges—including the property taxes—up to the amount of income the farm could generate. Because the farm's fair rental value was substantial, the husband was obligated to pay all the property taxes. But since the niece (remainderman) ultimately paid those taxes, she is entitled to reimbursement from the husband. (Choices A & C) Life tenants—not remaindermen—are solely responsible for all current charges unless the document creating the life estate provides otherwise. Here, the woman's will was silent on the matter, so the property taxes were the husband's sole responsibility. (Choice B) The fact that the county assessor sent the tax bills to the niece has no effect on the husband's obligation to pay or reimburse the property taxes.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the niece paid an obligation that was the sole responsibility of the husband."},"questionZh":"一名妇女去世时留下遗嘱。在她的遗嘱中，她设计了一个农场，终身归她丈夫所有，其余部分归她侄女所有。她的遗嘱没有具体说明丈夫和侄女在农场相关维护和费用方面的职责。丈夫独自拥有农场，不耕种土地，也不将土地出租给第三人，尽管公平租金价值很高。女子去世后，县税官连续两年将税单寄给侄女。然而，侄女没有支付账单，因为她和丈夫无法就谁来支付账单达成一致。最后，侄女缴纳了税款，以避免取消抵押品赎回权的税收出售。侄女随后起诉丈夫，要求偿还两年的财产税。没有适用的法规。侄女有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为剩下的人全权负责缴纳财产税。","B":"不，因为县评估员把账单寄给了侄女。","C":"不，因为女方的遗嘱中没有提及缴纳财产税的责任。","D":"是的，因为侄女承担了丈夫独自承担的义务。"},"explanationZh":"终身租客的职责\n\n支付义务 终身租客必须支付终身租赁期间应付的所有当前费用（例如，当前费用、财产税、抵押贷款利息），直至从财产获得的经济利益\n\n防止的责任 终身租户必须防止肯定性浪费（即自愿性浪费）、允许性浪费和改善性浪费\n\n终身租客必须进行合理维修以保护财产的责任 普通维修\n\n终身财产的持有人（即终身租户）有义务支付当前费用，例如财产税和抵押贷款利息，除非创建终身财产的文件另有规定。该税仅限于土地可产生的收入金额。如果剩余的持有人（即剩余的人）改为支付当前的财产费用，则剩余的人有权从终身租户那里获得报销。在这里，妇女的遗嘱授予丈夫终身财产，但没有提及支付当前费用的义务。因此，丈夫（终身佃户）有义务支付这些费用（包括财产税），金额不超过农场可以产生的收入。由于农场的公平租金价值很高，丈夫有义务缴纳所有财产税。但由于侄女（剩余人）最终缴纳了这些税款，因此她有权从丈夫那里获得报销。 （选项 A 和 C）终身租户（而非剩余租户）独自承担所有当前费用，除非创建终身财产的文件另有规定。在这里，女方的遗嘱对此事保持沉默，因此财产税是丈夫的唯一责任。 （选择B） 县估税员将税单寄给侄女这一事实并不影响丈夫缴纳或偿还财产税的义务。\n\n教育目标：除非创建终身财产的文件另有规定，否则终身租户有义务支付所有当前费用（例如财产税、抵押贷款利息），直至土地可产生的收入金额。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）第 §§ 129–130（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（终身租客有义务支付当前费用）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非创建终身财产的文件另有规定，否则终身租户有义务支付所有当前费用（例如财产税、抵押贷款利息），直至土地可产生的收入金额。","iracZh":{"issue":"侄女有可能获胜吗？","rule":"除非创建终身财产的文件另有规定，否则终身租户有义务支付所有当前费用（例如财产税、抵押贷款利息），直至土地可产生的收入金额。","analysis":"终身租客的职责\n\n支付义务 终身租客必须支付终身租赁期间应付的所有当前费用（例如，当前费用、财产税、抵押贷款利息），直至从财产获得的经济利益\n\n防止的责任 终身租户必须防止肯定性浪费（即自愿性浪费）、允许性浪费和改善性浪费\n\n终身租客必须进行合理维修以保护财产的责任 普通维修\n\n终身财产的持有人（即终身租户）有义务支付当前费用，例如财产税和抵押贷款利息，除非创建终身财产的文件另有规定。该税仅限于土地可产生的收入金额。如果剩余的持有人（即剩余的人）改为支付当前的财产费用，则剩余的人有权从终身租户那里获得报销。在这里，妇女的遗嘱授予丈夫终身财产，但没有提及支付当前费用的义务。因此，丈夫（终身佃户）有义务支付这些费用（包括财产税），金额不超过农场可以产生的收入。由于农场的公平租金价值很高，丈夫有义务缴纳所有财产税。但由于侄女（剩余人）最终缴纳了这些税款，因此她有权从丈夫那里获得报销。 （选项 A 和 C）终身租户（而非剩余租户）独自承担所有当前费用，除非创建终身财产的文件另有规定。在这里，女方的遗嘱对此事保持沉默，因此财产税是丈夫的唯一责任。 （选择B） 县估税员将税单寄给侄女这一事实并不影响丈夫缴纳或偿还财产税的义务。\n\n教育目标：除非创建终身财产的文件另有规定，否则终身租户有义务支付所有当前费用（例如财产税、抵押贷款利息），直至土地可产生的收入金额。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）第 §§ 129–130（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（终身租客有义务支付当前费用）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为侄女承担了丈夫独自承担的义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":116,"sourceQuestionPage":340,"sourceQuestionPages":[340],"sourceExplanationPages":[341,342],"sourceAnswerPages":[341,342],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-117","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"Three years ago, a man conveyed a tract of land to a friend for $50,000 by a deed that provided: \"By accepting this deed, [the friend] covenants for herself, her heirs and assigns, that the premises herein conveyed shall be used solely for residential purposes and, if the premises are used for nonresidential purposes, [the man], his heirs and assigns, shall have the right to repurchase the premises for the sum of one thousand dollars ($1,000).\" In order to pay the $50,000 purchase price for land, the friend obtained a $35,000 mortgage loan from the bank. The man had full knowledge of the mortgage transaction. The deed and mortgage were promptly and properly recorded in proper sequence. The mortgage, however, made no reference to the quoted language in the deed. Two years ago, the friend converted her use of the land from residential to commercial without the knowledge or consent of the man or of the bank. The friend's commercial venture failed, and she defaulted on her mortgage payments to the bank. The land now has a fair market value of $25,000. The bank began appropriate foreclosure proceedings against the friend. The man properly intervened, tendered $1,000, and sought judgment that the friend and the bank be ordered to convey the land to the man, free and clear of the mortgage. The common-law Rule Against Perpetuities is unmodified by statute. If the court rules against the man, what will be the reason?","choices":{"A":"The bank had no actual knowledge of, and did not consent to, the violation of the covenant.","B":"The consideration of $1,000 was inadequate.","C":"The provision quoted from the deed violates the Rule Against Perpetuities.","D":"The rights reserved by the man were subordinated, by necessary implication, to the rights of the bank as the lender of the purchase money."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) applies only to contingent future interests—ie, future interests that are held by unknown/unborn persons or subject to a condition precedent. Here, the deed conveyed the friend a present possessory interest in the tract, and the man received an option to purchase the premises contingent upon the friend's use of the premises for nonresidential purposes. Therefore, the man's future interest is subject to RAP. Under the common law, RAP renders a contingent future interest void ab initio if it could vest more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest. Therefore, an option to purchase is almost always void if it is not set to terminate within the perpetuities period. Here, the option to purchase will vest if the friend, her heirs, and assigns ever use the premises for nonresidential purposes. This means that the man's option to purchase violates RAP because it is possible that it could vest well after the perpetuities period. (Choices A & D) Since a deed is recorded before a purchase-money mortgage, the mortgagee's (bank's) rights are subject to whatever rights the mortgagor (friend) holds in the land. Therefore, the bank's mortgage is subordinate to the man's purchase option in the deed—regardless of whether the bank had actual knowledge of or consented to its existence. (Choice B) Adequate consideration exists so long as there is a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. Since the man gave $1,000 in exchange for the friend's promise to give the man a purchase option, his consideration was adequate.\n\nEducational objective: The Rule Against Perpetuities applies only to contingent future interests (eg, purchase options) and voids any such interest that may not vest for more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.\n\nReferences\n61 Am. Jur. 2d Perpetuities and Restraints on Alienation § 5 (2020) (Rule Against Perpetuities).","educationalObjective":"The Rule Against Perpetuities applies only to contingent future interests (eg, purchase options) and voids any such interest that may not vest for more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.","references":"61 Am. Jur. 2d Perpetuities and Restraints on Alienation § 5 (2020) (Rule Against Perpetuities).","rule":"The Rule Against Perpetuities applies only to contingent future interests (eg, purchase options) and voids any such interest that may not vest for more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.","irac":{"issue":"If the court rules against the man, what will be the reason?","rule":"The Rule Against Perpetuities applies only to contingent future interests (eg, purchase options) and voids any such interest that may not vest for more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.","analysis":"The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) applies only to contingent future interests—ie, future interests that are held by unknown/unborn persons or subject to a condition precedent. Here, the deed conveyed the friend a present possessory interest in the tract, and the man received an option to purchase the premises contingent upon the friend's use of the premises for nonresidential purposes. Therefore, the man's future interest is subject to RAP. Under the common law, RAP renders a contingent future interest void ab initio if it could vest more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest. Therefore, an option to purchase is almost always void if it is not set to terminate within the perpetuities period. Here, the option to purchase will vest if the friend, her heirs, and assigns ever use the premises for nonresidential purposes. This means that the man's option to purchase violates RAP because it is possible that it could vest well after the perpetuities period. (Choices A & D) Since a deed is recorded before a purchase-money mortgage, the mortgagee's (bank's) rights are subject to whatever rights the mortgagor (friend) holds in the land. Therefore, the bank's mortgage is subordinate to the man's purchase option in the deed—regardless of whether the bank had actual knowledge of or consented to its existence. (Choice B) Adequate consideration exists so long as there is a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. Since the man gave $1,000 in exchange for the friend's promise to give the man a purchase option, his consideration was adequate.","conclusion":"Answer C: The provision quoted from the deed violates the Rule Against Perpetuities."},"questionZh":"三年前，一名男子以 50,000 美元的价格将一块土地转让给一位朋友，契约中规定：“通过接受这份契约，[朋友] 为她自己、她的继承人和受让人立下契约，所转让的房屋仅用于住宅用途，如果该房屋用于非住宅用途，[该男子]、他的继承人和受让人有权以 1000 美元的价格回购该房屋。 （1,000 美元）。”为了支付5万美元的土地购买价，朋友从银行获得了3.5万美元的抵押贷款。该男子对抵押贷款交易了如指掌。契约和抵押按适当的顺序及时、正确地记录。然而，抵押贷款并未提及契约中引用的语言。两年前，这位朋友在该男子或银行不知情或未同意的情况下，将该土地的用途从住宅转为商业。这位朋友的商业冒险失败了，她拖欠了银行的抵押贷款。该土地目前的公平市场价值为 25,000 美元。银行对这位朋友启动了适当的取消抵押品赎回权程序。该男子进行了适当的干预，支付了 1,000 美元，并寻求判决，命令朋友和银行将土地转让给该男子，且不附带抵押。普通法中的“反对永续规则”未经成文法修改。如果法庭判决该男子败诉，原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该银行实际上并不了解也未同意违反该公约的行为。","B":"1,000 美元的代价是不够的。","C":"契约中引用的条款违反了“反对永久规则”。","D":"必然意味着，该人保留的权利从属于银行作为购买资金贷方的权利。"},"explanationZh":"反对永续规则 (RAP) 仅适用于或有未来权益，即由未知/未出生的人持有或受先决条件约束的未来权益。在这里，该契约让朋友对该地块目前拥有所有权，并且该男子获得了购买该房产的选择权，条件是该朋友将该房产用于非住宅目的。因此，该男子的未来利益取决于RAP。根据普通法，如果或有未来权益可以在权益产生时的相关寿命超过 21 年之后归属，则 RAP 使或有未来权益从一开始就无效。因此，如果购买选择权未在永久期限内终止，则购买选择权几乎总是无效。在这里，如果朋友、她的继承人和受让人曾经将该房产用于非住宅目的，则将授予购买选择权。这意味着该男子的购买选择权违反了 RAP，因为它有可能在永续期之后很久才行权。 （选项 A 和 D）由于契约是在购房款抵押之前记录的，因此抵押权人（银行）的权利受抵押人（朋友）在土地上所拥有的任何权利的约束。因此，银行的抵押贷款从属于该男子在契约中的购买选择权——无论银行是否实际了解或同意其存在。 （选择 B）只要存在讨价还价的承诺或履行交换，就存在充分的对价。由于该男子付出了 1,000 美元来换取朋友承诺给予该男子购买选择权，因此他的考虑是充分的。\n\n教育目标：反对永续权规则仅适用于或有未来权益（例如，购买选择权），并使任何在权益创建时的相关寿命结束后超过 21 年不归属的此类权益无效。\n\n参考文献\n上午 61 点。尤尔。 2d 永久权和转让限制§ 5 (2020)（反对永久权的规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"反对永续权的规则仅适用于或有未来权益（例如，购买选择权），并且使任何在权益创建时的相关寿命结束后超过 21 年才归属的权益无效。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法庭判决该男子败诉，原因是什么？","rule":"反对永续权的规则仅适用于或有未来权益（例如，购买选择权），并且使任何在权益创建时的相关寿命结束后超过 21 年才归属的权益无效。","analysis":"反对永续规则 (RAP) 仅适用于或有未来权益，即由未知/未出生的人持有或受先决条件约束的未来权益。在这里，该契约让朋友对该地块目前拥有所有权，并且该男子获得了购买该房产的选择权，条件是该朋友将该房产用于非住宅目的。因此，该男子的未来利益取决于RAP。根据普通法，如果或有未来权益可以在权益产生时的相关寿命超过 21 年之后归属，则 RAP 使或有未来权益从一开始就无效。因此，如果购买选择权未在永久期限内终止，则购买选择权几乎总是无效。在这里，如果朋友、她的继承人和受让人曾经将该房产用于非住宅目的，则将授予购买选择权。这意味着该男子的购买选择权违反了 RAP，因为它有可能在永续期之后很久才行权。 （选项 A 和 D）由于契约是在购房款抵押之前记录的，因此抵押权人（银行）的权利受抵押人（朋友）在土地上所拥有的任何权利的约束。因此，银行的抵押贷款从属于该男子在契约中的购买选择权——无论银行是否实际了解或同意其存在。 （选择 B）只要存在讨价还价的承诺或履行交换，就存在充分的对价。由于该男子付出了 1,000 美元来换取朋友承诺给予该男子购买选择权，因此他的考虑是充分的。\n\n教育目标：反对永续权规则仅适用于或有未来权益（例如，购买选择权），并使任何在权益创建时的相关寿命结束后超过 21 年不归属的此类权益无效。\n\n参考文献\n上午 61 点。尤尔。 2d 永久权和转让限制§ 5 (2020)（反对永久权的规则）。","conclusion":"答案 C：契约中引用的条款违反了“反对永久规则”。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-d8b11b789c24ad2e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":117,"sourceQuestionPage":343,"sourceQuestionPages":[343],"sourceExplanationPages":[344,345],"sourceAnswerPages":[344,345],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-118","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"In September, a college student signed a one-year written agreement to lease a fully furnished apartment from a landlord. The student paid rent on a timely basis for four months. In December, the student discovered a crack in the kitchen floor after cutting his foot on the broken tile. Although the student notified the landlord of the crack, the landlord failed to repair the floor. Fed up with the landlord, and after being offered a scholarship to study abroad for the spring semester, the student moved to Europe in January and ceased paying rent on the apartment. Upon discovering that the student had abandoned the apartment, the landlord took possession of the apartment and re-rented it to a visiting professor. Based upon these facts, which of the following statements is true?","choices":{"A":"The lease has been terminated, because the apartment was re-rented to a visiting professor.","B":"The lease has been terminated, because the student justifiably abandoned the lease.","C":"The lease has not been terminated, and the student is liable for any future rents.","D":"The lease has not been terminated, because the student was not constructively evicted."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A tenant may justifiably abandon leased property if the landlord's action or inaction results in a constructive eviction. A constructive eviction occurs when the landlord breaches a duty to the tenant (eg, by failing to make a repair) that substantially interferes with the tenant's use and enjoyment of the leased premises. In contrast, a tenant unjustifiably abandons leased property by leaving the property with the intent to terminate the lease without legal justification. Intent is generally evidenced by the tenant's refusal to pay rent. An unjustifiable abandonment is treated as an offer to surrender rights under the lease. If the landlord accepts the offer, the lease is terminated and the tenant is not liable for any future rent. The offer can be accepted by: express agreement (not seen here) or\n\nretaking possession of the property coupled with (1) the landlord's own use of the premises or (2) re-renting the property to a third party.\n\nHere, the student's abandonment was not justified because, unlike a broken heating system or a lack of running water, a cracked tile does not amount to a substantial interference for constructive eviction (Choice B). The student's unjustifiable abandonment of the premises should be treated as an offer to surrender his rights under the lease. The landlord accepted this offer by retaking possession of the apartment and re-renting it to a visiting professor. As a result, the lease has been terminated, and the student is not liable for future rent (Choices C & D).\n\nEducational objective: Unjustifiable abandonment of leased property is treated as an offer to surrender rights under the lease. The landlord can accept the offer by (1) express agreement or (2) retaking possession plus either using the property for the landlord's own use or re-renting it. Acceptance terminates the lease, and the tenant is not liable for future rent.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 12.1(3) (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (discussing the effect of the tenant's abandonment on the obligation to pay rent).\n\n49 Am. Jur. 2d Landlord & Tenant § 606 (2020) (explaining the effect of a landlord's acceptance of a tenant's abandonment).","educationalObjective":"Unjustifiable abandonment of leased property is treated as an offer to surrender rights under the lease. The landlord can accept the offer by (1) express agreement or (2) retaking possession plus either using the property for the landlord's own use or re-renting it. Acceptance terminates the lease, and the tenant is not liable for future rent.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 12.1(3) (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (discussing the effect of the tenant's abandonment on the obligation to pay rent).\n\n49 Am. Jur. 2d Landlord & Tenant § 606 (2020) (explaining the effect of a landlord's acceptance of a tenant's abandonment).","rule":"Unjustifiable abandonment of leased property is treated as an offer to surrender rights under the lease. The landlord can accept the offer by (1) express agreement or (2) retaking possession plus either using the property for the landlord's own use or re-renting it. Acceptance terminates the lease, and the tenant is not liable for future rent.","irac":{"issue":"Based upon these facts, which of the following statements is true?","rule":"Unjustifiable abandonment of leased property is treated as an offer to surrender rights under the lease. The landlord can accept the offer by (1) express agreement or (2) retaking possession plus either using the property for the landlord's own use or re-renting it. Acceptance terminates the lease, and the tenant is not liable for future rent.","analysis":"A tenant may justifiably abandon leased property if the landlord's action or inaction results in a constructive eviction. A constructive eviction occurs when the landlord breaches a duty to the tenant (eg, by failing to make a repair) that substantially interferes with the tenant's use and enjoyment of the leased premises. In contrast, a tenant unjustifiably abandons leased property by leaving the property with the intent to terminate the lease without legal justification. Intent is generally evidenced by the tenant's refusal to pay rent. An unjustifiable abandonment is treated as an offer to surrender rights under the lease. If the landlord accepts the offer, the lease is terminated and the tenant is not liable for any future rent. The offer can be accepted by: express agreement (not seen here) or\n\nretaking possession of the property coupled with (1) the landlord's own use of the premises or (2) re-renting the property to a third party.\n\nHere, the student's abandonment was not justified because, unlike a broken heating system or a lack of running water, a cracked tile does not amount to a substantial interference for constructive eviction (Choice B). The student's unjustifiable abandonment of the premises should be treated as an offer to surrender his rights under the lease. The landlord accepted this offer by retaking possession of the apartment and re-renting it to a visiting professor. As a result, the lease has been terminated, and the student is not liable for future rent (Choices C & D).","conclusion":"Answer A: The lease has been terminated, because the apartment was re-rented to a visiting professor."},"questionZh":"9月，一名大学生签署了一份为期一年的书面协议，从房东那里租了一套设施齐全的公寓。该学生按时缴纳了四个月的房租。 12月，这名学生在破碎的瓷砖上割伤了脚，发现厨房地板上有一条裂缝。尽管学生将裂缝问题通知了房东，但房东却未能修复地板。该学生厌倦了房东，并在获得春季学期出国留学的奖学金后，于一月份搬到了欧洲，并停止支付公寓租金。在发现学生放弃了公寓后，房东接管了该公寓，并将其重新出租给了一位客座教授。根据这些事实，以下哪项陈述是正确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"租约已终止，因为该公寓已重新租给一位客座教授。","B":"租约已终止，因为学生合理地放弃了租约。","C":"租约尚未终止，学生需承担未来的任何租金。","D":"租约尚未终止，因为该学生并未被推定驱逐。"},"explanationZh":"如果房东的作为或不作为导致推定驱逐，租户可以合理地放弃租赁财产。当房东违反对租户的义务（例如，未进行维修），严重干扰租户对租赁房屋的使用和享受时，就会发生推定驱逐。相比之下，承租人在没有法律依据的情况下，出于终止租赁的目的而离开租赁财产，从而无理放弃租赁财产。租户拒绝支付租金通常可以证明其意图。无理放弃被视为放弃租赁权利的要约。如果房东接受该要约，则租约终止，租户无需承担任何未来的租金。该要约可以通过以下方式接受：明确协议（此处未显示）或\n\n重新取得财产的占有，加上 (1) 房东自己使用该房产或 (2) 将财产重新出租给第三方。\n\n在这里，学生的遗弃是不合理的，因为与损坏的供暖系统或缺乏自来水不同，破裂的瓷砖并不构成建设性驱逐的实质性干扰（选择 B）。学生无理放弃房屋的行为应被视为放弃其租约权利的要约。房东接受了这一提议，重新收回了公寓的所有权，并将其重新出租给一位客座教授。因此，租约已终止，学生无需承担未来的租金（选择 C ​​和 D）。\n\n教育目标：不合理地放弃租赁财产被视为放弃租赁权利的要约。房东可以通过以下方式接受要约：(1) 明确同意或 (2) 重新占有该房产并自用或重新出租。验收即终止租约，租户不承担未来租金的责任。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户 § 12.1(3)（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（讨论租户放弃对支付租金义务的影响）。\n\n49 上午。尤尔。 2d Landlord & Tenant § 606 (2020)（解释房东接受租户放弃的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"无理放弃租赁财产被视为放弃租赁权利的要约。房东可以通过以下方式接受要约：(1) 明确同意或 (2) 重新占有该房产并自用或重新出租。验收即终止租约，租户不承担未来租金的责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"根据这些事实，以下哪项陈述是正确的？","rule":"无理放弃租赁财产被视为放弃租赁权利的要约。房东可以通过以下方式接受要约：(1) 明确同意或 (2) 重新占有该房产并自用或重新出租。验收即终止租约，租户不承担未来租金的责任。","analysis":"如果房东的作为或不作为导致推定驱逐，租户可以合理地放弃租赁财产。当房东违反对租户的义务（例如，未进行维修），严重干扰租户对租赁房屋的使用和享受时，就会发生推定驱逐。相比之下，承租人在没有法律依据的情况下，出于终止租赁的目的而离开租赁财产，从而无理放弃租赁财产。租户拒绝支付租金通常可以证明其意图。无理放弃被视为放弃租赁权利的要约。如果房东接受该要约，则租约终止，租户无需承担任何未来的租金。该要约可以通过以下方式接受：明确协议（此处未显示）或\n\n重新取得财产的占有，加上 (1) 房东自己使用该房产或 (2) 将财产重新出租给第三方。\n\n在这里，学生的遗弃是不合理的，因为与损坏的供暖系统或缺乏自来水不同，破裂的瓷砖并不构成建设性驱逐的实质性干扰（选择 B）。学生无理放弃房屋的行为应被视为放弃其租约权利的要约。房东接受了这一提议，重新收回了公寓的所有权，并将其重新出租给一位客座教授。因此，租约已终止，学生无需承担未来的租金（选择 C ​​和 D）。\n\n教育目标：不合理地放弃租赁财产被视为放弃租赁权利的要约。房东可以通过以下方式接受要约：(1) 明确同意或 (2) 重新占有该房产并自用或重新出租。验收即终止租约，租户不承担未来租金的责任。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户 § 12.1(3)（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（讨论租户放弃对支付租金义务的影响）。\n\n49 上午。尤尔。 2d Landlord & Tenant § 606 (2020)（解释房东接受租户放弃的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 A：租约已终止，因为该公寓已重新租给一位客座教授。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-0f7c532764017293"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":118,"sourceQuestionPage":346,"sourceQuestionPages":[346],"sourceExplanationPages":[347,348],"sourceAnswerPages":[347,348],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-119","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A son loaned his father a large sum of money. The father signed a 10-year promissory note and secured the note with a mortgage on his farm. The mortgage was promptly recorded. The next year, the father died intestate. The father's heirs were the son and his two sisters. The sisters first learned of the mortgage just after their father died. The sisters now assert that the son, their brother, holds the mortgage for their benefit as well as for himself because as a tenant in common with his sisters, he has a fiduciary duty toward them. There are no applicable statutes. Are the sisters correct?","choices":{"A":"No, because the mortgage was granted before the tenancy in common was created.","B":"No, because the three siblings took the farm as tenants in common rather than as joint tenants with right of survivorship.","C":"Yes, because a fiduciary relationship exists among tenants in common.","D":"Yes, because their three interests are equal."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Cotenants generally do not share a fiduciary relationship unless they became cotenants at the same time (eg, by the same deed or will, by inheritance from a common ancestor). Additionally, this fiduciary relationship exists only with respect to transactions involving co-owned property that take place during the tenancy (Choice C). Here, the son's mortgage on the farm was granted before the siblings inherited the farm from their father as tenants in common. As a result, the son does not have a fiduciary duty toward his sisters with respect to the mortgage, and the sisters are incorrect that he holds the mortgage for their benefit as well as for himself. (Choice B) The son would hold the mortgage solely for his own benefit even if the siblings had taken the farm as joint tenants with right of survivorship. (Choice D) The three siblings have equal interests in the farm because this is presumed when cotenants inherit property by intestate succession. However, this fact has no effect on the outcome of their dispute.\n\nEducational objective: Cotenants share a fiduciary relationship if they become cotenants at the same time. But this fiduciary relationship exists only with respect to transactions that occur during the tenancy.","educationalObjective":"Cotenants share a fiduciary relationship if they become cotenants at the same time. But this fiduciary relationship exists only with respect to transactions that occur during the tenancy.","references":"","rule":"Cotenants share a fiduciary relationship if they become cotenants at the same time. But this fiduciary relationship exists only with respect to transactions that occur during the tenancy.","irac":{"issue":"Are the sisters correct?","rule":"Cotenants share a fiduciary relationship if they become cotenants at the same time. But this fiduciary relationship exists only with respect to transactions that occur during the tenancy.","analysis":"Cotenants generally do not share a fiduciary relationship unless they became cotenants at the same time (eg, by the same deed or will, by inheritance from a common ancestor). Additionally, this fiduciary relationship exists only with respect to transactions involving co-owned property that take place during the tenancy (Choice C). Here, the son's mortgage on the farm was granted before the siblings inherited the farm from their father as tenants in common. As a result, the son does not have a fiduciary duty toward his sisters with respect to the mortgage, and the sisters are incorrect that he holds the mortgage for their benefit as well as for himself. (Choice B) The son would hold the mortgage solely for his own benefit even if the siblings had taken the farm as joint tenants with right of survivorship. (Choice D) The three siblings have equal interests in the farm because this is presumed when cotenants inherit property by intestate succession. However, this fact has no effect on the outcome of their dispute.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the mortgage was granted before the tenancy in common was created."},"questionZh":"一个儿子借给父亲一大笔钱。父亲签署了一份十年期本票，并以他的农场抵押作为该票据的担保。抵押贷款立即被记录。第二年，父亲去世时没有留下遗嘱。父亲的继承人是儿子和他的两个姐妹。姐妹俩第一次得知抵押贷款是在她们父亲去世后不久。姐妹们现在声称，儿子（她们的兄弟）为了她们的利益以及他自己的利益而持有抵押贷款，因为作为与姐妹们共同的租户，他对她们负有信托义务。没有适用的法规。姐妹们说得对吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为抵押贷款是在共同共有（joint tenancy）建立之前授予的。","B":"不，因为三个兄弟姐妹将农场作为共同佃户而不是具有生存权的联名佃户。","C":"是的，因为按份共有人（tenant in common）之间存在信托关系。","D":"是的，因为他们的三个利益是平等的。"},"explanationZh":"共同承租人通常不具有信托关系，除非他们同时成为共同承租人（例如，通过相同的契约或遗嘱，通过共同祖先的继承）。此外，这种信托关系仅存在于租赁期间发生的涉及共同拥有财产的交易（选项 C）。在这里，在兄弟姐妹作为共同佃户从父亲那里继承农场之前，儿子的农场抵押贷款就被授予了。因此，儿子在抵押贷款方面对他的姐妹不负有信托义务，而姐妹们错误地认为他为了她们和自己的利益而持有抵押贷款。 （选择B）即使兄弟姐妹以联权共有者的身份取得了农场并拥有生存权，儿子也只会为了自己的利益而持有抵押贷款。 （选项 D） 三个兄弟姐妹在农场中拥有平等的利益，因为当共同承租人通过无遗嘱继承继承财产时，这是假定的。然而，这一事实并没有影响他们争论的结果。\n\n教育目标：如果按份共有人（tenant in common）同时成为按份共有人（tenant in common），则共同享有信托关系。但这种信托关系仅存在于租赁期间发生的交易。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果共同承租人同时成为共同承租人，则他们共享信托关系。但这种信托关系仅存在于租赁期间发生的交易。","iracZh":{"issue":"姐妹们说得对吗？","rule":"如果共同承租人同时成为共同承租人，则他们共享信托关系。但这种信托关系仅存在于租赁期间发生的交易。","analysis":"共同承租人通常不具有信托关系，除非他们同时成为共同承租人（例如，通过相同的契约或遗嘱，通过共同祖先的继承）。此外，这种信托关系仅存在于租赁期间发生的涉及共同拥有财产的交易（选项 C）。在这里，在兄弟姐妹作为共同佃户从父亲那里继承农场之前，儿子的农场抵押贷款就被授予了。因此，儿子在抵押贷款方面对他的姐妹不负有信托义务，而姐妹们错误地认为他为了她们和自己的利益而持有抵押贷款。 （选择B）即使兄弟姐妹以联权共有者的身份取得了农场并拥有生存权，儿子也只会为了自己的利益而持有抵押贷款。 （选项 D） 三个兄弟姐妹在农场中拥有平等的利益，因为当共同承租人通过无遗嘱继承继承财产时，这是假定的。然而，这一事实并没有影响他们争论的结果。\n\n教育目标：如果按份共有人（tenant in common）同时成为按份共有人（tenant in common），则共同享有信托关系。但这种信托关系仅存在于租赁期间发生的交易。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为抵押贷款是在共同共有（joint tenancy）建立之前授予的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-a4a76ea1d8a30654"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":119,"sourceQuestionPage":349,"sourceQuestionPages":[349],"sourceExplanationPages":[350],"sourceAnswerPages":[350],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-120","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man owned a parcel of land in fee simple. Five years ago, he conveyed the parcel by deed \"to my daughter until she graduates from college, and then to my son in fee simple if he turns 21.\" The daughter has just graduated from college. The son is only 18 years old. Who is entitled to possession of the land?","choices":{"A":"The daughter, because she is entitled to possession until her interest is divested by the son's shifting executory interest.","B":"The man, because his fee simple interest has not yet been divested by the son's shifting executory interest.","C":"The man, because his fee simple interest has not yet been divested by the son's springing executory interest.","D":"The son, because his remainder vested when the daughter graduated from college."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nA defeasible fee simple estate is an ownership interest that may last for an indeterminate duration but is limited by specific durational or conditional language (see table above). There are three types of defeasible fees: Fee simple determinable (FSD) – terminates automatically upon the happening of the stated event, then ownership returns to the grantor\n\nFee simple subject to a condition subsequent (FSSCS) – upon the occurrence of a specific condition, the grantor has the right to terminate the estate\n\nFee simple subject to an executory limitation (FSSEL) – upon the occurrence of the stated event or condition, title passes to a third party (ie, someone other than the grantor or the holder of the present fee)\n\nHere, the man conveyed the parcel to his daughter until she graduated from college. This durational language (\"until\") means that the daughter received an FSD or FSSEL. The deed then states that, upon her college graduation, the parcel will go to the son if he turns 21. Since title will pass to the son (third party), the daughter received an FSSEL and the son holds an executory interest. There are two types of executory interests: Shifting – divests the grantee's estate, such that the estate shifts from the grantee to the executory-interest holder\n\nSpringing – divests the grantor's estate, such that the estate springs from the grantor to the executory-interest holder\n\nHere, the daughter's interest was divested when she graduated from college (Choice A). But since the son is not yet 21, the estate reverted back to the man (grantor).* This means that the man currently holds an FSD in the parcel—and is therefore entitled to possession—until the son meets the condition of turning 21. Once he turns 21, the parcel will spring from the man to the son, who holds a springing executory interest (Choices B & D). *Had the son been at least 21 when the daughter graduated from college, the estate would have shifted (not sprung) to the son upon her graduation.\n\nEducational objective: A shifting executory interest arises when title shifts from the grantee to a third party, while a springing executory interest arises when title springs from the grantor to a third party.","educationalObjective":"A shifting executory interest arises when title shifts from the grantee to a third party, while a springing executory interest arises when title springs from the grantor to a third party.","references":"","rule":"A shifting executory interest arises when title shifts from the grantee to a third party, while a springing executory interest arises when title springs from the grantor to a third party.","irac":{"issue":"Who is entitled to possession of the land?","rule":"A shifting executory interest arises when title shifts from the grantee to a third party, while a springing executory interest arises when title springs from the grantor to a third party.","analysis":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nA defeasible fee simple estate is an ownership interest that may last for an indeterminate duration but is limited by specific durational or conditional language (see table above). There are three types of defeasible fees: Fee simple determinable (FSD) – terminates automatically upon the happening of the stated event, then ownership returns to the grantor\n\nFee simple subject to a condition subsequent (FSSCS) – upon the occurrence of a specific condition, the grantor has the right to terminate the estate\n\nFee simple subject to an executory limitation (FSSEL) – upon the occurrence of the stated event or condition, title passes to a third party (ie, someone other than the grantor or the holder of the present fee)\n\nHere, the man conveyed the parcel to his daughter until she graduated from college. This durational language (\"until\") means that the daughter received an FSD or FSSEL. The deed then states that, upon her college graduation, the parcel will go to the son if he turns 21. Since title will pass to the son (third party), the daughter received an FSSEL and the son holds an executory interest. There are two types of executory interests: Shifting – divests the grantee's estate, such that the estate shifts from the grantee to the executory-interest holder\n\nSpringing – divests the grantor's estate, such that the estate springs from the grantor to the executory-interest holder\n\nHere, the daughter's interest was divested when she graduated from college (Choice A). But since the son is not yet 21, the estate reverted back to the man (grantor).* This means that the man currently holds an FSD in the parcel—and is therefore entitled to possession—until the son meets the condition of turning 21. Once he turns 21, the parcel will spring from the man to the son, who holds a springing executory interest (Choices B & D). *Had the son been at least 21 when the daughter graduated from college, the estate would have shifted (not sprung) to the son upon her graduation.","conclusion":"Answer C: The man, because his fee simple interest has not yet been divested by the son's springing executory interest."},"questionZh":"一个人以简单的费用拥有一块土地。五年前，他通过契约将包裹“转让给我的女儿，直到她大学毕业，然后在我儿子年满 21 岁时，以简单的费用转让给我的儿子”。女儿刚刚大学毕业。儿子才18岁。谁有权占有土地？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿，因为她有权占有，直到她的权益被儿子不断变化的执行权益剥夺为止。","B":"该男子，因为他的费用单利尚未被儿子转移的执行权益剥离。","C":"这名男子，因为他的单利利息尚未被儿子不断增加的执行利息剥夺。","D":"儿子，因为他的剩余财产是在女儿大学毕业时归属的。"},"explanationZh":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n可废止的费用简单遗产是一种所有权权益，可以持续不确定的期限，但受到特定期限或条件语言的限制（见上表）。可废止的费用分为三种类型： 简单可确定费用 (FSD) – 在所述事件发生时自动终止，然后所有权返回授予人\n\n费用简单，受后续条件限制 (FSSCS) – 在发生特定条件时，授予人有权终止遗产\n\n受执行限制的费用简单 (FSSEL) – 在发生所述事件或条件时，所有权转移给第三方（即除现有费用的授予人或持有人之外的其他人）\n\n在这里，男子将包裹转交给女儿，直到她大学毕业。这种持续性语言（“直到”）意味着女儿收到了 FSD 或 FSSEL。该契约还规定，大学毕业后，如果儿子年满 21 岁，包裹将归她所有。由于所有权将转移给儿子（第三方），因此女儿收到了 FSSEL，而儿子则拥有执行权益。执行权益有两种类型： 转移 – 剥离受让人的遗产，使遗产从受让人转移到执行权益持有人\n\n涌现——剥离设保人的遗产，使遗产从设保人转移至执行权益持有人\n\n在这里，女儿的兴趣在大学毕业后就被剥夺了（选择A）。但由于儿子尚未满 21 岁，遗产将返还给男子（授予人）。*这意味着该男子目前持有该地块中的 FSD，因此有权占有，直到儿子满足 21 岁的条件。一旦他年满 21 岁，该地块将从男子手中转移到儿子手中，而儿子则持有新的执行权益（Choices B & D）。 *如果女儿大学毕业时儿子年满 21 岁，那么遗产将在她毕业时转移（而不是突然转移）给儿子。\n\n教育目标：当所有权从受让人转移到第三方时，就会产生转移的执行权益，而当所有权从授予人转移到第三方时，就会产生执行权益。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当所有权从受让人转移到第三方时，就会产生转移的执行权益，而当所有权从设保人转移到第三方时，就会产生新兴的执行权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有权占有土地？","rule":"当所有权从受让人转移到第三方时，就会产生转移的执行权益，而当所有权从设保人转移到第三方时，就会产生新兴的执行权益。","analysis":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n可废止的费用简单遗产是一种所有权权益，可以持续不确定的期限，但受到特定期限或条件语言的限制（见上表）。可废止的费用分为三种类型： 简单可确定费用 (FSD) – 在所述事件发生时自动终止，然后所有权返回授予人\n\n费用简单，受后续条件限制 (FSSCS) – 在发生特定条件时，授予人有权终止遗产\n\n受执行限制的费用简单 (FSSEL) – 在发生所述事件或条件时，所有权转移给第三方（即除现有费用的授予人或持有人之外的其他人）\n\n在这里，男子将包裹转交给女儿，直到她大学毕业。这种持续性语言（“直到”）意味着女儿收到了 FSD 或 FSSEL。该契约还规定，大学毕业后，如果儿子年满 21 岁，包裹将归她所有。由于所有权将转移给儿子（第三方），因此女儿收到了 FSSEL，而儿子则拥有执行权益。执行权益有两种类型： 转移 – 剥离受让人的遗产，使遗产从受让人转移到执行权益持有人\n\n涌现——剥离设保人的遗产，使遗产从设保人转移至执行权益持有人\n\n在这里，女儿的兴趣在大学毕业后就被剥夺了（选择A）。但由于儿子尚未满 21 岁，遗产将返还给男子（授予人）。*这意味着该男子目前持有该地块中的 FSD，因此有权占有，直到儿子满足 21 岁的条件。一旦他年满 21 岁，该地块将从男子手中转移到儿子手中，而儿子则持有新的执行权益（Choices B & D）。 *如果女儿大学毕业时儿子年满 21 岁，那么遗产将在她毕业时转移（而不是突然转移）给儿子。\n\n教育目标：当所有权从受让人转移到第三方时，就会产生转移的执行权益，而当所有权从授予人转移到第三方时，就会产生执行权益。","conclusion":"答案 C：这名男子，因为他的单利利息尚未被儿子不断增加的执行利息剥夺。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":120,"sourceQuestionPage":351,"sourceQuestionPages":[351],"sourceExplanationPages":[352,353],"sourceAnswerPages":[352,353],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-121","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"An aunt owned a commercial property consisting of a one-story building rented to various retail stores and a large parking lot. Two years ago, the aunt died and left the property to her nephew for life, with remainder to her godson, his heirs and assigns. The devise of the property was made subject to any mortgage on the property in effect at the time of the aunt's death. A year before her death, the aunt mortgaged the property to secure a loan of $150,000. The entire principal of the mortgage remained outstanding when she died. As a result, the net annual income from the property was reduced not only by real estate taxes and regular maintenance costs, but also by the substantial mortgage interest payments due each month. A commercial real estate developer recently proposed to purchase the property and pay off the mortgage in order to demolish the existing building and construct a 30-story office building. The nephew was willing to accept the developer's offer. However, the godson refused the offer even though he, as the remainderman, paid the principal portion of each monthly mortgage payment. The godson wanted to convert the property into a public park when he became entitled to possession. When the developer realized that the godson would not change his mind, it modified its proposal to a purchase of the nephew's life estate. When the godson learned that the nephew had agreed to the developer's modified proposal, the godson brought an appropriate action against them to enjoin their carrying it out. What is the godson's best argument?","choices":{"A":"Continued existence of the one-story building is more in harmony with the ultimate use as a park than the proposed change in use.","B":"The developer cannot purchase the nephew's life estate, because life estates are not assignable.","C":"The godson's payment of the mortgage principal has subrogated him to the nephew's rights as a life tenant and bars the nephew's assignment of the life estate without the godson's consent.","D":"The proposed demolition of the building constitutes waste."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Life tenant's duties\n\nDuty to pay Life tenant must pay all current charges due during life tenancy (eg, current charges property taxes, mortgage interest) up to financial benefit received from property\n\nDuty to prevent Life tenant must prevent affirmative waste (ie, voluntary waste), waste permissive waste & ameliorative waste\n\nDuty to make Life tenant must make reasonable repairs to preserve property ordinary repairs\n\nA life estate is a present possessory interest that terminates on the death of an individual. This interest is freely transferable (Choice B). But since it is followed by a future interest that will become possessory once the life estate terminates, the life tenant has a duty to prevent waste. There are three types of waste: Affirmative waste – changes that decrease the property's value\n\nPermissive waste – deterioration caused by neglect or the failure to reasonably protect the property\n\nAmeliorative waste – changes that increase the property's value but are not necessary to make reasonable use of the property amid substantial and permanent changes in the local area\n\nThe future interest holder (the godson) may inspect the land for waste and may seek damages or an injunction to prevent waste. This ensures that the life tenant (the nephew) delivers the property to the future interest holder in essentially the same condition it was in when the life tenant took possession. Here, the demolition of the building would change the condition of the property. Although the construction of a 30-story office building would likely increase the property value, there is no indication that this change is necessary. Therefore, the godson's best argument is that the proposed demolition constitutes waste. (Choice A) A life tenant can use the property in any manner that does not constitute waste—even if that use affects the future interest holder's plans. Therefore, the godson's plan to use the property as a park is irrelevant. (Choice C) The future interest holder is responsible for the mortgage principal, but this does not bar the life tenant from assigning the life estate or allow the future interest holder to subrogate the life tenant's rights. Instead, subrogation allows a third party who fully\n\npays off a senior mortgage with no knowledge of junior interests to take that senior mortgage's priority.\n\nEducational objective: A life tenant has a duty to prevent waste so that the property can be delivered in essentially the same condition it was in when the life tenant took possession.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (First) of Property § 140 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (life tenant's duty to prevent waste).","educationalObjective":"A life tenant has a duty to prevent waste so that the property can be delivered in essentially the same condition it was in when the life tenant took possession.","references":"Restatement (First) of Property § 140 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (life tenant's duty to prevent waste).","rule":"A life tenant has a duty to prevent waste so that the property can be delivered in essentially the same condition it was in when the life tenant took possession.","irac":{"issue":"What is the godson's best argument?","rule":"A life tenant has a duty to prevent waste so that the property can be delivered in essentially the same condition it was in when the life tenant took possession.","analysis":"Life tenant's duties\n\nDuty to pay Life tenant must pay all current charges due during life tenancy (eg, current charges property taxes, mortgage interest) up to financial benefit received from property\n\nDuty to prevent Life tenant must prevent affirmative waste (ie, voluntary waste), waste permissive waste & ameliorative waste\n\nDuty to make Life tenant must make reasonable repairs to preserve property ordinary repairs\n\nA life estate is a present possessory interest that terminates on the death of an individual. This interest is freely transferable (Choice B). But since it is followed by a future interest that will become possessory once the life estate terminates, the life tenant has a duty to prevent waste. There are three types of waste: Affirmative waste – changes that decrease the property's value\n\nPermissive waste – deterioration caused by neglect or the failure to reasonably protect the property\n\nAmeliorative waste – changes that increase the property's value but are not necessary to make reasonable use of the property amid substantial and permanent changes in the local area\n\nThe future interest holder (the godson) may inspect the land for waste and may seek damages or an injunction to prevent waste. This ensures that the life tenant (the nephew) delivers the property to the future interest holder in essentially the same condition it was in when the life tenant took possession. Here, the demolition of the building would change the condition of the property. Although the construction of a 30-story office building would likely increase the property value, there is no indication that this change is necessary. Therefore, the godson's best argument is that the proposed demolition constitutes waste. (Choice A) A life tenant can use the property in any manner that does not constitute waste—even if that use affects the future interest holder's plans. Therefore, the godson's plan to use the property as a park is irrelevant. (Choice C) The future interest holder is responsible for the mortgage principal, but this does not bar the life tenant from assigning the life estate or allow the future interest holder to subrogate the life tenant's rights. Instead, subrogation allows a third party who fully\n\npays off a senior mortgage with no knowledge of junior interests to take that senior mortgage's priority.","conclusion":"Answer D: The proposed demolition of the building constitutes waste."},"questionZh":"一位阿姨拥有一处商业地产，包括一栋出租给各个零售店的单层建筑和一个大型停车场。两年前，姨妈去世，将财产留给了她的侄子终身，其余的则留给了她的教子、他的继承人和受让人。该财产的设计受到姑妈去世时有效的财产抵押的约束。在她去世前一年，阿姨抵押了房产以获得 15 万美元的贷款。当她去世时，抵押贷款的全部本金仍未偿还。结果，房产的年净收入不仅因房地产税和定期维护成本而减少，还因每月到期的大量抵押贷款利息而减少。近日，一家商业地产开发商提出购买该房产并偿还抵押贷款，以拆除现有建筑并建造一栋30层的办公楼。侄子愿意接受开发商的报价。然而，教子拒绝了这一提议，尽管他作为剩余人支付了每月抵押贷款的主要部分。教子想在获得占有权后将这处房产改建成公园。当开发商意识到教子不会改变主意时，他们修改了提案，改为购买侄子的终身财产。当教子得知侄子同意了开发商的修改提案后，教子对他们采取了适当的行动，禁止他们执行。教子最好的论据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"与提议的用途改变相比，单层建筑的继续存在更符合公园的最终用途。","B":"开发商不能购买侄子的终身财产，因为终身财产不可转让。","C":"教子支付抵押贷款本金后，他取代了侄子作为终身租户的权利，并禁止侄子在未经教子同意的情况下转让终身财产。","D":"拟议的拆除建筑物构成废物。"},"explanationZh":"终身租客的职责\n\n支付义务 终身租客必须支付终身租赁期间应付的所有当前费用（例如，当前费用、财产税、抵押贷款利息），直至从财产获得的经济利益\n\n防止的责任 终身租户必须防止肯定性浪费（即自愿性浪费）、允许性浪费和改善性浪费\n\n终身租客必须进行合理维修以保护财产的责任 普通维修\n\n终身财产是目前的占有权益，在个人死亡时终止。该利息可自由转让（选择 B）。但由于一旦终身财产终止，随之而来的未来权益将成为占有，因此终身租户有责任防止浪费。浪费分为三种类型： 肯定性浪费 – 降低财产价值的变化\n\n允许的浪费——由于疏忽或未能合理保护财产而造成的恶化\n\n改善性浪费——增加财产价值的改变，但在当地发生重大和永久性变化的情况下，对于合理利用财产来说不是必需的\n\n未来的利益持有人（教子）可以检查土地是否浪费，并可能寻求损害赔偿或禁止浪费的禁令。这确保了终身租户（侄子）将财产交付给未来的利益持有人时，其状况与终身租户占有时的状况基本相同。在这里，建筑物的拆除将改变财产的状况。尽管建造 30 层办公楼可能会增加房产价值，但没有迹象表明这种改变是必要的。因此，教子的最佳论据是，拟议的拆除构成浪费。 （选择 A）终身租户可以以不构成浪费的任何方式使用该财产，即使该使用会影响未来权益持有人的计划。因此，教子计划将该房产用作公园的计划是无关紧要的。 （选项C）未来利息持有人对抵押本金负责，但这并不禁止终身租户分配终身财产或允许未来利息持有人代位终身租户的权利。相反，代位权允许第三方完全\n\n在不了解次级权益的情况下还清了高级抵押贷款，以优先考虑该高级抵押贷款。\n\n教育目标：终身租客有责任防止浪费，以便财产能够以与终身租客占有时基本相同的状态交付。\n\n参考文献\n《财产重述（第一）》第 140 条（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（终身租户防止浪费的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"终身租客有责任防止浪费，以便财产能够以与终身租客占有时基本相同的状态交付。","iracZh":{"issue":"教子最好的论据是什么？","rule":"终身租客有责任防止浪费，以便财产能够以与终身租客占有时基本相同的状态交付。","analysis":"终身租客的职责\n\n支付义务 终身租客必须支付终身租赁期间应付的所有当前费用（例如，当前费用、财产税、抵押贷款利息），直至从财产获得的经济利益\n\n防止的责任 终身租户必须防止肯定性浪费（即自愿性浪费）、允许性浪费和改善性浪费\n\n终身租客必须进行合理维修以保护财产的责任 普通维修\n\n终身财产是目前的占有权益，在个人死亡时终止。该利息可自由转让（选择 B）。但由于一旦终身财产终止，随之而来的未来权益将成为占有，因此终身租户有责任防止浪费。浪费分为三种类型： 肯定性浪费 – 降低财产价值的变化\n\n允许的浪费——由于疏忽或未能合理保护财产而造成的恶化\n\n改善性浪费——增加财产价值的改变，但在当地发生重大和永久性变化的情况下，对于合理利用财产来说不是必需的\n\n未来的利益持有人（教子）可以检查土地是否浪费，并可能寻求损害赔偿或禁止浪费的禁令。这确保了终身租户（侄子）将财产交付给未来的利益持有人时，其状况与终身租户占有时的状况基本相同。在这里，建筑物的拆除将改变财产的状况。尽管建造 30 层办公楼可能会增加房产价值，但没有迹象表明这种改变是必要的。因此，教子的最佳论据是，拟议的拆除构成浪费。 （选择 A）终身租户可以以不构成浪费的任何方式使用该财产，即使该使用会影响未来权益持有人的计划。因此，教子计划将该房产用作公园的计划是无关紧要的。 （选项C）未来利息持有人对抵押本金负责，但这并不禁止终身租户分配终身财产或允许未来利息持有人代位终身租户的权利。相反，代位权允许第三方完全\n\n在不了解次级权益的情况下还清了高级抵押贷款，以优先考虑该高级抵押贷款。\n\n教育目标：终身租客有责任防止浪费，以便财产能够以与终身租客占有时基本相同的状态交付。\n\n参考文献\n《财产重述（第一）》第 140 条（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（终身租户防止浪费的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 D：拟议的拆除建筑物构成废物。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"harmony","ipa":"/hˈɑɹməni/","syllables":"har·mony","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 协调, 和睦, 调和；[电] 和声学","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“harmony”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:harmonies","sourceSentence":"Continued existence of the one-story building is more in harmony with the ultimate use as a park than the proposed change in use.","synonyms":["harmoniousness","musical harmony","concord","concordance"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":121,"sourceQuestionPage":354,"sourceQuestionPages":[354],"sourceExplanationPages":[355,356],"sourceAnswerPages":[355,356],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-122","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A testator owned a tract of land in fee simple. The testator wrote and executed, with the required formalities, a will that devised the land to \"my daughter for life with remainder to my descendants per stirpes.\" At the time of writing the will, the testator had a husband and no descendants living other than her two children, the daughter named in the will and a son. The testator died and the will was duly admitted to probate. The testator's husband predeceased her, but she was survived by her daughter, her son, four grandchildren, and one great-grandchild. The testator's children were her sole heirs at law. The testator's children brought an appropriate action for declaratory judgment as to title of the land. Guardians ad litem were appointed and all other steps were taken so that the judgment would bind all persons interested. In that action, the court ruled that the daughter has a life estate in the whole of the land and that the remainder is contingent. The court must have chosen a construction that did which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Implied a condition that remaindermen survive the daughter.","B":"Implied a gift of a life estate to the son.","C":"Related all vesting to the death of the testator.","D":"Related all vesting to the time of writing of the will."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Vested remainders v. Contingent remainders\n\nDefinition Examples\n\nVested Remainder not subject to condition precedent & \"... remainder to A\" held by identifiable living person\n\nContingent Remainder subject to condition precedent or \"... remainder to A, but only held by unknown / unborn person if A outlives B\" \"... remainder to my heirs\" A remainder is a future interest in real property that is capable of becoming possessory upon the expiration of a life estate or term of years. Remainders are either: vested – not subject to any condition precedent AND held by an identifiable living person or\n\ncontingent – subject to some condition precedent (other than the natural termination of the prior estate) OR held by an unknown or unborn person.\n\nHere, the testator wrote a will that devised a life estate to her daughter and a remainder to her descendants. After the testator's death, the court ruled that that the remainder was contingent—not vested. This means that the court cannot have chosen a construction (ie, interpretation) of the testator's will that related all vesting to: the time of the writing of the will – in which case, the testator's descendants who were alive and identifiable when she wrote the will (her two children) would have had vested remainders (Choice D) or\n\nthe death of the testator – in which case, the testator's descendants who were alive and identifiable when she died (her children, grandchildren, and great-grandchild) would have had vested remainders (Choice C).\n\nInstead, the court must have chosen a construction of the testator's will that implied a condition that remaindermen survive the daughter. In that case, the remainder would be contingent because the testator's descendants who will survive the daughter and eventually take possession of the land are still unknown. (Choice B) There is no indication that the court implied a life estate to the son. And even if it did, this would not create a contingent remainder.\n\nEducational objective: A vested remainder is not subject to any condition precedent and is held by an identifiable living person, whereas a contingent remainder is either subject to a condition precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 157 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing the types of remainders).","educationalObjective":"A vested remainder is not subject to any condition precedent and is held by an identifiable living person, whereas a contingent remainder is either subject to a condition precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person.","references":"Restatement of Property § 157 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing the types of remainders).","rule":"A vested remainder is not subject to any condition precedent and is held by an identifiable living person, whereas a contingent remainder is either subject to a condition precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person.","irac":{"issue":"The court must have chosen a construction that did which of the following?","rule":"A vested remainder is not subject to any condition precedent and is held by an identifiable living person, whereas a contingent remainder is either subject to a condition precedent or held by an unknown or unborn person.","analysis":"Vested remainders v. Contingent remainders\n\nDefinition Examples\n\nVested Remainder not subject to condition precedent & \"... remainder to A\" held by identifiable living person\n\nContingent Remainder subject to condition precedent or \"... remainder to A, but only held by unknown / unborn person if A outlives B\" \"... remainder to my heirs\" A remainder is a future interest in real property that is capable of becoming possessory upon the expiration of a life estate or term of years. Remainders are either: vested – not subject to any condition precedent AND held by an identifiable living person or\n\ncontingent – subject to some condition precedent (other than the natural termination of the prior estate) OR held by an unknown or unborn person.\n\nHere, the testator wrote a will that devised a life estate to her daughter and a remainder to her descendants. After the testator's death, the court ruled that that the remainder was contingent—not vested. This means that the court cannot have chosen a construction (ie, interpretation) of the testator's will that related all vesting to: the time of the writing of the will – in which case, the testator's descendants who were alive and identifiable when she wrote the will (her two children) would have had vested remainders (Choice D) or\n\nthe death of the testator – in which case, the testator's descendants who were alive and identifiable when she died (her children, grandchildren, and great-grandchild) would have had vested remainders (Choice C).\n\nInstead, the court must have chosen a construction of the testator's will that implied a condition that remaindermen survive the daughter. In that case, the remainder would be contingent because the testator's descendants who will survive the daughter and eventually take possession of the land are still unknown. (Choice B) There is no indication that the court implied a life estate to the son. And even if it did, this would not create a contingent remainder.","conclusion":"Answer A: Implied a condition that remaindermen survive the daughter."},"questionZh":"立遗嘱人以简单的费用拥有一块土地。立遗嘱人按照规定的手续撰写并执行了一份遗嘱，将这片土地设计为“我的女儿终身拥有，其余的则归我的后代所有”。在撰写遗嘱时，遗嘱人有一个丈夫，除了她的两个孩子（遗嘱中指定的女儿和一个儿子）外，没有任何后代。立遗嘱人去世，遗嘱正式接受遗嘱认证。立遗嘱人的丈夫先于她去世，但她身后留下了女儿、儿子、四个孙子和一个曾孙。立遗嘱人的孩子是她唯一的法律继承人。立遗嘱人的子女提起适当的诉讼，要求对土地所有权进行宣告性判决。指定了诉讼监护人并采取了所有其他步骤，以便判决对所有利害关系人具有约束力。在那起诉讼中，法院裁定女儿拥有整块土地的终身财产，其余部分是或有的。法院一定选择了具有以下哪项功能的结构？","choicesZh":{"A":"暗示了一个条件，即剩下的人比女儿还活着。","B":"意味着将终身财产赠予儿子。","C":"将所有归属与遗嘱人的死亡相关联。","D":"将所有归属与遗嘱书写时间相关联。"},"explanationZh":"既得剩余物 v. 或有剩余物\n\n定义示例\n\n既得剩余部分不受先决条件约束且“...剩余部分归A”由可识别的在世人士持有\n\n或有剩余物受先决条件限制或“...剩余物归A，但仅由未知/未出生的人持有，如果A比B更长寿”“...剩余物归我的继承人”剩余物是不动产的未来权益，能够在终身财产或年限到期时成为占有人。剩余部分是： 既得——不受任何先决条件的约束并且由可识别的在世人士持有，或者\n\n或有 – 受某些先决条件限制（先前遗产的自然终止除外）或由未知或未出生的人持有。\n\n在这里，遗嘱人写了一份遗嘱，为她的女儿制定了终身财产，并将剩余财产留给了她的后代。遗嘱人去世后，法院裁定剩余部分是偶然的，而不是既得的。这意味着法院不能选择与所有归属相关的立遗嘱人遗嘱的解释（即解释）： 撰写遗嘱的时间 - 在这种情况下，立遗嘱人在撰写遗嘱时活着且可识别的后代（她的两个孩子）将拥有归属的剩余部分（选择 D）或\n\n立遗嘱人死亡——在这种情况下，立遗嘱人死时仍活着且可辨认的后代（她的孩子、孙子和曾孙）将拥有既得的剩余财产（选项C）。\n\n相反，法院必须选择对立遗嘱人遗嘱的解释，该解释暗示了剩余人比女儿存活的条件。在这种情况下，剩余的部分将是偶然的，因为遗嘱人的后代将在女儿之后幸存并最终占有土地仍然未知。 （选项B） 没有迹象表明法院暗示儿子拥有终身财产。即使确实如此，也不会产生或有剩余。\n\n教育目标：既定剩余部分不受任何先决条件限制，并且由可识别的在世人持有，而或有剩余部分要么受先决条件限制，要么由未知或未出生的人持有。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 157（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论剩余物的类型）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"既得剩余部分不受任何先决条件的约束，并且由可识别的在世人持有，而或有剩余部分要么受先决条件约束，要么由未知或未出生的人持有。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院一定选择了具有以下哪项功能的结构？","rule":"既得剩余部分不受任何先决条件的约束，并且由可识别的在世人持有，而或有剩余部分要么受先决条件约束，要么由未知或未出生的人持有。","analysis":"既得剩余物 v. 或有剩余物\n\n定义示例\n\n既得剩余部分不受先决条件约束且“...剩余部分归A”由可识别的在世人士持有\n\n或有剩余物受先决条件限制或“...剩余物归A，但仅由未知/未出生的人持有，如果A比B更长寿”“...剩余物归我的继承人”剩余物是不动产的未来权益，能够在终身财产或年限到期时成为占有人。剩余部分是： 既得——不受任何先决条件的约束并且由可识别的在世人士持有，或者\n\n或有 – 受某些先决条件限制（先前遗产的自然终止除外）或由未知或未出生的人持有。\n\n在这里，遗嘱人写了一份遗嘱，为她的女儿制定了终身财产，并将剩余财产留给了她的后代。遗嘱人去世后，法院裁定剩余部分是偶然的，而不是既得的。这意味着法院不能选择与所有归属相关的立遗嘱人遗嘱的解释（即解释）： 撰写遗嘱的时间 - 在这种情况下，立遗嘱人在撰写遗嘱时活着且可识别的后代（她的两个孩子）将拥有归属的剩余部分（选择 D）或\n\n立遗嘱人死亡——在这种情况下，立遗嘱人死时仍活着且可辨认的后代（她的孩子、孙子和曾孙）将拥有既得的剩余财产（选项C）。\n\n相反，法院必须选择对立遗嘱人遗嘱的解释，该解释暗示了剩余人比女儿存活的条件。在这种情况下，剩余的部分将是偶然的，因为遗嘱人的后代将在女儿之后幸存并最终占有土地仍然未知。 （选项B） 没有迹象表明法院暗示儿子拥有终身财产。即使确实如此，也不会产生或有剩余。\n\n教育目标：既定剩余部分不受任何先决条件限制，并且由可识别的在世人持有，而或有剩余部分要么受先决条件限制，要么由未知或未出生的人持有。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 157（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论剩余物的类型）。","conclusion":"答案 A：暗示了一个条件，即剩下的人比女儿还活着。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":122,"sourceQuestionPage":357,"sourceQuestionPages":[357],"sourceExplanationPages":[358,359],"sourceAnswerPages":[358,359],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-123","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A landowner died, validly devising his land to his wife \"for life or until remarriage, then to\" their daughter. Shortly after the landowner's death, his daughter executed an instrument in the proper form of a deed, purporting to convey the land to her friend. A year later, the daughter died intestate, with her mother, the original landowner's wife, as her sole heir. The following month, the wife remarried. The wife then executed an instrument in the proper form of a deed, purporting to convey the land to her new husband as a wedding gift. Who now owns what interest in the land?","choices":{"A":"The daughter's friend owns the fee simple.","B":"The wife owns the fee simple.","C":"The wife's new husband has a life estate in the land for the wife's life, with the remainder in the daughter's friend.","D":"The wife's new husband owns the fee simple."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A defeasible life estate is a present interest that terminates upon the death of an individual OR the happening of a stated event. The future-interest holder (ie, remainderman) acquires possession of the estate when the death or event occurs. Future interests are alienable—ie, the remainderman can freely convey that interest. Here, the landowner devised a defeasible life estate in the land to his wife, which would pass to their daughter upon the wife's death or remarriage. Therefore, the daughter had a future interest that she properly conveyed to a friend, making the friend the new remainderman. When the wife remarried (and terminated her life estate), the land automatically transferred to the remainderman—the friend—in fee simple. (Choices B & D) The wife had a defeasible life estate—not a fee simple—that ended when she remarried. As a result, the wife could only acquire a fee simple (or convey it to her new husband) if she inherited the daughter's future interest. Since the daughter did not hold the future interest when she died (she had already conveyed it to her friend), that interest never passed to the wife. Therefore, neither the wife nor the new husband owns the fee simple. (Choice C) Since the wife's life estate terminated as soon as she remarried, she did not have a property interest to deed to the new husband. As a result, the new husband does not have an interest in the land.\n\nEducational objective: A future interest that follows a defeasible life estate becomes possessory upon the death of an individual or the happening of a stated event. Future interests are alienable, meaning that the future-interest holder (ie, remainderman) can freely convey his/her interest.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Wills & Other Donative Transfers § 24.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (life estates).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Wills & Other Donative Transfers § 25.2 (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (reversions and remainders).","educationalObjective":"A future interest that follows a defeasible life estate becomes possessory upon the death of an individual or the happening of a stated event. Future interests are alienable, meaning that the future-interest holder (ie, remainderman) can freely convey his/her interest.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Wills & Other Donative Transfers § 24.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (life estates).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Wills & Other Donative Transfers § 25.2 (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (reversions and remainders).","rule":"A future interest that follows a defeasible life estate becomes possessory upon the death of an individual or the happening of a stated event. Future interests are alienable, meaning that the future-interest holder (ie, remainderman) can freely convey his/her interest.","irac":{"issue":"Who now owns what interest in the land?","rule":"A future interest that follows a defeasible life estate becomes possessory upon the death of an individual or the happening of a stated event. Future interests are alienable, meaning that the future-interest holder (ie, remainderman) can freely convey his/her interest.","analysis":"A defeasible life estate is a present interest that terminates upon the death of an individual OR the happening of a stated event. The future-interest holder (ie, remainderman) acquires possession of the estate when the death or event occurs. Future interests are alienable—ie, the remainderman can freely convey that interest. Here, the landowner devised a defeasible life estate in the land to his wife, which would pass to their daughter upon the wife's death or remarriage. Therefore, the daughter had a future interest that she properly conveyed to a friend, making the friend the new remainderman. When the wife remarried (and terminated her life estate), the land automatically transferred to the remainderman—the friend—in fee simple. (Choices B & D) The wife had a defeasible life estate—not a fee simple—that ended when she remarried. As a result, the wife could only acquire a fee simple (or convey it to her new husband) if she inherited the daughter's future interest. Since the daughter did not hold the future interest when she died (she had already conveyed it to her friend), that interest never passed to the wife. Therefore, neither the wife nor the new husband owns the fee simple. (Choice C) Since the wife's life estate terminated as soon as she remarried, she did not have a property interest to deed to the new husband. As a result, the new husband does not have an interest in the land.","conclusion":"Answer A: The daughter's friend owns the fee simple."},"questionZh":"一位地主去世后，有效地将他的土地划给他的妻子“终身或直到再婚，然后划给”他们的女儿。地主去世后不久，他的女儿以适当的契约形式签署了一份文书，旨在将土地转让给她的朋友。一年后，女儿去世时没有留下遗嘱，她的母亲（原地主的妻子）是她唯一的继承人。次月，妻子再婚。然后，妻子以适当的契约形式签署了一份文书，旨在将土地转让给她的新丈夫作为结婚礼物。现在谁拥有这块土地的什么权益？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿的朋友拥有简单的费用。","B":"妻子拥有简单的费用。","C":"妻子的新丈夫在妻子的一生中拥有土地上的终身财产，其余的则归女儿的朋友所有。","D":"妻子的新丈夫拥有简单的费用。"},"explanationZh":"可废止的终身财产是指在个人死亡或发生规定事件时终止的现有权益。当死亡或事件发生时，未来权益持有人（即剩余人）获得遗产的占有。未来的利益是可以让渡的——也就是说，剩下的人可以自由地表达这种利益。在这里，地主为他的妻子设计了一份可废除的终身财产，在妻子去世或再婚时，该财产将转移给他们的女儿。因此，女儿有一个未来的利益，她适当地传达给朋友，使朋友成为新的剩余人。当妻子再婚（并终止她的终身财产）时，土地自动转移给剩下的人——朋友——费用简单。 (Choices B & D) 妻子拥有可废止的终身财产——不仅仅是一笔费用——在她再婚时就终止了。因此，如果妻子继承了女儿未来的权益，她只能获得一笔简单的费用（或转嫁给她的新丈夫）。由于女儿去世时并不持有未来利息（她已经将其转嫁给了她的朋友），因此该利息从未传递给妻子。因此，无论是妻子还是新丈夫都没有简单地拥有这笔费用。 （选项C） 由于妻子再婚后她的终身财产就终止了，所以她没有财产权益可以转让给新丈夫。因此，新丈夫对土地没有兴趣。\n\n教育目标：可废止的生命财产之后的未来权益在个人死亡或发生规定事件时成为占有人。未来权益是可转让的，这意味着未来权益持有人（即剩余人）可以自由转让他/她的权益。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转让 § 24.5（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（终身财产）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转移 § 25.2（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（恢复和剩余）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当个人死亡或发生指定事件时，可废止的终身财产所产生的未来权益将成为占有人。未来权益是可转让的，这意味着未来权益持有人（即剩余人）可以自由转让他/她的权益。","iracZh":{"issue":"现在谁拥有这块土地的什么权益？","rule":"当个人死亡或发生指定事件时，可废止的终身财产所产生的未来权益将成为占有人。未来权益是可转让的，这意味着未来权益持有人（即剩余人）可以自由转让他/她的权益。","analysis":"可废止的终身财产是指在个人死亡或发生规定事件时终止的现有权益。当死亡或事件发生时，未来权益持有人（即剩余人）获得遗产的占有。未来的利益是可以让渡的——也就是说，剩下的人可以自由地表达这种利益。在这里，地主为他的妻子设计了一份可废除的终身财产，在妻子去世或再婚时，该财产将转移给他们的女儿。因此，女儿有一个未来的利益，她适当地传达给朋友，使朋友成为新的剩余人。当妻子再婚（并终止她的终身财产）时，土地自动转移给剩下的人——朋友——费用简单。 (Choices B & D) 妻子拥有可废止的终身财产——不仅仅是一笔费用——在她再婚时就终止了。因此，如果妻子继承了女儿未来的权益，她只能获得一笔简单的费用（或转嫁给她的新丈夫）。由于女儿去世时并不持有未来利息（她已经将其转嫁给了她的朋友），因此该利息从未传递给妻子。因此，无论是妻子还是新丈夫都没有简单地拥有这笔费用。 （选项C） 由于妻子再婚后她的终身财产就终止了，所以她没有财产权益可以转让给新丈夫。因此，新丈夫对土地没有兴趣。\n\n教育目标：可废止的生命财产之后的未来权益在个人死亡或发生规定事件时成为占有人。未来权益是可转让的，这意味着未来权益持有人（即剩余人）可以自由转让他/她的权益。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转让 § 24.5（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（终身财产）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转移 § 25.2（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（恢复和剩余）。","conclusion":"答案 A：女儿的朋友拥有简单的费用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-c30d01b8b3fbc52a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":123,"sourceQuestionPage":360,"sourceQuestionPages":[360],"sourceExplanationPages":[361,362],"sourceAnswerPages":[361,362],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-124","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"An elderly woman had two children, a son and daughter. In the months before the woman's death, the son had lived in the woman's home and cared for her. Although the daughter had provided no assistance to the woman, both children inherited the woman's home upon her death and became tenants in common. The son continued to live in the woman's home and paid all taxes, insurance, and other carrying charges on the property. The daughter never visited the property and did not seek rent or other compensation from the son until they got into a heated dispute 15 years later. The son has filed an action to quiet title and claims that he owns the property in fee simple. The period of time to acquire title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 10 years. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The daughter, because one co-tenant cannot acquire title by adverse possession against another.","B":"The daughter, because there is no evidence that the son has ousted the daughter.","C":"The son, because he has acquired title in fee simple by adverse possession.","D":"The son, because the parties' acts indicate the daughter's intention to renounce her inheritance."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Adverse possession allows a person to acquire title to another's property when his/her possession of the land is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the land Nonpermissive – hostile and adverse to the owner But for a co-tenant to acquire sole ownership of co-owned property through adverse possession, he/she must also oust the other co-tenants. This is done by preventing them from using or accessing the property (eg, changing the locks, constructing a fence, physical removal) (Choice A). Here, the son (co-tenant) lived alone on the property and paid all taxes, insurance, and other carrying charges for more than the 10-year statutory period. But since he took no action to prevent the daughter (other co-tenant) from using the property, he did not oust her. Therefore, he never acquired sole ownership by adverse possession, and the daughter will likely prevail (Choice C). (Choice D) An inheritance can only be renounced if the recipient unequivocally rejects the inheritance within a reasonable time after learning of it. Actions that merely indicate an intent to renounce are not sufficient. Therefore, the parties' actions—absent an explicit rejection—did not eliminate the daughter's right to the inherited property.\n\nEducational objective: A co-tenant can acquire sole ownership of co-owned property through adverse possession if he/she ousts the other co-tenants by preventing them from using or accessing the property (and meets all other requirements for adverse possession).","educationalObjective":"A co-tenant can acquire sole ownership of co-owned property through adverse possession if he/she ousts the other co-tenants by preventing them from using or accessing the property (and meets all other requirements for adverse possession).","references":"","rule":"A co-tenant can acquire sole ownership of co-owned property through adverse possession if he/she ousts the other co-tenants by preventing them from using or accessing the property (and meets all other requirements for adverse possession).","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"A co-tenant can acquire sole ownership of co-owned property through adverse possession if he/she ousts the other co-tenants by preventing them from using or accessing the property (and meets all other requirements for adverse possession).","analysis":"Adverse possession allows a person to acquire title to another's property when his/her possession of the land is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Exclusive – not shared with the owner Actual – physical presence on the land Nonpermissive – hostile and adverse to the owner But for a co-tenant to acquire sole ownership of co-owned property through adverse possession, he/she must also oust the other co-tenants. This is done by preventing them from using or accessing the property (eg, changing the locks, constructing a fence, physical removal) (Choice A). Here, the son (co-tenant) lived alone on the property and paid all taxes, insurance, and other carrying charges for more than the 10-year statutory period. But since he took no action to prevent the daughter (other co-tenant) from using the property, he did not oust her. Therefore, he never acquired sole ownership by adverse possession, and the daughter will likely prevail (Choice C). (Choice D) An inheritance can only be renounced if the recipient unequivocally rejects the inheritance within a reasonable time after learning of it. Actions that merely indicate an intent to renounce are not sufficient. Therefore, the parties' actions—absent an explicit rejection—did not eliminate the daughter's right to the inherited property.","conclusion":"Answer B: The daughter, because there is no evidence that the son has ousted the daughter."},"questionZh":"一位老妇人有两个孩子，一儿一女。在该妇女去世前的几个月里，儿子一直住在该妇女的家里并照顾她。尽管女儿没有向该妇女提供任何帮助，但两个孩子在该妇女去世后继承了该妇女的房屋，并成为共同房客。儿子继续住在女人的家里，并支付所有税款、保险和其他财产费用。女儿从未去过该房产，也没有向儿子索要租金或其他补偿，直到15年后他们发生了激烈的争执。儿子已提起诉讼，要求平息所有权，并声称他拥有该房产的所有权。在管辖区内通过逆权占有取得所有权的期限为10年。谁有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿，因为一个按份共有人（tenant in common）不能通过逆权占有另一人来获得所有权。","B":"女儿，因为没有证据表明儿子驱逐了女儿。","C":"儿子，因为他通过逆权管有简单地获得了所有权。","D":"儿子，因为双方的行为表明女儿打算放弃继承权。"},"explanationZh":"逆权侵占允许一个人在以下情况下获得他人财产的所有权： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者而言明显或可见 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 排他 – 不与所有者共享 实际 – 实际存在于土地上 不允许 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 但对于按份共有人（tenant in common）来说，通过逆权占有获得共有财产的唯一所有权，他/她还必须驱逐其他按份共有人（tenant in common）。这是通过阻止他们使用或访问财产（例如，更换锁、建造围栏、物理拆除）来完成的（选择 A）。在这里，儿子（按份共有人（tenant in common））独自居住在该房产上，并支付了超过 10 年法定期限的所有税款、保险和其他携带费用。但由于他没有采取任何行动阻止女儿（另一位按份共有人（tenant in common））使用该房产，因此他没有驱逐她。因此，他从未通过逆权占有获得单独所有权，女儿很可能获胜（选项C）。 （选项D） 只有接受者在得知继承权后的合理时间内明确拒绝继承权，才可以放弃继承权。仅仅表明放弃意图的行动是不够的。因此，双方的行为——没有明确拒绝——并没有消除女儿对继承财产的权利。\n\n教育目标：如果按份共有人（tenant in common）通过阻止其他按份共有人（tenant in common）使用或访问该财产（并满足逆权占有的所有其他要求）来驱逐其他按份共有人（tenant in common），则他/她可以通过逆权占有获得共同拥有的财产的唯一所有权。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果按份共有人（tenant in common）通过阻止其他按份共有人（tenant in common）使用或访问该财产（并满足逆权占有的所有其他要求）来驱逐其他按份共有人（tenant in common），则他/她可以通过逆权占有获得共同拥有的财产的唯一所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"如果按份共有人（tenant in common）通过阻止其他按份共有人（tenant in common）使用或访问该财产（并满足逆权占有的所有其他要求）来驱逐其他按份共有人（tenant in common），则他/她可以通过逆权占有获得共同拥有的财产的唯一所有权。","analysis":"逆权侵占允许一个人在以下情况下获得他人财产的所有权： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者而言明显或可见 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 排他 – 不与所有者共享 实际 – 实际存在于土地上 不允许 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 但对于按份共有人（tenant in common）来说，通过逆权占有获得共有财产的唯一所有权，他/她还必须驱逐其他按份共有人（tenant in common）。这是通过阻止他们使用或访问财产（例如，更换锁、建造围栏、物理拆除）来完成的（选择 A）。在这里，儿子（按份共有人（tenant in common））独自居住在该房产上，并支付了超过 10 年法定期限的所有税款、保险和其他携带费用。但由于他没有采取任何行动阻止女儿（另一位按份共有人（tenant in common））使用该房产，因此他没有驱逐她。因此，他从未通过逆权占有获得单独所有权，女儿很可能获胜（选项C）。 （选项D） 只有接受者在得知继承权后的合理时间内明确拒绝继承权，才可以放弃继承权。仅仅表明放弃意图的行动是不够的。因此，双方的行为——没有明确拒绝——并没有消除女儿对继承财产的权利。\n\n教育目标：如果按份共有人（tenant in common）通过阻止其他按份共有人（tenant in common）使用或访问该财产（并满足逆权占有的所有其他要求）来驱逐其他按份共有人（tenant in common），则他/她可以通过逆权占有获得共同拥有的财产的唯一所有权。","conclusion":"答案 B：女儿，因为没有证据表明儿子驱逐了女儿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-9d7764152ffc5428"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":124,"sourceQuestionPage":363,"sourceQuestionPages":[363],"sourceExplanationPages":[364,365],"sourceAnswerPages":[364,365],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-125","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A real estate developer owned a 100-acre tract of land. Six years ago, the real estate developer leased a one-acre lot located in the northeasterly corner of the tract for a term of 30 years to a fast-food company. The fast-food company intended to, and did, construct a fast-food restaurant on the one-acre lot. The lease provided that: The fast-food company was to maintain the lot and improvements thereon, to maintain full insurance coverage on the lot, and to pay all taxes assessed against the lot.\n\nThe real estate developer was to maintain the access roads and the parking lot areas platted on those portions of the tract that adjoined the lot and to permit the company's customers to use them in common with the customers of the other commercial users of the remainder of the tract.\n\nThe fast-food company was to pay its share of the expenses for the off-site improvements according to a stated formula.\n\nFive years ago, the real estate developer sold the one-acre lot to an investor; the conveyance was made subject to the lease to the fast-food company. However, the investor did not assume the obligations of the lease and the real estate developer retained the remainder of the 100-acre tract of land. Since that conveyance five years ago, the fast-food company has paid rent to the investor. However, the fast-food company refused to pay its formula share of the off-site improvement costs as provided in the lease. The real estate developer brought an appropriate action against the fast-food company to recover such costs. Is the real estate developer likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the conveyance of the one-acre lot to the investor terminated the privity of estate between the real estate developer and the fast-food company.","B":"No, because the investor, as the fast-food company's landlord, has the obligation to pay the maintenance costs by necessary implication.","C":"Yes, because the conveyance of the one-acre lot to the investor did not terminate the real estate developer's right to enforce the covenant to contribute.","D":"Yes, because the use of the improvements by the customers of the fast-food company imposes an implied obligation on the fast-food company."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A landlord may generally transfer his/her property interest to a third party (ie, assignee- landlord) without the tenant's consent. However, the assignee-landlord only has the right to enforce a covenant (ie, promise) in the lease if it runs with the land. Otherwise, the original landlord retains the right to enforce it. A covenant runs with the land when: the original parties intended to bind successors in interest (eg, assignee-landlord)\n\nthe covenant touches and concerns (ie, affects the use or value of) the land and\n\nthe transfer brings the assignee-landlord into privity of estate (ie, a mutual or successive relationship in the same property interest) with the tenant.\n\nHere, a lease covenant required that the fast-food company contribute to costs associated with off-site improvements on the real estate developer's adjoining property. This covenant did not touch and concern the one-acre lot because it only affected the adjoining property. Therefore, the covenant does not run with the land, and the conveyance to the investor did not terminate the developer's right to enforce that covenant. (Choice A) Privity of estate between the real estate developer (landlord) and the fast-food company (tenant) terminated when the one-acre lot was sold to the investor. But the developer retains the right to enforce the covenant to contribute because the covenant does not run with the land. (Choice B) The implied warranty of habitability obligates landlords to pay maintenance costs to keep property fit for residential purposes. But this obligation does not apply to commercial leases (as seen here). Even if it did, this would not affect the real estate developer's recovery of off-site improvement costs. (Choice D) An implied obligation to contribute to repair and maintenance costs arises under an easement. But the use of the off-site improvements by the fast-food company's customers (easement) did not obligate the company to contribute to improvement costs.\n\nEducational objective: A lease covenant can only be enforced by an assignee-landlord if it runs with the land. This occurs when (1) the original parties intended to bind their successors, (2) the covenant touches and concerns the land, and (3) there is privity of estate.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 16.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (landlord's right to enforce lease provision against former tenant after transfer of ownership).","educationalObjective":"A lease covenant can only be enforced by an assignee-landlord if it runs with the land. This occurs when (1) the original parties intended to bind their successors, (2) the covenant touches and concerns the land, and (3) there is privity of estate.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 16.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (landlord's right to enforce lease provision against former tenant after transfer of ownership).","rule":"A lease covenant can only be enforced by an assignee-landlord if it runs with the land. This occurs when (1) the original parties intended to bind their successors, (2) the covenant touches and concerns the land, and (3) there is privity of estate.","irac":{"issue":"Is the real estate developer likely to prevail?","rule":"A lease covenant can only be enforced by an assignee-landlord if it runs with the land. This occurs when (1) the original parties intended to bind their successors, (2) the covenant touches and concerns the land, and (3) there is privity of estate.","analysis":"A landlord may generally transfer his/her property interest to a third party (ie, assignee- landlord) without the tenant's consent. However, the assignee-landlord only has the right to enforce a covenant (ie, promise) in the lease if it runs with the land. Otherwise, the original landlord retains the right to enforce it. A covenant runs with the land when: the original parties intended to bind successors in interest (eg, assignee-landlord)\n\nthe covenant touches and concerns (ie, affects the use or value of) the land and\n\nthe transfer brings the assignee-landlord into privity of estate (ie, a mutual or successive relationship in the same property interest) with the tenant.\n\nHere, a lease covenant required that the fast-food company contribute to costs associated with off-site improvements on the real estate developer's adjoining property. This covenant did not touch and concern the one-acre lot because it only affected the adjoining property. Therefore, the covenant does not run with the land, and the conveyance to the investor did not terminate the developer's right to enforce that covenant. (Choice A) Privity of estate between the real estate developer (landlord) and the fast-food company (tenant) terminated when the one-acre lot was sold to the investor. But the developer retains the right to enforce the covenant to contribute because the covenant does not run with the land. (Choice B) The implied warranty of habitability obligates landlords to pay maintenance costs to keep property fit for residential purposes. But this obligation does not apply to commercial leases (as seen here). Even if it did, this would not affect the real estate developer's recovery of off-site improvement costs. (Choice D) An implied obligation to contribute to repair and maintenance costs arises under an easement. But the use of the off-site improvements by the fast-food company's customers (easement) did not obligate the company to contribute to improvement costs.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the conveyance of the one-acre lot to the investor did not terminate the real estate developer's right to enforce the covenant to contribute."},"questionZh":"一位房地产开发商拥有一块100英亩的土地。六年前，该房地产开发商将位于该地区东北角的一英亩土地租给了一家快餐公司，租期为30年。这家快餐公司打算并且确实在占地一英亩的土地上建造了一家快餐店。租约规定： 快餐公司应维护该地块并对其进行改进，维持该地块的全额保险，并支付针对该地块评估的所有税款。\n\n房地产开发商负责维护毗邻该地块的道路和停车场区域，并允许该公司的客户与该地块其余部分的其他商业用户的客户共同使用它们。\n\n快餐公司将根据规定的公式支付其场外改进费用的份额。\n\n五年前，房地产开发商将一英亩土地卖给了一位投资者；该转让是在向快餐公司租赁的情况下进行的。但投资者并未承担租赁义务，房地产开发商保留了剩余的100英亩土地。自五年前转让以来，快餐公司已向投资者支付租金。然而，快餐公司拒绝支付租约中规定的场外改善费用的公式份额。房地产开发商对快餐公司提起适当诉讼，以收回此类成本。房地产开发商有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为将一英亩地块转让给投资者就终止了房地产开发商和快餐公司之间的房地产相互关系。","B":"不能，因为投资者作为快餐公司的业主，必然隐含有支付维修费用的义务。","C":"是的，因为将一英亩土地转让给投资者并没有终止房地产开发商执行出资契约的权利。","D":"是的，因为快餐公司的顾客对改进的使用对快餐公司施加了隐含的义务。"},"explanationZh":"房东通常可以在未经租户同意的情况下将其财产权益转让给第三方（即受让人-房东）。然而，受让人-房东只有在租约与土地一起运行时才有权执行租约中的契约（即承诺）。否则，原房东保留强制执行的权利。在以下情况下，契约与土地一起生效： 原当事方打算约束利益继承人（例如受让人-房东）\n\n该契约涉及并涉及（即影响其使用或价值）土地和\n\n转让使受让人-房东与承租人建立了财产关系（即同一财产权益中的相互关系或继承关系）。\n\n在这里，租赁契约要求快餐公司承担与房地产开发商毗邻房产的场外改善相关的费用。该契约没有触及和涉及一英亩土地，因为它只影响毗邻的财产。因此，该契约并不与土地同时适用，而且向投资者转让土地并没有终止开发商执行该契约的权利。 （选项A）当一英亩土地出售给投资者时，房地产开发商（房东）和快餐公司（租户）之间的房地产所有权终止。但开发商保留执行契约出资的权利，因为契约并不与土地一起运行。 （选择 B） 隐含的可居住性保证要求房东支付维护费用，以保持房产适合居住用途。但此义务不适用于商业租赁（如此处所示）。即使这样做，也不影响房地产开发商收回场外装修费用。 （选择 D）地役权产生了承担维修和维护费用的隐含义务。但快餐公司的客户使用场外改进（地役权）并不要求公司承担改进成本。\n\n教育目标：租赁契约只有与土地一起运行时才能由受让人-房东执行。这种情况发生在以下情况：(1) 原当事方打算约束其继承人，(2) 契约涉及并涉及土地，以及 (3) 存在遗产相互关系。\n\n参考文献\n提案重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 16.2 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（业主在所有权转让后对前租户执行租赁条款的权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果租赁契约与土地一起运行，则只能由受让人-房东执行。这种情况发生在以下情况：(1) 原当事方打算约束其继承人，(2) 契约涉及并涉及土地，以及 (3) 存在遗产相互关系。","iracZh":{"issue":"房地产开发商有可能获胜吗？","rule":"如果租赁契约与土地一起运行，则只能由受让人-房东执行。这种情况发生在以下情况：(1) 原当事方打算约束其继承人，(2) 契约涉及并涉及土地，以及 (3) 存在遗产相互关系。","analysis":"房东通常可以在未经租户同意的情况下将其财产权益转让给第三方（即受让人-房东）。然而，受让人-房东只有在租约与土地一起运行时才有权执行租约中的契约（即承诺）。否则，原房东保留强制执行的权利。在以下情况下，契约与土地一起生效： 原当事方打算约束利益继承人（例如受让人-房东）\n\n该契约涉及并涉及（即影响其使用或价值）土地和\n\n转让使受让人-房东与承租人建立了财产关系（即同一财产权益中的相互关系或继承关系）。\n\n在这里，租赁契约要求快餐公司承担与房地产开发商毗邻房产的场外改善相关的费用。该契约没有触及和涉及一英亩土地，因为它只影响毗邻的财产。因此，该契约并不与土地同时适用，而且向投资者转让土地并没有终止开发商执行该契约的权利。 （选项A）当一英亩土地出售给投资者时，房地产开发商（房东）和快餐公司（租户）之间的房地产所有权终止。但开发商保留执行契约出资的权利，因为契约并不与土地一起运行。 （选择 B） 隐含的可居住性保证要求房东支付维护费用，以保持房产适合居住用途。但此义务不适用于商业租赁（如此处所示）。即使这样做，也不影响房地产开发商收回场外装修费用。 （选择 D）地役权产生了承担维修和维护费用的隐含义务。但快餐公司的客户使用场外改进（地役权）并不要求公司承担改进成本。\n\n教育目标：租赁契约只有与土地一起运行时才能由受让人-房东执行。这种情况发生在以下情况：(1) 原当事方打算约束其继承人，(2) 契约涉及并涉及土地，以及 (3) 存在遗产相互关系。\n\n参考文献\n提案重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 16.2 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（业主在所有权转让后对前租户执行租赁条款的权利）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为将一英亩土地转让给投资者并没有终止房地产开发商执行出资契约的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"touch and concern","ipa":"/tˈətʃ ənd kʌnsˈɝn/","syllables":"touch and con·cern","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"涉及土地：承诺实质影响相关土地使用、价值或享受","contextualMeaning":"涉及土地：承诺实质影响相关土地使用、价值或享受","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"This covenant did not touch and concern the one-acre lot because it only affected the adjoining property.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-244be97499e06ded"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":125,"sourceQuestionPage":366,"sourceQuestionPages":[366],"sourceExplanationPages":[367,368],"sourceAnswerPages":[367,368],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-126","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Co-ownership","question":"A mother owned a two-family apartment house on a small city lot not suitable for partition- in-kind. Upon the mother's death, her will devised the apartment house to \"my two sons, in equal shares.\" A week ago, a creditor obtained a money judgment against the youngest son and properly filed the judgment in the county where the apartment house is located. A statute in the jurisdiction provides: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for ten years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" The youngest son needed cash, but the oldest son did not wish to sell the apartment house. Nevertheless, the youngest son commenced a partition action against the oldest son and the creditor. The court properly ordered a partition by judicial sale. After the sale, the creditor's judgment will be a lien on what interest?","choices":{"A":"All of the apartment house.","B":"All of the proceeds of sale of the apartment house.","C":"Only a one-half interest in the apartment house.","D":"Only the portion of the proceeds of sale due to the youngest son."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Co-tenants can freely encumber their separate interests in the property without consent of the other tenants (except in a tenancy by the entirety). This means that a tenant can use his/her share of the property to secure repayment of a debt, and creditors will only have a lien on that tenant's property interest. And if any tenant later seeks to divide the property through a partition by sale, that lien will either: remain on the tenant-debtor's property interest if the creditor is not a party to the partition action or\n\nattach to the tenant-debtor's share of proceeds from the sale if the creditor is a party to the partition action.\n\nHere, the two sons became co-tenants when the mother devised them a duplex in equal shares. A creditor later obtained and filed a money judgment against the youngest son, creating a judgment lien on his one-half interest in the duplex. The youngest son then commenced an action for partition by sale against the oldest son and the creditor—which the court granted. Since the creditor was a party to the partition action, the judgment lien will attach to the youngest son's portion of the proceeds from the judicial sale (Choices A, B & C).\n\nEducational objective: When a co-tenancy is divided in an action for partition by sale, any lien on a tenant's property interest will stay attached to that interest if the creditor is not a party to the action. But if the creditor is a party, then the lien will attach to the sale proceeds attributable to that interest.","educationalObjective":"When a co-tenancy is divided in an action for partition by sale, any lien on a tenant's property interest will stay attached to that interest if the creditor is not a party to the action. But if the creditor is a party, then the lien will attach to the sale proceeds attributable to that interest.","references":"","rule":"When a co-tenancy is divided in an action for partition by sale, any lien on a tenant's property interest will stay attached to that interest if the creditor is not a party to the action. But if the creditor is a party, then the lien will attach to the sale proceeds attributable to that interest.","irac":{"issue":"After the sale, the creditor's judgment will be a lien on what interest?","rule":"When a co-tenancy is divided in an action for partition by sale, any lien on a tenant's property interest will stay attached to that interest if the creditor is not a party to the action. But if the creditor is a party, then the lien will attach to the sale proceeds attributable to that interest.","analysis":"Co-tenants can freely encumber their separate interests in the property without consent of the other tenants (except in a tenancy by the entirety). This means that a tenant can use his/her share of the property to secure repayment of a debt, and creditors will only have a lien on that tenant's property interest. And if any tenant later seeks to divide the property through a partition by sale, that lien will either: remain on the tenant-debtor's property interest if the creditor is not a party to the partition action or\n\nattach to the tenant-debtor's share of proceeds from the sale if the creditor is a party to the partition action.\n\nHere, the two sons became co-tenants when the mother devised them a duplex in equal shares. A creditor later obtained and filed a money judgment against the youngest son, creating a judgment lien on his one-half interest in the duplex. The youngest son then commenced an action for partition by sale against the oldest son and the creditor—which the court granted. Since the creditor was a party to the partition action, the judgment lien will attach to the youngest son's portion of the proceeds from the judicial sale (Choices A, B & C).","conclusion":"Answer D: Only the portion of the proceeds of sale due to the youngest son."},"questionZh":"一位母亲在城市的一块小土地上拥有一套两户公寓，不适合实物分割。母亲去世后，她的遗嘱将公寓设计为“我的两个儿子平分”。一周前，债权人获得了针对小儿子的金钱判决，并在公寓所在的县正确提交了判决。该司法管辖区的一项法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起十年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”小儿子需要现金，但大儿子不想卖掉公寓。尽管如此，最小的儿子对大儿子和债权人发起了分割诉讼。法院正确地下令通过司法出售进行分割。出售后，债权人判断将留置哪些利息？","choicesZh":{"A":"都是公寓楼。","B":"出售公寓的所有收益。","C":"公寓只有二分之一的权益。","D":"仅将出售收益的一部分归给最小的儿子。"},"explanationZh":"按份共有人（tenant in common）可以自由地抵押其在房产中的单独权益，无需其他租户同意（整体租赁除外）。这意味着租户可以使用他/她的财产份额来偿还债务，债权人只能留置该租户的财产权益。如果任何租户后来寻求通过出售分割财产，则该留置权将： 如果债权人不是分割诉讼的一方，则该留置权将保留在租户债务人的财产权益上，或者\n\n如果债权人是分割诉讼的一方，则附加到租户债务人的出售收益份额。\n\n在这里，当母亲为两个儿子设计了一套平分的复式公寓后，他们就成为了按份共有人（tenant in common）。债权人后来获得并提交了针对最小儿子的金钱判决，对他在复式公寓中的一半权益设定了判决留置权。然后，最小的儿子向大儿子和债权人提起出售分割诉讼，法院批准了这一诉讼。由于债权人是分割诉讼的一方，判决留置权将附加在司法出售收益中最小儿子的部分（选项 A、B 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：当共同共有（joint tenancy）在出售分割诉讼中被分割时，如果债权人不是该诉讼的一方，则对租户财产权益的任何留置权将保留在该权益上。但如果债权人是一方，那么留置权将附加到归属于该利息的销售收益上。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当共同共有（joint tenancy）在出售分割诉讼中被分割时，如果债权人不是该诉讼的一方，则对租户财产权益的任何留置权将保留在该权益上。但如果债权人是一方，那么留置权将附加到归属于该利息的销售收益上。","iracZh":{"issue":"出售后，债权人判断将留置哪些利息？","rule":"当共同共有（joint tenancy）在出售分割诉讼中被分割时，如果债权人不是该诉讼的一方，则对租户财产权益的任何留置权将保留在该权益上。但如果债权人是一方，那么留置权将附加到归属于该利息的销售收益上。","analysis":"按份共有人（tenant in common）可以自由地抵押其在房产中的单独权益，无需其他租户同意（整体租赁除外）。这意味着租户可以使用他/她的财产份额来偿还债务，债权人只能留置该租户的财产权益。如果任何租户后来寻求通过出售分割财产，则该留置权将： 如果债权人不是分割诉讼的一方，则该留置权将保留在租户债务人的财产权益上，或者\n\n如果债权人是分割诉讼的一方，则附加到租户债务人的出售收益份额。\n\n在这里，当母亲为两个儿子设计了一套平分的复式公寓后，他们就成为了按份共有人（tenant in common）。债权人后来获得并提交了针对最小儿子的金钱判决，对他在复式公寓中的一半权益设定了判决留置权。然后，最小的儿子向大儿子和债权人提起出售分割诉讼，法院批准了这一诉讼。由于债权人是分割诉讼的一方，判决留置权将附加在司法出售收益中最小儿子的部分（选项 A、B 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：当共同共有（joint tenancy）在出售分割诉讼中被分割时，如果债权人不是该诉讼的一方，则对租户财产权益的任何留置权将保留在该权益上。但如果债权人是一方，那么留置权将附加到归属于该利息的销售收益上。","conclusion":"答案 D：仅将出售收益的一部分归给最小的儿子。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"partition in kind","ipa":"/pɑɹtˈɪʃən ɪn kˈaɪnd/","syllables":"par·ti·tion in kind","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"实物分割：按比例将共有土地实际划分给共有人","contextualMeaning":"实物分割：按比例将共有土地实际划分给共有人","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A mother owned a two-family apartment house on a small city lot not suitable for partition- in-kind.","synonyms":["physical partition"]},{"term":"partition by sale","ipa":"/pɑɹtˈɪʃən bˈaɪ sˈeɪl/","syllables":"par·ti·tion by sale","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"出售分割：出售共有土地并按权益比例分配价款","contextualMeaning":"出售分割：出售共有土地并按权益比例分配价款","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"When a co-tenancy is divided in an action for partition by sale, any lien on a tenant's property interest will stay attached to that interest if the creditor is not a party to the action.","synonyms":["judicial sale partition"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-a738e98c43847f9e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":126,"sourceQuestionPage":369,"sourceQuestionPages":[369],"sourceExplanationPages":[370,371],"sourceAnswerPages":[370,371],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-127","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A landlord leased a commercial building to a tenant for five years. Rent was payable on the first day of each month, and the landlord retained the right to terminate the lease if the tenant defaulted. The lease term ended 18 months ago. However, the tenant has stayed in possession of the building and has continued to pay the rent on time. The landlord has continued to accept the rent. The fair rental value of the building is now substantially more than what the tenant has been paying, and the landlord recently found a third party who will pay the higher rent. When the tenant paid the rent two months ago, the landlord accepted the rent. At the end of the month, the landlord told the tenant in writing that she had to vacate at the end of the following month. The tenant, who is still in possession of the building, has informed the landlord that she does not want to move and is willing to pay the current fair rental value. The landlord has asked his lawyer for advice regarding the right to possession. Who should the lawyer say is entitled to possession?","choices":{"A":"The landlord, because he gave proper notice to terminate the tenancy.","B":"The landlord, because he retained the right to reenter.","C":"The tenant, because she has stated that she is willing to pay the current fair rental value.","D":"The tenant, because the landlord has not shown good cause to terminate the tenancy."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Types of tenancies\n\n(leases on land)\n\nType Creation Duration Termination\n\nTerm of Agreement (ie, lease) Fixed period (eg, End of term years one month, three (automatic) years) Breach of certain covenants (eg, rent, habitability) Periodic Agreement Periodically End of period (proper tenancy Presumed when lease has renews until notice) no specified termination terminated Breach of certain date covenants Operation of law (eg, holdover tenant) Agreement Indefinite period At any time Tenancy at Implied when person (reasonable notice) will allowed to possess premises without paying rent Tenancy at Implied when tenant keeps Continues until Landlord evicts sufferance possession after lease terminated Tenant vacates expires (ie, holdover Landlord accepts rent tenancy) (forms periodic tenancy) A tenant has the right to possess the premises during the term of the lease. A tenant who remains in possession after the lease term expires is a holdover tenant (ie, tenant at sufferance). The landlord can then: file a lawsuit to remove the tenant from the property (ie, an unlawful detainer action) or\n\naccept rent and hold the tenant to a periodic tenancy—ie, a tenancy that automatically renews for successive periods of time depending on how rent was computed under the original lease (eg, monthly).\n\nEither party (ie, landlord or tenant) can terminate a periodic tenancy at the end of a period by giving proper notice. Notice is proper if it is given before the final period\n\nstarts—eg, to terminate a month-to-month periodic tenancy on June 30, notice must be given by May 31. Here, the tenant remained in possession of the building after her five-year lease term expired (holdover tenant). Since rent was computed monthly under the original lease, the landlord held her to a month-to-month periodic tenancy by accepting rent. And since the landlord gave her notice to vacate at the end of the following month before the start of that final month, notice was proper. Therefore, the landlord is entitled to possession. (Choice B) Unless the lease expressly states otherwise, a landlord does not retain a right of reentry—ie, a right to reenter and retake possession of the premises if the tenant breaches the lease. Here, the landlord only retained the right to terminate the lease if the tenant defaulted on rent—not the right to reenter. (Choice C) Although the tenant was willing to pay the current fair rental value for the property, the landlord was not obligated to lease the property to her. (Choice D) A periodic tenancy can generally be terminated without cause. However, some courts have held that landlords must show good cause to terminate a periodic tenancy of federally subsidized residential housing. But since the landlord's property was a commercial building, he does not need to show good cause to gain possession.\n\nEducational objective: A holdover tenant—ie, one who remains in possession of the premises after his/her lease term expires—can be held to a periodic tenancy if the landlord accepts additional payments of rent. Either party can terminate the periodic tenancy at the end of a period if notice is given before that period starts.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (periodic tenancy).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 14.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (holdover tenant).","educationalObjective":"A holdover tenant—ie, one who remains in possession of the premises after his/her lease term expires—can be held to a periodic tenancy if the landlord accepts additional payments of rent. Either party can terminate the periodic tenancy at the end of a period if notice is given before that period starts.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (periodic tenancy).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 14.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (holdover tenant).","rule":"A holdover tenant—ie, one who remains in possession of the premises after his/her lease term expires—can be held to a periodic tenancy if the landlord accepts additional payments of rent. Either party can terminate the periodic tenancy at the end of a period if notice is given before that period starts.","irac":{"issue":"Who should the lawyer say is entitled to possession?","rule":"A holdover tenant—ie, one who remains in possession of the premises after his/her lease term expires—can be held to a periodic tenancy if the landlord accepts additional payments of rent. Either party can terminate the periodic tenancy at the end of a period if notice is given before that period starts.","analysis":"Types of tenancies\n\n(leases on land)\n\nType Creation Duration Termination\n\nTerm of Agreement (ie, lease) Fixed period (eg, End of term years one month, three (automatic) years) Breach of certain covenants (eg, rent, habitability) Periodic Agreement Periodically End of period (proper tenancy Presumed when lease has renews until notice) no specified termination terminated Breach of certain date covenants Operation of law (eg, holdover tenant) Agreement Indefinite period At any time Tenancy at Implied when person (reasonable notice) will allowed to possess premises without paying rent Tenancy at Implied when tenant keeps Continues until Landlord evicts sufferance possession after lease terminated Tenant vacates expires (ie, holdover Landlord accepts rent tenancy) (forms periodic tenancy) A tenant has the right to possess the premises during the term of the lease. A tenant who remains in possession after the lease term expires is a holdover tenant (ie, tenant at sufferance). The landlord can then: file a lawsuit to remove the tenant from the property (ie, an unlawful detainer action) or\n\naccept rent and hold the tenant to a periodic tenancy—ie, a tenancy that automatically renews for successive periods of time depending on how rent was computed under the original lease (eg, monthly).\n\nEither party (ie, landlord or tenant) can terminate a periodic tenancy at the end of a period by giving proper notice. Notice is proper if it is given before the final period\n\nstarts—eg, to terminate a month-to-month periodic tenancy on June 30, notice must be given by May 31. Here, the tenant remained in possession of the building after her five-year lease term expired (holdover tenant). Since rent was computed monthly under the original lease, the landlord held her to a month-to-month periodic tenancy by accepting rent. And since the landlord gave her notice to vacate at the end of the following month before the start of that final month, notice was proper. Therefore, the landlord is entitled to possession. (Choice B) Unless the lease expressly states otherwise, a landlord does not retain a right of reentry—ie, a right to reenter and retake possession of the premises if the tenant breaches the lease. Here, the landlord only retained the right to terminate the lease if the tenant defaulted on rent—not the right to reenter. (Choice C) Although the tenant was willing to pay the current fair rental value for the property, the landlord was not obligated to lease the property to her. (Choice D) A periodic tenancy can generally be terminated without cause. However, some courts have held that landlords must show good cause to terminate a periodic tenancy of federally subsidized residential housing. But since the landlord's property was a commercial building, he does not need to show good cause to gain possession.","conclusion":"Answer A: The landlord, because he gave proper notice to terminate the tenancy."},"questionZh":"房东将一栋商业建筑出租给租户，为期五年。租金应在每个月的第一天支付，如果租客违约，房东保留终止租约的权利。租期已于 18 个月前结束。然而，租户仍保留该建筑物的所有权，并继续按时支付租金。房东继续接受租金。现在，该建筑的公平租金价值大大高于租户所支付的价格，而且房东最近找到了愿意支付更高租金的第三方。当租客两个月前支付租金时，房东接受了租金。月底，房东书面通知房客，她必须在下个月月底搬出。仍拥有该建筑物的租户已通知房东，她不想搬家，并愿意支付当前的公平租金。房东已向他的律师征求有关占有权的建议。律师应该说谁有权占有？","choicesZh":{"A":"房东，因为他发出了终止租约的适当通知。","B":"房东，因为他保留了重新进入的权利。","C":"租客，因为她已表示愿意支付当前的公平租金价值。","D":"租客，因为房东没有提供充分理由终止租约。"},"explanationZh":"租赁类型\n\n（土地租赁）\n\n类型创建持续时间终止\n\n协议期限（即租赁） 固定期限（例如，期限结束年 一个月、三年（自动）） 违反某些契约（例如，租金、可居住性） 定期协议 定期协议 期限结束（在租赁续签直至收到通知时推定为正当租赁） 无指定终止终止 违反特定日期契约 法律运作（例如，滞留租户） 协议 无限期 任何时间 隐含的租赁 允许个人（合理通知）拥有房屋时无需支付租金 租客保留时隐含的租赁权 继续直至房东在租约终止后驱逐出暂占权 租客腾出期满（即滞留 房东接受租金租赁）（形成定期租约） 租客有权在租约期限内占有该房屋。租赁期满后仍保留占有权的租户称为滞留租户（即，忍受租户）。然后，房东可以： 提起诉讼，将租户驱逐出房产（即非法拘留行动），或者\n\n接受租金并让租户定期租赁，即根据原始租约下租金的计算方式（例如每月）自动续订连续一段时间的租赁。\n\n任何一方（即房东或租户）都可以在期限结束时通过发出适当的通知来终止定期租赁。如果在最后期限之前发出通知是适当的\n\n开始——例如，要在 6 月 30 日终止按月定期租赁，则必须在 5 月 31 日之前发出通知。在此，租户在五年租期到期后仍拥有该建筑物（滞留租户）。由于原来的租约规定租金是按月计算的，房东通过接受租金的方式让她按月定期租赁。由于房东在最后一个月开始前的下个月月底发出了搬出通知，因此通知是适当的。因此，业主有权占有。 （选择 B）除非租约另有明确规定，否则房东不保留重新进入的权利，即如果租户违反租约，则重新进入并重新占有房屋的权利。在这里，房东仅保留在租户拖欠租金的情况下终止租约的权利，而不是重新入住的权利。 （选项C）虽然租客愿意支付该房产当前的公平租金价值，但房东没有义务将该房产出租给她。 （选择 D） 定期租赁通常可以无故终止。然而，一些法院认为，房东必须提出充分的理由才能终止联邦补贴住宅的定期租赁。但由于房东的财产是商业建筑，他不需要提出充分的理由即可获得占有。\n\n教育目标：如果房东接受额外的租金支付，则滞留租户（即在租赁期满后仍然拥有房屋的租户）可以保留定期租赁。如果在期限开始之前发出通知，任何一方都可以在期限结束时终止定期租赁。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（定期租赁）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 14.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（暂缓租户）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果房东接受额外的租金，则滞留租户（即在租赁期满后仍然拥有房屋的租户）可以保留定期租赁。如果在期限开始之前发出通知，任何一方都可以在期限结束时终止定期租赁。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师应该说谁有权占有？","rule":"如果房东接受额外的租金，则滞留租户（即在租赁期满后仍然拥有房屋的租户）可以保留定期租赁。如果在期限开始之前发出通知，任何一方都可以在期限结束时终止定期租赁。","analysis":"租赁类型\n\n（土地租赁）\n\n类型创建持续时间终止\n\n协议期限（即租赁） 固定期限（例如，期限结束年 一个月、三年（自动）） 违反某些契约（例如，租金、可居住性） 定期协议 定期协议 期限结束（在租赁续签直至收到通知时推定为正当租赁） 无指定终止终止 违反特定日期契约 法律运作（例如，滞留租户） 协议 无限期 任何时间 隐含的租赁 允许个人（合理通知）拥有房屋时无需支付租金 租客保留时隐含的租赁权 继续直至房东在租约终止后驱逐出暂占权 租客腾出期满（即滞留 房东接受租金租赁）（形成定期租约） 租客有权在租约期限内占有该房屋。租赁期满后仍保留占有权的租户称为滞留租户（即，忍受租户）。然后，房东可以： 提起诉讼，将租户驱逐出房产（即非法拘留行动），或者\n\n接受租金并让租户定期租赁，即根据原始租约下租金的计算方式（例如每月）自动续订连续一段时间的租赁。\n\n任何一方（即房东或租户）都可以在期限结束时通过发出适当的通知来终止定期租赁。如果在最后期限之前发出通知是适当的\n\n开始——例如，要在 6 月 30 日终止按月定期租赁，则必须在 5 月 31 日之前发出通知。在此，租户在五年租期到期后仍拥有该建筑物（滞留租户）。由于原来的租约规定租金是按月计算的，房东通过接受租金的方式让她按月定期租赁。由于房东在最后一个月开始前的下个月月底发出了搬出通知，因此通知是适当的。因此，业主有权占有。 （选择 B）除非租约另有明确规定，否则房东不保留重新进入的权利，即如果租户违反租约，则重新进入并重新占有房屋的权利。在这里，房东仅保留在租户拖欠租金的情况下终止租约的权利，而不是重新入住的权利。 （选项C）虽然租客愿意支付该房产当前的公平租金价值，但房东没有义务将该房产出租给她。 （选择 D） 定期租赁通常可以无故终止。然而，一些法院认为，房东必须提出充分的理由才能终止联邦补贴住宅的定期租赁。但由于房东的财产是商业建筑，他不需要提出充分的理由即可获得占有。\n\n教育目标：如果房东接受额外的租金支付，则滞留租户（即在租赁期满后仍然拥有房屋的租户）可以保留定期租赁。如果在期限开始之前发出通知，任何一方都可以在期限结束时终止定期租赁。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（定期租赁）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 14.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1977)（暂缓租户）。","conclusion":"答案 A：房东，因为他发出了终止租约的适当通知。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"detainer","ipa":"/di'teinə/","syllables":"de·tainer","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 拘留者, 非法留置, 继续监禁令；[法] 阻止者, 拘者者, 扣押者","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“detainer”","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"The landlord can then: file a lawsuit to remove the tenant from the property (ie, an unlawful detainer action) or accept rent and hold the tenant to a periodic tenancy—ie, a tenancy that automatically renews for successive periods of time depending on how rent was computed under the original lease (eg, monthly).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":127,"sourceQuestionPage":372,"sourceQuestionPages":[372],"sourceExplanationPages":[373,374],"sourceAnswerPages":[373,374],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-128","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"By a writing, a homeowner leased his home to a law student for a term of three years, ending December 31 of last year, at the rent of $1,000 per month. The lease provided that the law student could sublet and assign. The law student lived in the home for one year and paid the rent promptly. After one year, he leased the home to a graduate student for one year at a rent of $1,000 per month. The graduate student took possession of the home and lived there for six months but, because of her unemployment, paid no rent. After six months, on June 30, the graduate student abandoned the home, which remained vacant for the balance of that year. The law student again took possession of the home at the beginning of the third and final year of the term but paid the homeowner no rent. At the end of the lease term, the homeowner brought an appropriate action against both the law student and the graduate student to recover $24,000, the unpaid rent. In such action, the homeowner is entitled to a judgment against whom?","choices":{"A":"The law student individually for $12,000, and against the law student and graduate student jointly and severally for $12,000.","B":"The law student individually for $18,000, and against the law student and graduate student jointly and severally for $6,000.","C":"The law student individually for $18,000, and against the graduate student individually for $6,000.","D":"The law student individually for $24,000, and no judgment against the graduate student."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A landlord and tenant have a legal relationship based on both: privity of contract – their shared interest in the lease agreement and privity of estate – the landlord's successive right to possess the property (ie, the tenant's current right of possession is immediately followed by the landlord's future right of possession). Unless the lease states otherwise, either party can freely transfer his/her interest under the lease. Subletting is a partial transfer of a tenant's interest to a third party (sublessee) for less than the remainder of the tenant's lease term. Despite that transfer, a sublessee is not liable to the landlord for a breach of the lease because: no lease agreement exists between the landlord and the sublessee (no privity of contract) and the original tenant will regain possession of the property before the landlord does (no privity of estate). Therefore, the original tenant is individually liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease by the sublessee. Here, the homeowner leased his home to the law student for three years at $1,000 per month. After one year, the law student sublet the home to a graduate student for one year (less than the remainder of the lease). Since the graduate student was a sublessee (no privity with homeowner), she is not liable to the homeowner. However, the law student— the original tenant—is individually liable for the homeowner's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease (Choices A, B & C). And since no rent was paid for the final two years of the lease (breach), the homeowner is entitled to a judgment against the law student for the full amount ($24,000).\n\nEducational objective: A tenant who sublets—ie, transfers his/her interest to a third party (sublessee) for less than the remainder of the lease term—is individually liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease by the sublessee. However, the sublessee has no liability to the landlord.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 15.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (right to transfer lease interest).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 16.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (tenant remains liable after sublet based on privity of contract and privity of estate).","educationalObjective":"A tenant who sublets—ie, transfers his/her interest to a third party (sublessee) for less than the remainder of the lease term—is individually liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease by the sublessee. However, the sublessee has no liability to the landlord.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 15.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (right to transfer lease interest).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 16.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (tenant remains liable after sublet based on privity of contract and privity of estate).","rule":"A tenant who sublets—ie, transfers his/her interest to a third party (sublessee) for less than the remainder of the lease term—is individually liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease by the sublessee. However, the sublessee has no liability to the landlord.","irac":{"issue":"In such action, the homeowner is entitled to a judgment against whom?","rule":"A tenant who sublets—ie, transfers his/her interest to a third party (sublessee) for less than the remainder of the lease term—is individually liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease by the sublessee. However, the sublessee has no liability to the landlord.","analysis":"A landlord and tenant have a legal relationship based on both: privity of contract – their shared interest in the lease agreement and privity of estate – the landlord's successive right to possess the property (ie, the tenant's current right of possession is immediately followed by the landlord's future right of possession). Unless the lease states otherwise, either party can freely transfer his/her interest under the lease. Subletting is a partial transfer of a tenant's interest to a third party (sublessee) for less than the remainder of the tenant's lease term. Despite that transfer, a sublessee is not liable to the landlord for a breach of the lease because: no lease agreement exists between the landlord and the sublessee (no privity of contract) and the original tenant will regain possession of the property before the landlord does (no privity of estate). Therefore, the original tenant is individually liable for the landlord's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease by the sublessee. Here, the homeowner leased his home to the law student for three years at $1,000 per month. After one year, the law student sublet the home to a graduate student for one year (less than the remainder of the lease). Since the graduate student was a sublessee (no privity with homeowner), she is not liable to the homeowner. However, the law student— the original tenant—is individually liable for the homeowner's entire harm arising from a breach of the lease (Choices A, B & C). And since no rent was paid for the final two years of the lease (breach), the homeowner is entitled to a judgment against the law student for the full amount ($24,000).","conclusion":"Answer D: The law student individually for $24,000, and no judgment against the graduate student."},"questionZh":"根据书面记录，一名房主将自己的房屋出租给一名法律系学生，租期为三年，截至去年 12 月 31 日，租金为每月 1,000 美元。租约规定法律系学生可以转租和转让。这位法律系学生在家里住了一年，并及时支付了租金。一年后，他将房子租给一名研究生一年，租金为每月 1000 美元。这名研究生拥有了这套房子并在那里住了六个月，但由于失业，她没有支付房租。六个月后，即 6 月 30 日，这名研究生放弃了该房屋，该房屋在当年剩余时间内一直空置。法学院学生在学期的第三年也是最后一年开始时再次拥有了该房屋，但没有向房主支付租金。租期结束时，房主对法学院学生和研究生提起适当诉讼，追回 24,000 美元的未付租金。在这种诉讼中，房主有权获得针对谁的判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"法律系学生个人赔偿 12,000 美元，法律系学生和研究生共同赔偿 12,000 美元。","B":"法律系学生个人赔偿 18,000 美元，法律系学生和研究生共同赔偿 6,000 美元。","C":"法学院学生个人支付 18,000 美元，研究生个人支付 6,000 美元。","D":"法学院学生个人赔偿 24,000 美元，并且没有针对研究生的判决。"},"explanationZh":"房东和租客之间的法律关系基于以下两个方面：合同相对性（双方在租赁协议中的共同利益）和房地产相对性（房东对财产的继承权）（即，租户当前的占有权紧随房东未来的占有权）。除非租约另有规定，任何一方都可以自由转让其在租约下的权益。转租是指将租户的权益部分转让给第三方（转承租人），期限少于租户的剩余租赁期限。尽管进行了转让，转租人仍无需因违反租约而对房东承担责任，因为：房东和转租人之间不存在租赁协议（无合同关系），并且原租户将在房东之前重新获得财产的占有（无遗产关系）。因此，原租户应对房东因转租人违反租约而造成的全部损害承担个人责任。在此，房主将自己的房屋以每月 1,000 美元的价格出租给法律系学生，为期三年。一年后，法律系学生将房屋转租给研究生一年（少于剩余租期）。由于研究生是转租人（与房主没有关系），因此她不对房主承担责任。然而，法学院学生（原租户）对房主因违反租约而造成的全部损害负有个人责任（选项 A、B 和 C）。由于租约的最后两年没有支付租金（违约），房主有权获得针对法律系学生的全额赔偿（24,000 美元）。\n\n教育目标：转租的租户（即将其权益转让给第三方（转承租人）的时间少于剩余租赁期限）应对房东因转租人违反租约而造成的全部损害承担个人责任。但是，转租人对房东不承担任何责任。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户第 15.1 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（转让租赁权益的权利）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户 § 16.1（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（基于合同相互关系和遗产相互关系，租户在转租后仍需承担责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"转租的租户（即将其权益转让给第三方（转承租人）的时间少于剩余租赁期限）应对房东因转承租人违反租约而造成的全部损害承担个人责任。但是，转租人对房东不承担任何责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这种诉讼中，房主有权获得针对谁的判决？","rule":"转租的租户（即将其权益转让给第三方（转承租人）的时间少于剩余租赁期限）应对房东因转承租人违反租约而造成的全部损害承担个人责任。但是，转租人对房东不承担任何责任。","analysis":"房东和租客之间的法律关系基于以下两个方面：合同相对性（双方在租赁协议中的共同利益）和房地产相对性（房东对财产的继承权）（即，租户当前的占有权紧随房东未来的占有权）。除非租约另有规定，任何一方都可以自由转让其在租约下的权益。转租是指将租户的权益部分转让给第三方（转承租人），期限少于租户的剩余租赁期限。尽管进行了转让，转租人仍无需因违反租约而对房东承担责任，因为：房东和转租人之间不存在租赁协议（无合同关系），并且原租户将在房东之前重新获得财产的占有（无遗产关系）。因此，原租户应对房东因转租人违反租约而造成的全部损害承担个人责任。在此，房主将自己的房屋以每月 1,000 美元的价格出租给法律系学生，为期三年。一年后，法律系学生将房屋转租给研究生一年（少于剩余租期）。由于研究生是转租人（与房主没有关系），因此她不对房主承担责任。然而，法学院学生（原租户）对房主因违反租约而造成的全部损害负有个人责任（选项 A、B 和 C）。由于租约的最后两年没有支付租金（违约），房主有权获得针对法律系学生的全额赔偿（24,000 美元）。\n\n教育目标：转租的租户（即将其权益转让给第三方（转承租人）的时间少于剩余租赁期限）应对房东因转租人违反租约而造成的全部损害承担个人责任。但是，转租人对房东不承担任何责任。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户第 15.1 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（转让租赁权益的权利）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户 § 16.1（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（基于合同相互关系和遗产相互关系，租户在转租后仍需承担责任）。","conclusion":"答案 D：法学院学生个人赔偿 24,000 美元，并且没有针对研究生的判决。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-cd2a2fdf590b4375"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":128,"sourceQuestionPage":375,"sourceQuestionPages":[375],"sourceExplanationPages":[376,377],"sourceAnswerPages":[376,377],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-129","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A husband owned a farm of 300 acres in fee simple. He died and by will duly admitted to probate devised the farm to his surviving widow for life with remainder in fee simple to his three children (twin sons and a daughter). All three children survived the husband. At the time of the husband's death, there existed a mortgage on the farm that the husband had given 10 years before to secure a loan for the purchase of the farm. At his death, there remained unpaid $40,000 in principal, payable in installments of $4,000 per year for the next 10 years. In addition, there was due interest at the rate of 10% per annum, payable annually with the installment of principal. The widow took possession and out of a gross income of $50,000 per year realized $25,000 net after paying all expenses and charges except the installment of principal and interest due on the mortgage. The twin sons wanted the three children, including the daughter, to each contribute one- third of the amounts needed to pay the mortgage installments. The daughter objected, contending that the widow should pay all of these amounts out of the profits she had made in operation of the farm. When foreclosure of the mortgage seemed imminent, the daughter sought legal advice. If the daughter obtained sound advice relating to her rights, what was she told?","choices":{"A":"Her only protection would lie in instituting an action for partition to compel the sale of the life estate of the widow and to obtain the value of the daughter's one-third interest in remainder.","B":"She could be compelled personally to pay her share of the amounts due because discharge of the mortgage enhances the principal.","C":"She could not be held personally liable for any amount but her share in remainder could be lost if the mortgage installments are not paid.","D":"She could obtain appropriate relief to compel the widow personally to pay the sums due because the income is more than adequate to cover these amounts."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Payment of preexisting mortgage on life estate\n\nAllocation of Periodic payment of interest only – life tenant is responsible for mortgage obligation interest payments; future-interest holder is responsible for principal payment due at later date Periodic payment of interest & principal – life tenant & future- interest holder are responsible for payments in proportion with their respective interests Liability for No liability absent assumption of mortgage nonpayment Failure to pay allocation may result in loss of property interest A mortgage obligation that was incurred before the creation of a life estate in the mortgaged property is subject to allocation between the life tenant and the future-interest holder (eg, remainderman). The allocation can vary depending on the type of obligation: If the obligation requires the periodic payment of interest until the principal amount is due, then the life tenant is responsible for interest payments and the future-interest holder is responsible for principal payments due at a later date.\n\nIf the obligation requires the periodic payment of both interest and the principal amount (in whole or in part), then the obligation is allocated between the life tenant and the future- interest holder based on the present value of each interest.\n\nHere, both interest and principal payments were due on the preexisting mortgage. As a result, the widow (life tenant) and the three children, including the daughter (remaindermen), are responsible for payment of the farm's mortgage based on the present value of their respective interests. However, they are not personally liable for the mortgage obligation because they did not assume the mortgage (Choices B & D). Instead, their failure to pay their share of the debt may result in the loss of their property interest (eg, the daughter's share in remainder). (Choice A) Partition is a remedy available to owners of concurrent interests in property (eg, tenants in common)—not owners of successive interests in property (eg, life tenants and remaindermen).\n\nEducational objective: A mortgage obligation incurred before the creation of a life estate in the mortgaged property is subject to allocation between the life tenant and the future-interest holder. The parties are not personally liable for the mortgage obligation unless they assumed the mortgage, but failure to pay may result in the loss of their property interest.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 132 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing life tenant's obligation to contribute to a preexisting mortgage obligation when the principal amount has become due).\n\n139 A.L.R. 711 (1942) (personal liability of heir or devisee of real property for debt secured by mortgage thereon).","educationalObjective":"A mortgage obligation incurred before the creation of a life estate in the mortgaged property is subject to allocation between the life tenant and the future-interest holder. The parties are not personally liable for the mortgage obligation unless they assumed the mortgage, but failure to pay may result in the loss of their property interest.","references":"Restatement of Property § 132 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing life tenant's obligation to contribute to a preexisting mortgage obligation when the principal amount has become due).\n\n139 A.L.R. 711 (1942) (personal liability of heir or devisee of real property for debt secured by mortgage thereon).","rule":"A mortgage obligation incurred before the creation of a life estate in the mortgaged property is subject to allocation between the life tenant and the future-interest holder. The parties are not personally liable for the mortgage obligation unless they assumed the mortgage, but failure to pay may result in the loss of their property interest.","irac":{"issue":"If the daughter obtained sound advice relating to her rights, what was she told?","rule":"A mortgage obligation incurred before the creation of a life estate in the mortgaged property is subject to allocation between the life tenant and the future-interest holder. The parties are not personally liable for the mortgage obligation unless they assumed the mortgage, but failure to pay may result in the loss of their property interest.","analysis":"Payment of preexisting mortgage on life estate\n\nAllocation of Periodic payment of interest only – life tenant is responsible for mortgage obligation interest payments; future-interest holder is responsible for principal payment due at later date Periodic payment of interest & principal – life tenant & future- interest holder are responsible for payments in proportion with their respective interests Liability for No liability absent assumption of mortgage nonpayment Failure to pay allocation may result in loss of property interest A mortgage obligation that was incurred before the creation of a life estate in the mortgaged property is subject to allocation between the life tenant and the future-interest holder (eg, remainderman). The allocation can vary depending on the type of obligation: If the obligation requires the periodic payment of interest until the principal amount is due, then the life tenant is responsible for interest payments and the future-interest holder is responsible for principal payments due at a later date.\n\nIf the obligation requires the periodic payment of both interest and the principal amount (in whole or in part), then the obligation is allocated between the life tenant and the future- interest holder based on the present value of each interest.\n\nHere, both interest and principal payments were due on the preexisting mortgage. As a result, the widow (life tenant) and the three children, including the daughter (remaindermen), are responsible for payment of the farm's mortgage based on the present value of their respective interests. However, they are not personally liable for the mortgage obligation because they did not assume the mortgage (Choices B & D). Instead, their failure to pay their share of the debt may result in the loss of their property interest (eg, the daughter's share in remainder). (Choice A) Partition is a remedy available to owners of concurrent interests in property (eg, tenants in common)—not owners of successive interests in property (eg, life tenants and remaindermen).","conclusion":"Answer C: She could not be held personally liable for any amount but her share in remainder could be lost if the mortgage installments are not paid."},"questionZh":"丈夫拥有一个 300 英亩的农场，费用简单。他去世后，遗嘱正式承认遗嘱认证，将农场设计给他幸存的遗孀终身，剩余的费用留给他的三个孩子（双胞胎儿子和一个女儿）。丈夫的三个孩子全部幸存。丈夫去世时，农场上存在抵押贷款，该抵押贷款是丈夫 10 年前为购买农场而提供的贷款。在他去世时，尚有 40,000 美元本金未付，未来 10 年每年需分期支付 4,000 美元。此外，还有年利率10%的到期利息，每年与本金分期支付。寡妇取得了财产，每年 50,000 美元的总收入在支付除抵押贷款本金和利息以外的所有费用和费用后，净收入为 25,000 美元。双胞胎儿子希望包括女儿在内的三个孩子各贡献三分之一的抵押贷款分期付款。女儿表示反对，认为寡妇应该用她经营农场的利润来支付所有这些款项。当抵押贷款丧失赎回权似乎迫在眉睫时，女儿寻求法律建议。如果女儿获得了有关她权利的合理建议，她会被告知什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"她唯一的保护是提起分割诉讼，强制出售寡妇的终身财产，并获得女儿三分之一剩余权益的价值。","B":"她可能被迫亲自支付她应得的份额，因为抵押贷款的解除会增加本金。","C":"她个人不能承担任何金额的责任，但如果不支付抵押贷款分期付款，她在剩余份额中的份额可能会损失。","D":"她可以获得适当的救济，迫使寡妇亲自支付应付款项，因为她的收入足以支付这些金额。"},"explanationZh":"支付现有的生命财产抵押贷款\n\n仅分配定期支付利息 – 终身租户负责抵押贷款利息支付；未来利息持有人负责稍后到期的本金支付 定期支付利息和本金 – 终身租户和未来利息持有人负责按各自利息比例支付 责任 如果假设抵押不还款，则不承担任何责任 未能支付分配可能会导致财产利息损失 在抵押财产中创建终身财产之前产生的抵押债务应在终身租户和未来利息持有人（例如，剩余人）之间进行分配。分配可能因债务类型而异：如果债务要求定期支付利息直至本金到期，则终身租户负责支付利息，而未来利息持有人则负责支付稍后到期的本金。\n\n如果该义务要求定期支付利息和本金（全部或部分），则该义务将根据每项利息的现值在终身租户和未来利息持有人之间分配。\n\n在这里，先前存在的抵押贷款的利息和本金均已到期。因此，寡妇（终身佃户）和三个孩子，包括女儿（剩余人），有责任根据各自权益的现值支付农场抵押贷款。然而，他们个人不承担抵押贷款义务，因为他们没有承担抵押贷款（Choices B & D）。相反，他们未能偿还自己应承担的债务可能会导致其财产权益的损失（例如，女儿应得的剩余份额）。 （选择A） 分割是一种补救措施，适用于同时拥有财产权益的所有人（例如，按份共有人（tenant in common）），而不是连续拥有财产权益的所有人（例如，终身租户和剩余人）。\n\n教育目标：在抵押财产中创建终身财产之前产生的抵押债务应在终身租户和未来利息持有人之间进行分配。除非承担了抵押，否则当事人个人不承担抵押义务，但不履行抵押义务可能会导致其财产权益的损失。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 132（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论当本金到期时终身租户有义务为先前存在的抵押贷款义务供款）。\n\n第139章711 (1942)（不动产继承人或受托人对以其抵押担保的债务的个人责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在抵押财产中创建终身财产之前产生的抵押债务须在终身租户和未来利息持有人之间进行分配。除非承担了抵押，否则当事人个人不承担抵押义务，但不履行抵押义务可能会导致其财产权益的损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果女儿获得了有关她权利的合理建议，她会被告知什么？","rule":"在抵押财产中创建终身财产之前产生的抵押债务须在终身租户和未来利息持有人之间进行分配。除非承担了抵押，否则当事人个人不承担抵押义务，但不履行抵押义务可能会导致其财产权益的损失。","analysis":"支付现有的生命财产抵押贷款\n\n仅分配定期支付利息 – 终身租户负责抵押贷款利息支付；未来利息持有人负责稍后到期的本金支付 定期支付利息和本金 – 终身租户和未来利息持有人负责按各自利息比例支付 责任 如果假设抵押不还款，则不承担任何责任 未能支付分配可能会导致财产利息损失 在抵押财产中创建终身财产之前产生的抵押债务应在终身租户和未来利息持有人（例如，剩余人）之间进行分配。分配可能因债务类型而异：如果债务要求定期支付利息直至本金到期，则终身租户负责支付利息，而未来利息持有人则负责支付稍后到期的本金。\n\n如果该义务要求定期支付利息和本金（全部或部分），则该义务将根据每项利息的现值在终身租户和未来利息持有人之间分配。\n\n在这里，先前存在的抵押贷款的利息和本金均已到期。因此，寡妇（终身佃户）和三个孩子，包括女儿（剩余人），有责任根据各自权益的现值支付农场抵押贷款。然而，他们个人不承担抵押贷款义务，因为他们没有承担抵押贷款（Choices B & D）。相反，他们未能偿还自己应承担的债务可能会导致其财产权益的损失（例如，女儿应得的剩余份额）。 （选择A） 分割是一种补救措施，适用于同时拥有财产权益的所有人（例如，按份共有人（tenant in common）），而不是连续拥有财产权益的所有人（例如，终身租户和剩余人）。\n\n教育目标：在抵押财产中创建终身财产之前产生的抵押债务应在终身租户和未来利息持有人之间进行分配。除非承担了抵押，否则当事人个人不承担抵押义务，但不履行抵押义务可能会导致其财产权益的损失。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 132（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论当本金到期时终身租户有义务为先前存在的抵押贷款义务供款）。\n\n第139章711 (1942)（不动产继承人或受托人对以其抵押担保的债务的个人责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：她个人不能承担任何金额的责任，但如果不支付抵押贷款分期付款，她在剩余份额中的份额可能会损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"assumption of mortgage","ipa":"/ʌsˈəmpʃən ˈəv mˈɔɹɡədʒ/","syllables":"as·sump·tion of mort·gage","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"承接抵押债务：受让人明确同意对原抵押债务承担个人责任","contextualMeaning":"承接抵押债务：受让人明确同意对原抵押债务承担个人责任","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Payment of preexisting mortgage on life estate Allocation of Periodic payment of interest only – life tenant is responsible for mortgage obligation interest payments; future-interest holder is responsible for principal payment due at later date Periodic payment of interest & principal – life tenant & future- interest holder are responsible for payments in proportion with their respective interests Liability for No li","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":129,"sourceQuestionPage":378,"sourceQuestionPages":[378],"sourceExplanationPages":[379,380],"sourceAnswerPages":[379,380],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-130","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man and a woman owned property as joint tenants with the right of survivorship. Without the woman's knowledge or consent, the man signed a 10-year promissory note to a bank and secured the note with a mortgage on the property. The mortgage was promptly recorded. Two years later, the man died intestate, leaving his son as his sole heir. The woman first learned of the mortgage after the man died. The woman and the son did not make any payments on the mortgage. As a result, the bank initiated judicial foreclosure proceedings, naming both the son and the woman as parties to the action. The woman and the son have consulted an attorney about the enforceability of the mortgage. The jurisdiction follows the lien theory of mortgages. What is the attorney likely to say regarding the mortgage?","choices":{"A":"The mortgage is enforceable against the son and the woman, because the mortgage encumbered the entire property.","B":"The mortgage is enforceable against the son only, because he is the man's sole heir.","C":"The mortgage is enforceable against the woman only, because she is the surviving joint tenant.","D":"The mortgage is not enforceable against the son or the woman, because the mortgage expired upon the man's death."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The man and the woman owned the property as joint tenants with the right of survivorship. A joint tenant may only grant a mortgage on his/her own joint-tenancy interest without the other joint tenant's consent. Therefore, a mortgage executed by one joint tenant will only attach to that tenant's property interest. But whether the joint tenancy remains intact depends on the jurisdiction: Lien theory (majority rule) – a mortgage is merely a lien on the property, which means that granting a mortgage does not sever the joint tenancy, but a severance will occur upon a foreclosure sale following a default\n\nTitle theory (minority rule) – a mortgage constitutes a transfer of title, which means that granting a mortgage does sever the joint tenancy and the mortgagor-tenant becomes tenants in common with the other joint tenants\n\nHere, the man mortgaged the property without the woman's consent, so the mortgage only encumbered the man's joint-tenancy interest—not the entire property (Choice A). And since this jurisdiction follows the lien theory of mortgages, the joint tenancy between the man and the woman was not severed when the man mortgaged his joint-tenancy interest. Instead, the joint tenancy remained intact until the man's death. When a joint tenant dies, that tenant's interest disappears and the remaining joint tenant's interest automatically expands to absorb it due to the right of survivorship. This means that any mortgage attached to a joint tenant's interest also disappears when that tenant dies and cannot be enforced against the deceased tenant's heirs or the surviving tenant (the son and the woman, respectively) (Choices B & C). Therefore, the mortgage on the man's tenancy expired upon his death, leaving the bank with no interest in the property on which to foreclose.\n\nEducational objective: A joint tenant may only grant a mortgage on his/her joint-tenancy interest without the other joint tenant's consent. In a lien-theory jurisdiction (majority rule), granting a mortgage does not sever the joint tenancy. But in a title-theory jurisdiction (minority rule), granting a mortgage does sever the joint tenancy.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 4.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (explaining the lien theory of mortgages).\n\n20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 93 (2018) (discussing a joint tenant's ability to bind other tenants).","educationalObjective":"A joint tenant may only grant a mortgage on his/her joint-tenancy interest without the other joint tenant's consent. In a lien-theory jurisdiction (majority rule), granting a mortgage does not sever the joint tenancy. But in a title-theory jurisdiction (minority rule), granting a mortgage does sever the joint tenancy.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 4.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (explaining the lien theory of mortgages).\n\n20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 93 (2018) (discussing a joint tenant's ability to bind other tenants).","rule":"A joint tenant may only grant a mortgage on his/her joint-tenancy interest without the other joint tenant's consent. In a lien-theory jurisdiction (majority rule), granting a mortgage does not sever the joint tenancy. But in a title-theory jurisdiction (minority rule), granting a mortgage does sever the joint tenancy.","irac":{"issue":"What is the attorney likely to say regarding the mortgage?","rule":"A joint tenant may only grant a mortgage on his/her joint-tenancy interest without the other joint tenant's consent. In a lien-theory jurisdiction (majority rule), granting a mortgage does not sever the joint tenancy. But in a title-theory jurisdiction (minority rule), granting a mortgage does sever the joint tenancy.","analysis":"The man and the woman owned the property as joint tenants with the right of survivorship. A joint tenant may only grant a mortgage on his/her own joint-tenancy interest without the other joint tenant's consent. Therefore, a mortgage executed by one joint tenant will only attach to that tenant's property interest. But whether the joint tenancy remains intact depends on the jurisdiction: Lien theory (majority rule) – a mortgage is merely a lien on the property, which means that granting a mortgage does not sever the joint tenancy, but a severance will occur upon a foreclosure sale following a default\n\nTitle theory (minority rule) – a mortgage constitutes a transfer of title, which means that granting a mortgage does sever the joint tenancy and the mortgagor-tenant becomes tenants in common with the other joint tenants\n\nHere, the man mortgaged the property without the woman's consent, so the mortgage only encumbered the man's joint-tenancy interest—not the entire property (Choice A). And since this jurisdiction follows the lien theory of mortgages, the joint tenancy between the man and the woman was not severed when the man mortgaged his joint-tenancy interest. Instead, the joint tenancy remained intact until the man's death. When a joint tenant dies, that tenant's interest disappears and the remaining joint tenant's interest automatically expands to absorb it due to the right of survivorship. This means that any mortgage attached to a joint tenant's interest also disappears when that tenant dies and cannot be enforced against the deceased tenant's heirs or the surviving tenant (the son and the woman, respectively) (Choices B & C). Therefore, the mortgage on the man's tenancy expired upon his death, leaving the bank with no interest in the property on which to foreclose.","conclusion":"Answer D: The mortgage is not enforceable against the son or the woman, because the mortgage expired upon the man's death."},"questionZh":"一名男子和一名女子作为联权共有人拥有财产，并享有生存权。在女子不知情或未同意的情况下，男子向银行签署了一份十年期本票，并以房产抵押作为该票据的担保。抵押贷款立即被记录。两年后，该男子去世时没有立下遗嘱，留下他的儿子作为他唯一的继承人。女人是在男人死后才知道抵押贷款的。该妇女和儿子没有支付任何抵押贷款。结果，银行启动了司法止赎程序，将儿子和女子列为诉讼当事人。该妇女和儿子已就抵押贷款的可执行性咨询了律师。管辖权遵循抵押留置权理论。关于抵押贷款，律师可能会说什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"抵押贷款可以对儿子和妇女强制执行，因为抵押贷款抵押了整个财产。","B":"抵押贷款仅对儿子有效，因为他是该男子的唯一继承人。","C":"抵押贷款仅对女性有效，因为她是幸存的联名共有人。","D":"抵押贷款不适用于儿子或妇女，因为抵押贷款在男子去世后就到期了。"},"explanationZh":"男子和女子作为联权共有人拥有该房产，并享有生存权。未经其他联权共有人同意，联权共有人只能以其自己的联权共有权益授予抵押贷款。因此，由一名联名租户执行的抵押贷款将仅附加于该租户的财产权益。但联权共有权是否完整取决于司法管辖区： 留置权理论（多数规则）——抵押只是对财产的留置权，这意味着授予抵押权并不会切断联权共有权，但在违约后取消抵押品赎回权出售时会产生切断权\n\n所有权理论（少数规则）——抵押贷款构成所有权转让，这意味着授予抵押贷款确实会切断联权共有权，并且抵押人-承租人成为与其他联权共有人共同的承租人\n\n在这里，男方在未经女方同意的情况下抵押了财产，因此抵押贷款仅影响男方的共同共有权益，而不是整个财产（选项A）。由于该司法管辖区遵循抵押留置权理论，因此当男子抵押其联权共有权益时，男女之间的联权共有权并未被切断。相反，联名共有权一直保持不变，直到该男子去世。当联权共有人死亡时，该联权共有人的权益消失，剩余的联权共有人的权益由于生存权而自动扩大并吸收该权益。这意味着，当该租户去世时，附加到联名租户权益上的任何抵押贷款也会消失，并且不能对已故租户的继承人或幸存租户（分别为儿子和妇女）强制执行（选择 B 和 C）。因此，该男子的租赁抵押在他去世后就到期了，银行对取消抵押品赎回权的财产没有任何利息。\n\n教育目标：未经其他联权共有人同意，联权共有人只能就其联权共有权益授予抵押贷款。在留置权理论司法管辖区（多数规则），授予抵押贷款不会中断联合租赁。但在产权理论司法管辖区（少数服从多数规则），授予抵押贷款确实会切断联合租赁。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三）：抵押贷款 § 4.1（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（解释抵押贷款的留置权理论）。\n\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 93 (2018)（讨论联名租户约束其他租户的能力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"未经其他联权共有人同意，联权共有人只能就其联权共有权益授予抵押贷款。在留置权理论司法管辖区（多数规则），授予抵押贷款不会中断联合租赁。但在产权理论司法管辖区（少数服从多数规则），授予抵押贷款确实会切断联合租赁。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于抵押贷款，律师可能会说什么？","rule":"未经其他联权共有人同意，联权共有人只能就其联权共有权益授予抵押贷款。在留置权理论司法管辖区（多数规则），授予抵押贷款不会中断联合租赁。但在产权理论司法管辖区（少数服从多数规则），授予抵押贷款确实会切断联合租赁。","analysis":"男子和女子作为联权共有人拥有该房产，并享有生存权。未经其他联权共有人同意，联权共有人只能以其自己的联权共有权益授予抵押贷款。因此，由一名联名租户执行的抵押贷款将仅附加于该租户的财产权益。但联权共有权是否完整取决于司法管辖区： 留置权理论（多数规则）——抵押只是对财产的留置权，这意味着授予抵押权并不会切断联权共有权，但在违约后取消抵押品赎回权出售时会产生切断权\n\n所有权理论（少数规则）——抵押贷款构成所有权转让，这意味着授予抵押贷款确实会切断联权共有权，并且抵押人-承租人成为与其他联权共有人共同的承租人\n\n在这里，男方在未经女方同意的情况下抵押了财产，因此抵押贷款仅影响男方的共同共有权益，而不是整个财产（选项A）。由于该司法管辖区遵循抵押留置权理论，因此当男子抵押其联权共有权益时，男女之间的联权共有权并未被切断。相反，联名共有权一直保持不变，直到该男子去世。当联权共有人死亡时，该联权共有人的权益消失，剩余的联权共有人的权益由于生存权而自动扩大并吸收该权益。这意味着，当该租户去世时，附加到联名租户权益上的任何抵押贷款也会消失，并且不能对已故租户的继承人或幸存租户（分别为儿子和妇女）强制执行（选择 B 和 C）。因此，该男子的租赁抵押在他去世后就到期了，银行对取消抵押品赎回权的财产没有任何利息。\n\n教育目标：未经其他联权共有人同意，联权共有人只能就其联权共有权益授予抵押贷款。在留置权理论司法管辖区（多数规则），授予抵押贷款不会中断联合租赁。但在产权理论司法管辖区（少数服从多数规则），授予抵押贷款确实会切断联合租赁。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三）：抵押贷款 § 4.1（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（解释抵押贷款的留置权理论）。\n\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 93 (2018)（讨论联名租户约束其他租户的能力）。","conclusion":"答案 D：抵押贷款不适用于儿子或妇女，因为抵押贷款在男子去世后就到期了。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-03ab33ea0e73e9fe"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":130,"sourceQuestionPage":381,"sourceQuestionPages":[381],"sourceExplanationPages":[382,383],"sourceAnswerPages":[382,383],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-131","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Co-ownership","question":"A rectangular parcel of undeveloped land contained three acres and had 150 feet of frontage on a public street. The applicable zoning ordinance required that a buildable lot contain at least two acres and have frontage of not less than 100 feet on a public street. A brother and sister owned the land as tenants in common, the brother owning a one-third interest and the sister owning a two-thirds interest. Neither of them owned any other real property. The sister brought an appropriate action to partition the land and proposed that a two-acre rectangular lot with 100 feet of frontage be set off to her and that a one-acre rectangular lot with 50 feet of frontage be set off to the brother. The brother's defense included a demand that the land be sold and its proceeds be divided one-third to the brother and two-thirds to the sister. Who will prevail?","choices":{"A":"The brother, because partition by sale is the preferred remedy, unless a fair price is not the likely result of a sale.","B":"The brother, because the zoning ordinance makes it impossible to divide the land fairly.","C":"The sister, because partition by sale is not appropriate if the subject property can be physically divided.","D":"The sister, because the ratio of the two lots that would result from her proposal conforms exactly to the ownership ratio."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Tenancy in common is shared ownership of property where each co-tenant has a separate share of the property, but the right to possess and enjoy the entire property (ie, separate but undivided interests). A co-tenant may bring an action to partition (ie, divide) the property and, absent an agreement to the contrary, a court must grant that request. There are two types of partition: In kind – physical division of the property into distinct lots that are proportionate to each co-tenant's ownership interest\n\nBy sale – forced sale of the property where the proceeds are divided in accordance with each co-tenant's ownership interest\n\nPartition in kind is the preferred method of partition. But when physical division of the land is impossible, impracticable, or inequitable, a court will grant a partition by sale (Choices A & C). Here, the brother and sister are tenants in common on three acres of undeveloped land with 150 feet of street frontage. The sister proposed a partition in kind where the brother would receive his one-third interest (one acre with 50 feet of frontage). But the ordinance makes it impossible to divide the land fairly since it would prohibit the brother from building on his lot. Therefore, the brother will prevail in his demand for a partition by sale. (Choice D) Although the sister's proposal divides the land in conformity with the ownership ratio, a partition in kind would be inequitable since the brother would not be able to build on his lot. Therefore, the land will be partitioned by sale and the proceeds will be divided proportionately—ie, one-third to the brother and two-thirds to the sister.\n\nEducational objective: In a tenancy in common, a co-tenant may bring an action for partition in kind or by sale. If a partition in kind (ie, physical division of the land) is impossible, impracticable, or inequitable, a court will grant a partition by sale.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (First) of Property § 72 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (right to partition).","educationalObjective":"In a tenancy in common, a co-tenant may bring an action for partition in kind or by sale. If a partition in kind (ie, physical division of the land) is impossible, impracticable, or inequitable, a court will grant a partition by sale.","references":"Restatement (First) of Property § 72 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (right to partition).","rule":"In a tenancy in common, a co-tenant may bring an action for partition in kind or by sale. If a partition in kind (ie, physical division of the land) is impossible, impracticable, or inequitable, a court will grant a partition by sale.","irac":{"issue":"Who will prevail?","rule":"In a tenancy in common, a co-tenant may bring an action for partition in kind or by sale. If a partition in kind (ie, physical division of the land) is impossible, impracticable, or inequitable, a court will grant a partition by sale.","analysis":"Tenancy in common is shared ownership of property where each co-tenant has a separate share of the property, but the right to possess and enjoy the entire property (ie, separate but undivided interests). A co-tenant may bring an action to partition (ie, divide) the property and, absent an agreement to the contrary, a court must grant that request. There are two types of partition: In kind – physical division of the property into distinct lots that are proportionate to each co-tenant's ownership interest\n\nBy sale – forced sale of the property where the proceeds are divided in accordance with each co-tenant's ownership interest\n\nPartition in kind is the preferred method of partition. But when physical division of the land is impossible, impracticable, or inequitable, a court will grant a partition by sale (Choices A & C). Here, the brother and sister are tenants in common on three acres of undeveloped land with 150 feet of street frontage. The sister proposed a partition in kind where the brother would receive his one-third interest (one acre with 50 feet of frontage). But the ordinance makes it impossible to divide the land fairly since it would prohibit the brother from building on his lot. Therefore, the brother will prevail in his demand for a partition by sale. (Choice D) Although the sister's proposal divides the land in conformity with the ownership ratio, a partition in kind would be inequitable since the brother would not be able to build on his lot. Therefore, the land will be partitioned by sale and the proceeds will be divided proportionately—ie, one-third to the brother and two-thirds to the sister.","conclusion":"Answer B: The brother, because the zoning ordinance makes it impossible to divide the land fairly."},"questionZh":"一块长方形的未开发土地占地 3 英亩，临街面积为 150 英尺。适用的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例要求可建设地块至少包含两英亩，并且在公共街道上的临街面积不少于 100 英尺。一对兄弟姐妹作为共同佃户拥有这片土地，兄弟拥有三分之一的权益，姐妹拥有三分之二的权益。他们两人都不拥有任何其他不动产。姐姐提出了适当的分割土地的诉讼，并建议将一块两英亩、临街面100英尺的矩形地块划给她，并将一块一英亩、临面面50英尺的矩形地块划给哥哥。兄弟的辩护包括要求出售土地，并将其收益分成三分之一给兄弟，三分之二给姐妹。谁会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"兄弟，因为通过出售分割是首选的补救措施，除非公平的价格不是出售的可能结果。","B":"兄弟，因为土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例使得无法公平地划分土地。","C":"妹妹，因为如果标的财产可以物理分割，那么通过出售分割是不合适的。","D":"姐姐，因为她提议的两块土地的比例完全符合所有权比例。"},"explanationZh":"共同共有（joint tenancy）是财产的共同所有权，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）拥有单独的财产份额，但有权占有和享受整个财产（即单独但不可分割的利益）。按份共有人（tenant in common）可以提起分割（即分割）财产的诉讼，如果没有相反协议，法院必须批准该请求。有两种类型的分割： 实物分割 – 将财产物理分割为与每个按份共有人（tenant in common）的所有权权益成比例的不同地块\n\n通过出售——强制出售财产，收益根据每个按份共有人（tenant in common）的所有权权益进行分配\n\n实物土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）是首选的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）方法。但是，当对土地进行物理分割是不可能、不切实际或不公平时，法院将批准通过出售进行分割（选择 A 和 C）。在这里，兄妹俩是三英亩未开发土地的按份共有人（tenant in common），临街面积为 150 英尺。姐姐提议以实物方式进行土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning），哥哥将获得三分之一的权益（一英亩，临街面积 50 英尺）。但该法令使得公平分配土地变得不可能，因为它将禁止兄弟在他的土地上建造房屋。因此，弟兄提出的以出售方式分割的要求，将会获得胜利。 （选项D） 虽然姐姐的提议是按照所有权比例分割土地，但以实物方式分割土地是不公平的，因为哥哥无法在自己的土地上进行建设。因此，土地将通过出售进行分割，收益将按比例分配——即三分之一给兄弟，三分之二给姐妹。\n\n教育目标：在共同共有（joint tenancy）中，按份共有人（tenant in common）可以提起实物分割或出售分割的诉讼。如果实物分割（即土地的实际分割）是不可能、不切实际或不公平的，法院将批准通过出售进行分割。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 72（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（分割权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在共同共有（joint tenancy）中，按份共有人（tenant in common）可以提起实物分割或出售分割的诉讼。如果实物分割（即土地的实际分割）是不可能、不切实际或不公平的，法院将批准通过出售进行分割。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁会获胜？","rule":"在共同共有（joint tenancy）中，按份共有人（tenant in common）可以提起实物分割或出售分割的诉讼。如果实物分割（即土地的实际分割）是不可能、不切实际或不公平的，法院将批准通过出售进行分割。","analysis":"共同共有（joint tenancy）是财产的共同所有权，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）拥有单独的财产份额，但有权占有和享受整个财产（即单独但不可分割的利益）。按份共有人（tenant in common）可以提起分割（即分割）财产的诉讼，如果没有相反协议，法院必须批准该请求。有两种类型的分割： 实物分割 – 将财产物理分割为与每个按份共有人（tenant in common）的所有权权益成比例的不同地块\n\n通过出售——强制出售财产，收益根据每个按份共有人（tenant in common）的所有权权益进行分配\n\n实物土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）是首选的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）方法。但是，当对土地进行物理分割是不可能、不切实际或不公平时，法院将批准通过出售进行分割（选择 A 和 C）。在这里，兄妹俩是三英亩未开发土地的按份共有人（tenant in common），临街面积为 150 英尺。姐姐提议以实物方式进行土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning），哥哥将获得三分之一的权益（一英亩，临街面积 50 英尺）。但该法令使得公平分配土地变得不可能，因为它将禁止兄弟在他的土地上建造房屋。因此，弟兄提出的以出售方式分割的要求，将会获得胜利。 （选项D） 虽然姐姐的提议是按照所有权比例分割土地，但以实物方式分割土地是不公平的，因为哥哥无法在自己的土地上进行建设。因此，土地将通过出售进行分割，收益将按比例分配——即三分之一给兄弟，三分之二给姐妹。\n\n教育目标：在共同共有（joint tenancy）中，按份共有人（tenant in common）可以提起实物分割或出售分割的诉讼。如果实物分割（即土地的实际分割）是不可能、不切实际或不公平的，法院将批准通过出售进行分割。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 72（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（分割权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：兄弟，因为土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例使得无法公平地划分土地。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"zoning ordinance","ipa":"/zˈoʊnɪŋ ˈɔɹdənəns/","syllables":"zon·ing or·di·nance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"土地分区条例：地方政府按区域规范土地用途和建筑的法规","contextualMeaning":"土地分区条例：地方政府按区域规范土地用途和建筑的法规","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The applicable zoning ordinance required that a buildable lot contain at least two acres and have frontage of not less than 100 feet on a public street.","synonyms":["land-use ordinance"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-fcb4389b03540b10"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":131,"sourceQuestionPage":384,"sourceQuestionPages":[384],"sourceExplanationPages":[385,386],"sourceAnswerPages":[385,386],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-132","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A man died, leaving a will that devised his land to \"my wife for life, remainder to my son and the heirs of his body. But if my son dies without children, then the land shall go to my nephew.\" Subsequently the wife died, devising all of her estate to the son. The son then sold the land to a neighbor. The son died shortly thereafter, survived only by a daughter. The daughter has now brought an appropriate action to quiet title against the neighbor and the nephew. The common law Rule Against Perpetuities is unmodified in the jurisdiction, and the fee tail has been abolished by statute. In this action, who will likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"The daughter, because the son's conveyance to the neighbor extinguished the nephew's interest.","B":"The neighbor, because the son held the land in fee simple absolute.","C":"The nephew, because he held the remainder following the son's interest.","D":"The nephew, because the language in the man's will did not create an interest in the son."},"answer":"B","explanation":"There are three types of freehold estates, all of which can be held as a present interest or future interest: Fee simple – lasts for an indefinite and potentially infinite duration (eg, \"to A and his heirs\") Fee tail – lasts until the original grantee's lineage dies out (eg, \"to A and the heirs of his body\") Life estate – terminates upon the death of an individual serving as the governing life (eg, \"to A for life\") Fee tails were once commonly used to pass land from generation to generation through a series of life estates in the original grantee's heirs. Today, the fee tail has been abolished by statute in nearly every jurisdiction—including this one.* As a result, these jurisdictions typically interpret the fee tail as giving the first grantee a fee simple absolute, which has no corresponding future interest due to its indefinite duration. Here, the man devised the son a fee tail that became possessory when the mother's life estate terminated upon her death (Choice D). Since this jurisdiction likely interprets the fee tail as a fee simple absolute, neither the daughter nor the nephew held a future interest in the estate (Choices A & C). And when the son sold the land to the neighbor, the neighbor acquired the son's fee simple absolute. Therefore, the neighbor will likely prevail in this quiet title action. *In jurisdictions that still recognize the fee tail, the fee-tail holder can eliminate the fee tail (ie, disentail) by conveying that interest in fee simple absolute to a third party, who then acquires the property in fee simple absolute.\n\nEducational objective: Nearly every jurisdiction has abolished the fee tail, and a conveyance of this estate is commonly interpreted as giving the first grantee a fee simple absolute.","educationalObjective":"Nearly every jurisdiction has abolished the fee tail, and a conveyance of this estate is commonly interpreted as giving the first grantee a fee simple absolute.","references":"","rule":"Nearly every jurisdiction has abolished the fee tail, and a conveyance of this estate is commonly interpreted as giving the first grantee a fee simple absolute.","irac":{"issue":"In this action, who will likely prevail?","rule":"Nearly every jurisdiction has abolished the fee tail, and a conveyance of this estate is commonly interpreted as giving the first grantee a fee simple absolute.","analysis":"There are three types of freehold estates, all of which can be held as a present interest or future interest: Fee simple – lasts for an indefinite and potentially infinite duration (eg, \"to A and his heirs\") Fee tail – lasts until the original grantee's lineage dies out (eg, \"to A and the heirs of his body\") Life estate – terminates upon the death of an individual serving as the governing life (eg, \"to A for life\") Fee tails were once commonly used to pass land from generation to generation through a series of life estates in the original grantee's heirs. Today, the fee tail has been abolished by statute in nearly every jurisdiction—including this one.* As a result, these jurisdictions typically interpret the fee tail as giving the first grantee a fee simple absolute, which has no corresponding future interest due to its indefinite duration. Here, the man devised the son a fee tail that became possessory when the mother's life estate terminated upon her death (Choice D). Since this jurisdiction likely interprets the fee tail as a fee simple absolute, neither the daughter nor the nephew held a future interest in the estate (Choices A & C). And when the son sold the land to the neighbor, the neighbor acquired the son's fee simple absolute. Therefore, the neighbor will likely prevail in this quiet title action. *In jurisdictions that still recognize the fee tail, the fee-tail holder can eliminate the fee tail (ie, disentail) by conveying that interest in fee simple absolute to a third party, who then acquires the property in fee simple absolute.","conclusion":"Answer B: The neighbor, because the son held the land in fee simple absolute."},"questionZh":"一个人死了，留下了一份遗嘱，将他的土地划给“我的妻子终身，其余部分归我的儿子和他身体的继承人。但如果我的儿子死后没有孩子，那么土地将归我的侄子”。随后妻子去世，将她所有的财产留给了儿子。儿子随后将土地卖给了邻居。儿子不久就去世了，只剩下一个女儿。女儿现已提起适当诉讼，以平息对邻居和侄子的所有权。普通法中的“反永续规则”在管辖范围内未作修改，并且费用尾部已被法规废除。在这场行动中，谁会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿，因为儿子把房产转让给邻居，浇灭了侄子的兴趣。","B":"邻居，因为儿子占有土地费简单绝对。","C":"侄子，因为他按照儿子的利益持有剩余部分。","D":"侄子，因为男子遗嘱中的语言并没有对儿子产生兴趣。"},"explanationZh":"永久业权共有三种类型，所有这些都可以作为当前权益或未来权益持有： 简单费 – 持续无限期且可能无限期（例如，“A 及其继承人”） 尾费 – 持续到原始受让人的血统消失为止（例如，“A 及其身体的继承人”） 终生财产 – 在作为统治生命的个人去世时终止（例如，“终身为 A”） 尾费曾经很常见用于通过原始受让人的继承人的一系列终身遗产将土地代代相传。如今，几乎每个司法管辖区（包括这个司法管辖区）都已通过法规废除了费用尾部。*因此，这些司法管辖区通常将费用尾部解释为向第一个受让人提供简单绝对费用，由于其无限期，因此没有相应的未来利息。在这里，该男子为儿子设计了一条费尾，当母亲的终身财产因她去世而终止时，该费尾就成为占有者（选项D）。由于该司法管辖区可能将费用尾部解释为简单绝对费用，因此女儿和侄子都不拥有该遗产的未来权益（选择 A 和 C）。当儿子将土地卖给邻居时，邻居获得了儿子的绝对费用。因此，邻居很可能会在这场安静的产权诉讼中获胜。 *在仍然承认费用尾部的司法管辖区，费用尾部持有者可以通过将绝对完全所有权（fee simple absolute）权益转让给第三方来消除费用尾部（即解除），然后第三方获得绝对完全所有权（fee simple absolute）财产。\n\n教育目标：几乎每个司法管辖区都废除了费用尾部，并且该遗产的转让通常被解释为给予第一个受让人简单的绝对费用。","educationalObjectiveZh":"几乎每个司法管辖区都废除了费用尾部，并且该遗产的转让通常被解释为向第一个受让人提供简单绝对费用。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这场行动中，谁会获胜？","rule":"几乎每个司法管辖区都废除了费用尾部，并且该遗产的转让通常被解释为向第一个受让人提供简单绝对费用。","analysis":"永久业权共有三种类型，所有这些都可以作为当前权益或未来权益持有： 简单费 – 持续无限期且可能无限期（例如，“A 及其继承人”） 尾费 – 持续到原始受让人的血统消失为止（例如，“A 及其身体的继承人”） 终生财产 – 在作为统治生命的个人去世时终止（例如，“终身为 A”） 尾费曾经很常见用于通过原始受让人的继承人的一系列终身遗产将土地代代相传。如今，几乎每个司法管辖区（包括这个司法管辖区）都已通过法规废除了费用尾部。*因此，这些司法管辖区通常将费用尾部解释为向第一个受让人提供简单绝对费用，由于其无限期，因此没有相应的未来利息。在这里，该男子为儿子设计了一条费尾，当母亲的终身财产因她去世而终止时，该费尾就成为占有者（选项D）。由于该司法管辖区可能将费用尾部解释为简单绝对费用，因此女儿和侄子都不拥有该遗产的未来权益（选择 A 和 C）。当儿子将土地卖给邻居时，邻居获得了儿子的绝对费用。因此，邻居很可能会在这场安静的产权诉讼中获胜。 *在仍然承认费用尾部的司法管辖区，费用尾部持有者可以通过将绝对完全所有权（fee simple absolute）权益转让给第三方来消除费用尾部（即解除），然后第三方获得绝对完全所有权（fee simple absolute）财产。\n\n教育目标：几乎每个司法管辖区都废除了费用尾部，并且该遗产的转让通常被解释为给予第一个受让人简单的绝对费用。","conclusion":"答案 B：邻居，因为儿子占有土地费简单绝对。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-6ee58be52ca74433"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":132,"sourceQuestionPage":387,"sourceQuestionPages":[387],"sourceExplanationPages":[388],"sourceAnswerPages":[388],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-133","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"An individual owned land in fee simple. She executed a will, which devised the land \"to my husband for life, and then to my daughter, but if my grandson graduates from college, to my grandson.\" At the time of the individual's death, her husband, daughter, and grandson are alive, and her grandson is in high school. What interest in the land does the individual's grandson have?","choices":{"A":"Contingent remainder.","B":"Power of termination.","C":"Shifting executory interest.","D":"Springing executory interest."},"answer":"C","explanation":"An executory interest is a future interest in a grantee that generally cuts short, or divests, a prior estate upon the happening of a specified condition. There are two types of executory interests: Shifting – divests an estate held by another grantee, such that the estate shifts from one grantee to another (the executory-interest holder) Springing – divests an estate held by a grantor, such that the estate springs from the grantor to the executory-interest holder Here, the grandson (grantee) has a future interest in the land because he is not entitled to immediate possession. Since his interest will cut short the daughter's (other grantee's) interest in the land if and when he graduates from college, the grandson has a shifting executory interest (Choice D). (Choice A) A remainder is a future interest created in a grantee that is capable of becoming a present possessory estate upon the natural expiration of a life estate or term of years.* Here, the daughter (not the grandson) has a remainder interest that will become possessory as soon as the husband dies and his life estate naturally ends. *A remainder can also follow a fee tail, but this estate has been eliminated in most states. (Choice B) A power of termination (ie, right of entry) is a future interest created in a grantor. It follows a fee simple subject to a condition subsequent and allows the grantor or his/her successor in interest to terminate the estate upon the happening of a specified condition. Here, the future interest in question belongs to the grandson—not the individual (grantor)—so it cannot be a power of termination.\n\nEducational objective: A shifting executory interest divests an estate held by another grantee, such that the estate shifts from one grantee to another (the executory-interest holder). In contrast, a springing executory interest divests an estate held by a grantor, such that the estate springs from the grantor to the executory-interest holder.","educationalObjective":"A shifting executory interest divests an estate held by another grantee, such that the estate shifts from one grantee to another (the executory-interest holder). In contrast, a springing executory interest divests an estate held by a grantor, such that the estate springs from the grantor to the executory-interest holder.","references":"","rule":"A shifting executory interest divests an estate held by another grantee, such that the estate shifts from one grantee to another (the executory-interest holder). In contrast, a springing executory interest divests an estate held by a grantor, such that the estate springs from the grantor to the executory-interest holder.","irac":{"issue":"What interest in the land does the individual's grandson have?","rule":"A shifting executory interest divests an estate held by another grantee, such that the estate shifts from one grantee to another (the executory-interest holder). In contrast, a springing executory interest divests an estate held by a grantor, such that the estate springs from the grantor to the executory-interest holder.","analysis":"An executory interest is a future interest in a grantee that generally cuts short, or divests, a prior estate upon the happening of a specified condition. There are two types of executory interests: Shifting – divests an estate held by another grantee, such that the estate shifts from one grantee to another (the executory-interest holder) Springing – divests an estate held by a grantor, such that the estate springs from the grantor to the executory-interest holder Here, the grandson (grantee) has a future interest in the land because he is not entitled to immediate possession. Since his interest will cut short the daughter's (other grantee's) interest in the land if and when he graduates from college, the grandson has a shifting executory interest (Choice D). (Choice A) A remainder is a future interest created in a grantee that is capable of becoming a present possessory estate upon the natural expiration of a life estate or term of years.* Here, the daughter (not the grandson) has a remainder interest that will become possessory as soon as the husband dies and his life estate naturally ends. *A remainder can also follow a fee tail, but this estate has been eliminated in most states. (Choice B) A power of termination (ie, right of entry) is a future interest created in a grantor. It follows a fee simple subject to a condition subsequent and allows the grantor or his/her successor in interest to terminate the estate upon the happening of a specified condition. Here, the future interest in question belongs to the grandson—not the individual (grantor)—so it cannot be a power of termination.","conclusion":"Answer C: Shifting executory interest."},"questionZh":"个人拥有土地，费用简单。她签署了一份遗嘱，将这片土地“划给我的丈夫终身，然后划给我的女儿，但如果我的孙子大学毕业，则划给我的孙子”。在该人死亡时，她的丈夫、女儿和孙子还活着，她的孙子正在上高中。该个人的孙子对土地有什么兴趣？","choicesZh":{"A":"或有余数。","B":"终止权。","C":"转移执行利益。","D":"执行兴趣不断涌现。"},"explanationZh":"执行权益是受让人的未来权益，通常在特定条件发生时缩短或剥离先前的遗产。执行权益有两种类型： 转移 – 剥离另一位受让人持有的遗产，使得该遗产从一个受让人转移到另一位（执行权益持有人） 涌现 – 剥离授予人持有的遗产，使得该遗产从授予人转移到执行权益持有人 这里，孙子（受让人）对土地拥有未来权益，因为他无权立即占有。由于如果他大学毕业，他的权益将缩短女儿（其他受赠者）对土地的权益，因此孙子的执行权益会发生变化（选择 D）。 （选项 A） 剩余权益是在受让人身上创建的未来权益，在终身财产或年限自然到期时能够成为当前占有性财产。*在这里，女儿（不是孙子）拥有剩余权益，一旦丈夫去世且其终身财产自然终止，该剩余权益将成为占有性财产。 *剩余部分也可以跟随在费用尾部之后，但这种遗产在大多数州已被消除。 （选择B） 终止权（即进入权）是在授予人中创建的未来权益。它遵循简单的费用，并受后续条件的约束，并允许授予人或其利益继承人在发生特定条件时终止遗产。在这里，所讨论的未来利益属于孙子，而不是个人（设保人），因此它不能成为终止权。\n\n教育目标：转移的执行权益剥离了另一位受让人持有的遗产，从而使遗产从一个受让人转移到另一位受让人（执行权益持有人）。相比之下，新兴执行权益剥离了设保人持有的遗产，使得遗产从设保人转移到执行权益持有人。","educationalObjectiveZh":"转移执行权益会剥离另一位受让人持有的遗产，从而使遗产从一个受让人转移到另一位受让人（执行权益持有人）。相比之下，新兴执行权益剥离了设保人持有的遗产，使得遗产从设保人转移到执行权益持有人。","iracZh":{"issue":"该个人的孙子对土地有什么兴趣？","rule":"转移执行权益会剥离另一位受让人持有的遗产，从而使遗产从一个受让人转移到另一位受让人（执行权益持有人）。相比之下，新兴执行权益剥离了设保人持有的遗产，使得遗产从设保人转移到执行权益持有人。","analysis":"执行权益是受让人的未来权益，通常在特定条件发生时缩短或剥离先前的遗产。执行权益有两种类型： 转移 – 剥离另一位受让人持有的遗产，使得该遗产从一个受让人转移到另一位（执行权益持有人） 涌现 – 剥离授予人持有的遗产，使得该遗产从授予人转移到执行权益持有人 这里，孙子（受让人）对土地拥有未来权益，因为他无权立即占有。由于如果他大学毕业，他的权益将缩短女儿（其他受赠者）对土地的权益，因此孙子的执行权益会发生变化（选择 D）。 （选项 A） 剩余权益是在受让人身上创建的未来权益，在终身财产或年限自然到期时能够成为当前占有性财产。*在这里，女儿（不是孙子）拥有剩余权益，一旦丈夫去世且其终身财产自然终止，该剩余权益将成为占有性财产。 *剩余部分也可以跟随在费用尾部之后，但这种遗产在大多数州已被消除。 （选择B） 终止权（即进入权）是在授予人中创建的未来权益。它遵循简单的费用，并受后续条件的约束，并允许授予人或其利益继承人在发生特定条件时终止遗产。在这里，所讨论的未来利益属于孙子，而不是个人（设保人），因此它不能成为终止权。\n\n教育目标：转移的执行权益剥离了另一位受让人持有的遗产，从而使遗产从一个受让人转移到另一位受让人（执行权益持有人）。相比之下，新兴执行权益剥离了设保人持有的遗产，使得遗产从设保人转移到执行权益持有人。","conclusion":"答案 C：转移执行利益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"power of termination","ipa":"/pˈaʊɝ ˈəv tɝmʌnˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"power of ter·mi·na·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"终止权：附解除条件权益发生条件时出让人收回土地的权力","contextualMeaning":"终止权：附解除条件权益发生条件时出让人收回土地的权力","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Power of termination.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-44517d95eb8ac5e3"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":133,"sourceQuestionPage":389,"sourceQuestionPages":[389],"sourceExplanationPages":[390,391],"sourceAnswerPages":[390,391],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-134","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"An owner of land devised the land to a specific university \"for the purpose of conducting field research for the improvement of agricultural products and for no other purpose.\" The owner devised all of her other real property interests to her granddaughter. The owner's only heir was her son. Later, the university decided to build a science classroom on the land even though using the land for the enumerated purpose was still practicable. The owner's granddaughter, asserting ownership of the land, sought to evict the university from the land. There is no applicable statute, and the common law Rule Against Perpetuities is unmodified in the jurisdiction. If the court rules in favor of the university, what is the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The cy pres doctrine applies to the university's current use of the land.","B":"The granddaughter's executory interest in the land is void under the Rule Against Perpetuities.","C":"The owner's son, not the granddaughter, is the proper plaintiff to seek eviction.","D":"The university owns the land in fee simple absolute because the devise did not include conditional or durational language."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nLike a fee simple absolute (FSA), a defeasible fee is ownership of potentially infinite duration. But unlike an FSA, a defeasible fee can be terminated by the occurrence of a stated event. Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language (eg, \"so long as,\" \"but if\"). However, language that limits only the purpose of the transfer (eg, \"for the purpose of\") creates an FSA. Here, the owner's devise did not include conditional or durational language. Instead, the owner devised the land to the university \"for the purpose of conducting field research for the improvement of agricultural products and for no other purpose.\" This conveyance created an FSA—not a defeasible fee—so the university is free to do with the land as it sees fit (eg, build a science classroom thereon). For that reason, the court will most likely rule in favor of the university. (Choice A) The cy pres doctrine allows courts to reform the conveyance of a future interest—not the university's present interest—to come as close as possible to the transferor's intent while preventing it from violating the Rule Against Perpetuities.* Additionally, this doctrine only applies to a charitable gift or trust when the charitable objective is or later becomes impossible or impracticable to fulfill (not seen here). (Choices B & C) The owner's conveyance created an FSA, which has no associated executory interest or other future interest. As a result, neither the son nor the granddaughter has an interest in the land. But had the owner conveyed a defeasible fee and retained the associated future interest, the granddaughter (not the son) would hold that\n\nfuture interest. That is because the owner left all of her other real property interests to the granddaughter. *The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) does not apply to future interests held by the grantor. Therefore, had the granddaughter succeeded to a future interest held by the owner (eg, possibility of reverter), it would not be subject to RAP.\n\nEducational objective: Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language (eg, \"so long as,\" \"but if\"). Language that limits only the purpose of the transfer creates a fee simple absolute.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 44 cmt. m (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing designated purpose of conveyance).","educationalObjective":"Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language (eg, \"so long as,\" \"but if\"). Language that limits only the purpose of the transfer creates a fee simple absolute.","references":"Restatement of Property § 44 cmt. m (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing designated purpose of conveyance).","rule":"Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language (eg, \"so long as,\" \"but if\"). Language that limits only the purpose of the transfer creates a fee simple absolute.","irac":{"issue":"If the court rules in favor of the university, what is the most likely reason?","rule":"Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language (eg, \"so long as,\" \"but if\"). Language that limits only the purpose of the transfer creates a fee simple absolute.","analysis":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nLike a fee simple absolute (FSA), a defeasible fee is ownership of potentially infinite duration. But unlike an FSA, a defeasible fee can be terminated by the occurrence of a stated event. Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language (eg, \"so long as,\" \"but if\"). However, language that limits only the purpose of the transfer (eg, \"for the purpose of\") creates an FSA. Here, the owner's devise did not include conditional or durational language. Instead, the owner devised the land to the university \"for the purpose of conducting field research for the improvement of agricultural products and for no other purpose.\" This conveyance created an FSA—not a defeasible fee—so the university is free to do with the land as it sees fit (eg, build a science classroom thereon). For that reason, the court will most likely rule in favor of the university. (Choice A) The cy pres doctrine allows courts to reform the conveyance of a future interest—not the university's present interest—to come as close as possible to the transferor's intent while preventing it from violating the Rule Against Perpetuities.* Additionally, this doctrine only applies to a charitable gift or trust when the charitable objective is or later becomes impossible or impracticable to fulfill (not seen here). (Choices B & C) The owner's conveyance created an FSA, which has no associated executory interest or other future interest. As a result, neither the son nor the granddaughter has an interest in the land. But had the owner conveyed a defeasible fee and retained the associated future interest, the granddaughter (not the son) would hold that\n\nfuture interest. That is because the owner left all of her other real property interests to the granddaughter. *The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) does not apply to future interests held by the grantor. Therefore, had the granddaughter succeeded to a future interest held by the owner (eg, possibility of reverter), it would not be subject to RAP.","conclusion":"Answer D: The university owns the land in fee simple absolute because the devise did not include conditional or durational language."},"questionZh":"一位土地所有者将土地划给一所特定的大学，“目的是进行实地研究以改进农产品，没有其他目的”。业主将她所有其他不动产权益设计给了她的孙女。业主唯一的继承人是她的儿子。后来，尽管使用该土地用于所列举的目的仍然可行，但大学还是决定在该土地上建造一个科学教室。业主的孙女声称拥有这片土地，并试图将大学从这片土地上驱逐出去。没有适用的法规，并且普通法的反永续规则在管辖范围内未作修改。如果法院做出有利于大学的裁决，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"cy pres原则适用于大学目前对土地的使用。","B":"根据“反对永久规则”，孙女对土地的执行权益无效。","C":"业主的儿子，而不是孙女，是寻求驱逐的适当原告。","D":"该大学完全拥有土地，因为该设计不包括条件或持续语言。"},"explanationZh":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n与简单绝对费用 (FSA) 一样，可废止的费用是潜在无限期的所有权。但与 FSA 不同的是，可废止的费用可以因规定事件的发生而终止。可废止的费用受到特定期限或条件语言（例如“只要”、“但是如果”）的限制。然而，仅限制转让目的的语言（例如“为了……的目的”）会创建 FSA。在这里，所有者的设计不包括条件或持续语言。相反，业主将土地划给大学“是为了进行实地研究以改进农产品，没有其他目的”。这种转让产生了 FSA，而不是可取消的费用，因此大学可以自由地按照自己认为合适的方式处理土地（例如，在其上建造科学教室）。因此，法院很可能会做出有利于大学的裁决。 （选择 A）半实物原则允许法院改革未来利益（而不是大学当前利益）的转让，以尽可能接近转让人的意图，同时防止其违反“反对永久规则”。*此外，该原则仅适用于当慈善目标是或后来变得不可能或不切实际时的慈善捐赠或信托（此处未看到）。 （选择 B 和 C） 业主的产权转让创建了 FSA，该 FSA 没有相关的执行权益或其他未来权益。因此，儿子和孙女都对这块土地没有兴趣。但是，如果所有者传达了可废止的费用并保留了相关的未来权益，那么孙女（而不是儿子）将认为\n\n未来的兴趣。这是因为业主将她所有其他不动产权益留给了孙女。 *反对永续规则（RAP）不适用于授予人持有的未来权益。因此，如果孙女继承了所有者持有的未来权益（例如，归还者的可能性），则无需缴纳移民安置计划。\n\n教育目标：可废止的费用受到特定持续时间或条件语言的限制（例如，“只要”、“但是如果”）。仅限制转账目的的语言会产生简单的绝对费用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述§ 44 cmt。 m（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论指定的运输目的）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"可废止的费用受到特定期限或条件语言（例如“只要”、“但是如果”）的限制。仅限制转账目的的语言会产生简单的绝对费用。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院做出有利于大学的裁决，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"可废止的费用受到特定期限或条件语言（例如“只要”、“但是如果”）的限制。仅限制转账目的的语言会产生简单的绝对费用。","analysis":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n与简单绝对费用 (FSA) 一样，可废止的费用是潜在无限期的所有权。但与 FSA 不同的是，可废止的费用可以因规定事件的发生而终止。可废止的费用受到特定期限或条件语言（例如“只要”、“但是如果”）的限制。然而，仅限制转让目的的语言（例如“为了……的目的”）会创建 FSA。在这里，所有者的设计不包括条件或持续语言。相反，业主将土地划给大学“是为了进行实地研究以改进农产品，没有其他目的”。这种转让产生了 FSA，而不是可取消的费用，因此大学可以自由地按照自己认为合适的方式处理土地（例如，在其上建造科学教室）。因此，法院很可能会做出有利于大学的裁决。 （选择 A）半实物原则允许法院改革未来利益（而不是大学当前利益）的转让，以尽可能接近转让人的意图，同时防止其违反“反对永久规则”。*此外，该原则仅适用于当慈善目标是或后来变得不可能或不切实际时的慈善捐赠或信托（此处未看到）。 （选择 B 和 C） 业主的产权转让创建了 FSA，该 FSA 没有相关的执行权益或其他未来权益。因此，儿子和孙女都对这块土地没有兴趣。但是，如果所有者传达了可废止的费用并保留了相关的未来权益，那么孙女（而不是儿子）将认为\n\n未来的兴趣。这是因为业主将她所有其他不动产权益留给了孙女。 *反对永续规则（RAP）不适用于授予人持有的未来权益。因此，如果孙女继承了所有者持有的未来权益（例如，归还者的可能性），则无需缴纳移民安置计划。\n\n教育目标：可废止的费用受到特定持续时间或条件语言的限制（例如，“只要”、“但是如果”）。仅限制转账目的的语言会产生简单的绝对费用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述§ 44 cmt。 m（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论指定的运输目的）。","conclusion":"答案 D：该大学完全拥有土地，因为该设计不包括条件或持续语言。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":134,"sourceQuestionPage":392,"sourceQuestionPages":[392],"sourceExplanationPages":[393,394],"sourceAnswerPages":[393,394],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-135","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A manicurist entered into a one-year agreement to lease a nail salon from a landlord. During the first six months of the lease, the plumbing in the building functioned properly. During the seventh month, a pipe under the bathroom sink began leaking over a three-day weekend, causing flooding throughout the nail salon. The manicurist took reasonable steps to mitigate damages, including turning off the water to the bathroom sink and cleaning up the excess water to prevent water damage to the floors. The manicurist was still able to operate the nail salon despite the leaking pipe but not at full capacity. She notified the landlord about the leaking pipe, but the landlord refused to repair the faulty plumbing. The manicurist refused to pay the following month's rent, and the landlord has filed a breach-of-contract action against her. For whom is the court likely to decide?","choices":{"A":"For the landlord, because he did not have a duty to repair the plumbing.","B":"For the landlord, because the implied covenant of quiet enjoyment does not apply to commercial leases.","C":"For the manicurist, because she took reasonable steps to mitigate damages.","D":"For the manicurist, because the leaking pipe resulted in a constructive eviction."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Landlord's duty to repair leased premises\n\nType of Common law Majority rule lease\n\nResidential No duty to repair unless Duty to repair substantial defects under statute or contract requires implied warranty of habitability\n\nCommercial No duty to make ordinary repairs unless statute or contract requires\n\nAt common law and in most jurisdictions, courts are reluctant to impose the implied warranty of habitability on commercial leases. Therefore, absent some statutory or contractual obligation, a commercial landlord has no duty to make repairs unless: the repair is so substantial that it would not ordinarily fall within the tenant's common- law duty to repair or the repair would primarily benefit the value of the landlord's property. This duty to repair most commonly arises in cases of major structural damage (eg, repairing a roof or damaged exterior wall). Here, there is no indication that a statute or the commercial lease agreement required the landlord to make repairs to the nail salon. Additionally, the leaking pipe required an ordinary repair that primarily benefited the manicurist's use of the property (as opposed to the landlord's property's value). Therefore, the landlord did not have a duty to repair the plumbing, and the court will likely decide in his favor. (Choice B) Every commercial and residential lease contains an implied covenant of quiet enjoyment, which promises that the landlord will not interfere with the tenant's possession of the leased premises. Since the landlord did not interfere with the manicurist's possession of the nail salon, this covenant does not apply. (Choice C) The fact that the manicurist took reasonable steps to mitigate damages is irrelevant. The landlord had no duty to repair the plumbing, and even if he did, the manicurist would have a duty to mitigate only if she were statutorily or contractually obligated to do so. (Choice D) When a landlord breaches a duty to a tenant that substantially interferes with the tenant's use and enjoyment of the leasehold (eg, fails to provide heat or water), this constructive eviction excuses the tenant's obligation to pay rent. Here, no constrictive eviction occurred because the landlord did not breach a duty to the manicurist and the leak did not substantially interfere with the manicurist's ability to operate the nail salon.\n\nEducational objective: Absent a statutory or contractual duty, a commercial landlord has no duty to make repairs unless the repair (1) is so substantial that it falls outside the tenant's common-law duty to repair or (2) would primarily benefit the value of the landlord's property.\n\nReferences\n49 Am. Jur. 2d Landlord & Tenant § 443 (2020) (stating that the implied warranty of habitability does not apply to commercial leases).","educationalObjective":"Absent a statutory or contractual duty, a commercial landlord has no duty to make repairs unless the repair (1) is so substantial that it falls outside the tenant's common-law duty to repair or (2) would primarily benefit the value of the landlord's property.","references":"49 Am. Jur. 2d Landlord & Tenant § 443 (2020) (stating that the implied warranty of habitability does not apply to commercial leases).","rule":"Absent a statutory or contractual duty, a commercial landlord has no duty to make repairs unless the repair (1) is so substantial that it falls outside the tenant's common-law duty to repair or (2) would primarily benefit the value of the landlord's property.","irac":{"issue":"For whom is the court likely to decide?","rule":"Absent a statutory or contractual duty, a commercial landlord has no duty to make repairs unless the repair (1) is so substantial that it falls outside the tenant's common-law duty to repair or (2) would primarily benefit the value of the landlord's property.","analysis":"Landlord's duty to repair leased premises\n\nType of Common law Majority rule lease\n\nResidential No duty to repair unless Duty to repair substantial defects under statute or contract requires implied warranty of habitability\n\nCommercial No duty to make ordinary repairs unless statute or contract requires\n\nAt common law and in most jurisdictions, courts are reluctant to impose the implied warranty of habitability on commercial leases. Therefore, absent some statutory or contractual obligation, a commercial landlord has no duty to make repairs unless: the repair is so substantial that it would not ordinarily fall within the tenant's common- law duty to repair or the repair would primarily benefit the value of the landlord's property. This duty to repair most commonly arises in cases of major structural damage (eg, repairing a roof or damaged exterior wall). Here, there is no indication that a statute or the commercial lease agreement required the landlord to make repairs to the nail salon. Additionally, the leaking pipe required an ordinary repair that primarily benefited the manicurist's use of the property (as opposed to the landlord's property's value). Therefore, the landlord did not have a duty to repair the plumbing, and the court will likely decide in his favor. (Choice B) Every commercial and residential lease contains an implied covenant of quiet enjoyment, which promises that the landlord will not interfere with the tenant's possession of the leased premises. Since the landlord did not interfere with the manicurist's possession of the nail salon, this covenant does not apply. (Choice C) The fact that the manicurist took reasonable steps to mitigate damages is irrelevant. The landlord had no duty to repair the plumbing, and even if he did, the manicurist would have a duty to mitigate only if she were statutorily or contractually obligated to do so. (Choice D) When a landlord breaches a duty to a tenant that substantially interferes with the tenant's use and enjoyment of the leasehold (eg, fails to provide heat or water), this constructive eviction excuses the tenant's obligation to pay rent. Here, no constrictive eviction occurred because the landlord did not breach a duty to the manicurist and the leak did not substantially interfere with the manicurist's ability to operate the nail salon.","conclusion":"Answer A: For the landlord, because he did not have a duty to repair the plumbing."},"questionZh":"一名美甲师与房东签订了一份为期一年的美甲沙龙租赁协议。在租约的前六个月内，建筑物内的管道系统运行正常。在第七个月，浴室水槽下的一根管道在为期三天的周末开始漏水，导致整个美甲沙龙被淹。美甲师采取了合理的措施来减轻损害，包括关闭浴室水槽的水并清理多余的水，以防止水损坏地板。尽管管道漏水，美甲师仍然能够经营美甲沙龙，但不是满负荷运转。她向房东通报了水管漏水的情况，但房东拒绝修理有问题的水管。美甲师拒绝支付下个月的房租，房东已对她提起违约诉讼。法院可能会为谁做出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"对于房东来说，因为他没有修理水管的义务。","B":"对于房东来说，因为安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）的默示契约并不适用于商业租赁。","C":"对于美甲师来说，因为她采取了合理的措施来减轻损失。","D":"对于美甲师来说，因为管道泄漏导致建设性驱逐。"},"explanationZh":"房东修复租赁房屋的责任\n\n普通法类型 多数规则租赁\n\n住宅 没有维修义务，除非法律或合同规定的重大缺陷维修义务需要暗示可居住性保证\n\n商业 除非法规或合同要求，否则没有义务进行普通维修\n\n在普通法和大多数司法管辖区，法院不愿意对商业租赁强加可居住性的默示保证。因此，在没有某些法定或合同义务的情况下，商业房东没有义务进行维修，除非： 维修量太大，通常不属于租户的普通法维修义务范围，或者维修主要有利于房东财产的价值。这种修复义务最常出现在重大结构损坏的情况下（例如，修复屋顶或损坏的外墙）。在这里，没有任何迹象表明法规或商业租赁协议要求房东对美甲沙龙进行维修。此外，泄漏的管道需要进行普通维修，这主要有利于美甲师对房产的使用（而不是房东房产的价值）。因此，房东没有修理管道的义务，法院很可能会做出对他有利的判决。 （选项B） 每一份商业和住宅租约都包含一个隐含的安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）契约，该契约承诺房东不会干涉租户对租赁房屋的占有。由于房东没有干涉美甲师对美甲沙龙的占有，因此该盟约不适用。 （选项 C）美甲师采取合理措施来减轻损害的事实无关紧要。房东没有义务修理管道，即使他这样做，美甲师也只有在法定或合同有义务这样做的情况下才有义务这样做。 （选项D）当房东违反对租户的义务，严重干扰租户使用和享受租赁权（例如，不提供暖气或水）时，这种推定驱逐可以免除租户支付租金的义务。在这里，没有发生限制性驱逐，因为房东没有违反对美甲师的义务，而且泄密事件也没有严重影响美甲师经营美甲沙龙的能力。\n\n教育目标：在没有法定或合同义务的情况下，商业房东没有义务进行维修，除非维修量 (1) 非常大，超出了租户的普通法维修义务，或者 (2) 主要有利于房东财产的价值。\n\n参考文献\n49 上午。尤尔。 2d Landlord & Tenant § 443 (2020)（规定可居住性的默示保证不适用于商业租赁）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在没有法定或合同义务的情况下，商业房东没有义务进行维修，除非维修 (1) 规模太大，超出了租户的普通法维修义务，或者 (2) 主要有利于房东财产的价值。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会为谁做出裁决？","rule":"在没有法定或合同义务的情况下，商业房东没有义务进行维修，除非维修 (1) 规模太大，超出了租户的普通法维修义务，或者 (2) 主要有利于房东财产的价值。","analysis":"房东修复租赁房屋的责任\n\n普通法类型 多数规则租赁\n\n住宅 没有维修义务，除非法律或合同规定的重大缺陷维修义务需要暗示可居住性保证\n\n商业 除非法规或合同要求，否则没有义务进行普通维修\n\n在普通法和大多数司法管辖区，法院不愿意对商业租赁强加可居住性的默示保证。因此，在没有某些法定或合同义务的情况下，商业房东没有义务进行维修，除非： 维修量太大，通常不属于租户的普通法维修义务范围，或者维修主要有利于房东财产的价值。这种修复义务最常出现在重大结构损坏的情况下（例如，修复屋顶或损坏的外墙）。在这里，没有任何迹象表明法规或商业租赁协议要求房东对美甲沙龙进行维修。此外，泄漏的管道需要进行普通维修，这主要有利于美甲师对房产的使用（而不是房东房产的价值）。因此，房东没有修理管道的义务，法院很可能会做出对他有利的判决。 （选项B） 每一份商业和住宅租约都包含一个隐含的安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）契约，该契约承诺房东不会干涉租户对租赁房屋的占有。由于房东没有干涉美甲师对美甲沙龙的占有，因此该盟约不适用。 （选项 C）美甲师采取合理措施来减轻损害的事实无关紧要。房东没有义务修理管道，即使他这样做，美甲师也只有在法定或合同有义务这样做的情况下才有义务这样做。 （选项D）当房东违反对租户的义务，严重干扰租户使用和享受租赁权（例如，不提供暖气或水）时，这种推定驱逐可以免除租户支付租金的义务。在这里，没有发生限制性驱逐，因为房东没有违反对美甲师的义务，而且泄密事件也没有严重影响美甲师经营美甲沙龙的能力。\n\n教育目标：在没有法定或合同义务的情况下，商业房东没有义务进行维修，除非维修量 (1) 非常大，超出了租户的普通法维修义务，或者 (2) 主要有利于房东财产的价值。\n\n参考文献\n49 上午。尤尔。 2d Landlord & Tenant § 443 (2020)（规定可居住性的默示保证不适用于商业租赁）。","conclusion":"答案 A：对于房东来说，因为他没有修理水管的义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"leasehold","ipa":"/lˈishˌoʊld/","syllables":"lease·hold","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 租赁的；n. 租赁权, 租赁","contextualMeaning":"租赁地产：承租人在租期内享有的占有性权益","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:leaseholds/i:leaseholding","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) When a landlord breaches a duty to a tenant that substantially interferes with the tenant's use and enjoyment of the leasehold (eg, fails to provide heat or water), this constructive eviction excuses the tenant's obligation to pay rent.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":135,"sourceQuestionPage":395,"sourceQuestionPages":[395],"sourceExplanationPages":[396,397],"sourceAnswerPages":[396,397],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-136","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"An elderly man leased a barn to his neighbor for a term of three years. The neighbor took possession of the barn and used it for his farming purposes. The lease made the elderly man responsible for structural repairs to the barn, unless they were made necessary by actions of the neighbor. One year later, the elderly man conveyed the barn and its associated land to his granddaughter \"subject to the lease to my neighbor.\" The neighbor paid the next month's rent to the granddaughter. The next day a portion of an exterior wall of the barn collapsed because of rot in the interior structure of the wall. The wall had appeared to be sound, but a competent engineer, on inspection, would have discovered its condition. Neither the granddaughter nor the neighbor had the barn inspected by an engineer. The neighbor was injured as a result of the collapse of the wall. The elderly man had known that the wall was dangerously weakened by rot and needed immediate repairs when the neighbor took possession but had not told the neighbor or his granddaughter. There is no applicable statute. The neighbor brought an appropriate action against the elderly man to recover damages for the injuries he sustained. The granddaughter was not a party. Which of the following is the most appropriate comment concerning the outcome of this action?","choices":{"A":"The neighbor should lose, because he failed to inspect the barn.","B":"The neighbor should lose, because the granddaughter assumed all of the elderly man's obligations by reason of the neighbor's attornment to her.","C":"The neighbor should recover, because the elderly man knew of the danger but did not warn the neighbor.","D":"The neighbor should recover, because there is privity between lessor and lessee and it cannot be broken unilaterally."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Dangerous conditions on land\n\n(landlord's duties)\n\nWarn tenant of known latent (hidden) defects on land Safely perform repairs Ensure common areas are in safe condition Maintain areas necessary for safe use of land (eg, walls, roof) Keep land in good repair (lease provision required) Protect entrants from dangerous condition on land leased for public purpose Landlords have no duty to warn tenants about dangerous conditions on the premises unless those conditions are latent. Dangerous latent defects are conditions that: the landlord knows, or should know, pose an unreasonable risk of physical harm exist when the tenant takes possession and could not be discovered by the tenant's reasonable inspection. A landlord who breaches this duty is liable for physical harm caused by the condition— even after the landlord conveys the leased property to another. Here, the elderly man knew that the barn wall was dangerously weakened by rot when the neighbor took possession. Since the neighbor's inspection of the barn would not have revealed the rot (only an engineer would have found it), the man had a duty to warn the neighbor about the wall. The man failed to do so and is therefore liable for the neighbor's injuries—even after conveying the barn to his granddaughter. (Choice A) The neighbor's failure to inspect the barn is of no consequence because he could not have discovered the defect upon inspection. (Choice B) Attornment occurs when a tenant accepts a new landlord in place of an old one. However, the new landlord does not become liable for dangerous latent defects until he/she has had a reasonable opportunity to discover and take effective precautions against them. Here, just one day had passed since the barn was conveyed to the granddaughter, so she had no reasonable opportunity to discover the defect. (Choice D) There is privity of contract (shared interest under the lease) and privity of estate (successive rights to possess the property) between a lessor and lessee. Privity of estate can be broken unilaterally when either party conveys his/her entire interest under the lease, but privity of contract cannot. And though privity may create a duty, the neighbor cannot recover unless he also shows that duty was breached.\n\nEducational objective: A landlord must warn a tenant about dangerous latent conditions that (1) the landlord knows, or should know, pose an unreasonable risk of physical harm, (2) exist when the tenant takes possession, and (3) could not be discovered by reasonable inspection.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property § 17.1, cmt. l (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (landlord's liability for latent defects following a transfer of the landlord's interest).","educationalObjective":"A landlord must warn a tenant about dangerous latent conditions that (1) the landlord knows, or should know, pose an unreasonable risk of physical harm, (2) exist when the tenant takes possession, and (3) could not be discovered by reasonable inspection.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property § 17.1, cmt. l (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (landlord's liability for latent defects following a transfer of the landlord's interest).","rule":"A landlord must warn a tenant about dangerous latent conditions that (1) the landlord knows, or should know, pose an unreasonable risk of physical harm, (2) exist when the tenant takes possession, and (3) could not be discovered by reasonable inspection.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the most appropriate comment concerning the outcome of this action?","rule":"A landlord must warn a tenant about dangerous latent conditions that (1) the landlord knows, or should know, pose an unreasonable risk of physical harm, (2) exist when the tenant takes possession, and (3) could not be discovered by reasonable inspection.","analysis":"Dangerous conditions on land\n\n(landlord's duties)\n\nWarn tenant of known latent (hidden) defects on land Safely perform repairs Ensure common areas are in safe condition Maintain areas necessary for safe use of land (eg, walls, roof) Keep land in good repair (lease provision required) Protect entrants from dangerous condition on land leased for public purpose Landlords have no duty to warn tenants about dangerous conditions on the premises unless those conditions are latent. Dangerous latent defects are conditions that: the landlord knows, or should know, pose an unreasonable risk of physical harm exist when the tenant takes possession and could not be discovered by the tenant's reasonable inspection. A landlord who breaches this duty is liable for physical harm caused by the condition— even after the landlord conveys the leased property to another. Here, the elderly man knew that the barn wall was dangerously weakened by rot when the neighbor took possession. Since the neighbor's inspection of the barn would not have revealed the rot (only an engineer would have found it), the man had a duty to warn the neighbor about the wall. The man failed to do so and is therefore liable for the neighbor's injuries—even after conveying the barn to his granddaughter. (Choice A) The neighbor's failure to inspect the barn is of no consequence because he could not have discovered the defect upon inspection. (Choice B) Attornment occurs when a tenant accepts a new landlord in place of an old one. However, the new landlord does not become liable for dangerous latent defects until he/she has had a reasonable opportunity to discover and take effective precautions against them. Here, just one day had passed since the barn was conveyed to the granddaughter, so she had no reasonable opportunity to discover the defect. (Choice D) There is privity of contract (shared interest under the lease) and privity of estate (successive rights to possess the property) between a lessor and lessee. Privity of estate can be broken unilaterally when either party conveys his/her entire interest under the lease, but privity of contract cannot. And though privity may create a duty, the neighbor cannot recover unless he also shows that duty was breached.","conclusion":"Answer C: The neighbor should recover, because the elderly man knew of the danger but did not warn the neighbor."},"questionZh":"一位老人将一个谷仓租给了邻居，租期三年。邻居占有了谷仓并将其用于农耕目的。租约规定老人负责谷仓的结构维修，除非邻居有必要这样做。一年后，老人将谷仓及其相关土地转让给他的孙女，“但须租给我的邻居”。邻居给孙女支付了下个月的房租。第二天，谷仓外墙的一部分因墙体内部结构腐烂而倒塌。这堵墙看起来很完好，但一个有能力的工程师在检查时就会发现它的状况。孙女和邻居都没有请工程师检查谷仓。邻居因墙倒塌而受伤。当邻居接管时，老人知道这堵墙已经严重腐烂，需要立即修复，但他没有告诉邻居或他的孙女。没有适用的法规。邻居对老人提起了适当的诉讼，要求赔偿他所受的伤害。孙女不是当事人。对于该行动的结果，以下哪一项是最恰当的评论？","choicesZh":{"A":"邻居应该输，因为他没有检查谷仓。","B":"邻居应该输，因为孙女因为邻居对她的侮辱而承担了老人的所有义务。","C":"邻居应该康复了，因为老人知道有危险，但没有警告邻居。","D":"邻居应该恢复，因为出租人和承租人之间存在相互关系，不能单方面打破。"},"explanationZh":"陆地上的危险状况\n\n（房东的义务）\n\n警告租户土地上已知的潜在（隐藏）缺陷 安全进行维修 确保公共区域处于安全状态 维护安全使用土地所需的区域（例如墙壁、屋顶） 保持土地良好维修（需要租赁条款） 保护进入者免受公共用途租赁土地上的危险状况 房东没有义务警告租户有关场所内的危险状况，除非这些状况是潜在的。危险的潜在缺陷是指以下情况：房东知道或应该知道，在租客占有房屋时存在不合理的人身伤害风险，并且租客的合理检查无法发现。违反这一义务的房东应对这种情况造成的人身伤害负责——即使在房东将租赁财产转让给他人之后也是如此。在这里，老人知道，当邻居占领谷仓时，谷仓的墙壁已经腐烂，变得很脆弱。由于邻居对谷仓的检查不会发现腐烂的地方（只有工程师才能发现），因此该男子有责任警告邻居有关墙壁的情况。该男子没有这样做，因此对邻居的伤害负有责任——即使在将谷仓转让给他的孙女之后也是如此。 （选项A） 邻居没有检查谷仓并没有什么后果，因为他不可能在检查时发现缺陷。 （选择 B）当租户接受新房东代替旧房东时，就会发生侵权行为。然而，新房东在有合理机会发现潜在危险缺陷并采取有效预防措施之前，不对这些缺陷承担责任。在这里，谷仓转让给孙女才过去一天，所以她没有合理的机会发现这个缺陷。 （选项D）出租人和承租人之间存在合同关系（租赁下的共同利益）和财产关系（占有财产的继承权）。当任何一方转让其在租赁下的全部权益时，遗产相对性可以单方面被打破，但合同相对性则不能。尽管相互关系可能产生义务，但邻居无法恢复，除非他也证明义务被违反。\n\n教育目标：房东必须警告租户有关危险的潜在情况，(1) 房东知道或应该知道，会造成不合理的人身伤害风险，(2) 租户占用房产时存在，以及 (3) 无法通过合理检查发现。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二）§ 17.1，cmt。 l（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（房东权益转让后，房东对潜在缺陷的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"房东必须警告租户以下危险的潜在情况：(1) 房东知道或应该知道，会造成不合理的人身伤害风险；(2) 租户占用房产时存在；以及 (3) 无法通过合理检查发现。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于该行动的结果，以下哪一项是最恰当的评论？","rule":"房东必须警告租户以下危险的潜在情况：(1) 房东知道或应该知道，会造成不合理的人身伤害风险；(2) 租户占用房产时存在；以及 (3) 无法通过合理检查发现。","analysis":"陆地上的危险状况\n\n（房东的义务）\n\n警告租户土地上已知的潜在（隐藏）缺陷 安全进行维修 确保公共区域处于安全状态 维护安全使用土地所需的区域（例如墙壁、屋顶） 保持土地良好维修（需要租赁条款） 保护进入者免受公共用途租赁土地上的危险状况 房东没有义务警告租户有关场所内的危险状况，除非这些状况是潜在的。危险的潜在缺陷是指以下情况：房东知道或应该知道，在租客占有房屋时存在不合理的人身伤害风险，并且租客的合理检查无法发现。违反这一义务的房东应对这种情况造成的人身伤害负责——即使在房东将租赁财产转让给他人之后也是如此。在这里，老人知道，当邻居占领谷仓时，谷仓的墙壁已经腐烂，变得很脆弱。由于邻居对谷仓的检查不会发现腐烂的地方（只有工程师才能发现），因此该男子有责任警告邻居有关墙壁的情况。该男子没有这样做，因此对邻居的伤害负有责任——即使在将谷仓转让给他的孙女之后也是如此。 （选项A） 邻居没有检查谷仓并没有什么后果，因为他不可能在检查时发现缺陷。 （选择 B）当租户接受新房东代替旧房东时，就会发生侵权行为。然而，新房东在有合理机会发现潜在危险缺陷并采取有效预防措施之前，不对这些缺陷承担责任。在这里，谷仓转让给孙女才过去一天，所以她没有合理的机会发现这个缺陷。 （选项D）出租人和承租人之间存在合同关系（租赁下的共同利益）和财产关系（占有财产的继承权）。当任何一方转让其在租赁下的全部权益时，遗产相对性可以单方面被打破，但合同相对性则不能。尽管相互关系可能产生义务，但邻居无法恢复，除非他也证明义务被违反。\n\n教育目标：房东必须警告租户有关危险的潜在情况，(1) 房东知道或应该知道，会造成不合理的人身伤害风险，(2) 租户占用房产时存在，以及 (3) 无法通过合理检查发现。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二）§ 17.1，cmt。 l（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（房东权益转让后，房东对潜在缺陷的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 C：邻居应该康复了，因为老人知道有危险，但没有警告邻居。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":136,"sourceQuestionPage":398,"sourceQuestionPages":[398],"sourceExplanationPages":[399,400],"sourceAnswerPages":[399,400],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-137","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Twenty years ago, the owner of a one-acre tract of land duly delivered a deed of the land \"to the school district so long as it is used for school purposes.\" The deed was promptly and properly recorded. Five years ago, the owner died, leaving a son as his only heir; however, by his duly probated will, he left \"all my estate to my friend.\" Last month, the school district closed its school on the land and for valid consideration duly executed and delivered a quitclaim deed of the land to a developer, who planned to use the land for commercial development. The developer has now brought an appropriate action to quiet title against the son, the friend, and the school district. The only applicable statute is a provision in the jurisdiction's probate code which provides that any property interest that is descendible is devisable. In such action, the court should find that title is now in whom?","choices":{"A":"The developer.","B":"The friend.","C":"The school district.","D":"The son."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nA fee simple determinable (FSD) is created with durational language (eg, \"until\") and automatically terminates upon the happening of a specified event or condition (eg, when land is no longer used for school purposes). An FSD is followed by either: a possibility of reverter – when the estate automatically reverts back to the grantor upon the happening of the specified event (presumed if not expressly stated) or\n\nan executory interest – when the estate automatically passes to a third party upon the happening of the specified event (must be expressly stated).\n\nBoth these future interests are freely transferable during life, devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance. Here, the deed conveyed created an FSD in the school district and a possibility of reverter in the owner. The owner devised all of his estate—including the possibility of reverter—to the friend, leaving no interest for the son to inherit upon the owner's death (Choice D). And since the school district's estate automatically terminated when it closed the school, it had nothing to convey to the developer in the quitclaim deed (Choices A & C). Instead, title to the land automatically transferred to the friend, the reversionary interest holder.\n\nEducational objective: A fee simple determinable is followed by either a possibility of reverter (if it reverts to the grantor) or an executory interest (if it passes to a third party). Both future interests are freely transferable during life, devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property §§ 159, 164–65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (transferability of reversionary interests).","educationalObjective":"A fee simple determinable is followed by either a possibility of reverter (if it reverts to the grantor) or an executory interest (if it passes to a third party). Both future interests are freely transferable during life, devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance.","references":"Restatement of Property §§ 159, 164–65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (transferability of reversionary interests).","rule":"A fee simple determinable is followed by either a possibility of reverter (if it reverts to the grantor) or an executory interest (if it passes to a third party). Both future interests are freely transferable during life, devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance.","irac":{"issue":"In such action, the court should find that title is now in whom?","rule":"A fee simple determinable is followed by either a possibility of reverter (if it reverts to the grantor) or an executory interest (if it passes to a third party). Both future interests are freely transferable during life, devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance.","analysis":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nA fee simple determinable (FSD) is created with durational language (eg, \"until\") and automatically terminates upon the happening of a specified event or condition (eg, when land is no longer used for school purposes). An FSD is followed by either: a possibility of reverter – when the estate automatically reverts back to the grantor upon the happening of the specified event (presumed if not expressly stated) or\n\nan executory interest – when the estate automatically passes to a third party upon the happening of the specified event (must be expressly stated).\n\nBoth these future interests are freely transferable during life, devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance. Here, the deed conveyed created an FSD in the school district and a possibility of reverter in the owner. The owner devised all of his estate—including the possibility of reverter—to the friend, leaving no interest for the son to inherit upon the owner's death (Choice D). And since the school district's estate automatically terminated when it closed the school, it had nothing to convey to the developer in the quitclaim deed (Choices A & C). Instead, title to the land automatically transferred to the friend, the reversionary interest holder.","conclusion":"Answer B: The friend."},"questionZh":"二十年前，一块一英亩土地的所有者正式向学区交付了一份土地契约，“只要它用于学校目的”。该行为被及时、正确地记录下来。五年前，主人去世，留下一个儿子作为他唯一的继承人；然而，根据他的正式遗嘱，他将“我所有的财产留给了我的朋友”。上个月，学区关闭了该土地上的学校，并以有效考虑正式签署并向开发商交付了该土地的无偿所有权契据，该开发商计划将该土地用于商业开发。开发商现已采取适当行动，平息对儿子、朋友和学区的所有权。唯一适用的法规是司法管辖区遗嘱认证法中的一项规定，该规定规定任何可继承的财产权益都是可设计的。在这样的诉讼中，法院应该认定所有权现在属于谁？","choicesZh":{"A":"开发商。","B":"朋友。","C":"学区。","D":"儿子。"},"explanationZh":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n费用简单确定（FSD）是用持续性语言（例如“直到”）创建的，并在发生指定事件或条件时自动终止（例如，当土地不再用于学校目的时）。 FSD 之后是： 归还者的可能性 – 当发生指定事件（如果没有明确说明则假定）时，遗产自动归还给授予人，或者\n\n执行权益——当指定事件发生时，遗产自动转移给第三方（必须明确说明）。\n\n这两种未来利益都可以在一生中自由转让、可以通过意愿设计、可以通过继承继承。在这里，所传达的契约在学区创造了 FSD，并为业主提供了恢复的可能性。所有者将其所有财产（包括归还的可能性）设计给了朋友，在所有者去世后，儿子没有继承权（选择 D）。由于学区的财产在关闭学校时自动终止，因此在退出索赔契约（选择 A 和 C）中没有任何内容可以向开发商传达。相反，土地所有权自动转移给朋友，即复归利益持有人。\n\n教育目标：费用可简单确定，之后是归还者的可能性（如果归还给授予人）或执行权益（如果它转移给第三方）。两种未来的利益都可以在一生中自由转让，可以通过意志来设计，也可以通过继承来传承。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 159, 164–65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936)（复归权益的可转让性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"费用可简单确定，之后是恢复者的可能性（如果它恢复给设保人）或执行权益（如果它转移给第三方）。两种未来的利益都可以在一生中自由转让，可以通过意志来设计，也可以通过继承来传承。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这样的诉讼中，法院应该认定所有权现在属于谁？","rule":"费用可简单确定，之后是恢复者的可能性（如果它恢复给设保人）或执行权益（如果它转移给第三方）。两种未来的利益都可以在一生中自由转让，可以通过意志来设计，也可以通过继承来传承。","analysis":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n费用简单确定（FSD）是用持续性语言（例如“直到”）创建的，并在发生指定事件或条件时自动终止（例如，当土地不再用于学校目的时）。 FSD 之后是： 归还者的可能性 – 当发生指定事件（如果没有明确说明则假定）时，遗产自动归还给授予人，或者\n\n执行权益——当指定事件发生时，遗产自动转移给第三方（必须明确说明）。\n\n这两种未来利益都可以在一生中自由转让、可以通过意愿设计、可以通过继承继承。在这里，所传达的契约在学区创造了 FSD，并为业主提供了恢复的可能性。所有者将其所有财产（包括归还的可能性）设计给了朋友，在所有者去世后，儿子没有继承权（选择 D）。由于学区的财产在关闭学校时自动终止，因此在退出索赔契约（选择 A 和 C）中没有任何内容可以向开发商传达。相反，土地所有权自动转移给朋友，即复归利益持有人。\n\n教育目标：费用可简单确定，之后是归还者的可能性（如果归还给授予人）或执行权益（如果它转移给第三方）。两种未来的利益都可以在一生中自由转让，可以通过意志来设计，也可以通过继承来传承。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 159, 164–65 (Am. Law Inst. 1936)（复归权益的可转让性）。","conclusion":"答案 B：朋友。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":137,"sourceQuestionPage":401,"sourceQuestionPages":[401],"sourceExplanationPages":[402,403],"sourceAnswerPages":[402,403],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-138","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"An elderly man and a landlord entered into a one-year agreement to lease a one-bedroom apartment. Six months into the lease, the man accidentally fell down a flight of stairs and broke his hip. As a result, he was no longer able to care for himself, and his family moved him into a nursing home. The man ceased paying rent for the next two months. When the landlord finally reached the man at the nursing home, the man informed the landlord that he had left the apartment two months earlier and did not intend to return. The landlord notified the man that he did not accept the man's abandonment of the lease, and for the remaining four months of the lease, the landlord did not take any steps to re-rent the abandoned apartment despite receiving multiple inquiries about the apartment. The landlord has sued the man for breach of contract to recover damages for unpaid rent. What amount of damages will the court likely hold the man liable for?","choices":{"A":"Two months' rent, because the man is not liable for future rent after he provided notice of abandonment to the landlord.","B":"Four months' rent, because the landlord affirmatively rejected the man's abandonment of the lease.","C":"Six months' rent, because the landlord did not have a duty to mitigate damages upon the man's unjustifiable abandonment.","D":"None, because the man's abandonment of the lease was justifiable due to necessity."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A tenant's unjustifiable abandonment of leased property is treated as an offer to surrender rights under the lease. The landlord can then accept or reject the tenant's offer. If the offer is rejected, the lease continues and the tenant must continue paying rent. However, in a majority of jurisdictions, the landlord has a duty to mitigate damages by making a reasonable effort to re-rent the property.* If the landlord fails to do so, then the landlord's damages will be reduced by what the landlord could have reasonably received by re-renting the property. Here, the man stopped paying rent for two months before informing the landlord that he had abandoned the leased apartment. Although the man abandoned the lease because of his medical needs, a tenant's personal circumstances rarely justify abandonment (Choice D). Therefore, the man is liable for those two months of unpaid rent. The landlord then rejected the man's offer to surrender his rights under the lease, so the man remained obligated to pay rent for the four months left under the lease. But since the landlord failed to take any steps to re-rent the apartment pursuant to his duty to mitigate damages—even after receiving multiple inquiries about the apartment—the court will likely find that the man is not liable for the remaining four months of rent (Choices B & C). *A landlord who re-rents the premises for less than the price in the lease may hold the tenant liable for any deficiency.\n\nEducational objective: If a landlord rejects a tenant's offer of abandonment, the tenant is obligated to pay rent for the remainder of the lease. However, in most jurisdictions, a landlord has a duty to mitigate damages by making a reasonable effort to re-rent the property and the tenant's liability for rent will be reduced accordingly.\n\nReferences\n49 Am. Jur. 2d Landlord & Tenant § 609 (2020) (discussing a landlord's duty to mitigate damages).","educationalObjective":"If a landlord rejects a tenant's offer of abandonment, the tenant is obligated to pay rent for the remainder of the lease. However, in most jurisdictions, a landlord has a duty to mitigate damages by making a reasonable effort to re-rent the property and the tenant's liability for rent will be reduced accordingly.","references":"49 Am. Jur. 2d Landlord & Tenant § 609 (2020) (discussing a landlord's duty to mitigate damages).","rule":"If a landlord rejects a tenant's offer of abandonment, the tenant is obligated to pay rent for the remainder of the lease. However, in most jurisdictions, a landlord has a duty to mitigate damages by making a reasonable effort to re-rent the property and the tenant's liability for rent will be reduced accordingly.","irac":{"issue":"What amount of damages will the court likely hold the man liable for?","rule":"If a landlord rejects a tenant's offer of abandonment, the tenant is obligated to pay rent for the remainder of the lease. However, in most jurisdictions, a landlord has a duty to mitigate damages by making a reasonable effort to re-rent the property and the tenant's liability for rent will be reduced accordingly.","analysis":"A tenant's unjustifiable abandonment of leased property is treated as an offer to surrender rights under the lease. The landlord can then accept or reject the tenant's offer. If the offer is rejected, the lease continues and the tenant must continue paying rent. However, in a majority of jurisdictions, the landlord has a duty to mitigate damages by making a reasonable effort to re-rent the property.* If the landlord fails to do so, then the landlord's damages will be reduced by what the landlord could have reasonably received by re-renting the property. Here, the man stopped paying rent for two months before informing the landlord that he had abandoned the leased apartment. Although the man abandoned the lease because of his medical needs, a tenant's personal circumstances rarely justify abandonment (Choice D). Therefore, the man is liable for those two months of unpaid rent. The landlord then rejected the man's offer to surrender his rights under the lease, so the man remained obligated to pay rent for the four months left under the lease. But since the landlord failed to take any steps to re-rent the apartment pursuant to his duty to mitigate damages—even after receiving multiple inquiries about the apartment—the court will likely find that the man is not liable for the remaining four months of rent (Choices B & C). *A landlord who re-rents the premises for less than the price in the lease may hold the tenant liable for any deficiency.","conclusion":"Answer A: Two months' rent, because the man is not liable for future rent after he provided notice of abandonment to the landlord."},"questionZh":"一位老人和一位房东签订了一份为期一年的协议，租用一套一居室公寓。租约六个月后，该男子不小心从楼梯上摔下来，摔断了臀部。结果，他再也无法照顾自己，家人把他搬进了疗养院。该男子在接下来的两个月内不再支付房租。当房东最终找到疗养院的这名男子时，该男子告诉房东，他两个月前就离开了公寓，并且不打算回来。房东通知该男子，他不接受该男子放弃租约的决定，并且在剩余的四个月租期内，尽管收到多次有关该公寓的询问，但房东没有采取任何措施重新出租该废弃的公寓。房东已起诉该男子违反合同，要求赔偿未付租金的损失。法院可能会要求该男子承担多少赔偿责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"两个月的房租，因为该男子向房东发出遗弃通知后，不再承担未来的租金。","B":"四个月的房租，因为房东断然拒绝了男子放弃租约的要求。","C":"六个月的租金，因为房东没有义务减轻该男子无理遗弃造成的损失。","D":"没有，因为该男子放弃租约是出于必要而合理的。"},"explanationZh":"承租人无理放弃租赁财产的行为被视为放弃租赁权利的要约。然后房东可以接受或拒绝租户的报价。如果要约被拒绝，租约将继续，租户必须继续支付租金。然而，在大多数司法管辖区，房东有责任通过合理努力重新出租房产来减轻损失。*如果房东没有这样做，那么房东的损失将减少房东通过重新出租房产本可以合理收到的金额。在这里，该男子停止支付租金两个月后，才通知房东，他已经放弃了租赁的公寓。尽管该男子因医疗需要而放弃了租约，但租户的个人情况很少成为放弃的理由（选择 D）。因此，该男子应对这两个月未付的房租承担责任。随后，房东拒绝了该男子放弃租约权利的提议，因此该男子仍有义务支付租约剩余四个月的租金。但由于房东未能根据减轻损失的义务采取任何措施重新出租公寓，即使在收到多次有关公寓的询问后，法院也可能会裁定该男子无需承担剩余四个月的租金（选择 B 和 C）。 *以低于租约价格重新出租房屋的房东可能会要求租户承担任何不足的责任。\n\n教育目标：如果房东拒绝租客的放弃提议，则租客有义务支付剩余租约的租金。然而，在大多数司法管辖区，房东有责任通过合理努力重新出租房产来减轻损失，而租户的租金责任将相应减少。\n\n参考文献\n49 上午。尤尔。 2d Landlord & Tenant § 609 (2020)（讨论房东减轻损害的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果房东拒绝租客的放弃提议，则租客有义务支付剩余租约的租金。然而，在大多数司法管辖区，房东有责任通过合理努力重新出租房产来减轻损失，而租户的租金责任将相应减少。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会要求该男子承担多少赔偿责任？","rule":"如果房东拒绝租客的放弃提议，则租客有义务支付剩余租约的租金。然而，在大多数司法管辖区，房东有责任通过合理努力重新出租房产来减轻损失，而租户的租金责任将相应减少。","analysis":"承租人无理放弃租赁财产的行为被视为放弃租赁权利的要约。然后房东可以接受或拒绝租户的报价。如果要约被拒绝，租约将继续，租户必须继续支付租金。然而，在大多数司法管辖区，房东有责任通过合理努力重新出租房产来减轻损失。*如果房东没有这样做，那么房东的损失将减少房东通过重新出租房产本可以合理收到的金额。在这里，该男子停止支付租金两个月后，才通知房东，他已经放弃了租赁的公寓。尽管该男子因医疗需要而放弃了租约，但租户的个人情况很少成为放弃的理由（选择 D）。因此，该男子应对这两个月未付的房租承担责任。随后，房东拒绝了该男子放弃租约权利的提议，因此该男子仍有义务支付租约剩余四个月的租金。但由于房东未能根据减轻损失的义务采取任何措施重新出租公寓，即使在收到多次有关公寓的询问后，法院也可能会裁定该男子无需承担剩余四个月的租金（选择 B 和 C）。 *以低于租约价格重新出租房屋的房东可能会要求租户承担任何不足的责任。\n\n教育目标：如果房东拒绝租客的放弃提议，则租客有义务支付剩余租约的租金。然而，在大多数司法管辖区，房东有责任通过合理努力重新出租房产来减轻损失，而租户的租金责任将相应减少。\n\n参考文献\n49 上午。尤尔。 2d Landlord & Tenant § 609 (2020)（讨论房东减轻损害的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 A：两个月的房租，因为该男子向房东发出遗弃通知后，不再承担未来的租金。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-0f7c532764017293"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":138,"sourceQuestionPage":404,"sourceQuestionPages":[404],"sourceExplanationPages":[405,406],"sourceAnswerPages":[405,406],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-139","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A brother and sister were jointly in possession of land in fee simple as tenants in common. They joined in a mortgage of the land to a bank. The brother erected a fence along what he considered to be the true boundary between the land and the adjoining property, owned by the neighbor. Shortly thereafter, the brother had an argument with the sister and gave up his possession of the land. The debt secured by the mortgage had not been paid. The neighbor surveyed his land and found that the fence erected a year earlier by the brother did not follow the true boundary. Part of the fence was within the brother and sister's land. Part of the fence encroached on the neighbor's land. The neighbor and the sister executed an agreement fixing the boundary line in accordance with the fence constructed by the brother. The agreement, which met all the formalities required in the jurisdiction, was promptly and properly recorded. A year after the agreement was recorded, the brother temporarily reconciled his differences with the sister and resumed joint possession of the land. Thereafter, the brother repudiated the boundary-line agreement and brought an appropriate action against the neighbor and the sister to quiet title along the original true boundary. In such action, will the brother prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the agreement, as a matter of law, was mutually beneficial to the brother and sister.","B":"No, because the sister was in sole possession of the land at the time the agreement was signed.","C":"Yes, because one tenant in common cannot bind another tenant in common to a boundary-line agreement.","D":"Yes, because the bank was not a party to the agreement."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Consent between cotenants in common\n\n(common examples)\n\nConsent required Convey entire co-owned property Obtain exclusive possession over part of property Bind other cotenants' interests in contract with third party No consent Sell, lease, or devise personal ownership interest required Possess entire co-owned property Extract minerals from co-owned property (unless committing waste) Tenancy in common is concurrent ownership of land where each co-tenant has a share of the property but an equal right to possess the whole property (ie, separate but undivided interests). A co-tenant may transfer his/her own interest without the other co-tenants' consent. However, a co-tenant cannot transfer other co-tenants' interests or bind them under a contract affecting the property (eg, a boundary-line agreement). Here, the brother and sister share the land in fee simple as tenants in common. The sister and the neighbor executed a contract fixing the boundary line in accordance with a fence erected by the brother. Although the brother was not in possession of the land at the time the agreement was signed, he was still a co-tenant (Choice B). Therefore, the sister could not bind him to that agreement, and he will likely prevail. (Choice A) When a contract is mutually beneficial to all co-tenants, it is presumed that they have all ratified (ie, confirmed or accepted) the contract. And though ratification renders an otherwise invalid contract valid, a presumed ratification is rebutted when a co-tenant repudiates the agreement (as seen here). (Choice D) Under the lien theory of mortgages (majority rule), a lender has a security interest—but not title—in the mortgaged property. Therefore, the fact that the bank was not a party to the boundary-line agreement does not render it unenforceable against the brother. Instead, the brother is not bound because he was not a party.\n\nEducational objective: Although a tenant in common may freely transfer his/her interest to a third party, that tenant cannot transfer another co-tenant's interest or bind another co-tenant to a contract affecting the shared property.\n\nReferences\n20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 93 (2018) (ability to bind other co-tenants).","educationalObjective":"Although a tenant in common may freely transfer his/her interest to a third party, that tenant cannot transfer another co-tenant's interest or bind another co-tenant to a contract affecting the shared property.","references":"20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 93 (2018) (ability to bind other co-tenants).","rule":"Although a tenant in common may freely transfer his/her interest to a third party, that tenant cannot transfer another co-tenant's interest or bind another co-tenant to a contract affecting the shared property.","irac":{"issue":"In such action, will the brother prevail?","rule":"Although a tenant in common may freely transfer his/her interest to a third party, that tenant cannot transfer another co-tenant's interest or bind another co-tenant to a contract affecting the shared property.","analysis":"Consent between cotenants in common\n\n(common examples)\n\nConsent required Convey entire co-owned property Obtain exclusive possession over part of property Bind other cotenants' interests in contract with third party No consent Sell, lease, or devise personal ownership interest required Possess entire co-owned property Extract minerals from co-owned property (unless committing waste) Tenancy in common is concurrent ownership of land where each co-tenant has a share of the property but an equal right to possess the whole property (ie, separate but undivided interests). A co-tenant may transfer his/her own interest without the other co-tenants' consent. However, a co-tenant cannot transfer other co-tenants' interests or bind them under a contract affecting the property (eg, a boundary-line agreement). Here, the brother and sister share the land in fee simple as tenants in common. The sister and the neighbor executed a contract fixing the boundary line in accordance with a fence erected by the brother. Although the brother was not in possession of the land at the time the agreement was signed, he was still a co-tenant (Choice B). Therefore, the sister could not bind him to that agreement, and he will likely prevail. (Choice A) When a contract is mutually beneficial to all co-tenants, it is presumed that they have all ratified (ie, confirmed or accepted) the contract. And though ratification renders an otherwise invalid contract valid, a presumed ratification is rebutted when a co-tenant repudiates the agreement (as seen here). (Choice D) Under the lien theory of mortgages (majority rule), a lender has a security interest—but not title—in the mortgaged property. Therefore, the fact that the bank was not a party to the boundary-line agreement does not render it unenforceable against the brother. Instead, the brother is not bound because he was not a party.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because one tenant in common cannot bind another tenant in common to a boundary-line agreement."},"questionZh":"一对兄弟姐妹以共同佃户的身份共同拥有土地。他们将土地抵押给银行。兄弟沿着他认为是土地和邻居拥有的毗邻财产之间的真正边界竖起了栅栏。此后不久，哥哥与妹妹发生争执，并放弃了土地的所有权。抵押担保的债务尚未偿还。邻居勘察了自己的土地，发现哥哥一年前搭建的栅栏并没有遵循真正的边界。栅栏的一部分位于兄妹的土地内。栅栏的一部分侵占了邻居的土地。邻居和姐姐签订了一份协议，按照哥哥建造的围栏确定边界线。该协议满足了司法管辖区所需的所有手续，并得到了及时、正确的记录。协议登记一年后，哥哥暂时和解了与妹妹的分歧，并恢复了对土地的共同占有。此后，兄弟否认了边界线协议，并对邻居和姐妹提起适当诉讼，以平息原来的真实边界沿线的所有权。这样的行动，哥哥会占上风吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为从法律角度来看，该协议对兄弟姐妹是互惠互利的。","B":"不，因为协议签署时，该土地是由姐姐独自拥有的。","C":"是的，因为一个按份共有人（tenant in common）不能通过边界线协议约束另一按份共有人（tenant in common）。","D":"是的，因为银行不是协议的一方。"},"explanationZh":"共同参与者之间的同意\n\n（常见例子）\n\n需要同意 转让全部共有财产 获得部分财产的排他性占有 通过与第三方签订的合同约束其他共有人的利益 不同意 需要出售、租赁或设计个人所有权权益 拥有全部共有财产 从共有财产中提取矿物（除非产生废物） 共同共有（joint tenancy）是对土地的共同所有权，其中每个共同共有人拥有财产的份额，但拥有拥有整个财产的平等权利（即单独但不可分割的权益）。按份共有人（tenant in common）可以在未经其他按份共有人（tenant in common）同意的情况下转让自己的权益。然而，按份共有人（tenant in common）不能转让其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的利益，也不能通过影响财产的合同（例如边界线协议）来约束他们。在这里，兄弟姐妹以共同佃户的身份简单地分享土地。姐姐和邻居签订了一份合同，按照哥哥竖起的栅栏确定边界线。尽管在协议签署时兄弟并不拥有土地，但他仍然是按份共有人（tenant in common）（选择 B）。因此，姐姐无法约束他遵守该协议，他很可能会获胜。 （选择A） 当一份合同对所有按份共有人（tenant in common）来说是互惠互利的，则推定他们都已批准（即确认或接受）该合同。尽管批准会使原本无效的合同有效，但当按份共有人（tenant in common）拒绝协议时，推定的批准就会被推翻（如此处所示）。 （选项 D）根据抵押留置权理论（多数规则），贷款人在抵押财产中拥有担保权益，但没有所有权。因此，该银行不是边界线协议的一方这一事实并不意味着该协议无法对兄弟执行。相反，兄弟不受约束，因为他不是当事人。\n\n教育目标：虽然按份共有人（tenant in common）可以自由地将其利益转让给第三方，但该租户不能转让其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的利益或使其他按份共有人（tenant in common）遵守影响共有财产的合同。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 93 (2018)（约束其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的能力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"尽管按份共有人（tenant in common）可以自由地将其利益转让给第三方，但该租户不能转让其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的利益或使其他按份共有人（tenant in common）遵守影响共有财产的合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"这样的行动，哥哥会占上风吗？","rule":"尽管按份共有人（tenant in common）可以自由地将其利益转让给第三方，但该租户不能转让其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的利益或使其他按份共有人（tenant in common）遵守影响共有财产的合同。","analysis":"共同参与者之间的同意\n\n（常见例子）\n\n需要同意 转让全部共有财产 获得部分财产的排他性占有 通过与第三方签订的合同约束其他共有人的利益 不同意 需要出售、租赁或设计个人所有权权益 拥有全部共有财产 从共有财产中提取矿物（除非产生废物） 共同共有（joint tenancy）是对土地的共同所有权，其中每个共同共有人拥有财产的份额，但拥有拥有整个财产的平等权利（即单独但不可分割的权益）。按份共有人（tenant in common）可以在未经其他按份共有人（tenant in common）同意的情况下转让自己的权益。然而，按份共有人（tenant in common）不能转让其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的利益，也不能通过影响财产的合同（例如边界线协议）来约束他们。在这里，兄弟姐妹以共同佃户的身份简单地分享土地。姐姐和邻居签订了一份合同，按照哥哥竖起的栅栏确定边界线。尽管在协议签署时兄弟并不拥有土地，但他仍然是按份共有人（tenant in common）（选择 B）。因此，姐姐无法约束他遵守该协议，他很可能会获胜。 （选择A） 当一份合同对所有按份共有人（tenant in common）来说是互惠互利的，则推定他们都已批准（即确认或接受）该合同。尽管批准会使原本无效的合同有效，但当按份共有人（tenant in common）拒绝协议时，推定的批准就会被推翻（如此处所示）。 （选项 D）根据抵押留置权理论（多数规则），贷款人在抵押财产中拥有担保权益，但没有所有权。因此，该银行不是边界线协议的一方这一事实并不意味着该协议无法对兄弟执行。相反，兄弟不受约束，因为他不是当事人。\n\n教育目标：虽然按份共有人（tenant in common）可以自由地将其利益转让给第三方，但该租户不能转让其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的利益或使其他按份共有人（tenant in common）遵守影响共有财产的合同。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 93 (2018)（约束其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的能力）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为一个按份共有人（tenant in common）不能通过边界线协议约束另一按份共有人（tenant in common）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":139,"sourceQuestionPage":407,"sourceQuestionPages":[407],"sourceExplanationPages":[408,409],"sourceAnswerPages":[408,409],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-140","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Co-ownership","question":"A married couple purchased a residence. They took title to the property as tenants by the entirety. Subsequently, they separated, with the husband moving out and the wife remaining in the residence with their minor child. The husband has filed an action to partition the residence, which the wife opposes. How should the court rule on the husband's partition action?","choices":{"A":"For the husband, because the couple is no longer living together in the residence.","B":"For the husband, because the court can order the sale of the residence rather than its physical division.","C":"For the wife, because she is living in the residence with their minor child.","D":"For the wife, because the couple is married."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A tenancy by the entirety (TBE) is a type of cotenancy that is available only to married couples. As a result, the TBE terminates upon divorce and becomes a tenancy in common. While tenants in common can unilaterally request a partition (which the court must grant), a TBE can only be partitioned if both spouses consent. That is because tenants by the entirety are viewed as a single marital unit. Here, the married couple took title to the residence as tenants by the entirety. Although the couple separated and no longer live together in the residence, they are still married (Choice A). This means that the TBE remains intact, and the husband cannot partition the residence without the wife's consent. Therefore, the court should rule for the wife. (Choice B) Although courts prefer a partition in kind (ie, physical division of property), a court may order a partition by sale if physically dividing the property is not feasible or fair. But here, the court should not partition the residence by either method because the couple is still married and the wife opposes the partition action. (Choice C) In many states, after a divorce, a former spouse residing in the family residence with a minor child may have the right to remain there for a statutory period. This would be relevant in a divorce action, but not this partition action.\n\nEducational objective: A tenancy by the entirety is only available to married couples and can only be partitioned if both spouses consent.\n\nReferences\n41 Am. Jur. 2d Husband and Wife § 32 (2020) (no right to partition tenancy by the entirety without consent).","educationalObjective":"A tenancy by the entirety is only available to married couples and can only be partitioned if both spouses consent.","references":"41 Am. Jur. 2d Husband and Wife § 32 (2020) (no right to partition tenancy by the entirety without consent).","rule":"A tenancy by the entirety is only available to married couples and can only be partitioned if both spouses consent.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule on the husband's partition action?","rule":"A tenancy by the entirety is only available to married couples and can only be partitioned if both spouses consent.","analysis":"A tenancy by the entirety (TBE) is a type of cotenancy that is available only to married couples. As a result, the TBE terminates upon divorce and becomes a tenancy in common. While tenants in common can unilaterally request a partition (which the court must grant), a TBE can only be partitioned if both spouses consent. That is because tenants by the entirety are viewed as a single marital unit. Here, the married couple took title to the residence as tenants by the entirety. Although the couple separated and no longer live together in the residence, they are still married (Choice A). This means that the TBE remains intact, and the husband cannot partition the residence without the wife's consent. Therefore, the court should rule for the wife. (Choice B) Although courts prefer a partition in kind (ie, physical division of property), a court may order a partition by sale if physically dividing the property is not feasible or fair. But here, the court should not partition the residence by either method because the couple is still married and the wife opposes the partition action. (Choice C) In many states, after a divorce, a former spouse residing in the family residence with a minor child may have the right to remain there for a statutory period. This would be relevant in a divorce action, but not this partition action.","conclusion":"Answer D: For the wife, because the couple is married."},"questionZh":"一对已婚夫妇购买了一套住宅。他们以租户的身份获得了该房产的所有权。随后，他们分居，丈夫搬出，妻子带着未成年的孩子留在住所。丈夫提出分割住宅的诉讼，但妻子反对。法院该如何判决丈夫的分割诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"对于丈夫来说，因为夫妻俩不再一起居住在该住所。","B":"对于丈夫来说，因为法院可以命令出售住宅而不是物理分割。","C":"对于妻子来说，因为她和未成年的孩子住在该住所。","D":"对于妻子来说，因为夫妻俩已经结婚了。"},"explanationZh":"整体租赁 (TBE) 是一种仅适用于已婚夫妇的共同共有（joint tenancy）类型。因此，TBE 在离婚时终止并成为共同共有（joint tenancy）。虽然按份共有人（tenant in common）可以单方面请求分割（法院必须批准），但 TBE 只有在配偶双方同意的情况下才能分割。这是因为整个租户被视为一个单一的婚姻单位。在这里，这对已婚夫妇作为整个租户拥有该住宅的所有权。尽管夫妻俩已分居且不再同居，但他们仍然是夫妻（选项 A）。这意味着 TBE 保持不变，未经妻子同意，丈夫不能分割住宅。因此，法院应作出有利于妻子的判决。 （选择B）虽然法院更倾向于实物分割（即财产的实际分割），但如果实际分割财产不可行或不公平，法院可以命令通过出售进行分割。但在这里，法院不应该通过任何一种方法分割住宅，因为夫妻双方仍处于婚姻状态，并且妻子反对分割行动。 （选择 C） 在许多州，离婚后，居住在家庭住所且有未成年子女的前配偶可能有权在法定期限内继续居住。这与离婚诉讼有关，但与分割诉讼无关。\n\n教育目标： 整体租赁仅适用于已婚夫妇，并且只有在配偶双方同意的情况下才能分割。\n\n参考文献\n上午 41 点。尤尔。 2d 丈夫和妻子 § 32 (2020)（未经同意，无权分割全部租赁）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"整体租赁仅适用于已婚夫妇，并且只有在配偶双方同意的情况下才能分割。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院该如何判决丈夫的分割诉讼？","rule":"整体租赁仅适用于已婚夫妇，并且只有在配偶双方同意的情况下才能分割。","analysis":"整体租赁 (TBE) 是一种仅适用于已婚夫妇的共同共有（joint tenancy）类型。因此，TBE 在离婚时终止并成为共同共有（joint tenancy）。虽然按份共有人（tenant in common）可以单方面请求分割（法院必须批准），但 TBE 只有在配偶双方同意的情况下才能分割。这是因为整个租户被视为一个单一的婚姻单位。在这里，这对已婚夫妇作为整个租户拥有该住宅的所有权。尽管夫妻俩已分居且不再同居，但他们仍然是夫妻（选项 A）。这意味着 TBE 保持不变，未经妻子同意，丈夫不能分割住宅。因此，法院应作出有利于妻子的判决。 （选择B）虽然法院更倾向于实物分割（即财产的实际分割），但如果实际分割财产不可行或不公平，法院可以命令通过出售进行分割。但在这里，法院不应该通过任何一种方法分割住宅，因为夫妻双方仍处于婚姻状态，并且妻子反对分割行动。 （选择 C） 在许多州，离婚后，居住在家庭住所且有未成年子女的前配偶可能有权在法定期限内继续居住。这与离婚诉讼有关，但与分割诉讼无关。\n\n教育目标： 整体租赁仅适用于已婚夫妇，并且只有在配偶双方同意的情况下才能分割。\n\n参考文献\n上午 41 点。尤尔。 2d 丈夫和妻子 § 32 (2020)（未经同意，无权分割全部租赁）。","conclusion":"答案 D：对于妻子来说，因为夫妻俩已经结婚了。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-9943e35cd40e0fee"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":140,"sourceQuestionPage":410,"sourceQuestionPages":[410],"sourceExplanationPages":[411,412],"sourceAnswerPages":[411,412],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-141","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Thirty years ago, a landowner conveyed land by warranty deed to a church (a charity) \"so long as the land herein conveyed is used as the site for the principal religious edifice maintained by said church.\" Twenty years ago, the landowner died intestate, survived by a single heir. One year ago, the church dissolved and its church building situated on the land was demolished. There is no applicable statute. The common law Rule Against Perpetuities is unmodified in the jurisdiction. In an appropriate action, the landowner's heir and the attorney general, who is the appropriate official to assert public interests in charitable trusts, contest the right to the land. In this action, who will prevail?","choices":{"A":"The attorney general, because cy pres should be applied to devote the land to religious purposes to carry out the charitable intent of the landowner.","B":"The attorney general, because the landowner's attempt to restrict the church's fee simple violated the common law Rule Against Perpetuities.","C":"The landowner's heir, as successor to the landowner's possibility of reverter.","D":"The landowner's heir, because a charity cannot convey assets donated to it."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nDefeasible fees are fee simple estates that automatically terminate if a specified event or condition occurs. One type of defeasible fee is a fee simple determinable (FSD), which is created with durational language (eg, \"so long as\"). An FSD is followed by either: a possibility of reverter – when the estate automatically reverts back to the grantor upon the happening of the specified event (presumed if not expressly stated) or\n\nan executory interest – when the estate automatically passes to a third party upon the happening of the specified event (must be expressly stated).\n\nHere, the landowner conveyed the church an FSD followed by the landowner's possibility of reverter. When the landowner died, his possibility of reverter was inherited by his sole heir. So when the church dissolved and its principal religious edifice (ie, the church building) was demolished, title automatically reverted to the landowner's heir. Therefore, the heir will prevail. (Choice A) When a charitable trust no longer serves the purpose for which it was created, courts can use the cy pres doctrine to redirect the trust to a similar charity or purpose. However, this doctrine does not apply when the conveyance includes a possibility of reverter (as seen here) or otherwise dictates what should happen when the assets for the trust are no longer used. (Choice B) Under the Rule Against Perpetuities, specific future interests are void if there is any possibility that the interest will not vest within 21 years after the end of all lives in\n\nbeing at the creation of the interest. But this rule does not apply to future interests held by the grantor (eg, a possibility of reverter) OR charitable trusts. (Choice D) A charity can convey assets donated to it.\n\nEducational objective: A fee simple determinable is a defeasible fee created with durational language. Unless otherwise stated, it is presumed that the estate will automatically revert back to the grantor if the terminating event or condition occurs.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 44 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (fee simple determinable).","educationalObjective":"A fee simple determinable is a defeasible fee created with durational language. Unless otherwise stated, it is presumed that the estate will automatically revert back to the grantor if the terminating event or condition occurs.","references":"Restatement of Property § 44 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (fee simple determinable).","rule":"A fee simple determinable is a defeasible fee created with durational language. Unless otherwise stated, it is presumed that the estate will automatically revert back to the grantor if the terminating event or condition occurs.","irac":{"issue":"In this action, who will prevail?","rule":"A fee simple determinable is a defeasible fee created with durational language. Unless otherwise stated, it is presumed that the estate will automatically revert back to the grantor if the terminating event or condition occurs.","analysis":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nDefeasible fees are fee simple estates that automatically terminate if a specified event or condition occurs. One type of defeasible fee is a fee simple determinable (FSD), which is created with durational language (eg, \"so long as\"). An FSD is followed by either: a possibility of reverter – when the estate automatically reverts back to the grantor upon the happening of the specified event (presumed if not expressly stated) or\n\nan executory interest – when the estate automatically passes to a third party upon the happening of the specified event (must be expressly stated).\n\nHere, the landowner conveyed the church an FSD followed by the landowner's possibility of reverter. When the landowner died, his possibility of reverter was inherited by his sole heir. So when the church dissolved and its principal religious edifice (ie, the church building) was demolished, title automatically reverted to the landowner's heir. Therefore, the heir will prevail. (Choice A) When a charitable trust no longer serves the purpose for which it was created, courts can use the cy pres doctrine to redirect the trust to a similar charity or purpose. However, this doctrine does not apply when the conveyance includes a possibility of reverter (as seen here) or otherwise dictates what should happen when the assets for the trust are no longer used. (Choice B) Under the Rule Against Perpetuities, specific future interests are void if there is any possibility that the interest will not vest within 21 years after the end of all lives in\n\nbeing at the creation of the interest. But this rule does not apply to future interests held by the grantor (eg, a possibility of reverter) OR charitable trusts. (Choice D) A charity can convey assets donated to it.","conclusion":"Answer C: The landowner's heir, as successor to the landowner's possibility of reverter."},"questionZh":"三十年前，一位土地所有者通过保修契约将土地转让给一家教堂（慈善机构），“只要此处转让的土地用作该教堂维护的主要宗教建筑的所在地”。二十年前，地主去世时没有留下遗嘱，仅留下一名继承人。一年前，教堂解散，位于该地块上的教堂建筑也被拆除。没有适用的法规。普通法中的反永续规则在管辖范围内未作修改。在适当的行动中，土地所有者的继承人和总检察长（在慈善信托中维护公共利益的适当官员）对土地权利提出了争议。这场行动中，谁会占上风？","choicesZh":{"A":"总检察长，因为 cy pres 应该适用于将土地用于宗教目的，以实现土地所有者的慈善意图。","B":"总检察长认为，土地所有者试图限制教堂的费用，这违反了普通法的“反对永久原则”。","C":"土地所有者的继承人，作为土地所有者回归可能性的继承人。","D":"土地所有者的继承人，因为慈善机构无法转让捐赠给它的资产。"},"explanationZh":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n可废止的费用是简单的费用，如果发生指定的事件或条件，它会自动终止。一种类型的可废除费用是简单可确定费用（FSD），它是用持续语言（例如“只要”）创建的。 FSD 之后是： 归还者的可能性 – 当发生指定事件（如果没有明确说明则假定）时，遗产自动归还给授予人，或者\n\n执行权益——当指定事件发生时，遗产自动转移给第三方（必须明确说明）。\n\n在这里，地主向教会传达了 FSD，然后是地主恢复的可能性。当地主去世后，他的回归权由他的唯一继承人继承。因此，当教堂解散并且其主要宗教建筑（即教堂建筑）被拆除时，所有权自动恢复到地主的继承人手中。因此，继承人将占上风。 （选择 A）当慈善信托不再满足其创建目的时，法院可以使用 cy pres 原则将信托重新定向到类似的慈善机构或目的。然而，当转让包括恢复的可能性（如此处所示）或以其他方式规定当信托资产不再使用时应该发生什么时，该原则不适用。 （选择B）根据反对永续规则，如果特定的未来权益有可能在所有生命周期结束后21年内无法归属，则该权益无效。\n\n正在创造兴趣。但该规则不适用于授予人持有的未来权益（例如，恢复的可能性）或慈善信托。 （选项 D）慈善机构可以转让捐赠给它的资产。\n\n教育目标：可简单确定的费用是用持续语言创建的可废止的费用。除非另有说明，否则假定如果发生终止事件或条件，遗产将自动返还给授予人。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 44（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（费用可简单确定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"简单确定的费用是用持续语言创建的可废止的费用。除非另有说明，否则假定如果发生终止事件或条件，遗产将自动返还给授予人。","iracZh":{"issue":"这场行动中，谁会占上风？","rule":"简单确定的费用是用持续语言创建的可废止的费用。除非另有说明，否则假定如果发生终止事件或条件，遗产将自动返还给授予人。","analysis":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n可废止的费用是简单的费用，如果发生指定的事件或条件，它会自动终止。一种类型的可废除费用是简单可确定费用（FSD），它是用持续语言（例如“只要”）创建的。 FSD 之后是： 归还者的可能性 – 当发生指定事件（如果没有明确说明则假定）时，遗产自动归还给授予人，或者\n\n执行权益——当指定事件发生时，遗产自动转移给第三方（必须明确说明）。\n\n在这里，地主向教会传达了 FSD，然后是地主恢复的可能性。当地主去世后，他的回归权由他的唯一继承人继承。因此，当教堂解散并且其主要宗教建筑（即教堂建筑）被拆除时，所有权自动恢复到地主的继承人手中。因此，继承人将占上风。 （选择 A）当慈善信托不再满足其创建目的时，法院可以使用 cy pres 原则将信托重新定向到类似的慈善机构或目的。然而，当转让包括恢复的可能性（如此处所示）或以其他方式规定当信托资产不再使用时应该发生什么时，该原则不适用。 （选择B）根据反对永续规则，如果特定的未来权益有可能在所有生命周期结束后21年内无法归属，则该权益无效。\n\n正在创造兴趣。但该规则不适用于授予人持有的未来权益（例如，恢复的可能性）或慈善信托。 （选项 D）慈善机构可以转让捐赠给它的资产。\n\n教育目标：可简单确定的费用是用持续语言创建的可废止的费用。除非另有说明，否则假定如果发生终止事件或条件，遗产将自动返还给授予人。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 44（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（费用可简单确定）。","conclusion":"答案 C：土地所有者的继承人，作为土地所有者回归可能性的继承人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":141,"sourceQuestionPage":413,"sourceQuestionPages":[413],"sourceExplanationPages":[414,415],"sourceAnswerPages":[414,415],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-142","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A man owned a tract of land in fee simple. By will duly admitted to probate after his death, the man devised the land to \"any wife who survives me for life with remainder to such of my children as are living at her death.\" The man was survived by his wife and by two daughters and a son. Thereafter, the son died and by will duly admitted to probate devised his entire estate to a friend. The son's sole heirs at law were the two daughters. Later, the wife died. In an appropriate lawsuit to which the two daughters and the friend are parties, title to the land is at issue. In such lawsuit, judgment should be that title to the land is in which party?","choices":{"A":"The two daughters and the friend, because the earliest vesting of remainders is favored and reference to the wife's death should be construed as relating to time of taking possession.","B":"The two daughters and the friend, because the provision requiring survival of children violates the Rule Against Perpetuities since the surviving wife might have been a person unborn at the time of writing of the will.","C":"The two daughters, because the remainders were contingent upon surviving the life tenant.","D":"The two daughters, because the son's remainder must descend by intestacy and is not devisable."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Vested remainders v. Contingent remainders\n\nDefinition Examples\n\nVested Remainder not subject to condition precedent & \"... remainder to A\" held by identifiable living person\n\nContingent Remainder subject to condition precedent or \"... remainder to A, but only held by unknown / unborn person if A outlives B\" \"... remainder to my heirs\" A life estate is a present possessory interest in real property that expires upon the death of an individual. The future interest that follows a life estate is either a reversion (if it is held by the grantor) or a remainder (if it is created in a grantee—as seen here). A remainder is contingent if it is held by an unknown or unborn person OR subject to a condition precedent. Here, the man devised a life estate in the land to his wife and a remainder to his children— but only if they survived the wife. Because the children's remainders were contingent upon surviving the wife, no child's interest can vest before the wife's death. This is true regardless of whether there is a policy favoring early vesting (Choice A). And since the son died before the wife, the son never acquired an interest in the land to devise to his friend. Therefore, the two daughters—both of whom were alive when the wife died—have title to the land. (Choice B) A future interest that violates the Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) is void. Therefore, had the children's remainders violated RAP, the daughters would not take title to the land. (Choice D) Remainders (whether they are vested or contingent) are transferable inter vivos, devisable by will, and inheritable by intestate succession.\n\nEducational objective: Future interests following a life estate are called remainders if they are created in a grantee. And remainders are contingent if they are (1) held by an unknown or unborn person or (2) subject to a condition precedent.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 157 cmt. u (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (contingent remainders).","educationalObjective":"Future interests following a life estate are called remainders if they are created in a grantee. And remainders are contingent if they are (1) held by an unknown or unborn person or (2) subject to a condition precedent.","references":"Restatement of Property § 157 cmt. u (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (contingent remainders).","rule":"Future interests following a life estate are called remainders if they are created in a grantee. And remainders are contingent if they are (1) held by an unknown or unborn person or (2) subject to a condition precedent.","irac":{"issue":"In such lawsuit, judgment should be that title to the land is in which party?","rule":"Future interests following a life estate are called remainders if they are created in a grantee. And remainders are contingent if they are (1) held by an unknown or unborn person or (2) subject to a condition precedent.","analysis":"Vested remainders v. Contingent remainders\n\nDefinition Examples\n\nVested Remainder not subject to condition precedent & \"... remainder to A\" held by identifiable living person\n\nContingent Remainder subject to condition precedent or \"... remainder to A, but only held by unknown / unborn person if A outlives B\" \"... remainder to my heirs\" A life estate is a present possessory interest in real property that expires upon the death of an individual. The future interest that follows a life estate is either a reversion (if it is held by the grantor) or a remainder (if it is created in a grantee—as seen here). A remainder is contingent if it is held by an unknown or unborn person OR subject to a condition precedent. Here, the man devised a life estate in the land to his wife and a remainder to his children— but only if they survived the wife. Because the children's remainders were contingent upon surviving the wife, no child's interest can vest before the wife's death. This is true regardless of whether there is a policy favoring early vesting (Choice A). And since the son died before the wife, the son never acquired an interest in the land to devise to his friend. Therefore, the two daughters—both of whom were alive when the wife died—have title to the land. (Choice B) A future interest that violates the Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) is void. Therefore, had the children's remainders violated RAP, the daughters would not take title to the land. (Choice D) Remainders (whether they are vested or contingent) are transferable inter vivos, devisable by will, and inheritable by intestate succession.","conclusion":"Answer C: The two daughters, because the remainders were contingent upon surviving the life tenant."},"questionZh":"一个人简单地拥有一块土地。根据他死后正式接受遗嘱认证的遗嘱，该男子将这块土地设计给“在我身后生存的任何妻子，其余的则留给我在她去世时仍然活着的孩子”。该男子身后留下了妻子、两个女儿和一个儿子。此后，儿子去世了，遗嘱正式承认将他的全部遗产转让给了一位朋友。儿子的唯一合法继承人是两个女儿。后来，妻子去世了。在两个女儿和朋友作为当事人的适当诉讼中，土地所有权存在争议。在这样的诉讼中，应该判断土地所有权属于哪一方？","choicesZh":{"A":"两个女儿和朋友，因为剩余的最早归属受到青睐，并且提及妻子的死亡应被解释为与占有时间有关。","B":"两个女儿和朋友，因为要求孩子生存的条款违反了“反对永久规则”，因为在写遗嘱时幸存的妻子可能是未出生的人。","C":"两个女儿，因为剩下的孩子取决于终身房客的生存。","D":"两个女儿，因为儿子的剩余部分必须通过无遗嘱继承，并且不可设计。"},"explanationZh":"既得剩余物 v. 或有剩余物\n\n定义示例\n\n既得剩余部分不受先决条件约束且“...剩余部分归A”由可识别的在世人士持有\n\n或有剩余物受先决条件限制或“...剩余物归A，但仅由未知/未出生的人持有，如果A比B更长寿”“...剩余物归我的继承人”终身遗产是不动产中现有的占有权益，在个人死亡时到期。终身财产之后的未来利息要么是归还（如果由授予人持有），要么是剩余权益（如果是由受让人创建——如此处所示）。如果剩余部分由未知或未出生的人持有或受先决条件约束，则剩余部分是或有的。在这里，男人将这块土地的终身财产留给了他的妻子，并将剩余的财产留给了他的孩子——但前提是他们在妻子去世后还活着。由于孩子的剩余财产取决于妻子的去世，因此在妻子去世之前，任何孩子的权益都不能归属。无论是否有支持提前兑现的政策（选择 A），情况都是如此。由于儿子先于妻子去世，儿子从未获得土地的权益来分配给他的朋友。因此，两个女儿——妻子去世时都还活着——拥有土地的所有权。 （选择 B）违反永续规则 (RAP) 的未来权益无效。因此，如果孩子们的剩余财产违反了移民安置计划，女儿们将不会获得土地所有权。 （选项 D） 剩余物（无论是既得的还是偶然的）可以在生前转让，可以通过遗嘱设计，并且可以通过无遗嘱继承来继承。\n\n教育目标：如果终身财产的未来权益是由受让人创造的，则称为剩余权益。如果剩余部分是（1）由未知或未出生的人持有或（2）受先决条件约束，则剩余部分是或有的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述§ 157 cmt。 u（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（或有余数）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果终身财产的未来权益是由受让人创建的，则称为剩余权益。如果剩余部分是（1）由未知或未出生的人持有或（2）受先决条件约束，则剩余部分是或有的。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这样的诉讼中，应该判断土地所有权属于哪一方？","rule":"如果终身财产的未来权益是由受让人创建的，则称为剩余权益。如果剩余部分是（1）由未知或未出生的人持有或（2）受先决条件约束，则剩余部分是或有的。","analysis":"既得剩余物 v. 或有剩余物\n\n定义示例\n\n既得剩余部分不受先决条件约束且“...剩余部分归A”由可识别的在世人士持有\n\n或有剩余物受先决条件限制或“...剩余物归A，但仅由未知/未出生的人持有，如果A比B更长寿”“...剩余物归我的继承人”终身遗产是不动产中现有的占有权益，在个人死亡时到期。终身财产之后的未来利息要么是归还（如果由授予人持有），要么是剩余权益（如果是由受让人创建——如此处所示）。如果剩余部分由未知或未出生的人持有或受先决条件约束，则剩余部分是或有的。在这里，男人将这块土地的终身财产留给了他的妻子，并将剩余的财产留给了他的孩子——但前提是他们在妻子去世后还活着。由于孩子的剩余财产取决于妻子的去世，因此在妻子去世之前，任何孩子的权益都不能归属。无论是否有支持提前兑现的政策（选择 A），情况都是如此。由于儿子先于妻子去世，儿子从未获得土地的权益来分配给他的朋友。因此，两个女儿——妻子去世时都还活着——拥有土地的所有权。 （选择 B）违反永续规则 (RAP) 的未来权益无效。因此，如果孩子们的剩余财产违反了移民安置计划，女儿们将不会获得土地所有权。 （选项 D） 剩余物（无论是既得的还是偶然的）可以在生前转让，可以通过遗嘱设计，并且可以通过无遗嘱继承来继承。\n\n教育目标：如果终身财产的未来权益是由受让人创造的，则称为剩余权益。如果剩余部分是（1）由未知或未出生的人持有或（2）受先决条件约束，则剩余部分是或有的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述§ 157 cmt。 u（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（或有余数）。","conclusion":"答案 C：两个女儿，因为剩下的孩子取决于终身房客的生存。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":142,"sourceQuestionPage":416,"sourceQuestionPages":[416],"sourceExplanationPages":[417,418],"sourceAnswerPages":[417,418],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-143","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A grantor owned two tracts of land, one of 15 acres and another of 5 acres. The two tracts were a mile apart. Fifteen years ago, the grantor conveyed the smaller tract to a grantee. The grantor retained the larger tract. The deed to the grantee contained, in addition to proper legal descriptions of both properties and identifications of the parties, the following language: \"I, the grantor, bind myself and my heirs and assigns that in the event that the larger tract that I now retain is ever offered for sale, I will notify the grantee and his heirs and assigns in writing, and the grantee and his heirs and assigns shall have the right to purchase the larger tract for its fair market value as determined by a board consisting of three qualified expert independent real estate appraisers.\" With appropriate references to the other property and the parties, there followed a reciprocal provision that conferred upon the grantor and her heirs and assigns a similar right to purchase the smaller tract, purportedly binding the grantee and his heirs and assigns. Ten years ago, a corporation acquired the larger tract from the grantor. At that time, the grantee had no interest in acquiring the larger tract and by an appropriate written document released any interest he or his heirs or assigns might have had in the larger tract. Last year, the grantee died. The smaller tract passed by the grantee's will to his daughter. She has decided to sell the smaller tract. However, because she believes that the corporation has been a very poor steward of the larger tract, she refuses to sell the smaller tract to the corporation even though she has offered it for sale in the local real estate market. The corporation has brought an appropriate action for specific performance of the right of first refusal after taking all the necessary preliminary steps in its effort to exercise its right to purchase the smaller tract. The daughter has asserted all possible defenses. The common law Rule Against Perpetuities is unmodified in the jurisdiction, and there are no applicable statutes. If the court rules for the daughter, what will be the likely reason?","choices":{"A":"Noncompliance with a right to purchase gives rise to a claim for money damages, but not for specific performance.","B":"The grantee's release 10 years ago operates as a waiver regarding any right to purchase that the corporation might have.","C":"The provision setting out the right to purchase violates the Rule Against Perpetuities.","D":"The two tracts of land were not adjacent parcels of real estate, so the right to purchase is in gross and therefore unenforceable."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A right of first refusal is a contractual right to purchase property before any other person if the owner later decides to sell. As a result, it is a contingent future interest that is subject to the Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP). RAP renders such interests void if there is any possibility that they could vest more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest. Therefore, rights of first refusal almost always violate RAP if they are not set to terminate—either temporally (eg, calendar date) or by referencing a life in being (eg, within 21 years of the grantee's death)—within the perpetuities period. Here, the corporation seeks to enforce its right of first refusal over the daughter's smaller tract. Since the deed creating this interest references the parties' heirs and assigns without any mention of a termination date, this right of first refusal could be exercised after the perpetuities period. Therefore, the right of first refusal is invalid under RAP, and the court will likely rule for the daughter on this basis.* *Since this jurisdiction follows the common-law RAP, this right of first refusal is struck ab initio (ie, from the beginning). But had this jurisdiction adopted the majority \"wait and see\" stance, the right would have been valid since it vested within 90 years of its creation. (Choice A) Specific performance is available when money damages are inadequate. Since land is considered unique and money damages cannot compensate for the loss of unique property, this remedy is generally available to those with a right to purchase. (Choice B) The grantee's waiver of his own right of first refusal as to the larger parcel does not waive the corporation's right to purchase the smaller parcel at issue here. (Choice D) The adjacency of the tracts is irrelevant. Had the right of first refusal been valid, it would have ripened into a right to purchase in gross (ie, in full) when the seller received an offer from a third-party purchaser. And a right to purchase in gross is enforceable if it complies with RAP.\n\nEducational objective: To comply with the Rule Against Perpetuities, a right of first refusal must be set to vest or fail within the perpetuities period—ie, within 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.\n\nReferences\n61 Am. Jur. 2d Perpetuities and Restraints on Alienation § 5 (2020) (Rule Against Perpetuities).","educationalObjective":"To comply with the Rule Against Perpetuities, a right of first refusal must be set to vest or fail within the perpetuities period—ie, within 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.","references":"61 Am. Jur. 2d Perpetuities and Restraints on Alienation § 5 (2020) (Rule Against Perpetuities).","rule":"To comply with the Rule Against Perpetuities, a right of first refusal must be set to vest or fail within the perpetuities period—ie, within 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.","irac":{"issue":"If the court rules for the daughter, what will be the likely reason?","rule":"To comply with the Rule Against Perpetuities, a right of first refusal must be set to vest or fail within the perpetuities period—ie, within 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest.","analysis":"A right of first refusal is a contractual right to purchase property before any other person if the owner later decides to sell. As a result, it is a contingent future interest that is subject to the Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP). RAP renders such interests void if there is any possibility that they could vest more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest. Therefore, rights of first refusal almost always violate RAP if they are not set to terminate—either temporally (eg, calendar date) or by referencing a life in being (eg, within 21 years of the grantee's death)—within the perpetuities period. Here, the corporation seeks to enforce its right of first refusal over the daughter's smaller tract. Since the deed creating this interest references the parties' heirs and assigns without any mention of a termination date, this right of first refusal could be exercised after the perpetuities period. Therefore, the right of first refusal is invalid under RAP, and the court will likely rule for the daughter on this basis.* *Since this jurisdiction follows the common-law RAP, this right of first refusal is struck ab initio (ie, from the beginning). But had this jurisdiction adopted the majority \"wait and see\" stance, the right would have been valid since it vested within 90 years of its creation. (Choice A) Specific performance is available when money damages are inadequate. Since land is considered unique and money damages cannot compensate for the loss of unique property, this remedy is generally available to those with a right to purchase. (Choice B) The grantee's waiver of his own right of first refusal as to the larger parcel does not waive the corporation's right to purchase the smaller parcel at issue here. (Choice D) The adjacency of the tracts is irrelevant. Had the right of first refusal been valid, it would have ripened into a right to purchase in gross (ie, in full) when the seller received an offer from a third-party purchaser. And a right to purchase in gross is enforceable if it complies with RAP.","conclusion":"Answer C: The provision setting out the right to purchase violates the Rule Against Perpetuities."},"questionZh":"设保人拥有两块土地，一块 15 英亩，另一块 5 英亩。两条路相距一英里。十五年前，授予人将较小的土地转让给受赠人。设保人保留较大的土地。受让人的契约，除了对各方财产和身份的适当法律描述外，还包含以下内容：“我，授予人，约束我自己和我的继承人，并指定，如果我现在保留的较大土地被出售，我将以书面形式通知受让人及其继承人和受让人，受让人及其继承人和受让人有权按照由三名合格的董事会组成的委员会确定的公平市场价值购买较大的土地。专业的独立房地产评估师。”在适当提及其他财产和当事人的情况下，随后出现了一项互惠条款，该条款赋予设保人及其继承人并转让购买较小土地的类似权利，据称对受让人及其继承人和受让人具有约束力。十年前，一家公司从授予人手中收购了更大的土地。当时，受让人没有兴趣获得更大的土地，并通过适当的书面文件释放了他或其继承人或受让人可能在更大的土地上拥有的任何利益。去年，受资助者去世了。较小的单张根据受赠者的遗嘱传给了他的女儿。她决定出售较小的土地。然而，由于她认为该公司对大片土地的管理非常糟糕，因此她拒绝将较小的土地出售给该公司，尽管她已将其在当地房地产市场上出售。在采取了所有必要的初步步骤以行使其购买较小土地的权利之后，该公司已针对具体履行优先购买权提起了适当的诉讼。女儿提出了所有可能的辩护。普通法中的“反永续规则”在管辖范围内未经修改，并且没有适用的法规。如果法院判决女儿胜诉，可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"不遵守购买权会导致金钱损失索赔，但不会针对具体履行情况提出索赔。","B":"受赠者 10 年前的释放相当于放弃该公司可能拥有的任何购买权。","C":"规定购买权的规定违反了“反对永续权规则”。","D":"这两块土地不是相邻的房地产，因此购买权是总量的，因此无法执行。"},"explanationZh":"优先购买权是一种合同权利，如果业主后来决定出售，则可以在任何其他人之前购买财产。因此，或有未来权益受到永续规则（RAP）的约束。如果这些权益有可能在权益创建时的相关寿命超过 21 年后归属，那么 RAP 就会使这些权益无效。因此，如果优先购买权没有设定在永久期限内终止——无论是暂时终止（例如，日历日期）还是通过引用存在的生命（例如，受让人死亡后21年内），那么优先购买权几乎总是违反RAP。在这里，公司寻求对女儿较小的土地行使优先购买权。由于产生该权益的契约涉及双方的继承人和受让人，而没有提及终止日期，因此可以在永久期限后行使优先购买权。因此，优先购买权在 RAP 下无效，法院可能会据此作出对女儿有利的裁决。* *由于该司法管辖区遵循普通法 RAP，因此优先购买权是从头开始（即从一开始）就被行使的。但如果这个司法管辖区采取了多数人的“观望”立场，那么该权利自其创建之日起 90 年内就将一直有效。 （选项A）当金钱赔偿不足时，可以采取特定履行。由于土地被认为是独特的，金钱损失无法弥补独特财产的损失，因此有权购买的人通常可以使用这种补救措施。 （选项B）受让人放弃其对较大地块的优先购买权，并不放弃公司购买此处争议的较小地块的权利。 （选项 D） 区域的相邻性无关紧要。如果优先购买权有效，那么当卖方收到第三方买方的要约时，它就会成熟为全额（即全部）购买的权利。如果符合 RAP，则总购买权是可执行的。\n\n教育目标：为了遵守反对永续规则，优先购买权必须设定为在永续期限内归属或失效，即在权益产生的相关寿命后的 21 年内。\n\n参考文献\n上午 61 点。尤尔。 2d 永久权和转让限制§ 5 (2020)（反对永久权的规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为了遵守反对永续规则，优先购买权必须设定为在永续期限内归属或失效，即在权益产生的相关寿命后的 21 年内。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院判决女儿胜诉，可能的原因是什么？","rule":"为了遵守反对永续规则，优先购买权必须设定为在永续期限内归属或失效，即在权益产生的相关寿命后的 21 年内。","analysis":"优先购买权是一种合同权利，如果业主后来决定出售，则可以在任何其他人之前购买财产。因此，或有未来权益受到永续规则（RAP）的约束。如果这些权益有可能在权益创建时的相关寿命超过 21 年后归属，那么 RAP 就会使这些权益无效。因此，如果优先购买权没有设定在永久期限内终止——无论是暂时终止（例如，日历日期）还是通过引用存在的生命（例如，受让人死亡后21年内），那么优先购买权几乎总是违反RAP。在这里，公司寻求对女儿较小的土地行使优先购买权。由于产生该权益的契约涉及双方的继承人和受让人，而没有提及终止日期，因此可以在永久期限后行使优先购买权。因此，优先购买权在 RAP 下无效，法院可能会据此作出对女儿有利的裁决。* *由于该司法管辖区遵循普通法 RAP，因此优先购买权是从头开始（即从一开始）就被行使的。但如果这个司法管辖区采取了多数人的“观望”立场，那么该权利自其创建之日起 90 年内就将一直有效。 （选项A）当金钱赔偿不足时，可以采取特定履行。由于土地被认为是独特的，金钱损失无法弥补独特财产的损失，因此有权购买的人通常可以使用这种补救措施。 （选项B）受让人放弃其对较大地块的优先购买权，并不放弃公司购买此处争议的较小地块的权利。 （选项 D） 区域的相邻性无关紧要。如果优先购买权有效，那么当卖方收到第三方买方的要约时，它就会成熟为全额（即全部）购买的权利。如果符合 RAP，则总购买权是可执行的。\n\n教育目标：为了遵守反对永续规则，优先购买权必须设定为在永续期限内归属或失效，即在权益产生的相关寿命后的 21 年内。\n\n参考文献\n上午 61 点。尤尔。 2d 永久权和转让限制§ 5 (2020)（反对永久权的规则）。","conclusion":"答案 C：规定购买权的规定违反了“反对永续权规则”。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-d8b11b789c24ad2e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":143,"sourceQuestionPage":419,"sourceQuestionPages":[419],"sourceExplanationPages":[420,421],"sourceAnswerPages":[420,421],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-144","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Co-ownership","question":"Seven years ago, a man, his sister, and his cousin became equal owners, as tenants in common, of a house. Until a year ago, the man lived in the house alone. The sister and the cousin are longtime residents of another state. One year ago, the man moved to an apartment and rented the house to a tenant for three years under a lease that the man and the tenant both signed. The tenant has since paid the rent each month to the man. Recently, the sister and the cousin learned about the rental. They brought an appropriate action against the tenant to have the lease declared void and to have the tenant evicted. The tenant raised all available defenses. What will the court likely decide?","choices":{"A":"The lease is valid, and the tenant is not evicted but must share possession with the sister and the cousin.","B":"The lease is valid, and the tenant retains exclusive occupancy rights for the balance of the term.","C":"The lease is valid, but the tenant is evicted because one-third of the lease term has expired and the man had only a one-third interest to transfer.","D":"The lease is void, and the tenant is evicted."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Consent between cotenants in common\n\n(common examples)\n\nConsent required Convey entire co-owned property Obtain exclusive possession over part of property Bind other cotenants' interests in contract with third party No consent Sell, lease, or devise personal ownership interest required Possess entire co-owned property Extract minerals from co-owned property (unless committing waste) Tenancy in common is concurrent ownership of property where each co-tenant has a separate share of the property but the right to possess and enjoy the entire property (ie, separate but undivided interests). A tenant in common may lease or otherwise transfer his/her property interest to a third party without the other co-tenants' consent. But the other co-tenants are entitled to: possess and use the entire property with the third party and share expenses and income (eg, rent) generated by the property in proportion to their property interests. Here, the man, sister, and cousin share the house as tenants in common. The man leased the house to a tenant, who then paid rent to the man. And though the sister and cousin only recently learned of this lease, it is valid (not void) because the man did not need their consent to lease his property interest. As a result, the court will likely decide that the tenant is not evicted but must share possession with the sister and cousin (Choices B & D). (Choice C) Although the man (an equal owner) had only a one-third interest in the house, he was entitled to lease his right to possess and enjoy the entire house to the tenant. And since two-thirds of the lease term still remain, the tenant should not be evicted.\n\nEducational objective: A tenant in common has the right to lease (or otherwise transfer) his/her separate, undivided property interest to a third party without the other co-tenants' consent. But the other co-tenants have the right to share possession of the property with the third party and receive a share of the rental income in proportion to their property interests.\n\nReferences\n20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 31 (2018) (defining tenancy in common).","educationalObjective":"A tenant in common has the right to lease (or otherwise transfer) his/her separate, undivided property interest to a third party without the other co-tenants' consent. But the other co-tenants have the right to share possession of the property with the third party and receive a share of the rental income in proportion to their property interests.","references":"20 Am. Jur. 2d Cotenancy and Joint Ownership § 31 (2018) (defining tenancy in common).","rule":"A tenant in common has the right to lease (or otherwise transfer) his/her separate, undivided property interest to a third party without the other co-tenants' consent. But the other co-tenants have the right to share possession of the property with the third party and receive a share of the rental income in proportion to their property interests.","irac":{"issue":"What will the court likely decide?","rule":"A tenant in common has the right to lease (or otherwise transfer) his/her separate, undivided property interest to a third party without the other co-tenants' consent. But the other co-tenants have the right to share possession of the property with the third party and receive a share of the rental income in proportion to their property interests.","analysis":"Consent between cotenants in common\n\n(common examples)\n\nConsent required Convey entire co-owned property Obtain exclusive possession over part of property Bind other cotenants' interests in contract with third party No consent Sell, lease, or devise personal ownership interest required Possess entire co-owned property Extract minerals from co-owned property (unless committing waste) Tenancy in common is concurrent ownership of property where each co-tenant has a separate share of the property but the right to possess and enjoy the entire property (ie, separate but undivided interests). A tenant in common may lease or otherwise transfer his/her property interest to a third party without the other co-tenants' consent. But the other co-tenants are entitled to: possess and use the entire property with the third party and share expenses and income (eg, rent) generated by the property in proportion to their property interests. Here, the man, sister, and cousin share the house as tenants in common. The man leased the house to a tenant, who then paid rent to the man. And though the sister and cousin only recently learned of this lease, it is valid (not void) because the man did not need their consent to lease his property interest. As a result, the court will likely decide that the tenant is not evicted but must share possession with the sister and cousin (Choices B & D). (Choice C) Although the man (an equal owner) had only a one-third interest in the house, he was entitled to lease his right to possess and enjoy the entire house to the tenant. And since two-thirds of the lease term still remain, the tenant should not be evicted.","conclusion":"Answer A: The lease is valid, and the tenant is not evicted but must share possession with the sister and the cousin."},"questionZh":"七年前，一个男人、他的妹妹和他的表弟成为一所房子的平等所有者，作为按份共有人（tenant in common）。直到一年前，该男子还独自住在这所房子里。姐姐和表弟是另一个州的长期居民。一年前，该男子搬进了一套公寓，并根据该男子和租客双方签署的租约将房子租给了一名租客，为期三年。此后，租客每月向该男子支付租金。最近，姐姐和表弟得知了租房的消息。他们对租户采取了适当的行动，宣布租约无效并驱逐租户。房客提出了所有可用的辩护。法院可能会做出什么裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"租约有效，租户不会被驱逐，但必须与姐姐和表弟分享财产。","B":"租约有效，租户在余下的期限内保留独家占用权。","C":"租约有效，但租户被驱逐，因为三分之一的租期已到期，而该男子只有三分之一的权益可供转让。","D":"租约无效，租客被驱逐。"},"explanationZh":"共同参与者之间的同意\n\n（常见例子）\n\n需要同意 转让全部共有财产 获得对部分财产的排他性占有 通过与第三方签订的合同约束其他合租人的利益 不同意 需要出售、租赁或设计个人所有权权益 拥有全部共有财产 从共有财产中提取矿物（除非产生废物） 共同共有（joint tenancy）是财产的共同所有权，其中每个共同承租人拥有单独的财产份额，但有权占有和享有整个财产（即单独但不可分割的权益）。按份共有人（tenant in common）可以在未经其他按份共有人（tenant in common）同意的情况下，将其财产权益出租或以其他方式转让给第三方。但其他按份共有人（tenant in common）有权：与第三方共同占有和使用全部财产，并按照其财产权益的比例分享该财产产生的费用和收入（例如租金）。在这里，男人、姐姐和表弟作为按份共有人（tenant in common）共享这所房子。该男子将房子租给一名租客，然后租客向该男子支付租金。尽管姐姐和表弟最近才知道这份租约，但它是有效的（不是无效的），因为该男子不需要他们的同意就可以出租他的财产权益。因此，法院可能会裁定租户不被驱逐，但必须与姐姐和表弟分享财产（Choices B & D）。 （选项C）虽然男方（平等的业主）只拥有房屋的三分之一权益，但他有权将其占有和享用整栋房屋的权利出租给房客。由于租赁期限仍有三分之二，因此不应驱逐租户。\n\n教育目标：按份共有人（tenant in common）有权在未经其他按份共有人（tenant in common）同意的情况下将其单独的、不可分割的财产权益出租（或以其他方式转让）给第三方。但其他按份共有人（tenant in common）有权与第三方分享财产的占有，并按照其财产权益的比例分享租金收入。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 31 (2018)（定义共同共有（joint tenancy））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"按份共有人（tenant in common）有权将其单独的、不可分割的财产权益出租（或以其他方式转让）给第三方，而无需其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的同意。但其他按份共有人（tenant in common）有权与第三方分享财产的占有，并按照其财产权益的比例分享租金收入。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会做出什么裁决？","rule":"按份共有人（tenant in common）有权将其单独的、不可分割的财产权益出租（或以其他方式转让）给第三方，而无需其他按份共有人（tenant in common）的同意。但其他按份共有人（tenant in common）有权与第三方分享财产的占有，并按照其财产权益的比例分享租金收入。","analysis":"共同参与者之间的同意\n\n（常见例子）\n\n需要同意 转让全部共有财产 获得对部分财产的排他性占有 通过与第三方签订的合同约束其他合租人的利益 不同意 需要出售、租赁或设计个人所有权权益 拥有全部共有财产 从共有财产中提取矿物（除非产生废物） 共同共有（joint tenancy）是财产的共同所有权，其中每个共同承租人拥有单独的财产份额，但有权占有和享有整个财产（即单独但不可分割的权益）。按份共有人（tenant in common）可以在未经其他按份共有人（tenant in common）同意的情况下，将其财产权益出租或以其他方式转让给第三方。但其他按份共有人（tenant in common）有权：与第三方共同占有和使用全部财产，并按照其财产权益的比例分享该财产产生的费用和收入（例如租金）。在这里，男人、姐姐和表弟作为按份共有人（tenant in common）共享这所房子。该男子将房子租给一名租客，然后租客向该男子支付租金。尽管姐姐和表弟最近才知道这份租约，但它是有效的（不是无效的），因为该男子不需要他们的同意就可以出租他的财产权益。因此，法院可能会裁定租户不被驱逐，但必须与姐姐和表弟分享财产（Choices B & D）。 （选项C）虽然男方（平等的业主）只拥有房屋的三分之一权益，但他有权将其占有和享用整栋房屋的权利出租给房客。由于租赁期限仍有三分之二，因此不应驱逐租户。\n\n教育目标：按份共有人（tenant in common）有权在未经其他按份共有人（tenant in common）同意的情况下将其单独的、不可分割的财产权益出租（或以其他方式转让）给第三方。但其他按份共有人（tenant in common）有权与第三方分享财产的占有，并按照其财产权益的比例分享租金收入。\n\n参考文献\n上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 共同共有（joint tenancy）和共同所有权 § 31 (2018)（定义共同共有（joint tenancy））。","conclusion":"答案 A：租约有效，租户不会被驱逐，但必须与姐姐和表弟分享财产。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":144,"sourceQuestionPage":422,"sourceQuestionPages":[422],"sourceExplanationPages":[423,424],"sourceAnswerPages":[423,424],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-145","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A pastor owned a vacant one-acre tract of land. Five years ago, he executed a deed conveying the land to \"my church for the purpose of erecting a church building thereon.\" Three years ago, the pastor died, leaving his son as his sole heir at law. His duly probated will left \"all my Estate, both real and personal, to my friend.\" The church never constructed a church building on the land, and last month the church, for a valid consideration, conveyed the land to a real estate developer. The real estate developer brought an appropriate action to quiet title against the son, the friend, and the church, and joined the appropriate state official. Such official asserted that a charitable trust was created that has not terminated. In such action, who should the court find for?","choices":{"A":"The friend.","B":"The real estate developer.","C":"The son.","D":"The state official."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nLike fee simple absolutes (FSAs) and other freehold estates,* defeasible fees are ownership interests in land that last for an indeterminate duration. But unlike FSAs, defeasible fees may be terminated by the happening of a stated event. Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language (eg, \"so long as,\" \"but if\"). However, language that limits only the purpose of the transfer (eg, \"for the purpose of\") creates an FSA. Here, the pastor conveyed the land \"to my church for the purpose of erecting a church building thereon.\" This conveyance created an FSA—not a defeasible fee—so the fact that the church never constructed a church building on the land did not terminate its ownership interest in the land. Therefore, the church effectively conveyed the land to the real estate developer, and the real estate developer should win the action. *To be categorized as a freehold (eg, life estates, estates in fee), an estate must be immobile (eg, land, fixtures) AND last for an indeterminate duration (eg, for the life of X). In contrast, a leasehold is an immobile estate that lasts for a limited duration (eg, 15 years). (Choices A & C) Had the church's ownership interest in the land terminated and reverted back to the pastor, the friend—not the son—would own the land because the pastor's will left his entire estate to the friend. (Choice D) A trust is an arrangement in which a trustee holds property at the request of another (settlor) for the benefit of a third party (beneficiary). A charitable trust is one created for a charitable purpose, and it is enforceable by the appropriate state official (ie,\n\nthe attorney general). But here, no trust was created since the church is the beneficiary and the holder of the property (no trustee).\n\nEducational objective: Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language. Language that limits only the purpose of the transfer instead creates a fee simple absolute.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 44 cmt. m (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing designated purpose of conveyance).","educationalObjective":"Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language. Language that limits only the purpose of the transfer instead creates a fee simple absolute.","references":"Restatement of Property § 44 cmt. m (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing designated purpose of conveyance).","rule":"Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language. Language that limits only the purpose of the transfer instead creates a fee simple absolute.","irac":{"issue":"In such action, who should the court find for?","rule":"Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language. Language that limits only the purpose of the transfer instead creates a fee simple absolute.","analysis":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nLike fee simple absolutes (FSAs) and other freehold estates,* defeasible fees are ownership interests in land that last for an indeterminate duration. But unlike FSAs, defeasible fees may be terminated by the happening of a stated event. Defeasible fees are limited by specific durational or conditional language (eg, \"so long as,\" \"but if\"). However, language that limits only the purpose of the transfer (eg, \"for the purpose of\") creates an FSA. Here, the pastor conveyed the land \"to my church for the purpose of erecting a church building thereon.\" This conveyance created an FSA—not a defeasible fee—so the fact that the church never constructed a church building on the land did not terminate its ownership interest in the land. Therefore, the church effectively conveyed the land to the real estate developer, and the real estate developer should win the action. *To be categorized as a freehold (eg, life estates, estates in fee), an estate must be immobile (eg, land, fixtures) AND last for an indeterminate duration (eg, for the life of X). In contrast, a leasehold is an immobile estate that lasts for a limited duration (eg, 15 years). (Choices A & C) Had the church's ownership interest in the land terminated and reverted back to the pastor, the friend—not the son—would own the land because the pastor's will left his entire estate to the friend. (Choice D) A trust is an arrangement in which a trustee holds property at the request of another (settlor) for the benefit of a third party (beneficiary). A charitable trust is one created for a charitable purpose, and it is enforceable by the appropriate state official (ie,\n\nthe attorney general). But here, no trust was created since the church is the beneficiary and the holder of the property (no trustee).","conclusion":"Answer B: The real estate developer."},"questionZh":"一位牧师拥有一块一英亩的闲置土地。五年前，他签署了一份契约，将土地转让给“我的教堂，以便在上面建造一座教堂建筑”。三年前，牧师去世，他的儿子成为他唯一的法定继承人。他经过正式认证的遗嘱将“我所有的财产，无论是真实的还是个人的，都留给了我的朋友”。该教会从未在该土地上建造教堂建筑，上个月，出于合理考虑，该教会将这块土地转让给了一家房地产开发商。房地产开发商提起了适当的诉讼，以平息儿子、朋友和教会的所有权，并加入了适当的州官员的行列。该官员声称，慈善信托已经设立，但尚未终止。在这样的诉讼中，法院应该找谁呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"朋友。","B":"房地产开发商。","C":"儿子。","D":"国家官员。"},"explanationZh":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n与简单绝对费用 (FSA) 和其他永久业权不动产一样，*可废止费用是持续不确定期限的土地所有权权益。但与 FSA 不同的是，可废止的费用可能会因规定事件的发生而终止。可废止的费用受到特定期限或条件语言（例如“只要”、“但是如果”）的限制。然而，仅限制转让目的的语言（例如“为了……的目的”）会创建 FSA。在这里，牧师将这块土地“转让给我的教堂，以便在上面建造一座教堂”。这种转让产生了 FSA，而不是可废止的费用，因此教会从未在该土地上建造教堂的事实并没有终止其对该土地的所有权权益。因此，教会有效地将土地转让给了房地产开发商，房地产开发商应该胜诉。 *要归类为永久业权（例如，终生不动产、收费不动产），不动产必须是不可移动的（例如，土地、固定装置）并且持续时间不确定（例如，在 X 的生命周期内）。相比之下，租赁权是一种不可移动的不动产，其持续时间有限（例如 15 年）。 （选项 A 和 C）如果教会在土地上的所有权权益终止并归还给牧师，那么朋友（而不是儿子）将拥有土地，因为牧师的遗嘱将他的全部财产留给了朋友。 （选项 D） 信托是一种安排，其中受托人应另一位（委托人）的要求为第三方（受益人）的利益持有财产。慈善信托是为了慈善目的而设立的信托，由适当的国家官员（即，\n\n总检察长）。但在这里，没有建立信托，因为教会是受益人和财产持有人（无受托人）。\n\n教育目标：可废止的费用受到特定持续时间或条件语言的限制。仅限制转账目的的语言反而会产生简单的绝对费用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述§ 44 cmt。 m（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论指定的运输目的）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"可废止的费用受到特定期限或条件语言的限制。仅限制转账目的的语言反而会产生简单的绝对费用。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这样的诉讼中，法院应该找谁呢？","rule":"可废止的费用受到特定期限或条件语言的限制。仅限制转账目的的语言反而会产生简单的绝对费用。","analysis":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n与简单绝对费用 (FSA) 和其他永久业权不动产一样，*可废止费用是持续不确定期限的土地所有权权益。但与 FSA 不同的是，可废止的费用可能会因规定事件的发生而终止。可废止的费用受到特定期限或条件语言（例如“只要”、“但是如果”）的限制。然而，仅限制转让目的的语言（例如“为了……的目的”）会创建 FSA。在这里，牧师将这块土地“转让给我的教堂，以便在上面建造一座教堂”。这种转让产生了 FSA，而不是可废止的费用，因此教会从未在该土地上建造教堂的事实并没有终止其对该土地的所有权权益。因此，教会有效地将土地转让给了房地产开发商，房地产开发商应该胜诉。 *要归类为永久业权（例如，终生不动产、收费不动产），不动产必须是不可移动的（例如，土地、固定装置）并且持续时间不确定（例如，在 X 的生命周期内）。相比之下，租赁权是一种不可移动的不动产，其持续时间有限（例如 15 年）。 （选项 A 和 C）如果教会在土地上的所有权权益终止并归还给牧师，那么朋友（而不是儿子）将拥有土地，因为牧师的遗嘱将他的全部财产留给了朋友。 （选项 D） 信托是一种安排，其中受托人应另一位（委托人）的要求为第三方（受益人）的利益持有财产。慈善信托是为了慈善目的而设立的信托，由适当的国家官员（即，\n\n总检察长）。但在这里，没有建立信托，因为教会是受益人和财产持有人（无受托人）。\n\n教育目标：可废止的费用受到特定持续时间或条件语言的限制。仅限制转账目的的语言反而会产生简单的绝对费用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述§ 44 cmt。 m（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（讨论指定的运输目的）。","conclusion":"答案 B：房地产开发商。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"fixture","ipa":"/fˈɪkstʃɝ/","syllables":"fix·ture","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 固定(状态), 固定物, 设备；[计] 夹具","contextualMeaning":"附着物：原为动产但因附着和客观意图而成为不动产的一部分","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:fixtures/i:fixturing","sourceSentence":"*To be categorized as a freehold (eg, life estates, estates in fee), an estate must be immobile (eg, land, fixtures) AND last for an indeterminate duration (eg, for the life of X).","synonyms":["annexed chattel"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":145,"sourceQuestionPage":425,"sourceQuestionPages":[425],"sourceExplanationPages":[426,427],"sourceAnswerPages":[426,427],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-146","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A woman died and in her duly probated will devised a tract of land to her son \"for life\" and then to \"the heirs of his body in fee simple absolute.\" The son used the tract to secure a loan from a bank. When the son defaulted on the debt, the bank initiated foreclosure proceedings. A buyer acquired the tract at the foreclosure sale and promptly sold the tract to a friend. Both the buyer's deed and the friend's deed referenced the woman's will for the description of the parcel, and both deeds were promptly recorded. Twenty years later, the son died intestate, leaving two children as his only heirs. The children have sued the friend, claiming ownership of the land under a 30-year marketable title act. If common law rules have not been modified in the jurisdiction, who will likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"The children, because the friend acquired the son's life estate, which terminated when the son died.","B":"The children, because the friend's deed referenced the woman's will, which served as a notice of claim under the marketable title act.","C":"The friend, because the children failed to record a notice of claim.","D":"The friend, because the friend acquired the son's interest in fee simple absolute."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under the common law, the Rule in Shelley's Case applies when an instrument grants: a freehold estate (eg, life estate) to a person and a remainder to the same person's heirs or the heirs of that person's body. When this occurs, that person is considered to hold the freehold estate AND the remainder, so the person's heirs take nothing under the granting instrument. And under the doctrine of merger, if there is no intervening interest, the freehold estate and the remainder merge so that the person takes in fee simple absolute (FSA). Here, the woman's will devised a life estate to the son and the remainder to the heirs of his body. The son is therefore considered to hold the life estate and the remainder. Since there is no intervening interest (eg, had another life estate followed the son's life estate), the son's interests merged into an FSA that the buyer acquired at the foreclosure sale and subsequently conveyed to the friend. Therefore, the friend will likely prevail. Had this jurisdiction abolished the Rule in Shelley's Case (as most others have done), the parties would have taken the freehold estate and remainder in accordance with the language in the deed. This means that the son would have received a life estate— subsequently acquired by the buyer and then the friend—that would have terminated upon the son's death. And the children would have received the remainder and therefore prevailed (Choice A). (Choices B & C) Marketable title acts extinguish any title defects that have not been recorded within the statutory period (here, 30 years). Since the buyer's and friend's recorded deeds referenced the granting language from the woman's will, this likely satisfied the act's recording requirement. However, the children still have no claim to enforce.\n\nEducational objective: Under the Rule in Shelley's Case, an instrument that grants a freehold estate to a person and a remainder to that same person's heirs gives that person both interests. If there is no intervening interest, then the merger rule consolidates those interests into a fee simple absolute.","educationalObjective":"Under the Rule in Shelley's Case, an instrument that grants a freehold estate to a person and a remainder to that same person's heirs gives that person both interests. If there is no intervening interest, then the merger rule consolidates those interests into a fee simple absolute.","references":"","rule":"Under the Rule in Shelley's Case, an instrument that grants a freehold estate to a person and a remainder to that same person's heirs gives that person both interests. If there is no intervening interest, then the merger rule consolidates those interests into a fee simple absolute.","irac":{"issue":"If common law rules have not been modified in the jurisdiction, who will likely prevail?","rule":"Under the Rule in Shelley's Case, an instrument that grants a freehold estate to a person and a remainder to that same person's heirs gives that person both interests. If there is no intervening interest, then the merger rule consolidates those interests into a fee simple absolute.","analysis":"Under the common law, the Rule in Shelley's Case applies when an instrument grants: a freehold estate (eg, life estate) to a person and a remainder to the same person's heirs or the heirs of that person's body. When this occurs, that person is considered to hold the freehold estate AND the remainder, so the person's heirs take nothing under the granting instrument. And under the doctrine of merger, if there is no intervening interest, the freehold estate and the remainder merge so that the person takes in fee simple absolute (FSA). Here, the woman's will devised a life estate to the son and the remainder to the heirs of his body. The son is therefore considered to hold the life estate and the remainder. Since there is no intervening interest (eg, had another life estate followed the son's life estate), the son's interests merged into an FSA that the buyer acquired at the foreclosure sale and subsequently conveyed to the friend. Therefore, the friend will likely prevail. Had this jurisdiction abolished the Rule in Shelley's Case (as most others have done), the parties would have taken the freehold estate and remainder in accordance with the language in the deed. This means that the son would have received a life estate— subsequently acquired by the buyer and then the friend—that would have terminated upon the son's death. And the children would have received the remainder and therefore prevailed (Choice A). (Choices B & C) Marketable title acts extinguish any title defects that have not been recorded within the statutory period (here, 30 years). Since the buyer's and friend's recorded deeds referenced the granting language from the woman's will, this likely satisfied the act's recording requirement. However, the children still have no claim to enforce.","conclusion":"Answer D: The friend, because the friend acquired the son's interest in fee simple absolute."},"questionZh":"一名妇女去世后，在她经过正式遗嘱认证的遗嘱中，为她的儿子“终身”设计了一块土地，然后“作为他身体的继承人”。儿子用这张单张从银行获得了贷款。当儿子拖欠债务时，银行启动了止赎程序。一位买家在止赎拍卖中获得了这块土地，并立即将其卖给了一位朋友。买家的房产契和朋友的房产契都参考了该女子的遗嘱来描述包裹，并且两份房产契都被及时记录下来。二十年后，儿子去世时没有留下遗嘱，留下两个孩子作为他唯一的继承人。孩子们起诉了这位朋友，声称根据 30 年可出售产权法拥有这片土地。如果普通法规则在管辖范围内没有修改，谁可能会占上风？","choicesZh":{"A":"孩子们，因为朋友获得了儿子的终身财产，该财产在儿子去世后终止。","B":"孩子们，因为朋友的契约引用了女人的遗嘱，该遗嘱作为可出售所有权法下的索赔通知。","C":"这位朋友，因为孩子们没有记录索赔通知。","D":"朋友，因为朋友在费简单绝对取得了儿子的兴趣。"},"explanationZh":"根据普通法，雪莱案中的规则适用于以下情况：文书授予某人永久业权的财产（例如终身财产），并将剩余财产授予同一人的继承人或该人身体的继承人。当这种情况发生时，该人被视为持有永久业权财产和其余财产，因此该人的继承人不会根据授予文书获取任何东西。根据合并原则，如果没有介入利益，永久业权财产和剩余财产将合并，以便该人获得简单绝对费用（FSA）。在这里，女人的遗嘱将终身财产划给了儿子，其余的则划给了他身体的继承人。因此，儿子被认为拥有终身财产和剩余财产。由于不存在干预权益（例如，儿子的终身财产之后还有另一项终身财产），儿子的权益合并到买方在取消抵押品赎回权出售时获得的 FSA 中，并随后转让给朋友。因此，朋友很可能会占上风。如果该司法管辖区废除了雪莱案中的规则（正如大多数其他案件所做的那样），双方将根据契约中的语言取得永久业权财产和剩余财产。这意味着儿子将获得终身遗产——随后由买家和朋友获得——该遗产将在儿子去世时终止。孩子们会收到剩余的钱，因此获胜（选择 A）。 （选择 B 和 C）可出售的所有权行为消除了在法定期限（此处为 30 年）内未记录的任何所有权缺陷。由于买方和朋友的记录行为引用了妇女遗嘱中的授予语言，这可能满足了该法案的记录要求。然而，孩子们仍然没有权利要求执行。\n\n教育目标：根据雪莱案的规则，将永久业权财产授予某人并将剩余财产授予同一人的继承人的文书赋予该人两种利益。如果没有干预利益，那么合并规则会将这些利益合并为简单绝对利益。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据雪莱案的规则，将永久业权财产授予某人并将剩余财产授予同一人的继承人的文书赋予该人两种权益。如果没有干预利益，那么合并规则会将这些利益合并为简单绝对利益。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果普通法规则在管辖范围内没有修改，谁可能会占上风？","rule":"根据雪莱案的规则，将永久业权财产授予某人并将剩余财产授予同一人的继承人的文书赋予该人两种权益。如果没有干预利益，那么合并规则会将这些利益合并为简单绝对利益。","analysis":"根据普通法，雪莱案中的规则适用于以下情况：文书授予某人永久业权的财产（例如终身财产），并将剩余财产授予同一人的继承人或该人身体的继承人。当这种情况发生时，该人被视为持有永久业权财产和其余财产，因此该人的继承人不会根据授予文书获取任何东西。根据合并原则，如果没有介入利益，永久业权财产和剩余财产将合并，以便该人获得简单绝对费用（FSA）。在这里，女人的遗嘱将终身财产划给了儿子，其余的则划给了他身体的继承人。因此，儿子被认为拥有终身财产和剩余财产。由于不存在干预权益（例如，儿子的终身财产之后还有另一项终身财产），儿子的权益合并到买方在取消抵押品赎回权出售时获得的 FSA 中，并随后转让给朋友。因此，朋友很可能会占上风。如果该司法管辖区废除了雪莱案中的规则（正如大多数其他案件所做的那样），双方将根据契约中的语言取得永久业权财产和剩余财产。这意味着儿子将获得终身遗产——随后由买家和朋友获得——该遗产将在儿子去世时终止。孩子们会收到剩余的钱，因此获胜（选择 A）。 （选择 B 和 C）可出售的所有权行为消除了在法定期限（此处为 30 年）内未记录的任何所有权缺陷。由于买方和朋友的记录行为引用了妇女遗嘱中的授予语言，这可能满足了该法案的记录要求。然而，孩子们仍然没有权利要求执行。\n\n教育目标：根据雪莱案的规则，将永久业权财产授予某人并将剩余财产授予同一人的继承人的文书赋予该人两种利益。如果没有干预利益，那么合并规则会将这些利益合并为简单绝对利益。","conclusion":"答案 D：朋友，因为朋友在费简单绝对取得了儿子的兴趣。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"shelley's case","ipa":"/ʃˈɛliz kˈeɪs/","syllables":"shel·ley's case","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"谢利案规则：符合条件时将受让人的终身地产与其继承人的剩余权合并","contextualMeaning":"谢利案规则：符合条件时将受让人的终身地产与其继承人的剩余权合并","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Under the Rule in Shelley's Case, an instrument that grants a freehold estate to a person and a remainder to that same person's heirs gives that person both interests.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-5bb7af78642c910d"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":146,"sourceQuestionPage":428,"sourceQuestionPages":[428],"sourceExplanationPages":[429,430],"sourceAnswerPages":[429,430],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-147","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A father owned property in fee simple. The small house on the property was occupied, with the father's oral permission, rent-free by his son and the son's college classmate. The son was then 21 years old. The father, by properly executed instrument, conveyed the property to \"my beloved son, his heirs and assigns, upon the condition precedent that he earn a college degree by the time he reaches the age of 30. If, for any reason, he does not meet this condition, then the property shall become the sole property of my beloved daughter, her heirs and assigns.\" At the time of the conveyance, the son and his classmate attended a college located several blocks from the property. Neither had earned a college degree. One week after the delivery of the deed to the son, he recorded the deed and immediately told the classmate that he was going to begin charging the classmate rent since \"I am now your landlord.\" There is no applicable statute. The son and the classmate did not reach agreement, and the son served the appropriate notice to terminate whatever tenancy the classmate had. The son then sought, in an appropriate action, to oust the classmate. Who should prevail?","choices":{"A":"The classmate, because he is a tenant of the father, not of the son.","B":"The classmate, because the father's permission to occupy preceded the father's conveyance to the son.","C":"The son, because the conveyance created a fee simple subject to divestment in the son.","D":"The son, because the father's conveyance terminated the classmate's tenancy."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A tenancy at will—a tenancy with no fixed term that can be created by express agreement or by implication if the tenant occupies the premises rent-free—generally continues until terminated by either the landlord or the tenant. A tenancy at will terminates automatically when the landlord conveys a present interest in the property to a third party. But when the landlord conveys a future interest, the tenancy continues. In that case, the future interest holder has no right to terminate the tenancy. Here, the son and the classmate lived in the father's house rent-free (tenancy at will). The father then conveyed a property interest in the home to the son that could become possessory if he earned a college degree before age 30 (future interest). Therefore, that conveyance did not automatically terminate the classmate's tenancy, so the classmate was still the father's tenant (Choice D). And since the son (future interest holder) had no right to terminate that tenancy, the classmate should prevail in the son's action to oust the classmate. (Choice B) Although the father's permission to occupy the house preceded the conveyance, the son would prevail in his ouster action if the father had conveyed a present interest to the son (since that would have terminated the classmate's tenancy automatically). Therefore, the classmate should not prevail on this basis. (Choice C) After the conveyance, the father had a fee simple subject to divestment—a present possessory interest that could be cut short upon the happening of a stated condition (ie, if the son graduated college before age 30). The son only had a future interest that could become possessory if that condition occurred.\n\nEducational objective: A tenancy at will can be terminated (1) by the landlord or the tenant or (2) automatically when the landlord conveys a present interest in the property to a third party. But when the landlord conveys a future interest, the tenancy continues, and the future interest holder has no right to terminate that tenancy.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 1.6 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (tenancy at will).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 15.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (termination of tenancy at will upon landlord's conveyance).","educationalObjective":"A tenancy at will can be terminated (1) by the landlord or the tenant or (2) automatically when the landlord conveys a present interest in the property to a third party. But when the landlord conveys a future interest, the tenancy continues, and the future interest holder has no right to terminate that tenancy.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 1.6 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (tenancy at will).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 15.1 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (termination of tenancy at will upon landlord's conveyance).","rule":"A tenancy at will can be terminated (1) by the landlord or the tenant or (2) automatically when the landlord conveys a present interest in the property to a third party. But when the landlord conveys a future interest, the tenancy continues, and the future interest holder has no right to terminate that tenancy.","irac":{"issue":"Who should prevail?","rule":"A tenancy at will can be terminated (1) by the landlord or the tenant or (2) automatically when the landlord conveys a present interest in the property to a third party. But when the landlord conveys a future interest, the tenancy continues, and the future interest holder has no right to terminate that tenancy.","analysis":"A tenancy at will—a tenancy with no fixed term that can be created by express agreement or by implication if the tenant occupies the premises rent-free—generally continues until terminated by either the landlord or the tenant. A tenancy at will terminates automatically when the landlord conveys a present interest in the property to a third party. But when the landlord conveys a future interest, the tenancy continues. In that case, the future interest holder has no right to terminate the tenancy. Here, the son and the classmate lived in the father's house rent-free (tenancy at will). The father then conveyed a property interest in the home to the son that could become possessory if he earned a college degree before age 30 (future interest). Therefore, that conveyance did not automatically terminate the classmate's tenancy, so the classmate was still the father's tenant (Choice D). And since the son (future interest holder) had no right to terminate that tenancy, the classmate should prevail in the son's action to oust the classmate. (Choice B) Although the father's permission to occupy the house preceded the conveyance, the son would prevail in his ouster action if the father had conveyed a present interest to the son (since that would have terminated the classmate's tenancy automatically). Therefore, the classmate should not prevail on this basis. (Choice C) After the conveyance, the father had a fee simple subject to divestment—a present possessory interest that could be cut short upon the happening of a stated condition (ie, if the son graduated college before age 30). The son only had a future interest that could become possessory if that condition occurred.","conclusion":"Answer A: The classmate, because he is a tenant of the father, not of the son."},"questionZh":"父亲拥有简单的财产。经父亲口头许可，该房产上的小房子已被儿子和儿子的大学同学免租。当时儿子21岁。父亲通过适当签署的文书，将财产转让给“我心爱的儿子、他的继承人和受让人，条件是他在30岁之前获得大学学位。如果出于任何原因，他不满足这一条件，那么该财产将成为我心爱的女儿、她的继承人和受让人的唯一财产。”在转让时，儿子和他的同学就读于距离房产几个街区的一所大学。两人都没有获得大学学位。在将房产证交给儿子一周后，他记录了房产证，并立即告诉同学，他要开始向同学收取租金，因为“我现在是你的房东”。没有适用的法规。儿子和同学未能达成协议，儿子发出了适当的通知，终止了同学所拥有的任何租约。然后，儿子试图采取适当的行动，驱逐这位同学。谁应该占上风？","choicesZh":{"A":"同学，因为他是父亲的租客，而不是儿子的租客。","B":"同学，因为父亲允许占用先于父亲转让给儿子。","C":"儿子，因为产权转让产生了费用，因此可以在儿子中撤资。","D":"儿子，因为父亲的房产转让终止了同学的租约。"},"explanationZh":"随意租赁——没有固定期限的租赁，可以通过明确协议或暗示（如果租户免租占用该房屋）来建立——通常会持续到房东或租户终止为止。当房东将财产的现有权益转让给第三方时，随意租赁自动终止。但当房东表达出未来的兴趣时，租赁就会继续。在这种情况下，未来的利益持有人无权终止租赁。在这里，儿子和同学免租（租赁随意）住在父亲的房子里。然后，父亲将房屋的财产权益转让给儿子，如果儿子在 30 岁之前获得大学学位，该财产权益就可以成为财产权益（未来权益）。因此，该产权转让并没有自动终止该同学的租约，因此该同学仍然是父亲的租客（选项D）。由于儿子（未来的利益持有人）无权终止该租约，因此，在儿子驱逐同学的诉讼中，同学应占上风。 （选择B）尽管父亲在转让之前就允许占用该房屋，但如果父亲向儿子转让了当前的权益（因为这将自动终止同学的租约），则儿子在驱逐诉讼中将占上风。所以，同学不要以此为上。 （选择C）转让后，父亲有一笔可撤资的费用——一种现有的占有权益，在发生规定条件时（即，如果儿子在30岁之前大学毕业），可以缩短该权益。儿子只有在这种情况发生时才可以成为占有人的未来权益。\n\n教育目标： (1) 房东或租户可以随意终止租赁，或 (2) 当房东将财产的现有权益转让给第三方时自动终止。但是，当房东转让未来权益时，租赁继续，未来权益持有人无权终止该租赁。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户 § 1.6（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（随意租赁）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 15.1 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（房东转让时随意终止租赁）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"租赁可以（1）由房东或租客随意终止，或者（2）当房东将财产的现有权益转让给第三方时自动终止。但是，当房东转让未来权益时，租赁继续，未来权益持有人无权终止该租赁。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁应该占上风？","rule":"租赁可以（1）由房东或租客随意终止，或者（2）当房东将财产的现有权益转让给第三方时自动终止。但是，当房东转让未来权益时，租赁继续，未来权益持有人无权终止该租赁。","analysis":"随意租赁——没有固定期限的租赁，可以通过明确协议或暗示（如果租户免租占用该房屋）来建立——通常会持续到房东或租户终止为止。当房东将财产的现有权益转让给第三方时，随意租赁自动终止。但当房东表达出未来的兴趣时，租赁就会继续。在这种情况下，未来的利益持有人无权终止租赁。在这里，儿子和同学免租（租赁随意）住在父亲的房子里。然后，父亲将房屋的财产权益转让给儿子，如果儿子在 30 岁之前获得大学学位，该财产权益就可以成为财产权益（未来权益）。因此，该产权转让并没有自动终止该同学的租约，因此该同学仍然是父亲的租客（选项D）。由于儿子（未来的利益持有人）无权终止该租约，因此，在儿子驱逐同学的诉讼中，同学应占上风。 （选择B）尽管父亲在转让之前就允许占用该房屋，但如果父亲向儿子转让了当前的权益（因为这将自动终止同学的租约），则儿子在驱逐诉讼中将占上风。所以，同学不要以此为上。 （选择C）转让后，父亲有一笔可撤资的费用——一种现有的占有权益，在发生规定条件时（即，如果儿子在30岁之前大学毕业），可以缩短该权益。儿子只有在这种情况发生时才可以成为占有人的未来权益。\n\n教育目标： (1) 房东或租户可以随意终止租赁，或 (2) 当房东将财产的现有权益转让给第三方时自动终止。但是，当房东转让未来权益时，租赁继续，未来权益持有人无权终止该租赁。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户 § 1.6（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（随意租赁）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 15.1 条（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（房东转让时随意终止租赁）。","conclusion":"答案 A：同学，因为他是父亲的租客，而不是儿子的租客。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"tenancy at will","ipa":"/tˈɛnənsi ˈæt wˈɪl/","syllables":"ten·ancy at will","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"任意租赁：无固定期限、原则上可由任一方终止的租赁","contextualMeaning":"任意租赁：无固定期限、原则上可由任一方终止的租赁","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A tenancy at will can be terminated (1) by the landlord or the tenant or (2) automatically when the landlord conveys a present interest in the property to a third party.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-6afb1e50020340f3"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":147,"sourceQuestionPage":431,"sourceQuestionPages":[431],"sourceExplanationPages":[432,433],"sourceAnswerPages":[432,433],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-148","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"By deed, a landowner conveyed land to a charity \"on the condition that the land be used as a wildlife refuge, and if not, the landowner and his heirs may enter and terminate the estate hereby created.\" The following year the landowner died. His sister was his sole heir. By will, the landowner devised \"all of my real property interests to my friend.\" One year after the landowner's death, the sister, by quitclaim deed, conveyed any interest she had in the land to a developer. Two years after the landowner's death, the landowner's sister died. Her sole heir was her husband. Recently, three years after the landowner's death, the charity ceased to use the land as a wildlife refuge. Who may properly bring an action against the charity to recover fee simple ownership of the land?","choices":{"A":"The developer.","B":"The landowner's friend.","C":"The sister's husband.","D":"No one."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A fee simple subject to a condition subsequent (FSSCS) is created through specific conditional language, such as \"but if\" or \"on the condition that.\" Upon the occurrence of the condition, the grantor (or his/her successor in interest) has the right to enter and terminate the estate if the grantor explicitly retained this right of entry* in the conveyance. In most jurisdictions, the right of entry is freely alienable during life and fully transferable upon death—ie, devisable by will, or if not devised, descendible through intestate succession. Here, the landowner conveyed an FSSCS to the charity and retained a right of entry that could be exercised if the land was no longer used as a wildlife refuge. The landowner then devised all of his real property interests—including the right of entry—to his friend. And since the charity recently ceased to use the land as a wildlife refuge, the friend can exercise the right of entry and bring an action to recover ownership of the land (Choice D). *This right is also known as the \"power of termination\" or the \"right of reentry.\" (Choices A & C) The landowner devised all of his real property interests to the friend, leaving no interest in the land for the sister to inherit upon his death. Because the sister had no interest in the land to convey to the developer by quitclaim deed or for her husband to inherit upon her death, neither of them may bring an action to recover ownership of the land from the charity.\n\nEducational objective: The grantor of a fee simple subject to a condition subsequent can explicitly retain a right of entry, which allows the grantor (or his/her successor in interest) to enter and terminate the estate if the stated condition occurs. This right is freely alienable during life, and it is devisable by will or descendible through intestate succession upon death.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (First) of Property § 45 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (fee simple subject to a condition subsequent).\n\nRestatement (First) of Property § 155 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (power of termination or right of entry).","educationalObjective":"The grantor of a fee simple subject to a condition subsequent can explicitly retain a right of entry, which allows the grantor (or his/her successor in interest) to enter and terminate the estate if the stated condition occurs. This right is freely alienable during life, and it is devisable by will or descendible through intestate succession upon death.","references":"Restatement (First) of Property § 45 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (fee simple subject to a condition subsequent).\n\nRestatement (First) of Property § 155 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (power of termination or right of entry).","rule":"The grantor of a fee simple subject to a condition subsequent can explicitly retain a right of entry, which allows the grantor (or his/her successor in interest) to enter and terminate the estate if the stated condition occurs. This right is freely alienable during life, and it is devisable by will or descendible through intestate succession upon death.","irac":{"issue":"Who may properly bring an action against the charity to recover fee simple ownership of the land?","rule":"The grantor of a fee simple subject to a condition subsequent can explicitly retain a right of entry, which allows the grantor (or his/her successor in interest) to enter and terminate the estate if the stated condition occurs. This right is freely alienable during life, and it is devisable by will or descendible through intestate succession upon death.","analysis":"A fee simple subject to a condition subsequent (FSSCS) is created through specific conditional language, such as \"but if\" or \"on the condition that.\" Upon the occurrence of the condition, the grantor (or his/her successor in interest) has the right to enter and terminate the estate if the grantor explicitly retained this right of entry* in the conveyance. In most jurisdictions, the right of entry is freely alienable during life and fully transferable upon death—ie, devisable by will, or if not devised, descendible through intestate succession. Here, the landowner conveyed an FSSCS to the charity and retained a right of entry that could be exercised if the land was no longer used as a wildlife refuge. The landowner then devised all of his real property interests—including the right of entry—to his friend. And since the charity recently ceased to use the land as a wildlife refuge, the friend can exercise the right of entry and bring an action to recover ownership of the land (Choice D). *This right is also known as the \"power of termination\" or the \"right of reentry.\" (Choices A & C) The landowner devised all of his real property interests to the friend, leaving no interest in the land for the sister to inherit upon his death. Because the sister had no interest in the land to convey to the developer by quitclaim deed or for her husband to inherit upon her death, neither of them may bring an action to recover ownership of the land from the charity.","conclusion":"Answer B: The landowner's friend."},"questionZh":"通过契约，土地所有者将土地转让给慈善机构，“条件是该土地被用作野生动物保护区，否则，土地所有者及其继承人可以进入并终止由此创建的遗产。”次年，地主去世。他的妹妹是他唯一的继承人。根据意愿，土地所有者将“我所有的不动产权益转让给我的朋友”。土地所有者去世一年后，姐姐通过无偿债权契据，将她对该土地的任何权益转让给开发商。地主死后两年，地主的妹妹也去世了。她唯一的继承人是她的丈夫。最近，在土地所有者去世三年后，该慈善机构停止将这片土地用作野生动物保护区。谁可以对慈善机构提起诉讼，以收回土地的所有权？","choicesZh":{"A":"开发商。","B":"地主的朋友。","C":"姐姐的丈夫。","D":"没有人。"},"explanationZh":"后续条件简单费用 (FSSSS) 是通过特定的条件语言创建的，例如“但是如果”或“条件是”。当该条件发生时，如果设保人在产权转让中明确保留了进入和终止遗产的权利，则设保人（或其利益继承人）有权进入和终止该遗产。在大多数司法管辖区，入境权在生前可自由转让，死亡后可完全转让，即可以通过遗嘱设计，或者如果没有设计，则可以通过无遗嘱继承继承。在这里，土地所有者向慈善机构转交了 FSSCS，并保留了进入权，如果该土地不再用作野生动物保护区，则可以行使该权利。然后，地主将他所有的不动产权益（包括进入权）设计给了他的朋友。由于该慈善机构最近停止使用该土地作为野生动物保护区，该朋友可以行使进入权并提起诉讼以恢复该土地的所有权（选择 D）。 *该权利也称为“终止权”或“重新进入权”。 （选项 A 和 C） 地主将其所有不动产权益分配给了朋友，在他去世后，妹妹没有留下任何土地权益可以继承。由于姐妹对通过放弃所有权契据转让给开发商的土地或由她的丈夫在她去世后继承的土地没有兴趣，因此他们两人都不得提起诉讼以从慈善机构收回土地的所有权。\n\n教育目标：费用简单的授予人在随后的条件下可以明确保留进入权，这允许授予人（或其利益继承人）在规定的条件发生时进入和终止遗产。这项权利在生前可以自由转让，并且可以通过遗嘱设计或在死亡时通过无遗嘱继承来继承。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 45（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（费用简单，但须遵守后续条件）。\n\n财产重述（第一）§ 155（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（终止权或进入权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"受后续条件约束的费用简单的授予人可以明确保留进入权，这允许授予人（或其利益继承人）在所述条件发生时进入和终止遗产。这项权利在生前可以自由转让，并且可以通过遗嘱设计或在死亡时通过无遗嘱继承来继承。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁可以对慈善机构提起诉讼，以收回土地的所有权？","rule":"受后续条件约束的费用简单的授予人可以明确保留进入权，这允许授予人（或其利益继承人）在所述条件发生时进入和终止遗产。这项权利在生前可以自由转让，并且可以通过遗嘱设计或在死亡时通过无遗嘱继承来继承。","analysis":"后续条件简单费用 (FSSSS) 是通过特定的条件语言创建的，例如“但是如果”或“条件是”。当该条件发生时，如果设保人在产权转让中明确保留了进入和终止遗产的权利，则设保人（或其利益继承人）有权进入和终止该遗产。在大多数司法管辖区，入境权在生前可自由转让，死亡后可完全转让，即可以通过遗嘱设计，或者如果没有设计，则可以通过无遗嘱继承继承。在这里，土地所有者向慈善机构转交了 FSSCS，并保留了进入权，如果该土地不再用作野生动物保护区，则可以行使该权利。然后，地主将他所有的不动产权益（包括进入权）设计给了他的朋友。由于该慈善机构最近停止使用该土地作为野生动物保护区，该朋友可以行使进入权并提起诉讼以恢复该土地的所有权（选择 D）。 *该权利也称为“终止权”或“重新进入权”。 （选项 A 和 C） 地主将其所有不动产权益分配给了朋友，在他去世后，妹妹没有留下任何土地权益可以继承。由于姐妹对通过放弃所有权契据转让给开发商的土地或由她的丈夫在她去世后继承的土地没有兴趣，因此他们两人都不得提起诉讼以从慈善机构收回土地的所有权。\n\n教育目标：费用简单的授予人在随后的条件下可以明确保留进入权，这允许授予人（或其利益继承人）在规定的条件发生时进入和终止遗产。这项权利在生前可以自由转让，并且可以通过遗嘱设计或在死亡时通过无遗嘱继承来继承。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 45（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（费用简单，但须遵守后续条件）。\n\n财产重述（第一）§ 155（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（终止权或进入权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：地主的朋友。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"wildlife","ipa":"/wˈaɪldlˌaɪf/","syllables":"wildlife","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 野生动植物","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“wildlife”","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"By deed, a landowner conveyed land to a charity \"on the condition that the land be used as a wildlife refuge, and if not, the landowner and his heirs may enter and terminate the estate hereby created.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-95ff4cdf467c6b64"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":148,"sourceQuestionPage":434,"sourceQuestionPages":[434],"sourceExplanationPages":[435,436],"sourceAnswerPages":[435,436],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-149","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"An individual who was domiciled in State A throughout her life deeded land in State A to a charity organized under the laws of State B but retained a right of entry if the land was not used for a charitable purpose. Later in her life, the individual transferred the right of entry to a creditor. The individual then devised her entire estate to her son shortly before she died. Recently, the charity ceased to use the land for a charitable purpose. The son and the creditor are domiciled in State B. The law of State A, adhering to the common law, does not permit an inter vivos transfer of a right of entry. The law of State B permits a right of entry to be transferred during the owner's life. The son has initiated a lawsuit in a state court in State B seeking a declaratory judgment that he is the owner of the land and naming the charity and the creditor as parties in this action. Is the court likely to rule in the son's favor?","choices":{"A":"No, because the court in State B must apply the law of State B.","B":"No, because the parties to this action are domiciled in State B.","C":"Yes, because the individual was domiciled in State A throughout her life.","D":"Yes, because the law of State A likely governs this matter."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Conflict-of-laws rule for real property issues\n\nGeneral In cases involving real property, law applied by forum court should be rule determined by conflict-of-laws rule that would be applied by state where property is located\n\nExceptions Document specifies application of specific jurisdiction's laws Issues regarding effect of marriage on interest in land Collateral issues (eg, whether transferor perpetrated fraud) Issues regarding mortgage note (eg, validity of note) Foreclosure-related issues that do not affect interest in land (eg, mortgagee's right to sue mortgagor for underlying debt before foreclosure) A conflict-of-laws issue arises when significant aspects of a legal action are divided between two or more states and there is a conflict as to which state's law applies (as seen here). If such a conflict arises in a case involving real property, the general conflict-of- laws rule is as follows: The law applied by the forum court should be determined by the conflict-of-laws rule that would be applied by the courts of the situs state—ie, the state where the property is located. The conflict-of-laws rule of the situs state typically directs the application of its own local law on that issue (ie, the law of the situs) in resolving the matter. Here, the land is located in State A, so the law of State A (law of the situs) likely governs this matter. Since the law of State A does not permit an inter vivos transfer of a right of entry, the individual could not transfer her right of entry to the creditor during her lifetime. Instead, it passed to her son under her will. Therefore, the State B court will likely rule in the son's favor (Choice A). (Choice B) The fact that the parties to this action are domiciled in State B shows that the State B court has personal jurisdiction over them. But this does not determine which state's law governs the matter. (Choice C) Although the individual was domiciled in State A throughout her life, the situs of the property (not the domicile of the transferor) determines the law to be applied in this matter.\n\nEducational objective: When there is a conflict-of-laws issue in a case involving real property, the general rule is that the applicable law should be determined by the conflict-of-laws rule of the state where\n\nthe property is located (ie, situs state). This usually results in the application of the situs state's local law on that issue.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Conflict of Laws § 223 (Am. Law Inst. 1971) (validity and effect of conveyance of interest in land).","educationalObjective":"When there is a conflict-of-laws issue in a case involving real property, the general rule is that the applicable law should be determined by the conflict-of-laws rule of the state where\n\nthe property is located (ie, situs state). This usually results in the application of the situs state's local law on that issue.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Conflict of Laws § 223 (Am. Law Inst. 1971) (validity and effect of conveyance of interest in land).","rule":"When there is a conflict-of-laws issue in a case involving real property, the general rule is that the applicable law should be determined by the conflict-of-laws rule of the state where\n\nthe property is located (ie, situs state). This usually results in the application of the situs state's local law on that issue.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to rule in the son's favor?","rule":"When there is a conflict-of-laws issue in a case involving real property, the general rule is that the applicable law should be determined by the conflict-of-laws rule of the state where\n\nthe property is located (ie, situs state). This usually results in the application of the situs state's local law on that issue.","analysis":"Conflict-of-laws rule for real property issues\n\nGeneral In cases involving real property, law applied by forum court should be rule determined by conflict-of-laws rule that would be applied by state where property is located\n\nExceptions Document specifies application of specific jurisdiction's laws Issues regarding effect of marriage on interest in land Collateral issues (eg, whether transferor perpetrated fraud) Issues regarding mortgage note (eg, validity of note) Foreclosure-related issues that do not affect interest in land (eg, mortgagee's right to sue mortgagor for underlying debt before foreclosure) A conflict-of-laws issue arises when significant aspects of a legal action are divided between two or more states and there is a conflict as to which state's law applies (as seen here). If such a conflict arises in a case involving real property, the general conflict-of- laws rule is as follows: The law applied by the forum court should be determined by the conflict-of-laws rule that would be applied by the courts of the situs state—ie, the state where the property is located. The conflict-of-laws rule of the situs state typically directs the application of its own local law on that issue (ie, the law of the situs) in resolving the matter. Here, the land is located in State A, so the law of State A (law of the situs) likely governs this matter. Since the law of State A does not permit an inter vivos transfer of a right of entry, the individual could not transfer her right of entry to the creditor during her lifetime. Instead, it passed to her son under her will. Therefore, the State B court will likely rule in the son's favor (Choice A). (Choice B) The fact that the parties to this action are domiciled in State B shows that the State B court has personal jurisdiction over them. But this does not determine which state's law governs the matter. (Choice C) Although the individual was domiciled in State A throughout her life, the situs of the property (not the domicile of the transferor) determines the law to be applied in this matter.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the law of State A likely governs this matter."},"questionZh":"一生居住在 A 州的个人将 A 州的土地转让给根据 B 州法律组织的慈善机构，但如果该土地不用于慈善目的，则保留进入权。在她生命的后期，该人将进入权转让给债权人。在她去世前不久，该人将她的全部财产分配给了她的儿子。最近，该慈善机构停止将这片土地用于慈善目的。儿子和债权人的住所都在B国。A国的法律遵循普通法，不允许入境权的生前转让。 B 国法律允许在所有者生前转让进入权。儿子已向 B 州的州法院提起诉讼，寻求宣告性判决，证明他是土地所有者，并将慈善机构和债权人列为诉讼当事人。法庭可能做出对儿子有利的裁决吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为 B 国法院必须适用 B 国法律。","B":"否，因为该诉讼当事人的住所位于 B 国。","C":"是的，因为该人一生都在 A 州居住。","D":"是的，因为 A 国的法律可能管辖此事。"},"explanationZh":"不动产问题的法律冲突规则\n\n一般性 在涉及不动产的案件中，法院适用的法律应由财产所在州适用的法律冲突规则确定。\n\n例外文件规定了具体司法管辖区法律的适用情况有关婚姻对土地权益的影响的问题抵押问题（例如转让人是否实施了欺诈）有关抵押票据的问题（例如票据的有效性）不影响土地权益的止赎相关问题（例如抵押权人在止赎之前就基础债务起诉抵押人的权利）当法律诉讼的重要方面在两个或多个州之间划分并且存在冲突时，就会出现法律冲突问题适用哪个州的法律（如此处所示）。如果涉及不动产的案件中出现此类冲突，一般的法律冲突规则如下：法院地法院适用的法律应由所在地国（即财产所在州）法院适用的法律冲突规则确定。所在地国的法律冲突规则通常指导在解决问题时适用其本国关于该问题的当地法律（即所在地法律）。此处，土地位于 A 州，因此 A 州法律（所在地法）可能管辖此事项。由于A国法律不允许进入权生前转让，个人在其生前不能将其进入权转让给债权人。相反，它根据她的遗嘱传给了她的儿子。因此，B 州法院可能会做出对儿子有利的裁决（选择 A）。 （选项B）本诉讼当事人住所地在B国，表明B国法院对他们具有属人管辖权。但这并不能确定哪个州的法律管辖此事。 （选项C）虽然该个人一生都在A国居住，但财产所在地（而不是转让人的住所）决定了本案适用的法律。\n\n教育目标：当涉及不动产的案件中存在法律冲突问题时，一般规则是应根据案件发生国的法律冲突规则来确定适用的法律。\n\n该财产位于（即所在地状态）。这通常会导致就该问题适用所在地州的当地法律。\n\n参考文献\n法律冲突重述（第二）§ 223（Am. Law Inst. 1971）（土地权益转让的有效性和效果）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当涉及不动产案件存在法律冲突问题时，一般规则是应根据案件发生地所在国的法律冲突规则确定适用的法律。\n\n该财产位于（即所在地状态）。这通常会导致就该问题适用所在地州的当地法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"法庭可能做出对儿子有利的裁决吗？","rule":"当涉及不动产案件存在法律冲突问题时，一般规则是应根据案件发生地所在国的法律冲突规则确定适用的法律。\n\n该财产位于（即所在地状态）。这通常会导致就该问题适用所在地州的当地法律。","analysis":"不动产问题的法律冲突规则\n\n一般性 在涉及不动产的案件中，法院适用的法律应由财产所在州适用的法律冲突规则确定。\n\n例外文件规定了具体司法管辖区法律的适用情况有关婚姻对土地权益的影响的问题抵押问题（例如转让人是否实施了欺诈）有关抵押票据的问题（例如票据的有效性）不影响土地权益的止赎相关问题（例如抵押权人在止赎之前就基础债务起诉抵押人的权利）当法律诉讼的重要方面在两个或多个州之间划分并且存在冲突时，就会出现法律冲突问题适用哪个州的法律（如此处所示）。如果涉及不动产的案件中出现此类冲突，一般的法律冲突规则如下：法院地法院适用的法律应由所在地国（即财产所在州）法院适用的法律冲突规则确定。所在地国的法律冲突规则通常指导在解决问题时适用其本国关于该问题的当地法律（即所在地法律）。此处，土地位于 A 州，因此 A 州法律（所在地法）可能管辖此事项。由于A国法律不允许进入权生前转让，个人在其生前不能将其进入权转让给债权人。相反，它根据她的遗嘱传给了她的儿子。因此，B 州法院可能会做出对儿子有利的裁决（选择 A）。 （选项B）本诉讼当事人住所地在B国，表明B国法院对他们具有属人管辖权。但这并不能确定哪个州的法律管辖此事。 （选项C）虽然该个人一生都在A国居住，但财产所在地（而不是转让人的住所）决定了本案适用的法律。\n\n教育目标：当涉及不动产的案件中存在法律冲突问题时，一般规则是应根据案件发生国的法律冲突规则来确定适用的法律。\n\n该财产位于（即所在地状态）。这通常会导致就该问题适用所在地州的当地法律。\n\n参考文献\n法律冲突重述（第二）§ 223（Am. Law Inst. 1971）（土地权益转让的有效性和效果）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为 A 国的法律可能管辖此事。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":149,"sourceQuestionPage":437,"sourceQuestionPages":[437],"sourceExplanationPages":[438,439],"sourceAnswerPages":[438,439],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-150","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Sixty years ago by a properly executed and recorded deed, a man conveyed a tract of land: \"To my grandson for life, then to my grandson's widow for her life, then to my grandson's child or children in equal shares.\" At that time, the grandson was six years old. Shortly thereafter, the man died testate. The grandson was the man's only heir at law. The man's will left his entire estate to his church. Twenty-five years ago, when he was 41, the grandson married a woman who was then 20 years old; they had one child, a son. The grandson's wife and son were killed in an automobile accident three years ago when the son was 21. The son died testate, leaving his entire estate to a charity. His father, the grandson, was his sole heir at law. Two years ago, the grandson married another woman. They had no children. This year, the grandson died testate, survived by his widow, to whom he left his entire estate. The common law Rule Against Perpetuities is unchanged by statute in the jurisdiction. In an appropriate action to determine ownership of the land, the court should find that title is vested in whom?","choices":{"A":"The charity, because the son had a vested remainder interest (as the only child of the grandson) that it inherited, the life estate to the grandson's widow being of no force and effect.","B":"The church, because the grandson's widow was unborn at the time of conveyance and, hence, the remainder violated the Rule Against Perpetuities.","C":"The grandson's widow, because her life estate and her inheritance from the grandson (who was the man's sole heir at law and who was the son's sole heir at law) merged the entire title in her.","D":"The grandson's widow for life under the terms of the man's deed, with the remainder to the charity as the successor in interest to the son, the grandson's only child."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A life estate is a present possessory interest in land that terminates on the death of an individual. The future-interest holder becomes entitled to possession once that death occurs. The future interest is either a reversion (if retained by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in someone other than the grantor). Both are fully transferable inter vivos,* devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance. Here, the man conveyed a life estate to his grandson, which would pass first to the grandson's widow for life (remainder in life estate) and then to the grandson's child or children (remainder in fee simple absolute). The grandson had just one child, a son. Since the son died testate (ie, with a will), he effectively left his remainder interest in the land to the charity. Therefore, after the grandson's death, the widow had a life estate and the charity had the son's remainder interest (Choices A & C). *At common law and in a minority of jurisdictions, contingent remainders cannot be transferred inter vivos. (Choice B) The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) renders a future interest void if there is any possibility that it could vest more than 21 years after the death of some relevant person alive when the interest was created. But here, the widow's remainder interest did not violate RAP because it necessarily vested at the death of the grandson.\n\nEducational objective: The future interest that follows a life estate is either a reversion (if held by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in someone other than the grantor). Both are transferable, devisable, and descendible.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 157 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing the classification of remainders).","educationalObjective":"The future interest that follows a life estate is either a reversion (if held by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in someone other than the grantor). Both are transferable, devisable, and descendible.","references":"Restatement of Property § 157 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (discussing the classification of remainders).","rule":"The future interest that follows a life estate is either a reversion (if held by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in someone other than the grantor). Both are transferable, devisable, and descendible.","irac":{"issue":"In an appropriate action to determine ownership of the land, the court should find that title is vested in whom?","rule":"The future interest that follows a life estate is either a reversion (if held by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in someone other than the grantor). Both are transferable, devisable, and descendible.","analysis":"A life estate is a present possessory interest in land that terminates on the death of an individual. The future-interest holder becomes entitled to possession once that death occurs. The future interest is either a reversion (if retained by the grantor) or a remainder (if created in someone other than the grantor). Both are fully transferable inter vivos,* devisable by will, and descendible by inheritance. Here, the man conveyed a life estate to his grandson, which would pass first to the grandson's widow for life (remainder in life estate) and then to the grandson's child or children (remainder in fee simple absolute). The grandson had just one child, a son. Since the son died testate (ie, with a will), he effectively left his remainder interest in the land to the charity. Therefore, after the grandson's death, the widow had a life estate and the charity had the son's remainder interest (Choices A & C). *At common law and in a minority of jurisdictions, contingent remainders cannot be transferred inter vivos. (Choice B) The Rule Against Perpetuities (RAP) renders a future interest void if there is any possibility that it could vest more than 21 years after the death of some relevant person alive when the interest was created. But here, the widow's remainder interest did not violate RAP because it necessarily vested at the death of the grandson.","conclusion":"Answer D: The grandson's widow for life under the terms of the man's deed, with the remainder to the charity as the successor in interest to the son, the grandson's only child."},"questionZh":"六十年前，通过一份正确执行和记录的契约，一名男子转让了一块土地：“终身转让给我的孙子，然后转让给我孙子的遗孀，然后平分给我孙子的一个或多个孩子。”那一年，孙子六岁了。此后不久，该男子去世时留下了遗嘱。孙子是该男子唯一的法定继承人。该男子的遗嘱将他的全部财产留给了他的教会。 25年前，41岁的孙子娶了一个当时20岁的女人；他们有一个孩子，一个儿子。三年前，孙子21岁时，他的妻子和儿子在一场车祸中丧生。儿子去世时留下了遗嘱，将全部遗产留给了慈善机构。他的父亲，即孙子，是他唯一的法定继承人。两年前，孙子又娶了另一个女人。他们没有孩子。今年，孙子去世时留下了遗嘱，遗孀幸存，他将全部遗产留给了她。普通法中的“反永续规则”不受辖区法规的影响。在确定土地所有权的适当行动中，法院应确定所有权归属于谁？","choicesZh":{"A":"慈善机构，因为儿子拥有其继承的既得剩余权益（作为孙子的独生子女），因此孙子的遗孀的终身财产没有任何效力。","B":"教会，因为孙子的遗孀在转让时尚未出生，因此，剩余的人违反了“反对永久规则”。","C":"孙子的遗孀，因为她的终身财产和她从孙子（孙子是该男子的唯一法律继承人，也是儿子的唯一法律继承人）继承的全部头衔合并在她身上。","D":"根据该男子的契约条款，孙子终身守寡，其余部分捐给慈善机构，作为孙子唯一的孩子的儿子的利益继承人。"},"explanationZh":"终身财产是对土地的现有占有权益，在个人死亡时终止。一旦死亡发生，未来权益持有人就有权占有。未来利息要么是归还（如果由设保人保留），要么是剩余利息（如果由设保人以外的其他人创建）。两者都是完全可在生前转移的，*可以通过意志设计，并且可以通过遗传传承。在这里，该男子将终身财产转让给他的孙子，该财产将首先终身转移给孙子的遗孀（终身财产的剩余部分），然后转移给孙子的一个或多个孩子（简单绝对的剩余部分）。孙子只有一个孩子，一个儿子。由于儿子死时留有遗嘱（即有遗嘱），他实际上将剩余的土地权益留给了慈善机构。因此，孙子去世后，寡妇拥有终身遗产，慈善机构拥有儿子的剩余权益（选择A和C）。 *根据普通法和少数司法管辖区，或有余额不能在生前转移。 （选择 B）如果在权益创建时某些活着的相关人员死亡超过 21 年后，未来权益有可能归属，则反对永续规则 (RAP) 将使未来权益无效。但在这里，寡妇的剩余权益并不违反 RAP，因为它必然在孙子去世时归属。\n\n教育目标：终身财产之后的未来利息要么是归还（如果由授予人持有），要么是剩余利益（如果由授予人以外的其他人创建）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 157 (Am. Law Inst. 1936)（讨论剩余物的分类）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"终身财产之后的未来利息要么是归还（如果由授予人持有），要么是剩余权益（如果由授予人以外的其他人创建）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。","iracZh":{"issue":"在确定土地所有权的适当行动中，法院应确定所有权归属于谁？","rule":"终身财产之后的未来利息要么是归还（如果由授予人持有），要么是剩余权益（如果由授予人以外的其他人创建）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。","analysis":"终身财产是对土地的现有占有权益，在个人死亡时终止。一旦死亡发生，未来权益持有人就有权占有。未来利息要么是归还（如果由设保人保留），要么是剩余利息（如果由设保人以外的其他人创建）。两者都是完全可在生前转移的，*可以通过意志设计，并且可以通过遗传传承。在这里，该男子将终身财产转让给他的孙子，该财产将首先终身转移给孙子的遗孀（终身财产的剩余部分），然后转移给孙子的一个或多个孩子（简单绝对的剩余部分）。孙子只有一个孩子，一个儿子。由于儿子死时留有遗嘱（即有遗嘱），他实际上将剩余的土地权益留给了慈善机构。因此，孙子去世后，寡妇拥有终身遗产，慈善机构拥有儿子的剩余权益（选择A和C）。 *根据普通法和少数司法管辖区，或有余额不能在生前转移。 （选择 B）如果在权益创建时某些活着的相关人员死亡超过 21 年后，未来权益有可能归属，则反对永续规则 (RAP) 将使未来权益无效。但在这里，寡妇的剩余权益并不违反 RAP，因为它必然在孙子去世时归属。\n\n教育目标：终身财产之后的未来利息要么是归还（如果由授予人持有），要么是剩余利益（如果由授予人以外的其他人创建）。两者都是可转让、可设计和可传承的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 157 (Am. Law Inst. 1936)（讨论剩余物的分类）。","conclusion":"答案 D：根据该男子的契约条款，孙子终身守寡，其余部分捐给慈善机构，作为孙子唯一的孩子的儿子的利益继承人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-0d1f4c99e738dda3"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":150,"sourceQuestionPage":440,"sourceQuestionPages":[440],"sourceExplanationPages":[441,442],"sourceAnswerPages":[441,442],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-151","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A tenant entered into a one-year agreement to lease a two-bedroom home from a landlord for $1,000 per month, with rent due at the beginning of each month. The lease further provided that either party could properly terminate the lease by giving at least 30-days' notice. For eight months, the tenant paid rent on a timely basis. At the beginning of the following month, the tenant failed to pay rent. The landlord immediately contacted the tenant and demanded payment, but the tenant refused to pay. Pursuant to the terms of the lease, the tenant gave 30 days' notice of his intent to vacate the premises. The landlord subsequently sued the tenant for damages. In the meantime, the landlord re-rented the home for the remaining three months of the lease to another tenant for $800 per month. Under the majority rule, what is the most that the landlord could potentially collect from the tenant?","choices":{"A":"Nothing, because the landlord did not give the tenant the opportunity to cure before re-renting.","B":"$1,000, because the lease was properly terminated and the tenant is not obligated to pay future rents.","C":"$1,600, because the tenant is liable for any deficiency resulting from his early termination of the lease.","D":"$2,000, because the tenant is liable for the current month and rent that accrues during his 30-day notice period."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Landlord's remedies for tenant's failure to pay rent\n\nSue for rent Can sue for rent as it comes due, several accrued rents, or entire amount at end of lease term Majority rule – cannot recover future rents unless lease contains acceleration clause Minority rule – can recover future rents minus (1) reasonable rental value of premises for remainder of lease or (2) actual rent collected on re-rental Terminate & Majority rule – must give tenant notice & opportunity to cure before evict terminating lease or evicting tenant Minority (common law) rule – cannot evict tenant for failure to pay rent A tenant's primary duty is to pay rent. If the tenant breaches this duty by failing to pay rent as it comes due, then the landlord can sue the tenant for damages. In determining the landlord's damages, the majority rule is that the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation does not apply to leases. This means that the landlord is not entitled to damages for future rents that would have been due under the lease.* Therefore, under the majority rule, the most the landlord could potentially collect from the tenant in this scenario is the one month's rent that the tenant failed to pay—ie, $1,000. *Some leases contain rent-acceleration clauses, which allow the landlord to declare all future rents immediately due and payable if the tenant fails to pay rent. But there is no indication that this lease contains such a clause. (Choice A) Notice and an opportunity to cure are only required when a landlord attempts to evict a tenant. But here, the tenant sought termination of the lease. (Choices C & D) In the minority of states that apply the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation to leases, a landlord is generally entitled to the difference between future rents that would have been due for the remainder of the lease and either: the reasonable rental value of the premises for that period or the actual rent collected on a re-renting of the premises for that period. Here, the reasonable rental value of the premises is not stated. However, the tenant would have owed $4,000 for the remaining four months of the lease ($1,000 per month × 4), and the actual rent collected during that time is expected to be $2,400 ($800 per month × 3). So had this jurisdiction followed this doctrine, the landlord could have recovered a total of $1,600 ($4,000 - $2,400) in past and future rents.\n\nEducational objective: A landlord can sue a tenant for damages if the tenant fails to pay rent as it comes due. But since the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation does not apply to leases (majority rule), the landlord is not entitled to damages for any future rents that would have been due under the lease.\n\nReferences\n49 Am. Jur. 2d Landlord and Tenant § 615 (2020) (time to sue in actions for recovery of rent).","educationalObjective":"A landlord can sue a tenant for damages if the tenant fails to pay rent as it comes due. But since the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation does not apply to leases (majority rule), the landlord is not entitled to damages for any future rents that would have been due under the lease.","references":"49 Am. Jur. 2d Landlord and Tenant § 615 (2020) (time to sue in actions for recovery of rent).","rule":"A landlord can sue a tenant for damages if the tenant fails to pay rent as it comes due. But since the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation does not apply to leases (majority rule), the landlord is not entitled to damages for any future rents that would have been due under the lease.","irac":{"issue":"Under the majority rule, what is the most that the landlord could potentially collect from the tenant?","rule":"A landlord can sue a tenant for damages if the tenant fails to pay rent as it comes due. But since the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation does not apply to leases (majority rule), the landlord is not entitled to damages for any future rents that would have been due under the lease.","analysis":"Landlord's remedies for tenant's failure to pay rent\n\nSue for rent Can sue for rent as it comes due, several accrued rents, or entire amount at end of lease term Majority rule – cannot recover future rents unless lease contains acceleration clause Minority rule – can recover future rents minus (1) reasonable rental value of premises for remainder of lease or (2) actual rent collected on re-rental Terminate & Majority rule – must give tenant notice & opportunity to cure before evict terminating lease or evicting tenant Minority (common law) rule – cannot evict tenant for failure to pay rent A tenant's primary duty is to pay rent. If the tenant breaches this duty by failing to pay rent as it comes due, then the landlord can sue the tenant for damages. In determining the landlord's damages, the majority rule is that the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation does not apply to leases. This means that the landlord is not entitled to damages for future rents that would have been due under the lease.* Therefore, under the majority rule, the most the landlord could potentially collect from the tenant in this scenario is the one month's rent that the tenant failed to pay—ie, $1,000. *Some leases contain rent-acceleration clauses, which allow the landlord to declare all future rents immediately due and payable if the tenant fails to pay rent. But there is no indication that this lease contains such a clause. (Choice A) Notice and an opportunity to cure are only required when a landlord attempts to evict a tenant. But here, the tenant sought termination of the lease. (Choices C & D) In the minority of states that apply the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation to leases, a landlord is generally entitled to the difference between future rents that would have been due for the remainder of the lease and either: the reasonable rental value of the premises for that period or the actual rent collected on a re-renting of the premises for that period. Here, the reasonable rental value of the premises is not stated. However, the tenant would have owed $4,000 for the remaining four months of the lease ($1,000 per month × 4), and the actual rent collected during that time is expected to be $2,400 ($800 per month × 3). So had this jurisdiction followed this doctrine, the landlord could have recovered a total of $1,600 ($4,000 - $2,400) in past and future rents.","conclusion":"Answer B: $1,000, because the lease was properly terminated and the tenant is not obligated to pay future rents."},"questionZh":"一名租户签订了一份为期一年的协议，以每月 1,000 美元的价格向房东租赁一套两居室房屋，租金在每个月初到期。租约还规定，任何一方都可以通过至少提前 30 天的通知来适当终止租约。八个月来，租户按时缴纳租金。次月初，租客未能缴纳租金。房东立即联系租客并要求付款，但租客拒绝付款。根据租约条款，承租人提前 30 天通知其打算搬出该房屋。房东随后起诉房客，要求赔偿损失。与此同时，房东将剩余三个月的房屋以每月 800 美元的价格重新出租给另一位租户。根据多数决定原则，房东最多可以向租客收取多少费用？","choicesZh":{"A":"没什么，因为房东在重新出租之前没有给租客治愈的机会。","B":"1,000 美元，因为租约已正确终止，租户没有义务支付未来的租金。","C":"1,600 美元，因为租户应对提前终止租约造成的任何不足承担责任。","D":"2,000 美元，因为租户应对当月以及 30 天通知期内产生的租金负责。"},"explanationZh":"房东对租客未能支付租金的补救措施\n\n起诉租金 可针对到期租金、若干应计租金或租赁期结束时的全部金额提起诉讼 多数规则 – 除非租约包含加速条款，否则无法收回未来租金 少数规则 – 可以收回未来租金减去 (1) 剩余租约的房屋合理租金价值或 (2) 重新出租时收取的实际租金 终止和多数规则 – 在驱逐终止租约或驱逐租户之前必须向租户发出通知并提供补救的机会 少数（普通法）规则 –不能因不支付租金而驱逐租户 租户的主要职责是支付租金。如果租客违反了这项义务，未能按时支付租金，那么房东可以起诉租客，要求其赔偿损失。在确定房东的损害赔偿时，多数规则是预期毁约原则不适用于租赁。这意味着房东无权就根据租约应支付的未来租金获得损害赔偿。*因此，根据多数决定原则，在这种情况下，房东最多可以向租户收取租户未能支付的一个月租金，即 1,000 美元。 *某些租约包含租金加速条款，允许房东在租户未能支付租金的情况下宣布所有未来租金立即到期应付。但没有迹象表明这份租约包含这样的条款。 （选择 A）仅当房东试图驱逐租户时才需要通知和补救机会。但在这里，租户要求终止租约。 （选项 C 和 D）在少数对租赁适用预期毁约原则的州中，房东通常有权获得剩余租约中应支付的未来租金与以下两者之间的差额：该期间房屋的合理租金价值或该期间重新出租该房屋所收取的实际租金。这里没有说明房屋的合理租金价值。然而，租户在剩余四个月的租约中欠下 4,000 美元（每月 1,000 美元 × 4），而在此期间实际收取的租金预计为 2,400 美元（每月 800 美元 × 3）。因此，如果该管辖区遵循这一原则，房东可以收回过去和未来租金总计 1,600 美元（4,000 美元 - 2,400 美元）的费用。\n\n教育目标：如果租客未能按时支付租金，房东可以起诉租客，要求其赔偿损失。但由于预期毁约原则不适用于租赁（多数规则），因此房东无权就根据租约应支付的任何未来租金获得损害赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n49 上午。尤尔。 2d 房东和租户 § 615 (2020)（提起追回租金诉讼的时间）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果租客未能按时支付租金，房东可以起诉租客，要求其赔偿损失。但由于预期毁约原则不适用于租赁（多数规则），因此房东无权就根据租约应支付的任何未来租金获得损害赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"根据多数决定原则，房东最多可以向租客收取多少费用？","rule":"如果租客未能按时支付租金，房东可以起诉租客，要求其赔偿损失。但由于预期毁约原则不适用于租赁（多数规则），因此房东无权就根据租约应支付的任何未来租金获得损害赔偿。","analysis":"房东对租客未能支付租金的补救措施\n\n起诉租金 可针对到期租金、若干应计租金或租赁期结束时的全部金额提起诉讼 多数规则 – 除非租约包含加速条款，否则无法收回未来租金 少数规则 – 可以收回未来租金减去 (1) 剩余租约的房屋合理租金价值或 (2) 重新出租时收取的实际租金 终止和多数规则 – 在驱逐终止租约或驱逐租户之前必须向租户发出通知并提供补救的机会 少数（普通法）规则 –不能因不支付租金而驱逐租户 租户的主要职责是支付租金。如果租客违反了这项义务，未能按时支付租金，那么房东可以起诉租客，要求其赔偿损失。在确定房东的损害赔偿时，多数规则是预期毁约原则不适用于租赁。这意味着房东无权就根据租约应支付的未来租金获得损害赔偿。*因此，根据多数决定原则，在这种情况下，房东最多可以向租户收取租户未能支付的一个月租金，即 1,000 美元。 *某些租约包含租金加速条款，允许房东在租户未能支付租金的情况下宣布所有未来租金立即到期应付。但没有迹象表明这份租约包含这样的条款。 （选择 A）仅当房东试图驱逐租户时才需要通知和补救机会。但在这里，租户要求终止租约。 （选项 C 和 D）在少数对租赁适用预期毁约原则的州中，房东通常有权获得剩余租约中应支付的未来租金与以下两者之间的差额：该期间房屋的合理租金价值或该期间重新出租该房屋所收取的实际租金。这里没有说明房屋的合理租金价值。然而，租户在剩余四个月的租约中欠下 4,000 美元（每月 1,000 美元 × 4），而在此期间实际收取的租金预计为 2,400 美元（每月 800 美元 × 3）。因此，如果该管辖区遵循这一原则，房东可以收回过去和未来租金总计 1,600 美元（4,000 美元 - 2,400 美元）的费用。\n\n教育目标：如果租客未能按时支付租金，房东可以起诉租客，要求其赔偿损失。但由于预期毁约原则不适用于租赁（多数规则），因此房东无权就根据租约应支付的任何未来租金获得损害赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n49 上午。尤尔。 2d 房东和租户 § 615 (2020)（提起追回租金诉讼的时间）。","conclusion":"答案 B：1,000 美元，因为租约已正确终止，租户没有义务支付未来的租金。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":151,"sourceQuestionPage":443,"sourceQuestionPages":[443],"sourceExplanationPages":[444,445],"sourceAnswerPages":[444,445],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-152","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Six months ago, a man told his cousin that he would give her his farm as a gift on her next birthday. The cousin then entered into a valid written contract to sell the farm to an investor with the closing to take place \"one week after [the cousin's] next birthday.\" The man failed to convey the farm to the cousin on her birthday. One week after the cousin's birthday, on the intended closing date, the investor first learned of the cousin's inability to convey the farm because the man had breached his promise. The investor considered suing the cousin but realized that the cousin could not be compelled to convey the farm because it was still owned by the man. Two weeks after the cousin's birthday, the man died. Under his valid will, the man devised the farm to the cousin. Within a week, the executor of the man's estate gave the cousin an executor's deed to the farm in compliance with state law. The investor promptly learned of this transfer and demanded that the cousin convey the farm to her. The cousin refused. The investor sued the cousin for specific performance. Who will likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"The cousin, because she received title by devise rather than by conveyance.","B":"The cousin, because the contract to convey was not signed by the legal owner of the farm as of the date of the contract and was therefore void.","C":"The investor, because the contract to convey merged into the executor's deed to the cousin.","D":"The investor, because the contract to convey remained enforceable by her within a reasonable period of time after the proposed closing date."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The sale of real property typically comprises three parts: Execution of the sales contract – the buyer promises to pay the purchase price in exchange for the seller's promise to deliver title Executory period – the buyer can inspect the property, secure a loan, and review title documents; the seller can repair the property or correct imperfections in the title Closing – the seller formally transfers title by delivering the deed to the buyer; the buyer pays the purchase price A closing date is not an essential term of a land-sale contract, so the seller's performance is due at or within a reasonable time after the closing date unless time is of the essence. Time is of the essence when the contract or circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended that the closing date be strictly enforced—not the case here. Therefore, the contract between the cousin and the investor was still enforceable when the cousin acquired title to the farm shortly after the proposed closing date, and the investor will likely prevail. (Choice A) Since the cousin acquired title within a reasonable time after the proposed closing date, she was contractually obligated to transfer title to the investor. The method through which she received title (by devise or conveyance) is irrelevant. (Choice B) The seller need not have title at the time the land-sale contract is executed since performance is not due until closing. As a result, the cousin could sign the contract with the investor even though she was not the legal owner of the farm at that time. (Choice C) Under the merger rule, a land-sale contract merges into the deed of conveyance at the time of closing, so only the guarantees reflected in the deed remain. This means that the contract will merge into the cousin's deed (not the executor's deed) once the deed is conveyed.\n\nEducational objective: A seller need not transfer title until closing or a reasonable time thereafter unless time is of the essence—ie, if the contract or circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended the closing date to be strictly enforced.","educationalObjective":"A seller need not transfer title until closing or a reasonable time thereafter unless time is of the essence—ie, if the contract or circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended the closing date to be strictly enforced.","references":"","rule":"A seller need not transfer title until closing or a reasonable time thereafter unless time is of the essence—ie, if the contract or circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended the closing date to be strictly enforced.","irac":{"issue":"Who will likely prevail?","rule":"A seller need not transfer title until closing or a reasonable time thereafter unless time is of the essence—ie, if the contract or circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended the closing date to be strictly enforced.","analysis":"The sale of real property typically comprises three parts: Execution of the sales contract – the buyer promises to pay the purchase price in exchange for the seller's promise to deliver title Executory period – the buyer can inspect the property, secure a loan, and review title documents; the seller can repair the property or correct imperfections in the title Closing – the seller formally transfers title by delivering the deed to the buyer; the buyer pays the purchase price A closing date is not an essential term of a land-sale contract, so the seller's performance is due at or within a reasonable time after the closing date unless time is of the essence. Time is of the essence when the contract or circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended that the closing date be strictly enforced—not the case here. Therefore, the contract between the cousin and the investor was still enforceable when the cousin acquired title to the farm shortly after the proposed closing date, and the investor will likely prevail. (Choice A) Since the cousin acquired title within a reasonable time after the proposed closing date, she was contractually obligated to transfer title to the investor. The method through which she received title (by devise or conveyance) is irrelevant. (Choice B) The seller need not have title at the time the land-sale contract is executed since performance is not due until closing. As a result, the cousin could sign the contract with the investor even though she was not the legal owner of the farm at that time. (Choice C) Under the merger rule, a land-sale contract merges into the deed of conveyance at the time of closing, so only the guarantees reflected in the deed remain. This means that the contract will merge into the cousin's deed (not the executor's deed) once the deed is conveyed.","conclusion":"Answer D: The investor, because the contract to convey remained enforceable by her within a reasonable period of time after the proposed closing date."},"questionZh":"六个月前，一名男子告诉他的表弟，他将在她下一个生日时把他的农场作为礼物送给她。然后，表弟签订了一份有效的书面合同，将农场出售给投资者，交割将于“[表弟]下一个生日后一周”进行。这名男子未能在表弟生日那天将农场转让给她。表弟生日一周后，也就是预定的交割日，投资者第一次得知表弟无法转让农场，因为该男子违反了承诺。投资者考虑起诉表弟，但意识到不能强迫表弟转让农场，因为农场仍然归该男子所有。表弟生日两周后，该男子去世了。根据他的有效遗嘱，该男子将农场设计给了表弟。一周之内，该男子的遗产执行人根据州法律向他的表弟提供了农场的遗嘱执行人。投资者很快得知了这笔转让，并要求表弟将农场转让给她。表弟拒绝了。投资人因具体表现起诉表弟。谁可能会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"表弟，因为她通过设计而不是通过转让获得了头衔。","B":"表弟，因为截至合同签订之日，转让合同尚未由农场的合法所有者签署，因此无效。","C":"投资者，因为合同转让并入执行人的契约给堂兄。","D":"投资者，因为要转让的合同在拟议的交割日期后的合理时间内仍然可以由她执行。"},"explanationZh":"不动产的销售通常包括三个部分： 销售合同的执行 – 买方承诺支付购买价，以换取卖方承诺交付产权 执行期 – 买方可以检查房产、获得贷款并审查产权文件；卖方可以修复财产或纠正所有权中的缺陷。 成交——卖方通过将契约交付给买方来正式转让所有权；买方支付购买价款 交割日不是土地买卖合同的重要条款，因此卖方应在交割日或交割日之后的合理时间内履行履约义务，除非时间至关重要。当合同或情况强烈表明双方有意严格执行交割日期时，时间至关重要（但此处的情况并非如此）。因此，当表兄弟在拟议的交割日期后不久获得农场所有权时，表兄弟与投资者之间的合同仍然有效，投资者很可能会获胜。 （选择 A）由于表弟在拟定交割日期后的合理时间内获得了所有权，因此根据合同，她有义务将所有权转让给投资者。她获得所有权的方式（通过设计或转让）无关紧要。 （选择 B） 卖方在执行土地销售合同时无需拥有所有权，因为履约直到交割才到期。结果，尽管表弟当时不是农场的合法所有者，但她仍然可以与投资者签订合同。 （选项C） 根据合并规则，土地销售合同在交割时合并到转让契据中，因此仅保留契据中体现的担保。这意味着一旦契约被转让，合同将合并到表兄弟的契约（而不是遗嘱执行人的契约）中。\n\n教育目标：卖方无需在交割之前或之后的合理时间内转让所有权，除非时间至关重要，即，如果合同或情况强烈表明双方打算严格执行交割日期。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非时间至关重要，即合同或情况强烈表明双方打算严格执行交割日期，否则卖方无需在交割前或此后合理的时间内转让所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁可能会获胜？","rule":"除非时间至关重要，即合同或情况强烈表明双方打算严格执行交割日期，否则卖方无需在交割前或此后合理的时间内转让所有权。","analysis":"不动产的销售通常包括三个部分： 销售合同的执行 – 买方承诺支付购买价，以换取卖方承诺交付产权 执行期 – 买方可以检查房产、获得贷款并审查产权文件；卖方可以修复财产或纠正所有权中的缺陷。 成交——卖方通过将契约交付给买方来正式转让所有权；买方支付购买价款 交割日不是土地买卖合同的重要条款，因此卖方应在交割日或交割日之后的合理时间内履行履约义务，除非时间至关重要。当合同或情况强烈表明双方有意严格执行交割日期时，时间至关重要（但此处的情况并非如此）。因此，当表兄弟在拟议的交割日期后不久获得农场所有权时，表兄弟与投资者之间的合同仍然有效，投资者很可能会获胜。 （选择 A）由于表弟在拟定交割日期后的合理时间内获得了所有权，因此根据合同，她有义务将所有权转让给投资者。她获得所有权的方式（通过设计或转让）无关紧要。 （选择 B） 卖方在执行土地销售合同时无需拥有所有权，因为履约直到交割才到期。结果，尽管表弟当时不是农场的合法所有者，但她仍然可以与投资者签订合同。 （选项C） 根据合并规则，土地销售合同在交割时合并到转让契据中，因此仅保留契据中体现的担保。这意味着一旦契约被转让，合同将合并到表兄弟的契约（而不是遗嘱执行人的契约）中。\n\n教育目标：卖方无需在交割之前或之后的合理时间内转让所有权，除非时间至关重要，即，如果合同或情况强烈表明双方打算严格执行交割日期。","conclusion":"答案 D：投资者，因为要转让的合同在拟议的交割日期后的合理时间内仍然可以由她执行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"time is of the essence","ipa":"/tˈaɪm ˈɪz ˈəv ðə ˈɛsəns/","syllables":"time is of the essence","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"时间为要件：约定期限被视为严格履行条件，迟延可构成重大违约","contextualMeaning":"时间为要件：约定期限被视为严格履行条件，迟延可构成重大违约","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A seller need not transfer title until closing or a reasonable time thereafter unless time is of the essence—ie, if the contract or circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended the closing date to be strictly enforced.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"land sale contract","ipa":"/lˈænd sˈeɪl kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"land sale con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"土地买卖合同：约定转让特定不动产所有权的合同","contextualMeaning":"土地买卖合同：约定转让特定不动产所有权的合同","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Six months ago, a man told his cousin that he would give her his farm as a gift on her next birthday.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-fafb7d52ab72d1ec"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":152,"sourceQuestionPage":446,"sourceQuestionPages":[446],"sourceExplanationPages":[447,448],"sourceAnswerPages":[447,448],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-153","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A man and his neighbor owned homes on adjacent lots in a subdivision. The subdivision's recorded restrictions did not prohibit detached storage sheds, and several homeowners in the subdivision had placed such sheds in their backyards. Because the man and the neighbor thought the sheds were unsightly, they both agreed in writing not to place detached storage sheds in their respective yards. Their agreement was drafted in recordable form and stated that it was enforceable by and against all assignees, heirs, and successors. The agreement was promptly recorded. Three years later, the neighbor gave his home to his daughter. Shortly after moving into the home, the daughter learned of the restriction. She informed the man that she planned to put a detached storage shed in her backyard, claiming that the restriction was not enforceable against her. Does the man have the right to enjoin the neighbor's daughter from placing a detached storage shed in her yard?","choices":{"A":"No, because several homeowners in the subdivision have storage sheds in their yards.","B":"No, because there was no horizontal privity between the man and the neighbor.","C":"Yes, because the neighbor conveyed the home to the daughter by gift rather than by sale.","D":"Yes, because the restriction is binding on the daughter as a successor."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes\n\nReal covenants Equitable servitudes\n\n(enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief)\n\nFor burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied\n\nWriting Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice privity privity Notice Notice No notice required An equitable servitude is a covenant to do (ie, affirmative covenant) or not do (ie, negative or restrictive covenant) something on land that is enforceable in equity against the promising parties and their successors in interest. An express equitable servitude requires the following elements: Writing – the covenant is expressed in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, the agreement between the man and the neighbor)\n\nIntent to run – the promising parties intended for the promise to run to (ie, bind) their successors in interest (eg, the agreement says \"enforceable by and against all assignees, heirs, and successors\")\n\nTouch and concern – the covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of both the dominant and servient estates (eg, prohibits placing detached storage sheds in both yards)\n\nNotice – the owner of the servient (ie, burdened) estate has actual, record, or inquiry notice of the covenant (eg, the daughter has record notice because the agreement was recorded)\n\nTherefore, the agreement between the man and the neighbor constitutes a valid equitable servitude, and the man is entitled to enjoin the daughter (neighbor's successor in interest) from placing a detached storage shed in her yard. (Choice A) The other homeowners—unlike the daughter—are free to place storage sheds in their yards because they are not successors in interest bound by the servitude created by the man and the neighbor. (Choice B) Privity is required to create a real covenant that is enforceable by money damages, but it is not required to create an equitable servitude that is enforceable by equitable relief (eg, injunction—as seen here).\n\n(Choice C) The fact that the neighbor conveyed the home to the daughter by gift rather than by sale has no effect on the outcome of this dispute.\n\nEducational objective: There are four elements that must be present to create an express equitable servitude: (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for the covenant to run to successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient- estate owner must have notice of the covenant.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 5.1 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (automatic passage of the burden and benefit with appurtenant land).\n\nRestatement of Property § 539 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of equitable servitudes).","educationalObjective":"There are four elements that must be present to create an express equitable servitude: (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for the covenant to run to successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient- estate owner must have notice of the covenant.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 5.1 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (automatic passage of the burden and benefit with appurtenant land).\n\nRestatement of Property § 539 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of equitable servitudes).","rule":"There are four elements that must be present to create an express equitable servitude: (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for the covenant to run to successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient- estate owner must have notice of the covenant.","irac":{"issue":"Does the man have the right to enjoin the neighbor's daughter from placing a detached storage shed in her yard?","rule":"There are four elements that must be present to create an express equitable servitude: (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for the covenant to run to successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient- estate owner must have notice of the covenant.","analysis":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes\n\nReal covenants Equitable servitudes\n\n(enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief)\n\nFor burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied\n\nWriting Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice privity privity Notice Notice No notice required An equitable servitude is a covenant to do (ie, affirmative covenant) or not do (ie, negative or restrictive covenant) something on land that is enforceable in equity against the promising parties and their successors in interest. An express equitable servitude requires the following elements: Writing – the covenant is expressed in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, the agreement between the man and the neighbor)\n\nIntent to run – the promising parties intended for the promise to run to (ie, bind) their successors in interest (eg, the agreement says \"enforceable by and against all assignees, heirs, and successors\")\n\nTouch and concern – the covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of both the dominant and servient estates (eg, prohibits placing detached storage sheds in both yards)\n\nNotice – the owner of the servient (ie, burdened) estate has actual, record, or inquiry notice of the covenant (eg, the daughter has record notice because the agreement was recorded)\n\nTherefore, the agreement between the man and the neighbor constitutes a valid equitable servitude, and the man is entitled to enjoin the daughter (neighbor's successor in interest) from placing a detached storage shed in her yard. (Choice A) The other homeowners—unlike the daughter—are free to place storage sheds in their yards because they are not successors in interest bound by the servitude created by the man and the neighbor. (Choice B) Privity is required to create a real covenant that is enforceable by money damages, but it is not required to create an equitable servitude that is enforceable by equitable relief (eg, injunction—as seen here).\n\n(Choice C) The fact that the neighbor conveyed the home to the daughter by gift rather than by sale has no effect on the outcome of this dispute.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the restriction is binding on the daughter as a successor."},"questionZh":"一名男子和他的邻居在一个小区的相邻地段拥有房屋。该土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）记录的限制并未禁止独立的储藏棚，该土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）的几位房主已将此类棚屋放置在自家后院。由于男子和邻居都认为这些棚子不雅观，双方书面同意不在各自院子里设置独立的储物棚。他们的协议以可记录的形式起草，并声明所有受让人、继承人和继承人均可强制执行该协议。该协议立即被记录下来。三年后，邻居将自己的房子送给了女儿。搬进家后不久，女儿就得知了这一限制。她告诉该男子，她计划在她的后院建一个独立的储物棚，并声称该限制对她不具有强制执行力。男方是否有权禁止邻居的女儿在她的院子里放置一个独立的储物棚？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）的几位房主在他们的院子里设有储物棚。","B":"不，因为该人和邻居之间不存在横向关系。","C":"是的，因为邻居通过礼物而不是出售的方式将房子转让给了女儿。","D":"是的，因为该限制对作为继承人的女儿具有约束力。"},"explanationZh":"真实契约和公平劳役的要素\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant） 公平的劳役\n\n（可通过金钱赔偿强制执行）（可通过衡平法救济强制执行）\n\n为了负担而运行 为了利益而运行明示暗示\n\n写作 写作 写作 意图创造共同 意图运行 意图运行 意图运行计划 接触与关注 接触与关注 接触与限制性劳役 水平与垂直 有限垂直关注 通知 相互关系 通知 通知 无需通知 衡平法劳役是一种在土地上做（即肯定性契约）或不做（即消极性或限制性契约）某事的契约，可在衡平法上对承诺方及其利益继承人强制执行。明确的公平劳役需要满足以下要素： 书面形式——契约以符合欺诈法规的书面形式表达（例如，男子与邻居之间的协议）\n\n意图竞选——有承诺的各方打算承诺竞选（即约束）其继任者的利益（例如，协议规定“可由所有受让人、继承人和继任者强制执行”）\n\n接触和关注——该契约涉及主导地产和从属地产的使用、享有或占用（例如，禁止在两个院子里放置独立的储藏棚）\n\n通知 – 受役（即负担）遗产的所有者拥有契约的实际、记录或查询通知（例如，女儿拥有记录通知，因为协议已记录）\n\n因此，男子与邻居之间的协议构成有效的公平劳役，男子有权禁止女儿（邻居的利益继承人）在她的院子里放置独立的储藏棚。 （选项 A）其他房主——与女儿不同——可以自由地在自己的院子里放置储物棚，因为他们不是受该男子和邻居创造的奴役所约束的利益继承人。 （选择 B） 需要相互关系来建立可通过金钱赔偿强制执行的真正契约，但不需要建立可通过公平救济（例如，禁令 - 如此处所示）强制执行的公平劳役。\n\n（选项C）邻居以赠与而非出售的方式将房屋转让给女儿的事实对本次纠纷的结果没有影响。\n\n教育目标：要建立明确的公平劳役，必须具备四个要素：(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将契约传给利益继承人，(3) 契约必须涉及并涉及土地，(4) 劳役财产所有者必须了解契约。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 5.1（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（随从属土地自动转移负担和利益）。\n\n财产重述 § 539（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（公平劳役的要素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要建立明确的公平劳役，必须具备四个要素：(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将契约传给利益继承人，(3) 契约必须涉及并涉及土地，(4) 劳役财产所有者必须了解契约。","iracZh":{"issue":"男方是否有权禁止邻居的女儿在她的院子里放置一个独立的储物棚？","rule":"要建立明确的公平劳役，必须具备四个要素：(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将契约传给利益继承人，(3) 契约必须涉及并涉及土地，(4) 劳役财产所有者必须了解契约。","analysis":"真实契约和公平劳役的要素\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant） 公平的劳役\n\n（可通过金钱赔偿强制执行）（可通过衡平法救济强制执行）\n\n为了负担而运行 为了利益而运行明示暗示\n\n写作 写作 写作 意图创造共同 意图运行 意图运行 意图运行计划 接触与关注 接触与关注 接触与限制性劳役 水平与垂直 有限垂直关注 通知 相互关系 通知 通知 无需通知 衡平法劳役是一种在土地上做（即肯定性契约）或不做（即消极性或限制性契约）某事的契约，可在衡平法上对承诺方及其利益继承人强制执行。明确的公平劳役需要满足以下要素： 书面形式——契约以符合欺诈法规的书面形式表达（例如，男子与邻居之间的协议）\n\n意图竞选——有承诺的各方打算承诺竞选（即约束）其继任者的利益（例如，协议规定“可由所有受让人、继承人和继任者强制执行”）\n\n接触和关注——该契约涉及主导地产和从属地产的使用、享有或占用（例如，禁止在两个院子里放置独立的储藏棚）\n\n通知 – 受役（即负担）遗产的所有者拥有契约的实际、记录或查询通知（例如，女儿拥有记录通知，因为协议已记录）\n\n因此，男子与邻居之间的协议构成有效的公平劳役，男子有权禁止女儿（邻居的利益继承人）在她的院子里放置独立的储藏棚。 （选项 A）其他房主——与女儿不同——可以自由地在自己的院子里放置储物棚，因为他们不是受该男子和邻居创造的奴役所约束的利益继承人。 （选择 B） 需要相互关系来建立可通过金钱赔偿强制执行的真正契约，但不需要建立可通过公平救济（例如，禁令 - 如此处所示）强制执行的公平劳役。\n\n（选项C）邻居以赠与而非出售的方式将房屋转让给女儿的事实对本次纠纷的结果没有影响。\n\n教育目标：要建立明确的公平劳役，必须具备四个要素：(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将契约传给利益继承人，(3) 契约必须涉及并涉及土地，(4) 劳役财产所有者必须了解契约。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 5.1（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（随从属土地自动转移负担和利益）。\n\n财产重述 § 539（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（公平劳役的要素）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该限制对作为继承人的女儿具有约束力。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"equitable servitude","ipa":"/ˈɛkwətəbəl sˈɝvʌtˌud/","syllables":"eq·ui·table servi·tude","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"衡平地役权：满足衡平要件、通常以禁令执行的土地使用承诺","contextualMeaning":"衡平地役权：满足衡平要件、通常以禁令执行的土地使用承诺","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"There are four elements that must be present to create an express equitable servitude: (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for the covenant to run to successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient- estate owner must have notice of the covenant.","synonyms":["equitable land-use covenant"]},{"term":"horizontal privity","ipa":"/hˌɔɹʌzˈɑntəl 'priviti/","syllables":"hor·i·zon·tal priv·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"横向关系：原承诺双方在设立契约时具有规定的土地交易关系","contextualMeaning":"横向关系：原承诺双方在设立契约时具有规定的土地交易关系","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because there was no horizontal privity between the man and the neighbor.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"real covenant","ipa":"/ɹˈil kˈəvənənt/","syllables":"real covenant","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"土地实质契约：随土地转移并可请求金钱赔偿执行的书面土地承诺","contextualMeaning":"土地实质契约：随土地转移并可请求金钱赔偿执行的书面土地承诺","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes Real covenants Equitable servitudes (enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief) For burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied Writing Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice pri","synonyms":["covenant running with the land"]},{"term":"recordable","ipa":"/ɹɪkˈɔɹdəbəl/","syllables":"record·able","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 可记录的, 可录音的, 适于记录的, 适于录音的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“recordable”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Their agreement was drafted in recordable form and stated that it was enforceable by and against all assignees, heirs, and successors.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"dominant","ipa":"/dˈɑmənənt/","syllables":"dom·i·nant","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 占优势的, 支配的；[医] 优性的, 显性的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“dominant”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:dominants","sourceSentence":"An express equitable servitude requires the following elements: Writing – the covenant is expressed in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, the agreement between the man and the neighbor) Intent to run – the promising parties intended for the promise to run to (ie, bind) their successors in interest (eg, the agreement says \"enforceable by and against all assignees, heirs, and successors\") Touch and con","synonyms":["dominant allele","prevailing","prevalent","predominant"]},{"term":"servient","ipa":"/'sə:viənt/","syllables":"servient","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"a. [律]从属的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“servient”","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"There are four elements that must be present to create an express equitable servitude: (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for the covenant to run to successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient- estate owner must have notice of the covenant.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":153,"sourceQuestionPage":449,"sourceQuestionPages":[449],"sourceExplanationPages":[450,451],"sourceAnswerPages":[450,451],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-154","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Real Property","question":"When a buyer and a seller executed a valid contract for the sale of a house, the buyer gave the seller $1,000 as earnest money. The contract noted that the earnest money tendered would be applied to the purchase price at the time of sale but was silent as to remedies in the event of any default. Just before the closing, the buyer lost her job. The buyer told the seller that she could no longer purchase the house and asked him to return the earnest money. The seller accurately told the buyer that the seller's actual losses exceeded the amount of the earnest money; that if the seller sued the buyer for damages, he would receive a minimum of $5,000; and that it would be difficult for him to sell the house in the current market. Should the buyer get the earnest money back?","choices":{"A":"No, because the contract was silent regarding remedies.","B":"No, because the seller's actual losses exceeded the amount of the earnest money.","C":"Yes, because the buyer is no longer ready, willing, and able to purchase the house.","D":"Yes, because the reason for the buyer's default was not anticipated."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Seller's remedies\n\nRescission & Rescind (ie, cancel) agreement & recover possession of property restitution Rental value & physical damages are recoverable Buyer not liable for purchase price Specific Seek an injunction ordering buyer to pay agreed purchase price performance May be unavailable if money damages provide adequate compensation & property is not unique eg, condominium identical to other units in complex Damages Loss of bargain = contract price – market value at time of breach Liquidated damages = amount designated in the sales contract as compensation for breach Retain buyer's deposit (up to amount of seller's actual damages) Incidental & consequential damages that were contemplated during sales contract or reasonably foreseeable Earnest money is a deposit paid by a buyer to show a good-faith intent to complete the transaction. Once accepted, the seller cannot recover other damages for breach. That is because earnest money typically constitutes liquidated damages—ie, an amount agreed upon during good-faith negotiations to reasonably compensate the injured party. These damages are recoverable unless they constitute a penalty (eg, when liquidated damages far exceed actual damages). In such a case, the seller can only recover actual damages. Here, the buyer gave the seller $1,000 as earnest money. The contract was silent as to remedies, so the $1,000 constitutes liquidated damages (Choice A). The buyer breached the contract when she reported that she could no longer purchase the house, causing the seller to sustain losses. Since those actual losses exceed the amount of the earnest money, the liquidated damages provision is likely enforceable. As a result, the seller should receive the full $1,000 and the buyer should not receive any earnest money back. (Choice C) The seller is entitled to the $1,000 because the buyer is no longer ready, willing, and able to purchase the house. This fact would not allow the buyer to get the earnest money back. (Choice D) Unless otherwise stated, liquidated damages will be paid to the injured party as compensation for any breach, regardless of whether the breach was anticipated.\n\nEducational objective: Earnest money typically constitutes liquidated damages, which are generally recoverable upon the buyer's breach. But if liquidated damages are deemed a penalty (eg, when they far exceed actual damages), then the seller may recover only actual damages.\n\nReferences\n22 Am. Jur. 2d Damages § 540 (2020) (earnest deposit considered liquidated damages).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 478 (2020) (seller's recovery of liquidated damages).","educationalObjective":"Earnest money typically constitutes liquidated damages, which are generally recoverable upon the buyer's breach. But if liquidated damages are deemed a penalty (eg, when they far exceed actual damages), then the seller may recover only actual damages.","references":"22 Am. Jur. 2d Damages § 540 (2020) (earnest deposit considered liquidated damages).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 478 (2020) (seller's recovery of liquidated damages).","rule":"Earnest money typically constitutes liquidated damages, which are generally recoverable upon the buyer's breach. But if liquidated damages are deemed a penalty (eg, when they far exceed actual damages), then the seller may recover only actual damages.","irac":{"issue":"Should the buyer get the earnest money back?","rule":"Earnest money typically constitutes liquidated damages, which are generally recoverable upon the buyer's breach. But if liquidated damages are deemed a penalty (eg, when they far exceed actual damages), then the seller may recover only actual damages.","analysis":"Seller's remedies\n\nRescission & Rescind (ie, cancel) agreement & recover possession of property restitution Rental value & physical damages are recoverable Buyer not liable for purchase price Specific Seek an injunction ordering buyer to pay agreed purchase price performance May be unavailable if money damages provide adequate compensation & property is not unique eg, condominium identical to other units in complex Damages Loss of bargain = contract price – market value at time of breach Liquidated damages = amount designated in the sales contract as compensation for breach Retain buyer's deposit (up to amount of seller's actual damages) Incidental & consequential damages that were contemplated during sales contract or reasonably foreseeable Earnest money is a deposit paid by a buyer to show a good-faith intent to complete the transaction. Once accepted, the seller cannot recover other damages for breach. That is because earnest money typically constitutes liquidated damages—ie, an amount agreed upon during good-faith negotiations to reasonably compensate the injured party. These damages are recoverable unless they constitute a penalty (eg, when liquidated damages far exceed actual damages). In such a case, the seller can only recover actual damages. Here, the buyer gave the seller $1,000 as earnest money. The contract was silent as to remedies, so the $1,000 constitutes liquidated damages (Choice A). The buyer breached the contract when she reported that she could no longer purchase the house, causing the seller to sustain losses. Since those actual losses exceed the amount of the earnest money, the liquidated damages provision is likely enforceable. As a result, the seller should receive the full $1,000 and the buyer should not receive any earnest money back. (Choice C) The seller is entitled to the $1,000 because the buyer is no longer ready, willing, and able to purchase the house. This fact would not allow the buyer to get the earnest money back. (Choice D) Unless otherwise stated, liquidated damages will be paid to the injured party as compensation for any breach, regardless of whether the breach was anticipated.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the seller's actual losses exceeded the amount of the earnest money."},"questionZh":"当买家和卖家签订有效的房屋买卖合同时，买家向卖家支付了1000美元的保证金。合同指出，保证金将用于出售时的购买价格，但没有提及任何违约情况下的补救措施。就在成交前，买家失业了。买家告诉卖家，她不能再购买该房子，并要求他退还定金。卖方如实地告知买方，卖方实际损失超过了定金金额；如果卖方起诉买方要求赔偿，他将获得至少 5,000 美元的赔偿；而且在目前的市场上他很难卖掉房子。买家应该拿回定金吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为合同没有提及补救措施。","B":"不能，因为卖家的实际损失超过了定金的金额。","C":"是的，因为买家不再准备好、愿意、也没有能力购买房子。","D":"是的，因为买方违约的原因是没有预料到的。"},"explanationZh":"卖家的补救措施\n\n解除和解除（即取消）协议并恢复财产所有权归还 租金价值和有形损害可收回 买方不对购买价格承担具体责任 寻求禁令，命令买方支付约定的购买价格履约 如果金钱损害提供足够的赔偿且财产不是唯一的，例如，公寓与综合体中的其他单元相同，则可能不可用 损害赔偿 议价损失 = 合同价格 – 违约时的市场价值 违约赔偿金 = 销售合同中指定的违约赔偿金额 保留买方押金（最高可达卖方实际损失的金额） 销售合同期间预期的或可合理预见的附带和间接损失 保证金是买方为表明完成交易的善意意图而支付的押金。一旦接受，卖方将无法追偿因违约而造成的其他损失。这是因为保证金通常构成违约金，即在善意谈判中商定的用于合理补偿受害方的金额。这些损害赔偿是可以追偿的，除非它们构成处罚（例如，当违约损害赔偿远远超过实际损害赔偿时）。在这种情况下，卖家只能赔偿实际损失。在这里，买方给了卖方1000美元作为保证金。合同没有提及补救措施，因此 1,000 美元构成违约金（选项 A）。买方因无法再购买该房屋而违约，导致卖方蒙受损失。由于这些实际损失超过了保证金的金额，因此违约金条款很可能是可执行的。因此，卖方应收到全额 1,000 美元，而买方不应收到任何保证金。 （选项 C） 卖方有权获得 1,000 美元，因为买方不再准备好、愿意且无法购买该房屋。这一事实不允许买方拿回保证金。 （选项D）除非另有说明，违约金将支付给受害方作为任何违约行为的补偿，无论违约行为是否是预期的。\n\n教育目标：定金通常构成违约金，通常可在买方违约时追回。但如果违约金被视为罚款（例如，当其远远超过实际损失时），那么卖方只能追偿实际损失。\n\n参考文献\n上午 22 点尤尔。 2d 损害赔偿 § 540 (2020)（保证金视为违约金）。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 478 (2020)（卖方追偿违约金）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"定金通常构成违约金，通常可以在买方违约时收回。但如果违约金被视为罚款（例如，当其远远超过实际损失时），那么卖方只能追偿实际损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"买家应该拿回定金吗？","rule":"定金通常构成违约金，通常可以在买方违约时收回。但如果违约金被视为罚款（例如，当其远远超过实际损失时），那么卖方只能追偿实际损失。","analysis":"卖家的补救措施\n\n解除和解除（即取消）协议并恢复财产所有权归还 租金价值和有形损害可收回 买方不对购买价格承担具体责任 寻求禁令，命令买方支付约定的购买价格履约 如果金钱损害提供足够的赔偿且财产不是唯一的，例如，公寓与综合体中的其他单元相同，则可能不可用 损害赔偿 议价损失 = 合同价格 – 违约时的市场价值 违约赔偿金 = 销售合同中指定的违约赔偿金额 保留买方押金（最高可达卖方实际损失的金额） 销售合同期间预期的或可合理预见的附带和间接损失 保证金是买方为表明完成交易的善意意图而支付的押金。一旦接受，卖方将无法追偿因违约而造成的其他损失。这是因为保证金通常构成违约金，即在善意谈判中商定的用于合理补偿受害方的金额。这些损害赔偿是可以追偿的，除非它们构成处罚（例如，当违约损害赔偿远远超过实际损害赔偿时）。在这种情况下，卖家只能赔偿实际损失。在这里，买方给了卖方1000美元作为保证金。合同没有提及补救措施，因此 1,000 美元构成违约金（选项 A）。买方因无法再购买该房屋而违约，导致卖方蒙受损失。由于这些实际损失超过了保证金的金额，因此违约金条款很可能是可执行的。因此，卖方应收到全额 1,000 美元，而买方不应收到任何保证金。 （选项 C） 卖方有权获得 1,000 美元，因为买方不再准备好、愿意且无法购买该房屋。这一事实不允许买方拿回保证金。 （选项D）除非另有说明，违约金将支付给受害方作为任何违约行为的补偿，无论违约行为是否是预期的。\n\n教育目标：定金通常构成违约金，通常可在买方违约时追回。但如果违约金被视为罚款（例如，当其远远超过实际损失时），那么卖方只能追偿实际损失。\n\n参考文献\n上午 22 点尤尔。 2d 损害赔偿 § 540 (2020)（保证金视为违约金）。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 478 (2020)（卖方追偿违约金）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不能，因为卖家的实际损失超过了定金的金额。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":154,"sourceQuestionPage":452,"sourceQuestionPages":[452],"sourceExplanationPages":[453,454],"sourceAnswerPages":[453,454],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-155","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Covenants & Servitudes","question":"A woman owned a house on a lot abutting a public street. Six months ago, the city validly revised its zoning ordinances and placed the woman's lot and the surrounding lots abutting the public street from the north in a zone limited to residential use; the lots abutting the public street on the south side were zoned for both residential and light business use. The woman asked the city's zoning appeals board to approve her proposal to operate a court-reporting service from her house. This type of use would be permitted on the south side of the public street and, in fact, one such business has existed there for several years. The board approved the woman's proposal. Why?","choices":{"A":"A variance was granted.","B":"The doctrine of amortization applied.","C":"The doctrine of change of circumstances applied.","D":"The woman's use of her house was a nonconforming use."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A variance is created when a local government (often a zoning board) gives a landowner permission to deviate from a zoning requirement. There are two types of variances: use variances – allow land to be useddifferently than the currently zoned use (eg, commercial use in residential area) and\n\narea variances – allow structures on the land to lookdifferent than structures on the rest of the zoned area (eg, height, lot size, building placement).\n\nVariances are often granted if the landowner can show that (1) enforcement of a zoning requirement would cause the landowner unnecessary hardship* regarding the use of the land and (2) the variance would not be contrary to public interest (eg, public safety, comprehensive planning). Here, the revised ordinance placed the woman's house in a zone limited to residential use. The enforcement of this ordinance would cause hardship to the woman's use of the land by preventing the operation of a court-reporting service from her house. This hardship would be unnecessary, and a variance would have little impact on public interest, because light business use is permitted across the street. As a result, the board likely approved the woman's proposal by granting a variance. *Courts often reject landowners' claims of unnecessary hardship when the hardship is self- imposed or the landowner can still make use of the property without a variance. (Choices B & D) The doctrine of amortization applies to a nonconforming use—ie, one that lawfully existed before but is now prohibited by a zoning law. Since the woman's court- reporting service does not yet exist, this doctrine does not apply. But even if it did, it would allow the government to terminate (not approve) a nonconforming use after a reasonable time that allows a landowner to gradually phase out or recoup an investment in that use. (Choice C) The doctrine of changed circumstances (ie, changed conditions) terminates or prevents the enforcement of a real covenant or equitable servitude that no longer provides its intended benefit—but it does not apply to zoning ordinances.\n\nEducational objective: Variances are often granted if (1) enforcement of a zoning requirement would cause the landowner unnecessary hardship regarding the use of the land and (2) a variance would not be contrary to a public interest.\n\nReferences\n83 Am. Jur. 2d Zoning & Planning §§ 706–93 (2019) (providing an overview on variances).\n\nE & F Assocs., LLC v. Zoning Bd. of Appeals, 127 A.3d 986, 990 (Conn. 2015) (defining variance and identifying considerations in granting a variance).","educationalObjective":"Variances are often granted if (1) enforcement of a zoning requirement would cause the landowner unnecessary hardship regarding the use of the land and (2) a variance would not be contrary to a public interest.","references":"83 Am. Jur. 2d Zoning & Planning §§ 706–93 (2019) (providing an overview on variances).\n\nE & F Assocs., LLC v. Zoning Bd. of Appeals, 127 A.3d 986, 990 (Conn. 2015) (defining variance and identifying considerations in granting a variance).","rule":"Variances are often granted if (1) enforcement of a zoning requirement would cause the landowner unnecessary hardship regarding the use of the land and (2) a variance would not be contrary to a public interest.","irac":{"issue":"Why?","rule":"Variances are often granted if (1) enforcement of a zoning requirement would cause the landowner unnecessary hardship regarding the use of the land and (2) a variance would not be contrary to a public interest.","analysis":"A variance is created when a local government (often a zoning board) gives a landowner permission to deviate from a zoning requirement. There are two types of variances: use variances – allow land to be useddifferently than the currently zoned use (eg, commercial use in residential area) and\n\narea variances – allow structures on the land to lookdifferent than structures on the rest of the zoned area (eg, height, lot size, building placement).\n\nVariances are often granted if the landowner can show that (1) enforcement of a zoning requirement would cause the landowner unnecessary hardship* regarding the use of the land and (2) the variance would not be contrary to public interest (eg, public safety, comprehensive planning). Here, the revised ordinance placed the woman's house in a zone limited to residential use. The enforcement of this ordinance would cause hardship to the woman's use of the land by preventing the operation of a court-reporting service from her house. This hardship would be unnecessary, and a variance would have little impact on public interest, because light business use is permitted across the street. As a result, the board likely approved the woman's proposal by granting a variance. *Courts often reject landowners' claims of unnecessary hardship when the hardship is self- imposed or the landowner can still make use of the property without a variance. (Choices B & D) The doctrine of amortization applies to a nonconforming use—ie, one that lawfully existed before but is now prohibited by a zoning law. Since the woman's court- reporting service does not yet exist, this doctrine does not apply. But even if it did, it would allow the government to terminate (not approve) a nonconforming use after a reasonable time that allows a landowner to gradually phase out or recoup an investment in that use. (Choice C) The doctrine of changed circumstances (ie, changed conditions) terminates or prevents the enforcement of a real covenant or equitable servitude that no longer provides its intended benefit—but it does not apply to zoning ordinances.","conclusion":"Answer A: A variance was granted."},"questionZh":"一名妇女在紧邻公共街道的一块土地上拥有一所房子。六个月前，该市有效地修改了土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例，将女性用地和北面紧邻公共街道的周边地块划入仅限住宅使用的区域；南侧毗邻公共街道的地块被划为住宅和轻型商业用途。该女子请求该市土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）上诉委员会批准她在她家中提供法庭报告服务的提议。这种类型的用途在公共街道的南侧是允许的，事实上，这样的业务已经在那里存在了好几年了。董事会批准了这位女士的提议。为什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"批准了差异。","B":"适用摊销原则。","C":"适用情势变更原则。","D":"该妇女对其房屋的使用属于不合规用途。"},"explanationZh":"当地方政府（通常是土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会）允许土地所有者偏离土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）要求时，就会产生差异。有两种类型的差异： 使用差异 – 允许土地的使用方式与当前土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）用途不同（例如，住宅区的商业用途）和\n\n面积差异——允许土地上的建筑物看起来与其他土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）上的建筑物不同（例如，高度、地块大小、建筑物布局）。\n\n如果土地所有者能够证明 (1) 执行土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）要求将导致土地所有者在土地使用方面遭受不必要的困难*，并且 (2) 差异不会违背公共利益（例如公共安全、综合规划），则通常会批准差异。此处，修订后的法令将妇女的房屋置于仅限住宅用途的区域。该法令的执行将阻碍该妇女在其家中提供法庭报告服务，从而给该妇女使用土地带来困难。这种困难是不必要的，而且差异对公共利益影响不大，因为街对面允许轻度商业用途。因此，董事会很可能通过给予差异来批准这位女士的提议。 *当困难是自己造成的，或者土地所有者仍然可以在没有差异的情况下使用财产时，法院经常会驳回土地所有者关于不必要的困难的主张。 （选择 B 和 D） 摊销原则适用于不符合规定的用途，即以前合法存在但现在被土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法禁止的用途。由于该妇女的法庭报告服务尚不存在，因此该原则不适用。但即使这样做，也将允许政府在合理的时间后终止（不批准）不合格的用途，从而允许土地所有者逐步淘汰或收回对该用途的投资。 （选择 C） 情况改变（即条件改变）的原则终止或阻止了不再提供其预期利益的真正契约或公平劳役的执行，但它不适用于土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 执行土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）要求会导致土地所有者在土地使用方面遭受不必要的困难，并且 (2) 差异不会违背公共利益，则通常会批准差异。\n\n参考文献\n83 上午。尤尔。 2d 土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）和规划 §§ 706–93 (2019)（提供差异概述）。\n\nE & F Assocs., LLC 诉 Zoning Bd. of Appeals, 127 A.3d 986, 990 (Conn. 2015)（定义差异并确定批准差异时的考虑因素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果 (1) 执行土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）要求会导致土地所有者在土地使用方面出现不必要的困难，并且 (2) 差异不会违背公共利益，则通常会批准差异。","iracZh":{"issue":"为什么？","rule":"如果 (1) 执行土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）要求会导致土地所有者在土地使用方面出现不必要的困难，并且 (2) 差异不会违背公共利益，则通常会批准差异。","analysis":"当地方政府（通常是土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会）允许土地所有者偏离土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）要求时，就会产生差异。有两种类型的差异： 使用差异 – 允许土地的使用方式与当前土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）用途不同（例如，住宅区的商业用途）和\n\n面积差异——允许土地上的建筑物看起来与其他土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）上的建筑物不同（例如，高度、地块大小、建筑物布局）。\n\n如果土地所有者能够证明 (1) 执行土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）要求将导致土地所有者在土地使用方面遭受不必要的困难*，并且 (2) 差异不会违背公共利益（例如公共安全、综合规划），则通常会批准差异。此处，修订后的法令将妇女的房屋置于仅限住宅用途的区域。该法令的执行将阻碍该妇女在其家中提供法庭报告服务，从而给该妇女使用土地带来困难。这种困难是不必要的，而且差异对公共利益影响不大，因为街对面允许轻度商业用途。因此，董事会很可能通过给予差异来批准这位女士的提议。 *当困难是自己造成的，或者土地所有者仍然可以在没有差异的情况下使用财产时，法院经常会驳回土地所有者关于不必要的困难的主张。 （选择 B 和 D） 摊销原则适用于不符合规定的用途，即以前合法存在但现在被土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法禁止的用途。由于该妇女的法庭报告服务尚不存在，因此该原则不适用。但即使这样做，也将允许政府在合理的时间后终止（不批准）不合格的用途，从而允许土地所有者逐步淘汰或收回对该用途的投资。 （选择 C） 情况改变（即条件改变）的原则终止或阻止了不再提供其预期利益的真正契约或公平劳役的执行，但它不适用于土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 执行土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）要求会导致土地所有者在土地使用方面遭受不必要的困难，并且 (2) 差异不会违背公共利益，则通常会批准差异。\n\n参考文献\n83 上午。尤尔。 2d 土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）和规划 §§ 706–93 (2019)（提供差异概述）。\n\nE & F Assocs., LLC 诉 Zoning Bd. of Appeals, 127 A.3d 986, 990 (Conn. 2015)（定义差异并确定批准差异时的考虑因素）。","conclusion":"答案 A：批准了差异。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"nonconforming use","ipa":"/'nɔnkən'fɔ:miŋ jˈus/","syllables":"non·con·form·ing use","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"既有不合规用途：新分区规则生效前合法存在、获有限继续保护的用途","contextualMeaning":"既有不合规用途：新分区规则生效前合法存在、获有限继续保护的用途","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The woman's use of her house was a nonconforming use.","synonyms":["grandfathered use"]},{"term":"area variance","ipa":"/ˈɛɹiə vˈɛɹiəns/","syllables":"area vari·ance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"面积变更许可：对退界、高度或面积等尺寸要求作的分区例外","contextualMeaning":"面积变更许可：对退界、高度或面积等尺寸要求作的分区例外","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"There are two types of variances: use variances – allow land to be useddifferently than the currently zoned use (eg, commercial use in residential area) and area variances – allow structures on the land to lookdifferent than structures on the rest of the zoned area (eg, height, lot size, building placement).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"use variance","ipa":"/jˈus vˈɛɹiəns/","syllables":"use vari·ance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"用途变更许可：因特殊困难而允许禁用用途的分区例外","contextualMeaning":"用途变更许可：因特殊困难而允许禁用用途的分区例外","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"There are two types of variances: use variances – allow land to be useddifferently than the currently zoned use (eg, commercial use in residential area) and area variances – allow structures on the land to lookdifferent than structures on the rest of the zoned area (eg, height, lot size, building placement).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-f210601bccbb83ab"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":155,"sourceQuestionPage":455,"sourceQuestionPages":[455],"sourceExplanationPages":[456,457],"sourceAnswerPages":[456,457],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-156","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Nuisance, Zoning & Support","question":"Ten years ago, a couple bought a building and moved into its second-floor apartment with their teenage daughter. The couple operated a shoe store on the first floor of the building for many years. When the couple purchased the building, the area was predominantly rural and was zoned for nonresidential use. The municipality's zoning is cumulative. Five years ago, the municipality rezoned the area to single-family residential use. The daughter was not aware of this change because she was away at college. Recently, the daughter inherited the building from her parents. The daughter immediately moved into the apartment and took over the operation of the shoe store on the first floor. The daughter has learned that a developer is planning to build a large residential community in the area surrounding her building. The daughter has asked her lawyer for advice regarding her ability to continue operating the shoe store. Should the lawyer advise the daughter that she can continue to operate her shoe store?","choices":{"A":"No, because the nonconforming use of the building terminated when the daughter's parents died.","B":"No, but the municipality must pay her reasonable compensation for her loss resulting from the change in zoning.","C":"Yes, because the shoe store is a nonconforming use.","D":"Yes, because the zoning is cumulative and the building is also used for single-family residential purposes."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A nonconforming use arises when property was used in a lawful manner before a zoning law was enacted or amended and that use continues even though it is now prohibited by the zoning law. When this occurs, the owner of the nonconforming use has a \"grandfathered right\" to continue that use until (1) it is enlarged, changed, or abandoned or (2) if provided in the zoning law, the amortization period has passed or the nonconforming use has not been registered. Here, the building was in an area zoned for nonresidential use when the couple began to lawfully operate a shoe store. When the area was rezoned to single-family residential use, the shoe store became a nonconforming use. And since the shoe store was continuously operated by the couple and then their daughter—and there is no indication that the zoning law has an amortization period or registration requirement—the daughter should be advised that she can continue to operate her shoe store (Choice A). (Choice B) Under the Fifth Amendment takings clause, the municipality would have been required to pay the daughter reasonable compensation had the zoning law taken her private property for public use (eg, by depriving her property of its economic value). But since the law does not affect the daughter's operation of the shoe store, no compensation is required. (Choice D) Zoning is cumulative when it allows a higher use to exist in a lower use zone, as illustrated above. Here, the area was rezoned to the highest use (residential), so the shoe store's lower use (commercial) cannot exist in that zone. This is true even though the building also has a residential use since that other use does not change the commercial character of the store.\n\nEducational objective: A nonconforming use may continue until (1) the owner enlarges, changes, or abandons that use or (2) if provided in the zoning law, an amortization period has passed or the use has not been registered.\n\nReferences\nPa. Nw. Distribs., Inc. v. Zoning Hearing Bd., 584 A.2d 1372, 1375–76 (Pa. 1991) (describing the right to and termination of a nonconforming use).\n\n83 Am. Jur. 2d Zoning & Planning §§ 524–93 (2019).","educationalObjective":"A nonconforming use may continue until (1) the owner enlarges, changes, or abandons that use or (2) if provided in the zoning law, an amortization period has passed or the use has not been registered.","references":"Pa. Nw. Distribs., Inc. v. Zoning Hearing Bd., 584 A.2d 1372, 1375–76 (Pa. 1991) (describing the right to and termination of a nonconforming use).\n\n83 Am. Jur. 2d Zoning & Planning §§ 524–93 (2019).","rule":"A nonconforming use may continue until (1) the owner enlarges, changes, or abandons that use or (2) if provided in the zoning law, an amortization period has passed or the use has not been registered.","irac":{"issue":"Should the lawyer advise the daughter that she can continue to operate her shoe store?","rule":"A nonconforming use may continue until (1) the owner enlarges, changes, or abandons that use or (2) if provided in the zoning law, an amortization period has passed or the use has not been registered.","analysis":"A nonconforming use arises when property was used in a lawful manner before a zoning law was enacted or amended and that use continues even though it is now prohibited by the zoning law. When this occurs, the owner of the nonconforming use has a \"grandfathered right\" to continue that use until (1) it is enlarged, changed, or abandoned or (2) if provided in the zoning law, the amortization period has passed or the nonconforming use has not been registered. Here, the building was in an area zoned for nonresidential use when the couple began to lawfully operate a shoe store. When the area was rezoned to single-family residential use, the shoe store became a nonconforming use. And since the shoe store was continuously operated by the couple and then their daughter—and there is no indication that the zoning law has an amortization period or registration requirement—the daughter should be advised that she can continue to operate her shoe store (Choice A). (Choice B) Under the Fifth Amendment takings clause, the municipality would have been required to pay the daughter reasonable compensation had the zoning law taken her private property for public use (eg, by depriving her property of its economic value). But since the law does not affect the daughter's operation of the shoe store, no compensation is required. (Choice D) Zoning is cumulative when it allows a higher use to exist in a lower use zone, as illustrated above. Here, the area was rezoned to the highest use (residential), so the shoe store's lower use (commercial) cannot exist in that zone. This is true even though the building also has a residential use since that other use does not change the commercial character of the store.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the shoe store is a nonconforming use."},"questionZh":"十年前，一对夫妇买了一座大楼，并带着十几岁的女儿搬进了二楼的公寓。这对夫妇在大楼一楼经营一家鞋店多年。当这对夫妇购买这栋建筑时，该地区主要是农村地区，被划为非住宅用途。市政府的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）是累积的。五年前，市政府将该地区重新规划为单户住宅用途。女儿并不知道这一变化，因为她正在上大学。最近，女儿从父母那里继承了这栋大楼。女儿立即搬进了公寓，并接管了一楼鞋店的经营。女儿得知一名开发商正计划在她的大楼周围地区建造一个大型住宅社区。女儿已向律师寻求有关她继续经营鞋店的能力的建议。律师是否应该建议女儿继续经营鞋店？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为当女儿的父母去世时，该建筑物的不合规使用终止了。","B":"不可以，但市政府必须就土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更造成的损失向她支付合理的赔偿。","C":"是的，因为鞋店是不合格用途。","D":"是的，因为土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）是累积的，而且该建筑也用于单户住宅。"},"explanationZh":"如果在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法颁布或修订之前以合法方式使用财产，并且即使现在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法禁止使用，但这种使用仍在继续，则出现不符合规定的使用。发生这种情况时，不合格用途的所有者拥有继续使用该用途的“祖父权利”，直到 (1) 扩大、更改或放弃，或 (2) 如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法有规定，则摊销期已过或不合格用途尚未登记。当这对夫妇开始合法经营一家鞋店时，该建筑位于非住宅区。当该地区被重新规划为单户住宅用途时，鞋店就成为了不合格用途。由于鞋店是由这对夫妇和他们的女儿持续经营的——并且没有迹象表明土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法有摊销期或注册要求——因此应告知女儿她可以继续经营她的鞋店（选项 A）。 （选项B）根据第五修正案征用条款，如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法将女儿的私人财产用于公共用途（例如，剥夺其财产的经济价值），市政府将需要向女儿支付合理的补偿。但由于法律不影响女儿经营鞋店，因此不需要赔偿。 （选择 D）当土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）允许较低使用区域中存在较高使用时，土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）是累积的，如上所示。在这里，该区域被重新划分为最高用途（住宅），因此鞋店的较低用途（商业）不能存在于该区域。即使该建筑还具有住宅用途，情况也是如此，因为其他用途不会改变商店的商业特征。\n\n教育目标：不符合规定的使用可以继续，直到 (1) 所有者扩大、改变或放弃该使用，或 (2) 如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法有规定，摊销期已过或该使用尚未注册。\n\n参考文献\n宾夕法尼亚州西北Distribs., Inc. v. Zoning Hearing Bd., 584 A.2d 1372, 1375–76 (Pa. 1991)（描述不合格使用的权利和终止）。\n\n83 上午。尤尔。二维土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）和规划 §§ 524–93 (2019)。","educationalObjectiveZh":"不符合规定的使用可以继续，直到 (1) 所有者扩大、改变或放弃该使用，或 (2) 如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法有规定，摊销期已过或该使用尚未注册。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师是否应该建议女儿继续经营鞋店？","rule":"不符合规定的使用可以继续，直到 (1) 所有者扩大、改变或放弃该使用，或 (2) 如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法有规定，摊销期已过或该使用尚未注册。","analysis":"如果在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法颁布或修订之前以合法方式使用财产，并且即使现在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法禁止使用，但这种使用仍在继续，则出现不符合规定的使用。发生这种情况时，不合格用途的所有者拥有继续使用该用途的“祖父权利”，直到 (1) 扩大、更改或放弃，或 (2) 如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法有规定，则摊销期已过或不合格用途尚未登记。当这对夫妇开始合法经营一家鞋店时，该建筑位于非住宅区。当该地区被重新规划为单户住宅用途时，鞋店就成为了不合格用途。由于鞋店是由这对夫妇和他们的女儿持续经营的——并且没有迹象表明土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法有摊销期或注册要求——因此应告知女儿她可以继续经营她的鞋店（选项 A）。 （选项B）根据第五修正案征用条款，如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法将女儿的私人财产用于公共用途（例如，剥夺其财产的经济价值），市政府将需要向女儿支付合理的补偿。但由于法律不影响女儿经营鞋店，因此不需要赔偿。 （选择 D）当土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）允许较低使用区域中存在较高使用时，土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）是累积的，如上所示。在这里，该区域被重新划分为最高用途（住宅），因此鞋店的较低用途（商业）不能存在于该区域。即使该建筑还具有住宅用途，情况也是如此，因为其他用途不会改变商店的商业特征。\n\n教育目标：不符合规定的使用可以继续，直到 (1) 所有者扩大、改变或放弃该使用，或 (2) 如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法有规定，摊销期已过或该使用尚未注册。\n\n参考文献\n宾夕法尼亚州西北Distribs., Inc. v. Zoning Hearing Bd., 584 A.2d 1372, 1375–76 (Pa. 1991)（描述不合格使用的权利和终止）。\n\n83 上午。尤尔。二维土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）和规划 §§ 524–93 (2019)。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为鞋店是不合格用途。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-9155e085ad086c9a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":156,"sourceQuestionPage":458,"sourceQuestionPages":[458],"sourceExplanationPages":[459,460],"sourceAnswerPages":[459,460],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-157","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"An unmarried couple had been living together in an apartment for several years when they decided to purchase a starter home as tenants in common. In lieu of drafting wills, the couple verbally agreed that if either of them died, the other should own the entire home. The couple was later involved in a serious plane crash. The man died instantly, and the woman died one day later. Each was survived by a single heir. Common law marriage is not recognized in the jurisdiction in which the home is situated. The only applicable statute is the statute of frauds. In an appropriate action between the couple's heirs to determine ownership of the home, how will the court likely rule?","choices":{"A":"Each heir owns an undivided one-half interest in the home because the couple died in the same plane crash.","B":"Each heir owns an undivided one-half interest in the home because the statute of frauds applies.","C":"The woman's heir owns the entire home because the couple reasonably relied upon their verbal agreement and never made wills.","D":"The woman's heir owns the entire home because the deed will be reformed to reflect the subsequent verbal agreement."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A tenancy in common is a concurrent estate in which each co-tenant holds a separate share of the property and the right to possess the entire property with no right of survivorship. But a right of survivorship can be created by an agreement that satisfies the statute of frauds—ie, the agreement must: be in writing be signed by the parties against whom it is to be enforced and contain all essential terms. A right of survivorship causes a co-tenant's property interest to disappear upon his/her death and allows the remaining co-tenants' interests to expand proportionately. Here, the couple (tenants in common) agreed that whoever survived the other would own the entire home (right of survivorship). But since their agreement was verbal, it is unenforceable under the statute of frauds. Therefore, when the man died, his one-half interest transferred to his sole heir—not the woman. And when the woman died one day later, her one-half interest transferred to her sole heir. (Choice A) Under the Uniform Simultaneous Death Act, when two or more persons die without a will and within 120 hours of each other, each is considered to have predeceased the others. This act does not apply here since the only applicable statute is the statute of frauds. (Choice C) The doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to enforce an otherwise unenforceable promise upon which it reasonably and detrimentally relied. But reliance on a conditional promise (eg, a promise of property conditioned upon survivorship) is unreasonable. Therefore, the couple's reliance upon the right-of-survivorship agreement does not make it enforceable. (Choice D) Reformation is an equitable remedy that is used to correct mistakes in a deed. Since the deed here accurately reflects the couple's apparent agreement to create a tenancy in common, the court will not reform it to reflect the later verbal agreement.\n\nEducational objective: A right of survivorship can be created by an agreement that satisfies the statute of frauds— ie, (1) is in writing, (2) is signed by the parties against whom it will be enforced, and (3) contains all essential terms.","educationalObjective":"A right of survivorship can be created by an agreement that satisfies the statute of frauds— ie, (1) is in writing, (2) is signed by the parties against whom it will be enforced, and (3) contains all essential terms.","references":"","rule":"A right of survivorship can be created by an agreement that satisfies the statute of frauds— ie, (1) is in writing, (2) is signed by the parties against whom it will be enforced, and (3) contains all essential terms.","irac":{"issue":"In an appropriate action between the couple's heirs to determine ownership of the home, how will the court likely rule?","rule":"A right of survivorship can be created by an agreement that satisfies the statute of frauds— ie, (1) is in writing, (2) is signed by the parties against whom it will be enforced, and (3) contains all essential terms.","analysis":"A tenancy in common is a concurrent estate in which each co-tenant holds a separate share of the property and the right to possess the entire property with no right of survivorship. But a right of survivorship can be created by an agreement that satisfies the statute of frauds—ie, the agreement must: be in writing be signed by the parties against whom it is to be enforced and contain all essential terms. A right of survivorship causes a co-tenant's property interest to disappear upon his/her death and allows the remaining co-tenants' interests to expand proportionately. Here, the couple (tenants in common) agreed that whoever survived the other would own the entire home (right of survivorship). But since their agreement was verbal, it is unenforceable under the statute of frauds. Therefore, when the man died, his one-half interest transferred to his sole heir—not the woman. And when the woman died one day later, her one-half interest transferred to her sole heir. (Choice A) Under the Uniform Simultaneous Death Act, when two or more persons die without a will and within 120 hours of each other, each is considered to have predeceased the others. This act does not apply here since the only applicable statute is the statute of frauds. (Choice C) The doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to enforce an otherwise unenforceable promise upon which it reasonably and detrimentally relied. But reliance on a conditional promise (eg, a promise of property conditioned upon survivorship) is unreasonable. Therefore, the couple's reliance upon the right-of-survivorship agreement does not make it enforceable. (Choice D) Reformation is an equitable remedy that is used to correct mistakes in a deed. Since the deed here accurately reflects the couple's apparent agreement to create a tenancy in common, the court will not reform it to reflect the later verbal agreement.","conclusion":"Answer B: Each heir owns an undivided one-half interest in the home because the statute of frauds applies."},"questionZh":"一对未婚夫妇在一套公寓里共同生活了几年，当他们决定以按份共有人（tenant in common）的身份购买一套入门房时。这对夫妇没有起草遗嘱，而是口头同意，如果其中一方死亡，另一方应拥有整个房屋。这对夫妇后来遭遇了严重的飞机失事。男子当场死亡，女子一天后死亡。每个人都有一个继承人幸存。普通法婚姻在房屋所在的司法管辖区不被承认。唯一适用的法规是欺诈法​​规。在这对夫妇的继承人之间为确定房屋所有权而采取的适当行动中，法院可能会如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"由于这对夫妇在同一场飞机失事中丧生，因此每位继承人都拥有该房屋的不可分割的一半权益。","B":"由于适用欺诈法，每位继承人都拥有该房屋的不可分割的一半权益。","C":"该女子的继承人拥有整个房屋，因为这对夫妇合理地依赖他们的口头协议，从未立下遗嘱。","D":"该妇女的继承人拥有整个房屋，因为契约将被修改以反映随后的口头协议。"},"explanationZh":"共同共有（joint tenancy）是一种共存财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）持有各自的财产份额，并有权占有整个财产，但没有生存权。但是，可以通过满足欺诈法规的协议来设定生存权，即该协议必须： 以书面形式由其执行对象方签署并包含所有基本条款。生存权使按份共有人（tenant in common）的财产权益在他/她去世后消失，并允许剩余按份共有人（tenant in common）的权益按比例扩大。在这里，这对夫妇（按份共有人（tenant in common））同意，无论谁幸存，谁都将拥有整个房屋（幸存者权）。但由于他们的协议是口头协议，根据欺诈法，该协议无法执行。因此，当男人去世时，他的二分之一利益转移给他唯一的继承人，而不是女人。一天后，当这位女士去世时，她的一半权益转移给了她唯一的继承人。 （选项 A） 根据《统一同时死亡法》，当两个或两个以上的人在没有遗嘱的情况下在 120 小时内死亡时，每个人都被视为先于其他人死亡。该法案在这里不适用，因为唯一适用的法规是欺诈法​​规。 （选项 C） 承诺禁止反言原则允许一方强制执行其合理且有害地依赖的原本无法执行的承诺。但依赖有条件的承诺（例如，以生存为条件的财产承诺）是不合理的。因此，这对夫妇对生存权协议的依赖并不使其具有可执行性。 （选项D）改革是一种公平的补救措施，用于纠正行为中的错误。由于这里的契约准确地反映了这对夫妇明显同意建立共同共有（joint tenancy），因此法院不会对其进行修改以反映后来的口头协议。\n\n教育目标：生存权可以通过满足欺诈法规的协议来设定，即，(1) 为书面形式，(2) 由将要执行该协议的各方签署，并且 (3) 包含所有基本条款。","educationalObjectiveZh":"生存权可以通过满足欺诈法规的协议来设定，即，(1) 采用书面形式，(2) 由将要执行该协议的各方签署，并且 (3) 包含所有基本条款。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这对夫妇的继承人之间为确定房屋所有权而采取的适当行动中，法院可能会如何裁决？","rule":"生存权可以通过满足欺诈法规的协议来设定，即，(1) 采用书面形式，(2) 由将要执行该协议的各方签署，并且 (3) 包含所有基本条款。","analysis":"共同共有（joint tenancy）是一种共存财产，其中每个按份共有人（tenant in common）持有各自的财产份额，并有权占有整个财产，但没有生存权。但是，可以通过满足欺诈法规的协议来设定生存权，即该协议必须： 以书面形式由其执行对象方签署并包含所有基本条款。生存权使按份共有人（tenant in common）的财产权益在他/她去世后消失，并允许剩余按份共有人（tenant in common）的权益按比例扩大。在这里，这对夫妇（按份共有人（tenant in common））同意，无论谁幸存，谁都将拥有整个房屋（幸存者权）。但由于他们的协议是口头协议，根据欺诈法，该协议无法执行。因此，当男人去世时，他的二分之一利益转移给他唯一的继承人，而不是女人。一天后，当这位女士去世时，她的一半权益转移给了她唯一的继承人。 （选项 A） 根据《统一同时死亡法》，当两个或两个以上的人在没有遗嘱的情况下在 120 小时内死亡时，每个人都被视为先于其他人死亡。该法案在这里不适用，因为唯一适用的法规是欺诈法​​规。 （选项 C） 承诺禁止反言原则允许一方强制执行其合理且有害地依赖的原本无法执行的承诺。但依赖有条件的承诺（例如，以生存为条件的财产承诺）是不合理的。因此，这对夫妇对生存权协议的依赖并不使其具有可执行性。 （选项D）改革是一种公平的补救措施，用于纠正行为中的错误。由于这里的契约准确地反映了这对夫妇明显同意建立共同共有（joint tenancy），因此法院不会对其进行修改以反映后来的口头协议。\n\n教育目标：生存权可以通过满足欺诈法规的协议来设定，即，(1) 为书面形式，(2) 由将要执行该协议的各方签署，并且 (3) 包含所有基本条款。","conclusion":"答案 B：由于适用欺诈法，每位继承人都拥有该房屋的不可分割的一半权益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-3b66450fd1e83d13"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":157,"sourceQuestionPage":461,"sourceQuestionPages":[461],"sourceExplanationPages":[462,463],"sourceAnswerPages":[462,463],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-158","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A couple owned a tract of land called Lot 1. An old road ran through Lot 1 from the abutting public highway, which the couple used to haul wood from Lot 1. Without the couple's permission and with no initial right, their neighbor—the owner of Lot 2, which adjoined Lot 1—traveled over the old road for a period of 15 years to obtain access to Lot 2, although Lot 2 abutted another public road. Occasionally, the neighbor made repairs to the old road. The period of time to acquire rights by prescription in the jurisdiction is 10 years. After the expiration of 15 years, the couple conveyed a portion of Lot 1 to a friend. The deed included the following clause: \"together with the right to pass and repass at all times and for all purposes over the old road.\" The friend built a house fronting on the old road. The road was severely damaged by a spring flood, and the friend made substantial repairs to the road. The friend asked the neighbor and the couple to contribute one-third each to the cost of repairing the flood damage. They both refused, and the friend brought an appropriate action to compel contribution from the couple and the neighbor. What is the likely outcome of this action?","choices":{"A":"The friend will lose as to both defendants.","B":"The friend will win as to both defendants.","C":"The friend will win as to the couple, but lose as to the neighbor.","D":"The friend will win as to the neighbor, but lose as to the couple."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Repairing & maintaining easement\n\nDuty to repair & Easement holder must repair & maintain easement to extent necessary maintain to:\n\nprevent unreasonable interference with enjoyment of servient estate protect servient-estate owner from liability to third parties Duty to Other easement holders and/or servient-estate owner who share contribute easement must contribute to reasonable costs of repairs & maintenance\n\nThe duty to repair and maintain an easement falls on the easement holder*—not the servient-estate owner (unless the parties agree otherwise). However, the servient-estate owner has a duty to contribute to the reasonable costs of repairs and maintenance if he/she also uses the easement. The same applies to other easement holders who share the use of the easement. Here, the couple (servient-estate owner) conveyed an easement to the friend that allowed the friend to use the old road on their land. But the couple also used the road to haul wood. And the neighbor obtained a prescriptive easement to use the road by openly and continuously using the road for 15 years without the couple's permission. As a result, the couple and the neighbor were obligated to contribute to the costs incurred by the friend in repairing the road. Therefore, the friend will likely win against both in his action to compel contribution (Choices A, C & D). *This obligation also extends to repair and maintenance of portions of the servient estate and improvements used to enjoy the easement, so long as they are within the easement holder's control.\n\nEducational objective: An easement holder has a duty to repair and maintain the easement. If the easement is shared by other easement holders or the servient-estate owner, those parties are obligated to contribute to the reasonable costs of repairs and maintenance.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.13 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (duty to repair and maintain easement).","educationalObjective":"An easement holder has a duty to repair and maintain the easement. If the easement is shared by other easement holders or the servient-estate owner, those parties are obligated to contribute to the reasonable costs of repairs and maintenance.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.13 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (duty to repair and maintain easement).","rule":"An easement holder has a duty to repair and maintain the easement. If the easement is shared by other easement holders or the servient-estate owner, those parties are obligated to contribute to the reasonable costs of repairs and maintenance.","irac":{"issue":"What is the likely outcome of this action?","rule":"An easement holder has a duty to repair and maintain the easement. If the easement is shared by other easement holders or the servient-estate owner, those parties are obligated to contribute to the reasonable costs of repairs and maintenance.","analysis":"Repairing & maintaining easement\n\nDuty to repair & Easement holder must repair & maintain easement to extent necessary maintain to:\n\nprevent unreasonable interference with enjoyment of servient estate protect servient-estate owner from liability to third parties Duty to Other easement holders and/or servient-estate owner who share contribute easement must contribute to reasonable costs of repairs & maintenance\n\nThe duty to repair and maintain an easement falls on the easement holder*—not the servient-estate owner (unless the parties agree otherwise). However, the servient-estate owner has a duty to contribute to the reasonable costs of repairs and maintenance if he/she also uses the easement. The same applies to other easement holders who share the use of the easement. Here, the couple (servient-estate owner) conveyed an easement to the friend that allowed the friend to use the old road on their land. But the couple also used the road to haul wood. And the neighbor obtained a prescriptive easement to use the road by openly and continuously using the road for 15 years without the couple's permission. As a result, the couple and the neighbor were obligated to contribute to the costs incurred by the friend in repairing the road. Therefore, the friend will likely win against both in his action to compel contribution (Choices A, C & D). *This obligation also extends to repair and maintenance of portions of the servient estate and improvements used to enjoy the easement, so long as they are within the easement holder's control.","conclusion":"Answer B: The friend will win as to both defendants."},"questionZh":"一对夫妇拥有一块名为“1 号地块”的土地。一条旧路从毗邻的公共高速公路穿过 1 号地块，这对夫妇曾用这条公路从 1 号地块运木材。未经这对夫妇的许可，也没有初始权利，他们的邻居（毗邻 1 号地块的 2 号地块的所有者）在旧道路上行驶了 15 年，才获得通往 2 号地块的通道，尽管 2 号地块毗邻另一条公共道路。偶尔，邻居也会修复旧路。在管辖范围内凭时效取得权利的期限为10年。 15 年期满后，这对夫妇将 1 号地块的一部分转让给了一位朋友。该契约包括以下条款：“连同在任何时候、出于任何目的在旧道路上通行和重新通行的权利。”朋友在老路上建了一栋房子。这条路在春季洪水中被严重损坏，朋友对路进行了大规模的修缮。朋友请求邻居和这对夫妇各捐出三分之一的水灾修复费用。他们都拒绝了，朋友采取了适当的行动，迫使这对夫妇和邻居捐款。这一行动可能产生什么结果？","choicesZh":{"A":"对于两名被告来说，朋友都会输。","B":"就两名被告而言，朋友将获胜。","C":"对于夫妻来说，朋友会赢，但是对于邻居来说，朋友会输。","D":"对于邻居来说朋友会赢，对于夫妻来说朋友会输。"},"explanationZh":"维修和保养地役权\n\n维修责任和地役权持有人必须维修和维护地役权，以达到必要的维护目的：\n\n防止对使用地役权的不合理干扰 保护使用权所有人免于对第三方承担责任 对其他地役权持有人和/或分享使用权的使用权所有人的责任必须承担合理的维修和保养费用\n\n修理和维护地役权的责任由地役权持有人*承担，而不是供役地所有者（除非双方另有约定）。然而，如果服务业业主也使用地役权，则他/她有义务承担合理的维修和保养费用。这同样适用于共享地役权使用权的其他地役权持有人。在这里，这对夫妇（役用庄园所有者）向朋友转让了一份地役权，允许朋友使用他们土地上的旧道路。但这对夫妇也用这条路来运输木材。邻居在未经这对夫妇许可的情况下，公开并连续使用该道路15年，从而获得了使用该道路的规定地役权。结果，夫妻俩和邻居有义务分担朋友修路的费用。因此，朋友在强制贡献的行动中很可能会战胜双方（选项A、C、D）。 *此义务还适用于部分供役地产的维修和保养以及用于享受地役权的改进，只要它们在地役权持有人的控制范围内。\n\n教育目标：地役权持有人有义务修理和维护地役权。如果地役权由其他地役权持有人或供役地所有者共享，这些各方有义务分摊合理的维修和保养费用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.13 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（修复和维护地役权的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"地役权持有人有义务修理和维护地役权。如果地役权由其他地役权持有人或供役地所有者共享，这些各方有义务分摊合理的维修和保养费用。","iracZh":{"issue":"这一行动可能产生什么结果？","rule":"地役权持有人有义务修理和维护地役权。如果地役权由其他地役权持有人或供役地所有者共享，这些各方有义务分摊合理的维修和保养费用。","analysis":"维修和保养地役权\n\n维修责任和地役权持有人必须维修和维护地役权，以达到必要的维护目的：\n\n防止对使用地役权的不合理干扰 保护使用权所有人免于对第三方承担责任 对其他地役权持有人和/或分享使用权的使用权所有人的责任必须承担合理的维修和保养费用\n\n修理和维护地役权的责任由地役权持有人*承担，而不是供役地所有者（除非双方另有约定）。然而，如果服务业业主也使用地役权，则他/她有义务承担合理的维修和保养费用。这同样适用于共享地役权使用权的其他地役权持有人。在这里，这对夫妇（役用庄园所有者）向朋友转让了一份地役权，允许朋友使用他们土地上的旧道路。但这对夫妇也用这条路来运输木材。邻居在未经这对夫妇许可的情况下，公开并连续使用该道路15年，从而获得了使用该道路的规定地役权。结果，夫妻俩和邻居有义务分担朋友修路的费用。因此，朋友在强制贡献的行动中很可能会战胜双方（选项A、C、D）。 *此义务还适用于部分供役地产的维修和保养以及用于享受地役权的改进，只要它们在地役权持有人的控制范围内。\n\n教育目标：地役权持有人有义务修理和维护地役权。如果地役权由其他地役权持有人或供役地所有者共享，这些各方有义务分摊合理的维修和保养费用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.13 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（修复和维护地役权的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 B：就两名被告而言，朋友将获胜。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"prescription","ipa":"/pɹʌskɹˈɪpʃən/","syllables":"pre·scrip·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 处方, 命令, 指定；[医] 处方, 药方","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“prescription”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:prescriptions","sourceSentence":"The period of time to acquire rights by prescription in the jurisdiction is 10 years.","synonyms":["prescription drug","prescription medicine","ethical drug"]},{"term":"prescriptive","ipa":"/pri'skriptiv/","syllables":"pre·scrip·tive","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"a. 规定的, 指定的, 规范的；[法] 规定的, 指示的, 命令","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“prescriptive”","wordForm":"形容词；词形 s:prescriptives","sourceSentence":"And the neighbor obtained a prescriptive easement to use the road by openly and continuously using the road for 15 years without the couple's permission.","synonyms":["normative"]},{"term":"severely","ipa":"/sʌvˈɪɹli/","syllables":"se·verely","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 严格, 尖锐, 严肃, 严重, 严厉, 朴素","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“severely”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The road was severely damaged by a spring flood, and the friend made substantial repairs to the road.","synonyms":["badly","gravely","seriously","hard"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":158,"sourceQuestionPage":464,"sourceQuestionPages":[464],"sourceExplanationPages":[465,466],"sourceAnswerPages":[465,466],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-159","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"A seller and a buyer signed a contract for the sale of vacant land. The contract was silent concerning the quality of title, but the seller agreed in the contract to convey the land to the buyer by a warranty deed without any exceptions. When the buyer conducted a title search for the land, she learned that the applicable zoning did not allow for her planned commercial use. She also discovered that there was a recorded restrictive covenant limiting the use of the land to residential use. The buyer no longer wants to purchase the land. Must the buyer purchase the land?","choices":{"A":"No, because the restrictive covenant renders the title unmarketable.","B":"No, because the zoning places a cloud on the title.","C":"Yes, because the buyer would receive a warranty deed without any exceptions.","D":"Yes, because the contract was silent regarding the quality of the title."},"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession All land-sale contracts, unless otherwise provided, imply that the seller will convey the buyer marketable title on the date of closing. Title need not be perfect to be marketable. But it must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. A restrictive covenant is a promise to restrict the use of one's land. Here, the covenant limits the use of the land to residential use. This allows a third party to enforce the covenant, thereby posing a threat of litigation. As a result, the restrictive covenant renders title unmarketable and the buyer need not purchase the land. (Choice B) An existing zoning violation may render title unmarketable, but the mere existence of a zoning code does not. (Choice C) A buyer is not required to accept a warranty deed if the seller cannot deliver marketable title upon closing. The reason is that the doctrine of merger will cause the deed, once accepted, to subsume the land-sale contract. Any title issues will then be based on the deed instead of the guarantees in the contract. (Choice D) When, as here, a contract is silent regarding the quality of title, it is implied that the seller will convey marketable title.\n\nEducational objective: It is generally implied that the seller will convey marketable title upon closing. Title is marketable if it is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Therefore, a restrictive covenant renders title unmarketable.\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (marketable title).","educationalObjective":"It is generally implied that the seller will convey marketable title upon closing. Title is marketable if it is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Therefore, a restrictive covenant renders title unmarketable.","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (marketable title).","rule":"It is generally implied that the seller will convey marketable title upon closing. Title is marketable if it is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Therefore, a restrictive covenant renders title unmarketable.","irac":{"issue":"Must the buyer purchase the land?","rule":"It is generally implied that the seller will convey marketable title upon closing. Title is marketable if it is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Therefore, a restrictive covenant renders title unmarketable.","analysis":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession All land-sale contracts, unless otherwise provided, imply that the seller will convey the buyer marketable title on the date of closing. Title need not be perfect to be marketable. But it must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. A restrictive covenant is a promise to restrict the use of one's land. Here, the covenant limits the use of the land to residential use. This allows a third party to enforce the covenant, thereby posing a threat of litigation. As a result, the restrictive covenant renders title unmarketable and the buyer need not purchase the land. (Choice B) An existing zoning violation may render title unmarketable, but the mere existence of a zoning code does not. (Choice C) A buyer is not required to accept a warranty deed if the seller cannot deliver marketable title upon closing. The reason is that the doctrine of merger will cause the deed, once accepted, to subsume the land-sale contract. Any title issues will then be based on the deed instead of the guarantees in the contract. (Choice D) When, as here, a contract is silent regarding the quality of title, it is implied that the seller will convey marketable title.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the restrictive covenant renders the title unmarketable."},"questionZh":"卖方和买方签订了一份空置土地出售合同。合同没有提及所有权的质量，但卖方在合同中同意通过担保契据将土地转让给买方，没有任何例外。当买家对土地进行产权调查时，她得知适用的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）不允许她计划用于商业用途。她还发现，有记录的限制性契约限制土地的用途为住宅用途。买家不再想购买土地。买家必须购买土地吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为限制性契约使该所有权无法销售。","B":"不，因为土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）给标题带来了阴影。","C":"是的，因为买方将毫无例外地收到保修契约。","D":"是的，因为合同没有提及所有权的质量。"},"explanationZh":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租赁 留置权 所有权链中的空白 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 所有土地销售合同，除非另有规定，都意味着卖方将在成交之日将可出售的所有权转让给买方。标题不一定要完美才能有销路。但它必须合理地没有疑问，并且不受诉讼威胁，以便一个理性的人会接受并支付费用。限制性契约是限制某人土地使用的承诺。在这里，契约将土地的用途限制为住宅用途。这使得第三方能够执行该公约，从而构成诉讼的威胁。因此，限制性契约使所有权无法出售，买方无需购买土地。 （选择 B）现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规行为可能会导致产权无法销售，但土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）代码的存在则不会。 （选择 C）如果卖方在交割时无法交付可出售的所有权，则买方无需接受保修契约。原因是，合并原则将导致契约一旦被接受，就包含在土地销售合同中。任何所有权问题都将基于契约而不是合同中的担保。 （选项D）当合同没有提及所有权的质量时，就如此处所示，这意味着卖方将转让适销所有权。\n\n教育目标：通常暗示卖方将在成交时转让可出售的所有权。如果产权合理地没有任何疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以致理性的人会接受并支付费用，则该产权是可出售的。因此，限制性契约使所有权无法出售。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（可销售所有权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"通常暗示卖方将在交割时转让可出售的所有权。如果产权合理地没有任何疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以致理性的人会接受并支付费用，则该产权是可出售的。因此，限制性契约使所有权无法出售。","iracZh":{"issue":"买家必须购买土地吗？","rule":"通常暗示卖方将在交割时转让可出售的所有权。如果产权合理地没有任何疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以致理性的人会接受并支付费用，则该产权是可出售的。因此，限制性契约使所有权无法出售。","analysis":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租赁 留置权 所有权链中的空白 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 所有土地销售合同，除非另有规定，都意味着卖方将在成交之日将可出售的所有权转让给买方。标题不一定要完美才能有销路。但它必须合理地没有疑问，并且不受诉讼威胁，以便一个理性的人会接受并支付费用。限制性契约是限制某人土地使用的承诺。在这里，契约将土地的用途限制为住宅用途。这使得第三方能够执行该公约，从而构成诉讼的威胁。因此，限制性契约使所有权无法出售，买方无需购买土地。 （选择 B）现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规行为可能会导致产权无法销售，但土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）代码的存在则不会。 （选择 C）如果卖方在交割时无法交付可出售的所有权，则买方无需接受保修契约。原因是，合并原则将导致契约一旦被接受，就包含在土地销售合同中。任何所有权问题都将基于契约而不是合同中的担保。 （选项D）当合同没有提及所有权的质量时，就如此处所示，这意味着卖方将转让适销所有权。\n\n教育目标：通常暗示卖方将在成交时转让可出售的所有权。如果产权合理地没有任何疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以致理性的人会接受并支付费用，则该产权是可出售的。因此，限制性契约使所有权无法出售。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（可销售所有权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为限制性契约使该所有权无法销售。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":159,"sourceQuestionPage":467,"sourceQuestionPages":[467],"sourceExplanationPages":[468,469],"sourceAnswerPages":[468,469],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-160","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A landlord owned a house in fee simple. He leased the house to a tenant for a term of 10 years by properly executed written instrument. The lease was promptly and properly recorded. It contained an option for the tenant to purchase the house by tendering $250,000 as purchase price any time \"during the term of this lease.\" One year later, the tenant, by a properly executed written instrument, purported to assign the option to her daughter, expressly retaining all of the remaining term of the lease. The instrument of assignment was promptly and properly recorded. Two years later, the landlord contracted to sell the house to a broker and to convey marketable title \"subject to the rights of [the tenant] under her lease.\" The broker refused to close because of the outstanding option assigned to the daughter. The landlord brought an appropriate action against the broker for specific performance. If judgment is rendered in favor of the landlord, it will be because the relevant jurisdiction has adopted a rule on a key issue as to which various state courts have split. Which of the following identifies the determinative rule or doctrine upon which the split occurs, and states the position favorable to the landlord?","choices":{"A":"In a contract to buy, any form of \"subject to a lease\" clause that fails to mention expressly an existing option means that the seller is agreeing to sell free and clear of any option originally included in the lease.","B":"Marketable title can be conveyed so long as any outstanding option not mentioned in the purchase contract has not yet been exercised.","C":"Options to purchase by lessees are subject to the Rule Against Perpetuities.","D":"Options to purchase contained in a lease cannot be assigned separately from the lease."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Types of option contracts\n\nOption to Gives exclusive right to purchase property at specified price, usually purchase within specified time\n\nRight of first Gives first opportunity to purchase property if it ever goes up for sale refusal\n\nA contract for the sale of land impliedly warrants that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer at the time of closing. If the seller cannot do so, then the buyer can rescind (ie, cancel) the contract and refuse to close. Here, the broker has refused to close because of the outstanding option to purchase assigned to the daughter. The holder of an option to purchase has the exclusive right to purchase the property during a specified time (eg, \"during the term of this lease\"). Jurisdictions are split as to whether an option contained in a lease agreement can be assigned separately from the lease: Minority rule – an option is an independent provision that can be assigned separately from the lease Majority rule – an option is an integral part of the lease agreement that cannot be separately assigned Since the majority rule would render the assignment of the option to the daughter invalid, this rule supports the landlord's action against the broker. However, title may remain unmarketable because the option to purchase is still held by the tenant. This is true regardless of whether the option is mentioned in the purchase contract (Choice B). (Choice A) A contract with a \"subject to a lease\" clause means that the land is being sold subject to an option to purchase included in the lease, even if the contract does not expressly mention an existing option. (Choice C) An option to purchase is subject to the Rule Against Perpetuities, which voids an option if there is any possibility that it could vest more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest. The option here will vest (or not vest) by the end of the tenant's 10-year lease, so it satisfies this rule.\n\nEducational objective: An option to purchase gives the option-holder the exclusive right to purchase the property during a specified time. If the option is contained within a lease, it cannot be assigned separately from that lease in most jurisdictions.","educationalObjective":"An option to purchase gives the option-holder the exclusive right to purchase the property during a specified time. If the option is contained within a lease, it cannot be assigned separately from that lease in most jurisdictions.","references":"","rule":"An option to purchase gives the option-holder the exclusive right to purchase the property during a specified time. If the option is contained within a lease, it cannot be assigned separately from that lease in most jurisdictions.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following identifies the determinative rule or doctrine upon which the split occurs, and states the position favorable to the landlord?","rule":"An option to purchase gives the option-holder the exclusive right to purchase the property during a specified time. If the option is contained within a lease, it cannot be assigned separately from that lease in most jurisdictions.","analysis":"Types of option contracts\n\nOption to Gives exclusive right to purchase property at specified price, usually purchase within specified time\n\nRight of first Gives first opportunity to purchase property if it ever goes up for sale refusal\n\nA contract for the sale of land impliedly warrants that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer at the time of closing. If the seller cannot do so, then the buyer can rescind (ie, cancel) the contract and refuse to close. Here, the broker has refused to close because of the outstanding option to purchase assigned to the daughter. The holder of an option to purchase has the exclusive right to purchase the property during a specified time (eg, \"during the term of this lease\"). Jurisdictions are split as to whether an option contained in a lease agreement can be assigned separately from the lease: Minority rule – an option is an independent provision that can be assigned separately from the lease Majority rule – an option is an integral part of the lease agreement that cannot be separately assigned Since the majority rule would render the assignment of the option to the daughter invalid, this rule supports the landlord's action against the broker. However, title may remain unmarketable because the option to purchase is still held by the tenant. This is true regardless of whether the option is mentioned in the purchase contract (Choice B). (Choice A) A contract with a \"subject to a lease\" clause means that the land is being sold subject to an option to purchase included in the lease, even if the contract does not expressly mention an existing option. (Choice C) An option to purchase is subject to the Rule Against Perpetuities, which voids an option if there is any possibility that it could vest more than 21 years after some relevant life in being at the creation of the interest. The option here will vest (or not vest) by the end of the tenant's 10-year lease, so it satisfies this rule.","conclusion":"Answer D: Options to purchase contained in a lease cannot be assigned separately from the lease."},"questionZh":"房东以简单的费用拥有一套房子。他通过正确签署的书面文书，将房子租给了一位房客，租期为 10 年。租赁被及时、正确地记录。它包含租户可以选择在“租约期限内”随时以 250,000 美元作为购买价格购买房屋。一年后，租户通过一份正确签署的书面文书，声称将选择权转让给她的女儿，并明确保留所有剩余的租约期限。分配文书被及时、正确地记录。两年后，房东签订合同，将房屋出售给经纪人，并转让可出售的所有权，“但须遵守[租户]根据其租约所享有的权利”。由于分配给女儿的未决期权，经纪人拒绝关闭。房东针对经纪人的具体表现提起了适当的诉讼。如果判决对房东有利，那是因为相关司法管辖区就一个关键问题采纳了一项规则，而各州法院对此存在分歧。以下哪项确定了发生分割的决定性规则或原则，并陈述了对房东有利的立场？","choicesZh":{"A":"在购买合同中，任何形式的“受租赁约束”条款如果未明确提及现有选择权，则意味着卖方同意出售，且不包含租赁中最初包含的任何选择权。","B":"只要购买合同中未提及的任何未行使的选择权尚未行使，可出售的所有权就可以转让。","C":"承租人的购买选择权须遵守“反对永续规则”。","D":"租赁中包含的购买选择权不能与租赁分开转让。"},"explanationZh":"期权合约的类型\n\n期权 赋予以指定价格购买房产的专有权利，通常在指定时间内购买\n\n优先权 如果房产被拒绝出售，则给予优先购买房产的机会\n\n土地出售合同默示保证卖方将在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。如果卖方不能这样做，那么买方可以解除（即取消）合同并拒绝成交。在这里，经纪人拒绝关闭，因为未完成的购买选择权分配给了女儿。购买选择权的持有者拥有在指定时间（例如，“在本租赁期限内”）购买房产的专有权利。关于租赁协议中包含的选择权是否可以与租约分开转让，司法管辖区存在分歧： 少数规则 – 选择权是一项独立条款，可以与租赁分开分配 多数规则 – 选择权是租赁协议不可分割的一部分，不能单独转让 由于多数规则会导致将选择权转让给女儿无效，因此该规则支持房东对经纪人提起的诉讼。然而，所有权可能仍然无法出售，因为购买选择权仍然由租户持有。无论购买合同中是否提及该选项（选择 B），情况都是如此。 （选择 A） 带有“受租赁约束”条款的合同意味着，土地的出售取决于租赁中包含的购买选择权，即使合同没有明确提及现有选择权。 （选择 C）购买期权受“永续规则”约束，如果期权有可能在权益创建时的相关寿命超过 21 年后归属，则该期权无效。这里的选择权将在租户的 10 年租约结束时归属（或不归属），因此它满足此规则。\n\n教育目标：购买期权赋予期权持有者在指定时间内购买房产的专有权利。如果选择权包含在租约中，则在大多数司法管辖区中，不能将其与租约分开转让。","educationalObjectiveZh":"购买选择权赋予选择权持有人在指定时间内购买房产的专有权利。如果选择权包含在租约中，则在大多数司法管辖区中，不能将其与租约分开转让。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项确定了发生分割的决定性规则或原则，并陈述了对房东有利的立场？","rule":"购买选择权赋予选择权持有人在指定时间内购买房产的专有权利。如果选择权包含在租约中，则在大多数司法管辖区中，不能将其与租约分开转让。","analysis":"期权合约的类型\n\n期权 赋予以指定价格购买房产的专有权利，通常在指定时间内购买\n\n优先权 如果房产被拒绝出售，则给予优先购买房产的机会\n\n土地出售合同默示保证卖方将在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。如果卖方不能这样做，那么买方可以解除（即取消）合同并拒绝成交。在这里，经纪人拒绝关闭，因为未完成的购买选择权分配给了女儿。购买选择权的持有者拥有在指定时间（例如，“在本租赁期限内”）购买房产的专有权利。关于租赁协议中包含的选择权是否可以与租约分开转让，司法管辖区存在分歧： 少数规则 – 选择权是一项独立条款，可以与租赁分开分配 多数规则 – 选择权是租赁协议不可分割的一部分，不能单独转让 由于多数规则会导致将选择权转让给女儿无效，因此该规则支持房东对经纪人提起的诉讼。然而，所有权可能仍然无法出售，因为购买选择权仍然由租户持有。无论购买合同中是否提及该选项（选择 B），情况都是如此。 （选择 A） 带有“受租赁约束”条款的合同意味着，土地的出售取决于租赁中包含的购买选择权，即使合同没有明确提及现有选择权。 （选择 C）购买期权受“永续规则”约束，如果期权有可能在权益创建时的相关寿命超过 21 年后归属，则该期权无效。这里的选择权将在租户的 10 年租约结束时归属（或不归属），因此它满足此规则。\n\n教育目标：购买期权赋予期权持有者在指定时间内购买房产的专有权利。如果选择权包含在租约中，则在大多数司法管辖区中，不能将其与租约分开转让。","conclusion":"答案 D：租赁中包含的购买选择权不能与租赁分开转让。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"contract for the sale of land","ipa":"/kˈɑntɹˌækt fˈɔɹ ðə sˈeɪl ˈəv lˈænd/","syllables":"con·tract for the sale of land","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"土地买卖合同：约定转让特定不动产所有权的合同","contextualMeaning":"土地买卖合同：约定转让特定不动产所有权的合同","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Types of option contracts Option to Gives exclusive right to purchase property at specified price, usually purchase within specified time Right of first Gives first opportunity to purchase property if it ever goes up for sale refusal A contract for the sale of land impliedly warrants that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer at the time of closing.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":160,"sourceQuestionPage":470,"sourceQuestionPages":[470],"sourceExplanationPages":[471,472],"sourceAnswerPages":[471,472],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-161","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A tenant leased a commercial property from a landlord for a 12-year term. The property included a large store and a parking lot. At the start of the lease period, the tenant took possession and with the landlord's oral consent installed counters, display cases, removable shelving, and special lighting. Both parties complied with all lease terms. The lease is set to expire next month. Two weeks ago, when the landlord contacted the tenant about a possible lease renewal, she learned that the tenant had decided not to renew the lease and planned to remove all of the above-listed items on or before the lease termination date. The landlord claimed that all the items had become part of the real estate and had to remain on the premises. The tenant asserted his right and intention to remove all the items. Both the lease and the statutes of the jurisdiction are silent on the matter in dispute. At the time the landlord consented and the tenant installed the items, nothing was said about the tenant's right to retain or remove the items. The landlord has sued the tenant to enjoin his removal of the items. How is the court likely to rule?","choices":{"A":"For the landlord as to items bolted or otherwise attached to the premises, and for the tenant as to items not attached to the premises other than by weight.","B":"For the landlord, because the items have become part of the landlord's real estate.","C":"For the tenant, because all of the items may be removed as trade fixtures without any obligation to restore the premises.","D":"For the tenant, provided that the tenant reasonably restores the premises to the prior condition or pays for the cost of restoration."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A tenant cannot remove fixtures—ie, items so connected with (or attached to) real property that they are considered a part of that property—without the landlord's consent. However, a tenant can remove trade fixtures—ie, items attached to real property by the tenant for use in his/her trade or business—without the landlord's consent if: the items are removed before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease terminates and the removal will not substantially harm the property (eg, will not degrade the building's structural integrity). But after the removal, the tenant must reasonably restore the property to its prior condition or pay restoration costs. Here, the counters, display cases, removable shelving, and special lighting are trade fixtures since the tenant installed them for use in the store and parking lot that he leased from the landlord. Since there is no evidence that removing these items will substantially harm the property and the lease will not expire until next month, the tenant can remove them. However, he must reasonably restore the premises to its prior condition or pay restoration costs (Choice C). (Choice A) Although some of the tenant's trade fixtures may be bolted or otherwise attached to the premises, there is no evidence that removing them will substantially harm the property. Therefore, the court will likely allow the tenant to remove all of these items regardless of how they were attached. (Choice B) Fixtures are considered part of the landlord's property (ie, real estate), but trade fixtures are not. Instead, the tenant retains ownership of the trade fixtures and can remove them from the landlord's property if the above conditions are met.\n\nEducational objective: Trade fixtures—ie, items attached to real property by a tenant for use in his/her trade or business—can be removed without the landlord's consent if the removal (1) occurs before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease ends and (2) will not substantially harm the property. However, the tenant must reasonably restore the property to its prior condition or pay restoration costs.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant §§ 12.2–12.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (restoration after removal of trade fixtures).","educationalObjective":"Trade fixtures—ie, items attached to real property by a tenant for use in his/her trade or business—can be removed without the landlord's consent if the removal (1) occurs before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease ends and (2) will not substantially harm the property. However, the tenant must reasonably restore the property to its prior condition or pay restoration costs.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant §§ 12.2–12.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (restoration after removal of trade fixtures).","rule":"Trade fixtures—ie, items attached to real property by a tenant for use in his/her trade or business—can be removed without the landlord's consent if the removal (1) occurs before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease ends and (2) will not substantially harm the property. However, the tenant must reasonably restore the property to its prior condition or pay restoration costs.","irac":{"issue":"How is the court likely to rule?","rule":"Trade fixtures—ie, items attached to real property by a tenant for use in his/her trade or business—can be removed without the landlord's consent if the removal (1) occurs before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease ends and (2) will not substantially harm the property. However, the tenant must reasonably restore the property to its prior condition or pay restoration costs.","analysis":"A tenant cannot remove fixtures—ie, items so connected with (or attached to) real property that they are considered a part of that property—without the landlord's consent. However, a tenant can remove trade fixtures—ie, items attached to real property by the tenant for use in his/her trade or business—without the landlord's consent if: the items are removed before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease terminates and the removal will not substantially harm the property (eg, will not degrade the building's structural integrity). But after the removal, the tenant must reasonably restore the property to its prior condition or pay restoration costs. Here, the counters, display cases, removable shelving, and special lighting are trade fixtures since the tenant installed them for use in the store and parking lot that he leased from the landlord. Since there is no evidence that removing these items will substantially harm the property and the lease will not expire until next month, the tenant can remove them. However, he must reasonably restore the premises to its prior condition or pay restoration costs (Choice C). (Choice A) Although some of the tenant's trade fixtures may be bolted or otherwise attached to the premises, there is no evidence that removing them will substantially harm the property. Therefore, the court will likely allow the tenant to remove all of these items regardless of how they were attached. (Choice B) Fixtures are considered part of the landlord's property (ie, real estate), but trade fixtures are not. Instead, the tenant retains ownership of the trade fixtures and can remove them from the landlord's property if the above conditions are met.","conclusion":"Answer D: For the tenant, provided that the tenant reasonably restores the premises to the prior condition or pays for the cost of restoration."},"questionZh":"一名租户从房东那里租了一套商业地产，租期为 12 年。该物业包括一家大型商店和一个停车场。在租赁期开始时，租户接管房屋，并在房东口头同意的情况下安装柜台、展示柜、可移动架子和特殊照明。双方均遵守所有租赁条款。租约将于下个月到期。两周前，当房东联系租户询问可能续租事宜时，她得知租户已决定不再续租，并计划在租约终止日期或之前拆除上述所有物品。房东声称所有物品都已成为房地产的一部分，必须保留在房屋内。租户声称他有权利并打算移除所有物品。租约和司法管辖区法规均未提及争议事项。当房东同意并且租户安装这些物品时，没有提及租户保留或移除这些物品的权利。房东已起诉房客，要求其移走这些物品。法院可能会如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"对于房东来说，是用螺栓或其他方式附着在房屋上的物品，对于租户来说，是指除按重量以外未附着在房屋上的物品。","B":"对于房东来说，因为物品已经成为房东房产的一部分。","C":"对于租户来说，因为所有物品都可以作为贸易固定物移除，而没有任何义务恢复房屋。","D":"对于承租人来说，前提是承租人合理地将房屋恢复到原来的状态或支付恢复费用。"},"explanationZh":"未经房东同意，租户不得拆除固定装置，即与不动产密切相关（或附着）而被视为该不动产一部分的物品。然而，租户可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除商业固定装置（即租户附着在不动产上用于其贸易或业务的物品），如果： 在租赁终止之前或之后的合理时间内拆除这些物品，并且拆除不会对财产造成实质性损害（例如，不会降低建筑物的结构完整性）。但搬迁后，承租人必须合理地将财产恢复到原来的状态或支付恢复费用。在这里，柜台、展示柜、可移动的架子和特殊照明都是商业装置，因为租户安装它们是为了在他从房东那里租用的商店和停车场使用。由于没有证据表明移除这些物品会对财产造成严重损害，并且租约要到下个月才会到期，因此租户可以移除它们。然而，他必须合理地将房屋恢复到原来的状态或支付恢复费用（选择C）。 （选择 A）虽然租户的一些商业固定装置可能用螺栓固定或以其他方式固定在房屋上，但没有证据表明拆除它们会严重损害财产。因此，法院可能会允许租户移除所有这些物品，无论它们是如何附着的。 （选择B） 固定装置被视为房东财产（即房地产）的一部分，但贸易固定装置不是。相反，租户保留贸易固定装置的所有权，并且如果满足上述条件，可以将其从房东的财产中移走。\n\n教育目标：贸易固定物（即租户附着在不动产上用于其贸易或业务的物品）可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除，前提是拆除（1）发生在租赁结束之前或租赁结束后的合理时间内，并且（2）不会对财产造成实质性损害。但是，承租人必须合理地将财产恢复到原来的状态或支付恢复费用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 12.2–12.3 条（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（拆除贸易设施后恢复）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"贸易固定装置（即租户在其贸易或业务中使用的附着在不动产上的物品）可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除，前提是拆除（1）发生在租赁结束之前或租赁结束后的合理时间内，并且（2）不会对财产造成实质性损害。但是，承租人必须合理地将财产恢复到原来的状态或支付恢复费用。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会如何裁决？","rule":"贸易固定装置（即租户在其贸易或业务中使用的附着在不动产上的物品）可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除，前提是拆除（1）发生在租赁结束之前或租赁结束后的合理时间内，并且（2）不会对财产造成实质性损害。但是，承租人必须合理地将财产恢复到原来的状态或支付恢复费用。","analysis":"未经房东同意，租户不得拆除固定装置，即与不动产密切相关（或附着）而被视为该不动产一部分的物品。然而，租户可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除商业固定装置（即租户附着在不动产上用于其贸易或业务的物品），如果： 在租赁终止之前或之后的合理时间内拆除这些物品，并且拆除不会对财产造成实质性损害（例如，不会降低建筑物的结构完整性）。但搬迁后，承租人必须合理地将财产恢复到原来的状态或支付恢复费用。在这里，柜台、展示柜、可移动的架子和特殊照明都是商业装置，因为租户安装它们是为了在他从房东那里租用的商店和停车场使用。由于没有证据表明移除这些物品会对财产造成严重损害，并且租约要到下个月才会到期，因此租户可以移除它们。然而，他必须合理地将房屋恢复到原来的状态或支付恢复费用（选择C）。 （选择 A）虽然租户的一些商业固定装置可能用螺栓固定或以其他方式固定在房屋上，但没有证据表明拆除它们会严重损害财产。因此，法院可能会允许租户移除所有这些物品，无论它们是如何附着的。 （选择B） 固定装置被视为房东财产（即房地产）的一部分，但贸易固定装置不是。相反，租户保留贸易固定装置的所有权，并且如果满足上述条件，可以将其从房东的财产中移走。\n\n教育目标：贸易固定物（即租户附着在不动产上用于其贸易或业务的物品）可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除，前提是拆除（1）发生在租赁结束之前或租赁结束后的合理时间内，并且（2）不会对财产造成实质性损害。但是，承租人必须合理地将财产恢复到原来的状态或支付恢复费用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 12.2–12.3 条（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（拆除贸易设施后恢复）。","conclusion":"答案 D：对于承租人来说，前提是承租人合理地将房屋恢复到原来的状态或支付恢复费用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"trade fixture","ipa":"/tɹˈeɪd fˈɪkstʃɝ/","syllables":"trade fix·ture","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"营业附着物：承租人为经营安装、通常可在租期内拆除的物件","contextualMeaning":"营业附着物：承租人为经营安装、通常可在租期内拆除的物件","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"For the tenant, because all of the items may be removed as trade fixtures without any obligation to restore the premises.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-ac638eca6901c642"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":161,"sourceQuestionPage":473,"sourceQuestionPages":[473],"sourceExplanationPages":[474,475],"sourceAnswerPages":[474,475],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-162","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A man owned a tract of land in fee simple. He and the owner of a neighboring tract entered into a written agreement under which the neighbor agreed to buy the man's tract for $100,000, its fair market value. The agreement contained all the essential terms of a real estate contract to sell and buy, including a date for closing. The neighbor made the required $50,000 down payment that, pursuant to the contract, the man could retain as liquidated damages in the event of the neighbor's breach. Before the date set for the closing in the contract, the neighbor died. After the closing date, the neighbor's daughter was duly qualified as administrator of the estate and immediately made demand for return of the $50,000 deposit. The man responded by stating that he took such demand to be a declaration that the daughter did not intend to complete the contract and that he considered the contract at an end. The man further asserted that he was entitled to retain, as liquidated damages, the $50,000. The reasonable market value of the disputed tract had increased to $110,000 by this time. The daughter brought an appropriate action against the man to recover the $50,000. In his answer, the man made no affirmative claim but asserted that he was entitled to retain the $50,000 as liquidated damages as provided in the contract. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The daughter, because the neighbor's death terminated the contract as a matter of law.","B":"The daughter, because the provision relied upon by the man is unenforceable.","C":"The man, because the court should enforce the express agreement of the contracting parties.","D":"The man, because the doctrine of equitable conversion prevents termination of the contract upon the death of a party."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The doctrine of equitable conversion applies during the period between contract formation and closing. During this executory period, the seller acquires the equitable right to the purchase price and the buyer acquires equitable title to the property. Since equitable title is treated as an interest in real property, it can be transferred like any other property interest—subject to the seller's equitable lien for the purchase price. So when the neighbor died, equitable title passed to the beneficiary of his estate and the contract remained in effect (Choices A & D). The daughter, as the administrator of the neighbor's estate, then breached the land-sale contract when she demanded return of the $50,000 deposit in lieu of completing the contract. The deposit constitutes liquidated damages since the contract allows the seller to retain this money in the event of the neighbor's breach. Liquidated damages are recoverable only if they reasonably compensate the injured party. As a result, they are not recoverable if they constitute a penalty—eg, when the liquidated amount exceeds 15% of the purchase price. Here, the neighbor's $50,000 down payment is equivalent to 50% of the purchase price. This means that the court will likely hold that the liquidated damages constitute a penalty and that this express contractual provision is unenforceable (Choice C). As a result, the daughter is likely to prevail.\n\nEducational objective: Equitable conversion allows a buyer's equitable title to be transferred upon his/her death, thereby keeping the land-sale contract in effect. Liquidated damages under the contract are recoverable only if they reasonably compensate the injured party—eg, if the liquidated amount is less than 15% of the purchase price.\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 478 (2020) (seller's recovery of liquidated damages).\n\n27A Am. Jur. 2d Equitable Conversion § 13 (2020) (equitable conversion under land-sale contracts).","educationalObjective":"Equitable conversion allows a buyer's equitable title to be transferred upon his/her death, thereby keeping the land-sale contract in effect. Liquidated damages under the contract are recoverable only if they reasonably compensate the injured party—eg, if the liquidated amount is less than 15% of the purchase price.","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 478 (2020) (seller's recovery of liquidated damages).\n\n27A Am. Jur. 2d Equitable Conversion § 13 (2020) (equitable conversion under land-sale contracts).","rule":"Equitable conversion allows a buyer's equitable title to be transferred upon his/her death, thereby keeping the land-sale contract in effect. Liquidated damages under the contract are recoverable only if they reasonably compensate the injured party—eg, if the liquidated amount is less than 15% of the purchase price.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"Equitable conversion allows a buyer's equitable title to be transferred upon his/her death, thereby keeping the land-sale contract in effect. Liquidated damages under the contract are recoverable only if they reasonably compensate the injured party—eg, if the liquidated amount is less than 15% of the purchase price.","analysis":"The doctrine of equitable conversion applies during the period between contract formation and closing. During this executory period, the seller acquires the equitable right to the purchase price and the buyer acquires equitable title to the property. Since equitable title is treated as an interest in real property, it can be transferred like any other property interest—subject to the seller's equitable lien for the purchase price. So when the neighbor died, equitable title passed to the beneficiary of his estate and the contract remained in effect (Choices A & D). The daughter, as the administrator of the neighbor's estate, then breached the land-sale contract when she demanded return of the $50,000 deposit in lieu of completing the contract. The deposit constitutes liquidated damages since the contract allows the seller to retain this money in the event of the neighbor's breach. Liquidated damages are recoverable only if they reasonably compensate the injured party. As a result, they are not recoverable if they constitute a penalty—eg, when the liquidated amount exceeds 15% of the purchase price. Here, the neighbor's $50,000 down payment is equivalent to 50% of the purchase price. This means that the court will likely hold that the liquidated damages constitute a penalty and that this express contractual provision is unenforceable (Choice C). As a result, the daughter is likely to prevail.","conclusion":"Answer B: The daughter, because the provision relied upon by the man is unenforceable."},"questionZh":"一个人简单地拥有一块土地。他和邻近土地的所有者签订了一份书面协议，根据协议，邻居同意以 10 万美元（其公平市场价值）购买该男子的土地。该协议包含房地产买卖合同的所有基本条款，包括交割日期。邻居支付了所需的 50,000 美元首付款，根据合同，该男子可以保留这笔钱作为邻居违约时的违约金。在合同规定的交割日期之前，邻居去世了。截止日期后，邻居的女儿正式获得了遗产管理人的资格，并立即要求退还 50,000 美元的押金。该男子回应称，他认为这样的要求是女儿无意完成合同的声明，他认为合同已经结束。该男子进一步声称，他有权保留 50,000 美元作为违约金。此时，争议土地的合理市场价值已增至11万美元。女儿对该男子提起适当诉讼，追回 50,000 美元。该男子在答复中没有提出肯定的主张，但声称他有权保留合同规定的5万美元作为违约金。谁有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿则因邻居死亡而依法终止了合同。","B":"女儿，因为该男子所依据的条款无法执行。","C":"男方说，因为法院应该执行当事人的明示协议。","D":"男人，因为衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则防止合同因一方死亡而终止。"},"explanationZh":"衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则适用于合同订立和结束之间的期间。在此执行期间，卖方获得购买价格的衡平法权利，买方获得财产的衡平法所有权。由于衡平法所有权被视为不动产权益，因此它可以像任何其他财产权益一样转让，但须遵守卖方对购买价格的衡平法留置权。因此，当邻居去世时，衡平法上的所有权转移给其遗产的受益人，并且合同仍然有效（选择 A 和 D）。女儿作为邻居庄园的管理人，要求退还 5 万美元押金以代替完成合同，从而违反了土地买卖合同。这笔押金构成违约金，因为合同允许卖方在邻居违约时保留这笔钱。违约金只有在合理补偿受害方的情况下才可以追偿。因此，如果构成罚款，例如当清算金额超过购买价格的 15% 时，则无法收回。在这里，邻居的5万美元首付相当于购房价格的50%。这意味着法院可能会认为违约金构成处罚，并且该明确的合同条款不可执行（选项C）。结果，女儿很可能获胜。\n\n教育目标：衡平转换（equitable conversion）允许买方的公平所有权在其死亡时转移，从而保持土地销售合同的效力。合同项下的违约金只有在对受害方进行合理补偿的情况下才能得到追偿——例如，违约金低于购买价格的15%。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 478 (2020)（卖方追偿违约金）。\n\n27A 上午尤尔。 2d Equitable Conversion § 13 (2020)（土地销售合同下的衡平转换（equitable conversion））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"衡平法转换允许买方的衡平法所有权在其死亡时转移，从而保持土地销售合同的效力。合同项下的违约金只有在对受害方进行合理补偿的情况下才能得到追偿——例如，违约金低于购买价格的15%。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"衡平法转换允许买方的衡平法所有权在其死亡时转移，从而保持土地销售合同的效力。合同项下的违约金只有在对受害方进行合理补偿的情况下才能得到追偿——例如，违约金低于购买价格的15%。","analysis":"衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则适用于合同订立和结束之间的期间。在此执行期间，卖方获得购买价格的衡平法权利，买方获得财产的衡平法所有权。由于衡平法所有权被视为不动产权益，因此它可以像任何其他财产权益一样转让，但须遵守卖方对购买价格的衡平法留置权。因此，当邻居去世时，衡平法上的所有权转移给其遗产的受益人，并且合同仍然有效（选择 A 和 D）。女儿作为邻居庄园的管理人，要求退还 5 万美元押金以代替完成合同，从而违反了土地买卖合同。这笔押金构成违约金，因为合同允许卖方在邻居违约时保留这笔钱。违约金只有在合理补偿受害方的情况下才可以追偿。因此，如果构成罚款，例如当清算金额超过购买价格的 15% 时，则无法收回。在这里，邻居的5万美元首付相当于购房价格的50%。这意味着法院可能会认为违约金构成处罚，并且该明确的合同条款不可执行（选项C）。结果，女儿很可能获胜。\n\n教育目标：衡平转换（equitable conversion）允许买方的公平所有权在其死亡时转移，从而保持土地销售合同的效力。合同项下的违约金只有在对受害方进行合理补偿的情况下才能得到追偿——例如，违约金低于购买价格的15%。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 478 (2020)（卖方追偿违约金）。\n\n27A 上午尤尔。 2d Equitable Conversion § 13 (2020)（土地销售合同下的衡平转换（equitable conversion））。","conclusion":"答案 B：女儿，因为该男子所依据的条款无法执行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-af603b8a5e6f8dad"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":162,"sourceQuestionPage":476,"sourceQuestionPages":[476],"sourceExplanationPages":[477,478],"sourceAnswerPages":[477,478],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-163","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A landowner's driveway separated her property from that of her neighbor. Although the neighbor could access his property from the other side, he also wanted the ability to enter his property on the side that ran parallel to the driveway. He asked the landowner for permission to do so, and the landowner granted the neighbor an easement to use her driveway in order to access his property for a period of 20 years. Two years later, the landowner decided to build a fence at the entrance to the driveway. After it was built, the landowner locked the fence and did not give a key to the neighbor despite his requests for one. Twelve years later, the landowner took the fence down. The neighbor attempted to use the driveway, but the landowner prevented him from doing so, claiming that he no longer had the right to use it. The period of time to acquire rights by prescription in the jurisdiction is 10 years. Does the neighbor still have the right to use the landowner's driveway?","choices":{"A":"No, because the easement was terminated by estoppel.","B":"No, because the landowner prevented the neighbor from using the easement for the prescriptive period.","C":"Yes, because the neighbor did not act in an affirmative way to show a clear intent to relinquish his easement right.","D":"Yes, because the neighbor was granted an easement for a term of years, which has not expired."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Ways to terminate easements\n\nDestruction – servient estate condemned or destroyed by natural forces Abandonment - dominant estate abandons easement Merger - dominant & servient estates united in common ownership Prescription - servient owner prevents use for prescriptive period Estoppel - dominant owner estopped from asserting easement Release - express release in writing satisfying statute of frauds Mnemonic: DAMPER\n\nAn easement is a nonpossessory right to make specific, limited use of another's land (servient estate). Just as an easement can be created by prescription, an easement can be terminated by prescription. This requires the servient-estate owner to act in a manner inconsistent with the continued existence of the easement. The prescriptive activity must be hostile and adverse to the easement holder (nonpermissive), open and notorious (visible), and continued without interruption for the statutory period. Here, the landowner (servient-estate owner) granted the neighbor an easement to use her driveway. Without the neighbor's permission, the landowner later built a fence at the entrance to the driveway, locked the fence, and refused to give the neighbor a key. And since the landowner continuously maintained the fence for longer than the 10-year statutory period, the neighbor's easement was terminated by prescription. Therefore, the neighbor does not still have a right to use the driveway. (Choice A) An easement may be terminated by estoppel if the servient-estate owner changes position to his/her detriment in reliance on the easement holder's statements or conduct indicating that the easement has been abandoned (not seen here). (Choice C) An easement may be terminated by abandonment if the easement holder acts in a manner that shows a clear intent to relinquish his easement right. Here, the neighbor did nothing to abandon the easement, but it was still terminated by prescription. (Choice D) An easement granted for a term of years (here, 20 years) can be terminated prior to its expiration in the same manner as any other easement. So had the neighbor's easement not been terminated by prescription, it would have continued until the end of the 20-year term.\n\nEducational objective: Terminating an easement by prescription requires the servient-estate owner to act in a manner inconsistent with the continued existence of the easement. That prescriptive\n\nactivity must be hostile and adverse, open and notorious, and continued without interruption for the statutory period.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.7 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination of an easement by prescription).","educationalObjective":"Terminating an easement by prescription requires the servient-estate owner to act in a manner inconsistent with the continued existence of the easement. That prescriptive\n\nactivity must be hostile and adverse, open and notorious, and continued without interruption for the statutory period.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.7 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination of an easement by prescription).","rule":"Terminating an easement by prescription requires the servient-estate owner to act in a manner inconsistent with the continued existence of the easement. That prescriptive\n\nactivity must be hostile and adverse, open and notorious, and continued without interruption for the statutory period.","irac":{"issue":"Does the neighbor still have the right to use the landowner's driveway?","rule":"Terminating an easement by prescription requires the servient-estate owner to act in a manner inconsistent with the continued existence of the easement. That prescriptive\n\nactivity must be hostile and adverse, open and notorious, and continued without interruption for the statutory period.","analysis":"Ways to terminate easements\n\nDestruction – servient estate condemned or destroyed by natural forces Abandonment - dominant estate abandons easement Merger - dominant & servient estates united in common ownership Prescription - servient owner prevents use for prescriptive period Estoppel - dominant owner estopped from asserting easement Release - express release in writing satisfying statute of frauds Mnemonic: DAMPER\n\nAn easement is a nonpossessory right to make specific, limited use of another's land (servient estate). Just as an easement can be created by prescription, an easement can be terminated by prescription. This requires the servient-estate owner to act in a manner inconsistent with the continued existence of the easement. The prescriptive activity must be hostile and adverse to the easement holder (nonpermissive), open and notorious (visible), and continued without interruption for the statutory period. Here, the landowner (servient-estate owner) granted the neighbor an easement to use her driveway. Without the neighbor's permission, the landowner later built a fence at the entrance to the driveway, locked the fence, and refused to give the neighbor a key. And since the landowner continuously maintained the fence for longer than the 10-year statutory period, the neighbor's easement was terminated by prescription. Therefore, the neighbor does not still have a right to use the driveway. (Choice A) An easement may be terminated by estoppel if the servient-estate owner changes position to his/her detriment in reliance on the easement holder's statements or conduct indicating that the easement has been abandoned (not seen here). (Choice C) An easement may be terminated by abandonment if the easement holder acts in a manner that shows a clear intent to relinquish his easement right. Here, the neighbor did nothing to abandon the easement, but it was still terminated by prescription. (Choice D) An easement granted for a term of years (here, 20 years) can be terminated prior to its expiration in the same manner as any other easement. So had the neighbor's easement not been terminated by prescription, it would have continued until the end of the 20-year term.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the landowner prevented the neighbor from using the easement for the prescriptive period."},"questionZh":"一位地主的车道将她的财产与邻居的财产分开。尽管邻居可以从另一侧进入他的房产，但他也希望能够从与车道平行的一侧进入他的房产。他请求土地所有者允许这样做，土地所有者授予邻居使用她的车道的地役权，以便在 20 年内使用他的财产。两年后，土地所有者决定在车道入口处修建围栏。建成后，地主锁上了栅栏，尽管邻居要求一把钥匙，但他没有给他钥匙。十二年后，地主把栅栏拆掉了。邻居试图使用车道，但土地所有者阻止他这样做，声称他不再有权使用它。在管辖范围内凭时效取得权利的期限为10年。邻居仍然有权使用土地所有者的车道吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为地役权已因禁止反言而终止。","B":"不可以，因为土地所有者阻止邻居在规定期限内使用地役权。","C":"是的，因为邻居没有以积极的方式表现出放弃地役权的明确意图。","D":"是的，因为邻居获得了多年的地役权，但尚未到期。"},"explanationZh":"终止地役权的方法\n\n毁坏 - 被自然力量谴责或破坏的受役财产 遗弃 - 主导财产放弃地役权 合并 - 主导财产和受役财产合并为共同所有权 时效 - 受役所有者在规定期限内禁止使用 禁止反言 - 主导所有者禁止主张地役权 释放 - 以书面形式明确释放，满足欺诈法规要求 助记符：DAMPER\n\n地役权是对他人土地（役产）进行特定、有限使用的非占有性权利。正如可以根据时效创建地役权一样，地役权也可以根据时效终止。这要求供役地所有人的行为方式与地役权的持续存在不一致。规定活动必须对地役权持有者怀有敌意和不利（非许可性）、公开且臭名昭著（可见），并且在法定期限内不间断地持续进行。在这里，土地所有者（役用地产所有者）授予邻居使用其车道的地役权。未经邻居许可，地主后来在车道入口处筑起栅栏，锁上栅栏，并拒绝给邻居钥匙。由于土地所有者持续维护围栏的时间超过了10年法定期限，邻居的地役权被按时终止。因此，邻居仍然无权使用车道。 （选择 A）如果地役权所有人依据地役权持有人表明地役权已被放弃的声明或行为（此处未见）而改变立场，对其造成损害，则地役权可以通过禁止反言终止。 （选择C）如果地役权持有人的行为方式表明明确意图放弃其地役权，则地役权可以通过放弃而终止。在这里，邻居没有采取任何放弃地役权的措施，但地役权仍然根据规定终止。 （选项 D）授予期限为数年（此处为 20 年）的地役权可以在到期前以与任何其他地役权相同的方式终止。因此，如果邻居的地役权没有按规定终止，它会一直持续到 20 年期限结束。\n\n教育目标：通过时效终止地役权要求供役地所有人的行为方式与地役权的继续存在不一致。那个规定\n\n活动必须是敌对和不利的、公开的和臭名昭著的，并且在法定期限内不间断地持续进行。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.7（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（通过规定终止地役权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"通过时效终止地役权要求供役地所有人的行为方式与地役权的持续存在不一致。那个规定\n\n活动必须是敌对和不利的、公开的和臭名昭著的，并且在法定期限内不间断地持续进行。","iracZh":{"issue":"邻居仍然有权使用土地所有者的车道吗？","rule":"通过时效终止地役权要求供役地所有人的行为方式与地役权的持续存在不一致。那个规定\n\n活动必须是敌对和不利的、公开的和臭名昭著的，并且在法定期限内不间断地持续进行。","analysis":"终止地役权的方法\n\n毁坏 - 被自然力量谴责或破坏的受役财产 遗弃 - 主导财产放弃地役权 合并 - 主导财产和受役财产合并为共同所有权 时效 - 受役所有者在规定期限内禁止使用 禁止反言 - 主导所有者禁止主张地役权 释放 - 以书面形式明确释放，满足欺诈法规要求 助记符：DAMPER\n\n地役权是对他人土地（役产）进行特定、有限使用的非占有性权利。正如可以根据时效创建地役权一样，地役权也可以根据时效终止。这要求供役地所有人的行为方式与地役权的持续存在不一致。规定活动必须对地役权持有者怀有敌意和不利（非许可性）、公开且臭名昭著（可见），并且在法定期限内不间断地持续进行。在这里，土地所有者（役用地产所有者）授予邻居使用其车道的地役权。未经邻居许可，地主后来在车道入口处筑起栅栏，锁上栅栏，并拒绝给邻居钥匙。由于土地所有者持续维护围栏的时间超过了10年法定期限，邻居的地役权被按时终止。因此，邻居仍然无权使用车道。 （选择 A）如果地役权所有人依据地役权持有人表明地役权已被放弃的声明或行为（此处未见）而改变立场，对其造成损害，则地役权可以通过禁止反言终止。 （选择C）如果地役权持有人的行为方式表明明确意图放弃其地役权，则地役权可以通过放弃而终止。在这里，邻居没有采取任何放弃地役权的措施，但地役权仍然根据规定终止。 （选项 D）授予期限为数年（此处为 20 年）的地役权可以在到期前以与任何其他地役权相同的方式终止。因此，如果邻居的地役权没有按规定终止，它会一直持续到 20 年期限结束。\n\n教育目标：通过时效终止地役权要求供役地所有人的行为方式与地役权的继续存在不一致。那个规定\n\n活动必须是敌对和不利的、公开的和臭名昭著的，并且在法定期限内不间断地持续进行。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.7（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（通过规定终止地役权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为土地所有者阻止邻居在规定期限内使用地役权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"easement by prescription","ipa":"/ˈizmənt bˈaɪ pɹʌskɹˈɪpʃən/","syllables":"ease·ment by pre·scrip·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"时效地役权：公开、敌意且连续使用达到法定期限而取得","contextualMeaning":"时效地役权：公开、敌意且连续使用达到法定期限而取得","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Terminating an easement by prescription requires the servient-estate owner to act in a manner inconsistent with the continued existence of the easement.","synonyms":["prescriptive easement"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":163,"sourceQuestionPage":479,"sourceQuestionPages":[479],"sourceExplanationPages":[480,481],"sourceAnswerPages":[480,481],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-164","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A landowner orally gave his neighbor permission to share the use of a private road on the landowner's land so that the neighbor could have more convenient access to the neighbor's land. Only the landowner maintained the road. After the neighbor had used the road on a daily basis for three years, the landowner conveyed his land to a grantee, who immediately notified the neighbor that the neighbor was not to use the road. The neighbor sued the grantee, seeking a declaration that the neighbor had a right to continue to use the road. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The grantee, because an oral license is invalid.","B":"The grantee, because the neighbor had a license that the grantee could terminate at any time.","C":"The neighbor, because the grantee is estopped from terminating the neighbor's use of the road.","D":"The neighbor, because the neighbor's use of the road was open and notorious when the grantee purchased the land."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Licenses, easements & profits\n\nLicense Revocable privilege to enter & use another's land for specific purpose\n\nEasement Nonpossessory interest in land that gives easement holder right to:\n\nuse another's land for specific purpose (ie, affirmative easement) or prohibit other owner from doing something on land that owner would otherwise be allowed to do (ie, negative easement) Profit Nonpossessory interest in land that gives profit holder right to enter another's land to remove products of soil (eg, timber, minerals, oil)\n\nA license is a grant of permission to enter and use another's land for a specific purpose. Unlike easements, licenses do not create an interest in the land. As a result, they are not subject to the statute of frauds and can be created orally or by another act demonstrating the licensor's intent to let the licensee enter the property (Choice A). Licenses can generally be revoked at any time and terminate automatically upon (1) the death of either party or (2) conveyance of the licensed property. Here, the landowner orally gave his neighbor permission to use a private road on his land. This created a license, which terminated automatically when the landowner conveyed his land to the grantee. Additionally, the grantee's immediately notifying the neighbor to stop using the road demonstrates that the grantee did not intend to revive the license. As a result, the neighbor has no right to continue to use the road, and the grantee is likely to prevail. (Choice C) A license may become irrevocable, and the licensor may be estopped (ie, precluded) from revoking it, if the licensee expends substantial money, labor, or other resources in reliance on the license. But estoppel does not apply here since the landowner alone maintained the road and there is nothing to suggest that the neighbor expended any resources to use it. (Choice D) Open and notorious use goes to determining whether a prescriptive easement was acquired. But the neighbor's use of the road was with the landowner's permission, which negates the nonpermissive use required for such an easement.\n\nEducational objective: A license is a grant of permission to enter and use another's land for a specific purpose. It can be revoked at any time and terminates automatically upon (1) the death of either party or (2) conveyance of the licensed property.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property §§ 515, 519 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (creation and revocation of licenses).\n\nRau v. Collins, 891 A.2d 1175, 1184 (Md. App. 2006) (stating that a license terminates automatically upon sale of the licensed land).","educationalObjective":"A license is a grant of permission to enter and use another's land for a specific purpose. It can be revoked at any time and terminates automatically upon (1) the death of either party or (2) conveyance of the licensed property.","references":"Restatement of Property §§ 515, 519 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (creation and revocation of licenses).\n\nRau v. Collins, 891 A.2d 1175, 1184 (Md. App. 2006) (stating that a license terminates automatically upon sale of the licensed land).","rule":"A license is a grant of permission to enter and use another's land for a specific purpose. It can be revoked at any time and terminates automatically upon (1) the death of either party or (2) conveyance of the licensed property.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"A license is a grant of permission to enter and use another's land for a specific purpose. It can be revoked at any time and terminates automatically upon (1) the death of either party or (2) conveyance of the licensed property.","analysis":"Licenses, easements & profits\n\nLicense Revocable privilege to enter & use another's land for specific purpose\n\nEasement Nonpossessory interest in land that gives easement holder right to:\n\nuse another's land for specific purpose (ie, affirmative easement) or prohibit other owner from doing something on land that owner would otherwise be allowed to do (ie, negative easement) Profit Nonpossessory interest in land that gives profit holder right to enter another's land to remove products of soil (eg, timber, minerals, oil)\n\nA license is a grant of permission to enter and use another's land for a specific purpose. Unlike easements, licenses do not create an interest in the land. As a result, they are not subject to the statute of frauds and can be created orally or by another act demonstrating the licensor's intent to let the licensee enter the property (Choice A). Licenses can generally be revoked at any time and terminate automatically upon (1) the death of either party or (2) conveyance of the licensed property. Here, the landowner orally gave his neighbor permission to use a private road on his land. This created a license, which terminated automatically when the landowner conveyed his land to the grantee. Additionally, the grantee's immediately notifying the neighbor to stop using the road demonstrates that the grantee did not intend to revive the license. As a result, the neighbor has no right to continue to use the road, and the grantee is likely to prevail. (Choice C) A license may become irrevocable, and the licensor may be estopped (ie, precluded) from revoking it, if the licensee expends substantial money, labor, or other resources in reliance on the license. But estoppel does not apply here since the landowner alone maintained the road and there is nothing to suggest that the neighbor expended any resources to use it. (Choice D) Open and notorious use goes to determining whether a prescriptive easement was acquired. But the neighbor's use of the road was with the landowner's permission, which negates the nonpermissive use required for such an easement.","conclusion":"Answer B: The grantee, because the neighbor had a license that the grantee could terminate at any time."},"questionZh":"土地所有者口头允许邻居共享土地所有者土地上的私人道路，以便邻居可以更方便地进入邻居的土地。只有土地所有者维护道路。邻居每天使用这条路三年后，土地所有者将其土地转让给受让人，受让人立即通知邻居不要使用这条路。邻居起诉受让人，要求声明邻居有权继续使用该道路。谁有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"受让人，因为口头许可无效。","B":"受让人，因为邻居拥有受让人可以随时终止的许可证。","C":"邻居，因为受让人不得终止邻居对道路的使用。","D":"邻居，因为在受让人购买土地时，邻居对道路的使用是公开的并且臭名昭著。"},"explanationZh":"许可证、地役权和利润\n\n出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的许可可撤销特权\n\n地役权 土地的非占有权益，赋予地役权持有人以下权利：\n\n将他人的土地用于特定目的（即，肯定地役权）或禁止其他所有者在所有者可以做的事情上做某事（即，消极地役权） 利润 对土地的非占有权益，使利润持有者有权进入他人的土地以去除土壤产品（例如，木材、矿物、石油）\n\n许可证是出于特定目的而授予进入和使用他人土地的许可。与地役权不同，许可证不会产生土地权益。因此，它们不受欺诈法的约束，并且可以通过口头或通过其他行为来创建，表明许可人让被许可人进入该财产的意图（选择 A）。许可证通常可以随时撤销，并在 (1) 任何一方死亡或 (2) 许可财产转让时自动终止。在这里，土地所有者口头允许他的邻居在他的土地上使用私人道路。这就产生了许可证，当土地所有者将其土地转让给受让人时，该许可证自动终止。此外，受让人立即通知邻居停止使用该道路表明受让人无意恢复许可证。结果，邻居无权继续使用该道路，受让人很可能获胜。 （选择C）如果被许可人花费大量金钱、劳动力或其他资源来依赖许可，则许可可能变得不可撤销，并且许可人可能被禁止（即排除）撤销许可。但禁止反言在这里并不适用，因为土地所有者独自维护了这条道路，并且没有任何迹象表明邻居花费了任何资源来使用它。 （选项 D）公开且臭名昭著的使用用于确定是否获得了规定的地役权。但邻居使用这条道路是经过土地所有者许可的，这就否定了这种地役权所要求的非许可使用。\n\n教育目标：许可证是授予出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的许可。它可以随时撤销，并在 (1) 任何一方死亡或 (2) 许可财产转让时自动终止。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 515, 519（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（许可证的创建和撤销）。\n\nRau v. Collins, 891 A.2d 1175, 1184 (Md. App. 2006)（规定许可证在出售许可土地后自动终止）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"许可证是出于特定目的而授予进入和使用他人土地的许可。它可以随时撤销，并在 (1) 任何一方死亡或 (2) 许可财产转让时自动终止。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"许可证是出于特定目的而授予进入和使用他人土地的许可。它可以随时撤销，并在 (1) 任何一方死亡或 (2) 许可财产转让时自动终止。","analysis":"许可证、地役权和利润\n\n出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的许可可撤销特权\n\n地役权 土地的非占有权益，赋予地役权持有人以下权利：\n\n将他人的土地用于特定目的（即，肯定地役权）或禁止其他所有者在所有者可以做的事情上做某事（即，消极地役权） 利润 对土地的非占有权益，使利润持有者有权进入他人的土地以去除土壤产品（例如，木材、矿物、石油）\n\n许可证是出于特定目的而授予进入和使用他人土地的许可。与地役权不同，许可证不会产生土地权益。因此，它们不受欺诈法的约束，并且可以通过口头或通过其他行为来创建，表明许可人让被许可人进入该财产的意图（选择 A）。许可证通常可以随时撤销，并在 (1) 任何一方死亡或 (2) 许可财产转让时自动终止。在这里，土地所有者口头允许他的邻居在他的土地上使用私人道路。这就产生了许可证，当土地所有者将其土地转让给受让人时，该许可证自动终止。此外，受让人立即通知邻居停止使用该道路表明受让人无意恢复许可证。结果，邻居无权继续使用该道路，受让人很可能获胜。 （选择C）如果被许可人花费大量金钱、劳动力或其他资源来依赖许可，则许可可能变得不可撤销，并且许可人可能被禁止（即排除）撤销许可。但禁止反言在这里并不适用，因为土地所有者独自维护了这条道路，并且没有任何迹象表明邻居花费了任何资源来使用它。 （选项 D）公开且臭名昭著的使用用于确定是否获得了规定的地役权。但邻居使用这条道路是经过土地所有者许可的，这就否定了这种地役权所要求的非许可使用。\n\n教育目标：许可证是授予出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的许可。它可以随时撤销，并在 (1) 任何一方死亡或 (2) 许可财产转让时自动终止。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 515, 519（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（许可证的创建和撤销）。\n\nRau v. Collins, 891 A.2d 1175, 1184 (Md. App. 2006)（规定许可证在出售许可土地后自动终止）。","conclusion":"答案 B：受让人，因为邻居拥有受让人可以随时终止的许可证。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":164,"sourceQuestionPage":482,"sourceQuestionPages":[482],"sourceExplanationPages":[483,484],"sourceAnswerPages":[483,484],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-165","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A man's home was mortgaged to a bank by a duly recorded purchase-money mortgage. Last year, the man replaced all of the home's old windows with new windows. Each new window consists of a window frame with three inserts: regular windows, storm windows, and screens. The windows are designed so that each insert can be easily inserted or removed from the window frame without tools to adjust to seasonal change and to facilitate the cleaning of the inserts. The new windows were expensive. The man purchased them on credit, signed a financing statement, and granted a security interest in the windows to the supplier of the windows. The supplier promptly and properly filed and recorded the financing statement before the windows were installed. The man stored the old windows in the basement of the home. This year, the man has suffered severe financial reverses and has defaulted on his mortgage obligation to the bank and on his obligation to the supplier. The bank brought an appropriate action to enjoin the supplier from its proposed repossession of the window inserts. In the action, for whom should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"The bank, because its mortgage was recorded first.","B":"The bank, because windows and screens, no matter their characteristics, are an integral part of a house.","C":"The supplier, because the availability of the old windows enables the bank to return the home to its original condition.","D":"The supplier, because the inserts are removable."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A mortgage is a lien against real property that is used to secure repayment of a debt. This gives the recipient of the mortgage (ie, the mortgagee) a legal interest in the mortgaged property and any fixtures later added to that property. A fixture is an object (ie, personal property) that is: attached to real property with the intent that it remain attached and\n\nused for some larger component (eg, lighting, water drainage) or function (eg, plumbing, septic) of the real property.\n\nIn other words, a fixture is an object that is so attached to real property that it is essentially a part of that property. Here, the new window inserts were integral as a larger component of the man's house. But they are not fixtures because they are easily removable and therefore not attached to the house. As a result, the window inserts are not part of the bank's purchase-money mortgage. This means that the bank has no standing to enjoin (ie, stop) the supplier from repossessing them (Choice B). (Choice A) A first recorded lien generally has priority over subsequent liens on the same property, and a purchase-money mortgage (recorded or not) generally has priority over all other liens on the property. But here, priority is irrelevant since the bank and the supplier have mortgages over different property—the home and new windows, respectively. (Choice C) Since the window inserts are easily removable, they would not be fixtures even if the old windows were unavailable and the bank could not return the home to its original condition.\n\nEducational objective: A mortgage includes the property and any fixtures added to that property before or after the mortgage was created. A fixture is an item that is (1) attached to property with the intent that it remain attached and (2) used for a larger component or function of the property.\n\nReferences\n35A Am. Jur. 2d Fixtures § 1 (2019) (defining fixture).","educationalObjective":"A mortgage includes the property and any fixtures added to that property before or after the mortgage was created. A fixture is an item that is (1) attached to property with the intent that it remain attached and (2) used for a larger component or function of the property.","references":"35A Am. Jur. 2d Fixtures § 1 (2019) (defining fixture).","rule":"A mortgage includes the property and any fixtures added to that property before or after the mortgage was created. A fixture is an item that is (1) attached to property with the intent that it remain attached and (2) used for a larger component or function of the property.","irac":{"issue":"In the action, for whom should the court rule?","rule":"A mortgage includes the property and any fixtures added to that property before or after the mortgage was created. A fixture is an item that is (1) attached to property with the intent that it remain attached and (2) used for a larger component or function of the property.","analysis":"A mortgage is a lien against real property that is used to secure repayment of a debt. This gives the recipient of the mortgage (ie, the mortgagee) a legal interest in the mortgaged property and any fixtures later added to that property. A fixture is an object (ie, personal property) that is: attached to real property with the intent that it remain attached and\n\nused for some larger component (eg, lighting, water drainage) or function (eg, plumbing, septic) of the real property.\n\nIn other words, a fixture is an object that is so attached to real property that it is essentially a part of that property. Here, the new window inserts were integral as a larger component of the man's house. But they are not fixtures because they are easily removable and therefore not attached to the house. As a result, the window inserts are not part of the bank's purchase-money mortgage. This means that the bank has no standing to enjoin (ie, stop) the supplier from repossessing them (Choice B). (Choice A) A first recorded lien generally has priority over subsequent liens on the same property, and a purchase-money mortgage (recorded or not) generally has priority over all other liens on the property. But here, priority is irrelevant since the bank and the supplier have mortgages over different property—the home and new windows, respectively. (Choice C) Since the window inserts are easily removable, they would not be fixtures even if the old windows were unavailable and the bank could not return the home to its original condition.","conclusion":"Answer D: The supplier, because the inserts are removable."},"questionZh":"一名男子的房屋通过正式记录的购房款抵押贷款抵押给了一家银行。去年，该男子将家里所有的旧窗户换成了新窗户。每个新窗户都由带有三个插入物的窗框组成：普通窗户、防风窗和纱窗。窗户的设计使得每个插件都可以轻松地插入窗框或从窗框上取下，无需工具即可适应季节变化并方便插件的清洁。新窗户很贵。该男子赊购了这些窗户，签署了一份融资声明，并向窗户供应商授予了窗户的担保权益。供应商在窗户安装前及时正确地提交并记录财务报表。该男子将旧窗户存放在家中的地下室。今年，该男子遭受了严重的财务困境，并且拖欠了对银行的抵押贷款义务和对供应商的义务。该银行采取了适当行动，禁止供应商收回窗饰的提议。在诉讼中，法院应该为谁作出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"银行，因为它的抵押贷款是先记录的。","B":"银行，因为窗户和屏风，无论其特性如何，都是房屋不可分割的一部分。","C":"供应商，因为旧窗户的可用性使银行能够将房屋恢复到原来的状态。","D":"供应商，因为插件是可拆卸的。"},"explanationZh":"抵押贷款是针对不动产的留置权，用于确保债务偿还。这使抵押贷款的接受者（即抵押权人）对抵押财产以及后来添加到该财产的任何固定装置享有合法权益。固定装置是一个物体（即个人财产），它： 附着在不动产上，目的是使其保持附着状态，并且\n\n用于不动产的一些较大组件（例如照明、排水）或功能（例如管道、化粪池）。\n\n换句话说，固定装置是一个与不动产紧密相连的物体，它本质上是该不动产的一部分。在这里，新的窗户插件成为了男人房子的一个更大的组成部分。但它们不是固定装置，因为它们很容易拆卸，因此不固定在房子上。因此，窗户嵌条不属于银行购房款抵押贷款的一部分。这意味着银行无权禁止（即阻止）供应商收回它们（选择 B）。 （选择 A）第一个记录的留置权通常优先于同一财产的后续留置权，而购房款抵押贷款（记录或未记录）通常优先于该财产的所有其他留置权。但在这里，优先权无关紧要，因为银行和供应商分别对不同的财产（房屋和新窗户）拥有抵押贷款。 （选项C）由于窗插件很容易拆卸，因此即使旧窗户不可用并且银行无法将房屋恢复到原来的状态，它们也不会成为固定装置。\n\n教育目标：抵押贷款包括房产以及在抵押贷款创建之前或之后添加到该房产的任何固定装置。固定装置是一个项目，它 (1) 附加到财产上，并使其保持附加状态；(2) 用于财产的更大组件或功能。\n\n参考文献\n35A 上午。尤尔。 2d Fixtures § 1 (2019)（定义夹具）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"抵押贷款包括房产以及在抵押贷款创建之前或之后添加到该房产的任何固定装置。固定装置是一个项目，它 (1) 附加到财产上，并使其保持附加状态；(2) 用于财产的更大组件或功能。","iracZh":{"issue":"在诉讼中，法院应该为谁作出裁决？","rule":"抵押贷款包括房产以及在抵押贷款创建之前或之后添加到该房产的任何固定装置。固定装置是一个项目，它 (1) 附加到财产上，并使其保持附加状态；(2) 用于财产的更大组件或功能。","analysis":"抵押贷款是针对不动产的留置权，用于确保债务偿还。这使抵押贷款的接受者（即抵押权人）对抵押财产以及后来添加到该财产的任何固定装置享有合法权益。固定装置是一个物体（即个人财产），它： 附着在不动产上，目的是使其保持附着状态，并且\n\n用于不动产的一些较大组件（例如照明、排水）或功能（例如管道、化粪池）。\n\n换句话说，固定装置是一个与不动产紧密相连的物体，它本质上是该不动产的一部分。在这里，新的窗户插件成为了男人房子的一个更大的组成部分。但它们不是固定装置，因为它们很容易拆卸，因此不固定在房子上。因此，窗户嵌条不属于银行购房款抵押贷款的一部分。这意味着银行无权禁止（即阻止）供应商收回它们（选择 B）。 （选择 A）第一个记录的留置权通常优先于同一财产的后续留置权，而购房款抵押贷款（记录或未记录）通常优先于该财产的所有其他留置权。但在这里，优先权无关紧要，因为银行和供应商分别对不同的财产（房屋和新窗户）拥有抵押贷款。 （选项C）由于窗插件很容易拆卸，因此即使旧窗户不可用并且银行无法将房屋恢复到原来的状态，它们也不会成为固定装置。\n\n教育目标：抵押贷款包括房产以及在抵押贷款创建之前或之后添加到该房产的任何固定装置。固定装置是一个项目，它 (1) 附加到财产上，并使其保持附加状态；(2) 用于财产的更大组件或功能。\n\n参考文献\n35A 上午。尤尔。 2d Fixtures § 1 (2019)（定义夹具）。","conclusion":"答案 D：供应商，因为插件是可拆卸的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-c108affaa420ef8e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":165,"sourceQuestionPage":485,"sourceQuestionPages":[485],"sourceExplanationPages":[486,487],"sourceAnswerPages":[486,487],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-166","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A buyer and a seller entered into a written contract for the sale of land. The contract satisfied the requirements of the statute of frauds. Because the buyer needed time to obtain financing, the buyer and the seller did not agree upon a closing date, and the written contract did not contain a stated closing date. Ten days after signing the contract, the buyer and the seller orally agreed to rescind the contract. The next day, the seller sold the land to a third party. Two days after that sale, the original buyer told the seller that she had changed her mind and wanted to complete their contract. When the seller told her that he had sold the land to a third party, she sued him for breach of the written contract. For whom will the court find?","choices":{"A":"For the buyer, because she informed the seller within a reasonable time that she desired to close the transaction.","B":"For the buyer, because the agreement to rescind the contract was not in a writing signed by the buyer and the seller.","C":"For the seller, because the contract failed to contain a stated closing date.","D":"For the seller, because the oral rescission was valid."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Statute of frauds requirements for real estate contracts\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed\n\neg, receipt, check, emails Signature Handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged\n\neg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials Essential Typically includes: terms identity of parties words of intent to buy or sell property description sales price The statute of frauds requires a writing to create or modify an enforceable contract for the sale of land, but a writing is not required to rescind (ie, cancel) the contract. Therefore, the buyer and the seller's oral agreement to rescind the contract was valid (Choice B). (Choice A) The contract had already been rescinded when the buyer told the seller that she desired to close the transaction. As a result, the seller was no longer required to close the transaction. (Choice C) A writing memorializing a land-sale contract must contain all essential terms to be enforceable under the statute of frauds. However, a closing date is not an essential term.\n\nEducational objective: Under the statute of frauds, a writing is needed to create or modify an enforceable contract for the sale of land but is not needed to rescind the contract.","educationalObjective":"Under the statute of frauds, a writing is needed to create or modify an enforceable contract for the sale of land but is not needed to rescind the contract.","references":"","rule":"Under the statute of frauds, a writing is needed to create or modify an enforceable contract for the sale of land but is not needed to rescind the contract.","irac":{"issue":"For whom will the court find?","rule":"Under the statute of frauds, a writing is needed to create or modify an enforceable contract for the sale of land but is not needed to rescind the contract.","analysis":"Statute of frauds requirements for real estate contracts\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed\n\neg, receipt, check, emails Signature Handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged\n\neg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials Essential Typically includes: terms identity of parties words of intent to buy or sell property description sales price The statute of frauds requires a writing to create or modify an enforceable contract for the sale of land, but a writing is not required to rescind (ie, cancel) the contract. Therefore, the buyer and the seller's oral agreement to rescind the contract was valid (Choice B). (Choice A) The contract had already been rescinded when the buyer told the seller that she desired to close the transaction. As a result, the seller was no longer required to close the transaction. (Choice C) A writing memorializing a land-sale contract must contain all essential terms to be enforceable under the statute of frauds. However, a closing date is not an essential term.","conclusion":"Answer D: For the seller, because the oral rescission was valid."},"questionZh":"买方和卖方签订了书面土地出售合同。该合同满足欺诈法的要求。由于买方需要时间获得融资，买方和卖方没有就交割日期达成一致，书面合同中也没有规定交割日期。合同签订十天后，买卖双方口头同意解除合同。第二天，卖方将土地出售给第三方。出售两天后，原买家告诉卖家她改变了主意并希望完成合同。当卖方告诉她他已将土地卖给第三方时，她起诉他违反书面合同。法庭会为谁判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"对于买方来说，因为她在合理的时间内通知卖方她希望完成交易。","B":"对于买方来说，因为解除合同的协议不是买卖双方以书面形式签署的。","C":"对于卖方来说，因为合同未包含规定的截止日期。","D":"对于卖方来说，因为口头撤销有效。"},"explanationZh":"房地产合同欺诈法规要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件\n\n例如，收据、支票、电子邮件 签名 被指控方的手写签名或其他识别标记\n\n例如，信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名缩写 基本内容 通常包括： 条款 当事人身份 购买或出售财产的意向 描述 销售价格 欺诈法要求以书面形式创建或修改可执行的土地销售合同，但撤销（即取消）合同不需要书面形式。因此，买卖双方口头约定解除合同的协议有效（选项B）。 （选项A） 当买方告诉卖方她希望结束交易时，合同已经解除。因此，卖方不再需要关闭交易。 （选择 C）土地销售合同的书面记录必须包含根据欺诈法可强制执行的所有基本条款。然而，截止日期并不是一个必要术语。\n\n教育目标：根据欺诈法，制定或修改可执行的土地出售合同需要书面文件，但撤销合同不需要书面文件。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据欺诈法，制定或修改可执行的土地出售合同需要书面文件，但撤销合同不需要书面文件。","iracZh":{"issue":"法庭会为谁判决？","rule":"根据欺诈法，制定或修改可执行的土地出售合同需要书面文件，但撤销合同不需要书面文件。","analysis":"房地产合同欺诈法规要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件\n\n例如，收据、支票、电子邮件 签名 被指控方的手写签名或其他识别标记\n\n例如，信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名缩写 基本内容 通常包括： 条款 当事人身份 购买或出售财产的意向 描述 销售价格 欺诈法要求以书面形式创建或修改可执行的土地销售合同，但撤销（即取消）合同不需要书面形式。因此，买卖双方口头约定解除合同的协议有效（选项B）。 （选项A） 当买方告诉卖方她希望结束交易时，合同已经解除。因此，卖方不再需要关闭交易。 （选择 C）土地销售合同的书面记录必须包含根据欺诈法可强制执行的所有基本条款。然而，截止日期并不是一个必要术语。\n\n教育目标：根据欺诈法，制定或修改可执行的土地出售合同需要书面文件，但撤销合同不需要书面文件。","conclusion":"答案 D：对于卖方来说，因为口头撤销有效。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":166,"sourceQuestionPage":488,"sourceQuestionPages":[488],"sourceExplanationPages":[489],"sourceAnswerPages":[489],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-167","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Easements","question":"A fan had a season ticket for the local team's hockey games at an arena (Section B, Row 12, Seat 16). During the intermission between the first and second periods of a game between the local team and the visiting team, the fan solicited signatures for a petition urging that the coach of the local team be fired. The arena and the local team are owned by a privately owned entity. As evidenced by many prominently displayed signs, the entity prohibits all solicitations anywhere within the arena at any time and in any manner. The entity notified the fan to cease her solicitation of signatures. The fan continued to seek signatures on her petition during the local team's next three home games at the arena. Each time, the entity notified the fan to cease such solicitation. The fan announced her intention to seek signatures on her petition again during the local team's next home game at the arena. The entity wrote a letter informing the fan that her season ticket was canceled and tendering a refund for the unused portion. The fan refused the tender and brought an appropriate action to establish the right to attend all home games. In this action, for whom will the court decide?","choices":{"A":"The entity, because it has a right and obligation to control activities on realty it owns and has invited the public to visit.","B":"The entity, because the fan's ticket to hockey games created only a license.","C":"The fan, because, having paid value for the ticket, her right to be present cannot be revoked.","D":"The fan, because she was not committing a nuisance by her activities."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Licenses\n\nDefinition Revocable privilege to enter & use another's land for specific purpose\n\nCreation Orally, in writing, or by another act demonstrating licensor's intent to create license Termination At any time upon revocation by licensor Automatically upon (1) death of licensor or licensee or (2) conveyance of licensed property A ticket to an event is a license that creates a limited right for the ticketholder (licensee) to enter and use the venue for a specific purpose. The license is revocable at any time and for any lawful reason, including failure to comply with any conditions that the owner of the venue (licensor) has placed on the ticketholder (as seen here). This is true even though the ticketholder paid value for the ticket. However, the owner may be required to pay damages for revoking the license (eg, refunding the unused portion of the ticket) (Choice C). (Choice A) Even if the entity has a duty to control activities on property it owns and has invited the public to visit, that duty would arise out of a need to protect the health and safety of the public. Prohibiting solicitations does not fall within protection of health and safety, so this rationale would not justify the entity's revocation of the ticket. (Choice D) A nuisance arises from a person's activities on his/her own land, so the fan could not commit a nuisance on the entity's property. But the entity need not prove that the fan was committing a nuisance to revoke her license to attend hockey games.\n\nEducational objective: A license (eg, ticket to an event) is revocable at any time and for any lawful reason. But if the licensee paid value for the license, the licensor may be required to pay damages for revoking it.\n\nReferences\nYarde Metals, Inc. v. New England Patriots Ltd. P'ship, 834 N.E.2d 1233, 1236 (Mass. App. 2005) (upholding revocation of season tickets based on licensee's misconduct).","educationalObjective":"A license (eg, ticket to an event) is revocable at any time and for any lawful reason. But if the licensee paid value for the license, the licensor may be required to pay damages for revoking it.","references":"Yarde Metals, Inc. v. New England Patriots Ltd. P'ship, 834 N.E.2d 1233, 1236 (Mass. App. 2005) (upholding revocation of season tickets based on licensee's misconduct).","rule":"A license (eg, ticket to an event) is revocable at any time and for any lawful reason. But if the licensee paid value for the license, the licensor may be required to pay damages for revoking it.","irac":{"issue":"In this action, for whom will the court decide?","rule":"A license (eg, ticket to an event) is revocable at any time and for any lawful reason. But if the licensee paid value for the license, the licensor may be required to pay damages for revoking it.","analysis":"Licenses\n\nDefinition Revocable privilege to enter & use another's land for specific purpose\n\nCreation Orally, in writing, or by another act demonstrating licensor's intent to create license Termination At any time upon revocation by licensor Automatically upon (1) death of licensor or licensee or (2) conveyance of licensed property A ticket to an event is a license that creates a limited right for the ticketholder (licensee) to enter and use the venue for a specific purpose. The license is revocable at any time and for any lawful reason, including failure to comply with any conditions that the owner of the venue (licensor) has placed on the ticketholder (as seen here). This is true even though the ticketholder paid value for the ticket. However, the owner may be required to pay damages for revoking the license (eg, refunding the unused portion of the ticket) (Choice C). (Choice A) Even if the entity has a duty to control activities on property it owns and has invited the public to visit, that duty would arise out of a need to protect the health and safety of the public. Prohibiting solicitations does not fall within protection of health and safety, so this rationale would not justify the entity's revocation of the ticket. (Choice D) A nuisance arises from a person's activities on his/her own land, so the fan could not commit a nuisance on the entity's property. But the entity need not prove that the fan was committing a nuisance to revoke her license to attend hockey games.","conclusion":"Answer B: The entity, because the fan's ticket to hockey games created only a license."},"questionZh":"一名球迷持有当地球队在竞技场（B 区，第 12 排，第 16 座位）举行的曲棍球比赛的季票。在本地队与客队比赛第一节和第二节比赛的中场休息期间，这名球迷征集请愿书，要求解雇本地队教练。球馆和当地球队归私营实体所有。正如许多显眼的标志所证明的那样，该实体禁止在任何时间、任何时间、在竞技场内以任何方式进行任何招揽活动。该实体通知该粉丝停止征集签名。在当地球队接下来的三场主场比赛中，这名球迷继续在她的请愿书上寻求签名。每次，该实体都会通知粉丝停止此类招揽行为。该球迷宣布，她打算在当地球队下一场主场比赛期间再次在请愿书上寻求签名。该实体写了一封信，通知该球迷她的季票已被取消，并为未使用的部分退款。该球迷拒绝了投标，并采取了适当的行动来确立观看所有主场比赛的权利。在这起诉讼中，法院将为谁做出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"该实体，因为它有权利和义务控制其拥有的不动产上的活动，并邀请公众参观。","B":"该实体，因为球迷的曲棍球比赛门票仅创建了一个许可证。","C":"粉丝，因为，她已经支付了门票的费用，她在场的权利就不能被撤销。","D":"粉丝，因为她的活动并没有造成滋扰。"},"explanationZh":"许可证\n\n定义 出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的可撤销特权\n\n创建 口头、书面或通过其他行为表明许可方意图创建许可 终止 许可方随时撤销 在 (1) 许可方或被许可方死亡或 (2) 许可财产转让时自动终止 活动门票是一种许可，为持票人（被许可方）为特定目的进入和使用场地提供有限权利。该许可证可随时出于任何合法原因撤销，包括未能遵守场馆所有者（许可方）向持票人提出的任何条件（如此处所示）。即使持票人为门票支付了一定的费用，情况也是如此。然而，所有者可能需要因吊销许可证而支付损害赔偿金（例如，退还门票的未使用部分）（选择C）。 （选择 A）即使该实体有义务控制其拥有的财产上的活动并邀请公众参观，该义务也是出于保护公众健康和安全的需要而产生的。禁止招揽行为不属于对健康和安全的保护范围，因此这一理由不能成为该实体撤销罚单的理由。 （选项D） 滋扰是由一个人在他/她自己的土地上的活动引起的，因此粉丝不能对实体的财产造成滋扰。但该实体无需证明该球迷有滋扰行为，即可吊销她观看曲棍球比赛的执照。\n\n教育目标：许可证（例如活动门票）可随时因任何合法原因撤销。但如果被许可人为许可支付了费用，则许可人可能需要支付撤销许可的损害赔偿金。\n\n参考文献\nYarde Metals, Inc. 诉 New England Patriots Ltd. P'ship, 834 N.E.2d 1233, 1236（马萨诸塞州上诉案，2005 年）（维持基于被许可人不当行为撤销季票）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"许可证（例如活动门票）可随时因任何合法原因撤销。但如果被许可人为许可支付了费用，则许可人可能需要支付撤销许可的损害赔偿金。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这起诉讼中，法院将为谁做出裁决？","rule":"许可证（例如活动门票）可随时因任何合法原因撤销。但如果被许可人为许可支付了费用，则许可人可能需要支付撤销许可的损害赔偿金。","analysis":"许可证\n\n定义 出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的可撤销特权\n\n创建 口头、书面或通过其他行为表明许可方意图创建许可 终止 许可方随时撤销 在 (1) 许可方或被许可方死亡或 (2) 许可财产转让时自动终止 活动门票是一种许可，为持票人（被许可方）为特定目的进入和使用场地提供有限权利。该许可证可随时出于任何合法原因撤销，包括未能遵守场馆所有者（许可方）向持票人提出的任何条件（如此处所示）。即使持票人为门票支付了一定的费用，情况也是如此。然而，所有者可能需要因吊销许可证而支付损害赔偿金（例如，退还门票的未使用部分）（选择C）。 （选择 A）即使该实体有义务控制其拥有的财产上的活动并邀请公众参观，该义务也是出于保护公众健康和安全的需要而产生的。禁止招揽行为不属于对健康和安全的保护范围，因此这一理由不能成为该实体撤销罚单的理由。 （选项D） 滋扰是由一个人在他/她自己的土地上的活动引起的，因此粉丝不能对实体的财产造成滋扰。但该实体无需证明该球迷有滋扰行为，即可吊销她观看曲棍球比赛的执照。\n\n教育目标：许可证（例如活动门票）可随时因任何合法原因撤销。但如果被许可人为许可支付了费用，则许可人可能需要支付撤销许可的损害赔偿金。\n\n参考文献\nYarde Metals, Inc. 诉 New England Patriots Ltd. P'ship, 834 N.E.2d 1233, 1236（马萨诸塞州上诉案，2005 年）（维持基于被许可人不当行为撤销季票）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该实体，因为球迷的曲棍球比赛门票仅创建了一个许可证。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":167,"sourceQuestionPage":490,"sourceQuestionPages":[490],"sourceExplanationPages":[491],"sourceAnswerPages":[491],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-168","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A woman inherited a house from a distant relative. The woman had never visited the house, which was located in another state, and did not want to own it. Upon learning this, a man who lived next door to the house called the woman and asked to buy the house. The woman agreed, provided that the house was sold \"as is.\" The man agreed, and the woman conveyed the house to the man by a warranty deed. The man had purchased the house for investment purposes, intending to rent it out while continuing to live next door. After the sale, the man started to renovate the house and discovered serious termite damage. The man sued the woman for breach of contract. How should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"For the man, because he received a warranty deed.","B":"For the man, because of the doctrine of caveat emptor.","C":"For the woman, because she sold the house \"as is.\"","D":"For the woman, because the man planned to change the use of the house for investment purposes."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Seller's duty to disclose\n\nCommon law Caveat emptor No duty to disclose property defects unless (minority) rule otherwise provided\n\nModern (majority) Commercial rule property\n\nResidential Must disclose known material defects that property buyer could not reasonably discover\n\nException: \"as is\" clause or specific disclaimers without seller's fraud Under the common-law rule of caveat emptor, sellers had no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer. This rule still largely applies to commercial sales. But for residential sales, the majority of jurisdictions now require sellers to disclose material defects that are known to the seller and cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer (Choice B). Here, the woman never visited the house so she likely did not know about the termite damage. Even if she did know, a seller can disclaim the duty to disclose if (1) the disclaimer is clearly and specifically stated in the sales contract and (2) the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property. Here, the contract plainly stated that the house was being sold \"as is,\" and the woman made no misrepresentations regarding its condition. Therefore, the court should rule for the woman. (Choice A) A warranty deed promises that title is free from defects, but it makes no promises regarding the physical quality of the property (eg, termite damage). (Choice D) The man's plans to change the use of the house have no impact on the woman's ability to prevail because (1) the contract contained an \"as is\" clause that relieved her of any liability for property defects and (2) the woman had no knowledge of his plans.\n\nEducational objective: Sellers of residential property have a duty to disclose known material defects that the buyer cannot reasonably discover. However, the seller can disclaim this duty (eg, with an \"as is\" clause) so long as the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property.\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 265 (2020) (discussing \"as is\" sales clauses).","educationalObjective":"Sellers of residential property have a duty to disclose known material defects that the buyer cannot reasonably discover. However, the seller can disclaim this duty (eg, with an \"as is\" clause) so long as the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property.","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 265 (2020) (discussing \"as is\" sales clauses).","rule":"Sellers of residential property have a duty to disclose known material defects that the buyer cannot reasonably discover. However, the seller can disclaim this duty (eg, with an \"as is\" clause) so long as the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule?","rule":"Sellers of residential property have a duty to disclose known material defects that the buyer cannot reasonably discover. However, the seller can disclaim this duty (eg, with an \"as is\" clause) so long as the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property.","analysis":"Seller's duty to disclose\n\nCommon law Caveat emptor No duty to disclose property defects unless (minority) rule otherwise provided\n\nModern (majority) Commercial rule property\n\nResidential Must disclose known material defects that property buyer could not reasonably discover\n\nException: \"as is\" clause or specific disclaimers without seller's fraud Under the common-law rule of caveat emptor, sellers had no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer. This rule still largely applies to commercial sales. But for residential sales, the majority of jurisdictions now require sellers to disclose material defects that are known to the seller and cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer (Choice B). Here, the woman never visited the house so she likely did not know about the termite damage. Even if she did know, a seller can disclaim the duty to disclose if (1) the disclaimer is clearly and specifically stated in the sales contract and (2) the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property. Here, the contract plainly stated that the house was being sold \"as is,\" and the woman made no misrepresentations regarding its condition. Therefore, the court should rule for the woman. (Choice A) A warranty deed promises that title is free from defects, but it makes no promises regarding the physical quality of the property (eg, termite damage). (Choice D) The man's plans to change the use of the house have no impact on the woman's ability to prevail because (1) the contract contained an \"as is\" clause that relieved her of any liability for property defects and (2) the woman had no knowledge of his plans.","conclusion":"Answer C: For the woman, because she sold the house \"as is.\""},"questionZh":"一名妇女从远房亲戚那里继承了一套房子。该女子从未去过这所位于另一个州的房子，也不想拥有它。住在该房子隔壁的一名男子得知此事后，给该女子打电话，要求买下这套房子。这位女士同意了，但前提是房子要“按原样”出售。男方同意了，女方通过保修契约将房子转让给男方。该男子购买该房屋是出于投资目的，打算出租并继续住在隔壁。出售后，该男子开始装修房子，发现白蚁损坏严重。男子起诉女子违反合同。法院应该如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"对于那个男人来说，因为他收到了一份保修契约。","B":"对于男人来说，因为买者自负的教义。","C":"对于女人来说，因为她“按原样”卖掉了房子。","D":"对于女方来说，因为男方打算改变房子的用途，用于投资目的。"},"explanationZh":"卖方的披露义务\n\n普通法 买者自负 没有义务披露财产缺陷，除非（少数）规则另有规定\n\n现代（多数）商业规则财产\n\n住宅 必须披露购房者无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷\n\n例外：“按原样”条款或不存在卖方欺诈的具体免责声明 根据普通法的买者自负规则，卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷。该规则仍然主要适用于商业销售。但对于住宅销售，大多数司法管辖区现在要求卖方披露卖方已知且买方无法合理发现的重大缺陷（选择 B）。这名妇女从未去过这所房子，因此她可能不知道白蚁造成的损害。即使她确实知道，如果（1）销售合同中明确明确地规定了免责声明，并且（2）卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒财产状况，卖方也可以免除披露义务。在这里，合同明确指出房子是“按原样”出售的，并且该妇女没有对其状况做出任何虚假陈述。因此，法院应作出有利于女方的判决。 （选择 A） 保修契约承诺所有权不存在缺陷，但不承诺财产的物理质量（例如，白蚁损坏）。 （选项D）男方改变房屋用途的计划对女方胜诉的能力没有影响，因为（1）合同包含“按原样”条款，免除了她对财产缺陷的任何责任；（2）女方不知道他的计划。\n\n教育目标：住宅物业的卖方有义务披露买方无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷。然而，只要卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒财产的状况，卖方就可以免除这项义务（例如，使用“现状”条款）。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 供应商和买方 § 265 (2020)（讨论“按原样”销售条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"住宅物业的卖方有义务披露买方无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷。然而，只要卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒财产的状况，卖方就可以免除这项义务（例如，使用“现状”条款）。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该如何判决？","rule":"住宅物业的卖方有义务披露买方无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷。然而，只要卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒财产的状况，卖方就可以免除这项义务（例如，使用“现状”条款）。","analysis":"卖方的披露义务\n\n普通法 买者自负 没有义务披露财产缺陷，除非（少数）规则另有规定\n\n现代（多数）商业规则财产\n\n住宅 必须披露购房者无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷\n\n例外：“按原样”条款或不存在卖方欺诈的具体免责声明 根据普通法的买者自负规则，卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷。该规则仍然主要适用于商业销售。但对于住宅销售，大多数司法管辖区现在要求卖方披露卖方已知且买方无法合理发现的重大缺陷（选择 B）。这名妇女从未去过这所房子，因此她可能不知道白蚁造成的损害。即使她确实知道，如果（1）销售合同中明确明确地规定了免责声明，并且（2）卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒财产状况，卖方也可以免除披露义务。在这里，合同明确指出房子是“按原样”出售的，并且该妇女没有对其状况做出任何虚假陈述。因此，法院应作出有利于女方的判决。 （选择 A） 保修契约承诺所有权不存在缺陷，但不承诺财产的物理质量（例如，白蚁损坏）。 （选项D）男方改变房屋用途的计划对女方胜诉的能力没有影响，因为（1）合同包含“按原样”条款，免除了她对财产缺陷的任何责任；（2）女方不知道他的计划。\n\n教育目标：住宅物业的卖方有义务披露买方无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷。然而，只要卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒财产的状况，卖方就可以免除这项义务（例如，使用“现状”条款）。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 供应商和买方 § 265 (2020)（讨论“按原样”销售条款）。","conclusion":"答案 C：对于女人来说，因为她“按原样”卖掉了房子。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":168,"sourceQuestionPage":492,"sourceQuestionPages":[492],"sourceExplanationPages":[493,494],"sourceAnswerPages":[493,494],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-169","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"By a valid written contract, a seller agreed to sell land to a buyer for $100,000. The contract was silent as to quality of title. Shortly after entering into the contract, the buyer discovered that the record owner of the land was not the seller, but another individual. The buyer also learned that the seller had been in adverse possession of the land for 12 years. The period of time to acquire title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 10 years. The buyer no longer wants to purchase the land. Must the buyer purchase the land?","choices":{"A":"No, because the seller's failure to disclose her lack of record title constituted fraud.","B":"No, because the title is not free from an unreasonable risk of litigation.","C":"Yes, because the contract is silent regarding the quality of title.","D":"Yes, because the seller acquired good and marketable title by adverse possession."},"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession Absent contrary language, every land-sales contract implies that the seller will give marketable title to the buyer at closing. To meet this standard, the title must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Here, the seller acquired title to the land by adverse possession. Title obtained by adverse possession is generally unmarketable unless it has been quieted—ie, supported by a judicial decree (not seen here). That is because of the risk that the record owner or someone claiming entitlement to the land through the record owner could challenge the seller's ownership of the land. Therefore, the buyer need not purchase the land (Choice D). (Choice A) Fraud occurs if (1) one party to a contract knowingly or recklessly misrepresented a material fact with the intent to mislead the other party, (2) the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. Here, there is no such evidence of fraud. (Choice C) When, as here, a contract is silent regarding the quality of title, it is implied that the seller will convey marketable title.\n\nEducational objective: It is generally implied that a seller will convey marketable title to a buyer—ie, title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Title obtained through adverse possession is unmarketable unless it has been quieted (ie, supported by judicial decree).\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (marketable title).","educationalObjective":"It is generally implied that a seller will convey marketable title to a buyer—ie, title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Title obtained through adverse possession is unmarketable unless it has been quieted (ie, supported by judicial decree).","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (marketable title).","rule":"It is generally implied that a seller will convey marketable title to a buyer—ie, title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Title obtained through adverse possession is unmarketable unless it has been quieted (ie, supported by judicial decree).","irac":{"issue":"Must the buyer purchase the land?","rule":"It is generally implied that a seller will convey marketable title to a buyer—ie, title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Title obtained through adverse possession is unmarketable unless it has been quieted (ie, supported by judicial decree).","analysis":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession Absent contrary language, every land-sales contract implies that the seller will give marketable title to the buyer at closing. To meet this standard, the title must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Here, the seller acquired title to the land by adverse possession. Title obtained by adverse possession is generally unmarketable unless it has been quieted—ie, supported by a judicial decree (not seen here). That is because of the risk that the record owner or someone claiming entitlement to the land through the record owner could challenge the seller's ownership of the land. Therefore, the buyer need not purchase the land (Choice D). (Choice A) Fraud occurs if (1) one party to a contract knowingly or recklessly misrepresented a material fact with the intent to mislead the other party, (2) the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. Here, there is no such evidence of fraud. (Choice C) When, as here, a contract is silent regarding the quality of title, it is implied that the seller will convey marketable title.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the title is not free from an unreasonable risk of litigation."},"questionZh":"通过一份有效的书面合同，卖方同意以 10 万美元的价格将土地出售给买方。合同没有提及所有权的性质。签订合同后不久，买方发现土地的记录所有者不是卖方，而是另一个人。买方还了解到，卖方对该土地的逆权管有已有12年。在管辖区内通过逆权占有取得所有权的期限为10年。买家不再想购买土地。买家必须购买土地吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为卖方未能披露其缺乏记录所有权的行为构成了欺诈。","B":"不会，因为该所有权并非没有不合理的诉讼风险。","C":"是的，因为合同没有提及所有权的质量。","D":"是的，因为卖方通过逆权占有获得了良好且适销对路的所有权。"},"explanationZh":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租赁 留置权 所有权链中的缺口 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 如果没有相反的语言，每一份土地销售合同都意味着卖方将在交割时向买方授予可出售的所有权。为了满足这一标准，所有权必须合理地没有疑问，并且不受诉讼威胁，这样一个理性的人就会接受并支付费用。在这里，卖方通过逆权占有获得了土地的所有权。通过逆权占有获得的所有权通常无法出售，除非它已被平息——即得到司法法令的支持（此处未见）。这是因为记录所有者或通过记录所有者声称拥有土地权利的人可能会质疑卖方对土地的所有权。因此，买方无需购买土地（选择D）。 （选择 A）如果 (1) 合同一方故意或罔顾后果地歪曲重要事实，意图误导另一方，(2) 歪曲事实导致同意合同，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖该歪曲事实，则构成欺诈。在这里，没有此类欺诈证据。 （选择C）当合同没有提及所有权的质量时，就如此处所示，这意味着卖方将转让适销所有权。\n\n教育目标：通常暗示卖方将向买方转让可销售的所有权，即合理地毫无疑问且不受诉讼威胁的所有权，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。通过逆权占有获得的所有权是不可出售的，除非它已被平息（即得到司法法令的支持）。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（可销售所有权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"通常暗示卖方将向买方转让适销所有权，即合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁的所有权，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。通过逆权占有获得的所有权是不可出售的，除非它已被平息（即得到司法法令的支持）。","iracZh":{"issue":"买家必须购买土地吗？","rule":"通常暗示卖方将向买方转让适销所有权，即合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁的所有权，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。通过逆权占有获得的所有权是不可出售的，除非它已被平息（即得到司法法令的支持）。","analysis":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租赁 留置权 所有权链中的缺口 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 如果没有相反的语言，每一份土地销售合同都意味着卖方将在交割时向买方授予可出售的所有权。为了满足这一标准，所有权必须合理地没有疑问，并且不受诉讼威胁，这样一个理性的人就会接受并支付费用。在这里，卖方通过逆权占有获得了土地的所有权。通过逆权占有获得的所有权通常无法出售，除非它已被平息——即得到司法法令的支持（此处未见）。这是因为记录所有者或通过记录所有者声称拥有土地权利的人可能会质疑卖方对土地的所有权。因此，买方无需购买土地（选择D）。 （选择 A）如果 (1) 合同一方故意或罔顾后果地歪曲重要事实，意图误导另一方，(2) 歪曲事实导致同意合同，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖该歪曲事实，则构成欺诈。在这里，没有此类欺诈证据。 （选择C）当合同没有提及所有权的质量时，就如此处所示，这意味着卖方将转让适销所有权。\n\n教育目标：通常暗示卖方将向买方转让可销售的所有权，即合理地毫无疑问且不受诉讼威胁的所有权，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。通过逆权占有获得的所有权是不可出售的，除非它已被平息（即得到司法法令的支持）。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（可销售所有权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为该所有权并非没有不合理的诉讼风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":169,"sourceQuestionPage":495,"sourceQuestionPages":[495],"sourceExplanationPages":[496,497],"sourceAnswerPages":[496,497],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-170","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"An individual who enjoyed paragliding lived next to a large parcel of undeveloped land. The individual secured oral permission from the owner of the undeveloped land to access a bluff on the land from which the individual could launch his paraglider. For 12 years, the individual crossed the owner's land with his paraglider to launch himself off the bluff, until a workplace injury permanently prevented him from doing so. A year after his injury, the individual sold his residence. Although the individual told the purchaser about the right to access the bluff, neither the contract nor the deed made mention of it. Prior to this sale, the owner of the undeveloped land had sold it to a developer. The developer has since told the purchaser of the residence that she can no longer access the bluff. The applicable jurisdiction requires that a use of a servient estate extend for at least 10 years before the user has a right to the use by prescription. Can the developer legally prevent the purchaser of the residence from accessing the bluff?","choices":{"A":"No, because the individual accessed the bluff for more than 10 years before the land was sold to the developer.","B":"No, because the individual had an easement appurtenant that passed automatically to the purchaser of the residence.","C":"Yes, because neither the contract of sale nor the deed for the residence mentioned a right of access.","D":"Yes, because the individual's access to the bluff was with the oral permission of the owner of the land."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Licenses, easements & profits\n\nLicense Revocable privilege to enter & use another's land for specific purpose\n\nEasement Nonpossessory interest in land that gives easement holder right to:\n\nuse another's land for specific purpose (ie, affirmative easement) or prohibit other owner from doing something on land that owner would otherwise be allowed to do (ie, negative easement) Profit Nonpossessory interest in land that gives profit holder right to enter another's land to remove products of soil (eg, timber, minerals, oil)\n\nA license is a grant of permission to enter and make specific, limited use of another's land (the servient estate). Unlike easements, licenses are not interests in the land subject to the statute of frauds. As a result, they can be created orally or by another act demonstrating the licensor's intent to let the licensee enter the property. Licenses can generally be revoked at any time and terminate automatically upon either: the death of either party or conveyance of the servient estate. Here, the owner (licensor) orally gave the individual (licensee) permission to access the bluff on the owner's land for paragliding. This created a license, which terminated automatically when the owner sold the land (servient estate) to the developer. Therefore, the developer can legally prevent the purchaser of the individual's residence from accessing the bluff. (Choices A & B) An easement appurtenant is tied to the land and passes automatically to subsequent owners of the dominant (benefited) estate. Although easements are presumed to be appurtenant, there must first be an easement. Here, there was no writing to satisfy the statute of frauds, so no express easement was created. And though the individual accessed the bluff for more than 10 years (statutory period), he did not acquire an easement by prescription since he had the owner's permission to access the bluff. (Choice C) Had there been an easement, the failure to mention the easement in either the contract of sale or the deed for the residence would not have prevented the easement from being transferred to the purchaser.\n\nEducational objective: A license is a grant of permission to enter and use another's land for a specific purpose. Licenses can be revoked at any time and terminate automatically upon the death of either party or conveyance of the servient estate.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property §§ 515, 519 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (creation and revocation of licenses).\n\n25 Am. Jur. 2d Easements and Licenses in Real Property § 109 (2020) (effect of conveyance of licensed property).","educationalObjective":"A license is a grant of permission to enter and use another's land for a specific purpose. Licenses can be revoked at any time and terminate automatically upon the death of either party or conveyance of the servient estate.","references":"Restatement of Property §§ 515, 519 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (creation and revocation of licenses).\n\n25 Am. Jur. 2d Easements and Licenses in Real Property § 109 (2020) (effect of conveyance of licensed property).","rule":"A license is a grant of permission to enter and use another's land for a specific purpose. Licenses can be revoked at any time and terminate automatically upon the death of either party or conveyance of the servient estate.","irac":{"issue":"Can the developer legally prevent the purchaser of the residence from accessing the bluff?","rule":"A license is a grant of permission to enter and use another's land for a specific purpose. Licenses can be revoked at any time and terminate automatically upon the death of either party or conveyance of the servient estate.","analysis":"Licenses, easements & profits\n\nLicense Revocable privilege to enter & use another's land for specific purpose\n\nEasement Nonpossessory interest in land that gives easement holder right to:\n\nuse another's land for specific purpose (ie, affirmative easement) or prohibit other owner from doing something on land that owner would otherwise be allowed to do (ie, negative easement) Profit Nonpossessory interest in land that gives profit holder right to enter another's land to remove products of soil (eg, timber, minerals, oil)\n\nA license is a grant of permission to enter and make specific, limited use of another's land (the servient estate). Unlike easements, licenses are not interests in the land subject to the statute of frauds. As a result, they can be created orally or by another act demonstrating the licensor's intent to let the licensee enter the property. Licenses can generally be revoked at any time and terminate automatically upon either: the death of either party or conveyance of the servient estate. Here, the owner (licensor) orally gave the individual (licensee) permission to access the bluff on the owner's land for paragliding. This created a license, which terminated automatically when the owner sold the land (servient estate) to the developer. Therefore, the developer can legally prevent the purchaser of the individual's residence from accessing the bluff. (Choices A & B) An easement appurtenant is tied to the land and passes automatically to subsequent owners of the dominant (benefited) estate. Although easements are presumed to be appurtenant, there must first be an easement. Here, there was no writing to satisfy the statute of frauds, so no express easement was created. And though the individual accessed the bluff for more than 10 years (statutory period), he did not acquire an easement by prescription since he had the owner's permission to access the bluff. (Choice C) Had there been an easement, the failure to mention the easement in either the contract of sale or the deed for the residence would not have prevented the easement from being transferred to the purchaser.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the individual's access to the bluff was with the oral permission of the owner of the land."},"questionZh":"一个喜欢滑翔伞的人住在一大片未开发的土地旁边。该个人获得了未开发土地所有者的口头许可，可以进入该土地上的悬崖，并可以从该悬崖上发射滑翔伞。 12 年来，这个人一直带着滑翔伞穿过业主的土地，从悬崖上跳下来，直到工伤永久阻止了他这样做。受伤一年后，该人卖掉了他的住所。尽管该人告诉购买者有权进入虚张声势，但合同和契约都没有提及这一点。在此之前，未开发土地的所有者已将其出售给开发商。此后，开发商告诉该住宅的购买者，她无法再进入悬崖。适用的司法管辖区要求，对供役财产的使用期限至少要延长 10 年，之后使用者才有权根据处方使用该财产。开发商可以合法地阻止住宅购买者进入虚张声势吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为在土地出售给开发商之前，该人已在悬崖上待了 10 多年。","B":"不会，因为该人有一份地役权附属物，该附属物会自动转移给住宅的购买者。","C":"是的，因为销售合同和居住契据都没有提到使用权。","D":"是的，因为个人进入悬崖是经过土地所有者的口头许可的。"},"explanationZh":"许可证、地役权和利润\n\n出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的许可可撤销特权\n\n地役权 土地的非占有权益，赋予地役权持有人以下权利：\n\n将他人的土地用于特定目的（即，肯定地役权）或禁止其他所有者在所有者可以做的事情上做某事（即，消极地役权） 利润 对土地的非占有权益，使利润持有者有权进入他人的土地以去除土壤产品（例如，木材、矿物、石油）\n\n许可证是授予进入他人土地（奴役地产）并对其进行特定、有限使用的许可。与地役权不同，许可证不是受欺诈法管辖的土地权益。因此，它们可以通过口头或通过其他行为来创建，表明许可人让被许可人进入该财产的意图。许可证通常可以随时撤销，并在以下任一情况下自动终止：任何一方死亡或奴役财产转让。在这里，所有者（许可人）口头授予个人（被许可人）进入所有者土地上的悬崖进行滑翔伞的许可。这创建了一个许可证，当所有者将土地（服务地产）出售给开发商时，该许可证自动终止。因此，开发商可以合法地阻止个人住宅的购买者进入虚张声势。 （选项 A 和 B）地役权附属物与土地挂钩，并自动传递给主要（受益）遗产的后续所有者。尽管地役权被认为是附属物，但首先必须有地役权。在这里，没有任何文字可以满足欺诈法规，因此没有创建明确的地役权。尽管该人进入该悬崖已超过 10 年（法定期限），但他并未通过时效获得地役权，因为他已获得业主的进入该悬崖的许可。 （选项C）如果存在地役权，则在销售合同或住宅契约中未提及地役权不会妨碍地役权转让给买方。\n\n教育目标：许可证是授予出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的许可。许可证可以随时撤销，并在任何一方死亡或奴役财产转让时自动终止。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 515, 519（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（许可证的创建和撤销）。\n\n上午 25 点。尤尔。 2d 不动产地役权和许可证 § 109 (2020)（许可财产转让的效果）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"许可证是出于特定目的而授予进入和使用他人土地的许可。许可证可以随时撤销，并在任何一方死亡或奴役财产转让时自动终止。","iracZh":{"issue":"开发商可以合法地阻止住宅购买者进入虚张声势吗？","rule":"许可证是出于特定目的而授予进入和使用他人土地的许可。许可证可以随时撤销，并在任何一方死亡或奴役财产转让时自动终止。","analysis":"许可证、地役权和利润\n\n出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的许可可撤销特权\n\n地役权 土地的非占有权益，赋予地役权持有人以下权利：\n\n将他人的土地用于特定目的（即，肯定地役权）或禁止其他所有者在所有者可以做的事情上做某事（即，消极地役权） 利润 对土地的非占有权益，使利润持有者有权进入他人的土地以去除土壤产品（例如，木材、矿物、石油）\n\n许可证是授予进入他人土地（奴役地产）并对其进行特定、有限使用的许可。与地役权不同，许可证不是受欺诈法管辖的土地权益。因此，它们可以通过口头或通过其他行为来创建，表明许可人让被许可人进入该财产的意图。许可证通常可以随时撤销，并在以下任一情况下自动终止：任何一方死亡或奴役财产转让。在这里，所有者（许可人）口头授予个人（被许可人）进入所有者土地上的悬崖进行滑翔伞的许可。这创建了一个许可证，当所有者将土地（服务地产）出售给开发商时，该许可证自动终止。因此，开发商可以合法地阻止个人住宅的购买者进入虚张声势。 （选项 A 和 B）地役权附属物与土地挂钩，并自动传递给主要（受益）遗产的后续所有者。尽管地役权被认为是附属物，但首先必须有地役权。在这里，没有任何文字可以满足欺诈法规，因此没有创建明确的地役权。尽管该人进入该悬崖已超过 10 年（法定期限），但他并未通过时效获得地役权，因为他已获得业主的进入该悬崖的许可。 （选项C）如果存在地役权，则在销售合同或住宅契约中未提及地役权不会妨碍地役权转让给买方。\n\n教育目标：许可证是授予出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的许可。许可证可以随时撤销，并在任何一方死亡或奴役财产转让时自动终止。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 515, 519（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（许可证的创建和撤销）。\n\n上午 25 点。尤尔。 2d 不动产地役权和许可证 § 109 (2020)（许可财产转让的效果）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为个人进入悬崖是经过土地所有者的口头许可的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":170,"sourceQuestionPage":498,"sourceQuestionPages":[498],"sourceExplanationPages":[499,500],"sourceAnswerPages":[499,500],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-171","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"The owner of an undeveloped city lot and a developer executed a written document in which the owner agreed to sell the lot to the developer and the developer agreed to buy the lot for $100,000. The document did not provide for an earnest money down payment. The owner recorded the document, as authorized by statute. The owner orally gave the developer permission to park his car on the lot without charge prior to the closing. Thereafter, the developer frequently parked his car there. Another property came on the market that the developer wanted more than the owner's lot. The developer decided to try to escape any obligation to the owner. The developer had been told that contracts for the purchase and sale of real property require consideration and concluded that because he had made no earnest money down payment, he could refuse to close and not be liable. The developer notified the owner of his intention not to close and, in fact, did refuse to close on the date set for the closing. The owner brought an appropriate action to compel specific performance by the developer. If the owner wins, what will be the likely reason?","choices":{"A":"General contract rules regarding consideration apply to real estate contracts.","B":"The developer's use of the lot constitutes part performance.","C":"The doctrine of equitable conversion applies.","D":"The document was recorded."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A land-sale contract, like any other contract, is legally enforceable only if it is supported by valuable consideration. Valuable consideration is evidenced by a bargained-for exchange of legal positions between the parties. This requires that each party: perform, or promise to perform, some act that is not legally required or refrain, or promise to refrain, from performing some legally permissible act. Land-sale contracts are supported by valuable consideration because the seller promises to convey title to the buyer, and in exchange the buyer promises to pay the seller the purchase price. A buyer can make an earnest money down payment to show a good faith intent to complete the purchase, but this is not needed to form a valid contract. Therefore, the reason the owner will win is that the contract meets the general contract rules regarding consideration. (Choice B) Part performance allows a buyer to enforce an oral agreement if the buyer has taken possession of the property, made substantial improvements to it, and/or paid some or all of the purchase price. However, this exception to the statute of frauds does not apply here because the parties executed a written contract. (Choice C) The doctrine of equitable conversion governs the ownership of property and risk of loss during the period between contract formation and closing. However, it has no relevance to whether a land-sale contract is valid and enforceable upon closing. (Choice D) Recording acts protect buyers from subsequent purchasers claiming superior title to the property, but they have no bearing on whether the owner can compel specific performance by the developer under this land-sale contract.\n\nEducational objective: Land-sale contracts are supported by valuable consideration because the seller promises to convey title to the buyer; in exchange, the buyer promises to pay the seller the purchase price.\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 24 (2020) (consideration).","educationalObjective":"Land-sale contracts are supported by valuable consideration because the seller promises to convey title to the buyer; in exchange, the buyer promises to pay the seller the purchase price.","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 24 (2020) (consideration).","rule":"Land-sale contracts are supported by valuable consideration because the seller promises to convey title to the buyer; in exchange, the buyer promises to pay the seller the purchase price.","irac":{"issue":"If the owner wins, what will be the likely reason?","rule":"Land-sale contracts are supported by valuable consideration because the seller promises to convey title to the buyer; in exchange, the buyer promises to pay the seller the purchase price.","analysis":"A land-sale contract, like any other contract, is legally enforceable only if it is supported by valuable consideration. Valuable consideration is evidenced by a bargained-for exchange of legal positions between the parties. This requires that each party: perform, or promise to perform, some act that is not legally required or refrain, or promise to refrain, from performing some legally permissible act. Land-sale contracts are supported by valuable consideration because the seller promises to convey title to the buyer, and in exchange the buyer promises to pay the seller the purchase price. A buyer can make an earnest money down payment to show a good faith intent to complete the purchase, but this is not needed to form a valid contract. Therefore, the reason the owner will win is that the contract meets the general contract rules regarding consideration. (Choice B) Part performance allows a buyer to enforce an oral agreement if the buyer has taken possession of the property, made substantial improvements to it, and/or paid some or all of the purchase price. However, this exception to the statute of frauds does not apply here because the parties executed a written contract. (Choice C) The doctrine of equitable conversion governs the ownership of property and risk of loss during the period between contract formation and closing. However, it has no relevance to whether a land-sale contract is valid and enforceable upon closing. (Choice D) Recording acts protect buyers from subsequent purchasers claiming superior title to the property, but they have no bearing on whether the owner can compel specific performance by the developer under this land-sale contract.","conclusion":"Answer A: General contract rules regarding consideration apply to real estate contracts."},"questionZh":"一块未开发的城市地块的业主和开发商签署了一份书面文件，业主同意将该地块出售给开发商，开发商同意以 10 万美元的价格购买该地块。该文件没有规定保证金首付。所有者根据法规授权记录了该文件。业主口头允许开发商在交割前将车免费停放在该地块上。此后，开发商经常将车停在那里。市场上还有另一处房产，开发商想要的不仅仅是业主的土地。开发商决定试图逃避对业主的任何义务。开发商被告知，房地产买卖合同需要考虑，并得出结论，由于他没有支付定金，他可以拒绝成交，也不承担责任。开发商通知业主他打算不关闭，事实上，开发商确实拒绝在预定的关闭日期关闭。业主采取了适当的行动，迫使开发商履行特定义务。如果业主获胜，可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"有关对价的一般合同规则适用于房地产合同。","B":"开发商对地块的使用构成部分履约。","C":"适用衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则。","D":"该文件被记录下来。"},"explanationZh":"与任何其他合同一样，土地出售合同只有在有有价值的对价支持的情况下才具有法律效力。双方通过讨价还价交换法律地位证明了有价值的对价。这要求各方：执行或承诺执行某些法律不要求的行为，或者不执行或承诺不执行某些法律允许的行为。土地销售合同得到有价值的对价的支持，因为卖方承诺将所有权转让给买方，而作为交换，买方承诺向卖方支付购买价。买方可以支付保证金以表明完成购买的诚意，但这并不是形成有效合同所必需的。因此，业主获胜的原因是合同符合有关对价的一般合同规则。 （选择 B）如果买方已占有该财产、对其进行了实质性改进和/或支付了部分或全部购买价款，则部分履约允许买方执行口头协议。然而，欺诈法的这一例外情况在此并不适用，因为双方签署了书面合同。 （选项C） 衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则管辖合同订立和结束期间财产的所有权和损失风险。然而，它与土地出售合同在成交时是否有效和可执行无关。 （选项D）记录行为可以保护买家免受后续购买者声称拥有该房产的优先所有权的影响，但它们与业主是否可以强制开发商根据该土地销售合同进行具体履行无关。\n\n教育目标：土地买卖合同得到有价值的对价的支持，因为卖方承诺将所有权转让给买方；作为交换，买方承诺向卖方支付货款。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 24 (2020)（考虑）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地买卖合同有有价值的对价作为支持，因为卖方承诺将所有权转让给买方；作为交换，买方承诺向卖方支付货款。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果业主获胜，可能的原因是什么？","rule":"土地买卖合同有有价值的对价作为支持，因为卖方承诺将所有权转让给买方；作为交换，买方承诺向卖方支付货款。","analysis":"与任何其他合同一样，土地出售合同只有在有有价值的对价支持的情况下才具有法律效力。双方通过讨价还价交换法律地位证明了有价值的对价。这要求各方：执行或承诺执行某些法律不要求的行为，或者不执行或承诺不执行某些法律允许的行为。土地销售合同得到有价值的对价的支持，因为卖方承诺将所有权转让给买方，而作为交换，买方承诺向卖方支付购买价。买方可以支付保证金以表明完成购买的诚意，但这并不是形成有效合同所必需的。因此，业主获胜的原因是合同符合有关对价的一般合同规则。 （选择 B）如果买方已占有该财产、对其进行了实质性改进和/或支付了部分或全部购买价款，则部分履约允许买方执行口头协议。然而，欺诈法的这一例外情况在此并不适用，因为双方签署了书面合同。 （选项C） 衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则管辖合同订立和结束期间财产的所有权和损失风险。然而，它与土地出售合同在成交时是否有效和可执行无关。 （选项D）记录行为可以保护买家免受后续购买者声称拥有该房产的优先所有权的影响，但它们与业主是否可以强制开发商根据该土地销售合同进行具体履行无关。\n\n教育目标：土地买卖合同得到有价值的对价的支持，因为卖方承诺将所有权转让给买方；作为交换，买方承诺向卖方支付货款。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 24 (2020)（考虑）。","conclusion":"答案 A：有关对价的一般合同规则适用于房地产合同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-8e9fa40e36ca345f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":171,"sourceQuestionPage":501,"sourceQuestionPages":[501],"sourceExplanationPages":[502,503],"sourceAnswerPages":[502,503],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-172","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A woman acquired land by a deed that contained the following language in the grantee section: \"to [the woman], her heirs and assigns, provided, however, that said grantee may not transfer any interest in the land for 10 years from the date of this instrument.\" Two years later, the woman contracted to sell the land to an investor for a price based on a recent appraisal. When the investor's title search revealed the above language in the grantee section of the deed to the woman, the investor refused to close the transaction. The contract was silent as to the woman's title obligation. The woman has sued the investor for specific performance. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The investor, because the woman cannot sell the land during the 10-year period specified in the deed.","B":"The investor, because the woman's heirs did not join in the contract.","C":"The woman, because the contract did not obligate her to provide marketable title.","D":"The woman, because the deed's restraint on transfer is void as a matter of law."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Restraints on alienation\n\n(restrictions on transferability of real property)\n\nValidity Examples\n\nDirect Only valid if reasonable Prohibiting some or all types Balance utility of restraint against harm of transfers (disfavored) resulting from enforcement Prohibiting transfer without consent Prohibiting transfer to particular persons Indirect Valid unless lacking rational justification Imposing transfer fees Limiting property's use/reducing its value A restraint on alienation is a provision that restricts the transferability of real property. Direct restraints on alienation (eg, a restriction that prohibits some or all types of transfers—as seen here) are disfavored and are valid only if they are deemed reasonable. To determine reasonableness, courts weigh the utility of the restraint against the harm resulting from its enforcement. A total restraint on the alienability of a fee simple estate is always unreasonable because freedom of alienation is a defining attribute of this type of estate. Here, the woman's deed states that she may not transfer any interest in the land for 10 years, which constitutes a direct and total restraint on alienation. But the woman has a fee simple estate in the land—as evidenced by the words of limitation \"and her heirs\" traditionally used to convey this type of estate. Therefore, the deed's restraint on transfer is a void restraint on alienation of a fee simple estate, and the woman can sell the land to the investor (Choice A). As a result, she is likely to prevail in her suit against the investor for specific performance. (Choice B) The words \"and her heirs\" are used to convey a fee simple estate, but they do not create an ownership interest in the grantee's heirs or make them the only persons to whom the grantee can transfer the estate. Therefore, the woman's heirs need not join the contract for it to be enforceable. (Choice C) In contracts for the sale of land, the seller has an implied obligation to the buyer to deliver marketable title—ie, title that is reasonably free from doubt and not under threat of litigation. Therefore, it does not matter that the contract here did not expressly obligate the woman to provide marketable title.\n\nEducational objective: Direct restraints on alienation (eg, prohibitions on some or all types of transfers) are valid only if they are deemed reasonable. Total restraints on the alienability of fee simple estates are always unreasonable.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 3.4 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (direct restraints on alienation).","educationalObjective":"Direct restraints on alienation (eg, prohibitions on some or all types of transfers) are valid only if they are deemed reasonable. Total restraints on the alienability of fee simple estates are always unreasonable.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 3.4 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (direct restraints on alienation).","rule":"Direct restraints on alienation (eg, prohibitions on some or all types of transfers) are valid only if they are deemed reasonable. Total restraints on the alienability of fee simple estates are always unreasonable.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"Direct restraints on alienation (eg, prohibitions on some or all types of transfers) are valid only if they are deemed reasonable. Total restraints on the alienability of fee simple estates are always unreasonable.","analysis":"Restraints on alienation\n\n(restrictions on transferability of real property)\n\nValidity Examples\n\nDirect Only valid if reasonable Prohibiting some or all types Balance utility of restraint against harm of transfers (disfavored) resulting from enforcement Prohibiting transfer without consent Prohibiting transfer to particular persons Indirect Valid unless lacking rational justification Imposing transfer fees Limiting property's use/reducing its value A restraint on alienation is a provision that restricts the transferability of real property. Direct restraints on alienation (eg, a restriction that prohibits some or all types of transfers—as seen here) are disfavored and are valid only if they are deemed reasonable. To determine reasonableness, courts weigh the utility of the restraint against the harm resulting from its enforcement. A total restraint on the alienability of a fee simple estate is always unreasonable because freedom of alienation is a defining attribute of this type of estate. Here, the woman's deed states that she may not transfer any interest in the land for 10 years, which constitutes a direct and total restraint on alienation. But the woman has a fee simple estate in the land—as evidenced by the words of limitation \"and her heirs\" traditionally used to convey this type of estate. Therefore, the deed's restraint on transfer is a void restraint on alienation of a fee simple estate, and the woman can sell the land to the investor (Choice A). As a result, she is likely to prevail in her suit against the investor for specific performance. (Choice B) The words \"and her heirs\" are used to convey a fee simple estate, but they do not create an ownership interest in the grantee's heirs or make them the only persons to whom the grantee can transfer the estate. Therefore, the woman's heirs need not join the contract for it to be enforceable. (Choice C) In contracts for the sale of land, the seller has an implied obligation to the buyer to deliver marketable title—ie, title that is reasonably free from doubt and not under threat of litigation. Therefore, it does not matter that the contract here did not expressly obligate the woman to provide marketable title.","conclusion":"Answer D: The woman, because the deed's restraint on transfer is void as a matter of law."},"questionZh":"妇女通过在受让人部分包含以下语言的契约获得土地：“授予[该妇女]、她的继承人和受让人，但条件是，该受让人自本文件签署之日起 10 年内不得转让该土地的任何权益。”两年后，该妇女签订合同，按照最近评估的价格将土地出售给投资者。当投资者的产权搜索显示该女士的契约受让人部分有上述语言时，投资者拒绝完成交易。合同没有明确规定女方的所有权义务。该女子已就具体表现起诉投资者。谁有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"投资者，因为该妇女在契约规定的 10 年内不能出售土地。","B":"投资者，因为该女子的继承人没有加入合同。","C":"这名女子，因为合同没有要求她提供可出售的所有权。","D":"这名女子，因为该契约对转让的限制在法律上是无效的。"},"explanationZh":"对异化的限制\n\n（不动产转让的限制）\n\n有效性示例\n\n直接 仅在合理的情况下有效 禁止部分或所有类型 平衡限制的效用以防止因执行而造成的转让损害（不受欢迎） 禁止未经同意的转让 禁止转让给特定人 间接 有效，除非缺乏合理的理由 征收转让费 限制财产的使用/降低其价值 限制转让是一项限制不动产可转让性的规定。对转让的直接限制（例如，禁止某些或所有类型的转让的限制——如此处所示）是不受欢迎的，并且只有在被认为合理的情况下才有效。为了确定合理性，法院会权衡限制的效用及其执行所造成的危害。对简单遗产转让性的完全限制总是不合理的，因为转让自由是此类遗产的一个定义属性。这里，该妇女的契约规定，她在10年内不得转让该土地的任何权益，这构成了对转让的直接和彻底的限制。但该妇女在土地上拥有一份简单的财产——传统上用来转让此类财产的“和她的继承人”限制一词就证明了这一点。因此，契约对转让的限制是对收费简单房地产转让的无效限制，妇女可以将土地出售给投资者（选择A）。因此，她在针对具体业绩而针对投资者提起的诉讼中很可能胜诉。 （选项B）“及其继承人”一词用于转让简单的遗产，但它们不会在受让人的继承人中产生所有权权益，也不使他们成为受让人可以向其转让遗产的唯一人。因此，该妇女的继承人无需加入该合同即可生效。 （选择 C） 在土地出售合同中，卖方对买方有隐含义务交付可出售的所有权，即合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁的所有权。因此，这里的合同没有明确规定妇女有义务提供可出售的所有权，这并不重要。\n\n教育目标：对转让的直接限制（例如，禁止某些或所有类型的转让）只有在被认为合理的情况下才有效。对简单财产转让性的全面限制总是不合理的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 3.4（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（对转让的直接限制）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对转让的直接限制（例如，禁止某些或所有类型的转让）只有在被认为合理的情况下才有效。对简单财产转让性的全面限制总是不合理的。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"对转让的直接限制（例如，禁止某些或所有类型的转让）只有在被认为合理的情况下才有效。对简单财产转让性的全面限制总是不合理的。","analysis":"对异化的限制\n\n（不动产转让的限制）\n\n有效性示例\n\n直接 仅在合理的情况下有效 禁止部分或所有类型 平衡限制的效用以防止因执行而造成的转让损害（不受欢迎） 禁止未经同意的转让 禁止转让给特定人 间接 有效，除非缺乏合理的理由 征收转让费 限制财产的使用/降低其价值 限制转让是一项限制不动产可转让性的规定。对转让的直接限制（例如，禁止某些或所有类型的转让的限制——如此处所示）是不受欢迎的，并且只有在被认为合理的情况下才有效。为了确定合理性，法院会权衡限制的效用及其执行所造成的危害。对简单遗产转让性的完全限制总是不合理的，因为转让自由是此类遗产的一个定义属性。这里，该妇女的契约规定，她在10年内不得转让该土地的任何权益，这构成了对转让的直接和彻底的限制。但该妇女在土地上拥有一份简单的财产——传统上用来转让此类财产的“和她的继承人”限制一词就证明了这一点。因此，契约对转让的限制是对收费简单房地产转让的无效限制，妇女可以将土地出售给投资者（选择A）。因此，她在针对具体业绩而针对投资者提起的诉讼中很可能胜诉。 （选项B）“及其继承人”一词用于转让简单的遗产，但它们不会在受让人的继承人中产生所有权权益，也不使他们成为受让人可以向其转让遗产的唯一人。因此，该妇女的继承人无需加入该合同即可生效。 （选择 C） 在土地出售合同中，卖方对买方有隐含义务交付可出售的所有权，即合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁的所有权。因此，这里的合同没有明确规定妇女有义务提供可出售的所有权，这并不重要。\n\n教育目标：对转让的直接限制（例如，禁止某些或所有类型的转让）只有在被认为合理的情况下才有效。对简单财产转让性的全面限制总是不合理的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 3.4（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（对转让的直接限制）。","conclusion":"答案 D：这名女子，因为该契约对转让的限制在法律上是无效的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":172,"sourceQuestionPage":504,"sourceQuestionPages":[504],"sourceExplanationPages":[505,506],"sourceAnswerPages":[505,506],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-173","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man owned a large tract of land. During his lifetime, the man conveyed the easterly half to his son and the westerly half to his daughter. Both of the conveyances, which were promptly and properly recorded, contained the following language: \"The parties agree for themselves and their heirs and assigns that the premises herein conveyed shall be used only for residential purposes; that each lot created within the premises herein conveyed shall contain not less than five acres; and that each lot shall have not more than one single-family dwelling. This agreement shall bind all successor owners of all or any portion of the premises and any owner of any part of the premises may enforce this covenant.\" After the man's death, the son decided to build houses on half-acre lots in the easterly tract as authorized by current applicable zoning and building codes. The area surrounding this tract was developed as a residential community with homes built on half-acre lots. The westerly tract is in a residential area covered by a different zoning code, which allows residential development only on five-acre tracts of land. The daughter has filed an appropriate action to enjoin the son's proposed construction on half-acre lots, claiming that the proposal violates the restrictions set forth in the man's conveyance. Is the court likely to find the restrictions in the conveyance valid?","choices":{"A":"No, because of the change of circumstance in the neighborhood.","B":"No, because the restriction conflicts with the applicable zoning code.","C":"Yes, because the original grantees still own their respective tracts.","D":"Yes, because the provision imposed an equitable servitude."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes\n\nReal covenants Equitable servitudes\n\n(enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief)\n\nFor burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied\n\nWriting Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice privity privity Notice Notice No notice required Equitable servitudes are express or implied covenants (ie, promises) regarding land that are enforceable in equity (usually by injunction) against the promising parties and their successors in interest. Express equitable servitudes have four elements: Writing – the covenant is expressed in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, the deeds from the man to his son and daughter, respectively)\n\nIntent to run – the promising parties intend for the covenant to run to (ie, bind) their successors in interest (eg, the deed says \"heirs and assigns\" and \"all successor owners\")\n\nTouch and concern – the covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of both the dominant and servient estates (eg, restricts use to residential purposes and requires lots to contain five acres)\n\nNotice – the owner of the servient (ie, burdened) estate has actual, record, or inquiry notice of the covenant (eg, the son and daughter have actual notice of the restrictions in their respective deeds)\n\nTherefore, the court will likely find that the restrictions in the man's conveyance imposed a valid equitable servitude. (Choice A) The Changed Conditions Doctrine allows a court to modify or terminate a servitude if the surrounding area or circumstances have changed so much that the servitude can no longer fulfill its purpose and it would be unfair to continue to enforce it—eg, the servitude requires residential use in an area that has become commercial. (Choice B) The restrictions at issue here (which require five-acre lots) do not conflict with, and therefore will not be overridden by, the applicable zoning code (which authorizes half- acre lots).\n\n(Choice C) The restrictions imposed a valid equitable servitude that runs with the land, so it can be enforced even if the original grantees no longer own their tracts (so long as the notice requirement is met).\n\nEducational objective: To create an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run to their successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient-estate owner must have notice.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 539 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (equitable servitudes).","educationalObjective":"To create an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run to their successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient-estate owner must have notice.","references":"Restatement of Property § 539 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (equitable servitudes).","rule":"To create an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run to their successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient-estate owner must have notice.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to find the restrictions in the conveyance valid?","rule":"To create an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run to their successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient-estate owner must have notice.","analysis":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes\n\nReal covenants Equitable servitudes\n\n(enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief)\n\nFor burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied\n\nWriting Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice privity privity Notice Notice No notice required Equitable servitudes are express or implied covenants (ie, promises) regarding land that are enforceable in equity (usually by injunction) against the promising parties and their successors in interest. Express equitable servitudes have four elements: Writing – the covenant is expressed in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, the deeds from the man to his son and daughter, respectively)\n\nIntent to run – the promising parties intend for the covenant to run to (ie, bind) their successors in interest (eg, the deed says \"heirs and assigns\" and \"all successor owners\")\n\nTouch and concern – the covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of both the dominant and servient estates (eg, restricts use to residential purposes and requires lots to contain five acres)\n\nNotice – the owner of the servient (ie, burdened) estate has actual, record, or inquiry notice of the covenant (eg, the son and daughter have actual notice of the restrictions in their respective deeds)\n\nTherefore, the court will likely find that the restrictions in the man's conveyance imposed a valid equitable servitude. (Choice A) The Changed Conditions Doctrine allows a court to modify or terminate a servitude if the surrounding area or circumstances have changed so much that the servitude can no longer fulfill its purpose and it would be unfair to continue to enforce it—eg, the servitude requires residential use in an area that has become commercial. (Choice B) The restrictions at issue here (which require five-acre lots) do not conflict with, and therefore will not be overridden by, the applicable zoning code (which authorizes half- acre lots).\n\n(Choice C) The restrictions imposed a valid equitable servitude that runs with the land, so it can be enforced even if the original grantees no longer own their tracts (so long as the notice requirement is met).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the provision imposed an equitable servitude."},"questionZh":"一个人拥有一大片土地。该男子生前将东半部传给了儿子，将西半部传给了女儿。两份转让书均已及时正确记录，均包含以下语言：“双方同意其本人及其继承人并指定此处转让的房屋仅用于住宅目的；此处转让的房屋内创建的每块地块面积不得少于五英亩；每块地块不得超过一栋单户住宅。本协议应对全部或任何部分房屋的所有继承所有者具有约束力，并且该房屋任何部分的任何所有者都可以执行本契约。”该男子去世后，儿子决定按照现行适用的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）和建筑规范的授权，在东部地区半英亩的土地上建造房屋。该地区周围的地区被开发为一个住宅社区，在半英亩的土地上建造了住宅。西风区位于一个住宅区，由不同的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）规范覆盖，该规范仅允许在五英亩的土地上进行住宅开发。女儿已提起适当诉讼，禁止儿子提议在半英亩土地上进行建设，声称该提议违反了该男子的房产转让中规定的限制。法院可能会认为转让中的限制有效吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，是因为附近的情况发生了变化。","B":"不可以，因为该限制与适用的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）代码冲突。","C":"是的，因为最初的受赠者仍然拥有各自的土地。","D":"是的，因为该条款规定了公平的劳役。"},"explanationZh":"真实契约和公平劳役的要素\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant） 公平的劳役\n\n（可通过金钱赔偿强制执行）（可通过衡平法救济强制执行）\n\n为了负担而运行 为了利益而运行明示暗示\n\n写作 写作 写作 意图创建共同 意图运行 意图运行 意图运行计划 接触与关注 接触与关注 接触与限制性劳役 水平与垂直 有限垂直关注 通知 相互关系 通知 通知 无需通知 衡平法劳役是关于土地的明示或暗示的契约（即承诺），可通过衡平法（通常通过禁令）对承诺方及其利益继承人强制执行。明确的公平劳役有四个要素： 书面——契约以符合欺诈法规的书面形式表达（例如，男子分别对其儿子和女儿的行为）\n\n意图运行——有承诺的各方打算将契约运行到（即约束）其利益继承人（例如，契约上写着“继承人和受让人”和“所有继承人所有者”）\n\n接触和关注——契约涉及主要和服务性地产的使用、享受或占用（例如，限制用于住宅目的，并要求地块包含五英亩）\n\n通知 – 役用（即负担）遗产的所有者拥有契约的实际、记录或查询通知（例如，儿子和女儿拥有各自契约中的限制的实际通知）\n\n因此，法院可能会认为对该男子的财产转让的限制施加了有效的公平劳役。 （选择 A）如果周围地区或情况发生了很大变化，以致地役权不再能实现其目的，并且继续执行它是不公平的，则条件变更原则允许法院修改或终止地役权，例如，地役权要求在已成为商业的区域中用于住宅用途。 （选择 B）这里讨论的限制（要求五英亩地块）与适用的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法规（授权半英亩地块）并不冲突，因此不会被适用的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法规所覆盖。\n\n（选项 C）这些限制规定了与土地一起存在的有效的公平劳役，因此即使原始受让人不再拥有其土地（只要满足通知要求），也可以强制执行。\n\n教育目标：为了建立明确的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将其传给其利益继承人，(3) 契约必须触及并涉及土地，(4) 劳役财产所有者必须予以通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 539（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（衡平法劳役）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为了建立明确的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将契约传给其利益继承人，(3) 契约必须涉及并涉及土地，(4) 劳役财产所有者必须得到通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会认为转让中的限制有效吗？","rule":"为了建立明确的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将契约传给其利益继承人，(3) 契约必须涉及并涉及土地，(4) 劳役财产所有者必须得到通知。","analysis":"真实契约和公平劳役的要素\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant） 公平的劳役\n\n（可通过金钱赔偿强制执行）（可通过衡平法救济强制执行）\n\n为了负担而运行 为了利益而运行明示暗示\n\n写作 写作 写作 意图创建共同 意图运行 意图运行 意图运行计划 接触与关注 接触与关注 接触与限制性劳役 水平与垂直 有限垂直关注 通知 相互关系 通知 通知 无需通知 衡平法劳役是关于土地的明示或暗示的契约（即承诺），可通过衡平法（通常通过禁令）对承诺方及其利益继承人强制执行。明确的公平劳役有四个要素： 书面——契约以符合欺诈法规的书面形式表达（例如，男子分别对其儿子和女儿的行为）\n\n意图运行——有承诺的各方打算将契约运行到（即约束）其利益继承人（例如，契约上写着“继承人和受让人”和“所有继承人所有者”）\n\n接触和关注——契约涉及主要和服务性地产的使用、享受或占用（例如，限制用于住宅目的，并要求地块包含五英亩）\n\n通知 – 役用（即负担）遗产的所有者拥有契约的实际、记录或查询通知（例如，儿子和女儿拥有各自契约中的限制的实际通知）\n\n因此，法院可能会认为对该男子的财产转让的限制施加了有效的公平劳役。 （选择 A）如果周围地区或情况发生了很大变化，以致地役权不再能实现其目的，并且继续执行它是不公平的，则条件变更原则允许法院修改或终止地役权，例如，地役权要求在已成为商业的区域中用于住宅用途。 （选择 B）这里讨论的限制（要求五英亩地块）与适用的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法规（授权半英亩地块）并不冲突，因此不会被适用的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法规所覆盖。\n\n（选项 C）这些限制规定了与土地一起存在的有效的公平劳役，因此即使原始受让人不再拥有其土地（只要满足通知要求），也可以强制执行。\n\n教育目标：为了建立明确的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将其传给其利益继承人，(3) 契约必须触及并涉及土地，(4) 劳役财产所有者必须予以通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 539（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（衡平法劳役）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该条款规定了公平的劳役。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"changed conditions doctrine","ipa":"/tʃˈeɪndʒd kʌndˈɪʃənz dˈɑktɹən/","syllables":"changed con·di·tions doc·trine","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"情势重大变更原则：社区变化使限制目的无法实现时拒绝禁令执行","contextualMeaning":"情势重大变更原则：社区变化使限制目的无法实现时拒绝禁令执行","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) The Changed Conditions Doctrine allows a court to modify or terminate a servitude if the surrounding area or circumstances have changed so much that the servitude can no longer fulfill its purpose and it would be unfair to continue to enforce it—eg, the servitude requires residential use in an area that has become commercial.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":173,"sourceQuestionPage":507,"sourceQuestionPages":[507],"sourceExplanationPages":[508,509],"sourceAnswerPages":[508,509],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-174","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"The owner of a five-acre parcel of land constructed a house on the land. The following year, the owner installed an underground drainage system on the land in order to prevent future flooding of his house. Several years later, the owner gave one acre of the land in fee simple to his niece. The deed contained a provision in which the niece promised that she and her heirs and assigns would maintain the portion of the drainage system on her one acre. This deed was not recorded. Five years later, the niece sold her one acre, in fee simple, at its fair market value to a buyer. Although the niece informed the buyer of her promise with regard to the drainage system, the deed by which the niece conveyed her property to the buyer did not mention this promise. The buyer promptly recorded this deed. Ten months later, there was a severe rainstorm. Because of the buyer's failure to maintain the drainage system, the owner's house flooded. The owner has sued the buyer for damages resulting from this failure. A statute in the applicable jurisdiction provides, \"No conveyance of an interest in real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value unless the same be first recorded according to law.\" Should the court enforce the niece's promise against the buyer?","choices":{"A":"No, because the deed by which the owner conveyed the one acre to his niece was not recorded.","B":"No, because the doctrine of estoppel by deed does not apply here.","C":"Yes, because both horizontal and vertical privity exist.","D":"Yes, because the buyer learned of the promise from the niece herself."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A real covenant is an express promise to do (affirmative) or not do (negative/restrictive) something on land that is enforceable by an action for damages.* Although the promising parties are bound to the covenant under contract law, the covenant will only bind their successors in interest if it runs with the land. This requires proof of the following elements: Writing – covenant is in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, deed from owner to niece)\n\nIntent to run – promising parties intended for the covenant to run to their successors in interest (eg, deed says \"heirs and assigns\")\n\nTouch and concern – covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of the benefited and burdened lands (eg, maintaining drainage system on one parcel to benefit the other)\n\nHorizontal privity – promising parties simultaneously transferred the land and created the covenant (eg, covenant created when one-acre parcel was deeded to niece)\n\nVertical privity – successor of the burdened estate (buyer) has a promising party's entire estate or ownership interest (eg, niece's entire fee simple interest) OR successor of the benefited estate (not seen here) has a possessory interest (Choice C)\n\nNotice – person to be bound had notice of the covenant (eg, buyer learned about covenant from niece) (Choice D)\n\nTherefore, the covenant here did run with the land and could have been imposed on the buyer. However, the buyer is not subject to the covenant in this pure race jurisdiction. This recording act prevents a real property interest from being enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting interest who recorded first. Since the buyer's deed (which did not mention the covenant) was recorded first and the deed from the owner to the niece (which created the covenant) was not recorded, the niece's promise should not be enforced. *In contrast, when an injunction is sought to enforce a covenant, it is called an equitable servitude. (Choice B) Estoppel by deed prevents a grantor who conveys an interest in land by warranty deed before actually owning it from later denying the effectiveness of the deed.\n\nEducational objective: Under a race recording act, a real property interest (eg, real covenant) cannot be enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting real property interest (eg, land burdened by the covenant) who records first.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property §§ 530–38 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of real covenants).","educationalObjective":"Under a race recording act, a real property interest (eg, real covenant) cannot be enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting real property interest (eg, land burdened by the covenant) who records first.","references":"Restatement of Property §§ 530–38 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of real covenants).","rule":"Under a race recording act, a real property interest (eg, real covenant) cannot be enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting real property interest (eg, land burdened by the covenant) who records first.","irac":{"issue":"The owner of a five-acre parcel of land constructed a house on the land. The following year, the owner installed an underground drainage system on the land in order to prevent future flooding of his house. Several years later, the owner gave one acre of the land in fee simple to his niece. The deed contained a provision in which the niece promised that she and her heirs and assigns would maintain the portion of the drainage system on her one acre. This deed was not recorded. Five years later, the niece sold her one acre, in fee simple, at its fair market value to a buyer. Although the niece informed the buyer of her promise with regard to the drainage system, the deed by which the niece conveyed her property to the buyer did not mention this promise. The buyer promptly recorded this deed. Ten months later, there was a severe rainstorm. Because of the buyer's failure to maintain the drainage system, the owner's house flooded. The owner has sued the buyer for damages resulting from this failure. A statute in the applicable jurisdiction provides, \"No conveyance of an interest in real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value unless the same be first recorded according to law.\" Should the court enforce the niece's promise against the buyer?","rule":"Under a race recording act, a real property interest (eg, real covenant) cannot be enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting real property interest (eg, land burdened by the covenant) who records first.","analysis":"A real covenant is an express promise to do (affirmative) or not do (negative/restrictive) something on land that is enforceable by an action for damages.* Although the promising parties are bound to the covenant under contract law, the covenant will only bind their successors in interest if it runs with the land. This requires proof of the following elements: Writing – covenant is in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, deed from owner to niece)\n\nIntent to run – promising parties intended for the covenant to run to their successors in interest (eg, deed says \"heirs and assigns\")\n\nTouch and concern – covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of the benefited and burdened lands (eg, maintaining drainage system on one parcel to benefit the other)\n\nHorizontal privity – promising parties simultaneously transferred the land and created the covenant (eg, covenant created when one-acre parcel was deeded to niece)\n\nVertical privity – successor of the burdened estate (buyer) has a promising party's entire estate or ownership interest (eg, niece's entire fee simple interest) OR successor of the benefited estate (not seen here) has a possessory interest (Choice C)\n\nNotice – person to be bound had notice of the covenant (eg, buyer learned about covenant from niece) (Choice D)\n\nTherefore, the covenant here did run with the land and could have been imposed on the buyer. However, the buyer is not subject to the covenant in this pure race jurisdiction. This recording act prevents a real property interest from being enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting interest who recorded first. Since the buyer's deed (which did not mention the covenant) was recorded first and the deed from the owner to the niece (which created the covenant) was not recorded, the niece's promise should not be enforced. *In contrast, when an injunction is sought to enforce a covenant, it is called an equitable servitude. (Choice B) Estoppel by deed prevents a grantor who conveys an interest in land by warranty deed before actually owning it from later denying the effectiveness of the deed.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the deed by which the owner conveyed the one acre to his niece was not recorded."},"questionZh":"一块五英亩土地的主人在这块土地上建造了一座房子。第二年，业主在这片土地上安装了地下排水系统，以防止他的房子将来被洪水淹没。几年后，业主将一英亩土地免费送给了他的侄女。该契约包含一项条款，其中侄女承诺她和她的继承人和受让人将维护她一英亩土地上的部分排水系统。这一行为没有被记录下来。五年后，侄女以公平的市场价值将她的一英亩土地以简单的价格卖给了一位买家。尽管侄女告知买方她对排水系统的承诺，但侄女将其财产转让给买方的契约并未提及这一承诺。买方立即记录了该契约。十个月后，一场特大暴雨。由于买家未能维护排水系统，业主的房屋被淹。业主已起诉买方，要求其赔偿由此造成的损失。适用司法管辖区的法规规定：“除非首先依法记录，否则任何不动产权益的转让都不会对后续购买者有利。”法院是否应该执行侄女对买方的承诺？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为业主将一英亩土地转让给他的侄女的契约没有记录。","B":"不，因为行为禁止反言原则在这里不适用。","C":"是的，因为水平和垂直的相关性都存在。","D":"是的，因为买家是从侄女本人那里得知这个承诺的。"},"explanationZh":"土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做（肯定的）或不做（否定的/限制性的）某事的明确承诺，可通过损害赔偿诉讼强制执行。*尽管承诺方根据合同法受契约约束，但只有在契约与土地一起运行时，契约才会对其继承人的利益产生约束力。这需要证明以下要素： 书面形式——契约是满足欺诈法规的书面形式（例如，业主给侄女的契约）\n\n竞选意图 – 承诺各方有意向其利益继承者竞选（例如，契约上写着“继承人和受让人”）\n\n接触和关注——契约涉及受益和负担土地的使用、享有或占用（例如，维护一个地块的排水系统以使另一地块受益）\n\n横向相互关系——承诺方同时转让土地并签订契约（例如，将一英亩地块转让给侄女时签订的契约）\n\n垂直相关性——负担遗产的继承人（买方）拥有承诺方的全部遗产或所有权权益（例如，侄女的全部费用单利）或受益遗产的继承人（此处未看到）拥有占有权益（选择C）\n\n通知 – 受约束人已获悉该契约（例如，买方从侄女处得知该契约）（选项 D）\n\n因此，这里的契约确实与土地一起存在，并且可以强加给买方。然而，在这个纯粹的种族管辖范围内，买方不受该契约的约束。这种记录行为可以防止对先记录的冲突利益的后续购买者强制执行不动产权益。由于买方的契约（未提及契约）首先被记录，而业主给侄女的契约（建立契约）没有被记录，因此侄女的承诺不应被执行。 *相反，当寻求禁令来执行契约时，它被称为公平劳役。 （选择 B）契约禁止反言可防止在实际拥有土地之前通过保证契约转让土地权益的授予人后来否认该契约的有效性。\n\n教育目标：根据比赛记录法，不动产权益（例如，不动产契约）不能对先记录的冲突不动产权益（例如，由契约负担的土地）的后续购买者强制执行。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 530–38（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（真实契约的要素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据比赛记录法，不动产权益（例如，不动产契约）不能对先记录的冲突不动产权益（例如，由契约负担的土地）的后续购买者强制执行。","iracZh":{"issue":"一块五英亩土地的主人在这块土地上建造了一座房子。第二年，业主在这片土地上安装了地下排水系统，以防止他的房子将来被洪水淹没。几年后，业主将一英亩土地免费送给了他的侄女。该契约包含一项条款，其中侄女承诺她和她的继承人和受让人将维护她一英亩土地上的部分排水系统。这一行为没有被记录下来。五年后，侄女以公平的市场价值将她的一英亩土地以简单的价格卖给了一位买家。尽管侄女告知买方她对排水系统的承诺，但侄女将其财产转让给买方的契约并未提及这一承诺。买方立即记录了该契约。十个月后，一场特大暴雨。由于买家未能维护排水系统，业主的房屋被淹。业主已起诉买方，要求其赔偿由此造成的损失。适用司法管辖区的法规规定：“除非首先依法记录，否则任何不动产权益的转让都不会对后续购买者有利。”法院是否应该执行侄女对买方的承诺？","rule":"根据比赛记录法，不动产权益（例如，不动产契约）不能对先记录的冲突不动产权益（例如，由契约负担的土地）的后续购买者强制执行。","analysis":"土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做（肯定的）或不做（否定的/限制性的）某事的明确承诺，可通过损害赔偿诉讼强制执行。*尽管承诺方根据合同法受契约约束，但只有在契约与土地一起运行时，契约才会对其继承人的利益产生约束力。这需要证明以下要素： 书面形式——契约是满足欺诈法规的书面形式（例如，业主给侄女的契约）\n\n竞选意图 – 承诺各方有意向其利益继承者竞选（例如，契约上写着“继承人和受让人”）\n\n接触和关注——契约涉及受益和负担土地的使用、享有或占用（例如，维护一个地块的排水系统以使另一地块受益）\n\n横向相互关系——承诺方同时转让土地并签订契约（例如，将一英亩地块转让给侄女时签订的契约）\n\n垂直相关性——负担遗产的继承人（买方）拥有承诺方的全部遗产或所有权权益（例如，侄女的全部费用单利）或受益遗产的继承人（此处未看到）拥有占有权益（选择C）\n\n通知 – 受约束人已获悉该契约（例如，买方从侄女处得知该契约）（选项 D）\n\n因此，这里的契约确实与土地一起存在，并且可以强加给买方。然而，在这个纯粹的种族管辖范围内，买方不受该契约的约束。这种记录行为可以防止对先记录的冲突利益的后续购买者强制执行不动产权益。由于买方的契约（未提及契约）首先被记录，而业主给侄女的契约（建立契约）没有被记录，因此侄女的承诺不应被执行。 *相反，当寻求禁令来执行契约时，它被称为公平劳役。 （选择 B）契约禁止反言可防止在实际拥有土地之前通过保证契约转让土地权益的授予人后来否认该契约的有效性。\n\n教育目标：根据比赛记录法，不动产权益（例如，不动产契约）不能对先记录的冲突不动产权益（例如，由契约负担的土地）的后续购买者强制执行。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 530–38（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（真实契约的要素）。","conclusion":"答案 A：没有，因为业主将一英亩土地转让给他的侄女的契约没有记录。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"vertical privity","ipa":"/vˈɝtɪkəl 'priviti/","syllables":"ver·ti·cal priv·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"纵向关系：原当事人与其后继权利人之间的地产承继关系","contextualMeaning":"纵向关系：原当事人与其后继权利人之间的地产承继关系","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because both horizontal and vertical privity exist.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-9f0bb98ef342f636"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":174,"sourceQuestionPage":510,"sourceQuestionPages":[510],"sourceExplanationPages":[511,512],"sourceAnswerPages":[511,512],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-175","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A son entered into a valid written contract to sell a large tract of land to a friend. At that time, the tract was owned by the son's father. The son had no title to the tract and was not the agent of the father. After the contract was executed and before the scheduled closing date, the father died intestate, leaving the son as his sole heir. Shortly thereafter, the son received an offer of purchase that was substantially higher than the purchase price in the contract with the friend. The son refused to close with the friend although she was ready, willing, and able to close pursuant to the contract. The friend brought an appropriate action for specific performance against the son. In that action, what should the friend be awarded?","choices":{"A":"Judgment for specific performance, because the son acquired title before the scheduled closing.","B":"Judgment for specific performance, to prevent unjust enrichment of the son.","C":"Nothing, because the doctrine of after-acquired title does not apply to executory contracts.","D":"Nothing, because the son had no authority to enter into the contract with the friend."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The sale of real property typically comprises three parts: Execution of the sales contract – the buyer promises to pay the purchase price in exchange for the seller's promise to deliver title Executory period – the buyer can inspect the property, secure a loan, and review title documents, AND the seller can repair the property or correct imperfections in the title Closing – the seller formally transfers title by delivering the deed to the buyer and the buyer pays the purchase price Once a sales contract is executed, the exchanged promises become binding. Therefore, if the seller acquires marketable title at closing but is unwilling to perform, the buyer can sue for breach of the contract or seek specific performance. Here, the son did not have title to the tract when he entered the sales contract with the friend. However, he inherited title before the scheduled closing. As a result, the seller has marketable title and must perform his promise to transfer it to the friend. Since he is unwilling to do so, the friend should be awarded judgment for specific performance. (Choice B) Specific performance is typically used when a monetary remedy cannot make the plaintiff whole. The goal is to adequately compensate the plaintiff—not to prevent unjust enrichment of the defendant. (Choice C) The doctrine of after-acquired title (ie, estoppel by deed) would apply to executory contracts (eg, sales contracts for real estate) if a seller formally transferred title by warranty deed at closing and later acquired title to that property. Title would then automatically transfer to the buyer once the seller received it. (Choice D) The son had the authority to enter into the contract with the friend but lacked the ability to perform until he inherited the tract. Once that occurred, he was obligated to sell the property to the friend.\n\nEducational objective: Once a buyer and seller execute a sales contract, their exchanged promises become binding. Therefore, if the seller is able to transfer title upon closing but refuses to do so, the buyer can sue for breach of contract or seek specific performance.","educationalObjective":"Once a buyer and seller execute a sales contract, their exchanged promises become binding. Therefore, if the seller is able to transfer title upon closing but refuses to do so, the buyer can sue for breach of contract or seek specific performance.","references":"","rule":"Once a buyer and seller execute a sales contract, their exchanged promises become binding. Therefore, if the seller is able to transfer title upon closing but refuses to do so, the buyer can sue for breach of contract or seek specific performance.","irac":{"issue":"In that action, what should the friend be awarded?","rule":"Once a buyer and seller execute a sales contract, their exchanged promises become binding. Therefore, if the seller is able to transfer title upon closing but refuses to do so, the buyer can sue for breach of contract or seek specific performance.","analysis":"The sale of real property typically comprises three parts: Execution of the sales contract – the buyer promises to pay the purchase price in exchange for the seller's promise to deliver title Executory period – the buyer can inspect the property, secure a loan, and review title documents, AND the seller can repair the property or correct imperfections in the title Closing – the seller formally transfers title by delivering the deed to the buyer and the buyer pays the purchase price Once a sales contract is executed, the exchanged promises become binding. Therefore, if the seller acquires marketable title at closing but is unwilling to perform, the buyer can sue for breach of the contract or seek specific performance. Here, the son did not have title to the tract when he entered the sales contract with the friend. However, he inherited title before the scheduled closing. As a result, the seller has marketable title and must perform his promise to transfer it to the friend. Since he is unwilling to do so, the friend should be awarded judgment for specific performance. (Choice B) Specific performance is typically used when a monetary remedy cannot make the plaintiff whole. The goal is to adequately compensate the plaintiff—not to prevent unjust enrichment of the defendant. (Choice C) The doctrine of after-acquired title (ie, estoppel by deed) would apply to executory contracts (eg, sales contracts for real estate) if a seller formally transferred title by warranty deed at closing and later acquired title to that property. Title would then automatically transfer to the buyer once the seller received it. (Choice D) The son had the authority to enter into the contract with the friend but lacked the ability to perform until he inherited the tract. Once that occurred, he was obligated to sell the property to the friend.","conclusion":"Answer A: Judgment for specific performance, because the son acquired title before the scheduled closing."},"questionZh":"一个儿子签订了一份有效的书面合同，将一大片土地出售给朋友。当时，这块土地归儿子的父亲所有。儿子没有该土地的所有权，也不是父亲的代理人。合同执行后，在预定的交割日期之前，父亲去世时没有留下遗嘱，留下儿子作为他唯一的继承人。此后不久，儿子收到了远高于与朋友合同中的购买价格的购买要约。尽管她已经准备好、愿意并且能够根据合同完成交易，但儿子拒绝与朋友完成交易。朋友针对儿子的具体表现提出了适当的诉讼。在该行动中，朋友应该获得什么奖励？","choicesZh":{"A":"具体表现判断，因为儿子在预定交割前获得了所有权。","B":"针对具体表现进行判断，防止儿子不当得利。","C":"没什么，因为事后取得所有权的原则不适用于可执行合同。","D":"没什么，因为儿子无权与朋友签订合同。"},"explanationZh":"不动产的销售通常包括三个部分： 销售合同的执行 - 买方承诺支付购买价，以换取卖方交付所有权的承诺 执行期 - 买方可以检查财产，获得贷款并审查所有权文件，卖方可以修复财产或纠正所有权中的缺陷 交割 - 卖方通过将契约交付给买方来正式转让所有权，买方支付购买价 一旦销售合同执行，交换的承诺就具有约束力。因此，如果卖方在交割时获得了可流通的所有权但不愿意履行合同，买方可以起诉违约或寻求具体履行。在这里，儿子在与朋友签订销售合同时并不拥有该土地的所有权。然而，他在预定的交割前继承了所有权。因此，卖方拥有可出售的所有权，并且必须履行其承诺，将其转让给朋友。既然他不愿意这样做，那么就应该对这位朋友的具体表现给予评判。 （选择 B）当金钱补救措施无法使原告获得完整时，通常会使用具体履行。目标是充分补偿原告，而不是防止被告不当得利。 （选择C）如果卖方在交割时通过担保契据正式转让所有权并随后获得该财产的所有权，则事后所有权原则（即契约禁止反言）将适用于正在执行的合同（例如房地产销售合同）。一旦卖方收到所有权，所有权就会自动转移给买方。 （选项D） 儿子有权与朋友签订合同，但缺​​乏履行合同的能力，直到他继承了该土地。一旦发生这种情况，他就有义务将房产卖给朋友。\n\n教育目标：一旦买方和卖方签署了销售合同，他们交换的承诺就具有约束力。因此，如果卖方能够在交割时转让所有权但拒绝这样做，买方可以起诉违约或寻求具体履行。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一旦买方和卖方签署了销售合同，他们交换的承诺就具有约束力。因此，如果卖方能够在交割时转让所有权但拒绝这样做，买方可以起诉违约或寻求具体履行。","iracZh":{"issue":"在该行动中，朋友应该获得什么奖励？","rule":"一旦买方和卖方签署了销售合同，他们交换的承诺就具有约束力。因此，如果卖方能够在交割时转让所有权但拒绝这样做，买方可以起诉违约或寻求具体履行。","analysis":"不动产的销售通常包括三个部分： 销售合同的执行 - 买方承诺支付购买价，以换取卖方交付所有权的承诺 执行期 - 买方可以检查财产，获得贷款并审查所有权文件，卖方可以修复财产或纠正所有权中的缺陷 交割 - 卖方通过将契约交付给买方来正式转让所有权，买方支付购买价 一旦销售合同执行，交换的承诺就具有约束力。因此，如果卖方在交割时获得了可流通的所有权但不愿意履行合同，买方可以起诉违约或寻求具体履行。在这里，儿子在与朋友签订销售合同时并不拥有该土地的所有权。然而，他在预定的交割前继承了所有权。因此，卖方拥有可出售的所有权，并且必须履行其承诺，将其转让给朋友。既然他不愿意这样做，那么就应该对这位朋友的具体表现给予评判。 （选择 B）当金钱补救措施无法使原告获得完整时，通常会使用具体履行。目标是充分补偿原告，而不是防止被告不当得利。 （选择C）如果卖方在交割时通过担保契据正式转让所有权并随后获得该财产的所有权，则事后所有权原则（即契约禁止反言）将适用于正在执行的合同（例如房地产销售合同）。一旦卖方收到所有权，所有权就会自动转移给买方。 （选项D） 儿子有权与朋友签订合同，但缺​​乏履行合同的能力，直到他继承了该土地。一旦发生这种情况，他就有义务将房产卖给朋友。\n\n教育目标：一旦买方和卖方签署了销售合同，他们交换的承诺就具有约束力。因此，如果卖方能够在交割时转让所有权但拒绝这样做，买方可以起诉违约或寻求具体履行。","conclusion":"答案 A：具体表现判断，因为儿子在预定交割前获得了所有权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-fafb7d52ab72d1ec"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":175,"sourceQuestionPage":513,"sourceQuestionPages":[513],"sourceExplanationPages":[514,515],"sourceAnswerPages":[514,515],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-176","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"A man conveyed a valid easement appurtenant across his ranch to a woman who owned the adjoining property, on which she operated a commercial farm. The easement was not recorded. Shortly before his death, the man devised his ranch to his son. The will made no mention of the woman's easement. The son, who lived out-of-state, never came to visit the ranch. The woman decided to expand the size of her crops and openly used the man's ranch to do so. She paid the taxes on the property and used the land exclusively for 20 years. However, she never used the easement during that time. After facing some financial hardship, the son decided to sell the ranch. To prevent the sale, the woman filed and won a quiet-title suit confirming that she had acquired ownership of the ranch through adverse possession. The son felt guilty about losing his father's ranch, so he worked out a deal with the woman to repurchase the ranch. After the sale, the woman resumed her use of the easement across the ranch that had been granted to her by the man. The son, already angry over having to repurchase the ranch, immediately objected to the woman entering his property. The woman has filed suit to enforce the easement. The jurisdiction has a notice recording act. Will the woman likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because she acquired title to the ranch through adverse possession.","B":"No, because the son was not provided proper notice of the easement.","C":"Yes, because an easement appurtenant automatically runs with the land.","D":"Yes, because failure to use an easement does not extinguish it."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The man granted the woman an easement appurtenant* across his ranch. An easement appurtenant is a nonpossessory right to use another's land (servient estate) that benefits the easement holder's land (dominant estate). This type of easement runs with the land, so it automatically transfers with the conveyance of the dominant or servient estate. But since easements are a real property interest, they are subject to the jurisdiction's recording act. Under a notice recording act (as seen here), real property interests cannot be enforced against subsequent purchasers of a conflicting real property interest who lack notice of the prior interest. But since the son is not a purchaser for value (he was devised the ranch), he is not protected by the recording act. Therefore, the fact that he was not provided proper notice of the easement is of no consequence (Choice B). However, the woman's adverse possession of the ranch is of consequence. That is because, under the merger doctrine, an easement will terminate when the dominant estate (the farm) and the servient estate (the ranch) merge into the hands of a single owner. That is because a landowner cannot own an easement across his/her own land. Therefore, the easement was extinguished once the woman acquired title to both the farm and the ranch (Choice C). Once terminated, an easement cannot be revived by merely resevering the dominant and servient estates—a new easement must be created. Therefore, the woman's easement over the ranch was not revived when she sold the ranch to the son. And since no new easement was created, the woman likely will not prevail in her suit to enforce the easement. *Easements are presumed to be appurtenant unless otherwise stated. (Choice D) An easement can be terminated by abandonment if the easement holder acts in an affirmative way that shows a clear intent to relinquish the easement—mere nonuse of the easement is not enough. However, the woman will not prevail because the easement was terminated by merger.\n\nEducational objective: Under the merger doctrine, an easement terminates once the dominant and servient estates merge into the hands of a single owner.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination of easement by merger).","educationalObjective":"Under the merger doctrine, an easement terminates once the dominant and servient estates merge into the hands of a single owner.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination of easement by merger).","rule":"Under the merger doctrine, an easement terminates once the dominant and servient estates merge into the hands of a single owner.","irac":{"issue":"Will the woman likely prevail?","rule":"Under the merger doctrine, an easement terminates once the dominant and servient estates merge into the hands of a single owner.","analysis":"The man granted the woman an easement appurtenant* across his ranch. An easement appurtenant is a nonpossessory right to use another's land (servient estate) that benefits the easement holder's land (dominant estate). This type of easement runs with the land, so it automatically transfers with the conveyance of the dominant or servient estate. But since easements are a real property interest, they are subject to the jurisdiction's recording act. Under a notice recording act (as seen here), real property interests cannot be enforced against subsequent purchasers of a conflicting real property interest who lack notice of the prior interest. But since the son is not a purchaser for value (he was devised the ranch), he is not protected by the recording act. Therefore, the fact that he was not provided proper notice of the easement is of no consequence (Choice B). However, the woman's adverse possession of the ranch is of consequence. That is because, under the merger doctrine, an easement will terminate when the dominant estate (the farm) and the servient estate (the ranch) merge into the hands of a single owner. That is because a landowner cannot own an easement across his/her own land. Therefore, the easement was extinguished once the woman acquired title to both the farm and the ranch (Choice C). Once terminated, an easement cannot be revived by merely resevering the dominant and servient estates—a new easement must be created. Therefore, the woman's easement over the ranch was not revived when she sold the ranch to the son. And since no new easement was created, the woman likely will not prevail in her suit to enforce the easement. *Easements are presumed to be appurtenant unless otherwise stated. (Choice D) An easement can be terminated by abandonment if the easement holder acts in an affirmative way that shows a clear intent to relinquish the easement—mere nonuse of the easement is not enough. However, the woman will not prevail because the easement was terminated by merger.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because she acquired title to the ranch through adverse possession."},"questionZh":"一名男子将其牧场上的有效地役权附属物转让给一名拥有毗邻财产的妇女，她在该财产上经营着一个商业农场。地役权没有记录。在他去世前不久，该男子为他的儿子设计了他的牧场。遗嘱没有提及该女子的地役权。这个住在州外的儿子从来没有来参观过牧场。这名女子决定扩大农作物种植面积，并公开使用男子的牧场。她缴纳了房产税并独占使用了这片土地20年。然而，她在那段时间从未使用过地役权。在面临一些经济困难后，儿子决定卖掉牧场。为了阻止出售，该妇女提起并赢得了一项安静所有权诉讼，确认她通过逆权占有获得了牧场的所有权。儿子对失去父亲的牧场感到内疚，所以他与女人达成协议，重新购买了牧场。出售后，该妇女恢复使用该男子授予她的整个牧场的地役权。儿子已经对必须重新购买牧场感到愤怒，立即反对该女子进入他的财产。该女子已提起诉讼以强制执行地役权。该司法管辖区有通知记录法。女人可能会获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为她通过逆权占有获得了牧场的所有权。","B":"不，因为儿子没有收到有关地役权的适当通知。","C":"是的，因为地役权附属物自动随土地一起运行。","D":"是的，因为不使用地役权并不会消除地役权。"},"explanationZh":"男人授予女人一份横跨他牧场的地役权*。地役权附属物是使用他人土地（役产）的非占有性权利，使地役权持有人的土地（主导产业）受益。这种类型的地役权随土地一起运行，因此它会随着支配性或受役性遗产的转让而自动转移。但由于地役权是不动产权益，因此须遵守司法管辖区的记录法。根据通知记录法（如此处所示），如果没有通知先前的权益，则不能对冲突的不动产权益的后续购买者强制执行不动产权益。但由于儿子不是价值购买者（他是设计牧场的），因此他不受记录法的保护。因此，他没有收到有关地役权的适当通知这一事实并不重要（选择 B）。然而，该妇女对牧场的逆权占有却产生了后果。这是因为，根据合并原则，当主导地产（农场）和从属地产（牧场）合并到单一所有者手中时，地役权将终止。这是因为土地所有者不能在自己的土地上拥有地役权。因此，一旦妇女获得了农场和牧场的所有权，地役权就消失了（选项C）。一旦终止，地役权不能仅通过保留主导和从属财产来恢复，必须创建新的地役权。因此，当该妇女将牧场卖给儿子时，她对牧场的地役权并没有恢复。由于没有创建新的地役权，该妇女很可能不会在强制执行地役权的诉讼中获胜。 *除非另有说明，地役权被认为是附属的。 （选项 D）如果地役权持有人以明确的方式明确表示放弃地役权，仅不使用地役权是不够的，则地役权可以通过放弃而终止。然而，这位女士不会获胜，因为地役权因合并而终止。\n\n教育目标：根据合并原则，一旦支配性财产和服务性财产合并到单一所有者手中，地役权即终止。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.5（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（通过合并终止地役权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据合并原则，一旦支配性财产和从属财产合并到单一所有者手中，地役权即终止。","iracZh":{"issue":"女人可能会获胜吗？","rule":"根据合并原则，一旦支配性财产和从属财产合并到单一所有者手中，地役权即终止。","analysis":"男人授予女人一份横跨他牧场的地役权*。地役权附属物是使用他人土地（役产）的非占有性权利，使地役权持有人的土地（主导产业）受益。这种类型的地役权随土地一起运行，因此它会随着支配性或受役性遗产的转让而自动转移。但由于地役权是不动产权益，因此须遵守司法管辖区的记录法。根据通知记录法（如此处所示），如果没有通知先前的权益，则不能对冲突的不动产权益的后续购买者强制执行不动产权益。但由于儿子不是价值购买者（他是设计牧场的），因此他不受记录法的保护。因此，他没有收到有关地役权的适当通知这一事实并不重要（选择 B）。然而，该妇女对牧场的逆权占有却产生了后果。这是因为，根据合并原则，当主导地产（农场）和从属地产（牧场）合并到单一所有者手中时，地役权将终止。这是因为土地所有者不能在自己的土地上拥有地役权。因此，一旦妇女获得了农场和牧场的所有权，地役权就消失了（选项C）。一旦终止，地役权不能仅通过保留主导和从属财产来恢复，必须创建新的地役权。因此，当该妇女将牧场卖给儿子时，她对牧场的地役权并没有恢复。由于没有创建新的地役权，该妇女很可能不会在强制执行地役权的诉讼中获胜。 *除非另有说明，地役权被认为是附属的。 （选项 D）如果地役权持有人以明确的方式明确表示放弃地役权，仅不使用地役权是不够的，则地役权可以通过放弃而终止。然而，这位女士不会获胜，因为地役权因合并而终止。\n\n教育目标：根据合并原则，一旦支配性财产和服务性财产合并到单一所有者手中，地役权即终止。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.5（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（通过合并终止地役权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为她通过逆权占有获得了牧场的所有权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-71c257c50bd9e181"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":176,"sourceQuestionPage":516,"sourceQuestionPages":[516],"sourceExplanationPages":[517,518],"sourceAnswerPages":[517,518],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-177","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A man owned a lakefront home in a gated community with a pier where he docked his boat. His neighbor, whose home did not have a pier, told him that she was interested in purchasing a boat but that she had nowhere to dock it except for the community clubhouse. The clubhouse was located at the other end of the gated community far from the neighbor's home. However, the man told the neighbor that she could use his pier to dock her boat, and as a result, the neighbor purchased a boat knowing that she could dock it close to home. For the next 11 years, the neighbor used the man's pier to dock her boat. During this time, the neighbor also paid for the pier to be rebuilt due to corrosion. Thereafter, the man told the neighbor that she could only use the pier if she paid a monthly fee of $600. When the neighbor refused to pay the fee, the man built a fence around the pier and refused to give the neighbor a key to unlock the fence. The statutory period for acquiring rights by prescription in the jurisdiction is 10 years. Does the neighbor have the right to use the man's pier to dock her boat?","choices":{"A":"No, because the easement was not in a writing that satisfied the statute of frauds.","B":"No, because the neighbor could have docked her boat at the community clubhouse.","C":"Yes, because the neighbor detrimentally relied on the man's permission to use the pier.","D":"Yes, because the neighbor's use of the pier was continuous, actual, and open."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Distinctions between easements\n\nType Appurtenant Benefits easement holder's land Benefit & burden automatically transfer when land is conveyed In gross Benefits easement holder personally Burden (not benefit) automatically transfers when land is conveyed Purpose Affirmative Allows easement holder to do something on servient estate\n\nNegative Restricts servient-estate owner's lawful use of servient estate\n\nCreation Express Created by parties in writing that satisfies statute of frauds\n\nIrrevocable License becomes irrevocable due to estoppel or coupling with license another servitude\n\nImplied Implied by law due to necessity (easement by necessity) Implied by fact due to prior use (easement by implication) By prescription Adversely acquired through OCAN—open/notorious, actual, continuous & nonpermissive use An easement is a right held by one person to make specific, limited use of land owned by another. The land that is subject to the easement is the servient estate, while the land that benefits from the easement is the dominant estate. Most easements are expressly created in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds, but they also can be created by other methods—including estoppel. An easement by estoppel arises from good-faith, reasonable, detrimental reliance on the servient-estate owner's permission to make a limited use of his/her land.* Here, the man (servient-estate owner) gave the neighbor (dominant-estate owner) permission to use his pier to dock her boat. The neighbor reasonably relied on that permission to her detriment by purchasing a boat, having no reason to believe that the permission would be revoked. She even paid for the man's pier to be rebuilt due to corrosion. Therefore, the neighbor obtained an easement by estoppel, giving her the right to use the man's pier. *An easement by estoppel can also be characterized as an irrevocable license. (Choice A) While a valid easement can be expressly created by a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds, it can also be created by other means (here, by estoppel).\n\n(Choice B) The neighbor's ability to dock her boat at the community clubhouse has no bearing on her right to use the man's pier. Although she would be inconvenienced if she had to use the community clubhouse, this does not create an easement by necessity on the man's pier. Such an easement can be implied only when property is rendered virtually useless (typically from being landlocked) without an easement across the neighboring property. (Choices D) Although the neighbor openly and continuously used the pier for more than the 10-year statutory period, that use was with the man's permission. This negates the hostile (nonpermissive) use required to establish an easement by prescription.\n\nEducational objective: An easement by estoppel is created through good-faith, reasonable, detrimental reliance on the servient-estate owner's permission to make a limited use of his/her land.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.10 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (servitudes created by estoppel).","educationalObjective":"An easement by estoppel is created through good-faith, reasonable, detrimental reliance on the servient-estate owner's permission to make a limited use of his/her land.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.10 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (servitudes created by estoppel).","rule":"An easement by estoppel is created through good-faith, reasonable, detrimental reliance on the servient-estate owner's permission to make a limited use of his/her land.","irac":{"issue":"Does the neighbor have the right to use the man's pier to dock her boat?","rule":"An easement by estoppel is created through good-faith, reasonable, detrimental reliance on the servient-estate owner's permission to make a limited use of his/her land.","analysis":"Distinctions between easements\n\nType Appurtenant Benefits easement holder's land Benefit & burden automatically transfer when land is conveyed In gross Benefits easement holder personally Burden (not benefit) automatically transfers when land is conveyed Purpose Affirmative Allows easement holder to do something on servient estate\n\nNegative Restricts servient-estate owner's lawful use of servient estate\n\nCreation Express Created by parties in writing that satisfies statute of frauds\n\nIrrevocable License becomes irrevocable due to estoppel or coupling with license another servitude\n\nImplied Implied by law due to necessity (easement by necessity) Implied by fact due to prior use (easement by implication) By prescription Adversely acquired through OCAN—open/notorious, actual, continuous & nonpermissive use An easement is a right held by one person to make specific, limited use of land owned by another. The land that is subject to the easement is the servient estate, while the land that benefits from the easement is the dominant estate. Most easements are expressly created in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds, but they also can be created by other methods—including estoppel. An easement by estoppel arises from good-faith, reasonable, detrimental reliance on the servient-estate owner's permission to make a limited use of his/her land.* Here, the man (servient-estate owner) gave the neighbor (dominant-estate owner) permission to use his pier to dock her boat. The neighbor reasonably relied on that permission to her detriment by purchasing a boat, having no reason to believe that the permission would be revoked. She even paid for the man's pier to be rebuilt due to corrosion. Therefore, the neighbor obtained an easement by estoppel, giving her the right to use the man's pier. *An easement by estoppel can also be characterized as an irrevocable license. (Choice A) While a valid easement can be expressly created by a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds, it can also be created by other means (here, by estoppel).\n\n(Choice B) The neighbor's ability to dock her boat at the community clubhouse has no bearing on her right to use the man's pier. Although she would be inconvenienced if she had to use the community clubhouse, this does not create an easement by necessity on the man's pier. Such an easement can be implied only when property is rendered virtually useless (typically from being landlocked) without an easement across the neighboring property. (Choices D) Although the neighbor openly and continuously used the pier for more than the 10-year statutory period, that use was with the man's permission. This negates the hostile (nonpermissive) use required to establish an easement by prescription.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the neighbor detrimentally relied on the man's permission to use the pier."},"questionZh":"一名男子在一个封闭的社区拥有一套湖滨住宅，该社区有一个码头，他的船就停泊在码头上。他的邻居家没有码头，她告诉他，她有兴趣购买一艘船，但除了社区会所之外，她没有地方可以停泊。俱乐部会所位于封闭社区的另一端，远离邻居的家。然而，该男子告诉邻居，她可以使用他的码头停泊她的船，结果，邻居知道她可以把它停在离家很近的地方，就购买了一艘船。接下来的11年里，邻居一直用这个男人的码头来停泊她的船。这段时间，邻居还出钱重建因腐蚀的码头。此后，该男子告诉邻居，必须每月缴纳600美元的费用才能使用码头。当邻居拒绝支付费用时，该男子在码头周​​围筑起了栅栏，并拒绝给邻居一把打开栅栏的钥匙。辖区内凭时效取得权利的法定期限为10年。邻居有权利使用男方的码头停泊她的船吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为地役权的书面形式不符合欺诈法。","B":"不，因为邻居可以将她的船停泊在社区俱乐部会所。","C":"是的，因为邻居不利地依赖了该男子的许可来使用码头。","D":"是的，因为邻居对码头的使用是连续的、实际的、开放的。"},"explanationZh":"地役权之间的区别\n\n类型 附属福利 地役权持有人的土地 土地转让时，利益和负担自动转移 总利益 地役权持有人个人 土地转让时，负担（而非福利）自动转移 目的 肯定式 允许地役权持有人在受役地做某事\n\n负面 限制供役地所有人合法使用供役地\n\nCreation Express 由各方以书面形式创建，符合欺诈法规\n\n不可撤销的许可由于禁止反言或与许可另一奴役相结合而变得不可撤销\n\n默示 因必要性而由法律默示（必要性地役权） 由于先前使用而根据事实默示（默示地役权） 通过时效 通过 OCAN 反向获得 - 公开/臭名昭著、实际、连续和非许可使用 地役权是一个人持有的一项权利，可以对另一个人拥有的土地进行特定、有限的使用。受地役权约束的土地是供役地，而受益于地役权的土地是支配地。大多数地役权都是以符合欺诈法规的书面形式明确创建的，但它们也可以通过其他方法（包括禁止反言）创建。禁止反言的地役权源于善意、合理、有害地依赖役用地产所有者对其土地进行有限使用的许可。*在此，该男子（役用地产所有者）允许邻居（支配性地产所有者）使用他的码头来停靠她的船。邻居合理地依赖该许可购买了一艘船，对她造成了损害，没有理由相信该许可会被撤销。她甚至出钱重建该男子因腐蚀的码头。因此，邻居通过禁止反悔的方式获得了地役权，赋予了她使用该男子码头的权利。 *禁止反言的地役权也可以被视为不可撤销的许可。 （选项 A）虽然有效的地役权可以通过满足欺诈法规的书面形式明确创建，但也可以通过其他方式（此处为禁止反言）创建。\n\n（选项 B） 邻居将她的船停泊在社区会所的能力与她使用该男子码头的权利无关。虽然如果她必须使用社区会所会给她带来不便，但这并不必然会在该男子的码头上产生地役权。只有当财产变得几乎无用（通常是由于内陆）而没有邻近财产的地役权时，才可以隐含这种地役权。 （选项D） 虽然邻居公开并持续使用该码头超过10年法定期限，但该使用是经过该男子许可的。这否定了通过时效建立地役权所需的敌意（非许可）使用。\n\n教育目标：禁止反言地役权是通过善意、合理、有害地依赖奴役地所有者对其土地进行有限使用的许可而产生的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.10 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（禁止反言规定的劳役）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"禁止反言地役权是通过善意、合理、有害地依赖农奴地所有者对其土地进行有限使用的许可而创建的。","iracZh":{"issue":"邻居有权利使用男方的码头停泊她的船吗？","rule":"禁止反言地役权是通过善意、合理、有害地依赖农奴地所有者对其土地进行有限使用的许可而创建的。","analysis":"地役权之间的区别\n\n类型 附属福利 地役权持有人的土地 土地转让时，利益和负担自动转移 总利益 地役权持有人个人 土地转让时，负担（而非福利）自动转移 目的 肯定式 允许地役权持有人在受役地做某事\n\n负面 限制供役地所有人合法使用供役地\n\nCreation Express 由各方以书面形式创建，符合欺诈法规\n\n不可撤销的许可由于禁止反言或与许可另一奴役相结合而变得不可撤销\n\n默示 因必要性而由法律默示（必要性地役权） 由于先前使用而根据事实默示（默示地役权） 通过时效 通过 OCAN 反向获得 - 公开/臭名昭著、实际、连续和非许可使用 地役权是一个人持有的一项权利，可以对另一个人拥有的土地进行特定、有限的使用。受地役权约束的土地是供役地，而受益于地役权的土地是支配地。大多数地役权都是以符合欺诈法规的书面形式明确创建的，但它们也可以通过其他方法（包括禁止反言）创建。禁止反言的地役权源于善意、合理、有害地依赖役用地产所有者对其土地进行有限使用的许可。*在此，该男子（役用地产所有者）允许邻居（支配性地产所有者）使用他的码头来停靠她的船。邻居合理地依赖该许可购买了一艘船，对她造成了损害，没有理由相信该许可会被撤销。她甚至出钱重建该男子因腐蚀的码头。因此，邻居通过禁止反悔的方式获得了地役权，赋予了她使用该男子码头的权利。 *禁止反言的地役权也可以被视为不可撤销的许可。 （选项 A）虽然有效的地役权可以通过满足欺诈法规的书面形式明确创建，但也可以通过其他方式（此处为禁止反言）创建。\n\n（选项 B） 邻居将她的船停泊在社区会所的能力与她使用该男子码头的权利无关。虽然如果她必须使用社区会所会给她带来不便，但这并不必然会在该男子的码头上产生地役权。只有当财产变得几乎无用（通常是由于内陆）而没有邻近财产的地役权时，才可以隐含这种地役权。 （选项D） 虽然邻居公开并持续使用该码头超过10年法定期限，但该使用是经过该男子许可的。这否定了通过时效建立地役权所需的敌意（非许可）使用。\n\n教育目标：禁止反言地役权是通过善意、合理、有害地依赖奴役地所有者对其土地进行有限使用的许可而产生的。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.10 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（禁止反言规定的劳役）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为邻居不利地依赖了该男子的许可来使用码头。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"easement by implication","ipa":"/ˈizmənt bˈaɪ ˌɪmplʌkˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"ease·ment by im·pli·ca·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"默示地役权：分割前存在明显持续使用且分割后合理必要时产生","contextualMeaning":"默示地役权：分割前存在明显持续使用且分割后合理必要时产生","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Distinctions between easements Type Appurtenant Benefits easement holder's land Benefit & burden automatically transfer when land is conveyed In gross Benefits easement holder personally Burden (not benefit) automatically transfers when land is conveyed Purpose Affirmative Allows easement holder to do something on servient estate Negative Restricts servient-estate owner's lawful use of servient estate Creation Expres","synonyms":["implied easement"]},{"term":"easement by estoppel","ipa":"/ˈizmənt bˈaɪ i'stɒpl/","syllables":"ease·ment by estop·pel","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"禁反言地役权：土地所有人的表示使他人合理信赖并重大投入时产生","contextualMeaning":"禁反言地役权：土地所有人的表示使他人合理信赖并重大投入时产生","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"An easement by estoppel is created through good-faith, reasonable, detrimental reliance on the servient-estate owner's permission to make a limited use of his/her land.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"irrevocable license","ipa":"/ˌɪɹˈɛvəkəbəl lˈaɪsəns/","syllables":"ir·rev·o·ca·ble li·cense","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不可撤销许可：因禁反言或与权益结合而暂不能撤销的土地使用许可","contextualMeaning":"不可撤销许可：因禁反言或与权益结合而暂不能撤销的土地使用许可","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Distinctions between easements Type Appurtenant Benefits easement holder's land Benefit & burden automatically transfer when land is conveyed In gross Benefits easement holder personally Burden (not benefit) automatically transfers when land is conveyed Purpose Affirmative Allows easement holder to do something on servient estate Negative Restricts servient-estate owner's lawful use of servient estate Creation Expres","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":177,"sourceQuestionPage":519,"sourceQuestionPages":[519],"sourceExplanationPages":[520,521],"sourceAnswerPages":[520,521],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-178","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A little more than five years ago, a builder completed construction of a single-family home located on a lot that the builder owned. Five years ago, the builder and a barista entered into a valid five-year written lease of the home that included the following language: \"This house is rented as is, without certain necessary or useful items. The parties agree that the barista may acquire and install such items as she wishes at her expense, and that she may remove them if she wishes at the termination of this lease.\" The barista decided that the house needed, and she paid cash to have installed, standard- sized combination screen/storm windows, a freestanding refrigerator to fit a kitchen alcove built for that purpose, a built-in electric stove and oven to fit a kitchen counter opening left for that purpose, and carpeting to cover the plywood living room floor. Last month, by legal description of the land, the builder conveyed the home to a salesman for $100,000 subject to the barista's lease. The salesman knew of the barista's soon- expiring tenancy but did not examine the written lease. As the lease expiration date approached, the salesman learned that the barista planned to vacate on schedule and learned for the first time that the barista claimed and planned to remove all of the above- listed items that she had installed. The salesman promptly brought an appropriate action to enjoin the barista from removing those items. Which items should the court allow the barista to remove?","choices":{"A":"None of the items.","B":"Only the refrigerator.","C":"All items except the carpet.","D":"All of the items."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When a tenant attaches tangible personal property (ie, a chattel) to the leased premises, the tenant can remove the chattel before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease expires if the chattel is not a fixture—ie, if: the attachment of the chattel was not intended to be permanent or\n\nthe chattel is not being used for some larger component (eg, lighting, water drainage) or function (eg, plumbing, septic) of the land.\n\nAnd when a landlord conveys leased premises subject to an existing lease, the terms of the lease govern the new landlord-tenant relationship. Here, the lease agreement between the builder and the barista expressly allowed the barista to install certain items in the home and remove them at the end of her tenancy (no intent to permanently attach chattel). And since the builder conveyed the leased premises to the salesman subject to the barista's lease, the terms of the lease govern. Therefore, the court should allow the barista to remove all of the items she installed. (Choices A & B) An easily movable appliance like a freestanding refrigerator is usually not a fixture and can be removed by a tenant. But here, the barista could remove all of the items based on the lease agreement. As a result, none of the items are fixtures and the court should allow her to remove them. (Choice C) Carpet may be a fixture if it is particularly adapted to the premises (eg, cut to fit a specific room) since that shows intent to permanently attach it. But since the lease permits removal, the court should allow the barista to remove the carpet—even if it would otherwise be an irremovable fixture.\n\nEducational objective: A tenant can generally remove a chattel attached to the leased premises if (1) the attachment was not intended to be permanent or (2) the chattel is not being used for some larger component or function of the land. And when a landlord conveys land subject to an existing lease, the lease terms govern the new landlord-tenant relationship.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 12.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (tenant's rights and obligations as to changes in the physical condition of the leased property).","educationalObjective":"A tenant can generally remove a chattel attached to the leased premises if (1) the attachment was not intended to be permanent or (2) the chattel is not being used for some larger component or function of the land. And when a landlord conveys land subject to an existing lease, the lease terms govern the new landlord-tenant relationship.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 12.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (tenant's rights and obligations as to changes in the physical condition of the leased property).","rule":"A tenant can generally remove a chattel attached to the leased premises if (1) the attachment was not intended to be permanent or (2) the chattel is not being used for some larger component or function of the land. And when a landlord conveys land subject to an existing lease, the lease terms govern the new landlord-tenant relationship.","irac":{"issue":"Which items should the court allow the barista to remove?","rule":"A tenant can generally remove a chattel attached to the leased premises if (1) the attachment was not intended to be permanent or (2) the chattel is not being used for some larger component or function of the land. And when a landlord conveys land subject to an existing lease, the lease terms govern the new landlord-tenant relationship.","analysis":"When a tenant attaches tangible personal property (ie, a chattel) to the leased premises, the tenant can remove the chattel before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease expires if the chattel is not a fixture—ie, if: the attachment of the chattel was not intended to be permanent or\n\nthe chattel is not being used for some larger component (eg, lighting, water drainage) or function (eg, plumbing, septic) of the land.\n\nAnd when a landlord conveys leased premises subject to an existing lease, the terms of the lease govern the new landlord-tenant relationship. Here, the lease agreement between the builder and the barista expressly allowed the barista to install certain items in the home and remove them at the end of her tenancy (no intent to permanently attach chattel). And since the builder conveyed the leased premises to the salesman subject to the barista's lease, the terms of the lease govern. Therefore, the court should allow the barista to remove all of the items she installed. (Choices A & B) An easily movable appliance like a freestanding refrigerator is usually not a fixture and can be removed by a tenant. But here, the barista could remove all of the items based on the lease agreement. As a result, none of the items are fixtures and the court should allow her to remove them. (Choice C) Carpet may be a fixture if it is particularly adapted to the premises (eg, cut to fit a specific room) since that shows intent to permanently attach it. But since the lease permits removal, the court should allow the barista to remove the carpet—even if it would otherwise be an irremovable fixture.","conclusion":"Answer D: All of the items."},"questionZh":"五年多前，一名建筑商在其拥有的土地上完成了一座单户住宅的建设。五年前，建筑商和一名咖啡师签订了一份为期五年的有效书面房屋租约，其中包括以下内容：“该房屋按原样出租，没有某些必要或有用的物品。双方同意，咖啡师可以根据自己的意愿购买和安装此类物品，费用由她自己承担，并且如果她愿意，她可以在租约终止时将其移走。”咖啡师认为这所房子需要，她支付现金安装了标准尺寸的组合屏风/防风窗、一台独立式冰箱以适应为此目的建造的厨房凹室、一个内置电炉和烤箱以适应为此目的而留下的厨房柜台开口，并在胶合板客厅地板上铺上地毯。上个月，根据土地的法律描述，建筑商以 10 万美元的价格将房屋转让给一名推销员，但须遵守咖啡师的租约。推销员知道咖啡师的租约即将到期，但没有检查书面租约。随着租约到期日的临近，销售人员得知该咖啡师计划按期搬出，并第一次得知该咖啡师声称并计划将她安装的上述所有物品全部拆除。推销员立即采取了适当行动，禁止咖啡师移走这些物品。法院应该允许咖啡师移走哪些物品？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有任何项目。","B":"只有冰箱。","C":"除地毯外的所有物品。","D":"所有的物品。"},"explanationZh":"当承租人将有形动产（即动产）附加到租赁房屋时，如果该动产不是固定物，则承租人可以在租约到期之前或之后的合理时间内移除该动产，即如果： 动产的附加不是永久性的，或者\n\n该动产未用于土地的某些较大组成部分（例如照明、排水）或功能（例如管道、化粪池）。\n\n当房东根据现有租约转让租赁房屋时，租约条款将管辖新的房东与租客关系。在这里，建筑商和咖啡师之间的租赁协议明确允许咖啡师在家中安装某些物品，并在租期结束时将其移除（无意永久附加动产）。由于建筑商根据咖啡师的租约将租赁场地转让给推销员，因此应以租约条款为准。因此，法院应该允许咖啡师移除她安装的所有物品。 （选项 A 和 B）像独立式冰箱这样易于移动的设备通常不是固定装置，可以由租户拆除。但在这里，咖啡师可以根据租赁协议移除所有物品。因此，这些物品都不是固定装置，法院应该允许她移除它们。 （选择C）如果地毯特别适合场所（例如，剪裁以适合特定房间），则地毯可以是固定装置，因为这表明了永久固定它的意图。但由于租约允许拆除，法院应该允许咖啡师拆除地毯——即使它是不可拆除的固定装置。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 附着物并非永久性的，或者 (2) 动产不用于土地的某些较大组成部分或功能，则租户通常可以移除附着在租赁房屋上的动产。当房东根据现有租约转让土地时，租赁条款将管辖新的房东与租客关系。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 12.2 条（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（租户对租赁财产物理状况变化的权利和义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果 (1) 附着物并非永久性的，或者 (2) 动产不用于土地的某些较大组成部分或功能，则租户通常可以移除附着在租赁房屋上的动产。当房东根据现有租约转让土地时，租赁条款将管辖新的房东与租客关系。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该允许咖啡师移走哪些物品？","rule":"如果 (1) 附着物并非永久性的，或者 (2) 动产不用于土地的某些较大组成部分或功能，则租户通常可以移除附着在租赁房屋上的动产。当房东根据现有租约转让土地时，租赁条款将管辖新的房东与租客关系。","analysis":"当承租人将有形动产（即动产）附加到租赁房屋时，如果该动产不是固定物，则承租人可以在租约到期之前或之后的合理时间内移除该动产，即如果： 动产的附加不是永久性的，或者\n\n该动产未用于土地的某些较大组成部分（例如照明、排水）或功能（例如管道、化粪池）。\n\n当房东根据现有租约转让租赁房屋时，租约条款将管辖新的房东与租客关系。在这里，建筑商和咖啡师之间的租赁协议明确允许咖啡师在家中安装某些物品，并在租期结束时将其移除（无意永久附加动产）。由于建筑商根据咖啡师的租约将租赁场地转让给推销员，因此应以租约条款为准。因此，法院应该允许咖啡师移除她安装的所有物品。 （选项 A 和 B）像独立式冰箱这样易于移动的设备通常不是固定装置，可以由租户拆除。但在这里，咖啡师可以根据租赁协议移除所有物品。因此，这些物品都不是固定装置，法院应该允许她移除它们。 （选择C）如果地毯特别适合场所（例如，剪裁以适合特定房间），则地毯可以是固定装置，因为这表明了永久固定它的意图。但由于租约允许拆除，法院应该允许咖啡师拆除地毯——即使它是不可拆除的固定装置。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 附着物并非永久性的，或者 (2) 动产不用于土地的某些较大组成部分或功能，则租户通常可以移除附着在租赁房屋上的动产。当房东根据现有租约转让土地时，租赁条款将管辖新的房东与租客关系。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 12.2 条（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（租户对租赁财产物理状况变化的权利和义务）。","conclusion":"答案 D：所有的物品。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-d28321184fdd2964"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":178,"sourceQuestionPage":522,"sourceQuestionPages":[522],"sourceExplanationPages":[523,524],"sourceAnswerPages":[523,524],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-179","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Twenty-five years ago, a man who owned a 45-acre tract of land conveyed 40 of the 45 acres to a developer by warranty deed. The man retained the rear five-acre portion of the land and continues to live there in a large farmhouse. The deed to the 40-acre tract was promptly recorded. It contained the following language: \"It is a term and condition of this deed, which shall be a covenant running with the land and binding on all owners, their heirs and assigns, that no use shall be made of the 40-acre tract of land except for residential purposes.\" Subsequently, the developer fully developed the 40-acre tract into a residential subdivision consisting of 40 lots with a single-family residence on each lot. Although there have been multiple transfers of ownership of each of the 40 lots within the subdivision, none of them included a reference to the quoted provision in the deed from the man to the developer, nor did any deed to a subdivision lot create any new covenants restricting use. Last year, a major new medical center was constructed adjacent to the subdivision. A doctor who owns a house in the subdivision wishes to relocate her medical office to her house. For the first time, the doctor learned of the restrictive covenant in the deed from the man to the developer. The applicable zoning ordinance permits the doctor's intended use. The man, as owner of the five-acre tract, however, objects to the doctor's proposed use of her property. There are no governing statutes other than the zoning code. The common law Rule Against Perpetuities is unmodified in the jurisdiction. May the doctor convert her house in the subdivision into a medical office?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man owns property benefitted by the original restrictive covenant and has a right to enforce it.","B":"No, because the owners of lots in the subdivision own property benefitted by the original residential covenant and have the sole right to enforce it.","C":"Yes, because the original restrictive covenant violates the Rule Against Perpetuities.","D":"Yes, because the zoning ordinance allows the doctor's proposed use and preempts the restrictive covenant."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A real covenant is an express promise to do (affirmative) or not do (negative/restrictive) something on land that is enforceable by an action for money damages. The promising parties are bound to the covenant under contract law. But successors in interest are bound only if the covenant runs with the land. This occurs when the following elements are met: Writing – covenant is in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, deed to 40-acre tract)\n\nIntent to run – promising parties intended for the covenant to run to their successors in interest (eg, deed says \"running with the land,\" \"binding to heirs and assigns\")\n\nTouch and concern – covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of the benefited and burdened lands (eg, limiting use of 40-acre tract to benefit five-acre tract)\n\nHorizontal privity – promising parties simultaneously transferred the land and created the covenant (eg, covenant created when 40-acre tract was deeded to developer)\n\nVertical privity – successor of the benefited estate has a possessory interest (not applicable here since the man is a promising party) and successor of the burdened estate has a promising party's entire estate or ownership interest (eg, doctor has same ownership interest that developer had)\n\nNotice – person to be bound had notice of the covenant (eg, doctor had record notice of promptly recorded deed containing the covenant)\n\nTherefore, the man has a right to enforce the restrictive covenant and stop the doctor from converting her house into a medical office. (Choice B) The lot owners are successors in interest to the developer's 40-acre estate. As lot owners, they have a possessory interest in that estate that creates vertical privity. They—along with the man—can therefore enforce the covenant. (Choice C) The Rule Against Perpetuities only applies to future interests that have a possibility of vesting (eg, contingent remainders)—not to real covenants or other nonpossessory property interests. (Choice D) A zoning ordinance overrides a restrictive covenant only when the two are in direct conflict (eg, a covenant restricting use to commercial purposes in an area zoned only for residential use).\n\nEducational objective: A real covenant will run to successors in interest if the following elements are met: (1) writing, (2) intent to run, (3) touch and concern, (4) horizontal privity, (5) vertical privity, and (6) notice.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property §§ 530–38 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (setting out the elements for a real covenant).","educationalObjective":"A real covenant will run to successors in interest if the following elements are met: (1) writing, (2) intent to run, (3) touch and concern, (4) horizontal privity, (5) vertical privity, and (6) notice.","references":"Restatement of Property §§ 530–38 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (setting out the elements for a real covenant).","rule":"A real covenant will run to successors in interest if the following elements are met: (1) writing, (2) intent to run, (3) touch and concern, (4) horizontal privity, (5) vertical privity, and (6) notice.","irac":{"issue":"May the doctor convert her house in the subdivision into a medical office?","rule":"A real covenant will run to successors in interest if the following elements are met: (1) writing, (2) intent to run, (3) touch and concern, (4) horizontal privity, (5) vertical privity, and (6) notice.","analysis":"A real covenant is an express promise to do (affirmative) or not do (negative/restrictive) something on land that is enforceable by an action for money damages. The promising parties are bound to the covenant under contract law. But successors in interest are bound only if the covenant runs with the land. This occurs when the following elements are met: Writing – covenant is in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, deed to 40-acre tract)\n\nIntent to run – promising parties intended for the covenant to run to their successors in interest (eg, deed says \"running with the land,\" \"binding to heirs and assigns\")\n\nTouch and concern – covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of the benefited and burdened lands (eg, limiting use of 40-acre tract to benefit five-acre tract)\n\nHorizontal privity – promising parties simultaneously transferred the land and created the covenant (eg, covenant created when 40-acre tract was deeded to developer)\n\nVertical privity – successor of the benefited estate has a possessory interest (not applicable here since the man is a promising party) and successor of the burdened estate has a promising party's entire estate or ownership interest (eg, doctor has same ownership interest that developer had)\n\nNotice – person to be bound had notice of the covenant (eg, doctor had record notice of promptly recorded deed containing the covenant)\n\nTherefore, the man has a right to enforce the restrictive covenant and stop the doctor from converting her house into a medical office. (Choice B) The lot owners are successors in interest to the developer's 40-acre estate. As lot owners, they have a possessory interest in that estate that creates vertical privity. They—along with the man—can therefore enforce the covenant. (Choice C) The Rule Against Perpetuities only applies to future interests that have a possibility of vesting (eg, contingent remainders)—not to real covenants or other nonpossessory property interests. (Choice D) A zoning ordinance overrides a restrictive covenant only when the two are in direct conflict (eg, a covenant restricting use to commercial purposes in an area zoned only for residential use).","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the man owns property benefitted by the original restrictive covenant and has a right to enforce it."},"questionZh":"25 年前，一名拥有 45 英亩土地的男子通过保修契约将这 45 英亩土地中的 40 英亩转让给了开发商。该男子保留了后面五英亩的土地，并继续住在那里的一座大农舍里。 40 英亩土地的契约立即被记录下来。它包含以下语言：“本契约的条款和条件应成为与土地一起运行的契约，对所有所有者、其继承人和受让人具有约束力，除住宅用途外，不得使用这 40 英亩的土地。”随后，开发商将这片40英亩的土地全面开发为由40块地块组成的住宅小区，每块地块上都有一座独栋住宅。尽管该细分内的 40 个地块的所有权均已多次转让，但均未提及该男子向开发商的契约中引用的条款，也没有任何细分地块的契约产生任何限制使用的新契约。去年，该土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）附近建造了一个大型新医疗中心。一位在该小区拥有房屋的医生希望将她的医疗办公室搬到她的房子里。医生第一次了解到该男子与开发商的契约中存在限制性条款。适用的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例允许医生的预期用途。然而，该男子作为五英亩土地的所有者，反对医生使用她的财产的提议。除土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法规外，没有其他管理法规。普通法中的反永续规则在管辖范围内未作修改。医生可以把她小区的房子改造成医务室吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该男子拥有受益于原始限制性契约的财产，并且有权执行该契约。","B":"不会，因为该土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）内地块的所有者拥有的财产受益于原始住宅契约，并且拥有强制执行该契约的唯一权利。","C":"是的，因为原来的限制性契约违反了“反对永久规则”。","D":"是的，因为土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例允许医生提议的使用并优先于限制性契约。"},"explanationZh":"土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做（肯定的）或不做（否定的/限制性的）某事的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼强制执行。根据合同法，承诺方受该契约的约束。但只有在土地契约生效的情况下，利益继承人才受到约束。当满足以下要素时，就会发生这种情况： 书面形式——契约是满足欺诈法规的书面形式（例如，40 英亩土地的契约）\n\n意图竞选 – 承诺各方希望盟约将其利益传给其继承人（例如，契约上写着“与土地一起竞选”、“对继承人和受让人具有约束力”）\n\n接触和关注——与受益和负担土地的使用、享有或占用有关的契约（例如，限制使用 40 英亩土地以惠及 5 英亩土地）\n\n横向相互关系——承诺方同时转让土地并签订契约（例如，将 40 英亩土地转让给开发商时签订契约）\n\n纵向关联性——受益遗产的继承人拥有占有权益（此处不适用，因为该男子是承诺方），负担遗产的继承人拥有承诺方的全部遗产或所有权权益（例如，医生与开发商拥有相同的所有权权益）\n\n通知 – 受约束人已收到该契约的通知（例如，医生已收到包含该契约的立即记录契约的记录通知）\n\n因此，该男子有权执行限制性契约并阻止医生将她的房子改造成医疗办公室。 （选择 B） 地块所有者是开发商 40 英亩庄园权益的继承人。作为土地所有者，他们对该地产拥有所有权权益，从而创造了垂直相关性。因此，他们——以及男人——可以执行该契约。 （选项C）反对永续权规则仅适用于有可能归属的未来权益（例如或有剩余），不适用于真实契约或其他非占有性财产权益。 （选项D）只有当两者直接冲突时，土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例才会优先于限制性契约（例如，在仅划为住宅用途的区域中限制用于商业目的的契约）。\n\n教育目标：如果满足以下要素，真正的盟约就会传达给利益继承者：（1）书写，（2）跑步意图，（3）触摸和关心，（4）水平相互关系，（5）垂直相互关系，以及（6）通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 530–38（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（列出真正契约的要素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果满足以下要素，真正的盟约就会流向继任者：（1）书面，（2）跑步意图，（3）接触和关心，（4）横向相互关系，（5）纵向相互关系，以及（6）通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"医生可以把她小区的房子改造成医务室吗？","rule":"如果满足以下要素，真正的盟约就会流向继任者：（1）书面，（2）跑步意图，（3）接触和关心，（4）横向相互关系，（5）纵向相互关系，以及（6）通知。","analysis":"土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做（肯定的）或不做（否定的/限制性的）某事的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼强制执行。根据合同法，承诺方受该契约的约束。但只有在土地契约生效的情况下，利益继承人才受到约束。当满足以下要素时，就会发生这种情况： 书面形式——契约是满足欺诈法规的书面形式（例如，40 英亩土地的契约）\n\n意图竞选 – 承诺各方希望盟约将其利益传给其继承人（例如，契约上写着“与土地一起竞选”、“对继承人和受让人具有约束力”）\n\n接触和关注——与受益和负担土地的使用、享有或占用有关的契约（例如，限制使用 40 英亩土地以惠及 5 英亩土地）\n\n横向相互关系——承诺方同时转让土地并签订契约（例如，将 40 英亩土地转让给开发商时签订契约）\n\n纵向关联性——受益遗产的继承人拥有占有权益（此处不适用，因为该男子是承诺方），负担遗产的继承人拥有承诺方的全部遗产或所有权权益（例如，医生与开发商拥有相同的所有权权益）\n\n通知 – 受约束人已收到该契约的通知（例如，医生已收到包含该契约的立即记录契约的记录通知）\n\n因此，该男子有权执行限制性契约并阻止医生将她的房子改造成医疗办公室。 （选择 B） 地块所有者是开发商 40 英亩庄园权益的继承人。作为土地所有者，他们对该地产拥有所有权权益，从而创造了垂直相关性。因此，他们——以及男人——可以执行该契约。 （选项C）反对永续权规则仅适用于有可能归属的未来权益（例如或有剩余），不适用于真实契约或其他非占有性财产权益。 （选项D）只有当两者直接冲突时，土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例才会优先于限制性契约（例如，在仅划为住宅用途的区域中限制用于商业目的的契约）。\n\n教育目标：如果满足以下要素，真正的盟约就会传达给利益继承者：（1）书写，（2）跑步意图，（3）触摸和关心，（4）水平相互关系，（5）垂直相互关系，以及（6）通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 530–38（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（列出真正契约的要素）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为该男子拥有受益于原始限制性契约的财产，并且有权执行该契约。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-9f0bb98ef342f636"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":179,"sourceQuestionPage":525,"sourceQuestionPages":[525],"sourceExplanationPages":[526,527],"sourceAnswerPages":[526,527],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-180","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"The owner of a tract of land mortgaged the tract to a bank to secure his preexisting obligation to the bank. The mortgage was promptly and properly recorded. The owner and a buyer then entered into a valid written contract for the purchase and sale of the tract, which provided for the transfer of \"a marketable title, free of encumbrances.\" The contract did not expressly refer to the mortgage. Shortly after entering into the contract, the buyer found another property that much better suited her needs and decided to try to avoid her contract with the owner. When the buyer discovered the existence of the mortgage, she asserted that the title was encumbered and that she would not close. The owner responded by offering to provide for payment and discharge of the mortgage at the closing from the proceeds of the closing. The buyer refused to go forward, and the owner brought an appropriate action against her for specific performance. If the court holds for the owner in this action, what would be the likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The doctrine of equitable conversion supports the result.","B":"The existence of the mortgage was not the buyer's real reason for refusing to close.","C":"The mortgage is not entitled to priority because it was granted for preexisting obligations.","D":"The owner's arrangements for the payment of the mortgage fully satisfied his obligation to deliver marketable title."},"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession All contracts for the sale of land have an implied warranty that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing unless otherwise stated. Title need not be perfect to be marketable. However, it must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. If the seller cannot convey marketable title by the time of closing, the buyer can rescind (ie, cancel) the contract. Title can be rendered unmarketable by encumbrances (eg, easements, mortgages, other liens). Although a mortgage is an encumbrance, the seller has the right to satisfy the mortgage up to the time of closing and will often use the sale proceeds to do so—as the owner has made arrangements to do here. As a result, the court will hold for the owner because the closing itself will likely render title marketable. (Choice A) The doctrine of equitable conversion relates to the ownership of the property and the risk of loss in the period between contract formation and closing. It does not affect the parties' performance under the land-sale contract. (Choice B) The buyer could have refused to close if she had an actual basis for doing so— even if that was not the buyer's actual reason for the refusal. (Choice C) Priority refers to the order in which creditors receive sale proceeds from a foreclosure; whether a mortgage was granted for preexisting obligations does not affect its priority. Regardless, this will not affect the owner's success because (1) there is no indication that there are other encumbrances on the property, and (2) the mortgage will likely be satisfied and thereby extinguished upon closing.\n\nEducational objective: A mortgage is an encumbrance that can render title unmarketable. However, the seller has the right to satisfy the mortgage up to the time of closing and will often use the sale proceeds to do so.","educationalObjective":"A mortgage is an encumbrance that can render title unmarketable. However, the seller has the right to satisfy the mortgage up to the time of closing and will often use the sale proceeds to do so.","references":"","rule":"A mortgage is an encumbrance that can render title unmarketable. However, the seller has the right to satisfy the mortgage up to the time of closing and will often use the sale proceeds to do so.","irac":{"issue":"If the court holds for the owner in this action, what would be the likely reason?","rule":"A mortgage is an encumbrance that can render title unmarketable. However, the seller has the right to satisfy the mortgage up to the time of closing and will often use the sale proceeds to do so.","analysis":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession All contracts for the sale of land have an implied warranty that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing unless otherwise stated. Title need not be perfect to be marketable. However, it must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. If the seller cannot convey marketable title by the time of closing, the buyer can rescind (ie, cancel) the contract. Title can be rendered unmarketable by encumbrances (eg, easements, mortgages, other liens). Although a mortgage is an encumbrance, the seller has the right to satisfy the mortgage up to the time of closing and will often use the sale proceeds to do so—as the owner has made arrangements to do here. As a result, the court will hold for the owner because the closing itself will likely render title marketable. (Choice A) The doctrine of equitable conversion relates to the ownership of the property and the risk of loss in the period between contract formation and closing. It does not affect the parties' performance under the land-sale contract. (Choice B) The buyer could have refused to close if she had an actual basis for doing so— even if that was not the buyer's actual reason for the refusal. (Choice C) Priority refers to the order in which creditors receive sale proceeds from a foreclosure; whether a mortgage was granted for preexisting obligations does not affect its priority. Regardless, this will not affect the owner's success because (1) there is no indication that there are other encumbrances on the property, and (2) the mortgage will likely be satisfied and thereby extinguished upon closing.","conclusion":"Answer D: The owner's arrangements for the payment of the mortgage fully satisfied his obligation to deliver marketable title."},"questionZh":"一块土地的所有者将这块土地抵押给银行，以确保他对银行已有的债务。抵押贷款被及时、正确地记录。随后，业主和买方就该土地的购买和销售签订了有效的书面合同，其中规定转让“可出售的所有权，无产权负担”。合同中没有明确提及抵押贷款。签订合同后不久，买方发现另一处房产更适合她的需求，并决定尝试避免与业主签订合同。当买家发现抵押贷款的存在时，她声称所有权已受到限制，并且她不会关闭。业主对此作出回应，提出在交割时用交割收益支付和解除抵押贷款。买方拒绝继续前进，业主针对她的具体表现对她提起了适当的诉讼。如果法院在该诉讼中支持业主，可能的理由是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则支持了这一结果。","B":"抵押贷款的存在并不是买家拒绝成交的真正原因。","C":"抵押贷款不享有优先权，因为它是为预先存在的义务而授予的。","D":"业主对抵押贷款支付的安排完全履行了其交付可出售所有权的义务。"},"explanationZh":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租赁 留置权 所有权链中的缺口 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 所有土地销售合同均包含默示保证，除非另有说明，否则卖方将在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。标题不一定要完美才能有销路。然而，它必须合理地没有疑问，并且不受诉讼威胁，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。如果卖方在成交时无法转让可流通的所有权，买方可以解除（即取消）合同。产权负担（例如地役权、抵押贷款、其他留置权）可能会导致产权无法出售。尽管抵押贷款是一种负担，但卖方有权在成交前偿还抵押贷款，并且通常会使用出售收益来偿还抵押贷款——正如业主在这里所做的安排。因此，法院将支持业主，因为交割本身可能会使所有权变得可流通。 （选项A） 衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则涉及财产的所有权以及合同订立和结束期间的损失风险。不影响当事人履行土地出让合同。 （选项B）如果买方有实际依据，她可以拒绝成交——即使这不是买方拒绝的真正原因。 （选项C）优先权是指债权人从取消抵押品赎回权中获得出售收益的顺序；是否为先前存在的债务授予抵押贷款并不影响其优先权。无论如何，这不会影响业主的成功，因为（1）没有迹象表明该房产存在其他产权负担，（2）抵押贷款很可能会在交割时得到清偿并因此消失。\n\n教育目标：抵押贷款是一种负担，可能导致产权无法出售。然而，卖方有权在交割前偿还抵押贷款，并且通常会使用销售收益来偿还抵押贷款。","educationalObjectiveZh":"抵押贷款是一种负担，可能导致所有权无法出售。然而，卖方有权在交割前偿还抵押贷款，并且通常会使用销售收益来偿还抵押贷款。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院在该诉讼中支持业主，可能的理由是什么？","rule":"抵押贷款是一种负担，可能导致所有权无法出售。然而，卖方有权在交割前偿还抵押贷款，并且通常会使用销售收益来偿还抵押贷款。","analysis":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租赁 留置权 所有权链中的缺口 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 所有土地销售合同均包含默示保证，除非另有说明，否则卖方将在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。标题不一定要完美才能有销路。然而，它必须合理地没有疑问，并且不受诉讼威胁，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。如果卖方在成交时无法转让可流通的所有权，买方可以解除（即取消）合同。产权负担（例如地役权、抵押贷款、其他留置权）可能会导致产权无法出售。尽管抵押贷款是一种负担，但卖方有权在成交前偿还抵押贷款，并且通常会使用出售收益来偿还抵押贷款——正如业主在这里所做的安排。因此，法院将支持业主，因为交割本身可能会使所有权变得可流通。 （选项A） 衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则涉及财产的所有权以及合同订立和结束期间的损失风险。不影响当事人履行土地出让合同。 （选项B）如果买方有实际依据，她可以拒绝成交——即使这不是买方拒绝的真正原因。 （选项C）优先权是指债权人从取消抵押品赎回权中获得出售收益的顺序；是否为先前存在的债务授予抵押贷款并不影响其优先权。无论如何，这不会影响业主的成功，因为（1）没有迹象表明该房产存在其他产权负担，（2）抵押贷款很可能会在交割时得到清偿并因此消失。\n\n教育目标：抵押贷款是一种负担，可能导致产权无法出售。然而，卖方有权在交割前偿还抵押贷款，并且通常会使用销售收益来偿还抵押贷款。","conclusion":"答案 D：业主对抵押贷款支付的安排完全履行了其交付可出售所有权的义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":180,"sourceQuestionPage":528,"sourceQuestionPages":[528],"sourceExplanationPages":[529,530],"sourceAnswerPages":[529,530],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-181","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A businesswoman owned two adjoining tracts of land, one that was improved with a commercial rental building and another that was vacant and abutted a river. Twenty years ago, the businesswoman conveyed the vacant tract to a grantee by a warranty deed that the businesswoman signed but the grantee did not. The deed contained a covenant by the grantee as owner of the vacant tract that neither he nor his heirs or assigns would \"erect any building\" on the vacant tract, in order to preserve the view of the river from the commercial building on the improved tract. The grantee intended to use the vacant tract as a nature preserve. The grantee promptly and properly recorded the deed. Last year, the businesswoman conveyed the improved tract to a businessman. A month later, the grantee died, devising all of his property, including the vacant land, to his cousin. Six weeks ago, the cousin began construction of a building on the vacant tract. The businessman objected and sued to enjoin construction of the building. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The businessman, because the commercial building was constructed before the cousin began his construction project.","B":"The businessman, because the cousin is bound by the covenant made by the grantee.","C":"The cousin, because an equitable servitude does not survive the death of the promisor.","D":"The cousin, because the grantee did not sign the deed."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes\n\nReal covenants Equitable servitudes\n\n(enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief)\n\nFor burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied\n\nWriting Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice privity privity Notice Notice No notice required An equitable servitude is a covenant (ie, promise) to do or not do something on land, enforceable by an action for equitable relief against the parties who made the promise and their successors in interest. An express equitable servitude requires: Writing – the covenant is expressed in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, the deed from the businesswoman to the grantee)\n\nIntent to run – the promising parties intended for the covenant to run to (ie, bind) their successors in interest (eg, the deed says \"heirs or assigns\")\n\nTouch and concern – the covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of both the dominant and servient estates (eg, prohibits building on one tract to preserve the view on the other) and\n\nNotice – the owner of the servient (ie, burdened) estate has actual, record, or inquiry notice of the covenant (eg, the cousin has record notice because the grantee's deed was properly recorded).\n\nTherefore, the cousin is bound by the covenant made by the grantee, and the businessman will prevail in his action to enjoin construction of the building on the vacant tract. (Choice A) The fact that the commercial building was constructed before the cousin began his construction project has no effect on the outcome of this case. (Choice C) An equitable servitude does survive the death of the promisor because the parties intend to bind their successors in interest. (Choice D) A valid deed need only be signed by the grantor (here, the businesswoman), so it does not matter that the grantee did not sign the deed.\n\nEducational objective: To create an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run with the land, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the owner of the servient estate must have had notice of the covenant.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property § 539 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (equitable servitudes).","educationalObjective":"To create an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run with the land, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the owner of the servient estate must have had notice of the covenant.","references":"Restatement of Property § 539 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (equitable servitudes).","rule":"To create an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run with the land, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the owner of the servient estate must have had notice of the covenant.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"To create an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run with the land, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the owner of the servient estate must have had notice of the covenant.","analysis":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes\n\nReal covenants Equitable servitudes\n\n(enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief)\n\nFor burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied\n\nWriting Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice privity privity Notice Notice No notice required An equitable servitude is a covenant (ie, promise) to do or not do something on land, enforceable by an action for equitable relief against the parties who made the promise and their successors in interest. An express equitable servitude requires: Writing – the covenant is expressed in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, the deed from the businesswoman to the grantee)\n\nIntent to run – the promising parties intended for the covenant to run to (ie, bind) their successors in interest (eg, the deed says \"heirs or assigns\")\n\nTouch and concern – the covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of both the dominant and servient estates (eg, prohibits building on one tract to preserve the view on the other) and\n\nNotice – the owner of the servient (ie, burdened) estate has actual, record, or inquiry notice of the covenant (eg, the cousin has record notice because the grantee's deed was properly recorded).\n\nTherefore, the cousin is bound by the covenant made by the grantee, and the businessman will prevail in his action to enjoin construction of the building on the vacant tract. (Choice A) The fact that the commercial building was constructed before the cousin began his construction project has no effect on the outcome of this case. (Choice C) An equitable servitude does survive the death of the promisor because the parties intend to bind their successors in interest. (Choice D) A valid deed need only be signed by the grantor (here, the businesswoman), so it does not matter that the grantee did not sign the deed.","conclusion":"Answer B: The businessman, because the cousin is bound by the covenant made by the grantee."},"questionZh":"一位女商人拥有两块相邻的土地，一块土地经过改建，建有商业出租大楼，另一块土地则空置，毗邻河流。二十年前，女商人通过一份保修契约将空地转让给受让人，该女商人签署了但受让人没有签署的保修契约。该契约包含受让人作为空置土地所有者的一项契约，即他及其继承人或受让人都不会在空置土地上“建造任何建筑物”，以保留改良土地上商业建筑的河流景观。受赠者打算将这块空地用作自然保护区。受让人及时、正确地记录了该行为。去年，这位女商人将改进后的单张转交给了一位商人。一个月后，受赠者去世，将他的所有财产，包括空置土地，划给了他的表弟。六周前，表弟开始在空地上建造一座建筑物。该商人表示反对并提起诉讼，要求禁止该建筑的施工。谁有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"商人，因为商业楼是在表弟开始建设项目之前建造的。","B":"商人，因为表弟受到受赠人订立的契约的约束。","C":"表弟，因为公平的奴役不会在承诺人死亡后继续存在。","D":"表弟，因为受赠人没有在契约上签字。"},"explanationZh":"真实契约和公平劳役的要素\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant） 公平的劳役\n\n（可通过金钱赔偿强制执行）（可通过衡平法救济强制执行）\n\n为了负担而运行 为了利益而运行明示暗示\n\n写作 写作 写作 意图创造共同 意图运行 意图运行 意图运行计划 接触与关注 接触与关注 接触与限制性劳役 水平与垂直 有限垂直关注 通知 相互关系 通知 通知 无需通知 衡平法劳役是在土地上做或不做某事的契约（即承诺），可以通过对做出承诺的各方及其利益继承人采取公平救济诉讼来强制执行。明确的公平劳役要求： 书面形式——契约以符合欺诈法规的书面形式表达（例如，女商人给受让人的契约）\n\n意图竞选——有承诺的各方打算通过盟约竞选（即约束）其利益继承人（例如，契约上写着“继承人或受让人”）\n\n接触和关注——该契约涉及主导地产和从属地产的使用、享有或占用（例如，禁止在一块土地上进行建筑以保护另一块土地的景观）以及\n\n通知——供役（即负担）遗产的所有者拥有契约的实际、记录或查询通知（例如，表兄弟拥有记录通知，因为受让人的契约已正确记录）。\n\n因此，表兄受受让人所订立的契约的约束，而商人在其禁止在空地上建造建筑物的诉讼中将占上风。 （选项A）该商业楼是在表弟开始施工之前建造的，这一事实对本案的结果没有影响。 （选择 C） 衡平法上的劳役在承诺人死亡后仍然有效，因为当事人打算约束其继承人的利益。 （选项D）有效的契约只需由授予人（此处为女商人）签署即可，因此受让人不签署契约也没关系。\n\n教育目标：为了建立明确的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将其与土地一起经营，(3) 契约必须触及并涉及土地，以及 (4) 劳役财产的所有者必须已知晓该契约。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 539（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（衡平法劳役）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为了建立明确的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将其与土地一起经营，(3) 契约必须触及并涉及土地，以及 (4) 劳役财产的所有者必须已知晓该契约。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"为了建立明确的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将其与土地一起经营，(3) 契约必须触及并涉及土地，以及 (4) 劳役财产的所有者必须已知晓该契约。","analysis":"真实契约和公平劳役的要素\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant） 公平的劳役\n\n（可通过金钱赔偿强制执行）（可通过衡平法救济强制执行）\n\n为了负担而运行 为了利益而运行明示暗示\n\n写作 写作 写作 意图创造共同 意图运行 意图运行 意图运行计划 接触与关注 接触与关注 接触与限制性劳役 水平与垂直 有限垂直关注 通知 相互关系 通知 通知 无需通知 衡平法劳役是在土地上做或不做某事的契约（即承诺），可以通过对做出承诺的各方及其利益继承人采取公平救济诉讼来强制执行。明确的公平劳役要求： 书面形式——契约以符合欺诈法规的书面形式表达（例如，女商人给受让人的契约）\n\n意图竞选——有承诺的各方打算通过盟约竞选（即约束）其利益继承人（例如，契约上写着“继承人或受让人”）\n\n接触和关注——该契约涉及主导地产和从属地产的使用、享有或占用（例如，禁止在一块土地上进行建筑以保护另一块土地的景观）以及\n\n通知——供役（即负担）遗产的所有者拥有契约的实际、记录或查询通知（例如，表兄弟拥有记录通知，因为受让人的契约已正确记录）。\n\n因此，表兄受受让人所订立的契约的约束，而商人在其禁止在空地上建造建筑物的诉讼中将占上风。 （选项A）该商业楼是在表弟开始施工之前建造的，这一事实对本案的结果没有影响。 （选择 C） 衡平法上的劳役在承诺人死亡后仍然有效，因为当事人打算约束其继承人的利益。 （选项D）有效的契约只需由授予人（此处为女商人）签署即可，因此受让人不签署契约也没关系。\n\n教育目标：为了建立明确的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将其与土地一起经营，(3) 契约必须触及并涉及土地，以及 (4) 劳役财产的所有者必须已知晓该契约。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 § 539（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（衡平法劳役）。","conclusion":"答案 B：商人，因为表弟受到受赠人订立的契约的约束。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":181,"sourceQuestionPage":531,"sourceQuestionPages":[531],"sourceExplanationPages":[532,533],"sourceAnswerPages":[532,533],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-182","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A woman owned land that abutted a public highway to the south. A neighbor owned the land immediately to the north. The neighbor's predecessor had received an easement from the woman's predecessor in title to cross the woman's land for access to the public highway. This access was desired even though the neighbor and his predecessor had other access to public roads. The easement was in writing and properly recorded. Recently, the woman erected a large solar collector on a portion of her land subject to the easement, even though the woman has other power resources. The location was essential to acquire the maximum sunlight. However, by erecting the collector there, the woman effectively cut off the neighbor's ability to use the easement. The local zoning code permits the use of solar collectors. The neighbor immediately sued to compel the woman to remove the solar collector. Must the solar collector be removed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the neighbor has other means of access.","B":"No, because the zoning code permits the use of solar collectors.","C":"Yes, because the easement remains valid.","D":"Yes, because the woman has other power resources."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Distinctions between easements\n\nType Appurtenant Benefits easement holder's land Benefit & burden automatically transfer when land is conveyed In gross Benefits easement holder personally Burden (not benefit) automatically transfers when land is conveyed Purpose Affirmative Allows easement holder to do something on servient estate\n\nNegative Restricts servient-estate owner's lawful use of servient estate\n\nCreation Express Created by parties in writing that satisfies statute of frauds\n\nIrrevocable License becomes irrevocable due to estoppel or coupling with license another servitude\n\nImplied Implied by law due to necessity (easement by necessity) Implied by fact due to prior use (easement by implication) By prescription Adversely acquired through OCAN—open/notorious, actual, continuous & nonpermissive use Easements are either appurtenant (ie, tied to the land) OR in gross (ie, personal to the easement holder).* An easement is appurtenant if it enhances the use and enjoyment of the dominant estate by allowing the easement holder to use the servient estate in a limited way (eg, a right-of-way across the servient estate—as seen here). Since this type of easement is tied to the land, the benefit and burden of the easement rest with the current owners of the dominant and servient estates, respectively. Here, the neighbor's predecessor received an easement appurtenant from the woman's predecessor in title to cross her land for access to the public highway. That easement is tied to the land, so both the neighbor (current dominant-estate owner) and the woman (current servient-estate owner) are bound by it. And though the woman is free to use the valid easement in any way that does not unreasonably interfere with the neighbor's use, her solar collector effectively cut off his ability to use the easement. Therefore, the solar collector must be removed. *Easements are presumed to be appurtenant unless there are clear facts to the contrary (eg, if the easement is set to last for only a limited time).\n\n(Choice A) The neighbor's ability to access his land from other public roads would defeat an implied easement by necessity. But it does not prevent him from enforcing the express easement granted to his predecessor. (Choice B) Because the zoning ordinance permits solar collectors, the woman can lawfully use them on her land. But she cannot do so in a way that unreasonably interferes with the neighbor's use of the easement. (Choice D) The fact that the woman has other power sources does not compel her to remove the solar collector. Instead, she must remove the solar collector because it violates the neighbor's ability to use the easement.\n\nEducational objective: An easement appurtenant is tied to the land, meaning that the benefit and burden of the easement rest with the current owners of the dominant and servient estates, respectively. And though the servient-estate owner is free to use the easement, he/she may not unreasonably interfere with the dominant-estate owner's use.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (distinguishing easements appurtenant from easements in gross).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.9 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (discussing limitations on servient-estate owner's use of easement).","educationalObjective":"An easement appurtenant is tied to the land, meaning that the benefit and burden of the easement rest with the current owners of the dominant and servient estates, respectively. And though the servient-estate owner is free to use the easement, he/she may not unreasonably interfere with the dominant-estate owner's use.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (distinguishing easements appurtenant from easements in gross).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.9 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (discussing limitations on servient-estate owner's use of easement).","rule":"An easement appurtenant is tied to the land, meaning that the benefit and burden of the easement rest with the current owners of the dominant and servient estates, respectively. And though the servient-estate owner is free to use the easement, he/she may not unreasonably interfere with the dominant-estate owner's use.","irac":{"issue":"Must the solar collector be removed?","rule":"An easement appurtenant is tied to the land, meaning that the benefit and burden of the easement rest with the current owners of the dominant and servient estates, respectively. And though the servient-estate owner is free to use the easement, he/she may not unreasonably interfere with the dominant-estate owner's use.","analysis":"Distinctions between easements\n\nType Appurtenant Benefits easement holder's land Benefit & burden automatically transfer when land is conveyed In gross Benefits easement holder personally Burden (not benefit) automatically transfers when land is conveyed Purpose Affirmative Allows easement holder to do something on servient estate\n\nNegative Restricts servient-estate owner's lawful use of servient estate\n\nCreation Express Created by parties in writing that satisfies statute of frauds\n\nIrrevocable License becomes irrevocable due to estoppel or coupling with license another servitude\n\nImplied Implied by law due to necessity (easement by necessity) Implied by fact due to prior use (easement by implication) By prescription Adversely acquired through OCAN—open/notorious, actual, continuous & nonpermissive use Easements are either appurtenant (ie, tied to the land) OR in gross (ie, personal to the easement holder).* An easement is appurtenant if it enhances the use and enjoyment of the dominant estate by allowing the easement holder to use the servient estate in a limited way (eg, a right-of-way across the servient estate—as seen here). Since this type of easement is tied to the land, the benefit and burden of the easement rest with the current owners of the dominant and servient estates, respectively. Here, the neighbor's predecessor received an easement appurtenant from the woman's predecessor in title to cross her land for access to the public highway. That easement is tied to the land, so both the neighbor (current dominant-estate owner) and the woman (current servient-estate owner) are bound by it. And though the woman is free to use the valid easement in any way that does not unreasonably interfere with the neighbor's use, her solar collector effectively cut off his ability to use the easement. Therefore, the solar collector must be removed. *Easements are presumed to be appurtenant unless there are clear facts to the contrary (eg, if the easement is set to last for only a limited time).\n\n(Choice A) The neighbor's ability to access his land from other public roads would defeat an implied easement by necessity. But it does not prevent him from enforcing the express easement granted to his predecessor. (Choice B) Because the zoning ordinance permits solar collectors, the woman can lawfully use them on her land. But she cannot do so in a way that unreasonably interferes with the neighbor's use of the easement. (Choice D) The fact that the woman has other power sources does not compel her to remove the solar collector. Instead, she must remove the solar collector because it violates the neighbor's ability to use the easement.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the easement remains valid."},"questionZh":"一名妇女拥有毗邻南边一条公路的土地。紧邻北面的土地由一位邻居拥有。邻居的前任从该妇女的前任那里获得了地役权，有权穿过该妇女的土地进入公共高速公路。尽管邻居和他的前任有其他通往公共道路的通道，但这种通道仍然是人们所希望的。地役权是书面形式并正确记录的。最近，尽管该妇女拥有其他电力资源，但该妇女在其受地役权约束的部分土地上安装了大型太阳能集热器。该位置对于获得最大的阳光至关重要。然而，通过在那里安装收集器，该妇女实际上切断了邻居使用地役权的能力。当地的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法规允许使用太阳能集热器。邻居立即提起诉讼，要求女子拆除太阳能集热器。太阳能集热器必须拆除吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为邻居有其他访问方式。","B":"不可以，因为土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法规允许使用太阳能集热器。","C":"是的，因为地役权仍然有效。","D":"是的，因为女人还有其他权力资源。"},"explanationZh":"地役权之间的区别\n\n类型 附属福利 地役权持有人的土地 土地转让时，利益和负担自动转移 总利益 地役权持有人个人 土地转让时，负担（而非福利）自动转移 目的 肯定式 允许地役权持有人在受役地做某事\n\n负面 限制供役地所有人合法使用供役地\n\nCreation Express 由各方以书面形式创建，符合欺诈法规\n\n不可撤销的许可由于禁止反言或与许可另一奴役相结合而变得不可撤销\n\n默示 因必要性而由法律默示（因必要性地役权） 因先前使用而因事实默示（默示地役权） 通过时效 通过 OCAN 反向获得 — 开放/臭名昭著、实际、连续和非许可使用 地役权可以是从属的（即与土地挂钩），也可以是总的（即地役权持有人个人的）。* 如果地役权通过允许地役权增强了主导财产的使用和享受，则地役权是从属的持有人以有限的方式使用供役地产（例如，穿过供役地产的通行权——如此处所示）。由于这种类型的地役权与土地挂钩，地役权的利益和负担分别由主导和受役地产的当前所有者承担。在这里，邻居的前任从该妇女的前任那里获得了一份地役权附属物，有权穿越她的土地进入公共高速公路。该地役权与土地挂钩，因此邻居（当前的主导地产所有者）和妇女（当前的从属地产所有者）都受到它的约束。尽管该妇女可以以任何不无理干扰邻居使用的方式自由使用有效地役权，但她的太阳能集热器实际上切断了他使用地役权的能力。因此，必须拆除太阳能集热器。 *地役权被推定为附属物，除非有明确的相反事实（例如，如果地役权设置为仅持续有限的时间）。\n\n（选择 A） 邻居从其他公共道路进入其土地的能力将不可避免地击败隐含的地役权。但这并不妨碍他执行授予其前任的明确地役权。 （选项B）由于土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例允许太阳能集热器，因此该妇女可以在她的土地上合法使用它们。但她不能以无理干扰邻居使用地役权的方式这样做。 （选项 D） 该妇女拥有其他电源这一事实并不迫使她拆除太阳能集热器。相反，她必须拆除太阳能集热器，因为它侵犯了邻居使用地役权的能力。\n\n教育目标：地役权附属物与土地挂钩，这意味着地役权的利益和负担分别由主导和从属地产的当前所有者承担。尽管供役地所有者可以自由使用地役权，但他/她不得无理地干扰支配性地所有者的使用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.5（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（区分附属地役权和总地役权）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.9（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（讨论对劳役地产所有者使用地役权的限制）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"地役权附属物与土地挂钩，这意味着地役权的利益和负担分别由主导和从属地产的当前所有者承担。尽管供役地所有者可以自由使用地役权，但他/她不得无理地干扰支配性地所有者的使用。","iracZh":{"issue":"太阳能集热器必须拆除吗？","rule":"地役权附属物与土地挂钩，这意味着地役权的利益和负担分别由主导和从属地产的当前所有者承担。尽管供役地所有者可以自由使用地役权，但他/她不得无理地干扰支配性地所有者的使用。","analysis":"地役权之间的区别\n\n类型 附属福利 地役权持有人的土地 土地转让时，利益和负担自动转移 总利益 地役权持有人个人 土地转让时，负担（而非福利）自动转移 目的 肯定式 允许地役权持有人在受役地做某事\n\n负面 限制供役地所有人合法使用供役地\n\nCreation Express 由各方以书面形式创建，符合欺诈法规\n\n不可撤销的许可由于禁止反言或与许可另一奴役相结合而变得不可撤销\n\n默示 因必要性而由法律默示（因必要性地役权） 因先前使用而因事实默示（默示地役权） 通过时效 通过 OCAN 反向获得 — 开放/臭名昭著、实际、连续和非许可使用 地役权可以是从属的（即与土地挂钩），也可以是总的（即地役权持有人个人的）。* 如果地役权通过允许地役权增强了主导财产的使用和享受，则地役权是从属的持有人以有限的方式使用供役地产（例如，穿过供役地产的通行权——如此处所示）。由于这种类型的地役权与土地挂钩，地役权的利益和负担分别由主导和受役地产的当前所有者承担。在这里，邻居的前任从该妇女的前任那里获得了一份地役权附属物，有权穿越她的土地进入公共高速公路。该地役权与土地挂钩，因此邻居（当前的主导地产所有者）和妇女（当前的从属地产所有者）都受到它的约束。尽管该妇女可以以任何不无理干扰邻居使用的方式自由使用有效地役权，但她的太阳能集热器实际上切断了他使用地役权的能力。因此，必须拆除太阳能集热器。 *地役权被推定为附属物，除非有明确的相反事实（例如，如果地役权设置为仅持续有限的时间）。\n\n（选择 A） 邻居从其他公共道路进入其土地的能力将不可避免地击败隐含的地役权。但这并不妨碍他执行授予其前任的明确地役权。 （选项B）由于土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例允许太阳能集热器，因此该妇女可以在她的土地上合法使用它们。但她不能以无理干扰邻居使用地役权的方式这样做。 （选项 D） 该妇女拥有其他电源这一事实并不迫使她拆除太阳能集热器。相反，她必须拆除太阳能集热器，因为它侵犯了邻居使用地役权的能力。\n\n教育目标：地役权附属物与土地挂钩，这意味着地役权的利益和负担分别由主导和从属地产的当前所有者承担。尽管供役地所有者可以自由使用地役权，但他/她不得无理地干扰支配性地所有者的使用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.5（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（区分附属地役权和总地役权）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.9（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（讨论对劳役地产所有者使用地役权的限制）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为地役权仍然有效。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":182,"sourceQuestionPage":534,"sourceQuestionPages":[534],"sourceExplanationPages":[535,536],"sourceAnswerPages":[535,536],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-183","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"Six years ago, a landlord and a tenant entered into a 10-year commercial lease of land. The written lease provided that if a public entity under the power of eminent domain condemned any part of the land but not all of it, the lease would terminate and the landlord would receive the entire condemnation award. Thereafter, the city condemned approximately two-thirds of the land. The tenant notified the city and the landlord that an independent appraisal of the value of the tenant's possessory interest established that it substantially exceeded the tenant's obligation under the lease and that the tenant was entitled to share the award. The appraisal was accurate. In an appropriate action among the landlord, the tenant, and the city as to the right of the tenant to a portion of the condemnation award, for whom will the court likely find?","choices":{"A":"The landlord, because the condemnation superseded and canceled the lease.","B":"The landlord, because the parties specifically agreed as to the consequences of a partial condemnation.","C":"The tenant, because otherwise the landlord would be unjustly enriched.","D":"The tenant, because the landlord breached the landlord's implied warranty of quiet enjoyment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A taking of all or part of a leasehold under the power of eminent domain generally entitles the tenant to a share of the condemnation award. The tenant's share of the award is equal to the fair market value of the condemned leasehold plus any amount necessary to compensate the tenant for any continued obligation to pay rent that may be required under the lease. However, the parties can expressly agree to alternative provisions in the lease, and courts will typically enforce those agreed-upon provisions. Here, the parties specifically agreed that a partial condemnation would terminate the lease and that the landlord would receive the entire condemnation award. Since the lease controls, the tenant is not entitled to any portion of the condemnation award. (Choice A) The condemnation would have canceled the lease had the city condemned all the land for the entire lease term. Since the city condemned only two-thirds of the land, the lease was terminated in accordance with the landlord and tenant's lease agreement. (Choice C) Although the landlord may be enriched by the condemnation award, it is not unjust because the parties agreed to the lease provision. (Choice D) A landlord's implied warranty of quiet enjoyment provides that the tenant's possession and enjoyment of the leased premises will not be disturbed by the landlord or someone with superior title. The taking of leased land under the power of eminent domain does not breach this warranty because the condemnation occurred through no fault of the landlord.\n\nEducational objective: Unless otherwise provided under a lease, a taking of all or part of a leasehold under the power of eminent domain entitles the tenant to a share of the condemnation award. The tenant's share is equal to the fair market value of the condemned leasehold plus compensation for any continued obligation to pay rent.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord and Tenant §§ 8.1–8.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1977).","educationalObjective":"Unless otherwise provided under a lease, a taking of all or part of a leasehold under the power of eminent domain entitles the tenant to a share of the condemnation award. The tenant's share is equal to the fair market value of the condemned leasehold plus compensation for any continued obligation to pay rent.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Property: Landlord and Tenant §§ 8.1–8.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1977).","rule":"Unless otherwise provided under a lease, a taking of all or part of a leasehold under the power of eminent domain entitles the tenant to a share of the condemnation award. The tenant's share is equal to the fair market value of the condemned leasehold plus compensation for any continued obligation to pay rent.","irac":{"issue":"In an appropriate action among the landlord, the tenant, and the city as to the right of the tenant to a portion of the condemnation award, for whom will the court likely find?","rule":"Unless otherwise provided under a lease, a taking of all or part of a leasehold under the power of eminent domain entitles the tenant to a share of the condemnation award. The tenant's share is equal to the fair market value of the condemned leasehold plus compensation for any continued obligation to pay rent.","analysis":"A taking of all or part of a leasehold under the power of eminent domain generally entitles the tenant to a share of the condemnation award. The tenant's share of the award is equal to the fair market value of the condemned leasehold plus any amount necessary to compensate the tenant for any continued obligation to pay rent that may be required under the lease. However, the parties can expressly agree to alternative provisions in the lease, and courts will typically enforce those agreed-upon provisions. Here, the parties specifically agreed that a partial condemnation would terminate the lease and that the landlord would receive the entire condemnation award. Since the lease controls, the tenant is not entitled to any portion of the condemnation award. (Choice A) The condemnation would have canceled the lease had the city condemned all the land for the entire lease term. Since the city condemned only two-thirds of the land, the lease was terminated in accordance with the landlord and tenant's lease agreement. (Choice C) Although the landlord may be enriched by the condemnation award, it is not unjust because the parties agreed to the lease provision. (Choice D) A landlord's implied warranty of quiet enjoyment provides that the tenant's possession and enjoyment of the leased premises will not be disturbed by the landlord or someone with superior title. The taking of leased land under the power of eminent domain does not breach this warranty because the condemnation occurred through no fault of the landlord.","conclusion":"Answer B: The landlord, because the parties specifically agreed as to the consequences of a partial condemnation."},"questionZh":"六年前，房东和租户签订了为期 10 年的商业土地租赁协议。书面租约规定，如果拥有征用权的公共实体谴责土地的任何部分而不是全部，则租约将终止，房东将获得全部谴责奖。此后，该市征用了大约三分之二的土地。租户通知市政府和房东，对租户占有权益价值的独立评估表明，该价值大大超出了租户在租约下的义务，并且租户有权分享奖励。评价很准确。在房东、租客和市政府就租客获得部分谴责裁决的权利采取适当行动时，法院可能会为谁判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"房东因谴责而取消了租约。","B":"楼主，因为双方特别同意部分谴责的后果。","C":"租客，因为否则房东就会不公正地致富。","D":"租客，因为房东违反了房东关于安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）的默示保证。"},"explanationZh":"根据征用权取得全部或部分租赁权通常使承租人有权获得一部分谴责裁决。租户获得的奖励份额等于被废止的租赁权的公平市场价值加上补偿租户根据租约可能需要支付租金的任何持续义务所需的任何金额。然而，双方可以明确同意租约中的替代条款，法院通常会执行这些商定的条款。在此，双方特别同意，部分谴责将终止租约，房东将获得全部谴责奖。由于租约控制，租户无权获得谴责奖的任何部分。 （选项 A）如果市政府在整个租赁期内谴责所有土地，那么谴责就会取消租约。由于市政府只谴责了三分之二的土地，因此根据房东和租户的租赁协议终止了租约。 （选项C）虽然房东可能因谴责奖而发财，但由于双方同意租赁条款，这并非不公平。 （选项D） 房东对安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）的默示保证规定，承租人对租赁房屋的占有和享受不会受到房东或拥有更高所有权的人的干扰。根据征用权征用租赁土地并不违反本保证，因为该谴责并非因房东的过错而发生。\n\n教育目标：除非租约另有规定，在征用权下取得全部或部分租赁权将使租户有权获得一部分谴责奖。租户的份额等于被废止的租赁权的公平市场价值加上任何继续支付租金义务的补偿。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 §§ 8.1–8.2（Am. Law Inst. 1977）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非租约另有规定，在征用权下取得全部或部分租赁权将使租户有权获得一部分谴责裁决。租户的份额等于被废止的租赁权的公平市场价值加上任何继续支付租金义务的补偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"在房东、租客和市政府就租客获得部分谴责裁决的权利采取适当行动时，法院可能会为谁判决？","rule":"除非租约另有规定，在征用权下取得全部或部分租赁权将使租户有权获得一部分谴责裁决。租户的份额等于被废止的租赁权的公平市场价值加上任何继续支付租金义务的补偿。","analysis":"根据征用权取得全部或部分租赁权通常使承租人有权获得一部分谴责裁决。租户获得的奖励份额等于被废止的租赁权的公平市场价值加上补偿租户根据租约可能需要支付租金的任何持续义务所需的任何金额。然而，双方可以明确同意租约中的替代条款，法院通常会执行这些商定的条款。在此，双方特别同意，部分谴责将终止租约，房东将获得全部谴责奖。由于租约控制，租户无权获得谴责奖的任何部分。 （选项 A）如果市政府在整个租赁期内谴责所有土地，那么谴责就会取消租约。由于市政府只谴责了三分之二的土地，因此根据房东和租户的租赁协议终止了租约。 （选项C）虽然房东可能因谴责奖而发财，但由于双方同意租赁条款，这并非不公平。 （选项D） 房东对安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）的默示保证规定，承租人对租赁房屋的占有和享受不会受到房东或拥有更高所有权的人的干扰。根据征用权征用租赁土地并不违反本保证，因为该谴责并非因房东的过错而发生。\n\n教育目标：除非租约另有规定，在征用权下取得全部或部分租赁权将使租户有权获得一部分谴责奖。租户的份额等于被废止的租赁权的公平市场价值加上任何继续支付租金义务的补偿。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 §§ 8.1–8.2（Am. Law Inst. 1977）。","conclusion":"答案 B：楼主，因为双方特别同意部分谴责的后果。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-77d1cccb8d52284b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":183,"sourceQuestionPage":537,"sourceQuestionPages":[537],"sourceExplanationPages":[538,539],"sourceAnswerPages":[538,539],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-184","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A seller owned a single-family house. A buyer gave the seller a signed, handwritten offer to purchase the house. The offer was unconditional and sufficient to satisfy the statute of frauds, and when the seller signed an acceptance, an enforceable contract resulted. The house had been the seller's home, but he had moved to an apartment, so the house was vacant at all times relevant to the proposed transaction. Two weeks after the parties had entered into their contract, one week after the buyer had obtained a written mortgage lending commitment from a lender, and one week before the agreed-upon closing date, the house was struck by lightning and burned to the ground. The loss was not insured, because three years earlier the seller had let his homeowner's insurance policy lapse after he had paid his mortgage debt in full. The handwritten contract was wholly silent as to matters of financing, risk of loss, and insurance. The buyer declared the contract voided by the fire, but the seller asserted a right to enforce the contract despite the loss. There is no applicable statute. If a court finds for the seller, what will be the likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The contract was construed against the buyer, who drafted it.","B":"The lender's written commitment to make a mortgage loan to the buyer made the contract of sale fully binding on the buyer.","C":"The risk of loss falls on the party in possession, and constructive possession passed to the buyer on the contract date.","D":"The risk of loss passed to the buyer on the contract date under the doctrine of equitable conversion."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When, as here, a real estate contract is silent as to the risk of loss, the doctrine of equitable conversion places that risk on the party with equitable title to the property. Equitable title is transferred to the buyer once the contract is formed and can be specifically enforced. A contract can be specifically enforced if no conditions exist (as seen here) or all contract conditions have been performed. Therefore, the court likely found in favor of the seller because the risk of loss had already passed to the buyer when the house was destroyed. However, had this jurisdiction followed the Uniform Vendor and Purchaser Act (a minority rule), the risk of loss would have remained with the seller until the buyer took actual possession of or received title to the property (Choice C). (Choice A) A contract is generally interpreted according to its plain meaning; however, ambiguities within the contract may be construed against the drafter. But the rules of contract interpretation are irrelevant here because the contract is silent as to the risk of loss and the doctrine of equitable conversion controls. (Choice B) There were no conditions in the contract of sale (e.g., that the buyer secure financing before closing), so the contract became binding once the seller signed the acceptance. Therefore, the buyer would have been obligated to purchase the property even if the buyer had not received a loan commitment.\n\nEducational objective: Unless otherwise provided, the doctrine of equitable conversion places the risk of loss on the party with equitable title to the property. The buyer acquires equitable title once a real estate contract is formed and can be specifically enforced.","educationalObjective":"Unless otherwise provided, the doctrine of equitable conversion places the risk of loss on the party with equitable title to the property. The buyer acquires equitable title once a real estate contract is formed and can be specifically enforced.","references":"","rule":"Unless otherwise provided, the doctrine of equitable conversion places the risk of loss on the party with equitable title to the property. The buyer acquires equitable title once a real estate contract is formed and can be specifically enforced.","irac":{"issue":"If a court finds for the seller, what will be the likely reason?","rule":"Unless otherwise provided, the doctrine of equitable conversion places the risk of loss on the party with equitable title to the property. The buyer acquires equitable title once a real estate contract is formed and can be specifically enforced.","analysis":"When, as here, a real estate contract is silent as to the risk of loss, the doctrine of equitable conversion places that risk on the party with equitable title to the property. Equitable title is transferred to the buyer once the contract is formed and can be specifically enforced. A contract can be specifically enforced if no conditions exist (as seen here) or all contract conditions have been performed. Therefore, the court likely found in favor of the seller because the risk of loss had already passed to the buyer when the house was destroyed. However, had this jurisdiction followed the Uniform Vendor and Purchaser Act (a minority rule), the risk of loss would have remained with the seller until the buyer took actual possession of or received title to the property (Choice C). (Choice A) A contract is generally interpreted according to its plain meaning; however, ambiguities within the contract may be construed against the drafter. But the rules of contract interpretation are irrelevant here because the contract is silent as to the risk of loss and the doctrine of equitable conversion controls. (Choice B) There were no conditions in the contract of sale (e.g., that the buyer secure financing before closing), so the contract became binding once the seller signed the acceptance. Therefore, the buyer would have been obligated to purchase the property even if the buyer had not received a loan commitment.","conclusion":"Answer D: The risk of loss passed to the buyer on the contract date under the doctrine of equitable conversion."},"questionZh":"一位卖家拥有一栋单户住宅。买家向卖家提供了一份签名的手写购买房屋的要约。该要约是无条件的，足以满足欺诈法的要求，当卖方签署接受书时，就产生了一份可执行的合同。该房子曾是卖方的家，但他已搬到公寓，因此该房子在与拟议交易相关的任何时候都是空置的。双方签订合同两周后，买家从贷方获得书面抵押贷款承诺一周后，距离约定的交割日期一周，房子被闪电击中并被烧毁。该损失没有投保，因为三年前，卖方在全额偿还抵押贷款债务后，让其房主的保险单失效。这份手写合同完全没有提及融资、损失风险和保险等事宜。买方宣布合同因火灾而无效，但卖方主张尽管遭受损失，仍有权执行合同。没有适用的法规。如果法院做出有利于卖方的裁决，可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该合同是对起草合同的买方进行解释的。","B":"贷款人向买方提供抵押贷款的书面承诺使销售合同对买方具有完全的约束力。","C":"损失风险由占有方承担，推定占有在合同日期转移给买方。","D":"根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，损失风险在合同日期转移给买方。"},"explanationZh":"当房地产合同没有提及损失风险时（如此处所示），衡平法转换原则将风险置于对财产享有衡平法所有权的一方身上。一旦合同成立，衡平法所有权就转移给买方，并且可以具体执行。如果不存在任何条件（如此处所示）或所有合同条件均已履行，则可以专门执行合同。因此，法院很可能做出对卖方有利的判决，因为当房屋被毁时，损失风险已经转移给买方。然而，如果该管辖区遵循《统一卖方和买方法》（少数派规则），则损失风险将由卖方承担，直到买方实际占有或获得财产所有权为止（选择 C）。 （选项A）合同一般按照其简单含义进行解释；然而，合同中的含糊之处可能会被解释为对起草者不利。但合同解释规则在这里无关紧要，因为合同没有提及损失风险和衡平转换（equitable conversion）控制原则。 （选择B）销售合同中没有任何条件（例如，买方在成交前获得融资），因此一旦卖方签署了承诺书，合同就具有约束力。因此，即使买方没有收到贷款承诺，买方也有义务购买该房产。\n\n教育目标：除非另有规定，衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则将损失风险置于拥有财产公平所有权的一方身上。一旦房地产合同成立并可以具体执行，买方就获得衡平法上的所有权。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非另有规定，衡平法转换原则将损失风险置于对财产具有衡平法所有权的一方身上。一旦房地产合同成立并可以具体执行，买方就获得衡平法上的所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院做出有利于卖方的裁决，可能的原因是什么？","rule":"除非另有规定，衡平法转换原则将损失风险置于对财产具有衡平法所有权的一方身上。一旦房地产合同成立并可以具体执行，买方就获得衡平法上的所有权。","analysis":"当房地产合同没有提及损失风险时（如此处所示），衡平法转换原则将风险置于对财产享有衡平法所有权的一方身上。一旦合同成立，衡平法所有权就转移给买方，并且可以具体执行。如果不存在任何条件（如此处所示）或所有合同条件均已履行，则可以专门执行合同。因此，法院很可能做出对卖方有利的判决，因为当房屋被毁时，损失风险已经转移给买方。然而，如果该管辖区遵循《统一卖方和买方法》（少数派规则），则损失风险将由卖方承担，直到买方实际占有或获得财产所有权为止（选择 C）。 （选项A）合同一般按照其简单含义进行解释；然而，合同中的含糊之处可能会被解释为对起草者不利。但合同解释规则在这里无关紧要，因为合同没有提及损失风险和衡平转换（equitable conversion）控制原则。 （选择B）销售合同中没有任何条件（例如，买方在成交前获得融资），因此一旦卖方签署了承诺书，合同就具有约束力。因此，即使买方没有收到贷款承诺，买方也有义务购买该房产。\n\n教育目标：除非另有规定，衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则将损失风险置于拥有财产公平所有权的一方身上。一旦房地产合同成立并可以具体执行，买方就获得衡平法上的所有权。","conclusion":"答案 D：根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，损失风险在合同日期转移给买方。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-af603b8a5e6f8dad"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":184,"sourceQuestionPage":540,"sourceQuestionPages":[540],"sourceExplanationPages":[541],"sourceAnswerPages":[541],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-185","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Easements","question":"Pursuant to a city ordinance, a particular area was zoned for single-family residences. The ordinance allowed a licensed professional, upon acquiring a permit, to maintain an office within one of the residences for use in meeting with clients and other parties in connection with the pursuit of the individual's profession. A licensed psychiatrist purchased a residence within the area zoned for single-family residences. The psychiatrist properly applied for a permit from the zoning commission and established that she met the conditions for granting the permit, but she cannot prove that the denial of the permit would result in an unnecessary hardship to her. Can the zoning commission properly deny the psychiatrist a permit?","choices":{"A":"No, because she has a vested right in maintaining a home office in her residence.","B":"No, because she properly applied for and established her entitlement to the permit.","C":"Yes, because she cannot establish that the denial of the permit would result in an unnecessary hardship to her.","D":"Yes, because the granting of a permit is a matter within the discretion of the zoning commission."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A zoning ordinance is a governmental regulation regarding land use that, for example, limits the use of a particular area to single-family residences. The ordinance may include a special exception permitting a particular future use of property if a special-use permit is obtained. If a property owner complies with the procedures for obtaining a special-use permit and establishes entitlement to that permit (as the psychiatrist did here), the responsible government agency must issue the permit. Therefore, the zoning commission cannot properly deny the psychiatrist a permit.* If the property owner is denied a permit, procedural due process typically gives the owner the right to appeal the denial to a reviewing entity (eg, a board of zoning appeals). (Choice A) A vested right regarding a nonconforming use of property exists when, at the time a zoning ordinance takes effect, the property owner is using the property in a manner that violates the ordinance. Here, the psychiatrist did not have a home office when the ordinance was enacted, so she has no vested right in this use. (Choice C) Proof of unnecessary hardship is not needed to obtain a special-use permit. However, such proof is required to obtain a variance. Under a variance, a local government (often a zoning board) gives a landowner permission to use his/her property in a manner that is not permitted under the zoning ordinance—eg, the use falls outside of a special exception. (Choice D) The granting of a special-use permit is not subject to the discretion of the zoning commission when, as here, the property owner has established that the conditions for granting the permit have been met.\n\nEducational objective: A property owner must be granted a special-use permit if the property owner has complied with the procedures for obtaining the permit and has established entitlement to the permit.\n\nReferences\n83 Am. Jur. 2d Zoning and Planning § 814 (2020) (discussing special exceptions or uses).","educationalObjective":"A property owner must be granted a special-use permit if the property owner has complied with the procedures for obtaining the permit and has established entitlement to the permit.","references":"83 Am. Jur. 2d Zoning and Planning § 814 (2020) (discussing special exceptions or uses).","rule":"A property owner must be granted a special-use permit if the property owner has complied with the procedures for obtaining the permit and has established entitlement to the permit.","irac":{"issue":"Can the zoning commission properly deny the psychiatrist a permit?","rule":"A property owner must be granted a special-use permit if the property owner has complied with the procedures for obtaining the permit and has established entitlement to the permit.","analysis":"A zoning ordinance is a governmental regulation regarding land use that, for example, limits the use of a particular area to single-family residences. The ordinance may include a special exception permitting a particular future use of property if a special-use permit is obtained. If a property owner complies with the procedures for obtaining a special-use permit and establishes entitlement to that permit (as the psychiatrist did here), the responsible government agency must issue the permit. Therefore, the zoning commission cannot properly deny the psychiatrist a permit.* If the property owner is denied a permit, procedural due process typically gives the owner the right to appeal the denial to a reviewing entity (eg, a board of zoning appeals). (Choice A) A vested right regarding a nonconforming use of property exists when, at the time a zoning ordinance takes effect, the property owner is using the property in a manner that violates the ordinance. Here, the psychiatrist did not have a home office when the ordinance was enacted, so she has no vested right in this use. (Choice C) Proof of unnecessary hardship is not needed to obtain a special-use permit. However, such proof is required to obtain a variance. Under a variance, a local government (often a zoning board) gives a landowner permission to use his/her property in a manner that is not permitted under the zoning ordinance—eg, the use falls outside of a special exception. (Choice D) The granting of a special-use permit is not subject to the discretion of the zoning commission when, as here, the property owner has established that the conditions for granting the permit have been met.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because she properly applied for and established her entitlement to the permit."},"questionZh":"根据一项城市法令，特定区域被划为单户住宅。该法令允许持照专业人士在获得许可证后，在其中一处住宅内保留一间办公室，用于会见客户和其他与个人职业发展有关的各方。一名持有执照的精神科医生在单户住宅区购买了一套住宅。精神科医生向土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会正确申请了许可证，并证明她符合授予许可证的条件，但她无法证明拒绝许可证会给她带来不必要的困难。土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会可以适当地拒绝精神科医生的许可吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为她拥有在自己的住所保留家庭办公室的既定权利。","B":"不，因为她正确申请并确立了获得许可证的权利。","C":"是的，因为她无法确定拒绝许可会给她带来不必要的困难。","D":"是的，因为授予许可证是土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会酌情决定的事项。"},"explanationZh":"土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例是有关土地使用的政府法规，例如，将特定区域的使用限制为单户住宅。该法令可能包括一项特殊例外，如果获得特殊使用许可证，则允许未来对财产进行特定的使用。如果业主遵守获得特殊用途许可证的程序并确定了获得该许可证的权利（正如精神病医生在此处所做的那样），则负责的政府机构必须颁发该许可证。因此，土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会不能适当地拒绝向精神科医生发放许可证。*如果业主被拒绝发放许可证，程序性正当程序通常赋予业主向审查实体（例如土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）上诉委员会）提出上诉的权利。 （选项 A）当土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例生效时，业主正在以违反该条例的方式使用财产，则存在有关不合规使用财产的既定权利。在这里，该法令颁布时，精神科医生并没有家庭办公室，因此她没有这种用途的既定权利。 （选择 C） 获得特殊使用许可证不需要证明不必要的困难。然而，需要这样的证明才能获得差异。根据差异，地方政府（通常是土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会）允许土地所有者以土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例不允许的方式使用其财产，例如，该使用不属于特殊例外。 （选择 D）当业主已确定已满足授予许可证的条件时，特殊用途许可证的授予不受土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会的酌处权。\n\n教育目标：如果业主已遵守获得许可证的程序并已确定获得许可证的权利，则必须向业主授予特殊用途许可证。\n\n参考文献\n83 上午。尤尔。 2d 土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）和规划 § 814 (2020)（讨论特殊例外或用途）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果业主已履行获得许可证的程序并已确定获得许可证的权利，则必须向业主授予特殊用途许可证。","iracZh":{"issue":"土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会可以适当地拒绝精神科医生的许可吗？","rule":"如果业主已履行获得许可证的程序并已确定获得许可证的权利，则必须向业主授予特殊用途许可证。","analysis":"土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例是有关土地使用的政府法规，例如，将特定区域的使用限制为单户住宅。该法令可能包括一项特殊例外，如果获得特殊使用许可证，则允许未来对财产进行特定的使用。如果业主遵守获得特殊用途许可证的程序并确定了获得该许可证的权利（正如精神病医生在此处所做的那样），则负责的政府机构必须颁发该许可证。因此，土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会不能适当地拒绝向精神科医生发放许可证。*如果业主被拒绝发放许可证，程序性正当程序通常赋予业主向审查实体（例如土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）上诉委员会）提出上诉的权利。 （选项 A）当土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例生效时，业主正在以违反该条例的方式使用财产，则存在有关不合规使用财产的既定权利。在这里，该法令颁布时，精神科医生并没有家庭办公室，因此她没有这种用途的既定权利。 （选择 C） 获得特殊使用许可证不需要证明不必要的困难。然而，需要这样的证明才能获得差异。根据差异，地方政府（通常是土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会）允许土地所有者以土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例不允许的方式使用其财产，例如，该使用不属于特殊例外。 （选择 D）当业主已确定已满足授予许可证的条件时，特殊用途许可证的授予不受土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会的酌处权。\n\n教育目标：如果业主已遵守获得许可证的程序并已确定获得许可证的权利，则必须向业主授予特殊用途许可证。\n\n参考文献\n83 上午。尤尔。 2d 土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）和规划 § 814 (2020)（讨论特殊例外或用途）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为她正确申请并确立了获得许可证的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"special use permit","ipa":"/spˈɛʃəl jˈus pɝmˈɪt/","syllables":"spe·cial use per·mit","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"特别用途许可：条例预先允许但须个案审批的土地用途","contextualMeaning":"特别用途许可：条例预先允许但须个案审批的土地用途","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Pursuant to a city ordinance, a particular area was zoned for single-family residences.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":185,"sourceQuestionPage":542,"sourceQuestionPages":[542],"sourceExplanationPages":[543],"sourceAnswerPages":[543],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-186","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Two adjacent, two-story, commercial buildings were owned by a landlord. The first floors of both buildings were occupied by various retail establishments. The second floors were rented to various other tenants. Access to the second floor of each building was reached by a common stairway located entirely in Building 1. While the buildings were being used in this manner, the landlord sold Building 1 to a man by warranty deed that made no mention of any rights concerning the stairway. About two years later, the landlord sold Building 2 to a woman. The stairway continued to be used by the occupants of both buildings. The stairway became unsafe as a consequence of regular wear and tear. The woman entered the man's building and began the work of repairing the stairway. The man demanded that the woman discontinue the repair work and vacate the man's building. When the woman refused, the man brought an action to enjoin the woman from continuing the work. For which party should judgment be entered?","choices":{"A":"The man, because the woman has no rights in the stairway.","B":"The man, because the woman's rights in the stairway do not extend beyond the normal life of the existing structure.","C":"The woman, because she has a right to take whatever action is necessary to protect herself from possible tort liability to persons using the stairway.","D":"The woman, because she has an easement in the stairway and an implied right to keep the stairway in repair."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Implied easements\n\nType Elements\n\nNecessity Severance of common ownership Strict necessity for use & enjoyment of land Existing prior use Severance of common ownership Prior existing use Apparent & continuous Reasonable necessity for use & enjoyment of land An easement can be implied by an existing prior use when the following elements are met: Severance of common ownership – the dominant and servient estates were a single tract of land, and the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another\n\nPrior existing use – the owner used the servient estate to benefit the dominant estate (ie, a \"quasi-easement\")\n\nApparent and continuous – the prior use was discoverable by reasonable inspection and continued until the tract was severed\n\nReasonable necessity – continuance of the prior use is reasonably necessary to use the land\n\nHere, the landlord owned both buildings before selling Building 1 to the man and later selling Building 2 to the woman (severance). The stairway in Building 1 had been used to access the second floor of Building 2 before the buildings were sold (prior existing use) and continued to be used by occupants of both buildings after those sales (apparent and continuous). And since the continued use of the stairway is reasonably necessary to use the second floor of Building 2 (reasonable necessity), the woman has an implied easement in the stairway (Choice A). As the easement holder, the woman has the sole duty to maintain* the easement. Repairs must be made to avoid harm to the servient estate and to protect the servient owner (not the easement holder) from liability for harm to third parties (Choice C). As a result, the easement holder has the implied right to enter the servient estate to make necessary repairs—as the woman did here. Judgment should therefore be entered for her. *However, if the easement is used by the easement holder and the servient owner, then repair costs will be apportioned between the parties based on their relative use.\n\n(Choice B) An easement over a structure (eg, building) continues until that structure is destroyed by natural causes. And since Building 2 still exists, the woman's easement over the stairway remains in effect.\n\nEducational objective: An easement will be implied by prior use if (1) common ownership was severed, (2) the prior existing use was apparent and continued after severance, and (3) continuation of that use is reasonably necessary to use the land.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.12 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by prior use)","educationalObjective":"An easement will be implied by prior use if (1) common ownership was severed, (2) the prior existing use was apparent and continued after severance, and (3) continuation of that use is reasonably necessary to use the land.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.12 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by prior use)","rule":"An easement will be implied by prior use if (1) common ownership was severed, (2) the prior existing use was apparent and continued after severance, and (3) continuation of that use is reasonably necessary to use the land.","irac":{"issue":"For which party should judgment be entered?","rule":"An easement will be implied by prior use if (1) common ownership was severed, (2) the prior existing use was apparent and continued after severance, and (3) continuation of that use is reasonably necessary to use the land.","analysis":"Implied easements\n\nType Elements\n\nNecessity Severance of common ownership Strict necessity for use & enjoyment of land Existing prior use Severance of common ownership Prior existing use Apparent & continuous Reasonable necessity for use & enjoyment of land An easement can be implied by an existing prior use when the following elements are met: Severance of common ownership – the dominant and servient estates were a single tract of land, and the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another\n\nPrior existing use – the owner used the servient estate to benefit the dominant estate (ie, a \"quasi-easement\")\n\nApparent and continuous – the prior use was discoverable by reasonable inspection and continued until the tract was severed\n\nReasonable necessity – continuance of the prior use is reasonably necessary to use the land\n\nHere, the landlord owned both buildings before selling Building 1 to the man and later selling Building 2 to the woman (severance). The stairway in Building 1 had been used to access the second floor of Building 2 before the buildings were sold (prior existing use) and continued to be used by occupants of both buildings after those sales (apparent and continuous). And since the continued use of the stairway is reasonably necessary to use the second floor of Building 2 (reasonable necessity), the woman has an implied easement in the stairway (Choice A). As the easement holder, the woman has the sole duty to maintain* the easement. Repairs must be made to avoid harm to the servient estate and to protect the servient owner (not the easement holder) from liability for harm to third parties (Choice C). As a result, the easement holder has the implied right to enter the servient estate to make necessary repairs—as the woman did here. Judgment should therefore be entered for her. *However, if the easement is used by the easement holder and the servient owner, then repair costs will be apportioned between the parties based on their relative use.\n\n(Choice B) An easement over a structure (eg, building) continues until that structure is destroyed by natural causes. And since Building 2 still exists, the woman's easement over the stairway remains in effect.","conclusion":"Answer D: The woman, because she has an easement in the stairway and an implied right to keep the stairway in repair."},"questionZh":"两栋相邻的两层商业建筑属于一位房东。两栋大楼的一楼都设有各种零售店。二楼租给了其他不同的租户。通往每栋建筑二楼的通道均位于 1 号楼内的共用楼梯。在以这种方式使用这些建筑的同时，房东通过保修契约将 1 号楼卖给了一名男子，该契约没有提及与楼梯有关的任何权利。大约两年后，房东将2号楼卖给了一名妇女。两栋大楼的居住者继续使用楼梯。由于经常磨损，楼梯变得不安全。女人进了男人的楼，开始修楼梯的工作。男子要求女子停止维修工作并搬出男子的大楼。当女子拒绝后，男子提起诉讼，禁止女子继续工作。应由哪一方作出判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"男人，因为女人没有上楼梯的权利。","B":"男人，因为女人在楼梯上的权利不超出现有结构的正常生活范围。","C":"女人，因为她有权采取一切必要的行动来保护自己免受使用楼梯的人可能承担的侵权责任。","D":"女人，因为她拥有楼梯的地役权，并且拥有维护楼梯的默示权利。"},"explanationZh":"隐含地役权\n\n类型元素\n\n必要性 分割共同所有权 对使用和享有土地的严格必要性 现有在先使用 分割共同所有权 在先现有使用 明显且持续 对使用和享有土地的合理必要性 当满足以下要素时，现有在先使用可暗示地役权： 分割共同所有权 - 主要和从属地产是一块土地，所有者保留部分土地并将其余部分转让给另一方\n\n先前的现有用途 – 所有者使用役用财产来使支配性财产受益（即“准地役权”）\n\n明显且持续——通过合理检查可以发现先前的使用情况，并持续到该道被切断为止\n\n合理必要性——继续使用先前的用途是使用土地的合理必要性\n\n在这里，房东在将 1 号楼出售给该男子之前拥有两栋大楼，后来又将 2 号楼出售给该女子（遣散费）。 1号楼的楼梯在建筑物被出售之前（之前的现有用途）用于进入2号楼的二楼，并且在出售后继续被两栋建筑物的居住者使用（明显且持续）。并且由于继续使用楼梯是使用 2 号楼二楼的合理必要性（合理必要性），因此该妇女在楼梯上拥有隐含的地役权（选项 A）。作为地役权持有人，妇女有唯一的责任维持*地役权。必须进行维修以避免对受役财产造成损害，并保护受役所有人（而非地役权持有人）免受对第三方损害的责任（选择 C）。因此，地役权持有人拥有进入役用地产进行必要维修的隐含权利——就像这位女士在这里所做的那样。因此，应当对她作出判决。 *但是，如果地役权由地役权持有人和受役人使用，则维修费用将根据其相对用途在双方之间分摊。\n\n（选择B）建筑物（例如建筑物）的地役权持续到该建筑物因自然原因被毁坏为止。由于 2 号楼仍然存在，该女士对楼梯的地役权仍然有效。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 共同所有权被分割，(2) 先前的现有使用是明显的，并且在分割后继续存在，并且 (3) 继续使用对于使用土地来说是合理必要的，则先前使用将暗示地役权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.12 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过先前使用获得地役权）","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果 (1) 共同所有权被分割，(2) 先前现有的使用是明显的，并且在分割后继续存在，并且 (3) 继续使用该土地是使用土地的合理必要性，则先前的使用将暗示地役权。","iracZh":{"issue":"应由哪一方作出判决？","rule":"如果 (1) 共同所有权被分割，(2) 先前现有的使用是明显的，并且在分割后继续存在，并且 (3) 继续使用该土地是使用土地的合理必要性，则先前的使用将暗示地役权。","analysis":"隐含地役权\n\n类型元素\n\n必要性 分割共同所有权 对使用和享有土地的严格必要性 现有在先使用 分割共同所有权 在先现有使用 明显且持续 对使用和享有土地的合理必要性 当满足以下要素时，现有在先使用可暗示地役权： 分割共同所有权 - 主要和从属地产是一块土地，所有者保留部分土地并将其余部分转让给另一方\n\n先前的现有用途 – 所有者使用役用财产来使支配性财产受益（即“准地役权”）\n\n明显且持续——通过合理检查可以发现先前的使用情况，并持续到该道被切断为止\n\n合理必要性——继续使用先前的用途是使用土地的合理必要性\n\n在这里，房东在将 1 号楼出售给该男子之前拥有两栋大楼，后来又将 2 号楼出售给该女子（遣散费）。 1号楼的楼梯在建筑物被出售之前（之前的现有用途）用于进入2号楼的二楼，并且在出售后继续被两栋建筑物的居住者使用（明显且持续）。并且由于继续使用楼梯是使用 2 号楼二楼的合理必要性（合理必要性），因此该妇女在楼梯上拥有隐含的地役权（选项 A）。作为地役权持有人，妇女有唯一的责任维持*地役权。必须进行维修以避免对受役财产造成损害，并保护受役所有人（而非地役权持有人）免受对第三方损害的责任（选择 C）。因此，地役权持有人拥有进入役用地产进行必要维修的隐含权利——就像这位女士在这里所做的那样。因此，应当对她作出判决。 *但是，如果地役权由地役权持有人和受役人使用，则维修费用将根据其相对用途在双方之间分摊。\n\n（选择B）建筑物（例如建筑物）的地役权持续到该建筑物因自然原因被毁坏为止。由于 2 号楼仍然存在，该女士对楼梯的地役权仍然有效。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 共同所有权被分割，(2) 先前的现有使用是明显的，并且在分割后继续存在，并且 (3) 继续使用对于使用土地来说是合理必要的，则先前使用将暗示地役权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.12 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过先前使用获得地役权）","conclusion":"答案 D：女人，因为她拥有楼梯的地役权，并且拥有维护楼梯的默示权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"prior existing use","ipa":"/pɹˈaɪɝ ɪɡzˈɪstɪŋ jˈus/","syllables":"prior ex·ist·ing use","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"既存使用：统一土地分割前已有的明显持续土地使用安排","contextualMeaning":"既存使用：统一土地分割前已有的明显持续土地使用安排","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"An easement will be implied by prior use if (1) common ownership was severed, (2) the prior existing use was apparent and continued after severance, and (3) continuation of that use is reasonably necessary to use the land.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":186,"sourceQuestionPage":544,"sourceQuestionPages":[544],"sourceExplanationPages":[545,546],"sourceAnswerPages":[545,546],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-187","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"A farmer executed a document granting to a local gun club and its assignees the exclusive right to hunt geese to the extent permitted by law on a portion of his farm. The club recorded the document. Two years later the club disbanded, but before doing so, the club assigned its hunting rights on the farm to its 10 members, individually. The farmer denied the former members access to his farm to hunt geese. The former members filed a court action to compel the farmer to permit them access. If a court rules in favor of the former members, which of the following is it likely to apply in dividing this profit?","choices":{"A":"The cy pres doctrine.","B":"The shelter rule.","C":"The one-stock rule.","D":"The rule of convenience."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Profit\n\n(right to enter another's land & remove natural resources)\n\nCharacteristics Transferability Apportionability\n\nExclusive Unlimited & Can be transferred If transferable, can be exclusive right unless profit is divided unless contrary to personal or contrary parties' intent or burden on Nonexclusive Right limited by to parties' intent servient estate quantity, use, or unreasonably increased time OR shared with another\n\nThe gun club's right to hunt geese on the farm was a profit since it gave the club the right to enter another's land to remove natural resources. When a profit is apportioned or divided up among multiple transferees, the \"one stock\" rule applies. Under this rule, the transferees are limited to the amount of material taken by the transferor (ie, the transferor's \"stock\"), and this quantity is divided up by the transferees taking the profit. This ensures that the burden on the servient (burdened) estate is not unreasonably increased. Therefore, the court is likely to apply the \"one stock\" rule in dividing the former members' right to hunt geese on the farm. (Choice A) The cy pres doctrine permits a court to alter a legal instrument to come \"as close as possible\" to the creator's intent when the stated purpose cannot be accomplished due to impracticability or illegality. It is typically applied in the context of a charitable gift in a will or trust when the named beneficiary has ceased to exist and the donor has died. Here, the farmer is alive and challenging the former members' right to hunt on his farm. (Choice B) Under the shelter rule, grantors who are protected by a recording act protect (or \"shelter\") their grantees who would otherwise be unprotected from subsequent purchasers. Since profits are not subject to recording acts, the shelter rule does not apply here. (Choice D) The rule of convenience applies to a class gift and closes the class when any member of the class becomes eligible to take immediate possession of the property. This rule can prevent a future interest from violating the Rule Against Perpetuities. Here, the club transferred the profit to its members individually—not as a class—so this rule is inapplicable.\n\nEducational objective: The \"one stock\" rule applies to the division of a profit. Under this rule, the transferees are limited to the amount of material taken by the transferor (ie, his/her \"stock\"), and this quantity is divided up by the transferees taking the profit.\n\nReferences\nMiller v. Lutheran Conference & Camp Ass'n, 200 A. 646, 651–52 (Pa. Super. Ct. 1938) (explaining that the one-stock rule applies to the division of profits or easements in gross).","educationalObjective":"The \"one stock\" rule applies to the division of a profit. Under this rule, the transferees are limited to the amount of material taken by the transferor (ie, his/her \"stock\"), and this quantity is divided up by the transferees taking the profit.","references":"Miller v. Lutheran Conference & Camp Ass'n, 200 A. 646, 651–52 (Pa. Super. Ct. 1938) (explaining that the one-stock rule applies to the division of profits or easements in gross).","rule":"The \"one stock\" rule applies to the division of a profit. Under this rule, the transferees are limited to the amount of material taken by the transferor (ie, his/her \"stock\"), and this quantity is divided up by the transferees taking the profit.","irac":{"issue":"If a court rules in favor of the former members, which of the following is it likely to apply in dividing this profit?","rule":"The \"one stock\" rule applies to the division of a profit. Under this rule, the transferees are limited to the amount of material taken by the transferor (ie, his/her \"stock\"), and this quantity is divided up by the transferees taking the profit.","analysis":"Profit\n\n(right to enter another's land & remove natural resources)\n\nCharacteristics Transferability Apportionability\n\nExclusive Unlimited & Can be transferred If transferable, can be exclusive right unless profit is divided unless contrary to personal or contrary parties' intent or burden on Nonexclusive Right limited by to parties' intent servient estate quantity, use, or unreasonably increased time OR shared with another\n\nThe gun club's right to hunt geese on the farm was a profit since it gave the club the right to enter another's land to remove natural resources. When a profit is apportioned or divided up among multiple transferees, the \"one stock\" rule applies. Under this rule, the transferees are limited to the amount of material taken by the transferor (ie, the transferor's \"stock\"), and this quantity is divided up by the transferees taking the profit. This ensures that the burden on the servient (burdened) estate is not unreasonably increased. Therefore, the court is likely to apply the \"one stock\" rule in dividing the former members' right to hunt geese on the farm. (Choice A) The cy pres doctrine permits a court to alter a legal instrument to come \"as close as possible\" to the creator's intent when the stated purpose cannot be accomplished due to impracticability or illegality. It is typically applied in the context of a charitable gift in a will or trust when the named beneficiary has ceased to exist and the donor has died. Here, the farmer is alive and challenging the former members' right to hunt on his farm. (Choice B) Under the shelter rule, grantors who are protected by a recording act protect (or \"shelter\") their grantees who would otherwise be unprotected from subsequent purchasers. Since profits are not subject to recording acts, the shelter rule does not apply here. (Choice D) The rule of convenience applies to a class gift and closes the class when any member of the class becomes eligible to take immediate possession of the property. This rule can prevent a future interest from violating the Rule Against Perpetuities. Here, the club transferred the profit to its members individually—not as a class—so this rule is inapplicable.","conclusion":"Answer C: The one-stock rule."},"questionZh":"一名农民签署了一份文件，授予当地枪支俱乐部及其受让人在法律允许的范围内在其农场的一部分猎鹅的专有权。俱乐部记录了该文件。两年后，俱乐部解散，但在此之前，俱乐部将农场的狩猎权分别分配给了 10 名会员。农场主拒绝前成员进入他的农场捕鹅。前成员向法院提起诉讼，迫使农民允许他们进入。如果法院作出有利于前成员的裁决，则以下哪项可能适用于分配该利润？","choicesZh":{"A":"赛普斯主义。","B":"庇护规则。","C":"单一股票规则。","D":"方便法则。"},"explanationZh":"利润\n\n（进入他人土地并清除自然资源的权利）\n\n特性 可转移性 可分配性\n\n排他性 无限制且可转让 如果可转让，则可以是排他性权利，除非利润被分割，除非违反个人或相对方的意图或对非排他性权利的负担，受各方意向的受役财产数量、使用或不合理增加时间或与他人共享的限制\n\n枪支俱乐部在农场猎鹅的权利是一种利润，因为它赋予了俱乐部进入他人土地以获取自然资源的权利。当利润分配或分配给多个受让人时，适用“一股”规则。根据这一规则，受让者的数量仅限于让与者取得的材料（即让与者的“股票”）的数量，并且该数量除以受让者获取的利润。这确保了奴役（负担）财产的负担不会不合理地增加。因此，法院很可能适用“一股”规则来分割前成员在农场捕鹅的权利。 （选项 A） 半近似原则允许法院在由于不切实际或非法而无法实现所声明的目的时，更改法律文书以“尽可能接近”创作者的意图。它通常适用于遗嘱或信托中的慈善捐赠，当指定的受益人已不复存在且捐赠人已去世时。在这里，农民还活着，并挑战前成员在他的农场狩猎的权利。 （选择B）根据庇护规则，受记录法保护的授予人保护（或“庇护”）其受赠人，否则这些受赠人将无法受到后续购买者的保护。由于利润不受记录行为的约束，因此庇护规则在此不适用。 （选项 D） 便利规则适用于集体赠与，并在集体中的任何成员有资格立即占有该财产时结束集体。该规则可以防止未来权益违反永续规则。在这里，俱乐部将利润单独转移给其会员，而不是作为一个班级，因此这条规则不适用。\n\n教育目标：“一股”规则适用于利润分配。根据这一规则，受让者的数量仅限于让与者获取的材料（即他/她的“股票”）的数量，并且该数量除以受让者获取的利润。\n\n参考文献\nMiller v. Lutheran Conference & Camp Ass'n, 200 A. 646, 651–52 (Pa. Super. Ct. 1938)（解释说单一股票规则适用于利润或地役权的总分配）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"“一股”规则适用于利润分配。根据这一规则，受让者的数量仅限于让与者获取的材料（即他/她的“股票”）的数量，并且该数量除以受让者获取的利润。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院作出有利于前成员的裁决，则以下哪项可能适用于分配该利润？","rule":"“一股”规则适用于利润分配。根据这一规则，受让者的数量仅限于让与者获取的材料（即他/她的“股票”）的数量，并且该数量除以受让者获取的利润。","analysis":"利润\n\n（进入他人土地并清除自然资源的权利）\n\n特性 可转移性 可分配性\n\n排他性 无限制且可转让 如果可转让，则可以是排他性权利，除非利润被分割，除非违反个人或相对方的意图或对非排他性权利的负担，受各方意向的受役财产数量、使用或不合理增加时间或与他人共享的限制\n\n枪支俱乐部在农场猎鹅的权利是一种利润，因为它赋予了俱乐部进入他人土地以获取自然资源的权利。当利润分配或分配给多个受让人时，适用“一股”规则。根据这一规则，受让者的数量仅限于让与者取得的材料（即让与者的“股票”）的数量，并且该数量除以受让者获取的利润。这确保了奴役（负担）财产的负担不会不合理地增加。因此，法院很可能适用“一股”规则来分割前成员在农场捕鹅的权利。 （选项 A） 半近似原则允许法院在由于不切实际或非法而无法实现所声明的目的时，更改法律文书以“尽可能接近”创作者的意图。它通常适用于遗嘱或信托中的慈善捐赠，当指定的受益人已不复存在且捐赠人已去世时。在这里，农民还活着，并挑战前成员在他的农场狩猎的权利。 （选择B）根据庇护规则，受记录法保护的授予人保护（或“庇护”）其受赠人，否则这些受赠人将无法受到后续购买者的保护。由于利润不受记录行为的约束，因此庇护规则在此不适用。 （选项 D） 便利规则适用于集体赠与，并在集体中的任何成员有资格立即占有该财产时结束集体。该规则可以防止未来权益违反永续规则。在这里，俱乐部将利润单独转移给其会员，而不是作为一个班级，因此这条规则不适用。\n\n教育目标：“一股”规则适用于利润分配。根据这一规则，受让者的数量仅限于让与者获取的材料（即他/她的“股票”）的数量，并且该数量除以受让者获取的利润。\n\n参考文献\nMiller v. Lutheran Conference & Camp Ass'n, 200 A. 646, 651–52 (Pa. Super. Ct. 1938)（解释说单一股票规则适用于利润或地役权的总分配）。","conclusion":"答案 C：单一股票规则。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":187,"sourceQuestionPage":547,"sourceQuestionPages":[547],"sourceExplanationPages":[548,549],"sourceAnswerPages":[548,549],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-188","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A landlord orally leased a commercial building to a tenant for a 10-year term, which ended one year ago. At the beginning of the lease term, the tenant installed numerous appliances in the building as well as a large air-conditioning system. The tenant alone paid for these items; they were installed with the landlord's permission and were used for the tenant's commercial enterprise. At the end of the lease term, the tenant failed to vacate the premises in a timely manner and continued to pay rent while remaining in possession for another four months. Before vacating the building at the end of the fourth month, the tenant removed the appliances and the air-conditioning system that he had installed, causing no damage to the building. Was the removal by the tenant proper?","choices":{"A":"No, because he was a holdover tenant.","B":"No, because the lease was oral.","C":"Yes, because they were accessions.","D":"Yes, because they were trade fixtures."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A tenant can remove trade fixtures—items the tenant attached to real property for use in his/her trade or business—without the landlord's consent if: the removal will not substantially harm the property (eg, will not degrade the building's structural integrity) and\n\nthe items are removed before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease terminates.\n\nSince a periodic tenancy has no defined end date, it continues until either the landlord or tenant gives express notice to terminate it. This tenancy can be created by agreement (eg, a written lease) or by implication (eg, when the tenant continues to pay, and the landlord continues to accept, rent after a prior lease has ended). Here, the appliances and air-conditioning system are trade fixtures since the tenant installed them for use in his commercial enterprise. And though the tenant's oral 10-year lease ended one year ago, he then initiated a periodic tenancy by continuing to use the premises and pay rent for another four months. And since the tenant removed the trade fixtures before the end of the fourth month (before the tenancy terminated) and caused no damage to the building, the removal was proper. (Choice A) Although he was a holdover tenant—a tenant who entered a valid lease and then stayed past the end of the lease term—he became a periodic tenant when he kept possession of the premises and continued to pay rent. Therefore, he had the right to remove the trade fixtures before the periodic tenancy ended. (Choice B) Since the 10-year lease was oral and could not be performed within a year, it was unenforceable under the statute of frauds. But the landlord and tenant then formed a periodic tenancy that (1) was not subject to the statute of frauds and (2) was still in effect when the tenant removed the trade fixtures. Therefore, the removal was proper (regardless of the prior oral lease). (Choice C) Accessions are items that are so attached to other property that they cannot be removed without causing substantial damage. Here, the appliances and air-conditioning system were not accessions since they were removed without damaging the building. But had they been accessions, removal would have been improper.\n\nEducational objective: Trade fixtures—items attached to real property by a tenant for use in his/her trade or business—can be removed without the landlord's consent if (1) the removal occurs before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease ends and (2) the removal will not substantially harm the property.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (periodic tenancy).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant §§ 12.2–12.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (restoration after removal of trade fixtures).","educationalObjective":"Trade fixtures—items attached to real property by a tenant for use in his/her trade or business—can be removed without the landlord's consent if (1) the removal occurs before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease ends and (2) the removal will not substantially harm the property.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (periodic tenancy).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant §§ 12.2–12.3 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (restoration after removal of trade fixtures).","rule":"Trade fixtures—items attached to real property by a tenant for use in his/her trade or business—can be removed without the landlord's consent if (1) the removal occurs before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease ends and (2) the removal will not substantially harm the property.","irac":{"issue":"Was the removal by the tenant proper?","rule":"Trade fixtures—items attached to real property by a tenant for use in his/her trade or business—can be removed without the landlord's consent if (1) the removal occurs before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease ends and (2) the removal will not substantially harm the property.","analysis":"A tenant can remove trade fixtures—items the tenant attached to real property for use in his/her trade or business—without the landlord's consent if: the removal will not substantially harm the property (eg, will not degrade the building's structural integrity) and\n\nthe items are removed before, or within a reasonable time after, the lease terminates.\n\nSince a periodic tenancy has no defined end date, it continues until either the landlord or tenant gives express notice to terminate it. This tenancy can be created by agreement (eg, a written lease) or by implication (eg, when the tenant continues to pay, and the landlord continues to accept, rent after a prior lease has ended). Here, the appliances and air-conditioning system are trade fixtures since the tenant installed them for use in his commercial enterprise. And though the tenant's oral 10-year lease ended one year ago, he then initiated a periodic tenancy by continuing to use the premises and pay rent for another four months. And since the tenant removed the trade fixtures before the end of the fourth month (before the tenancy terminated) and caused no damage to the building, the removal was proper. (Choice A) Although he was a holdover tenant—a tenant who entered a valid lease and then stayed past the end of the lease term—he became a periodic tenant when he kept possession of the premises and continued to pay rent. Therefore, he had the right to remove the trade fixtures before the periodic tenancy ended. (Choice B) Since the 10-year lease was oral and could not be performed within a year, it was unenforceable under the statute of frauds. But the landlord and tenant then formed a periodic tenancy that (1) was not subject to the statute of frauds and (2) was still in effect when the tenant removed the trade fixtures. Therefore, the removal was proper (regardless of the prior oral lease). (Choice C) Accessions are items that are so attached to other property that they cannot be removed without causing substantial damage. Here, the appliances and air-conditioning system were not accessions since they were removed without damaging the building. But had they been accessions, removal would have been improper.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because they were trade fixtures."},"questionZh":"一名房东口头将一栋商业建筑出租给一名租户，租期为 10 年，租期已于一年前到期。在租赁期开始时，租户在建筑物内安装了许多电器以及大型空调系统。租户独自支付这些物品的费用；它们是经房东许可安装的，用于租户的商业企业。租赁期满后，承租人未能及时腾出房屋，并继续支付租金，并继续占有四个月。第四月底搬出大楼前，租户拆除了自己安装的电器和空调系统，没有对大楼造成任何损坏。租客的搬迁是否妥当？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为他是滞留租户。","B":"不，因为租约是口头的。","C":"是的，因为它们是添加品。","D":"是的，因为它们是贸易固定装置。"},"explanationZh":"租户可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除商业固定装置（租户附着在不动产上用于其贸易或业务的物品），如果： 拆除不会对财产造成重大损害（例如，不会降低建筑物的结构完整性）并且\n\n这些物品在租赁终止之前或租赁终止后的合理时间内被移除。\n\n由于定期租赁没有明确的结束日期，因此它会一直持续到房东或租户明确通知终止为止。这种租赁可以通过协议（例如，书面租约）或暗示（例如，当租户在先前的租约结束后继续支付租金并且房东继续接受租金时）建立。在这里，电器和空调系统是商业固定装置，因为租户安装它们是为了在他的商业企业中使用。尽管租户的十年期口头租约在一年前结束，但他随后开始定期租赁，继续使用该房屋并支付另外四个月的租金。由于承租人在第四个月末（租约终止前）之前拆除了商业装置，并且没有对建筑物造成任何损坏，因此拆除是适当的。 （选项 A）虽然他是滞留租户（即签订有效租约并在租期结束后继续居住的租户），但当他继续占有该房屋并继续支付租金时，他就成为定期租户。因此，他有权在定期租约结束前取消交易装置。 （选项B）由于10年租约是口头的，并且无法在一年内履行，因此根据欺诈法，该租约无法执行。但房东和租户随后形成了定期租赁，该租赁（1）不受欺诈法的约束，并且（2）在租户拆除商业固定装置时仍然有效。因此，拆除是适当的（无论之前的口头租赁如何）。 （选项 C） 附属物是指与其他财产紧密相连的物品，以至于在不造成重大损害的情况下无法将其移除。在这里，电器和空调系统不是附属物，因为它们被拆除时没有损坏建筑物。但如果它们是添加物，移除就不合适了。\n\n教育目标：贸易固定装置（租户附着在不动产上用于其贸易或业务的物品）可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除，如果 (1) 拆除发生在租约结束之前或租赁结束后的合理时间内，并且 (2) 拆除不会对财产造成实质性损害。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户 § 1.5（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（定期租赁）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 12.2–12.3 条（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（拆除贸易设施后恢复）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商业固定装置（租户在其贸易或业务中使用而附着在不动产上的物品）可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除，如果 (1) 拆除发生在租约结束之前或租赁结束后的合理时间内，并且 (2) 拆除不会对财产造成实质性损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"租客的搬迁是否妥当？","rule":"商业固定装置（租户在其贸易或业务中使用而附着在不动产上的物品）可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除，如果 (1) 拆除发生在租约结束之前或租赁结束后的合理时间内，并且 (2) 拆除不会对财产造成实质性损害。","analysis":"租户可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除商业固定装置（租户附着在不动产上用于其贸易或业务的物品），如果： 拆除不会对财产造成重大损害（例如，不会降低建筑物的结构完整性）并且\n\n这些物品在租赁终止之前或租赁终止后的合理时间内被移除。\n\n由于定期租赁没有明确的结束日期，因此它会一直持续到房东或租户明确通知终止为止。这种租赁可以通过协议（例如，书面租约）或暗示（例如，当租户在先前的租约结束后继续支付租金并且房东继续接受租金时）建立。在这里，电器和空调系统是商业固定装置，因为租户安装它们是为了在他的商业企业中使用。尽管租户的十年期口头租约在一年前结束，但他随后开始定期租赁，继续使用该房屋并支付另外四个月的租金。由于承租人在第四个月末（租约终止前）之前拆除了商业装置，并且没有对建筑物造成任何损坏，因此拆除是适当的。 （选项 A）虽然他是滞留租户（即签订有效租约并在租期结束后继续居住的租户），但当他继续占有该房屋并继续支付租金时，他就成为定期租户。因此，他有权在定期租约结束前取消交易装置。 （选项B）由于10年租约是口头的，并且无法在一年内履行，因此根据欺诈法，该租约无法执行。但房东和租户随后形成了定期租赁，该租赁（1）不受欺诈法的约束，并且（2）在租户拆除商业固定装置时仍然有效。因此，拆除是适当的（无论之前的口头租赁如何）。 （选项 C） 附属物是指与其他财产紧密相连的物品，以至于在不造成重大损害的情况下无法将其移除。在这里，电器和空调系统不是附属物，因为它们被拆除时没有损坏建筑物。但如果它们是添加物，移除就不合适了。\n\n教育目标：贸易固定装置（租户附着在不动产上用于其贸易或业务的物品）可以在未经房东同意的情况下拆除，如果 (1) 拆除发生在租约结束之前或租赁结束后的合理时间内，并且 (2) 拆除不会对财产造成实质性损害。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户 § 1.5（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（定期租赁）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东与租户第 12.2–12.3 条（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（拆除贸易设施后恢复）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为它们是贸易固定装置。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-859946d55bc40c90"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":188,"sourceQuestionPage":550,"sourceQuestionPages":[550],"sourceExplanationPages":[551,552],"sourceAnswerPages":[551,552],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-189","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"In a written and signed agreement, a woman, for a fee, granted her neighbor the right to access his property by way of a driveway he was to construct across her property. The neighbor, who could access his property via other reasonable means, had sought the access right because it was more convenient. The neighbor did not record the agreement. Due to financial concerns, the neighbor did not construct the driveway, but he intended to do so in the future. Six months later, the woman sold her property to a buyer. The buyer did not record her deed from the woman. The buyer had no knowledge of the neighbor's right of access across the property she had purchased. Yesterday, a year after the sale, the neighbor informed the buyer that he plans to construct the driveway next week. The jurisdiction has a notice recording act. If the buyer sues to prevent the neighbor from constructing the driveway, will the buyer be successful?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer failed to record her deed from the woman.","B":"No, because the neighbor's agreement with the woman created an express easement, which cannot be terminated by mere nonuse.","C":"Yes, because the buyer lacked notice of the neighbor's right to construct a driveway on her property.","D":"Yes, because the neighbor failed to construct the driveway on the property before the woman sold the property."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Types of recording acts\n\nType Effect Statutory language\n\nRace Interest holder who records first will prevail \"First record\"\n\nNotice Subsequent BFP without notice of earlier property \"Without notice,\" \"In interest will prevail good faith\"\n\nRace- Subsequent BFP (1) without notice of earlier \"Without notice, who Notice property interest (2) who records first will prevail shall first record\"\n\nBFP = Bona fide purchaser.\n\nAn easement is a nonpossessory interest in real property that gives the easement holder the right to use another's land for a specific, limited purpose—eg, the neighbor's right to construct a driveway across the woman's property. As real property interests, easements are subject to the jurisdiction's recording act. Under a notice recording act (as seen here), a real property interest cannot be enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting real property interest who lacks notice (actual, record, or inquiry) of the prior interest. Here, the buyer had no knowledge of the neighbor's easement when she purchased the property (no actual notice). Additionally, the neighbor had neither recorded the agreement in which the woman granted him the easement (no record notice) nor constructed the driveway (no inquiry notice) at that time. Therefore, the neighbor cannot enforce his easement against the buyer, and she can prevent him from constructing a driveway across her property. (Choice A) A purchaser who is protected by a recording act (eg, the buyer) does not have to record his/her property interest to enjoy the act's protection from a person who had previously acquired a conflicting interest in the same property (eg, the neighbor). (Choice B) An easement is terminated by abandonment if the easement holder acts in an affirmative way that shows a clear intent to relinquish the easement right—mere nonuse is insufficient. Therefore, the neighbor's express easement was not terminated by his mere failure to construct the driveway. But that easement is unenforceable because the buyer is protected by the notice recording act. (Choice D) The neighbor's failure to construct a driveway on the property before the woman sold it helps show that the buyer lacked inquiry notice of the neighbor's easement. But the buyer will only be successful because she also lacked actual and record notice.\n\nEducational objective: Under a notice recording act, a real property interest (eg, easement) cannot be enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting real property interest (eg, the land subject to the easement) who lacks notice of the prior interest.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property § 7.14 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (extinguishment of servitude benefits under recording act).","educationalObjective":"Under a notice recording act, a real property interest (eg, easement) cannot be enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting real property interest (eg, the land subject to the easement) who lacks notice of the prior interest.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property § 7.14 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (extinguishment of servitude benefits under recording act).","rule":"Under a notice recording act, a real property interest (eg, easement) cannot be enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting real property interest (eg, the land subject to the easement) who lacks notice of the prior interest.","irac":{"issue":"If the buyer sues to prevent the neighbor from constructing the driveway, will the buyer be successful?","rule":"Under a notice recording act, a real property interest (eg, easement) cannot be enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting real property interest (eg, the land subject to the easement) who lacks notice of the prior interest.","analysis":"Types of recording acts\n\nType Effect Statutory language\n\nRace Interest holder who records first will prevail \"First record\"\n\nNotice Subsequent BFP without notice of earlier property \"Without notice,\" \"In interest will prevail good faith\"\n\nRace- Subsequent BFP (1) without notice of earlier \"Without notice, who Notice property interest (2) who records first will prevail shall first record\"\n\nBFP = Bona fide purchaser.\n\nAn easement is a nonpossessory interest in real property that gives the easement holder the right to use another's land for a specific, limited purpose—eg, the neighbor's right to construct a driveway across the woman's property. As real property interests, easements are subject to the jurisdiction's recording act. Under a notice recording act (as seen here), a real property interest cannot be enforced against a subsequent purchaser of a conflicting real property interest who lacks notice (actual, record, or inquiry) of the prior interest. Here, the buyer had no knowledge of the neighbor's easement when she purchased the property (no actual notice). Additionally, the neighbor had neither recorded the agreement in which the woman granted him the easement (no record notice) nor constructed the driveway (no inquiry notice) at that time. Therefore, the neighbor cannot enforce his easement against the buyer, and she can prevent him from constructing a driveway across her property. (Choice A) A purchaser who is protected by a recording act (eg, the buyer) does not have to record his/her property interest to enjoy the act's protection from a person who had previously acquired a conflicting interest in the same property (eg, the neighbor). (Choice B) An easement is terminated by abandonment if the easement holder acts in an affirmative way that shows a clear intent to relinquish the easement right—mere nonuse is insufficient. Therefore, the neighbor's express easement was not terminated by his mere failure to construct the driveway. But that easement is unenforceable because the buyer is protected by the notice recording act. (Choice D) The neighbor's failure to construct a driveway on the property before the woman sold it helps show that the buyer lacked inquiry notice of the neighbor's easement. But the buyer will only be successful because she also lacked actual and record notice.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the buyer lacked notice of the neighbor's right to construct a driveway on her property."},"questionZh":"在一份书面并签署的协议中，一名妇女在付费的情况下，授予她的邻居通过他在她的财产上建造的车道进入他的财产的权利。邻居可以通过其他合理的方式进入他的财产，他寻求进入权，因为这样更方便。邻居没有记录协议。由于经济原因，邻居没有修建车道，但他打算将来这样做。六个月后，这名妇女将她的财产卖给了一位买家。买家没有记录该女子的行为。买家不知道邻居有权进入她所购买的房产。昨天，在出售一年后，邻居通知买家，他计划下周建造车道。该司法管辖区有通知记录法。如果买家起诉阻止邻居修建车道，买家会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为买家没有记录该女子的行为。","B":"不可以，因为邻居与该妇女的协议产生了明确的地役权，不能仅因不使用而终止该地役权。","C":"是的，因为买家没有注意到邻居有权在她的房产上修建车道。","D":"是的，因为在该妇女出售房产之前，邻居未能在该房产上修建车道。"},"explanationZh":"录音行为的类型\n\n类型 效果 法定语言\n\n最先记录的比赛兴趣持有人将优先“第一记录”\n\n通知后续 BFP，无需通知先前的财产“无需通知”，“利益优先，诚信为本”\n\n竞赛-后续BFP（1）无通知前“无通知，谁通知财产利益（2）谁先记录为准，以先记录为准”\n\nBFP = 善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）。\n\n地役权是不动产的非占有性权益，它赋予地役权持有人将他人的土地用于特定的、有限的目的的权利，例如，邻居有权在妇女的财产上修建一条车道。作为不动产权益，地役权须遵守司法管辖区的记录法。根据通知记录法（如此处所示），如果后续购买了冲突的不动产权益，而该购买者缺乏对先前权益的通知（实际、记录或查询），则不能对不动产权益强制执行。在这里，买家在购买房产时并不知道邻居的地役权（没有实际通知）。此外，邻居当时既没有记录该女子授予他地役权的协议（没有记录通知），也没有建造车道（没有询问通知）。因此，邻居不能对买方强制执行地役权，而且她可以阻止他在她的财产上修建一条车道。 （选择A）受记录法保护的购买者（例如，买方）不必记录他/她的财产权益，即可享受该法的保护，免受先前在同一财产中获得冲突利益的人（例如，邻居）的影响。 （选择 B）如果地役权持有人以明确的方式明确表示放弃地役权，仅不使用是不够的，则地役权因放弃而终止。因此，邻居的明示地役权并不会因为他未能修建车道而终止。但该地役权无法执行，因为买方受到通知记录法的保护。 （选项D）在该妇女出售该房产之前，邻居未能在该房产上修建车道，这有助于表明买方缺乏对邻居地役权的询问通知。但买家之所以会成功，是因为她也缺乏实际和记录的通知。\n\n教育目标：根据通知记录法，如果后续购买者购买了冲突的不动产权益（例如，受地役权约束的土地），而该购买者没有事先通知该权益，则不能强制执行该不动产权益（例如，地役权）。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三）§ 7.14（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（根据记录法消灭劳役福利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据通知记录法，如果后续购买了冲突的不动产权益（例如，受地役权约束的土地），但未通知先前的权益，则不能对不动产权益（例如，地役权）强制执行。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果买家起诉阻止邻居修建车道，买家会胜诉吗？","rule":"根据通知记录法，如果后续购买了冲突的不动产权益（例如，受地役权约束的土地），但未通知先前的权益，则不能对不动产权益（例如，地役权）强制执行。","analysis":"录音行为的类型\n\n类型 效果 法定语言\n\n最先记录的比赛兴趣持有人将优先“第一记录”\n\n通知后续 BFP，无需通知先前的财产“无需通知”，“利益优先，诚信为本”\n\n竞赛-后续BFP（1）无通知前“无通知，谁通知财产利益（2）谁先记录为准，以先记录为准”\n\nBFP = 善意有偿购买人（bona fide purchaser）。\n\n地役权是不动产的非占有性权益，它赋予地役权持有人将他人的土地用于特定的、有限的目的的权利，例如，邻居有权在妇女的财产上修建一条车道。作为不动产权益，地役权须遵守司法管辖区的记录法。根据通知记录法（如此处所示），如果后续购买了冲突的不动产权益，而该购买者缺乏对先前权益的通知（实际、记录或查询），则不能对不动产权益强制执行。在这里，买家在购买房产时并不知道邻居的地役权（没有实际通知）。此外，邻居当时既没有记录该女子授予他地役权的协议（没有记录通知），也没有建造车道（没有询问通知）。因此，邻居不能对买方强制执行地役权，而且她可以阻止他在她的财产上修建一条车道。 （选择A）受记录法保护的购买者（例如，买方）不必记录他/她的财产权益，即可享受该法的保护，免受先前在同一财产中获得冲突利益的人（例如，邻居）的影响。 （选择 B）如果地役权持有人以明确的方式明确表示放弃地役权，仅不使用是不够的，则地役权因放弃而终止。因此，邻居的明示地役权并不会因为他未能修建车道而终止。但该地役权无法执行，因为买方受到通知记录法的保护。 （选项D）在该妇女出售该房产之前，邻居未能在该房产上修建车道，这有助于表明买方缺乏对邻居地役权的询问通知。但买家之所以会成功，是因为她也缺乏实际和记录的通知。\n\n教育目标：根据通知记录法，如果后续购买者购买了冲突的不动产权益（例如，受地役权约束的土地），而该购买者没有事先通知该权益，则不能强制执行该不动产权益（例如，地役权）。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三）§ 7.14（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（根据记录法消灭劳役福利）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为买家没有注意到邻居有权在她的房产上修建车道。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":189,"sourceQuestionPage":553,"sourceQuestionPages":[553],"sourceExplanationPages":[554,555],"sourceAnswerPages":[554,555],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-190","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A seller entered into a written contract with a purchaser to sell a farm. The contract was dated June 19 and called for a closing date on August 19. There was no other provision in the contract concerning the closing date. The contract contained the following clause: \"subject to the purchaser obtaining a satisfactory mortgage at the current rate.\" On the date provided for closing, the buyer advised the seller that he was unable to close because his mortgage application was still being processed by a bank. The seller desired to declare the contract at an end and consulted his attorney in regard to his legal position. Which of the following are relevant in advising the seller of his legal position?","choices":{"A":"Specific performance.","B":"Statute of frauds.","C":"Time is of the essence.","D":"All of the above."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under the statute of frauds, real estate contracts are enforceable only if they are in writing, are signed by the party to be charged, and contain all essential terms. As a result, this statute is relevant in advising the seller of his ability to enforce this contract for the sale and purchase of the farm (Choice B). Real estate contracts need not include a closing date. However, a closing date will be strictly enforced when the contract includes express language that makes time of the essence or the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be. Therefore, the buyer would have materially breached the contract by failing to obtain a mortgage loan from the bank by August 19 if this closing date is found to be a strict deadline (Choice C). When a buyer has breached a contract, one of the seller's remedies includes specific performance. This allows the seller to seek an injunction ordering the buyer to pay the agreed purchase price in lieu of monetary damages. Since it is important to inform a client of all available remedies, specific performance is also relevant in advising the seller of his legal position (Choice A).\n\nEducational objective: Real estate contracts must comply with the statute of frauds—ie, be in writing, be signed by the party to be charged, and contain all essential terms. A closing date is not an essential term, but it will be strictly enforced if the contract makes time of the essence or the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be.\n\nReferences\n73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 445 (2020) (seller's remedies).","educationalObjective":"Real estate contracts must comply with the statute of frauds—ie, be in writing, be signed by the party to be charged, and contain all essential terms. A closing date is not an essential term, but it will be strictly enforced if the contract makes time of the essence or the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be.","references":"73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 445 (2020) (seller's remedies).","rule":"Real estate contracts must comply with the statute of frauds—ie, be in writing, be signed by the party to be charged, and contain all essential terms. A closing date is not an essential term, but it will be strictly enforced if the contract makes time of the essence or the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following are relevant in advising the seller of his legal position?","rule":"Real estate contracts must comply with the statute of frauds—ie, be in writing, be signed by the party to be charged, and contain all essential terms. A closing date is not an essential term, but it will be strictly enforced if the contract makes time of the essence or the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be.","analysis":"Under the statute of frauds, real estate contracts are enforceable only if they are in writing, are signed by the party to be charged, and contain all essential terms. As a result, this statute is relevant in advising the seller of his ability to enforce this contract for the sale and purchase of the farm (Choice B). Real estate contracts need not include a closing date. However, a closing date will be strictly enforced when the contract includes express language that makes time of the essence or the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be. Therefore, the buyer would have materially breached the contract by failing to obtain a mortgage loan from the bank by August 19 if this closing date is found to be a strict deadline (Choice C). When a buyer has breached a contract, one of the seller's remedies includes specific performance. This allows the seller to seek an injunction ordering the buyer to pay the agreed purchase price in lieu of monetary damages. Since it is important to inform a client of all available remedies, specific performance is also relevant in advising the seller of his legal position (Choice A).","conclusion":"Answer D: All of the above."},"questionZh":"卖方与买方签订了出售农场的书面合同。合同日期为6月19日，并要求截止日期为8月19日。合同中没有关于截止日期的其他条款。合同包含以下条款：“以买方按当前利率获得满意的抵押贷款为准。”在规定的交割日期，买方告知卖方他无法交割，因为银行仍在处理他的抵押贷款申请。卖方希望宣布合同终止，并就其法律地位咨询了他的律师。以下哪项与向卖方告知其法律地位有关？","choicesZh":{"A":"具体表现。","B":"欺诈法规。","C":"时间至关重要。","D":"上述所有的。"},"explanationZh":"根据反欺诈法，房地产合同只有采用书面形式、由被指控方签署并包含所有基本条款后才可执行。因此，该法规与告知卖方其执行农场买卖合同的能力相关（选择 B）。房地产合同不需要包含截止日期。然而，当合同中包含将时间视为本质的明确语言或情况强烈表明双方有意这样做时，将严格执行截止日期。因此，如果截止日期被认定为严格的最后期限，则买方未能在 8 月 19 日之前从银行获得抵押贷款，将严重违反合同（选项 C）。当买方违反合同时，卖方的补救措施之一包括特定履行。这使得卖方可以寻求禁令，命令买方支付约定的购买价格以代替金钱赔偿。由于告知客户所有可用的补救措施很重要，因此具体履行也与向卖方告知其法律地位有关（选择 A）。\n\n教育目标：房地产合同必须符合欺诈法规，即采用书面形式，由被指控方签署，并包含所有基本条款。截止日期不是一个必要条款，但如果合同将时间作为重要条款或情况强烈表明双方有意如此，则将严格执行。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 445 (2020)（卖方的补救措施）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"房地产合同必须符合反欺诈法规，即采用书面形式，由被指控方签署，并包含所有基本条款。截止日期不是一个必要条款，但如果合同将时间作为重要条款或情况强烈表明双方有意如此，则将严格执行。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项与向卖方告知其法律地位有关？","rule":"房地产合同必须符合反欺诈法规，即采用书面形式，由被指控方签署，并包含所有基本条款。截止日期不是一个必要条款，但如果合同将时间作为重要条款或情况强烈表明双方有意如此，则将严格执行。","analysis":"根据反欺诈法，房地产合同只有采用书面形式、由被指控方签署并包含所有基本条款后才可执行。因此，该法规与告知卖方其执行农场买卖合同的能力相关（选择 B）。房地产合同不需要包含截止日期。然而，当合同中包含将时间视为本质的明确语言或情况强烈表明双方有意这样做时，将严格执行截止日期。因此，如果截止日期被认定为严格的最后期限，则买方未能在 8 月 19 日之前从银行获得抵押贷款，将严重违反合同（选项 C）。当买方违反合同时，卖方的补救措施之一包括特定履行。这使得卖方可以寻求禁令，命令买方支付约定的购买价格以代替金钱赔偿。由于告知客户所有可用的补救措施很重要，因此具体履行也与向卖方告知其法律地位有关（选择 A）。\n\n教育目标：房地产合同必须符合欺诈法规，即采用书面形式，由被指控方签署，并包含所有基本条款。截止日期不是一个必要条款，但如果合同将时间作为重要条款或情况强烈表明双方有意如此，则将严格执行。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 445 (2020)（卖方的补救措施）。","conclusion":"答案 D：上述所有的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":190,"sourceQuestionPage":556,"sourceQuestionPages":[556],"sourceExplanationPages":[557],"sourceAnswerPages":[557],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-191","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A woman owned two adjacent parcels, Lot 1 and Lot 2. Lot 1 fronts on a poor unpaved public road, while Lot 2 fronts a paved major highway. Fifteen years ago, the woman conveyed Lot 1 to her son \"together with a right-of-way 25 feet wide over the east side of Lot 2 to [the major highway].\" At that time, Lot 1 was improved with a 10-unit motel. Ten years ago, the woman died. Her will devised Lot 2 \"to my son for life, remainder to my daughter.\" Five years ago, the son executed an instrument in the proper form of a deed, purporting to convey Lot 1 and Lot 2 to his friend in fee simple. The friend then enlarged the motel to 12 units. Six months ago, the son died and the daughter took possession of Lot 2. She brought an appropriate action to enjoin the friend from using the right-of-way. In this action, who should prevail?","choices":{"A":"The daughter, because merger extinguished the easement.","B":"The daughter, because the friend has overburdened the easement.","C":"The friend, because he has an easement by necessity.","D":"The friend, because he has the easement granted by the woman to the son."},"answer":"D","explanation":"An easement is generally terminated by merger when the dominant and servient estates are united in common ownership. But merger does not occur—and the easement does not terminate—if there are any future interests (or other outstanding interests) in either estate. If there is a future interest, use of the easement is suspended until the future- interest holder becomes entitled to possession. Here, the woman conveyed Lot 1 (dominant estate) to her son, together with an easement to cross Lot 2 (servient estate) to access the highway. When the woman died, her will devised Lot 2 to her son for life, remainder to her daughter. Because the daughter had a future interest entitling her to possession of Lot 2 once the son dies, the easement was suspended during the son's lifetime—not terminated (Choice A). The son then purported to convey Lots 1 and 2 to the friend in fee simple. But since the son had only a life estate in Lot 2, that is all he could convey to the friend. So when the son died, the daughter's future interest vested and she became entitled to possession of Lot 2. At that time, the easement over Lot 2 became active. And since the friend owns Lot 1 in fee simple, he now holds the easement. (Choice B) Normal development of the dominant estate—including a change in the intensity or frequency of use—does not overburden the servient estate. Therefore, the friend did not overburden the easement by increasing the motel from 10 to 12 units. (Choice C) An easement by necessity only arises when the easement is strictly necessary for the use and enjoyment of property—eg, when property cannot be accessed by a public road (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: An easement cannot be terminated by merger if there are any future interests (or other outstanding interests) in the dominant or servient estate. Where there is a future interest, use of the easement is suspended until the future-interest holder becomes entitled to possession.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination by merger).","educationalObjective":"An easement cannot be terminated by merger if there are any future interests (or other outstanding interests) in the dominant or servient estate. Where there is a future interest, use of the easement is suspended until the future-interest holder becomes entitled to possession.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination by merger).","rule":"An easement cannot be terminated by merger if there are any future interests (or other outstanding interests) in the dominant or servient estate. Where there is a future interest, use of the easement is suspended until the future-interest holder becomes entitled to possession.","irac":{"issue":"In this action, who should prevail?","rule":"An easement cannot be terminated by merger if there are any future interests (or other outstanding interests) in the dominant or servient estate. Where there is a future interest, use of the easement is suspended until the future-interest holder becomes entitled to possession.","analysis":"An easement is generally terminated by merger when the dominant and servient estates are united in common ownership. But merger does not occur—and the easement does not terminate—if there are any future interests (or other outstanding interests) in either estate. If there is a future interest, use of the easement is suspended until the future- interest holder becomes entitled to possession. Here, the woman conveyed Lot 1 (dominant estate) to her son, together with an easement to cross Lot 2 (servient estate) to access the highway. When the woman died, her will devised Lot 2 to her son for life, remainder to her daughter. Because the daughter had a future interest entitling her to possession of Lot 2 once the son dies, the easement was suspended during the son's lifetime—not terminated (Choice A). The son then purported to convey Lots 1 and 2 to the friend in fee simple. But since the son had only a life estate in Lot 2, that is all he could convey to the friend. So when the son died, the daughter's future interest vested and she became entitled to possession of Lot 2. At that time, the easement over Lot 2 became active. And since the friend owns Lot 1 in fee simple, he now holds the easement. (Choice B) Normal development of the dominant estate—including a change in the intensity or frequency of use—does not overburden the servient estate. Therefore, the friend did not overburden the easement by increasing the motel from 10 to 12 units. (Choice C) An easement by necessity only arises when the easement is strictly necessary for the use and enjoyment of property—eg, when property cannot be accessed by a public road (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: The friend, because he has the easement granted by the woman to the son."},"questionZh":"一名妇女拥有两块相邻的地块，即地块 1 和地块 2。地块 1 面向一条未铺砌的简陋公共道路，而地块 2 面向一条铺砌的主要高速公路。十五年前，这位女士将 1 号地块转让给了她的儿子，“连同从 2 号地块东侧到 [主要高速公路] 的 25 英尺宽的通行权”。当时，1 号地块经过改造，建造了一座拥有 10 间客房的汽车旅馆。十年前，那个女人去世了。她的遗嘱设计了第二批“终身归我儿子，其余归我女儿”。五年前，儿子以契约的适当形式签署了一份文书，旨在将第 1 号和第 2 号地块以简单费用转让给他的朋友。这位朋友随后将汽车旅馆扩大到了 12 个单位。六个月前，儿子去世，女儿占有 2 号地块。她提起适当诉讼，禁止朋友使用通行权。在这场行动中，谁应该占上风？","choicesZh":{"A":"女儿，因为合并消灭了地役权。","B":"女儿，因为朋友负担过重的地役权。","C":"朋友，因为他有必要地役权。","D":"朋友，因为他拥有女人授予儿子的地役权。"},"explanationZh":"当支配性财产和从属财产统一为共同所有权时，地役权通常会因合并而终止。但如果任一遗产中存在任何未来权益（或其他未偿付权益），合并就不会发生，地役权也不会终止。如果存在未来权益，则暂停使用地役权，直到未来权益持有人有权占有为止。在这里，该妇女将第 1 号地块（主要地产）转让给她的儿子，以及穿过第 2 号地块（服务性地产）进入高速公路的地役权。当这位女士去世后，她在遗嘱中将第 2 号遗嘱留给了她的儿子终身，其余的则留给了她的女儿。因为一旦儿子去世，女儿就有权利拥有 2 号地块的未来权益，因此地役权在儿子生前暂停，而不是终止（选择 A）。随后，儿子声称将第 1 号和第 2 号拍品以简单的方式转让给朋友。但由于儿子只有第 2 号地块的终身财产，这就是他能向朋友传达的全部信息。因此，当儿子去世时，女儿的未来权益归属，她有权占有第 2 号地块。此时，第 2 号地块的地役权开始生效。由于该朋友拥有 1 号地块的费用，因此他现在拥有地役权。 （选择B）主导产业的正常发展——包括使用强度或频率的变化——不会给从属产业带来过重的负担。因此，这位朋友并没有将汽车旅馆从 10 间增加到 12 间，从而导致地役权负担过重。 （选项C）只有当地役权对于财产的使用和享有而言是严格必要的时，例如，当财产无法通过公共道路（此处未示出）进入时，才会产生必要的地役权。\n\n教育目标：如果支配性或受役性遗产中存在任何未来权益（或其他未决权益），则地役权不能通过合并终止。如果存在未来权益，则暂停使用地役权，直到未来权益持有人有权占有为止。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过合并终止）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果支配性或受役性遗产中存在任何未来权益（或其他未偿付权益），则地役权不能通过合并终止。如果存在未来权益，则暂停使用地役权，直到未来权益持有人有权占有为止。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这场行动中，谁应该占上风？","rule":"如果支配性或受役性遗产中存在任何未来权益（或其他未偿付权益），则地役权不能通过合并终止。如果存在未来权益，则暂停使用地役权，直到未来权益持有人有权占有为止。","analysis":"当支配性财产和从属财产统一为共同所有权时，地役权通常会因合并而终止。但如果任一遗产中存在任何未来权益（或其他未偿付权益），合并就不会发生，地役权也不会终止。如果存在未来权益，则暂停使用地役权，直到未来权益持有人有权占有为止。在这里，该妇女将第 1 号地块（主要地产）转让给她的儿子，以及穿过第 2 号地块（服务性地产）进入高速公路的地役权。当这位女士去世后，她在遗嘱中将第 2 号遗嘱留给了她的儿子终身，其余的则留给了她的女儿。因为一旦儿子去世，女儿就有权利拥有 2 号地块的未来权益，因此地役权在儿子生前暂停，而不是终止（选择 A）。随后，儿子声称将第 1 号和第 2 号拍品以简单的方式转让给朋友。但由于儿子只有第 2 号地块的终身财产，这就是他能向朋友传达的全部信息。因此，当儿子去世时，女儿的未来权益归属，她有权占有第 2 号地块。此时，第 2 号地块的地役权开始生效。由于该朋友拥有 1 号地块的费用，因此他现在拥有地役权。 （选择B）主导产业的正常发展——包括使用强度或频率的变化——不会给从属产业带来过重的负担。因此，这位朋友并没有将汽车旅馆从 10 间增加到 12 间，从而导致地役权负担过重。 （选项C）只有当地役权对于财产的使用和享有而言是严格必要的时，例如，当财产无法通过公共道路（此处未示出）进入时，才会产生必要的地役权。\n\n教育目标：如果支配性或受役性遗产中存在任何未来权益（或其他未决权益），则地役权不能通过合并终止。如果存在未来权益，则暂停使用地役权，直到未来权益持有人有权占有为止。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过合并终止）。","conclusion":"答案 D：朋友，因为他拥有女人授予儿子的地役权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-6943efafeee3d8ea"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":191,"sourceQuestionPage":558,"sourceQuestionPages":[558],"sourceExplanationPages":[559,560],"sourceAnswerPages":[559,560],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-192","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Easements","question":"A builder purchased 10 acres of undeveloped land. He intended to construct five homes, and each home was to be constructed on a two-acre lot, which was the minimum size permitted under the zoning regulations. The builder employed an architect to design the basic plan for each home, but the builder was forced to shelve those plans due to a sudden downturn in the economy. Six years later, the builder engaged a surveyor to subdivide the land into two-acre lots. He then discovered that the local zoning board had validly rezoned the area in which the 10 acres were located a year ago, increasing the minimum lot size to two and one-half acres for new residences. The zoning ordinance does not provide for special exceptions, and the builder cannot profitably construct only four of the five homes designed by the architect. Can the builder proceed with his plans to build five homes on two-acre lots?","choices":{"A":"No, because the ordinance does not provide for special exceptions.","B":"No, because the two and one-half acre restriction properly applies to the builder's land.","C":"Yes, because he cannot profitably construct only four homes on the 10 acres.","D":"Yes, because his rights with regard to the land vested when he purchased the land."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Nonconforming use\n\nExisting Property owner's existing use of property that conflicts with new nonconformance or modified zoning law can continue until: use is enlarged, changed, or abandoned or if provided by zoning law, amortization period passes or use not registered Planned Property owner's planned nonconforming use of property can nonconformance proceed if right to use has vested Vested right exists if, at time zoning law or modification takes effect, property owner has: secured any necessary permits in good faith and made substantial progress toward achieving use When a zoning law is enacted or modified, certain properties within that zone may not conform to the new requirements. Any existing nonconforming uses are \"grandfathered in,\" meaning that they are allowed to continue in spite of the conflict. However, a property owner who has merely prepared for a nonconforming use when a zoning change occurs is not allowed to proceed with that use unless he/she has a vested right in the use. A vested right will generally be found if, at the time the zoning change takes effect, the property owner has: secured any necessary permit from the proper local authority in good faith (eg, building permit) and\n\nmade substantial progress toward achieving the nonconforming use (note that in a few states, securing a permit by itself is sufficient).\n\nHere, the builder does not have a vested right in building five homes on two-acre lots because the above requirements had not been met at the time the two and one-half acre restriction took effect. Therefore, the restriction properly applies to the builder's land, and he cannot proceed with his plans (Choice D). (Choice A) A zoning law may recognize that certain conforming uses require a special- exception or special-use permit—eg, a gas station may require such a permit even in a commercially zoned area. But this has no bearing on whether the builder has a vested right to proceed with his nonconforming use. (Choice C) The fact that the builder cannot profitably construct four homes on the 10 acres does not entitle him to build five homes on two-acre lots. However, had the zoning change\n\neliminated any economically viable use of the land, he may have been entitled to reasonable compensation under the Fifth Amendment takings clause.\n\nEducational objective: A property owner may proceed with plans for nonconforming use after a zoning change if he/she has a vested right in that use. A vested right exists if, at the time a zoning change takes effect, the property owner has (1) secured any necessary permits in good faith and (2) made substantial progress toward achieving the use.\n\nReferences\n83 Am. Jur. 2d Zoning & Planning §§ 537–44 (2019) (nature and extent of nonconforming use).","educationalObjective":"A property owner may proceed with plans for nonconforming use after a zoning change if he/she has a vested right in that use. A vested right exists if, at the time a zoning change takes effect, the property owner has (1) secured any necessary permits in good faith and (2) made substantial progress toward achieving the use.","references":"83 Am. Jur. 2d Zoning & Planning §§ 537–44 (2019) (nature and extent of nonconforming use).","rule":"A property owner may proceed with plans for nonconforming use after a zoning change if he/she has a vested right in that use. A vested right exists if, at the time a zoning change takes effect, the property owner has (1) secured any necessary permits in good faith and (2) made substantial progress toward achieving the use.","irac":{"issue":"Can the builder proceed with his plans to build five homes on two-acre lots?","rule":"A property owner may proceed with plans for nonconforming use after a zoning change if he/she has a vested right in that use. A vested right exists if, at the time a zoning change takes effect, the property owner has (1) secured any necessary permits in good faith and (2) made substantial progress toward achieving the use.","analysis":"Nonconforming use\n\nExisting Property owner's existing use of property that conflicts with new nonconformance or modified zoning law can continue until: use is enlarged, changed, or abandoned or if provided by zoning law, amortization period passes or use not registered Planned Property owner's planned nonconforming use of property can nonconformance proceed if right to use has vested Vested right exists if, at time zoning law or modification takes effect, property owner has: secured any necessary permits in good faith and made substantial progress toward achieving use When a zoning law is enacted or modified, certain properties within that zone may not conform to the new requirements. Any existing nonconforming uses are \"grandfathered in,\" meaning that they are allowed to continue in spite of the conflict. However, a property owner who has merely prepared for a nonconforming use when a zoning change occurs is not allowed to proceed with that use unless he/she has a vested right in the use. A vested right will generally be found if, at the time the zoning change takes effect, the property owner has: secured any necessary permit from the proper local authority in good faith (eg, building permit) and\n\nmade substantial progress toward achieving the nonconforming use (note that in a few states, securing a permit by itself is sufficient).\n\nHere, the builder does not have a vested right in building five homes on two-acre lots because the above requirements had not been met at the time the two and one-half acre restriction took effect. Therefore, the restriction properly applies to the builder's land, and he cannot proceed with his plans (Choice D). (Choice A) A zoning law may recognize that certain conforming uses require a special- exception or special-use permit—eg, a gas station may require such a permit even in a commercially zoned area. But this has no bearing on whether the builder has a vested right to proceed with his nonconforming use. (Choice C) The fact that the builder cannot profitably construct four homes on the 10 acres does not entitle him to build five homes on two-acre lots. However, had the zoning change\n\neliminated any economically viable use of the land, he may have been entitled to reasonable compensation under the Fifth Amendment takings clause.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the two and one-half acre restriction properly applies to the builder's land."},"questionZh":"一位建筑商购买了 10 英亩未开发的土地。他打算建造五栋房屋，每栋房屋都将建在两英亩的土地上，这是土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法规允许的最小面积。建筑商聘请了一名建筑师来设计每栋房屋的基本计划，但由于经济突然低迷，建筑商被迫搁置了这些计划。六年后，建筑商聘请了一位测量员将土地细分为两英亩的地块。随后，他发现当地土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）委员会一年前有效地重新规划了这 10 英亩的土地，将新住宅的最小用地面积增加到两英亩半。土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例没有规定特殊例外，建筑商只能建造建筑师设计的五栋房屋中的四栋，无法盈利。建筑商能否继续实施在两英亩土地上建造五栋房屋的计划？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该条例没有规定特殊例外情况。","B":"不，因为两英亩半的限制正确适用于建筑商的土地。","C":"是的，因为他在 10 英亩的土地上只建造四栋房屋就无法盈利。","D":"是的，因为他对土地的权利在他购买土地时就已归属。"},"explanationZh":"不合格使用\n\n现有业主对与新的不符合规定或修改的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法相冲突的财产的现有使用可以继续，直到：用途扩大、改变或放弃，或者如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法规定，摊销期已过或使用未注册。 规划财产所有者对财产的计划不符合规定的使用可以继续进行，如果使用权已既得既得权利存在，如果在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法或修改生效时，业主已经：真诚地获得了任何必要的许可，并在实现目标方面取得了实质性进展使用 当土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法颁布或修改时，该区域内的某些房产可能不符合新的要求。任何现有的不合格用途都被“保留”，这意味着尽管存在冲突，它们仍然可以继续使用。然而，在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更发生时仅准备不符合规定的用途的业主不得继续进行该用途，除非他/她拥有该用途的既定权利。如果在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更生效时，业主已满足以下条件，则通常会获得既得权利： 善意地从适当的地方当局获得任何必要的许可（例如建筑许可）并且\n\n在实现不合格使用方面取得了实质性进展（请注意，在少数州，仅获得许可就足够了）。\n\n在这里，建筑商没有在两英亩土地上建造五栋房屋的既定权利，因为在两英亩半限制生效时尚未满足上述要求。因此，该限制适当地适用于建筑商的土地，他无法继续实施他的计划（选项D）。 （选择 A） 土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法可能承认某些合规用途需要特殊例外或特殊用途许可证，例如，即使在商业土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）区域，加油站也可能需要此类许可证。但这与建筑商是否拥有继续其不合格使用的既定权利无关。 （选项 C） 建筑商无法在 10 英亩的土地上建造 4 所房屋并从中获利，这一事实并不意味着他有权在 2 英亩的土地上建造 5 所房屋。然而，如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）发生变化\n\n如果消除了对土地的任何经济上可行的使用，他可能有权根据第五修正案征收条款获得合理补偿。\n\n教育目标：如果业主拥有该用途的既得权利，则他/她可以在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更后继续实施不合格用途的计划。如果在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更生效时，业主已 (1) 真诚地获得任何必要的许可，并且 (2) 在实现使用方面取得了实质性进展，则存在既得权利。\n\n参考文献\n83 上午。尤尔。 2d 土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）和规划 §§ 537–44 (2019)（不合格使用的性质和范围）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果业主拥有该用途的既定权利，则他/她可以在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更后继续实施不符合规定的用途的计划。如果在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更生效时，业主已 (1) 真诚地获得任何必要的许可，并且 (2) 在实现使用方面取得了实质性进展，则存在既得权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"建筑商能否继续实施在两英亩土地上建造五栋房屋的计划？","rule":"如果业主拥有该用途的既定权利，则他/她可以在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更后继续实施不符合规定的用途的计划。如果在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更生效时，业主已 (1) 真诚地获得任何必要的许可，并且 (2) 在实现使用方面取得了实质性进展，则存在既得权利。","analysis":"不合格使用\n\n现有业主对与新的不符合规定或修改的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法相冲突的财产的现有使用可以继续，直到：用途扩大、改变或放弃，或者如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法规定，摊销期已过或使用未注册。 规划财产所有者对财产的计划不符合规定的使用可以继续进行，如果使用权已既得既得权利存在，如果在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法或修改生效时，业主已经：真诚地获得了任何必要的许可，并在实现目标方面取得了实质性进展使用 当土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法颁布或修改时，该区域内的某些房产可能不符合新的要求。任何现有的不合格用途都被“保留”，这意味着尽管存在冲突，它们仍然可以继续使用。然而，在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更发生时仅准备不符合规定的用途的业主不得继续进行该用途，除非他/她拥有该用途的既定权利。如果在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更生效时，业主已满足以下条件，则通常会获得既得权利： 善意地从适当的地方当局获得任何必要的许可（例如建筑许可）并且\n\n在实现不合格使用方面取得了实质性进展（请注意，在少数州，仅获得许可就足够了）。\n\n在这里，建筑商没有在两英亩土地上建造五栋房屋的既定权利，因为在两英亩半限制生效时尚未满足上述要求。因此，该限制适当地适用于建筑商的土地，他无法继续实施他的计划（选项D）。 （选择 A） 土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）法可能承认某些合规用途需要特殊例外或特殊用途许可证，例如，即使在商业土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）区域，加油站也可能需要此类许可证。但这与建筑商是否拥有继续其不合格使用的既定权利无关。 （选项 C） 建筑商无法在 10 英亩的土地上建造 4 所房屋并从中获利，这一事实并不意味着他有权在 2 英亩的土地上建造 5 所房屋。然而，如果土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）发生变化\n\n如果消除了对土地的任何经济上可行的使用，他可能有权根据第五修正案征收条款获得合理补偿。\n\n教育目标：如果业主拥有该用途的既得权利，则他/她可以在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更后继续实施不合格用途的计划。如果在土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更生效时，业主已 (1) 真诚地获得任何必要的许可，并且 (2) 在实现使用方面取得了实质性进展，则存在既得权利。\n\n参考文献\n83 上午。尤尔。 2d 土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）和规划 §§ 537–44 (2019)（不合格使用的性质和范围）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为两英亩半的限制正确适用于建筑商的土地。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"nonconformance","ipa":"/'nɔnkən'fɔməns/","syllables":"non·con·for·mance","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 不信奉国教, 不顺从","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“nonconformance”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Nonconforming use Existing Property owner's existing use of property that conflicts with new nonconformance or modified zoning law can continue until: use is enlarged, changed, or abandoned or if provided by zoning law, amortization period passes or use not registered Planned Property owner's planned nonconforming use of property can nonconformance proceed if right to use has vested Vested right exists if, at time zo","synonyms":["nonconformity","nonconformism"]},{"term":"profitably","ipa":"/pɹˈɑfətəbli/","syllables":"prof·itably","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 有利地, 有益地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“profitably”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The zoning ordinance does not provide for special exceptions, and the builder cannot profitably construct only four of the five homes designed by the architect.","synonyms":["productively","fruitfully"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":192,"sourceQuestionPage":561,"sourceQuestionPages":[561],"sourceExplanationPages":[562,563],"sourceAnswerPages":[562,563],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-193","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A man owned a tract of land. His friend wanted to buy the tract and offered $20,000 for it. The man knew that the friend was insolvent but replied, \"As a favor to you as an old friend, I will sell my tract to you for $20,000, even though it is worth much more, if you can raise the money within one month.\" The friend wrote the following words, and no more, on a piece of paper: \"I agree to sell my tract to [the friend] for $20,000.\" The man then signed the piece of paper and gave it to the friend. Three days later, the man received an offer of $40,000 for the tract. He asked the friend if he had raised the $20,000. When the friend answered, \"Not yet,\" the man told him that their deal was off and that he was going to accept the $40,000 offer. The next week, the friend secured a bank commitment to enable him to purchase the tract. The friend immediately brought an appropriate action against the man to compel the man to convey the tract to the friend. Who will likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"The friend, because he has equitable title.","B":"The friend, because the parties' antecedent agreement is enforceable.","C":"The man, because the man's performance was excused by the friend's anticipatory breach.","D":"The man, because the terms of the parties' fully integrated written agreement control."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Statute of frauds requirements for real estate contracts\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed\n\neg, receipt, check, emails Signature Handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged\n\neg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials Essential Typically includes: terms identity of parties words of intent to buy or sell property description sales price Contracts for the sale of land are enforceable only if they comply with the statute of frauds. This statute requires that land-sale contracts be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Essential terms often include: the identity of the parties (eg, \"I,\" \"the friend,\" and the man's signature) words of intent to buy or sell (eg, \"I agree to sell\") a description of the property (eg, \"my tract\") and the sales price (eg, $20,000). Therefore, the antecedent agreement—ie, the piece of paper on which the friend wrote \"I agree to sell my tract to [the friend] for $20,000\" and that the man signed—is enforceable against the man under the statute of frauds. Therefore, the friend will likely prevail. (Choice A) The doctrine of equitable conversion relates to the ownership of property and the risk of loss in the period between contract formation and closing. However, it does not impact the validity of, or the ability to enforce, a land-sale contract. (Choice C) Anticipatory repudiation arises when a party to a contract clearly and unequivocally expresses an unwillingness to perform. The other party may then treat that repudiation as a breach. But here, the friend's statement that he had \"not yet\" raised the $20,000 expressed his willingness to perform, so no anticipatory repudiation occurred. (Choice D) The parol evidence rule generally bars the use of extrinsic evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement that contradicts the written terms of a fully integrated\n\ncontract. As a result, the terms of a final written agreement will control. But since the friend is able to perform under that contract, this will not help the man prevail.\n\nEducational objective: Under the statute of frauds, a contract for the sale of land is enforceable only if it is in writing, is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contains all essential terms—ie, identifies the parties, contains words of intent to buy or sell, describes the property, and lists the sales price.","educationalObjective":"Under the statute of frauds, a contract for the sale of land is enforceable only if it is in writing, is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contains all essential terms—ie, identifies the parties, contains words of intent to buy or sell, describes the property, and lists the sales price.","references":"","rule":"Under the statute of frauds, a contract for the sale of land is enforceable only if it is in writing, is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contains all essential terms—ie, identifies the parties, contains words of intent to buy or sell, describes the property, and lists the sales price.","irac":{"issue":"Who will likely prevail?","rule":"Under the statute of frauds, a contract for the sale of land is enforceable only if it is in writing, is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contains all essential terms—ie, identifies the parties, contains words of intent to buy or sell, describes the property, and lists the sales price.","analysis":"Statute of frauds requirements for real estate contracts\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed\n\neg, receipt, check, emails Signature Handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged\n\neg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials Essential Typically includes: terms identity of parties words of intent to buy or sell property description sales price Contracts for the sale of land are enforceable only if they comply with the statute of frauds. This statute requires that land-sale contracts be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Essential terms often include: the identity of the parties (eg, \"I,\" \"the friend,\" and the man's signature) words of intent to buy or sell (eg, \"I agree to sell\") a description of the property (eg, \"my tract\") and the sales price (eg, $20,000). Therefore, the antecedent agreement—ie, the piece of paper on which the friend wrote \"I agree to sell my tract to [the friend] for $20,000\" and that the man signed—is enforceable against the man under the statute of frauds. Therefore, the friend will likely prevail. (Choice A) The doctrine of equitable conversion relates to the ownership of property and the risk of loss in the period between contract formation and closing. However, it does not impact the validity of, or the ability to enforce, a land-sale contract. (Choice C) Anticipatory repudiation arises when a party to a contract clearly and unequivocally expresses an unwillingness to perform. The other party may then treat that repudiation as a breach. But here, the friend's statement that he had \"not yet\" raised the $20,000 expressed his willingness to perform, so no anticipatory repudiation occurred. (Choice D) The parol evidence rule generally bars the use of extrinsic evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement that contradicts the written terms of a fully integrated\n\ncontract. As a result, the terms of a final written agreement will control. But since the friend is able to perform under that contract, this will not help the man prevail.","conclusion":"Answer B: The friend, because the parties' antecedent agreement is enforceable."},"questionZh":"一个人拥有一块土地。他的朋友想买下这本小册子，并出价 20,000 美元。那人知道朋友资不抵债，却回答说：“算作老朋友的恩情，如果你能在一个月内筹集到钱，我就以两万美元的价格把我的单张卖给你，尽管它的价值更高。”这位朋友在一张纸上写下了以下文字：“我同意以 20,000 美元的价格将我的单张卖给[这位朋友]。”该男子随后在纸上签名并将其交给了朋友。三天后，该男子收到了购买该单张的 4 万美元报价。他问朋友是否筹集到了 20,000 美元。当朋友回答“还没有”时，该男子告诉他，他们的交易已经结束，他将接受 40,000 美元的报价。接下来的一周，这位朋友获得了银行的承诺，使他能够购买该单张。朋友立即对该男子采取适当行动，迫使该男子将单张转交给朋友。谁可能会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"朋友，因为他有公平的头衔。","B":"这位朋友，因为双方之前达成的协议是可以强制执行的。","C":"男子，因为该男子的表现因朋友的预期违约而得到原谅。","D":"男方，因为该条款完全受双方书面协议的综合控制。"},"explanationZh":"房地产合同欺诈法规要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件\n\n例如，收据、支票、电子邮件 签名 被指控方的手写签名或其他识别标记\n\n例如，信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名首字母缩写 基本内容 通常包括： 条款 当事人身份 购买或出售财产的意向词 说明 销售价格 土地销售合同只有在符合欺诈法的情况下才可执行。该法规要求土地销售合同采用书面形式，由被强制执行方签署，并包含所有基本条款。基本术语通常包括： 当事人的身份（例如，“我”、“朋友”和该人的签名） 购买或出售意向的词语（例如，“我同意出售”） 财产描述（例如，“我的土地”）和销售价格（例如，20,000 美元）。因此，先前的协议——即朋友在上面写下“我同意以 20,000 美元的价格将我的单张卖给[朋友]”并且该男子签署的纸——可以根据欺诈法对该男子强制执行。因此，朋友很可能会占上风。 （选项A） 衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则涉及财产的所有权以及合同订立和结束期间的损失风险。但是，它不会影响土地出售合同的有效性或执行能力。 （选项C）当合同一方明确且毫不含糊地表示不愿意履行合同时，就会出现预期否认。然后，另一方可以将该拒绝视为违约。但在这里，朋友声明他“尚未”筹集到 20,000 美元，表达了他愿意履行的义务，因此没有发生预期的拒绝。 （选项 D）口头证据规则通常禁止使用先前或同期协议的外在证据，这些证据与完全整合的书面条款相矛盾。\n\n合同。因此，以最终书面协议的条款为准。但由于朋友能够履行该合同，这无助于该男子获胜。\n\n教育目标：根据反欺诈法，土地出售合同只有采用书面形式、由被强制执行方签署并包含所有基本条款（即确定当事人身份、包含购买或出售意向的文字、描述财产并列出销售价格）才可执行。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据反欺诈法，土地出售合同只有采用书面形式、由被强制执行方签署并包含所有基本条款（即确定当事人身份、包含购买或出售意向的文字、描述财产并列出销售价格）才可执行。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁可能会获胜？","rule":"根据反欺诈法，土地出售合同只有采用书面形式、由被强制执行方签署并包含所有基本条款（即确定当事人身份、包含购买或出售意向的文字、描述财产并列出销售价格）才可执行。","analysis":"房地产合同欺诈法规要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件\n\n例如，收据、支票、电子邮件 签名 被指控方的手写签名或其他识别标记\n\n例如，信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名首字母缩写 基本内容 通常包括： 条款 当事人身份 购买或出售财产的意向词 说明 销售价格 土地销售合同只有在符合欺诈法的情况下才可执行。该法规要求土地销售合同采用书面形式，由被强制执行方签署，并包含所有基本条款。基本术语通常包括： 当事人的身份（例如，“我”、“朋友”和该人的签名） 购买或出售意向的词语（例如，“我同意出售”） 财产描述（例如，“我的土地”）和销售价格（例如，20,000 美元）。因此，先前的协议——即朋友在上面写下“我同意以 20,000 美元的价格将我的单张卖给[朋友]”并且该男子签署的纸——可以根据欺诈法对该男子强制执行。因此，朋友很可能会占上风。 （选项A） 衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则涉及财产的所有权以及合同订立和结束期间的损失风险。但是，它不会影响土地出售合同的有效性或执行能力。 （选项C）当合同一方明确且毫不含糊地表示不愿意履行合同时，就会出现预期否认。然后，另一方可以将该拒绝视为违约。但在这里，朋友声明他“尚未”筹集到 20,000 美元，表达了他愿意履行的义务，因此没有发生预期的拒绝。 （选项 D）口头证据规则通常禁止使用先前或同期协议的外在证据，这些证据与完全整合的书面条款相矛盾。\n\n合同。因此，以最终书面协议的条款为准。但由于朋友能够履行该合同，这无助于该男子获胜。\n\n教育目标：根据反欺诈法，土地出售合同只有采用书面形式、由被强制执行方签署并包含所有基本条款（即确定当事人身份、包含购买或出售意向的文字、描述财产并列出销售价格）才可执行。","conclusion":"答案 B：这位朋友，因为双方之前达成的协议是可以强制执行的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":193,"sourceQuestionPage":564,"sourceQuestionPages":[564],"sourceExplanationPages":[565,566],"sourceAnswerPages":[565,566],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-194","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"The builder of a new house sold the house to a buyer, conveying it to the buyer by warranty deed. One year later, the buyer sold the house to a woman for $50,000 more than the buyer had paid, conveying it to the woman by a quitclaim deed. Four months after moving in, the woman discovered a number of latent defects in the foundation that caused the house's basement to take on water during heavy rains. The woman contacted the builder about the problem. Toward the end of their heated conversation, the builder yelled at her, \"So get it fixed!\" After the woman had the foundation repaired by a cement contractor at a cost of $25,000, she successfully sued the builder to recover that amount from him. What is the most likely reason that the woman was successful?","choices":{"A":"The court found an implied warranty of habitability from the builder to the buyer that was enforceable by a subsequent buyer.","B":"The court found that by paying the buyer $50,000 more for the house than the buyer had paid the builder, the woman did not get the benefit of her bargain.","C":"The court found that by yelling at the woman to \"get it fixed,\" the builder had committed himself to paying for the repair.","D":"The court found that the defects in the foundation were a breach of the covenant of further assurances in the warranty deed from the builder to the buyer."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Implied warranty of habitability\n\n(ie, implied warranty of fitness or suitability, quality, workmanlike construction)\n\nImplied promise by builder, developer, or other commercial seller of new house that house was built in reasonably workmanlike manner & is suitable for living Builder, developer, or other commercial seller liable for defects that interfere with buyer's ability to live in home Buyer's damages based on cost of repairs to bring house into compliance with warranty Under the implied warranty of habitability (ie, implied warranty of fitness or suitability), the builder of a new home impliedly guarantees that the home is habitable and fit for its intended purposes when sold. This allows buyers to recover damages for losses caused by the builder's defective construction or poor workmanship. And in most jurisdictions, this warranty extends to subsequent purchasers as to latent construction defects. Here, the buyer purchased a new home from the builder and later sold it to the woman. After moving in, the woman discovered latent defects in the foundation that caused the house's basement to take on water during heavy rains (habitability concerns). Therefore, the most likely reason that the woman was successful against the builder is that the court found an implied warranty of habitability that was enforceable by her (a subsequent buyer). (Choice B) Damages for breach of the implied warranty of habitability are based on the cost of repairs to bring the home into compliance with the warranty. Therefore, the additional $50,000 the woman paid to the buyer for the home does not affect her ability to recover $25,000 in repair costs from the builder for breach of this warranty. (Choice C) The builder did not commit to paying for the repair by telling the woman to \"get it fixed.\" Instead, he must pay for the repair because he is liable for latent construction defects under the implied warranty of habitability. (Choice D) A warranty deed contains certain covenants of title, including the covenant of further assurances—ie, the grantor's promise to take any actions reasonably necessary to cure defects in title. But since defects in the foundation affect the physical condition (not title) of the property, this covenant does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: The implied warranty of habitability guarantees that a new home is habitable and fit for its intended purposes. This allows buyers to recover for losses caused by poor workmanship and, in most jurisdictions, allows subsequent purchasers to recover for losses caused by latent construction defects.\n\nReferences\n13 Am. Jur. 2d Building and Construction Contracts § 10 (2018) (duties implied by law).","educationalObjective":"The implied warranty of habitability guarantees that a new home is habitable and fit for its intended purposes. This allows buyers to recover for losses caused by poor workmanship and, in most jurisdictions, allows subsequent purchasers to recover for losses caused by latent construction defects.","references":"13 Am. Jur. 2d Building and Construction Contracts § 10 (2018) (duties implied by law).","rule":"The implied warranty of habitability guarantees that a new home is habitable and fit for its intended purposes. This allows buyers to recover for losses caused by poor workmanship and, in most jurisdictions, allows subsequent purchasers to recover for losses caused by latent construction defects.","irac":{"issue":"What is the most likely reason that the woman was successful?","rule":"The implied warranty of habitability guarantees that a new home is habitable and fit for its intended purposes. This allows buyers to recover for losses caused by poor workmanship and, in most jurisdictions, allows subsequent purchasers to recover for losses caused by latent construction defects.","analysis":"Implied warranty of habitability\n\n(ie, implied warranty of fitness or suitability, quality, workmanlike construction)\n\nImplied promise by builder, developer, or other commercial seller of new house that house was built in reasonably workmanlike manner & is suitable for living Builder, developer, or other commercial seller liable for defects that interfere with buyer's ability to live in home Buyer's damages based on cost of repairs to bring house into compliance with warranty Under the implied warranty of habitability (ie, implied warranty of fitness or suitability), the builder of a new home impliedly guarantees that the home is habitable and fit for its intended purposes when sold. This allows buyers to recover damages for losses caused by the builder's defective construction or poor workmanship. And in most jurisdictions, this warranty extends to subsequent purchasers as to latent construction defects. Here, the buyer purchased a new home from the builder and later sold it to the woman. After moving in, the woman discovered latent defects in the foundation that caused the house's basement to take on water during heavy rains (habitability concerns). Therefore, the most likely reason that the woman was successful against the builder is that the court found an implied warranty of habitability that was enforceable by her (a subsequent buyer). (Choice B) Damages for breach of the implied warranty of habitability are based on the cost of repairs to bring the home into compliance with the warranty. Therefore, the additional $50,000 the woman paid to the buyer for the home does not affect her ability to recover $25,000 in repair costs from the builder for breach of this warranty. (Choice C) The builder did not commit to paying for the repair by telling the woman to \"get it fixed.\" Instead, he must pay for the repair because he is liable for latent construction defects under the implied warranty of habitability. (Choice D) A warranty deed contains certain covenants of title, including the covenant of further assurances—ie, the grantor's promise to take any actions reasonably necessary to cure defects in title. But since defects in the foundation affect the physical condition (not title) of the property, this covenant does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer A: The court found an implied warranty of habitability from the builder to the buyer that was enforceable by a subsequent buyer."},"questionZh":"新房子的建筑商将房子卖给买家，并通过保修契约将其转让给买家。一年后，买家以比买家实际支付的价格高出 5 万美元的价格将房子卖给了一名妇女，并通过无偿债权契据将其转让给了该妇女。搬进来四个月后，这位女士发现地基存在一些潜在的缺陷，导致房子的地下室在大雨期间进水。这位女士就这个问题联系了建筑商。在他们激烈的谈话即将结束时，建筑商对她大喊：“所以把它修好！”这位女士请水泥承包商花费 25,000 美元修复了地基后，她成功起诉建筑商，从他那里收回了这笔款项。这位女士成功的最可能原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"法院认定建筑商向买方提供了可居住性的默示保证，并且后续买方可以强制执行。","B":"法院认为，买方支付给建筑商的费用比买方多支付了 50,000 美元，该妇女并没有从讨价还价中获益。","C":"法院认为，建筑商通过对这名女子大喊“把它修好”，就表明自己承诺支付维修费用。","D":"法院认为，地基缺陷违反了建筑商向买方提供的保修契约中的进一步保证承诺。"},"explanationZh":"可居住性的默示保证\n\n（即对适用性或适用性、质量、工艺结构的默示保证）\n\n新房的建筑商、开发商或其他商业卖方默示承诺，房屋以合理的工艺方式建造并适合居住 建筑商、开发商或其他商业卖方对干扰买方居住能力的缺陷承担责任 买方根据使房屋符合保修规定的维修费用承担损害赔偿 根据可居住性的默示保证（即适用性或适用性的默示保证），新房的建筑商默示保证房屋在以下情况下适合居住并适合其预期用途出售。这使得买家可以就因建筑商施工缺陷或工艺不良造成的损失获得赔偿。在大多数司法管辖区，此保修延伸至后续购买者的潜在建筑缺陷。在这里，买家从建筑商那里购买了一套新房，然后卖给了这位女士。搬进去后，这位女士发现了地基的潜在缺陷，导致房子的地下室在大雨期间进水（居住性问题）。因此，该妇女胜诉建筑商的最可能原因是法院发现了她（后来的买家）可以强制执行的可居住性默示保证。 （选择 B） 违反可居住性默示保证的损害赔偿基于使房屋符合保修要求的维修费用。因此，该妇女向买家额外支付的 50,000 美元购买房屋并不影响她因违反本保证而向建筑商追偿 2​​5,000 美元的维修费用。 （选项 C） 建筑商并没有承诺支付维修费用，而是告诉这位女士“修好它”。相反，他必须支付维修费用，因为根据隐含的可居住性保证，他应对潜在的建筑缺陷负责。 （选项 D） 保证契约包含某些所有权契约，包括进一步保证的契约，即设保人承诺采取任何合理必要的行动来纠正所有权缺陷。但由于地基缺陷影响财产的物理状况（而不是所有权），因此该契约不适用。\n\n教育目标：可居住性的隐含保证保证新房适合居住并适合其预期用途。这使得买家能够赔偿因工艺不良造成的损失，并且在大多数司法管辖区，允许后续买家赔偿因潜在建筑缺陷造成的损失。\n\n参考文献\n上午 13 点。尤尔。 2d 建筑和施工合同 § 10 (2018)（法律隐含的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"隐含的可居住性保证保证新房适合居住并适合其预期用途。这使得买家能够赔偿因工艺不良造成的损失，并且在大多数司法管辖区，允许后续买家赔偿因潜在建筑缺陷造成的损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"这位女士成功的最可能原因是什么？","rule":"隐含的可居住性保证保证新房适合居住并适合其预期用途。这使得买家能够赔偿因工艺不良造成的损失，并且在大多数司法管辖区，允许后续买家赔偿因潜在建筑缺陷造成的损失。","analysis":"可居住性的默示保证\n\n（即对适用性或适用性、质量、工艺结构的默示保证）\n\n新房的建筑商、开发商或其他商业卖方默示承诺，房屋以合理的工艺方式建造并适合居住 建筑商、开发商或其他商业卖方对干扰买方居住能力的缺陷承担责任 买方根据使房屋符合保修规定的维修费用承担损害赔偿 根据可居住性的默示保证（即适用性或适用性的默示保证），新房的建筑商默示保证房屋在以下情况下适合居住并适合其预期用途出售。这使得买家可以就因建筑商施工缺陷或工艺不良造成的损失获得赔偿。在大多数司法管辖区，此保修延伸至后续购买者的潜在建筑缺陷。在这里，买家从建筑商那里购买了一套新房，然后卖给了这位女士。搬进去后，这位女士发现了地基的潜在缺陷，导致房子的地下室在大雨期间进水（居住性问题）。因此，该妇女胜诉建筑商的最可能原因是法院发现了她（后来的买家）可以强制执行的可居住性默示保证。 （选择 B） 违反可居住性默示保证的损害赔偿基于使房屋符合保修要求的维修费用。因此，该妇女向买家额外支付的 50,000 美元购买房屋并不影响她因违反本保证而向建筑商追偿 2​​5,000 美元的维修费用。 （选项 C） 建筑商并没有承诺支付维修费用，而是告诉这位女士“修好它”。相反，他必须支付维修费用，因为根据隐含的可居住性保证，他应对潜在的建筑缺陷负责。 （选项 D） 保证契约包含某些所有权契约，包括进一步保证的契约，即设保人承诺采取任何合理必要的行动来纠正所有权缺陷。但由于地基缺陷影响财产的物理状况（而不是所有权），因此该契约不适用。\n\n教育目标：可居住性的隐含保证保证新房适合居住并适合其预期用途。这使得买家能够赔偿因工艺不良造成的损失，并且在大多数司法管辖区，允许后续买家赔偿因潜在建筑缺陷造成的损失。\n\n参考文献\n上午 13 点。尤尔。 2d 建筑和施工合同 § 10 (2018)（法律隐含的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 A：法院认定建筑商向买方提供了可居住性的默示保证，并且后续买方可以强制执行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"covenant of further assurances","ipa":"/kˈəvənənt ˈəv fˈɝðɝ ʌʃˈʊɹənsɪz/","syllables":"covenant of fur·ther as·sur·ances","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"进一步保证担保：出让人承诺采取必要后续行为完善产权","contextualMeaning":"进一步保证担保：出让人承诺采取必要后续行为完善产权","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The court found that the defects in the foundation were a breach of the covenant of further assurances in the warranty deed from the builder to the buyer.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"habitable","ipa":"/hˈæbətəbəl/","syllables":"hab·it·able","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 适于居住的, 可居住的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“habitable”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The implied warranty of habitability guarantees that a new home is habitable and fit for its intended purposes.","synonyms":["inhabitable"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":194,"sourceQuestionPage":567,"sourceQuestionPages":[567],"sourceExplanationPages":[568,569],"sourceAnswerPages":[568,569],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-195","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Thirty years ago, a brother owned Lot 1, a lot contiguous to Lot 2, in fee simple. He executed and delivered to his sister an instrument in writing which was denominated \"Deed of Conveyance.\" In pertinent part it read, \"[Brother] does grant to [sister] and her heirs and assigns a right-of-way for egress and ingress to Lot 2.\" If the quoted provision was sufficient to create an interest in land, the instrument met all other requirements for a valid grant. The deed was recorded. The sister held record title in fee simple to Lot 2, which adjoined Lot 1. Twelve years ago, a cousin succeeded to the brother's title in fee simple in Lot 1. Seven years ago, a friend succeeded to the sister's title in fee simple in Lot 2 by a deed that made no mention of a right-of-way or driveway. At the time the friend took title, there existed a driveway across Lot 1 which showed evidence that it had been used regularly to travel between a public highway and Lot 2. Lot 2 did have frontage on a public side road, but this means of access was seldom used because it was not as convenient to the dwelling situated on Lot 2 as was the highway. The driveway originally was established by the sister. The friend has regularly used the driveway since acquiring title. The period of time required to acquire rights by prescription in the jurisdiction is 10 years. Six months ago, the cousin notified the friend that he planned to develop a portion of Lot 1 as a residential subdivision and that the friend should cease any use of the driveway. After some negotiations, the cousin offered to permit the friend to construct another driveway to connect with the streets of the proposed subdivision. She declined this offer on the ground that travel from Lot 2 to the highway would be more circuitous. The friend brought an appropriate action against the cousin to obtain a definitive adjudication of their respective rights. In such lawsuit, the cousin relied upon the defense that the location of the easement created by the grant from the brother to the sister was governed by reasonableness and that the cousin's proposed solution was reasonable. Should the cousin prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the location had been established by the acts of the brother and the sister.","B":"No, because the location of the easement had been fixed by prescription.","C":"Yes, because the reasonableness of the cousin's proposal was established by the friend's refusal to suggest any alternative location.","D":"Yes, because the servient owner is entitled to select the location of a right-of-way if the grant fails to identify its location."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Location & relocation of easements\n\nLocation Initial location set by:\n\nexpress easement's terms circumstances surrounding easement's creation servient owner, provided location is set within reasonable time & reasonably suited to easement's purpose (if neither of above provisions applies) Relocation Servient owner may relocate (at own expense) to permit normal use/development of servient estate if relocation is reasonable & does not:\n\nviolate express easement's terms significantly lessen easement's utility increase burden on dominant owner's use or enjoyment of easement frustrate easement's purpose The location of an express easement is set by its terms. But when those terms are unclear (eg, \"a right-of-way for egress and ingress to Lot 2\"), courts will look to the parties' intent to determine the easement's location. Intent is often evidenced by the parties' conduct (eg, the sister using a driveway across Lot 1 without objection from the brother). Once the location of the easement has been established, the servient-estateowner may relocate the easement at his/her own expense to permit normal use or development of the servient estate. But the new location must be reasonable and may not: violate the easement's terms significantly lessen the easement's utility increase the burden on the dominant-estate owner's use or enjoyment of the easement or frustrate the easement's purpose. Here, the cousin became the servient-estate owner when he succeeded to the brother's title in Lot 1. He later sought to relocate the easement to develop a portion of Lot 1 as a residential subdivision. But the cousin asked the friend, who had succeeded to the sister's title in Lot 2, to bear the expense of building the new driveway. Additionally, the proposed location would significantly lessen the easement's utility by making travel from Lot 2 to the highway more circuitous. Therefore, the cousin should not prevail. (Choice B) The easement's location was not fixed adversely by prescription (ie, OCAN) since the brother did not object to the sister establishing or using the driveway. (Choice C) The cousin's proposal did not satisfy the requirements for relocating the easement, and the friend was not required to suggest an alternative location.\n\n(Choice D) The servient-estate owner is only entitled to select the initial location of an easement if it has not been set by the easement's terms or the circumstances surrounding its creation (eg, the parties' conduct—as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The servient-estate owner may relocate the easement at his/her own expense to permit normal use or development of the servient estate. But this right has limitations—eg, relocation cannot significantly lessen the easement's utility.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.8 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (location and relocation of easements).","educationalObjective":"The servient-estate owner may relocate the easement at his/her own expense to permit normal use or development of the servient estate. But this right has limitations—eg, relocation cannot significantly lessen the easement's utility.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.8 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (location and relocation of easements).","rule":"The servient-estate owner may relocate the easement at his/her own expense to permit normal use or development of the servient estate. But this right has limitations—eg, relocation cannot significantly lessen the easement's utility.","irac":{"issue":"Should the cousin prevail?","rule":"The servient-estate owner may relocate the easement at his/her own expense to permit normal use or development of the servient estate. But this right has limitations—eg, relocation cannot significantly lessen the easement's utility.","analysis":"Location & relocation of easements\n\nLocation Initial location set by:\n\nexpress easement's terms circumstances surrounding easement's creation servient owner, provided location is set within reasonable time & reasonably suited to easement's purpose (if neither of above provisions applies) Relocation Servient owner may relocate (at own expense) to permit normal use/development of servient estate if relocation is reasonable & does not:\n\nviolate express easement's terms significantly lessen easement's utility increase burden on dominant owner's use or enjoyment of easement frustrate easement's purpose The location of an express easement is set by its terms. But when those terms are unclear (eg, \"a right-of-way for egress and ingress to Lot 2\"), courts will look to the parties' intent to determine the easement's location. Intent is often evidenced by the parties' conduct (eg, the sister using a driveway across Lot 1 without objection from the brother). Once the location of the easement has been established, the servient-estateowner may relocate the easement at his/her own expense to permit normal use or development of the servient estate. But the new location must be reasonable and may not: violate the easement's terms significantly lessen the easement's utility increase the burden on the dominant-estate owner's use or enjoyment of the easement or frustrate the easement's purpose. Here, the cousin became the servient-estate owner when he succeeded to the brother's title in Lot 1. He later sought to relocate the easement to develop a portion of Lot 1 as a residential subdivision. But the cousin asked the friend, who had succeeded to the sister's title in Lot 2, to bear the expense of building the new driveway. Additionally, the proposed location would significantly lessen the easement's utility by making travel from Lot 2 to the highway more circuitous. Therefore, the cousin should not prevail. (Choice B) The easement's location was not fixed adversely by prescription (ie, OCAN) since the brother did not object to the sister establishing or using the driveway. (Choice C) The cousin's proposal did not satisfy the requirements for relocating the easement, and the friend was not required to suggest an alternative location.\n\n(Choice D) The servient-estate owner is only entitled to select the initial location of an easement if it has not been set by the easement's terms or the circumstances surrounding its creation (eg, the parties' conduct—as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the location had been established by the acts of the brother and the sister."},"questionZh":"三十年前，一位兄弟拥有 1 号地块，该地块与 2 号地块相邻，费用简单。他执行并向他的妹妹交付了一份名为“产权转让契据”的书面文书。相关部分写道，“[兄弟]确实授予[姐妹]及其继承人，并分配了2号地块的出入通行权。”如果所引用的条款足以产生土地权益，则该文书满足有效授予的所有其他要求。该行为被记录下来。该姐妹拥有与第 1 号地块相邻的第 2 号地块的产权记录。十二年前，一位表弟继承了兄弟在第 1 号地块的产权。七年前，一位朋友通过一份未提及通行权或车道的契约继承了姐姐在第 2 号地块的产权。当这位朋友取得产权时，有一条横跨 1 号地块的车道，有证据表明它经常用于在公共高速公路和 2 号地块之间行驶。 2 号地块确实有一条公共小路临街，但这种通道很少使用，因为它不像高速公路那样方便到达 2 号地块上的住宅。这条车道最初是由姐姐修建的。自从获得所有权以来，这位朋友经常使用该车道。在管辖范围内通过时效取得权利所需的期限为10年。六个月前，表弟通知朋友，他计划将 1 号地块的一部分开发为住宅小区，并且朋友应停止使用该车道。经过一番谈判，表弟提出允许朋友修建另一条车道，以连接拟议土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）的街道。她拒绝了这一提议，理由是从 2 号地块到高速公路会更加迂回。这位朋友对表弟提起了适当的诉讼，以获得对他们各自权利的明确裁决。在该诉讼中，堂兄弟的辩护理由是，兄弟向姐妹授予的地役权的地点是合理的，并且堂兄弟提出的解决方案是合理的。表弟应该占上风吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为这个位置是由兄妹俩的行为所确定的。","B":"不，因为地役权的位置已按规定确定。","C":"是的，因为表兄的提议的合理性是通过朋友拒绝建议任何替代地点而确定的。","D":"是的，因为如果赠款未能确定通行权的位置，则役权所有者有权选择通行权的位置。"},"explanationZh":"地役权的位置和搬迁\n\n位置 初始位置设置为：\n\n明示地役权条款 围绕地役权创建受役权所有人的情况，前提是地点是在合理时间内设定的并且合理地适合地役权的目的（如果上述条款均不适用） 搬迁 如果搬迁合理且不存在以下情况，受役权所有人可以搬迁（自费）以允许受役地产的正常使用/开发：\n\n违反明示地役权的条款 显着降低地役权的效用 增加主要所有者使用或享受地役权的负担 阻碍地役权的目的 明示地役权的位置由其条款规定。但当这些条款不清楚时（例如，“第 2 号地块的出入通行权”），法院将根据双方的意图来确定地役权的位置。意图通常可以通过双方的行为来证明（例如，姐妹使用穿过 1 号地块的车道而兄弟没有反对）。一旦确定了地役权的位置，供役地所有人可以自费重新安置地役权，以允许供役地的正常使用或开发。但新地点必须合理，不得： 违反地役权条款 显着降低地役权的效用 增加占主导地位的地产所有者使用或享受地役权的负担或阻碍地役权的目的。在这里，表弟继承了兄弟在第 1 号地块的所有权后，成为了役用庄园的所有者。后来，他试图重新安置地役权，将第 1 号地块的一部分开发为住宅小区。但表弟要求继承了姐姐在第 2 号地段的所有权的朋友承担修建新车道的费用。此外，拟议的位置将使从 2 号地块到高速公路的交通更加迂回，从而显着降低地役权的效用。所以表弟不宜占上风。 （选择B） 地役权的位置并未通过规定（即OCAN）不利地固定，因为兄弟并不反对姐妹建立或使用车道。 （选项C）表弟的建议不符合地役权迁移的要求，朋友不需要建议替代地点。\n\n（选项 D） 供役权所有人只有在地役权条款或地役权创建环境（例如，当事人的行为——如此处所示）没有规定的情况下才有权选择地役权的初始位置。\n\n教育目标：供役地产所有者可以自费迁移地役权，以保证供役地产的正常使用或开发。但这项权利也有局限性——例如，搬迁不能显着降低地役权的效用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.8（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（地役权的位置和搬迁）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"供役地所有人可以自费迁移地役权，以保证供役地的正常使用或开发。但这项权利也有局限性——例如，搬迁不能显着降低地役权的效用。","iracZh":{"issue":"表弟应该占上风吗？","rule":"供役地所有人可以自费迁移地役权，以保证供役地的正常使用或开发。但这项权利也有局限性——例如，搬迁不能显着降低地役权的效用。","analysis":"地役权的位置和搬迁\n\n位置 初始位置设置为：\n\n明示地役权条款 围绕地役权创建受役权所有人的情况，前提是地点是在合理时间内设定的并且合理地适合地役权的目的（如果上述条款均不适用） 搬迁 如果搬迁合理且不存在以下情况，受役权所有人可以搬迁（自费）以允许受役地产的正常使用/开发：\n\n违反明示地役权的条款 显着降低地役权的效用 增加主要所有者使用或享受地役权的负担 阻碍地役权的目的 明示地役权的位置由其条款规定。但当这些条款不清楚时（例如，“第 2 号地块的出入通行权”），法院将根据双方的意图来确定地役权的位置。意图通常可以通过双方的行为来证明（例如，姐妹使用穿过 1 号地块的车道而兄弟没有反对）。一旦确定了地役权的位置，供役地所有人可以自费重新安置地役权，以允许供役地的正常使用或开发。但新地点必须合理，不得： 违反地役权条款 显着降低地役权的效用 增加占主导地位的地产所有者使用或享受地役权的负担或阻碍地役权的目的。在这里，表弟继承了兄弟在第 1 号地块的所有权后，成为了役用庄园的所有者。后来，他试图重新安置地役权，将第 1 号地块的一部分开发为住宅小区。但表弟要求继承了姐姐在第 2 号地段的所有权的朋友承担修建新车道的费用。此外，拟议的位置将使从 2 号地块到高速公路的交通更加迂回，从而显着降低地役权的效用。所以表弟不宜占上风。 （选择B） 地役权的位置并未通过规定（即OCAN）不利地固定，因为兄弟并不反对姐妹建立或使用车道。 （选项C）表弟的建议不符合地役权迁移的要求，朋友不需要建议替代地点。\n\n（选项 D） 供役权所有人只有在地役权条款或地役权创建环境（例如，当事人的行为——如此处所示）没有规定的情况下才有权选择地役权的初始位置。\n\n教育目标：供役地产所有者可以自费迁移地役权，以保证供役地产的正常使用或开发。但这项权利也有局限性——例如，搬迁不能显着降低地役权的效用。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.8（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（地役权的位置和搬迁）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为这个位置是由兄妹俩的行为所确定的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":195,"sourceQuestionPage":570,"sourceQuestionPages":[570],"sourceExplanationPages":[571,572],"sourceAnswerPages":[571,572],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-196","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A franchisor who owned two adjoining parcels conveyed the eastern parcel to an entrepreneur. The franchisor covenanted in the deed to the entrepreneur that when she, the franchisor, sold the western parcel, she would impose restrictive covenants to prohibit uses that would compete with the filling station that the entrepreneur intended to construct and operate on the eastern parcel. The deed was not recorded. The entrepreneur constructed and operated a filling station on the eastern parcel and then conveyed the eastern parcel to his station manager, who continued the filling station use. The deed did not refer to the restrictive covenant and was promptly and properly recorded. The franchisor then conveyed the western parcel to her friend, who knew about the franchisor's covenant with the entrepreneur to impose a covenant prohibiting the filling station use but nonetheless completed the transaction when he noted that no such covenant was contained in the franchisor's deed to him. The friend began to construct a filling station on the western parcel. The station manager brought an appropriate action to enjoin the friend from using the western parcel for filling station purposes. If the station manager prevails, what will be the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"Business-related restrictive covenants are favored in the law.","B":"The friend had actual knowledge of the covenant to impose restrictions.","C":"The friend has constructive notice of the possibility of the covenant.","D":"The friend is bound by the covenant because of the doctrine of negative reciprocal covenants."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes\n\nReal covenants Equitable servitudes\n\n(enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief)\n\nFor burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied\n\nWriting Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice privity privity Notice Notice No notice required Real covenants and equitable servitudes are promises to do or not do something on the land and can be enforced against the parties who made the promise and their successors in interest. The distinction turns on the type of remedy sought. Money damages are available for breach of a real covenant, while equitable relief (eg, injunction—as seen here) is available for equitable servitudes. Therefore, the station manager will prevail if he can satisfy the following requirements for an express equitable servitude: Writing – covenant is expressed in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, deed from franchisor to entrepreneur)\n\nIntent to run – promising parties intended that the covenant run to (ie, bind) their successors in interest (eg, agreeing to impose restrictive covenants on future buyers) Touch and concern – covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of the land (eg, prohibits uses that would compete with the filling station)\n\nNotice – servient (ie, burdened) estate owner has actual, record, or inquiry notice of the covenant (eg, friend had actual knowledge of the covenant between franchisor and entrepreneur)\n\nTherefore, if the station manager prevails, it will likely be because the friend had actual knowledge of this covenant. (Choice A) Restrictive covenants—including those that are business-related—are generally disfavored in the law because they interfere with the free use and alienability of land. (Choice C) The friend did not have constructive (ie, inquiry or record) notice of the covenant because (1) there were no facts suggesting that there would be a restriction\n\nprohibiting use of the land for a filling station and (2) the deed containing the covenant was not recorded. (Choice D) Negative reciprocal covenants* can be implied from a common scheme (eg, residential subdivision) when there is no express covenant between the parties—not seen here. *Also called implied equitable servitudes or implied reciprocal negative easements.\n\nEducational objective: To enforce an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run to their successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient-estate owner must have notice.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 5.2 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (persons to whom an appurtenant benefit or burden runs).","educationalObjective":"To enforce an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run to their successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient-estate owner must have notice.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 5.2 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (persons to whom an appurtenant benefit or burden runs).","rule":"To enforce an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run to their successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient-estate owner must have notice.","irac":{"issue":"If the station manager prevails, what will be the most likely reason?","rule":"To enforce an express equitable servitude, (1) the covenant must be in writing, (2) the promising parties must intend for it to run to their successors in interest, (3) it must touch and concern the land, and (4) the servient-estate owner must have notice.","analysis":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes\n\nReal covenants Equitable servitudes\n\n(enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief)\n\nFor burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied\n\nWriting Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice privity privity Notice Notice No notice required Real covenants and equitable servitudes are promises to do or not do something on the land and can be enforced against the parties who made the promise and their successors in interest. The distinction turns on the type of remedy sought. Money damages are available for breach of a real covenant, while equitable relief (eg, injunction—as seen here) is available for equitable servitudes. Therefore, the station manager will prevail if he can satisfy the following requirements for an express equitable servitude: Writing – covenant is expressed in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, deed from franchisor to entrepreneur)\n\nIntent to run – promising parties intended that the covenant run to (ie, bind) their successors in interest (eg, agreeing to impose restrictive covenants on future buyers) Touch and concern – covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of the land (eg, prohibits uses that would compete with the filling station)\n\nNotice – servient (ie, burdened) estate owner has actual, record, or inquiry notice of the covenant (eg, friend had actual knowledge of the covenant between franchisor and entrepreneur)\n\nTherefore, if the station manager prevails, it will likely be because the friend had actual knowledge of this covenant. (Choice A) Restrictive covenants—including those that are business-related—are generally disfavored in the law because they interfere with the free use and alienability of land. (Choice C) The friend did not have constructive (ie, inquiry or record) notice of the covenant because (1) there were no facts suggesting that there would be a restriction\n\nprohibiting use of the land for a filling station and (2) the deed containing the covenant was not recorded. (Choice D) Negative reciprocal covenants* can be implied from a common scheme (eg, residential subdivision) when there is no express covenant between the parties—not seen here. *Also called implied equitable servitudes or implied reciprocal negative easements.","conclusion":"Answer B: The friend had actual knowledge of the covenant to impose restrictions."},"questionZh":"一位拥有两块相邻地块的特许人将东部地块转让给了一位企业家。特许人在与企业家的契约中承诺，当特许人出售西部地块时，她将施加限制性契约，禁止与企业家打算在东部地块上建造和运营的加油站竞争的用途。该行为没有被记录。该企业家在东部地块上建造并运营了一个加油站，然后将东部地块运送给他的加油站经理，后者继续使用加油站。该契约并未提及限制性契约，并且已被及时、正确地记录。然后，特许人将西部包裹转交给她的朋友，她的朋友知道特许人与企业家签订了禁止使用加油站的契约，但当他注意到特许人给他的契约中没有包含这样的契约时，她还是完成了交易。朋友开始在西边的地块上建造一个加油站。加油站经理采取了适当行动，禁止该朋友将西部包裹用于加油站用途。如果站长胜诉，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"法律倾向于与业务相关的限制性契约。","B":"这位朋友确实了解施加限制的契约。","C":"朋友对立约的可能性有建设性的认识。","D":"由于消极互惠契约的教义，朋友受到契约的约束。"},"explanationZh":"真实契约和公平劳役的要素\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant） 公平的劳役\n\n（可通过金钱赔偿强制执行）（可通过衡平法救济强制执行）\n\n为了负担而运行 为了利益而运行明示暗示\n\n写作 写作 写作 意图创造共同 意图运行 意图运行 意图运行计划 接触与关注 接触与关注 接触与限制性劳役 水平与垂直 有限垂直关注 通知 相互关系 通知 通知 无需通知 真正契约和衡平法劳役是在土地上做或不做某事的承诺，可以对做出承诺的各方及其利益继承人强制执行。区别取决于所寻求的补救措施的类型。违反土地实质契约（real covenant）可以进行金钱赔偿，而公平的救济（例如，禁令——如此处所见）则可以用于公平的劳役。因此，如果车站经理能够满足明确的公平劳役的以下要求，则他将获胜： 书面形式 - 契约以符合欺诈法规的书面形式表达（例如，特许人给企业家的契约）\n\n经营意图 – 承诺各方希望契约能够影响（即约束）其继承者的利益（例如，同意对未来买家施加限制性契约） 接触和关注 – 契约涉及土地的使用、享有或占用（例如，禁止与加油站竞争的用途）\n\n通知 – 服务（即负担）财产所有者拥有该契约的实际、记录或查询通知（例如，朋友实际了解特许人和企业家之间的契约）\n\n因此，如果站长胜诉，很可能是因为这位朋友确实了解该契约。 （选项 A）限制性契约（包括与商业相关的契约）通常不受法律青睐，因为它们会干扰土地的自由使用和转让。 （选项C）该朋友没有关于该契约的建设性（即查询或记录）通知，因为（1）没有事实表明会有限制\n\n禁止将土地用于加油站，并且 (2) 未记录载有该契约的契约。 （选项D）当双方之间没有明确的契约时，消极的互惠契约*可以从共同方案（例如住宅细分）中隐含——此处未见。 *也称为隐含公平劳役或隐含互惠负地役权。\n\n教育目标：为了执行明确的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将契约传给其利益继承人，(3) 契约必须触及并涉及土地，(4) 劳役财产所有者必须予以通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 5.2（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（享有附属利益或负担的人员）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为了执行明示的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将其转嫁给其利益继承人，(3) 契约必须触及并涉及土地，以及 (4) 劳役财产所有者必须得到通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果站长胜诉，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"为了执行明示的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将其转嫁给其利益继承人，(3) 契约必须触及并涉及土地，以及 (4) 劳役财产所有者必须得到通知。","analysis":"真实契约和公平劳役的要素\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant） 公平的劳役\n\n（可通过金钱赔偿强制执行）（可通过衡平法救济强制执行）\n\n为了负担而运行 为了利益而运行明示暗示\n\n写作 写作 写作 意图创造共同 意图运行 意图运行 意图运行计划 接触与关注 接触与关注 接触与限制性劳役 水平与垂直 有限垂直关注 通知 相互关系 通知 通知 无需通知 真正契约和衡平法劳役是在土地上做或不做某事的承诺，可以对做出承诺的各方及其利益继承人强制执行。区别取决于所寻求的补救措施的类型。违反土地实质契约（real covenant）可以进行金钱赔偿，而公平的救济（例如，禁令——如此处所见）则可以用于公平的劳役。因此，如果车站经理能够满足明确的公平劳役的以下要求，则他将获胜： 书面形式 - 契约以符合欺诈法规的书面形式表达（例如，特许人给企业家的契约）\n\n经营意图 – 承诺各方希望契约能够影响（即约束）其继承者的利益（例如，同意对未来买家施加限制性契约） 接触和关注 – 契约涉及土地的使用、享有或占用（例如，禁止与加油站竞争的用途）\n\n通知 – 服务（即负担）财产所有者拥有该契约的实际、记录或查询通知（例如，朋友实际了解特许人和企业家之间的契约）\n\n因此，如果站长胜诉，很可能是因为这位朋友确实了解该契约。 （选项 A）限制性契约（包括与商业相关的契约）通常不受法律青睐，因为它们会干扰土地的自由使用和转让。 （选项C）该朋友没有关于该契约的建设性（即查询或记录）通知，因为（1）没有事实表明会有限制\n\n禁止将土地用于加油站，并且 (2) 未记录载有该契约的契约。 （选项D）当双方之间没有明确的契约时，消极的互惠契约*可以从共同方案（例如住宅细分）中隐含——此处未见。 *也称为隐含公平劳役或隐含互惠负地役权。\n\n教育目标：为了执行明确的公平劳役，(1) 契约必须采用书面形式，(2) 承诺方必须打算将契约传给其利益继承人，(3) 契约必须触及并涉及土地，(4) 劳役财产所有者必须予以通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 5.2（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（享有附属利益或负担的人员）。","conclusion":"答案 B：这位朋友确实了解施加限制的契约。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"common scheme","ipa":"/kˈɑmən skˈim/","syllables":"com·mon scheme","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"共同开发方案：开发者对一片土地实行统一限制的总体计划","contextualMeaning":"共同开发方案：开发者对一片土地实行统一限制的总体计划","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) Negative reciprocal covenants* can be implied from a common scheme (eg, residential subdivision) when there is no express covenant between the parties—not seen here.","synonyms":["common development plan"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":196,"sourceQuestionPage":573,"sourceQuestionPages":[573],"sourceExplanationPages":[574,575],"sourceAnswerPages":[574,575],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-197","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Three months ago, a buyer agreed in writing to buy a seller's single-family residence for $110,000. The buyer paid the seller a $5,000 deposit to be applied to the purchase price. The contract stated that the seller had the right at his option to retain the deposit as liquidated damages in the event of the buyer's default. The closing was to have taken place last week. Six weeks ago, the buyer was notified by his employer that he was to be transferred to another job 1,000 miles away. The buyer immediately notified the seller that he could not close, and therefore he demanded the return of his $5,000 deposit. The seller refused and waited until after the contract closing date to sell the residence to a real estate broker for $108,000. The broker paid the full purchase price and immediately recorded his deed. The broker knew of the prior contract with the buyer. In an appropriate action, the buyer seeks to recover the $5,000 deposit from the seller. What is the most probable result?","choices":{"A":"The seller may keep the $5,000 deposit, because the buyer breached the contract.","B":"The seller must return $3,000 to the buyer, because the seller's damages were only $2,000.","C":"The seller must return the $5,000 to the buyer, because the buyer was legally justified in not completing the contract.","D":"The seller must return the $5,000 to the buyer, because the seller can no longer carry out his contract with the buyer."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Seller's remedies\n\nRescission & Rescind (ie, cancel) agreement & recover possession of property restitution Rental value & physical damages are recoverable Buyer not liable for purchase price Specific Seek an injunction ordering buyer to pay agreed purchase price performance May be unavailable if money damages provide adequate compensation & property is not unique eg, condominium identical to other units in complex Damages Loss of bargain = contract price – market value at time of breach Liquidated damages = amount designated in the sales contract as compensation for breach Retain buyer's deposit (up to amount of seller's actual damages) Incidental & consequential damages that were contemplated during sales contract or reasonably foreseeable Land-sale contracts can include a liquidated damages provision, which allows a seller to recover a certain sum in the event of the buyer's breach. These provisions are enforceable if the liquidated amount bears a reasonable relation to anticipated damages. Courts have consistently held that an amount less than 15% of the purchase price is reasonable. However, an amount that exceeds this percentage may constitute an unenforceable penalty. Here, the buyer breached the contract when he notified the seller that he could not close. The land-sale contract allows the seller to recover the $5,000 deposit as liquidated damages in the event of the buyer's breach. Since this amount is around 5% of the $110,000 purchase price, the liquidated-damages provision is reasonable and will likely be enforced. Therefore, the most probable result is that the seller will be permitted to keep the $5,000 deposit. (Choice B) Had the liquidated-damages provision been unenforceable, the seller would have been required to return the deposit and could only recover his actual damages, which amounted to $2,000. (Choice C) Changed circumstances may provide legal justification for not completing a contract if those circumstances make performance impossible or impracticable. However, the buyer's job transfer does not make it impossible or impracticable to purchase the residence since he can later sell the home.\n\n(Choice D) The seller could have carried out the contract with the buyer at the time of closing since he still had title to the residence at that time. The fact that he cannot carry out his contract with the buyer at this time is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: A liquidated damages provision is enforceable if the amount bears a reasonable relation to anticipated damages. An amount less than 15% of the purchase price is likely reasonable.\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 478 (2020) (seller's recovery of liquidated damages).","educationalObjective":"A liquidated damages provision is enforceable if the amount bears a reasonable relation to anticipated damages. An amount less than 15% of the purchase price is likely reasonable.","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 478 (2020) (seller's recovery of liquidated damages).","rule":"A liquidated damages provision is enforceable if the amount bears a reasonable relation to anticipated damages. An amount less than 15% of the purchase price is likely reasonable.","irac":{"issue":"What is the most probable result?","rule":"A liquidated damages provision is enforceable if the amount bears a reasonable relation to anticipated damages. An amount less than 15% of the purchase price is likely reasonable.","analysis":"Seller's remedies\n\nRescission & Rescind (ie, cancel) agreement & recover possession of property restitution Rental value & physical damages are recoverable Buyer not liable for purchase price Specific Seek an injunction ordering buyer to pay agreed purchase price performance May be unavailable if money damages provide adequate compensation & property is not unique eg, condominium identical to other units in complex Damages Loss of bargain = contract price – market value at time of breach Liquidated damages = amount designated in the sales contract as compensation for breach Retain buyer's deposit (up to amount of seller's actual damages) Incidental & consequential damages that were contemplated during sales contract or reasonably foreseeable Land-sale contracts can include a liquidated damages provision, which allows a seller to recover a certain sum in the event of the buyer's breach. These provisions are enforceable if the liquidated amount bears a reasonable relation to anticipated damages. Courts have consistently held that an amount less than 15% of the purchase price is reasonable. However, an amount that exceeds this percentage may constitute an unenforceable penalty. Here, the buyer breached the contract when he notified the seller that he could not close. The land-sale contract allows the seller to recover the $5,000 deposit as liquidated damages in the event of the buyer's breach. Since this amount is around 5% of the $110,000 purchase price, the liquidated-damages provision is reasonable and will likely be enforced. Therefore, the most probable result is that the seller will be permitted to keep the $5,000 deposit. (Choice B) Had the liquidated-damages provision been unenforceable, the seller would have been required to return the deposit and could only recover his actual damages, which amounted to $2,000. (Choice C) Changed circumstances may provide legal justification for not completing a contract if those circumstances make performance impossible or impracticable. However, the buyer's job transfer does not make it impossible or impracticable to purchase the residence since he can later sell the home.\n\n(Choice D) The seller could have carried out the contract with the buyer at the time of closing since he still had title to the residence at that time. The fact that he cannot carry out his contract with the buyer at this time is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer A: The seller may keep the $5,000 deposit, because the buyer breached the contract."},"questionZh":"三个月前，一名买家书面同意以 11 万美元的价格购买一名卖家的单户住宅。买方向卖方支付了 5,000 美元的定金，用于支付购买价格。合同规定，如果买方违约，卖方有权选择保留定金作为违约金。交易原定于上周完成。六周前，买家接到雇主的通知，要调到 1,000 英里外的另一份工作。买家立即通知卖家无法成交，并要求返还5000美元的押金。卖方拒绝了，并等到合同截止日期后才以 108,000 美元的价格将住宅出售给房地产经纪人。经纪人支付了全额购买价并立即记录了他的行为。经纪人知道之前与买方签订的合同。通过适当的行动，买方寻求向卖方收回 5,000 美元的押金。最可能的结果是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"由于买方违约，卖方可以保留 5,000 美元的定金。","B":"卖方必须向买方退还 3,000 美元，因为卖方的损失仅为 2,000 美元。","C":"卖方必须将 5,000 美元退还给买方，因为买方不履行合同在法律上是合理的。","D":"卖方必须将 5,000 美元退还给买方，因为卖方无法再履行与买方的合同。"},"explanationZh":"卖家的补救措施\n\n解除和解除（即取消）协议并恢复财产所有权归还 租金价值和有形损害可收回 买方不对购买价格承担具体责任 寻求禁令，命令买方支付约定的购买价格履约 如果金钱损害提供足够的赔偿且财产不是唯一的，例如，公寓与综合体中的其他单元相同，则可能不可用 损害赔偿 议价损失 = 合同价格 – 违约时的市场价值 违约赔偿金 = 销售合同中指定的违约赔偿金额 保留买方押金（最高可达卖方实际损害赔偿金额）销售合同或合理可预见的土地销售合同中预期的附带和间接损害可包括违约赔偿金条款，该条款允许卖方在买方违约时获得一定金额的赔偿。如果清算金额与预期损失具有合理关系，则这些规定可以强制执行。法院一贯认为低于购买价格 15% 的金额是合理的。但是，超过此百分比的金额可能会构成无法执行的处罚。在此，买方通知卖方无法成交，从而违反了合同。土地出售合同允许卖方在买方违约时收回 5,000 美元的押金作为违约金。由于该金额约为 110,000 美元购买价格的 5%，因此违约金条款是合理的，并且很可能会得到执行。因此，最可能的结果是卖家将被允许保留 5,000 美元的押金。 （选项B）如果违约金条款无法执行，卖方将被要求退还押金，并且只能收回其实际损失，金额为2,000美元。 （选择 C）如果情况发生变化使得履行不可能或不切实际，则可以为不履行合同提供法律理由。然而，买家的工作调动并不意味着购买住宅变得不可能或不切实际，因为他可以稍后出售房屋。\n\n（选项D） 卖方可以在成交时履行与买方的合同，因为当时他仍然拥有该住宅的所有权。他目前无法履行与买方的合同这一事实无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：如果违约金金额与预期损害赔偿存在合理关系，则违约金条款是可执行的。低于购买价格 15% 的金额可能是合理的。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 478 (2020)（卖方追偿违约金）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果违约金金额与预期损害赔偿有合理关系，则违约金条款可以强制执行。低于购买价格 15% 的金额可能是合理的。","iracZh":{"issue":"最可能的结果是什么？","rule":"如果违约金金额与预期损害赔偿有合理关系，则违约金条款可以强制执行。低于购买价格 15% 的金额可能是合理的。","analysis":"卖家的补救措施\n\n解除和解除（即取消）协议并恢复财产所有权归还 租金价值和有形损害可收回 买方不对购买价格承担具体责任 寻求禁令，命令买方支付约定的购买价格履约 如果金钱损害提供足够的赔偿且财产不是唯一的，例如，公寓与综合体中的其他单元相同，则可能不可用 损害赔偿 议价损失 = 合同价格 – 违约时的市场价值 违约赔偿金 = 销售合同中指定的违约赔偿金额 保留买方押金（最高可达卖方实际损害赔偿金额）销售合同或合理可预见的土地销售合同中预期的附带和间接损害可包括违约赔偿金条款，该条款允许卖方在买方违约时获得一定金额的赔偿。如果清算金额与预期损失具有合理关系，则这些规定可以强制执行。法院一贯认为低于购买价格 15% 的金额是合理的。但是，超过此百分比的金额可能会构成无法执行的处罚。在此，买方通知卖方无法成交，从而违反了合同。土地出售合同允许卖方在买方违约时收回 5,000 美元的押金作为违约金。由于该金额约为 110,000 美元购买价格的 5%，因此违约金条款是合理的，并且很可能会得到执行。因此，最可能的结果是卖家将被允许保留 5,000 美元的押金。 （选项B）如果违约金条款无法执行，卖方将被要求退还押金，并且只能收回其实际损失，金额为2,000美元。 （选择 C）如果情况发生变化使得履行不可能或不切实际，则可以为不履行合同提供法律理由。然而，买家的工作调动并不意味着购买住宅变得不可能或不切实际，因为他可以稍后出售房屋。\n\n（选项D） 卖方可以在成交时履行与买方的合同，因为当时他仍然拥有该住宅的所有权。他目前无法履行与买方的合同这一事实无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：如果违约金金额与预期损害赔偿存在合理关系，则违约金条款是可执行的。低于购买价格 15% 的金额可能是合理的。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 478 (2020)（卖方追偿违约金）。","conclusion":"答案 A：由于买方违约，卖方可以保留 5,000 美元的定金。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":197,"sourceQuestionPage":576,"sourceQuestionPages":[576],"sourceExplanationPages":[577,578],"sourceAnswerPages":[577,578],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-198","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A large tract of land is owned by a religious order. The order erected a large residential building on the land where its members reside. The order's land is surrounded by rural residential properties and its only access to a public way is afforded by an easement over a strip of land 30 feet wide. The easement was granted to the order by deed from a woman who owned one of the adjacent residential properties. The order built a driveway on the strip, and the easement was used for 20 years without incident or objection. Last year, as permitted by the applicable zoning ordinance, the order constructed a 200-bed nursing home and a parking lot on its land, using all of the land that was available for such development. The nursing home was very successful, and on Sundays visitors to the nursing home overflowed the parking facilities on the land and parked all along the driveway from early in the morning through the evening hours. After two Sundays of the resulting congestion and inconvenience, the woman erected a barrier across the driveway on Sundays preventing any use of the driveway by anyone seeking access to the order's land. The order objected. The woman brought an appropriate action to terminate the easement. In this action, for whom will the court likely hold?","choices":{"A":"The order, because expanded use of the easement does not terminate the easement.","B":"The order, because the woman's use of self-help denies her the right to equitable relief.","C":"The woman, because the order excessively expanded the use of the dominant tenement.","D":"The woman, because the parking on the driveway exceeded the scope of the easement."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Permissible use of easement\n\nType Easement holder's use Servient owner's use\n\nExpress Uses intended by parties OR Use consistent with easement's easement reasonably necessary to use/enjoy terms AND does not unreasonably easement if intent is unknown interfere with its use/enjoyment Expansion of use permitted Easement by Limited to scope of prior use prior use No expansion of use Easement by Normal uses needed for dominant necessity estate (eg, ingress/egress, utility lines) Expansion of use permitted Prescriptive Limited to scope of adverse use easement during prescriptive period No expansion of use An easement is generally a nonpossessory right to use another's land (ie, servient estate) for a specific purpose. Unless limited by the express terms of the easement, the easement holder has the right to use the servient estate in any manner that is reasonably necessary to use and enjoy the easement—eg, building a driveway on the easement and using the driveway for parking (Choice D). An easement also anticipates reasonable and natural development of the easement holder's land (ie, dominant estate). Therefore, the easement holder may increase the manner, frequency, and intensity of the easement's use—so long as that increase does not unreasonably damage or interfere with the use or enjoyment of the servient estate. Here, the order reasonably developed its land as permitted by the zoning ordinance by constructing a 200-bed nursing home and parking lot on it. This caused an increase in the intensity and frequency of the easement's use. But that use was not excessive because there is no evidence that it unreasonably damaged or interfered with the woman's servient estate (Choice C). Even if it did, available remedies would include self-help abatement (eg, blocking off the easement), damages, or an injunction—not termination or forfeiture of the easement (Choice B).\n\nEducational objective: An easement holder may increase the manner, frequency, or intensity of an easement's use so long as that increase does not unreasonably damage or interfere with the use or enjoyment of the servient estate.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.10 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement holder's right to use easement).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.9 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (servient owner's right to use servient estate).","educationalObjective":"An easement holder may increase the manner, frequency, or intensity of an easement's use so long as that increase does not unreasonably damage or interfere with the use or enjoyment of the servient estate.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.10 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement holder's right to use easement).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 4.9 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (servient owner's right to use servient estate).","rule":"An easement holder may increase the manner, frequency, or intensity of an easement's use so long as that increase does not unreasonably damage or interfere with the use or enjoyment of the servient estate.","irac":{"issue":"In this action, for whom will the court likely hold?","rule":"An easement holder may increase the manner, frequency, or intensity of an easement's use so long as that increase does not unreasonably damage or interfere with the use or enjoyment of the servient estate.","analysis":"Permissible use of easement\n\nType Easement holder's use Servient owner's use\n\nExpress Uses intended by parties OR Use consistent with easement's easement reasonably necessary to use/enjoy terms AND does not unreasonably easement if intent is unknown interfere with its use/enjoyment Expansion of use permitted Easement by Limited to scope of prior use prior use No expansion of use Easement by Normal uses needed for dominant necessity estate (eg, ingress/egress, utility lines) Expansion of use permitted Prescriptive Limited to scope of adverse use easement during prescriptive period No expansion of use An easement is generally a nonpossessory right to use another's land (ie, servient estate) for a specific purpose. Unless limited by the express terms of the easement, the easement holder has the right to use the servient estate in any manner that is reasonably necessary to use and enjoy the easement—eg, building a driveway on the easement and using the driveway for parking (Choice D). An easement also anticipates reasonable and natural development of the easement holder's land (ie, dominant estate). Therefore, the easement holder may increase the manner, frequency, and intensity of the easement's use—so long as that increase does not unreasonably damage or interfere with the use or enjoyment of the servient estate. Here, the order reasonably developed its land as permitted by the zoning ordinance by constructing a 200-bed nursing home and parking lot on it. This caused an increase in the intensity and frequency of the easement's use. But that use was not excessive because there is no evidence that it unreasonably damaged or interfered with the woman's servient estate (Choice C). Even if it did, available remedies would include self-help abatement (eg, blocking off the easement), damages, or an injunction—not termination or forfeiture of the easement (Choice B).","conclusion":"Answer A: The order, because expanded use of the easement does not terminate the easement."},"questionZh":"大片土地归宗教团体所有。该命令在其成员居住的土地上建造了一座大型住宅楼。该骑士团的土地周围都是农村住宅区，通往公共道路的唯一通道是通过一块 30 英尺宽的土地上的地役权提供的。地役权是由一名拥有相邻住宅物业的妇女通过契约授予的。该命令在地带上修建了一条车道，地役权使用了 20 年，没有发生任何事件或异议。去年，根据适用的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例的允许，该命令在其土地上建造了一座拥有 200 个床位的疗养院和一个停车场，并使用了所有可用于此类开发的土地。这家疗养院非常成功，周日，来疗养院的游客挤满了土地上的停车设施，从清晨到晚上都把车停在车道上。在经历了两个周日造成的拥堵和不便之后，这名妇女在周日在车道上竖起了一道屏障，阻止任何想要进入该命令土地的人使用车道。该命令表示反对。该妇女提起适当诉讼终止地役权。在这次诉讼中，法院可能会为谁辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"该命令是因为地役权的扩大使用并不终止地役权。","B":"该命令是因为该妇女的自助行为剥夺了她获得公平救济的权利。","C":"女人，因为命令过度扩大了主导物业的使用。","D":"这名女子，因为车道上的停车超出了地役权的范围。"},"explanationZh":"允许使用地役权\n\n类型 地役权持有人的使用 仆人所有者的使用\n\n明确使用 各方有意使用 或 符合使用/享受地役权合理必要条件的使用，并且如果意图未知，则不会无理地役权干扰其使用/享受 允许扩大使用范围 地役权 仅限于先前使用的范围 在先使用 不扩大使用 地役权按主要必要性房地产所需的正常用途（例如，入口/出口、公用设施线路） 允许扩大使用 规定性 仅限于规定期间的不利使用地役权范围 不扩大使用范围使用 地役权通常是出于特定目的使用他人土地（即役用地产）的非占有性权利。除非受地役权明确条款的限制，地役权持有人有权以使用和享受地役权合理必要的任何方式使用服务性房地产，例如，在地役权上修建车道并使用车道停车（选择 D）。地役权还预期地役权持有人的土地（即主导财产）的合理和自然的开发。因此，地役权持有人可以增加地役权使用的方式、频率和强度，只要这种增加不会不合理地损害或干扰使用或享受使用权。在这里，该命令按照土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例的允许合理开发了土地，在上面建造了一座拥有200个床位的疗养院和停车场。这导致地役权使用强度和频率的增加。但这种使用并不过分，因为没有证据表明它不合理地损害或干扰了妇女的奴役财产（选项C）。即使确实如此，可用的补救措施也包括自助减免（例如，封锁地役权）、损害赔偿或禁令，而不是终止或没收地役权（选择 B）。\n\n教育目标：地役权持有人可以增加地役权使用的方式、频率或强度，只要这种增加不会不合理地损害或干扰使用或享受使用权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.10 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（地役权持有人使用地役权的权利）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.9（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（役役所有者使用役役财产的权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"地役权持有人可以增加地役权使用的方式、频率或强度，只要这种增加不会不合理地损害或干扰使用或享受使用权。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这次诉讼中，法院可能会为谁辩护？","rule":"地役权持有人可以增加地役权使用的方式、频率或强度，只要这种增加不会不合理地损害或干扰使用或享受使用权。","analysis":"允许使用地役权\n\n类型 地役权持有人的使用 仆人所有者的使用\n\n明确使用 各方有意使用 或 符合使用/享受地役权合理必要条件的使用，并且如果意图未知，则不会无理地役权干扰其使用/享受 允许扩大使用范围 地役权 仅限于先前使用的范围 在先使用 不扩大使用 地役权按主要必要性房地产所需的正常用途（例如，入口/出口、公用设施线路） 允许扩大使用 规定性 仅限于规定期间的不利使用地役权范围 不扩大使用范围使用 地役权通常是出于特定目的使用他人土地（即役用地产）的非占有性权利。除非受地役权明确条款的限制，地役权持有人有权以使用和享受地役权合理必要的任何方式使用服务性房地产，例如，在地役权上修建车道并使用车道停车（选择 D）。地役权还预期地役权持有人的土地（即主导财产）的合理和自然的开发。因此，地役权持有人可以增加地役权使用的方式、频率和强度，只要这种增加不会不合理地损害或干扰使用或享受使用权。在这里，该命令按照土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例的允许合理开发了土地，在上面建造了一座拥有200个床位的疗养院和停车场。这导致地役权使用强度和频率的增加。但这种使用并不过分，因为没有证据表明它不合理地损害或干扰了妇女的奴役财产（选项C）。即使确实如此，可用的补救措施也包括自助减免（例如，封锁地役权）、损害赔偿或禁令，而不是终止或没收地役权（选择 B）。\n\n教育目标：地役权持有人可以增加地役权使用的方式、频率或强度，只要这种增加不会不合理地损害或干扰使用或享受使用权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.10 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（地役权持有人使用地役权的权利）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 4.9（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（役役所有者使用役役财产的权利）。","conclusion":"答案 A：该命令是因为地役权的扩大使用并不终止地役权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"dominant tenement","ipa":"/dˈɑmənənt tˈɛnəmənt/","syllables":"dom·i·nant ten·e·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"优势地：享有附属地役权利益的土地","contextualMeaning":"优势地：享有附属地役权利益的土地","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The woman, because the order excessively expanded the use of the dominant tenement.","synonyms":["dominant estate"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":198,"sourceQuestionPage":579,"sourceQuestionPages":[579],"sourceExplanationPages":[580,581],"sourceAnswerPages":[580,581],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-199","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A man contacted his lawyer regarding his right to use a path that was on his neighbor's vacant land. Fifteen years ago, after part of a path located on his land and connecting his cabin to the public highway washed out, the man cleared a small part of his neighbor's land and rerouted a section of the path through the neighbor's land. Twelve years ago, the neighbor leased her land to some hunters. For the next 12 years, the hunters and the man who had rerouted the path used the path for access to the highway. A month ago, the neighbor discovered that part of the path was on her land. The neighbor told the man that she had not given him permission to cross her land and that she would be closing the rerouted path after 90 days. The man's land and the neighbor's land have never been in common ownership. The period of time necessary to acquire rights by prescription in the jurisdiction is 10 years. The period of time necessary to acquire title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 10 years. What should the lawyer tell the man concerning his right to use the rerouted path on the neighbor's land?","choices":{"A":"The man has an easement by necessity to use the path.","B":"The man has an easement by prescription to use the path.","C":"The man has fee title by adverse possession of the land included in the path.","D":"The man has no right to use the path."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An easement is a nonpossessory interest in land that gives the easement holder the right to use another's land for a limited purpose (eg, to use the path). One type of easement is an easement by prescription, which is acquired through use that is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Actual – use of the land Nonpermissive – hostile and adverse to the owner (ie, without the owner's permission) Unlike with adverse possession, the use need not be exclusive of the owner or other users—including the owner's tenants (eg, the hunters). Here, the man cleared a small portion of his neighbor's land and created a path through the land. For 12 years—which exceeds the 10-year statutory period—the man openly and continuously used the path to access the highway. And since he did so without the neighbor's permission, the man acquired an easement by prescription to use the path (Choice D). (Choice A) An easement by necessity arises when two parcels of land are separated from common ownership (not seen here), making it absolutely necessary for the owner of one parcel to cross the other parcel to access the land. (Choice C) To acquire title by adverse possession, a person must have exclusive possession of the land—mere use is not enough.\n\nEducational objective: To acquire an easement by prescription, a person must use another's land in a way that is (1) open and notorious, (2) continuous, (3) actual, and (4) nonpermissive.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes §§ 2.16–2.17 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by prescription).","educationalObjective":"To acquire an easement by prescription, a person must use another's land in a way that is (1) open and notorious, (2) continuous, (3) actual, and (4) nonpermissive.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes §§ 2.16–2.17 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by prescription).","rule":"To acquire an easement by prescription, a person must use another's land in a way that is (1) open and notorious, (2) continuous, (3) actual, and (4) nonpermissive.","irac":{"issue":"What should the lawyer tell the man concerning his right to use the rerouted path on the neighbor's land?","rule":"To acquire an easement by prescription, a person must use another's land in a way that is (1) open and notorious, (2) continuous, (3) actual, and (4) nonpermissive.","analysis":"An easement is a nonpossessory interest in land that gives the easement holder the right to use another's land for a limited purpose (eg, to use the path). One type of easement is an easement by prescription, which is acquired through use that is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Actual – use of the land Nonpermissive – hostile and adverse to the owner (ie, without the owner's permission) Unlike with adverse possession, the use need not be exclusive of the owner or other users—including the owner's tenants (eg, the hunters). Here, the man cleared a small portion of his neighbor's land and created a path through the land. For 12 years—which exceeds the 10-year statutory period—the man openly and continuously used the path to access the highway. And since he did so without the neighbor's permission, the man acquired an easement by prescription to use the path (Choice D). (Choice A) An easement by necessity arises when two parcels of land are separated from common ownership (not seen here), making it absolutely necessary for the owner of one parcel to cross the other parcel to access the land. (Choice C) To acquire title by adverse possession, a person must have exclusive possession of the land—mere use is not enough.","conclusion":"Answer B: The man has an easement by prescription to use the path."},"questionZh":"一名男子联系了他的律师，询问他有权使用邻居空地上的一条小路。十五年前，在他的土地上连接他的小屋和高速公路的部分道路被冲毁后，该男子清理了邻居土地的一小部分，并重新规划了穿过邻居土地的一段道路。十二年前，邻居把她的土地租给了一些猎人。在接下来的 12 年里，猎人和改道的人一直使用这条路通往高速公路。一个月前，邻居发现部分道路位于她的土地上。邻居告诉该男子，她没有允许他穿越她的土地，并且她将在 90 天后关闭改道的道路。该男子的土地和邻居的土地从未属于共同所有。在管辖范围内通过时效取得权利所需的期限为10年。在管辖范围内通过逆权占有获得所有权所需的时间为 10 年。关于该男子使用邻居土地上改道道路的权利，律师应该告诉他什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该男子因需要使用该道路而拥有地役权。","B":"该男子根据规定拥有使用该路径的地役权。","C":"该男子通过逆权占有路径中的土地而拥有收费所有权。","D":"该人无权使用该路径。"},"explanationZh":"地役权是对土地的非占有性权益，它赋予地役权持有人出于有限目的（例如，使用道路）使用他人土地的权利。一种类型的地役权是通过时效而获得的地役权，其通过如下使用获得： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者来说明显或可见 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 实际 – 使用土地 非许可 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利（即未经所有者许可） 与逆权占有不同，使用不必排除所有者或其他使用者，包括所有者的租户（例如猎人）。在这里，该男子清理了邻居的一小部分土地，并在这片土地上开辟了一条道路。在超过10年法定期限的12年里，该男子公开、持续地使用该道路进入高速公路。由于他在未经邻居许可的情况下这样做，该男子通过处方获得了使用该路径的地役权（选择 D）。 （选择 A）当两块土地从共同所有权中分离出来时（此处未示出），必然会产生地役权，使得其中一块土地的所有者绝对有必要穿过另一块土地才能使用该土地。 （选择C）要通过逆权占有获得所有权，一个人必须拥有土地的排他占有——仅仅使用是不够的。\n\n教育目标：要通过规定获得地役权，一个人必须以以下方式使用他人的土地：(1) 公开且臭名昭著，(2) 连续，(3) 实际，以及 (4) 非许可。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 §§ 2.16–2.17 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过规定的地役权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要通过规定获得地役权，一个人必须以以下方式使用他人的土地：(1) 公开且臭名昭著，(2) 连续，(3) 实际，以及 (4) 非许可。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于该男子使用邻居土地上改道道路的权利，律师应该告诉他什么？","rule":"要通过规定获得地役权，一个人必须以以下方式使用他人的土地：(1) 公开且臭名昭著，(2) 连续，(3) 实际，以及 (4) 非许可。","analysis":"地役权是对土地的非占有性权益，它赋予地役权持有人出于有限目的（例如，使用道路）使用他人土地的权利。一种类型的地役权是通过时效而获得的地役权，其通过如下使用获得： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者来说明显或可见 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 实际 – 使用土地 非许可 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利（即未经所有者许可） 与逆权占有不同，使用不必排除所有者或其他使用者，包括所有者的租户（例如猎人）。在这里，该男子清理了邻居的一小部分土地，并在这片土地上开辟了一条道路。在超过10年法定期限的12年里，该男子公开、持续地使用该道路进入高速公路。由于他在未经邻居许可的情况下这样做，该男子通过处方获得了使用该路径的地役权（选择 D）。 （选择 A）当两块土地从共同所有权中分离出来时（此处未示出），必然会产生地役权，使得其中一块土地的所有者绝对有必要穿过另一块土地才能使用该土地。 （选择C）要通过逆权占有获得所有权，一个人必须拥有土地的排他占有——仅仅使用是不够的。\n\n教育目标：要通过规定获得地役权，一个人必须以以下方式使用他人的土地：(1) 公开且臭名昭著，(2) 连续，(3) 实际，以及 (4) 非许可。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 §§ 2.16–2.17 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过规定的地役权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该男子根据规定拥有使用该路径的地役权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-6733150112815d57"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":199,"sourceQuestionPage":582,"sourceQuestionPages":[582],"sourceExplanationPages":[583,584],"sourceAnswerPages":[583,584],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-200","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A building with 40 residential units is owned by a corporation and operated as a cooperative. Each resident is a shareholder in the corporation as well as its tenant pursuant to a lease that is subject to the mortgage on the building. Each tenant is assessed an annual fee to cover part of the corporation's taxes, maintenance expenses, and mortgage obligation. Through poor management by the corporation's board of directors, the corporation failed to raise sufficient funds to pay the mortgage obligation. The bank that holds the mortgage on the building has begun foreclosure proceedings. A resident of the building who paid the annual assessment fee and is not a member of the board of directors has sought a declaratory judgment that a buyer at the foreclosure sale takes the building subject to her lease. The applicable jurisdiction has not adopted a statute that addresses this situation. How should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"Against the resident, because she is personally liable for the mortgage obligation as a shareholder of the corporation.","B":"Against the resident, because the lease is subject to the mortgage on the building.","C":"For the resident, because she is not a member of the corporation's board of directors.","D":"For the resident, because she paid her annual assessment fee."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Main types of common-interest communities\n\nProperty owners' Property owners must, as consequence of property ownership, association belong to association & pay dues to it or Property owners in real-estate development must pay for use/maintenance of property held in common by individual owners, but association membership is optional Condominium Created by state statutes by filing declaration (or master deed) & plat with recording office in condominium's locality Declaration & bylaws set out rights & obligations of unit owners & fix shares for allocating expenses, taxes, voting rights Each owner is responsible for his/her own mortgage Cooperative Typically consists of land & one or more buildings owned by corporation that leases individual residential units to shareholders Resident is tenant who also owns one or more shares of stock in corporation & is prohibited from transferring interest separately from lease Cooperative has blanket mortgage A cooperative is a common-interest community that typically consists of land and one or more buildings owned by a corporation that leases individual residential units to its shareholders. Unlike a condominium, in which each unit owner is responsible for his/her own mortgage, a cooperative has a blanket mortgage that generally has priority over occupancy leases. Since the leases are subject to the mortgage on the cooperative, a default on the blanket mortgage can result in foreclosure proceedings on the property, which in turn can terminate all leases. Therefore, the buyer at a foreclosure sale will not take the building subject to a resident's lease. (Choice A) A shareholder in a cooperative is not personally liable for the cooperative's debts. (Choice C) Members of a corporation's board of directors may incur liability for the board's poor management of the corporation.* However, this does not prevent the resident from suffering the consequences of the corporation's default on its mortgage obligation. *However, many courts apply the business-judgment rule to insulate a director or an officer from liability for an honest but mistaken business decision.\n\n(Choice D) Although the resident has paid her annual assessment fee, which includes her share of the mortgage obligation on the building, she can nevertheless lose her right to occupy her residential unit if the bank forecloses on that mortgage.\n\nEducational objective: Unlike a condominium, in which each unit owner is responsible for his/her own mortgage, a cooperative has a blanket mortgage that generally has priority over occupancy leases. A default on that mortgage can therefore result in foreclosure proceedings on the property, which in turn can terminate all leases.","educationalObjective":"Unlike a condominium, in which each unit owner is responsible for his/her own mortgage, a cooperative has a blanket mortgage that generally has priority over occupancy leases. A default on that mortgage can therefore result in foreclosure proceedings on the property, which in turn can terminate all leases.","references":"","rule":"Unlike a condominium, in which each unit owner is responsible for his/her own mortgage, a cooperative has a blanket mortgage that generally has priority over occupancy leases. A default on that mortgage can therefore result in foreclosure proceedings on the property, which in turn can terminate all leases.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule?","rule":"Unlike a condominium, in which each unit owner is responsible for his/her own mortgage, a cooperative has a blanket mortgage that generally has priority over occupancy leases. A default on that mortgage can therefore result in foreclosure proceedings on the property, which in turn can terminate all leases.","analysis":"Main types of common-interest communities\n\nProperty owners' Property owners must, as consequence of property ownership, association belong to association & pay dues to it or Property owners in real-estate development must pay for use/maintenance of property held in common by individual owners, but association membership is optional Condominium Created by state statutes by filing declaration (or master deed) & plat with recording office in condominium's locality Declaration & bylaws set out rights & obligations of unit owners & fix shares for allocating expenses, taxes, voting rights Each owner is responsible for his/her own mortgage Cooperative Typically consists of land & one or more buildings owned by corporation that leases individual residential units to shareholders Resident is tenant who also owns one or more shares of stock in corporation & is prohibited from transferring interest separately from lease Cooperative has blanket mortgage A cooperative is a common-interest community that typically consists of land and one or more buildings owned by a corporation that leases individual residential units to its shareholders. Unlike a condominium, in which each unit owner is responsible for his/her own mortgage, a cooperative has a blanket mortgage that generally has priority over occupancy leases. Since the leases are subject to the mortgage on the cooperative, a default on the blanket mortgage can result in foreclosure proceedings on the property, which in turn can terminate all leases. Therefore, the buyer at a foreclosure sale will not take the building subject to a resident's lease. (Choice A) A shareholder in a cooperative is not personally liable for the cooperative's debts. (Choice C) Members of a corporation's board of directors may incur liability for the board's poor management of the corporation.* However, this does not prevent the resident from suffering the consequences of the corporation's default on its mortgage obligation. *However, many courts apply the business-judgment rule to insulate a director or an officer from liability for an honest but mistaken business decision.\n\n(Choice D) Although the resident has paid her annual assessment fee, which includes her share of the mortgage obligation on the building, she can nevertheless lose her right to occupy her residential unit if the bank forecloses on that mortgage.","conclusion":"Answer B: Against the resident, because the lease is subject to the mortgage on the building."},"questionZh":"一栋拥有 40 个住宅单元的大楼归一家公司所有，并作为合作社运营。每位居民都是公司的股东，也是公司的租户，根据租赁合同，该租赁须以建筑物抵押。每个租户都要缴纳年费，以支付公司的部分税款、维护费用和抵押贷款债务。由于公司董事会管理不善，公司未能筹集到足够的资金来支付抵押贷款。持有该大楼抵押贷款的银行已开始取消抵押品赎回权程序。该大楼的一位居民支付了年度评估费，但不是董事会成员，他寻求一份宣告性判决，要求取消抵押品赎回权出售的买家将这座大楼纳入其租赁范围。适用的司法管辖区尚未通过解决这种情况的法规。法院应该如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"针对居民，因为她作为公司股东对抵押债务负有个人责任。","B":"对住户来说，因为租约需要抵押建筑物。","C":"对于居民来说，因为她不是公司董事会成员。","D":"对于居民来说，因为她支付了年度评估费。"},"explanationZh":"共同利益共同体的主要类型\n\n业主的 业主必须，由于财产所有权，协会属于协会并缴纳会费，或者 房地产开发中的业主必须支付个人业主共同持有的财产的使用/维护费用，但协会会员资格是可选的 公寓 由州法规通过向公寓所在地的记录办公室提交声明（或主契约）和平台而创建 声明和附则规定了单位所有者的权利和义务，并固定份额以分配费用，税收，投票权 每个业主都是负责自己的抵押贷款 合作社 通常由将个人住宅单元出租给股东的公司拥有的土地和一栋或多栋建筑物组成 居民是同时拥有一股或多股公司股票的租户，并且禁止与租赁分开转让权益 合作社拥有一揽子抵押贷款 合作社是一个共同利益社区，通常由向其股东出租个人住宅单元的公司所拥有的土地和一栋或多栋建筑物组成。与共管公寓不同，在共管公寓中，每个单元业主负责自己的抵押贷款，而合作社则拥有一揽子抵押贷款，通常优先于占用租赁。由于租赁受到合作社抵押的约束，一揽子抵押的违约可能会导致财产丧失抵押品赎回权，进而可能终止所有租赁。因此，丧失抵押品赎回权出售的买方不会将建筑物作为居民租赁。 （选择 A） 合作社的股东个人不对合作社的债务承担责任。 （选择C）公司董事会成员可能因董事会对公司的管理不善而承担责任。*但是，这并不能阻止居民承担公司违约抵押贷款义务的后果。 *然而，许多法院应用商业判断规则来使董事或高级管理人员免于因诚实但错误的商业决策而承担责任。\n\n（选择D）虽然居民已经支付了年度评估费，其中包括她在建筑物上的抵押债务份额，但如果银行取消该抵押贷款的赎回权，她仍然可能会失去占用其住宅单元的权利。\n\n教育目标：与公寓不同，在公寓中，每个单元业主负责自己的抵押贷款，合作社拥有一揽子抵押贷款，通常优先于占用租赁。因此，抵押贷款违约可能会导致财产丧失抵押品赎回权，进而终止所有租赁。","educationalObjectiveZh":"与共管公寓不同，在共管公寓中，每个单元业主负责自己的抵押贷款，而合作社则拥有一揽子抵押贷款，通常优先于占用租赁。因此，抵押贷款违约可能会导致财产丧失抵押品赎回权，进而终止所有租赁。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该如何判决？","rule":"与共管公寓不同，在共管公寓中，每个单元业主负责自己的抵押贷款，而合作社则拥有一揽子抵押贷款，通常优先于占用租赁。因此，抵押贷款违约可能会导致财产丧失抵押品赎回权，进而终止所有租赁。","analysis":"共同利益共同体的主要类型\n\n业主的 业主必须，由于财产所有权，协会属于协会并缴纳会费，或者 房地产开发中的业主必须支付个人业主共同持有的财产的使用/维护费用，但协会会员资格是可选的 公寓 由州法规通过向公寓所在地的记录办公室提交声明（或主契约）和平台而创建 声明和附则规定了单位所有者的权利和义务，并固定份额以分配费用，税收，投票权 每个业主都是负责自己的抵押贷款 合作社 通常由将个人住宅单元出租给股东的公司拥有的土地和一栋或多栋建筑物组成 居民是同时拥有一股或多股公司股票的租户，并且禁止与租赁分开转让权益 合作社拥有一揽子抵押贷款 合作社是一个共同利益社区，通常由向其股东出租个人住宅单元的公司所拥有的土地和一栋或多栋建筑物组成。与共管公寓不同，在共管公寓中，每个单元业主负责自己的抵押贷款，而合作社则拥有一揽子抵押贷款，通常优先于占用租赁。由于租赁受到合作社抵押的约束，一揽子抵押的违约可能会导致财产丧失抵押品赎回权，进而可能终止所有租赁。因此，丧失抵押品赎回权出售的买方不会将建筑物作为居民租赁。 （选择 A） 合作社的股东个人不对合作社的债务承担责任。 （选择C）公司董事会成员可能因董事会对公司的管理不善而承担责任。*但是，这并不能阻止居民承担公司违约抵押贷款义务的后果。 *然而，许多法院应用商业判断规则来使董事或高级管理人员免于因诚实但错误的商业决策而承担责任。\n\n（选择D）虽然居民已经支付了年度评估费，其中包括她在建筑物上的抵押债务份额，但如果银行取消该抵押贷款的赎回权，她仍然可能会失去占用其住宅单元的权利。\n\n教育目标：与公寓不同，在公寓中，每个单元业主负责自己的抵押贷款，合作社拥有一揽子抵押贷款，通常优先于占用租赁。因此，抵押贷款违约可能会导致财产丧失抵押品赎回权，进而终止所有租赁。","conclusion":"答案 B：对住户来说，因为租约需要抵押建筑物。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":200,"sourceQuestionPage":585,"sourceQuestionPages":[585],"sourceExplanationPages":[586,587],"sourceAnswerPages":[586,587],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-201","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Water & Natural Rights","question":"A rancher whose land borders a stream used it to water his livestock. The owner of a small waterpark, in existence before the ranch, was located upstream of the rancher's land. The water park required a significant amount of water in order to function properly, so the owner diverted almost all of the stream water into the water park. No longer able to adequately water his livestock from the stream, the rancher filed an action against the owner. In a jurisdiction that recognizes the riparian doctrine with regard to water usage, who will prevail?","choices":{"A":"The owner, because he made reasonable use of the water.","B":"The owner, because his use of water has priority as he was on the land first.","C":"The rancher, because he made beneficial use of the water.","D":"The rancher, because the owner's commercial use of the water unreasonably interfered with the rancher's domestic use."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Water rights doctrines\n\nRiparian doctrine Water belongs to owners of adjoining land Reasonable use that does not unreasonably interfere with (eastern states) downstream use permitted (reasonable-use doctrine) Domestic use trumps commercial use Water rights cannot be sold or transferred separate from adjoining land Prior-appropriation Water rights determined by priority of beneficial use (\"first in doctrine time, first in right\") Water rights are unconnected to adjoining land & can be sold or (western states) transferred separately Water rights have evolved differently in eastern states (where water is plentiful) and western states (where water is scarce). Eastern states favor the riparian doctrine, where water rights belong to riparians—ie, those who own the land that borders the watercourse. A riparian is generally free to make any reasonable use of the water that does not unreasonably interfere with downstream use.* However, domestic (\"natural\") use trumps commercial (\"artificial\") use and can be unlimited. Here, the rancher used the stream to water his livestock (domestic use), while the owner used it to run his water park (commercial use). The owner's use of the water was likely unreasonable since he diverted so much of it into the water park that the rancher was no longer able to adequately water his livestock (Choice A). And since domestic use of water trumps commercial use in this riparian jurisdiction, the rancher is likely to prevail. *This is known as the reasonable-use doctrine. (Choices B & C) Western states follow the prior-appropriation doctrine (not applicable here), under which water rights are determined by the \"first in time, first in right\" rule. This gives the first person who uses or diverts water for a beneficial purpose (ie, agricultural, industrial, household) priority to use the water.\n\nEducational objective: Under the riparian doctrine (favored in eastern states), water rights belong to the owners of the land that borders the watercourse (ie, riparians). A riparian may make any reasonable use of water that does not unreasonably interfere with downstream use, and domestic (\"natural\") use of water trumps the commercial (\"artificial\") use of it.\n\nReferences\n78 Am. Jur. 2d Waters §§ 35, 37 (2020) (riparian rights generally and requirement for reasonable use).","educationalObjective":"Under the riparian doctrine (favored in eastern states), water rights belong to the owners of the land that borders the watercourse (ie, riparians). A riparian may make any reasonable use of water that does not unreasonably interfere with downstream use, and domestic (\"natural\") use of water trumps the commercial (\"artificial\") use of it.","references":"78 Am. Jur. 2d Waters §§ 35, 37 (2020) (riparian rights generally and requirement for reasonable use).","rule":"Under the riparian doctrine (favored in eastern states), water rights belong to the owners of the land that borders the watercourse (ie, riparians). A riparian may make any reasonable use of water that does not unreasonably interfere with downstream use, and domestic (\"natural\") use of water trumps the commercial (\"artificial\") use of it.","irac":{"issue":"In a jurisdiction that recognizes the riparian doctrine with regard to water usage, who will prevail?","rule":"Under the riparian doctrine (favored in eastern states), water rights belong to the owners of the land that borders the watercourse (ie, riparians). A riparian may make any reasonable use of water that does not unreasonably interfere with downstream use, and domestic (\"natural\") use of water trumps the commercial (\"artificial\") use of it.","analysis":"Water rights doctrines\n\nRiparian doctrine Water belongs to owners of adjoining land Reasonable use that does not unreasonably interfere with (eastern states) downstream use permitted (reasonable-use doctrine) Domestic use trumps commercial use Water rights cannot be sold or transferred separate from adjoining land Prior-appropriation Water rights determined by priority of beneficial use (\"first in doctrine time, first in right\") Water rights are unconnected to adjoining land & can be sold or (western states) transferred separately Water rights have evolved differently in eastern states (where water is plentiful) and western states (where water is scarce). Eastern states favor the riparian doctrine, where water rights belong to riparians—ie, those who own the land that borders the watercourse. A riparian is generally free to make any reasonable use of the water that does not unreasonably interfere with downstream use.* However, domestic (\"natural\") use trumps commercial (\"artificial\") use and can be unlimited. Here, the rancher used the stream to water his livestock (domestic use), while the owner used it to run his water park (commercial use). The owner's use of the water was likely unreasonable since he diverted so much of it into the water park that the rancher was no longer able to adequately water his livestock (Choice A). And since domestic use of water trumps commercial use in this riparian jurisdiction, the rancher is likely to prevail. *This is known as the reasonable-use doctrine. (Choices B & C) Western states follow the prior-appropriation doctrine (not applicable here), under which water rights are determined by the \"first in time, first in right\" rule. This gives the first person who uses or diverts water for a beneficial purpose (ie, agricultural, industrial, household) priority to use the water.","conclusion":"Answer D: The rancher, because the owner's commercial use of the water unreasonably interfered with the rancher's domestic use."},"questionZh":"一位牧场主的土地毗邻一条小溪，用它来浇灌他的牲畜。一个小型水上乐园的主人在牧场之前就已经存在，位于牧场主土地的上游。水上乐园需要大量的水才能正常运转，因此业主将几乎所有的溪水都引到了水上乐园。由于无法再从溪流中充分浇灌牲畜，牧场主对业主提起诉讼。在承认用水方面的河岸原则的司法管辖区中，谁会占上风？","choicesZh":{"A":"主人，因为他合理利用了水。","B":"业主，因为他对水的使用具有优先权，因为他首先在土地上。","C":"牧场主，因为他有效地利用了水。","D":"牧场主，因为业主的商业用水无理干扰了牧场主的家庭用水。"},"explanationZh":"水权学说\n\n河岸主义 水属于毗邻土地的所有者 合理使用，不会无理干扰（东部各州）允许下游使用（合理使用原则） 家庭使用胜过商业使用 水权不能与毗邻土地分开出售或转让 优先拨款 由受益使用优先权确定的水权（“原则上时间第一，权利第一”） 水权与毗邻土地无关，可以单独出售或（西部各州）转让 水权在东部的演变有所不同州（水资源丰富）和西部各州（水资源匮乏）。东方国家赞成河岸主义，即水权属于河岸人，即那些拥有水道边界土地的人。河岸居民通常可以自由地合理利用水，但不会无理干扰下游的使用。*但是，家庭（“自然”）用水胜过商业（“人工”）用水，并且可以不受限制。在这里，牧场主用溪流浇灌他的牲畜（家庭用途），而业主则用它来经营他的水上乐园（商业用途）。业主对水的使用可能不合理，因为他将大量水转移到水上公园，导致牧场主无法再充分浇灌他的牲畜（选项 A）。由于在这个河岸管辖区，家庭用水胜过商业用水，因此牧场主很可能会占上风。 *这被称为合理使用原则。 （选项B和C） 西方国家遵循优先分配原则（此处不适用），根据该原则，水权按照“时间在先、权利在先”的规则确定。这使得第一个出于有益目的（即农业、工业、家庭）使用或转移水的人优先使用水。\n\n教育目标：根据河岸原则（在东部各州受到青睐），水权属于水道沿岸土地的所有者（即河岸人）。河岸人可以合理利用水，但不会无理干扰下游的使用，并且家庭（“自然”）用水胜过商业（“人工”）用水。\n\n参考文献\n78 上午。尤尔。 2d Waters §§ 35, 37 (2020)（一般河岸权利和合理使用的要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据河岸原则（在东部各州受到青睐），水权属于水道沿岸土地的所有者（即河岸人）。河岸人可以合理利用水，但不会无理干扰下游的使用，并且家庭（“自然”）用水胜过商业（“人工”）用水。","iracZh":{"issue":"在承认用水方面的河岸原则的司法管辖区中，谁会占上风？","rule":"根据河岸原则（在东部各州受到青睐），水权属于水道沿岸土地的所有者（即河岸人）。河岸人可以合理利用水，但不会无理干扰下游的使用，并且家庭（“自然”）用水胜过商业（“人工”）用水。","analysis":"水权学说\n\n河岸主义 水属于毗邻土地的所有者 合理使用，不会无理干扰（东部各州）允许下游使用（合理使用原则） 家庭使用胜过商业使用 水权不能与毗邻土地分开出售或转让 优先拨款 由受益使用优先权确定的水权（“原则上时间第一，权利第一”） 水权与毗邻土地无关，可以单独出售或（西部各州）转让 水权在东部的演变有所不同州（水资源丰富）和西部各州（水资源匮乏）。东方国家赞成河岸主义，即水权属于河岸人，即那些拥有水道边界土地的人。河岸居民通常可以自由地合理利用水，但不会无理干扰下游的使用。*但是，家庭（“自然”）用水胜过商业（“人工”）用水，并且可以不受限制。在这里，牧场主用溪流浇灌他的牲畜（家庭用途），而业主则用它来经营他的水上乐园（商业用途）。业主对水的使用可能不合理，因为他将大量水转移到水上公园，导致牧场主无法再充分浇灌他的牲畜（选项 A）。由于在这个河岸管辖区，家庭用水胜过商业用水，因此牧场主很可能会占上风。 *这被称为合理使用原则。 （选项B和C） 西方国家遵循优先分配原则（此处不适用），根据该原则，水权按照“时间在先、权利在先”的规则确定。这使得第一个出于有益目的（即农业、工业、家庭）使用或转移水的人优先使用水。\n\n教育目标：根据河岸原则（在东部各州受到青睐），水权属于水道沿岸土地的所有者（即河岸人）。河岸人可以合理利用水，但不会无理干扰下游的使用，并且家庭（“自然”）用水胜过商业（“人工”）用水。\n\n参考文献\n78 上午。尤尔。 2d Waters §§ 35, 37 (2020)（一般河岸权利和合理使用的要求）。","conclusion":"答案 D：牧场主，因为业主的商业用水无理干扰了牧场主的家庭用水。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"prior appropriation doctrine","ipa":"/pɹˈaɪɝ ʌpɹˌoʊpɹiˈeɪʃən dˈɑktɹən/","syllables":"prior ap·pro·pri·a·tion doc·trine","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"先占用水权原则：以先为有益用途取水者取得较优水权","contextualMeaning":"先占用水权原则：以先为有益用途取水者取得较优水权","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A rancher whose land borders a stream used it to water his livestock.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"riparian","ipa":"/ri'pєəriən/","syllables":"ri·par·ian","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"a. 河岸的, 生于河岸的；n. 河岸土地所有人","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“riparian”","wordForm":"形容词；词形 s:riparians","sourceSentence":"In a jurisdiction that recognizes the riparian doctrine with regard to water usage, who will prevail?","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":201,"sourceQuestionPage":588,"sourceQuestionPages":[588],"sourceExplanationPages":[589,590],"sourceAnswerPages":[589,590],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-202","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man owned a large tract of land that had frontage on a public highway. The land had no access to any other road. Fifteen years ago, the man conveyed the rear half of the land to a woman and at the same time conveyed an express easement to the woman that provided access from her land across his retained land to the public highway. The woman used the easement until she reconveyed the land back to the man 10 years ago. The deed to the man made no reference to the easement. Five years ago, the man again conveyed the rear half of the land, this time to an investor in a deed that made no reference to any easement to the public highway. Recently, the man told the investor that he could no longer cross the man's land for access to the public highway. A neighbor has told the investor that he can use her land for access to another public road \"for a price.\" The investor has sued the man for the right to cross the man's land to the public highway. For whom will the court likely decide?","choices":{"A":"The investor, because an easement will be implied.","B":"The investor, because the man is estopped by his grant of an easement to the woman.","C":"The man, because the express easement was terminated by the reconveyance.","D":"The man, because the investor can reasonably acquire another means of access to a public road."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The man conveyed an easement to the woman that allowed her to cross his land to access a public highway. That easement terminated when the dominant estate (woman's tract) and servient estate (man's tract) merged into the hands of one owner after the woman conveyed her tract back to the man (Choices B & C). And though the man subsequently conveyed the rear half of his lot to the investor, a terminated easement is not re-created merely by re-severing the land into two parcels. Additionally, the investor did not receive an easement by express grant because his deed made no reference to any easement to the public highway. But an easement by necessity will be implied if two elements are met: Severance of common ownership – the dominant and servient estates were a single tract of land, and the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another\n\nStrict necessity – the easement is absolutely necessary for the use and enjoyment of the land (eg, the dominant tract is landlocked and has no access to a public road)\n\nHere, the man owned both tracts (unity of estates) before he conveyed the back half to the investor (severance). And since the rear tract has no access to a public highway or any other road, an easement over the man's tract is absolutely necessary for the investor to use and enjoy his tract. Therefore, an easement will be implied and the court will likely decide for the investor. (Choice D) The ability to create alternative access to a landlocked parcel by purchase of an easement over a neighbor's land does not defeat the creation of an easement by necessity.\n\nEducational objective: An easement by necessity is implied if (1) there was a unity of estates, (2) common ownership was severed, and (3) an easement is absolutely necessary to use and enjoy the land.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by necessity).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination of easement by merger).","educationalObjective":"An easement by necessity is implied if (1) there was a unity of estates, (2) common ownership was severed, and (3) an easement is absolutely necessary to use and enjoy the land.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by necessity).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination of easement by merger).","rule":"An easement by necessity is implied if (1) there was a unity of estates, (2) common ownership was severed, and (3) an easement is absolutely necessary to use and enjoy the land.","irac":{"issue":"For whom will the court likely decide?","rule":"An easement by necessity is implied if (1) there was a unity of estates, (2) common ownership was severed, and (3) an easement is absolutely necessary to use and enjoy the land.","analysis":"The man conveyed an easement to the woman that allowed her to cross his land to access a public highway. That easement terminated when the dominant estate (woman's tract) and servient estate (man's tract) merged into the hands of one owner after the woman conveyed her tract back to the man (Choices B & C). And though the man subsequently conveyed the rear half of his lot to the investor, a terminated easement is not re-created merely by re-severing the land into two parcels. Additionally, the investor did not receive an easement by express grant because his deed made no reference to any easement to the public highway. But an easement by necessity will be implied if two elements are met: Severance of common ownership – the dominant and servient estates were a single tract of land, and the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another\n\nStrict necessity – the easement is absolutely necessary for the use and enjoyment of the land (eg, the dominant tract is landlocked and has no access to a public road)\n\nHere, the man owned both tracts (unity of estates) before he conveyed the back half to the investor (severance). And since the rear tract has no access to a public highway or any other road, an easement over the man's tract is absolutely necessary for the investor to use and enjoy his tract. Therefore, an easement will be implied and the court will likely decide for the investor. (Choice D) The ability to create alternative access to a landlocked parcel by purchase of an easement over a neighbor's land does not defeat the creation of an easement by necessity.","conclusion":"Answer A: The investor, because an easement will be implied."},"questionZh":"一个男人拥有一大片土地，临街是一条公共高速公路。这片土地无法通往任何其他道路。十五年前，该男子将土地的后半部分转让给一名女子，同时向该女子转让了一份明确的地役权，允许从她的土地穿过他保留的土地进入公共高速公路。该女子一直使用地役权，直到 10 年前将土地重新归还给该男子。该男子的契约没有提及地役权。五年前，该男子再次将土地的后半部分转让给一位投资者，但契约中没有提及任何公共高速公路的地役权。最近，该男子告诉投资者，他无法再穿过该男子的土地进入公共高速公路。一位邻居告诉投资者，他可以“以一定的价格”使用她的土地进入另一条公共道路。投资者已起诉该男子，要求其有权穿过该男子的土地进入公共高速公路。法院可能会为谁做出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"投资者，因为将隐含地役权。","B":"投资者，因为男人因向女人授予地役权而被阻止。","C":"该男子，因为明示地役权因重新转让而终止。","D":"男人，因为投资者可以合理地获得另一种进入公共道路的方式。"},"explanationZh":"该男子向该女子转让了一份地役权，允许她穿过他的土地进入公共高速公路。当女性将她的土地归还给男性后，支配性地产（女性的土地）和从属土地（男性的土地）合并到一个所有者手中时，地役权终止（选择 B 和 C）。尽管该男子随后将其土地的后半部分转让给了投资者，但仅仅通过将土地重新分成两块，并不能重新创建终止的地役权。此外，投资者没有通过明确授予获得地役权，因为他的契约没有提及任何公共高速公路的地役权。但是，如果满足两个要素，则必然意味着地役权： 共同所有权的分离——支配性和从属性庄园是一块土地，所有者保留该土地的一部分，并将其余部分转让给另一块土地。\n\n严格必要性——地役权对于土地的使用和享受是绝对必要的（例如，主要土地是内陆地区，无法通往公共道路）\n\n在这里，该男子在将后半部分转让给投资者（遣散费）之前拥有两块土地（统一的遗产）。由于后区无法通往公共高速公路或任何其他道路，因此对于投资者使用和享受他的土地来说，对男子土地的地役权是绝对必要的。因此，将暗示地役权，法院可能会做出有利于投资者的裁决。 （选项 D）通过购买邻居土地上的地役权来创建进入内陆地块的替代通道的能力并不妨碍建立地役权的必要性。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 存在统一的财产，(2) 共同所有权被分割，以及 (3) 使用和享有土地绝对需要地役权，则意味着必要的地役权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（必要时的地役权）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.5（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（通过合并终止地役权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果 (1) 存在统一的财产，(2) 共同所有权被分割，以及 (3) 使用和享有土地绝对需要地役权，则意味着必要地役权。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会为谁做出裁决？","rule":"如果 (1) 存在统一的财产，(2) 共同所有权被分割，以及 (3) 使用和享有土地绝对需要地役权，则意味着必要地役权。","analysis":"该男子向该女子转让了一份地役权，允许她穿过他的土地进入公共高速公路。当女性将她的土地归还给男性后，支配性地产（女性的土地）和从属土地（男性的土地）合并到一个所有者手中时，地役权终止（选择 B 和 C）。尽管该男子随后将其土地的后半部分转让给了投资者，但仅仅通过将土地重新分成两块，并不能重新创建终止的地役权。此外，投资者没有通过明确授予获得地役权，因为他的契约没有提及任何公共高速公路的地役权。但是，如果满足两个要素，则必然意味着地役权： 共同所有权的分离——支配性和从属性庄园是一块土地，所有者保留该土地的一部分，并将其余部分转让给另一块土地。\n\n严格必要性——地役权对于土地的使用和享受是绝对必要的（例如，主要土地是内陆地区，无法通往公共道路）\n\n在这里，该男子在将后半部分转让给投资者（遣散费）之前拥有两块土地（统一的遗产）。由于后区无法通往公共高速公路或任何其他道路，因此对于投资者使用和享受他的土地来说，对男子土地的地役权是绝对必要的。因此，将暗示地役权，法院可能会做出有利于投资者的裁决。 （选项 D）通过购买邻居土地上的地役权来创建进入内陆地块的替代通道的能力并不妨碍建立地役权的必要性。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 存在统一的财产，(2) 共同所有权被分割，以及 (3) 使用和享有土地绝对需要地役权，则意味着必要的地役权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（必要时的地役权）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.5（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（通过合并终止地役权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：投资者，因为将隐含地役权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-d7b2517e9ae9f2d8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":202,"sourceQuestionPage":591,"sourceQuestionPages":[591],"sourceExplanationPages":[592,593],"sourceAnswerPages":[592,593],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-203","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"Last year, a buyer and a seller entered into a valid contract for the sale of a parcel of real property. The contract contained no contingencies. The seller was killed in a car accident before the parcel was conveyed, but the closing eventually took place with the conveyance by a deed from the personal representative of the seller's estate. The personal representative of the seller's estate wants to distribute the proceeds of the real property sale. The seller's will was executed many years ago and was duly admitted to probate. Paragraph 5 of his will leaves all of the seller's real property to his son, and Paragraph 6 leaves the residue of the estate to the seller's daughter. No other provisions of the will are pertinent to the question regarding to whom the proceeds of the sale should be distributed. What will determine who receives the proceeds?","choices":{"A":"Whether Paragraph 5 refers specifically to the parcel of real property that was sold or simply to \"all of my real property.\"","B":"Whether the closing date originally specified in the contract was a date before or after the seller's death.","C":"Whether the jurisdiction has adopted the doctrine of equitable conversion.","D":"Whether the sale was completed in accordance with a court order."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The doctrine of equitable conversion controls the ownership and risk of loss of real property during the executory period. Under this doctrine, the buyer receives equitable title to the property once the parties enter a specifically enforceable land-sales contract (eg, one with no contingencies). In contrast, the seller retains legal title to the property and acquires the equitable right to receive the purchase price upon closing. The buyer's equitable title is treated as a real property interest that is freely alienable, subject to the seller's equitable right to the purchase price. In contrast, the seller's right to the sale proceeds is treated as a personal property interest. Therefore, when the seller in this case died before closing, the doctrine of equitable conversion gave the right to the sale proceeds to his daughter, the beneficiary of his residuary estate. (Choice A) Whether Paragraph 5 refers specifically to the parcel sold or simply to \"all of my real property\" is of no consequence since the seller's interest in the sale proceeds is a personal property (not real property) interest. The son had only a legal interest in the parcel that transferred to the buyer upon closing. (Choice B) The sale proceeds would have passed to the daughter regardless of whether the closing date was scheduled before or after the seller's death. (Choice D) The right to the sale proceeds depends on who had equitable title to the proceeds—not on the manner in which the sale was completed.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, the buyer acquires equitable title to the property (real property interest) and the seller acquires an equitable right to the sale proceeds (personal property interest). These interests will be distributed accordingly if either party dies before closing.\n\nReferences\n27A Am. Jur. 2d Equitable Conversion § 13 (2020) (equitable conversion of interests under land-sale contract).","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, the buyer acquires equitable title to the property (real property interest) and the seller acquires an equitable right to the sale proceeds (personal property interest). These interests will be distributed accordingly if either party dies before closing.","references":"27A Am. Jur. 2d Equitable Conversion § 13 (2020) (equitable conversion of interests under land-sale contract).","rule":"Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, the buyer acquires equitable title to the property (real property interest) and the seller acquires an equitable right to the sale proceeds (personal property interest). These interests will be distributed accordingly if either party dies before closing.","irac":{"issue":"What will determine who receives the proceeds?","rule":"Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, the buyer acquires equitable title to the property (real property interest) and the seller acquires an equitable right to the sale proceeds (personal property interest). These interests will be distributed accordingly if either party dies before closing.","analysis":"The doctrine of equitable conversion controls the ownership and risk of loss of real property during the executory period. Under this doctrine, the buyer receives equitable title to the property once the parties enter a specifically enforceable land-sales contract (eg, one with no contingencies). In contrast, the seller retains legal title to the property and acquires the equitable right to receive the purchase price upon closing. The buyer's equitable title is treated as a real property interest that is freely alienable, subject to the seller's equitable right to the purchase price. In contrast, the seller's right to the sale proceeds is treated as a personal property interest. Therefore, when the seller in this case died before closing, the doctrine of equitable conversion gave the right to the sale proceeds to his daughter, the beneficiary of his residuary estate. (Choice A) Whether Paragraph 5 refers specifically to the parcel sold or simply to \"all of my real property\" is of no consequence since the seller's interest in the sale proceeds is a personal property (not real property) interest. The son had only a legal interest in the parcel that transferred to the buyer upon closing. (Choice B) The sale proceeds would have passed to the daughter regardless of whether the closing date was scheduled before or after the seller's death. (Choice D) The right to the sale proceeds depends on who had equitable title to the proceeds—not on the manner in which the sale was completed.","conclusion":"Answer C: Whether the jurisdiction has adopted the doctrine of equitable conversion."},"questionZh":"去年，买方和卖方就一块不动产的销售签订了有效合同。合同中不包含任何意外事件。卖方在运送包裹之前因车祸身亡，但最终通过卖方遗产私人代表的契据完成了交易。卖方遗产的个人代表想要分配房地产销售的收益。卖方的遗嘱多年前已执行，并已正式接受遗嘱认证。他的遗嘱第 5 段将卖方的所有不动产留给他的儿子，第 6 段将剩余的遗产留给卖方的女儿。遗嘱中没有其他条款与出售收益应分配给谁的问题相关。什么将决定谁获得收益？","choicesZh":{"A":"第 5 段是否特指已出售的不动产包裹，还是仅指“我的所有不动产”。","B":"合同中最初指定的截止日期是卖方去世之前还是之后的日期。","C":"司法管辖区是否采用了衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则。","D":"出售是否按照法院命令完成。"},"explanationZh":"衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则控制了执行期间不动产的所有权和损失风险。根据这一原则，一旦双方签订了具体可执行的土地销售合同（例如，没有意外事件的合同），买方就获得了财产的公平所有权。相比之下，卖方保留对该财产的合法所有权，并获得在成交时收取购买价格的衡平权利。买方的衡平法所有权被视为可自由转让的不动产权益，但须遵守卖方对购买价格的衡平法权利。相反，卖方对销售收益的权利被视为个人财产权益。因此，当本案中的卖方在成交前死亡时，衡平转换原则将出售收益的权利赋予了他的女儿，即他的剩余遗产的受益人。 （选择 A）第 5 段是否具体指所出售的地块或仅指“我的所有不动产”并不重要，因为卖方在销售收益中的权益是动产（而非不动产）权益。儿子仅拥有该包裹的合法权益，该权益在成交时转移给买方。 （选择 B）无论交割日期是安排在卖方去世之前还是之后，出售收益都将转移给女儿。 （选项 D） 销售收益的权利取决于谁对收益拥有公平的所有权，而不取决于销售完成的方式。\n\n教育目标：根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，买方获得财产的公平所有权（不动产权益），卖方获得销售收益的公平权利（动产权益）。如果任何一方在交割前死亡，这些权益将相应分配。\n\n参考文献\n27A 上午尤尔。 2d Equitable Conversion § 13 (2020)（土地销售合同下权益的衡平转换（equitable conversion））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据衡平转换原则，买方获得财产的衡平所有权（不动产权益），卖方获得销售收益的衡平权利（动产权益）。如果任何一方在交割前死亡，这些权益将相应分配。","iracZh":{"issue":"什么将决定谁获得收益？","rule":"根据衡平转换原则，买方获得财产的衡平所有权（不动产权益），卖方获得销售收益的衡平权利（动产权益）。如果任何一方在交割前死亡，这些权益将相应分配。","analysis":"衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则控制了执行期间不动产的所有权和损失风险。根据这一原则，一旦双方签订了具体可执行的土地销售合同（例如，没有意外事件的合同），买方就获得了财产的公平所有权。相比之下，卖方保留对该财产的合法所有权，并获得在成交时收取购买价格的衡平权利。买方的衡平法所有权被视为可自由转让的不动产权益，但须遵守卖方对购买价格的衡平法权利。相反，卖方对销售收益的权利被视为个人财产权益。因此，当本案中的卖方在成交前死亡时，衡平转换原则将出售收益的权利赋予了他的女儿，即他的剩余遗产的受益人。 （选择 A）第 5 段是否具体指所出售的地块或仅指“我的所有不动产”并不重要，因为卖方在销售收益中的权益是动产（而非不动产）权益。儿子仅拥有该包裹的合法权益，该权益在成交时转移给买方。 （选择 B）无论交割日期是安排在卖方去世之前还是之后，出售收益都将转移给女儿。 （选项 D） 销售收益的权利取决于谁对收益拥有公平的所有权，而不取决于销售完成的方式。\n\n教育目标：根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，买方获得财产的公平所有权（不动产权益），卖方获得销售收益的公平权利（动产权益）。如果任何一方在交割前死亡，这些权益将相应分配。\n\n参考文献\n27A 上午尤尔。 2d Equitable Conversion § 13 (2020)（土地销售合同下权益的衡平转换（equitable conversion））。","conclusion":"答案 C：司法管辖区是否采用了衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-af603b8a5e6f8dad"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":203,"sourceQuestionPage":594,"sourceQuestionPages":[594],"sourceExplanationPages":[595,596],"sourceAnswerPages":[595,596],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-204","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man owned 80 acres of land, fronting on a town road. Two years ago, the man sold the back 40 acres to his cousin. The 40 acres sold to the cousin did not adjoin any public road. The man's deed to the cousin expressly granted a right-of-way over a specified strip of the man's retained 40 acres, so the cousin could reach the town road. The deed was promptly and properly recorded. Last year, the cousin conveyed the back 40 acres to his friend. They had discussed the right- of-way over the man's land to the road, but the cousin's deed to the friend made no mention of it. The friend began to use the right-of-way as the cousin had, but the man sued to enjoin such use by the friend. For whom should the court decide?","choices":{"A":"The friend, because he has an easement by implication.","B":"The friend, because the easement appurtenant passed to him as a result of the cousin's deed to him.","C":"The man, because the cousin's deed failed expressly to transfer the right-of-way to the friend.","D":"The man, because the cousin's easement in gross was not transferable."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Distinctions between easements\n\nType Appurtenant Benefits easement holder's land Benefit & burden automatically transfer when land is conveyed In gross Benefits easement holder personally Burden (not benefit) automatically transfers when land is conveyed Purpose Affirmative Allows easement holder to do something on servient estate\n\nNegative Restricts servient-estate owner's lawful use of servient estate\n\nCreation Express Created by parties in writing that satisfies statute of frauds\n\nIrrevocable License becomes irrevocable due to estoppel or coupling with license another servitude\n\nImplied Implied by law due to necessity (easement by necessity) Implied by fact due to prior use (easement by implication) By prescription Adversely acquired through OCAN—open/notorious, actual, continuous & nonpermissive use An easement appurtenant is an easement that benefits a parcel of land (dominant estate) by allowing the easement holder to use or control another parcel of land (servient estate) in a limited way. One common example is a right-of-way over the servient estate that provides access to the dominant estate. This type of easement is attached to the land, so subsequent owners of both estates are bound by the easement.* Here, the man conveyed the back 40 acres to the cousin, along with an express right-of-way that allowed the cousin to cross the man's land to access the town road. Since that easement appurtenant is attached to the land, it passed to the friend when the cousin conveyed the back 40 acres to the friend—even though the deed did not expressly transfer the easement to the friend (Choice C). Therefore, the court should decide for the friend. *However, an easement will not bind subsequent owners of the servient estate who take without actual, record, or inquiry notice of the easement. (Choice A) An easement by implication will be created by law only if it is necessary for the use and enjoyment of property—eg, if the property cannot be accessed from a public road or existing easement. Here, an easement need not be implied because the friend has an easement appurtenant that provides access to the road.\n\n(Choice D) Unlike an easement appurtenant (as seen here), an easement in gross benefits the easement holder personally but does not benefit a parcel of land (ie, has no dominant estate).\n\nEducational objective: An easement appurtenant benefits one parcel of land (dominant estate) by allowing the easement holder to use or control another parcel of land (servient estate) in a limited way. This type of easement is attached to the land, so subsequent owners of both the dominant and servient estates are bound by the easement.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (defining appurtenant and in gross).","educationalObjective":"An easement appurtenant benefits one parcel of land (dominant estate) by allowing the easement holder to use or control another parcel of land (servient estate) in a limited way. This type of easement is attached to the land, so subsequent owners of both the dominant and servient estates are bound by the easement.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 1.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (defining appurtenant and in gross).","rule":"An easement appurtenant benefits one parcel of land (dominant estate) by allowing the easement holder to use or control another parcel of land (servient estate) in a limited way. This type of easement is attached to the land, so subsequent owners of both the dominant and servient estates are bound by the easement.","irac":{"issue":"For whom should the court decide?","rule":"An easement appurtenant benefits one parcel of land (dominant estate) by allowing the easement holder to use or control another parcel of land (servient estate) in a limited way. This type of easement is attached to the land, so subsequent owners of both the dominant and servient estates are bound by the easement.","analysis":"Distinctions between easements\n\nType Appurtenant Benefits easement holder's land Benefit & burden automatically transfer when land is conveyed In gross Benefits easement holder personally Burden (not benefit) automatically transfers when land is conveyed Purpose Affirmative Allows easement holder to do something on servient estate\n\nNegative Restricts servient-estate owner's lawful use of servient estate\n\nCreation Express Created by parties in writing that satisfies statute of frauds\n\nIrrevocable License becomes irrevocable due to estoppel or coupling with license another servitude\n\nImplied Implied by law due to necessity (easement by necessity) Implied by fact due to prior use (easement by implication) By prescription Adversely acquired through OCAN—open/notorious, actual, continuous & nonpermissive use An easement appurtenant is an easement that benefits a parcel of land (dominant estate) by allowing the easement holder to use or control another parcel of land (servient estate) in a limited way. One common example is a right-of-way over the servient estate that provides access to the dominant estate. This type of easement is attached to the land, so subsequent owners of both estates are bound by the easement.* Here, the man conveyed the back 40 acres to the cousin, along with an express right-of-way that allowed the cousin to cross the man's land to access the town road. Since that easement appurtenant is attached to the land, it passed to the friend when the cousin conveyed the back 40 acres to the friend—even though the deed did not expressly transfer the easement to the friend (Choice C). Therefore, the court should decide for the friend. *However, an easement will not bind subsequent owners of the servient estate who take without actual, record, or inquiry notice of the easement. (Choice A) An easement by implication will be created by law only if it is necessary for the use and enjoyment of property—eg, if the property cannot be accessed from a public road or existing easement. Here, an easement need not be implied because the friend has an easement appurtenant that provides access to the road.\n\n(Choice D) Unlike an easement appurtenant (as seen here), an easement in gross benefits the easement holder personally but does not benefit a parcel of land (ie, has no dominant estate).","conclusion":"Answer B: The friend, because the easement appurtenant passed to him as a result of the cousin's deed to him."},"questionZh":"一名男子拥有 80 英亩的土地，面向一条城镇道路。两年前，该男子将后面的 40 英亩土地卖给了他的表弟。卖给表弟的 40 英亩土地不毗邻任何公共道路。该男子向其表弟的契约明确授予该男子保留的 40 英亩土地中指定地带的通行权，以便该表弟可以到达城镇道路。该行为被及时、正确地记录下来。去年，表弟将后面的40英亩土地转让给了他的朋友。他们讨论了从该男子的土地到公路的通行权，但表弟给朋友的契约没有提及这一点。这位朋友开始像表弟一样使用通行权，但该男子提起诉讼，禁止这位朋友使用该通行权。法院应该为谁做出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"这位朋友，因为他隐含地拥有地役权。","B":"朋友，因为地役权附属物由于表弟的行为而转移给了他。","C":"该男子因表弟的行为未明确将通行权转让给朋友。","D":"这名男子，因为表弟的地役权总计不可转让。"},"explanationZh":"地役权之间的区别\n\n类型 附属福利 地役权持有人的土地 土地转让时，利益和负担自动转移 总利益 地役权持有人个人 土地转让时，负担（而非福利）自动转移 目的 肯定式 允许地役权持有人在受役地做某事\n\n负面 限制供役地所有人合法使用供役地\n\nCreation Express 由各方以书面形式创建，符合欺诈法规\n\n不可撤销的许可由于禁止反言或与许可另一奴役相结合而变得不可撤销\n\n默示 因必要性而由法律默示（因必要性地役权） 因先前使用而因事实默示（默示地役权） 通过时效 通过 OCAN 反向获得 — 公开/臭名昭著、实际、连续和非许可使用 地役权附属物是一种地役权，通过允许地役权持有人以有限的方式使用或控制另一块土地（受役地），从而使一块土地（主导财产）受益。一个常见的例子是服务性地产的通行权，它提供了通往主导性地产的通道。这种类型的地役权是附属于土地的，因此两个庄园的后续所有者都受到地役权的约束。*在这里，该男子将后面的 40 英亩土地转让给表弟，并附有一份明确的通行权，允许表弟穿过该男子的土地进入城镇道路。由于地役权附属物依附于土地，当表弟将后面的 40 英亩土地转让给朋友时，地役权就转移给了朋友——尽管契约没有明确将地役权转让给朋友（选项 C）。因此，法院应判决该友人。 *但是，地役权不会对在没有实际、记录或查询通知的情况下取得地役权的后续所有者产生约束力。 （选择 A）只有在使用和享有财产所必需的情况下（例如，如果无法从公共道路或现有地役权进入该财产），法律才会根据隐含地役权设​​立地役权。在这里，不需要暗示地役权，因为朋友有一个提供通往道路的地役权附属物。\n\n（选项 D）与地役权附属物（如此处所见）不同，地役权总体受益于地役权持有人个人，但不会使一块土地受益（即，没有主导财产）。\n\n教育目标：地役权附属物通过允许地役权持有人以有限的方式使用或控制另一块土地（服务性地产）而使一块土地（主导地产）受益。这种类型的地役权依附于土地，因此支配性地产和从属地产的后续所有者都受到地役权的约束。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 1.5（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（定义附属物和总价值）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"地役权附属物通过允许地役权持有人以有限的方式使用或控制另一块土地（服务性房地产）而使一块土地（主导性房地产）受益。这种类型的地役权依附于土地，因此支配性地产和从属地产的后续所有者都受到地役权的约束。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该为谁做出裁决？","rule":"地役权附属物通过允许地役权持有人以有限的方式使用或控制另一块土地（服务性房地产）而使一块土地（主导性房地产）受益。这种类型的地役权依附于土地，因此支配性地产和从属地产的后续所有者都受到地役权的约束。","analysis":"地役权之间的区别\n\n类型 附属福利 地役权持有人的土地 土地转让时，利益和负担自动转移 总利益 地役权持有人个人 土地转让时，负担（而非福利）自动转移 目的 肯定式 允许地役权持有人在受役地做某事\n\n负面 限制供役地所有人合法使用供役地\n\nCreation Express 由各方以书面形式创建，符合欺诈法规\n\n不可撤销的许可由于禁止反言或与许可另一奴役相结合而变得不可撤销\n\n默示 因必要性而由法律默示（因必要性地役权） 因先前使用而因事实默示（默示地役权） 通过时效 通过 OCAN 反向获得 — 公开/臭名昭著、实际、连续和非许可使用 地役权附属物是一种地役权，通过允许地役权持有人以有限的方式使用或控制另一块土地（受役地），从而使一块土地（主导财产）受益。一个常见的例子是服务性地产的通行权，它提供了通往主导性地产的通道。这种类型的地役权是附属于土地的，因此两个庄园的后续所有者都受到地役权的约束。*在这里，该男子将后面的 40 英亩土地转让给表弟，并附有一份明确的通行权，允许表弟穿过该男子的土地进入城镇道路。由于地役权附属物依附于土地，当表弟将后面的 40 英亩土地转让给朋友时，地役权就转移给了朋友——尽管契约没有明确将地役权转让给朋友（选项 C）。因此，法院应判决该友人。 *但是，地役权不会对在没有实际、记录或查询通知的情况下取得地役权的后续所有者产生约束力。 （选择 A）只有在使用和享有财产所必需的情况下（例如，如果无法从公共道路或现有地役权进入该财产），法律才会根据隐含地役权设​​立地役权。在这里，不需要暗示地役权，因为朋友有一个提供通往道路的地役权附属物。\n\n（选项 D）与地役权附属物（如此处所见）不同，地役权总体受益于地役权持有人个人，但不会使一块土地受益（即，没有主导财产）。\n\n教育目标：地役权附属物通过允许地役权持有人以有限的方式使用或控制另一块土地（服务性地产）而使一块土地（主导地产）受益。这种类型的地役权依附于土地，因此支配性地产和从属地产的后续所有者都受到地役权的约束。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 1.5（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（定义附属物和总价值）。","conclusion":"答案 B：朋友，因为地役权附属物由于表弟的行为而转移给了他。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"easement in gross","ipa":"/ˈizmənt ɪn ɡɹˈoʊs/","syllables":"ease·ment in gross","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"个人地役权：利益归特定个人或实体而非特定优势地","contextualMeaning":"个人地役权：利益归特定个人或实体而非特定优势地","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The man, because the cousin's easement in gross was not transferable.","synonyms":["personal easement"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":204,"sourceQuestionPage":597,"sourceQuestionPages":[597],"sourceExplanationPages":[598,599],"sourceAnswerPages":[598,599],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-205","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"A rancher owned in fee simple two adjoining lots, Lots 1 and 2. He conveyed Lot 1 in fee simple to his cousin. The deed was in usual form of a warranty deed with the following provision inserted in the appropriate place: \"Grantor, for himself, his heirs and assigns, does covenant and agree that any reasonable expense incurred by grantee, his heirs and assigns, as the result of having to repair the retaining wall presently situated on Lot 1 at the common boundary with Lot 2, shall be reimbursed one-half the costs of repairs; and by this provision the parties intend a covenant running with the land.\" The cousin conveyed Lot 1 in fee simple to a woman by warranty deed in usual and regular form. The deed omitted any reference to the retaining wall or any covenant. Fifty years after the rancher's conveyance to the cousin, the woman conveyed Lot 1 in fee simple to a developer by warranty deed in usual form. This deed omitted any reference to the retaining wall or the covenant. There is no statute that applies to any aspect of the problems presented except a recording act and a statute providing for acquisition of title after 10 years of adverse possession. The deed from the rancher to the cousin was never recorded. All other deeds were promptly and properly recorded. Lot 2 is now owned by the rancher's daughter, who took by intestate succession. The developer expended $3,500 on the retaining wall. Then he obtained all the original deeds in the chain of title from the rancher to himself. Shortly thereafter, the developer discovered the covenant in the rancher's deed to the cousin. The developer demanded that the daughter pay $1,750. When she refused, he instituted an appropriate action to recover that sum. If judgment is for the daughter, what will be the likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The cousin's deed from the rancher was never recorded.","B":"The developer did not know about the covenant until after he had incurred the expenses and, hence, could not have relied on it.","C":"The developer is barred by adverse possession.","D":"The developer's expenditures were not proved to be reasonable and customary."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A real covenant is an express promise regarding land that is enforceable by an action for money damages. Although the promising parties can enforce the covenant under contract law, the covenant can only bind successors in interest if the benefit/burden runs with the land. This requires proof of the following elements: Writing – covenant is in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, Lot 1 deed to the cousin)\n\nIntent to run – promising parties intended for the covenant to run to their successors in interest (eg, deed says \"heirs and assigns\" and \"running with the land\")\n\nTouch and concern – covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of the benefited and burdened lands (eg, shared enjoyment of retaining wall)\n\nHorizontal privity – promising parties simultaneously transferred the land and created the covenant (eg, covenant was created when Lot 1 was deeded to the cousin)\n\nVertical privity – successor of the benefited estate has a possessory interest (eg, developer owns Lot 1) and successor of the burdened estate has a promising party's entire estate or ownership interest (eg, daughter has rancher's fee simple in Lot 2)\n\nNotice – person to be bound had notice of the covenant if he/she purchased the property (eg, daughter inherited Lot 2, so it is irrelevant that the cousin's deed was never recorded) (Choice A)\n\nTherefore, the daughter is bound by the covenant to pay reasonable expenses for the repairs. Judgment would likely be entered for the daughter if the developer's expenditures were not proved to be reasonable and customary. (Choice B) Notice is not a requirement for successors in interest of land benefited by a real covenant (eg, the developer) to enforce it. (Choice C) Persons who acquire land subject to a real covenant through adverse possession cannot enforce the covenant. But the developer acquired Lot 1 by deed—not through adverse possession.\n\nEducational objective: A real covenant will only bind successors in interest if the following elements are met: (1) writing, (2) intent to run, (3) touch and concern, (4) horizontal privity, (5) vertical privity, and (6) notice.\n\nReferences\nRestatement of Property §§ 530–38 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of real covenants).","educationalObjective":"A real covenant will only bind successors in interest if the following elements are met: (1) writing, (2) intent to run, (3) touch and concern, (4) horizontal privity, (5) vertical privity, and (6) notice.","references":"Restatement of Property §§ 530–38 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of real covenants).","rule":"A real covenant will only bind successors in interest if the following elements are met: (1) writing, (2) intent to run, (3) touch and concern, (4) horizontal privity, (5) vertical privity, and (6) notice.","irac":{"issue":"If judgment is for the daughter, what will be the likely reason?","rule":"A real covenant will only bind successors in interest if the following elements are met: (1) writing, (2) intent to run, (3) touch and concern, (4) horizontal privity, (5) vertical privity, and (6) notice.","analysis":"A real covenant is an express promise regarding land that is enforceable by an action for money damages. Although the promising parties can enforce the covenant under contract law, the covenant can only bind successors in interest if the benefit/burden runs with the land. This requires proof of the following elements: Writing – covenant is in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, Lot 1 deed to the cousin)\n\nIntent to run – promising parties intended for the covenant to run to their successors in interest (eg, deed says \"heirs and assigns\" and \"running with the land\")\n\nTouch and concern – covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of the benefited and burdened lands (eg, shared enjoyment of retaining wall)\n\nHorizontal privity – promising parties simultaneously transferred the land and created the covenant (eg, covenant was created when Lot 1 was deeded to the cousin)\n\nVertical privity – successor of the benefited estate has a possessory interest (eg, developer owns Lot 1) and successor of the burdened estate has a promising party's entire estate or ownership interest (eg, daughter has rancher's fee simple in Lot 2)\n\nNotice – person to be bound had notice of the covenant if he/she purchased the property (eg, daughter inherited Lot 2, so it is irrelevant that the cousin's deed was never recorded) (Choice A)\n\nTherefore, the daughter is bound by the covenant to pay reasonable expenses for the repairs. Judgment would likely be entered for the daughter if the developer's expenditures were not proved to be reasonable and customary. (Choice B) Notice is not a requirement for successors in interest of land benefited by a real covenant (eg, the developer) to enforce it. (Choice C) Persons who acquire land subject to a real covenant through adverse possession cannot enforce the covenant. But the developer acquired Lot 1 by deed—not through adverse possession.","conclusion":"Answer D: The developer's expenditures were not proved to be reasonable and customary."},"questionZh":"一位牧场主拥有两块相邻的地块，即地块 1 和地块 2。他将地块 1 转让给了他的表弟。该契约采用通常形式的保证契约，并在适当位置插入以下条款：“授予人为其本人、其继承人和受让人立约并同意，受让人、其继承人和受让人因必须修复目前位于与 2 号地块共同边界的 1 号地块上的挡土墙而产生的任何合理费用，应报销一半的维修费用；通过这一条款，双方打算与土地。”表弟通过普通和常规形式的保修契约将 1 号地块以简单费用转让给一名妇女。该契约省略了任何对挡土墙或任何契约的提及。在牧场主将土地转让给表弟 50 年后，该妇女通过通常形式的保修契约，将 1 号地块以简单费用转让给开发商。该契约省略了任何对挡土墙或契约的提及。除了记录法和规定在逆权占有 10 年后获得所有权的法规外，没有适用于所提出问题的任何方面的法规。牧场主与表弟的契约从未被记录下来。所有其他行为均得到及时、正确的记录。地块 2 现在由牧场主的女儿所有，她通过无遗嘱继承。开发商在挡土墙上花费了 3,500 美元。然后他获得了牧场主的所有权链中的所有原始契约。此后不久，开发商在牧场主与表弟的契约中发现了该契约。开发商要求女儿支付 1,750 美元。当她拒绝后，他采取了适当的行动来追回这笔款项。如果审判是针对女儿的话，可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"牧场主表弟的事迹从未被记录下来。","B":"开发商直到支付费用后才知道该契约，因此不能依赖该契约。","C":"开发商因逆权占有而被禁止。","D":"开发商的支出并未被证明是合理且符合惯例的。"},"explanationZh":"土地实质契约（real covenant）是关于土地的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼强制执行。尽管承诺方可以根据合同法执行契约，但只有在利益/负担与土地相关的情况下，契约才能对利益继承人产生约束力。这需要证明以下要素： 书面形式 - 契约是满足欺诈法规的书面形式（例如，表兄弟的第 1 批契约）\n\n竞选意图 – 承诺各方有意向其利益继承者竞选（例如，契约上写着“继承人和受让人”和“与土地一起竞选”）\n\n接触和关注——与受益和负担土地的使用、享有或占用有关的契约（例如，共同享有挡土墙）\n\n横向相互关系——承诺方同时转让土地并订立契约（例如，当第 1 号地块转让给堂兄时，契约成立）\n\n纵向关联性——受益遗产的继承人拥有占有权益（例如，开发商拥有地块 1），而负担遗产的继承人则拥有承诺方的全部遗产或所有权权益（例如，女儿在地块 2 中拥有牧场主费用）\n\n通知 – 受约束人如果购买了该财产，则已收到该契约的通知（例如，女儿继承了第 2 号地段，因此表弟的契约从未被记录是无关紧要的）（选择 A）\n\n因此，女儿有义务支付合理的维修费用。如果开发商的支出没有被证明是合理和符合惯例的，那么女儿可能会受到判决。 （选择 B） 受益于真正契约的土地利益的继承人（例如开发商）不需要发出通知来强制执行。 （选项C）通过逆权管有获得受真实契约约束的土地的人不能执行该契约。但开发商通过契约获得了 Lot 1，而不是通过逆权占有。\n\n教育目标：真正的盟约只有在满足以下要素的情况下才能约束继承人的利益：（1）书面，（2）跑步意图，（3）接触和关心，（4）横向相互关系，（5）纵向相互关系，以及（6）通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 530–38（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（真实契约的要素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地实质契约（real covenant）只有在满足以下要素的情况下才能约束继承人的利益：（1）书面，（2）跑步意图，（3）接触和关心，（4）横向相互关系，（5）纵向相互关系，以及（6）通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果审判是针对女儿的话，可能的原因是什么？","rule":"土地实质契约（real covenant）只有在满足以下要素的情况下才能约束继承人的利益：（1）书面，（2）跑步意图，（3）接触和关心，（4）横向相互关系，（5）纵向相互关系，以及（6）通知。","analysis":"土地实质契约（real covenant）是关于土地的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼强制执行。尽管承诺方可以根据合同法执行契约，但只有在利益/负担与土地相关的情况下，契约才能对利益继承人产生约束力。这需要证明以下要素： 书面形式 - 契约是满足欺诈法规的书面形式（例如，表兄弟的第 1 批契约）\n\n竞选意图 – 承诺各方有意向其利益继承者竞选（例如，契约上写着“继承人和受让人”和“与土地一起竞选”）\n\n接触和关注——与受益和负担土地的使用、享有或占用有关的契约（例如，共同享有挡土墙）\n\n横向相互关系——承诺方同时转让土地并订立契约（例如，当第 1 号地块转让给堂兄时，契约成立）\n\n纵向关联性——受益遗产的继承人拥有占有权益（例如，开发商拥有地块 1），而负担遗产的继承人则拥有承诺方的全部遗产或所有权权益（例如，女儿在地块 2 中拥有牧场主费用）\n\n通知 – 受约束人如果购买了该财产，则已收到该契约的通知（例如，女儿继承了第 2 号地段，因此表弟的契约从未被记录是无关紧要的）（选择 A）\n\n因此，女儿有义务支付合理的维修费用。如果开发商的支出没有被证明是合理和符合惯例的，那么女儿可能会受到判决。 （选择 B） 受益于真正契约的土地利益的继承人（例如开发商）不需要发出通知来强制执行。 （选项C）通过逆权管有获得受真实契约约束的土地的人不能执行该契约。但开发商通过契约获得了 Lot 1，而不是通过逆权占有。\n\n教育目标：真正的盟约只有在满足以下要素的情况下才能约束继承人的利益：（1）书面，（2）跑步意图，（3）接触和关心，（4）横向相互关系，（5）纵向相互关系，以及（6）通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述 §§ 530–38（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（真实契约的要素）。","conclusion":"答案 D：开发商的支出并未被证明是合理且符合惯例的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-49813d2383f4cad0"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":205,"sourceQuestionPage":600,"sourceQuestionPages":[600],"sourceExplanationPages":[601,602],"sourceAnswerPages":[601,602],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-206","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A seller owned a lot improved with an aging four-story warehouse. The warehouse was built to the lot lines on all four sides. On the street side, recessed loading docks permitted semi-trailers to be backed in. After the tractors were unhooked, the trailers extended into the street and occupied most of one lane of the street. Over the years, as the trailers became larger, the blocking of the street became more severe. The municipality advised the seller that the loading docks could not continue to be used because the trailers blocked the street; it gave the seller 90 days to cease and desist. During the 90 days, the seller sold and conveyed the lot by warranty deed for a substantial consideration to a buyer. The problem of the loading docks was not discussed in the negotiations. Upon expiration of the 90 days, the municipality required the buyer to stop using the loading docks. This action substantially reduced the value of the lot. If the buyer seeks cancellation of the deed and return of all monies paid, which claim should the buyer bring against the seller?","choices":{"A":"Breach of the covenant of warranty.","B":"Failure of consideration.","C":"Misrepresentation.","D":"Mutual mistake."},"answer":"C","explanation":"When disclosure is required to avoid misrepresentation\n\nPrevious assertion Disclosure would prevent previous assertion from being a misrepresentation or fraudulent or material Mistake about basic Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about basic assumption assumption of contract AND\n\nFailure to disclose would constitute lack of good faith & fair dealing Fiduciary relationship Other party is entitled to know fact because of confidential/fiduciary relationship Mistake about writing Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about contents or effect of writing that embodies agreement A misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact that makes a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when: the misrepresentation is fraudulent OR material the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. Nondisclosure of a known fact is tantamount to an assertion that it does not exist if, among other things, the party not disclosing the fact knows that: disclosure would correct the other party's mistake about a basic assumption of the contract and the failure to disclose would violate the duty of good faith and fair dealing. Here, the seller misrepresented that the loading docks could continue to be used (basic assumption of the contract) by failing to disclose to the buyer that this was untrue (lack of good faith and fair dealing). As a result of this misrepresentation, the buyer purchased the lot. And because the misrepresentation was not obviously false or known by the buyer to be false, the buyer justifiably relied on it. Therefore, misrepresentation is the claim the buyer should bring against the seller. (Choice A) A covenant of warranty is the seller's promise to protect the buyer against claims of superior title brought by another party (not seen here). (Choice B) The buyer should not claim failure of consideration because the buyer's agreement with the seller was supported by consideration (ie, conveyance of the lot in exchange for the purchase price).\n\n(Choice D) There was no mutual mistake that would make the contract voidable here because only one party, the buyer, was mistaken about the use of loading docks.\n\nEducational objective: Nondisclosure of a known fact is tantamount to an assertion that it does not exist if the party not disclosing the fact knows that (1) disclosure would correct the other party's mistake about a basic assumption of the contract and (2) the failure to disclose would violate the duty of good faith and fair dealing.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 161 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when nondisclosure is a misrepresentation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"Nondisclosure of a known fact is tantamount to an assertion that it does not exist if the party not disclosing the fact knows that (1) disclosure would correct the other party's mistake about a basic assumption of the contract and (2) the failure to disclose would violate the duty of good faith and fair dealing.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 161 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when nondisclosure is a misrepresentation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).","rule":"Nondisclosure of a known fact is tantamount to an assertion that it does not exist if the party not disclosing the fact knows that (1) disclosure would correct the other party's mistake about a basic assumption of the contract and (2) the failure to disclose would violate the duty of good faith and fair dealing.","irac":{"issue":"If the buyer seeks cancellation of the deed and return of all monies paid, which claim should the buyer bring against the seller?","rule":"Nondisclosure of a known fact is tantamount to an assertion that it does not exist if the party not disclosing the fact knows that (1) disclosure would correct the other party's mistake about a basic assumption of the contract and (2) the failure to disclose would violate the duty of good faith and fair dealing.","analysis":"When disclosure is required to avoid misrepresentation\n\nPrevious assertion Disclosure would prevent previous assertion from being a misrepresentation or fraudulent or material Mistake about basic Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about basic assumption assumption of contract AND\n\nFailure to disclose would constitute lack of good faith & fair dealing Fiduciary relationship Other party is entitled to know fact because of confidential/fiduciary relationship Mistake about writing Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about contents or effect of writing that embodies agreement A misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact that makes a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when: the misrepresentation is fraudulent OR material the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. Nondisclosure of a known fact is tantamount to an assertion that it does not exist if, among other things, the party not disclosing the fact knows that: disclosure would correct the other party's mistake about a basic assumption of the contract and the failure to disclose would violate the duty of good faith and fair dealing. Here, the seller misrepresented that the loading docks could continue to be used (basic assumption of the contract) by failing to disclose to the buyer that this was untrue (lack of good faith and fair dealing). As a result of this misrepresentation, the buyer purchased the lot. And because the misrepresentation was not obviously false or known by the buyer to be false, the buyer justifiably relied on it. Therefore, misrepresentation is the claim the buyer should bring against the seller. (Choice A) A covenant of warranty is the seller's promise to protect the buyer against claims of superior title brought by another party (not seen here). (Choice B) The buyer should not claim failure of consideration because the buyer's agreement with the seller was supported by consideration (ie, conveyance of the lot in exchange for the purchase price).\n\n(Choice D) There was no mutual mistake that would make the contract voidable here because only one party, the buyer, was mistaken about the use of loading docks.","conclusion":"Answer C: Misrepresentation."},"questionZh":"一位卖家拥有一座老化的四层仓库，经过了很大的改善。仓库的四个侧面都建在地块线上。在街道一侧，凹进的装卸码头允许半挂车倒车。拖拉机脱钩后，拖车延伸到街道上，占据了街道的大部分车道。多年来，随着拖车越来越大，街道的堵塞也越来越严重。市政府告知卖方，由于拖车堵塞了街道，装货码头无法继续使用；它给了卖方 90 天的时间来停止。在这 90 天内，卖方通过保修契约出售并转让了该拍品，以向买方支付实质性对价。谈判中没有讨论装卸码头的问题。 90 天到期后，市政府要求买方停止使用装货码头。这一行动大大降低了该地块的价值。如果买方要求取消契约并退还所有已付款项，买方应向卖方提出哪些索赔？","choicesZh":{"A":"违反保证承诺。","B":"考虑失败。","C":"失实陈述。","D":"互相错误。"},"explanationZh":"当需要披露以避免虚假陈述时\n\n先前的断言披露将防止先前的断言成为失实陈述、欺诈或重大错误。有关基本披露的错误将纠正其他方关于合同基本假设的错误，并且\n\n不披露将构成缺乏诚信和公平交易 信托关系 由于保密/信托关系，另一方有权了解事实 书写错误 披露将纠正另一方对体现协议的书面内容或效果的错误 失实陈述是对事实的不真实陈述，在以下情况下，会使受到不利影响的一方宣告合同无效： 失实陈述是欺诈性的，或者 失实陈述合理地诱导了受不利影响方同意合同依赖于虚假陈述。如果不披露该事实的一方知道以下事实，则不披露已知事实就等于断言该事实不存在：披露该事实将纠正另一方关于合同基本假设的错误，并且不披露将违反诚实信用和公平交易的义务。在此，卖方谎称装货码头可以继续使用（合同的基本假设），但未向买方披露这一情况是不真实的（缺乏诚信和公平交易）。由于这种虚假陈述，买家购买了该拍品。而且由于该虚假陈述并非明显虚假或买方不知道是虚假的，因此买方有理由依赖该虚假陈述。因此，虚假陈述是买方应对卖方提出的索赔。 （选择 A） 保修契约是卖方承诺保护买方免受另一方（此处未见）提出的高级所有权索赔的影响。 （选择B） 买方不应主张未对价，因为买方与卖方的协议是有对价支持的（即，以转让拍卖品换取购买价款）。\n\n（选项D）这里不存在导致合同无效的共同错误，因为只有一方（即买方）对装货码头的使用产生了错误。\n\n教育目的：如果不披露事实的一方知道（1）披露将纠正另一方关于合同基本假设的错误，以及（2）不披露将违反诚实信用和公平交易的义务，那么不披露已知事实就等于断言该事实不存在。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二）§ 161 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当不披露属于失实陈述时）。\n\n合同重述（第二）§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果不披露事实的一方知道（1）披露将纠正另一方关于合同基本假设的错误，以及（2）不披露将违反诚实信用和公平交易的义务，那么不披露已知事实就相当于断言该事实不存在。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果买方要求取消契约并退还所有已付款项，买方应向卖方提出哪些索赔？","rule":"如果不披露事实的一方知道（1）披露将纠正另一方关于合同基本假设的错误，以及（2）不披露将违反诚实信用和公平交易的义务，那么不披露已知事实就相当于断言该事实不存在。","analysis":"当需要披露以避免虚假陈述时\n\n先前的断言披露将防止先前的断言成为失实陈述、欺诈或重大错误。有关基本披露的错误将纠正其他方关于合同基本假设的错误，并且\n\n不披露将构成缺乏诚信和公平交易 信托关系 由于保密/信托关系，另一方有权了解事实 书写错误 披露将纠正另一方对体现协议的书面内容或效果的错误 失实陈述是对事实的不真实陈述，在以下情况下，会使受到不利影响的一方宣告合同无效： 失实陈述是欺诈性的，或者 失实陈述合理地诱导了受不利影响方同意合同依赖于虚假陈述。如果不披露该事实的一方知道以下事实，则不披露已知事实就等于断言该事实不存在：披露该事实将纠正另一方关于合同基本假设的错误，并且不披露将违反诚实信用和公平交易的义务。在此，卖方谎称装货码头可以继续使用（合同的基本假设），但未向买方披露这一情况是不真实的（缺乏诚信和公平交易）。由于这种虚假陈述，买家购买了该拍品。而且由于该虚假陈述并非明显虚假或买方不知道是虚假的，因此买方有理由依赖该虚假陈述。因此，虚假陈述是买方应对卖方提出的索赔。 （选择 A） 保修契约是卖方承诺保护买方免受另一方（此处未见）提出的高级所有权索赔的影响。 （选择B） 买方不应主张未对价，因为买方与卖方的协议是有对价支持的（即，以转让拍卖品换取购买价款）。\n\n（选项D）这里不存在导致合同无效的共同错误，因为只有一方（即买方）对装货码头的使用产生了错误。\n\n教育目的：如果不披露事实的一方知道（1）披露将纠正另一方关于合同基本假设的错误，以及（2）不披露将违反诚实信用和公平交易的义务，那么不披露已知事实就等于断言该事实不存在。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二）§ 161 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当不披露属于失实陈述时）。\n\n合同重述（第二）§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。","conclusion":"答案 C：失实陈述。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":206,"sourceQuestionPage":603,"sourceQuestionPages":[603],"sourceExplanationPages":[604,605],"sourceAnswerPages":[604,605],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-207","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A landowner granted an exclusive easement to a farmer to grow wheat for commercial use on a portion of the landowner's land for a period of 10 years. The easement made no mention of the transferability of the easement. For five years, the farmer grew wheat on the land. In year six of the easement, the farmer apportioned one-half of the easement to a rancher for grazing his cattle and the other half of the easement to a horticulturist for growing sunflowers. Unhappy with this arrangement, the landowner filed an action in court to invalidate the apportionment of the easement that he originally granted to the farmer. Which of the following is the landowner's strongest argument that the apportionment of the easement is invalid?","choices":{"A":"Easements that are for commercial use are not transferable.","B":"The burden of the easement did not automatically transfer with the land when the easement was transferred by the farmer.","C":"The exclusive easement was apportioned without the landowner's consent.","D":"The rancher and the horticulturist did not use the land in the same way as the farmer."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Distinctions between easements\n\nType Appurtenant Benefits easement holder's land Benefit & burden automatically transfer when land is conveyed In gross Benefits easement holder personally Burden (not benefit) automatically transfers when land is conveyed Purpose Affirmative Allows easement holder to do something on servient estate\n\nNegative Restricts servient-estate owner's lawful use of servient estate\n\nCreation Express Created by parties in writing that satisfies statute of frauds\n\nIrrevocable License becomes irrevocable due to estoppel or coupling with license another servitude\n\nImplied Implied by law due to necessity (easement by necessity) Implied by fact due to prior use (easement by implication) By prescription Adversely acquired through OCAN—open/notorious, actual, continuous & nonpermissive use Easements are either appurtenant (for the benefit of another parcel of land) OR in gross (for the benefit of the easement holder personally—as seen here). Traditionally, the benefit of an easement in gross could not be transferred, but most courts now allow transfer if the easement is for commercial use or if the parties intended it to be transferable. Whether the easement can also be apportioned (ie, divided up) turns on whether it is: nonexclusive – in which case the easement cannot be divided among multiple transferees or exclusive – in which case the easement generally can be divided among multiple transferees. However, the apportionment of an exclusive easement in gross is subject to the \"one stock\" rule. Under this rule, the collective use that the transferees can make of the easement is limited to the use that the transferor made of the easement (ie, the transferor's \"stock\"). Here, the farmer's easement in gross was transferable because it gave him the right to grow wheat for commercial use, and it could be apportioned because it was exclusive to him (Choice A). Therefore, the landowner's strongest argument that the apportionment is invalid is that the rancher and the horticulturist did not use the land in the same way as the farmer (ie, to grow wheat).\n\n(Choice B) The burden of an easement in gross automatically transfers when the land burdened by the easement (ie, the servient estate) is transferred. But this is irrelevant since the land in question was never transferred. (Choice C) The servient-estate owner's consent is not required to apportion an exclusive easement.\n\nEducational objective: When an exclusive easement in gross is apportioned (ie, divided up), the \"one stock\" rule applies. This rule limits the collective use that the transferees can make of the easement to the use that the transferor made of the easement (ie, the transferor's \"stock\").\n\nReferences\nMiller v. Lutheran Conference & Camp Ass'n, 200 A. 646, 651–52 (Pa. Super. Ct. 1938) (explaining that the one-stock rule applies to the division of profits or easements in gross).\n\n25 Am. Jur. 2d Easements and Licenses § 79 (transferability of easement in gross).","educationalObjective":"When an exclusive easement in gross is apportioned (ie, divided up), the \"one stock\" rule applies. This rule limits the collective use that the transferees can make of the easement to the use that the transferor made of the easement (ie, the transferor's \"stock\").","references":"Miller v. Lutheran Conference & Camp Ass'n, 200 A. 646, 651–52 (Pa. Super. Ct. 1938) (explaining that the one-stock rule applies to the division of profits or easements in gross).\n\n25 Am. Jur. 2d Easements and Licenses § 79 (transferability of easement in gross).","rule":"When an exclusive easement in gross is apportioned (ie, divided up), the \"one stock\" rule applies. This rule limits the collective use that the transferees can make of the easement to the use that the transferor made of the easement (ie, the transferor's \"stock\").","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the landowner's strongest argument that the apportionment of the easement is invalid?","rule":"When an exclusive easement in gross is apportioned (ie, divided up), the \"one stock\" rule applies. This rule limits the collective use that the transferees can make of the easement to the use that the transferor made of the easement (ie, the transferor's \"stock\").","analysis":"Distinctions between easements\n\nType Appurtenant Benefits easement holder's land Benefit & burden automatically transfer when land is conveyed In gross Benefits easement holder personally Burden (not benefit) automatically transfers when land is conveyed Purpose Affirmative Allows easement holder to do something on servient estate\n\nNegative Restricts servient-estate owner's lawful use of servient estate\n\nCreation Express Created by parties in writing that satisfies statute of frauds\n\nIrrevocable License becomes irrevocable due to estoppel or coupling with license another servitude\n\nImplied Implied by law due to necessity (easement by necessity) Implied by fact due to prior use (easement by implication) By prescription Adversely acquired through OCAN—open/notorious, actual, continuous & nonpermissive use Easements are either appurtenant (for the benefit of another parcel of land) OR in gross (for the benefit of the easement holder personally—as seen here). Traditionally, the benefit of an easement in gross could not be transferred, but most courts now allow transfer if the easement is for commercial use or if the parties intended it to be transferable. Whether the easement can also be apportioned (ie, divided up) turns on whether it is: nonexclusive – in which case the easement cannot be divided among multiple transferees or exclusive – in which case the easement generally can be divided among multiple transferees. However, the apportionment of an exclusive easement in gross is subject to the \"one stock\" rule. Under this rule, the collective use that the transferees can make of the easement is limited to the use that the transferor made of the easement (ie, the transferor's \"stock\"). Here, the farmer's easement in gross was transferable because it gave him the right to grow wheat for commercial use, and it could be apportioned because it was exclusive to him (Choice A). Therefore, the landowner's strongest argument that the apportionment is invalid is that the rancher and the horticulturist did not use the land in the same way as the farmer (ie, to grow wheat).\n\n(Choice B) The burden of an easement in gross automatically transfers when the land burdened by the easement (ie, the servient estate) is transferred. But this is irrelevant since the land in question was never transferred. (Choice C) The servient-estate owner's consent is not required to apportion an exclusive easement.","conclusion":"Answer D: The rancher and the horticulturist did not use the land in the same way as the farmer."},"questionZh":"土地所有者向农民授予专有地役权，允许农民在其部分土地上种植用于商业用途的小麦，为期 10 年。该地役权没有提及地役权的可转让性。五年来，农民在这片土地上种植小麦。在地役权的第六年，农民将地役权的一半分配给牧场主用于放牧牛群，另一半分配给园艺师用于种植向日葵。土地所有者对这一安排不满意，向法院提起诉讼，要求使他最初授予农民的地役权分配无效。以下哪一项是土地所有者认为地役权分配无效的最有力论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"用于商业用途的地役权不可转让。","B":"当农民转让地役权时，地役权的负担并不自动随土地转移。","C":"专属地役权的分配未经土地所有者的同意。","D":"牧场主和园艺师使用土地的方式与农民不同。"},"explanationZh":"地役权之间的区别\n\n类型 附属福利 地役权持有人的土地 土地转让时，利益和负担自动转移 总利益 地役权持有人个人 土地转让时，负担（而非福利）自动转移 目的 肯定式 允许地役权持有人在受役地做某事\n\n负面 限制供役地所有人合法使用供役地\n\nCreation Express 由各方以书面形式创建，符合欺诈法规\n\n不可撤销的许可由于禁止反言或与许可另一奴役相结合而变得不可撤销\n\n默示 因必要性而由法律默示（因必要性地役权） 因先前使用而因事实默示（因地役权而暗示地役权） 通过时效 通过 OCAN 反向获得 - 公开/臭名昭著、实际、连续和非许可使用 地役权可以是从属的（为了另一块土地的利益），也可以是总量（为了地役权持有人个人的利益 - 如此处所示）。传统上，地役权的总利益不能转让，但如果地役权用于商业用途或当事人打算转让，大多数法院现在允许转让。地役权是否也可以分配（即分割）取决于它是否： 非排他性 – 在这种情况下，地役权不能在多个受让人之间分配或排他性 – 在这种情况下，地役权通常可以在多个受让人之间分配。然而，排他性地役权总额的分配须遵守“一股”规则。根据该规则，受让人对地役权的集体使用仅限于转让人对地役权（即转让人的“股票”）的使用。在这里，农民的地役权总额是可以转让的，因为它赋予他种植商业用途小麦的权利，并且可以分配，因为它是他专有的（选择A）。因此，土地所有者认为分配无效的最有力论据是牧场主和园艺师没有以与农民相同的方式使用土地（即种植小麦）。\n\n（选择B） 当地役权负担的土地（即役用地）转移时，地役权负担自动转移。但这无关紧要，因为相关土地从未转让。 （选项C） 分配专属地役权不需要征得供役地所有人的同意。\n\n教育目标：当分配（即分割）总的排他性地役权时，适用“一股”规则。该规则将受让人对地役权的集体使用限制为转让人对地役权（即转让人的“股票”）的使用。\n\n参考文献\nMiller v. Lutheran Conference & Camp Ass'n, 200 A. 646, 651–52 (Pa. Super. Ct. 1938)（解释说单一股票规则适用于利润或地役权的总分配）。\n\n上午 25 点。尤尔。 2d 地役权和许可证 § 79（地役权总量的可转让性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当分配（即分割）总排他性地役权时，适用“一股”规则。该规则将受让人对地役权的集体使用限制为转让人对地役权（即转让人的“股票”）的使用。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是土地所有者认为地役权分配无效的最有力论据？","rule":"当分配（即分割）总排他性地役权时，适用“一股”规则。该规则将受让人对地役权的集体使用限制为转让人对地役权（即转让人的“股票”）的使用。","analysis":"地役权之间的区别\n\n类型 附属福利 地役权持有人的土地 土地转让时，利益和负担自动转移 总利益 地役权持有人个人 土地转让时，负担（而非福利）自动转移 目的 肯定式 允许地役权持有人在受役地做某事\n\n负面 限制供役地所有人合法使用供役地\n\nCreation Express 由各方以书面形式创建，符合欺诈法规\n\n不可撤销的许可由于禁止反言或与许可另一奴役相结合而变得不可撤销\n\n默示 因必要性而由法律默示（因必要性地役权） 因先前使用而因事实默示（因地役权而暗示地役权） 通过时效 通过 OCAN 反向获得 - 公开/臭名昭著、实际、连续和非许可使用 地役权可以是从属的（为了另一块土地的利益），也可以是总量（为了地役权持有人个人的利益 - 如此处所示）。传统上，地役权的总利益不能转让，但如果地役权用于商业用途或当事人打算转让，大多数法院现在允许转让。地役权是否也可以分配（即分割）取决于它是否： 非排他性 – 在这种情况下，地役权不能在多个受让人之间分配或排他性 – 在这种情况下，地役权通常可以在多个受让人之间分配。然而，排他性地役权总额的分配须遵守“一股”规则。根据该规则，受让人对地役权的集体使用仅限于转让人对地役权（即转让人的“股票”）的使用。在这里，农民的地役权总额是可以转让的，因为它赋予他种植商业用途小麦的权利，并且可以分配，因为它是他专有的（选择A）。因此，土地所有者认为分配无效的最有力论据是牧场主和园艺师没有以与农民相同的方式使用土地（即种植小麦）。\n\n（选择B） 当地役权负担的土地（即役用地）转移时，地役权负担自动转移。但这无关紧要，因为相关土地从未转让。 （选项C） 分配专属地役权不需要征得供役地所有人的同意。\n\n教育目标：当分配（即分割）总的排他性地役权时，适用“一股”规则。该规则将受让人对地役权的集体使用限制为转让人对地役权（即转让人的“股票”）的使用。\n\n参考文献\nMiller v. Lutheran Conference & Camp Ass'n, 200 A. 646, 651–52 (Pa. Super. Ct. 1938)（解释说单一股票规则适用于利润或地役权的总分配）。\n\n上午 25 点。尤尔。 2d 地役权和许可证 § 79（地役权总量的可转让性）。","conclusion":"答案 D：牧场主和园艺师使用土地的方式与农民不同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":207,"sourceQuestionPage":606,"sourceQuestionPages":[606],"sourceExplanationPages":[607,608],"sourceAnswerPages":[607,608],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-208","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A seller owns a 400-acre tract of land with 5,000 feet of frontage on a county highway. The seller and a buyer entered into a written agreement for the sale of a portion of the tract identified only as \"a parcel of land, containing not less than 100 acres and having not less than 1,000 feet of frontage on the county highway, whose exact location and dimensions are to be determined by the parties hereto, at a price of $8,000 per acre.\" Shortly after the execution of the agreement, the parties met to stake out the parcel of land to be sold, but they could not agree. The disagreement intensified, and the seller repudiated the contract. The buyer has sued the seller for specific performance. The seller has asserted all available defenses. Is the buyer entitled to specific performance of the contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because a contract for the sale of real property that requires further agreement on an essential element cannot be specifically enforced.","B":"No, because the purchase price was not fixed by, nor determinable under, the contract terms.","C":"Yes, because the contract bound the parties to act in good faith and to agree upon the specific land to be conveyed.","D":"Yes, because the equity powers of the court enable the court to appoint a master, or to take other appropriate action, to identify the land to be conveyed."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of land (ie, real estate contracts) are enforceable only if they are in writing, are signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Essential terms include: the identity of the parties words of intent to buy or sell a description of the property and the sales price. Although courts may liberally construe the property-description requirement, the writing must contain some evidence of the specific property to be sold. Here, the writing expressly stated that the parties had yet to agree on what land was to be conveyed. Therefore, the statute of frauds makes the contract unenforceable. (Choice B) The sales price is also an essential term. It can be listed in gross (eg, at a set price) or determined by the actual quantity of land sold (eg, at a per-acre price). Therefore, the agreement to purchase the land \"at a price of $8,000 per acre\" satisfies this requirement. (Choices C & D) The statute of frauds requires that a contract for the sale of land identify the land to be sold. Good faith and equity cannot provide relief for this omission.\n\nEducational objective: The statute of frauds requires that land-sale contracts contain all essential terms—ie, the identity of the parties, words of intent to buy or sell, a description of the property, and the sales price.\n\nReferences\n73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).","educationalObjective":"The statute of frauds requires that land-sale contracts contain all essential terms—ie, the identity of the parties, words of intent to buy or sell, a description of the property, and the sales price.","references":"73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).","rule":"The statute of frauds requires that land-sale contracts contain all essential terms—ie, the identity of the parties, words of intent to buy or sell, a description of the property, and the sales price.","irac":{"issue":"Is the buyer entitled to specific performance of the contract?","rule":"The statute of frauds requires that land-sale contracts contain all essential terms—ie, the identity of the parties, words of intent to buy or sell, a description of the property, and the sales price.","analysis":"Under the statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of land (ie, real estate contracts) are enforceable only if they are in writing, are signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Essential terms include: the identity of the parties words of intent to buy or sell a description of the property and the sales price. Although courts may liberally construe the property-description requirement, the writing must contain some evidence of the specific property to be sold. Here, the writing expressly stated that the parties had yet to agree on what land was to be conveyed. Therefore, the statute of frauds makes the contract unenforceable. (Choice B) The sales price is also an essential term. It can be listed in gross (eg, at a set price) or determined by the actual quantity of land sold (eg, at a per-acre price). Therefore, the agreement to purchase the land \"at a price of $8,000 per acre\" satisfies this requirement. (Choices C & D) The statute of frauds requires that a contract for the sale of land identify the land to be sold. Good faith and equity cannot provide relief for this omission.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because a contract for the sale of real property that requires further agreement on an essential element cannot be specifically enforced."},"questionZh":"一位卖家在县高速公路上拥有一块 400 英亩的土地，临街面积为 5,000 英尺。卖方和买方签订了一份书面协议，出售部分土地，仅指定为“一块土地，面积不少于 100 英亩，临县公路的临街面积不少于 1,000 英尺，其确切位置和尺寸由双方确定，价格为每英亩 8,000 美元。”协议签订后不久，双方就该块土地进行了放样，但未能达成一致。分歧愈演愈烈，卖方取消了合同。买方已就具体履行情况起诉卖方。卖方已提出所有可用的抗辩理由。买方是否有权要求具体履行合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为需要就基本要素达成进一步协议的不动产销售合同无法具体执行。","B":"不可以，因为购买价格不是由合同条款确定的，也不是根据合同条款确定的。","C":"是的，因为合同约束双方真诚行事并就要转让的具体土地达成一致。","D":"是的，因为法院的股权权力使法院能够任命一位主人，或采取其他适当的行动，以确定要转让的土地。"},"explanationZh":"根据反欺诈法，土地销售合同（即房地产合同）只有在书面形式、由被执行方签署并包含所有基本条款的情况下才可执行。基本条款包括：当事人身份、购买或出售意向、房产描述和销售价格等。尽管法院可以自由地解释财产描述要求，但书面文件必须包含要出售的特定财产的一些证据。此处，书面明确指出，双方尚未就转让哪些土地达成一致。因此，欺诈法使合同无法执行。 （选项B） 销售价格也是一个必不可少的术语。它可以按总量列出（例如，按固定价格），也可以根据实际出售的土地数量确定（例如，按每英亩价格）。因此，“以每英亩8,000美元的价格”购买土地的协议满足了这一要求。 （选项 C 和 D） 欺诈法要求土地出售合同明确要出售的土地。善意和公平不能弥补这种疏忽。\n\n教育目标：欺诈法要求土地销售合同包含所有基本条款，即当事人的身份、购买或出售的意向、财产描述和销售价格。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。","educationalObjectiveZh":"欺诈法要求土地销售合同包含所有基本条款，即当事人的身份、购买或出售意向的文字、财产的描述以及销售价格。","iracZh":{"issue":"买方是否有权要求具体履行合同？","rule":"欺诈法要求土地销售合同包含所有基本条款，即当事人的身份、购买或出售意向的文字、财产的描述以及销售价格。","analysis":"根据反欺诈法，土地销售合同（即房地产合同）只有在书面形式、由被执行方签署并包含所有基本条款的情况下才可执行。基本条款包括：当事人身份、购买或出售意向、房产描述和销售价格等。尽管法院可以自由地解释财产描述要求，但书面文件必须包含要出售的特定财产的一些证据。此处，书面明确指出，双方尚未就转让哪些土地达成一致。因此，欺诈法使合同无法执行。 （选项B） 销售价格也是一个必不可少的术语。它可以按总量列出（例如，按固定价格），也可以根据实际出售的土地数量确定（例如，按每英亩价格）。因此，“以每英亩8,000美元的价格”购买土地的协议满足了这一要求。 （选项 C 和 D） 欺诈法要求土地出售合同明确要出售的土地。善意和公平不能弥补这种疏忽。\n\n教育目标：欺诈法要求土地销售合同包含所有基本条款，即当事人的身份、购买或出售的意向、财产描述和销售价格。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为需要就基本要素达成进一步协议的不动产销售合同无法具体执行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-0ee184140caffe0f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":208,"sourceQuestionPage":609,"sourceQuestionPages":[609],"sourceExplanationPages":[610],"sourceAnswerPages":[610],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-209","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A contractor retained a tract of 100 acres after he had developed the adjoining 400 acres as a residential subdivision. The contractor had effectively imposed restrictive covenants on each lot in the 400 acres. A buyer offered the contractor a good price for a five-acre tract located in a corner of the retained 100-acre tract far away from the existing 400-acre residential subdivision. The contractor conveyed the five-acre tract to the buyer and imposed the same restrictive covenants on the five-acre tract as he had imposed on the lots in the adjoining 400 acres. The contractor further covenanted that when he sold the remaining 95 acres of the 100-acre tract, he would impose the same restrictive covenants in the deed or deeds for the 95 acres. The contractor's conveyance to the buyer was promptly and properly recorded. However, shortly thereafter, the contractor conveyed the remaining 95 acres to a real estate developer for $1,000,000 by a deed that made no mention of any restrictive covenants. The real estate developer had no actual knowledge of the restrictive covenants in the buyer's deed. The real estate developer now proposes to build an industrial park that would violate such restrictive covenants if they are applicable. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.\" The buyer filed an appropriate action to enforce the restrictive covenants against the real estate developer's 95-acre tract. If the real estate developer prevails, what will be the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"A covenant to impose restrictions is an illegal restraint on alienation.","B":"Negative reciprocal covenants are not generally recognized.","C":"The deed imposing the restrictions was not in the chain of title for the 95 acres when the real estate developer purchased the land.","D":"The disparity in acreage means that the covenant can be personal only to the contractor."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Notice of competing property interests\n\nActual Buyer has direct knowledge of prior interest in land notice\n\nRecord Document showing prior interest in land is properly recorded & appears in notice buyer's chain of title\n\nInquiry Buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt reasonable notice person to investigate (eg, visible use of property, reference to unrecorded transaction in recorded instrument)\n\nA real covenant is an express promise to do (affirmative) or not do (restrictive) something on the land that is enforceable by an action for money damages. When a real covenant is not in writing—as seen with the real estate developer's deed—it can be enforced as an equitable servitude that is implied from a common scheme if three elements are met: Intent to create common scheme – the owner intended to impose a covenant on all lots in the subdivision (eg, covenant imposed on each lot in the 400 acres and the buyer's 5-acre tract)\n\nRestrictive servitude – the intended covenant was a promise not to do something on the land (explicitly stated here)\n\nNotice – the person to be bound by the covenant had actual, record, or inquiry notice of it\n\nHere, the real estate developer had no actual notice of the restrictive covenant in the buyer's deed. And since the buyer's 5-acre tract was separate from the 400-acre residential subdivision, the real estate developer lacked inquiry notice that his 95-acre lot was meant to be a part of either development. However, the buyer's deed containing the covenant was recorded. Therefore, the real estate developer would lack record notice, and therefore prevail, if the buyer's deed was not in the chain of title for the 95 acres when the real estate developer purchased the land. (Choice A) A covenant is an illegal restraint on alienation if it imposes unreasonable restrictions on the sale or transfer of land—not merely on the use of land (as seen here). (Choice B) Negative reciprocal covenants (ie, implied equitable servitudes) are generally recognized when there is a common scheme of development (eg, a residential subdivision). (Choice D) The disparity in lot acreage is immaterial in determining whether the covenant is personal (ie, enforceable only between the promising parties) or runs with the land (ie, enforceable against successors in interest).\n\nEducational objective: Equitable servitudes can be implied from a common scheme if three elements are met: (1) intent to create a common scheme, (2) restrictive covenant, and (3) actual, record, or inquiry notice.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.14 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (equitable servitude implied from a common scheme in a residential subdivision).\n\nRestatement of Property § 539 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of equitable servitudes).","educationalObjective":"Equitable servitudes can be implied from a common scheme if three elements are met: (1) intent to create a common scheme, (2) restrictive covenant, and (3) actual, record, or inquiry notice.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.14 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (equitable servitude implied from a common scheme in a residential subdivision).\n\nRestatement of Property § 539 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of equitable servitudes).","rule":"Equitable servitudes can be implied from a common scheme if three elements are met: (1) intent to create a common scheme, (2) restrictive covenant, and (3) actual, record, or inquiry notice.","irac":{"issue":"If the real estate developer prevails, what will be the most likely reason?","rule":"Equitable servitudes can be implied from a common scheme if three elements are met: (1) intent to create a common scheme, (2) restrictive covenant, and (3) actual, record, or inquiry notice.","analysis":"Notice of competing property interests\n\nActual Buyer has direct knowledge of prior interest in land notice\n\nRecord Document showing prior interest in land is properly recorded & appears in notice buyer's chain of title\n\nInquiry Buyer knows, or should know, of circumstances that would prompt reasonable notice person to investigate (eg, visible use of property, reference to unrecorded transaction in recorded instrument)\n\nA real covenant is an express promise to do (affirmative) or not do (restrictive) something on the land that is enforceable by an action for money damages. When a real covenant is not in writing—as seen with the real estate developer's deed—it can be enforced as an equitable servitude that is implied from a common scheme if three elements are met: Intent to create common scheme – the owner intended to impose a covenant on all lots in the subdivision (eg, covenant imposed on each lot in the 400 acres and the buyer's 5-acre tract)\n\nRestrictive servitude – the intended covenant was a promise not to do something on the land (explicitly stated here)\n\nNotice – the person to be bound by the covenant had actual, record, or inquiry notice of it\n\nHere, the real estate developer had no actual notice of the restrictive covenant in the buyer's deed. And since the buyer's 5-acre tract was separate from the 400-acre residential subdivision, the real estate developer lacked inquiry notice that his 95-acre lot was meant to be a part of either development. However, the buyer's deed containing the covenant was recorded. Therefore, the real estate developer would lack record notice, and therefore prevail, if the buyer's deed was not in the chain of title for the 95 acres when the real estate developer purchased the land. (Choice A) A covenant is an illegal restraint on alienation if it imposes unreasonable restrictions on the sale or transfer of land—not merely on the use of land (as seen here). (Choice B) Negative reciprocal covenants (ie, implied equitable servitudes) are generally recognized when there is a common scheme of development (eg, a residential subdivision). (Choice D) The disparity in lot acreage is immaterial in determining whether the covenant is personal (ie, enforceable only between the promising parties) or runs with the land (ie, enforceable against successors in interest).","conclusion":"Answer C: The deed imposing the restrictions was not in the chain of title for the 95 acres when the real estate developer purchased the land."},"questionZh":"一名承包商将毗邻的 400 英亩土地开发为住宅小区后，保留了 100 英亩土地。承包商实际上对这 400 英亩的每一块土地都施加了限制性契约。一位买家向承包商提出了一个很好的价格，购买了一块 5 英亩的土地，该土地位于保留的 100 英亩土地的一角，远离现有的 400 英亩住宅小区。承包商将 5 英亩的土地转让给买方，并对这 5 英亩的土地施加了与对邻近 400 英亩土地施加的相同的限制性契约。承包商进一步承诺，当他出售 100 英亩土地中剩余的 95 英亩土地时，他将在这 95 英亩土地的契约中施加相同的限制性契约。承包商向买方的运输得到及时、正确的记录。然而，此后不久，承包商通过一份未提及任何限制性契约的契约，以 1,000,000 美元的价格将剩余的 95 英亩土地转让给了一家房地产开发商。房地产开发商实际上并不了解买方契约中的限制性条款。房地产开发商现在提议建设一个工业园区，如果这些限制性条款适用的话，就会违反这些条款。该司法管辖区的登记法（recording act）定：“除非依法记录，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者。”买方提起适当诉讼，要求对房地产开发商的 95 英亩土地执行限制性契约。如果房地产开发商胜诉，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"施加限制的契约是对转让的非法限制。","B":"消极的互惠契约并未得到普遍认可。","C":"当房地产开发商购买这片土地时，施加限制的契约并不在这 95 英亩土地的所有权链中。","D":"面积的差异意味着该契约只能针对承包商个人。"},"explanationZh":"竞争财产利益的通知\n\n实际买家直接了解土地通知中的优先权益\n\n显示先前对土地的兴趣的记录文件已正确记录并出现在通知买方的所有权链中\n\n询问 买方知道或应该知道会促使合理通知人调查的情况（例如，财产的明显使用、提及记录文书中未记录的交易）\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做（肯定的）或不做（限制性的）某事的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼强制执行。当土地实质契约（real covenant）不是书面形式时（如房地产开发商的契约所示），如果满足三个要素，则可以作为共同计划中隐含的公平劳役来强制执行： 意图制定共同计划 - 业主打算对细分中的所有地块施加契约（例如，对 400 英亩和买方的 5 英亩土地中的每个地块施加契约）\n\n限制性奴役——预期的契约是承诺不在土地上做任何事情（此处明确说明）\n\n通知——受该契约约束的人拥有该契约的实际、记录或查询通知\n\n在此，房地产开发商并没有在买方契约中实际告知限制性条款。由于买家的 5 英亩土地与 400 英亩的住宅小区是分开的，房地产开发商没有收到询问通知，表明他的 95 英亩土地应该属于任一开发项目的一部分。然而，包含该契约的买方契约已被记录。因此，如果房地产开发商购买土地时买方的契约不在这 95 英亩土地的所有权链中，则房地产开发商将缺乏记录通知，因此优先。 （选项 A）如果契约对土地的出售或转让施加不合理的限制，而不仅仅是对土地的使用（如此处所示），则它是对转让的非法限制。 （选择B） 当存在共同的开发计划（例如，住宅土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning））时，消极互惠契约（即隐含的公平劳役）通常得到承认。 （选项D）地块面积的差异对于确定契约是个人契约（即仅在承诺方之间可执行）还是随土地一起执行（即可对利益继承人强制执行）而言并不重要。\n\n教育目标：如果满足三个要素，则可以从共同计划中隐含公平的劳役：(1) 制定共同计划的意图，(2) 限制性契约，以及 (3) 实际、记录或查询通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.14（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（住宅土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）中的共同计划隐含的公平劳役）。\n\n财产重述 § 539（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（公平劳役的要素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果满足三个要素，则可以从共同计划中隐含公平的劳役：(1) 制定共同计划的意图，(2) 限制性契约，以及 (3) 实际、记录或查询通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果房地产开发商胜诉，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"如果满足三个要素，则可以从共同计划中隐含公平的劳役：(1) 制定共同计划的意图，(2) 限制性契约，以及 (3) 实际、记录或查询通知。","analysis":"竞争财产利益的通知\n\n实际买家直接了解土地通知中的优先权益\n\n显示先前对土地的兴趣的记录文件已正确记录并出现在通知买方的所有权链中\n\n询问 买方知道或应该知道会促使合理通知人调查的情况（例如，财产的明显使用、提及记录文书中未记录的交易）\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做（肯定的）或不做（限制性的）某事的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼强制执行。当土地实质契约（real covenant）不是书面形式时（如房地产开发商的契约所示），如果满足三个要素，则可以作为共同计划中隐含的公平劳役来强制执行： 意图制定共同计划 - 业主打算对细分中的所有地块施加契约（例如，对 400 英亩和买方的 5 英亩土地中的每个地块施加契约）\n\n限制性奴役——预期的契约是承诺不在土地上做任何事情（此处明确说明）\n\n通知——受该契约约束的人拥有该契约的实际、记录或查询通知\n\n在此，房地产开发商并没有在买方契约中实际告知限制性条款。由于买家的 5 英亩土地与 400 英亩的住宅小区是分开的，房地产开发商没有收到询问通知，表明他的 95 英亩土地应该属于任一开发项目的一部分。然而，包含该契约的买方契约已被记录。因此，如果房地产开发商购买土地时买方的契约不在这 95 英亩土地的所有权链中，则房地产开发商将缺乏记录通知，因此优先。 （选项 A）如果契约对土地的出售或转让施加不合理的限制，而不仅仅是对土地的使用（如此处所示），则它是对转让的非法限制。 （选择B） 当存在共同的开发计划（例如，住宅土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning））时，消极互惠契约（即隐含的公平劳役）通常得到承认。 （选项D）地块面积的差异对于确定契约是个人契约（即仅在承诺方之间可执行）还是随土地一起执行（即可对利益继承人强制执行）而言并不重要。\n\n教育目标：如果满足三个要素，则可以从共同计划中隐含公平的劳役：(1) 制定共同计划的意图，(2) 限制性契约，以及 (3) 实际、记录或查询通知。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.14（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（住宅土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）中的共同计划隐含的公平劳役）。\n\n财产重述 § 539（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（公平劳役的要素）。","conclusion":"答案 C：当房地产开发商购买这片土地时，施加限制的契约并不在这 95 英亩土地的所有权链中。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":209,"sourceQuestionPage":611,"sourceQuestionPages":[611],"sourceExplanationPages":[612,613],"sourceAnswerPages":[612,613],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-210","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man owned two adjoining parcels known as Lot 1 and Lot 2. Both parcels fronted a main street and abutted a public alley in the rear. Lot 1 was improved with a commercial building that covered all the main-street frontage of Lot 1. There was a large parking lot on the rear of Lot 1 with access from the alley only. Fifteen years ago, the man leased Lot 1 to a tenant for 15 years. The tenant has continuously occupied Lot 1 since that time. Thirteen years ago, without the man's permission, the tenant began to use a driveway on Lot 2 as a better access between the main street and the parking lot than the alley. Eight years ago, the man conveyed Lot 2 to his friend and, five years ago, the man conveyed Lot 1 to the tenant by a deed that recited \"together with all the appurtenances.\" Until last week, the tenant continuously used the driveway over Lot 2 to reach the parking lot in the rear of Lot 1. Last week the friend commenced construction of a building on Lot 2 and blocked the driveway over Lot 2 used by the tenant to reach the parking lot in the rear of Lot 1 from the main street. The tenant has commenced an action to enforce an easement by prescription and to restrain the friend from blocking the driveway. The period of time to acquire rights by prescription in the jurisdiction is 10 years. If the tenant loses, what will be the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"No easement was mentioned in the deed from the man to the friend.","B":"The man owned both Lot 1 and Lot 2 until eight years ago.","C":"The mere use of an easement is not adverse possession.","D":"The tenant has access to the parking lot from the alley."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An easement by prescription is a presumptive nonpossessory right to use another's land for a limited purpose that is acquired through OCAN—ie, use that is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Actual – use of the land Nonpermissive – hostile and adverse to the owner Tenants cannot acquire a prescriptive easement over (1) the leased premises since they have permission to use them or (2) other lands owned by their landlord because tenants are said to stand in their landlord's place during the lease and therefore lack an adverse interest. Here, the man leased Lot 1 to the tenant, who began to use the driveway on Lot 2 without the man's permission. Since the man owned both lots, the tenant's use did not become adverse until the man conveyed Lot 2 to his friend eight years ago. As a result, the tenant has not satisfied the 10-year statutory period to acquire a prescriptive easement and will likely lose. (Choice A) Although no easement was mentioned in the friend's deed, an easement transfers automatically with the transfer of the servient (ie, burdened) estate. So had the tenant acquired an easement by prescription over Lot 2, the burden of the easement would have automatically transferred from the man to the friend when Lot 2 was conveyed. (Choice C) Adverse possession requires that a person possess the land, but mere use is sufficient to obtain a prescriptive easement so long as the other elements are met (not seen here). (Choice D) The tenant's access to the Lot 1 parking lot from the alley would not prevent him from acquiring an easement by prescription over Lot 2. But it would prevent him from acquiring an easement by necessity since this type of easement only arises when it is strictly necessary for the use and enjoyment of property. This includes when property (eg, Lot 1 parking lot) cannot be reached from a public road (eg, public alley).\n\nEducational objective: Tenants cannot acquire an easement by prescription (ie, OCAN) over the leased premises or other lands owned by their landlord.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes §§ 2.16–2.17 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by prescription).","educationalObjective":"Tenants cannot acquire an easement by prescription (ie, OCAN) over the leased premises or other lands owned by their landlord.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes §§ 2.16–2.17 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by prescription).","rule":"Tenants cannot acquire an easement by prescription (ie, OCAN) over the leased premises or other lands owned by their landlord.","irac":{"issue":"If the tenant loses, what will be the most likely reason?","rule":"Tenants cannot acquire an easement by prescription (ie, OCAN) over the leased premises or other lands owned by their landlord.","analysis":"An easement by prescription is a presumptive nonpossessory right to use another's land for a limited purpose that is acquired through OCAN—ie, use that is: Open and notorious – apparent or visible to a reasonable owner Continuous – uninterrupted for the statutory period Actual – use of the land Nonpermissive – hostile and adverse to the owner Tenants cannot acquire a prescriptive easement over (1) the leased premises since they have permission to use them or (2) other lands owned by their landlord because tenants are said to stand in their landlord's place during the lease and therefore lack an adverse interest. Here, the man leased Lot 1 to the tenant, who began to use the driveway on Lot 2 without the man's permission. Since the man owned both lots, the tenant's use did not become adverse until the man conveyed Lot 2 to his friend eight years ago. As a result, the tenant has not satisfied the 10-year statutory period to acquire a prescriptive easement and will likely lose. (Choice A) Although no easement was mentioned in the friend's deed, an easement transfers automatically with the transfer of the servient (ie, burdened) estate. So had the tenant acquired an easement by prescription over Lot 2, the burden of the easement would have automatically transferred from the man to the friend when Lot 2 was conveyed. (Choice C) Adverse possession requires that a person possess the land, but mere use is sufficient to obtain a prescriptive easement so long as the other elements are met (not seen here). (Choice D) The tenant's access to the Lot 1 parking lot from the alley would not prevent him from acquiring an easement by prescription over Lot 2. But it would prevent him from acquiring an easement by necessity since this type of easement only arises when it is strictly necessary for the use and enjoyment of property. This includes when property (eg, Lot 1 parking lot) cannot be reached from a public road (eg, public alley).","conclusion":"Answer B: The man owned both Lot 1 and Lot 2 until eight years ago."},"questionZh":"一名男子拥有两块相邻的地块，分别为 1 号地块和 2 号地块。这两块地块都面向一条主要街道，后面紧邻一条公共小巷。 1 号地块进行了改进，修建了一座商业建筑，覆盖了 1 号地块的所有主街道临街面。1 号地块的后面有一个大型停车场，只能从小巷进入。 15 年前，该男子将 1 号地块租给一名租户，为期 15 年。从那时起，租户就一直占用1号地块。十三年前，在未经该男子许可的情况下，租户开始使用 2 号地段的车道作为主街和停车场之间比小巷更好的通道。八年前，该男子将 2 号地块转让给了他的朋友，五年前，该男子通过一份注明“连同所有附属物”的契约将 1 号地块转让给了房客。直到上周，租户一直使用2号地块上的车道到达1号地块后方的停车场。上周，该朋友开始在2号地块上建造一栋建筑物，并堵塞了租户从主街道通往1号地块后方停车场的2号地块上的车道。租户已开始采取行动，通过时效强制执行地役权，并阻止朋友堵塞车道。在管辖范围内凭时效取得权利的期限为10年。如果租客败诉，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该男子给朋友的契约中没有提及地役权。","B":"直到八年前，该男子还拥有 1 号地块和 2 号地块。","C":"仅仅使用地役权并不构成逆权占有。","D":"租客可以从小巷进入停车场。"},"explanationZh":"时效地役权是通过 OCAN 获得的、为有限目的使用他人土地的推定非占有性权利，即使用： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者来说明显或可见 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 实际 – 使用土地 非许可 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 租户不能获得对 (1) 租赁房屋（因为他们已获得使用许可）或 (2) 其拥有的其他土地的规定地役权房东，因为据说租客在租赁期间代表房东的立场，因此没有不利利益。该男子将 1 号地块租给了租户，而租户未经该男子许可就开始使用 2 号地块的车道。由于该男子拥有这两个地块，因此租户的使用并没有变得不利，直到该男子八年前将2号地块转让给他的朋友。因此，租户未满足获得规定地役权的 10 年法定期限，并可能会损失。 （选项A） 虽然朋友的契约中没有提及地役权，但地役权会随着役（即负担）遗产的转移而自动转移。因此，如果租户通过规定获得了第 2 号地块的地役权，那么当第 2 号地块转让时，地役权的负担将自动从该男子转移到朋友身上。 （选择C）逆权占有要求一个人拥有土地，但只要满足其他要素（此处未看到），仅仅使用就足以获得规定的地役权。 （选项 D）租户从巷子进入 1 号地块停车场不会妨碍他通过规定获得 2 号地块的地役权。但这会阻止他获得必要的地役权，因为这种类型的地役权仅在使用和享受财产绝对必要时才会出现。这包括无法从公共道路（例如公共小巷）到达财产（例如，1 号停车场）的情况。\n\n教育目标：租户不能通过时效（即 OCAN）获得对其出租房屋或房东拥有的其他土地的地役权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 §§ 2.16–2.17 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过规定的地役权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"租户不能通过时效（即 OCAN）获得对其出租房屋或房东拥有的其他土地的地役权。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果租客败诉，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"租户不能通过时效（即 OCAN）获得对其出租房屋或房东拥有的其他土地的地役权。","analysis":"时效地役权是通过 OCAN 获得的、为有限目的使用他人土地的推定非占有性权利，即使用： 公开且臭名昭著 – 对于合理的所有者来说明显或可见 连续 – 在法定期限内不间断 实际 – 使用土地 非许可 – 对所有者怀有敌意和不利 租户不能获得对 (1) 租赁房屋（因为他们已获得使用许可）或 (2) 其拥有的其他土地的规定地役权房东，因为据说租客在租赁期间代表房东的立场，因此没有不利利益。该男子将 1 号地块租给了租户，而租户未经该男子许可就开始使用 2 号地块的车道。由于该男子拥有这两个地块，因此租户的使用并没有变得不利，直到该男子八年前将2号地块转让给他的朋友。因此，租户未满足获得规定地役权的 10 年法定期限，并可能会损失。 （选项A） 虽然朋友的契约中没有提及地役权，但地役权会随着役（即负担）遗产的转移而自动转移。因此，如果租户通过规定获得了第 2 号地块的地役权，那么当第 2 号地块转让时，地役权的负担将自动从该男子转移到朋友身上。 （选择C）逆权占有要求一个人拥有土地，但只要满足其他要素（此处未看到），仅仅使用就足以获得规定的地役权。 （选项 D）租户从巷子进入 1 号地块停车场不会妨碍他通过规定获得 2 号地块的地役权。但这会阻止他获得必要的地役权，因为这种类型的地役权仅在使用和享受财产绝对必要时才会出现。这包括无法从公共道路（例如公共小巷）到达财产（例如，1 号停车场）的情况。\n\n教育目标：租户不能通过时效（即 OCAN）获得对其出租房屋或房东拥有的其他土地的地役权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 §§ 2.16–2.17 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过规定的地役权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：直到八年前，该男子还拥有 1 号地块和 2 号地块。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-26b8b6bcaca52d0d"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":210,"sourceQuestionPage":614,"sourceQuestionPages":[614],"sourceExplanationPages":[615,616],"sourceAnswerPages":[615,616],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-211","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A seller entered into a written contract to sell a tract of land to an investor. The contract made no mention of the quality of title to be conveyed. The seller and the investor later completed the sale, and the seller delivered a warranty deed to the investor. Soon thereafter, the value of the land increased dramatically. The investor entered into a written contract to sell the land to a buyer. The contract between the investor and the buyer expressly provided that the investor would convey a marketable title. The buyer's attorney discovered that the title to the land was not marketable and had not been marketable when the original seller had conveyed to the investor. The buyer refused to complete the sale. The investor sued the original seller for breach of contract, claiming damages from the seller's failure to convey marketable title, which resulted in the investor's loss of the sale to the subsequent buyer. Who is likely to prevail on this count?","choices":{"A":"The investor, because the law implies in such a contract a covenant that the title will be marketable.","B":"The investor, because the original seller is liable for all reasonably foreseeable damages.","C":"The original seller, because her contract obligations as to title merged into the deed.","D":"The original seller, because she did not expressly agree to convey marketable title."},"answer":"C","explanation":"All contracts for the sale of land, unless otherwise stated, impliedly warrant that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing (Choices A & D). However, once a deed is delivered to and accepted by the buyer, the doctrine of merger provides that all obligations contained within the land-sale contract merge into the deed. Those obligations can be enforced thereafter only if they are incorporated into the deed. Here, the investor has sued the original seller for breach of contract, claiming damages from the seller's failure to convey marketable title. Although the investor's allegations appear to be true, he can only seek a remedy that arises from the deed—not the contract that merged with the deed. As a result, the original seller is likely to prevail.* *The investor likely would have prevailed had he instead sued the original seller for breaching a covenant of title contained within the warranty deed that he received. (Choice B) A seller may be liable for reasonably foreseeable damages that arise from a breach of a warranty in the deed or from fraudulent assertions in the land-sale contract. But neither ground has been asserted by the investor in this case.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of merger, all obligations contained within the land-sale contract merge into the deed and can be enforced thereafter only if they are incorporated into the deed.","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of merger, all obligations contained within the land-sale contract merge into the deed and can be enforced thereafter only if they are incorporated into the deed.","references":"","rule":"Under the doctrine of merger, all obligations contained within the land-sale contract merge into the deed and can be enforced thereafter only if they are incorporated into the deed.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail on this count?","rule":"Under the doctrine of merger, all obligations contained within the land-sale contract merge into the deed and can be enforced thereafter only if they are incorporated into the deed.","analysis":"All contracts for the sale of land, unless otherwise stated, impliedly warrant that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing (Choices A & D). However, once a deed is delivered to and accepted by the buyer, the doctrine of merger provides that all obligations contained within the land-sale contract merge into the deed. Those obligations can be enforced thereafter only if they are incorporated into the deed. Here, the investor has sued the original seller for breach of contract, claiming damages from the seller's failure to convey marketable title. Although the investor's allegations appear to be true, he can only seek a remedy that arises from the deed—not the contract that merged with the deed. As a result, the original seller is likely to prevail.* *The investor likely would have prevailed had he instead sued the original seller for breaching a covenant of title contained within the warranty deed that he received. (Choice B) A seller may be liable for reasonably foreseeable damages that arise from a breach of a warranty in the deed or from fraudulent assertions in the land-sale contract. But neither ground has been asserted by the investor in this case.","conclusion":"Answer C: The original seller, because her contract obligations as to title merged into the deed."},"questionZh":"卖方签订了一份书面合同，将一块土​​地出售给投资者。合同没有提及要转让的所有权的性质。卖方和投资者随后完成了销售，卖方向投资者交付了保修契约。此后不久，土地的价值急剧增加。投资者签订书面合同将土地出售给买方。投资者和买方之间的合同明确规定投资者将转让可出售的所有权。买方律师发现，该土地的所有权不可出售，并且当原卖方将其转让给投资者时，该土地所有权也尚未可出售。买方拒绝完成销售。投资者起诉原卖方违反合同，要求因卖方未能转让适销所有权而造成损失而造成损失，从而导致投资者失去向后续买方的销售。在这一点上谁可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"投资者，因为法律在此类合同中暗示了所有权可出售的契约。","B":"投资者，因为原始卖方应对所有合理可预见的损害负责。","C":"原来的卖方，因为她的合同义务与所有权合并到了契约中。","D":"原来的卖家，因为她没有明确同意转让适销对路的所有权。"},"explanationZh":"除非另有说明，所有土地销售合同均默示保证卖方在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方（选择 A 和 D）。然而，一旦契约被交付给买方并被买方接受，合并原则规定，土地销售合同中包含的所有义务都将合并到契约中。只有将这些义务纳入契约后，才能强制执行。在此，投资者起诉原卖方违反合同，要求因卖方未能转让适销所有权而造成损失。尽管投资者的指控似乎属实，但他只能寻求因契约而产生的补救措施，而不是与契约合并的合同。因此，原卖方很可能胜诉。* *如果投资者起诉原卖方违反他收到的保修契约中包含的所有权契约，他很可能会胜诉。 （选择 B） 卖方可能对因违反契约中的保证或土地销售合同中的欺诈性主张而产生的合理可预见的损害承担责任。但在本案中，投资者并未提出任何理由。\n\n教育目标：根据合并原则，土地销售合同中包含的所有义务都合并到契约中，并且只有在纳入契约后才能执行。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据合并原则，土地销售合同中包含的所有义务都合并到契约中，并且只有在纳入契约后才能执行。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这一点上谁可能获胜？","rule":"根据合并原则，土地销售合同中包含的所有义务都合并到契约中，并且只有在纳入契约后才能执行。","analysis":"除非另有说明，所有土地销售合同均默示保证卖方在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方（选择 A 和 D）。然而，一旦契约被交付给买方并被买方接受，合并原则规定，土地销售合同中包含的所有义务都将合并到契约中。只有将这些义务纳入契约后，才能强制执行。在此，投资者起诉原卖方违反合同，要求因卖方未能转让适销所有权而造成损失。尽管投资者的指控似乎属实，但他只能寻求因契约而产生的补救措施，而不是与契约合并的合同。因此，原卖方很可能胜诉。* *如果投资者起诉原卖方违反他收到的保修契约中包含的所有权契约，他很可能会胜诉。 （选择 B） 卖方可能对因违反契约中的保证或土地销售合同中的欺诈性主张而产生的合理可预见的损害承担责任。但在本案中，投资者并未提出任何理由。\n\n教育目标：根据合并原则，土地销售合同中包含的所有义务都合并到契约中，并且只有在纳入契约后才能执行。","conclusion":"答案 C：原来的卖方，因为她的合同义务与所有权合并到了契约中。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":211,"sourceQuestionPage":617,"sourceQuestionPages":[617],"sourceExplanationPages":[618],"sourceAnswerPages":[618],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-212","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man conveyed the eastern half of a tract of vacant land to a woman by a warranty deed. The woman promptly recorded the deed. The land conveyed to the woman fronted on a public highway. The land retained by the man was landlocked. One year later, the man died intestate, leaving a cousin as his only heir. The cousin visited the man's land for the first time and discovered that it had no access to a public highway. A neighbor who owned adjoining land fronting on the public highway offered to sell the cousin a right to cross the neighbor's land for access to the highway. Although the neighbor's price was reasonable, the cousin rejected the offer. The woman has refused to allow the cousin to cross her land for access to the public highway even though the woman's land is still vacant. The cousin has sued the woman, seeking access across the woman's land to the public highway. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The cousin, based on necessity.","B":"The cousin, because the woman's land is still vacant.","C":"The woman, because the cousin could obtain an alternative access to the highway from the neighbor.","D":"The woman, because the man failed to reserve an easement in his deed to the woman."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Implied easements\n\nType Elements\n\nNecessity Severance of common ownership Strict necessity for use & enjoyment of land Existing prior use Severance of common ownership Prior existing use Apparent & continuous Reasonable necessity for use & enjoyment of land An easement by necessity will be implied if the following elements are met: Severance of common ownership – the dominant and servient estates were a single tract of land, and the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another\n\nStrict necessity – the easement is absolutely necessary for the use and enjoyment of the land (eg, the dominant tract is landlocked and has no access to a public road)\n\nThis type of easement runs with the land, meaning that subsequent owners of the dominant and servient estates are bound by the easement. Here, the man owned the entire tract before he conveyed the eastern half to the woman, thereby severing common ownership. Since the land retained by the man has no access to a public highway or any other road, an easement over the woman's land was absolutely necessary for the man to use and enjoy his land. Therefore, an easement will be implied based on necessity (Choice D). And since the easement runs with the land, the cousin— who inherited the man's land—will likely prevail in his suit against the woman. (Choice B) The fact that the woman's land is vacant rather than developed has no bearing on whether the cousin has the right to cross her land to reach the public highway. (Choice C) The cousin's ability to obtain alternative access to the public highway by purchasing an easement over the neighbor's land does not extinguish his easement by necessity over the woman's land.\n\nEducational objective: An easement by necessity will be implied if (1) the dominant and servient estates were once a single tract of land, and the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another, and (2) the easement is absolutely necessary for the use and enjoyment of the land.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by necessity).","educationalObjective":"An easement by necessity will be implied if (1) the dominant and servient estates were once a single tract of land, and the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another, and (2) the easement is absolutely necessary for the use and enjoyment of the land.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by necessity).","rule":"An easement by necessity will be implied if (1) the dominant and servient estates were once a single tract of land, and the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another, and (2) the easement is absolutely necessary for the use and enjoyment of the land.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"An easement by necessity will be implied if (1) the dominant and servient estates were once a single tract of land, and the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another, and (2) the easement is absolutely necessary for the use and enjoyment of the land.","analysis":"Implied easements\n\nType Elements\n\nNecessity Severance of common ownership Strict necessity for use & enjoyment of land Existing prior use Severance of common ownership Prior existing use Apparent & continuous Reasonable necessity for use & enjoyment of land An easement by necessity will be implied if the following elements are met: Severance of common ownership – the dominant and servient estates were a single tract of land, and the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another\n\nStrict necessity – the easement is absolutely necessary for the use and enjoyment of the land (eg, the dominant tract is landlocked and has no access to a public road)\n\nThis type of easement runs with the land, meaning that subsequent owners of the dominant and servient estates are bound by the easement. Here, the man owned the entire tract before he conveyed the eastern half to the woman, thereby severing common ownership. Since the land retained by the man has no access to a public highway or any other road, an easement over the woman's land was absolutely necessary for the man to use and enjoy his land. Therefore, an easement will be implied based on necessity (Choice D). And since the easement runs with the land, the cousin— who inherited the man's land—will likely prevail in his suit against the woman. (Choice B) The fact that the woman's land is vacant rather than developed has no bearing on whether the cousin has the right to cross her land to reach the public highway. (Choice C) The cousin's ability to obtain alternative access to the public highway by purchasing an easement over the neighbor's land does not extinguish his easement by necessity over the woman's land.","conclusion":"Answer A: The cousin, based on necessity."},"questionZh":"一名男子通过一份保修契约将一块空地的东半部转让给一名女子。女子立即记录下了这一过程。土地转让给了面向公路的妇女。该男子保留的土地是内陆土地。一年后，该男子去世时没有立下遗嘱，留下一个表弟作为他唯一的继承人。表弟第一次来到该男子的土地时，发现该地没有通往公共高速公路的通道。一位拥有毗邻公共高速公路的土地的邻居提出向表弟出售穿过邻居土地进入高速公路的权利。尽管邻居开出的价格合理，但表弟还是拒绝了。尽管该妇女的土地仍然空置，但该妇女仍拒绝让她的表弟穿过她的土地进入高速公路。表弟起诉了该女子，要求穿过该女子的土地进入公共高速公路。谁有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"表弟，基于必要性。","B":"表弟，因为女方的土地还空着。","C":"那个女人，因为表弟可以从邻居那里获得通往高速公路的替代通道。","D":"女子，因为男子未能在其契约中向女子保留地役权。"},"explanationZh":"隐含地役权\n\n类型元素\n\n必要性 分割共同所有权 对使用和享有土地的严格必要性 现有的在先使用 分割共同所有权 在先的现有使用 明显且连续 对使用和享有土地的合理必要性 如果满足以下要素，则意味着必要地役权： 分割共同所有权 – 支配性和从属性庄园是一块土地，所有者保留部分土地并将其余部分转让给另一方\n\n严格必要性——地役权对于土地的使用和享受是绝对必要的（例如，主要土地是内陆地区，无法通往公共道路）\n\n这种类型的地役权与土地一起存在，这意味着主要和从属地产的后续所有者都受到地役权的约束。在这里，男人在将东半部分转让给女人之前拥有整片土地，从而切断了共同所有权。由于男性保留的土地无法通往公共高速公路或任何其他道路，因此男性必须使用女性土地的地役权才能使用和享受其土地。因此，根据必要性将暗示地役权（选择D）。由于地役权随土地一起归属，继承了该男子土地的表弟很可能会在针对该女子的诉讼中胜诉。 （选项 B） 妇女的土地是空置的而不是开发的，这一事实与表弟是否有权穿过她的土地到达公共高速公路无关。 （选项C）表弟有能力通过购买邻居土地的地役权来获得通往公共高速公路的替代通道，但这并不能消除他对女方土地的地役权。\n\n教育目标：如果（1）支配性庄园和从属庄园曾经是一块土地，所有者保留部分土地并将其余部分转让给另一块土地，并且（2）地役权对于使用和享受土地是绝对必要的，则必然存在地役权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（必要时的地役权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果 (1) 支配性庄园和从属庄园曾经是一块土地，所有者保留部分土地并将其余部分转让给另一方，并且 (2) 地役权对于使用和享有土地是绝对必要的，则必然存在地役权。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"如果 (1) 支配性庄园和从属庄园曾经是一块土地，所有者保留部分土地并将其余部分转让给另一方，并且 (2) 地役权对于使用和享有土地是绝对必要的，则必然存在地役权。","analysis":"隐含地役权\n\n类型元素\n\n必要性 分割共同所有权 对使用和享有土地的严格必要性 现有的在先使用 分割共同所有权 在先的现有使用 明显且连续 对使用和享有土地的合理必要性 如果满足以下要素，则意味着必要地役权： 分割共同所有权 – 支配性和从属性庄园是一块土地，所有者保留部分土地并将其余部分转让给另一方\n\n严格必要性——地役权对于土地的使用和享受是绝对必要的（例如，主要土地是内陆地区，无法通往公共道路）\n\n这种类型的地役权与土地一起存在，这意味着主要和从属地产的后续所有者都受到地役权的约束。在这里，男人在将东半部分转让给女人之前拥有整片土地，从而切断了共同所有权。由于男性保留的土地无法通往公共高速公路或任何其他道路，因此男性必须使用女性土地的地役权才能使用和享受其土地。因此，根据必要性将暗示地役权（选择D）。由于地役权随土地一起归属，继承了该男子土地的表弟很可能会在针对该女子的诉讼中胜诉。 （选项 B） 妇女的土地是空置的而不是开发的，这一事实与表弟是否有权穿过她的土地到达公共高速公路无关。 （选项C）表弟有能力通过购买邻居土地的地役权来获得通往公共高速公路的替代通道，但这并不能消除他对女方土地的地役权。\n\n教育目标：如果（1）支配性庄园和从属庄园曾经是一块土地，所有者保留部分土地并将其余部分转让给另一块土地，并且（2）地役权对于使用和享受土地是绝对必要的，则必然存在地役权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（必要时的地役权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：表弟，基于必要性。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":212,"sourceQuestionPage":619,"sourceQuestionPages":[619],"sourceExplanationPages":[620,621],"sourceAnswerPages":[620,621],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-213","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A seller who owned land entered into a valid written agreement to sell the land to a buyer by installment purchase. The contract stipulated that the seller would deliver to the buyer, upon payment of the last installment due, \"a warranty deed sufficient to convey a fee simple title.\" The contract contained no other provision that could be construed as referring to title. The buyer entered into possession of the land. After making 10 of the 300 installment payments obligated under the contract, the buyer discovered that there was outstanding a valid and enforceable mortgage on the land, securing the payment of a debt in the amount of 25 percent of the purchase price that the buyer had agreed to pay. There was no evidence that the seller had ever been late in payments due under the mortgage, and there was no evidence of any danger of insolvency of the seller. The value of the land was then four times the amount due on the debt secured by the mortgage. The buyer quit possession of the land, stopped making payments on the contract, and demanded that the seller repay the amounts that the buyer had paid under the contract. After the seller refused the demand, the buyer sued the seller to recover damages for the seller's alleged breach of the contract. Should damages be awarded to the buyer?","choices":{"A":"No, because an installment purchase contract is treated as a security device.","B":"No, because the time for the seller to deliver marketable title has not arrived.","C":"Yes, because an installment purchase contract is treated as a mortgage, and the outstanding mortgage impairs the buyer's equity of redemption.","D":"Yes, because in the absence of a contrary express agreement, an obligation to convey marketable title is implied."},"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession All land-sale contracts have an implied warranty that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing unless otherwise stated. Title does not have to be perfect to be marketable. However, it must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. In the case of an installment land contract—ie, one in which the buyer takes possession of the property and pays the purchase price over time—the seller need not deliver marketable title until the price is fully paid. Here, after making 10 of the 300 installment payments, the buyer discovered that there was an outstanding mortgage on the land. Although an existing mortgage can render title unmarketable, the seller does not need to deliver marketable title until the buyer has made all installment payments. Therefore, damages should not be awarded to the buyer because the time for the seller to deliver marketable title has not arrived (Choice D). (Choice A) The fact that an installment land contract is treated as a security device (namely, a mortgage) does not prevent the buyer from recovering damages if the seller is unable to deliver marketable title when it is due (not seen here). (Choice C) An installment land contract is often treated as a mortgage, so the seller can bring a foreclosure action to regain possession of the property if the buyer defaults on payments. Although the buyer stopped making payments here, the seller has not yet attempted to enforce the contract. As a result, the buyer's equity of redemption (ie, pre- foreclosure right to cure the default by paying the entire past-due amount) is not at issue.\n\nEducational objective: Under an installment land contract, the seller does not need to deliver marketable title until the purchase price is fully paid.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 3.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (installment land contracts).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (marketable title).","educationalObjective":"Under an installment land contract, the seller does not need to deliver marketable title until the purchase price is fully paid.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 3.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (installment land contracts).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (marketable title).","rule":"Under an installment land contract, the seller does not need to deliver marketable title until the purchase price is fully paid.","irac":{"issue":"Should damages be awarded to the buyer?","rule":"Under an installment land contract, the seller does not need to deliver marketable title until the purchase price is fully paid.","analysis":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession All land-sale contracts have an implied warranty that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing unless otherwise stated. Title does not have to be perfect to be marketable. However, it must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. In the case of an installment land contract—ie, one in which the buyer takes possession of the property and pays the purchase price over time—the seller need not deliver marketable title until the price is fully paid. Here, after making 10 of the 300 installment payments, the buyer discovered that there was an outstanding mortgage on the land. Although an existing mortgage can render title unmarketable, the seller does not need to deliver marketable title until the buyer has made all installment payments. Therefore, damages should not be awarded to the buyer because the time for the seller to deliver marketable title has not arrived (Choice D). (Choice A) The fact that an installment land contract is treated as a security device (namely, a mortgage) does not prevent the buyer from recovering damages if the seller is unable to deliver marketable title when it is due (not seen here). (Choice C) An installment land contract is often treated as a mortgage, so the seller can bring a foreclosure action to regain possession of the property if the buyer defaults on payments. Although the buyer stopped making payments here, the seller has not yet attempted to enforce the contract. As a result, the buyer's equity of redemption (ie, pre- foreclosure right to cure the default by paying the entire past-due amount) is not at issue.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the time for the seller to deliver marketable title has not arrived."},"questionZh":"拥有土地的卖方签订有效的书面协议，以分期付款的方式将土地出售给买方。合同规定，在支付最后一期付款后，卖方将向买方交付“一份足以转让费用简单所有权的保证契约”。该合同不包含任何其他可被解释为涉及所有权的条款。买方取得了土地的所有权。在支付了合同规定的 300 笔分期付款中的 10 笔后，买方发现该土地上还有未偿还的有效且可执行的抵押贷款，从而确保支付买方同意支付的购买价格 25% 的债务。没有证据表明卖方曾逾期按揭付款，也没有证据表明卖方有任何破产的危险。当时土地的价值是抵押贷款到期债务金额的四倍。买方放弃了对土地的占有，停止支付合同款项，并要求卖方偿还买方根据合同支付的款项。在卖方拒绝要求后，买方起诉卖方，要求卖方赔偿其所谓的违约损失。是否应判给买方赔偿损失？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为分期付款购买合同被视为安全措施。","B":"不，因为卖方交付适销所有权的时间尚未到来。","C":"是的，因为分期购房合同被视为抵押，未偿还的抵押会损害买方的赎回权益。","D":"是的，因为在没有相反的明确协议的情况下，隐含了转让适销所有权的义务。"},"explanationZh":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租约 留置权 所有权链中的缺口 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 所有土地销售合同都有默示保证，除非另有说明，否则卖方将在交割时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。标题不一定要完美才能有销路。然而，它必须合理地没有疑问，并且没有诉讼的威胁，这样一个理性的人就会接受并支付费用。在分期土地合同的情况下，即买方占有财产并随时间支付购买价款的情况下，卖方在完全支付价格之前无需交付可出售的所有权。在这里，买家支付了 300 笔分期付款中的 10 笔后，发现土地上还有未偿还的抵押贷款。尽管现有的抵押贷款可能会导致所有权无法出售，但在买方支付所有分期付款之前，卖方不需要交付可出售的所有权。因此，不应判给买方损害赔偿，因为卖方交付适销所有权的时间尚未到来（选项D）。 （选择A）分期付款土地合同被视为担保手段（即抵押）的事实并不妨碍买方在卖方无法在到期时交付可出售的所有权时获得损害赔偿（此处未看到）。 （选择C）分期付款土地合同通常被视为抵押，因此如果买方拖欠付款，卖方可以提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼以重新获得财产的占有。尽管买方在此停止付款，但卖方尚未尝试执行合同。因此，买方的赎回权益（即通过支付全部逾期金额来解决违约问题的取消抵押品赎回权前的权利）不存在问题。\n\n教育目标：根据分期土地合同，卖方在全额支付购买价之前无需交付可出售的所有权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 3.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（分期付款土地合同）。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（可销售所有权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据分期土地合同，卖方在全额支付购买价之前无需交付可出售的所有权。","iracZh":{"issue":"是否应判给买方赔偿损失？","rule":"根据分期土地合同，卖方在全额支付购买价之前无需交付可出售的所有权。","analysis":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租约 留置权 所有权链中的缺口 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 所有土地销售合同都有默示保证，除非另有说明，否则卖方将在交割时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。标题不一定要完美才能有销路。然而，它必须合理地没有疑问，并且没有诉讼的威胁，这样一个理性的人就会接受并支付费用。在分期土地合同的情况下，即买方占有财产并随时间支付购买价款的情况下，卖方在完全支付价格之前无需交付可出售的所有权。在这里，买家支付了 300 笔分期付款中的 10 笔后，发现土地上还有未偿还的抵押贷款。尽管现有的抵押贷款可能会导致所有权无法出售，但在买方支付所有分期付款之前，卖方不需要交付可出售的所有权。因此，不应判给买方损害赔偿，因为卖方交付适销所有权的时间尚未到来（选项D）。 （选择A）分期付款土地合同被视为担保手段（即抵押）的事实并不妨碍买方在卖方无法在到期时交付可出售的所有权时获得损害赔偿（此处未看到）。 （选择C）分期付款土地合同通常被视为抵押，因此如果买方拖欠付款，卖方可以提起取消抵押品赎回权诉讼以重新获得财产的占有。尽管买方在此停止付款，但卖方尚未尝试执行合同。因此，买方的赎回权益（即通过支付全部逾期金额来解决违约问题的取消抵押品赎回权前的权利）不存在问题。\n\n教育目标：根据分期土地合同，卖方在全额支付购买价之前无需交付可出售的所有权。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 3.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（分期付款土地合同）。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（可销售所有权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为卖方交付适销所有权的时间尚未到来。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":213,"sourceQuestionPage":622,"sourceQuestionPages":[622],"sourceExplanationPages":[623,624],"sourceAnswerPages":[623,624],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-214","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A seller contracted to sell land to a buyer for $300,000. The contract provided that the closing would be 60 days after the contract was signed and that the seller would convey to the buyer a \"marketable title\" by a quitclaim deed at closing. The contract contained no other provisions regarding the title to be delivered to the buyer. A title search revealed that the land was subject to an unsatisfied $50,000 mortgage and a right-of-way easement over a portion of the land. The buyer now claims that the title is unmarketable and has refused to close. Is the buyer correct?","choices":{"A":"No, because nothing under these facts renders title unmarketable.","B":"No, because the buyer agreed to accept a quitclaim deed.","C":"Yes, because the right-of-way easement makes the title unmarketable.","D":"Yes, because the unsatisfied mortgage makes the title unmarketable."},"answer":"C","explanation":"All land-sale contracts have an implied warranty that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing unless otherwise stated. For example, the parties can eliminate this warranty by expressly contracting to transfer whatever title the grantor has to the buyer by a quitclaim deed (ie, without any warranties). But since the seller here has expressly promised to convey to the buyer a \"marketable title\" by quitclaim deed, this warranty remains in their land-sale contract (Choice B). A warranty of marketable title (ie, merchantability) is a promise that title is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Title can be rendered unmarketable by encumbrances, existing zoning violations, or defects in the chain of title. Although a mortgage is an encumbrance, the seller has the right to satisfy the mortgage up to the time of closing—and will often use the sale proceeds to do so. As a result, the closing itself often renders title marketable (Choice D). An easement is another type of encumbrance that will render title unmarketable if it reduces the property's value. Utility easements, for example, benefit the property by allowing others to service it, so they often have no effect on marketability. But right-of-way easements often burden the property by allowing another to travel over it—thereby reducing its value. Therefore, the buyer's claim that the title is unmarketable is correct, and the buyer can refuse to close (Choice A).\n\nEducational objective: Marketable title is implied in all land-sale contracts. Title can be rendered unmarketable by easements that reduce the land's value or by existing zoning violations—but not typically by mortgages since sale proceeds are often used to satisfy the mortgage debt.","educationalObjective":"Marketable title is implied in all land-sale contracts. Title can be rendered unmarketable by easements that reduce the land's value or by existing zoning violations—but not typically by mortgages since sale proceeds are often used to satisfy the mortgage debt.","references":"","rule":"Marketable title is implied in all land-sale contracts. Title can be rendered unmarketable by easements that reduce the land's value or by existing zoning violations—but not typically by mortgages since sale proceeds are often used to satisfy the mortgage debt.","irac":{"issue":"Is the buyer correct?","rule":"Marketable title is implied in all land-sale contracts. Title can be rendered unmarketable by easements that reduce the land's value or by existing zoning violations—but not typically by mortgages since sale proceeds are often used to satisfy the mortgage debt.","analysis":"All land-sale contracts have an implied warranty that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing unless otherwise stated. For example, the parties can eliminate this warranty by expressly contracting to transfer whatever title the grantor has to the buyer by a quitclaim deed (ie, without any warranties). But since the seller here has expressly promised to convey to the buyer a \"marketable title\" by quitclaim deed, this warranty remains in their land-sale contract (Choice B). A warranty of marketable title (ie, merchantability) is a promise that title is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Title can be rendered unmarketable by encumbrances, existing zoning violations, or defects in the chain of title. Although a mortgage is an encumbrance, the seller has the right to satisfy the mortgage up to the time of closing—and will often use the sale proceeds to do so. As a result, the closing itself often renders title marketable (Choice D). An easement is another type of encumbrance that will render title unmarketable if it reduces the property's value. Utility easements, for example, benefit the property by allowing others to service it, so they often have no effect on marketability. But right-of-way easements often burden the property by allowing another to travel over it—thereby reducing its value. Therefore, the buyer's claim that the title is unmarketable is correct, and the buyer can refuse to close (Choice A).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the right-of-way easement makes the title unmarketable."},"questionZh":"卖方签订合同，以 30 万美元的价格向买方出售土地。合同规定，交割将于合同签署后 60 天进行，卖方将在交割时通过无偿债权契约向买方转让“适销所有权”。该合同没有包含有关交付给买方的所有权的其他条款。产权调查显示，该土地的 50,000 美元抵押贷款尚未清偿，且部分土地有通行权地役权。买家现在声称该产权无法销售并拒绝成交。买家的说法正确吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为根据这些事实，所有权不会变得无法销售。","B":"不，因为买方同意接受放弃索赔契约。","C":"是的，因为通行权地役权使该所有权无法销售。","D":"是的，因为未偿还的抵押贷款使产权无法出售。"},"explanationZh":"所有土地销售合同都有默示保证，除非另有说明，否则卖方将在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。例如，双方可以通过明确签订合同，通过无偿债权契约（即无任何保证）将设保人拥有的任何所有权转让给买方来消除此保证。但由于此处的卖方已明确承诺通过无偿债权契据向买方转让“可出售的所有权”，因此该保证保留在其土地销售合同中（选择B）。适销所有权的保证（即适销性）是一种承诺，即所有权合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁，因此理性的人会接受并为其付费。产权负担、现有土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规或产权链缺陷可能导致产权无法销售。尽管抵押贷款是一种负担，但卖方有权在成交前偿还抵押贷款，并且通常会使用出售收益来偿还抵押贷款。因此，成交本身通常会使产权具有市场价值（选项 D）。地役权是另一种产权负担，如果它降低了房产的价值，就会导致产权无法销售。例如，公用事业地役权通过允许其他人为其提供服务而使财产受益，因此它们通常对适销性没有影响。但通行权地役权通常会因为允许他人通行而给财产带来负担，从而降低其价值。因此，买方关于产权无法销售的主张是正确的，买方可以拒绝成交（选择A）。\n\n教育目标：所有土地销售合同中都隐含了可出售的所有权。降低土地价值的地役权或现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规行为可能会导致所有权无法出售，但通常不会通过抵押贷款，因为出售收益通常用于偿还抵押贷款债务。","educationalObjectiveZh":"所有土地出售合同中都隐含了可出售的所有权。降低土地价值的地役权或现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规行为可能会导致所有权无法出售，但通常不会通过抵押贷款，因为出售收益通常用于偿还抵押贷款债务。","iracZh":{"issue":"买家的说法正确吗？","rule":"所有土地出售合同中都隐含了可出售的所有权。降低土地价值的地役权或现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规行为可能会导致所有权无法出售，但通常不会通过抵押贷款，因为出售收益通常用于偿还抵押贷款债务。","analysis":"所有土地销售合同都有默示保证，除非另有说明，否则卖方将在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。例如，双方可以通过明确签订合同，通过无偿债权契约（即无任何保证）将设保人拥有的任何所有权转让给买方来消除此保证。但由于此处的卖方已明确承诺通过无偿债权契据向买方转让“可出售的所有权”，因此该保证保留在其土地销售合同中（选择B）。适销所有权的保证（即适销性）是一种承诺，即所有权合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁，因此理性的人会接受并为其付费。产权负担、现有土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规或产权链缺陷可能导致产权无法销售。尽管抵押贷款是一种负担，但卖方有权在成交前偿还抵押贷款，并且通常会使用出售收益来偿还抵押贷款。因此，成交本身通常会使产权具有市场价值（选项 D）。地役权是另一种产权负担，如果它降低了房产的价值，就会导致产权无法销售。例如，公用事业地役权通过允许其他人为其提供服务而使财产受益，因此它们通常对适销性没有影响。但通行权地役权通常会因为允许他人通行而给财产带来负担，从而降低其价值。因此，买方关于产权无法销售的主张是正确的，买方可以拒绝成交（选择A）。\n\n教育目标：所有土地销售合同中都隐含了可出售的所有权。降低土地价值的地役权或现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规行为可能会导致所有权无法出售，但通常不会通过抵押贷款，因为出售收益通常用于偿还抵押贷款债务。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为通行权地役权使该所有权无法销售。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":214,"sourceQuestionPage":625,"sourceQuestionPages":[625],"sourceExplanationPages":[626],"sourceAnswerPages":[626],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-215","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Nuisance, Zoning & Support","question":"Two siblings, a brother and sister, own adjoining lots in the central portion of a city. Each of their lots had an office building. The sister decided to raze the existing building on her lot and erect a building of greater height. No statute or ordinance applies to prevent her from doing so and she has received all governmental approvals required to pursue her project. After the sister had torn down the existing building, she proceeded to excavate deeper. She used shoring that met all local, state, and federal safety regulations, and the shoring was placed in accordance with those standards. The brother notified the sister that cracks were developing in the building situated on his lot. The sister took the view that any subsidence suffered by the brother was due to the weight of his building, and correctly asserted that none would have occurred had the brother's soil been in its natural state. The sister continued to excavate. The building on the brother's lot did suffer extensive damage, requiring the expenditure of $750,000 to remedy the defects. Which of the following is the best comment concerning the brother's action to recover damages from the sister?","choices":{"A":"Once land is improved with a building, the owner cannot invoke the common law right of lateral support.","B":"The sister cannot be held liable simply upon proof that support was removed, but may be held liable if negligence is proved.","C":"The sister is liable because she removed necessary support for the brother's lot.","D":"The sister's only obligation was to satisfy all local, state, and federal safety regulations."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Withdrawal of lateral & subjacent support\n\nNatural state Improved\n\nLateral Strictly liable for damage to If damage to land & improvements would adjacent land caused by have occurred in land's natural state, strictly Subjacent subsidence liable If not, liable if negligent Landowners have a common law duty to provide lateral support to maintain adjacent land in its natural state—even if that land has been improved (Choice A). As a result, a landowner who withdraws lateral support from improved land is strictly liable for any resulting subsidence to the adjacent land if that harm would have occurred had the land been in its natural state. If not, the landowner is only liable if the withdrawal was negligent. Here, the brother is only entitled to the amount of lateral support needed to maintain his lot in its natural state—ie, without the weight of his building. Since his building would not have been damaged had his soil been in its natural state, the sister cannot be held strictly liable for removing support (Choice C). However, she can still be held liable if negligence is proved. (Choice D) The sister was obligated to satisfy all applicable local, state, and federal safety regulations—but she was also obligated to provide lateral support to the brother's adjacent lot. If she failed to use reasonable care to provide such support, then she can be liable for negligence.\n\nEducational objective: A landowner must provide lateral support to maintain adjacent land in its natural state— even if that land is improved. If the landowner removes lateral support, strict liability is imposed if the resulting harm would have occurred had the land been in its natural state. Otherwise, negligence liability is imposed.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 817–18 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (withdrawal of lateral support).","educationalObjective":"A landowner must provide lateral support to maintain adjacent land in its natural state— even if that land is improved. If the landowner removes lateral support, strict liability is imposed if the resulting harm would have occurred had the land been in its natural state. Otherwise, negligence liability is imposed.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 817–18 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (withdrawal of lateral support).","rule":"A landowner must provide lateral support to maintain adjacent land in its natural state— even if that land is improved. If the landowner removes lateral support, strict liability is imposed if the resulting harm would have occurred had the land been in its natural state. Otherwise, negligence liability is imposed.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the best comment concerning the brother's action to recover damages from the sister?","rule":"A landowner must provide lateral support to maintain adjacent land in its natural state— even if that land is improved. If the landowner removes lateral support, strict liability is imposed if the resulting harm would have occurred had the land been in its natural state. Otherwise, negligence liability is imposed.","analysis":"Withdrawal of lateral & subjacent support\n\nNatural state Improved\n\nLateral Strictly liable for damage to If damage to land & improvements would adjacent land caused by have occurred in land's natural state, strictly Subjacent subsidence liable If not, liable if negligent Landowners have a common law duty to provide lateral support to maintain adjacent land in its natural state—even if that land has been improved (Choice A). As a result, a landowner who withdraws lateral support from improved land is strictly liable for any resulting subsidence to the adjacent land if that harm would have occurred had the land been in its natural state. If not, the landowner is only liable if the withdrawal was negligent. Here, the brother is only entitled to the amount of lateral support needed to maintain his lot in its natural state—ie, without the weight of his building. Since his building would not have been damaged had his soil been in its natural state, the sister cannot be held strictly liable for removing support (Choice C). However, she can still be held liable if negligence is proved. (Choice D) The sister was obligated to satisfy all applicable local, state, and federal safety regulations—but she was also obligated to provide lateral support to the brother's adjacent lot. If she failed to use reasonable care to provide such support, then she can be liable for negligence.","conclusion":"Answer B: The sister cannot be held liable simply upon proof that support was removed, but may be held liable if negligence is proved."},"questionZh":"两个兄弟姐妹，一个兄弟和一个姐妹，在城市中心拥有毗邻的土地。他们的每个地块都有一座办公楼。姐姐决定将她所在地块上的现有建筑物夷为平地，并建造一座更高的建筑物。没有适用的法规或条例阻止她这样做，并且她已获得开展其项目所需的所有政府批准。姐姐拆掉了现有的建筑后，又开始更深的挖掘。她使用的支撑符合所有当地、州和联邦安全法规，并且支撑是根据这些标准放置的。哥哥通知妹妹，他所在地段的建筑物正在出现裂缝。姐姐认为哥哥所遭受的任何沉降都是由于其建筑物的重量造成的，并且正确地断言，如果哥哥的土壤处于自然状态，则不会发生任何沉降。姐姐继续挖掘。兄弟地块上的建筑物确实遭受了严重损坏，需要花费75万美元来修复缺陷。关于弟弟向妹妹追偿损失的行为，以下哪一项是最好的评论？","choicesZh":{"A":"一旦用建筑物改善土地，业主就不能援引普通法的横向支持权。","B":"妹妹不能仅凭证据证明抚养被取消而承担责任，但如果证明存在疏忽，则可能承担责任。","C":"姐姐要承担责任，因为她取消了对哥哥命运的必要支持。","D":"姐姐唯一的义务是满足所有地方、州和联邦安全法规。"},"explanationZh":"撤回横向和下部支撑\n\n自然状态改善\n\n横向损害严格承担责任 如果在土地自然状态下发生了对邻近土地造成的损害，则严格承担下方沉降责任 如果不是，如果疏忽则承担责任 土地所有者有普通法义务提供横向支撑以维持邻近土地的自然状态——即使该土地已得到改善（选择 A）。因此，如果土地处于自然状态，那么从改良土地上撤回横向支撑的土地所有者应对相邻土地造成的任何沉降负严格责任。如果没有，土地所有者只有在疏忽撤回的情况下才承担责任。在这里，兄弟仅有权获得维持他的土地处于自然状态所需的横向支撑量，即没有建筑物的重量。由于如果他的土壤处于自然状态，他的建筑物就不会被损坏，因此妹妹不能因拆除支撑物而承担严格责任（选项C）。不过，如果证明她有疏忽，她仍然要承担责任。 （选项 D） 姐姐有义务遵守所有适用的地方、州和联邦安全法规，但她也有义务为哥哥的相邻地段提供横向支持。如果她未能采取合理的谨慎措施来提供此类支持，那么她可能要承担疏忽责任。\n\n教育目标：土地所有者必须提供横向支持，以维持相邻土地的自然状态——即使该土地得到了改善。如果土地所有者拆除横向支撑，如果土地处于自然状态时就会发生由此造成的损害，则应承担严格责任。否则，将追究过失责任。\n\n参考文献\n《侵权法重述（第二）》第 §§ 817–18（Am. Law Inst. 1979）（撤销横向支持）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地所有者必须提供横向支持，以维持相邻土地的自然状态——即使该土地得到了改善。如果土地所有者拆除横向支撑，如果土地处于自然状态时就会发生由此造成的损害，则应承担严格责任。否则，将追究过失责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于弟弟向妹妹追偿损失的行为，以下哪一项是最好的评论？","rule":"土地所有者必须提供横向支持，以维持相邻土地的自然状态——即使该土地得到了改善。如果土地所有者拆除横向支撑，如果土地处于自然状态时就会发生由此造成的损害，则应承担严格责任。否则，将追究过失责任。","analysis":"撤回横向和下部支撑\n\n自然状态改善\n\n横向损害严格承担责任 如果在土地自然状态下发生了对邻近土地造成的损害，则严格承担下方沉降责任 如果不是，如果疏忽则承担责任 土地所有者有普通法义务提供横向支撑以维持邻近土地的自然状态——即使该土地已得到改善（选择 A）。因此，如果土地处于自然状态，那么从改良土地上撤回横向支撑的土地所有者应对相邻土地造成的任何沉降负严格责任。如果没有，土地所有者只有在疏忽撤回的情况下才承担责任。在这里，兄弟仅有权获得维持他的土地处于自然状态所需的横向支撑量，即没有建筑物的重量。由于如果他的土壤处于自然状态，他的建筑物就不会被损坏，因此妹妹不能因拆除支撑物而承担严格责任（选项C）。不过，如果证明她有疏忽，她仍然要承担责任。 （选项 D） 姐姐有义务遵守所有适用的地方、州和联邦安全法规，但她也有义务为哥哥的相邻地段提供横向支持。如果她未能采取合理的谨慎措施来提供此类支持，那么她可能要承担疏忽责任。\n\n教育目标：土地所有者必须提供横向支持，以维持相邻土地的自然状态——即使该土地得到了改善。如果土地所有者拆除横向支撑，如果土地处于自然状态时就会发生由此造成的损害，则应承担严格责任。否则，将追究过失责任。\n\n参考文献\n《侵权法重述（第二）》第 §§ 817–18（Am. Law Inst. 1979）（撤销横向支持）。","conclusion":"答案 B：妹妹不能仅凭证据证明抚养被取消而承担责任，但如果证明存在疏忽，则可能承担责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"subjacent support","ipa":"/.sʌb'dʒeisnt sʌpˈɔɹt/","syllables":"sub·ja·cent sup·port","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"地下支撑：地表土地从下方土层获得支撑的权利","contextualMeaning":"地下支撑：地表土地从下方土层获得支撑的权利","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Withdrawal of lateral & subjacent support Natural state Improved Lateral Strictly liable for damage to If damage to land & improvements would adjacent land caused by have occurred in land's natural state, strictly Subjacent subsidence liable If not, liable if negligent Landowners have a common law duty to provide lateral support to maintain adjacent land in its natural state—even if that land has been improved (Choic","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"lateral support","ipa":"/lˈætɝəl sʌpˈɔɹt/","syllables":"lat·eral sup·port","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"侧向支撑：相邻土地在自然状态下获得横向支撑的权利","contextualMeaning":"侧向支撑：相邻土地在自然状态下获得横向支撑的权利","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Once land is improved with a building, the owner cannot invoke the common law right of lateral support.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":215,"sourceQuestionPage":627,"sourceQuestionPages":[627],"sourceExplanationPages":[628],"sourceAnswerPages":[628],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-216","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Easements","question":"Five years ago, a woman acquired a lot improved with a 15-year-old dwelling. This year, the woman listed the property (lot and dwelling) for sale with a licensed real estate broker. The woman informed the broker of several defects in the house that were not readily discoverable by a reasonable inspection, including a leaky basement, an inadequate water supply, and a roof that leaked. A man responded to the broker's advertisement, was taken by the broker to view the property, and decided to buy it. The broker saw to it that the contract specified the property to be \"as is,\" but neither the broker nor the woman pointed out the defects to the man, who did not ask about the condition of the dwelling. After closing and taking possession, the man discovered the defects, had them repaired, and demanded that the woman reimburse him for the cost of the repairs. The woman refused, and the man brought an appropriate action against the woman for damages. If the woman wins, what is the likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The \"as is\" provision in the contract controls the rights of the parties.","B":"The broker became the agent of both the man and the woman and thus knowledge of the defects was imputed to the man.","C":"The seller of a used dwelling that has been viewed by the buyer has no responsibility toward the buyer.","D":"The woman fulfilled the duty to disclose defects by disclosure to the broker."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Seller's duty to disclose\n\nCommon law Caveat emptor No duty to disclose property defects unless (minority) rule otherwise provided\n\nModern (majority) Commercial rule property\n\nResidential Must disclose known material defects that property buyer could not reasonably discover\n\nException: \"as is\" clause or specific disclaimers without seller's fraud Under the common-law rule of caveat emptor, a seller had no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer. However, the majority of jurisdictions now require sellers of residential property to disclose material defects that are known to the seller and cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer. A defect is material if it: substantially affects the value of the residence impacts the health or safety of a resident or affects the desirability of the residence to the buyer. The seller can disclaim the duty to disclose if (1) the disclaimer is clearly and specifically stated in the real estate contract and (2) the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property. Here, the broker disclaimed the woman's duty to disclose by ensuring that the contract specified the property to be \"as is.\" Since the man did not ask about the condition of the dwelling, the woman made no misrepresentations regarding its condition. Therefore, the woman will likely prevail because the \"as is\" provision in the contract controls the rights of the parties. (Choice B) The real estate broker is the woman's agent since only she contracted with the broker to list the property. The broker would have been a dual agent representing both parties had the man also hired the broker. But dual representation would not have imputed knowledge of the defects to the man. (Choice C) Unless otherwise provided, the seller of a used dwelling that has been viewed by the buyer still has a duty to disclose known material defects that cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer. (Choice D) Disclosure to the broker was not sufficient to fulfill this duty. The woman could have been vicariously liable for the broker's failure to disclose the defects had this duty not been disclaimed.\n\nEducational objective: Sellers of residential property must disclose known material defects that the buyer cannot reasonably discover. However, the seller can disclaim this duty (eg, with an \"as is\" clause) so long as the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property.\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 265 (2020) (discussing \"as is\" sales clauses).","educationalObjective":"Sellers of residential property must disclose known material defects that the buyer cannot reasonably discover. However, the seller can disclaim this duty (eg, with an \"as is\" clause) so long as the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property.","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 265 (2020) (discussing \"as is\" sales clauses).","rule":"Sellers of residential property must disclose known material defects that the buyer cannot reasonably discover. However, the seller can disclaim this duty (eg, with an \"as is\" clause) so long as the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property.","irac":{"issue":"If the woman wins, what is the likely reason?","rule":"Sellers of residential property must disclose known material defects that the buyer cannot reasonably discover. However, the seller can disclaim this duty (eg, with an \"as is\" clause) so long as the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property.","analysis":"Seller's duty to disclose\n\nCommon law Caveat emptor No duty to disclose property defects unless (minority) rule otherwise provided\n\nModern (majority) Commercial rule property\n\nResidential Must disclose known material defects that property buyer could not reasonably discover\n\nException: \"as is\" clause or specific disclaimers without seller's fraud Under the common-law rule of caveat emptor, a seller had no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer. However, the majority of jurisdictions now require sellers of residential property to disclose material defects that are known to the seller and cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer. A defect is material if it: substantially affects the value of the residence impacts the health or safety of a resident or affects the desirability of the residence to the buyer. The seller can disclaim the duty to disclose if (1) the disclaimer is clearly and specifically stated in the real estate contract and (2) the seller has not fraudulently misrepresented or concealed the condition of the property. Here, the broker disclaimed the woman's duty to disclose by ensuring that the contract specified the property to be \"as is.\" Since the man did not ask about the condition of the dwelling, the woman made no misrepresentations regarding its condition. Therefore, the woman will likely prevail because the \"as is\" provision in the contract controls the rights of the parties. (Choice B) The real estate broker is the woman's agent since only she contracted with the broker to list the property. The broker would have been a dual agent representing both parties had the man also hired the broker. But dual representation would not have imputed knowledge of the defects to the man. (Choice C) Unless otherwise provided, the seller of a used dwelling that has been viewed by the buyer still has a duty to disclose known material defects that cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer. (Choice D) Disclosure to the broker was not sufficient to fulfill this duty. The woman could have been vicariously liable for the broker's failure to disclose the defects had this duty not been disclaimed.","conclusion":"Answer A: The \"as is\" provision in the contract controls the rights of the parties."},"questionZh":"五年前，一位女士的一套已有 15 年历史的住宅得到了很大改善。今年，该女子将房产（地块和住宅）挂牌出售给有执照的房地产经纪人。这位女士告诉经纪人，房子里有一些缺陷，通过合理的检查很难发现，包括地下室漏水、供水不足和屋顶漏水。一名男子回复了经纪人的广告，被经纪人带去查看房产，并决定购买。经纪人确保合同规定房产“按原样”，但经纪人和女子都没有向男子指出缺陷，男子也没有询问房屋的状况。收房并拿走后，男子发现了缺陷，让人修理，并要求女子报销修理费用。女子拒绝了，男子对女子提起适当的诉讼，要求赔偿损失。如果女性获胜，可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"合同中的“现状”条款控制着双方的权利。","B":"经纪人成为男人和女人的代理人，因此对缺陷的了解被归咎于男人。","C":"买家看过二手住宅后，卖家对买家不承担任何责任。","D":"该女子通过向经纪人披露，履行了披露缺陷的义务。"},"explanationZh":"卖方的披露义务\n\n普通法 买者自负 没有义务披露财产缺陷，除非（少数）规则另有规定\n\n现代（多数）商业规则财产\n\n住宅 必须披露购房者无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷\n\n例外：“按原样”条款或不存在卖方欺诈的具体免责声明 根据普通法的“买者自负”规则，卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷。然而，现在大多数司法管辖区都要求住宅物业的卖方披露卖方已知且买方无法合理发现的重大缺陷。如果缺陷符合以下条件，则该缺陷属于重大缺陷： 严重影响住宅的价值 影响居民的健康或安全或影响买方对住宅的需求。如果满足以下条件，卖方可以放弃披露义务：(1) 房地产合同中明确、具体地规定了免责声明，并且 (2) 卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒房产状况。在这里，经纪人通过确保合同规定财产“按原样”，免除了该妇女的披露义务。由于该男子没有询问该住宅的状况，该女子也没有对其状况做出任何虚假陈述。因此，妇女很可能会获胜，因为合同中的“现状”条款控制着双方的权利。 （选项 B） 房地产经纪人是该女士的经纪人，因为只有她与经纪人签订了挂牌房产的合同。如果该男子也聘请了经纪人，那么该经纪人将成为代表双方的双重代理人。但双重代表不会将缺陷的知识归咎于该人。 （选项C）除非另有规定，买方查看过的二手住宅的卖方仍有义务披露买方无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷。 （选择 D）向经纪人披露不足以履行这一义务。如果经纪人没有放弃这一义务，那么该妇女可能会对经纪人未能披露缺陷承担间接责任。\n\n教育目标：住宅物业的卖方必须披露买方无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷。然而，只要卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒财产的状况，卖方就可以免除这项义务（例如，使用“现状”条款）。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 供应商和买方 § 265 (2020)（讨论“按原样”销售条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"住宅物业的卖方必须披露买方无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷。然而，只要卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒财产的状况，卖方就可以免除这项义务（例如，使用“现状”条款）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果女性获胜，可能的原因是什么？","rule":"住宅物业的卖方必须披露买方无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷。然而，只要卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒财产的状况，卖方就可以免除这项义务（例如，使用“现状”条款）。","analysis":"卖方的披露义务\n\n普通法 买者自负 没有义务披露财产缺陷，除非（少数）规则另有规定\n\n现代（多数）商业规则财产\n\n住宅 必须披露购房者无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷\n\n例外：“按原样”条款或不存在卖方欺诈的具体免责声明 根据普通法的“买者自负”规则，卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷。然而，现在大多数司法管辖区都要求住宅物业的卖方披露卖方已知且买方无法合理发现的重大缺陷。如果缺陷符合以下条件，则该缺陷属于重大缺陷： 严重影响住宅的价值 影响居民的健康或安全或影响买方对住宅的需求。如果满足以下条件，卖方可以放弃披露义务：(1) 房地产合同中明确、具体地规定了免责声明，并且 (2) 卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒房产状况。在这里，经纪人通过确保合同规定财产“按原样”，免除了该妇女的披露义务。由于该男子没有询问该住宅的状况，该女子也没有对其状况做出任何虚假陈述。因此，妇女很可能会获胜，因为合同中的“现状”条款控制着双方的权利。 （选项 B） 房地产经纪人是该女士的经纪人，因为只有她与经纪人签订了挂牌房产的合同。如果该男子也聘请了经纪人，那么该经纪人将成为代表双方的双重代理人。但双重代表不会将缺陷的知识归咎于该人。 （选项C）除非另有规定，买方查看过的二手住宅的卖方仍有义务披露买方无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷。 （选择 D）向经纪人披露不足以履行这一义务。如果经纪人没有放弃这一义务，那么该妇女可能会对经纪人未能披露缺陷承担间接责任。\n\n教育目标：住宅物业的卖方必须披露买方无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷。然而，只要卖方没有欺诈性地歪曲或隐瞒财产的状况，卖方就可以免除这项义务（例如，使用“现状”条款）。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 供应商和买方 § 265 (2020)（讨论“按原样”销售条款）。","conclusion":"答案 A：合同中的“现状”条款控制着双方的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":216,"sourceQuestionPage":629,"sourceQuestionPages":[629],"sourceExplanationPages":[630,631],"sourceAnswerPages":[630,631],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-217","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Recording Acts & Title","question":"A seller owned a house situated on a two-acre lot in an area zoned for single-family residential uses only. Although it was not discernible from the outside, the house had been converted by the seller from a single-family house to a structure that contained three separate apartments, in violation of the zoning ordinance. The conversion was also in violation of the building code. The seller and a buyer entered into a valid written contract for the purchase and sale of the house. The contract provided that the seller was to convey to the buyer marketable title. The contract was silent as to zoning. The buyer then fully inspected the house. Prior to the closing, the buyer learned that the house did not conform to the zoning ordinance and refused to close although the seller was ready, willing, and able to perform his contract obligations. The seller brought an appropriate action for specific performance against the buyer. Is the seller likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the illegal conversion of the house creates the risk of litigation.","B":"No, because the illegal conversion of the house was done by the seller rather than by a predecessor.","C":"Yes, because the buyer was charged with knowledge of the zoning ordinance prior to entering the contract.","D":"Yes, because the seller was able to convey a marketable title."},"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession The warranty of marketable title is implied in all real estate contracts unless otherwise provided. This guarantees that, upon closing, the seller will convey the buyer title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. This does not mean that title must be free from any defect, but it should ensure peaceable and quiet enjoyment of the property. An existing violation of a zoning ordinance exposes the real estate owner to potential litigation, so such a violation renders title unmarketable.* This means that the seller's illegal conversion of the house in violation of the zoning ordinance prevents him from delivering marketable title to the buyer (Choice D). Therefore, his action for specific performance to compel the buyer to purchase the house will likely fail. *While existing zoning violations render title unmarketable, courts have consistently held that existing building code violations do not. (Choice B) The illegal conversion of the house renders title unmarketable regardless of whether the seller or a predecessor did the conversion. (Choice C) Although the buyer was charged with knowledge of the zoning ordinance prior to entering the contract, the buyer was not charged with knowing whether the house complied with that ordinance. Instead, the buyer could rely on the implied warranty of marketability in the land-sale contract and inspect the property for noncompliance prior to closing.\n\nEducational objective: Marketable title exists when title is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Therefore, an existing zoning violation renders title unmarketable.\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (defining marketable title).","educationalObjective":"Marketable title exists when title is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Therefore, an existing zoning violation renders title unmarketable.","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (defining marketable title).","rule":"Marketable title exists when title is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Therefore, an existing zoning violation renders title unmarketable.","irac":{"issue":"Is the seller likely to prevail?","rule":"Marketable title exists when title is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Therefore, an existing zoning violation renders title unmarketable.","analysis":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession The warranty of marketable title is implied in all real estate contracts unless otherwise provided. This guarantees that, upon closing, the seller will convey the buyer title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. This does not mean that title must be free from any defect, but it should ensure peaceable and quiet enjoyment of the property. An existing violation of a zoning ordinance exposes the real estate owner to potential litigation, so such a violation renders title unmarketable.* This means that the seller's illegal conversion of the house in violation of the zoning ordinance prevents him from delivering marketable title to the buyer (Choice D). Therefore, his action for specific performance to compel the buyer to purchase the house will likely fail. *While existing zoning violations render title unmarketable, courts have consistently held that existing building code violations do not. (Choice B) The illegal conversion of the house renders title unmarketable regardless of whether the seller or a predecessor did the conversion. (Choice C) Although the buyer was charged with knowledge of the zoning ordinance prior to entering the contract, the buyer was not charged with knowing whether the house complied with that ordinance. Instead, the buyer could rely on the implied warranty of marketability in the land-sale contract and inspect the property for noncompliance prior to closing.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the illegal conversion of the house creates the risk of litigation."},"questionZh":"一名卖家拥有一栋房屋，位于一块两英亩的土地上，该土地仅供单户住宅用途。虽然从外面看不出来，但该房屋已被卖方从单户住宅改建为包含三间独立公寓的结构，违反了土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例。此次改建也违反了建筑规范。卖方与买方签订有效的书面房屋买卖合同。合同规定卖方应将适销对路的所有权转让给买方。合同没有提及土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）。买家随后全面检查了房子。在交割前，买方得知该房屋不符合土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例，并拒绝交割，尽管卖方已经准备好、愿意并且能够履行其合同义务。卖方针对买方的具体履约行为提起了适当的诉讼。卖家有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为非法改装房屋会产生诉讼风险。","B":"不会，因为非法改装房屋的是卖方而不是前任。","C":"是的，因为买方被指控在签订合同之前了解土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例。","D":"是的，因为卖方能够传达适销对路的所有权。"},"explanationZh":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租赁 留置权 所有权链中的缺口 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 除非另有规定，所有房地产合同均隐含了适销所有权的保证。这保证了在成交时，卖方将转让买方所有权，该所有权是合理且毫无疑问且不受诉讼威胁的，以便合理的人会接受并付款。这并不意味着所有权必须没有任何缺陷，但它应该确保和平、安静地享受财产。现有的违反土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例的行为会使房地产所有者面临潜在的诉讼，因此这种违规行为会使所有权无法出售。*这意味着卖方违反土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例非法转换房屋，导致他无法向买方交付可出售的所有权（选择 D）。因此，他以具体履约来强制购房者购买房屋的行为很可能会失败。 *虽然现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规行为导致产权无法出售，但法院始终认为现有的建筑规范违规行为不会。 （选择 B） 房屋的非法转换导致所有权无法出售，无论是卖方还是前任进行了转换。 （选项 C） 尽管买方在签订合同前被指控了解土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例，但买方并未被指控了解房屋是否符合该条例。相反，买方可以依赖土地销售合同中隐含的适销性保证，并在成交前检查房产是否不合规。\n\n教育目标：当所有权合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以至于理性的人会接受并支付费用时，就存在可出售的所有权。因此，现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规导致所有权无法销售。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（定义可销售所有权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当所有权合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以至于理性的人会接受并支付费用时，就存在可出售的所有权。因此，现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规导致所有权无法销售。","iracZh":{"issue":"卖家有可能获胜吗？","rule":"当所有权合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以至于理性的人会接受并支付费用时，就存在可出售的所有权。因此，现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规导致所有权无法销售。","analysis":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租赁 留置权 所有权链中的缺口 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 除非另有规定，所有房地产合同均隐含了适销所有权的保证。这保证了在成交时，卖方将转让买方所有权，该所有权是合理且毫无疑问且不受诉讼威胁的，以便合理的人会接受并付款。这并不意味着所有权必须没有任何缺陷，但它应该确保和平、安静地享受财产。现有的违反土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例的行为会使房地产所有者面临潜在的诉讼，因此这种违规行为会使所有权无法出售。*这意味着卖方违反土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例非法转换房屋，导致他无法向买方交付可出售的所有权（选择 D）。因此，他以具体履约来强制购房者购买房屋的行为很可能会失败。 *虽然现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规行为导致产权无法出售，但法院始终认为现有的建筑规范违规行为不会。 （选择 B） 房屋的非法转换导致所有权无法出售，无论是卖方还是前任进行了转换。 （选项 C） 尽管买方在签订合同前被指控了解土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例，但买方并未被指控了解房屋是否符合该条例。相反，买方可以依赖土地销售合同中隐含的适销性保证，并在成交前检查房产是否不合规。\n\n教育目标：当所有权合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以至于理性的人会接受并支付费用时，就存在可出售的所有权。因此，现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规导致所有权无法销售。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（定义可销售所有权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为非法改装房屋会产生诉讼风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":217,"sourceQuestionPage":632,"sourceQuestionPages":[632],"sourceExplanationPages":[633,634],"sourceAnswerPages":[633,634],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-218","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"For 22 years, the land records have shown a man as the owner of an 80-acre farm. The man has never physically occupied the land. Nineteen years ago, a woman entered the farm. The character and duration of the woman's possession of the farm caused her to become the owner of the farm under the adverse possession law of the jurisdiction. Three years ago, when the woman was not present, a neighbor took over possession of the farm. The neighbor repaired fences, put up \"no trespassing\" signs, and did some plowing. When the woman returned, she found the neighbor in possession of the farm. The neighbor vigorously rejected the woman's claimed right to possession and threatened force. The woman withdrew. The woman then went to the man and told him of the history of activity on the farm. The woman orally told the man that she had been wrong to try to take his farm. She expressly waived any claim she had to the land. The man thanked her. Last month, unsure of the effect of her conversation with the man, the woman executed a deed purporting to convey the farm to her son. The son promptly recorded the deed. The period of time to acquire title by adverse possession in the jurisdiction is 10 years. Who now owns the farm?","choices":{"A":"The man, because the woman's later words and actions released title to the man.","B":"The neighbor, because the neighbor succeeded to the woman's adverse possession title by privity of possession.","C":"The son, because he succeeded to the woman's adverse possession title by privity of conveyance.","D":"The woman, because she must bring a quiet title action to establish her title to the farm before she can convey the farm to her son."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Contracts subject to statute of frauds\n\nMarriage – promise in consideration of marriage Year – contract cannot be completed within one year Land – sale or other transfer of real property Executor – promise to pay estate's debt from executor's personal funds Goods – sale of goods for $500 or more Suretyship – promise to pay another's debt Mnemonic: MY LEGS\n\nThe statute of frauds requires that a conveyance of real property: be in writing identify the parties and property and be signed by the donor (the person conveying the property). Here, the woman acquired title to the man's farm by adverse possession. Although the woman later apologized to the man and orally waived her claim to the farm (Choice A), the man did not regain title because the woman's oral statement did not satisfy the statute of frauds since there was no signed writing. Therefore, the woman still owned the farm when she conveyed it to her son. (Choice B) The neighbor did not succeed the woman's adverse possession title because they lacked privity—a mutual or successive interest in the same property right. Instead, the neighbor took possession of the farm without the woman's permission and excluded her from the property. But since the neighbor has only possessed the farm for 3 years, he has not acquired title by adverse possession and he has no other ownership interest. (Choice D) The woman could have brought a quiet title action to establish her title to the farm (acquired by adverse possession) above all others before conveying it, but was not required to do so.\n\nEducational objective: The statute of frauds requires that a conveyance of real property (1) be in writing, (2) identify the parties and property, and (3) be signed by the donor.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Wills & Other Donative Transfers § 6.3 (Am. Law Inst. 2003) (gifts of land subject to statute of frauds).","educationalObjective":"The statute of frauds requires that a conveyance of real property (1) be in writing, (2) identify the parties and property, and (3) be signed by the donor.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Wills & Other Donative Transfers § 6.3 (Am. Law Inst. 2003) (gifts of land subject to statute of frauds).","rule":"The statute of frauds requires that a conveyance of real property (1) be in writing, (2) identify the parties and property, and (3) be signed by the donor.","irac":{"issue":"Who now owns the farm?","rule":"The statute of frauds requires that a conveyance of real property (1) be in writing, (2) identify the parties and property, and (3) be signed by the donor.","analysis":"Contracts subject to statute of frauds\n\nMarriage – promise in consideration of marriage Year – contract cannot be completed within one year Land – sale or other transfer of real property Executor – promise to pay estate's debt from executor's personal funds Goods – sale of goods for $500 or more Suretyship – promise to pay another's debt Mnemonic: MY LEGS\n\nThe statute of frauds requires that a conveyance of real property: be in writing identify the parties and property and be signed by the donor (the person conveying the property). Here, the woman acquired title to the man's farm by adverse possession. Although the woman later apologized to the man and orally waived her claim to the farm (Choice A), the man did not regain title because the woman's oral statement did not satisfy the statute of frauds since there was no signed writing. Therefore, the woman still owned the farm when she conveyed it to her son. (Choice B) The neighbor did not succeed the woman's adverse possession title because they lacked privity—a mutual or successive interest in the same property right. Instead, the neighbor took possession of the farm without the woman's permission and excluded her from the property. But since the neighbor has only possessed the farm for 3 years, he has not acquired title by adverse possession and he has no other ownership interest. (Choice D) The woman could have brought a quiet title action to establish her title to the farm (acquired by adverse possession) above all others before conveying it, but was not required to do so.","conclusion":"Answer C: The son, because he succeeded to the woman's adverse possession title by privity of conveyance."},"questionZh":"22 年来，土地记录显示一名男子拥有 80 英亩的农场。该男子从未实际占领过这片土地。十九年前，一名妇女进入农场。该妇女占有农场的性质和持续时间使她根据管辖区的逆权占有法成为农场的所有者。三年前，当这名妇女不在场时，一位邻居接管了农场。邻居修理了栅栏，张贴了“禁止侵入”的标志，并进行了一些犁耕。当女人回来时，她发现邻居拥有农场。邻居强烈拒绝该妇女声称的占有权，并威胁使用武力。女子退了出去。然后，女人去找男人，告诉他农场活动的历史。女人口头告诉男人，她试图夺取他的农场是错误的。她明确放弃对这片土地的任何主张。男人向她道谢。上个月，由于不确定与该男子谈话的效果，该女子签署了一份契约，旨在将农场转让给她的儿子。儿子立即记录了这一行为。在管辖区内通过逆权占有取得所有权的期限为10年。现在谁拥有农场？","choicesZh":{"A":"男人，因为女人后来的言行向男人释放了头衔。","B":"邻居，因为邻居通过占有关系继承了妇女的逆权占有所有权。","C":"儿子，因为他通过产权转让继承了女人的逆权占有所有权。","D":"女人，因为她必须先提起一个安静的所有权诉讼，以确立她对农场的所有权，然后才能将农场转让给她的儿子。"},"explanationZh":"受欺诈法约束的合同\n\n婚姻 – 以婚姻为代价的承诺 年份 – 合同不能在一年内完成 土地 – 出售或以其他方式转让不动产 遗嘱执行人 – 承诺用遗嘱执行人的个人资金偿还遗产债务 货物 – 以 500 美元或以上的价格出售货物 担保 – 承诺偿还他人的债务 助记符：我的腿\n\n欺诈法要求不动产转让：采用书面形式，明确当事人和财产，并由捐赠人（财产转让人）签字。在这里，女人通过逆权占有获得了男人农场的所有权。尽管该女子后来向该男子道歉并口头放弃了她对农场的所有权（选择A），但该男子并未重新获得所有权，因为该女子的口头陈述没有签署书面声明，不符合欺诈法的规定。因此，当这位妇女将农场转让给她的儿子时，她仍然拥有该农场。 （选择B）邻居没有继承该妇女的逆权占有权，因为他们缺乏相互关系——对同一财产权的共同或连续的利益。相反，邻居在未经该妇女许可的情况下占有了该农场，并将她排除在该财产之外。但由于邻居只拥有该农场3年，因此他并没有通过逆权管有取得所有权，也没有其他所有权权益。 （选项 D） 妇女本可以在转让农场之前提起一项安静的所有权诉讼，以确立她对农场的所有权（通过逆权占有而获得），但没有被要求这样做。\n\n教育目标：欺诈法要求不动产转让 (1) 采用书面形式，(2) 注明当事人和财产，以及 (3) 由捐赠人签名。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转让 § 6.3（Am. Law Inst. 2003）（土地赠与须遵守欺诈法）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"欺诈法要求不动产转让 (1) 采用书面形式，(2) 注明当事人和财产，以及 (3) 由捐赠人签名。","iracZh":{"issue":"现在谁拥有农场？","rule":"欺诈法要求不动产转让 (1) 采用书面形式，(2) 注明当事人和财产，以及 (3) 由捐赠人签名。","analysis":"受欺诈法约束的合同\n\n婚姻 – 以婚姻为代价的承诺 年份 – 合同不能在一年内完成 土地 – 出售或以其他方式转让不动产 遗嘱执行人 – 承诺用遗嘱执行人的个人资金偿还遗产债务 货物 – 以 500 美元或以上的价格出售货物 担保 – 承诺偿还他人的债务 助记符：我的腿\n\n欺诈法要求不动产转让：采用书面形式，明确当事人和财产，并由捐赠人（财产转让人）签字。在这里，女人通过逆权占有获得了男人农场的所有权。尽管该女子后来向该男子道歉并口头放弃了她对农场的所有权（选择A），但该男子并未重新获得所有权，因为该女子的口头陈述没有签署书面声明，不符合欺诈法的规定。因此，当这位妇女将农场转让给她的儿子时，她仍然拥有该农场。 （选择B）邻居没有继承该妇女的逆权占有权，因为他们缺乏相互关系——对同一财产权的共同或连续的利益。相反，邻居在未经该妇女许可的情况下占有了该农场，并将她排除在该财产之外。但由于邻居只拥有该农场3年，因此他并没有通过逆权管有取得所有权，也没有其他所有权权益。 （选项 D） 妇女本可以在转让农场之前提起一项安静的所有权诉讼，以确立她对农场的所有权（通过逆权占有而获得），但没有被要求这样做。\n\n教育目标：欺诈法要求不动产转让 (1) 采用书面形式，(2) 注明当事人和财产，以及 (3) 由捐赠人签名。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：遗嘱和其他捐赠转让 § 6.3（Am. Law Inst. 2003）（土地赠与须遵守欺诈法）。","conclusion":"答案 C：儿子，因为他通过产权转让继承了女人的逆权占有所有权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":218,"sourceQuestionPage":635,"sourceQuestionPages":[635],"sourceExplanationPages":[636,637],"sourceAnswerPages":[636,637],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-219","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A seller had promised a buyer that, if at any time the seller decided to sell his summer cottage property, he would give the buyer the opportunity to purchase the cottage. At a time when the buyer was serving overseas with the United States Navy, the seller decided to sell the cottage for $200,000 and spoke to the buyer's mother. Before the buyer sailed, he had arranged for the mother to become a joint owner of his various bank accounts so that the mother would be able to pay his bills when he was gone. When she heard from the seller, the mother took the necessary funds from the buyer's account and paid the seller $200,000. Two weeks later, the seller executed and delivered to the mother a deed in the proper form purporting to convey the cottage to the buyer. The mother promptly and properly recorded the deed. Shortly thereafter, the mother learned that the buyer had been killed in an accident at sea one week before the delivery of the deed. The buyer's last will, which has now been duly probated, leaves his entire estate to his church. The mother is the sole heir-at-law of the buyer. There is no statute dealing with conveyances to dead persons. Who has title to the cottage?","choices":{"A":"The church.","B":"The mother.","C":"The seller.","D":"The seller, subject to a lien to secure $200,000 to the buyer's estate."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Although the buyer left his entire estate to the church, the cottage would only be part of that estate if the buyer had an interest in the cottage when he died. He did not have legal title upon death since the seller had not yet delivered the deed. But under the doctrine of equitable conversion, the buyer did have equitable title—ie, the right to obtain full ownership of the property (ie, legal and equitable title) upon closing. That is because an enforceable sales contract was formed when the buyer's mother exercised the option to buy the cottage on his behalf. Since equitable title is a real property interest, it passed to the church under the buyer's will. The church then obtained legal title to, and full ownership of, the cottage upon closing—when the seller delivered the deed to the mother (Choices B & C). (Choice D) If a buyer conveys his/her equitable title before the purchase price has been paid, that title will be subject to the seller's equitable lien. But here, the mother paid the purchase price ($200,000) before the cottage was devised to the church. Therefore, the seller has no lien on the church's title or any other interest in the cottage.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, a purchaser receives equitable title to property when he/she enters an enforceable sales contract (eg, by paying the sale price). Equitable title is a real property interest that gives the purchaser the right to obtain full ownership of the property upon closing (eg, when the seller delivers the deed).\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 26 (2018) (option contract).","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, a purchaser receives equitable title to property when he/she enters an enforceable sales contract (eg, by paying the sale price). Equitable title is a real property interest that gives the purchaser the right to obtain full ownership of the property upon closing (eg, when the seller delivers the deed).","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 26 (2018) (option contract).","rule":"Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, a purchaser receives equitable title to property when he/she enters an enforceable sales contract (eg, by paying the sale price). Equitable title is a real property interest that gives the purchaser the right to obtain full ownership of the property upon closing (eg, when the seller delivers the deed).","irac":{"issue":"Who has title to the cottage?","rule":"Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, a purchaser receives equitable title to property when he/she enters an enforceable sales contract (eg, by paying the sale price). Equitable title is a real property interest that gives the purchaser the right to obtain full ownership of the property upon closing (eg, when the seller delivers the deed).","analysis":"Although the buyer left his entire estate to the church, the cottage would only be part of that estate if the buyer had an interest in the cottage when he died. He did not have legal title upon death since the seller had not yet delivered the deed. But under the doctrine of equitable conversion, the buyer did have equitable title—ie, the right to obtain full ownership of the property (ie, legal and equitable title) upon closing. That is because an enforceable sales contract was formed when the buyer's mother exercised the option to buy the cottage on his behalf. Since equitable title is a real property interest, it passed to the church under the buyer's will. The church then obtained legal title to, and full ownership of, the cottage upon closing—when the seller delivered the deed to the mother (Choices B & C). (Choice D) If a buyer conveys his/her equitable title before the purchase price has been paid, that title will be subject to the seller's equitable lien. But here, the mother paid the purchase price ($200,000) before the cottage was devised to the church. Therefore, the seller has no lien on the church's title or any other interest in the cottage.","conclusion":"Answer A: The church."},"questionZh":"卖家向买家承诺，如果卖家在任何时候决定出售他的夏季别墅，他都会给买家购买别墅的机会。当时买家正在美国海军海外服役，卖家决定以 20 万美元的价格出售这间小屋，并与买家的母亲进行了交谈。在买家起航之前，他已安排母亲成为他各个银行账户的共同所有者，以便母亲在他去世后能够支付他的账单。当收到卖家的消息后，母亲从买家的账户中取出了必要的资金，并向卖家支付了 20 万美元。两周后，卖方签署并向母亲交付了一份适当形式的契约，旨在将小屋转让给买方。母亲及时并正确地记录了这一行为。此后不久，母亲得知买家在房产交付前一周在海上事故中丧生。买家的最后遗嘱现已正式生效，将他的全部财产留给了他的教堂。母亲是买家的唯一继承人。没有法律涉及死者的运输。谁拥有小屋的所有权？","choicesZh":{"A":"教堂。","B":"母亲。","C":"卖家。","D":"卖方享有留置权，以确保买方的财产获得 200,000 美元的担保。"},"explanationZh":"尽管买家将其全部财产留给了教堂，但只有当买家去世时对小屋拥有权益时，小屋才会成为该遗产的一部分。由于卖方尚未交付契约，他死后没有合法所有权。但根据衡平法转换原则，买方确实拥有衡平法所有权，即在交割时获得财产完全所有权的权利（即合法且衡平法上的所有权）。这是因为，当买方的母亲代表他行使购买小屋的选择权时，就形成了可执行的销售合同。由于衡平所有权是不动产权益，因此它根据买方的遗嘱转移给教会。然后，当卖方将契约交给母亲时，教会在成交时获得了小屋的合法所有权和完全所有权（选择 B 和 C）。 （选择 D）如果买方在支付购买价格之前转让其衡平法所有权，则该所有权将受卖方衡平法留置权的约束。但在这里，母亲在为教堂设计小屋之前支付了购买价（20万美元）。因此，卖方对教堂的所有权或小屋的任何其他权益没有留置权。\n\n教育目标：根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，购买者在签订可执行的销售合同（例如，通过支付销售价格）时获得财产的公平所有权。衡平法所有权是一种不动产权益，赋予买方在成交时（例如，当卖方交付契约时）获得财产完全所有权的权利。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 26 (2018)（期权合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，购买者在签订可执行的销售合同（例如，通过支付销售价格）时获得财产的公平所有权。衡平法所有权是一种不动产权益，赋予买方在成交时（例如，当卖方交付契约时）获得财产完全所有权的权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁拥有小屋的所有权？","rule":"根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，购买者在签订可执行的销售合同（例如，通过支付销售价格）时获得财产的公平所有权。衡平法所有权是一种不动产权益，赋予买方在成交时（例如，当卖方交付契约时）获得财产完全所有权的权利。","analysis":"尽管买家将其全部财产留给了教堂，但只有当买家去世时对小屋拥有权益时，小屋才会成为该遗产的一部分。由于卖方尚未交付契约，他死后没有合法所有权。但根据衡平法转换原则，买方确实拥有衡平法所有权，即在交割时获得财产完全所有权的权利（即合法且衡平法上的所有权）。这是因为，当买方的母亲代表他行使购买小屋的选择权时，就形成了可执行的销售合同。由于衡平所有权是不动产权益，因此它根据买方的遗嘱转移给教会。然后，当卖方将契约交给母亲时，教会在成交时获得了小屋的合法所有权和完全所有权（选择 B 和 C）。 （选择 D）如果买方在支付购买价格之前转让其衡平法所有权，则该所有权将受卖方衡平法留置权的约束。但在这里，母亲在为教堂设计小屋之前支付了购买价（20万美元）。因此，卖方对教堂的所有权或小屋的任何其他权益没有留置权。\n\n教育目标：根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，购买者在签订可执行的销售合同（例如，通过支付销售价格）时获得财产的公平所有权。衡平法所有权是一种不动产权益，赋予买方在成交时（例如，当卖方交付契约时）获得财产完全所有权的权利。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 26 (2018)（期权合同）。","conclusion":"答案 A：教堂。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-2f1350320e3df630"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":219,"sourceQuestionPage":638,"sourceQuestionPages":[638],"sourceExplanationPages":[639],"sourceAnswerPages":[639],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-220","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A man conveyed his house to his wife for life, remainder to his only child, a son by a previous marriage. Thereafter, the man died, devising his entire estate to his son. The wife later removed a light fixture in the dining room of the house and replaced it with a chandelier that was one of her family heirlooms. She then informed her nephew and her late husband's son that after her death, the chandelier should be removed from the dining room and replaced with the former light fixture, which she had stored in the basement. The wife died and under her will bequeathed her entire estate to her nephew. She also named the nephew as the personal representative of her estate. After the nephew, in his capacity as personal representative, removed the chandelier and replaced it with the original light fixture shortly after the wife's death, the son sued to have the chandelier reinstalled. Who will likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"The nephew, because he had the right to remove the chandelier within a reasonable time after the wife's death.","B":"The nephew, because of the doctrine of accession.","C":"The son, because a personal representative can remove only trade fixtures from real property.","D":"The son, because the chandelier could not be legally removed after the death of the wife."},"answer":"A","explanation":"When a life tenant, the holder of a life estate, annexes (ie, attaches) a chattel to the property, the life tenant—or his/her personal representative—has the right to remove the chattel within a reasonable time after the estate ends if: the life tenant did not intend for the annexation to be permanent and the chattel can be removed without substantial damage to the property or the chattel. Here, the man conveyed a life estate in the house to his wife (life tenant), who installed a chandelier (chattel) in the dining room. The wife did not intend to permanently annex the chandelier because she said it should be removed after her death. Additionally, there is no evidence that its removal caused any damage. Therefore, the nephew (the wife's personal representative) had the right to remove the chandelier within a reasonable time after her death (the termination of her life estate). (Choice B) Under the doctrine of accession, ownership of an annexed chattel passes to the real property owner when the chattel cannot be removed without causing substantial damage. This doctrine does not apply here because the chandelier can likely be removed without causing damage. But if this doctrine did apply, then the son—not the nephew— would prevail. (Choice C) The right to remove a chattel when a life estate ends is not limited to trade fixtures (chattels installed for use in the tenant's trade or business—eg, store display counters, restaurant equipment). (Choice D) As the wife's personal representative, the nephew could legally remove the chandelier within a reasonable time after her death.\n\nEducational objective: A life tenant (or his/her personal representative) may remove an annexed chattel within a reasonable time after the life estate ends if (1) the life tenant did not intend to permanently annex the chattel and (2) its removal will not cause substantial damage.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (First) of Property § 122 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (life tenant's right to remove fixtures).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 12.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (tenant's rights and obligations as to physical changes to the property).","educationalObjective":"A life tenant (or his/her personal representative) may remove an annexed chattel within a reasonable time after the life estate ends if (1) the life tenant did not intend to permanently annex the chattel and (2) its removal will not cause substantial damage.","references":"Restatement (First) of Property § 122 (Am. Law Inst. 1936) (life tenant's right to remove fixtures).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Property: Landlord & Tenant § 12.2 (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (tenant's rights and obligations as to physical changes to the property).","rule":"A life tenant (or his/her personal representative) may remove an annexed chattel within a reasonable time after the life estate ends if (1) the life tenant did not intend to permanently annex the chattel and (2) its removal will not cause substantial damage.","irac":{"issue":"Who will likely prevail?","rule":"A life tenant (or his/her personal representative) may remove an annexed chattel within a reasonable time after the life estate ends if (1) the life tenant did not intend to permanently annex the chattel and (2) its removal will not cause substantial damage.","analysis":"When a life tenant, the holder of a life estate, annexes (ie, attaches) a chattel to the property, the life tenant—or his/her personal representative—has the right to remove the chattel within a reasonable time after the estate ends if: the life tenant did not intend for the annexation to be permanent and the chattel can be removed without substantial damage to the property or the chattel. Here, the man conveyed a life estate in the house to his wife (life tenant), who installed a chandelier (chattel) in the dining room. The wife did not intend to permanently annex the chandelier because she said it should be removed after her death. Additionally, there is no evidence that its removal caused any damage. Therefore, the nephew (the wife's personal representative) had the right to remove the chandelier within a reasonable time after her death (the termination of her life estate). (Choice B) Under the doctrine of accession, ownership of an annexed chattel passes to the real property owner when the chattel cannot be removed without causing substantial damage. This doctrine does not apply here because the chandelier can likely be removed without causing damage. But if this doctrine did apply, then the son—not the nephew— would prevail. (Choice C) The right to remove a chattel when a life estate ends is not limited to trade fixtures (chattels installed for use in the tenant's trade or business—eg, store display counters, restaurant equipment). (Choice D) As the wife's personal representative, the nephew could legally remove the chandelier within a reasonable time after her death.","conclusion":"Answer A: The nephew, because he had the right to remove the chandelier within a reasonable time after the wife's death."},"questionZh":"一名男子将他的房子终身转让给他的妻子，其余的则留给他唯一的孩子，即前一段婚姻所生的儿子。此后，该男子去世，将其全部财产留给了他的儿子。妻子后来拆掉了房子餐厅里的一个灯具，换上了她的传家宝之一的枝形吊灯。然后，她告诉她的侄子和已故丈夫的儿子，在她去世后，应该将吊灯从餐厅移走，并用她存放在地下室的以前的灯具代替。妻子去世后，她将全部财产遗赠给她的侄子。她还任命侄子为她遗产的个人代表。在妻子去世后不久，侄子以个人代表的身份拆除了枝形吊灯并用原来的灯具代替，之后儿子提起诉讼，要求重新安装枝形吊灯。谁可能会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"侄子，因为他有权在妻子去世后的合理时间内拆除吊灯。","B":"侄子，因教义即位。","C":"儿子，因为个人代表只能从不动产中删除交易装置。","D":"儿子，因为妻子去世后无法合法拆除吊灯。"},"explanationZh":"当终身承租人，即终身遗产的持有人，将动产附加（即附加）到财产上时，终身承租人或其私人代表有权在遗产终止后的合理时间内移走动产，如果： 终身承租人并不打算使兼并成为永久性的，并且可以在不对财产或动产造成重大损害的情况下移走动产。在这里，该男子将房屋中的终身财产转让给他的妻子（终身租户），后者在餐厅安装了枝形吊灯（动产）。妻子并不打算永久吞并这盏吊灯，因为她说她死后应该将其拆除。此外，没有证据表明其移除造成任何损害。因此，侄子（妻子的私人代表）有权在她去世（她的终身财产终止）后的合理时间内拆除吊灯。 （选择B）根据加入原则，当所附动产无法在不造成重大损害的情况下被移除时，该动产的所有权就转移给不动产所有者。这一原则在这里并不适用，因为枝形吊灯很可能被拆除而不会造成损坏。但如果这一学说确实适用，那么儿子——而不是侄子——将会占上风。 （选项C） 当终身财产结束时，移走动产的权利不仅限于贸易固定装置（为租户的贸易或业务而安装的动产，例如商店展示柜台、餐厅设备）。 （选项D）作为妻子的私人代表，侄子可以在她死后的合理时间内合法地拆除吊灯。\n\n教育目标：终身承租人（或其个人代表）可以在终身财产结束后的合理时间内移除所附的动产，前提是（1）终身承租人不打算永久兼并动产，并且（2）其移除不会造成重大损害。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 122（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（终身租户拆除固定装置的权利）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 12.2（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（租户对财产物理变化的权利和义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果 (1) 终身承租人不打算永久兼并动产，并且 (2) 其移除不会造成重大损害，则终身承租人（或其个人代表）可以在终身财产结束后的合理时间内移除所附动产。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁可能会获胜？","rule":"如果 (1) 终身承租人不打算永久兼并动产，并且 (2) 其移除不会造成重大损害，则终身承租人（或其个人代表）可以在终身财产结束后的合理时间内移除所附动产。","analysis":"当终身承租人，即终身遗产的持有人，将动产附加（即附加）到财产上时，终身承租人或其私人代表有权在遗产终止后的合理时间内移走动产，如果： 终身承租人并不打算使兼并成为永久性的，并且可以在不对财产或动产造成重大损害的情况下移走动产。在这里，该男子将房屋中的终身财产转让给他的妻子（终身租户），后者在餐厅安装了枝形吊灯（动产）。妻子并不打算永久吞并这盏吊灯，因为她说她死后应该将其拆除。此外，没有证据表明其移除造成任何损害。因此，侄子（妻子的私人代表）有权在她去世（她的终身财产终止）后的合理时间内拆除吊灯。 （选择B）根据加入原则，当所附动产无法在不造成重大损害的情况下被移除时，该动产的所有权就转移给不动产所有者。这一原则在这里并不适用，因为枝形吊灯很可能被拆除而不会造成损坏。但如果这一学说确实适用，那么儿子——而不是侄子——将会占上风。 （选项C） 当终身财产结束时，移走动产的权利不仅限于贸易固定装置（为租户的贸易或业务而安装的动产，例如商店展示柜台、餐厅设备）。 （选项D）作为妻子的私人代表，侄子可以在她死后的合理时间内合法地拆除吊灯。\n\n教育目标：终身承租人（或其个人代表）可以在终身财产结束后的合理时间内移除所附的动产，前提是（1）终身承租人不打算永久兼并动产，并且（2）其移除不会造成重大损害。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第一）§ 122（Am. Law Inst. 1936）（终身租户拆除固定装置的权利）。\n\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 12.2（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（租户对财产物理变化的权利和义务）。","conclusion":"答案 A：侄子，因为他有权在妻子去世后的合理时间内拆除吊灯。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-5c6cf94bd116e517"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":220,"sourceQuestionPage":640,"sourceQuestionPages":[640],"sourceExplanationPages":[641,642],"sourceAnswerPages":[641,642],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-221","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"An owner of land contracted to sell it to a buyer for $100,000, its fair market value at that time. After an unanticipated zoning change increased the land's fair market value to $150,000 during the executory period, the owner refused to close. Wishing to avoid a lawsuit, the buyer assigned the contract (which the contract did not forbid) in an arm's-length transaction to an investor, who is experienced in buying and selling real estate. The investor paid the buyer $25,000. The investor knew of the owner's refusal to close, and the owner continued to refuse to close despite the investor's demand that he do so. The investor has sued the owner for specific performance. Who will likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"The investor, because an assignee of the original party purchaser in a real estate contract is entitled to specific performance under these circumstances.","B":"The investor, because she has elected to waive the owner's lack of marketable title caused by the zoning change.","C":"The owner, because the investor is not a bona fide assignee without notice, and thus does not have clean hands.","D":"The owner, because the investor, who is experienced in buying and selling real estate, is entitled to money damages but not to specific performance."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Buyer's remedies\n\nRescission & Rescind (ie, cancel) agreement restitution Seek return of paid deposits & other restitution Specific Seek injunction ordering seller to transfer title with abatement of performance purchase price Generally available since property is considered unique (ie, money damages would not make buyer whole) Damages Loss of bargain = market value at time of breach − contract price Liquidated damages = amount designated in sales contract as compensation for breach Incidental & consequential damages that were contemplated during sales contract or reasonably foreseeable In a contract for the sale of real property, the buyer promises to pay the agreed purchase price and, in exchange, the seller promises to transfer marketable title. The parties can assign (ie, transfer) their respective contractual rights to a third party—even without the other party's consent or consideration*—unless the assignment would: materially change or increase the obligor's duty or risk or materially impair or reduce the obligor's chance of obtaining return performance. Here, the buyer assigned his contractual rights to the investor (third party). Since there is no indication that this assignment would impact the owner's contractual duties, it was permissible. As a result, the investor now stands in the buyer's shoes and can enforce the sales contract accordingly. If a seller refuses to perform, the buyer can (1) rescind the contract and seek restitution, (2) seek specific performance, or (3) sue for damages. Specific performance is only available when money damages are inadequate. Since land is considered unique and money damages cannot compensate for the loss of unique property, this remedy is generally available to buyers (Choice D). Therefore, the investor will likely prevail. *Although consideration is not required for an assignment, it is required to make the assignment irrevocable. (Choice B) Real estate contracts are typically conditioned on the seller's ability to convey marketable title—ie, title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Since the zoning change increased the land's fair market value, it did not render title unmarketable. And there is no evidence that the investor waived this condition.\n\n(Choice C) The investor is a bona fide assignee since there is no indication that she acted in bad faith. If she had, the owner could raise unclean hands as a defense to preclude her from obtaining specific performance and other equitable remedies.\n\nEducational objective: A buyer can generally assign a contract for the sale of real property to a third party (same as the seller). The third party can then enforce the contract to the same extent as the buyer— eg, by seeking specific performance.","educationalObjective":"A buyer can generally assign a contract for the sale of real property to a third party (same as the seller). The third party can then enforce the contract to the same extent as the buyer— eg, by seeking specific performance.","references":"","rule":"A buyer can generally assign a contract for the sale of real property to a third party (same as the seller). The third party can then enforce the contract to the same extent as the buyer— eg, by seeking specific performance.","irac":{"issue":"Who will likely prevail?","rule":"A buyer can generally assign a contract for the sale of real property to a third party (same as the seller). The third party can then enforce the contract to the same extent as the buyer— eg, by seeking specific performance.","analysis":"Buyer's remedies\n\nRescission & Rescind (ie, cancel) agreement restitution Seek return of paid deposits & other restitution Specific Seek injunction ordering seller to transfer title with abatement of performance purchase price Generally available since property is considered unique (ie, money damages would not make buyer whole) Damages Loss of bargain = market value at time of breach − contract price Liquidated damages = amount designated in sales contract as compensation for breach Incidental & consequential damages that were contemplated during sales contract or reasonably foreseeable In a contract for the sale of real property, the buyer promises to pay the agreed purchase price and, in exchange, the seller promises to transfer marketable title. The parties can assign (ie, transfer) their respective contractual rights to a third party—even without the other party's consent or consideration*—unless the assignment would: materially change or increase the obligor's duty or risk or materially impair or reduce the obligor's chance of obtaining return performance. Here, the buyer assigned his contractual rights to the investor (third party). Since there is no indication that this assignment would impact the owner's contractual duties, it was permissible. As a result, the investor now stands in the buyer's shoes and can enforce the sales contract accordingly. If a seller refuses to perform, the buyer can (1) rescind the contract and seek restitution, (2) seek specific performance, or (3) sue for damages. Specific performance is only available when money damages are inadequate. Since land is considered unique and money damages cannot compensate for the loss of unique property, this remedy is generally available to buyers (Choice D). Therefore, the investor will likely prevail. *Although consideration is not required for an assignment, it is required to make the assignment irrevocable. (Choice B) Real estate contracts are typically conditioned on the seller's ability to convey marketable title—ie, title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Since the zoning change increased the land's fair market value, it did not render title unmarketable. And there is no evidence that the investor waived this condition.\n\n(Choice C) The investor is a bona fide assignee since there is no indication that she acted in bad faith. If she had, the owner could raise unclean hands as a defense to preclude her from obtaining specific performance and other equitable remedies.","conclusion":"Answer A: The investor, because an assignee of the original party purchaser in a real estate contract is entitled to specific performance under these circumstances."},"questionZh":"土地所有者签订合同，以 10 万美元（当时的公平市场价值）将其出售给买家。在执行期间，意外的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更使土地的公平市场价值增加到 150,000 美元，但业主拒绝关闭。为了避免诉讼，买方在公平交易中将合同（合同并未禁止）转让给了一位在房地产买卖方面经验丰富的投资者。投资者向买家支付了 25,000 美元。投资者知道业主拒绝关闭，尽管投资者要求业主关闭，但业主仍然拒绝关闭。投资者已就具体表现起诉业主。谁可能会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"投资者，因为房地产合同中原购买方的受让人在这种情况下有权具体履行。","B":"投资者，因为她选择放弃因土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更而导致业主缺乏适销所有权的情况。","C":"业主，由于投资者在未经通知的情况下不是善意受让人，因而不干净。","D":"由于投资者在房地产买卖方面经验丰富，业主有权获得金钱赔偿，但无权获得具体履约。"},"explanationZh":"买方的补救措施\n\n撤销和撤销（即取消）协议恢复原状 寻求返还已付定金和其他赔偿 具体寻求禁令，命令卖方转让所有权并减少履约购买价格 一般情况下可以使用，因为财产被认为是独特的（即金钱损失不会使买方完整） 损害赔偿 讨价还价的损失 = 违约时的市场价值 - 合同价格 违约赔偿金 = 销售合同中指定的违约赔偿金额 销售合同期间或合理预期的附带和间接损害赔偿可预见 在不动产销售合同中，买方承诺支付约定的购买价格，作为交换，卖方承诺转让可流通的所有权。双方可以将各自的合同权利转让（即转让）给第三方，即使没有另一方的同意或考虑*，除非转让会： 实质性改变或增加债务人的责任或风险，或实质性损害或减少债务人获得回报的机会。在这里，买方将其合同权利转让给投资者（第三方）。由于没有迹象表明这项转让会影响业主的合同义务，因此是允许的。因此，投资者现在站在买方的立场上，可以相应地执行销售合同。如果卖方拒绝履行，买方可以（1）解除合同并寻求赔偿，（2）寻求具体履行，或（3）起诉要求赔偿。仅当金钱损害赔偿不足时，才能提供特定的履行。由于土地被认为是独特的，金钱损失无法弥补独特财产的损失，因此买家通常可以使用这种补救措施（选择 D）。因此，投资者很可能会占上风。 *虽然转让不需要考虑，但需要使转让不可撤销。 （选择B） 房地产合同通常以卖方转让适销所有权的能力为条件，即，所有权是合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁的，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。由于土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更增加了土地的公平市场价值，因此并没有导致所有权无法出售。并且没有证据表明投资者放弃了这一条件。\n\n（选项C）投资者是善意受让人，因为没有迹象表明她有恶意行为。如果她这样做了，所有者可以举起不干净的手作为辩护，以阻止她获得特定的表现和其他公平的补救措施。\n\n教育目标：买方通常可以将不动产销售合同转让给第三方（与卖方相同）。然后，第三方可以在与买方相同的程度上执行合同——例如，通过寻求具体履行。","educationalObjectiveZh":"买方通常可以将不动产销售合同转让给第三方（与卖方相同）。然后，第三方可以在与买方相同的程度上执行合同——例如，通过寻求具体履行。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁可能会获胜？","rule":"买方通常可以将不动产销售合同转让给第三方（与卖方相同）。然后，第三方可以在与买方相同的程度上执行合同——例如，通过寻求具体履行。","analysis":"买方的补救措施\n\n撤销和撤销（即取消）协议恢复原状 寻求返还已付定金和其他赔偿 具体寻求禁令，命令卖方转让所有权并减少履约购买价格 一般情况下可以使用，因为财产被认为是独特的（即金钱损失不会使买方完整） 损害赔偿 讨价还价的损失 = 违约时的市场价值 - 合同价格 违约赔偿金 = 销售合同中指定的违约赔偿金额 销售合同期间或合理预期的附带和间接损害赔偿可预见 在不动产销售合同中，买方承诺支付约定的购买价格，作为交换，卖方承诺转让可流通的所有权。双方可以将各自的合同权利转让（即转让）给第三方，即使没有另一方的同意或考虑*，除非转让会： 实质性改变或增加债务人的责任或风险，或实质性损害或减少债务人获得回报的机会。在这里，买方将其合同权利转让给投资者（第三方）。由于没有迹象表明这项转让会影响业主的合同义务，因此是允许的。因此，投资者现在站在买方的立场上，可以相应地执行销售合同。如果卖方拒绝履行，买方可以（1）解除合同并寻求赔偿，（2）寻求具体履行，或（3）起诉要求赔偿。仅当金钱损害赔偿不足时，才能提供特定的履行。由于土地被认为是独特的，金钱损失无法弥补独特财产的损失，因此买家通常可以使用这种补救措施（选择 D）。因此，投资者很可能会占上风。 *虽然转让不需要考虑，但需要使转让不可撤销。 （选择B） 房地产合同通常以卖方转让适销所有权的能力为条件，即，所有权是合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁的，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。由于土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更增加了土地的公平市场价值，因此并没有导致所有权无法出售。并且没有证据表明投资者放弃了这一条件。\n\n（选项C）投资者是善意受让人，因为没有迹象表明她有恶意行为。如果她这样做了，所有者可以举起不干净的手作为辩护，以阻止她获得特定的表现和其他公平的补救措施。\n\n教育目标：买方通常可以将不动产销售合同转让给第三方（与卖方相同）。然后，第三方可以在与买方相同的程度上执行合同——例如，通过寻求具体履行。","conclusion":"答案 A：投资者，因为房地产合同中原购买方的受让人在这种情况下有权具体履行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":221,"sourceQuestionPage":643,"sourceQuestionPages":[643],"sourceExplanationPages":[644,645],"sourceAnswerPages":[644,645],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-222","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A landlord leased a building to a tenant for a term of six years. The lease complied with the statute of frauds and was not recorded. During the lease term, the tenant sent an email to the landlord that stated: \"I hereby offer to purchase for $250,000 the building that I am now occupying under a six-year lease with you.\" The tenant's name was placed below the word \"signed\" on the message. In response, the landlord emailed the tenant: \"That's fine. We'll close in 60 days.\" The landlord's name was placed below the word \"signed\" on the reply message. Sixty days later, the landlord refused to tender the deed to the building when the tenant tendered the $250,000 purchase price. The tenant has sued for specific performance. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The landlord, because formation of an enforceable contract to convey the building could not occur until after the lease term expired.","B":"The landlord, because the landlord's email response did not contain a sufficient signature under the statute of frauds.","C":"The tenant, because the email messages constitute an insufficient attornment of the lease.","D":"The tenant, because the email messages constitute a sufficient memorandum under the statute of frauds."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Statute of frauds requirements for real estate contracts\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed\n\neg, receipt, check, emails Signature Handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged\n\neg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials Essential Typically includes: terms identity of parties words of intent to buy or sell property description sales price Under the statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of land must meet three requirements to be enforceable: Writing – physical and electronic document(s) evidencing a contract (eg, the parties' emails) Signature – an identifying mark from the party against whom enforcement is sought Essential terms – the parties' identity, words of intent to buy and sell, land description, and price term (all of which appear in the parties' emails) The signature requirement is met so long as the identifying mark reflects an intent to authenticate the writing. Here, the tenant's and the landlord's names were placed below the word \"signed,\" so their signatures show their desire to be bound (Choice B). And since their emails fulfill the other statute of frauds requirements, they constitute an enforceable memorandum. Therefore, the tenant is likely to prevail in his suit for specific performance. (Choice A) An enforceable contract to convey a building can be formed during the lease term (as seen here) or thereafter. (Choice C) Attornment refers to a tenant's agreement to be bound by a lease when there is a new landlord (eg, by sale or foreclosure). Here, the tenant seeks to purchase the property, so attornment is not an issue in this case.\n\nEducational objective: The signature requirement for the statute of frauds is met if an identifying mark from the party against whom enforcement is sought reflects an intent to authenticate the writing.\n\nReferences\n73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).","educationalObjective":"The signature requirement for the statute of frauds is met if an identifying mark from the party against whom enforcement is sought reflects an intent to authenticate the writing.","references":"73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).","rule":"The signature requirement for the statute of frauds is met if an identifying mark from the party against whom enforcement is sought reflects an intent to authenticate the writing.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"The signature requirement for the statute of frauds is met if an identifying mark from the party against whom enforcement is sought reflects an intent to authenticate the writing.","analysis":"Statute of frauds requirements for real estate contracts\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed\n\neg, receipt, check, emails Signature Handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged\n\neg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials Essential Typically includes: terms identity of parties words of intent to buy or sell property description sales price Under the statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of land must meet three requirements to be enforceable: Writing – physical and electronic document(s) evidencing a contract (eg, the parties' emails) Signature – an identifying mark from the party against whom enforcement is sought Essential terms – the parties' identity, words of intent to buy and sell, land description, and price term (all of which appear in the parties' emails) The signature requirement is met so long as the identifying mark reflects an intent to authenticate the writing. Here, the tenant's and the landlord's names were placed below the word \"signed,\" so their signatures show their desire to be bound (Choice B). And since their emails fulfill the other statute of frauds requirements, they constitute an enforceable memorandum. Therefore, the tenant is likely to prevail in his suit for specific performance. (Choice A) An enforceable contract to convey a building can be formed during the lease term (as seen here) or thereafter. (Choice C) Attornment refers to a tenant's agreement to be bound by a lease when there is a new landlord (eg, by sale or foreclosure). Here, the tenant seeks to purchase the property, so attornment is not an issue in this case.","conclusion":"Answer D: The tenant, because the email messages constitute a sufficient memorandum under the statute of frauds."},"questionZh":"房东将一栋建筑物出租给租户，期限为六年。该租约符合欺诈法，且未记录在案。在租赁期内，租户向房东发送了一封电子邮件，其中写道：“我特此提出以 25 万美元的价格购买我现在与您签订六年租约的建筑物。”租户的名字被放置在消息中“签名”一词的下方。作为回应，房东给租户发了一封电子邮件：“没关系。我们将在 60 天后关门。”房东的名字被放在回复信息中“签名”一词的下方。六十天后，当租户提出 25 万美元的购买价时，房东拒绝向该建筑物提交契约。租户已就具体表现提起诉讼。谁有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"房东，因为要到租赁期满后才能签订可执行的建筑物转让合同。","B":"房东，因为根据欺诈法，房东的电子邮件回复没有包含足够的签名。","C":"租户，因为电子邮件信息不足以构成对租约的充分约束。","D":"租户，因为根据欺诈法，电子邮件构成了充分的备忘录。"},"explanationZh":"房地产合同欺诈法规要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件\n\n例如，收据、支票、电子邮件 签名 被指控方的手写签名或其他识别标记\n\n例如，信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名首字母缩写 基本条款 通常包括： 条款 各方身份 购买或出售财产的意向文字 描述 销售价格 根据反欺诈法，土地出售合同必须满足三个要求才能强制执行： 书写 – 证明合同的纸质和电子文件（例如各方的电子邮件） 签名 – 寻求执行的一方的识别标记 基本条款 – 各方的身份、买卖意向文字、土地描述和价格条款（所有这些都包括在内）出现在双方的电子邮件中）只要识别标记反映了验证书写内容的意图，就满足签名要求。在这里，租客和房东的名字被放在“签名”一词下方，因此他们的签名表明他们希望受到约束（选项 B）。由于他们的电子邮件满足其他欺诈法规的要求，因此它们构成了一份可执行的备忘录。因此，租客就具体表现提起诉讼，很可能胜诉。 （选择 A）可以在租赁期内（如此处所示）或之后签订可执行的建筑物转让合同。 （选项C） 授权是指当有新房东时，租户同意受租约约束（例如，通过出售或取消抵押品赎回权）。在这里，租户寻求购买房产，因此在这种情况下，侵权不是问题。\n\n教育目标：如果寻求执行的一方的识别标记反映了对书面内容进行认证的意图，则满足欺诈法规的签名要求。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果寻求执行的一方的识别标记反映了对书面内容进行认证的意图，则满足欺诈法规的签名要求。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"如果寻求执行的一方的识别标记反映了对书面内容进行认证的意图，则满足欺诈法规的签名要求。","analysis":"房地产合同欺诈法规要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件\n\n例如，收据、支票、电子邮件 签名 被指控方的手写签名或其他识别标记\n\n例如，信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名首字母缩写 基本条款 通常包括： 条款 各方身份 购买或出售财产的意向文字 描述 销售价格 根据反欺诈法，土地出售合同必须满足三个要求才能强制执行： 书写 – 证明合同的纸质和电子文件（例如各方的电子邮件） 签名 – 寻求执行的一方的识别标记 基本条款 – 各方的身份、买卖意向文字、土地描述和价格条款（所有这些都包括在内）出现在双方的电子邮件中）只要识别标记反映了验证书写内容的意图，就满足签名要求。在这里，租客和房东的名字被放在“签名”一词下方，因此他们的签名表明他们希望受到约束（选项 B）。由于他们的电子邮件满足其他欺诈法规的要求，因此它们构成了一份可执行的备忘录。因此，租客就具体表现提起诉讼，很可能胜诉。 （选择 A）可以在租赁期内（如此处所示）或之后签订可执行的建筑物转让合同。 （选项C） 授权是指当有新房东时，租户同意受租约约束（例如，通过出售或取消抵押品赎回权）。在这里，租户寻求购买房产，因此在这种情况下，侵权不是问题。\n\n教育目标：如果寻求执行的一方的识别标记反映了对书面内容进行认证的意图，则满足欺诈法规的签名要求。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。","conclusion":"答案 D：租户，因为根据欺诈法，电子邮件构成了充分的备忘录。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":222,"sourceQuestionPage":646,"sourceQuestionPages":[646],"sourceExplanationPages":[647,648],"sourceAnswerPages":[647,648],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-223","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A developer and a commercial real estate investment group own adjoining lots near a city's downtown. The developer decided to raze the existing building on its lot and erect an office building of greater height. The developer received all governmental approvals required to pursue the project and no statute or ordinance applies (other than those dealing with various approvals for zoning, building, etc.). When the building was completed, the investment group discovered that the substantial shadow cast by the developer's new building diminished its ability to lease apartments, substantially lowered the rent it could charge, and greatly reduced the apartment complex's overall occupancy rate. Assume that these facts are proved in an appropriate action the investment group instituted against the developer for all and any relief available. Which of the following is the most appropriate comment concerning this lawsuit?","choices":{"A":"Judgment should be for the developer, because the investment group has no cause of action.","B":"The investment group is entitled to a mandatory injunction requiring the developer to restore conditions to those existing with the prior building insofar as the shadow is concerned.","C":"The court should award damages for losses suffered to the date of trial and leave open recovery of future damages.","D":"The court should award permanent damages, in lieu of an injunction, equal to the present value of all rents lost and loss on rents for the reasonable life of the apartment complex."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Available negative easements\n\nCommon law Modern view\n\nLight View Air Solar access Support Conservation Water access The Doctrine of Ancient Lights is an English common law doctrine that enabled landowners to prevent their neighbors from constructing buildings or other structures that blocked their access to sunlight. This doctrine has been rejected in every jurisdiction, so landowners can only restrict another's blockage of light—or other lawful use of their own land—through a negative easement (ie, restrictive covenant). This type of easement can only be created by express written agreement. Here, the developer's new building casts a substantial shadow over the investment group's apartment complex. But the developer received all necessary governmental approvals to pursue its project and there is no applicable statute. Additionally, there is no indication that the investment group obtained a negative easement or covenant from the developer that would prevent it from blocking the sunlight. Therefore, judgment should be for the developer because the investment group has no cause of action. (Choices B, C & D) Had the investment group obtained a negative easement, it would have been entitled to an injunction and/or damages. Courts consider several factors in determining the appropriate remedy but tend to issue an injunction for breach of a negative easement. That is because the easement holder is denied the benefit of the easement—ie, the ability to restrict another's lawful use of their own land—if only damages are issued.\n\nEducational objective: A landowner can only restrict another's lawful use of their own land by obtaining a negative easement (ie, restrictive covenant). And negative easements can only be created by express written agreement.\n\nReferences\nMohr v. Midas Realty Corp., 431 N.W.2d 380, 382 (Iowa 1988) (explaining that every state has rejected the Doctrine of Ancient Lights).","educationalObjective":"A landowner can only restrict another's lawful use of their own land by obtaining a negative easement (ie, restrictive covenant). And negative easements can only be created by express written agreement.","references":"Mohr v. Midas Realty Corp., 431 N.W.2d 380, 382 (Iowa 1988) (explaining that every state has rejected the Doctrine of Ancient Lights).","rule":"A landowner can only restrict another's lawful use of their own land by obtaining a negative easement (ie, restrictive covenant). And negative easements can only be created by express written agreement.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the most appropriate comment concerning this lawsuit?","rule":"A landowner can only restrict another's lawful use of their own land by obtaining a negative easement (ie, restrictive covenant). And negative easements can only be created by express written agreement.","analysis":"Available negative easements\n\nCommon law Modern view\n\nLight View Air Solar access Support Conservation Water access The Doctrine of Ancient Lights is an English common law doctrine that enabled landowners to prevent their neighbors from constructing buildings or other structures that blocked their access to sunlight. This doctrine has been rejected in every jurisdiction, so landowners can only restrict another's blockage of light—or other lawful use of their own land—through a negative easement (ie, restrictive covenant). This type of easement can only be created by express written agreement. Here, the developer's new building casts a substantial shadow over the investment group's apartment complex. But the developer received all necessary governmental approvals to pursue its project and there is no applicable statute. Additionally, there is no indication that the investment group obtained a negative easement or covenant from the developer that would prevent it from blocking the sunlight. Therefore, judgment should be for the developer because the investment group has no cause of action. (Choices B, C & D) Had the investment group obtained a negative easement, it would have been entitled to an injunction and/or damages. Courts consider several factors in determining the appropriate remedy but tend to issue an injunction for breach of a negative easement. That is because the easement holder is denied the benefit of the easement—ie, the ability to restrict another's lawful use of their own land—if only damages are issued.","conclusion":"Answer A: Judgment should be for the developer, because the investment group has no cause of action."},"questionZh":"一家开发商和一家商业房地产投资集团在市中心附近拥有毗邻的地块。开发商决定将其地块上的现有建筑夷为平地，并建造一座更高的办公楼。开发商已获得开展该项目所需的所有政府批准，并且不适用任何法规或条例（涉及土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）、建筑等各种批准的法规或条例除外）。当大楼竣工后，投资集团发现，开发商新楼的巨大阴影削弱了其出租公寓的能力，大幅降低了其可收取的租金，并大大降低了公寓楼的整体入住率。假设这些事实已在投资集团对开发商采取的适当行动中得到证实，以获得所有可用的救济。以下哪项是关于该诉讼的最恰当的评论？","choicesZh":{"A":"判决应该是开发商的，因为投资集团没有诉讼理由。","B":"投资集团有权获得强制性禁令，要求开发商恢复先前建筑中阴影部分的现有条件。","C":"法院应判决赔偿截至审判之日所遭受的损失，并保留未来损害赔偿的可能性。","D":"法院应判给永久性损害赔偿，以代替禁令，其金额相当于所有租金损失和公寓合理使用期限内的租金损失的现值。"},"explanationZh":"可用的负地役权\n\n普通法现代观点\n\n光视图 空气 太阳能获取 支持保护 水资源获取 古代光学说是一项英国普通法学说，它使土地所有者能够阻止邻居建造建筑物或其他阻碍他们接触阳光的建筑物。这一原则在每个司法管辖区都被拒绝，因此土地所有者只能通过消极地役权（即限制性契约）来限制他人对光线的遮挡或对自己土地的其他合法使用。这种类型的地役权只能通过明确的书面协议创建。在这里，开发商的新建筑给投资集团的公寓大楼投下了巨大的阴影。但开发商获得了开展其项目所需的所有政府批准，并且没有适用的法规。此外，没有迹象表明该投资集团从开发商那里获得了负面地役权或契约，以防止其遮挡阳光。因此，判断应由开发商承担，因为投资集团没有诉由。 （选项 B、C 和 D）如果投资集团获得了负地役权，它将有权获得禁令和/或损害赔偿。法院在确定适当的补救措施时会考虑几个因素，但往往会针对违反消极地役权的行为发布禁令。这是因为，如果仅发出损害赔偿金，地役权持有人就无法享受地役权的好处，即限制他人合法使用自己土地的能力。\n\n教育目标：土地所有者只能通过获得消极地役权（即限制性契约）来限制他人合法使用自己的土地。负地役权只能通过明确的书面协议创建。\n\n参考文献\nMohr v. Midas Realty Corp., 431 N.W.2d 380, 382 (Iowa 1988)（解释说每个州都拒绝了远古之光学说）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地所有者只能通过获得消极地役权（即限制性契约）来限制他人合法使用自己的土地。负地役权只能通过明确的书面协议创建。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项是关于该诉讼的最恰当的评论？","rule":"土地所有者只能通过获得消极地役权（即限制性契约）来限制他人合法使用自己的土地。负地役权只能通过明确的书面协议创建。","analysis":"可用的负地役权\n\n普通法现代观点\n\n光视图 空气 太阳能获取 支持保护 水资源获取 古代光学说是一项英国普通法学说，它使土地所有者能够阻止邻居建造建筑物或其他阻碍他们接触阳光的建筑物。这一原则在每个司法管辖区都被拒绝，因此土地所有者只能通过消极地役权（即限制性契约）来限制他人对光线的遮挡或对自己土地的其他合法使用。这种类型的地役权只能通过明确的书面协议创建。在这里，开发商的新建筑给投资集团的公寓大楼投下了巨大的阴影。但开发商获得了开展其项目所需的所有政府批准，并且没有适用的法规。此外，没有迹象表明该投资集团从开发商那里获得了负面地役权或契约，以防止其遮挡阳光。因此，判断应由开发商承担，因为投资集团没有诉由。 （选项 B、C 和 D）如果投资集团获得了负地役权，它将有权获得禁令和/或损害赔偿。法院在确定适当的补救措施时会考虑几个因素，但往往会针对违反消极地役权的行为发布禁令。这是因为，如果仅发出损害赔偿金，地役权持有人就无法享受地役权的好处，即限制他人合法使用自己土地的能力。\n\n教育目标：土地所有者只能通过获得消极地役权（即限制性契约）来限制他人合法使用自己的土地。负地役权只能通过明确的书面协议创建。\n\n参考文献\nMohr v. Midas Realty Corp., 431 N.W.2d 380, 382 (Iowa 1988)（解释说每个州都拒绝了远古之光学说）。","conclusion":"答案 A：判决应该是开发商的，因为投资集团没有诉讼理由。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":223,"sourceQuestionPage":649,"sourceQuestionPages":[649],"sourceExplanationPages":[650,651],"sourceAnswerPages":[650,651],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-224","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Easements","question":"A landowner granted a sawmill the exclusive right to remove timber from the landowner's property for 10 years in exchange for a quarterly payment based on the size, type, and number of trees removed. Three years later, the sawmill purchased the landowner's property and built a facility for processing timber on the property. Eight years after purchasing the property, the sawmill sold the timbered portion of the land to a buyer, while retaining the portion of the land on which the processing facility was located. The contract made no specific mention of the prior recorded agreement, but the sawmill had come to rely on the supply of timber from the property. Although there are other sources of timber, timber is a necessary raw material for the sawmill. The buyer has refused to permit the sawmill to continue to remove timber from the property purchased by the buyer. Which of the following is the sawmill's best argument for the continued existence of a profit?","choices":{"A":"The buyer is estopped from denying the existence of the profit due to the sawmill's reliance on the property as its source for timber.","B":"The profit exists by implication.","C":"The profit was created by written agreement.","D":"Timber is necessary for the sawmill to operate."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A profit (ie, profit à prendre) is a nonpossessory right to enter another's land and remove specific natural resources (eg, oil, game, timber—as seen here). Profits are generally created and analyzed similarly to easements. As a result, a profit can be implied from an existing prior use if the following elements are met: Severance of common ownership – the dominant (benefited) and servient (burdened) estates were once a single tract of land, after which the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another\n\nPrior existing use – the owner used the servient estate to benefit the dominant estate\n\nApparent and continuous – prior use was discoverable by a reasonable inspection and continued until the tract was severed\n\nReasonable necessity – continuance of the prior use is reasonably necessary for the use and enjoyment of the land\n\nHere, the sawmill owned the entire property before selling the timbered portion to a buyer. The sawmill had been apparently and continuously removing timber from the land until the sale. And the continued removal of timber is reasonably necessary since the sawmill has come to rely on the supply of timber from the buyer's land to operate. Therefore, the sawmill's best argument is that the profit exists by implication. (Choice A) A profit can be created by estoppel if the profit holder, in good faith, reasonably and detrimentally relies on permission from the owner of the land that serves as the source of the profit (not seen here). (Choice C) Although the original landowner granted the sawmill a profit by written agreement (ie, an express profit), the profit terminated by merger when the sawmill acquired the property to which the profit was tied. (Choice D) Profits are usually analyzed in the same manner as easements, but profits cannot be created by necessity. Additionally, the availability of other sources of timber would prevent the sawmill from establishing the strict necessity required to create an easement by necessity.\n\nEducational objective: Profits are generally created and analyzed like easements, so a profit can be implied from an existing prior use.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes &#167; 1.2(2), cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement and profit defined).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes &#167; 2.12 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (servitudes implied from prior use).","educationalObjective":"Profits are generally created and analyzed like easements, so a profit can be implied from an existing prior use.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes &#167; 1.2(2), cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement and profit defined).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes &#167; 2.12 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (servitudes implied from prior use).","rule":"Profits are generally created and analyzed like easements, so a profit can be implied from an existing prior use.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the sawmill's best argument for the continued existence of a profit?","rule":"Profits are generally created and analyzed like easements, so a profit can be implied from an existing prior use.","analysis":"A profit (ie, profit à prendre) is a nonpossessory right to enter another's land and remove specific natural resources (eg, oil, game, timber—as seen here). Profits are generally created and analyzed similarly to easements. As a result, a profit can be implied from an existing prior use if the following elements are met: Severance of common ownership – the dominant (benefited) and servient (burdened) estates were once a single tract of land, after which the owner retained part of the tract and conveyed the rest to another\n\nPrior existing use – the owner used the servient estate to benefit the dominant estate\n\nApparent and continuous – prior use was discoverable by a reasonable inspection and continued until the tract was severed\n\nReasonable necessity – continuance of the prior use is reasonably necessary for the use and enjoyment of the land\n\nHere, the sawmill owned the entire property before selling the timbered portion to a buyer. The sawmill had been apparently and continuously removing timber from the land until the sale. And the continued removal of timber is reasonably necessary since the sawmill has come to rely on the supply of timber from the buyer's land to operate. Therefore, the sawmill's best argument is that the profit exists by implication. (Choice A) A profit can be created by estoppel if the profit holder, in good faith, reasonably and detrimentally relies on permission from the owner of the land that serves as the source of the profit (not seen here). (Choice C) Although the original landowner granted the sawmill a profit by written agreement (ie, an express profit), the profit terminated by merger when the sawmill acquired the property to which the profit was tied. (Choice D) Profits are usually analyzed in the same manner as easements, but profits cannot be created by necessity. Additionally, the availability of other sources of timber would prevent the sawmill from establishing the strict necessity required to create an easement by necessity.","conclusion":"Answer B: The profit exists by implication."},"questionZh":"土地所有者授予锯木厂 10 年内从土地所有者财产中采伐木材的专有权，以换取根据采伐树木的大小、类型和数量按季度付款。三年后，锯木厂购买了土地所有者的财产，并在该财产上建造了一个木材加工设施。购买该房产八年后，锯木厂将土地的木材部分出售给买家，同时保留加工设施所在的部分土地。合同没有具体提及先前记录的协议，但锯木厂已经开始依赖该地产的木材供应。尽管还有其他木材来源，但木材是锯木厂必需的原材料。买方拒绝允许锯木厂继续从买方购买的房产中移除木材。以下哪一项是锯木厂维持利润持续存在的最佳论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"由于锯木厂依赖该房产作为木材来源，买方不得否认利润的存在。","B":"利润是隐含存在的。","C":"利润是通过书面协议创造的。","D":"木材是锯木厂运转所必需的。"},"explanationZh":"利润（即profit à prendre）是进入他人土地并获取特定自然资源（例如石油、野味、木材——如此处所示）的非占有性权利。利润的创建和分析通常与地役权类似。因此，如果满足以下要素，则可以从现有的先前使用中隐含利润： 共同所有权的分割 - 主导（受益）和服务（负担）庄园曾经是一块土地，之后所有者保留了该土地的一部分并将其余部分转让给另一块土地\n\n先前的现有用途 – 业主使用从属财产来使主导财产受益\n\n明显且持续——通过合理的检查可以发现之前的使用情况，并持续到管道被切断为止\n\n合理必要性——继续先前的使用对于使用和享有土地而言是合理必要的\n\n在这里，锯木厂在将木材部分出售给买家之前拥有整个财产。锯木厂显然一直在不断地从土地上采伐木材，直到出售为止。由于锯木厂已经开始依赖买方土地上的木材供应来运营，因此继续清除木材是合理必要的。因此，锯木厂最好的论据是利润是隐含存在的。 （选项 A）如果利润持有者善意地合理且有害地依赖作为利润来源的土地所有者的许可（此处未示出），则可以通过禁止反言创造利润。 （选项C） 尽管原土地所有者通过书面协议给予锯木厂利润（即明示利润），但当锯木厂收购了与利润相关的财产时，该利润因合并而终止。 （选项D） 利润通常以与地役权相同的方式进行分析，但利润不能是必然创造的。此外，其他木材来源的可用性将阻止锯木厂建立必要性地役权所需的严格必要性。\n\n教育目标：利润通常像地役权一样被创造和分析，因此可以从现有的先前使用中隐含利润。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 1.2(2)，cmt。 e（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（地役权和利润的定义）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.12 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（先前使用中暗示的劳役）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"利润通常像地役权一样创建和分析，因此可以从现有的先前使用中隐含利润。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是锯木厂维持利润持续存在的最佳论据？","rule":"利润通常像地役权一样创建和分析，因此可以从现有的先前使用中隐含利润。","analysis":"利润（即profit à prendre）是进入他人土地并获取特定自然资源（例如石油、野味、木材——如此处所示）的非占有性权利。利润的创建和分析通常与地役权类似。因此，如果满足以下要素，则可以从现有的先前使用中隐含利润： 共同所有权的分割 - 主导（受益）和服务（负担）庄园曾经是一块土地，之后所有者保留了该土地的一部分并将其余部分转让给另一块土地\n\n先前的现有用途 – 业主使用从属财产来使主导财产受益\n\n明显且持续——通过合理的检查可以发现之前的使用情况，并持续到管道被切断为止\n\n合理必要性——继续先前的使用对于使用和享有土地而言是合理必要的\n\n在这里，锯木厂在将木材部分出售给买家之前拥有整个财产。锯木厂显然一直在不断地从土地上采伐木材，直到出售为止。由于锯木厂已经开始依赖买方土地上的木材供应来运营，因此继续清除木材是合理必要的。因此，锯木厂最好的论据是利润是隐含存在的。 （选项 A）如果利润持有者善意地合理且有害地依赖作为利润来源的土地所有者的许可（此处未示出），则可以通过禁止反言创造利润。 （选项C） 尽管原土地所有者通过书面协议给予锯木厂利润（即明示利润），但当锯木厂收购了与利润相关的财产时，该利润因合并而终止。 （选项D） 利润通常以与地役权相同的方式进行分析，但利润不能是必然创造的。此外，其他木材来源的可用性将阻止锯木厂建立必要性地役权所需的严格必要性。\n\n教育目标：利润通常像地役权一样被创造和分析，因此可以从现有的先前使用中隐含利润。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 1.2(2)，cmt。 e（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（地役权和利润的定义）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.12 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（先前使用中暗示的劳役）。","conclusion":"答案 B：利润是隐含存在的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-d38f475a62c952d2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":224,"sourceQuestionPage":652,"sourceQuestionPages":[652],"sourceExplanationPages":[653,654],"sourceAnswerPages":[653,654],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-225","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Easements","question":"A restauranteur's friend owned land that included a lake within its boundaries. In a writing, the friend granted the restauranteur a profit that gave her the right to access the lake for the purpose of taking fish from it. For nine years, the restauranteur took fish from the lake and served them in various dishes at her restaurant. During the tenth year, the restauranteur entered negotiations to sell the restaurant to a chef who planned to continue operating the restaurant. The terms of the profit did not address its transferability. Which of the following would be most important in determining whether the restauranteur could assign her right to remove fish from the lake to the chef as part of the sale of the restaurant?","choices":{"A":"Whether the friend and the restauranteur intended the right to be personal to the restauranteur.","B":"Whether the friend granted others the right to take fish from the lake.","C":"Whether the friend retained the right to take fish from the lake for himself.","D":"Whether the restauranteur recorded the right."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Profit\n\n(right to enter another's land & remove natural resources)\n\nCharacteristics Transferability Apportionability\n\nExclusive Unlimited & Can be transferred If transferable, can be exclusive right unless profit is divided unless contrary to personal or contrary parties' intent or burden on Nonexclusive Right limited by to parties' intent servient estate quantity, use, or unreasonably increased time OR shared with another\n\nA profit (ie, profit à prendre) is a nonpossessory right to enter another's land and remove specific natural resources (eg, oil, gas, minerals, timber, fish). A profit may generally be transferred (eg, assigned) unless the profit is personal or transfer would be contrary to the intent of the parties creating the profit. A profit is personal if the relationship of the parties or other circumstances indicate that it was not intended to be transferable. This means that the restauranteur's ability to assign her right to remove fish from the lake to the chef turns on whether the friend and restauranteur intended the profit to be personal to the restauranteur (eg, because they were friends). If so, then the restauranteur cannot assign the profit to the chef. But if the profit was intended to benefit the restauranteur's business, then the restauranteur can assign the profit to the chef purchasing her restaurant. (Choices B & C) When someone else—including the owner of the servient (burdened) estate—enjoys the same right as the profit holder, the profit is nonexclusive. The nonexclusivity of a profit may affect whether the profit holder can divide the profit among other persons, and it may be a factor in determining whether the parties intended the profit to be personal. But it is not itself determinative of whether the profit may be transferred to another. (Choice D) Recording the profit would protect the restauranteur if the profit is exclusive and the friend subsequently grants a similar right to a third party. However, this would not preclude the profit from being considered personal to the restauranteur and therefore nontransferable.\n\nEducational objective: A profit—ie, nonpossessory right to enter another's land and remove specific natural resources—may generally be transferred unless the profit is personal or transfer would be contrary to the intent of the parties creating the profit.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property (Servitudes) § 4.6 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (transferability of servitude benefits).","educationalObjective":"A profit—ie, nonpossessory right to enter another's land and remove specific natural resources—may generally be transferred unless the profit is personal or transfer would be contrary to the intent of the parties creating the profit.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property (Servitudes) § 4.6 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (transferability of servitude benefits).","rule":"A profit—ie, nonpossessory right to enter another's land and remove specific natural resources—may generally be transferred unless the profit is personal or transfer would be contrary to the intent of the parties creating the profit.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be most important in determining whether the restauranteur could assign her right to remove fish from the lake to the chef as part of the sale of the restaurant?","rule":"A profit—ie, nonpossessory right to enter another's land and remove specific natural resources—may generally be transferred unless the profit is personal or transfer would be contrary to the intent of the parties creating the profit.","analysis":"Profit\n\n(right to enter another's land & remove natural resources)\n\nCharacteristics Transferability Apportionability\n\nExclusive Unlimited & Can be transferred If transferable, can be exclusive right unless profit is divided unless contrary to personal or contrary parties' intent or burden on Nonexclusive Right limited by to parties' intent servient estate quantity, use, or unreasonably increased time OR shared with another\n\nA profit (ie, profit à prendre) is a nonpossessory right to enter another's land and remove specific natural resources (eg, oil, gas, minerals, timber, fish). A profit may generally be transferred (eg, assigned) unless the profit is personal or transfer would be contrary to the intent of the parties creating the profit. A profit is personal if the relationship of the parties or other circumstances indicate that it was not intended to be transferable. This means that the restauranteur's ability to assign her right to remove fish from the lake to the chef turns on whether the friend and restauranteur intended the profit to be personal to the restauranteur (eg, because they were friends). If so, then the restauranteur cannot assign the profit to the chef. But if the profit was intended to benefit the restauranteur's business, then the restauranteur can assign the profit to the chef purchasing her restaurant. (Choices B & C) When someone else—including the owner of the servient (burdened) estate—enjoys the same right as the profit holder, the profit is nonexclusive. The nonexclusivity of a profit may affect whether the profit holder can divide the profit among other persons, and it may be a factor in determining whether the parties intended the profit to be personal. But it is not itself determinative of whether the profit may be transferred to another. (Choice D) Recording the profit would protect the restauranteur if the profit is exclusive and the friend subsequently grants a similar right to a third party. However, this would not preclude the profit from being considered personal to the restauranteur and therefore nontransferable.","conclusion":"Answer A: Whether the friend and the restauranteur intended the right to be personal to the restauranteur."},"questionZh":"一位餐馆老板的朋友拥有一块土地，其边界内有一个湖泊。在一封书面信中，这位朋友授予餐馆老板一笔利润，使她有权进入湖中捕鱼。九年来，这位餐馆老板从湖里捞鱼，并在她的餐厅里将它们制成各种菜肴。第十年，餐厅老板开始谈判，将餐厅出售给计划继续经营餐厅的厨师。利润条款并未涉及其可转让性。在确定餐馆老板是否可以将其从湖中捕捞鱼的权利转让给厨师作为出售餐馆的一部分时，以下哪一项最重要？","choicesZh":{"A":"朋友和餐馆老板是否打算享有餐馆老板的私人权利。","B":"朋友是否授予他人从湖里打鱼的权利。","C":"这位朋友是否保留为自己从湖里捞鱼的权利。","D":"餐馆老板的记录是否正确。"},"explanationZh":"利润\n\n（进入他人土地并清除自然资源的权利）\n\n特性 可转移性 可分配性\n\n排他性 无限制且可转让 如果可转让，则可以是排他性权利，除非利润被分割，除非违反个人或相对方的意图或对非排他性权利的负担，受各方意向的受役财产数量、使用或不合理增加时间或与他人共享的限制\n\n利润（即profit à prendre）是进入他人土地并获取特定自然资源（例如石油、天然气、矿物、木材、鱼类）的非占有性权利。利润通常可以被转移（例如，分配），除非利润是个人的或者转移与创造利润的各方的意图相悖。如果各方关系或其他情况表明利润不可转让，则该利润属于个人利润。这意味着餐馆老板将她从湖中捕捞鱼的权利转让给厨师的能力取决于朋友和餐馆老板是否打算将利润归餐馆老板个人所有（例如，因为他们是朋友）。如果是这样，那么餐馆老板就不能将利润分配给厨师。但如果利润的目的是有利于餐馆老板的业务，那么餐馆老板可以将利润分配给购买其餐厅的厨师。 （选择B和C）当其他人——包括役用（负担）财产的所有者——享有与利润持有者相同的权利时，利润是非排他性的。利润的非排他性可能会影响利润持有者是否可以将利润分配给其他人，并且可能是确定当事人是否打算将利润归为个人的一个因素。但它本身并不决定利润是否可以转移给他人。 （选择 D）如果利润是排他性的，并且朋友随后向第三方授予类似的权利，记录利润将保护餐馆老板。然而，这并不妨碍利润被视为餐馆老板的个人利润，因此不可转让。\n\n教育目标：利润——即进入他人土地并获取特定自然资源的非占有性权利——通常可以转让，除非利润是个人的或转让与创造利润的各方的意图相悖。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）（劳役）§ 4.6（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（劳役福利的可转让性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"利润（即进入他人土地并获取特定自然资源的非占有性权利）通常可以转让，除非利润是个人的或转让与创造利润的各方的意图相悖。","iracZh":{"issue":"在确定餐馆老板是否可以将其从湖中捕捞鱼的权利转让给厨师作为出售餐馆的一部分时，以下哪一项最重要？","rule":"利润（即进入他人土地并获取特定自然资源的非占有性权利）通常可以转让，除非利润是个人的或转让与创造利润的各方的意图相悖。","analysis":"利润\n\n（进入他人土地并清除自然资源的权利）\n\n特性 可转移性 可分配性\n\n排他性 无限制且可转让 如果可转让，则可以是排他性权利，除非利润被分割，除非违反个人或相对方的意图或对非排他性权利的负担，受各方意向的受役财产数量、使用或不合理增加时间或与他人共享的限制\n\n利润（即profit à prendre）是进入他人土地并获取特定自然资源（例如石油、天然气、矿物、木材、鱼类）的非占有性权利。利润通常可以被转移（例如，分配），除非利润是个人的或者转移与创造利润的各方的意图相悖。如果各方关系或其他情况表明利润不可转让，则该利润属于个人利润。这意味着餐馆老板将她从湖中捕捞鱼的权利转让给厨师的能力取决于朋友和餐馆老板是否打算将利润归餐馆老板个人所有（例如，因为他们是朋友）。如果是这样，那么餐馆老板就不能将利润分配给厨师。但如果利润的目的是有利于餐馆老板的业务，那么餐馆老板可以将利润分配给购买其餐厅的厨师。 （选择B和C）当其他人——包括役用（负担）财产的所有者——享有与利润持有者相同的权利时，利润是非排他性的。利润的非排他性可能会影响利润持有者是否可以将利润分配给其他人，并且可能是确定当事人是否打算将利润归为个人的一个因素。但它本身并不决定利润是否可以转移给他人。 （选择 D）如果利润是排他性的，并且朋友随后向第三方授予类似的权利，记录利润将保护餐馆老板。然而，这并不妨碍利润被视为餐馆老板的个人利润，因此不可转让。\n\n教育目标：利润——即进入他人土地并获取特定自然资源的非占有性权利——通常可以转让，除非利润是个人的或转让与创造利润的各方的意图相悖。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）（劳役）§ 4.6（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（劳役福利的可转让性）。","conclusion":"答案 A：朋友和餐馆老板是否打算享有餐馆老板的私人权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":225,"sourceQuestionPage":655,"sourceQuestionPages":[655],"sourceExplanationPages":[656,657],"sourceAnswerPages":[656,657],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-226","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A vendor owned a tract of land in fee simple. The vendor entered into a valid written agreement with a purchaser under which the vendor agreed to sell and the purchaser agreed to buy the land by installment purchase. The contract stipulated that the vendor would deliver to the purchaser, upon the payment of the last installment due, \"a warranty deed sufficient to convey fee simple.\" The contract contained no other provision that could be construed as referring to title. The purchaser entered into possession of the land. After making 10 of the 300 installment payments obligated under the contract, the purchaser discovered that there was outstanding a valid and enforceable mortgage on the land, securing the payment of a debt in the amount of 25% of the purchase price the purchaser had agreed to pay. There was no evidence that the vendor had ever been late in payments due under the mortgage and there was no evidence of any danger of insolvency of the vendor. The value of the land now is four times the amount due on the debt secured by the mortgage. The purchaser quit possession of the land and demanded that the vendor repay the amounts the purchaser had paid under the contract. After the vendor refused the demand, the purchaser brought an appropriate action against the vendor to recover damages for the vendor's alleged breach of the contract. In such action, should damages be awarded to the purchaser?","choices":{"A":"No, because the purchaser assumed the risk by taking possession.","B":"No, because the time for the vendor to deliver marketable title has not arrived.","C":"Yes, because in the absence of a contrary express agreement, an obligation to convey marketable title is implied.","D":"Yes, because the risk of loss assumed by the purchaser in taking possession relates only to physical loss."},"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession The warranty of marketable title is implied in all land-sale contracts unless otherwise stated. This guarantees that, upon closing, the seller will convey the buyer title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. In the case of an installment land contract—ie, one in which the buyer takes possession of the property and pays the purchase price over time—the seller need not deliver marketable title until the buyer makes the final payment. Here, the purchaser made 10 of the 300 installment payments before discovering the outstanding mortgage on the land. Although an existing mortgage can render title unmarketable, the vendor does not need to deliver marketable title until the purchaser has made the final installment payment. Therefore, damages should not be awarded to the purchaser because the time for the vendor to deliver marketable title has not arrived (Choice C). (Choices A & D) Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, the risk of loss is on the buyer during the period between contract formation and closing—regardless of whether the buyer takes possession. The risk of loss assumed by the buyer typically relates to physical loss (eg, from fire), but the doctrine may also apply to legal changes (eg, zoning changes). However, this has no relevance to the seller's obligation to deliver title as promised.\n\nEducational objective: Under an installment land contract, the time for the seller to deliver marketable title arrives when the buyer makes the final installment payment.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 3.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (installment land contracts).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (marketable title).","educationalObjective":"Under an installment land contract, the time for the seller to deliver marketable title arrives when the buyer makes the final installment payment.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Mortgages § 3.4 (Am. Law Inst. 1997) (installment land contracts).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (marketable title).","rule":"Under an installment land contract, the time for the seller to deliver marketable title arrives when the buyer makes the final installment payment.","irac":{"issue":"In such action, should damages be awarded to the purchaser?","rule":"Under an installment land contract, the time for the seller to deliver marketable title arrives when the buyer makes the final installment payment.","analysis":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession The warranty of marketable title is implied in all land-sale contracts unless otherwise stated. This guarantees that, upon closing, the seller will convey the buyer title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. In the case of an installment land contract—ie, one in which the buyer takes possession of the property and pays the purchase price over time—the seller need not deliver marketable title until the buyer makes the final payment. Here, the purchaser made 10 of the 300 installment payments before discovering the outstanding mortgage on the land. Although an existing mortgage can render title unmarketable, the vendor does not need to deliver marketable title until the purchaser has made the final installment payment. Therefore, damages should not be awarded to the purchaser because the time for the vendor to deliver marketable title has not arrived (Choice C). (Choices A & D) Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, the risk of loss is on the buyer during the period between contract formation and closing—regardless of whether the buyer takes possession. The risk of loss assumed by the buyer typically relates to physical loss (eg, from fire), but the doctrine may also apply to legal changes (eg, zoning changes). However, this has no relevance to the seller's obligation to deliver title as promised.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the time for the vendor to deliver marketable title has not arrived."},"questionZh":"一个供应商以简单的费用拥有一块土地。卖方与买方订立有效的书面协议，卖方同意出售该土地，买方同意以分期付款的方式购买该土地。合同规定，在支付最后一期付款后，卖方将向买方交付“一份足以支付简单费用的保修契约”。该合同不包含任何其他可被解释为涉及所有权的条款。买方取得了该土地的所有权。在支付了合同规定的 300 笔分期付款中的 10 笔后，买方发现该土地上存在未偿还的有效且可执行的抵押贷款，从而确保支付买方同意支付的购买价格 25% 的债务。没有证据表明卖方曾逾期按揭付款，也没有证据表明卖方有任何破产的危险。现在土地的价值是抵押贷款到期债务金额的四倍。买方放弃了对土地的占有，并要求卖方偿还买方根据合同支付的款项。卖方拒绝要求后，买方对卖方提起适当诉讼，要求卖方赔偿其涉嫌违反合同的损失。在这种诉讼中，买方是否应该获得损害赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为购买者通过占有承担了风险。","B":"不，因为卖方交付适销所有权的时间尚未到来。","C":"是的，因为在没有相反的明确协议的情况下，隐含了转让适销所有权的义务。","D":"是的，因为购买者在占有时承担的损失风险仅与实物损失有关。"},"explanationZh":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租约 留置权 所有权链中的空白 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 除非另有说明，所有土地销售合同中都隐含了适销对路所有权的保证。这保证了在成交时，卖方将转让买方的所有权，该所有权是合理且毫无疑问且不受诉讼威胁的，以便理性的人会接受并付款。在分期土地合同的情况下，即买方占有财产并随着时间的推移支付购买价格的合同，卖方无需交付可出售的所有权，直到买方支付最后一笔款项。在这里，买家在发现土地上未偿还的抵押贷款之前支付了 300 笔分期付款中的 10 笔。尽管现有的抵押贷款可能会导致所有权无法出售，但在买方支付最后的分期付款之前，卖方不需要交付可出售的所有权。因此，不应判给买方损害赔偿，因为卖方交付适销所有权的时间尚未到来（选择C）。 （选项 A 和 D）根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，在合同订立和成交之间的期间，损失风险由买方承担——无论买方是否占有。买方承担的损失风险通常与物理损失（例如火灾）有关，但该原则也可能适用于法律变更（例如土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更）。然而，这与卖方按承诺交付所有权的义务无关。\n\n教育目标：根据分期付款土地合同，当买方支付最后一笔分期付款时，卖方交付可出售所有权的时间就到了。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 3.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（分期付款土地合同）。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（可销售所有权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据分期付款土地合同，当买方支付最后一笔分期付款时，卖方交付可出售所有权的时间就到了。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这种诉讼中，买方是否应该获得损害赔偿？","rule":"根据分期付款土地合同，当买方支付最后一笔分期付款时，卖方交付可出售所有权的时间就到了。","analysis":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租约 留置权 所有权链中的空白 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 除非另有说明，所有土地销售合同中都隐含了适销对路所有权的保证。这保证了在成交时，卖方将转让买方的所有权，该所有权是合理且毫无疑问且不受诉讼威胁的，以便理性的人会接受并付款。在分期土地合同的情况下，即买方占有财产并随着时间的推移支付购买价格的合同，卖方无需交付可出售的所有权，直到买方支付最后一笔款项。在这里，买家在发现土地上未偿还的抵押贷款之前支付了 300 笔分期付款中的 10 笔。尽管现有的抵押贷款可能会导致所有权无法出售，但在买方支付最后的分期付款之前，卖方不需要交付可出售的所有权。因此，不应判给买方损害赔偿，因为卖方交付适销所有权的时间尚未到来（选择C）。 （选项 A 和 D）根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，在合同订立和成交之间的期间，损失风险由买方承担——无论买方是否占有。买方承担的损失风险通常与物理损失（例如火灾）有关，但该原则也可能适用于法律变更（例如土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）变更）。然而，这与卖方按承诺交付所有权的义务无关。\n\n教育目标：根据分期付款土地合同，当买方支付最后一笔分期付款时，卖方交付可出售所有权的时间就到了。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：抵押贷款 § 3.4（Am. Law Inst. 1997）（分期付款土地合同）。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（可销售所有权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为卖方交付适销所有权的时间尚未到来。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":226,"sourceQuestionPage":658,"sourceQuestionPages":[658],"sourceExplanationPages":[659,660],"sourceAnswerPages":[659,660],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-227","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Estates & Future Interests","question":"A man owned in fee simple a lot in a properly approved subdivision, designed and zoned for industrial use. His friend owned the adjoining lot in the same subdivision. The plat of the subdivision was recorded as authorized by statute. Twelve years ago, the man erected an industrial building wholly situated on his lot but with one wall along the boundary common with the friend's lot. The construction was done as authorized by a building permit that was validly obtained under applicable statutes, ordinances, and regulations. Further, the construction was regularly inspected and found in compliance with all building code requirements. The friend's lot remained vacant until six months ago, when she began excavation pursuant to a building permit authorizing the erection of an industrial building situated on her lot but with one wall along the boundary common with the man's lot. The excavation caused subsidence of a portion of the man's lot that resulted in injury to his building. The excavation was not done negligently or with any malicious intent to injure. In the jurisdiction, the time to acquire title by adverse possession or rights by prescription is 10 years. The man has brought an appropriate action against the friend to recover damages resulting from the injuries to the building on his lot. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The friend, because both lots are urban land, as distinguished from rural land, so the man had the duty to protect any improvements on his lot.","B":"The friend, because the construction and use of her building were authorized by the applicable law.","C":"The man, because a right for support, appurtenant to his lot, had been acquired by adverse possession or prescription.","D":"The man, if the subsidence would have occurred without the weight of the building on his lot."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Withdrawal of lateral & subjacent support\n\nNatural state Improved\n\nLateral Strictly liable for damage to If damage to land & improvements would adjacent land caused by have occurred in land's natural state, strictly Subjacent subsidence liable If not, liable if negligent Landowners have a right to lateral support from all adjoining land. Therefore, an adjoining landowner who withdraws lateral support for land in a natural state is strictly liable for any subsidence (ie, cave-in, slippage) that occurs because of that withdrawal. But if the adjoining landowner withdraws lateral support for improved land—as seen with the man's industrial building—the adjoining landowner may be: strictly liable if the landowner can show that damage to the land and improvements would have occurred in the land's natural state or\n\nliable for negligence if the landowner cannot make such a showing.\n\nHere, the friend's excavation was not done negligently or with any malicious intent to injure the man. This means that the man can only recover damages resulting from the injuries to his improved land if the friend is subject to strict liability. And if the man shows that the subsidence would have occurred without the weight of the building on his lot, he will likely prevail. (Choice A) A possessor of land in an urban area has a duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent an unreasonable risk of harm arising from conditions on his/her land. Here, there is no evidence that the man (or the friend) breached this duty, so this principle would not affect the outcome of the lawsuit. (Choice B) The fact that the construction and use of the friend's building were authorized by law helps show that the friend was not negligent, but it does not shield her from strict liability since it is imposed regardless of fault. (Choice C) A landowner cannot acquire a right to lateral support by adverse possession or prescription. That is because there is no actual possession or use of the adjoining land to satisfy the elements of those claims.\n\nEducational objective: An adjoining landowner who withdraws lateral support for improved land is (1) strictly liable if the landowner can show that damage to the land and improvements would have occurred in the land's natural state or (2) liable for negligence if no such showing is made.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Torts §§ 817–18 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (withdrawal of lateral support).","educationalObjective":"An adjoining landowner who withdraws lateral support for improved land is (1) strictly liable if the landowner can show that damage to the land and improvements would have occurred in the land's natural state or (2) liable for negligence if no such showing is made.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 817–18 (Am. Law Inst. 1979) (withdrawal of lateral support).","rule":"An adjoining landowner who withdraws lateral support for improved land is (1) strictly liable if the landowner can show that damage to the land and improvements would have occurred in the land's natural state or (2) liable for negligence if no such showing is made.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"An adjoining landowner who withdraws lateral support for improved land is (1) strictly liable if the landowner can show that damage to the land and improvements would have occurred in the land's natural state or (2) liable for negligence if no such showing is made.","analysis":"Withdrawal of lateral & subjacent support\n\nNatural state Improved\n\nLateral Strictly liable for damage to If damage to land & improvements would adjacent land caused by have occurred in land's natural state, strictly Subjacent subsidence liable If not, liable if negligent Landowners have a right to lateral support from all adjoining land. Therefore, an adjoining landowner who withdraws lateral support for land in a natural state is strictly liable for any subsidence (ie, cave-in, slippage) that occurs because of that withdrawal. But if the adjoining landowner withdraws lateral support for improved land—as seen with the man's industrial building—the adjoining landowner may be: strictly liable if the landowner can show that damage to the land and improvements would have occurred in the land's natural state or\n\nliable for negligence if the landowner cannot make such a showing.\n\nHere, the friend's excavation was not done negligently or with any malicious intent to injure the man. This means that the man can only recover damages resulting from the injuries to his improved land if the friend is subject to strict liability. And if the man shows that the subsidence would have occurred without the weight of the building on his lot, he will likely prevail. (Choice A) A possessor of land in an urban area has a duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent an unreasonable risk of harm arising from conditions on his/her land. Here, there is no evidence that the man (or the friend) breached this duty, so this principle would not affect the outcome of the lawsuit. (Choice B) The fact that the construction and use of the friend's building were authorized by law helps show that the friend was not negligent, but it does not shield her from strict liability since it is imposed regardless of fault. (Choice C) A landowner cannot acquire a right to lateral support by adverse possession or prescription. That is because there is no actual possession or use of the adjoining land to satisfy the elements of those claims.","conclusion":"Answer D: The man, if the subsidence would have occurred without the weight of the building on his lot."},"questionZh":"一个人在经过适当批准的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）中拥有一套简单的土地，该土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）是为工业用途而设计和土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）的。他的朋友拥有同一土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）的相邻地块。该土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）的平面图已按法规授权记录。十二年前，该男子在自己的地块上建造了一座工业建筑，但与朋友的地块边界上有一堵墙。该施工是根据根据适用的法规、条例和法规有效获得的建筑许可证授权进行的。此外，我们还定期检查该建筑，并发现其符合所有建筑规范的要求。这位朋友的地块一直空置，直到六个月前，她根据建筑许可证开始挖掘，该许可证授权在她的地块上建造一栋工业建筑，但沿着与该男子地块共同的边界有一堵墙。挖掘工作导致该男子所在土地的一部分下沉，导致他的建筑物受损。挖掘工作并非出于疏忽或恶意伤害。在司法管辖区，通过逆权占有或通过时效取得权利的时间为10年。该男子已对朋友提起适当诉讼，要求赔偿因他所在地块上的建筑物受损而造成的损失。谁有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"这位朋友，因为这两块土地都是城市土地，与农村土地不同，所以该男子有责任保护他的土地上的任何改进。","B":"这位朋友，因为她的建筑物的建造和使用是经过适用法律授权的。","C":"这个人，因为从属于他的命运的赡养权是通过逆权占有或时效获得的。","D":"这名男子，如果没有他所在土地上的建筑物的重量就会发生沉降。"},"explanationZh":"撤回横向和下部支撑\n\n自然状态改善\n\n横向损害严格承担责任 如果在土地自然状态下发生了对邻近土地造成的损害，则严格承担下方沉降责任 如果不是，如果疏忽则承担责任 土地所有者有权从所有邻近土地获得横向支撑。因此，在自然状态下撤回土地横向支撑的相邻土地所有者对因撤回而发生的任何沉降（即塌陷、滑移）负有严格责任。但是，如果毗邻的土地所有者撤回对改良土地的横向支持（如该男子的工业建筑所示），则毗邻的土地所有者可能会： 如果土地所有者能够证明在土地的自然状态下会发生对土地的损害和改良，则必须承担严格责任，或者\n\n如果土地所有者无法出示，则应承担疏忽责任。\n\n在这里，朋友的挖掘并不是疏忽大意，也不是恶意伤害该男子。这意味着，只有在朋友承担严格责任的情况下，该男子才能获得因其改良土地受到损害而造成的损失。如果该男子证明，如果没有他所在地块上的建筑物的重量，就会发生沉降，那么他很可能会获胜。 （选择 A） 城市地区的土地所有者有义务采取合理的谨慎措施，以防止其土地条件造成不合理的损害风险。在此，没有证据表明该人（或朋友）违反了这一义务，因此该原则不会影响诉讼结果。 （选项B） 朋友的建筑物的建造和使用是经法律授权的，这一事实有助于表明朋友没有疏忽，但这并不能免除她的严格责任，因为无论过错与否，她都会承担严格责任。 （选择 C） 土地所有者不能通过逆权占有或时效获得横向支持的权利。这是因为没有实际占有或使用毗邻土地来满足这些索赔的要素。\n\n教育目标：撤回对改良土地的横向支持的相邻土地所有者 (1) 如果土地所有者能够证明在土地自然状态下会发生对土地的损害和改良，则应承担严格责任；(2) 如果没有提供此类证明，则应承担疏忽责任。\n\n参考文献\n《侵权法重述（第二）》第 §§ 817–18（Am. Law Inst. 1979）（撤销横向支持）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"撤回对改良土地的横向支持的相邻土地所有者应 (1) 如果土地所有者能够证明在土地自然状态下会发生对土地的损害和改良，则应承担严格责任；或 (2) 如果没有提供此类证明，则应承担疏忽责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"撤回对改良土地的横向支持的相邻土地所有者应 (1) 如果土地所有者能够证明在土地自然状态下会发生对土地的损害和改良，则应承担严格责任；或 (2) 如果没有提供此类证明，则应承担疏忽责任。","analysis":"撤回横向和下部支撑\n\n自然状态改善\n\n横向损害严格承担责任 如果在土地自然状态下发生了对邻近土地造成的损害，则严格承担下方沉降责任 如果不是，如果疏忽则承担责任 土地所有者有权从所有邻近土地获得横向支撑。因此，在自然状态下撤回土地横向支撑的相邻土地所有者对因撤回而发生的任何沉降（即塌陷、滑移）负有严格责任。但是，如果毗邻的土地所有者撤回对改良土地的横向支持（如该男子的工业建筑所示），则毗邻的土地所有者可能会： 如果土地所有者能够证明在土地的自然状态下会发生对土地的损害和改良，则必须承担严格责任，或者\n\n如果土地所有者无法出示，则应承担疏忽责任。\n\n在这里，朋友的挖掘并不是疏忽大意，也不是恶意伤害该男子。这意味着，只有在朋友承担严格责任的情况下，该男子才能获得因其改良土地受到损害而造成的损失。如果该男子证明，如果没有他所在地块上的建筑物的重量，就会发生沉降，那么他很可能会获胜。 （选择 A） 城市地区的土地所有者有义务采取合理的谨慎措施，以防止其土地条件造成不合理的损害风险。在此，没有证据表明该人（或朋友）违反了这一义务，因此该原则不会影响诉讼结果。 （选项B） 朋友的建筑物的建造和使用是经法律授权的，这一事实有助于表明朋友没有疏忽，但这并不能免除她的严格责任，因为无论过错与否，她都会承担严格责任。 （选择 C） 土地所有者不能通过逆权占有或时效获得横向支持的权利。这是因为没有实际占有或使用毗邻土地来满足这些索赔的要素。\n\n教育目标：撤回对改良土地的横向支持的相邻土地所有者 (1) 如果土地所有者能够证明在土地自然状态下会发生对土地的损害和改良，则应承担严格责任；(2) 如果没有提供此类证明，则应承担疏忽责任。\n\n参考文献\n《侵权法重述（第二）》第 §§ 817–18（Am. Law Inst. 1979）（撤销横向支持）。","conclusion":"答案 D：这名男子，如果没有他所在土地上的建筑物的重量就会发生沉降。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":227,"sourceQuestionPage":661,"sourceQuestionPages":[661],"sourceExplanationPages":[662,663],"sourceAnswerPages":[662,663],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-228","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"By a valid written contract executed in March, a seller agreed to sell land to a buyer. The contract stated, \"The parties agree that closing will occur on May 1 at 10 a.m.\" There was no other reference to closing. The contract was silent as to quality of title. On April 27, the seller notified the buyer that she had discovered that the land was subject to a long-standing easement in favor of a corporation for a towpath for a canal, should the corporation ever want to build a canal. The buyer thought it so unlikely that a canal would ever be built that the closing should occur notwithstanding this easement. Therefore, the buyer notified the seller on April 28 that he would expect to close on May 1. When the seller later refused to close, the buyer sued for specific performance. Will the buyer be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because rights of third parties are unresolved.","B":"No, because the easement renders the seller's title unmarketable.","C":"Yes, because the decision to terminate the contract for title not being marketable belongs only to the buyer.","D":"Yes, because the seller did not give notice of the easement a reasonable time before the closing date."},"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession All contracts for the sale of land have an implied warranty that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing unless otherwise stated. To be marketable, title must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. If the seller cannot convey marketable title at the time of closing, only the buyer can rescind (ie, terminate) the sales contract and refuse to close. Title can be rendered unmarketable by encumbrances (eg, easements, mortgages, other liens). However, buyers can waive encumbrances—as the buyer did here when he notified the seller that he expected to close notwithstanding the corporation's easement. Because the buyer chose to accept the land with the easement instead of terminating the contract, the buyer will be likely to prevail in an action for specific performance (Choice B). (Choice A) The fact that rights of third parties are unresolved (eg, whether the corporation's easement is valid) is irrelevant to the buyer's suit against the seller for specific performance of the sales contract. (Choice D) The seller was obligated to disclose the easement to the buyer promptly after learning of it, which she did. The buyer then chose to go through with the deal rather than terminate the contract, so the seller is obligated to complete the sale.\n\nEducational objective: An easement is an encumbrance that can render title unmarketable. However, the buyer can choose to waive the encumbrance and insist that the sale be completed.","educationalObjective":"An easement is an encumbrance that can render title unmarketable. However, the buyer can choose to waive the encumbrance and insist that the sale be completed.","references":"","rule":"An easement is an encumbrance that can render title unmarketable. However, the buyer can choose to waive the encumbrance and insist that the sale be completed.","irac":{"issue":"Will the buyer be likely to prevail?","rule":"An easement is an encumbrance that can render title unmarketable. However, the buyer can choose to waive the encumbrance and insist that the sale be completed.","analysis":"\"Red flags\" for marketable title\n\nCovenants Easements Leases Liens Gaps in chain of title Boundary disputes Existing zoning violations Adverse possession All contracts for the sale of land have an implied warranty that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing unless otherwise stated. To be marketable, title must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. If the seller cannot convey marketable title at the time of closing, only the buyer can rescind (ie, terminate) the sales contract and refuse to close. Title can be rendered unmarketable by encumbrances (eg, easements, mortgages, other liens). However, buyers can waive encumbrances—as the buyer did here when he notified the seller that he expected to close notwithstanding the corporation's easement. Because the buyer chose to accept the land with the easement instead of terminating the contract, the buyer will be likely to prevail in an action for specific performance (Choice B). (Choice A) The fact that rights of third parties are unresolved (eg, whether the corporation's easement is valid) is irrelevant to the buyer's suit against the seller for specific performance of the sales contract. (Choice D) The seller was obligated to disclose the easement to the buyer promptly after learning of it, which she did. The buyer then chose to go through with the deal rather than terminate the contract, so the seller is obligated to complete the sale.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the decision to terminate the contract for title not being marketable belongs only to the buyer."},"questionZh":"通过三月份签署的有效书面合同，卖方同意将土地出售给买方。合同中写道：“双方同意交割将于 5 月 1 日上午 10 点进行。”没有其他提及关闭的内容。合同没有提及所有权的性质。 4 月 27 日，卖方通知买方，她发现这块土地享有长期地役权，如果某公司想要修建运河，则可以为该公司修建一条运河拖道。买方认为修建运河的可能性极低，因此尽管有这项地役权，仍应完成交割。因此，买方于4月28日通知卖方，预计于5月1日成交。当卖方后来拒绝成交时，买方提起诉讼，要求具体履行。买方可能会获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为第三方的权利尚未解决。","B":"不可以，因为地役权使卖方的所有权无法出售。","C":"是的，因为因所有权无法流通而终止合同的决定仅属于买方。","D":"是的，因为卖方没有在交割日期之前的合理时间内发出地役权通知。"},"explanationZh":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租赁 留置权 所有权链中的缺口 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 所有土地销售合同均包含默示保证，除非另有说明，否则卖方将在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。为了可销售，所有权必须合理地没有疑问，并且不受诉讼威胁，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。如果卖方在成交时无法转让适销所有权，则只有买方可以解除（即终止）销售合同并拒绝成交。产权负担（例如地役权、抵押贷款、其他留置权）可能会导致产权无法出售。然而，买方可以放弃产权负担——就像买方在通知卖方尽管公司有地役权但仍希望关闭时所做的那样。由于买方选择接受带有地役权的土地而不是终止合同，因此买方很可能在具体履约诉讼中获胜（选择 B）。 （选项A）第三方权利未解决（例如公司地役权是否有效）这一事实与买方因具体履行销售合同而向卖方提起诉讼无关。 （选项D） 卖方有义务在得知地役权后立即向买方披露，买方也这样做了。然后买方选择继续交易而不是终止合同，因此卖方有义务完成销售。\n\n教育目标：地役权是一种负担，可能导致产权无法出售。然而，买方可以选择放弃产权负担并坚持完成销售。","educationalObjectiveZh":"地役权是一种负担，可能导致产权无法出售。然而，买方可以选择放弃产权负担并坚持完成销售。","iracZh":{"issue":"买方可能会获胜吗？","rule":"地役权是一种负担，可能导致产权无法出售。然而，买方可以选择放弃产权负担并坚持完成销售。","analysis":"畅销书名的“危险信号”\n\n契约 地役权 租赁 留置权 所有权链中的缺口 边界纠纷 现有的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）违规 逆权占有 所有土地销售合同均包含默示保证，除非另有说明，否则卖方将在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。为了可销售，所有权必须合理地没有疑问，并且不受诉讼威胁，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。如果卖方在成交时无法转让适销所有权，则只有买方可以解除（即终止）销售合同并拒绝成交。产权负担（例如地役权、抵押贷款、其他留置权）可能会导致产权无法出售。然而，买方可以放弃产权负担——就像买方在通知卖方尽管公司有地役权但仍希望关闭时所做的那样。由于买方选择接受带有地役权的土地而不是终止合同，因此买方很可能在具体履约诉讼中获胜（选择 B）。 （选项A）第三方权利未解决（例如公司地役权是否有效）这一事实与买方因具体履行销售合同而向卖方提起诉讼无关。 （选项D） 卖方有义务在得知地役权后立即向买方披露，买方也这样做了。然后买方选择继续交易而不是终止合同，因此卖方有义务完成销售。\n\n教育目标：地役权是一种负担，可能导致产权无法出售。然而，买方可以选择放弃产权负担并坚持完成销售。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为因所有权无法流通而终止合同的决定仅属于买方。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":228,"sourceQuestionPage":664,"sourceQuestionPages":[664],"sourceExplanationPages":[665],"sourceAnswerPages":[665],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-229","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"An individual owned 20 acres of undeveloped land. She gave her son a 12-acre portion of the land. Because the remaining eight-acre parcel lacked any access to a public road, the deed, which was properly recorded, provided that she retained the right to construct a road across the larger parcel to access the public highway that ran adjacent to the larger parcel. Although she lived for another 15 years, neither she nor her son took any action with regard to their parcels, nor was there any change in the surrounding area. When she died, her son inherited all of her property. Subsequently, the son sold the larger parcel to a buyer and gave the smaller parcel to his daughter. The daughter, intending to build a house on her parcel, has approached the buyer about building an access road across the buyer's property to the house. The buyer, asserting that the daughter has no right to do so, has refused to discuss the matter with her. Does the daughter have an easement across the buyer's land to access her land?","choices":{"A":"No, because the individual could not reserve an easement for her own benefit.","B":"No, because the merger of the two parcels through ownership terminated the individual's easement.","C":"Yes, because an express easement is not terminated by nonuse.","D":"Yes, because there is an easement by necessity."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The individual gave her son a portion of her land and properly reserved an easement that allowed her to construct a road across his land to access a public highway (Choice A). That easement terminated when the dominant estate (individual's 8-acre parcel) and servient estate (son's 12-acre parcel) merged into the hands of a single owner after the son inherited all of the individual's property. And that easement was not revived when the son later sold the 12-acre parcel to a buyer and gave the 8-acre parcel to his daughter. However, an easement by necessity will be implied if all of the following elements are met: Necessity – the dominant (benefited) estate is virtually useless without an easement across the servient (burdened) estate\n\nCommon ownership – the dominant and servient estates were under common ownership in the past (ie, they were once a single tract of land)\n\nSeverance – the necessity arose when the land was severed and the dominant and servient estates were created\n\nHere, the daughter's 8-acre parcel is virtually useless without an easement over the 12-acre parcel because the 8-acre parcel is landlocked and has no access to a public road (necessity). The son previously owned both parcels (common ownership), and the 8-acre parcel was landlocked when he sold the 12-acre parcel to the buyer (severance). As a result, the daughter has an easement by necessity across the buyer's land to access the public highway (Choice B).* *That easement will continue until the necessity ends (eg, when an alternative means of access becomes available). (Choice C) An express easement is not terminated by mere nonuse, so the individual's easement was not terminated by her failure to create an access road across the 12-acre parcel. Instead, her easement was terminated by merger. Nevertheless, the daughter has an easement by necessity over the 12-acre parcel to reach the public highway.\n\nEducational objective: An easement by necessity is created when (1) the dominant estate is virtually useless (eg, landlocked) without the benefit of an easement across the servient estate, (2) the two estates were once a single tract of land, and (3) the necessity arose when the land was severed and the two estates were created.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by necessity).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination of easement by merger).","educationalObjective":"An easement by necessity is created when (1) the dominant estate is virtually useless (eg, landlocked) without the benefit of an easement across the servient estate, (2) the two estates were once a single tract of land, and (3) the necessity arose when the land was severed and the two estates were created.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (easement by necessity).\n\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination of easement by merger).","rule":"An easement by necessity is created when (1) the dominant estate is virtually useless (eg, landlocked) without the benefit of an easement across the servient estate, (2) the two estates were once a single tract of land, and (3) the necessity arose when the land was severed and the two estates were created.","irac":{"issue":"Does the daughter have an easement across the buyer's land to access her land?","rule":"An easement by necessity is created when (1) the dominant estate is virtually useless (eg, landlocked) without the benefit of an easement across the servient estate, (2) the two estates were once a single tract of land, and (3) the necessity arose when the land was severed and the two estates were created.","analysis":"The individual gave her son a portion of her land and properly reserved an easement that allowed her to construct a road across his land to access a public highway (Choice A). That easement terminated when the dominant estate (individual's 8-acre parcel) and servient estate (son's 12-acre parcel) merged into the hands of a single owner after the son inherited all of the individual's property. And that easement was not revived when the son later sold the 12-acre parcel to a buyer and gave the 8-acre parcel to his daughter. However, an easement by necessity will be implied if all of the following elements are met: Necessity – the dominant (benefited) estate is virtually useless without an easement across the servient (burdened) estate\n\nCommon ownership – the dominant and servient estates were under common ownership in the past (ie, they were once a single tract of land)\n\nSeverance – the necessity arose when the land was severed and the dominant and servient estates were created\n\nHere, the daughter's 8-acre parcel is virtually useless without an easement over the 12-acre parcel because the 8-acre parcel is landlocked and has no access to a public road (necessity). The son previously owned both parcels (common ownership), and the 8-acre parcel was landlocked when he sold the 12-acre parcel to the buyer (severance). As a result, the daughter has an easement by necessity across the buyer's land to access the public highway (Choice B).* *That easement will continue until the necessity ends (eg, when an alternative means of access becomes available). (Choice C) An express easement is not terminated by mere nonuse, so the individual's easement was not terminated by her failure to create an access road across the 12-acre parcel. Instead, her easement was terminated by merger. Nevertheless, the daughter has an easement by necessity over the 12-acre parcel to reach the public highway.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because there is an easement by necessity."},"questionZh":"一个人拥有20英亩未开发土地。她给了儿子一块 12 英亩的土地。由于剩余的八英亩地块无法通往公共道路，该契约已正确记录，前提是她保留在较大地块上修建道路的权利，以进入与较大地块相邻的公共高速公路。虽然她又活了15年，但她和儿子都没有对自己的包裹采取任何行动，周围也没有任何变化。她去世后，她的儿子继承了她的全部财产。随后，儿子将较大的地块卖给了一位买家，并将较小的地块交给了女儿。女儿打算在她的地块上建造一座房子，她已与买家接洽，希望修建一条穿过买家房产通往房子的道路。买家声称女儿无权这样做，拒绝与她讨论此事。女儿是否拥有跨越买方土地的地役权来使用她的土地？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为个人不能为自己的利益保留地役权。","B":"不可以，因为两块土地通过所有权合并终止了个人的地役权。","C":"是的，因为明示地役权不会因不使用而终止。","D":"是的，因为有必要存在地役权。"},"explanationZh":"该人给了她儿子一部分土地，并适当保留了地役权，允许她在他的土地上修建一条道路，通往公共高速公路（选项 A）。当儿子继承了个人的所有财产后，主导地产（个人的 8 英亩地块）和从属地产（儿子的 12 英亩地块）合并到单一所有者手中时，地役权终止。当儿子后来将 12 英亩地块卖给买家并将 8 英亩地块交给女儿时，地役权并没有恢复。然而，如果满足以下所有要素，则意味着必然存在地役权： 必要性 – 如果没有对从属（负担）财产的地役权，则占主导地位（受益）的财产实际上毫无用处\n\n共同所有权——过去，主导地产和从属地产属于共同所有权（即它们曾经是一块土地）\n\n分割——当土地被分割并创建主导和从属庄园时，就出现了必要性\n\n在这里，如果没有 12 英亩土地的地役权，女儿的 8 英亩土地实际上毫无用处，因为这 8 英亩土地位于内陆，无法通往公共道路（必需）。儿子之前拥有这两块地块（共同所有权），当他将 12 英亩地块卖给买家（遣散费）时，8 英亩地块已被内陆地区锁定。因此，女儿必须拥有跨越买方土地的地役权，以进入公共高速公路（选择 B）。* *该地役权将持续到这种必要性结束为止（例如，当有替代的进入方式可用时）。 （选项 C） 快速地役权不会因仅仅不使用而终止，因此该个人的地役权不会因她未能在 12 英亩地块上修建一条通道而终止。相反，她的地役权因合并而终止。尽管如此，女儿必须拥有这块 12 英亩地块的地役权，才能通往高速公路。\n\n教育目标：当以下情况时，必然会产生地役权：(1) 如果没有从属地产的地役权的好处，主导地产实际上毫无用处（例如，内陆）；(2) 两个地产曾经是一块土地；(3) 当土地被分割并创建两个地产时，就产生了必要性。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（必要时的地役权）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.5（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（通过合并终止地役权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"必要性地役权是在以下情况下创建的：(1) 支配性地产实际上毫无用处（例如，内陆），而没有从属地产的地役权的好处；(2) 两个地产曾经是一块土地；(3) 当土地被分割并创建两个地产时，就产生了必要性。","iracZh":{"issue":"女儿是否拥有跨越买方土地的地役权来使用她的土地？","rule":"必要性地役权是在以下情况下创建的：(1) 支配性地产实际上毫无用处（例如，内陆），而没有从属地产的地役权的好处；(2) 两个地产曾经是一块土地；(3) 当土地被分割并创建两个地产时，就产生了必要性。","analysis":"该人给了她儿子一部分土地，并适当保留了地役权，允许她在他的土地上修建一条道路，通往公共高速公路（选项 A）。当儿子继承了个人的所有财产后，主导地产（个人的 8 英亩地块）和从属地产（儿子的 12 英亩地块）合并到单一所有者手中时，地役权终止。当儿子后来将 12 英亩地块卖给买家并将 8 英亩地块交给女儿时，地役权并没有恢复。然而，如果满足以下所有要素，则意味着必然存在地役权： 必要性 – 如果没有对从属（负担）财产的地役权，则占主导地位（受益）的财产实际上毫无用处\n\n共同所有权——过去，主导地产和从属地产属于共同所有权（即它们曾经是一块土地）\n\n分割——当土地被分割并创建主导和从属庄园时，就出现了必要性\n\n在这里，如果没有 12 英亩土地的地役权，女儿的 8 英亩土地实际上毫无用处，因为这 8 英亩土地位于内陆，无法通往公共道路（必需）。儿子之前拥有这两块地块（共同所有权），当他将 12 英亩地块卖给买家（遣散费）时，8 英亩地块已被内陆地区锁定。因此，女儿必须拥有跨越买方土地的地役权，以进入公共高速公路（选择 B）。* *该地役权将持续到这种必要性结束为止（例如，当有替代的进入方式可用时）。 （选项 C） 快速地役权不会因仅仅不使用而终止，因此该个人的地役权不会因她未能在 12 英亩地块上修建一条通道而终止。相反，她的地役权因合并而终止。尽管如此，女儿必须拥有这块 12 英亩地块的地役权，才能通往高速公路。\n\n教育目标：当以下情况时，必然会产生地役权：(1) 如果没有从属地产的地役权的好处，主导地产实际上毫无用处（例如，内陆）；(2) 两个地产曾经是一块土地；(3) 当土地被分割并创建两个地产时，就产生了必要性。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.15 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（必要时的地役权）。\n\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.5（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（通过合并终止地役权）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为有必要存在地役权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-88381d8cea669e52"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":229,"sourceQuestionPage":666,"sourceQuestionPages":[666],"sourceExplanationPages":[667,668],"sourceAnswerPages":[667,668],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-230","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A condominium consists of 25 units located within a single building. In order to protect aging electrical and plumbing systems located in the common areas within the building, the board of the condominium association has adopted a reasonable rule that prohibits an owner of a condominium unit from installing or replacing a washer or dryer in the unit. The declaration that created the condominium is silent with regard to the matter. An owner wants to replace an existing combination washer/dryer appliance in her condominium unit. Can the association enforce this rule against the owner?","choices":{"A":"No, because the declaration that created the condominium is silent with regard to the installation of a washer or dryer within a condominium unit.","B":"No, because the rule seeks to dictate what the owner can do within her own condominium unit.","C":"Yes, because the board adopted the rule to protect the building's electrical and plumbing systems.","D":"Yes, because the board has the power to adopt rules restricting the use of individually owned property within the condominium."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Main types of common-interest communities\n\nProperty owners' Property owners must, as consequence of property ownership, association belong to association & pay dues to it or Property owners in real-estate development must pay for use/maintenance of property held in common by individual owners, but association membership is optional Condominium Created by state statutes by filing declaration (or master deed) & plat with recording office in condominium's locality Declaration & bylaws set out rights & obligations of unit owners & fix shares for allocating expenses, taxes, voting rights Each owner is responsible for his/her own mortgage Cooperative Typically consists of land & one or more buildings owned by corporation that leases individual residential units to shareholders Resident is tenant who also owns one or more shares of stock in corporation & is prohibited from transferring interest separately from lease Cooperative has blanket mortgage A condominium is a common-interest community—ie, a real-estate development in which individually owned lots or units are burdened by a covenant that imposes an obligation to pay dues to an association. These communities, typically acting through a board, have the power reasonably necessary to manage common property and carry out functions set forth in the declaration or granted by statute. This includes the following powers: To levy assessments against individually owned property and charge reasonable fees for services or use of common property\n\nTo manage and protect common property, including making substantial alterations, improvements, and additions to such property\n\nTo adopt reasonable rules to govern the use of common and individually owned property to protect the common property (this is an implied power)\n\nHere, the board of the association adopted a reasonable rule prohibiting the installation or replacement of a washer or dryer within a condominium unit. The rule seeks to protect the electrical and plumbing systems located within the building's common areas. Since the association has the implied power to adopt such rules, it can enforce this rule against the owner of the condominium unit.\n\n(Choice A) A condominium association has the implied power to adopt reasonable rules governing the use of individually owned property if the rule seeks to protect common property. This is true unless that power is expressly limited by the declaration or a statute. Therefore, the declaration's silence regarding washer/dryer installations will not prevent the association from enforcing this rule. (Choice B) The owner of a condominium unit generally has the freedom to use the unit as he/she sees fit. However, that use is subject to reasonable restrictions (as seen here). (Choice D) The board has the implied power to adopt this rule to protect the common property, but it does not have the broader power to adopt any rule restricting the use of individually owned property within the condominium.\n\nEducational objective: Common-interest communities (eg, condominiums) have the implied power to adopt reasonable rules to govern the use of common and individually owned property to protect common property in the community.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes §§ 6.4–6.7 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (setting forth the powers of common-interest communities).","educationalObjective":"Common-interest communities (eg, condominiums) have the implied power to adopt reasonable rules to govern the use of common and individually owned property to protect common property in the community.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes §§ 6.4–6.7 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (setting forth the powers of common-interest communities).","rule":"Common-interest communities (eg, condominiums) have the implied power to adopt reasonable rules to govern the use of common and individually owned property to protect common property in the community.","irac":{"issue":"Can the association enforce this rule against the owner?","rule":"Common-interest communities (eg, condominiums) have the implied power to adopt reasonable rules to govern the use of common and individually owned property to protect common property in the community.","analysis":"Main types of common-interest communities\n\nProperty owners' Property owners must, as consequence of property ownership, association belong to association & pay dues to it or Property owners in real-estate development must pay for use/maintenance of property held in common by individual owners, but association membership is optional Condominium Created by state statutes by filing declaration (or master deed) & plat with recording office in condominium's locality Declaration & bylaws set out rights & obligations of unit owners & fix shares for allocating expenses, taxes, voting rights Each owner is responsible for his/her own mortgage Cooperative Typically consists of land & one or more buildings owned by corporation that leases individual residential units to shareholders Resident is tenant who also owns one or more shares of stock in corporation & is prohibited from transferring interest separately from lease Cooperative has blanket mortgage A condominium is a common-interest community—ie, a real-estate development in which individually owned lots or units are burdened by a covenant that imposes an obligation to pay dues to an association. These communities, typically acting through a board, have the power reasonably necessary to manage common property and carry out functions set forth in the declaration or granted by statute. This includes the following powers: To levy assessments against individually owned property and charge reasonable fees for services or use of common property\n\nTo manage and protect common property, including making substantial alterations, improvements, and additions to such property\n\nTo adopt reasonable rules to govern the use of common and individually owned property to protect the common property (this is an implied power)\n\nHere, the board of the association adopted a reasonable rule prohibiting the installation or replacement of a washer or dryer within a condominium unit. The rule seeks to protect the electrical and plumbing systems located within the building's common areas. Since the association has the implied power to adopt such rules, it can enforce this rule against the owner of the condominium unit.\n\n(Choice A) A condominium association has the implied power to adopt reasonable rules governing the use of individually owned property if the rule seeks to protect common property. This is true unless that power is expressly limited by the declaration or a statute. Therefore, the declaration's silence regarding washer/dryer installations will not prevent the association from enforcing this rule. (Choice B) The owner of a condominium unit generally has the freedom to use the unit as he/she sees fit. However, that use is subject to reasonable restrictions (as seen here). (Choice D) The board has the implied power to adopt this rule to protect the common property, but it does not have the broader power to adopt any rule restricting the use of individually owned property within the condominium.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the board adopted the rule to protect the building's electrical and plumbing systems."},"questionZh":"公寓由位于一栋大楼内的 25 个单元组成。为了保护建筑物内公共区域老化的电气和管道系统，公寓协会董事会采取了一项合理的规则，禁止公寓单元的业主在单元内安装或更换洗衣机或烘干机。创建该公寓的声明对此事只字不提。业主想要更换其公寓单元中现有的洗衣机/烘干机组合设备。协会可以对业主强制执行这条规则吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为创建公寓的声明没有提及在公寓单元内安装洗衣机或烘干机。","B":"不可以，因为该规则旨在规定业主在自己的公寓单元内可以做什么。","C":"是的，因为董事会采用了保护建筑物电气和管道系统的规则。","D":"是的，因为董事会有权制定限制在公寓内使用个人所有财产的规则。"},"explanationZh":"共同利益共同体的主要类型\n\n业主的 业主必须，由于财产所有权，协会属于协会并缴纳会费，或者 房地产开发中的业主必须支付个人业主共同持有的财产的使用/维护费用，但协会会员资格是可选的 公寓 由州法规通过向公寓所在地的记录办公室提交声明（或主契约）和平台而创建 声明和附则规定了单位所有者的权利和义务，并固定份额以分配费用，税收，投票权 每个业主都是负责自己的抵押贷款 合作社 通常由向股东出租个人住宅单元的公司拥有的土地和一栋或多栋建筑物组成 居民是同时拥有一股或多股公司股票的租户，并且禁止与租赁分开转让利息 合作社拥有一揽子抵押贷款 公寓是一个共同利益社区，即个人拥有的土地或单位受一项契约负担的房地产开发项目，该契约规定有义务向协会支付会费。这些社区通常通过董事会行事，拥有合理必要的权力来管理共同财产并履行声明中规定或法规授予的职能。这包括以下权力： 对个人拥有的财产征收评估费用，并对服务或公共财产的使用收取合理的费用\n\n管理和保护共同财产，包括对此类财产进行重大改造、改进和增建\n\n采用合理的规则来管理共同财产和个人财产的使用，以保护共同财产（这是一种默示的权力）\n\n在这里，协会董事会通过了一项合理的规则，禁止在公寓单元内安装或更换洗衣机或烘干机。该规则旨在保护建筑物公共区域内的电气和管道系统。由于协会拥有采用此类规则的默示权力，因此它可以针对公寓单元的所有者强制执行此规则。\n\n（选项 A）如果规则旨在保护共同财产，则公寓协会拥有默示权力采用合理的规则来管理个人拥有的财产的使用。除非该权力受到声明或法规的明确限制，否则情况确实如此。因此，声明对洗衣机/烘干机安装的沉默不会妨碍协会执行这一规则。 （选择 B） 公寓单位的业主通常可以自由地使用他/她认为合适的单位。但是，该使用受到合理的限制（如此处所示）。 （选项 D） 董事会拥有采用此规则来保护共同财产的默示权力，但它没有更广泛的权力来采用任何限制在公寓内使用个人拥有财产的规则。\n\n教育目标：共同利益社区（例如，公寓）具有隐含的权力，可以采用合理的规则来管理共同和个人拥有的财产的使用，以保护社区的共同财产。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三）：劳役 §§ 6.4–6.7（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（规定共同利益共同体的权力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"共同利益社区（例如，公寓）具有隐含的权力，可以采用合理的规则来管理共同和个人拥有的财产的使用，以保护社区的共同财产。","iracZh":{"issue":"协会可以对业主强制执行这条规则吗？","rule":"共同利益社区（例如，公寓）具有隐含的权力，可以采用合理的规则来管理共同和个人拥有的财产的使用，以保护社区的共同财产。","analysis":"共同利益共同体的主要类型\n\n业主的 业主必须，由于财产所有权，协会属于协会并缴纳会费，或者 房地产开发中的业主必须支付个人业主共同持有的财产的使用/维护费用，但协会会员资格是可选的 公寓 由州法规通过向公寓所在地的记录办公室提交声明（或主契约）和平台而创建 声明和附则规定了单位所有者的权利和义务，并固定份额以分配费用，税收，投票权 每个业主都是负责自己的抵押贷款 合作社 通常由向股东出租个人住宅单元的公司拥有的土地和一栋或多栋建筑物组成 居民是同时拥有一股或多股公司股票的租户，并且禁止与租赁分开转让利息 合作社拥有一揽子抵押贷款 公寓是一个共同利益社区，即个人拥有的土地或单位受一项契约负担的房地产开发项目，该契约规定有义务向协会支付会费。这些社区通常通过董事会行事，拥有合理必要的权力来管理共同财产并履行声明中规定或法规授予的职能。这包括以下权力： 对个人拥有的财产征收评估费用，并对服务或公共财产的使用收取合理的费用\n\n管理和保护共同财产，包括对此类财产进行重大改造、改进和增建\n\n采用合理的规则来管理共同财产和个人财产的使用，以保护共同财产（这是一种默示的权力）\n\n在这里，协会董事会通过了一项合理的规则，禁止在公寓单元内安装或更换洗衣机或烘干机。该规则旨在保护建筑物公共区域内的电气和管道系统。由于协会拥有采用此类规则的默示权力，因此它可以针对公寓单元的所有者强制执行此规则。\n\n（选项 A）如果规则旨在保护共同财产，则公寓协会拥有默示权力采用合理的规则来管理个人拥有的财产的使用。除非该权力受到声明或法规的明确限制，否则情况确实如此。因此，声明对洗衣机/烘干机安装的沉默不会妨碍协会执行这一规则。 （选择 B） 公寓单位的业主通常可以自由地使用他/她认为合适的单位。但是，该使用受到合理的限制（如此处所示）。 （选项 D） 董事会拥有采用此规则来保护共同财产的默示权力，但它没有更广泛的权力来采用任何限制在公寓内使用个人拥有财产的规则。\n\n教育目标：共同利益社区（例如，公寓）具有隐含的权力，可以采用合理的规则来管理共同和个人拥有的财产的使用，以保护社区的共同财产。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三）：劳役 §§ 6.4–6.7（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（规定共同利益共同体的权力）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为董事会采用了保护建筑物电气和管道系统的规则。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":230,"sourceQuestionPage":669,"sourceQuestionPages":[669],"sourceExplanationPages":[670,671],"sourceAnswerPages":[670,671],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-231","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man owned land along the south side of a highway. To the south of the man's land was a lot owned by a woman who lived on it in a house. Five years ago, the man conveyed a right-of-way easement over his land to the woman because it provided a more direct route between her house and the highway. The easement was evidenced by a clearly marked path. The document granting the easement was promptly recorded. Three years ago, the woman conveyed the house to the man. The man never took actual possession of the house. Two years ago, the man conveyed the house to a purchaser by a warranty deed. Two months after the purchaser moved into the house, a neighbor informed him about the easement. He then began using the path that had been marked on the man's land. When the man noticed the purchaser using the path, he erected a barricade on his land that effectively prevented the purchaser from using the path. The purchaser has sued the man, claiming that he has an easement over the man's land. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The man, because the easement was extinguished.","B":"The man, because the purchaser did not have actual notice of the easement at the time of acquisition.","C":"The purchaser, because he purchased the house by warranty deed.","D":"The purchaser, because the easement was of public record when he acquired the house."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Ways to terminate easements\n\nDestruction – servient estate condemned or destroyed by natural forces Abandonment - dominant estate abandons easement Merger - dominant & servient estates united in common ownership Prescription - servient owner prevents use for prescriptive period Estoppel - dominant owner estopped from asserting easement Release - express release in writing satisfying statute of frauds Mnemonic: DAMPER\n\nAn easement is extinguished by merger when the dominant estate (ie, land benefited by the easement) and the servient estate (ie, land burdened by the easement) are united in common ownership. Once extinguished, the easement is not revived simply because the land is re-severed into two parcels. Instead, a new easement must be created. Here, the man conveyed an easement to the woman that allowed her to cross his land to access the highway. The easement was extinguished when the dominant estate (woman's lot) and servient estate (man's land) merged into the hands of one owner after the woman conveyed her house to the man. And it was not revived when the man re-severed the land by conveying the house to the purchaser—regardless of the purchaser's actual or record notice of the prior easement (Choices B & D). Therefore, the purchaser has no easement over the man's land. (Choice C) The fact that the purchaser acquired the house by warranty deed has no impact on whether the prior easement terminated (or was then revived). But if the man had purported to convey an easement over his land to the purchaser, then the purchaser could have sued the man for breach of warranties under the deed.\n\nEducational objective: Under the merger doctrine, an easement is terminated if the dominant and servient estates are united in common ownership. The terminated easement is not revived by re-severing the land into two parcels.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination by merger).","educationalObjective":"Under the merger doctrine, an easement is terminated if the dominant and servient estates are united in common ownership. The terminated easement is not revived by re-severing the land into two parcels.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination by merger).","rule":"Under the merger doctrine, an easement is terminated if the dominant and servient estates are united in common ownership. The terminated easement is not revived by re-severing the land into two parcels.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"Under the merger doctrine, an easement is terminated if the dominant and servient estates are united in common ownership. The terminated easement is not revived by re-severing the land into two parcels.","analysis":"Ways to terminate easements\n\nDestruction – servient estate condemned or destroyed by natural forces Abandonment - dominant estate abandons easement Merger - dominant & servient estates united in common ownership Prescription - servient owner prevents use for prescriptive period Estoppel - dominant owner estopped from asserting easement Release - express release in writing satisfying statute of frauds Mnemonic: DAMPER\n\nAn easement is extinguished by merger when the dominant estate (ie, land benefited by the easement) and the servient estate (ie, land burdened by the easement) are united in common ownership. Once extinguished, the easement is not revived simply because the land is re-severed into two parcels. Instead, a new easement must be created. Here, the man conveyed an easement to the woman that allowed her to cross his land to access the highway. The easement was extinguished when the dominant estate (woman's lot) and servient estate (man's land) merged into the hands of one owner after the woman conveyed her house to the man. And it was not revived when the man re-severed the land by conveying the house to the purchaser—regardless of the purchaser's actual or record notice of the prior easement (Choices B & D). Therefore, the purchaser has no easement over the man's land. (Choice C) The fact that the purchaser acquired the house by warranty deed has no impact on whether the prior easement terminated (or was then revived). But if the man had purported to convey an easement over his land to the purchaser, then the purchaser could have sued the man for breach of warranties under the deed.","conclusion":"Answer A: The man, because the easement was extinguished."},"questionZh":"一名男子拥有高速公路南侧的土地。男人土地的南边有一块土地，属于一位女人，她住在这块土地上的一所房子里。五年前，该男子将其土地的通行权转让给该女子，因为这为她的房子和高速公路之间提供了一条更直接的路线。一条清晰标记的道路证明了地役权。授予地役权的文件立即被记录下来。三年前，女方将房子转让给男方。该男子从未真正拥有这所房子。两年前，该男子通过保修契约将房子转让给买家。买家搬进房子两个月后，邻居告诉他地役权的事。然后他开始走那人土地上标记的道路。当该男子注意到购买者使用该道路时，他在自己的土地上设置了路障，有效地阻止了购买者使用该道路。买家已起诉该男子，声称他拥有该男子土地的地役权。谁有可能获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"那个男人，因为地役权被消灭了。","B":"该男子，因为购买者在购买时并未实际收到地役权通知。","C":"购买者，因为他通过保修契约购买了房屋。","D":"购买者，因为当他购买房屋时，地役权是公开记录的。"},"explanationZh":"终止地役权的方法\n\n毁坏 - 被自然力量谴责或破坏的受役财产 遗弃 - 主导财产放弃地役权 合并 - 主导财产和受役财产合并为共同所有权 时效 - 受役所有者在规定期限内禁止使用 禁止反言 - 主导所有者禁止主张地役权 释放 - 以书面形式明确释放，满足欺诈法规要求 助记符：DAMPER\n\n当主要财产（即受益于地役权的土地）和从属财产（即受地役权负担的土地）合并为共同所有权时，地役权将因合并而消失。一旦消失，地役权不会仅仅因为土地被重新分成两块而恢复。相反，必须创建新的地役权。在这里，该男子向该女子转让了一份地役权，允许她穿过他的土地进入高速公路。当女性将房屋转让给男性后，主导地产（女性的土地）和从属地产（男性的土地）合并到一个所有者手中时，地役权就消失了。当该男子通过将房屋转让给购买者重新分割土地时，无论购买者实际或记录的先前地役权通知如何（Choices B＆D），它都没有恢复。因此，购买者对该人的土地没有地役权。 （选项C）购买者通过保修契约获得房屋的事实对于先前的地役权是否终止（或随后恢复）没有影响。但如果该男子声称将其土地的地役权转让给买方，那么买方可以起诉该男子违反契约项下的保证。\n\n教育目标：根据合并原则，如果支配性财产和受役性财产统一为共同所有权，则地役权终止。终止的地役权不会通过将土地重新分成两块而恢复。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过合并终止）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据合并原则，如果支配性财产和受役性财产统一为共同所有权，则地役权终止。终止的地役权不会通过将土地重新分成两块而恢复。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"根据合并原则，如果支配性财产和受役性财产统一为共同所有权，则地役权终止。终止的地役权不会通过将土地重新分成两块而恢复。","analysis":"终止地役权的方法\n\n毁坏 - 被自然力量谴责或破坏的受役财产 遗弃 - 主导财产放弃地役权 合并 - 主导财产和受役财产合并为共同所有权 时效 - 受役所有者在规定期限内禁止使用 禁止反言 - 主导所有者禁止主张地役权 释放 - 以书面形式明确释放，满足欺诈法规要求 助记符：DAMPER\n\n当主要财产（即受益于地役权的土地）和从属财产（即受地役权负担的土地）合并为共同所有权时，地役权将因合并而消失。一旦消失，地役权不会仅仅因为土地被重新分成两块而恢复。相反，必须创建新的地役权。在这里，该男子向该女子转让了一份地役权，允许她穿过他的土地进入高速公路。当女性将房屋转让给男性后，主导地产（女性的土地）和从属地产（男性的土地）合并到一个所有者手中时，地役权就消失了。当该男子通过将房屋转让给购买者重新分割土地时，无论购买者实际或记录的先前地役权通知如何（Choices B＆D），它都没有恢复。因此，购买者对该人的土地没有地役权。 （选项C）购买者通过保修契约获得房屋的事实对于先前的地役权是否终止（或随后恢复）没有影响。但如果该男子声称将其土地的地役权转让给买方，那么买方可以起诉该男子违反契约项下的保证。\n\n教育目标：根据合并原则，如果支配性财产和受役性财产统一为共同所有权，则地役权终止。终止的地役权不会通过将土地重新分成两块而恢复。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.5 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过合并终止）。","conclusion":"答案 A：那个男人，因为地役权被消灭了。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":231,"sourceQuestionPage":672,"sourceQuestionPages":[672],"sourceExplanationPages":[673,674],"sourceAnswerPages":[673,674],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-232","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A grantor executed an instrument in the proper form of a warranty deed purporting to convey a tract of land to his church. The granting clause of the instrument ran to the church \"and its successors forever, so long as the premises are used for church purposes.\" The church took possession of the land and used it as its site of worship for many years. Subsequently, the church decided to relocate and entered into a valid written contract to sell the land to a buyer for a substantial price. The buyer wanted to use the land as a site for business activities and objected to the church's title. The contract contained no provision relating to the quality of title the church was bound to convey. There is no applicable statute. When the buyer refused to close, the church sued the buyer for specific performance and properly joined the grantor as a party. Is the church likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the grantor's interest prevents the church's title from being marketable.","B":"No, because the quoted provision is a valid restrictive covenant.","C":"Yes, because a charitable trust to support religion will attach to the proceeds of the sale.","D":"Yes, because the grantor cannot derogate from his warranty to the church."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nIn a land-sale contract, the seller promises to deliver marketable title to the buyer upon closing. Title is marketable if it is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. If the seller fails to satisfy this promise, the buyer can rescind the contract and refuse to close. Here, the church has a defeasible fee because its title will automatically terminate if the specified event or condition occurs—ie, when the land is no longer used for church purposes. If the buyer were to purchase the land and use it for business purposes, the specified event would occur. The buyer's title would then terminate and revert back to the grantor. As a result, the church cannot deliver marketable title and its suit against the buyer will likely fail. (Choice B) A restrictive covenant is a promise to do or not do something on one's land that has no impact on title. Since the quoted provision will divest the church (or its assigns) of title if the land is no longer used for church purposes, it is not a restrictive covenant but a defeasible fee. (Choice C) Attaching the sale proceeds to a charitable trust will have no impact on the church's ability to convey marketable title. This merely means that a trustee will hold those proceeds at the church's request for some charitable purpose and title will still terminate if the specified condition occurs.\n\n(Choice D) The grantor cannot and has not derogated any warranty (ie, covenant of title) to the church. He had the right to convey the church a defeasible fee and the right to retain a possibility of reverter in that land.\n\nEducational objective: Marketable title is title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. The owner of a defeasible fee cannot convey marketable title since title will automatically terminate upon the happening of a specified event or condition.\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (marketable title).","educationalObjective":"Marketable title is title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. The owner of a defeasible fee cannot convey marketable title since title will automatically terminate upon the happening of a specified event or condition.","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 93 (2020) (marketable title).","rule":"Marketable title is title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. The owner of a defeasible fee cannot convey marketable title since title will automatically terminate upon the happening of a specified event or condition.","irac":{"issue":"Is the church likely to prevail?","rule":"Marketable title is title that is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. The owner of a defeasible fee cannot convey marketable title since title will automatically terminate upon the happening of a specified event or condition.","analysis":"Fee simple estates\n\nType Language Future interest\n\nIndefeasible Fee simple absolute \"To A\" None \"To A and his heirs\" Defeasible Fee simple determinable Durational Grantor's possibility \"so long as\" of reverter \"during\" \"until\" Third party's\n\nFee simple subject to Conditional executory interest condition subsequent \"but if\" (fee simple subject to \"provided executory limitation) that\" \"unless\" Grantor's right of entry\n\nIn a land-sale contract, the seller promises to deliver marketable title to the buyer upon closing. Title is marketable if it is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. If the seller fails to satisfy this promise, the buyer can rescind the contract and refuse to close. Here, the church has a defeasible fee because its title will automatically terminate if the specified event or condition occurs—ie, when the land is no longer used for church purposes. If the buyer were to purchase the land and use it for business purposes, the specified event would occur. The buyer's title would then terminate and revert back to the grantor. As a result, the church cannot deliver marketable title and its suit against the buyer will likely fail. (Choice B) A restrictive covenant is a promise to do or not do something on one's land that has no impact on title. Since the quoted provision will divest the church (or its assigns) of title if the land is no longer used for church purposes, it is not a restrictive covenant but a defeasible fee. (Choice C) Attaching the sale proceeds to a charitable trust will have no impact on the church's ability to convey marketable title. This merely means that a trustee will hold those proceeds at the church's request for some charitable purpose and title will still terminate if the specified condition occurs.\n\n(Choice D) The grantor cannot and has not derogated any warranty (ie, covenant of title) to the church. He had the right to convey the church a defeasible fee and the right to retain a possibility of reverter in that land.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the grantor's interest prevents the church's title from being marketable."},"questionZh":"授予人以保修契约的适当形式签署了一份文书，旨在将一块土地转让给他的教堂。该文书的授予条款适用于教会“及其继承者，只要该场所用于教会目的”。教会占领了这片土地，并多年来将其用作礼拜场所。随后，教会决定搬迁，并签订了有效的书面合同，以高价将土地出售给买家。买家希望将这块土地用作商业活动场所，并反对教堂的所有权。该合同没有包含与教会必须转让的所有权性质有关的条款。没有适用的法规。当买方拒绝交割时，教会就买方的具体履行情况起诉买方，并适当地加入设保人作为一方。教会有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为授予人的利益阻碍了教会的所有权的流通。","B":"不，因为所引用的条款是有效的限制性契约。","C":"是的，因为支持宗教的慈善信托将附加在销售收入中。","D":"是的，因为授予人不能减损他对教会的保证。"},"explanationZh":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n在土地销售合同中，卖方承诺在成交时向买方交付可出售的所有权。如果产权合理地没有任何疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以致理性的人会接受并支付费用，则该产权是可出售的。如果卖方未能履行这一承诺，买方可以解除合同并拒绝成交。在这里，教堂有一项可废止的费用，因为如果发生指定的事件或条件，即当土地不再用于教堂目的时，其所有权将自动终止。如果买方购买土地并将其用于商业目的，则会发生指定事件。买方的所有权随后将终止并返还给设保人。因此，教会无法交付可出售的所有权，其针对买方的诉讼很可能会失败。 （选择 B） 限制性契约是承诺在自己的土地上做或不做某事，对所有权没有影响。由于如果土地不再用于教会目的，所引用的条款将剥夺教会（或其受让人）的所有权，因此这不是限制性契约，而是可废止的费用。 （选择 C） 将销售收益附加到慈善信托不会影响教会转让适销所有权的能力。这仅仅意味着受托人将根据教会的要求出于某种慈善目的持有这些收益，并且如果发生指定的条件，所有权仍将终止。\n\n（选项D）授予人不能也没有减损对教会的任何保证（即所有权契约）。他有权向教会转交一笔可废止的费用，并有权保留在该土地上回归的可能性。\n\n教育目标：可出售的所有权是指合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁的所有权，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。可废止费用的所有者不能转让可出售的所有权，因为所有权将在发生特定事件或条件时自动终止。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（可销售所有权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"可出售的所有权是指合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁的所有权，以便合理的人会接受并支付费用。可废止费用的所有者不能转让可出售的所有权，因为所有权将在发生特定事件或条件时自动终止。","iracZh":{"issue":"教会有可能获胜吗？","rule":"可出售的所有权是指合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁的所有权，以便合理的人会接受并支付费用。可废止费用的所有者不能转让可出售的所有权，因为所有权将在发生特定事件或条件时自动终止。","analysis":"费用简单庄园\n\n类型 语言 未来兴趣\n\n不可撤销费用 简单绝对 “对 A” 无 “对 A 及其继承人” 可撤销费用 简单可确定 持续性 授予人“只要”恢复者“期间”“直到”第三方的可能性\n\n费用简单服从有条件执行利息条件随后的“但是如果”（费用简单服从“规定的执行限制”）“除非”授予人的进入权\n\n在土地销售合同中，卖方承诺在成交时向买方交付可出售的所有权。如果产权合理地没有任何疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以致理性的人会接受并支付费用，则该产权是可出售的。如果卖方未能履行这一承诺，买方可以解除合同并拒绝成交。在这里，教堂有一项可废止的费用，因为如果发生指定的事件或条件，即当土地不再用于教堂目的时，其所有权将自动终止。如果买方购买土地并将其用于商业目的，则会发生指定事件。买方的所有权随后将终止并返还给设保人。因此，教会无法交付可出售的所有权，其针对买方的诉讼很可能会失败。 （选择 B） 限制性契约是承诺在自己的土地上做或不做某事，对所有权没有影响。由于如果土地不再用于教会目的，所引用的条款将剥夺教会（或其受让人）的所有权，因此这不是限制性契约，而是可废止的费用。 （选择 C） 将销售收益附加到慈善信托不会影响教会转让适销所有权的能力。这仅仅意味着受托人将根据教会的要求出于某种慈善目的持有这些收益，并且如果发生指定的条件，所有权仍将终止。\n\n（选项D）授予人不能也没有减损对教会的任何保证（即所有权契约）。他有权向教会转交一笔可废止的费用，并有权保留在该土地上回归的可能性。\n\n教育目标：可出售的所有权是指合理地没有疑问且不受诉讼威胁的所有权，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。可废止费用的所有者不能转让可出售的所有权，因为所有权将在发生特定事件或条件时自动终止。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 93 (2020)（可销售所有权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为授予人的利益阻碍了教会的所有权的流通。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":232,"sourceQuestionPage":675,"sourceQuestionPages":[675],"sourceExplanationPages":[676,677],"sourceAnswerPages":[676,677],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-233","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A widow owned a house in fee simple. Three years ago, the widow and her sister agreed to a month-to-month tenancy with the sister paying the widow rent each month. After six months of the sister's occupancy, the widow suggested to the sister that she could buy the house for a monthly payment of no more than her rent. The widow and the sister orally agreed that the sister would pay $25,000 in cash, the annual real estate taxes, the annual fire insurance premiums, the costs of maintaining the house, plus the monthly mortgage payments that the widow owed on the house. They further orally agreed that within six years, the sister could pay whatever mortgage balances were then due and the widow would give the sister a warranty deed to the property. The sister fully complied with all the obligations she had undertaken. She made structural modifications to the house that increased its value by more than 50% compared to when the widow and the sister made their oral agreement. The sister then made her financing arrangements and was ready to complete the purchase of the house, but the widow refused to close. The sister brought an appropriate action for specific performance against the widow to enforce the agreement. Who will likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"The sister, because the doctrine of part performance applies.","B":"The sister, because the statute of frauds does not apply to oral purchase and sale agreements between landlords and tenants in possession.","C":"The widow, because the agreements were oral and violated the statute of frauds.","D":"The widow, subject to the return of the $25,000, because the arrangement was still a tenancy."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Statute of frauds requirements for real estate contracts\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed\n\neg, receipt, check, emails Signature Handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged\n\neg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials Essential Typically includes: terms identity of parties words of intent to buy or sell property description sales price The statute of frauds applies to all real estate contracts—including purchase and sale agreements between landlords and tenants in possession—and requires that the contract (1) be in writing, (2) be signed by the party against whom it is to be enforced, and (3) contain all essential terms. Therefore, the widow and the sister's oral agreement regarding the sale and purchase of the house violates this statute (Choice B). However, an oral agreement may still be enforceable if it falls within the doctrine of part performance. This exception to the statute of frauds applies when the buyer has done the following: taken possession of the property (eg, the sister continued to live in the house) made substantial improvements to the property (eg, the sister's modification increased its value by 50%) and/or paid some or all of the purchase price* (eg, the sister's $25,000 cash payment and monthly payments) The buyer can then enforce the oral agreement in equity, typically by seeking specific performance. As a result, the sister will likely prevail (Choice C). *Payment of some or all of the purchase price is not independently sufficient to establish part performance. (Choice D) Although the arrangement started as a tenancy, it transformed into an enforceable real estate contract when the sister fully complied with her contractual obligations and was ready to purchase the home. As a result, the sister has the right to enforce the agreement under the doctrine of part performance.\n\nEducational objective: Part performance is an exception to the statute of frauds. This exception entitles the buyer to equitable relief (eg, specific performance) if the buyer has (1) taken possession of the property, (2) substantially improved the property, and/or (3) paid any amount of the purchase price.\n\nReferences\n73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).","educationalObjective":"Part performance is an exception to the statute of frauds. This exception entitles the buyer to equitable relief (eg, specific performance) if the buyer has (1) taken possession of the property, (2) substantially improved the property, and/or (3) paid any amount of the purchase price.","references":"73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).","rule":"Part performance is an exception to the statute of frauds. This exception entitles the buyer to equitable relief (eg, specific performance) if the buyer has (1) taken possession of the property, (2) substantially improved the property, and/or (3) paid any amount of the purchase price.","irac":{"issue":"Who will likely prevail?","rule":"Part performance is an exception to the statute of frauds. This exception entitles the buyer to equitable relief (eg, specific performance) if the buyer has (1) taken possession of the property, (2) substantially improved the property, and/or (3) paid any amount of the purchase price.","analysis":"Statute of frauds requirements for real estate contracts\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed\n\neg, receipt, check, emails Signature Handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged\n\neg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials Essential Typically includes: terms identity of parties words of intent to buy or sell property description sales price The statute of frauds applies to all real estate contracts—including purchase and sale agreements between landlords and tenants in possession—and requires that the contract (1) be in writing, (2) be signed by the party against whom it is to be enforced, and (3) contain all essential terms. Therefore, the widow and the sister's oral agreement regarding the sale and purchase of the house violates this statute (Choice B). However, an oral agreement may still be enforceable if it falls within the doctrine of part performance. This exception to the statute of frauds applies when the buyer has done the following: taken possession of the property (eg, the sister continued to live in the house) made substantial improvements to the property (eg, the sister's modification increased its value by 50%) and/or paid some or all of the purchase price* (eg, the sister's $25,000 cash payment and monthly payments) The buyer can then enforce the oral agreement in equity, typically by seeking specific performance. As a result, the sister will likely prevail (Choice C). *Payment of some or all of the purchase price is not independently sufficient to establish part performance. (Choice D) Although the arrangement started as a tenancy, it transformed into an enforceable real estate contract when the sister fully complied with her contractual obligations and was ready to purchase the home. As a result, the sister has the right to enforce the agreement under the doctrine of part performance.","conclusion":"Answer A: The sister, because the doctrine of part performance applies."},"questionZh":"一位寡妇拥有一栋简单的房子。三年前，寡妇和她的姐姐同意按月租赁，姐姐每月支付寡妇租金。姐姐入住六个月后，寡妇向姐姐建议，她可以每月支付不超过房租的价格买下这套房子。寡妇和姐姐口头约定，姐姐将支付25,000美元现金、每年的房产税、每年的火灾保险费、房屋维护费用，加上寡妇每月所欠房屋的抵押贷款。他们进一步口头同意，在六年内，妹妹可以支付当时到期的所有抵押贷款余额，寡妇将为妹妹提供一份财产保证契据。姐姐完全履行了她所承担的所有义务。她对房子进行了结构改造，与寡妇和姐姐口头协议时相比，其价值增加了​​ 50% 以上。随后姐姐做了融资安排，准备完成房子的购买，但寡妇拒绝成交。姐姐对寡妇提起适当的具体履行诉讼，以执行协议。谁可能会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"妹妹，因为部分绩效原则适用。","B":"妹妹，因为欺诈法不适用于房东和租户之间的口头买卖协议。","C":"寡妇，因为协议是口头的，违反了欺诈法。","D":"寡妇需要退还 25,000 美元，因为该安排仍然是租赁。"},"explanationZh":"房地产合同欺诈法规要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件\n\n例如，收据、支票、电子邮件 签名 被指控方的手写签名或其他识别标记\n\n例如，信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名首字母缩写 基本内容 通常包括： 条款 当事人身份 购买或出售财产的意向 描述 销售价格 欺诈法适用于所有房地产合同，包括业主与占有租户之间的购买和销售协议，并要求合同 (1) 采用书面形式，(2) 由合同执行对象方签署，以及 (3) 包含所有基本条款。因此，寡妇与姐姐就房屋买卖达成的口头协议违反了本法规定（选项B）。然而，如果口头协议属于部分履行原则，则它仍然可以强制执行。当买方做出以下行为时，适用欺诈法的例外情况： 占有房产（例如，姐姐继续住在房子里） 对房产进行实质性改进（例如，姐姐的修改使其价值增加了​​ 50%）和/或支付了部分或全部购买价*（例如，姐姐支付了 25,000 美元的现金和每月付款） 然后，买方可以强制执行股权方面的口头协议，通常通过寻求具体履行。结果，姐姐很可能会获胜（选项C）。 *支付部分或全部购买价格并不足以单独确定零件性能。 （选项D）虽然该安排最初是租赁，但当姐姐完全履行合同义务并准备购买房屋时，它就转变为可执行的房地产合同。因此，姐妹有权根据部分履行原则强制执行该协议。\n\n教育目标：部分绩效是欺诈法规的例外。如果买方已 (1) 占有该财产，(2) 实质性改善该财产，和/或 (3) 支付了任何金额的购买价，则此例外使买方有权获得衡平法救济（例如，具体履约）。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。","educationalObjectiveZh":"部分履行是欺诈法的一个例外。如果买方已 (1) 占有该财产，(2) 实质性改善该财产，和/或 (3) 支付了任何金额的购买价，则此例外使买方有权获得衡平法救济（例如，具体履约）。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁可能会获胜？","rule":"部分履行是欺诈法的一个例外。如果买方已 (1) 占有该财产，(2) 实质性改善该财产，和/或 (3) 支付了任何金额的购买价，则此例外使买方有权获得衡平法救济（例如，具体履约）。","analysis":"房地产合同欺诈法规要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件\n\n例如，收据、支票、电子邮件 签名 被指控方的手写签名或其他识别标记\n\n例如，信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名首字母缩写 基本内容 通常包括： 条款 当事人身份 购买或出售财产的意向 描述 销售价格 欺诈法适用于所有房地产合同，包括业主与占有租户之间的购买和销售协议，并要求合同 (1) 采用书面形式，(2) 由合同执行对象方签署，以及 (3) 包含所有基本条款。因此，寡妇与姐姐就房屋买卖达成的口头协议违反了本法规定（选项B）。然而，如果口头协议属于部分履行原则，则它仍然可以强制执行。当买方做出以下行为时，适用欺诈法的例外情况： 占有房产（例如，姐姐继续住在房子里） 对房产进行实质性改进（例如，姐姐的修改使其价值增加了​​ 50%）和/或支付了部分或全部购买价*（例如，姐姐支付了 25,000 美元的现金和每月付款） 然后，买方可以强制执行股权方面的口头协议，通常通过寻求具体履行。结果，姐姐很可能会获胜（选项C）。 *支付部分或全部购买价格并不足以单独确定零件性能。 （选项D）虽然该安排最初是租赁，但当姐姐完全履行合同义务并准备购买房屋时，它就转变为可执行的房地产合同。因此，姐妹有权根据部分履行原则强制执行该协议。\n\n教育目标：部分绩效是欺诈法规的例外。如果买方已 (1) 占有该财产，(2) 实质性改善该财产，和/或 (3) 支付了任何金额的购买价，则此例外使买方有权获得衡平法救济（例如，具体履约）。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。","conclusion":"答案 A：妹妹，因为部分绩效原则适用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":233,"sourceQuestionPage":678,"sourceQuestionPages":[678],"sourceExplanationPages":[679,680],"sourceAnswerPages":[679,680],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-234","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"The existence of an express right-of-way easement over land was discovered during a title search conducted by an individual who had contracted to buy the land. The seller desired to secure a release of the easement, so he traveled to the easement holder's neighboring property. When the seller arrived, the neighbor's adult daughter informed him that the neighbor had been living in a nursing home for the past year and that she had been taking care of his land. In exchange for a small sum, the neighbor's daughter executed the requested document. The document stated that the neighbor \"hereby releases\" the easement over the seller's land, which was described in full. The seller then deeded the land to the buyer, and the neighbor died shortly thereafter. The neighbor's property passed by will to the neighbor's daughter. The daughter claims that she continues to possess a right-of-way easement over the buyer's land. Is the neighbor's daughter correct?","choices":{"A":"No, because the daughter signed the release of the easement.","B":"No, because the release was valid and terminated the easement.","C":"Yes, because the property interest was not a license and did not expire upon the neighbor's death.","D":"Yes, because the release was not signed by the neighbor and violated the statute of frauds."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Ways to terminate easements\n\nDestruction – servient estate condemned or destroyed by natural forces Abandonment - dominant estate abandons easement Merger - dominant & servient estates united in common ownership Prescription - servient owner prevents use for prescriptive period Estoppel - dominant owner estopped from asserting easement Release - express release in writing satisfying statute of frauds Mnemonic: DAMPER\n\nAn easement is a nonpossessory interest in someone else's land that gives the easement holder the right to enter and use the land for a specific purpose. An easement can be terminated by a release—ie, a writing that expressly relinquishes the easement right— so long as it complies with the statute of frauds. To comply with the statute of frauds, the writing must do the following: Reasonably identify the parties (the neighbor and the seller) Contain words of transfer (\"hereby releases\") Describe the nature of the easement (the easement over the seller's land was described in full) Be signed by the easement holder (the neighbor) Since the neighbor did not sign the release (the daughter did), the document secured by the seller was not a valid release that terminated the easement (Choices B & D). But since the daughter did sign the release, she will be estopped from asserting that she inherited the neighbor's right-of-way over the buyer's land. (Choice C) Although a license typically expires upon the death of the licensee, an easement does not expire upon the death of the easement holder. This means that the neighbor's easement passed by will to his daughter. However, she will be estopped from asserting the easement's validity because she signed the release.\n\nEducational objective: An easement can be terminated by a written release that complies with the statute of frauds. To do so, the release must (1) reasonably identify the parties, (2) contain words of transfer, (3) describe the nature of the easement, and (4) be signed by the easement holder.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.3 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination of an easement by release).","educationalObjective":"An easement can be terminated by a written release that complies with the statute of frauds. To do so, the release must (1) reasonably identify the parties, (2) contain words of transfer, (3) describe the nature of the easement, and (4) be signed by the easement holder.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 7.3 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (termination of an easement by release).","rule":"An easement can be terminated by a written release that complies with the statute of frauds. To do so, the release must (1) reasonably identify the parties, (2) contain words of transfer, (3) describe the nature of the easement, and (4) be signed by the easement holder.","irac":{"issue":"Is the neighbor's daughter correct?","rule":"An easement can be terminated by a written release that complies with the statute of frauds. To do so, the release must (1) reasonably identify the parties, (2) contain words of transfer, (3) describe the nature of the easement, and (4) be signed by the easement holder.","analysis":"Ways to terminate easements\n\nDestruction – servient estate condemned or destroyed by natural forces Abandonment - dominant estate abandons easement Merger - dominant & servient estates united in common ownership Prescription - servient owner prevents use for prescriptive period Estoppel - dominant owner estopped from asserting easement Release - express release in writing satisfying statute of frauds Mnemonic: DAMPER\n\nAn easement is a nonpossessory interest in someone else's land that gives the easement holder the right to enter and use the land for a specific purpose. An easement can be terminated by a release—ie, a writing that expressly relinquishes the easement right— so long as it complies with the statute of frauds. To comply with the statute of frauds, the writing must do the following: Reasonably identify the parties (the neighbor and the seller) Contain words of transfer (\"hereby releases\") Describe the nature of the easement (the easement over the seller's land was described in full) Be signed by the easement holder (the neighbor) Since the neighbor did not sign the release (the daughter did), the document secured by the seller was not a valid release that terminated the easement (Choices B & D). But since the daughter did sign the release, she will be estopped from asserting that she inherited the neighbor's right-of-way over the buyer's land. (Choice C) Although a license typically expires upon the death of the licensee, an easement does not expire upon the death of the easement holder. This means that the neighbor's easement passed by will to his daughter. However, she will be estopped from asserting the easement's validity because she signed the release.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the daughter signed the release of the easement."},"questionZh":"签订土地购买合同的个人在进行产权调查时发现了土地上明确的通行权地役权的存在。卖方希望获得地役权的释放，因此他前往地役权持有人的邻近房产。当卖家到达时，邻居已成年的女儿告诉他，邻居过去一年一直住在疗养院，她一直在照顾他的土地。为了换取一小笔钱，邻居的女儿执行了所要求的文件。该文件称，邻居“特此释放”卖方土地的地役权，并进行了详细描述。卖方随后将土地转让给买方，不久之后邻居就去世了。邻居的财产通过遗嘱转移给了邻居的女儿。女儿声称她继续拥有买方土地的通行权。邻居的女儿说得对吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为女儿签署了地役权释放书。","B":"不，因为释放是有效的并终止了地役权。","C":"是的，因为财产权益不是许可证，并且不会在邻居去世后到期。","D":"是的，因为该授权书未经邻居签字，违反了欺诈法。"},"explanationZh":"终止地役权的方法\n\n毁坏 - 被自然力量谴责或破坏的受役财产 遗弃 - 主导财产放弃地役权 合并 - 主导财产和受役财产合并为共同所有权 时效 - 受役所有者在规定期限内禁止使用 禁止反言 - 主导所有者禁止主张地役权 释放 - 以书面形式明确释放，满足欺诈法规要求 助记符：DAMPER\n\n地役权是对其他人土地的非占有性权益，赋予地役权持有人出于特定目的进入和使用土地的权利。地役权可以通过释放（即明确放弃地役权的书面形式）终止，只要它符合反欺诈法。为了遵守欺诈法，书面文件必须做到以下几点： 合理地识别当事人（邻居和卖方） 包含转让字样（“特此释放”） 描述地役权的性质（完整描述了卖方土地上的地役权） 由地役权持有人（邻居）签署 由于邻居没有签署释放权（女儿签署了），因此卖方担保的文件不是终止地役权的有效释放（选择 B & D）。但由于女儿确实签署了授权书，她将无法声称自己继承了邻居对买方土地的通行权。 （选择 C）虽然许可证通常会在被许可人死亡时到期，但地役权不会在地役权持有人死亡时到期。这意味着邻居的地役权通过遗嘱转移给了他的女儿。然而，由于她签署了免责声明，她将无法主张地役权的有效性。\n\n教育目标：地役权可以通过符合欺诈法规的书面释放来终止。为此，释放必须 (1) 合理地识别当事人，(2) 包含转让文字，(3) 描述地役权的性质，以及 (4) 由地役权持有人签署。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.3 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过释放终止地役权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"地役权可以通过符合欺诈法规的书面释放来终止。为此，释放必须 (1) 合理地识别当事人，(2) 包含转让文字，(3) 描述地役权的性质，以及 (4) 由地役权持有人签署。","iracZh":{"issue":"邻居的女儿说得对吗？","rule":"地役权可以通过符合欺诈法规的书面释放来终止。为此，释放必须 (1) 合理地识别当事人，(2) 包含转让文字，(3) 描述地役权的性质，以及 (4) 由地役权持有人签署。","analysis":"终止地役权的方法\n\n毁坏 - 被自然力量谴责或破坏的受役财产 遗弃 - 主导财产放弃地役权 合并 - 主导财产和受役财产合并为共同所有权 时效 - 受役所有者在规定期限内禁止使用 禁止反言 - 主导所有者禁止主张地役权 释放 - 以书面形式明确释放，满足欺诈法规要求 助记符：DAMPER\n\n地役权是对其他人土地的非占有性权益，赋予地役权持有人出于特定目的进入和使用土地的权利。地役权可以通过释放（即明确放弃地役权的书面形式）终止，只要它符合反欺诈法。为了遵守欺诈法，书面文件必须做到以下几点： 合理地识别当事人（邻居和卖方） 包含转让字样（“特此释放”） 描述地役权的性质（完整描述了卖方土地上的地役权） 由地役权持有人（邻居）签署 由于邻居没有签署释放权（女儿签署了），因此卖方担保的文件不是终止地役权的有效释放（选择 B & D）。但由于女儿确实签署了授权书，她将无法声称自己继承了邻居对买方土地的通行权。 （选择 C）虽然许可证通常会在被许可人死亡时到期，但地役权不会在地役权持有人死亡时到期。这意味着邻居的地役权通过遗嘱转移给了他的女儿。然而，由于她签署了免责声明，她将无法主张地役权的有效性。\n\n教育目标：地役权可以通过符合欺诈法规的书面释放来终止。为此，释放必须 (1) 合理地识别当事人，(2) 包含转让文字，(3) 描述地役权的性质，以及 (4) 由地役权持有人签署。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 7.3 (Am. Law Inst. 2000)（通过释放终止地役权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为女儿签署了地役权释放书。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":234,"sourceQuestionPage":681,"sourceQuestionPages":[681],"sourceExplanationPages":[682,683],"sourceAnswerPages":[682,683],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-235","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Easements","question":"A man purchased a large tract of land that consisted entirely of timberland. In order to take full advantage of the commercial value of his land, the man entered into a written agreement with a timber company. That agreement gave the timber company the right to enter the man's land to harvest as much timber as it wished, at its fair market value, for the next five years. The agreement was promptly and properly recorded. The timber company's right to harvest timber on the man's land was not exclusive, and the man reserved the right to grant similar rights to others or to harvest timber himself. The agreement had been in effect for just one year when the state brought a condemnation action to take the man's land for an airport. In the condemnation action, is the timber company entitled to compensation?","choices":{"A":"No, because the agreement between the man and the timber company created only a license.","B":"No, because the timber company's right to harvest timber on the man's land was not exclusive.","C":"Yes, because the agreement between the man and the timber company created a profit à prendre.","D":"Yes, because the timber company paid value for the right to harvest timber on the man's land."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Licenses, easements & profits\n\nLicense Revocable privilege to enter & use another's land for specific purpose\n\nEasement Nonpossessory interest in land that gives easement holder right to:\n\nuse another's land for specific purpose (ie, affirmative easement) or prohibit other owner from doing something on land that owner would otherwise be allowed to do (ie, negative easement) Profit Nonpossessory interest in land that gives profit holder right to enter another's land to remove products of soil (eg, timber, minerals, oil)\n\nA profit à prendre—also called simply a profit—is a nonpossessory interest in land that gives the profit holder the right to enter another's land to remove specific natural resources (eg, timber). Because a profit is an interest in land, the profit holder (eg, timber company) is entitled to compensation if the government condemns the property under the power of eminent domain. This is true regardless of whether the profit holder paid value or has an exclusive right to remove the resources (Choices B & D). (Choice A) Unlike easements and profits, licenses do not create an interest in the land. As a result, a licensee is not entitled to compensation in a condemnation action. But here, the timber company had a profit—not a license.\n\nEducational objective: A profit à prendre is a nonpossessory interest in land that allows the profit holder to remove natural resources from another's land. This interest entitles the profit holder to compensation if the government condemns the property.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 1.2(2) (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (defining profit à prendre).\n\nBates Sand & Gravel Co., Inc. v. Commonwealth, 404 N.E.2d 81, 82 (Mass. 1980) (finding that a profit à prendre is an interest in land entitled to compensation in a condemnation action).","educationalObjective":"A profit à prendre is a nonpossessory interest in land that allows the profit holder to remove natural resources from another's land. This interest entitles the profit holder to compensation if the government condemns the property.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 1.2(2) (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (defining profit à prendre).\n\nBates Sand & Gravel Co., Inc. v. Commonwealth, 404 N.E.2d 81, 82 (Mass. 1980) (finding that a profit à prendre is an interest in land entitled to compensation in a condemnation action).","rule":"A profit à prendre is a nonpossessory interest in land that allows the profit holder to remove natural resources from another's land. This interest entitles the profit holder to compensation if the government condemns the property.","irac":{"issue":"In the condemnation action, is the timber company entitled to compensation?","rule":"A profit à prendre is a nonpossessory interest in land that allows the profit holder to remove natural resources from another's land. This interest entitles the profit holder to compensation if the government condemns the property.","analysis":"Licenses, easements & profits\n\nLicense Revocable privilege to enter & use another's land for specific purpose\n\nEasement Nonpossessory interest in land that gives easement holder right to:\n\nuse another's land for specific purpose (ie, affirmative easement) or prohibit other owner from doing something on land that owner would otherwise be allowed to do (ie, negative easement) Profit Nonpossessory interest in land that gives profit holder right to enter another's land to remove products of soil (eg, timber, minerals, oil)\n\nA profit à prendre—also called simply a profit—is a nonpossessory interest in land that gives the profit holder the right to enter another's land to remove specific natural resources (eg, timber). Because a profit is an interest in land, the profit holder (eg, timber company) is entitled to compensation if the government condemns the property under the power of eminent domain. This is true regardless of whether the profit holder paid value or has an exclusive right to remove the resources (Choices B & D). (Choice A) Unlike easements and profits, licenses do not create an interest in the land. As a result, a licensee is not entitled to compensation in a condemnation action. But here, the timber company had a profit—not a license.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the agreement between the man and the timber company created a profit à prendre."},"questionZh":"一个人购买了一大片完全由林地组成的土地。为了充分利用自己土地的商业价值，该男子与一家木材公司签订了书面协议。该协议赋予木材公司在未来五年内进入该男子土地的权利，以公平的市场价值采伐尽可能多的木材。该协议已被及时、正确地记录下来。木材公司在该人的土地上采伐木材的权利不是排他性的，该人保留向他人授予类似权利或自己采伐木材的权利。该协议生效仅一年，当时该州就采取了谴责行动，征用该男子的土地修建机场。在谴责行动中，木材公司是否有权获得赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该男子与木材公司之间的协议仅创建了许可证。","B":"不，因为木材公司在该人的土地上采伐木材的权利不是排他性的。","C":"是的，因为该人和木材公司之间的协议创造了利润。","D":"是的，因为木材公司为在该人的土地上采伐木材的权利付出了代价。"},"explanationZh":"许可证、地役权和利润\n\n出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的许可可撤销特权\n\n地役权 土地的非占有权益，赋予地役权持有人以下权利：\n\n将他人的土地用于特定目的（即，肯定地役权）或禁止其他所有者在所有者可以做的事情上做某事（即，消极地役权） 利润 对土地的非占有权益，使利润持有者有权进入他人的土地以去除土壤产品（例如，木材、矿物、石油）\n\n利润（也简称为利润）是对土地的非占有性权益，赋予利润持有者进入他人土地以获取特定自然资源（例如木材）的权利。由于利润是土地权益，因此如果政府根据征用权谴责该财产，利润持有者（例如木材公司）有权获得赔偿。无论利润持有者是否支付了价值或是否拥有移除资源的专有权（选择 B 和 D），情况都是如此。 （选择 A）与地役权和利润不同，许可证不会产生土地权益。因此，被许可人无权在谴责行动中获得赔偿。但在这里，木材公司获得的是利润，而不是许可证。\n\n教育目标：利润是对土地的非占有性权益，允许利润持有者从他人的土地上获取自然资源。如果政府谴责该财产，则该利息使利润持有者有权获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 1.2(2)（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（定义利润 à prendre）。\n\nBates Sand & Gravel Co., Inc. v. Commonwealth, 404 N.E.2d 81, 82 (Mass. 1980)（认定利润是指在谴责诉讼中有权获得补偿的土地权益）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"预付利润是对土地的非占有性权益，允许利润持有者从他人的土地上获取自然资源。如果政府谴责该财产，则该利息使利润持有者有权获得赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"在谴责行动中，木材公司是否有权获得赔偿？","rule":"预付利润是对土地的非占有性权益，允许利润持有者从他人的土地上获取自然资源。如果政府谴责该财产，则该利息使利润持有者有权获得赔偿。","analysis":"许可证、地役权和利润\n\n出于特定目的进入和使用他人土地的许可可撤销特权\n\n地役权 土地的非占有权益，赋予地役权持有人以下权利：\n\n将他人的土地用于特定目的（即，肯定地役权）或禁止其他所有者在所有者可以做的事情上做某事（即，消极地役权） 利润 对土地的非占有权益，使利润持有者有权进入他人的土地以去除土壤产品（例如，木材、矿物、石油）\n\n利润（也简称为利润）是对土地的非占有性权益，赋予利润持有者进入他人土地以获取特定自然资源（例如木材）的权利。由于利润是土地权益，因此如果政府根据征用权谴责该财产，利润持有者（例如木材公司）有权获得赔偿。无论利润持有者是否支付了价值或是否拥有移除资源的专有权（选择 B 和 D），情况都是如此。 （选择 A）与地役权和利润不同，许可证不会产生土地权益。因此，被许可人无权在谴责行动中获得赔偿。但在这里，木材公司获得的是利润，而不是许可证。\n\n教育目标：利润是对土地的非占有性权益，允许利润持有者从他人的土地上获取自然资源。如果政府谴责该财产，则该利息使利润持有者有权获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 1.2(2)（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（定义利润 à prendre）。\n\nBates Sand & Gravel Co., Inc. v. Commonwealth, 404 N.E.2d 81, 82 (Mass. 1980)（认定利润是指在谴责诉讼中有权获得补偿的土地权益）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为该人和木材公司之间的协议创造了利润。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":235,"sourceQuestionPage":684,"sourceQuestionPages":[684],"sourceExplanationPages":[685],"sourceAnswerPages":[685],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-236","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Landlord-Tenant","question":"A landowner leased a plot of land to a bamboo farmer on a month-to-month basis, with rent due at the beginning of each month. The farmer planted dwarf bamboo crops on the land. For several years thereafter, the farmer would divide the roots of each bamboo plant and replant them on the land in early spring. Each October, the farmer would harvest some of the plants to sell to local landscapers. On October 31, the farmer paid rent to the landowner, at which time the landowner notified the farmer that the lease would terminate as of November 30 because the landowner had sold the land to a large farming corporation. Although harvesting season was over, the farmer attempted to reenter the land on December 15 and remove the remaining bamboo plants. The landowner filed suit against the farmer to prevent the removal of the plants. In whose favor should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"The farmer, because all emblements remain personal property.","B":"The farmer, because the farmer planted the bamboo and the lease was for an indefinite duration.","C":"The landowner, because the farmer had already harvested the bamboo plants.","D":"The landowner, because the owner of the land is irrebuttably presumed to be the owner of the crops."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Ownership of emblements\n\nType Status Upon transfer of land\n\nPlanted, cultivated Personal property Pass with land except* when: crops harvested – severed from land (fructus industriales) ripe – mature (some courts) planted by tenant with indefinite leasehold or planted by adverse possessor under claim of right Wild, uncultivated Part of real Pass automatically with land crops property\n\n(fructus naturales)\n\n*Then prior owner has right to reenter land to remove crops.\n\nThere are two types of emblements (ie, crops) that are generally conveyed with the land: Fructus industriales – produced through cultivation and considered personal property Fructus naturales – occur naturally (ie, without human assistance) and considered real property A landowner is presumed to own both types of emblements grown on the land and to convey them with the land upon its sale. However, there are certain exceptions to this presumption with respect to fructus industriales. One exception, called the doctrine of emblements, applies when a lease of uncertain duration is terminated through no fault of the tenant. Under this exception, the tenant has the right to reenter the land to remove, harvest, or cultivate crops that the tenant planted during the tenancy. Here, the landowner is presumed to own the bamboo crops planted by the farmer (fructus industriales). However, the farmer planted the bamboo on land that was leased month-to- month indefinitely, and the landlord terminated the lease to sell the land to a farming corporation—not due to any fault of the farmer. This gave the farmer the right to reenter the land and remove the bamboo plants under the doctrine of emblements. Therefore, the court should rule in favor of the farmer. (Choice A) Fructus industriales are considered personal property, but fructus naturales are considered real property. As a result, not all emblements remain personal property.\n\n(Choice C) Even though the farmer had already harvested some of the bamboo, the farmer can still reenter and remove the rest of the plants under the doctrine of emblements. (Choice D) Although a landowner is presumed to be the owner of crops on his/her land, this presumption can be rebutted by evidence of the parties' contrary intent—ie, this presumption is not irrebuttable.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of emblements, a tenant has the right to reenter land to remove, harvest, and cultivate crops that the tenant planted before the tenancy terminated if the tenant's lease was for an uncertain duration and terminated through no fault of the tenant.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 12.3 cmt. h (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (harvesting agricultural crops from leased property).","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of emblements, a tenant has the right to reenter land to remove, harvest, and cultivate crops that the tenant planted before the tenancy terminated if the tenant's lease was for an uncertain duration and terminated through no fault of the tenant.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Prop.: Landlord & Tenant § 12.3 cmt. h (Am. Law Inst. 1977) (harvesting agricultural crops from leased property).","rule":"Under the doctrine of emblements, a tenant has the right to reenter land to remove, harvest, and cultivate crops that the tenant planted before the tenancy terminated if the tenant's lease was for an uncertain duration and terminated through no fault of the tenant.","irac":{"issue":"In whose favor should the court rule?","rule":"Under the doctrine of emblements, a tenant has the right to reenter land to remove, harvest, and cultivate crops that the tenant planted before the tenancy terminated if the tenant's lease was for an uncertain duration and terminated through no fault of the tenant.","analysis":"Ownership of emblements\n\nType Status Upon transfer of land\n\nPlanted, cultivated Personal property Pass with land except* when: crops harvested – severed from land (fructus industriales) ripe – mature (some courts) planted by tenant with indefinite leasehold or planted by adverse possessor under claim of right Wild, uncultivated Part of real Pass automatically with land crops property\n\n(fructus naturales)\n\n*Then prior owner has right to reenter land to remove crops.\n\nThere are two types of emblements (ie, crops) that are generally conveyed with the land: Fructus industriales – produced through cultivation and considered personal property Fructus naturales – occur naturally (ie, without human assistance) and considered real property A landowner is presumed to own both types of emblements grown on the land and to convey them with the land upon its sale. However, there are certain exceptions to this presumption with respect to fructus industriales. One exception, called the doctrine of emblements, applies when a lease of uncertain duration is terminated through no fault of the tenant. Under this exception, the tenant has the right to reenter the land to remove, harvest, or cultivate crops that the tenant planted during the tenancy. Here, the landowner is presumed to own the bamboo crops planted by the farmer (fructus industriales). However, the farmer planted the bamboo on land that was leased month-to- month indefinitely, and the landlord terminated the lease to sell the land to a farming corporation—not due to any fault of the farmer. This gave the farmer the right to reenter the land and remove the bamboo plants under the doctrine of emblements. Therefore, the court should rule in favor of the farmer. (Choice A) Fructus industriales are considered personal property, but fructus naturales are considered real property. As a result, not all emblements remain personal property.\n\n(Choice C) Even though the farmer had already harvested some of the bamboo, the farmer can still reenter and remove the rest of the plants under the doctrine of emblements. (Choice D) Although a landowner is presumed to be the owner of crops on his/her land, this presumption can be rebutted by evidence of the parties' contrary intent—ie, this presumption is not irrebuttable.","conclusion":"Answer B: The farmer, because the farmer planted the bamboo and the lease was for an indefinite duration."},"questionZh":"一位地主将一块土地按月租给一位竹农，租金在每个月初到期。农民在土地上种植了矮竹作物。此后的几年里，农民将每株竹子的根分开，并在早春重新种植在土地上。每年十月，农民都会收获一些植物出售给当地的园艺师。 10月31日，农民向土地所有者支付了租金，此时土地所有者通知农民，租赁将于11月30日终止，因为土地所有者已将土地出售给一家大型农业公司。尽管收获季节已经结束，但农民仍试图于 12 月 15 日重新进入土地并清除剩余的竹子。土地所有者对农民提起诉讼，以阻止移除植物。法院应该做出对谁有利的裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"农民，因为所有标志仍然是个人财产。","B":"农民，因为竹子是农民种植的，而且租约是无限期的。","C":"地主，因为农民已经收割了竹子。","D":"土地所有者，因为土地所有者无可辩驳地被认为是农作物的所有者。"},"explanationZh":"徽章的所有权\n\n类型 状态 土地转让时\n\n种植的、耕种的 个人财产随土地转移，除非*以下情况： 收获的作物 – 从土地上分离（工业果） 成熟 – 成熟（某些法院） 由拥有无限期租赁权的租户种植或由逆权占有人根据权利主张种植 野生、未耕种 部分不动产自动随土地作物财产转移\n\n（天然果子）\n\n*然后，先前的所有者有权重新进入土地以清除农作物。\n\n一般而言，有两种类型的标志（即农作物）随土地一起转移： 工业果 - 通过种植生产并被视为个人财产 自然果 - 自然产生（即无需人工协助）并被视为不动产 土地所有者被推定拥有土地上种植的两种类型的标志，并在出售土地时将其与土地一起转移。然而，对于工业果来说，这一推定有某些例外。一种例外情况称为象征原则，适用于期限不确定的租赁因非承租人的过错而终止的情况。在这种例外情况下，承租人有权重新进入土地，以移除、收割或种植承租人在租赁期间种植的农作物。在这里，土地所有者被认为拥有农民种植的竹子作物（工业果）。然而，农民在无限期地按月租赁的土地上种植竹子，房东终止租约，将土地出售给农业公司——这并不是农民的任何过错。这赋予了农民重新进入土地并根据象征原则清除竹子的权利。因此，法院应作出有利于农民的裁决。 （选项 A） 工业果被视为个人财产，但天然果被视为不动产。因此，并非所有徽章都是个人财产。\n\n（选项C）即使农民已经收获了一些竹子，农民仍然可以根据象征原则重新进入并移除其余的植物。 （选项D）虽然土地所有者被推定为其土地上农作物的所有者，但这种推定可以通过当事人相反意图的证据来反驳——即，这种推定不是不可反驳的。\n\n教育目标：根据象征原则，如果租户的租约期限不确定且非因租户的过错而终止，则租户有权重新进入土地，以移走、收割和种植租户在租赁终止前种植的农作物。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 12.3 cmt。 h（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（从租赁财产中收获农作物）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据象征原则，如果租户的租约期限不确定且非因租户的过错而终止，则租户有权重新进入土地，移走、收割和种植租户在租赁终止前种植的农作物。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该做出对谁有利的裁决？","rule":"根据象征原则，如果租户的租约期限不确定且非因租户的过错而终止，则租户有权重新进入土地，移走、收割和种植租户在租赁终止前种植的农作物。","analysis":"徽章的所有权\n\n类型 状态 土地转让时\n\n种植的、耕种的 个人财产随土地转移，除非*以下情况： 收获的作物 – 从土地上分离（工业果） 成熟 – 成熟（某些法院） 由拥有无限期租赁权的租户种植或由逆权占有人根据权利主张种植 野生、未耕种 部分不动产自动随土地作物财产转移\n\n（天然果子）\n\n*然后，先前的所有者有权重新进入土地以清除农作物。\n\n一般而言，有两种类型的标志（即农作物）随土地一起转移： 工业果 - 通过种植生产并被视为个人财产 自然果 - 自然产生（即无需人工协助）并被视为不动产 土地所有者被推定拥有土地上种植的两种类型的标志，并在出售土地时将其与土地一起转移。然而，对于工业果来说，这一推定有某些例外。一种例外情况称为象征原则，适用于期限不确定的租赁因非承租人的过错而终止的情况。在这种例外情况下，承租人有权重新进入土地，以移除、收割或种植承租人在租赁期间种植的农作物。在这里，土地所有者被认为拥有农民种植的竹子作物（工业果）。然而，农民在无限期地按月租赁的土地上种植竹子，房东终止租约，将土地出售给农业公司——这并不是农民的任何过错。这赋予了农民重新进入土地并根据象征原则清除竹子的权利。因此，法院应作出有利于农民的裁决。 （选项 A） 工业果被视为个人财产，但天然果被视为不动产。因此，并非所有徽章都是个人财产。\n\n（选项C）即使农民已经收获了一些竹子，农民仍然可以根据象征原则重新进入并移除其余的植物。 （选项D）虽然土地所有者被推定为其土地上农作物的所有者，但这种推定可以通过当事人相反意图的证据来反驳——即，这种推定不是不可反驳的。\n\n教育目标：根据象征原则，如果租户的租约期限不确定且非因租户的过错而终止，则租户有权重新进入土地，以移走、收割和种植租户在租赁终止前种植的农作物。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第二次）：房东和租户 § 12.3 cmt。 h（Am. Law Inst. 1977）（从租赁财产中收获农作物）。","conclusion":"答案 B：农民，因为竹子是农民种植的，而且租约是无限期的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"landscapers","ipa":"/lˈændskˌeɪpɝz/","syllables":"land·scap·ers","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"(landscaper 的复数) n. 庭园设计家","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题不动产法语境中的“landscapers”","wordForm":"词；原形 landscaper；词形 0:landscaper/1:s","sourceSentence":"Each October, the farmer would harvest some of the plants to sell to local landscapers.","synonyms":["landscape architect","landscape gardener","landscapist"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":236,"sourceQuestionPage":686,"sourceQuestionPages":[686],"sourceExplanationPages":[687,688],"sourceAnswerPages":[687,688],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-237","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Easements","question":"An architect and a developer own adjacent parcels of land. On each of their parcels was a low-rise office building. The two office buildings were of the same height. Last year the developer decided to demolish the low-rise office building on her parcel and to erect a new high-rise office building of substantially greater height on her parcel as permitted by the zoning and building ordinances. She secured all the governmental approvals necessary to pursue her project. As the developer's new building was in the course of construction, the architect realized that the shadows it would create would place the architect's building in such deep shade that the rent she could charge for space in her building would be substantially reduced. The architect brought an appropriate action against the developer to enjoin the construction in order to eliminate the shadow problem and for damages. The architect presented uncontroverted evidence that her evaluation as to the impact of the shadow on the fair rental value of her building was correct. There is no statute or ordinance (other than the building and zoning ordinances) that is applicable to the issues before the court. How should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"Award the architect damages measured by the loss of rental value, but not an injunction.","B":"Grant judgment for the developer, because she had secured all the necessary governmental approvals for the new building.","C":"Grant judgment for the developer, because the architect has no legal right to have sunshine continue to reach the windows of her building.","D":"Grant the architect the requested injunction."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Remedies for nuisance & easement actions\n\nTemporary Permanent Misuse / interference of easement nuisance nuisance\n\nDamages Diminished rental Diminished land Rents received value value Decreased value of servient estate Reasonable repair and/or easement costs Value of excessive use Incidental loss Cost of restoration Loss of use & enjoyment Loss of use Punitive damages Injunction Available N/A Available\n\nSelf-help Available N/A Available\n\nForfeiture N/A Unavailable unless injunction will not relieve servient estate At English common law, a landowner's right to light was protected as a prescriptive negative easement under the Doctrine of Ancient Lights. This doctrine has been rejected in the United States. Courts also generally decline to recognize blockage of light as a public or private nuisance unless the blockage was motivated by malice. As a result, landowners only have a right to light if they are protected by statute or enter into an express agreement with the other landowner to create a negative easement (ie, restrictive covenant). Here, the architect sought to prevent the developer from blocking the light to the architect's building. However, there is no indication that the developer is erecting a high-rise building out of malice or that the architect has obtained a negative easement or covenant from the developer. There is also no statute prohibiting interference with the light. Therefore, the court should grant judgment for the developer because the architect has no legal right to the light reaching her windows (Choices A & D). (Choice B) The fact that the developer had secured all the necessary governmental approvals does not allow her to bar others from exercising their legal rights. Therefore, this fact would not prevent the architect from prevailing in a lawsuit had she had a legal right to continued access to the light.\n\nEducational objective: A landowner only has a right to light if (1) this right is protected by statute or (2) the landowner enters into an express agreement with the other landowner to create a negative easement or covenant.\n\nReferences\nMohr v. Midas Realty Corp., 431 N.W.2d 380, 382 (Iowa 1988) (explaining that every state has rejected the Doctrine of Ancient Lights).\n\nSher v. Leiderman, 226 Cal. Rptr. 698, 701 (Ct. App. 1986) (holding that blockage of light is not an actionable nuisance except in cases of malice).","educationalObjective":"A landowner only has a right to light if (1) this right is protected by statute or (2) the landowner enters into an express agreement with the other landowner to create a negative easement or covenant.","references":"Mohr v. Midas Realty Corp., 431 N.W.2d 380, 382 (Iowa 1988) (explaining that every state has rejected the Doctrine of Ancient Lights).\n\nSher v. Leiderman, 226 Cal. Rptr. 698, 701 (Ct. App. 1986) (holding that blockage of light is not an actionable nuisance except in cases of malice).","rule":"A landowner only has a right to light if (1) this right is protected by statute or (2) the landowner enters into an express agreement with the other landowner to create a negative easement or covenant.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule?","rule":"A landowner only has a right to light if (1) this right is protected by statute or (2) the landowner enters into an express agreement with the other landowner to create a negative easement or covenant.","analysis":"Remedies for nuisance & easement actions\n\nTemporary Permanent Misuse / interference of easement nuisance nuisance\n\nDamages Diminished rental Diminished land Rents received value value Decreased value of servient estate Reasonable repair and/or easement costs Value of excessive use Incidental loss Cost of restoration Loss of use & enjoyment Loss of use Punitive damages Injunction Available N/A Available\n\nSelf-help Available N/A Available\n\nForfeiture N/A Unavailable unless injunction will not relieve servient estate At English common law, a landowner's right to light was protected as a prescriptive negative easement under the Doctrine of Ancient Lights. This doctrine has been rejected in the United States. Courts also generally decline to recognize blockage of light as a public or private nuisance unless the blockage was motivated by malice. As a result, landowners only have a right to light if they are protected by statute or enter into an express agreement with the other landowner to create a negative easement (ie, restrictive covenant). Here, the architect sought to prevent the developer from blocking the light to the architect's building. However, there is no indication that the developer is erecting a high-rise building out of malice or that the architect has obtained a negative easement or covenant from the developer. There is also no statute prohibiting interference with the light. Therefore, the court should grant judgment for the developer because the architect has no legal right to the light reaching her windows (Choices A & D). (Choice B) The fact that the developer had secured all the necessary governmental approvals does not allow her to bar others from exercising their legal rights. Therefore, this fact would not prevent the architect from prevailing in a lawsuit had she had a legal right to continued access to the light.","conclusion":"Answer C: Grant judgment for the developer, because the architect has no legal right to have sunshine continue to reach the windows of her building."},"questionZh":"一位建筑师和一位开发商拥有相邻的土地。他们的每个地块上都有一座低层办公楼。两栋办公楼高度相同。去年，开发商决定拆除其地块上的低层办公楼，并根据土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）和建筑条例的允许，在其地块上建造一座高度更高的新高层办公楼。她获得了开展她的项目所需的所有政府批准。由于开发商的新建筑正在施工过程中，建筑师意识到它所产生的阴影将使建筑师的建筑处于如此深的阴影中，以至于她可以为建筑中的空间收取的租金将大大减少。建筑师对开发商提起了适当的诉讼，要求其停止施工，以消除阴影问题并赔偿损失。建筑师提供了无可争议的证据，证明她对阴影对其建筑公平租金价值影响的评估是正确的。没有任何法规或条例（建筑和土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例除外）适用于法院审理的问题。法院应该如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"判给建筑师以租金损失来衡量的损害赔偿，但不颁发禁令。","B":"请对开发商做出判断，因为她已经为新建筑获得了所有必要的政府批准。","C":"请对开发商作出判决，因为建筑师没有合法权利让阳光继续照射到其建筑物的窗户上。","D":"授予建筑师所请求的禁令。"},"explanationZh":"滋扰和地役权诉讼的补救措施\n\n临时 永久 滥用/地役权干扰 滋扰 滋扰\n\n损害赔偿 租金减少 土地减少 收到的租金价值 服役地产价值减少 合理维修和/或地役权费用 过度使用的价值 附带损失 恢复成本 使用和享受损失 使用损失 惩罚性赔偿 禁令可用 不适用 可用\n\n自助 可用 不适用 可用\n\n没收 不适用 不可使用，除非禁令不能解除役权财产 在英国普通法中，土地所有者的采光权作为一项规定性的消极地役权，受到古代采光学说的保护。这一学说在美国已被拒绝。法院通常也拒绝将遮光视为公共或私人滋扰，除非遮光是出于恶意。因此，土地所有者只有在受到法规保护或与其他土地所有者签订明确协议以创建消极地役权（即限制性契约）的情况下才拥有采光权。在这里，建筑师试图阻止开发商遮挡建筑师建筑的光线。然而，没有迹象表明开发商出于恶意而建造高层建筑，也没有迹象表明建筑师从开发商那里获得了负地役权或契约。也没有禁止干扰光的法规。因此，法院应该对开发商作出判决，因为建筑师没有合法权利让光线到达她的窗户（选择A和D）。 （选项B）开发商已获得所有必要的政府批准这一事实并不意味着她可以阻止其他人行使其合法权利。因此，如果建筑师拥有继续接触光线的合法权利，这一事实不会阻止建筑师在诉讼中胜诉。\n\n教育目标：只有在以下情况下，土地所有者才拥有采光权：(1) 该权利受法律保护，或者 (2) 土地所有者与其他土地所有者签订明确协议以建立消极地役权或契约。\n\n参考文献\nMohr v. Midas Realty Corp., 431 N.W.2d 380, 382 (Iowa 1988)（解释说每个州都拒绝了远古之光学说）。\n\n谢尔诉莱德曼，226 Cal。报告。 698, 701（Ct. App. 1986）（认为除非出于恶意，否则光线阻挡不属于可起诉的滋扰行为）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地所有者只有在以下情况下才享有采光权：(1) 该权利受法律保护，或 (2) 土地所有者与其他土地所有者签订明确协议以建立消极地役权或契约。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该如何判决？","rule":"土地所有者只有在以下情况下才享有采光权：(1) 该权利受法律保护，或 (2) 土地所有者与其他土地所有者签订明确协议以建立消极地役权或契约。","analysis":"滋扰和地役权诉讼的补救措施\n\n临时 永久 滥用/地役权干扰 滋扰 滋扰\n\n损害赔偿 租金减少 土地减少 收到的租金价值 服役地产价值减少 合理维修和/或地役权费用 过度使用的价值 附带损失 恢复成本 使用和享受损失 使用损失 惩罚性赔偿 禁令可用 不适用 可用\n\n自助 可用 不适用 可用\n\n没收 不适用 不可使用，除非禁令不能解除役权财产 在英国普通法中，土地所有者的采光权作为一项规定性的消极地役权，受到古代采光学说的保护。这一学说在美国已被拒绝。法院通常也拒绝将遮光视为公共或私人滋扰，除非遮光是出于恶意。因此，土地所有者只有在受到法规保护或与其他土地所有者签订明确协议以创建消极地役权（即限制性契约）的情况下才拥有采光权。在这里，建筑师试图阻止开发商遮挡建筑师建筑的光线。然而，没有迹象表明开发商出于恶意而建造高层建筑，也没有迹象表明建筑师从开发商那里获得了负地役权或契约。也没有禁止干扰光的法规。因此，法院应该对开发商作出判决，因为建筑师没有合法权利让光线到达她的窗户（选择A和D）。 （选项B）开发商已获得所有必要的政府批准这一事实并不意味着她可以阻止其他人行使其合法权利。因此，如果建筑师拥有继续接触光线的合法权利，这一事实不会阻止建筑师在诉讼中胜诉。\n\n教育目标：只有在以下情况下，土地所有者才拥有采光权：(1) 该权利受法律保护，或者 (2) 土地所有者与其他土地所有者签订明确协议以建立消极地役权或契约。\n\n参考文献\nMohr v. Midas Realty Corp., 431 N.W.2d 380, 382 (Iowa 1988)（解释说每个州都拒绝了远古之光学说）。\n\n谢尔诉莱德曼，226 Cal。报告。 698, 701（Ct. App. 1986）（认为除非出于恶意，否则光线阻挡不属于可起诉的滋扰行为）。","conclusion":"答案 C：请对开发商作出判决，因为建筑师没有合法权利让阳光继续照射到其建筑物的窗户上。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":237,"sourceQuestionPage":689,"sourceQuestionPages":[689],"sourceExplanationPages":[690,691],"sourceAnswerPages":[690,691],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-238","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A niece inherited vacant land from her uncle. She lived in a distant state and decided to sell the land to a colleague who was interested in purchasing the land as an investment. They orally agreed upon a price, and, at the colleague's insistence, the niece agreed to provide him with a warranty deed without any exceptions. The price was paid, the warranty deed was delivered, and the deed was promptly recorded. Neither the niece nor the colleague had, at that point, ever seen the land. After recording the deed, the colleague visited the land for the first time and discovered that it had no access to any public right-of-way and that none of the surrounding lands had ever been held in common ownership with any previous owner of the land. The colleague sued the niece for damages. For whom will the court find?","choices":{"A":"The colleague, because lack of access makes title unmarketable.","B":"The colleague, because the covenants of warranty and quiet enjoyment in the deed were breached.","C":"The niece, because no title covenants were breached.","D":"The niece, because the agreement to sell was oral."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Covenants of title\n\n(warranty & special warranty deeds)\n\nPresent covenants Seisin Grantor owns interest being conveyed\n\n(breached at time of Right to convey Grantor has legal right to convey interest conveyance) Against No encumbrances (eg, easements, encumbrances mortgages) against interest conveyed\n\nFuture covenants Warranty Grantor will defend & compensate grantee for lawful claims made against grantee's (breached upon title interference with possession) Quiet enjoyment Grantee's possession & enjoyment will not be disturbed by another's lawful claim of title\n\nFurther Grantor will take any actions reasonably assurances necessary to perfect grantee's title\n\nUnless otherwise stated, contracts for the sale of land impliedly warrant that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing. To be marketable, title must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Lack of access to a public road may render title unmarketable.* However, the time for the buyer to object to marketability of title is prior to delivery and acceptance of the deed—which have already occurred here (Choice A). Once a deed is delivered to and accepted by the buyer, the sales contract merges with the deed and the seller is no longer liable on the implied warranty of marketable title. If a problem arises with title thereafter, the buyer's remedy, if any, would be under an express provision or a covenant of title in the deed (listed above). Lack of access to a public road is not a breach of warranty, quiet enjoyment, or any other title covenant. The reason is that the colleague's lack of access does not detract from his ownership rights in the land or create a superior claim to the land by a third party (Choice B). As a result, the court will find in favor of the niece. *Courts are split as to whether lack of access to a public road is an encumbrance that makes title to property unmarketable.\n\n(Choice D) The statute of frauds requires that land-sale contracts be in writing to be enforceable. But since the oral land-sale contract has been executed and the colleague has accepted the deed, he can seek relief only under the deed.\n\nEducational objective: Under the merger doctrine, a contract for the sale of land merges with the deed once the deed has been delivered to and accepted by the buyer. If any problems with title arise thereafter, the buyer must rely on the covenants of title or other provisions contained within the deed.\n\nReferences\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 227 (2020) (merger doctrine).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 143 (2020) (lack of access to land).","educationalObjective":"Under the merger doctrine, a contract for the sale of land merges with the deed once the deed has been delivered to and accepted by the buyer. If any problems with title arise thereafter, the buyer must rely on the covenants of title or other provisions contained within the deed.","references":"77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 227 (2020) (merger doctrine).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 143 (2020) (lack of access to land).","rule":"Under the merger doctrine, a contract for the sale of land merges with the deed once the deed has been delivered to and accepted by the buyer. If any problems with title arise thereafter, the buyer must rely on the covenants of title or other provisions contained within the deed.","irac":{"issue":"For whom will the court find?","rule":"Under the merger doctrine, a contract for the sale of land merges with the deed once the deed has been delivered to and accepted by the buyer. If any problems with title arise thereafter, the buyer must rely on the covenants of title or other provisions contained within the deed.","analysis":"Covenants of title\n\n(warranty & special warranty deeds)\n\nPresent covenants Seisin Grantor owns interest being conveyed\n\n(breached at time of Right to convey Grantor has legal right to convey interest conveyance) Against No encumbrances (eg, easements, encumbrances mortgages) against interest conveyed\n\nFuture covenants Warranty Grantor will defend & compensate grantee for lawful claims made against grantee's (breached upon title interference with possession) Quiet enjoyment Grantee's possession & enjoyment will not be disturbed by another's lawful claim of title\n\nFurther Grantor will take any actions reasonably assurances necessary to perfect grantee's title\n\nUnless otherwise stated, contracts for the sale of land impliedly warrant that the seller will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing. To be marketable, title must be reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation, such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Lack of access to a public road may render title unmarketable.* However, the time for the buyer to object to marketability of title is prior to delivery and acceptance of the deed—which have already occurred here (Choice A). Once a deed is delivered to and accepted by the buyer, the sales contract merges with the deed and the seller is no longer liable on the implied warranty of marketable title. If a problem arises with title thereafter, the buyer's remedy, if any, would be under an express provision or a covenant of title in the deed (listed above). Lack of access to a public road is not a breach of warranty, quiet enjoyment, or any other title covenant. The reason is that the colleague's lack of access does not detract from his ownership rights in the land or create a superior claim to the land by a third party (Choice B). As a result, the court will find in favor of the niece. *Courts are split as to whether lack of access to a public road is an encumbrance that makes title to property unmarketable.\n\n(Choice D) The statute of frauds requires that land-sale contracts be in writing to be enforceable. But since the oral land-sale contract has been executed and the colleague has accepted the deed, he can seek relief only under the deed.","conclusion":"Answer C: The niece, because no title covenants were breached."},"questionZh":"一个侄女从她叔叔那里继承了空置土地。她住在一个遥远的州，决定将土地卖给一位有兴趣购买土地作为投资的同事。他们口头商定了价格，在同事的坚持下，侄女同意毫无例外地向他提供保修契约。价格已支付，保修契约已交付，并且契约已立即记录。那时，侄女和同事都没有见过这片土地。记录完契约后，这位同事第一次参观了这块土地，发现它没有任何公共通行权，而且周围的土地从未与任何以前的土地所有者共同拥有。同事起诉侄女要求赔偿。法庭会为谁判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"这位同事，因为缺乏访问权限导致该头衔滞销。","B":"这位同事，因为契约中的保证和安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）的契约被违反。","C":"侄女，因为没有违反所有权契约。","D":"侄女，因为出售协议是口头的。"},"explanationZh":"所有权契约\n\n（保修和特殊保修契约）\n\n目前的契约 Seisin Grantor 拥有正在转让的权益\n\n（在转让权时违反 授予人有转让利息的合法权利） 所转让的利息无产权负担（例如地役权、抵押贷款）\n\n未来契约 保证 授予人将为受让人针对受让人提出的合法索赔进行辩护并赔偿（因所有权干扰占有而违反） 安静的享受 受让人的占有和享受不会受到他人合法所有权主张的干扰\n\n此外，授予人将采取任何必要的行动合理保证完善受让人的所有权\n\n除非另有说明，土地销售合同默示保证卖方在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。为了便于销售，所有权必须合理地没有疑问，并且不受诉讼威胁，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。缺乏通往公共道路的通道可能会导致产权无法出售。*然而，买方反对产权适销性的时间是在交付和接受契约之前——这已经发生在这里（选择 A）。一旦契约交付给买方并被买方接受，销售合同就与契约合并，卖方不再对适销所有权的默示保证承担责任。如果此后出现所有权问题，买方的补救措施（如果有）将根据契约中的明确规定或所有权契约（如上所述）进行。无法进入公共道路并不违反保证、安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）或任何其他所有权契约。原因是，该同事无法进入该土地并不会减损他对土地的所有权，也不会造成第三方对土地的优先主张（选择 B）。因此，法院将作出有利于侄女的裁决。 *对于无法进入公共道路是否是导致财产所有权无法出售的一种负担，法院存在分歧。\n\n（选项 D） 欺诈法要求土地买卖合同必须采用书面形式才能强制执行。但由于口头卖地合同已经履行，同事也接受了契约，他只能根据契约寻求救济。\n\n教育目标：根据合并原则，一旦契约交付给买方并被买方接受，土地出售合同就与契约合并。如果此后出现任何所有权问题，买方必须依赖所有权契约或契约中包含的其他条款。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 227 (2020)（合并原则）。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 143 (2020)（缺乏土地使用权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据合并原则，一旦契约被交付给买方并被买方接受，土地出售合同就与契约合并。如果此后出现任何所有权问题，买方必须依赖所有权契约或契约中包含的其他条款。","iracZh":{"issue":"法庭会为谁判决？","rule":"根据合并原则，一旦契约被交付给买方并被买方接受，土地出售合同就与契约合并。如果此后出现任何所有权问题，买方必须依赖所有权契约或契约中包含的其他条款。","analysis":"所有权契约\n\n（保修和特殊保修契约）\n\n目前的契约 Seisin Grantor 拥有正在转让的权益\n\n（在转让权时违反 授予人有转让利息的合法权利） 所转让的利息无产权负担（例如地役权、抵押贷款）\n\n未来契约 保证 授予人将为受让人针对受让人提出的合法索赔进行辩护并赔偿（因所有权干扰占有而违反） 安静的享受 受让人的占有和享受不会受到他人合法所有权主张的干扰\n\n此外，授予人将采取任何必要的行动合理保证完善受让人的所有权\n\n除非另有说明，土地销售合同默示保证卖方在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。为了便于销售，所有权必须合理地没有疑问，并且不受诉讼威胁，以便理性的人会接受并支付费用。缺乏通往公共道路的通道可能会导致产权无法出售。*然而，买方反对产权适销性的时间是在交付和接受契约之前——这已经发生在这里（选择 A）。一旦契约交付给买方并被买方接受，销售合同就与契约合并，卖方不再对适销所有权的默示保证承担责任。如果此后出现所有权问题，买方的补救措施（如果有）将根据契约中的明确规定或所有权契约（如上所述）进行。无法进入公共道路并不违反保证、安宁使用权（quiet enjoyment）或任何其他所有权契约。原因是，该同事无法进入该土地并不会减损他对土地的所有权，也不会造成第三方对土地的优先主张（选择 B）。因此，法院将作出有利于侄女的裁决。 *对于无法进入公共道路是否是导致财产所有权无法出售的一种负担，法院存在分歧。\n\n（选项 D） 欺诈法要求土地买卖合同必须采用书面形式才能强制执行。但由于口头卖地合同已经履行，同事也接受了契约，他只能根据契约寻求救济。\n\n教育目标：根据合并原则，一旦契约交付给买方并被买方接受，土地出售合同就与契约合并。如果此后出现任何所有权问题，买方必须依赖所有权契约或契约中包含的其他条款。\n\n参考文献\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 227 (2020)（合并原则）。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 143 (2020)（缺乏土地使用权）。","conclusion":"答案 C：侄女，因为没有违反所有权契约。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":238,"sourceQuestionPage":692,"sourceQuestionPages":[692],"sourceExplanationPages":[693,694],"sourceAnswerPages":[693,694],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-239","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A buyer validly contracted in writing to buy improved land from a seller. The contract had no contingencies and was silent as to risk of loss if there was damage to, or destruction of, property improvements between contract and closing, and as to any duty to carry insurance. As soon as the parties signed the contract, the seller (who had already moved out) canceled her insurance covering the land. The buyer did not know this and did not obtain insurance. A few days later, three weeks before the agreed closing date, the building on the land was struck by lightning and burned to the ground. There is no applicable statute. In an appropriate action, the buyer asserted the right to cancel the contract and to recover his earnest money. The seller said that because the risk of fire loss had passed to the buyer before the fire, the buyer must perform. If the seller prevails, what will be the most likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"Once the parties signed the contract, only the buyer had an insurable interest and so could have protected against this loss.","B":"The buyer's constructive possession arising from the contract gave him the affirmative duty of protecting against loss by fire.","C":"The seller's cancellation of her casualty insurance caused the risk of loss to transfer to the buyer.","D":"Upon execution of the contract, the buyer became the equitable owner of the land under the doctrine of equitable conversion."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A majority of jurisdictions apply the doctrine of equitable conversion when a land-sale contract is silent regarding the risk of loss (as seen here). Under this doctrine, the risk of loss is placed on the party with equitable title at the time the property was destroyed. The buyer receives equitable title once the contract is formed and can be specifically enforced. If no conditions/contingencies appear in the contract (eg, a condition that the buyer secure financing before closing), the contract is specifically enforceable as soon as it is formed. Therefore, the seller likely prevailed because the buyer was the equitable owner of the land when the building was destroyed. (Choices A, B & C) Unless otherwise provided, a land-sale contract does not impose a duty on either the seller or the buyer to carry property insurance. Therefore, neither the signing of the contract nor the seller's cancellation of the insurance transferred the risk of loss to the buyer.\n\nEducational objective: Unless otherwise provided, the doctrine of equitable conversion places the risk of loss on the buyer once the land-sale contract is formed and can be specifically enforced.","educationalObjective":"Unless otherwise provided, the doctrine of equitable conversion places the risk of loss on the buyer once the land-sale contract is formed and can be specifically enforced.","references":"","rule":"Unless otherwise provided, the doctrine of equitable conversion places the risk of loss on the buyer once the land-sale contract is formed and can be specifically enforced.","irac":{"issue":"If the seller prevails, what will be the most likely explanation?","rule":"Unless otherwise provided, the doctrine of equitable conversion places the risk of loss on the buyer once the land-sale contract is formed and can be specifically enforced.","analysis":"A majority of jurisdictions apply the doctrine of equitable conversion when a land-sale contract is silent regarding the risk of loss (as seen here). Under this doctrine, the risk of loss is placed on the party with equitable title at the time the property was destroyed. The buyer receives equitable title once the contract is formed and can be specifically enforced. If no conditions/contingencies appear in the contract (eg, a condition that the buyer secure financing before closing), the contract is specifically enforceable as soon as it is formed. Therefore, the seller likely prevailed because the buyer was the equitable owner of the land when the building was destroyed. (Choices A, B & C) Unless otherwise provided, a land-sale contract does not impose a duty on either the seller or the buyer to carry property insurance. Therefore, neither the signing of the contract nor the seller's cancellation of the insurance transferred the risk of loss to the buyer.","conclusion":"Answer D: Upon execution of the contract, the buyer became the equitable owner of the land under the doctrine of equitable conversion."},"questionZh":"买方以书面形式签订有效合同，从卖方购买改良土地。该合同没有任何意外事件，也没有提及在合同签订和成交之间财产改良发生损坏或毁坏的损失风险，以及任何携带保险的义务。双方一签署合同，卖方（已搬出）就取消了该土地的保险。买方不知道这一点，也没有购买保险。几天后，距离约定的截止日期还有三周，地上的建筑物被闪电击中，被烧毁了。没有适用的法规。在适当的行动中，买方主张取消合同并收回保证金的权利。卖方表示，由于火灾损失风险在火灾发生前已转移给买方，因此买方必须履行。如果卖家胜诉，最可能的解释是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"一旦双方签订合同，只有买方拥有保险利益，因此可以免受这种损失。","B":"买方因合同产生的推定占有赋予他防止火灾造成损失的肯定义务。","C":"卖方取消了伤亡保险，导致损失风险转移给买方。","D":"合同执行后，根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，买方成为土地的公平所有者。"},"explanationZh":"当土地出售合同未提及损失风险时（如此处所示），大多数司法管辖区都适用衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则。根据这一原则，损失风险由财产被毁坏时拥有衡平法所有权的一方承担。一旦合同成立，买方就获得衡平法上的所有权，并且可以具体执行。如果合同中没有出现任何条件/或有事项（例如，买方在成交前获得融资的条件），则合同一经成立即具有明确的可执行性。因此，卖方很可能获胜，因为当建筑物被毁时，买方是土地的公平所有者。 （选项 A、B 和 C）除非另有规定，土地买卖合同不要求卖方或买方承担财产保险的义务。因此，无论是合同的签订还是卖方取消保险，均未将损失风险转移给买方。\n\n教育目的：除非另有规定，一旦土地出售合同成立并可以具体执行，衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则就将损失风险置于买方身上。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非另有规定，一旦土地买卖合同成立并可以具体执行，衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则就将损失风险置于买方身上。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果卖家胜诉，最可能的解释是什么？","rule":"除非另有规定，一旦土地买卖合同成立并可以具体执行，衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则就将损失风险置于买方身上。","analysis":"当土地出售合同未提及损失风险时（如此处所示），大多数司法管辖区都适用衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则。根据这一原则，损失风险由财产被毁坏时拥有衡平法所有权的一方承担。一旦合同成立，买方就获得衡平法上的所有权，并且可以具体执行。如果合同中没有出现任何条件/或有事项（例如，买方在成交前获得融资的条件），则合同一经成立即具有明确的可执行性。因此，卖方很可能获胜，因为当建筑物被毁时，买方是土地的公平所有者。 （选项 A、B 和 C）除非另有规定，土地买卖合同不要求卖方或买方承担财产保险的义务。因此，无论是合同的签订还是卖方取消保险，均未将损失风险转移给买方。\n\n教育目的：除非另有规定，一旦土地出售合同成立并可以具体执行，衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则就将损失风险置于买方身上。","conclusion":"答案 D：合同执行后，根据衡平转换（equitable conversion）原则，买方成为土地的公平所有者。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-af603b8a5e6f8dad"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":239,"sourceQuestionPage":695,"sourceQuestionPages":[695],"sourceExplanationPages":[696],"sourceAnswerPages":[696],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-240","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A landowner lawfully subdivided his land into 10 large lots. The recorded subdivision plan imposed no restrictions on any of the 10 lots. Within two months after recording the plan, the landowner conveyed Lot 1 to a buyer, by a deed that contained no restrictions on the lot's use. There was then a lull in sales. Two years later, the real estate market in the state had generally improved, and during the next six months the landowner sold and conveyed eight of the remaining nine lots. In each of the eight deeds of conveyance, the landowner included the following language: \"It is a term and condition of this conveyance, which shall be a covenant running with the land for the benefit of each of the 10 lots [with an appropriate reference to the recorded subdivision plan], that for 15 years from the date of recording of the plan, no use shall be made of the premises herein conveyed except for single-family residential purposes.\" The buyer of Lot 1 had actual knowledge of what the landowner had done. The landowner included the quoted language in part because the municipality had amended its zoning ordinance a year earlier to permit professional offices in any residential zone. Shortly after the landowner's most recent sale, when he owned only one unsold lot, the buyer of Lot 1 constructed a one-story house on Lot 1 and then conveyed Lot 1 to a doctor. The deed to the doctor contained no reference to any restriction on the use of Lot 1. The doctor applied for an appropriate certificate of occupancy to enable her to use a part of the house on Lot 1 as a medical office. The landowner, on behalf of himself as the owner of the unsold lot, and on behalf of the other lot owners, sued to enjoin the doctor from carrying out her plans and to impose the quoted restriction on Lot 1. Who is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The doctor, because Lot 1 was conveyed without the restrictive covenant in the deed to the first buyer and the subsequent deed to the doctor.","B":"The doctor, because zoning ordinances override private restrictive covenants as a matter of public policy.","C":"The landowner, because the doctor, as a successor in interest to the first buyer, is estopped from denying that Lot 1 remains subject to the zoning ordinance as it existed when the landowner conveyed Lot 1 to the first buyer.","D":"The landowner, because with the first buyer's knowledge of the facts, Lot 1 became incorporated into a common scheme."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes\n\nReal covenants Equitable servitudes\n\n(enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief)\n\nFor burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied\n\nWriting Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice privity privity Notice Notice No notice required Equitable servitudes are covenants (ie, promises) regarding land that are enforceable in equity (eg, by injunction) against the promising parties and their successors in interest. They are typically created by an express written agreement. But one can be implied from a common scheme* in a planned subdivision if three elements are met: Intent to create common scheme – the owner intended to impose a servitude on all lots in the subdivision (eg, most of the lot deeds contain common restrictions)\n\nRestrictive servitude – the intended servitude is a promise not to do something on land\n\nNotice – the person to be bound by the servitude had actual, record, or inquiry notice of it\n\nIf the common scheme arises after some of the lots have already been sold, those previously sold lots will not be incorporated into the common scheme or subject to the implied servitude (Choice D). Here, the landowner subdivided his land into 10 lots. He first conveyed Lot 1 to a buyer by a deed that contained no restrictions on the lot's use. The landowner then conveyed the next eight lots by deeds that contained covenants restricting the lots' use to residential purposes. But since that common scheme did not exist when Lot 1 was first conveyed to the buyer, there is no implied servitude on Lot 1. And since the doctor's deed for Lot 1 contained no express restrictions on the lot's use, she will likely prevail. *Equitable servitudes implied from a common scheme of development are also known as implied reciprocal negative easements or implied reciprocal servitudes. (Choice B) Zoning ordinances override restrictive covenants only if the two are in direct conflict (eg, covenants restricting use to commercial purposes in areas zoned only for residential use).\n\n(Choice C) The doctor's use of Lot 1 is governed by the zoning in existence during her ownership of Lot 1—not the zoning as it existed when the landowner first conveyed Lot 1 to the buyer.\n\nEducational objective: For an equitable servitude to be implied from a common scheme, (1) the owner must have intended to impose a servitude on all lots in the subdivision, (2) the servitude must be restrictive, and (3) the person to be bound must have had notice of the servitude.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.14 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (equitable servitude implied from a common scheme in a residential subdivision).","educationalObjective":"For an equitable servitude to be implied from a common scheme, (1) the owner must have intended to impose a servitude on all lots in the subdivision, (2) the servitude must be restrictive, and (3) the person to be bound must have had notice of the servitude.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 2.14 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (equitable servitude implied from a common scheme in a residential subdivision).","rule":"For an equitable servitude to be implied from a common scheme, (1) the owner must have intended to impose a servitude on all lots in the subdivision, (2) the servitude must be restrictive, and (3) the person to be bound must have had notice of the servitude.","irac":{"issue":"Who is likely to prevail?","rule":"For an equitable servitude to be implied from a common scheme, (1) the owner must have intended to impose a servitude on all lots in the subdivision, (2) the servitude must be restrictive, and (3) the person to be bound must have had notice of the servitude.","analysis":"Elements of real covenants & equitable servitudes\n\nReal covenants Equitable servitudes\n\n(enforceable by money damages) (enforceable by equitable relief)\n\nFor burden to run For benefit to run Express Implied\n\nWriting Writing Writing Intent to create common Intent to run Intent to run Intent to run scheme Touch & concern Touch & concern Touch & Restrictive servitude Horizontal & vertical Limited vertical concern Notice privity privity Notice Notice No notice required Equitable servitudes are covenants (ie, promises) regarding land that are enforceable in equity (eg, by injunction) against the promising parties and their successors in interest. They are typically created by an express written agreement. But one can be implied from a common scheme* in a planned subdivision if three elements are met: Intent to create common scheme – the owner intended to impose a servitude on all lots in the subdivision (eg, most of the lot deeds contain common restrictions)\n\nRestrictive servitude – the intended servitude is a promise not to do something on land\n\nNotice – the person to be bound by the servitude had actual, record, or inquiry notice of it\n\nIf the common scheme arises after some of the lots have already been sold, those previously sold lots will not be incorporated into the common scheme or subject to the implied servitude (Choice D). Here, the landowner subdivided his land into 10 lots. He first conveyed Lot 1 to a buyer by a deed that contained no restrictions on the lot's use. The landowner then conveyed the next eight lots by deeds that contained covenants restricting the lots' use to residential purposes. But since that common scheme did not exist when Lot 1 was first conveyed to the buyer, there is no implied servitude on Lot 1. And since the doctor's deed for Lot 1 contained no express restrictions on the lot's use, she will likely prevail. *Equitable servitudes implied from a common scheme of development are also known as implied reciprocal negative easements or implied reciprocal servitudes. (Choice B) Zoning ordinances override restrictive covenants only if the two are in direct conflict (eg, covenants restricting use to commercial purposes in areas zoned only for residential use).\n\n(Choice C) The doctor's use of Lot 1 is governed by the zoning in existence during her ownership of Lot 1—not the zoning as it existed when the landowner first conveyed Lot 1 to the buyer.","conclusion":"Answer A: The doctor, because Lot 1 was conveyed without the restrictive covenant in the deed to the first buyer and the subsequent deed to the doctor."},"questionZh":"一位地主合法地将他的土地细分为 10 大块土地。记录的细分计划对这 10 个地块中的任何一个都没有施加任何限制。记录计划后两个月内，土地所有者通过一份不限制该地块使用的契约将 1 号地块转让给买家。随后销售陷入停滞。两年后，该州的房地产市场总体有所改善，在接下来的六个月里，土地所有者出售并转让了剩余九块土地中的八块。在这八份转让契约中，土地所有者均包含以下内容：“这是该转让契约的一项条款和条件，应是为了 10 块土地中每一块土地的利益而与土地一起运行的契约（适当参考已记录的分割计划），自计划记录之日起 15 年内，除单户住宅用途外，不得使用此处转让的房屋。”第 1 号地块的买家实际了解土地所有者的所作所为。土地所有者引用了上述语言，部分原因是市政府一年前修改了土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例，允许在任何住宅区设立专业办公室。土地所有者最近一次出售后不久，当他只拥有一处未售出的地块时，1 号地块的买家在 1 号地块上建造了一栋单层房屋，然后将 1 号地块转让给医生。给医生的契约中没有提及对 1 号地块的使用有任何限制。医生申请了适当的占用证书，以便她能够将 1 号地块上的房屋的一部分用作医疗办公室。土地所有者代表自己（作为未售出地块的所有者）并代表其他地块所有者提起诉讼，要求禁止医生执行她的计划并对 1 号地块实施所引述的限制。谁可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"医生，因为第 1 批地契是在没有限制性契约的情况下转让给第一个买家和随后的医生的。","B":"医生，因为作为公共政策问题，土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例凌驾于私人限制性契约之上。","C":"土地所有者，因为医生作为第一个买家的利益继承人，不得否认第 1 号地块仍受土地所有者将第 1 号地块转让给第一个买家时存在的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例的约束。","D":"土地所有者，因为第一个买家了解了事实，1号地块被纳入一个共同计划中。"},"explanationZh":"真实契约和公平劳役的要素\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant） 公平的劳役\n\n（可通过金钱赔偿强制执行）（可通过衡平法救济强制执行）\n\n为了负担而运行 为了利益而运行明示暗示\n\n写作 写作 写作 意图创建共同 意图运行 意图运行 意图运行计划 接触与关注 接触与关注 接触与限制性劳役 水平与垂直 有限垂直关注 通知 相互关系 通知 通知 无需通知 衡平法劳役是关于土地的契约（即承诺），可通过衡平法对承诺方及其利益继承人强制执行（例如通过禁令）。它们通常是通过明确的书面协议创建的。但是，如果满足三个要素，则可以从规划的细分中的共同方案*中暗示出这一点： 意图创建共同方案 - 业主打算对细分中的所有地块施加劳役（例如，大多数地契包含共同限制）\n\n限制性奴役——预期的奴役是承诺不在陆地上做某事\n\n通知——受劳役约束的人有实际的、记录的或询问的通知\n\n如果共同计划是在部分地块已售出后产生的，则那些先前出售的地块将不会被纳入共同计划或受到隐含地役权的约束（选择D）。在这里，地主将他的土地细分为10块地。他首先通过一份不限制该地块使用的契约将 1 号地块转让给买家。然后，土地所有者通过契约转让了接下来的八块土地，其中包含限制这些土地用于住宅目的的契约。但由于当第 1 号地块首次转让给买方时，该共同方案并不存在，因此第 1 号地块不存在隐含的奴役权。而且由于医生对第 1 号地块的使用契约没有明确限制该地块的使用，因此她很可能会获胜。 *共同开发计划中隐含的公平劳役也称为隐含互惠负地役权或隐含互惠劳役。 （选择B）只有当两者直接冲突时，土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例才会优先于限制性契约（例如，在仅划为住宅用途的区域中限制用于商业目的的契约）。\n\n（选项 C）医生对 1 号地块的使用受她拥有 1 号地块期间存在的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）管辖，而不是土地所有者首次将 1 号地块转让给买方时存在的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）。\n\n教育目标：为了从共同方案中隐含公平的劳役，(1) 业主必须有意对细分中的所有地块施加劳役，(2) 劳役必须是限制性的，(3) 受约束的人必须已经知道劳役。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.14（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（住宅土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）中的共同计划隐含的公平劳役）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为了从共同方案中隐含公平的劳役，(1) 所有人必须有意对细分中的所有地块施加劳役，(2) 劳役必须是限制性的，以及 (3) 受约束人必须已经知道劳役的通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"谁有可能获胜？","rule":"为了从共同方案中隐含公平的劳役，(1) 所有人必须有意对细分中的所有地块施加劳役，(2) 劳役必须是限制性的，以及 (3) 受约束人必须已经知道劳役的通知。","analysis":"真实契约和公平劳役的要素\n\n土地实质契约（real covenant） 公平的劳役\n\n（可通过金钱赔偿强制执行）（可通过衡平法救济强制执行）\n\n为了负担而运行 为了利益而运行明示暗示\n\n写作 写作 写作 意图创建共同 意图运行 意图运行 意图运行计划 接触与关注 接触与关注 接触与限制性劳役 水平与垂直 有限垂直关注 通知 相互关系 通知 通知 无需通知 衡平法劳役是关于土地的契约（即承诺），可通过衡平法对承诺方及其利益继承人强制执行（例如通过禁令）。它们通常是通过明确的书面协议创建的。但是，如果满足三个要素，则可以从规划的细分中的共同方案*中暗示出这一点： 意图创建共同方案 - 业主打算对细分中的所有地块施加劳役（例如，大多数地契包含共同限制）\n\n限制性奴役——预期的奴役是承诺不在陆地上做某事\n\n通知——受劳役约束的人有实际的、记录的或询问的通知\n\n如果共同计划是在部分地块已售出后产生的，则那些先前出售的地块将不会被纳入共同计划或受到隐含地役权的约束（选择D）。在这里，地主将他的土地细分为10块地。他首先通过一份不限制该地块使用的契约将 1 号地块转让给买家。然后，土地所有者通过契约转让了接下来的八块土地，其中包含限制这些土地用于住宅目的的契约。但由于当第 1 号地块首次转让给买方时，该共同方案并不存在，因此第 1 号地块不存在隐含的奴役权。而且由于医生对第 1 号地块的使用契约没有明确限制该地块的使用，因此她很可能会获胜。 *共同开发计划中隐含的公平劳役也称为隐含互惠负地役权或隐含互惠劳役。 （选择B）只有当两者直接冲突时，土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例才会优先于限制性契约（例如，在仅划为住宅用途的区域中限制用于商业目的的契约）。\n\n（选项 C）医生对 1 号地块的使用受她拥有 1 号地块期间存在的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）管辖，而不是土地所有者首次将 1 号地块转让给买方时存在的土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）。\n\n教育目标：为了从共同方案中隐含公平的劳役，(1) 业主必须有意对细分中的所有地块施加劳役，(2) 劳役必须是限制性的，(3) 受约束的人必须已经知道劳役。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 2.14（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（住宅土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）中的共同计划隐含的公平劳役）。","conclusion":"答案 A：医生，因为第 1 批地契是在没有限制性契约的情况下转让给第一个买家和随后的医生的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":240,"sourceQuestionPage":697,"sourceQuestionPages":[697],"sourceExplanationPages":[698,699],"sourceAnswerPages":[698,699],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-241","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Mortgages & Foreclosure","question":"A rancher was the owner of a large equestrian facility. The rancher entered into a binding written contract with a company for the sale and purchase of the facility for $500,000. The contract required the rancher to convey marketable record title. The company decided to protect its interest and promptly and properly recorded the contract. Thereafter, but before the date scheduled for the closing, a trainer obtained and properly filed a final judgment against the rancher in the amount of $1 million in a personal-injury suit. A statute in the jurisdiction provides: \"Any judgment properly filed shall, for ten years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered.\" The recording act of the jurisdiction authorizes recording of contracts and also provides: \"No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law.\" There are no other relevant statutory provisions. At the closing, the company declined to accept the title of the rancher on the ground that the trainer's judgment lien encumbered the title it would receive and rendered it unmarketable. The rancher brought an appropriate action against the company for specific performance of the contract and joined the trainer as a party. In this action, for whom will the court render judgment?","choices":{"A":"The company, because the rancher cannot benefit from the company's action in recording the contract.","B":"The company, because the statute creating judgment liens takes precedence over the recording act.","C":"The rancher, because in equity a purchaser takes free of judgment liens.","D":"The rancher, because the contract had been recorded."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under a land sales contract, a buyer must receive marketable title—ie, title that is free from substantial defects at the time of closing. Competing property interests render title unmarketable unless the buyer's interest has priority over those competing interests. For example, a buyer with a recorded equitable interest has priority over a subsequent judgment lien. Here, the company received equitable title—ie, the right to receive legal title upon the performance of an obligation—when the company and the rancher entered into a land sales contract. The company then recorded that contract before the trainer obtained the judgment lien. As a result, the company's property interest has priority over the trainer's competing interest, and the rancher can convey marketable title. The court should therefore render judgment for the rancher and order the company to specifically perform the land sales contract. (Choice A) The rancher can benefit from the company's action in recording the contract since that action ensured that the rancher was able to convey marketable title. (Choice B) The statute creating judgment liens permits a recorded judgment to be a lien on real property owned by the person liable under that judgment. It does not take precedence over the recording act, which determines the priority of competing property interests (including judgment liens). (Choice C) Equity does not require that purchasers (eg, the company) always take free of judgment liens. But here, the company did take free of the trainer's judgment lien because the company got priority when it recorded the land sales contract before the trainer acquired a competing interest.\n\nEducational objective: A buyer receives marketable title if the buyer's interest has priority over competing interests in the same property. And a buyer with a recorded equitable interest has priority over a subsequent judgment lien.\n\nReferences\n46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 352 (2018) (priority of judgment liens).","educationalObjective":"A buyer receives marketable title if the buyer's interest has priority over competing interests in the same property. And a buyer with a recorded equitable interest has priority over a subsequent judgment lien.","references":"46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 352 (2018) (priority of judgment liens).","rule":"A buyer receives marketable title if the buyer's interest has priority over competing interests in the same property. And a buyer with a recorded equitable interest has priority over a subsequent judgment lien.","irac":{"issue":"In this action, for whom will the court render judgment?","rule":"A buyer receives marketable title if the buyer's interest has priority over competing interests in the same property. And a buyer with a recorded equitable interest has priority over a subsequent judgment lien.","analysis":"Under a land sales contract, a buyer must receive marketable title—ie, title that is free from substantial defects at the time of closing. Competing property interests render title unmarketable unless the buyer's interest has priority over those competing interests. For example, a buyer with a recorded equitable interest has priority over a subsequent judgment lien. Here, the company received equitable title—ie, the right to receive legal title upon the performance of an obligation—when the company and the rancher entered into a land sales contract. The company then recorded that contract before the trainer obtained the judgment lien. As a result, the company's property interest has priority over the trainer's competing interest, and the rancher can convey marketable title. The court should therefore render judgment for the rancher and order the company to specifically perform the land sales contract. (Choice A) The rancher can benefit from the company's action in recording the contract since that action ensured that the rancher was able to convey marketable title. (Choice B) The statute creating judgment liens permits a recorded judgment to be a lien on real property owned by the person liable under that judgment. It does not take precedence over the recording act, which determines the priority of competing property interests (including judgment liens). (Choice C) Equity does not require that purchasers (eg, the company) always take free of judgment liens. But here, the company did take free of the trainer's judgment lien because the company got priority when it recorded the land sales contract before the trainer acquired a competing interest.","conclusion":"Answer D: The rancher, because the contract had been recorded."},"questionZh":"一位牧场主是一家大型马术设施的所有者。牧场主与一家公司签订了一份具有约束力的书面合同，以 50 万美元的价格出售和购买该设施。合同要求牧场主转让可销售的记录所有权。公司决定保护自己的利益，并及时、正确地记录了合同。此后，但在预定的交割日期之前，一名培训师在人身伤害诉讼中获得并正确提交了针对牧场主的 100 万美元的最终判决。该司法管辖区的一项法规规定：“任何正确提交的判决，自提交之日起十年内，应成为该判决所针对的任何人当时拥有或随后获得的不动产的留置权。”该司法管辖区的记录法授权记录合同，并规定：“除非依法记录，否则任何不动产转让或抵押均不得在未经通知的情况下对后续购买者有利于后续购买者的价值。”没有其他相关的法律规定。交易结束时，该公司拒绝接受牧场主的所有权，理由是培训师的判决留置权阻碍了其将获得的所有权，并使其无法销售。牧场主因具体履行合同而对该公司提起适当诉讼，并加入培训师作为当事人。在这起诉讼中，法院将判决谁呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"公司，因为牧场主无法从公司记录合同的行为中受益。","B":"公司，因为创建判决留置权的法规优先于记录行为。","C":"牧场主，因为在股权中，购买者不受判决留置权的影响。","D":"牧场主，因为合同已经备案了。"},"explanationZh":"根据土地销售合同，买方必须获得可出售的所有权，即在成交时不存在重大缺陷的所有权。相互竞争的财产利益使得所有权无法出售，除非买方的利益优先于那些相互竞争的利益。例如，具有记录衡平法权益的买方优先于随后的判决留置权。在这里，当公司和牧场主签订土地销售合同时，公司获得了衡平法上的所有权，即在履行义务后获得法定所有权的权利。然后，在培训师获得判决留置权之前，该公司记录了该合同。因此，公司的财产利益优先于培训师的竞争利益，牧场主可以转让适销对路的所有权。因此，法院应当对牧场主作出判决，责令该公司具体履行土地出让合同。 （选择 A） 牧场主可以从公司记录合同的行为中受益，因为该行为确保牧场主能够转让可销售的所有权。 （选择 B） 设立判决留置权的法规允许记录判决作为该判决下责任人所拥有的不动产的留置权。它并不优先于记录法，记录法决定了竞争性财产利益（包括判决留置权）的优先权。 （选择C） 股本并不要求购买者（例如公司）始终享有无判决留置权。但在这里，公司确实免除了培训师的判断留置权，因为公司在培训师获得竞争利益之前记录土地销售合同时获得了优先权。\n\n教育目标：如果买方的利益优先于同一财产中的竞争利益，则买方将获得可出售的所有权。具有记录衡平法利益的买方优先于随后的判决留置权。\n\n参考文献\n46 上午。尤尔。 2d 判决 § 352 (2018)（判决留置权的优先权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果买方的利益优先于同一财产中的竞争利益，则买方将获得可出售的所有权。具有记录衡平法利益的买方优先于随后的判决留置权。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这起诉讼中，法院将判决谁呢？","rule":"如果买方的利益优先于同一财产中的竞争利益，则买方将获得可出售的所有权。具有记录衡平法利益的买方优先于随后的判决留置权。","analysis":"根据土地销售合同，买方必须获得可出售的所有权，即在成交时不存在重大缺陷的所有权。相互竞争的财产利益使得所有权无法出售，除非买方的利益优先于那些相互竞争的利益。例如，具有记录衡平法权益的买方优先于随后的判决留置权。在这里，当公司和牧场主签订土地销售合同时，公司获得了衡平法上的所有权，即在履行义务后获得法定所有权的权利。然后，在培训师获得判决留置权之前，该公司记录了该合同。因此，公司的财产利益优先于培训师的竞争利益，牧场主可以转让适销对路的所有权。因此，法院应当对牧场主作出判决，责令该公司具体履行土地出让合同。 （选择 A） 牧场主可以从公司记录合同的行为中受益，因为该行为确保牧场主能够转让可销售的所有权。 （选择 B） 设立判决留置权的法规允许记录判决作为该判决下责任人所拥有的不动产的留置权。它并不优先于记录法，记录法决定了竞争性财产利益（包括判决留置权）的优先权。 （选择C） 股本并不要求购买者（例如公司）始终享有无判决留置权。但在这里，公司确实免除了培训师的判断留置权，因为公司在培训师获得竞争利益之前记录土地销售合同时获得了优先权。\n\n教育目标：如果买方的利益优先于同一财产中的竞争利益，则买方将获得可出售的所有权。具有记录衡平法利益的买方优先于随后的判决留置权。\n\n参考文献\n46 上午。尤尔。 2d 判决 § 352 (2018)（判决留置权的优先权）。","conclusion":"答案 D：牧场主，因为合同已经备案了。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-3dc5990ac3417406"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":241,"sourceQuestionPage":700,"sourceQuestionPages":[700],"sourceExplanationPages":[701,702],"sourceAnswerPages":[701,702],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-242","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Real Property","question":"A man owned a residential lot improved with a dwelling and conveyed it for a valuable consideration to a buyer. The dwelling had been constructed by a prior owner. The buyer had inspected the dwelling prior to the purchase and discovered no defects. After moving in, the buyer became aware that sewage seeped into the basement when the toilets were flushed. The man said that this defect had been present for years and that he had taken no steps to hide this fact from the buyer. The buyer paid for the necessary repairs and brought an appropriate action against the man to recover his cost of repair. If the buyer wins, what will be the likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The buyer made a proper inspection.","B":"The man breached the implied warranty of habitability and fitness for purpose.","C":"The man failed to disclose a latent defect.","D":"The situation constitutes a health hazard."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Seller's duty to disclose\n\nCommon law Caveat emptor No duty to disclose property defects unless (minority) rule otherwise provided\n\nModern (majority) Commercial rule property\n\nResidential Must disclose known material defects that property buyer could not reasonably discover\n\nException: \"as is\" clause or specific disclaimers without seller's fraud The majority of jurisdictions impose a duty on sellers of residential property to disclose any material defects that are known to the seller and cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer. A defect is material if it: substantially affects the value of the residence impacts the health or safety of a resident or affects the desirability of the residence to the buyer. Here, the man admitted that he had known for years that sewage seeped into the basement when the toilets were flushed. This clearly material defect could not be, and was not, reasonably discovered by the buyer after inspecting the dwelling. Therefore, the buyer will likely win because the man breached his duty to disclose this latent defect. (Choice A) A proper or reasonable inspection may not unearth every defect with a property. As a result, the duty to disclose defects that are not readily discoverable applies even when a buyer conducts an inspection. (Choice B) The warranty of habitability and fitness* is implied in almost all jurisdictions in a contract for the sale of a newly constructed residence, so it does not apply here. *This is also referred to as the implied warranty of suitability, quality, or workmanlike construction. (Choice D) The mere fact that the defect in question poses a health hazard is not enough to impose liability on the seller. For example, the man would not be liable for the hazardous defect if he had been unaware of the defect or if he had included an \"as is\" clause to disclaim the duty to disclose.\n\nEducational objective: A seller of residential property has a duty to disclose any known material defects that cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer.","educationalObjective":"A seller of residential property has a duty to disclose any known material defects that cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer.","references":"","rule":"A seller of residential property has a duty to disclose any known material defects that cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer.","irac":{"issue":"If the buyer wins, what will be the likely reason?","rule":"A seller of residential property has a duty to disclose any known material defects that cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer.","analysis":"Seller's duty to disclose\n\nCommon law Caveat emptor No duty to disclose property defects unless (minority) rule otherwise provided\n\nModern (majority) Commercial rule property\n\nResidential Must disclose known material defects that property buyer could not reasonably discover\n\nException: \"as is\" clause or specific disclaimers without seller's fraud The majority of jurisdictions impose a duty on sellers of residential property to disclose any material defects that are known to the seller and cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer. A defect is material if it: substantially affects the value of the residence impacts the health or safety of a resident or affects the desirability of the residence to the buyer. Here, the man admitted that he had known for years that sewage seeped into the basement when the toilets were flushed. This clearly material defect could not be, and was not, reasonably discovered by the buyer after inspecting the dwelling. Therefore, the buyer will likely win because the man breached his duty to disclose this latent defect. (Choice A) A proper or reasonable inspection may not unearth every defect with a property. As a result, the duty to disclose defects that are not readily discoverable applies even when a buyer conducts an inspection. (Choice B) The warranty of habitability and fitness* is implied in almost all jurisdictions in a contract for the sale of a newly constructed residence, so it does not apply here. *This is also referred to as the implied warranty of suitability, quality, or workmanlike construction. (Choice D) The mere fact that the defect in question poses a health hazard is not enough to impose liability on the seller. For example, the man would not be liable for the hazardous defect if he had been unaware of the defect or if he had included an \"as is\" clause to disclaim the duty to disclose.","conclusion":"Answer C: The man failed to disclose a latent defect."},"questionZh":"一名男子拥有一块经过改造的住宅区，并以有价值的代价将其转让给买家。该住宅是由前任业主建造的。买家在购买前已检查过该住宅，没有发现任何缺陷。入住后，买家发现冲厕所时污水渗入地下室。该男子表示，这种缺陷已经存在多年，并且他没有采取任何措施向买家隐瞒这一事实。买方支付了必要的维修费用，并向该男子提起适当的诉讼，以收回维修费用。如果买家获胜，可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"买方进行了适当的检查。","B":"该男子违反了可居住性和适用性的默示保证。","C":"该男子未能透露潜在的缺陷。","D":"这种情况构成健康危害。"},"explanationZh":"卖方的披露义务\n\n普通法 买者自负 没有义务披露财产缺陷，除非（少数）规则另有规定\n\n现代（多数）商业规则财产\n\n住宅 必须披露购房者无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷\n\n例外：“按原样”条款或无卖方欺诈的特定免责声明 大多数司法管辖区规定住宅物业的卖方有义务披露卖方已知且买方无法合理发现的任何重大缺陷。如果缺陷符合以下条件，则该缺陷属于重大缺陷： 严重影响住宅的价值 影响居民的健康或安全或影响买方对住宅的需求。在这里，该男子承认，他多年来就知道冲厕所时污水会渗入地下室。买方在检查住宅后不可能也没有合理地发现这一明显的材料缺陷。因此，买方很可能会获胜，因为该男子违反了披露这一潜在缺陷的义务。 （选择 A）适当或合理的检查可能无法发现财产的所有缺陷。因此，即使买方进行检查，披露不易发现的缺陷的义务也适用。 （选项B）几乎所有司法管辖区的新建住宅销售合同中都隐含了可居住性和适用性*的保证，因此不适用于此处。 *这也称为适用性、质量或工艺结构的默示保证。 （选择 D） 所涉缺陷造成健康危害这一事实不足以让卖方承担责任。例如，如果该人不知道该缺陷，或者如果他包含了“按现状”条款来放弃披露义务，则该人将不对危险缺陷承担责任。\n\n教育目标：住宅物业的卖方有义务披露买方无法合理发现的任何已知材料缺陷。","educationalObjectiveZh":"住宅物业的卖方有义务披露买方无法合理发现的任何已知材料缺陷。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果买家获胜，可能的原因是什么？","rule":"住宅物业的卖方有义务披露买方无法合理发现的任何已知材料缺陷。","analysis":"卖方的披露义务\n\n普通法 买者自负 没有义务披露财产缺陷，除非（少数）规则另有规定\n\n现代（多数）商业规则财产\n\n住宅 必须披露购房者无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷\n\n例外：“按原样”条款或无卖方欺诈的特定免责声明 大多数司法管辖区规定住宅物业的卖方有义务披露卖方已知且买方无法合理发现的任何重大缺陷。如果缺陷符合以下条件，则该缺陷属于重大缺陷： 严重影响住宅的价值 影响居民的健康或安全或影响买方对住宅的需求。在这里，该男子承认，他多年来就知道冲厕所时污水会渗入地下室。买方在检查住宅后不可能也没有合理地发现这一明显的材料缺陷。因此，买方很可能会获胜，因为该男子违反了披露这一潜在缺陷的义务。 （选择 A）适当或合理的检查可能无法发现财产的所有缺陷。因此，即使买方进行检查，披露不易发现的缺陷的义务也适用。 （选项B）几乎所有司法管辖区的新建住宅销售合同中都隐含了可居住性和适用性*的保证，因此不适用于此处。 *这也称为适用性、质量或工艺结构的默示保证。 （选择 D） 所涉缺陷造成健康危害这一事实不足以让卖方承担责任。例如，如果该人不知道该缺陷，或者如果他包含了“按现状”条款来放弃披露义务，则该人将不对危险缺陷承担责任。\n\n教育目标：住宅物业的卖方有义务披露买方无法合理发现的任何已知材料缺陷。","conclusion":"答案 C：该男子未能透露潜在的缺陷。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":242,"sourceQuestionPage":703,"sourceQuestionPages":[703],"sourceExplanationPages":[704,705],"sourceAnswerPages":[704,705],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-243","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A man owned a tract of land improved with a one-family house. His friend orally offered to pay the man $250,000 for the tract and the house, the fair market value, and the man accepted. The friend paid the man $5,000 down in cash and agreed to pay the balance of $245,000 at an agreed closing time and place. Before the closing, the friend inherited another home and asked the man to return his $5,000. The man refused and, at the time set for the closing, the man tendered a good deed to the friend and declared his intention to vacate the house on the tract the next day. The man demanded that the friend complete the purchase. The friend refused. The fair market value of the tract has remained $250,000. The man has brought an appropriate action against the friend for specific performance. If the man loses, what is the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"Retaining the $5,000 down payment is the owner's exclusive remedy.","B":"The friend had a valid reason for not closing.","C":"The owner remained in possession on the day set for the closing.","D":"There was no written agreement."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Statute of frauds requirements for real estate contracts\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed\n\neg, receipt, check, emails Signature Handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged\n\neg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials Essential Typically includes: terms identity of parties words of intent to buy or sell property description sales price Real estate contracts are subject to the statute of frauds. As a result, such contracts are enforceable only if they are in writing, are signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Since the man and the friend's agreement was oral, it is unenforceable. Therefore, the lack of a written agreement is the most likely reason the man will lose. (Choice A) Most states allow sellers to retain the buyer's deposit if the buyer breaches the land-sale contract. However, most jurisdictions also allow a seller to seek specific performance to compel the buyer to pay the agreed-upon sales price.* But neither remedy is available here since the contract is unenforceable. (Choice B) A buyer's failure to close may be excused by a contingency in the agreement (eg, when closing is conditioned on the buyer's ability to obtain a loan). However, inheriting another home is not a valid reason for not closing. (Choice C) A seller must deliver a valid deed to the buyer at the time of closing. Delivery occurs when the deed is in the buyer's possession—even if the seller remains on the property at the time. Therefore, had there been an enforceable contract, the man would have satisfied his duty despite staying in the house until the following day. *A minority of jurisdiction may deny specific performance to the seller if the land is not unique and monetary damages provide adequate compensation.\n\nEducational objective: Under the statute of frauds, an oral agreement for the sale of land is unenforceable. Instead, the agreement must be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms.\n\nReferences\n73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).","educationalObjective":"Under the statute of frauds, an oral agreement for the sale of land is unenforceable. Instead, the agreement must be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms.","references":"73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).","rule":"Under the statute of frauds, an oral agreement for the sale of land is unenforceable. Instead, the agreement must be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms.","irac":{"issue":"If the man loses, what is the most likely reason?","rule":"Under the statute of frauds, an oral agreement for the sale of land is unenforceable. Instead, the agreement must be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms.","analysis":"Statute of frauds requirements for real estate contracts\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed\n\neg, receipt, check, emails Signature Handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged\n\neg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials Essential Typically includes: terms identity of parties words of intent to buy or sell property description sales price Real estate contracts are subject to the statute of frauds. As a result, such contracts are enforceable only if they are in writing, are signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Since the man and the friend's agreement was oral, it is unenforceable. Therefore, the lack of a written agreement is the most likely reason the man will lose. (Choice A) Most states allow sellers to retain the buyer's deposit if the buyer breaches the land-sale contract. However, most jurisdictions also allow a seller to seek specific performance to compel the buyer to pay the agreed-upon sales price.* But neither remedy is available here since the contract is unenforceable. (Choice B) A buyer's failure to close may be excused by a contingency in the agreement (eg, when closing is conditioned on the buyer's ability to obtain a loan). However, inheriting another home is not a valid reason for not closing. (Choice C) A seller must deliver a valid deed to the buyer at the time of closing. Delivery occurs when the deed is in the buyer's possession—even if the seller remains on the property at the time. Therefore, had there been an enforceable contract, the man would have satisfied his duty despite staying in the house until the following day. *A minority of jurisdiction may deny specific performance to the seller if the land is not unique and monetary damages provide adequate compensation.","conclusion":"Answer D: There was no written agreement."},"questionZh":"一个男人拥有一块土地，上面建有一座独户住宅。他的朋友口头提出向该男子支付 25 万美元购买这片土地和房子，这是公平的市场价值，该男子接受了。这位朋友向该男子支付了 5,000 美元的现金首付，并同意在约定的交割时间和地点支付余款 245,000 美元。交割前，这位朋友继承了另一处房产，并要求该男子归还他的 5,000 美元。该男子拒绝了，在预定的交割时间，该男子向朋友做了一件好事，并宣布他打算第二天腾出该地块上的房子。该男子要求朋友完成购买。朋友拒绝了。该土地的公平市场价值仍为 250,000 美元。该男子已针对该朋友的具体表现提起适当的诉讼。如果男人输了，最可能的原因是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"保留 5,000 美元的首付是业主的唯一补救措施。","B":"这位朋友有充分的理由不关门。","C":"在预定交割日，业主仍拥有该房屋的所有权。","D":"没有书面协议。"},"explanationZh":"房地产合同欺诈法规要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件\n\n例如，收据、支票、电子邮件 签名 被指控方的手写签名或其他识别标记\n\n例如，信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名首字母缩写 基本内容 通常包括： 条款 各方身份 购买或出售房产意向的词语 描述 销售价格 房地产合同受欺诈法的约束。因此，此类合同只有采用书面形式、由被寻求执行方签署并包含所有基本条款时才可执行。由于该男子与朋友的协议是口头协议，因此无法执行。因此，缺乏书面协议是该男子败诉的最可能原因。 （选择 A） 如果买方违反土地买卖合同，大多数州允许卖方保留买方的押金。然而，大多数司法管辖区还允许卖方寻求特定履行，以迫使买方支付商定的销售价格。*但这两种补救措施均不可用，因为合同不可执行。 （选择B） 买方未能成交可以因协议中的意外事件而得到原谅（例如，当成交以买方获得贷款的能力为条件时）。然而，继承另一个房屋并不是不关闭的正当理由。 （选择 C）卖方必须在成交时向买方交付有效的契约。当契约由买方占有时即发生交付——即使卖方当时仍在该房产内。因此，如果有一份可执行的合同，该男子尽管在房子里待到第二天，仍然履行了自己的义务。 *如果土地不是唯一的并且金钱赔偿可以提供足够的补偿，少数司法管辖区可能会拒绝向卖方履行具体义务。\n\n教育目标：根据欺诈法，口头土地出售协议是不可执行的。相反，该协议必须采用书面形式，由寻求执行的一方签署，并包含所有基本条款。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据欺诈法，口头土地出售协议不可执行。相反，该协议必须采用书面形式，由寻求执行的一方签署，并包含所有基本条款。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果男人输了，最可能的原因是什么？","rule":"根据欺诈法，口头土地出售协议不可执行。相反，该协议必须采用书面形式，由寻求执行的一方签署，并包含所有基本条款。","analysis":"房地产合同欺诈法规要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件\n\n例如，收据、支票、电子邮件 签名 被指控方的手写签名或其他识别标记\n\n例如，信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名首字母缩写 基本内容 通常包括： 条款 各方身份 购买或出售房产意向的词语 描述 销售价格 房地产合同受欺诈法的约束。因此，此类合同只有采用书面形式、由被寻求执行方签署并包含所有基本条款时才可执行。由于该男子与朋友的协议是口头协议，因此无法执行。因此，缺乏书面协议是该男子败诉的最可能原因。 （选择 A） 如果买方违反土地买卖合同，大多数州允许卖方保留买方的押金。然而，大多数司法管辖区还允许卖方寻求特定履行，以迫使买方支付商定的销售价格。*但这两种补救措施均不可用，因为合同不可执行。 （选择B） 买方未能成交可以因协议中的意外事件而得到原谅（例如，当成交以买方获得贷款的能力为条件时）。然而，继承另一个房屋并不是不关闭的正当理由。 （选择 C）卖方必须在成交时向买方交付有效的契约。当契约由买方占有时即发生交付——即使卖方当时仍在该房产内。因此，如果有一份可执行的合同，该男子尽管在房子里待到第二天，仍然履行了自己的义务。 *如果土地不是唯一的并且金钱赔偿可以提供足够的补偿，少数司法管辖区可能会拒绝向卖方履行具体义务。\n\n教育目标：根据欺诈法，口头土地出售协议是不可执行的。相反，该协议必须采用书面形式，由寻求执行的一方签署，并包含所有基本条款。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。","conclusion":"答案 D：没有书面协议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":243,"sourceQuestionPage":706,"sourceQuestionPages":[706],"sourceExplanationPages":[707,708],"sourceAnswerPages":[707,708],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-244","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A seller and a purchaser signed a contract for the sale of a 60-year-old house. The contract required a warranty deed to be given at closing. The contract was silent regarding the condition of the house, and the purchaser did not ask. The purchaser received a warranty deed with all covenants of title at the closing and promptly recorded the deed. Approximately one month after the closing, the furnace in the house stopped working, the basement flooded, and the roof leaked so badly that the second floor could not be occupied. The seller, when told of the house's condition, was genuinely surprised. There is no applicable statute. The purchaser has sued the seller for damages. Will the purchaser likely be successful?","choices":{"A":"No, because of the doctrine of merger.","B":"No, because the seller gave no warranty regarding the condition of the house.","C":"Yes, based on the covenants of title contained in the deed the purchaser received.","D":"Yes, because with a conveyance of residential real property, a warranty of fitness is implied."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Seller's duty to disclose\n\nCommon law Caveat emptor No duty to disclose property defects unless (minority) rule otherwise provided\n\nModern (majority) Commercial rule property\n\nResidential Must disclose known material defects that property buyer could not reasonably discover\n\nException: \"as is\" clause or specific disclaimers without seller's fraud The majority of jurisdictions have enacted statutes that require sellers of residential property to disclose any known material defects that cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer. But since there is no applicable statute in this jurisdiction, the common-law rule of caveat emptor controls. This rule (meaning \"let the buyer beware\") provides that sellers have no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer unless otherwise agreed. Here, the seller gave no warranties and the contract was silent as to the condition of the house, so the seller had no duty to disclose these defects to the buyer. Furthermore, the seller did not know about the defects in the house because she was \"genuinely surprised\" when she was told of the house's condition. So even if this jurisdiction had enacted a statute that followed the majority rule, the seller would not have breached any duty to disclose. (Choice A) Under the doctrine of merger, all obligations as to title contained within the land-sale contract merge into the deed once it is delivered to and accepted by the buyer. This doctrine does not relate to issues having to do with the physical condition of the property. (Choice C) The covenants in a warranty deed relate to the title of the property, not its physical condition. (Choice D) A warranty of fitness or suitability* is implied in almost all jurisdictions in a contract for the sale of a newly constructed residence, so it does not apply to the sale of this 60-year-old house. *This is also referred to as the implied warranty of quality, workmanlike construction, and habitability.\n\nEducational objective: The common-law doctrine of caveat emptor provides that a seller has no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer unless otherwise agreed.","educationalObjective":"The common-law doctrine of caveat emptor provides that a seller has no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer unless otherwise agreed.","references":"","rule":"The common-law doctrine of caveat emptor provides that a seller has no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer unless otherwise agreed.","irac":{"issue":"Will the purchaser likely be successful?","rule":"The common-law doctrine of caveat emptor provides that a seller has no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer unless otherwise agreed.","analysis":"Seller's duty to disclose\n\nCommon law Caveat emptor No duty to disclose property defects unless (minority) rule otherwise provided\n\nModern (majority) Commercial rule property\n\nResidential Must disclose known material defects that property buyer could not reasonably discover\n\nException: \"as is\" clause or specific disclaimers without seller's fraud The majority of jurisdictions have enacted statutes that require sellers of residential property to disclose any known material defects that cannot be reasonably discovered by the buyer. But since there is no applicable statute in this jurisdiction, the common-law rule of caveat emptor controls. This rule (meaning \"let the buyer beware\") provides that sellers have no duty to disclose property defects to the buyer unless otherwise agreed. Here, the seller gave no warranties and the contract was silent as to the condition of the house, so the seller had no duty to disclose these defects to the buyer. Furthermore, the seller did not know about the defects in the house because she was \"genuinely surprised\" when she was told of the house's condition. So even if this jurisdiction had enacted a statute that followed the majority rule, the seller would not have breached any duty to disclose. (Choice A) Under the doctrine of merger, all obligations as to title contained within the land-sale contract merge into the deed once it is delivered to and accepted by the buyer. This doctrine does not relate to issues having to do with the physical condition of the property. (Choice C) The covenants in a warranty deed relate to the title of the property, not its physical condition. (Choice D) A warranty of fitness or suitability* is implied in almost all jurisdictions in a contract for the sale of a newly constructed residence, so it does not apply to the sale of this 60-year-old house. *This is also referred to as the implied warranty of quality, workmanlike construction, and habitability.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the seller gave no warranty regarding the condition of the house."},"questionZh":"一名卖家和一名买家签订了一套60年楼龄房屋的出售合同。该合同要求在成交时提供保修契约。合同中没有提及房屋的状况，买家也没有询问。买方在成交时收到了一份包含所有所有权契约的保修契约，并立即记录了该契约。关门后大约一个月，屋内的炉子停止工作，地下室被淹，屋顶漏水严重，二楼无法入住。当卖家得知房子的状况时，他真的很惊讶。没有适用的法规。买方已起诉卖方要求赔偿损失。购买者可能会成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为合并原则。","B":"不，因为卖方没有对房屋状况做出任何保证。","C":"是的，根据买方收到的契约中包含的所有权契约。","D":"是的，因为住宅不动产的转让意味着适用性保证。"},"explanationZh":"卖方的披露义务\n\n普通法 买者自负 没有义务披露财产缺陷，除非（少数）规则另有规定\n\n现代（多数）商业规则财产\n\n住宅 必须披露购房者无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷\n\n例外：无卖方欺诈的“按原样”条款或特定免责声明 大多数司法管辖区已颁布法规，要求住宅物业的卖方披露买方无法合理发现的任何已知材料缺陷。但由于该司法管辖区没有适用的法规，因此以普通法的“买者自负”规则为准。该规则（意思是“让买方当心”）规定，除非另有约定，否则卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷。在此，卖方没有做出任何保证，合同也没有提及房屋的状况，因此卖方没有义务向买方披露这些缺陷。此外，卖家并不知道房子的缺陷，因为当她被告知房子的状况时，她“真的很惊讶”。因此，即使该司法管辖区颁布了遵循多数规则的法规，卖方也不会违反任何披露义务。 （选择A）根据合并原则，土地销售合同中包含的所有有关所有权的义务一旦交付给买方并被买方接受，就合并到契约中。该原则不涉及与财产的实际状况有关的问题。 （选择 C） 保修契约中的契约与财产的所有权有关，而不是与财产的实际状况有关。 （选项 D） 几乎所有司法管辖区的新建住宅销售合同中都隐含了适用性或适用性*的保证，因此不适用于出售这栋拥有 60 年历史的房屋。 *这也称为质量、工艺结构和居住性的默示保证。\n\n教育目标：普通法的“买者自负”原则规定，除非另有约定，否则卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷。","educationalObjectiveZh":"普通法中的“买者自负”原则规定，除非另有约定，否则卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷。","iracZh":{"issue":"购买者可能会成功吗？","rule":"普通法中的“买者自负”原则规定，除非另有约定，否则卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷。","analysis":"卖方的披露义务\n\n普通法 买者自负 没有义务披露财产缺陷，除非（少数）规则另有规定\n\n现代（多数）商业规则财产\n\n住宅 必须披露购房者无法合理发现的已知材料缺陷\n\n例外：无卖方欺诈的“按原样”条款或特定免责声明 大多数司法管辖区已颁布法规，要求住宅物业的卖方披露买方无法合理发现的任何已知材料缺陷。但由于该司法管辖区没有适用的法规，因此以普通法的“买者自负”规则为准。该规则（意思是“让买方当心”）规定，除非另有约定，否则卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷。在此，卖方没有做出任何保证，合同也没有提及房屋的状况，因此卖方没有义务向买方披露这些缺陷。此外，卖家并不知道房子的缺陷，因为当她被告知房子的状况时，她“真的很惊讶”。因此，即使该司法管辖区颁布了遵循多数规则的法规，卖方也不会违反任何披露义务。 （选择A）根据合并原则，土地销售合同中包含的所有有关所有权的义务一旦交付给买方并被买方接受，就合并到契约中。该原则不涉及与财产的实际状况有关的问题。 （选择 C） 保修契约中的契约与财产的所有权有关，而不是与财产的实际状况有关。 （选项 D） 几乎所有司法管辖区的新建住宅销售合同中都隐含了适用性或适用性*的保证，因此不适用于出售这栋拥有 60 年历史的房屋。 *这也称为质量、工艺结构和居住性的默示保证。\n\n教育目标：普通法的“买者自负”原则规定，除非另有约定，否则卖方没有义务向买方披露财产缺陷。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为卖方没有对房屋状况做出任何保证。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":244,"sourceQuestionPage":709,"sourceQuestionPages":[709],"sourceExplanationPages":[710,711],"sourceAnswerPages":[710,711],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-245","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A seller contracted to sell a vacant lot to a purchaser. The written contract required the seller to provide evidence of marketable title of record, specified a closing date, stated that \"time is of the essence,\" and provided that at closing, the seller would convey by warranty deed. The purchaser paid the seller $7,000 earnest money toward the $140,000 purchase price. The title evidence showed that an undivided one-eighth interest in the lot was owned by a third party. The purchaser immediately objected to title and said she would not close on the seller's title. The seller responded, accurately, that the third party was his daughter who would be out of the country until two weeks after the specified closing date. He said that she would gladly deed her interest upon her return, and that meanwhile his deed warranting title to all of the lot would fully protect the purchaser. The seller duly tendered his deed on the specified closing date but the purchaser refused to close. The purchaser brought an appropriate action to recover the $7,000 earnest money promptly after the specified closing date. The seller counterclaimed for specific performance, tendering a deed from himself and his daughter, who had by then returned. Which party will likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"The purchaser, because the seller's title was not marketable and time was of the essence.","B":"The purchaser, because under the circumstances the earnest money amount was excessive.","C":"The seller, because his warranty deed would have given the purchaser adequate interim protection.","D":"The seller, because the daughter's deed completing the transfer was given within a reasonable time."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A real estate contract is executed when the seller promises to deliver marketable title and the buyer promises to pay the purchase price. However, these promises need not be performed until closing or a reasonable time thereafter—unless the parties set a strict closing deadline. The closing date will be strictly enforced when the contract includes express language that makes time of the essence or the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be. Here, the contract stated that \"time is of the essence,\" so the seller was required to deliver marketable title at the time of closing—no later. Title is marketable if it is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Since 1/8 of the seller's title was held by his daughter who could not deed her interest to him until two weeks after closing, the seller could not timely deliver marketable title with a warranty deed. Therefore, the seller breached the contract (Choices C & D). When a seller has breached, the buyer's remedies include rescission and restitution. This allows the buyer to cancel the contract and receive the return of any paid deposits and other restitution. As a result, the purchaser will likely prevail in her action to recover the $7,000 earnest-money deposit. (Choice B) A seller may retain the buyer's earnest-money deposit as liquidated damages so long as the deposit is not so excessive that it constitutes a penalty. An amount exceeding 15% of the purchase price is likely excessive, so the buyer's $7,000 deposit (5% of the purchase price) was not excessive.\n\nEducational objective: Performance is strictly due at the time of closing when the real estate contract includes express language that makes time of the essence or when the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be.","educationalObjective":"Performance is strictly due at the time of closing when the real estate contract includes express language that makes time of the essence or when the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be.","references":"","rule":"Performance is strictly due at the time of closing when the real estate contract includes express language that makes time of the essence or when the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be.","irac":{"issue":"Which party will likely prevail?","rule":"Performance is strictly due at the time of closing when the real estate contract includes express language that makes time of the essence or when the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be.","analysis":"A real estate contract is executed when the seller promises to deliver marketable title and the buyer promises to pay the purchase price. However, these promises need not be performed until closing or a reasonable time thereafter—unless the parties set a strict closing deadline. The closing date will be strictly enforced when the contract includes express language that makes time of the essence or the circumstances strongly suggest that the parties intended it to be. Here, the contract stated that \"time is of the essence,\" so the seller was required to deliver marketable title at the time of closing—no later. Title is marketable if it is reasonably free from doubt and under no threat of litigation such that a reasonable person would accept and pay for it. Since 1/8 of the seller's title was held by his daughter who could not deed her interest to him until two weeks after closing, the seller could not timely deliver marketable title with a warranty deed. Therefore, the seller breached the contract (Choices C & D). When a seller has breached, the buyer's remedies include rescission and restitution. This allows the buyer to cancel the contract and receive the return of any paid deposits and other restitution. As a result, the purchaser will likely prevail in her action to recover the $7,000 earnest-money deposit. (Choice B) A seller may retain the buyer's earnest-money deposit as liquidated damages so long as the deposit is not so excessive that it constitutes a penalty. An amount exceeding 15% of the purchase price is likely excessive, so the buyer's $7,000 deposit (5% of the purchase price) was not excessive.","conclusion":"Answer A: The purchaser, because the seller's title was not marketable and time was of the essence."},"questionZh":"卖方签订合同将一块空地出售给买方。书面合同要求卖方提供可销售记录所有权的证据，指定了截止日期，声明“时间至关重要”，并规定在成交时，卖方将通过保修契约进行转让。买方为 140,000 美元的购买价格向卖方支付了 7,000 美元的保证金。产权证据显示，该地块不可分割的八分之一权益由第三方拥有。买方立即反对所有权，并表示她不​​会关闭卖方的所有权。卖家准确地回答说，第三方是他的女儿，她将在指定截止日期后两周离开该国。他说，她回来后很乐意将其权益转让，同时他保证所有土地所有权的契约将充分保护购买者。卖方在指定的交割日期正式提交了其契约，但买方拒绝交割。买方在指定的截止日期后立即采取了适当的行动，收回了 7,000 美元的保证金。卖方提出反诉，要求履行具体义务，并提交了他本人和当时已返回的女儿的契约。哪一方可能会获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"买方，因为卖方的所有权没有市场价值，而且时间至关重要。","B":"买方因当时情况下保证金金额过高。","C":"卖方，因为他的保修契约将为买方提供充分的临时保护。","D":"卖方，因为女儿完成转让的契约是在合理的时间内完成的。"},"explanationZh":"当卖方承诺交付可出售的所有权并且买方承诺支付购买价款时，房地产合同即告执行。然而，这些承诺在交割之前或之后的合理时间内不需要履行——除非双方设定了严格的交割期限。当合同中包含明确规定时间的语言或情况强烈表明双方有意这样做时，截止日期将被严格执行。在此，合同规定“时间至关重要”，因此卖方必须在成交时（不得晚于）交付可出售的所有权。如果产权合理地没有任何疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以致理性的人会接受并支付费用，则该产权是可出售的。由于卖方1/8的所有权由其女儿持有，而女儿直到交割后两周才将其权益转让给他，因此卖方无法及时交付带有保修契约的可出售所有权。因此，卖方违反了合同（选择C&D）。当卖方违约时，买方的补救措施包括解除合同和恢复原状。这允许买方取消合同并收到所有已付押金和其他赔偿的返还。因此，买方很可能会在诉讼中获胜，收回 7,000 美元的保证金。 （选择B） 卖方可以保留买方的保证金作为违约金，只要该保证金不至于构成罚款。超过购买价格 15% 的金额可能过高，因此买家 7,000 美元的定金（购买价格的 5%）并不算过高。\n\n教育目标：当房地产合同包含将时间视为本质的明确语言或当情况强烈表明双方有意这样做时，履约必须在成交时严格履行。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当房地产合同包含将时间视为本质的明确语言或当情况强烈表明双方有意这样做时，履约严格在交割时到期。","iracZh":{"issue":"哪一方可能会获胜？","rule":"当房地产合同包含将时间视为本质的明确语言或当情况强烈表明双方有意这样做时，履约严格在交割时到期。","analysis":"当卖方承诺交付可出售的所有权并且买方承诺支付购买价款时，房地产合同即告执行。然而，这些承诺在交割之前或之后的合理时间内不需要履行——除非双方设定了严格的交割期限。当合同中包含明确规定时间的语言或情况强烈表明双方有意这样做时，截止日期将被严格执行。在此，合同规定“时间至关重要”，因此卖方必须在成交时（不得晚于）交付可出售的所有权。如果产权合理地没有任何疑问且不受诉讼威胁，以致理性的人会接受并支付费用，则该产权是可出售的。由于卖方1/8的所有权由其女儿持有，而女儿直到交割后两周才将其权益转让给他，因此卖方无法及时交付带有保修契约的可出售所有权。因此，卖方违反了合同（选择C&D）。当卖方违约时，买方的补救措施包括解除合同和恢复原状。这允许买方取消合同并收到所有已付押金和其他赔偿的返还。因此，买方很可能会在诉讼中获胜，收回 7,000 美元的保证金。 （选择B） 卖方可以保留买方的保证金作为违约金，只要该保证金不至于构成罚款。超过购买价格 15% 的金额可能过高，因此买家 7,000 美元的定金（购买价格的 5%）并不算过高。\n\n教育目标：当房地产合同包含将时间视为本质的明确语言或当情况强烈表明双方有意这样做时，履约必须在成交时严格履行。","conclusion":"答案 A：买方，因为卖方的所有权没有市场价值，而且时间至关重要。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"earnest money deposit","ipa":"/ˈɝnɪst mˈəni dʌpˈɑzɪt/","syllables":"earnest money de·posit","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"定金：买方签约时支付、依合同用于价款或违约救济的款项","contextualMeaning":"定金：买方签约时支付、依合同用于价款或违约救济的款项","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A seller contracted to sell a vacant lot to a purchaser.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-03d7b2fdf4c598a5"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":245,"sourceQuestionPage":712,"sourceQuestionPages":[712],"sourceExplanationPages":[713],"sourceAnswerPages":[713],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-246","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Deeds & Conveyancing","question":"A woman owned five adjoining rectangular lots, numbered 1 through 5, all fronting a main street. All the lots are in a zone limited to one-family and two-family residences under the zoning ordinance. Two years ago, the woman conveyed Lots 1, 3, and 5 to various owners. None of the three deeds contained any restrictions. Each of the new owners built a one-family residence. One year ago, the woman conveyed Lot 2 to a friend. The deed provided that the friend and the woman, their respective heirs and assigns, would use Lots 2 and 4, respectively, only for one-family residential purposes. The deed was promptly and properly recorded. The friend promptly built a one-family residence on Lot 2. Last month, the woman conveyed Lot 4 to a banker. The deed contained no restrictions. The deed from the woman to the friend was in the title report examined by the banker's lawyer. The banker obtained a building permit and commenced construction of a two-family residence on Lot 4. The friend, joined by the owners of Lots 1, 3, and 5, brought an appropriate action against the banker to enjoin the proposed use of Lot 4 or, alternatively, damages caused by the banker's breach of covenant. Which is the most appropriate comment concerning the outcome of this action?","choices":{"A":"All plaintiffs should be awarded an injunction because there was a common development scheme, but award of damages should be denied to all.","B":"All plaintiffs should be denied any recovery or relief because the zoning preempts any private scheme of covenants.","C":"An injunction should be denied, but damages should be awarded to all plaintiffs, measured by diminution of market value, if any, caused by the proximity of the banker's two-family residence.","D":"The friend should be awarded an appropriate remedy, but recovery by the other plaintiffs is doubtful."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A real covenant is an express promise to do or not do something on land that can be enforced by an action for money damages and binds successors in interest. A real covenant requires the following: Writing – covenant is in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, Lot 2 deed)\n\nIntent to run – promising parties intended for the covenant to run to their successors in interest (eg, deed says \"heirs and assigns\")\n\nTouch and concern – covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of the benefited and burdened lands (eg, limiting Lot 4's use for Lot 2's benefit)\n\nHorizontal privity – promising parties simultaneously transferred the land and created the covenant (eg, covenant created when woman conveyed Lot 2 to the friend)\n\nVertical privity – successor of the benefited estate has a possessory interest (eg, friend owns Lot 2) and successor of the burdened estate has a promising party's same ownership interest (eg, banker owns Lot 4)\n\nNotice – person to be bound had notice of the covenant (eg, recorded deed was in the title report examined by banker's lawyer)\n\nAn express equitable servitude is essentially a real covenant enforced in equity (often by an injunction). Since the promise here has the same elements as a real covenant except privity, the friend can receive an injunction or money damages for the bank's breach of covenant—whichever the court deems appropriate (Choice C). Since the restriction pertains only to Lots 2 and 4, the other plaintiffs can prevail only if an equitable servitude can be implied from a common scheme or development plan. One cannot be implied here because Lots 1, 3, and 5 were conveyed without any restrictions and before the restriction was created in the Lot 2 deed. Therefore, their recovery is doubtful (Choice A). (Choice B) Zoning ordinances override restrictive covenants only when they are in direct conflict—eg, if Lot 4 had been zoned for one-family residences and the covenant restricted use to two-family residences.\n\nEducational objective: A real covenant is an express promise to do or not do something on land that can be enforced by an action for money damages. An express equitable servitude is a real covenant enforced in equity. An implied equitable servitude may be created by a common scheme.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 5.1 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (automatic passage of the burden and benefit with appurtenant land).\n\nRestatement of Property § 539 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of equitable servitudes).\n\nRestatement of Property §§ 530–38 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of real covenants).","educationalObjective":"A real covenant is an express promise to do or not do something on land that can be enforced by an action for money damages. An express equitable servitude is a real covenant enforced in equity. An implied equitable servitude may be created by a common scheme.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Property: Servitudes § 5.1 (Am. Law Inst. 2000) (automatic passage of the burden and benefit with appurtenant land).\n\nRestatement of Property § 539 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of equitable servitudes).\n\nRestatement of Property §§ 530–38 (Am. Law Inst. 1944) (elements of real covenants).","rule":"A real covenant is an express promise to do or not do something on land that can be enforced by an action for money damages. An express equitable servitude is a real covenant enforced in equity. An implied equitable servitude may be created by a common scheme.","irac":{"issue":"Which is the most appropriate comment concerning the outcome of this action?","rule":"A real covenant is an express promise to do or not do something on land that can be enforced by an action for money damages. An express equitable servitude is a real covenant enforced in equity. An implied equitable servitude may be created by a common scheme.","analysis":"A real covenant is an express promise to do or not do something on land that can be enforced by an action for money damages and binds successors in interest. A real covenant requires the following: Writing – covenant is in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (eg, Lot 2 deed)\n\nIntent to run – promising parties intended for the covenant to run to their successors in interest (eg, deed says \"heirs and assigns\")\n\nTouch and concern – covenant relates to the use, enjoyment, or occupation of the benefited and burdened lands (eg, limiting Lot 4's use for Lot 2's benefit)\n\nHorizontal privity – promising parties simultaneously transferred the land and created the covenant (eg, covenant created when woman conveyed Lot 2 to the friend)\n\nVertical privity – successor of the benefited estate has a possessory interest (eg, friend owns Lot 2) and successor of the burdened estate has a promising party's same ownership interest (eg, banker owns Lot 4)\n\nNotice – person to be bound had notice of the covenant (eg, recorded deed was in the title report examined by banker's lawyer)\n\nAn express equitable servitude is essentially a real covenant enforced in equity (often by an injunction). Since the promise here has the same elements as a real covenant except privity, the friend can receive an injunction or money damages for the bank's breach of covenant—whichever the court deems appropriate (Choice C). Since the restriction pertains only to Lots 2 and 4, the other plaintiffs can prevail only if an equitable servitude can be implied from a common scheme or development plan. One cannot be implied here because Lots 1, 3, and 5 were conveyed without any restrictions and before the restriction was created in the Lot 2 deed. Therefore, their recovery is doubtful (Choice A). (Choice B) Zoning ordinances override restrictive covenants only when they are in direct conflict—eg, if Lot 4 had been zoned for one-family residences and the covenant restricted use to two-family residences.","conclusion":"Answer D: The friend should be awarded an appropriate remedy, but recovery by the other plaintiffs is doubtful."},"questionZh":"一名妇女拥有五块相邻的矩形地块，编号为 1 至 5，全部面向一条主要街道。根据土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例，所有地块都位于仅限一户和两户住宅的区域内。两年前，该女子将1号、3号和5号地块转让给了不同的业主。这三份契约均不包含任何限制。每个新主人都建造了一套独户住宅。一年前，该女士将第 2 号拍品转让给一位朋友。该契约规定，朋友和女子（他们各自的继承人和受让人）将分别使用 2 号和 4 号地块，仅用于单户住宅用途。该行为被及时、正确地记录下来。这位朋友立即在 2 号地块上建造了一套独户住宅。上个月，该女子将 4 号地块转让给了一位银行家。该契约不包含任何限制。银行家律师审查过的产权报告中包含了该女子与朋友之间的契约。银行家获得了建筑许可证，并开始在 4 号地块上建造一栋两户住宅。他的朋友与 1、3 和 5 号地块的业主一起，对银行家提起适当诉讼，禁止对 4 号地块的拟议使用，或者要求银行家因违反契约而造成损害。对于这一行动的结果，哪项最恰当的评论是？","choicesZh":{"A":"所有原告都应获得禁令，因为存在共同的开发计划，但不应向所有人授予损害赔偿金。","B":"所有原告都应被拒绝获得任何赔偿或救济，因为土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）优先于任何私人契约计划。","C":"应驳回禁令，但应向所有原告判以损害赔偿，损害赔偿的衡量标准是由于银行家的两户住宅靠近而造成的市场价值（如果有的话）。","D":"该朋友应该获得适当的补救措施，但其他原告的赔偿情况值得怀疑。"},"explanationZh":"土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做或不做某事的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼来强制执行，并约束继承人的利益。土地实质契约（real covenant）需要满足以下条件： 书面形式 – 契约是满足欺诈法规的书面形式（例如，Lot 2 契约）\n\n竞选意图 – 承诺各方有意向其利益继承者竞选（例如，契约上写着“继承人和受让人”）\n\n接触和关注——与受益和负担土地的使用、享有或占用有关的契约（例如，限制地块 4 的使用以获得地块 2 的利益）\n\n横向相互关系——承诺方同时转让土地并订立契约（例如，妇女将第 2 号地块转让给朋友时订立的契约）\n\n纵向关联性——受益遗产的继承人拥有占有权益（例如，朋友拥有2号地块），而负担遗产的继承人则拥有承诺方相同的所有权权益（例如，银行家拥有4号地块）\n\n通知 – 受约束人已收到该契约的通知（例如，记录的契约包含在银行家律师审查的产权报告中）\n\n明示的公平劳役实质上是按照衡平法强制执行的真正契约（通常通过禁令）。由于这里的承诺与土地实质契约（real covenant）具有相同的要素（除了相互关系之外），因此朋友可以因银行违反契约而获得禁令或金钱赔偿——以法院认为适当的为准（选择C）。由于该限制仅适用于第 2 号和第 4 号地块，因此只有在共同计划或开发计划中可以暗示公平的劳役时，其他原告才能胜诉。这里不能暗示这一点，因为第 1、3 和 5 号地块是在没有任何限制的情况下转让的，并且是在第 2 号地块契约中创建限制之前。因此，他们的康复是值得怀疑的（选择A）。 （选择 B）土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例仅在直接冲突时才凌驾于限制性契约之上，例如，如果第 4 号地块已划为一户住宅，而契约限制为两户住宅使用。\n\n教育目标：土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做或不做某事的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼来强制执行。明确的公平劳役是公平执行的真正契约。隐含的公平劳役可以通过共同方案来创建。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 5.1（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（随从属土地自动转移负担和利益）。\n\n财产重述 § 539（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（公平劳役的要素）。\n\n财产重述 §§ 530–38（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（真实契约的要素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做或不做某事的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼来强制执行。明确的公平劳役是公平执行的真正契约。隐含的公平劳役可以通过共同方案来创建。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于此行动的结果最恰当的评论是什么？","rule":"土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做或不做某事的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼来强制执行。明确的公平劳役是公平执行的真正契约。隐含的公平劳役可以通过共同方案来创建。","analysis":"土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做或不做某事的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼来强制执行，并约束继承人的利益。土地实质契约（real covenant）需要满足以下条件： 书面形式 – 契约是满足欺诈法规的书面形式（例如，Lot 2 契约）\n\n竞选意图 – 承诺各方有意向其利益继承者竞选（例如，契约上写着“继承人和受让人”）\n\n接触和关注——与受益和负担土地的使用、享有或占用有关的契约（例如，限制地块 4 的使用以获得地块 2 的利益）\n\n横向相互关系——承诺方同时转让土地并订立契约（例如，妇女将第 2 号地块转让给朋友时订立的契约）\n\n纵向关联性——受益遗产的继承人拥有占有权益（例如，朋友拥有2号地块），而负担遗产的继承人则拥有承诺方相同的所有权权益（例如，银行家拥有4号地块）\n\n通知 – 受约束人已收到该契约的通知（例如，记录的契约包含在银行家律师审查的产权报告中）\n\n明示的公平劳役实质上是按照衡平法强制执行的真正契约（通常通过禁令）。由于这里的承诺与土地实质契约（real covenant）具有相同的要素（除了相互关系之外），因此朋友可以因银行违反契约而获得禁令或金钱赔偿——以法院认为适当的为准（选择C）。由于该限制仅适用于第 2 号和第 4 号地块，因此只有在共同计划或开发计划中可以暗示公平的劳役时，其他原告才能胜诉。这里不能暗示这一点，因为第 1、3 和 5 号地块是在没有任何限制的情况下转让的，并且是在第 2 号地块契约中创建限制之前。因此，他们的康复是值得怀疑的（选择A）。 （选择 B）土地土地分区（zoning）（zoning）条例仅在直接冲突时才凌驾于限制性契约之上，例如，如果第 4 号地块已划为一户住宅，而契约限制为两户住宅使用。\n\n教育目标：土地实质契约（real covenant）是在土地上做或不做某事的明确承诺，可以通过金钱赔偿诉讼来强制执行。明确的公平劳役是公平执行的真正契约。隐含的公平劳役可以通过共同方案来创建。\n\n参考文献\n财产重述（第三次）：劳役 § 5.1（Am. Law Inst. 2000）（随从属土地自动转移负担和利益）。\n\n财产重述 § 539（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（公平劳役的要素）。\n\n财产重述 §§ 530–38（Am. Law Inst. 1944）（真实契约的要素）。","conclusion":"答案 D：该朋友应该获得适当的补救措施，但其他原告的赔偿情况值得怀疑。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-0834031a6b1b2e2e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":246,"sourceQuestionPage":714,"sourceQuestionPages":[714],"sourceExplanationPages":[715,716],"sourceAnswerPages":[715,716],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-247","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Real Property","question":"A homeowner listed his home for sale with a real estate broker. The written six-month exclusive-right-to-sell listing agreement provided for the payment of a commission if the home sold. In accordance with the listing agreement, the broker promptly took reasonable steps to market the home, incurring expenses for her efforts. Five months into the listing period, without involving the broker, the homeowner accepted an offer to purchase from his cousin. The broker learned of the contract only when the sale of the home to the cousin closed, one month after the listing period had expired. Is the broker entitled to any payment?","choices":{"A":"No, because the broker engaged in no negotiations with the cousin.","B":"No, because the closing occurred after the listing period had expired.","C":"Yes, but only reimbursement for her expenses.","D":"Yes, the full commission, because the homeowner accepted the cousin's offer to purchase during the listing period."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A real estate broker is someone employed by a seller to handle the sale of real property. The employment agreement is typically referred to as a listing agreement. There are three types of listing agreements: Open – where the seller may use other brokers to sell the listed property and the commission goes to whichever broker finds a buyer during the listing period Exclusive agency contract – where the seller can find a purchaser and, if the seller does so without the broker's assistance, the broker receives no commission Exclusive right to sell – where the commission goes to the broker if the property is sold during the listing period, no matter who finds the buyer Unless the listing agreement states otherwise, the broker is entitled to commission if (1) a ready, willing, and able buyer is produced or (2) the seller enters into a sales contract with a buyer during the listing period. This is true even if the buyer backs out of the sale or the sale is not otherwise closed (Choice B). Here, the homeowner and broker entered into an exclusive-right-to-sell listing agreement. The homeowner entered into a contract of sale when he accepted the cousin's offer to purchase the home during the listing period. As a result, the broker is entitled to the full commission—regardless of whether the broker engaged in negotiations with the cousin (Choices A & C).\n\nEducational objective: A real estate broker is entitled to commission if (1) a ready, willing, and able buyer is produced or (2) the seller enters into a sales contract with a buyer during the listing period.","educationalObjective":"A real estate broker is entitled to commission if (1) a ready, willing, and able buyer is produced or (2) the seller enters into a sales contract with a buyer during the listing period.","references":"","rule":"A real estate broker is entitled to commission if (1) a ready, willing, and able buyer is produced or (2) the seller enters into a sales contract with a buyer during the listing period.","irac":{"issue":"Is the broker entitled to any payment?","rule":"A real estate broker is entitled to commission if (1) a ready, willing, and able buyer is produced or (2) the seller enters into a sales contract with a buyer during the listing period.","analysis":"A real estate broker is someone employed by a seller to handle the sale of real property. The employment agreement is typically referred to as a listing agreement. There are three types of listing agreements: Open – where the seller may use other brokers to sell the listed property and the commission goes to whichever broker finds a buyer during the listing period Exclusive agency contract – where the seller can find a purchaser and, if the seller does so without the broker's assistance, the broker receives no commission Exclusive right to sell – where the commission goes to the broker if the property is sold during the listing period, no matter who finds the buyer Unless the listing agreement states otherwise, the broker is entitled to commission if (1) a ready, willing, and able buyer is produced or (2) the seller enters into a sales contract with a buyer during the listing period. This is true even if the buyer backs out of the sale or the sale is not otherwise closed (Choice B). Here, the homeowner and broker entered into an exclusive-right-to-sell listing agreement. The homeowner entered into a contract of sale when he accepted the cousin's offer to purchase the home during the listing period. As a result, the broker is entitled to the full commission—regardless of whether the broker engaged in negotiations with the cousin (Choices A & C).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, the full commission, because the homeowner accepted the cousin's offer to purchase during the listing period."},"questionZh":"一位房主向房地产经纪人挂牌出售自己的房屋。书面的六个月独家销售权挂牌协议规定，如果房屋出售，则需支付佣金。根据上市协议，经纪人迅速采取合理措施推销该房屋，并因此产生了费用。上市五个月后，在没有经纪人介入的情况下，房主接受了他表弟的购买要约。经纪人直到挂牌期满一个月后，将房子出售给表兄的交易结束后，才得知了这份合同。经纪人是否有权获得任何付款？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为经纪人没有与表弟进行任何谈判。","B":"不可以，因为交割发生在上市期满之后。","C":"是的，但只是报销她的费用。","D":"是的，全额佣金，因为房主在挂牌期间接受了表弟的购买要约。"},"explanationZh":"房地产经纪人是卖方雇用的负责处理房地产销售的人员。雇佣协议通常被称为上市协议。挂牌协议分为三种类型： 开放式 – 卖方可以使用其他经纪人来出售挂牌房产，而佣金将归于在挂牌期间找到买家的经纪人 独家代理合同 – 卖方可以找到买家，如果卖方在没有经纪人协助的情况下这样做，经纪人不会收到佣金 排他性出售权 – 如果房产在挂牌期间出售，无论谁找到买家，佣金将归经纪人 除非挂牌协议另有规定，否则经纪人有权收取佣金，如果 (1) 已准备好，产生愿意且有能力的买家或（2）卖方在挂牌期间与买方签订销售合同。即使买方退出销售或销售并未以其他方式结束（选择 B），情况也是如此。在这里，房主和经纪人签订了独家销售权挂牌协议。房主在挂牌期间接受表弟购买房屋的要约，并签订了销售合同。因此，经纪人有权获得全额佣金——无论经纪人是否与表兄弟进行谈判（选择 A 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 出现了准备好的、愿意且有能力的买家，或者 (2) 卖方在挂牌期间与买方签订了销售合同，则房地产经纪人有权获得佣金。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果 (1) 出现了准备好的、愿意且有能力的买家，或者 (2) 卖方在挂牌期间与买方签订了销售合同，则房地产经纪人有权收取佣金。","iracZh":{"issue":"经纪人是否有权获得任何付款？","rule":"如果 (1) 出现了准备好的、愿意且有能力的买家，或者 (2) 卖方在挂牌期间与买方签订了销售合同，则房地产经纪人有权收取佣金。","analysis":"房地产经纪人是卖方雇用的负责处理房地产销售的人员。雇佣协议通常被称为上市协议。挂牌协议分为三种类型： 开放式 – 卖方可以使用其他经纪人来出售挂牌房产，而佣金将归于在挂牌期间找到买家的经纪人 独家代理合同 – 卖方可以找到买家，如果卖方在没有经纪人协助的情况下这样做，经纪人不会收到佣金 排他性出售权 – 如果房产在挂牌期间出售，无论谁找到买家，佣金将归经纪人 除非挂牌协议另有规定，否则经纪人有权收取佣金，如果 (1) 已准备好，产生愿意且有能力的买家或（2）卖方在挂牌期间与买方签订销售合同。即使买方退出销售或销售并未以其他方式结束（选择 B），情况也是如此。在这里，房主和经纪人签订了独家销售权挂牌协议。房主在挂牌期间接受表弟购买房屋的要约，并签订了销售合同。因此，经纪人有权获得全额佣金——无论经纪人是否与表兄弟进行谈判（选择 A 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 出现了准备好的、愿意且有能力的买家，或者 (2) 卖方在挂牌期间与买方签订了销售合同，则房地产经纪人有权获得佣金。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，全额佣金，因为房主在挂牌期间接受了表弟的购买要约。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-fafb7d52ab72d1ec"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":247,"sourceQuestionPage":717,"sourceQuestionPages":[717],"sourceExplanationPages":[718],"sourceAnswerPages":[718],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-real-property-248","subject":"Real Property","topic":"Land Sale Contracts","question":"A buyer and a seller entered into a written contract for the sale of an identified parcel of land. The contract expressly provided that the buyer was to pay $150,000 cash for the land at the time of the closing but did not state the closing date. The parties had not agreed on the closing date because the buyer was not sure at the time the contract was signed how she would raise the cash. Fifteen days after the contract was signed, the seller learned that he could sell the land to a third party for $200,000. The seller asked the buyer if she would agree to rescind the contract. The buyer refused. The seller then told her that he would not complete the transaction, contending that the contract was unenforceable under the statute of frauds because an essential element (time for performance) was not agreed upon by the parties and was not expressly stated in the written agreement. The seller sold the land to the third party. The buyer brought an appropriate action against the seller for breach of contract. For which party is the court likely to find?","choices":{"A":"The buyer, because of the doctrine of unjust enrichment.","B":"The buyer, because the court will infer that performance within a reasonable time was intended.","C":"The seller, because the contract is unenforceable under the statute of frauds.","D":"The seller, because time of performance is presumed to be of the essence."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Contracts for the sale of land are enforceable only if they comply with the statute of frauds. This statute requires that these contracts be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Essential terms often include: the identity of the parties words of intent to buy or sell a description of the property and the sales price. However, time for performance is not an essential term. Instead, courts will infer that performance within a reasonable time was intended. And since the contract here satisfies all the aforementioned requirements, it is enforceable under the statute of frauds (Choice C). (Choice A) Unjust enrichment does not apply when, as here, the claimant has not given anything of value. Therefore, the buyer will not prevail on this basis. (Choice D) Time is not presumed to be of the essence unless it is expressly stated in the contract (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Under the statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of land must be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Time for performance is not an essential term, and courts will presume that performance with a reasonable time was intended if no time is listed.\n\nReferences\n73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).","educationalObjective":"Under the statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of land must be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Time for performance is not an essential term, and courts will presume that performance with a reasonable time was intended if no time is listed.","references":"73 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds §§ 289, 305, 325, 329 (2020).","rule":"Under the statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of land must be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Time for performance is not an essential term, and courts will presume that performance with a reasonable time was intended if no time is listed.","irac":{"issue":"For which party is the court likely to find?","rule":"Under the statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of land must be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Time for performance is not an essential term, and courts will presume that performance with a reasonable time was intended if no time is listed.","analysis":"Contracts for the sale of land are enforceable only if they comply with the statute of frauds. This statute requires that these contracts be in writing, be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and contain all essential terms. Essential terms often include: the identity of the parties words of intent to buy or sell a description of the property and the sales price. However, time for performance is not an essential term. Instead, courts will infer that performance within a reasonable time was intended. And since the contract here satisfies all the aforementioned requirements, it is enforceable under the statute of frauds (Choice C). (Choice A) Unjust enrichment does not apply when, as here, the claimant has not given anything of value. Therefore, the buyer will not prevail on this basis. (Choice D) Time is not presumed to be of the essence unless it is expressly stated in the contract (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: The buyer, because the court will infer that performance within a reasonable time was intended."},"questionZh":"买方和卖方签订了一份书面合同，出售一块指定的土地。合同明确规定买方在交割时支付15万美元现金购买土地，但没有说明交割日期。双方尚未就交割日期达成一致，因为买方在签订合同时不确定如何筹集现金。合同签订十五天后，卖方得知他可以将土地以20万美元的价格出售给第三方。卖方询问买方是否同意解除合同。买家拒绝了。随后，卖方告诉她，他不会完成交易，并声称根据欺诈法，该合同无法执行，因为双方未就一项基本要素（履行时间）达成一致，并且书面协议中也没有明确说明。卖方将土地出售给第三方。买方因卖方违约而对卖方提起适当诉讼。法院可能会支持哪一方？","choicesZh":{"A":"买方，因不当得利原则。","B":"买方，因为法院会推断买方有意在合理时间内履行义务。","C":"卖方，因为根据欺诈法，合同无法执行。","D":"卖方，因为履行时间被认为是至关重要的。"},"explanationZh":"土地出售合同只有在符合欺诈法的情况下才可执行。该法规要求这些合同采用书面形式，由寻求执行的一方签署，并包含所有基本条款。基本条款通常包括：当事人身份、购买或出售意向、财产描述和销售价格等。然而，表演时间并不是一个必要术语。相反，法院将推断在合理时间内的履行是有意的。由于此处的合同满足所有上述要求，因此根据欺诈法可以强制执行（选项 C）。 （选择 A）当索赔人没有给予任何有价物时，不当得利不适用，就像这里一样。因此，买方不会以此为依据获胜。 （选项 D）除非合同中明确规定（此处未见），否则时间不被认为是至关重要的。\n\n教育目标：根据反欺诈法，土地出售合同必须采用书面形式，由被强制执行方签署，并包含所有基本条款。履行时间不是一个必要术语，如果没有列出时间，法院将推定以合理时间履行。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据反欺诈法，土地出售合同必须采用书面形式，由被强制执行方签署，并包含所有基本条款。履行时间不是一个必要术语，如果没有列出时间，法院将推定以合理时间履行。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会支持哪一方？","rule":"根据反欺诈法，土地出售合同必须采用书面形式，由被强制执行方签署，并包含所有基本条款。履行时间不是一个必要术语，如果没有列出时间，法院将推定以合理时间履行。","analysis":"土地出售合同只有在符合欺诈法的情况下才可执行。该法规要求这些合同采用书面形式，由寻求执行的一方签署，并包含所有基本条款。基本条款通常包括：当事人身份、购买或出售意向、财产描述和销售价格等。然而，表演时间并不是一个必要术语。相反，法院将推断在合理时间内的履行是有意的。由于此处的合同满足所有上述要求，因此根据欺诈法可以强制执行（选项 C）。 （选择 A）当索赔人没有给予任何有价物时，不当得利不适用，就像这里一样。因此，买方不会以此为依据获胜。 （选项 D）除非合同中明确规定（此处未见），否则时间不被认为是至关重要的。\n\n教育目标：根据反欺诈法，土地出售合同必须采用书面形式，由被强制执行方签署，并包含所有基本条款。履行时间不是一个必要术语，如果没有列出时间，法院将推定以合理时间履行。\n\n参考文献\n73 上午。尤尔。 2d 《反欺诈法》第 289、305、325、329 (2020) 条。","conclusion":"答案 B：买方，因为法院会推断买方有意在合理时间内履行义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-03","noteZh":"已按当前 NCBE MBE Real Property 考试范围复核不动产所有权、土地交易、抵押、登记、租赁、地役权与土地使用规则；本题所用规则仍属当前 MBE 可考范围，未发现需要改变答案的后续法律变化。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Real Property)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"NCBE — Preparing for the MBE","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/exams/mbe/preparing-mbe"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["real-property-chart-0ee184140caffe0f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Real Property.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":248,"sourceQuestionPage":719,"sourceQuestionPages":[719],"sourceExplanationPages":[720,721],"sourceAnswerPages":[720,721],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"}];
